<h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs, male patronymic suffix)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix added to a father's name to form a masculine patronymic</li></ol><p>Parallel to <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs), presumably masculine <i>-ης</i> (-ēs) appended to adjective <i>-ᾱ́ς</i> (-ā́s, “of, pertaining to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form verbs from nouns, adjectives, and other verbs.</li><li>Added to verb stems to create a frequentative form.</li></ol><p>Metanalyzed suffix, formed by analogy to verbs from nominal stems in -αδ-, -αγ- with <i>&#42;-yéti</i> suffix added. Possibly cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-atjaną</i>, compare <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to the stems of cardinal numerals, adjectives, and pronouns to form adverbs of repetition</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) an adjective-forming suffix</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix for instrument nouns.</li></ol><p>Perhaps <i>-νος</i> (-nos) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē) on α stems like Latin <i>-ānus</i> and Latin <i>-ārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A neuter diminutive suffix.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-arch (ruler, leader)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρχή</i> (ărkhḗ, “rule; government”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms nouns with participial function.</li><li>Forms nouns with collective function.</li><li>Forms designations of young animals.</li><li>Forms derivatives of nasal stems extended with -t-.</li><li>Forms abstract or collective nouns of number from numerals.</li><li>Forms feminine adjectives or nouns.</li><li>Forms female patronymics from name of father.</li></ol><p>Related to <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, verb-forming suffix) and possibly <i>-ων</i> (-ōn). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>-अत्</i> (-at) and Hittite <i>-𒀭𒍝</i>.</p><p>Possibly related to -ις (-is), also to Etymology 1. The form <i>-ιάς</i> (-iás) comes from analogy to forms like <i>Θεστιάς</i> (Thestiás) from <i>Θέστιος</i> (Théstios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms verbs, usually from nouns in <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā), <i>-η</i> (-ē)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-eh₂yéti</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>-āre</i> (the whole first conjugation in the present) together with its Proto-Italic ancestor <i>*-āō</i>, <i>-अयति</i> (-ayati) (for the causative in Sanskrit), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;-āti</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ōną</i> (referring again to the whole conjugation in which the infinitive is <i>*-ōną</i>) and Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ā́ˀtei</i> (whence the infinite Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ati</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms the names of trees</li></ol><p>Feminine nominalization of <i>-εος</i> (-eos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form verbs from nouns, adjectives and other verbs. Often, not always, added to the stems of thematic nouns or adjectives in <i>-ος</i> (-os).</li></ol><p>There are five main sources:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>denotes an instrument or place of doing something</li></ol><p>From <i>-ή-</i> (-ḗ-) +&lrm; <i>-θρᾱ</i> (-thrā), from <i>-θρον</i> (-thron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>oxytone form of <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjective-forming suffix)</li><li>oxytone form of <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, proper noun-forming suffix)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to stems of adjectives, rarely to the stems of verbs, and even more rarely to the stems of nouns, to form feminine abstract nouns</li></ol><p>Like <i>-ια</i> (-ia), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-i-eh₂</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>-ia</i> and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ьja</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to words to form a male name.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to a father's name to form a masculine patronymic: son of</li></ol><p>Presumably masculine <i>-ης</i> (-ēs) appended to adjective <i>-ίς</i> (-ís, “of, pertaining to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffixed to nouns of any gender, forms neuter diminutive nouns</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used to form similative verbs from nouns, adjectives and other verbs.</li><li>Used to form verbs from proper nouns of cities, demonyms, to denote&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>adherence to the policy of the city</li><li>mimicking the style and behaviour of the demonym</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-íďďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-idyéti</i> (verbal suffix), from <i>&#42;-id-</i> + <i>&#42;-yéti</i>. Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-itjaną</i>.</p><p>The suffix started in denominative verbs in <i>*-id-yō</i> (e.g. <i>elpid-yō</i> &gt; <i>ἐλπίζω</i> (elpízō), cf. <i>ἐλπίς</i> (elpís)), but was later used on stems of all kinds to the number of many thousands.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to masculine or neuter nouns to give a feminine form.</li></ol><p>From <i>-νος</i> (-nos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾰ</i> (-ĭ́ă), with metathesis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>oxytone form of <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Noun-forming diminutive suffix.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-iskos</i>, and cognate with English <i>-ish</i>. For more, see <i>-ish</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix forming a masculine noun: one connected to, a member of; one from a particular place (<i>demonym</i>)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from a rebracketing of <i>πολῑ́της</i> (polī́tēs, <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, demonym suffix)), by reanalysing it as <i>πόλ-</i> (pól-) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>a suffix added to stems of nouns to form neuter diminutives</li></ol><p>From the suffix <i>-ίκιον</i> (-íkion), itself from a reanalysis of diminutive forms as Ancient Greek <i>περδίκιον</i> (perdíkion) from <i>πέρδιξ</i> (pérdix), or <i>σκωλήκιον</i> (skōlḗkion) from <i>σκώληξ</i> (skṓlēx), hence Byzantine Greek <i>σκουλήκι</i> (skoulḗki), both formed with the diminutive suffix <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p><p>A parallel to the supposed form <i>&#42;κορίκιον</i> (&#42;koríkion) can be seen in Byzantine Greek <i>σκουλαρίκιον</i> (skoularíkion, “earring”) from <i>σχολάριος</i> (skholários, “member of the Palace guard”), hence Greek <i>σκουλαρίκι</i> (skoularíki).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) Suffix added to father's name that forms masculine patronymic, meaning "son of"</li><li>Suffix added to some adjectival stems to form a comparative adjective: -er</li></ol><p>Perhaps taken from Anatolian.</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-ihō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-isō</i>, from <i>&#42;-yōs</i> (whence <i>-ων</i> (-ōn)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to adjectival roots to form an adverb&#58; -ly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-ā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-éh₂</i>. Cognate with Latin feminine <i>-a</i>, from Old Latin and Proto-Italic <i>&#42;-ā</i> (both in names and adjectives).</p><p>Uncertain. Some proposals have been:</p><p>Or from a combination of those.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix of nouns in the first declension.</li><li>Added to verbal stems ending in a consonant to form an action noun.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Added to <i>o</i>-grade of the verbal stem</li><li>Added to zero-grade of the verbal stem</li><li>Added to <i>e</i>-grade of the verbal stem</li></ol><li>Added to masculine or neuter nouns to give a feminine form.</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā), from Proto-Hellenic <i>*-ā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-eh₂</i>. The earlier <i>-ᾱ</i> is retained in certain dialects, and in Attic after ε, ι or ρ, whence -εā, -ιā and -ρā (e.g., <i>ἀγορά agorā</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Latin feminine <i>-a</i>, from Old Latin and Proto-Italic <i>*-ā</i> both in names and adjectives, e.g., <i>φυγή</i> (phugḗ, "retreat, escape") &lt; Proto-Hellenic <i>*pʰugā́</i> &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>*bʰugéh₂</i>; at the same time, <i>*bʰugéh₂</i> &gt; Proto-Italic <i>*fugā</i> (preserved in Old Latin) &gt; Classical Latin <i>fuga</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms mostly stative verbs from nominal stems in <i>-ᾰν-</i> (-ăn-) plus Proto-Indo-European verbal suffixes <i>&#42;-yeti</i> or <i>&#42;-yéti</i></li><li>Later, forms mostly factitive verbs from nouns with original n-stem</li><li>Then added to liquid consonant adjective stems</li><li>Then added to other nominal stems such as o-stem adjectives</li></ol><p>Originally from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-əňňō</i>, from either Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-n̥yeti</i> or <i>&#42;-m̥yeti</i>, then freely used.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to the stem of a masculine noun to form a corresponding feminine noun.</li></ol><p>From <i>-ινα</i> (-ina) on α stems.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to noun stems to form adjectives: of or pertaining to, in the manner of; -ac</li></ol><p>From <i>-κός</i> (-kós) with an added α from <i>ā</i>-stems and later extended to other nouns; piecewise doublet of <i>-ηκός</i> (-ēkós). Cognate with Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;-ākos</i> (whence Old Irish <i>-ach</i> and Welsh <i>-og</i>), Latin <i>-āx</i>, and perhaps some cases of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-agaz</i> (whence Old Norse <i>-agr</i> and Gothic <i>-𐌰𐌲𐍃</i> (-ags)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>hundred</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-kətio-</i> (compare <i>&#42;hekətón</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ḱm̥tiyo-</i> or <i>&#42;-ḱm̥tiHo-</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱm̥tóm</i> (“hundred”). The preceding long <i>ā</i> as well as the <i>o</i> vocalism in Attic–Ionic are taken over from the tens, e.g. <i>τριᾱ́κοντα</i> (triā́konta, “thirty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms adjectives from roots</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>&#42;-αλος</i> (&#42;-alos) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos), but the second element is semantically problematic, and further etymology is unknown. The first element is related to the Caland system.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ing, tending to</li><li>-able</li><li>forms relational adjectives, especially from place names</li></ol><p>Rebracketing of <i>-νός</i> (-nós) when attached to a stem ending in <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-archy (form of government or rule)</li></ol><p>From <i>-ᾰ́ρχης</i> (-ắrkhēs, “leader”, “ruler”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The nominative singular ending of masculine first-declension nouns in most dialects. Replaced by <i>-ης</i> (-ēs) in all cases in Ionic, and in most cases in Attic.</li><li>The genitive singular ending of feminine first-declension nouns and adjectives in many dialects. Replaced by <i>-ης</i> (-ēs) in all cases in Ionic, and in most cases in Attic.</li><li>The accusative plural ending of all first-declension nouns and adjectives in many dialects. <i>-ᾰς</i> (-ăs) or <i>-ᾰνς</i> (-ăns), <i>-αις</i> (-ais), and <i>-αιρ</i> (-air) are used by dialects attested from inscriptions.</li><li>The accusative plural ending of most third-declension nouns and many third-declension adjectives. <i>-ᾰνς</i> (-ăns) is also used in the Cretan dialect.</li><li>The accusative plural ending of first-declension nouns and adjectives in various dialects attested in inscriptions&#58; see Appendix&#58;Ancient Greek dialectal declension § First declension.</li><li>first aorist active participle ending</li></ol><p>Metanalysis of <i>&#42;-sants</i>, a combination of the typical suffix of the first aorist active stem <i>*-sa</i> and <i>&#42;-nts</i> (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-onts</i>). Several other participial formations also descend from a form in <i>&#42;-nts</i>: the present active <i>-ων</i> (-ōn), the second aorist active in <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn), the contracted present active and contracted future active in <i>-ῶν</i> (-ôn), the aorist passive in <i>-είς</i> (-eís), and several present active and aorist active athematic forms like <i>ἱστᾱ́ς</i> (histā́s) and <i>στᾱ́ς</i> (stā́s).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to nouns or adjectives, forming adjectives: pertaining to, belonging to ("of").</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>-α-</i> (-a-) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>forming nouns</i>) masculine: one who walks on what is defined by the first combining form</li></ol><p>From <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “I walk”), <i>βᾰτός</i> (bătós, “passable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>birth</li></ol><p>From <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs) + <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>forming adjectives</i>) born in a certain place or condition</li><li>(<i>forming adjectives</i>) of a kind</li></ol><p>From <i>γένος</i> (génos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>writer</li><li>writing, describing</li></ol><p>From <i>γρᾰ́φω</i> (grắphō, “to write”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to a country</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-n̥kʷos</i>, seen in Latin <i>prop-inquus</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ingaz</i>. The <i>d</i> may come from a reanalysis of <i>ποδαπός</i> (podapós, “from what country?”), which would be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷod-n̥kʷós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to noun to form an adverb of motion towards: to, for...;...-wards</li><li>Added to demonstratives to form proximal demonstratives</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;de</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verbal nouns or the roots of verbs to form a deverbal adverb</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</li></ol><p>For <i>&#42;-εϝῐ́ᾱ</i> (&#42;-ewĭ́ā), originally found in derivations from υ-stems.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Superficially appears in abstract nouns derived from σ-stem adjectives, with the true suffix being <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă)</li><li>Forming action nouns from verbs, skipping an intermediate adjective in -ης</li><li>Added to various stems to form feminine nouns of person or thing concerned</li><li>Added to adjectival stems to form a feminine form</li></ol><p>From σ-stem adjectives in -ης (-ēs) or -ής (-ḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă), equivalent to <i>&#42;-εσια</i> (&#42;-esia) and <i>&#42;-esih₂</i>.</p><p>From <i>-υς</i> (-us, adjective suffix) or <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agent noun suffix) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă, feminine suffix), equivalent to <i>&#42;-εϝῐᾰ</i> (&#42;-ewĭă). Nouns formed with this suffix always have recessive accent, while feminine adjectives have accent on the penult if the corresponding masculine form has accent on the ultima.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(forms adjectives from nouns): -id; -like; connected to; from the nature of; full of</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;-ϝειδής</i> (&#42;-weidḗs), from <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “form; appearance”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>infinitive forming suffix</li></ol><p>Seen in thematic verbs it may be from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-ehen</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-esen</i>, locative case of action noun. Similar to Latin <i>-ere</i>. See also <i>-εναι</i> (-enai), <i>-έμεν</i> (-émen), <i>-μεναι</i> (-menai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Usually added to neuter nouns in <i>-ος</i> (-os) to form an adjective</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-eh-nos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-es-</i>, the oblique stem of the verbal noun suffix <i>&#42;-os</i> + the adjectival suffix <i>&#42;-nós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-wénts</i>. See <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs) and Latin <i>-ōsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>infinitive forming suffix</li></ol><p>Seen in athematic verbs it seems to be an elaboration of <i>-ειν</i> (-ein), i.e. <i>-εεν</i> (in thematic verbs), with <i>-αι</i> seen elsewhere. See also <i>-έμεν</i> (-émen), <i>-μεναι</i> (-menai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to</li><li>made of</li></ol><p>Cognate to Latin <i>-eus</i> (from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;-eos</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ful, characterized by</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms verbal adjectives usually connected to verbs in <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō)</li></ol><p>From verbs in <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>forming adjectives</i>) hateful, full of enmity</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχθος</i> (ékhthos, “hate”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to noun or adjective stems to form a masculine noun of the person concerned with a thing</li><li>Added to an ancestor's or place name to form a demonym: -ian</li><li>Added to verbal stems to form a masculine agent noun: -er</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to the stems of agent or other nouns in <i>-εύς</i> (-eús) to form a denominative verb of condition or activity&#58; meaning "be <i>x</i>" or "do what <i>x</i> typically does"</li><li>Added to other nouns</li></ol><p>Denominative of <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms instrument nouns</li><li>forms toponyms (names for places)</li></ol><p>Formed through a union of a stem vowel <i>-ε-</i> (-e-) and <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), <i>&#42;‑es‑y‑on</i> like <i>ἀγγεῖον</i> (angeîon); usually from stems in <i>‑εύς</i> (‑eús)—originally <i>&#42;‑ēw‑</i>, whence the Ionic diaeresis, thus <i>&#42;‑ḗw‑ion</i>—like <i>κναφεῖον</i> (knapheîon), and sometimes applied to other stems, subsequently autonomised.</p><p>Inflectional form.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms adjectives, usually with a meaning of "of" or "from".</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms adjectives, usually with a meaning of "of" or "from".</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A suffix forming the present stem of some verbs from stems ending in the consonants <i>γ, δ</i> (g, d)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-ďďō</i>, from a stem ending in the consonants <i>&#42;g, &#42;gʷ,</i> or <i>&#42;d</i> + the Proto-Indo-European present suffixes <i>&#42;-yeti</i> (transitive imperfective suffix) or <i>&#42;-yéti</i> (intransitive imperfective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix of nouns in the first declension.</li><li>Added to verbal stems ending in a consonant to form an action noun.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Added to <i>o</i>-grade of the verbal stem</li><li>Added to zero-grade of the verbal stem</li><li>Added to <i>e</i>-grade of the verbal stem</li></ol><li>Added to masculine or neuter nouns to give a feminine form.</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā), from Proto-Hellenic <i>*-ā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-eh₂</i>. The earlier <i>-ᾱ</i> is retained in certain dialects, and in Attic after ε, ι or ρ, whence -εā, -ιā and -ρā (e.g., <i>ἀγορά agorā</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Latin feminine <i>-a</i>, from Old Latin and Proto-Italic <i>*-ā</i> both in names and adjectives, e.g., <i>φυγή</i> (phugḗ, "retreat, escape") &lt; Proto-Hellenic <i>*pʰugā́</i> &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>*bʰugéh₂</i>; at the same time, <i>*bʰugéh₂</i> &gt; Proto-Italic <i>*fugā</i> (preserved in Old Latin) &gt; Classical Latin <i>fuga</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>creates compound nouns with the meaning of -leader, -guide, -driver</li></ol><p>Traditionally considered a derived suffix from <i>ᾰ̓́γω</i> (ắgō, “to lead, guide”), though a derivation from <i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai, “to lead, guide”) appears more phonetically apt.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>indicating a direction</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>added to place names to form an adjective: -ian, -ese</li></ol><p>η-stem + <i>-νός</i> (-nós), later reanalyzed and used freely. Compare Latin <i>-ānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms third-declension adjectives with stems in <i>-εσ-</i> (-es-)&#59; very common in compounds</li><li>forms third-declension proper nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ēs</i>, from masculine <i>&#42;-s</i> over <i>&#42;-os ~ -es-</i> neuter substantive nouns. Afterwards used freely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>deverbal suffix, typically indicating a space in which things are put or stored</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>θήκη</i> (thḗkē), from <i>τῐ́θημῐ</i> (tĭ́thēmĭ, “to put; to place; to set”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to stems to form demonstratives: to, toward</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-dʰe</i>, whence <i>-θεν</i> (-then). Doublet of <i>-σε</i> (-se). Conflated with or derived from those two. Displaced <i>-θι</i> (-thi) in medial/proximal/distal place demonstratives.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>creates passive aorist participles</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to nouns to form adverbs of place from which: from; also used, like genitive, with prepositions</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-dʰe</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The passive aorist and passive future suffix</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-dʰh₁eti</i>, which made resultative verbs</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to nouns to form adverbs of location: at, in, on</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-dʰi</i>. Doublet of <i>-θεν</i> (-then). Possibly a doublet of <i>-θα</i> (-tha) and <i>-σε</i> (-se).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms instrument nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-tron ~ &#42;-tʰron</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-trom ~ &#42;-dʰrom</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Derives verbs from nominals, fusing with their stems in particular ways</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-yō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-yoh₂</i>. Cognates include Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-janą</i> and Sanskrit <i>-यति</i> (-yati). See progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms nouns denoting a place</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Primitive suffix added to the stems of adjectives in <i>-ύς</i> (-ús) and some nouns to form feminine gender</li><li>Suffix added to the stems of adjectives in <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs) to form abstract nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ih₂ ~ &#42;-yéh₂s</i>, related to <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-i-eh₂</i>). Cognate with Latin <i>-ia</i>. In some contexts (e.g., in words like <i>ἀλήθεια</i> (alḗtheia) and <i>πᾰρᾰ́νοιᾰ</i> (părắnoiă)), the use of this suffix may have been an innovative replacement of earlier formations in <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā) (which was kept or changed to <i>-ίη</i> (-íē) in some dialects).</p><p>After a consonant, the vowel <i>&#42;i</i> changed to a semivowel <i>&#42;y</i> and triggered palatalization in Proto-Greek, resulting in many nouns and adjectives ending in <i>-σσᾰ</i> (-ssă), <i>-ττᾰ</i> (-ttă), <i>-ζᾰ</i> (-ză). It also undergoes metathesis, resulting in <i>-ιρᾰ</i> (-iră), and <i>-ινᾰ</i> (-ină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form relational adjectives, especially from proper nouns</li></ol><p>Rebracketing of <i>-ᾱνός</i> (-ānós) when attached to a stem ending in ι, e.g. <i>Ἀσιᾱνός</i> (Asiānós) from <i>Ἀσίᾱ</i> (Asíā). Rare until the Roman era, when it functioned essentially as a borrowing from the (far more productive) Latin <i>-iānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to noun stems to form adjectives: of or pertaining to, in the manner of; -ic</li></ol><p>From <i>-κός</i> (-kós) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-kos</i>, <i>&#42;-ḱos</i>, with an added ι from <i>i</i>-stems such as <i>φυσι-κός</i> (phusi-kós, “natural”), through the same process by which <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs) developed from <i>-της</i> (-tēs), occurring in some original case and later used freely. Cognate with Latin <i>-icus</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-ungō</i>, whence Old English <i>-ig</i> (English <i>-y</i>), Old High German <i>-ig</i>, Gothic <i>-𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (-eigs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A noun-forming suffix, often forming nouns denoting concrete objects.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-illos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-illos</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to the stems of verbs or verbal nouns in <i>-σις</i> (-sis) to form an adjective of possibility or capability: -able, -ible, -like, -ed</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to masculine or neuter nouns to give a feminine form.</li></ol><p>From <i>-νος</i> (-nos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă), with metathesis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to nouns or adverbs to form adjectives relating to material, time, and so on: made of, during the time of</li><li>(<i>with long ῑ</i>) Forms adjectives of place: -ine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-iHnos</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>-īnus</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-īnaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>termination of neuter substantivations of adjectives in <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “belonging to”, also forms parasynthetic compounds)</li><li>forms diminutive nouns</li><li>forms toponyms (names for places)</li></ol><p>Perhaps neuter substantivisation of <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “adjective suffix”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-yós</i>.</p><p>For the development from adjective to diminutive, compare Sanskrit <i>-क</i> (-ka) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;-ъka</i>. From the development from adjective to place, compare Latin <i>-ārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix added to nouns or adjectives, forming adjectives: pertaining to, belonging to ("of").</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Often added to a combination of preposition and noun (parasynthesis)</li></ol></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-yós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms feminine nouns, or feminine forms of adjectives (<i>often accented on the ultima</i>)</li><li>Suffix added to a father's name to form a feminine patronymic: daughter of</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but compare Latin <i>-idus</i> (forming adjectives). Furnée and Beekes also consider it a common Pre-Greek suffix, related to <i>-ιτ-</i> (-it-), <i>-ινδ-</i> (-ind-) and <i>-ινθ-</i> (-inth-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms abstract nouns describing a condition or doctrine.</li></ol><p>Rebracketing of the suffix <i>-μός</i> (-mós) appended to verbs in <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō) / <i>-ῐ́ζομαι</i> (-ĭ́zomai), such as in <i>λογῐσμός</i> (logĭsmós) (<i>λογῐ́ζομαι</i> (logĭ́zomai) + <i>-μός</i> (-mós)). In some cases terms ending on <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós) can be analyzed both as arising from this rebracketed suffix as as arising without rebracketing, such as the alternative analyses of <i>Ἑλληνισμός</i> (Hellēnismós) as either <i>Ἕλλην</i> (Héllēn, “Greek”) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós) or <i>ἑλληνίζω</i> (hellēnízō, “to Hellenize”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>femininizes masculine nouns in the first declension</li></ol><p>Extracted from feminine derivations in <i>-ια</i> (-ia) of demonyms in -ικ- (with palatalisation), like <i>Κίλισσα</i> (Kílissa) from <i>Κίλιξ</i> (Kílix), or <i>Φοίνισσα</i> (Phoínissa) from <i>Φοῖνιξ</i> (Phoînix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs)</li></ol><p>From <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō, verbal suffix) + <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>appended to names of peoples to denote to speak in their language or dialect</li></ol><p>Adverbial form derived from <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Superlative adverb suffix.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to some adjectives to form a superlative adjective</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-istHos</i>, from <i>&#42;-yōs</i> (elative suffix) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tHós</i> (adjectival suffix). Cognate with Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;-ištʰas</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-istaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ώνη</i> (-ṓnē, feminine patronymic suffix)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The active perfect suffix, also used in kappatic aorists</li></ol><p>Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-kt</i>, which formed perfective verbs.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>An element found in given names.</li></ol><p>For <i>-κλέης</i> (-kléēs), from <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) [from earlier <i>κλέϝος</i> (kléwos)] +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs). Compare with the Proto-Slavic name element <i>&#42;-slavъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-cracy (government, rule)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “strength, power”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-cratic</li></ol><p>From <i>-κρατίᾱ</i> (-kratíā) + <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-cidal; added to nouns to form an adjective indicating a killing agent for the first noun</li><li>added to nouns to form an adjective indicating a killing agent for the first noun</li></ol><p>From <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms adjectives with the sense of 'of or pertaining to', 'in the manner of'</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-kos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-kos</i>, <i>&#42;-ḱos</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>-cus</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-gaz</i>, Sanskrit <i>-शस्</i> (-śas) and Old Church Slavonic <i>-ъkъ</i> (-ŭkŭ).</p><p>PIE *-ko- on noun stems carried the meaning 'characteristic of, like, typical, pertaining to', and on adjectival stems it acted emphatically.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-later (server, to be subject, to serve gods)</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰτρ-</i> (lătr-), stem of <i>λατρεύω</i> (latreúō, “to serve, serve gods”) + <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A suffix forming the present stem of some verbs from stems in the consonant <i>λ</i> (l)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-ľľō</i>, from a stem ending in the consonant <i>&#42;l</i> + the Proto-Indo-European present suffixes <i>&#42;-yeti</i> (transitive imperfective suffix) or <i>&#42;-yéti</i> (intransitive imperfective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Base for nouns denoting the study of something, or the branch of knowledge of a discipline.</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “explanation”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>combining form of <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “word, speech”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>denoting a student of or specialist in a field; -logist</li></ol><p>From <i>λέγω</i> (légō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-μενος</i> (-menos), added to perfect stems of verbs to form perfect mediopassive participles</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verbal stems to form neuter nouns denoting the effect or result of an action, a particular instance of an action, or the object of an action</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-mə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mn̥</i>, with the oblique stem <i>-ματ-</i> (-mat-) from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-mət-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mn̥-to-</i> (seen also in Latin <i>-mentum</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to tense stems of verbs to form middle, passive, and mediopassive participles</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>present mediopassive <i>like</i> <i>λῡόμενος</i> (lūómenos)</li><li>future middle and passive <i>like</i> <i>λῡσόμενος</i> (lūsómenos), <i>λῠθησόμενος</i> (lŭthēsómenos)</li><li>aorist middle <i>like</i> <i>λῡσᾰ́μενος</i> (lūsắmenos)</li><li>perfect mediopassive <i>like</i> <i>λελῠμένος</i> (lelŭménos) &rarr; <i>see</i> -μένος (-ménos)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mh₁nos</i> (“mediopassive participle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms noun pertaining to measurement: -metry</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “measurement”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>ending of the first-person singular athematic present</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mi</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>combining form of <i>μορφή</i> (morphḗ, “shape, form”)</li></ol><p>From <i>μορφή</i> (morphḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms abstract nouns.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mos</i> or <i>&#42;-mós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms agent nouns and adjectives.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mṓ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms abstract nouns.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mos</i> or <i>&#42;-mós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The accusative-singular inflection of nouns, adjectives, and some pronouns in the first and second declensions, and of some third-declension nouns</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to roots or words to form adjectives or nouns&#59; often accented on the ultima</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-nós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A suffix forming transitive verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-néwti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>primitive suffix used to form present-tense stems</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-né-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-νος</i> (-nos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Nominative plural of masculine and feminine second-declension nouns</li><li>Masculine nominative plural of first- and second-declension adjectives</li><li>Masculine and feminine nominative plural of second-declension adjectives</li><li>Archaic locative singular of some second-declension nouns</li><li>Dative singular of <i>o</i>-stem third-declension nouns</li></ol><p>From the pronominal declension. Displaced <i>-ες</i> (-es) except in the third declension. For the same development, compare Latin <i>-ī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Dative plural of second-declension nouns and adjectives</li><li>Masculine dative plural of first- and second-declension adjectives</li><li>nominative masculine singular of present and future active participles from contracted verbs in <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Marks the first person singular present mediopassive indicative of non-deponent verbs and the first person singular present active indicative of deponent verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-oh₂er</i>, the regular first person present middle indicative ending of thematic verbs, with renewal of the <i>*-r</i> by <i>*-i</i> as marker of present tense (as in active endings), along with analogical introduction of the <i>*m</i> in the corresponding active form in athematic verbs.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verbal roots to form an o-grade action noun.</li><li>Added to verbal roots to form a noun of result or an abstract noun of action.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Added to the zero-grade.</li><li>Added to the <i>e</i>-grade.</li></ol><li>Nominative singular of masculine and feminine second-declension nouns</li><li>Masculine nominative singular of first- and second-declension adjectives</li><li>Masculine and feminine nominative singular of second-declension adjectives</li><li>Genitive singular of consonant-stem or uncontracted third-declension nouns</li><li>Genitive singular of consonant-stem or uncontracted third-declension masculine or neuter adjectives</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-os</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-os</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>-us</i>&nbsp;m.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-os</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Genitive singular of second-declension nouns and adjectives</li><li>Masculine and neuter genitive singular of first- and second-declension adjectives</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-ohyo</i> from the Proto-Indo-European thematic genitive Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-osyo</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>creates compound nouns or adjectives with the meaning of -worker, -maker, -wright</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon), via a contraction of -οϝεργός &gt; -οεργός &gt; -ουργός</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>sight, view: -opsia, -opsy</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄψις</i> (ópsis) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-ful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-o-wénts</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>-ōsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Marks the third person plural present active indicative of non-deponent contracted verbs</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Marks the third person plural present active indicative of non-deponent contracted verbs&#59; variant of -οῦσι with movable nu</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms adjectives: one who falls from, where, or in the way the first combining form denotes</li></ol><p>From <i>πίπτω</i> (píptō, “to fall”), from <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “to fly”) and from <i>πετᾰ́ννῡμῐ</i> (petắnnūmĭ, “to spread”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pet-</i>; stem <i>πετ-</i> + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-fold (used to make adjectives meaning ‘times’)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pl̥-h₂-t-yo-s</i>, from <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to fold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>-πλόος</i> (-plóos, “-fold”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms an adjective indicating repetition or multiplication: -fold</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A suffix forming the present stem of some verbs from stems in the consonant <i>π</i> (p)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-pťō</i>, from a stem ending in the consonant <i>&#42;p</i> + the Proto-Indo-European present suffixes <i>&#42;-yeti</i> (transitive imperfective suffix) or <i>&#42;-yéti</i> (intransitive imperfective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>seller, monger</li></ol><p>From <i>πώλης</i> (pṓlēs, “vendor, seller”), from <i>πωλέω</i> (pōléō, “to sell, to offer for sale”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-able, tending to be X'ed (for a verb <i>X</i>)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-rós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-rós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix of nouns in the third declension.</li><li>suffix added to some masculine foreign names to make them declinable</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-s</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>-s</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The first aorist suffix, causing the preceding vowel to be lengthened.</li></ol><p>Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-st</i>, which formed perfective verbs.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to words to form adverbs of direction: to, toward</li></ol><p>Possibly cognate with <i>-θα</i> (-tha).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verb stems to form abstract nouns or nouns of action, process, or result</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>-τις</i> (-tis) (retained after dentals), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tis</i>. The <i>&#42;t</i> changed to <i>&#42;s</i> by assibilation and palatalization, triggered by the following <i>&#42;i</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>-tiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Primitive suffix used to form present-tense stems, very rarely associated with the inchoative meaning of becoming</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) Suffix used to form the imperfect tense of many verbs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-sḱéti</i>, inchoative or inceptive suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>A suffix forming the present stem of some verbs from stems in the consonants <i>τ, θ, κ, χ, γ</i> (t, th, k, kh, g)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-ťťō</i>, from a stem ending in the consonants <i>&#42;k, &#42;kʰ, &#42;kʷ, &#42;kʷʰ, &#42;t, &#42;tʰ,</i> or <i>&#42;g</i> + the Proto-Indo-European present suffixes <i>&#42;-yeti</i> (transitive imperfective suffix) or <i>&#42;-yéti</i> (intransitive imperfective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</li></ol><p>Alternative form of <i>-της</i> (-tēs) or <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine suffix), with a sigma σ added by rebracketing of agent nouns from verbs in <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō). Used on stems ending in α, such as <i>δυνά-στης</i> (duná-stēs) from <i>δύνα-μαι</i> (dúna-mai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-τος</i> (-tos), forming ordinal and fractional numerals</li></ol><p>Rebracketed from ordinals like <i>τεσσαρακοστός</i> (tessarakostós), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷetwərəkontstós</i> equivalent to <i>τεσσαράκοντα</i> (tessarákonta) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós). The <i>*tst</i> (> <i>st</i>) results from the Indo-European double dental rule.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>The future suffix: applied to the bare stem, causing a lengthening of the vowel (with alpha becoming eta).</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) The future perfect suffix: applied to the perfect stem without personal endings, the tense is rarely attested to begin with and it is unknown if a future perfect passive would use -θ or not.</li></ol><p>Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-(h₁)seti</i>, which formed desiderative verbs.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms abstract nouns from adjectives or nouns</li></ol><p>Formerly the feminine gender of adjective <i>-συνος</i> (-sunos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tunos</i>, from <i>&#42;-tus</i> + <i>&#42;-nós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>creates verbal adjectives of necessity, either active or passive (<i>accented on the penultima</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verb stems to form masculine agent nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tḗr</i>, with nominative η made common to all cases with original syncopation or ε; compare its use in <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr) (always with ε) and <i>γαστήρ</i> (gastḗr) (either with oblique ε throughout or original syncopation).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verb stems to form adjectives pertaining to the stem</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Appended to verbs to form agent nouns</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>With -ίζω (-ízō) verbs&#58;</li><li>With -άζω (-ázō) verbs&#58;</li><li>With -όζω (-ózō) verbs&#58;</li><li>With -ύζω (-úzō) verbs&#58;</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-tās</i> (whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀑𐀴𐀲</i> (ki-ti-ta /⁠ktitā(s)⁠/, <i>κτίτης</i>)), probably a masculine formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-teh₂</i> (perhaps still seen in Latin <i>Monēta</i>), feminine of <i>&#42;-tós</i>. Originally used in adjectival compounds, the suffix's narrowing to agentivity may be by analogy to <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr) and <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>extended form of <i>-ῐ́ς</i> (-ĭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms nouns representing a state of being</li><li>Added to noun stems to form masculine nouns of the person concerned with a thing</li><li>Added to verb stems to form masculine agent nouns: -er</li><li>Added to place names to form masculine demonyms: -ian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-tāts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-teh₂ts</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>-ताति</i> (-tāti), and Latin <i>-tās</i>, from which English <i>-ity</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-tās</i> (whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀑𐀴𐀲</i> (ki-ti-ta /⁠ktitā(s)⁠/)), probably a masculine formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-teh₂</i> (perhaps still seen in Latin <i>Monēta</i>), feminine of <i>&#42;-tós</i>. Originally used in adjectival compounds, the suffix's narrowing to agentivity may be by analogy to <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr) and <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to adjective stems to form superlative forms</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tm̥mos</i> with influence from <i>&#42;-istos</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form feminine agent nouns</li></ol><p>From <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Used on adjectives that express some notion of contrast with an antonym</li><li>Added to adjective stems to form comparative forms</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-teros</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms abstract nouns denoting a quality or property (i.e. a “state of being”)</li><li>Added to noun stems to form masculine nouns of the person concerned with a thing</li><li>Added to verb stems to form masculine agent nouns: -er</li><li>Added to place names to form masculine demonyms: -ian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-tāts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-teh₂ts</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>-ताति</i> (-tāti), and Latin <i>-tās</i>, from which English <i>-ity</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-tās</i> (whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀑𐀴𐀲</i> (ki-ti-ta /⁠ktitā(s)⁠/, <i>κτίτης</i>)), probably a masculine formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-teh₂</i> (perhaps still seen in Latin <i>Monēta</i>), feminine of <i>&#42;-tós</i>. Originally used in adjectival compounds, the suffix's narrowing to agentivity may be by analogy to <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr) and <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr). Compare Latin <i>poeta</i> from Ancient Greek <i>ποιητής</i> (poiētḗs), and Latin <i>agricola</i> from Latin <i>-a</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verbal stems to form adjectives: relating to, suited to, skilled in, able to, -ive</li><li>Added to other stems to form adjectives, particularly those ending in vowels</li></ol><p>From <i>-σις</i> (-sis, verbal noun suffix) or <i>-τος</i> (-tos, verbal adjective suffix) +&lrm; <i>-κός</i> (-kós, adjective suffix), occurring in some original cases and later used freely by metanalysis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Creates perfective passive verbal adjectives (<i>usually accented recessively</i>)</li><li>Forms adjectives from nouns (<i>accented recessively</i>)</li><li>Forms ordinal and fractional numerals (<i>also with accent on the ultima</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms feminine agent nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-trih₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verb stems to form feminine agent nouns. Usually accented on the ultima.</li></ol><p>From <i>-τρος</i> (-tros) or a similar zero-grade masculine agent-noun suffix + <i>-ίς</i> (-ís, feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms instrument nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-tron</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-trom</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>turn, direction</li><li>way, manner</li><li>habit, custom</li></ol><p>From <i>τρόπος</i> (trópos, “a turn”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō)</li></ol><p>See <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms feminine abstract nouns indicating printing processes.</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>τύπος</i> (túpos, “text”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>used to form agent nouns</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tōr</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>-tor</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms verbal adjectives, often expressing a completed or resultant state, and may overlap semantically with passive participles.</li><li>Forms verbal adjectives of possibility or suitability, either active or passive.</li><li>Forms ordinal and fractional numerals.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tós</i>. Related to <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix forming nouns of action</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tus</i> (<i>suffix deriving action nouns from verb roots</i>). Cognate with Latin <i>-tus</i>, as in <i>habitus</i> (“demeanor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>eating</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔφαγον</i> (éphagon) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Creates a transnumeral case-form used with several different meanings&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>instrumental</li><li>locative</li><li>ablative, often with the prepositions <i>ἐκ</i> (ek) and <i>ἀπό</i> (apó)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) genitive</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) dative</li></ol></ol><p>From the Proto-Indo-European instrumental plural inflection <i>&#42;-bʰi(s)</i>, reanalyzed as a suffix. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>भिस्</i> (-bhis, plural instrumental marker), Old Armenian <i>-ւ</i> (-w).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-φῐ</i> (-phĭ) with movable nu</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>creates compound adjectives denoting growth in the manner indicated by the first term in the compound&#58; -natured</li></ol><p>From <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “to grow, be prone to”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>combining form of <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “sound”)</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>sounding</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “carry, bring”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) emphatic particle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰí</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>ending of the first-person singular thematic present</li><li>forms adverbs from prepositions</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-oh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>a suffix indicating a structure or mass (especially pathological)</li></ol><p>From <i>-όω</i> (-óō) + <i>-μα</i> (-ma) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mn̥</i>), which lengthens a preceding vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Genitive plural of second- and third-declension nouns and adjectives not accented on the ultima</li><li>Masculine and neuter genitive plural of first- and second-declension not accented on the ultima</li><li>Masculine singular of present, future, and second aorist active participles</li><li>Added to adjective stems to form comparative forms</li><li>Added to nouns to form possessive derivatives</li><li>Added to adjectives to form nominalisations</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-oHom</i> and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-esoHom</i>, genitive plural of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-os</i> (thematic) and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-os</i> (athematic).</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-onts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-onts</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-yōs</i>, with contamination.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-Hō</i>, dubbed "Hoffmann's suffix", meaning "one who possesses X".</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ō</i>. It is sometimes referred to as the "Strabon suffix", after the personal name, which features it.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to the stem of adjectives or pronouns to form adverbs</li><li>(<i>unproductive</i>) Added to verbal roots to form a noun of result or an abstract noun of action.</li></ol><p>Beekes states that this common adverbial suffix cannot be demonstrated to go back to the Proto-Indo-European era.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ōs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>indicating a transformative action</li></ol><p>Originally the ending <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs) when suffixed to verbs ending in <i>-όω</i> (-óō) or occasionally verbs whose root ends in <i>-ω</i> (-ō), but later reinterpreted as a suffix in its own right.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms agentive or patientive adjectives and nouns from the o-grade of a verbal root.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ós</i> (deverbal nominal suffix).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to verbal roots to form an o-grade action noun.</li><li>Added to verbal roots to form a noun of result or an abstract noun of action.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Added to the zero-grade.</li><li>Added to the <i>e</i>-grade.</li></ol><li>Nominative singular of masculine and feminine second-declension nouns</li><li>Masculine nominative singular of first- and second-declension adjectives</li><li>Masculine and feminine nominative singular of second-declension adjectives</li><li>Genitive singular of consonant-stem or uncontracted third-declension nouns</li><li>Genitive singular of consonant-stem or uncontracted third-declension masculine or neuter adjectives</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-os</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-os</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>-us</i>&nbsp;m.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-os</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to a noun or adjective to make a verb with a causative or factitive meaning&#58; to make someone do or be something.</li></ol><p>From a potential Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-oyéti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Forms factitive verbs from u-stem adjectives</li><li>Forms factitive verbs from s-stem abstract nouns, previously ones that had u-stem adjectives</li><li>Forms factitive verbs from o-stem adjectives</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-unyeti</i>. Doublet of <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō), whose factitiveness is also from the ν. The nasal suffix is from an earlier nasal infix used without y.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>suffix forming female given names</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ṓy</i> (noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>forms adjectives from nouns</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>strictly</i>) smelling of</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) full of; like</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὄζω</i> (ózō, “to smell”). For the generalized sense, compare the semantic evolution in Latin <i>-osus</i>, Old Armenian <i>-ոտ</i> (-ot).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>applied to noun stems</i>) forms words for places</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of humans</i>) apartment or quarters</li><li>(<i>of animals</i>) stable or cote</li><li>(<i>of vegetation</i>) plantation or grove</li><li>(<i>of objects</i>) storage room</li></ol></ol><p>. For the meaning, compare Latin <i>-ētum</i> (“plantation, grove”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Added to the names of people to form a feminine patronym: daughter of</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>perfect active participle ending</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-wṓs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Suffix used to form various kinds of nouns, including demonyms and other nouns referring to types of persons</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Dative singular of second-declension nouns and adjectives</li><li>Dative singular of second-declension adjectives</li><li>Masculine and neuter dative singular of second-declension adjectives</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Suffix</b></p><ol><li>Genitive plural of first-declension nouns</li><li>Feminine genitive plural of first- and second-declension adjectives</li><li>Feminine genitive plural of first- and third-declension adjectives</li><li>Masculine and neuter genitive plural of second-declension nouns accented on the ultima</li><li>Masculine and neuter genitive plural of first- and second-declension adjectives accented on the ultima</li><li>Present active participle of contracted verbs in <i>-όω</i> (-óō) and <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</li><li>Future active participle of verbs with Attic contracted futures in <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</li><li>Present active participle of contracted verbs in <i>-άω</i> (-áō)</li></ol><p>From earlier -άων (-áōn) from -η (-ē) (and alternative forms) plus genitive plural suffix -ων (-ōn).</p><p>See <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p><p>Contracted from <i>&#42;-έων</i> (&#42;-éōn) or <i>&#42;-όων</i> (&#42;-óōn), from <i>-έω</i> (-éō) or <i>-όω</i> (-óō) + <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, “participial suffix”).</p><p>Contracted from <i>&#42;-άων</i> (&#42;-áōn), from <i>-άω</i> (-áō) + <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, “participial suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Punctuation Mark</b></p><ol><li>? (the Greek question mark)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Punctuation Mark</b></p><ol><li>The high point, used for the end of a sentence like a full stop, then for a medium pause like a semicolon.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pamphylian form of <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arta, from Proto-Saka, brother of Maues and father of Kharahostes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;arθya</i> (“pious”) (cf. Avestan <i>𐬀𐬱𐬫𐬀</i> (ašya, “pious”)).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨀𐨪𐨿𐨚</i> (arṭa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>ἄνθρωπε</i> (ánthrōpe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>ἄνθρωποι</i> (ánthrōpoi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>ἀνθρώποις</i> (anthrṓpois)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>ἄνθρωπον</i> (ánthrōpon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>ἀνθρώπου</i> (anthrṓpou)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>ἀνθρώπους</i> (anthrṓpous)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>ἀνθρώπῳ</i> (anthrṓpōi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>ἀνθρώπων</i> (anthrṓpōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ahtopol (a town in Bulgaria)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγαθός</i> (agathós) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Azilises, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian king</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;Azalīźa</i> (“commander-in-chief”), composed of <i>&#42;aza</i> (“leader”) (cf. Khotanese <i>hinajha</i> (“commander of army”)) + <i>&#42;rīźa</i> (“desiring, commanding”) (cf. Khotanese <i>rriś</i> (“to desire”), Sogdian <i>𐽀𐼷𐼵𐼸𐽀𐼰𐼸</i> (ryzkrʾk)).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨀𐨩𐨁𐨫𐨁𐨮</i> (ayiliṣa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aeëtes (a king of Colchis, son of Helios and Perseis)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;Aiwā́tās</i>, compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀷𐀲</i> (a3-wa-ta). Connected with ἀετός (aetós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) The mythical island said to be the home of Circe.</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>ἀετός</i> (aetós) and <i>Αἰήτης</i> (Aiḗtēs). Thus from an unattested <i>&#42;Αιϝαία</i> (&#42;Aiwaía), from <i>&#42;αἴϝα</i> (&#42;aíwa, “(related to) bird”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, “pertaining to, belonging to ("of")”), the first from <i>&#42;h₂éwis</i> (“bird”). Similar outcome of Ancient Greek <i>Γαῖᾰ</i> (Gaîă). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>वि</i> (ví), Latin <i>avis</i>, Old Armenian <i>հաւ</i> (haw).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Aeacus; an Aeacid</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἰᾰκός</i> (Aiăkós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aeacus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aeaean (of or pertaining to Aea)</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>Αἶα</i> (Aîa)Ancient Greek <i>-α-</i> (-a-)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-yós</i>der.Ancient Greek <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)Ancient Greek <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos)Ancient Greek <i>Αἰαῖος</i>From <i>Αἶα</i> (Aîa, “Aea”) + <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegae, the name of various places in ancient Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegaean Sea</li></ol><p>Literally, “sea of Aegeus”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegean Sea (a sea in the northeastern part of the Mediterranean Sea)</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>Αἰγαῖον πέλαγος</i> (Aigaîon pélagos), from <i>Αἰγαῖος</i> (Aigaîos, “Aegean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aegean</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Αἰγαῖον</i> (Aigaîon)</li></ol><p><i>Αἰγεύς</i> (Aigeús, “Aegeus”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjectival suffix). The name might be connected to <i>αἶγες</i> (aîges, “waves”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aegeus</li></ol><p>The name is said to mean "goat-man," from <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Aegialeus</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰγῐᾰλεύς</i> (aigĭăleús, “littoral”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Aeginetan, inhabitant of Aegina</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴγινα</i> (Aígina, “Aegina”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A female form of Egyptian</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonym</i>) Egyptian</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴγυπτος</i> (Aíguptos, “Egypt”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix). Cognate to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀓𐀠𐀴𐀍</i> (a3-ku-pi-ti-jo).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the Egyptians</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἰγύπτιος</i> (Aigúptios, “Egyptian”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the Egyptian language</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴγυπτος</i> (Aíguptos) +&lrm; <i>-ιστί</i> (-istí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like an Egyptian</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴγυπτος</i> (Aíguptos) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born in Egypt</li><li>of Egyptian race</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴγυπτος</i> (Aíguptos) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nationality</i>) the nation of Egyptians</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonym</i>) Egyptian</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴγυπτος</i> (Aíguptos, “Egypt”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix). Cognate to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀓𐀠𐀴𐀍</i> (a3-ku-pi-ti-jo).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neuter form of Egyptian</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonym</i>) Egyptian</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴγυπτος</i> (Aíguptos, “Egypt”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix). Cognate to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀓𐀠𐀴𐀍</i> (a3-ku-pi-ti-jo).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonym</i>) Egyptian</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴγυπτος</i> (Aíguptos, “Egypt”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix). Cognate to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀓𐀠𐀴𐀍</i> (a3-ku-pi-ti-jo).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aedepsus, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elba (the largest island of the Tuscan Archipelago, in the Tyrrhenian Sea)</li><li>synonym of <i>Λῆμνος</i> (Lêmnos) (Lemnos&#58; an island in the northeastern Aegaean Sea)</li><li>synonym of <i>Χίος</i> (Khíos) (Chios&#58; an island in the eastern Aegaean Sea)</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἰθᾰλῐ́ων</i> (Aithălĭ́ōn, “Aethalion”) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă). Perhaps from <i>αἰθαλίων</i> (aithalíōn, “browned, toasted”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Αἰθᾰ́λειᾰ</i> (Aithắleiă) (<i>only in reference to Elba</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopian</li></ol><p>Explained since antiquity as a compound of <i>αἴθω</i> (aíthō, “to burn”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “face”), though this is generally thought to be a folk etymology.</p><p>Beekes argues that the first assumed root always has senses related to brightness and never to darkness, that the &#42;-ί- as well as &#42;-ο- instead of &#42;-ώ- are unexplained (the explanation actually is something), and that &#42;-οπ- (&#42;-οψ) is a typical Pre-Greek substrate suffix observed in ethnonyms like <i>Δόλοψ</i> (Dólops) and <i>Δρύοψ</i> (Drúops). The suffix is shaped differently in Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀴𐀍𐀦</i> (a3-ti-jo-qo).</p><p>A connection to this colour however brings closer the native Ethiopian Semitic <i>ዕጣን</i> (ʿəṭan, “incense”) to explain the unsuffixed etymon. Frankincense had the primary characteristic of being white, so also the source of <i>λίβανος</i> (líbanos, “incense”) is a Semitic root related to whiteness. The word might ultimately refer to the incense trade passing from Abyssinia. The folk of Ethiopia was so noted for earning its living by olibanum commerce that the Arabs even derived a term حَبَشَ (ḥabaša, “to earn, to obtain for the household”) from their other ethnonym اَلْحَبَشَة (al-ḥabaša).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Αἰθᾰ́λειᾰ</i> (Aithắleiă) (<i>only in reference to Elba</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name — famously held by&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a son of Hermes and Eupolemia, the herald of the Argonauts</li><li>an Achaean warrior, killed by Aeneas</li><li>a Tyrrhenian</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>Αἰθᾰλίων</i> (Aithălíōn, “Aethalion”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Αἰθᾰ́λειᾰ</i> (Aithắleiă) (<i>only in reference to Elba</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopia (a historical region of East Africa)</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἰθίοψ</i> (Aithíops) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), of uncertain further origin (see the article).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopian</li><li>sunburned</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) an Ethiopian (person)</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἰθίοψ</i> (Aithíops) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Catherine or Katharine</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Often said to be from <i>Ἑκάτη</i> (Hekátē, “Hecate”), though the Oxford Dictionary of First Names finds this unconvincing. Or, from <i>Ἑκατερ(ός)</i> (Hekater(ós)) + <i>-ίνη</i> (-ínē) from <i>ἑκάτερος</i> (hekáteros, “each of the two”). An early folk etymology to καθαρός (katharós, “clean, pure”) resulted to <i>Medieval Latin</i> Katerina, Catharīna and descendants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aemilius; any individual among the Aemilius family</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Aemilius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aemilia; any female individual among the Aemilius family</li><li>Emilia (a region of Italy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Aemilia</i>; feminine of Αἰμίλιος (Aimílios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aeneas</li></ol><p>The name's origin is uncertain, but is traditionally linked to <i>αἶνος</i> (aînos, “praise, tale”).</p><p><b>The <i>Homeric Hymn to Aphrodite</i></b> on Wikipedia links it to <i>αἰνός</i> (ainós, “horrible, dreadful”), on account of Aphrodite's sorrow at having lain with the mortal Anchises.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aenesidemus</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴνεσις</i> (aínesis, “praise”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “the people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Αἰνεσῐ́δημος</i> (Ainesĭ́dēmos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolia; the land or lands of the Aeolians</li><li>the previous name for Thessaly before the Greek Dark Ages, an older homeland of the Aeolians</li><li>Aeolis, a region in northwestern Asia Minor including the island of Lesbos, a later homeland of the Aeolians</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴολος</i> (Aíolos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolis</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴολος</i> (Aíolos) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Aeolian; an inhabitant of Aeolis</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴολος</i> (Aíolos, “Aeolus”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴολος</i> (Aíolos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aipy, Elis, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰπῠ́ς</i> (aipŭ́s, “steep; sheer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>Ἡσῐ́οδος</i> (Hēsĭ́odos, “Hesiod”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἴσιμος</i> (Aísimos, literally “destined, agreeable”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aeschines</li><li>Aeschines, a 4th century <small>BCE</small> orator</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aeschylus</li><li>Aeschylus, a tragic playwright</li></ol><p>Possibly derived from <i>ἰσχύς</i> (iskhús) ("strength, power") and negation particle <i>ἀ-</i> (a-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeson, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aetolia (a mountainous region of Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἰτωλός</i> (Aitōlós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female inhabitant of Aetolia; a female Aetolian</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἰτωλός</i> (Aitōlós) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aetolus</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Aetolia; an Aetolian</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō, “to ask”) and <i>λαός</i> (laós, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Haemimontus (an ancient region and later former Roman province situated in the Balkans, mainly in what is now Bulgaria and Turkey)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Haemimontus</i>, from <i>Haemus</i> +&lrm; <i>mons</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantine form of <i>Αἴᾱς</i> (Aíās)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Aiāx</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Αἴᾱς</i> (Aíās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Ajax</li></ol><p>From earlier Αἴϝᾱς (Aíwās) of uncertain further origin; compare Etruscan <i>𐌀𐌉𐌅𐌀𐌔</i> (aivas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegira; Aigeira, Greece</li></ol><p>Before Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegina (an island of Greece)</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aegina (nymph of the island that bears her name)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegium; Aigio, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aegisthus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) the Aegyptus river, i.e. the Nile River</li><li>(<i>masculine</i>) King Aegyptus</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) Egypt (an ancient country and region of North Africa and West Asia)</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>ḥwt-kꜣ-ptḥ</i> (literally “The temple of the ka of Ptah”), referring to Ptah’s temple in the important city of Memphis.</p><p>See also the Mycenaean Greek demonym <i>𐁁𐀓𐀠𐀴𐀍</i> (a3-ku-pi-ti-jo, “Egyptian”) (Ancient Greek <i>Αἰγύπτιος</i> (Aigúptios)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Aedui; an individual member of the Aedui</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aethilla, daughter of Laomedon and sister of Priamus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Αἰθᾰ́λειᾰ</i> (Aithắleiă) (<i>only in reference to Chios</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elath (ancient city on the Gulf of Aqaba in modern-day Israel)</li></ol><p>Related to Hebrew <i>אֵילַת</i> (ʼÊláṯ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aeolus</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰόλος</i> (aiólos, “moving, swift”), of uncertain origin: possibly from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;walwólos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welwel-</i>, reduplicative form of the root <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aesepus (an important river in Mysia, modern-day Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aesop</li></ol><p>From <i>αἶσα</i> (aîsa, “fate”) +&lrm; <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “voice”). Also see <i>-ωπός</i> (-ōpós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aetna (Sicilian nymph), related to the homonymous place.</li><li>Aetna (an ancient city in Sicily); Diodorus Siculus and Strabo write that Hiero conquered Κατάνη (Katánē) and deported its inhabitants to Λεοντῖνοι (Leontînoi), repopulating it with Greeks of Doric descent and changing its name to <i>Aítnā</i>.</li><li>Mount Etna</li></ol><p>Uncertain, possibly from a Pre-Greek substrate. Suggested connection with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eydʰ-</i> (“burn; fire”), with <i>dʰ</i> &gt; <i>t</i> through Siculan dialect. If so, then cognate with Ancient Greek <i>αἴθω</i> (aíthō, “to burn”), Latin <i>aestus</i> (“hot”), <i>aestās</i> (“summer”), possibly <i>aedis</i> (“shrine, temple”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Haemon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Αἰπῠ́</i> (Aipŭ́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Haemus</li></ol><p>From <i>αἷμᾰ</i> (haîmă, “blood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Αὐγείας</i> (Augeías)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Augeas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Augeiae (a town in Locris, Greece)</li><li>Augeiae (a town in Laconia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Augustine</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Augustīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Audynaius, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to December</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aventine Hill (a hill in Rome, Italy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Aventīnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aulis (a port town in Boeotia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aurelius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Aurēlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aurelian</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Aurēliānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>Αὔσων</i> (Aúsōn, “Auson, a son of Odysseus who was thought to have settled there”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Automedon, the charioteer of Achilles in the <i>Iliad</i></li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “self”) +&lrm; <i>μέδων</i> (médōn, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Autonoe</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτός</i> (autós) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Autophradates, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>satrap of Lydia under Artaxerxes II</li><li>navy commander for Darius III</li><li>satrap of the Amardi and Tapuri</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Vātafradātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Autolycus, a mythical robber, son of Hermes</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Auge</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Auge, the daughter of king Aleus and the virgin priestess of Athena Alea</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Auge, one of the Horae.</li></ol></ol><p>From the noun <i>αὐγή</i> (augḗ, “sunbeam”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Augustus, the Roman emperor</li><li>the month of August</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Augustus</i> (but the Greek calque Σεβαστός (Sebastós) was traditionally preferred).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Auximum; Osimo, Marche, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Auximum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ausones (an Italic tribe known also as Aurunci)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aulus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Aulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>βασιλεία</i> (basileía)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>βασιλεύς</i> (basileús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baal, a Canaanite deity (referred to biblically as a symbol of idolatry)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>בַּעַל</i> (báʿal).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Badres, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Badrah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Μάκεχ</i> (Mákekh)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bakis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bactra, Balkh (a town in Afghanistan)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Bactria; a Bactrian</li></ol><p>From <i>Βακτρίᾱ</i> (Baktríā, “Bactria”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bacchic, of or belonging to Bacchus and his rites</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in masculine</i>) Bacchus</li><li>(<i>in feminine</i>) a Bacchic revelry</li><li>(<i>in masculine</i>) (<i>elliptically for</i> Βάχκειος πούς (poús)) the bacchius, a metrical foot of three syllables (¯ ¯ ˘)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>Βᾰ́κχος</i> (Bắkkhos, “Bacchus”) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bacchus</li></ol><p>Related to Latin <i>bacca</i> (“pearl, beard, olive-berry, berry”); both possibly derive from the name of a Thracian fertility god. Payne claims a derivation from Lydian <i>𐤡𐤠𐤨𐤦</i> (paki), the name of a Lydian god seen as equivalent to Dionysus.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Balbura, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Balla, Pieria, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;collective) the Varangians (a medieval tribe or ethnic grouping of Norse origin)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Varangian; an ethnic Norseman with ancestral roots in medieval Scandinavia and other areas of Norse settlement during the Viking Age</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Varangian; a soldier of Nordic or Anglo-Saxon origin, most distinguished for forming the elite Varangian Guard army unit in the Byzantine Empire, serving as the personal bodyguard of the Byzantine Roman Emperor in Constantinople</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>Varingus</i>, <i>Waringus</i>, from a Germanic origin, likely via Old Norse <i>væringi</i> (literally “oath sworn”), from <i>vár</i> (“pledge, oath”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“suffix to form gerund noun”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Varangian; an ethnic Norseman with ancestral roots in medieval Scandinavia and other areas of Norse settlement during the Viking Age</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Varangian; a soldier of Nordic or Anglo-Saxon origin, most distinguished for forming the elite Varangian Guard army unit in the Byzantine Empire, serving as the personal bodyguard of the Byzantine Roman Emperor in Constantinople</li></ol><p>From Medieval Latin <i>Varingus</i>, <i>Waringus</i>, from a Germanic origin, likely via Old Norse <i>væringi</i> (literally “oath sworn”), from <i>vár</i> (“pledge, oath”) +&lrm; <i>-ing</i> (“suffix to form gerund noun”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Illyrian: Bardylis, a chief of the Illyrians defeated by Philip of Macedonia</li></ol><p>From Illyrian. Widely connected to Albanian <i>bardhë</i> (“white”) and Messapic <i>&#42;barzides</i>, although a more scrupulous investigation would note the unusual and rather early deaffricativisation of the original <i>&#42;-ǵ-</i> into what Greek authors heard as <i>-δ-</i> (-d-). A noteworthy mention is also the similarity with the Illyrian tribe <i>Bardaeī</i>, which as variant of <i>Vardaeī</i>, <i>Vardiaeī</i>, <i>Ἀρδιαῖοι</i> (Ardiaîoi), <i>Οὐαρδαῖοι</i> (Ouardaîoi) points to the plosivisation of an original initial <i>&#42;w-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marj (a city in Libya)</li></ol><p>From the local tribe of the Barraci.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Varro</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Varrō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Batnae; Suruç, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Battus</li></ol><p>The name is said to mean "tongue-tied" or "stuttering, stammering," related to <i>βατταρίζω</i> (battarízō, “to stutter, say nonsense”), eventually imitative. Herodotus and Pindar challenged this origin and promoted a folk etymology of the name being a translation for the Libyan/Berber word for "king."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>An island situated on the south-east of Cape Sunium</li><li>A city of Laconia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the Belgae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bembina, Argolis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berga, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beroea (an ancient city in Aleppo, Syria)</li><li>Beroea (ancient city of Veria, Greece)</li><li>Beroea (city of ancient Thrace near modern Stara Zagora)</li></ol><p>Compare Ancient Macedonian <i>Φέροιᾰ</i> (Phéroiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bessus, satrap of Bactria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian, and suggested to be from Late Old Persian <i>&#42;Bēçā-</i>, from Old Persian <i>&#42;Bayaçā-</i>, formed from nouns related to Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;báyHati</i> (“to fear”), and <i>&#42;θráHyati</i> (“to protect”), respectively, however, no such nouns are attested in Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bephyrus or Baphyras, a river of Pieria flowing through the city of Dium</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Οὐηΐοι</i> (Ouēḯoi, “Veii (city in Etruria)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bias</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marcus Calpurnius Bibulus</li><li>Any individual named Bibulus (<i>male</i>)</li><li>toponym (name of rivers, places)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Bibulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Biennus, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Victor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Victor</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the Bistones of Bithynia; a Biston</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baiae; Baia, Italy</li></ol><p>Compare <i>Baiae</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baebius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Baebius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Babylonia (an ancient empire and geographic region of Mesopotamia, existing from 1850 <small>BCE</small> to 539 <small>BCE</small>, based around the city of Babylon; at its maximum extent, covering parts of modern-day Iraq, Kuwait, Syria and Iran)</li></ol><p>From <i>Βαβυλών</i> (Babulṓn, “Babylon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Babylonian</li></ol><p>From <i>Βαβυλών</i> (Babulṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Babel</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Babylon (an ancient city, the ancient capital of Babylonia in modern Iraq, built on the banks of the Euphrates)</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒆍𒀭𒊏𒆠</i> (Bābilim).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Babylonian</li></ol><p>From <i>Βαβυλών</i> (Babulṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ancient region in modern Iran, now known as Bisotun</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagastānah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Bagadates</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a general of Tigranes the Great</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagadātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Bagapates</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a eunuch of Cyrus the Great, Cambyses II and Darius the Great</li><li>beheader of Cyrus the Younger</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagapātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Bagasaces, from Old Persian, a Persian general</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagasakah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baghdad</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Bagrada; Medjerda River, Tunisia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Bagoas, from Old Persian, a Persian courtier of Alexander the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagavahyah</i>, from <i>𐏎</i> (BG /⁠bagaʰ⁠/, “god”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;vahyah</i> (“better”). Cognate with Imperial Aramaic <i>𐡁𐡂𐡅𐡄𐡉</i> (bgwhy).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Bathycles, one of the Myrmidons who took part in the Trojan War</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s, “deep, profound”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bathynias, a river of Thrace</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Baeoraspus, from Proto-Sarmatian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Baivaraspa</i> (“having ten thousand horses”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bactria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bactrian</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) an inhabitant of Bactria; a Bactrian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Bactriānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical clan</i>) The Bacchiadae, a Doric clan</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>Βάκχος</i> (Bákkhos) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Bacchylides</li></ol><p><i>Βᾰκχῠλῐ́ς</i> (Băkkhŭlĭ́s, female name) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs, masculine patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Balanea; Baniyas, Syria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Medieval Greek foreign given name</i>) Vladimir</li></ol><p>From Old Bulgarian *Валдимѣръ, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Voldiměrъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bilhah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>בִּלְהָה</i> (Bilhā́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Akkadian, equivalent to English Balthazar or Belshazzar</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>בֵּלְטְשַׁאצַּר</i> (Bēlṭəšaṣṣar), from Akkadian <i>Bēl-šarra-uṣur</i> (literally “may Baal protect the king”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Banadaspus, the name of a king of the Iazyges</li></ol><p>From Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Vanataspa</i> (literally “having victorious horses”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barak</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>בָּרָק</i> (bārāq).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barabbas, a criminal released by Pontius Pilate instead of Jesus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>בּר אַבָּא</i> (bār ábbā, “son of [a] father”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Byzantine,&#32;historical) Barbalissus (an ancient city in Aleppo Governorate, Syria), Balis (a medieval city)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Barbalissus</i>, from Aramaic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Byzantine,&#32;historical) alternative form of <i>Βαρβαλισσός</i> (Barbalissós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bargylia, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bartholomew, a disciple of Jesus.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>בַּר תַּלְמַי</i> (“son of Tolmai”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barcelona (the capital city of Catalonia, Spain)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Barcinō</i>, itself named after <i>Barca</i>, a Carthaginian with a name ultimately derived (via Phoenician <i>𐤁𐤓𐤒</i> (brq)) from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;baraḳ-</i> (“lightning”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a male given name from Middle Georgian, equivalent to English Barlaam</li></ol><p>From Middle Georgian <i>ბალაჰვარ</i> (balahvar).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barnabas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>בַּר נְבִיָּא</i> (bar nəḇiyyā, literally “prophet’s son”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Barygaza; Bharuch, India</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Prakrit <i>&#42;𑀪𑀭𑀼𑀕𑀘𑁆𑀙</i> (&#42;bharugaccha), older form of <i>𑀪𑀭𑀼𑀅𑀘𑁆𑀙</i> (bharuaccha), from Sanskrit <i>भरुकच्छ</i> (bharukaccha).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Basilisene (an ancient canton of the kingdom of Armenia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bastarnae, a people of Sarmatia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bate, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bohemond</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Baetis, Guadalquivir (a river in southern Spain)</li></ol><p>According to Villar, from an Old European Hydronym <i>&#42;bai-t-</i>, also found in the place name <i>Biturgia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Beelzebub (a name for Satan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>בַּעַל זְבוּל</i> (báʿal zəḇūl). Compare <i>Βεελζεβούβ</i> (Beelzeboúb), from <i>בַּעַל זְבוּב</i> (báʿal zəḇūḇ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Belisarius, a Byzantine general</li></ol><p>Unknown, possibly of Thracian or Illyrian origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bellerophon</li></ol><p>From <i>Βέλλερος</i> (Bélleros) +&lrm; <i>-φόντης</i> (-phóntēs, “slayer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Βελλεροφόντης</i> (Bellerophóntēs, “Bellerophon”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) synonym of <i>Ἐνετία</i> (Enetía), Venice (a port city in northeastern Italy; former capital of an independent republic)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>Ἐνετία</i> (Enetía) under influence from Latin <i>Venetia</i> (“land of the Veneti; Venice”), from <i>Venetī</i> + <i>-ia</i>, of uncertain but presumably Venetic origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bendis, a Thracian goddess identified with Artemis</li></ol><p>According to Hawkins, no explanation proposed hitherto looks entirely plausible. He suggests to connect the theonym with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wenh₁-</i> (“to wish, love”), the same root of Latin <i>Venus</i>: this theory is interesting but not very convincing. Phonologically, the rendering of <i>*u̯-</i> as <i>b-</i> in Thracian seems to be indeed possible, but this etymology does not comply with the main and most essential characteristic of the goddess, her association with hunting. On the other hand, Tomaschek prefers connecting the word with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i> (“to bind”), which is fully compatible with hunting associations. First, the name can be interpreted simply as "Binder" and refers to the goddess' ability to tame wild animals. Second, the meaning of <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i> might develop to the notion of "catching": in this case, the name would mean "Catcher", "Huntress". A third, more speculative possibility would be to assume that in Thracian the development of <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i> has taken a similar turn as in Old English <i>bindan</i> (“to bind”), <i>bendan</i> (“to stretch a bow”) and that at the base of the name lies a noun "bow": in this case Bendis would mean "Archer".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) synonym of <i>Ἐνετικός</i> (Enetikós), Venetian, of or related to Venice</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>Ἐνετικός</i> (Enetikós) under influence from Latin <i>veneticus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Biblical Hebrew, equivalent to English Benjamin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>בִּנְיָמִין</i> (binyāmīn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Venusia, Venosa (a town in Potenza, Basilicata)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Venusia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berecynthus; Malaxa Mountain, Crete, Greece</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-κύνθος</i> suggests a Pre-Greek origin. Compare other mountain names like <i>Κύνθος</i> (Kúnthos) and <i>Ἀράκυνθος</i> (Arákunthos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bergule; Lüleburgaz, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Ancient Macedonian, equivalent to English Berenice, Bernice, or Veronica</li><li>Berenice, now Benghazi (a city in Cyrenaica, Libya)</li><li>Berenice, now بَرْنِيس (barnīs) (a city in Upper Egypt) on the Red Sea 280km east of Aswan – Berenice Troglodytica</li><li>Berenice (a town in the northeastern coast of modern Djibouti) – Βερενίκη ἐπὶ Δειρῆς</li><li>Berenice (a town on the coast of modern Sudan) – Βερενίκη Πάνχρυσος</li><li>Berenice, now Aqaba (a city in modern Jordan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ancient Macedonian <i>Βερενῑ́κη</i> (Berenī́kē). Doublet of <i>Φερενῑ́κη</i> (Pherenī́kē).<br/>The name of the city in Libya, formerly called <i>Εὐεσπερίδες</i> (Euesperídes) or <i>Ἑσπερίς</i> (Hesperís), originated when it was refounded following the marriage of Ptolemy III of the Ptolemaic Kingdom to Berenice II of Egypt, who was born in the region of Cyrenaica.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethabara, a place on the Jordan where John the Baptist was baptizing.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>בית עברא</i> (literally “ferry house”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethany</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>בֵּית עַנְיָה</i> (“house of Anaiah”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Βηθλεέμ</i> (Bēthleém)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>בֵּית לֶחֶם</i> (bēṯ léḥem).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethlehem (a city in the West Bank, Palestine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>בֵּית לֶחֶם</i> (bêṯ leḥem).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bethsaida</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>בית צידה</i> (literally “house of the hunt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berytus, Beirut (the capital and largest city of Lebanon)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤁𐤓𐤕</i> (bērūt, “Beirut”), from Proto-West Semitic <i>&#42;biʾrāt-</i> (“wells”). Doublet of <i>Βηρώθ</i> (Bērṓth, “Beeroth”) from Biblical Hebrew <i>בְּאֵרוֹת</i> (bəʾērōṯ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Berytian</li></ol><p><i>Βηρυτός</i> (Bērutós) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bestia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Bēstia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bedriacum; Calvatone, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Bianor</li></ol><p>From <i>βίᾱ</i> (bíā, “bodily strength”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man, husband”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Ὑδάσπης</i> (Hudáspēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bizye, Thrace; nowadays Vize, in Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Bithynia (a geographic region, ancient kingdom, and historical province of the Roman Empire in the northwest of Asia Minor in modern Turkey)</li></ol><p>From <i>Βῑθῡνός</i> (Bīthūnós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Bithyni</li><li>an inhabitant of Bithynia; a Bithynian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Thracian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vincentius; Vicentius; Vincent</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Vicentius</i>, a variant of <i>Vincentius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Filyos (a river in Turkey, which flows into the Black Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the Bisaltae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bisanthe, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Blanda; Maratea, Basilicata, Italy</li><li>Blanda; Blanes, Catalonia, Spain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Blanda</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Vlach</li><li>an East Romance-speaking inhabitant of Greece</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>влахъ</i> (vlaxŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;volxъ</i> (“Roman, Romance-speaker”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walhaz</i> (“foreigner, Celt, Roman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wallachia (a historical region and former principality in Eastern Europe, now part of southern Romania)</li></ol><p><i>Βλάχος</i> (Blákhos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Blaudus or Blaundus, Phrygia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Blossius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Blossius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boagrius (a river in Locris, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boebe, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boanerges, an epithet given by Jesus to the disciples James and John.</li></ol><p>Traditionally glossed "sons of thunder"; apparently from Aramaic <i>בני רגז</i> (bəney rəgaz, literally “sons of rage”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boedromion: the third month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around September and October</li></ol><p>From <i>Βοηδρόμῐᾰ</i> (Boēdrómĭă), the name of an Athenian festival in which was celebrated the help given by Theseus against the Amazons.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boeotia (a historical region in central Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>Βοιωτός</i> (Boiōtós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boeotus</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Boeotia; a Boeotian</li></ol><p>From <i>βοώτης</i> (boṓtēs, “herdsman”), from <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow, cattle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Voconius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Voconius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) A nymph loved by Apollo</li><li>Bolina, Achaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bolissus; Volissos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boniface</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Bonifātius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the north wind, personified as Boreas</li><li>the North</li></ol><p>Named as the god of the north wind. Perhaps related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gora</i> (“mountain, hill, mountainous elevation covered in forests”) and Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;gr̥Híš</i> (“mountain”), in which case from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷorH-eh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷerH-</i> (“mountain, forest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Βορέᾱς</i> (Boréās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Borinus, an ancient city of Phoenicia situated between Beirut and Sidon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>Βορέᾱς</i> (Boréās, “Boreas”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Borysthenes; Dnieper river</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Baurēštana</i>, from <i>&#42;baurēšta</i> (“dark brown”), superlative of <i>&#42;baura</i> (“brown, red, dun”) + <i>&#42;-na</i> (nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant along the Bosporus; a Bosporite</li></ol><p>From <i>Βόσπορος</i> (Bósporos, “Bosporus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Βοττῐαίᾱ</i> (Bottĭaíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bottiaea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Bottiaeans</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bottike, Bottice (an area in western Chalcidice, Greece)</li></ol><p>Feminine substantivization of an adjective derived from <i>Βοττίᾱ</i> (Bottíā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Bubares, from Old Persian, a Persian nobleman and son of Megabazus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Būbarah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bugonia (the title of a poem by Eumelus of Corinth according to Varro)</li></ol><p>From <i>βουγονής</i> (bougonḗs, “born of an ox”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>γονή</i> (gonḗ, “offspring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Budini, an ancient Scythian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buthrotum; Butrint, Albania</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Budva, Butua</li></ol><p>According to Ernst Eichler and others, Budva, Butua and <i>Βουθόη</i> (Bouthóē) is ultimately derived from the word <i>Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;bukta-</i></i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bucephalus</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>κεφᾰλή</i> (kephălḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Buprasium, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Wadjet, the cobra-shaped patron goddess of Lower Egypt</li><li>Per-Wadjet, the aforementioned deity’s major cult center</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./fee40bb1.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="wAD" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./76993ec3.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="t" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6de4f971.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="I12" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (wꜣḏt).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bubastis (a city in Egypt)</li><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Bastet: a goddess, Isis or her daughter, identified with Artemis by Herodotus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>pr-bꜣstt</i>, from <i>pr</i> (“house”) + <i>bꜣstt</i> (“Bastet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Buddha (founder of Buddhism)</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>बुद्ध</i> (buddha) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Budium, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol><p>Of obscure, but probably non-Greek origin. Compare the Lycian settlement <i>Βουβών</i> (Boubṓn), thought by Ptolemy, Pliny, and Stephanus to be related to <i>Βούδειον</i> (Boúdeion). Also compare <i>Βούθεια</i> (Boútheia), Boutheia, also of Anatolian origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Budorus (a river in Euboea, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Busiris</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Butes, name of several different people of the Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>βούτης</i> (boútēs, “herdsman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bulis (a town in Phocis, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Bunus, son of Hermes and Alcidamia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bura, Achaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Burcas, a river of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Busae, a Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Butos, Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brenae, a Thracian tribe settled near the course of the river Hebrus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brenthe, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brennus</li></ol><p>Likely from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;brigantīnos</i> (“preeminent, outstanding”), and thus related to Welsh <i>brenin</i> (“king”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the Bruttii</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brixellum; Brescello, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>any individual among the Branchidae; a member of the Branchides family</li></ol><p>From <i>Βρᾰ́γχος</i> (Brắnkhos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Brasidas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brauron (an ancient city in Attica, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Brahmin</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Sanskrit <i>ब्राह्मण</i> (brā́hmaṇa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brindisi (a city in southern Italy)</li></ol><p>Said by ancient sources (e.g. Strabo) to be a corruption of the Messapic word for the head of a male deer (see <i>βρέντιον</i>, <i>βρένδος</i>), possibly based on the shape of the port. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrendos</i> (“stag, red deer”). Smith noted: "It seems probable that the real native word was Brendon or Brenda... whence Festus tells us that Brenda was used by some writers as a poetic form for Brundusium."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Great Britain, Britain (a large island off the north-west coast of Western Europe)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the British Isles</li></ol><p>From <i>Βρεττανός</i> (Brettanós) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of the British Isles or of Great Britain; a Briton; a Brython</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Breuci, a Pannonian tribe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Briareus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Brizo, goddess who divines by dreams</li></ol><p>From <i>βρίζω</i> (brízō, “to slumber”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Brilessus; Mount Pentelicus, Greece</li></ol><p>Probably from Pre-Greek, because of the suffix <i>-ησσ-</i>, which is found also in other placenames, like <i>Μυκαλησσός</i> (Mukalēssós) and <i>Καρμυλησσός</i> (Karmulēssós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Briseus, king and priest in Lyrnessus and father of Briseis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Briseis</li></ol><p>From <i>Βρῑσεύς</i> (Brīseús) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), literally “daughter of Briseus”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Brutus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Brūtus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bryclice (an ancient district of Cilicia in modern-day Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bryseae, Laconia, Greece</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>βρύση</i> (brúsē, “source, spring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Brongus; West Morava, Serbia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bubassus, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantium (an ancient Greek city situated on the Bosporus in modern Turkey, renamed Constantinople in 330 <small>C.E.</small>; modern Istanbul)</li></ol><p>From <i>Βῡ́ζᾱς</i> (Bū́zās, “Byzas”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), after the city's legendary founder.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a citizen of Byzantium, a Byzantine</li></ol><p><i>Βῡζᾰ́ντῐον</i> (Būzắntĭon, “Byzantium”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the Byzantine Empire, Byzantine (<i>entitling two fifth-century histories by the sophists Malchus and Priscus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Βῡζᾰ́ντῐον</i> (Būzắntĭon, “Byzantium”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰκός</i> (-ăkós, “of or pertaining to, -ac”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bylazora; Veles, Macedonia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byllis (an ancient city in Illyria, now in Albania)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bysnaei, a tribe of the Bebryces</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Boges, from Old Persian, a governor of Eion</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Baugah</i> (“releasing”) or <i>𐏎</i> (BG /⁠Bagaʰ⁠/, “god”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bolbe, a mythological lake goddess</li><li>Lake Volvi in Greece, named after the goddess</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Iranian, possibly composed from the elements <i>&#42;bawrah</i> (“bay, brown”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;Hácwah</i> (“horse”), via Late Old Median <i>&#42;Bōraspah</i>, alongside earlier <i>Βουρασπος</i> (Bouraspos), cognate with Late Old Persian <i>&#42;Bōrasiyah</i> (whence borrowing Elamite (bu-raš-še)). Alternatively borrowed via Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Bōraspi</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Borsippa, Babylonia, Iraq</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bosporus</li></ol><p>Unknown. Reinterpreted (by the ancient Greeks) as <i>βοός</i> (boós, “cow”) + <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “passage”) and given a folk etymology to place it within Greek mythology.</p><p>The folk etymology refers to the myth of Io (told, for example, by Aeschylus in his <i>Prometheus Bound</i>), in which she travelled there after being turned into a heifer by Hera (or in other versions by Zeus). (See <b>Io (mythology)</b> on Wikipedia.)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bostra; Bosra, Syria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantes, a tribe of North Africa mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Byzas, from Thracian, legendary founder of Byzantium</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;būzas</i> (“he-goat”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuǵ-</i> (“buck, he-goat”). Compare Persian بز (boz), English <i>buck</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzeres, a people of Pontus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byces, a river of Crimea mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Batis, from Old Persian, a Persian military commander who resisted Alexander the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Bātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bel; Belus</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒂗</i> (bēlum).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gabala (a coastal town in Syria; modern Jableh)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gabii (an ancient city in Latium, Italy)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Gabii</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gagae, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Ganges, India</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>गङ्गा</i> (gaṅgā, literally “swift-goer”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gad</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>גד</i> (Gāḏ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gadara (a town in Palestine, the site of modern Umm Qais)</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>גֶּדֶר</i> (geḏer, “wall”), from <i>גָּדֵר</i> (gāḏēr, “enclosing wall”), from <i>גָּדַר</i> (gāḏar, “to wall up <i>or</i> off”). Compare to other names of fortified towns:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cadiz (a port city in Andalusia, Spain)</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤂𐤃𐤓</i> (gdr /⁠gādēr, gādīr⁠/, literally “wall”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gedera</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>גְּדֵרָה</i> (gəḏērā). More at <i>Γᾰ́δᾰρᾰ</i> (Gắdără).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gazorus, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galba</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Galba</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gamala; Gamla, Golan Heights</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaphara, Africa, Tunisia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Gaius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gela (a city in Sicily)</li></ol><p>Named after Γέλᾱς (Gélās, “the river Gela”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Gela</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>Γελλώ</i> (Gellṓ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Gelo</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Genua; Genoa, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Genua</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gerara, Palestine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gerasa; Jerash, Jordan</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Germa, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Gerrhus; river Molochna, Ukraine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gisco</li></ol><p>From Phoenician.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Getae</li><li>in Late Antiquity usage, a poetic word for Visigoth</li></ol><p>Of obscure origin. Due to the heavy Scythian influence (evidently Strabo and Pliny went as far as to call them a Scythian tribe), Isidore of Seville thought that the name was a Greek corruption of <i>Scythia</i>, calling the names "not that much different...with one letter changed and one removed."</p><p>Compare with <i>Μασσαγέται</i> (Massagétai) and <i>Θυσσαγέται</i> (Thussagétai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gigonus, Crossaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gilgamesh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Akkadian <i>𒄑𒂆𒈦</i> (Gilgāmeš), from Sumerian <i>𒀭𒄑𒉋𒂵𒎌</i> (<sup>d</sup>gilgameš₂ /⁠Gilgameš⁠/)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gindarus; Jindires, Syria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Gaeson, in Ionia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaetuli, a tribe settled in North Africa</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gabinius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Gabīnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Gabriel</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Gabriel (archangel)</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>גַּבְרִיאֵל</i> (gaḇrīʾēl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gadatas, name of several Persian satraps</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Gadātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Gadara, Gadarene</li></ol><p>From <i>Γᾰ́δᾰρᾰ</i> (Gắdără) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gatheae, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galabrii, an ancient Illyrian tribe settled in what is now eastern Kosovo</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Γᾰλᾰ́της</i> (Gălắtēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) name of several different mythological characters</li></ol><p>Said to be from <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”), but this could be mere folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaul (native or inhabitant of the historical region of Gaul, or poetically the modern nation of France) (<i>usually male</i>)</li><li>a Galatian</li></ol><p>Likely derived from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;galnati</i> (“to be able”). See also <i>Κελτός</i> (Keltós) and Latin <i>Gallus</i>, which might be from the same source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galerius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Galērius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galatia (a historical region of ancient Asia Minor, in what is now central Turkey)# Galatia (a former province of the Roman Empire, existing from 25 BC to the late 7th century AD)</li><li>Gaul (a historical region of Western Europe referring to areas occupied by Celts during Roman times, roughly corresponding to modern France, Luxembourg, Belgium, most of Switzerland, and parts of Northern Italy (Lombardy), the Netherlands, and Germany west of the Rhine)</li></ol><p>From <i>Γᾰλᾰ́της</i> (Gălắtēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Galaesus; river Galaso, Apulia, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galilee (a mountainous geographic region in northern Israel)</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>גָּלִיל</i> (gālīl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Galilean</li></ol><p>From <i>Γαλιλαία</i> (Galilaía, “Galilee”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaul</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Gallia</i>. Greeks earlier called Gaul <i>Γαλατία</i> (Galatía), also the name for Galatia. Greeks still call France <i>Γαλλία</i> (Gallía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Gamaliel</i>:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Γαμαλιήλ ὁ Πρεσβύτερος (Gamaliḗl ho Presbúteros), “Gamaliel the Elder”, a leading authority in the Sanhedrin in the early first century <small>CE</small></li></ol></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>גַמַלִיאיֵל</i> (Ḡamalīʾēl) from Biblical Hebrew <i>גַּמְלִיאֵל</i> (Gamlīʾēl). Compare Classical Syriac <i>ܓ݂ܰܡܰܠܺܝܐܝܶܠ</i> (Ḡamalīʾēl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gamelion: the seventh month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around January and February</li></ol><p>From <i>γᾰμηλῐ́ᾱ</i> (gămēlĭ́ā, “wedding feast”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn), because it was the fashionable time for weddings.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gandarii, an ancient tribe of India mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Ganymede</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ganymede</li></ol><p>Socrates in Xenophon's Symposium (8.29–30) implies the etymology <i>γᾰ́νῠμαι</i> (gắnŭmai, “to be glad”) +&lrm; <i>μήδεᾰ</i> (mḗdeă, “plans”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Garamantes, an ancient Saharan people settled in Libya</li></ol><p>From a Berber word meaning "castles" or "cities".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gargettus (a town in Attica, Greece; birthplace of Epicurus)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Garonne</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Garumna</i>, probably from Gaulish <i>&#42;garunda</i> (“shallows, riverbank”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Garsaura; Aksaray, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Γηρῠόνης</i> (Gērŭónēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaugamela</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gavrio, Andros, Greece, port town of the Cyclades dating to the Hellenistic period; Latin: Gaurium</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Geganius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Gegānius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gideon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>גִּדְעוֹן</i> (giḏʿōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Koine) Gethsemane (garden in ancient Jerusalem)</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>גת שמנא</i> (Gaṯ Šəmānê, literally “oil press”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Γεθσημανί</i> (Gethsēmaní)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gello (a kind of vampiress, demoness, or goblin supposed to carry off young children and cause infertility)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Akkadian <i>𒂵𒀠𒇻𒌑</i> (ga-al-lu-ú /⁠gallû⁠/), <i>𒋼𒇲</i> (GAL<sub>5</sub>.LÁ, gallû, “Gallu”), a word from the Assyro-Babylonian religion perhaps related to <i>ghoul</i>, as a demonic revenant who brings sickness and death, from Sumerian <i>𒋼𒇲</i> (<i>GAL<sub>5</sub>.LÁ</i>, galla, “demon; constable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>Γενέσιον</i> (Genésion), <i>Γενέση</i> (Genésē, name of a town in Argolis) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a male given name, Gennadius</li></ol><p>From <i>γεννάδας</i> (gennádas, “noble”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gennesaret</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>כִּנֶּרֶת</i> (Kinnéret).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Genusus; river Shkumbin, Albania</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Gerasim</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gerenian (an epithet of Nestor)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Geraestus, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gerasenes (inhabitants of Gerasa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gerenia, Laconia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Germania (an ancient region of Central Europe, roughly corresponding to modern Germany)</li></ol><p>From <i>Γερμᾱνός</i> (Germānós, “German”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā) or borrowed from Latin <i>Germānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Germanicus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Germānicus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A Germanic person</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Germānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English George</li></ol><p>From <i>γεωργός</i> (geōrgós, “farmer”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Γηρῠόνης</i> (Gērŭónēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Γηρῠόνης</i> (Gērŭónēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Geryon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Γηρῠόνης</i> (Gērŭónēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Glisas, Boeotia; Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Glaucia</li><li>Gaius Servilius Glaucia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Glaucia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Glaphyrae; Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Glaucon</li></ol><p>From <i>γλαυκός</i> (glaukós, “gleaming”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, Strabon suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Glaucus</li></ol><p>From <i>γλαυκός</i> (glaukós, “grey”) with shift of accent.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Glyppia, Laconia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Gobryas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎥𐎢𐎲𐎽𐎢𐎺</i> (g-u-b-ru-u-v /⁠Gaubᵃruva⁠/), possibly meaning “chewing cattle” or “having cattle (or beef) for food”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a male given name</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Alanic, possibly from <i>&#42;Ɣozin</i>, cognate with Old Median <i>&#42;Gauzainah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Goetosyrus, the Scythian name of Apollo</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;gaiθā</i> (“herd, possessions”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;sūra</i> (“strong, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Golgi, Cyprus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Golgotha</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>גּוּלְגַּלְתָּא</i> (Gulgaltā); compare Biblical Hebrew <i>גֻּלְגֹּלֶת</i> (Gulgôlet), Classical Syriac <i>ܓ݁ܳܓ݂ܽܘܠܬ݁ܳܐ</i> (Gogūltā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Goliath</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>גָּלְיָת</i> (golyāṯ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Γόμορρᾰ</i> (Gómorrhă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antigonus II Gonatas</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Either from <i>Γόννοι</i> (Gónnoi, “Gonnoi, a town in Thessaly”) or from <i>γόνυ</i> (gónu, “knee”), in reference to armour worn over the knee.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gonoessa, Achaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gorgias</li></ol><p>From <i>γοργός</i> (gorgós, “grim”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gorgippia; Anapa, Russia</li></ol><p>From <i>Γόργιππος</i> (Górgippos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Gorgythion, son of Priamus and <i>Castianeira</i></li></ol><p>From <i>Γόργυθος</i> (Górguthos) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>gorgon</li></ol><p>From <i>γοργός</i> (gorgós, “terrible”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek history</i>) Gordias</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phrygian <i>&#42;Gordiyas</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gorpiaeus, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to August</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Γόρτῡς</i> (Górtūs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gothic (of or relating to the Goths or their language)</li></ol><p>From <i>Γότθος</i> (Gótthos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Guneus, the legendary leader of the Perrhaebians during the Trojan War</li></ol><p>From <i>γουνός</i> (gounós, “fruitful land”) and +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gracchus; a member of the Gracchi, a Roman plebeian family</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Gracchus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Greek</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Graecus, a character in Greek mythology, said to be a son of Thessalos, the king of Phthia; or else a son of Pandora and Zeus.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Graecian, a member of an ancient Boeotian tribe that migrated to Italy.</li></ol><p>Aristotle was one of the first to use the name Γραικοί (Graikoí), saying that the area around Dodona and Achelous was inhabited by the <i>Σελλοί</i> (“Selloi”) and a people formerly called Graeci and now <i>Ἕλληνες</i> (“Hellenes”).</p><p>According to one hypothesis, the name means “from <i>Γραῖα</i> (Graîa, “Graea”)”, a city on the coast of <i>Βοιωτῐ́ᾱ</i> (Boiōtĭ́ā, “Boeotia”), and it was given to the Greeks of southern Italy by the Romans. The city’s name itself means “grey, venerable” from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵerh₂-</i> (“to grow old”). This is in stark contrast to eastern names for the Greeks based on <i>Ἰωνία</i> (Iōnía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Granicus, Biga Çayı (a river in Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gratian, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Grātiānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Graea, Boeotia; Greece</li></ol><p>A name meaning "grey", from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵerh₂-</i> (“to grow old”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Gregory used chiefly by Christians. Its sense is synonymous with <i>ἄγρυπνος</i> (ágrupnos, “awake”).</li></ol><p>From <i>γρήγορος</i> (grḗgoros, “watchful; alert; awake”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), from <i>ἐγρήγορᾰ</i> (egrḗgoră) (perfect form used as present) of <i>ἐγείρω</i> (egeírō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Grumentum, Lucania, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Grūmentum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Grynium, Aeolis, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Gyrtias</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Gobryes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎥𐎢𐎲𐎽𐎢𐎺</i> (g-u-b-ru-u-v /⁠Gaubaruvaʰ⁠⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gogarene (an ancient canton of the kingdom of Armenia, in present day northern Armenia, northeast Turkey and southwest Georgia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Gosithres, from Old Persian, a Persian man, brother of Artaxerxes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Gāuciθrah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gomorrah.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>עֲמֹרָה</i> (ʿămōrā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gomphi; Gomfoi, Trikala, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gonnus; Gonnoi, Larissa, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Gorgippus</li></ol><p>From <i>γοργός</i> (gorgós, “fierce, terrible”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gordium, Gordion (a city in Turkey)</li></ol><p>From Phrygian <i>𐊩𐌏𐌛𐊅𐊄𐌌</i> (Gordum, literally “city”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰórdʰ-os</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gorillai (a tribe of hairy women described by Hanno the Navigator)</li></ol><p>Likely Punic, though possibly ultimately from an African word (used to describe hairy people) that entered Greek via Punic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Γόρτῡς</i> (Górtūs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gortyn (a city in Crete, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Goth</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Gothic <i>&#42;𐌲𐌿𐍄𐌰</i> (&#42;guta), attested in <i>𐌲𐌿𐍄𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰</i> (gutþiuda).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gyaros, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gyges</li></ol><p>From Lydian <i>𐤨𐤰𐤨𐤠𐤮</i> (kukas, “grandfather”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gythium; Gytheio, Laconia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Γύθειον</i> (Gútheion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Gylippus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gyndes, a river of Assyria and tributary of the Tigris</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;gunda</i> (literally “thick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gyrton (a town in Thessaly, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 10 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. An abbreviation of δέκα (déka, “ten”). Equivalent to the Roman numeral X.</li></ol><p>From the Phoenician letter <i>𐤃</i> (d, “dālet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Δαυῑ̈́δ</i> (Dauī̈́d)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Dacian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dan</li><li>Doric form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>דָּן</i> (Dān).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dnieper (a major river in Eastern Europe which flows south through Belarus, Russia and Ukraine into the Black Sea)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Δānāfr</i> (literally “deep river”). Cognate with Old East Slavic <i>Дънѣпръ</i> (Dŭněprŭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dai, a Scythian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol><p>Of Indo-Iranian and eventually Proto-Indo-European origin, but the exact meaning is unclear. See Dahae.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dapyx, a chieftain of the Getae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dardanus, a hero in Greek mythology.</li><li>Dardanus, a mythological city on the Hellespont.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Dardanian, a Trojan</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>δαρδάπτω</i> (dardáptō, “to slay, to burn up”) or <i>δαρδάπτω</i> (dardáptō, “eat”), <i>δάπτω</i> (dáptō, “to devour”) (see Latin <i>daps</i>). Otherwise from Thracian or Pre-Greek.</p><p>After the hero.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Derna, Lybia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Datum, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Daphnis, a Sicilian herdsman and the inventor of bucolic poetry</li></ol><p>Probably from Ancient Greek <i>δᾰ́φνη</i> (dắphnē, “bay laurel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Decelus, the mythological founder of Decelea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dexippus</li></ol><p>A compound of <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “to receive”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “a horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Derbe, Lycaonia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Derdas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dereium, Laconia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(a festival of Apollo at Delos)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Delian (of or from Delos)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) (epithet of Artemis)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter&#32;plural) (festival of Apollo at Delos)</li></ol><p>From <i>Δῆλος</i> (Dêlos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Delian (of or from Delos)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) (epithet of Artemis)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter&#32;plural) (festival of Apollo at Delos)</li></ol><p>From <i>Δῆλος</i> (Dêlos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Didius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Dīdius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Dicte; Mount Dikti, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dictynna; Britomartis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dindymon or Dindymum, a mountain in Kyzikos and one in Phrygia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dipaea, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Dipylon gate; the main gate of Athens located in the Kerameikos.</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-, “two; double”) +&lrm; <i>πύλη</i> (púlē, “gate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dirce</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dirphe, a mountain in the central part of Euboea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diphilus</li></ol><p>From <i>Διί</i> (Dií, “dative singular of Ζεύς (Zeús)”) +&lrm; <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “loving”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daedala, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daesius, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to May</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>David</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>David</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>David</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Dadaces, from Old Persian, a Persian general in the army of Xerxes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Dātakaʰ</i>, from the retrenchment of name with the element <i>𐎭𐎠𐎫𐎶</i> (d-a-t-m /⁠dātam⁠/, “law”) + extension <i>-𐎣</i> (-k /⁠-kaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dadastana, Bithynia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dacia</li></ol><p>Named after the Dacians (Δάκαι (Dákai))</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Delilah (mistress of Samson)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>דְּלִילָה</i> (dəlīlā, “[she who] weakened”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dalmatia, a region on the east coast of the Adriatic Sea.</li></ol><p>From <i>Δαλμᾰτεῖς</i> (Dalmăteîs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Damastium (an ancient city in the borderlands of Illyria and Paeonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric, Arcadian, and Boeotian form of <i>Δημήτηρ</i> (Dēmḗtēr)</li></ol><p>From the same source as <i>Δημήτηρ</i> (Dēmḗtēr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Damasithymus, son of Candaules</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰ́μᾰσῐς</i> (dắmăsĭs, “taming, subduing”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “wrath, fury”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Damascene</li></ol><p><i>Δαμασκός</i> (Damaskós) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a city in modern Syria; an ancient settlement, the ancient capital of various polities, most notably the Umayyad Caliphate from 661 to 744 <small>CE</small> and Aram-Damascus, existing from the 12th to 8th centuries <small>BCE</small></li></ol><p>Of Northwest Semitic origin. See Hebrew <i>דַּמֶּשֶׂק</i> (dammeśeq).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Damaspia, from Old Persian, queen of Persia and wife of Artaxerxes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Jāmāspiyā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Apparently from the root of <i>δαμάζω</i> (damázō, “to tame”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;demh₂-</i>, + <i>-ιᾱνός</i> (-iānós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Δημοκλῆς</i> (Dēmoklês, “Damocles”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Danaba (a city in Syria)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Danae</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek. Compare <i>Δαναός</i> (Danaós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Danaus</li></ol><p>From <i>Δαναός</i> (Danaós, “Danaus”); masculine of <i>Δαναΐς</i> (Danaḯs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Danaid (daughter of Danaus)</li></ol><p>From <i>Δαναός</i> (Danaós, “Danaus”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is); feminine of <i>Δαναΐδης</i> (Danaḯdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Danaans, the subjects of Danaus</li><li>the Greeks</li></ol><p>From <i>Δᾰνᾰός</i> (Dănăós, “Danaus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Danaus</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dandace (a city in Crimea, Ukraine)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Denthelethi, a Thracian tribe that lived near the sources of the river Strymon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Biblical Hebrew, equivalent to English Daniel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>דָּנִיֵּאל</i> (Dāniyyêl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a descendant of Dardanus, a Trojan</li></ol><p>From <i>Δάρδανος</i> (Dárdanos) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Dardania</li></ol><p>From <i>Δάρδανος</i> (Dárdanos) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Darius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎢𐏁</i> (d-a-r-y-u-š /⁠Dārayauš⁠/), shortened form of <i>𐎭𐎠𐎼𐎹𐎺𐎢𐏁</i> (d-a-r-y-v-u-š /⁠Dārayavaʰuš⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daritae, a Persian tribe settled near the Caspian Sea mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dascylium</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dascylium (a city in Bithynia, on the Sea of Marmara in modern Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Datames, satrap of Cappadocia</li></ol><p>Of disputed etymology. Either borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Dātamah</i>, or from <i>&#42;Dātāmah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Dataphernes, from Old Persian, a Persian killed by Alexander the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Dātafarnāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>David</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>דָּוִד</i> (dāwíḏ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>David</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>David</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daulis (a city in Phocis, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Daurises, a Persian commander and son-in-law of Hystaspes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Δahyuvraisah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daphnae, a city in Ancient Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daphnus, Phocis, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>δάφνη</i> (dáphnē, “laurel, bay”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”). See <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dinah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>דִּינָה</i> (Dīnā́, “Dinah”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dinarchus</li></ol><p><i>δεινός</i> (deinós, “terrible; mighty, powerful”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Develtus, Thrace, Bulgaria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Δῑδώ</i> (Dīdṓ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Deinocrates</li></ol><p><i>δεινός</i> (deinós, “terrible”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dinosthenes</li></ol><p>From <i>δεινός</i> (deinós) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dinolochus</li></ol><p>From <i>δεινός</i> (deinós) +&lrm; <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dinostratus</li></ol><p>From <i>δεινός</i> (deinós, “terrible”) +&lrm; <i>στρατός</i> (stratós, “army”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Disenor, a Lycian who fought in the Trojan War</li></ol><p>From <i>δεισήνωρ</i> (deisḗnōr, “fearing man”), from <i>δείδω</i> (deídō, “to fear”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Decapolis, the ten city region in Syria and Judea.</li></ol><p>From <i>δέκᾰ</i> (dékă, “ten”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Decebalus, from Dacian</li></ol><p>From Dacian <i>&#42;Dekebalus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Decelea (a city in Attica, Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>Δέκελος</i> (Dékelos) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) of December</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>December</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of the Decapolis; a Decapolitan</li></ol><p>From <i>Δεκᾰ́πολῐς</i> (Dekắpolĭs, “Decapolis”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Delminium, Duvno (a city in Dalmatia, in modern Bosnia and Herzegovina)</li></ol><p>Believed to be borrowed from Illyrian, akin to Albanian <i>delmë</i>, <i>dele</i> (“sheep”). The toponym <i>Dalmatia</i> has the same origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Delphi (a town in Phocis, Greece, the site of the Delphic oracle)</li></ol><p>From the plural of <i>δελφύς</i> (delphús, “womb”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelbʰ-</i>, see also Sanskrit <i>गर्भ</i> (garbha).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Delphi; a Delphian</li></ol><p>From <i>Δελφοί</i> (Delphoí, “Delphi”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dentatus (Roman cognomen)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Dentatus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dexiphanes</li></ol><p>From <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “to receive”) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs, “seeming, appearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>another name of the Syrian goddess Atargatis</li></ol><p>Northwest Semitic, identical to Ugaritic <i>𐎄𐎗𐎋𐎚</i> (drkt, “rule, power”), from the root <i>ד־ר־ך</i> / د ر ك (d r k).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dercylidas</li><li>the name of several historical figures</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satyrs (<i>in Illyrian</i>)</li></ol><p>From Illyrian, possibly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewH-</i>, whence also Ancient Greek <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “to rage, seethe”) and Sanskrit <i>धूनोति</i> (dhūnoti, “he shakes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Deucalion</li></ol><p>Folk etymology derives the name from Ancient Greek <i>δεῦκος</i> (deûkos), variation of <i>γλεῦκος</i> (gleûkos, “sweet new wine”), and <i>ἁλιεύς</i> (halieús, “sailor”), from <i>ἅλς</i> (háls).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Boeotian and Laconian form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;Dzéus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dyḗws</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Deimos, Terror (son of Ares)</li></ol><p>From <i>δειμός</i> (deimós, “terror”), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Deiphobus</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾱ́ῐ̈ος</i> (dā́ĭ̈os, “hostile”) +&lrm; <i>φόβος</i> (phóbos, “terror”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Demaratus</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρᾱτός</i> (arātós, “prayed against, cursed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Demeter</li></ol><p>The second half is generally believed to be <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr, “mother”). The first element, <i>δᾶ</i> (dâ), is classically explained as the Attic/Ionic variant of an archaic Doric form of <i>γῆ</i> (gê, “earth”); other theories include a connection with Albanian <i>dhe</i> (“earth”) or <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “ground, earth”).</p><p>The supposed Aeolic form <i>Δωμάτηρ</i> (Dōmátēr) is only attested through a reconstructed inscription from the 2nd century B.C. in Aegae. The inscription reads <i>&#91;&#93;ω&#91;&#93;ατρος</i> (&#91;&#93;ō&#91;&#93;atros), which has been read, as reported in Richard Bohn's "Altertümer von Aegae" as a genitive form of <i>Δωμάτηρ</i> (Dōmátēr). However, as Chantraine says, "the dialectal forms, notably Δωμάτηρ, do not offer any help to etymology," and it is debatable whether the inscription reads as such.</p><p>M. L. West (2007) proposes that the name is a borrowing from an Illyrian deity, attested in the Messapic <i>Damatura</i>, composed of <i>dā-</i> (“earth”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰǵʰ(e)m-</i>) + <i>-matura</i> (“mother”). This parallels the Illyrian god <i>&#42;Dei-paturos</i> (“sky-father”). The Ancient Greek <i>Δω-</i> (Dō-) form in the Lesbian dialect likely reflects a colloquial pronunciation of the non-Greek name.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Demetrius</li></ol><p>From <i>Δημήτηρ</i> (Dēmḗtēr, “Demeter”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Demetrias, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Δημήτριος</i> (Dēmḗtrios) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Democleides</li></ol><p>From <i>Δημοκλῆς</i> (Dēmoklês) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Democles or Damocles</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Demoleon, son of Antenor slain by Achilles</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Demomeles</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos) +&lrm; <i>μέλος</i> (mélos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Demoptolemus</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “the people”) +&lrm; <i>πτόλεμος</i> (ptólemos, “war”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Demosthenes</li><li>in particular, the 4th century <small>BCE</small> orator Demosthenes</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Demophanes</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs, “seeming, appearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Demophon</li></ol><p><i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Demuchus, a Trojan and son of Philetor</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to bear, carry, bring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Demodocus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Democlitus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Democritus</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in particular</i>) the pre-Socratic philosopher Democritus of Abdera</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>κρῐτός</i> (krĭtós, “chosen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Demostratus</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos) +&lrm; <i>στρατός</i> (stratós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Demophilus</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “loving”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Derusiaei, a Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Deidamia or Deidamea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Deioces</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;Δahyukah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Diagon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diagoras</li></ol><p>From <i>Διός</i> (Diós, genitive of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)) +&lrm; <i>ἀγορά</i> (agorá) +&lrm; <i>-ας</i> (-as).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for Apollo</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dido (legendary foundress and queen of Carthage)</li></ol><p>Probably a Phoenician honorary title related to Hebrew <i>דּוֹדָה</i> (doda, “aunt”) or from the same Proto-Semitic verbal root <i>&#42;d-w-d</i> ~ <i>&#42;w-d-d</i> (“to love”) as Hebrew <i>דָּוִד</i> (dāwiḏ, “David”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dicaearchus</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́κη</i> (dĭ́kē, “justice”) +&lrm; <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to rule”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dicaeopolis, name of cities</li><li>Dicaeopolis, name of persona at comedy The Acharnians by Aristophanes</li></ol><p>From <i>δίκαιος</i> (díkaios, “righteous”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diogenes</li></ol><p>Stress-shifted form of <i>διογενής</i> (diogenḗs, literally “born of Zeus”), from <i>Διός</i> (Diós, genitive of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs, “born of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dioclides</li></ol><p>From <i>Διοκλῆς</i> (Dioklês) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs) masculine patronymic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Diocletianopolis, name of cities</li></ol><p>From <i>Δῐοκλητῐᾱνοῦ</i> (Dĭoklētĭānoû, “of Diocletian”, genitive) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Diocletian (Roman emperor)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Dioclētiānus</i>, Latinized form of Ancient Greek <i>Διοκλῆς</i> (Dioklês).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diocles</li></ol><p>From either <i>δῖος</i> (dîos, “divine”) or <i>Διός</i> (Diós, genitive of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “glory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diomedon</li></ol><p>From <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>μέδων</i> (médōn, “ruler, lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diomedes</li><li>in particular, a mythological figure of the <i>Iliad</i></li></ol><p>From <i>Διός</i> (Diós, genitive of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)) +&lrm; <i>μήδεα</i> (mḗdea) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dionysicles</li></ol><p>From <i>Δῐόνῡσος</i> (Dĭónūsos, “Dionysus”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>nickname given to the <i>τεχνῖται Διονυσιακοί</i> (tekhnîtai Dionusiakoí, “thespians”)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>flatterers of Dionysius the Tyrant, including the school of Plato</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>Δῐόνῡσος</i> (Dĭónūsos, “Dionysus”) +&lrm; <i>κόλᾱξ</i> (kólāx, “flatterer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dionysodorus</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>Δῐόνῡσος</i> (Dĭónūsos, “the god Dionysos”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A festival of Dionysus, the Dionysia</li></ol><p>Neuter plural of <i>Διονῡ́σιος</i> (Dionū́sios, “of Dionysus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dionysius or Dennis</li></ol><p>From <i>Διόνῡσος</i> (Diónūsos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diopeithes</li></ol><p>From <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “persuade”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diophanes</li></ol><p>From <i>Διός</i> (Diós, genitive of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs, “seeming, appearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diognetus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diodotus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>δοτός</i> (dotós, “granted”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diodorus</li></ol><p>From <i>Διός</i> (Diós, “genitive of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Dionysus</li></ol><p>Attested in Mycenaean Greek (13th to 12th century <small>B.C.E.</small>) as <i>𐀇𐀺𐀝𐀰</i> (di-wo-nu-so). Dialectal variants include <i>Διένῡσος</i> (Diénūsos), <i>Δεύνῡσος</i> (Deúnūsos), <i>Δίννῡσος</i> (Dínnūsos) and others.</p><p>Popular etymology often connected it with <i>Διός</i> (Diós), the genitive of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”).</p><p>The <i>dio-</i> forms are probably built by analogy from an original stem <i>die-</i>. The compound <i>die-nūs-os</i> is analysed as from a verbal stem <i>die-</i> (from <i>δίεμαι</i> (díemai, “to chase, to impel”)). The <i>nūs-</i> element gave rise to a toponym <i>Νῦσα</i> (Nûsa, “Nysa”), a mountain where the god was nursed by nymphs (the <i>Nysiads</i>, <i>Nysa</i> is also the name given to one of these nymphs). According to the testimony of Pherecydes of Syros (6th c. <small>B.C.E.</small>), <i>nūsa</i> is a word for "tree". Janda (<i>Die Musik nach dem Chaos</i>, 2010) suggests an original meaning of "impeller of the (world-)tree" (the axis mundi), connecting the god with archaic cosmology. The close association or indeed identity of Dionysus with a tree (especially the fig tree) is well attested in the classical period.</p><p>More at Dionysus.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Dioscurus; one of the two Dioscuri; either one of Castor or Pollux</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>Διός</i> (Diós, “Zeus's”) +&lrm; <i>κόρος</i> (kóros, “boy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Δῐόσκορος</i> (Dĭóskoros)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>Δῐός</i> (Dĭós, “Zeus's”) +&lrm; <i>κοῦρος</i> (koûros, “boy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diotimus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honor”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diophantus</li><li>in particular, the third century mathematician Diophantus of Alexandria</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>Δῐο-</i> (Dĭo-, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>φᾰν-</i> (phăn-, “to appear”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Diophilus</li></ol><p>From <i>Διός</i> (Diós, genitive of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)) +&lrm; <i>φίλος</i> (phílos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dione</li></ol><p>Derived by syncope from early Ancient Greek <i>Διϝωνη</i> (Diwōnē), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;DiwHóneh₂</i> (“one under authority of Zeus or heaven”), from <i>&#42;dyew-</i> + <i>&#42;-Hō</i>. Compare Old Latin <i>Dīvāna</i>, Latin <i>Diāna</i>, from a shared root whence may have also developed Latin <i>Iūnō, Iūnōnis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) rare and costly food</li></ol><p>Literally, “Zeus' brain”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doanas, possibly the river Irrawaddy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) Docetae, a group of people holding the heretical belief that Jesus was fully divine and that his humanity and sufferings were illusory</li></ol><p>From <i>δοκέω</i> (dokéō, “to seem, appear”) +&lrm; <i>-ταί</i> (-taí)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Dolius; in the <i>Odyssey</i> a slave of Penelope, father of Melanthius and Melantho</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>δόλιος</i> (dólios, “crafty, treacherous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any individual among the Dolonci, an ancient Thracian tribe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Domitius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Domitius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Domitian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Domitiānus</i>, logically adapted from Δομίτιος (Domítios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doriscus (a city in Thrace, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dorylaeum, Phrygia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dositheus</li></ol><p>From <i>δόσις</i> (dósis, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”). For the semantics, compare <i>Θεόδωρος</i> (Theódōros). Possibly a calque of Biblical Hebrew <i>נְתַנְאֵל</i> (“nəṯanʾēl”) borrowed as <i>Ναθαναήλ</i> (Nathanaḗl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ducetius (name of an ancient leader amongst the Sicel people)</li></ol><p>Presumably from Sicel. The name be derived from an Italic form of the shape <i>&#42;Douk-et-jos</i>, which is itself probably derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewk-</i> (“to lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Δούκᾱς</i> (Doúkās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Durostorum; Silistra, Bulgaria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a male surname</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Οἶκος τῶν Δουκῶν (9th–11th centuries), Byzantine Greek noble family whose branches provided several notable generals and rulers to the Byzantine Empire</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>Ἰωάννης Δούκας (<abbr title=floruit><i>fl.</i></abbr> 1155–1181), senior military commander and diplomat, <i>sebastos</i> and <i>megas hetaireiarches</i> under Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus (<abbr title=regnavit><i>r.</i></abbr> 1143–1180)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>Probably a cognominal use of the military rank <i>δούξ</i> (doúx), from Latin <i>dux</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dunax, a mountain of Thrace</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Duris, the name of a famous historian of Samos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dracanum, Ikaria, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dratae, Cappadocia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Drin, in Albania</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Drabescus, Thrace, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dragmus, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dromichaetes, a king of the Getae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dropici, a Persian nomadic tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a woodnymph, a dryad (a nymph whose life is bound up with that of her tree)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a tree</li></ol><p>From <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Drypetis, from Old Persian, a Persian princess and wife of Hephaestion</li></ol><p>From Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dreros, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dyrrhachium, Durrës (a city in Albania, earlier called Epidamnus )</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dodona (a town and religious shrine of ancient in Greece)</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek origin, therefore of unknown ultimate meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dorimachus</li></ol><p>From <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “releasing”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Dorian</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dorieus, a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>Δῶρος</i> (Dôros, “Dorus”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, “-ian”, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Doric, Dorian</li></ol><p>From <i>Δῶρος</i> (Dôros) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Dorothy</li></ol><p>From <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron) +&lrm; <i>θεά</i> (theá). Feminine equivalent of <i>Δωρόθεος</i> (Dōrótheos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dorotheus</li></ol><p>From <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós). Compare <i>Θεόδωρος</i> (Theódōros), which uses the same elements in the reverse order.</p><p>Masculine equivalent of <i>Δωροθέα</i> (Dōrothéa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doberes, a Paeonian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) Doberus (a city in Paeonia, in modern North Macedonia)</li><li>(<i>masculine</i>) Doberus (a mountain in Macedonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any individual among the Dolopes</li></ol><p>Unknown; probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Donusa, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doriscus, a location in Thrace</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dyme (a city in Achaea, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dystus, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dystrus, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to February</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dorium, Messenia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Daos</li></ol><p>From Phrygian?</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Datis, from Old Persian, an Achaemenid admiral who led Darius I's invasion of Greece</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Dātiyah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Deianira (third wife of Heracles)</li></ol><p>From <i>δήϊος</i> (dḗïos, “hostile, destroying”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man, husband”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă), referring to her killing of her husband, Heracles.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dorus</li></ol><p>From <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ilesium (a city in Boeotia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eileithyia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ilei, Argolis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iresiae, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Helot</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boeotian form of <i>Ἡσῐ́οδος</i> (Hēsĭ́odos, “Hesiod”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a helot</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ἁλίσκομαι</i> (halískomai, “to be captured, to be made prisoner”), but it has also been linked to <i>Ἕλος</i> (Hélos), a Laconian town whose inhabitants were enslaved.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Evadne</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euhemerus (philosopher)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐήμερος</i> (euḗmeros, “happy, prosperous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Evenor</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐήνωρ</i> (euḗnōr, “of good men”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Euaemon, son of Ormenus and father of Eurypylus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Evagoras</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́, “assembly”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Euboea; a Euboean</li></ol><p>Irregularly formed from <i>Εὔβο(ιᾰ)</i> (Eúbo(iă), “Euboea”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix forming demonyms). The twelfth-century <i>Etymologicum Magnum</i> criticises the omission of the <i>ι</i> (i, “iota”), prescribing the form <i>Εὐβοιεύς</i> (Euboieús), whilst acknowledging that such omission is common in terms formed on iota-terminal stems suffixed with <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Euboean</li></ol><p>From <i>Εὔβοια</i> (Eúboia) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eugenius; Eugene</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐγενής</i> (eugenḗs, “well-born”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix). Compare Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Esugenos</i>, Sanskrit <i>सुजन</i> (sujana).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eugenia</li></ol><p>Feminine form of Εὐγένῐος (Eugénĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Euthycles</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐθῠ́ς</i> (euthŭ́s, “straight”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Euthymenes</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Euthydemus</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐθῠ́ς</i> (euthŭ́s, “straight”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Euthyphro</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐθῠ́ς</i> (euthŭ́s, “straight, direct”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Euclid</li><li>in particular, the mathematician Euclid</li></ol><p>From <i>Εὐκλῆς</i> (Euklês) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eucles</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”). Possibly formed as long ago as Proto-Indo-European; compare Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hsućráwās</i>, Latin <i>Vesclevesis</i> and Sanskrit <i>वसुश्रवस्</i> (vasuśravas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔλαλος</i> (eúlalos, “sweetly speaking”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā), from <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) + <i>λάλος</i> (lálos, “talkative”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eumenes</li><li>a male given name, Eumenes, from Old Persian</li></ol><p><i>εὖ</i> (eû) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Haumanah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Eumenides; a Eumenis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Eumolpus; one of the Eumolpidae; a Eumolpid</li></ol><p>From <i>Εὔμολπος</i> (Eúmolpos, “Eumolpus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Eunice</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>νίκη</i> (níkē, “victory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eupalium, Locris, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eupithes, father of Antinous of Ithaca</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Euripidean (of or like Euripides)</li></ol><p>From <i>Εὐρῑπῐ́δης</i> (Eurīpĭ́dēs) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Euripides (ancient Athenian tragedian)</li></ol><p>From <i>Εὔρῑπος</i> (Eúrīpos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Euryale</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide”) +&lrm; <i>ἄλη</i> (álē, “wandering, roaming”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Eurybiades</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide, broad”) +&lrm; <i>βία</i> (bía, “force”) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Eurydice</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρῠ́ς</i> (eurŭ́s, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́κη</i> (dĭ́kē, “justice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eurycles</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρῠ́ς</i> (eurŭ́s, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eurycrates</li></ol><p><i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eurymedon</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρῠ́ς</i> (eurŭ́s, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>μέδων</i> (médōn, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurymenae, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρυμενής</i> (eurumenḗs, “broad and strong”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurynome, any of a number of figures in Greek mythology</li><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>νομός</i> (nomós, “pasture”), thus "having broad pastures".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Euryptolemus</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρῠ́ς</i> (eurŭ́s, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>πτόλεμος</i> (ptólemos, “war”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eurysaces</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρῠ́ς</i> (eurŭ́s, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>σᾰ́κος</i> (sắkos, “shield”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eurystheus</li></ol><p>Thought to be a contraction of <i>Εὐρυσθένης</i> (Eurusthénēs), from <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide, broad”) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength, might, power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Euryphaessa, the wife of Hyperion and mother of Helios, also called Theia</li></ol><p>First part is from <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide”). The second part is modeled after the adjectives in <i>-εις, -εσσα, -εν</i> (-eis, -essa, -en, “-ful”) and based on <i>φάος</i> (pháos, “light”), but the formation is atypical; the word is furthermore only attested twice in a single homeric hymn. According to Beekes, the word was formed this way for the sole sake of the dactylic meter.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>European</li></ol><p>From <i>Εὐρώπᾱ</i> (Eurṓpā, “Europe”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “belonging to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Euryalus</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>ἅλως</i> (hálōs, “threshing floor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Personalization of <i>εὐρύβατος</i> (eurúbatos, “wide-stepping”) (an epithet of Zeus), from <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide”) +&lrm; <i>βατός</i> (batós, “speeding, stepping”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euryelus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Euryclea or Eurycleia</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>in particular</i>: Eurycleia of Ithaca (the nurse of Odysseus, and faithful housekeeper in his palace)</li></ol></ol><p>A compound of <i>εὐρῠ́ς</i> (eurŭ́s, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia). Compare <i>Ἀντῐ́κλειᾰ</i> (Antĭ́kleiă) and <i>Μεγᾰ́κλειᾰ</i> (Megắkleiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eurylochus</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide, broad”) +&lrm; <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos, “ambush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>Εὐρύμαχε</i> (Eurúmakhe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eurymachus</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide, broad”) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide”) +&lrm; <i>πύλη</i> (púlē, “gate”), thus a bahuvrihi compound meaning ‘having wide gates’.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύτῑμος</i> (eurútīmos, “honored far and wide”), from <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide”) + <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Doric form of <i>Εὐρώπη</i> (Eurṓpē):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Europa (a Phoenician princess abducted to Crete by Zeus)</li><li>Europe (a continent)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Europa (several characters, most notably a Phoenician princess abducted to Crete by Zeus)</li><li>Europe (a continent located west of Asia and north of Africa)</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but either from:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythological characters</i>) Eurotas, a man in Greek mythology</li><li>Evrotas (a river in Laconia, Greece, said to be named after the man)</li><li>The external female genitalia.</li></ol><p>Perhaps derived from <i>εὐρώς</i> (eurṓs, “mold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eusebius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Euterpe, muse of music</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good, well”) +&lrm; <i>τέρπω</i> (térpō, “to please”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euphemia</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐφημῐ́ᾱ</i> (euphēmĭ́ā, “use of words of good omen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Euphratas, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Hufratah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euphrates, a river in Asia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎢𐎳𐎼𐎠𐎬𐎢</i> (u-f-r-a-tu-u /⁠ʰUfrātuš⁠/), from Akkadian <i>𒀀𒇉𒌓𒄒𒉣</i> (<sup>ÍD<</sup>pu-rat-tu), itself from Sumerian <i>𒀀𒇉𒌓𒄒𒉣</i> (<sup>ÍD</sup>Buranun). Cognates include Hebrew <i>פְּרָת</i> (pərāṯ) and Elamite <i>𒌑𒅁𒊏𒌅𒅖</i> (ú-ip-ra-du-iš).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Euchenor, a Corinthian slain by Paris</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖχος</i> (eûkhos, “glory”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eve (<i>wife of Adam</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>חַוָּה</i> (khavá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Evander</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euboea</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>βο-</i> (bo-, “cow”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia, “country of”), literally “good cow country”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eudemus</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eudorus, son of Hermes and one of the five commanders of Achilles</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Evenus, the name of more than one figure in Greek mythology and history</li><li>the river Evenus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Evippus, a Lycian slain by Patroclus</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔιππος</i> (eúippos, “delighting in horses”), from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>Εὔμαιε</i> (Eúmaie)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Eumaeus</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the swineherd of Odysseus</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek, proposals include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eumelus, son of Admetus and Alcestis</li><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eumelus</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, good) +&lrm; <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “sheep, goat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eumolpus</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) +&lrm; <i>μολπή</i> (molpḗ, “song, dance”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Πόντος Εὔξεινος</i> (Póntos Eúxeinos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eupolis, an Athenian poet of the classical age.</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euripus Strait</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔρῑπος</i> (eúrīpos, “any strait or narrow sea, where the flux and reflux is violent”), from <i>ῥιπή</i> (rhipḗ, “swing or force with which anything is thrown”), from <i>ῥίπτω</i> (rhíptō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurytus</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) and/or <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to draw, pull”) + adjectival suffix <i>-τος</i> (-tos), as in "drawer of the bow."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Euphorbus, a figure in the Iliad</li></ol><p>Proper use of <i>εὔφορβος</i> (eúphorbos, “well fed”), from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>φορβή</i> (phorbḗ, “fodder”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Zab</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zancle (a city in Sicily; modern Messina)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Thucydides states that the city was named for the Sicel word <i>ζάγκλον</i> (zánklon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zakynthos, Zacynthus, Zante (an island of the Ionian Sea, Greece)</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek (assumed Pelasgian dialect), but Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;zákuntʰos</i> can be inferred.</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀼𐀓𐀯𐀍</i> (za-ku-si-jo).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zalmoxis, a divinity among the Getae</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Dacian.</p><p>Compare <i>ѕѣло</i> (dzělo, “very”) + <i>мощь</i> (moštĭ, “power, ability”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Doric form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zarax, Laconia, Greece</li><li>Mount Zarax, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Doric form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zeleia, Troad, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zephyrus, the west wind</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀽𐁆𐀫</i> (ze-pu2-ro), pointing to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;Dzépʰuros</i>. Probably related to <i>ζόφος</i> (zóphos, “darkness, west”), both occasionally connected to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(h₃)yebʰ-</i> (“to cover &gt; to copulate, to set (of the sun)”) (whence <i>οἴφω</i> (oíphō)), though either the reflex of the laryngeal in <i>οἴφω</i> (oíphō) or its loss from <i>ζεφ-</i> (zeph-) would be unexplained. More likely Pre-Greek in view of the suffix <i>-υρ-</i> (-ur-) and distant semantics, though Beekes is unsure.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) A cape of East Africa referred to by Ptolemy (Ptol. Geog.4.7.11), see w:Zengisa Acra</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Somali <i>sanka</i> (“the nose”). The cape was probably Kizingitini (-zingitini matches the phonetics, ki- is a common prefix). It seems we are looking at an ancient name that has been remoulded (kizingiti) to look Bantu. map</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) poetic form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dyḗm</i>, accusative case of <i>&#42;dyḗws</i>, which gives <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús). Cognate with Latin <i>diem</i> (whence fabricated nominative <i>diēs</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Zeno</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dzéus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dyḗws</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zerynthus, Thrace, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) alternative form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Zabergan, chieftain of the Kutrigurs</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Oghur, itself from Middle Persian <i>𐭦𐭯𐭫𐭪𐭠𐭭</i> (zplkʾn), ultimately from Old Persian <i>&#42;Zabrakānah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Zaberganes, a Sasaanian officer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle Persian <i>𐭦𐭯𐭫𐭪𐭠𐭭</i> (zplkʾn), ultimately from Old Persian <i>&#42;Zabrakānah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zebulun</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>זְבוּלוּן</i> (Zəḇūlū́n).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Zabrican</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle Persian <i>𐭦𐭯𐭫𐭪𐭠𐭭</i> (zplkʾn), ultimately from Old Persian <i>&#42;Zabrakānah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zacatae, a tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to Zakynthos</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person from Zakynthos</li></ol><p>From <i>Ζᾰ́κῠνθος</i> (Zắkŭnthos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zaliscus, a river of Galatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Jamasp, from Old Persian, a Sasanian king</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Jāmāspah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Zapatas, now the Great Zab, a tributary river of the Tigris</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zerah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>זֶרַח</i> (zérakh).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Zariaspes, from Old Median</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Zaryāspah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zechariah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>זְכַרְיָה</i> (zəḵaryā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zaueces, a Libyan tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) Zibelthiurdos, a Thracian storm-god</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Zbelthurdos</i> (“lightning holder, lightning thrower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zebedee, the father of Jesus' disciples: James and John.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>זַבְדִי</i>, an alteration of <i>זַבְדִּי</i> (“my gift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A non-standard rendition of <i>Sabaoth</i>, a Jewish epithet of the God of Israel, historically used in magical papyri.</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Hebrew <i>צְבָאוֹת</i> (ṣəḇāʾōṯ, literally “armies”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Zeionises, from Scythian, an Indo-Scythian king</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka. See parallel borrowing Gandhari <i>𐨗𐨁𐨱𐨆𐨞𐨁𐨐</i> (jihoṇika).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zilpah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>זִלְפָּה</i> (Zilpā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Zeuacus, from Proto-Sarmatian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>father of Nauarthacus</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;J̌īwag</i>. Compare Old Persian <i>&#42;Jīvakah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zephyrium, the name of various cities in Anatolia</li></ol><p>From <i>Ζέφυρος</i> (Zéphuros, “Zephyrus”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zeuxippe</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεύγνῡμῐ</i> (zeúgnūmĭ, “to yoke”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē): "she who yokes horses".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Zeuxippus</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεύξιππος</i> (zeúxippos, “desultor, junctor”), from <i>ζεύγνῡμῐ</i> (zeúgnūmĭ, “to yoke”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Zeus</li><li>a name of the emperors of Rome</li><li>referring to other gods, such as Amun, Baal, Ahura Mazda</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with <i>καταχθόνιος</i> (katakhthónios) or <i>χθόνιος</i> (khthónios)) = Hades</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;Dzéus</i> (whence Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀇𐀺</i> (di-wo /⁠diwos⁠/)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dyḗws</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>द्यु</i> (dyú), Latin <i>Iovis</i> (and <i>&#42;Iu-</i> in <i>Iuppiter</i>), Old English <i>Tīw</i>, Hittite <i>𒅆𒍑</i> (sius).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zecas, envoy of Ardashir I to Artabanes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Zenobia</li></ol><p>From <i>Ζήν</i> (Zḗn, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>βῐ́ᾱ</i> (bĭ́ā, “power”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Zenobius</li></ol><p>From <i>Ζήν</i> (Zḗn, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>βῐ́ος</i> (bĭ́os, “life”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Zenodorus</li></ol><p><i>Ζήν</i> (Zḗn, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os): perhaps “gift of Zeus”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>rare form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zinchi or Zicchi, an ancient tribe of Sarmatia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ziselmius, from Thracian</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Ziselmis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zoara (a town in Judaea, modern-day Jordan, situated at the southern end of the Dead Sea)</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>צוֹעַר</i> (ṣôʿar).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zoilus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) <i>a male surname, famously held by:</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Ἰωάννης Ζωναρᾶς (<i>fl.</i> 12th C.), Byzantine Constantinopolitan chronicler and theologian</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zarathustra; Zoroaster</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Avestan <i>𐬰𐬀𐬭𐬀𐬚𐬎𐬱𐬙𐬭𐬀</i> (zaraθuštra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Zoltes, from Thracian, the name of a Thracian king</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Zoltēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zopyrus</li></ol><p>From <i>ζώπῠρος</i> (zṓpŭros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zosimos, Zosimus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zethus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 100 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. An abbreviation of ἑκατόν (hekatón, “hundred”). Equivalent to the Roman numeral C.</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤇</i> (ḥ, “ḥēt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Θεέ</i> (Theé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Θεοτόκε</i> (Theotóke)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Θεοτόκον</i> (Theotókon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Θεοτόκῳ</i> (Theotókōi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Θεόν</i> (Theón)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Θεός</i> (Theós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Θεοῦ</i> (Theoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Θεῷ</i> (Theōî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Thalia, muse of comedy and idyllic poetry.</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Thalia, one of the three Charites (Graces).</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́λειᾰ</i> (thắleiă), from the verb <i>θάλλω</i> (thállō, “to thrive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Thalpius, son of Eurytus and one of the suitors of Helen</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tamar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>תָּמָר</i> (tamár).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Thamyris or Thamyras</li></ol><p>Of Thracian origin. Possibly also related to the river <i>Tamyras</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tharybis, a man mentioned by Aeschylus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thasos, North Aegean Islands, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thapsacus; Tiphsah</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Thecla</li></ol><p>Perhaps a vernacular feminine form of <i>Θεοκλῆς</i> (Theoklês, literally “God’s fame”); or, since the first saint with this name was from Iconium, perhaps of Luwic origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Theognis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Thermos (the ancient capital and sanctuary in Aetolia, Greece).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thespis</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) and <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon, “to said”) (aorist active form of <i>λέγω</i> (légō)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thestius</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) and <i>ἐσθίειν</i> (esthíein, “to eat”), (see <i>ἐσθίω</i> (esthíō, “to eat”)) whereas he feeds the gods.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thestor</li></ol><p>From <i>θέσσασθαι</i> (théssasthai, “to pray for”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thetis</li></ol><p>Possibly a variation of <i>τήθη</i> (tḗthē, “grandmother”) / <i>τηθίς</i> (tēthís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>Θῆβαι</i> (Thêbai, “Thebes (city in Boeotia)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Theron</li></ol><p>Can be connected to Latin <i>torus</i> (“swelling, bulge, knot”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sterh₃-</i> (“to spread, extend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thigrus, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thimbron</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thisbae, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thisbe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Thais</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tabor (a large hill of Biblical significance in Lower Galilee, Northern Israel at the eastern end of the Jezreel Valley)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thaddaeus, one of the Apostles</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>תַדַּי</i> (Ṯaday), of uncertain origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thalamae, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) Tiamat</li></ol><p><i>θάλαττα</i> (thálatta), a variant of <i>θάλασσα</i> (thálassa, “sea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Thales</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>θᾰ́λλω</i> (thắllō, “to thrive”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs), in which case “one who thrives”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thamanaei, an ancient Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>Θᾰ́μῠρῐς</i> (Thắmŭrĭs, “Thamyris; Thamyras”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thargelion: the eleventh month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around May and June</li></ol><p>From <i>Θαργήλῐᾰ</i> (Thargḗlĭă), a festival dedicated to Apollo and Artemis held at Athens.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thaumacia, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Thapsacus or Tiphsah; a Thapsacene</li></ol><p>From <i>Θάψᾰκος</i> (Thápsăkos, “Thapsacus; Tiphsah”) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Thaumas</li></ol><p><i>θαῦμα</i> (thaûma, “wonder, marvel”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās, “male name suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theaetetus</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>αἰτητός</i> (aitētós, “asked, required”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Theano, wife of Antenor</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tel Aviv</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>תֵּל אָבִיב</i> (Tel 'Avív)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Themiscyra, Pontus, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Themistius— famously held by&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Θεμίστιος Εὐφραδής (AD 317–388), Roman statesman, rhetorician, and philosopher</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>θεμῐστός</i> (themĭstós, “oracular”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Themistocles</li><li>the Athenian general Themistocles</li></ol><p>From <i>θεμῐστός</i> (themĭstós, “oracular”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “glory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thenae, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theogenes</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós) +&lrm; <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Theodosia</li><li>Theodosia (an ancient city in Crimea; modern Feodosia)</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>δοτός</i> (dotós, “given”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), literally meaning “given by god”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Theodosiopolis</li></ol><p>From <i>Θεοδοσῐ́ου</i> (Theodosĭ́ou, “of Theodosius”, genitive) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theodosius</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>δόσις</i> (dósis, “a giving”), literally “given by God”. Compare Classical Sanskrit <i>भगदत्त</i> (bhagadatta, literally “given by Bhaga”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) a female given name, equivalent to English Theodora</li></ol><p>Female form of <i>Θεόδωρος</i> (Theódōros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Theoclymenus</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>κλῠ́μενος</i> (klŭ́menos, “famous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Theonoe</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) A hymn to Mary, the Theotokos, read or chanted during the Divine Services of the Eastern Orthodox and Eastern Catholic churches.</li></ol><p>From <i>Θεοτόκος</i> (Theotókos, “God-bearer/birther; Mother of God; Holy Mother of God;”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, “A noun-forming diminutive suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theophanes</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs, “seeming, appearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Therapne, Therapnae, Therapnes (a city in Laconia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Θερᾰ́πναι</i> (Therắpnai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Thermaean</li></ol><p><i>Θέρμη</i> (Thérmē, “Therma”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Thermaic Gulf; the Macedonian Gulf; the Gulf of Thessaloniki</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thermopylae (a narrow mountain pass in east-central Greece, site of a famous ancient battle)</li></ol><p>From <i>θερμο-</i> (thermo-, “warm, hot”) +&lrm; <i>πῠ́λη</i> (pŭ́lē, “gates”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thermodon, Terme (a river in Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name: <i>Thersites</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Thesmophoria</li></ol><p>From <i>θεσμός</i> (thesmós, “law”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “bearing”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thespiae (a city in Boeotia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Thespiae; a Thespian</li></ol><p>From <i>Θεσπῐαί</i> (Thespĭaí, “Thespiae”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thesprotis; Thesprotia, Epirus, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Θεσπρωτός</i> (Thesprōtós, “Thesprotian”) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Thesprotia; a Thesprotian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a historical region in north-central Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Θεσσᾰλός</i> (Thessălós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female inhabitant of Thessaly; a female Thessalian</li></ol><p>From <i>Θεσσᾰλός</i> (Thessălós) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Thessalian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in the phrase <i>Θεσσᾰλῐκὸν ἕδος</i>) denoting a sort of chair or couch</li></ol><li>of the Thessalian dialect</li></ol><p><i>Θεσσᾰλός</i> (Thessălós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thessaloniki (a city in the region of Macedonia, Greece)</li><li>Thessalonica, daughter of Philip II, half-sister of Alexander the Great, and wife of Cassander, all three of whom were kings of Macedonia at one point.</li></ol><p>From <i>Θεσσᾰλός</i> (Thessălós, “Thessalian”) +&lrm; <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”). The woman was named by her father, Philip II, in honor of his military victory in Thessaly, and later the city was named after her by Cassander, her husband.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Thessalonian</li></ol><p>From <i>Θεσσᾰλονῑ́κη</i> (Thessălonī́kē, “Thessaloniki”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Thessalus</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Thessalian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Thessaly; a Thessalian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰeťťalós</i>, likely from Pre-Greek. But compare <i>&#42;kʷoiwéō</i> (“to make, produce, invent”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷoywós</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷey-</i> (“pile, stow, to gather”). The variation of the initial consonant between <i>Θ</i> (Th) and <i>Φ</i> (Ph) in different dialects is the reason for reconstructing a labiovelar <i>&#42;kʷʰ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thessyrius, a river of Asian Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a daughter of Thestius; a Thestiad</li></ol><p>From <i>Θέστῐος</i> (Théstĭos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Gothic, equivalent to Latin Theodericus or English Theoderic, Theodoric, or Derek; variant form Θεοδώριχος (Theodṓrikhos)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Flāvius Theodericus Magnus (Theoderic the Great) (c. 454–526 <small>CE</small>), King of the Ostrogoths (from 475), of the Ostrogothic Kingdom of Italy (from 493), and of the Visigoths (from 511)</li><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Theodoric I (c. 390–451 <small>CE</small>), King of the Visigothic Kingdom from 418 to 451</li><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Theodoric II (c. 426–466 <small>CE</small>), King of the Visigothic Kingdom from 453 to 466</li></ol></ol><p>Via Late Latin <i>Theodericus</i> from a Germanic origin, likely via Gothic <i>&#42;𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃</i> (&#42;þiudareiks) from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Þeudōrīks</i>, from <i>&#42;þeudō</i> (“people”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;rīks</i> (“king”). Etymologically unrelated to Ancient Greek <i>Θεόδωρος</i> (Theódōros, “Theodore”, literally “gift of God”), although medieval conflation of the Germanic and Hellenic names may have given rise to Byzantine Greek <i>Θεοδώριχος</i> (Theodṓrikhos, “Theodoric”), Medieval Latin <i>Theodōrīchus</i> (“Theodoric”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>Θεό</i> (Theó, “God”) +&lrm; <i>δοῦλος</i> (doûlos, “slave”), literally “Slave of God”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theodore</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theocritus</li><li>in particular, the 3rd century <small>BCE</small> poet Theocritus</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>κρίνω</i> (krínō, “to judge”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theombrotus</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>βροτός</i> (brotós, “mortal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theomnestus</li></ol><p><i>θεός</i> (theós) +&lrm; <i>μιμνήσκω</i> (mimnḗskō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theopompus</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō, “to send”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os); literally "godsent".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theophilus</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós) +&lrm; <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Theophobos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Theophrastus</li><li>in particular, the 3rd century <small>BCE</small> philosopher Theophrastus</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós) +&lrm; <i>φράζω</i> (phrázō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thoth, an Egyptian god, or godlike human, who reportedly discovered many sciences and invented writing.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>ḏḥwtj</i> (“Thoth”). Doublet of <i>Θωΰθ</i> (Thōǘth).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Theban</li></ol><p>From <i>Θῆβαι</i> (Thêbai, “Thebes”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Themacus, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Theramenes</li><li>the fifth century <small>BCE</small> Athenian statesman Theramenes</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>θήρα</i> (thḗra, “the hunt”) +&lrm; <i>μένω</i> (ménō, “to stay”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Therasia, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Thericles</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>θήρα</i> (thḗra, “the hunt”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “famous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Theseus</li></ol><p>From <i>Θησεύς</i> (Thēseús) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Theseus</li></ol><p>Related to words describing law and institutions, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put”). See <i>θεσμός</i> (thesmós, “institution”) and <i>θέσις</i> (thésis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thorae, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thoricus, a city of Attica</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Thucydides</li><li>in particular, the fifth century BC historian Thucydides</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “ruler, god”) +&lrm; <i>κῦδος</i> (kûdos, “glory”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thutmose</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>ḏḥwtj-ms</i>, <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./4aeaa2ba.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="G26" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./cc95fca4.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="ms" alt="glyph" height="92"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thule (legendary island in the far North)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thurii (an ancient city in Magna Graecia, near modern Sibari, Calabria, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Thurii; a Thurian</li></ol><p>From Θούρῐοι (Thoúrĭoi, “Thurii”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Thrasyllus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thria (a town in Attica, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Thrasymedes, the eldest son of Nestor and king of Pylus</li></ol><p>From <i>θρασύς</i> (thrasús, “bold, courageous”) +&lrm; <i>μήδεα</i> (mḗdea, “counsel, plan”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Thrasybulus</li><li>in particular, the Athenian general Thrasybulus</li></ol><p>From <i>θρᾰσύς</i> (thrăsús) +&lrm; <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Thrasymachus</li></ol><p><i>θρᾰσῠ́ς</i> (thrăsŭ́s, “bold”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os): perhaps “bold in battle” or “bold fighter”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Thrasymelus, charioteer of Sarpedon</li></ol><p>From <i>θρᾰσῠ́ς</i> (thrăsŭ́s, “bold”) +&lrm; <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple; sheep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Thrasian Plain</li></ol><p>From <i>Θρῐ́ᾱ</i> (Thrĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thronium (a town in Locris, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thrace (a historical region of Southeast Europe, now divided between Greece, Bulgaria and Turkey)</li></ol><p>From <i>Θρᾷξ</i> (Thrāîx, “Thracian”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thrax</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Thrace; a Thracian</li></ol><p>From the base of <i>θράσσω</i>, alternate form of <i>ταράσσω</i> (tarássō, “to trouble, stir”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰreh₂gʰ-</i> (“to confuse, agitate, disturb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female inhabitant of Thrace; Thracian woman</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>Θρᾷξ</i> (Thrāîx, “Thracian”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>Θρᾷσσᾰ</i> (Thrāîssă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Poetic form of <i>Θρᾴκη</i> (Thrāíkē, “Thrace”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) the Lamentations of Jeremiah (book of the Old Testament)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tragic and Epic form of <i>Θρᾷξ</i> (Thrāîx)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tragic and Epic form of <i>Θρᾷξ</i> (Thrāîx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tragic form of <i>Θρᾷσσᾰ</i> (Thrāîssă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thyatira; Akhisar, Turkey</li></ol><p>From the Lydian for "castle of Thya," from the name <i>𐤣𐤧𐤠</i> (dya). Stephanus of Byzantium thought it to be from <i>θυγάτηρ</i> (thugátēr, “daughter”), though this has been dismissed as folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thyestes</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀶𐀸𐀲</i> (tu-we-ta)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thynia</li></ol><p><i>Θῡνός</i> (Thūnós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Thyni</li><li>an inhabitant of Thynia; a Thynian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thyrea (the ancient capital of Cynuria district, Peloponnesus, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thyssagetae, an ancient tribe of Scythia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thoth, the first month of the Coptic calendar</li><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Thoth (an Egyptian god)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Coptic <i>ⲑⲱⲟⲩⲧ</i> (thōout), <i>ⲑⲟⲟⲩⲧ</i> (thoout). Doublet of <i>Θεύθ</i> (Theúth).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thomas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>תאומא</i> (“Thomas”), from <i>תאמא</i>&nbsp;m (“twin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, <i>Thoas</i>, having a rough meaning of "dasher" or "racer".</li></ol><p>Deverbal proper noun from <i>θέω</i> (théō, “to dash, to race; to fly”) +&lrm; <i>-ας</i> (-as, a nominalizing suffix), with expected o-grade monophthongization of the verbal stem. Compare θοός.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Thoön, a Trojan slain by Odysseus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Thyamis, Epirus, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thymbrara, Lydia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thymbrium, Phrygia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thymena, Paphlagonia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thyssus, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Θωΰθ</i> (Thōǘth, “Thoth, an Egyptian god”) <i>also in human vessel form of V. R.</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thebes, the name of a city in Boeotia</li><li>Thebes, the name of a city in Upper Egypt (including today's <i>Karnak</i> and <i>Luxor</i>)</li></ol><p>From Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀳𐀣</i> (te-qa /⁠tʰēgʷā⁠/), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰēgʷā</i>.</p><p>From Egyptian Demotic Egyptian <i>tꜣ-jpy</i> (“Thebes”, literally “the temple”), from earlier Egyptian <i>tꜣ</i> (“the”) + <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">p&#58;t</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./a9209ce7.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="O45" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (jpt, “Luxor”, literally “inner sanctum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>culture</i>) alternative form of <i>Θῖναι</i> (Thînai): a people of East Asia usually identified as the southern Chinese; their homeland, China</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but probably from Sanskrit <i>चीन</i> (Cīna, “China”), possibly via Arabic صِين (Ṣīn, “China; the Chinese”) and usually held to derive from Old Chinese <i>秦</i> (&#42;zin, “Qin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>culture</i>) synonym of <i>Σῖναι</i> (Sînai): A people of East Asia usually identified as the southern Chinese; their homeland, China; their chief city.</li></ol><p>From <i>Θῖνα</i> (Thîna) + <i>-αι</i> (-ai, “<i>fem. pl.</i>”), of uncertain etymology, but probably from Sanskrit <i>चीन</i> (Cīna, “China”), possibly via Arabic صِين (Ṣīn, “China; the Chinese”) and usually held to derive from Old Chinese <i>秦</i> (&#42;zin, “Qin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thonis, Egypt</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>tꜣ-ḥnt</i>. Compare Coptic <i>ⲧϩⲱⲛⲓ</i> (thōni).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 1 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. A tally mark equivalent to the Roman digit I.</li><li>(<i>money</i>) A tally for obols.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Ἰησοῦς</i> (Iēsoûs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Ἰσραήλ</i> (Israḗl)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Ἰησοῦν</i> (Iēsoûn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Ἰησοῦς</i> (Iēsoûs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰησοῦ</i> (Iēsoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰησοῦν</i> (Iēsoûn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰησοῦς</i> (Iēsoûs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Ἰησοῦ</i> (Iēsoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Ἱερουσαλήμ</i> (Hierousalḗm)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Ἰησοῦν</i> (Iēsoûn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Ἰησοῦς</i> (Iēsoûs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i><b>Ἰ</b>ησοῦ<b>ς</b> <b>Χ</b>ριστό<b>ς</b></i> (<b>I</b>ēsoû<b>s</b> <b>Kh</b>ristó<b>s</b>, “Jesus Christ”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Ἰησοῦ</i> (Iēsoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>acronym of <i><b>Ἰ</b>ησοῦς <b>Χ</b>ριστός <b>Θ</b>εοῦ <b>Υ</b>ἱός <b>Σ</b>ωτήρ</i> (<b>I</b>ēsoûs <b>Kh</b>ristós <b>Th</b>eoû <b>U</b>hiós <b>S</b>ōtḗr, “Jesus Christ, Son of God, Savior”)</li><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Yahweh; the Tetragrammaton YHWH</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>יהוה</i> (YHWH), altered to be composed of all vowels of the Greek alphabet.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>κύριον</i> (kúrion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>κῡ́ριος</i> (kū́rios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>κῡρίου</i> (kūríou)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>κυρίῳ</i> (kuríōi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Cabeiri, a group of enigmatic chthonic deities worshipped especially in Samothrace, Lemnos and Boeotia</li></ol><p>Unknown, though unlikely to be related to Sanskrit <i>कुबेर</i> (kubera, “god of riches and treasure”). The root of the name has been suggested to be the same as that in <i>Κάβαρνοι</i> (Kábarnoi, “priests of Demeter”), indicating a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cadmus</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek origin, possibly Semitic (often thought to be Phoenician for “man from the east”), from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;q-d-m</i> (“to precede, come before”), with influence from <i>κέκασμαι</i> (kékasmai, “to have excelled, shined”). Compare Classical Syriac <i>ܩܕܡ</i> (“to come before”), Arabic قدم, Hebrew <i>קדם</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cadi, Phrygia; now Gediz, in Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>rare spelling of <i>Κάϊος</i> (Káïos) <i>found in epigraphies</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calpe; Gibraltar</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Calchas</li></ol><p>Liddell &amp; Scott, J.B. Hoffman: from Κάλχη, murex purple, here "dark," sense "ponderer," because people that are pondering look melancholic (blue). Diviners were known for their pondering. Not traceable to an Indo-European root, thus hypothetically borrowed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Camirus; Kameiros, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Camillus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Camillus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cannae; Canne della Battaglia, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cantabri, an Iberian tribe settled in Hispania Tarraconensis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cantabrī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canopus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Capys, the mythological king of Dardania and father of Anchises</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Car or Kar, the legendary founder of Caria</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Caria; a Carian</li></ol><p>From an Anatolian language, possibly Carian or Hittite <i>𒌷𒋼𒀀𒆠𒅀</i> (<sup>URU</sup>kar-ki-ya /⁠Karkiya⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the Carbo family</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Carbō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caresus, a river of Troas mentioned in the Iliad</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to ancient Caria; Carian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(epithet,&#32;of Zeus) the Carian equivalent of</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>Καρία</i> (Karía, “Caria”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carcinus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carmel, a mountain range in Israel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>כַּרְמֶל</i> (karmél, “fertile land”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carnea (a festival and a month in honour of Apollo Karneios in the Peloponnese and in the Dorian states of Magna Graecia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Καρνεῖος</i> (Karneîos), variant spelling <i>Κάρνειος</i> (Kárneios), an adjectival form of <i>Κάρνος</i> (Kárnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carussa, Paphlagonia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Karpathos, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carpis, a still unidentified tributary of the Danube</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carrhae; Harran, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carystus, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Servilius Casca</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Casca</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kassos, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Caspian</li><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) Caspian (member of the ancient people dwelling along the southern and southwestern shores of the Caspian Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cassander</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cassius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cassius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Castabus, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Catulus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Catulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cato</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Catō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>קַיִן</i> (Qayin).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Caius</i>, a variant of <i>Gaius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cayster river; Küçük Menderes, Turkey</li></ol><p>Probably a Luwian name. Folk etymology (possibly Strabo) described Cayster as the son of Achilles and Penthesilea, with possible influence from <i>κύκνος</i> (kúknos, “swan”), of which many were found in its valleys.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cebes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cethegus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cecrops</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Celydnus, Epirus, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cerberus, the three-headed dog which guards the entrance to Hades</li></ol><p>Disputed. Suggested to be cognate with Sanskrit <i>शबल</i> (śabala), <i>कर्बुर</i> (karbura, “speckled, spotted”), from Late Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerbero-</i> (“dappled, spotted”) (perhaps compare <i>Spot</i> as a popular dog's name in English), but—according to Beekes—this equation is phonetically problematic. Instead, Beekes opts for a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A nymph, daughter of Asopus and the river nymph Metope.</li><li>Corcyra; Corfu, Ionian Islands, Greece</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀒𐀫𐀓𐀨𐀂𐀍</i> (ko-ro-ku-ra-i-jo /⁠korkuraijoi⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cersus, a river of Cilicia mentioned by Xenophon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ceos; Kea, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol><p>Solmsen suggests derivation from unattested <i>&#42;κῆϝος</i> (&#42;kêwos, “fire”), from the same root of <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “to burn”), which must be wrong as it is a Pre-Greek name. Fick compares this word with other placenames like <i>Τέως</i> (Téōs) and <i>Κόως</i> (Kóōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cibyra, Phrygia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ciconians, a Thracian people dwelling under Mount Ismarus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Cilician</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek, likely Anatolian. Compare with Hittite <i>𒌷𒄭𒇷𒅅𒋡</i> (<sup>URU</sup>ḫi-li-ik-ka₄ /⁠Ḫilika⁠/), and Luwian <i>𔗒𔔹𔗧𔔂</i> (hi-li-ka<sup>URBS</sup> /⁠Ḫilikka⁠/) and <i>𔗒𔖱𔓯𔗧𔔂</i> (hi+ra/i-i-ka<sup>URBS</sup> /⁠Ḫirikka⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cilician woman</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name meaning “Cilician woman”</li></ol><p>From <i>Κῐ́λῐξ</i> (Kĭ́lĭx, “Cilician”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cilla, Troas, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an individual member of the Cimbri; a Cimbrian</li></ol><p>Of Proto-Indo-European origin, but whether the name came through Celtic or Germanic is uncertain; possibly from <i>&#42;tḱim-ro-</i> (“inhabitant”), from <i>&#42;tḱoi-m-</i> (“home”), a derivation of <i>&#42;tḱey-</i> (“to settle, to live”). Probably from the same ultimate source as Himmerland, a peninsula in Jutland, Denmark, the region where the people are thought to originate. Perhaps related to Κιμμέριος (Kimmérios, “Cimmerian”). Not etymologically related to Welsh <i>Cymry</i>. More at Cimbri.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kimolos, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name Cimon</li><li>in particular, the 5th century <small>BCE</small> Athenian statesman Cimon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cinarus; Kinaros, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cinna; any individual among the Roman Cinna family</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cinna</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cinolis, Paphlagonia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cius; Gemlik, Turkey</li><li>A river flowing near this city</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Circe, a name, particularly the sorceress who appears in Homer's <i>Odyssey</i>.</li></ol><p>From <i>κίρκος</i> (kírkos, “falcon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cirta; Constantine, Algeria</li></ol><p>From Punic <i>𐤊𐤓𐤕𐤍</i> (krtn), perhaps from an older Berber placename.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cisamus; Kissamos, Crete</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Citium; Larnaca, Cyprus</li></ol><p><div>From Phoenician <i>𐤊𐤕</i> (kt), <i>𐤊𐤕𐤉</i> (kty) and connected to Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">k&#58;A&#42;Z1</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./3edf0576.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="TA" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Z1&#58;H&#95;SPACE</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">N17&#58;Z1&#42;N21</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./a14a7564.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="xAst" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (kꜣṯꜣj), <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./cb5476b2.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="k" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./3edf0576.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="TA" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Z1&#58;H&#95;SPACE</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">N17&#58;Z1&#42;N21</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f80658a3.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="N18" alt="glyph" height="20"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (kꜣṯꜣj); connected by <i>Josephus</i> to Hebrew <i>כתים</i> (Kittim), used in Hebrew to refer to Cyprus. <i>Kittim</i> could be from Akkadian for "invaders" or could instead be related to <i>Khatti</i>, <i>Hatti</i>.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cabalii, an ancient tribe of Anatolia mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cabyle, Thrace, Bulgaria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cadmean</li></ol><p><i>Κάδμος</i> (Kádmos) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Name of one of the Cabeiri in Samothrace, son of Kabeiro and Hephaistos.</li><li>Name of a character identified with Hermes.</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek, potentially identical to Hattic <i>𒁹𒄩𒊭𒄠𒈪𒇷</i> (<sup>m</sup>ḫa-ša-am-mi-li /⁠Ḫašammili⁠/), from <i>*Hatˢmilʸ</i>, and potentially related to <i>Κάδμος</i> (Kádmos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cadusii, an ancient Iranian people settled near the Caspian Sea</li></ol><p>Likely of Iranian origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) The nurse of Aeneas.</li><li>Gaeta (a town and harbour in Latium, Italy)</li></ol><p>From <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “to burn”) or <i>κοῖλος</i> (koîlos, “hollow”), referring to the funeral mound that Aeneas raises to his nurse.</p><p>According to Strabo, it was a Samian town, from the word <i>καιέτα</i> (kaiéta, “hollow, cavity”). However, compare the Roman settlement <i>Caiatia</i> (modern Italian <i>Caiazzo</i>) in Caserta. Nevertheless, it could still be related to the name of Aeneas' nurse (see above).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lucius Caecilius Iucundus</li><li>Any individual named Caecilius or Cecilius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Caecilius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caecina</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Caecīna</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual named Caecilia or Cecilia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Caecilia</i>; feminine form of Καικίλιος (Kaikílios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caepio</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Caepiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caesarea (any of numerous cities in the Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>Named after a Caesar: <i>Καῖσᾰρ</i> (Kaîsăr, “Caesar”) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă), as a calque of Latin <i>Caesarēa</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caesaraugusta; Zaragoza, Spain</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Caesaraugusta</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Caesarean; of Caesarea</li></ol><p>From <i>Καισᾰ́ρειᾰ</i> (Kaisắreiă, “Caesarea”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Calesius, the charioteer of Axylus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calingae, an Indian tribe whose capital was Parthalis</li></ol><p>Likely from Sanskrit <i>कलिङ्ग</i> (kaliṅga, “a region of ancient India”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calabria</li></ol><p>Unknown. Suggestions include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calvinus; Calvin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Calvīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caledonia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Calēdonia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Caledonia; a Caledonian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Calēdonius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caligula</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Caligula</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calliarus (a town in Locris, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Callimachus</li><li>in particular, the Hellenistic scholar and poet Callimachus</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰλλῐ́μᾰχος</i> (kăllĭ́măkhos, “fighting nobly”), from <i>κάλλος</i> (kállos, “beauty, nobility”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, masculine nominal ending).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Callixenus</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰ́λλος</i> (kắllos, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “foreign”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Callipolis (an ancient town in Aetolia, Greece)</li><li>Callipolis (an ancient town in Caria, Turkey)</li><li>Callipolis (an ancient town in Mysia, Turkey)</li><li>Callipolis (a peninsula in Thrace, Turkey)</li><li>Callipolis (a port town in Apulia, Italy)</li><li>Callipolis (a port town on the Hellespont, modern Gelibolu Turkey)</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) Kallipolis (utopian city state ruled by philosopher kings as theorised by Plato in the <i>Republic</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κάλλος</i> (kállos, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Callistratus</li></ol><p><i>κάλλος</i> (kállos, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>στρατός</i> (stratós, “army”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Callias</li></ol><p>From <i>Καλλίας</i> (Kallías) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Kallicles</li></ol><p>From <i>κάλλος</i> (kállos, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>κάλλος</i> (kállos, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Callicratidas</li></ol><p>From <i>Κᾰλλῐκρᾰ́της</i> (Kăllĭkrắtēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δας</i> (-ĭ́das, “patronymic suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Callipidae, a Scythian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol><p>Callipidae derives from Greek, meaning “of Beautiful Horses”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Callisthenes</li></ol><p><i>κάλλος</i> (kállos, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Callisto</li></ol><p>From <i>κάλλιστος</i> (kállistos, “most beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ), thus literally meaning "the most beautiful one".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Calliphon</li></ol><p><i>κᾰ́λλος</i> (kắllos, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”): perhaps “having a beautiful voice”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Calliope, the muse of eloquence and epic poetry</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰλλῐ-</i> (kăllĭ-, “beautiful”, see κᾰ́λλος (kắllos)) +&lrm; <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calpurnia</li></ol><p>From Latin Calpurnia; feminine form of Καλπούρνιος (Kalpoúrnios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Titus Calpurnius Siculus</li><li>Any individual among Gens Calpurnia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Calpurnius</i>; masculine form of Καλπουρνία (Kalpournía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) Calydon (an ancient city in Aetolia, Greece)</li><li>(Greek mythology,&#32;masculine) Calydon (legendary founding king and eponym of Calydon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Calypso</li></ol><p>From <i>καλύπτω</i> (kalúptō, “to cover”), probably from the aorist <i>καλύψαι</i> (kalúpsai) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a group of islands located near Cos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kamarina (an ancient city on the southern coast of Sicily in Magna Graecia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Camilla</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Camilla</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cambyses</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎣𐎲𐎢𐎪𐎡𐎹</i> (Kaᵐbūjiyaʰ). See also <i>Kamboja</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cameria, Latium, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cameria</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Camicus, a city of Sicily</li></ol><p>Probably from Sicanian. It was described as the residence of the mythical Sicanian king Cocalus. The ending is similar to another Sicanian town, <i>Inicus.</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Campania</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Campānia</i> (“the level country; Campania”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canidius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cānidius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caninius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Canīnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Candaules, the name of a Lydian king</li></ol><p>The idea that this name means "dog-throttler" has been brought to life by Tzetzes and it was further elaborated by comparativists who claimed that the first part demonstrates some similarity with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwṓ</i> (“dog”), while the second can be compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;daviti</i> (“to suffocate, strangle”) and, accordingly, reconstructed as <i>&#42;dʰeh₂w-</i>. However, Hawkins acknowledges another plausible explanation and suggests that this name actually meant "king", connecting it with Luwian <i>𔐑𔑣𔗔</i> (REX-ti-sa /⁠hantawattis⁠/), with a Carian influence. Being not entirely free of difficulties, the latter explanation has the advantage of being based on inner-Anatolian linguistic and cultural comparisons. In the end, it seems that it will be hardly possible to definitively prove any etymology of this name.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Kantakouzenos, a Byzantine noble family</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canusium; Canosa di Puglia, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Capaneus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Καφαρναούμ</i> (Kapharnaoúm)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Καφαρναούμ</i> (Kapharnaoúm)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Capitoline Hill</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Capitōlium</i> (“Capitoline Hill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cappadocia (a historical region and ancient kingdom in central Asia Minor, in present-day Turkey)# Cappadocia (a former province of the Roman Empire, existing from 18 AD until the 7th century)</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎣𐎫𐎱𐎬𐎢𐎣</i> (k-t-p-tu-u-k /⁠katpatuka⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Κᾰππᾰδοκῐ́ᾱ</i> (Kăppădokĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Capua, an Etruscan city</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Etruscan <i>𐌂𐌀𐌐𐌄𐌅𐌀</i> (capeva, “Capua”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caria (a historical region in the southwest corner of Asia Minor, in modern Turkey)</li></ol><p>From <i>Κᾱ́ρ</i> (Kā́r) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carine, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carvilius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Carvilius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cardamyle (a town in Messenia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Kurd</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carthaea, Ceos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to Caria; Carian</li></ol><p>From <i>Καρία</i> (Karía, “Caria”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carmylessus, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carneades, a philosopher of the 2nd century <small>BCE</small></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Carouchas, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Kārukah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Carpocrates</li></ol><p><i>κᾰρπός</i> (kărpós, “fruit”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woman from Caryae</li><li>epithet of Artemis, who had a temple in Caryae</li><li>caryatid</li></ol><p>From <i>Καρύαι</i> (Karúai, “Caryae”, a town in Laconia) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Carthaginian</li></ol><p><i>Καρχηδών</i> (Karkhēdṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Carthage (an ancient city in North Africa, in modern Tunisia)</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed from unattested Phoenician <i>&#42;𐤊𐤓𐤊-𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕</i> (&#42;krk-ḥdšt, “new city”) (cf. Aramaic <i>כַּרְכָא</i>, Hebrew <i>כְּרַךְ</i>), variant of <i>𐤒𐤓𐤕-𐤇𐤃𐤔𐤕</i> (qrt-ḥdšt), hence doublet of <i>Χαλκηδών</i> (Khalkēdṓn, “Chalcedon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caryanda, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ancient city and colony in Sicily, founded by Syracusans in 644 BC, located on the Hyblaean Mountains, nearby current Buscemi.</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The first element could possibly be derived from</p><p>The second could derive from</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Κασμέναι</i> (Kasménai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caspatyrus, an unidentified city of Pactyica mentioned by Herodotus and Scylax of Caryanda</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caspian Sea</li></ol><p>An elliptic form of <i>Κασπῐ́ᾱ Θᾰ́λᾰσσᾰ</i> (Kaspĭ́ā Thắlăssă, “the Caspian sea”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Caspian</li><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) Caspian (member of the ancient people dwelling along the southern and southwestern shores of the Caspian Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li><li>Cassandra, daughter of Priam</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>Κάσσανδρος</i> (Kássandros). Etymology unclear: first element possibly from <i>κέ<b>κασ</b>μαι</i> (ké<b>kas</b>mai, “to shine; excel over”). Connected to Latin <i>censeō</i> (“to honour”) and Sanskrit <i>शंसति</i> (śaṃsati, “to announce”). Second element likely comes from <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”). The rarer form <i>Κεσ(σ)άνδρα</i> (Kes(s)ándra) appears to be more ancient; compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀐𐀭𐀅𐀨</i> (ke-sa-da-ra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Cassandane, from Old Persian, a Persian noblewoman and wife of Cyrus the Great</li></ol><p>From Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Cassiopeia, name of several figures in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kassiani or Kassia, a ninth-century Byzantine composer, abbess, poet and hymnographer</li><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>Κασσιανός</i> (Kassianós), from <i>Κάσσιος</i> (Kássios), from Latin <i>Cassius</i>. Compare also <i>Κασσία</i> (Kassía), another name by which Kassiani was known.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Κασσιέπεια</i> (Kassiépeia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Κασσιέπεια</i> (Kassiépeia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Castabala, Cilicia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Castalia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Catiline</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Catilina</i>, assimilated to the prefix <i>κατα-</i> (kata-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caulonia (a city on the east coast of Calabria, ancient Bruttium)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Καφηρεύς</i> (Kaphēreús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Capernaum (an ancient town in Galilee, Israel, a biblical and archaeological site)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>כְּפַר נַחוּם</i> (kəp̄ar naḥūm, “Nahum's village”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caphereus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caphyae (an ancient town in Arcadia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Caiaphas or Kaiaphas, the name of the Jewish high priest to whom Jesus was taken after his capture in Gethsemane</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>קַיָפָא</i> +&lrm; <i>-ς</i> (-s, declension suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caicus; Bakırçay river</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caucasus</li></ol><p>Uncertain; perhaps ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewk-</i> (“to elevate; height”), or borrowed from Iranian, compare Middle Persian <i>&#42;Kaf kōf</i> (“Mount Qaf, Caucasus mountains”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caucones, an Anatolian people settled in Elis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caesar</li><li>emperor</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Caesar</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caunus; Kaunos, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Cebren, Troas, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cebriones; Kebriones</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cenchreae, Kechries (a region, a Corinthia in Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>κέγχρος</i> (kénkhros, “millet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Κεγχρεαί</i> (Kenkhreaí, “Cenchrea; Kechries, Greece”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cedreae, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Celenderis; Aydıncık, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Proto-Slavic, equivalent to English Kelagast</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Čělogostъ</i>. Perhaps the 6th century.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Celaenae, Phrygia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Celtic</li></ol><p>From <i>Κελτός</i> (Keltós) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Celt</li><li>(<i>poetic adjective</i>) Celtic, synonym of <i>Κελτικός</i> (Keltikós)</li></ol><p>Hellenistic singular number of the Ancient Greek ethnonym <i>Κελτοί</i>&nbsp;m&nbsp;pl (Keltoí). Later form, <i>Κέλται</i> (Kéltai), from Latin <i>Celtae</i>. Probably derived from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;kel-to</i> (“to strike; to fight”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₂-</i> (“to beat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a neighbourhood of Athens in Greece, Ceramicus</li><li>a gulf of the Mediterranean Sea, Ceramic Gulf; the contemporary Gulf of Kerme; Gulf of Gökova</li></ol><p>From <i>κεραμεικός</i> (kerameikós, “ceramic”) &lt; <i>κεραμεύς</i>&nbsp;m (kerameús, “potter”) κεραμε- + <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p><p>From <i>Κέραμος</i>&nbsp;f (Kéramos, “Ceramus”), an ancient city of Caria on the gulf.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cerasus; Giresun, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρασος</i> (kérasos, “cherry”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cercetae, a people of Sarmatia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Cercine; Belasica, a mountain range shared by Greece, Bulgaria and North Macedonia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Corcyraean, Corfiot, originating from or located in Corcyra or Corfu</li></ol><p>From <i>Κέρκῡρᾰ</i> (Kérkūră, “Corcyra, Corfu”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cersobleptes, from Thracian, the name of a king of the Odrysae</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Kersobleptes</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Certonium, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ceryneia, a polis of ancient Achaea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cestria (a town in Epirus, Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>κεστρῖνος</i> (kestrînos), alternative form of <i>κεστρεύς</i> (kestreús, “mullet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cetriporis, from Thracian, the name of a king of the Odrysae</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Ketriporis</i> (“fourth son”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Cephalonian (Person from Cephalonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cephallenia; Kefalonia, Greece</li></ol><p><i>Κεφαλλήν</i> (Kephallḗn) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cepheus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cephisophon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Κηφῑσός</i> (Kēphīsós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cephisodorus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cephisus (a river in Boeotia, Greece)</li><li>Cephisus (a river in Attica, Greece)</li><li>Cephisus (a river in Argolis, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an eponym of St. Peter</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>כיפא</i> (kēp̄ā, “rock”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cierium, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cithaeron; Kithairon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Cicero</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cicerō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cicynethus; Paleo Trikeri, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cilicia (an ancient region in southeastern Asia Minor in modern Turkey, famed for cilice, i.e. haircloth made from the hair of Cilician goats)</li></ol><p>From <i>Κῐ́λῐξ</i> (Kĭ́lĭx) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā). Compare Neo-Assyrian <i>𒆳𒄭𒋃𒆪</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>ḫi-lak-ku /⁠Ḫilakku⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) The indigenous popular class descending from Sicels who lived in Syracuse before the conquest by the Corinthians in 733 BC.</li><li>(historical,&#32;Ancient Syracuse) A group of the indigenous class of Sicel serfs.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>by extension</i>) slaves.</li></ol></ol><p>Uncertain. Variant forms suggest a Pre-Greek origin. It can nonetheless be compared with other Pre-Greek words, such as: <i>κιλλίβας</i> (killíbas), <i>Κιλλικῶν</i> (Killikôn), <i>Κιλλάκτωρ</i> (Killáktōr), <i>Κίλιξ</i> (Kílix). Compare also <i>κῡ́ρῐος</i> (kū́rĭos), as a hypothetical second element of the other two variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Cimmerian, one of an ancient people from the steppes who later invaded Asia Minor.</li></ol><p>Possibly a borrowing from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;gamīra-</i>, <i>&#42;gmīra-</i> (“mobile unit”), or from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;gaya-mira-</i> (“union of clans”). Cognate with Neo-Assyrian <i>𒆳𒄀𒂆𒀀𒀀</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>gi-mir-a-a /⁠Gimirrāya⁠/). Compare Old Armenian <i>Գամիրք</i> (Gamirkʻ) and Biblical Hebrew <i>גֹּמֶר</i> (Gomer).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cimmeria (a the ancient name of Crimea, a peninsula north of the Black Sea)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female Cimmerian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cineas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Circeo</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Circean</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>Κίρκη</i> (Kírkē)Ancient Greek <i>-α-</i> (-a-)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-yós</i>der.Ancient Greek <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)Ancient Greek <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos)Ancient Greek <i>Κιρκαῖος</i>From <i>Κίρκη</i> (Kírkē) + <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cisseus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kyiv (the capital of Ukraine)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Kiovia</i>, from Old Polish <i>Kijów</i>, from Old East Slavic <i>Кꙑевъ</i> (Kyjevŭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clarus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleander</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Clearchus</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “glory”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρχός</i> (ărkhós, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleon</li><li>in particular, the 5th century <small>BCE</small> Athenian general Cleon</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “glory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clement</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Clēmēns</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clazomenae; Klazomenai; Kilizman, Turkey</li></ol><p>Ancient Greek κλάζω (klázō) + -μέναι (-ménai), referring to the honking of wild swans.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clastidium; Casteggio, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Clastidium</i>, probably of Celtic origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Claudia; any female individual among the Roman Claudius family</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Claudia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Claudius; any individual among the Roman Claudius family</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Claudius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleanthes</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cleanor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;klewā́nōr</i>, from <i>&#42;kléwos</i> + <i>&#42;anḗr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱléwos</i> (“fame”) + <i>&#42;h₂nḗr</i> (“man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cleitarchus or Clitarchus</li></ol><p><i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós, “renowned”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρχός</i> (ărkhós, “ruler”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os): perhaps “renowned ruler”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clitor (an ancient town in Arcadia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleaenetus</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos) +&lrm; <i>αἰνετός</i> (ainetós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cleinias</li><li>the name of several historical figures</li></ol><p>From <i>κλεινός</i> (kleinós, “famous”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleisthenes</li><li>in particular, the father of Athenian democracy Cleisthenes</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “glory”) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Clitophon or Cleitophon</li></ol><p>From <i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós, “famed”) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Clitomachus</li></ol><p><i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós, “famed”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os): “famed fighter”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clio</li></ol><p><i>κλείω</i> (kleíō, “to make famous”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleomenes</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleomedes</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos) +&lrm; <i>μήδεα</i> (mḗdea) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cleopatra</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>Κλεόπᾰτρος</i> (Kleópătros, “glory of the father”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleoptolemus</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>πτόλεμος</i> (ptólemos, “war”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleopas</li><li>Cleopas, a Christian who was met by Jesus on the way to Emmaus, according to the gospel of Luke.</li></ol><p>Contracted form of <i>Κλεόπᾰτρος</i> (Kleópătros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cleophon</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cleonae, Kleones (an ancient city in Argolis, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleobulus</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos) +&lrm; <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleombrotus</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “glory”) +&lrm; <i>βροτός</i> (brotós, “mortal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleoxenus</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleopater</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “glory”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr, “father”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), literally “glory of the father”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleopompus</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos) +&lrm; <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cleonymus</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνῠμα</i> (ónŭma, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cleitus or Clitus</li></ol><p>From <i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós, “renowned”), with recessive accent.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clusium (a city in Tuscany; modern-day Chiusi)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Clūsium</i>, from Etruscan <i>𐌂𐌋𐌄𐌅𐌔𐌉</i> (clevsi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Clytemnestra</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>Κλῠταιμήστρᾱ</i> (Klŭtaimḗstrā), perhaps from <i>κλῠτός</i> (klŭtós, “celebrated”) +&lrm; <i>μήδομαι</i> (mḗdomai, “to plan, be cunning”). Often mistakenly glossed as "famed for her suitors", based on the unrelated word <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai, “to woo, court”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clodia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Clōdia</i>, a plebeian alternate of <i>Claudia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clotho (youngest of the three Fates&#59; the spinner of the thread of life)</li></ol><p>From <i>κλώθω</i> (klṓthō, “to spin”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clodius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Clōdius</i>, a plebeian alternate of <i>Claudius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cnidus (Knidos, south-western Asia Minor, modern-day Turkey)</li></ol><p>Possibly form <i>κνίδη</i> (knídē, “stinging needle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Κνωσός</i> (Knōsós, “Cnossus; Gnossus; Knossos, Crete, Greece”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cnossus; Gnossus; Knossos, Crete, Greece</li></ol><p>Unknown; likely Pre-Greek.</p><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀒𐀜𐀰</i> (ko-no-so), <i>𐀒𐀜𐀰𐀆</i> (ko-no-so-de), Egyptian <i>kꜣnywšꜣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cnemus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cnopus, a river in Boeotia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>small river in the island of Corfu</li></ol><p>From <i>κόγχος</i> (kónkhos, “mussel, cockle”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Kozoulo, from Proto-Saka, a last name borne by two Indo-Scythian satraps</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;kuzulaka</i> (“striving, ambitious, energetic”), (cf. Khotanese <i>kūys</i> (“to seek, search”)).</p><p>Possibly related to the name <i>Κοζουλου</i> (Kozoulou) and <i>Κοζολα</i> (Kozola) used by Kushan kings.</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨐𐨂𐨯𐨂𐨫𐨂𐨐</i> (kusuluka).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cocceius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Coccēius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Kolaxais, from Scythian, a Scythian deity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Kolaxšayah</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;Kauδaxšayah</i>, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;ka(H)ud</i> (“to pound, to crush”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;xšayah</i> (“to rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kollouthos, a male given name</li></ol><p>From Demotic Egyptian <i>ḳꜣlꜣwḏꜣ</i>. Сompare Coptic <i>ⲕⲉⲗⲗⲟⲩϫ</i> (kellouj).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>An ancient demos of Attica</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colossae, a city in Phrygia, a region in Asia Minor</li></ol><p>Apparently feminine plural of κολοσσός (kolossós, “colossal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colophon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Colchis (a historical region and ancient Georgian kingdom on the coast of the Black Sea in the South Caucasus on the border between Europe and Asia, corresponding to what is now western Georgia (including Abkhazia), as well as parts of Russia and Turkey)</li></ol><p>From <i>Κολχός</i> (Kolkhós) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Colchis; a Colchian</li></ol><p>From Urartian <i>𒆳𒄣𒌌𒄩</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>qu-ul-ḫa /⁠Qulḫa⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) the city of Kolonai</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colonus, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Commagene (an ancient Greco-Iranian kingdom located around the present-day village of Samsat in southeastern Turkey)</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Luwian (Kummuh).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Comnenus, a Byzantine Imperial family</li></ol><p>From <i>Κόμνη</i> (Kómnē), a village in Thrace.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Conapseni, an ancient tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Conthyle, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coptos; Qift, Egypt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>gbtjw</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Corinthian, from Corinth</li></ol><p><i>Κόρῐνθος</i> (Kórĭnthos, “Corinth”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coracesium; Alanya, Turkey</li></ol><p>Probably from Luwian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coracae, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corduba; Cordova; Cordoba, Spain</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤒𐤓𐤕𐤉𐤅𐤁𐤏𐤉</i> (qrtywbʿy); originally <i>𐤒𐤓𐤕</i> (qrt, “city”) +&lrm; <i>𐤉𐤅𐤁𐤏𐤉</i> (ywbʿy, “Juba I”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cornelius; any individual among the Cornelii</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cornēlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cornelia; any female individual among the Cornelii</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cornēlia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corseae, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) Corsica (an island in the Mediterranean to the north of Sardinia)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Corsica</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corydallus, Attica, Greece</li></ol><p>Identic with Ancient Greek <i>κορυδαλλός</i> (korudallós), a variant of <i>κόρυδος</i> (kórudos, “crested lark”), further connected to <i>κόρυς</i> (kórus), <i>κόρυμβος</i> (kórumbos) and <i>κορυφή</i> (koruphḗ) as well as the verbal forms <i>κορυγγεῖν</i> (korungeîn), <i>κορύπτω</i> (korúptō, “push with the head or horns”) and <i>κορύσσομαι</i> (korússomai, “to rear up”), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“horn, head, top”). Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;herutaz</i> (“deer, stag”), Latin <i>cervus</i> (“deer, stag”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any individual among the Korybantes</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>Ἄβαντες</i> (Ábantes) and <i>ἀλίβας</i> (alíbas, “corpse, dead body”). Given their origin, a Phrygian word would be the first guess. However, the variation shows that the word was originally Pre-Greek, so Kretschmer's connection to Old Norse <i>hverba</i> (“to turn”) must be abandoned. It is difficult to establish which of the two forms was original. Kretschmer thought it was <i>Κύρβαντες</i> (Kúrbantes), whence <i>Κορύβ-</i> would have arisen by adaptation to <i>κόρυς</i> (kórus, “helmet”); however, Beekes finds this uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coronea or Koroneia</li></ol><p>From <i>κορώνη</i> (korṓnē) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cosmas</li></ol><p>From <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “world order, universe”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾶς</i> (-âs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cossinites, a river of Thrace</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cotyora; Ordu, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>any Roman individual named Curio</li><li>Gaius Scribonius Curio</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cūriō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river of Thrace mentioned in the Periplus of Pseudo-Scylax</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cunaxa (an ancient village and site of a major Persian battle in 401 <small>BCE</small>, located 50 miles north of Bablyon and usually identified with the village of Al Nasiffiyat in modern-day Iraq)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kourion, Cyprus; Curium</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Curtius; any individual from the Roman Curtia gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Curtius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cranii, Cephallenia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cranon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Κρᾰταιῐ̈́ς</i> (Krătaiĭ̈́s, “Crataeis”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Crates</li></ol><p>From <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cratippus</li></ol><p>From <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō, “to rule”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”). Compare <i>Ἱπποκράτης</i> (Hippokrátēs), which uses the same elements in the reverse order.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Creusa</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan</li></ol><p>From <i>Κρήτη</i> (Krḗtē, “Crete”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crete.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crimisa, Bruttium, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crito</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cranaa, Athens</li></ol><p>Elliptic form of <i>Κρᾰνᾰᾱ̀ πόλῐς</i> (Krănăā̀ pólĭs, “the rocky city; Athens”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crataeis</li></ol><p>From <i>κρᾰταιός</i> (krătaiós, “mighty”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cratylus</li></ol><p>From <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō, “to rule”) +&lrm; <i>ὕλη</i> (húlē, “wood, trees, forest; timber, firewood; stuff, substance, matter”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cratinus</li></ol><p>From <i>κρά̆τος</i> (krá̆tos, “might, strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cremaste, Troad, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cremona, Lombardy, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crius, one of the Titans in Greek mythology</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Creusis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Creston, in Macedonia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crithote, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Critias</li></ol><p>From <i>κριτής</i> (kritḗs, “judge”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Critobulus</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῐτός</i> (krĭtós, “excellent”) +&lrm; <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ, “counsel”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A city in modern-day Albania, now known as Krujë</li></ol><p>The name of the city is borrowed from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;krāna</i> (“fountain”) (modern <i>krua</i>).</p><p>The city was attested for the first time as Byzantine Greek <i>Κροαί</i> (Kroaí) in Byzantine documents of the early 7th century. In Medieval Latin it was known as <i>Croia</i>, <i>Croya</i>, and <i>Croarum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crocyleium, Aetolia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crommyon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic epithet of Zeus, Poseidon, and Hades</i>) Son of Cronus</li></ol><p>From <i>Κρόνος</i> (Krónos, “Cronus”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, “son of”, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(patronymic,&#32;poetic epithet of Zeus, Poseidon, and Hades) Son of Cronus</li></ol><p>From <i>Κρόνος</i> (Krónos, “Cronus”) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn, “son of”, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A receptacle for old follies</li></ol><p>From <i>Κρόνος</i> (Krónos, “Cronus”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) Crossaea, Crusis (an ancient maritime district of Chalcidice, Macedonia, in the modern-day regional unit of Chalkidiki, Greece; situated near Mygdonia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Croton (Crotone), Calabria, Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>Κροτωνῐ́ᾱ</i> (Krotōnĭ́ā) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs), from <i>Κρότων</i> (Krótōn, “Croton/Crotone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Croesus</li></ol><p>From Lydian. Proposals for origin include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crobyzi, a Thracian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Cronus, Kronos</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) The planet Saturn</li></ol><p>Uncertain; several proposed. See Cronus for details.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Wednesday</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Cronus, Kronos</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) The planet Saturn</li></ol><p>Uncertain; several proposed. See Cronus for details.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Croton, Crotone (a city in Calabria, Italy)</li></ol><p>Unknown, possibly related with Ancient Greek <i>κροτών</i> (krotṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crotopus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cremna, Pisidia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Crisa, a city in Phocis.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cromna, Paphlagonia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cromnus, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Ctesippus</li></ol><p>From <i>κτήσιππος</i> (ktḗsippos, “possessing horses”), from <i>κτᾰ́ομαι</i> (ktắomai, “to acquire”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Ctesias, a 5th-century physician and historian</li></ol><p>From <i>κτῆσις</i> (ktêsis, “possession”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Ctesibius</li></ol><p>From <i>κτᾰ́ομαι</i> (ktắomai) +&lrm; <i>βία</i> (bía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ctesiphon</li><li>Ctesiphon, an ancient city</li></ol><p>The name of the city was borrowed from Manichaean Middle Persian <i>𐫤𐫏𐫘𐫛𐫇𐫗</i> (tyspwn /⁠Tīsfōn/, /Tēsifōn⁠/). The spelling reflects a folk etymology from the personal name, which is from <i>κτᾰ́ομαι</i> (ktắomai, “to acquire”) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) The younger sister of Odysseus</li><li>A city of Thessaly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the two Cyanean Rocks</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠᾰ́νεος</i> (kŭắneos, “dark-blue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyaxares</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;Huvaxšarah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cybele</li></ol><p>From <i>Κῠ́βελον</i> (Kŭ́belon), <i>Κῠ́βελᾰ</i> (Kŭ́belă), from Phrygian or Lydian (see Cybele at Wikipedia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cyberniscus, a Lycian general and son of Sicas</li></ol><p>From <i>κυβερνάω</i> (kubernáō, “to steer, pilot”) +&lrm; <i>-ίσκος</i> (-ískos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cydantidae, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cydonia, Crete, Greece</li><li>an epithet of the goddess Athena</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek source as Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀓𐀈𐀛𐀊</i> (ku-do-ni-ja).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Cydonia, Crete, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Κῠδωνῐ́ᾱ</i> (Kŭdōnĭ́ā, “Cydonia”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Cyzicus; a Cyzicene</li></ol><p>From <i>Κύζῐκος</i> (Kúzĭkos, “Cyzicus”) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) The goddess Aphrodite.</li></ol><p><i>Κύθηρα</i> (Kúthēra, “Kythira”, Aphrodite's place of birth) +&lrm; <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>An Aegean island which is part of the Cyclades</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́κλος</i> (kŭ́klos, “circle, ring”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyllene, an oread nymph in Greek mythology</li><li>Kyllini, a mountain in the Peloponnese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cynosura</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρᾱ́</i> (ourā́, “tail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cynuria</li></ol><p>From <i>Κύνουρος</i> (Kúnouros, “Cynurus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Quintilius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Quintilius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cynoscephalae, the location of the Battle of Cynoscephalae in Thessaly, near modern Larissa</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠνός</i> (kŭnós, genitive of <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”)) +&lrm; <i>κεφᾰλαί</i> (kephălaí, plural of <i>κεφᾰλή</i> (kephălḗ, “head”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cynossema, a location on the Gallipoli peninsula near which the Battle of Cynossema was fought</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠνός</i> (kŭnós, genitive of <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”)) +&lrm; <i>σῆμᾰ</i> (sêmă, “mark”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female name</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Sicel. Compare <i>Κυπυρα</i> (Kupura) and <i>Κυπρα</i> (Kupra), which are also attested in Sicily. It is possible that the form <i>Κυπάρα</i> (Kupára) emerged from the anaptyxis of <i>Κυπρα</i> (Kupra), though <i>α</i> (a) and <i>υ</i> (u) anaptyxis were both present in the Osco-Umbrian languages, which may have influenced the Greek term. It is possibly related to the Italic goddess <i>dea Cupra</i>, and the term probably ultimately derives from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;kupros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kwep-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyrene, an ancient city in what is now Libya.</li><li>Cyrene or Kyrene, a character in Greek mythology.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Quirites; a Quiris</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Quirīs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyrenaica (a geographic region, an and former province of northeastern Libya; in modern usage, extended to the entire eastern portion of Libya)</li></ol><p>From <i>Κῡρηνᾰῐ̈κός</i> (Kūrēnăĭ̈kós, “Cyrenean, <i>adj.</i>”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ, “-a: <i>forming feminine nouns</i>”), from <i>Κῡρηναῖος</i> (Kūrēnaîos, “Cyrenean, <i>n.</i>”) + <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “-ic: <i>forming adjectives</i>”), from <i>Κυρήνη</i> (Kurḗnē, “Cyrene”) + <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, “-y: <i>forming demonyms</i>”), q.v.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Cyrenian, a person from Cyrene</li></ol><p>From <i>Κῡρήνη</i> (Kūrḗnē, “Cyrene”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “-y: <i>forming demonyms</i>”), q.v.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) Sunday, Lord's Day</li></ol><p>Short for <i>ἡ Κυριακὴ ἡμέρα</i> (hē Kuriakḕ hēméra, “the Lord's Day”), from <i>κυριακή</i> (kuriakḗ), feminine form of <i>κυριακός</i> (kuriakós, “belonging to the lord”), from <i>κύριος</i> (kúrios, “lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Quirinus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Quirīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cytinium, Doris, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Constantius, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cōnstantius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Constantinople; a Constantinopolitan</li></ol><p>From <i>Κωνστᾰντῑνούπολῐς</i> (Kōnstăntīnoúpolĭs, “Constantinople”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Constantinople (the former name, from 330–1930 <small>C.E.</small>, of Istanbul, the largest city in Turkey; the former capital of the Ottoman Empire and of the Byzantine Empire before that)</li></ol><p>From the phrase <i>Κωνστᾰντῑ́νου</i> (Kōnstăntī́nou, “Constantine's”, genitive) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Constantine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cōnstantīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Copais, a former lake in Boeotia</li></ol><p>From <i>Κῶπαι</i> (Kôpai, “Copia”) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corycian Cave</li></ol><p>From <i>Κώρῠκος</i> (Kṓrŭkos, “Corycus”) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Codrus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bay of Bengal (a gulf of the Indian Ocean)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Conon</li></ol><p>Presumably from <i>κονέω</i> (konéō, “raise the dust, fast, rapid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corinth</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek <i>&#42;kar</i> (“point, peak”), from whence Ancient Greek <i>κόρυς</i> (kórus, “helmet”). The suffix <i>-νθος</i> (-nthos) indicates the source as a lost pre-Indo-European language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic and Corcyraean form of <i>Κέρκῡρᾰ</i> (Kérkūră, “Corfu”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coronta, Acarnania, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coronus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual named Cotta</li><li>Lucius Aurunculeius Cotta</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cotta</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Cotys, from Thracian, the name of two kings of the Sapaei</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Thracian <i>&#42;Kotus</i>, itself a loanword from Phrygian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Quintus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Quintus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cybistra; Ereğli, Turkey</li></ol><p>From a Late Luwian cognate of Hittite <i>𒌷𒄷𒌋𒁉𒅖𒈾</i> (<sup>URU</sup>ḫu-u-pi₂-iš-na /⁠Ḫūpišna⁠/). Compare with Neo-Assyrian cognates <i>𒆳𒄷𒁍𒍑𒈾</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>ḫu-bu-uš-na /⁠Ḫubušna⁠/), <i>𒆳𒄷𒁍𒍑𒉡</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>ḫu-bu-uš-nu /⁠Ḫubušnu⁠/), <i>𒆳𒄷𒁉𒅖𒈾</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>ḫu-bi-iš-na /⁠Ḫubišna⁠/)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cydnus river; Tarsus çay, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyzicus; Kyzikos, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kythira (also spelt Cythera, Kythera or Kithira), one of the seven major Ionian Islands, Greece.</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, stem ending -ηρ implies a Pre-Greek origin (except for -τηρ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kythnos, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cyclops</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;Kúklōps</i>. Superficially decomposes as <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “wheel, circle”) + <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”), but this may be a folk etymology: another suggested etymology is Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Pḱúklōps</i> (“cattle thief”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pḱú-klōps</i> (“cattle thief”) becoming obscured after the loss of the word <i>&#42;péḱu</i>. (See <i>κλώψ</i> (klṓps) for the second component.) Cattle thieves are pervasive in Indo-European mythology; before the folk etymology, the Cyclops would have been a cattle thief of no unusual facial disposition.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cylon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyme, an ancient Greek city in Euboea; Kymi, Euboea, Greece</li><li>Cyme, an ancient Greek city in Aeolis</li><li>Cumae, an ancient Greek city in Magna Graecia; Cuma, Campania, Italy</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>κῦμᾰ</i> (kûmă, “wave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cynthian (of or relating to Mount Cynthus)</li></ol><p><i>Κύνθος</i> (Kúnthos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cynthus, a mountain in Delos, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol><p>The name's meaning is unknown, but suggestive of Pre-Greek forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypris (another name for Aphrodite)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a beautiful girl</li><li>love, passion</li></ol><p>From <i>Κύπρος</i> (Kúpros) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cyril</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>κῡ́ριος</i> (kū́rios)Ancient Greek <i>-ιλλος</i> (-illos)Ancient Greek <i>Κῦρος</i> (Kûros)Ancient Greek <i>-ιλλος</i> (-illos)Ancient Greek <i>Κύριλλος</i> From <i>κῡ́ριος</i> (kū́rios, “lord”) or <i>Κῦρος</i> (Kûros, “Cyrus”) +&lrm; <i>-ιλλος</i> (-illos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corsica</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyrrhus, Cyrrhestica, Syria</li><li>Cyrrhus, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cytorus; Gideros, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyphus, Perrhaebia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypsela; İpsala, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Legendary Sican king, ruler of Camicus, whose myth tells that he was protecting Daedalus intent on hiding from the Cretan ruler Minos; this latter will find a violent death in Sicania (present-day Sicily).</li></ol><p>Likely related to <i>κόκκᾰλος</i> (kókkălos) (or a potential doublet of it, because of the vowel lengthening), a diminutive of <i>κόκκος</i> (kókkos, “grain, seed, kernel”), which leads to Pre-Greek. Compare, for more, the variant forms of <i>κόχλος</i> (kókhlos, “shellfish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Combreia, Crossaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Constans, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Cōnstāns</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Geb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>gbb</i> (“Geb, earth”), <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./1d52ffb5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="gb" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./d035306b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A40" alt="glyph" height="90"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river <i>Cynips</i>, Tripolitania, Libya</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cynus, Locris, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cobrys, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Copae, Boeotia; Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kos, an island in the Dodecanese</li></ol><p>First attested in the Iliad. Explained as the Phoenician cognate of Hebrew <i>כּוֹס</i> (kōs, “little owl”); the owl was the sacred bird of the island.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laban</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>לָבָן</i> (Lāḇā́n).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Labdacus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Labdalum (an ancient fortress in modern-day Syracuse, Sicily, Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Labranda, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Langon, Achaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lagina, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lade (former island off the coast of Miletus, now part of the mainland of Asia Minor)</li></ol><p>Probably of Anatolian/Pre-Greek origin. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. Perhaps Luwian? What's the meaning?</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ladon (river), Arcadia, Greece</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the god of this river and father of Daphne</li><li>Ladon, a serpent-like mythological dragon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) a male given name, Lazarus, from Hebrew</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אֶלְעָזָר</i> (ʾelʿāzār, “<i>El</i> has helped.”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female Laconian</li><li>(<i>adjectival use</i>) of or relating to Laconian women</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laconia (a region in the southern Peloponnese, Greece)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>Λᾰ́κων</i> (Lắkōn, “Laconian”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lacmon, the highest peak of the Pindus chain</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laconian, inhabitant of Lacedaemon, a Lacedaemonian</li><li>(<i>adjectival use</i>) of or relating to Laconians</li><li>a throw of a dice</li></ol><p>Mediterranean Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lamachus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lampsacum; Lapseki, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lapethus; Lapithos, Cyprus</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤋‬𐤐‬𐤈</i> (l‬p‬ṭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Larissa, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol><p>There were many Greek cities with this name, the most well-known being the one situated in Thessaly or Pelasgiotis. This lead some to suppose the name was of Pelasgic origin. However, the frequency of the name throughout the Greek world rather argues for a Greek origin. It might be found in the adjective <i>λαρός</i> (larós, “sweet, delicious, pleasing”). The city might have been named for the pleasantness or fertility of its surroundings. Compare the closeby Πιερία (Piería), possibly derivated from <i>&#42;peyH-</i> (“fat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Λᾱ́ρῑσᾰ</i> (Lā́rīsă, “Larissa, Thessaly, Greece”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Larcius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Larcius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Larymna, Phthiotis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Latium (a historical region of central Italy, in which the city of Rome was founded and grew to be the capital city of the Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Latium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Latmus; Beşparmak Mountains, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lachesis, one of the three Fates</li></ol><p>The personification of <i>λάχεσις</i> (lákhesis, “obtainment by lot”), from <i>λαχεῖν</i> (lakheîn, “to obtain by lot”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, “-ing”), the former being the aorist of <i>λαγχάνειν</i> (lankhánein). The thing obtained is lifetime, represented by Lachesis allocating a certain length of the life-thread spun by <i>Κλωθώ</i> (Klōthṓ) to each individual.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Leander (lover of Hero)</li></ol><p>From <i>λεώς</i> (leṓs) (Attic form of <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”)) + <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lebedus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lebinthos; Levitha, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lentulus; a member of the Roman Lentulus family</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Lentulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lepreum</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leptis; Leptis Magna, Libya</li></ol><p>From Punic <i>𐤋𐤐𐤒</i> (lpq).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lerna</li></ol><p>The name Lerna is suggested to be connected with the Hattic plural prefix le- plus arinna, arna 'spring', 'pool', 'well', 'source'.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lesbian (of or pertaining to the isle of Lesbos)</li></ol><p><i>Λέσβος</i> (Lésbos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lesbos</li></ol><p>Name said to originally mean 'forested' or 'woody,' possibly from Hittite <i>𒌷𒆷𒊍𒉺</i> (<sup>URU</sup>la-az-pa), as the original Hittite name for the island was <i>Lazpa.</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lechaeum; Lechaio, Corinthia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leda</li></ol><p>In 20th-century sources Leda is traditionally derived from Lycian lada, "wife", as her earliest cult was centered in Lycia. Lycian lada may also be the origin of the Greek name <i>Λητώ</i> (Lētṓ, “Leto”). Other scholars (Paul Kretschmer, Erich Bethe, Pierre Chantraine and R. S. P. Beekes) have suggested a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Livy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Līvius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Berber</li></ol><p>From the name of the <i>Libu</i>, a tribal people of North Africa, attested in Egyptian inscriptions of the 13th century BC as <i>rbw</i> <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./71f37cec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="r" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./c374402d.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="T14" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./a14a7564.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="N25" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table> and as <i>𐤋𐤁𐤉</i> (lby) in later Punic inscriptions.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Libyssa, a city in Bithynia; now Gebze in Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Ligures; a Ligurian</li><li>an inhabitant of Liguria; a Ligurian</li></ol><p>The name is of uncertain origin. Room suggests an Etruscan, Basque, or other language isolate ethnonym, hinted by the <i>-sc-</i> element found in older Latin forms <i>ligusc&#42;</i>; Compare Latin <i>Vascones</i> and <i>Etruscus</i>.</p><p>Imberciadori more recently connects it with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leyg-</i> (“to smear, make smooth”), deriving possessive <i>&#42;(s)lig-wés- &#47; &#42;(s)lig-us-</i> (“bright”) through an internally derived s-stem <i>&#42;(s)léig-us</i> (“brightness”), from the semantics of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)léyg-u- &#47; &#42;(s)lig-éw-</i> (“flat, even; equal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lilaea, Phocis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lindum Colonia; Lincoln, England, United Kingdom</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Lindum</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;lindos</i> (“pool, lake”) (compare Welsh <i>llyn</i> (“lake”) and Scottish Gaelic <i>lionn</i> (“liquid, fluid”)).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lindos, Rhodes, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lindos, Rhodes, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Lixus; Loukkos River, Morocco</li><li>Lixus, Mauretania, Morocco</li></ol><p>From Punic <i>𐤋𐤊𐤔</i> (lkš).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lipaxus, Crossaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lisae, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lissus; Lezhë, Albania</li><li>Lissus, a river of Thrace mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol><p>Found in inscriptions as <i>ΛΙΣΣΙΤΑΝ</i> (LISSITAN), considered to be from <i>λισσός</i> (lissós, “smooth rock”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lichas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Lais</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Laertes</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”) +&lrm; <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “to fasten together”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs), literally "gatherer of the people".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laevinus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Laevīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laelius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Laelius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lavinium</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Lāvīnium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Labdacus; a Labdacid</li></ol><p>From <i>Λάβδᾰκος</i> (Lábdăkos, “Labdacus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Labeates, a tribe of Illyricum, whose chief town was Scodra</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Illyrian <i>&#42;Labeati</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Labienus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Labiēnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hellenized form of the name of the Babylonian king Nabonidus</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒀭𒀝𒈾𒀪𒀉</i> (Nabû-naʾid, “May Nabu be exalted”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Lombard</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) son of Laertes, i.e. Odysseus/Ulysses</li></ol><p><i>Λᾱέρτης</i> (Lāértēs) +&lrm; <i>-ιαδης</i> (-iadēs, patronymic suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lazi, a people of ancient Colchis</li></ol><p>Uncertain. See Laz <i>ლაზი</i> (lazi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Laestrygonian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Laestrygonian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laceria, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lacedaemon</li></ol><p>From <i>Λάκων</i> (Lákōn) and <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn) (or <i>δᾶμος</i> (dâmos), a Doric form of <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Lacedaemon; a Lacedaemonian</li></ol><p>From <i>Λᾰκεδαίμων</i> (Lăkedaímōn, “Lacedaemon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laciadae, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laconia</li></ol><p>Elliptic form of <i>Λᾰκωνῐκή γῆ</i> (Lăkōnĭkḗ gê, literally “Laconian land”) or χώρᾱ (khṓrā, “land, country”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or pertaining to Laconia</li><li>laconic (brief) in speech</li><li>Substantivized forms:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>feminine plural</i>) αἱ Λᾰκωνῐκαί (ἐμβάδες (“Laconian shoes -for men-”).</li><li>(<i>neuter singular</i>) τὸ Λᾰκωνῐκόν (“the state of Lacedaemon”)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>Λᾰ́κων</i> (Lắkōn, “Laconian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lamia</li></ol><p>See <i>Λάμια</i> (Lámia, “man-eating, flesh-eating monster”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Lamprocles</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπρός</i> (lamprós, “bright”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lamptrai, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lamponia, Troad, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Lampsacus; a Lampsacene</li></ol><p>From <i>Λάμψᾰκος</i> (Lámpsăkos, “Lampsacus”) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Laodamas (<i>name of various characters in Greek mythology</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”) +&lrm; <i>δαμάζω</i> (damázō, “to tame”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās), thus Literally, “tamer of the people”</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laodicea, the name of numerous Hellenistic cities, generally named after one of many women by the name of Λαοδίκη (Laodíkē) (Laodice).</li></ol><p>From <i>Λαοδίκη</i> (Laodíkē) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Laodice</li></ol><p><b>Λαοδίκη</b> &lt; <i>λαός</i> (laós, “people”) + <i>δίκη</i> (díkē, “justice”), thus literally "justice of the people".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Laothoe, a consort of Priam and mother of Lycaon</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”) +&lrm; <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “quick, swift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Laocoön, a Trojan priest</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Laocoön, an Argonaut</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”) +&lrm; <i>κοέω</i> (koéō, “to know”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Laomedon</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “men”) +&lrm; <i>μέδων</i> (médōn, “ruler”). Compare <i>λᾱομέδων</i> (lāomédōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Lapithae; a Lapith</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lasaea, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latopolis; Esna, Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰ́τος</i> (lắtos, “Nile perch”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Latinus, a figure in Greek and Roman mythology</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Latin person</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a Roman Catholic Christian</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Latin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Latīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laurentius; Lawrence</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Laurentius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Laogonus, a Trojan slain by <i>Meriones</i></li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”) +&lrm; <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “fruit; offspring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laurium; Lavrio, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laurium; Lavrio, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Loire</li></ol><p>From Gaulish.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lebadea; Livadeia, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lebaea, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Leiocritus, son of Arisbas, slain by Aeneas</li></ol><p>From <i>λεῖος</i> (leîos, “smooth”) +&lrm; <i>κρῐτός</i> (krĭtós, “chosen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Lexiphanes</li></ol><p>From <i>λέξις</i> (léxis, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs, “seeming, appearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leontini (an ancient city, modern-day Lentini in the province of Syracuse, Sicily, Italy)</li></ol><p>Probably from the genitive stem of <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”) + the plural form of <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos, “suffix forming adjectives of place”), supposedly meaning “the ones related to lions”. Compare the demonym <i>Βυζαντῖνος</i> (Buzantînos).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Leontini or Lentini</li></ol><p><i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos, “suffix forming adjectives of place”), meaning “the one related to lions”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Leontini or Lentini</li></ol><p><i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos, “suffix forming adjectives of place”), meaning “the one related to lions”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Lepreum</li></ol><p>From <i>Λέπρεον</i> (Lépreon, “Lepreum”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ́της</i> (-ā́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Letrini, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Levi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>לֵוִי</i> (lēwî).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Levi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>לֵוִי</i> (lēwî) and -ς (-s, declension suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Levite</li></ol><p>From <i>Λευῑ̈́ς</i> (Leuī̈́s) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Leucadian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leucadian</li></ol><p><i>Λευκάς</i> (Leukás) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lucia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lūcia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leucimme (a promontory in Corfu, Greece)</li><li>Lefkimmi (a town in Corfu, Greece)</li></ol><p>A derivative of <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lucania</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lūcānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Lucani; a Lucanian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lūcānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lutetia (ancient Roman city and island in modern France; modern Paris)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Lūtetia</i>, modified by folk etymology to appear to be a derivative of <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leucothea</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>θέᾱ</i> (théā, “goddess”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Nicosia (the capital city of Cyprus and Northern Cyprus)</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) or <i>λεύκη</i> (leúkē, “poplar”) +&lrm; <i>οὐσίᾱ</i> (ousíā, “property, estate”), but in fact altered by folk etymology from various forms including <i>Λευτεῶν</i> (Leuteôn), <i>Λευκοθέον</i> (Leukothéon), and <i>Λευκούπολις</i> (Leukoúpolis) adapting an early or pre-Greek name variously recorded as <i>Λέδραι</i> (Lédrai), <i>Λῆδρον</i> (Lêdron), <i>Λεδρῶν</i> (Ledrôn), <i>Λήδρα</i> (Lḗdra), etc.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an island off Campania</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) alternative form of <i>Λευκουσία</i> (Leukousía, “Nicosia”)</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) or <i>λεύκη</i> (leúkē, “poplar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leuconium, Chios, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to the Levites, levitical</li></ol><p>From <i>Λευῑ̈́της</i> (Leuī̈́tēs, “Levite”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Leokoreion (a shrine at the agora in Athens)</li></ol><p>From <i>Λεώς</i> (Leṓs) +&lrm; <i>κόρη</i> (kórē, “girl, daughter”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon), as according to Greek mythology the daughters of Leos were sacrificed there.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Leonidas</li></ol><p><i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δᾱς</i> (-ĭ́dās, “patronymic suffix”): “son of a lion”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic and Ionic form of <i>Λεωνῐ́δᾱς</i> (Leōnĭ́dās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Leosthenes</li></ol><p>From <i>λεώς</i> (leṓs, “the people”) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength, power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs). Compare <i>Δημοσθένης</i> (Dēmosthénēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Leotychidas or Leotychides</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “the people”) +&lrm; <i>τύχη</i> (túkhē, “fate”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leontius, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>From <i>Λέων</i> (Léōn, “Leo”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lucius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lūcius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Leucippus</li><li>in particular, the philosopher Leucippus</li></ol><p><i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lucullus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Lūcullus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leuctra; Lefktra, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Λεῦκτρον</i> (Leûktron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Leto</li></ol><p>In 20th-century sources Leto is traditionally derived from Lycian lada, "wife", as her earliest cult was centered in Lycia. Lycian lada may also be the origin of the Greek name <i>Λήδα</i> (Lḗda, “Leda”). Other scholars (Paul Kretschmer, Erich Bethe, Pierre Chantraine and R. S. P. Beekes) have suggested a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Letoia, a small island off the Cretan coast mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Liaka Kusulaka, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian satrap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;ryaka</i> (“youth”) (cf. Khotanese <i>rya</i> (“youth”)).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨫𐨁𐨀𐨐</i> (liaka).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Livia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Līvia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Liburni; a Liburnian</li></ol><p>From the tribe's self-designation, most likely Illyrian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>North Africa</li><li>Northwestern Africa</li></ol><p>Homeric toponym (Odyssey IX.95, XXIII.311).</p><p>From <i>Λῐ́βῠς</i> (Lĭ́bŭs) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē), from the name of the <i>Libu</i>, a tribal people of North Africa, attested in Egyptian inscriptions of the 13th century BC as <i>rbw</i> <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./71f37cec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="r" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./c374402d.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="T14" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./a14a7564.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="N25" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table> and as <i>𐤋𐤁𐤉</i> (lby) in later Punic inscriptions.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Liguria</li></ol><p>From <i>Λῐγῠστῐκός</i> (Lĭgŭstĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ligurian</li></ol><p>From <i>Λῐ́γῠς</i> (Lĭ́gŭs) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Licinius; a member of the Roman Licinia gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Licinius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Licinia; a female member of the Roman Licinia gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Licinia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lilybaeum; Marsala, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Limyrus, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἀργῷος Λῐμήν</i> (Argōîos Lĭmḗn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lipari, Aeolian Islands, Sicily, Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>Liparus,</i> name of a mythical founder, which could be of local substrate origin. However, compare <i>λιπαρός</i> (liparós, “rich, fat”), which could have been a nickname.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Lipoxais, from Scythian, a Scythian deity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Lipoxšayah</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;Δipoxšayah</i>, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;diHp</i> (“to shine, to light up”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;xšayah</i> (“to rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Locris (an ancient region of Greece, the homeland of the Locrians, made up of three distinct districts)</li></ol><p>From <i>Λοκρός</i> (Lokrós, “Locrian”) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Locrian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Londinium; London</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Compare Latin <i>Londīnium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Londinium; London</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Londinium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lampedusa (an island of the Mediterranean Sea, south of Sicily)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lucilius; a member of the Roman Lucilia gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lūcīlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luceria; Lucera, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lūceria</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lucretius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lūcrētius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lucretia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lūcrētia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luke, Lucas</li></ol><p>Formed with the suffix <i>-ᾶς</i> (-âs) as the hypocoristic of the Greco-Roman name <i>Λούκιος</i> (Loúkios, “Lucius”) or <i>Λουκανός</i> (Loukanós, “Lucanus”) or both.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lusi, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lutatius; a member of the Roman Lutatia gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lutātius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lugdunum; Lyon, France</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Lugdūnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Luca; Lucca, Tuscany, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Latin, feminine equivalent Λουκία (Loukía), equivalent to Latin Lūcius</li><li><i>Lūcius</i> (regularly abbreviated L.) a common ancient Roman praenōmen shared by many male historical figures; used by both patrician and plebeian families, it gave rise to the patronymic gentēs Lūcia and Lūcilia, as well as the cognomen Lūcullus.</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Lūcius</i> from <i>lūx, lūcis</i> (“light”) from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;louks</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“brightness”). Cognate of Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀩𐀄𐀏</i> (re-u-ka), Ancient Greek <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white, light, bright”), Old English <i>lēoht</i> and English <i>light</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lusius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lusius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Locrian form of <i>Λοκρός</i> (Lokrós, “Locrian”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Lydia (a historical region and ancient kingdom in western Asia Minor, in modern-day Turkey)</li></ol><p>From <i>Λῡδός</i> (Lūdós, “Lydus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Lydias; Mavroneri River, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lydus, legendary first king of Lydia</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Lydia; a Lydian</li></ol><p>From Lydian (*luwdja, “Luwia”), where the Lydians came to live after being driven away by the Phrygians. Here the -d- is result of affrication of proto-Luwian <i>*-j-</i>. Cognate to Hittite <i>𒆳𒇻𒌑𒄿𒅀</i> (Luwiya).</p><p>The name's ultimate origin is unclear. Beekes suggests that they received the name of the area's original inhabitants (Pre-Greek/substrate), as they were originally called <i>Μαίονες</i> (Maíones). It was connected by Josephus to <i>Lûḏîm</i> (Hebrew <i>לודים</i>) and by Hippolytus to <i>Ludim,</i> son of Mizraim. It could instead ultimately be an endonym from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁lewdʰ-</i> (“people”). Also see Lydia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lycaon</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Lycaonia; a Lycaonian</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́κος</i> (lŭ́kos, “wolf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lycia (a historical region in southwestern Asia Minor, in modern-day Turkey)# Lycia (a former province of the Roman Empire, existing on its own from 43 AD to 74 AD, and later as part of Lycia et Pamphylia)</li></ol><p>Likely of Anatolian origin, but of uncertain meaning: compare Hittite <i>𒇻𒊌𒅗𒊑</i> (“to light up”), and the placename <i>Λυκαονία</i> (Lukaonía) (related to Lukka).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Lycian (native or inhabitant of ancient Lycia)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lycian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lycidas</li></ol><p>From <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδας</i> (-ídas, “patronymic suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>Λυκίᾱ</i> (Lukíā)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>Λυκίᾱ</i> (Lukíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hill in Athens, mount Lycabettus</li></ol><p>In his book <i>Αι Τοπωνυμίαι Αθηνών και Περιχώρων των</i> (<i>The Toponyms of Athens and its Surroundings</i>) Kostas Biris suggests a Pelasgian (pre-Greek) origin, <i>Luk-ab-ettú</i>, a name meaning “breast-shaped height” (quoted by Mihalis Stukas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lycaonia</li></ol><p>From Proto-Anatolian <i>&#42;Lukkawanna</i> (“Lukka's land”), influenced by the Greek personal name <i>Λῠκᾱ́ων</i> (Lŭkā́ōn). Lukka was a historical region of southwestern Anatolia, probably a native Anatolian name (compare <i>Λυκία</i> (Lukía)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Lycomedes</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́κος</i> (lŭ́kos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>μήδεᾰ</i> (mḗdeă, “counsel”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Lycurgus</li><li>in particular, the legendary lawgiver of Sparta Lycurgus</li></ol><p>From <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós), literally “worker-wolf”; compare <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) for the second element.</p><p>Or Λυκοῦργος &lt; Λυκόοργος &lt; ΛυκόFοργος [luko-worgos] from <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>ἐέργω</i> (eérgō, “shut out”), literally “shutting out the wolf”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Λυκοῦργος</i> (Lukoûrgos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asyut, Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́κος</i> (lŭ́kos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Lycophron</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́κος</i> (lŭ́kos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lyrnessus (a city in Mysia, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Lysias, a fifth century <small>BCE</small> Athenian orator</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́σῐς</i> (lŭ́sĭs) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Lysimachus</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́σῐς</i> (lŭ́sĭs, “releasing”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Lysistratus</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́σῐς</i> (lŭ́sĭs, “releasing”) +&lrm; <i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name: Lysidice</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Lysidice, the daughter of Pelops and Hippodamia</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́σῐς</i> (lŭ́sĭs, “releasing”) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́κη</i> (dĭ́kē, “justice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Lysicles</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́σῐς</i> (lŭ́sĭs) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lysimachia; Eksemil, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>Λῡσίμαχος</i> (Lūsímakhos, “Lysimachus”) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Lysistrata</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́σῐς</i> (lŭ́sĭs, “releasing”) +&lrm; <i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lusitania</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lūsitānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonym</i>) from Λυταί (Lutaí), a place in Thessaly</li><li>(religion,&#32;as a proper noun, masculine) epithet of Poseidon in Thessaly</li></ol><p>From <i>Λυταί</i> (Lutaí, “Lytae, a place in Thessaly”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, a suffix forming adjectives indicating origin)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Lois</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Lotophagi</li></ol><p>From <i>λωτός</i> (lōtós, “lotus”) +&lrm; <i>φᾰγεῖν</i> (phăgeîn, “to eat”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lollius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Lollius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A king of the Cimmerians</li><li>The father of Artemisia I of Caria</li><li>Lygdamis, tyrant of Naxos</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Cimmerian Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Luɣdamiyā</i>, <i>&#42;Duɣdamyā</i>, perhaps from <i>&#42;duɣdā</i> (“milk” or “milk cow”), related to Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;dáwgdi</i> (“to milk”), + <i>&#42;-mā</i> (possessive suffix), literally, “having milk” or “having milk cows” (compare Avestan personal name <i>𐬔𐬀𐬊𐬨𐬀𐬧𐬙</i> (Gaomaṇt, literally “having cattle”)), + <i>&#42;-yā</i> (adjectival suffix).</p><p>Alternatively a two-stem hypocoristic of a name such as <i>&#42;Duɣdamēš́ī</i>, compare Avestan personal name <i>𐬛𐬀𐬡𐬭𐬀𐬨𐬀𐬉𐬱𐬌</i> (Daβramaēši), with second element <i>𐬨𐬀𐬉𐬱𐬍</i> (maēšī, “ewe”). Parallel borrowing with Neo-Assyrian <i>𒁹𒌇𒁮𒈨𒄿</i> (Dugdammī) and <i>𒁹𒌇𒁮𒈬𒌑</i> (Dugdammū).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lyncus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lydae, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lydda; Lod, Israel</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>לוֹד</i> (lōḏ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lycastus, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lyceum, a gymnasium in Athens at which Aristotle founded a philosophical school.</li></ol><p>From the neighboring temple to <i>Ἀπόλλων Λύκειος</i> (Apóllōn Lúkeios, “Apollo of Lyceus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Lycian (native or inhabitant of ancient Lycia)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Lycian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lyctus, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Lysander</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́σῐς</i> (lŭ́sĭs, “releasing”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Lysippos or Lysippus</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́σῐς</i> (lŭ́sĭs, “releasing”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lystra, Lycaonia, Turkey</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Loryma, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lestadae, Naxos, Greece</li></ol><p>Related to <i>λῃστής</i> (lēistḗs, “pirate”), because this city was a hideout of pirates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Legae, an ancient tribe which dwelt on the northern shore of the Caspian Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lemnos</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤋𐤁𐤍</i> (lbn), from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;laban-</i> (“white”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Linus</li></ol><p>From <i>λίνον</i> (línon, “flax”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Loios, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to July</li></ol><p>Compare <i>Ὁμολώιος</i> (Homolṓios), the name of an Aetolian, Boeotian and Thessalian month.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 10,000 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. An abbreviation of μυριάς (muriás, “myriad”). Equivalent to the Roman digit ↂ, i.e. ⅭⅭⅠↃↃ.</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤌</i> (m, “mēm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>μῆτερ</i> (mêter)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>μητρός</i> (mētrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i><b>Μ</b>ήτη<b>ρ</b> <b>Θ</b>εο<b>ῦ</b></i> (<b>M</b>ḗtē<b>r</b> <b>Th</b>eo<b>û</b>, “Mother of God”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>μητέρα</i> (mētéra)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>μητρί</i> (mētrí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>μητρός</i> (mētrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mago</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Magō</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(common,&#32;nonspecific) magician, and derogatorily sorcerer, trickster, conjurer, charlatan</li><li>(common,&#32;specific) a Zoroastrian priest. Compare e.g. Herodotus <i>Hist.</i> 1.132f, Xenophon <i>Cyropedia</i> 8.3.11, Porphyry <i>Life of Pythagoras</i> 12, Heraclitus apud Clemens <i>Protrepticus 12</i>, etc.</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) name of one of the tribes of the Medes. This usage is only attested once; Herodotus <i>Histories</i> 1.101.</li></ol><p>From an Old Iranian language presumably akin to Old Median/Old Persian <i>𐎶𐎦𐎢𐏁</i> (m-gu-u-š /⁠maguš⁠/) and Avestan <i>𐬨𐬊𐬕𐬎</i> (moġu). Both attested Old Iranian words are hapaxes, and of indeterminable meaning. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₂gʰ-</i> (“to be able”). Probably unrelated to Avestan <i>𐬨𐬀𐬔𐬀</i> (maga) (cognate with Sanskrit <i>मघ</i> (magha, “gift”)). Attestation in Greek predates attestation in Old Iranian. See also <i>μῆχος</i> (mêkhos), <i>μηχανή</i> (mēkhanḗ).</p><p>Compare also Aramaic <i>𐡌𐡂𐡅𐡔</i> (mgwš), Aramaic <i>מגושא</i>, Arabic مجوس (majūs) and Hebrew <i>אמגושים</i> (amgushim) or Hebrew <i>מגושים</i> (mgushim) in the plural, in the Talmud.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Madytus; Eceabat, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macae, a people of Libya</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Μάκεχ</i> (Mákekh)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mecca</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic مَكَّة (makka).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ancient city in Elis district</li><li>A mountain of Lesbos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Macrones</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Macer</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manlius; a member of the Roman Manlia gens</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Manlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Malchus, from Hebrew</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>מֶלֶךְ</i> (mɛ́lɛḵ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mani, Manes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Syriac <i>ܡܐܢܝ</i> (Mānī).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maxyes, a Libyan tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marea, Lake Mariout</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">U7&#58;r</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">N23&#58;t&#42;Z1</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (mryt, “bank, shore, quay”).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Aramaic, typically translated as Martha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>מָרְתָא</i> (mārtā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marium, Cyprus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Latin, equivalent to English Marcellus</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Marcellus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marpessa</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) March</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mārtius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maro</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mases, Argolis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mastaura, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Machlyes, a Libyan tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>May</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Maius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Greek mythology,&#32;Roman mythology) One of the Erinyes/Dirae, (Furies, the goddesses of envy and jealousy who induced to commit crimes, such as marital infidelity.</li></ol><p>From <i>μεγαίρω</i> (megaírō, “to grudge, to envy”).</p><p>This was the name of one of the Furies or <i>Ἐρινύες</i> (Erinúes, “Erinyes”) in Greek mythology. The name is used as a word in several European languages to denote a shrewish, ill-tempered woman.</p><p>Compare French <i>mégère</i>, Italian <i>megera</i> and Sicilian <i>majara</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Megara, Megaris (a town in Attica, Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>μέγᾰρον</i> (mégăron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Megara Hyblaea, an ancient Greek colony in Sicily, situated near Augusta. It took the adjective "hyblaea" after Hyblon, a Sicel leader, finally settled at a place afterwards called the "Hyblaean Megara", named after the goddess Hybla.</li></ol><p>From <i>Μέγαρα</i> (Mégara) and <i>Ὑβλαῖα</i> (Hublaîa), this latter is the neuter plural of <i>Ὑβλαῖος</i> (Hublaîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Meges, son of Phyleus and commander of the Epeii</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Medusa</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a female given name</li></ol><p>Feminine participle of <i>μέδω</i> (médō, “rule, protect”), i.e. "she who rules/protects".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Methana (a city in Attica, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Μάκεχ</i> (Mákekh)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meletus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river of Magna Graecia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Memmius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Memmius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Memnon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Memphis, mythological founder of the city of Memphis</li><li>Memphis, an ancient city of Egypt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">mn&#58;n</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./a3cf17af.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="nfr" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">f&#58;r</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./094c028a.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="O24" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9d93c55f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="niwt" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>mn-nfr</i>, “Memphis”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Menaechmus</li></ol><p><i>μένω</i> (ménō, “to stay”) +&lrm; <i>αἰχμή</i> (aikhmḗ, “spear point, battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os): “staying in battle”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Menander</li><li>in particular, the comic playwright Menander</li></ol><p>From <i>μένος</i> (ménos, “force”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mende, Chalcidice, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Menippus</li><li>a Cynic satirist from Gadara of the 3rd century BCE</li></ol><p>From <i>μένος</i> (ménos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Mentor</i>, a mythological figure</li><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;méntōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méntōr</i>, from <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>मन्तृ</i> (mantṛ, “advisor, counselor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meno</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Merbalus, a Phoenician man and son of Agbalus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mespila; Mosul, Iraq</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Messa, Laconia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mestrius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Mestrius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Metaurus; the river Metauro</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Metibi, an ancient tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Metellus; one of the Roman Metelli</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Metellus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meton</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Medea</li></ol><p>Literally, “cunning”, from <i>μήδεα</i> (mḗdea, “cunning, counsels, device, planning”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă, feminine suffix), from <i>μέδω</i> (médō, “to rule over, protect”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to measure, give advice, heal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Methymna; Mithymna, Lesbos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Melian</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῆλος</i> (Mêlos, “Milos”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, adjective suffix).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to Milos; Melian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Melian</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῆλος</i> (Mêlos, “Milos”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to Milos; Melian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Melian</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῆλος</i> (Mêlos, “Milos”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Egyptian, of historical usage, notably borne by Menes, uniter of Upper and Lower Egypt and founder of the First Dynasty of Egypt</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">mn&#58;n</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>mnj</i>).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Mesacus</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Maiθaka</i> (“sending out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Midea, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mieza, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greek form of Iranian Mithra (e.g. Herodotus <i>Histories</i> I,131; Plutarch, <i>Isis and Osiris</i> 46.7)</li><li>Greek name of the figures of various syncretic Helleno-Zoroastrian cults of Asia Minor (100 BC-200 AD)</li><li>Greek form of Latin Mithras, cult figure of the Roman mystery religion that flourished between 100 and 400 AD.</li></ol><p>From an Old Iranian language <i>&#42;Miθra</i>. Compare Avestan <i>𐬨𐬌𐬚𐬭𐬀</i> (miθra), Old Persian <i>𐎷𐎰𐎼</i> (mi-θ-r /⁠miθra⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Μίθρας</i> (Míthras) — literary variant in the -ης declension, used by authors such as Xenophon, Plutarch, Lucian, Nonnus, and Hesychius.</li></ol><p>From an Old Iranian language <i>&#42;Miθra</i>, compare Avestan <i>𐬨𐬌𐬚𐬭𐬀</i> (miθra), Old Persian <i>𐎷𐎰𐎼</i> (mi-θ-r /⁠miθra⁠/).</p><p>Literary variant of <i>Μίθρας</i> (Míthras), formed with the -ης masculine declension pattern (cf. Σωκράτης).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Miletus, an ancient city on the western coast of Anatolia.</li></ol><p>Of Anatolian origin, possibly related to the Hittite <i>𒈪𒂖𒆷𒉿𒀭𒁕</i> (Millawanda).</p><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀖𐀨𐀴𐀍</i> (mi-ra-ti-jo, “Miletian”), <i>𐀖𐀨𐀴𐀊</i> (mi-ra-ti-ja, “women from Miletus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Milo; Milon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a promontory and mountain range in Ionia, opposite Chios; modern Urla-Karaburun-Çeşme peninsula</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the name of several figures in Greek mythology, including&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a centaur</li><li>a giant</li><li>the grandfather of Aeolus</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mindarus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Minos</li></ol><p>From the Cretan Ancient Greek word for "king" ; further origin unknown. Appearing in Minoan Linear A tablets is <i>𐘻𐘯𐘃</i> (mi-nu-te), which is possibly related.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Maipharnos, from Scythian, son of Theoteimos, ruler of Pontic Olbia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Māifārnē</i>, itself borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Māhifarnāh</i>. Doublet of <i>Μαιφέρνης</i> (Maiphérnēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Maeander; the river Büyük Menderes</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. According to Bernal, possibly connected to <i>μαῖα</i> (maîa, “old woman, midwife”), although he considers a Semitic origin more probable: Biblical Hebrew <i>מְעִי</i> (məʿy, “bowels, intestines; womb, source of procreation”), plural <i>מֵעַיִם</i> (mēʿayim), cf. Egyptian <i>qꜣb</i> (“intestine”), plural <i>qꜣbw</i> (“windings of a waterway”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maenalus; Mainalo</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Maeonia or Lydia; one of the Maeones; a Maeonian</li></ol><p>Possibly related to the Anatolian name Masa, itself related to <i>Μυσία</i> (Musía, “Mysia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Magdala.</li></ol><p>Of Aramaic origin (Hebrew <i>מִגְדָּלָהּ</i> (migdaláh)). The tower Magadala, Migdala is a place in Palestine.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a surname of the biblical figure Mary Magdalene, typically interpreted as a toponym referencing Magdala, a city noted in the Talmud.</li></ol><p>From <i>Μᾰγδᾱλά</i> (Măgdālá), Semitic origin (compare Hebrew <i>מִגְדָּלָהּ</i> (migdaláh)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mazaces, from Old Persian, last satrap of Egypt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Mazdākah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mazares, from Old Persian, a Median general who defected to Cyrus the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Mazdara</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mazaeus, from Old Persian, a Persian nobleman and satrap of Babylon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Mazdāyah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ματθαῖος</i> (Matthaîos, “Matthew”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Μαθουσάλα</i> (Mathousála)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Μαῖᾰ</i> (Maîă, “Maia”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Maebuzanes, from Old Persian, prytanis of Comana and Sarus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Māhibaujanah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persianː Maedatas</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>An Achaemenid cavalry commander</li><li>A magistrate of Anisa</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Māhidātah</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maecenas</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Maecēnās</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maimacterion: the fifth month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around November and December</li></ol><p>From <i>Μαιμάκτης</i> (Maimáktēs, “boisterous, stormy”), an epithet of Zeus at Athens.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maeonia; Lydia</li></ol><p>From <i>Μαίων</i> (Maíōn, “Maeonian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maephernes, a name attested in coins from Phrygia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Māhifarnāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(fully <i>Μαιῶτῐς λῐ́μνη</i>) Sea of Azov</li><li>(fully <i>Μαιῶτῐς χώρᾱ</i>) Pryazovia (the land around the Sea of Azov)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Islands of the Blessed</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰκᾰ́ρων</i> (măkắrōn, “blessed”) +&lrm; <i>νῆσοι</i> (nêsoi, “island”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macedon, Macedonia (an an ancient Greek kingdom in the north of the Greek peninsula)</li></ol><p>From <i>Μᾰκεδών</i> (Măkedṓn, “Macedonian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to Macedon; Macedonian</li></ol><p>From <i>Μᾰκεδών</i> (Măkedṓn, “Macedonian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Macedonia; a Macedonian</li></ol><p>Related to <i>μᾰκεδνός</i> (măkednós, “tall, slim”), which is derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₂ḱ-</i> (“long, slender”). Also related to Doric Greek <i>μᾶκος</i> (mâkos) and Attic Greek <i>μῆκος</i> (mêkos, “length”), <i>Μάκιστος</i> (Mákistos) (the mythological eponym of a town in Elis and an epithet of Heracles), <i>𐬨𐬀𐬯𐬀𐬵</i> (masah, “length”), Hittite (maklant, “thin”), Latin <i>macer</i> (“meagre”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;magraz</i> (“lean, meager”). The same root and meaning has been duly assigned to the tribal name of the Macedonians, which is commonly explained as having originally meant "the tall ones" or "highlanders" in Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) a Maccabee</li></ol><p>Septuagint transliteration of Hebrew <i>מַכַּבִּי</i> (makkabbī).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malaga (a port city in Andalusia, Spain)</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤌𐤋𐤊</i> (Málaka or Malake), possibly from Proto-West Semitic <i>&#42;milḥ-</i> (“salt”). Compare modern Greek <i>Μάλαγα</i> (Málaga) from Spanish.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malabar, Kerala</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Malayalam <i>മല</i> (mala).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cape Malea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Malachi (prophet)</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Malachi (book of the Bible)</li><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Malachi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>מַלְאָכִי</i> (mal'achí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malao; Berbera, Somalia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manasseh</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>מְנַשֶּׁה</i> (Mənaššé) and -ς (-s, noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mandane</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;Mandanā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Mandaean</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mandonion; Manduria, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Manetho, from Egyptian, an Egyptian priest at Sebennytus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian; candidates for the original etymon include <i>mry-nṯr-ꜥꜣ</i> (literally “beloved of the Elder God”), <i>mꜣꜥ-n-ḏḥwtj</i> (literally “true to Thoth”), and <i>mry-n-nt</i> (literally “beloved of Neith”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Manichaic, Manichaean</li></ol><p><i>Μανιχαῖος</i> (Manikhaîos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Manichaism/Manichaeism</li></ol><p><i>Μανιχαῖος</i> (Manikhaîos) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Manichaeus</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Manichaean</li></ol><p>From Classical Syriac <i>ܡܐܢܝ ܚܝܐ</i> (Mānī ḥayyā, “Living Mani”), from Classical Syriac <i>ܡܐܢܝ</i> (Mānī, “Mani”) + Classical Syriac <i>ܚܝܐ</i> (ḥayyā, “living, alive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mantinea; Mantineia</li></ol><p>From <i>Μαντινεύς</i> (Mantineús) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Mantinea or Mantineia</li></ol><p>From an unknown element +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maracanda; Samarkand, Uzbekistan</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maraphii, an ancient Iranian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Marafiya</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mary, Maria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marathesium, Ionia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marathon, Attica, Greece</li></ol><p>According to Strabo, from <i>μάραθον</i> (márathon, “fennel”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn), referring to the prevalence of the plant in the area. This explanation appears to be implicitly accepted by Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mardonius, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>father of Darius the Great's companion Gobryas who helped him overthrow Gaumata</li><li>son of Gobryas and of a daughter of Darius the Great and grandson of the previous Μαρδόνιος, husband of Artazostre and father of Artontes, and brother-in-law and cousin of Xerxes I</li><li>a bodyguard of Xerxes I killed at the Battle of Artemisium by a brother of Themistocles</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎶𐎼𐎯𐎢𐎴𐎡𐎹</i> (m-r-du-u-n-i-y /⁠Mr̥duniyaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Μᾰρῐ́ᾱ</i> (Mărĭ́ā, “Mary, Maria”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mariandyni, an ancient people of Bithynia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marcion, a diminutive of the male given name Μᾶρκος (Mârkos), from Latin, particularly (<i>historical</i>) Marcion of Sinope, founder of Marcionism.</li></ol><p>From <i>Μᾶρκος</i> (Mârkos) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn, “-son: <i>forming patronymics</i>”), from Latin <i>Mārcus</i>, from <i>Mārs</i> +&lrm; <i>-cus</i>, from Old Latin <i>Māvors</i> (“Mars, god of war”) and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-kos</i> (“<i>forming desubstantival adjectives</i>”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marcian, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Mārciānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marmarium, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Μαρῖνος</i> (Marînos).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Marsi; a Marsian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marsyas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maroneia (a city in Thrace, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian: Masistes, a Persian prince and son of Darius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎶𐎰𐎡𐏁𐎫</i> (m-θ-i-š-t /⁠Maθištaʰ⁠/, literally “greatest, supreme”). Might originally have been a title <i>maθišta pasā tanūm</i> (“the greatest after myself (the king)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Masistres, from Old Persian, a Persian mentioned by Aeschylus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mascames, from Old Persian, a Persian commander</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Maškāmah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Massicytus, a mountain range of Lycia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Massalia (ancient Greek city and colony in modern southern France; modern Marseille)</li></ol><p>Probably from a pre-Latin language of Italy, perhaps an unknown “Massaliot” language or Ancient Ligurian <i>mas</i> (“spring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Massalia or Marseille; a Massaliote; a Marseillais</li></ol><p>From <i>Μασσαλία</i> (Massalía, “Marseille”) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Masinissa or Massinissa</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Christianity) a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Matthias</li></ol><p>Shortened form of <i>Ματταθίας</i> (Mattathías), from Biblical Hebrew <i>מַתִּתְיָהוּ</i> (mattiṯyāhū).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Aramaic, equivalent to English Matthew (disciple of Jesus in Christianity)</li></ol><p>According to Strong, from Aramaic <i>מַתַּי</i> (mattay). Compare also <i>Ματταθίας</i> (Mattathías, “Mattathias”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Judaism,&#32;Christianity) a male given name from Hebrew, Mattathias</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>מַתִּתְיָהוּ</i> (mattiṯyāhū).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Materi, an ancient tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Maudaca</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Maudaka</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male surname of historical usage, equivalent to English Maurice or Latin Mauricius, notably borne by <b>Maurice</b> (539–602 <small>CE</small>), <small>Latin:</small> Flāvius Tiberius <b>Mauricius</b>, <small>Greek:</small> Φλάβιος Τιβέριος <b>Μαυρίκιος</b> (Phlábios Tibérios <b>Mauríkios</b>); Roman Emperor who ruled the Byzantine Empire from 582 until deposed in 602, last Byzantine emperor of the Justinian imperial dynasty</li><li>a male given name, from the surname</li></ol><p>A parallel development with Latin <i>Mauricius</i>, from <i>Μαύρικος</i> (Maúrikos) (from <i>Μαῦρος</i> (Maûros, “Moor”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mauretania (an ancient Berber kingdom)</li></ol><p>From <i>Μαῦρος</i> (Maûros, “Moor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Mauretania; a Mauretanian</li></ol><p>From <i>Μαῦρος</i> (Maûros, “Moor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Mausoleum of Mausolus in Halicarnassus</li></ol><p>From <i>Μαύσωλος</i> (Maúsōlos) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Machaon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Machanidas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Ancient Greek form of the surname of the medieval Sephardic Jewish philosopher Maimonides</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>מַיְמוֹן</i> (maymōn) or Arabic مَيْمُون (maymūn) + <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix). The patronymic represents a translation of the Hebrew <i>בֶּן</i> (ben, “son of”) or Arabic اِبْن (ibn, “son of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Maues, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian king</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;mava</i>, <i>&#42;mauka</i> (“hero, tiger”) (cf. Khotanese <i>mauya</i>, <i>muyi</i> (“tiger”); Sogdian <i>𐼺𐼷𐼴</i> (myu), <i>𐫖𐫏𐫇</i> (myu, “tiger”)).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨨𐨆𐨀</i> (moa) and <i>𐨨𐨆𐨒</i> (moga).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Carian: Mausolus or Maussolus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Carian <i>&#42;𐊪𐊠𐊲𐊸𐊫𐊦</i> (*Mauśoλ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mauretanian, Moorish</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine plural) Mauretanians, Moors</li></ol><p>One theory is that it is a specific use of <i>μαυρός</i> (maurós, “black”), from Classical <i>ἀμαυρός</i> (amaurós, “dark, dusky”). Another possibility is that it derives from the Moors' native name (perhaps from a dialect of Berber; compare Central Atlas Tamazight <i>ⵎⵖⵓⵔ</i> (mɣur, “big”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Megabazus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagavazdā</i> or Old Median <i>&#42;Bagabāzu</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Megabyzus</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎲𐎥𐎲𐎢𐎧𐏁</i> (b-g-b-u-x-š /⁠Bagabuxšaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Megabyxus</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎲𐎥𐎲𐎢𐎧𐏁</i> (b-g-b-u-x-š /⁠Bagabuxšaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) Magna Graecia; Hellenic parts of southern Italy and Sicily extensively settled by Ancient Greek colonists in such vast numbers that the Ancient Romans came to refer to the region as <i>Great Greece</i></li></ol><p><i>μεγᾰ́λη</i> (megắlē), feminine of <i>μέγας</i> (mégas, “great”) &amp; <i>Ἑλλᾰ́ς</i> (Hellắs, “Greece”), calque of Latin <i>Magna Graecia</i> (literally “Great Greece”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Megara, wife of Heracles</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Megabates</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a cousin of Darius the Great, satrap of Dascylitis and father of Seisamas and Megabazos</li><li>a commander of the Achaemenid fleet</li><li>an inhabitant of Halicarnassus</li><li>a son of the rebellious general Spithridates and lover of Agesilaus of Sparta</li><li>a hyparch of king Artapates of Armenia</li><li>a brother of Vardanes I</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagapātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Megadostes, from Old Persian, father of Mascames</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagaduštah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Poetic and Ionic form of <i>Μεγᾰκλῆς</i> (Megăklês)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Megacles</li></ol><p>From <i>μέγᾰς</i> (mégăs, “great”) +&lrm; <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Megalopolis; a megalopolitan</li></ol><p>From <i>Μεγᾰλόπολῐς</i> (Megălópolĭs, “Megalopolis”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Megalopolis, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>μέγᾰς</i> (mégăs, “big”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Megaris or Megarid, the land around Megara</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to Megara</li></ol><p>From <i>Μέγᾰρᾰ</i> (Mégără, “Megara”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Megara; a Megarian</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Megareus</li></ol><p>From <i>Μέγᾰρᾰ</i> (Mégără, “Megara”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Megasidres</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>father of Dotus</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagaciθrah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Megasthenes</li></ol><p>From <i>μέγας</i> (mégas, “great”) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A city of Boeotia</li><li>a city in Acarnania</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>any one of the ancient Celtic and/or Roman settlements named Mediolanum</li><li>Mediolanum; Milan, Lombardy, Italy</li><li>Mediolanum; Whitchurch, Shropshire, England, United Kingdom</li><li>Mediolanum Santonum; Saintes, Charente-Maritime, France</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Mediolānum</i>, of Celtic origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Methydrium</li></ol><p>From <i>Μεθύδριον</i> (Methúdrion, “Methydrium”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a male given name, equivalent to English Methodius</li></ol><p>From <i>μέθοδος</i> (méthodos, “pursuit of knowledge”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Methydrium, a town in Arcadia</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰ-</i> (metă-, “between”) +&lrm; <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), i.e. "between the waters", so called because of its location at the divide between Peloponessian watersheds.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Methoni (a village in Peloponnese, Greece)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Methone (an ancient city near present-day Methoni in Peloponnese, Greece)</li><li>Methoni (a village in Central Macedonia, Greece)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Methone (an ancient city-state near present-day Methoni in Central Macedonia, Greece)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Methone (an ancient city-state near present-day Ano Lechonia in Thessaly, Greece)</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Methone (former name of <i>Μέθανα</i> (Méthana) (= Methana): a city in Attica, Greece)</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Methone (one of the Alkyonides)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meidias</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melanchlaeni, a Scythian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol><p>From <i>μελάγχλαινος</i> (melánkhlainos, “black-cloaked”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Melampus</i></li></ol><p>From <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās, “dark; black”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Melanthius</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās, “black”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Melanippus</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās, “dark; black”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Meleager</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλω</i> (mélō, “to concern, to be concerned”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γρός</i> (ăgrós, “field”) or *<i>ϝάγρος</i> (wágros), cognate of Sanskrit <i>वज्र</i> (vájra, name of the Storm God's weapon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meliboea, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melitaea, Phthiotis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malta (an island in the Mediterranean Sea, Malta)</li><li>Mdina (a city on the island of Malta, Malta)</li><li>Mljet (an island in the Adriatic Sea, Croatia)</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, “-a: <i>forming feminine place names</i>”), although its use for the island of Malta is considered uncertain. Some scholars consider it to represent the importance of the islands' indigenous bees and their output. Others consider it a transcription of Phoenician <i>𐤌𐤋𐤈</i> (mlṭ, “Grand Harbor, Cospicua”), from <i>𐤌𐤋𐤈</i> (mlṭ, “refuge, port”), reapplied first to the island and then, after its Roman conquest, to its capital via Latin <i>Melita</i>. See Wikipedia's articles on Melite and Malta.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Melanippe</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλᾰν</i> (mélăn, “dark; black”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melantho, a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>μελάνθιον</i> (melánthion) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melpomene, muse of singing and later of tragedy</li></ol><p>From the feminine mediopassive participle of <i>μέλπω</i> (mélpō, “to sing”), see <i>-μενος</i> (-menos) for the suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Melchite</li></ol><p>From Classical Syriac <i>ܡܠܟܝܐ</i> (malkāyāʾ, “royal; royalist”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melchizedek, a biblical character</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>מַלְכִּי־צֶדֶק</i> (malkī-ṣéḏeq).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mephibosheth</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Biblical Hebrew <i>מְפִיבֹשֶׁת</i> (Məp̄īḇṓšeṯ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Menedemus</li></ol><p><i>μένω</i> (ménō, “stay”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os): perhaps “staying with his people”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) a male given name, Menelaus</li></ol><p>From either <i>μένω</i> (ménō, “stay, wait for”) or <i>μένος</i> (ménos, “vigor, power”) and <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Menexenus</li></ol><p><i>μένω</i> (ménō, “to stay”) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “stranger, guest”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os): perhaps “staying as a guest”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Menesaechmus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Menenius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Menēnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Menae; Mineo, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Menecrates</li></ol><p><i>μένω</i> (ménō, “to stay”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs): perhaps “staying in power”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the name of a titan</li><li>a male given name, Menoetius</li></ol><p>From <i>μένος</i> (ménos, “strength, force, might”) +&lrm; <i>οἶτος</i> (oîtos, “fate”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Menoeceus</li></ol><p>From <i>μένος</i> (ménos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Menoetius: patronym of Patroclus</li></ol><p><i>Μενοίτιος</i> (Menoítios) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Menostanes, from Old Persian, a Persian prince</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Manuštanah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meroe, Nubia, Sudan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Meroitic <i>𐦨𐦡𐦷𐦡𐦥𐦢</i> (medewi /⁠məɾəwi⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Merope</li></ol><p>From <i>μέρος</i> (méros, “lot, fate”) +&lrm; <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “voice, word”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Mesopotamia (a geographic region in southwest Asia, spanning from the rivers Euphrates and Tigris, located in modern Iraq, eastern Syria, and southeast Turkey; the site of one of the most ancient civilizations in the history of man)# (<i>historical</i>) Mesopotamia (a former province of the Roman Empire, existing from 116 to 117 AD and again from 198 to 637 AD)</li></ol><p>The feminine substantive form of the adjective μεσοποτάμιος (mesopotámios) from μέσος (mésos, “middle, between”) and ποταμός (potamós, “river”), compare with the Hebrew <i>אֲרַם נַהֲרַיִם</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mesore, the twelfth month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to August</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>mswt-rꜥ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Messala</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Messalla</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Messapii; a Messapian</li></ol><p>According to Pokorny, the Messapic name means "amid waters," from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰyos</i> + <i>&#42;h₂ep-</i>, the latter of which gave their region <i>Apulia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Messene, Messenia, Greece</li><li>Messina, Sicily, Italy</li></ol><p>Found in Mycenaean Greek inscriptions (Linear B) as <i>𐀕𐀼𐀙</i> (me-za-na). Possibly from μέσσος (méssos) &mdash; <i>Doric</i>, "in between".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Messenian, of Messene</li></ol><p><i>Μεσσήνη</i> (Messḗnē, “Messene”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Messiah or Christ; the Anointed one</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>ܡܫܺܝܚܳܐ</i> (məšīḥā), from Biblical Hebrew <i>מָשִׁיחַ</i> (māšîaḥ, “anointed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Messenia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Μεσσήνη</i> (Messḗnē, “Messene”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Metageitnion: the second month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around August and September</li></ol><p>From <i>Μετᾰγείτνιᾰ</i> (Metăgeítniă, “wedding feast”), a festival in celebration of emigration. Ultimately from <i>μετά</i> (metá) +&lrm; <i>γείτων</i> (geítōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Metapontum</li></ol><p>From <i>Μετᾰπόντῐον</i> (Metăpóntĭon, “Metapontum”), from <i>μετᾰπόντῐος</i> (metăpóntĭos, “in the midst of the sea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Metapontum, Magna Graecia; Metaponto, Basilicata, Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰπόντῐος</i> (metăpóntĭos, “in the midst of the sea”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Metapontum</li></ol><p>From <i>Μετᾰπόντῐον</i> (Metăpóntĭon) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mecheir, the sixth month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to February</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>pꜣ-n-pꜣ-mḫrw</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Media (a geographic region and ancient satrapy of the Persian Empire in northwestern Iran, originally inhabited by the Medes)# Media (a possible ancient kingdom ruled by the Medes from approximately 700 to 550 BCE, whose extent and sometimes even existence is debated)</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῆδος</i> (Mêdos, “Mede”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Median (from Media&#59; pertaining to the Medes)</li></ol><p><i>Μῆδος</i> (Mêdos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Mecisteus, son of Talaus, brother of Adrastus and father of Euryalus</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Mecisteus, companion of Antilochus, slain by Polidamas</li></ol><p>From <i>μήκῐστος</i> (mḗkĭstos, “tallest, greatest”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a female Melian</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῆλος</i> (Mêlos, “Milos”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Malian</li><li>an inhabitant of Malis</li></ol><p>From <i>Μηλῐ́ς</i> (Mēlĭ́s) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melinoe</li></ol><p>Possibly derived from <i>μήλινος</i> (mḗlinos, “of a quince-yellow color”). The color was supposedly associated with the pallor of illness for the Ancient Greeks, connecting with Melinoe's status as a chthonic figure.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Meriones (hero of the Trojan War)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Metion, the son of king Erechtheus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Metrodorus</li></ol><p>From <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr, “mother”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temple of Demeter (Ceres)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithrates, from Old Median</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθrātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithrabauzanes, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθrabaujanah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithradates, from Old Persian, equivalent to English Mithradates</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθradātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian Mithraphernes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a eunuch of Artaeus of Media</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθrafarnāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithraustes, from Old Persian, commander of the Armenian soldiers in the Achaemenid army at the Battle of Gaugamela</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθravahištah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithraeus, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθrayah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or pertaining to Mithras: Mithraic</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῐ́θρᾱς</i> (Mĭ́thrās) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰκός</i> (-ăkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithridates, from Old Median, equivalent to English Mithridates</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθradātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithrobarzanes, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>father-in-law and betrayer of Datames</li><li>a Persian Magian in Babylon</li><li>commander in Tigranes the Great's army against Lucullus at the Battle of Tigranocerta in 69 BCE</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθrabr̥zans</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithrobauzanes, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθrabaujanah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithropaustes, from Old Persian, a cousin of Xerxes I</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθraupastah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithropastes, from Old Persian, son the satrap Arsites</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθraupastah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mithrostes, from Old Persian, son of the satrap Hidarnes III executed under the orders of Parysatis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθravahištah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῑκίων</i> (Mīkíōn) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Miletus</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of Miletus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Miltiades</li></ol><p>From <i>μῐ́λτος</i> (mĭ́ltos, “red earth”) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a city in India, or Pakistan</li></ol><p>Unknown. The latter element is clearly borrowed from Sanskrit <i>नगर</i> (nagara, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Minucius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Minucius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Minturnae, Latium; Minturno, Lazio, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Minturnae</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Minyae; a minyan</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Minoan civilization</li></ol><p>Contemporary classicist coinage, derived from <i>Μῑ́νως</i> (Mī́nōs, “Minos”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Minotaur</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῑ́νως</i> (Mī́nōs, “Minos”) +&lrm; <i>ταῦρος</i> (taûros, “bull”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. Semantic development?</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Minoa, Sicily, Italy</li><li>Minoa, Crete, Greece</li><li>Minoa, Gaza, Palestine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Misenum, Campania, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Misēnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mitranes, from Old Median</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθrānah</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mitradates, from Old Persian, equivalent to English Mitradates, a Median shepherd and foster father of Cyrus the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθradātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mitraeus, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθrayah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Median: Mitrobates</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a satrap of Hellespontine Phrygia</li><li>a civil servant for Pharnabazus</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Miθrapātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Hebrew and Christian male given name, Michael, from Hebrew, famously held by the Archangel Michael</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>מִיכָאֵל</i> (mīḵāʾēl, “Michael”, literally “Who is like God?”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Micah (prophet)</li><li>Book of Micah</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>מִיכָיָה</i> (Mîḵāyāh, “Who is like Yah?”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Mnesarchus</li></ol><p>From <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Mnevis, a bull-shaped aspect of the god Atum-Ra venerated in Heliopolis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>mr-wr</i>, <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./54bc7b8f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="U7" alt="glyph" height="45"/><br /><img src="./71f37cec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="D21" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./0683160a.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="G36" alt="glyph" height="58"/><br /><img src="./569eb7c1.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="O5" alt="glyph" height="47"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./a035ee6b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="E1" alt="glyph" height="75"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table> This form apparently came from the alternate name form <i>mn-wr</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mnesalces, an Armenian man mentioned by Plutarch</li></ol><p>From <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai, “to be mindful”) +&lrm; <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Mnesus, a Paeonian slain by Achilles</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Modocae, a tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moesia</li></ol><p>From <i>Μοισός</i> (Moisós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Maybe a patronymic with the suffix <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Molossia</li></ol><p>From <i>Μολοσσός</i> (Molossós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Molossus (son of Neoptolemus and Andromache)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Molossian (inhabitant of Molossia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Molycreum; Molykreio, Aetolia-Acarnania, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Monaeses, from Parthian, a Parthian nobleman</li></ol><p>From Parthian <i>𐭌𐭍𐭉𐭔</i> (mnyš /⁠Manēč⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Monemvasia (a town in Laconia, Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>μονο-</i> (mono-, “single”) +&lrm; <i>ἔμβασις</i> (émbasis, “embarkation point”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Monneses, from Parthian, a Parthian nobleman</li></ol><p>From Parthian <i>𐭌𐭍𐭉𐭔</i> (mnyš /⁠Manēč⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Morgantia, Sicily, Italy</li></ol><p>Identified by Strabo as a pre-Roman Italic tribe. The name could ultimately be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mórǵs</i> (“edge, boundary, border”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Morpheus</li></ol><p>From <i>μορφή</i> (morphḗ, “shape, form”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mossynoeci, a tribe of Pontus</li></ol><p>From <i>μόσσῡν</i> (móssūn, “wooden tower”) +&lrm; <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō, “to dwell, live”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Motya; Mozia, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>toponym</i>) The name of an ancient city of Sicily (now Modica).</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Most likely from a pre-Hellenic and Siculian substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Muhammad</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic مُحَمَّد (muḥammad).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Mongol; a Mongolian</li></ol><p>Borrowred from Persian مغول (moġol, “a Mongol”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Munychia, Piraeus, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Munychion: the tenth month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around April and May</li></ol><p>From <i>Μουνῠχίᾰ</i> (Mounŭkhíă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muriane (one of the four ancient districts of Cataonia in Lesser Armenia in modern-day Turkey, mentioned only by Ptolemy)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of the Murena family</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Murena</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Musaeus</i></li></ol><p>From <i>μοῦσα</i> (moûsa, “music, Muse”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muziris; Kodungallur, India</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mundus; Heis, Somalia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lake Moeris, Egypt</li><li>the town of Moeris near Crocodilopolis (modern Faiyum)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>mr-wr</i> (“Moeris”), <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./2ea40d4f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="N36" alt="glyph" height="22"/><br /><img src="./a6021014.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="N23" alt="glyph" height="27"/> <img src="./b207efda.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z1" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./0683160a.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="wr" alt="glyph" height="62"/><br /><img src="./71f37cec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="r" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td>
</tr></table>
</td></tr></table>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>Μοῦσᾰ</i> (Moûsă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mygdonia, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Μύγδων</i> (Múgdōn) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mycale</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Mycene (daughter of Inachus, king of Argos)</li><li>alternative form of <i>Μυκῆναι</i> (Mukênai, “Mycenae”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mycalessus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mycerinus (Menkaure, Menkheres), an ancient Egyptian king</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>mn-kꜣw-rꜥ</i>, whence also the alternative rendering <i>Μεγχέρης</i> (Menkhérēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mycenaean</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mycenaean</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῠκῆναι</i> (Mŭkênai, “Mycenae”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mycenae</li></ol><p>Probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mylae, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myus, a city in Ionia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myriandrus, Syria, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>μυρίανδρος</i> (muríandros, “containing 10.000 inhabitants”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Myrmecium</i>, Scythia, Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Myrmidon; one of a mythical tribe of warriors led by Achilles in the Trojan War</li></ol><p>Folk etymology in Ovid's time derived the tribe's name from <i>μυρμηδών</i> (murmēdṓn, “ant-nest”), from <i>μύρμηξ</i> (múrmēx, “ant”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greek name of the Lydian king Candaules</li><li>tyrant of Mytilene</li></ol><p>Likely from Hittite <i>𒁹𒈬𒌨𒅆𒇷</i> (<sup>m</sup>mu-ur-ši-li /⁠Muršili⁠/, “Mursili”). Related to Old Armenian <i>Մուշեղ</i> (Mušeł), and its descendant in modern Armenian <i>Մուշեղ</i> (Mušeġ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Myrtilus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>μύρτος</i> (múrtos) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myronides</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῠ́ρων</i> (Mŭ́rōn) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mysia</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῡσός</i> (Mūsós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Mysian</li><li>an inhabitant of Mysia</li></ol><p><i>Μυσός</i> is possibly an exonym from <i>μύσος</i> (músos, “defilement, uncleanness”), as Mysians were described as being weak and subpar, which could be related to <i>μῖσος</i> (mîsos, “hatred”) or <i>μαδάω</i> (madáō, “to be wet, moist”), or a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the city of Mytilene, in Lesbos, Greece</li><li>the island, Lesbos</li></ol><p>Anatolian origin. Possibly related to Hittite (Muwatalli). Compare Ancient Greek <i>Μοτύλου</i> (Motúlou).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Mytilenean, of or from Mytilene</li></ol><p><i>Μῠτῐλήνη</i> (Mŭtĭlḗnē, “Mytilene”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moabite</li></ol><p>From <i>Μωάβ</i> (Mōáb, “Moab”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female inhabitant of Moab; a Moabite woman, a Moabitess</li></ol><p>From <i>Μωᾰβῑ́της</i> (Mōăbī́tēs, “Moabite”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) alternative form of <i>Μωυσῆς</i> (Mōusês, “Moses”)</li></ol><p>From the monophthongisation of the non-Attic diphthong ⟨ωυ⟩ to ⟨ω⟩ in earlier <i>Μωυσῆς</i> (Mōusês, disyllabic), which was likely formed under Ionic influence, from Biblical Hebrew <i>מֹשֶׁה</i> (mōšê). Compare the trisyllabic regularisation <i>Μωϋσῆς</i> (Mōüsês).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical) a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Moses</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>מֹשֶׁה</i> (mōšê). The non-Attic diphthong ⟨ωυ⟩ is found in the Septuagint, and was likely of Ionic origin, though was later regularised in disyllabic <i>Μωσῆς</i> (Mōsês) and trisyllabic <i>Μωϋσῆς</i> (Mōüsês).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) alternative form of <i>Μωυσῆς</i> (Mōusês, “Moses”)</li></ol><p>From the split of the non-Attic diphthong ⟨ωυ⟩ in earlier <i>Μωυσῆς</i> (Mōusês, disyllabic), which was likely formed under Ionic influence, from Biblical Hebrew <i>מֹשֶׁה</i> (mōšê). Compare the disyllabic regularisation <i>Μωσῆς</i> (Mōsês).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mummius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Mummius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Monalus, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Morsimus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Moryllus, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Morys, a Mysian, the son of Hippotium, slain by <i>Meriones</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mosyllon; Bosaso, Somalia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mopsium, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myci, a Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mykonos, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol><p>This etymology is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mylasa, Caria; Milas, Muğla, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myndus, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myra, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myrae, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myrina, Lemnos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myrcinus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myrlea, Bithynia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myrsinus, Elis, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>μύρσινος</i> (múrsinos), alternative form of <i>μύρτινος</i> (múrtinos, “made of myrtle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Myron</li></ol><p>From <i>μύρον</i> (múron, “myrrh, perfume”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Momemphis; Menouf, Egypt</li></ol><p>From Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Marcus, Mark</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Mārcus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Malos or Melos or Milos, an island in the Cyclades, Greece</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Pre-Greek culture in the island, so possibly a variation of Βῆλος (Bêlos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Nabis, held by</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a ruler of Sparta</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nagidus, Cilicia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naxos</li></ol><p>Most likely of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Naparis; Ialomița (river), Romania</li></ol><p>Possibly from a Dacian native name for the river</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Narbo, Gallia Narbonensis; Narbonne, Aude, Languedoc-Roussillon, France</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Narbō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Narnia; Narni, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Narnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nasi, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Neæra, a daughter of Oceanus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Nearchus</li></ol><p><i>νέος</i> (néos, “new”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neda, a river of Elis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Netum; Noto, Italy</li></ol><p>Unclear. If not related with <i>νέατος</i> (néatos, “the last, the extreme, the lowest”) (irregular superlative of <i>νέος</i> (néos, “new, young”)), perhaps of Pre-Greek origin, possibly a Sicel one.</p><p>The unattested Doric variant could have been <i>&#42;Νέᾱτον</i> (&#42;Néāton) (<i>Néāton</i>). Hypothetically from an earlier *Νέϝητον (<i>*Néwēton</i>). In any case, problems of morphological reconstruction remain with reference to the vowel quantity or untraceable consonants disappeared from the lemma.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Nut (an Egyptian goddess)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">nw&#42;t&#58;pt</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i>.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nepos</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Nepōs</i> (“nephew; grandson”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Nervii</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Nervius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nero</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Nerō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Nessus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Mesta; the river Nestos</li></ol><p>According to MacBain, from the same Proto-Indo-European root that gave the River Ness, possibly <i>&#42;ned-</i> (“water”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nestor</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>νέομαι</i> (néomai, “to return”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Nephthys (an Egyptian goddess)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./c80eb9c2.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="O9" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">t&#58;H8</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i></div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nelia, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nericus, Acarnania, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>any one of a number of cities named Nicaea:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Nicaea (ancient Greek and later Roman city in the province of Transalpine Gaul, in modern southeastern France; modern Nice)</li><li>Nicaea (ancient Greek city in northwestern Anatolia, in modern Turkey; modern Iznik)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nicander</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νδρο-</i> (ăndro-, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Compare <i>Ᾰ̓νδρόνῑκος</i> (Ăndrónīkos, “Andronicus”), which contains the first two components in the opposite order.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Nicarchus</li></ol><p>From <i>νίκη</i> (níkē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>m Ninus, the founder of Nineveh in Greek mythology</li><li>f Nineveh; Mosul, Iraq</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nisaea</li></ol><p>From <i>Νῖσος</i> (Nîsos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nisibis; Nusaybin, Mardin, Turkey</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>ܢܨܝܼܒܝܼܢ</i> (nəṣīḇīn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nisyros, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Nitocris</i></li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">R24&#58;t</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">q&#58;r&#58;t</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (nt-jqr.t(j)); as a name for a pharaoh, this seems to have originated as an error for <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./130279ec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="ra" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./eaa91352.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="nTr" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./35e77b15.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="kA" alt="glyph" height="60"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (nṯrj-kꜣ-rꜥ).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nahshon, an Israelite mentioned in the genealogy of Jesus.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>נַחְשׁוֹן</i> (Naḥšon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonym</i>) Nabataean, one of the Nabataeans, ancient inhabitants of Nabataea, a region of Arabia that covers parts of northern Arabia and the Southern Levant, lying between Arabia and Syria, and stretching from the Euphrates river to the Red Sea. During the Hellenistic Period, the Nabataeans were involved in a nexus of trade routes reaching as far as Italy to the west and India to the east, which centered at their city of Petra in what is now Western Jordan near the Negev Desert from before 310 BCE until the Roman conquest in 106 CE.</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Any of a group of people who once lived around modern Jordan.</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Relating to the Nabataean people, their kings, art, architecture, religion, language, or script.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Nabataean Aramaic <i>𐢕𐢃𐢋𐢈</i> (nbṭw).</p><p>Possibly cognate with Arabic النبطي (an-Nabaṭī, “Nabataean, Nabaṭ”), Arabic أَنْبَاط (ʔanbāṭ), and Hebrew <i>נבטים</i> (nabaṭim, “Nabataeans”), and Hebrew <i>נבטית</i> (nabaṭit, “Nabataean (adj.)”), all of which might be ultimately derived from the same Semitic root, perhaps Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;nabat-</i>, possibly cognate with Akkadian nabāṭu ("to shine brightly").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Νᾱζᾰρέτ</i> (Nāzărét)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Νᾱζᾰρέτ</i> (Nāzărét)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Νᾱζᾰρέτ</i> (Nāzărét)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Νᾱζᾰρέτ</i> (Nāzărét)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nazareth, the hometown of Jesus</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>נָצְרַת</i> or Biblical Hebrew <i>נָצְרַת</i> (Nāzərát).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Nazareth; a Nazarene</li></ol><p>From <i>Νᾱζᾰρέτ</i> (Nāzărét) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nazarene</li></ol><p>Compare <i>Ναζαρά</i> (Nazará, “Nazareth”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nahapana, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian satrap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;Nahapāna</i> (“protector of the clan”), composed of a term cognate with Khotanese <i>nāha</i> (navel) + a term cognate with Khotanese <i>pānä</i> (“keeper”), <i>pā</i> (“to protect”).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨣𐨱𐨤𐨣</i> (nahapana) and Prakrit <i>𑀦𑀳𑀧𑀸𑀦</i> (nahapāna).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nathanael (apostle)</li><li>a male given name from Hebrew</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>נְתַנְאֵל</i> (nəṯanʾēl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Narthacium, Phthiotis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nasamones, a people of Libya</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nasica</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Nāsīca</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nauplia; Nafplio, Argolis, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “to sail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Nausithous, the king of the Phaeacians</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “quick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Nausicaa</li></ol><p>Endearing form of <i>&#42;Ναυσικάστη</i> (&#42;Nausikástē).</p><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>κᾱ́ω</i> (kā́ō, “burn”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Nausiphanes</li></ol><p>A compound of <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “a boat”) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “appear”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Ancient Greek form of the surname of the medieval Sephardic Jewish philosopher Nachmanides</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>נַחְמָן</i> (nakhmán) + <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix). The patronymic represents a translation of the Hebrew <i>בֶּן</i> (ben, “son of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a naiad</li></ol><p>From <i>νᾱ́ω</i> (nā́ō, “to flow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Naubolus, the name of various mythological characters</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>βόλος</i> (bólos, “throw, cast of a net”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naucratis</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “boat”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “strength”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naupactus</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>πήγνῡμῐ</i> (pḗgnūmĭ, “to fasten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nauplius</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “to sail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neandria, Troad, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any one of various Greek and Hellenistic cities named Neapolis</li><li>Naples, Italy</li><li>Nablus, Palestine</li><li>Nabeul, Tunisia</li></ol><p>From <i>νέᾱ</i> (néā, “new”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Neapolis (or Naples or Nablus); a Neapolitan</li></ol><p>From <i>Νεᾱ́πολῐς</i> (Neā́polĭs) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in or on the Nile</li></ol><p>From <i>Νεῖλος</i> (Neîlos) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nectanabis, from Egyptian, the name of two Egyptian pharaohs</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">n&#58;&#42;xt</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">x&#42;t&#58;D40</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">nb&#58;f</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (nḫt-nb.f).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the eleventh month in Crete</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Necho</li></ol><p><div>From Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">n&#58;kA</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (n-kꜣw).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nemea</li></ol><p>From <i>νέμος</i> (némos), i.e. <i>the wooded district</i>, and formally from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;neméhā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neméseh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;nem-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Novgorod</li></ol><p>From misspelled <i>&#42;Νεβογαρδάς</i> (&#42;Nebogardás), from South Slavic *Новогардъ, from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;Novogordъ</i>&nbsp;/ <i>&#42;Novъgordъ</i>, from <i>&#42;novъ</i> (“new”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gordъ</i>. Compare Old East Slavic <i>Новъгородъ</i> (Novŭgorodŭ).</p><p>Misspelling:</p><p>-γαρδ-:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Neocles</li></ol><p>From <i>νεο-</i> (neo-, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Neoptolemus</li></ol><p>From <i>νεο-</i> (neo-, “new”) +&lrm; <i>πτόλεμος</i> (ptólemos, “war”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic meaning “son of Νέστωρ (Néstōr) [=Nestor]”</li></ol><p><i>Νέστωρ</i> (Néstōr) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Neuri, a Scythian tribe settled along the course of the river Hypanis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nephelis, Cilicia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud-cuckoo-land</li></ol><p>From <i>νεφέλη</i> (nephélē, “cloud”) +&lrm; <i>κόκκῡξ</i> (kókkūx, “cuckoo”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Naphtali</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>נַפְתָּלִי</i> (Nap̄tālī).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Neophron</li></ol><p>From <i>νεο-</i> (neo-, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Nile</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>νεῖλος</i> (neîlos, “river valley”), possibly from Semitic root naḥal. Compare Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;nahar-</i> (“river”). The Egyptian name for it was <i>ḥꜥpj</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Neleus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male patronymic meaning “son of Νηλεύς (Nēleús) [=Neleus]”</li></ol><p><i>Νηλεύς</i> (Nēleús) +&lrm; <i>-ιάδης</i> (-iádēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nereus</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Most theories are based on Nereus being a sea god:</p><p>It is possible that the name <i>Νηρεύς</i> (Nēreús) is a back-formation from <i>Νηρῇς</i> (Nērēîs, “a Nereid”), the name of his daughters, as the latter hold much more significance in Greek mythology.</p><p>Tsantsanoglou suggests Hesiod (folk-)etymologically linked the name to <i>νηρός</i> (nērós) (contracted from <i>νεαρός</i> (nearós, “new, fresh”)), as a play on him also being called "the old man".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a daughter of Nereus; a Nereid</li></ol><p>From <i>Νηρεύς</i> (Nēreús, “Nereus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a daughter of Nereus, a Nereid</li></ol><p>From <i>Νηρεύς</i> (Nēreús, “Nereus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Niceratus</li></ol><p>From <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>ἐρᾰτός</i> (erătós, “lovely”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>νῑκᾰ́ω</i> (nīkắō, “win, conquer, be victorious”) +&lrm; <i>-τᾱς</i> (-tās, suffix forming masculine agent nouns) = literally “(the) Winner, Conqueror, Victor”; compare <i>Νῑκήτης</i> (Nīkḗtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nicias</li></ol><p>From <i>νίκη</i> (níkē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Byzantine,&#32;demonym) Nicaean (person from Nicaea)</li></ol><p>Irregularly formed from <i>Νῑ́κα(ιᾰ)</i> (Nī́ka(iă), “Nicaea”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix forming demonyms). The twelfth-century <i>Etymologicum Magnum</i> criticises the omission of the <i>ι</i> (i, “iota”) whilst acknowledging that such omission is common in terms formed on iota-terminal stems suffixed with <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nicocles</li></ol><p><i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês): “famous for winning”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Nicholas</li></ol><p>From <i>Νῑκόλᾱος</i> (Nīkólāos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a daughter of Nicholas</li></ol><p>From <i>Νῑκόλᾱος</i> (Nīkólāos, “Nicholas”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nicomedia</li></ol><p>Named after <i>Νικομήδης</i> (Nikomḗdēs, “Nicomedes”), with the feminine suffix <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Nicomedes</li></ol><p>From <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē) +&lrm; <i>μήδεα</i> (mḗdea).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Nicodemus</li><li>Nicodemus, a Pharisee who showed favour to Jesus.</li></ol><p>From <i>νίκη</i> (níkē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Nicholas</li><li>One of the seven chosen in Acts 6 and of St. Nicholas.</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē)Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lāwós</i>Ancient Greek <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós)Ancient Greek <i>Νικόλαος</i> From <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nicomachus</li></ol><p><i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, masculine nominal ending): “victory fighter”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Nicopolis (a city in the Roman Empire), the modern Preveza.</li></ol><p>From <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Nicostratus</li></ol><p>From <i>νίκη</i> (níkē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>στρατός</i> (stratós, “army”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Niconium, Scythia, Ukraine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Nireus, the legendary king of Syme</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Niphates, a mountain chain of Armenia</li><li>Niphates, a Persian general</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nipsaei, an ancient Thracian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Niobe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) of November</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>November</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Numitor</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Numidia</li></ol><p>From <i>Νομᾰδῐκός</i> (Nomădĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Numantia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Numantia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Numantia; a Numantine</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Numantīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nuceria; Nocera, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Nūceria</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Numerius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Numerius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nudium, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nursia; Norcia, Umbria, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Nymphodorus</li></ol><p><i>νῠ́μφη</i> (nŭ́mphē, “young woman”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os): perhaps “giver of maidens”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of Dionysus, Nysan</li></ol><p>From <i>Νῦσᾰ</i> (Nûsă, “the name of several mountains sacred to Dionysus”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonym-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Nola</li></ol><p>Related to <i>Νῶλᾰ</i> (Nôlă, “Nola”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Norbanus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Norbānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noes, a river of Thrace mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nonacris, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nisa, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nisos, Nisus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Nysa (mountainous land where the Hyades raised the infant god Dionysus)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nola (a city in Campania, in southern Italy)</li></ol><p>From Oscan <i>𐌍𐌞𐌅𐌋𐌀</i> (núvla, “New City”), from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;nowolā</i>, from <i>&#42;nowos</i> (“new”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Νῶε</i> (Nôe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Xanthippus</li></ol><p>From <i>ξανθός</i> (xanthós, “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) Xanthus (a river in Lycia, Turkey)</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) Xanthus (a city in Lycia, Turkey)</li></ol><p>From <i>ξανθός</i> (xanthós, “yellow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Xerxes, from Old Persian, the name of two kings of Persia.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎧𐏁𐎹𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠</i> (Xšayāršā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Utnapishtim</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sumerian <i>𒍣𒌓𒋤𒁺</i> (ṣi₂-u₄-sud-ra₂ /⁠Ziusudra⁠/) through Akkadian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xandicus, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to April</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Xanthias, commonly given to slaves in the plays of Aristophanes</li></ol><p>From <i>ξᾰνθός</i> (xănthós, “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Xanthippe</li></ol><p>From <i>ξᾰνθός</i> (xănthós, “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Xenias</li></ol><p>From <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “stranger”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Doric form of <i>Ξενοκλείδης</i> (Xenokleídēs)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ξενοκλῆς</i> (Xenoklês) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δᾱς</i> (-ĭ́dās, masculine patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Xenocleides</li></ol><p>From <i>Ξενοκλῆς</i> (Xenoklês) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, masculine patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Xenocles</li></ol><p>From <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “foreign”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Xenocrates</li><li>in particular, the 4th century <small>BCE</small> philosopher Xenocrates</li></ol><p><i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “foreigner, guest”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Xenophanes</li></ol><p>From <i>ξένος</i> (xénos) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs), literally “strange-looking”</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Xenophonian, Xenophontean</li></ol><p>From <i>Ξενοφῶν</i> (Xenophôn, “Xenophon”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, “-ic”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ξενοφώντειος</i> (Xenophṓnteios)</li></ol><p><i>Ξενοφῶν</i> (Xenophôn, “Xenophon”) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios, adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or by Xenophon, Xenophonian/Xenophontean</li></ol><p><i>Ξενοφῶν</i> (Xenophôn, “Xenophon”) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Xenophon, notably borne by Xenophon of Athens</li></ol><p><i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “guest”) +&lrm; <i>φῶν</i> (phôn, “shining”), the latter contracted from <i>φάων</i> (pháōn), explaining the accentuation, participle of <i>φάω</i> (pháō, “to shine”). The word <i>φῶν</i> (phôn) was indirectly attested in the works of Hesychios of Alexandria, where the accusative form <i>φῶντα</i> (phônta) was equated to <i>λάμποντα</i> (lámponta) from <i>λάμπω</i> (lámpō, “to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xuthus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xyline, Cappadocia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xypete, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Xois, a city in the delta of the Nile</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>οὐρανέ</i> (ourané)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>οὐρανοί</i> (ouranoí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>οὐρανοῖς</i> (ouranoîs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>οὐρανόν</i> (ouranón)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>οὐρανός</i> (ouranós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>οὐρανοῦ</i> (ouranoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>οὐρανούς</i> (ouranoús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>οὐρανῷ</i> (ouranōî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>οὐρανῶν</i> (ouranôn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Oebares, from Old Persian, a Persian officer of Cyrus the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Vahubarah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Oedipus</li></ol><p>Literally, “with a swollen foot”, showing the regular Caland system change */ro/ → */i/, as if from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂oydros</i>, whence also Old High German <i>eittar</i> (“pus”), + <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”). Compare <i>οἰδέω</i> (oidéō, “to swell”), from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a son of Oecles</li></ol><p>From <i>Οἰκλῆς</i> (Oiklês) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Oecles</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>οἶς</i> (oîs, “sheep”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “famous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Oileus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Oeneus</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oeniadae; Oiniades, Aetolia-Acarnania, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Οἰνιάδης</i> (Oiniádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oeniades</li></ol><p>From <i>Οἰνίᾱς</i> (Oiníās) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oenotria</li></ol><p>From <i>Οἴνωτρος</i> (Oínōtros, “Oenotrus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oenoanda, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol><p>Of Anatolian origin and related to Hittite <i>Wiyanawanda</i>, its meaning is connected with Hittite <i>𒃾𒅖</i> (wiyanis, “wine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>any one of a number of cities named Oenoe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oenomaus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oenophyta</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰνόφῠτος</i> (oinóphŭtos, “planted or grown with vines”), from <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>φῠτόν</i> (phŭtón, “plant”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oenone, a woman in Greek mythology</li><li>Oenone, the older name for the island of Aegina</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oesyma, Thrace, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Γοιτόσῠρος</i> (Goitósŭros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oechalia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Oeagrus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oenotrus</li></ol><p>The Italic tribe of the Oenotrians has been linked to both Oenotrus and the word <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”), supposedly referring to the fertile vineyards of southern Italy, but it is unclear if the connection stems from folk etymology. Also compare <i>οἴνωτρον</i> (oínōtron, “vine stake”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Oeta</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oetylus; Oitylo, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Valens</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Vandal</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Varro</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Varrō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vindex</li></ol><p>From Gaulish or Latin <i>Vindex</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valerius; a member of the Roman Valeria gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Valerius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valentinian, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Valentīniānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Valeria; a female member of the Roman Valeria gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Valeria</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vatinius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Vatīnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Venedi, a tribe of Sarmatia</li></ol><p>Compare Latin <i>Venedī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vergilius; Virgilius; Virgil</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Vergilius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vergilia; Virgilia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Vergilia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vespasian</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Vespasiānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Veii (a city in Etruria)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Vēiī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Uthman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عُثْمَان (ʕuṯmān). Cognate with Greek <i>Οσμάν</i> (Osmán).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Visigoth</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vitellius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Vitellius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ucalegon, one of the friends of Priam.</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐκ</i> (ouk, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓λέγω</i> (ălégō, “to mind”), literally “doesn't worry”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Doric) Odysseus, Ulysses</li></ol><p>According to Visser, a dialectal (probably Doric) form of <i>Ὀδυσσεύς</i> (Odusseús). Probably from a Pre-Greek source tentatively reconstructed by Beekes as *Od/luk<sup>y</sup>eu. Compare Etruscan <i>𐌖𐌈𐌖𐌆𐌄</i> (uθuze).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Volumnia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Volumnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Volsci; a Volscian</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Volscī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Urania</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρᾰνός</i> (ourănós, “sky; heaven”) or <i>Οὐρᾰνός</i> (Ourănós, “Uranus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the gods</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) the Titans</li></ol><p><i>Οὐρανός</i> (Ouranós, “Uranus”) +&lrm; <i>-ίωνες</i> (-íōnes, patronymic suffix), thus literally ‘children of Uranus’</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Urban or Urbane</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Urbānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uriel.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אוּרִיאֵל</i> (ʾūrīʾēl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Vicenza (Italy)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Varus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Vārus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Ὄλυμπος</i> (Ólumpos)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὄλυμπος</i> (Ólumpos) by lengthening of the first syllable for metrical purposes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to/towards Olympus</li></ol><p><i>Οὔλυμπον</i> (Oúlumpon) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Utii, an ancient Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hun</li></ol><p>Borrowed through Middle Iranian. See Huns.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 5 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. An abbreviation of πέντε (pénte, “five”). Equivalent to the Roman digit V.</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤐</i> (p, “pē”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πατήρ</i> (patḗr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πνεῦμα</i> (pneûma)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πνεύμασι</i> (pneúmasi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πνεύματι</i> (pneúmati)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πνεύματος</i> (pneúmatos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πνεύματος</i> (pneúmatos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πνεύμασι</i> (pneúmasi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πνεύματα</i> (pneúmata)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πνευμάτων</i> (pneumátōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πατήρ</i> (patḗr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πατέρα</i> (patéra)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πατρί</i> (patrí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πατρός</i> (patrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πνεύματος</i> (pneúmatos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pagrae; Bagras, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pacorus, a king of the Parthians</li></ol><p>From Parthian <i>𐭐𐭊𐭅𐭓</i> (pkwr /⁠Pakur⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pallas, the name of a number of male figures in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō, “to poise, sway, or swing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paltus; Arab al-Mulk, Syria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Pamisus, Messenia, Greece</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-ῑσο-</i> is found in other river names, like <i>Κηφισός</i> (Kēphisós) and <i>Ἰλισός</i> (Ilisós), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pamphilus</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “beloved, dear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Pamphyli, a Doric tribe</li><li>an inhabitant of Pamphylia</li></ol><p><i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>φῡλή</i> (phūlḗ, “tribe”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Pan, the Greek god of shepherds and nature</li></ol><p>The deity has been argued to be of Indo-European origin; see Sanskrit <i>पूषन्</i> (pūṣan, “Pushan, literally ‘nourisher’”), a Vedic god associated with guarding and multiplying cattle, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péh₂-us-ōn</i> from <i>&#42;peh₂-</i>.</p><p>In the <i>Homeric Hymn to Hermes</i>, it is claimed that <i>Πάν</i> (Pán) derives from <i>πᾶν</i> (pân), neuter nominative singular of <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “every”) because “he delighted all”, but the stems differ (cf. gen. <i>πάντος</i> vs. <i>Πάνος</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panactum</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pandarus</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, flay”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Pandocus, a Trojan wounded by Ajax</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “to accept, receive”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pandrosus</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>δρόσος</i> (drósos, “dew”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panemus, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to June</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Panthous, son of Othrys and elder of Troy</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “quick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panormus (an ancient city in Sicily, situated on the northern coast of the island, now Palermo)</li><li>Panormus; Bandırma, Marmara, Turkey</li><li>Panormus (a city in Achaea; modern Agios Vasileios)</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>ὅρμος</i> (hórmos, “port”), because of the good natural harbor.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pansa</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pansa</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Papremis, Lower Egypt, Egypt</li></ol><p>From Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paralus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Parthian</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎱𐎼𐎰𐎺</i> (p-r-θ-v /⁠Parθavaʰ⁠/, “Parthia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Parium, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Paris</li></ol><p>Probably from Luwian <i>𒉺𒊑𒍣𒋾𒅖</i> (Parizitis), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i> (foremost) + 𒍣𒋾𒅖 (“man”). Compare Hittite <i>𒉺𒊑𒇽</i> (Pa.ri.LÚ /⁠Parizitis⁠/). By folk etymology connected with <i>πήρα</i> (pḗra, “bag, pouch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Parmys, from Old Persian, a Persian princess and granddaughter of Cyrus the Great</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Pā̆rmūš</i>, of unclear meaning. Compare with <i>Παρμίσης</i> (Parmísēs), itself from Old Median <i>&#42;Pā̆rmicah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Parnes; Mount Parnitha</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Patara, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Patmos, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Patras, Achaea, Greece</li></ol><p>Named after <i>Πᾰτρεύς</i> (Pătreús, “Patreus”), the city's legendary founder</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Patroclus</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr, “father”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Compare <i>Κλεόπατρος</i> (Kleópatros, “Cleopater”), which contains the same components in the opposite order.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paphos, Cyprus</li><li>A given name of the son of Pygmalion</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps a given name derived from <i>πάθος</i> (páthos, “Passion”). Traditional etymology links the town's name to the Greek goddess of passion <i>Ἀφροδίτη</i> (Aphrodítē), as Paphos was the son of Pygmalion, a worshiper of the goddess.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paches</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cape Pachynus; Capo Passero, Sicily, Italy</li></ol><p>Certainly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peligni, an Italic tribe settled on the Apennines</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Pelignī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pelops</li></ol><p>Most likely <i>πέλλος</i> (péllos, “dark”) + <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peltae, Phrygia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pergamon; Pergamum; Bergama, İzmir, Turkey</li></ol><p>Probably of Pre-Greek origin and related to <i>πύργος</i> (púrgos) if likewise.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Πέργᾰμον</i> (Pérgămon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perga, Pamphylia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perinthus; Heraclea, Thrace; Marmara Ereğli, Tekirdağ, Turkey</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;perunt</i> (“rocky”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perses, the son of Perseus and Andromeda</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿</i> (p-a-r-s /⁠pārsa⁠/).</p><p>Connected by folk etymology to the verb <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō, “to sack, ravage, destroy (a city); to slay (a person)”), due to the fact that ancient Greek-Persian relationships involved major military conflicts. This etymology is also found in Homer's Odyssey. This is also the explanation proferred for the name of the Titan, being the embodiment of destruction.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pegasus</li></ol><p>Traditionally associated with <i>πηγή</i> (pēgḗ, “spring, fountain, fountain fed by a spring”), especially used to denote springs of Ocean, where Perseus killed Medusa, from whose blood Pegasus sprang.</p><p>One alternative etymology of this name is Luwian (pihassas, “lightning”), and <i>Pihassassi</i>, a local Luwian-Hittite name in southern Cilicia of a weather god represented with thunder and lightning. The proponents of this etymology adduce Pegasus' role, reported as early as Hesiod, as the bringer of thunderbolts to Zeus. It was first suggested in 1952 and remains widely accepted, but Robin Lane Fox (2009) has criticized it as implausible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pedasa</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pedasus, the name of two different towns mentioned in the Iliad</li><li>Pedasus, the name of a Trojan soldier mentioned in the Iliad</li><li>Pedasus, the name of a horse mentioned in the Iliad</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peleces, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pelion, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pigres</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pieres, a Thracian tribe settled in Pieria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pinara, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pindar</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Pindus</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek, usually something like Mount (Pre-Greek etymon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pistyrus, Thrace, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Paionian</li><li>an inhabitant of Paionia</li></ol><p>Folk etymology / Greek mythology derived the name from Paeon of Elis, the son of Endymion. The Paionians' real origins are murkier and their language is poorly attested, with theories including Thracian, Illyrian, or native Greek. More at Paeonia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pagasae; Pagases, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Πᾰγγαῖος</i> (Păngaîos, “Pangaion hills”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pangaion hills</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>Γαῖᾰ</i> (Gaîă, “Gaia”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a male given name, equivalent to English Pancras</li></ol><p>From <i>παγκρατής</i> (pankratḗs, “all-powerful”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pagondas, a Theban general</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Padagus, from Proto-Sarmatian</li></ol><p>From a Proto-Sarmatian cognate of Old Persian <i>&#42;Pādakah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Padaei, an ancient Indian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paeonia (the land of the Paeonians, a people inhabiting between the lands of Macedonians, Thracians, and Illyrians)</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>Παίων</i> (Paíōn)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-i-eh₂</i>Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-íā</i>Ancient Greek <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)Ancient Greek <i>Παιονία</i>From <i>Παίων</i> (Paíōn, “Paeonian”) + <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paesus, Troad, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Pactolus; the river Sart Çayı</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pactye, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palatine Hill, Rome</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Palātium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palaemon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Palaeologus, a Byzantine dynasty</li></ol><p>From <i>παλαιός</i> (palaiós, “old”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “word”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palestine, the Land of Israel, Syria Palaestina (a historical region of Western Asia)</li></ol><p><div>Perhaps from Hebrew <i>פְּלֶשֶׁת</i> (pəléšeṯ), Egyptian <i>pwrꜣsꜣtj</i> <div style="display:inline;"><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./78c461f0.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Q3" alt="glyph" height="37"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./d0914c94.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z7" alt="glyph" height="42"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./71f37cec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="D21" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./b207efda.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z1" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./b207efda.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z1" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./13c76e46.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Aa18" alt="glyph" height="72"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./490fdc4a.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="U33" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="M17" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./df0a418b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="U28" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></div>, or Akkadian <i>𒆳𒉺𒆷𒊍𒌓</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>pa-la-as-tú /⁠Palastu⁠/), <i>𒆳𒉿𒇺𒋾</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>pi-liš-ti /⁠Pilišti⁠/), <i>𒆳𒉿𒇺𒋫</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>pi-lis-ta /⁠Pilista⁠/), <i>𒆳𒉿𒇺𒌅</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>pi-liš-tu /⁠Pilištu⁠/) being reshaped to directly match <i>παλαιστής</i> (palaistḗs, “wrestler”); compare perhaps Hebrew <i>יִשְׂרָאֵל</i> (Yiśrāʾēl), sometimes translated as “he wrestles with God”, therefore a Greek exonym to the region, and the similar adaptation of <i>Ἱερουσᾱλήμ</i> (Hierousālḗm, “Jerusalem”) to the word <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy”). See timeline of the name <i>Palestine</i> on Wikipedia.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palestinian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palaestinus, the ancient name of the river Strymon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Palamedes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paleis (an area of Cephalonia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pataliputra (an ancient city in India, situated at the junction of the rivers Ganges and Erannaboas; modern Patna)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>पाटलिपुत्र</i> (pāṭaliputra), remodeled by folk-etymological association with <i>πάλιν</i> (pálin, “back”) +&lrm; <i>βόθρος</i> (bóthros, “trench”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>Παλλάδα</i> (Palláda)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>Παλλάδι</i> (Palládi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pallantium, Latium</li><li>Palatine Hill, Rome</li></ol><p>From <i>Πᾰ́λλᾱς</i> (Pắllās, “Pallas”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pallas (Athena)</li><li>Pallas, daughter of Triton</li></ol><p>Likely derived from πάλλω (pállō), meaning "to brandish", given to Athena as an epithet of "she who brandishes" a spear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pallene, one of the Alkyonides in Greek mythology</li><li>one of a number of places called Pallene or Pallini</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Pallas (male name); a Pallantid</li></ol><p>From <i>Πᾰ́λλᾱς</i> (Pắllās, “Pallas”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Pallene or Pallini</li></ol><p>From <i>Παλλήνη</i> (Pallḗnē, “Pallene or Pallini”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palmyrena (a historical region of Syria)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Palmyrene</li></ol><p><i>Παλμύρα</i> (Palmúra, “Palmyra”) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Palmyra</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>Παλείς</i> (Paleís)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>πᾰμ-</i> (păm-) +&lrm; <i>μένω</i> (ménō) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a ward (ἄμφοδον) of the city of Oxyrhynchus, Roman Egypt</li></ol><p>Literally, “Pammenes’ Garden”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pamphylia, an Ancient region in Anatolia</li></ol><p>From <i>Πάμφῡλος</i> (Pámphūlos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “every”) +&lrm; <i>φῡλή</i> (phūlḗ, “tribe”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pamphylian</li></ol><p><i>Παμφῡλίᾱ</i> (Pamphūlíā) +&lrm; <i>-κός</i> (-kós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>all Greeks, the Greek World</li></ol><p><i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>Ἕλλην</i> (Héllēn, “Greek”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panaetius of Rhodes (Stoic philosopher)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Panathenaea</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>Ἀθήνη</i> (Athḗnē, “Athena”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Panathenaic</li></ol><p><i>Παναθήναια</i> (Panathḗnaia) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>all the Achaeans</li></ol><p><i>παν-</i> (pan-) +&lrm; <i>Ἀχαιοί</i> (Akhaioí)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pandion</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pandosia, Epirus, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Pandora</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panthialaei, a tribe of Persia mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Pannonia (a geographic region and former province of the Roman Empire in central-eastern Europe, existing as a single province from c. 9 AD to 107 AD and as multiple related provinces until 433 AD; located in modern western Hungary, western Slovakia, eastern Austria, northern Croatia, northwestern Serbia, northern Slovenia and northern Bosnia and Herzegovina)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pannonia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panopeus, Phocis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pantagnotos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pantimathi, a Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus, settled on the river Oxus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Pantacyas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) a male given name meaning “all-merciful”, equivalent to English Pantaleon or Panteleimon</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰντελεήμων</i> (pănteleḗmōn), from <i>πᾰντο-</i> (pănto-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλεήμων</i> (eleḗmōn, “merciful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panticapaeum; Kerch, Crimea, Ukraine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Pantikapa</i> (literally “fish-path”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Panyasus, a river of Macedonia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paxos, Ionian Islands, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Papaeus, the Scythian name of Zeus</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Pāpayi</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Papirius</li><li>a member of the Roman Papiria gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Papīrius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paraetonium; Mersa Matruh, Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paralatae, the self-designation of the Royal Scythians</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Paralātah</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paretaceni, a Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Parthaon</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō, “to destroy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bartın (a river in northern Turkey that flows into the Black Sea)</li></ol><p>From <i>παρθένιος</i> (parthénios, “maidenly, virginal”), from <i>παρθένος</i> (parthénos, “maiden, virgin”), as the river was considered sacred to the virgin goddess Artemis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Parthia, Parthian Empire (a former empire in Central Asia and West Asia, ruled by dynasts from Parthia; the Parthian Empire; the Arsacid Empire)# Parthia (a region in northeastern Iran)</li></ol><p>From <i>Πάρθος</i> (Párthos, “Parthian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Parthenopaeus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a female given name, Parthenope, the name of several figures in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>παρθένος</i> (parthénos, “virgin, maiden”) +&lrm; <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “voice, eye, face”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Parthenon, the famous temple to Athena which resides in Athens.</li></ol><p>From <i>παρθενών</i> (parthenṓn, “women's chamber”), from <i>παρθένος</i> (parthénos, “maid, young woman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Parmises, from Old Median</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;Pā̆rmicah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Parmenides, notably borne by the pre-Socratic philosopher Parmenides of Elea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Parmenion; Parmenio</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Παρνᾱσός</i> (Parnāsós, “Mount Parnassus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Parnassus</li></ol><p>As proposed by Palmer, may be related to Luwian <i>𒈦𒈾𒀭𒍝</i> (parnanza, “house”) (stem parna-) and productive possessive suffix <small>[script needed]</small> (-ašša-). A similar toponym <small>[Anatolian hieroglyphic needed]</small> (pa+ra/i-na-sa), apparently a town in Anatolia, is attested in a hieroglyphic inscription on a Luwian monument found in Karkamıš (A24a).</p><p>However, Beekes argues that according to 6th century encyclopedist Stephanus of Byzantium the mountain had an older name starting from Λ, but this seems to be his folk-etymology explanation that the chest (λάρναξ) which saved Deucalion from the great flood washed up here. Judging by the variation σσ/σ it may be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>Παρνησόνδε</i> (Parnēsónde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to Parnassus</li></ol><p>From <i>Παρνησόν</i> (Parnēsón, accusative of <i>Παρνησός</i> (Parnēsós)) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de, “to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Epic form of <i>Παρνᾱσός</i> (Parnāsós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paroecopolis, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰ́ροικος</i> (pắroikos, “neighbouring”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic) Parrhasia, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Parysatis, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wife of Hystaspes and mother of Darius the Great</li><li>daughter of Artaxerxes I and Andia and wife of her step-brother Darius II and mother of Artaxerxes II and Cyrus the Younger</li><li>youngest daughter of Artaxerxes II, whom Alexander the Great married after Roxane and at the same time as Stateira II</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Parušātiš</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;Parušyātiš</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pasion</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pasargadae</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Pāθragadā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pasicles</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾶσῐ</i> (pâsĭ, “to all”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pasiphae</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾶσῐ</i> (pâsĭ, “to all”) +&lrm; <i>φᾰ́ος</i> (phắos, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Passaron, Epirus, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Patavium; Padua; Padova, Veneto, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Patavium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Name of a Magian, Pseudo-Smerdis's brother</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Pātixšayaθah</i> via an unattested form <i>&#42;Πατιξείθης</i> (&#42;Patixeíthēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Patiramphes, from Old Persian, charioteer of Xerxes I</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Patiramfa</i> (“counterattacker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Patreus, the legendary founder of Patras</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Patras</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>Πάτροκλος</i> (Pátroklos) attested only in the genitive, accusative and vocative singular</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paulinus</li></ol><p>From Latin Paulinus.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pausanias</li></ol><p>From <i>παῦσις</i> (paûsis, “stopping, ending”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνία</i> (anía, “sorrow, grief”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pausi, a tribe dwelling near the Caspian Sea mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paphlagonia (a historical region in northern Asia Minor in modern Turkey, on the shore of the Black Sea, between Bithynia and Pontus)</li></ol><p>From <i>Παφλᾰγών</i> (Paphlăgṓn, “Paphlagonian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Paphlagonia; a Paphlagonian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pachon, the ninth month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to May</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>pꜣ-n-ḫnsw</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paul, a common Roman name, famously held by Paul, the Christian apostle.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Paulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pisander</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “to convince”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “a man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pisaurum; Pesaro, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Pisaurum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Piso; a member of the Roman Piso family</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pīsō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pedaritus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pednelissus, Pisidia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pirene</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pirithous</li></ol><p>Either from:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Πειρῐ́θοος</i> (Peirĭ́thoos, “Pirithous”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Piraeus, Athens, Attica, Greece</li><li>An inhabitant of Piraeus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Piraeus, Athens, Attica, Greece</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>πέρα</i> (péra, “beyond”). The name roughly translates as "the place over the passage."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Peisistratus or <i>Pisistratus</i></li></ol><p>From <i>πεῖσῐς</i> (peîsĭs, “persuasion”) +&lrm; <i>στρατός</i> (stratós, “army”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Pisidice, the name of various individuals of the Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>πεῖσις</i> (peîsis, “persuasion”) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́κη</i> (dĭ́kē, “justice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Peisistratus or Pisistratus; a Peisistratid or Pisistratid</li></ol><p>From <i>Πεισῐ́στρᾰτος</i> (Peisĭ́strătos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pelias</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pelasgia</li></ol><p>From <i>Πελασγός</i> (Pelasgós, “Pelasgian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female Pelasgian</li></ol><p>From <i>Πελασγός</i> (Pelasgós, “Pelasgian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pelasgian</li></ol><p><i>Πελασγίᾱ</i> (Pelasgíā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Pelasgian</li></ol><p>From <i>Πελασγῐ́ᾱ</i> (Pelasgĭ́ā, “Pelasgia”) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female Pelasgian</li></ol><p>From <i>Πελασγῐώτης</i> (Pelasgĭṓtēs, “Pelasgian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Pelasgian</li></ol><p>Likely of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a daughter of Pelias; one of the <i>Peliades</i></li></ol><p>From <i>Πελίᾱς</i> (Pelíās, “Pelias”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰς</i> (-ăs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pelinnaeum, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pellana; Pellene, Laconia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Pellana or Pellene</li></ol><p>From <i>Πελλήνη</i> (Pellḗnē, “Pellana, Pellene”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic and Doric form of <i>Πελοπῐ́δης</i> (Pelopĭ́dēs)</li><li>Pelopidas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Pelops; a Pelopid; one of the Pelopidae</li></ol><p>From <i>Πέλοψ</i> (Pélops) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Peloponnesian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Peloponnese or Peloponnesus</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>Πέλοπος</i> <i>νῆσος</i> <i>(Pélopos nêsos)</i> ("Isle of Pelops").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a harvest festival to Zeus celebrated in Thessaly</li></ol><p>From <i>πελώρῐος</i> (pelṓrĭos, “monstrous; prodigious”), an epithet of Zeus.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pentheus</li></ol><p>From <i>πένθος</i> (pénthos, “grief, sorrow”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Greek island of Skopelos in the ancient times.</li><li>Peparethus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Periander</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peritius, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to January</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Periphas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pergamene</li></ol><p>From <i>Πέργαμον</i> (Pérgamon) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pergase, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perdiccas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Poriclymenus; Periclymenus</li></ol><p><i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “about”) +&lrm; <i>κλῠ́μενος</i> (klŭ́menos, “heard”), participle from <i>κλῠ́ω</i> (klŭ́ō, “hear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pericles</li><li>in particular, the 5th century <small>BCE</small> Athenian statesman Pericles</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “very”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Percote, Troad, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Permessus, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perrhaebia</li></ol><p>From <i>Περραιβός</i> (Perrhaibós, “Perrhaebian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Perrhaebian</li><li>an inhabitant of Perrhaebia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Persian</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persia</li><li>Persis, a given name</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female Persian</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎱𐎠𐎼𐎿</i> (p-a-r-s /⁠Pārsa⁠/) + <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child or descendant of Perseus; one of the Perseids</li></ol><p>From <i>Περσεύς</i> (Perseús) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homeric form of <i>Περσεφόνη</i> (Persephónē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persephone; Proserpina</li></ol><p>Possibly composed of the Proto-Indo-European elements <i>&#42;pers-o-</i> (“sheaf of corn, grain, seed”, whence also Sanskrit <i>पर्ष</i> (parṣá-) and perhaps Latin <i>porrum</i>) +&lrm; <i>&#42;gʷʰn̥-t-</i> (“to strike down, slay”, compare <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “killer”)) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-eh₂</i> (a suffix of feminine agent nouns), thus originally meaning “female thresher of corn”. Compare Rigvedic <i>पर्षान् हन्ति</i> (parṣā́n hanti, “to thresh ears of corn”). Alternatively, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pers-é-bʰ(h₂)n̥t-ih₂</i> (“she who brings the light through”), akin to Albanian <i>Prenda</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perseus, a mythological figure</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Perseus</li></ol><p>Perhaps Pre-Greek, perhaps from <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō).</p><p>More at Perseus.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alcmene (grand-daughter of Perseus)</li><li>a city in Paeonia, named after Perseus of Macedon</li><li>Hecate (daughter of Perses)</li></ol><p>From <i>Περσεύς</i> (Perseús, “Perseus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), literally “sprung of Perseus”.</p><p><i>Πέρσης</i> (Pérsēs, “Perses”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), literally “sprung of Perses”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persia</li></ol><p>From an ellipsis of <i>Περσῐκή γῆ</i> (Persĭkḗ gê, “Persian land”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Persian (from Persia&#59; pertaining to the Persians)</li></ol><p><i>Πέρσης</i> (Pérsēs) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>In Persian, in the language of the Persians</li></ol><p>From <i>Πέρσης</i> (Pérsēs, “Persian”) +&lrm; <i>-ιστί</i> (-istí, “a suffix added to a country adjective to make an adverb indicating use of that specific language”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pertinax</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Pertināx</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peteon (a city in Boeotia district, Greece)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peteos (a character in the <i>Iliad</i>&#58; son of Orneus and father of Menestheus)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peteos (a character in the <i>Iliad</i>&#58; son of Orneus and father of Menestheus)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Petelia, Bruttium, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Pedasa</li></ol><p>From <i>Πήδᾰσᾰ</i> (Pḗdăsă, “Pedasa”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homeric form of <i>Πηλείδης</i> (Pēleídēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(patronymic,&#32;Homeric&#32;epithet of Achilles) Son of Peleus</li></ol><p>From <i>Πηλεύς</i> (Pēleús, “Peleus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́ων</i> (-ī́ōn, “son of”, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(patronymic,&#32;Homeric epithet of Achilles) Son of Peleus</li></ol><p>From <i>Πηλεύς</i> (Pēleús, “Peleus”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, “son of”, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peleus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Πηλείδης</i> (Pēleídēs, “son of Peleus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from Pelion (<i>epithet of the ashen spear given by Chiron to Peleus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Πήλιον</i> (Pḗlion) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pelusium, Lower Egypt</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>pr-jmn</i> (literally “house of Amun”), from <i>pr</i> (“house, temple”) + <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”). More at Pelusium.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Greek mythology) Peneleos, an Achaean soldier in the Trojan War</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Peneus, a river god</li><li>the river Peneus</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pen-</i> (“mire, bog”). Cognate with Latin <i>Pannonia</i>, Old Prussian <i>pannean</i> (“bog”), Middle Irish <i>en</i> (“water”), Dutch <i>veen</i>, Old English <i>fenn</i> (English <i>fen</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Πηνελόπη</i> (Pēnelópē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Penelope</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>πηνέλοψ</i> (pēnélops, “a particolored duck”). The traditional association with <i>πήνη</i> (pḗnē, “thread, bobbin”) and <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “face”) is a folk etymology based on the story of Penelope’s weaving and fails to explain the -ελ-.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pieria, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῑ́ερες</i> (Pī́eres, “Pierians”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pithecusae; Ischia, Campania, Italy</li></ol><p>From the contracted feminine <i>&#42;πῐθηκόεσσᾰ</i> (&#42;pĭthēkóessă), from <i>&#42;πῐθηκόεις</i> (&#42;pĭthēkóeis, “ape-like”), from <i>πῐ́θηκος</i> (pĭ́thēkos, “ape”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Picenum, Marche, Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῑκηνός</i> (Pīkēnós, “Picene”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Piceni; a Picene</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pīcēnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A Roman cognomen — famously held by&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Πόντιος Πιλᾶτος (died AD 37), fifth prefect of the Roman province of Judaea (AD 26–36)</li></ol></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pīlātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ashoka</li></ol><p>Most likely from Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀧𑀺𑀬𑀤𑀲𑀺</i> (piyadasi), from Sanskrit <i>प्रियदर्शी</i> (priyadarśī, “one who looks with kindness; pleasant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Pisa; a Pisan</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῖσᾰ</i> (Pîsă, “Pisa”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Pisidia; a Pisidian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pisidia</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῐσῐ́δης</i> (Pĭsĭ́dēs, “Pisidian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persianː Pissuthnes, a satrap of Lydia and grandson of Darius I</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Pišišyauθnah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pitane, Aeolis, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Pitanate; a person from Pitane</li></ol><p><i>Πιτάνη</i> (Pitánē) +&lrm; <i>-άτης</i> (-átēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pittheus</li><li>An inhabitant of deme of Pittheus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pittacus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plancus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Plancus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Plataea</i>, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plato</li></ol><p>From <i>πλατύς</i> (platús, “broad, wide”, either because of Plato's robust body, or wide forehead or the breadth of his eloquence) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, suffix nominalizing adjectives, the "Strabon Suffix").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Placia, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Placentia; Piacenza, Emilia-Romagna, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Placentia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Πλᾰ́ταιᾰ</i> (Plắtaiă, “Plataea, Boeotia, Greece”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of <i>Plataea</i>; a Plataean</li></ol><p>From <i>Πλᾰ́ταιᾰ</i> (Plắtaiă), <i>Πλᾰταιαί</i> (Plătaiaí, “Plataea”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pleistarchus</li></ol><p>From <i>πλεῖστος</i> (pleîstos, “most, greatest, largest”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Pleiades (star cluster)</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Pleiades (seven daughters of Atlas and Pleione)</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “to sail”) because of the star cluster’s importance in delimiting the sailing season in the Mediterranean Sea: "the season of navigation began with their heliacal rising". The mythological sisters were probably named after the star cluster, rather than vice versa.</p><p>The singular <i>Πλειάς</i> (Pleiás) is sometimes found referring to the star cluster.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) one of the Pleiades; a Pleiad</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) Pleiades (star cluster)</li></ol><p>Singular of <i>Πλειάδες</i> (Pleiádes).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pleistoanax</li></ol><p>From <i>πλεῖστος</i> (pleîstos, “most, greatest, largest”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνᾰξ</i> (ánăx, “lord, master”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Pleistus; the river Xeropotamos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Pleuron, a son of Aetolus</li><li>Pleuron, a city in Aetolia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plemmyrium</li></ol><p>From <i>πλήμμῡρᾰ</i> (plḗmmūră), <i>πλήμῡρᾰ</i> (plḗmūră, “flood-tide, flood”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plinthine, Lower Egypt, Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to Greek Πλούταρχος (Ploútarchos), Latin Plūtarchus, or English Plutarch</li><li><b>Plutarch</b> of Chaeronea, <small>full name</small> <i>Λούκιος Μέστριος <b>Πλούταρχος</b></i> (Loúkios Méstrios <b>Ploútarkhos</b>), <small>romanized</small> Lūcius Mestrius Plūtarchus, <small>lived</small> ca. 46–120 <small>CE</small>. A celebrated Graeco-Roman philosopher, historian, biographer, essayist, and priest at the Temple of Apollo in Delphi; best known for writing his <i>Lives of the Noble Greeks and Romans</i>, a biographical series commonly referred to as <i>Plutarch's Parallel Lives</i>.</li></ol><p><i>πλοῦτος</i> (ploûtos, “riches; Pluto”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plotinus</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>Plōtīnus</i>, diminutive of <i>Plōtus</i>, a late form of Latin <i>Plautus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) The Holy Ghost, the Holy Spirit.</li></ol><p>From <i>πνεῦμᾰ</i> (pneûmă, “breath, vital force, soul”) + <i>ἅγῐος</i> (hágĭos, “holy”), a calque of Hebrew <i>רוּחַ הַקֹּדֶשׁ</i> (rū́aḥ haqqṓḏeš).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Pnyx, Athens, Attica, Greece</li></ol><p>Since ancient times it has been claimed the word is derived from <i>πυκνός</i> (puknós, “close-packed; dense; thick”), but the <i>Oxford English Dictionary</i> considers this a folk etymology with no evidence supporting it.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Podaleirios, son of Asclepius and brother of Machaon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Stoa Poikile on the Agora in Athens, where Zeno of Citium founded the school of stoicism</li></ol><p>Literally, “variegated porch”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polichna, Troad, Turkey</li><li>Polichna, Messenia, Greece</li><li>Polichna, Laconia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>πολίχνη</i> (políkhnē, “small city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polydamas (Trojan hero)</li><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “much, many”) +&lrm; <i>δαμάζω</i> (damázō, “to tame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polydeuces; Pollux</li></ol><p><i>πολύς</i> (polús, “much”) +&lrm; <i>δεῦκος</i> (deûkos, “sweet”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Polycrates</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Polymestor</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>μήστωρ</i> (mḗstōr, “adviser”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polyhymnia</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>&#42;Πολῠῠ́μνῐᾰ</i> (&#42;Polŭŭ́mnĭă), from <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́μνος</i> (hŭ́mnos, “song”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polynices</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús) +&lrm; <i>νεῖκος</i> (neîkos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Polyxena</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ́ξενος</i> (polŭ́xenos, “entertaining many guests”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē), from <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “guest; stranger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Polyxo, the name of several figures in Greek mythology</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Polypoetes</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “much”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, agentive suffix), the literally “the one who makes much”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uncontracted form of Πολύμνια</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ́ς</i> (polŭ́s, “many”) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́μνος</i> (hŭ́mnos, “hymn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polybius</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>βίος</i> (bíos, “life”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Polygnotus</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>γνωτός</i> (gnōtós, “known”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Polydorus</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Polyhymnia, muse of sacred hymns and poetry</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>Πολῠῠ́μνῐᾰ</i> (Polŭŭ́mnĭă), from <i>πολῠ́ς</i> (polŭ́s, “many”) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́μνος</i> (hŭ́mnos, “hymn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Polyphemus</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-) +&lrm; <i>φήμη</i> (phḗmē) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), literally “many-voiced”, “much spoken of”, or “abounding in songs and legends”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pompeii; Pompei, Campania, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pompeiī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pompey</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pompeius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pompilius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pompilius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pomponia; a female member of the Roman Pomponia gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pompōnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pomponius; a member of the Roman Pomponia gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pompōnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazelnut</li></ol><p>From <i>Ποντικόν</i> (Pontikón, “Pontic”) + <i>κάρυον</i> (káruon, “nut”). From the nut's indigenousness and abundance in Pontus, a former Hellenic kingdom in northern Anatolia along the southern shore of the Black Sea.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pontic</li></ol><p>From <i>Πόντος</i> (Póntos, “Black Sea, Pontus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic: <i>forming adjectives</i>”), from <i>πόντος</i> (póntos, “sea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Publicius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Publicius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Publicola</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pūblicola</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pordoselene, Aeolis, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lars Porsena</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Porsina</i>, <i>Porsena</i>, from Etruscan.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Posidippus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Ποσειδῶν</i> (Poseidôn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Posideon: the sixth month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around December and January</li></ol><p>From <i>Ποσειδῶν</i> (Poseidôn, “Poseidon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Posidonius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Poseidon</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀡𐀮𐀅𐀃𐀚</i> (po-se-da-o-ne); possibly from a vocative <i>&#42;Πότ(ε)ι Δᾶς</i> (&#42;Pót(e)i Dâs) from <i>πόσις</i> (pósis, “master, husband”) and <i>&#42;Δα</i> (&#42;Da) (<i>Γῆ</i> (Gê), see <i>Δημήτηρ</i> (Dēmḗtēr) and Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀚𐀯𐀅𐀃𐀚</i> (e-ne-si-da-o-ne), which would be cognate with <i>ἐννοσίγαιος</i> (ennosígaios)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Postumius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Postumius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Potidaea</li></ol><p>From Doric <i>Ποτειδάν</i> (Poteidán).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>Ποτῑ́δαιᾰ</i> (Potī́daiă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ποτῑδαιᾱ́της</i> (Potīdaiā́tēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Potidaea; a Potidaean</li></ol><p>From <i>Ποτῑ́δαιᾰ</i> (Potī́daiă, “Potidaea”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Πολυδάμᾱς</i> (Poludámās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pudens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Pudēns</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Byzantine,&#32;sports) the Greens, one of the usual chariot-racing factions of Rome, Constantinople, and other major cities of the empire</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>prasinī</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>πρᾰ́σῐνος</i> (prắsĭnos, “leek-green”), from <i>πράσον</i> (práson, “leek”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos, “-ine: <i>forming adjectives</i>”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Priam</li></ol><p>From Luwian <i>𒉺𒊑𒀀𒈬𒀀</i> (Pa-ri-a-mu-a-, “exceptionally courageous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Priapus</li></ol><p>(This etymology is missing or incomplete. Please add to it, or discuss it at the Etymology scriptorium. )</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Praeneste, Latium; Palestrina, Lazio, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Praeneste</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Praeneste or Palestrina; a Praenestine</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Praenestīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Praxagoras</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾶξις</i> (prâxis, “deed, work”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́, “marketplace”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās, masculine noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li><i>In particular</i>: Praxiteles, Athenian sculptor from the Classical period.</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾶξις</i> (prâxis, “deed, work”) +&lrm; <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “fulfillment, accomplishment”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Praxiphanes</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾶξις</i> (prâxis, “deed, work”) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs, “seeming, appearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prasiae, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prepesinthos; Despotiko, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Prexaspes, from Old Median, a commander of the Persian fleet</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Pr̥kšāspa</i> (“having strong horses”) or <i>&#42;Parušaspa</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Priene, Ionia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Priam; a Priamid</li></ol><p>From <i>Πρῐ́ᾰμος</i> (Prĭ́ămos, “Priam”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Priene</li></ol><p>From <i>Πριήνη</i> (Priḗnē, “Priene”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Proculeius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Proculēius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Procles</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”). Cognate to Sanskrit <i>प्रश्रवस्</i> (praśravas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Procrustes</li></ol><p>From <i>προκρούω</i> (prokroúō, “stretch”) +&lrm; <i>-στης</i> (-stēs, “-er”, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Proconnesus; Marmara Island, Turkey</li></ol><p>Possibly a compound of <i>πρόξ</i> (próx, “roe deer”) +&lrm; <i>νῆσος</i> (nêsos, “island”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Procopius</i> or <i>Prokopios</i>:</li></ol><p>From Koine <i>προκοπ(ή)</i>&nbsp;f (prokop(ḗ), “progress forward to prosperity; advancement toward success; improvement by diligence”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Procyon, a star in Canis Minor.</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Prometheus</li></ol><p>From <i>προμηθής</i> (promēthḗs, “forethinking”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sea of Marmara</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before; in front of”) +&lrm; <i>πόντος</i> (póntos, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Protothyes, from Scythian, a king of the Scythians</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Scythian, possibly from:</p><p>Parallel borrowing with Neo-Assyrian <i>𒁹𒁇𒋫𒌅𒀀</i> (<sup>m</sup>par₂-ta-tu-a /⁠Partatua⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prusias</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Proetus</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prusa; Bursa, Turkey</li></ol><p>Named after Prusias (Προυσίας (Prousías)), an ancient king of Bithynia</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Protagoras</li></ol><p>From <i>πρωτο-</i> (prōto-, “first”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγορᾱ́</i> (agorā́, “assembly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Protesilaus</li></ol><p>From <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”) +&lrm; <i>ἕσῐς</i> (hésĭs, “sending forth”) +&lrm; <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Proteus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Prodicus</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-, “very”) +&lrm; <i>δῐκή</i> (dĭkḗ, “justice”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Prothous, one of the Greek leaders in the Trojan War and commander of the Magnesians</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “quick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Proclus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Proculus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Procne</li></ol><p>From <i>περκνός</i> (perknós, “dark-spotted”), used to refer to eagles, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i>, <i>&#42;perḱ-</i> (“motley, coloured”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Procris</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Proschium, Aetolia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pras, Phthiotis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pteleon, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pteria (an ancient city in Cappadocia, modern Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>history</i>) Ptolemaic; pertaining to Ptolemy, the Ptolemies dynasty, or the Hellenistic <i>Ptolemaic Kingdom</i> of Ancient Egypt (305 BC – 30 BC).</li></ol><p>From <i>Πτολεμαῖος</i> (Ptolemaîos, “Ptolemy”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ptolemy, equivalent to English Ptolemy</li></ol><p>From <i>πτόλεμος</i> (ptólemos, “battle, war”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Ptoion, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyanepsion: the fourth month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around October and November</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῠᾰνέψῐᾰ</i> (Pŭănépsĭă), a festival dedicated to Apollo, in which a dish of beans was cooked and offered.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pythia: priestess of Apollo at Delphi, who uttered prophetic oracles</li></ol><p><i>Πῡθώ</i> (Pūthṓ, “Pytho, Delphi”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, feminine adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Pythagorean</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῡθᾰγόρᾱς</i> (Pūthăgórās, “Pythagoras”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pythagoras, notably borne by the pre-Socratic philosopher Pythagoras of Samos</li></ol><p>Aristippus explained his name by saying, "He spoke (-agor-) the truth no less than did the Pythian (Pyth-)", thus referring his name to <i>Πυθία</i> (Puthía, “Apollonian oracle of Delphi”) and <i>ἀγορεύω</i> (agoreúō, “to speak publicly in the agora”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pythocles</li></ol><p>From <i>Πυθώ</i> (Puthṓ, “Python”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “glory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pythonides</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῡθώ</i> (Pūthṓ, “Delphi; Python”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, “son of”, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pythodorus</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῡθώ</i> (Pūthṓ, “Pytho; Python”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pytho; the ancient name of Delphi</li><li>Python, the monstrous snake said to have been slain by Pythian Apollo at Delphi</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewb-ṓ</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰewb-</i> (“depths, hollow, deep, bottom”), a reference to the monsters who inhabited the caverns, but it has also been linked to <i>πύθω</i> (púthō, “to rot, to decay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pylades</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pylene, Aetolia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Pyrenees mountains</li></ol><p>Said by Herodotus and Pliny to be from Celtic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyrilampes</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>λάμπω</i> (lámpō, “to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Polybus — famously held by&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a son of King Priam’s counsellor, Antenor, killed in the Trojan War by Neoptolemus</li><li>an Egyptian</li><li>the father of the Ithacan nobleman, Eurymachus</li><li>a suitor of Penelope (the wife of Odysseus)</li><li>a Phaeacian</li></ol></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”), literally “having many cattle”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Black Sea (an inland sea bound by Southeastern Europe in the west, Eastern Europe in the north and northeast, and Turkey in the south)</li></ol><p>Literally, “hospitable sea”; a euphemistic alteration of the older name <i>Πόντος Ἄξεινος</i> (Póntos Áxeinos, literally “inhospitable sea”), itself an alteration under folk etymology of an Iranian name derived from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;axšáyHnah</i> (“green, blue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Publius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Publius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pygela, Ionia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pydna, Pieria, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Python (earth-dragon of Delphi)</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῡθώ</i> (Pūthṓ) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Pylian, person from Pylos</li></ol><p><i>Πύλος</i> (Púlos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to Pylos</li></ol><p>From <i>Πύλον</i> (Púlon), accusative of <i>Πύλος</i> (Púlos, “Pylos”), + <i>-δε</i> (-de, “-wards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pylos, Messenia, Greece</li><li>Pylus, a figure in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀢𐀫</i> (pu-ro)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyxae, Kos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Pyramus; the river Ceyhan</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyrasus, Phthiotis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyrrha</li></ol><p>From <i>Πῠ́ρρος</i> (Pŭ́rrhos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pyrrhus</li></ol><p>From <i>πῠρρός</i> (pŭrrhós, “flame-colored, yellowish-red”), from <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pisa (ancient place situated in ancient Elis, Greece)</li><li>Pisa (city on the mouth of the Arno in Tuscany, central Italy)</li></ol><p>From Etruscan.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rarus (father of Triptolemus)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A counter for staters. An abbreviation of στατήρ (statḗr). Used as a tally mark, as in ΣΣΣΣ (4 staters) and Τ𐅐𐅏ΣΣΣ (1 talent 18 staters).</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤔</i> (š, “šīn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σῶτερ</i> (sôter)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σωτήρ</i> (sōtḗr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σωτῆρα</i> (sōtêra)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σωτῆρι</i> (sōtêri)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σωτῆρος</i> (sōtêros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σταυρέ</i> (stauré)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σταυρόν</i> (staurón)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σταυρός</i> (staurós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σταυροῦ</i> (stauroû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>σταυρῷ</i> (staurōî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Aramaic</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>סָבָא</i> (sāḇā, “old man, grandfather”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sagra, a river of Bruttium, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sadocus, a Thracian king and son of Sitalces</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Sacae; a Saka</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salvius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Salvius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sale, Thrace, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Salii</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Salius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Same, an island in the Ionian Sea, near Ithaca and Cephalonia, mentioned in the <i>Odyssey</i>; its exact identity is uncertain.</li></ol><p>Probably from the same source as <i>Σάμος</i> (Sámos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Samos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samos, Northern Aegean Islands, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰ́μος</i> (sắmos, “seaside hill, dune, height”), derived from the Phoenician word <i>𐤔𐤌𐤀</i> (sama, “high”). See also English <i>Samian</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sandanus, a river flowing near Olynthus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sane; Sani, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Saos, in Samothrace; now called Fengari</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>σαός</i> (saós, “safe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sapires, an ancient people settled between Colchis and Media</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>שָׂרָה</i> (sārā́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Serapis</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>wsjr-ḥp</i>, from <i>wsjr</i> (“Osiris”) + <i>ḥp</i> (“Apis”). Equivalent to a combination of <i>Ὄσιρις</i> (Ósiris) and <i>Ἆπις</i> (Âpis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sardis, Lydia; Sart, Manisa, Turkey</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Lydian <i>𐤮𐤱𐤠𐤭</i> (sfar).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Σᾰ́ρδεις</i> (Sắrdeis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarepta</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>צָרְפַת</i> (ṣārĕp̄aṯ) or a cognate in a different Semitic language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Sarno, Campania, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Sarnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σᾱ́ρᾱ</i> (Sā́rā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarta, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Satala, Lesser Armenia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Satrae, a Thracian people settled between the rivers Nestus and Strymon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sais, Egypt</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Late Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">z&#58;zA</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9d93c55f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="niwt" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>zꜣy</i>, “Sais”), from earlier <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">z&#58;zA</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9d93c55f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="niwt" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>zꜣw</i>), probably ultimately from the verb <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./1778f851.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A47" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./dc43dd89.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A24" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>zꜣw</i>, “to guard, to protect”).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Selagus, a character of the Iliad</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Selge, Pisidia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Seleucus</li></ol><p>Probably from Aeolian <i>ζάλευκος</i> (záleukos, “very white”) [with -ᾱ-!], from regular Attic δι⁽ά⁾-λευκός [with -ῐ-, -ᾰ-]</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sentius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Sentius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A masculine praenomen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Sextus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σάρᾱπις</i> (Sárāpis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Servius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Servius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Etruscan</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sergius</i>, possibly from Etruscan.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seriphus; Serifos, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Serrheum</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sesostris</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Set (an Egyptian god)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">t&#58;X</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i>.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shem, one of the sons of Noah.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>שֵׁם</i> (šem).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sestius</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Sestus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sestius</i>.</p><p>From <i>Σηστός</i> (Sēstós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sibyl</li></ol><p>Possibly from Doric Greek <i>Σίοβολλα</i> (Síobolla), akin to Attic <i>Θεοβούλη</i> (Theoboúlē, “divine will”), derived from <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) + <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ, “volition, will”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Singus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sigeum</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Signia; Segni, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Signia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sidyma, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sicas, a Lycian man mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sikinos, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σῑλᾶς</i> (Sīlâs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Silbium, Phrygia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Simon or Simo, a common Ancient Greek name held by one of the Telchines and often used as a Greek counterpart to the Hebrew שמעון (cf. <i>Σῠμεών</i> (Sŭmeṓn)).</li><li>The name of a throw of the dice.</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>σῑμός</i> (sīmós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sindi, an ancient people settled on the shores of the Black Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sindus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indus (a river in Pakistan and India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>सिन्धु</i> (síndhu). Doublet of <i>Ἰνδός</i> (Indós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sinis</li></ol><p>From <i>σῑ́νομαι</i> (sī́nomai, “to plunder; to pillage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sinnaca, Babylonia, Iraq</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Sipylus; Spil Dağı</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of Sipylus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siromus, king of Tyrus and father of Matten</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sisyphus</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin. Possibilities include:</p><p>Possible cognates include Proto-Slavic&#32;<i>&#42;sypati</i>&#32;(“to strew, to drop”) and Lithuanian <i>sū́puoti</i> (“to rock, cradle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siphnus; Sifnos, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Sais; a Saite</li></ol><p>From <i>Σάϊς</i> (Sáïs, “Sais”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sabazios, a Phrygian deity, whose mysteries resembled those of Bacchus</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Bacchus</li><li>(<i>adjectival</i>) Bacchic</li></ol></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Sabazios</li></ol><p>Unknown. From Phrygian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Sabaoth (<i>epithet of the God Yahweh</i>)</li></ol><p>Transliteration of Biblical Hebrew <i>צְבָאוֹת</i> (ṣvā'ōṯ, literally “powers”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sabinus, mythological ancestor of the Sabines</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Sabine</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sabīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sagartii, an ancient Iranian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sagalassus, Pisidia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sangarius, now the Sakarya River</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sanchuniathon (a Phoenician author)</li></ol><p>From Phoenician.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>typically in plural</i>) Sadducee</li></ol><p>Apparently borrowed from Hebrew <i>צדוקי</i> (ts'dokí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sadyattes</li></ol><p>From Lydian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Saeprus; Flumendosa River, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Saitapharnes or Saetapharnes, from Proto-Sarmatian, the name of a Sarmatian or Scythian king</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Sarmatian, with the second from <i>&#42;fārnē</i> (“glory, splendour”), from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;fārnē</i> (“glory, splendour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sacaraucae (an ancient Saka/Scythian tribe from Central Asia, known to have migrated into Bactria and later into Gandhara)</li></ol><p>From a Proto-Saka name <i>&#42;Sakā raukā</i>, composed of <i>&#42;Sakā</i> (“Sacae, Scythians”, cf. Old Persian <i>𐎿𐎣𐎠</i> (Sakā)) + <i>&#42;rauk</i>, a cognate with Khotanese <i>rūkya</i> (“commander, lord”); ultimately from an earlier Proto-Saka form <i>&#42;Sakā mravakā</i> (“Saka lords, Saka kings”).</p><p>Both Proto-Saka <i>&#42;rauk</i> and Khotanese <i>rūkya</i> (“commander, lord”) are derived from an earlier Proto-Saka <i>&#42;raukya</i>, itself from an even earlier <i>&#42;mravaka</i>/<i>&#42;mrauka</i>, from the root <i>&#42;mrav-</i>/<i>&#42;mru-</i> (“to declare, to order”).</p><p>Equivalent in meaning to but not cognates with Sanskrit <i>शकमुरुण्ड</i> (Śakamuruṇḍa) and Chinese <i>塞旺</i> (Sāi Wàng).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salamis, a Greek island between Athens and Megara.</li><li>Salamis, a city on Cyprus founded by Teucer of <b>Salamis</b>, the island.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅλς</i> (háls) +&lrm; <i>-αμίς</i> (-amís, “the middle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Salaminia</li></ol><p>From <i>Σᾰλᾰμῑ́ς</i> (Sălămī́s, “Salamis”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salganeus, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Salmoneus, the mythological king of Elis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salmydessus, Kırklareli, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σολομών</i> (Solomṓn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) alternative form of <i>Θεσσαλονίκη</i> (Thessaloníkē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sallustius; Sallust</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sallustius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salonius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Salōnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samaria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>שֹׁמְרוֹן</i> (šōmrōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Shemaiah</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>שְׁמַעְיָה</i> (Šəmaʿyā́, “Shemaiah”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samaritan</li></ol><p>From <i>Σᾰμᾰ́ρειᾰ</i> (Sămắreiă) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samothrace, East Macedonia and Thrace, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Σᾰ́μος</i> (Sắmos, “Samos, the other island whose people colonized Samothrace”) +&lrm; <i>Θρᾴκη</i> (Thrāíkē, “Thrace, near which Samothrace is situated”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samuel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>שְׁמוּאֵל</i> (šmuʾél).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Samson, a judge of Israel.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>שִׁמְשׁוֹן</i> (śimśōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Samothrace; a Samothracian</li></ol><p>A back-formation of <i>Σᾰμοθρᾴκη</i> (Sămothrāíkē, “Samothrace”) with <i>Θρᾷξ</i> (Thrāîx, “Thracian”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Samosata; Samsat, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sennacherib</li></ol><p>Derived from Akkadian <i>𒁹𒌍𒉽𒈨𒌍𒋢</i> (/⁠Sîn-ahhī-erība⁠/, “Sîn has replaced the brothers”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Sandace, from Old Persian, sister of Xerxes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chandragupta Maurya (first emperor of the Mauryan Empire).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>चन्द्रगुप्त</i> (candragupta).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σαῦλος</i> (Saûlos)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Hebrew <i>שָׁאוּל</i> (sha'úl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sapaei, a Thracian tribe close to the city of Abdera</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;fully <i>Σαπρὰ λῐ́μνη</i>) the Syvash (network of lagoons forming a western arm of the Sea of Azov)</li></ol><p>Nominalization of <i>σᾰπρᾱ́</i> (săprā́), the feminine singular of <i>σᾰπρός</i> (săprós, “rotten, putrid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sappho</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, maybe from Pre-Greek due to the presence of the cluster -πφ-.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shapur; Sapor or Sapores</li></ol><p>From Middle Persian <i>𐭱𐭧𐭯𐭥𐭧𐭥𐭩</i> (šḥpwḥry /⁠Šāhpūr⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarangae, a Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a desert-dwelling Arabic or para-Arabic nomad of North Arabia; a bedouin</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a Muslim; a Saracen</li></ol><p>Perhaps from an Old Arabic predecessor of Arabic شَرْقِيِّينَ (šarqiyyīna, “easterners”, oblique case), from شَرْق (šarq, “east”). In this case the word may originally have referred to the Arabs residing to the east of the fertile strip along the Mediterranean. Compare also Arabic شَرْقِيِّينَ (šarqiyyīna), a bedouin tribe, which gave its name to Arabic الشَّرْقِيّ (aš-šarqiyy), ruling dynasty of Emirate of Fujairah; though this may be entirely unrelated. The <i>Oxford English Dictionary</i> disputes this etymology.</p><p>According to Michael C. A. Macdonald, likely from Old Arabic or Ancient North Arabian <i>𐪆𐪇𐪑𐪄𐪚𐪌</i> (s²rʾqyn /⁠*s²arrāqīn⁠/, “those who migrate to the inner desert”), derived from <i>𐪆𐪇𐪄</i> (s²rq /⁠*s²arraqa⁠/, “to migrate in the inner desert”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Συραστρηνή</i> (Surastrēnḗ)</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>सुराष्ट्र</i> (surāṣṭra, literally “having good dominion”), from <i>सु-</i> (su-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>राष्ट्र</i> (rāṣṭra, “realm, dominion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sargarausene (an ancient district of Cappadocia in modern-day Turkey, mentioned by Ptolemy)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sardanapallus; Sardanapalus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sardanapallus; Sardanapalus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Akkadian <i>𒀸𒋩𒆕𒀀</i> (Aššur-bāni-apli).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Serdica; Sofia, Bulgaria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sardinia (an island in the western Mediterranean Sea, now in Italy)</li></ol><p>From its name in a pre-Roman substrate language, <i>&#42;sard</i>, an indigenous name that likely had religious connotations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Sarmatia (an ill-defined ancient geographic region of Eastern Europe occupied by the nomadic Sarmatians, generally considered to include modern Ukraine, southern Russia, Belarus, parts of Lithuania as well as Poland as far west as the Vistula River)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarpedon</li></ol><p>Possibly from an unattested Anatolian name or title deriving from the compound <i>&#42;sar-pēdan-</i> with the meaning "(someone of) top position".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Saronic</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Saronic Gulf</li></ol><p>From <i>Σᾰ́ρων</i> (Sắrōn, “a mythical king”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Satan</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>שָׂטָן</i> (śɔṭɔn, “adversary”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sataspes, from Old Median, a Persian navigator and commander</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Satāspah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σᾰτᾰ́ν</i> (Sătắn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Satibarzanes, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>chamberlain of Artaxerxes I</li><li>a eunuch who lived during the early reign of Artaxerxes II</li><li>a satrap of Aria and supporter of Bessus</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Šyātibr̥zans</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian: Satiphernes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>friend of Cyrus the Younger killed by Artaxerxes II at the Battle of Cunaxa</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Šyātifarnāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Satnioeis, Troas, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Satraces, from Scythian, the name of a Saka king who fought Alexander the Great</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian via Old Persian <i>&#42;Xšaθrakah</i>, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;xšaθram</i> (literally “reign, kingdom”) + <i>&#42;-kah</i> (suffix), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kšatrám</i> + <i>&#42;-kas</i>), whence also Ossetian <i>Ӕхсӕртӕггaтӕ</i> (Æxsærtæggatæ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sattagydae, a Persian tribe settled in Sattagydia</li></ol><p>From Old Persian. Compare <i>Sattagydia</i> and Old Persian <i>𐎰𐎫𐎦𐎢𐏁</i> (θataguš, literally “hundred cows”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saturninus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Saturnīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Σᾰτᾰ́ν</i> (Sătắn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Samnium; a Samnite</li></ol><p>A cognate of Latin <i>Sabīnī</i> +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Sarmatian</li></ol><p>According to classical Greek authors, the Sarmatae owe the name to their typical red hair.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bay on the southern coast of <i>Arabia Felix</i>, now Khuriya Muriya Bay in Oman</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a people inhabiting the southern coast of <i>Arabia Felix</i>, centred on Khuriya Muriya Bay</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Sao (Nereid)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sirius, the dog star.</li></ol><p>Assuming an original meaning "sparkling, flickering", the word has been connected with <i>σείω</i> (seíō, “to shake”), which is compared to a verb "to be excited, sparkle, gleam" in Sanskrit <i>त्विष्</i> (tviṣ, “brilliance, glitter”), <i>त्वेष</i> (tveṣa, “vehement; brilliant”) and especially Avestan <i>𐬚𐬡𐬌𐬯𐬭𐬀</i> (θβisra, “glitter”). The basis for the Greek form would then be <i>&#42;tweys-ro-</i> or <i>&#42;twis-ro-</i>. Furnée compares <i>τίριος</i> (tírios, “Cretan word for summer”); if correct the word could be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sivas (a city in Turkey), the ancient and medieval Sebastia</li><li>Sebastia (a city in the West Bank, Palestine), the ancient Samaria</li></ol><p>From <i>Σεβαστός</i> (Sebastós) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia, “-ia: <i>forming place names</i>”), from use of <i>σεβαστός</i> (sebastós, “venerable, august”) as a calque of Latin <i>Augustus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sebennytus, Lower Egypt, Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sebastian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Sebastiānus</i>, a derivation of <i>Sebastus</i>, an adaptation of Ancient Greek <i>Σεβαστός</i> (Sebastós), itself a calque of Latin <i>Augustus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σῑληνός</i> (Sīlēnós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σῑών</i> (Sīṓn)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>צִיּוֹן</i> (ṣiyyôn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Σελήνη</i> (Selḗnē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Seleucus; a Seleucid</li></ol><p>From <i>Σέλευκος</i> (Séleukos, “Seleucus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Seleucia</li></ol><p>From <i>Σέλευκος</i> (Séleukos, “Seleucos”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seleucia, the name of a number of cities founded by Seleucus.</li></ol><p>From <i>Σέλευκος</i> (Séleukos, “Seleucus”) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia, “-ia: <i>forming place names</i>”), the name of Seleucus I Nicator the founder of the Seleucid Dynasty and several of his descendants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic, Ionic, and poetic form of <i>Σελήνη</i> (Selḗnē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Selinus; Selinunte, Sicily, Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>σέλινον</i> (sélinon, “celery”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Selleis (a river in Elis, Greece)</li><li>Selleis (a river in Troad, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sellasia, Laconia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Semele, mother of Dionysus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Semiramis</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Akkadian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Shimei</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>שִׁמְעִי</i> (Šimʿī, “Shimei”, literally “renowned”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sempronius; a member of the Roman Sempronius gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Semprōnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seneca, a Roman philosopher</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Seneca</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of September</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) September</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>Septembrius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Septimius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Septimius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Serastere, Aravene, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Servilius; a member of the Roman Servilius family</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Servīlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sergentium, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant of Sermyle</li></ol><p>From <i>Σερμύλη</i> (Sermúlē) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sermyle (a town in Chalcidice, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sertorius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sertōrius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sesindium, India</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Seuthopolis</li></ol><p>From <i>Σεύθης</i> (Seúthēs, “Seuthes”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Seuthes, from Thracian</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Seúthē</i> (“priest, sacrificer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σηκουᾰ́νᾱς</i> (Sēkouắnās, “the river Sequana; the river Seine”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Σηκουᾰνός</i> (Sēkouănós, “one of the Sequani”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Sequana; the river Seine</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sēquana</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Sequani</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sēquanus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Selymbria, Thrace; Silivri, İstanbul, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Selymbria or Silivri; a Selymbrian</li></ol><p>From <i>Σηλυμβρίᾱ</i> (Sēlumbríā, “Selymbria, Silivri”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱνός</i> (-ānós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sepias, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>σηπία</i> (sēpía, “cuttlefish”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sestus, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Setia; Sezze, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Sētia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sthenelus</li></ol><p>From <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”); literally "strong one" or "forcer".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sthenelaus or Sthenelaos</li></ol><p><i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sthenelaidas</li></ol><p>From <i>Σθενέλᾱος</i> (Sthenélāos) +&lrm; <i>-ίδας</i> (-ídas, “patronymic suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sthenoboea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Stheno</li></ol><p>From <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”), hence meaning "forceful".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Siastansas, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian satrap</li></ol><p>(On coins, printed with the lunate sigma: Ϲιαϲτανϲας.) From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;caṣṭana</i> (“master”) (cf. Khotanese <i>caṣṭeṃ</i>)).</p><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀱𑁆𑀝𑀦</i> (caṣṭana), Gandhari <i>𐨖𐨛𐨞</i> (chaṭhaṇa) and <i>Τιαστανης</i> (Tiastanēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Siauacus, from Proto-Sarmatian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Syāvaka</i> (“black”).</p><p>Compare with Ancient Greek <i>Σεαύαγος</i> (Seaúagos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Siauus, from Proto-Sarmatian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Syāva</i> (“black”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;ćyaHwás</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siderus, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>σίδηρος</i> (sídēros, “iron”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sidus, Corinthia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>σίδη</i> (sídē, “pomegranate”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Men of Sidon, Sidonians</li></ol><p>From a plural form of Σῑδών (Sīdṓn), from Phoenician <i>𐤑𐤉𐤃𐤅𐤍</i> (ṣydwn /⁠Ṣīdūn⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sidon or Zidon, a Phoenician city.</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤑𐤉𐤃𐤅𐤍</i> (ṣydwn /⁠Ṣīdūn⁠/). Cognate with Hebrew <i>צִידוֹן</i> (Ṣīḏōn) and Aramaic <i>ܨܝܕܘܢ</i> (Ṣidon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sithonia</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῑθών</i> (Sīthṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sicania</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῐκᾱνός</i> (Sĭkānós, “one of the Sicani”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Sican or Sicanian (one of the Sicani)</li></ol><p>A Proto-Indo-European tribe of uncertain, possibly Ancient Ligurian affiliation, the Sicani's language is the earliest recorded stratum in Sicily. More at Sicani. Also compare <i>Σικελός</i> (Sikelós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sicily (an island and former kingdom in Europe)</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>Σῐ́κελος</i> (Sĭ́kelos)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-i-eh₂</i>Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-íā</i>Ancient Greek <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)Ancient Greek <i>Σικελία</i> From <i>Σῐ́κελος</i> (Sĭ́kelos, “Sicel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sicilian (relating to Sicily or its people)</li></ol><p>From <i>Σικελίᾱ</i> (Sikelíā, “Sicily”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Greek inhabitant of Sicily; a Siceliote; a Siceliot</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῐκελῐ́ᾱ</i> (Sĭkelĭ́ā, “Sicily”) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sicel</li><li>Sicilian, of or from Sicily</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Sicel</li><li>a non-Greek inhabitant of Sicily</li><li>(<i>post-Hellenic</i>) a Sicilian</li></ol><p>A tribe name of probable Proto-Indo-European origin, though uncertain affiliation, possibly Italic. More at Sicels. Also compare Latin <i>Sicani</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sicyon; Sikyona, Corinthia, Greece</li></ol><p>Formally identical to <i>σικυών</i> (sikuṓn, “cucumber bed”). However, there is no variant form **σεκυ- to complement <i>Σεκυών</i> (Sekuṓn), with the exception of a form σεκούα in Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Sicyonian (of or from Sicyon)</li></ol><p><i>Σικυών</i> (Sikuṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Silvanus or Silas</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Silvānus</i>. Doublet of <i>Σῑληνός</i> (Sīlēnós) and <i>σῑληνός</i> (sīlēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Silwan, a neighbourhood of Jerusalem, where Jesus cured a man born blind</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>שִּׁילוֹחַ</i> (Shiloaḥ), whose meaning "gushing forth" is also rendered in Greek as ἀπεσταλμένος (apestalménos, “having been sent”), passive perfect participle of ἀποστέλλω (apostéllō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Aramaic, equivalent to English Silas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>שְׁאִילָא</i> (šəʾīlā). Compare Hebrew <i>שָׁאוּל</i> (sha'úl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Simonides</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῐ́μων</i> (Sĭ́mōn) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Simois</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Simyra; Tell Kazel, Syria</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤎𐤌𐤓</i> (smr). Compare Egyptian <i>smr</i>, Akkadian <i>𒍮𒈬𒊒</i> (ṣu-mu-ru /⁠Ṣumuru⁠/) and Hebrew <i>צְמָרִי</i> (“inhabitants of Simyra”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Sintian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Sinope or Sinop</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῐνώπη</i> (Sĭnṓpē, “Sinope; Sinop”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sinope, Paphlagonia; Sinop, Turkey</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Sinope, a woman whom the city was named for</li></ol><p>From Hittite <i>𒌷𒅆𒄿𒉡𒉿</i> (<sup>URU</sup>ši-i-nu-wa /⁠Šīnuwa⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siraceni, an ancient tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Siromitres, from Old Median, a Persian mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Srīramiθra-</i> (literally “beautiful friend”), from <i>&#42;srīrā-</i> (“beautiful”) + <i>&#42;miθra-</i> (“friend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sisamnes, from Old Persian, a Persian corrupt judge</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Čiçamanah-</i> (literally “of brilliant mind”), from <i>&#42;čiça-</i> (“shining, splendid”) + <i>&#42;manah-</i> (“mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sisamaces, from Old Persian, a Persian commander mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Čiçamaga-</i> (literally “with shining gifts”), from <i>&#42;čiça-</i> (“shining, splendid”) + <i>&#42;maga-</i> (“gift, present”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sisimithres, from Old Persian, a Persian satrap</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Čiçamiθrah</i>, from <i>𐎨𐎡𐏂</i> (c-i-ç /⁠čiçaʰ⁠/, “shining; appearance; lineage”) +&lrm; <i>𐎷𐎰𐎼</i> (mi-θ-r /⁠Miθraʰ⁠/, “Mithra”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sitalces, from Thracian</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Sitalkas</i>. By surface analysis, <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos) +&lrm; <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sittace, Assyria, Iraq</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sitones, an ancient tribe settled in Northern Europe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Siomachus, from Proto-Sarmatian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Šōmax</i>, from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Š́yāmak(k)i</i>, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;CyaHmákah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Zion (a hill in Jerusalem, Israel, on which ancient Jerusalem was partly built; a centrepiece to Biblical accounts of old days and future eschatological events)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Jerusalem</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>צִיּוֹן</i> (ṣiyyôn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Scamander; the river Karamenderes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scandia, a town on the island of Cythera</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sciathus; Skiathos, Sporades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scidrus, Lucania, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Sciron (a Corinthian outlaw killed by the hero Theseus on the way from Troezen to Athens)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scaean Gates (gateway in the walls of Troy)</li></ol><p>Nominalization of <i>σκαιός</i> (skaiós, “western, left-hand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scaevola</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Scaevola</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Illyrian: Scerdilaidas, a king of Illyria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Illyrian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scipio</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Scīpiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scillus, Elis, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>σκίλλα</i> (skílla, “squill”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scirophorion: the twelfth month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around June and July</li></ol><p>From <i>Σκῐροφόρῐᾰ</i> (Skĭrophórĭă), the name of a festival.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scione</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Slav</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>Σκλαβηνός</i> (Sklabēnós), <i>Σθλαβηνός</i> (Sthlabēnós), ultimately from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slověninъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scribonia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Scrībōnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scythia (a geographic region encompassing the Pontic-Caspian steppe in Eastern Europe and Central Asia, inhabited by nomadic Scythians from at least the 11th century <small>BCE</small> to the 2nd century <small>CE</small>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Σκῠ́θης</i> (Skŭ́thēs, “Scythian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scythia</li></ol><p>From <i>Σκῠθῐκός</i> (Skŭthĭkós, “Scythian”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Scythian</li></ol><p>From <i>Σκύθης</i> (Skúthēs) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Scythian</li></ol><p>From <i>Σκύθης</i> (Skúthēs) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scylace, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) <i>a male surname, famously held by:</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>John <b>Skylitzes</b>, <small>Greek:</small> <i>Ἰωάννης <b>Σκυλίτζης</b></i> (Iōánnēs <b>Skulítzēs</b>), Byzantine Roman Greek historian (<i>circa</i> 1040–1100); author of the <i>Synopsis of Histories</i>, recorded on an eponymous manuscript known as the <i>Madrid <b>Skylitzes</b></i></li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scyrmiadae, a Thracian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scopas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scotusa; Skotousa, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female inhabitant of Scythia; a female Scythian</li></ol><p>From <i>Σκῠ́θης</i> (Skŭ́thēs, “Scythian”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Scythia; a Scythian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Skuδi</i>. Cognate with Old Persian <i>𐎿𐎤𐎢𐎭𐎼</i> (s-ku-u-d-r /⁠Skudra⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scylla</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>σκύλλω</i> (skúllō, “pluck, tear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Σκύλλα</i> (Skúlla, “Scylla”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Smerdis (Bardiya)</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎲𐎼𐎮𐎡𐎹</i> (b-r-di-i-y /⁠Br̥diyaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Smila, Crossaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian: Smerdomenes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;B⁠r̥dimanah</i> (from <i>⁠br̥diš</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>𐎶𐎴𐏃</i> (manah, “mind”); cognate with Old Median <i>&#42;B⁠r̥zimanah</i>), or <i>&#42;B⁠r̥dimaniš</i> (from <i>⁠br̥diš</i> (“high”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;maniš</i> (“thought, mind”)); compare with Sanskrit <i>मनस् बृहत्</i> (<i>मनस्</i> (manas, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>बृहत्</i> (bṛhat, “high”)) and <i>बृहत्मनस्</i> (Bṛhát-manas-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Smintheus, epithet of Apollo</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Sminthian or mouse-killer</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σμῐ́νθος</i> (smĭ́nthos, “mouse”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús). Or from <i>Σμίνθη</i> (Smínthē, “a town in the Troad”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Σμύρνᾱ</i> (Smúrnā, “Smyrna; İzmir, Turkey”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Solomon (the son of David and third king of Israel)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>שְׁלֹמֹה</i> (šĕlōmōʰ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Solus, Sicily, Italy</li></ol><p>Probably a Punic name, hinted by the spelling <i>Σολόεις</i> (Solóeis) which is said to transliterate the Punic word for "cape." Another Punic name for the settlement was <i>𐤊𐤐𐤓𐤀</i> (kprʾ, “village”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Solois</li></ol><p>Probably of Punic origin, compare <i>Σολοῦς</i> (Soloûs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Solygeia, Corinthia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sosthanes, from Old Persian, a Persian commander</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Suetonius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Suētōnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sulpicius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sulpicius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Cape Sunium</li></ol><p>From <i>Σούνῐον</i> (Soúnĭon, “Cape Sunium”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Susanna.</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>שׁוֹשַׁנָּה</i> (šōšannā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Susiana</li></ol><p>From <i>Σουσιανός</i> (Sousianós), from <i>Σοῦσα</i> (Soûsa, “Susa”) +&lrm; <i>-ιανός</i> (-ianós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Susian</li></ol><p>From <i>Σοῦσα</i> (Soûsa, “Susa”) +&lrm; <i>-ιανός</i> (-ianós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Susiscanes, from Egyptian, an Egyptian man mentioned by Aeschylus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ecclesiasticus</li></ol><p>Literally, “Wisdom of Sirach”, one of the many names by which this book is known.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Book of the Wisdom of Solomon (book of the Septuagint)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sophaenetus</li></ol><p>From <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “clever”) +&lrm; <i>αἰνετός</i> (ainetós, “praiseworthy”, from <i>αἶνος</i> (aînos, “fable; praise”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Sophocles</li><li><i>In particular</i>, the 5th century <small>BCE</small> tragic playwright Sophocles</li></ol><p>From <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “clever”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to Sophocles, Sophoclean</li></ol><p><i>Σοφοκλῆς</i> (Sophoklês) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Sicambri</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Suebi, Suevi</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Suēbus</i>, <i>Suēvus</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swēbaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cape Sunium, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sura</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sura</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) the Suda, a 10th-century Byzantine encyclopedia</li></ol><p>Probably from Byzantine Greek <i>σούδα</i> (soúda, “trench, ditch”), later "stronghold" (presumably of knowledge), the variant title <i>Σουΐδας</i> (Souḯdas) being the result of an error made by Eustathius of Thessalonica, who mistook the title for the author's name.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Susa (an ancient city in Iran)</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐏂𐎢𐏁𐎠</i> (ç-u-š-a /⁠Çūšā⁠/), from Elamite <i>𒀸𒋗𒊺𒂗</i> (Šušen).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spadacus, from Proto-Sarmatian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Spādaka</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spalathra, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>σπάλαθρον</i> (spálathron, “oven rake”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Σπάρτη</i> (Spártē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spartacus, from Thracian</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Spartakos</i> (“renowned by the spear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sparta, Sparti (a city in Laconia, Greece)</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but possibly from <i>σπάρτον</i> (spárton, “rope, cable”), a reference to the cords laid as the city's foundation boundaries, though this could be folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spartolus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spadagas, from Proto-Sarmatian</li></ol><p>From Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Spādaka</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spalahores, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian king</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;Spalahaura</i> (“commander of army”), composed of <i>&#42;spala</i> (“army”), itself from Parthian <i>𐭏𐭎𐭐𐭀𐭃</i> (ispāδ, “army”) (cf. Old Persian <i>𐎿𐎱𐎠𐎭</i> (spāda, “army”))) + <i>&#42;haura</i> (“leader, commander”) (cf. Khotanese <i>haura</i> (“leader, commander”)).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨭𐨿𐨤𐨫𐨱𐨆𐨪</i> (śpalahora) and <i>Σπαλύρις</i> (Spalúris).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spalirises, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian king</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;Spalarīźa</i> (“commander of army”), composed of <i>&#42;spala</i> (“army”) (cf. Old Persian <i>𐎿𐎱𐎠𐎭</i> (spāda, “army”), Parthian <i>𐭏𐭎𐭐𐭀𐭃</i> (ispāδ, “army”)) + <i>&#42;rīźa</i> (“desiring, commanding”) (cf. Khotanese <i>rriś</i> (“to desire”), Sogdian <i>𐽀𐼷𐼵𐼸𐽀𐼰𐼸</i> (rēš-karē)).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨭𐨿𐨤𐨫𐨁𐨪𐨁𐨭</i> (śpaliriśa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spalahores, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian king</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;Spalahaura</i> (“commander of army”), composed of <i>&#42;spala</i> (“army”), itself from Parthian <i>𐭏𐭎𐭐𐭀𐭃</i> (ispāδ, “army”) (cf. Old Persian <i>𐎿𐎱𐎠𐎭</i> (spāda, “army”))) + <i>&#42;haura</i> (“leader, commander”) (cf. Khotanese <i>haura</i> (“leader, commander”)).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨭𐨿𐨤𐨫𐨱𐨆𐨪</i> (śpalahora) and <i>Σπαλαχόρης</i> (Spalakhórēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) alternative form of <i>Ἱσπᾱνίᾱ</i> (Hispāníā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sparadacus, from Thracian, name of an Odrysian king</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Sparatakos</i> (“renowned by the spear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spargapises, from Saka, a general of the Massagetae defeated by Cyrus the Great</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Sparɣapēsi</i>. Compare with <i>Σπαργαπείθης</i> (Spargapeíthēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spargapeithes, from Scythian, a king of the Agathyrsi</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Sparɣapēsi</i>, with the second element likely altered through folk etymology, alongside <i>Ἀριαπείθης</i> (Ariapeíthēs), by Ancient Greek <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “persuade”), as found in genuine Ancient Greek names, such as <i>Διοπείθης</i> (Diopeíthēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Sparta; a Spartan</li></ol><p>from <i>Σπᾰ́ρτη</i> (Spắrtē, “Sparta”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ́της</i> (-ā́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female inhabitant of Sparta; a Spartan woman</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the land of Sparta; Spartiatis</li></ol><p>From <i>Σπᾰρτῐᾱ́της</i> (Spărtĭā́tēs, “Spartan”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Spercheus; the river Spercheios</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Spercheus, the Greek mythological river god of the aforementioned river</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a police force in Athens</li></ol><p>From <i>Σπεύσινος</i> (Speúsinos), the name of its alleged founder.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Speusippus</li></ol><p>From <i>σπεύδω</i> (speúdō) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spithames, from Old Median</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>father of Satrabates</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Spitāmah</i>. Compare with Ancient Greek <i>Σπιταμᾶς</i> (Spitamâs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spithradates, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an Achaemenid military officer send by Darius II to quell the rebellion of Pissuthnes</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Spiθradātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spithridates, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an Achaemenid general who revolted against Pharnabazus II</li><li>brother of Rhosaces and satrap of Lydia and Ionia who smashed Alexander the Great's helmet at the Battle of the Granicus</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Spiθradātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spitaces, from Old Median</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a son Amytis and Spitamas, and grandson of Astyages</li><li>an Indian nomarch in the army of Porus</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Spitaka</i> (literally “abundant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spitamenes, from Proto-Sogdic, Sogdian warlord</li></ol><p>Borrowed from an Proto-Sogdic <i>&#42;Spitamanah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spitamas, from Old Median</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>son-in-law of Astyages and husband of Amytis</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Spitāmah</i>. Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>Σπιθάμης</i> (Spithámēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Spyridon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spurius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Spurius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Στάγειρος</i> (Stágeiros, “Stagirus; Stagira; Stageira, Chalcidice, Greece”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stagirus; Stagira; Stageira, Chalcidice, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Statira; Stateira</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Stātirā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stentor</li></ol><p>From <i>στένω</i> (sténō, “to moan, whoosh noisily”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr), from imitative Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tenh₂-</i>, see also Old English <i>þunor</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Stilpon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Stagirus or Stagira or Stageira; a Stagirite</li></ol><p>From <i>Στάγειρος</i> (Stágeiros), <i>Στάγειρᾰ</i> (Stágeiră, “Stagirus; Stagira; Stageira”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stamenia, Pontus, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stauracius, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stenyclarus, Messenia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stiris, Phocis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Stesimbrotos</li></ol><p><i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi) +&lrm; <i>βροτός</i> (brotós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Stesichorus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to set”) +&lrm; <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “chorus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a row of islands off Marseille, now the Frioul archipelago</li></ol><p>A substantivisation of <i>στοιχάς</i> (stoikhás, “in rows one behind another”, adjective), in elliptical use for <i>αἱ στοιχάδες &#91;νῆσοι&#93;</i> (hai stoikhádes &#91;nêsoi&#93;), used as a proper noun. Compare <i>Κυκλάδες</i> (Kukládes) and <i>Σποράδες</i> (Sporádes).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a work by Epicurus</li></ol><p>Use as a proper noun of the plural of <i>στοιχείωσῐς</i> (stoikheíōsĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Strabo (Greek geographer, philosopher and historian (64/63 BC–c.24 AD))</li></ol><p>From <i>στραβός</i> (strabós, “squint-eyed”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Stratus or Stratos; a Stratian</li></ol><p>From <i>Στρᾰ́τος</i> (Strắtos, “Stratus; Stratos”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stratus; Stratos, Aetolia-Acarnania, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Strattis, held by</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>An Athenian comic poet</li><li>A tyrant of Chios</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Strepsa, a city of Mygdonia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic) Stratia, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Stratonice or Stratonica</li></ol><p>From <i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army”) +&lrm; <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Strongyle, the name of various Greek-settled isles</li><li>Strongyle, an island on the western rim of the Thera/Santorini volcanic island complex in the Cyclades, Greece</li><li>Strongyle, an island in the eastern-most Dodecanese islands</li><li>the older name for Stromboli, Aeolian Islands, Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>στρογγῠ́λος</i> (strongŭ́los, “round, spherical”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Strombichides</li></ol><p>From <i>Στρόμβιχος</i> (Strómbikhos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Struthas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Struchates, a Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Strymon; the river Struma</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;strúmā</i>, <i>&#42;strūmōn</i> (“stream, river”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow”). Compare Lithuanian dialectal <i>straumuo</i>, <i>sriaumė</i>, Latvian <i>stràume</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>στρόμβος</i> (strómbos, “whirlwind”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Strophius</li></ol><p>From <i>στρόφος</i> (stróphos, “twisted band, cord”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stryme, Thrace, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Styllangium, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lake Stymphalia</li></ol><p>From <i>Στύμφᾱλος</i> (Stúmphālos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>referring to stoicism or Stoics</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a follower of stoicism; a Stoic</li></ol><p>From <i>στωῐ̈κός</i> (stōĭ̈kós, “of a colonnade or porch”), from <i>&#42;στωᾱ́</i> (&#42;stōā́), <i>στῳά</i> (stōiá), spelling of <i>στοᾱ́</i> (stoā́) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós). From Ποικίλη Στοᾱ́ (Poikílē Stoā́), a stoa where Zeno of Citium taught. Also substantivised.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Stygian</li><li>synonym of <i>στυγητός</i> (stugētós, “hateful, abominable”)</li></ol><p>From <i>Στύξ</i> (Stúx) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stymbara, Pelagonia, North Macedonia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stymphalus, a city and mountain of Arcadia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Styx</li><li>Styx, a goddess or a nymph in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From the same source as <i>στυγνός</i> (stugnós, “gloomy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Styra, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syennesis</li></ol><p>From Luwian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aswan (a city in southern Egypt)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>swnw</i>, <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./b3f586c8.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="wn" alt="glyph" height="60"/><br /><img src="./6c9cd1c6.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="n" alt="glyph" height="12"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f9c3b57e.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="nw" alt="glyph" height="42"/><br /><img src="./d0914c94.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="W" alt="glyph" height="42"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./c77fdbdb.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="T11" alt="glyph" height="20"/><br /><img src="./9d93c55f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="niwt" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td>
