</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Sybaris; a Sybarite</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῠ́βᾰρῐς</i> (Sŭ́bărĭs, “Sybaris”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female inhabitant of Sybaris; a Sybarite woman</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῠβᾰρῑ́της</i> (Sŭbărī́tēs, “Sybarite”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>person from Aswan</li></ol><p><i>Συήνη</i> (Suḗnē) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sycurium, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Syloson</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Symaean (of, belonging to, or pertaining to Syme)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Symaean (a native or inhabitant of Syme)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>Σῡ́μη</i> (Sū́mē) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Simeon; Simon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>שִׁמְעוֹן</i> (shim'ón).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the two Symplegades</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠμπλήσσομαι</i> (sŭmplḗssomai, “strike together”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰς</i> (-ăs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Synesius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a slave’s female given name, equivalent to French Synétè</li></ol><p>Use as a proper noun of <i>σῠνετή</i> (sŭnetḗ, “intelligent, sagacious, wise”, adjective). Compare <i>Σῠνετός</i> (Sŭnetós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sypalettus, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Συρᾱ́κουσαι</i> (Surā́kousai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syracuse (a city and port in Sicily; an ancient Doric city-state in Magna Graecia)</li></ol><p>Attested by 8th century BC, from a Pre-Greek word, possibly Phoenician <i>𐤔𐤄𐤓𐤀𐤇</i> (šhrʾḥ, “to feel ill”) in reference to the port's proximity to a swamp.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Syracusan</li></ol><p><i>Σῠρᾱ́κουσαι</i> (Sŭrā́kousai, “Syracuse”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Saurashtra (a region of Gujarat, India)</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>𑀲𑀼𑀭𑀸𑀱𑁆𑀝𑁆𑀭</i> (surāṣṭra), from <i>𑀲𑀼</i> (su, “good”) +&lrm; <i>𑀭𑀸𑀱𑁆𑀝𑁆𑀭</i> (rāṣṭra, “realm, dominion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syro-Attic (<i>a nickname</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῠ́ρος</i> (Sŭ́ros) +&lrm; <i>Ᾰ̓ττῐκός</i> (Ăttĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syriarch</li></ol><p><i>Συρία</i> (Suría) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Syrian</li></ol><p><i>Συρία</i> (Suría, “Syria”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syriarchate</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>from Syria, Syrian</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῠ́ρος</i> (Sŭ́ros, “a Syrian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shechem, Sichem (an ancient city in Palestine)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>שְׁכָם</i> (šəḵem).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sphinx, sphinx</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from <i>σφῐ́γγω</i> (sphĭ́ngō, “to squeeze, to strangle”) (a Pre-Greek word) or from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Szp&#58;p</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./213c0331.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A53" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./fed24f8f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="anx" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">n&#58;x</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (šzp-ꜥnḫ, “living image”) or <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Szp&#58;p</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./213c0331.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A53" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (šzp, “image; statue; sphinx”). Beekes assumes a general substrate origin, and suspects the traditional connection and resemblance to <i>σφῐ́γγω</i> (sphĭ́ngō) to be driven by folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sphacteria; Sphagia, Messenia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sphendadates, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a Magian who usurped the Achaemenid throne after the murder of Smerdis and the death of Cambyses II; likely an epithet of Gaumāta</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Spantadātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sphendale, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>An ancient demos of Attica</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sphodrias</li></ol><p>From <i>σφοδρός</i> (sphodrós, “vehement”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Schedius, the name of two Phocians slain by Hector</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scheria, the ancient name of the island of Corcyra</li></ol><p>Unknown. May be related to <i>σχερός</i> (skherós, “headland, beach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a town in Bulgaria</li></ol><p>From <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Socrates</li><li>in particular, the 5th century <small>BCE</small> philosopher Socrates</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe and sound”, the root of σῴζω (sōízō)) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the philosophers of Socrates’ school, the Socratics</li></ol><p>From <i>Σωκρᾰτῐκός</i> (Sōkrătĭkós): as a noun, a substantivisation of its masculine plural forms in elliptical use for <i>Σωκρατικοὶ φιλόσοφοι</i> (Sōkratikoì philósophoi, “Socratic philosophers”), <i>Σωκρατικοὶ μαθηταί</i> (Sōkratikoì mathētaí, “pupils of Socrates”), <i>Σωκρατικοὶ ἑπέται</i> (Sōkratikoì hepétai, “followers of Socrates”), or similar; as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Socrates, Socratic</li></ol><p><i>Σωκρᾰ́της</i> (Sōkrắtēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Sosias</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe and sound”, the root of σῴζω (sōízō)) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sosipater</li></ol><p>From <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō, “save”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr, “father”), literally “savior of the father.” Compare <i>Σωσῐκρᾰ́της</i> (Sōsĭkrắtēs) / <i>Σωκρᾰ́της</i> (Sōkrắtēs, “Socrates”), <i>Σωσῐκλείδης</i> (Sōsĭkleídēs) / <i>Σωκλείδης</i> (Sōkleídēs, “Socleides”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Sosthenes</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶς</i> (sôs) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Sosigenes</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō, “to save”) +&lrm; <i>γένος</i> (génos, “race, kin”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Sostrata</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe and sound”) +&lrm; <i>στρατός</i> (stratós, “army”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sophene (an ancient province of the kingdom of Armenia in modern-day southeastern Turkey)</li></ol><p>Probably of Urartian origin. Compare the Armenian name: <i>Ծոփք</i> (Copʻkʻ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Sodom (a historical city in the Middle East)</li></ol><p>From the Biblical Hebrew <i>סְדֹם</i> (səḏōm).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sollax, the ancient name of the river Tigris</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sollium</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Soli (a city in Cilicia)</li><li>Soli (a city in Cyprus)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Solon</li></ol><p>The origin is Pre-Greek. A relation to <i>σόλος</i> (sólos) has been proposed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Sossius, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sybaris, an ancient Achaean settlement in Magna Graecia</li><li>Sybaris, the river flowing by the town of Sybaris</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sybota, Thesprotia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠβότης</i> (sŭbótēs), <i>σῠβώτης</i> (sŭbṓtēs, “swineherd”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sybrita, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sulla</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Sulla</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syllium, Pamphylia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Symaethus; river Simeto, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syme (an island between Rhodes and Cnidus in Caria)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from Syme</li></ol><p><i>Σῡ́μη</i> (Sū́mē, “Syme”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”); compare <i>Ᾰ̓θήνηθεν</i> (Ăthḗnēthen), <i>Τροίηθεν</i> (Troíēthen)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Synnada, Phrygia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A common female slave name</li></ol><p>From <i>Σύρος</i> (Súros) +&lrm; <i>-α</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syrmus, king of the Triballi</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sorrento (a town and comune of the province of Naples, Campania, Italy)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Surrentum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gulf of Sidra (a gulf of the Mediterranean, Libya)</li><li>Gulf of Gabes (a gulf of the Mediterranean, Libya)</li><li>Sirte (a city in Libya)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, ruin</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but possibly from <i>συρτός</i> (surtós, “swept, dragged along”), from <i>σῡ́ρειν</i> (sū́rein, “to drag along”) after the proverbially treacherous effect of the wind and the waves on the quicksand of the area. The figurative sense of "destruction" derives from the difficulty of navigating the gulfs due to shoreward drag produced.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sozusa, Apollonia</li></ol><p>In the Roman period a byname used of any city called Apollonia, from the feminine participle σῴζουσα (sōízousa) of σῴζω (sōízō, “to save”) (from Apollo's title of σωτήρ (sōtḗr, “saviour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Sostratus</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe, sound”) +&lrm; <i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sestos, Thracian Chersonese</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>culture</i>) a people of East Asia usually identified as the southern Chinese: the Cantonese, Vietnamese, and other Yue peoples reached via the maritime Silk Road to Panyu (Guangzhou), not known at the time to be related to the <i>Seres</i> reached by the overland route to Chang'an (Xi'an)</li><li>their homeland in southern China: Guangdong and northern Vietnam</li><li>their chief city</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but possibly from Arabic صِين (Ṣīn, “China”) + <i>-αι</i> (-ai), from Middle Persian <i>𐭰𐭩𐭭</i> (čīn, “China”), from Sanskrit <i>चीन</i> (Cīna, “China”), and usually held to derive from Old Chinese <i>秦</i> (&#42;zin, “Qin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siris, a city of Magna Graecia</li></ol><p>Possibly a hydronym extended from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seykʷ-</i> (“to drip, moisten”), which would be related to <i>ἰκμάς</i> (ikmás, “moisture”). Folk etymology connected the name to a daughter of <i>Morges</i> (king and founder of the <i>Morgetes</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Siphae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syros, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Sothis (an Egyptian god)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f11dc961.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="M44" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">t&#58;N14</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i>.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Socus, a Trojan, son of Hippasus, slain by Odysseus</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶκος</i> (sôkos, “saviour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Shu (an Egyptian god)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./39176b9c.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="S" alt="glyph" height="30"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./4551831f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Sw" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./d035306b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A40" alt="glyph" height="90"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i>.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A counter for talents. An abbreviation of τάλαντον (tálanton). Used as a tally mark, as in ΤΤΤ (3 talents) and Τ𐅐𐅏ΣΣΣ (1 talent 18 staters).</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤕</i> (t, “tāw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tabalus, from Old Persian, the first satrap of Sardis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talaura, Pontus, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tanagra, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tanais, Don (a river, the fifth-longest in Europe, in modern Tula, Lipetsk, Voronezh, Volgograd and Rostov Oblasts, Russia)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰenh₂-</i>, found in many other river names, see also <i>Doncaster</i>, <i>Danube</i>, <i>Dniester</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tanis (a historical city in Egypt)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowing from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">D&#58;a</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">n&#58;t&#42;Z5</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9465917c.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="mw" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">N36&#58;N23</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9d93c55f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="niwt" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (ḏꜥnt).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tantalus</li></ol><p>Unexplained and likely Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taxila (an ancient city and archaeological site in the modern province of Punjab, Pakistan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>तक्षशिला</i> (takṣaśilā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taras, a colony in Magna Graecia founded by Sparta; Tarentum; Taranto, Italy</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps related to, Illyrian <i>&#42;darandos</i> (“oak”), hence possibly Pre-Greek. Compare the declension of Ancient Greek <i>Ἀκράγας</i> (Akrágas) and <i>Γίγᾱς</i> (Gígās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ancient name of Sardis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tartarus</li><li>(Christendom) metaphor for Hell</li></ol><p>According to Strabo, this word had been fancifully believed to have been invented by Homer with the city of <i>Ταρτησσός</i> (Tartēssós) in mind, with a slight change of letters, it being west of the <i>Ἡράκλειοι Στῆλαι</i> (Hērákleioi Stêlai), or Pillars of Heracles, beyond which the sun sinks past Oceanus as it enters into Tartarus. A semantic connection of Definition 2 with the doomed, sunken civilization of Atlantis has not been overlooked.</p><p>Klein suggests (at least partly) otherwise: "prob[ably] word of imitative origin, suggestive of something frightful."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tarphe, Locris, Greece</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>τάρφος</i> (tárphos, “thicket”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tatius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tatius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of Taphus</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taphius, the colonizer in Greek mythology of the islands of Taphos</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of the islands of Taphos; a Taphian</li></ol><p>From <i>Τᾰ́φος</i> (Tắphos, “Taphus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Tecmessa</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tellis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Τέμπη</i> (Témpē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Vale of Tempe, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tenedos; Bozcaada, Çanakkale, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tentyra; Dendera, Egypt</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">tA&#58;n</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">tA&#58;r&#58;r</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">t&#58;niwt</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (tꜣ-n-tꜣ-rrt).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Termera, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Terminus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Terminus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Tetius, Cyprus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Telandrus, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Telemus, a mythological seer and son of Eurymus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Telephus</li></ol><p>From <i>τῆλε</i> (têle, “far”) +&lrm; <i>φάος</i> (pháos, “light”), literally “far-shining”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Temenus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Teres, from Thracian</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Tērēs</i> (“combatant, competitor”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Tiber</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tiberis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Tibisis; Timiș (river), a tributary river of the Danube</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Tigris</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎫𐎡𐎥𐎼𐎠</i> (t-i-g-r-a /⁠Tigrā⁠/), from Akkadian <i>𒀀𒇉𒈦𒄘𒃼</i> (<sup>ÍD</sup>Idiqlat) from Sumerian <i>𒀀𒇉𒈦𒄘𒃼</i> (<sup>ÍD</sup>Idigna, <sup>ÍD</sup>Idigina, literally “fast as an arrow”), because Tigris is rough and fast flowing compared to the neighboring Euphrates. Compare with Classical Syriac <i>ܕܩܠܬ</i> (deqlāṯ), Old Armenian <i>Դգլաթ</i> (Dglatʻ), Arabic دِجْلَة (dijla), Hebrew <i>חידקל &#47; חִדֶּקֶל</i> (ḥiddéqel)), all borrowed from Akkadian <i>𒀀𒇉𒈦𒄘𒃼</i> (<sup>ÍD</sup>Idiqlat).</p><p>Compare Middle Persian (Arvand, “Tigris”, literally “swift”) (compare Avestan <i>-𐬀𐬎𐬭𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬥𐬙</i> (-a<sup><small>u</small></sup>ruuant)). Compare also Achaemenid Elamite <i>𒋾𒅅𒊏</i> (ti-ig-ra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ticinus; river Ticino, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Tīcīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timon</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honor”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, "Hoffmann suffix").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiryns; Tiryntha, Argolis, Greece</li></ol><p>A Pre-Greek name, hinted by the presence of <i>-νθ-</i> (-nth-). Compare <i>ἕλμινς</i> (hélmins, stem <i>ἕλμινθ-</i> (hélminth-)), <i>Ζᾰ́κῠνθος</i> (Zắkŭnthos) and <i>Ζώμῐνθος</i> (Zṓmĭnthos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiro</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tīrō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Titarus, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Titus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Titus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cape Taenarus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Taygetus Mountains</li></ol><p>Perhaps derived from <i>Ταϋγέτη</i> (Taügétē, “Taygete”). First element is probably <i>τηΰς</i> (tēǘs, “big”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Τᾱῠ̈́γετον</i> (Tāŭ̈́geton, “the Taygetus Mountains”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tabiti, a Scythian goddess corresponding to Hestia</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Tapatī́</i>, ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tep-</i> (“be hot, burn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Tagonius; river Tajuña, Spain</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talaus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Talthybius</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>&#42;Ταλεθόβιος</i> (&#42;Talethóbios), via Grassmann's Law, from <i>θᾰ́λλω</i> (thắllō, “sprout, grow”) +&lrm; <i>βῐ́ος</i> (bĭ́os, “life”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tamassus, Cyprus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tamynae; Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Tanūvazr̥kah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sri Lanka</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀢𑀁𑀩𑀧𑀁𑀦𑀺</i> (taṃbapaṃni, “Sri Lanka”), from Sanskrit <i>ताम्रपर्णी</i> (tāmraparṇī, literally “copper-leaved”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tarquinii, Latium; Tarquinia, Lazio, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tarquiniī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tarquinius; a member of the Etruscan-Roman Tarquinius family</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tarquinius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Tarpeian Rock, Rome</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tarpēius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tarpeia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tarpēia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tarracina; Terracina, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tarracina</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Tarsus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ταρσός</i> (Tarsós, “Tarsus”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ταρσός</i> (Tarsós, “Tarsus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tartessus; Tartessos</li></ol><p>Uncertain; some scholars connect the name to the Hebrew city of <i>תַּרְשִׁישׁ</i> ("Tarshish") or the Anatolian city of <i>Ταρσός</i> (Tarsós), among others. <i>Pausanias</i> wrote that the name was from an Iberian river. More at Tartessos.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taulantii, an Illyrian tribe settled on the coast of modern Albania.</li></ol><p>Possibly from an Illyrian word meaning "swallow," from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delh₁-</i> (“to cut”) + <i>&#42;dwóh₁</i> (“two”), representing modern Albanian <i>dallëndyshe</i> (“swallow”).</p><p>The name <i>Taulantii</i> appears to be connected with the word <i>dallëndyshe</i>, from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;dalna</i> and Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;duwai</i>, meaning "swallow".</p><p>The name was also attested as "Daulantioi" (Δαυλαντιοι) by Nonnus of Panopolis in the 5th century CE, which might be closer to how the Taulanti said the name.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worshippers of Poseidon at Ephesus</li></ol><p><i>Ταύρεος</i> (Taúreos, “[Boeotian epithet of] Poseidon”) +&lrm; <i>-ασταί</i> (-astaí, “-ists”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the name of a month at Amorgos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the name of a month at Miletus, Cyzicus, etc.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Tauris, Taurica (an ancient geographic region and peninsula, the name used by the Greeks and Romans for the modern Crimean Peninsula in Eastern Europe, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but occupied by Russia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ταῦρος</i> (Taûros, “one of the Tauri”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκή</i> (-ĭkḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tauromenium; Taormina, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Tauromenium or Taormina; a Tauromenite</li></ol><p>From <i>Ταυρομένῐον</i> (Tauroménĭon, “Tauromenium; Taormina”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a name of Artemis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the name of a month at Alexandria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Ταύρεος</i> (Taúreos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Teispes, from Old Persian, an ancestor of Cyrus the Great</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎨𐎡𐏁𐎱𐎡𐏁</i> (c-i-š-p-i-š /⁠Čišpiš⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Teichium, Aetolia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tegea, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Tegea; a Tegeate</li></ol><p>From <i>Τεγέᾱ</i> (Tegéā, “Tegea”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tegyra, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiristasis, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiresias</li></ol><p>Probably from Ancient Greek <i>τείρεα</i> (teírea, “celestial sign”), a variant of <i>τέρας</i> (téras, “divine sign, omen, marvel, wonder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Telamon (<i>name of Achilles</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Τελαμών</i> (Telamṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ιάδης</i> (-iádēs) or <i>Τελαμώνιος</i> (Telamṓnios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Telamon, Telamonian (<i>epithet of Ajax the Great</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Τελαμών</i> (Telamṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Teleutias</li></ol><p>From <i>τελευτή</i> (teleutḗ, “completion”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Teleutas</li></ol><p>From <i>τελευτᾰ́ω</i> (teleutắō, “to bring to pass”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The largest city of Lydia</li><li>a city in Caria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Temesa, Bruttium, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tenea, Corinthia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Tencteri; a Tencterite</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tenchthērī</i>, <i>Tenctērī</i> +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Terentius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Terentius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tertullian</li></ol><p>Borrowing either from Latin <i>Tertulliānus</i> or Latin <i>Tertylliānus</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Terpsichore</li></ol><p>From <i>τέρψῐς</i> (térpsĭs, “enjoyment”) +&lrm; <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “dance”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Terpsichore</li></ol><p>From <i>τέρψις</i> (térpsis, “enjoyment, delight”) +&lrm; <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “chorus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a name given to the followers of the fourth-century heretical movement of Audianism which honored the death of Christ on the eve of Jewish Passover on fourteenth day of Nisan instead of Easter Sunday; Quartodecimans, Audians</li></ol><p>From <i>τεσσᾰρεσκαιδέκᾰτος</i> (tessăreskaidékătos, “fourteenth”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs). Compare to <i>Quārtadecimānī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tetramnestus, a Sidonian officer and son of Anysus</li></ol><p>From <i>τετρᾰ-</i> (tetră-, four) +&lrm; <i>μνηστός</i> (mnēstós, “memorable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian: Tetraphernes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Ciθrafarnāh</i>. Doublet of <i>Τισσαφέρνης</i> (Tissaphérnēs) from <i>&#42;Ciçafarnāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Teucri</li></ol><p>Etymologically identical to <i>Τεῦκρος</i> (Teûkros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Teuthras, a Greek from Magnesia slain by Hector</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Teutones, a Germanic tribe</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Teutonēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Teutones; a Teuton</li></ol><p>From Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þeudanaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Teucer</li></ol><p>Anatolian name, probably Luwian, from Proto-Anatolian <i>&#42;tr̥Hʷánts</i> (“Anatolian storm god”), participle form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₂-</i> (“to overcome, pass through”). Cognate with Hittite <i>𒅎</i>; more at Tarḫunz.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) <i>a male surname, famously held by:</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Ἰωάννης Τζέτζης (<i>circa</i> 1110–1180), Byzantine poet and grammarian</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>culture</i>) a land and people of East Asia usually identified as the southern Chinese</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but probably from Sanskrit <i>चीन</i> (Cīna, “China”), possibly via Arabic صِين (Ṣīn, “China; the Chinese”) and usually held to derive from Old Chinese <i>秦</i> (&#42;zin, “Qin”). See "Names of China" at Wikipedia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;culture) alternative form of <i>Τζίνιστα</i> (Tzínista): the southern Chinese; their land, China</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tzimiskes, the surname of Emperor John I</li></ol><p>From Old Armenian <i>Չմշկիկ</i> (Čʻmškik, literally “small boot”), the diminutive of <i>չմուշկ</i> (čʻmušk).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tethys (a Titan)</li></ol><p>Possibly connected with <i>τήθη</i> (tḗthē, “grandmother”), as she is referred to in Homer on two occasions as “mother of the gods”. Alternatively, derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kweth₂-</i> (“to bubble, foam”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Telegonus, the son of Circe and Odysseus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>Τηλέμαχε</i> (Tēlémakhe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Telemachus</li></ol><p>From <i>τηλέμᾰχος</i> (tēlémăkhos, “fighting from afar”), from <i>τῆλε</i> (têle, “far”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, masculine nominal ending).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Telepylus, a town of the Laestrygonians</li></ol><p>From <i>τῆλε</i> (têle, “far off, far away”) +&lrm; <i>πύλη</i> (púlē, “wing of gates”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Temenus; a Temenid</li></ol><p>From <i>Τήμενος</i> (Tḗmenos) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) Tereia, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tereus</li></ol><p>Of Thracian origin</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiarantus, a tributary river of the Danube</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tiastanes, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian satrap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;caṣṭana</i> (“master”) (cf. Khotanese <i>caṣṭeṃ</i>)).</p><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀘𑀱𑁆𑀝𑀦</i> (caṣṭana), Gandhari <i>𐨖𐨛𐨞</i> (chaṭhaṇa) and <i>Ϲιαϲτανϲας</i> (Siastansas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiberius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tiberius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tabal; Tibareni, Anatolia</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Tibareni</li></ol><p>From <i>Τῐβᾰρηνός</i> (Tĭbărēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Tabal or Tibareni; one of the Tibareni</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiberias, Israel</li></ol><p>From <i>Τῐβέρῐος</i> (Tĭbérĭos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tigranes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Tigrānah</i>. See Old Armenian <i>Տիգրան</i> (Tigran).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tigranocerta; Tigranakert</li></ol><p>See Old Armenian <i>Տիգրանակերտ</i> (Tigranakert); Greek adaptation logically derived from <i>Τιγρᾱ́νης</i> (Tigrā́nēs, “Tigranes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tithorea, Phocis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tithraustes, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a natural son of Xerxes I and commander-in-chief against Cimon</li><li>a satrap of Lydia and executor and successor of Tissaphernes</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Ciθravahištah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tithronium, Phocis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tithonos, Tithonus</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>τιτώ</i> (titṓ, “day”) and possibly <i>tītiō</i> (“firebrand”); because of the τ/θ variation, the word could be from a Pre-Greek substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tilataei, a Thracian tribe mentioned by Thucydides</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timanthes</li></ol><p>From <i>τιμή</i> (timḗ) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honor”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Timasion</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμᾰ́ω</i> (tīmắō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ων</i> (-ĭ́ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timocrates</li></ol><p>From <i>τιμή</i> (timḗ) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timocreon</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ) +&lrm; <i>κρέων</i> (kréōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timoleon</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honour”) +&lrm; <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timosthenes</li></ol><p>From <i>τιμή</i> (timḗ, “honor, offer, worth, price”) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength, power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timophanes</li></ol><p>From <i>τιμή</i> (timḗ, “honor, esteem”) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs, “seeming, appearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timodemus</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honor, esteem”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timothy</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμάω</i> (tīmáō, “to honour”) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Timoxenus</li></ol><p><i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honor, esteem”) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “stranger, guest”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Timonax, a Cypriot general and son of Timagoras</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honour, esteem”) +&lrm; <i>ἄναξ</i> (ánax, “king”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tiribazus, from Old Median</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Tīrībāzuš</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tiraeus, from Old Persian, name of two kings of Characene</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Tīrayah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tiridates</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Tīrīdātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tisias</li></ol><p><i>τίσις</i> (tísis) +&lrm; <i>-ας</i> (-as)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tisamenus</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑσάμενος</i> (tīsámenos), aorist middle participle of <i>τίω</i> (tíō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Greek mythology,&#32;Roman mythology) Tisiphone (one of the Erinyes&#47;Dirae, and a companion of Nemesis&#47;Invidia)</li></ol><p>From <i>τίσις</i> (tísis, “payment, punishment, vengeance”) +&lrm; <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder”), literally “avenging murder”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tissaphernes, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>son of Hydarnes III and satrap of Lydia and Ionia under Darius II</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Ciçafarnāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Titans in Greek mythology; a Titan</li><li>Titan, another name for the mythological Greek god Helios</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>τίτο</i> (títo, “sun, day”), which is an Anatolian loan-word, or from <i>τιταίνω</i> (titaínō, “to stretch, to extend”), from <i>τείνω</i> (teínō), or from <i>τίσις</i> (tísis, “retribution”), from <i>τίνω</i> (tínō, “to pay a price as a penalty, expiate”), respectively, as Hesiod attempts to explain. Compare Doric Greek <i>τίτας</i> (títas), Ionic Greek <i>τίτης</i> (títēs, “avenger, punisher”). However, modern scholars doubt Hesiod's etymology.</p><p>Jane Ellen Harrison asserts that the word "Titan" comes from the Greek τίτανος, signifying white "earth, clay, or gypsum," and that the Titans were "white clay men", or men covered by white clay or gypsum dust in their rituals.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Titane, Corinthia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Titanids in Greek mythology; a female Titan; a Titaness</li></ol><p>From <i>Τῑτᾱ́ν</i> (Tītā́n, “Titan”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a battle of Titans</li></ol><p>From <i>Τῑτᾱ́ν</i> (Tītā́n, “Titan”) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Titaresius; Titarisios, Greece</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>from or pertaining to Titarus</li></ol><p>From <i>Τίταρος</i> (Títaros) +&lrm; <i>-ήσιος</i> (-ḗsios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tityus; Tityos</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to swell, to crowd”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ti-tuh₂-o-</i> (a reduplication of <i>*tewh₂-</i>?).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Tlepolemus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔτλην</i> (étlēn, “suffer, endure”) +&lrm; <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos, “battle, war”). See also ἔτλην (étlēn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tlos, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Tmolus, a king of Lydia in Greek mythology</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Tmolus, a later king of Lydia and a mountain god in Greek mythology</li><li>Mount Tmolus, a mountain overlooking Sardis, Lydia, Asia Minor, so-named because the latter king Tmolus was killed on it</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tolmides</li></ol><p>From <i>τόλμᾰ</i> (tólmă, “courage”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tolumnius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tolumnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Toretae, a people of Sarmatia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Torquatus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Torquātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Torone; Toroni, Chalcidice, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Tusculum or Tuscolo; a Tusculan</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tusculānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tullius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Tullius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) A Turk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Classical Persian ترک (turk), from Middle Persian (twlk' /⁠Turk⁠/), from Old Turkic <i>𐱅𐰇𐰼𐰜</i> (t²ür²k̥).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tusculum, Latium; Tuscolo, Lazio, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Tusculum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aydin (a city in Ionia and Lydia, now within modern Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trarium, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tricca, now Trikala in Thessaly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A city in Phoenicia (now Tripoli, Lebanon), founded and administered by a consortium of the cities Tyrus, Sidon and Aradus.</li><li>A region in ancient Libya, known as <i>Regio Tripolitana</i> in Latin, formed by the cities Oea (now Tripoli, the capital of Libya), Sabratha and Leptis Magna.</li><li>A district in ancient Arcadia, Greece, consisting of the cities Calliae, Dipoena and Nonacris; the relation with the capital Tripoli of modern Arcadia is not clear.</li></ol><p>From <i>τρι-</i> (tri-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”); however, for Tripolis in Phoenicia, the name was possibly formed by folk etymology from an earlier, phonetically similar, Semitic name.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Triton (god of the sea, son of Poseidon)</li></ol><p>Uncertain etymology. Related to the second combining form of <i>Ἀμφιτρίτη</i>&nbsp;f (Amphitrítē), his mother's name. Neither belongs to the <i>Τριτογένεια</i> (Tritogéneia) etymological group.</p><p>It has been connected to Ancient Greek <i>τρεῖς</i> (treîs, “three”), in reference to his trident, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tréyes</i>. Also connected to Old Irish <i>triath</i>. Not related to <i>Ἀμφιτρύων</i> (Amphitrúōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tragasae, Troad, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tranipsae, a Thracian tribe mentioned by Xenophon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Trapezuntine (of Trebizond)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Trapezuntine (an inhabitant of Trebizond)</li></ol><p><i>Τρᾰπεζοῦς</i> (Trăpezoûs, “Trebizond”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trapezus, a colony in Pontus founded by Miletus.</li><li>Trabzon, Turkey.</li></ol><p>From <i>τράπεζα</i> (trápeza, “table, flat surface”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trausi, a Thracian tribe settled in the southwestern region of the Rhodopes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trachis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trajanopolis</li></ol><p>Named after the Roman emperor Trajan (Τραϊανός (Traïanós))</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trajan</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Trāiānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trebendae, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Trebia; the river Trebbia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Trebia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tremithus; Tremetousia, Cyprus</li></ol><p>From <i>τρέμιθος</i> (trémithos, “terebinth”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Triballi, a Triballian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trinacria or Thrinacia, an island in Greek mythology, often associated with Sicily</li><li>Trinacria or Thrinacia, a poetic name for Sicily</li></ol><p>Folk-etymologically remodelled from <i>Θρινακία</i> (Thrinakía) on the basis of <i>τρι-</i> (tri-, “three”) and <i>ἄκρᾱ</i> (ákrā, “headland”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trinasus, Laconia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-, three) +&lrm; <i>νῆσος</i> (nêsos, “island”), because of three small rocks situated near the coast.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tripolitan.</li></ol><p>From <i>Τρίπολις</i> (Trípolis, “Tripoli”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, demonym suffix), reanalyzed as <i>Τρίπολ-ῑ́της</i> (Trípol-ī́tēs) (compare <i>πολίτης</i> (polítēs) and Latin <i>-ī̆tānus</i>), from <i>τρι-</i> (tri-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”), referring to the three cities Oea, Sabratha and Leptis Magna.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tripolitania.</li></ol><p>From <i>Τριπολίτης</i> (Tripolítēs, “Tripolitan”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, forming abstract nouns), from <i>Τρίπολις</i> (Trípolis, “Tripoli”) + <i>-της</i> (-tēs, demonym suffix), reanalyzed as <i>Τρίπολ-ῑ́της</i> (Trípol-ī́tēs) (compare <i>πολίτης</i> (polítēs) and Latin <i>-ī̆tānus</i>), from <i>τρι-</i> (tri-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”), referring to the three cities Oea, Sabratha and Leptis Magna.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Triptolemus</li></ol><p>From <i>τρι-</i> (tri-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>πτόλεμος</i> (ptólemos, “war”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tritaea; Tritaia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐταῖος</i> (trĭtaîos, “of the third day”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tritantaechmes, from Old Median, king of the Sagartians, crucified by Darius the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Ciθrantaxmah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tritogenia (an epithet of Athena)</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ́τος</i> (trĭ́tos) +&lrm; <i>-γένεια</i> (-géneia, “birth”). The <i>ῐ</i> (ĭ) of <i>τρῐ́τος</i> (trĭ́tos) is lengthened to <i>ῑ</i> (ī) for the sake of meter: the three short syllables in the sequence <i>short</i>&ndash;<i>short</i>&ndash;<i>short</i>&ndash;<i>long</i>&ndash;<i>short</i> of <i>&#42;Τρῐτογένειᾰ</i> (&#42;Trĭtogéneiă) would break the rules of dactylic hexameter.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tritogenia (an epithet of Athena)</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ́τος</i> (trĭ́tos) + <i>γεν-</i> (gen-), the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai), <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix). The <i>ῐ</i> (ĭ) of <i>τρῐ́τος</i> (trĭ́tos) is lengthened to <i>ῑ</i> (ī) for the sake of meter: the three short syllables in the sequence <i>short</i>&ndash;<i>short</i>&ndash;<i>short</i>&ndash;<i>long</i>&ndash;<i>long</i> of <i>&#42;Τρῐτογενής</i> (&#42;Trĭtogenḗs) would break the rules of dactylic hexameter.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Triphylia</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ́φῡλον</i> (trĭ́phūlon, “triple gate”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Triopian Promontory, Datça Peninsula, Muğla, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Troy</li></ol><p>Of Anatolian origin. Compare Hittite <i>𒋫𒊒𒄿𒊭</i> (Ta-ru-(ú-)i-ša, “Troy”), which must be read /Trūisa/, and also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀵𐀫𐀊</i> (to-ro-ja, “Trojan woman”). in Egyptian (Rʾ-ʾwy, "Troy").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>Τροίᾱ</i> (Troíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic) from Troy</li></ol><p><i>Τροίη</i> (Troíē) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to Troy</li></ol><p>From <i>Τροίην</i> (Troíēn), accusative of <i>Τροίη</i> (Troíē, “Troy”), + <i>-δε</i> (-de, “-wards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Troezen, Peloponnese; Troizina, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from Troezen</li></ol><p><i>Τροιζήν</i> (Troizḗn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trophonius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Troas; the Troad</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female inhabitant of Troy or of the Troad; a female Trojan</li></ol><p>From <i>Τρώς</i> (Trṓs, “Trojan”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the times of Troy: the Trojan War</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Trojan</li></ol><p>From <i>Τρώς</i> (Trṓs, “Trojan”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Trojan</li></ol><p>From <i>Τρώς</i> (Trṓs, “Trojan”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Trogilus, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>Τρώεσσι</i> (Trṓessi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Troy or of the Troas/Troad; a Trojan</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) Trojan</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) Trojan</li></ol><p><i>Τρώς</i> (Trṓs) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Treres, a Thracian tribe</li></ol><p>Likely from Thracian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Troas, Troad</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>Τρώϊος</i> (Trṓïos)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>Τρώϊος</i> (Trṓïos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) Trojans</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Troy or of the Troas/Troad; a Trojan</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tybi, the fifth month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to January</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./76993ec3.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="t" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9b32fa22.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="a" alt="glyph" height="30"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">t&#58;Z5</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">W3&#58;ra</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (tꜣ-ꜥꜣbt).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tydeides, son of Tydeus (epithet of Diomedes)</li></ol><p><i>Τῡδεύς</i> (Tūdeús) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Tydeus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) Tyndareus</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewd-</i> (“to push, hit”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyndareus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Tyndareus; a Tyndarid</li></ol><p>From <i>Τῠνδᾰ́ρεως</i> (Tŭndắreōs, “Tyndareus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a daughter of Tyndareus; a female Tyndarid</li><li>Tyndaris, a city in Sicily</li></ol><p>From <i>Τῠνδᾰ́ρεως</i> (Tŭndắreōs, “Tyndareus”) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyrambae, a tribe of Sarmatia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyrimmas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyrrhenia; Etruria</li></ol><p>From <i>Τυρρηνός</i> (Turrhēnós, “Etruscan”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Etruscan</li></ol><p>From <i>τύρρις</i> (túrrhis, “tower”), from <i>τύρσις</i> (túrsis), itself of Mediterranean (pre-Indo-European) substrate origin; see Latin <i>Etrūscus</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Τυρρηνῐ́ᾱ</i> (Turrhēnĭ́ā, “Tyrrhenia; Etruria”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Τυρρηνός</i> (Turrhēnós, “an Etruscan”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyrodiza, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Typhon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Typhonomachy</li></ol><p><i>Τῡφῶν</i> (Tūphôn) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, typically translated as Tychicus</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠχῐκός</i> (tŭkhĭkós, “casual, fortuitous”), from <i>τῠ́χη</i> (tŭ́khē, “act of god”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tosarene (an ancient province of the kingdom of Armenia, mentioned by Ptolemy)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tomarus, a mountain of Epirus</li></ol><p>A mountain with the same name exists in Berat, Albania, and other places also share a similar name, namely <i>Tomorrica</i> in Skrapar, Related to Greek temno "cut" + oros "mountain" (sharp mountain), from PIE *tom-/*tem- "cut", Vale of Tempe, Tomi city, Tomouroi priests of Dodona (wood-cutters), or made chaste (with tail cut, line oura), or tomarion, diminutive of tomos "slice, piece of land", tome, volume. <i>Τόμαρος</i> (Tómaros) and Illyrian <i>&#42;Temus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰh₁s-o-</i>. Altogether suggests a common Illyrian origin. Alternatively related to Albanian <i>marr</i> (“obtain, get”) from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;mar(en)-</i>, thus meaning "obtainer".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Tomyris, from Proto-Saka: The queen of the Massagetae</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;Taumuriyah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of the Tochari</li></ol><p>Probably from an Indo-Iranian source such as Old Persian (tuxāri-), Khotanese (ttahvāra), Sanskrit <i>तुखार</i> (tukhāra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyana, Cappadocia, Turkey</li></ol><p>From Luwian <i>𔑢𔗬𔐤𔔂</i> (tu-wa/i-na(URBS) /⁠Tuwana⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tymnes, a Carian man mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Tyras, now the river Dniester</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;tūra</i> (“quick, rapid, strong”), from an Indo-Iranian root shared with Sanskrit <i>तुर</i> (tura, “quick, strong”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teur</i> (“strong”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyre, a Phoenician city.</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤑𐤓</i> (ṣr), cognate with Biblical Hebrew <i>צוֹר</i> (ṣôr) and Aramaic <i>טור</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Telos; Tilos, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol><p>Possibly from τηλοῦ (tēloû, “far away”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Temnus, Aeolis, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tenos; Tinos, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tium, Bithynia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>υἱόν</i> (huión)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>υἱός</i> (huiós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>υἱοῦ</i> (huioû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>υἱῷ</i> (huiōî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fabius</li><li>a member of the Roman Fabia gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Fabius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phacium, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phalanna, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phalerum; Faliro, Attica, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>φαλός</i> (phalós, “white”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phantasos</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾰντᾰ́ζω</i> (phăntắzō, “I make visible”), from <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “I shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharbaethos, Lower Egypt, Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharsalus; Farsala, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phereclus, a shipbuilder killed by <i>Meriones</i></li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring, carry”) +&lrm; <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “glory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pheres</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phemius</li></ol><p>from φημί (to speak, to say) + ος (added to verb roots to form o-grade action nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phigalus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philae</li></ol><p>From Demotic Egyptian <i>p-ꜣlq</i>, from Late Egyptian <i>p(ꜣ)-jw-rq</i> (“Philae”, literally “the Island of the Turning”). Compare Sahidic Coptic <i>ⲡⲓⲗⲁⲕ</i> (pilak), Bohairic Coptic <i>ⲡⲓⲗⲁⲕϩ</i> (pilakh), Meroitic <i>𐦧𐦢𐦬𐦡𐦳𐦡</i> (pileqe).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Philebus</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “friend”) +&lrm; <i>ἥβη</i> (hḗbē, “youth”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philippi; a city in Macedon, founded by Philip II.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Philiscus</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐ́λῐσκος</i> (phĭ́lĭskos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Philo</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phinni, an ancient tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol><p>Plural form of <i>Φίννος</i> (Phínnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Fenni or Phinnoi</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finnaz</i>.</p><p>Compare Old Norse <i>Finnr</i> (“a Sami”), Latin <i>Fenni</i>. The name as used by Tacitus is unclear about whom it actually describes. More at Fenni.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boeotian form of <i>Σφίγξ</i> (Sphínx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the light-bringing steeds of Eos</li><li>son of Eos and Cephalus</li><li>Phaëthon (son of Helios, famous for his unlucky driving of the sun-chariot, and subject of a play by Euripides)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the Sun</li><li>the constellation Auriga</li></ol><li>the planet Jupiter</li></ol><p>Use as a proper noun of the masculine forms of <i>φᾰέθων</i> (phăéthōn, “shining”, “radiant”), the present active participle of <i>φᾰέθω</i> (phăéthō, “to shine”, “to be radiant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phaeax</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Phaeacian</li></ol><p>From <i>φαιός</i> (phaiós, “grey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phaedo</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Epic form of <i>Φαίᾱξ</i> (Phaíāx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the planet Saturn (literally “the shiner”)</li></ol><p>Use as a proper noun of the masculine forms of <i>φαίνων</i> (phaínōn, “shining”), the present active participle of <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fabricius</li><li>a member of the Roman Fabricia gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Fābricius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phaedria</li></ol><p>From <i>Φαῖδρος</i> (Phaîdros) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phaesana, Arcadia or Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phaestum (a town in Crete, Greece)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a Pre-Greek name. Compare also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀂𐀵</i> (pa-i-to).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phalasarna, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Falerii; a Faliscan</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Falērius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Phalerum or Faliro</li></ol><p>From <i>Φάληρον</i> (Phálēron, “Phalerum”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phamenoth, the seventh month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to March</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>pꜣ-n-jmn-ḥtp</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phanagoria, Sarmatia, Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perez</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>פָּרֶץ</i> (párets), pausal form of <i>פֶּרֶץ</i> (pérets).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pharandates, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>leader of the Colchian and Marres soldiers at the Battle of Plataea</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Farnadātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Φαραώ</i> (Pharaṓ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Φαραώ</i> (Pharaṓ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Pharisee, a member of an ancient religious sect of Judaism.</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>פְּרִישַׁיָּא</i> (pərîšayyâ’), emphatic plural of <i>פְּרִישׁ</i> (pərîš, “separatist”, literally “separated”), related to Hebrew <i>פרוש</i> (parush), qal passive participle of the verb <i>פָּרַשׁ</i> (pāraš), meaning one who is separated for a life of purity.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharcadon, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharmouthi, the eighth month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to April</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>pꜣ-n-rnn-wtt</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pharnabazus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Farnabāzuš</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pharnaces</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Farnakah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pharnaspes, from Old Persian, father of Cassandane and father-in-law of Cyrus the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Farnāspah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharnacia; Giresun, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>Φαρνάκης</i> (Pharnákēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pharnuches, from Old Median, a Lycian general of Alexander the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Farnukah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pharnouchus, from Old Median</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Farnukah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharsalia</li></ol><p>From <i>Φᾰ́ρσᾱλος</i> (Phắrsālos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharygae, Locris, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phaselis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Phaselis</li></ol><p>From <i>Φᾰσηλῐ́ς</i> (Phăsēlĭ́s, “Phaselis”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phausiades, son of Phausius</li></ol><p>From <i>Φάυσιος</i> (Pháusios) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phaophi, the second month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to October</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">p&#58;n</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">p&#42;t&#58;pr</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (pꜣ-n-jpt).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Faustulus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Faustulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Favonius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Favōnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phaedrus</li></ol><p>From <i>φαιδρός</i> (phaidrós, “bright”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phaenops, son of Asius from Abydus</li></ol><p>From <i>φαῖνοψ</i> (phaînops, “bright-eyed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Faunus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Faunus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) <i>Phidippus</i>, one of the suitors of Helen</li></ol><p>From <i>φείδομαι</i> (pheídomai, “to spare”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phidon; Pheido</li></ol><p>From <i>φείδων</i> (pheídōn, “oil-can with a narrow neck”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to February</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) February</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Februārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Febronia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Febrōnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pheia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pheidias or Phidias</li><li>in particular, the 5th century <small>BCE</small> Greek sculptor and architect Phidias</li></ol><p>From <i>φείδομαι</i> (pheídomai, “to spare”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pheidippides; Phidippides</li></ol><p>From <i>Φείδῐππος</i> (Pheídĭppos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phelloe, Achaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pheneus, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pherae, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pherecrates (celebrated Athenian poet of the Old Comedy)</li></ol><p><i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “bring, bear, carry”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “might, strength, power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, suffix forming third-declension proper nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Pherecydes</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring, bear, carry”) +&lrm; <i>κῦδος</i> (kûdos, “renown, glory”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō) +&lrm; <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē), literally “bringer of victory”. Doublet of <i>Βερενῑ́κη</i> (Berenī́kē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Pherendates, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a son of Megabazus and leader of the Sarangians</li><li>a nephew of Xerxes I and military commander at the Battle of the Eurymedon</li><li>an Achaemenid satrap of Egypt</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Farnadātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name: <i>Pheretima</i>, queen of Cyrenaica and wife of Battus III of Cyrene</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring”) +&lrm; <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “worship, honour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Pheres; a Pheretid</li></ol><p>From <i>Φέρης</i> (Phérēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phegeus, priest of Hephaestus in Troy, slain by Diomedes</li></ol><p>From <i>φηγός</i> (phēgós, “oak”) and +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phegus, Attica, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>φηγός</i> (phēgós, “oak”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phthia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Φθῑ́ᾱ</i> (Phthī́ā, “Phthia, Greece”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phthinthia, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Phthia or Phthiotis; a Phthiote</li></ol><p>From <i>Φθῑ́ᾱ</i> (Phthī́ā, “Phthia”) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the land of Phthia; Phthiotis, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Φθῑώτης</i> (Phthīṓtēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phigalia, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fidenae</li></ol><p>From the singular of Latin <i>Fīdēnae</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Fidenae</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Fīdēnās</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philammon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philaidae, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philadelphia, the name of a number of Ancient cities.</li><li>The name of a festival given in honour of Ptolemy II Philadelphus.</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>Φιλάδελφος</i> (Philádelphos)Ancient Greek <i>Φιλαδέλφεια</i></p><p>From Φιλάδελφος (Philádelphos, “brother-loving”), a surname given to Attalus II Philadelphus by his brother because of his loyalty, from φιλέω (philéō) "I love" and ἀδελφός (adelphós) "brother."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philiades, tyrant of Messina</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλία</i> (philía, “friendship, love, affection”) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Philippian</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>Φίλιπποι</i>&nbsp;m (Phílippoi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philippicus, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>From <i>Φίλιππος</i> (Phílippos, “Philip”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philippopolis</li></ol><p>From <i>Φῐλῐ́ππου</i> (Phĭlĭ́ppou, “Philip's”, genitive) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Philoetius, a cowherd loyal to Odysseus in the Odyssey</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “beloved”) +&lrm; <i>οἶτος</i> (oîtos, “fate”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), literally “beloved of fate”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Philocrates</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλος</i> (phílos) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Philoctetes</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “friend”) +&lrm; <i>κτάομαι</i> (ktáomai, “to acquire”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Philomela, notably borne by the sister of Procne who was raped and mutilated by her brother-in-law Tereus</li><li>(literary&#32;zoology,&#32;originally) swallow</li><li>(literary&#32;zoology,&#32;later) nightingale</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “love”) + <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple; fruit; sheep”), but folk-etymologized since at least the time of Ovid as + <i>μέλος</i> (mélos, “song”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Philopoemen</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Philodemus</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “beloved, loving”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Philolaus</li></ol><p><i>φίλος</i> (phílos) +&lrm; <i>λαός</i> (laós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phineus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flavius</li><li>a member of the Flavia gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Flāvius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flaccus (Roman cognomen)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Flaccus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phlegra</li></ol><p>From <i>φλέγω</i> (phlégō, “burn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flamininus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Flāminīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flaminius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Flāminius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Φλᾱ́βῐος</i> (Phlā́bĭos, “Flavius”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flavius</li><li>a member of the Flavia gens</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Phlegethon</li></ol><p>From <i>φλέγω</i> (phlégō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Phlegraean Fields; Campi Flegrei, Campania, Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>Φλέγρα</i> (Phlégra, “Phlegra”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phlegyas</li></ol><p>From <i>φλέγω</i> (phlégō, “burn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Phliasian (of or from Phlius, a city of Peloponnesus)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phliasian (a native or inhabitant of Phlius, a city of Peloponnesus)</li></ol><p><i>Φλειοῦς</i> (Phleioûs) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phlius, Argolis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Φλειοῦς</i> (Phleioûs, “Phlius”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Florius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Flōrius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoebe</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>Φοῖβος</i> (Phoîbos, “Phoebus”), with the suffix <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoenicia</li></ol><p>From <i>Φοῖνῐξ</i> (Phoînĭx, “Phoenician”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phobetor</li><li>frightener</li></ol><p>From <i>φοβέω</i> (phobéō, “I threaten”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Phoebidas</li></ol><p>From <i>Φοίβη</i> (Phoíbē) +&lrm; <i>-ίδας</i> (-ídas, “patronymic suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoenicia</li></ol><p>From <i>Φοῖνῐξ</i> (Phoînĭx, “Phoenician”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoenicus, Ionia, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix, “date palm”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Folegandros, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Pholoe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a daughter of Phorcys; a Phorcid</li></ol><p>From <i>Φόρκῡς</i> (Phórkūs, “Phorcys”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phormio</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(6th C.,&#32;North African,&#32;historical) a male given name</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>fuscus</i> (“dark, brown”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, male name suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fulvius</li><li>a member of the Roman <i>Fulvia</i> gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Fulvius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Furius</li><li>a member of the Roman Furia gens</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Fūrius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phraata; Ardabil, Iran</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Frank</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Western European; crusader</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>francus</i> (“a Frank”), from Frankish <i>&#42;Franko</i> (“a Frank”), from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;frankô</i> (“javelin”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;prAng-</i> (“pole, stalk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phrearrhi, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fregellae</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Fregellae</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phrixa, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phrixus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Phraates, from Parthian, the name of several kings of Parthia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Parthian <i>&#42;Frahāta</i> (literally “gained, earned”). See Persian فرهاد (farhâd) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Francia</li><li>France</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Francia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phratagune, a wife of Darius the Great</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Frātagaunā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Phrataphernes, from Old Median, a satrap of Parthia and Hyrcania</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;Fratafarnāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Phraortes, from Old Median, son of Deioces and king of Media</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>𐎳𐎼𐎺𐎼𐎫𐎡𐏁</i> (“Fravartiš”, literally “guardian angel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>“the Well-Water Collector”: a punning nickname for the Stoic philosopher Cleanthes, conferred on him because of his means of supporting himself by working nocturnally as a water-carrier to a gardener</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>φρέᾱρ</i> (phréār, “well”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ντλέω</i> (ăntléō, “to draw water”), modelled after <i>Κλεᾰ́νθης</i> (Kleắnthēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>&#42;Φρεᾰττώ</i> (&#42;Phreăttṓ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a court in Peiraeus, where banished men accused of murder were allowed to present themselves for trial, the accused being on board ship, the judges on shore</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Phrygia (a geographic region and ancient kingdom in the west central part of Asia Minor, in what is now modern-day Turkey)</li></ol><p>From <i>Φρῠ́ξ</i> (Phrŭ́x, “Phrygian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phrynichus</li></ol><p>From <i>φρῦνος</i> (phrûnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Phrygian</li></ol><p>Unknown, perhaps of Phrygian origin. Generally connected with the Bryges, an ancient tribe of the Balkans, whose name could be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerǵʰ-</i> (“high, mountain, hill”). More at Bryges.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phylace, Phthiotis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) son of Phyleus (<i>epithet of Meges</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Φῡλεύς</i> (Phūleús) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phytia, Acarnania, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phocis, a region in Greece</li></ol><p><i>Φῶκος</i> (Phôkos, “Phocus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phocion</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>Φωκαιεύς</i> (Phōkaieús, “Phocaean”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Phocaea or Foça; a Phocaean</li></ol><p>From <i>Φώκαιᾰ</i> (Phṓkaiă, “Phocaea”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix forming demonyms). The twelfth-century <i>Etymologicum Magnum</i> criticises the omission of the <i>ι</i> (i, “iota”) in the form <i>Φωκαεύς</i> (Phōkaeús), whilst acknowledging that such omission is common in terms formed on iota-terminal stems suffixed with <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Phocis; a Phocian</li></ol><p>From <i>Φωκῐ́ς</i> (Phōkĭ́s, “Phocis”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Phocas or Phokas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phorbas</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρβω</i> (phérbō, “feed, nourish”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Φόρκῡς</i> (Phórkūs, “Phorcys”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phorcys</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Physca, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin, but perhaps Hellenized from an Ancient Macedonian toponym and fit to match words like <i>φύσκη</i> (phúskē, “wineskin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ancient Greece</i>) Phocaea (an ancient Ionian Greek city on the western coast of Anatolia, now located in İzmir Province, Turkey; modern Foça)</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>Φωκίς</i> (Phōkís, “Phocis”), the region that the settlers of Phocaea supposedly came from; ultimately from <i>φώκη</i> (phṓkē, “seal [sea mammal]”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Photius, notably held by Photios I of Constantinople</li></ol><p>From <i>φῶς</i> (phôs, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharis, Laconia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Phasis; the river Rioni</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek hydronym of an uncertain language; possibly Proto-Georgian-Zan <i>&#42;Poti</i>, Svan <i>&#42;Pasid</i>, or Semitic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Felix, a Roman cognomen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Fēlīx</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Festus, a Roman cognomen</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Fēstus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phocus</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>φώκη</i> (phṓkē, “seal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 1,000 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. An abbreviation of χίλιοι (khílioi, “thousand”). Equivalent to the Roman numeral M.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστέ</i> (Khristé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστόν</i> (Khristón)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστός</i> (Khristós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστέ</i> (Khristé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστόν</i> (Khristón)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστός</i> (Khristós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστοῦ</i> (Khristoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστῷ</i> (Khristōî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστός</i> (Khristós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστοῦ</i> (Khristoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>Χριστῷ</i> (Khristōî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chalce; Chalki; Halki</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós, “bronze”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Chalcon, one of the Myrmidons and father of Bathycles</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “copper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chaones, inhabitants of Chaonia</li></ol><p>From <i>Χάων</i> (Kháōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Chares</li></ol><p>From <i>χάρις</i> (kháris, “beauty, elegance, charm, grace”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Charops, son of Hippasus, brother of Socus, slain by Odysseus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Charybdis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Charon</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰ́ρων</i> (khắrōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chaon</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An inhabitant of Chaonia; a Chaonian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chemmis; Akhmim, Egypt</li></ol><p>From Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homs</li></ol><p>From Arabic حِمْص (ḥimṣ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheops, a king of Egypt</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>ḫw.f-wj</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chersis, a king of Cyprus mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chios (an island in the eastern Aegaean Sea)</li></ol><p>Likely to have come from a Pre-Greek language. Related to <i>Κίος</i> or <i>Κέως</i> ("Ceos island").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chabrias</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chaereas</li></ol><p>From <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō, “rejoice”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Chaerephon</li></ol><p>From <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Chaeronea</li></ol><p>From <i>Χαίρων</i> (Khaírōn) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chaeronea, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Χαίρων</i> (Khaírōn) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chalastra, Mygdonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Chaldea (an ancient geographic region of West Asia, in the southern portion of Babylonia in modern eastern Iraq and southwestern Iran, lying chiefly on the right bank of the Euphrates, but commonly used to refer to the whole of the Mesopotamian plain)</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒆳𒆗𒁺</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>kal-du /⁠Kaldu⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) in Chaldean; in the Chaldean language</li><li>(historical,&#32;used by Philo of Alexandria, now considered a misnomer) in Biblical Aramaic</li></ol><p>From <i>Χαλδαῖος</i> (Khaldaîos) +&lrm; <i>-ιστί</i> (-istí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Chaldean</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒆳𒆗𒁺</i> (/⁠Kaldu⁠/), variant of <i>𒅗𒃻𒁺</i> (/⁠Kašdu⁠/), either by dissimilation or as a rendering of the Chaldean lateral <i>ŝ</i>. Cognate of Biblical Hebrew <i>כַּשְׂדִּי</i> (kaśdī, “Chaldean”), Aramaic <i>כַּשְׂדָּי</i> (kaśdāy, “Chaldaean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chalcidian, inhabitant of Chalcis</li></ol><p><i>Χᾰλκῐ́ς</i> (Khălkĭ́s, “Chalcis”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chalcidice, Chalkidiki (a region of Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>Χᾰλκῐδῐκός</i> (Khălkĭdĭkós) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē). From <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós) + <i>δίκη</i> (díkē), or <i>Χαλκιδ-</i> (Khalkid-) (from <i>Χαλκίς</i> (Khalkís)) + <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós) + <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canaan, a son of Ham in the Torah.</li><li>Canaan, an ancient region corresponding to the Levant.</li></ol><p>Unknown, cognate with Hebrew <i>כְּנַעַן</i> (k'ná'an).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Canaan, an ancient region corresponding to the Levant</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>כְּנַעַן</i> (kənaʿan).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Canaanite</li></ol><p>From <i>Χαναάν</i> (Khanaán, “Canaan”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chaonia</li></ol><p>From <i>Χᾱ́ων</i> (Khā́ōn, “Chaon, its claimed founder”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Charidemus</li></ol><p>From <i>χάρις</i> (kháris) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Charilaus</li></ol><p>From <i>χάρις</i> (kháris, “beauty, elegance”) +&lrm; <i>λαός</i> (laós, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Chariton</li></ol><p>From <i>χάρις</i> (kháris, “grace”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Charadrus, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>χαράδρα</i> (kharádra, “torrent; gorge, ravine”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Kharahostes, from Proto-Saka, an Indo-Scythian satrap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;Xārahōsta</i> (“splendid, mighty”), composed of <i>&#42;xāra</i> (“splendid”) + <i>&#42;hauvasta</i> (“powerful”) (cf. Khotanese <i>hauvasta</i> (“powerful”).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨑𐨪𐨀𐨆𐨯𐨿𐨟</i> (kharaosta).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Charandaei, a tribe of Asia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Chariclo, a nymph married to Chiron</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰ́ρῐς</i> (khắrĭs, “grace”) +&lrm; <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Charicles</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰ́ρῐς</i> (khắrĭs, “grace”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Charmande, Mesopotamia, Iraq</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Charmides</li></ol><p>From <i>Χαρμός</i> (Kharmós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Charnabon, a mythological king of the Getae mentioned by Sophocles</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Kharapallana, from Proto-Saka, the name of an Indo-Scythian satrap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;Xāravalāna</i> (“splendid youth”), composed of Proto-Saka <i>&#42;xāra</i> (“splendid”) (cf. Khotanese <i>&#42;khāra</i> in <i>khārāva</i> (“shining”)) + Proto-Saka <i>&#42;valāna</i> (“youth”) (cf. Khotanese <i>valaka</i> (“young”)).</p><p>Cognate with Prakrit <i>𑀔𑀭𑀧𑀮𑁆𑀮𑀸𑀦</i> (kharapallāna).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ancient Rome,&#32;historical) Orcinus (term used satirically to refer to Senators confirmed by Julius Caesar post-mortem)</li></ol><p>Calque of Latin <i>Orcinus</i>. By surface analysis, <i>Χᾰ́ρων</i> (Khắrōn) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Kislev, the ninth month of the Jewish ecclesiastical calendar</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>כִּסְלֵו</i> (Kislev)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chilon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Hebron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>חֶבְרוֹן</i> (ḥeḇrôn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cheimerium (a city in Thesprotia, Epirus, Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>χειμέριος</i> (kheimérios, “wintry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Cheirisophus, <i>Chirisophus</i></li></ol><p>From <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “skillful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Chephren, from Egyptian, an Egyptian pharaoh and the builder of one of the pyramids</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>ḫꜥ.f-rꜥ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egypt</li></ol><p><div>From Demotic Egyptian <i>kmj</i>, from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6f907176.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="km" alt="glyph" height="42"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./490fdc4a.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="ti" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (kmt, literally “black land”). Cognate with Bohairic Coptic <i>ⲭⲏⲙⲓ</i> (khēmi) and Sahidic Coptic <i>ⲕⲏⲙⲉ</i> (kēme).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Khnum (an Egyptian god)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./978291bb.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="W9" alt="glyph" height="60"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./2b750275.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="E10" alt="glyph" height="67"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i>.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>Χαναάν</i> (Khanaán)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Choaspes; Karkheh River, Iran</li></ol><p>From an Old Persian and Old Median compound word <i>&#42;Uvaspa</i> or <i>&#42;Hṷaspa-</i> (“pos­sessing good horses”), from Old Persian <i>&#42;hu-</i> (“good”) + Old Median <i>&#42;aspa-</i> (“horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Choiak, the fourth month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to December</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./35e77b15.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="kA" alt="glyph" height="60"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Hr&#58;Z1</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./35e77b15.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="kA" alt="glyph" height="60"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (kꜣ-ḥr-kꜣ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Choereae, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chorasmii, an ancient tribe of Chorasmia</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎢𐎺𐎠𐎼𐏀𐎷𐎡𐏁</i> (u-v-a-r-z-mi-i-š /⁠(H)uwārazmiš⁠/). More at Khwarazm.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chorasmia, Khwarazm (a large oasis geographical region on the Amu Darya river delta in western Central Asia; currently split politically between Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan)</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎢𐎺𐎠𐎼𐏀𐎷𐎡𐏁</i> (u-v-a-r-z-mi-i-š /⁠ʰUvārazmiš⁠/). More at Khwarazm.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) A Horite.</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>חֹרִי</i> (ḥōrî). Speculated by some to be related to or identical to Hurrians. See Hurrian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Khosrow; Chosroes</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;Husrawah-</i> , with the suffix adapted to <i>-ης</i> (-ēs); compare Persian خسرو (xosrow) as well as Old Armenian <i>Խոսրով</i> (Xosrov) and Old Georgian <i>ხოსრო</i> (xosro) from the same or a related Iranian source, ultimately from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;Hhucráwāh</i> (literally “Good Fame”) (see there for more). Distantly related to the native Greek name <i>Εὐκλῆς</i> (Euklês).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Οὗννος</i> (Hoûnnos, “Hun”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cusae; El Quseyya, Egypt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>qjs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chremetes, a river of Libya mentioned by Aristotle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrem-</i> (“to resound, thunder, grumble, roar”), the same root of <i>χρόμος</i> (khrómos, “crashing sound”) and <i>χρόμαδος</i> (khrómados, “fragor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>named after Christ</li></ol><p>From <i>Χρῐστός</i> (Khrĭstós, “Christ”) +&lrm; <i>ἐπώνῠμος</i> (epṓnŭmos, “named”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or befitting Christianity or Christians</li></ol><p>From <i>Χρῐστῐᾱνός</i> (Khrĭstĭānós, “Christian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Christopher</li><li>Saint Christopher</li></ol><p>From <i>Χρῑστός</i> (Khrīstós, “Christ”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Χρόμῐος</i> (Khrómĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Chrysis</i></li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chryseis</li></ol><p>From <i>Χρῡ́σης</i> (Khrū́sēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chrysothemis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chrysopolis; Üsküdar, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “golden”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) A Croatian; a Croat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Church Slavonic <i>Хръватъ</i> (Xrŭvatŭ), from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xъrvatъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Croatia (a country on the Balkan Peninsula in Southeast Europe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Chromius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chryse, the name of several people and places in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Chryses</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Chrysippus</li></ol><p>From <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river of Colchis mentioned in the Periplus of Pseudo-Scylax</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Chian</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>&#42;Χῐ́ῐος</i> (&#42;Khĭ́ĭos), from <i>Χῐ́ος</i> (Khĭ́os, “Chios”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Psammis, from Egyptian, an Egyptian pharaoh and son of Necho</li></ol><p>From Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Psammyntus, a king of Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Psenophis, an Egyptian priest mentioned by Plutarch</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Psessii, an ancient tribe of Scythia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Psamathus, Laconia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>ψάμαθος</i> (psámathos, “sand”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Psammetichus, from Egyptian, the name of various Egyptian pharaohs</li></ol><p><div>From Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./78c461f0.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="p" alt="glyph" height="37"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./be92bc63.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="m" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">T&#58;k</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (psmṯk).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Psaumis, famously held by:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Psaumis of Camarina, tyrant of Camarina and Olympic winner</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Egyptian</li></ol><p>From Late Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./97539f73.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="pA" alt="glyph" height="85"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">sbA&#42;xa&#58;n</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">niwt&#58;t</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (pꜣ-sbꜣ-ḫꜥ-n-nwt).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Psyria; Psara, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Psophis, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unwaveringly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not to be transgressed, inviolable, binding</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of laws, decrees, covenants) irrevocable, fixed, permanent</li><li>(of oracles and prophecy) infallible, irrefragable</li><li>(in natural philosophy) immutable, unalterable</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>εἰ</i> (ei, “if”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Tragic form of <i>ἀεί</i> (aeí)</li></ol><p>This was the pre-archaic locative case of αἰών (aiṓn, “(an) eternity, (an) age”), which grammatical case, along with the instrumental case, was later merged and subsumed in Greek under the dative case.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haver-grass (<i>Aegilops neglecta</i>)</li><li>Turkey oak (<i>Quercus cerris</i>) or valonia oak (<i>Quercus ithaburensis</i> subsp. <i>macrolepis</i>)</li><li>ulcer in the eye, lachrymal fistula</li><li>a bulbous plant</li></ol><p>Unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) aegis-bearing (<i>epithet of Zeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰγίς</i> (aigís, “aegis”) +&lrm; <i>ὄχος</i> (ókhos, “bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the aegis; a shield of Zeus or cloak of Athena</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a goat-skin coat</li></ol><li>a rushing storm, hurricane</li><li>a yellow kernel in the pith of the pine</li><li>a speck in the eye</li></ol><p>The original sense was probably "goatskin", in which case, the term is most likely from <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”) + <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, “nominal suffix”), with formation similar to that of <i>νεβρίς</i> (nebrís, “fawnskin”) from <i>νεβρός</i> (nebrós, “fawn”). From this sense developed the "goatskin shield" > "mantle of Zeus and Athena" senses, whose shaking frightens gods and men in traditional mythology.</p><p>The "storm wind" sense appears to have developed or otherwise be influenced by the derived verb <i>ἐπαιγίζω</i> (epaigízō, “to rush furiously upon”), i.e. "rushing upon (with a shield or shaking mantle)" > "a rushing wind". The "kernel, speck" senses would thus seem to be from the "storm" sense, as "eyes of a storm", though this appears to be unmentioned in etymological dictionaries.</p><p>Other theories of the term's origin include derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eyg-</i> (“to stir, set in motion”), via an unattested verb <i>&#42;αἴγω</i> (&#42;aígō), and subsequent connection to Sanskrit <i>एजति</i> (éjati, “to tremble, shake”), as well as tentative links to <i>αἴγλη</i> (aíglē, “gleam, radiance”). There exists a possibility of a folk-etymological conflation and merging of these senses within Hellenic, if the above theories are all correct. Has also been connected to <i>ἀΐσσω</i> (aḯssō, “to move violently”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the poplar</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴγειρος</i> (aígeiros, “black poplar”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warden of the shore</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰγῐᾰλός</i> (aigĭălós, “shore, beach”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beach; shore</li></ol><p>Most likely from <i>ᾱ̓ῐ̈́σσω</i> (āĭ̈́ssō, “to move violently”) and <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “sea”). Connections can also be made with <i>αἶγες</i> (aîges, “waves”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long-eared owl (<i>Asio otus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>goat-faced</li><li>stamped with a goat's face</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>πρόσωπον</i> (prósōpon, “face”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulture</li></ol><p>The comparison with Sanskrit <i>ऋजिप्य</i> (ṛjipya, “epithet of falcon”) is formally difficult; influence by <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”) and <i>γύψ</i> (gúps, “vulture”) has been assumed, but this seems unlikely. The presence of the alternative form and the suffix <i>-οπ-</i> lead to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small kind of owl</li></ol><p>The etymology is still unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fenugreek (<i>Trigonella foenum-graecum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>κέρᾰς</i> (kérăs, “horn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>goat-horned</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;astrology) (<i>astrology</i>) Capricorn (the zodiac sign for the goat, ruled by Saturn and covering December 22 – January 20 (tropical astrology) or January 15 – February 14 (sidereal astrology))</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰγ-</i> (aig-, “goat”, stem prefix) +&lrm; <i>-ο-</i> (-o-, connective interfix) +&lrm; <i>κέρας</i> (kéras, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, Attic declension suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pontic rhododendron (<i>Rhododendron ponticum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>ὄλεθρος</i> (ólethros, “plague, bane”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ashamed</li><li>to fear</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to revere, stand in awe of</li></ol></ol><p><i>αἰδώς</i> (aidṓs, “shame”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳομαι</i> (&#42;-jomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aedile (an official in Ancient Rome)</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>aedilis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>frequently&#32;in the plural</i>) the genitals; pudenda, vulva; penis</li></ol><p>Substantivization of the neuter of <i>αἰδοῖος</i> (aidoîos, “modest, bashful”). Compare similar use of English <i>shame</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modest, bashful</li><li>honored, respected</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰδώς</i> (aidṓs, “shame”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>modest, bashful</li><li>honored, respected</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰδώς</i> (aidṓs, “shame”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame</li><li>respect, awe</li><li>reverence</li><li>modesty</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes mentions that derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eysd-</i> (“to laud, honour”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ōs</i>, though semantically attractive, is formally difficult, as the expected form would be <i>&#42;&#42;αἰζώς</i> (&#42;&#42;aizṓs).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Αἰδώς)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aidos</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰδώς</i> (aidṓs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἀεί</i> (aeí)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eyu-</i> (“lifetime, long time”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>ἀείμνηστος</i> (aeímnēstos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) eternal ancestor, everlasting forefather, never-ending progenitor (<i>epithet of the gods</i>)</li></ol><p><i>αἰεί</i> (aieí) +&lrm; <i>γενέτης</i> (genétēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>αἰείμνηστος</i> (aieímnēstos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>αἰείμνηστος</i> (aieímnēstos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ᾱ̓ετός</i> (āetós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vigorous (physically strong and active)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αἰθέρα</i> (aithéra)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>αἰθέρε</i> (aithére)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>αἰθέρι</i> (aithéri)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sky</li><li>aether; ether</li><li>theoretical medium of great elasticity and extreme thinness of consistency supposed to fill all unoccupied space and transmit light and heat</li><li>the clear sky</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴθω</i> (aíthō, “to kindle”), with the ending <i>-ήρ</i> (-ḗr) perhaps from analogy with <i>ᾱ̓ήρ</i> (āḗr, “mist; air”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>browned, toasted (<i>in the quotation: of cicadae</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>αιθάλη</i> (aithálē, “soot”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sooty</li><li>smoky</li><li>burning</li></ol><p><i>αἴθᾰλος</i> (aíthălos) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>αἴθᾰλος</i> (aíthălos)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>αἴθᾰλος</i> (aíthălos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mediterranean sage (<i>Salvia aethiopis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Αἰθιοπίς</i> (Aithiopís, “Ethiopian”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insult; outrage; affront</li><li>assault; blow</li><li>suffering; disgrace; mistreatment; torture</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>&#42;ἀεικείᾱ</i> (&#42;aeikeíā), from <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>εἰκός</i> (eikós, “reasonable, fair”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to maltreat, mistreat, injure, plague, torment</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a storm</i>) to mar, damage, spoil</li></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;rarely in present, more common in aorist) to be tortured</li></ol><p>Attic form of Epic Greek ἀεικίζω (aeikízō). By surface analysis, <i>αἰκής</i> (aikḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tell of, speak of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to praise, approve</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(Christianity,&#32;transitive) to praise, glorify</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to approve, advise, recommend</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to promise or vow</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>αἶνος</i> (aînos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>praiseworthy</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰνέω</i> (ainéō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horrible, dread, dreadful</li></ol><p>Connected with Sanskrit <i>एनस्</i> (énas, “evil, calamity, atrocity”) by Pokorny.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>horribly, dreadfully</li><li>terribly (very, extremely), dreadfully (exceptionally, eminently, very much), awfully (very, exceedingly)</li></ol><p><i>αἰνός</i> (ainós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with fast steeds</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰόλος</i> (aiólos, “swift, nimble”) +&lrm; <i>πῶλος</i> (pôlos, “foal, colt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of an oracle</i>) shifting in speech</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰόλος</i> (aiólos, “swift, nimble”) +&lrm; <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high, steep</li><li>rocky, rugged</li><li>(<i>of cities</i>) set on a steep slope, on the top of a hill: high, lofty</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goatherd</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”) + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-kʷolos</i> (“-herd”) (compare <i>βουκόλος</i> (boukólos, “cowherd”)), from <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to turn, cultivate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of cities</i>) on a slope: high, lofty</li><li>(<i>of streams</i>) flowing steeply down</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>steep; sheer</li><li>(<i>of cities</i>) set on a steep slope, on the top of a hill: high, lofty</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sheer, utter</li></ol><p>Furnée connects it with <i>ἐξαίφνης</i> (exaíphnēs, “on a sudden”) and <i>ἄφνω</i> (áphnō, “suddenly”). Probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>Compare <i>αἰπός</i> (aipós), <i>αἰπεινός</i> (aipeinós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of hawk mentioned by Aristotle, perhaps the Bonelli's eagle (<i>Aquila fasciata</i>) or the merlin (<i>Falco columbarius</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée suggests a Pre-Greek origin in view of the variation <i>λ/ρ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to perceive, apprehend, notice [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> nominative participle <small>‘oneself doing, that one is doing’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> genitive <small>‘doing something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> <b>ὅτι</b> (hóti) <i>or</i> <b>ὡς</b> (hōs, + verb) <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to understand</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to learn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awistʰomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewis-dʰh₁-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂ew-</i> (“to see, perceive”), from which also comes <i>ἀΐω</i> (aḯō). Cognates include Latin <i>audiō</i> (“to hear”), Sanskrit <i>आविस्</i> (āvís, “openly, manifestly, evidently”), and Hittite <i>𒌋𒀪𒄭</i> (u-uḫ-ḫi, “I see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who perceives</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσθάνομαι</i> (aisthánomai) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for perception of the senses; sensitive, perceptive</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) perceptible</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴσθησῐς</i> (aísthēsĭs, “perception, sensation”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameful, base, ignoble</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”), with adjectival suffix <i>-ημων</i> (-ēmōn); possibly influenced by <i>ἀσχήμων</i> (askhḗmōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>αἰσχρουργέω</i> (aiskhrourgéō)</li></ol><p>Uncontracted form of αἰσχρουργέω (aiskhrourgéō), from <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”) + <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “deed, work”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sordidly greedy for gain; shamefully avaricious</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”) + <i>κέρδος</i> (kérdos, “profit, gain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foul language, abuse</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρολόγος</i> (aiskhrológos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foul-mouthed, obscene in speech</li><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) with obscene or shameful language</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”) + <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “speech, word”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>foully, obscenely; with foul or indecent language</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρολόγος</i> (aiskhrológos) + <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) obscene act</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful, obscene”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to do, make”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>moral</i>) to practise unspeakable or shameful acts; to engage in taboo conduct</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρρητος</i> (árrhētos, “unspeakable”) + <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to do, make”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>moral</i>) acting shamefully; performing obscene acts</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) committing a specific obscene sexual act</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful, obscene”) + <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to do, make”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>engaged in shameful acts; disgraceful in conduct</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”) +&lrm; <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō, “to do; to act”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shamefulness; turpitude; moral baseness; public disgrace</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perform shameful or obscene acts</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to perform sexual acts</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;lexicography) a general explanatory gloss for verbs denoting obscene acts involving the tongue, such as <i>γλωττοδεψέω</i> (glōttodepséō)</li></ol></ol><p>Contracted form of <i>αἰσχροεργέω</i> (aiskhroergéō), from <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”) + <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “deed, work”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>moral</i>) shameful or disgraceful conduct; licentiousness</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) obscenity; lewd or indecent speech</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”) + <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “deed, work”), with abstract noun suffix <i>-ία</i> (-ía); compare <i>αἰσχρουργός</i> (aiskhrourgós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obscene, shameful; characterized by sexually indecent conduct</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”) + <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “deed, work”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>moral</i>) shameful or obscene act; sexually indecent deed</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρουργέω</i> (aiskhrourgéō, “to commit shameful acts”), from <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful”) + <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “deed, work”), with result noun suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) causing shame; dishonoring; reproachful; disgraceful</li><li>(<i>of appearance</i>) ugly; ill-favored; deformed</li><li>ill-suited; improper; inappropriate; unfitting (especially to circumstances or purpose)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) physically deformed; disfigured</li></ol><p>From <i>αἶσχ(ος)</i> (aîskh(os), “shame”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ugliness' deformity</li><li>(ethics,&#32;metaphysics) the disordered and misshapen condition of a soul</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) obscene or socially offensive conduct</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>shameful behavior</li><li>obscene speech</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) bodily fluids from sex</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós, “shameful, ugly”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>shamefully, basely</li><li>disgracefully, in a dishonourable way</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) obscenely, in an obscene way</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós) (“shameful, base, ugly”) + <i>-ῶς</i> (-ôs), forming an adverb of manner.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make shameful, disgrace, dishonor</li><li>to treat as shameful; to bring reproach upon</li></ol><p>Denominative from <i>αἶσχος</i> (aîskhos, “shame, disgrace”). A rare factitive verb, overlapping in meaning with <i>αἰσχύνω</i> (aiskhúnō), and noted as stylistically marked by ancient grammarians.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame, disgrace; a state of dishonor or social ignominy</li><li>sense of shame, modesty, or moral restraint (subjective, ethical sense)</li><li>(<i>countable, often plural</i>) a shameful or disgraceful act</li><li>(<i>late, rare</i>) the genital organs (euphemistic)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>αἶσχος</i> (aîskhos, “disgrace”). According to Beekes, a back-formation from <i>αἰσχύνω</i> (aiskhúnō, “to disgrace”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to make ugly; disfigure</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to dishonor; disgrace; violate</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to feel shame; be ashamed; to shrink from disgrace</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσχῡ́νη</i> (aiskhū́nē) +&lrm; <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;transitive</i>) to ask for, crave, demand, beg [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘from someone’</small>]; or with object omitted</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ask for [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(logic,&#32;transitive) to postulate, assume</li></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;transitive) to ask for oneself, for one's own use or purpose, to claim</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of persons) to have a thing begged of one</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) to be asked for</li></ol></ol><p>A denominative of an unattested <i>&#42;αἶτος</i> (&#42;aîtos, “something given: allotment, share”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ey-</i> (“to give, take”), whence also <i>αἴνυμαι</i> (aínumai), <i>αἶσα</i> (aîsa), <i>αἴτιος</i> (aítios), <i>διαιτάω</i> (diaitáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αἰτίᾱ</i> (aitíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charge, accusation, imputation, blame, guilt, fault</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a good sense</i>) credit</li><li>expostulation, admonition</li></ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) cause</li><li>occasion, opportunity, motive</li><li>category</li></ol><p>Either a derivative of <i>αἴτῐος</i> (aítĭos, “causing; guilty”) or directly from unattested <i>&#42;αἶτος</i> (&#42;aîtos, “share”). In either case, from <i>&#42;h₂ey-</i> (“to give”), whence also <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō, “to ask”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to beg (for), importune</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>asked for, requested</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō, “to ask”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accuse</li><li>to blame</li><li>to charge</li></ol><p><i>αἰτίᾱ</i> (aitíā) +&lrm; <i>-άομαι</i> (-áomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;grammar) the accusative case &ndash; elliptically for <i>αἰτῐᾱτῐκὴ πτῶσις</i> (aitĭātĭkḕ ptôsis, literally “inflection expressing an effect”)</li></ol><p>See <i>αἰτῐᾱτῐκός</i> (aitĭātĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>causal</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰτῐᾱτός</i> (aitĭātós, “caused”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, adjective suffix): “relating to an effect”. The Latin calque <i>accūsātīvus</i> (from which comes English <i>accusative</i>) came from an analysis of the word as deriving from <i>αἰτιάομαι</i> (aitiáomai, “to accuse”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix), but if that were its derivation, it would have a different meaning: “related to accusing”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) giving the cause of something</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰτῐ́ᾱ</i> (aitĭ́ā, “cause”) +&lrm; <i>-λογίᾱ</i> (-logíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>αἴτιος</i> (aítios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>αἴτῐος</i> (aítĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw or hurl a spear</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to arm with a spear</li></ol><li>to perform as a warrior, perform feats of arms</li><li>to wound</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰχμή</i> (aikhmḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>captured, taken prisoner in war</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prisoner of war</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰχμᾰλόω</i> (aikhmălóō, “to take prisoner”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos), from <i>αἰχμή</i> (aikhmḗ, “spear, war”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point of a spear</li><li>head of an arrow</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) war</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) warlike spirit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aiksmā</i>, whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀏𐀭𐀔</i> (a3-ka-sa-ma /⁠*aiksmans⁠/); from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eyḱ-sm-o&#47;eh₂-</i>, whence also Old Prussian <i>aysmis</i> and Lithuanian <i>iēšmas</i>, <i>jiēšmas</i> (“spit, broach”); ultimately from <i>&#42;h₂eyḱ-</i> (“to sting; sharp tip, barb”), the same root as possibly Latin <i>īcō</i> (“to stab, sting”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiglaz</i>, <i>&#42;aigin</i> (“shoot, barb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take prisoner, capture</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰχμάλωτος</i> (aikhmálōtos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;rare) alternative form of <i>αἰχμαλωτίζω</i> (aikhmalōtízō, “to take prisoner”)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰχμή</i> (aikhmḗ, “spear, war”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, verb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) spearman</li></ol><p><i>αἰχμή</i> (aikhmḗ, “spear”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eternity, perpetuity, temporal endlessness (<i>in abstract and non-countable senses</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰώνιος</i> (aiṓnios, “pertaining to an age/an eternity/a very long time”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick, nimble, swift</li><li>wriggling (of worms)</li><li>glittering, sheeny (of armours)</li><li>chequered, changeful</li><li>shifty, slippery</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. The word has been connected with <i>αἰών</i> (aiṓn, “lifetime”) and Sanskrit <i>आयु</i> (āyu, “life, vital force”) (&lt; <i>&#42;h₂óyu</i>), but this is formally improbable. Alternatively, the word may be from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;walwólos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welwel-</i>, a reduplication of the root <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lifetime; life</li><li>generation; race</li><li>an undefined, relatively long period of time</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(an) eon; (an) epoch, (an) age</li><li>(<i>as countable things</i>): (an) eternity, (a) perpetuity</li></ol><li>forever (in a nominal sense)</li><li>remote past</li><li>very long stretch of time ahead</li><li>a world to come; a future age</li><li>the current world; current age</li><li>world; space</li><li>(Judaism,&#32;Christianity) messianic age</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>αἰϝών</i> (aiwṓn), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eyu-</i> (“vital force, life, long life, eternity”), whence also <i>ἀεί</i> (aeí, “always”). Cognate with Latin <i>aevum</i>, English <i>aye</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Αἰών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>personified</i>) a personification or divine hypostasis of time, lifespan, or eternity</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>age-long; lasting for a long or indefinite period of time; long-lasting</li><li>lasting for life; permanent (esp. of an office or civic title)</li><li>(<i>philosophical</i>) perpetual, eternal, everlasting; denoting a mode of duration not measured by ordinary time (contrasted with χρόνος or πρόσκαιρος)</li><li>(<i>eschatological</i>) perpetual, eternal, everlasting; lasting without end</li><li>(rare,&#32;technical,&#32;Roman) pertaining to a formally defined age-cycle or ceremonial period (≈ Latin <i>saecularis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰών</i> (aiṓn, “age, eon”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swing, hammock, chariot on springs</li><li>noose, halter</li><li>oscillatory movement, seesaw</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) passive exercise</li><li>(<i>of mind</i>) fluctuation</li></ol><p>Previously, an iterative verb <i>&#42;ϝαι-ϝωρ-έω</i> (&#42;wai-wōr-éō) was reconstructed, from which this word was a derivation. This type is not accepted anymore. Taillardat assumes a derivation from the root of <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō, “to lift, raise up”), with a successive reduplication. However, Beekes finds this uncertain and leaves the etymology open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of blood, bloody</li><li>blood red (of glass)</li><li>physical, biological, human</li></ol><p>From <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall of dry stones</li></ol><p>Beekes compares the word with Latin <i>saepēs</i> (“hedge”), with a change from "p" to "m".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodlike (<i>used chiefly in fixed expressions; see Derived terms</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered or mingled with blood, bloody, bloodstained, bleeding</li><li>suffused with blood, flushed</li><li>of the color of blood, blood-red</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) bloody: murderous, violent</li></ol><p>From <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hemorrhage</li></ol><p>From <i>αἱμο-</i> (haimo-) + the root of <i>ῥήγνυμι</i> (rhḗgnumi) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) veins liable to discharge blood, especially haemorrhoids, piles</li><li>a kind of shellfish, perhaps <i>Aporrhais pespelecani</i></li></ol><p>From <i>αἱμορροέω</i> (haimorrhoéō, “to lose blood”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), from <i>αἱμο-</i> (haimo-, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”). Also serves as the feminine of <i>αἱμόρροος</i> (haimórrhoos, “flowing with blood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) blood-fearing, afraid of bleeding</li></ol><p>From <i>αἱμο-</i> (haimo-, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>φοβέω</i> (phobéō, “to fear”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) dues paid on discharge of cargoes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take, grasp, seize</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to win, gain</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to convict, win a conviction</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;transitive) to grasp with the mind, understand</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;transitive) to take for oneself, choose, select</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;transitive) to prefer</li></ol><p>Rix derives the word from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;həřřéyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ser-</i> (“to take, grasp, plunder”) (though Beekes seems implicitly skeptical and leaves the origin open). The aorist stem appears to be from the synonymous <i>&#42;selh₁-</i> (“to take”), which is also the source of English <i>sell</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader of a school</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) heresiarch</li></ol><p>From <i>αἵρεσις</i> (haíresis, “school of thought”) + <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of a sect</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>heretic</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>αἵρεσις</i> (haíresis, “sect”) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs, suffix for person concerned).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to choose</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) heretical</li><li>(Koine,&#32;nominalized) heretic</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) conditional</li></ol><p>From <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>expresses astonishment or grief</li></ol><p>Of expressive origin.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wild goat</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”) and <i>ᾰ̓γρός</i> (ăgrós, “land”) (compare with <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black poplar (<i>Populus nigra</i>)</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>αἰγίλωψ</i> (aigílōps, “a kind of oak”) is uncertain. According to Beekes, the noun is of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common linnet (<i>Linaria cannabina</i>)</li></ol><p>A typical Pre-Greek word, discernible from its prenasalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>αἰγίλωψ</i> (aigílōps, “haver-grass”)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”). Considered present in the toponym Ancient Greek <i>Αἴγιλα</i>&nbsp;f (Aígila), <i>Αἰγιλία</i>&nbsp;f (Aigilía) which is also attested as Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁀𐀑𐀨</i> (a2-ki-ra) in Na 856.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of goat</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴξ</i> (aíx, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light of the sun or moon</li><li>radiance of Olympus</li><li>radiance, gleam</li><li>splendour, glory</li></ol><p>The connection with Sanskrit <i>एजति</i> (éjati, “to tremble, shake”) has been rejected. The epithets of Apollo <i>Ἀσγελατας</i> (Asgelatas) and <i>Αἰγλάτας</i> (Aiglátas) are often compared. The epithets are probably from Pre-Greek. According to Beekes, the noun may be of Pre-Greek origin too.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Homeric form of <i>αἰδέομαι</i> (aidéomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αἴθε</i> (aíthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoky flame and thick black smoke rising up from the sacked, scorched Troy</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>soot</li><li>a cinder, an ember, a scintilla</li></ol><li>a kind of black grape grown in Egypt</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burnt-coloured, dark brown, dusky, pitchy, blackish, dark</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀲𐀫</i> (a3-ta-ro), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;áitʰalos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eydʰ-</i> (“fire”). Compare Albanian <i>jezull</i> (“cloud of dust or smoke; ashes”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ailą</i> (“fire”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Αἴθαλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Cognate to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀲𐀫</i> (a3-ta-ro).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εἴθε</i> (eíthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of smoke</i>) blackish</li><li>burning, combustible</li></ol><p>From <i>αἶθος</i> (aîthos, “heat, fire”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) portico, veranda</li><li>poison hemlock (<i>Conium maculatum</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally explained as a derivation from <i>αἴθω</i> (aíthō, “to glow”) but this is a folk etymology. The word is more likely from Pre-Greek (albeit with semantic reinforcement from the folk etymology), due to the likely-related <i>αἴδῶσσα</i> (aídôssa, “the walls of a court of hall”) demonstrating significant consonantal variation, as well as the variant <i>αἴθουσσα</i> (aíthoussa) showing σ/σσ variation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clear sky</li><li>nice weather</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰθήρ</i> (aithḗr) +&lrm; <i>-α</i> (-a).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Αἴθρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aethra</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a diving bird, perhaps the shearwater</li></ol><p>Usually derived from <i>αἴθω</i> (aíthō, “to kindle”), comparing <i>αἰθός</i> (aithós, “sparkling, glowing; dark-coloured”). However, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin of the word, in view of the suffix "-υια", found also in <i>ἄγυια</i> (águia, “street, highway”), <i>ἅρπυια</i> (hárpuia, “harpy”) and <i>κώδυια</i> (kṓduia, “head; bulb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ignite, kindle, light</li><li>(rarely&#32;intransitive,&#32;more commonly&#32;in the&#32;middle voice) to burn, blaze</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;áitʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eydʰ-</i> (“burn; fire”).</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>aestus</i>, <i>aestās</i>, and <i>aedis</i>, and Sanskrit <i>इन्द्धे</i> (inddhé, “to light, set on fire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instance of burning, blazing, lightning</li><li>a thing which blazes and flashes like burnished metal</li><li>of various animals: horse, lion, bull, eagle, fox</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>men who are fiery</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>αἴθω</i> (aíthō, “to kindle, blaze”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Αἴθων)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of the eagle that eats the liver of Prometheus</li></ol><p>See <i>αἴθων</i> (aíthōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat; domestic cat, tom (<i>male</i>), queen (<i>female</i>)</li><li>weasel</li></ol><p>Possibly referring originally to wild cats (as opposed to domestic cats); longstanding tradition holds the word to derive from a compound of <i>αἰόλος</i> (aiólos, “fast moving, nimble”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”), though the <i>-ε-</i> (-e-) in the earlier-attested form <i>αἰέλουρος</i> (aiélouros) raises phonetic issues. While this derivation has the feel of folk etymology, which Beekes notes is a possibility, it may nonetheless be the case. Has also been compared to Latin <i>viverra</i> (“ferret”), <i>feles</i> (“cat”), and Lithuanian <i>vaiveris</i> (“polecat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>praise</li></ol><p><i>αἰνέω</i> (ainéō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riddle</li><li>taunt</li><li>An obscure method of communication</li><li>ambush</li></ol><p>Verbal noun from the root of <i>αἰνίσσομαι</i> (ainíssomai, “speak in riddles”), from <i>αἶνος</i> (aînos, “fable”), +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;chiefly&#32;Epic) to take, take off, take hold of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ainúmai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ey-</i> (“to give, be given”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-néw-</i>. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁁𐀝𐀕𐀜</i> (a3-nu-me-no /⁠Ainumenos⁠/), Umbrian <i>𐌀𐌉𐌕𐌖</i> (aitu), further with Tocharian B <i>ai-</i>, Tocharian A <i>e-</i> (“to give, take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goat (especially a she-goat)</li><li>a kind of waterfowl; possibly a goose</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aíks</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eyǵ-</i> (“goat”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>այծ</i> (ayc), Sanskrit <i>एड</i> (eḍa), and Albanian <i>dhi</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of darnel</li></ol><p>From <i>αἶρα</i> (aîra, “darnel”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A branch of myrtle or laurel</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but according to Nehring from Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Αἴσακος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a male given name</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Aesacus (son of Priam who mourned Cebren’s daughter)</li><li>the name of a centaur</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perception from the senses, feeling, hearing, seeing</li><li>Perception by the intellect as well as the senses</li><li>That which is perceived: scent</li><li>Ability to perceive: discernment</li><li>Cognition or discernment of moral discernment in ethical matters</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰσθάνομαι</i> (aisthánomai, “to perceive”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destined</li><li>due, suitable, right</li></ol><p>From <i>αἶσα</i> (aîsa, “fate, destiny”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unseemly, evil, impious</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the overall appearance is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>request, demand</li><li>(logic,&#32;mathematics) postulate, assumption</li></ol><p>From <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō, “to demand”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>request, demand</li></ol><p><i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>causing, being the author of, responsible for</li><li>to blame, blameworthy, guilty, reprehensible, culpable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defendant, the accused, culprit</li></ol><p>From an unattested <i>&#42;αἶτος</i> (&#42;aîtos, “share”) + <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ey-</i> (“to give”). Cognates include <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō, “to ask”), Tocharian B <i>ai-</i> (“to give, take”), Oscan <i>aetis</i> (“part (of a possession)”), and perhaps Avestan <i>𐬀𐬉𐬙𐬀</i> (aēta) (though the latter is disputed by Beekes).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking, receiving</li><li>a choice, selection</li><li>a purpose</li><li>a system of principles</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;religion) a school of thought, sect</li><li>(Catholicism,&#32;Koine) a heresy</li></ol><p><i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>γαῖᾰ</i> (gaîă)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Αἶα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aea, Colchis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) waves</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goat (especially a she-goat)</li><li>a kind of waterfowl; possibly a goose</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aíks</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eyǵ-</i> (“goat”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>այծ</i> (ayc), Sanskrit <i>एड</i> (eḍa), and Albanian <i>dhi</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat, fire</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>αἴθω</i> (aíthō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). The term could be considered a descendant of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;áitʰos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eydʰ-</i>. The same Proto-Indo-European s-stem is reflected in Sanskrit <i>एधस्</i>&nbsp;n (édhas). However, the Greek term is attested relatively late, and the semantics of the Greek word (which means "heat") and the Sanskrit word (which means "firewood") are incongruent. Therefore, the term is often considered a deverbative of <i>αἴθω</i> (aíthō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of metal</i>) bright, sparkling, gleaming (especially of copper and iron, as Homeric epithets)</li><li>(<i>of wine</i>) sparkling</li><li>(<i>of smoke or combustion</i>) mixed with flame, blazing</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fiery; intense</li></ol><p><i>αἶθος</i> (aîthos, “heat, fire”) +&lrm; <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “face, appearance”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the clear chill air (of morning)</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴθρᾱ</i> (aíthrā) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening meal at Sparta</li></ol><p>Furnée points to <i>εἶκλον</i> (eîklon) and compares <i>ἰκνεῖος</i> (ikneîos), concluding that this is a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tale or story; story with a moral, fable</li><li>praise</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Pokorny suggests Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ey-</i> (“important speech”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Αἶνος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aenus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ryegrass, darnel (<i>Lolium temulentum</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to Sanskrit <i>एरका</i> (erakā, “a kind of grass”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destiny, fate</li><li>(<i>personified</i>) a goddess of destiny</li><li>portion, morsel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ey-</i> (“to give, take”), whence also <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō, “to ask for, beg”) and <i>&#42;αἶτος</i> (&#42;aîtos, “something given: allotment, share”), + the suffix +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă). Cognate with Oscan <i>aitis</i> (“part, portion”); likely related to <i>οἶτος</i> (oîtos). For a semantic parallel (from a different root), compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlutą</i> (“lot; fate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shame; disgrace; a state of public reproach or dishonor</li><li>(Epic,&#32;in the plural) disgraceful deeds, shameful acts or words</li><li>ugliness; deformity; repulsiveness, of body or mind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) physical deformity; disfigurement</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) blemish; impropriety (especially of expression or name)</li></ol></ol><p>The older comparison with Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐍅𐌹𐍃𐌺𐌹</i> (aiwiski, “shame”) is generally abandoned in view of formal difficulties. De Lamberterie plausibly compares <i>αἰδέομαι</i> (aidéomai, “to be ashamed”), positing <i>&#42;aid-sk-</i> for Greek, next to <i>&#42;aid-st-</i> in Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (aistan, “to have regard for”). Furnée connects the word with Georgian <i>ურცხვი</i> (urcxvi, “shameless”), <i>მორცხვი</i> (morcxvi, “shy, coy”) and <i>სირცხვილი</i> (sircxvili, “shame, blemish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αἶψα</i> (aîpsa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, all of a sudden</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) streams of blood</li><li>bloodshed; murder</li><li>race; stock; kin, relationship by blood</li></ol><p>Uncertain and contested, as many competing theories exist. One possibility is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂y-</i> (traditionally <i>&#42;say-</i>) (“thick or viscous liquid”), related to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;saim</i> (whence German <i>Seim</i> (“viscous fluid”)), although some sources reject this as phonologically improbable. A different theory relates it to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;s(w)okʷós</i> (“plant juice, resin”), which would make it related to <i>ὀπός</i> (opós, “plant juice”). Other suggestions include a relation to Ancient Greek <i>αἰονάω</i> (aionáō, “to moisten”) or to Ancient Greek <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi, “to throw, to flow”), thereby making it a doublet of <i>ἧμᾰ</i> (hêmă, “projectile”). Finally, a Northwest Semitic borrowing has been posited, related to Aramaic <i>חַיִּים</i> (ḥayyim, “life”), Hebrew <i>חַיִּים</i> (ḥayím, “life”), Phoenician <i>𐤇𐤉𐤌</i> (ḥym, “long life”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light of the sun, sunlight</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) rays, beams</li><li>dawn</li><li>any bright light, as of fire</li><li>any gleam on the surface of bright objects, sheen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewg-</i> (“to lighten up”), which is likely a semantic extension of <i>&#42;h₂ewg-</i> in the usual "to increase" sense. Cognates might include Albanian <i>ag</i> (“dawn”) and <i>agój</i> (“to dawn”) and Old Church Slavonic <i>югъ</i> (jugŭ, “South, south wind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak, utter</li><li>to speak to, address</li></ol><p><i>αὐδή</i> (audḗ, “human voice”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō, verb suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>human voice; speech</li><li>sound</li><li>report; account</li><li>oracle</li><li>song; ode</li></ol><p>Likely cognate to Sanskrit <i>वदति</i> (vádati, “to say, speak”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vaditi</i> (“to argue”), which would suggest derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wed-</i> (“to speak”). The details of the potential relation with Ancient Greek <i>ἀείδω</i> (aeídō, “to sing”) are unclear; see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking with a human voice</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐδή</i> (audḗ) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>αὐδήσασκε</i> (audḗsaske)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αὐδήσασκε</i> (audḗsaske) (<i>before rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the same year</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same year</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ᾰ̓ϝετής</i> (ăwetḗs), from <i>ᾰ̔-</i> (hă-, copulative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ϝέτος</i> (wétos, “year”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same year</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ᾰ̓ϝετής</i> (ăwetḗs), from <i>ᾰ̔-</i> (hă-, copulative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ϝέτος</i> (wétos, “year”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a perpetrator of any act, especially that he commits a murder with his own hand</li><li>a suicide, a person who has intentionally killed himself</li><li>an absolute ruler, an autocrat</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;έντης</i> (&#42;éntēs), from the full-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;senh₂-</i> (“to prepare, work on, succeed”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix), whence also <i>ἀνύω</i> (anúō, “I effect, accomplish”), <i>συνέντης</i> (sunéntēs, “helper, accomplice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-chosen, self-elected</li><li>by free choice, of one's own accord, independent</li><li>self-incurred, voluntary</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō, “to take”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warranted, authentic, authoritative</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐθέντης</i> (authéntēs) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan form of <i>ἀλκήν</i> (alkḗn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan form of <i>ἀλκυόνα</i> (alkuóna)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play on the flute</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of tunes) to be played on the flute</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(passive voice,&#32;of persons) to be played to, to hear music</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>open court, courtyard</li><li>quadrangle</li><li>hall, chamber</li><li>dwelling, house</li></ol><p>Most plausibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wes-</i> (“pass the night”) (> <i>&#42;h₂ews-</i> by Schwebeablaut) + <i>&#42;-leh₂</i>: it has been suggested that Tocharian A <i>olar</i> (“companion”) and Tocharian B <i>aulāre</i> are cognate and contain the same suffix. Related to <i>αὖλις</i> (aûlis), <i>ἰαύω</i> (iaúō), <i>ἄεσα</i> (áesa), as well as Old Armenian <i>աւթ</i> (awtʻ, “place to spend the night”). Despite similar phonetics, apparently unrelated to <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “hollow tube, pipe, flute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curtain, especially in the theatre</li><li>hunting net</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) screens to protect a wall against missiles</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) tent</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐλή</i> (aulḗ) + <i>-αίᾱ</i> (-aíā), feminine of <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) a flute-player, flautist.</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐλέω</i> (auléō, “to play the flute”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) A flute-girl in ancient Greece, whose services may also have been sexual.</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>αὐλητής</i> (aulētḗs, “flute-player”), from <i>αὐλέω</i> (auléō, “to play the flute”) +&lrm; <i>-τρις</i> (-tris, feminine agent-noun suffix) from <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the court, courtier-like</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courtier</li></ol><p><i>αὐλή</i> (aulḗ, “courtyard”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flute-case</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown kind of fish, perhaps:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>dusky grouper (<i>Epinephelus marginatus</i>), or</li><li>albacore, longfin tuna (<i>Thunnus alalunga</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “tube, pipe”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “face”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās), but compare also <i>ἀλλοπίας</i> (allopías, “kind of tuna”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) any pipe-shaped instrument&#58; flute, clarinet, pipe</li><li>hollow tube, pipe, groove, shaft</li><li>blowhole, duct</li><li>stadium</li><li>haulm (of grain)</li><li>cowbane (<i>Cicuta virosa</i>)</li><li>razor shell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aulós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewlós</i>, nominalization of <i>&#42;h₂ewlo-</i> (“tube, hollow, channel”).</p><p>Cognates include Lithuanian <i>aulas</i>, <i>avilỹs</i>, Norwegian <i>aul</i>, Hittite (auli-, “tube-shaped organ in the neck”), Albanian <i>hollë</i>, Old Armenian <i>օղ</i> (ōł), and perhaps also Latin <i>alvus</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>улица</i> (ulica, “small lane, narrow street”), and Old Armenian <i>ուղի</i> (ułi, “road, way, passage”). Semantically compare Sanskrit <i>वेणु</i> (veṇu, “reed, tube; flute, pipe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who sings to the flute</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute”) +&lrm; <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make grow, increase</li><li>to promote, honour, exalt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂weg-</i> (“to enlarge”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wahsijaną</i>. The present tense system includes a <b>N</b> progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>astringent, lending to a dry mouthfeel (usually translated as bitter, harsh)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>αὖος</i> (aûos, “dry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(marking contrast or transition,&#32;sometimes paired with <i>μέν</i> (mén)) but, however, besides, moreover</li></ol><p>From <i>αὖτε</i> (aûte) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ρᾰ</i> (ắră).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>self-sufficiency, independence, self-rule; autarky, autarchy</li><li>contentment, satisfaction with one's resources</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτάρκης</i> (autárkēs, “self-sufficient”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă, abstract noun suffix), from <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρκέω</i> (arkéō, “to suffice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-sufficient (able to provide for oneself independently of others)</li><li>content (<i>with one's lot, with what one has etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-) +&lrm; <i>ἀρκέω</i> (arkéō) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αὐτίκα</i> (autíka) (<i>before a smooth breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at once, immediately</li><li>for the moment</li><li>for example</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute power; autocracy, despotism</li><li>autarchy, self-government, self-rule, sovereignty</li></ol><p><i>αὔταρχος</i> (aútarkhos, “autocratic, absolute; autarchic”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), from <i>αὐτ-</i> (aut-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to rule, govern”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>offering of oneself, offering of one's own free will</li></ol><p><i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-) +&lrm; <i>ἐπαγγέλλω</i> (epangéllō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) a soldier who rows his own transport</li></ol><p><i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>ἐρέτης</i> (erétēs, “rower”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>self-</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “himself/herself/oneself”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-taught</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-) +&lrm; <i>διδακτός</i> (didaktós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Form of Desire</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “same, self”) +&lrm; <i>ἐπῐθῡμῐ́ᾱ</i> (epĭthūmĭ́ā, “desire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>politically independent</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) ecclesiastically independent</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-) +&lrm; <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute ruler; autocrat</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>κράτειρα</i> (kráteira, “ruler”) (from <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō, “I rule, command”) and <i>-τειρα</i> (-teira, “feminine agent suffix.”), from <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power, strength”))</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute ruler; autocrat</li><li>a title applied to such ruler; equivalent of Latin title <i>imperator</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the Emperor of the Roman Empire</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-) +&lrm; <i>κράτωρ</i> (krátōr, “ruler”) (from <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō, “to rule, command”) and <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “masculine agent suffix.”), from <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power, strength”))</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ruling by oneself, absolute, independent</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, auto-) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power, strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) autocracy, empire</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-) +&lrm; <i>-κρατία</i> (-kratía)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autonomy, independence, freedom to use its own laws</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>dogmatism</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>αὐτόνομος</i> (autónomos, “autonomous, independent”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, “-y, -ia”, nominal suffix), from <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “usage, custom; law, ordinance”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>compact, thick (of earth)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) rigid, stiff (of persons)</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “to make solid, stiff”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-tormenting</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of pronouns</i>) reflexive</li><li>(<i>of verbs</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>intransitive</li><li>passive</li></ol></ol></ol><p><i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰ́θος</i> (pắthos, “suffering”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, suffix forming third-declension compound adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;philosophy) wisdom in itself; absolute wisdom</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>σοφῐ́ᾱ</i> (sophĭ́ā, “wisdom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hand-to-hand (<i>combat etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>αὐτός</i> (autós) +&lrm; <i>σχεδόν</i> (skhedón)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seeing with one's own eyes</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-) +&lrm; <i>-οψίᾱ</i> (-opsíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here, there</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>without article</i>) self</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in nominative, emphasizing the subject</i>)</li><li>(<i>reflexive pronoun, in oblique cases</i>) himself, herself, itself, themselves</li><li>(<i>in philosophy, of an abstract idea</i>) by or in itself</li></ol><li>(<i>without article, in oblique cases, 3rd person personal pronoun</i>) he, she, it, they</li><li>(<i>with definite article</i>) same</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ew</i> (“again, away from”) + <i>&#42;to-</i> (“that”); compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auþijaz</i> (“barren, empty”). Risch derives it from the Ancient Greek reflexes of those words, <i>αὖ</i> (aû, “back, again, other”) +&lrm; <i>τόν</i> (tón, “the”). Related to Phrygian <i>αυτος</i> (autos), thus probably representing a common innovation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>written with one's own hand</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) one's own handwriting</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αὐτόθι</i> (autóthi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from that very place; often combined with a prepositional phrase headed by <i>ἐξ</i> (ex)</li><li>from oneself, of one's own accord</li><li>at once, immediately</li></ol><p><i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “the very, oneself”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the spot; here, there</li><li>forthwith, straightway</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “same”) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accident, mere chance</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>self-willed, unbidden</li><li>self-moving, self-propelled, spontaneous</li><li>(<i>of plants</i>) growing wild, unsown</li><li>without external cause or support, automatic</li><li>without cause, accidental, by chance</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self”) + Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mətós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mn̥tós</i>, from <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think”) (whence <i>μένος</i> (ménos) and others).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deserter</li></ol><p><i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>μολ-</i> (mol-), the root of <i>βλώσκω</i> (blṓskō, “to go, come”), + <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subject only to one's own laws, independent (<i>of people and states</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in general</i>) of one's own free will</li><li>feeding and ranging at will (<i>of animals</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self-”) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “usage, custom; law, ordinance”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>without article</i>) self</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in nominative, emphasizing the subject</i>)</li><li>(<i>reflexive pronoun, in oblique cases</i>) himself, herself, itself, themselves</li><li>(<i>in philosophy, of an abstract idea</i>) by or in itself</li></ol><li>(<i>without article, in oblique cases, 3rd person personal pronoun</i>) he, she, it, they</li><li>(<i>with definite article</i>) same</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ew</i> (“again, away from”) + <i>&#42;to-</i> (“that”); compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auþijaz</i> (“barren, empty”). Risch derives it from the Ancient Greek reflexes of those words, <i>αὖ</i> (aû, “back, again, other”) +&lrm; <i>τόν</i> (tón, “the”). Related to Phrygian <i>αυτος</i> (autos), thus probably representing a common innovation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thither, to the very place</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτός</i> (autós) +&lrm; <i>-σε</i> (-se).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>very&#32;emphatic</i>) superlative degree of <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “self; same”): precisely the same; the one and only</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>emphatic</i>) comparative degree of <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “self; same”): one's very self; the very same</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indigenous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a native, an aborigine</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐτο-</i> (auto-, “self”) +&lrm; <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “earth, soil”), sprung from the land itself.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αὐχένα</i> (aukhéna)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neck</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂emǵʰ-w-eh₁-n</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂énǵʰus</i> (“narrow, tight”), and compared in particular to Old Armenian <i>օձիք</i> (ōjikʻ, “collar”), though this is controversial. Beekes tentatively accepts this etymology, but additionally suggests possible Pre-Greek origin; according to him, the <i>-ήν</i> (-ḗn) suffix renders a Pre-Greek source tentatively reconstructed as <i>&#42;ankʷēn</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry, parched, without rain</li><li>dry, rough, squalid</li><li>miserable, wretched</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐχμ(ός)</i> (aukhm(ós)) +&lrm; <i>-ηρός</i> (-ērós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drought, dearth</li><li>squalor, as effect of drought</li><li>dryness, meagreness (of style)</li><li>thirst</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a compound of <i>αὖος</i> (aûos, “dry”) and an element "-χμ-" from the word for earth (see <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “ground, soil”)). A similar formation can be found in <i>νεοχμός</i> (neokhmós, “new”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>ἑαυτοῦ</i> (heautoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the same</li></ol><p>Crasis of <i>ὁ</i> (ho) + <i>αὐτός</i> (autós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augur (Roman diviner)</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>augur</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>αὐδή</i> (audḗ)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak, utter</li><li>to speak to, address</li></ol><p><i>αὐδή</i> (audḗ, “human voice”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō, verb suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cottage</li><li>sheepfold, stable</li><li>chamber, cave, grotto</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐλή</i> (aulḗ, “open court, courtyard”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the sheepfold</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐλή</i> (aulḗ, “open court, courtyard”) or <i>αὔλιον</i> (aúlion, “sheepfold”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the sheepfold</li></ol><p>From <i>αὐλή</i> (aulḗ, “open court, courtyard”) or <i>αὔλιον</i> (aúlion, “sheepfold”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>growth, increase</li></ol><p>From <i>αὔξω</i> (aúxō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>promoting or supporting growth</li><li>growing, developing</li></ol><p>From <i>αὔξω</i> (aúxō, “to make grow”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐμος</i> (-ĭmos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>αὐξάνω</i> (auxánō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cool air in motion, breeze, fresh air of the morning</li><li>steam</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;auhrā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éwsr̥h₂</i> (“morning air”), from <i>&#42;h₂ews-</i> (“dawn; east”). From a separate ablaut grade <i>&#42;h₂ewsḗr</i>, the ancestor of <i>ᾱ̓ήρ</i> (āḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>tomorrow</li><li>soon</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;αὖρι</i> (&#42;aûri), the old locative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ews-r-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂ews-</i> (“dawn”). Cognate with <i>ᾱ̓ήρ</i> (āḗr, “mist, air”), <i>ἠώς</i> (ēṓs, “daybreak, dawn”), and perhaps <i>ἦρι</i> (êri, “early in the morning”); outside Greek, compare Lithuanian <i>aušrà</i> (“dawn”) and Sanskrit <i>उस्र</i> (usrá, “morning light, daybreak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auspex; augur; a Roman diviner</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Latin <i>auspex</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>just so</li><li>merely</li><li>in vain</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout, call</li><li>(originally<sup>?</sup>&#32;and&#32;in prefixed forms) to scoop, draw (fire or water)</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to light a fire, to ignite, singe</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to catch fire</li><li>alternative form of <i>ἰαύω</i> (iaúō)</li></ol><p>Probably of imitative origin, similar to Latin <i>ululō</i> (“to howl”).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ews-</i> (“to scoop, draw (water)”), whence also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ausaną</i> (“to scoop, draw water”) and Latin <i>hauriō</i> (“to draw water, drain”). The Greek verb's connection with fire appears to be a secondary development.</p><p>Unreduplicated form of <i>ἰαύω</i> (iaúō).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry (<i>of timber</i>), dried (<i>of fruit</i>), withered (<i>of leaves</i>)</li><li>dried up, exhausted</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ahúhos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂súsos</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₂sews-</i> (“to be dry”). Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>соухъ</i> (suxŭ, “dry”), English <i>sear</i> (“dry, withered”), Sanskrit <i>शोष</i> (śoṣa, “dryness”) (which reflect a similar Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂sowsós</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ἠώς</i> (ēṓs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ahúhyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂sús-ye-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂sews-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Again, anew, afresh, in turn; often after ordinal numerals&#59; in a question, expressing impatience</li><li>Further, moreover</li><li>On the other hand after <i>δέ</i> (dé) in <i>μέν... δέ...</i> (mén... dé...)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ew</i> (“again”). Cognate to Albanian <i>a</i> (“or”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αὖθι</i> (aûthi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>αὐτόθι</i> (autóthi, “on the spot”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(of place) back, back again</li><li>(of time) again, afresh, anew, in turn</li><li>(of sequence) moreover, in turn, on the other hand</li></ol><p>Lengthened form of <i>αὖ</i> (aû); i.e. extension of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewti</i>. Compare <i>αὖτε</i> (aûte).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrow</li></ol><p>Compared with Lithuanian <i>vilkti</i> (“to drag, haul”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;velťi</i> (“to draw”). Beekes claims that the variants point to a Pre-Greek origin, in particular a form <i>&#42;alʷ-ak-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tent or place for passing the night in, bivouac</li><li>bird's roosting place</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wes-</i> (“pass the night”), the same root of <i>αὐλή</i> (aulḗ, “courtyard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry (<i>of timber</i>), dried (<i>of fruit</i>), withered (<i>of leaves</i>)</li><li>dried up, exhausted</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ahúhos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂súsos</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₂sews-</i> (“to be dry”). Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>соухъ</i> (suxŭ, “dry”), English <i>sear</i> (“dry, withered”), Sanskrit <i>शोष</i> (śoṣa, “dryness”) (which reflect a similar Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂sowsós</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>αὖτε</i> (aûte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) again; furthermore; on the contrary (following <i>μέν</i> (mén))</li></ol><p>Either from <i>αὖ</i> (aû) +&lrm; <i>τε</i> (te) or for *αὖτι, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewti</i>. Compare <i>αὖτις</i> (aûtis), which might be its extension.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back (to a previous place)</li><li>again, anew</li><li>in turn</li></ol><p>Lengthened form of <i>αὖ</i> (aû).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chatterer, chatterbox</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic word, like <i>βαβάζω</i> (babázō, “to speak inarticulately”), <i>βάβιον</i> (bábion, “child”), <i>βάβακοι</i> (bábakoi, “cicadas”) and <i>βάβαλον</i> (bábalon, “shouter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>step-by-step</li><li>more and more, gradually</li><li>on foot</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to step”) +&lrm; <i>-δην</i> (-dēn, deverbal adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) child</li></ol><p>Possibly from a Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;wādya</i>, whence Albanian <i>voc</i> (“young boy”), although Proto-Albanian */dy/ usually becomes Albanian /z/.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A walk, a gait</li></ol><p>From <i>βαδίζω</i> (badízō, “to go, to march”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) speak, talk, say</li></ol><p>Related to <i>βάσκειν</i> (báskein).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extension in space: depth, height, breadth, fullness</li><li>profundity</li></ol><p>From <i>βαθύς</i> (bathús, “deep”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeh₂dʰ-</i> (“to sink, submerge”) (though Beekes rejects the link to the Indo-European root).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that on which anything steps or stands</li><li>base, pedestal of a statue</li><li>stage, scaffold</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) solid base, foundation</li><li>step, rung of a ladder</li><li>bench, seat of a lecture-room or school</li></ol><p>From <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to step”) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron, “instrumental suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallus, one of the priests of Cybele in Phrygia that typically castrated themselves</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) any effeminate or womanish man</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is probably of Anatolian origin, perhaps originally Pre-Greek. He proceeds to compare synonymous <i>κάβηλος</i> (kábēlos) and <i>κάληβος</i> (kálēbos), both found in the lexicon of Hesychius of Alexandria.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild spikenard (<i>Asarum europaeum</i>)</li><li>sowbread (<i>Cyclamen hederifolium</i>)</li><li>red everlasting (<i>Helichrysum sanguineum</i>)</li><li>unguent made with wild spikenard</li></ol><p>According to a scholium of Aeschylus, the word is from Lydian, while Furnée suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff, stick, cudgel</li></ol><p>Uncertain. According to Beekes, the term may relate to Latin <i>baculum</i>, perhaps hinting at Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bak-tlom</i>. However, Kroonen instead derives the Greek form from <i>&#42;bak-tro-</i> and De Vaan alternatively derives the Latin term from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bak-(k)elo-</i>. Due to the a-vocalism, this term may be construed as a loanword from a substrate source. It is probably related to <i>βακτηρία</i> (baktēría).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>βάλε</i> (bále)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of freshwater fish, perhaps a kind of carp</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>βάρακος</i> (bárakos, “kind of fish”), a word listed in the lexicon of Hesychius of Alexandria. According to Beekes, the word is of Pre-Greek origin in view of the many variants existing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acorn</li><li>the tree that bears acorns, the oak</li><li>(<i>by resemblance</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>barnacle</li><li>glans penis</li><li>iron peg, bolt-pin, bolt of a door</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) suppository, pledget, pessary</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷə́lanos</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelh₂-</i> (“acorn”); see there for cognates. Probably unrelated to Ancient Greek <i>βᾰλᾰνεῖον</i> (bălăneîon, “bath, bathroom”). Per Schwyzer and Curtius, mentioned by Vasmer, akin to <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw, cast”), though Beekes rejects the relation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rose campion (<i>Silene coronaria</i>, syn. <i>Lychnis coronaria</i>)</li><li>sea lettuce (<i>Ulva lactuca</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes suggests a possible Pre-Greek origin in view of the variant with a doubled -λλ-. Compare also other plant names that share the same prefix, such as <i>βᾰλαύστῐον</i> (bălaústĭon, “flower of the pomegranate”), <i>βᾰλλωτή</i> (băllōtḗ, “black horehound”) and <i>βᾰ́λλῐς</i> (bắllĭs, unknown kind of plant).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>βάλετε</i> (bálete)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>βάλετο</i> (báleto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plant with wonderful medicinal properties</li></ol><p>Depending on the exact medicinal properties possibly related to <i>βᾰ́λλω</i> (bắllō, “to throw; to strike; to tumble”). In Dioskurides it is given as another name of the squirting cucumber. Depending on the exterior appearance it can also be from <i>βαλιός</i> (baliós, “spotted, dappled”), itself suspected to be from Thracian, Illyrian, or Pre-Greek origin. According to Beekes, it's together with <i>βαλαύστιον</i> (balaústion, “flower of the pomegranate”), <i>βάλαρις</i> (bálaris, “<i>uncertain plant</i>”) and <i>βαλλωτή</i> (ballōtḗ, “black horehound”) from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw, cast, hurl</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to let fall</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strike, touch</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put, place</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall, tumble</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷəlnō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷl̥-ne-h₁-</i>, nasal-infix from <i>&#42;gʷelH-</i> (“to hit by throwing”). Cognates are uncertain, but compare Sanskrit <i>उद्गूर्ण</i> (ud-gūrṇa) and Old Irish <i>at·baill</i> (“dies”). Sihler argues for a <b>N</b> nasal progressive aspect marker, but Curtius and Smyth argue for a <b>*y</b> progressive aspect marker. English <i>ball</i> has a similar sound and meaning, but is unrelated.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arabian balsam tree (<i>Commiphora gileadensis</i>)</li><li>balsam, the fragrant oil produced by this tree</li><li>costmary (<i>Tanacetum balsamita</i>)</li></ol><p>Of Semitic origin, borrowed from Hebrew <i>בָּשָׂם</i> (bāśām, “sweet spice, sweet smell”), the consonant sequence in Greek being explained as an attempt of rendering <i>שׂ</i> (ś) sounding back then [ɬ]; the semantic development may later have been attached to Aramaic and Ancient North Arabian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton</li></ol><p>Herodotus describes the word as Indian, while Theophrastus says that it was found near the Persian Gulf. From the same root are Middle Persian <i>pmbk'</i>, Old Armenian <i>բամբակ</i> (bambak) and Ottoman Turkish پاموق (pamuk).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artisanal, handmade, handcrafted</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) vulgar, base, unworthy</li><li>(<i>later</i>) fastidious, meticulous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artisan, craftsman, handicraftsman</li></ol><p>Generally taken as an haplology from <i>*βαύναυσος</i>, which would be a compound of <i>βαῦνος</i> (baûnos, “furnace”) and <i>αὔω</i> (aúō, “to scoop, light a fire”). However, Beekes disregards this proposal as folk etymology and suggests a Pre-Greek origin, in view also of the suffix <i>-σος</i> found in other words like <i>κόμπασος</i> (kómpasos) and <i>ὄρυξος</i> (óruxos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baptism</li></ol><p>From <i>βαπτίζω</i> (baptízō, “to dip in liquid”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip; to submerge</li><li>to dye; to colour</li><li>to baptise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷápťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷh₂bʰ-yé-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷeh₂bʰ-</i> (“to dive, dive in”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwēbijaną</i>, Avestan <i>𐬘𐬀𐬟𐬭𐬀</i> (jafra), Persian ژرف (žarf), Lurish گںهںر (gəhər, a lake), and Sanskrit <i>ग॒भी॒र</i> (gabhīrá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gulf, cleft, pit (especially one at Athens behind the acropolis into which criminals were thrown)</li><li>ruin, perdition</li><li>glutton, spendthrift</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷerh₃-</i> (“to devour”). Compare Latin <i>vorāgō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of freshwater fish, perhaps similar to a perch</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>βάλαγρος</i> (bálagros, “kind of barb”) and suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: a Libyan hawk or falcon.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>non-Greek-speaking, foreign</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) non-Greek peoples</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>Medes or Persians</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) barbaric, brutal, rude</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic, from the perceived <i>βαρ-βαρ</i> (bar-bar) sounds incomprehensible to Ancient Greeks and spoken by foreigners. As an onomatopoeic construction, βαρ-βαρ is similar to modern English <i>blah blah</i>, but meaning <i>gibberish, gabble</i>; compare also <i>babble</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰa-bʰa-</i>. Cognate to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀞𐀫</i> (pa-pa-ro) and Sanskrit <i>बर्बर</i> (barbara, “barbarian, non-Aryan, stammering, blockhead”).</p><p>Possibly related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;balb-</i>, <i>&#42;balbal-</i> (“tongue-tied”). Compare with Latin <i>balbus</i> (“stammering, stuttering”), Russian <i>болтать</i> (boltatʹ, “to chatter, babble”) and балабол (“over-talkative”, chatterbox), Lithuanian <i>balbė́ti</i> (“to talk, babble”), Sanskrit <i>बल्बला</i> (balbalā, “stammering”), Albanian <i>belbët</i> (“stammering”).</p><p>For the semantic development, compare Arabic عَجَم (ʕajam, “non-Arab; Persian”), from the root ع ج م (ʕ j m), referring to people who speak unclearly, or Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;němьcь</i> (“non-Slav, German”), from <i>&#42;němъ</i> (“mute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;music) synonym of <i>βάρβιτος</i> (bárbitos)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) barbitos</li></ol><p>Uncertain, probably from Phrygian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) barbitos</li></ol><p>Uncertain, probably from Phrygian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A burden, a load</li></ol><p>From <i>βαρέω</i> (baréō, “to load”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weight; burden; load</li><li>grief; sorrow</li><li>plenty</li><li>dignity; influence; power</li></ol><p>Related to <i>βαρύς</i> (barús, “heavy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) genitals (<i>in Taranto</i>); pin</li></ol><p>From Messapic, possibly closely related to Albanian <i>bark</i> (“belly”). Suggested to be a formation from the <i>o</i>-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerH-</i> (“pierce, strike”), or of <i>&#42;bʰer-</i> (“to carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold touchstone (stone which is rubbed on gold to ascertain its authenticity by leaving a yellow streak)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>test, trial (whether a thing is genuine or real)</li><li>inquiry by torture</li></ol></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>bḫn</i> (“a hard stone”), <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./4a1fe83d.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="x" alt="glyph" height="45"/><br /><img src="./6c9cd1c6.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="n" alt="glyph" height="12"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./91b37de8.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="O39" alt="glyph" height="25"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table> via Lydia, which was used by the Egyptians as a touchstone of gold.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepping, step; (<i>collective</i>) steps</li><li>measured step or movement; rhythmical or metrical movement</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) rhythmical close of a sentence; clause forming transition from protasis to apodosis</li><li>(<i>prosody</i>) metrical unit, monometer</li></ol><li>foot, leg</li><li>base, pedestal; foundation, basement</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) base of a solid or plane figure</li><li>position, fixedness</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) horoscope</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷə́tis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷémtis</i>, from the root <i>&#42;gʷem-</i> (“step”). By surface analysis, <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to walk, step”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>βάσκε</i> (báske)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slanderous, envious, malignant, malicious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slanderer, defamer, talebearer</li><li>sorcerer, magician, bewitcher</li></ol><p>Connected with <i>βάσκειν</i> (báskein, “to slander”), which is probably from <i>βάζω</i> (bázō, “to speak, say”), but might be related to Latin <i>fascinum</i> (“witchcraft; penis”), in which case both would derive from a European substrate language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;found only in Hesychius of Alexandria's lexicon) to talk, speak</li></ol><p>Probably from the same root as <i>βάζω</i> (bázō, “to speak, say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>imperative only</i>) come, go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷəskō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷm̥-sḱé-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷem-</i> (“to step”) with <i>-σκω</i> (-skō). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>गच्छति</i> (gácchati). Compare the alternative present <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A load</li></ol><p>From <i>βαστάζω</i> (bastázō, “I lift up, I raise”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) anus</li><li>catamite, lewd man</li><li>stammerer, stutterer</li></ol><p>Tentatively connected with the verb <i>βατέω</i> (batéō, “to mount”), but the presence of the alternative forms dismisses this theory. Furnée suggested a relation with <i>σπάταλος</i> (spátalos, “lewd, lascivious”), which would indicate a common Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackberry (<i>Rubus ulmifolius</i>)</li><li>(<i>masculine</i>) fish, a kind of skate</li><li>bath, a Hebrew liquid measure</li></ol><p>Said by Beekes to be a Mediterranean wanderwort; substrate common to <i>μαντία</i> (mantía, “blackberry”) (Dacian loan), and Albanian <i>man</i> (“mulberry”), Gheg <i>mand</i>.</p><p>From Hebrew <i>בַּת</i> (baṯ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frog (small, hopping amphibian)</li><li>anglerfish (<i>Lophius piscatorius</i>)</li><li>frog (organ in horse's hoof)</li></ol><p>Seemingly imitative of croaking, with original form something like <i>&#42;βρατ-αχ-</i> (&#42;brat-akh-), but this imitation was probably not formed in Greek, but borrowed from Pre-Greek or Semitic; compare Hebrew <i>צְפַרְדֵּעַ</i> (ṣəp̄ardḗaʿ), Arabic ضَفْدَع (ḍafdaʕ), considering that in the dialect of Zakynthos the frog is matching the Semitic with <i>σπορδακάς</i> (spordakás) – unless of course one must find that Semitic and Greek have borrowed from an unknown third.</p><p>Concerning the wide range of dialectal variation, Beekes explains that much may be due to folk etymology or taboo, combined with other phonetic alterations such as vowel displacement and even prenasalization, which indicate substrate origin: “<i>A priori</i>, a local (i.e. Pre-Greek) form is to be expected for all of these forms; the variation α&#47;ο points to this.” The suffix <i>&#42;-χ-</i> (&#42;-kh-) is also found in names of other animals.</p><p>An older hypothesis (cf. Pokorny 1959) links Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;krodu</i> and Latin <i>bruscus</i> (“frog or toad”), all from a hypothetical Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷredʰ-</i> (“frog, toad”), supposing metathesis (cf. Ionic <i>βρότᾰχος</i> (brótăkhos)) and an original *dʰ whence perhaps the /tʰ/ of the Ionic variant <i>βᾰ́θρᾰκος</i> (bắthrăkos), but this is phonetically very difficult. Moreover, as Beekes explains, the form with /t/ is original, whereas Ionic frequently displaces aspiration in this way.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm, steady</li><li>steadfast, durable</li><li>sure, certain</li></ol><p>Related to <i>βῆναι</i> (bênai), aorist of <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “step”), though the exact morphology is uncertain. One theory derives the word from <i>βεβᾰ-</i> (bebă-), short-vowel grade of the stem of <i>βέβηκα</i> (bébēka, “stand”), the perfect of <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “step”), + <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>allowed to be gone through</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>permitted, allowed, public</li></ol><li>not holy; uninitiated [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From a reduplicated version of the root of <i>ἔβην</i> (ébēn), the aorist of <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go”), +&lrm; <i>-λος</i> (-los).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread</li></ol><p>From Phrygian <i>βεκος</i> (bekos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₃g-</i> (“to bake”). Cognate with <i>φώγω</i> (phṓgō, “roast”), Old English <i>bacan</i> (“to bake”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>βέλεα</i> (bélea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of pulse</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin due to the presence of a geminate "-κκ-". Furnée compares the word with <i>πέλεκυς</i> (pélekus, “battle axe”) and <i>πέλεκκον</i> (pélekkon, “axe handle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dart, javelin</li><li>projectile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelH-</i> (“to hit by throwing”), the same root of <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>missile, arrow, dart</li><li>weapon</li><li>something quickly moving</li><li>artillery</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷélHos</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷelH-</i> (“to throw, pierce”). The expected form is *δέλος, but the initial consonant has evidently been leveled with that of the cognate verb <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō). Cognates include Old High German <i>quelan</i> and Lithuanian <i>gelti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spinning top</li><li>whirlpool, eddy, vortex</li><li>cyclone, whirlwind, vortex</li><li>bumblebee</li></ol><p>Probably of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix <i>-ῑκ-</i>. See also <i>πέμφιξ</i> (pémphix, “breath, blast”) and <i>πομφόλυξ</i> (pomphólux, “bubble”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the sea</i>) depth</li></ol><p>Poetic form of <i>βάθος</i> (báthos, “depth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) virgae, twigs, sticks</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>virgae</i> (“twigs, young shoots”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veredus, posthorse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>verēdus</i>, itself from Gaulish <i>&#42;werēdos</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>δραπέτης</i> (drapétēs, “runaway”)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from an earlier form <i>&#42;ϝέρρης</i> (&#42;wérrhēs) related to <i>ἔρρω</i> (érrhō, “to wander”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βέρρης)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Verres</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Verrēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cough</li></ol><p>Furnée argues for a Pre-Greek origin in view of the variant <i>πήχιον</i> (pḗkhion) for <i>βήχιον</i> (bḗkhion), but the word might also be of onomatopoeic origin. Compare Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷeh₂s-</i>, itself of imitative origin; in this case <i>βήξ</i> (bḗx) could point to a protoform <i>&#42;gʷeh₂(s)-gʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of loaf or cake made of barley</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it is a typical Pre-Greek word, given the suffix <i>-ακ-</i> and the variation <i>β/π</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beryl</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Middle Indic forms like Pali <i>veḷuriya</i> or Prakrit <i>𑀯𑁂𑀭𑀼𑀮𑀺𑀅</i> (verulia), from Dravidian, probably named after <i>Velur</i> (modern day Belur) in southern India.</p><p>The variants <i>βηρύλλιον</i> (bērúllion) and <i>βηρύλλιος</i> (bērúllios) reflect the source <i>veruḷiya</i> more faithfully than <i>βήρυλλος</i> (bḗrullos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) brick</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>bessālis</i> (“of eight unciae”). The original Latin term was used to describe to describe the dimensions of building materials; this usage may have led it to the term being adopted into Greek as a term for bricks.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cough</li></ol><p>Verbal denominative from <i>βήξ</i> (bḗx, “cough”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>βήσσω</i> (bḗssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coltsfoot (<i>Tussilago farfara</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βήξ</i> (bḗx, “cough”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bodily strength, force</li><li>act of violence</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷiā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷey-</i> (“to win”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>ज्या</i> (jyā́, “overwhelming force; power; vanquishing”), <i>जय</i> (jayá, “victory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forcible, violent</li><li>constrained, compulsory</li></ol><p>From <i>βῐ́ᾱ</i> (bĭ́ā) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>βῐ́ᾱ</i> (bĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>βῐ́ᾳ</i> (bĭ́āi, “forcefully; violently”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>βῐ́ᾳ</i> (bĭ́āi, “forcefully; violently”) with movable nu</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pudendum virile, the genitals of a man</li></ol><p>Furnée compares this word with the adjective <i>ἀβίλλιον</i> (abíllion, “manly, masculine”); according to Beekes, the prothetic vowel would prove a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life (often with a positive connotation: the good life)</li></ol><p>Either from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷīwos</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷih₃wós</i> (“alive”), and cognate with Old English <i>cwic</i> (English <i>quick</i>), Latin <i>vīvus</i>, and Old Church Slavonic <i>живъ</i> (živŭ); or a syncope of <i>βίοτος</i> (bíotos).</p><p>In either case, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeyh₃-</i> (“to live”), whence also <i>ζωός</i> (zōós) and <i>ζῷον</i> (zōîon), and cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>жити</i> (žiti), Sanskrit <i>जीवति</i> (jīvati), Latin <i>vīvō</i>, Old Armenian <i>կեամ</i> (keam).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷih₃wo-teh₂</i> or <i>&#42;gʷih₃-etos</i> (“life”), from <i>&#42;gʷeyh₃-</i> (“to live”). Cognate with Latin <i>vīta</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>πυράγρα</i> (purágra, “pair of fire tongs”), <i>δρέπανον</i> (drépanon, “pruning-knife”).</li></ol><p>Furnée suggests a derivation from Asia Minor and compares Svan <i>ბერეჟ</i> (berež, “iron”), Akkadian <i>𒀭𒁇</i> (parzillum), Hebrew <i>בַּרְזֶל</i> (barzel, “iron”), Ugaritic <i>𐎁𐎗𐎏𐎍</i> (brḏl) and Phoenician <i>𐤁𐤓𐤆𐤋</i> (brzl); furthermore Old English <i>bræs</i> (“brass”) and Basque <i>burdin</i> (“iron”). Or, according to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, in view of the geminate consonants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type of cloak or mantle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>birrus</i>, from Gaulish <i>&#42;birros</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;birros</i> (“short”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Messapic</i>) pruning hook, billhook</li></ol><p>Unknown. Probably from Messapic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>life (often with a positive connotation: the good life)</li></ol><p>Either from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷīwos</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷih₃wós</i> (“alive”), and cognate with Old English <i>cwic</i> (English <i>quick</i>), Latin <i>vīvus</i>, and Old Church Slavonic <i>живъ</i> (živŭ); or a syncope of <i>βίοτος</i> (bíotos).</p><p>In either case, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeyh₃-</i> (“to live”), whence also <i>ζωός</i> (zōós) and <i>ζῷον</i> (zōîon), and cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>жити</i> (žiti), Sanskrit <i>जीवति</i> (jīvati), Latin <i>vīvō</i>, Old Armenian <i>կեամ</i> (keam).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βίων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Bion</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forcefully; by force; strong-handedly</li><li>violently; by violence</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>βῐ́ᾳ</i> (bĭ́āi, “forcefully; violently”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) bunch of grapes</li></ol><p>Possibly from a Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;waiša</i> whence Albanian <i>vesh</i> (“vine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go, step, move on foot</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mount (a chariot)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to depart, go away</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to die</li></ol><li>perfect <i>βέβηκα</i> (bébēka): (<i>intransitive</i>) to stand, be somewhere</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to be [<i>with</i> adverb <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li><i>εὖ</i> (eû) <i>βεβηκώς</i> (bebēkṓs) well off</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) to stand on a base</li></ol><li>future <i>βήσω</i> (bḗsō) and aorist <i>ἔβησα</i> (ébēsa): (<i>causative</i>) to make someone dismount</li></ol><p>The present stem is from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷəňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷm̥yéti</i>, from zero-grade of <i>&#42;gʷem-</i> + <i>&#42;-yéti</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Old English <i>cuman</i> (English <i>come</i>), Latin <i>veniō</i>, and Sanskrit <i>गन्ति</i> (ganti).</p><p>The aorist <i>ἔβην</i> (ébēn), as well as the other tense-forms whose stems do not contain <i>ν</i> (n), is from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;égʷēn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷéh₂t</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coat of skins worn by shepherds or peasants</li><li>tent or shelter made of skins</li></ol><p>Unknown origin. Somehow connected to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;paidō</i>; according to Beekes, it is borrowed from this Greek word. The same author connects the word with Albanian <i>petk</i> (“coat”), which shares the same root with the addition of a suffix <i>-k-</i>, suggesting a derivation from a common European substrate. Furnée argues for a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baetyl (a meteorite or similar-looking rough stone thought to be of divine origin and worshipped as sacred)</li></ol><p>Of Semitic origin, usually considered to be a rendering of a collocation like Biblical Hebrew <i>בֵּית אֵל</i> (bēṯ ʔel, literally “house of God”). However, this identification is doubted by Gaifman, who marks a semantic and formal mismatch between the Semitic term and the Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>βᾰβαί</i> (băbaí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>wow! (an expression of surprise or amazement)</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>βαί</i> (baí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>βᾰβαί</i> (băbaí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μάταιος</i> (mátaios, “vain, empty, idle”).</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) title of Zeus in Phrygia</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the gloss consists of two or more elements. One may think of a connection with Persian بغ (baġ, “god, deity”); if so, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂g-</i> (“to divide”) (see Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;bʰagás</i>).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βαγαῖος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Bagaeus, from Old Persian, an Achaemenid nobleman</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Bagayah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Christianity) apheretic form of <i>εὐαγγέλιον</i> (euangélion, “gospel”)</li></ol><p>Apheretic form of <i>εὐαγγέλιον</i> (euangélion, “good news; gospel”), from <i>εὐάγγελος</i> (euángelos, “bringing good news”), from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos, “messenger; angel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Christianity) apheretic form of <i>εὐαγγέλιον</i> (euangélion, “gospel”)</li></ol><p>Apheretic form of <i>εὐαγγέλιον</i> (euangélion, “good news; gospel”), from <i>εὐάγγελος</i> (euángelos, “bringing good news”), from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos, “messenger; angel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go, to go about</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>travel by foot</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>also in the mediopassive</i>)</li><li>(<i>of an army</i>) march</li><li>(<i>in opposition to τρέχω and πλέω</i>)</li></ol></ol></ol><p><i>βάδην</i> (bádēn), from <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō), with the suffix <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>step or threshold</li><li>base, pedestal</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) socket, hollow in a joint</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to step, walk”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>step</li><li>(figurative,&#32;Koine) degree</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having deep eddies (<i>epithet of rivers and the ocean</i>)</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>βαθύς</i> (bathús, “deep”) +&lrm; <i>δῑ́νη</i> (dī́nē, “whirlpool, eddy”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plunged deep in war</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s, “deep”) +&lrm; <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos, “war, battle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a thought, reflection</i>) deep-counselling</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s, “deep”) +&lrm; <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ, “counsel”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep-wooded</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s, “deep”) +&lrm; <i>δένδρον</i> (déndron, “tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bathykolpian, with dress falling in deep folds</li><li>with deep bosom (of the earth)</li><li>with deep foundations</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s, “deep”) +&lrm; <i>κόλπος</i> (kólpos, “bosom; bay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a torrent</i>) to deepen, hollow out, dig</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become deep, be deepened</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to add a third dimension</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sink deep, crumble</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to go deeply into a subject</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s, “deep, profound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep-flowing, brimming</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s) +&lrm; <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being a large vertical distance from: high, deep</li><li>thick</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) strong, intense, full</li><li>rich, wealthy, (of wealth) abundant</li><li>profound</li><li>(<i>of colour</i>) deep</li><li>(<i>time</i>) twilight</li></ol><p>Zero-grade of the root of <i>βένθος</i> (bénthos), of uncertain origin. Alternatively from earlier <i>&#42;βαφύς</i> (&#42;baphús), related to Sanskrit <i>गभीर</i> (gabhīrá, “deep”). Or perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeh₂dʰ-</i> (“to sink, submerge”). Unrelated to <i>βυσσός</i> (bussós) and <i>βυθός</i> (buthós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep grown with rushes</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s, “deep”) +&lrm; <i>σχοῖνος</i> (skhoînos, “rush, reed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Ionic) comparative degree of <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little, small</li><li>scanty, few</li><li>mean, humble</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) short</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly related to <i>ἠβαιός</i> (ēbaiós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>βακτηρία</i> (baktēría)</li></ol><p>From <i>βακτηρία</i> (baktēría, “staff, cane”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rod, staff, cane</li><li>baton</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes proposes a possible pre-form <i>&#42;Βακτήρ</i> (&#42;Baktḗr), which may relate to <i>βάκτρον</i> (báktron) in the same manner as <i>ἀροτήρ</i> (arotḗr) connects to <i>ἄροτρον</i> (árotron). Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bak-</i> (“rod, staff, club”), which appears to be a European substrate word. Cognates include Latin <i>baculum</i> and Old Irish <i>bacc</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bacchic frenzy, revelry</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Bacchic orgies</li></ol><p>From <i>Βάκχος</i> (Bákkhos, “Bacchus”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of acorns</li><li>of the color of acorns</li></ol><p>From <i>βάλανος</i> (bálanos, “acorn”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>βαλλάντιον</i> (ballántion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>key or hook for pulling out the <i>βάλανος</i> (bálanos, “iron peg, bolt”)</li></ol><p>From <i>βάλανος</i> (bálanos, “acorn; iron peg, bolt”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγρᾱ</i> (ágrā, “hunting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of a bath or bathhouse</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>βᾰλᾰνεῖον</i> (bălăneîon, “bath; bathhouse”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathman</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰλᾰνεῖον</i> (bălăneîon, “bath”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bath, an area or room where bathing takes place</li><li>bathhouse, thermae, baths, especially Roman-style</li></ol><p>According to Beekes all the various attempts to link this word with <i>βάλανος</i> (bálanos, “acorn”) are semantically unconvincing. The same author considers that, given the semantic category and lack of convincing Indo-European cognates, the word must be of Pre-Greek origin. Furnée compares the noun with Old Georgian <i>აბანოჲ</i> (abanoy, “bathroom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acorn-eating</li></ol><p>From <i>βάλανος</i> (bálanos, “acorn”) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pouch (for money)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower of the wild pomegranate</li></ol><p>Put by Beekes as acquired via Pre-Greek, because of the ⟨αυ⟩ / ⟨ω⟩ variation and the similar prefix shared by other phytonyms, such as <i>βᾰ́λᾰρῐς</i> (bắlărĭs, an unknown plant), <i>βᾰλλωτή</i> (băllōtḗ, “black horehound”) and <i>βᾰ́λλῐς</i> (bắllĭs, “a plant with wonderful medicinal properties”). Given its late date, however explained as a Semitic borrowing close to Classical Syriac <i>ܒܠܳܨܳܐ</i> (blāṣā, “bud, shoot; the part of a pomegranate fruit when it ripened to split”) to the satisfaction of 19th-century philologists already. See also Arabic رُمَّان (rummān) for another difficult pomegranate term.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the color of pomegranate flowers</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰλαύστῐον</i> (bălaústĭon, “pomegranate flower”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope drawn across the racecourse signalling the starting and finishing point of the course</li><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) posts to which this rope was attached</li><li>turning post in a racecourse</li><li>the platform or area from which the discus was thrown</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) beginning, start, onset, origin</li></ol><p>According to Beekes of Pre-Greek origin, given the technicality of its meaning and the similar formation in <i>-ῖδ-</i> found in other words, such as <i>κηκίς</i> (kēkís), <i>κνημίς</i> (knēmís) and <i>κρηπίς</i> (krēpís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spotted, dappled, mottled</li><li>swift, rapid, fast</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>πολιός</i> (poliós, “grey”) and other colour adjectives. Since <i>*b-</i> is rare in Proto-Indo-European, it has been considered a loanword from another language, perhaps Thracian, Phrygian and Illyrian. If so, the genuinely Greek cognate would have to be <i>φαλιός</i> (phaliós, “with a white spot”). Pokorny posits Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“shiny, white”). Also Comparing Albanian <i>ballë</i> (“horse with a white spot on its forehead”), close to Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;balā</i>, or from it.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of bag, pouch, or purse generally for holding money</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Beekes the word is perhaps Pre-Greek, in view of the variation <i>-λλ-/-λ-</i>. It has also been connected with Latin <i>follis</i> (“bellows; purse, sack”) by Krahe, suggesting a derivation from an unprecise language of the northern Balkans.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king</li></ol><p>Probably from Semitic, compare Phoenician <i>𐤁𐤏𐤋</i> (bʿl), Biblical Hebrew <i>בַּעַל</i> (báʿal), from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;baʿl-</i> (“owner, lord, husband”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Magna Graecia</i>) to dance, jump about</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷl̥H-id-yé-</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷelH-</i> (“to throw”). By surface analysis, <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>φαλλός</i> (phallós, “phallus”)</li></ol><p>May come from the same root as Ancient Greek <i>φαλλός</i> (phallós). Possibly a dialectal variant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catapult, engine of war</li></ol><p>From <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black horehound (<i>Ballota nigra</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek and related to <i>βᾰλαύστῐον</i> (bălaústĭon, “flower of the pomegranate”), <i>βᾰ́λᾰρῐς</i> (bắlărĭs) and <i>βᾰ́λλῐς</i> (bắllĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxeye (<i>Anacyclus radiatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βάλσαμον</i> (bálsamon, “balsam-tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of cotton</li></ol><p>From <i>βάμβαξ</i> (bámbax, “cotton”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) cotton</li></ol><p>From <i>βάμβαξ</i> (bámbax, “cotton”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chatter with the teeth, stammer</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic word. Not related to <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to walk”). Compare also <i>βαβάζω</i> (babázō, “to speak inarticulately”) and <i>βάβάλον</i> (bábálon, “bawler, shouter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dip, plunge</li><li>to immerse</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to drown, sink (of ships)</li><li>to get wet, soak</li><li>to wash, clean with water, clean by dipping or submerging</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to bathe</li><li>to draw water (or other liquid)</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to baptize</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to perform ablutions</li></ol><p>From <i>βάπτω</i> (báptō, “to dip”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dipping, a washing</li><li>baptism</li></ol><p>From <i>βαπτίζω</i> (baptízō, “dip in liquid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swimming-bath</li><li>baptistery</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰπτῐ́ζω</i> (băptĭ́zō, “to immerge”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baptist (commonly as an epithet of John the Baptist)</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰπτῐ́ζω</i> (băptĭ́zō, “to baptise”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thistle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>בַּרְקָן</i> (barqán, “thistle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a certain kind of plaster</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barbaric</li><li>non-Greek</li></ol><p>From <i>βάρβαρος</i> (bárbaros) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to coerce, rape women</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from a Paleo-Balkan language, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰer-</i> (“to bring”), possibly akin to Latin <i>forda</i> (“pregnant (cow)”) and Albanian <i>mbars</i> (“to impregnate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baria (the grave accent, indicating low or normal pitch)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sullenness</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰρῠ́θῡμος</i> (bărŭ́thūmos, “sullen”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s, “heavy, grievous”) + <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrument to find the weight of liquids</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰ́ρος</i> (bắros, “weight”) +&lrm; <i>-ύλλιον</i> (-úllion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to weigh down, oppress, depress</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be heavy, weary, oppressed</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to oppress, weary, afflict</li><li>to mark with the grave accent</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s, “heavy, weighty”) +&lrm; <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavy; weighty</li><li>heavy; burdensome; oppressive</li><li>deep; hollow; loud (voice)</li><li>grievous; troublesome; painful</li><li>unwholesome</li><li>hard; cruel</li><li>strong; mighty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷarus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷréh₂us</i>. Cognate to Sanskrit <i>गुरु</i> (gurú), Latin <i>gravis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weight, heaviness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>heaviness of limb</li><li>(<i>of digestion</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>of men</i>) troublesomeness, importunity</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>disagreeableness</li></ol><li>arrogance</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>gravity</li></ol><li>(<i>of sound</i>) depth, low pitch</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the grave accent</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>absence of accent</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) adoption of an injured tone</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s, “heavy”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deep-sounding</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) (<i>of words</i>) barytone; having its acute accent on the last syllable changed to a grave accent (i.e. no accent at all)</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s) +&lrm; <i>τόνος</i> (tónos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>queen</li><li>princess</li><li>noblewoman</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰσῐλεύς</i> (băsĭleús, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă, feminine ending), synchronically also analyzable as <i>βᾰσῐλεύς</i> (băsĭleús, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă). Intervocalic <i>ῐ</i> (ĭ) developed from a sonorant cluster /wj/, which assimilated to /jj/, then became the second element in the diphthong <i>ει</i> (ei); the formation is thus <i>&#42;βᾰσῐ́λεϝϳᾰ</i> (&#42;băsĭ́lewjă) &gt; <i>&#42;βᾰσῐ́λεϳϳᾰ</i> (&#42;băsĭ́lejjă) &gt; <i>βᾰσῐ́λειᾰ</i> (băsĭ́leiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The home of a king: palace (<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>)</li><li>The place from which a king rules: capital</li><li>Royal treasury</li><li>tiara, diadem</li></ol><p>From <i>βασιλεύς</i> (basileús, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the king, kingly, royal</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰσῐλεύς</i> (băsĭleús, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βασίλειος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Basil</li></ol><p>From <i>βασίλειος</i> (basíleios, “of the king, kingly, royal”), from <i>βασιλεύς</i> (basileús, “king”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>queen, a female monarch</li><li>(<i>at Rome</i>) empress</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰσῐλεύς</i> (băsĭleús, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐσσᾰ</i> (-ĭssă, female suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>βάσανος</i> (básanos, “touchstone”)</li></ol><p>From <i>βάσανος</i> (básanos) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>βᾰσῐ́λειον</i> (băsĭ́leion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>princelet, chieftain</li><li>basilisk, kind of serpent</li><li>wren (<i>Troglodytes troglodytes</i>)</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Regulus, a star in the constellation Leo</li><li>The given name of a Roman Emperor, Basiliscus (written in uppercase as <i>Βασιλισκός</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰσῐλεύς</i> (băsĭleús, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kingdom, a dominion</li><li>A hereditary monarchy</li><li>The office of king</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in Athens</i>) the office of archon</li></ol><li>(<i>with passive meaning</i>) being ruled by a king</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>βασιλεύς</i> (basileús, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>βασιλείᾱ</i> (basileíā, “kingdom”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief, master</li><li>emperor, king, lord, patron</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷatiléus</i>, with <i>σ</i> (s) developing by assibilation. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀣𐀯𐀩𐀄</i> (qa-si-re-u), that is, <i>&#42;gʷasileus</i>. Further etymology uncertain. Multiple Indo-European etymologies have been advanced, while Beekes considers it Pre-Greek in origin. Possibly from an Anatolian language (see Lydian <i>𐤡𐤠𐤯𐤯𐤬𐤳</i> (pattoš, “king”)), although the Mycenaean (Linear B) evidence points to an original form with initial <i>gʷ</i>.</p><p>Case forms show Attic shortening of the original stem <i>βασιληϝ-</i> (basilēw-); see quantitative metathesis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be king, to rule, reign as king</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ἡ βασιλεύσουσα πόλις</i> (hē basileúsousa pólis)) the imperial city, Rome</li><li>to enjoy as absolute master</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to live right royally</li><li>(<i>causal</i>) to appoint as king</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>βασιλεύς</i> (basileús, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury, royal bank</li><li>palace</li><li>royal decree</li><li>name for various remedies, including&#58; plaster and eyesalve</li><li>basil (<i>Ocimum basilicum</i>)</li></ol><p>The meaning basil appears first attested in Aëtius of Amida and seems calqued from the Middle Persian term continued in contemporary Persian as شاهسپرم (šâhesparam), شاهسپرغم (šâhesparaġm), from شاه (šâh, “king”) +&lrm; اسپرم (esparam, “fragrant herb”), as also Arabic calqued it as رَيْحَان الْمَلِك (rayḥān al-malik) from رَيْحَان (rayḥān, “fragrant herb”) and مَلِك (malik, “king”). See also the synonymous presumed Oriental borrowing <i>ὤκῐμον</i> (ṓkĭmon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>royal, kingly, regal</li></ol><p><i>βᾰσῐλεύς</i> (băsĭleús, “king”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amulet, talisman, charm, especially one against the evil eye</li></ol><p>From <i>βάσκᾰνος</i> (báskănos, “sorcerer, bewitcher”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of duck</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to other bird names, such as <i>ἀτταγᾶς</i> (attagâs, “black francolin”) and <i>ἐλασᾶς</i> (elasâs, “kind of teal”). According to Beekes, the alternative form <i>βοσκάς</i> (boskás) has not been influenced by the verb <i>βόσκω</i> (bóskō, “to feed, nourish”), nor is the initial <i>β-</i> of Thracian or Illyrian origin, if compared to the <i>φ-</i> of the variant <i>φασκάς</i> (phaskás). The same author believes that these variations rather confirm a Pre-Greek origin. On the other hand, Furnée connects the word to Serbo-Croatian <i>pȁtka</i> (“duck”), Spanish <i>pato</i> (“duck”) and Sardinian <i>busciu</i>, suggesting a common root even though unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fox (<i>Vulpes vulpes</i>)</li><li>dress of Thracian bacchanals, made of fox skins</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) Thracian bacchanal</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) impudent woman, courtesan</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Herodotus, the word derives from a Libyan language, a theory that could be supported by the etymological connection with Coptic <i>ⲃⲁϣⲁⲣ</i> (bašar, “fox”); Černý, however, rather regards the Coptic as a loanword from Greek. Szemerényi looks for a connection with Hittite (u̯aššuu̯ar, “clothing”), but this discarded by Neumann.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baldric, a broad belt used to hold a sword</li></ol><p>From <i>βασταγή</i> (bastagḗ, “transport”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift, lift up, raise</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to lift up, exalt, ennoble</li></ol><li>to bear, carry</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hold in one's hands</li><li>to have in mind, consider, weigh, make proof of</li></ol><li>to carry off, take away</li><li>to handle, touch</li></ol><p>Probably Pre-Greek. Has been compared to <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go, move”), but Beekes finds this unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basterna, palanquin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>basterna</i> (“litter”) + <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transport</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰστᾰ́ζω</i> (băstắzō, “to bear, carry”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cake made with flour and honey</li></ol><p>According to Beekes of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the word <i>βασυμνιάτης</i> (basumniátēs, “pastry cook”), which shows a variation <i>-ν-/-μν-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of flatfish, perhaps the skate or ray</li><li>stonechat, any bird of the genus <i>Saxicola</i></li><li>samphire (<i>Crithmum maritimum</i>)</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin in view of the similarity with <i>βότις</i> (bótis, “kind of fish”), which would display a vowel interchange that is typical of substrate words. In addition, the same author finds the technicality of this ichthyonym a further proof for a Pre-Greek etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cup</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;bātiyakah</i> (literally “phiale”), from <i>&#42;bātah</i> (“wine, must”), whence also Middle Persian <i>bʾtk'</i> (bādag, “wine, must”). Compare Persian بادیه (bâdiye, “large bowl”). Furnée compares <i>βάτος</i> (bátos, “measure for liquids”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buttercup (plant of the genus <i>Ranunculus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰ́τρᾰχος</i> (bắtrăkhos, “frog”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>rānunculus</i> (diminutive of <i>rāna</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of frog-green stone</li></ol><p>From <i>βάτραχος</i> (bátrakhos, “frog”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stammer</li><li>to speak idly</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Aramaic <i>אמר בטלתא</i>, or from <i>Βάττος</i> (Báttos, “Battus, stuttering king of Cyrene”), or possibly onomatopoeic + <i>λογέω</i> (logéō). Compare Latin <i>butubatta</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>βατταλογέω</i> (battalogéō)</li></ol><p>From <i>Βάττος</i> (Báttos) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stuttering</li><li>idle repetition in speech</li></ol><p>From <i>βαττολογέω</i> (battologéō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to be gone through; passable, accessible</li><li>speeding</li></ol><p>From zero-grade of the root of <i>ἔβην</i> (ébēn), the aorist of <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go”), +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dildo</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same onomatopoeic root of <i>βαυβάω</i> (baubáō, “to sleep”) and <i>βαυβώ</i> (baubṓ, “nurse of Demeter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cradle</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it is, together with <i>βαυκαλάω</i> (baukaláō, “to lull to sleep”), a derivation from <i>βαύκαλον</i> (baúkalon, “softened, effeminate, affected”). This, in turn, originates from <i>βαυκός</i> (baukós, “affected, prudish”), probably ultimately from a Pre-Greek adjective.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affected, prudish</li></ol><p>According to Beekes this is probably a Pre-Greek word; he further compares other adjectives, such as <i>γλαυκός</i> (glaukós), <i>σαυκός</i> (saukós), <i>φολκός</i> (pholkós), for the suffix. The connection with <i>βαυκίδες</i> (baukídes, “woman's shoes”) remains still unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dipping</li><li>the temper of a blade</li><li>dye</li></ol><p>From <i>βάπτω</i> (báptō, “to dip”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dyer</li><li>gilder</li></ol><p>From <i>βάπτω</i> (báptō, “to dye; to dip, immerse”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for dyeing, tinctorial</li><li>(<i>of books</i>) about gilding and silvering</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰφή</i> (băphḗ, “dye”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>bark! woof!</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel for cooling wine or water</li></ol><p>Probably of Afroasiatic origin, in particular, from Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pelican (<i>Pelecanus onocrotalus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes of Pre-Greek origin, as seens in other words sharing the same suffix, like <i>δοῖδυξ</i> (doîdux, “pestle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>βαῖνε</i> (baîne)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furnace, forge</li><li>stand for a pot</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes doesn't dismiss a Pre-Greek origin in view of the technicality of this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to milk, to extract milk from cows</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(middle voice,&#32;of a cow) to yield to milking</li></ol><li>to suck</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a Pre-Greek source, in view of the prefix <i>βδ-</i> and the presence of the geminate <i>-λλ</i>. If the gloss <i>βδαλοί</i> (bdaloí, “garfish; varicose veins”) mentioned by Hesychius of Alexandria is really connected to this verb, then this would strengthen the theory of a Pre-Greek origin,</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leech, a blood-sucking annelid of the subclass Hirudinea</li><li>lamprey, a eel-like fish of the order Petromyzontiformes</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>βδάλλω</i> (bdállō, “to milk; to suck”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bdellium, an aromatic gum obtained from <i>Commiphora africana</i></li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a word of Middle Eastern origin, perhaps from Hebrew <i>בְּדֹלַח</i> (bəḏṓlaḥ), cognate with Akkadian <i>𒁉𒁺𒌨𒄷</i> (bidurḫu). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. The reverse hypothesis is at the Hebrew entry.</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horror, abomination (horrific thing or act)</li></ol><p>Hellenistic noun from ancient verb <i>βδελύσσομαι</i> (bdelússomai)/<i>βδελύττομαι</i> (bdelúttomai, “to be disgusted; despise”) (stem: <i>&#42;βδελύτj</i> (&#42;bdelútj)) from adjective <i>βδελῠρός</i> (bdelŭrós) from <i>&#42;βδελυλός</i> (&#42;bdelulós), which originates from the verb <i>βδέω</i> (bdéō, “fart”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pesd-</i> (cognates, Latin <i>pēdō</i> &gt; French <i>péter</i>). Also see <i>βδύλλω</i> (bdúllō, “to be terribly afraid, to fart from fear”).</p><p>Unlike all the other cognates which were connected to the sense "fart", this noun first appears (with frequency) at the Old Testament translation in Greek, with sense "deeds or idols repugnant to God".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stench</li></ol><p>From <i>βδέω</i> (bdéō, “I break wind”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break wind, fart</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;bzdḗō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;psdéh₁yeti</i>, from <i>&#42;pesd-</i> (“to fart”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nausea, sickness</li><li>filth, nastiness</li></ol><p>From <i>βδελυγμός</i> (bdelugmós, “abomination”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) disgusting, abominable</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>βδελύσσομαι</i> (bdelússomai) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disgusting, loathsome, repulsive, repugnant</li></ol><p>This adjective is usually derived from the verb <i>βδέω</i> (bdéō, “to break wind”) with a suffix "-λυ-", but Beekes finds this etymology untenable, since the connection between the two words is not clear semantically. The author prefers to suggest a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the prefix <i>βδ-</i> and the suffix <i>-υρ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>constantly, without interruption</li></ol><p><i>βέβαιος</i> (bébaios, “constant”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confirmation</li></ol><p>From <i>βεβαιόω</i> (bebaióō, “I confirm”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to confirm, assure, secure</li></ol><p><i>βέβαιος</i> (bébaios) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stupid, idiotic, moronic</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the prenasalized variant mentioned by Hesychius of Alexandria is proof of a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A quiver</li></ol><p>From <i>βέλος</i> (bélos, “arrow”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>needlecase</li></ol><p>From <i>βελόνη</i> (belónē, “needle”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dart wrapped with pitch and tow, and thrown while on fire from an engine</li></ol><p>From <i>βέλος</i> (bélos, “missile, arrow, dart”) +&lrm; <i>σφενδόνη</i> (sphendónē, “sling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to improve</li></ol><p>From <i>βελτῑ́ων</i> (beltī́ōn, “better”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>needle</li><li>pipefish</li><li>garfish</li></ol><p>From <i>βέλος</i> (bélos, “arrow, dart”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πραικόκιον</i> (praikókion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) apricot tree</li></ol><p>From <i>βερίκοκκον</i> (beríkokkon, “apricot (fruit)”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía); the root, like <i>πραικόκιον</i> (praikókion), is from Latin <i>praecoquum</i>, alternative form of <i>praecox</i> (“early-ripening, premature”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appoint (first-person singular optative present)</li></ol><p>Possibly akin to <i>μείρομαι</i> (meíromai, “to receive”), <i>μέρος</i> (méros, “part”). Alternatively borrowed from a Paleo-Balkan language, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰer-</i> (“to bring”) akin to Ancient Greek <i>φερνή</i> (phernḗ, “dowry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ironwort of subspecies <i>Sideritis romana</i> subsp. <i>purpurea</i> (syn. <i>Sideritis purpurea</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>vettōnica</i> (“betony”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threshold, the bottom-most part of a doorway</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeh₂-</i> (“to step”), the same root of <i>ἔβην</i> (ébēn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) paved with bricks</li></ol><p><i>βήσσαλον</i> (bḗssalon, brick) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjective forming suffix). Possibly from &#42;βησσαλόω (&#42;bēssalóō) combined with the same suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use force, apply force</li><li>to inflict violence on</li><li>to force, constrain</li></ol><p><i>βίᾱ</i> (bíā) +&lrm; <i>-αζομαι</i> (-azomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;poetic) synonym of <i>βῐάζομαι</i> (bĭázomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>βῐ́αιος</i> (bĭ́aios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must overpower or be overpowered</li></ol><p><i>βιάζω</i> (biázō, “overpower”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, verbal adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) violent person</li></ol><p>From <i>βιάζομαι</i> (biázomai, “to inflict violence”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to mount</li><li>to raise, exalt</li><li>to put on</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strip of papyrus</li><li>small book, tablet, letter</li><li>any book or writing</li></ol><p>Attic corruption, possibly by assimilation, of <i>βῠβλῐ́ον</i> (bŭblĭ́on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer of books, scribe</li></ol><p>From <i>βῐβλῐ́ον</i> (bĭblĭ́on, “small book, tablet”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) writing of books</li></ol><p>From <i>βῐβλῐ́ον</i> (bĭblĭ́on) +&lrm; <i>-γρᾰφῐ́ᾱ</i> (-grăphĭ́ā) and/or <i>βῐβλῐογρᾰ́φος</i> (bĭblĭogrắphos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bookcase</li><li>library</li><li>archive</li><li>compilation</li></ol><p>From <i>βῐβλῐ́ον</i> (bĭblĭ́on, “book”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “box; chest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dealer in books</li></ol><p>From <i>βῐβλῐ́ον</i> (bĭblĭ́on, “small book, tablet”) +&lrm; <i>κᾰ́πηλος</i> (kắpēlos, “retail dealer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bookseller’s shop, a bookshop, bookstore</li></ol><p>From <i>βῐβλῐοπώλης</i> (bĭblĭopṓlēs, “bookseller”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bookseller</li></ol><p>From <i>βιβλίον</i> (biblíon, “book”) +&lrm; <i>-πώλης</i> (-pṓlēs, “seller, dealer”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper of archives</li></ol><p>From <i>βῐβλῐ́ον</i> (bĭblĭ́on, “small book, tablet”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, eat up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷigʷrṓskō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷi-gʷr̥h₃-sḱé-ti</i>, from i-reduplicated <i>&#42;gʷerh₃-</i> (“to swallow, devour, eat”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-sḱéti</i>. Cognate Latin <i>vorō</i> (“to devour”), Sanskrit <i>गिरति</i> (giráti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small stone, pebble</li></ol><p>Lewy proposes a Semitic derivation, comparing the word with Aramaic <i>ܒܙܩ̈ܐ</i> (bizqā, “pebble, gravel”). On the other hand, Beekes sustains a Pre-Greek origin for this noun, in view of the suffix "-ακ-" which is typical of substrate words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive,&#32;vulgar) to have sexual intercourse with; to fuck</li></ol><p>Probably connected with <i>βία</i> (bía) (“force, violence”) and <i>βιάζω</i> (biázō) (“to use force”), with a vulgar or expressive formation. The etymology is uncertain, and no secure Indo-European cognates are established.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;vulgar) to be in a state of sexual desire; to lust after intercourse</li></ol><p>desiderative of <i>βινέω</i> (binéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) biography</li></ol><p>From <i>βίος</i> (bíos, life) +&lrm; <i>-γραφία</i> (-graphía, writing)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to live</li></ol><p><i>βίοτος</i> (bíotos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) of or pertaining to life</li></ol><p>From <i>βίος</i> (bíos) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow</li><li>bow-string, tendon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷiH-</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>ज्या</i> (jyā́, “bow-string”), Old Church Slavonic <i>жила</i> (žila, “vein, tendon”), and Albanian <i>mzith</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live</li></ol><p><i>βίος</i> (bíos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō). Doublet of inherited <i>ζῶ</i> (zô).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hurt, harm, damage, mischief</li></ol><p>A nominal formation in <i>βλαβ-</i> (blab-), perhaps from an assimilation of an earlier form <i>&#42;βλαπ-</i> (&#42;blap-), based on evidence from Cretan forms (<i>ἀβλοπής</i> (ablopḗs) for <i>ἀβλαβής</i> (ablabḗs) and <i>ἀβλοπία</i> (ablopía) for <i>ἀβλαβεία</i> (ablabeía)). This form is traditionally compared with Latin <i>mulcō</i> (“to beat, maltreat”), <i>mulceō</i> (“to stroke”), Sanskrit <i>मृच्</i> (mṛc, “to threaten, injure”), <i>मर्चयति</i> (marcayati, “to hurt, injure”), with a hypothetical ancestor reconstructed as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melkʷ-</i> (“to stroke, hit”), though the interchanges <i>α/ο</i> and <i>β/π</i> in the word's variants lead Beekes to suspect a Pre-Greek origin instead. See also <i>βλάσφημος</i> (blásphēmos, “slanderous, blasphemous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harm, damage</li></ol><p>From <i>βλᾰ́πτω</i> (blắptō, “to harm, injure”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slack; stupid</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dolt</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥h₂-k-s</i>, from <i>&#42;ml̥h₂(e)k-</i> (“weak”), which seems to be a zero-grade <i>k</i>-extension of <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (“to grind; crush”). Compare Sanskrit <i>म्ला॒त</i> (mlātá, “softened”, of leather), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;mlātis</i> (“tender; soft”), and perhaps Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьlčàti</i> (“to be silent”); see also <i>μᾰλᾰκός</i> (mălăkós, “soft; gentle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disable; to hinder</li><li>to harm; to hurt; to damage</li></ol><p>Verbal formation from the same root as <i>βλᾰ́βη</i> (blắbē, “hurt, damage”), and traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥kʷyéti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melkʷ-</i> (“to damage”). Compare Sanskrit <i>मृच्यति</i> (mṛcyati, “to hurt; to injure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sprout, shoot</li><li>offspring, offshoot</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) excrescence, eruption on the skin</li></ol><p>From <i>βλαστάνω</i> (blastánō, “to bud, sprout, grow”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uttering ill-omened words; jinx</li><li>reviling, defaming</li><li>(<i>of words</i>) slanderous, libellous</li><li>uttering impious words; blasphemous</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mləspʰāmos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥-s-bʰeh₂-mo-s</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)mel-</i> (“to deceive”) + <i>φημί</i> (phēmí, “to say”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective-forming suffix). Beekes is implicitly skeptical of the connection of the first syllable to <i>&#42;(s)mel-</i>, however, and leaves the first syllable's origin open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mucus, a slippery secretion from the mucous membranes</li></ol><p>Often connected with Sanskrit <i>मृदु</i> (mṛdu, “soft, gentle”) and Middle Irish <i>blind</i> (“slime from the mouth of a dead man”). However, according to Furnée, the variations would rather prove a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look at</li><li>to see</li><li>to be aware of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷlépō</i>, of unclear further origin. Beekes claims it is Pre-Greek based on the existence of the variant <i>γλέπω</i> (glépō), while Hamp had suggested a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷlep-</i> (“to look”), though he offers no extra-Hellenic comparanda for such a root. Cognate with <i>βλέφαρον</i> (blépharon, “eyelid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>βδέλλαι</i> (bdéllai, “leeches”).</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a stem <i>βλε-</i> which is found also in the Hesychian glosses <i>καβλέει</i> (kabléei, “to gulp down”) and <i>βλεῖ</i> (bleî, “to collect honey, to milk”). According to Beekes, this stem cannot be traced from an Indo-European source, but rather from Pre-Greek, which would also be confirmed by the alternative form in <i>-υξ</i>, typical of several words of substrate origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>βλέφαρα</i> (bléphara)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) eyelid (a thin skin membrane that covers and moves over an eye)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin and cognate with <i>βλέπω</i> (blépō, “to see, to perceive”). Hamp had suggested Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷlep-</i> (“to look”), though he offers no extra-Hellenic comparanda for such a root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pennyroyal, pudding grass, a plant of the species <i>Mentha pulegium</i></li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin in view of the lack of etymology and the strange formation of the stem. Nonetheless a Proto-Hellenic reconstruction <i>&#42;gʷlā́kʰōn</i> is possible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off combs of honey, take honey, squeeze honey</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to squeeze people</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to rub, fondle</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mlíťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mlít-ye-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;mélit</i> (“honey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple amaranth (<i>Amaranthus blitum</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Not related to <i>μύλη</i> (múlē, “mill”) or <i>ἀμαλδύνω</i> (amaldúnō, “to soften, mitigate”) through a reconstructed form <i>&#42;μλίτον</i> (&#42;mlíton). Perhaps related to Albanian <i>lëpjetë</i> (“sorrel”), but compare also the cognates listed at Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;loboda</i> (“goosefoot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>βλῐ́σσω</i> (blĭ́ssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmful, pernicious</li></ol><p>From <i>βλᾰ́β(η)</i> (blắb(ē), “harm, damage”) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) weak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥dus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>splay-footed, having abnormally flat feet</li><li>bent, twisted, crooked, distorted</li></ol><p>The word shares the formation with <i>γαμψός</i> (gampsós, “curved, crooked”), <i>γαυσός</i> (gausós, “crooked”) and <i>λοξός</i> (loxós, “oblique”). According to Beekes, the word is from Pre-Greek due to the presence of the alternative form beginning in <i>π-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hurtful, mischievous</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Harmful [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><li>adverb <i>βλᾰπτῐκῶς</i> (blăptĭkôs)</li></ol><p>From <i>βλαβ-</i> (blab-), the stem of <i>βλάπτω</i> (bláptō, “to harm”), + <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of plants) to sprout, bud, begin to grow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive,&#32;chiefly&#32;poetic,&#32;figuratively,&#32;of people) to sprout up: be born</li></ol><li>(transitive,&#32;causative) to produce, propagate</li></ol><p>Denominal of <i>βλαστός</i> (blastós, “germ, sprout”). Origin uncertain, often compared to either <i>βλωθρός</i> (blōthrós, “tall”), which see, or German <i>Blatt</i> (“leaf, blade”). As for the latter, if from <i>&#42;bʰleh₃-</i>, compare <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon) and German Blütenstand from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sprout, shoot, bud</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) offspring, scion</li></ol><p>From <i>βλᾰστᾰ́νω</i> (blăstắnō, “to bud, sprout”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak profanely of sacred things, blaspheme</li><li>to speak ill of, defame, malign, slander</li></ol><p><i>βλάσφημος</i> (blásphēmos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>word of evil omen, profane speech</li><li>defamation, slander, libel</li><li>blasphemy, irreverent speech against God</li></ol><p>From <i>βλᾰ́σφημος</i> (blắsphēmos, “blasphemous”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of sandal or elegant slipper</li></ol><p>According to Beekes from Pre-Greek, in view of the variation <i>τ-δ</i> found in the alternative form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exult haughtily, boast, brag</li></ol><p>The word is probably from an unattested noun <i>&#42;βλέμος</i> (&#42;blémos) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō). The root noun can also be found in <i>ἀβλεμής</i> (ablemḗs, “feeble”). No etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) eyelash</li></ol><p>From <i>βλέφαρον</i> (blépharon, “eyelid”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) terrestrial scorpion</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested in Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bleat, bleating</li><li>(<i>of infants</i>) wailing</li></ol><p>An onomatopoeic formation with many parallels, like Czech <i>blekati</i>, German <i>blöken</i>, Latvian <i>blêt</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with χείρ</i>) taking honey</li></ol><p>From <i>βλῐ́σσω</i> (blĭ́ssō, “to cut off honeycomb”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, agent suffix) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, forms feminine nouns).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hairy, shaggy, bristling</li><li>rough, coarse, not refined</li><li>masculine, manly, virile</li><li>fierce, grim, ferocious</li><li>solemn, dignified, impressive</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Leumann derives it from a root common to Latin <i>vultur</i> (“vulture”). There is no evidence, however, that the word is Indo-European.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of trees</i>) high, tall, elongated</li></ol><p>Conventionally connected with Sanskrit <i>मूर्धन्</i> (mūrdhan, “head, top”) and Old English <i>molda</i> (“upper part of the head, crown”) through the reconstructed form <i>&#42;μλωθρός</i> (&#42;mlōthrós). However, this does not explain the variation, which points to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷ-</i>: likewise with Frisk’s suggested connections <i>μέλαθρον</i> (mélathron, “ridgepole”) or <i>βλαστάνω</i> (blastánō, “to sprout, grow”). According to Beekes, the variant rather suggests a Pre-Greek origin. On the other hand, linguists Romain Garnier and Benoît Sagot posit that the term may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḷh₃-dʰ-r-ó-</i>, which they suggest may belong to the root <i>&#42;melh₃-</i> (“to come”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morsel of bread</li></ol><p>The formation resembles the synonymous word <i>ψωμός</i> (psōmós). However, the etymology remains unknown.</p><p>According to Georgescu, it is synonymous with Latin <i>panis quadratus</i> (“square bread”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bdellium</li></ol><p>Probably from a Semitic source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of liquids</i>) to bubble or gush forth</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bubbling</li></ol><p>From <i>βλῠ́ζω</i> (blŭ́zō, “to bubble”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>βλύζω</i> (blúzō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) come, go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥h₃-sḱé-ti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;melh₃-</i> (“to come”) with inchoative suffix <i>&#42;-sḱéti</i> (= <i>-σκω</i> (-skō)). According to Beekes, the aorist stem <i>βλω-</i> (blō-) probably emerged from a zero-grade plural form. The LIV suggests that the aorist stem emerged from a PIE root aorist <i>&#42;mélh₃-t ~ ml̥h₃-ént</i>. Beekes explains the aorist stem <i>-μολ-</i> (-mol-) as the result of the metathesis of the full-grade of the PIE root. Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>iz-mòlīti</i> (“let appear”) and Slovene <i>molíti</i> (“pass, hand over”). Possibly related to Tocharian A <i>mlusk-</i> (“escape”) and Tocharian B <i>mlutk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throw, cast of dice</li><li>a missile (thrown weapon)</li><li>a shot, wound</li><li>coverlet</li></ol><p>From <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “throw”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fastening: band or hoop</li><li>rivet, bolt, plug</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷl̥H-trom</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷelH-</i> (“to throw”), even though Beekes finds the connection with <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw, cast”) uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male fern, aspidium, any fern of the species <i>Dryopteris filix-mas</i></li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variation "ρ/ν" seen in the alternative forms could suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garland chrysanthemum (<i>Glebionis coronaria</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθεμον</i> (ánthemon, “flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout</li></ol><p>From <i>βοή</i> (boḗ, “shout”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō). Possibly cognate to Sanskrit <i>जोगु॑वे</i> (jóguve, “to sound; to shout; to proclaim”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shout, cry</li><li>battle cry</li></ol><p>Of imitative origin, but the exact Proto-Indo-European root is unclear. Compared are Sanskrit <i>गु</i> (√gu, “to sound, proclaim, shout”), Lithuanian <i>gausti</i> (“dull sound”), but these could instead be related to <i>γοάω</i> (goáō, “to wail”) (from <i>&#42;gewH-</i> (“to cry out”), <i>&#42;geH-</i> (“to cry”)). Also compare <i>βύας</i> (búas, “eagle owl”), <i>βοηθέω</i> (boēthéō, “to help someone”), <i>βωστρέω</i> (bōstréō, “to call on for aid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aid, help, assistance</li></ol><p>From <i>βοηθέω</i> (boēthéō, “to help, assist”), from <i>βοηθόος</i> (boēthóos, “heeding the call to arms, rushing to battle”, Homeric).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) oxhide, cowhide</li></ol><p>Substantivization of <i>βόειος</i> (bóeios), feminine in agreement with an understood <i>δορᾱ́</i> (dorā́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help somebody, aid, assist [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>βοηθός</i> (boēthós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heeding the call to arms, rushing to battle</li><li>assisting, auxiliary; aiding, helping</li></ol><p>Homeric univerbation of <i>(ἐπί) βοὴν θεῖν</i> ((epí) boḕn theîn), from <i>βοή</i> (boḗ, “shout, call”) + <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “swift, running”), post-classically shortened to <i>βοηθός</i> (boēthós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>assisting, auxiliary</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assistant, helper</li></ol><p>Shortened form of <i>βοηθόος</i> (boēthóos, “hasting to the cry for help”), from <i>βοή</i> (boḗ, “shout, call”) +&lrm; <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “swift”, from <i>θέω</i> (théō, “run”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small trench to set plants in</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) small ulcer</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) (<i>in plural</i>) small sockets of the teeth</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>βόθρος</i> (bóthros, “trench, pit”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stroke or wound of a missile</li><li>a throw or cast of the dice</li></ol><p>From <i>βᾰ́λλω</i> (bắllō, “to throw”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female slave in Crete</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>javelin, dart</li><li>cast of the dice</li><li>die</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) lightray</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) jab (mild verbal insult)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelH-</i> (“to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) bulb of various plants, especially onion</li><li>tassel hyacinth (<i>Muscari comosum</i>)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) eyeball</li></ol><p>An onomatopoeic term with syllable reduplication; similar words are found in other languages including Indo-European ones, such as Latin <i>bulla</i> (“water bubble”) and Latvian <i>burbulis</i> (“water bubble”). Note also Armenian <i>բողկ</i> (boġk, “radish”), Sanskrit <i>बल्बज</i> (balbaja, “Indian goosegrass”), and Albanian <i>bajgë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>buzz!</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic, from the same source as <i>βόμβος</i> (bómbos, “buzzing, humming”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buzzing insect, especially the bumblebee</li><li>cocoon of the silkworm</li><li>narrow-necked vessel, that gurgles in pouring</li></ol><p>From the same onomatopoeic source of <i>βόμβος</i> (bómbos, “buzzing, humming”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silken</li></ol><p>From <i>βόμβῡξ</i> (bómbūx, “silkworm”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a cow, cow-shaped</li></ol><p><i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-id, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, meat, properly of carnivorous beasts</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “to eat”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē). The term may have been inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷorā́</i>, itself perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷorh₃éh₂</i>, whence also perhaps Latin <i>vorō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intestinal rumbling</li></ol><p>From <i>βορβορύζω</i> (borborúzō, “to rumble”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós), an imitative reduplicated formation, see also <i>βόρβορος</i> (bórboros, “filth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Homeric form of <i>ἐβόσκοντο</i> (ebóskonto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass, fodder, pasture</li><li>herb</li></ol><p>From <i>βόσκω</i> (bóskō, “to feed, tend”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, instrument noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herdsman</li><li>soothsayer</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;attributive,&#32;of a working animal such as a dog) herding; for herding; which herds</li></ol><p>From <i>βόσκω</i> (bóskō) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, masculine agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to herbs</li><li>pertaining to herbal remedies</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine plural) herb-gatherers</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;masculine) vegan hermit</li></ol><p>From <i>βοτάνη</i> (botánē, “herb”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squirting cucumber (<i>Ecballium elaterium</i>)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) kind of bracelet</li></ol><p>From <i>βούβαλος</i> (boúbalos, “buffalo”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groin, abdomen</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) a bubo in the groin (a swelling of lymph nodes due to infection)</li></ol><p>Unknown; possibly Pre-Greek. Alternatively from a Proto-Indo-European expressive root <i>&#42;bew-</i>, <i>&#42;bow-</i> (“to swell; ball”); see <i>βῠβός</i> (bŭbós, “full, great”), <i>βου-</i> (bou-, augmentative prefix), <i>βουνός</i> (bounós, “hill, mound; clot”), <i>βοῦᾰ</i> (boûă, “group of children; herd?”), <i>βῶλος</i> (bôlos, “clod”), <i>βολβός</i> (bolbós, “bulb”), but these cannot be regular morphological derivations of the same root.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βουβών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bubon, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol><p>From Lycian. According to Stephanus of Byzantium, the expected name would be <i>Βουβώνιος</i> (Boubṓnios), but the Lycians instead spelled <i>Βουβωνεύς</i> (Boubōneús). Connected by Ptolemy, Pliny, and Stephanus to <i>Βούδειον</i> (Boúdeion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>βούπρηστις</i> (boúprēstis, “kind of poisonous beetle”).</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow”) +&lrm; <i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō, “to bite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flaying oxen</li><li>of or for flaying</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, flay”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having the head of a bull</li><li>bullheaded (epithet of a renowned breed of Thessalian horses)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) thistle; one of various prickly plants:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>water chestnut, water caltrop (<i>Trapa natans</i>)</li><li>caltrop (<i>Tribulus terrestris</i>)</li></ol></ol><p><i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>κεφᾰλή</i> (kephălḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective forming suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βουκέφαλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bucephalus</li><li>a port of Corinth in the Saronic Gulf</li><li>a port in Attica</li></ol><p>From <i>βουκέφᾰλος</i> (bouképhălos, “bull-headed”), from <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>κεφᾰλή</i> (kephălḗ, “head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rustic, pastoral, bucolic</li><li>(substantive,&#32;neuter plural) pastoral poetry</li></ol><p>From <i>βουκόλος</i> (boukólos, “cowherd”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ox-head, bucranium</li><li>black bryony (<i>Dioscorea communis</i>, syn. <i>Tamus communis</i>)</li><li>calf's snout (<i>Antirrhinum orontium</i>)</li><li>fleawort (<i>Plantago psyllium</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) machine for reducing dislocations</li></ol><p>From of <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾱνίον</i> (krāníon, “cranium, head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A person who tends cattle: cowherd</li><li>A follower of Dionysus</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷoukólos</i>, whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀦𐀄𐀒𐀫</i> (qo-u-ko-ro /⁠gʷowkolos⁠/), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷow-kólos</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷṓws</i> (“cow”) + <i>&#42;kʷól-os</i> (“herder”), from <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to turn, to cultivate”). This Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷow-kólos</i> shows the phonological labial dissimilation known as the eponymous boukólos rule. Compare Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;boukolyos</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>severe cold in the head</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>κόρῡζᾰ</i> (kórūză, “rheum, mucus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>French sorrel (<i>Rumex scutatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>λάπαθον</i> (lápathon, “monk's rhubarb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>will, determination (esp. of the gods)</li><li>plan, project, intention</li><li>counsel, advice</li><li>council, senate</li></ol><p>From <i>βούλομαι</i> (boúlomai, “I wish, will”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must take counsel</li></ol><p>From <i>βουλεύω</i> (bouleúō) +&lrm; <i>-τέον</i> (-téon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>councillor, senator, bouleutes</li></ol><p><i>βουλεύω</i> (bouleúō, “to deliberate”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devised, plotted</li></ol><p><i>βουλεύ(ω)</i> (bouleú(ō)) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take counsel, deliberate, concert measures; (<i>in past tenses</i>) to determine or resolve after deliberation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deliberate on, plan, devise</li><li>(<i>control verb</i>) to take counsel; to resolve [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do’</small>]</li></ol><li>to give counsel</li><li>(<i>in political writers</i>) to be a member of council</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to take counsel with oneself, deliberate</li><li>to act as member of a council, to originate measures</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to determine with oneself, resolve on</li><li>(<i>control verb</i>) to resolve [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do’</small>]</li><li>(<i>rarely followed by a relative</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ, “decision, counsel”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, denominative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>counselling, advising</li></ol><p>From <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ, “counsel”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) ravenous hunger (<i>considered as a medical condition</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βούλῑμος</i> (boúlīmos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>towards the afternoon, to the western half of the sky</li></ol><p>From <i>βουλυτόν</i> (boulutón), accusative of <i>βουλυτός</i> (boulutós, “(early) afternoon”), + <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ellipsis of <i>καιρός βουλυτός</i> (kairós boulutós): time for unyoking oxen, i.e. early afternoon</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, “a suffix that creates verbal adjectives of possibility, either active or passive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common ash (<i>Fraxinus excelsior</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>μελία</i> (melía, “manna ash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rapeseed, <i>Brassica napus</i></li></ol><p>From <i>βουνός</i> (bounós, “hill”) +&lrm; <i>-ιάς</i> (-iás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill, heap</li><li>mound</li><li>altar</li><li>blood clot</li></ol><p>According to Herodotus, a Cyrenaic word; however, more recent research by Solmsen indicates a dialectal Doric origin instead. Based on variants of the related <i>βουνιάς</i> (bouniás, “Brassica napus”), such as <i>μουνιάς</i> (mouniás) and <i>μουνιαδικόν</i> (mouniadikón), Furnee takes the word as Pre-Greek and compares Basque <i>muno</i> (“hill”), apparently from the same substrate source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ox-piercing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large spit, big enough for a whole ox</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>πείρω</i> (peírō, “to pierce”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mule</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>burdō</i> (“mule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan alexanders (<i>Smyrnium perfoliatum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>σέλινον</i> (sélinon, “celery”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>epigraphy</i>) boustrophedonically (as a boustrophedon or in a boustrophedonic manner, such that the text directionality alternates on each line, resulting in a continuous stream of characters spanning multiple lines)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>στροφή</i> (strophḗ, “turning”) +&lrm; <i>-ηδόν</i> (-ēdón, adverbial suffix), in reference to the back-and-forth course traced by boustrophedon text resembling the path taken by an ox ploughing a field.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasture or path for oxen</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;βου-σόϝος</i> (&#42;bou-sówos), from a compound of <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>σεύω</i> (seúō, “to chase, drive, impel”). Compare also <i>μηλοσόη</i> (mēlosóē, “road on which cattle is driven”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ox-feeding</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to feed”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of funerary urn or sarcophagus</li></ol><p>Probably from Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of butter</li></ol><p>From <i>βούτῡρον</i> (boútūron, “butter”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an antelope, possibly a bubal hartebeest (<i>Alcelaphus buselaphus buselaphus</i>)</li><li>buffalo</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs), but it may be a loanword from an unknown source (such as Pre-Greek) that was associated with the native word secondarily.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italian bugloss (<i>Anchusa azurea</i>)</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) tongue depressor</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fenugreek (<i>Trigonella foenum-graecum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>κέρᾰς</i> (kérăs, “horn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ravenous hunger</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>λῑμός</i> (līmós, “hunger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tin</li><li>seal; bulla</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>bulla</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to will, wish, be willing, want [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>] (usually stronger than <i>ἐθέλω</i> (ethélō), implying choice or preference)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mean</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pretend, claim [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to prefer [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>, <i>along with</i> <b>μᾶλλον ἤ</b> (mâllon ḗ) <i>or</i> <b>ἤ</b> (ḗ) <small>‘over something else’</small>]</li></ol><p>According to Beekes and LIV, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷel(h₃)-</i> (“to want, wish”) (with no certain cognates), but with unexplained and pervasive o-grade in the present. The root may be identical to <i>&#42;gʷelH-</i> (“to hit by throwing”), via semantic shift "to throw" > "to throw (desires)" > "to want", but this is speculative.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) earthnut, <i>Bunium ferulaceum</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>οἶνος διὰ βουνίου</i>) the aromatic wine of earthnuts</li><li>vervain, <i>Verbena officinalis</i></li></ol><li>diminutive of <i>βουνός</i> (bounós), hillock</li></ol><p>From <i>βουνός</i> (bounós, “hill”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>big boy</li><li>(<i>of bees</i>) child of the ox (in allusion to the rite of būgonia)</li><li>some type of fish</li><li>herdsman</li></ol><p><i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bishopsweed (<i>Ammi majus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>πλευρά</i> (pleurá, “rib, side”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poisonous beetle, which being eaten by cattle in the grass causes them to swell up and die</li><li>hare's ear (<i>Bupleurum lancifolium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow”) +&lrm; <i>πρήθω</i> (prḗthō, “to burn; to inflame; to inflate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of large, coarse fig</li></ol><p><i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow”) +&lrm; <i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of sedge (<i>Carex riparia</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fabulous animal, half ox and half goat</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “goat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) diminutive of <i>βοῦττις</i> (boûttis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butter</li></ol><p><i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow”) +&lrm; <i>τυρός</i> (turós, “cheese”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>βούτυρον</i> (boúturon)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow”) +&lrm; <i>τῡρός</i> (tūrós, “cheese”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxeye (<i>Anacyclus radiatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>ὀφθαλμός</i> (ophthalmós, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Sparta</i>) a band of boys grouped together for training</li></ol><p>Usually related to <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow”). See Kaczyńska (2019) for discussion.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow, ox, cattle</li><li>shield</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷous</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀦𐀃</i> (qo-o /⁠gʷōns⁠/)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷṓws</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>cū</i> (English <i>cow</i>), Irish <i>bó</i>, Latin <i>bōs</i>, and Sanskrit <i>गो</i> (gó).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place for burning funeral pyres</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>būstum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cask, barrel, a round vessel shaped having flat ends</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is of Pre-Greek origin in view of the alternation <i>-τ-/-ττ-</i> visible in the variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagle owl (<i>Bubo bubo</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably of onomatopoeic origin, like Latin <i>būbō</i> (“owl”), Old Armenian <i>բուէճ</i> (buēč, “eagle owl”), and North Persian بوم (bum, “owl”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of Hera and women</i>) doe-eyed (having large, attractive eyes)</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow; ox”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, feminine adjectival suffix), literally “ox-eyed”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Elean,&#32;hapax legomenon) brother(s)</li></ol><p>Elean. Possibly borrowed from Illyrian, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰréh₂tēr</i> (“brother”), in which case a doublet of <i>φράτηρ</i> (phrátēr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sloe, blackthorn, a tree of the species <i>Prunus spinosa</i> and its small, astringent fruit</li></ol><p>Unknown, but maybe a loanword from Pre-Greek. See also <i>βραβύλη</i> (brabúlē, “broad-leaved anemone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fin</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) gill</li></ol><p>Possible relation to <i>βρόγχος</i> (brónkhos, “trachea, windpipe, throat”), <i>βρογχία</i> (bronkhía, “system of ducts connecting heart with liver”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoarseness or sore throat causing it</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) kind of disease of swine, either anthrax or foot-and-mouth disease</li></ol><p>Furnée connects the word with <i>βραχώδης</i> (brakhṓdēs, “rough, harsh”), <i>βρακίας</i> (brakías, “rough places”) and <i>βαρακινῇσιν</i> (barakinēîsin, “thorns, palisade”); according to Beekes, the set of variants <i>βρακ-/βραχ-/βραγχ-</i> is proof of Pre-Greek origin. The same author claims that the additional <i>-α-</i> found in the alternative form <i>βάραγχος</i> (bárankhos) is a further element that corroborates the Pre-Greek theory, even though he doesn't dismiss a purely phonetic epenthesis as a cause. The similarity with <i>βρόγχος</i> (brónkhos, “windpipe”) may have caused the semantic shift of <i>βράγχιον</i> (bránkhion, “fin; gill”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βράγχος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Branchus, the son of Smyrcus and lover of Apollo</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savin (<i>Juniperus sabina</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ἄσχυ</i> (áskhu), <i>μῶλυ</i> (môlu), <i>μίσυ</i> (mísu) and <i>σῶρυ</i> (sôru). The word has been compared to Akkadian <i>𒄑𒇷</i> (burāšum), Aramaic <i>בְּרָתָא</i> (bərāṯā) and Hebrew <i>בְּרוֹשׁ</i> (bərṓš), all meaning "cypress", suggesting a Semitic origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ῥάκος</i> (rhákos, “long robe”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiling</li><li>A shaking motion</li></ol><p>From <i>βράζω</i> (brázō, “to boil”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the sea</i>) to shake violently, throw up</li><li>to boil, seethe</li><li>to winnow grain</li></ol><p>Unknown. Bezzenberger connects the word with Latvian <i>murdēt</i> (“to boil up”) and Lithuanian <i>mùrdyti</i> (“to treat something by shaking it in water”). However, this is uncertain, as the basic regular sound laws seem to prohibit a connection.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shallows</li></ol><p>A plural neuter as though from <i>βράχος</i> (brákhos), traditionally derived from <i>βραχέα</i> (brakhéa, “<i>neuter plural of <i>βραχύς</i> (brakhús, “short, brief”)</i>”), as in the phrase <i>βραχέα ὕδατα</i> (brakhéa húdata, “shallow waters”), already in Herodotus.</p><p>Beekes neither espouses nor rejects this derivation, but offers an alternative theory that the word is related to the scantly-attested <i>βράγος</i> (brágos, “marsh-meadow”), and along with it, related to Albanian <i>bërrakë</i> (“swampy land”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>βρᾰχῠ́ς</i> (brăkhŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the bone directly above the brain, (<i>anatomy</i>) bregma; brainpan</li></ol><p>Originally <i>βρέχμα</i> (brékhma), <i>βρεχμός</i> (brekhmós); the ⟨γ⟩ has resulted from voicing assimilation. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mregʰ-</i> (“brain, skull”) and cognate with English <i>brain</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of waves</i>) to roar</li><li>(<i>of arms</i>) to clash</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) to shout; to rage</li></ol><p>Resembles Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrem-</i> (“to make noise”), whence Latin <i>fremō</i>, Middle High German <i>bremen</i>, and obsolete English <i>breme</i> (“famous; tempestuous”). However, the change from PIE <i>&#42;bʰ</i> to Greek /b/ (instead of the expected */pʰ/) would be unexplained. Thus, Beekes takes the word as an independent onomatopoeic formation. However, it has been alternatively suggested that the term may have been borrowed from an Indo-European substrate language where the sound shift of <i>&#42;bʰ</i> to /b/ was regular. If this theory is accepted, then the term may still ultimately derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrémeti</i>, the same thematic root present that provides the source for Latin <i>fremō</i>.</p><p>Not related to <i>βρόμος</i> (brómos, “oats”), which has been traditionally compared. The potential connection to <i>βρῶμος</i> (brômos, “stink, stench”), if any, is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deer</li></ol><p>A Messapic word like <i>βρέντιον</i> (bréntion, “stag's head”), possibly also found in place names like <i>Βρεντέσιον</i> (Brentésion, “Brindisi”). Further possible cognates are Latvian <i>briedis</i> (“deer”), Old Prussian <i>braidis</i> (“elk”), Elfdalian <i>brinde</i> (“elk”) and Albanian <i>bri</i> (“horn, antler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrogance, haughtiness</li><li>unknown kind of waterbird</li><li>tomb</li></ol><p>The meanings "arrogance" and "kind of waterbird" are probably not related. If <i>βρίνθος</i> (brínthos, “kind of bird”) is reliable, the bird name may be Pre-Greek. On the other hand, <i>βρινδεῖν</i> (brindeîn, “to be agitated, provoke”) is not related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stag's head</li></ol><p>A Messapic word like <i>βρένδος</i> (bréndos, “deer”), <i>βρένδον</i> (bréndon), possibly also found in place names like <i>Βρεντέσιον</i> (Brentésion, “Brindisi”). Further possible cognates are Latvian <i>briedis</i> (“deer”), Old Prussian <i>braidis</i> (“elk”), Elfdalian <i>brinde</i> (“elk”) and Albanian <i>bri</i> (“horn, antler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baby, infant</li><li>babe in the womb, foetus/fetus</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷrebʰ-</i>. Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>жрѣбѧ</i> (žrěbę) ("foal") and Irish <i>bromach</i> ("colt"). Compare also Old Armenian <i>կորիւն</i> (koriwn) ("cub").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet, to drench</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>send rain</li><li>(<i>in 3rd singular as impersonal</i>) it rains</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mergʰ-</i> (“to moisten, wet”) via a metathesized schwebeablaut form of <i>&#42;mregʰ-</i> or zero-grade form <i>&#42;mr̥gʰ-</i>; cognates include Latvian <i>merguot</i> (“to rain slowly”), <i>merga</i> (“soft rain”).</p><p>The shift of PIE <i>&#42;mr</i> &gt; Greek <i>&#42;b</i> is also seen in the unrelated <i>βρεχμός</i> (brekhmós, “front part of the head”), from <i>&#42;mregʰ-</i> (“brain, skull”), as well as <i>βραχύς</i> (brakhús, “short, brief; few”), from <i>&#42;mr̥ǵʰús</i> (“short, brief”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Thracian word for city</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;bria</i> (“town”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rye (<i>Secale cereale</i>) in Thrace and Macedonia</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;briza</i> (“spelt, rye”). Possibly ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;breǵʰ-</i>, related to Norwegian <i>brok</i> (“kind of grass”), or related to Sanskrit <i>व्रीहि</i> (vrīhi, “grain, rice”), <i>ὄρυζα</i> (óruza, “rice”), but this is less likely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, might, power</li><li>bellowing, roaring</li><li>threat, menace</li><li>womanly vice</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>βρῖθος</i> (brîthos, “weight”), <i>βρῑθῠ́ς</i> (brīthŭ́s, “heavy, ponderous”) and <i>ὄβρῐμος</i> (óbrĭmos, “strong; mighty”). Furnée further connects the word with the verb <i>φριμάσσομαι</i> (phrimássomai, “to snort and leap”), supporting the theory of a substrate origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broad-leaved anemone, a flowering plant of the species <i>Anemone hortensis</i></li></ol><p>Compare <i>βράβυλον</i> (brábulon, “sloe, blackthorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slowly</li></ol><p><i>βρᾰδῠ́ς</i> (brădŭ́s) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slowness</li></ol><p>From <i>βραδύς</i> (bradús) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make slow, delay</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to loiter, delay</li></ol><p>From <i>βρᾰδῠ́ς</i> (brădŭ́s, “slow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow</li><li>slow-witted, dull</li><li>(<i>time</i>) late, tardy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷr̥dus</i> (“slow”) (which bears some resemblance in form and meaning to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷreh₂-</i> (“heavy”)). Cognates include Lithuanian <i>gurdùs</i> (“slow”), Latvian <i>gur̃ds</i> (“tired”), Latin <i>gurdus</i> (“dolt, heavy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upper arm</li><li>shoulder</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a symbol of strength</li></ol><p>From <i>βρᾰχῠ́ς</i> (brăkhŭ́s, “short”) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn), because the upper arm is shorter than the forearm.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short-fingered, brachydactylous</li></ol><p><i>βρᾰχῠ́ς</i> (brăkhŭ́s, “short”) +&lrm; <i>δᾰ́κτῠλος</i> (dắktŭlos, “finger”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abridge; to shorten</li></ol><p>From <i>βρᾰχῠ́ς</i> (brăkhŭ́s, “short; brief”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) short, brief</li><li>(<i>of distance, height</i>) short, small, little</li><li>few</li><li>(<i>of importance</i>) humble, insignificant</li><li>(grammar,&#32;of vowels) short</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;brəkʰús</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mréǵʰus</i> (“short, brief”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>मुहुर्</i> (múhur), <i>मुहु</i> (múhu), Avestan <i>𐬩𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬰𐬎⸱𐬘𐬍𐬙𐬌</i> (m̨ər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>zu.jīti, “short-lived”), Latin <i>brevis</i>, Old English <i>miriġe</i> (English <i>merry</i>), and Albanian <i>murriz</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>βραχύς</i> (brakhús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shortness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>narrowness, deficiency</li></ol><li>smallness</li><li>(<i>prosody</i>) shortness of a syllable</li><li>scantiness</li><li>shallowness</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) use of a condensed or allusive expression</li></ol><p>From <i>βρᾰχῠ́ς</i> (brăkhŭ́s, “short”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>onomatopoeia</i>) ribbit (the sound of a croaking frog), a croak.</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic, a sound to imitate the croaking of frogs.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orphanage, in which children were fed</li></ol><p>From <i>βρέφος</i> (bréphos, “baby”) +&lrm; <i>τροφεῖον</i> (tropheîon, “small room, chamber”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) heavy, ponderous, weighty</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek root of <i>βρῖθος</i> (brîthos, “weight”), <i>βρῑ́</i> (brī́), <i>βρῑ́μη</i> (brī́mē, “strength; might”) and <i>ὄβρῐμος</i> (óbrĭmos, “strong; mighty”). Not related to <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s, “heavy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ὄνον</i> (ónon, “donkey, ass”) (<i>in Cyrene</i>).</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>βάρβαρον</i> (bárbaron, “foreign; barbaric”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>βρίκελος</i> (bríkelos, “name of a tragic mask”) and <i>βρύκος</i> (brúkos, “barbarian”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weighting of chariot, chariot-pressing, the one who presses the chariot with his own weight, chariot-rider</li></ol><p>From <i>βρῑ́θω</i> (brī́thō, “to be heavy”) +&lrm; <i>ἅρματος</i> (hármatos, “of chariot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thunder</li></ol><p>From <i>βρέμω</i> (brémō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bane of men, plague of man, epithet of Ares</li></ol><p>From <i>βροτός</i> (brotós, “mortal”) + <i>λοίγιος</i> (loígios) / <i>λοιγός</i> (“deadly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gory</li></ol><p>From <i>βρότος</i> (brótos) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) mortal man, human being (<i>often in plural</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mrətós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mr̥twós</i> or <i>&#42;mr̥tós</i> (“dead, mortal”), ultimately from the root <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to die”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>मृत</i> (mṛtá), Old Armenian <i>մարդ</i> (mard), Latin <i>mortuus</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>мрътвъ</i> (mrŭtvŭ), Persian مرد (mard) and Old English <i>morþ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) rain</li></ol><p>From <i>βρέχω</i> (brékhō, “to rain”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A locust, or its wingless larva</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, clearly a Pre-Greek word, due to the vocalic variations. The form <i>βρύκος</i> (brúkos) was compared with <i>βρύκω</i> (brúkō, “to eat greedily”), but the agreement may be secondary.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush, reed used for mats or beds</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell, teem</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to act wantonly; to revel</li></ol><p>From <i>βρῠ́ω</i> (brŭ́ō, “to swell”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>χιτῶνες</i> (khitônes, “tunics”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek of <i>βρύθακες</i> (brúthakes, “silken tunics”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inclined to eat, voracious</li><li>promoting the inclination to eat</li><li>a gnawing</li></ol><p>From <i>βρῶσῐς</i> (brôsĭs, “eating”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) bronchial tubes; bronchi</li></ol><p>From <i>βρόγχος</i> (brónkhos, “windpipe, trachea”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix), in the plural.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) trachea; windpipe</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) throat</li><li>gulp; draught</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is clearly connected with <i>βρόξαι</i> (bróxai, “to gulp down, swallow (again)”) and <i>βρόχθος</i> (brókhthos). The nasal infix leads to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: boorish person.</li></ol><p>Related to <i>βροκός</i> (brokós, “stupid, dull”). Furnée connects these words with <i>πρόκον</i> (prókon, “numb”) and, more doubtfully, with Latin <i>bargus</i> (“without intelligence”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oat (<i>Avena sativa</i>)</li><li>wild oat, slender oat (<i>Avena barbata</i>)</li><li>loud noise</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin, based on the alternating ρο/ορ vocalism.</p><p>From <i>βρέμω</i> (brémō, “to roar, grumble”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood that has run from a wound, gore</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p><p>Perhaps related to Sanskrit <i>मू॒र्त</i> (mūrtá, “clotted, coagulated”) (related to the verb <i>मूर्छति</i> (mūrchati, “to congeal, thicken, become solid, stiffen”); see <i>मूर्छ्</i> (mūrch) for more), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mr̥H-tó-</i>, but this requires an unexplained loss of the laryngeal.</p><p>Various attempts to link this term with <i>βροτός</i> (brotós) and <i>ἄμβροτος</i> (ámbrotos) have been made; Frisk and Beekes following him reject these as improbable. Schulze proposes a common origin with <i>ἀμφιβρότη</i> (amphibrótē, “covering the body on all sides”) from an unattested <i>&#42;βροτόν</i> (&#42;brotón, “body”) which may be further related to Sanskrit <i>मूर्ति</i> (mū́rti-, “body, figure, form”); this is also rejected.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) throat</li><li>gulp, draft, throatful</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>βρόζαι</i> (brózai) and <i>βρόγχος</i> (brónkhos). Compare also English <i>craw</i> (though Beekes expresses skepticism at a relation).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noose, slip knot</li><li>snare for birds</li><li>mesh of a net</li><li>halter round one's neck</li></ol><p>If the gloss on <i>βρυγχός</i> (brunkhós) is reliable, then the prenasalization points to a Pre-Greek origin. The word has been compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mèrža</i> (“net”) and Lithuanian <i>márška</i> (“net”). Another connection has been made with <i>μόροττον</i> (mórotton, “plaiting of bark”) but it is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herald</li><li>barbarian</li><li>alternative form of <i>βροῦκος</i> (broûkos)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the interchange <i>κ/χ</i> with the alternative form <i>βρυχός</i> (brukhós).</p><p>Beekes compares the word <i>βρίκελοι</i> (bríkeloi, “barbarians”) and concludes that these words are Pre-Greek, in view of the alternation <i>βρικ-/βρυκ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to devour, gobble</li><li>to bite, chew</li><li>to gnash, grind one's teeth</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests that it derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷruHǵʰ-</i> and compares Old Church Slavonic <i>грꙑсти</i> (grysti), and possibly Old Armenian <i>կրծեմ</i> (krcem).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea lettuce (<i>Ulva lactuca</i>)</li><li>tree moss (<i>Usnea barbata</i>)</li><li>liverwort (<i>Marchantia polymorpha</i>)</li><li>clustering male blossom of the hazel</li><li>(botany,&#32;in general) catkin, amentum</li></ol><p>From <i>βρύω</i> (brúō, “to abound, grow luxuriantly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bubbling up</li></ol><p><i>βρῡ́ω</i> (brū́ō, “to abound with, be bursting with”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of sea urchin</li></ol><p>The variants, together with the prenasalization, prove that this is a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) refuse of olives or grapes after pressing</li></ol><p>From <i>βρῦτος</i> (brûtos, “beer made of barley”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of or for beer</li></ol><p>From <i>βρῦτος</i> (brûtos, “beer”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a lion</i>) roar, roaring</li></ol><p>From <i>βρῡχάομαι</i> (brūkháomai, “to roar, bellow”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>βρύκω</i> (brúkō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be full to bursting</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to swell or teem with, especially of plants</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to be full of</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to abound, grow luxuriantly</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to burst forth with, gush with</li></ol></ol><p>Various theories have been suggested, including connection with Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;brusū</i> (“belly; breast”), Latin <i>frutex</i> (“shrub; trunk”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krūdaną</i> (“to crowd, press”). None of them are particularly convincing. Thus, taken by Beekes as unknown. Pokorny tentatively compares Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷéru</i> (“skewer, spit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>βρῦτος</i> (brûtos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beer made of barley</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;brutos</i> (“beer, a type of barley beer”). According to Pokorny, this is ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrewh₁-</i> (“to boil, be hot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shipworm (<i>Teredo navalis</i>) that bores into wooden piers, ships, etc.</li><li>that which is eaten, food, meat</li><li>(<i>dentistry</i>) cavity</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “I eat”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, object noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stink, stench, noisome smell</li></ol><p>Unknown. The word has been supposed to be identical with <i>βρόμος</i> (brómos, “loud noise”) (with hypothetical semantic development "emanating noise" > "emanating smell"), and, within modern Greek, has apparently been folk-etymologically conflated with it (see <i>βρόμικος</i> (vrómikos, “dirty, stinky”) for more).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of eating (food); eating a meal</li><li>food that is eaten, or is to be eaten</li><li>rust, corrosion</li></ol><p>From <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “I eat”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strip of papyrus</li><li>small book, tablet, letter</li><li>any book or writing</li></ol><p>From <i>βῠ́βλος</i> (bŭ́blos, “papyrus”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μεστός</i> (mestós, “full, sated”), <i>πλήρης</i> (plḗrēs, “full, satisfied”), <i>μέγας</i> (mégas, “big”).</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic word, related to <i>βύω</i> (búō, “to stuff full of, plug”), either with reduplication or with suffix <i>-βος</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>πυκνόν</i> (puknón, “close, compact”), <i>συνετόν</i> (sunetón, “intelligent, wise”), <i>γαῦρον</i> (gaûron, “haughty”), <i>μέγα</i> (méga, “big”).</li></ol><p>Related to <i>βυνέω</i> (bunéō, “to stuff, cram”) and <i>βύζην</i> (búzēn, “closely, close pressed”). Pokorny derives it from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰew-</i> (“to blow up, swell”) (Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bujьnъ</i>, Albanian <i>mbush</i>), but Beekes rejects this due to variance between initial <i>p-</i> and <i>b-</i> in the cognates he collects.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>depth</li><li>depth of the sea, deep water</li></ol><p>Traditionally considered a metathesis from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewb-</i> (“hazy, deep”), with cognates including Old Church Slavonic <i>дъно</i> (dŭno), Old English <i>dēop</i> (English <i>deep</i>) and Albanian <i>det</i> (from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;deubeta</i>). Unrelated to <i>βᾰθῠ́ς</i> (băthŭ́s, “deep, thick, profound”) and <i>βένθος</i> (bénthos, “sea depth”), despite similar semantics and superficially similar phonetics.</p><p>Alternately, cognate to Sanskrit <i>गाध</i> (gādha, “bottom, ford, shallows, standing-ground in water”).</p><p>Also compare the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰew-</i> with meaning related to swelling, as in a deepening.</p><p>However, Beekes rejects Indo-European origin, based on phonetic difficulties with theories like the above, as well as θ/σσ variation displayed by the word's variant <i>βυσσός</i> (bussós) which is bolstered by the latter's substrate-like formation <i>βυσσαλ-</i> (bussal-), and assigns it to Pre-Greek instead.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiral trumpet, horn</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>būcina</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to brood over, ponder deeply</li></ol><p>From <i>βυσσός</i> (bussós, “depth”) +&lrm; <i>δέμω</i> (démō, “build”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō), literally “build in the deep”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>βῠθός</i> (bŭthós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mushroom, especially champignon (<i>Agaricus bisporus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the original form <i>βωλήτης</i> (bōlḗtēs) was borrowed from Latin <i>bōlētus</i>, named after the town <i>Boletum</i> (present day Boltaña in Spain, famous for its mushrooms). <b>βωλίτης</b> was remodelled after its derivatives and borrowed back into Latin again by Pliny. The meaning of "root" is influenced by <i>βῶλος</i> (bôlos).</p><p>Perhaps cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bъdla</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clod-breaking</li></ol><p>From <i>βῶλος</i> (bôlos, “lump, clod of earth”) +&lrm; <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to strike, cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>altar-shaped vessel</li><li>bandage</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) polyhedron of unequal height, width and depth</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Ara, a constellation</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) base of molar teeth</li></ol><p>From <i>βωμός</i> (bōmós, “platform; altar”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raised platform; pedestal</li><li>altar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷōmós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷoh₂mós</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷeh₂-</i> (“to step”) (whence <i>ἔβην</i> (ébēn)). Compare <i>βῆμᾰ</i> (bêmă, “step”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flathead mullet (<i>Mugil cephalus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>br</i> (Bohairic Coptic <i>ⲫⲟⲣⲓ</i> (phori)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bogue (<i>Boops boops</i>) (<i>small fish</i>)</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. An old folk etymology derives the word from <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”); another folk theory considers the word onomatopoeic for the fish's ability to cry.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bovine (pertaining to cows and oxen)</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole, trench or pit dug in the ground</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) hole, hollow, cavity</li></ol><p>This word and the synonymous <i>βόθυνος</i> (bóthunos) have been connected with Latin <i>fodiō</i> (“to dig”), Lithuanian <i>bedù</i> (“sting, dig”) and Middle Welsh <i>bedd</i> (“canal”), assuming either a dissimilation of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰodʰ-</i> to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;bodʰ-</i>, which would explain the discrepancy between Greek <i>β-</i> and Latin <i>f-</i>, or a direct influence of <i>βαθύς</i> (bathús, “deep, profound”). However, Beekes prefers a derivation from Pre-Greek, citing both the impossibility of a connection with a Proto-Indo-European root and the meaning itself of this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowpie, cow pat, the excrement produced by a cow</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is probably Pre-Greek, in view of the numerous variations and the suffix <i>-ιτον</i>. Close to Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;balgā</i> (“dung”), from which Albanian <i>bajgë</i> (“dung”), which may be from the same substratum, but would point to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bolgʷ-</i> if inherited.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European bison</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a throw with a casting-net, a cast of a net</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transferred senses</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>a net</li><li>the thing caught, a draught or catch</li></ol></ol><li>a casting of teeth</li><li>a cast of dice</li><li>synonym of <i>θύρα</i> (thúra)</li><li>alternative form of <i>βῶλος</i> (bôlos)</li></ol><p>From <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw or cast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any deep, hollow sound, humming, buzzing, booming, rumbling</li></ol><p>An onomatopoeic term borrowed from the Pre-Greek substrate. Compare Lithuanian <i>bim̃balas</i>, Latvian <i>bam̃bals</i> (“beetle”), Russian <i>бу́бен</i> (búben, “tambourine”), Albanian <i>bubullimë</i> (“thunder”), and Old Norse <i>bumla</i> (“drum”) for similar formations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silkworm (<i>Bombyx mori</i>)</li><li>garment made of silk</li><li>deeptoned flute</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>cap of a flute</li><li>lowest note on the flute</li></ol></ol><p>The word must be of Anatolian origin, as is also suggested by its structure. Probably related also to <i>βάμβαξ</i> (bámbax, “cotton”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European bison</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Generally considered to be a loanword from an Proto-Indo-European language. There have been attempts to connect it with <i>βόλινθος</i> (bólinthos), which is quite uncertain. Furnée tries to connect it with <i>μόναπος</i> (mónapos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) growing spadix of the date with immature fruit</li></ol><p>An Egyptian word. Compare Arabic بسر (busr, “unripe dates”), Classical Syriac <i>ܒܣܪܐ</i> (*busrā, “unripe grape”), Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>בֻּסְרָא</i> (busrā) and Hebrew <i>בֹּסֶר</i> (boser, “unripe grape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mire, filth</li><li>clay, moist earth</li><li>foul abuse</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of onomatopoeic origin. A connection with Armenian <i>կորկ</i> (kork, “dirt”) remains very doubtful. Saycc saw a Hittite loanword in it, from <small>[script needed]</small> (pūrpura, “ball, lump; ball-shaped cakes”), which however does not fit either semantically nor phonologically.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>northern, from the quarter of the north wind</li><li>of the north wind or boreas</li></ol><p>From <i>Βορέας</i> (Boréas) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>βόσκειν</i> (bóskein)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of herdsmen</i>) feed, tend</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>generally</i>) feed, nourish, support, maintain, keep</li></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;of cattle) feed, graze</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be fed or nurtured</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeh₃-</i> (“to feed, tend”) with inchoative suffix <i>-σκω</i> (-skō). Cognates include <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “cow”) and Lithuanian <i>gúotas</i> (“herd”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curl, lock of hair</li><li>anything twisted or wreathed</li><li>tendril</li><li>ornament</li><li>male of the glowworm</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) seaweed</li></ol><p>Unknown, but Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin in view of the suffix <i>-υχος</i>. Often connected with <i>βότρυς</i> (bótrus, “bunch of grapes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cluster of berries</li><li>shepherd's purse (<i>Capsella bursa-pastoris</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) kind of medicine made with excrements</li></ol><p>From <i>βότρῠς</i> (bótrŭs, “bunch of grapes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bunch of grapes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) clustered earring</li></ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) ambrosia (<i>Ambrosia maritima</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Jerusalem oak (<i>Dysphania botrys</i> [syn. <i>Chenopodium botrys</i>])</li></ol><li>the Pleiades</li></ol><p>Considered Pre-Greek, but also Semitic, relating to Hebrew <i>בֹּסֶר</i> (boser, “unripe grape”), Arabic بُسْر (busr, “unripe dates”), Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>בֻּסְרָא</i> (busrā), <i>בּוּסְרָא</i> (busrā, “unripe grape”), Classical Syriac <i>ܒܣܪܐ</i> (*busrā, “unripe grape”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βότρυς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Botrys; Batroun, Lebanon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of ox's hide</li></ol><p>From <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox, cattle”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagle owl</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic, compare Latin <i>būbō</i>, Bulgarian <i>буч</i> (buč), Old Armenian <i>բու</i> (bu), Persian بوم (bum), Arabic بُوم (būm), Classical Syriac <i>ܒܐܘܐ</i> (baʾwāʾ) and Caucasian languages such as Old Georgian <i>ბუვი</i> (buvi), Chechen <i>бухӏа</i> (buha), and Aghul <i>бу́гьу</i> (búhu).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of papyrus</li></ol><p>From <i>βῠ́βλος</i> (bŭ́blos, “papyrus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papyrus (both in reference to the plant and writings using the paper-like material derived from the plant)</li><li>book</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>Βύβλος</i> (Búblos, “Byblos (ancient city)”) (whence it was imported). However, it has been suggested that the town was named after this word rather than the other way around (which would render the Semitic etymologies given for the town name as folk etymology), and that the word is of Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βύβλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byblos (a port city in Keserwan-Jbeil Governorate, Lebanon)</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤂𐤁𐤋</i> (gbl /⁠gebal⁠/), from Canaanite G-B-L (Gubla).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>close pressed, closely</li></ol><p>From the same source of <i>βυνέω</i> (bunéō, “to stuff, cram”) and <i>βυζόν</i> (buzón, “close, compact”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) dung beetle</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malt for brewing</li></ol><p>Matches Classical Syriac <i>ܒܽܘܢܳܐ</i> (būnā, “barley soaked in water”).</p><p>The origin can be a technical term restricted from the well-known Middle Iranian word for “base, grounds” found as Classical Persian بن (bun) and others, securely reconstructed Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;buHnáh</i>, Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;bʰudʰnás</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the skin stripped off a hide, leather</li><li>the skin of a live animal</li></ol><p>A loanword of unknown, possibly Pre-Greek, origin. Perhaps cognate with Hittite <i>𒆳𒊭𒀸</i> (kur-ša-aš); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of leather</li></ol><p>From <i>βύρσα</i> (búrsa, “skin stripped off”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A plug, a bung</li></ol><p>From <i>βύω</i> (búō, “to stop, to plug”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown kind of bird</li><li>alternative form of <i>βυσσός</i> (bussós)</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of flax</li></ol><p>From <i>βύσσος</i> (bússos, “flax”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flax (<i>Linum usitatissimum</i>)</li><li>linen</li><li>Levant cotton (<i>Gossypium herbaceum</i>)</li><li>silk</li></ol><p>From a Semitic source cognate to Hebrew <i>בּוּץ</i> (būṣ, “byssus”) and Aramaic <i>בּוּצָא</i> (būṣā, “byssus”), from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;būṣ-</i> (“fibrous grass, rush etc.”). Compare also Old Armenian <i>բեհեզ</i> (behez, “byssus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Troezen</i>) spring</li></ol><p>Lewy connects the word with Hebrew <i>ביכורים</i> (bikkūrīm, “first fruits”), which comes from the stem <i>ב־כ־ר</i> (bakār, “to be premature”), suggesting a Semitic origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βώκαρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bocarus, a brook running in the island of Salamis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of clay, of brick</li></ol><p>From <i>βῶλος</i> (bôlos, “lump, clod of earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baris (a type of flat-bottomed freighter used on the Nile in Ancient Egypt)</li><li>farmhouse, country house</li><li>large house, tower</li></ol><p>From Coptic <i>ⲃⲁⲁⲣⲉ</i> (baare), from Demotic Egyptian <i>br</i>, from Egyptian <i>bꜣjr</i> (“transport ship”), <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./7bd73ca7.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="bA" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./1afca51f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="y" alt="glyph" height="35"/><br /><img src="./71f37cec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="r" alt="glyph" height="27"/> <img src="./b207efda.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z1" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./b3c0d527.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="P1" alt="glyph" height="55"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></p><p>Probably from Illyrian, confronting <i>βαυρία</i> (bauría, “house, dwelling”) and <i>βύριον</i> (búrion, “house”). Might be related to Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;bhou̯onā</i> (“dwelling, abode”), <i>&#42;bhunā</i> (“hut”), but the lack of an -r- or even an initial -a- makes this unlikely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>baa (“cry of sheep”)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go, step, move on foot</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to mount (a chariot)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to depart, go away</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to die</li></ol><li>perfect <i>βέβηκα</i> (bébēka): (<i>intransitive</i>) to stand, be somewhere</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to be [<i>with</i> adverb <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li><i>εὖ</i> (eû) <i>βεβηκώς</i> (bebēkṓs) well off</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) to stand on a base</li></ol><li>future <i>βήσω</i> (bḗsō) and aorist <i>ἔβησα</i> (ébēsa): (<i>causative</i>) to make someone dismount</li></ol><p>The present stem is from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷəňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷm̥yéti</i>, from zero-grade of <i>&#42;gʷem-</i> + <i>&#42;-yéti</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Old English <i>cuman</i> (English <i>come</i>), Latin <i>veniō</i>, and Sanskrit <i>गन्ति</i> (ganti).</p><p>The aorist <i>ἔβην</i> (ébēn), as well as the other tense-forms whose stems do not contain <i>ν</i> (n), is from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;égʷēn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷéh₂t</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a step, pace, footfall, gait</li><li>raised place or tribune or podium or pulpit from which men spoke in a public assembly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷā́mə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷéh₂mn̥</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷeh₂-</i> (“to step”) (whence <i>ἔβην</i> (ébēn)). Compare <i>βωμός</i> (bōmós, “altar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooded combe, glen, dale, vale</li><li>kind of drinking cup used in Egypt</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected with <i>βαθύς</i> (bathús, “deep”), as an e-grade root variant, but Furnée doesn't dismiss a Pre-Greek origin and links the noun to the gloss <i>βρῆσσαι</i> (brêssai) found in Hesychius. The same author further compares this word with Basque <i>basa</i> (“precipice”), Occitan <i>bassià</i> (“steep spot among the rocks”) and Catalan <i>basè</i> (“crag, cliff, precipice”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βῆσσα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bessa (a town in Locris, Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>βῆσσα</i> (bêssa, “glen, narrow valley”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beta, the name for the second letter Β (B), β (b) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>second</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤁</i> (b‬ /⁠bēt⁠/). The letter name, beth, comes from Phoenician <i>𐤁𐤕</i> (bt), <i>𐤁𐤉𐤕</i> (byt, “house”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amphora, jar, any vase with handles, especially one containing wine</li><li>unit of measure for land of unknown extension</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) vicus, a small civilian settlement under the Romans</li></ol><p>Probably from Egyptian <i>bꜣkt</i> (“oil flask”). Beekes dismisses any relation with Latin <i>fiscus</i> (“basket”) and <i>βαυκάλιον</i> (baukálion, “jug, pitcher”).</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>vīcus</i> (“street; hamlet”), from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;weikos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyḱ-</i> (“village”). Doublet of <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clod, clump of earth</li><li>ground, soil</li></ol><p>From <i>βῶλος</i> (bôlos, “lump, clod of earth”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Βῶλαξ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Bolax, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lump, clod of earth, turf</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) land, soil</li><li>lump of gold, nugget</li></ol><p>Unknown; a candidate for Pre-Greek origin. Alternatively from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bow-lo-</i>, from an expressive root <i>&#42;bew-</i> (“to swell, bubble; round object”) (compare Latin <i>bulla</i> (“bubble, knob”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;pullaz</i> (“round object”)). Compare also Ancient Greek <i>βολβός</i> (bolbós, “bulb”), <i>βουνός</i> (bounós, “hill, mound; clot”) and <i>βουβών</i> (boubṓn, “swelling; groin”), but according to Beekes these can hardly be related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>γε</i> (ge)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small round net, especially for catching oysters</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) umbilical region</li></ol><p>Technical term, certainly of foreign origin and probably Pre-Greek. Not related to <i>γέντο</i> (génto, “he grasped”). Neumann connects it with Hittite (kank-, “to hang”) but this is most uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) gangrene</li></ol><p>In antiquity it was compared with <i>γράω</i> (gráō, “to devour”) (a theory which <i>Etymonline</i> corroborates), but this is likely incorrect. It is most probably a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>donkey</li></ol><p>Obscure. Mentioned in 2nd century CE by Diogenianus <i>paroemiographus</i> (“proverb writer”). <i>Also see</i> <i>Etymology of</i> γάιδαρος.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cod (<i>Gadus morhua</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée compares the word with <i>γάζας</i> (gázas, “kind of fish”), assuming a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury, treasure</li><li>great riches, a large sum of money</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;ganǰam</i> (“treasure”) spread by the Achaemenid administration.</p><p>The Liddell-Scott-Jones dictionary marks the first α long in this word and all its derivatives, but if the second α is short (as the declension class leads us to expect), then the accentuation proves the first must also be short, as it would take a circumflex (&#42;γᾶζα) if it were long.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Γάζα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaza (a city in the Gaza Strip, Palestine in the Levant; in full, Gaza City)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>עַזָּה</i> (ʿazzā), which at the time (ca. 3rd century BCE) was pronounced something like /ɣɑˈzːɑː/ or /ʁɑˈzːɑː/.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀλλαντία</i> (allantía, “sausages”).</li></ol><p>Unknown. Furnée connects the word with <i>γηθυλλίς</i> (gēthullís, “spring onion”) and <i>ἀγαθίς</i> (agathís, “cake made of sesame”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk</li><li>milky sap</li><li>the Milky Way galaxy</li></ol><p>Possibly inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gláktn̥</i> (“milk”) (compare Latin <i>lact</i>, Old Armenian <i>կաթն</i> (katʻn), Albanian <i>dhallë</i>), earlier <i>&#42;glákt</i> (or, if the Hittite cognate is valid, <i>&#42;gláktr̥</i>), perhaps from a root <i>&#42;glag-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tr̥</i>, but the phonetic irregularities, presence of <i>*a</i>, and Mediterranean distribution of this word have cast doubt on its existence in PIE. Instead, it may have been borrowed from an agricultural wanderword related to or descended from Hittite <i>𒂵𒌨𒋻</i> (galaktar, “soothing substance, nutrient”), itself possibly derived from <i>𒅗𒆷𒀭𒃰𒁺</i> (galank-, “to soothe”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gleǵʰ-</i> (“soft, weak”); cf. Bulgarian <i>гле́зя</i> (glézja, “to pamper”). However, Beekes doubts that there is any connection with the Hittite.</p><p>The variant <i>γλᾰ́γος</i> (glắgos) was contracted from the second oblique stem.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galangal (<i>Alpinia galanga</i>)</li></ol><p>A loanword from Arabic خَلَنْجَان (ḵalanjān), from Persian قولنجان (qulenjân, qulanjân), from Sanskrit <i>कुलञ्जन</i> (kulañjana), perhaps from Chinese 高良薑／高良姜 (<i>gāoliángjiāng</i>), from <i>高涼</i>&nbsp;/ <i>高凉</i> (Gāoliáng) (a prefecture or county in China) + <i>薑</i>&nbsp;/ <i>姜</i> (jiāng, “ginger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rayed trough shell (<i>Mactra corallina</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”), because of the color of its shell.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) (<i>According to Hesychius of Alexandria:</i>) earth</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>Unknown. According to Belardi, the word is from the Mediterranean substrate.</i>"</p><p>Compare Italian <i>galestro</i> (“marl”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) galley (ship propelled primarily by oars)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from <i>γαλέη</i> (galéē, “weasel; kind of small fish”) and/or <i>γαλεός</i> (galeós, “shark”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἐρέβινθοι</i> (erébinthoi, “chickpeas”).</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix <i>-ινθος</i> and the variant without prenasalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedstraw (<i>Galium verum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”), because it was used in place of rennet.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of a Dionysiac cultic society</li></ol><p>Probably from the same Phrygian source of <i>γάλλος</i> (gállos, “eunuch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gallus, a priest of Cybele in Phrygia</li><li>eunuch, castrated man</li></ol><p>In antiquity, the word was believed to be Phrygian. Probably, <i>γάλλαρος</i> (gállaros, “member of a Dionysiac cultic society”) is related. See <i>gallus</i> for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sister-in-law: husband's sister or brother's wife</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;galawos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵl̥h₂ōws</i>. Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>зълъва</i> (zŭlŭva) (Russian <i>золовка</i> (zolovka)), Latin <i>glōs</i>, Phrygian <i>γέλαρος</i> (gélaros) and Old Armenian <i>տալ</i> (tal).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gamma, the name for the third letter Γ (G), γ (g) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤂</i> (g /⁠gīml⁠/). The letter's name is from Phoenician <i>𐤂𐤌𐤋</i> (gml, “camel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriage, wedding, bridelock</li><li>matrimony, the state of being married, wedlock</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵem-</i> (“to marry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>κιβώτιον</i> (kibṓtion, “box, voting urn”).</li></ol><p>Maybe related to <i>γανίς</i> (ganís, “kind of Egyptian vessel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brightness, sheen</li><li>gladness, joy, pride</li><li>freshness, limpidity, transparency (of liquids)</li><li>garden</li><li>alternative form of <i>γλάνος</i> (glános)</li></ol><p>From <i>γάνυμαι</i> (gánumai, “to brighten up, to rejoice”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gh₂-n-u-</i>, probably related to <i>γηθέω</i> (gēthéō, “to rejoice”).</p><p>From Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>גַּן</i> (gan, “garden”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Γάνος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ganus, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to brighten up</li><li>to be glad [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘at’</small>]</li></ol><p>According to Rix, from a nasal-infix present of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;geh₂w-</i>. Related to <i>γαίω</i> (gaíō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>for</li><li>since, because</li></ol><p>From <i>γε</i> (ge) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ρᾰ</i> (ắră).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: iron implement in the nave of a wheel, which pounds the axle.</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garum, a kind of sauce or paste made of brine and small fish</li></ol><p>Unknown, possibly the name of a fish or a different sauce.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) rod, wand</li></ol><p>Unclear. Compare <i>γέρρον</i> (gérrhon, “wicker”) and <i>γάρσανα</i> (gársana, “brushwood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) glossed by Hesychius as <i>φρύγανα</i> (phrúgana, “sticks, firewood”)</li></ol><p>Compare <i>γέρρον</i> (gérrhon, “wicker”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belly of a vase</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>γᾰστήρ</i> (găstḗr, “belly; paunch; womb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>γᾰ́στρᾱ</i> (gắstrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make one's voice heard</li><li>to shout</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Traditionally connected with <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “to perceive, observe”); however, the lengthened <i>-ω-</i> (-ō-) is left unexplained. A newer theory by Hackstein and Vine, supported by De Vaan, derives the word from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ge-gon-s-</i>, a desiderative from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵh₃en-</i> (“to call”), whence also Tocharian A <i>ken-</i> (“to call”) and perhaps Latin <i>gemō</i> (“to sigh, groan”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gehenna</li><li>hell</li></ol><p>From the Biblical Hebrew <i>גֵּיהִנּוֹם</i> (gēhinnṓm, “the valley of Hinnom”), from <i>גַּיְא</i> (gáyʾ, “valley”) and <i>הִנֹּם</i> (hinnṓm, “Hinnom”). The Hinnom Valley was a valley on the southern border of Jerusalem where refuse and the bodies of people denied a proper burial were burned. According to tradition, child sacrifices also took place in this valley. In most usages, this is an allegorical reference to hell.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smile</li><li>cause of laughter</li></ol><p>From <i>γελάω</i> (geláō, “to laugh”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head of garlic</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the cloves in a head of garlic</li></ol><p>Frequently assumed to be a reduplicated formation, with Beekes concluding it to be Pre-Greek, and seemingly related to <i>ᾰ̓́γλῑς</i> (ắglīs, “clove of garlic”) and <i>γᾰγγλῐ́ον</i> (gănglĭ́on).</p><p>Kroonen (2012) suggests an ultimate Akkadian etymology of the word, comparing it to Arabic جَدِيلَة (jadīla, “braid”). He assumes a metathesis from a by-form <i>&#42;γέγλῑς</i> (&#42;géglīs), from which he reconstructs <i>&#42;γέδλῑς</i> (&#42;gédlīs), which he compares to Akkadian <i>𒄀𒀉𒇻</i> (/⁠gidlu⁠/, “string of garlic or onions”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀσφόδελος</i> (asphódelos, “asphodel”), <i>νάρκισσος</i> (nárkissos, “daffodil”).</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, from Pre-Greek and related to <i>σχέλινος</i> (skhélinos, “wild cypress”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive sense</i>) laughable, ludicrous, deridable, derisive</li></ol><p>From <i>γελάω</i> (geláō, “to laugh”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>γέλως</i> (gélōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laughter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) waves</li></ol><li>an occasion for laughter</li><li>dimple</li></ol><p>Related to <i>γελάω</i> (geláō, “laugh”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be full (of)</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gem-</i> (“to take, seize”). Cognate with Umbrian <i>𐌊𐌖𐌌𐌉𐌀𐌚</i> (kumiaf), whence Latin <i>gumia</i> (“glutton, gourmand”), as well as <i>γέντο</i> (génto, “(he, she) grasped”). Other possible, though less certain, comparanda include Latin <i>gemō</i> (“to sigh, groan”) (perhaps indicative of the noises one makes when full after eating).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chin</li><li>beard</li><li>lion's mane</li><li>chaps, jaws</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) teeth (of a saw)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;génewyom</i>, from the root of <i>γένῠς</i> (génŭs, “jaw, mouth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>origin, source</li><li>manner of birth</li><li>creation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;génetis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵénh₁tis</i>. Synchronically, a verbal noun from <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to come into being”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Γένεσις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Genesis. The title of the first book of the Bible and Pentateuch as in the Septuagint translation in Greek.</li></ol><p>Hellenistic Koine from <i>γένεσις</i> (“origin, creation”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>γένετο</i> (géneto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Ancient Greek</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>synonym of <i>γένος</i> (génos)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>descent, lineage</li><li>origin</li><li>offspring, generation</li><li>family (children), race</li></ol><li>coming forth (<i>of the Moon</i>)</li></ol><li><i>Medieval</i> (<i>Byzantine</i>) <i>Greek</i>: γέννα (génna)&nbsp;f (<i>genitive</i> <b>γέννας</b>); <i>first declension</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>childbirth (<i>process of childbearing</i>)</li><li>family</li><li>“γέννα τῆς σελήνης (génna tês selḗnēs)”: moonrise (literally: the birth of the Moon) (<i>of the Moon, expression</i>)</li><li><i>also see</i> τὰ Γέννα (tà Génna)&nbsp;n&nbsp;pl (<i>genitive</i> <b>Γέννων</b>); <i>second declension</i> (6th century&nbsp;CE): Christmas</li></ol></ol><p>A derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i> (“to beget”), though the exact morphological and phonological development is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) mother's brother, uncle from mother's side</li></ol><p>Inflectional form of noun <i>γέννα</i> (génna).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beget, give birth to</li><li>to bring forth, produce, generate</li></ol><p>From <i>γέννα</i> (génna) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō), poetic form of <i>γένος</i> (génos, “offspring”). Causal counterpart of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born, to become”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>γέννησῐς</i> (génnēsĭs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is produced, a product</li><li>That which is born, a child.</li></ol><p>From <i>γεννάω</i> (gennáō, “to generate”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>producing</li><li>a birth</li></ol><p>From <i>γεννᾰ́ω</i> (gennắō, “I beget”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, verbal noun suffix). Compare simple <i>γένεσις</i> (génesis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>race, stock, kin</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>direct descent, as opposed to collateral relationship</li></ol><li>offspring, descendant</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>collective</i>) offspring, posterity</li></ol><li>(<i>in general</i>) race of beings</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>the family as considered over time</i>): clan, house, dynasty, lineage.</li><li>tribe, nation, race, as a subdivision of <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos)</li><li>caste</li><li>breed of animals</li></ol><li>age, generation, time of life</li><li>sex, gender</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>grammar</i>) grammatical gender</li></ol><li>class, sort, kind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>logic</i>) the opposite of <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “species”)</li><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) class</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>taxonomy</i>) genus</li><li>species of plant; crop, produce; material</li></ol><li>element</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;génos</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>genus</i>, Sanskrit <i>जनस्</i> (jánas), Old Armenian <i>ծին</i> (cin), among others; see also <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog excrement</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(defective,&#32;Epic) (he/she) grasped</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) contraction of <i>γένετο</i> (géneto)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gémto</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gém-t</i>, root thematic aorist of <i>&#42;gem-</i> (“to grasp”); cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>жѧти</i> (žęti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jaw</li><li>mouth</li><li>edge of an axe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;génus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵénu-</i>, <i>&#42;ǵénus</i> (“chin, jaw”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>हनु</i> (hánu), Latin <i>gena</i>, Old Armenian <i>ծնաւտ</i> (cnawt, “jaw”), Persian چانه (čâne), Tocharian A <i>śanweṃ</i>, Lithuanian <i>žandas</i>, Welsh <i>gên</i>, and English <i>chin</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a crane, <i>Grus cinerea</i></li><li>a crane (instrument for lifting)</li><li>a type of dance resembling the movements of a crane</li><li>a type of fish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gerh₂-</i>. Cognates include Old Armenian <i>կռունկ</i> (kṙunk), Latin <i>grūs</i>, and Old English <i>cran</i> (English <i>crane</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift of honor</li><li>gift (<i>generally</i>)</li><li>privilege, prerogative</li><li>perquisite</li><li>reward</li></ol><p>Perhaps originally "privilege of age", from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵerh₂-</i> (“to grow old”).</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀐𐀨</i> (ke-ra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weaver</li></ol><p>As the word is very late, a loan is probable. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵers-</i> (“to twist; to plait”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything made of wicker-work</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an oblong shield, covered with ox-hide, such as the Persians used</li><li>wattled screens or booths, used in the Athens marketplace</li><li>the wicker body of a cart</li><li>penthouse</li><li>rod, stake, dart</li></ol></ol><p>Compare Old Armenian <i>ծառ</i> (caṙ), Old Norse <i>kjarr</i> (“copsewood, brush-wood, thicket”). Seems to be a widespread borrowing from a substrate language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point of a fishing rod</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, related to <i>γέρρον</i> (gérrhon, “anything made of wicker-work”). The variations point to a substrate origin of these words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an old man</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the elders</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;géronts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵérh₂onts</i> (“old”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>जर॑त्</i> (járat, “old man”), Old Armenian <i>ծերուն</i> (cerun), and Middle Irish <i>gérat</i> (“champion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dam; dyke</li><li>battlefield</li><li>bridge</li></ol><p>Compare Old Armenian <i>կամուրջ</i> (kamurǰ, “bridge”) and Hattic <i>𒄩𒄠𒈬𒊒𒊀</i> (ḫa-am-mu-ru-waₐ /⁠ḫamuruwa, ḫammuruwa, ḫāmuruwaₐ⁠/, “beam”), with further origin unknown. Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek or other substrate borrowing, perhaps via Anatolian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of onion</li></ol><p>Due to the variations, probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to earth</li></ol><p>From <i>γῆ</i> (gê, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>γήμασθε</i> (gḗmasthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plot of ground</li></ol><p>From <i>γῆ</i> (gê, “land, earth”) +&lrm; <i>πέδον</i> (pédon, “ground”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grapestone, seed of the grape</li><li>pit of the olive</li></ol><p>Reduplicated formation. It is often connected with <i>grānum</i> (“seed, kernel”) and Old High German <i>kerno</i> (“pit, stone”), which is formally improbable. According to Beekes, the word is rather a Pre-Greek loan.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Γῐ́γᾱς</i> (Gĭ́gās, “one of the Gigantes”)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) anything large and powerful</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) mighty; powerful</li></ol><p>From <i>Γίγᾱς</i> (Gígās).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Γίγας)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Gigantes; a Gigas</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly Pre-Greek, or perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gag-</i>, with cognates including Lithuanian <i>gògas</i>. Compare the morphology of Ancient Greek <i>Ἀκράγᾱς</i> (Akrágās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hinge, joint, pivot, gudgeon</li></ol><p>The origin is uncertain. Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin. Martirosyan connects with Old Armenian <i>ծղխնի</i> (cłxni, “door hinge”) and derives both from a Mediterranean substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) small Phoenician flute or fife</li></ol><p>Probably of onomatopoeic origin and thus not related to <i>γῆρυς</i> (gêrus) or <i>γέρανος</i> (géranos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>γίγνετο</i> (gígneto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come into being</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of people</i>) to be born</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to be produced</li><li>(<i>of events</i>) to take place</li></ol><li>(<i>followed by a predicate</i>) to become</li><li>(<i>aorist participle</i>) having ceased to be: former, ex-</li><li>(<i>present participle</i>) something that is due (<i>of payments</i>); regular, normal, usual</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gígnomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵíǵn̥h₁eti</i>, the reduplicated present stem of <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i> (“to beget; to give birth”). Cognate with Latin <i>gignō</i> and <i>nāscor</i>. The active forms were displaced by <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō), causing Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵíǵn̥h₁eti</i> to degenerate into a deponent verb.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offspring of a mare by a mule</li><li>small mule, hinny</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin. It can hardly be from <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic, Koine, and Byzantine form of <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice, exult, take pride in</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;γαϝϳω</i> (&#42;gawjō); ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;geh₂w-</i>; compare <i>γάνυμαι</i> (gánumai) and Latin <i>gaudeo</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) bowl</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>gabata</i> or together with it borrowed from Semitic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>τρύβλιον</i> (trúblion, “cup, bowl”).</li></ol><p>A Semitic origin has been assumed but Beekes doesn't dismiss a derivation from Pre-Greek, comparing the word with <i>γάβενα</i> (gábena) and <i>κάβος</i> (kábos). See also Latin <i>gabata</i> and Byzantine Greek <i>γαβάθα</i> (gabátha).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dewlap</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>present active infinitive of <b>&#42;γαγγαίνω</b>. Hesychius glosses it as <i>τὸ μετὰ γέλωτος προσπαίζειν</i> (tò metà gélōtos prospaízein, “to laugh at, make fun of”).</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gang-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>गञ्ज</i> (gañja) and Old English <i>canc</i> (“insult”). Compare also <i>γογγύζω</i> (gongúzō, “I murmur, grumble”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>oncology</i>) encysted tumour on a tendon or aponeurosis</li></ol><p>Unknown. Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gel-</i> (“round, swollen”), like Latin <i>galla</i> (“oak apple”). However, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin, comparing the word with <i>ᾰ̓́γλῑς</i> (ắglīs, “clove of garlic”), <i>γέλγῑς</i> (gélgīs, “clove of garlic”) and <i>γᾰ́λῐνθοι</i> (gắlĭnthoi, “chickpeas”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) the courtyard of the temple in Jerusalem</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>גִּזְרָה</i> (gizrâ, “separated area”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) treasury (place where treasure is stored safely)</li></ol><p>From <i>γαζοφύλαξ</i> (gazophúlax) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasurer</li></ol><p>From <i>γάζα</i> (gáza, “treasure”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>γηθέω</i> (gēthéō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;geh₂dʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earth-bearing, earth-holding (<i>epithet of Poseidon and sometimes other gods</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>γαῖα</i> (gaîa) +&lrm; <i>ὄχος</i> (ókhos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap of earth, boundary-heap</li></ol><p>From <i>γαῖα</i> (gaîa, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white as milk, milky</li></ol><p>From <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fancy bread made with milk</li></ol><p>From <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantine form of <i>γαλέη</i> (galéē, “weasel”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name given to various animals of the Mustelidae family, like martens, weasels and polecats</li><li>kind of small fish</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gl̥h₁éys</i> (“weasel, mouse”) and cognate with Latin <i>glīs</i> (“dormouse”), Sanskrit <i>गिरि</i> (girí, “mouse”), though the veracity and existence of the Sanskrit comparandum has been contested. According to Beekes, the word originally indicated the skin and later the animal itself.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name given to various animals of the Mustelidae family, like martens, weasels and polecats</li><li>kind of small fish</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gl̥h₁éys</i> (“weasel, mouse”) and cognate with Latin <i>glīs</i> (“dormouse”), Sanskrit <i>गिरि</i> (girí, “mouse”), though the veracity and existence of the Sanskrit comparandum has been contested. According to Beekes, the word originally indicated the skin and later the animal itself.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of wind or sea</i>) calm; stillness &lsqb;Homer&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>serenity; tranquility; calmness</li></ol><li>galena; lead sulphide</li><li>a type of antidote</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵelh₂-</i>, the same root of <i>γελᾰ́ω</i> (gelắō, “to laugh”). This word must originally have meant "cheerfulness"; compare also <i>γελεῖν</i> (geleîn, “to shine, flourish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>milk-white</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) milky</li></ol><p>From <i>γάλα</i> (gála) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>milk-fed</li></ol><p>From <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Milky Way galaxy (with implied <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle, sphere”))</li><li>tailor's chalk (with implied <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”))</li><li>dogfish</li></ol><p>From <i>γᾰ́λᾰ</i> (gắlă, “milk”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās). Originally an adjective.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trap for weasels</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) big cage for animals</li><li>kind of winepress</li></ol><p>From <i>γαλέη</i> (galéē, “weasel”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγρᾱ</i> (ágrā, “hunting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small shark, dogfish</li></ol><p>Possibly named after the weasel, <i>γαλέη</i> (galéē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially of the sea</i>) calm</li></ol><p>Related to <i>γᾰλήνη</i> (gălḗnē)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mediterranean figwort (<i>Scrophularia peregrina</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>χορδαψός</i> (khordapsós), <i>σκινδαψός</i> (skindapsós) and <i>λύκαψος</i> (lúkapsos), so it is probably a Pre-Greek word. Compare <i>Γαληψός</i> (Galēpsós), considered Pre-Greek for the same reason. <i>γαληψός</i> (galēpsós) may be derived from the place name.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Γαληψός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Galepsus</li></ol><p>Unknown. Appears to be Pre-Greek. Compare <i>γαληψός</i> (galēpsós).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband</li></ol><p>From <i>γαμέω</i> (gaméō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to marry (a wife)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to give oneself in marriage; to marry (a man)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to get a spouse for; to betroth (generally of parents for their children)</li><li>to take (a woman) as a lover</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to have sex with</li></ol><p>From <i>γᾰ́μος</i> (gắmos, “marriage; matrimony”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix) (though Beekes considers the noun a back-formation from the verb rather than the verb being derived from the noun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nuptials (the various rituals that take place on a wedding day)</li><li>gifts given at the wedding reception</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedding</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bridal; nuptial (relating to a wedding)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedding cake</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>&#42;γαμηλός</i> (&#42;gamēlós, from <i>γᾰμέω</i> (găméō, “to marry”)) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos), but the origin of the <i>-ηλ-</i> (-ēl-) segment is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give in marriage</li></ol><p>From <i>γάμος</i> (gámos, “marriage, matrimony”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>γαμίζω</i> (gamízō)</li></ol><p>From <i>γάμος</i> (gámos, “marriage, matrimony”) +&lrm; <i>-σκω</i> (-skō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son-in-law</li><li>brother-in-law</li><li>father-in-law</li><li>bridegroom</li><li>in general, any in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵem-</i> (“to marry”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>जामातृ</i> (jā́mātṛ), Avestan <i>𐬰𐬁𐬨𐬁𐬙𐬀𐬭</i> (zāmātar).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife</li></ol><p>From <i>γαμέω</i> (gaméō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedding party</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the official announcement of a wedding in the phratry</li><li>the presentation of the bride to the phratry</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for marriage, marital</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) of marriageable age</li></ol><p><i>γᾰ́μος</i> (gắmos, “a wedding”; “marriage”, “wedlock”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “of or pertaining to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) jaws of animals</li><li>bill or beak of birds</li></ol><p>Generally connected with <i>γόμφος</i> (gómphos, “peg, nail”), but the α-vocalism is problematic. Neither an influence from <i>γαμψός</i> (gampsós, “curved, crooked”) or <i>γναμπτήρ</i> (gnamptḗr, “jaw”) seems to be a sufficient explanation. Pedersen pointed to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gǫba</i> (“lip, mouth”). According to Beekes, the word may be Pre-Greek given the suffix <i>-ηλ-</i>, which can be found also in <i>τράχηλος</i> (trákhēlos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curved, crooked</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word may well be from Pre-Greek and related to <i>γνάμπτω</i> (gnámptō, “to bend”) and <i>κάμπτω</i> (kámptō, “to curve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of birds of prey</i>) with crooked talons</li></ol><p>From <i>γᾰμψός</i> (gămpsós, “crooked”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνῠξ</i> (ónŭx, “nail; talon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>γεωμόρος</i> (geōmóros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be bright, shine</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of metals</i>) to glitter, gleam</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be happy, rejoice</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to clean</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to glorify</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>γάνος</i> (gános, “brightness, sheen”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō, verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guinea fowl (<i>Numida meleagris</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Γαράμαντες</i> (Garámantes), because they exported the guinea fowl to the Greeks.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paste made of garum and oil</li></ol><p>From <i>γάρος</i> (gáros, “fish sauce”) +&lrm; <i>ἔλαιον</i> (élaion, “oil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to tickle, titillate</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to feel tickling or irritation</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic formation with reduplication.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) uvula</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) swollen uvula</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) trachea</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic formation with intensive reduplication. Related to <i>γᾰργᾰρῐ́ζω</i> (gărgărĭ́zō, “to gargle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown daisy (<i>Glebionis coronaria</i>)</li></ol><p>From an unattested Etruscan <i>&#42;𐌙𐌀𐌓𐌖𐌋𐌄</i> (&#42;χarule), according to Pittau (2013). It is an <i>hapax</i> recorded by Pedanius Dioscorides in volume IV of his work <i>De materia medica</i>. First attested in <i>c.</i> 1st century. Given its dubious status, the term has no secure etymology in Etruscan itself and is therefore thought to be part of a Mediterranean substrate.</p><p>Possibly akin to Sardinian <i>galileu</i> (“pollen; royal jelly”) and maybe also <i>ghirielle</i> (“wild chrysanthemum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>γᾰστέρᾰ</i> (găstéră), <i>γᾰστέρῐ</i> (găstérĭ), and <i>γᾰστέρε</i> (găstére)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paunch, belly, hollow, wide part</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>belly (for craving food); gluttony</li><li>sausage, haggis</li></ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) womb</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a dissimilation of an unattested earlier <i>&#42;γρᾰστήρ</i> (&#42;grăstḗr, “devourer”), from <i>γρᾰ́ω</i> (grắō, “to gnaw, eat”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, agent noun suffix). If so, Sanskrit <i>ग्रस्तर्</i> (grastar, “(astronomy) eclipser (originally "devourer")”) would be an exact cognate; for a semantic parallel to the Sanskrit term, compare Chinese <i>蝕</i>&nbsp;/ <i>蚀</i> (shí, “to corrode, eclipse”), which is derived from 食 (<i>shí</i>, “to eat”).</p><p>However, Beekes is skeptical of the semantics (it is somewhat of a stretch to consider the belly as an "eater"), and tentatively prefers a derivation from <i>&#42;gnt-tēr</i>, with <i>*-tt-</i> &gt; <i>-st-</i>, comparing <i>γέντα</i> (génta, “intestines”) (which itself is of unclear origin).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gluttonous, voracious, ravenous</li></ol><p>From <i>γᾰστήρ</i> (găstḗr, “belly”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́ργος</i> (mắrgos, “greedy, gluttonous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) calf of the leg</li></ol><p>From <i>γαστήρ</i> (gastḗr, “paunch, belly”) +&lrm; <i>κνήμη</i> (knḗmē, “leg”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel for cooking sausages</li></ol><p>From <i>γαστήρ</i> (gastḗr, “belly, womb”) +&lrm; <i>ὀπτάω</i> (optáō, “to roast, broil”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk pail</li><li>water bucket</li><li>any round vessel, beehive</li></ol><p>A connection with <i>γύαλον</i> (gúalon), <i>γωλεός</i> (gōleós), Old Norse <i>kjóll</i> (“ship”) has been considered. But this word can be considered, as <i>γαῦλος</i> (gaûlos) denoting a Phoenician merchant-ship, as being via Phoenician or a near language from Akkadian <i>𒄖𒌌𒇻</i> (gullu, “a container”), <i>𒄖𒌌𒆷𒌅</i> (gullatu, “ewer”), a vinicultural word of unascertainable non-Semitic origin, being via its variant <i>𒄣𒇷𒌋</i> (qulliu, “a bowl”) the source of Biblical Hebrew <i>גֻלָּה</i> (gullāh), Ugaritic <i>𐎂𐎍</i> (gl), Aramaic <i>קוּלְתָא</i> (qulləṯā), Arabic قُلَّة (qulla), جُلَّة (julla), Classical Syriac <i>ܩܽܘܠܬܳܐ</i> (qulləṯā), which passed into Latin <i>culullus</i> (“wine jug”) and into Ancient Greek <i>κόλλαθον</i> (kóllathon, “a unit of liquid measure used in Syria”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, bent outwards</li></ol><p>The word shares the formation with <i>βλαισός</i> (blaisós, “crooked”) and <i>λοξός</i> (loxós, “oblique”). The adjective has been compared with <i>γαυλός</i> (gaulós), <i>γύρος</i> (gúros) and <i>γύαλον</i> (gúalon) without success. According to Beekes, the word is from Pre-Greek instead.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Syracuse</i>) earthworm</li></ol><p>From <i>γᾶ</i> (gâ, “earth, land”) +&lrm; <i>φαγεῖν</i> (phageîn, “to eat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) land, country</li><li>earth (material)</li><li>earth (element)</li><li>the Earth (see Γαῖα (Gaîa))</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>γῆ</i> (gê, “earth”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Γαῖα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Gaia or Gaea, the Earth personified as a goddess</li></ol><p>From <i>γαῖα</i> (gaîa, “earth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gaesum, a Gaulish javelin</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>gaesum</i>, itself from Gaulish <i>&#42;gaisos</i>, from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;gaisos</i> (“spear”). Compare also <i>χαῖος</i> (khaîos, “shepherd's staff”), Sanskrit <i>हेषस्</i> (héṣas, “missile”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoenician merchant vessel</li></ol><p>Probably from Phoenician, etymologically identical to the nomen vasis <i>γαυλός</i> (gaulós).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Γαῦλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gaulos; Gozo, Malta</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conceited, haughty, arrogant</li><li>proud, gallant, exulting</li><li>splendid, elegant, magnific (of athletes or ephebes)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Usually connected with <i>γηθέω</i> (gēthéō, “to rejoice”), <i>γάνυμαι</i> (gánumai, “to be glad”) and Middle Irish <i>guaire</i> (“noble”). Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;geh₂w-</i> (“to rejoice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>often translatable with italics or stress</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>limiting</i>) at least, at any rate, only</li><li>(<i>intensifying</i>) in fact</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gó ~ &#42;ge</i> (emphatic, delimiting particle), which is sometimes unified with, but is formally impossible to derive from, the similar particle <i>&#42;gʰo ~ &#42;gʰe</i>. Possible cognates include Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;-k</i> (pronominal accusative marker, see <i>&#42;mek</i>, <i>&#42;þek</i>, <i>&#42;sek</i>) and Tocharian B <i>-k</i> (emphatic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be born</li><li>to beget, bring into being</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵn̥h₁-yé-tor</i>. Compare <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai), Latin <i>gigno</i>, <i>gnatus</i> and Sanskrit <i>जनति</i> (jánati).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbour</li></ol><p>Beekes does not propose an etymology, and leaves its origin open, though notes that the seemingly-old ablaut in the suffix <i>-των</i> (-tōn) suggests ultimate Indo-European origin. In either case, not related to <i>γεῖσον</i> (geîson, “projecting part of a roof, cornice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loud-sounding</li><li>shrill</li><li>loud of voice</li></ol><p>From γεγωνώς, the participle of γέγωνα</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh</li><li>to laugh at</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to deride</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵelh₂-</i> (“to laugh”) (which Beekes incorrectly reconstructs as <i>&#42;gelh₂-</i>). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>ծաղր</i> (całr, “laughter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as <i>ψυχρόν</i> (psukhrón), so: cold, chill, frost</li></ol><p>Unknown. Compare Latin <i>gelidus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>laughable, ridiculous</li></ol><p>From <i>γελάω</i> (geláō, “I laugh”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós). The seemingly additional sigma is in fact part of the verb stem and surfaces before consonants. Attested as early as in Homer.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>γελοῖος</i> (geloîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>γελοῖος</i> (geloîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive sense</i>) laughable, ludicrous, deridable, derisive</li></ol><p>From <i>γελάω</i> (geláō, “to laugh”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀτυχές</i> (atukhés, “unfortunate”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of an "-σ-" after a resonant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>γελάω</i> (geláō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵelh₂-</i> (“to laugh”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to one's birthday</li><li>of or pertaining to one's race or family</li><li>giving birth, generative</li><li>(<i>substantive, feminine</i>) a birthday, birthdate</li><li>(<i>substantive, neuter plural</i>) a birthday party or celebration</li></ol><p>Probably derived from <i>γένεθλον</i> (génethlon) or <i>γενέθλη</i> (genéthlē, “race, stock, family, birthplace”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i> (“to give birth”) + <i>&#42;-dʰlom</i> (“instrumental suffix”). See also <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthday</li></ol><p>Neuter plural of <i>γενέσιος</i> (genésios, “concerning a birthday”) from <i>γένεσις</i> (génesis, “birth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the hour of birth</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>begetter, ancestor, father</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born”) +&lrm; <i>-τᾱς</i> (-tās, <i>agentive suffix</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>begetter, ancestor</li><li>father</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) parents</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) author</li><li>the begotten, son</li><li>(attributive,&#32;with <b><i>θεοί</i></b>) familial, of one's race</li><li>(<i>attributive</i>) produced</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, <i>agentive suffix</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>γενέτης</i> (genétēs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;génetōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵénh₁tōr</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i> (“to produce, to give birth”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tōr</i> (<i>agent noun</i>), equivalent to <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, masculine agent noun suffix). Compare Latin <i>genitor</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birth</li><li>race, descent</li><li>generation</li><li>offspring</li></ol><p>Formally it would be from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;genehā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵenh₁eseh₂</i>, feminine derivation from <i>&#42;ǵénh₁os</i> (whence <i>γένος</i> (génos)), from <i>&#42;ǵenh₁-</i>. Compare <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos) - <i>ἰδέα</i> (idéa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>γενεᾱ́</i> (geneā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trace ancestry, make a pedigree</li></ol><p>From <i>γενεᾱλόγος</i> (geneālógos, “genealogist”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genealogy</li></ol><p>From <i>γενεᾱλογέω</i> (geneālogéō, “to trace descent”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>genealogical</li></ol><p>From <i>γενεᾱλόγος</i> (geneālógos, “genealogist”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genealogist</li></ol><p>From <i>γενεᾱ́</i> (geneā́) +&lrm; <i>-λόγος</i> (-lógos, “-logist”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or pertaining to a birthday.</li></ol><p><i>γενέθλια</i> (genéthlia) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beard</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cheeks</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) jaw of a horse</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) bandage for the chin</li></ol><p>From <i>γένειον</i> (géneion, “chin”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the genitive case &ndash; ellipsis of the phrase <i>γενῐκὴ</i> <i>πτῶσῐς</i> (ptôsĭs)&#58; literally “inflection expressing a kind”</li></ol><p>See <i>γενῐκός</i> (genĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to the <i>γένος</i> (génos), racial</li><li>principal, typical</li><li>consisting of families</li><li>sexual</li><li>in kind</li></ol><p>From <i>γένος</i> (génos, “race, stock, kin”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>γεννήτωρ</i> (gennḗtōr, “parent, father; forefather, ancestor, progenitor; begetter, creator, generator”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beget, give birth to</li><li>to bring forth, produce, generate</li></ol><p>From <i>γέννα</i> (génna) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō), poetic form of <i>γένος</i> (génos, “offspring”). Causal counterpart of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born, to become”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>γεννήτειρᾰ</i> (gennḗteiră)</li></ol><p>From <i>γεννάω</i> (gennáō, “to beget”) +&lrm; <i>-τρια</i> (-tria, feminine agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parent; genitor; father</li><li>progenitor; procreator; forefather; ancestor</li><li>begetter; creator; generator; producer; maker</li></ol><p>From <i>γεννᾰ́ω</i> (gennắō, “to beget, give birth to; produce, generate”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, masculine agent noun suffix) from <i>γέννα</i> (génna) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō), poetic form of <i>γένος</i> (génos, “offspring”). Doublet of <i>γενέτωρ</i> (genétōr).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Γεννήτωρ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) The Creator, God, The Father</li></ol><p>From <i>γεννᾰ́ω</i> (gennắō, “to beget, give birth to; produce, generate”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, masculine agent noun suffix) from <i>γέννα</i> (génna) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō), poetic form of <i>γένος</i> (génos, “offspring”). Doublet of <i>γενέτωρ</i> (genétōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nobility (of mind)</li></ol><p>From <i>γενναῖος</i> (gennaîos) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble, high-born</li><li>honourable</li></ol><p>From <i>γέννα</i> (génna, “descent, birth, origin”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios). Compare <i>ἀγενής</i> (agenḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>gentian (plant of the genus <i>Gentiana</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Pedanius Dioscorides, the plant is named after the Illyrian king Gentius, which has been associated with the fact that the plant was prominent in the Alps.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cranesbill (<i>Geranium tuberosum</i>)</li><li>storksbill (<i>Erodium malacoides</i>)</li><li>kind of whitening substance used in alchemy</li></ol><p>From <i>γέρανος</i> (géranos, “crane”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix). The reference to cranes in the plant names is due to the elongated fruits of these plants, which have a characteristic shape reminiscent of the elongated bill of a bird such as a crane (see picture). Compare English <i>storksbill</i>, Dutch <i>reigersbek</i>, <i>ooievaarsbek</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>γεραιός</i> (geraiós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵerh₂-yó-s</i> (“old”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of reverend bearing, majestic, honorable</li></ol><p>From unattested *γέραρ, parallel form of <i>γέρας</i>&nbsp;n (géras, “gift, reward”). Morphologically, stem γερ- + <i>-αρός</i> (-arós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of olives</i>) ripened on a tree</li></ol><p>Semitic origin seems possible: Hemmerdinger connects the word with Hebrew <i>גַּרְגְּרִים</i> (garg'rīm, “ripe olives”). Therefore, the word is rather not related to <i>γέρων</i> (gérōn, “old”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hospital for the elders, old people's home, nursing home</li></ol><p>From <i>γέρων</i> (gérōn, “old man”) +&lrm; <i>κομέω</i> (koméō, “to take care of”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The council of elders, the Senate (especially at Sparta)</li><li>A sacred college</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>γέρων</i> (gérōn, “old man”). Related to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀐𐀫𐀯𐀊</i> (ke-ro-si-ja)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mantlet, a portable screen made of wicker used in siegeworks</li></ol><p>From <i>γέρρον</i> (gérrhon, “wicker-work”) +&lrm; <i>χελώνη</i> (khelṓnē, “tortoise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of fish</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup for tasting wine</li></ol><p>From <i>γεύω</i> (geúō, “to taste”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geographer</li></ol><p>From <i>γεω-</i> (geō-, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-γρᾰ́φος</i> (-grắphos, “writer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geography</li><li>geographical work, map</li></ol><p>From <i>γεωγράφος</i> (geōgráphos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā) and <i>γεω-</i> (geō-) +&lrm; <i>-γρᾰφῐ́ᾱ</i> (-grăphĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land-dividing</li><li>mensuration</li></ol><p>From <i>γεω-</i> (geō-, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>δαίω</i> (daíō, “to divide, share”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surveyor</li><li>geometer</li></ol><p>From <i>γεω-</i> (geō-, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>μετρέω</i> (metréō, “to measure”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure the earth, to practise or profess geometry</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to measure</li></ol><p>From <i>γεωμέτρης</i> (geōmétrēs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) geometry</li><li>land survey</li><li>land tax</li></ol><p><i>γεω-</i> (geō-, “land”) +&lrm; <i>-μετρίᾱ</i> (-metríā, “measurement”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landowner; one who owns a portion of land</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) geomori: a class of oligarchic landowners descending from the first Corinthian settlers who in 733 <small>BCE</small>, according to the chronology of Thucydides, had founded Syracuse</li></ol><p>From <i>γεω-</i> (geō-, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>μέρος</i> (méros, “part, share, portion”), whence the latter comes from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mer-</i> (“to assign, allot”). See also Ancient Greek <i>μείρομαι</i> (meíromai), Latin <i>mereō</i>, <i>merx</i> and Hittite <i>𒈥𒀝𒍣</i> (mar-ak-zi /⁠markzi⁠/, “to divide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tillage</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) farms; tilled land</li></ol><p>From <i>γεωργός</i> (geōrgós) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farms; tilled land</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>γεωργῐ́ᾱ</i> (geōrgĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tilling the ground</li><li>fertilizing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmer</li></ol><p>From <i>γεω-</i> (geō-, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give to taste</li><li>to feed</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to taste</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to eat</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to try, experience, examine</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to partake, enjoy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵews-</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>ċēosan</i> (English <i>choose</i>), Latin <i>gustō</i>,, Sanskrit <i>जुषते</i> (juṣate).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) projecting part of the roof, cornice</li><li>coping of a wall</li><li>hem or border of a garment</li></ol><p>According to Stephanus of Byzantium, a Carian word related to <i>γίσσα</i> (gíssa, “stone”). Furnée compares Georgian <i>ხრეში</i> (xreši, “gravel”) and <i>κίσηρις</i> (kísēris, “pumice stone”). In any case, the word is a loan, either from an Anatolian language or from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taste, smack</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>food</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>γεύω</i> (geúō, “to taste”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sense of taste, tasting</li><li>the action of tasting</li><li>food</li><li>taste, flavor</li><li>back of the tongue</li></ol><p><i>γεύω</i> (geúō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἄγριος μῦς</i> (ágrios mûs, literally “field mouse”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word shows typical Pre-Greek reduplication and prenasalization, for which <i>γίγγλυμος</i> (gínglumos) and <i>γάγγαμον</i> (gángamon) may be compared.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>γηγενεῖς</i> (gēgeneîs)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born of the earth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a person</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>historical</i>) primeval (relating to early man)</li><li>native; indigenous</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) rustic; rural</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) stupid</li></ol><li>(<i>of objects</i>) made from earth</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) born of Gaia (especially of titans and giants)</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) from the body, by the will of the soul</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mortal</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born of the earth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a person</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>historical</i>) primeval (relating to early man)</li><li>native; indigenous</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) rustic; rural</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) stupid</li></ol><li>(<i>of objects</i>) made from earth</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) born of Gaia (especially of titans and giants)</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) from the body, by the will of the soul</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mortal</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the Gigantes; the Giants</li><li>(Judaism,&#32;Christianity) the spirits of the dead</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born of the earth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a person</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>historical</i>) primeval (relating to early man)</li><li>native; indigenous</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) rustic; rural</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) stupid</li></ol><li>(<i>of objects</i>) made from earth</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) born of Gaia (especially of titans and giants)</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) from the body, by the will of the soul</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mortal</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rejoice, be glad</li><li>to exult, triumph</li></ol><p>Because of Latin <i>gaudeō</i> (“to rejoice”), a pre-form <i>&#42;γαϝ-εθ-έω</i> (&#42;gaw-eth-éō) was reconstructed, but the contraction then would have to have been very early and have spread even to the perfect. Recently, the word has been compared with Tocharian A <i>kātk-</i> (“to be glad”), which has yielded the Proto-Indo-European reconstruction <i>&#42;geh₂dʰ-</i> (“to rejoice”) for Greek. The same root is found in <i>γαίω</i> (gaíō, “to rejoice”), <i>γάνυμαι</i> (gánumai, “to be glad”), <i>γαῦρος</i> (gaûros, “conceited, arrogant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joyful, glad [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘at’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>γηθέω</i> (gēthéō, “to rejoice, exult”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow old</li></ol><p>From <i>γῆρας</i> (gêras, “old age”) +&lrm; <i>-σκω</i> (-skō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrot (<i>Daucus gingidium</i>)</li><li>dittander (<i>Lepidium latifolium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>γῐγγῐ́ς</i> (gĭngĭ́s, “kind of turnip”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of turnip</li></ol><p>Solmsen assumed an earlier form <i>&#42;γεγγίς</i> (&#42;gengís), with assimilation, connecting the word with <i>γογγύλος</i> (gongúlos, “round”). However, according to Beekes, it is rather a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the Phoenician flute</li></ol><p>From <i>γίγγρος</i> (gíngros, “Phoenician flute”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be aware of; to perceive, observe, know, learn</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to know, understand</li><li>to distinguish, discern</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to be aware of</li><li>(<i>followed by relative clauses</i>) to perceive</li></ol><li>(<i>in prose</i>) to observe, form a judgment, judge, determine, think</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(passive voice,&#32;of persons) to be judged guilty</li><li>(<i>perfect passive with active sense</i>)</li></ol><li>to know carnally, have sex with</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gignṓskō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵn̥h₃sḱéti</i>.</p><p>Cognates include English <i>know</i>, Latin <i>gnōscō</i>, Albanian <i>njoh</i>, Old Armenian <i>ծան-</i> (can-, “to know”), Sanskrit <i>जा॒नाति॑</i> (jānā́ti, “to know”), and Old Persian <i>𐎧𐏁𐎴𐎿𐏃𐎡𐎹</i> (x-š-n-s-h-i-y /⁠xšnāsāhiy⁠/, “you shall know”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἑτερόφθαλμος</i> (heteróphthalmos, “one-eyed”).</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic, Koine, and Aeolic form of <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μύξα</i> (múxa, “mucus, slime”).</li></ol><p>Unknown. The comparisons with Lithuanian <i>glėmės</i> (“slime”), glẽmės (“slime”) and Albanian <i>ngjomë</i> (“humid, fresh”) are very doubtful. According to Beekes, the word may be Pre-Greek. Compare also <i>λήμη</i> (lḗmē, “sleep, rheum that collects in the corner of the eyes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyena</li></ol><p>Unknown. The word may be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) eyeball</li><li>doll, plaything</li><li>socket of a joint</li><li>honeycomb</li></ol><p>Has been connected with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klainiz</i> (“shining”), but Beekes finds this comparison uncertain. It has also been compared with <i>γαλήνη</i> (galḗnē, “calm weather”) and <i>γλαινοί</i> (glainoí, “ornaments of head-bands”). If this hypothesis be true, then the word would be of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) pennyroyal (<i>Mentha pulegium</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, since the word has no etymology, and since the stem formation is strange, it is probably a Pre-Greek word. Nonetheless a Proto-Hellenic reconstruction <i>&#42;gʷlā́kʰōn</i> is possible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glue</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gley-</i> (“to stick; to spread, to smear”). Cognate with <i>γλίνη</i> (glínē), <i>γλοιός</i> (gloiós), Latin <i>gluten</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klajjaz</i> and Lithuanian <i>glitùs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any viscous substance, slime, ooze</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gley-</i> (“to stick; to spread, to smear”). Cognate with <i>γλία</i> (glía), <i>γλοιός</i> (gloiós), Latin <i>gluten</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;klajjaz</i> and Lithuanian <i>glitùs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sticky; claggy; adhesive</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) sticking close; importunate</li><li>clinging; clingy</li><li>penurious; niggardly; stingy</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) mean; shabby</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gley-</i> (“to glue; to paste; to stick together”), the same root of <i>γλῑ́νη</i> (glī́nē, “any viscous substance”), <i>γλῑ́ᾱ</i> (glī́ā, “glue”), <i>γλοιός</i> (gloiós, “any glutinous substance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Crete</i>) common peony (<i>Paeonia officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably a local name, hence Pre-Greek. Ending like <i>ἀπήνη</i> (apḗnē), <i>λαμπήνη</i> (lampḗnē) and <i>ὑπήνη</i> (hupḗnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be milky</li><li>to be juicy</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>γλάγος</i> (glágos), a poetic form of <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chisel, whether for wood or stonework</li></ol><p>Unknown. The formation is semantically related to <i>γραφίς</i> (graphís), <i>γλυφίς</i> (gluphís) and <i>κοπίς</i> (kopís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gleaming, bright</li><li>blue-green or blue-gray</li><li>(<i>of an eye color</i>) light blue or gray</li></ol><p>Uncertain origin. Barber reconstructs Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gleh₂w-ko-</i>, noting that the root only appears in Greek (Homer, Aeschylus), but Beekes finds an Indo-European origin unlikely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of Athena</i>) bright-eyed</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>γλαυκός</i> (glaukós, “gleaming; grayish”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, feminine adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) hollow, hollowed (<i>epithet of caves and ships</i>)</li><li>polished</li><li>neat, delicate; subtle, exact, refined, elegant</li></ol><p>From <i>γλάφω</i> (gláphō, “scrape, dig up, hollow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bluish grey</li></ol><p>From <i>γλαυκός</i> (glaukós, “light blue, grey”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horned poppy (<i>Glaucium flavum</i>)</li><li>common goldeneye (<i>Bucephala clangula</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>γλαυκός</i> (glaukós, “blue-green, blue-grey”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cataract (opacity of the lens in the eye)</li></ol><p>From <i>γλαυκόω</i> (glaukóō, “to color blue-grey”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>γλαῦξ</i> (glaûx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owl (<i>Athene noctua</i>)</li><li>the Athenian coins, from the owls depicted thereon (usually in plural)</li><li>γλ. θαλαττία, an unknown species of birds</li><li>a form of dance</li><li>wart cress, i.e. <i>Coronopus procumbens</i></li></ol><p>In ancient times considered to derive from <i>γλαυκός</i> (glaukós, “bright”) in reference to their gleaming eyes; possibly pre-Greek according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweet new wine, must</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>grape juice</li></ol><li>sweetness</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dléukus</i>. Related to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀆𐀩𐀄𐀒</i> (de-re-u-ko /⁠dleukos⁠/) and Ancient Greek <i>γλυκύς</i> (glukús, “sweet”). Beekes suggests that the full-grade of this term is a later innovation, though he notes that the term <i>ἀγλευκής</i> (agleukḗs) appears to be archaic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>γλίᾱ</i> (glíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>γλίᾱ</i> (glíā)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any glutinous substance; gum</li><li>mud, mire; sludge</li><li>oil and dirt scraped off with the strigil</li><li>oily sediment in baths</li><li>oil used as lube for machines</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slippery; knavish</li></ol><p>Two theories:</p><p>All these words derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gley-</i> (“to glue, paste, stick together”). See also <i>γλίνη</i> (glínē, “any viscous substance”), <i>γλία</i> (glía, “glue”), <i>γλίσχρος</i> (glískhros, “sticky, penurious”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any glutinous substance; gum</li><li>mud, mire; sludge</li><li>oil and dirt scraped off with the strigil</li><li>oily sediment in baths</li><li>oil used as lube for machines</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slippery; knavish</li></ol><p>Two theories:</p><p>All these words derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gley-</i> (“to glue, paste, stick together”). See also <i>γλίνη</i> (glínē, “any viscous substance”), <i>γλία</i> (glía, “glue”), <i>γλίσχρος</i> (glískhros, “sticky, penurious”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold</li></ol><p>Considered by Frisk a loan from Phrygian, likely not Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) rump, buttock</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a horse</i>) a haunch (contrasted with <i>ἰσχίον</i> (iskhíon, “hip-joint”))</li></ol></ol><p>Compared with Slovene <i>glûta</i> (“lump, swelling”); further with Old English <i>clūd</i> (“rock, boulder”), which is semantically less evident. Without <i>t-</i>suffix, there is Sanskrit <i>ग्लौ</i> (gláu, “round lump, wen-like excrescence”). The Indo-European material is not very convincing. Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medullary tubercles near the pineal gland of the brain</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) greater trochanter</li></ol><p>From <i>γλουτός</i> (gloutós, “rump, buttock”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) comparative degree of <i>γλῠκῠ́ς</i> (glŭkŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>γλυκύς</i> (glukús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweetness of mind</li><li>kind disposition, kindliness, benevolence</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῠκῠ́θῡμος</i> (glŭkŭ́thūmos, “sweet-minded”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>γλῠκῠ́ς</i> (glŭkŭ́s, “sweet”) + <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male peony (<i>Paeonia mascula</i>)</li><li>common peony (<i>Paeonia officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>γλυκύς</i> (glukús, “sweet”) +&lrm; <i>σίδη</i> (sídē, “pomegranate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bittersweet</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dléwkus</td></tr></table></p><p>From <i>γλυκύς</i> (glukús, “sweet”) +&lrm; <i>πικρός</i> (pikrós, “bitter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>licorice (<i>Glycyrrhiza glabra</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>γλυκύς</i> (glukús, “sweet”) + <i>ῥίζα</i> (rhíza, “root”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet (taste)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dlukús</i>, <i>&#42;dléukus</i>. On the basis of Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀆𐀩𐀄𐀒</i> (de-re-u-ko /⁠dleukos⁠/) and Latin <i>dulcis</i>, Beekes suggests that a possible proto-form with initial <i>&#42;dl-</i> may be reconstructed. Old Armenian <i>քաղցր</i> (kʻałcʻr) has also been connected, albeit tentatively. These cognates may suggest at an inheritance from a Proto-Indo-European form of the shape <i>&#42;dl̥kus</i>. De Vaan suggests that the Latin and Greek terms are difficult to formally connect, and are likely both borrowings from the same substrate source. Likewise, Mallory and Adams argue that there is little justification for a Proto-Indo-European reconstruction as the only cognates are found in languages with an extensive history of contact.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a sweet tone (said of musical instruments)</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῠκῠ́ς</i> (glŭkŭ́s, “sweet”) +&lrm; <i>φθόγγος</i> (phthóngos, “sound, voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of wood or stone</i>) fit for carving</li><li>carved, engraved</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῠ́φω</i> (glŭ́phō, “to carve, engrave”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carving, carved work</li><li>hole cut</li></ol><p>From <i>γλύφω</i> (glúphō, “to carve”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>notched end of the arrow</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the arrow itself</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) notches in the arrowhead</li><li>penknife</li><li>chisel</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) capitals of columns</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῠ́φω</i> (glŭ́phō, “to carve, engrave”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) mouth of the windpipe; glottis</li><li>(<i>music</i>) mouthpiece of a wind instrument, in which the reed was inserted</li><li>wryneck; corncrake; landrail</li><li>shoelace; latchet</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;medicine) inflammation of the tongue</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῶσσᾰ</i> (glôssă, “tongue”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, feminine noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>case to keep the reeds or tongues of musical instruments</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”) +&lrm; <i>κομέω</i> (koméō, “to take care of”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of precious stone resembling a human tongue</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”) +&lrm; <i>πέτρᾱ</i> (pétrā, “stone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(reed) mouthpiece-making (reed mouthpieces, especially for wind instruments)</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῶσσᾰ</i> (glôssă, “mouthpiece”) +&lrm; <i>-ποιῐ̈́ᾱ</i> (-poiĭ̈́ā, “making”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form or fashion into tongues; to make tongue-shaped</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”) + <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make, fashion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>making mouthpieces</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῶσσᾰ</i> (glôssă, “mouthpiece”) +&lrm; <i>ποιός</i> (poiós, “making”), from <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>γλωσσῐ́ς</i> (glōssĭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to perform obscene acts with the tongue</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῶττα</i> (glôtta, “tongue”) + <i>δέψω</i> (dépsō, “to knead, work, dress (leather)”); literally “to work the tongue”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) a child of tongue</li><li>One who is tongue shaped</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῶττα</i> (glôtta, “tongue”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;euphemistic) to do it with the tongue (to give cunnilingus)</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῶττα</i> (glôtta, “tongue”) + <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make; to do”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>γλωχίς</i> (glōkhís)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>projecting point</li><li>end of the yoke strap</li><li>barb of an arrow</li><li>point of a penknife</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) stigma of saffron</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;glōgʰ-s</i> (“point”) but there are no certain cognates; possibly Old Norse <i>kleggi</i> (“gadfly”) (see English <i>cleg</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>engraved figure; signet</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῠ́φω</i> (glŭ́phō, “to carve”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>γλῠ́πτης</i> (glŭ́ptēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a carver, a sculptor</li></ol><p>From <i>γλῠ́φω</i> (glŭ́phō, “to carve”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, suffix forming masculine agent nouns). The stem of <i>γλῠ́φω</i> (glŭ́phō) also occurs without aspiration, as <i>γλῠπ-</i> (glŭp-), in the verb’s third-person singular mediopassive indicative perfect – <i>γέγλῠπται, ἔγλῠπται</i> (géglŭptai, églŭptai) – and pluperfect – <i>ἐγέγλῠπτο, ἔγλῠπτο</i> (egéglŭpto, églŭpto) – forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carve, cut out with a knife, engrave</li><li>to note down, write</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;glúpʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;glewbʰ-</i> (“to split, carve”). Cognates include Old English <i>clēofan</i> (English <i>cleave</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) gaudy things, costume jewelry, trinkets</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek root of <i>γλήνη</i> (glḗnē, “eyeball; doll”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan maple (<i>Acer sempervirens</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the tongue</li><li>a language</li><li>anything shaped like the tongue</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>music</i>) the mouthpiece of a pipe</li><li>the tongue or thong of leather, shoe-latchet</li><li>the tongue of land</li><li>ingot</li><li>(<i>divination</i>) a marking on the liver</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;glṓťťā</i>; further etymology uncertain. Per Beekes, perhaps originally “provided with a point”, equivalent to <i>γλῶχες</i> (glôkhes, “beard of corn”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;glṓgʰs ~ &#42;gl̥gʰós</i> (“point”) (assuming cognacy with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glogъ</i> (“thorn, hawthorn”), but the connection is disputed). Alternatively, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tulgaz</i> (“tongue”), a poetic word, from a different Proto-Indo-European root noun <i>&#42;dlṓgʰs ~ &#42;dl̥gʰós</i>; compare <i>γλυκύς</i> (glukús) for the phonetics. Or, possibly related to Albanian <i>gjuhë</i> (“tongue”), but the irregular correspondence would point to a substrate language. Compare also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golgolъ</i> (“utterance, speech”), Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;galsás</i> (“voice; sound”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalzōną</i> (“to call, shout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people speaking a foreign tongue</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the tongue</li><li>a language</li><li>anything shaped like the tongue</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>music</i>) the mouthpiece of a pipe</li><li>the tongue or thong of leather, shoe-latchet</li><li>the tongue of land</li><li>ingot</li><li>(<i>divination</i>) a marking on the liver</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;glṓťťā</i>; further etymology uncertain. Per Beekes, perhaps originally “provided with a point”, equivalent to <i>γλῶχες</i> (glôkhes, “beard of corn”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;glṓgʰs ~ &#42;gl̥gʰós</i> (“point”) (assuming cognacy with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glogъ</i> (“thorn, hawthorn”), but the connection is disputed). Alternatively, related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tulgaz</i> (“tongue”), a poetic word, from a different Proto-Indo-European root noun <i>&#42;dlṓgʰs ~ &#42;dl̥gʰós</i>; compare <i>γλυκύς</i> (glukús) for the phonetics. Or, possibly related to Albanian <i>gjuhë</i> (“tongue”), but the irregular correspondence would point to a substrate language. Compare also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;golgolъ</i> (“utterance, speech”), Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;galsás</i> (“voice; sound”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kalzōną</i> (“to call, shout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jaw</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>narrow strait</li></ol><li>point; edge</li><li>a type of stone</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵn̥h₂dʰ-os</i>, from a root <i>&#42;ǵneh₂dʰ-</i> related to <i>&#42;ǵénu-</i> (“jaw, cheek, chin”), despite (as Beekes objects) not yielding <i>&#42;γνᾱθος</i> (&#42;gnāthos) as may be expected. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>žándas</i> (“cheek”) and English <i>chin</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend</li></ol><p>Unknown. Although it is commonly assumed that it was influenced by <i>κάμπτω</i> (kámptō, “to bend, curve”), this verb does not have the <i>κ-</i> (k-), nor can the <i>-ν-</i> (-n-) have been taken from there. An Indo-European etymology seems excluded, because <i>γναμπ-</i> (gnamp-) cannot have an Indo-European preform. Even though an alternation <i>γν&#47;κ</i> (gn&#47;k) is not typical for Pre-Greek either, Beekes does not reject this hypothesis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) curved, bent</li></ol><p>Apparently related to <i>καμπτός</i> (kamptós, “flexible”); compare <i>κάμπτω</i> (kámptō, “to bend, curve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cottonweed (<i>Otanthus maritimus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>γνάφαλλον</i> (gnáphallon, “flock of wool”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black horehound (<i>Ballota nigra</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>ᾰ̓γνωτῐ́δῐον</i> (ăgnōtĭ́dĭon, a kind of fish), suggesting a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>νάπαι</i> (nápai, “glen, vale”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>γνύθος</i> (gnúthos, “pit, hollow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>judging by rule; fit to judge of or skilled in a thing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of or concerning sun-dials</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>forming a γνώμων (gnṓmōn)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>γνώμων</i> (gnṓmōn, “interpreter, discerner”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, suffix to form an adjective).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known (in passive: become known)</li><li>to learn, discover</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵneh₃-</i> (“to know”) (whence <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō)). Compare <i>γνώριμος</i> (gnṓrimos) and <i>gnarus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for knowing, cognitive</li></ol><p>From <i>γνῶσις</i> (gnôsis, “knowledge, knowing”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>known</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “to know”) +&lrm; <i>-στός</i> (-stós). A later variant form of <i>γνωτός</i> (gnōtós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>known</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinsman</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵn̥h₃tós</i>. By surface analysis, from the root of <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pit, hollow</li></ol><p>The formation can be compared with <i>βάθος</i> (báthos) and <i>βυθός</i> (buthós). The word has been compared with <i>γνυφαί</i> (gnuphaí, “vales, dells”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin, like in <i>σκύφος</i> (skúphos)/<i>σκύθος</i> (skúthos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on bended knee</li></ol><p>From the zero grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵónu</i> (“knee”), whence also <i>γόνῠ</i> (gónŭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means of knowing: sign, mark</li><li>mind, intelligence</li><li>judgment, understanding, reason</li><li>will</li><li>opinion</li><li>decision</li></ol><p>From <i>γνω-</i> (gnō-), the root of <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “to know”) +&lrm; <i>-μη</i> (-mē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpreter, discerner</li><li>carpenter's square</li><li>sundial</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) gnomon</li></ol><p>From <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-known</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acquaintance</li><li>pupil</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>γνωρ(ίζω)</i> (gnōr(ízō), “to make known; learn, discover”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos). Also substantivised. If the proposed connection between Latin <i>glōria</i> (“glory”) and a putative Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵne&#47;oh₃ri-</i> were accepted, the Greek term may also ultimately derive from the same PIE form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inquiry</li><li>knowledge</li><li>fame</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵneh₃tis</i>. By surface analysis, <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “I know”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wail in lamentation</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bewail; to lament</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷewH-</i> (“to call; to invoke; to cry”); probably related to <i>βοᾰ́ω</i> (boắō, “to shout”) via a paradigmatic split. Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kawjaną</i> (“to call; to name”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gȍvorъ</i> (“to talk”), Sanskrit <i>जोगु॑वे</i> (jóguve, “to speak out loudly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turnip (<i>Brassica rapa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>γογγύλος</i> (gongúlos, “round, spherical”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mutter, murmur, grumble</li><li>to whisper</li></ol><p>Unclear. Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gong-</i>; compare Sanskrit <i>गङ्गूयति</i> (gaṅgūyati, “to shout”) and <i>गुञ्जति</i> (guñjati, “to buzz, purr, hum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>round, spherical</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>στρογγύλος</i> (strongúlos), <i>ἀγκύλος</i> (ankúlos), and <i>καμπύλος</i> (kampúlos). The etymology is unknown: a connection with Old Norse <i>kǫkkr</i> (“clump”) is highly doubtful, as is the comparison with Lithuanian <i>gungulỹs</i> (“ball”); nor does a relationship with <i>γιγγίς</i> (gingís, “kind of turnip”) seem probable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lamenting; mournful; distressful</li></ol><p>From <i>γόος</i> (góos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witchcraft</li><li>trickery</li></ol><p>From <i>γόης</i> (góēs, “sorcerer, wizard”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bewitching, beguiling</li></ol><p>From <i>γοητεύω</i> (goēteúō, “to bewitch”) +&lrm; <i>-ευτικός</i> (-eutikós, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bewitch, beguile, fascinate as a snake</li><li>to play the wizard</li></ol><p>From the genitive of <i>γόης</i> (góēs, “sorcerer”) + <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κέλυφος</i> (kéluphos, “case; pod, shell”), as they say in Tarentum.</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) molar, back tooth, grinder tooth</li></ol><p>From <i>γόμφος</i> (gómphos, “nail, peg”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>γονή</i> (gonḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offspring</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>of the fruits of the earth</i>)</li><li>race, stock, family; parentage</li><li>generation</li></ol><li>that which engenders, seed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>organs of generation (genitalia)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>womb</li></ol></ol><li>act of generation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the mother</i>) childbirth</li><li>(<i>of the child</i>) birth</li><li>cure for sterility</li></ol><li>(<i>Pythagorean nomenclature</i>) unity</li></ol><p>From <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>νάρκισσος</i> (nárkissos, “narcissus”).</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>κονίλη</i> (konílē, “marjoram”) and <i>γωνώνη</i> (gōnṓnē, “oregano”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) parent</li><li>progenitor; ancestor</li></ol><p>From <i>o</i>-grade of the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, “-er”, masculine agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on bended knee, falling on the knee</li></ol><p>From <i>γόνῠ</i> (gónŭ, “knee”) +&lrm; <i>-πετής</i> (-petḗs, “falling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) gonorrhea</li><li>blennorrhea (in women)</li></ol><p>From <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “sperm, seed”) +&lrm; <i>ῥοία</i> (rhoía, “flux”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field eryngo (<i>Eryngium campestre</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Γοργώ</i> (Gorgṓ, “gorgon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grim, fierce, terrible</li><li>spirited, vigorous</li><li>(<i>of literary style</i>) vehement, vigorous</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Frisk, this word is not related to Old Irish <i>garg</i> (“fierce, terrible”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;groza</i> (“horror”) nor to Old Armenian <i>կարծր</i> (karcr, “hard, solid, stiff”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton (<i>Gossypium herbaceum</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Sanskrit <i>कर्पास</i> (karpāsa, “cotton”), whence also Hebrew <i>כַּרְפַּס</i> (karpás, “fabric of cotton”), <i>κάρπᾰσος</i> (kárpăsos, “fabric of cotton”), Latin <i>carbasus</i> (“fabric of cotton”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruitful land, terrace</li><li>hill, height, elevated place</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is not related to Thessalian place names <i>Γόννος</i> (Gónnos), <i>Γόννοι</i> (Gónnoi) and <i>Γονοῦσσα</i> (Gonoûssa), nor to <i>γόνυ</i> (gónu, “knee”), and is instead Pre-Greek word, in view of the variations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hide, skin, pelt, fur (<i>of an animal</i>)</li><li>fur clothing, fur garment</li></ol><p>Ultimately likely from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;guna</i>, <i>&#42;guňa</i> (“coarse fur clothing”). Possibly via Late Latin <i>gunna</i> if not vice versa.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>γούνᾰτᾰ</i> (goúnătă) (<i>used before the rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>γούνᾰτᾰ</i> (goúnătă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>at least, then</li><li>surely, certainly</li><li>however</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>γε</i> (ge) and <i>οὖν</i> (oûn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trough, a long, narrow container, open on top, for feeding or watering animals</li></ol><p>From Classical Syriac <i>ܓܘܪܢܐ</i> (gūrnā, “large vessel; coffin, sarcophagus”) or another Aramaic language. Unrelated to Latin <i>urna</i> (“urn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of water snake mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</li></ol><p>From an Indo-Aryan word related to Sanskrit <i>ग्रह</i> (graha, “crocodile”, literally “seizing, holding, catching, receiving”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch, brand</li></ol><p>From a non-Indo-European Balkan substrate word meaning "beech, oak", found also in Greek <i>γάβρος</i> (gávros, “hornbeam”), Ancient Greek <i>κρᾰ́ββᾰτος</i> (krắbbătos, “couch; mattress”), Russian <i>граб</i> (grab, “hornbeam”), and Old Prussian <i>wosigrabis</i>. Furnée further compares <i>γοβρίαι</i> (gobríai, “torches”), which cannot be ignored.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is written, that which is drawn, picture</li><li>letter</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) alphabet</li><li>writing, book</li><li>a kind of small weight, 1/24 of an ounce, scruple</li></ol><p>From <i>γρᾰ́φω</i> (grắphō, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p><p>There are two theories:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) to scratch, cut into</li><li>to draw, sketch, paint</li><li>to write</li><li>to write down, propose a law</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>indirect reflexive</i>) write down for oneself, note down</li><li>to indict, prosecute</li></ol><li>(<i>perfect passive</i>) be written down, be in written form</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;grə́pʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gerbʰ-</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>ċeorfan</i> (English <i>carve</i>), Old Church Slavonic <i>жрѣбъ</i> (žrěbŭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw, eat</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gres-</i> (“to gnaw, eat”) (although Frisk considers <i>&#42;gr̥s-</i> a more appropriate reconstruction). Possible cognates include Sanskrit <i>ग्रसति</i> (grasati, “to devour”), Old Norse <i>krás</i>&nbsp;f (“delicious food”), and the less likely Latin <i>grāmen</i>&nbsp;n (“grass, herb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stroke or line of a pen</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in forming letters</i>) a line traced by a teacher</li><li>an outline</li></ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) a line as in mathematical figures</li><li>a line across the course, a starting- or winning-point</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>used metaphorically of life</li><li>a boundary-line, an edge</li><li>the cutting edge of a knife</li></ol><li>a line or square on a chequer-board</li><li>(<i>law</i>) the “long line”, i.e. a death sentence</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) <i>linea alba</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>synonym of <i>ζέα</i></li></ol></ol><p>From <i>γρᾰ́φω</i> (grắphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small tablet to write on</li><li>memorandum, written document</li><li>menu, bill of fare</li></ol><p>From <i>γραμματεῖον</i> (grammateîon, “tablet”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δῐον</i> (-ĭ́dĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secretary, registrar; clerk (<i>title of officials in Greek poleis</i>)</li><li>scribe, scholar</li></ol><p>From <i>γράμμα</i> (grámma, “writing”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine suffix of person concerned).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animate senses&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a female teacher of the rudiments</li><li>a woman who occupies herself with literary texts, a female grammarian or critic</li></ol><li>inanimate senses&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>grammar</li><li>the faculty of scholarship</li><li>alphabet, script, writing</li></ol></ol><p>As an animate noun, a feminisation of <i>γραμματικός</i> (grammatikós, “teacher of the rudiments, grammarian”) and as an inanimate noun, an ellipsis of <i>γραμματικὴ τέχνη</i> (grammatikḕ tékhnē, “grammatical art, grammar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fee paid to scribes in Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>knowing one’s letters, of a good scholar</li><li>(in the phrase <i>γραμματικὸν ἔκπωμα</i>) a cup engraved with the alphabet or an inscription</li><li>concerned with textual criticism</li><li>(in the phrase <i>γραμματικὴ τέχνη</i>) the grammatical art or craft, grammar</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher of the rudiments</li><li>one who occupies himself with literary texts, grammarian, critic</li></ol><p>From <i>γρᾰ́μμᾰ</i> (grắmmă) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>geometrical</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(figuratively,&#32;of a proof) rigorous</li></ol><li>linear (of, relating to, or having the form of a line or lines)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in the phrase <i>γρᾰμμῐκὸς ἀρῐθμός</i>) a linear number</li></ol><li>synonym of <i>γρᾰμμᾰτῐκός</i></li></ol><p><i>γρᾰμμή</i> (grămmḗ, “a stroke or line of a pen”, “a line as in mathematical figures”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “of or pertaining to”, “in the manner of”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painted</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>marked as with letters</li></ol><li>written</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective of <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō), with suffix <i>-τός</i> (-tós). From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;grəptós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gr̥bʰtós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawing</li><li>painting</li><li>writing, a writing</li><li>description</li></ol><p>From <i>γρᾰ́φω</i> (grắphō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stilus</li><li>paintbrush</li><li>graving tool</li><li>embroidery needle</li><li>embroidery</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) paintings</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) inscription plate</li></ol><p>From <i>γρᾰ́φω</i> (grắphō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ς</i> (-ĭ́s)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>painter</li><li>secretary</li><li>writer</li><li>scribe</li></ol><p>From <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, “-er”, masculine agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pencil</li><li>paintbrush</li><li>engraving tool, chisel</li><li>registry, record office</li><li>tax on writing materials</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fees for copying</li></ol><p>From <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to writing</li><li>pertaining to painting</li></ol><p>From <i>γραφή</i> (graphḗ, “drawing, painting, writing, a writing, description, etc.”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós), from <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A pencil case</li></ol><p>From <i>γραφεῖον</i> (grapheîon, “pencil”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an old woman</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;γρᾱ́ϝ-</i> (&#42;grā́w-), a contraction of the stem <i>&#42;γρᾰέϝ-</i> (&#42;grăéw-), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;grayéus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵr̥h₂-yéw-s</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵerh₂-</i> (“grow old”). The variety of dialectal forms result from different attempts to phonologically repair the sequence <i>-ᾱϝ</i> (-āw, Ionic <i>-ηϝ-</i> (-ēw-)) before consonant-endings; before vowel-endings, <i>-ϝ-</i> (-w-) was simply lost.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>γραῦς</i> (graûs, “old woman”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or become fully awake</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gruel, porridge</li></ol><p>Ultimately related to Latin <i>grutum</i> (“flour, meal”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grūtiz</i> (“ground material; grit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grunt (<i>of swine</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>γρῦλος</i> (grûlos), from <i>γρῦ</i> (grû) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag or chest for old clothes</li></ol><p>In origin, the word probably denoted small things of little value. The formation suggests a Pre-Greek origin and perhaps it is connected to <i>γρῦ</i> (grû, “dirt under the nail”). The comparison with Latin <i>grumus</i> (“hillock”) is unconvincing; better, but still doubtful, is that with Old English <i>cruma</i> (“crumb, morsel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) squirting cucumber, <i>Ecballium elaterium</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bundle of sticks</li><li>torch; firebrand</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a hooknose; aquiline</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fist</li><li>breadth of a hand</li><li>grasp, spoke of a machine</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a root <i>&#42;γρόν</i> (&#42;grón) with a suffix <i>-θος</i> (-thos) supposedly equivalent to that found in <i>μασθός</i> (masthós), <i>βρόχθος</i> (brókhthos), and <i>κύσθος</i> (kústhos). Said root is often compared with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;krimmaną</i> (“to crumble, squeeze”), Old Norse <i>krumma</i> (“hollow of the hand”), Latin <i>gremium</i> (“lap, womb”), and Lithuanian <i>grùmti</i> (“to press into, fill”), suggesting a derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;grem-</i> or similar, though phonetic mismatches make these comparisons uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of javelin</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of dance performed in Egypt</li><li>performer in such a dance</li><li>comic figure, caricature</li></ol><p>Deemed an Egyptian word by Phrynichus (tragic poet). According to Beekes, there is no relation between this word and <i>γρῦλος</i> (grûlos, “pig; porker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) dirt under the fingernails</li></ol><p>Cognate with <i>γρῦ</i> (grû); compare Latin <i>grȳ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>griffin</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Commonly linked to <i>γρῡπός</i> (grūpós, “hook-nosed; aquiline”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ger-</i> (“to turn, twist, bend, wind”), related to Old English <i>crumb</i>, <i>crump</i> (“bent, crooked”), Old High German <i>krump</i>.</p><p>Beekes and Klein dismiss an Indo-European origin and instead suggest Pre-Greek; possibly borrowed via Hittite or some other Anatolian medium from a Semitic word related to Akkadian <i>𒅗𒆕𒁍</i> (karūbu) and Hebrew <i>כרוב</i> (kerúv), in which case a doublet of <i>χερούβ</i> (kheroúb).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cavern (from the phrase γρώνη πέτρα (grṓnē pétra, “cavernous cave”))</li><li>hollow vessel</li><li>kneading trough</li></ol><p>Nominalised female form of the adjective <i>γρῶνος</i> (grônos, “hollow, cavernous”) which is of unknown origin. The traditional connection to <i>γράω</i> (gráō, “gnaw, eat”) via the unattested form <i>&#42;γρώσνος</i> (&#42;grṓsnos) is doubtful since the verb does not show ablaut, with the semantic connection also being poor.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creel, trap for fish, fishing basket</li></ol><p>Like <i>γρῖφος</i> (grîphos, “creel; riddle, enigma”), from Pre-Greek, given the variation <i>π/φ</i>. Comparison with Middle High German <i>krēbe</i> (“basket”) and Old Norse <i>kerfi</i> (“bundle”) must therefore be rejected.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creel, trap for fish, fishing basket</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) riddle, enigma, anything intricate saying</li></ol><p>Like <i>γρῖπος</i> (grîpos, “creel, fishing basket”), from Pre-Greek, given the variation <i>π/φ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small sound; a grunt</li><li>(<i>chiefly in the negative</i>) a small quantity; a bit; a morsel</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic of the sound pigs make; compare Latin <i>grunniō</i> (“to grunt like a pig”), German <i>grunzen</i> (“to grunt”) for similar formations. Possibly connected to <i>γρῡ́ξ</i> (grū́x) and <i>γρῡ́τη</i> (grū́tē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lame</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“to bend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knapsack, haversack</li><li>hedgehog</li></ol><p>Unknown. It has been compared with Old Norse <i>kýll</i> (“bag for victuals”) and Old High German <i>kiulla</i> (“bag”). Maybe related to <i>γύαλον</i> (gúalon, “hollow of a cuirass”). According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“to bend, bow, arch, vault, curve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to train naked</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) to train</li></ol><p>From <i>γυμνός</i> (gumnós) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) bodily exercises</li><li>gymnastic school, school</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>γυμνάζω</i> (gumnázō, “to exercise”), from <i>γυμνός</i> (gumnós, “naked”), and <i>-ιον</i> (-ion), neuter of adjective suffix <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exercise</li></ol><p>From <i>γυμνός</i> (gumnós, “naked”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The director of a gymnasium.</li><li>The office overseeing athletic training in ancient Athens.</li></ol><p>From <i>γυμνάσιον</i> (gumnásion, “gymnasium, athletic school”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler, leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trainer of professional athletes</li><li>athlete, gymnast</li></ol><p>From <i>γῠμνᾰ́ζω</i> (gŭmnắzō, “to train, exercise”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fond of and/or skilled in athletic exercises</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) gymnastics</li></ol><p>From <i>γυμναστής</i> (gumnastḗs, “athlete”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gymnosophist, one of the ancient Indian ascetics reported in antiquity to forgo clothing</li></ol><p>From <i>γυμνός</i> (gumnós, “naked”) +&lrm; <i>σοφιστής</i> (sophistḗs, “teacher, philosopher”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naked, nude, unclad</li><li>unarmed, without armor, defenseless</li><li>bare, uncovered</li><li>stripped, destitute</li><li>lightly clad</li><li>mere</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷomnós</i> by Cowgill's Law (o → u between labial and resonant), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nogʷmós</i> by metathesis (possible taboo deformation; if the metathesis didn't happen we would have <i>&#42;νυγμός</i> (&#42;nugmós)), from <i>&#42;nogʷós</i> (“naked”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to strip (someone/something) naked, denude</li><li>(<i>middle voice&#32;or&#32;passive voice</i>) to strip (oneself) naked</li><li>(<i>middle voice&#32;or&#32;passive voice</i>) to be unarmed</li></ol><p><i>γυμνός</i> (gumnós) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman; female</li><li>wife</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷənā</i>. Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀓𐀙𐀊</i> (ku-na-ja), Sanskrit <i>ग्ना</i> (gnā́), <i>जनि॑</i> (jáni), Old Armenian <i>կին</i> (kin), and Old English <i>cwēn</i> (English <i>queen</i>).</p><p>The weak stem is probably from the strong stem suffixed with <i>&#42;-keh₂</i> (compare <i>μέγᾰς</i> (mégăs)). Compare also Latin <i>mulier</i> for the development of using hypocorisms to refer to women.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or pertaining to a woman</li><li>feminine, womanish</li></ol><p>From <i>γυνή</i> (gunḗ, “woman”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supervisor of women, the title of magistrate at Athens who maintains manners among women</li></ol><p><i>γῠνή</i> (gŭnḗ, “woman”) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gynaeceum (the portion of a house reserved for women)</li><li>women which reside in the gynaeceum</li></ol><p>From <i>γῠναικών</i> (gŭnaikṓn, “gynaeceum”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτῐς</i> (-îtĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>women's apartment in a house</li></ol><p>From <i>γυνή</i> (gunḗ, “woman”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net used in alchemy for suspending substances in fluids</li></ol><p>From <i>γυργαθός</i> (gurgathós, “creel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wicker basket, creel</li><li>net woven by tarantulas</li><li>cage for insane patients</li></ol><p>According to Beekes either from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵers-</i> (“to twist; to plait”), like <i>γέρρον</i> (gérrhon), or from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>round</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“to curve, bend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make round, bend, wind</li><li>to bind up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be bent</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the elderly</i>) to be weak</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to coil oneself up</li><li>to plant in a ringe, circle</li><li>to make a circle around a tree</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to dig, trench</li></ol><p>From <i>γῦρος</i> (gûros) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tadpole</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) limbs</li><li>hand</li><li>body</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“hand; to bend”). See also <i>ἐγγῠ́ς</i> (engŭ́s, “nearby”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>φάρυγξ</i> (phárunx, “windpipe, throat”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gwol-</i>, and compared with Lithuanian <i>guõlis</i> (“den”), Latvian <i>guoļa</i> (“lair, nest”), and Armenian <i>կաղաղ</i> (kaġaġ, “hole”). However, this comparison is uncertain and the influence of <i>φωλεός</i> (phōleós), as well as the slight similarity with <i>γύαλον</i> (gúalon, “hollow of a vessel”), must be considered.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) corner, angle</li><li>joiner's square</li><li>cornerstone</li></ol><p>From a derivative of <i>γόνυ</i> (gónu, “knee”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵónu</i> (“id”). The long <i>ω</i> (ō) is from the regular Doric development of an unattested <i>&#42;γονϝ-ία</i> (&#42;gonw-ía), as the paradigm of <i>&#42;ǵónu</i> shows no lengthened grade (thus precluding a derivation from such an ablaut), while most mathematicians in Greek antiquity (who were most likely responsible for the word's formation) were Pythagorean (and thus wrote in Doric). Despite superficial and coincidental similarities, unrelated to Sanskrit <i>कोण</i> (koṇa, “corner, angle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiver</li></ol><p>According to Benveniste, a loanword from a Scythian compound that contains the word for 'cow' (compare Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;gā́wš</i>) as a first member. The interpretation of the second member is unclear, however we can hardly identify it with Persian روده (rude, “intestine”) and interpret the whole compound as 'cow-hide'. Alternatively, Lubotsky suggests that the word may derive from a Proto-Iranian source similar to Sanskrit <i>वरूतृ</i> (varūtṛ, “one who wards off or protects”), thus explaining the long <i>ῡ</i> (ū). Remains uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conger eel (eel of the family Congridae)</li><li>(<i>phytopathology</i>) tubercular disease in olive trees</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorcerer, wizard</li><li>juggler, cheat</li></ol><p>From <i>γοάω</i> (goáō, “to groan, wail”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freight; cargo; the goods and items carried by a ship or other vessel</li><li>load; burden; a weight carried by a beast of burden or draught animal</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gómos</i>, a derivation of <i>&#42;gem-</i> (“to press together; to grasp”) and so related to <i>γέμω</i> (gémō, “to be full of”), <i>γέντο</i> (génto, “he/she grasped”), Latin <i>gemō</i> (“to sigh, groan”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žęti</i> (“to press, squeeze”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bolt, dowel, nail</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) bond, fastener</li><li>cross-ribs of Egyptian canoes</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) instrument for cautery</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ankle joint</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gómpʰos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵómbʰos</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Ancient Macedonian <i>κόμβος</i> (kómbos), Sanskrit <i>जम्भ</i> (jámbha, “tooth”), Latin <i>gemma</i>, Old Armenian <i>ծամեմ</i> (camem), Old English <i>camb</i> (English <i>comb</i>), and Albanian <i>dhëmb</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>productive, fertile, fruitful [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘the product or fruit’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(figuratively,&#32;of an author) genius</li></ol><li>of a legitimate child: born in lawful wedlock</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>viable</li><li>(<i>rarely</i>) favorable to generation (<i>of uneven days,&#32;or&#32;days in illness</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>o</i>-grade of the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born, come into existence”) + <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos, suffix for adjective of capability): literally "able to produce".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is begotten: child, grandchild, offspring</li><li>fruit, product</li><li>race, stock, descent</li><li>procreation, begetting</li><li>seed</li><li>genitals</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵónh₁os</i> (“race”). Equivalent to <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Cognate to Sanskrit <i>जन</i> (jana, “human, race, people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knee</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gónu</i> (“knee”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵónu</i> (“knee”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>जानु॑</i> (jā́nu), Latin <i>genu</i>, Old Armenian <i>ծունգք</i> (cungkʻ), Gothic <i>𐌺𐌽𐌹𐌿</i> (kniu), and Old English <i>cnēow</i> (English <i>knee</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wailing; weeping</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷówHos</i>, from <i>&#42;gewH-</i> (“to cry out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hartwort (<i>Tordylium officinale</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underground sewer or drain</li><li>underground prison; dungeon; jail</li><li>ewer, water jug</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, definitely from Pre-Greek, but compare Sanskrit <i>गर्ग॑र</i> (gárgara, “whirlpool; eddy; churn; water-jar”) and Latin <i>gurges</i> (“whirlpool; eddy”). Compare also <i>κορκόδρῠᾰ</i> (korkódrŭă, “drain”) and <i>κορχῠρέᾱ</i> (korkhŭréā, “subterranean drain”). The similar forms may be due to onomatopoeia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hollow of the cuirass</li><li>hollow of a vessel</li><li>hollow of a rock, cave</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) vales, dells</li></ol><p>For the formation, one might compare <i>ἀγκάλη</i> (ankálē, “bent arm”) and <i>ὀμφαλός</i> (omphalós, “navel”). The word is generally connected with <i>γυῖον</i> (guîon, “hand”) and <i>γύης</i> (gúēs, “earth”), so it may derive either from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“hand”) or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew(H)-</i> (“to bend”) (which are either identical or have been conflated early in Indo-European).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a vulture</li></ol><p>From <i>γύψ</i> (gúps, “vulture”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulture</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of chalk</li></ol><p>From <i>γῠ́ψος</i> (gŭ́psos, “chalk”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chalk</li><li>cement</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>γράμμα</i> (grámma)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>γῆ</i> (gê), "land"</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth; land</li><li>country</li><li>soil</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>γαῖᾰ</i> (gaîă), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gaya</i>, from a pre-Indo-European Pre-Greek substrate. The names <i>Δημήτηρ</i> (Dēmḗtēr) and <i>Ποσειδῶν</i> (Poseidôn), and also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀚𐀯𐀅𐀃𐀚</i> (e-ne-si-da-o-ne, “earthshaking (epithet of Poseidon)”), point to an alternative form <i>&#42;δᾶ</i> (&#42;dâ).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Γῆ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Earth, personified as a goddess</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old age</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵērh₂-s</i>, a lengthened-grade derivative of <i>&#42;ǵerh₂-</i> (“to grow old”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>जरा</i> (jarā́, “old age”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice, speech</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵeh₂r-</i> (“to shout, call”) (reconstructed by Beekes as <i>&#42;geh₂r-</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring; circle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“to curve, to bend”), the same source as Old Armenian <i>կոր</i> (kor), Lithuanian <i>gur̃nas</i> (“ankle, hip, bone”), and Norwegian <i>kaure</i> (“curly lock of hair”). See also <i>γυῖον</i> (guîon, “hand; limbs”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δέ</i> (dé)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δέ</i> (dé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;denḱ-</i> (“to bite”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दशति</i> (daśati, “to bite”), Old English <i>ġetingan</i> (“to press upon”), and Albanian <i>darë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal of which the bite is dangerous, noxious beast</li><li>bite, sting</li></ol><p>From <i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō, “I bite”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tear (drop of liquid from the eye)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) anything which drips like a tear, such as gum or sap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dáḱru</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>lacruma</i>, Gothic <i>𐍄𐌰𐌲𐍂</i> (tagr), Old English <i>tēar</i>, Sanskrit <i>अश्रु॑</i> (áśru) and Old Armenian <i>արտաւսր</i> (artawsr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>δᾰ́κρῠ</i> (dắkrŭ, “tear”, drop of liquid from the eye)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>δᾰ́κρῠᾰ</i> (dắkrŭă), the nominative plural of <i>δᾰ́κρῠ</i> (dắkrŭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finger</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>toe</li></ol><li>measure of length, the breadth of a finger, about 7/10 of an inch</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>dactyl, a metrical foot</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) a dance</li></ol><li>date (fruit)</li><li>type of grape</li></ol><p>Uncertain; probably Pre-Greek. Possibly related to Latin <i>digitus</i>, though the details are unclear. Note also Boeotian Greek <i>δακκύλιος</i> (dakkúlios).</p><p>The sense "date" is probably a folk-etymological alteration of a word from a Semitic source such as Arabic دَقَل (daqal, “variety of date palm”) or Hebrew <i>דֶּקֶל</i> (deqel, “date palm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heifer</li><li>girl</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;demh₂-</i> (“to domesticate, tame”) with the same <i>&#42;-l-</i> suffix as found in <i>δαμάλης</i> (damálēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;Koine) calf</li></ol><p>A masculine formation from the much more common word <i>δάμαλις</i>&nbsp;f (dámalis, “heifer”). From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;demh₂-</i> (“to tame, domesticate”). Cognate with Albanian <i>dem</i> (“bull”), Old Irish <i>dam</i> (“ox”), Persian دام (dâm, “livestock”), and Welsh <i>dafad</i> (“sheep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife; spouse</li></ol><p>Unknown. Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dṓm</i> (“house, home”), like <i>δόμος</i> (dómos, “dwelling”), and the root <i>ἀρ-</i> in <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “to join, fit together”). Perhaps more likely from an -r/n-stem like Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dómh₂-r̥</i> (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;demh₂-</i> (“tame”)), with the -τ- taken over from the n-stem forms.</p><p>However, the word could also be of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix <i>-αρ</i> (-ar).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>δαμάζω</i> (damázō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dm̥-né-h₂-ti</i>, a nasal-infix present from <i>&#42;demh₂-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>दाम्यति</i> (dāmyati) and Latin <i>domō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jug for wine</li></ol><p>Presumably a Semitic borrowing, deriving from Akkadian <i>𒂁𒆗𒉡</i> (/⁠dannu⁠/, “storage vat mostly for storing beer and wine”), also found as Ugaritic <i>𐎄𐎐</i> (dn, “vessel, vat”), Aramaic <i>דַּנָּא</i> / <i>ܕܰܢܳܐ</i> (dannā, “wine jug, vat”) and Arabic دَنّ (dann, “wine jug”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of big dog</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>finance</i>) loan</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰ́νος</i> (dắnos, “gift, present”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὀδάξ</i> (odáx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firebrand; torch</li><li>Hesychius gives the definition as&#58; Phrygian word for <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”).</li></ol><p>From <i>δαίω</i> (daíō, “to kindle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p><p>From Phrygian <i>δάος</i> (dáos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₂w-</i> (per Derksen); elsewhere reconstructed as <i>&#42;dʰew-</i> (“to die”). Possibly cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dawjaną</i> (“to die”), Avestan <i>𐬛𐬀𐬎𐬎-</i> (dauu-, “to crush, to oppress”) (attested as <i>𐬛𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬌𐬛𐬍</i> (duuaidī, 1&nbsp;du. past middle)). Further, akin to Ancient Greek <i>Ζεὺς θαύλιος</i> (Zeùs thaúlios, “Zeus the strangler? (Phrygian god)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>floor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dḿ̥-pedom</i>, from <i>&#42;dem-</i> + <i>&#42;ped-</i>. Possibly cognate to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tumftiz</i> (“ground around a building; foundation”). The second element is equivalent to <i>πέδον</i> (pédon) and cognate to Sanskrit <i>प॒द</i> (padá, “step; footstep; footprint”), Old Armenian <i>հետ</i> (het, “footprint; track”), English <i>foot</i>, Latin <i>pēs</i> and Hittite <i>𒁉𒂊𒁕𒀭</i> (pēdan).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, devour, as wild beasts</li><li>to consume, corrode</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dapťō</i>. If this verb is formed from a root <i>δαπ-</i>, this may be compared with Latin <i>daps</i> (“banquet, feast”) and perhaps with Tocharian A <i>tāp-</i> (“to consume, eat”), although the Tocharian initial <i>t-</i> is problematic. Further connections have been proposed with Latin <i>damnum</i> (“loss, damage”) and Old Norse <i>tafn</i> (“sacrifice”), as well as Old Armenian <i>տօն</i> (tōn, “festival, celebration”). All these connections point to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂p-</i> (“to sacrifice, lose”). However, in spite of the proposed cognates, which do not convince Beekes on the semantic side, it can also be considered a Pre-Greek origin, especially if it should be related to <i>δεῖπνον</i> (deîpnon, “meal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) stain</li></ol><p>Unknown, but clearly related to another Hesychian gloss, <i>δαρδαίνει</i> (dardaínei, “to stain, polute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Delphic form of <i>δέρμᾰ</i> (dérmă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A basket</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin and clearly related to <i>τάρπη</i> (tárpē). Older suggestions tended to connect the word with Sanskrit <i>दर्भ</i> (darbha, “bundle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thicket, copse</li><li>shagginess, roughness</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>δᾰσῠ́ς</i> (dăsŭ́s, “shaggy”) + <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweet bay (<i>Laurus nobilis</i>)</li></ol><p>Beekes reconstructs a possible Pre-Greek stem <i>*dakʷ-(n-)</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δάφνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daphne</li></ol><p>From <i>δάφνη</i> (dáphnē, “laurel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparation made with the fruit of the laurel</li></ol><p>From <i>δάφνινος</i> (dáphninos, “made of laurel”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of bay</li></ol><p>From <i>δάφνη</i> (dáphnē, “laurel, bay”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of bay</li></ol><p>From <i>δάφνη</i> (dáphnē, “laurel, bay”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hostile; destructive</li><li>burning; consuming</li><li>unhappy; wretched</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>δᾰ́ῐ̈ς</i> (dắĭ̈s, “battle”), which Beekes claims is of Pre-Greek origin. However, the linguist Peter Barber alternatively suggests a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂w-i(H)os</i>, itself from the root <i>&#42;deh₂w-</i> (“to burn”), whence also Ancient Greek <i>δαίω</i> (daíō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) battle</li></ol><p>Possibly from an original meaning of “burning”, connected to <i>δᾰῐ̈́ς</i> (dăĭ̈́s, “torch”). However, the Mycenaean form <i>𐀅𐀂𐀦𐀲</i> (da-i-qo-ta), lacking <i>-ϝ-</i> (-w-), suggests a non-Indo-European origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>but, and</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>but, and</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dḗ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;de</i> (deictic particle). See also <i>δή</i> (dḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;defective) it seemed, it appeared</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deyh₂-</i> (“to shine, be bright”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दी</i> (dī, “to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dékə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déḱm̥</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>दश</i> (dáśa), Latin <i>decem</i>, Old Armenian <i>տասն</i> (tasn) and Old English <i>tīen</i> (English <i>ten</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tenth (ordinal number)</li><li>one tenth (fraction)</li></ol><p>From <i>δέκᾰ</i> (dékă, “ten”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beggar</li></ol><p>From <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “to receive”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs), thus literally, “recipient”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bait</li></ol><p>Via &#42;δέλεϝαρ (&#42;délewar) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelh₁wr̥</i>, perhaps connected to βάλλω (bállō), possibly also to Latin <i>gula</i>, Old Armenian <i>կլանեմ</i> (klanem) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glъtati</i>. Compare δελήτιον (delḗtion), δέλετρον (déletron) and δείλατα (deílata).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of wasp</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the geminate <i>-λλ-</i> (-ll-) leads to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delta, the name for the fourth letter Δ (D), δ (d) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li><li>anything shaped like a Δ</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>river delta</li><li>vulva</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤃</i> (d /⁠dalet⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;feminine) a writing-tablet</li><li>(Attic,&#32;Koine,&#32;feminine) any writing (e.g., a letter, a will, vel sim.), document</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;neuter) a book</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Semitic origin, related to <i>dalet</i>. (A connection to <i>delta</i>, ultimately from the same Semitic source, has also been proposed.)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother-swine, sow</li></ol><p>For the suffix, compare <i>κόραξ</i> (kórax, “crow”) and <i>σκύλαξ</i> (skúlax, “puppy”). From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelbʰ-</i> (“womb”), the same root of <i>δελφύς</i> (delphús, “womb”), <i>δολφός</i> (dolphós, “womb”) and <i>δελφίς</i> (delphís, “dolphin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) clamp, dowel</li><li>towrope</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) roadblock, barricade</li></ol><p>From <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) in such a form or fashion</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δέμω</i> (démō, “build”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δέμνια</i> (démnia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in plural</i>) bedstead, cot, bedframe</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) bed</li></ol><p>Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dem-</i>, the same root of <i>δέμω</i> (démō, “to build”) and <i>δόμος</i> (dómos, “house, dwelling”). The word has also been connected to <i>κρήδεμνον</i> (krḗdemnon, “headband”), suggesting a derivation from the same root of <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, construct, prepare, make</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;démō</i>, per Beekes, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dem-h₁-</i>, an extension of the root <i>&#42;dem-</i> (“to build”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀆𐀕𐀃𐀳</i> (de-me-o-te).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;Doric,&#32;rare&#32;in&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>δένδρον</i> (déndron, “tree”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of wood</li></ol><p>From <i>δένδρον</i> (déndron, “tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;Attic,&#32;rare&#32;in&#32;Epic&#32;and&#32;Ionic) tree</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-drew-om</i> with dissimilation, reduplicated from <i>&#42;dóru</i> (“tree”), but this type of reduplication is highly atypical, so the formation must be regarded as uncertain. Per Janda, alternatively from a compound <i>&#42;dem-</i> (“to build; house”) + <i>&#42;drew-</i>, thus perhaps meaning “tree (planted near a) house”, but the semantic basis for such an assumption is weak.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>take that</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accept, receive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱ-</i> (“to take, accept”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is binding, needful, right, proper.</li></ol><p>Neuter present participle of <i>δεῖ</i> (deî, “it is necessary, it behoves”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear, alarm</li><li>reason for fear</li></ol><p>For <i>&#42;δϝέϳος</i> (&#42;dwéjos), the σ-stem abstract noun formed from</p><p>Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwey-</i> (“to fear”), whence also <i>δεινός</i> (deinós). Cognate to Old Armenian <i>երկն</i> (erkn), Latin <i>dīrus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beaker, goblet</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it is a Pre-Greek word, like many other words for vessels. This word is generally connected to Luwian (tipas, “sky, heaven”). Another theory derives the noun from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;deupa</i> (“cradle, deposit”), comparing it to Albanian <i>djep</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beaker, goblet</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>&#42;δεπάζω</i> (&#42;depázō) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron), ultimately from δέπας (dépas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necklace, collar</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρη</i> (dérē, “neck, throat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A look, a glance</li><li>A sight</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρκομαι</i> (dérkomai, “I see clearly”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) neck, throat</li><li>mane of a lion</li><li>collar</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) gully, glen</li></ol><p>Unclear. Has been compared with Sanskrit <i>ग्रीवा</i> (grīvā́, “neck”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;griva</i> (“mane”) and Latvian <i>grīva</i> (“estuary”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷer-</i> (“to swallow”) (see <i>&#42;gʷriHwéh₂</i> (“throat”)). This suffers from a problematic initial in the Greek form. However, according to Beekes, a much better etymology is offered by taking the word as a formation related to <i>δειράς</i> (deirás), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see, see clearly</li><li>to watch</li><li>to live</li><li>to flash, gleam</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dérkomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derḱ-</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दृश्</i> (dṛś, “see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, hide</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, flay”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>τρίχινον σακίον</i> (tríkhinon sakíon, literally “small bag made of fur”).</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, flay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, hide</li><li>leathern covering, curtain</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) screens of skin, hung before fortifications to deaden the enemy's missiles</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, flay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omentum, membrane which contains the bowels</li><li>vulture's beak, sharp point</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, flay”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to skin, flay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dérō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i> (“to tear, tear apart”).</p><p>Cognate with English <i>tear</i>, Scots <i>tere</i>, <i>teir</i>, <i>tair</i> (“to rend, lacerate, wound, rip, tear out”), Lithuanian <i>di̇̀rti</i> (“to flay”), Dutch <i>teren</i> (“to eliminate, efface, live, survive by consumption”), German <i>zehren</i> (“to consume, misuse”), German <i>zerren</i> (“to tug, rip, tear”), Danish <i>tære</i> (“to consume”), Swedish <i>tära</i> (“to fret, consume, deplete, use up”), Icelandic <i>tæra</i> (“to clear, corrode”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Binding together, setting of stones, tying in bundles.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Complication of a dramatic plot.</li><li>synonym of <i>δέσμη</i> (désmē), probably of a belt purse.</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) A joint.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind, tie, fasten”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bond or fetter</li></ol><p>Poetic form of <i>δεσμός</i> (desmós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lady, mistress</li><li>princess, queen</li><li>owneress</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;déspoňňa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déms pótnih₂</i> (“lady of the house”). Cognate with Avestan <i>𐬛𐬆𐬨𐬄𐬥𐬋.𐬞𐬀𐬚𐬥𐬍</i> (dəmąnō.paθnī, “mistress, housekeeper”) and Persian بانو (bânu, “lady”). Female counterpart of <i>δεσπότης</i> (despótēs, “lord”). By surface analysis, <i>δεσπότης</i> (despótēs) +&lrm; <i>-ινα</i> (-ina).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to soften, knead with the hands</li></ol><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to masturbate</li></ol></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin, if it is indeed related to <i>διφθέρα</i> (diphthéra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accept, receive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱ-</i> (“to take, accept”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind, tie, fasten, fetter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to tie onto oneself</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to hinder from</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to brace</li></ol><li>to lack, need, require [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>with <i>πολλοῦ</i> (polloû) to be far from being able to do [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>participle <i>δέων</i> (déōn)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>modifying a noun, with genitive of the number by which something is less than another thing: lacking a number: a number less than something, something minus a number; often used to express numbers ending in 8 or 9</li><li>with both the participle and the number in the genitive&#58; genitive absolute: with a number lacking, a number less than</li></ol></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;never impersonal,&#32;transitive) to lack, not have, or need [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to need [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be in need</li><li>(<i>ditransitive</i>) to beg [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> genitive <small>‘from someone’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><li>alternative form of <i>δήω</i> (dḗō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;déyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dh₁yéti</i> (“to bind”), from <i>&#42;deh₁-</i> (“to bind”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>द्यति</i> (dyati), Avestan <i>𐬥𐬍𐬛𐬌𐬌𐬁𐬙𐬄𐬨</i> (nīdiiātąm) and Albanian <i>duaj</i>.</p><p>Apparently from a Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dew(s)-</i> (“to miss, want, need”) (without the <i>&#42;-s-</i> if the earlier form was <i>&#42;δέϝω</i> (&#42;déwō), with the <i>&#42;-s-</i> if the earlier form was <i>&#42;δεύσ-ω</i> (&#42;deús-ō)). In the latter case, perhaps cognate with Sanskrit <i>दोष</i> (doṣa, “fault, deficiency”). See also <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “dys-, bad”), <i>δεῖ</i> (deî, “one must”). If the root had no <i>*-s-</i>, compare perhaps Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teuną</i> (“lack; damage, destruction”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Adds temporal specificity: now, already</li><li>Adds emphasis: truly, !, indeed, in truth</li><li>Adds specificity: exactly</li><li>Sometimes ironical: no doubt, of course</li><li>With pronouns: of all people</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>but, and</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dḗ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;de</i> (“instrumental particle”). See also <i>δέ</i> (dé).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δηθά</i> (dēthá)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A biter.</li></ol><p>From <i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō, “to bite”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er”, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Clearly, obviously</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆλος</i> (dêlos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>explanation</li><li>representation</li></ol><p><i>δηλόω</i> (dēlóō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>public, belonging to the people</li><li>(<i>masculine substantive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>public executioner</li><li>public physician</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “the people”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a long time</li><li>long ago</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dwān</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dweh₂-m</i>, from the root <i>&#42;dweh₂-</i> (“long, far”). Related to <i>δηρός</i> (dērós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only in plural</i>) counsels, plans, arts, whether good or bad</li></ol><p>Brugmann assumed <i>&#42;δάνσεα</i> (&#42;dánsea), with analogical <i>α</i> from <i>δαῆναι</i> (daênai, “to learn”), <i>δαΐφρων</i> (daḯphrōn, “wise, prudent”) for older <i>&#42;δένσεα</i> (&#42;dénsea) and compares Sanskrit <i>दंसन</i> (daṃsana, “wonderful deed”), suggesting a Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;densos-</i> (“counsel, skill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm in wood</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;denḱ-</i> (“to bite”), the same root of <i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō, “to bite”), with the influence of <i>σφήξ</i> (sphḗx, “wasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>perhaps, it may be</li><li>doubtless, I presume</li><li>surely, obviously</li></ol><p>From the combination of <i>δή</i> and <i>που.</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find, reach, attain</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>δᾱ́ῐ̈ος</i> (dā́ĭ̈os)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way of living, way of life, mode of life, lifestyle</li><li>accommodation, residence</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>dwelling, abode</li><li>refuge, retreat, lair of an animal</li><li>a room (separate part of a building, enclosed by walls, a floor, and a ceiling)</li></ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) prescribed manner of life, health regimen</li><li>state, condition, situation</li><li>sustenance, food</li><li>(<i>at Athens and elsewhere</i>) arbitration</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the office of arbiter</li></ol><li>discussion, investigation, enquiry, research</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dwís</td></tr></table></p><p>Nominal formation from <i>δῐαιτᾰ́ω</i> (dĭaitắō, “to treat, handle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digamma, the name for the sixth letter Ϝ, ϝ of the archaic Ancient Greek alphabet, pronounced /w/, which fell out of use before the Classical period of Ancient Greek.</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-, “double”) +&lrm; <i>γάμμα</i> (gámma, “gamma”), due to resembling two gammas stacked on top of each other.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double, twofold</li><li>twin</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) twins</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Gemini</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;in the plural) testicles</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;in the plural) ovaries</li></ol><p>Reduplication from <i>δύο</i> (dúo, “two”), with a suffix <i>-μος</i> (-mos); compare <i>ἀμφίδυμος</i> (amphídumos, “double”). Analogical formations like <i>τρίδυμος</i> (trídumos) show that this word was connected with <i>δίς</i> (dís, “twice”), at least at a later date.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give; to present; to offer; to provide</li><li>to grant; to allow; to permit</li><li>(<i>perfect</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to allow</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be allowed</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dédeh₃ti</i>, reduplicated present of <i>&#42;deh₃-</i> (“to give”). Cognates include Latin <i>dō</i>, Sanskrit <i>ददा॑ति</i> (dádāti), Old Persian <i>𐎭𐎭𐎠𐎬𐎢𐎺</i> (d-d-a-tu-u-v /⁠dadātuv⁠/) and Old Armenian <i>տամ</i> (tam).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she-goat</li></ol><p>Possibly borrowed from a Paleo-Balkan language, from a tentative Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déygʰ-ih₂ ~ digʰ-yéh₂-s</i> often connected to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;tigā</i> (“she-goat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) to seek out, look for</li></ol><p>Reduplicated present from the root of <i>ζητέω</i> (zētéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>observant of custom, orderly, civilized</li><li>righteous</li><li>equal, even, balanced</li><li>exact, specific</li><li>lawful, just, right</li><li>fitting, normal</li><li>real, genuine</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́κη</i> (dĭ́kē, “custom, right”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δίκαιος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a son of Poseidon and brother of Syleus</li><li>(more fully <i>Δίκαιος ὁ Θεοκύδεος</i> (Díkaios ho Theokúdeos), “Dicaeus, the son of Theocydes”) an Athenian exile and supporter of the Medes during the Second Greco-Persian War (480–479&nbsp;<small>B.C.E.</small>) who foretold the destruction of Xerxes’ fleet at the Battle of Salamis (480&nbsp;<small>B.C.E.</small>)</li><li>(more fully <i>Δίκαιος ἐκ Ταρσοῦ</i> (Díkaios ek Tarsoû), “Dicaeus of Tarsus”) a pupil of Panaetius</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́καιος</i> (dĭ́kaios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-pronged fork, hoe</li><li>adze, pickaxe, mattock</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>μάκελλα</i> (mákella, “mattock”). Since this word is from Pre-Greek, also <i>δίκελλα</i> (díkella) should be too.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custom, manner, fashion</li><li>order, law, right</li><li>judgment, justice</li><li>lawsuit, trial</li><li>punishment, penalty, vengeance, satisfaction</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;díkā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;díḱeh₂</i>, from the root <i>&#42;deyḱ-</i> (“to point, show”). Doublet of <i>δείκνῡμῐ</i> (deíknūmĭ, “to show”). Compare Sanskrit <i>दिशा॑</i> (díśā).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δίκη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) Dike</li></ol><p>From <i>δίκη</i> (díkē, “justice, custom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forked, cloven</li></ol><p>The adjective goes back to <i>&#42;δί-κροϝ-ος</i> (&#42;dí-krow-os), from <i>δῐ-</i> (dĭ-) + a word for "horn". Nussbaum assumes that there were forms without laryngeal, like Sanskrit <i>शृङ्ग</i> (śṛṅga, “horn, tusk”) and <i>κάρνος</i> (kárnos, “cattle”), to explain the discrepancy between this word and <i>κέρας</i> (kéras, “horn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Double-beating.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ships</i>) With only two banks of oars manned.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>Late Greek</i>) synonym of <i>δῐήρης</i> (dĭḗrēs)</li></ol><li>δίκροτος ἁμαξῐτός: (<i>road transport</i>) For two carriages.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δίς</i> (dís, “doubly”) +&lrm; <i>κρότος</i> (krótos, “rattling noise, beat, snapping, clash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dittany (<i>Origanum dictamnus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin, or from <i>Δίκτη</i> (Díktē) (the name of a mountain of Crete).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing net</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a <i>u</i>-stem formation of <i>δικεῖν</i> (dikeîn, “to throw”). Beekes expresses mild doubts about this, but concludes that the word is likely Pre-Greek regardless, due to the older form of Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀆𐀓𐀶𐀺𐀒</i> (de-ku-tu-wo-ko (dektuworgos), “net-maker”) demonstrating ε-ι variation in the first vowel. See also <i>δίσκος</i> (dískos, “discus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a conclusion from two premises</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-, “twice”) +&lrm; <i>λῆμμα</i> (lêmma, “premise, assumption”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insincere, questionable, doubtful, unfaithful, double-tongued, of double language</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dimity</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>δίμιτος</i> (dímitos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of double thread</li></ol><p>From <i>δίς</i> (dís) +&lrm; <i>μίτος</i> (mítos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having two shapes or forms</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-) +&lrm; <i>-μορφος</i> (-morphos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirlpool, eddy</li></ol><p>In view of the lack of a good etymology, Beekes considers the possibility of a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passage, way through, thoroughfare</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way, path”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commander</li><li>captain of a ship</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having two holes</li></ol><p>From <i>διέπω</i> (diépō, “I administer”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, noun suffix).</p><p><i>δι-</i> (di-, “two”) +&lrm; <i>ὀπή</i> (opḗ, “hole”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a view through (something)</li><li>examination</li><li>transparency</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>ὄψις</i> (ópsis, “view”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) twofold; double; in double folds</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a double-folded mantle</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ship-planks (doubled one over the one below)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwi-pleḱ-s</i>; compare Latin <i>duplex</i>. By surface analysis, <i>δῐ-</i> (dĭ-) +&lrm; <i>-πλᾰξ</i> (-plăx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something double</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the parallel streams of the Milky Way</li><li>the fetal position</li></ol><li>folded paper</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>letter of recommendation, passport, order enabling a traveller to use the public post, receipt of payment of fees or taxes</li><li>duplicate, counterpart</li></ol><li>double pot, for boiling unguents</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐπλόω</i> (dĭplóō, “I double”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a double city</li></ol><p><i>δι-</i> (di-) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>entomology</i>) Two-winged.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of temples</i>) With double peristyle.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-, “two”) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “feather, wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a double fold</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-folded, doubled</li><li>twofold</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-) +&lrm; <i>πτυχή</i> (ptukhḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-folded, doubled</li><li>twofold</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-) +&lrm; <i>πτυχή</i> (ptukhḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>twice; doubly; again</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>δϝῐ́ς</i> (dwĭ́s), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dwís</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwís</i>; related to <i>δῠ́ο</i> (dŭ́o, “two”). Cognates include the Sanskrit <i>द्विस्</i> (dvís) and the Latin <i>bis</i> and <i>dis-</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δίς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>rare form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quoit, disc, discus</li><li>dish, trencher</li><li>round mirror</li><li>reliquary</li><li>marigold (<i>Calendula arvensis</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée pointed out that the traditional derivation from <i>&#42;δίκ-σκος</i> (&#42;dík-skos), from <i>δικεῖν</i> (dikeîn, “to cast”) is hardly possible, because the suffix “-σκο-” is rare in nominal derivation. Therefore, he suggested to start from <i>&#42;δισκ-</i> (&#42;disk-), a variant (as opposed to direct formation) of <i>δικεῖν</i> (dikeîn), which has Pre-Greek origin. See also <i>δίκτυον</i> (díktuon, “fishing net; strick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of two verses</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) distich</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-, “two”) +&lrm; <i>στίχος</i> (stíkhos, “row, line, verse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;phonology,&#32;orthography) a diphthong</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with two sounds</li><li>(phonology,&#32;orthography) diphthongal (pertaining to a diphthong)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;phonology,&#32;orthography) a diphthong</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-, “two”) +&lrm; <i>φθόγγος</i> (phthóngos, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chariot-board (on which the warrior and the driver stood)</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>chariot; travelling car; litter</li><li>seat; couch; stool</li><li>royal throne</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>δῐ-</i> (dĭ-, “two”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in two, asunder, apart</li><li>in doubt</li><li>differently, oppositely</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apart from, without, away from</li><li>unlike</li></ol><p>From <i>δίς</i> (dís, “twice”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thirst</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek, based on an analysis <i>δίπ-σα</i> (díp-sa) lacking clear semantic and morphological correspondences in other Indo-European branches, as well as the existence of variants with <i>φ</i> and <i>β</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) diabetes, attended with violent thirst</li><li>teasel (<i>Dipsacus fullonum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δίψᾰ</i> (dípsă, “thirst”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰκος</i> (-ăkos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run away; to take to flight; to flee; to speed</li><li>to be afraid; to fear</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to drive away; to chase; to put to flight</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to pursue; to give chase</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deyh₁-</i> (“to chase”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fear, to dread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dedwóye</i> (“to be afraid”), from <i>&#42;dwey-</i> (“to fear”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) heavenly; divine; noble (<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of persons and gods</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;díwiyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;diwyós</i> (“heavenly”), from <i>&#42;dyew-</i> (“sky”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yós</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दि॒व्य</i> (divyá), Latin <i>dīus</i>, and related to <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”), Latin <i>deus</i> (“god”), and the first element of English <i>Tuesday</i>. The Indo-Iranian and Greek terms (which are formally identical) may alternatively be independently formed to the root <i>&#42;dyew-</i>.</p><p>The feminine nominative/accusative/vocative <i>δῖᾰ</i> (dîă) was earliest used in the substantive function "goddess", and is similar in formation and meaning to Sanskrit <i>दे॒वी</i> (devī́, “goddess; celestial”), though it is likely formed independently to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deywós</i> (“god”), and thus not inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déywih₂</i> (“goddess”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) heavenly; divine; noble (<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of persons and gods</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;díwiyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;diwyós</i> (“heavenly”), from <i>&#42;dyew-</i> (“sky”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yós</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दि॒व्य</i> (divyá), Latin <i>dīus</i>, and related to <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”), Latin <i>deus</i> (“god”), and the first element of English <i>Tuesday</i>. The Indo-Iranian and Greek terms (which are formally identical) may alternatively be independently formed to the root <i>&#42;dyew-</i>.</p><p>The feminine nominative/accusative/vocative <i>δῖᾰ</i> (dîă) was earliest used in the substantive function "goddess", and is similar in formation and meaning to Sanskrit <i>दे॒वी</i> (devī́, “goddess; celestial”), though it is likely formed independently to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deywós</i> (“god”), and thus not inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déywih₂</i> (“goddess”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δίων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Dion</li><li>Dion of Syracuse</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chase, pursuit</li><li>(<i>law</i>) prosecution</li></ol><p>From <i>διώκω</i> (diṓkō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide</li><li>to rend, tear</li><li>to cleave (asunder)</li><li>to slay, smite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂y-</i> (“share, divide”) + <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pinewood</li><li>torch</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>δαίω</i> (daíō, “to kindle”), from earlier <i>&#42;δᾰϝῐ́ω</i> (&#42;dăwĭ́ō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂w-</i> (“to burn”), related to Old High German <i>zuscen</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warlike, fiery</li><li>wise, prudent</li></ol><p>From <i>δάϊς</i> (dáïs, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn), combining form of <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “mind”).</p><p>From <i>ἐδάην</i> (edáēn, “to learn”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn), combining form of <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband's brother, brother-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dayh₂wḗr</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>देवृ</i> (devṛ́), Lithuanian <i>dieveris</i>, Latin <i>lēvir</i> and Old Armenian <i>տայգր</i> (taygr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>δή</i> (dḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cunningly wrought</li></ol><p>From <i>δαιδάλλω</i> (daidállō, “work, craft”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delh₁-</i> (“to hew, to split”, verbal root). However, according to Beekes, both words are from Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δαίδαλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Daedalus</li></ol><p>From <i>δαιδάλλω</i> (daidállō, “work, craft”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delh₁-</i> (“to hew, to split”, verbal root). However, according to Beekes, both words are from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>god, goddess demigod</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>divine power, deity</li><li>guardian spirit (Latin <i>genius</i>), and so one's fate, destiny, fortune</li></ol><li>departed soul</li><li>(ecclesiastical,&#32;biblical) demon, evil spirit</li></ol><p>From <i>δαίομαι</i> (daíomai, “to divide”) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂y-</i> (“to share, to divide”). For similar semantic development compare Old Persian <i>𐎲𐎥</i> (b-g /⁠baga⁠/, “god”), Sanskrit <i>भग</i> (bhága, “dispenser, patron”) (usually applied to gods) beside Avestan <i>𐬠𐬀𐬔𐬀-</i> (baga-, “part”) and Sanskrit <i>भजति</i> (bhájati, “to divide, apportion”). Compare the etymology of English <i>time</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to give a feast, host a feast</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to partake in a feast</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂y-</i> (“to share, divide”) +&lrm; <i>-νῡμι</i> (-nūmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δαίνυντο</i> (daínunto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast, banquet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂i-t-s</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to light up, to kindle, to set on fire</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to burn, to blaze</li><li>(transitive,&#32;usually&#32;middle voice) to divide, to share</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to host (<i>a feast</i>)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be torn, to ache</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dáyyō</i>; ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂w-</i>. The linguist Douglas Adams suggests that the term derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dh₂u-yé-ti</i>. However, it may also be derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déh₂u-ye-ti</i>. Cognates include Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;dauyeti</i>, Tocharian A <i>twās</i>; perhaps related to Latin <i>duellum</i>, <i>bellum</i> (“war”). Perhaps etymologically related to dagger and Sanskrit <i>दु॒नोति॑</i> (dunóti).</p><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂y-</i> (“to share”). Compare Sanskrit <i>दयते</i> (dayate).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Koine and Byzantine form of <i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō, “to bite”)</li></ol><p>Hellenistic Koine and Medieval from <i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;denḱ-</i>. Based on the <i>aorist</i> stem <i>ἔδακον</i> (édakon) with voicing the [k] between vowels.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be distracted</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>Since Roscher-Osthoff, the word has been connected with Latin <i>dūcō</i> (“to lead, guide, conduct”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tugōną</i> (“to pull, tow”), suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewk-</i> (“to pull, lead”), through an intensive reduplication </i>*δαι-δυκ-ιο-<i>. However, different from that tradition, DELG notes that the word has no etymology. Indeed, if </i>δα-<i> is not a late notation for </i>δαι-<i>, the variation may point to Pre-Greek origin.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cunningly wrought</li></ol><p>From <i>δαιδάλλω</i> (daidállō, “work, craft”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delh₁-</i> (“to hew, to split”, verbal root). However, according to Beekes, both words are from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to work cunningly, embellish, adorn</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be spotted or marked</li></ol><p>The relation between this verb and the adjective <i>δαίδᾰλος</i> (daídălos, “curiously wrought”) is debated. Leumann started from a Mediterranean word <i>&#42;δαίδᾰλον</i> (&#42;daídălon, “ornament”), from which this verb and the compound <i>πολῠδαίδᾰλος</i> (polŭdaídălos, “rich in ornament”) would be derived. Others have taken the verb, which was thought to be an intensive reduplicated formation, as the starting point. Within Greek <i>δέλτος</i> (déltos, “writing tablet”) and <i>δηλέομαι</i> (dēléomai, “to hurt, damage, spoil, waste”) have been compared; further <i>δάλλει</i> (dállei, “to do wrong”) and <i>δόλων</i> (dólōn, “poniard, stiletto”). Not related are several words for "build, split" in other Indo-European languages, like Latin <i>dolō</i> (“to hew”) and Old Irish <i>delb</i> (“form, shape, appearance, statue”). Instead, Beekes considers Pre-Greek origin, for instance through a connection with the personal name <i>Δαίδαλος</i> (Daídalos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>demonic, devilish</li></ol><p>From <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>possessed or sent by a daimon</li></ol><p>From <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δαιμόνιε</i> (daimónie)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the divine Power, the Deity, the Divinity</li><li>an inferior divine being, demon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the name by which Socrates calls his genius, or the spirit that dwelt within him</li><li>a demon, evil spirit</li></ol></ol><p>Neuter gender of <i>δαιμόνῐος</i> (daimónĭos, “extraordinary, divine; pertaining to <i>daimones</i>”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) proceeding from an evil spirit, demon, see cacodemon.</li><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) strange, extraordinary, inscrutable; that is, resembling a δαίμων</li><li>proceeding from a deity, heaven-sent, divine, miraculous, marvelous</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>superhuman (said of strength, wisdom, etc.)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn, “god, divine power, evil spirit”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art of carving meat into portions</li></ol><p>From <i>δαιτρός</i> (daitrós, “one that carves meat”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one that carves and portions out meat at table</li><li>hereditary priest who officiated at the Dipolia</li></ol><p>From <i>δαίω</i> (daíō, “to divide”) +&lrm; <i>-τρος</i> (-tros, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one that is entertained, an invited guest</li></ol><p>From <i>δαίς</i> (daís, “feast, banquet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tearful (crying or about to cry)</li><li>causing tears, sorrowful</li></ol><p>From <i>δάκρυον</i> (dákruon) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cry; to weep; to shed tears</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of eyes) to run</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of trees) to exude tree sap</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cry or weep for (someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰ́κρῠ</i> (dắkrŭ). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A collection of gems</li><li>A ring case</li></ol><p>From <i>δακτύλιος</i> (daktúlios, “ring”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like a finger</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰ́κτῠλος</i> (dắktŭlos, “finger”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-form, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) first phalanx</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰ́κτῠλος</i> (dắktŭlos, “finger”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring, finger ring, signet ring</li></ol><p>From <i>δάκτυλος</i> (dáktulos, “finger”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tame, subdue, control</li><li>(<i>of women</i>) to give in marriage</li><li>(<i>of women</i>) to seduce or rape</li><li>to subdue, conquer, rule over</li><li>to kill</li><li>to overpower</li></ol><p>Thematicization of <i>δάμνημι</i> (dámnēmi, “to subdue”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>दाम्यति</i> (dāmyati), Latin <i>domō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tamer, subduer</li><li>young steer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;demh₂-</i> (“to domesticate, tame”) and cognate with Latin <i>domō</i> (“to tame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>δαμάζω</i> (damázō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;demh₂-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>दाम्यति</i> (dāmyati), Latin <i>domō</i>, and English <i>tame</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>δημοκρᾰτίᾱ</i> (dēmokrătíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a Persian coin used as an obol offered to corpses</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;dānakā̆-</i>, from Old Persian <i>&#42;dānā-</i> (“grain”). Compare Middle Persian (/⁠dānag⁠/) and Persian دانگ (dâng, “sixth of something”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moneylender or creditor</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰνείζω</i> (dăneízō, “to lend money”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cost, expenditure</li><li>money spent or for spending</li><li>extravagance, prodigality</li></ol><p>From <i>δάπτω</i> (dáptō, “to devour, consume”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend (on)</li><li>to consume</li><li>to waste</li></ol><p><i>δαπάνη</i> (dapánē) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat, devour, as wild beasts</li></ol><p>Intensive reduplicated formation with unclear <i>-ρ-</i>, probably connected to <i>δᾰ́πτω</i> (dắptō, “to devour, consume”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daric (a gold coin from the Persian Empire)</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;darikah</i>, by folk etymology shaped to mean <i>Δαρεῖος</i> (Dareîos, “King Darius”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, adjective suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δαρεικός)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>δᾱρεικός</i> (dāreikós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to sleep, fall asleep</li></ol><p>Probably from or related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;drem-</i> (“to sleep”), with the <i>-θ-</i> (-th-) being a secondary formant. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>द्राति</i> (drā́ti, “to sleep”), Latin <i>dormiō</i>, Old Armenian <i>տարտամ</i> (tartam) and Old Church Slavonic <i>дрємлю</i> (dremlju).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Flayed.</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, to flay”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;orthography) rough breathing (diacritical mark indicating aspiration)</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>δᾰσῠ́ς</i> (dăsŭ́s): as a noun, a substantivisation of its feminine forms, in elliptical usage for <i>ἡ δᾰσεῖᾰ προσῳδῐ́ᾱ</i> (hē dăseîă prosōidĭ́ā, “the aspiratory diacritic”); as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the hair (on the head)</li><li>a thicket</li><li>(Koine,&#32;phonetics,&#32;orthography):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a vowel with rough breathing: ἁ (ha), ἑ (he), ἡ (hē), ἱ (hi), ὁ (ho), ὑ (hu), ὡ (hō)</li><li>an aspirated consonant, φ (ph), θ (th), χ (kh), typically in contrast with the tenuis consonants π (p), τ (t), κ (k)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>woolly, having a thick fleece</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰσῠ́ς</i> (dăsŭ́s, “hairy, shaggy”) +&lrm; <i>μαλλός</i> (mallós, “flock of wool”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European hare (<i>Lepus europaeus</i>)</li><li>European rabbit (<i>Oryctolagus cuniculus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰσῠ́ς</i> (dăsŭ́s, “hairy, shaggy”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hairy, shaggy</li><li>(<i>of birds, animals, etc.</i>) downy</li><li>(<i>of shields of skin</i>) with the hair on</li><li>(<i>of places</i>) thickly wooded, bushy</li><li>(<i>of terrain</i>) rough</li><li>(<i>of urine</i>) cloudy</li><li>hoarse</li><li>(phonetics,&#32;of a vowel) with rough breathing, as in ἁ (ha), ἑ (he), ἡ (hē), ἱ (hi), ὁ (ho), ὑ (hu), ὡ (hō)</li><li>(phonetics,&#32;of a consonant) aspirated, as in φ (ph), θ (th), χ (kh)</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dn̥s-</i>, zero-grade of <i>&#42;dens-</i> (“thick, dense”), and cognate with Latin <i>dēnsus</i> (“dense”), Hittite (daššu-, “heavy, strong”), though this depends on whether <i>&#42;-n̥s-</i> does yield <i>&#42;-σ-</i> (&#42;-s-) in Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having a hairy, shaggy chest.</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰσῠ́ς</i> (dăsŭ́s, “hairy, shaggy”) +&lrm; <i>στέρνον</i> (stérnon, “chest, breast”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roughness, hairiness</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) aspiration</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰσῠ́ς</i> (dăsŭ́s, “shaggy”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs). Compare the similar formation of Latin <i>dēnsitās</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>phonology</i>) rough breathing; the sound /h/</li></ol><p><i>δασύς</i> (dasús, “hairy, wooded, rough”) +&lrm; <i>πνεῦμα</i> (pneûma, “breath”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide into portions</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>δαίω</i> (daíō, “to divide, share”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spurge-laurel (<i>Daphne laureola</i>)</li><li>periwinkle (<i>Vinca herbacea</i>)</li><li>kind of cassia resembling the laurel</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰ́φνη</i> (dắphnē, “laurel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blood red, reddish, tawny</li><li>bloodstained, covered in blood</li><li>bloodthirsty, bloodlusty</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾰ-</i> (dă-, “very”) +&lrm; <i>φοινός</i> (phoinós, “blood red”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abundant, plentiful, copious</li><li>(<i>of space</i>) ample, wide</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) liberal, profuse</li></ol><p>From the aorist stem of <i>δάπτω</i> (dáptō, “to devour, consume”), with a suffix in <i>-ιλ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δαῖτα</i> (daîta)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of several umbelliferous plants</li><li>An Attic measure for liquids</li></ol><p>Has been connected with <i>δαίω</i> (daíō, “to kindle, burn”), because of the burning taste of the root, and with <i>δάφνη</i> (dáphnē, “laurel”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fear, to dread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dedwóye</i> (“to be afraid”), from <i>&#42;dwey-</i> (“to fear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, point out [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>to bring to light, display, portray, represent</li><li>to make known, explain, teach, prove</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;deiknéumi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deyḱ-</i> (“to show, point out”) +&lrm; <i>-νῡμῐ</i> (-nūmĭ), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-néwti</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>dīcō</i>, Sanskrit <i>दिशति</i> (diśáti) and Old English <i>tǣċan</i> (English <i>teach</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δειλέ</i> (deilé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afternoon</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dyḗws</i> (“heaven, sky”), thus related to <i>δῆλος</i> (dêlos, “clear, visible”) and cognate to Latin <i>dies</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Locrian and Delphic form of <i>βούλομαι</i> (boúlomai)</li><li>to verge towards the afternoon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the sun</i>) to wester</li></ol></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷel(h₃)-</i> (“to want, wish”) or <i>&#42;gʷelH-</i>.</p><p>From <i>δείλη</i> (deílē) +&lrm; <i>-ομαι</i> (-omai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Homeric form of <i>ἐδεδειπνήκειν</i> (ededeipnḗkein)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrion crow (<i>Corvus corone</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to appear</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Koine)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to make an example of, expose, disgrace, make a show of</li><li>to furnish a sample; to prove</li><li>to test, make trial of</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δεῖγμα</i> (deîgma, “example”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>δείκνυμι</i> (deíknumi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(logic,&#32;of a syllogism) able to be reduced (to a simpler or clearer form)</li><li>(<i>of a statement</i>) categorical</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) demonstrative</li></ol><p>From <i>δεικ-</i> (deik-), the root of <i>δείκνῡμῐ</i> (deíknūmĭ, “show”), <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix): literally “able to show”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) cowardice</li><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) timidity</li></ol><p>From <i>δειλός</i> (deilós, “craven”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cowardly</li><li>wretched, sorrowful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwey-</i> (“to fear”). Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>δεινός</i> (deinós), Latin <i>dīrus</i>, Old Armenian <i>երկն</i> (erkn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) cowardice</li></ol><p>From <i>δειλός</i> (deilós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frighten</li></ol><p>From <i>δεῖμα</i> (deîma) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear, terror</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwey-mós</i>, from <i>&#42;dwey-</i> (“fear”). Compare <i>δέος</i> (déos), Latin <i>dirus</i>, Old Armenian <i>երկն</i> (erkn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible; horrible; fearful; astounding</li><li>marvelous; mighty; powerful</li><li>wondrous; strange</li><li>able; skillful; clever</li><li>shamefully timid; cowardly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dweynós</i>, from <i>&#42;dwey-</i> (“fear”); equivalent to <i>δει-</i> (dei-), the root of <i>δείδω</i> (deídō), +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos). Compare <i>δέος</i> (déos). Cognate with Latin <i>dirus</i>, Old Armenian <i>երկն</i> (erkn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible; horrible; fearful; astounding</li><li>marvelous; mighty; powerful</li><li>wondrous; strange</li><li>able; skillful; clever</li><li>shamefully timid; cowardly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dweynós</i>, from <i>&#42;dwey-</i> (“fear”); equivalent to <i>δει-</i> (dei-), the root of <i>δείδω</i> (deídō), +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos). Compare <i>δέος</i> (déos). Cognate with Latin <i>dirus</i>, Old Armenian <i>երկն</i> (erkn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>δεινός</i> (deinós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>terribly</li></ol><p><i>δεινός</i> (deinós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dine; to take the chief meal</li><li>to make or serve a meal</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to entertain</li></ol><p>From <i>δεῖπνον</i> (deîpnon) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poem on dining</li></ol><p>From <i>δεῖπνον</i> (deîpnon, “meal”) +&lrm; <i>-λογῐ́ᾱ</i> (-logĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deipnosophist</li></ol><p>From <i>δεῖπνον</i> (deîpnon, “meal”) + <i>σοφιστής</i> (sophistḗs, “teacher”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The ridge of a column of hills</li></ol><p>Disputed. Liddell and Scott propose Sanskrit <i>दृषद्</i> (dṛṣád, “millstone”) as a cognate. However, Pokorny argues for an origin from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷer-</i>, citing Sanskrit <i>गिरि</i> (giri) as a cognate. Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin, based on the suffix and lack of clear cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>δέρη</i> (dérē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superstitious</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) god-fearing: pious or superstitious.</li></ol><p>From <i>δείδω</i> (deídō) + <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) Decalogue, the Ten Commandments</li></ol><p>From <i>δέκα</i> (déka, “ten”) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos, “sentence, utterance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the number ten</li><li>a group of ten, decade (<i>i.e. ten of something, not necessarily years</i>)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>δέκᾰ</i> (dékă) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs); compare <i>δυάς</i> (duás), <i>τριάς</i> (triás), <i>τετράς</i> (tetrás), <i>χιλιάς</i> (khiliás), <i>μυριάς</i> (muriás). Formal similarities with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;desętь</i>, Lithuanian <i>dešimt</i>, Latvian <i>desmit</i> (dental suffix indicating collectivity).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Rome</i>) decanus, decurion</li><li>(<i>in Egypt</i>) police officer</li><li>(astrology,&#32;in the plural) decans</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>decānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>racecourse of ten stadia</li></ol><p>From <i>δέκᾰ</i> (dékă, “ten”) +&lrm; <i>στᾰ́δῐον</i> (stắdĭon, “stadium”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>τεσσᾰρεσκαίδεκᾰ</i> (tessăreskaídekă)</li></ol><p><i>δέκᾰ</i> (dékă, “ten”) +&lrm; <i>τέσσᾰρες</i> (téssăres, “four”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baited trap or noose</li></ol><p>From <i>δέλεαρ</i> (délear, “bait”) +&lrm; <i>-τρα</i> (-tra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a short piece of writing, a small book</li></ol><p><i>δέλτος</i> (déltos, “a writing”, <i>Byzantine:</i> “a book”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δῐον</i> (-ĭ́dĭon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>delta-shaped, triangular</li></ol><p>From <i>δέλτᾰ</i> (déltă, “delta”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-form, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>δελφῑ́ς</i> (delphī́s, “dolphin”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rocket larkspur (<i>Consolida ajacis</i>, syn. <i>Delphinium ajacis</i>)</li><li>forking larkspur (<i>Consolida regalis</i>, syn. <i>Delphinium consolida</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δελφῑ́ς</i> (delphī́s, “dolphin”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dolphin</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Delphinus, a constellation</li><li>mass of lead shaped like a dolphin, hung at the yardarm and suddenly let down on the decks of the enemy's ships</li><li>weight used to steady a ship under sail</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) stops in a machine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelbʰ-</i> (“womb”), with formation similar to <i>ᾰ̓κτῑ́ς</i> (ăktī́s), <i>γλωχῑ́ς</i> (glōkhī́s) and <i>ὠδῑ́ς</i> (ōdī́s), thus originally meaning "fish with a womb". Cognate with <i>δελφύς</i> (delphús, “womb”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δελφίς)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female inhabitant of Delphi; a female Delphian</li></ol><p>From Δελφοί (Delphoí, “Delphi”); feminine of Δελφός (Delphós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the womb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷelbʰ-</i> (“womb”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>गर्भ॑</i> (gárbha) and Avestan <i>𐬔𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬡𐬀</i> (gar<sup><small>ə</small></sup>βa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wink, blink</li><li>to turn the eyes, glance quickly</li></ol><p>Although this verb certainly has intensive reduplication, its etymology is unknown. The word has nothing to do with <i>δανδαίνω</i> (dandaínō, “to gaze, look intently”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of trees, arboreal</li><li>suitable for planting</li></ol><p>From <i>δένδρον</i> (déndron, “tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rosemary (<i>Salvia rosmarinus</i>, syn. <i>Rosmarinus officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δένδρον</i> (déndron, “tree”) +&lrm; <i>λίβανος</i> (líbanos, “frankincense”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree mallow (<i>Malva arborea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δένδρον</i> (déndron, “tree”) +&lrm; <i>μαλάχη</i> (malákhē, “mallow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>δεξιός</i> (dexiós, “right (opposite of left)”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;deksiterós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱs-i-terós</i>, from <i>&#42;deḱs-</i> (“right”); compare Latin <i>dexter</i>. By surface analysis, <i>δεξιός</i> (dexiós) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to greet with the right hand [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>δεξιᾱ́</i> (dexiā́, “the right hand”) +&lrm; <i>-όομαι</i> (-óomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>right (opposite of left)</li><li>fortunate</li><li>able</li><li>courteous</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) northward; northerly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;deksiwós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱs-</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दक्षिण</i> (dákṣiṇa) and Latin <i>dexter</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leathern, made of leather</li><li>cutaneous, dermic</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρμα</i> (dérma, “leather; skin, hide”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A case made of leather</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρμα</i> (dérma, “skin, leather”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clothed in skins</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρμᾰ</i> (dérmă, “skin, hide”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm which eats skin or leather</li></ol><p><i>δέρμᾰ</i> (dérmă, “skin”) +&lrm; <i>ἔδω</i> (édō, “eat”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jailer</li></ol><p>From <i>δεσμός</i> (desmós, “bonds; imprisonment”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prison, jail</li></ol><p>From <i>δεσμόω</i> (desmóō, “to bind”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bond; fetter</li><li>collar; halter</li><li>bondage; imprisonment</li><li>spell; charm</li></ol><p>From <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to one's master</li><li>despotic</li></ol><p>From <i>δεσπότης</i> (despótēs) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master, lord (most commonly in the context of one who owns slaves)</li><li>ruler, despot</li><li>generally: owner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;déspotis</i>, an original phrase from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déms pótis</i> (“master of the house”), from <i>&#42;dṓm</i> (“house”), whence also Ancient Greek <i>δόμος</i> (dómos, “house”); and <i>&#42;pótis</i> (“master”), whence also Ancient Greek <i>πόσις</i> (pósis, “husband”); with an ending influenced by <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine adjectival suffix). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दम्पति</i> (dám-pati, “lord of the house”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that may be tied, bound up</li></ol><p>From <i>δέω</i> (déō, “tie up”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, “a suffix that creates verbal adjectives of possibility, either active or passive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kettle, cauldron</li></ol><p>From <i>δεύω</i> (deúō, “to wet, drench; knead up”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) supporting actor, deuteragonist</li></ol><p>From <i>δεύτερος</i> (deúteros) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γωνῐστής</i> (ăgōnĭstḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) repetition of the law, a copy of the law</li></ol><p>From <i>δεύτερος</i> (deúteros, “second”) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion). Translates Biblical Hebrew <i>מִשְׁנֵה הַתּוֹרָה</i> (mišnê hattôrâ).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δευτερονόμιον)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Koine) Deuteronomy</li></ol><p>Proper use of <i>δευτερονόμιον</i> (deuteronómion, “repetition of the law”), from <i>δεύτερος</i> (deúteros, “second”) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>second of two</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>conjoined with <i>εἷς</i> (heîs) or other numerals: another, a second</li></ol><li>(<i>of things set in order</i>) second, next, another</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>modifying a noun of time</i>) after [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>], next</li><li>(<i>connected with ranking</i>) next best [<i>with</i> <b>μετά</b> (metá, + accusative) <small>‘after something or someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>of quality</i>) inferior</li><li>less important, secondary [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘than, to something or someone’</small>]</li><li><i>δεύτερος οὐδενός</i> (deúteros oudenós): second to none</li></ol></ol><p>Uncertain. Traditionally viewed as a <i>δύο</i> (dúo, “two”) + <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, comparative suffix), but possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dews-</i>, the root of <i>δεύομαι</i> (deúomai, “to be inferior”) (for which see <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to lack”)). Beekes cites a hypothesis by Cornelis Ruijgh that it is based on an adverbial stem <i>&#42;δευ</i> (&#42;deu) found also in <i>δεῦτε</i> (deûte), <i>δεῦρο</i> (deûro, “come here!”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet, moisten, drench</li><li>to stir liquid with dry mass</li><li>to make flow</li><li>Epic and Aeolic form of <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to lack, need, require”)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes suggests that the term may be compared with <i>διαίνω</i> (diaínō) and <i>δύω</i> (dúō). The linguist Daniel Kölligan suggests that the term may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déwH-ye-ti</i>, which may be compared to Old Armenian <i>եներկ</i> (enerk), the aorist of <i>ներկանեմ</i> (nerkanem). However, the Armenian term is probably unrelated.</p><p>From the same source as <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to lack”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with personal accusative and infinitive</i>) it behoves one to, it is necessary to, one must</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with impersonal accusative and infinitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>when used in absolute an infinitive may be supplied</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>with impersonal genitive</i>) there is need of, there is wanting</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with a personal dative</i>)</li><li>(<i>with personal accusative</i>)</li><li>(nominative,&#32;rare)</li></ol><li>(neuter participle,&#32;absolute use) it being needful, fitting</li></ol><p>Impersonal forms of <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>specimen, sample</li><li>pattern</li><li>a specific place in the harbors where merchants displayed samples of their goods for sale. The samples themselves were also called deigmata.</li></ol><p>From the root <i>δεικ-</i> (deik-), whence also <i>δείκνῡμῐ</i> (deíknūmĭ, “I show”), +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, object noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>as an emotion</i>) fear, terror, horror, fright</li><li>(<i>as the object of fear</i>) terror, horror</li></ol><p>From <i>δείδω</i> (deídō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mode of proof</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>proof, specimen</li></ol><li>display, exhibition</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) demonstrative force, reference</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>δείκνυμι</i> (deíknumi, “to show, point out”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meal</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) food, provender</li></ol><p>Unknown, with Beekes suspecting Pre-Greek origin. Furnée assumes an earlier form <i>&#42;δαιπνον</i> (&#42;daipnon) and compares <i>δάπτω</i> (dáptō, “to consume”), Latin <i>daps</i> (“sacrificial banquet”) and <i>damnum</i> (“damage, injury”), suggesting derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂p-</i> (“to sacrifice”). Kluge compares Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tēwō</i> (“to regular, arrange (a meal)”), source of Gothic <i>𐍄𐌴𐍅𐌰</i> (tēwa), German <i>Zeche</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱ-</i> (“to fit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet (taste)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dlukús</i>, <i>&#42;dléukus</i>. On the basis of Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀆𐀩𐀄𐀒</i> (de-re-u-ko /⁠dleukos⁠/) and Latin <i>dulcis</i>; cognate with Ancient Greek <i>γλυκύς</i> (glukús, “sweet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is seethed, boiled</li></ol><p>From <i>δεύω</i> (deúō, “to wet, drench; stir”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δεῦρο</i> (deûro)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of locations</i>) hither</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>later</i>) here</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) up to this point of the argument</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) until now, hitherto</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with 2nd person singular imperative</i>) come on!</li><li>(<i>with 1st person plural subjunctive</i>) come let us...</li><li>(<i>without a verb</i>) come here!</li><li>(<i>later</i>) go away!</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>things nearby</li><li>(<i>metaphysics</i>) tangible objects; things of the material world</li></ol><p>Probably a combination of demonstrative Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;de</i>, whence also the enclitic <i>-δε</i> (-de, denoting motion towards), + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ure</i>, <i>&#42;uro</i> (“where”), whence Lithuanian <i>aurè</i>, Avestan <i>𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬭𐬀</i> (auuara), Old Armenian <i>ուր</i> (ur), Umbrian <i>𐌖𐌓𐌖</i> (uru). A Mycenaean Greek cognate may exist in the first element, *<i>𐀆𐀸𐀫</i> (de-we-ro), of <i>𐀆𐀸𐀫𐁁𐀒𐀨𐀂𐀊</i> (de-we-ro-a3-ko-ra-i-ja, “a part of the kingdom of Pylos”).</p><p>The expected proximal destination demonstrative reflex is <i>ἐνθάδε</i> (entháde), but it has become the proximal place one, leaving this word as the specifically destination one.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) for a long time</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dyew-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>diū</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to tarry, delay</li></ol><p>Related to <i>δηθά</i> (dēthá). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurt, damage, spoil, waste</li><li>to destroy</li></ol><p>Under the assumption of an original meaning 'split', this word was connected with <i>δαιδάλλω</i> (daidállō, “to adorn”) and <i>δέλτος</i> (déltos, “writing tablet”), presumed to be derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delh₁-</i> (“to hew, to split”). However, the</p><p>ablaut forms are incompatible, and long ā can hardly be derived from PIE but Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destroyer</li></ol><p>From <i>δηλέομαι</i> (dēléomai, “to destroy”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmful, damaging, destructive</li><li>mischievous</li></ol><p>From <i>δηλητήρ</i> (dēlētḗr, “destroyer”), from <i>δηλέομαι</i> (dēléomai, “to hurt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmful, poisonous, noxious</li></ol><p>From <i>δηλητήρῐον</i> (dēlētḗrĭon, “poison”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “like”), from <i>δηλητήρῐος</i> (dēlētḗrĭos, “noxious”), from <i>δηλέομαι</i> (dēléomai, “I destroy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>δῆλος</i> (dêlos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to show, to make apparent or known, clear</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆλος</i> (dêlos, “manifest”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leadership of the people</li></ol><p>From <i>δημαγωγός</i> (dēmagōgós) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for or like a demagogue</li><li>(in general,&#32;of a dancer) popular</li></ol><p>From <i>δημᾰγωγός</i> (dēmăgōgós, “demagogue”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a popular leader</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a bad sense</i>) a leader of the mob, an unprincipled, factious orator, demagogue</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γωγός</i> (ăgōgós, “leading, guiding”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>falsa lectio</i>) synonym of <i>δήμιον</i> (dḗmion, “the sovereign people”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practice a trade or handicraft, do work</li><li>to fabricate, create</li><li>to administer, be a civil official</li></ol><p>From <i>δημῐουργός</i> (dēmĭourgós, “craftsman”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a craftsman, artisanal</li><li>creative, tending to create things</li><li>of or for the magistrates</li></ol><p>From <i>δημῐουργός</i> (dēmĭourgós, “craftsman”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who works for the people, a skilled workman, handicraftsman</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the maker of the world</li></ol><li>(<i>in some Peloponnesian states</i>) magistrate</li></ol><p>From an earlier unattested <i>&#42;δημιο-ϝεργός</i> (&#42;dēmio-wergós), from a univerbation of <i>δήμια ἔργᾰ</i> (dḗmia érgă). By surface analysis, <i>δήμῐος</i> (dḗmĭos, “public”) +&lrm; <i>ουργός</i> (ourgós, “worker”); for the second element, compare <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “labor, work”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>confiscated by public authorities and put up for sale</li></ol><p>From <i>δήμιος</i> (dḗmios, “public”) +&lrm; <i>πρᾱτός</i> (prātós, “for sale”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devourer of the people (epithet of reproach)</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people, free citizens”) +&lrm; <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “to eat up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to have a democratic constitution</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) democratic principles prevail</li></ol><p>From <i>δημοκρατίᾱ</i> (dēmokratíā, “democracy, rule of the people”) (from <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “common people, assembly of the people”) + <i>-κρατίᾱ</i> (-kratíā, “power, rule”), from <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “rule, strength”)) + <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;uncountable) democracy, popular government</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) a democratic government</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “common people", "assembly of the people”) +&lrm; <i>-κρατία</i> (-kratía, “power”, “rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a democracy</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) favouring democracy or suited to democracy</li></ol><p>From <i>δημοκρᾰτῐ́ᾱ</i> (dēmokrătĭ́ā, “democracy”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mob-flatterer</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people, mob”) +&lrm; <i>κόλᾰξ</i> (kólăx, “flatterer, fawner”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mob-jackanapes, charlatan</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”) +&lrm; <i>πῐ́θηκος</i> (pĭ́thēkos, “ape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at public expense</li><li>by public consent</li><li>in public courts</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make public or common</li><li>to confiscate</li><li>to publish</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be manifested, displayed</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be produced as evidence</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be in the public service</li></ol><p>From <i>δημόσιος</i> (dēmósios, “public”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the people, common</li><li>of the populace, plebeian</li><li>on the popular or democratic side</li><li>affable, kind</li></ol><p>From <i>δημότης</i> (dēmótēs, “commoner, fellow citizen”), from <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “country, land”), + <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fat</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂mo-</i> (“oil, fluid”), from <i>&#42;deh₂-</i> (“to flow”), whence Old Armenian <i>տամուկ</i> (tamuk, “humid, moist”), Albanian <i>dhjamë</i> (“fat”), Sanskrit <i>दानु॑</i> (dā́nu, “fluid; drop”). Beekes is skeptical of the connection to the Albanian and Sanskrit (and doesn't mention Armenian), but does note Demiraj's theory that the terms may be from a shared non-Indo-European substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the state or people, public</li><li>one of the class of Demosioi, a publicly-owned slave</li></ol><p>From *δημότ<i>-ιος</i> (-ios) with <i>assibilation</i> of *τ &gt; σ, from <i>δημότης</i> (dēmótēs, “one of the people; fellow citizen”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commoner, plebeian</li><li>fellow citizen, member of the same deme</li></ol><p>From <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the people</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>popular, plebeian</li><li>vulgar, common</li></ol><li>public</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>given to or shared by all</li><li>known by everyone</li></ol></ol><p><i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a denarius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dēnārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long, too long</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dwārós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;duh₂rós</i>, <i>&#42;dweh₂rós</i> (whence Old Armenian <i>երկար</i> (erkar), Sanskrit <i>दूर</i> (dūrá, “far”), Latin <i>dūrus</i>, Avestan <i>𐬛𐬏𐬭𐬁𐬝</i> (dūrāt̰), <i>𐬛𐬏𐬌𐬭𐬉</i> (dūirē)), from <i>&#42;dweh₂-</i> (“long”) (whence <i>δήν</i> (dḗn), Latin <i>dūdum</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) conflict; combat</li></ol><p>From <i>δήῐ̈ος</i> (dḗĭ̈os, “hostile”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slay</li><li>to tear, rend, cleave</li><li>to lay waste to</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>διά</i> (diá)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Twice, double.</li><li>alternative form of <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-), before a vowel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dwi-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwi-</i>.</p><p>From <i>δῐᾰ́</i> (dĭắ, “through”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set apart, place separately</li><li>(active voice,&#32;middle voice) to separate, divide</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to stand apart, be divided</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be at variance</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to differ, be different</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a place</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>in a line</li><li>through</li><li>in the midst of, between</li><li>along</li><li>at intervals of, at every</li></ol><li>(<i>time</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>between</li><li>after</li><li>every (interval of time)</li></ol><li>(<i>causality</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>through, by</li><li>(attested from 1<sup>st</sup> century <small>BCE</small>) out of (materials from which something is made)</li></ol></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of a place,&#32;poetic) through, among</li><li>(<i>time</i>) during</li><li>(<i>causality</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>thanks to, by aid of</li><li>because of</li><li>for the sake of</li></ol></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>throughout</li></ol><p>From *<i>δισά</i> (disá), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwís</i> (“twice, in two”) (related to <i>δίς</i> (dís, “twice”) and <i>δύο</i> (dúo, “two”)). Cognates include Latin <i>dis-</i> and Gothic <i>𐌳𐌹𐍃-</i> (dis-).</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of comparison/extent. The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional and the PIE accusative of direct object.</p><p>Compare typologically English <i>between</i> (akin to <i>two</i>). Also compare Mongolian <i>хооронд</i> (xoorond) (akin to <i>хоёр</i> (xojor)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of slipper worn by women</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰβαίνω</i> (dĭăbaínō, “to walk with legs apart”) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slanderous; libellous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slanderer</li><li>The Devil (Biblical figure).</li></ol><p>From <i>διαβάλλω</i> (diabállō, “to slander”, from <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”)).</p><p>For the meaning development, compare Russian <i>кида́ла</i> (kidála), <i>кида́лово</i> (kidálovo) related to <i>кида́ть</i> (kidátʹ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distinguishing</li><li>means of distinguishing or discerning</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) diagnosis</li><li>power of discernment</li></ol><li>resolving, deciding</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(law,&#32;Christianity) examination, opinion, decision</li></ol></ol><p><i>διαγιγνώσκω</i> (diagignṓskō, “distinguish, discern”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), from <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “know”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is marked out in lines, plan, diagram.</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>γράμμα</i> (grámma), from <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “I write”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headband</li><li>diadem (especially that of the Persian kings), crown</li></ol><p>From <i>διαδέω</i> (diadéō, “to bind round”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>succeeding a person in a thing, aftercoming</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Diadochi, the generals of Alexander the Great</li><li>(<i>at Alexandria</i>) lowest grade of court officials</li><li>substitute, deputy</li><li>head of a school of philosophers</li><li>kind of gem mentioned by Pliny</li></ol><p>From <i>διαδέχομαι</i> (diadékhomai, “to succeed”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a way of living</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰζάω</i> (dĭăzáō, “live through”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girdle, drawers, loincloth</li><li>diaphragm</li></ol><p>From <i>διαζώννυμι</i> (diazṓnnumi) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>placing in order, arrangement</li><li>disposition or composition in a work of art (as opp. to <i>εὕρεσις</i> (heúresis))</li><li>(<i>oratory</i>) delivery</li><li>a disposition of property (= <i>διαθήκη</i> (diathḗkē)), will, testament</li><li>a disposing of, selling, sale</li><li>bodily state, condition</li><li>a disposition towards persons</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) state, condition</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) force, function, esp. of the voices of a verb</li></ol><p>From <i>διατίθημι</i> (diatíthēmi, “to arrange”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in a certain state, to be disposed or affected (<i>in a certain way</i>)</li><li>to be settled, fixed, or order</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to be apart from each other, be a distance apart</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>messenger, courier</li><li>servant</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) minister, deacon, deaconess (female deacon)</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-) + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kón-os</i>, from <i>&#42;ken-</i> (“to set oneself in motion”). The length of the <i>ᾱ</i> (ā) is explained by Brugmann and Boisacq as by analogy to long vowels that occurred in compounds where the second element started with a vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separation, dissolution</li></ol><p><i>διακρίνω</i> (diakrínō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) runner, guide (<i>epithet of Hermes</i>)</li></ol><p>Said by Autenrieth to be from <i>διώκω</i> (diṓkō, “to chase”) and not <i>διάγω</i> (diágō, “to carry over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intervening space, gap</li><li>interval, pause</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰλείπω</i> (dĭăleípō, “to cease”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma), the former component from <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through, dia-”) +&lrm; <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “to leave behind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discourse, conversation</li><li>manner of speech, language</li><li>dialect, accent</li></ol><p>From <i>διαλέγομαι</i> (dialégomai, “to discuss”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conversation, dialogue</li></ol><p>From <i>διαλέγω</i> (dialégō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free day, a day during a festival on which no events take place</li></ol><p>From <i>διαλείπω</i> (dialeípō, “to leave, remain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a separating or parting</li><li>an ending, cessation</li><li>a solution to a problem</li><li>refutation of an argument</li><li>discharge</li><li>deed of separation or divorce</li><li>division of inheritance</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰλῡ́ω</i> (dĭălū́ō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>releaser, coined as an epithet for Dionysius</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ́λῠσῐς</i> (dĭắlŭsĭs, “separating, parting”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, “a suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relaxed</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective of <i>δῐᾰλῡ́ω</i> (dĭălū́ō, “loose one from another, part asunder”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, “a suffix that creates perfective passive verbal adjectives”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diagonal of a parallelogram</li><li>hypotenuse</li><li>diameter of a circle, of other curves</li><li>axis of a cone</li><li>mitre square</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the diameter or diagonal of a parallelogram</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the diameter of a circle</li></ol><li>a rule for drawing the diameter</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “measure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the diameter or diagonal of a parallelogram</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the diameter of a circle</li></ol><li>a rule for drawing the diameter</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “measure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) two ways, in two</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>ἄνδιχα</i> (ándikha).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intention, purpose</li><li>process of thinking</li><li>capacity of thought: intelligence, understanding</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-, “across, through”) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia, abstract noun forming suffix). Compare <i>διανοέομαι</i> (dianoéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Stumble, slip; error; failure.</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>πτῶμᾰ</i> (ptômă, “fall”, noun), from <i>πίπτω</i> (píptō, “to fall”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, “result noun suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diarium, day-wage, day's allowance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>diārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sufficiency</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰρκής</i> (dĭărkḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(medicine,&#32;once fully as <i>κοιλίας διάρροια</i>) flowing through, diarrhoea</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰρρέω</i> (dĭărrhéō) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separation</li><li>distension</li><li>contrast</li><li>disagreement</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) divorce</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐῐ̈́στημῐ</i> (dĭĭ̈́stēmĭ, “separate, stand apart”) with stem as in <i>στᾰ́σῐς</i> (stắsĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An interval or distance</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disposition, arrangement</li><li>command</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰτᾰ́σσω</i> (dĭătắssō, “to appoint”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tension, dilatation</li><li>extension (of a fractured or dislocated limb)</li><li>stretching across</li><li>tension, exertion</li><li>contention, quarrel</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰτείνω</i> (dĭăteínō, “to stretch to the uttermost”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>various, different</li></ol><p>From <i>διαφέρω</i> (diaphérō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partition or barrier</li><li>lock in a canal</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) diaphragm, midriff</li><li>septum, the cartilage which divides the nostrils</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) soft palate</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) septum pellucidum</li></ol><p>From <i>διαφράσσω</i> (diaphrássō, “to divide, obstruct”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interwoven with gold</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) musical interlude</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “apart”) +&lrm; <i>ψᾰ́λμᾰ</i> (psắlmă, “psalm”); hence literally “apart from the psalm”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) out through</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ-</i> (dĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ἐκ</i> (ek).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) sailing across or through, passing across or through</li><li>(the act of) breaking the enemy's line in a sea fight</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>πλόος</i> (plóos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manage, administer, arrange</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to be engaged in</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>ἕπω</i> (hépō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go through, pass through</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to pass through, complete</li><li>(<i>of reports</i>) to go abroad, spread</li><li>(<i>of sensations, thoughts</i>) to shoot through one</li><li>to pass through and reach, to arrive at</li><li>to go through in detail, tell all through</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;of time) to pass, elapse</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “to come, go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narration, narrative</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a speech</i>) statement of a case</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῐηγέομαι</i> (dĭēgéomai, “set out in detail, describe”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>through, right through, across, over</li><li>in different directions</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of separation: asunder, apart</li><li>at variance, against</li><li>of mutual relation: one with another</li></ol><li>of preeminence</li><li>of completion: to the end, utterly, out</li><li>to add strength: thoroughly, out and out</li><li>of mixture: between, partly, somewhat</li></ol><p>Prefixing form of the preposition <i>διά</i> (diá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divisibility</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) dissection</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) venesection</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) surgical operation</li><li>(medicine,&#32;in the plural) wounds</li></ol><li>division, distribution</li><li>distinction</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) division into logical genus and species (<i>γένος (génos)</i> and <i>εἶδος (eîdos)</i>)</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) separation of the subject and predicate</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) the fallacy of division</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) division or distribution of an argument into its component subjects or subtopics (known formerly as "heads")</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the resolution of a diphthong into two syllables, diaeresis</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the resolution of one word into two</li><li>(<i>poetry</i>) division of a poetic line when the end of a word and a metrical foot coincide, diaeresis</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) transformation of a dividend yield</li><li>(<i>military</i>) division of troops in the Roman cohors</li><li>(<i>music</i>) division or separation between sounds, interval</li></ol><p>From <i>διαιρέω</i> (diairéō, “take apart, cleave, divide”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis), from <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “apart”) +&lrm; <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō, “take, grasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise up, lift up; (<i>figuratively</i>) to exaggerate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to rise, become prominent; to lift up oneself; to lift up (what is one’s own)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li></ol><li>to separate, remove</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><li>(by extension,&#32;rhetoric,&#32;διηρμένος) lofty, sublime</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;scilicet ἑαυτόν, etc.) to lift oneself over, cross</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-) +&lrm; <i>αἴρω</i> (aírō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) food, diet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sustenance, provisions</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́αιτᾰ</i> (dĭ́aită) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw over or across</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to pass over, cross</li></ol><li>to object, contradict, set at variance, make a quarrel between</li><li>to slander, libel</li><li>to deceive by false accounts</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “across”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pair of compasses (so called from its outstretched legs)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>carpenter's or stonemason's ruler</li></ol><li>siphon</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) diabetes</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰβαίνω</i> (dĭăbaínō, “to step across, pass over”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er, -or”, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stride, walk or stand with legs apart</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to step across, pass over</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to cross over</li><li>to bestride</li><li>to decide</li><li>to come home to, to affect</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “in different directions, apart, asunder; dia-”) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to walk, step”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calumny, slander, defamation</li></ol><p>From <i>διαβάλλω</i> (diabállō, “to calumniate”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slanderous</li><li>devilish</li></ol><p>From <i>διάβολος</i> (diábolos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go through; to pass; to go through life; to survive</li><li>to intervene; to elapse</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to become; to come into being”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distinguish, discern</li><li>to resolve, vote to do something</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to decide (<i>a suit</i>), give judgment</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to distinguish</li></ol><p>From <i>διάγνωσις</i> (diágnōsis) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós) and/or <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>γνωστικός</i> (gnōstikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decree, resolution</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>διαγιγνώσκω</i> (diagignṓskō) (from <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō)) + <i>-μη</i> (-mē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delineation, outline, scheme</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) plans, specifications of a building</li><li>table, syllabus</li><li>register, description of goods sold, list of articles</li><li>decree, ordinance</li><li>(<i>of a debt</i>) crossing out, cancelling, hence payment</li><li>certificate that such payment has been made</li><li>contract</li><li>levy, tax</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰγρᾰ́φω</i> (dĭăgrắphō, “to mark out lines, delineate”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>deponent</i>) to contend, struggle or fight against</li><li>to fight desperately, contend earnestly</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “across”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γωνῐ́ζομαι</i> (ăgōnĭ́zomai, “contend for a prize”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a great straining, intense effort</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰγωνῐ́ζομαι</i> (dĭăgōnĭ́zomai, “to contend desperately”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐσμός</i> (-ĭsmós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diagonal</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>γωνίᾱ</i> (gōníā) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to receive one from another</li><li>to succeed (follow in sequence), come after [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to relieve one another [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “to accept”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind round</li><li>to bind fast</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give judgment in a case</li><li>to hold inquiry</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “dia-, through-”) +&lrm; <i>δῐκᾰ́ζω</i> (dĭkắzō, “to judge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a suit to decide between claimants</li><li>a judicial decision or settlement</li></ol><p><i>διαδικάζω</i> (diadikázō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diadumenos, a form of Ancient Greek sculpture featuring a young man holding a knotted diadem</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind or gird all around</li><li>to gird oneself with a thing</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>ζώννυμι</i> (zṓnnumi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live through (time or circumstance), to live long, to live to an old age or to senectitude, to live to one's full lifespan, to outlive, to overlive, to survive</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-, “across, through”) +&lrm; <i>ζῶ</i> (zô, “to live”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>testament, will (legal document)</li><li>covenant</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰτῐ́θημῐ</i> (dĭătĭ́thēmĭ); also analyzable as <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affecting</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ́θεσῐς</i> (dĭắthesĭs, “a placing in order”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divider, distributor</li></ol><p>From <i>διαιρέω</i> (diairéō) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take apart, cleave in twain, divide</li><li>to divide</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) divide for themselves; divide among themselves</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to break up, <i>opposed to</i> συντιθέναι</li><li>to dispense</li></ol></ol><li>to distinguish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to determine, decide</li><li>to define expressly</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to interpret</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>logic</i>) to divide; divide a genus into its species</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to divide</li><li>to divide words, punctuate in reading; <i>in the language of the Grammarians</i>, resolve a diphthong or contracted form</li><li>to allocate revenues</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divided, separated</li><li>distributed</li><li>distinguishable</li></ol><p>From <i>διαιρέω</i> (diairéō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat (handle, deal with or behave towards in a specific way)</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to lead one’s life, live</li><li>to arbitrate, regulate</li><li>to reconcile</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;dwís</td></tr></table></p><p>From either <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) (per Bailly) or <i>δῐ-</i> (dĭ-, “twice, double”) (per Beekes) + <i>&#42;αἰτᾰ́ω</i> (&#42;aitắō), frequentative of <i>αἴνῠμαι</i> (aínŭmai, “to take”); compare <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō, “to ask for”). Some forms are augmented after the prefix as usual, i.e. <i>δῐῃ-</i> (dĭēi-), but due to the obscurity of the unprefixed verb others are augmented to <i>ἐδῐαι-</i> (edĭai-), treating the prefix as part of the root. Still other forms have a double augment in <i>ἐδῐῃ-</i> (edĭēi-) or in <i>δεδῐῃ-</i> (dedĭēi-) or a triple augment in <i>ἐδεδῐῃ-</i> (ededĭēi-). These augments are especially frequent in the prefixed verbs <i>ᾰ̓ποδῐαιτᾰ́ω</i> (ăpodĭaitắō), <i>ἐκδῐαιτᾰ́ω</i> (ekdĭaitắō), etc.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judge</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>διαιτάω</i> (diaitáō, “arbitrate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>δῐᾱκόσῐοι</i> (dĭākósĭoi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run a risk, make a desperate attempt, hazard all [<i>with</i> <b>πρός</b> (prós, + accusative); <i>or</i> <i>with</i> <b>ὑπέρ</b> (hupér) <i>or</i> <b>πρός</b> (prós) <i>or</i> <b>περί</b> (perí, + genitive)]</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>κινδῡνεύω</i> (kindūneúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry over or across</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to pass over, cross</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>κομίζω</i> (komízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait on, serve</li><li>to serve up, supply</li><li>to help</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) to serve as deacon</li></ol><p>From <i>διάκονος</i> (diákonos, “servant”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service</li><li>ministry</li><li>body of servants</li><li>instruments of service</li></ol><p>From <i>διάκονος</i> (diákonos, “servant”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gash, cleft</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>κοπή</i> (kopḗ)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide and marshal,</li><li>to muster in array</li><li>to regulate, set in order</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>κοσμέω</i> (kosméō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, part</li><li>to dissolve</li><li>to distinguish</li><li>to settle, decide</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to doubt, hesitate</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be distinguished</li></ol><p>From <i>διακρῑ́νω</i> (diakrī́nō) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>penetrating, piercing</li><li>distinctive</li><li>distinct</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “apart”) +&lrm; <i>κριτικός</i> (kritikós, “discerning”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide or disguise</li><li>to bury</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play at dice with</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “dia-, through-”) +&lrm; <i>κῠβεύω</i> (kŭbeúō, “to play dice, gamble”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two hundred</li></ol><p><i>δι-</i> (di-, “twice”) +&lrm; <i>-ακόσιοι</i> (-akósioi, “a hundred”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setting in order, regulation</li><li>the orderly arrangement of the Universe (<i>especially in the Pythagorean system</i>)</li><li>Stoic technical term, of the "new order" after <i>ἐκπύρωσις</i> (ekpúrōsis)</li><li>order, class of beings</li><li>ornamentation</li></ol><p>Stem διακοσμη- of verb <i>διακοσμέω</i> (diakosméō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to converse, hold a discussion, debate, argue</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) to practise dialectic, elicit conclusions</li><li>(<i>late</i>) to lecture, discourse</li><li>to speak articulately, write in prose, use a dialect or language</li><li>(euphemistic,&#32;Attic) to have intercourse</li><li>to have dealings (with someone)</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to say, to speak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choose, gather, pick (out), select</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intermit, delay, cease, stop; to leave an interval, gap, space between</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of time</i>) to leave an interval of time, to wait</li></ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be an interval</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stand at intervals, be discontinuous</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to die, perish</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>λείπω</i> (leípō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dialectic, discussion by question and answer, invented by Zeno of Elea</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰλεκτῐκός</i> (dĭălektĭkós). See also <i>dialectic</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) Conversational</li><li>Skilled in dialectic</li><li>Dialectical</li></ol><p>From <i>διαλέγομαι</i> (dialégomai) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, adjectival suffix), literally “related to conversation”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>computation</li><li>conversation</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰλέγω</i> (dĭălégō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to balance accounts</li><li>to consider</li><li>to discuss, dispute, converse, argue</li><li>to hold a circuit court for (a district)</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>λογῐ́ζομαι</i> (logĭ́zomai, “to count, reckon”), from <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>love-dissolving</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ́λῠσῐς</i> (dĭắlŭsĭs, “dissolving”) +&lrm; <i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “love”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>who must dissolve</li><li>what must be dissolved</li></ol><p><i>διαλύω</i> (dialúō, “dissolve”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, verbal adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissolver, breaker-up</li></ol><p>From <i>διαλύω</i> (dialúō, “dissolve”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “a suffix that creates agent nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to sever</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>destructive</li></ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) relaxing</li><li>embodying a settlement or compromise</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰλῡ́ω</i> (dĭălū́ō, “loose one from another, part asunder”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable of dissolution</li><li>destructible, perishable</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰλῡ́ω</i> (dĭălū́ō, “loose one from another, part asunder”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, “a suffix that creates verbal adjectives of possibility, either active or passive”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissolving, undoing, parting asunder</li></ol><p>From <i>διαλύω</i> (dialúō, “dissolve”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, suffix that forms nouns representing a state of being)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ransom</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “mutally”) +&lrm; <i>λυτρόω</i> (lutróō, “ransom”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a manner that can be dissolved, separated</li><li>relentingly, meekly, relaxedly</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ́λῠτος</i> (dĭắlŭtos, “relaxed”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverb-creating suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loose one from another, to part asunder, undo, dissolve</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-, “apart, asunder”) +&lrm; <i>λύω</i> (lúō, “loosen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delay continually</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>μέλλω</i> (méllō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay or remain through (time or circumstance), to remain for an indefinitely long time, to remain perpetually, to last continually, to abide continuously</li><li>to stay to the end, hold out; to live on, perdure, persevere, persist</li><li>(<i>figuratively, by implication</i>): to survive, outlive</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “across, through”) + <i>μένω</i> (ménō, “to stay, to remain”). Related to and synonymous with Latin <i>permaneō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to point out clearly</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ-</i> (“through”) +&lrm; <i>μηνύω</i> (“disclose, inform”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call to mind, remember</li><li>to record, mention</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>μνημονεύω</i> (mnēmoneúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>through and through, right through</li><li>continuously</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) + the root of <i>πείρω</i> (peírō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intend [<i>with</i> present <i>or</i> aorist <i>or</i> future infinitive <small>‘to do’</small>]</li><li>to think about, consider</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>διανοέομαι</i> (dianoéomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, bring to an end, accomplish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to finish oneʼs course over the sea</li></ol><li>to traverse</li><li>to arrive at a place</li></ol><p><i>δῐ(ᾰ)-</i> (dĭ(ă)-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νῠ́ω</i> (ănŭ́ō, “effect, accomplish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of cities or town</i>) to sack, plunder, ravage</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-, intensifier) +&lrm; <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch out by nailing the extremities of the body</li><li>(<i>of animal skin</i>) to stretch out for tanning</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>πασσαλεύω</i> (passaleúō), equivalent to <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>πάσσαλος</i> (pássalos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) octave</li></ol><p>Extracted from the phrase <i>ἡ <b>διὰ πᾱσῶν</b> χορδῶν συμφωνίᾱ</i> (hē <b>dià pāsôn</b> khordôn sumphōníā, literally “the concord <b>through all</b> strings (of an instrument)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>διαπασσαλεύω</i> (diapassaleúō)</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>παττᾰλεύω</i> (pattăleúō), equivalent to <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>πάτταλος</i> (páttalos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail across, sail through a strait or gap</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ-</i> (“through”) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (“sail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow through</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to admit air</li><li>(medicine,&#32;Koine,&#32;passive voice) to dissipate by exhalation</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to breathe between times, get breath</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to evaporate</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to dissipate by exhalation</li><li>(<i>of plants</i>) to exhale</li></ol></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “blow, breathe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass over [<i>with</i> genitive], go through</li><li>to finish (<i>doing something</i>)</li><li>to bring about, accomplish, effect, settle, manage</li><li>to put an end to, destroy, slay</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>διαπρᾱ́σσω</i> (diaprā́ssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Homeric form of <i>δῐᾰπρᾰθεῖν</i> (dĭăprătheîn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>effective, operative</li></ol><p>From <i>διαπράσσω</i> (diaprássō) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>right through</li></ol><p>From <i>διά</i> (diá) +&lrm; <i>πρό</i> (pró).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>piercingly, shrilly</li><li>penetratingly</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) + a derivative of <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to go through”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out, hear about thoroughly or in detail</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-, intensifier) +&lrm; <i>πυνθάνομαι</i> (punthánomai, “to inquire, find out”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubting, perplexity, uncertainty</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰπορέω</i> (dĭăporéō, “to be in doubt or difficulty”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffice</li><li>to endure, hold out against, last</li><li>to supply nourishment, sustain</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “dia-”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρκέω</i> (ărkéō, “to defend, suffice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sufficient</li><li>lasting</li></ol><p><i>διαρκέω</i> (diarkéō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear in pieces</li><li>to efface</li><li>to spoil, plunder</li><li>to seize as plunder</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow (through), to run (of liquids)</li><li>to slip through</li><li>to leak</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to fade away, wane, go to waste</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dash in pieces, destroy</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to perish</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>ῥαίω</i> (rhaíō, “to break, shatter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that through which something flows: a channel, pipe</li><li>flowing through, current</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰρρέω</i> (dĭărrhéō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) suffering from diarrhoea</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ́ρροιᾰ</i> (dĭắrrhoiă) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dart through, rush across</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>σεύω</i> (seúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) synonym of <i>δῐᾰσκοπέω</i> (dĭăskopéō) look at in different ways, examine or consider well</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter abroad, disperse, disband</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>σκεδάννῡμι</i> (skedánnūmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get ready, set in order</li><li>to equip</li><li>to revise, edit, compile</li><li>to elaborate (using rhetorical devices)</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>σκευάζω</i> (skeuázō, “to make, prepare”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>editor, revisor</li></ol><p>From <i>διασκευάζω</i> (diaskeuázō, “to revise, edit, prepare”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look at in different ways, to examine or consider</li><li>to keep watching</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>σκοπέω</i> (skopéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tearing in pieces, complete severance</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) interruption, interval</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) distraction</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰσπᾰ́ω</i> (dĭăspắō, “to tear asunder”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter, disperse</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>σπείρω</i> (speírō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scattering, dispersion</li></ol><p>From <i>διασπείρω</i> (diaspeírō, “to sow, to scatter”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dilatation</li></ol><p>From <i>διαστέλλω</i> (diastéllō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix), from <i>διά</i> (diá, “apart”) + <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “send”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preserve through (<i>a danger</i>), bring safe</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to come safe through, arrive safely, escape</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to recover (<i>from illness</i>)</li><li>to preserve, maintain</li><li>to bear in mind</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to retain</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to make whole, heal</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō, “to save”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) place separately, arrange each in their own places, distribute</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with an adverb</i>) to manage well or ill</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to handle or treat well or ill</li><li>to dispose one in such a manner</li></ol><li>to set forth, recite</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to describe</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to arrange as one likes, to dispose of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to dispose of one's property, devise it by will</li><li>to set out for sale, dispose of merchandise</li><li>to arrange or settle mutually, make a covenant</li><li>to compose, make</li><li>to set forth, recite</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-) +&lrm; <i>τῐ́θημῐ</i> (tĭ́thēmĭ, “put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accomplish</li><li>to continue, go on, persevere, live on</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>τελέω</i> (teléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to cut apart, cleave, separate</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>τμήγω</i> (tmḗgō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub hard; to wear away, fritter away, consume</li><li>to spend time</li><li>to be busy</li><li>to waste time, delay</li><li>to hinder</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>τρῑ́βω</i> (trī́bō, “rub”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a way of spending time</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>pastime, hobby, amusement</li><li>occupation, employment, study</li><li>waste of time, delay</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰτρῑ́βω</i> (dĭătrī́bō, “to spend time”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry over or across, to carry from one to another</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of time</i>) to go through, live, continue</li><li>to bear through, bear to the end</li><li>to bear to the end, go through with</li></ol><li>to carry different ways, to toss about</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to spread</li><li>to tear asunder</li><li>to give</li><li>(<i>ἐράνους διαφέρω = διαλύσομα</i>) to pay them up</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to differ</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) it makes a difference</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) the difference, the odds</li><li>to be different from a man, generally in point of excess, to surpass</li><li>to prevail</li><li>to quarrel, struggle</li><li>to come between, intervene</li><li>to belong to</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to differ, be at variance, quarrel</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “through, between”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring, carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to show through (<i>transitive</i>), shine through</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to appear or show through (<i>intransitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to glow, to be red hot</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be proved, show itself, be conspicuous among others</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diaphanous, translucent, transparent</li><li>red-hot, fiery</li><li>manifest, distinct, clear</li><li>conspicuous, noticeable</li></ol><p>From <i>διαφαίνω</i> (diaphaínō, “to shine; to be manifest”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>differently [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from’</small>]</li><li>eminently, especially</li></ol><p>From <i>διαφέρων</i> (diaphérōn, present participle of <i>διαφέρω</i> (diaphérō)) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to escape</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>φεύγω</i> (pheúgō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy</li><li>lead astray</li><li>corrupt</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>φθείρω</i> (phtheírō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction</li></ol><p>From <i>διαφθείρω</i> (diaphtheírō, “to destroy”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference</li><li>discord, disagreement</li></ol><p>From <i>διαφέρω</i> (diaphérō, “to differ”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>δῐᾰφέρω</i> (dĭăphérō, “to spread abroad, disperse”)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>carry away</li><li>plunder</li><li>tear in pieces</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) (<i>of ice</i>) break up</li></ol><li>synonym of <i>δῐᾰφέρω</i> (dĭăphérō, “to carry across from one place to another”)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) dissipate by evaporation, perspiration, etc.</li><li>discuss, disperse by drugs or treatment</li><li>exhaust by dissipating, weaken</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) dispute, debate</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “in different directions, asunder”) +&lrm; <i>φορέω</i> (phoréō, “to wear; to hold, possess, bear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak distinctly, tell plainly</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>φράζω</i> (phrázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plundering, stealing</li><li>evaporation, dissipation, perspiration</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>dispersion, discussion</li><li>exhaustion</li></ol><li>dubitation, perplexity</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰφορέω</i> (dĭăphoréō, “to carry away, plunder; to dissipate, disperse”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, action noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter</li><li>to disperse</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to confound</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to be poured from one vessel to another</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to run through, spread</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to be dissolved</li></ol><p><i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through; in different directions”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate</li></ol><p><i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>χωρίζω</i> (khōrízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>δῐᾰχέω</i> (dĭăkhéō)</li></ol><p>Later form of <i>δῐᾰχέω</i> (dĭăkhéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>translucency</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) clarity</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-) +&lrm; <i>αὐγή</i> (augḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of doubtful or twofold sex, androgynous, hermaphrodite</li><li>of two genders</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) of dual lineage</li></ol><p><i>δῐ-</i> (dĭ-) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher, master, director</li><li>(theater,&#32;music) choirmaster of a chorus</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) producer of a play</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐδᾰ́σκω</i> (dĭdắskō, “to teach”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀇𐀅𐀏𐀩</i> (di-da-ka-re).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach, instruct, train</li></ol><p>Causative reduplication of the root of <i>ἐδάην</i> (edáēn, “learned, taught”, aorist; not attested in the present) with inchoative suffix <i>-σκω</i> (-skō), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;di-dəs-skō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dens-</i> (“to teach”). See <i>ἐδάην</i> (edáēn) for more cognates.</p><p>Compare also Sanskrit <i>दिदेष्टि</i> (dideṣṭi), <i>दीक्षयति</i> (dīkṣayati, “initiate, teach, prepare, make ready”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) apt at teaching</li></ol><p>From <i>διδάσκω</i> (didáskō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>taught, learnt</li><li>teachable, learnable</li><li>instructed [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘in’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>διδάσκω</i> (didáskō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teaching, instruction, education</li><li>preparation, rehearsing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>Catalogues of the Dramas</i> of Aristotle</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>διδάσκαλος</i> (didáskalos, “teacher”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for teaching</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐδᾰσκᾰλῐ́ᾱ</i> (dĭdăskălĭ́ā, “teaching, instruction”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teaching, instruction</li></ol><p>From <i>διδάσκω</i> (didáskō, “to teach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dreh₂-</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>द्रा</i> (drā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail out (through)</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>ἐκπλέω</i> (ekpléō, “sail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or lasting two years</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ-</i> (dĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ἔτος</i> (étos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>year-round, lasting through the year</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “through”) +&lrm; <i>ἔτος</i> (étos, “year”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or keep straight</li><li>to set right, amend</li><li>to settle an account</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “dia-, through-”) +&lrm; <i>εὐθῡ́νω</i> (euthū́nō, “to make straight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;deponent) to explain, relate, go through</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>διακονέω</i> (diakonéō)</li></ol><p>From <i>διήκονος</i> (diḗkonos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>δῐᾱκόσῐοι</i> (dĭākósĭoi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissension</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́θῡμος</i> (dĭ́thūmos, “at variance”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>δῐ́ς</i> (dĭ́s, “twice”) + <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dithyrambic</li></ol><p>From <i>δῑθῠ́ρᾰμβος</i> (dīthŭ́rămbos, “dithyramb”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer of dithyrambs</li></ol><p>From <i>δῑθῠ́ρᾰμβος</i> (dīthŭ́rămbos, “dithyramb”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fitted for the dithyramb</li></ol><p>From <i>δῑθῠ́ρᾰμβος</i> (dīthŭ́rămbos, “dithyramb”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “full of, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dithyramb, a hymn in honor of Dionysus</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bombastic language</li><li>name for the god Dionysus himself</li></ol><p>Like <i>ἴαμβος</i> (íambos), <i>θρίαμβος</i> (thríambos) and <i>ἴθυμβος</i> (íthumbos), this word is probably a loanword from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fallen from Zeus, from heaven</li></ol><p><i>διῑ</i> (diī, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>-πετής</i> (-petḗs, “one who falls”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive,&#32;law) to judge, sit in judgement; to sit as a juror</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give judgement on (something); to decide, determine</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to adjudge a penalty</li><li>(<i>ditransitive</i>) to assign [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘a penalty’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pass judgement on [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go to court or to law</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go to law against [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> genitive <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol></ol><p><i>δῐ́κη</i> (dĭ́kē, “justice”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, denominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act by which wrong is set right</li><li>a judgment, punishment, penalty</li><li>a plea of right</li><li>justification</li><li>an ordinance, decree</li><li>righteousness</li></ol><p>From <i>δικαιόω</i> (dikaióō, “to set right, deem right”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>decently</li><li>legally</li><li>justly</li></ol><p>Adverb, from <i>δῐ́καιος</i> (dĭ́kaios) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neither just nor unjust</li></ol><p>From <i>δίκαιος</i> (díkaios, “just, right”) +&lrm; <i>ἄδικος</i> (ádikos, “unjust, wrong”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>righteousness, justice</li><li>fulfillment of the law</li><li>Pythagorean name for four</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́καιος</i> (dĭ́kaios, “just”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>δῐ́καιος</i> (dĭ́kaios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>δῐ́καιος</i> (dĭ́kaios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set right</li><li>to hold or deem right, to think fit, to claim or demand as right</li><li>to do a man right or justice, to judge</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to condemn, chastise, punish</li><li>to make just, pronounce and treat as righteous, justify, vindicate</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δίκαιος</i> (díkaios, “decent, righteous”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, “cause someone to be”, factitive verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) tribunal, court</li></ol><p>From <i>δικάζω</i> (dikázō, “to judge”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) judge</li><li>(law,&#32;in Athens) juror, juryman</li></ol><p><i>δῐκᾰ́ζω</i> (dĭkắzō, “to judge”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er”, masculine agent-noun suffix): literally, “judger”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, cast</li><li>to aim (on high)</li></ol><p>From an unattested <i>&#42;δίκω</i> (&#42;díkō), of uncertain origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of dittany</li></ol><p>From <i>δίκταμνον</i> (díktamnon, “dittany”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repetition, redundancy</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́λογος</i> (dĭ́logos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) double company</li><li>(<i>military</i>) a body of thirty-two men</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́ς</i> (dĭ́s, “twice, double”) +&lrm; <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos, “ambush; company”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) a commander of a δῐλοχίᾱ (dĭlokhíā)</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐλοχῐ́ᾱ</i> (dĭlokhĭ́ā, “a double company”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whirl</li></ol><p>From <i>δῑ́νη</i> (dī́nē)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whirling, eddying</li><li>rounded</li></ol><p><i>δῑ́νη</i> (dī́nē) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic spelling of <i>δισσός</i> (dissós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>housekeeping, control, government, administration</li><li>one of the lesser Roman provinces</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>as an Ecclesiastical division</i>) a bishop's jurisdiction, diocese</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>διοικέω</i> (dioikéō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), from <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born of Zeus</li></ol><p>From <i>Διο-</i> (Dio-) (combining form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)) + <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manage, control, govern, administer</li><li>to provide, furnish</li><li>to inhabit (<i>distinct places</i>)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to live apart</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>administrator, governor, treasurer</li><li>(<i>in Egypt</i>) chief financial official</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) controller, of the seven planets</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐοικέω</i> (dĭoikéō, “to manage, administer”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build across, wall off</li><li>to barricade</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, “across”) +&lrm; <i>οἰκοδομέω</i> (oikodoméō, “to build a house”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortification</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐοικοδομέω</i> (dĭoikodoméō, “wall off, barricade”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) a fifth (musical interval between one note and another five tones higher)</li></ol><p><i>δῐ(ὰ)</i> (dĭ(à), preposition) +&lrm; <i>ὀξειῶν</i> (oxeiôn, genitive plural feminine)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) Fallen from Zeus, from heaven</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) An image of the Ephesian Artemis which was supposed to have fallen from heaven</li></ol><p><i>διο-</i> (dio-, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>-πετής</i> (-petḗs, “one who falls”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nourished by Zeus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to nourish”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diuretic</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐουρέω</i> (dĭouréō, “to have diuretic properties”) +&lrm; <i>-κός</i> (-kós).</p><p>Morphologically, <i>δῐουρέω</i> (dĭouréō) can be analysed as <i>δι-</i> (di-) + <i>οὐρέω</i> (ouréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double; twofold</li></ol><p>From an earlier verbal adjective <i>&#42;δῐ́πλᾰτος</i> (&#42;dĭ́plătos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwi-</i><i>&#42;pl̥-to-s</i> (“twofold”); compare English <i>twofold</i>. By surface analysis, <i>δῐ-</i> (dĭ-) +&lrm; <i>-πλᾰ́σῐος</i> (-plắsĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become twofold</li></ol><p>From <i>διπλάσιος</i> (diplásios) +&lrm; <i>-όομαι</i> (-óomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>δῐπλόος</i> (dĭplóos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>double thickness</li><li>folded so as to be double; folded in half</li><li>mutual (done by two sides against each other)</li><li>in two minds; doubtful</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) compound (word)</li><li>(<i>comparative</i>) twice as much; twice as large [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘as’</small>]</li><li>(<i>Tragic</i>) two</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́ς</i> (dĭ́s, “twice”) +&lrm; <i>-πλόος</i> (-plóos, “-fold”). Compare <i>ᾰ̔πλόος</i> (hăplóos, “simple”), as well as Latin <i>duplus</i> (“double”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;twīflaz</i> (“doubt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to repeat a process</li><li>to double, multiply by two</li><li>(<i>perfect passive</i>) to be bent in half (of a sword or bowstring)</li><li>to repay twice the original amount</li></ol><p>From <i>διπλόος</i> (diplóos, “double”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, factitive verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black swallow-wort (<i>Vincetoxicum nigrum</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Pedanius Dioscorides, the plant was named after the sorceress Circe. Alternatively, the word could be related to the proper noun <i>Δίρκη</i> (Dírkē). Furnée points to a Pre-Greek name in <i>-αιο-</i>. Compare also <i>δίρκαιον</i> (dírkaion, “winter cherry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twofold, double</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) two</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) divided, disagreeing in mind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>doubtful, ambiguous</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δίχα</i> (díkha, “asunder; differently”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two thousand</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́ς</i> (dĭ́s, “twice”) + <i>χῑ́λῐοι</i> (khī́lĭoi, “thousand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic spelling of <i>δισσός</i> (dissós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strain, filter</li></ol><p>From <i>δια-</i> (dia-) +&lrm; <i>ῡ̔λίζω</i> (hūlízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prepared hide, piece of leather; especially as writing material</li><li>anything made of leather</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wallet, bag</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) skins used as tents</li></ol></ol><p>The origin is uncertain, possibly from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dipʰtʰérā</i>. Related to <i>διψάρα</i> (dipsára, “writing-tablet; piece of leather”) and Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀇𐁇𐀨</i> (di-pte-ra).</p><p>Since de Saussure, connected with <i>δέφω</i> (déphō) or <i>δέψω</i> (dépsō, “to soften (with the hand)”). Beekes argues that this connection and the alternation between <i>φ</i> (ph) and <i>ψ</i> (ps) point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>Compare also Old Persian <i>𐎮𐎡𐎱𐎡</i> (di-i-p-i /⁠dipi⁠/), Akkadian <i>𒁾</i> (ṭuppu, “tablet, document, letter”) and Sumerian <i>𒁾</i> (dub, “tablet”), Sumerian <i>𒁾𒊬</i> (dub-sar, “writer, scribe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of tanned leather</li></ol><p>From <i>διφθέρα</i> (diphthéra, “hide, leather”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>δίφρος</i> (díphros)</li></ol><p>From <i>δίφρος</i> (díphros, “chariot board”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of double form, dual-natured</li></ol><p>From <i>δι-</i> (di-) +&lrm; <i>φυή</i> (phuḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>δῐ́χᾰ</i> (dĭ́khă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the moon's quarter</i>) dividing in two</li><li>bisection, point of bisection</li><li>point of division between wings of army</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) division in two parts, dichotomy</li></ol><p>From <i>διχότομος</i> (dikhótomos, “cut in two”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cut in half, equally divided</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́χᾰ</i> (dĭ́khă, “apart”) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “I cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>venomous serpent, whose bite caused intense thirst</li></ol><p><i>δίψα</i> (dípsa) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thirst; to be thirsty</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to yearn</li></ol><p><i>δῐ́ψᾰ</i> (dĭ́psă, “thirst”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>διψάω</i> (dipsáō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) thirsty</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) dry, parched</li><li>causing thirst</li></ol><p>From <i>δῐ́ψ(ᾰ)</i> (dĭ́ps(ă), “thirst”) +&lrm; <i>-ηρός</i> (-ērós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>δῐψᾱ́ω</i> (dĭpsā́ō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>policeman, gendarme</li></ol><p>From <i>διωγμός</i> (diōgmós) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the chase, pursuit</li><li>persecution</li></ol><p>From <i>διώκω</i> (diṓkō, “to pursue, persecute”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must pursue or be pursued</li></ol><p><i>διώκω</i> (diṓkō, “pursue”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, verbal adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) an oath sworn by both the defendant and the prosecution before a trial</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>hypercorrect form of <i>διυλίζω</i> (diulízō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>wherefore, on which account</li><li>because</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>δι’ ὅ</i> (di’ hó); see <i>δῐᾰ́</i> (dĭắ, “because of”) and <i>ὅ</i> (hó, “which”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit of the nettle tree, <i>Celtis australis</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>because</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>δι’</i> (di’), apocopic form of <i>διά</i> (diá, “because of”), and <i>ὅτι</i> (hóti, “that”, conjunction).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make to run, set in quick motion</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to pursue a person, to chase, hunt</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to be a follower of a person, attach oneself to</li></ol><li>to pursue an object, seek after</li><li>to pursue an argument, to describe</li></ol><li>to drive or chase away</li><li>to drive, impel, speed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>seemingly intransitive</i>) to drive, drive on, gallop, speed, run</li><li>to urge, impel</li></ol><li>(<i>law</i>) to prosecute, bring an action against</li><li>to persecute</li></ol><p>Beekes claims it is of Pre-Greek origin. Probably from δίω.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>forever, always</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave taken in war</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) slave (<i>in general</i>)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>δᾰμᾰ́ζω</i> (dămắzō, “to tame”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;demh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female slave taken in war</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) female slave (<i>in general</i>)</li></ol><p>Feminine singular of <i>δμῳός</i> (dmōiós), alternative form of <i>δμώς</i> (dmṓs), from the root of <i>δᾰμᾰ́ζω</i> (dămắzō, “to tame”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;demh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, gloomy, murky</li></ol><p>From <i>δνόφ(ος)</i> (dnóph(os)) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) darkness, dusk, gloom</li></ol><p>The word recalls <i>ζόφος</i> (zóphos), <i>κνέφας</i> (knéphas) and <i>ψέφας</i> (pséphas), but these words have not been explained. The consonant cluster <i>δν-</i> seems to point to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;defective) it seemed</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>δέατο</i> (déato).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dogmatic</li></ol><p>From <i>δόγμα</i> (dógma) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small abscess; boil</li></ol><p>Probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in two ways</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) twofold; double</li><li>(in the dual,&#32;in the plural) two</li></ol><p>Related to <i>δῠ́ο</i> (dŭ́o, “two”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doubt, perplexity</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) twofold; double</li><li>(in the dual,&#32;in the plural) two</li></ol><p>Related to <i>δῠ́ο</i> (dŭ́o, “two”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) twofold; double</li><li>(in the dual,&#32;in the plural) two</li></ol><p>Related to <i>δῠ́ο</i> (dŭ́o, “two”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expect, think, suppose, imagine</li><li>to seem, to be thought [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to/by someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(impersonal,&#32;<i>δοκεῖ</i> (dokeî)) it seems [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>think</i> with the dative becoming the subject)</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;<i>δοκεῖ</i> (dokeî)) it seems good, it is decided [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>decide</i> or <i>resolve</i> with the dative becoming the subject)</li></ol><li>to be reputed [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, derived from <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “to take, accept”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱ-</i> (“to take”). Cognate to Latin <i>doceō</i> (“to teach, show, rehearse”) and Latin <i>dignus</i> (“fitting, worthy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep an eye on, watch closely</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱ-</i> (“to take, perceive”), though the semantic connection with other Greek words from this root is tenuous.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>it seems to me, methinks [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expect, think, suppose, imagine</li><li>to seem, to be thought [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to/by someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(impersonal,&#32;<i>δοκεῖ</i> (dokeî)) it seems [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>think</i> with the dative becoming the subject)</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;<i>δοκεῖ</i> (dokeî)) it seems good, it is decided [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>] (idiomatically translated by English <i>decide</i> or <i>resolve</i> with the dative becoming the subject)</li></ol><li>to be reputed [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, derived from <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “to take, accept”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deḱ-</i> (“to take”). Cognate to Latin <i>doceō</i> (“to teach, show, rehearse”) and Latin <i>dignus</i> (“fitting, worthy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assay, test, put to the test, make trial of</li><li>to approve, sanction</li></ol><p><i>δόκῐμος</i> (dókĭmos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>test, trial, examination</li><li>The passing of a trial, character</li></ol><p>From <i>δόκῐμος</i> (dókĭmos, “approved”), from <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “I receive, I accept”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>examination, scrutiny of magistrates after election, to see if they fulfil the legal requirements of legitimacy, full citizenship, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>δοκιμάζω</i> (dokimázō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) A holed stone for insertion of roof beams</li></ol><p>From <i>δοκός</i> (dokós, “beam”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the main beam, especially in a house</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>beam</li></ol><li>a type of meteor</li></ol><p>From <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “to accept, receive”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to furnish with rafters</li></ol><p>From <i>δοκός</i> (dokós) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a long tube</li></ol><p>From <i>δολῐχός</i> (dolĭkhós, “long”) +&lrm; <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute; tube”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deceitful, treacherous</li></ol><p>From <i>δόλ(ος)</i> (dól(os)) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running the <i>δόλιχος</i> (dólikhos)</li></ol><p>From <i>δόλῐχος</i> (dólĭkhos, “long course in the arena”) +&lrm; <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos, “race, running”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) long</li><li>protracted, wearisome</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dl̥h₁gʰós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of spears</i>) casting a long shadow</li><li>long</li><li>far-reaching</li></ol><p>From <i>δολιχός</i> (dolikhós, “long”) +&lrm; <i>σκιά</i> (skiá, “shadow”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crafty</li></ol><p>From <i>δόλιος</i> (dólios, “deceitful”) +&lrm; <i>τρόπος</i> (trópos, “manner”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill by treachery</li></ol><p>From <i>δολοφόνος</i> (dolophónos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>planning tricks</li></ol><p><i>δόλος</i> (dólos, “trick, wile”) +&lrm; <i>φρονέω</i> (phronéō, “to intend”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slaying by treachery, stealth, etc.; treacherous</li></ol><p>From <i>δόλος</i> (dólos, “deceit”) +&lrm; <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beguile, ensnare, take by craft</li></ol><p>From <i>δόλος</i> (dólos, “trick, deceit”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to construct, build</li></ol><p>From <i>δόμος</i> (dómos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>builder, constructer</li></ol><p>From <i>δομέω</i> (doméō, “to build”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide with lodging</li></ol><p>From <i>δόμος</i> (dómos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of reeds</li></ol><p>From <i>δόναξ</i> (dónax, “reed”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake, cause to quiver, especially of the wind</li><li>to drive about; disturb, terrify</li><li>(<i>of love</i>) to agitate, excite</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of troops) to wheel</li><li>(<i>of sound</i>) to murmur, buzz, rouse</li></ol><p>Unknown. Windekens speculates a link to Sanskrit <i>धूनोति</i> (dhūnóti, “to shake to and fro”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thicket of reeds</li></ol><p>From <i>δόναξ</i> (dónax, “reed”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn, plantation, grove).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think, suppose, imagine</li><li>to glorify</li></ol><p>From <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa, “opinion”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa)</li></ol><p><i>δόξα</i> (dóxa) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laudation, paean</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>δοξολόγος</i> (doxológos, “telling the glory of”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa, “appearance”) +&lrm; <i>-λογία</i> (-logía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conceit of wisdom</li></ol><p>From <i>δοξόσοφος</i> (doxósophos, “wise in one's own conceit”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Wise in one's own conceit.</li><li>Pretending to wisdom.</li></ol><p>From <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa, “opinion”) +&lrm; <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “wise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Skin when taken off, hide.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>rare</i>) Skin on the living body.</li></ol><li>Flaying.</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, to flay”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā, “nominal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pudendum muliebre</li></ol><p>Unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spear-case</li></ol><p>From <i>δόρῠ</i> (dórŭ, “spear”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mighty with the spear (epithet of Mars)</li></ol><p>From <i>δορί</i> (dorí, “with spear”) +&lrm; <i>σθενής</i> (sthenḗs, “strong, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roe, gazelle, antelope</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Compare Cornish <i>yorch</i>, Welsh <i>iwrch</i>, which leads to a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yorkos</i> (“roe deer”). However, the δ is unexplained, except perhaps by connection with <i>δέρκομαι</i> (dérkomai, “to see clearly”). Has traditionally been explained as a simple derivative of <i>δέρκομαι</i> (dérkomai), because of their bright eyes.</p><p>The suffix might be identified as <i>-άς</i> (-ás); cf. semantically related <i>κεμάς</i> (kemás, “pricket”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of halberd used for cutting off halyards in sea-fights</li></ol><p>From <i>δόρυ</i> (dóru, “spear, lance”) +&lrm; <i>δρέπανον</i> (drépanon, “scythe/sickle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong-speared, with the mighty spear</li></ol><p>From <i>δόρῠ</i> (dórŭ, “spear”) +&lrm; <i>σθενής</i> (sthenḗs, “strong, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spear-bearing, lanciferous</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) defending, escorting, supporting</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spearman</li><li>a member of a ruler’s retinue of bodyguards (equivalent to the Latin <i>satelles</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in Rome</i>) a Praetorian guardsman</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a pleasure or indulgence that merely accompanies or is attendant upon and secondary to another, greater pleasure or indulgence</li><li>(<i>drama</i>) a mute character</li></ol><p><i>δόρῠ</i> (dórŭ, “spear, lance”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “bear, bring, carry”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming two-termination second-declension compound adjectives)</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δορυφόρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a secretary of the Emperor Nero</li></ol><p>Use as a proper name of <i>δορῠφόρος</i> (dorŭphóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leathern bag or wallet</li></ol><p>From <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, flay”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Convolvulus oleifolius</i></li></ol><p>No etymology. Furnée compares <i>στρύχνον</i> (strúkhnon). On the epenthesis, compare <i>κόνυζα</i> (kónuza)/<i>κνύζα</i> (knúza); thus he assumes that this word stands for <i>&#42;δρύκνιον</i> (&#42;drúknion), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giver, dispenser</li></ol><p>From <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi, “I give”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, “-er”). This suffixion uses the zero grade of the verb rather than its full grade, as in <i>δωτήρ</i> (dōtḗr). Formations of this kind follow the usual practice, which is why <i>δοτήρ</i> (dotḗr) is attested more often than <i>δωτήρ</i> (dōtḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the dative case</li></ol><p>From <i>δοτῐκός</i> (dotĭkós): as a noun, a substantivisation of its feminine forms in elliptical use for <i>ἡ δοτικὴ πτῶσις</i> (hē dotikḕ ptôsis, “the grammatical case pertaining to giving”); as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Byzantine,&#32;grammar) appearing like the dative case</li></ol><p><i>δοτῐκή</i> (dotĭkḗ) +&lrm; <i>-φᾰνής</i> (-phănḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>generous</li><li>dative</li></ol><p><i>δόσις</i> (dósis) +&lrm; <i>-κός</i> (-kós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>granted</li><li>that may or must be granted</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) gift</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dh₃tós</i> (“given”). By surface analysis, the root of <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>duchy, dukedom (a country or territory ruled by a duke or duchess)</li><li>(historical,&#32;numismatics) ducat (a gold coin minted as currency)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Medieval Latin <i>ducātus</i>, from Latin <i>dux, ducis</i> (“leader; duke”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slavery, bondage</li><li>servility</li><li>group of slaves</li><li>service for hire</li></ol><p>From <i>δουλεύω</i> (douleúō) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a slave [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>παρά</b> (pará, + dative) <small>‘to’</small>]</li><li>to serve or be subject to [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to pay as a tax or duty</li></ol><p>From <i>δοῦλος</i> (doûlos, “slave”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad like a slave</li></ol><p>From <i>δοῦλος</i> (doûlos, “slave”) +&lrm; <i>πονηρός</i> (ponērós, “in a bad state”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enslave</li></ol><p><i>δοῦλος</i> (doûlos, “slave”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall with a thud, to crash (collide, fall or come down violently)</li></ol><p><i>δοῦπος</i> (doûpos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō); earlier <i>&#42;γδουπέω</i> (&#42;gdoupéō) as shown by augmented aorist forms beginning <i>ἐγδ-</i> (egd-) as well as the prefixed form <i>ἐπιγδουπέω</i> (epigdoupéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>δουρικλυτός</i> (douriklutós, “famed for the spear”) (<i>Homeric epithet for various heroes</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δόρυ</i> (dóru, “spear”) +&lrm; <i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós, “famous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) famed for the spear (<i>Homeric epithet for various heroes</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δόρυ</i> (dóru, “spear”) +&lrm; <i>κλῠτός</i> (klŭtós, “famous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>receptacle</li><li>reception, feast</li></ol><p>From <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oblique, aslant</li></ol><p>In its formation, the word is identical to Sanskrit <i>जिह्म</i> (jihmá, “crooked, tortuous”). According to Beekes, both come from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dh₃ǵʰmo-</i> (“oblique”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Containing, able to hold.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A receptacle.</li></ol><p>From <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “I take, receive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female slave</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>δοῦλος</i> (doûlos, “slave”)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slavish, servile, subject</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>born slave or bondman</li></ol><p>Related to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀈𐀁𐀫</i> (do-e-ro /⁠dohelos⁠/), possibly from Canaanite <i>*dōʾēlu</i> “servant, attendant” (compare Late Babylonian <i>𒁕𒀝𒂵𒇻</i> (daggālu, “subject, one who waits on another, does their bidding”).</p><p>According to Parpola, though not put forward by Beekes, related to the ethnonym Dahae (found as <i>Δάοι</i> (Dáoi), <i>Δάαι</i> (Dáai), <i>Δαι</i> (Dai) or <i>Δάσαι</i> (Dásai) in Greek sources) and thus related to Sanskrit <i>दस्यु</i> (dasyu, “bandit, brigand”) and Sanskrit <i>दास</i> (dāsa) which originally meant "demon" and later also "slave" or "fiend".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) military leader; leader</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) leader; duke</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>dux</i> (“leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δούρατα</i> (doúrata)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestle</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-ῡκ-</i> (-ūk-) is typical of Pre-Greek words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slavish, servile, subject</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>born slave or bondman</li></ol><p>Related to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀈𐀁𐀫</i> (do-e-ro /⁠dohelos⁠/), possibly from Canaanite <i>*dōʾēlu</i> “servant, attendant” (compare Late Babylonian <i>𒁕𒀝𒂵𒇻</i> (daggālu, “subject, one who waits on another, does their bidding”).</p><p>According to Parpola, though not put forward by Beekes, related to the ethnonym Dahae (found as <i>Δάοι</i> (Dáoi), <i>Δάαι</i> (Dáai), <i>Δαι</i> (Dai) or <i>Δάσαι</i> (Dásai) in Greek sources) and thus related to Sanskrit <i>दस्यु</i> (dasyu, “bandit, brigand”) and Sanskrit <i>दास</i> (dāsa) which originally meant "demon" and later also "slave" or "fiend".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>association, community, commune – whether familial or religious</li></ol><p>From Phrygian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thud, any dull and heavy sound</li></ol><p>The word shows some resemblance to Latvian <i>dupêtiês</i> (“dead sound”), Serbo-Croatian <i>dűpiti</i> (“to slay”), and Tocharian B <i>täp-</i> (“to give a loud sound, announce”), but the initial <i>γδ-</i> (gd-) found in some compounds suggests a Pre-Greek origin. Compare also Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewp-</i> (“to push, stick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whitetop (<i>Lepidium draba</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of javelin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>trāgula</i> (“javelin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handful, bunch</li><li>sheaf, bundle of ears of corn</li><li>(<i>later</i>) uncut corn</li></ol><p>From <i>δράσσομαι</i> (drássomai, “to grasp”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragoness</li><li>scourge</li></ol><p>From <i>δράκων</i> (drákōn, “dragon”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li><li>handful</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>δέρκομαι</i> (dérkomai, “I see”), possibly reflecting a thematicized Proto-Indo-European root noun <i>&#42;dérḱs ~ &#42;dr̥ḱós</i>; compare Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;derkos</i> (“eye”). Ultimately from the root <i>&#42;derḱ-</i> (“to see”).</p><p>From <i>δρᾰ́σσομαι</i> (drắssomai, “I grasp, seize”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragon; serpent</li><li>serpent-shaped bracelet</li></ol><p>Traditionally related to <i>δέρκομαι</i> (dérkomai, “to see”) with the literal meaning of “one who stares” or, alternatively, “sharp-sighted”.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δράκων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Draco</li></ol><p>See <i>δράκων</i> (drákōn, “dragon, sharp sighted”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of bread</li></ol><p>Related to Ancient Macedonian <i>δράμις</i> (drámis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fist, clenched hand</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) claw of the constellation Leo</li><li>handful, fistful, also as unit of measure</li><li>palm of the hand</li></ol><p>Retrograde formation from <i>δράσσομαι</i> (drássomai, “to grasp, take handfuls”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dregʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp with the hand, clutch</li><li>to lay hold of, seize, catch</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to take by handfuls</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dregʰ-</i>. The LIV suggests either <i>&#42;dr-n̥-gʰ-yé-ti</i> or <i>&#42;dr̥gʰ-yé-ti</i> as the possible pre-forms. If the former theory is accepted, then both the Greek verb and Avestan <i>𐬛𐬭𐬀𐬲𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬈</i> (draža<sup><small>i</small></sup>te, “holds”) can be derived from the same PIE verb. Regardless, of these options, Beekes supports the latter. However, van Beek suggests that it is "attractive" to unify both the Avestan and Greek words under one unified ancestral term.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act, to take action, to achieve</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes mentions a connection with Lithuanian <i>darýti</i> (“to make, do”). Though the Lithuanian is a strong semantic match, it is generally thought to be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰer-</i> (“to hold”) and thus related to Sanskrit <i>धर्म</i> (dhárma, “dharma, morality”), phonetically precluding a relation with the Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>δρεπάνη</i> (drepánē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pluck, cull</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Elis</i>) leech</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copse; thicket</li></ol><p>Pedersen compared Old Irish <i>driss</i> (“bramble, thornbush”); however, this is uncertain. The word is often combined with <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “wood, oak”), but the formation remains unclear in any case.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δρίος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Drius, the name of several mountains in Ancient Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) a plant within the family Araceae</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) a disease, causing blisters on the hoofs of horses</li></ol><p>Possibly a feminine derivative of <i>δρακόντιον</i> (drakóntion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of wheat with coarse straw</li><li>kind of domestic pigeon</li><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) stone said to be found in a serpent's head</li></ol><p>From <i>δρᾰ́κων</i> (drắkōn, “dragon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>δρᾰκόντῐος</i> (drăkóntĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>δρᾰκόντῐος</i> (drăkóntĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to a dragon</li></ol><p>From <i>δράκων</i> (drákōn, “dragon”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragonwort, edder-wort (<i>Dracunculus vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p><i>δρᾰ́κων</i> (drắkōn, oblique stem: <i>δρᾰκοντ-</i>, <i>drakont-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the dragon, dragon’s, dragon-</li></ol><p><i>δρᾰ́κων</i> (drắkōn, oblique stem: <i>δρᾰκοντ-</i>, <i>drakont-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dramatic</li></ol><p>From <i>δρᾶμα</i> (drâma) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dramatic composition</li><li>pretence, fiction</li><li>plot, conspiracy</li></ol><p>From <i>δρᾱμᾰτουργός</i> (drāmătourgós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contriver</li></ol><p>From <i>δρᾶμα</i> (drâma) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a runaway, especially a runaway slave</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps related to <i>διδράσκω</i> (didráskō, “to run”) and suffixed with <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix), though the origin of the sequence <i>-πέ-</i> (-pé-) is unclear. Beekes suggests a possible further comparison to <i>δράψ</i> (dráps).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run away</li></ol><p>From <i>δρᾱπέτης</i> (drāpétēs) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>efficient</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>active</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) drastic</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δρᾶσῐς</i> (drâsĭs, “strength, efficacy”), from <i>δράω</i> (dráō, “I do”), + <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Cretan form of <i>δρᾰχμή</i> (drăkhmḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drachma</li><li>drachm</li></ol><p>Typically derived as a verbal noun (“holding”) of <i>δρᾰ́σσομαι</i> (drắssomai, “to holdl to seize”). Beekes, however, argues that the existence of the variants <i>δαρχμά</i> (darkhmá) and <i>δαρχνά</i> (darkhná) means <i>δραχ-</i> (drakh-) and <i>δαρχ-</i> (darkh-) do not continue the zero-grade of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dr̥kʰ-</i> and instead derive from an otherwise unknown Pre-Greek substrate term.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of necklace</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickle</li></ol><p>From <i>δρέπω</i> (drépō, “to pluck, cut off”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, instrumental nominal suffix), closely cognate with Old Armenian <i>արտեւան</i> (artewan).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickle</li></ol><p>From <i>δρέπανον</i> (drépanon) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix). See <i>δρεπάνη</i> (drepánē) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alpine swift (<i>Tachymarptis melba</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δρεπᾰ́νη</i> (drepắnē, “sickle”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), so called from the shape of its wings.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp; piercing; cutting</li><li>pungent; bitter; acrid</li><li>keen; shrewd</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tub, basin</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>cradle</li><li>coffin, bier</li></ol><li>a type of dance</li></ol><p>Compare perhaps English <i>tray</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i> (“tree; wood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running, whirling, swift</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) streetwalker, prostitute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;drom-</i> +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running at full speed, swift, fleet</li></ol><p>From <i>δρομή</i> (dromḗ, “a run”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dromedary, <i>Camelus dromedarius</i></li></ol><p>Literally, “running camel”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dewy, watery</li><li>tender, soft</li></ol><p>From <i>δρόσ(ος)</i> (drós(os)) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of gem</li></ol><p>From <i>δρόσος</i> (drósos, “dew”) +&lrm; <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as <i>ἰσχυρόν</i> (iskhurón), neuter of <i>ἰσχυρός</i> (iskhurós, “strong, powerful”).</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deru-</i>, <i>&#42;drew-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>dūrus</i> and Old English <i>trum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A copse, a thicket, a wood, forest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;drumós</i>, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;drumós</i>, from the same root as of <i>δόρυ</i> (dóru, “tree”), <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “oak”), and <i>δροόν</i> (droón, “strong”). The length of the vowel <i>ῡ</i> (ū) is taken analogically from <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;trumaz</i>, Sanskrit <i>द्रुम</i> (druma), Russian <i>дром</i> (drom).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δρυμός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A city of Phocis destroyed by the Persians, now Glunitsa</li><li>A fortified place situated between Attica and Boeotia</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>δρῡμός</i> (drūmós, “forest, wood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodpecker (bird of the subfamily Picinae)</li></ol><p>From <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “wood, oak”) +&lrm; <i>κολᾰ́πτω</i> (kolắptō, “to peck”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black oak-fern (<i>Asplenium onopteris</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “oak”) +&lrm; <i>πτέρις</i> (ptéris, “fern”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ripened on a tree, quite ripe</li></ol><p>From <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “tree”) +&lrm; <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō, “to ripen”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting wood</li><li>woodcutter; lumberjack</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs) +&lrm; <i>τόμος</i> (tómos). From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;drutómos</i> (whence also <i>𐀉𐀬𐀵𐀗</i> (du-ru-to-mo)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i> (“tree”) (whence <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “tree”)) + <i>&#42;tómh₂os</i> (“cutting”) (whence <i>τόμος</i> (tómos, “slice”)), from <i>&#42;temh₂-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>race, running</li><li>racetrack</li><li>course, path</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;drem-</i> (“to run”) + <i>-ος</i> (-os); cognate with Sanskrit <i>द्राति</i> (drā́ti, “to run”), <i>द्रम्</i> (drámati, “to run”); Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tradō</i> (“way, track, spoor”). Related to <i>δραμεῖν</i> (drameîn), a suppletive aorist of <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō, “to run”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>τρύγητος</i> (trúgētos, “gathering of fruits, harvest”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dew</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) pure water</li><li>down on the cheek</li><li>young of animals</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of an intervocalic <i>-σ-</i> (-s-). Older theories include a derivation from a Proto-Indo-European root related to Sanskrit <i>दानु</i> (dānu, “dew”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of snake or serpent</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree; timber</li><li>oak</li></ol><p>From oblique case forms with <i>&#42;drew-</i>, a stem of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i> (“tree”) (whence also <i>δόρῠ</i> (dórŭ)). Cognates include Albanian <i>dru</i> (“tree”), Sanskrit <i>दारु॑</i> (dā́ru), Old Persian <i>𐎭𐎠𐎽𐎢𐎺</i> (d-a-ru-u-v /⁠dāruv⁠/) (whence Persian دار (dâr)), and Old English <i>trēow</i> (whence English <i>tree</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodpecker</li></ol><p>From <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “tree, timber, oak”) and the suffix <i>-οψ</i> (-ops).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear, strip or lacerate; especially in mourning</li></ol><p>Expressive form, usually connected with <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, flay”) and <i>δρέπω</i> (drépō, “to pluck, cut off”). However, the word was hardly reshaped after <i>θρύπτω</i> (thrúptō, “to shatter”). The variant <i>δρυφ-</i> and <i>δρυψ-</i> clearly point to a Pre-Greek word, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oaken</li><li>stubborn; obstinate</li></ol><p>From <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “oak”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derp-</i> (“to see”) and related to Sanskrit <i>दर्पण</i> (darpaṇa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos, “man”).</li></ol><p>Thought to be a compound <i>&#42;νρ-ώψ</i> (&#42;nr-ṓps, “with manly face”) from <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”), but the absence of <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) is strange. According to Latte, it is a creation of the grammarians. Kuiper accepts the gloss as Pre-Greek, explaining <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos) as arising through prenasalization and prothetic vowel. Pisani considers this word Macedonian and connects it to <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to breed, bring up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a deed, act</li><li>one of the three types of ancient Greek poetry (the other two are epic and lyric poetry)</li><li>a play, an action represented on the stage (a tragedy, a comedy, or a satyr play)</li></ol><p>From <i>δράω</i> (dráō) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, efficacy</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sacrifice</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) active force of a verb</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δρᾰ́ω</i> (drắō, “I do”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>verpus</li><li>(<i>conjectured meaning</i>) earthworm</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes calls connections to <i>δρίος</i> (dríos, “thicket”), in the sense of "swelling," a "mere guess." Pokorny suggests the latter connection and also compares <i>δριμύς</i> (drimús, “piercing, sharp”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i> (“to flay, split open”).</p><p>Has been compared to <i>δρίλαξ</i> (drílax, “leech”), with a meaning "earthworm" mediating between "penis" and "leech". The "worm"-meaning has then been used to explain <i>κροκόδειλος</i> (krokódeilos, “lizard”). If the "<i>verpus</i> = penis without foreskin"-meaning is primary, one could adduce Old English <i>teors</i> (“penis”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i> (“to flay, split open”).<br/></p><p>The meaning "earthworm" was adopted in Bailly and Pape, and thus made its way into taxonomic nomenclature with <i>Drilus</i> and <i>Eudrilus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree; timber</li><li>oak</li></ol><p>From oblique case forms with <i>&#42;drew-</i>, a stem of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i> (“tree”) (whence also <i>δόρῠ</i> (dórŭ)). Cognates include Albanian <i>dru</i> (“tree”), Sanskrit <i>दारु॑</i> (dā́ru), Old Persian <i>𐎭𐎠𐎽𐎢𐎺</i> (d-a-ru-u-v /⁠dāruv⁠/) (whence Persian دار (dâr)), and Old English <i>trēow</i> (whence English <i>tree</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitch plaster used in medicine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;drep-</i> (“to pluck”) and related to <i>δρέπω</i> (drépō, “to pluck, cut off”) and <i>δρεπάνη</i> (drepánē, “sickle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the number two</li><li>a group of two; couple, pair, duo, twosome, dyad</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>δύο</i> (dúo) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs); compare <i>τριάς</i> (triás), <i>τετράς</i> (tetrás), <i>δεκάς</i> (dekás), <i>χιλιάς</i> (khiliás), <i>μυριάς</i> (muriás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lord, ruler</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) <i>Epithet of</i> Zeus</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) <i>Epithet of</i> Poseidon</li><li>A princeling, a petty ruler</li></ol><li>Master</li><li>The chief men of a state or place</li></ol><p>From <i>δύναμαι</i> (dúnamai, “be able”) +&lrm; <i>-στης</i> (-stēs, <i>masculine agentive suffix</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δῠναίμεθᾰ</i> (dŭnaímethă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able, powerful</li></ol><p>From <i>δύναμις</i> (dúnamis, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dominion, lordship, political power.</li><li>The exercise of political power.</li><li>A close oligarchy.</li><li>A show of power, a mighty deed.</li></ol><p>From <i>δῠνᾰ́στης</i> (dŭnắstēs, “ruler, petty ruler”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, mighty</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) able to do</li><li>(<i>of outward power</i>) powerful, influential</li><li>able to produce</li></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;of things) possible, practical</li><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) strongly, powerfully</li><li>(in phrases,&#32;such as,&#32;κατὰ τὸ δυνατόν,&#32;ὅσον δυνατόν) as much as is possible</li></ol><p>From <i>δύναμαι</i> (dúnamai, “to be able”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forcefully, skillfully</li><li>possible</li></ol><p><i>δυνατός</i> (dunatós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>δώδεκα</i> (dṓdeka, “twelve”)</li></ol><p>From <i>δύο</i> (dúo, “two”) +&lrm; <i>καί</i> (kaí, “and”) +&lrm; <i>δέκα</i> (déka, “ten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>bad, hard, unfortunate</li></ol><p>Cognate to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tuz-</i> (“hard, difficult, wrong, bad”) (English <i>tore</i>) and Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;duš-</i> (“bad”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of wind</i>) blowing contrarily or violently</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “bad, unfortunate”, pejorative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ἄημι</i> (áēmi, “I breathe, blow”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lowborn, lowly born</li></ol><p>From <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-, “ill, bad”) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs, “of race or stock”); literally “bad of race”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard to dissolve, hard to digest</li><li>hard to reconcile</li></ol><p>From <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-, “ill, bad”) +&lrm; <i>διάλυτος</i> (diálutos, “dissolved”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dysentery</li></ol><p>From <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>ἔντερα</i> (éntera, “bowels”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ill-sounding, noisy, cacophonous (<i>in Homer, an epithet of war</i>)</li></ol><p><i>δυσ-</i> (dus-) +&lrm; <i>ἠχή</i> (ēkhḗ)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard to warm, chilly</li><li>overwarm, burning hot</li></ol><p>From <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-, “bad, hard, unfortunate”) +&lrm; <i>θάλπω</i> (thálpō, “to warm up, heat”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>despondency, despair</li></ol><p>From <i>δῠ́σθῡμος</i> (dŭ́sthūmos, “despondent”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ill-sounding, shrieking</li><li>foulmouthed</li></ol><p>From <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>κέλαδος</i> (kélados, “noise, sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the setting of the Sun or other stars</li><li>the quarter of sunset; the region where the sun sets; <i>ergo</i>, the west, the occident</li></ol><p>From <i>δύω</i> (dúō, “to cause to sink; to plunge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the setting of the Sun or other stars</li><li>the quarter of sunset; the region where the sun sets; <i>ergo</i>, the west, the occident</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) bearing ill-will</li></ol><p>Formed as a present participle to an unattested verb <i>&#42;δυσμενέω</i> (&#42;dusmenéō), from <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-) + a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hostile [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) enemy [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘of someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dusmenḗs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dusmenēs</i> (whence also Sanskrit <i>दुर्मनस्</i> (durmanas), Avestan <i>𐬛𐬎𐬱𐬨𐬀𐬥𐬀𐬵</i> (dušmanah), Middle Persian (dwšmn'), Middle Persian (dwšmyn'), Manichaean Middle Persian <i>𐫅𐫇𐫢𐫖𐫏𐫗</i> (dwšmyn /⁠dušmen⁠/)), from <i>&#42;dus-</i> + <i>&#42;ménos</i> (“mind, thought”), from <i>&#42;men-</i> (“think”). Equivalent to <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos, “spirit”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rough and steep</li></ol><p>A variant on epic παιπαλόεις, from <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>παίπᾰλον</i> (paípălon, “steep place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inaccessible, difficult to access</li></ol><p>From <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-) +&lrm; <i>προσιτός</i> (prositós), from <i>πρόσειμι</i> (próseimi) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misfortune; ill luck</li></ol><p>From <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>τῠ́χη</i> (tŭ́khē, “fortune, fate”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, “feminine abstract substantive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extreme or unbearable pain</li><li>discomfort, distress</li></ol><p>From <i>δῠ́σφορος</i> (dŭ́sphoros, “grievous”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “bad”) + <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “I bear, carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wintry or stormy</li><li>bearing winter ill</li></ol><p>From <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-) +&lrm; <i>χείμερος</i> (kheímeros, “wintry, stormy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>δωδέκατος</i> (dōdékatos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Locrian</i>) twelvefold</li></ol><p>Non-lemma form of an unattested lemma form of an Epizephyrian Locrian term meaning “twelvefold”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>δώδεκα</i> (dṓdeka, “twelve”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwoΗdeḱm̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twelfth</li></ol><p>From <i>δώδεκα</i> (dṓdeka) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) dodecahedron; a polyhedron with twelve flat faces</li></ol><p>From <i>δώδεκα</i> (dṓdeka, “twelve”) +&lrm; <i>ἕδρα</i> (hédra, “face”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chamber, bedchamber</li></ol><p>From <i>δῶμα</i> (dôma, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Late Koine,&#32;Early Byzantine) a type of peach ("kingstone")</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>dūracinum</i>, nominative neuter singular of <i>dūracinus</i> (“hard-berried”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(also in the middle voice) to gift a thing to one</li><li>(also in the middle voice) to begift one with a thing</li></ol><p>From <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple viper's bugloss (<i>Echium plantagineum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift, present”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δωρίς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doris, an Oceanid nymph in Greek mythology</li><li>Doris, one of the Nereid daughters of the Oceanic nymph of the same name</li><li>the lands inhabited by the ancient Dorians</li><li>a region of central Greece inhabited chiefly by the Dorians</li><li>a region of southwest Anatolia colonized by Dorians in ancient times</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female Dorian</li></ol><p>From Δῶρος (Dôros, “Dorus”); masculine of Δωριεύς (Dōrieús, “Dorian”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gift, a present, and especially bounty</li><li>an estate granted by a king, a fief</li></ol><p>Related to <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”), albeit with a suffix <i>-εᾱ́</i> (-eā́) of seemingly unclear form and function.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as a free gift, freely, for free; also, by grant</li><li>to no purpose, for naught</li></ol><p>A regularly declined accusative form of <i>δωρεᾱ́</i> (dōreā́, “a gift”, “bounty”), also used as an adverb.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giver</li></ol><p><i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi) + <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>among the Huns</i>) funeral service</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Alanic, compare: Ossetian <i>дугъ</i> (duǧ), <i>догъ</i> (doǧ, “horse race [in honour of the dead]; running”), <i>дугъон</i> (duǧon, “steed”), <i>дугъуат</i> (duǧwat, “hippodrome, racetrack”). Georgian <i>დოღი</i> (doɣi, “horse race”), Svan <i>ა-<b>დღ</b>-ულ-ი̄ნ-ე</i> (a-<b>dɣ</b>-ul-īn-e, “to gallop”) are borrowed from the same source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opinion, belief</li><li>decision, judgment</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>decree, edict</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>δοκέω</i> (dokéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acceptable</li><li>trustworthy, reliable, approved, esteemed</li><li>excellent, notable, considerable, approved</li></ol><p>From <i>δοκέω</i> (dokéō) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crafty, deceitful, treacherous</li></ol><p>From <i>δόλος</i> (dólos, “bait, trick”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long course in the arena</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a measure of length based on this</li></ol><li>yardlong bean (<i>Vigna unguiculata</i> subsp. <i>sesquipedalis</i>)</li></ol><p><i>δολιχός</i> (dolikhós). The bean has perhaps been made known to the Greeks by Alexander’s campaign.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trick, deceit</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wiles</li><li>bait (e.g. for fish)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delH-</i> (“to reckon”) (which De Vaan considers to be possibly the same root as <i>&#42;delh₁-</i> (“to split”)), and compared with Latin <i>dolus</i> (“fraud, malice”), Oscan (dolom, “intention, ruse”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;talō</i> (“narration, assessment”) (whence Old English <i>talu</i> (“account reckoning”)), Hittite (talliije/azi, “to pray to, evoke (a deity)”), and Old Armenian <i>տող</i> (toł, “line, row”).</p><p>Beekes is skeptical of most of these cognates except for the Italic terms (which he considers borrowed from Greek), and tentatively derives the Greek term from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trick, deceit</li><li>stratagem, ruse</li></ol><p>From <i>δολόω</i> (dolóō, “to beguile, to ensnare”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trick, deceit</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wiles</li><li>bait (e.g. for fish)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;delH-</i> (“to reckon”) (which De Vaan considers to be possibly the same root as <i>&#42;delh₁-</i> (“to split”)), and compared with Latin <i>dolus</i> (“fraud, malice”), Oscan (dolom, “intention, ruse”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;talō</i> (“narration, assessment”) (whence Old English <i>talu</i> (“account reckoning”)), Hittite (talliije/azi, “to pray to, evoke (a deity)”), and Old Armenian <i>տող</i> (toł, “line, row”).</p><p>Beekes is skeptical of most of these cognates except for the Italic terms (which he considers borrowed from Greek), and tentatively derives the Greek term from Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δόλων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dolon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) gift, present</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) payment</li></ol><p>From <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) homeward, home (to&#47;toward one's own house)</li></ol><p><i>δόμον</i> (dómon) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house</li><li>household</li><li>fixed dwelling for some animals</li><li>horizontal beam at the top of some columns serving as a fixed portico or porch</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) domicile of a planet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dómos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dṓm</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>domus</i>, Sanskrit <i>दम</i> (dáma), Russian <i>дом</i> (dom), and Old Armenian <i>տուն</i> (tun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of reed, the donax (<i>Arundo donax</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a bed of reeds</li></ol><li>anything made of reed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a shaft of an arrow</li><li>a shepherd's pipe</li><li>a fishing rod</li><li>the bridge of the lyre</li></ol><li>a kind of shellfish</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin. The word has also been compared with Ancient Greek <i>δονέω</i> (donéō, “to shake”), Latvian <i>duonis</i> (“reed”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tainaz</i> (“rod, twig”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitation, waving (of trees)</li></ol><p>From <i>δονέω</i> (donéō, “to shake”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expectation &lsqb;Homer&rsqb;</li><li>opinion, judgement, belief &lsqb;5th c. BC&rsqb;</li><li>glory, honor, magnificence, praise &lsqb;5th c. BC&rsqb;</li><li>glory, splendor (especially of the shechinah), theophany, mandorla &lsqb;3rd c. BC&rsqb;</li><li>reputation</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóḱs(e)h₂</i>, from <i>&#42;deḱ-</i> (“to accept”) (whence <i>δοκέω</i> (dokéō)) or with some other influence directly from <i>δοκέω</i> (dokéō).</p><p>The development of the meaning "glory" develops naturally from the classical meaning of "opinion, estimation, repute", especially "good repute, honour, glory". It acquires important religious overtones from its use in the Septuagint to translate <i>כבוד</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expectation</li><li>judgement</li><li>opinion</li></ol><p>From <i>δοκέω</i> (dokéō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening meal, dinner, supper</li><li>(<i>later&#32;Epic</i>) food, meal</li></ol><p>Related to Albanian <i>darkë</i> (“evening meal”), but further connections are unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood, tree, stem</li><li>spear shaft, spear, lance, pole</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dóru</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóru</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>दारु</i> (dā́ru), Old Persian <i>𐎭𐎠𐎽𐎢𐎺</i> (d-a-ru-u-v /⁠dāruv⁠/) and Old English <i>trēow</i> (English <i>tree</i>). Also see <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giving, a gift</li><li>license, permission</li><li>portion</li><li>dose of medicine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dótis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déh₃tis</i>. By surface analysis, <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi, “to give”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δύο</i> (dúo)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) misery, pain, anguish</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;duh₂-éh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;deh₂w-</i> (“to burn”). Beekes suggests a possible semantic development from "burning pain" to "anguish." Nevertheless, Beekes considers this etymology uncertain. If this proposal is accepted, then the term may be compared to Sanskrit <i>दू</i> (dū, “pain, distress”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δῠ́ναιντο</i> (dŭ́nainto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δῠ́ναιτο</i> (dŭ́naito)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to be able, capable, strong enough to do, can</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of moral possibility</i>) to be able, to dare, to bear to do</li><li>(with <i>ὡς</i> (hōs) and a superlative) as much as one can</li></ol><li>to pass for</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of money</i>) to be worth</li><li>(<i>of numbers</i>) to be equivalent, to equal</li><li>(<i>of words</i>) to signify, mean</li><li>(<i>as a mathematical term</i>) to be the root of a square number, to be the side of a square</li></ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) it is possible, it can be</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dunamai</i>, from a nasal-infixed form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dewh₂-</i> (“to fit together”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power, might, strength</li><li>ability, skill</li><li>power, authority, influence</li><li>force of war</li><li>magic, magically potent substance or object, magic powers</li><li>manifestation of divine power: miracle</li><li>faculty, capacity</li><li>worth, value</li><li>the force of a word: meaning</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) square root</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) power</li></ol><p>From <i>δῠ́νᾰμαι</i> (dŭ́nămai), formed after <i>θέμις</i> (thémis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δύνατο</i> (dúnato)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>δύω</i> (dúō, “to plunge; to enter”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>two</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dúwo</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;duwó</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;dwóh₁</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>द्व</i> (dvá), Old Armenian <i>երկու</i> (erku), Latin <i>duo</i>, and Old English <i>twā</i> (English <i>two</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>desponding, melancholy, depressed</li><li>repentant</li></ol><p>From <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setting of the sun or stars</li><li>quarter in which the sun sets, west</li><li>hiding-place</li></ol><p>From <i>δύω</i> (dúō, “to cause to sink, plunge”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bad-tempered, irritable, peevish, grouchy, cantankerous</li><li>unpleasant, annoying</li><li>difficult, troublesome</li></ol><p>Apparently a formation from <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-, “bad, hard, unfortunate”) + a component <i>κόλος</i> (kólos) of unknown meaning and origin, though it could possibly be a variant of <i>χολή</i> (kholḗ, “bile”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Difficult to digest</li><li>unripe</li></ol><p>From <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>πέπτω</i> (péptō, “I digest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difficulty breathing, shortness of breath</li><li>contrary winds</li></ol><p>From <i>δύσπνοος</i> (dúspnoos, “short of breath”, adjective) (also <i>δύσπνους</i> (dúspnous) by contraction) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă, suffix forming abstract nouns), from <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “bad-, hard-, ill-”) +&lrm; <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “blow, breathe”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming adjectives).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short of breath, breathing with difficulty</li><li>(<i>of air</i>) unbreathable, poor for breathing</li><li>(<i>of wind</i>) ill-blowing</li></ol><p>From <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “bad-, hard-, ill-”) +&lrm; <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “blow, breathe”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) unhappy, miserable</li></ol><p>From <i>δυσ-</i> (dus-) +&lrm; <i>στῆναι</i> (stênai); latter element related to ἵστημι (hístēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diver</li></ol><p>From <i>δύω</i> (dúō, “to sink; to plunge”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>δύο</i> (dúo, “two”)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to sink; to plunge</li><li>to get into; to enter</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwóh₁</i> (“two”), with <i>δῠ́ο</i> (dŭ́o) coming from the indeclinable variant <i>&#42;d(u)wó</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dew-</i> (“to go in; to enter”). Cognates include rare Vedic Sanskrit <i>उपादुत्य</i> (upādútya, “put on”). See also <i>δείελος</i> (deíelos, “of the afternoon or evening”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twelve</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dwoΗdeḱm̥</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>द्वादश</i> (dvā́daśa) and Latin <i>duodecim</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δώματα</i> (dṓmata)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>σπλήν</i> (splḗn, “spleen”), as they say in Macedonia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift, present</li></ol><p>From <i>δωρέω</i> (dōréō, “to make a present”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dṓtōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déh₃tōr</i> (“giver, donor”), from <i>&#42;deh₃-</i> (“to give”) (whence also <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi, “to give”)). Synchronically <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>दातृ</i> (dā́tṛ) and Latin <i>dator</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of pinewood</li></ol><p>From <i>δᾴς</i> (dāís, “pinewood”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos), variant of <i>δαΐς</i> (daḯs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) earth?</li></ol><p>Possibly from Paleo-Balkan, closely related to Albanian <i>dhe</i> (“earth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bite, sting</li></ol><p>From <i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō, “to bite”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li><li>conspicuous</li><li>manifest</li><li>(Septuagint,&#32;plural form) the Urim</li></ol><p>According to Beekes and LIV, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deyh₂-</i> (“to light up, shine”), and related to <i>δέατο</i> (déato, “seemed”). Compare Sanskrit <i>दीप्</i> (dīp, “to blaze, glow”), and see there for more cognates.</p><p>Another theory derives the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dyew-</i> (“heaven, sky; to shine”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δῆλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Delos</li></ol><p>Unknown. Folk etymology connects the name with <i>δῆλος</i> (dêlos, “visible, manifest”), due to a mythological account of the island's sudden manifestation. Babiniotis posits a likely Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>district, country, land</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the inhabitants of a district or land</li></ol><li>the common people</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>rare</i>) commoner</li></ol><li>free citizens, sovereign people</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>popular government, democracy</li><li>popular assembly</li></ol><li>township, commune; deme</li><li>name for a prostitute</li><li>faction in a circus</li><li>yellow serradella, <i>Ornithopus compressus</i></li></ol><p>Together with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀅𐀗</i> (da-mo), traceable back to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dā́mos</i>, inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déh₂mos</i> (“part, division”), from <i>&#42;deh₂-</i> (“to share, divide”) + <i>&#42;-mos</i>. Equivalent to <i>δαίομαι</i> (daíomai) +&lrm; <i>-μος</i> (-mos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bite, biting</li><li>mental anguish</li><li>biting joke</li></ol><p><i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō, “to bite”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle; combat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;der-</i> (“to tear; to tear apart”); formal correspondence with Sanskrit <i>दारि</i> (dāri, “splitting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>In answers, mostly added to a word which echoes a statement or question</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>aye, yes, indeed, nay</li></ol><li>(<i>In questions, to mark an inference or consequence.</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>so, then</li></ol><li>(<i>In prayers or wishes</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>oh</li></ol></ol><p>Please fill out the etymology</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δῖᾰ</i> (dîă) and <i>δῖε</i> (dîe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) heavenly; divine; noble (<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of persons and gods</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;díwiyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;diwyós</i> (“heavenly”), from <i>&#42;dyew-</i> (“sky”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yós</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दि॒व्य</i> (divyá), Latin <i>dīus</i>, and related to <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”), Latin <i>deus</i> (“god”), and the first element of English <i>Tuesday</i>. The Indo-Iranian and Greek terms (which are formally identical) may alternatively be independently formed to the root <i>&#42;dyew-</i>.</p><p>The feminine nominative/accusative/vocative <i>δῖᾰ</i> (dîă) was earliest used in the substantive function "goddess", and is similar in formation and meaning to Sanskrit <i>दे॒वी</i> (devī́, “goddess; celestial”), though it is likely formed independently to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deywós</i> (“god”), and thus not inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déywih₂</i> (“goddess”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δῖον)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dion or Dium, the name of several ancient Greek settlements</li></ol><p>Of Ancient Macedonian origin, a name meaning "dedicated to Zeus," from <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) heavenly; divine; noble (<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of persons and gods</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;díwiyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;diwyós</i> (“heavenly”), from <i>&#42;dyew-</i> (“sky”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yós</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दि॒व्य</i> (divyá), Latin <i>dīus</i>, and related to <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús, “Zeus”), Latin <i>deus</i> (“god”), and the first element of English <i>Tuesday</i>. The Indo-Iranian and Greek terms (which are formally identical) may alternatively be independently formed to the root <i>&#42;dyew-</i>.</p><p>The feminine nominative/accusative/vocative <i>δῖᾰ</i> (dîă) was earliest used in the substantive function "goddess", and is similar in formation and meaning to Sanskrit <i>दे॒वी</i> (devī́, “goddess; celestial”), though it is likely formed independently to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deywós</i> (“god”), and thus not inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déywih₂</i> (“goddess”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δῖος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dius, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to October</li></ol><p>From <i>δῖος</i> (dîos, “divine, heavenly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dṓm</i>.</p><p>Alternatively, not from a noun, but from a particle cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;tō</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind, tie, fasten, fetter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to tie onto oneself</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to hinder from</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to brace</li></ol><li>to lack, need, require [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>with <i>πολλοῦ</i> (polloû) to be far from being able to do [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>participle <i>δέων</i> (déōn)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>modifying a noun, with genitive of the number by which something is less than another thing: lacking a number: a number less than something, something minus a number; often used to express numbers ending in 8 or 9</li><li>with both the participle and the number in the genitive&#58; genitive absolute: with a number lacking, a number less than</li></ol></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;never impersonal,&#32;transitive) to lack, not have, or need [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to need [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be in need</li><li>(<i>ditransitive</i>) to beg [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> genitive <small>‘from someone’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><li>alternative form of <i>δήω</i> (dḗō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;déyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dh₁yéti</i> (“to bind”), from <i>&#42;deh₁-</i> (“to bind”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>द्यति</i> (dyati), Avestan <i>𐬥𐬍𐬛𐬌𐬌𐬁𐬙𐬄𐬨</i> (nīdiiātąm) and Albanian <i>duaj</i>.</p><p>Apparently from a Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dew(s)-</i> (“to miss, want, need”) (without the <i>&#42;-s-</i> if the earlier form was <i>&#42;δέϝω</i> (&#42;déwō), with the <i>&#42;-s-</i> if the earlier form was <i>&#42;δεύσ-ω</i> (&#42;deús-ō)). In the latter case, perhaps cognate with Sanskrit <i>दोष</i> (doṣa, “fault, deficiency”). See also <i>δῠσ-</i> (dŭs-, “dys-, bad”), <i>δεῖ</i> (deî, “one must”). If the root had no <i>*-s-</i>, compare perhaps Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;teuną</i> (“lack; damage, destruction”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give; to present; to offer; to provide</li><li>to grant; to allow; to permit</li><li>(<i>perfect</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to allow</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be allowed</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dédeh₃ti</i>, reduplicated present of <i>&#42;deh₃-</i> (“to give”). Cognates include Latin <i>dō</i>, Sanskrit <i>ददा॑ति</i> (dádāti), Old Persian <i>𐎭𐎭𐎠𐎬𐎢𐎺</i> (d-d-a-tu-u-v /⁠dadātuv⁠/) and Old Armenian <i>տամ</i> (tam).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δῶκα</i> (dôka)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>δῶκε</i> (dôke)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>chief room, hall</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) a single house</li><li>halls of the gods</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>the nether world</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) temple</li></ol><li>(<b>δῶμα <i>Καδμεῖον</i></b>) Thebes</li><li>housetop</li></ol><li>household, family</li></ol><p>Reanalyzed as a <i>-μᾰ</i>-stem from Epic <i>δῶ</i> (dô), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dṓm</i> (“home; house”). Compare <i>δόμος</i> (dómos), <i>δεσπότης</i> (despótēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>δῶρα</i> (dôra)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift</li><li>hand's width</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀈𐀨</i> (do-ra), Old Armenian <i>տուր</i> (tur), Old Church Slavonic <i>даръ</i> (darŭ), and Albanian <i>dhunti</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Δῶρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dora; Tantura, Palestine</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>דור</i> (Dor).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift</li><li>hand's width</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀈𐀨</i> (do-ra), Old Armenian <i>տուր</i> (tur), Old Church Slavonic <i>даръ</i> (darŭ), and Albanian <i>dhunti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(h₁)ey</i>, a locative form of <i>&#42;ís</i> (“this”). See its sense, “that”, in <i>ἐπεί</i> (epeí) and compare the history of Latin <i>sī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἐᾰρῐνός</i> (eărĭnós, “of or in spring”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of hateful look, ugly</li></ol><p>From <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos) +&lrm; <i>-εχθής</i> (-ekhthḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>constituting an <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “appearance, form”), figurate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>logic</i>) formal</li><li>concerning type, specific</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “appearance, form”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) The ides, used in reckoning Roman dates: the 15th day of March, May, Quintilis or July, and October and the 13th day of the other months.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>īdūs</i> (“ides”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an idol-worshipper, idolater</li></ol><p><i>εἴδωλον</i> (eídōlon, “image”) +&lrm; <i>-λάτρης</i> (-látrēs, “worshipper”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) idolatry (the worship of idols)</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰδωλολᾰτρος</i> (eidōlolătros, “idolater”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idyll (a short descriptive poem, mostly on pastoral subjects)</li></ol><p>From <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “shape, form”) +&lrm; <i>-ύλλιον</i> (-úllion, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>εἰ</i> (ei), used before a word starting with a vowel</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to portray, represent</li><li>to liken, compare</li><li>to infer from comparison, to conjecture</li></ol><p>Possibly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“to become equal”), although there are no cognates outside of Greek. Cognate with <i>εἰκών</i> (eikṓn), <i>ἔοικᾰ</i> (éoikă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The twentieth day of the month</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) The last ten days of the month</li></ol><p>From <i>εἴκοσῐ</i> (eíkosĭ, “twenty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>representing a figure, portrait-like</li><li>counterfeited</li><li>belonging to or employing images</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰκών</i> (eikṓn, “image”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>portrait painter</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰκών</i> (eikṓn, “image”) +&lrm; <i>-γρᾰ́φος</i> (-grắphos, “drawer, painter, writer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sketch, description</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰκονογράφος</i> (eikonográphos, “portrait painter”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) iconoclast</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰκών</i> (eikṓn, “figure, icon”) +&lrm; <i>κλάω</i> (kláō, “to break”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, agent suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iconomachy, iconoclasm</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰκών</i> (eikṓn, “icon”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty-one</li></ol><p>From <i>εἴκοσι</i> (eíkosi) + <i>ἕν</i> (hén).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twentieth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ewīkətstós</i>, equivalent to <i>εἴκοσι</i> (eíkosi) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>likelihood; probability</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) a probable proposition</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>εἰκώς</i> (eikṓs), participle of <i>ἔοικα</i> (éoika, “appear, seem”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suitably, in keeping with [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>εἰκώς</i> (eikṓs, “suitable”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>εἰκών</i> (eikṓn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>figure, image, likeness, portrait</li><li>image in a mirror, reflection</li><li>personal description</li><li>similitude, semblance, phantom</li><li>pattern, archetype</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;Christianity) icon</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“to resemble”), and cognate with <i>ἔοικα</i> (éoika, “to seem (likely that)”), <i>εἴκελος</i> (eíkelos, “like, similar”), <i>εἰκάζω</i> (eikázō, “to portray, liken”), and others; however, no certain cognates exist outside of Greek. It appears plausible that <i>&#42;weyk-</i> in the "resemble" sense is a Hellenic innovation and semantic shift of the more-common "curve, exchange" sense.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without cause</li><li>without planning, heedlessly, rashly, haphazardly</li><li>without purpose, for no reason</li><li>vainly, to no avail</li><li>slightly, moderately</li></ol><p>Perhaps for <i>&#42;ἐϝεκῇ</i> (&#42;ewekēî), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weḱ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>εἴλω</i> (eílō, “roll up”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a rolling gait (<i>said mostly of cattle</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind”) (compare <i>ἐλῡ́ω</i> (elū́ō, “to roll around”)) + <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solemn feast, banquet</li></ol><p>No clear etymology. According to Beekes, it could well be of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feaster, guest, boon-companion</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰλᾰπῐνᾰ́ζω</i> (eilăpĭnắzō, “to celebrate with a feast”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maker of feasts</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰλαπίνη</i> (eilapínē, “banquet, feast”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) intestinal obstruction, ileus</li><li>den, hole</li><li>alternative form of <i>ἐλεός</i> (eleós, “butcher's block”)</li><li>a kind of vine</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō), <i>εἴλω</i> (eílō, “to shut in”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure, absolute, genuine, unmixed</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pure, clean, honest</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin.</p><p>Traditionally derived as a compound of <i>εἵλη</i> (heílē, “heat or warmth of the sun”) +&lrm; <i>κρίνω</i> (krínō, “to separate, distinguish”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, “adjectival suffix”), thus "distinguished in or by the sun". However, this interpretation makes little sense when using <i>εἵλη</i> (heílē)'s basic sense of "heat of the sun"; thus, a secondary meaning of "sunlight" is apparently invoked. However, as Beekes notes, such a meaning has only been attested for the Doric variant <i>ϝέλα</i> (wéla) and is relatively recent, making the etymology more tenuous.</p><p>Other theories connecting the first element of the word to <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō) in either the "turn, wind" or "press together" senses are semantically difficult.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrap, enfold, cover</li><li>(<i>of a lame man</i>) to crawl, wriggle</li></ol><p>The gloss <i>γέλουτρον</i> (géloutron, “case, pod”) points to an earlier form <i>&#42;ϝέλυτρον</i> (&#42;wélutron), identical with Sanskrit <i>वरुत्र</i> (varutra, “cloak, mantle, upper garment”). Theoretically, this verb could derive from a pre-form <i>&#42;ϝελ-ν-ύ-ω</i> (&#42;wel-n-ú-ō) and agree with Sanskrit <i>वृणोति</i> (vṛṇoti, “to cover, screen”), but the Greek word is late and rare, which makes the identification less probable. This verb has also been linked to Old Armenian <i>գելում</i> (gelum, “to twist, squeeze”), Latin <i>volvō</i> (“to roll, tumble”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walwijaną</i> (“to roll”). Note that <i>εἴλῡμα</i> (eílūma) has the same secondary long vowel as in Latin <i>volūmen</i> (“book, scroll, volume”); thus, the verb is ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind, roll”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be; to exist</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of events</i>) to happen</li><li>to be the case</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to live</li><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to be [<i>with</i> nominative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>(third person,&#32;impersonal) it is possible [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ehmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésmi</i> (“to be, to exist”).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>eom</i> (whence English <i>am</i>), Latin <i>sum</i>, Sanskrit <i>अस्मि॑</i> (ásmi), Old Armenian <i>եմ</i> (em), and so on. More at <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be, exist”). Not to be confused with εἶμι (eîmi) (to go).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐν</i> (en)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nine years long</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Lyric form of <i>ἐνάλιος</i> (enálios, “in, on or of the sea, marine”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in, on, at”) (<i>as needed for meter</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) having quivering foliage</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔνοσις</i> (énosis, “shaking”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace</li></ol><p>According to Beekes and Chantraine, probably from Pre-Greek. Older theories link the word to <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “to fasten together”) or <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “to speak, say”); compare <i>ῥήτρα</i> (rhḗtra, “verbal agreement, treaty”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Εἰρήνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Irene</li></ol><p>From εἰρήνη (eirḗnē, “peace”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>police magistrate</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰρηνοφύλαξ</i> (eirēnophúlax, “policeman”) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler, leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for an irenarch</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰρηνᾰ́ρχης</i> (eirēnắrkhēs, “irenarch”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>peaceful, peaceable</li></ol><p><i>εἰρήνη</i> (eirḗnē) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos)</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Εἰρηναῖος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Specific use of <i>εἰρηναῖος</i> (eirēnaîos, “peaceable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of peace, the title of a police magistrate</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰρήνη</i> (eirḗnē, “peace”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocrisy, deception, especially a feigned ignorance</li><li>An assumed appearance, pretext</li><li>The act of sealioning.</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰρωνεύομαι</i> (eirōneúomai, “to feign ignorance”, from εἴρων) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insincere, feigning ignorance</li></ol><p><i>εἴρων</i> (eírōn, “someone who feigns ignorance”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἐρωτάω</i> (erōtáō, “to ask”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>into <i>εἰς τὴν πόλιν</i><dl><dd><i>eis tḕn pólin</i></dd><dd><i>into</i> the city</dd></dl></li><li>to; unto <i>εἰς τοὺς αἰῶνας τῶν αἰώνων</i><dl><dd><i>eis toùs aiônas tôn aiṓnōn</i></dd><dd><i>unto</i> the ages of the ages</dd></dl></li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ἐνς</i> (ens), as attested in Doric, from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;en-s</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁én</i> (“in”), from which also <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in, at, on”). Possibly created to contrast with <i>ἐν</i> (en) as the result of analogy with the pair <i>ἐκ</i> (ek) / <i>ἐξ</i> (ex), of which the latter is the older form (see <i>&#42;h₁éǵʰs</i>); the -/s/ in <i>ἐξ</i> (ex) is ultimately from the PIE genitive–ablative marker <i>&#42;-(e)s</i>.</p><p>The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li><i>ad unguem</i></li></ol><p>Literally “to a nail”: an expression borrowed from sculptors, who, in modelling, give the finishing touch with the fingernail, or from joiners, who test the accuracy of joints in wood by the fingernail.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εἰσι</i> (eisi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>into</li></ol><p>Prefixal form of <i>εἰς</i> (eis, “into”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead in or into (esp. into one’s dwelling)</li><li>to bring in, bring forward (esp. as a law-term, e.g. "bring forward a case")</li><li>to introduce to a subject, instruct</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to enter</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰς</i> (eis, “into”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) right opposite</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go into, enter, invade</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the chorus or of actors</i>) to come upon the stage, to enter</li><li>(as an Attic law term,&#32;of the accuser) to come into court</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(of the parties,&#32;with accusative) to enter upon the charge</li><li>(<i>of the accused</i>) to come before the court</li><li>(<i>of the cause</i>) to be brought in</li></ol><li>to enter on an office</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(impersonal,&#32;with infinitive)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-, “into”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bringing in</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an introduction (<i>as of heirs by adoption or of children to a φρατρία</i>)</li><li>importation of goods etc.</li><li>raising of taxes</li></ol><li>(<i>law</i>) a bringing of causes into court</li><li>an introduction to a subject, an elementary teaching</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an elementary treatise (<i>as the title of a work by Chrysippus</i>)</li></ol><li>a channel of entrance to a harbour</li><li>the office of an εἰσαγωγεύς</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-, “into”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγή</i> (agōgḗ, “bringing, leading”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to listen, give ear [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘to’</small>]</li><li>(poetic,&#32;Koine) to hear</li><li>to hearken, give heed [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) +&lrm; <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go up to or into</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) +&lrm; <i>ἀναβαίνω</i> (anabaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw into</li><li>(<i>of an army</i>) to assault, invade</li><li>(<i>of a river</i>) to empty into</li><li>to begin</li></ol><p><i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-, “into”) +&lrm; <i>βᾰ́λλω</i> (bắllō, “to throw”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embark, board ship</li></ol><p><i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make to go into</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) to put on board ship</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) +&lrm; <i>βιβάζω</i> (bibázō). Functions as the causative of <i>εἰσβαίνω</i> (eisbaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attack, invasion</li><li>entrance, pass</li><li>mouth of a river</li><li>beginning</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσβάλλω</i> (eisbállō, “to attack”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write in, inscribe</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εἰσιδεῖν</i> (eisideîn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look upon, behold</li></ol><p><i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) +&lrm; <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail into</li></ol><p><i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (pléō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to breathe upon</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) +&lrm; <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get in or exact debts, taxes, dues</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) +&lrm; <i>πράσσω</i> (prássō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contribution; tax</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσφέρω</i> (eisphérō, “to contribute”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riverside pasture or water meadow, lowland</li><li>place mentioned as the home of the poplar</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is Pre-Greek in view of the variations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) one who wraps his cloak about him</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gaoler, jailer</li></ol><p>From <i>εἱργμός</i> (heirgmós, “prison”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cage, prison</li><li>imprisonment</li></ol><p>From <i>εἴργω</i> (eírgō, “to fence in, shut out”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós), with aspiration.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be seen, appear</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to appear, seem to do</li><li>(reflexive,&#32;with dative) to make oneself like</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be like, to look like</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to know; see”), compare the very closely related <i>οἶδα</i> (oîda). Cognate with Latin <i>videō</i>, Sanskrit <i>वेत्ति॑</i> (vétti), Old Armenian <i>գիտեմ</i> (gitem), Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣдѣти</i> (věděti), and Old English <i>witan</i> (English <i>wit</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phantom, ghost</li><li>shape, figure, image</li><li>image of the mind: idea, fancy</li><li>representation, statue, idol</li></ol><p>From <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “form”) + diminutive -olon.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εἴτε</i> (eíte) (<i>before /h/</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εἴθε</i> (eíthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>if only (signifies a wish), would that</li></ol><p><i>εἴ</i> (eí) +&lrm; <i>-θε</i> (-the)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like, similar, resembling (<i>followed by the dative</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“to become equal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εἴκοσι</i> (eíkosi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>twenty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ewī́kəti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(h₁)wídḱm̥ti</i> (“twenty”).</p><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>विंशति</i> (viṃśatí), Latin <i>vīgintī</i>, Old Armenian <i>քսան</i> (kʻsan), Welsh <i>ugain</i>, Tocharian A <i>wiki</i>, Albanian <i>-zet</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>εἴκοσῐ</i> (eíkosĭ) with movable nu</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yield, give way</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wéikō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“to curve, bend; exchange”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll up, pack close</li><li>to smite</li><li>to collect</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to draw oneself up, shrink up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to go to and fro</li><li>to wind, turn round, (cause to) revolve</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind; to press together”). The "turn, wind" and "press together" senses were probably originally distinct from each other, but became confused and conflated in many branches, including Hellenic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εἰπέ</i> (eipé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if really, if indeed, even if, even though</li><li>(in Attic,&#32;to imply that the supposition agrees with the fact,&#32;with the imperfect it implies that it is contrary to the fact)</li></ol><p>Strengthened form of <i>εἰ</i> (ei, “if”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if anywhere, if at all</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>indirect</i>) whether anywhere</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>εἰ</i> (ei) + <i>που</i> (pou).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, speak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eweikʷon</i>, dissimilated from <i>&#42;eweukʷon</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éwewkʷom</i>, from <i>&#42;wekʷ-</i> (“to speak, make a sound”), from which also came <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “voice, word”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>अवोचम्</i> (á-vocam) and Latin <i>vōx</i> (“voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔργω</i> (érgō, “to close off, enclose”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slavery, bondage, captivity</li></ol><p>Unclear. Frisk argues against the connection with Latin <i>servus</i> (“servant, slave”), instead suggesting a basis <i>&#42;ϝερϝερος</i> (&#42;werweros), and connecting the word with Armenian <i>գերեմ</i> (gerem, “to take prisoner”). It is probably not related to <i>εὑρίσκω</i> (heurískō, “to find”), nor to <i>ἀρύω</i> (arúō, “to draw water”), although based on the meaning the word has been compared to Sanskrit <i>ग्रहण</i> (grahaṇa, “capture, seizing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie, join, fasten, string together</li><li>to insert</li><li>to say, speak, tell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;héřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ser-</i> (“to bind, put together”).</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéryeti</i>, <i>ye</i>-present from the root <i>&#42;werh₁-</i> (“to speak”).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>verbum</i>, Old Armenian <i>յորջորջեմ</i> (yorǰorǰem), Old English <i>word</i> (English <i>word</i>), and maybe Sanskrit <i>व्रत</i> (vratá) and Avestan <i>𐬎𐬭𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬙𐬀</i> (uruuata).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie, join, fasten, string together</li><li>to insert</li><li>to say, speak, tell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;héřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ser-</i> (“to bind, put together”).</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéryeti</i>, <i>ye</i>-present from the root <i>&#42;werh₁-</i> (“to speak”).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>verbum</i>, Old Armenian <i>յորջորջեմ</i> (yorǰorǰem), Old English <i>word</i> (English <i>word</i>), and maybe Sanskrit <i>व्रत</i> (vratá) and Avestan <i>𐬎𐬭𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬙𐬀</i> (uruuata).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who says less than they think, dissembler, pretender</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps related to <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “I speak”) or <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai, “I ask”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way in, entrance, entry</li><li>an act of going in, entrance</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>entrance into the lists to contend in the games</li><li>a right or privilege of entrance</li><li>a visit</li></ol><li>that which comes in, income, revenue</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-, “into”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, hasten</li><li>to go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyh₁-</i> (“to chase, pursue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of motion</i>) into</li><li>(<i>of location</i>) inside, within</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of time</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>εἰς</i> (eis, “into”) +&lrm; <i>-ω</i> (-ō, adverbial suffix). Compare <i>ἄνω</i> (ánō, “upwards”), <i>κάτω</i> (kátō, “downwards”), <i>πρόσω</i> (prósō, “onwards”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be seen, appear</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to appear, seem to do</li><li>(reflexive,&#32;with dative) to make oneself like</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be like, to look like</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to know; see”), compare the very closely related <i>οἶδα</i> (oîda). Cognate with Latin <i>videō</i>, Sanskrit <i>वेत्ति॑</i> (vétti), Old Armenian <i>գիտեմ</i> (gitem), Old Church Slavonic <i>вѣдѣти</i> (věděti), and Old English <i>witan</i> (English <i>wit</i>).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, hasten</li><li>to go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyh₁-</i> (“to chase, pursue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>generally doubled</i>) either...or, whether..or</li><li>(like <i>εἰ</i> (ei),&#32;in indirect questions)</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰ</i> (ei, “if”) +&lrm; <i>τε</i> (te, “even, and”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to be accustomed, to tend to</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;séswōtʰa</i>, a perfect verb (with unexplained long *<i>ō</i>) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swe-dʰh₁-</i>, from the reflexive pronoun <i>&#42;swé</i> (“self”) + <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put, place, set”), equivalent to the acc. <i>ἕ</i> (hé) of <i>οὗ</i> (hoû, “him”) + <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to set”): thus the original sense is to "set as one's own". Cognates include Latin <i>suēscō</i>, <i>soleō</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्वधा</i> (svadhā́) and Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐌳𐌿𐍃</i> (sidus).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εἵατο</i> (heíato)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the heat or warmth of the sun</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swelH-</i> (“to shine, warm, smolder, burn”). According to Beekes, from earlier <i>&#42;hϝέλα</i> (&#42;hwéla), whence perhaps <i>ϝέλᾱ</i> (wélā) and <i>ἕλᾱ</i> (hélā). However, the variation between initial <i>εἵλ-</i> (heíl-) and <i>ἕλ-</i> (hél-) is problematic, though Beekes suggests a possible analogical spread of <i>εἵλ-</i> (heíl-). Moreover, it is difficult to explain the variation between <i>εἴλη</i> (eílē) and <i>εἴλη</i> (eílē). Beekes suggests that such a development could perhaps have emerged due to the addition of a prothetic vowel <i>&#42;ἐ-</i> (&#42;e-) to the pre-form <i>&#42;hϝέλα</i> (&#42;hwéla), although he considers such an occurrence to be "impossible." The linguist Robert Woodhouse suggests that it could have emerged via the contraction of the terms <i>&#42;swelH-</i> and <i>&#42;seh₂w-el-</i> (“sun”) in speech, which—according to Woodhouse—may have created a term <i>&#42;sew-el(H)-</i>. Afterwards, this new form may have then produced <i>εἴλη</i> (eílē) and, though psilosis, also <i>εἵλη</i> (heílē). It is possible that it may relate to Ancient Greek <i>ἀλέα</i> (aléa, “warmth of the sun”).</p><p>If derived from <i>&#42;swelH-</i>, then cognate with English <i>sweal</i>, German <i>schwelen</i> (“to smolder”), and Lithuanian <i>svi̇̀lti</i> (“to grill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εἵματα</i> (heímata) (<i>before /h/</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εἵνεκα</i> (heíneka)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἕνεκα</i> (héneka)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old name of the letter ε (epsilon, see ἒ ψιλόν (è psilón), έψιλον).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>come on!, let’s go!</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) food</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἔδω</i> (édō, “to eat”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ed-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see; to behold; to perceive</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>strengthened</i>) to look at; to observe</li><li>to see a person; to meet; to speak with them</li><li>to see; experience; become acquainted with</li></ol><li>to look at or towards</li><li>to see mentally; to perceive</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to examine; to investigate</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;widét</i>, from <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see”). See <i>εἴδομαι</i> (eídomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form, image, shape (seen)</li><li>appearance, look, beauty (comeliness)</li><li>sight</li><li>fashion, sort, kind</li><li>species</li><li>wares, goods</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wéidos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéydos</i> (“seeing, image”), from <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see”). Cognate to Sanskrit <i>वेदस्</i> (védas), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vidъ</i>, English <i>wit</i>; see also <i>εἴδομαι</i> (eídomai, “to appear, seem, resemble”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at once; immediately; forthwith</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>&#42;εὖθαρ</i> (&#42;eûthar), compare <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús) ~ <i>&#42;εἰθύς</i> (&#42;eithús) ~ <i>ἰθῠ́ς</i> (ithŭ́s, “straight”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyéwdʰ-r̥</i> from the root <i>&#42;Hyewdʰ-</i>. Maybe directly cognate to Latin <i>iubar</i> (“dawning light”).</p><p>Beekes is skeptical, and leaves the origin open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parapet; protective wall</li><li>covering; shelter; defence</li></ol><p>Often considered to be an action noun <i>&#42;ϝέλ-ϝαρ</i> (&#42;wél-war). Perhaps from <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to press together”), but this does not fit very well and neither is related to <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to roll, wind”). Beekes, instead, suggests a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to cover, protect”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εἶμι</i> (eîmi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to be</li></ol><p>See <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>εἶναι</i> (eînai)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be; to exist</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of events</i>) to happen</li><li>to be the case</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to live</li><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to be [<i>with</i> nominative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>(third person,&#32;impersonal) it is possible [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ehmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésmi</i> (“to be, to exist”).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>eom</i> (whence English <i>am</i>), Latin <i>sum</i>, Sanskrit <i>अस्मि॑</i> (ásmi), Old Armenian <i>եմ</i> (em), and so on. More at <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be, exist”). Not to be confused with εἶμι (eîmi) (to go).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;in the present&#32;indicative) to be going to go</li><li>(Epic,&#32;Attic&#32;otherwise) to go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;éimi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éyti</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀂𐀍𐀳</i> (i-jo-te), Latin <i>eō</i> (“to go”), Sanskrit <i>एति॑</i> (éti, “goes”), Hittite <i>𒄿𒄿𒀀𒀜𒋫</i> (i-ya-at-ta /⁠iyatta⁠/, “goes”), Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎡𐎫𐎡𐎹</i> (a-i-t-i-y /⁠aitiy⁠/, “goes”), Old Church Slavonic <i>ити</i> (iti), Old English <i>ēode</i> (“went”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say, speak</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eweikʷon</i>, dissimilated from <i>&#42;eweukʷon</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éwewkʷom</i>, from <i>&#42;wekʷ-</i> (“to speak, make a sound”), from which also came <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “voice, word”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>अवोचम्</i> (á-vocam) and Latin <i>vōx</i> (“voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool</li><li>cottonweed (<i>Otanthus maritimus</i>)</li><li>kind of fever</li></ol><p>This word ultimately continues <i>&#42;ϝέρϝος</i> (&#42;wérwos), possibly from the same root as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥h₁ḗn</i> (“lamb”) ; compare Latin <i>vervēx</i> (“wether”) and <i>ἀρήν</i> (arḗn, “lamb”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of mere sequence</i>) then, next</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>often stands with the finite verb after a participle, where the participle may be resolved into a finite verb and εἶτα rendered "and then", "and yet", expressing surprise or incongruity</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>to denote consequence</i>) and so, so then, therefore, accordingly</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰ</i> (ei, “if”) and root of <i>τά</i> (tá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ᾗ</i> (hēî, “which way, where, whither”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a garment, article of clothing</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) clothing, clothes</li><li>a rug, carpet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wéhmə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wésmn̥</i> (compare Sanskrit <i>वस्मन्</i> (vásman)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“to wear”), whence ἕννῡμῐ (hénnūmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἕως</i> (héōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>one</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hens, &#42;hmia</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḗm, &#42;smih₂</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सकृत्</i> (sakṛt) and Latin <i>semper</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>good; well</li></ol><p>Prefix form of <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing good news</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγγέλλειν</i> (angéllein, “to announce”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry <i>εὐαί</i> (euaí) in honor of Dionysus</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. However, it could be an inherited onomatopoeia, from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;euāiyō</i>, cognate with Latin <i>ovō</i>. Related to <i>εὖα</i> (eûa), <i>εὐαί</i> (euaí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) topical remedy</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐαφής</i> (euaphḗs, “soft to the touch”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) easily refuted</li><li>easily detected or tested</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>ἔλεγχος</i> (élenkhos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>a cry of joy</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reward for good news</li><li>good news</li><li>(biblical,&#32;Christianity) gospel</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐάγγελος</i> (euángelos, “bringing good news”), from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγγέλλω</i> (angéllō, “to deliver”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring or announce good news</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to preach the gospel, evangelize</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐαγγέλιον</i> (euangélion, “good news”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bearer of good news</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) evangelist</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐαγγελίζω</i> (euangelízō) +&lrm; <i>-ιστής</i> (-istḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quickly recovering</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνασφάλλω</i> (anasphállō, “to recover”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft to the touch</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the mind</i>) easily swayed, flexible</li></ol><li>having a soft touch</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) unforced</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἁφή</i> (haphḗ, “sense of touch”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good counsel, discretion</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔβουλος</i> (eúboulos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>εὐγενής</i> (eugenḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-born, noble</li><li>(in Tragedy,&#32;figurative) noble-minded, generous</li><li>(<i>of animals</i>) well-bred, thoroughbred</li><li>(of a wife,&#32;figurative) fertile, fecund</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>γένος</i> (génos, “birth, offspring, stock”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix) from <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “become”). Compare <i>Εὐγένιος</i> (Eugénios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fortunate, in a good situation, happy</li><li>prosperous, wealthy</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a good genius</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn, “god, destiny”), literally “possessed by a good genius or spirit”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>εὐδαίμων</i> (eudaímōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happiness, well-being</li><li>(<i>ethics</i>) Eudaimonia (in Aristotelian ethics, a condition of living a life of the highest virtue; the state of human flourishing, which is desirable in and of itself, rather than as a means towards some other end)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐδαίμων</i> (eudaímōn, “fortunate”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, “feminine abstract substantive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of traps</i>) easy to undo or open</li><li>easy to dissolve or break up</li><li>easy to solve or refute</li><li>easy to dissolve, and so to digest</li><li>easy to reconcile</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well, good”) +&lrm; <i>δῐᾰ́λῠτος</i> (dĭắlŭtos, “dissolved”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be well pleased or content, to acquiesce in a thing (<i>with participle&#32;or&#32;dative of thing</i>)</li><li>to be well-pleasing or acceptable, find favor with (<i>with dative of person</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>δοκέω</i> (dokéō, “to seem, appear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>εὔδοξος</i> (eúdoxos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A benefactor, euergetist (<i>sometimes as honorary title</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female <i>εὐεργέτης</i>, a benefactress</li><li>(<i>as a feminine adjective</i>) beneficent</li></ol><p><i>εὐεργέτης</i> (euergétēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well wrought, well made, well done</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good deed, kindness</li><li>a public service</li></ol><p><i>εὐεργέτης</i> (euergétēs, “benefactor”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well-being, prosperity</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>ἐστί</i> (estí, “is”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ, feminine noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a good season</li><li>thriving</li><li>prosperity, plenty</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἔτος</i> (étos, “year”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, suffix forming feminine abstract nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good, correct style</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔζηλος</i> (eúzēlos, “having good taste”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “good”) + <i>ζῆλος</i> (zêlos, “zeal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, at once, straightway</li><li>as soon as, shortly</li></ol><p>Adverb from <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús, “straight”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs), using the stem <i>εὐθε-</i> (euthe-).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight; direct</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a moral sense</i>) straightforward; open; frank</li></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) straight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>simply</li><li>in the face of</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) straightaway, forthwith, at once</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of manner</i>) directly, simply</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyewdʰ-dh-ú-s</i>, from the root <i>&#42;Hyewdʰ-</i> (“become straight”, in most other formations &gt; "targeted motion, assault"). Willi explains the Ionic form <i>ῑ̓θύς</i> (īthús) as a secondary variant of <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús) by dissimilation (> <i>&#42;εἰθύς</i> (&#42;eithús)) and metaphony (> <i>ῑ̓θύς</i> (īthús)).</p><p>Possibly connected to <i>εἶθᾰρ</i> (eîthăr, “at once, immediately”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blooming, flourishing, thriving</li><li>Doric form of <i>εὐθηλής</i> (euthēlḗs)</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good, well”) +&lrm; <i>θᾰ́λλω</i> (thắllō, “bloom, flourish, thrive”, verb) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, suffix forming third-declension compound adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>easy, happy death</li><li>noble death</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>θάνατος</i> (thánatos, “death”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrive, flourish</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good, well”) + a root <i>&#42;θεν</i> (&#42;then) + <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “verbal suffix”). The root <i>&#42;θεν</i> (&#42;then) is of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ellipsis of <i>εὐθεῖα πτῶσις</i> (eutheîa ptôsis, “nominative case”, literally “straight case”)</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) ellipsis of <i>εὐθεῖα γραμμή</i> (eutheîa grammḗ, “straight line”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight; direct</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a moral sense</i>) straightforward; open; frank</li></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) straight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>simply</li><li>in the face of</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) straightaway, forthwith, at once</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of manner</i>) directly, simply</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyewdʰ-dh-ú-s</i>, from the root <i>&#42;Hyewdʰ-</i> (“become straight”, in most other formations &gt; "targeted motion, assault"). Willi explains the Ionic form <i>ῑ̓θύς</i> (īthús) as a secondary variant of <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús) by dissimilation (> <i>&#42;εἰθύς</i> (&#42;eithús)) and metaphony (> <i>ῑ̓θύς</i> (īthús)).</p><p>Possibly connected to <i>εἶθᾰρ</i> (eîthăr, “at once, immediately”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrive, flourish</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐθηνός</i> (euthēnós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō). Variant form of <i>εὐθενέω</i> (euthenéō, “to thrive, flourish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thriving, flourishing</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) cheap</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) wasteful</li></ol><p>See <i>εὐθενέω</i> (euthenéō) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheerfulness, good mood</li><li>contentment, tranquility</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔθῡμος</i> (eúthūmos, “cheerful”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The part of a ship where the rudder was attached.</li><li>The uppermost course of a building's foundations, partly emerging from groundline.</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐθῡντήρῐος</i> (euthūntḗrĭos, “making straight, directing”), from <i>εὐθῡντήρ</i> (euthūntḗr, “corrector”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos), from <i>εὐθῡ́νω</i> (euthū́nō, “I lead straight”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr), from <i>εὐθῠ́ς</i> (euthŭ́s, “straight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guide straight, direct</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to direct, govern</li></ol><li>to make straight</li><li>to examine someone's conduct</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with genitive,&#32;of a criminal) to call to account for</li><li>to refute, censure</li><li>to examine by torture</li></ol></ol><p>Denominal verb from <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús, “straight”) +&lrm; <i>-ύνω</i> (-únō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight; direct</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a moral sense</i>) straightforward; open; frank</li></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) straight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>simply</li><li>in the face of</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) straightaway, forthwith, at once</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of manner</i>) directly, simply</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyewdʰ-dh-ú-s</i>, from the root <i>&#42;Hyewdʰ-</i> (“become straight”, in most other formations &gt; "targeted motion, assault"). Willi explains the Ionic form <i>ῑ̓θύς</i> (īthús) as a secondary variant of <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús) by dissimilation (> <i>&#42;εἰθύς</i> (&#42;eithús)) and metaphony (> <i>ῑ̓θύς</i> (īthús)).</p><p>Possibly connected to <i>εἶθᾰρ</i> (eîthăr, “at once, immediately”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with beautiful horns</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good, well”) +&lrm; <i>κέρᾰς</i> (kérăs, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming second-declension adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easily moved [<i>with</i> <b>εἰς</b> (eis, + accusative)]</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>κῑνέω</i> (kīnéō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>convenient for dwelling in</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “intensifying affix”) +&lrm; <i>οἰκητός</i> (oikētós, “habitable”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ease of motion, mobility, agility</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐκῑ́νητος</i> (eukī́nētos, “easily moved”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prayed for, desired</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) The optative mood</li></ol><p>By ellipsis, from the phrase <i>ἡ εὐκτῐκὴ ἔγκλῐσῐς</i> (hē euktĭkḕ énklĭsĭs, “the inflection expressing wishing”): see <i>εὐκτῐκός</i> (euktĭkós). The optative mood was so named because in independent clauses it expresses a wish:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Related to wishing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>grammar</i>) Used to express a wish: the forms of the optative mood</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>εὐχ-</i> (eukh-), the stem of <i>εὔχομαι</i> (eúkhomai, “to wish, pray, or vow”), + <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm, maggot, larva of the fly</li></ol><p>According to Frisk, it is an old verbal noun from <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to turn, roll”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind”), properly meaning "that which winds or coils". However, a prothetic vowel <i>*ἐ-ϝλ-ή</i> is impossible, unless <i>&#42;h₁uel-</i> is assumed; however, this would give <i>ὑλ-</i>. In view of all these considerations, Beekes prefers a Pre-Greek origin for this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beware (with μὴ + subjunctive)</li><li>to take care (with ὅπως + future indicative)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beware of</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to revere</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐλαβής</i> (eulabḗs) +&lrm; <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holding fast, clinging</li><li>cautious</li><li>reverent</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) easy to take hold of</li><li>cautiously undertaken</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well-”) +&lrm; <i>λαβεῖν</i> (labeîn, “to take hold of”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak well of, praise</li><li>to call down the gods' favour upon, bless</li><li>to bestow benefits upon</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to curse</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good, well”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “speech”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix). Compare <i>εὔλογος</i> (eúlogos, “reasonable, sensible”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good speaking, good words</li><li>flattery</li><li>praise, panegyric, eulogy</li><li>fame, glory</li><li>blessing, thanksgiving</li><li>gift</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔλογος</i> (eúlogos, “reasonable, sensible”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “word, utterance, narrative”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>large</li><li>important</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>μέγεθος</i> (mégethos, “size”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay or place in ambush</li><li>to put to bed, lull to sleep</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to lie asleep</li><li>to have sex with (the main meaning in Homer)</li></ol><p><i>εὐνή</i> (eunḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed</li><li>bedding</li><li>abode, layer</li><li>marriage bed</li><li>grave</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) stones thrown from a ship and used as anchors</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Compare Old Irish <i>úam</i> (“cave, hole, an animal's lair”), Avestan <i>𐬎𐬥𐬁</i> (unā, “hole, slit in the earth”). Others wanted to relate it to Old Armenian <i>ունիմ</i> (unim, “to have, own”).</p><p>Van Beek suggests a derivation from an older adjective, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(H)yew-mn-o-</i> (“secluded, private”). He further points to a separate origin for the sense 'anchor-stones', referencing a proposal by Szemerényi to connect it with a Semitic word for 'stone', perhaps Phoenician <i>𐤀𐤁𐤍</i> (ʾbn, “stone”).</p><p>Has also been suggested as related to Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;wentā</i> (“place, town”), from a hypothetical Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;h₁wen-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have good laws, to be well-ordered</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos) +&lrm; <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>benevolent, friendly</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well, good”) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castrated person, eunuch</li><li>castrated animal</li><li>date fruit without stone</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>εὐνή</i> (eunḗ, “bed”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”); literally, someone who takes care of the nuptial chamber, a chamberlain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>The cry used by worshipers of Dionysus.</li></ol><p>From an epithet of Dionysus, <i>Εὔιος</i> (Eúios) or <i>Εὐάν</i> (Euán), probably from <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “good”) +&lrm; <i>υἱός</i> (huiós, “son”) (implying "Well done, my son", said by Zeus to Dionysus after he won a fight in the Gigantomachy), or from <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “good”) +&lrm; <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-educated</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>παιδεύω</i> (paideúō, “to educate”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, passive adjective ending).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodness of education, culture or scholarship</li></ol><p>from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, good) +&lrm; <i>παίδευσις</i> (paídeusis, “education”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy to wrestle with, easy to accomplish</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>πάλη</i> (pálē, “wrestling, fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common agrimony (<i>Agrimonia eupatoria</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Εὐπάτωρ</i> (Eupátōr) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prosper, thrive</li><li>to have plenty of, abound in</li><li>to find a way, find means</li><li>to supply or furnish</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) to have one's doubts resolved, gain clear knowledge (opp. <i>ἀπορέω</i> (aporéō))</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔπορος</i> (eúporos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ease [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘of doing something’</small>], easy means of providing or satisfying [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>plenty, abundance [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small>], wealth</li></ol><p><i>εὔπορος</i> (eúporos, “easy to get or do, wealthy”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well-doing; well-being; welfare; success</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐπρᾱγέω</i> (euprāgéō, from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō)) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good behavior</li><li>welfare, well-being</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>πρᾶξις</i> (prâxis) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good-looking, goodly, comely</li><li>decent, seemly, fitting, becoming, glorious</li><li>specious, plausible</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>πρέπω</i> (prépō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>decently, fittingly, becomingly, gloriously</li><li>speciously, plausibly</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐπρεπής</i> (euprepḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>handsome, fair of face, attractive</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good at war, successful in war (epithet of Nike)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) +&lrm; <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos, “war”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fluency, eloquence</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, good) +&lrm; <i>ῥῆμα</i> (rhêma, “word, sentence”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) having wide streets (<i>epithet chiefly of cities</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhythmical order</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔρυθμος</i> (eúruthmos, “rhythmical”) (from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>ῥυθμός</i> (rhuthmós, “rhythm”)) + <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of oxen</i>) with a broad forehead</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>μέτωπον</i> (métōpon, “forehead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broad-sandalled</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>πέδῑλον</i> (pédīlon, “sandal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) having wide roads (epithet of <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn))</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “road, way”) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>southeastern wind</li></ol><p>From <i>Εὖρος</i> (Eûros, “east wind”) +&lrm; <i>νότος</i> (nótos, “south wind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to broaden, to widen</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “broad”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far-sounding (<i>epithet of Zeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁uru-wokʷ-</i> (“far-sounding”), compare Vedic Sanskrit <i>उरूची</i> (urūcī́), an epithet of the Earth, from <i>&#42;h₁uru-wkʷ-</i> with the zero grade of both elements.</p><p>The conventional English translation ‘far-seeing’ comes from an alternative interpretation of the etymology in which the second element is <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i> (“eye, to see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the sea</i>) wide-traversed, with broad ways</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “broad”) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “way, passage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;vulgar,&#32;sex) having a wide anus (<i>used to imply a man is the receptive partner in anal sex</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús) +&lrm; <i>πρωκτός</i> (prōktós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wide; broad; spacious (<i>especially of heaven, earth, and sea</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eurús</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wérus</i> (“wide”). Compare Sanskrit <i>उ॒रु</i> (urú, “wide; large; spacious”) and Avestan <i>𐬬𐬊𐬎𐬭𐬎</i> (vo<sup><small>u</small></sup>ru, “wide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>εὐρῠ́ς</i> (eurŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>εὐρῠ́ς</i> (eurŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>εὐρύχωρος</i> (eurúkhōros, “spacious”)</li></ol><p>Alternative form of <i>εὐρύχωρος</i> (eurúkhōros), with metrical shortening of ω to ο to avoid having a single light syllable (ρυ) between two heavy syllables (εὐ and χω). However, some dictionaries believe it to have a separate etymology, being <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús, “wide, broad”) +&lrm; <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “dance ring”) with the original meaning “having broad dancing places, spacious enough for dancing”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moldy, dank (<i>especially of the underworld</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐρώς</i> (eurṓs) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mold, mildew, dank decay</li></ol><p>Comparing the word with <i>ἱδρώς</i> (hidrṓs), <i>γέλως</i> (gélōs), <i>ἔρως</i> (érōs), Schwyzer concludes an original s-stem. Furnée takes the alternative form given by Hesychius as proof of Pre-Greek origin, a hypothesis that convinces Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) piety</li><li>filial piety</li><li>loyalty</li><li>(Buddhism,&#32;Hinduism) dharma</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐσεβής</i> (eusebḗs, “pious”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia), synchronically analyzable as <i>εὐσεβής</i> (eusebḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia). The sense “dharma” is a semantic loan from Ashokan Prakrit <i>𑀥𑀁𑀫</i> (dhaṃma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pious, righteous, reverent to the gods</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>reverent to the Emperor &lsqb;AD 4th C.&rsqb;</li></ol><li>holy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well based, standing firm, stable (relatively unchanging)</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) + a derivative of <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equable, tranquil</li><li>good for the stomach, wholesome</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>στόμᾰχος</i> (stómăkhos, “stomach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gracefulness, elegance</li><li>proper treatment, adequate maintenance</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔσχημος</i> (eúskhēmos, “elegant”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Good arrangement, good condition.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Good order, discipline; orderly behavior; orderliness, order.</li><li>Moderation in diet.</li><li>Continence.</li></ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;Stoic) Practical judgement, tact.</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔτακτος</i> (eútaktos, “well-ordered, orderly”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, “nominal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having good walls, well-fortified</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>τεῖχος</i> (teîkhos) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheap, easily paid for</li><li>slight, easy</li><li>mean, paltry, worthless</li><li>thrifty, frugal</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “result, product”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to have good fortune, to be prosperous, to be well off, to be successful</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐτυχής</i> (eutukhḗs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having good fortune or a good outcome: fortunate, successful, lucky</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>τῠ́χη</i> (tŭ́khē, “luck”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>good luck</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>τῠ́χη</i> (tŭ́khē, “luck”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, abstract noun suffix): Compare <i>εὐτυχής</i> (eutukhḗs, “lucky”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece of good luck, a success</li></ol><p><i>εὐτυχέω</i> (eutukhéō, “to have good fortune”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, “instance of”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be well spoken</li><li>to speak sounds of good omen</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔφημος</i> (eúphēmos, “well speaking, from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>φήμη</i> (phḗmē, “voice, a prophetic voice, rumor, talk”)”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, “a verb forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a euphemism</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐφημίζω</i> (euphēmízō, “speak sounds of good omen”) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós, “suffix forming abstract nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power of bearing/enduring easily</li><li>fertility</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔφορος</i> (eúphoros, “bearing well”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to gladden</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to make merry, to enjoy oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔφρων</i> (eúphrōn) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-meaning, with good intent</li></ol><p>Apparent present participle of an unattested verb <i>εὐφρονέω</i> (euphronéō), from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>φρονέω</i> (phronéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mirth, merriment</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔφρων</i> (eúphrōn, “merry”) +&lrm; <i>-σύνη</i> (-súnē, “-ness”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Εὐφροσύνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Euphrosyne (one of the Charites)</li></ol><p>The personified <i>εὐφροσύνη</i> (euphrosúnē, “mirth, merriment”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the kindly time, i.e., night</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔφρων</i> (eúphrōn, “merry”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, “noun ending”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>natural goodness of growth or shape, shapeliness</li><li>good natural parts; natural cleverness; genius; goodness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of places</i>) fertility, favorable situation</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, “to grow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodness of voice</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>-φωνίᾱ</i> (-phōníā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resin spurge (<i>Euphorbia resinifera</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔφορβος</i> (eúphorbos, “well fed”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant, agreeable</li><li>well-favoured, popular</li><li>grateful, thankful</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>χᾰ́ρῐς</i> (khắrĭs, “grace”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer, wish</li><li>vow</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔχομαι</i> (eúkhomai, “pray, wish”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, suffix forming action nouns).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bestow a favour on, oblige</li><li>to be grateful, thankful</li><li>to thank, give thanks [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to pray</li><li>to wish well, congratulate</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐχάριστος</i> (eukháristos, “grateful”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thanks, gratitude</li><li>giving of thanks, thanksgiving</li><li>Eucharist</li><li>gracefulness</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐχᾰ́ρῐστος</i> (eukhắrĭstos, “thankful, grateful”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>χᾰ́ρῐς</i> (khắrĭs, “grace”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of hymns or prayers</i>) of gratitude</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐχᾰ́ρῐστος</i> (eukhắrĭstos, “grateful”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to pray</li></ol><p><i>εὐχέτης</i> (eukhétēs) +&lrm; <i>-άομαι</i> (-áomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer, wish</li><li>boast, shout of triumph</li></ol><p>Epic variant of <i>εὐχή</i> (eukhḗ), from <i>εὔχομαι</i> (eúkhomai, “I pray, wish”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εὐχόμεθα</i> (eukhómetha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courage, high spirit, <i>eupsychia</i></li></ol><p><i>εὔψῡχ(ος)</i> (eúpsūkh(os)) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweet smell</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐώδης</i> (euṓdēs) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with beautiful eyes</li><li>keen-eyed, watchful</li><li>pleasing to the eye</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) specious, fair only to the eye</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ὀφθαλμός</i> (ophthalmós, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweetsmelling, fragrant</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of wines</i>) having a bouquet</li></ol></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “smelling”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of good name; honored</li><li>having an auspicious name; fortunate; prosperous</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>on the left side</li><li>ominous; ill-boding</li></ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) southern; southerly</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spindle tree (<i>Euonymus europaeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma, “name”). Euphemistically refers to "left" because bad omens came from the left side.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic, Doric, and Ionic form of <i>οὗ</i> (hoû) (<i>enclitic</i>): "of him", "than him"</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen upon by chance, find</li><li>to find out, discover</li><li>to acquire, fetch, obtain</li></ol><p>Inchoative present (with suffix <i>-σκω</i> (-skō)) formed from the aorist <i>εὗρον</i> (heûron). The exact formation of the word's root is unclear, appearing to be a reduplicative of a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreh₁-</i> (“to find”). Cognate with Old Irish <i>·fúair</i> (preterite of <i>fo·gaib</i> (“to find”)) and Oscan <i>ϝουρουστ</i> (wouroust), as well as possibly Old Church Slavonic <i>обрѣсти</i> (obrěsti, “to find”), Old Armenian <i>գերեմ</i> (gerem, “to capture, take prisoner”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inventive, ingenious</li></ol><p>From <i>εὑρίσκω</i> (heurískō, “to find”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-fed, thriving</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>βόσκω</i> (bóskō, “feed, graze”, verb) or <i>βοτόν</i>&nbsp;n (botón, “a grazing beast”, noun) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming second-declension compound adjectives); for the <i>βοτ-</i> (bot-) root, compare <i>βοτᾰ́νη</i> (botắnē, “pasture”) and <i>βοτήρ</i> (botḗr, “herdsman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-advised, prudent</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ, “counsel, advice”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Εὔβουλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eubulus</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔβουλος</i> (eúboulos, “well-advised”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of good repute, honoured, famous, glorious</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) hopeful; cheerful</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) promising; encouraging; which gives hope</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλπίς</i> (elpís, “hope”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rocket, arugula (<i>Eruca sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>εὔζωμος</i> (eúzōmos, “making good broth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-girdled (of women)</li><li>girt up for exercise, active (of men)</li><li>unencumbered</li><li>workaday, unpretending, cheap</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ζώνη</i> (zṓnē, “belt, girdle”) and the suffix <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easily indented or bruised</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>θλᾰστός</i> (thlăstós, “bruised”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a beautiful throne</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Of good spirit, happy, generous, kind</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>setting straight, correction, chastisement</li><li>public examination of the conduct of officials</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐθῡ́νω</i> (euthū́nō, “to guide straight”) +&lrm; <i>-α</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lovely-haired, having beautiful hair</li><li>well-fleeced (<i>of sheep</i>)</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>κόμη</i> (kómē)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy, smooth</li></ol><p>From <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to beat, strike, cut”), possibly with the semantics being "easy to hit."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>behaving well, orderly, decorous</li><li>well adorned, graceful</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “order”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perfectly round (<i>of a shield</i>)</li><li>well-wheeled (<i>of a chariot</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well, good”) +&lrm; <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle, wheel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) reins</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to turn, wind, round”), the same root of Latin <i>lōrum</i> (“thong”), Old Armenian <i>լար</i> (lar).</p><p>Or, according to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, given the presence of variations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fair of form, comely, shapely</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>μορφή</i> (morphḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodwill, favour</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐνοέω</i> (eunoéō, “to wish well to somebody”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>welcoming, friendly, hospitable</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ξεῖνος</i> (xeînos, “foreign”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Εὔξεινος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euxine Sea; Black Sea</li></ol><p>By ellipsis from <i>εὔξεινος</i> (“welcoming, friendly”) [<i>πόντος</i> (“sea”)].</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>εὔξεινος</i> (eúxeinos)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “foreign”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ease of breathing</li><li>free blowing</li><li>fragrance</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔπνοος</i> (eúpnoos, “breathing well”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă), from <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) + <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “breathe, blow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy to pass or travel through; easily got, easily done, easy</li><li>ready, glib; (<i>of persons</i>) full of resources or devices, ingenious, inventive (opp. <i>ἄπορος</i> (áporos))</li><li>well-provided with, rich in, well off</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “passage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with good stem</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with goodly stern or poop</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>πρύμνα</i> (prúmna).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having good decks or rowing benches (<i>epithet of ships</i>)</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>σέλμα</i> (sélma, “deck, rowing bench”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-shaded</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>σκῐᾱ́</i> (skĭā́, “shadow”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-aimed</li><li>aiming well</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>making good shots, guessing well, hitting the right nail on the head, shrewd</li></ol><li>successful</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>στόχος</i> (stókhos, “aim, aiming”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-ordered, orderly</li><li>(<i>military</i>) orderly, well-disciplined, well-behaved</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well, good”) +&lrm; <i>τάσσω</i> (tássō, “draw up in order of battle, form, array, marshal”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easily melted or dissolved</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) +&lrm; <i>τήκω</i> (tḗkō, “to melt”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>versatile</li><li>morally good; (<i>of diseases</i>) mild</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good, well”) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō, “turn, direct; alter, change”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix), or <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good, well”) +&lrm; <i>τρόπος</i> (trópos, “turn, direction; way, manner”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having good wheels</li><li>smooth-running</li><li>smooth-tongued, glib</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>τροχός</i> (trokhós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) well-made, well-wrought</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) + the root of <i>τεύχω</i> (teúkhō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uttering sound of good omen</li><li>abstaining from inauspicious words</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>φήμη</i> (phḗmē, “a voice, a prophetic voice, rumor, talk”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), from <i>φημί</i> (phēmí, “to speak, say”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Εὔφημος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Euphemus</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) +&lrm; <i>φήμη</i> (phḗmē, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearing well</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “I bear”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheerful</li><li>kindly, gracious, friendly</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “well-”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “-minded”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Εὔφρων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Euphron</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔφρων</i> (eúphrōn, “cheerful, merry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>attractive; enticing; charming</li></ol><p>From <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) +&lrm; <i>χᾰ́ρῐς</i> (khắrĭs, “grace; charm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εὔχετο</i> (eúkheto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray, offer prayers</li><li>to pray for, wish for, long for</li><li>to vow or promise to do</li><li>to profess loudly, to boast, vaunt</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eukʰomai</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀄𐀐𐀵</i> (e-u-ke-to, “εὔχεται (eúkhetai)”)), thematized from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁é-h₁ugʰ-tor</i>, reduplicated present from <i>&#42;h₁wegʷʰ-</i> (“to promise, to praise”). The Mycenaean evidence indicates that the thematicization must have occurred at an early date, prior to the time of Homer. However, a possibly imperfect athematic form <i>εὖκτο</i> (eûkto) has been discovered in an inscription from Thebais. This term could reflect a possible original athematic flexion, though the linguist Oswald Szemerényi suggests that it is more likely an aorist formed innovatively on the model of terms such as <i>λέκτο</i> (lékto) and <i>λέχομαι</i> (lékhomai).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>ओहते</i> (óhate), <i>वाघत्</i> (vāghát),</p><p>Avestan <i>𐬀𐬊𐬘𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬈</i> (aoja<sup><small>i</small></sup>te), Old Armenian <i>գոգեմ</i> (gogem), and Latin <i>voveō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of good courage, stout of heart</li><li>(<i>in comparative</i>) cooling</li></ol><p><i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ψῡχή</i> (psūkhḗ, “spirit, soul”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abounding in fish</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-, “good, well”) +&lrm; <i>ἰχθῡ́ς</i> (ikhthū́s, “fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep</li></ol><p>Unknown. There are several unconvincing suggestions:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An invention, a discovery (not by chance but by thought)</li><li>An invention, for or against things, a remedy</li><li>An excuse</li><li>That which is found unexpectedly, a piece of good luck, a windfall</li><li>A foundling</li></ol><p>From <i>εὑρίσκω</i> (heurískō, “to find out”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to singe, of singeing off swine's bristles</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;éuhō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éwseti</i>, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ews-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>ओषति</i> (oṣati), Latin <i>ūrō</i>, and Old English <i>ǣmerġe</i> (English <i>ember</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>well</li></ol><p>Adverbial use of the stem of <i>ἐΰς</i> (eǘs, “good”), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ehu-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁su-</i> (“good”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>well, rightly</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>good! well said! well done! bravo!</li><li>(sarcastically) well well!</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”) +&lrm; <i>γε</i> (ge, emphatic particle).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εὖτε</i> (eûte) (<i>before /h/</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedfellow, wife</li><li>one who is bereaved</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐνή</i> (eunḗ, “bed, abode”).</p><p>Perhaps ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁weh₂-</i> (“to abandon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>width, breadth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;éuros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wéros</i> (“width, breadth, expanse, space”) (compare Sanskrit <i>वरस्</i> (váras)), from the root <i>&#42;h₁wer(H)-</i>. Related to <i>εὐρύς</i> (eurús).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Εὖρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the east wind (later east-southeast), personified as a god</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εὖτε</i> (eûte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>when</li><li>as; since</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the thing prayed for, object of prayer, prayer</li><li>a boast, vaunt</li><li>(<i>later Greek</i>) a vow, votive offering</li></ol><p>From <i>εὔχομαι</i> (eúkhomai, “pray, vow, boast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Homeric and Epic form of <i>οὗ</i> (hoû) (<i>strong form</i>): "of him", "than him"</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>εὗρε</i> (heûre)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>δῐᾰ́</i> (dĭắ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>διάβολος</i> (diábolos)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>διάβολος</i> (diábolos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Sicily</i>) reaping hook, sickle</li></ol><p>A Sicilian word without etymology. According to Niedermann, it is a Ligurian word, from which Latin <i>falx</i> (“scythe”) would be a loan as well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very sacred, holy, sacrosanct</li><li>of places favoured by the gods</li></ol><p>From <i>ζᾰ-</i> (ză-) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attendant in a temple, more honorable than νεωκόρος (neōkóros)</li></ol><p>Hieratic professional term. Semantically related and formally comparable is <i>νεωκόρος</i> (neōkóros, “custodian of a temple”). Here <i>ζα-</i> may stand for <i>δα-</i>, as in <i>ζάπεδον</i> (zápedon) for <i>δάπεδον</i> (dápedon, “floor”). Then this word could be related to <i>κορέω</i> (koréō, “to sweep”) and thus meaning "cleaner of the house". However, this seems rather doubtful. According to Beekes, the word must in any case be Aeolic.</p><p>It is explainable as a Semitic borrowing, attested in Jewish Palestinian Aramaic <i>זכורא</i> / <i>ܙܟܘܪܐ</i> (zakkūrā, “soothsayer, necromancer”), Akkadian <i>𒊓𒆠𒊒</i> (sa-qi₂-ru /⁠zākiru, sāqiru⁠/, “one who invokes (names)”), which, also rendered <i>דכורא</i> (dakkūrā) to appear more genuinely Aramaic (Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ḏ</i> regularly gives Akkadian and Hebrew <i>z</i> but Aramaic <i>d</i>), is a reinterpretation rare in Akkadian itself after the cultically relevant root <i>&#42;ḏ-k-r</i> (“recollection, commemoration”), from Akkadian <i>𒊓𒄭𒊒</i> (sa-ḫi-ru /⁠sāḫiru⁠/, “encircling, ensnaring; a kind of sorcerer; itinerant, tramp, peddler”), which relates to the Arabic س ح ر (s ḥ r) “bewitching”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exceeding wroth, surly, morose</li></ol><p>From <i>ζᾰ-</i> (ză-, “very”) +&lrm; <i>κότος</i> (kótos, “grudge, rancour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squall, storm, downpour</li></ol><p>Poetic word without etymology. It has been connected with <i>δίνη</i> (dínē, “whirlpool, eddy”), but this is formally difficult.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very wealthy</li></ol><p>From <i>ζᾰ-</i> (ză-, “very”, intensive) +&lrm; <i>πλοῦτος</i>&nbsp;m (ploûtos, “wealth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very fiery</li></ol><p>From <i>ζᾰ-</i> (ză-, “very”) +&lrm; <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown kind of bird of prey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>violent</li></ol><p>The word is little attested alone and thus its origin remains unknown. Perhaps the first part corresponds to <i>ζᾰ-</i> (ză-), the Aeolic form of <i>δια-</i> (dia-, “through”). Furnée suggests connection with <i>ζᾰ́ψ</i> (zắps, “surf”), and takes it as Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ζῶ</i> (zô)</li></ol><p>Fabricated by ancient grammarians as the full form of contracted <i>ζῶ</i> (zô), but this is truly from <i>ζώω</i> (zṓō), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ďṓwō</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷyṓwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷyéh₃woh₂</i>. Doublet of <i>βῐόω</i> (bĭóō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: Phrygian word for <i>λάχανα</i> (lákhana, “vegetables”).</li></ol><p>From Phrygian, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“green”), the same root of Latin <i>holus</i> (“vegetables”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zelènъ</i> (“green”) and <i>χλωρός</i> (khlōrós, “pale green”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fermentation, boiling</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>That which is boiled, a decoction</li><li>lewdness</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ζέω</i> (zéō, “to boil, to seethe”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galingale (<i>Cyperus longus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Lewy, from a Semitic source common to Hebrew <i>זֶרַע</i> (zéra‛, “seed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seething, effervescence, boiling</li></ol><p>From <i>ζέω</i> (zéō, “I boil, I seethe”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil, seethe, effervesce</li><li>to heat</li><li>to bubble up, well up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yes-</i> (“to boil, foam”). Compare Sanskrit <i>येषति</i> (yeṣati, “to boil, bubble up”), <i>यसति</i> (yásati, “to froth up, foam”), English <i>yeast</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inquiry, question</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) claims, subjects of dispute</li></ol><p><i>ζητέω</i> (zētéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of seeking, searching for</li><li>a search</li><li>inquiry, investigation</li><li>question</li></ol><p>From <i>ζητέω</i> (zētéō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>humming, buzzing of bees</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic. This word has also been connected to Latin <i>zinzala</i> (“type of gnat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jujube (<i>Ziziphus jujuba</i>)</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin. Maybe of Oriental origin, but Beekes doesn't dismiss a Pre-Greek origin, comparing the word with <i>σέσυφος</i> (sésuphos) and <i>Σίσυφος</i> (Sísuphos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>δῐᾰ-</i> (dĭă-, intensifying prefix)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chess</li><li>chessboard</li></ol><p>From Middle Persian <i>𐭰𐭠𐭲‎𐭫𐭠𐭭𐭢</i> (cʾt‎lʾng /⁠čatrang⁠/, “chess”), from Sanskrit <i>चतुरङ्ग</i> (caturaṅga, ““four parts” of an army: king, elephants, horses, foot soldiers”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spelt-giving</li></ol><p><i>ζειά</i> (zeiá, “spelt”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of mice living in Libya</li></ol><p>According to Herodotus, a Libyan word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>einkorn wheat, <i>Triticum monococcum</i>, used as fodder for horses</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) spelt (<i>Triticum spelta</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dzeyyā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yéwos</i> (“barley, cereal”). Compare Persian جو (jow, “barley, rye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A wide upper garment worn by Arabians and Thracians</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;zeirá</i> (“that which is worn on the shoulders”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>γογγυλίδες</i> (gongulídes, “turnips”) or <i>κολοκύνται</i> (kolokúntai, “gourds”).</li></ol><p>Unknown. Furnée compares <i>θικέλιον</i> (thikélion, “turnip”) and believes the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seethed, boiled</li><li>hot</li></ol><p>From <i>ζέω</i> (zéō, “to seethe”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thracian word for <i>χύτρα</i> (khútra, “earthen pot, pipkin”)</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;zetráia</i> (“pot”). Possibly ultimately from the same source as <i>χύτρα</i> (khútra, “pot”) (Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰew-</i> (“to pour”)), but they could be independent developments.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a puny pair or team</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεῦγος</i> (zeûgos, “pair, yoke”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon, neuter diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεῦγος</i> (zeûgos, “yoke”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animals yoked in pairs.</li><li>soldiers in the same rank.</li><li>a reed which formed the mouthpiece of a double flute.</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεῦγος</i> (zeûgos, “pair of beasts”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The third of four classes from Solon's Constitution, who owned land that produced two hundred medimnes.</li></ol><p>Plural form of ζευγίτης, two origins have been suggested. The first from <i>ζεῦγος</i> (zeûgos, “pair of yoked animals”) a reference to the class' ability to upkeep two oxen. The second from <i>ζευγίτης</i> (zeugítēs, “soldiers in the same rank”), as individuals from this class served together in the phalanx.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animals yoked in pairs.</li><li>soldiers in the same rank.</li><li>a reed which formed the mouthpiece of a double flute.</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεῦγος</i> (zeûgos, “pair of beasts”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loop attached to the yoke, through which the beasts's head were put</li><li>crossbar of the double rudder</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεύγνυμι</i> (zeúgnumi, “to harness”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to yoke, saddle, bridle a beast of burden (horses, cattle, mules); to get ready (a chariot)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to have one's beasts yoked</li><li>to fasten tightly</li></ol><li>to join or link together</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to join in wedlock</li><li>(<i>active voice&#32;or&#32;middle voice</i>) to build a bridge from bank to bank</li><li>to furnish ships with crossbenches</li><li>to pair gladiators</li></ol></ol><p>A <i>nu</i>-stem root present from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yewg-</i>. Compare <i>δείκνῡμι</i> (deíknūmi, “to show”). Cognate with Latin <i>iungō</i>, Sanskrit <i>युनक्ति</i> (yunákti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is used for joining, band, bond</li><li>bridge of boats</li><li>canal lock</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) zeugma</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ζεύγνῡμι</i> (zeúgnūmi, “to join”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ζεῦγμα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zeugma, Commagene, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεῦγμα</i> (zeûgma, “bridge of boats”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pair (two things, persons or animals considered as pair)</li><li>yoke (two animals joined together)</li><li>a vehicle drawn by two or more animals, carriage, chariot</li><li>two persons or animals of different sex, in common bond, liaison</li><li>by two, in set of two</li><li>a set of more than two</li><li>(<i>music</i>) double reed of a wind instrument (e.g. flute)</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) plethron, equivalent of a land area ploughed by two oxen in one day</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yéwgos</i> (“team of yoked animals”). See also <i>ζεύγνυμι</i> (zeúgnumi, “to join”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act or manner of yoking oxen</li><li>bridging two points separated by water</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεύγνῡμῐ</i> (zeúgnūmĭ, “I join”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, “abstract noun suffix”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ζεῦξις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Zeuxis</li></ol><p>From <i>ζεύγνῡμῐ</i> (zeúgnūmĭ, “to yoke, saddle”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jealousy, rivalry, envy</li></ol><p>From <i>ζηλότῠπος</i> (zēlótŭpos, “jealous”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emulator, zealous admirer or follower</li><li>zealot</li></ol><p>From <i>ζηλόω</i> (zēlóō, “to emulate”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>jealous</li><li>eager</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῆλος</i> (zêlos, “eager rivalry, zeal”) +&lrm; <i>τῠ́πος</i> (tŭ́pos, “blow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to emulate</li><li>to be jealous</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῆλος</i> (zêlos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loss, damage</li><li>penalty in money, fine</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) penalty</li><li>expense, expenditure</li><li>dead loss, bad bargain</li></ol><p>Sommer connected the word with <i>ζῆλος</i> (zêlos, “fervour, zeal”), <i>ζητέω</i> (zētéō, “to inquire, search, investigate”), and <i>δίζημαι</i> (dízēmai, “to seek out, look for”), based on a breakdown of the word as <i>ζη-μία</i> (zē-mía), which suggests a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yeh₂-</i> (“to go, seek”). For the semantic association between "zeal" and "fine, penalty", compare the Germanic pair Old English <i>anda</i> (“envy, jealousy”) with Old High German <i>antōn</i> (“to punish”) (which stem from a different root, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enh₁-</i> (“to breathe, blow”)). Connection with Sanskrit <i>दीन</i> (dīná, “poor, miserable, wretched”) and <i>δειλός</i> (deilós, “cowardly”) appears unlikely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek, search after, look for</li><li>to inquire into, examine, consider</li><li>to strive for, desire, wish</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Could be from unattested <i>&#42;ζᾱτός</i> (&#42;zātós), from the same root of <i>δίζημαι</i> (dízēmai, “to seek”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yeh₂-</i> (“to go, seek”), whence <i>ζῆλος</i> (zêlos, “zeal”) and <i>ζημία</i> (zēmía, “damage, loss”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disposed to searching, inquiry</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in masculine,&#32;substantive,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) Sceptic</li><li>(in feminine,&#32;substantive) Sceptic philosophy</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ζητέω</i> (zētéō, “I seek”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the aristocracy of the ancient Thracians</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;zibut</i> (“illustrious”), suffixed with the Greek ending <i>-ιδες</i> (-ides).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ginger</li></ol><p>Via unattested Middle Iranian from Sauraseni Prakrit <i>𑀲𑀺𑀁𑀕𑀺𑀯𑁂𑀭</i> (siṃgivera), which see for comparisons.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of lizard</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Beekes, the alternation <i>δ/ζ</i> could lead to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darnel, ryegrass (<i>Lolium temulentum</i>)</li></ol><p>Loaned through an Aramaic form attested as Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>זזניא</i>, <i>זזנייא</i>, Mishnaic Hebrew <i>זזנין</i>, Classical Syriac <i>ܙܺܝܙܳܢܳܐ</i> (zīzānā, “ryegrass”), from Sumerian <i>𒍣𒍝𒀭</i> (zizān, “wheat”). See also living Arabic زِوَان (ziwān) for the term’s semantic range.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) alternative form of <i>σμᾰ́ρᾰγδος</i>&nbsp;f or m (smắrăgdos, “emerald”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) crippled, lame, disabled</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ζουγκλός</i> (zounklós), from Ancient Greek <i>ζᾰ́γκλον</i> (zắnklon, “sickle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ζουλάπιν</i> (zoulápin): rosewater, medicinal julep</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) rosewater, medicinal julep</li></ol><p>From Arabic جُلَّاب (jullāb, “rosewater, julep”), itself from Persian گلاب (golâb, “rosewater”). The term (as <i>ζουλάπιον</i>) is attested already in the 8<sup>th</sup> c. indicating a direct borrowing from Arabic rather than through Old Anatolian Turkish جلاب (cüllab).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field maple (<i>Acer campestre</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ζυγόν</i> (zugón, “yoke”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), because the field maple was mainly used to make yokes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild thyme (<i>Thymus serpyllum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῠγόν</i> (zŭgón, “yoke”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public official in charge of weighing &lsqb;3rd c. BCE&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ζυγόν</i> (zugón, “scale”) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “settle”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of oxen</i>) drawing the yoke</li></ol><p>From <i>ζυγόν</i> (zugón, “yoke”) +&lrm; <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “to drag”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yoked</li></ol><p>From <i>ζυγόω</i> (zugóō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoke-band (a cord or strap for fastening the yoke to the pole)</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῠγόν</i> (zŭgón, “yoke”) +&lrm; <i>δεσμός</i> (desmós, “bond; fetter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoke, for joining animals</li><li>yoke, burden</li><li>in general, anything which joins two pieces together: cross-bar, plank, beam</li><li>the balancing beam of a scale; the scale itself</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) the constellation Libra</li><li>rank (of soldiers)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dzugón</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yugóm</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ζυγόν</i> (zugón)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yoke or join together</li><li>to close or shut off</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to bring under the yoke or subdue</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῠγόν</i> (zŭgón, “the yoke of a plow or carriage; a crossbar of the lyre”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a living, property</li><li>life</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῶ</i> (zô, “to live”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ζωή)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Zoe, Zoë, Zoé, Zoja, Zoya, or Zoey, meaning “life”</li></ol><p>Already in the Septuagint translation. From <i>ζωή</i> (zōḗ, “life”), fashioned as a semantic loan from Hebrew <i>חַוָּה</i> (ḥawwā, “Eve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave-king at festivals in Babylonia</li></ol><p>Lewy connects the word to Hebrew <i>סגן</i> (ségen, “title for a Jewish priest of the Temple in Jerusalem; second-in-command”), suggesting a Semitic origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who paints from life or from nature</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in metaphorical use</li></ol><li>(<i>generally</i>) a painter</li></ol><p><i>ζωός</i> (zōós, “alive”, “living”) <b>or</b> <i>ζωή</i> (zōḗ, “living”, “life”) +&lrm; <i>-γρᾰ́φος</i> (-grắphos, “drawer”, “painter”, “writer”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, take alive, take captive (<i>instead of killing</i>)</li><li>to restore to life, revive</li></ol><p>From <i>ζωός</i> (zōós) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γρέω</i> (ăgréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of painting</li><li>in metaphorical use</li></ol><p><i>ζωγρᾰ́φος</i> (zōgrắphos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a painter’s studio</li></ol><p><i>ζωγρᾰ́φος</i> (zōgrắphos, “a painter”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of painting</li></ol><p>From <i>ζωγρᾰφῐκός</i> (zōgrăphĭkós): as a noun, a substantivisation of its feminine forms in elliptical use for <i>ἡ ζωγρᾰφῐκὴ τέχνη</i> (hē zōgrăphĭkḕ tékhnē, “the painting craft”); as an adjective, regularly declined forms. Zography means literally the writing of life.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled in painting</li><li>connected with painting, used by painters</li></ol><p><i>ζωγρᾰ́φος</i> (zōgrắphos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A small room wherein to rest by day</li><li>A niche in a wall, used as a chapel</li></ol><p>From <i>ζωή</i> (zōḗ, “life”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soup-ladle</li></ol><p>From <i>ζωμός</i> (zōmós, “soup”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρύω</i> (arúō, “to draw water”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soup or sauce to eat with meat or fish</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bloodshed</li><li>fat, greasy fellow</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Generally connected with <i>ζύμη</i> (zúmē, “leaven, beer yeast”), but the ablaut required (<i>ō(u)</i> : <i>ū</i>) is improbable. Connection with <i>ζέω</i> (zéō, “to ferment”) presupposes that Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yos-mo-</i> developed into this word regularly.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lesser calamint (<i>Clinopodium nepeta</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure, sheer, unmixed (<i>of wine without water</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Solmsen and to Vasmer, the word is related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jarъ</i> (“vehement, furious”), suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yeh₃-ro-</i> or <i>&#42;yoH-ro-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior’s belt</li><li>girdle</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ζώννυμι</i> (zṓnnumi), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yeh₃s-</i> (“gird”), +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for giving or maintaining life</li><li>full of life, alive</li><li>characteristic of life, vital</li><li>(<i>of works of art</i>) true to life</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or pertaining to animals</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῷον</i> (zōîon) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alive, living</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ďōwós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷyéh₃-w-os</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷeyh₃-</i>, whence also <i>βίος</i> (bíos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness, gloom</li><li>the dark quarter; the west</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dzópʰos</i>. Beekes claims it's Pre-Greek. But compare <i>Ζέφυρος</i> (Zéphuros), connected by some to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃yebʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hammerhead shark</li></ol><p>From <i>ζυγόν</i> (zugón, “yoke”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest, box</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fastenings</li></ol><p>From <i>ζυγόω</i> (zugóō, “to yoke, join together”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of field maple</li></ol><p>From <i>ζυγία</i> (zugía, “field maple”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bolt, bar</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>engineering</i>) cross rod</li></ol><li>synonym of <i>ζῠγόν</i> (zŭgón)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cheekbone, zygomatic bone</li><li>(<i>navigation</i>) canal lock</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῠγόω</i> (zŭgóō, “to yoke or join together”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>codical variant of <i>ζῦθε</i> (zûthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>codical variant of <i>ζῦθοι</i> (zûthoi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>codical variant of <i>ζῦθον</i> (zûthon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>codical variant of <i>ζῦθος</i> (zûthos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaven</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yúHs-meh₂</i> (“fermentation, soup”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belt, sash</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ζώννῡμι</i> (zṓnnūmi, “to gird”); ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yeh₃s-neh₂</i>, from the root <i>&#42;yeh₃s-</i> (“gird”). Compare Old Church Slavonic <i>(по)ꙗсъ</i> ((po)jasŭ), Sanskrit <i>रास्ना</i> (rā́snā) (&lt; <i>&#42;yā́snā</i>).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ζώνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Zone, Thrace, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gird; especially to gird round the loins in wrestling</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to gird oneself, especially of athletes</li><li>(<i>with dative or accusative</i>) to gird oneself with something</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) to gird up one's loins for battle</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be fastened by means of girths</li><li>to be formed in belts and seams</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yeh₃s-</i> +&lrm; <i>-νῡμι</i> (-nūmi), related to Lithuanian <i>júosta</i> (“girdle”), Latvian <i>jôsta</i> (“girdle”), Avestan <i>𐬫𐬁𐬵-</i> (yāh-, “girdle”), <i>𐬫𐬁𐬯𐬙𐬀</i> (yāsta, “girdled”), Albanian <i>gjesh</i> (“to gird”). See also <i>ζώνη</i> (zṓnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ζῶ</i> (zô, “to live”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eager rivalry, zealous imitation, emulation, a noble passion</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) zeal for one</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) the object of emulation or desire, happiness, bliss, honour, glory</li><li>(<i>of the style of Asiatic Orators</i>) extravagance, fierceness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yeh₂-</i> (“to go, travel, search, inquire”). Related to <i>Ζητήρ</i> (Zētḗr).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ζῆλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Zelus</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῆλος</i> (zêlos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zeta, the name for the sixth letter Ζ (Z), ζ (z) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤆</i> (z‬ /⁠zayin⁠/), with influence from <i>ἦτα</i> (êta) and <i>θῆτα</i> (thêta).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Egyptian kind of beer brewed with barley</li><li>beer of northern nations</li></ol><p>Unknown; suggested sources have included Egyptian, Proto-Scythian, Pre-Greek, or a native origin, in which case it would be related to <i>ζύμη</i> (zúmē, “yeast”) and <i>ζέω</i> (zéō, “to boil”). See even farther Angami <i>zhūthō</i> (“rice beer”) whence English <i>zutho</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>&lsqb;3rd C. BC–AD 4th C.&rsqb; alternative spelling of <i>ζῦθος</i> (zûthos, “Egyptian beer brewed with barley”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ζωγρᾰφῐ́ᾱ</i> (zōgrăphĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ζωγρᾰφῐκή</i> (zōgrăphĭkḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ζωγρᾰφῐκός</i> (zōgrăphĭkós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or relating to little animals</li><li>Of or relating to the zodiac</li></ol><p>From <i>ζῴδῐον</i> (zōídĭon, “little animal”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós, “-ac”, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make life; to give life</li><li>to quicken</li><li>to vivify, to animate</li></ol><p><i>ζῷον</i> (zōîon) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little animal</li><li>sign of the zodiac</li></ol><p>The diminutive form of <i>ζῷον</i> (zōîon, “animal”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>ζώω</i> (zṓō), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ďṓwō</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷyṓwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷyéh₃woh₂</i>. Doublet of <i>βῐόω</i> (bĭóō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loincloth, drawers, worn next the body in a boxing contest</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) band used in surgery</li><li>woman's girdle</li></ol><p>From <i>ζώννυμι</i> (zṓnnumi, “to gird”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ζῷον</i> (zōîon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal, beast</li><li>(<i>art</i>) form, image</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ζώϊον</i> (zṓïon), from <i>ζώς</i> (zṓs, “living”, contracted from <i>ζωός</i> (zōós)) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ηὐδᾶτο</i> (ēudâto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τε</i> (te) (<i>used before a rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan form of <i>θάλασσα</i> (thálassa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inner chamber, room</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a bedroom</li><li>a bed</li><li>a bridechamber</li><li>a storeroom, especially for valuables</li></ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) a shrine or chapel dedicated to Apis</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) the lowest part of the ship</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, most likely of Pre-Greek origin and perhaps cognate with <i>θόλος</i> (thólos). Kroonen however reconstructs Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰl̥h₂-em-o&#47;eh₂-</i>, and tentatively compares Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dalą</i> (“valley”); compare <i>κάλαμος</i> (kálamos), <i>καλάμη</i> (kalámē) for the formation.</p><p>Compare Phrygian <i>θαλαμειδη</i> (thalameidē, “sepulchral chamber”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea</li><li>the Mediterranean Sea</li><li>channel</li><li>salt water</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a Pre-Greek substrate borrowing tentatively reconstructed as *talakʸa; the element "-σσ-", as well as the local geographic meaning, points to a Pre-Greek origin. Compare the possible cognate Luwian <i>𒀀𒆷𒀸𒊭𒄠𒈪𒅖</i> (/⁠alassammis⁠/).</p><p>Older theories related the word to <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “salt”), cf. Latin <i>sāl</i> (“salt”), <i>salum</i> (“sea”), <i>Salacia</i> (“sea goddess”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>θᾰ́λᾰσσᾰ</i> (thắlăssă); sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meadowrue (<i>Thalictrum minus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for young shoots</li></ol><p>From <i>θαλλός</i> (thallós, “young shoot”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bloom, sprout</li><li>to grow, flourish, thrive</li><li>to swell, abound</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰelh₁-</i>. Cognates include Albanian <i>dal</i> (“to sprout”), Old Armenian <i>դալար</i> (dalar, “verdant; verdure, herb, grass”), <i>դեղ</i> (deł, “herb, medicine, poison, amulet, perfume, dye, kindling”), Middle Irish <i>duille</i> (“leaf”), Welsh <i>dalen</i> (“leaf”), <i>deillio</i> (“to emanate, derive”), Breton <i>delienn</i> (“leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warmth, heat, esp. summer heat</li><li>metaph. a sting, smart caused by an arrow</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm up, to heat</li></ol><p>Related to <i>θαλυκρός</i> (thalukrós, “warm, glowing”), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰal-ukʷ-</i>, likely of Pre-Greek origin. The connection with <i>θάλλω</i> (thállō, “make green”) is unlikely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A warming, a fomenting</li><li>A cherishing</li></ol><p>From <i>θάλπω</i> (thálpō, “to warm up, to heat”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>θαμά</i> (thamá)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amazement, astonishment</li><li>awe of the divine</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Often connected to such forms as <i>θαῦμα</i> (thaûma, “wonder, marvel”), <i>θώψ</i> (thṓps, “flatterer, false friend”), <i>τάφος</i> (táphos, “amazement”) and <i>τέθηπα</i> (téthēpa, “to be astonished”). Beekes rejects additional connections to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dōbnaną</i> (“to become dull”), Middle English <i>dabben</i> (“to strike”), and German <i>tappen</i> (“to frisk”), and claims all the Greek terms are borrowed from non-Indo-European Pre-Greek. The philologist Oswald Szemerényi has suggested a possible connection to <i>&#42;dʰembʰ-</i>, itself possibly a nasalized variant of <i>&#42;dʰebʰ-</i> (“to harm”), whence also perhaps Sanskrit <i>द॒भ्नोति॑</i> (dabhnóti, “to harm, deceive”). According to the linguist Bilyana Mihaylova, this proposal is convincing semantically—compare Ancient Greek <i>ἐκπλήσσω</i> (ekplḗssō, “to strike, frighten”) for a possible parallel example of a development from "to strike" > "to frighten". However, according to Mihaylova, this proposal faces "serious phonetic difficulties". The phonological issues concerning this term are linked to the issue of post-nasal deaspiration in Ancient Greek (see Miller's Law). According to the linguist Roberto Batisti, if such deaspiration is accepted, then the term could be derived from a neuter s-stem <i>&#42;dʰembʰ-es-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀλώπηξ</i> (alṓpēx, “fox”).</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) bush, shrub</li><li>thick copse</li></ol><p>This word exists next to <i>θαμινός</i> (thaminós, “crowded, close-set”) and <i>θαμά</i> (thamá, “often”) just as <i>πυκνός</i> (puknós, “thick, dense”) next to <i>πυκινός</i> (pukinós, “thick”) and <i>πύκα</i> (púka, “thickly, strongly”); the barytonesis is caused by the substantivization. Not, as per Alessio, related to Latin <i>tamnus</i> (“kind of grape”). With its ending in <i>-αμνος</i>, the word seems Pre-Greek; its meaning makes this quite possible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death</li><li>corpse</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>θνῄσκω</i> (thnēískō, “to die”), with the suffix from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-etos</i>, also seen in <i>βίοτος</i> (bíotos, “life”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θάνατος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Thanatos</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́νᾰτος</i> (thắnătos, “death”). Cognate with Ancient Macedonian <i>Δάνος</i> (Dános).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to honour with funeral rites</li><li>to bury</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰə́pťō</i>, perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰm̥bʰyéti</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰembʰ-</i> (“to dig”), with cognates including Old Armenian <i>դամբան</i> (damban, “tomb”) and possibly Avestan <i>𐬛𐬀𐬑𐬨𐬀</i> (daxma), Middle Persian (dhmk'), Old Prussian <i>dambo</i> (“ground”), or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰₔgʷʰtós</i>. Beekes suggests that this term could possibly be a loanword and doubts Indo-European origin. Hrach Martirosyan proposes that the Greek and Armenian terms derive from a Mediterranean-Pontic substratum language.</p><p>The present tense system includes a <b>T</b> progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courage; confidence; audacity</li><li>that which gives courage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰers-</i> (“to dare”). Cognate with English <i>dare</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьrzъ</i> (“bold”), Sanskrit <i>धृषु</i> (dhṛṣú, “proud”) and Albanian <i>nder</i> (“honor, favour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) relying [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘on’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́ρσος</i> (thắrsos, “courage”) +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>sit; sit idly</li></ol><p>Contraction of earlier Epic <i>θᾰᾰ́σσω</i> (thăắssō), found in Homer and the <i>Homeric Hymn to Hermes</i>, itself from <i>θᾰϝᾰ́κψω</i> (thăwắkpsō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>τὰ</i> (tà) +&lrm; <i>ἕτερᾰ</i> (héteră)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>θᾱ́σσων</i> (thā́ssōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow, sallow, of the color of the fustic</li></ol><p>From <i>θάψος</i> (thápsos, “fustic”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fustic (<i>Cotinus coggygria</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>Θάψος</i> (Thápsos), because the plant was originally from this place.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θάψος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thapsus, Tunisia</li><li>Thapsos, Sicily</li></ol><p>Phoenician name, but of unknown meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>view, sight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰáwā</i>, from the root of <i>θεάομαι</i> (theáomai) and <i>θαῦμα</i> (thaûma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) goddess</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós) +&lrm; <i>-αινα</i> (-aina)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theatre, gathering place</li><li>play, spectacle</li></ol><p>From <i>θεάομαι</i> (theáomai, “to view”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sulphur-colored</li></ol><p>From <i>θεῖον</i> (theîon, “sulphur”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos), through the earlier form <i>θέειον</i> (théeion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos, “divine, holy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to charm, enchant, bewitch</li><li>to cheat, cozen</li><li>to charm, beguile, deceive</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Several unconvincing hypotheses: that is related to Lithuanian <i>žvelgiù</i> (“to look at”) (connecting it as "enchanting by an evil look"); to Sanskrit <i>ह्वरते</i> (hvarate, “to turn, diverge”) with Greek enlargment <i>-γ-</i>; to Germanic words like Old English <i>dolg</i> (“wound, scar”) and Old High German <i>tolg</i> (“wound”), both from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dulgą</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>will, desire</li><li>errand</li></ol><p>From <i>θέλω</i> (thélō, “I resolve”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐθέλω</i> (ethélō)</li></ol><p>Metanalysis of <i>ἐθέλω</i> (ethélō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is placed or laid down</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>deposited money; treasure; (grain) deposit</li><li>loaf heap</li><li>coffer</li><li>position; (land) situation</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) nativity; horoscope.</li><li>common burial place; communal cemetery</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>by extension</i>) common land</li><li>(<i>also</i>) private burial ground</li></ol></ol><li>something proposed as a prize</li><li>case proposed for discussion; an argument's theme</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>proposition; premiss</li><li>(<i>law</i>) case</li><li>arbitrary determination</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) canonical wordform; lemma; lexeme</li><li>(logic,&#32;Stoicism) mode of reduction of an irregular syllogism</li></ol><li>a Theme (regional unit of government of the Byzantine Empire from c. the 7<sup>th</sup>–11<sup>th</sup> centuries)</li></ol><p>From <i>τῐ́θημῐ</i> (tĭ́thēmĭ, “to set, put, place”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundations, lowest part, roots</li><li>eye socket (Homer)</li></ol><p>From <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>βέβαιος</i> (bébaios, “firm, steady”), <i>σεμνός</i> (semnós, “revered, holy”), <i>εὐσταθής</i> (eustathḗs, “stable”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put”), the same root of <i>θεμέλιος</i> (themélios, “foundation, basis”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old, established law or custom</li><li>divinely ordained justice</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to place”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i>, possibly combined with the stem of <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>. Beekes prefers to consider it of Pre-Greek origin, but this is dismissed by Beckwith, Nikolaev, and others.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θέμις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Themis</li></ol><p>From <i>θέμις</i> (thémis, “law, custom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the palm of the hand</li><li>the sole of the foot</li><li>the flat top of an altar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰénwr̥</i> (“flat of the hand, palm”). Compare Sanskrit <i>धन्व॑न्</i> (dhánvan) and Old High German <i>tenar</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>προσήκοντες</i> (prosḗkontes, “kinsmen, relatives”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θέρμη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Therma, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>θέρμη</i> (thérmē, “heat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of lupines</li></ol><p>From <i>θερμός</i> (thermós, “lupine”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat</li><li>summer</li><li>harvest; crop</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰéros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰéros</i> (“heat; warm weather”, from <i>&#42;gʷʰer-</i> (“warm, hot”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-os</i> (action noun suffix)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heat, warm, make hot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰérō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰéreti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;gʷʰer-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a setting, placement, arrangement</li><li>deposit</li><li>adoption (of a child)</li><li>adoption (in the more general sense of accepting as one's own)</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) position, conclusion, thesis</li><li>(<i>dance</i>) putting down the foot</li><li>(<i>metre</i>) the last half of the foot</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) affirmation</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) stop</li></ol><p>From <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to put, place”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis), although it could either have been formed in Greek or go back earlier. In the latter case, would be from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéh₁tis</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (root of <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi)). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>अपिहिति</i> (ápihiti), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬭𐬋𐬌𐬛𐬍𐬙𐬌</i> (arōidīti), Latin <i>conditiō</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌲𐌰𐌳𐌴𐌸𐍃</i> (gadēþs). More at deed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marvellous, wondrous, supernatural</li></ol><p>Compounded from <i>θεσ-</i> (thes-, which is the root of <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”)) +&lrm; <i>κέλομαι</i> (kélomai, “to urge, exhort”), thus properly "moved by a god".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divinely ordained or decreed</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) amazing, wondrous, powerful</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰés-pʰatos</i>; equivalent to <i>θεσ-</i> (thes-, “god”) +&lrm; <i>φατός</i> (phatós, “spoken”, from <i>φημί</i> (phēmí, “to speak”)); compare <i>θέσκελος</i> (théskelos), <i>θεσπέσιος</i> (thespésios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>θέτα</i> (théta)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>θέτε</i> (théte)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>θέτο</i> (théto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who places</li><li>mortgager</li><li>adoptive father of a child</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to set, place”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run fast: to dash, race, sprint, speed</li><li>to fly</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>θεϝ-</i> (thew-), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰew-</i> (“to run, flow”). Cognate to Sanskrit <i>धाव॑ति</i> (dhā́vati, “to run; to fly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) theosis, deification</li></ol><p>From <i>θεόω</i> (theóō, “to deify”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box; chest</li><li>grave; tomb; burial</li><li>scabbard</li></ol><p>From some derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to do; to set”), and compared in particular to Sanskrit <i>धाक</i> (dhāká, “container”). See also <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to place; to put”), from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wild beast, beast of prey, especially a lion</li><li>any animal</li><li>a fantastic animal, such as the Sphinx</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰḗr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰwer-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>ferus</i> (“wild, savage”), and Old Church Slavonic <i>звѣрь</i> (zvěrĭ, “wild animal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) hunting</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) hunt, chase</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰwer-</i> via Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰḗr</i>. Compare Ancient Greek <i>θήρ</i> (thḗr, “wild animal, beast”) and Tocharian B <i>śerwe</i> (“hunter”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θήρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thira, Santorini (an island in the Cyclades, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prey, spoil, game</li></ol><p>From <i>θηρᾰ́ω</i> (thērắō, “to hunt, chase”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>θήρᾱ</i> (thḗrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) serf or bondsman</li><li>(<i>Attic</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hired laborer; (male) servant</li><li>mercenary</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) an Athenian citizen of the fourth and lowest class according to the Solonian constitution of Athens</li></ol><li>(<i>Cypriot</i>) slave</li></ol><p>From <i>τῐ́θημῐ</i> (tĭ́thēmĭ, “to place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thiasus, Bacchic revel, rout</li><li>religious guild, confraternity</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) company, troop</li><li>feast, banquet</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>θύρσος</i> (thúrsos). An expression of the Dionysiac religion, and as such suspected of foreign origin: probably Pre-Greek, in spite of older interpretations in Indo-European terms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touching</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) apprehension of the mind</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>θιγγάνω</i> (thingánō, “to touch, grasp”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap (especially of sand)</li><li>beach, shore</li><li>sandbank, bar at the mouth of the river</li><li>sand or mud at the bottom of the sea</li><li>shoreweed</li></ol><p>Due to the improbability of the other explanations, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>θᾱ́σσω</i> (thā́ssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pivot of a door or gate</li><li>axle of a chariot</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a technical term, which Brugmann derived from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰwaryós</i>, itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰwer-</i> (“door”). Compare <i>θύρα</i> (thúra, “door”).</p><p>On the other hand, Lubotsky and Mayrhofer derive the term from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰurh₁-</i> (“chariot pole”), and compare it with Hittite (tūrii̯a-, “to harness”), Sanskrit <i>धुर्</i> (dhur, “yoke, pole”), and perhaps Tocharian A <i>tursko</i> (“draught ox”). Given the similarity in form and, to some extent, meaning between the two proto-Indo-European forms, it is possible they are connected.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of, in, on, or from the sea, marine, maritime</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>into the sea</li><li>sea-going</li></ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) skilled in the sea, nautical</li><li>like the sea</li></ol><p><i>θᾰ́λᾰσσᾰ</i> (thắlăssă, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>θᾰλᾰ́σσῐος</i> (thălắssĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chambermaid; a waiting maid</li><li>a eunuch, especially one of the bedchamber</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a bridegroom</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) bridal</li></ol><p>From <i>θάλαμος</i> (thálamos) +&lrm; <i>πέλω</i> (pélō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cannabis, bhang (<i>Cannabis sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>θάλασσα</i> (thálassa, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>αἴγλη</i> (aíglē, “light, radiance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>describing the sea</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́λᾰσσᾰ</i> (thắlăssă, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea kale (<i>Crambe maritima</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́λᾰσσᾰ</i> (thắlăssă, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>κράμβη</i> (krámbē, “cabbage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>empire of the sea, maritime power</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́λᾰσσᾰ</i> (thắlăssă, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>-κρᾰτῐ́ᾱ</i> (-krătĭ́ā, “rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who works on the sea, a fisherman or sailor</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́λᾰσσᾰ</i> (thắlăssă, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drink of brine and honey</li></ol><p>From <i>θάλασσα</i> (thálassa, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blooming (thriving in health, beauty and vigor)</li><li>stout, sturdy</li><li>hale, vigorous</li><li>fresh, young, youthful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰl̥h₁rós</i>, whence possibly also Sanskrit <i>धीर</i> (dhīra, “steady, brave, energetic, resolute”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰelh₁-</i> (“to bloom”), the root of <i>θάλλω</i> (thállō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) young shoot, young branch (especially of the olive)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) palm leaves, which were plaited into baskets</li><li>a gift, especially one given at festivals, or given to a landlord by one whose bid for a lease was accepted</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰelh₁-</i> (“to bloom, be green”), whence also <i>θάλλω</i> (thállō, “to bloom”). Compare Welsh <i>dail</i> (“leaves”) and Middle Irish <i>duille</i> (“foliage”). The sense “gift” comes about as a semantic loan from Egyptian, where two etymologically unrelated terms <i>mnḥ</i> (“papyrus stalk”) and <i>mnḥtj</i> (“tribute, gift”) were homophonous in Late Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warming, fostering</li></ol><p>From <i>θάλπω</i> (thálpō) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warming, warmth</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) comfort, consolation, refreshment</li></ol><p>Nominal derivative of <i>θάλπω</i> (thálpō, “to warm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hot, glowing</li></ol><p>Probably from Pre-Greek and related to <i>θάλπω</i> (thálpō, “to warm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>often</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>θάμνος</i> (thámnos, “thick copse”) and <i>θαμινός</i> (thaminós, “crowded”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) astonished</li></ol><p>See <i>θάμβος</i> (thámbos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crowded, close-set</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>θάμνος</i> (thámnos, “bush, shrub”) and <i>θαμά</i> (thamá, “often”); compare the synonymous <i>θαμέες</i> (thamées).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crowded; close-set; thick</li></ol><p>Cognate with <i>θαμινός</i> (thaminós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deadly</li><li>(<i>relational</i>) of death, deathly</li><li>near death</li><li>mortal</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>θάνατος</i> (thánatos) and <i>θνῄσκω</i> (thnēískō) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deadly, lethal, mortiferous</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́νᾰτος</i> (thắnătos, “death”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deadly, fatal</li><li>capital (punishment by death)</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́νᾰτος</i> (thắnătos, “death”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>θᾰρσέω</i> (thărséō)</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́ρρος</i> (thắrrhos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daring, courageous</li><li>(<i>negative</i>) overbold, audacious</li></ol><p>From <i>θάρρος</i> (thárrhos, “courage”) +&lrm; <i>-αλέος</i> (-aléos) <i>(Late Attic)</i>. Also <i>θαρσαλέος</i> (tharsaléos) from <i>θάρσος</i> (thársos) <i>(Ionic and Ancient Attic)</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare; to be of courage</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́ρσος</i> (thắrsos, “courage”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daring; courageous; bold</li><li>audacious</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>daringly; courageously; boldly</li><li>audaciously</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰρσᾰλέος</i> (thărsăléos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to encourage; to cheer; to embolden</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be of good courage</li></ol><p>From <i>θάρσος</i> (thársos, “courage”) +&lrm; <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to wonder, marvel, be astonished</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to look on with wonder and amazement, to wonder at</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to revere, honor, admire, worship</li><li>to say with astonishment</li></ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to wonder at, marvel at</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;with dative</i>) to wonder at</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be looked at with wonder</li><li>to be admired</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>θαῦμα</i> (thaûma, “wonder, marvel”) +&lrm; <i>-ι̯ω</i> (-i̯ō, denominative verb suffix), or superficially +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wonderful, wonderly</li><li>admirable</li><li>strange</li></ol><p>From <i>θαῦμα</i> (thaûma, stem: <i>θαυματ-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wondering</li></ol><p>From <i>θαυμάζω</i> (thaumázō, “I wonder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wonderful, marvellous</li><li>admirable</li></ol><p>From <i>θαυμάζω</i> (thaumázō, “to admire, be surprised at”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>θαυματοποιός</i> (thaumatopoiós): wonder-working; acrobatic; juggler</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conjurer, magician, juggler</li><li>puppet-showman, puppet maker</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) miracle worker, wonderworker</li></ol><p>From <i>θαῦμα, θαυματ-</i> (thaûma, thaumat-) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós). Also substantivised.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deadly carrot (<i>Thapsia garganica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>θάψος</i> (thápsos, “fustic”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wonder, marvel, something strange</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) jugglers' tricks</li></ol><li>the feeling of wonder, astonishment</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₂w-</i> (whence <i>θεάομαι</i> (theáomai), <i>θῆβος</i> (thêbos), <i>θήγεια</i> (thḗgeia)), an extension of <i>&#42;dʰeh₂-</i>, with no clear cognates outside Hellenic; other extensions include <i>θάμβος</i> (thámbos) (&lt; <i>&#42;dʰh₂n̥bʰos</i>), <i>τάφος</i> (táphos) (&lt; <i>&#42;dʰh₂bʰos</i>), <i>θώψ</i> (thṓps) (&lt; <i>&#42;dʰoh₂ps</i>), <i>τέθηπα</i> (téthēpa) (&lt; <i>&#42;dʰedʰeh₂pm̥</i>), while a potential extra-Hellenic comparandum includes Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dawāną</i> (“to marvel”).</p><p>Beekes, on the other hand, speculates all these formations as Pre-Greek. See also <i>θεάομαι</i> (theáomai, “to view, contemplate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goddess</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>θεᾱ́</i> (theā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to view, watch, observe, gaze</li><li>to contemplate</li><li>to review</li></ol><p>Usually compared to Sanskrit <i>धी</i> (dhī́), <i>ध्यान</i> (dhyāna), Avestan <i>𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬨𐬀𐬥</i> (daēman), and other similar forms, ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeyh₂-</i>. May have been first Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰāwéomai</i>/<i>&#42;tʰāwáomai</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₂w-</i>. Forte suggests that <i>&#42;dʰeyh₂-</i> and <i>&#42;dʰeh₂w-</i> are both variants of a more basic Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₂-</i>, while Beekes takes the word and its relatives as Pre-Greek. See <i>θαῦμα</i> (thaûma) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>θεᾱ́</i> (theā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aunt</li></ol><p>Feminine counterpart of <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos, “uncle”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from the gods or God, divine</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) belonging or sacred to a god, holy</li><li>superhuman (<i>used of heroic figures</i>)</li><li>imperial</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the brother of one's father or mother, uncle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰḗhyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéh₁s-yo-</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰéh₁s</i>. Equivalent to <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix). Cognate with Latin <i>fēriae</i>.</p><p>Probably an onomatopoeic formation. Compare <i>τήθη</i> (tḗthē, “grandmother”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, wound</li></ol><p>Disputed. Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰéňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰén-ye-ti</i>. Alternatively, the linguist Andreas Willi suggests that it may have been secondarily formed from <i>&#42;θένω</i> (&#42;thénō), itself possibly from a thematic root present <i>&#42;gʷʰén-e-ti</i>. Either way, the verb traces back to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰen-</i> (“to strike, slay”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>हन्ति</i> (hanti); Avestan <i>𐬘𐬀𐬌𐬥𐬙𐬌</i> (jainti); Old Armenian <i>գան</i> (gan) and <i>ջնեմ</i> (ǰnem); Latin <i>offendō</i>, and Old English <i>bana</i> (English <i>bane</i>). See also the aorist <i>ἔπεφνον</i> (épephnon) and the noun <i>φόνος</i> (phónos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>θέω</i> (théō, “to run, fly”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divinity</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the acts of the gods</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) sulfur</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter form of <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos, “divine”).</p><p>From earlier <i>θέειον</i> (théeion), <i>&#42;θέϝειον</i> (&#42;théweion). Usually connected to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“smoke, haze”), implying an original meaning of "fumigant", the same root as <i>θῡμιάω</i> (thūmiáō, “to burn, smoke”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from the gods or God, divine</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) belonging or sacred to a god, holy</li><li>superhuman (<i>used of heroic figures</i>)</li><li>imperial</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the brother of one's father or mother, uncle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰḗhyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéh₁s-yo-</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰéh₁s</i>. Equivalent to <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix). Cognate with Latin <i>fēriae</i>.</p><p>Probably an onomatopoeic formation. Compare <i>τήθη</i> (tḗthē, “grandmother”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, place, set</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in phrases</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(with <i>πόδα</i> (póda)) to plant the foot, i.e. walk, run</li><li>(with <i>ἐν χειρί, ἐν χερσίν</i> (en kheirí, en khersín)) to put something into someone's hands</li><li>(with <i>παῖδα</i> (paîda), <i>υἱὸν</i> (huiòn), etc. <i>ὑπὸ ζώνῃ</i> (hupò zṓnēi)) to have a child put under one's girdle, i.e. to conceive</li><li>(with <i>ἐν ὄμμασι</i> (en ómmasi)) to set before one's eyes</li><li>(with <i>ψῆφον</i> (psêphon)) to give one's vote or opinion, to vote</li><li>(with <i>ἐν στήθεσσι</i> (en stḗthessi), <i>ἐν φρεσί</i> (en phresí), etc.) to put or plant in one's heart</li><li>(with <i>τὰ ὅπλα</i> (tà hópla))</li><li>(with <i>τὰ γόνατα</i> (tà gónata)) to kneel</li></ol><li>to deposit</li><li>to pay</li><li>to put down in writing</li><li>to bury</li><li>to offer, set before</li><li>to assign, award</li><li>(often with <i>νόμον</i> (nómon)) to lay down, enact</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to agree upon</li><li>(<i>of a legal document</i>) to execute</li></ol><li>to establish, institute</li><li>to order, ordain, cause to happen</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in board games</i>) to place (pieces)</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to make, cause to be</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with attributive substantive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to cause to be one's</li></ol><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>)</li><li>to regard, consider as; to hold, reckon that</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to assume</li><li>to affirm</li></ol></ol><li>to make</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in periphrasis</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;t(e&#47;i)tʰēmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰé-dʰeh₁-ti</i>, reduplicated present from <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put, place”). The pay/offer senses are from conflation with <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi) in PIE, see Latin <i>-dō</i>.<reF></p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>faciō</i>, Sanskrit <i>दधाति</i> (dadhāti), Old Armenian <i>դնեմ</i> (dnem), Old English <i>dōn</i> (English <i>do</i>), and Albanian <i>dhatë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who sees or goes to see</li><li>spectator, member of the audience in the theater</li><li>one who contemplates</li></ol><p><i>θεάομαι</i> (theáomai, “to gaze at”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rule exercised by the spectators in a theater</li></ol><p>From <i>θέᾱτρον</i> (théātron, “theater”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a priest</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consult oracles, to practice divination</li><li>to worship as divine</li><li>to fill with the god, inspire</li></ol><p>From <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos, “divine”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke with brimstone, to fumigate and purify</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to purify, hallow</li></ol><li>to make divine, dedicate to a god</li></ol><p>From <i>θεῖον</i> (theîon, “sulphur”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p><p>From <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos, “divine”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>οἰκτρόν</i> (oiktrón, “pitiable”), <i>ἥσυχον</i> (hḗsukhon, “quiet”).</li></ol><p>Unexplained. However, Furnée thinks this is a Pre-Greek word, due to suffix <i>-εμος</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any means of charming or winning, spell, charm (attributed to the girdle of Aphrodite)</li></ol><p>From <i>θέλγω</i> (thélgō, “to charm, bewitch”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the foundation</li><li>(as a masculine or neuter substantive, sc. <i>λίθος</i> (líthos)) foundation-stone, foundation, basis</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰh₁-m-eló-</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>genealogy of the gods</li><li>theogony, origination or birth of gods</li></ol><p>From <i>θεόγονος</i> (theógonos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>godlike</li></ol><p><i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “like”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(politics,&#32;religion) theocracy</li></ol><p>From <i>θεο-</i> (theo-, “god, deity”) +&lrm; <i>-κρατία</i> (-kratía, “power, rule”) from <i>θεός</i> and <i>κρᾰτέω</i>.</p><p>Coined in the 1st century CE by Josephus (<i>Against Apion</i> 2.17) to denote the idealistic political system of a Jewish state ordained by Moses, as contrasted with democracy, oligarchy and monarchy.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>god-slayer, deicide</li></ol><p><i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-κτόνος</i> (-któnos, “slayer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) theology</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) oration in praise of a god</li></ol><p>From <i>θεολογέω</i> (theologéō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who speaks of God and the divine nature, a theologian</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-λόγος</i> (-lógos, “-logist”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fighting against God</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>μάχομαι</i> (mákhomai, “I fight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight against God, or the gods</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>μάχομαι</i> (mákhomai, “to fight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the suffering of God</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god, divine”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰ́θη</i> (pắthē, “suffering”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the state or behaviour of one stricken by a god, madness, blindness</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “strike”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prophesy, prophesize</li></ol><p>From <i>θεοπρόπος</i> (theoprópos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prophetic</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) seer, diviner, prophet, oracle</li><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) servant sent to seek oracles</li></ol><p>From <i>θεο-</i> (theo-) +&lrm; <i>πρέπω</i> (prépō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religiousness, reverence for the divine</li></ol><p><i>θεοσεβής</i> (theosebḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia), synchronically analyzable as <i>θεοσεβής</i> (theosebḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>God-fearing</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós) +&lrm; <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Knowledge of divine things.</li></ol><p>From <i>θεόσοφος</i> (theósophos, “wise in divine things”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>With knowledge in divine things.</li></ol><p>From Koine Greek <i>θεόσοφος</i> (theósophos, “wise in divine things”, adjective).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a land</i>) which gives birth to gods</li><li>for the sense "Virgin Mary" see <i>Θεοτόκος</i> (Theotókos, “Mother of God; Theotokos”), (<i>Koine, first attested at w:Origen</i>)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>θεο-</i> (theo-, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-τόκος</i> (-tókos, “who bears, gives birth”) from <i>θεός</i>&nbsp;m (theós) + <i>τόκος</i>&nbsp;m (tókos, “birth”) (&lt; <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō, “bear”), by ellipsis of a noun like <i>γῆ</i>&nbsp;f (gê, “land, country”). Probably a translation from an Egyptian epithet with sense "birthplace of every god". The usage of the word in non-Christian sense (for which, see <i>Θεοτόκος</i> (Theotókos)) is unusual.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θεοτόκος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) God-bearer; Mother of God; Holy Mother of God; Theotokos, a sacred title given to the Virgin Mary as the mother of Jesus Christ</li></ol><p>As Koine adjective <i>θεοτόκος</i> (theotókos), substantivised feminine noun &lt; <i>Θεός</i> (Theós, “God”) +&lrm; <i>τόκος</i> (tókos, “childbirth; offspring”) from <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō, “I beget”). Sense "Virgin Mary" already by Origen of Alexandria (c. 185 – c. 253).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) fearing the gods</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós) +&lrm; <i>δέος</i> (déos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divine work</li><li>sacramental rite, mystery</li></ol><p>From <i>θεουργός</i> (theourgós) (from <i>θεός</i> (theós) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós) + <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of a god</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god, deity”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female servant, maid, maidservant</li><li>female slave</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion of lower rank; comrade; attendant; aide</li><li>servant; slave</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root as <i>τέραμνον</i> (téramnon, “chamber”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a waiting on service</li><li>medical treatment</li></ol><p>From <i>θεραπεύω</i> (therapeúō, “to heal”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who waits upon another; attendant.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>worshiper (who waits upon a deity)</li><li>courtier (who waits upon a ruler)</li><li>medical attendant (who waits upon someone who's ill)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>θεραπεύω</i> (therapeúō, “I wait on, attend, serve, cure”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inclined to serve, servile</li><li>(<i>in good and bad sense</i>) courteous, obsequious</li><li>inclined to take care of, careful of</li><li>(<i>of medical treatment</i>) therapeutic, curative</li></ol><p>From <i>θερᾰπευτής</i> (therăpeutḗs, “attendant”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait on, attend, serve</li><li>to obey</li><li>to flatter, placate</li><li>to consult</li><li>to cure, heal, restore</li><li>to cultivate, till (of land)</li><li>to protect</li></ol><p>From <i>θερᾰ́πων</i> (therắpōn, “attendant, aide”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or in summer</li></ol><p>From <i>θέρος</i> (théros, “summer”) +&lrm; <i>-ινός</i> (-inós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kettle or pot for boiling water</li></ol><p>From <i>θερμαίνω</i> (thermaínō, “to warm, heat”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kettle</li></ol><p>From <i>θερμο-</i> (thermo-, “hot, warm”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warm, hot, boiling, glowing</li><li>hotheaded, hasty, rash, headstrong</li><li>eager, active, fresh</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lupine, especially <i>Lupinus albus</i></li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰermós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰer-mo-s</i> (“warm”).</p><p>Cognates include Albanian <i>zjarr</i>, Persian گرم (garm), Sanskrit <i>घर्म</i> (gharma), Old Armenian <i>ջերմ</i> (ǰerm), Latin <i>formus</i>, and Old English <i>wearm</i> (English <i>warm</i>).</p><p>From Akkadian <i>𒋻𒄷</i> (TAR.MUŠ<sub>8</sub> /⁠tarmuš⁠/), from Sumerian <i>𒋻𒄷</i> (TAR.MUŠ<sub>8</sub> /⁠tarmuš⁠/). Cognate to Coptic <i>ⲑⲁⲣⲙⲟⲩⲥ</i> (tharmous), Aramaic <i>תורמוסא</i> / <i>ܬܘܪܡܣܐ</i> (tūrmūsā, “lupine”), Arabic تُرْمُس (turmus).</p><p>An alternative theory by Stromberg (favored by Beekes) considers the word a nominalization of Etymology 1, with regular accent shift.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>θερμός</i> (thermós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>θερμός</i> (thermós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is laid down, law, ordinance</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) rule, precept, rite</li><li>institution, tribunal</li><li>site, place</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰh₁-dʰmó-s</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to do, put, place”). Cognate with Welsh <i>deddf</i> (“act, statute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divine</li><li>prodigious, vast, wondrous, mighty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰeh-</i> (the root of <i>θεός</i> (theós)) + <i>&#42;-sp-</i> (zero grade of the root of <i>ἐνέπω</i> (enépō)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéh₁s</i> (“deity”) and <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“to say”) respectively.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) divinely kindled</li></ol><p><i>θέσπις</i> (théspis) +&lrm; <i>δαίω</i> (daíō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for placing, apposite</li><li>concerning adoption</li><li>belonging to a thesis, disputable</li><li>positive, affirmative</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>grammar</i>) positive</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>neuter substantive</i>) the positive degree</li><li>expressing obligation, of verbals ending in <i>-τέον</i> (-téon)</li></ol></ol><li>arbitrary</li></ol><p>From <i>θέσῐς</i> (thésĭs) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós), from <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “I place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>θετῐκός</i> (thetĭkós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>positively, affirmatively</li></ol><p>Adverbial form of <i>θετῐκός</i> (thetĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>placed, put, settled</li><li>adopted; adoptive</li></ol><p>From <i>θε-</i> (the-) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós), from <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sent (as a <i>θεωρός</i> (theōrós)) to consult an oracle</li><li>to look at, spectate, observe</li><li>to contemplate, consider (<i>of the mind</i>)</li><li>(<i>abstract</i>) to speculate, theorize</li></ol><p>From <i>θεωρός</i> (theōrós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sending of state-ambassadors (θεωροί)</li><li>embassy, mission</li><li>sight, spectacle, viewing</li><li>consideration, theory, speculation</li></ol><p>From <i>θεωρός</i> (theōrós, “spectator”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contemplative, speculative, fond of contemplating [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>ingenious</li><li>allegorical</li></ol><p>From <i>θεωρέω</i> (theōréō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>speculatively, from a theoretical perspective</li></ol><p>Hellenistic adverb from the ancient adjective <i>θεωρητῐκός</i> (theōrētĭkós, “speculative”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, “suffix for adverbs”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a θεωρία (theōría)</li></ol><p>From <i>θεωρία</i> (theōría, “embassy, mission; theory”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spectator</li><li>envoy sent to consult an oracle</li></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;θεᾱ-(ϝ)ορός</i> (&#42;theā-(w)orós), <i>&#42;θεη-(ϝ)ορός</i> (&#42;theē-(w)orós), from the roots of <i>θέᾱ</i> (théā, “sight, view, perspective”) + <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō, “I see”), i.e. "(of one who) watches a show". A relation to <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) is implausible according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>God-breathed, inspired by God</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “to blow, breathe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inspired of God</li></ol><p>From <i>θεό- (θεός)</i> (theó- (theós), “god”) +&lrm; <i>-πνοος</i> (-pnoos, “breath”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>divine (used only in comparative: <i>θεώτερος</i> (theṓteros))</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a deity; a god</li><li>an immortal being</li><li>divine or godly</li><li>title of a ruler</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;feminine</i>) a goddess</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰehós</i> (whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀳𐀃</i> (te-o)), a thematicization of amphikinetic Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéh₁-s-(s) ~ &#42;dʰh₁-s-és</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to do, to put, to place”) + <i>&#42;-s</i> (<i>s-</i>stem forming suffix). Cognate with Phrygian <i>δεως</i> (“to the gods”), Old Armenian <i>դիք</i> (dikʻ, “gods”) and Latin <i>fēriae</i> (“festival days”), <i>fānum</i> (“temple”) and <i>fēstus</i> (“festive”).</p><p>Despite its similarity in form and meaning, the word is not related to Latin <i>deus</i>; the two come from different roots. A true cognate of <i>deus</i> is <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θεός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>God</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feared as divine</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai, “fear, honor, worship”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wise in divine things</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós, “a god, God”) +&lrm; <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “skilled, wise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) divinity, divine nature, Godhead</li></ol><p>From <i>θεός</i> (theós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sight, spectacle</li><li>vision, intuition</li><li>speculation, theory, proposition</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) arts and sciences</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) theorem, mathematical statement</li><li>investigation, inquiry</li></ol><p>From <i>θεωρέω</i> (theōréō, “to look at; to consider, contemplate”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divinity</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the acts of the gods</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) sulfur</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter form of <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos, “divine”).</p><p>From earlier <i>θέειον</i> (théeion), <i>&#42;θέϝειον</i> (&#42;théweion). Usually connected to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“smoke, haze”), implying an original meaning of "fumigant", the same root as <i>θῡμιάω</i> (thūmiáō, “to burn, smoke”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from the gods or God, divine</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) belonging or sacred to a god, holy</li><li>superhuman (<i>used of heroic figures</i>)</li><li>imperial</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the brother of one's father or mother, uncle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰḗhyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéh₁s-yo-</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰéh₁s</i>. Equivalent to <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix). Cognate with Latin <i>fēriae</i>.</p><p>Probably an onomatopoeic formation. Compare <i>τήθη</i> (tḗthē, “grandmother”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>θεάομαι</i> (theáomai); to gaze, marvel</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whetstone, hone</li></ol><p>From <i>θήγω</i> (thḗgō, “to sharpen, whet”), with a formation similar to <i>δρεπάνη</i> (drepánē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a teat, a nipple</li><li>the head of a pole</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁léh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i> (“to suck, suckle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>womanlike, womanly, feminine</li><li>(<i>of women</i>) womanish, ultra-feminine</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) feminine</li></ol><p>From <i>θῆλῠς</i> (thêlŭs, “woman”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bracken (<i>Pteris aquilina</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>θῆλυς</i> (thêlus, “female”) +&lrm; <i>πτέρις</i> (ptéris, “fern”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow monkshood (<i>Aconitum anthora</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>θηλυφόνος</i> (thēluphónos, “killing women”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Arcadia</i>) cork oak (<i>Quercus suber</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>θῆλυς</i> (thêlus, “female”) +&lrm; <i>πρῖνος</i> (prînos, “holm oak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéh₁mō</i>, collective of <i>&#42;dʰéh₁mn̥</i>. Related to <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to put, to place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(enclitic,&#32;chiefly&#32;Epic&#32;and&#32;Doric) doubtless, surely, forsooth</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>θηρατήρ</i> (thēratḗr): hunter</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunt</li></ol><p>From <i>θήρ</i> (thḗr, “beast of prey”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰḗr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰwer-</i> (“wild”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild animal, beast, savage beast</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>animal</li><li>a poisonous animal</li></ol><li>(<i>diminutive</i>) a small animal, insect, worm</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a malignant ulcer</li><li>a term of reproach</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Lupus</li></ol><p>From <i>θήρ</i> (thḗr, “animal, beast”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunter</li></ol><p>From <i>θηρεύειν</i> (thēreúein, “to hunt”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hunt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an antidote against a poisonous bite</li></ol><p>From <i>θηρῐᾰκός</i> (thērĭăkós, “of little beasts”), here particularly associated with poisonous reptiles.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or related to smaller wildlife.</li></ol><p>From <i>θηρῐ́ον</i> (thērĭ́on, “little beast”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fighting against wild beasts</li></ol><p>From <i>θηρίον</i> (thēríon, “wild beast”) +&lrm; <i>μάχομαι</i> (mákhomai, “I fight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bite of a serpent</li></ol><p>From <i>θηρίον</i> (thēríon, “beast, poisonous animal”) +&lrm; <i>δῆγμα</i> (dêgma, “bite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the nature of a beast, savageness, brutality</li></ol><p>From <i>θηρῐ́ον</i> (thērĭ́on, “beast”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasurer</li><li>guard of the state-granaries</li></ol><p>From <i>θησαυρός</i> (thēsaurós, “treasure”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasure</li><li>vault, repository, chest</li></ol><p>Beekes derives it from Pre-Greek, based on its technical semantic domain and lack of resemblance to various Indo-European roots.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hot</li><li>delicate, dainty, luxurious</li></ol><p>Although the etymology is uncertain, the prenasalized form points to Pre-Greek origin. Therefore, not related to <i>φοῖβος</i> (phoîbos, “pure, bright, brilliant”), as per Ehrlich, who derives it from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰigʷ-ro-</i>, based on comparison with Slovene <i>žigra</i> (“tinder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch, handle</li><li>to take hold of</li><li>to touch, attempt</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of the feelings) to touch, reach the heart</li><li>(<i>in speaking or discussion</i>) to touch on, mention briefly</li><li>to reach, win</li></ol><p>This verb has been analyzed as a nasal present of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeyǵʰ-</i> (“to mold”), with supposed parallels in Latin <i>fingō</i> (“to shape; feign”) and Old Armenian <i>դիզանեմ</i> (dizanem, “to heap up, gather”). However, this faces the phonetic problem that <i>&#42;θιγχ-</i> (&#42;thinkh-) should be expected. Unresolved.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Boeotian form of <i>θεοπροπέω</i> (theopropéō)</li><li>to be a θεοπρόπος (theoprópos, “oracle, diviner”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Boeotian, Arcadocypriot, and Laconian form of <i>θεός</i> (theós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A bruise, a contusion</li></ol><p>From <i>θλάω</i> (thláō, “I crush, I bruise”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shepherd's purse (<i>Capsella bursa-pastoris</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. The traditional derivation from <i>θλᾰ́ω</i> (thlắō, “to crush; to bruise”) is folk-etymological.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, bruise</li><li>to overload</li><li>to oppress</li></ol><p>Unknown, though note also <i>θλίβω</i> (thlíbō, “to squeeze, distress”) and <i>φλάω</i> (phláō, “to crush, bruise”). Not related to <i>θλᾰ́σπῐς</i> (thlắspĭs, “shepherd's purse”), despite folk etymological linkage.</p><p>MacBain speculatively compares Old Irish <i>dlùth</i> (“close, compact”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze, chafe, exercise pressure</li><li>to compress, straiten</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to oppress, afflict, distress</li></ol><p>Unknown. It has been proposed to be a blend between <i>θλάω</i> (thláō, “to crush, bruise”) and <i>φλίβω</i> (phlíbō, “to squeeze”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pressure</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) oppression, affliction</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>θλίβω</i> (thlíbō, “to press”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>θνῄσκω</i> (thnēískō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>θνῄσκω</i> (thnēískō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>θνητογενής</i> (thnētogenḗs)</li></ol><p>From <i>θνᾱτός</i> (thnātós) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to the mortal race or kind</li></ol><p>From <i>θνητός</i> (thnētós) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liable to death, mortal</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) befitting mortals</li></ol><p>From <i>θνῄσκω</i> (thnēískō, “to die”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>θνῄσκω</i> (thnēískō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die; (<i>aorist and perfect</i>) to be dead</li><li>(serves as passive of <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō, “kill”)) to be killed</li></ol><p>From a root <i>&#42;θ(ᾰ)ν</i> (&#42;th(ă)n), with inchoative suffix <i>-σκω</i> (-skō).</p><p>The root is probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰenh₂-</i> (“to set oneself in motion, to take off”), whence Sanskrit <i>धन्वति</i> (dhánvati, “to flow”). The meaning "to die" would have developed as euphemism. θνῄσκω is then inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰn̥h₂-sḱé-ti</i>.</p><p>Older theories:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to move, dispatch quickly; ply rapidly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to urge on</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move quickly, rush, speed, dart</li><li>Epic form of <i>θάσσω</i> (thássō)</li></ol><p>From <i>θοός</i> (thoós) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p><p>Epic form of θάσσω (thássō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meal, feast, banquet</li><li>food</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, possibly from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>θώς</i> (thṓs, “jackal”) and <i>θῶσθαι</i> (thôsthai, “to eat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conical hat with broad brim</li><li>chest with conical lid</li></ol><p>From <i>θόλος</i> (thólos, “rotunda”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>muddy, turbid</li></ol><p>From <i>θολ(ός)</i> (thol(ós)) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mud, dirt, especially in water</li><li>menses, menstrual flow</li><li>ink of the cuttlefish</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The word has been connected with terms for turbidness of the mind, like Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dwelaną</i> (“to go astray”) and <i>&#42;dwalaz</i> (“confused, stunned”), resulting in a putative proto-form <i>&#42;θϝολός</i> (&#42;thwolós). The Germanic words may or may not be connected with Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;dallos</i> (“blind”). There is no indication, however, that these Indo-European forms are cognate. Furnée compares <i>ὀλός</i> (olós, “ink of the cuttlefish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semen, sperm</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰerh₃-</i> (“to leap, spring”), the same root of <i>θρῴσκω</i> (thrōískō, “to spring, to leap”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fast, fleet, quick, speedy, swift</li><li>sharp, pointed</li></ol><p>Deverbal adjective from <i>θέω</i> (théō, “to dash, to race; to fly”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós, an adjectival suffix), with expected o-grade monophthongization of the verbal stem. Compare Θόας.</p><p>Of unclear origin. Probably not related to Sanskrit <i>धारा</i> (dhā́rā, “cutting edge, blade (of a sword)”). Perhaps related to Etymology 1, with sense development "swift" > "swiftly cutting" > "sharp"; for a semantic parallel, note the English synonyms <i>quickwitted</i> and <i>sharpwitted</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rushing, impetuous</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰorwos</i> or <i>&#42;tʰorhos</i>, from the root of <i>θρῴσκω</i> (thrōískō, “to leap, spring”) (aorist <i>ἔθορον</i> (éthoron)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰerh₃-</i> (“to leap, spring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, swiftly</li></ol><p><i>θοός</i> (thoós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confidence, courage, boldness</li><li>rashness, impudence, audacity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰers-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>धृष्णोति</i> (dhṛṣṇóti), Czech <i>drzý</i> and Lithuanian <i>drąsus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble, disquiet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to disturb, destroy</li></ol></ol><p>According to Beekes, from older <i>&#42;θρᾱχ-jω</i> (&#42;thrākh-jō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰr̥h₂gʰ-yé-ti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰreh₂gʰ-</i>. Beekes suggests that that the perfect form is old and may derive from earlier <i>&#42;τε-θρᾱχ-α</i> (&#42;te-thrākh-a). Rix reconstructs a Proto-Indo-European stative form <i>&#42;dʰedʰróh₂gʰe</i>. However, Beekes suggests that the aorist forms are newer innovations formed by analogy to terms such as <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō). Doublet of Ancient Greek <i>ταράσσω</i> (tarássō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>θράσσω</i> (thrássō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰreh₂gʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nursling, creature</li><li>homeborn slave (translates Latin <i>verna</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to feed, to maintain”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry aloud, shriek forth</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thriambus (<i>hymn to Dionysus</i>)</li><li>A translation for Latin <i>triumphus</i>.</li></ol><p>The origin of the term is uncertain; most likely derived from Pre-Greek, possibly Phrygian or Illyrian. Ancient Greek <i>θρι-</i> (thri-) has also been connected with a term for <i>fig tree</i> (compare <i>θρῖον</i> (thrîon, “fig leaf”)). The suffix <i>-αμβος</i> (-ambos) is probably the same element that also occurs in <i>ἴαμβος</i> (íambos, “a poetic meter”), <i>δῑθύραμβος</i> (dīthúrambos, “hymn to Dionysus”) and might derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃engʷ-</i> (“to anoint”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θρίαμβος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>An epithet of Dionysus</li></ol><p>See θρῐ́ᾰμβος (thrĭ́ămbos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lettuce (<i>Lactuca sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Nehring, it is Pre-Greek. Because of the typical leaves, Strömberg thinks of <i>θρῖον</i> (thrîon, “fig leaf”) and compares <i>οἶδᾰξ</i> (oîdăx, “unripe figs”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hair</li></ol><p>Unknown; often compared with the second element of Middle Irish <i>gairb-driuch</i> (“bristle”) and Lithuanian <i>drikà</i> (“threads hanging from the loom”), though Beekes rejects both connections and leaves the origin open, noting that the semantic roots used to express the concept of "hair" vary significantly across Indo-European branches. Alternatively Pre-Greek (though Beekes surprisingly does not posit such a theory).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodworm</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) skinflint, miser</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests Pre-Greek.; Liddell &amp; Scott link it to τρίβω</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swordfish (<i>Xiphias gladius</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>θρᾶνος</i> (thrânos, “bench”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās), after the shape of the upper jaw.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rower's bench</li><li>close-stool</li></ol><p>From <i>θρᾶνος</i> (thrânos, “bench”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brave; courageous</li><li>bold; daring; reckless</li><li>impudent; insolent</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰrəsús</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰr̥sús</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰers-</i>. Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dьrzъ</i>, Sanskrit <i>धृषु</i> (dhṛṣú), and Lithuanian <i>drąsùs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>θρᾰσῠ́ς</i> (thrăsŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>θρᾰσῠ́ς</i> (thrăsŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of small bird, perhaps a kind of finch</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who breaks or crushes, breaker, crusher</li></ol><p>From <i>θραύω</i> (thraúō) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A fragment</li><li>A breakage</li><li>A destruction</li></ol><p>From <i>θραύω</i> (thraúō, “to break”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to feed or rear, nourishing, nutritious</li><li>of or promoting growth</li><li>causing to heal up</li></ol><p><i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, future/aorist stem: θρεπ-) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamentation, mourning</li></ol><p>From <i>θρηνῴδης</i> (thrēnōídēs, “like or fit for a dirge”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>θρῆνος</i> (thrênos, “lamenting”) + <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song, ode”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>religious worship, ritual</li><li>religion, service to god</li></ol><p>From <i>θρησκεύω</i> (thrēskeúō, “perform religious observances, worship”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) topmost course of stones in a wall, cornice, coping, frieze</li><li>capstone, last finish</li><li>wall, fence of any sort</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is undoubtedly from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of lettuce, resembling lettuce</li></ol><p>From <i>θρίδαξ</i> (thrídax, “lettuce”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plantain (<i>Plantago crassifolia</i>)</li><li>wick made with this plant</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-ίς</i> occurs mostly with plants and birds, so the word is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to crash, smash</li></ol><p>May be analyzed as a denominative verb from <i>&#42;θρῦλος</i> (&#42;thrûlos, “fragment”), which would belong to Middle Welsh <i>dryll</i> (“fragment”), Gaulish <i>&#42;drullia</i> (“waste”) and go back to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrews-</i> (“crumble”). The primary verb is seen in Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dreusaną</i> (“to fall”); Latin <i>frustum</i> (“morsel”) is probably derived from this verb, like Latvian <i>druska</i> (“morsel, crumb”) with velar suffix. Another hypothetical connection is with <i>θραύω</i> (thraúō, “to break in pieces, shatter”), but then its vowel would remain unexplained. One might also compare <i>θρύπτω</i> (thrúptō, “to break in pieces”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece, lump, clump</li><li>clot, gout of blood</li><li>curd of milk</li><li>coarse salt</li></ol><p>Compared with Icelandic <i>drambr</i> (“knag, knot”), however a direct connection is impossible (unless the proposed Miller's law is correct). The same holds with Lithuanian <i>dramblys</i> (“elephant”) and Latvian <i>dramblis</i> (“glutton”).</p><p>Can also be compared with Albanian <i>grumbull</i> (“heap, pile”).</p><p>Within Greek, the word is generally compared with <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to curdle”): since this verb does not have a convincing Indo-European etymology, the present word would be of Pre-Greek origin too.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curdling, clotting</li></ol><p>From <i>θρομβόομαι</i> (thrombóomai, “curdle”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat</li><li>throne</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰer-</i> (“to hold”), but this root presents a number of problems, such as the lack of the suffix <i>&#42;-onos</i> in Proto-Indo-European, the lack of any words meaning "chair" from this root in any other Indo-European languages, and the lack of any definite Greek descendants from the root itself. Comparisons to <i>θρᾶνος</i> (thrânos, “bench”) and <i>θρῆνυς</i> (thrênus, “footstool”) are also improbable, due to lack of evidence of their supposed shared root <i>&#42;dʰerh₂-</i>. Due to the suffix -όνος usually being indicative of a Pre-Greek word (as in <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos), <i>Κρόνος</i> (Krónos)), Beekes suggests that this could be the case for <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos) as well.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θρόνοι)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Throni, Cyprus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) flowers embroidered on cloth</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) (<i>botany</i>) herbs used as drugs and charms</li></ol><p>Furnée compares the word with <i>τρόνα</i> (tróna, “statues; colorful stitchings”), which proves Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat</li><li>throne</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰer-</i> (“to hold”), but this root presents a number of problems, such as the lack of the suffix <i>&#42;-onos</i> in Proto-Indo-European, the lack of any words meaning "chair" from this root in any other Indo-European languages, and the lack of any definite Greek descendants from the root itself. Comparisons to <i>θρᾶνος</i> (thrânos, “bench”) and <i>θρῆνυς</i> (thrênus, “footstool”) are also improbable, due to lack of evidence of their supposed shared root <i>&#42;dʰerh₂-</i>. Due to the suffix -όνος usually being indicative of a Pre-Greek word (as in <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos), <i>Κρόνος</i> (Krónos)), Beekes suggests that this could be the case for <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos) as well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat</li><li>throne</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰer-</i> (“to hold”), but this root presents a number of problems, such as the lack of the suffix <i>&#42;-onos</i> in Proto-Indo-European, the lack of any words meaning "chair" from this root in any other Indo-European languages, and the lack of any definite Greek descendants from the root itself. Comparisons to <i>θρᾶνος</i> (thrânos, “bench”) and <i>θρῆνυς</i> (thrênus, “footstool”) are also improbable, due to lack of evidence of their supposed shared root <i>&#42;dʰerh₂-</i>. Due to the suffix -όνος usually being indicative of a Pre-Greek word (as in <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos), <i>Κρόνος</i> (Krónos)), Beekes suggests that this could be the case for <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos) as well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise, as of many voices</li><li>murmur of a crowd</li><li>report, rumour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrew-</i>, the same root of <i>θρέομαι</i> (thréomai, “to shriek, proclaim”) and Old Armenian <i>երդնում</i> (erdnum, “to swear, take an oath”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragment</li></ol><p>From <i>θρῡλῐ́σσω</i> (thrūlĭ́ssō, “to crush, shiver, smash”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragment, piece, bit, morsel</li></ol><p>From <i>θρῠ́πτω</i> (thrŭ́ptō, “to shatter”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reed, rush</li><li>thorn apple (<i>Datura stramonium</i>)</li></ol><p>Formally, one may compare <i>βρύον</i> (brúon), but further details are unclear. The connection with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trъstь</i> (“cane”) is impossible because of the anlaut. The variants point to a Pre-Greek origin, which is not unexpected in the case of a plant name.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θρύον)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thryon, Elis, Greece</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>θρύον</i> (thrúon, “reed, rush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break in pieces, shatter</li><li>(<i>of air</i>) to be dispersed</li><li>(<i>in moral sense</i>) to enfeeble, by debauchery and luxury</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be enervated, unmanned</li><li>to wanton, riot, display moral weakness</li><li>to be coy and prudish, bridle up</li><li>to grow conceited in or of a thing</li></ol><p>This verb may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrewb-</i> (“to crumble, grind”) and is compared to Latvian <i>drubaža</i> (“piece, fragment”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;drybati</i> (“to scratch, scrumple”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drupô</i> (“drop”) and Old Irish <i>drúcht</i> (“dew”). It is probable that <i>δρύπτω</i> (drúptō, “to tear, strip”) was modelled on this verb. According to Beekes, it is probably a non-Indo-European substrate word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of reed, rush</li></ol><p>From <i>θρύον</i> (thrúon, “reed, rush”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bench, long seat</li><li>close-stool</li><li>supporting beam made of wood</li></ol><p>This word is often connected with <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos, “throne”), together with <i>θρῆνυς</i> (thrênus, “footstool”). Recently, De Lamberterie has argued that <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos) is a younger form of <i>&#42;θόρνος</i> (&#42;thórnos), comparing <i>θόρναξ</i> (thórnax, “footstool”), and reflecting the Indo-European root <i>&#42;dʰer-</i> (“to hold”), but the existence of variants <i>θόρν-/θρόν-</i> could also be an indication of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wailing, lamenting, dirge</li><li>complaint</li></ol><p>Cognate to Sanskrit <i>ध्रणति</i> (dhráṇati, “to sound”), Latin <i>drēnsō</i>, and Old English <i>drān</i> (English <i>drone</i>). Compare also <i>θρέομαι</i> (thréomai, “to cry aloud, shriek”). Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footstool</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>θρᾶνος</i> (thrânos, “bench”) and <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos, “throne”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>religious, pious, devout</li><li>(<i>in bad sense</i>) superstitious</li></ol><p>A deverbal from <i>θρησκεύω</i> (thrēskeúō, “to perform religious observances”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winnowing fork, three-pronged fork used to stir grain</li></ol><p>Mostly interpreted as a compound with <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-), but the attempts at finding an Indo-European etymology have failed. Furnée compares <i>τρῐ́νᾰξ</i> (trĭ́năx, “instrument used in agriculture”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the variation θρι-&#47;τρι- and the typical substrate suffix -ᾰξ.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig leaf</li><li>mixture of eggs, milk, lard, honey, cheese wrapped in figleaves</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>τρίς</i> (trís, “three”), because of the three lobes of the figleaf. According to Beekes, it is a Mediterranean word and compares the word with <i>θρινία</i> (thrinía, “vine in Crete”). Perhaps from Pre-Greek and related to <i>θρίαμβος</i> (thríambos, “thriambus”), <i>θρίδαξ</i> (thrídax, “lettuce”) and <i>θρῖναξ</i> (thrînax, “trident”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise as of many voices, murmur</li></ol><p>It has been suggested that this word is a zero grade derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrēu-</i> (“to drone”), but this root is not well attested. The frequent notation <i>-λλ-</i> may be an expressive gemination, but it may also point to Pre-Greek origin. Furnée separates the word from the Indo-European forms and connects it with <i>θόρυβος</i> (thórubos, “tumult, confusion”), with variant <i>&#42;θρυϝ-</i> (&#42;thruw-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap, spring</li><li>to leap upon, assault</li><li>to rush, dart</li></ol><p>The only plausible comparison is found in Middle Irish <i>dairid</i> (“to leap upon”), together with Old Irish <i>der</i> (“young girl”). The ablaut pattern is identical to that of <i>βλώσκω</i> (blṓskō, “to go or come”). The root is Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰerh₃-</i> (“to leap, spring”), the same of <i>θορός</i> (thorós, “semen, sperm”) and <i>θόρνῡμαι</i> (thórnūmai, “to mount, impregnate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Laconia</i>) drone</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰreh₁n-</i> (“drone; to murmur”), like English <i>drone</i> and Latin <i>drensō</i> (“to cry (of swans)”). However, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin, comparing the word with <i>ἀνθρηδών</i> (anthrēdṓn, “hornet”), <i>ἀνθρήνη</i> (anthrḗnē, “bee, wasp”) and <i>τενθρηδών</i> (tenthrēdṓn, “kind of wasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stinking juniper (<i>Juniperus foetidissima</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>θύον</i> (thúon, “cypress-pine”). The two words could be derived from <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “to burn”) but this is not clear. Possibly from <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “I sacrifice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter</li><li>female slave, maid-servant</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;θύγατηρ</i> (&#42;thúgatēr) (compare with the Homeric accusative form <i>θύγατρα</i> (thúgatra) created to avoid the four syllables of <i>θυγατέρα</i> (thugatéra) and fit the hexameter) via Limitation Law which required the accent to be placed on the last two syllables, due to the long final syllable. Further via Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰúgatēr</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀶𐀏𐀳</i> (tu-ka-te /⁠tʰúgatēr⁠/)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰugh₂tḗr</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>दुहितृ</i> (dúhitṛ), Gothic <i>𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌷𐍄𐌰𐍂</i> (dauhtar), Old Church Slavonic <i>дъщи</i> (dŭšti), Old English <i>dohtor</i> (whence English <i>daughter</i>) and Old High German <i>tohter</i> (cp. German <i>Tochter</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandson (<i>through one's daughter</i>), daughter's son</li></ol><p>From <i>θυγάτηρ</i> (thugátēr, “daughter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortar (vessel)</li><li>cup of the cottabus</li><li>oil press</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>θῠ́ος</i> (thŭ́os, “burnt offering; incense”), but the concept of a vase for pounding incense is rather strange. According to Beekes, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰuH-</i> (“to fly about; to dash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stormy, tempestuous</li></ol><p>From <i>θύελλα</i> (thúella, “storm, tempest”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “like, full of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>carrying a bag</li></ol><p>From <i>θύλακος</i> (thúlakos, “bag”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of burning wood or charcoal</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>αἱμάλωψ</i> (haimálōps, “mass of blood, clot”) and <i>νυκτάλωψ</i> (nuktálōps, “night-blindness”). Since the present word has nothing to do with "seeing", we are probably dealing with a Pre-Greek suffix <i>-λωψ</i>. Confront also <i>ἀγχίλωψ</i> (ankhílōps) and <i>αἰγίλωψ</i> (aigílōps).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place of sacrifice, altar, temple</li><li>In Athenian theater, the altar of Dionysus, an altar shaped platform in the middle of the orchestra, upon which stood the leader of the chorus.</li></ol><p>From <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “I sacrifice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is burnt as incense or fumigation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>mostly in plural</i>) fragrant stuffs for burning, incense</li><li>stuff for embalming</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>θῡμῐᾰ́ω</i> (thūmĭắō, “to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic) heart-grieving, distressing</li></ol><p><i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós) +&lrm; <i>ἄλγος</i> (álgos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flax-leaved daphne (<i>Daphne gnidium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>θύμον</i> (thúmon, “thyme”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλαία</i> (elaía, “olive tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn (<i>so as to produce smoke</i>)</li><li>(<i>specifically</i>) to burn incense</li></ol><p>From <i>θῦμα</i> (thûma) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a censer, thurible</li><li>vessel for fumigation</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Ara (a constellation)</li></ol><p>From <i>θῡμιάω</i> (thūmiáō, “to fumigate”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(that is) to be burnt as incense</li><li>capable of giving off fumes</li></ol><p><i>θῡμῐᾰ́ω</i> (thūmĭắō, “burn so as to produce smoke”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, suffix forming verbal adjectives of possibility)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) fragrant stuffs for burning, incense</li></ol><p>From <i>θῡμῐητός</i> (thūmĭētós), Ionic form of Attic Greek <i>θῡμῐᾱτός</i> (thūmĭātós, “[that is] to be burnt as incense, capable of giving off fumes”): as a noun, a neuter plural substantive; as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>θῡμῐᾱτός</i> (thūmĭātós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) lion-hearted</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”) +&lrm; <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soul, as the seat of emotion, feeling, and thought</li><li>soul, life, breath</li><li>soul, heart</li><li>desire, will</li><li>temper, passion, disposition</li><li>anger, rage, wrath</li><li>heart, love</li><li>thought, mind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰūmós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰuh₂mós</i> (“smoke”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>धूम</i> (dhūmá), Lithuanian <i>dūmas</i>, Latin <i>fūmus</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>дꙑмъ</i> (dymŭ) and Albanian <i>tym</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>window</li></ol><p>From <i>θύρα</i> (thúra) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Shield-shaped.</li></ol><p>From <i>θυρεός</i> (thureós, “oblong shield”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-form”), from <i>θύρα</i> (thúra, “door”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stone put against a door to keep it shut, a door-stone</li><li>a wide oblong shield (like a door), opp. to <i>ἀσπίς</i> (aspís, “the round shield”) (as Lat. <i>scūtum</i> to <i>clipeus</i>)</li><li>disk forming part of <i>καθετήρ</i> (kathetḗr)</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) oval</li></ol><p><i>θῠ́ρᾱ</i> (thŭ́rā) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doorlike</li></ol><p>From <i>θύρα</i> (thúra, “door”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-form, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thyrsus-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>θῠ́ρσος</i> (thŭ́rsos, “thyrsus”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doorkeeper, porter</li></ol><p>From <i>θύρα</i> (thúra, “door”) + the root underlying <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros, “watcher, guardian”) and <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to protect, guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering, sacrifice, a sacrificing</li><li>victim, offering</li></ol><p>From <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “I sacrifice”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tasseled, fringed</li></ol><p><i>θύσανος</i> (thúsanos) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an altar (usually a Christian or Jewish altar)</li></ol><p>From <i>θῠσῐᾰστήρῐος</i> (thŭsĭăstḗrĭos, “sacrificial”) from <i>θῠσῐᾰ́ζω</i> (thŭsĭắzō, “to sacrifice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>θυσανόεις</i> (thusanóeis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacrificer, slayer</li></ol><p>From <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “to sacrifice, slay”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Elean form of <i>θωάζω</i> (thōázō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay the penalty</li></ol><p>From <i>θωή</i> (thōḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Elean form of <i>θωάζω</i> (thōázō)</li><li>to penalize, fine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Delphic form of <i>θωάζω</i> (thōázō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine, penalty</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from an o-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put”), the same root of <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to put, place, set”), as "the settled penalty".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Cretan form of <i>θωάζω</i> (thōázō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>*tʰōmos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰóh₁mos</i>, from the root <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i>; compare <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm with a breastplate</li></ol><p>From <i>θώρηξ</i> (thṓrēx) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm with a breastplate</li></ol><p>From <i>θώρᾱξ</i> (thṓrāx, “corselet; breastplate”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suffering chest pains</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter&#32;plural) the region of the thorax</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) thoracic, pertaining to the thorax or ribcage</li></ol><p>From <i>θώρᾱξ</i> (thṓrāx) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) equipped with a cuirass or breastplate</li></ol><p>From <i>θώρηξ</i> (thṓrēx), Epic form of <i>θώρᾱξ</i> (thṓrāx, “cuirass, breastplate”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of bird</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>θαῦμᾰ</i> (thaûmă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) A round building with conical roof, a rotunda</li><li>A vaulted steam bath</li><li>A bandage for the head invented by Diocles</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Beekes, the comparison with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dalą</i> (“valley”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dȏlъ</i> (“valley”) should be discarded. The connection with <i>θάλαμος</i> (thálamos, “bedroom”) makes more sense and would lead to a Pre-Greek origin. A few scholars, such as Guus Kroonen, still maintain the Indo-European etymology and reconstruct Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰól(h₂)os</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise, especially of a crowd of people: uproar, clamor</li><li>tumult, confusion, trouble</li></ol><p>A formation like <i>ὄτοβος</i> (ótobos), <i>κόναβος</i> (kónabos) and <i>φλοῖσβος</i> (phloîsbos), likely from the same root as a reduplicated form <i>τονθορύζω</i> (tonthorúzō, “to mumble”), as well as <i>θρῦλος</i> (thrûlos, “murmur”). The phonetic variations between the words suggest a Pre-Greek origin, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) ryegrass, darnel (<i>Lolium temulentum</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>κόμαρος</i> (kómaros) and <i>κίσθαρος</i> (kístharos). The suffix in "-αρος" points to a Pre-Greek word and connection with <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “to rage, seethe”) is a mere guess.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hurricane, squall</li><li>thunderstorm</li></ol><p>From <i>θῠ́ω</i> (thŭ́ō, “to storm; to rage”), with a suffix by analogy with <i>ᾰ̓́ελλᾰ</i> (ắellă, “whirlwind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack, bag, used especially to carry meal in</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) sack in which the eggs of the tunny are enveloped</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) loose trousers of Persians and other Orientals</li><li>ball used for physical exercise</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Like <i>σάκκος</i> (sákkos, “sack”), probably foreign. The suffix <i>-ακ-</i> points to Pre-Greek origin. The word has also been compared with Lithuanian <i>dundùlis</i> (“puffed, big-bellied”), but this is only speculation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown kind of fish, perhaps the grayling</li></ol><p>The word has been connected with <i>θύμον</i> (thúmon, “thyme”), because of its scent. However, as the suffix <i>-αλλος</i> is Pre-Greek, it is improbable that the basic word was of inner-Greek formation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse fennel (<i>Prangos ferulacea</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savory of Crete (<i>Satureja thymbra</i>)</li></ol><p>Hardly derived from <i>θύμον</i> (thúmon, “thyme”), in which case the "-β-" could be epenthetic between "μ" and "ρ". Persson proposed a derivation from <i>τύφω</i> (túphō, “to smoke”) in "-ρ-", with nasalization and deaspiration; this is most improbable, unless the word is Pre-Greek. Niedermann recalls Anatolian <i>Θύμβριον</i> (Thúmbrion) and on the other hand <i>Τυμφρηστός</i> (Tumphrēstós). Probably a Pre-Greek word, in view of the metathesized form.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Θύμβρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thymbra</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thyme (plant of the genus <i>Thymus</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally taken as a derivative in <i>-μο-</i> (-mo-) from <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “to smoke”), though Beekes is skeptical of this theory and notes that "[a]s a local plant name, the word is liable to be of Pre-Greek origin".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a warty excrescence, so called from its likeness to a bunch of thyme-flower</li><li>the thymus gland</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to smoke, raise dust”). Compare <i>θύμον</i> (thúmon, “thyme”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuna (fish)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician equivalent to Ugaritic <i>𐎚𐎐𐎐</i> (tnn /⁠tunnanu⁠/) and Hebrew <i>תַּנִּין</i> (tannīn, “water animal, sea monster”), attested in three tophet stelae at Hadrumetum in the name of the peninsula in front of Monastir <i>𐤀𐤉𐤕𐤍𐤌</i> (ʾytnm /⁠ʾī tunnīm⁠/, literally “Island of Tunnies”) referring to its tonnara.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush along</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>θῡ́ω</i> (thū́ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandarac (<i>Tetraclinis articulata</i>)</li></ol><p>It has been suggested that it is a primary derivative of <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “to smoke”). It is clearly connected to <i>θυία</i> (thuía, “stinking juniper”); however, the relation of these forms is not clear. It does not seem very probable that two distinct trees had nearly identical names.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burnt sacrifice; offering</li><li>kind of cake</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to smoke”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>door</li><li>entrance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰurā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰwer-</i> (“door”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>द्वार्</i> (dvā́r), Latin <i>foris</i>, Old Armenian <i>դուռն</i> (duṙn) and Old English <i>duru</i> and <i>dor</i> (English <i>door</i>) and Russian <i>дверь</i> (dverʹ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the door</li><li>outside the door, outdoors</li></ol><p><i>θῠ́ρᾱς</i> (thŭ́rās) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from outside (the door)</li><li>outside (the door)</li></ol><p>From <i>θύρα</i> (thúra) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wings of a door, door</li><li>doorcase, doorframe</li></ol><p>From <i>θύρα</i> (thúra, “door”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>θύρᾱ</i> (thúrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thyrsus, a wand wreathed in ivy and vine-leaves with a pine-cone or a blooming artichoke at the top, carried by the devotees of Dionysus</li><li>the devotees themselves</li><li>Hesychius defines it as <i>κλάδος</i> (kládos, “stick, branch”), <i>ῥάβδος</i> (rhábdos, “stick, rod”)</li></ol><p>Probably an Anatolian loanword; compare Luwian (tuwarsa, “vine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) tassel, fringe</li><li>tuft of the Golden Fleece</li></ol><p>Technical word in <i>-ανος</i>. According to Persson, it is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰudʰ-ih₂</i>, the same root of Latvian <i>duša</i> (“bundle of straw”). However, the variation shown in <i>θυσανόεις</i> (thusanóeis)/<i>θυσσανόεις</i> (thussanóeis) rather points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacred implements of Bacchic orgies, such as wands and thyrsi</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) the Bacchic festival itself</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) sacrifice</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “to sacrifice”) by means of a suffix <i>-θλο-</i>. However, Beekes claims this derivation is inadequate, and argues it is a loan, either from Anatolian or from Pre-Greek. Hardly related to <i>θύρσος</i> (thúrsos, “staff carried by Bacchus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush in, storm, rage, seethe (in general of violent movements)</li><li>to offer in sacrifice, slay, burn, immolate</li><li>to kill, slaughter (in a general sense)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to cause to be sacrificed</li><li>to consult (of oracles, prophets, etc)</li><li>to celebrate</li></ol><p>Likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewH-</i> (“to shake, move violently”), though Beekes expresses semantic doubts. Cognate to Sanskrit <i>धूनोति</i> (dhūnóti, “to shake, move to and fro quickly”) and Old Norse <i>dýja</i> (“to shake”).</p><p>Unclear, but may be related to <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós), Latin <i>suffiō</i>, Hittite (tuhhae, “pant, sigh”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“to fume; smoke, vapor, breath”). The wide variety of semantic shifts presumed in descendants of Indo-European roots with the shape <i>&#42;dʰewH-</i> makes analysis difficult, as Beekes notes. Alternatively, from <i>&#42;dʰews-</i> (“to breathe; breath, spirit, living being”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of cedar</li></ol><p>From <i>θύον</i> (thúon, “citron-wood; thyine-wood, arar, <i>Tetraclinis articulata</i> (syn. <i>Callitris quadrivalvis</i>)”) or <i>θυία</i> (thuía, “cedar”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corselet</li><li>linen jerkin</li><li>coat of mail, cuirass</li><li>slough of a serpent</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) trunk, thorax</li><li>bandage for the chest</li></ol><p>Related to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀵𐀨𐀐</i> (to-ra-ke), a technical word without etymology; probably a loan from Pre-Greek or some other substrate, based on the variant <i>θύραξ</i> (thúrax) showing variation <i>ω/υ</i> and the suffix <i>-ᾱκ-</i>. Traditionally compared to, but unrelated to, Latin <i>firmus</i> (“strong, firm”), Sanskrit <i>धारका</i> (dhārakā, “container”) (see Pokorny); however, Latin <i>lōrīca</i> (“breastplate”) may be cognate via the same substrate. The medical "trunk" meaning is probably secondary from the "armor" meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>θώρᾱξ</i> (thṓrāx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackal</li></ol><p>According to Beekes it could be related to <i>θῶσθαι</i> (thôsthai, “to eat”) or alternatively from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulator, flatterer</li><li>A false friend</li></ol><p>Disputed. According to Beekes, possibly Pre-Greek, perhaps related to <i>θάμβος</i> (thámbos, “amazement, astonishment”). Alternatively, the term has been explained as inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dóh₂bʰ-s</i>, itself from the root <i>&#42;deh₂bʰ-</i> (“to strike”), whence also perhaps Lithuanian <i>dóbti</i> (“to smash, kill”). For a semantic connection between the sense “to deceive” and words related to physical force, see English <i>deceive</i>, which derives from Latin <i>dēcipiō</i> (“to ensnare”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat, chair</li><li>chair of office</li></ol><p>From the Hesychian gloss <i>θάβακον</i> (thábakon, “seat, throne”), it appears that this word was contracted from <i>&#42;θά(ϝ)ακος</i> (&#42;thá(w)akos). Connection with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put, place”) as zero grade is impossible because of <i>&#42;θά(ϝ)ακος</i> (&#42;thá(w)akos). The word must be Pre-Greek, as was observed by Furnée. A suffix <i>-ακ</i> is frequent in Pre-Greek; the variation <i>*-αϝ-/-οϝ-</i> is normal in substrate words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sooner; quicker</li><li>sooner; rather</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾱ́σσων</i> (thā́ssōn, “faster”, comparative adjective).</p><p>From <i>θᾱ́σσων</i> (thā́ssōn, “sitting, sitting idly”, present active participle).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>θῆκε</i> (thêke)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>female</li><li>of or belonging to women, womanly, feminine</li><li>soft, gentle</li><li>tender, delicate</li><li>female (having an internal socket, into which another part fits)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) feminine</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) even</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) negative or feminine sign (one of Taurus, Cancer, Virgo, Scorpio, Capricorn, or Pisces)</li><li>abundant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-lu-s</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i> (“to suck”). One especially close cognate is Sanskrit <i>धारु</i> (dhārú, “suckling”); others include Latin <i>fēmina</i>, <i>fētus</i>, <i>fīlius</i>, Sanskrit <i>धयति</i> (dhayati), Avestan <i>𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬥𐬎</i> (daēnu), Old Armenian <i>դիեմ</i> (diem), Old Church Slavonic <i>доити</i> (doiti). See also <i>θηλή</i> (thēlḗ, “breast, nipple”).</p><p>The "abundant" sense may be related to <i>θάλλω</i> (thállō, “to flourish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theta, the name for the eighth letter Θ (Th), θ (th) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤈</i> (ṭ‬ /⁠ṭēt⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket plaited from papyrus</li></ol><p>A loan from Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>תֵּבָה</i> (tēḇā, “ark, vessel”), Arabic تَابُوت (tābūt). Ultimately from Egyptian, for which see the last.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>θῖνα</i> (thîna)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victim, sacrifice (especially animals)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) animals slaughtered for food</li></ol><li>act of sacrifice</li></ol><p>From <i>θύω</i> (thúō, “to offer, sacrifice”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord; string</li><li>bowstring</li><li>fishing line</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the formation in <i>-ιγγ-</i> (-ing-) leads to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html>:/k/
<h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κε</i> (ke) (<i>used before the smooth breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glutton, gourmand</li></ol><p>In antiquity the word was analyzed as a compound of <i>κάβος</i> (kábos, “a corn-measure”) + <i>αἶσα</i> (aîsa, “one's lot, share”), which is of course nonsense. The meaning and structure of the word point to Pre-Greek origin. Furnée further connects <i>καμασός</i> (kamasós, “abyss”), which is possible but uncertain; the comparison with <i>καμασήν</i> (kamasḗn, “fish”) is even more uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corn-measure corresponding to four <i>ξέσται</i> (xéstai)</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>קַב</i> (kab, “measure of capacity”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) crab</li></ol><p>Likely a corruption of <i>πάγουρος</i> (págouros, “crab”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κᾰτᾰ́</i> (kătắ) used before <i>γ</i> (g)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bisabol, a gum produced by the plant <i>Commiphora kataf</i></li></ol><p>From an Oriental source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry, arid, barren</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kenk-</i> (“to burn, dry, pain, desire, hunger, thirst”) and cognate with Lithuanian <i>kenkti</i> (“to damage, blight”), Sanskrit <i>काङ्क्षति</i> (kāṅkṣati, “he wishes, desires”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hungruz</i> (“hunger”). However, Beekes finds the root structure typical of Pre-Greek and adds that the words compared mean hunger and pain and not primarily arid and dry.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>κᾰτᾰ́</i> (kătắ) (used before <i>δέ</i> (dé), <i>δ’</i> (d’))</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>τυφλός</i> (tuphlós, “blind”), as they say in Salamis</li></ol><p>If reliable, it may belong to Homeric <i>κεκαδήσω</i> (kekadḗsō, “to rob”). Not related to Old Latin <i>kadamitās</i>. One gets the impression of a Pre-Greek word, and it is preferable to abandon attempt at an inner Greek or Indo-European etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pail, bucket, jar, cask, vessel for water or wine</li><li>liquid measure</li><li>funerary urn</li></ol><p>A vinicultural loan of Semitic origin, compare Hebrew <i>כַּד</i> (kad), Ugaritic <i>𐎋𐎄</i> (kd), Imperial Aramaic <i>𐡊𐡃</i> (kd), Punic <i>𐤊𐤃</i> (kd). The Semitic word may ultimately trace to Sanskrit <i>कन्दु</i> (kandu, “pot”) and ultimately Proto-South Dravidian <i>&#42;kiṇṭV-</i> (“pot”); see Akkadian <i>𒃶𒁺</i> (kandu) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>εἶδος σταφυλῆς</i> (eîdos staphulês, literally “kind of grape”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek. For the suffix, compare <i>ἄγχουσα</i> (ánkhousa), <i>αἴθουσα</i> (aíthousa) and <i>νήθουσα</i> (nḗthousa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κάπρος ἔνορχις</i> (kápros énorkhis, literally “uncastrated boar”).</li></ol><p>Von Blumenthal connected the word with <i>κεκαδών</i> (kekadṓn, “to draw back, retire”), but this seems improbable. According to Beekes, the element <i>υρ-</i> rather points to Pre-Greek</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offscouring</li><li>outcast, pariah</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleansing, purification (in a moral/spiritual sense)</li><li>clarification, explanation</li><li>purging, evacuation (in a medical sense)</li><li>pruning trees, shearing grain, clearing land</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰθαίρω</i> (kăthaírō, “to cleanse”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having been let down (<i>of a fishing line, plumb line etc.</i>)</li><li>vertical, perpendicular</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) camouflaged, disguised</li></ol><p>From <i>καθίημι</i> (kathíēmi) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be seated</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be seated in court</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>present participle</i>) <i>οἱ</i> καθήμενοι (kathḗmenoi): the judges</li></ol><li>to sit still, sit quietly, sit idly, do nothing</li><li>to lead a sedentary, obscure life</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) to be settled</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to be set or placed</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>ἧμαι</i> (hêmai, “to sit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) buttocks, sit-upon</li><li>seat</li><li>base</li><li>sinking, settling down (<i>of a wall</i>)</li><li>sediment</li></ol><p>From <i>καθίζω</i> (kathízō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way down, descent</li></ol><p>From <i>κατά</i> (katá, “down”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way, road”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ancient Greek</i>) &mdash;</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to sit or be at a place</li><li>to stay, reside, to be at a place</li><li>to remain at a place</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to camp</li><li>to be at a state, condition of</li><li>to associate with people</li><li>to be occupied with</li><li>to remain idle</li></ol></ol><p>Medieval verb of the 10th century in synchronic use with Ancient Greek <i>κάθημαι</i> (káthēmai, “to sit, to sit down, to be idle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homer</i>) apocopic form of <i>κατά</i> (katá) (used before words starting with <i>κ</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κακά</i> (kaká)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wickedness, vice</li><li>cowardice</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκός</i> (kăkós, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κακκάβη</i> (kakkábē, “three-legged pot”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, excrement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kakkā</i>, fro Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kakka-</i> (“to defecate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) the cardoon, <i>Cynara cardunculus</i></li><li>(<i>masculine</i>) the artichoke (the fruit of the cardoon); also, the cardoon’s edible leaf-stalks</li></ol><p>A foreign loan, possibly from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket which is narrow at the base</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) capital of a column</li><li>cooler, especially for wine</li><li>mould for casting iron</li><li>reservoir of an oil lamp</li></ol><p>Compared by Beekes to <i>γυργαθός</i> (gurgathós, “wicker basket”), <i>κύαθος</i> (kúathos, “wine ladle”), and <i>ὁρμαθός</i> (hormathós, “cluster chain”) and declared Pre-Greek, likely based on semantic domain and suffix.</p><p>Contended by Rosół as from Egyptian <i>krḥt</i> (“a basket for fruits as well as for flowers”), after a previous suggestion by Bernal of <i>qrḥt</i> (“pot”), though both are implausible, due the specific form of the containers and phonetic shapes in question.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reed</li><li>anything made of reed or cane</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>staff, rod</li><li>measuring rod</li><li>pen</li><li>flute</li><li>shaft of an arrow</li></ol><li>collectively, of plants, which are neither bush, nor tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱolh₂mos</i> (“reed, straw”) via its zero-grade stem (such as genitive <i>&#42;ḱl̥h₂mós</i>, with a shift in accent to <i>&#42;ḱĺ̥h₂mos</i>), or (according to Beekes) independently thematicized from an original ablauting paradigm <i>&#42;ḱólh₂-m̥ ~ &#42;ḱl̥h₂-ém-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>culmus</i>, Russian <i>соло́ма</i> (solóma), Old Norse <i>halmr</i>, and Old English <i>healm</i> (English <i>haulm</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calandra lark (<i>Melanocorypha calandra</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, either from Pre-Greek or Anatolian. However, there is no evidence for a similar Anatolian word yet.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>countable</i>) turquoise (a sky-blue, greenish-blue, or greenish-gray semi-precious gemstone)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Not related to <i>κάλλαιον</i> (kállaion, “cock's comb”) or <i>καλαΐς</i> (kalaḯs, “hen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wooden</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾶλον</i> (kâlon, “wood, timber”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a precinct used as a court at Athens</li><li>a board or bench of magistrates</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>κᾱ̆λῶς</i> (kā̆lôs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>κᾱ̆λός</i> (kā̆lós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beauty</li><li>nobility</li><li>a beautiful person or thing</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰλός</i> (kălós, “good, beautiful, noble”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broom, brush, besom</li><li>unknown kind of shrub</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰλλῡ́νω</i> (kăllū́nō, “to sweep”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κᾰ́λως</i> (kắlōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitcher</li><li>kind of cup</li><li>box for unguents</li><li>urn for drawing lots</li><li>cinerary urn</li></ol><p>Without a certain explanation. Often connected with Breton <i>kelorn</i> (“bucket”) but this does not explain the -α-. Furnée connects it with Ancient Greek <i>κελέβη</i> (kelébē), for which there seems no reason. Possibly from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A head covering, a hood, a veil</li><li>A fishing net shaped like a sack</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) A skull (as the brain's covering)</li><li>A grave</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) The covering of the gills of fish</li><li>The covering of honeycomb</li><li>The shell of fruits</li><li>A shutter</li></ol><p>From <i>καλύπτω</i> (kalúptō, “to cover, to conceal”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A covering of a flower or fruit: calyx, husk, shell, pod</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;epic) Some kind of woman's ornament</li><li>The alkanet (<i>Anchusa tinctoria</i>, now <i>Alkanna matthioli</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>καλύπτω</i> (kalúptō, “to cover”), despite the difference in the final consonant. The expected form would be <i>&#42;κάλυψ</i> (&#42;kálups). Ultimately probably from Pre-Greek, perhaps related to Sumerian 𒃲(GAL).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple-fish (<i>Hexaplex trunculus</i>)</li><li>purple dye obtained from it</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) rosette on the capitals of columns</li><li>crown daisy (<i>Glebionis coronaria</i>)</li></ol><p>A loan of unknown origin. Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin. It cannot be decided whether the name of the mythological seer <i>Κάλχας</i> (Kálkhas) belongs here as well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reefing rope</li><li>sounding line</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vine pole; prop</li><li>any pole or shaft</li><li>tiller</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) steering paddles</li><li>tent pole</li><li>perch for fowls</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>ᾰ̓μᾰ́κῐον</i> (ămắkĭon). According to Beekes, the suffix <i>-ακ-</i> (-ak-) is highly frequent in Pre-Greek, and he considers the word to derive from such, rejecting traditional connections to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hamô</i> (“rod, shaft”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toil, trouble</li><li>weariness, exhaustion, fatigue, tiredness</li><li>product of toil; thing made</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;finance) interest (price of credit)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κᾰ́μνω</i> (kắmnō, “to be weary”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camel</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-West Semitic <i>&#42;gamal-</i>; compare Arabic جَمَل (jamal) and Hebrew <i>גָּמָל</i> (gāmā́l), Proto-Nakh <i>&#42;imkal</i>, Old Georgian <i>აქლემი</i> (aklemi) and Tocharian A <i>oṅkaläm</i> (“elephant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) mooring rope</li></ol><p>According to Lewy, of Semitic origin. Compare Arabic حَبْل (ḥabl) and Hebrew <i>חֶבֶל</i> (ḥéḇel).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oven, furnace, kiln</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) flue for warming a room</li></ol><p>A technical loan of unknown origin. The comparison with <i>καμάρα</i> (kamára, “vaulted chamber”) has little value, while the one with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kamy</i> (“stone”) is possible. Note that the suffix <i>-ῑνος</i> (-īnos) is typical of Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of lobster or shrimp</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a kind of aconite (used as a cooling medicine)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>larkspur, <i>Consolida ajacis</i> (syn. <i>Delphinium ajacis</i>)</li><li>mandrake, <i>Mandragora officinarum</i></li></ol></ol><p>From Pre-Greek. This term is potentially a cognate of Danish <i>hummer</i>, Old Norse <i>humarr</i> (“lobster”) (which is the source of French <i>homard</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) ill-fated</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>μόρος</i> (móros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exert oneself, labour, work hard</li><li>to be weary</li><li>to be sick, ill</li><li>to suffer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱemh₂-</i> (“exert oneself, get tired”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>शम्</i> (śam, “to toil, arrange”), Hindi <i>शांति</i> (śānti, “peace and quiet”), as well as perhaps Middle Irish <i>cuma</i> (“trouble”) and <i>cumal</i> (“female slave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley-beer</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>camum</i>. Also cited in some modern sources as <i>κάμος</i>&nbsp;m (kámos). All attested forms are ambiguous between masculine and neuter (see quotations). The neuter gender has been assigned here on the basis of the Latin form.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exert oneself, labour, work hard</li><li>to be weary</li><li>to be sick, ill</li><li>to suffer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱemh₂-</i> (“exert oneself, get tired”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>शम्</i> (śam, “to toil, arrange”), Hindi <i>शांति</i> (śānti, “peace and quiet”), as well as perhaps Middle Irish <i>cuma</i> (“trouble”) and <i>cumal</i> (“female slave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caterpillar</li><li>silkworm</li><li>an eruciform ornament</li><li>a fabled Indian monster</li></ol><p>From the Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂emp-</i>, from <i>&#42;kh₂em-</i> (“bend, curve”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) sea monster</li><li>saltwater fish</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) shark</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) plain (large, flat land)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>campus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) sea monster</li><li>saltwater fish</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) shark</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) plain (large, flat land)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>campus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend; to curve</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li></ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to turn</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to double (sail around)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kámptō</i>, <i>&#42;kamptos</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂emp-</i> (“to bend, curve”), possibly related to Latin <i>campus</i> and Lithuanian <i>kampas</i>, <i>kumpas</i>. Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek or European substrate origin for the root. Compare Latin <i>cambiāre</i>, Italian <i>cambiare</i>, Portuguese <i>cambiar</i>, Spanish <i>cambiar</i>, Occitan <i>cambiar</i>, Catalan <i>canviar</i>, Norman <i>changier</i>, Sicilian <i>cangiari</i>, French <i>changer</i>, and English <i>change</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket, case</li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>κάψα</i> (kápsa), from Latin <i>capsa</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κάμψα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Campsa, Crossaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend, (act of) bending</li></ol><p>From <i>κάμπτω</i> (kámptō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden framework round which artists moulded wax or clay, block figure</li><li>mannequin or rough drawing of the human frame</li><li>lean person, skeleton, bag of bones</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>κάκκαβος</i> (kákkabos), <i>κόλλαβος</i> (kóllabos) and <i>σίττυβος</i> (síttubos). Perhaps from <i>κάννα</i> (kánna, “reed”), if properly a reed frame. However, given the variation "ν/νν", the word would be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wicker basket</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>κάννα</i> (kánna, “reed”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἄνθραξ</i> (ánthrax, “charcoal”).</li></ol><p>Generally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kand-</i> (“to shine, glow”), like Latin <i>candeō</i> (“to glow”), Albanian <i>hënë</i> (“moon”), Sanskrit <i>चन्द्र</i> (candrá, “shining”) and Old Armenian <i>խանդ</i> (xand). However Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mantle with sleeves worn by Persians</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;kandu­š</i> (“cloak”) or <i>&#42;kantuš</i> (“covering”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket of reed or cane, especially a breadbasket</li><li>basket used for the sacred barley at sacrifices</li><li>vessel of basket shape, carried in processions as a votive offering</li></ol><p>From <i>κάννα</i> (kánna, “reed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mat made of reeds</li></ol><p>From <i>κάννα</i> (kánna, “cane”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung beetle (<i>Canthon pilularius</i> syn. <i>Scarabaeus pilularius</i>)</li><li>sort of drinking-cup with large handles</li><li>kind of boat typical of Naxos</li><li>black seabream (<i>Spondyliosoma cantharus</i>)</li><li>woman's ornament, probably a gem in scarab form</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giant reed (<i>Arundo donax</i>)</li><li>reed mat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Akkadian <i>𒂵𒉡𒌑𒌝</i> (qanûm, “reed”), whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀒𐀜𐀛𐀠</i> (ko-no-ni-pi /⁠konōni-pʰi⁠/). There are indications, according to Beekes, it may have come through a Pre-Greek source, perhaps Anatolian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) hemp (<i>Cannabis sativa</i>)</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) hemp seed</li></ol><p>A Kulturwort or Wanderwort of unknown ultimate origin:</p><p>Compare (within the Indo-European language family) Albanian <i>kërp</i>, Old Armenian <i>կանեփ</i> (kanepʻ), <i>կանափ</i> (kanapʻ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;konopь</i>, Lithuanian <i>kanãpė</i>, Latvian <i>kaņepe</i>, Old Prussian <i>knapios</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hanapiz</i> (> English <i>hemp</i>), Middle Persian (kʾnb /⁠kā̆naβ⁠/), Persian کنب (kanab), کنو (kanav), کنف (kanaf, “kenaf”), Northern Kurdish <i>kinif</i>, Sogdian <i>𐼸𐼷‎𐼻𐼾𐼰</i> (ky‎npʾ /⁠kēnapā⁠/), Khwarezmian <i>𐾻𐾾𐾲-𐾺𐾾𐾻</i> (knβ-ynk), Ossetian <i>гӕн</i> (gæn), <i>гӕнӕ</i> (gænæ), Khotanese <i>𐨐𐨎𐨱</i> (kaṃha), <i>𐨐𐨂𐨎𐨦𐨌</i> (kuṃbā), Wakhi <i>kəm</i>, perhaps also to Sanskrit <i>श॒ण</i> (śaṇá), Middle Persian (šn' /⁠šan⁠/), the satem variants of the same etymon, and to Sanskrit <i>भाङ्ग</i> (bhāṅga), Persian بنگ (bang), the reverse forms of it (due to a taboo).</p><p>Compare further Neo-Assyrian Akkadian <i>𒋆𒄣𒌦𒈾𒁍</i> (qunnabu), Classical Syriac <i>ܩܢܦܐ</i> (qnpʾ), Arabic قِنَّب (qinnab), Georgian <i>კანაფი</i> (ḳanapi), Svan <i>ქან</i> (kan), Mingrelian <i>კიფი</i> (ḳipi), Laz <i>კერფი</i> (ǩerpi), West Circassian <i>кӏэп</i> (kʼɛp), East Circassian <i>щӏэп</i> (śʼɛp), Abkhaz <i>ақәны</i> (akʷnə), Eastern Mari <i>кыне</i> (kyńe), Karakalpak (kenep), Turkish <i>kendir</i>. The interrelationship of all these forms is disputed.</p><p>Compare further Svan kan, Ossetic gæn[æ], Abxaz a-kon´,containing a biconsonantal form, unique to the Central Caucasus, of the widespread root found in</p><p>Indo-European, Semitic and other Eurasian language groups.</p><p>More at Etymology of cannabis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κάνναβις</i> (kánnabis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elderflower</li></ol><p>No etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch, trench</li><li>hole, grave</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>σκάπτω</i> (skáptō, “to dig”). However, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crib, manger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kápā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kap-</i> (“to seize, to understand, to grasp”), the same root of <i>κάπτω</i> (káptō, “to gulp down”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retail dealer, huckster</li><li>innkeeper</li></ol><p>Beekes finds improbable a derivation from <i>κάπη</i> (kápē, “crib, manger”). Probably related to Latin <i>caupō</i> (“innkeeper”); as the Greek could be from Pre-Greek, this would render the Latin a substrate borrowing, as well. Furnee additionally compares Hittite <i>𒄩𒀀𒀊𒈦</i> (ḫappar, “purchase, price”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kappa, the name for the tenth letter Κ (K), κ (k) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤊</i> (k /⁠kāp⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caper (<i>Capparis spinosa</i>)</li></ol><p>The word has been connected with Persian کبر (kabar, “caper”) and Burushaski (čopuri) (a language isolate), suggesting an Oriental origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κάππεσε</i> (káppese)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boar, especially wild boar</li><li>boarfish (<i>Capros aper</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kápros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱapros</i> (“male hooved animal”), possibly from <i>&#42;ḱapr̥</i> (“penis”). Cognate with Latin <i>caper</i> (“goat”), Old Irish <i>gabor</i> (“goat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gulp down</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kapťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kap-yé-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;kap-</i> (“seize, grab”). Exact cognate of Latin <i>capiō</i>, (English <i>have</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head; face</li><li>the head or top of anything (such as a mountain)</li><li>person</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kárahə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱr̥h₂-(e)s-n-</i> (“top of the head/skull”), from the root <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“head; horn; top”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of beetle; probably a longhorn beetle</li><li>a kind of crustacean; probably a crayfish</li><li>a small boat</li></ol><p>Origin unclear; the irregular suffix and phonetics suggest it could be a Pre-Greek, Ancient Macedonian, Semitic, or Slavic loan, although ideophonic origin has also been suggested (see خرچنگ); for more, see English <i>crab</i>. Latin <i>carabus</i> is thought to be borrowed from Greek. Compare Latin <i>scarabaeus</i>, Armenian <i>քարբ</i> (kʻarb); Arabic عَقْرَب (ʕaqrab) and قَارِب (qārib), perhaps from the same ultimate source. Compare also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korãbľь</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outlandish, foreign</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Kretschmer, it is from the toponym <i>Qarbana</i> in Egypt, whence the captive Danaans escaped to the Peloponnese. According to Hommel, this word is identical to <i>κορβᾶν</i> (korbân, “sacrificial gift”), which became an epithet for Phoenician merchants, but this is hardly convincing. Perhaps there is a relation with the name of the east wind in Cyrene, <i>Κάρβας</i> (Kárbas). According to Neumann, it is from Hittite (kureṷana, “foreign state that is not a vassal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garden cress (<i>Lepidium sativum</i>), of which the seed was eaten like mustard</li></ol><p>A wanderword, related to Middle Armenian <i>կոտեմ</i> (kotem, “garden cress”), which see for closer forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of mercenary soldier by the Persians</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;kāratākah</i> (literally “traveller”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kneading trough</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, undoubtedly of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homeric and Ionic form of <i>κᾰ́ρᾱ</i> (kắrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head; mountain top (used mostly in the plural form)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱŕ̥h₂-es-n-h₂</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“head, top”). Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>κάρα</i> (kára, “head, face”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hirzniją</i> (“brain”) and Old Armenian <i>սար</i> (sar, “tip, top; mountain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wages for clipping or shearing</li></ol><p>From <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to shear”) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool shorn off</li><li>cream skimmed off</li></ol><p>From <i>κείρω</i> (keírō) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gallic horn, a wind instrument used by Celts</li><li>kind of cart</li></ol><p>From Gaulish <i>carnon</i> (“horn”), from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;karnos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i>. From the same root are <i>κέρας</i> (kéras, “horn”), <i>κάρᾱ</i> (kárā, “head”) and <i>κάρηνον</i> (kárēnon, “head; mountain top”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carenum, sweet wine boiled down</li></ol><p>Uncertain; Grimme assumes a loan from Semitic, suggesting comparison to Akkadian <i>𒆪𒊒𒌦𒉡</i> (ku-ru-un-nu /⁠kurunnu⁠/, “alcoholic beverage, a choice kind of beer or wine, possibly made with sesame”) via Hittite. This was considered doubtful by Beekes without giving argument, perhaps from the lack of evidence of a definitive Hittite transmission. For possible further foreign connections compare Egyptian <i>kꜣm</i> especially its earlier forms, its Semitic counterparts, and the Akkadian <i>𒃾</i> (GEŠTIN /⁠karānu⁠/, “wine, vine, grapes”). Alternatively perhaps from or related to <i>καρύϊνος</i> (karúïnos, “made of nuts, to be nut-brown in color”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κᾰρώ</i> (kărṓ, “caraway”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavy sleep; torpor; drowsiness</li></ol><p>Said to be from <i>κᾰ́ρᾱ</i> (kắrā, “head”), as in "to have a heavy head", but Beekes finds this doubtful.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flax (<i>Linum usitatissimum</i> or <i>Linum bienne</i>)</li><li>cotton</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>כַּרְפַּס</i> (karpás, “a type of expensive fabric made of cotton”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fruit-bearing, fruitful</li></ol><p>From <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “fruit”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos, suffix for adjective of capability).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>screw pine (<i>Pandanus odorifer</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “fruit”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>car, cart</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>carrum</i>, neuter of <i>carrus</i> (<i>see the variant</i> <i>κάρρος</i>&nbsp;m (kárrhos)), itself from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;karros</i> (“wagon”). Also see the Italian <i>carro</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very; very much; extremely</li></ol><p>From an earlier unattested <i>&#42;καρτύς</i> (&#42;kartús) + <i>-α</i> (-a, “adverbial suffix, -ly”), from a metathesis of <i>κρατύς</i> (kratús, “strong, mighty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket with a pointed bottom</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic, Ionic, and Doric form of <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “strength”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nut (hard-shelled seed)</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin.</p><p>Traditionally compared with Latin <i>carīna</i> (“ship's keel; shell of a nut”) and Sanskrit <i>करक</i> (karaka, “water jar; coconut (shell)”). The Latin term is likely cognate, though whether via borrowing from Greek, a common third source, or via inheritance (traditionally taken as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ker-</i> (“hard”), whose existence has been doubted, including by Beekes) is uncertain, while the Sanskrit term is more dubious, particularly as the "coconut shell" meaning is listed as lexicographic, indicating that it may not have been the primary meaning.</p><p>Based on the Hesychian variant <i>ἄρυα</i> (árua, “Heraclitean nut”) displaying a <i>κ</i> (k) / ∅ interchange in the initial, Beekes takes the word as Pre-Greek.</p><p>An alternative theory derives the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“head”). Cognates include English <i>harns</i>, Old Norse <i>hjarni</i>, Latin <i>cerebrum</i> (all meaning “brain”) and Avestan <i>𐬯𐬁𐬭𐬀</i> (sāra), Persian سر (sar) and Sanskrit <i>शिर॑स्</i> (śíras) (all meaning “head”). Also compare <i>κρᾱνῐ́ον</i> (krānĭ́on, “skull”), <i>κέρᾰς</i> (kérăs, “horn”) and <i>κᾰ́ρᾱ</i> (kắrā, “face”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ποντικόν κάρυον</i> (Pontikón káruon), hazelnut</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arid stalk, dry twig, chip of wood</li><li>dry straw</li></ol><p>From <i>κάρφω</i> (kárphō, “to dry up, to wither”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up, wither, shrivel</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)krebʰ-</i> (“to wither, warp, stiffen”), which Frisk considers sound-symbolic, with cognates in Balto-Slavic and Germanic including Russian <i>коро́бить</i> (koróbitʹ, “to bend, warp”), Lithuanian <i>skrèbti</i> (“to get stiff”), Old Norse <i>skorpna</i> (“to writhe, wither”). However, the consistent <i>α</i>-vocalism of the Greek words is problematic; based on this, Beekes derives the Greek word from Pre-Greek. It is still possible that the Balto-Slavic and Germanic terms are cognate with the Greek, whether via borrowing from a common substrate source, or via irregular sound changes from <i>&#42;(s)krebʰ-</i> which are not particularly unusual for sound-symbolic roots, as Frisk notes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp-pointed, jagged, with sharp or jagged teeth</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sharp, biting (<i>of language</i>)</li></ol><p>Apparently an onomatopoeic reduplicative formation with proto-form along the lines of <i>&#42;kʰar-kʰar-</i>, with similar terms in many languages including Sanskrit <i>खर</i> (khára, “hard, raw, sharp”), Persian خار (xâr, “thorn”), Old Armenian <i>կարկառ</i> (karkaṙ, “heap of stones”), though there is a phonetic mismatch between Sanskrit <i>kh-</i> and Greek <i>χ-</i> (kh-). Beekes considers the <i>κ-χ</i> (k-kh) alternation implied by a related term in the Greek corpus, <i>κάρκαροι</i> (kárkaroi), to suggest Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heaviness in the head, drowsiness</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰρόω</i> (kăróō, “to plunge into deep sleep or torpor”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything stitched of leather</li><li>sole of a shoe or sandal</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;in the plural) patchings, botchings, of bad music</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰσσῡ́ω</i> (kăssū́ō, “to stitch, sew together”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweet chestnuts</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a substrate, either Pre-Greek or Anatolian. Compare also Old Armenian <i>կասկ</i> (kask, “chestnut”) and Albanian <i>thanë</i> (“cranberry shrub; cornel”) (&lt; Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;tsàna</i>), which appear to be from the same Mediterranean substrate. Has been claimed by ancient writers to derive from <i>Καστανέα</i> (Kastanéa), a location in Magnesia, Thessalia, but the location itself is most likely named after the tree.</p><p>Other theories link the word as potentially from Doric Greek <i>κάστον</i> (káston, “wood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rare singular form of <i>κᾰ́στᾰνᾰ</i> (kắstănă, “sweet chestnuts”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ξύλον</i> (xúlon, “wood, timber”).</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>ἄκαστος</i> (ákastos, “maple”), noting that the hard wood of this tree is excellent for building; he further compares <i>κόστον</i> (kóston, “wooden part of a carrier”), suggesting a possible Pre-Greek origin. Then again, compare Sanskrit <i>काष्ठ</i> (kāṣṭha, “wood, timber”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fort</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) camp (base of a military group)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>castrum</i>, from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;kastrom</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beaver</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Sanskrit <i>कस्तूरी</i> (kastūrī, “musk”) or Doric Greek <i>κάστον</i> (káston, “wood”).</p><p>Additional etymological informationThe existence of Sanskrit <i>कस्तूरी</i> (kastūrī, “musk”), Sanskrit <i>कस्तूरिका</i> (kastūrikā, “musk-deer, musk”), and related terms points to reconstructing Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kestor-</i> (“beaver, musk”) or a similar lemma. Note also Sanskrit <i>कशीका</i> (kaśīkā́, “weasel”), Sanskrit <i>कश</i> (káśa, “a type of rodent”), with etymologically suspect ś.Beekes' assertion that the Sanskrit terms were derived from the Greek is absurd, given the attestation of the Indic terms not only in Classical Sanskrit, but also early Buddhist Pali and Jain Ardhamagadhi Prakrit, as well as India being the primary source of European musk. More plausible is that <i>καστόριον</i> (kastórion, “castoreum”) is a borrowing from Sanskrit, given its late attestation in Greek, although it is equally likely that the Greek terms were internally developed.The traditional theory by Kretschmer derives the word from the name <i>Κᾰ́στωρ</i> (Kắstōr, “Castor”), who, in Greek mythology, was known as a savior of women, supposedly for the medicinal effect of castor fluid for women's diseases. Whether or not Castor was mentioned in relation to beavers in Greek mythology, the relationship between the two is presumably of Indo-European vintage, as reflected in Sanskrit <i>नकुल</i> (nakulá, “mongoose, weasel; Castor”), which similarly refers to both animal and the specific horse-twin. Beavers did not exist in Greece proper, as pointed out by some, but were nonetheless mentioned by Herodotus to exist in the North Pontic area, near the Proto-Indo-European homeland, so it is almost certain the Hellenic tribes were familiar with beavers before venturing into Greece.A candidate for a native Pre-Greek word for "beaver" might be <i>λᾰ́τᾰξ</i> (lắtăx).Note that the main Indo-European word for "beaver", <i>&#42;bʰébʰrus</i> (which did not survive or exist in Hellenic), may be a reduplicative (and thus non-atomic) formation from <i>&#42;bʰerH-</i> (“brown”).While the Sanskrit derivation of καστόριον in Greek describing a type of musk exuded by beavers is plausible, the Greek Κάστωρ (Kastor), the name of a mythological character, is another matter.In Mycenaean Linear B there is the man’s name Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀡𐀬𐀏𐀵</i> (po-ru-ka-to), assuming a typical nominative, ‘Polykastos’ (Πολύκαστος). An ending in <i>‑or</i> is possible too. If Castor’s mythical twin Pollux, also known as Polydeuces, was literally <i>very sweet</i>, κάστωρ may have been adjectival and had a meaning other than <i>sweet</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κάστωρ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Castor</li></ol><p>From <i>κάστωρ</i> (kástōr, “beaver”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantine spelling of <i>κάττα</i> (kátta)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>against, opposing</li><li>into</li><li>against</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>downwards</li><li>along, through, in</li><li>towards</li><li>during</li><li>for, for the purpose of</li><li>according to, in conformity with</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kətá</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱm̥-th₂</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> (“beside, with”).</p><p>Cognate with Hittite <i>𒅗𒀜𒋫</i> (katta, “down from, at, with, under”), Cuneiform Luwian (zanta, “down”), Tocharian B <i>kätk-</i> (“to lower”) and perhaps Old Welsh <i>cant</i>, Modern Welsh <i>gan</i> (“with”).</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of separation. The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional and the PIE direct object.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down, come down</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to come back, return</li><li>(<i>of revenue</i>) to come in</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “downwards, down; back”) +&lrm; <i>εἶμῐ</i> (eîmĭ, “to go, come”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intensive</i>) extremely thin, emaciated</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰ́τ-</i> (kắt-, “very”, intensive) +&lrm; <i>ἰ˘σχνός</i> (ĭskhnós, “thin”, adjective).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant</li><li>(<i>and especially</i>) a military colonist</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) in the house, indoors</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) inhabiting</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “fully”) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “home”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mirror</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>κατόψομαι</i> (katópsomai) (root <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i> (“to see”)), future of <i>καθοράω</i> (kathoráō, “to behold”) + <i>-τρον</i> (-tron, instrument suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cat</li></ol><p>Per Babiniotis, borrowed into Koiné Greek from Late Latin <i>catta</i> (“female cat”); see there for further information.</p><p>Per Beekes, of unknown ultimate origin, but related to the Latin counterpart as well as cognates in various other European languages.</p><p>Replaced <i>αἴλουρος</i> (aílouros) in later usage.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>downwards</li><li>beneath, below</li><li>in or to the underworld (Hades)</li><li>on the coast</li><li>southward</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>under, below</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ́</i> (kătắ) +&lrm; <i>-ω</i> (-ō, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>βούφθᾰλμον</i> (boúphthălmon, “oxeye”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pebble (in the bed of a river)</li><li>gravel; shingle</li></ol><p>The formation is like <i>τρᾰ́φηξ</i> (trắphēx), <i>ὄρπηξ</i> (órpēx) and <i>νᾰ́ρθηξ</i> (nắrthēx). The word has been compared with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haglaz</i> (“hail; hailstone”). Beekes claims that the presence of the alternative form points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parched barley</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) winter bud</li></ol><p>The word has been connected with <i>κέγχρος</i> (kénkhros, “millet”), but the meaning of this noun rather points towards a connection with <i>κάγκανος</i> (kánkanos, “arid, barren”). Probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket, case</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>capsa</i> (“case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gulping down</li></ol><p>From <i>κάπτω</i> (káptō, “to gulp down”) +&lrm; <i>σις</i> (sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of millet</li></ol><p>From <i>κέγχρος</i> (kénkhros, “millet”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millet (<i>Panicum miliaceum</i>)</li><li>anything in small grains</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>spawn of fish</li><li>small beads</li><li>speck, sty in the eye</li></ol><li>a small kind of diamond</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰénkʰros</i>, mostly derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰer-gʰ-r-o-</i> (“pulverized”), reduplicated from the root <i>&#42;gʰer-</i> (“to rub, grind”) with dissimilation <i>r…r</i> &gt; <i>n…r</i>, and further connected with Ancient Greek <i>χερμάς</i> (khermás), <i>χεράς</i> (kherás, “pebble, gravel”), but the etymology remains unclear. Per Beekes, this and similar words may be of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Certain morbid affections mentioned by Hippocrates which, in his opinion, resulted from the continual horse-exercise of the Scythians. Possibly aneurysmal or varicose dilatations, chronic tumors, or collections of fluid.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of cedar</li></ol><p>From <i>κέδρος</i> (kédros, “cedar”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cedar (tree of genus <i>Cedrus</i> or <i>Juniperus</i>)</li><li>anything made of cedar-wood</li><li>cedar-oil</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps a Mediterranean Pre-Greek borrowing, maybe the same word as Arabic قَطْرَان (qaṭrān, “resin, tar”) and related to Akkadian <i>𒌑𒆐𒊏𒀀𒉡</i> (<sup>U₂</sup>qat₆-ra-a-nu /⁠qatrānu⁠/, “cedar resin”), particularly considering the region to which the genus <i>Cedrus</i> is native. Compare also قَطَر (qaṭar, “Qatar”) and its root related to the dribbling of resins, secretions, and other aromatic resinous woods like agarwood. Note also Old Armenian <i>ցիրդ</i> (cʻird).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κέκλετο</i> (kékleto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loud noise, din, clamour</li><li>musical sound</li><li>clear voice; shout, cry</li><li>chirp of cicadas</li><li>twitter of birds</li></ol><p>From a root <i>κέλᾰ-</i> (kélă-) of unclear origin + a suffix <i>-δος</i> (-dos) seen also in <i>ἄραδος</i> (árados, “disturbance”), <i>ὅμαδος</i> (hómados, “noise, din”), <i>ῥοῖβδος</i> (rhoîbdos, “rushing or whirring noise”) and <i>χρόμαδος</i> (khrómados, “crash, din”). Probably not related to <i>κλητός</i> (klētós, “called, invited”) (which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₁-</i> (“to call”)), as the expected form would then be <i>&#42;κέλεδος</i> (&#42;kéledos). See also <i>κελαρύζω</i> (kelarúzō, “to murmur”), which has been compared.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) road, way, path</li></ol><p>Probably from the same root as <i>κέλομαι</i> (kélomai) and <i>κελεύω</i> (keleúō, “to urge, command”), that is, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kel-</i> (“to drive”); however, the -θ- is difficult to explain morphologically. If so, then cognate with Lithuanian <i>kẽlias</i> and Latvian <i>ceļš</i> (“road, way”) and with Albanian <i>kaloj</i> (“to pass, walk by”). Older theories attempted to connect the word to the root <i>&#42;ἐλεύθ-</i> (&#42;eleúth-) (for which see <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai) and <i>ἦλθον</i> (êlthon) for more) in order to explain the -θ-; however, the initial κ- then becomes unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an order, command, behest</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>call, summons</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κελεύω</i> (keleúō, “urge, order”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>racehorse</li><li>riding horse</li><li>fast-sailing yacht with one bank of oars</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) female genitals</li></ol><p>Perhaps a derivation in -ητ- from <i>κέλομαι</i> (kélomai, “to incite”), or directly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kel-</i> (“fast; swift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) cell, room, chamber</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cella</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive on, to run a ship to land, put a ship to shore</li><li>to put to shore, put to harbor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;keľľō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kel-</i> (“to drive”). Cognates include Latin <i>celer</i>, Sanskrit <i>कलयति</i> (kaláyati), and Old English <i>healdan</i> (English <i>hold</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to command</li><li>to urge, exhort</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kelomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kel-</i> (“to drive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheath, case</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) pod, shell</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) envelop of a chrysalis</li><li>eggshell</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow of the eye</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, connected with the group of <i>καλύπτω</i> (kalúptō, “to cover”), with a Pre-Greek suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) son</li><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>ἐκτομίας</i> (ektomías, “castrated person”), <i>γάλλος</i> (gállos, “eunuch”), <i>σπάδων</i> (spádōn, “eunuch”).</li><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”).</li></ol><p>The ending, which can be found also in <i>ἕλωρ</i> (hélōr) and <i>τέκμωρ</i> (tékmōr), suggests an original neuter descendance. Perhaps it arose from <i>*κέρωρ</i> by dissimilation, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱer-</i> (“to grow”), the same root of Old Armenian <i>սեռ</i> (seṙ, “kind, species, race”). Alternatively, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelH-</i> (“to rise”), the same root of Lithuanian <i>ki̇̀lti</i> (“to rise”).</p><p>If dissimilated from <i>*κέρωρ</i>, the word may derive from <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to cut”). However, given the meaning, one would rather expect a foreign word, perhaps of Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>From the same root of <i>κελαρύζω</i> (kelarúzō, “to murmur”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μέγα κῆτος</i> (méga kêtos, literally “large sea-monster”).</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>κάμπη</i> (kámpē, “sea-monster”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centaur; alternative form of <i>Κένταυρος</i> (Kéntauros)</li></ol><p>From <i>Κένταυρος</i> (Kéntauros, “a savage Thessalian race”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κένταυρος)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Centaur, a member of a savage race dwelling between Mt. Pelion and Mt. Ossa on the Northeastern coast of Thessaly.</li><li>centaur</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Centaurus</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>κεντέω</i> (kentéō, “I goad, wound”) +&lrm; <i>ταῦρος</i> (taûros, “bull”) either from bull-fighting or from herding. Often linked to the Indo-Iranian etymon of which Sanskrit <i>गन्धर्व</i> (gandharva) is a reflex, in which case substrate borrowing and/or substantial remodeling is indicated.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something with a sharp point: point, spike, spur</li><li>sting, quill, thorn (<i>of bees and wasps</i>)</li><li>goad, oxgoad (<i>used to prod animals or as torture device on humans</i>)</li><li>nail, rivet</li><li>stationary point of a pair of compasses</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) center of a circle</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;vulgar) penis</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kéntron</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱéntrom</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱent-</i> (“to prick; point”); direct cognate with Albanian <i>çandër</i> (“forked pole”) and probably Old Armenian <i>սանտր</i> (santr), <i>սանդր</i> (sandr, “comb”).</p><p>Equivalent to <i>κεντέω</i> (kentéō, “to sting”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron, “tool suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storm petrel (<i>Hydrobates pelagicus</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) simpleton, fool, dullard</li></ol><p>A by-form is <i>κεμπός</i> (kempós, “stupid person”), where the prenasalization shows that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potter's clay</li><li>earthen vessel, pottery, wine-jar</li><li>tile</li></ol><p>Unknown. Has long been compared to <i>κεράννυμι</i> (keránnumi, “to mix”), but could also be related to Latin <i>cremō</i>, Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;herþ</i> (English <i>hearth</i>), or Sanskrit <i>कूडयति</i> (kūḍayati, “to burn, scorch”). Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek or Anatolian origin. Compare Sanskrit <i>कर्द</i> (karda) and Sanskrit <i>कर्दम</i> (kardama).</p><p>Related is <i>κερᾰμεύς</i> (kerămeús, “potter”), which has a cognate in Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀐𐀨𐀕𐀄</i> (ke-ra-me-u /⁠kerameus⁠/) of the same meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horn (of an animal)</li><li>horn as a material, or anything made of horn, such as a bow.</li><li>(<i>music</i>) horn (musical instrument)</li><li>arm or branch of a river</li><li>the side branch (either left or right) of a military array for battle.</li><li>(metonymic,&#32;horn of a powerful animal as a tool) horn used in biblical interpretation as representing a person as focus of a group for power.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kérats</i>, probably remodeled from an earlier <i>s</i>-stem <i>&#42;kéras</i> (like <i>κᾰ́ρᾱ</i> (kắrā), <i>κᾰ́ρηνον</i> (kắrēnon), <i>κρᾱνίον</i> (krāníon)), as preserved in e.g. <i>κερασ-φόρος</i> (keras-phóros, “with a horn”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“head, top; front of the skull; horn”); see there for more. An older etymology supposes Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱer-n̥t-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>κερᾰσός</i> (kerăsós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gain, profit</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerd-</i> (“craft, cunning”) and cognate with Old Irish <i>cerd</i> (“craft, skill”) (&lt; <i>&#42;ḱerd-h₂-</i>), Welsh <i>cerdd</i> (“song”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treecreeper (<i>Certhia familiaris</i>)</li></ol><p>Unexplained; the word hardly belongs to <i>κρέξ</i> (kréx). Likely from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀκρίς</i> (akrís, “grasshopper, locust”).</li></ol><p>The word hardly belongs to <i>κέρκος</i> (kérkos, “tail”), as per Frisk. The word is rather Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teal (<i>Anas crecca</i>)</li></ol><p>Compared with Latin <i>querquedula</i> (“teal”), which is paraphrased with <i>κερκήδης</i> (kerkḗdēs). It is uncertain whether this word belongs to <i>κέρκος</i> (kérkos, “tail”) or to the group of <i>κρέξ</i> (kréx, “corncrake”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the tail of a beast</li><li>a penis</li><li>a handle</li><li>a small animal that attacks grapevines</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>synonym of <i>ἀλεκτρυών</i> (alektruṓn)</li></ol><li>a tongue of flame</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a stake, picket, or pale</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a burin (a chisel-like tool used for engraving)</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek origin. Compare <i>κερκίς</i> (kerkís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boat, small ship</li><li>type of fish</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>κέρκος</i> (kérkos, “tail”). Could also be of Semitic origin, compare Classical Syriac <i>ܩܪܩܘܪܐ</i> (qarqūrāʾ, “boat”), reshaped under the influence of <i>κέρκος</i> (kérkos) and <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”). Compare also Latin <i>cercūrus</i>, Old Armenian <i>կուր</i> (kur).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragment</li><li>coin, especially of copper</li><li>(<i>collectively</i>) cash</li></ol><p>From <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to cut short, shear, clip”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large earthen dish with small pots, in which fruits were offered to the Korybantes</li></ol><p>The noun has tentatively been linked to other Indo-European words, such as <i>κέραμος</i> (kéramos, “clay, tile”), Sanskrit <i>चरु</i> (caru, “pot, saucepan”), Latin <i>scrīnium</i> (“chest”), Old English <i>hwer</i> (“kettle, pot, basin”), but to no avail. According to Beekes and Chantraine the word is Pre-Greek, in view of the variant <i>κέρχνος</i> (kérkhnos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stinging, reproachful</li><li>mocking, delusive</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>κερτομέω</i> (kertoméō, “to taunt, sneer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kestrel (<i>Falco tinnunculus</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>κέρχνος</i> (kérkhnos, “raw voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rough excrescence</li><li>hoarseness, roughness of the throat</li><li>silver dust</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps of onomatopoeic origin, like Latin <i>hirriō</i> (“to snarl”) and English <i>yarr</i>. Alternatively of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharp hammer</li><li>barracuda</li></ol><p>From <i>κεντέω</i> (kentéō, “to sting, goad”) +&lrm; <i>-τρα</i> (-tra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ironwort of species <i>Sideritis romana</i> subsp. <i>purpurea</i> (syn. <i>Sideritis purpurea</i>)</li><li>woundwort of species <i>Stachys alopecuros</i></li><li>serrated implement, used in encaustic painting</li></ol><p>From <i>κεντέω</i> (kentéō, “to prick, goad”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharpness or roughness of the tongue</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) first sprout of seeds</li><li>bolt discharged from engines</li></ol><p>From <i>κεντέω</i> (kentéō, “to sting, goad”) +&lrm; <i>-τρος</i> (-tros).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κέστρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Cestrus; Aksu River (Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a species of mullet (<i>Mugil cephalus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κέφαλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cephalus</li></ol><p>From <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κέχυτο</i> (kékhuto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κήδεα</i> (kḗdea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to trouble, distress, vex</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to be concerned for, care for [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>κῆδος</i> (kêdos, “care, concern, mourning”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holly (<i>Ilex aquifolium</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimate origin is unknown. Formation like <i>δέπαστρον</i> (dépastron) and <i>κάναστρον</i> (kánastron). The suffix is Pre-Greek.</p><p>Some authors argue that this is possibly a Mediterranean Wanderwort, and relate it with Sardinian <i>golosti, colostri</i> (“holly”) and Basque <i>gorosti</i> (“holly”). See those for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) burning, blazing</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>καίω</i> (kaíō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>oncology</i>) tumour, swelling</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hernia, rupture</li><li>hump on a buffalo's back</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂u-l-eh₂</i> (“tumour”). Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kyla</i> (“rupture”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haulaz</i> (“hernia”). However the Attic form is still unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swape, swing-beam for drawing water, shadoof</li><li>stallion, male ass</li></ol><p>Unknown; possibly of Pre-Greek origin. A secondary formation in <i>-ων</i> (-ōn). Vendryes proposed to start from <i>κῆλον</i> (kêlon, “arrow, projectile”) in the unattested sense of "penis", but this cannot be further substantiated.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea swallow; tern (<i>Sterna hirundo</i>)</li></ol><p>Has been compared with bird names like Middle Welsh <i>cuan</i> (“screech-owl”) and other Celtic words, whence Latin <i>cāvannus</i> (“tawny owl”). Within Greek, one also finds forms with internal velar, like <i>καυκαλίας</i> (kaukalías, “kind of bird”), with which are compared Lithuanian <i>kaukȳs</i> (“kind of bird”) and primary verbs like Sanskrit <i>कौति</i> (kauti, “to cry; to hum”) and Lithuanian <i>kaũkti</i> (“to howl; to moan”). However, according to Beekes, the word is Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death; doom</li><li>goddess or demon of death</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kā́r</i>. No suitable Indo-European root exists, therefore the source is probably Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κήρ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ker, goddess of death or doom</li><li>one of the Keres</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beebread; sandarac (bee pollen with added honey and bee secretions, stored in brood cells)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word seems Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix <i>-ινθος</i> (-inthos). In any case, compare <i>κηρός</i> (kērós, “wax; honeycomb”), also believed to be a substrate word.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κήρινθος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cerinthus, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>waxen</li><li>pliable as wax</li><li>wax coloured, pallid</li></ol><p>From <i>κηρός</i> (kērós, “wax”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proclamation, edict, decree, especially if announced by herald</li><li>reward offered in a proclamation</li><li>Christian preaching, sermon</li></ol><p>From <i>κηρύσσω</i> (kērússō, “I proclaim”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix), from <i>κῆρῠξ</i> (kêrŭx, “herald”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>base, fake, counterfeit (especially of coins)</li><li>deceitful, dishonest, fraudulent</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibilities include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dross or alloy of gold</li></ol><p>Related to <i>κίβδηλος</i> (kíbdēlos, “fraudulent; fake, sham”), which see for the Semitic origin, through the idea of gold being adulterated by being made an alloy.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A pouch, a wallet, such as Perseus wore</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek and related to <i>κιβωτός</i> (kibōtós, “wooden box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dipper (<i>Cinclus cinclus</i>)</li></ol><p>The variations show that the word is Pre-Greek, and therefore not related to Sanskrit <i>चञ्चल</i> (cañcala, “nimble; shaking; flickering”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wily, cunning, sly</li></ol><p>According to Beekes the adjectival usage is secondary and the main usage for the word was as an animal name meaning "fox" and due to all the variations of the word he supports that it is a word of Pre-Greek origin. However, it also matches the Semitic root <i>כ־ד־ב</i> (k-ḏ-b) / ك ذ ب (k ḏ b) (Hebrew <i>כ־ז־ב</i> (k-z-b)) related to lies, most closely in Aramaic <i>כַּדָּבָא</i> (kaddāḇā, “frequently lying”) and in Arabic كَذَّاب (kaḏḏāb, “frequently lying”). In the latter case, the word is a doublet of <i>κίβδηλος</i> (kíbdēlos, “deceitful; fake”) in case that term is also of the same origin and not of Pre-Greek origin, contrary to what is suggested by Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the dawning day</i>) to be spread over</li><li>to be diffused</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castor oil</li><li>croton (<i>Ricinus communis</i>)</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>kꜣkꜣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock of curly hair</li><li>ringlet</li></ol><p>Unknown. A prenasalized <i>&#42;κίγκιννος</i> (&#42;kínkinnos) is assumed source of Latin <i>cincinnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made from the castor oil tree</li></ol><p>From <i>κίκι</i> (kíki, “castor oil tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ox with crooked horns</li><li>jar</li></ol><p>Furnée connects it with <i>κελλόν</i> (kellón, “twisted, athwart”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin in view of the variation <i>κιλ-/κελ-</i>.</p><p>Furnée connects it with <i>κύλιξ</i> (kúlix, “drinking cup”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin in view of the interchange <i>λ/λλ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinaedus, catamite</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) lewd fellow</li><li>public dancer</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) obscene poems</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Archigenes it is a Classical Syriac word, while Beekes prefers a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>danger, risk, hazard</li><li>trial, venture</li><li>battle</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>κίνδαξ</i> (kíndax, “quick, nimble”), <i>ὀνοκίνδιος</i> (onokíndios, “donkey-driver”) is formally attractive. For the semantics, Prellwitz and Vendryes compare Latin <i>sollicitus</i> (“agitated, disturbed”), which is from <i>sollus</i> (“whole, entire”) + <i>cieō</i> (“move, stir, shake”). Attempts at an Indo-European etymology should be given up. The Pre-Greek character is clear in view of the ending <i>-ῡν-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>οἱ μικροί ἰχθύες</i> (hoi mikroí ikhthúes, literally “the small fish”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movement</li></ol><p>From <i>κινέω</i> (kinéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motion</li><li>dance</li><li>political movement, revolt</li><li>change, revolution</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) inflection</li><li>(<i>law</i>) punitive action</li></ol><p>From <i>κῑνέω</i> (kīnéō, “I move”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladle or stick for stirring</li></ol><p>From <i>κῑνέω</i> (kīnéō, “to move, to stir”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>κιννάμωμον</i> (kinnámōmon)</li></ol><p>Alteration by haplology of <i>κιννάμωμον</i> (kinnámōmon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) cicada</li></ol><p>Unknown, only attested in Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket for corn</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>כִּיּוֹר</i> (kiyyōr, “pot, basin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type of hawk or falcon</li><li>type of wolf</li><li>circle, ring</li><li>racecourse, circus</li><li>type of stone</li><li>(<i>Hesychius</i>) κωπηλάτης (kōpēlátēs, “rower”)</li><li>(<i>Hesychius</i>) ἡ τοῦ αἰγείρου βλάστησις (hē toû aigeírou blástēsis, “sprouting of the black poplar”)</li></ol><p>Compare <i>κρέξ</i> (kréx, “crane, crex”) and <i>κρίκος</i> (kríkos, “ring”) for the "hawk" and "circle" meanings respectively (the different senses may well be of different origin). See also Georgian <i>კირკიტა</i> (ḳirḳiṭa, “kestrel”) for a similar formation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of thistle</li></ol><p>From <i>κιρσός</i> (kirsós, “varicose vein”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weevil (beetle in the superfamily Curculionoidea)</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Sanskrit <i>कीट</i> (kīṭa, “worm, insect”) is unrelated. According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pumice stone</li></ol><p>Furnée connects <i>γεῖσον</i> (geîson). Undoubtedly a Pre-Greek word, in view of the variation λ/ρ.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rockrose (<i>Cistus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: a kind of bird</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of the cluster "-ρν-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian jay (<i>Garrulus glandarius</i>)</li><li>craving for strange food during pregnancy</li></ol><p>From an onomatopoeic root, similar to Sanskrit <i>कीकि</i> (kīki, “jay”), <i>कि॒कि॒दी॒वि</i> (kikidīví, “partridge”) and Old English <i>higera</i> (“magpie”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of ivy</li></ol><p>From <i>κισσός</i> (kissós, “ivy”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box, chest, casket</li></ol><p>Of unknown substrate origin, traced back to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kisteh₂</i> (“woven container”) only with Middle Irish <i>cess</i> (“basket, causeway of wickerwork, beehive”), Old Welsh <i>cest</i> (“basket”). Bernal suggests, with formal problems as usual, borrowing from Egyptian <i>qrsw</i> (“coffin”) or <i>qrst</i> (“burial”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) of the citron tree</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) of a citron yellow (<i>color/colour</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κίτρον</i> (kítron, “citron tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) fruit of the citron tree</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>citrum</i> (“citron”) &lt; <i>citrus</i>, itself probably via Etruscan from Ancient Greek <i>κέδρος</i> (kédros, “cedar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thrush (bird)</li><li>wrasse</li></ol><p>Probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicory (<i>Cichorium intybus</i>)</li></ol><p>No etymology. Perhaps from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keyh₂-</i> (“to start to move”). Cognate with Latin <i>cieō</i> (“to set in motion, invoke”) (though not Sanskrit <i>च्यवते</i> (cyavate), as was traditionally assumed).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillar, column</li><li>a columnar gravestone</li><li>the uvula</li><li>the division of the nostrils, cartilage of the nose</li><li>a type of meteor</li><li>a type of wart</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱiHwon-</i>, whence also Old Armenian <i>սիւն</i> (siwn, “column, pillar”). No other cognates survive.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and</li><li>even, also</li><li>both... and... when used in the construction καί... καί...</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kahi</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;kasi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱm̥t-</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> (“with”). Compare Hittite <i>𒃰𒋾</i> (kat-ti, “along with”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surpass, overcome</li></ol><p>Re-analyzed from <i>κέκασμαι</i> (kékasmai), which could share an origin with Vedic Sanskrit <i>शाश॑दान</i> (śā́śadāna, “having ability, skill, excellence”). Less certain is a connection to Gaulish <i>&#42;kaddos</i> (“holy”), which could be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱeh₃-</i> (“to sharpen”). Also compare Sanskrit <i>छन्द्</i> (chand, “to appear, to gratify”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḱend-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, slay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tḱn̥-</i>, zero-grade of <i>&#42;tḱen-</i> (“strike”), with a <b>*y</b> progressive aspect marker and simplification of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tḱ-</i> to <i>&#42;ḱ-</i> before a syllabic nasal (compare <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí) and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱm̥tóm</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renewal</li></ol><p>From <i>καινόω</i> (kainóō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>although, albeit</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a place</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in or at the right place</li><li>parts of the body</li><li>vital part, (of wounds)</li><li>mortal, grave, serious</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in season</li><li>timely; at the exact or fatal moment</li><li>lasting but for a season</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>καιρός</i> (kairós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>and indeed, and further</li><li>Used to introduce an objection by the speaker, often in rhetorical questions&#58; and yet</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>καί</i> (kaí) and <i>τοι</i> (toi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light, kindle, set on fire</li><li>to burn, burn up</li><li>to cause to be extremely cold (e.g. of frost)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to burn with passion</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to make a fire for oneself</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to cauterize</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;káyyō</i>, of uncertain origin. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keh₂w-</i> (“to burn”), with cognates such as Lithuanian <i>kūlė̃</i> (“smut fungus ("burnt fungus")”), Latvian <i>kūla</i> (“old; dry; grass of last year”), and possibly Persian سو (su, “light”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nag, old and useless horse</li></ol><p>Like Latin <i>caballus</i> (“pack-horse”), is an Asiatic loan or Wanderwort. It has been compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kobýla</i> (“mare”) and Persian کول (kaval, “second class horse of mixed blood”). Maybe borrowed from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;kabah</i>, <i>&#42;kabalah</i>, compare Khotanese (kabä, “horse”), and possibly cognate with Latin <i>cabō</i> (“gelding”), perhaps ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kebʰ-</i> (“worn-out horse, nag”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Καδμεία</i> (Kadmeía), calamine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Καδμεία</i> (Kadmeía)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Κᾰδμείᾱ</i> (Kădmeíā, “calamine”)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Καδμεία)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cadmea</li><li>(alchemy,&#32;chemistry) calamine, a pink form of zinc oxide formed as a byproduct during zinc sublimation</li></ol><p>From <i>Κᾰ́δμος</i> (Kắdmos, “Cadmus”) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia, “-ia: <i>forming related substances</i>”), probably ultimately from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;q-d-m</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love vine (<i>Cassytha filiformis</i>)</li></ol><p>From Aramaic, represented by Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>כְּשׁוּתָא</i> (kəšūṯā, “dodder”), Classical Syriac <i>ܟܫܽܘܬܳܐ</i> (kəšūṯā, “dodder”), Arabic كُشُوث (kušūṯ, “dodder”). See these entries for their possible origin. According to de Lagarde <i>καδύτας</i> is completely unreal as a transcription error, that however Pliny already had in his manuscript of Theophrast, and only <i>κασύτας</i> (kasútas) has been spoken.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κᾰτᾰ́</i> (kătắ) (<i>used before a rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>according to which, just as</li></ol><p>From <i>καθ’</i> (kath’) +&lrm; <i>ἅ</i> (há); from <i>κατά</i> (katá) and the neuter plural of <i>ὅς</i> (hós) respectively.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exactly as</li></ol><p>From <i>καθά</i> (kathá, “just as”) and <i>περ</i> (per).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten, make fast</li><li>(Epic,&#32;middle voice) to address, accost</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἅπτω</i> (háptō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>chair</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) rower's seat</li><li>sitting part, posterior, bottom</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) base of a column</li></ol><li>sitting posture</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>seated idleness, inaction</li><li>session</li></ol><li>teacher's chair, professorial chair</li><li>imperial throne</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) imperial representative</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κατά</i> (katá, “down”) +&lrm; <i>ἕδρα</i> (hédra, “seat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>καθέζετο</i> (kathézeto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit down</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἕζομαι</i> (hézomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>shipping</i>) to draw to the see, launch</li><li>to drag down</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰθ-</i> +&lrm; <i>ἕλκω</i> (“drag”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come or go down (esp. to fight)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to come down to, reach to</li><li>to come in due course</li></ol><li>to be meet, fit, proper</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to belong to</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, back, down) +&lrm; <i>ἥκω</i> (hḗkō, “to have come, be present”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>causal</i>) to make to sit down, seat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to set or place</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to set or place for any purpose, post</li></ol><li>to set up</li><li>to make an assembly take their seats, convene or hold an assembly</li><li>to put into a certain condition</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sit down, be seated, take one's seat, sit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to sit at meals</li><li>to sit as judge</li><li>to sit down in a country, encamp</li><li>to settle, sink in</li><li>(<i>of ships</i>) to run aground, be stranded</li></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;intransitive)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of birds</i>) to settle, alight</li></ol></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>ἵζω</i> (hízō, “to sit”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send down, let fall, let down, lower</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to come down</li><li>to enter (<i>a competition, a plea etc.</i>)</li><li>to put in motion</li><li>to swoop down, run down</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;active voice of present, imperfect, future, and 1st aorist tenses)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to set down, stop, bring to land</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to bring down to a place</li><li>to bring before a magistrate or king</li></ol><li>to set in order</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to ordain, appoint</li><li>to bring into a certain state</li><li>(<i>with double accusative</i>) to make or render so</li><li>to make, continue</li></ol></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;middle and passive voices, active voice of perfect, pluperfect, and 2nd aorist tenses)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be set, to set oneself down, settle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to come before another, stand in their presence</li><li>to be set as guard, to be appointed</li><li>(<i>in a physical sense</i>) to settle, deposit a sediment</li><li>to stand quiet or calm</li><li>to be established or instituted</li><li>(<i>of purchases</i>) to cost</li><li>to stand against, oppose</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down, against”) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to make stand, set up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make clean, to clean, cleanse</li><li>to clear, purify; to purify from</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) to purify</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to purify oneself</li></ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to purge</li><li>(<i>horticulture</i>) to remove growth from a plant, prune</li><li>to sift</li></ol><p>From <i>καθαρός</i> (katharós, “clean”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demolish, destroy</li><li>to seize</li><li>to achieve</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clean, cleanse, purge, purify</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰθᾰρός</i> (kăthărós, “clean, pure”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be clean, to be pure</li><li>to keep clean, to keep pure</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰθᾰρός</i> (kăthărós, “pure”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleansing, purification</li><li>atonement</li><li>purging, discharge</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰθᾰρῐ́ζω</i> (kăthărĭ́zō, “I clean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or fit for cleansing or purifying</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) promoting catharsis, purgative</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰθαίρω</i> (kăthaírō, “to cleanse, to purify”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clean, clear, pure</li></ol><p>No suitable Indo-European cognates can be drawn; the traditional connection to Sanskrit <i>शिथिर</i> (śithirá, “loose, relaxed”) requires too many ad hoc assumptions, both phonetic and semantic. This, along with <i>α</i>/<i>ο</i> variation, implies a borrowing from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>κᾰθᾰρός</i> (kăthărós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>κᾰθᾰρός</i> (kăthărós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a surgical instrument for emptying the bladder, a catheter</li></ol><p>From <i>καθίημι</i> (kathíēmi, “to let down, descend”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down to sleep, sleep</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to lie asleep, lie idle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) to sleep, lie still, be at rest</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>εὕδω</i> (heúdō, “to sleep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make to sit down</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to sit down, settle</li></ol><li>to establish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be restored</li></ol><li>to consecrate, dedicate</li><li>to found</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτᾰ- (κᾰθ-)</i> (“fully”) +&lrm; <i>ἱδρῡ́ω</i> (“make to sit down; settle; found”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>causal</i>) to make sit</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sit</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ́</i> (kătắ) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔ζᾰ́νω</i> (hĭzắnō, “to cause to sit”) with apocopic form <i>καθ’</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>general</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰθόλου</i> (kăthólou, “on the whole, in general”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look down</li><li>to behold, observe, perceive</li><li>to have within view, see distinctly</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in what manner, so far as, inasmuch as</li></ol><p>From <i>κατά</i> (katá) +&lrm; <i>ὅ</i> (hó) +&lrm; <i>τι</i> (ti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κᾰθῠ́περθεν</i> (kăthŭ́perthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>down from above</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́περθεν</i> (hŭ́perthen). By surface analysis, <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Sparta</i>) pit or underground cavern, into which state-prisoners or their corpses were thrown</li></ol><p>The connection with Sanskrit <i>केवट</i> (kevaṭa, “pit, hollow”) must be rejected, and we cannot reconstruct Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kaiyr-t</i>. The word <i>καιετός</i> (kaietós, “fissure produced by an earthquake”) may be a reshaping after <i>ὀχετός</i> (okhetós). According to Beekes, it is clearly a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>καλαμίνθη</i> (kalamínthē, “catmint”), as they say in Boeotia.</li></ol><p>It has been connected with <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “to burn”), because of its burning taste. According to Beekes, the word is rather Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fissure produced by an earthquake</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>καιάδᾱς</i> (kaiádās, “underground cavern”), with a reshaping after <i>ὀχετός</i> (okhetós, “waterpipe, conduit”) and <i>κάπετος</i> (kápetos, “ditch, trench”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the north-east wind</li></ol><p>Achilles Tatius claimed that the name came from the river <i>Κάϊκος</i> (Káïkos), as this wind came over that river in its course.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>τὴν κεφαλήν</i> (tḕn kephalḗn, literally “the head”).</li></ol><p>Lewy suggests a Semitic origin, comparing the gloss with Semitic words for "skull", like Aramaic <i>גּוּלְגַּלְתָּא</i> (gulgaltā), Hebrew <i>גֻּלְגֹּלֶת</i> (gulgṓleṯ) and Akkadian <i>𒄢𒄢𒇻</i> (/⁠gulgullu⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>new, novel, recent</li><li>fresh, unused</li><li>strange, unusual</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kəňňós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kn̥-yós</i>, from <i>&#42;ken-</i> (“new, fresh”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>कनीन</i> (kanī́na, “young”), Sanskrit <i>कन्या</i> (kanyā̀, “girl, maiden”), Avestan <i>𐬐𐬀𐬌𐬥𐬈</i> (ka<sup><small>i</small></sup>ne, “a maiden”) and, more distantly, Latin <i>recēns</i> (“new, recent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newness, freshness</li><li>novelty</li><li>(biblical,&#32;Christianity) the new state of eternal life given by the Holy Spirit</li></ol><p>From <i>καινός</i> (kainós, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, suffix forming nouns of state).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make new, renew, change</li></ol><p>From <i>καινός</i> (kainós, “new”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, factitive verb&ndash;forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>καίρῐος</i> (kaírĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>καίρῐος</i> (kaírĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure; proportion; fitness</li><li>(<i>of a time</i>) period</li><li>(<i>often in a positive sense</i>) the proper time opportunity; prime, the right moment, the fatal spot</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) season; time</li><li>lifetime</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the times</li><li>advantage; profit</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Possibly related to <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to shear, cut short”) (as a "decisive moment"), <i>κεράννυμι</i> (keránnumi, “to mix”) (as an "atmospherical mix"), or <i>κύρω</i> (kúrō, “to meet (accidentally)”); Beekes seems to favor the connection to <i>κείρω</i> (keírō) the most. Might also be related to, or the source of, Albanian <i>qëro</i> (“time”) (an archaic term).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of quality</li><li>evil, wickedness, vice, depravity</li><li>cowardice</li><li>dishonour, shame</li><li>misfortune</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκός</i> (kăkós, “bad, evil”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>three legged pot</li><li>partridge</li></ol><p>Technical loanword of unknown origin. Given the variations and the variant in Latin <i>cascabus</i>, cited by Furnée, the word is likely to be Pre-Greek.</p><p>For the ending, Chantraine compared <i>ὄτοβος</i> (ótobos), <i>κόναβος</i> (kónabos) and <i>θόρυβος</i> (thórubos). Maybe of onomatopoeic origin. On the other hand, Akkadian <i>𒆕𒅗𒁀𒉡𒄷</i> (/⁠kakkabānu⁠/, “a bird, potentially the Barbary partridge for its neck pattern”, literally “covered with stars; speckled”) has also been compared. Alternatively, the Akkadian is derived as an Anatolian loan or of onomatopoeic origin with phono-semantic matching to designate such a native name. Günther Neumann suggests a Lydian origin, as there is Hittite <i>𒆕𒅗𒉺𒄷</i> (/⁠kakkapa⁠/) and with Ancient Greek <i>κακκαβίς</i> (kakkabís) the earliest form, which is reported to be onomatopoeic in nature, was attested by the poet Alcman suspected to originally been a native of Sardis.</p><p>Note also Proto-Georgian-Zan <i>&#42;ḳaḳab-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defecate, shit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kakkajō</i>, from the Proto-Indo-European Lallwort root <i>&#42;kakka-</i> (“to defecate”).</p><p>Related to Latin <i>cacō</i>, Middle Irish <i>caccaid</i>, Old Armenian <i>քակոր</i> (kʻakor), Middle Armenian <i>քաք</i> (kʻakʻ), and German <i>kacken</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ashwagandha (<i>Withania somnifera</i>)</li><li>kind of mercury (<i>Mercurialis tomentosa</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the similarity with other plant names, such as <i>ἀκακαλίς</i> (akakalís, “daffodil”) and <i>κάγκανον</i> (kánkanon, “mercury”). Witczak prefers a Dardanic source. Compare also Dacian <i>coicolida</i> (“nightshade”), Lithuanian <i>kankùlis</i> (“corncockle”), Lithuanian <i>kankali̇̀jos</i>&nbsp;f&nbsp;pl (“bellflower”), Old Prussian <i>kunklis</i> (“corncockle”), as well as Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫkoľь</i> (“corncockle”), whence Slovene <i>kokalj</i>, Polish <i>kąkol</i> and Russian <i>куколь</i> (kukolʹ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of humans and animals</i>) ill-disposed, malicious</li><li>(<i>of humans</i>) thinking evil; given to the worst disposition for a situation</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) infamous, abominable</li><li>(medicine,&#32;of sores, fevers, diseases) malignant</li><li>(neuter,&#32;substantive,&#32;κᾰκόηθες) a bad habit</li></ol><p>From <i>κακο-</i> (kako-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>ἦθος</i> (êthos, “character”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having or being possessed by an evil genius/spirit.</li><li>ill-starred, wretched</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an evil genius</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκο-</i> (kăko-, bad) +&lrm; <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn, “god, destiny”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>κᾰκοδαίμων</i> (kăkodaímōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>κᾰκοδαίμων</i> (kăkodaímōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad reputation</li><li>false or evil doctrine</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκόδοξος</i> (kăkódoxos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unhappy imitation or rivalry</li><li>affectation of style</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκόζηλος</i> (kăkózēlos, “having bad taste”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>κᾰκός</i> (kăkós, “bad”) + <i>ζῆλος</i> (zêlos, “zeal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malevolence</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκόθῡμος</i> (kăkóthūmos, “ill-disposed”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>κᾰκός</i> (kăkós, “bad”) + <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ill-fated, miserable</li></ol><p>Formed in Judeo-Greek, from <i>κᾰκο-</i> (kăko-, “bad”) +&lrm; Hebrew <i>מַזָּל</i> (mazál, “luck”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), but spread into general (Christian) Greek by the late Byzantine period.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mischievous, baneful</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκο-</i> (kăko-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>μηχανή</i> (mēkhanḗ, “contrivance”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer ill, be in ill plight, be in distress</li></ol><p>From <i>κακοπαθής</i> (kakopathḗs, “miserable”) (from <i>κακός</i> (kakós) +&lrm; <i>παθ-</i> (path-)) + <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ill-doing, evil-doing</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) injuries</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκοποιός</i> (kăkopoiós, “doing ill, mischievous, maleficent”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>κᾰκόποιέω</i> (kăkópoiéō, “I do evil”), from <i>κᾰκός</i> (kăkós, “bad”) + <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “I do”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scheming, evil planning.</li></ol><p>From <i>κακός</i> (kakós, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>ῥάπτω</i> (rháptō, “to sew”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a sensitive stomach</li><li>bad for the stomach, unwholesome</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκο-</i> (kăko-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>στόμᾰχος</i> (stómăkhos, “stomach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do evil, work wickedness, be vicious</li><li>(<i>of discussion</i>) to use captious or unfair arguments</li><li>to maltreat, injure</li><li>to ravage (<i>a country</i>)</li><li>to spoil (<i>the argument</i>)</li><li>to corrupt, falsify</li></ol><p>From <i>κακοῦργος</i> (kakoûrgos, “malicious, evildoer”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “verb forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ill-doing, wickedness, villainy, malice, viciousness</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) malpractices</li></ol><p>From <i>κακοῦργος</i> (kakoûrgos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ill-sound, cacophony</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰκόφωνος</i> (kăkóphōnos, “ill-sounding, producing disagreeable sounds”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā) and <i>κακο-</i> (kako-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>-φωνίᾱ</i> (-phōníā, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doing evil, mischievous, malicious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evildoer, malefactor</li><li>thief, robber</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>κακο-</i> (kako-, bad) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, worker; maker).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ill-disposed</li></ol><p>From <i>κακο-</i> (kako-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “spirit, soul”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ill-fated</li></ol><p>From <i>κακο-</i> (kako-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>μοῖρα</i> (moîra, “fate, lot”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>As a measure of quality&#58; bad, worthless, useless</li><li>As a measure of appearance&#58; ugly, hideous</li><li>Of circumstances&#58; injurious, wretched, unhappy</li><li>As a measure of character&#58; low, mean, vile, evil</li></ol><p>The origin is unknown, with multiple theories proposed:</p><p>See also Phrygian <i>κακον</i> (kakon, “harm”), which was borrowed from Greek, and Albanian <i>keq</i> (“bad”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artful, using evil practices, with bad art</li></ol><p>From <i>κακο-</i> (kako-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “art, craft, practice”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evil, badness, wickedness</li><li>cowardice</li></ol><p><i>κακός</i> (kakós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with bad juice or flavor</li></ol><p>From <i>κακο-</i> (kako-, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>χῡλός</i> (khūlós, “juice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat ill, maltreat, afflict, distress, vex</li><li>to hurt, harm</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to suffer ill, be in poor condition</li><li>to befoul, spoil, ruin</li></ol><p>From <i>κακός</i> (kakós) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>badly</li><li>miserably</li><li>maliciously</li></ol><p><i>κακός</i> (kakós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reed, stalk</li><li>stubble (remaining stalks in a field)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>κάλαμος</i> (kálamos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱolh₂-m-</i>.</p><p><br/>Or from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of reed or cane</li></ol><p>From <i>κάλαμος</i> (kálamos, “reed”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoemaker's last</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾶλον</i> (kâlon, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long Egyptian garment, with tassels or fringe at the bottom</li></ol><p>From Egyptian, even though the exact word remains unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>turquoise, shimmering blue-green</li></ol><p>From <i>κάλαϊς</i> (kálaïs, “a precious blue-green stone such as turquoise”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call, summon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to invite</li><li>to invoke</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to summon, sue</li><li>to demand, require</li></ol><li>to call by name</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be called, one's name is</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kəlḗyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kl̥h₁-</i>, zero-grade of <i>&#42;kelh₁-</i> (“to call”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p><p>Cognates include Old English <i>hlōwan</i> and English <i>low</i> (verb) (but the similarity to <i>call</i> is coincidental); Latin <i>calō</i>, <i>clāmō</i>, <i>clārus</i>, <i>classis</i>, and <i>concilium</i>; Old Irish <i>cailech</i>; Old Armenian <i>աքաղաղ</i> (akʻałał).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caliga, a leathern boot worn by the Romans</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>caliga</i> + <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) coffer, caisson, panel in a ceiling</li><li>kind of dance</li></ol><p>From <i>κάλαθος</i> (kálathos, “basket narrow at the base”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian crowfoot grass (<i>Dactyloctenium aegyptium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κάλαμος</i> (kálamos, “reed”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγρωστις</i> (ágrōstis, “quackgrass”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catmint (<i>Nepeta cataria</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. The formal agreement with <i>&#42;καλαμο-μίνθη</i> (&#42;kalamo-mínthē) does not permit a conclusion. A Pre-Greek word is most probable, because of the suffix and the meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limed twig</li><li>case for a writing-reed, pencase</li><li>toothpick or toothbrush</li><li>quill used as splint for broken noses</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) reeds put in layers to strengthen buildings</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰ́λᾰμος</i> (kắlămos, “reed”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing with a reed or pen</li></ol><p>From <i>κάλαμος</i> (kálamos, “reed”) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reed-case</li></ol><p>From <i>κάλαμος</i> (kálamos, “reed”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) The calends, used in reckoning Roman dates: the 1st day of a month.</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>kalendae</i> (“calends; 1st day of a month”), from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;kalēō</i>, gerundive formation from Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;kelh₁-</i> (“to call, summon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shepherd's staff or crook, which was thrown so as to drive back the cattle</li></ol><p>Explained as an Aeolic compound "καλα-ϝροψ" by Schwyzer and Chantraine, but with unexplained second member. The comparison of the first member with Sanskrit <i>शल</i> (śala, “spear, staff”) or with <i>κλάω</i> (kláō, “to break”) must be forgotten. According to Beekes, it is a typical Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden dwelling, hut</li><li>barn, granary</li><li>bird's nest</li><li>shrine or grotto containing the image of a god</li></ol><p>Unknown. Sometimes connected to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i> (“to cover, conceal”), whence Indo-European words for rooms and dwellings (compare Latin <i>cella</i> (“small room, cell; storeroom, granary; hut”), Sanskrit <i>शाला</i> (śā́lā, “house, mansion, hall”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hallō</i> (“hall”) and Old Irish <i>cuile</i> (“storeroom, kitchen”)), but Beekes makes no mention of this and leaves the origin open. Etymological connection with <i>καλύπτω</i> (kalúptō, “to cover”) in particular is rejected.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accountant</li><li>maths teacher</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>calculātor</i>. Documented from the 2nd c. AD to the 4th.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tame ape</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps a euphemistic usage of the personal name <i>Κᾰλλῐ́ᾱς</i> (Kăllĭ́ās), which has been connected with <i>κᾰ́λλος</i> (kắllos, “beauty”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Καλλίας)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Callias</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰλλῐ-</i> (kăllĭ-, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>κᾰλλῐ́ᾱς</i> (kăllĭ́ās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having beautiful manes (<i>of horses</i>)</li><li>having beautiful fleeces (<i>of sheep</i>)</li><li>having beautiful hair (<i>of humans</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterwort</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>κᾰλλῐ-</i> (kăllĭ-, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>θρῐ́ξ</i> (thrĭ́x, “hair”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>πάγκρεᾰς</i> (pánkreăs)</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰλλι-</i> (kălli-, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>κρέᾰς</i> (kréăs, “flesh, meat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>κᾱ̆λῶς</i> (kā̆lôs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>callipygian, callipygous (with beautifully shaped buttocks)</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰ́λλος</i> (kắllos, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>πῡγή</i> (pūgḗ, “buttocks”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>κᾱ̆λός</i> (kā̆lós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write beautifully</li><li>to paint beautifully</li></ol><p>From <i>καλλιγράφος</i> (kalligráphos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō), equivalent to <i>καλλι-</i> (kalli-, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>γραφή</i> (graphḗ, “drawing, writing”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calligraphy, beautiful writing</li></ol><p>From <i>καλλι-</i> (kalli-, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>-γραφίᾱ</i> (-graphíā, “writing”) and/or <i>καλλιγράφος</i> (kalligráphos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) having beautiful women (<i>epithet of various countries</i>)</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>κᾰλλῐ-</i> (kăllĭ-, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>γῠνή</i> (gŭnḗ, “woman”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) having beautiful cheeks (<i>epithet of various women or goddesses</i>)</li></ol><p><i>κᾰ́λλος</i> (kắllos, “beauty”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰρήῐ̈ον</i> (părḗĭ̈on, “cheek”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creeping cinquefoil (<i>Potentilla reptans</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>καλλι-</i> (kalli-, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>πέταλον</i> (pétalon, “petal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) comparative degree of <i>κᾱ̆λῶς</i> (kā̆lôs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with beautiful name</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlantic stargazer (<i>Uranoscopus scaber</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>καλλι-</i> (kalli-, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνυμα</i> (ónuma, “name”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beautify</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to gloss over</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to pride oneself in a thing</li><li>to sweep clean</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>καλός</i> (kalós, “beautiful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good-natured, virtuous</li><li>(substantive,&#32;in neuter) virtue</li></ol><p>From <i>καλο-</i> (kalo-, “good”) + <i>-ήθης</i> (-ḗthēs, “of character”) from Ancient Greek <i>καλός</i> (kalós, “beautiful, good”), <i>ἦθος</i>&nbsp;n (êthos, “disposition, character”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gentlemanliness; nobility (the traits and character of a gentleman)</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) kalokagathia</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰλοκᾰ́γᾰθος</i> (kălokắgăthos, “gentleman”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>κᾰλὸς καὶ ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (kălòs kaì ăgăthós, “beautiful and good”), shortened as <i>κᾰλός κᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (kălós kăgăthós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>εὐετηρῐ́ᾱ</i> (euetērĭ́ā)</li></ol><p><i>κᾰλός</i> (kălós) +&lrm; <i>καιρός</i> (kairós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the name of an eye-salve</li></ol><p><i>κᾰλοκαιρῐ́ᾱ</i> (kălokairĭ́ā) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>denoting a kind of <i>ἐγχυματισμός</i> (enkhumatismós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden shoes, probably a hobble tied to a cow's legs to keep her still while milking</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾶλον</i> (kâlon, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>πέδῑλον</i> (pédīlon, “sandal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoemaker's last</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾱλόπους</i> (kālópous) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion), the former a compound of <i>κᾶλον</i> (kâlon, “wood”) and <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covering, enfolding, enveloping</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>venerable</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine,&#32;Christianity) monk</li></ol><p>From <i>καλός</i> (kalós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>γῆρας</i> (gêras, “old age”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>this word’s etyma and its Latin gloss both admit of a wide range of interpretations of its meaning — see <i>κᾰλός</i> (kălós), <i>καιρός</i> (kairós); <i>bonus</i>, <i>tempus</i></li></ol><p><i>κᾰλός</i> (kălós) +&lrm; <i>καιρός</i> (kairós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoemaker's last</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having beautiful feet</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾶλον</i> (kâlon, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”)</p><p>From <i>καλός</i> (kalós, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beautiful; lovely</li><li>useful; helpful; appropriate</li><li>good; right; moral; virtuous; noble</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>κᾰλϝός</i> (kălwós), probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kal-wo-s</i>, formed from <i>&#42;kal-</i> (“beautiful”).</p><p>Cognate to Sanskrit <i>कल्य</i> (kalya), Sanskrit <i>क॒ल्याण॑</i> (kalyā́ṇa), Albanian <i>kolmë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A hut, a cabin</li><li>A bridal bower</li><li>A sleeping tent on the roof of a house</li><li>A cover, a screen</li></ol><p>Due to the presence of variations, probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>veil, headdress</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>bride’s veil</li></ol><li>land given to queens as “veil-money” (money given to Oriental queens for their small expenses. See also <i>ζώνη</i> (zṓnē))</li><li>cover, lid</li><li>seed capsule</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰλῠ́πτω</i> (kălŭ́ptō, “to cover”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to cover; to conceal</li><li>to cover with dishonour, throw a cloud over</li></ol><li>to put over as a covering</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The etymology determination is complicated by the fact that the word is similar to Ancient Greek <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “to hide; cover”) in form and sense, thus indicating possible (bi-directional) analogical influence.</p><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i> (“to cover”) (whence Latin <i>cēlō</i> (“to hide”)); however, Beekes considers <i>κᾰλῠ́βη</i> (kălŭ́bē, “hut; cabin”) and <i>κᾰλῠ́φη</i> (kălŭ́phē, “submerged land”) cognates, and argues that neither the α-vocalism nor the <i>ύ</i> (ú) + different labials can be convincingly explained by Indo-European etymology, and that therefore the root is likely from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>well; rightly</li></ol><p>From <i>καλός</i> (kalós, “beautiful”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of reed or cane</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰ́μᾰξ</i> (kắmăx, “pole; shaft”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field (accusative singular)</li></ol><p>Akin to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀏𐀔</i> (ka-ma), see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything with an arched cover such as a covered carriage or boat, a vaulted chamber; vault, span</li></ol><p>Possibly an Old Iranian borrowing, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;kamarā-</i> (“something curved”), from <i>&#42;kamárati</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kmárati</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂em-</i> (“to bend, curve”).</p><p>Compare Avestan <i>𐬐𐬀𐬨𐬀𐬭𐬁</i> (kamarā, “girdle”),</p><p>Middle Persian <small>[Book Pahlavi needed]</small> (kml /⁠kamar⁠/, “waist; belt, girdle”), Manichaean Middle Persian <i>𐫞𐫖𐫡</i> (qmr /⁠kamar⁠/, “vault”), Persian کمر (kamar, “waist; belt, girdle; arch, cupola, dome, arched bridge”) and the Old Armenian loanword <i>կամար</i> (kamar, “girdle; arch, vault”). The Hesychian gloss <i>καμαρίς</i> (kamarís, “woman's ornament”) is also from Iranian.</p><p>Alternatively, in view of the primary meaning “vault”, Kroonen derives καμάρα instead from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éḱmō</i> (“heavenly vault; anvil; meteorite”), with the same kind of metathesis commonly postulated for Germanic and Balto-Slavic, i.e. <i>&#42;ḱh₂mr̥h₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(late&#32;Koine,&#32;Byzantine) shirt</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>camisia</i>, from Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hamiþi</i> (“shirt”), + <i>-ιον</i> (-ion). Probably a doublet of <i>καμάσιον</i> (kamásion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of an unknown kind of fish</li></ol><p>Given the fish name <i>ἠλακατήν</i> (ēlakatḗn, “kind of tunny”), which derives from <i>ἠλακάτη</i> (ēlakátē, “distaff, spindle”), one would posit a basic word <i>*κάμασος</i>, with suffixal <i>-ασος</i> like in <i>πέτασος</i> (pétasos, “broad-brimmed hat”). It has been connected with Balto-Slavic words for the "sheatfish", such as Lithuanian <i>šāmas</i>, Latvian <i>sams</i> and Russian <i>сом</i> (som). Further connected with <i>κάμαξ</i> (kámax, “pole, bar”) by Solmsen. Probably a loan from the European substrate. Furnée connects it with <i>κάβαισος</i> (kábaisos, “glutton, gourmand”) and <i>καμασός</i> (kamasós, “gulf, pit”), but without evidence.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giraffe</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰ́μηλος</i> (kắmēlos, “camel”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰ́ρδᾰλῐς</i> (pắrdălĭs, “leopard”); from the fact that a giraffe has a long neck like a camel and is spotted like a leopard.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white horehound (<i>Marrubium vulgare</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κάμηλος</i> (kámēlos, “camel”) +&lrm; <i>πόδιον</i> (pódion, “little foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close or shut the eyes</li><li>to fall asleep, to doze</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>μύω</i> (múō, “to close, to shut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winding, such as of a river</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>flexion, bending</li><li>a curved part, curve</li></ol><li>the turning post in a racecourse</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the goal of a speech</li></ol><li>(<i>music</i>) a turn, a sudden change</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) rounding off of a period</li></ol><li>bend or flexure of a limb or of the skull</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kampā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂emp-</i>, from <i>&#42;kh₂em-</i> (“to bend, curve”). By surface analysis, <i>κάμπτω</i> (kámptō, “to bend”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend; angle</li><li>(<i>athletics</i>) turning point in the diaulos; goal</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) censer (container for burning incense)</li></ol><p>From <i>κάμπτω</i> (kámptō, “to bend; to turn”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent, curved</li></ol><p>From <i>κάμπτω</i> (kámptō, “to bend, curve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, bent</li></ol><p>From the Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂emp-</i>, from <i>&#42;kh₂em-</i> (“bend”, “curve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a block-figure</li></ol><p>From <i>κάναβος</i> (kánabos, “block-figure”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ring, clash, clang</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keh₂n-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>canō</i>, Old Irish <i>canim</i>, Persian خواندن (xândan), and Old English <i>henn</i> (English <i>hen</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise, any sharp sound</li><li>ring of clang of metal</li><li>gnashing of teeth</li><li>sound of flutes</li></ol><p>Explained as an expressive formation like <i>στοναχή</i> (stonakhḗ), derived from a verb "to sing" seen in Latin <i>canō</i> (“to sing”) and Old Irish <i>canaid</i>. Furnée compares <i>κόναβος</i> (kónabos, “din, clash”) and concludes that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A candace, any one of the ancient Nubian warrior queens.</li></ol><p>From Meroitic <i>𐦲𐦷𐦲𐦡</i> (kdke /⁠kandakə⁠/).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κανδάκη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Candace, a name derived from the Nubian candaces</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>κανδάκη</i> (kandákē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil lamp</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>candēla</i> (“a light made of wax or tallow”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothes-press</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Old Persian (*kandu-dāna)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panniers at the sides of a packsaddle</li><li>large baskets for carrying grapes</li><li>wooden lattice shelter at a ship's stern</li></ol><p>The relation with <i>κανθίαι</i> (kanthíai, “creels”), <i>κάνθων</i> (kánthōn, “pack-ass”) and <i>κανθίς</i> (kanthís, “dung of an ass”) is unclear, due to their specialized technical meanings. Formally, the word can be compared with <i>κειμήλια</i> (keimḗlia) and <i>γαμήλιος</i> (gamḗlios). According to Deroy, it is a Mediterranean substrate word. Furnée connects it with <i>ἀνθήλιον</i> (anthḗlion, “packsaddle”), with alternation k/-zero and assumes Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>σπυρίδες</i> (spurídes, “large baskets, creels”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a derivation without <i>-λ-</i> from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>κανθήλια</i> (kanthḗlia, “panniers”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Spanish fly (<i>Lytta vesicatoria</i>)</li><li>kind of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>κάνθαρος</i> (kántharos, “dung beetle”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corner of the eye</li><li>eyelids</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) eye</li><li>rim or tire of a wheel</li><li>chimney</li><li>pot, pan</li></ol><p>Uncertain, sometimes suggested to be related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kǫtъ</i> (“corner”), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;kantos</i> (“corner, rim (of a chariot wheel)”) through a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂ndʰ-</i>, but Beekes doesn't find this convincing, as -νθ- is often indicative of Pre-Greek, which he considers the most likely origin; moreover, Slavic and Celtic presuppose *<i>-t-</i>.</p><p>The primary sense is "corner of the eye", recorded in Aristotle; in the 6th century (John Philoponus) also glossed as "eyelids";</p><p>the secondary meaning "rim (of a wheel)" only arises in the Roman period (<i>Edict of Diocletian</i> AD 301), influenced by Latin (originally Gaulish) <i>cantus</i>.</p><p>The senses "chimney" and "pot, pan" are only known from Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>oncology</i>) swelling; tumour</li></ol><p>Beekes derives the term from Pre-Greek on the basis of the α / ο variation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hempen, like hemp</li></ol><p>From <i>κάνναβις</i> (kánnabis, “hemp”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure, regulate</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to conjugate (a verb); to parse</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰνον-</i> (kănon-), the oblique stem of <i>κᾰνών</i> (kănṓn, “rule, standard”), +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to a rule, regular</li><li>connected with assessment</li><li>belonging to an astronomical table</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized) clergyman</li></ol><p>From <i>κανών</i> (kanṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>κάνεον</i> (káneon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rod, pole, bar</li><li>rods used in shields</li><li>type of rod used in weaving</li><li>measuring rod, carpenter's rule, plumb line, level</li><li>(<i>music</i>) monochord</li><li>something to judge by, rule, norm, general principle</li><li>model, paradigm</li><li>tax assessment, tax, tariff</li><li>(<i>music</i>) qanun</li></ol><p>Likely Pre-Greek, and generally connected with <i>κάννα</i> (kánna, “reed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thessalian form of <i>ἀπήνη</i> (apḗnē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persian measure, corresponding to <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>48</sub></small> of the <i>ἀρτάβη</i> (artábē)</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;kapiča-</i> (literally “holder, something containing”), whence also <i>καπίθη</i> (kapíthē); see more cognates on Arabic قَفِيز (qafīz).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persian unit of measure containing two choenixes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;kapiča-</i> (literally “holder, something containing”), whence also <i>καπέτις</i> (kapétis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoke</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kapnós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kwep-</i> (“to smoke, boil, move violently”), which appears to be a European substrate word; see also Lithuanian <i>kūpėti</i> (“to boil over”), Old Church Slavonic <i>кꙑпѣти</i> (kypěti, “to boil”), Sanskrit <i>कुप्यति</i> (kupyati, “become agitated, bubbles up”), Latin <i>cupiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dried by the air</li><li>drying, parching</li><li>brittle, crisp, friable</li><li>crackly (of sound)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kwep-</i> (“to smoke, boil, move violently”), the same root of <i>καπνός</i> (kapnós, “smoke”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantine form of <i>καράβιον</i> (karábion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>κάραβος</i> (kárabos, “a small boat”)</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) ship</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾱ́ρᾰβος</i> (kā́răbos, “a small boat”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrimp, prawn</li></ol><p>The variations may point to a Pre-Greek word. Frisk derives it as a short popular form of <i>κάραβος</i> (kárabos, “kind of crayfish, beetle”). The exact derivation is unknown, but the element /kaɾ/ connects many names for shrimps, crayfish, scorpions and crabs, about which see Persian خرچنگ (xarčang, “crab”) and Old Armenian <i>կարիճ</i> (karič, “scorpion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cardamom (<i>Elettaria cardamomum</i>)</li></ol><p>Blend of <i>κάρδαμον</i> (kárdamon) +&lrm; <i>ἄμωμον</i> (ámōmon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heart (as the source of emotion, love, etc.)</li><li>mind</li><li>stomach</li><li>any hollow vessel</li><li>center or inner part:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>pith (of wood)</li><li>depth (of the sea)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kərdíyā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱr̥díyeh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱérd</i> (“heart”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>हृद॑य</i> (hṛ́daya), Latin <i>cor</i>, Old Armenian <i>սիրտ</i> (sirt), Old Church Slavonic <i>срьдьце</i> (srĭdĭce), Old English <i>heorte</i> (whence English <i>heart</i>).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Καρδία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cardia (an ancient city in Thrace)</li></ol><p>(This etymology is missing or incomplete. Please add to it, or discuss it at the Etymology scriptorium. )</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concerning the heart</li><li>suffering from a heart disease (of persons)</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰρδῐ́ᾱ</i> (kărdĭ́ā, “heart”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab</li><li>Cancer, the zodiac sign</li><li>sore, ulcer, cancer</li><li>items which resemble a crab's pincers</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>pair of pincers, a type of torture instrument</li><li>temple bones</li><li>type of shoe</li><li>type of bandage</li><li>pair of compasses</li><li>distillery</li></ol></ol><p>Uncertain. Could be from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;karkros</i>, with cognates including Latin <i>cancer</i>, and possibly Sanskrit <i>कर्कट</i> (karkaṭa), although relationship with the latter is less certain. There are elements which point to a non-Indo-European origin for the Greek and its comparanda, perhaps Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>oncology</i>) eating sore or ulcer; cancer</li></ol><p>From <i>καρκινόω</i> (karkinóō, “to suffer from cancer”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma), from <i>καρκίνος</i> (karkínos, “crab”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the earth</i>) to quake</li></ol><p>A <i>yod</i>-present with intensive reduplication, of onomatopoeic origin. Sanskrit has an athematic reduplicated <i>चर्कृति</i> (carkṛti, “praise, glory”). Not related to <i>κῆρυξ</i> (kêrux, “herald”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carruca, a four-wheeled carriage</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>carrūca</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of feet</i>) swift, quick</li><li>(<i>of jaws</i>) eager, ravenous</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>The etymology is unknown. Schrader connected it with <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “root of the hand”) as "handy", with further connection to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hwerban</i> (“to turn”). Others recognize the base form in <i>κάλπη</i> (kálpē, “trot”). A first syllable </i>καρπ-<i> can hardly be Indo-European. Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of κάρπασος</li></ol><p><i>κάρπᾰσος</i> (kárpăsos) [from the Hebrew <i>כַּרְפַּס</i> (karpás, “a type of expensive fabric made of cotton”)] + <i>-ινος</i> (-inos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of valerian (<i>Valeriana dioscoridis</i>)</li></ol><p>The etymology is unknown. Furnée compares <i>κέρπαθος</i> (kérpathos, “kind of incense”). According to Beekes, the word can hardly be of Indo-European origin; thus, probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mimic dance in arms of the Thessalians</li></ol><p>The description of the dance does not allow a comparison with <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “fruit”). The variations point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>swiftly, quickly</li></ol><p>From <i>καρπάλιμος</i> (karpálimos, “swift, quick”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watcher of fruit</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰρπός</i> (kărpós, “fruit”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit; grain; produce; harvest</li><li>the product of something:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>children (fruit of the body)</li><li>poetry (fruit of the mind)</li><li>profit</li></ol><li>wrist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kərpós</i> or <i>&#42;karpós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kerp-</i> (“pluck, harvest”), though debated whether via inheritance or borrowing from a different Indo-European branch due to the problematic <i>α</i> (a) in the Greek form. Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harbistaz</i> (“harvest, autumn”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čerpti</i> (“to scoop, draw”) and Latin <i>carpō</i> (“to pick”), as well as Ancient Greek <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to cut off”). More at Etymology 2 below.</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀏𐀡</i> (ka-po).</p><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷerp-</i> (“to turn”), and related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwerbaną</i> (“to turn”) (whence English <i>wharf</i>). Other theories take the origin to be the same as that of Etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sufficient to fill a wagon</li></ol><p>From <i>κάρρον</i> (kárrhon, “cart, wagon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian rhinoceros (<i>Rhinoceros unicornis</i>)</li></ol><p>From Middle Persian (klg /⁠karg⁠/, “rhinoceros; horn”) + <small>[script needed]</small> (-dʾn' /⁠-dān⁠/, “bearer, holder”), ultimately from Sanskrit <i>खड्ग</i> (khaḍga, “rhinoceros; literally sword bearer”). Compare Persian کرگدن (“rhino”), Arabic كَرْكَدَّن (karkaddan, “rhinoceros”), Classical Syriac <i>ܟܪܟܕܢܐ</i> (karkǝḏānā, “rhinoceros”), Sanskrit <i>खड्गधेनु</i> (khaḍgadhenu, “a female rhinoceros”) and Hindi <i>खाँग</i> (khāṅg).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, staunch, mighty</li><li>steadfast</li><li>obstinate</li><li>violent</li></ol><p>From <i>κάρτος</i> (kártos, “strength, might”) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong-handed</li></ol><p>From <i>καρτερός</i> (karterós, “strong, mighty”) +&lrm; <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shorn smooth (of clothes)</li><li>chopped, sliced (of leek)</li></ol><p>Zero grade verbal adjective of <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to shear, shave”), itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>dress with rich sauce</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of speech) season a story with nice words or phrases</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) embroil</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰρῡ́κη</i> (kărū́kē, “rich sauce made of blood and spices”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, verb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clove tree (<i>Syzygium aromaticum</i>)</li><li>its dried flower bud</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>κάρυον</i> (káruon, “nut”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”), but due to the semantic domain and similar terms of this meaning in other languages which did not borrow from Greek, this is evidently a folk-etymological adaptation of a Semitic term from an Indian language; see Arabic قَرَنْفُل (qaranful) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of dry straw</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰ́ρφος</i> (kắrphos, “dry straw”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinking-cup narrower in the middle than the top and bottom</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) masthead of a ship, through which the halyards worked</li><li>triangular instrument used in carpentry</li><li>cage or chamber in a torsion-engine</li><li>crane for unloading ships</li></ol><p>Foreign word of unknown origin. Maybe from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shark</li></ol><p>From <i>κάρχαρος</i> (kárkharos, “sharp, jagged”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱς</i> (-íās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) carotid arteries</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰρόω</i> (kăróō, “to plunge into deep sleep or torpor”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐ́δες</i> (-tĭ́des, plural nominal suffix), from the fact that the carotid artery supplies blood to the brain, and interruption of this flow causes loss of consciousness.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stupefying, soporific</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰ́ρωσῐς</i> (kắrōsĭs, “heaviness in the head, drowsiness”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrot</li></ol><p>From <i>καρώ</i> (karṓ, “caraway”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunge into deep sleep or torpor, to stun or stupefy</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰ́ρος</i> (kắros, “heavy sleep, torpor”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sauce composed by blood and spices</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Frisk suggested Lydian origin, but the variation points to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark red</li></ol><p>From <i>καρῡ́κη</i> (karū́kē, “kind of sauce made with blood”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>κηρύσσω</i> (kērússō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kāruťťō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of nuts</li></ol><p>From <i>κάρυον</i> (káruon, “nut, kernel”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>caraway (<i>Carum carvi</i>)</li></ol><p>Frisk derives it from <i>κάρη</i> (kárē, “head”); however, the form in <i>-ώ</i> seems Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cassia (<i>Cinnamomum iners</i>)</li></ol><p>From a Semitic source equalling Hebrew <i>קְצִיעָה</i> (qəṣīʿā), Aramaic <i>קְצִיעֲתָא</i> (qəṣīʿătā), from <i>קְצַע</i> (qṣaʿ, “to cut off”), referencing the manner the tree produce is reaped.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) brother</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) sister</li></ol><p>From <i>κάσις</i> (kásis, “brother, sister”) (see <i>καί</i> (kaí)) + the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strumpet</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a Pre-Greek word and clearly related to similar words like <i>κασαύρα</i> (kasaúra) and <i>κασωρίς</i> (kasōrís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sister</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>κασίγνητος</i> (kasígnētos) (which can also be feminine itself).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) little finger, pinkie</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tin</li></ol><p>An Elamite origin has been suggested but, according to Beekes, the group <i>σσ&#47;ττ</i> (ss&#47;tt) is typically Pre-Greek, so the word would have come from Greece or Western Anatolia, like <i>μόλυβδος</i> (mólubdos, “lead”), but even if so, the word might still go back to Mesopotamia, representing the name of the Kassites, who settled near Elam and major sources of tin. Compare also the etymology of Arabic مَرْقَشِيتَا (marqašītā, “marcasite”). Related to Sanskrit <i>कस्तीर</i> (kastīra, “tin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of tin</li></ol><p>From <i>κασσίτερος</i> (kassíteros, “tin”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tinker, tinsmith</li></ol><p>From <i>κασσίτερος</i> (kassíteros, “tin”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stitch, sew together like a shoemaker</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to stitch up a plot, trap, ensnare</li></ol><p>The presence of the related word <i>καττύς</i> (kattús, “piece of leather”) suggests a Pre-Greek etymology, in view of the variation <i>-σσ-/-ττ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κᾰ́στᾰνᾰ</i> (kắstănă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castoreum</li><li>beaver</li><li>the fur of the beaver</li></ol><p><i>κάστωρ</i> (kástōr) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strumpet</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek root of synonymous words <i>κασαλβάς</i> (kasalbás) and <i>κασαύρα</i> (kasaúra). There is no reason to connect these words with <i>κασᾶς</i> (kasâs, “horse-cloth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κᾰτᾰ́</i> (kătắ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>pre-eminent, par excellence</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>against, opposing</li><li>into</li><li>against</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>downwards</li><li>along, through, in</li><li>towards</li><li>during</li><li>for, for the purpose of</li><li>according to, in conformity with</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kətá</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱm̥-th₂</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> (“beside, with”).</p><p>Cognate with Hittite <i>𒅗𒀜𒋫</i> (katta, “down from, at, with, under”), Cuneiform Luwian (zanta, “down”), Tocharian B <i>kätk-</i> (“to lower”) and perhaps Old Welsh <i>cant</i>, Modern Welsh <i>gan</i> (“with”).</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of separation. The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional and the PIE direct object.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>descent</li></ol><p>From <i>καταβαίνω</i> (katabaínō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is talkative, chatty, loquacious</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative ,chatty ,loquacious</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “fully”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶττα</i> (glôtta, “tongue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break in pieces, shatter</li><li>to break up, weaken, enervate</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἄγνυμι</i> (ágnumi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead down, bring down, draw</li><li>to reduce</li><li>to escort</li><li>to derive</li><li>to lower</li><li>to bring back, bring home, recall, restore</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to return</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to tear down</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down, lie outstretched, recline</li><li>to lie hidden, lurk</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ending, termination</li><li>cadence</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλήγω</i> (katalḗgō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(act of) seizing</li><li>(act of) assaulting</li><li>(act of) taking possession, occupation</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>καταλαμβάνω</i> (katalambánō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enrolment</li><li>register, catalogue</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλέγω</i> (katalégō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lodging, billet, provision of quarters</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) guestchamber</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) ruin</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλύω</i> (katalúō, “stay over night”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, “a suffix forming neuter nouns denoting the effect or result of an action a particular instance of an action, or the object of an action”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dissolution, disbanding</li><li>lodging, quarters, accommodation</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλύω</i> (katalúō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis); also analyzable as <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>λύσις</i> (lúsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grater</li></ol><p>Possibly from Sicel <i>katane</i> (“grater”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱh₃tós</i> (“sharpened”), from <i>&#42;ḱeh₃-</i> (“to sharpen”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κατάνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Catana, Catania (a city on the east coast of Sicily)</li></ol><p>Of Sicel origin, from <i>katane</i> (“grater”). See <i>κατάνη</i> (katánē, “grater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) plaster, poultice</li></ol><p>From <i>καταπλάσσω</i> (kataplássō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma); also analyzable as <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>πλάσμα</i> (plásma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>downflowing</li><li>full of streams</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) running from the head; catarrh</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰρρέω</i> (kătărrhéō, “to flow down”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival and nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement, appointment, institution, ordinance</li><li>introduction, presentation</li><li>standing firm, a settled condition, fixedness</li><li>state, condition</li><li>constitution, system</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) ritual, ceremonial protocol</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) master of ceremonies</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) platform</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>καθίστημι</i> (kathístēmi) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis); also analyzable as <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>στάσις</i> (stásis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered in, roofed</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, against) +&lrm; <i>στέγος</i> (stégos, “roof”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a state, condition (e.g., weather conditions)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>bodily or mental condition</li><li>weather, time, season(al conditions)</li><li>demeanor, behavior</li><li>political condition, constitution</li><li>state of the case, state of affairs</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) position of the heavens</li><li>physical constitution</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κᾰθῐ́στημῐ</i> (kăthĭ́stēmĭ, “to set down, settle”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deck (floor-like covering on a ship)</li></ol><p><i>καταστρώννῡμι</i> (katastrṓnnūmi, “to spread out, cover”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma), the former from <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>στρώννῡμι</i> (strṓnnūmi, “to spread out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ordering, arranging; classification</li><li>ordinance, regulation</li><li>(<i>in medicine</i>) reduction (of dislocations)</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰτάσσω</i> (kătătássō, “to draw up in order, arrange”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>κατάφρακτος</i> (katáphraktos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered, shut up, enclosed (especially in armour or mail)</li></ol><p>From <i>καταφράσσειν</i> (kataphrássein, “to protect”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, “-ed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) analogical use of a word</li></ol><p>From <i>καταχράομαι</i> (katakhráomai) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a smearing on, application of a substance; an act of rubbing or coating</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a topical application used to draw, irritate, or treat</li><li>(<i>literary</i>) smearing or coating (e.g. of the body or face)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down, over”) +&lrm; <i>χρῖσις</i> (khrîsis, “smearing, anointing”), from <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) salve, ointment applied externally, especially to delicate or mobile parts of the body</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down, thoroughly”) +&lrm; <i>χρῖσμα</i> (khrîsma, “ointment, anointing”), from <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) fit or intended for use as ointments; suitable for application by rubbing on</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down, thoroughly”) +&lrm; <i>χρῑστός</i> (khrīstós, “rubbed; anointed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something that is poured or showered down:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a sauce</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) dried fruits and nuts showered on a bride on her wedding day, or on a new slave upon their arrival</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>καταχέω</i> (katakhéō, “I pour over”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, suffix forming neuter nouns).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watering can</li><li>portable showerbath</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>χύτλον</i> (khútlon, “water for washing, bath”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat up, devour</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἔδω</i> (édō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>κᾰτενᾰντῐ́ον</i> (kătenăntĭ́on)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to slay, kill</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἔπεφνον</i> (épephnon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come down, descend</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold down</li><li>to restrain</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to pause, make a stop</li><li>to detain</li><li>to seize, confiscate</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accuser</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “against”) + the root of <i>ᾰ̓γορεύω</i> (ăgoreúō, “to speak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instruction, especially oral</li><li>communication</li></ol><p>From <i>κατηχέω</i> (katēkhéō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>back</li><li>downwards</li><li>against</li><li>fully, completely</li></ol><p>See <i>κᾰτᾰ́</i> (kătắ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come down, swoop down</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to put into port, put in</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>αἴρω</i> (aírō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw down, cast down</li><li>to overthrow</li><li>to slay</li><li>to drop</li><li>to lay down, set down</li><li>to lay a foundation</li><li>to bring down, carry down</li><li>to pay</li><li>to sow</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step down, to go or come down</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to dismount from a chariot or a horse</li><li>to go down from the inland parts to the sea, especially from central Asia</li><li>to come to land, get safely ashore</li><li>to go down into the arena, to fight, wrestle, race</li><li>(<i>of an orator</i>) to come down from the tribune</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;of things</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to come to in a course of speaking</li><li>to come to the same point, agree</li><li>to come down, fall</li><li>to condescend</li></ol><li>to go one's way, advance</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make to go down, bring down</li><li>to force to come down</li><li>to drive away</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “back, downwards”) +&lrm; <i>βῐβᾰ́ζω</i> (bĭbắzō, “to cause to mount, put on”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry down, cry out against [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bawl down, outcry</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>βοάω</i> (boáō) and <i>καταβοή</i> (kataboḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outcry [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘against’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>βοή</i> (boḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>building, structure, foundation</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰβάλλω</i> (kătăbállō, “to set down”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ridiculous, absurd</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰγελᾰ́ω</i> (kătăgelắō, “I mock, jeer”), from <i>κᾰ́τᾰ-</i> (kắtă-, “against”) + <i>γελᾰ́ω</i> (gelắō, “I laugh at”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abide; to dwell</li><li>to busy oneself about; to be concerned with a thing</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “fully”) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to come into being”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mocker</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰγελᾰ́ω</i> (kătăgelắō, “to laugh or jeer at, mock”) +&lrm; <i>-στης</i> (-stēs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow old</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “fully, completely”) +&lrm; <i>γηρᾱ́σκω</i> (gērā́skō, “to grow old”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch away, lacerate</li><li>to engrave, inscribe</li><li>to paint over</li><li>to fill with writing</li><li>to write down, register, record</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawing, delineation, depiction</li><li>delineation in profile, in bas-relief</li><li>engraving of an inscription</li><li>list, register, roll</li><li>conveyance of land or houses</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰγρᾰ́φω</i> (kătăgrắphō, “to engrave, inscribe”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind on or to, bind fast, bind up</li><li>to put in bonds, imprison</li><li>to convict and condemn of a crime</li><li>to tie down, stop, check</li><li>to lack, need, want</li><li>to come after, be behind</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind, tie”).</p><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to lack, need, want”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give judgment or sentence against (a person, etc.)</li><li>to condemn</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to have judgment given in one's favor</li><li>to declare by express judgment</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be bound by a law</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>δικάζω</i> (dikázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pursue closely</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>διώκω</i> (diṓkō, “persecute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go down into, enter</li><li>to cause to sink</li><li>to set (<i>of the sun</i>)</li><li>to put on</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>δύω</i> (dúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A deposit of money</li></ol><p>A short form of <i>παρακαταθήκη</i> (parakatathḗkē, “deposit”). By surface analysis, <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mortal</li></ol><p><i>καταθνῄσκω</i> (katathnēískō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to die</li><li>(<i>in perfect</i>) to be dead</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>θνῄσκω</i> (thnēískō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>down-going, descending</li></ol><p>From <i>καταβαίνω</i> (katabaínō, “to go down, to descend”), from <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a storm descending from above, a squall, a hurricane</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a “gust” of passion</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) battle</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτα-</i> (kăta-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>αἰγῐ́ς</i> (aigĭ́s, “storm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storm descending from above</li><li>sensual desires</li></ol><p>From <i>καταιγίζω</i> (kataigízō, “I rush down like a storm”), from <i>καταιγίς</i> (kataigís, “a storm from above”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to maltreat, disfigure, spoil, despoil, mar</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>αἰκίζω</i> (aikízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call down, summon, invite</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off, break short</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>κλάω</i> (kláō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down</li><li>to set (<i>of the sun</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>κλῑ́νω</i> (klī́nō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood, inundation</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) deluge (e.g. a political deluge)</li></ol><p>From <i>κατακλύζω</i> (kataklúzō, “to wash off, wash away, deluge, inundate”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlewH-</i> (“clean”)) + <i>-μός</i> (-mós), equivalent to <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>κλῠσμός</i> (klŭsmós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash off, wash away, deluge, inundate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(transitive,&#32;figurative) to overwhelm or overflow</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wash down, wash away</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fill full of water</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to clean out (a bath)</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>κλύζω</i> (klúzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put (<i>someone</i>) to bed, put (<i>someone</i>) to sleep</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to lie down to sleep</li><li>(<i>passive voice&#32;with active meaning</i>) to have sex (<i>with someone</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>κοιμάω</i> (koimáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give as a sentence against</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative of person</i>) to condemn</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be judged or deemed</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō, “to judge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide; to conceal</li><li>to use concealment</li><li>to conceal one's true nature</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to put away</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “intensifying prefix”) +&lrm; <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, slay</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick out, to choose out of, to levy/enlist/enrol</li><li>to tell or count up, to recount, to reckon up (i.e. to go over in detail, e.g. a pedigree)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to repeat</li></ol><li>to lay down</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “denoting motion downwards”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to choose, to say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, grasp, hold</li><li>to grasp with the mind: comprehend</li><li>to catch, overtake</li><li>to find, detect</li><li>to occur, happen to, befall (often of events, especially negative events: death, disaster, defeat, etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave behind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to leave as an inheritance</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to leave in a certain state</li></ol><li>to forsake, abandon</li><li>to leave remaining</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to leave alone</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κατά</i> (katá) + <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “to leave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in conversation or prose</li><li>in detail, longwindedly</li></ol><p>From <i>o</i>-grade of the root of <i>καταλέγω</i> (katalégō, “to tell in detail”) +&lrm; <i>-δην</i> (-dēn, deverbal adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must be put down</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλύω</i> (katalúō, “to destroy”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, verbal adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arbitrator</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλύω</i> (katalúō, “put an end to something”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, “a suffix forming masculine agent nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>κατάλυμα</i> (katáluma, “lodging, billet, provision of quarters”)</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλύω</i> (katalúō, “stay somewhere for the night”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion, “a suffix added to verb stems to form adjectives pertaining to the stem”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) destroyer, resolver, abolisher</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλύω</i> (katalúō, “destroy, solve”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, agent noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to dissolve or put an end to</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλύω</i> (katalúō, “dissolve, destroy”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, adjective-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be dissolved, done away with</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτάλῠσῐς</i> (kătálŭsĭs, “dissolution”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos, “a suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lodger</li><li>stranger</li><li>arbitrator</li></ol><p>From <i>καταλύω</i> (katalúō, “loosen, destroy; stay the night”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, noun-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loosen, unbind</li><li>to unyoke, unharness</li><li>to take down something hung or someone hanged</li><li>to destroy</li><li>to abolish, do away with</li><li>to deprive someone of their authority</li><li>to put an end to</li><li>to stay somewhere for one night, seek shelter</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, intensifying prefix) +&lrm; <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “loosen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smile at, despise</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>μειδᾰ́ω</i> (meidắō, “smile”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close or shut the eyes</li><li>to fall asleep, to doze</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>μύω</i> (múō, “to close, to shut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means of constraint, spell</li><li>yellow serradella (<i>Ornithopus compressus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἀνάγκη</i> (anánkē, “constraint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;control verb) to force</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτ- (κᾰτᾰ-)</i> (kăt- (kătă-), “fully”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νᾰγκᾰ́ζω</i> (ănănkắzō, “force”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nod</li><li>to beckon</li><li>to grant, promise</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-) +&lrm; <i>νεύω</i> (neúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perceive, observe, watch</li><li>to learn</li><li>to take to heart, consider</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>νοέω</i> (noéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catapult</li><li>an instrument of torture</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰ́λλω</i> (pắllō, “to hurl”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Literary form of <i>κᾰτᾰπᾰ́λτης</i> (kătăpắltēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall down</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>πῑ́πτω</i> (pī́ptō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fuck away; to squander (money) with pederasty</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>παιδεραστέω</i> (paiderastéō, “to engage in pederasty”), from <i>παιδεραστίᾱ</i> (paiderastíā, “pederasty”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a catapult</li></ol><p>From <i>καταπέλτης</i> (katapéltēs, “catapult”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stone, stone to death</li><li>to throw from a rock</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>πέτρᾱ</i> (pétrā, “stone”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail from sea to land, come ashore</li><li>to sail down stream</li><li>to sail back</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “back, against”) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “sail, float”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike down</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to amaze, astound, terrify</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be panic-stricken, to panic</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>striking, astonishing</li><li>terrible</li></ol><p>From rare <i>κᾰτᾰ́πληκτος</i> (kătắplēktos, “astonishing”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós), itself from <i>κᾰτᾰπλήσσω</i> (kătăplḗssō, “to strike down, amaze”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exhaust by war, reduce, attempt to subdue</li><li>to make war against, carry on warfare</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “against, downwards”) +&lrm; <i>πολεμέω</i> (poleméō, “make war”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spell for achieving success</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fitted for accomplishing</li></ol><p>From <i>καταπράσσω</i> (kataprássō) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fitted for accomplishing</li></ol><p>From <i>καταπράσσω</i> (kataprássō) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to betray utterly, leave in the lurch</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>προδίδωμι</i> (prodídōmi, “to betray”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crouch, cower in fear</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to cower beneath</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>πτήσσω</i> (ptḗssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lecherous; lewd; inclined toward unnatural lust</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a debauchee (lecherous and lewd man)</li><li>(<i>Attic</i>) used for an obscene gesture</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “downwards”) +&lrm; <i>πῡγή</i> (pūgḗ, “buttocks”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κᾰταρρᾱ́κτης</i> (kătarrhā́ktēs, “waterfall”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave unemployed or idle</li><li>to make of no effect, to abolish, cease</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἀργέω</i> (argéō, “to do nothing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterfall</li><li>a kind of portcullis</li><li>a kind of seabird, perhaps a gull or osprey</li><li>(<i>used adjectivally</i>) rushing downward</li></ol><p>From <i>καταρρᾱ́σσω</i> (katarrhā́ssō) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs), the former from <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>ῥᾱ́σσω</i> (rhā́ssō, “to strike, dash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pat, stroke, caress</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ῥέζω</i> (rhézō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow down</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to stream, rush down (<i>of men</i>)</li><li>to fall, drop off (<i>of fruit, leaves, etc.</i>)</li><li>to fall in ruins</li><li>to burrow</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to fall into</li></ol><li>to run down, drip with</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “downwards, down”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow, run, stream, gush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defluxion, flowing down</li></ol><p>From <i>καταρρέω</i> (katarrhéō, “to flow down”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an encamping, taking up one's quarters</li><li>(<i>of birds</i>) resting place, nest</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτᾰσκηνόω</i> (kătăskēnóō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equip, furnish with</li><li>to construct, build</li><li>to fabricate, trump up</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-, strengthening prefix) +&lrm; <i>σκευάζω</i> (skeuázō, “equip”), from <i>σκευή</i> (skeuḗ, “equipment”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparation, construction, fitting out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>unpacking</li><li>training</li></ol><li>permanent or fixed assets, fixtures, plant</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>but also</i>) any furniture or fittings</li></ol><li>the state, condition, or constitution of a thing</li><li>a device, a trick</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) constructive reasoning</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>artistic treatment</li><li>manipulation</li><li>elaboration</li><li>correct style</li><li>technical resources</li></ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) construction</li><li>(<i>technical term</i>) a system of gymnastic exercise</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-) +&lrm; <i>σκευή</i> (skeuḗ)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fitted for providing, bringing about</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) constructive, positive</li><li>systematic</li></ol><p>From <i>κατασκευάζω</i> (kataskeuázō, “to equip, furnish”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pitch one's camp, to camp</li><li>to take up one's quarters, encamp</li><li>to rest, settle</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-) +&lrm; <i>σκηνή</i> (skēnḗ) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw down, pull down</li><li>to quaff down, swallow down</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>σπάω</i> (spáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn down, bring down, subdue</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-) +&lrm; <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overturning</li><li>subjugation, reduction</li><li>return of vibrating string to axial position</li><li>end, close, conclusion</li><li>ruin, undoing</li></ol><p>From <i>καταστρέφω</i> (katastréphō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put down, set down, lay down</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) to cast down to Tartarus</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>Τάρταρος</i> (Tártaros, “Tartarus”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, “cause someone to be”, factitive verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) run down, hasten down</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of seamen or passengers by sea</i>) run to land, disembark in haste</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) run down, inveigh against</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>overrun, ravage, lay waste</li><li>(<i>construed with the genitive</i>) run over</li><li>pursue</li><li>hurry</li><li>(<i>of a bandage</i>) slip down</li></ol></ol><p><i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-) +&lrm; <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clearly seen, in sight, in the open</li><li>manifest, evident</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affirmative, positive</li></ol><p>From <i>κατάφημι</i> (katáphēmi) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee for refuge or protection</li><li>to have recourse to, fall back upon</li><li>to appeal to</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>φεύγω</i> (pheúgō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look down on, think slightly of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to regard slightly, despise</li><li>to be disdainful, deal contemptuously</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to think contemptuously that..., to presume</li></ol><li>(with accusative,&#32;only in&#32;Ionic&#32;writers) to fix one's thoughts upon, aim at</li><li>to come to one's senses</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “downwards”) +&lrm; <i>φρονέω</i> (phronéō, “to think, to be arrogant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contemptuous, disdainful</li></ol><p>From <i>καταφρονητής</i> (kataphronētḗs, “despiser”) (from <i>καταφρονέω</i> (kataphronéō, “to look down upon, despise”)) + <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch in a theft</li><li>to catch in the act, detect, discover</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>φωράω</i> (phōráō, “to search for a thief”), from <i>φώρ</i> (phṓr) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour down, on, in</li><li>to shower upon</li><li>to throw down, cast down,</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to let fall</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “downwards”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make full use of</li><li>to misuse, abuse</li></ol><p>From <i>κατά</i> (katá, “under, down”) +&lrm; <i>χρᾰ́ομαι</i> (khrắomai, “to use, make use of, take part in, manage, administer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anoint, smear, or coat thoroughly or over a surface; to apply a substance by rubbing on</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to anoint or smear oneself</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be anointed, smeared, or coated</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “down, thoroughly”) +&lrm; <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear; anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leather helmet or skullcap</li></ol><p>Formally reminiscent of <i>ᾰ̓́ντῠξ</i> (ắntŭx). A Semitic connection has been proposed by Lewy, but is unconvincing according to Frisk. Ultimately, of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive into, shut in</li><li>to press hard, reduce to straits</li><li>to hem in, keep down</li><li>to hinder, prevent</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἔργω</i> (érgō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>over against, opposite</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἐνᾰντῐ́ος</i> (enăntĭ́os, “opposite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>above, overhead</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) prefect, chief, commander</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἐπάνω</i> (epánō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press down, depress</li><li>to press much, press hard, drive on, urge on, impel</li><li>to hasten, make haste</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to effect by labor, to achieve, accomplish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to earn or gain by labor</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to achieve one's object, to be successful</li><li>(<i>with accusative of person</i>) to make an end of, finish, kill</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to overpower, subdue, conquer</li><li>to prevail upon</li><li>(<i>with duplicate accusative</i>) to do something to one</li></ol></ol><li>to work up for use</li><li>to work at, practice</li><li>to level</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἐργάζομαι</i> (ergázomai, “to work, labor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) hold back, hinder, detain</li></ol><p><i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>ἐρῡ́κω</i> (erū́kō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>draw, haul down</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτ(ᾰ)-</i> (kăt(ă)-, “downwards”) +&lrm; <i>ἐρῠ́ω</i> (erŭ́ō, “draw, drag, pull”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or keep straight</li><li>to guide, direct</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make straight towards</li><li>to prosper, succeed</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτ-</i> (kăt-) +&lrm; <i>εὐθῡ́νω</i> (euthū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak against, especially before judges, to accuse, to denounce publicly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to state or bring as a charge against a person, accuse of it</li><li>(<i>with accusative of object</i>) to allege in accusation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be accused</li></ol><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to be an accuser, appear as prosecutor</li></ol><li>to signify, indicate, prove</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>followed by a relative pronoun</i>) to tell plainly, declare, assert</li></ol><li>(<i>in logic</i>) to predicate of a person or thing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to affirm</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτήγορος</i> (kătḗgoros) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō), or from <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγορεύω</i> (agoreúō, “to speak in assembly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charge, accusation</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) predication, category</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτηγορέω</i> (kătēgoréō, “I accuse, speak against”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an accusation</li><li>a predicate</li></ol><p>From <i>κατηγορέω</i> (katēgoréō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to resound</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to sound amiss</li></ol><li>to teach by word of mouth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to teach, instruct</li><li>(<i>in Christian writers</i>) to instruct in the elements of religion, catechize</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἠχέω</i> (ēkhéō, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) to instruct in the elements of religion, catechize</li></ol><p>From <i>κατήχησις</i> (katḗkhēsis) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instructor, teacher</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτηχέω</i> (kătēkhéō, “to teach”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for instruction</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτηχητής</i> (kătēkhētḗs, “instructor”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> genitive] to overpower; to prevail against</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>): to be worsted</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative] to strengthen; to encourage</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτ(ᾰ)-</i> (“fully”) +&lrm; <i>ἰσχύω</i> (“be strong, powerful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a home</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “intensifying prefix”) +&lrm; <i>οἴκημα</i> (oíkēma, “home, dwelling”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of settling in a place</li><li>dwelling, abode</li><li>an inhabited district</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “to inhabit, settle”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, “a suffix added to form abstract nouns, or nouns of action, result or process”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>household goods</li></ol><p>From <i>κάτοικος</i> (kátoikos, “inhabitant”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the household</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικίς</i> (katoikís) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a settlement, colony</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικίζω</i> (katoikízō, “establish a colony”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, “A suffix added to verb stems to form abstract nouns or nouns of action, result or proces”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a household</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “dwell, inhabit”) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás, “a noun forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an annual festival to commemorate the founding of a settlement</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικίζω</i> (katoikízō, “to found a settlement”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, “suffix for abstract nouns.”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell in, to settle in, to colonise</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to settle, dwell</li></ol><li>to administer, govern</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of cities) to lie, be situated</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) resident, inhabitant</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “dwell, inhabit”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habitation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>farm, village</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) dwelling-place, domicile</li></ol><li>settlement, colony</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>and especially</i>) military colonies (in Egypt)</li><li>= the Latin <i>colōnia</i></li></ol><li>body of residents in a foreign city</li></ol><p><i>κᾰ́τοικος</i> (kắtoikos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>domestic</li><li>stay-at-home</li></ol><p>From <i>κάτοικος</i> (kátoikos, “inhabitant, colonist”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδῐος</i> (-ídĭos, “related to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle, establish a colony</li></ol><p>From <i>κάτοικος</i> (kátoikos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō) and/or <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκίζω</i> (oikízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a household</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “inhabit, dwell”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, feminine suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be made very friendly, be very closely united</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “intensifying prefix”) +&lrm; <i>οἰκειόω</i> (oikeióō, “to make one's own”) +&lrm; <i>-ομαι</i> (-omai, “a suffix forming passive verbs”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settling in a place,</li><li>dwelling, abode</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living in or about a house</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “inhabit”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριος</i> (-tḗrios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dweller, inhabitant</li><li>a colonist</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “to inhabit, colonize”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “A suffix appended to verbs to form agent nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>assigned to κάτοικοι</li></ol><p><i>κᾰ́τοικος</i> (kắtoikos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planting with inhabitants, foundation of a state, colonization, settlement</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικίζω</i> (katoikízō, “settle, found a colony”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós, “a suffix forming abstract nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founder of a city</li></ol><p>From <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “inhabit, dwell, colonize”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “Suffix appended to verbs to form agent nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build upon or in a place</li><li>to use, squander in building</li><li>to shut up in a house</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be built up, blocked up</li></ol><p>Form <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “intensifying prefix”) +&lrm; <i>οἰκοδομέω</i> (oikodoméō, “build”), equivalent to <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκοδόμος</i> (oikodómos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manage well, to be well-managed</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “intensifying prefix”) +&lrm; <i>οἰκονομέω</i> (oikonoméō, “manage as a steward”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ruin utterly</li></ol><p>From <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “intensifying prefix”) +&lrm; <i>οἰκοφθορέω</i> (oikophthoréō, “be ruined”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(attributive,&#32;"of figs",&#32;<b>κᾰτορχῑ́της <i>(</i>οἶνος<i>)</b></i>) fig-wine</li></ol><p>Alternate reading of <i>τροχῑ́της</i> (trokhī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrouding</li></ol><p>From <i>κατειλέω</i> (kateiléō, “to wrap up, enclose”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a holding fast, detention</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hindrance, delay</li><li>retention (of breath, memory, etc.)</li></ol><li>possession, mental grasp</li></ol><p><i>κᾰτέχω</i> (kătékhō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of leather</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek root of <i>κᾰσσῡ́ω</i> (kăssū́ō, “to stitch, to sew together”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one looking down, down-looker</li><li>the name of an African animal</li></ol><p>Substantive masculine present active participle of <i>κατωβλέπω</i> (katōblépō, “to look downwards”), from <i>κᾰ́τω</i> (kắtō, “downwards”) +&lrm; <i>βλέπω</i> (blépō, “look”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind; after</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) after; hereafter</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-) +&lrm; <i>όπῐν</i> (ópĭn, root of <i>ὀπῐ́σω</i> (opĭ́sō)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κᾰτόπῐσθεν</i> (kătópĭsthen)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κᾰτόπῐσθεν</i> (kătópĭsthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the rear; behind</li><li>in the future; afterward</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after; behind</li></ol><p>From <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-) +&lrm; <i>ὄπῐσθεν</i> (ópĭsthen).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κᾰτωβλέπων</i> (kătōblépōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the order of the letters, alphabetically</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) An upper story, loft, or attic of a house.</li><li>(<i>uncertain</i>) A ladder or staircase.</li><li>(uncommon,&#32;technical) A roof-beam or part of the roofing structure; by extension, a scaffold.</li></ol><p>Unknown; perhaps from the prefix <i>κᾰτᾰ-</i> (kătă-, down) + an unknown root, possibly related to <i>ἀλείφω</i> (aleíphō, “to smear, plaster”), referring to a plastered ceiling or loft. Also sometimes compared with the formation of <i>αἰγῐ́λῐψ</i> (aigĭ́lĭps, cliff-hanging) and <i>ἄλῐψ</i> (álĭps, rock). Beekes leaves the origin open, noting the meaning is uncertain, though typically associated with high structural elements like a "ladder," "roof-beam," or "upper story".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κᾰτωβλέπων</i> (kătōblépōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stem, stalk</li><li>shaft</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₂ulós</i> or <i>&#42;kawlós</i> (“tubular bone, pipe, stalk”), possibly earlier <i>&#42;kéh₂wl̥</i>, from a root <i>&#42;keh₂-</i> or <i>&#42;kewh₂-, &#42;keh₂w-</i> (“hollow”) (compare <i>κοῖλος</i> (koîlos)); however there are unresolved phonetic problems, such as the lack of aspiration in Sanskrit. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>कुल्या</i> (kulyā), Latvian <i>kauls</i>, and Old English <i>cāl</i> (> English <i>cole</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick cloak said to be of Persian or Babylonian make</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;gaunakah</i> (“hairy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>royal hat made of felt, used by Macedonians</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Furnée refers to <i>γαύσαπος</i> (gaúsapos, “woolen cloth”). Alternatively, from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;xauδiya</i> via Thracian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burnable, combustible</li><li>corrosive, caustic</li><li>of or by means of burning</li><li>feverish, inflammatory</li></ol><p>from <i>καυστός</i> (kaustós, “burnt”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burnt</li></ol><p>from <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “I burn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burnt</li><li>red-hot</li></ol><p>from <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “I burn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak loud, to boast, brag, vaunt</li></ol><p>Unknown, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰewH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camphor tree (<i>Cinnamomum camphora</i>)</li></ol><p>From Arabic كَافُور (kāfūr), via an Austronesian language such as Malay <i>kapur</i> from Sanskrit <i>कर्पूर</i> (karpūra). It is possible that the Sanskrit term was borrowed from Malay rather than vice versa.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in a bad habit of body</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;in a political sense) disaffected</li></ol><p><i>κᾰκός</i> (kăkós) +&lrm; <i>ἕξῐς</i> (héxĭs) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of a stalk or stick</li></ol><p>From <i>καυλός</i> (kaulós, “stem, stalk”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning heat, summer heat, heatwave</li><li>sirocco, a hot southern wind</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “to kindle, burn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>row of thrums in the loom, ravel</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, “a technical expression of unclear meaning and therefore etymologically difficult.” According to Petersson, it is related to Old Armenian <i>սարիք</i> (sarikʻ, “sling, rope”) as well as to <i>սարդ</i> (sard, “spider”), while Cimochowski connected it with Albanian <i>thur</i> (“twine, weave”). All these connections derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱer-</i> (“to plait, weave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cup</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Schrijver compares Old Irish <i>cuäch</i> (“cup”), suggesting a derivation from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;kaɸukos</i> (“cup”), itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kap-</i> (“to seize, hold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning, glow, heat (especially of the sun)</li><li>fever</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) holes cut by cautery</li><li>brand (mark of ownership made by burning)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) love</li></ol><p>From <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “burn”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>ἄν</i> (án)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: ΚΕ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>κῡ́ριε</i> (kū́rie)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split, cleave wood</li><li>(<i>of lightening</i>) to shiver</li><li>to pound, rub to pieces</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱes-</i> (“to cut up, to slaughter”); compare <i>κείω</i> (keíō, “to cleave”), Latin <i>castrō</i> (“to castrate”), Sanskrit <i>शस्त्र</i> (śastra, “sword, dagger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shear, shave, cut hair short</li><li>to ravage, waste</li><li>to destroy, consume, devour</li><li>to cut short, lessen, reduce</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;keřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kér-ye-</i>, <i>&#42;ye</i>-present from the root <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”). Cognate with Old English <i>scieran</i> (English <i>shear</i>), Old Armenian <i>քերեմ</i> (kʻerem, “I scratch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macedonian form of <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”).</li></ol><p>From Ancient Macedonian, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰebʰ-l-</i>; doublet of <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ). Krahe alternatively assumes Illyrian origin and Mayer compares this word with <i>Cibalae</i>, a town of Pannonia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firecrest (<i>Regulus ignicapilla</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κεβλή</i> (keblḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careful, diligent, trusty</li><li>cherished, dear</li><li>good, valued</li></ol><p>From <i>κήδομαι</i> (kḗdomai, “I take care, attend to”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juniper berry</li><li>common juniper (<i>Juniperus communis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κέδρος</i> (kédros, “juniper tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(elliptically for <i>κεδρῑ́της οἶνος</i>) wine flavoured with the oil of the Syrian cedar</li></ol><p>From <i>κεδρῐ́ᾱ</i> (kedrĭ́ā) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greek juniper (<i>Juniperus excelsa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κέδρος</i> (kédros, “cedar”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλάτη</i> (elátē, “fir”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Something stored or saved up, <i>particularly</i>:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>An heirloom, a family's property</li><li>Something precious, a treasure</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) A holy relic, a sacred relic</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai, “to lay; to be laid up, to be stored”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girth of a bedstead</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) swathe, bandage</li><li>grave-clothes</li><li>tapeworm</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>καῖρος</i> (kaîros, “string”) seems obvious. The presence of the alternative forms leads to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shouting, yelling</li></ol><p>From <i>κέκληγᾰ</i> (kéklēgă), the perfect tense of <i>κλάζω</i> (klázō), but with the ending of a present participle. The regular perfect participle <i>κεκληγώς</i> (keklēgṓs) is also attested.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>covertly, secretly</li></ol><p>Related to <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “I hide, conceal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hairnet for women, snood</li><li>part of the headstall of a bridle</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) reticulum, the second stomach of ruminants</li><li>pouch or belly of a hunting net</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, undoubtedly of Pre-Greek origin even if traditionally linked to <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “to hide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A cup, a jar</li></ol><p>Probably of Pre-Greek origin. It has also been connected with <i>κάλπις</i> (kálpis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vertical beams in the upright loom, between which the web hung down</li></ol><p>According to Frisk, originally a present participle of a verb <i>&#42;κελέω</i> (&#42;keléō), which would be a denominative of <i>&#42;κέλος</i> (&#42;kélos, “rising up”). Formally, the latter could be compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čelo</i> (“forehead”), and further connected with <i>κολώνη</i> (kolṓnē, “hill”), both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kel</i> (“to raise, lift”). It does not belong to <i>κελοί</i> (keloí, “woods”). Furnée assumes a pre-form <i>&#42;κελέϝοντες</i> (&#42;keléwontes) as a variant of <i>&#42;κελεμος</i> (&#42;kelemos), which stands behind <i>-κελεμνο-</i> seen in <i>ἀμφικέλεμνον</i> (amphikélemnon, “chair carried by two men”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>κέλης</i> (kélēs)</li></ol><p>From <i>κέλης</i> (kélēs) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound as flowing water</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to shout aloud</li><li>(<i>of babies, birds, etc.</i>) to utter a cry, cry aloud</li><li>(<i>of bells</i>) to ring, tinkle</li><li>to sing of, celebrate loudly</li></ol><p>From <i>κέλᾰδος</i> (kélădos, “loud noise”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black with clouds</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) black, very dark</li></ol><p>By haplology from expected <i>&#42;κελαινονεφής</i> (&#42;kelainonephḗs), a bahuvrīhi compound of <i>κελαινός</i> (kelainós, “black”) +&lrm; <i>νέφος</i> (néphos, “cloud”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black, dark, swarthy, murky</li></ol><p>Morphologically isolated and therefore difficult to assess. The ending is similar to <i>περκνός</i> (perknós, “dusky”) and <i>ἐρεμνός</i> (eremnós, “murky, black”), but the first member remains unclear. It has been compared with several words, like <i>κηλίς</i> (kēlís, “spot, stain”) and Latin <i>cālīgō</i> (“mist”). According to Beekes, it could be a Pre-Greek word with a palatalized nasal.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of running water</i>) to babble, gurgle</li><li>to gush out like water, run gushing</li><li>to pour with a gush or gurgling sound</li></ol><p>Unclear. Expressive sound-word in <i>-ύζω</i>, like <i>γογγύζω</i> (gongúzō, “to mutter, murmur”), <i>κλύζω</i> (klúzō, “to wash, dash over”), <i>ὀλολύζω</i> (ololúzō, “to cry aloud”) and <i>τονθορύζω</i> (tonthorúzō, “to mumble”). Probably related to <i>κέλωρ</i> (kélōr, “voice”), first from an unattested adjective <i>&#42;κελαρός</i> (&#42;kelarós), or from a by-form <i>&#42;κέλαρ</i> (&#42;kélar); otherwise it could be from <i>κέλαδος</i> (kélados, “din, clamour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>green woodpecker (<i>Picus viridis</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps it belongs to <i>κολάπτω</i> (koláptō, “to peck”), <i>κόλος</i> (kólos, “docked, hornless”), together with <i>κελοί</i> (keloí, “chopper, cutter”). Bechtel connected Lithuanian <i>kùlti</i> (“to thresh”), but according to Huber the alternative forms are a sign of foreign origin. It is probable that these variations are due to Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κελεός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Celeus</i></li></ol><p>From <i>κελεύω</i> (keleúō, “urge, order”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urge, bid, exhort</li><li>to command, order</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes notes that, if it derives from <i>κέλομαι</i> (kélomai, “to urge, exhort”), the <i>-ευ-</i> (-eu-) 'remains unexplained', and compares it to the <i>-ευ-</i> (-eu-) found in <i>κέλευθος</i> (kéleuthos, “road”) and <i>τελευτή</i> (teleutḗ, “end; tip”). However, it appears feasible that the suffix is simply <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, “verbal suffix”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. what does beekes mean here? isn't this just the suffix -εύω [for which we have an entry]</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantine form of <i>κελλάριον</i> (kellárion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) cellar, pantry, larder</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Latin <i>cellārium</i> (“storeroom, cellar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) garret</li></ol><p>From <i>κέλλα</i> (kélla, “cell”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a contest for boys and youths at Sparta</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variations suggest a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pricket, two-year-old deer</li></ol><p>A derivation in <i>-άς</i> (-ás), probably from an <i>o</i>-stem <i>&#42;κέμος</i> (&#42;kémos, “hornless”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱem(h₂)-</i> (“hornless”). Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hindiz</i> (“hind, doe”) and Lithuanian <i>šmùlas</i> (“without horn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Lyric) synonym of <i>ἄν</i> (án)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrion, carcass</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, this looks like a Pre-Greek word, but he does not add any other information.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow between ribs and hip, flank</li><li>hollow of the peritonaeum</li><li>any hollow; vacant space</li></ol><p>From <i>κενός</i> (kenós, “empty, hollow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cenotaph, empty tomb</li></ol><p>From <i>κενός</i> (kenós, “empty”) +&lrm; <i>τάφος</i> (táphos, “tomb”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prick, sting, goad</li><li>to stab, pierce, wound</li><li>to torture, torment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱent-</i> (“to sting”). Cognate with Old High German <i>handag</i> (“pointed”), Latvian <i>sīts</i> (“hunting spear”); compare also English <i>hent</i>, <i>hunt</i>, and possibly <i>hand</i>, as well as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hinþaną</i> (“to reach for, obtain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(with <b>μέγα</b>) Certain centauries</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Croatian star-thistle (<i>Centaurea salonitana</i>)</li><li><i>Centaurion majus</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>Synonym of <i>Rhaponticoides centaurium</i></li><li>Synonym of <i>Centaurium erythraea</i> subsp. <i>grandiflorum</i></li></ol><li>yellowwort (<i>Blackstonia perfoliata</i>, syn. <i>Chlora perfoliata</i>)</li></ol><li>(with <b>μικρόν</b>) common centaury, European centaury, feverfew (<i>Centaurium erythraea</i>, syn. <i>Centaurion minus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κένταυρος</i> (kéntauros, “centaur”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon). The association with centaurs probably stems from the myth that the medical properties of the herbs were discovered by Chiron the Centaur.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κενταύρειον</i> (kentaúreion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) A unit of weight equal to 100 librae (Roman pounds), corresponding to about 32.7&nbsp;kilograms or 72&nbsp;avoirdupois pounds.</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>centēnārium</i>, neuter of <i>centēnārius</i> (“containing a hundred”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher's broom (<i>Ruscus aculeatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κέντρον</i> (kéntron, “goad, spur”) +&lrm; <i>μυρσίνη</i> (mursínē, “myrtle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>physics</i>) vacuum</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty</li><li>vain, fruitless</li><li>exhausted, void, destitute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kenwós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱen-</i>. Cognates with Old Armenian <i>սին</i> (sin, “vain, trifling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty</li><li>vain, fruitless</li><li>exhausted, void, destitute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kenwós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱen-</i>. Cognates with Old Armenian <i>սին</i> (sin, “vain, trifling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longhorn beetle</li></ol><p>Probably also the same beetle as <i>κάραβος</i> (kárabos), also written <i>καράμβιος</i> (karámbios), <i>καράβιος</i> (karábios), <i>καραβίς</i> (karabís), and see also <i>κεράμβηλον</i> (kerámbēlon, “kind of beetle”) and <i>κηραφίς</i> (kēraphís, “kind of locust”).</p><p>The formation is known in <i>κόλυμβος</i> (kólumbos), <i>κόρυμβος</i> (kórumbos), <i>σήραμβος</i> (sḗrambos) and <i>βόμβυξ</i> (bómbux), <i>ὄρτυξ</i> (órtux).</p><p>First element quite possibly related to <i>κέρας</i> (kéras, “horn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of clay</li></ol><p>From <i>κέραμος</i> (kéramos, “clay”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix, mingle, blend</li><li>to temper, cool by mixing</li><li>to compound</li><li>(grammar,&#32;passive voice) to coalesce by crasis</li><li>to multiply into</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kírnāmi</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cherry</li><li>cherry tree</li></ol><p>From <i>κερᾰσός</i> (kerăsós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horned viper (<i>Cerastes cerastes</i>)</li><li>pest which destroys fig-trees</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρας</i> (kéras, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit of the carob</li></ol><p>The plural of <i>κερᾱ́τῐον</i> (kerā́tĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of horn</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρας</i> (kéras, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small horn</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>zoology</i>) antenna of the cerambycid or of the hermit crab</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) curved ends of the womb</li><li>(<i>music</i>) a musical instrument (perhaps a fife or clarionet)</li></ol><li>carat, <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>1728</sub></small> of a pound</li><li>a Byzantine coin of a certain value</li><li>alternative form of <i>κερατωνία</i> (keratōnía)</li><li>fruit of the carob</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρᾰς</i> (kérăs, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, <i>diminutive suffix</i>). The sense of “carob“ stems from a Semitic loan and later mingled with the “hornlet” word, compare Arabic قَرَظ (qaraẓ, “acacia”), Classical Syriac <i>ܩܸܪܲܛܵܐ</i> (qerraṭṭā, “acacia; carob”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antennae of the crayfish or of insect</li><li>anything projecting like a horn, hence:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>nautical</i>) yardarm</li><li>projecting beam of a crane</li><li>projecting parts of the huckle bone</li><li>branching stake of wood, used as a pale in palisades</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;astronomy) horns of the moon</li><li>(<i>in writing</i>) apex of a letter</li><li>leg of a pair of compasses</li><li>projecting spur of a mountain</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) supposed teat-like projections inside the womb</li></ol><li>bow made of horn</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρας</i> (kéras, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small tile</li></ol><p>From <i>κερᾰμῐ́ς</i> (kerămĭ́s, “roof-tile”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), from <i>κέρᾰμος</i> (kérămos, “pottery”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof-tile</li><li>earthen vessel</li><li>clay</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρᾰμος</i> (kérămos, “pottery”) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>potter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) guild of potters</li></ol><li>(figuratively,&#32;in the phrases&#32;κεραμέως πλοῦτος, κεραμεὺς ἄνθρωπος) something frail and uncertain</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρᾰμος</i> (kérămos, “pottery”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix for person concerned).</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀐𐀨𐀕𐀄</i> (ke-ra-me-u /⁠kerameus⁠/, “potter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for pottery, of a potter</li></ol><p>From <i>κέραμος</i> (kéramos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird cherry, <i>Prunus avium</i> (tree)</li></ol><p>A loanword of uncertain origin, either Anatolian or Pre-Greek based on the intervocalic -σ-.</p><p>Comparison with Akkadian <i>𒅗𒅈𒋗</i> (/⁠karšu⁠/, “a plant, likely a leek”) is rejected by Rosół and Blažek.</p><p>Blažek argues that the ultimate source of the word may well be Semitic, e. g. Akkadian <i>𒄑𒈢𒆳𒊏</i> (/⁠kamiššaru, kamaššaru, kameššaru⁠/, “pear-tree”) or Classical Syriac <i>ܟܘܡܬܪܐ</i> (kummaṯrā, “pear”), compare the Aramaic borrowing Arabic كُمَّثْرَى (kummaṯrā, “pear”), where it has to be known that <i>Prunus</i> and <i>Pyrus</i> species can be denoted by the same word, for example إِجَّاص (ʔijjāṣ, “pear; plum”) and Old Armenian <i>տանձ</i> (tanj, “pear”) with Classical Syriac <i>ܬܰܢ̈ܓܳܝܳܬܳܐ</i>&nbsp;f&nbsp;pl (tangāyāṯā, “mahaleb cherry”). He argues that assimilation <i>*-mš- &gt; *-š-</i> is attested in several NW Semitic languages of the 2nd millennium BC, and hypothesizes that metathesis <i>*k-(m)š-r- &gt; *k-r-(m)š-</i> might have happened either in an unidentified mediator language or a Semitic donor or (less probably so) in Greek itself. The change <i>*t &gt; š</i>, necessary for the change of Classical Syriac <i>ܟܘܡܬܪܐ</i> (kummaṯrā) to the Greek word, is to be found in Phoenician and early Aramaic.</p><p>Blažek also notes that the related cultural term seems to be widespread in Caucasian languages; compare (not to list over a dozen lemmata) Lak <i>ккурмуз</i> (k&#58;urmuz, “(wild) cherry plum”) and Dargwa <i>гимиргьи</i> (gimirhi, “quince”), reconstructed protoform Proto-Northeast Caucasian <i>&#42;ḵurmäśV &#47; &#42;ḵurmäʒ́V</i> (“some kind of fruit”), and possibly Proto-Northwest Caucasian <i>&#42;mǝrǝgʷǝźʷǝ</i> (“some sort of plum or peach”) with metathesis, attested in some Abkhaz compound words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κερατωνία</i> (keratōnía)</li></ol><p>Compare <i>κερᾱτωνῐ́ᾱ</i> (kerātōnĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κερατωνία</i> (keratōnía)</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρᾰς</i> (kérăs, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), potentially referring secondarily to the horn shape of the carob fruit, or just suffixing to a Semitic source attested in Arabic قَرَظ (qaraẓ, “acacia”), Classical Syriac <i>ܩܸܪܲܛܵܐ</i> (qerraṭṭā, “acacia; carob”). Compare <i>κερᾱτωνῐ́ᾱ</i> (kerātōnĭ́ā), <i>κερωνῐ́ᾱ</i> (kerōnĭ́ā), <i>κεράτιον</i> (kerátion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carob tree, <i>Ceratonia siliqua</i></li></ol><p>From <i>κερᾱτών</i> (kerātṓn, “made of horns”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>κέρᾰς</i> (kérăs, “horn”), potentially referring to the horn shape of the carob fruit. Compare <i>κερωνῐ́ᾱ</i> (kerōnĭ́ā), <i>κερᾱτέᾱ</i> (kerātéā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wielding a thunderbolt (epithet of Zeus)</li></ol><p>From <i>κεραυνός</i> (keraunós, “thunderbolt”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “hold”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thunderbolt, lightning strike</li></ol><p>Thematic transformation of an <i>r/n</i> stem, from a verb "to shatter" that was replaced by the related <i>κεραΐζω</i> (keraḯzō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“to smash, shatter”). For the formation, compare the unrelated <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō, “to set in motion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike with a thunderbolt</li><li>to condemn</li></ol><p>From <i>κεραυνός</i> (keraunós) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horned</li><li>made of horn</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kerawós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>cervus</i> and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;korva</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a striking with thunder</li></ol><p>From <i>κεραυνόω</i> (keraunóō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Celtic wheat-beer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cervēs(i)a</i>. Attested 2nd–4th c. AD.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>more profitable, more advantageous</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρδος</i> (kérdos, “profit, advantage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gain as profit</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρδος</i> (kérdos) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the fox</li><li>the weasel</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρδος</i> (kérdos, “profit”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>weaving</i>) a relatively small, handheld device, used to strike the weft upwards after each pass, for better density and uniformity; weaver's pin beater; sley</li><li>any taper rod, of wood or ivory</li><li>measuring rod, gnomon</li><li>rod for stirring liquids</li><li>iron dowel</li><li>(carpentry,&#32;architecture) any object or device forming an angle</li><li>an oblique part of a truss</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) wedge shaped division of the seats in the theatre</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) tibia</li><li>aspen (<i>Populus tremula</i>)</li><li>Judas tree (<i>Cercis siliquastrum</i>)</li><li>white bryony</li></ol><p>Unclear; related to Ancient Greek <i>κρέκω</i> (krékō, “weave; strike, strum”) + <i>-ις</i> (-is). According to Mallory and Adams, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krek-</i>, with English <i>reel</i> as a cognate; But possibly a diminutive form of <i>κέρκος</i> (kérkos, “tail”). According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common hill myna (<i>Gracula religiosa</i>)</li></ol><p>Α suffix <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn) is found in other animal names, like <i>ἀκανθίων</i> (akanthíōn, “hedgehog”) and <i>πορφυρίων</i> (porphuríōn, “swamphen”). Frisk derives it from <i>κέρκος</i> (kérkos, “tail”), because this bird wags its tail.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of longtailed ape</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρκος</i> (kérkos, “tail”) +&lrm; <i>πίθηκος</i> (píthēkos, “ape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian coin</li></ol><p>Compare <i>κέρσα</i> (kérsa, “Asian coin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to taunt, sneer at</li></ol><p>Prellwitz assumed a univerbation of <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to shear, shave”) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut, hew”), a type of formation which remains hypothetical. Furnée refers to <i>καρτομιστής</i> (kartomistḗs, “mocker”): the varying vocalism would point to Pre-Greek origin. Another connection has been made to Latin <i>cārinō</i> (“to revile, blame, insult”) and <i>κάρνη</i> (kárnē, “penalty, punishment”), by Schrijver to <i>σκερβόλλω</i> (skerbóllō, “to insult, mock, slender”), which in turn leads to <i>σκέραφος</i> (skéraphos, “abuse, reproach”), alleging Pre-Greek substrate. Furnée adds a comparison with Hittite (kartimii̯e, “to be angry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κερατωνία</i> (keratōnía)</li></ol><p>From <i>κέρᾰς</i> (kérăs, “horn”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), potentially referring to the horn shape of the carob fruit. Compare <i>κερᾱτωνῐ́ᾱ</i> (kerātōnĭ́ā), <i>κερᾱτέᾱ</i> (kerātéā), <i>κερᾱτῐ́ᾱ</i> (kerātĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>engine which discharges bolts</li></ol><p>From <i>κέστρος</i> (késtros, “bolt discharged from engines”) +&lrm; <i>σφενδόνη</i> (sphendónē, “sling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>officer in charge of the bolts discharged from engines</li></ol><p>From <i>κέστρος</i> (késtros, “bolt discharged from engines”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stitched, embroidered</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girdle, belt</li></ol><p>From <i>κεντέω</i> (kentéō, “to prick, goad, spur on, stab”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>main point, gist</li><li>sum, total (of money)</li><li>chapter (of a book)</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive form of <i>κεφᾰ́λαιος</i> (kephắlaios, “primary”), from <i>κεφᾰλή</i> (kephălḗ, “head”).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>capitulum</i> &lt; <i>caput</i>, Bulgarian <i>глава</i> (glava), Russian <i>глава́</i> (glavá), Serbo-Croatian <i>поглавље</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>main, chief, principal, primary</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) capital, principal</li><li>sum, completion</li></ol><p>From <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little head</li></ol><p>From <i>κεφᾰλή</i> (kephălḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head</li><li>a person's life (often in the sense of being in danger, similar to the English idiom "head is on the line").</li><li>the topmost part</li><li>the most important part</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a provincial governor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰepʰəlā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰebʰ-l̥</i> (“head”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-éh₂</i> (“nominal suffix”). Compare <i>κεβλή</i> (keblḗ). False cognate of Sanskrit <i>कपाल</i> (kapāla).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decapitate, behead</li></ol><p><i>κεφᾰλοτόμος</i> (kephălotómos, “decapitation”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flathead mullet (<i>Mugil cephalus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forming a hiatus</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰ́σκω</i> (khắskō, perfect stem: κεχην-) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to cover, hide</li><li>to conceal, keep hidden</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kéutʰō</i>. A close relative to this verb is Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hūdijaną</i> (“to hide, conceal”). A further comparandum is Old Armenian <i>սուզանեմ</i> (suzanem, “to plunge, dive; to hide”), but this would presuppose an initial palatal <i>*ḱ-</i>, which does not fit with the other words adduced, like Sanskrit <i>कुहू</i> (kuhū, “new moon”) and <i>कुहर</i> (kuhara, “hole, cavity”). Beside the words from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewdʰ-</i> (“to cover, wrap, encase”), several form with root final <i>*t</i> are found, like <i>κύτος</i> (kútos, “hollow; vessel”) and <i>σκῦτος</i> (skûtos, “skin, hide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>ἐκεῖθεν</i> (ekeîthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>ἐκεῖθι</i> (ekeîthi, “there”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie, lie outstretched</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to lie asleep, repose, lie idle, lie still</li><li>to lie sick or wounded, lie in misery</li><li>to lie dead</li><li>to lie neglected, uncared for, unburied</li><li>(<i>of wrestlers</i>) to have a fall</li></ol><li>(<i>of places</i>) to lie, be situated</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) to lie in a place</li></ol><li>to be laid up, be in store (of goods, property, etc.)</li><li>to be set up, proposed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of laws</i>) to be laid down</li><li>(<i>of arguments</i>) to be laid down</li><li>(<i>of names</i>) to be given</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be in the power of</li><li>to be in such a manner</li><li>to be</li><li>to settle down, deposit a sediment</li><li>(grammar,&#32;of words and phrases) to be found, to occur</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱéytor</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱey-</i> (“to lie down”). Cognate with Latin <i>cūnae</i>, <i>cīvis</i>, Old Armenian <i>սէր</i> (sēr), Hittite <i>𒆠𒀉𒋫𒊑</i>, <i>𒆠𒀉𒁕𒊑</i> (kittari), Sanskrit <i>शेते</i> (śéte).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐκεῖνος</i> (ekeînos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κεῖσε</i> (keîse)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἐκεῖσε</i> (ekeîse, “there, thither”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κεῖτο</i> (keîto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innermost; depth; hiding</li></ol><p>From <i>κεύθω</i> (keúthō, “conceal, cover, hide”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>connection by marriage, alliance</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) care for the dead, funeral, mourning</li></ol><p>From <i>κηδεύω</i> (kēdeúō, “to care for”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p><p>The sense of "care for the dead" > "funeral" developed in the Hellenistic Koine era.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take charge of, tend</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to attend to a corpse, bury</li></ol><li>to contract a marriage</li></ol><p>From <i>κῆδος</i> (kêdos, “care for others”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dicebox</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, maybe of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything gushing or bubbling forth</li><li>ooze, of fat or juices drawn forth by fire</li><li>oak gall and the dye made therefrom</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>βαλβίς</i> (balbís), <i>κηλίς</i> (kēlís), <i>κνημίς</i> (knēmís) and <i>κρηπίς</i> (krēpís). Traditionally compared with Lithuanian <i>šókti</i> (“to jump, dance”), as if from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱeh₂k-</i>. The gloss <i>καγκύλας</i> (kankúlas, “oak-galls”) is compared with Lithuanian <i>šankùs</i> (“nimble”), but this cannot be connected here if the root was <i>&#42;ḱeh₂k-</i>. At any rate the connection with the Lithuanian word must be abandoned, and the word is Pre-Greek, because of the prenasalization in <i>καγκύλας</i> (kankúlas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to charm, bewitch, enchant, beguile, fascinate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱeh₁l-</i>, <i>&#42;keh₁l-</i> (“to beguile, deceive”). Cognate with Latin <i>calvor</i> (“to deceive, plot against”), Old English <i>hōl</i> (“calumny, slander”), and Welsh <i>celwydd</i> (“lie, falsehood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stain, spot, defilement</li><li>stain, blemish</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) naevus</li></ol><p>The formation is like <i>κνημίς</i> (knēmís, “greave”). A comparable meaning can be found in Latin <i>calidus</i> (“with a bless on the head”). To the same semantic sphere also belongs Lithuanian <i>kalýbas</i> (“white-necked”) and Old Irish <i>caile</i> (“stain”). Beekes proposes a common derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keh₂l-</i> (“white spot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muzzle put on a led horse</li><li>nosebag for horses</li><li>cloth used by bakers to cover the nose and the mouth</li><li>weely, a wicker vessel</li><li>funnel shaped top of the voting urn</li><li>female ornament</li></ol><p>Unexplained. The connection with Old Armenian <i>քամեմ</i> (kʻamem, “to press (out)”) is formally possible but it is incompatible with the probable basic meaning of the Greek word, wicker. The same holds with other supposed cognates like Lithuanian <i>kāmanos</i> (“harness”), Russian <i>ком</i> (kom, “clump”) and Middle High German <i>hemmen</i> (“to restrain, hamper”). Furnée suggests, instead, a derivation from a Pre-Greek source common to <i>χάβος</i> (khábos) and <i>χαμόν</i> (khamón).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garden door, back door</li><li>pink stonecrop (<i>Sedum cepaea</i>)</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>κηπαῖος</i> (kēpaîos, “of or from a garden”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gardener</li></ol><p>From <i>κῆπος</i> (kêpos, “garden”) and +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeycomb</li><li>wax tablet</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) wax; candle</li></ol><p>From <i>κηρός</i> (kērós, “beeswax, honeycomb”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wax candle, wax light, wax taper</li></ol><p>From <i>κηρός</i> (kērós) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of locust</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin and related to <i>κάραβος</i> (kárabos, “prickly crustacean”), <i>κεράμβηλον</i> (kerámbēlon, “kind of beetle”) and <i>κεράμβυξ</i> (kerámbux, “cerambyx”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arranged like a honeycomb</li></ol><p>From <i>κηρίον</i> (kēríon, “honeycomb”, diminutive of <i>κηρός</i> (kērós)) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) with all one's heart; heartily</li></ol><p>From <i>κῆρ</i> (kêr) +&lrm; <i>-θῐ</i> (-thĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beeswax; honeycomb</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wax tapers</li></ol><p>A foreign loan from a substrate language, cognate with Latin <i>cēra</i> and Albanian <i>qiri</i>, and possibly also with Lithuanian <i>korys</i>, Latvian <i>kāre</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>death; doom</li><li>goddess or demon of death</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kā́r</i>. No suitable Indo-European root exists, therefore the source is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herald's wand, caduceus</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to a herald</li></ol><p>From <i>κῆρυξ</i> (kêrux) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to a herald</li></ol><p>From <i>κῆρυξ</i> (kêrux) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a herald</li></ol><p>From <i>κῆρυξ</i> (kêrux, “herald”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A fabulous sea bird, identified with the <i>ἀλκυών</i> (alkuṓn)</li></ol><p>No etymology. Beekes states that the suffix "-υλο-" is typical of Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a herald or auctioneer</li><li>to make a proclamation as herald</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to summon by herald</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to proclaim, announce</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to command someone publicly to do something (<i>with infinitive&#32;or&#32;dative of thing</i>)</li><li>(<i>New Testament</i>) to preach the gospel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kāruťťō</i>. By surface analysis, <i>κῆρυξ</i> (kêrux) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>κηρῠ́σσω</i> (kērŭ́ssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an apian drone (male bee)</li><li>(<i>used disparagingly of people</i>) a lazy vagabond</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a literary plagiarist</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a worn-out, decrepit person</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from Pre-Greek. Previously related to <i>κωφός</i> (kōphós, “dumb, deaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an apian drone (male bee)</li><li>(<i>used disparagingly of people</i>) a lazy vagabond</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a literary plagiarist</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a worn-out, decrepit person</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from Pre-Greek. Previously related to <i>κωφός</i> (kōphós, “dumb, deaf”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κηφῆνες)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cephenes, an old name of the Persians</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>φέγγος</i> (phéngos, “splendour, lustre”), <i>αὐγή</i> (augḗ, “radiance, beam”), <i>φῶς</i> (phôs, “light”), <i>πάχνη</i> (pákhnē, “rime, hoarfrost”), <i>ὀμίχλη</i> (omíkhlē, “mist, haze”).</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden box, chest, coffer</li></ol><p>From an original <i>&#42;τῖβωτός</i> (&#42;tîbōtós), from Aramaic <i>תֵּיבוֹתָא</i> (tēḇōṯā), from a merger of Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./b25b17ce.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="DbA" alt="glyph" height="92"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">t&#58;pr</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (ḏbꜣt, “sarcophagus, coffin”) and <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./5d87241c.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="d" alt="glyph" height="30"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">t&#58;xt</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (dbt, “chest, box”), as also synonymous Arabic تَابُوت (tābūt) and Hebrew <i>תֵּבָה</i> (tēḇā́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) The seed vessel of the Egyptian water-lily</li><li>A cup used liturgically</li></ol><p>Probably from Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box for drawing lots</li><li>voting urn</li></ol><p>From <i>κῑβωτός</i> (kībōtós, “wooden chest”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) lyre</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>lyre-playing</li></ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the ribs of the horse</li><li>ivy</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, acquired from Pre-Greek. Said Pre-Greek term may be ultimately derived from or at least related to Proto-Hurro-Urartian <i>&#42;kinnar</i> (“lyre, harp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lyre player</li></ol><p>From <i>κῐθᾰ́ρᾱ</i> (kĭthắrā) +&lrm; <i>-ῐστής</i> (-ĭstḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little owl (<i>Athene noctua</i>)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic word, like <i>κικκαβαῦ</i> (kikkabaû, “sound of the screech owl”). However, according to Beekes, the variations point also to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey</li></ol><p>For the root vocalism, it is possible to compare <i>πιλνός</i> (pilnós, “grey”) beside <i>πελιός</i> (peliós, “livid”). Formally, this word is reminiscent of <i>κελαινός</i> (kelainós, “dark”), but ultimately there is no convincing etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artichoke (<i>Cynara scolymus</i>)</li></ol><p>The ι-υ variation has been referred to suggest a Pre-Greek origin, although it is only attested in the later Hellenistic period, and it could be borrowed from an Aramaic byform equivalent to the one which Persian کنگر (kangar, “cardoon”) is borrowed from, this meaning attested in Northeastern Neo-Aramaic <i>ܩܰܩܢܳܐ</i> (qaqna), <i>ܩܰܠܩܳܐ</i> (qalqa, “cardoon”), identical to Classical Syriac <i>ܩܠܩܐ</i> (qalqā, qelqā, “taro”), and therefore <i>ܩܠܩܐܣ</i> (qōlqās), <i>ܩܠܘܩܣ</i> (qōlqōs), <i>ܩܠܘܩܘܣ</i> (qōlōqōs, “taro”) and <i>κολοκασία</i> (kolokasía, “sacred lotus; taro”), implying a Western Aramaic emphatic state shaped /qɪˈŋarɑː/, since the <i>ܪܒܨܐ</i> (rəḇāṣā) diacritic for /ɛ/ in the best-attested Aramaic lect Syriac regularly corresponds to ḥiriq for /ɪ/ in other lects.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set in motion, move, remove</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to inflect</li><li>to meddle</li><li>to change, innovate</li><li>to begin, cause</li><li>to urge on, stir on</li><li>to arouse, exasperate, anger, taunt, abuse</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be moved, to stir, to move</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keyh₂-</i> (“to start to move”). Compare <i>κίω</i> (kíō, “to go”) and <i>κίνυμαι</i> (kínumai, “to go, move”), Latin <i>cieo</i> (“to move”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daring, adventuresome person</li></ol><p>From <i>κινδῡνέυω</i> (kindūnéuō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be daring, run a risk, venture</li><li>to run the risk [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘of doing’</small>]</li><li>Used to express possibility or probability</li></ol><p>From <i>κῐ́νδῡνος</i> (kĭ́ndūnos, “danger”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>a risk, venture</li></ol><p>From <i>κινδῡνεύω</i> (kindūneúō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for putting in motion</li><li>moveable, mobile</li></ol><p>From <i>κῑ́νησῐς</i> (kī́nēsĭs, “motion”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós), from <i>κῑνέω</i> (kīnéō, “I move”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cinnabar, bisulphuret of mercury</li><li>vermilion</li></ol><p>Probably an eastern loanword according to Beekes. Compare Arabic زِنْجَفْر (zinjafr), related to Persian شنگرف (šangarf) from Old Persian <i>𐎿𐎡𐎣𐎲𐎽𐎢𐏁</i> (s-i-k-b-ru-u-š /⁠si<sup>n</sup>kabruš⁠/, “carnelian”), of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chinese cinnamon (<i>Cinnamomum cassia</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Herodotus, from Phoenician; compare Hebrew <i>קִנָּמוֹן</i> (kinamón, “cinnamon”). The ending was modelled on that of <i>ἄμωμον</i> (ámōmon, “black cardamom”), or due to folk etymology, on that of <i>ἄμωμος</i> (ámōmos, “blameless”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like cinnabar, vermilion</li></ol><p>From <i>κιννάβαρι</i> (kinnábari, “cinnabar”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prepared with or from cinnamon</li></ol><p>From <i>κιννάμωμον</i> (kinnámōmon, “cinnamon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wailing, plaintive, lamenting</li></ol><p>The meaning was already unclear in antiquity. The parallelism between this word and <i>μινυρός</i> (minurós, “complaining, whining”) is clear. Further etymology is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinnor or harp of David</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>כִּנּוֹר</i> (kinnōr) and adapted to <i>κινυρός</i> (kinurós, “wailing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>venomous beast, especially a snake</li></ol><p>Ending like in <i>ἑρπετόν</i> (herpetón) and <i>δακετόν</i> (daketón). Probably related to <i>κνώψ</i> (knṓps, “venomous animal”). The vocalism points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>highway robber, pirate, thief</li></ol><p>The formation is reminiscent of <i>καβάλλης</i> (kabállēs) and <i>δαμάλης</i> (damálēs). According to Hoffmann, the word was Anatolian, but it is a clear example of Pre-Greek word, in view of the variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inspector of frontier posts</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>circitor</i>. Documented from the 2nd c. AD to the early 7th.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow-orange, tawny</li></ol><p>Unexplained, with the same geminate <i>-ρρ-</i> as in <i>πυρρός</i> (purrhós, “red”). The traditional derivation by Pokorny from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ḱeh₃-</i> (“shadow, darkness”) and comparisons with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;śěrъ</i> (“grey”) and Irish <i>ciar</i> (“dark”) are phonetically problematic, as is the link to Lithuanian <i>širmas</i> (“grey”), since Lithuanian <i>-ir-</i> is from a zero-grade.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) varicocele, enlargement of a vein</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variation "σ/ξ" shows that the word is from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Thessalian form of <i>τῐς</i> (tĭs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pellitory (<i>Parietaria officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κῐσσός</i> (kĭssós, “ivy”) +&lrm; <i>ἄμπελος</i> (ámpelos, “vine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ivy (<i>Hedera helix</i>), sacred to Dionysus</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek. Furnée compares <i>κιθάρα</i> (kithára), in the sense of "ivy".</p><p>Janda derives the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kitw-ó-</i>, interpreted as “belonging to a tree, mark, sacrificial post”, ultimately from <i>&#42;keyt-</i> (“shine, glow”). He connects the Greek word to Sanskrit <i>केतु</i> (ketú, “brightness, rays of light, apparition, sign, mark, flag”) in the sense of “sacrificial post”, used synonymously with Sanskrit <i>केतु</i> (sváru, “large piece of wood, stake, sacrificial post”), furthermore to Old Norse <i>Heiðrún</i> and <i>heiðvanr</i> (“holy, connected to heaven”). Both the adjective and the mythical goat from Norse mythology are associated with the cosmological ash tree <i>Yggdrasil</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ivy-leaf, used as a brand</li><li>part of a torsion-engine shaped like an ivy-leaf</li><li>honeysuckle (<i>Lonicera periclymenum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κισσός</i> (kissós, “ivy”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rustic drinking-cup of wood, used by the <i>Cyclops</i></li></ol><p>The ancients connected the word with <i>κισσός</i> (kissós, “ivy”), either after the material or after the ornamentation resembling ivy leaves. According to Beekes, the word is Pre-Greek, because of the variation <i>β/φ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citron tree</li></ol><p>From <i>κίτρον</i> (kítron, “fruit of the citron tree”) +&lrm; <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chinese cinnamon (<i>Cinnamomum cassia</i>)</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>קִדָּה</i> (qiddâ, “cassia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overtake</li><li>to come upon, find</li><li>to catch, grip, hit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰi-ǵʰn̥h₁-</i>, reduplicated nasal-infix present of the root <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₁-</i> (“to go, walk”). Cognate with Old English <i>gān</i> (English <i>go</i>) and Sanskrit <i>जहाति</i> (jáhāti, “to abandon, avoid”). The reduplicating syllable /ki/ was reinterpreted as part of the root and extended beyond the present and imperfect tenses to the entire conjugation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicory (<i>Cichorium intybus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κίχορα</i> (kíkhora) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>κιχόριον</i> (kikhórion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rameous, related to a branch</li></ol><p>From <i>κλάδος</i> (kládos, “young slip or shoot of a tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>κλάδος</i> (kládos, “branch, shoot”)</li></ol><p>From <i>κλάδος</i> (kládos, “branch, shoot”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) young slip or shoot of a tree, such as is broken off for grafting</li><li>olive-branch which was wound round with wool and presented by suppliants</li><li>laurel branches used in temples</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) branch of a blood vessel</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) arm</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;klə́dos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kl̥d-o-s</i> (“piece of wood”), which is traditionally linked to <i>&#42;kelh₂-</i> (“to break”). Often compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kòlda</i> (“block, log”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hultą</i> (“wood”). Beekes is skeptical enough of Indo-European origin and particularly the connection of the reconstructed root to <i>&#42;kelh₂-</i> to suggest a Pre-Greek origin, though not skeptical enough to reject an Indo-European origin entirely.</p><p>Other potential cognates include Latin <i>clādes</i>, Old Irish <i>caill</i>, Macedonian клада (klada).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a sharp piercing sound (scream, bay, clash)</li></ol><p>Cognate with Latin <i>clangō</i> (“to clang”), from a non-Indo-European root per Beekes. Possibly from a root noun *<i>κλάγξ</i> (klánx) +&lrm; <i>-ι̯ω</i> (-i̯ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden shoes, pattens, clogs</li><li>stocks, as a means of punishment</li></ol><p>No etymology. Perhaps of onomatopoeic origin, after the clattering.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaking, fracture</li><li>bending of the knee joint, refraction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a bandage</i>)</li><li>(<i>of the labyrinth of the ear</i>)</li></ol><li>modulation of the voice</li></ol><p><i>κλᾰ́ω</i> (klắō, “break”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A fragment, a morsel</li><li>A lesion, a rupture</li></ol><p>From <i>κλᾰ́ω</i> (klắō, “to break”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vinedresser</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break, to break off, to break into pieces</li><li>(of geometric lines,&#32;passive voice) to be broken or deflected</li><li>to break, to weaken, to frustrate</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₂-</i> (“to beat, break”), whence also Latin <i>percellō</i> (“to beat down, overturn”), but due to phonetic difficulties, Beekes prefers to take the verb as Pre-Greek.</p><p>Attic form of <i>κλαίω</i> (klaíō, “weep”). Root of <i>κλαϝ-</i> (klaw-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thing stolen</li><li>theft</li><li>stratagem in war</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέπτω</i> (kléptō, “to steal”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rumour, report</li><li>good report, fame, glory</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) bad report, disrepute</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kléwos</i> (whence Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀐𐀩𐀺</i> (ke-re-wo)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱléwos</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>श्रवस्</i> (śrávas), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬬𐬀𐬭𐬯</i> (havars), Old Armenian <i>լու</i> (lu), <i>լսեմ</i> (lsem), Old Church Slavonic <i>слово</i> (slovo), Old Irish <i>clú</i>, Welsh <i>clywed</i>. Compare <i>κλύω</i> (klúō, “I hear”) and <i>κλέω</i> (kléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief</li><li>deceitful person</li></ol><p>From <i>κλεπ-</i> (klep-, present stem of <i>κλέπτω</i> (kléptō, “to steal”)) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal, filch</li><li>to cheat</li><li>to mislead, keep secret</li><li>to disguise, conceal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;klépťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;klép-ye-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;klep-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>clepō</i> and Gothic <i>𐌷𐌻𐌹𐍆𐌰𐌽</i> (hlifan).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell of, make famous, celebrate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be famous</li></ol><li>alternative form of <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>clueō</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>слава</i> (slava), Sanskrit <i>शृणोति</i> (śr̥ṇṓti). See also <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alder (<i>Alnus glutinosa</i>)</li></ol><p>Unclear. It has been connected with Tyrolese German <i>Lutter</i> (“green alder”). The suffix -θρη is from the same PIE root as -τρον.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of alder wood</li></ol><p>From <i>κλήθρα</i> (klḗthra, “alder”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a slope, incline, inclination</li><li>the supposed slope of the earth from the equator to the pole; (terrestrial) latitude</li><li>a region, zone or belt of the earth; a clime</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῑ́νω</i> (klī́nō, “to slope, incline”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed; couch</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῑ́νω</i> (klī́nō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, slant</li><li>to cause to give way, cause to retreat</li><li>to lean, prop something on another</li><li>to turn aside</li><li>to decline, wane</li><li>to seat, cause to lie down</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to inflect, decline, conjugate</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to lean, be sloping</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to wander, stray</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;klíňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱl̥-né-y-ti</i> (“to lean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bending, inclination</li><li>a lying down; a place for lying down</li><li>(<i>of soldiers</i>) turning</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) inflection (of nouns and verbs)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) augment</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῑ́νω</i> (klī́nō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κλαίουσι</i> (klaíousi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cry, weep, wail, lament</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weep for, mourn</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to bewail oneself, weep aloud</li></ol><p>Stems:</p><p>Uncertain etymology. May be cognate with Albanian <i>klanj</i>, <i>qaj</i> (“to weep”), from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;klaunja</i>, pointing to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kleh₂u-</i>, but no other Indo-European cognates are apparent. Or, from Pre-Greek and/or ultimately onomatopoeic.</p><p>From <i>κλείω</i> (kleíō, “shut”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut, close, bar (e.g. the door)</li><li>to enclose, shut in</li><li>Epic form of <i>κλέω</i> (kléō, “celebrate, make famous”)</li><li>Epic form of <i>κλέω</i> (kléō), alternative form of <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō, “call”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;klāyyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kleh₂uyeti</i>, from <i>&#42;kleh₂u-</i> (“hook, key”), whence <i>κληΐς</i> (klēḯs), <i>κλήϊθρον</i> (klḗïthron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clang, clash, sharp sound</li></ol><p>From <i>κλάζω</i> (klázō, “make a sharp, piercing sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of cranes</i>) screaming</li></ol><p>From <i>κλᾰ́ζω</i> (klắzō, “to scream”) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>docked, cropped</li></ol><p>The word shares the formation with <i>κολοβός</i> (kolobós, “mutilated, docked”) and <i>σκαμβός</i> (skambós, “crooked”), denoting a derivation from Pre-Greek. According to Beekes, the similarity with Lithuanian <i>klumbas</i> (“limping”) and Old English <i>limpan</i> (“to limp”) is only accidental.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bracelet</li></ol><p>Connection with <i>κλάω</i> (kláō) seems improbable, in spite of <i>κλαστός</i> (klastós, “frizzly head”), <i>ἐγκλαστρίδια</i> (enklastrídia, “earring”). Maybe related to <i>χλανίαι</i> (khlaníai, “garment”) and <i>χλανίτιδες</i> (khlanítides, “necklaces”); if so, the variation between voiceless and aspirate shows the Pre-Greek origin of the word. Furnée further compares <i>πλανίς</i> (planís, “golden headband of the bride”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broken</li></ol><p>From <i>κλᾰ́ω</i> (klắō, “break”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) weeping, wailing</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κλαίω</i> (klaíō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) weeping, wailing</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) troubles, misfortunes</li></ol><p>From <i>κλαίω</i> (klaíō, “to cry, to wail”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bar, bolt</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a catch or hook passed through the door from the outside to catch the strap attached to the bar</li><li>key</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a means to something; key</li></ol><li>hook or tongue of a clasp</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>stopcock</li></ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) collarbone (probably so called from its hook shape)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) rowing bench of a ship</li><li>narrow strait, promontory or pass</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) sacred chaplets</li><li>(<i>poetry</i>) clausula, cadence</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;klāwī́ds</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kleh₂u-</i> (“nail, pin, hook - instruments, of old use for locking doors”). Cognate with Latin <i>clāvus</i> (“nail, pin”), Old Church Slavonic <i>ключь</i> (ključĭ, “key”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κλείς)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Clides</li></ol><p>From <i>κλείς</i> (kleís, “key”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut, close, bar (e.g. the door)</li><li>to enclose, shut in</li><li>Epic form of <i>κλέω</i> (kléō, “celebrate, make famous”)</li><li>Epic form of <i>κλέω</i> (kléō), alternative form of <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō, “call”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;klāyyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kleh₂uyeti</i>, from <i>&#42;kleh₂u-</i> (“hook, key”), whence <i>κληΐς</i> (klēḯs), <i>κλήϊθρον</i> (klḗïthron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Little key.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Stopcock.</li></ol><li>synonym of <i>κλείς</i> (kleís) (<i>of the tunny</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) A kind of astringent pill or astringent suppository.</li></ol><p>From <i>κλείς</i> (kleís, “bolt, catch, key”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, “diminutive suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lock up</li></ol><p><i>κλείς</i> (kleís) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>famous; renowned; well-known</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>often with <i>πᾶσι</i> (pâsi): well-known to all</li></ol></ol><p>From the root of <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>-εινός</i> (-einós, adjectival suffix). Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slavьnъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that can be closed or shut</li></ol><p>From <i>κλείω</i> (kleíō, “to shut”) +&lrm; <i>-στός</i> (-stós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) clitoris</li></ol><p>From <i>κλείω</i> (kleíō, “I shut in, I inclose”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. suffix?</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>renowned, famous; highly honored and admired.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) splendid, excellent</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κλέω</i> (kléō, “to celebrate”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix). Compare <i>κλυτός</i> (klutós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tortoise</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to that of synonymous words <i>χέλυς</i> (khélus) and <i>ἐμύς</i> (emús). Originally considered a folk-etymological adaptation to <i>κλέμμα</i> (klémma) and <i>κλέπτω</i> (kléptō, “to conceal”), because turtles can hide their bodies under their shell. Furnée compares <i>κρεμύς</i> (kremús, “a kind of fish”) and <i>χρέμυς</i> (khrémus, “a sort of cod”); these variants confirm that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέπτω</i> (kléptō, “to steal”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knavery, thievishness</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέπτω</i> (kléptō, “to steal”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipette</li><li>water clock; clepsydra. The ancient form was similar to the pipette.</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέπτω</i> (kléptō, “steal”) +&lrm; <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”), literally “water thief”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A bar for closing a door</li><li>The boom of an harbour</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) The entrance of the windpipe</li></ol><p>From <i>κλείω</i> (kleíō, “to close”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>κλείς</i> (kleís)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>omen</li><li>report, tidings</li><li>fame, glory</li></ol><p>From κλέος (kléos, “fame, glory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of vine twigs</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῆμα</i> (klêma, “vine twig”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vine branch</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) brushwood, faggots</li><li>periwinkle (<i>Vinca herbacea</i>)</li><li>traveller's joy (<i>Clematis vitalba</i>)</li><li>bindweed (<i>Convolvulus arvensis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῆμα</i> (klêma, “twig, branch”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concerning inheritances</li><li>of or pertaining to clergy; clerical</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clergyman</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῆρος</i> (klêros, “a lot, which is alotted, clergy”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inherit, receive a share or portion of an inheritance</li><li>to acquire, obtain</li><li>to be an inheritor or heir</li><li>to leave an heir behind</li></ol><p>From <i>κληρονόμος</i> (klēronómos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inheritance</li><li>possession</li></ol><p>From <i>κληρονόμος</i> (klēronómos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heir</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῆρος</i> (klêros) +&lrm; <i>νέμω</i> (némō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place where elections by lot or distributions of jurors were held</li><li>list of citizens, so called because jurors were selected from it by lot</li><li>kleroterion (machine used in sortition)</li></ol><p>From <i>κληρόω</i> (klēróō, “to appoint by lot”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the vocative case &ndash; elliptically for <i>κλητῐκὴ</i> <i>πτῶσῐς</i> (ptôsĭs)&#58; literally, “inflection for calling” or “addressing”</li></ol><p>See <i>κλητῐκός</i> (klētĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The vocative particle, <i>ὦ</i> (ô)</li></ol><p>See <i>κλητῐκός</i> (klētĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for invitation</li><li>invocatory</li></ol><p>From <i>κλη-</i> (klē-), a stem of <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō, “to call”), + <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix): literally “related to calling”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>called, invited, welcome</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>called out, chosen</li><li>invoked</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō, “to call”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rung of a ladder</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) critical point in human life</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῖμαξ</i> (klîmax) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>climacteric</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῑμᾰκτήρ</i> (klīmăktḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to a bed</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) bedridden</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a physician (who tends to a bedridden patient)</li></ol><p>From <i>κλίνη</i> (klínē, “bed”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós), from <i>κλίνω</i> (klínō, “to lean, incline”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lesser calamint (<i>Clinopodium nepeta</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῑνόπους</i> (klīnópous, “feet of a bed”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hut</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κλίνω</i> (klínō, “to lie down, recline”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>κλισία</i> (klisía)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from a/the hut</li></ol><p><i>κλισίη</i> (klisíē) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the hut</li></ol><p><i>κλῐσίην</i> (klĭsíēn) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couch</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῑ́νω</i> (klī́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog collar</li><li>wooden collar worn by prisoners</li><li>collar of gold, as an ornament</li></ol><p>Often connected to <i>κλείς</i> (kleís, “key”). Alternatively, the word could be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive in confusion</li><li>to cause to rush wildly</li><li>to ruffle</li></ol><p><i>κλόνος</i> (klónos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theft</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of authors</i>) plagiarism</li></ol><li>a secret act or transaction, fraud</li><li>the surprise of a military post</li></ol><p>From <i>κλέπτω</i> (kléptō, “to steal”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a syringe, a clyster-pipe</li></ol><p>From <i>κλύζω</i> (klúzō, “to wash”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>renowned, glorious</li><li>noble, splendid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;klutós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlutós</i>, past participle of <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i> (whence <i>κλέω</i> (kléō), <i>κλύω</i> (klúō) and English <i>loud</i>). Compare <i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>κλωβός</i> (klōbós): small cage, twined basket</li></ol><p>From <i>κλωβός</i> (klōbós, “birdcage”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birdcage</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>כְּלוּב</i> (kluv), Arabic كَلْبِشْ (kalbiš), and Old Armenian <i>կողով</i> (kołov).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spindle</li><li>thread, yarn</li><li>skein</li><li>thread of life</li></ol><p>From <i>κλώθω</i> (klṓthō, “to spin”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confused motion, turmoil, battle-rout</li><li>trembling, confusion, agitation</li><li>(<i>in physiological sense</i>) agitation, of wind in the bowels</li><li>(<i>of the body</i>) shaking, agitation, contraction</li></ol><p>Usually derived from <i>κέλομαι</i> (kélomai, “to urge, exhort”). Beekes instead thinks it’s a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wave, billow, and collectively, surf, rough water</li><li>splashing</li><li>flood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;klūd-</i>. Cognates include Gothic <i>𐌷𐌻𐌿𐍄𐍂𐍃</i> (hlūtrs), Welsh <i>clir</i>, and Latin <i>cluō</i>, <i>clovāca</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the sea</i>) to wash, dash over, surge up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be washed by the sea</li><li>to wash away, purge</li><li>to wash, rinse out, cleanse</li><li>to coat with wax</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱlewH-</i>. From the same root, we can find <i>κλύδων</i> (klúdōn, “billow”). Other cognates are Latin <i>cluō</i> (“to clean, purge”), Gothic <i>𐌷𐌻𐌿𐍄𐍂𐍃</i> (hlūtrs, “clean, pure”) and Welsh <i>clir</i> (“clean, pure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liquid used for washing out, especially an enema, a drench</li><li>place washed by the waves, seabeach</li></ol><p>From <i>κλύζω</i> (klúzō, “to wash, to purge”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κλύσμα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Clysma; Suez, Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>κλύσμᾰ</i> (klúsmă, “place washed by the waves, sea-beach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hear, listen to [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> participle <small>‘doing something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p>The present <i>κλύω</i> (klúō) is an innovation of the thematic aorist <i>ἔκλῠον</i> (éklŭon), which itself innovated on the older athematic aorist (as seen in <i>κλῦτε</i> (klûte)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱléwt</i>, from the root <i>&#42;ḱlew-</i> (“to hear”). Related to <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) and <i>κλέω</i> (kléō, “to make famous”). Compare Latin <i>inclutus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spin, twist by spinning</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Has been connected with Ancient Greek <i>κάλαθος</i> (kálathos) and Latin <i>colus</i>, although Beekes rejects these connections. Instead, Beekes explains the term as from Pre-Greek. The term has also been connected with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀒𐀫𐀵</i> (ko-ro-to), although this is also uncertain. The Mycenaean term may also be related to Ancient Greek <i>χροιά</i> (khroiá). Rix tentatively postulates that the term could constitute a <i>&#42;-dʰh₁eti</i> present of an otherwise unattested Indo-European root <i>&#42;ḱleh₃dʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twig, spray, slip</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Probably related to <i>κλάδος</i> (kládos, “branch, twig”) and <i>κλαστός</i> (klastós, “broken off”), as in <i>clastic</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thief</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;klṓps</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;klṓps</i>, from <i>&#42;klep-</i> (“to steal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twig, branch, tendril of the vine</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) sprout, shoot</li><li>cutting, slip</li><li>vine staff, cane carried by Roman centurions</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected with <i>κλάω</i> (kláō, “to break”), but the semantics are tenuous, and there is no evidence for a long <i>α</i> (a) in the former. Thus, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lot</li><li>that which is assigned by lot, an allotment</li><li>a farm</li><li>an inheritance</li><li>the clergy</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(act of) calling, a call</li><li>(<i>law</i>) summons, prosecution</li><li>invitation (<i>to a feast etc.</i>)</li><li>name, appellation</li><li>vocation</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladder</li><li>staircase</li><li>torture instrument shaped like a ladder</li><li>a certain wrestling move</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) climax</li><li>the blocks of wood placed above the wheels of a chariot</li></ol><p>From <i>κλῑ́νω</i> (klī́nō, “to bend, lean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap of stones, rocky place</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>λίθαξ</i> (líthax) and <i>βῶλαξ</i> (bôlax). Usually connected with <i>κλάω</i> (kláō, “to break”), by assumption of a verbal noun <i>&#42;κλῶμος</i> (&#42;klômos, “crack, split”). This is unlikely, especially given the by-form <i>κρῶμαξ</i> (krômax, “rocky place”) which shows a typical Pre-Greek variation "ρ/λ".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beam</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>μέλαθρον</i> (mélathron) and of Pre-Greek origin. It has also been compared to <i>καμάρα</i> (kamára).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to card, comb, to cleanse or disentangle something using a carding device</li><li>to mangle, to mutilate, or disfigure by cutting, tearing, rearranging, etc.</li></ol><p>Unknown. It has been connected with several Indo-European words, such as Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;knawī</i> (“fleece”) and Latvian <i>knābt</i> (“to peck”). On the other hand, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin for the verb, connecting it <i>κνάφος</i> (knáphos, “prickly teasel; carding comb”), <i>κνέφαλλον</i> (knéphallon, “wool torn off in carding”) and <i>κναφεύς</i> (knapheús, “fuller”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>κνήσαιο</i> (knḗsaio)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prickly teasel, used by fullers to card or clean cloth</li><li>carding-comb, also used as an instrument of torture</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variation <i>κν-/γν-</i> points to a Pre-Greek origin of the word. Not related to <i>κνίζω</i> (knízō, “to scratch, gash”), nor to <i>κνύω</i> (knúō, “to scratch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape, scratch</li><li>to tickle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kneh₂-</i> (“to scratch”). Cognate with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hnōjan</i>, Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;knāyeti</i> (“to bite, chew”), and Lithuanian <i>knóti</i> (“to peel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool torn off in carding or fulling cloth</li><li>flock used for stuffing pillows and cushions</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) cushion, pillow</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>κνάπτω</i> (knáptō, “to card wool”), compared with Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;knawī</i> (“fleece”), but the variation in forms suggests a Pre-Greek origin instead. See also <i>κνάφος</i> (knáphos, “prickly teasel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness, often referring to evening dusk or night</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(figuratively,&#32;of the mind)</li></ol></ol><p>Often connected with Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kšápš</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷséps</i> (“night”). Compare Avestan <i>𐬑𐬴𐬀𐬞𐬀𐬥</i> (xṣ̌apan), Old Persian <i>𐎧𐏁𐎱𐎺</i> (x-š-p-v /⁠xšap-⁠/), Sanskrit <i>क्षप्</i> (kṣáp) and Hittite <i>𒅖𒉺𒀭𒍝</i> (ispanza). Others have connected <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō), Latin <i>creper</i> (“dusky, dark”), <i>crepusculum</i>, and Albanian <i>ngrys</i> (“to darken”).</p><p>For Beekes, the word is “no doubt Pre-Greek”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to itch</li></ol><p>From <i>κνάω</i> (knáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from safflower</li></ol><p>From <i>κνῆκος</i> (knêkos, “safflower”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) shin; tibia</li><li>spoke of a wheel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kónh₂m</i> (“leg”); cognate with English <i>ham</i> (from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hammō</i>) and Old Irish <i>cnáim</i> (“bone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stinging nettle (<i>Urtica dioica</i>)</li><li>sea anemone in the genus <i>Actinia</i> with a nettle-like sting</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>κνίζω</i> (knízō, “scratch or gash”); yet the <i>-ι-</i> is long in this word, which may point to Pre-Greek origin, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch, gash</li><li>to pound, chop up or grate</li><li>to tickle, titillate</li><li>(<i>of feelings</i>) to chafe, tease, provoke</li></ol><p>Based on the stem forms <i>κνιδ-, κνισ-</i> (knid-, knis-), likely from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kneyd-</i> (“to scratch, knock”), from <i>&#42;ken-</i> (“to scratch; scrape; rub”). Cognates include Latvian <i>knidêt</i> (“to itch, geminate, creep”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hnītaną</i> (“to push, knock”), Middle Irish <i>cned</i> (“wound”). A root-final <i>-t-</i> is found as well, like in Lithuanian <i>kni̇̀sti</i> (“to scratch, itch, tickle”); a root-final <i>-s-</i> occurs in Lithuanian <i>knisù</i> (“to grub up”). Despite superficial similarities, probably not related to <i>κνῑ́δη</i> (knī́dē, “stinging nettle”), due to the latter's long vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) nidor, the smell of roasting meat and fat</li></ol><p>Cognate with Latin <i>nīdor</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of insect that infests fig and oak trees and devours the gallfly</li></ol><p>Formation rhyming with <i>θρῑ́ψ</i> (thrī́ps, “woodworm”) and <i>ῑ̓́ψ</i> (ī́ps, “kind of worm”). It has been compared with words for "pinching" in Baltic and Germanic, but these are rather European substrate words. Beekes points towards Pre-Greek, on the basis of long <i>-ῑ-</i> (-ī-) and prothetic <i>σ-</i> (s-) for a very small kind of animal.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>κνηκός</i> (knēkós, “tawny”)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dappled horse</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale yellow, tawny, yellow-brown</li></ol><p>From <i>κνῆκος</i> (knêkos) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greave; legging</li><li>spoke of a wheel</li></ol><p>From <i>κνήμη</i> (knḗmē, “shin; tibia”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>projecting limb or shoulder of a mountain</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of woodpecker</li></ol><p>From <i>κνίψ</i> (kníps, “insect”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to gather, pick up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) (<i>According to Hesychius of Alexandria:</i>) a herb similar to celery</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested in Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild creature, beast</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) brute, beast</li></ol><p>Frisk assumed that this word and <i>κνώδων</i> (knṓdōn, “projecting teeth on the blade of a hunting spear”), <i>κνώδαξ</i> (knṓdax, “pin, pivot”) go back to a verbal noun <i>&#42;κνωδ-</i> (&#42;knōd-, “tooth, biter, gnawer”), belonging to <i>κναίω</i> (knaíō, “to scrape, scratch”). The glosses <i>κάναδοι</i> (kánadoi, “jaws”), <i>κναδάλλεται</i> (knadálletai, “gnaws”) belong here too. The words do not belong to Latvian <i>kost</i> (“to bite”). According to Beekes, they are undoubtedly Pre-Greek, since <i>κναδ-</i> cannot continue an Indo-European pre-form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pin or pivot on which a body or machine turns</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) sockets in which the axes of a drum turn</li></ol><p>This word is probably connected to <i>κνώδᾰλον</i> (knṓdălon, “beast; wild animal”) and <i>κνώδων</i> (knṓdōn, “projecting teeth on the blade of a hunting spear”). Per Derksen, these words derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;k(ʷ)end-</i> or <i>&#42;k(ʷ)enHd(ʰ)-</i>, like Sanskrit <i>खाद॑ति</i> (khā́dati, “to chew; to bite”) and Persian خاییدن (xâyidan, “to chew”), while Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin for all these lemmas.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) two projecting teeth on the blade of a hunting spear</li><li>sword</li></ol><p>Per Derksen, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;k(ʷ)end-</i> or <i>&#42;k(ʷ)enHd(ʰ)-</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>खादति</i> (khādati, “to chew, to bite”) and Persian خاییدن (xâyidan, “to chew”). See also <i>κνώδαλον</i> (knṓdalon, “wild beast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sleep, slumber</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin, similar to <i>εὕδω</i> (heúdō, “to rest, be still”). Note also its semantic opposite <i>ἐγρήσσω</i> (egrḗssō, “to watch, awake”), from <i>ἐγείρω</i> (egeírō, “to awaken, rouse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>venomous beast, especially a serpent</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>κινώπετον</i> (kinṓpeton, “venomous beast”) and thus from Pre-Greek. Not a cross of <i>κνώδαλον</i> (knṓdalon, “wild animal”) and another word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>safflower (<i>Carthamus tinctorius</i>)</li></ol><p>Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀏𐀙𐀒</i> (ka-na-ko) points to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;knā́kos</i>. Related in some way (via an adjective “honey-colored”) to the Proto-Indo-European “honey” word reconstructed as <i>&#42;kn̥h₂ónks</i> (largely for pre-Germanic; compare English <i>honey</i>, Albanian <i>qengjë</i> (“beehive”) and Latin <i>canicae</i> (“bran”)), but no consistent paradigm for this word can be reconstructed, and thus it is uncertain. Further complicating the matter is that safflower is not native to Greece. Nonetheless, the adjective in question is possibly Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kn̥h₂-ḱó-s</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contracted form of <i>κνάω</i> (knáō, “to scratch, scrape, tickle”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the doum palm</li></ol><p>From <i>κόϊξ</i> (kóïx, “doum palm”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stupid fellow, booby, blockhead</li></ol><p>Ending like in <i>ἰάλεμος</i> (iálemos) and <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos). If from <i>&#42;κοϝᾱ́λεμος</i> (&#42;kowā́lemos), the word could be compared with <i>κόβαλος</i> (kóbalos, “rogue”), as well as <i>καυαλός</i> (kaualós, “speaking like an idiot”) and <i>κόαλοι</i> (kóaloi, “foreigners”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>croak (the sound made by a frog)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know, be aware</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kowéjō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kowh₁éyeti</i>, causative of the root <i>&#42;(s)kewh₁-</i> (“to perceive, pay attention”).</p><p>Cognate with <i>κῦδος</i> (kûdos), Latin <i>caveō</i>, Sanskrit <i>कवि</i> (kaví, “wise, poet, seer, sage”), Lithuanian <i>kavoti</i> (“to safeguard, to tend”), Old Armenian <i>ցուցանեմ</i> (cʻucʻanem, “to show”), Polish <i>cześć</i> (“glory”) and <i>cud</i> (“miracle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hollow, cavity</li></ol><p>From <i>κοιλόω</i> (koilóō, “hollow out”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king, ruler, leader, commander</li><li>lord, master</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kóryos</i>, from <i>&#42;ker-</i>. Direct cognate with Old Norse <i>Herjann</i>, from the same root as Old English <i>here</i>, Middle Irish <i>cuire</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed, resting place</li><li>(<i>geography</i>) riverbed</li><li>lair of a wild beast</li><li>pen, fold for cattle</li><li>act of going to bed; bedtime</li><li>lodging, entertainment</li><li>(<i>Septuagint, New Testament</i>) (conjugal) context wherein sexual activity occurs</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱey-</i> (“to lie down”), the same root of <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai, “to lie in a place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry as a porter</li></ol><p>From <i>κόβᾱλος</i> (kóbālos, “rogue”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, “denominative verb-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small kind of mussel or cockle</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) the shell of a bivalve</li></ol><p>From <i>κογχῠ́λη</i> (konkhŭ́lē) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix); the first element is itself a diminutive of <i>κόγχη</i> (kónkhē) and <i>κόγχος</i> (kónkhos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who roasts barley</li></ol><p>Comparison with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čadъ</i> (“smoke, fumes”) cannot be upheld. A similar meaning is found in <i>κίδναι</i> (kídnai, “barley roasted on the countryside”), but this has a different vowel. Probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the large hollow or cavity of the body, belly, abdomen, stomach</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the intestines, bowels</li><li>excrement</li></ol><li>any cavity in the body, ventricle, chamber</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the socket of a bone</li><li>the middle fleshy part of the muscles</li></ol><li>any hollow or cavity</li></ol><p>From <i>κοῖλος</i> (koîlos, “hollow”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the bowels, intestinal</li><li>suffering in the bowels</li></ol><p>From <i>κοιλίᾱ</i> (koilíā) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put to sleep, lull</li><li>(<i>middle voice&#32;and&#32;passive voice</i>) to sleep</li></ol><p>Perhaps from an unattested <i>&#42;κοῖμος</i> (&#42;koîmos) or <i>&#42;κοιμή</i> (&#42;koimḗ) <i>-άω</i> (-áō). The base of the verb may have derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱóymos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱey-</i> (“to lie down”). According to van Beek, if the term was perhaps derived from <i>&#42;ḱoymeh₂</i>, then it would showcase a "remarkable" morphological similarity to <i>εὐνή</i> (eunḗ). Related to <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai, “to lie outstretched”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedroom</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) burial place</li></ol><p>From <i>κοιμάω</i> (koimáō, “put to sleep”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>See etymology.</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Hesychius gives the definition as <i>χόρτος</i> (khórtos). The stem <i>κοιν-</i> (koin-) matches perfectly with the expected Greek descendant of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱóyno-</i>, with cognates including Old Church Slavonic <i>сѣно</i> (sěno), and Lithuanian <i>šienas</i>. This would work with the definition "grass, hay" of <i>χόρτος</i> (khórtos). However, some have argued that it may simply be an inflected form of <i>κοινός</i> (koinós, “common”), interpreting it as simply a communal piece of land, lining up with the "barn, pasture" sense of <i>χόρτος</i> (khórtos).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common</li><li>public, general</li><li>neutral</li><li>shared</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;koňňós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱomyós</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> (“with”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yós</i> (“adjectival suffix”), the ancestor of the suffix <i>-ιος</i> (-ios). Cognates include Latin <i>cum</i>, Gaulish <i>com-</i>, and Old English <i>ge-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common</li><li>public, general</li><li>neutral</li><li>shared</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;koňňós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱomyós</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> (“with”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yós</i> (“adjectival suffix”), the ancestor of the suffix <i>-ιος</i> (-ios). Cognates include Latin <i>cum</i>, Gaulish <i>com-</i>, and Old English <i>ge-</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κοινή)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Koine Greek</li></ol><p>Shortened form of <i>ἡ κοινή διάλεκτος</i> (hē koinḗ diálektos, “the common dialect”), from <i>κοινός</i> (koinós, “common”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have or do in common with, share, take part along with <i>(+dative of person)</i> <i>(+genitive of thing)</i></li><li>to have a share of or to take part in something <i>(+genitive of thing)</i></li><li>to go shares with, to have dealings with someone <i>(+dative of person)</i></li><li>to cohabit with a man or woman <i>(+dative of person)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>κοινών</i> (koinṓn, “partner”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “I act as”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>communion, association, partnership, fellowship</li><li>common gift, contribution, alms</li><li>sexual intercourse</li></ol><p>From <i>κοινωνός</i> (koinōnós, “companion, partner”) (from <i>κοινός</i> (koinós, “common, shared”)) + <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>held in common</li><li>relating to partnerships</li><li>social</li><li>sociable</li><li>giving a share of</li><li>in communion with</li><li>receptive, sharing in</li></ol><p>From <i>κοινωνός</i> (koinōnós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, partner, fellow</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>κοινωνέω</i> (koinōnéō), after pairs like <i>στρατηγέω</i> (stratēgéō) and <i>στρατηγός</i> (stratēgós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living in community with others</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>life in community</li><li>monastery</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κοινός</i> (koinós, “common, shared”) +&lrm; <i>βίος</i> (bíos, “live”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in the language of common life</li></ol><p>From <i>κοινός</i> (“common”) +&lrm; <i>λεκτός</i> (“capable of being spoken”). See Koine Greek <i>κοινολεκτέω</i> / <i>κοινολεκτῶ</i> (“use the language of common life”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common</li><li>public, general</li><li>neutral</li><li>shared</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;koňňós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱomyós</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> (“with”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yós</i> (“adjectival suffix”), the ancestor of the suffix <i>-ιος</i> (-ios). Cognates include Latin <i>cum</i>, Gaulish <i>com-</i>, and Old English <i>ge-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>κοινός</i> (koinós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sharing in common, community, partnership</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>common or universal quality</li><li>generality, vagueness, ambiguity</li></ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) absence of privileges or distinctions</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>affability, accessibility</li></ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) use of a common word in two clauses</li></ol><p><i>κοινός</i> (koinós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make common, make unholy</li><li>to share</li></ol><p>From <i>κοινός</i> (koinós, “common”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, partner, fellow</li></ol><p>From <i>κοινός</i> (koinós) +&lrm; <i>-έων</i> (-éōn), contracted <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn), an obscure suffix that appears as <i>-άων</i> (-áōn) in Doric and as <i>-ήων</i> (-ḗōn) in Ionic.</p><p>Compare the like <i>ξυνών</i> (xunṓn, “partner”) and its dialectal forms <i>ξυνάων</i> (xunáōn) <i>(Doric)</i>, <i>ξυνήων</i> (xunḗōn) <i>(Ionic)</i> and <i>ξυνέων</i> (xunéōn) <i>(Attic)</i>, from <i>ξυνός</i> (xunós, “common”). The same suffix also bear the Homeric words <i>ὀπάων</i> (opáōn, “comrade”) (in Herodotus <i>ὀπέων</i> (opéōn)) and <i>διδυμάων</i> (didumáōn, “twin”) instead of <i>δίδυμος</i> (dídumos), the common notion being the conjunction of two persons.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commonplace (a general or common topic in a literary passage, such as a statement of proverbial wisdom&#59; a commonplace)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in common, by agreement</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common</li><li>public, general</li><li>neutral</li><li>shared</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;koňňós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱomyós</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> (“with”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yós</i> (“adjectival suffix”), the ancestor of the suffix <i>-ιος</i> (-ios). Cognates include Latin <i>cum</i>, Gaulish <i>com-</i>, and Old English <i>ge-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in common, jointly, opposite to ἰδίᾳ</li><li>publicly</li><li>sociably, in a sociable fashion</li><li>in the Koine Greek dialect, in common language or fashion</li></ol><p>Adverb form of κοινός</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule (over), be lord (over) (<i>with genitive</i>)</li></ol><p><i>κοίρανος</i> (koíranos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put to bed, cause to rest</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to have a lair (<i>of a lion</i>), a nest (<i>of a bird</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κοίτη</i> (koítē, “bed”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box</li><li>basket</li></ol><p>From <i>κοίτη</i> (koítē) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of the bedchamber</li></ol><p>From <i>κοιτών</i> (koitṓn, “bedchamber”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain, seed, kernel</li><li>seed of the broad bean (<i>Vicia faba</i>)</li><li>pill</li><li>weight or monetary unit</li></ol><p>From <i>κόκκος</i> (kókkos) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meaning uncertain</i>) perhaps the grosbeak (glossed «ὄρνις ποιός» by Hesychius)</li></ol><p><i>κόκκος</i> (kókkos, “kernel”) +&lrm; <i>θραύστης</i> (thraústēs, “one who breaks or crushes”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fustic (<i>Cotinus coccygria</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κόκκυξ</i> (kókkux, “cuckoo”) +&lrm; <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple-red</li></ol><p>From <i>κοκκῡγέα</i> (kokkūgéa, “fustic”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos), because of the purple-colored leaves of this plant. Perhaps influenced by <i>κόκκινος</i> (kókkinos, “scarlet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plum</li></ol><p>From <i>κόκκῠξ</i> (kókkŭx, “cuckoo”) +&lrm; <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to check; to chastise</li><li>to chastise; to punish</li><li>to be badly in need of</li></ol><p>From <i>κόλος</i> (kólos, “docked”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peck</li><li>to carve; to chisel</li></ol><p>From <i>κόλᾰφος</i> (kólăphos, “buffet, blow”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlantic chub mackerel (<i>Scomber colias</i>)</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ἀκανθίας</i> (akanthías), <i>ξιφίας</i> (xiphías), and other fish and animal names; further unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flatter, adulate</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to soften, render mild</li></ol><p><i>κόλαξ</i> (kólax) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house of correction</li><li>instrument of correction</li></ol><p>From <i>κολᾰ́ζω</i> (kolắzō, “to chastise; to punish”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρῐον</i> (-tḗrĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of beetles</i>) sheath-winged</li></ol><p>From <i>κολεός</i> (koleós, “sheath”) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sword-sheath, a scabbard</li><li>a cinerary urn</li><li>a varia lectio of <i>κελεός</i> (keleós, “green woodpecker”)</li><li>(in the form <i>κουλεός</i>) the sheath of the heart, the pericardium</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel-</i> (“to cover”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glue, cement</li><li>to mend (a broken vessel, etc.)</li><li>to weld one metal or other substance to another</li><li>to join fast together, unite</li><li>to put together, build</li></ol><p><i>κόλλᾰ</i> (kóllă, “glue”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll or loaf of bread</li></ol><p>Like other words in <i>-ῡρᾱ</i>, probably Pre-Greek. If the variation <i>ῡ/ου</i> is old, this also points to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pessary, diaphragm</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) eyesalve, collyrium</li><li>fine clay on which a seal can be impressed</li></ol><p>From <i>κολλῡ́ρα</i> (kollū́ra, “roll”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>docked, curtailed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>maimed, mutilated</li><li>stunted (of trees)</li><li>short, undersized</li><li>broken, chipped (of a cup)</li><li>truncated (of a cone)</li></ol></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>dock, curtail, mutilate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of time</i>) curtail, shorten</li></ol></ol><p>Related to <i>κολάζω</i> (kolázō, “chastise, punish”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. suffix? from what root, too?</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grey sallow (<i>Salix cinerea</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>κολοιτία</i> (koloitía, “<i>Cytisus aeolicus</i>”), influenced by <i>ἰτέα</i> (itéa, “willow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Cytisus aeolicus</i>, a tree growing in the Lipari island</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>κολουτέα</i> (koloutéa, “bladder senna”) and <i>κολοιτέα</i> (koloitéa, “grey sallow”). A Pre-Greek word, given the variations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackdaw (<i>Coloeus monedula</i>)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic origin cannot be further substantiated; neither does the word belong to <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō, “to call, summon”). It would be highly unsatisfactory to separate <i>κολῳός</i> (kolōiós, “brawling”) from this word, but an explanation of the notation with <i>-ῳ-</i> has not been found. Confront also <i>κολουᾶν</i> (kolouân, “to make noise”) and <i>κολοίφρυξ</i> (koloíphrux, “Tanagrian cock”). These variants may prove a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhizome of the Egyptian water-lily (<i>Nymphaea lotus</i>) <small>(<i>Koine</i>)</small></li><li>rhizome of the Indian sacred lotus (<i>Nelumbo nucifera</i>) <small>(<i>Koine</i>)</small></li><li>taro, eddo (<i>Colocasia esculenta</i> plant and produce) &lsqb;from 4<sup>th</sup> century CE&rsqb;</li></ol><p>Of uncertain Asiatic origin shared with Mishnaic Hebrew <i>קולקס</i>, <i>קרקס</i> (“taro”) and Arabic قُلْقَاس (qulqās, “taro”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) wild gourd, colocynth</li></ol><p>From <i>κολοκύνθη</i> (kolokúnthē, “pumpkin”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottle gourd (<i>Lagenaria siceraria</i>)</li><li>round gourd</li></ol><p>Based on the suffix, frequent in plant names of substrate origin, Beekes takes the word as Pre-Greek. The word has also been compared with Sanskrit <i>कालिन्द</i> (kālinda, “watermelon”) (which is traditionally considered to be named after the Hindu goddess Kali), as well as with Proto-Kurdish (kalak, “melon”), because an informant in Athenaeus says that it was introduced from India. This does not reveal much more about the word's origins, however.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made or obtained from gourds</li></ol><p>From <i>κολοκῠ́νθη</i> (kolokŭ́nthē, “gourd”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large statue</li><li>any statue, large or small</li></ol><p>Unknown, possibly Pre-Greek (perhaps Minoan).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noisy rabble, uproar, tumult</li></ol><p>Frequently analyzed as <i>&#42;κολο-συρ-τός</i> (&#42;kolo-sur-tós), with a suffix shared also by <i>κονιορτός</i> (koniortós, “cloud of dust”) and <i>βουλυτός</i> (boulutós, “late afternoon, evenfall”), while the second member would be from <i>σύρω</i> (súrō, “to draw, sweep”) However, the first member remains unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a summit, top, finishing</li><li>a type of playing ball</li><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;according to Hesychius) jackdaw, daw, grackle</li><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;according to Hesychius) a type of sea fish</li></ol><p>According to Pokorny, related to <i>κολώνη</i> (kolṓnē, “hill”) and Latin <i>collis</i> (“hill”). The sense of “topping, finishing” derives figuratively from that of “top”.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κολοφών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Colophon, Ionia, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>κολοφών</i> (kolophṓn, “summit, top”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive pickled in brine</li><li>thorny burnet (<i>Sarcopterium spinosum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κολῠμβᾰ́ω</i> (kolŭmbắō, “to plunge, dive”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunge into the sea</li><li>to swim</li></ol><p>From <i>κόλυμβος</i> (kólumbos, “little grebe”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō, “verbal suffix”), due to the grebe's diving skills.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place for diving, swimming bath, swimming pool</li><li>wine-vat, tun</li><li>reservoir, cistern</li><li>baptismal font</li></ol><p>From <i>κολυμβάω</i> (kolumbáō, “to dive, plunge”) +&lrm; <i>-ήθρα</i> (-ḗthra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autumn crocus (<i>Colchicum speciosum</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>Κολχικός</i> (Kolkhikós, “Colchian”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short-sleeved tunic</li><li>clavus (purple stripe on the tunic)</li></ol><p>From <i>κολοβός</i> (kolobós, “docked, curtailed”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the part taken away in mutilation</li></ol><p>From <i>κολοβόω</i> (kolobóō, “to mutilate; to dock”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of crab</li></ol><p>A typically Pre-Greek word with interchange "βδ/β" and prenasalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill, mound, especially a sepulchral mound</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hilltop, peak</li></ol></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kolH-on-</i> (“hill”), from the root <i>&#42;kelH-</i> (“to rise”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hulliz</i> (“hill”), Latin <i>collis</i> (“hill”), and Lithuanian <i>kálnas</i> (“mountain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brawling, noisy wrangling, racket</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>κολοιός</i> (koloiós, “jackdaw”) and <i>κολοίφρυξ</i> (koloíphrux, “Tanagrian cock”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have long hair</li></ol><p><i>κόμη</i> (kómē, “hair of the head”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take care of, tend, look after</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱemh₂-</i> (“to be tired”), the same root of <i>κάμνω</i> (kámnō, “to exert oneself, labour”); see the latter for more cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wearing long hair, long-haired</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) feathered (<i>of an arrow</i>); grassy (<i>of a meadow</i>)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;astronomy) comet</li></ol><p>From <i>κομάω</i> (komáō, “to have long hair”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take care of</li><li>to carry, bring</li></ol><p>From <i>κομέω</i> (koméō) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attendance, care</li><li>provision, supplies</li><li>carriage, conveyance, importation</li><li>gathering in of harvest</li><li>recovery (<i>e.g. of a debt, payment etc.</i>)</li><li>going or coming</li><li>escape, safe return</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱemh₂-</i> (“to exert oneself, get tired”), the same root as <i>κάμνω</i> (kámnō) and <i>κομέω</i> (koméō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exactly, just</li><li>absolutely, altogether, quite</li><li>(<i>chiefly in the negative</i>) at all</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attendance, care</li><li>provision, supplies</li><li>carriage, conveyance, importation</li><li>gathering in of harvest</li><li>recovery (<i>e.g. of a debt, payment etc.</i>)</li><li>going or coming</li><li>escape, safe return</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱemh₂-</i> (“to exert oneself, get tired”), the same root as <i>κάμνω</i> (kámnō) and <i>κομέω</i> (koméō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) small logs</li><li>metrical phrase</li><li>short clause</li></ol><p>From <i>κόμμα</i> (kómma, “stamp, coinage; piece”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade, traffic, commerce</li><li>customs, custom house duty</li><li>proceeds, revenue</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>commercium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beautifier, embellisher</li><li>hairdresser</li><li>valet de chambre</li></ol><p><i>κομμόω</i> (kommóō, “to beautify, embellish”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embellish, adorn</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Unconvincing attempts have been made to link the word to similar words such as <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “order; mode, fashion”) and <i>κομψός</i> (kompsós, “refined, elegant; clever”). Solmsen's derivation from an earlier <i>&#42;κομώ</i> (&#42;komṓ), from <i>κομέω</i> (koméō, “to take care of, tend to”), as well as Bailly's connection to <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to strike, cut”), are rather tenuous. In view of the word's culture-based semantic category and lack of solid internal derivation, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek borrowing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nice, refined, elegant, gentlemanly</li><li>smart, clever, ingenious, subtle</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) nice, good, pleasant</li></ol><p>The old connection with Lithuanian <i>švánkus</i> (“decent, reasonable”), as if from <i>&#42;kuonkʷ-so-</i>, is meaningless, since there is no suffix <i>-so</i>. This objection also excludes connection with <i>κομέω</i> (koméō, “to take care”) and <i>κομμόομαι</i> (kommóomai, “to adorn”). The word can hardly be Indo-European, so Pre-Greek origin is probable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-haired</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust, as stirred up by men's feet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sand; soil</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ashes</li></ol><li>a fine powder, sprinkled over wrestlers' bodies after being oiled, to make them more easily grasped by the opponent</li><li>powdered lime; plaster; stucco</li><li>any kind of lyes or alkaline carbonates made from ashes and used for washing</li></ol><p>From <i>κόνῐς</i> (kónĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>κονῐ́ᾱ</i> (konĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marjoram (<i>Origanum majorana</i>)</li><li>oregano (<i>Origanum vulgare</i>)</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ζωμίλη</i> (zōmílē) and <i>μαρίλη</i> (marílē); further unclear. Furnée compares <i>γονώνη</i> (gonṓnē, “oregano”), and perhaps <i>γονής</i> (gonḗs, “narcissus”), with variation <i>γ/κ</i>. The suffix <i>-ῑ́λη</i> is typical of Pre-Greek words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) nit, egg of a flea, louse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-níd-</i>. Cognates include Old Armenian <i>անիծ</i> (anic), Albanian <i>thëri</i>, Old English <i>hnitu</i> (English <i>nit</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make dusty, cover with clouds of dust</li><li>(figurative,&#32;passive voice) to be in great haste</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be sprinkled as with dust</li></ol><p>From <i>κόνις</i> (kónis, “dust”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-fed, fat</li><li>vigorous, active</li></ol><p>In the sense of "vigorous, active", probably related to <i>ἐγκονέω</i> (enkonéō, “to be quick and active”). It is unknown whether the glosses "well-fed, fat" refer to a different word; they could be different suggestions for an obscure passage.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud of dust</li><li>dirt, sweepings</li><li>dirty fellow</li></ol><p>From <i>κόνις</i> (kónis, “dust”) +&lrm; <i>ὄρνυμι</i> (órnumi, “to raise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Kontakion, a type of Byzantine hymn</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱent-</i>der.Ancient Greek <i>κοντός</i> (kontós)Ancient Greek <i>κόνταξ</i> (kóntax)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-yós</i>der.Ancient Greek <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)?Ancient Greek <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)Ancient Greek <i>κοντάκιον</i></p><p>From <i>κόνταξ</i> (kóntax, “rod, stick”, referring to the pole around which the scroll [with the hymn on it] was wound) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) spear</li></ol><p>From <i>κοντός</i> (kontós, “pole; pike; goad”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>late&#32;Epic</i>) spear-case</li></ol><p>From <i>κοντᾰ́ρῐον</i> (kontắrĭon, “spear”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case; box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutter, a kind of ship</li></ol><p>Nominalized from the <i>κόντουρος</i> (kóntouros, epithet for a post-horse).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pole</li><li>pike</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) (<i>in plural</i>): crutch</li><li>goad</li></ol><p>From the o-grade <b>κοντ</b>&#8209; of the root <b>κεντ</b>&#8209; underlying <i>κεντέω, -ῶ</i> (kentéō, -ô, “prick”), <i>κέντρον</i> (kéntron, “centre”). The suffix is <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal adjective and noun suffix).</p><p>The Hellenistic Koine "short" sense is derived from the ancient "pole" sense, which referred to shorter kinds of spears, poles, hence "(short) pole" > "shortness". Compare <i>κοντοφόρος</i> (kontophóros, “carrying a (short) pole”) in contrast to <i>δορῠφόρος</i> (dorŭphóros, “carrying a (long) spear”).</p><p>Not related to <i>ἀκόντιον</i> (akóntion, “javelin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>κόπαιον</i> (kópaion)</li></ol><p>From <i>κοπᾰ́ς</i> (kopắs, “pruned, lopped”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get tired</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to beat, strike, cut”) + <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting in pieces</li><li>slaughter</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chopper, cleaver</li><li>broad, curved knife used by Thessalians</li><li>(<i>at Sparta</i>) feast given on certain festivals to strangers</li></ol><p>From <i>κοπή</i> (kopḗ, “cutting, slaughter”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be tired, grow weary</li><li>to work hard, toil</li></ol><p>From <i>κοπῐ́ᾱ</i> (kopĭ́ā, “rest from toil”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō), from <i>κόπος</i> (kópos, “beating, suffering, work”) from <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to strike”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place where dung is put</li></ol><p>From <i>κόπρος</i> (kópros, “dung”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dung-eating</li></ol><p>From <i>κόπρος</i> (kópros, “dung, ordure”) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raven-black</li></ol><p>From <i>κόραξ</i> (kórax, “raven”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coral</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a univerbation of <i>κόρη</i> (kórē, “girl, maiden”) +&lrm; <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “salt; sea”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix), literally “daughter of the sea”, which would be a calque of a similar Indian expression. However, Lewy suggests a Semitic origin, comparing this word with Hebrew <i>גּוֹרָל</i> (gōrāl, “small pebble”) and Arabic جَرَل (jaral, “small stone”), and Beekes prefers this latter theory, implicitly considering the former as folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to satisfy, satiate</li></ol><p>Ultimately may be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₃-</i> (“to feed, nourish, grow”) (whose relation to <i>&#42;ḱer-</i> (“to grow”), whence Latin <i>creō</i> (“to procreate”), is unclear) + <i>-νῡμι</i> (-nūmi). Cognate with Lithuanian <i>šérti</i> (“to feed”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъrmъ</i> (“fodder”), and Armenian <i>սեր</i> (ser, “origin; offspring; gender”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweep out</li></ol><p>An iterative deverbative verb, lacking a good etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κορίαννον</i> (koríannon, “coriander”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coriander (<i>Coriandrum sativum</i>)</li><li>ring worn on the forefinger</li></ol><p>Szemerényi points to the comparison with Akkadian <i>𒌑𒄷𒌷𒌝</i> (<sup>ú</sup>ḫurium; ḫuriʾānu), but this does not explain the <i>-d-</i> in Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀒𐀪𐀊𐀈𐀜</i> (ko-ri-ja-do-no /⁠koriʰadnon⁠/) / <i>𐀒𐀪𐁀𐀅𐀙</i> (ko-ri-a2-da-na /⁠koriʰadna⁠/). Beekes supposes that the cluster <i>-dn-</i> implies a Pre-Greek origin and hypothesizes that <i>&#42;koriaⁿdro-</i> may have been dissimilated from <i>&#42;koriaⁿdno-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fondle, caress, coax</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to whitewash (hide mistakes), to be hypocritical</li></ol><p>From <i>κόρη</i> (kórē, “girl”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζομαι</i> (-ízomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young raven</li><li>kind of fish found in the Nile</li></ol><p>From <i>κόραξ</i> (kórax, “raven”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raven-black</li></ol><p>From <i>κόραξ</i> (kórax, “raven, crow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corban, gift or votive offering for the service of God</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>קָרְבָּן</i> (qɔrbɔn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>club; cudgel</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) bump; swelling</li><li>wrapping for the head; headdress</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>κᾰνθῡ́λη</i> (kănthū́lē), <i>κοτῠ́λη</i> (kotŭ́lē) and <i>σχενδῡ́λη</i> (skhendū́lē). Güntert suggests a cross of <i>κόνδῠλος</i> (kóndŭlos, “swelling”) with <i>κορῠ́νη</i> (korŭ́nē, “club; cudgel”) but Beekes claims this is is incorrect, on the basis that the prothetic <i>σ-</i> (s-) and the suffix <i>-ῡλ-</i> (-ūl-) lead to a Pre-Greek origin. Compare also Georgian <i>კორძი</i> (ḳorʒi), perhaps from <i>&#42;კორდი</i> (&#42;ḳordi) via expressive affrication.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>newt, eft (<i>Lissotriton vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>κορδῡ́λη</i> (kordū́lē, “swelling”), after the crest on the back. According to Beekes, probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) to act like a person from Corinth</li><li>(<i>slang</i>) to consort with prostitutes (<i>since Corinth was famous in antiquity for its prostitutes</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Κόρινθος</i> (Kórinthos, “Corinth”) + <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō) + <i>-ομαι</i> (-omai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) trunk of a tree (with the boughs lopped off)</li><li>log of timber</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kormós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”), the same root of <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) fibula</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>cornūcōpia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modeler of small figures</li></ol><p>From <i>κόρος</i> (kóros, “puppet, doll”) +&lrm; <i>πλάσσω</i> (plássō, “to model”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thong</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>corrigia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with quivering helmet</li></ol><p><i>κόρυς</i> (kórus, “helmet”) +&lrm; <i>αἰόλλω</i> (aióllō, “to move quickly back and forth”) from <i>αἰόλος</i> (aiólos, “quick”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>top of the head, crown</li><li>mountain peak</li><li>top, apex</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) vertex of a triangle</li><li>the best thing, excellence</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a Pre-Greek root common to <i>κόρυμβος</i> (kórumbos, “tip of a ship or mountain”) and <i>κόρυς</i> (kórus, “helmet”), based on κορυφ-/κορυμβ- alternation with the aforementioned <i>κόρυμβος</i> (kórumbos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head man, chief, leader</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) coryphaeus (conductor of the dramatic chorus)</li><li>(<i>attributive</i>) at the top</li></ol><p>From <i>κορυφή</i> (koruphḗ) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subterranean channel; culvert</li></ol><p>Dittenberger refers to <i>γόργῡρᾰ</i> (górgūră, “sewer; drain”) and <i>κορκόδρῠᾰ</i> (korkódrŭă, “drain”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something crook-beaked or curved</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a cow with crumpled horns</li></ol><li>anything curved or bent</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a wreath or garland</li><li>a curved line or stroke, flourish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) end, completion</li><li>the mark of crasis or apostrophe, i.e. a breathing mark in the middle of a word, which only appears in words formed by crasis, such as in <i>ταὐτός</i> (tautós)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>κορωνός</i> (korōnós, “curved”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buck's horn plantain (<i>Plantago coronopus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κορωνός</i> (korōnós, “curved, crooked”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curved, crooked</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to turn, bend”). Cognates include Latin <i>curvus</i>, Old Irish <i>cruind</i>, and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krivъ</i> (Russian <i>кривой</i> (krivoj)). See also <i>κορώνη</i> (korṓnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mace; club shod with iron for fighting</li><li>shepherd's staff</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) knobby bud or shoot</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) penis</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>κόρῠς</i> (kórŭs, “helmet”), referring to the thick end of the weapon. According to Beekes, the word is of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to equip with a helmet</li><li>to arm, equip (<i>generally</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κόρυς</i> (kórus, “helmet”) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō); more formally from */korútʰ-jɔː/ with */tʰj/ becoming /ss/ by regular sound change.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dolphinfish (<i>Coryphaena hippurus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κορῠφή</i> (korŭphḗ, “top of the head; crown”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of seabird, perhaps a shearwater</li><li>crow</li><li>anything curved, especially a door handle</li><li>the tip of a bow, on which the string is hooked</li><li>the curved stern of a ship, especially its crown (ornamental top)</li><li>the tip of a plow-beam, upon which the yoke was attached</li><li>coronoid process of the ulna, apophysis (the part of a bone where the tendon is attached)</li><li>end, tip, point</li><li>nightingale</li><li>a type of crown</li><li>culmination of something, especially of a festival</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱor-u-</i> (“crow, raven”), apparently of imitative origin and with similar formations across multiple Indo-European branches; see Latin <i>cornīx</i> (“crow”) for more.</p><p>The senses referring to curved objects have been separated by linguists in the past from the "crow" sense, though it is likely that they are from the same etymological origin, with semantic extension "shaped like a crow's beak or claws" > "curved object".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, to arrange</li><li>to adorn</li></ol><p>From <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a commander</li></ol><p>From <i>κοσμέω</i> (kosméō, “order”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orderer, director</li><li>adorner</li><li>one who cleans temple statues</li></ol><p>From <i>κοσμέω</i> (kosméō, “to order, arrange”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled in arranging</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;f) the art of dress and ornament</li></ol><p>From <i>κόσμησις</i> (kósmēsis, “an adorning”) +&lrm; <i>-κός</i> (-kós), from <i>κοσμέω</i> (kosméō, “I adorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the world, of the universe</li><li>of this world, earthly, worldly</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) secular</li></ol><p>From <i>κόσμ(ος)</i> (kósm(os)) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orderliness</li></ol><p><i>κόσμιο(ς)</i> (kósmio(s)) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation or origin of the world</li></ol><p>From <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos) + the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>describing the world</li></ol><p>From <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “world”) +&lrm; <i>γρᾰ́φω</i> (grắphō, “I write”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord of the world, cosmocrat</li></ol><p>From <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “universe”) +&lrm; <i>κράτωρ</i> (krátōr, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>κοσμοπολῑ́της</i> (kosmopolī́tēs, “citizen of the world”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen of the world</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos, “world”) +&lrm; <i>πολίτης</i> (polítēs, “citizen”). Coined by 4th-century BC Greek Cynic philosopher Diogenes of Sinope.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>topknot</li><li>shepherd's coat</li></ol><p>Lewy compares Akkadian <i>𒆪𒍦𒁍</i> (ku-zip-pu /⁠kuzippu, guzippu⁠/, “cloak”), Arabic كُرْسُف (kursuf, “cotton”). Pre-Greek origin is likely, given the prenasalization and similar words like <i>κόσυμβος</i> (kósumbos, “hairnet”), <i>κότθυβος</i> (kótthubos, “piece of military equipment”) and <i>κοσυβάτας</i> (kosubátas, “sacrificer”). This does not exclude that the word is found in Semitic too, since it may be an old Kulturwort.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold a grudge [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘against’</small>]</li><li>to be angry</li></ol><p>From <i>κότος</i> (kótos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ)</li><li>hairstyle with long hair on the forehead</li></ol><p>Connection with <i>κοτύλη</i> (kotúlē, “bowl, dish”) is a mere guess. Furnée connects this word with <i>σκύτη</i> (skútē, “head”); the geminate is not expressive, but rather points to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small vessel or cup</li><li>kind of cake</li><li>pit used for sacrificing to Earth</li></ol><p>From <i>κοτῠ́λη</i> (kotŭ́lē, “vessel, cup”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any cup shaped hollow or cavity</li><li>hollow of a cup</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) socket of the hip joint</li><li>navelwort (<i>Umbilicus rupestris</i>)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) suckers on the arms of the octopus</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cotyledons, foetal and uterine vascular connections</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>κοτύλη</i> (kotúlē, “cup”) or the same Pre-Greek source. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. Suffix missing.</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small vessel; cup</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) socket of a joint</li><li>liquid measure; nearly a half-pint</li><li>hollow of the hand</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cymbals</li></ol><p>Furnée asserts a relation to Latin <i>catīnus</i> (“flat dish”), claiming that the deviation in vowel and formation suggests that they are independent loans from a third party, and adducing <i>κόνδῠ</i> (kóndŭ, “cup”) and <i>κονδῠ́λιον</i> (kondŭ́lion), with the observation that <i>-υλη</i> (-ulē) is a well-known suffix in Pre-Greek.</p><p>A widespread wanderword is also probable, given Sanskrit <i>कुन्तल</i> (kuntala, “drinking-cup”), <i>कन्दु</i> (kandu, “vessel”), <i>करताल</i> (karatāla, “cymbals”), and other nearby forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toper, drunkard</li></ol><p>From <i>κοτῠ́λη</i> (kotŭ́lē, “vessel; cup”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodlouse</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Perhaps related to Greek <i>κουβάρι</i> (kouvári, “clew”) and sharing the meaning of "something that rolls itself together".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>κολεός</i> (koleós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cropping of the hair</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) cropping, lopping, shearing of sheep</li><li>lock of hair</li><li>wool shorn, fleece</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) slips of wood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;korsā́</i>, a primary verbal noun from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kers-</i> (“to shave”), an <i>s</i>-extension of <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”), the same root of <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to cut, shear, crop”), <i>κόρση</i> (kórsē, “temple (of forehead)”), and <i>κορμός</i> (kormós, “trunk of a tree”). Direct cognates include Hittite (karš-ᶻᶦ, “to cut off”), Tocharian A <i>kärs-</i> (“to know, understand”), Tocharian B <i>kärst-</i> (“to cut off, destroy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wedded</li><li>nuptial, bridal</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κοῦρος</i> (koûros), <i>κόρος</i> (kóros, “young man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shears, used for removing the fleece from sheep</li></ol><p>From <i>κουρά</i> (kourá, “wool shorn, fleece”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barbershop, barber's shop</li></ol><p>From <i>κουρά</i> (kourá, “cropping of the hair”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rear as a child</li></ol><p>From <i>κουροτρόφος</i> (kourotróphos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nurturing, mothering, nursing, nourishing, child-rearing</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) divine epithet meaning “child-rearing”</li></ol><p>From <i>κοῦρος</i> (koûros) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be light, of low weight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive,&#32;of pain) to be lightened, alleviated, reduced, assuaged</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make light</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift, elevate, raise</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with genitive) to lighten of, relieve of</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to relieve, alleviate, assuage</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κοῦφος</i> (koûphos, “light, nimble”) +&lrm; <i>-ί̆ζω</i> (-í̆zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk lightly</li></ol><p>From <i>κουφολόγος</i> (kouphológos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light talking, light talk</li></ol><p>From <i>κουφολόγος</i> (kouphológos) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lightly talking</li></ol><p>From <i>κοῦφος</i> (koûphos) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snail with a spiral shell</li><li>anything twisted spirally:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>automation in form of snail</li><li>reel, spool, roller</li><li>screw, especially Archimedes screw</li><li>spiral stair</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) part of a surgical machine</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κόχλος</i> (kókhlos, “shellfish with a spiral shell”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the doum palm</li><li>made of its fibre</li></ol><p>From <i>κοῦκι</i> (koûki, “doum palm”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shearing</li><li>haircut</li></ol><p><i>κουρεύω</i> (koureúō, “cut hair, shear”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>κόρη</i> (kórē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hollow, mostly as an epithet of ships</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>κοίλη ναῦς</i>) the hollow or hold of a ship</li><li>roomy</li><li>empty</li></ol><li>(<i>of places</i>) lying in a hollow, or forming a hollow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>lying between cliffs</li></ol><li>sea swell</li><li>plate</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of the voice) hollow</li><li>(<i>neuter substantive</i>) a hollow, cavity</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) flanks</li></ol><li>concave</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱowilos</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱewH-</i> (“hollow”) (which may be identical to <i>&#42;ḱewh₁-</i> (“to swell”)). Cognates include Latin <i>cavus</i> (“cave”), Middle Irish <i>cúa</i> (“hollow”), Old English <i>hol</i> (English <i>hole</i>, <i>hollow</i>), Albanian <i>thellë</i> (“deep”), and Sanskrit <i>शून्य</i> (śūnyá, “zero, empty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ποῖος</i> (poîos)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κοῖος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Coeus, son of Uranus and Gaia; father of Leto</li><li>a river in Messenia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resting place</li><li>bed</li><li>sleep</li></ol><p>From <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai, “to lie down”), unrelated to Latin <i>coitus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doum palm (<i>Hyphaene thebaica</i>)</li></ol><p>Foreign word, perhaps of Egyptian origin, or related to <i>κόϊξ</i> (kóïx, “doum palm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) cask, barrel, vat</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of beer made of barley, made by Egyptians, Iberians and Bretons</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>κόρος</i> (kóros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light, not heavy, nimble</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin; Beekes notes that the full grade of the stem and barytonesis of the word suggest that it was originally a noun and not an adjective, though he leaves the origin open without mentioning any potential comparanda. Per Trubachev, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewbʰ-</i> (“to duck, to bow”) attested in <i>κουφάριν</i> (kouphárin, “carcass”), Old East Slavic <i>кубъ</i> (kubŭ, “drinking vessel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couch; mattress; pallet</li></ol><p>From Ancient Macedonian <i>&#42;γράβος</i> (grabos, “oak”), attested via <i>γράβιον</i> (grabion, “torch”); the Macedonian term appears to be of substrate origin. Compare Latin <i>grabātus</i> (“cot; pallet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) quivering spray at the end of a branch, especially of fig trees</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) branch, fig-branch</li><li>fig tree</li><li>diseased formation of small shoots in trees</li><li>scenic contrivance for exhibiting actors in comedy, sort of crane</li></ol><p>Unknown. It is uncertain whether this word is the origin of <i>κραδάω</i> (kradáō, “to swing, brandish”) or if it is the opposite. Traditionally connected to Latin <i>cardō</i> (“hinge, pivot”), but this remains hypothetical.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scream, shriek, cry, bawl, shout</li><li>to call, clamour for</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>κρώζω</i> (krṓzō, “I croak”) and <i>κραυγή</i> (kraugḗ, “cry”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabbage</li></ol><p>Usually connected with <i>κράμβος</i> (krámbos, “dry; blight in grapes, when they shrivel”), assuming that the plant was named after its shrunken leaves, but this is rejected by Beekes, who leaves the origin open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry</li><li>loud, ringing (of sound)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blight in grapes, when they shrivel before they are ripe</li></ol><p>The word has been compared with Old High German <i>(h)rimfan</i> (“to wrinkle, curb”). However, Furnée compares <i>κραῦρος</i> (kraûros, “fragile, frail”), assuming it is a form without prenasalization and with "υ" for "β". He also adduces <i>κόμβος</i> (kómbos, “ball, swlling; dry, parched”) and perhaps <i>κράβυζος</i> (krábuzos, “kind of shellfish”). Therefore the word is from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cornel (<i>Cornus mas</i>)</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krno-</i>, a root shared also by Latin <i>cornus</i> (“cornel”) and possibly Lithuanian <i>Kirnis</i> (“a god who protects cherries”). A Mediterranean substrate origin is also possible, which has to be assumed for <i>κέρασος</i> (kérasos, “cherry tree”) in any case.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helmet</li></ol><p>A nominal formation related to <i>κᾰ́ρᾱ</i> (kắrā, “head”) and <i>κρᾱνῐ́ον</i> (krānĭ́on, “cranium”), albeit with unclear morphology due to the form of the word precluding a laryngeal otherwise found in its relatives. Probably not related to <i>κάρυον</i> (káruon, “nut”) or <i>κραναός</i> (kranaós, “rocky”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edge, border, skirt, especially of cloth</li><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) skirts or edge of a country</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) fimbria, affection of the uvula</li></ol><p>Unknown. Traditionally analyzed as an old compound of <i>κάρᾱ</i> (kárā, “head”) (in the form <i>κράσ-</i>), with a faded second member <i>πέδον</i> (pédon, “plain, ground”), for which Sanskrit <i>द्रुपद</i> (drupada, “wooden pillar, post”) is compared. The first member would point to an original meaning "upper border", according to Risch. However, Nussbaum is very sceptical; there is no indication whatsoever that it would contain the word for "head".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greenfodder</li></ol><p>From <i>γράω</i> (gráō, “to gnaw, eat”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐς</i> (-tĭs, nominal suffix), but influenced by another unknown word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawthorn (<i>Crataegus heldreichii</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>κρᾰτῠ́ς</i> (krătŭ́s, “strong, mighty”) + the same <i>αἰγ-</i> (aig-) of <i>αἰγίλωψ</i> (aigílōps).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female ruler, potentate</li></ol><p>From <i>κρᾰτέω</i> (krătéō, “to rule”) +&lrm; <i>-τειρᾰ</i> (-teiră, feminine agent noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>might, power, dominion</li><li>accession (of an emperor, etc.)</li><li>prevalence, predominance</li><li>possession</li><li>(<i>law</i>) title to possession</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) retention</li><li>holding firm, steadying</li></ol><p>From <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>might; strength</li><li>act of strength, act of valour</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) acts of violence</li><li>dominion; power</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krét-os</i>, from <i>&#42;kret-</i> (“strength; intelligence”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>क्रतु</i> (krátu), Avestan <i>𐬑𐬭𐬀𐬙𐬎-</i> (xratu-), and Old English <i>heard</i> (“hard”) (whence English <i>hard</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ruler, potentate</li></ol><p>From <i>κρᾰτέω</i> (krătéō, “to rule”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, masculine agent nouns suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κρέα</i> (kréa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flesh, meat</li><li>carcass, body</li></ol><p>Earlier Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kréwas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kréwh₂s</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>क्रविस्</i> (kravís), Latin <i>cruor</i> (Italian <i>crudo</i>), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kry</i>, and Old English <i>hrǣw</i> (English <i>raw</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave</li><li>(<i>of an instrument</i>) to play</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to create a noise, song</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kreḱ-</i> (“to strike, beat”). Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kresati</i> (“to strike fire”), Lithuanian <i>krešė́ti</i> (“to clot”), and perhaps Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hragilą</i> (“garment”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castanets, clappers</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-αλ-</i> is found also in <i>κρόταλον</i> (krótalon), <i>ῥόπαλον</i> (rhópalon) and other loanwords. Connection with Latin <i>crepō</i> (“to rattle, creak”), Lithuanian <i>skrebeti</i> (“to rustle”) and Russian <i>кропота́ть</i> (kropotátʹ, “to grumble”) is impossible, as Greek "β" cannot be due to the proceeding nasal. This nasal rather points to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long-legged bird, perhaps:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>corncrake (<i>Crex crex</i>)</li><li>ruff (<i>Philomachus pugnax</i>)</li></ol><li>name of ill omen to the newly married</li><li>noisy braggart</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European root (of possible onomatopoeic origin) common to Sanskrit <i>कृकर</i> (kṛkara, a kind of partridge), Russian <i>кре́чет</i> (kréčet, “gyrfalcon”), Old Prussian <i>kerko</i> (“diver, loon”), and Irish <i>cearc</i> (“hen”). The word has also been compared with <i>κερκάς</i> (kerkás, “crake”), <i>κερκιθαλίς</i> (kerkithalís, “stork”) and <i>κέρκνος</i> (kérknos, “hawk or cock”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “strength”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homeric form of <i>κρείων</i> (kreíōn)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κρέων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Creon</li></ol><p>From <i>κρέων</i> (kréōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good, useful, agreeable</li><li>(<i>by a misunderstanding of Homer</i>) true, real</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) good, serviceable</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a woman</i>) honest</li></ol></ol><p>Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A veil or headscarf worn by women, keeping together three items of ornamental hair binding: <i>ἄμπυξ</i> (ámpux, “frontlet”), <i>κεκρύφαλος</i> (kekrúphalos, “hairnet, snood”) and <i>ἀναδέσμη</i> (anadésmē, “headband”)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) the edge or the top of city walls</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) the top covering of a wine or storage vessel</li></ol><p><i>κράς</i> (krás, “head”, poetic form of <i>κᾰ́ρᾱ</i> (kắrā)) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to tie”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well, spring, fountain</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;poetic) waters</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kr̥s-neh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;kres-</i> (“spring, fount”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hraznō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covered earthen vessel, wherein bread was baked by putting hot embers round it</li><li>funnel-shaped vessel, used for drawing water</li><li>underground channel or vaulted passage, in irrigation works</li><li>hollow, cavern in a rock</li></ol><p>A technical loan of unknown origin. Walde suggested a connection with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hlaibaz</i> (“loaf”), while Lewy proposed a Semitic origin; both proposals are unconvincing. Furnée lists the words among examples of the interchange of "ρ" and "λ" in Pre-Greek words, which is more probable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>πέλτη</i> (péltē, “Thracian shield”), <i>ἀσπίς</i> (aspís, “shield”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of the cluster -γδ.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of or from barley</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῑθή</i> (krīthḗ, “barley”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a reduplicated form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to bend, turn”). However, due to the relation with <i>κίρκος</i> (kírkos, “ring”), whose root structure cannot be smoothly reconstructed back to Proto-Indo-European, Beekes is skeptical of current explanations, and refrains from assigning any etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decision</li><li>decree</li><li>judgment</li><li>sentence, condemnation</li><li>lawsuit</li></ol><p>From <i>κρίνω</i> (krínō, “to judge”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coarse barley meal</li><li>coarse loaf</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) crumbs</li></ol><p>Unexplained. The connection with <i>κρῖ</i> (krî, “barley”) and <i>κριθή</i> (krithḗ, “barley”) is formally unwarranted and a derivation from <i>κρίνω</i> (krínō, “to separate”) does not seem preferable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of lilies</li></ol><p>From <i>κρίνον</i> (krínon, “lily”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lily</li></ol><p>Unknown; presumedly of foreign or substrate origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to separate, divide, part, distinguish between two things or people or among a group of things or people</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to order, arrange</li><li>to inquire, investigate</li><li>to select, choose, prefer</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to decide a dispute or contest, with accusative of the contest or dispute, or accusative of a person involved in the contest or dispute; (<i>intransitive</i>) to pass judgement, come to a decision</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to have a contest decided</li><li>(<i>middle voice&#32;and&#32;passive voice</i>) to contend, dispute, quarrel</li></ol><li>to decide or judge [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘that something’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘does something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> accusative <small>‘that something’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘is something’</small>]</li><li>to discern between good and bad</li><li>to judge, pronounce</li><li>to bring to court, accuse</li><li>to pass sentence on, condemn, criticize</li><li>to secrete</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kríňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kri-n-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;krey-</i> (“to sift, separate”). Cognates include English <i>rinse</i>, Latin <i>cernō</i> (“to separate, discern”), and Welsh <i>gogrynu</i>.</p><p>The root of the verb was originally short, but the Attic&ndash;Ionic&ndash;Doric present tense <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō) has a long <i>ῑ</i> (ī) because the vowel of the root was compensatorily lengthened after the shortening of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ňň</i>, and the aorist <i>ἔκρῑνᾰ</i> (ékrīnă) has long <i>ῑ</i> (ī) because of compensatory lengthening after the loss of the aorist tense-marker /s/ in the original form /ekrin-sa/. The Aeolic present <i>κρῐ́ννω</i> (krĭ́nnō) and aorist <i>ἔκρῐννε</i> (ékrĭnne) have a short <i>ῐ</i> (ĭ) and doubled <i>νν</i> (nn) because the consonant <i>ν</i> (n) was compensatorily lengthened instead of the vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a kind of vessel</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly a contracted variant of <i>κρεῖον</i> (kreîon, “meat-tray”), from <i>κρέας</i> (kréas, “meat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decision; determination; judgment</li><li>trial; sentence; accusation</li><li>quarrel; dispute</li><li>justice</li><li>turning point or decisive moment</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō, “to decide”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) terebinth</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested as a gloss in Hesychius. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀑𐀲𐀜</i> (ki-ta-no).</p><p>This word is glossed with <i>τέρμινθος</i> (términthos), which is usually interpreted as meaning 'terebinth', a kind of tree, but this identification may be questioned. Hescyhius himself states of τέρμινθος that it is a 'plant resembling flax, from which the Athenians weave their nets'. This equation with 'flax' would yield potentional Semitic cognates for κρίτανος, like Classical Syriac <i>ܟܬܢܐ</i> (kettānā) and Akkadian <i>𒃰</i> (kitûm, “flax”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accomplish, perform, fulfill</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of shrimp, mantis shrimp (<i>Squilla mantis</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin and not related to Sanskrit <i>शृङ्ग</i> (śṛṅga, “horn”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swing, shake, quiver</li><li>(<i>of trees</i>) to suffer from blight</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>Uncertain. It is probable that this verb is a denominative, so an original meaning "swinging" may be assumed for <i>κράδη</i> (krádē). This fits well with the meanings "crown of a tree" and "suspension-machine". Alternatively, this verb could be a zero grade iterative, derived from a lost primary verb, with <i>κράδη</i> (krádē) as back-formation. The word <i>κόρδαξ</i> (kórdax, “kind of dance”) is unrelated. The connection with Latin <i>cardō</i> (“hinge”) remains hypothetical.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>κᾰρδῐ́ᾱ</i> (kărdĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>κραίνω</i> (kraínō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bout of drinking</li><li>intoxication, inebriation</li><li>hangover</li></ol><p>From a root <i>κραιπ-</i> (kraip-) + a suffix <i>-άλη</i> (-álē) also found in <i>ᾰ̓γκᾰ́λη</i> (ănkắlē, “bent arm”), <i>μᾰσχᾰ́λη</i> (măskhắlē, “armpit”), and <i>σκῠτάλη</i> (skŭtálē, “cudgel”), with further origin of the root unclear. It has been connected with <i>κραιπνός</i> (kraipnós, “swift, rushing”), assuming an <i>ν-λ</i> interchange. Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin, based on the Latin borrowing of this term, <i>crāpula</i>, demonstrating <i>αι-ᾱ</i> variation in the first vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rapid, rushing</li><li>hasty, rash</li></ol><p>Unknown, possibly of Pre-Greek origin, with pre-form <i>*krapʸ-n-</i>. See also <i>κραιπᾰ́λη</i> (kraipắlē, “hangover”), to which it has been connected.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rapidly, hastily</li><li>rashly</li></ol><p>From <i>κραιπνός</i> (kraipnós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of an alloy</li></ol><p>From <i>κρᾶμα</i> (krâma, “alloy”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of cornel</li></ol><p>From <i>κράνον</i> (kránon, “cornel”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the upper part of the head, the cranium</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) the skull</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) the head</li></ol><li>a headache</li></ol><p>From the old oblique stem, <i>&#42;κρᾱν-</i> (&#42;krān-), of <i>κᾰ́ρη</i> (kắrē, “Homeric form of <i>κᾰ́ρᾱ</i> (kắrā, “the head”)”) + <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) rocky, rugged</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) turtle shell</li><li>rough, stinging</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winecup</li><li>wine</li></ol><p>From <i>κρᾶσις</i> (krâsis, “mixture”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κρᾰτερᾰ́</i> (krăterắ)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>κρᾰτερέ</i> (krăteré)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule, command</li><li>to conquer, prevail, gain the upper hand</li><li>to seize, hold</li></ol><p>From <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power, might”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixing bowl for wine</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) messenger who bears an ode</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the Crater (constellation)</li><li>cup-shaped basin</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>crater of a volcano</li></ol></ol><p>From a zero grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i>, whence also <i>κεράννυμι</i> (keránnumi, “to mix”), + <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lady's thumb (<i>Persicaria maculosa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κραταιός</i> (krataiós, “strong, mighty”) +&lrm; <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “fruit, seed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mighty (very strong, possessing might)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kret-</i>, the root of <i>κρατερός</i> (kraterós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>καρτερός</i> (karterós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having strong hooves or claws</li></ol><p>From <i>κρᾰτερός</i> (krăterós) +&lrm; <i>ὄνῠξ</i> (ónŭx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) strongly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō)</li></ol><p>From <i>κράτος</i> (krátos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strengthen</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become strong</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to harden</li><li>to rule, govern [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative]</li><li>to become master, get possession [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold, exercise (<i>power</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ply or throw (<i>a missile</i>) stoutly</li></ol><p>From <i>κράτος</i> (krátos) +&lrm; <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) strong; mighty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krétus</i>, from <i>&#42;kret-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a loud cry, scream, shout</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kraw-g-</i> (“to cry”) of imitative origin, similar to <i>κράζω</i> (krázō) and <i>κρώζω</i> (krṓzō). Compare Old Norse <i>hraukr</i> (“cormorant”), Old English <i>ġehrūxl</i> (“noise”), Gothic <i>𐌷𐍂𐌿𐌺</i> (hruk, “cry of the rooster”), Lithuanian <i>kraũkti</i> (“to screech”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krukъ</i> (“raven”), Sanskrit <i>क्रोश॑ति</i> (króśati, “to shriek”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brittle, friable, fragile</li></ol><p>A Pre-Greek variant of <i>κράμβος</i> (krámbos, “dry; loud, ringing”), with interchange ϝ/β and prenasalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleshhook to take meat out of the pot</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) hook to seize or drag by</li></ol><p>From <i>κρέας</i> (kréas, “meat”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγρᾱ</i> (ágrā, “hunting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>κρείσσων</i> (kreíssōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler, lord, master</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;krāhōn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱr̥h₂-s-n-</i>, from the root <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“head”). Earlier comparisons with Sanskrit <i>श्री</i> (śrī, “beauty”) are semantically and phonologically problematic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope hung from a hook</li></ol><p>From <i>κρεμάννυμι</i> (kremánnumi, “to hang”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to hang, hang up</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to hang, be hanging, be suspended</li></ol><p>Possibly evolved from an original sense of “weak, flaccid”, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱremh₂-</i> (“to hang”). Beekes postulates that the present form <i>κρέμαμαι</i> (krémamai) and the aorist <i>κρεμάσαι</i> (kremásai) are the oldest formations, and other present stems were all newer innovations. Rix, however, proposes that <i>κρίμνημι</i> (krímnēmi) may represent an old nasal-infix present and that <i>κρεμόω</i> (kremóō) may reflect an old desiderative. According to the philologist Julia Sturm, the verb <i>κρεμάννυμι</i> (kremánnumi) may represent a remodeling of older <i>κρίμνημι</i> (krímnēmi).</p><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>श्रम्</i> (śram, “to be or become weary or tired, be tired of doing anything”), <i>श्रान्त</i> (śrānta, “tired”), as well as possibly Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hramō</i> (“hanging frame”) (though Beekes is skeptical of the Germanic comparison).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κρεμάννυμι</i> (kremánnumi, “to hang up”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κρεμάννυμι</i> (kremánnumi)</li></ol><p>Thematicization of <i>κρεμάννυμι</i> (kremánnumi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cremaster</li><li>stalk by which a grape-cluster hangs</li></ol><p>From <i>κρεμάννυμι</i> (kremánnumi, “to hang”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A larder</li></ol><p>From <i>κρέᾰς</i> (kréăs, “meat”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher's steelyard</li></ol><p>From <i>κρέας</i> (kréas, “flesh, meat”) +&lrm; <i>στάθμη</i> (státhmē, “carpenter's line”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō, “to rule”)</li></ol><p>From <i>κρέτος</i> (krétos, “power, might”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meat-tray</li></ol><p>From <i>κρέας</i> (kréas, “meat”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon). A contracted form <i>κρίον</i> (kríon) is possibly attested in Hesychius.</p><p>See the main entry.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler, lord, master</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;krāhōn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱr̥h₂-s-n-</i>, from the root <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“head”). Earlier comparisons with Sanskrit <i>श्री</i> (śrī, “beauty”) are semantically and phonologically problematic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overhanging bank of a river; edge of a trench</li><li>beetling cliff, crag, precipice</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) (<i>medicine</i>) edges of an ulcer</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) labia</li></ol><p>Traditionally considered an old verbal noun from <i>κρεμάννυμι</i> (kremánnumi, “to hang”), however Beekes finds this impossible and leaves the etymology unexplained. Since a root-final <i>-α-</i> would be expected, and landscape terms are often borrowed from substrate languages, the word is probably Pre-Greek. The suffix <i>-(ᾱ)μν-ο-</i> (-(ā)mn-o-) is also considered Pre-Greek by Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warden of the springs</li><li>(<i>Athenian</i>) official in charge of the κλεψῠ́δρᾱ (klepsŭ́drā, “water clock”)</li><li>(<i>Athenian</i>) a bronze statue of a lion which stood over the spring that fed the κλεψῠ́δρᾱ (klepsŭ́drā, “water clock”)</li></ol><p><i>κρήνη</i> (krḗnē, “well, spring, fountain”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”). Compare <i>Ἰ̄νωποφῠ́λᾰξ</i> (Īnōpophŭ́lăx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) crepidoma, foundation</li></ol><p>From <i>κρηπῑδόω</i> (krēpīdóō, “to furnish with a foundation”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man's high boot, half-boot</li><li>soldier's boot</li><li>shoe-shaped cake</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) groundwork, foundation, basement of a building</li><li>walled edge of a river or canal, quay</li><li>abutment of a bridge</li><li>tiers of seats in a theatre</li><li>oxtongue (<i>Helminthotheca echioides</i>)</li></ol><p>Derived from a nominal base form, like <i>κνημίς</i> (knēmís) and <i>χειρίς</i> (kheirís), or perhaps this word just took over their ending. The technical meaning suggests a loan. The connection with words for shoe, like Lithuanian <i>kurpe</i> (“shoes”) is formally impeccable, but is rejected by Fraenkel. According to Beekes, the suffix <i>-ῖδ-</i> suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flour sieve, bolting sieve</li><li>fine net for fishing</li></ol><p>Isolated words in <i>-έρα</i>, like <i>διφθέρα</i> (diphthéra), <i>ἀσκέρα</i> (askéra), <i>χολέρα</i> (kholéra) and <i>κυσέρη</i> (kusérē), are likely to be loans. Theoretically, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krey-</i> (“to sieve”), the same root of <i>κρίνω</i> (krínō, “to sieve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>κρᾱτήρ</i> (krātḗr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) to act like a person from the island of Crete</li><li>to tell lies, since Cretans were said to be notorious liars</li></ol><p>From <i>Κρήτη</i> (Krḗtē, “Krete”) + <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnashing of teeth</li><li>shriek, screech, squeal</li><li>owl (<i>Athene noctua</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κρίζω</i> (krízō) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) barley</li><li>pustule on the eyelid, stye</li><li>barleycorn (unit of weight)</li><li>penis</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰérsdʰ-o&#47;eh₂-</i>, with cognates including Latin <i>hordeum</i>, Old High German <i>gersta</i>, but there are phonetic difficulties with this. Rather closer are Albanian <i>drithë</i>, Old Armenian <i>գարի</i> (gari), as if from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰr̥yo-</i>. Or perhaps of Proto-Albanian/Illyrian origin, which may explain the vowel. Egyptian origin has also been considered. Beekes, not convinced by any proposal, suggests an old Wanderwort; others have suggested a substrate. He notes that the Epic form points to an original root noun <i>&#42;krītʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Small grain of barley</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ring-shaped, annular.</li></ol><p>From <i>κρίκος</i> (kríkos, “ring”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of purple dye</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Arabic قِرْمِز (qirmiz), ultimately from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kŕ̥miš</i> (“worm”). Compare English <i>kermes</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpreter</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō, “I discern, judge”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, “masculine agent suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means of judging, criterion, test, standard</li><li>court, tribunal</li><li>lawsuit</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῐτήρ</i> (krĭtḗr, “interpreter”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), from <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō, “I discern, judge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>umpire</li><li>judge</li></ol><p>From κρῐ-, the root of <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō, “to decide, judge”), +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Able to judge, discerning, critical</li><li>(Especially as a substantive concerning language): critic, grammarian, academic</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῐτής</i> (krĭtḗs, “judge”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós), from <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō, “to judge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ram</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>battering ram</li><li>the constellation Aries</li></ol><li>sea-monster</li><li>type of mussel</li><li>volute (Ionic architecture)</li><li>type of ship</li><li>part of an irrigation system</li><li>type of chickpea</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Traditionally connected with <i>κέρας</i> (kéras, “horn”). The chickpea meaning possibly relates to Latin <i>cicer</i> (“chickpea”), Old Armenian <i>սիսեռն</i> (siseṙn, “chickpea”). Beekes argues for an original meaning of “curved”, comparing Lithuanian <i>kreivas</i>, Old East Slavic <i>кривъ</i> (krivŭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of crocodile</li></ol><p>From <i>κροκόδῑλος</i> (krokódīlos, “crocodile”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lizard</li><li>crocodile</li><li>a fallacy of the sophists</li></ol><p>Ionic word for "lizard" (common being <i>σαύρα</i> (saúra)), perhaps from <i>κρόκη</i> (krókē, “pebbles”) + <i>δρῖλος</i> (drîlos, “worm”), because crocodiles like resting on flat stones. Typologically compare Sanskrit <i>कृकलास</i> (kṛkalāsa, “lizard, chameleon”), said to be composed of the words for "pebble" and "sit".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) alternative spelling of <i>κροκόδειλος</i> (krokódeilos, “crocodile”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>residuum of the expression of the saffron unguent</li><li>compound drug</li></ol><p>From <i>κρόκος</i> (krókos, “saffron”) +&lrm; <i>μάγμα</i> (mágma, “paste”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a saffron-colored veil or robe</li></ol><p>From <i>κρόκος</i> (krókos, “saffron”) +&lrm; <i>πέπλος</i> (péplos, “veil, robe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flock or nap on woollen cloth, a piece of wool</li></ol><p>From <i>κρέκω</i> (krékō, “weave”, verb).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) fringe, tassel, lappet</li></ol><p>Frisk's suggestion that the expression originally derives from weaving, connecting the word with <i>κρόκη</i> (krókē, “thread of the woof”), is not likely. As he notes, it seems that this word was back formed from <i>κροσσωτός</i> (krossōtós, “tasselled, fringed”), which is attested much earlier and better. The word <i>κροσσωτός</i> (krossōtós) was formed to the original <i>κρόσσαι</i> (króssai, “stepped copings of parapets”) after <i>θυσανωτός</i> (thusanōtós, “tasselled, fringed”) and was transferred from the sphere of architecture to that of tailor-work.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To strike, to beat.</li><li>To clap with the hands, to make a clattering noise.</li><li>To applaud.</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>κρότος</i> (krótos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō). Probably from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;krotéō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krotéyeti</i>, a causative verb from the root <i>&#42;kret-</i> (“to rattle”). The same causative may also be attested in Lithuanian <i>kratýti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tick (<i>Ixodes ricinus</i>)</li><li>castor oil plant (<i>Ricinus communis</i>)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) castor beans</li></ol><p>Probably a Semitic borrowing; compare Classical Syriac <i>ܩܪܕܐ</i> (qerdā, “tick; castor oil plant”), cognate to Arabic قُرَاد (qurād, “tick”), Tigrinya <i>ቍርዲድ</i> (ḳʷərdid), <i>ቈርዳድ</i> (ḳʷärdad, “tick”), Tigre <i>ቀራድ</i> (ḳärad, “tick”), Harari <i>ቁርዱድ</i> (ḳurdud, “tick”). See also <i>κίκι</i> (kíki, “castor oil plant”) instead borrowed from Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring, wellhead, source of water</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) stream, torrent</li><li>watercourse</li><li>spout, nozzle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;krohnós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kros-nós</i>, from <i>&#42;kres-</i> (“spring, fount”); cognate with Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;krāna</i> (> modern Albanian <i>krua</i>). Related to <i>κρήνη</i> (krḗnē, “spring, well, fountain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) played by striking (with a plectron) (<i>of music instruments</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κρουσ-</i> (krous-) (from <i>κρούω</i> (kroúō, “to strike”)) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high wooden shoes, used in Boeotia for treading olives</li></ol><p>Traditionally explained as deriving from <i>κρούω</i> (kroúō, “to stamp, strike”) and <i>πεζός</i> (pezós, “on foot, walking”). However, the variant forms may also point to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, smite</li><li>to clap hands</li><li>to tap</li><li>to strike a string with a plectron</li><li>to knock at the door on the outside</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to back water</li><li>to fly backwards</li></ol></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krows-</i> (“push, bump, strike, break”), zero grade <i>&#42;krus-</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krušiti</i>, whence Russian <i>крушить</i> (krušitʹ, “to destroy, shatter”) and Czech <i>krušný</i> (“hard, strenuous, grim”), as well as Latvian <i>krausēt</i> and Lithuanian <i>kraušyti</i> (“to stamp (off)”), Lithuanian <i>krušti</i> (“to smash, crash, grind, fuck”). Compare Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;krušāˀ</i>, whence Latvian <i>krusa</i> (“hail”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krъxà</i> (“lump”), in turn leading to Russian <i>кроха</i> (kroxa, “morsel, crumb, little child”), and Gothic <i>𐌺𐍂𐌹𐌿𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (kriustan, “to gnash, grind”), perhaps a Balto-Slavic loan word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beat, stroke</li><li>sound produced by striking stringed instruments, note</li></ol><p>From <i>κρούω</i> (kroúō, “I beat, I strike”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;figurative</i>) icy, chilling</li><li>(<i>literal</i>) icy cold</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύος</i> (krúos) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frost</li></ol><p>See <i>κρύος</i> (krúos, “ice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret; clandestine</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῠπτός</i> (krŭptós, “hidden”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “adjective forming suffix”), from <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must hide</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, “a suffix forming masculine verbal adjectives of necessity”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a crypt, vault</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “to hide”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concealed, hidden, secret</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “I conceal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dungeon, crypt</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide, conceal”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, “a suffix added to verb stems to form masculine agent nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dungeon</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>convenient for concealing</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριος</i> (-tḗrios, “adjective-forming suffix added to verb stems”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>convenient for concealing</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριος</i> (-tḗrios, “adjective-forming suffix added to verb stems”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hide and seek</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “hide, conceal”) +&lrm; <i>-ίνδα</i> (-índa)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly; in secret</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῠπτᾰ́δῐος</i> (krŭptắdĭos, “secret”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverb forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hiding place</li><li>crypteia (a Spartan state organization which periodically sought out and killed helots, used for the purpose of subjugation)</li></ol><p>From κρῠπτεύω (krŭpteúō, “hide oneself”), from κρῠπτω (krŭptō, “to hide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to conceal; to hide</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) hide oneself; to lie concealed</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ensnare</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῠπτός</i> (krŭptós, “hidden”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, “a suffix forming verbs of condition or activity”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>obscuring</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide, conceal”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, “adjective-forming suffix added to verbal stems to form adjectives: relating to, suited to, skilled in”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concealed, hidden, secret</li></ol><p>From <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “I conceal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>icy</li><li>of crystal</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύσταλλος</i> (krústallos, “crystal”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleawort (<i>Plantago psyllium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύσταλλος</i> (krústallos, “ice”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concealment, hiding</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide, conceal”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, “a suffix added to stems of adjectives, rarely to the stems of verbs, and even more rarely to the stems of nouns, to form feminine abstract nouns”)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hidden, concealed</li><li>secret, clandestine</li><li>occult</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;in the vocative) my friend</li><li>a fabulous gem</li><li>title of a grade of initiates in the mysteries of Mithras</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide, conceal”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, “pertaining to, belonging to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hidden</li><li>secret, clandestine</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύφα</i> (krúpha, “secretly”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, “an adjective-forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>κρῠφῇ</i> (krŭphēî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly, in secret</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>topknot (decorative knot of hair on the crown of the head)</li></ol><p>According to Lewy, from Aramaic <i>כַּרְבָּלְתָּא</i> (karbaltā, “headgear, a type of head covering; cock's comb, crest”), while Rabin points to it's ancestor Akkadian <i>𒌆𒋼𒀀𒁀𒀠𒆷𒋫</i> (TUG kar-ba-al-la-ta /⁠karballatu⁠/, “hat, cowl, pointed cap, linen helm”), generally taken as a foreign term associated with Cimmerians. Lagard thinks that the first syllable could be related to Sanskrit <i>शिरस्</i> (śiras, “head”). Furnée points to Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix, while Prellwitz connects the word with <i>κόρυμβος</i> (kórumbos, “peak, summit”) and Meister with <i>κόρθυς</i> (kórthus, “heap”), <i>κόρθιλος</i> (kórthilos, “wren”), <i>κόρυς</i> (kórus, “helmet”) and <i>κορυφή</i> (koruphḗ, “top of the head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>croaking, cawing of a crow or chough</li></ol><p>From <i>κρώζω</i> (krṓzō, “to croak, caw”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water pail, pitcher</li><li>cinerary urn</li></ol><p>The element "-σσ-", as well as the technical meaning, points to a Pre-Greek origin. Previously the word has been compared with Old English <i>crocca</i> (“crock”), Dutch <i>kruik</i> (“jug”) and Middle Irish <i>croccán</i>. Note also Old Armenian <i>կարաս</i> (karas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ὁ μαλλός τῶν παιδίων</i> (ho mallós tôn paidíōn, literally “the tress of a young child”), <i>αἱ τρίχες τῶν αἰδοίων</i> (hai tríkhes tôn aidoíōn, literally “the hairs of the pudenda”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread which is passed between the threads of the warp, woof</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) thread</li><li>pebble on the seashore</li></ol><p>From <i>κρέκω</i> (krékō, “to weave”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix). The former is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krek-</i> (“to weave”), see also Serbo-Croatian <i>kresati</i>, English <i>reel</i>.</p><p>Possibly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱorkeh₂</i>, and related to Sanskrit <i>शर्कर</i> (śarkara, “pebble, gravel; later, sugar”) (whence also <i>शर्करा</i> (śarkarā)) or <i>कृशन</i> (kṛśana, “pearl”), though Beekes is doubtful.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made from or of saffron</li><li>yellow</li></ol><p>From <i>κρόκος</i> (krókos, “saffron”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saffron, <i>Crocus sativus</i></li><li>saffron (dye)</li><li>yolk (of an egg)</li></ol><p>A Semitic loanword. Compare Akkadian <i>𒌑𒆪𒄀𒆸𒈾</i> (/⁠kurkanū⁠/), Arabic كُرْكُم (kurkum), and Hebrew <i>כרכום</i> (karkom).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onion (<i>Allium cepa</i>)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) onion-market</li></ol><p>A similar word for "onion" and "garlic" is found in Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;hramusō</i> (“onion, garlic”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čermъša</i> (“ramson”) and Middle Irish <i>crim</i> (“garlic”), suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kermus-</i> (“wild garlic”). The <i>e-</i>vowel of Celtic and Balto-Slavic is also found in the placename <i>Κρεμμυών</i> (Kremmuṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepped copings of parapets</li><li>courses, steps of the pyramids</li></ol><p>A technical term of unclear origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clapper, rattle, castanet</li></ol><p>From <i>κροτέω</i> (krotéō, “to rattle”), with a suffix <i>-αλον</i> found also in <i>κύμβαλον</i> (kúmbalon, “cymbal”) and <i>ῥόπαλον</i> (rhópalon, “club, mace”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) temple; side of the forehead</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) side; edge; profil</li><li>slope of a mountain</li><li>back of a book</li><li>edge or narrow side of a stele</li></ol><p>Unclear. Usually derived from <i>κρότος</i> (krótos, “beating”), as "beating of the veins in the temples", while Furnée instead connects it with <i>κόρση</i> (kórsē, “temple”), as well as, more distantly, <i>κρόσσαι</i> (króssai, “steps of pyramid”). However, the function of the final <i>-ᾰφος</i> (-ăphos) is obscure regardless, and possibly speaks to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rattling sound, rattle</li><li>clapping</li><li>(<i>expression</i>) in token of ridicule</li></ol><p>The verb <i>κροτέω, κροτῶ</i> (krotéō, krotô) has earlier and more frequent attestations. According to Beekes, the verb likely stems from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kret-</i> (“to shake”). Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hradjaną</i> (“to loosen, set free, rescue, save”), Old High German <i>redan</i> (“to sieve”), Lithuanian <i>krė̃sti</i> (“to shake”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;krotiti</i> (“to tame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly; without the knowledge of (<i>with genitive of person</i>)</li></ol><p>From κρυφ-, the root of <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “to hide something”), +&lrm; <i>-δην</i> (-dēn, deverbal adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cold, chilliness</li><li>frost</li><li>ice</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krews-</i> (“to freeze, harden”) and cognate with Latin <i>crusta</i>, but according to Beekes, the word could alternatively derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krewh₂-mo-s</i> (“wound blood”) based on the derivative <i>κρυμός</i> (krumós, “frost”) (though this theory is semantically less convincing). For the former case, compare Tocharian B <i>kuraś</i>, <i>krośce</i> (“cold”), Latvian <i>kruvesis</i>, perhaps Ukrainian <i>крига</i> (kryha, “ice”) and Old English <i>hruse</i> (“surface”); for the latter, compare Avestan <i>𐬑𐬭𐬏𐬨𐬀</i> (xrūma, “horrible”). See also <i>κρύσταλλος</i> (krústallos, “ice; rock crystal”) (which Beekes considers Pre-Greek).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of the Spartan κρυπτεία (krupteía)</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “to hide”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide; to cover</li><li>to conceal; to obscure</li></ol><p>Unknown. Determination is complicated by the fact that the word is similar to Ancient Greek <i>καλύπτω</i> (kalúptō, “to cover”) in both form and sense, thus indicating possible analogical influence in both directions.</p><p>Although traditionally compared with semantically-close Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kráuˀtei</i> (“to cover, hide”) (whence Russian <i>крыть</i> (krytʹ, “id”), Old Church Slavonic <i>крꙑти</i> (kryti, “id”), Lithuanian <i>krovinỹs</i> (“cargo”)), De Vaan notes that the Balto-Slavic evidence demands a laryngeal while the Greek excludes it, making a formal relation difficult.</p><p>Beekes thus suggests possible Pre-Greek origin due to lack of solid Indo-European etymology, but acknowledges that the variation in labial (which he usually considered to be an argument for substrate origin) may be due to aforementioned analogy.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ice</li><li>rock crystal</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is Pre-Greek — which Beekes claims is the case for all Greek words with the <i>-αλλο-</i> suffix. Contrary to this, the apparent semantic and phonetic relationship to <i>κρύος</i> (krúos, “cold; ice”), Latin <i>crusta</i> (“crust, bark”), and Old English <i>hruse</i> (“ground”) is striking - perhaps the word is a derivative of <i>κρύος</i> (krúos) with the addition of the Pre-Greek suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>without the knowledge of</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “to hide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hidden, concealed</li><li>secret, clandestine</li><li>occult</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;in the vocative) my friend</li><li>a fabulous gem</li><li>title of a grade of initiates in the mysteries of Mithras</li></ol><p>From <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide, conceal”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, “pertaining to, belonging to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a concealment; a hiding</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a suppression</li><li>a concealment of stolen goods</li><li>a mystery; a secret</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “to hide; to conceal”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to caw, croak (<i>of crows and other birds</i>)</li><li>to croak, groan (<i>of people and objects</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately onomatopoeic, but related to words denoting sounds in other Indo-European languages, such as Latin <i>crociō</i>, Lithuanian <i>kriõkti</i>, Sanskrit <i>क्रोशति</i> (krośati) etc., as well as to <i>κράζω</i> (krázō) and <i>κλώζω</i> (klṓzō), which all seem to trace back to a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kroh₂k-</i>, <i>&#42;kroh₂ḱ-</i> (“to croak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scythe, billhook</li></ol><p>The traditional derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kerp-</i> (“to pluck, to harvest”) (whence Ancient Greek <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “fruit”), Sanskrit <i>कृपाण</i> (kṛpāṇa, “sword”), Lithuanian <i>kir̃pti</i> (“to cut off, shave”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;harbistaz</i> (“harvest”), Latin <i>carpō</i> (“to pluck”)) is highly unlikely in view of both the root vocalism and the consonant alternation <i>π ~ β</i> (p ~ b). Likely Pre-Greek; compare Hurrian (hurubbi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixture</li><li>alloy of metals</li></ol><p>From <i>κεράννυμι</i> (keránnumi, “to mix”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixture, compound, union</li><li>temperature, climate</li><li>temperament</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) crasis (e.g. τὸ ἔλαιον &gt; τοὔλαιον)</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) winecup</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κεράννυμι</i> (keránnumi, “to mix”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>samphire</li></ol><p>Unknown, but probably a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>κρῑθή</i> (krīthḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κρίμα</i> (kríma)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to get, obtain, acquire, gain, win</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of consequences) to bring on oneself, incur</li><li>(transitive,&#32;perfect and pluperfect) to have acquired, have, own, possess</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tk-éh₁-</i> (“acquisition”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tek-</i> (“to take by the hand; to receive, obtain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) possessions, property</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κτάομαι</i> (ktáomai), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tek-</i> (“to receive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) possession, piece of property</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>κτεάτεσσι</i> (kteátessi), dative plural of <i>κτέανον</i> (ktéanon), only the analogy of <i>r</i>/<i>n</i>-stem nouns like <i>ἧπαρ</i> (hêpar) with Epic dative plural <i>ἡπάτεσσι</i> (hēpátessi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>κτείς</i> (kteís)</li></ol><p>From <i>κτείς</i> (kteís, “a comb”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>κτάομαι</i> (ktáomai)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tek-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral gifts, burnt with the dead</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) funeral honors</li><li>wrappers for the dead, shrouds</li></ol><p>Beekes gives no etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κτήματα</i> (ktḗmata) (<i>before the rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κτήματα</i> (ktḗmata) (<i>before the smooth breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owner, possessor</li></ol><p>From <i>κτάομαι</i> (ktáomai, “I possess”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a helmet</i>) made of marten skin</li></ol><p>Truncated form of unattested <i>&#42;ἰκτίδεος</i> (&#42;iktídeos), corresponding to <i>ἴκτῐς</i> (íktĭs, “beech marten”) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to people</li><li>to found; to build; to establish (a city)</li><li>to plant (e.g. an orchard)</li><li>to create; to produce</li><li>to make so</li><li>to perpetrate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tḱey-</i> (“to settle”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>क्षेति॑</i> (kṣéti), Avestan <i>𐬱𐬀𐬉𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (šaē<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti) and, via Iranian borrowing, Old Armenian <i>շէն</i> (šēn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tame, docile, obedient</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ram</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tḱey-</i> (“to settle”), the same root of <i>κτίζω</i> (ktízō, “to found, build”). The suffix is found in <i>χωλός</i> (khōlós), <i>φαῦλος</i> (phaûlos) and various expressions for defects.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founding, settling</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an easy achievement</li><li>creation</li></ol><li>created thing, creature</li><li>authority created or ordained</li></ol><p><i>κτῐ́ζω</i> (ktĭ́zō, “found, build”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>क्षितिज</i> (kṣitija).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colony, foundation</li></ol><p>From <i>κτίζω</i> (ktízō, “to found, to build”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, slay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tḱen-</i> (“strike”), with a <b>*y</b> progressive aspect marker and metathesis of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tḱ-</i> into Proto-Greek <i>&#42;kt-</i> (compare <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn)). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>क्षन्</i> (kṣan, “hurt, injure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comb (for hair, or in weaving)</li><li>rake</li><li>horn of a lyre</li><li>pubes</li><li>scallop</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pḱtén-s</i> (“comb”), a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peḱ-</i> (“to pluck”). Cognate with Latin <i>pecten</i>, Persian شانه (šâne).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb</li></ol><p>From <i>κτείς</i> (kteís, “a comb”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bestialist</li></ol><p><i>κτῆνος</i> (ktênos, “beast, ox or sheep”) +&lrm; <i>-βάτης</i> (-bátēs, “suffix from verb <i>βαίνω</i>”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crash</li><li>to ring, resound, echo</li><li>to make to ring or resound</li></ol><p>From <i>κτύπος</i> (ktúpos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murder, slaughter</li></ol><p>From <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō, “kill, slay”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crash, bang, din</li></ol><p>Reminiscent of <i>δοῦπος</i> (doûpos, “thud”), but further details are unknown. The variation between voiced and unvoiced stop suggests Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece of property; a possession</li></ol><p>From <i>κτάομαι</i> (ktáomai, “to acquire”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>domestic animal</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) livestock</li></ol><p>From <i>κτᾰ́ομαι</i> (ktắomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possession (ownership&#59; taking, holding, keeping something as one's own)</li><li>property</li></ol><p>From <i>κτᾰ́ομαι</i> (ktắomai) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of beans</li></ol><p>From <i>κύαμος</i> (kúamos, “bean”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the color of <i>κῠ́ᾰνος</i> (kŭ́ănos): dark, dark-blue</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ᾰνος</i> (kŭ́ănos, “dark-blue enamel”) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be pregnant with (<i>said of both humans and animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be pregnant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱuh₁-éye-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱewh₁-</i> (“to swell”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) dark-haired</li></ol><p>From <i>κύανος</i> (kúanos, “dark blue”) +&lrm; <i>χαίτη</i> (khaítē, “hair”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) dark-prowed (<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of ships</i>)</li></ol><p><i>κύανος</i> (kúanos, “blue”) +&lrm; <i>πρῷρα</i> (prōîra, “bow, prow”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with blue and black feathers, like the raven</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) dark-winged</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ᾰνος</i> (kŭ́ănos, “blue”) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of the cluster -βδ.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steering, pilotage</li><li>government</li></ol><p>From <i>κυβερνάω</i> (kubernáō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steer, drive, pilot, to be a steersman</li><li>to guide, govern, direct</li></ol><p>Beekes attributes it to a Mediterranean substrate, probably Pre-Greek. Neumann sees it as a denominal verb from <i>κύρβεις</i> (kúrbeis, “turnable wooden cylinders”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷerb-</i> (“to turn”), via a variant <i>κύρβνα</i> (kúrbna) formed with the tool morpheme <i>-να-</i> (-na-) (compare Sanskrit <i>नयति</i> (náyati, “to lead, guide, govern”)), whence through metathesis the verbal stem <i>κυβερνα-</i> (kuberna-). Compare Ancient Greek <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “wrist”) and English <i>whirl</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>captain, steersman, pilot, navigator</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) guide, leader</li></ol><p><i>κῠβερνάω</i> (kŭbernáō, “to steer”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good at steering</li></ol><p>From <i>κυβερνάω</i> (kubernáō, “to steer”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steering, pilotage</li><li>government</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠβερνᾰ́ω</i> (kŭbernắō, “I steer”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐσμός</i> (-ĭsmós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play at dice</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to run a risk or hazard; to run the risk of, venture on</li><li>to cheat, deceive</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be staked</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́βος</i> (kŭ́bos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) glorious</li></ol><p>From <i>κῦδος</i> (kûdos, “glory”) +&lrm; <i>-άλιμος</i> (-álimos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) glorifying ennobling men, bringing men glory or renown (an epithet of <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē))</li></ol><p><i>κῦδος</i> (kûdos) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glorious; honored</li></ol><p>From <i>κῦδος</i> (kûdos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tumult; commotion; uproar; din (of war)</li></ol><p>Given the unusual formation, according to Beekes, the word is without a doubt Pre-Greek. Frisk and Chantraine discuss it under <i>κυδάζω</i> (kudázō, “to insult”), but the semantic relation seems weak.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quince (fruit)</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>μῆλον Κυδώνιον</i> (mêlon Kudṓnion, literally “Cydonian apple”), which was also compounded as <i>μηλοκυδώνιον</i> (mēlokudṓnion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir up, mix</li><li>to stir up, throw into confusion, confound</li></ol><p>Uncertain. According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keuk-</i> (“to whirl, mix”), related to Lithuanian <i>šaukštas</i> (“spoon”), but Beekes says this is unlikely. Furnee derives it from Pre-Greek and compares <i>κυρκανάω</i> (kurkanáō, “to contrive, mix”). Compare also Sanskrit <i>खजति</i> (khajati).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a potion or posset consisting of barley-meal, grated cheese, and Pramnian wine, among other things</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠκᾰ́ω</i> (kŭkắō, “I mix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Cyclamen graecum</i></li></ol><p>From <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle, ring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeysuckle (<i>Lonicera periclymenum</i>)</li></ol><p>Literally “the other cyclamen”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>circular</li></ol><p>From <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made round by turning; round, circular</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle”) +&lrm; <i>τείρω</i> (teírō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>into a circle, in a circle</li></ol><p>From <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle”) +&lrm; <i>-σε</i> (-se, “-wards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to encircle, surround</li><li>to go round</li><li>to form a circle</li></ol><p>From <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll, turn over</li></ol><p>Often connected with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i> (“to crook, bend”) (whence perhaps <i>κυλλός</i> (kullós, “crooked”)), but Beekes doesn't find this convincing. The same element <i>-νδ-</i> (-nd-) is found also in <i>ἀλινδέω</i> (alindéō, “to roll in the dust”) and <i>καλινδέομαι</i> (kalindéomai, “to roll about”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin. Compare also <i>κῶλον</i> (kôlon, “member”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roll along, wallow</li></ol><p>A secondary present form of <i>κῠλίνδω</i> (kŭlíndō, “to roll, turn over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clubfooted and bandy-legged</li><li>deformed, contracted, crippled</li><li>crooked (of things and limbs)</li></ol><p>Probably connected with <i>κελλόν</i> (kellón, “curved, twisted”). The word has been explained variously as a zero-grade derivation <i>&#42;kʷl̥-yós</i> or <i>&#42;kʷl̥-nós</i>, or o-grade <i>&#42;kʷol(H)-yós</i>, from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;(s)kʷel(H)-</i> (“to move; to turn (around)”) (with the word being interpreted as "turned outward or inward", that is, deformed), or alternatively and perhaps more plausibly from <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i> (“to bend, crook”); compare <i>κυλίνδω</i> (kulíndō), <i>κῶλον</i> (kôlon), <i>σκέλος</i> (skélos), <i>σκολιός</i> (skoliós). Compare Sanskrit <i>कुणि</i> (kuṇi, “having a crooked arm”). In view of the problematic Indo-European etymology, the word may well be of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Egyptian bread made from spelt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>kršt</i> <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./cb5476b2.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="k" alt="glyph" height="27"/><br /><img src="./71f37cec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="r" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./b207efda.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z1" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./39176b9c.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="S" alt="glyph" height="30"/><br /><img src="./76993ec3.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="t" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td>
</tr></table>
</td></tr></table>; possibly related to Egyptian Arabic جلاش (gulāš).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small cyma</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the volute of the Ionic capital</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>κῦμᾰ</i> (kûmă) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of cumin</li></ol><p>From <i>κύμῑνον</i> (kúmīnon, “cumin”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to billow, swell</li></ol><p><i>κῦμα</i> (kûma) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) player upon cymbals</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠμβᾰλῐ́ζω</i> (kŭmbălĭ́zō, “to play the cymbals”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box for cumin, spice-box, placed on the table like a salt cellar</li></ol><p>From <i>κύμινον</i> (kúminon, “cumin”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skinflint, miser, stingy person</li></ol><p>From <i>κύμῑνον</i> (kúmīnon) +&lrm; <i>πρίω</i> (príō) +&lrm; <i>-στης</i> (-stēs). Literally, “cumin-splitter”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>κῠμῑνοπρῐστοκᾰρδᾰμογλῠ́φος</i> (kŭmīnoprĭstokărdămoglŭ́phos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>κῠμῑνοπρῐστοκᾰρδᾰμογλῠ́φος</i> (kŭmīnoprĭstokărdămoglŭ́phos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(nonce word,&#32;humorous) extremely miserly, scrooge-like (literally a cumin-splitting-cress-scraper)</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠμῑνοπρῐ́στης</i> (kŭmīnoprĭ́stēs, “miser, scrooge”) +&lrm; <i>κᾰ́ρδᾰμον</i> (kắrdămon, “watercress”) +&lrm; <i>γλῠ́φω</i> (glŭ́phō, “carve, scrape”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tonsilitis</li><li>sore throat, an inflammation of the larynx</li><li>dog collar</li><li>pillory</li></ol><p><i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγχω</i> (ánkhō, “strangle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leather helmet for soldiers</li></ol><p>From <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) as such helmets were originally made of dog skin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kiss</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ku-ne-s-</i>, from <i>&#42;kw(e)s-</i> (“to kiss”), which appears to be onomatopoeic. Cognate with Hittite (ku̯aššzi ~ ku̯aššanzi, “idem”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kussaz</i> (“idem”). Beekes additionally proposes an alternative reconstruction of the Indo-European root as <i>&#42;ḱwes-</i>, which would suggest an equivalence of this root to the homophonous root meaning "to pant, hiss", the latter whence Sanskrit <i>श्वस्</i> (śvas, “idem”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a little dog, a puppy</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, stem: <i>κῠν-</i> (kŭn-)) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δῐον</i> (-ĭ́dĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunter, huntsman</li></ol><p>From <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwṓ</i> (“dog”)) + <i>ἡγέτης</i> (hēgétēs, “leader”) (ultimately from <i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai, “to lead”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related to hunting</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;ḱwṓ</td></tr></table></p><p>From <i>κυνηγέτης</i> (kunēgétēs, “hunter”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, suffix meaning ‘of or pertaining to’ forming adjectives).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunter</li></ol><p>From <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>-ηγός</i> (-ēgós, “-leader”), literally “dog-leader”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doglike</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κυνικός)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Cynic</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠνῐκός</i> (kŭnĭkós, “doglike”). It is uncertain whether the followers of Antisthenes were called this because of the gymnasium where he taught (<i>Κυνόσαργες</i> (Kunósarges)), from the coarse, filthy mode of life, or some other reason.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cynical philosophy or conduct, Cynicism</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠνῐ́ζω</i> (kŭnĭ́zō, “live like a dog; live like a Cynic”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kynodesme (fibula for the prepuce)</li></ol><p><i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>δέσμη</i> (désmē, “package”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleawort, <i>Plantago psyllium</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cynocephalus, doghead</li><li>baboon</li></ol><p>A compound of <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>κέφαλος</i> (képhalos, “head”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calf's snout (<i>Antirrhinum orontium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κυνοκέφαλον</i> (kunoképhalon, “fleawort”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog's mercury (<i>Mercurialis perennis</i>)</li><li>dogbane (<i>Cionura erecta</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>κράμβη</i> (krámbē, “cabbage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) beaver</li></ol><p>From <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>ποταμός</i> (potamós, “river”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knotgrass (<i>Polygonum aviculare</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>χάλκη</i> (khálkē, “murex; purple dye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog leash, dog collar</li><li>calfskin sack for carrying hunting nets</li><li>clothes-locker in the gymnasium</li><li>purse, moneybag</li></ol><p>From <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to bear, carry, bring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hound's tongue (<i>Cynoglossum columnae</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canine tooth; cuspid</li><li>fang</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the teeth of a saw</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>ὀδούς</i> (odoús, “tooth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>κῠνόδους</i> (kŭnódous)</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>ὀδών</i> (odṓn, “tooth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Cardopatium corymbosum</i></li></ol><p><i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”, oblique stem: <i>κῠν-</i>, <i>kun-</i>) +&lrm; <i>μᾶζᾰ</i> (mâză, “barley-cake”, “bread”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog-fly</li></ol><p><i>κύων</i> (kúōn) +&lrm; <i>μυῖα</i> (muîa)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog rose (<i>Rosa canina</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́ων</i> (kŭ́ōn, “dog”, oblique stem: <i>κῠν-</i>, <i>kun-</i>) +&lrm; <i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evergreen rose (<i>Rosa sempervirens</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>βάτος</i> (bátos, “blackberry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameless</li></ol><p>From <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs), thus Literally, “dog-eyed”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cypress (<i>Cupressus sempervirens</i>)</li><li>its wood, used as timber</li></ol><p>Of loanword origin, probably from a Mediterranean Pre-Greek language; compare Hebrew <i>כֹּפֶר &#47; כּוֺפֶר</i> (kōp̱er, “pitch, tar; henna”), Aramaic <i>כּוּפְרָא</i> / <i>ܟܘܦܪܐ</i> (kūp̄rā, “pitch, tar; henna”), Ugaritic <i>𐎋𐎔𐎗</i> (kpr, “henna”), Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀓𐀞𐀫</i> (ku-pa-ro, debatable plant), Coptic <i>ⲕⲟⲩⲡⲣ</i> (koupr, “henna”), Demotic Egyptian <i>qwpr</i> (“henna”), Egyptian <i>qwpr</i> (a given name), Dongolawi <i>kofrē</i> (“henna”), all ultimately from Akkadian <i>𒇒𒌓𒀀</i> (kupru, “bitumen, pitch”) from Akkadian <i>𒅗𒉺𒊒</i> (kapāru, “to smear, to daub on”).</p><p>"Gopher" was the name of the tree whose wood was used to make the ark (Genesis 6:14).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κυπάρισσος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyparissus, Phocis, Greece</li><li>(<i>mythology</i>) Cyparissus, turned into cypress</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>κυπάρισσος</i> (kupárissos, “cypress”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of or from cypress</li></ol><p>From <i>κυπάρισσος</i> (kupárissos, “cypress”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of spurge (<i>Euphorbia aleppica</i>)</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) kind of comet</li></ol><p>From <i>κυπάρισσος</i> (kupárissos, “cypress”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carp (<i>Cyprinus carpio</i>)</li></ol><p>Other names for this fish, like Sanskrit <i>शफर</i> (śaphara), Lithuanian <i>šāpalas</i> and Late Latin <i>carpa</i>, are not related. The suffix <i>-ῖν-</i> is well-known in Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lady, mistress</li></ol><p>From <i>κυρία</i> (kuría) or <i>κῦρ</i> (kûr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>authority, power</li><li>lady, mistress</li><li>ma'am, madam</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>κῡ́ρῐος</i> (kū́rĭos, “lord; ruler; possessor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrow-necked jug</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Persian bonnet or hat, with a peaked crown</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;kurpasyah</i>. The final part of the word is from a term cognate with Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬯-</i> (pas-, “to bind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) church</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter singular of <i>κῡριακός</i> (kūriakós, “of or pertaining to a lord”), from <i>κῡ́ριος</i> (kū́rios, “lord”). Literally, the place of the Lord (i.e. "the Lord's House").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) belonging or pertaining to the Lord</li></ol><p>From <i>κῡ́ριος</i> (kū́rios) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bulging, swelling</li><li>arched, vaulted</li><li>humped, hunchbacked, gibbous</li><li>convex, outcurved</li></ol><p>Unclear. The comparison with Latin <i>curvus</i> (“curved”) presupposes that the Greek adjective continues a reduced grade <i>&#42;kʷr-to-s</i> with <i>u</i> coloring, which is phonetically difficult and places the comparison in doubt. The comparison with <i>κορώνη</i> (korṓnē, “anything curved”) is also suspect.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ratify</li></ol><p>From <i>κῦρος</i> (kûros, “power, authority”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) pomegranate</li></ol><p>Possibly from a Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;k<sub>u</sub>šēgā</i>, whence Albanian <i>shegë</i> (“pomegranate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent forward, stooping, hunchbacked</li><li>curved, round (of a cup)</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>κύπτω</i> (kúptō, “to stoop”). According to Beekes, the variation "κυφ-/κυπ-" points to a Pre-Greek origin. However, the word shows phonetic and semantic similarities with other terms across Indo-European, such as Sanskrit <i>कुभ्र</i> (kubhrá, “humpbacked bull”), <i>कुब्ज</i> (kubjá, “humpbacked, crooked”) (though the Sanskrit terms have also been derived from Munda, precluding connection with the Greek), and Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kʰumbʰas</i> (“pot”), as well as Lithuanian <i>kuprà</i> (“hump”) and Old High German <i>hovar</i> (“hump”) (which show root-final <i>&#42;-p-</i>, making a relation more difficult).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest, box, any hollow vessel</li><li>beehive, hive</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow of the ear</li></ol><p>Not related to <i>κύπη</i> (kúpē, “kind of ship”), nor derived from <i>κύπτω</i> (kúptō, “to bend, stoop”). According to Beekes, clearly a Pre-Greek word, given the variant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of fish of the gudgeon kind</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably a loan from Pre-Greek. Furnée compares Akkadian <i>𒆪𒇥</i> (kuppū, “kind of fish”), suggesting a Semitic origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>κώδων</i> (kṓdōn)</li></ol><p>From <i>κώδων</i> (kṓdōn, “a bell”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamentation</li></ol><p>From <i>κωκῡ́ω</i> (kōkū́ō, “shriek, wail”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κωκυτός)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cocytus, a river in the underworld</li></ol><p>From <i>κωκῡτός</i> (kōkūtós, “lamentation”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially of women</i>) to shriek, wail</li><li>to lament or shriek over one dead</li></ol><p>Unknown. Traditionally from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kew(H)-</i>, a problematic and probably onomatopoeic root or roots, but formal derivation from it is impossible anyway, hence Pokorny assumes dissimilation from <i>&#42;kū-kū́-</i>. He further assumes that this form is an intensive reduplication, based on the comparison with Sanskrit <i>कौति</i> (kauti, “to cry, moan”) and <i>कोकूयते</i> (kokūyate), but, as Beekes notes, these are only attested by grammarians. Alternatively, compare Old Armenian <i>քուք</i> (kʻukʻ, “groan, moan, wail, sigh”). According to Beekes, the word is not Indo-European and, thus, probably Pre-Greek. Compare also Ancient Greek <i>κώκαλον</i> (kṓkalon, adjective) in the sense “a kind of cock”, similarly resembling onomatopoeia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suffering in the colon</li></ol><p>From <i>κῶλον</i> (kôlon, “member”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós); the word for “colon, part of the intestine” is actually <i>κόλον</i> (kólon) with ο rather than ω.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who walks on stilts</li></ol><p>From <i>κωλόβᾰθρον</i> (kōlóbăthron, “stilt”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐστής</i> (-ĭstḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>preventive</li></ol><p>From <i>κωλύω</i> (kōlúō, “I hinder, prevent”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριος</i> (-tḗrios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hinderer, preventer</li></ol><p>From <i>κωλύω</i> (kōlúō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stilt</li></ol><p>From <i>κῶλον</i> (kôlon, “limb”) +&lrm; <i>βᾰ́θρον</i> (bắthron, “base, pedestal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with accusative of person and infinitive</i>) to hinder, prevent someone from doing something</li></ol><p>Possibly a Pre-Greek word; see also Semitic: Akkadian <i>𒄢</i> (kalû /⁠GUL⁠/, “to detain, delay, hold back [a person], to keep in custody, in confinement, to distrain, to prevent, to hinder”) Biblical Hebrew <i>כֶּלֶא</i> (kéle, “prison, jail”), Aramaic <i>כלא</i> (kəlāʾ, “to hinder, to restrain, to forbid”), Classical Syriac <i>ܟ݁ܳܠܶܐ</i> (kale, “hinder, forbid, restrain”) and Arabic كَلَأَ (kalaʔa, “to guard, to protect, to stop, to keep from access”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>villager, countryman</li><li>(<i>urban</i>) inhabitant of the same quarter or district</li></ol><p><i>κώμη</i> (kṓmē, “unwalled village”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comic</li></ol><p><i>κῶμος</i> (kômos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comedy</li><li>play</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>κῶμος</i> (kômos)Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awoidā́</i>Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓οιδή</i> (ăoidḗ)Ancient Greek <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ)Ancient Greek <i>κῶμος</i> (kômos)Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awoidós</i>Ancient Greek <i>ἀοιδός</i> (aoidós)Ancient Greek <i>κωμῳδία</i></p><p>From <i>κῶμος</i> (kômos, “revel, carousing”) +&lrm; <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song”) or <i>κῶμος</i> (kômos, “revel, carousing”) +&lrm; <i>ἀοιδός</i> (aoidós, “singer, bard”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comic writer</li></ol><p>From <i>κωμῳδῐ́α</i> (kōmōidĭ́a, “comedy”) +&lrm; <i>-γρᾰ́φος</i> (-grắphos, “drawer, painter, writer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cone-shaped, conical</li></ol><p>From <i>κῶνος</i> (kônos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Egyptian couch with mosquito netting</li></ol><p>From <i>κώνωψ</i> (kṓnōps, “mosquito”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>κώνωψ</i> (kṓnōps, “mosquito”)</li></ol><p>From <i>κώνωψ</i> (kṓnōps, “mosquito”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blunt; dull; obtuse</li><li>(<i>of sound</i>) mute; noiseless</li><li>(<i>of men or animals</i>) dumb; mute</li><li>deaf; deaf-mute</li><li>(<i>of the mind</i>) dull; obtuse</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) senseless; unmeaning; obscure</li></ol><p>Probably from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>κηφήν</i> (kēphḗn, “drone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κολακεύματα</i> (kolakeúmata, “piece of flattery”), <i>πανουργήματα</i> (panourgḗmata, “knavish trick, villainy”).</li></ol><p>Related to <i>κάβαξ</i> (kábax, “crafty, knavish”). The element <i>κα/οβ-ακ-</i> is clearly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impudent rogue, arrogant knave</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) mischievous goblins invoked by rogues</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is probably of Pre-Greek origin, based presumably on semantic domain and lack of credible cognates. The original sense was probably "porter, transporter" (still retained in derivatives such as <i>κοβᾱλεύω</i> (kobāleúō)), which developed negatively into "rogue". See also <i>κοάλεμος</i> (koálemos, “blockhead”) and Georgian <i>კომბლე</i> (ḳomble, “wise fool in Georgian fairytales”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>congius, an ancient Roman unit of measure</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>congius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mussel, cockle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>shell-full</li></ol><li>anything like a mussel shell, especially a shell-like cavity in the body:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow of the ear</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) kneepan</li></ol><li>case around a seal attached to documents</li><li>niche for a statue; apse</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) fourth part of a sphere</li></ol><p>Probably from Pre-Greek, especially in view of the cluster <i>-γχ-</i> (-nkh-), which points to prenasalization in Pre-Greek terms. Compare <i>καγκύλας</i> (kankúlas, “mussel”) and <i>κόχλος</i> (kókhlos, “spiral conch, snail”), which both exhibit conclusively Pre-Greek variation.</p><p><br/>Possibly related to Sanskrit <i>शङ्ख</i> (śaṅkhá-, “conch, mussel”), but not by common inheritance from Proto-Indo-European. Doublet of <i>κόγχος</i> (kónkhos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mussel, cockle</li><li>small measure (especially of liquid)</li><li>mussel shaped container for liquid</li><li>The upper part of the skull</li><li>lentil soup</li></ol><p>Unknown. Speculated to be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱongʰ-</i> (“shell, mussel”) because of its striking similarity to Sanskrit <i>शङ्ख</i> (śaṅkhá, “conch, mussel”), but the phonological correspondence between Ancient Greek <i>⟨χ⟩</i> (⟨kh⟩) and Sanskrit <i>⟨ख⟩</i> (⟨kh⟩) is irregular. More likely from Pre-Greek; compare the similar words <i>καγκύλας</i> (kankúlas, “mussel”) and especially <i>κόχλος</i> (kókhlos, “spiral conch, snail”) and its variants, and note the cluster <i>⟨γχ⟩</i> (⟨nkh⟩) which is common in Pre-Greek–derived terms. The Sanskrit word may thus be from a related loan source or substrate.</p><p>Alternatively connected to Akkadian <i>𒅆𒄖𒇲</i> (IGI.GU.LA₂ /⁠qūqānu⁠/, “an insect; a disease; a parasitic worm”), whence also Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>קוּקָנָא</i> (qūqānā) and distorted Classical Syriac <i>ܩܘܪܩܥܐܢ</i>, <i>ܩܘܠܝܘܢ</i> (“parasitic worm”) derive (distinguished from the borrowings of <i>κογχύλιον</i> (konkhúlion) <i>ܩܘܟܐܠܝܘܢ</i> (qūkāliyawn), <i>ܩܘܟܠܝܘܢ</i> (qūkaliyawn), <i>ܩܘܟܠܝܐ</i> (qūkaliyā), <i>ܩܘܚܠܝܐ</i> (qūḥaliyā, “spiral shape”)); for the form-meaning connection compare also Arabic قَوْقَن (qawqan, “shell of a mollusk; mollusk of a shell”) which is allegedly from <i>κοχλίας</i> (kokhlías, “snail with a spiral-shell; spiral”) from Ancient Greek <i>κόχλος</i> (kókhlos, “spiral-shelled mollusk”) or the present word.</p><p>According to Lipiński a rendering of Aramaic <i>כּוּכָא</i> (kūḵā, “burial chamber, niche for a grave”) from Akkadian <i>𒆠𒈤</i> (KI.MAḪ /⁠kimaḫḫu, kimāḫu⁠/, “burial chamber, sepulchre, grave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>some architectural element, possibly a platform of a temple or square</li><li>ambiguous, possibly square or morsel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>quadra</i> (“square; morsel”). For the initial <i>κο-</i> (ko-) from <i>qua-</i> compare Ancient Greek <i>κοδράντης</i> (kodrántēs), Koine Greek <i>Κοδρᾶτος</i> (Kodrâtos), Byzantine Greek <i>κοτρίγα</i> (kotríga), as well as Ancient Greek <i>Κυρίτης</i> (Kurítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>buskin, high boot, which covered the whole foot and half the leg, with thick soles, used by actors to increase their height</li></ol><p>From Lydian according to Jonkees, but more probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) pine kernel, the seed of the pinecone</li></ol><p>From <i>κόκκος</i> (kókkos, “grain, seed, kernel”). Compare Ancient Greek <i>Κώκαλος</i> (Kṓkalos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scarlet; blushing</li></ol><p>From <i>κόκκος</i> (kókkos, “kermes oak”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain, seed, kernel</li><li>kermes oak (<i>Quercus coccifera</i>)</li><li>any insect of the genus <i>Kermes</i>, which live on the oak.</li><li>scarlet dye made from the crushed bodies of the kermes bugs</li><li>scarletberry, bittersweet nightshade (<i>Solanum dulcamara</i>)</li><li>the colour scarlet</li><li>testicle</li></ol><p>Uncertain; possibly from Pre-Greek. Potential cognates include Spanish <i>cuesco</i> (“stone (of fruit)”), though this is rejected by Furnee.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuckoo (<i>Cuculus canorus</i>)</li><li>red gurnard (<i>Chelidonichthys cuculus</i>)</li><li>fig that ripens early</li><li>tailbone, coccyx</li><li>mark on a horse's shoulder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kókkūks</i>, eventually from the same onomatopoeic root of <i>κόκκῡ</i> (kókkū, “cry of the cuckoo”) and <i>κοκκύζω</i> (kokkúzō, “to cry cuckoo”). Compare also English <i>cuckoo</i>, Latin <i>cucūlus</i> (“cuckoo”) and Greek <i>κούκος</i> (koúkos, “cuckoo”), which are all onomatopoeias.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flatterer, fawner</li></ol><p>A colloquial Attic word without established etymology. According to Beekes, it could be of Pre-Greek origin, due to the suffix <i>-ακ-</i> (-ak-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chastisement, correction, punishment</li></ol><p>From <i>κολάζω</i> (kolázō, “to check, punish”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, “nominal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a buffet, a blow</li></ol><p>Related to <i>κολάπτω</i> (koláptō, “to peck”), both Pre-Greek words according to Beekes, who doubts connections to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₂-</i> (“to beat”).</p><p>Compare also Persian گوپال (gupâl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glue</li><li>flour paste</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kol-</i>, with cognates including Russian <i>кле́й</i> (kléj), Middle Dutch <i>helen</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cake or roll</li></ol><p>The ending in "-βος" is found also in <i>κάκκαβος</i> (kákkabos), <i>κάναβος</i> (kánabos) and <i>σίττυβος</i> (síttubos). The noun is probably related to <i>κόλλιξ</i> (kóllix, “type of bread”) and <i>κολλύρα</i> (kollúra, “cake, tablet”). The word is no doubt Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll or loaf of coarse bread</li></ol><p>According to Beekes undoubtedly of Pre-Greek origin, because of the suffix "-ῑκ-".</p><p>Possibly an Iranian borrowing related to Persian کلیچه (količe), کلوچه (koluče).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peg or screw by which the strings of the lyre were tightened</li><li>bar by which a windlass was turned</li><li>thick skin on the upper part of the neck of oxen or swine</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) cinaedus, catamite</li></ol><p>A technical term which has been connected to Latin <i>callum</i> (“callosity, thick skin”) or to <i>σκόλοψ</i> (skólops, “pole”). The word is clearly Pre-Greek because of its suffix; this is further confirmed by the variant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) colon (last part of the digestive system)</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p><p>In modern scholarship, Beekes compares <i>χολάς</i> (kholás, “guts, bowels”), and Furnee connects it to <i>χοάς</i> (khoás, “intestines”), <i>χόλιξ</i> (khólix, “guts”), <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milky sap”), <i>γάλλος</i> (gállos, “eunuch”).</p><p>Grammarians of Hellenistic times proposed relation to <i>βουκόλος</i> (boukólos, “tending kine”), <i>δύσκολος</i> (dúskolos, “difficult”), <i>κόλαξ</i> (kólax, “flatterer”). Other theories include <i>κυλλός</i> (kullós, “crooked, club-footed”) and <i>κόλος</i> (kólos, “docked, hornless”). No relation to κῶλον (kôlon, “limb”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>docked</li><li>hornless or stump-horned</li><li>(<i>of a spear</i>) without point</li><li>(<i>of battle</i>) broken off</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared with Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kálˀtei</i> (“to forge, strike”) (whence Lithuanian <i>kálti</i> (“to beat, to hammer”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kòlti</i> (“to sting”)), which is either from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₂-</i> (“to beat, break”) or <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to cut, split”). However, Beekes has some doubts, particularly due to the likely-related <i>κολούω</i> (koloúō, “to mutilate, limit”) exhibiting strange morphology, and alternatively and tentatively suggests Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>docked</li><li>hornless or stump-horned</li><li>(<i>of a spear</i>) without point</li><li>(<i>of battle</i>) broken off</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared with Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;kálˀtei</i> (“to forge, strike”) (whence Lithuanian <i>kálti</i> (“to beat, to hammer”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kòlti</i> (“to sting”)), which is either from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₂-</i> (“to beat, break”) or <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to cut, split”). However, Beekes has some doubts, particularly due to the likely-related <i>κολούω</i> (koloúō, “to mutilate, limit”) exhibiting strange morphology, and alternatively and tentatively suggests Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dock-tailed, stump-tailed</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) truncated</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(astronomy,&#32;in the plural) colure, either of two great circles passing through the equinoctial and solstitial points, intersecting at the poles</li><li>kind of fig</li></ol><p>From <i>κόλος</i> (kólos, “docked”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bosom, lap</li><li>a fold in fabric around the bosom</li><li>any hollow space, pocket</li><li>especially of water, the hollow between waves</li><li>gulf, bay, creek</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷelp-</i> (“to curve, arch”), which would also be the source of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwalfą</i> (“vault, arch”), though Greek <i>&#42;πόλπος</i> (&#42;pólpos) would be expected, and the semantics are distant.</p><p>Alternatively, if the original meaning was closer to “crevice” and from a verb “to carve, cut, cleave”, compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;halbaz</i> (“half”) and Sanskrit <i>कल्प</i> (kálpa, “rule, precept, arrangement”, literally “something cut to shape?”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kolp-</i>, possibly an extension of <i>&#42;(s)kel(H)-</i> (“to cut, slice, chop, carve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κολυμβῐ́ς</i> (kolumbĭ́s, “diver; little grebe (<i>Podiceps minor</i>)”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>κολύμβησῐς</i> (kolúmbēsĭs, “pearl fishing”)</li><li>alternative form of <i>κολυμβήθρᾱ</i> (kolumbḗthrā, “place for swimming”)</li></ol><p>Possibly Pre-Greek. Compare Latin <i>columba</i> (“dove”), which may be a borrowing from Greek. Also compare with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kъlpь</i> (“swan”). Compare also Proto-Nuristani <i>&#42;carāmba</i> (“parrot”) and Old Armenian <i>սալամբ</i> (salamb).</p><p>According to Beekes, <i>κολυμβάω</i> (kolumbáō, “to dive”) is a denominative verbal derivation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strawberry tree (<i>Arbutus unedo</i>)</li></ol><p>Strömberg derives it from <i>κόμη</i> (kómē, “crown of a tree”) but Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roll, band, girth</li></ol><p>The traditional comparisons with Lithuanian <i>kabi̇̀nti</i> (“to hang, hook on”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skobà</i> (“bracket”) and, within Greek, <i>σκαμβός</i> (skambós, “crooked”) are considered by Frisk and Beekes to be phonetically and semantically unsatisfactory. This, along with the <i>β-π</i> (b-p) variation demonstrated by the related form <i>κομποθηλεία</i> (kompothēleía, “buckle”), suggests Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collectively</i>) hair of the head</li><li>gill or branchia of the cuttlefish</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) foliage of trees</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) luminous tail of a comet</li></ol><p>Not explained with certainty. The word may be taken as "well-tended hair", in opposition of <i>θρίξ</i> (thríx, “hair”), and connected with <i>κομέω</i> (koméō, “to take care of, tend”). Schwyzer considers it a back-formation from <i>κομάω</i> (komáō, “to let the hair grow long”), which could be a by-form of <i>κομέω</i> (koméō). However, since <i>κομάω</i> (komáō) is always connected with hair and is never used in a different sense of "to care", and growing long hair is likely considered the opposite of caring for it, the assumption is not very probable. It has also been suggested as a Pre-Greek word with an element <i>&#42;ko-</i> found in other branches of Indo-European and compared to Albanian <i>qime</i> (“thread of hair”).</p><p>The consonant choice in this word is also suggestive of a Semitic word, in Akkadian one has <i>𒋦</i> (qimmatum, “tuft of hair; mane, plume; crown, crest”), akin to Arabic قِمَّة (qimma, “crown, peak”), Amharic and Tigre <i>ጋሜ</i> (gamme, “a kind of straight haircut, clean-shaven save for a circumferential strip at the nethermost part”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a companion, comrade, partner</li><li>a count, an earl</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>comes</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stamp or impression of a coin; coinage</li><li>that which is cut off; piece</li><li>chaff</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) short clause</li><li>contusion</li></ol><p>From <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gum, obtained from babul (<i>Vachellia nilotica</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>qmy</i> (“anointing oil”) or <i>qmyt</i> (“acanthus resin, gum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ornamentation, embellishment</li><li>elaborate and sophisticated treatment</li></ol><p><i>κομμόω</i> (kommóō, “to beautify, embellish”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>din, clash, racket</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) boast, vaunt, bragging</li><li>praise (rarely in good sense)</li></ol><p>Probably onomatopoeic. Compare <i>βόμβος</i> (bómbos, “humming, buzzing”), <i>κόναβος</i> (kónabos, “din, clash”) and <i>κόμβα</i> (kómba, “shearwater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>din, clash, ringing</li></ol><p>The ending recalls other sound words like <i>ἄραβος</i> (árabos), <i>θόρυβος</i> (thórubos) and <i>ὄτοβος</i> (ótobos). Furnée assumes a Pre-Greek origin, comparing <i>καναχή</i> (kanakhḗ, “clattering, rattling”), with the interchanges <i>α/ο</i> and <i>χ/β</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of drinking vessel</li><li>a unit of volume derived from the vessel</li></ol><p>Like many words ending in <i>-ῠ</i> (-ŭ), it is a loanword. Furnée compares <i>κοτῠ́λη</i> (kotŭ́lē, “cup”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin, while Szemerényi refers to late Neo-Babylonian <i>𒃶𒁺</i> (kandu, “vessel”) which is considered a West Semitic borrowing from Aramaic <i>כנדא</i> (kandā, “vessel, pot; specifically for wine”) from Sanskrit <i>कन्दु</i> (kandu, “pot”) and ultimately Proto-South Dravidian <i>&#42;kiṇṭV-</i> (“pot”), found also in the forms <i>כַּד</i> (kaḏ), <i>כדא</i> (kaddā), Biblical Hebrew <i>כַּד</i> (kad), Punic <i>𐤊𐤃</i> (kd), Ugaritic <i>𐎋𐎄</i> (kd), and Ancient Greek <i>κᾰ́δος</i> (kắdos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) knuckle (of any joint)</li><li>knot in a string</li><li>any hard, bony knob</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) swelling of the gum</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>δάκτυλος</i> (dáktulos) and <i>σφόνδυλος</i> (sphóndulos); the bare stem is seen in <i>κόνδοι</i> (kóndoi, “vertebrae”). Most connections outside Greek, like Sanskrit <i>कन्द</i> (kanda, “bulb”), कन्दुक (kanduka, “playball, cushion”) and especially काण्ड (kā́ṇḍa, kāṇḍá) (see more) can be rejected straightaway. The word is probably of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the structure.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κύνικλος</i> (kúniklos, “rabbit”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the multitude</li><li>ashes</li><li>the powder sprinkled upon wrestlers</li></ol><p>From an <i>o</i>-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ken-</i> (“dust; ashes”). Cognate with Latin <i>cinis</i> (“cold ashes”), Tocharian B <i>kentse</i> (“dust, rust”), and Sanskrit <i>कण</i> (káṇa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleabane (the name of various plants of the genus <i>Inula</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is like other plant names, such as <i>μάνυζα</i> (mánuza), <i>μώλυζα</i> (mṓluza) and <i>ὄρυζα</i> (óruza). The assumption that it was formed from <i>κονίς</i> (konís, “nit”) is unwarranted. According to Beekes, the variations point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece</li></ol><p>From <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “I cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestle</li></ol><p>From <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to strike, smite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>striking, beating</li><li>toil and trouble, suffering</li><li>fatigue, weariness</li><li>work, exertion</li></ol><p>From <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Koppa, an archaic letter of the Ancient Greek alphabet ⟨Ϙ, ϙ⟩; replaced by kappa and reduced to use as the numeral for 90 by the Classical Greek period.</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ϙόππα</i> (qóppa), from Phoenician <i>𐤒</i> (q /⁠qōp⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of dung, filthy</li></ol><p>From <i>κόπρος</i> (kópros, “dung, ordure”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung; ordure; excrement</li><li>(<i>especially in husbandry</i>) dung; manure</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) filth; dirt</li><li>dunghill; byre</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱókʷ-ro-s</i>, thematicized from <i>&#42;ḱókʷr̥</i> (“excrement”). Cognates include Old Irish <i>cechar</i> (“quagmire, slough, bog”), Sanskrit <i>शकृत्</i> (śakṛt, “dung”), and Lithuanian <i>šiknà</i> (“arse”), <i>ši̇̀kti</i> (“defecate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strike; cut; shake</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to knock</li></ol></ol><p>Probably a <i>yod</i>-present formation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kop-</i> (“to strike, to beat”) (though there is a chance that the word is a substrate borrowing). Compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skopiti</i> (“to castrate”).</p><p>Further cognate with Lithuanian <i>kàpti</i> (“to hew, fell”), <i>kaplỹs</i> (“hatchet”), Albanian <i>kep</i> (“to hew, chisel”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopàti</i> (“to dig”), Old High German <i>happa</i> (“scythe”), English <i>hatchet</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raven, crow</li><li>cormorant</li><li>the constellation Corvus</li><li>anything hooked or curved</li><li>door handle</li><li>engine for grappling ships</li><li>hook</li><li>type of torture instrument</li><li>cock's bill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱorh₂k-</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱorh₂-</i> (“crow, raven”). Cognates include Latin <i>corvus</i>, <i>cornix</i>. Also see <i>κορώνη</i> (korṓnē, “crow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girl, young woman, maiden</li><li>bride, young wife</li><li>doll, puppet</li><li>pupil (of the eye)</li><li>a long sleeve reaching over the hand</li><li>the Attic drachma</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kórwā</i>, whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀒𐀷</i> (ko-wa). In this word, <i>ᾱ</i> (ā) shifted to <i>η</i> (ē) in Attic (see Attic–Ionic vowel shift on Wikipedia) because of the preceding <i>ϝ</i> (w). In words like <i>ὥρᾱ</i> (hṓrā), ρ prevented ᾱ from shifting to η, because there was no intervening <i>ϝ</i> (w).</p><p>Probably related to <i>κορίζομαι</i> (korízomai, “to caress, care for”). From <i>&#42;ḱerh₃-</i> (“to feed, grow”), related to <i>κορέννυμι</i> (korénnumi, “to satisfy”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κόρη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cora or Kore, Persephone</li></ol><p>From <i>κόρη</i> (kórē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) sweepings, refuse</li><li>besom, broom</li></ol><p>From <i>κορέω</i> (koréō, “to sweep”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian wren (<i>Troglodytes troglodytes</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée derives it from <i>κόρυς</i> (kórus, “helmet”), but the form looks Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap, pile, sheaf</li><li>swathe of mown corn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kerdʰ-</i> (“herd”), like Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;herdō</i> (“herd”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čerda</i> (“file, herd”) and Sanskrit <i>शर्धस्</i> (śardhas, “multitude, troop”). Connection of Welsh <i>cordd</i> (“group”) is less probable, as this may also derive from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;koryos</i> (“troop, tribe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedbug</li><li>a type of fish</li><li>a type of St. John's wort</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)koris</i> (“cutting, gnawing”), from <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“cut”). Cognate to Russian <i>корь</i> (korʹ, “moth”).</p><p>Alternatively related to <i>ἀκαρί</i> (akarí, “mite”) via Pre-Greek. Compare also <i>κᾱρῑ́ς</i> (kārī́s, “shrimp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Doric,&#32;Oetaean) a kind of locust</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>δρύοψ</i> (drúops), <i>κώνωψ</i> (kṓnōps) and <i>σκάλοψ</i> (skálops). The suffix is typical of Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boy, youth</li><li>soldier</li><li>son</li><li>puppet, doll</li><li>being satisfied, satiety, surfeit</li><li>insolence, petulance</li><li>kor (a dry measure containing 10 medimnes)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kórwos</i> (“boy”) (whence Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀒𐀺</i> (ko-wo)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱer-</i> (“to grow”); see also Old Armenian <i>սերիմ</i> (serim, “be born”) and <i>սերեմ</i> (serem, “bring forth”), Ancient Greek <i>κόρη</i> (kórē, “girl”) and <i>κορέννυμι</i> (korénnumi), Latin <i>creō</i> (“produce, create, bring forth”), <i>crēscō</i> and <i>Ceres</i> (“goddess of agriculture”).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱer-</i> (“to grow”).</p><p>From Hebrew <i>כֹּר</i> (kor), itself from Akkadian <i>kurru</i> (cognate with Aramaic <i>kor</i> כֹּר, Syriac-Aramaic <i>kora</i> ܟܽܘܪܳܐ), itself from the Sumerian measure GUR.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κόρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyrus</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎤𐎢𐎽𐎢𐏁</i> (ku-u-ru-u-š /⁠Kuruš⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) temple, side of the forehead</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) hair on the temple</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) part of a temple gate</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kors-ó-</i> (“that which is shaven”), from <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”), the same root of <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to cut”) and <i>κορμός</i> (kormós, “trunk”). Perhaps the original meaning of this word was "haircut at the temples", rather than directly "hair".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuber of the blue lotus (<i>Nymphaea nouchali</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crested lark (<i>Galerida cristata</i>)</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>κόρῠς</i> (kórŭs, “helmet”) may be correct, but only as a variant of the same Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rheum, mucous discharge from the nose</li><li>drivelling, stupidity</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared to Old English <i>hrot</i> (“mucus”) but Beekes doesn't dismiss a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) uppermost point of a ship</li><li>top of a mountain</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) cluster of the ivy fruit</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>κορυφή</i> (koruphḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cake</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helmet</li></ol><p>Probably from a Mediterranean Pre-Greek substrate word, as hinted by the suffixes. Traditionally linked to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱer-</i> (“head, top, skull, horn”).</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀒𐀬</i> (ko-ru).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue pimpernel (<i>Lysimachia monelli</i>)</li><li>jute (<i>Corchorus olitorius</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a reduplicated Pre-Greek formation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sieve</li></ol><p>Perhaps of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ornament, decoration</li></ol><p>From <i>κοσμέω</i> (kosméō, “to adorn, arrange”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ordered; moderate; regular; decent</li></ol><p>From <i>κόσμος</i> (kósmos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>order</li><li>lawful order, government</li><li>mode, fashion</li><li>ornament, decoration</li><li>honour, credit</li><li>ruler</li><li>world, universe, the earth</li><li>mankind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kónsmos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱónsmos</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱens-</i> (“to announce, put in order”). Related to Latin <i>cēnseō</i> (“to estimate”) and Sanskrit <i>शंसति</i> (śaṃsati, “to commend, praise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blackbird</li><li>a type of fish</li><li>a type of poultry at Tanagra</li></ol><p>Has been connected with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kosъ</i> (“blackbird”) and with Ancient Greek <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to strike, to blow”), but Beekes is skeptical of the aforementioned theories due to the word's phonetic variants and obscure morphology, and considers the word of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>costus (<i>Saussurea costus</i>), used as a spice</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>कुष्ठ</i> (kuṣṭha).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild olive tree (<i>Olea europaea</i>, syn. <i>Olea oleaster</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably a loanword from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grudge, rancour, animosity, ill will</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kh₃-to-s</i>, from root <i>&#42;keh₃-</i>. Probably related to Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;katus</i> (“battle”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;haþuz</i> (“battle, fight”) from <i>&#42;kéh₃-tus</i>. Perhaps also to Old Church Slavonic <i>котора</i> (kotora, “quarrel”), Czech <i>katiti se</i> (“to be annoyed”) and Sanskrit <i>शत्रु</i> (śatru, “enemy”), problematic because <i>ś</i> can only come from <i>*ḱ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cottabus</li></ol><p>Formally, it has been compared with <i>κοττίς</i> (kottís, “back of the head”), <i>κόττειν</i> (kóttein, “to hit, stamp”) and <i>κόττος</i> (kóttos, “cube”). The variation "ττ/σσ" points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chicken</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of vessel mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket</li></ol><p>Uncertain; Beekes and Furnee take the word as the usual Pre-Greek, while others have narrowed down the language of origin to possibly Semitic or Hurrian. Compare <i>κόφος</i> (kóphos, “basket-load”) and Arabic قُفَّة (quffa, “large wicker-basket”) and Arabic قَفِيز (qafīz, “a dry measure”) for potentially-related words relating to "containers" with guttural onset leading over an open vowel to a labial plosive or fricative. See Akkadian 𒄣𒌒𒁍 (/⁠quppu⁠/, “wicker basket”), as well as <i>κοψία</i> (kopsía, “earthen pot”), <i>κόψα</i> (kópsa, “water bucket, urn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>κοχλῐ́ᾱς</i> (kokhlĭ́ās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shellfish with a spiral shell, used for dyeing purple</li><li>land snail</li><li>kohl</li></ol><p>Connection with <i>κόγχος</i> (kónkhos, “mussel, cockle”) is evident; the prenasalization can be explained by borrowing from Pre-Greek Semitic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) water jar</li></ol><p>Unknown, only attested in Hesychius. Compare <i>κοψία</i> (kopsía, “pipkin, small earthern pot”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strike; cut; shake</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to knock</li></ol></ol><p>Probably a <i>yod</i>-present formation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kop-</i> (“to strike, to beat”) (though there is a chance that the word is a substrate borrowing). Compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skopiti</i> (“to castrate”).</p><p>Further cognate with Lithuanian <i>kàpti</i> (“to hew, fell”), <i>kaplỹs</i> (“hatchet”), Albanian <i>kep</i> (“to hew, chisel”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopàti</i> (“to dig”), Old High German <i>happa</i> (“scythe”), English <i>hatchet</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doum palm, <i>Hyphaene thebaica</i></li><li>basket made from palm leaves</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>σκοίκιον</i> (skoíkion, “vessel, receptacle”), in which case it is from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladle for drawing wine</li><li>Attic measure for liquids</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Sometimes connected with <i>κύαρ</i> (kúar, “eye of a needle”) but according to Beekes of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bean (<i>Vicia faba</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) A swelling of the paps</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin due to the presence of variations and rejects a derivation from <i>κυέω</i> (kuéō). Possibly connected to Classical Syriac <i>ܓܘܡܐ</i> (gūmā), Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>גוגמא</i>, <i>גגמא</i> (“bean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dark blue enamel, especially used to adorn armour</li><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) lapis lazuli</li><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) azurite</li><li>blue copper carbonate</li><li>cornflower (<i>Centaurea cyanus</i>)</li><li>blue rock thrush (<i>Monticola solitarius</i>)</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) blue or a shade of blue</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from Hittite (kuwannan-, “precious stone, copper, blue”), itself of unknown origin, possibly Sumerian <i>𒆬𒆪</i> (ku₃-an /⁠kù.an⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye of a needle</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) orifice of the ear, earhole</li></ol><p>An old <i>r/n</i>-stem from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱewh₁-</i> (“to swell”), which is found thematicized in Avestan <i>𐬯𐬏𐬭𐬀</i> (sūra, “hole, lacuna”), as well as in Old Armenian <i>սոր</i> (sor, “hole”); a thematic <i>n</i>-stem occurs in Sanskrit <i>शून</i> (śūna, “tumid, tumescent”), <i>सून</i> (sūna, “emptiness, lack”), <i>शून्य</i> (śūnya, “empty, void”). An <i>l</i>-stem is supposed in <i>κύλα</i> (kúla, “holes under the eyelids”), but see <i>κοῖλος</i> (koîlos, “hollow”) too. These words are generally connected with the group of <i>κῠ́ω</i> (kŭ́ō, “to be pregnant with”), assuming a basic meaning "curvation", whence both "hole" and "vaulting".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>σορός</i> (sorós, “vessel”) (according to Hesychius)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beehive, hive</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>כוורתא &#47; כורתא</i> / <i>ܟܘܪܬܐ</i> (kawwārtā, “basket; beehive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) head</li></ol><p>No known cognates, therefore probably from Pre-Greek. Alternatively from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewb(ʰ)-</i> (“to bend, bulge; protrusion, hill, bowl”) (cf. Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kubъ</i> (“bucket”), perhaps also Ancient Greek <i>κύβος</i> (kúbos, “hollow in the hips”)), but the presence of <i>*b</i> is suspicious and the semantics are distant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minister of Cybele</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) ecstatic or frantic person</li></ol><p>From <i>Κυβήβη</i> (Kubḗbē, “Cybele”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axe, cleaver, large knife with which cattle were slain</li></ol><p>Unexplained, but probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elbow; cubit</li><li>(<i>in Sicily</i>) point of the elbow</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cubitum</i> (“elbow; cubit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) square</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) cube</li><li>die, dice</li><li>chance</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) cube, cubic number</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) vertebra; hollow above the hip of cattle</li></ol><p>Difficult to trace. The word passed to and from a number of languages (compare Latin <i>cubus</i> (“mass, quantity; cube”)).</p><p>Often thought to be derived from the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewbh₂-</i> (“to lie down (actively)”), reconstructed in LIV based on Latin <i>cubō</i> (“to lie down (passively)”) and some other verb forms. Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hupiz</i> (“hip”) and Latin <i>cubitus</i> (“elbow”), and (formally unrelated) Ancient Greek <i>ἀστράγαλος</i> (astrágalos, “astragalus bone, knucklebone; dice (made from a knucklebone)”) for the semantic connection between “vertebra, bone (of an animal)” and “die, dice”. Animal bones were historically used as playing dice.</p><p>According to Beekes, words for dice are often loans and the Lydians claimed to have invented the game of κύβος (kúbos), suggesting a possible loan from Lydian. He additionally rejects the connections given above to the Italic and Germanic terms.</p><p>Appears to have no relation to Arabic كَعْبَة (kaʕba).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) grave, tomb</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix <i>-αρ</i>, which can be seen also in <i>ἴκταρ</i> (íktar), <i>σκίναρ</i> (skínar) and <i>σῦφαρ</i> (sûphar). Alternatively, it may be related to <i>κῦδος</i> (kûdos, “honour”), or Arabic قَبْر (qabr, “grave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of small ship</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>most glorious, most honored</li></ol><p><i>κῦδος</i> (kûdos) +&lrm; <i>-ιστος</i> (-istos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taunt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is conceived, embryo, foetus</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) That which is swollen as the result of growth, a sprout</li></ol><p>From <i>κυέω</i> (kuéō, “to be pregnant”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of an eyesalve</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>χύτρα</i> (khútra); an earthen pot, pipkin</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Some kind of game</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circle, ring</li><li>any circular object (examples&#58; wheel, disc, plate)</li><li>crowd (of people)</li><li>marketplace</li><li>circular movement</li><li>sphere, globe</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kúklos</i>, <i>&#42;kʷókʷlos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷékʷlos</i> (“circle, wheel”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀓𐀐𐀩𐀄</i> (ku-ke-re-u).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swan</li><li>type of ship</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱewk-</i> (“white”), with cognates including Sanskrit <i>शोचति</i> (śócati, “to shine, suffer”) and <i>शुक्र</i> (śukrá, “bright, white”), and possibly Old Norse <i>Hǿnir</i> (“god associated with swans and storks”). Compare typologically Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olbǫdь</i> &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂elbʰós</i>, Tatar <i>аккош</i> (aqqoş) (<i>ак</i> (aq) + <i>кош</i> (qoş)). Could also be onomatopoeic from the sound of the swan's call (compare Russian <i>кы-кы</i> (ky-ky, “cry of a swan”)).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κύκνος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>any individual named Cycnus</li><li>the constellation Cygnus</li></ol><p>From <i>κύκνος</i> (kúknos, “swan”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>round stone, tumbler, marble (child's toy)</li><li>roller, cylinder</li><li>roll of a book, volume</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠλίνδω</i> (kŭlíndō, “to roll”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cup, often for wine</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared to Latin <i>calix</i> (“deep bowl, cup”) but a variation *a/u is unknown in Indo-European words. For the same reason, <i>κᾰ́λῠξ</i> (kắlŭx, “husk”) is not identical with this word. The presence of the variation leads to a Pre-Greek origin, as well as the typical Pre-Greek suffix -ικ-.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Defined by Hesychius as <i>σκύλαξ</i> (skúlax, “puppy”)</li></ol><p>Likely related to <i>σκύλαξ</i> (skúlax).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>curl, lock of hair</li></ol></ol><p>Uncertain. According to Furnée, possibly related to Georgian <i>კულული</i> (ḳululi, “curl, strand”).</p><p>Frisk, Chantraine, and Beekes all list it as a derivative of <i>κυλλός</i> (kullós, “crooked, deformed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Elean) puppy</li></ol><p>See <i>σκύλαξ</i> (skúlax). Presupposes the existence of a collective noun <i>&#42;κύλλα</i>&nbsp;n (&#42;kúlla, “litter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κῡ́ματα</i> (kū́mata)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) cymbal</li></ol><p>From <i>κύμβη</i> (kúmbē, “hollow of a vessel”), with a suffix <i>-αλον</i> which is found also in <i>κρόταλον</i> (krótalon, “rattle”). Yakubovich suggested a derivation from Hittite (ḫuḫupal, “kind of musical instrument, perhaps a lute or a drum”); however, the unexplained dereduplication, the <i>-μβ-</i> instead of <i>-β-</i> and the still unclear meaning of the Hittite word exclude this proposal.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>falling headfirst</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crown, crest of a helmet</li></ol><p>According to Leumann, the divergent meanings should be explained from an original substantival, technical sense. The formation favors this as well: compare especially <i>οὐρίαχος</i> (ouríakhos, “end of a spear”) and <i>στόμαχος</i> (stómakhos, “throat, gullet; stomach”). The form <i>κύμβη</i> (kúmbē, “drinking cup”) has been taken as the basis, but this is unconvincing. Other have started from a nasalized form of <i>κυβή</i> (kubḗ, “head”): this form without prenasalization shows that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hollow of a vessel: drinking cup, bowl.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Phoenician</i>) Boat.</li><li>Knapsack, wallet.</li></ol><li>synonym of <i>κύβη</i> (kúbē) Head; hence, a kind of bird, perhaps tumbler-pigeon.</li></ol><p>A wanderwort not inherited from Indo-European but perhaps borrowed from (or from the source of) words like Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kʰumbʰás</i> (“pot”) (whence Sanskrit <i>कुम्भ</i> (kumbha, “pitcher, jar”)), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;kumbā</i> (“valley”), etc. Furnee considers it a Pre-Greek borrowing and compares <i>κύπη</i> (kúpē, “kind of ship”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of an unknown bird</li></ol><p>Clearly a loanword, because of the suffix "-νδ-"; perhaps of Anatolian or Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cumin</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀓𐀖𐀜</i> (ku-mi-no), borrowed from Semitic, compare Ge'ez <i>ከሚን</i> (kämin), <i>ካሚን</i> (kamin), <i>ከሙን</i> (kämun), <i>ከሜን</i> (kämen), Arabic كَمُّون (kammūn), Hebrew <i>כַּמֹּון</i> (kammōn), Aramaic <i>כַּמֹּונָֹא</i> / <i>ܩܰܡܽܘܢܳܐ</i> (kammōnā, “cumin”), ultimately from Akkadian <i>𒂵𒈬𒉡</i> (Ú.GAMUN /⁠kamūnu⁠/, “cumin”). Note also Old Armenian <i>չաման</i> (čʻaman).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden peg</li></ol><p>The formation recalls that of the synonymous <i>πάσσαλος</i> (pássalos). According to Beekes, the word has a typical Pre-Greek appearance.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rabbit</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cunīculus</i>, although attested earlier (in Polybius) than the latter.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>more shameless, more audacious</li><li>more horrible</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, comparative ending), thus literally, “more doglike”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ribwort (<i>Plantago lanceolata</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of a prothetic vowel in the alternative form. This form may be due to folk etymology, making it appear to be from <i>κυνο-</i> (kuno-, “dog”) + <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye, face”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galingale (<i>Cyperus longus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a foreign Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinking-cup, goblet</li></ol><p>A widespread wanderwort and probably of Pre-Greek origin given the variants. Furnee compares <i>κύπη</i> (kúpē, “kind of ship”), <i>κύβος</i> (kúbos, “cube, square”), <i>κύμβη</i> (kúmbē, “drinking vessel”), and <i>κόμβος</i> (kómbos, “roll”). Also compare Latin <i>cūpa</i> (“cask, vat, tub”) and Sanskrit <i>कूप</i> (kūpa, “pit, well, hole”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>some kind of ship</li><li>some kind of hut</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kewp-</i> (“a hollow”), perhaps of substrate origin. Cognate with Old English <i>hȳf</i> (modern English <i>hive</i>), Latin <i>cūpa</i> (“cup”), and Sanskrit <i>कूप</i> (kūpa, “well, hollow, vat”). The word is attested only in Hesychius’ lexicon, so the quantity of the υ cannot be determined, but the long vowels in the Germanic, Latin, and Sanskrit words suggest the Greek may have a long vowel as well. On the other hand, the υ in the presumably related term <i>κύπελλον</i> (kúpellon, “beaker, goblet”) is short.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made from the flower of henna</li></ol><p>From <i>κύπρος</i> (kúpros, “henna”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>henna (<i>Lawsonia inermis</i> plant and dye)</li></ol><p>Identical to Hebrew <i>כֹּפֶר &#47; כּוֺפֶר</i> (kōp̱er, “pitch, tar; henna”), Aramaic <i>כּוּפְרָא</i> / <i>ܟܘܦܪܐ</i> (kūp̄rā, “pitch, tar; henna”), Ugaritic <i>𐎋𐎔𐎗</i> (kpr, “henna”), Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀓𐀞𐀫</i> (ku-pa-ro, debatable plant), Coptic <i>ⲕⲟⲩⲡⲣ</i> (koupr, “henna”), Demotic Egyptian <i>qwpr</i> (“henna”), Egyptian <i>qwpr</i> (a given name), Dongolawi <i>kofrē</i> (“henna”), all ultimately from Akkadian <i>𒇒𒌓𒀀</i> (kupru, “bitumen, pitch”) from Akkadian <i>𒅗𒉺𒊒</i> (kapāru, “to smear, to daub on”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κύπρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyprus (an island in the Mediterranean Sea)</li></ol><p>Originally a Northwest Semitic goddess name from the root <i>כ־ב־ר</i> / ك ب ر (k b r) which is attested as Arabic الْكُبْرَى (al-kubrā, literally “the great”) in a variant reading of Sura 62, Aya 9.</p><p>The island of Cyprus was a major source of copper during the Near East’s Bronze Age, so the Semitic name of this goddess’s local cult became associated with the copper metal and with the island of Cyprus itself. The Ancient Greek term is notably cognate with Sumerian <i>𒌓𒅗𒁇</i> (ud-ka-bar /⁠zabar, kabar⁠/, “bronze, copper”), Eblaite <i>𒂵𒁀𒈝</i> (ga-ba-lum /⁠kabalum⁠/, “copper”), Hurrian (kabali, kābli, “copper”), and Latin <i>aes Cyprium</i> (“Cypriot copper”), later <i>cuprum</i>. Further compare <i>Cypria</i>, <i>Cypris</i>, alternative Latin names for the goddess Venus (Aphrodite).</p><p>Unrelated to the words for the Mediterranean cypress (<i>Cupressus sempervirens</i>) <i>κυπάρισσος</i> and the word for the plant henna (<i>Lawsonia alba</i>) <i>κύπρος</i> (kúpros), though the latter is also red in color.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend forward, stoop down</li><li>to hang one's head from shame</li><li>to bow down under a burden</li></ol><p>Related to, and perhaps derived from, <i>κυφός</i> (kuphós, “bent forward, hunchbacked”), which Beekes suspects is of Pre-Greek origin; see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Athens</i>) triangular tablets, forming a three-sided pyramid, turning on a pivot, upon which the early laws were inscribed</li><li>(<i>later</i>) all pillars or tablets with inscriptions</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the Pillars of Hercules</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pettifogging lawyer, as if a walking statute book</li></ol><p>As a technical expression, suspected of being a loan, perhaps from Pre-Greek. According to Beekes, the older connection with <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “wrist”) is unacceptable. Fick and Kretschmer also adduced <i>Κύρβαντες</i> (Kúrbantes), which would have been named after their whirling dances.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden masks</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek and related to <i>κυριττοί</i> (kurittoí, “players who wear wooden masks”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sovereign power</li><li>a decisive moment</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>main; major; primary; principal</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) ruling; governing; having power</li><li>(<i>of events</i>) decisive; critical; authorized; valid; legal; entitled</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) time being fixed; set; appointed</li><li>(<i>of language</i>) literal</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord; master; guardian; ruler; owner; teacher</li><li>(Koine,&#32;vocative) sir</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) God; Yahweh; Jehovah</li><li>(Koine,&#32;theology) the Holy Spirit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kúrios</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱewh₁-</i> (“to swell; to spread out; to be strong; to prevail”). By surface analysis, <i>κῦρος</i> (kûros, “supremacy”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix).</p><p>Cognate with <i>κῠ́ω</i> (kŭ́ō), Latin <i>cumulus</i>, <i>cavus</i>, Welsh <i>cawr</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booty, prey, spoil</li></ol><p>From <i>κυρέω, κύρω</i> (kuréō, kúrō, “to meet with, come upon, find”); <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit upon</li><li>to meet with (esp. accidentally)</li><li>to attain, obtain</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes rejects connections to <i>καιρός</i> (kairós, “measure; season”) and <i>κυρίττω</i> (kuríttō, “to butt with the horns”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pudenda muliebria</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes suspects a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bladder cherry, winter cherry (<i>Alkekengi officinarum</i>, syn. <i>Physalis alkekengi</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κῠ́στῐς</i> (kŭ́stĭs, “bladder”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bladder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewH-</i> (“to cover”) or from <i>κύω</i> (kúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower of the pomegranate</li><li>calyx of the pomegranate</li><li>hypocist (<i>Cytinus hypocistis</i>)</li></ol><p>Because of the meaning "calyx", connected with <i>κῠ́τος</i> (kŭ́tos, “hollow; vessel”). However, Furnée compares <i>κῠ́τᾰρον</i> (kŭ́tăron, “spoon”), <i>κῠ́δᾰρος</i> (kŭ́dăros, “small ship”), <i>κῠ́ττᾰρος</i> (kŭ́ttăros, “cell of a honeycomb”) and <i>κῠττός</i> (kŭttós, “receptacle”). This points to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree medick, moon trefoil (<i>Medicago arborea</i>)</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek. Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀓𐀳𐀰</i> (ku-te-so).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hollow</li><li>any vessel; e.g. a jar, an urn, a vase, etc.</li><li>(<i>used of any hollow container</i>) the occiput, the chest, a plant’s root, the uterus, an ox’s abomasum, the body in general, the trunk thereof, metaphorically the polis, etc.</li></ol><p>Uncertain. According to Beekes, derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewH-</i> (“cover, skin”) (via a form <i>&#42;(s)kHu-t-</i>); related to <i>σκῦτος</i> (skûtos, “hide, leather”), Latin <i>cutis</i> (“skin”), and English <i>hide</i>, but the presumed laryngeal metathesis is problematic. Alternatively from <i>&#42;kew- ~ &#42;ḱew-</i> (“hollow”), but this is also reconstructed as <i>&#42;ḱewh₁-</i>. In view of the formal problems and the word's semantic category, a substrate origin is possible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the bent yoke of the plough</li><li>a sort of pillory in which criminals were fastened by the neck, the stocks</li><li>one who has had his neck in the pillory, a knave</li></ol><p>From <i>κῡφός</i> (kūphós, “curved, round”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown migratory bird, perhaps:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>corncrake (<i>Crex crex</i>)</li><li>water rail (<i>Rallus aquaticus</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>Clearly a Pre-Greek word, in view of the variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swift (<i>Apus apus</i>)</li></ol><p>Clearly a Pre-Greek word, like <i>κῠψέλη</i> (kŭpsélē, “beehive”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κύψελος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypselus</li></ol><p>From κυψέλη (kupsélē, “chest”) + -ος (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>present and imperfect</i>) to conceive, become pregnant</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>rarely&#32;with accusative</i>) to become pregnant with...</li></ol><li>(<i>aorist</i>) to impregnate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱewh₁-</i> (“to swell”). Cognate with <i>κύριος</i> (kúrios), Latin <i>cumulus</i>, <i>cavus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dog</li><li>a bitch</li><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) a bitch (<i>used of women, to denote shamelessness or audacity</i>)</li><li>an offensive person</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱwṓ</i> (“dog”). Cognates include Latin <i>canis</i>, Sanskrit <i>श्वन्</i> (śván) and Old English <i>hund</i> (English <i>hound</i>). The final <i>-ν</i> (-n) in the nominative singular which was absent in <i>&#42;ḱwṓ</i> was restored in Greek by analogy to other forms in the paradigm.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) poppyhead, head of plants</li><li>cup shaped like a poppyhead</li></ol><p>According to Furnée and Beekes, the phonetic variation in the alternative forms points to a Pre-Greek origin, in addition to irregular similarities to other words for "cup", like <i>κώδων</i> (kṓdōn, “bell”), <i>κοτύλη</i> (kotúlē, “cup”), <i>κόνδυ</i> (kóndu, “cup”), <i>κώθων</i> (kṓthōn, “Laconian drinking vessel”), as well as, outside of Greek, Etruscan <i>𐌒𐌖𐌕𐌖𐌌</i> (qutum, “kind of vessel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bell</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin, based on the lack of a clear root and corresponding verb to which the word would trace back to, as well as similarities to words like <i>κώδεια</i> (kṓdeia, “poppyhead”), which suggest a substrate borrowing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laconian drinking vessell used by soldiers</li><li>drinking bout, carousal</li><li>name of the inner harbour at Carthage</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin. The term has been compared to Georgian (kotoxi, “cup”) and Mingrelian (koto, “cup”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow of the knee, poplit, ham</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek root of <i>κῶλον</i> (kôlon, “member, limb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hindrance, impediment</li><li>prevention, precaution</li></ol><p><i>κωλῡ́ω</i> (kōlū́ō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nutmeg (<i>Myristica fragrans</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an unwalled village or country town</li><li>a quarter or ward of a city</li></ol><p>Uncertain. In earlier literature, the term was derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱōymeh₂</i>, itself related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱóymos</i>. Beekes, however, rejects this reconstruction, arguing that the known rules of PIE do not allow for such a lengthened-grade form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bundle of hay, or (in the plural) of bamboo</li><li>a branch of laurel placed before the gates, per Hesychius</li><li>(in the plural only) reed bed, per Theophrastus's Historia Plantarum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poison hemlock (<i>Conium maculatum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>κόνις</i> (kónis, “dust”) +&lrm; <i>-ειον</i> (-eion). This reference to dust is often used figuratively in contexts involving deadly exertion, such as fights to the death. The suffix, which forms nouns indicating a place or tool, suggests that poison hemlock is the agent or setting of dire struggle, leading those who consume it to metaphorically bite the dust. This is distinct from the variant suffixes <i>-αιον</i> (-aion) and <i>-ιον</i> (-ion) and should not be confused with <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p><p>Historically, the plant was ground into a powder and used to carry out death sentences, notably through the 'hemlock cup' or 'poisoned cup,' as detailed in German Wikipedia about its preparation and use. Poison hemlock's association with <i>ἀκόνιτον</i> (akóniton) arises from their frequent confusion outside their native ranges, where both plants are infamous for their lethal properties.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnat, mosquito</li></ol><p>Beekes dismisses a derivation from <i>κῶνος</i> (kônos, “cone”) and <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “face”) and prefers to derive it from Pre-Greek. Compare, however, Egyptian <i>ḫnws</i>, <i>ḫnms</i> (“a kind of stinging insect”), which has also been suggested as a source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a handle (e.g. of an oar)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kṓpā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kóh₂p-eh₂</i>, from the root <i>&#42;keh₂p-</i> (“to seize, grab”), which also gives <i>κάπτω</i> (káptō, “gulp down”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leathern sack or wallet for provisions</li><li>punchbag in the gymnasium</li><li>leathern quiver</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) scrotum</li></ol><p>Cognate to Latin <i>corium</i> (“leather”) and Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;korukos</i> (“leather boat”), and ultimately a vriddhied derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i>. Nikolaev dismisses Beekes' claim that the word is from Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κώρυκος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corycus; Kızkalesi, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>crasis of <i>καὶ ἐκ</i> (kaì ek)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>καὶ</i> (kaì) +&lrm; <i>ἐκείνων</i> (ekeínōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>crasis of <i>καὶ</i> (kaì) and <i>ᾰ̓́ν</i> (ắn)</li><li>crasis of <i>καὶ</i> (kaì) and <i>ᾱ̓́ν</i> (ā́n)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) wood, logs, timber, especially for ships</li></ol><p>Connected with <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “to burn”) as "firewood", by comparison with the synonymous <i>δᾱλός</i> (dālós, “firebrand”). However, according to Beekes, a Pre-Greek origin is much more likely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>καὶ</i> (kaì) +&lrm; <i>εἶτᾰ</i> (eîtă), literally “and then”</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long-tailed monkey</li></ol><p>The Greek, but also the Latin variants, point to a Pre-Greek word. It was previously compared with Sanskrit <i>कपि</i> (kapi, “ape”) and Hebrew <i>קוּף</i> (qōf, “ape”) and assumed to be a loan from an unknown source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care for others</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) troubles</li><li>cares for the dead, mourning</li><li>an object of care</li><li>connection by marriage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kā́dos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱeh₂dos</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱeh₂d-</i> (“care, grief”). Cognate with Old English <i>hete</i> (English <i>hate</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) shafts of an arrow, arrows, projectiles</li></ol><p>It has been compared with Sanskrit <i>शर्या</i> (śaryā, “cane, arrow, missile”), <i>शल्य</i> (śalya, “spear, javelin”) and further Middle Irish <i>cail</i> (“spear”), Old Norse <i>hali</i> (“tail”). According to Pokorny, these are all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱel(h₃)-</i>. However, all these words go back to a root with a short vowel, as opposed to this word, with a long one. Connection with <i>κᾶλον</i> (kâlon, “wood, timber”) is rejected by Frisk and DELG.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garden, orchard or plantation</li><li>enclosure for the Olympic games</li><li>sort of fashion of cropping the hair</li><li>female genitals</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;keh₂po-s</i> (“land, garden”), which seems to be from <i>&#42;kap-</i> (“to seize”) (though Beekes is skeptical of this relation and tentatively separates the two). Almost identical in formation (except for the ending) to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hōbō</i> (“piece of land”) (&lt; <i>&#42;keh₂p-h₂</i>); Albanian <i>kopsht</i> (“garden”) is another cognate. More distant connections include Latin <i>capiō</i> (“to seize, capture”), while <i>κάπετος</i> (kápetos, “ditch, trench”) and Old High German <i>habaro</i> (“oats”) are probably not related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heart</li><li>the seat of the will</li><li>the seat of the passions</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱḗr</i>. If not simply directly inherited from that form, perhaps a contraction of <i>κέᾰρ</i> (kéăr), related to <i>κᾰρδῐ́ᾱ</i> (kărdĭ́ā). Alternatively, Liddell and Scott (1940) suggest that <i>κέᾰρ</i> (kéăr) may have been a back-formation from <i>κῆρ</i> (kêr), by analogy with <i>ἔαρ</i> (éar, “spring”) &gt; <i>ἦρ</i> (êr); however, this does not explain why the monosyllabic accent shift does not occur in the oblique cases, which would be expected if the term originated as a monosyllable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) with all one's heart; heartily</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heart</li><li>the seat of the will</li><li>the seat of the passions</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱḗr</i>. If not simply directly inherited from that form, perhaps a contraction of <i>κέᾰρ</i> (kéăr), related to <i>κᾰρδῐ́ᾱ</i> (kărdĭ́ā). Alternatively, Liddell and Scott (1940) suggest that <i>κέᾰρ</i> (kéăr) may have been a back-formation from <i>κῆρ</i> (kêr), by analogy with <i>ἔαρ</i> (éar, “spring”) &gt; <i>ἦρ</i> (êr); however, this does not explain why the monosyllabic accent shift does not occur in the oblique cases, which would be expected if the term originated as a monosyllable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herald, pursuivant</li><li>public messenger, envoy</li><li>crier, who made proclamation and kept order in assemblies</li><li>auctioneer</li><li>trumpet shell (<i>Charonia lampas</i>)</li><li>an attendant of kings or chiefs sometimes found discharging the general duties of a slave or servant, particularly in Homer</li></ol><p>By the long -ύ- and enlarged -κ-, Beekes suggests it to be a Pre-Greek word, but for Bernal this is from Egyptian <i>qꜣ ḫrw</i> (“loud voice”), both rejecting the frequent comparison to Sanskrit <i>का॒रु</i> (kārú, “singer; poet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whale, sea monster</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Cetus</li></ol><li>abyss</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) strength, vigour, force</li></ol><p>Meier-Brügger assumes a reduplicated form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱewh₁-</i> (“to swell, be strong”), like in <i>κύω</i> (kúō, “to conceive”). This is accepted by de Lamberterie, but the long <i>ῑ</i> cannot be explained this way. Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek instead.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renown, glory</li></ol><p>Assumed to be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewh₁-</i> (“to perceive, pay attention”) and therefore related to <i>κοέω</i> (koéō), Latin <i>caveō</i>, Sanskrit <i>कवि</i> (kaví, “wise, poet, seer, sage”), Persian <i>کی</i> (kay, “poet-king”), Lithuanian <i>kavoti</i> (“safeguard, tend”), Old Armenian <i>ցուցանեմ</i> (cʻucʻanem, “to show”), Polish <i>cześć</i> (“glory”) and <i>cud</i> (“miracle”), Old East Slavic <i>кудесникъ</i> (kudesnikŭ, “wizard, mage”).</p><p>However, Beekes rejects this and leads Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čudo</i> (“miracle”) and κῦδος back to a Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;kewd-s-</i> (“miraculous power”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κῦμα</i> (kûma)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wave, billow, swell</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a wave or flood of people</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) wave of adversity</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) a waved or ogee moulding, cyma</li></ol><li>fetus, embryo</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sprout of a plant</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kūmə</i>, equivalent to <i>κῠ́ω</i> (kŭ́ō, “conceive”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sir, lord (form of address)</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>κύρης</i> (kúrēs), the later form of <i>κύριος</i> (kúrios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supreme power, authority</li><li>confirmation, validity, certainty</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>κῡ́ρῐος</i> (kū́rĭos), from <i>&#42;ḱewh₁-</i> (“to swell, be strong”), the same root as <i>κυέω</i> (kuéō, “to be pregnant”), <i>κῦμα</i> (kûma, “wave”), Sanskrit <i>शवस्</i> (śavas, “strength, power”) and Irish <i>curadh</i> (“hero”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Κῦρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cyrus</li><li>Kura (a river in the Armenian Highland forming the boundary between Armenia and Caucasian Albania)</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎤𐎢𐎽𐎢𐏁</i> (ku-u-ru-u-š /⁠Kuruš⁠/).</p><p>Related to Old Armenian <i>Կուր</i> (Kur). Perhaps from Urartian <i>𒆳𒄣𒊑𒀀𒉌</i> (<sup>KUR</sup>qu-ri-a-ni /⁠Quriani⁠/), a land possibly in the upper reaches of the Kura river.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of Egyptian compound incense</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>kꜣpt</i> (“incense”), from <i>kꜣp</i> (“to cense”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepskin, fleece, used for a bed cover</li></ol><p><i>κῶᾰς</i> (kôăs, “fleece”) +&lrm; <i>-δῐον</i> (-dĭon, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) fleece</li></ol><p>Together with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀒𐀺</i> (ko-wo) borrowed from the Carian word for sheep attested as the glosses <i>κοῖον</i> (koîon) and <i>κόον</i> (kóon) in two scholia to the Iliad XIV, 255; ultimately a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ówis</i> (“sheep”). Doublet of <i>οἶς</i> (oîs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part of something, member</li><li>part of the body: limb, leg, arm</li><li>part of a building: side, front</li><li>part of a sentence: clause</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) carcass, carcasses</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek origin, or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deep sleep; lethargy</li><li>coma</li></ol><p>Unknown. Brugmann's derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kōi-mn̥</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱey-</i> (“to lie down, settle”) (whence also <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai, “to lie outstretched”)), is rejected by Beekes for what is in his view an unacceptable lengthened grade ablaut. Another theory tentatively derives the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱumbʰ-</i> (compare Latin <i>incumbō</i> (“to lie down”), English <i>coomb</i> and Old English <i>cumb</i> (“hollow; narrow valley”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>festival, procession, revel, carousal</li><li>the band of revellers of such an event</li><li>a song or ode sung at such an event</li></ol><p>The origin is uncertain.</p><p>Dunkel (<i>apud</i> <i>LIV</i>) derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱómso-</i>, from <i>&#42;ḱems-</i> (“to announce, proclaim”), whence Sanskrit <i>शंसति</i> (śaṃsati, “to declare”), Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;cánhati</i> (“to declare, explain”), Latin <i>cēnseō</i> (“to give an opinion”).</p><p>On the other hand, Janda (<i>apud</i> <i>LIV</i>) derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kóh₂-mo-</i>, from <i>&#42;keh₂-</i> (“to desire, wish”), whence Sanskrit <i>काम</i> (kā́ma, “desire, wish”), Latin <i>cārus</i> (“dear”).</p><p>Beekes remarks that, since the precise development of the meaning of <i>κῶμος</i> (kômos) is uncertain, etymological suggestions remain highly hypothetical. He suggests that this is a Pre-Greek word, though does not discount the possibility of derivation from <i>&#42;ḱómso-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) pine cone</li><li>edible seed of a pine tree</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) pine tree</li><li>cone or peak of a helmet</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) cone</li><li>iron pole round which grain is piled in conical shape</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱóh₃nos</i>, from the root <i>&#42;ḱeh₃-</i> (“to sharpen”), like Sanskrit <i>शाण</i> (śāṇa, “whetstone”), Latin <i>cōs</i> (“whetstone”) and Latin <i>catus</i> (“clever, sharp”). Schwyzer, however, considered the word to be a foreign borrowing; this suggestion is supported by the variant adduced by Furnée, who suggests a Pre-Greek origin, and supported by Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>κόρος</i> (kóros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lambda, the name for the eleventh letter Λ (L), λ (l) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤋</i> (l‬ /⁠lāmed⁠/).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λάβδα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythological character</i>) Labda</li></ol><p>Derived from λάβδα (lábda).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lambdoid figure, i.e., one like that of the letter <i>Λ</i> (L, “lambda”)</li></ol><p><i>λάβδα</i> (lábda) +&lrm; <i>-ωμᾰ</i> (-ōmă)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European seabass (<i>Dicentrarchus labrax</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λάβρος</i> (lábros, “furious, fierce”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Homeric,&#32;of wind and water) furious, boisterous</li><li>(Homeric,&#32;of the Hellespont personified) huge, mighty</li><li>(Post-Homeric,&#32;of men) boisterous, turbulent</li><li>(Post-Homeric,&#32;of men,&#32;of speech) hasty</li><li>(Post-Homeric,&#32;of men) fierce, violent, impetuous</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes rejects the traditional connections to <i>λαβεῖν</i> (labeîn) and <i>λάζομαι</i> (lázomai, “to seize, grasp”) and tentatively derives the word from Pre-Greek, albeit without strong evidence of typical Pre-Greek alternation patterns.</p><p>Another theory derives the word from a dissimilation of from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rabʰros</i>, from <i>&#42;rabʰ-</i> (“to rage, to be furious, to anger”) (whence also Latin <i>rabiō</i>, <i>rabiēs</i> and Sanskrit <i>रभस्</i> (rabhas)); though semantically sound, this is phonetically difficult, as the expected form going from <i>&#42;ράφρος</i> (&#42;ráphros) &gt; <i>λάβρος</i> (lábros) requires two consonant changes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lydian word for axe</li></ol><p>From Lydian <i>𐤩𐤠𐤡𐤭𐤧𐤳</i> (lapryš) and possibly related to <i>λαβύρινθος</i> (labúrinthos, “labyrinth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thin broad cake, pancake</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λᾰ́γῡνος</i> (lắgūnos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the hare</li></ol><p>From <i>λαγώς</i> (lagṓs, “hare”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cup or vase</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Furnée compares <i>λήκυθος</i> (lḗkuthos, “oil flask, lekythos”), but this is uncertain. Kronasser compares Akkadian <i>𒂁𒆷𒄩𒀭</i> (DUG.LA.ḪA.AN /⁠laḫannu⁠/, “flask, bottle”), itself derived from Sumerian <i>𒂁𒆷𒄩𒀭𒁍𒁕</i> (dug.la.ḫa.an.gid<sub>2</sub>.da /⁠laḫangiddû⁠/, “beer jug”). Perhaps from the same Pre-Greek source of <i>λάγῡνος</i> (lágūnos, “flask”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lecherous, lustful (of the male)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>λαγαίω</i> (lagaíō, “to release”) and <i>λαγαρός</i> (lagarós, “slack, loose”). Maybe these words derive from the same Proto-Indo-European root of Latin <i>laxus</i> (“loose, free”) and English <i>slack</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flagon, pitcher, flask</li></ol><p>Probably Pre-Greek. Compare Akkadian <i>𒆷𒄩𒉡</i> (laḫannu, “flask, bottle (of clay, glass or metal)”), Hittite <i>𒆷𒄩𒉌</i> (lahan(n)i), Ancient Greek <i>λεκάνη</i> (lekánē, “basin”), Aramaic <i>לִגְנָא</i> (lignā, “a unit of square measure”) &gt; rare Arabic لُجْنَة (lujna), Aramaic <i>לְגִינָא</i> (ləgīnā), <i>לְגִינְתָּא</i> (ləgīntā, “jug for wine and oil, lagin, a unit of volume measure”), Ge'ez <i>ልጕን</i> (ləgʷən), <i>ልጐን</i> (ləgʷän, “a unit of measure, a pot used for storing and for measuring”), Amharic <i>ለገን</i> (lägän, “a fictile vessel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λήδανον</i> (lḗdanon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize; to grasp</li><li>to receive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hlaďďomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sl̥h₂gʷ-yé-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;sleh₂gʷ-</i>, the same root as <i>λᾰμβᾰ́νω</i> (lămbắnō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>λάθρῃ</i> (láthrēi)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>λάθρῃ</i> (láthrēi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>λάθρῃ</i> (láthrēi)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>λάθρῃ</i> (láthrēi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>secretly, by stealth</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>unbeknownst to</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō, “to escape notice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chickling vetch (<i>Lathyrus sativus</i>)</li></ol><p>No etymology. Traditionally compared to similar words for "lentil", like Latin <i>lens</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>лѧща</i> (lęšta) and German <i>Linse</i>. According to Beekes, the structure (suffix <i>-υρ-</i>) could point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō, “to escape notice”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂-</i> (“to be hidden, be covered”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pond in which water-fowl are kept</li><li>cistern, tank</li><li>pit, reservoir</li><li>kind of garment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lə́kwos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lókus</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>lacus</i>, Old Irish <i>loch</i>, and Old English <i>lagu</i> (English <i>lay</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestle</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>λακτίζω</i> (laktízō, “to trample, tread”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) synonym of <i>λάβδα</i> (lábda, “the letter Λ, λ”, “lambda”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>babbler, croaker</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a name of the green frog</li><li>a name of a bird</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>λᾰλῐᾱ́</i> (lălĭā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talk, prattle</li><li>prater, chatterer</li><li>byword, a person talked about</li></ol><p>From <i>λαλέω</i> (laléō, “to chat, to prat”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pebbles (small stones rounded by the action of water)</li></ol><p>Said to be from <i>λαλέω</i> (laléō, “to prattle”) because pebbles prattle in the stream.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>λᾰλῐᾱ́</i> (lălĭā́)</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰ́λος</i> (lắlos): as a noun, a substantivisation of its neuter forms; as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>talkative, babbling, loquacious</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Related to Ancient Greek <i>λαλέω</i> (laléō), itself possibly of imitative origin. It may relate to German <i>lallen</i> (“to babble”). Beekes lists the term as a back-formation from <i>λαλέω</i> (laléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λάβδα</i> (lábda)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λάβδωμᾰ</i> (lábdōmă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Species of fierce shark</li></ol><p>Possible connection with <i>λαιμός</i> (laimós, “throat, gullet”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λάμια)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Lamia, a fabulous monster said to feed on man's flesh</li><li>a bugbear with which to frighten children; bogeyman</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from <i>λαμυρός</i> (lamurós, “avaricious, voracious, coquettish”), a Pre-Greek word probably related to <i>λαιμός</i> (laimós, “throat, gullet”). Others suggest a late Proto-Indo-European stem <i>&#42;lem-</i> (“ghost, nocturnal spirit”) that was ultimately borrowed from a substrate language such as Etruscan or Anatolian. Compare Latin <i>lemures</i> (“ghosts of the departed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>according to a scholium of Horace, this word means: <i>ingluvies</i> (“crop of birds”).</li></ol><p>Probably from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>λαιμός</i> (laimós, “throat, gullet”) and <i>λαμυρός</i> (lamurós, “full of abysses; gluttonous”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λάμος)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Lamus; Limonlu River, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scum, foam which forms on the surface of wine, or other liquids left to stand</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) filth, decay (of the underworld)</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>λάμπω</i> (lámpō, “to lighten, glow”) does not explain the forms without nasal, which would suggest Pre-Greek origin; see also <i>λέμφος</i> (lémphos, “snot”), which is likely related. On the other hand, Furnée prefers connecting the word to Georgian <i>ლოპო</i> (loṗo, “sap, slime”) and its variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, be bright, give light</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(transitive,&#32;primarily poetic or comedic) to illumine, cause to shine</li></ol><li>(<i>sound</i>) to be loud, clear</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be famous, conspicuous</li></ol><p>Possibly from a nasalized variant of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂p-</i> (“to shine”), but problematic due to said nasalization. Related to Scottish Gaelic <i>loisg</i> (“to burn”), Welsh <i>llachar</i> (“glow”), Hittite <i>𒆷𒀊</i> (lapp-, “to glow, flash”), Latvian <i>lāpa</i> (“torch, flame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with the foot, with the heel</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lagjaz</i> (English <i>leg</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monk's rhubarb (<i>Rumex patientia</i>)</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>λάπαθα</i> (lápatha, “faeces”) recalls <i>λαπάσσω</i> (lapássō, “to empty the bowels”). Because of the formation, foreign origin is suspected. Put as possibly from Pre-Greek by Beekes; likely from a Balkan source, see Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;loboda</i> (“orach”) for farther relations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitfall for wild beasts</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix <i>-θος</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λᾰ́μπη</i> (lắmpē, “foam on the top of liquids”)</li></ol><p>See <i>λᾰ́μπη</i> (lắmpē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lap with the tongue</li><li>to drink greedily, gulp down</li></ol><p>An onomatopoeic word. In other Indo-European languages we find Albanian <i>lap</i> (“to slurp”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lopati</i> (“to eat greedily”), Lithuanian <i>lapènti</i> (“to absorb greedily”), Old English <i>lapian</i> (“to lap”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stibium, antimony</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>larch (<i>Larix decidua</i>)</li><li>Venice turpentine</li></ol><p>Possibly from Gaulish, itself from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;daru</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charcoal basket</li></ol><p>Because of <i>ναρκίον</i> (narkíon, “bag, wineskin”), it has been derived from <i>&#42;νάρκος</i> (&#42;nárkos) by influence of <i>λάρναξ</i> (lárnax, “chest, coffin”). However, Beekes finds this hypothesis doubtful.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffer, box, chest</li><li>cinerary urn, coffin, larnax</li><li>bathtub</li></ol><p>Unknown. The suffix "-ακ-" is seen in other instrument names like <i>πίναξ</i> (pínax), <i>κάμαξ</i> (kámax), <i>δίφραξ</i> (díphrax). According to Beekes, this suffix is typical of Pre-Greek, as is also the cluster -ρν-. He considers <i>νάρναξ</i> (nárnax) a variant of this word, explaining the intial consonant from dissimilation (l-r &gt; n-r).</p><p>Likely related to Etruscan <i>𐌋𐌀𐌓𐌍𐌀𐌑</i> (larnaś), suggested to have been borrowed from Greek, if it denotes a type of vessel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of ravenous sea bird, perhaps the seamew, the gull</li><li>greedy demagogue</li></ol><p>Probably of onomatopoeic origin but compare Armenian <i>լոր</i> (lor, “quail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>larynx; upper part of windpipe</li><li>gullet; throat</li></ol><p>Connected to Latin <i>lurcō</i> (“to eat greedily, guzzle”) and Middle High German <i>slurc</i> (“throat”) by Pokorny, possibly reshaped according to <i>φᾰ́ρῠγξ</i> (phắrŭnx, “throat; pharynx”). Pre-Greek according to Beekes, due to the prenasalized suffix <i>-υγγ-</i> (-ung-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laser, juice of the asafoetida or silphium</li></ol><p>Unexplained foreign word. According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insult, mockery</li></ol><p>The connection with Latin <i>lascīvus</i> (“wanton, lusty”) is inadequate. Perhaps from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaggy, woolly</li><li>hairy</li><li>bushy, overgrown</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (for which compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;walþuz</i> (“forest”)) or <i>&#42;h₂welh₁-</i> (“wool”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ring, rattle, crash, crack</li><li>to shriek, scream, howl, bay</li><li>to shout, scream, cry aloud</li><li>to noise abroad</li><li>to sing</li><li>to shriek forth, utter aloud</li><li>to crack or burst asunder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;l̥h₂-k-sḱéti</i> (“to bark, growl”). Cognate with <i>λακάω</i> (lakáō, “to burst asunder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catamite, cinaedus, debauchee</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin together with <i>λάσται</i> (lástai, “whores”) and <i>λαίσιτος</i> (laísitos, “cinaedus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) the drops of wine in the bottom of the cup which were thrown into a basin with a splash</li><li>a water-quadruped&#59; perhaps a beaver</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lat-</i> (“wet, damp; swamp”) or Pre-Greek through an intermediary source such as Illyrian. Compare Albanian <i>lag</i> from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;lauga</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nile perch (<i>Lates niloticus</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably from Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hired servant</li><li>a handmaid</li><li>a slave</li><li>a god's servant</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰ́τρον</i> (lắtron, “hire, payment”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pay, hire</li></ol><p>Putatively from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂t-</i> (“to grant; to possess”) or <i>&#42;leh₁-t-</i> (“to let, grant”), thereby cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lēþą</i> (“possession, ownership”) (though there are phonetic problems) and Latin <i>latrō</i> (“mercenary”).</p><p>On the other hand, Beekes rejects the Indo-European etymology and suggests Pre-Greek origin. He considers <i>-ρ-</i> (-r-) in this case to be a Pre-Greek suffix. The Latin cognate may instead be borrowed from an unattested form <i>&#42;λάτρων</i> (&#42;látrōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>μυῖα</i> (muîa, “fly”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of the geminates "-ττ-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoils taken in war, pillage, booty</li></ol><p>Traditionally viewed as a suffix <i>-ρ-</i> (-r-), added to a <i>u-</i>stem from the root of Sanskrit <i>लभते</i> (labhate, “to take, seize”). Beekes considers the suffix <i>-ῡρ-</i> (-ūr-) to be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garden herb, opposite to wild plants</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) vegetables</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) vegetable market</li></ol><p>Probably of Pre-Greek origin, as suggested by the suffix <i>-ανον</i>. The denominative simplex <i>λαχαίνω</i> (lakhaínō, “to dig”), which has only late attestations, is probably a back-formation from compounded forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soft, woolly hair, down, as of the first beard</li><li>thin hair</li><li>soft nap or pile on cloth</li><li>hair or fur of wild beasts</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the quills of the hedgehog</li><li>leafage</li></ol><p>From earlier *ϝλακσνά (<i>wlaksná</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂welh₁-</i> (“hair, wool”), like Latin <i>vellus</i> (“fleece”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lot, destiny, fate</li><li>portion obtained by lot, share</li><li>appointed office</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lengʰ-</i> (“to obtain”), the same root of <i>λαγχάνω</i> (lankhánō, “to obtain by lot”) and <i>λόγχη</i> (lónkhē, “share”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) turnip</li></ol><p>Unknown, but probably a Wanderwort related to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;lapt-</i> (“turnip”), Sumerian <i>𒇻𒂠𒊬</i> (LU-UB₂<sup>ŠAR</sup> /⁠lub⁠/, “turnip”). Only attested as a gloss in Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see</li><li>to seize, hold</li><li>to want</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of stone, rocky</li><li>stonehearted</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾶας</i> (lâas, “stone, rock”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lioness</li></ol><p>From <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kettle, cauldron</li></ol><p>From λείβω.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coloured edging or border of a garment</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) border of the womb</li></ol><p>No etymology. Perhaps Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put in order, arrange, gather</li><li>to choose, count, reckon</li><li>to say, speak, converse, tell a story</li><li>(<i>middle passive</i>) to mean</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;légō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leǵ-</i> (“to collect, gather”). Cognates include Latin <i>legō</i> and Albanian <i>mbledh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) gruel of pulse or cereals</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) yolk of an egg</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek origin, based on the suffix and semantic category.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dish, pot, pan</li></ol><p>Alleged by Beekes to be similar to <i>ἄγγος</i> (ángos, “vessel”) and related to <i>λεκάνη</i> (lekánē, “basin, dish”) and derived from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed</li></ol><p>From <i>λεχ-</i> (lekh-, “to lie down”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron, instrument noun suffix), or directly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;légʰtrom</i>. Cognate with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lehtr</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dinghy, cockboat</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒄑𒈣</i> (eleppum, “wood boat, wooden ship, skiff”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is peeled off, rind, husk</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in the phrase <i>ἰχθύων λέμματα</i>) (fish)scales</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;used of one who has been swindled) a mere husk</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “I peel”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>star grass (plant of the genus <i>Callitriche</i>)</li></ol><p>Given the presence of the cluster <i>-μν-</i> and the specific meaning of the noun, it could be a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mucous discharge from the nose, snot</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) putrescent carcasses</li></ol><p>Likely related to <i>λάμπη</i> (lámpē, “scum, foam”), which, if so, shows characteristic Pre-Greek variation for the two words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a saying, speech</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a way of speaking, diction, style</li></ol><li>word, phrase</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>explanation</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) a word peculiar in form or significance</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “speak”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broad leather strap fastening the yoke to the neck, and joined to the girth</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the necessity</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>λεπίς</i> (lepís), <i>λέπω</i> (lépō) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lep-</i> (“to peel, flay”), or Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bare rock, scaur, crag</li></ol><p>The similarity with Latin <i>lapis</i> (“stone”) is hardly accidental; a Mediterranean substrate borrowing is most probable, perhaps through Pre-Greek. Otherwise, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lep-</i> (“to peel”) (compare <i>secō</i> – <i>saxum</i>; <i>rumpō</i> – <i>rupēs</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rind, husk, scale</li></ol><p>From <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to peel”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) psoriasis &amp; similar skin disorders</li><li>(Koine,&#32;medicine) leprosy</li></ol><p>Resembles <i>λεπίς</i> (lepís, “scale”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ρᾱ</i> (&#42;-rā), but with unclear morphology. Perhaps both words are ultimately Pre-Greek; compare <i>λεβηρῐ́ς</i> (lebērĭ́s, “shed skin”), and see <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to peel”) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) rind, shell, husk, chaff</li></ol><p>From <i>λέπος</i> (lépos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to peel, bark, strip off the rind or husks</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to give a hiding to, thrash</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to masturbate, indulge in indecent gestures</li></ol><p>The primary thematic present has no direct correspondences outside Greek. There are few nominal formations resembling the Greek forms: Lithuanian <i>lãpas</i> (“leaf”) and <i>lõpas</i> (“rag, piece”), Albanian <i>lapë</i> (“rag, leaf, peritoneum”), Russian <i>ла́поть</i> (lápotʹ, “bast shoe, bast sandal”). The form <i>λέπος</i> (lépos) was also compared with the <i>s-</i>stem Latin <i>lepōs</i> (“grace”). However, Beekes concludes that the verb is not Indo-European: in fact there is hardly any exact correspondence and the semantics are not very convincing. Note the forms <i>λεπάγιον</i> (lepágion), <i>λεπακινός</i> (lepakinós), <i>λέπασμα</i> (lépasma), <i>λέπυρον</i> (lépuron), <i>λεπύχανον</i> (lepúkhanon), <i>λέπρα</i> (lépra), <i>λώψ</i> (lṓps) and <i>λῶπος</i> (lôpos), with suffixes and/or alternations of Pre-Greek appearance.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of fish</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λέρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leros, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couch, hence funeral bier, tomb</li><li>lounging place, resort of idlers and beggars</li><li>public building or hall used as a lounge or meeting place</li><li>talk or gossip</li></ol><p>Traditionally taken to be from an earlier <i>&#42;λέχ-σκᾱ</i> (&#42;lékh-skā) with metathesis of the <i>-σ-</i> (-s-), a nominal formation from the same root as <i>λέχομαι</i> (lékhomai, “to recline”) and <i>λέχος</i>&nbsp;n (lékhos, “bed”). However, since Greek has no known nominal suffix <i>&#42;-sk-</i>, it has instead been suggested that the word is a nominal from an unattested <i>sk</i>-present verb <i>&#42;λέχ-σκ-εται</i> (&#42;lékh-sk-etai) &gt; <i>&#42;λέσχεται</i> (&#42;léskhetai) &gt; <i>λέσχη</i> (léskhē), though the lack of confirmed evidence for this verb renders this theory in doubt. Under this latter theory, similar formations include Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;leskaną</i> (“to cease, quench”), Old Irish <i>lesc</i> (“lazy”).</p><p>Beekes prefers instead to connect the Greek term with Hebrew <i>לִשְׁכָּה</i> (lišká, “office, bureau”), and suspects borrowing from an Anatolian language for both. The Hebrew is surely a foreign word within a Semitic context considering the variation with <i>נִשְׁכָּה</i> (nišká) and the lack of cognates outside Northwest Semitic, but it is unclear if the Hebrew word is from Greek or the word in both languages is carried over from an unknown language via viniculture in an original meaning of <i>pub</i>, <i>triclinium</i>, making the provenience as obscure as that of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wóyh₁nom</i> (“wine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie down</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;legʰ-</i> (“to lie”). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>лежати</i> (ležati), Old Irish <i>lige</i>, and Old English <i>licgan</i> (English <i>lie</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couch, bed</li><li>bier</li><li>marriage-bed: and generally, marriage</li><li>a bird's nest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;légʰos</i>, from <i>&#42;legʰ-</i>, whence also <i>λέχομαι</i> (lékhomai). Cognates include Russian <i>лог</i> (log, “cleft, valley”), Polish <i>odłóg</i>, and Albanian <i>lagje</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slanting, crosswise</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the presence of the adverb <i>λικριφίς</i> (likriphís, “sideways”) and the gloss <i>λίξ</i> (líx, “sideways, athwart”) suggests a Pre-Greek origin of the word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lion</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Leo, the zodiacal sign</li><li>lionheart, savage (one having the characteristics of a lion (positive or negative)&#58; savage, noble, or brave)</li></ol><p>Many ancient languages possessed similar words for lion, including Akkadian <i>𒌨</i> (labbu) (reconstructed to Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;labiʾ-</i>). It is unclear how they are related and who borrowed from whom. The ultimate source is likely not Indo-European, however.</p><p>The <i>τ</i> (t) in the stem <i>λεοντ-</i> (leont-) was influenced by the present participle. The original stem was <i>&#42;λεον-</i> (&#42;leon-), as shown by <i>λέαινα</i> (léaina, “female lion”) and Latin <i>leō</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λέων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Leon or Leo</li></ol><p>From <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stay, abate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>construed with the genitive</li></ol><li>(<i>more frequently&#32;intransitive</i>) to leave off, cease</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>construed with the genitive</li><li>construed with the dative</li><li>construed with participles</li><li>construed with prepositions</li></ol><li>(grammar,&#32;of a word) to terminate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>construed with prepositions</li><li>construed with the dative</li></ol><li>follow logically</li><li>(<i>of months</i>) synonym of <i>φθίνω</i> (phthínō)</li></ol><p>Likely from the root of <i>λᾰγᾰρός</i> (lăgărós, “hollow; slack”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labdanum, a resin obtained from rockroses</li></ol><p>A loan, originally from Semitic. Compare Akkadian <i>𒆷𒁷𒉡</i> (ladinnu).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forgetful</li><li>lethargic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lethargy</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) lethargic fever</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>λήθη</i> (lḗthē, “forgetting, forgetfulness”) +&lrm; <i>ᾱ̓ργός</i> (ārgós, “not working the ground, idle, lazy”). Beekes prefers to compare the word to <i>λαίθαργος</i> (laíthargos, “guileful, treacherous”) and take both as Pre-Greek, though he does note that the traditional etymology seems to have influenced its meaning via folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>λήθετε</i> (lḗthete)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>λήθετο</i> (lḗtheto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oblivion; forgetfulness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lā́tʰā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;léh₂dʰeh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;leh₂-</i> (“to be concealed”) (whence <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō)).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λήθη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Lethe</li><li>Lethe, a naiad nymph in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>λήθη</i> (lḗthē, “a forgetting, forgetfulness”) from Ancient Greek <i>λήθω</i> (lḗthō, “to forget”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂-</i> (“to hide”) (from which English <i>lethargy</i> (“sluggishness”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;Epic,&#32;Ionic) alternative form of <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō) used especially in compounds</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂-</i> (“to be hidden, be covered”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil flask, oil bottle (lekythos)</li><li>cosmetics case</li><li>trope, rhetorical figure, big word</li><li>Adam's apple</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek. Has been connected to Old Church Slavonic <i>лакъть</i> (lakŭtĭ, “pot”), but the connection is problematic. Probably related in some way to Etruscan <i>𐌋𐌄𐌙𐌕𐌖𐌌</i> (leχtum, “a kind of small bottle; lekythos”) (see <i>ληκύθιον</i> (lēkúthion)). Could also be related to <i>λάγυνος</i> (lágunos) and <i>λάγιον</i> (lágion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rheum, sleep, a humour that gathers in the corner of the eye</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Connection with Latin <i>lāma</i> (“marsh, mud”) and Lithuanian <i>lōmas</i> (“pit, hollow”) is semantically unconvincing. According to Beekes, it is rather a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) town-hall; council-room</li></ol><p>From <i>ληός</i> (lēós, “folk”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐτον</i> (-ĭton). A term used in Achaean Greek, according to Herodotus, but not otherwise attested; his use of <i>-η-</i> (-ē-) for etymological <i>-ᾱ-</i> (-ā-) - a standard feature of his native Ionic dialect - suggests the original form was <i>λᾱ́ῐ̈τον</i> (lā́ĭ̈ton) or similar.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frankincense</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) frankincense tree</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Semitic language, probably from a Canaanite word cognate to Hebrew <i>לְבוֹנָה</i> (ləḇōnā, “frankincense”) and Arabic لُبَان (lubān, “frankincense”), from Proto-West Semitic <i>&#42;laban-</i> (“white”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λίβανος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lebanon, Mount Lebanon (a mountain range in the country of Lebanon, West Asia; in full, Mount Lebanon)</li></ol><p>From a Semitic language, probably from a Canaanite word cognate with Hebrew <i>לְבוֹנָה</i> (ləḇōnā, “frankincense”) and Arabic لُبَان (lubān, “frankincense”), from Proto-West Semitic <i>&#42;laban-</i> (“white”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>libellus, petition</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>libellus</i> (“booklet; petition”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything that drips or trickles</li><li>(<i>especially&#32;in the plural</i>) streams of tears</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a cake or pancake</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐβός</i> (lĭbós), the genitive singular of <i>λῐ́ψ</i> (lĭ́ps, “stream”), with a recessive accent shift. Doublet of <i>λῐβᾰ́ς</i> (lĭbắs).</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lībum</i> (“cake”), possibly via the uncommon variant <i>lībus</i>; likely influenced by the primary sense, due to pancakes needing to be poured.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortar</li><li>clay mould</li><li>lye used as soap</li></ol><p>Related to <i>λῐ́γδᾰ</i> (lĭ́gdă, “whetstone”) and <i>λῐ́γδην</i> (lĭ́gdēn, “touching superifically; grazing”). According to Beekes, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sleyǵ-</i> (“to slide”) like Old High German <i>slīhhan</i> (“to creep”), Old Irish <i>slige</i> (“comb”) and Old Norse <i>slíkr</i> (“smooth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;líďďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lid-yé-ti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;leyd-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>lūdō</i> (“to play”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>λῑ́ᾱν</i> (lī́ān)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of stone</li></ol><p>From <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stone</li><li>stone as a substance</li><li>stone, pebble (<i>used as a piece in a board-game</i>)</li><li>large rock or stone block, used as a seat to a speaker's platform, especially in the Assembly or in the Athenian agora, where archons, arbitrators and certain witnesses swore oaths</li></ol><p>Unknown. Has been compared to <i>λεῖος</i> (leîos, “smooth”), <i>λιτός</i> (litós, “simple, plain”), Latin <i>laedō</i> (“to strike, hurt”), Lithuanian <i>slidùs</i> (“slippery, slick”), Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;ledús</i> (“ice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winnowing fan; winnowing basket</li><li>cradle</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>λικμάω</i> (likmáō, “to winnow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lake, marsh, basin</li><li>sea</li></ol><p>A zero-grade formation from or closely related to <i>λιμήν</i> (limḗn, “harbor”) and <i>λειμών</i> (leimṓn, “moist place, meadow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>τρέπομαι</i> (trépomai)</li></ol><p>According to the LIV, the term reflects Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;li-né-h₂-ti</i>, a nasal-infix present from <i>&#42;leyh₂-</i>, whence also perhaps Old English <i>linnan</i> (“to stop, cease”). It has also been connected with Sanskrit <i>लि॒नाति॑</i> (linā́ti), itself of doubtful attestation, and Old Irish <i>lenaid</i> (“to stop, cling”), which is more probably from <i>&#42;h₂leyH-</i>. Within Greek, it is perhaps related to <i>λιάζομαι</i> (liázomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to contend</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;li-né-d-ti ~ li-n-d-énti</i>, a nasal-infixed present from the root <i>&#42;leyd-</i> (“to let”). Semantically, after shifting its meaning to “to play” like in <i>λῐ́ζω</i> (lĭ́zō) and Latin <i>lūdō</i>, further evolved to “to contend”, like German <i>Schimpf</i> (“insult; affront”) from Middle High German <i>schimpf</i> (“jest; pastime; play; tournament”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flaxen</li></ol><p><i>λίνον</i> (línon) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of flax, linen</li></ol><p>From <i>λίνον</i> (línon, “flax”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flax (<i>Linum usitatissimum</i>)</li><li>anything made of flax:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>cord, fishing line, thread</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) thread of destiny spun by the Fates</li><li>fishing net, hunting net</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) linen cloths or garments</li><li>flax for spinning</li></ol></ol><p>Uncertain. First attested as Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀪𐀜</i> (ri-no), could be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;linom</i>, with cognates including Old Church Slavonic <i>льнъ</i> (lĭnŭ), Latin <i>līnum</i>, and Gothic <i>𐌻𐌴𐌹𐌽</i> (lein). However, the Latin and Germanic forms have a long i. Could also be a loanword to Latin and Ancient Greek which other languages borrowed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>πλάγιος</i> (plágios, “oblique, aslant”), <i>λίθος πλατύς</i> (líthos platús, literally “flat stone”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>λίπα</i> (lípa)</li></ol><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>λίπε</i> (lípe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>richly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>λίπετε</i> (lípete)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>λίπετο</i> (lípeto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>animal fat; lard; tallow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lípos</i>, from i-Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyp-</i> (“fat substance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) lion</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smooth</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”) Compare Latvian <i>lavis</i>.</p><p>Seemingly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lh₁i-t-</i> (“smooth”), with no certain cognates outside of Greek. The supposed Proto-Indo-European root may be related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ley-</i> (“smooth; slick; sticky; slimy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smooth, polished</li><li>(substantive,&#32;in the plural) dice, cut in two by friends, each of whom kept half as tallies, so that the reality of the bond could at any time be proved by producing them</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>λίσσομαι</i> (líssomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to pray; to beseech</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>λῐτός</i> (lĭtós, “supplicatory”) and <i>λῐτή</i> (lĭtḗ, “prayer, entreaty”), said root of unknown origin, but the fact that the <i>λ</i> (l) often makes a preceding short vowel into a heavy syllable, as well as the frequent doubling of the <i>λ</i> (l) after the augment <i>ἐ-</i> (e-), makes it likely that it comes from an earlier <i>&#42;slit-</i>, and thus perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leyt-</i>.</p><p>MacBain compares Scottish Gaelic <i>lide</i> (“syllable”); another potential cognate is Lithuanian <i>liẽsti</i> (“to touch”), see there for more discussion.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let or cede (first-person plural aorist active subjunctive)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps related to <i>λίσσωμα</i> (líssōma) and <i>λίσσωσις</i> (líssōsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoon</li></ol><p>From <i>λίστρον</i> (lístron, “spade, shovel”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tool for levelling or smoothing, spade, shovel</li></ol><p>From a root <i>&#42;λις</i> (&#42;lis) of uncertain interpretation and origin + the instrumental suffix <i>-τρον</i> (-tron). The root <i>&#42;λις</i> (&#42;lis) may be related to <i>λίς</i> (lís, “smooth, even”) and <i>λισσός</i> (lissós, “smooth”). Probably not related to Latvian <i>lîst</i> (“to clear woodland”), Lithuanian <i>lýdyti</i> (“to dig up, unearth”), or Latin <i>līra</i> (“furrow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>litra, a Sicilian silver coin</li><li>libra, a unit of weight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a box for holding libras</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from an Italic language, ultimately from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;līðrā</i> (“ingot”). The immediate source for the Greek borrowing could be Sicel <i>&#42;lītrā</i>. The Italic word also survives in Latin <i>lībra</i>. Compare the morphology of Ancient Greek <i>στατήρ</i> (statḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>southwest</li><li>southwestern wind</li><li>a yearning; urge; desire; craving</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyb-</i>, the same root as <i>λείβω</i> (leíbō, “to pour”).</p><p>From the same root as <i>λῑ́πτομαι</i> (lī́ptomai, “to yearn; to be eager”), <i>λῑπᾰρής</i> (līpărḗs, “persistent; earnest; indefatigable”) and later <i>λῑ́πτω</i> (lī́ptō, “to be eager; to desire ardently”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>πέμματα</i> (pémmata, “pastries”), <i>σπέρματα</i> (spérmata, “seeds”), <i>ἱερά ἀπάργματα</i> (hierá apárgmata, literally “holy firstlings”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variation <i>λαι-/λα-</i> points to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κίναιδος</i> (kínaidos, “cinaedus”), <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē, “prostitute”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek of <i>λάσται</i> (lástai, “whores”) and <i>λάσταυρος</i> (lástauros, “catamite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle, haft</li><li>(<i>as a pugilistic term</i>) grip, hold</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) handle, occasion</li><li>attack of fever</li><li>taking, accepting</li><li>turn of a bandage</li><li>(anatomy,&#32;in the plural) insertions, attachments of muscles</li><li>eye of a needle</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take, grasp”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) forceps</li><li>clamp, clasp</li><li>tongs or snuffers to trim lamps</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰβή</i> (lăbḗ, “handle”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lambdacism, lallation (defective pronunciation of <i>ρ</i> (r, “rho”) as <i>λ</i> (l, “lambda”))</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lambdal, lambdoid, lambdoidal (resembling the letter <i>Λ, λ</i> (L, l, “lambda”) in form)</li></ol><p><i>λάβδα</i> (lábda) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boast</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰβρᾰγόρης</i> (lăbrăgórēs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violence, greed</li><li>bold talking</li></ol><p>From <i>λάβρος</i> (lábros, “furious, fierce, violent”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large wide cup</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Athenaeus, a kind of Persian cup. The word has been probably influenced by <i>λάβρος</i> (lábros, “furious, hasty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A labyrinth, maze; a large building consisting of numerous halls connected by intricate and tortuous passages.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Something obscure or inscrutable.</li></ol><li>Any wreathed or coiled up body.</li></ol><p>Unknown, but Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dabúrintʰos</i> can be reconstructed; likely Pre-Greek (whence Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀅𐁆𐀪𐀵𐀍</i> (da-pu2-ri-to-jo)). Possibly from Lydian <i>𐤩𐤠𐤡𐤭𐤧𐤳</i> (lapryš, “double-edged axe”), a royal symbol, as <i>λαβύρινθος</i> (labúrinthos) supposedly originally referred to a Minoan palace on Crete. See also <i>λάβρυς</i> (lábrus, “axe”) and <i>λαύρα</i> (laúra, “lane, passage”).</p><p>The spelling in Linear B suggests an origin in another language, possibly Minoan.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to release, let go, free</li></ol><p>Disputed. Possibly related to Ancient Greek <i>λᾰγγᾰ́ζω</i> (lăngắzō) and perhaps to the Hesychian gloss <i>λαγάσσαι</i> (lagássai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hollow, sunken, flattish</li><li>slack, loose</li><li>thin, narrow, lanky</li><li>(<i>of plasters</i>) porous, absorbent</li></ol><p>Generally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leh₁g-</i> (“weak, faint”) and compared with <i>λαγαίω</i> (lagaíō, “to release”), <i>λάγνος</i> (lágnos, “lascivious”), <i>λαγών</i> (lagṓn, “flank”), <i>λήγω</i> (lḗgō, “to stop, cease”) and <i>λωγάνιον</i> (lōgánion, “dewlap of oxen”) within Greek. Other Indo-European cognates are Latin <i>laxus</i> (“loose”), English <i>slack</i>, Tocharian A <i>slākkär</i> (“sad”) and Sanskrit <i>श्लक्ष्ण</i> (ślakṣṇa, “tender, soft”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slacken, relax, become lazy</li></ol><p>Disputed. Perhaps a nasal-present from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leg-</i>. If this theory is accepted, then the term is cognate with Latin <i>langueō</i>. However, Beekes compares the term to <i>λαγαίω</i> (lagaíō) and argues that the a-vocalism and the <i>λαγ</i> (lag)/<i>λαγγ</i> (lang) variation indicates a possible Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light spear, lance</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lancea</i> (“spear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sex, sexual intercourse</li><li>lasciviousness, salaciousness</li></ol><p>From <i>λαγνεύω</i> (lagneúō, “to have sex”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hunt hares</li></ol><p>From <i>λαγοθήρας</i> (lagothḗras, “hare-hunter”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō). The first component itself derives from <i>λαγώς</i> (lagṓs, “hare”) and <i>θηράω</i> (thēráō, “to hunt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;with genitive</i>) to obtain (by lot), to receive</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to draw lots</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to bring (a suit) against</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lengʰ-</i> (“to obtain”), although—according to Beekes—no cognates are known.</p><p>Beekes suggests that the o-grade perfect <i>λέλογχᾰ</i> (lélonkhă) and the zero-grade aorist <i>ἔλᾰχον</i> (élăkhon) are from Proto-Indo-European, whereas the forms <i>εἴληχᾰ</i> (eílēkhă), <i>εἰλήχειν</i> (eilḗkhein), and <i>λήξομαι</i> (lḗxomai) were formed later according to the model of <i>λήψομαι</i> (lḗpsomai) and <i>εἴληφᾰ</i> (eílēphă)—both inflected forms of <i>λᾰμβᾰ́νω</i> (lămbắnō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow on each side below the ribs, flank</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;in Later Greek) womb</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) any hollow, also of cups</li><li>flank of a mountain</li><li>side of a grave</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leh₁g-</i> (“weak, faint”), the same root of <i>λαγαρός</i> (lagarós, “hollow, sunken”), <i>λαγαίω</i> (lagaíō, “to release”), <i>λάγνος</i> (lágnos, “lascivious”), <i>λήγω</i> (lḗgō, “to stop, cease”), and <i>λωγάνιον</i> (lōgánion, “dewlap of oxen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something with feet like a hare</li><li>type of grouse</li></ol><p><i>λᾰγώς</i> (lăgṓs, “hare”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hare</li><li>coward</li><li>sea hare (gastropod of the genus <i>Aplysia</i>)</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Lepus, a winter constellation</li><li>kind of bandage</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;λαγοωυσός</i> (&#42;lagoōusós, “with flabby ears”), from <i>&#42;λαγο-</i> (&#42;lago-) (possibly the same root as English <i>slack</i>) and <i>οὖς</i> (oûs). Typologically compare Ossetian <i>тӕрхъус</i> (tærqus, “hare”, literally “long-ear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hare's flesh</li><li>delicacy</li></ol><p>Contracted form of <i>λαγώειος</i> (lagṓeios, “of a hare”), from <i>λαγώς</i> (lagṓs, “hare”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leh₁g-</i> (“weak, feeble”). Compare Latin <i>laxus</i>, Sanskrit <i>लङ्ग</i> (laṅga, “lame”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lakaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweet bay (<i>Laurus nobilis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Λάδων</i> (Ládōn, “river god and father of Daphne”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>καλλαρίας ἰχθῦς</i> (kallarías ikhthûs, literally “cod-fish”).</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>μαζίνης</i> (mazínēs, “cod”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forgetful, absent-minded</li><li>heedless, foolhardy</li></ol><p>From <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō, “to make forget”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “minded”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂-</i> (“to be hidden, concealed”) and <i>&#42;gʷʰren-</i> (“mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgetfulness; loss of memory; amnesia</li><li>losing one's mind; dementia; insanity; madness</li></ol><p>From <i>λαθίφρων</i> (lathíphrōn, “forgetful”) +&lrm; <i>-σύνη</i> (-súnē, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deal secretly</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰθραῖος</i> (lăthraîos) +&lrm; <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret, clandestine</li></ol><p><i>λάθρῃ</i> (láthrēi, “secretly”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any plant of genus <i>Euphorbia</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>caper spurge (<i>Euphorbia lathyris</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>λάθυρος</i> (láthuros, “chickling vetch”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(sex,&#32;vulgar) to suck cock, to perform fellatio</li><li>(dismissive,&#32;offensive,&#32;vulgar) stop, desist</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) throat, gullet</li></ol><p>Likely of Pre-Greek origin, based on Furnée's comparison with <i>λαιφός</i> (laiphós) and <i>λαιφάσσω</i> (laiphássō, “to swallow, gulp down”) and the observed μ/φ variation. Possibly related also to <i>λαῖπος</i> (laîpos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of light shield made of raw skins</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>λάσιος</i> (lásios, “shaggy, woolly”), because of the fluttering apron of untanned leather hanging from the shield. Furnée compares <i>λαῖτα</i> (laîta, “shield”), <i>λαίδας</i> (laídas, “shield made of a hide”), as well as <i>λῆδος</i> (lêdos, “light summer dress”), although the latter is not very convincing. If the comparison is correct, the word must be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>λαφύσσω</i> (laphússō)</li></ol><p>Disputed. The term may have originated as a cross between <i>λαφύσσω</i> (laphússō) and <i>λαιμάσσω</i> (laimássō). However, Beekes argues that the variation between <i>μ</i> (m) and <i>φ</i> (ph) is indicative of a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light, nimble, swift</li></ol><p>The analysis as an expressive transformation of <i>αἰψερός</i> (aipserós, “quick, speedy”) after <i>λάβρος</i> (lábros, “violent, boisterous”) may be possible, but it is not evident.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left (opposite of right)</li><li>awkward</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blue rock thrush, <i>Monticola solitarius</i> (syn. <i>Petrocichla cyanus</i>)</li></ol><p>From &#42;λαιϝός, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂iwos</i> (“left”). Cognates include Latin <i>laevus</i> and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lěvъ</i>.</p><p>Probably related to <i>λᾶᾰς</i> (lâăs, “stone”), due to the rock thrush's habit of nesting in rock cavities.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cicada</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱκέω</i> (lākéō, “to crack, crackle”) +&lrm; <i>-τᾱς</i> (-tās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric and later Greek form of <i>ληκέω</i> (lēkéō) (to crack, crackle)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a back-formation from <i>λέλᾱκᾰ</i> (lélākă), which is a perfect form of <i>λάσκω</i> (láskō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often in the plural</i>) rent, rending, tatters</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lakíds</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂k-</i> (“to tear”). Cognate with Latin <i>lacer</i> and <i>lancinō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kick with the heel or foot</li><li>to trample, tread on something</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to kick, struggle</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰ́ξ</i> (lắx, “with the foot”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laconize; to imitate the Spartans, the Laconians, Lacedaemonians</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>by extension</i>) to speak laconically, in a concise or terse manner; to speak with brevity</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) to act in the interests of the Spartans, Laconians; to favor the Spartans</li></ol><li>to be a pederast</li></ol><p>From <i>Λᾰ́κων</i> (Lắkōn) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have Laconomania, be a Laconophile; to be obsessively interested in the Spartan lifestyle; to have an obsession with the Spartans</li></ol><p>From <i>Λάκων</i> (Lákōn) +&lrm; <i>μᾰνῐ́ᾱ</i> (mănĭ́ā) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to babble, prattle</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰλέω</i> (lăléō) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk, chat, prattle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to talk, speak</li></ol><li>to chatter, chirp</li><li>to make sound</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps a reduplicated form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂-</i>, with cognates including Latin <i>lallō</i>, Lithuanian <i>laluoti</i>, German <i>lallen</i>, English <i>loll</i>, and Welsh <i>llolian</i>. Could also simply be onomatopoeic. Beekes notes that the suffix <i>-έω</i> (-éō) is also present in other verbs pertaining to sound, such as <i>κελαδέω</i> (keladéō) and <i>βομβέω</i> (bombéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>λᾰ́λος</i> (lắlos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>λᾰ́λος</i> (lắlos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to babble, prattle</li><li>(<i>of birds and grasshoppers</i>) to chirp, chirrup</li></ol><p>From <i>λαλαγή</i> (lalagḗ) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prattle</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰλᾰ́ζω</i> (lălắzō, “to babble”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ). See <i>λαλέω</i> (laléō, “I prattle, talk, chat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λαχνόομαι</i> (lakhnóomai)</li></ol><p>From <i>λάχνος</i> (lákhnos) +&lrm; <i>-εύομαι</i> (-eúomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>talking, talk, chat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>talkativeness, loquacity</li></ol><li>a form of speech, dialect</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰ́λος</i> (lắlos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>λᾰλῐᾱ́</i> (lălĭā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heavygoing discordant talker</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰ́λος</i> (lắlos, “talkative”) +&lrm; <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s, “heavy”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ, “from”) +&lrm; <i>μέλος</i> (mélos, “melody”) +&lrm; <i>ῥῠθμός</i> (rhŭthmós, “rhythm”) +&lrm; <i>-βᾰ́της</i> (-bắtēs, “one that treads”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to take hold of, grasp, seize</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to take by force, plunder</li><li>to exact (punishment)</li></ol><li>(<i>of emotions</i>) to seize</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of a god</i>) to possess</li><li>(<i>of darkness, etc.</i>) to cover</li></ol><li>to catch, overtake</li><li>to catch, discover, detect</li><li>to bind (under oath)</li><li>to keep</li><li>to take (food or drugs)</li><li>to perceive, understand</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>logic</i>) to assume, take as granted</li></ol><li>to undertake</li></ol><li>to get</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to admit</li><li>(<i>of emotions</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to take hold of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to lay hands upon</li><li>to find fault with, censure</li><li>to check oneself</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hlagʷ-</i>, probably from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leh₂gʷ-</i> (“to take, grasp”), with cognates including Old English <i>læċċan</i> (English <i>latch</i>) and possibly Sanskrit <i>लग्</i> (lag, “to be attached”). This etymology is supported by the form <i>λͱαβων</i> (lhabōn, aorist participle) found in an inscription in Corfu indicating initial voiceless /l̥/, which would have developed from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sl-</i> cluster. Also see <i>λᾰ́ζομαι</i> (lắzomai) and <i>λᾰ́φῡρον</i> (lắphūron).</p><p>The present stem <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō) has zero-grade of the PIE root with nasal infix and suffix, like <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō, “do secretly”) and <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō, “happen”). The second aorist <i>ἔλᾰβον</i> (élăbon) has zero-grade and no further modifications, like <i>ἔλαθον</i> (élathon) and <i>ἔτυχον</i> (étukhon). The LIV considers the form <i>ἔλᾰβον</i> (élăbon) to reflect an original athematic root aorist, though it has alternatively been interpreted as a thematic root aorist. The forms with eta, such as future <i>λήψομαι</i> (lḗpsomai), have e-grade, like <i>λήσω</i> (lḗsō) and <i>τεύξομαι</i> (teúxomai). The e-grade perfect <i>εἴληφα</i> (eílēpha) derives from reduplication of the original stem in <i>&#42;sl-</i> (<i>&#42;sesl-</i> &gt; <i>&#42;εἱλ-</i> (&#42;heil-) regularly followed by unexpected loss of aspiration).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λαβδᾰκῐσμός</i> (labdăkĭsmós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λαβδοειδής</i> (labdoeidḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small torch</li><li>a bandage for wounds</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>torch-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>torch-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>poetic form of <i>λᾰ́μπω</i> (lắmpō)</li></ol><p>Related to Ancient Greek <i>λᾰ́μπω</i> (lắmpō), itself possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂p-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch, wax-light</li><li>lamp</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰ́μπω</i> (lắmpō, “shine”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covered chariot</li></ol><p>Ending like <i>ἀπήνη</i> (apḗnē) and <i>καπᾱ́να</i> (kapā́na). According to Beekes, the word is evidently Pre-Greek, because of the prenasalization and the suffix <i>-ήνη</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) torch holder, candelabra</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>lampadārius</i>, from <i>lampada</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás, “candle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holder of the office of λαμπαδαρχία (lampadarkhía)</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of comet resembling a torch</li><li>Aldebaran</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱς</i> (-íās)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>torch-bearing</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relational adjective of <i>λαμπάδιον</i> (lampádion, “torch”)</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάδιον</i> (lampádion) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run a torch-race</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be the individual responsible for the superintendence of a torch-race</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπᾰδάρχης</i> (lampădárkhēs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the superintendence of the λαμπαδηδρομίᾱ (lampadēdromíā)</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπαδάρχης</i> (lampadárkhēs) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torchlight procession</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or transform into a torch</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be lighted by torches</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(passive voice,&#32;of races) to be passed or handed on like a torch</li></ol><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to run in a torch-race</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπαδεία</i> (lampadeía) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch holder</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch race</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás, “torch”) +&lrm; <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos, “race”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry or bear the torch in a torch-race</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπαδηφόρος</i> (lampadēphóros) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>λαμπαδηδρομίᾱ</i> (lampadēdromíā)</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπαδηφόρος</i> (lampadēphóros) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>λαμπαδιστής</i> (lampadistḗs, “torchbearer”)</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relational adjective of <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás, “torch”)</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>runner in a torch race</li><li>torchbearer</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-ιστής</i> (-istḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run in the torch-race</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπαδοδρομία</i> (lampadodromía) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch-race, a ceremony in Athens in which runners carried lighted torches from the altar of the gods Hephaestus, Prometheus, and Athena in the Cerameicus to the Acropolis</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relational adjective of <i>λαμπαδοδρομίᾱ</i> (lampadodromíā, “torch race”)</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπαδοδρομίᾱ</i> (lampadodromíā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torchmaker</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold or carry a torch</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to light, illuminate</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπαδοῦχος</i> (lampadoûkhos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) torch-carrying</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) light</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπαδοῦχος</i> (lampadoûkhos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) shining like a torch</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to shine, gleam</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>φέγγος</i> (phéngos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>λαμπαδιστής</i> (lampadistḗs, “torchbearer”)</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>torch-carrying, bright beaming</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;masculine) candlestick, candelabra</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>ὄχος</i> (ókhos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>torchbearing</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) torchlike</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπάς</i> (lampás) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, radiant, brilliant</li><li>of a bright bluish white or silvery color</li><li>(<i>of water or air</i>) clear, limpid, transparent</li><li>(<i>of sound</i>) clear, distinct</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) clear, evident</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) decisive</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of wind, actions, etc.) vigorous, keen, fierce</li><li>splendid in appearance, magnificent</li><li>(<i>of people, cities, actions, etc.</i>) famed, illustrious, renowned</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) noble, munificent</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) bright, joyous</li></ol><p>From <i>λάμπω</i> (lámpō, “to shine”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>λᾰμπρός</i> (lămprós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>λᾰμπρός</i> (lămprós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brilliancy, splendour</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>clearness, distinctness</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) brilliancy, splendour, distinction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>munificence</li><li>brilliancy of style</li></ol></ol><p><i>λᾰμπρός</i> (lămprós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly, clearly, without concealment or obfuscation</li><li>vigorously</li><li>utterly, decisively</li><li>splendidly, magnificently</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμπρός</i> (lamprós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighter, torch, lantern</li></ol><p>From <i>λάμπω</i> (lámpō) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glowworm (<i>Lampyris noctiluca</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λάμπω</i> (lámpō, “to shine, gleam”) and <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of abysses</li><li>gluttonous, greedy</li><li>wanton, impudent, coquettish</li><li>piquant, charming</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>γλαφυρός</i> (glaphurós) and <i>βδελυρός</i> (bdelurós). There seems no basis for the comparison with Latin <i>lemurēs</i> (“roaming ghosts”). The word is probably Pre-Greek and related to <i>λαφύσσω</i> (laphússō, “to swallow”) with interchange "μ/φ". Perhaps <i>λαιμός</i> (laimós, “throat, gullet”) is also related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to escape notice</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) escape a person's notice</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to do [<i>with</i> participle <i>or</i> (<i>rarely</i>) infinitive <small>‘something’</small>] without being noticed [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘by someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to do [<i>with</i> participle <small>‘something’</small>] without knowing it</li></ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to cause to forget [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in compounds,&#32;<i>ἐκληθάνω</i> (eklēthánō),&#32;<i>ἐπιλήθω</i> (epilḗthō),&#32;<i>ἐπιλανθάνομαι</i> (epilanthánomai))</li><li>(Epic&#32;reduplicated aorist,&#32;λέλᾰθον)</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to forget [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lantʰánō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lh₂-n-dʰ-</i>, nasal infix present of <i>&#42;leh₂-dʰ-</i>, dental extension of <i>&#42;leh₂-</i> (“to be hidden, be covered”). Beekes alternatively suggests that it may have been an innovation after <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō), which also has a root aorist <i>μαθεῖν</i> (matheîn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hew, carve</li><li>to carve, eliminate</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>λᾶς</i> (lâs) + <i>&#42;ξεύω</i> (&#42;xeúō), itself presumably an alternative form of <i>ξεω</i> (xeō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rousing or stirring nations</li><li>to which the people flock</li><li>preserving the people or nations</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”) +&lrm; <i>σεύω</i> (seúō, “to drive, impel”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”) +&lrm; <i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flank (flesh between the last rib and the hip)</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>λαπαρός</i> (laparós, “slack; hollow”). Compare the same sense development between <i>λαγαρός</i> (lagarós, “hollow; slack, loose”) and <i>λᾰγών</i> (lăgṓn, “hollow below the ribs, flank”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>λαπάρᾱ</i> (lapárā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to empty, evacuate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be emptied, evacuated</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be softened</li><li>to sack, plunder, pillage</li></ol><p>From <i>λαπαρός</i> (laparós) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>λαπάσσω</i> (lapássō)</li></ol><p>From <i>λαπαρός</i> (laparós) +&lrm; <i>-ττω</i> (-ttō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slack, loose (of the bowels)</li><li>hollow</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>λαγαρός</i> (lagarós), <i>πλαδαρός</i> (pladarós) and <i>χαλαρός</i> (khalarós). A basic primary verb may have been retained in <i>ἔλαψα</i> (élapsa, “weakened, destroyed”). It is usually assumed that <i>λαπάσσω</i> (lapássō) is an extension of this, perhaps after <i>μαλάσσω</i> (malássō, “to make soft”). In the sense <i>to destroy</i>, <i>λαπάσσω</i> (lapássō) agrees with <i>ἀλαπάζω</i> (alapázō, “to destroy”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin in view of the prothetic vowel. Close to Albanian <i>laps</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fatted, fat</li></ol><p>Unclear. Perhaps from <i>λᾱρός</i> (lārós, “pleasent to the taste, sweet, dainty”) +&lrm; <i>-ινός</i> (-inós), however, the word also recalls Latin <i>laridum</i> (“lard”). May be of shared substrate origin with the Latin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coal-basket carrier</li></ol><p>From <i>λάρκος</i> (lárkos, “coal basket”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γωγός</i> (ăgōgós, “guide, escort”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry a charcoal basket</li></ol><p>From <i>λάρκος</i> (lárkos) + <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros) + <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sweet, delicious, pleasing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lawerós</i>, possibly related to <i>ἀπολαύω</i> (apolaúō, “to enjoy”). Pokorny draws it from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂w-</i> (“benefit, gain”), itself the source of <i>ἀπολαύω</i> (apolaúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thicket</li></ol><p>From <i>λάσιος</i> (lásios, “bushy, overgrown”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn). (cf. πυξών (puxṓn, “thicket of reeds”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λασιών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lasion; Lasiona, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>λαμυρός</i> (lamurós, “full of abysses; gluttonous”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix <i>-βος</i>, which is found also in <i>μάτταβος</i> (máttabos, “stupid, silly”), <i>ἀττέλαβος</i> (attélabos, “locust”) and <i>κόλλαβος</i> (kóllabos, “kind of cake or roll”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service</li><li>worship</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰτρεύω</i> (lătreúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Elean</i>) to render as an offering</li></ol><p>Elean form of λατρεύω (latreúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hired servant</li></ol><p>From <i>λάτρον</i> (látron) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work for hire or pay, to be in servitude, serve</li><li>to be subject to, to be bound or enslaved</li><li>to serve the gods with prayers and sacrifices</li></ol><p>From <i>λατρεύς</i> (latreús, “hired servant”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō), the former from <i>λάτρις</i> (látris, “hired servant, handmaid”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chips of stone in hewing</li><li>gypsum, lime</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾶας</i> (lâas, “stone”) +&lrm; <i>τυπή</i> (tupḗ, “blow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) throat</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ἀρτηρία</i> (artēría), from an unattested <i>&#42;λαυκάνη</i> (&#42;laukánē). An aspirated by-form appears in <i>λαυχάνη</i> (laukhánē, “tongue; language”). Since there are no certain correspondences (Lithuanian <i>liaukà</i> (“gland”) has been proposed, but does not convince), and as there is a variant with <i>-χ-</i>, Pre-Greek origin is almost certain. The form in <i>λευκ-</i> may be folk-etymological after <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”), but may also be due to substrate origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gulp down, consume greedily or gluttonously</li></ol><p>Disputed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig</li></ol><p>From the same root of <i>λάχανον</i> (lákhanon, “vegetables”) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>culture of potherbs</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾰ́χᾰνον</i> (lắkhănon, “potherb, garden herb”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plant vegetables</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be planted with vegetables</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be treated like a vegetable</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to gather herbs</li></ol><p>From <i>λάχανον</i> (lákhanon) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of vegetable kind</li></ol><p>From <i>λάχανον</i> (lákhanon) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A dish or pot for vegetables</li></ol><p>From <i>λάχανον</i> (lákhanon, “vegetable”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become or grow hairy, downy</li></ol><p>From <i>λάχνος</i> (lákhnos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charlock (<i>Rhamphospermum arvense</i>, syns. <i>Sinapis arvensis</i>, etc.)</li></ol><p>The variants support Pre-Greek origin, while the connection with <i>λάμπω</i> (lámpō, “to lighten, glow”) remains doubtful.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from the people, common</li><li>unofficial, civilian</li><li>not consecrated or holy</li><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) layman</li></ol><p>From <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>people; people assembled</li><li>the people of a country</li><li>the soldiers</li><li>common people (as opposed to leaders or priests)</li><li>subjects of a ruler</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lāwós</i>, with further origin uncertain:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alley, lane, passage</li><li>bazaar</li><li>avenue</li><li>block of houses surrounded by streets, ward, town quarter</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) privy, sewer</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek origin, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>λαύρᾱ</i> (laúrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirlwind, tornado, vortex</li></ol><p>Probably from Pre-Greek, with intensive reduplication.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) depth or gulf of the sea</li></ol><p>Connected with <i>λαιμός</i> (laimós, “throat”), assuming suffixes <i>-τ-μα</i>. If this connection is correct at all, it rather points to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀσπίς</i> (aspís, “shield”).</li></ol><p>Given the presence of variants, like <i>λαίβα</i> (laíba) and <i>λαῖτα</i> (laîta), the word is probably Pre-Greek. Furnée compares <i>λαῖφος</i> (laîphos, “sail, old garment”), but he gives no further explanation; one may imagine that the sails were made of skin, like the shield.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) shabby, tattered garment</li><li>piece of cloth or canvas, sail</li></ol><p>Unknown. Furnée unconvincingly compares <i>λαῖφα</i> (laîpha, “shield”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λάβρος</i> (lábros)</li></ol><p>Frequent misreading of <i>λάβρος</i> (lábros, “furious, boisterous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booty, plunder</li><li>the act of plunder</li><li>stolen property</li><li>herd, flock</li><li>tool for smoothing stone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂w-</i> (“to seize, gain”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>लोत</i> (lota), Latin <i>lucrum</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;launą</i>.</p><p>From <i>λεῖος</i> (leîos, “smooth”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smooth to the touch</li><li>smooth, plain, not embroidered (of cloths)</li><li>level, even, flat (of places or of the sea)</li><li>glabrous, hairless</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) smooth, soft (of sound, of taste)</li><li>rubbed or ground down</li></ol><p>From an older form <i>&#42;λεῖϝος</i> (&#42;leîwos), itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₁y-u-</i> (“smooth”) and cognate to Latin <i>lēvis</i> (“smooth”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λεία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>לֵאָה</i> (Leʾā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pour, pour forth</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to pour a libation [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to a god’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to let flow, shed</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to melt or pine away</li></ol><p>Disputed. According to the LIV, the term derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lḗyH-ti ~ &#42;léyH-énti</i>, a Narten-type present to <i>&#42;leyH-</i>, whence also Latvian <i>liet</i> (“to pour”). Beekes, however, requires the <i>-β-</i> (-b-) to have emerged as a secondary development, perhaps according to the model of <i>εἴβω</i> (eíbō, “to drip”).</p><p>The linguist Roman Garnier has alternatively suggested that the same root may have produced a noun <i>&#42;lóyH-p-o-s</i> according to the same model as Hittite <i>𒉿𒀸𒊭𒉺𒀸</i> (wa-aš-ša-pa-aš /⁠wašpaš⁠/), which Garnier derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wós-p-o-s</i>, from the root <i>&#42;wes-</i>. Kloekhorst, however, instead suggests a possibly derivation of the Hittite word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wos-bʰ-o-s</i>, ultimately from the same root. Nevertheless, Garnier suggests that this noun, <i>&#42;lóyH-p-o-s</i>, was the source of a denominative verb that manifested itself in an Indo-European substrate language as <i>&#42;loiβā́-</i>, which was borrowed into Greek as the Hesychian gloss <i>λοιβᾶται</i> (loibâtai), whence a deverbal noun <i>λοιβή</i> (loibḗ), to which the present <i>λείβω</i> (leíbō) was then back-formed. Garnier also proposes that this same substrate languaghe is the source of <i>lībō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>αἴλουρος</i> (aílouros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave, leave behind</li><li>to leave alone, release</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be left, remain, survive</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to leave, depart, disappear</li><li>to desert, fail</li><li>to lack, fall short, fail</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leykʷ-</i> (“to leave behind”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>रि॒णक्ति॑</i> (riṇákti), Latin <i>linquō</i>, Old Armenian <i>լքանեմ</i> (lkʻanem), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;līhwaną</i> (“to lend”), Old Church Slavonic <i>отлѣкъ</i> (otlěkŭ, “remains”), Lithuanian <i>ãt-laikas</i> (“remains”), <i>likti</i> (“to stay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of lilies</li><li>like a lily</li></ol><p>From <i>λείριον</i> (leírion, “lily”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Madonna lily (<i>Lilium candidum</i>)</li><li>paperwhite (<i>Narcissus tazetta</i>)</li><li>autumn narcissus (<i>Narcissus serotinus</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from an eastern Mediterranean substrate language. One likely candidate is the ancestor of Fayyumic Coptic <i>ϩⲗⲏⲣⲓ</i> (hlēri) (compare Sahidic Coptic <i>ϩⲣⲏⲣⲉ</i> (hrēre)). Similar words in other languages, probably from the same source, include Albanian <i>lule</i> (“flower”), Hittite (alil-, “flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priest</li></ol><p>Because of the form <i>λητῆρες</i> (lētêres), which is ascribed to the northwestern Athamanes, this word may be a loan from Boeotian. For this reason, the semantically attractive connection with <i>λειτουργός</i> (leitourgós, “religious minister”), which is from <i>λαός</i> (laós, “people”), presents difficulties. According to Beekes, the word could be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lick up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyǵʰ-</i>. Cognates include Old Armenian <i>լիզեմ</i> (lizem), Sanskrit <i>लेढि</i> (leḍhi), Latin <i>lingō</i>, and Old English <i>liccian</i> (English <i>lick</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is left behind</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>): remains, remnants, wrecks</li></ol><p>From <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “I leave behind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἐρέβινθοι</i> (erébinthoi, “chickpeas”).</li></ol><p>Probably from Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix <i>-ινθ-</i>. Compare also <i>γάλινθοι</i> (gálinthoi, “chickpeas”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin or slough (of serpents and beans)</li><li>European rabbit (<i>Oryctolagus cuniculus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it is a Pre-Greek word, in view of the suffix found also in <i>ἴμβηρις</i> (ímbēris), meaning <i>eel</i> and <i>κύπηρις</i> (kúpēris) referring to a plant. Compare <i>λέπρᾱ</i> (léprā).</p><p>Of Iberian origin, like Latin <i>lepus</i> (“hare”) and <i>laurex</i> (“young rabbit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legion</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>legiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a meadow</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) pudenda muliebria; a pussy, vagina</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>λίμνη</i> (límnē, “marshy lake”) and <i>λιμήν</i> (limḗn) via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leym-</i> (see <i>&#42;léymō</i> (“lake”)). Has been compared to Latin <i>līmus</i> in the "mud" and "oblique" senses, <i>līmen</i> (“threshold”), Latvian <i>leja</i> (“dale, valley”), as well as <i>λείβω</i> (leíbō, “to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Statice limonium</i>, sea lavender or snakeweed</li></ol><p>From <i>λειμώνῐος</i> (leimṓnĭos, “of a meadow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(at Athens,&#32;construed with a cognate accusative) to serve public offices at one’s own cost</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) to perform public duties, serve the state</li><li>(generally,&#32;construed with a dative) to serve (a master)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>perform religious service, minister</li></ol></ol><p><i>λειτουργός</i> (leitourgós, “one who performs a <i>λειτουργία</i>, a public servant”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public service</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) ministration</li></ol><p>From <i>λειτουργός</i> (leitourgós, “public servant”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun&ndash;forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public servant</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) minister</li></ol><p>From <i>λήϊτον</i> (lḗïton, “town-hall, council-room”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Liverwort</li><li>Lichen-like disease affecting the skin of animals; sycosis</li><li>Blight or canker of the ground</li><li>Callosity on the foreleg of horses</li></ol><p>From <i>λείχω</i> (leíkhō, “to lick”), derived with the meaning “the one who licks”, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyǵʰ-</i> (“to lick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dish, pot, pan, basin</li></ol><p>From <i>λέκος</i> (lékos, “dish, pot, pan”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, suffix for utensil names), alleged to be from Pre-Greek, however see <i>λᾰ́γῡνος&#47;λᾰ́γῠνος</i> (lắgūnos&#47;lắgŭnos, “flask”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Good at speaking, able to speak</li><li>Related to expression, stylistic</li></ol><p>From <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “speak”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) lexicon, dictionary</li></ol><p>Attested at least since 9th century (Photios' Bibliotheca). Ellipsis of <i>λεξικὸν βιβλίον</i> (lexikòn biblíon, literally “a book of or pertaining to words”), formally a Neuter substantive of adjective <i>λεξικός</i> (lexikós), from <i>λέξις</i> (léxis, “a saying, speech, word”), from <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to speak”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leǵ-</i> (“to gather, collect”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to words</li></ol><p>From <i>λέξῐς</i> (léxĭs, “speech, word”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós), from <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “I speak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) lionskin</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of mercury (<i>Mercurialis tomentosa</i>)</li><li>sort of dye</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>λεοντῐκός</i> (leontĭkós, “of a lion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lion’s leaf, <i>Leontice leontopetalum</i></li></ol><p>From <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”, stem: <i>λεοντ-</i>, <i>leont-</i>) +&lrm; <i>πέτᾰλον</i> (pétălon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>edelweiss (plant of the genus <i>Leontopodium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contracted form of <i>λεοντέη</i> (leontéē, “lionskin”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limpet (gastropod of the family Patellidae)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>λέπας</i> (lépas, “scaur, rock”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs), as the animal clings to the rock. Alternatively, it could derive from <i>λέπος</i> (lépos, “rind, husk”), <i>λεπίς</i> (lepís, “scale, flake”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small plate, capsule, used to close a tube</li><li>pepperwort (<i>Lepidium latifolium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λεπίς</i> (lepís, “scale, flake”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scale, flake, shell, husk</li><li>epithelial debris</li><li>plate of metal</li><li>blade of a saw</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to peel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limpet-shaped drinking-cup</li></ol><p>From <i>λεπάς</i> (lepás, “limpet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scaly, scabby, rough</li><li>leprous, mangy</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to peel”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jar</li></ol><p>From <i>λεπτός</i> (leptós, “peeled, husked”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(epic,&#32;literary) fine, delicate</li></ol><p>From <i>λεπτός</i> (leptós, “thin”), from <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to peel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having thin soil</li></ol><p><i>λεπτός</i> (leptós) +&lrm; <i>γῆ</i> (gê)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small coin, lepton</li><li>the small intestine</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;literally) peeled, husked (<i>of grain</i>)</li><li>fine-grained (<i>ashes, soil, etc.</i>)</li><li>thin, lean (<i>people or animals</i>)</li><li>straight, narrow (<i>spaces</i>)</li><li>small, weak, impotent</li><li>light, slight (<i>breezes</i>)</li><li>thin (<i>liquids</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) refined, delicate, subtle</li></ol><p>From <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “I peel”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;literally) peeled, husked (<i>of grain</i>)</li><li>fine-grained (<i>ashes, soil, etc.</i>)</li><li>thin, lean (<i>people or animals</i>)</li><li>straight, narrow (<i>spaces</i>)</li><li>small, weak, impotent</li><li>light, slight (<i>breezes</i>)</li><li>thin (<i>liquids</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) refined, delicate, subtle</li></ol><p>From <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “I peel”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make thin or meager</li><li>to comminute, or liquefy food in digestion</li><li>to thresh, winnow</li></ol><p>From <i>λεπτός</i> (leptós, “thin”) +&lrm; <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) to act like a person from the island of Lesbos</li><li>(<i>sex</i>) to perform fellatio (since the women of Lesbos were said to have invented fellatio, according to a <i>scholion</i> on line 1346 of Aristophanes's <i>Wasps</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Λέσβος</i> (Lésbos, “Lesbos”) + <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sad, wretched</li><li>sore, baneful</li><li>sorry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewǵ-</i>, with suffix <i>-αλέος</i> (-aléos). Cognates include Latin <i>lūgeō</i>, Sanskrit <i>रुजति</i> (rujáti, “to cause pain”), Lithuanian <i>lū́žti</i>, and Old High German <i>liohhan</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white thistle (<i>Tyrimnus leucographus</i>)</li><li>tuberous thistle (<i>Cirsium tuberosum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>ἄκανθα</i> (ákantha, “thorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of several plants of the genus <i>Anthemis</i></li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθεμον</i> (ánthemon, “flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, shining, gleaming</li><li>light in color; white</li><li>pale-skinned, weakly, cowardly</li><li>fair, happy, joyful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;leukós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“white; light; bright”). Cognates include Latin <i>lūx</i>, Sanskrit <i>रोचते</i> (rócate), Old Armenian <i>լոյս</i> (loys) and Old English <i>lēoht</i> (light, noun), whence English <i>light</i>. compare Arabic لهق.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λευκάς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Leucas; Lefkada, Ionian Islands, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “light, white”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bleak (<i>Alburnus alburnus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, turn white; to whitewash, blanch</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to cause to appear white</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become white</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be clarified</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to grow, become, turn white</li><li>to make bright; to brighten, lighten</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A white, heron-like bird, likely either the Eurasian spoonbill, <i>Platalea leucorodia</i>, or the great egret, <i>Ardea alba</i>.</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>ἐρῳδιός</i> (erōidiós, “heron”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) to paint in white</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós) +&lrm; <i>γρᾰφή</i> (grăphḗ) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) the act of painting in white</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) + <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “write, draw”) + <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey</li></ol><p><i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās, “black”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a ship</i>) white-winged</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, shining, gleaming</li><li>light in color; white</li><li>pale-skinned, weakly, cowardly</li><li>fair, happy, joyful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;leukós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“white; light; bright”). Cognates include Latin <i>lūx</i>, Sanskrit <i>रोचते</i> (rócate), Old Armenian <i>լոյս</i> (loys) and Old English <i>lēoht</i> (light, noun), whence English <i>light</i>. compare Arabic لهق.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>λευκός</i> (leukós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of gem</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>ὀφθαλμός</i> (ophthalmós, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of gem of a pale gold color</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;epithet of women and goddesses) white-armed</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) and <i>ὠλένη</i> (ōlénē, “forearm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) smooth, level, even</li><li>spacious, open (of fields)</li><li>smooth, polished</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Connection with Latin <i>lēvis</i> (“smooth”) is improbable, as this rather belongs to <i>λεῖος</i> (leîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woman in childbed, or one who has just given birth</li></ol><p>From <i>λέχομαι</i> (lékhomai, “I lay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>busy, full of people</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>main street</li></ol><p>From <i>λεώς</i> (leṓs, “people”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>may denote a leopard; at least after Pliny, a leopon/lipard (see also πάρδος/pard for the contemporary word for the leopard species)</li></ol><p>From <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”) +&lrm; <i>πάρδος</i> (párdos, “leopard, panther”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>λευκά</i> (leuká)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white poplar (<i>Populus alba</i>)</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) leprosy or elephantiasis</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) white spots on the nails</li><li>white clay or chalk</li></ol><p>From <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of white poplar</li><li>decorated with chaplets of white poplar (of soldiers)</li></ol><p>From <i>λεύκη</i> (leúkē, “white poplar”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look, gaze</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;leúťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;léwk-ye-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;lewk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic and Ionic form of <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λειμών</i> (leimṓn)</li><li>limax, snail</li></ol><p>Identical with Latin <i>līmax</i> (“snail”), which is probably a Greek loan, but also with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;slimakъ</i> (“snail”). Thus, it has to be a derivative in <i>-ak-</i> of the <i>m-</i>stem in Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;slīmą</i> (“slime, mucus”), Latin <i>līmus</i> (“mud, slime”) and Ancient Greek <i>λίμνη</i> (límnē, “marsh”). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sley-</i> (“smooth; slick; sticky; slimy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A remnant, a residue</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>music</i>) A semitone, a small musical interval</li><li>An intermission in fever</li><li>A deficiency</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “to leave, to quit”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smooth to the touch</li><li>smooth, plain, not embroidered (of cloths)</li><li>level, even, flat (of places or of the sea)</li><li>glabrous, hairless</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) smooth, soft (of sound, of taste)</li><li>rubbed or ground down</li></ol><p>From an older form <i>&#42;λεῖϝος</i> (&#42;leîwos), itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₁y-u-</i> (“smooth”) and cognate to Latin <i>lēvis</i> (“smooth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize; to carry off as booty</li><li>to despoil; to plunder</li></ol><p>From <i>ληῐ̈́ς</i> (lēĭ̈́s, “booty”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳομαι</i> (&#42;-jomai). Synchronically also analyzable as <i>ληῐ̈́ς</i> (lēĭ̈́s) +&lrm; <i>ῐ́ζομαι</i> (ĭ́zomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booty; plunder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lāwíts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂wíds</i>, from <i>&#42;leh₂w-</i> (“to seize, profit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō)</li></ol><p>Perhaps a cross of <i>λήθω</i> (lḗthō) and <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drowsiness, sleepiness</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>λήθᾱργος</i> (lḗthārgos, “forgetful, lethargic”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, nominal abstract derivative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance</li><li>(sex,&#32;euphemistic) to have sex</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes suggests that the term is an iterative-intensive formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lek-</i>, with a possible exact parallel in Latvian <i>lȩ̃kât</i>. However, on the basis of <i>λαικαζω</i> (laikazō), Beekes suggests that this term may showcase the Pre-Greek variation of <i>αι&#47;η</i> (ai&#47;ē). It is likely the meaning of "to have sex" is a secondary development.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crack, crackle, burst asunder</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a back-formation from <i>λέληκᾰ</i> (lélēkă), which is a perfect form of <i>λάσκω</i> (láskō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Woollen fillet or ribbon; surgical bandage; pledget.</li></ol><p>From <i>Λῆμνος</i> (Lêmnos, “a Greek island Lemnos”) +&lrm; <i>-ίσκος</i> (-ískos, noun-forming diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything shaped like a tub or trough</li><li>winepress, vat where grapes are pressed</li><li>trough, for watering cattle</li><li>socket into which the mast fitted</li><li>coffin, sarcophagus</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) torcular Herophili, the confluence of sinuses</li><li>lower parts of the nose</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Perhaps a technical term of the Pre-Greek substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disposed to accept</li><li>disposed to take</li></ol><p>From <i>λῆψις</i> (lêpsis, “accepting, taking”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apprehended; taken</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) impaired by the senses</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Stoic&#32;philosophy</i>) acceptable; not to be refused if offered</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>λῃστής</i> (lēistḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to recoil, drop, collapse, sink, slip away, be turned aside</li><li>to part, e.g., a pair of doors</li></ol><p>Unknown; Beekes considers a connection with Gothic <i>𐌰𐍆𐌻𐌹𐌽𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (aflinnan) plausible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warm, balmy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small spring, small stream</li><li>wet place</li><li>centaury (<i>Centaurium erythraea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λιβάς</i> (libás, “stream, spring”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of frankincense</li><li>of the color of the frankincense</li></ol><p>From <i>λίβανος</i> (líbanos, “frankincense”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything that drips or trickles</li><li>spring, fount, stream</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) pools of water that collect after rain</li><li>vessel that drips when under the influence of heat, a rudimentary thermometer</li></ol><p>From <i>λείβω</i> (leíbō, “to pour”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs). Doublet of <i>λῐ́βος</i> (lĭ́bos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incense box</li></ol><p>From <i>λίβανος</i> (líbanos, “incense”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rosemary</li></ol><p>From <i>λιβανωτός</i> (libanōtós, “frankincense”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, “nominal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frankincense</li></ol><p>From a Semitic sound plural of the etymon of <i>λίβανος</i> (líbanos), which Beekes says derives from a Semitic root cognate to Hebrew <i>לָבָן</i> (“white”) and Arabic لَبَن (laban, “milk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prepared with frankincense</li></ol><p>From <i>λιβανωτός</i> (libanōtós, “frankincense”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>shrilly, loudly</li></ol><p><i>λῐγύς</i> (lĭgús) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick smoke mixed with flame, murky fire (such as is made by burning resinous substances)</li><li>soot</li></ol><p>Unknown, perhaps Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear, whistling, shrill</li><li>clear-voiced, sweet-toned</li><li>pliant, flexible</li></ol><p>From <i>λιγύς</i> (ligús) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) laserwort, <i>Laserpitium</i></li></ol><p>Common use of <i>Λιγυστικόν</i> (Ligustikón), the neuter of <i>Λιγυστικός</i> (Ligustikós, “Ligurian”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Epic,&#32;Koine,&#32;of a sound) clear; shrill; loud; piercing</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly related to Dutch <i>lijster</i> (“thrush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) clear-voiced, loud-voiced, with a clear voice (said of heralds)</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐγῠ́ς</i> (lĭgŭ́s, “clear, shrill”) +&lrm; <i>φθόγγος</i> (phthóngos, “sound, voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>litharge, lead monoxide</li></ol><p>From <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”) +&lrm; <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geology</i>) the name of a type of carbuncle</li></ol><p>Present active participle of <i>λῐθῐ́ζω</i> (lĭthĭ́zō, “look like a stone”), from <i>λῐ́θος</i> (lĭ́thos, “stone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or like litharge</li></ol><p>From <i>λιθάργυρος</i> (lithárguros, “litharge”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrument for extracting stones</li></ol><p>From <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stonemason, sculptor</li></ol><p>From <i>λίθος</i> (líthos) +&lrm; <i>ἔργω</i> (érgō) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cement</li></ol><p>From <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”) +&lrm; <i>κόλλα</i> (kólla, “glue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stoneseed, gromwell (<i>Lithospermum officinale</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”) +&lrm; <i>σπέρμα</i> (spérma, “seed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to winnow</li><li>to scatter, crush, destroy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neyk-</i>. Cognates include Lithuanian <i>niekoti</i>, Latvian <i>niekāt</i>, and Welsh <i>nithio</i>.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. How did the initial n change to l?</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desire, be eager or anxious, vehemently long for [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘for something to happen, to do something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> genitive <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol><p>A reduplicated and <i>&#42;ye</i>-suffixed present, reconstructed as <i>&#42;li-lh₂s-ye-ti</i>. Α derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂s-</i> (“to be eager, desire”) has been proposed, and compared with Latin <i>lascīvus</i> (“luxuriant, wanton”) (whence English <i>lascivious</i>), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;làskati</i> (“to caress, flatter”), Sanskrit <i>लस्</i> (las, “to play, radiate”), and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lustuz</i> (“lust”), but Beekes calls this "hardly possible", and expresses skepticism at all comparisons mentioned above.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>λιμήν</i> (limḗn)</li></ol><p>From <i>λιμήν</i> (limḗn) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harbor</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a haven, retreat, refuge</li></ol><p>Ablaut derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;léymō</i> (“lake”); compare <i>λίμνη</i> (límnē). See <i>λειμών</i> (leimṓn) for a descendant in a different ablaut grade.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer from hunger, famine, starvation</li></ol><p>From <i>λῑμός</i> (līmós) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harbour-master</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐμήν</i> (lĭmḗn, “port”) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler, leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harbour-watcher</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐμήν</i> (lĭmḗn, “harbour, port”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hungry, causing hunger</li><li>furnished with a good harbour</li></ol><p>From <i>λῑμός</i> (līmós, “famine, hunger”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p><p>From <i>λῐμήν</i> (lĭmḗn, “harbour, port”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;active voice) to be or become swampy, marshy</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;active voice,&#32;of bodies of water) to be or become stagnant</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to flood</li></ol><p>From <i>λίμνη</i> (límnē) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to starve, to make someone fast</li><li>to kill by starvation</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to fast, to death, killed by starvation</li></ol><p>From <i>λιμός</i> (limós) +&lrm; <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō), equivalent to <i>λιμοκτόνος</i> (limoktónos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fasting</li><li>a killing by hunger, death from starvation</li></ol><p>From <i>λιμός</i> (limós) +&lrm; <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), equivalent to <i>λιμοκτόνος</i> (limoktónos) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>λῑμοκτονίᾱ</i> (līmoktoníā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) synonym of <i>λείπω</i> (leípō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leykʷ-</i>, though the exact process of derivation is uncertain. It is perhaps inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;linékʷti</i>, whence also perhaps Old Armenian <i>լքանեմ</i> (lkʻanem). However, the linguist Lucien van Beek has argued that the term may be a productive formation created on the basis of the aorist <i>ἔλιπον</i> (élipon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li></ol><p>From <i>λιμφός</i> (limphós) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informant</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>famine, hunger, starvation</li></ol><p>Uncertain, though probably connected to and from the same family of words as <i>λοιμός</i> (loimós, “plague”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hungry</li></ol><p>From <i>λῑμός</i> (līmós, “hunger”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “-ous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be hungry, starving, famished</li></ol><p>From <i>λῑμός</i> (līmós) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>λῑμώσσω</i> (līmṓssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of linseed</li></ol><p>From <i>λινόσπερμον</i> (linóspermon, “linseed”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to lay, set nets</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be caught by nets</li></ol><p>From <i>λίνον</i> (línon) +&lrm; <i>στατός</i> (statós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linen-weaver</li><li>kind of freshwater bird, perhaps the glossy ibis (<i>Plegadis falcinellus</i>)</li><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) kind of stone</li></ol><p>From <i>λίνον</i> (línon, “flax, linen”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercury (<i>Mercurialis annua</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λίνον</i> (línon, “flax”) +&lrm; <i>ζώννῡμι</i> (zṓnnūmi, “to gird”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linseed</li></ol><p>From <i>λίνον</i> (línon, “flax”) +&lrm; <i>σπέρμα</i> (spérma, “seed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatty poultice</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐπᾰρός</i> (lĭpărós, “oily; fatty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persistence; perseverence; tenacity</li><li>insistence; zeal</li></ol><p>From <i>λῑπᾰρής</i> (līpărḗs, “persistent; persevering”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of people</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>persistent; persevering; indefatigable; tenacious</li><li>earnest in one's requests or demands; insistent</li></ol><li>(of things,&#32;especially metonymically) tireless; ceaseless; zealous; assiduous</li><li>(<i>of negative conditions or circumstances</i>) obstinate; stubborn; incessant; slow to pass or subside</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an oily or fatty substance; especially greasy or fatty food</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oily, shiny with oil, anointed</li><li>fatty, greasy, unctuous</li><li>shining, sleek, smooth (of the skin)</li><li>rich, comfortable, easy (of condition of state)</li><li>bright, brilliant, splendid (of things)</li><li>fat, fruitful, fertile (of soil)</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐπᾰ́ω</i> (lĭpắō) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós), from <i>λίπος</i> (lípos, “fat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>richly fed</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐπᾰρός</i> (lĭpărós, “fatty, greasy”) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to feed, nourish”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>greasily (in or with grease)</li><li>with fat</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) with wealth; in comfortable circumstances</li><li>persistently; perseveringly; tenaciously</li><li>insistently; with earnest sincerity</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐπᾰρός</i> (lĭpărós, “oily; fatty”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p><p>From <i>λῑπᾰρής</i> (līpărḗs, “persistent; persevering”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor, forlorn, outcast</li></ol><p>Given the existence of <i>λιφερνοῦντας</i> (liphernoûntas, “poor, needy”), the word is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bare, smooth cliff</li></ol><p>From the same root of <i>λισσός</i> (lissós, “smooth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smooth</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>λίς</i> (lís, “smooth”), itself seemingly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lh₁i-t-</i> (“smooth”), with no certain cognates outside of Greek. The supposed Proto-Indo-European root may be related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ley-</i> (“smooth; slick; sticky; slimy”). Compare also <i>λισσάς</i> (lissás), <i>λίσπος</i> (líspos), <i>λίσφος</i> (lísphos), and <i>λίστρον</i> (lístron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer; entreaty</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>λῐ́σσομαι</i> (lĭ́ssomai) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entreaty</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐτᾰνεύω</i> (lĭtăneúō, “to pray; to entreat”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray; to entreat</li></ol><p>From <i>λῐτᾰνός</i> (lĭtănós, “praying”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō); from <i>λῐτή</i> (lĭtḗ, “prayer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simple; plain</li><li>inexpensive; frugal</li><li>poor</li><li>smooth; even; plane</li><li>paltry; petty; small</li><li>an epithet of <i>γαῖα</i> (gaîa)</li><li>suppliant; supplicatory</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>λῑ́ς</i> (lī́s, “smooth”)</p><p>See <i>λῐ́σσομαι</i> (lĭ́ssomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plainness, simplicity</li><li>(grammar,&#32;rhetoric) litotes, understatement</li></ol><p>From <i>λῑτός</i> (lītós, “plain, frugal, simple”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>λούω</i> (loúō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>railing, abusive, reviling</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>railer, reviler</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>λοιδορέω</i> (loidoréō, “to revile, rail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lobe of the ear</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lobe of the liver</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lobe of the lung</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) capsule or pod of leguminous plants</li></ol><p>Uncertain, though perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leb-</i> (“to hang loosely”), and cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;lappaz</i> (“flap, lobe”) (whence German <i>Lappen</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gathered, picked, chosen</li><li>unhewn (<i>of stones</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) white of the eye, sclera</li></ol><p>From <i>λέγω</i> (légō) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Koine spelling of <i>λογεία</i> (logeía)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>versed in tales or stories; learned, erudite</li><li>skilled in words, eloquent</li><li>oracular</li></ol><p><i>λόγος</i> (lógos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count, reckon</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to calculate, compute</li><li>to consider, ponder, take into account</li><li>to count on, expect</li><li>to think, believe</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “computation, reckoning”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζομαι</i> (-ĭ́zomai, denominative mediopassive verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collection (<i>of taxes or voluntary contributions</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λογεύω</i> (logeúō, “to arrange, gather”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place for speaking</li><li>(<i>in a theater</i>) stage, platform</li><li>(<i>place of speech</i>) mouth</li></ol><p>From <i>λογεύς</i> (logeús, “speaker”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, suffix of location).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λογεῖον)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>ancient Greek name of an online database of classical and medieval Latin and ancient Greek dictionaries, encyclopedias, concordances and other lexical resources (in English, French, Spanish, German, Dutch and Chinese), compiled by the University of Chicago</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>λογεῖον</i> (logeîon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, prose</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Belonging to speech: prose</li><li>Belonging to reason: intellectual</li><li>rational, logical, reasonable, thoughtful</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “speech, reason”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Belonging to speech: prose</li><li>Belonging to reason: intellectual</li><li>rational, logical, reasonable, thoughtful</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “speech, reason”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calculation, computation</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) numbers</li><li>reasoning, argument</li><li>reflection, thought</li><li>reasoning power, wisdom</li></ol><p>From <i>λογίζομαι</i> (logízomai, “I calculate”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reasonable; rational</li><li>skilled in calculating or deliberating</li></ol><p>From <i>λογίζομαι</i> (logízomai, “to reason; to calculate”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prose writer</li><li>chronicler</li><li>speechwriter</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “idea, conception”) + <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “write, draw”) + <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing of speeches, of proses</li><li>office or official recorder in a lawcourt</li></ol><p>From <i>λογογρᾰ́φος</i> (logogrắphos, “prose-writer”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>logorrhea, flux of words, verbal diarrhea</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγος, λογο-</i> (lógos, logo-) +&lrm; <i>δῐᾰ́ρροιᾰ</i> (dĭắrrhoiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>auditor</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) logothete, any of various state officials or functionaries in the Byzantine Empire</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, quarrel, war, dispute about words</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγος</i> (lógos) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fight about words</li></ol><p>From <i>λογομᾰχέω</i> (logomăkhéō, “to fight about words”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “word”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>author of speeches</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγος</i> (lógos) +&lrm; <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small spear-head</li><li>small spears</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγχη</i> (lónkhē) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce with a spear</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγχη</i> (lónkhē) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transfix with a spear</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγχη</i> (lónkhē) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide or furnish with a point</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγχη</i> (lónkhē) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tongue orchid (<i>Serapias lingua</i>)</li><li>holly fern (<i>Polystichum lonchitis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγχη</i> (lónkhē, “lance, spear”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bath-pourer; one who pours water for a bath</li></ol><p>From <i>λοετρόν</i> (loetrón) + <i>χόος</i> (khóos); Attic <i>λουτρόν</i> (loutrón) + <i>χοῦς</i> (khoûs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in religious sense</i>) pouring, drink-offering, libation</li></ol><p>From <i>λείβω</i> (leíbō, “to pour, libate”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, ruin</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>λοίγιος</i> (loígios): deadly, pestilent (epithet of Mars (Ares)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lig-ós</i>, from <i>&#42;leyg-</i> (“illness”). Synchronically, an agent noun meaning "destroyer" from an unattested verb preserved in Lithuanian <i>li̇́egti</i> (“to be ailing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abuse; to insult; to revile</li><li>to rebuke; to reproach; to rail</li></ol><p>Taken as a formation comparable to <i>πολῐορκέω</i> (polĭorkéō), <i>δειροτομέω</i> (deirotoméō), <i>οἰνοχέω</i> (oinokhéō), but further details are unknown. It is doubtful to compare the first part with Latin <i>lūdō</i> (“to play”), <i>λῐ́ζω</i> (lĭ́zō, “to play”), as is done by Perpillou, who also adduces the gloss <i>λῐ́νδομαι</i> (lĭ́ndomai, “to contend”). According to Beekes, it could be of Pre-Greek origin, like many other terms with this meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be pestilent</li></ol><p>From <i>λοιμός</i> (loimós) +&lrm; <i>-εύομαι</i> (-eúomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plague; pestilence (any deadly, infectious disorder)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;of person) a pest; a persistent annoyance</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) pestilent</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin, though perhaps related to <i>λῑμός</i> (līmós, “famine”) (via ablaut), as well as <i>λοιγός</i> (loigós, “ruin”) (via different suffix). Compare <i>λῡ́μη</i> (lū́mē), <i>λῦμᾰ</i> (lûmă), <i>λῡμαίνομαι</i> (lūmaínomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pestilential, pertaining to plague</li></ol><p><i>λοιμός</i> (loimós) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to register as a debt or debtor</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be registered as a debit or debtor</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to concede a deferment of payment</li></ol><p>From <i>λοιπογραφή</i> (loipographḗ) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debt, arrears</li></ol><p>From <i>λοιπογραφέω</i> (loipographéō) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>further, besides</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remaining, the rest</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(neuter,&#32;with infinitive) it remains to..</li><li>(<i>of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>neuter</i>) the rest</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;loykʷós</i> (“remaining, leftover”). Surface analysis <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “to leave, remain”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>remaining, the rest</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(neuter,&#32;with infinitive) it remains to..</li><li>(<i>of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>neuter</i>) the rest</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;loykʷós</i> (“remaining, leftover”). Surface analysis <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “to leave, remain”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not straight: slanting, crosswise, oblique</li><li>To the side: sideways, askance</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) mistrustful, suspicious</li><li>(<i>of language</i>) indirect, ambiguous</li></ol><p>Unknown, but generally related by Celticists to Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;loskos</i> (“crippled”). According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Heh₃l-</i> (“to bow”), see also Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;olīnā</i> (“angle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obliquity</li><li>ambiguity</li></ol><p><i>λοξός</i> (loxós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make slanting, oblique</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be or become slanted, sideways</li></ol><p>From <i>λοξός</i> (loxós) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flat dish, plate</li><li>pan</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) callus or knot caused by disease (especially on olive trees)</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>λοπός</i> (lopós, “shell, scale; bark”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs). Ultimately from the same root of <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to peel”). See also <i>λεπάς</i> (lepás, “limpet”) for a similar derivation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peel; rind; skin; shell</li></ol><p>From <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to peel”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bent backward, so as to be convex in front</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lerd-</i> (“to bend, crook”) and cognate with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lort</i> (“crooked”) and Scottish Gaelic <i>loirc</i> (“deformed foot”).</p><p>As such, it would be suffixed in <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) lucanica, loukaniko (a type of sausage)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>lūcānica</i> (“Lucanian sausage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathtub</li></ol><p>From <i>λούω</i> (loúō, “to wash, bathe”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish to bathe</li></ol><p>From <i>λούω</i> (loúō) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry water for bathing</li></ol><p>From <i>λουτροφόρος</i> (loutrophóros) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to bringing water for bathing or washing</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) black-urn placed upon the tombs of unmarried individuals</li></ol><p>From <i>λουτρόν</i> (loutrón, bath, bathing-place) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>λοετροχόος</i> (loetrokhóos, “bath-pourer”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bath, bathing-place</li><li>bath, water used for bathing</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) libation to the dead</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lewotrón</i>, whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀩𐀺𐀵𐀫</i> (re-wo-to-ro /⁠lewotrón⁠/), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;léwh₃trom</i> (“that which is used for washing”), from <i>&#42;lewh₃-</i> (“to wash, bathe”); see there for further cognates. By surface analysis, <i>λούω</i> (loúō) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bathe</li></ol><p>From <i>λουτρόν</i> (loutrón) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch made of vine bark</li></ol><p>From an unattested <i>&#42;λόφνος</i> (&#42;lóphnos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs). It may derive from the same root of <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to peel”) and <i>λοπός</i> (lopós, “shell; bark”); the suffix is also seen in <i>λύχνος</i> (lúkhnos, “lamp”). However, according to Beekes, this solution is rather uncertain. Another theory by Furnée links the word to Georgian <i>ლაფანი</i> (lapani, “bast”), suggesting a byform <i>&#42;λάφνη</i> (&#42;láphnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lie in wait for, waylay, ambush</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lie in wait or ambush</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to prepare an ambush on (<i>a road etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos, “ambush”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos, “ambush”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) leader of an armed band, especially the commander of a company (100 men)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos, “a file in the phalanx”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kite (<i>Milvus milvus</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée compares the word with <i>λώβηξ</i> (lṓbēx, “vulture”) and concludes that this is a Pre-Greek word, due to the interchange <i>λώβ-/λούπ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λούπης</i> (loúpēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash, bathe (generally of people)</li><li>to purify, cleanse</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to bathe oneself, take a bath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewh₃-</i> (“to wash, bathe”). Cognates include Albanian <i>laj</i>, Latin <i>lavō</i>, Old Armenian <i>լոգանամ</i> (loganam), and Old English <i>lēaþor</i> (English <i>lather</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>last, left behind, final</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boom, gaff or spar</li></ol><p>Uncertain etymology due to medial <i>-θ-</i>. Scheftelowitz posited <i>&#42;sloydʰ-to-</i>, which is compared to Russian <i>после́дний</i> (poslédnij, “last, latest”), Lithuanian <i>slýsti</i> (“to glide”) and <i>ὀλισθάνω</i> (olisthánō, “to slip, slide”), but it is unlikely that the cluster preserved its aspiration for such a long time.</p><p>Greek <i>λοστός</i> (lostós, “lever”) seems to presuppose a pre-form <i>&#42;λοϊσθός</i> (&#42;loïsthós). Further unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing, bathing</li><li>cleaning</li></ol><p>From <i>λούω</i> (loúō, “I bathe”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) white pith of the fir</li></ol><p>It may continue <i>&#42;λουκ-ιον</i> (&#42;louk-ion) as a derivative of a root noun, as is seen in Latin <i>lūx</i> (“light”), if from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“white; light; bright”). Compare also Latin <i>lūcus</i> (“grove, wood”), from the same source. The etymology remains highly improbable, though.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amber</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>λύγξ</i> (lúnx, “lynx”) +&lrm; <i>οὖρον</i> (oûron, “urine”), as the stone was thought to be the urine of the lynx. However, the presence of the word <i>λογούριον</i> (logoúrion, “glass”) rather shows that this is a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sore, baneful, mournful</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) baneful, mischievous</li></ol><p>Presumably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;luǵrós</i>, from <i>&#42;lewǵ-</i>. Apparently related to <i>λευγαλέος</i> (leugaléos, “wretched, unhappy”). Cognate with Latin <i>lūgeō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ludio, a Roman stage performer or dancer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lūdiō</i> (“stage performer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>year</li></ol><p>Often derived from an unattested <i>&#42;λύκη</i> (&#42;lúkē, “light”) + <i>&#42;βᾱ́ς</i> (&#42;bā́s, “going”) (seen in <i>βιβᾱ́ς</i> (bibā́s)), as “path of light”, but Beekes finds this unconvincing. He rather suggests a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the alternative form with "μ" and the suffix "-αντ-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>werewolf, lycanthrope</li></ol><p>From <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos, “man, human”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolfsbane (<i>Aconitum lycoctonum</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>λυκοκτόνος</i> (lukoktónos, “wolf-slaying”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jackal</li></ol><p>From <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”) and <i>πάνθηρ</i> (pánthēr, “panther”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian henbane (<i>Hyoscyamus muticus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>πέρσιον</i> (pérsion, “fruit of persea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white mignonette (<i>Reseda alba</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>σκυτάλιον</i> (skutálion, “little staff, baton”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild leek (<i>Allium ampeloprasum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́κος</i> (lŭ́kos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>σκόρδον</i> (skórdon, “garlic”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of anchovy</li></ol><p>From <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give pain to, to pain, distress, grieve, vex</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to cause pain or grief</li><li>(in historical writers,&#32;of cavalry and light troops) to harass, annoy</li></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;future middle) to be pained, grieved, distressed, sad, to grieve</li></ol><p>From <i>λῡ́πη</i> (lū́pē, “pain”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painful, distressing</li><li>causing sorrow or pain, troublesome, vexatious, offensive</li></ol><p>From <i>λῡπέω</i> (lūpéō, “to grieve”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós). Compare also <i>λῡπρός</i> (lūprós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>λῡπηρός</i> (lūpērós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of land, plants</i>) worthless; wretched; poor</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) causing pain; offensive</li><li>(<i>of states and conditions</i>) distressing; painful</li></ol><p>From <i>λῡπέω</i> (lūpéō, “to grieve”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós). Compare also <i>λῡπηρός</i> (lūpērós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or pertaining to the lyre.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) lyrist</li><li>(<i>poetry</i>) lyric poet</li></ol><p><i>λύρα</i> (lúra, “lyre”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “-ic”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) lyrist</li></ol><p><i>λύρα</i> (lúra, “lyre”) +&lrm; <i>-ιστής</i> (-istḗs, “-ist”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epirote</i>) a cup or can</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λυσι-</i> (lusi-), used before a vowel</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delivered from love</li><li>freeing from love</li></ol><p>From <i>λυσ-</i> (lus-, apocopic form of <i>λυσι-</i> (lusi-, “ended”)) +&lrm; <i>ἔρως</i> (érōs, “love”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that ends suffering</li></ol><p>From <i>λυσ-</i> (lus-, apocopic form of <i>λυσι-</i> (lusi-, “ending, dissolving”)) +&lrm; <i>ἄλγος</i> (álgos, “pain”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ending sorrow (<i>Scholia Aristophanica</i>, vol.1, 1896, p.251)</li></ol><p>From <i>λυσ-</i> (lus-, “ending”) +&lrm; <i>ἀν(ία)</i> (an(ía), “sorrow”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>ending, dissolving, deliverance</li></ol><p>From <i>λύσις</i> (lúsis, “loosening, releasing, parting”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loosestrife (<i>Lysimachia vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>λυσιμάχειος</i> (lusimákheios, “of Lysimachus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>limb-relaxing</li><li>epithet of sleep</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of love</li><li>of thirst</li><li>of wine</li><li>of sickness</li><li>of the Furies</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>λυσι-</i> (lusi-, “loosing, releasing”) (from <i>λύω</i> (lúō)) + <i>-μελής</i> (-melḗs) (from <i>μέλος</i> (mélos, “song, music”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solve expenses, to profit</li></ol><p>From <i>λῡσῐτελής</i> (lūsĭtelḗs, “profitable”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “a verb forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paying for expenses incurred, useful, profitable, advantageous, profitable in point of money</li><li>cheap</li><li>(<i>rarely of persons</i>) profitable, advantageous</li><li>(<i>adverb</i>) cheaply</li></ol><p>From <i>λυσι-</i> (lusi-, “loosing, releasing”), from <i>λύω</i> (lúō) + <i>-τελής</i> (-telḗs), from <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “end, completion; <i>here:</i> dues”). A contraction of the phrase <i>τέλη</i>&nbsp;n&nbsp;pl (télē) <i>λύειν</i> (lúein) («λύειν τέλη», "pay one's dues"), hence, I become advantageous.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one must loosen, solve, refute</li><li>that must be loosened, solved, refuted</li></ol><p>From <i>λύ(ω)</i> (lú(ō), “loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, “a suffix forming verbal adjectives of necessity”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who looses, deliverer</li></ol><p>From <i>λύ(ω)</i> (lú(ō), “loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, a suffix creating agent nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a remedy, cure</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loosing, delivering, a symptom of healing</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) the atonement or reward for all costs, deliverer from</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠτήρ</i> (lŭtḗr, “who loosens, liberates”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “pertaining to”, suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λύ̆τειρα</i> (lú̆teira)</li></ol><p>From <i>λύω</i> (lúō, “loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-τηριάς</i> (-tēriás, “a noun-forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to loose, laxative</li><li>antidote to a poison</li><li>dispersive of inflammation</li><li>(<i>of arguments</i>) able to refute, confutative</li></ol><p><i>λύω</i> (lúō, “loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must be ransomed</li><li>that must ransom</li></ol><p>From <i>λυτρόω</i> (lutróō, “to ransom”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, “a suffix forming verbal adjectives of necessity”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ransomer, redeemer</li></ol><p>From <i>λυτρόω</i> (lutróō, “to ransom”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “a suffix forming agent nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ransomable, redeemable</li></ol><p>From <i>λυτρόω</i> (lutróō, “to ransom”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, “a suffix that creates verbal adjectives of possibility, either active or passive”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to release on receipt of a ransom, hold to ransom, for such a sum as is agreed on, redeem a pledge, release by payment of ransom, redeem, to be ransomed</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be released from an obligation</li><li>(<i>mistranslation of hebrew</i>) to "break the neck"</li></ol><p>From <i>λύτρον</i> (lútron, “ransom”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, verb-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>redeemable</li></ol><p>From <i>λυτρόω</i> (lutróō, “to ransom”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐμος</i> (-ĭmos, suffix forming an adjective of possibility or capability)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that may be untied, loosed or dissolved</li><li>(<i>of arguments</i>) refutable, soluble</li></ol><p>From <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, “a suffix that creates verbal adjectives of possibility, either active or passive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rose campion (<i>Silene coronaria</i>, syn. <i>Lychnis coronaria</i>)</li><li>a precious stone that emits light&#59; perhaps ruby</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́χνος</i> (lŭ́khnos, “lamp”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>illumination produced by lamps, lighting</li><li>festival of lamps, held in Sais</li></ol><p>From <i>λύχνος</i> (lúkhnos, “lamp”) +&lrm; <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “to light, kindle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lampstand</li></ol><p>From <i>λύχνος</i> (lúkhnos, “lamp”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to bear, carry, bring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>candlewick (<i>Verbascum mallophorum</i>), of Greece, Albania, and southern Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>λύχνος</i> (lúkhnos, “lamp”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>more desirable; more agreeable; better</li></ol><p>Inherited from a comparative formation from the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂w-</i>. Cognate with Old Armenian <i>լաւ</i> (law, “good”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dewlap of oxen</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)leh₁g-</i> (“weak, faint”), the same root of <i>λαγαίω</i> (lagaíō, “to release”) and <i>λαγαρός</i> (lagarós, “slack, loose”), thus "skin hanging down". Intermediate forms, such as <i>&#42;λώγανον</i> (&#42;lṓganon), <i>&#42;λώγη</i> (&#42;lṓgē), cannot be determined, however.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>λέγω</i> (légō)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lōgā́yō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lōǵ-éh₂ye-ti</i>, itself from the root <i>&#42;leǵ-</i>. The term my parallel Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;lōkōn</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who slips into another’s clothes, a clothes-stealer, especially one who steals the clothes of bathers, or strips travellers</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) a thief, robber, footpad</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a plagiarist</li></ol></ol><p><i>λώπη, λῶπος</i> (lṓpē, lôpos, “covering, robe, mantle”); <i>δῡ́ω</i> (dū́ō, “I get into, put on”); <i>-της</i> (-tēs, suffix forming agent nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>βαθύτατα</i> (bathútata, “deepest”), <i>κατώτατα</i> (katṓtata, “lowest”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of various fodder plants:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>strawberry clover (<i>Trifolium fragiferum</i>)</li><li>birdsfoot trefoil (<i>Lotus corniculatus</i>)</li><li>fenugreek (<i>Trigonella foenum-graecum</i>)</li></ol><li>white lotus (<i>Nymphaea lotus</i>)</li><li>nettle tree (<i>Celtis australis</i>)</li><li>jujube (<i>Ziziphus lotus</i>)</li><li>(<i>poetical</i>) flute</li><li>(<i>music</i>) pipe inserted in the nabla</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) tube or stalk of vaginal speculum</li></ol><p>Probably from a Semitic source common to Hebrew <i>לוֹט</i> (lōṭ, “myrrh”, literally “covering, envelope”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit of the cowpea</li></ol><p>From <i>λοβός</i> (lobós, “lobe; capsule”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>announcement</li><li>oracle</li></ol><p>From <i>λόγος</i> (lógos) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>versed in tales or stories; learned, erudite</li><li>skilled in words, eloquent</li><li>oracular</li></ol><p><i>λόγος</i> (lógos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>versed in tales or stories; learned, erudite</li><li>skilled in words, eloquent</li><li>oracular</li></ol><p><i>λόγος</i> (lógos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is said: word, sentence, speech, story, debate, utterance, argument</li><li>that which is thought: idea, conception, consideration, reason, reckoning, computation</li><li>an account, explanation, or narrative</li><li>subject matter</li><li>(<i>theology</i>) the word or wisdom of God, identified with Jesus in the New Testament, by whom the world was created; God the Son</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lógos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leǵ-</i> (“to collect (words)”). See also <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to reckon, say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spearhead</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>lance, spear, javelin</li><li>troop of spearmen</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂k-</i> (“to hit”), compare Latin <i>lancea</i> (“lance”) and Old Armenian <i>գեղարդն</i> (gełardn, “spear”), or a Semitic borrowing corresponding to Hebrew <i>רֹמַח</i> (rómaḥ, “spear”), Arabic رُمْح (rumḥ, “spear”) etc. (see the Arabic), but Beekes leaves the etymology unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obliquity</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>ambiguity</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>λοξόω</i> (loxóō, “to make slanted”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the back of the neck, the withers (<i>of a horse</i>), the nape (<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>the crest (<i>of a hill</i>), a ridge</li><li>the crest (<i>of a helmet</i>)</li><li>(<i>after Homer</i>) the crest or tuft on the head of a bird; comb (<i>of flesh</i>)</li><li>a tuft of hair (<i>upon the crown of a person</i>)</li><li>synonym of <i>λοφιά</i> (lophiá)</li></ol><p>Probably from the same Indo-European source as Tocharian A <i>lap</i> (“head”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lъbъ</i> (“skull”).</p><p>In 1819, Rees thought it to be akin to Old English <i>hlifian</i> (“to raise up, tower, stand out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambush</li><li>the place of an ambush: ambuscade</li><li>a body of men for ambush, armed band</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a company of troops</li></ol><li>childbirth</li><li>a file in the phalanx</li></ol><p>O-grade verbal noun from <i>λέχομαι</i> (lékhomai, “to lie”). Cognate with Albanian <i>lagje</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) white poplar (<i>Populus alba</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of the cluster -γδ.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of white marble</li></ol><p>From <i>λύγδος</i> (lúgdos, “white marble”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white marble</li></ol><p>Ending as in <i>μόλυβδος</i> (mólubdos) and <i>κίβδος</i> (kíbdos). A connection with <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”) is morphologically hard to explain, as the suffix <i>-δος</i> is not productive. According to Beekes, the word is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of chaste tree</li></ol><p>From <i>λύγος</i> (lúgos, “chaste tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lynx</li><li>hiccup</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lúnks</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lú(n)ḱs</i> (“lynx”), probably from a nasal-infixed palatal variant of the root <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“white; light; bright”), because of the cat's seemingly glowing eyes and ability to see in the dark.</p><p>Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luhsaz</i>, Lithuanian <i>lū́šis</i>, Old Armenian <i>լուսանունք</i> (lusanunkʻ, “lynxes”)), and for the sense of "light," <i>λευκός</i> (leukós), Sanskrit <i>रोचते</i> (rocate), Middle Persian <i>𐭩𐭥𐭬</i> (rōz [YWM], “day”) and Old English <i>lēoht</i> (noun; English <i>light</i>).</p><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewǵ-</i> (“to break; to shatter”). Related to Latin <i>lugeō</i> (“to mourn; to grieve”), Sanskrit <i>रु॒जति॑</i> (rujáti, “to break open; to shatter; to injure; to cause pain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaste tree (<i>Vitex agnus-castus</i>)</li><li>flexible twig, withe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewg-</i> (“to bend, twist”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;luką</i> (“lock”), Sanskrit <i>रुग्ण</i> (rugṇá, “bent, broken”), Latin <i>luctor</i> (“to wrestle”) and Latin <i>luxus</i> (“dislocated”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hiccup</li><li>to sob violently, from fear or cold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;slewk-</i>, <i>&#42;slewg-</i> (“to swallow”). Cognates are found in Celtic and Germanic, like in Welsh <i>llyncu</i> (“to swallow”) and in Middle High German <i>slucken</i> (“to gobble, swallow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gore, defilement from blood</li><li>venom of the hydra</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lew-</i> (“dirt”), the same root of <i>λῦμα</i> (lûma, “dirt”), Latin <i>lutum</i> (“mire, mud”) and Albanian <i>lym</i> (“loam, clay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she-wolf</li></ol><p>From <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viper's herb (<i>Echium italicum</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>χορδαψός</i> (khordapsós), <i>σκινδαψός</i> (skindapsós) and <i>γαληψός</i> (galēpsós), so it is probably a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dyer's buckthorn (<i>Rhamnus saxatilis</i>)</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>Λυκία</i> (Lukía, “Lycia”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix), because this plant grows mainly in Cappadocia and Lycia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wolf</li><li>curb bit</li><li>a kind of jackdaw</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lúkos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wĺ̥kʷos</i> (“wolf”) with metathesis. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>वृक</i> (vṛ́ka), Latin <i>lupus</i> (also showing metathesis), Old English <i>wulf</i> (English <i>wolf</i>) and Russian <i>волк</i> (volk).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λύκος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Lycus, Lycos, or Lykos</li></ol><p>From <i>λῠ́κος</i> (lŭ́kos, “wolf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brutal outrage</li><li>maltreatment, defilement</li><li>maiming</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) outrages, indignities</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grief, sadness</li><li>pain (of mind or body), suffering, affliction, distress</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. The traditional connection to Sanskrit <i>लुप्</i> (lup, “to break, tear apart”) can be dismissed, as the Sanskrit root is much more likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hrewp-</i> (“to break, tear up”), which cannot produce the Greek term.</p><p>Beekes tentatively prefers a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewp-</i> (“to peel off”), whence Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;laupī́ˀtei</i> (“to cause to bend; to peel”). While phonetically sound and semantically possible, the lack of concrete evidence directly connecting the Greek sense of "grief, pain" to the hypothetical original "peeling, breaking" sense renders the etymology uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lyre (string instrument with a sounding board, traditionally formed from the shell of a tortoise)</li><li>lyric poetry and music</li><li>piper gurnard (<i>Trigla lyra</i>)</li></ol><p>A Mediterranean Pre-Greek substrate technical loan. Indo-European etymologies are unlikely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to loose or relieve</li><li>that can be redeemed, redeemable</li><li>that can be solved, refutable</li><li>that can be melted, meltable</li></ol><p>From <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “to loose, loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐμος</i> (-ĭmos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loosing, releasing, release, ransoming</li><li>deliverance from guilt by expiatory rites</li><li>redemption of mortgage or pledge</li><li>parting</li><li>emptying, evacuation</li><li>solution (to a difficulty or problem)</li><li>remission of fever</li></ol><p>From <i>λύω</i> (lúō, “loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Λύσις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lysis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rage; fury</li><li>mania; raging madness</li><li>fanaticism</li><li>rabies (<i>of dogs</i>)</li></ol><p>Somewhat disputedly, thought to be equivalent to <i>λῠ́κος</i>&nbsp;m (lŭ́kos, “wolf”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia, abstract noun suffix) (but formed already in Proto-Hellenic, like <i>ὄσσᾰ</i> (óssă) and <i>γλῶσσᾰ</i> (glôssă), which is necessary to account for the sound shift), thus the original meaning would have been “the state of being wolfish”.<br/>Beekes maintains this etymology but, like Pokorny, considers the Greek root for “wolf” to be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i> (“to shine; light”) rather than <i>&#42;wĺ̥kʷos</i> (“wolf”) with deformation. Compare the same root in such phrases as <i>λευκαὶ φρένες· μαινόμεναι</i> (leukaì phrénes&#59; mainómenai, “raging, frenzied”, literally “glowing minds raving”) and <i>λευκῶν πραπίδων· κακῶν φρενῶν</i> (leukôn prapídōn&#59; kakôn phrenôn, identical meaning, literally “of evil minds of glowing hearts”).</p><p>The less commonly held comparison to <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “to untie, let loose”) is probably to be abandoned.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(in the plural,&#32;Byzantine law) law-students who were in their fourth year of study</li></ol><p>As a term of Byzantine law, translation for the Latin term <i>lytae</i> at Justinian's <i>Constitution omnem</i>, 533, itself a transliteration of the plural of Hellenistic Koine singular <i>λύτης</i>&nbsp;m (lútēs, “who solves puzzles”), from <i>λύ(ω)</i> (lú(ō), “loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, a suffix added to verb stems to form masculine agent nouns).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>price of release, ransom</li><li>sum paid for redemption of a pledge</li><li>atonement, expiation</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) recompense</li><li>loosestrife (<i>Lysimachia vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>λύω</i> (lúō, “to set free, release”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron, “a suffix forming instrument nouns”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ransoming, redemption of a pledge</li><li>a release, discharge from an obligation</li><li>a spring of water</li></ol><p>From <i>λυτρόω</i> (lutróō, “to ransom”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, “a suffix added to verb stems to form abstract nouns or nouns of action, result or process”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>λυτήρ (lutḗr)</li></ol><p>From <i>λύω</i> (lúō, “loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “a suffix that forms agent nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamp</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;luk-s-nos</i>, from <i>&#42;lewk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loose; to loosen; to untie</li><li>to slacken</li><li>to unbend</li><li>to set free; to release</li><li>to redeem</li><li>to dissolve; sever</li><li>to break (up); to destroy</li><li>to abrogate; to annul</li><li>to atone; to amend</li><li>to profit; to be useful</li><li>to discharge; to fulfill; to pay off</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewh₁-</i> (“to cut off, separate, free”), (which Beekes reconstructs as <i>&#42;lh₁u-</i>). Cognates include Latin <i>luō</i> (“expiate, pay”), Sanskrit <i>लुनाति</i> (lunā́ti, “to sever, cut forth, destroy, annihilate”), English <i>loose</i>, and possibly Old Armenian <i>լուծանեմ</i> (lucanem) and Albanian <i>lirë</i>.</p><p>According to Beekes, the stem vowel was originally short (λῠ-), lengthened only later in Attic and sometimes in Homer (λῡ-) due to the influence of a laryngeal. The upsilon remains short (ῠ) before temporal endings beginning with ⟨κ, θ, μ⟩ and all derivatives, but the prosody of compounds remains that of <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō).</p><p>Derivatives, from the stems <i>λῠσ-</i> (lŭs-), <i>λῠσῐ-</i> (lŭsĭ-) (compounds with <i>λῡσῐ-</i> (lūsĭ-)), <i>λῠτ-</i> (lŭt-).</p><p>Unrelated to <i>κωλῡ́ω</i> (kōlū́ō, “prevent”), <i>ὄλλῡμῐ</i> (óllūmĭ, “destroy, lose”) or <i>λύσσᾰ</i> (lússă, “rage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disgrace, dishonor</li></ol><p>Commonly derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sloHgʷ-eh₂-</i> (“to mishandle, press”), from the root <i>&#42;sleh₂gʷ-</i> (“to seize”), with Balto-Slavic cognates including Latvian <i>slēgt</i> (“to shut”), Lithuanian <i>sluogas</i>, <i>slogus</i>. See also <i>λάζομαι</i> (lázomai, “I grasp, seize”), from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulture</li></ol><p>Furnée compares the word with <i>λούπης</i> (loúpēs, “kite”), suggesting a Pre-Greek root in view of the interchange <i>β/π</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering, robe, mantle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lep-</i> (“to peel, flay”), like <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to skin, flay”) and <i>λεπίς</i> (lepís, “flake, scale”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of lotus wood</li><li>covered with lotus</li></ol><p>From <i>λωτός</i> (lōtós, “lotus”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more desirably; more agreeably</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone</li></ol><p>Unknown and likely a substrate word. The traditional etymology connecting Albanian <i>lerë</i> (“boulder; rockslide”), Old Irish <i>lie</i> (“stone”) (via Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;līwos</i>; see there for more proposed cognates) and Old Armenian <i>լեառն</i> (leaṙn, “mountain”) for a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;law-</i> or <i>&#42;leh₁w-, &#42;l̥h₁w-</i> (“stone”) is impossible if Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀨𐀁𐀊</i> (ra-e-ja /⁠lāhejā⁠/, “stone”) and Arcadocypriot Greek <i>𐠏𐠃𐠩</i> (la-o-se) are considered cognate, as the second syllable shows no trace of <i>*-w-</i> (e.g. expected <i>&#42;𐀨𐀸𐀊</i> (*ra-we-ja) and <i>&#42;𐠏𐠵𐠩</i> (&#42;-la-wo-se) respectively). However, Doric Greek <i>λεύς</i> (leús, id.) and <i>λεύω</i> (leúō, “to stone”) complicate this. Alternative etymologies reconstruct <i>&#42;lḗh₂s-r̥</i> or <i>&#42;lḗh₂-wr̥</i> (the latter particularly as the preform for Celtic, though this is formally highly problematic and furthermore relies on a controversial sound law); Nikolaev supposes the root <i>&#42;leh₂-</i> gave rise to an <i>s</i>-stem <i>&#42;leh₂-es-</i>, whence a collective <i>&#42;leh₂-es-h₂</i> that was subsequently re-singularized to <i>&#42;leh₂-s-h₂-s</i>, as the preform to <i>λᾶας</i> (lâas), and invokes the controversial Eichner's law to explain <i>λεύς</i> (leús) as from a parallel <i>u</i>-stem <i>&#42;lēh₂-u-s</i>. For <i>&#42;lḗh₂-wr̥</i> he tentatively adds Hittite <i>𒆷𒄴𒄷𒊏𒀸</i> (la-aḫ-ḫu-ra-aš /⁠laḫḫuras⁠/, “offering table ~ stand”) and Milyan (laκre-). Similarly, a reconstruction <i>&#42;leh₁-</i> for the root may be possible with the addition of different suffixes, but such etymologies are increasingly <i>ad hoc</i> and still fail to explain all the variation within Celtic and Greek. Beekes suspects a Pre-Greek origin. Note also Ancient Greek <i>λέπας</i> (lépas, “bare rock, crag”) and <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”), similar words of unexplained origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robber</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Attic,&#32;Koine) bandit; brigand (land-based)</li><li>(Attic,&#32;Ionic) pirate; buccaneer (by sea)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) one who forcibly appropriates power, status, or abstract goods</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Judaism,&#32;Christianity) insurgent; rebel (one engaged in violent resistance to authority)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>λῄζομαι</i> (lēízomai, “to plunder”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robbery, piracy</li></ol><p><i>λῃστεύω</i> (lēisteúō) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>piratical</li></ol><p><i>λῃστής</i> (lēistḗs, “pirate”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pirate vessel</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>λῃστής</i> (lēistḗs, “pirate, robber”), from <i>ληΐς</i> (lēḯs, “booty, spoil”) +&lrm; <i>-τρις</i> (-tris, feminine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>λειτουργῐ́ᾱ</i> (leitourgĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gum rockrose (<i>Cistus ladanifer</i>), a shrub from which labdanum exudes</li></ol><p>Backformation from <i>λήδανον</i> (lḗdanon, “labdanum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light summer dress</li></ol><p>The variation between forms with and without <i>-ι-</i> shows that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gain, income</li><li>premise, assumption</li></ol><p>From the Ancient Greek root <i>λᾱβ-</i> (lāb-), whence <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take”), and <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fleece</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wlḗnos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wĺ̥h₁neh₂</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>ऊर्णा</i> (ū́rṇā), Latin <i>lāna</i>, Old English <i>wull</i> (whence English <i>wool</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>determination or appointment by lot</li><li>portion assigned by lot, allotment</li><li>section of a body, determined by lot</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fortunes, wealths</li><li>assigned sphere, world, province</li><li>written complaint lodged with the archon, as the first step in private actions</li><li>cessation, ceasing</li><li>death, decay</li><li>termination, suffix</li><li>end, extremity</li></ol><p>From <i>λαγχάνω</i> (lankhánō, “to obtain by lot”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p><p>Beekes suggests that the term was formed according to the model of <i>λῆψῐς</i> (lêpsĭs), from <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō).</p><p>From <i>λήγω</i> (lḗgō, “to leave off, cease”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trash, trumpery, trifle</li><li>delirium</li><li>gold ornament on women's tunic</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lāros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂-</i> (expressive root). Cognate with Latin <i>lātrō</i>, Latin <i>lamentum</i>, <i>λάλος</i> (lálos), <i>λάσκω</i> (láskō).</p><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἐπελάθετο</i> (epelátheto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking hold, seizing, catching</li><li>accepting, receiving</li><li>attack of fever or sickness, seizure</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) assumption</li><li>(<i>in a poem</i>) choice of matter</li><li>(<i>in music</i>) choice of pitch</li></ol><p>From <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take, grasp, seize”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water used in washing, bathwater</li><li>dirt removed by washing, offscouring</li><li>moral filth, defilement</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lew-</i> (“dirt, mud”) + <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hem, fringe, border of a robe</li></ol><p>Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂welh₁-</i> (“hair, wool”), like Latin <i>vellus</i> (“fleece”), or from the same root of Ancient Greek <i>εὔληρα</i> (eúlēra, “rein”), Latin <i>lōrum</i> (“thong”) and Old Armenian <i>լար</i> (lar, “rope”). Beekes finds both these proposals unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lorum, thong</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>lōrum</i> (“thong”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>με</i> (me)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a degree (°)</li></ol><p>Abbreviation of μοῖρα (moîra) (see μοίρα (moíra))</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>by [+accusative]</li><li>(<i>Thessalian</i>) but</li><li>alternative form of <i>μή</i> (mḗ)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes compares Sanskrit <i>स्म</i> (sma, emphatic particle) and Hittite (-ma, “but”) and says it's unclear whether μά continues Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mn̥</i> or <i>&#42;mh₂</i>. Alternatively, Dunkel derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ma</i>, with a variant <i>&#42;mṓ</i> seen in Latin <i>immō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of instrument with twenty strings arranged in octaves</li><li>Lydian flute producing a high and low note together</li></ol><p>Probably from Lydian, while others have proposed a Semitic origin. Furnée compares the word with <i>πηκτίς</i> (pēktís, “Lydian stringed instrument”) and reconstructs a pre-form <i>&#42;μάγδις</i> (&#42;mágdis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philtre, charm, means for bewitching others</li><li>block of a pulley</li><li>bolt, iron peg</li><li>catapult, ballista, tormentum</li></ol><p>Sanskrit <i>मञ्जुल</i> (mañjula, “charming, lovely”) and <i>मङ्गल</i> (maṅgala, “lucky charm, amulet, talisman”) have been proposed as cognates. Connection to the group of <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō, “to knead”) is unconvincing. Van Beek suggests a comparison with <i>μηχανή</i> (mēkhanḗ, “contrivance”); the pair of words shows prenasalization and interchange of γ and χ, which would be typical reflexes of a Pre-Greek word. However, these are typical Semitic variations, and if the movement patterns of a pulley or catapult are compared with that of a sickle, the Greek is easier derived as a borrowing from the family of Aramaic <i>מַגְּלָא</i> / <i>ܡܓܠܐ</i> (maggǝlā), Hebrew <i>מַגָּל</i> (maggā́l), Arabic مِنْجَل (minjal), words of transparent derivation meaning “sickle”. Reflexes of said family passed into Old Georgian <i>მანგალი</i> (mangali), Old Armenian <i>մանգաղ</i> (mangał), and formally and geographically remarkably Georgian and Mingrelian <i>მაგანა</i> (magana), still meaning sickle. The senses “philtre”, “charm” and “pulley block”, “bolt”, “catapult” may ultimately have different origins, however both are relatable due to the image of witchcraft being “cast”, like the charge of a catapult is warped or a sickle is rapped.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watchtower</li></ol><p>From a Semitic source; compare Hebrew <i>מגדל</i> (migdál, “tower”) and Akkadian <i>𒈠𒀜𒂵𒆷𒌅</i> (madgaltu, “watchtower”); for further etymology and connections see Arabic مِجْدَل (mijdal).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μάγδωλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Magdolus, Lower Egypt, Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>μάγδωλος</i> (mágdōlos, “watch-tower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cook</li><li>butcher</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Pre-Greek. Compare also <i>μάχαιρα</i> (mákhaira, “large sword”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of spice mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of thick unguent</li></ol><p>From <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō, “I knead”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magnet</li><li>lodestone</li></ol><p>From <i>Μάγνης</i> (Mágnēs).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μάγνης)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Magnetes</li><li>an inhabitant of Magnesia; a Magnesian</li></ol><p>Uncertain, possibly Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magical</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(common,&#32;nonspecific) magician, and derogatorily sorcerer, trickster, conjurer, charlatan</li><li>(common,&#32;specific) a Zoroastrian priest. Compare e.g. Herodotus <i>Hist.</i> 1.132f, Xenophon <i>Cyropedia</i> 8.3.11, Porphyry <i>Life of Pythagoras</i> 12, Heraclitus apud Clemens <i>Protrepticus 12</i>, etc.</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) name of one of the tribes of the Medes. This usage is only attested once; Herodotus <i>Histories</i> 1.101.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎶𐎦𐏁</i> (m-gu-š /⁠maguš⁠/).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μάγος)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(common,&#32;nonspecific) magician, and derogatorily sorcerer, trickster, conjurer, charlatan</li><li>(common,&#32;specific) a Zoroastrian priest. Compare e.g. Herodotus <i>Hist.</i> 1.132f, Xenophon <i>Cyropedia</i> 8.3.11, Porphyry <i>Life of Pythagoras</i> 12, Heraclitus apud Clemens <i>Protrepticus 12</i>, etc.</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) name of one of the tribes of the Medes. This usage is only attested once; Herodotus <i>Histories</i> 1.101.</li></ol><p>From an Old Iranian language presumably akin to Old Median/Old Persian <i>𐎶𐎦𐎢𐏁</i> (m-gu-u-š /⁠maguš⁠/) and Avestan <i>𐬨𐬊𐬕𐬎</i> (moġu). Both attested Old Iranian words are hapaxes, and of indeterminable meaning. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₂gʰ-</i> (“to be able”). Probably unrelated to Avestan <i>𐬨𐬀𐬔𐬀</i> (maga) (cognate with Sanskrit <i>मघ</i> (magha, “gift”)). Attestation in Greek predates attestation in Old Iranian. See also <i>μῆχος</i> (mêkhos), <i>μηχανή</i> (mēkhanḗ).</p><p>Compare also Aramaic <i>𐡌𐡂𐡅𐡔</i> (mgwš), Aramaic <i>מגושא</i>, Arabic مجوس (majūs) and Hebrew <i>אמגושים</i> (amgushim) or Hebrew <i>מגושים</i> (mgushim) in the plural, in the Talmud.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of boat mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plums</li></ol><p>The derivation from <i>μᾶλον</i> (mâlon, “apple”) +&lrm; <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “tree”) is a folk etymology. According to Beekes, the variant <i>ἀμάδρυα</i> (amádrua) suggests a Pre-Greek etymology, due to the presence of the prothetic vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something that is learned: a lesson</li><li>learning, knowledge</li><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) the mathematical sciences in particular, the quadrivium: arithmetic, geometry, astronomy and astrology, harmonics</li><li>a creed</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō, “I learn”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of learning, getting of knowledge</li><li>desire for learning</li><li>education, instruction</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>μᾰνθᾰ́νω</i> (mănthắnō, “to learn”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>learning, knowledge</li><li>habit, custom</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blessed; happy</li></ol><p>Probably from Pre-Greek because of the interchange <i>ᾰρ&#47;ᾱρ</i> (ăr&#47;ār) in its variants, as well as the word's seemingly isolated formation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mattock, pick, used for digging or breaking up</li></ol><p>The similarity with <i>δίκελλα</i> (díkella) cannot be coincidental but a convincing explanation has yet to be found. Armenian <i>մարկեղ</i> (markeġ, “mattock”) shows a remarkable similarity; it might be a loan from a common source. Unrelated are Lithuanian <i>makaras</i> (“big stave”) and Armenian <i>մաճ</i> (mač, “plough tail”). The variation "-ελλα/-έλη" shows a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclosure</li><li>macellum, market</li></ol><p>A foreign borrowing, either from Semitic (compare Hebrew <i>מִכְלָה</i> (mikla'ah, “enclosure”)) or from the same Pre-Greek word family as <i>μακέλη</i> (makélē, “mattock”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>macir, the fragrant resin of <i>Ailanthus triphysa</i></li></ol><p>An Oriental loanword. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kneading trough</li><li>bathing tub</li><li>mortar for pounding drugs</li></ol><p>From <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō, “to knead”) +&lrm; <i>-τρα</i> (-tra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μάλα</i> (mála)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very, very much, exceedingly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>simply strengthening the word with which it stands</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with adjectives</i>)</li><li>(<i>with adverbs</i>)</li><li>(<i>with verbs</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>strengthening an assertion</i>)</li><li>(in Homer,&#32;often after <i>εἰ</i>)</li><li>(in Attic,&#32;often in answers) yes, certainly, exactly so</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mel-</i> (“strong, better”). Cognates include Latin <i>melius</i>, <i>multus</i> and Latvian <i>milns</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) An emollient, a cataplasm</li><li>A padding used in sieges to blunt the force of engines and weapons</li></ol><p>From <i>μαλάσσω</i> (malássō, “to mollify, to knead”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bdellium</li></ol><p>Furnée thinks it is a Semitic loan standing for <i>&#42;μαδαλκον</i> (&#42;madalkon), and compares <i>בְּדֹלַח</i> (bdólakh) and Akkadian <i>𒁉𒁺𒌨𒄷</i> (bidurḫu).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) armpit, axilla</li></ol><p>Unknown. It does not seem probable that this word is a shortened form of <i>μασχάλη</i> (maskhálē, “armpit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mixture of wax and pitch used for caulking ships and on writing tablets</li><li>a large aquatic animal (perhaps after its tender or wax-like meat)</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected with <i>μαλθακός</i> (malthakós, “soft”) and derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥dʰ-</i> (“soft”). Beekes separates from <i>μαλθακός</i> (malthakós) and argues for a Pre-Greek technical loanword; note that <i>&#42;ml̥dʰ-</i> would yield <i>&#42;βλαθ-</i> (&#42;blath-). Compare Arabic مِلَاط (milāṭ) and the other Semitic words mentioned there, as well as Middle Armenian <i>մաղթ</i> (maġtʻ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μᾰ́λῐστᾰ</i> (mắlĭstă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most, most of all, above all</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(strengthened,&#32;preceded by <i>ὡς</i> or <i>ὅτι</i>)</li><li>(<i>with the article</i>) for the most part, mostly</li><li>(<i>added to a superlative</i>)</li><li>(for <i>μᾶλλον</i>)</li><li>(in loose accounts of numbers,&#32;used to show that they are not exact)</li><li>(καὶ μάλιστα,&#32;used in answers) most certainly</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́λᾰ</i> (mắlă) +&lrm; <i>-ῐστᾰ</i> (-ĭstă, superlative adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>numbness from cold, especially in hands and feet</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) chilblain</li></ol><p>A convincing explanation is still lacking. There have been attempts to connect this word with Lithuanian <i>mùlkis</i> (“blockhead”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьlčati</i> (“to be silent”) and other words of doubtful origin. The connection with Ancient Greek <i>μαλακός</i> (malakós, “soft”) is unconvincing. According to Beekes, maybe from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mamma, mammy</li><li>mother's breast</li><li>(<i>later</i>) grandmother</li></ol><p>Back formed from the onomatopoeic baby-talk <i>μᾰ́μμᾰ</i> (mắmmă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic, Doric, and Epic form of <i>μήν</i> (mḗn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bolt of a door</li></ol><p>Technical word in <i>-αλο-</i> without etymology. The formation can hardly be Indo-European. Unrelated is <i>ἀμάνδαλον</i> (amándalon, “invisible”), in view of the divergent meanings.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enclosed space</li><li>fold, pen, barn, or stable for cattle or sometimes horses</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) bezel of a ring depicting oxen</li><li>square on a draughtboard</li><li>cloister, monastery</li></ol><p>Some have proposed a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mand-</i> (“enclosure”), cognate with Sanskrit <i>मन्दुरा</i> (mandurā, “stable; bed”) and possibly related to <i>μανδάκης</i> (mandákēs, “band to tie trusses”), <i>μάνδαλος</i> (mándalos, “bolt”), and Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;mandu</i> (“basket”), with a possible Pre-Greek acquisition of one or more Pre-Indo-European wanderworts.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>מָן</i> (mān, “manna”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Doric</i>) necklace</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mono-</i> (“neck”). Compare Sanskrit <i>मणि</i> (maṇi, “neck ornament”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prophet or seer</li><li>mantis</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>μαίνομαι</i> (maínomai, “to be mad or raving”), per J.B. Hoffman perhaps reflecting earlier <i>&#42;μάτις</i> (&#42;mátis) with analogical restoration of the nasal (similar to that seen in <i>χανδάνω</i> (khandánō) &lt; <i>&#42;χαδάνω</i> (&#42;khadánō)), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mə́tis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mń̥tis</i> (“(the act of) thinking, thought; mind”), from <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think, perceive”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tis</i> (deverbal noun-forming suffix) (whence <i>-τις</i> (-tis), <i>-σις</i> (-sis)), but Beekes is hesitant, since most <i>ti</i>-stem nouns are abstract rather than agent nouns. However, note similar formations (from different roots) such as <i>μᾰ́ρπτῐς</i> (mắrptĭs, “brigand”), <i>πόρτῐς</i> (pórtĭs, “calf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μονοκέφαλον σκόροδον</i> (monoképhalon skórodon, literally “one-headed garlic”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, clearly from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of the suffix "-ζα".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) apheretic form of <i>σμᾰ́ρᾰγδος</i>&nbsp;f or m (smắrăgdos, “emerald”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lash, scourge</li></ol><p>The word corresponds to Classical Syriac <i>ܡܪܓܢܐ</i> (māragnā, “kind of scourge”).</p><p>Hübschmann already assumed that both were loans from Old Persian <i>&#42;māra-gna</i> (“killer of snakes”), and Walther Hinz derives it from an Old Iranian term <i>&#42;māragna-</i> (literally “snake repellant”); compare Persian مار (mâr, “snake”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fennel</li></ol><p>Probably a Pre-Greek loan of foreign origin. The old connection with Swedish <i>mjärde</i> (“fish-trap”) is to be rejected when considering the phonetics of Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀔𐀨𐀶𐀺</i> (ma-ra-tu-wo). Compare Albanian <i>maraj</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>causing to die away</li><li>dying or fading away</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰραίνω</i> (măraínō, “to quench; to waste, wither”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, action noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>μαργαρίτης</i> (margarítēs, “pearl”)</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>μαργᾰρῑ́της</i> (margărī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mad, furious, rampant</li><li>greedy, gluttonous (of appetite)</li><li>lewd, lustful</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, all the variations prove a Pre-Greek origin of the word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of flute</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) hand</li></ol><p>Unknown. Forssman doubts the authenticity of this word and Blanc supposes that the form was coined to explain <i>εὐμαρής</i> (eumarḗs). Former comparisons with Germanic and Celtic words, to be found in Frisk, are now obsolete. Mallory and Adams suggest Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₂-r̥ ~ &#42;mh₂-én-</i> (from <i>&#42;(s)meh₂-</i> (“to beckon”)), comparing Latin <i>manus</i>, Hittite (manii̯aḫḫ-<sup>i</sup>, “to distribute, entrust”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure for liquids, containing six cotyles</li></ol><p>Possible loan from Old Persian <i>&#42;Mari-</i> (literally “jar, pitcher”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μάρις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Maris; Mureș (river), Romania</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crystalline rock</li><li>marble</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>anything made of marble, tombstone</li><li>marble chips</li></ol></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Based on the original sense of "stone, rock", Frisk derives the word from the root of <i>μᾰ́ρνᾰμαι</i> (mắrnămai, “to fight, battle”) (perhaps in a sense of "stone weapons"), with folk-etymological semantic influence from <i>μαρμαίρω</i> (marmaírō, “to sparkle”); others, such as Mazon, prefer to directly connect the word to <i>μαρμαίρω</i> (marmaírō). Due to the lack of strong evidence of connection to other words, as well as perhaps the semantic category of "local rocks", Beekes suggests Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>An alternative theory by Roberts suggests a reduplicative formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to rub away, cause harm, to die”), in the sense of marble's fragility.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, battle</li><li>to contend, strive</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin.</p><p>Beekes derives the verb from a zero-grade nasal present of a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;merh₂-</i> (“to seize, grip”), and compares Sanskrit <i>मृणीहि</i> (mṛṇīhi) and <i>मृणति</i> (mṛṇati) (from the root <i>मृण्</i> (mṛṇ, “to crush, smash”)) as potential exact matches. The original meaning of the Greek would have been something like "to crush or grab one another". However, the Sanskrit is traditionally derived from a nasal extension of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (“to grind, crush”), which cannot produce the Greek form.</p><p>Other potential cognates adduced by Rix (which, as Beekes notes, have not been ascertained by other linguists) include Albanian <i>marr</i> (“to take”), Old Norse <i>merja</i> (“to beat, crush”), and Hittite (marritta, “is crushed”). The Albanian and Germanic terms have alternative plausible derivations, which along with the Sanskrit place the existence of the Indo-European root in doubt.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take hold of, catch, grasp</li><li>to lay hold of, seize, overtake</li></ol><p>Within Greek, a connection may be drawn with some glosses found in Hesychius:</p><p>Nikolaev rejects a pre-Greek etymology and instead reconstructs Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;merkʷ-</i>, to which he also assigns Latin <i>merx</i> (“goods, merchandise”) (with delabialization before <i>s</i> generalized throughout its paradigm) and Tocharian A <i>märk-</i> (“to take away”). Additionally assignable to this root would be Sanskrit <i>मृच्</i> (mṛ́c, “snare”) Sanskrit <i>म॒र्चय॑ति</i> (marcáyati, “seize, grasp, take”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron spade</li></ol><p>Possibly from Semitic. Compare Akkadian <i>𒄑𒈥</i> (marru [<sup>GEŠ</sup>MAR]), Classical Syriac <i>ܡܪܐ</i> (marra) and Arabic مَرّ (marr). Compare also Latin <i>marra</i> (“hoe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag, pouch</li></ol><p>The word is most probably Pre-Greek, because of its variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>μᾰ́ρτῠς</i> (mắrtŭs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>μᾰ́ρτῠς</i> (mắrtŭs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>witness</li><li>martyr</li></ol><p>Often assigned to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mer-</i> (“to fall into thinking, remember, care for”), which is acknowledged by Beekes, but he finds it unconvincing and instead derives it from Pre-Greek based on the ending of the word.</p><p>Compare Latin <i>mas</i> and Latin <i>maritus</i>.</p><p>Le Feuvre (2021) maintains the usual derivation from <i>&#42;(s)mer-</i> (“to fall into thinking, remember, care for”) and explains the ending of the word from a zero-grade of <i>&#42;twerH-</i> (“to seize, bind”), whence also <i>σειρά</i> (seirá, “rope, cord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Something to chew, a quid</li></ol><p>From <i>μασάομαι</i> (masáomai, “to chew”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The act of chewing</li></ol><p><i>μασάομαι</i> (masáomai) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to handle, work with one's hands, knead</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wipe</li></ol><p>There are two prevailing theories, based on which velar stop in the word's derivatives was the original:</p><p>It is worth noting that these two roots appear to have been confused in various branches (as many of the cognates listed for <i>&#42;meHnk-</i> have alternatively been derived from <i>&#42;meh₂ǵ-</i>, though for all except the Germanic, this is phonetically implausible). It is possible that forms in or derived from <i>κ</i> (such as <i>μακαρία</i> (makaría) and <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō)) are from <i>&#42;meHnk-</i> and suppletive with the forms in <i>γ</i> (such as <i>μαγῆμαι</i> (magêmai)), which would be from <i>&#42;meh₂ǵ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth, jaws, that with which one chews</li><li>mouthful, morsel</li><li>locust (because of its voracity)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, related to <i>μασάομαι</i> (masáomai, “to chew”) and <i>μαστάζω</i> (mastázō, “to chew”), both terms he suspects to be of Pre-Greek origin. The formation is similar to that of <i>πόρταξ</i> (pórtax) and <i>μύλαξ</i> (múlax).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horsewhip</li><li>scourge; lash (a whip used for punishment)</li><li>lash (a stroke from a whip or lash)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) scourge; plague</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) lash; cut; bite (of a criticism or insult)</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>frivolously; pointlessly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vain; futile; empty; idle</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) empty; foolish; worthless</li><li>rash; irreverent; profane</li></ol><p>From <i>μάτη</i> (mátē, “fault, folly”) +&lrm; <i>-αιος</i> (-aios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folly, fault</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, there is no convincing etymology, partly due to the difficulty of ascertaining the word's original meaning (which was likely not "fault"). Some theories:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain, fruitlessly</li><li>at random, without reason</li><li>idly, falsely</li></ol><p>From <i>μάτη</i> (mátē, “folly, fault”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr, “mother”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pimp</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek source of <i>μαστροπός</i> (mastropós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μωρός</i> (mōrós, “stupid, dull, sluggish”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek and related to <i>μάτη</i> (mátē, “folly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large knife; short sword; dirk; dagger</li></ol><p>Controversial:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle; combat</li><li>quarrel; strife; dispute</li><li>contest; game</li><li>battlefield</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) contradiction; inconsistency</li></ol><p>From <i>μάχομαι</i> (mákhomai, “to do battle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for battle, warlike</li><li>operational (of or relating to operations, especially military operations)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighting man, warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lewd, lustful (of women)</li><li>wanton, luxuriant</li></ol><p>Furnée adduces <i>Βάκχος</i> (Bákkhos) (with interchange β/μ) and compares Armenian <i>մահազ</i> (mahaz, “lascivious”), suggesting that all these words are from Asia Minor. The interchange may also lead to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to make war, fight, battle</li><li>to quarrel, wrangle, dispute</li><li>to contend, compete</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;maǵʰ-</i> (“to fight”), though Beekes considers Pre-Greek origin more likely. See also Old Armenian <i>մաքառիմ</i> (makʻaṙim, “to combat, quarrel”), which may continue the same root. Connections to <i>Ἀμαζών</i> (Amazṓn, “Amazon”) are highly uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) in vain</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of bird</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes suggests Pre-Greek, stating it is unrelated to Latin <i>mox</i> (“soon”).</p><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μέγα</i> (méga)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>greatly; mightily; loudly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>μέγεθος</i> (mégethos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μέγαρα</i> (mégara)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large room, hall</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) house, palace</li><li>sanctuary, shrine</li><li>tomb</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) pits sacred to Demeter and Persephone, into which young pigs were let down in the Thesmophoria</li></ol><p>In the "sacred pit" sense, borrowed from Semitic, perhaps with influence from the "hall" sense; compare Arabic مَغَارَة (maḡāra, “cave”), Hebrew <i>מְעָרָה</i> (məʿārâ, “cave”), Ugaritic <i>𐎎𐎙𐎗𐎚</i> (mġrt, “cave”). See also Latin <i>māgālia</i> (“huts”).</p><p>In the "hall" sense, borrowed from Pre-Greek, albeit influenced in form and meaning by <i>μέγας</i> (mégas, “big, large”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>big; large</li><li>great; mighty; loud</li><li>marvelous; awesome</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mégas</i>, from a conflation of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“great”) with its derivative <i>&#42;meǵh₂los</i> (“great”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>म॒ह</i> (mahá, “great; mighty; strong; abundant”), Middle Persian <i>ms</i> (meh, “great”) (&lt; <i>&#42;mas</i>), (Persian مه (meh)), Avestan <i>𐬨𐬀𐬰-</i> (maz-, “large”), Tocharian B <i>māka</i> (“large”), Hittite (mēkkis, “much; many; numerous”), Old Armenian <i>մեծ</i> (mec), Old Irish <i>maige</i> (“great; large”), Albanian <i>madh</i> (“large”), Latin <i>magnus</i> and Old English <i>miċel</i> (English <i>much</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greatness, size</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>always in Homer</i>) height</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) magnitude</li><li>(<i>sound</i>) loudness</li><li>power</li><li>(<i>character</i>) magnanimity</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most greatly, greatest</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>big; large</li><li>great; mighty; loud</li><li>marvelous; awesome</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mégas</i>, from a conflation of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“great”) with its derivative <i>&#42;meǵh₂los</i> (“great”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>म॒ह</i> (mahá, “great; mighty; strong; abundant”), Middle Persian <i>ms</i> (meh, “great”) (&lt; <i>&#42;mas</i>), (Persian مه (meh)), Avestan <i>𐬨𐬀𐬰-</i> (maz-, “large”), Tocharian B <i>māka</i> (“large”), Hittite (mēkkis, “much; many; numerous”), Old Armenian <i>մեծ</i> (mec), Old Irish <i>maige</i> (“great; large”), Albanian <i>madh</i> (“large”), Latin <i>magnus</i> and Old English <i>miċel</i> (English <i>much</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>most greatly</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>μέγῐστος</i> (mégĭstos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corn measure, bushel</li><li>(<i>in Magna Graecia</i>) pipe of a fountain</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to measure”) and cognate with Ancient Greek <i>μέδομαι</i> (médomai, “to provide for”), Latin <i>modus</i> (“measure; manner”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mētō</i> (“measure”). According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek because of the presence of the cluster "-μν-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to provide for, think on, be mindful of, bethink one of</li></ol><p>From the Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to measure, give advice, heal”). Compare Avestan <i>𐬬𐬍-𐬨𐬀𐬛</i> (vī-mad), Old Persian (azdā), Old Armenian <i>միտ</i> (mit), Old Irish <i>midithir</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (mitaþs), Latin <i>meditor</i>, Modern German <i>Maut</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) mead</li></ol><p>Likely represents a Slavic or Germanic word (if the latter, probably Gothic or a related East Germanic dialect); ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰu</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect, rule over</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méd-e-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to measure, limit, consider, advise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) counsellors</li></ol><p>From <i>μέδω</i> (médō, “I rule over; I protect”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μέδων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Medon (son of Oīleus, stepbrother of Ajax)</li></ol><p>From <i>μέδων</i> (médōn, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>participation, partaking</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) a form of theater involving audience participation and improvisation</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) the relation between a particular and a Platonic form</li></ol><p><i>μετέχω</i> (metékhō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strong drink; heavy drinking</li><li>drunkenness</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) intoxication</li></ol><p>From <i>μέθυ</i> (méthu, “wine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>following after, pursuit</li><li>pursuit of knowledge, investigation, inquiry; hence, treatise</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>mode of prosecuting such inquiry, method, system, plan</li><li>doctrine</li><li>“methodic” medicine</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) means</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>means of recognizing</li><li>mode of treating the subject-matter</li></ol></ol><li>trick, ruse, stratagem</li></ol><p>From <i>μετ’</i> (met’, “transcending, encompassing, median”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “road, way, path”), literally “the transcending path”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) wine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;métʰu</i> (“intoxicating drink; mead”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰu</i> (“honey; mead”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>मधु</i> (mádhu, “honey”), Lithuanian <i>medùs</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>медъ</i> (medŭ, “honey”), and Old English <i>medu</i> (English <i>mead</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roofbeam, ridgepole, vault of the roof</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) house, dwelling</li><li>lair of an animal</li></ol><p>Explained by the ancients as deriving from <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”), because of the soot which accumulates on the ceiling. The comparison with <i>βλωθρός</i> (blōthrós, “high”), proposed by Frisk, fails because the latter would presuppose a root <i>*mlh₃-</i>, which can never yield <i>μέλα-</i>. Connection with <i>κμέλεθρον</i> (kmélethron, “beam”) was tentatively considered by Güntert and Pisani, because of the remarkable formal and semantic similarity. According to Beekes, this proves that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ink</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive of <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās, “black”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark in color; sometimes so dark as to lack color, black</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) evil, black, dark</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dark, obscure</li><li>(<i>of the voice</i>) indistinct</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) causing black secretions</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mélās</i>, from a derivation of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (“dark, black”).</p><p>The feminine <i>-αινα</i> (-aina) originates from <i>&#42;-ih₂</i>; see <i>-ια</i> (-ia). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>मल</i> (mála, “dirt, filth, dust”), Latvian <i>melns</i>, Old Prussian <i>melne</i> and Lithuanian <i>mėlynas</i> (which changed the meaning to “blue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A black or livid spot</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A black hair dye</li><li>The solid ink in a pencil</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) spots in the moon</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>μελαίνω</i> (melaínō, “to blacken”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to melt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;méldomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)meld-</i> (“to melt, smelt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendly term of address for both sexes: my friend!</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice&#32;and&#32;middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with passive meaning</i>) to be an object of care or interest [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>with active meaning</i>) to care for, be interested in [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone, something’</small>]</li></ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;active voice) to be a matter of concern [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something of concern’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Traditionally connected to <i>μέλλω</i> (méllō, “to intend, to mean to”), but Beekes considers this semantically weak.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>idle, useless, futile, vain</li><li>unhappy, miserable, wretched</li></ol><p>As for its accent, this word agrees with secondary adjectives like <i>χρύσεος</i> (khrúseos), <i>λίθεος</i> (lítheos), <i>βόεος</i> (bóeos), but conceptually it rather belongs to primary oxytones, like <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós), <i>κενεός</i> (keneós), <i>στερεός</i> (stereós). Like <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós) is from <i>&#42;ἐτεϝός</i> (&#42;etewós), this adjective could derive from <i>&#42;μέλεϝος</i> (&#42;mélewos). Further details are unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>object of care, beloved object</li><li>charge, duty</li><li>care, anxiety</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλω</i> (mélō, “to care for”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honey</li><li>anything sweet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;méli</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mélit</i> (“honey”). Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀕𐀪</i> (me-ri), Latin <i>mel</i>, Gothic <i>𐌼𐌹𐌻𐌹𐌸</i> (miliþ), and Old Armenian <i>մեղր</i> (mełr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ashen (made of ash-wood)</li></ol><p><i>μελίᾱ</i> (melíā, “manna ash”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) honey</li><li>poet (from their culling of sweet things from nature)</li><li>priestess at Delphi</li><li>(<i>Neoplatonist&#32;philosophy</i>) any pure being</li></ol><p>Potentially haplologized from &#42;μελιλίχια (&#42;melilíkhia), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mélit</i> (“honey”) + <i>&#42;leyǵʰ-</i> (“lick”). Compare Albanian <i>bletë</i> and Sanskrit <i>मधुलिह्</i> (madhulih).</p><p>Alternatively from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;meliťťa</i>, equivalent to <i>μέλιτ-</i> (mélit-) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μέλισσα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Melissa</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλισσᾰ</i> (mélissă, “bee”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>μέλισσᾰ</i> (mélissă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cooling food made from sour milk</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>melca</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) spring, fountain</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested as a gloss in Hesychius.</p><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melk-</i>, a relative or variant of the root <i>&#42;h₂melǵ-</i> (“to milk”) preserving older semantics, with cognates such as Old Church Slavonic <i>млѣко</i> (mlěko), Lithuanian <i>malkas</i>, and Latvian <i>malks</i>, but the lack of reflex of <i>*h₂</i> in Greek and unusual <i>*ǵ</i> : <i>*k</i> alternation are unexplained. Perhaps both the Greek and Balto-Slavic are via a foreign intermediates (e.g., the Balto-Slavic may be via Germanic, where Grimm's law was applied), but the Greek word with its divergent semantics is inexplicable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think of doing, intend to do, to mean to</li><li>to be about to do</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>by fate</i>), to be destined to do, to be fated to do</li><li>(by the will of other men,&#32;rare)</li><li>(<i>to denote a foregone conclusion</i>)</li><li>(<i>to mark a strong possibility</i>) to be likely to do</li></ol><li>(to mark mere intention,&#32;to be always going to do without ever doing) to delay, put off, hesitate</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin.</p><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mel-</i> (“to linger, hesitate, be late”), and connected with Latin <i>prōmellō</i> (“to postpone a court case”), Old Irish <i>mall</i> (“slow, tardy”). This appears to be tentatively and mildly favored by Beekes.</p><p>Gray objects to this and instead takes the word as a denominal verb from an unattested <i>&#42;μέλος</i> (&#42;mélos), connected to <i>μέλω</i> (mélō, “to care for; to be of interest”) and Latin <i>melior</i> (“better”). The root in this case is <i>&#42;mel-</i> (“strong, big”).</p><p>Another theory by Szemerenyi takes the word as related to <i>μολεῖν</i> (moleîn, “aorist infinitive of <i>βλώσκω</i> (blṓskō, “to go or come”)”), <i>μέλος</i> (mélos, “member”), Latin <i>mōlior</i> (“to strive, put in motion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part of a body, limb, member, part</li><li>part of a group, member</li><li>song, strain</li><li>tune</li><li>melody</li></ol><p>Likely from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mel-</i> (“limb”); compare Breton <i>mell</i> (“joint”), Cornish <i>mal</i> (“id”), Welsh <i>cymmal</i> (“id”), which are from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;melsā</i> (“knuckle”). For a semantic parallel (albeit from a different root), note Irish <i>alt</i> (“joint; paragraph”). See also <i>μέρος</i> (méros, “part, share”), of similar meaning and superficial pronunciation but different origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means of playing, plaything</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλπω</i> (mélpō, “to celebrate with song and dance”) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to celebrate with song and dance</li><li>to sing to the lyre or harp</li><li>to sing of, celebrate</li></ol><p>No etymology. If somehow related to <i>μέλος</i> (mélos, “song, strain”), we have to start from an original "member", not from "song". Connection with the Celtic group of Old Irish <i>molaidir</i> (“to praise”) and Welsh <i>mawl</i> (“praise, exaltation”) is dubious.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice&#32;and&#32;middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with passive meaning</i>) to be an object of care or interest [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>with active meaning</i>) to care for, be interested in [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone, something’</small>]</li></ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;active voice) to be a matter of concern [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something of concern’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Traditionally connected to <i>μέλλω</i> (méllō, “to intend, to mean to”), but Beekes considers this semantically weak.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish eagerly, strive, yearn, desire</li></ol><p>Originally the zero grade form of <i>μέμονα</i> (mémona), with which it shares some forms, but with semantic differentiation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be minded or inclined</li><li>to be eager</li><li>to hasten</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;memóne</i>, from <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think”); cognate with Latin <i>meminī</i> (“to remember”) and Gothic <i>𐌼𐌿𐌽𐌰𐌽</i> (munan, “to think, reckon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with dative or accusative of person</i>) to accuse, to blame</li></ol><p>Sometimes derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;me(-)mbʰ-e-</i>, and compared with obsolete English <i>mump</i> (“to cheat, swindle”), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌹𐌼𐌰𐌼𐍀𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (bimampjan, “to mock”).</p><p>Although Kroonen claims <i>μέμφομαι</i> (mémphomai) and the Germanic forms are reconcilable, Beekes rejects this (though admitting a striking similarity), stating that Germanic <i>-p-</i> cannot here correspond to Greek <i>-φ-</i> and that this etymological connection is not widely considered reliable. He prefers to tentatively derive the terms from a hypothetical Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mebʰ-</i> (“to blame, disgrace”), via a reduplicated or nasal present, and compares them to Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;meblā</i> (“shame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>on the one hand, while, whereas (often left untranslated)</li><li>(<i>before other particles</i>) accordingly, and so</li></ol><p>Shortened from <i>μήν</i> (mḗn, “verily”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind</li><li>desire, ardor, wish, purpose</li><li>anger</li><li>courage, spirit, vigor</li><li>power, strength, force</li><li>violence</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ménos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ménos</i> (“mind”), from <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think”). Cognates include Avestan <i>𐬨𐬀𐬥𐬋</i> (manō) and Sanskrit <i>मनस्</i> (mánas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with a conjunctive force</i>) yet, however, nevertheless</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of course</li></ol><li>(<i>as an adverb in strong protestations</i>)</li><li>(<i>μέντοι γε</i>)</li><li>(<i>καὶ μέντοι καί</i>)</li><li>(<i>ἀλλὰ μέντοι</i>) but as a matter of fact, but really</li></ol><p>From <i>μέν</i> (mén) + <i>τοι</i> (toi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay, wait</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in battle</i>) to stand fast</li><li>to stay where one is</li><li>to lodge</li><li>to tarry; to loiter, be idle</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to be lasting; to remain, stand</li><li>(<i>of condition</i>) to remain</li><li>to abide by</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;with infinitive) it remains</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to await, expect</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to wait for (accusative) to (infinitive)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ménō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to stay, stand still”). Cognates include Latin <i>maneō</i> (“remain”), Persian ماندن (mândan, “to remain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, thought, solicitude</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>object of care or thought</li><li>pursuit, ambition</li><li>anxious mind, anxiety</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mérimnā</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mer-</i> (“to remember”).</p><p>Cognate with <i>μέρμερος</i> (mérmeros, “anxious”), Old English <i>ġemimor</i> (“mindful, remembering”) and Latin <i>memor</i> (“mindful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part</li></ol><p><i>μερῐ́ζω</i> (merĭ́zō, “divide”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mischievous, baneful</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) captious, fastidious</li></ol><p>Under <i>μέριμνα</i> (mérimna, “care, thought, solicitude”), the primary thematic root-verb Sanskrit <i>स्मरति</i> (smarati, “to remember, recall”), Avestan <i>𐬨𐬁𐬭𐬀𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (māraiie<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti, “to remember”) was adduced. The length of the vowel in <i>μέρμηρα</i> (mérmēra, “care, trouble”), was explained by Frisk from the verb <i>μερμηρίζω</i> (mermērízō), where he ascribed it to the meter. Yet, this is no sufficient explanation: the interchange <i>ε/η</i> rather points to Pre-Greek origin, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord, string, rope</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>γέλγῑς</i> (gélgīs), <i>δέλλῑς</i> (déllīs) and <i>ὄρνῑς</i> (órnīs). Cognates have been supposed in <i>μηρύω</i> (mērúō, “to wind up”) as well as in <i>βρόχος</i> (brókhos, “noose”) and <i>μάραθον</i> (márathon, “fennel”). The word may be of Pre-Greek origin and related to <i>μήρινθος</i> (mḗrinthos, “fishing line”) and <i>μῆριγξ</i> (mêrinx, “bristle, hair”) but compare Latvian <i>mer̂ga</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;merža</i> (“net”), and possibly Lithuanian <i>márška</i> (“fishing net”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of hawk</li></ol><p>Unknown, but the Oxyrhynchus Papyri claim that the word was Lydian, comparing the Lydian dynasty of the Mermnadae. However, Beekes prefers connecting the word with <i>μόρφνος</i> (mórphnos, “kind of eagle”), which shows that the word is Pre-Greek, in view of the alternation <i>μ/φ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part, component, region</li><li>share, portion</li><li>one's turn</li><li>heritage, lot, destiny</li><li>member of a set, kind, type</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;(h)méros</i>; equivalent to <i>μερ-</i> (mer-), the root of <i>μείρομαι</i> (meíromai, “to receive as one's portion”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Compare <i>μέλος</i> (mélos, “limb, member”), of similar meaning and superficial pronunciation but different origin; and the likely cognate Middle English <i>merit</i> and Latin <i>meritus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) epithet of men</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>mortal (<i>of men</i>)</li></ol><li>bee-eater (<i>Merops apiaster</i>)</li></ol><p>The original meaning of the word is unknown. However, the suffix "-οψ", which is found also in <i>δρύοψ</i> (drúops) and <i>πάρνοψ</i> (párnops), is typical of Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μέροψ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Merops</i></li></ol><p>From <i>μέροψ</i> (mérops, “mortals, mankind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weaver's beam</li></ol><p>Almost certainly a technical word of Pre-Greek origin, because of the many different variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a north-northeasterly wind</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>middle of, between, amidst</li><li>half</li><li>of middling quality: moderate, intermediate</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the middle finger</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the ecliptic of a celestial body</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) the middle term of a syllogism</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;métsos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰyos</i> (“between”). Cognate with Latin <i>medius</i>, Sanskrit <i>मध्य</i> (madhya), Avestan <i>𐬨𐬀𐬌𐬜𐬌𐬌𐬁𐬥𐬀</i> (ma<sup><small>i</small></sup>δiiāna), Old Armenian <i>մէջ</i> (mēǰ), etc.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepskin, fleece</li></ol><p>A Semitic borrowing, ultimately from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;mašk-</i>; cognates are Armenian <i>մաշկ</i> (mašk, “skin, hide”), Arabic مَسْك (mask) and Classical Syriac <i>ܡܫܟܐ</i> (meškā, “skin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>middle of, between, amidst</li><li>half</li><li>of middling quality: moderate, intermediate</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the middle finger</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the ecliptic of a celestial body</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) the middle term of a syllogism</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;métsos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰyos</i> (“between”). Cognate with Latin <i>medius</i>, Sanskrit <i>मध्य</i> (madhya), Avestan <i>𐬨𐬀𐬌𐬜𐬌𐬌𐬁𐬥𐬀</i> (ma<sup><small>i</small></sup>δiiāna), Old Armenian <i>մէջ</i> (mēǰ), etc.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medlar (<i>Mespilus germanica</i>)</li></ol><p>A loanword of Pre-Greek origin because no Indo-European cognates can be found, as plant names are more frequently borrowed and, particularly concerning this term, the medlar is native to the regions of Iran, southwest Asia and southeastern Europe and its Black Sea coasts so that it was unknown to the speakers of Proto-Indo-European and borrowing became necessary. Possibly a cognate to Proto-Kartvelian <i>&#42;sxmarṭl-</i> (“medlar”) with metathesis of the initial consonants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Doric form of <i>μέσος</i> (mésos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>paroxytone form of <i>μετᾰ́</i> (metắ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mine, quarry, or salt pit</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) metal</li></ol><p>Likely from Pre-Greek because of the presence of <i>-αλλ-</i> (-all-).</p><p>Traditionally explained by grammarians as a deverbal from <i>μετᾰλλᾰ́ω</i> (metăllắō, “to seek out”), as if from <i>μέτ-</i> (mét-, “next”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́λλος</i> (ắllos, “another”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō), but Frisk finds the semantic connection very tenuous, and points out that the verb could simply be a later derivation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raw silk</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be among</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to have a share in</li><li>to go between or among</li><li>to follow</li><li>to go after, pursue</li><li>to execute judgment upon</li><li>to request, beseech</li><li>to pass over to</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “to be”)</p><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>εἶμι</i> (eîmi, “will go”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metic; settler from abroad, alien resident in a foreign city-state, denizen, immigrant</li><li>(Koine&#32;only,&#32;rare) occupant of the same house</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-, indicating change) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “home”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharing in, partaking of</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partner, accomplice in</li><li>member of a board of officials</li><li>joint owner of a house</li></ol><p>From <i>μετέχω</i> (metékhō, “to take part”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moderate, average, mean</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτρον</i> (métron) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something used to measure: measure, rule, weight</li><li>length, width, breadth</li><li>(music,&#32;poetry) metre</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₁-</i> (“to measure”) + <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brow, forehead</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-, “between”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>until</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>until</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>of spatial extension: as far as</li><li>of temporal extension: until</li><li>of general extent: to the point of, so far as</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méǵʰ(s)ri</i> (locative), from <i>&#42;me</i> (“with”) (see also <i>μετά</i> (metá)) + <i>&#42;ǵʰes-</i> (“hand”) (whence <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”)). Cognate with its zero-grade <i>ἄχρι</i> (ákhri, “utterly, as far as”), Old Armenian <i>մերձ</i> (merj, “near, by”) and Albanian <i>ndjerë</i> (“until, near”).</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of comparison/extent.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μέχρι</i> (mékhri)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μέχρι</i> (mékhri)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μέχρι</i> (mékhri)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>not (<i>used in clauses expressing will or thought</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with an imperative or subjunctive in negative commands</i>)</li><li>(with an optative or past indicative,&#32;expressing a negative wish)</li><li>(<i>in a question expecting a negative answer</i>)</li><li>(<i>in dependent clauses</i>)</li><li>(<i>with participle representing conditional clause</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>in warnings or statements of fear</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in independent clauses</i>) that... not; that</li><li>(<i>introducing indirect statement after verb of fearing or apprehension</i>) that</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mḗ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₁</i> (prohibitive particle). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>մի</i> (mi), Sanskrit <i>मा</i> (mā́), Old Persian <i>𐎶𐎠</i> (m-a /⁠mā⁠/), and Albanian <i>mos</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μήδεα</i> (mḗdea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;plural only) counsel, plan, art, prudence, cunning</li><li>(Epic,&#32;plural only) male genitals</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mḗdeha</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₁desh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to measure, give advice”), whence also <i>μέδω</i> (médō), <i>μέδομαι</i> (médomai). Cognates include Armenian <i>միտ</i> (mit).</p><p>Uncertain. Has been connected with etymology 1 (compare Old High German <i>gimaht</i> for a similar double meaning), Latin <i>mentula</i>, and with <i>μαδάω</i> (madáō) and Middle Irish <i>mess</i>. Could also be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contrive, devise, plot</li></ol><p>From <i>μήδεα</i> (mḗdea, “counsel, plan”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μήτε</i> (mḗte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>μᾰκρός</i> (măkrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opium poppy (<i>Papaver somniferum</i>)</li><li>single poppyseed</li><li>quasi-liver of testaceans</li><li>ink sac of the cuttlefish</li><li>a metallic sand</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) the part of the ear at the root under the lobe</li></ol><p>Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;makъ</i>, (Old Church Slavonic <i>макъ</i> (makŭ)) and German <i>Mohn</i>; from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₂ko-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>surgery</i>) surgical probe</li></ol><p>Prellwitz proposed a derivation from the root of <i>μαίομαι</i> (maíomai, “to touch, examine”). Possible, but uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or made of apples</li><li>of quince-yellow colour</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan bryony (<i>Bryonia cretica</i>)</li><li>dyed wool</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>used to strengthen statements: verily, surely, truly, definitely</li><li>(<i>after other particles</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ἦ μήν</i>) absolutely</li><li>(<i>καὶ μήν</i>) used to introduce something new or convey affirmation</li><li>(<i>αλλὰ μήν</i>) yet truly, on the other hand</li><li>(<i>οὐ μήν</i>) certainly not</li><li>(<i>μήτε μήν</i>) not even</li></ol><li>(after interrogatives,&#32;τί μήν) of course, naturally</li><li>(<i>after a negative</i>) applies an adversative force</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;méns</i> (“month”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗh₁n̥s</i> (“moon, month”), probably from <i>&#42;meh₁-</i> (“to measure”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀕𐀜</i> (me-no), Latin <i>mēnsis</i> (“month”), and English <i>moon</i>, <i>month</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)me(h₂)</i> (“really, true”). Cognate with Old Irish <i>má</i> (“if”), Sanskrit <i>स्म</i> (sma, “verily, assertive particle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moon</li></ol><p>From, or cognate with, <i>μήν</i> (mḗn).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μήνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Moon.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗh₁n̥s</i> (“moon, month”), probably from Proto-Indo-European base <i>&#42;meh₁-</i> (“to measure”). Cognate with Persian ماه (mâh), Old Armenian <i>ամիս</i> (amis, “month”), Latin <i>mensis</i> (month), Old Church Slavonic <i>месеци</i> (meseci), Latvian <i>mēnesis</i>, Dutch <i>maan</i>, Welsh <i>mis</i>, Breton <i>miz</i> (month).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common peony (<i>Paeonia officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μήν</i> (mḗn, “month”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>never, on no account</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in prohibition or strong denial,&#32;with aorist subjunctive)</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) perhaps</li></ol></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that at not time, lest ever</li></ol><p>From <i>μή</i> (mḗ, “not”) + <i>ποτέ</i> (poté, “ever, at some time”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not yet</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that…not yet, lest…yet</li></ol><p>From <i>μή</i> (mḗ) +&lrm; <i>πω</i> (pō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord, line, string</li><li>fishing line</li></ol><p>The suffix is similar to <i>ἐρέβινθος</i> (erébinthos), <i>ὑάκινθος</i> (huákinthos) and <i>λαβύρινθος</i> (labúrinthos), so it is probably a Pre-Greek word. Adaptation to <i>μηρύομαι</i> (mērúomai, “to draw up, to furl”) has also been assumed, however there is little reason for this. Maybe related to <i>μῆριγξ</i> (mêrinx, “bristle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) A ruminant; something which ruminates or chews the cud.</li><li>Mediterranean parrotfish (<i>Sparisoma cretense</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) counselor, adviser</li></ol><p><i>μήδομαι</i> (mḗdomai) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μήτε</i> (mḗte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(mostly doubled,&#32;sometimes with <i>μηδέ</i> (mēdé)) and not, neither...nor</li></ol><p>From <i>μή</i> (mḗ, “not”) +&lrm; <i>τε</i> (te, “and”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother</li><li>source or origin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mā́tēr</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀔𐀳𐀩</i> (ma-te-re), Doric <i>μᾱ́τηρ</i> (mā́tēr)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₂tēr</i>. Cognates include Phrygian <i>ματαρ</i> (matar), Old Armenian <i>մայր</i> (mayr), Latin <i>māter</i>, Old Persian <i>𐎶𐎠𐎫𐎠</i> (m-a-t-a /⁠mātā⁠/), Avestan <i>𐬨𐬁𐬙𐬀𐬭</i> (mātar), Sanskrit <i>मातृ</i> (mā́tṛ), Old Church Slavonic <i>мати</i> (mati), and Old English <i>mōdor</i> (English <i>mother</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so that nothing, lest anything</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>used to introduce questions expecting a negative answer</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not at all, by no means</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>μή</i> (mḗ, “so that...not, lest”) +&lrm; <i>τι</i> (ti, “anything”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so that no one, lest anyone</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>μή</i> (mḗ, “so that...not, lest”) +&lrm; <i>τις</i> (tis, “anyone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>womb</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>paunch, rumen</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) source, origin</li></ol><li>the pith or heart of wood</li><li>a queen wasp</li></ol><p>From <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr, “mother”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maternal uncle</li><li>ancestral male relative of one's mother</li><li>(<i>Cretan</i>) any male relative of one's mother</li></ol><p>From <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr, “mother”). Compare <i>πᾰ́τρως</i> (pắtrōs, “paternal uncle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pollution, defilement</li><li>shameful action, crime</li></ol><p>From <i>μιαίνω</i> (miaínō, “to stain”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μίασμα</i> (míasma, “defilement”), <i>ἀσέβημα</i> (asébēma, “sacrilege”).</li></ol><p>From <i>μιαίνω</i> (miaínō, “to defile, pollute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mixture; a compound</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (mī́gnūmĭ, “to mix”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to mix; to mix up; to mingle</li><li>to join; to bring together</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to join battle hand-to-hand</li><li>to bring into connection with; to make acquainted</li></ol></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;with future middle)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be mixed up with; to be mingled among</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to be brought into contact with</li><li>to mix in fight</li><li>to have sex with</li></ol></ol></ol><p>By surface analysis, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meyǵ-, &#42;meyḱ-</i> (“to mix”) +&lrm; <i>-νῡμῐ</i> (-nūmĭ). Cognates include Welsh <i>mysgu</i> (“to mix”), Latin <i>misceō</i> (“mix”), Old Church Slavonic <i>мѣшати</i> (měšati, “to mix”) (Russian <i>мешать</i> (mešatʹ)), Lithuanian <i>maišyti</i> (“to mix”), Sanskrit <i>मि॒श्र</i> (miśrá, “mixed”), and Old English <i>&#42;mixian, miscian</i> (“to blend; to mix; to combine”) (English <i>mix</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A destructive insect in grains</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μίδας)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Midas</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phrygian <i>Μιδας</i> (Midas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman mile (1,535 metres)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of ruddle</li></ol><p>From <i>μίλτος</i> (míltos, “ruddle”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruddle, red ochre</li><li>red lead</li><li>rust in plants</li><li>(<i>taboo word</i>) blood</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hare-soup or jugged hare, with the blood of the animal in it</li></ol><p>This word seems to display reduplication. A tempting similarity exists with a synonymous Germanic word: compare Old English <i>mearh</i> (“sausage”), Old Norse <i>mǫrr</i> (“the fat inside a slaughtered animal”) and Norwegian Nynorsk <i>mòr</i> (“kind of Norwegian sausage”). If related, it would have to be a very old anatomical expression of cattle-breeders, which is unlikely.</p><p>According to Neumann, this word is a loan from Hittite or another Anatolian language, while Furnée suggests a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the reduplication.</p><p>Unrelated are Latin <i>murcus</i> (“maimed”) and Hittite (mark-, “to cut apart”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>simulation</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑμέομαι</i> (mīméomai, “to simulate”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μίμνετε</i> (mímnete)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>μίμνετο</i> (mímneto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay</li><li>to wait for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to stay”). Beekes rejects the comparison with Hittite <i>𒈪𒅎𒈠𒄿</i> (mi-im-ma-i /⁠mimmai⁠/, “to refuse, decline”), citing "semantic and formal" concerns.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mint (<i>Mentha viridis</i>)</li></ol><p>A foreign substrate word, like Latin <i>menta</i> (“mint”), likely Pre-Greek, because of the variant ending in ”-ᾰ” and the -νθ- cluster, see also <i>ἀψίνθιον</i> (apsínthion). See Armenian <i>մանդակ</i> (mandak) for more. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀖𐀲</i> (mi-ta).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shit, especially of humans</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ὄνθος</i> (ónthos, “dung of animals”), <i>σπέλεθος</i> (spélethos, “dung, ordure”) and <i>σπύραθος</i> (spúrathos, “droppings of goats and sheep”). According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>minium, a substance similar to cinnabar</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>minium</i>, itself from Iberian substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a little while</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“small, little”); compare <i>μινύθω</i> (minúthō, “to lessen, disappear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mixing; a mingling</li><li>intercourse</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sexual intercourse</li><li>commerce</li></ol></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;míḱtis</i>, from the root <i>&#42;meyḱ- ~ &#42;meyǵ-</i> (“to mix”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tis</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>mi̇̀šti</i>, Avestan <i>𐬨𐬌𐬱𐬙𐬌</i> (mišti). Compare <i>μῐ́σγω</i> (mĭ́sgō) and <i>μῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (mī́gnūmĭ, “to mix”), from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>μῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (mī́gnūmĭ, “to mix”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;miǵ-sḱeti</i>, from <i>&#42;meyḱ-, &#42;meyǵ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>object of hate</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑσέω</i> (mīséō, “to hate”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salaried, hired</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) hired labourer</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) hired servant</li><li>(substantive,&#32;in the plural) mercenaries</li></ol><p>From <i>μισθός</i> (misthós, “wages”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copper ore found in Cyprus</li><li>truffle (<i>Tuber aestivum</i>), growing near Cyrene</li></ol><p>Perhaps an adjective meaning "brilliant" borrowed from an Anatolian language; compare Hittite (mišriuant, “brilliant, shining”), from the same root of Sanskrit <i>मिषति</i> (miṣati, “to blink, open the eyes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) stalk of leaves or fruit, petiole</li><li>(<i>in Thessaly</i>) kind of spade or hoe</li></ol><p>Seemingly first agricultural then made botanical by Theophrastus. <i>μίσκαιος</i> (mískaios, “garden”) with Lithuanian <i>mi̇̀škas</i> (“wood, forest”) does not give a likely meaning connection, while <i>μίσκος</i> (mískos, “husk, pod”) is very similar. While Pre-Greek origin has been claimed, the form and variation <i>σχ/σκ</i> looks kind of Semitic, compare <i>μασχάλη</i> (maskhálē, “axil, hollow at the base of a shoot; branch, young palm twig”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread of the warp</li><li>thread of destiny</li><li>string of a lyre</li></ol><p>Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“to bind”), like <i>μίτρα</i> (mítra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A metal guard worn round the waist</li><li>A maiden's girdle</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) A surgical bandage</li><li>A headband, a snood</li><li>A victor's chaplet at the games</li><li>A kind of turban</li></ol><p>Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“to bind”) or a loan from an Indo-Iranian source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blotched picarel (<i>Spicara maena</i>)</li></ol><p>No etymology. The connection with Russian <i>мен</i> (men, “burbot”), Lithuanian <i>ménkė</i> (“haddock”) and Sanskrit <i>मीन</i> (mīna, “Pisces; sprat”) is extremely doubtful. Strömberg hesitatingly attempts to connect it with <i>μαίνομαι</i> (maínomai, “to rage”), by interpreting it as the "wild raging fish".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be mad, angry, to rage</li><li>to be mad, raving, out of one's mind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;məňňómai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mn̥yétor</i> (“to think”), from <i>&#42;men-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>मन्यते</i> (manyate), Old Church Slavonic <i>мьнѣти</i> (mĭněti), Old Irish <i>·muinethar</i>, and Lithuanian <i>miniu</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any kneaded mass, cake</li><li>cake offered to Hecate and Trophonius</li><li>kind of small cheese-pudding</li><li>kneading trough or dresser</li><li>small table</li><li>round pan or plate for placing on the tripod</li></ol><p>From <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō, “to knead”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warehouse</li><li>store</li></ol><p>From Venetan <i>magasín</i>, <i>magazén</i>, from Arabic مَخَازِن&nbsp;pl (maḵāzin), plural of مَخْزَن (maḵzan, “storeroom, storehouse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use charms or philtres, play tricks, cheat</li><li>to trick out (food), dress it artificially (making it appear better)</li></ol><p>From <i>μάγγανον</i> (mánganon, “means of charming or bewitching, philtre”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard of the watchtower</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́γδωλος</i> (mắgdōlos, “watchtower”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>theology of the Magians</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) magic, sorcery</li></ol><p>From <i>μάγος</i> (mágos) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cookery</li></ol><p>By ellipsis, from <i>μαγειρικὴ</i> [<i>τέχνη</i>], "the art of cooking", from <i>μαγειρικός</i> (mageirikós, “fit for cooking”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for a cook or cookery</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) skilled in cooking</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́γειρος</i> (mắgeiros) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magical</li><li>skilled in magic</li></ol><p>From <i>μάγος</i> (mágos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of several ores and metallic amalgams</li></ol><p>Clipping of <i>Μαγνησίᾱ λίθος</i> (Magnēsíā líthos, literally “Magnesian stone”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μαγνησία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Magnesia, Thessaly, Greece</li><li>Magnesia in Asia Minor; Manisa, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>Μάγνης</i> (Mágnēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inflorescence of the silphium</li><li>horse fennel (<i>Prangos ferulacea</i>)</li></ol><p>A foreign word of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a falling off of hair, baldness (especially of the eyelids)</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰδᾰρόω</i> (mădăróō, “make bald”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), from <i>μᾰδᾰρός</i> (mădărós, “bald”), from <i>μαδάω</i> (madáō, “I fall off, am bald”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be moist, wet</li><li>to melt away</li><li>(<i>of hair</i>) to fall off</li><li>to be bald</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₂d-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>मदति</i> (madati), <i>ममत्ति</i> (mamatti), Latin <i>madeō</i>, and Old Irish <i>maidid</i>. Compare Serbo-Croatian <i>modar</i> (“blue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moist, wet</li><li>bald</li></ol><p>From <i>μαδάω</i> (madáō, “I fall off, am bald”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>μαδαρός</i> (madarós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>μαδαρός</i> (madarós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>μᾰστός</i> (măstós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) pupil, student, schoolgirl</li></ol><p>From <i>μαθητής</i> (mathētḗs) +&lrm; <i>-τρια</i> (-tria)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cup, used as a unit of measure</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ἀγκαλίς</i> (ankalís), <i>τρυφαλίς</i> (truphalís) and <i>φυσαλίς</i> (phusalís), but without etymology. Connection with <i>μάθυαι</i> (máthuai, “jaws”) and <i>μασάομαι</i> (masáomai, “to chew”) seems impossible. Furnée compares Latin <i>matula</i> (“vessel for liquids; chamber pot”), Georgian <i>მათარა</i> (matara, “waterskin, flask”) and <i>βάτος</i> (bátos, “Hebrew unit of measure”). Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mathematics</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scientific, esp. mathematical</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>substantive, masculine</i>) mathematician</li><li>(<i>substantive, feminine</i>) mathematics</li></ol><li>astronomical</li><li>astrological</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mathematician</li><li>(<i>Pythagoreanism</i>) advanced student</li></ol><p>From <i>μάθημα</i> (máthēma, “learning; mathematics”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “-ic”, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>learner, pupil</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>pupil of a philosopher or rhetorician</li><li>disciple</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>μᾰθη-</i> (măthē-), a combining form of <i>μᾰνθᾰ́νω</i> (mănthắnō, “to learn”), + <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a pupil, become a pupil</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make a disciple of, instruct, teach</li></ol><p>From <i>μαθητής</i> (mathētḗs, “pupil, disciple”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maieutics</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>μαιευτικὴ τέχνη</i> (maieutikḕ tékhnē), from <i>μαιεύομαι</i> (maieúomai, “to serve as a midwife”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly of men</i>) blessed, happy</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>often in Plato as a plural substantive</i>) the rich and better educated</li><li>(<i>of the dead</i>)</li><li>(<i>of states, qualities, and the like</i>)</li></ol></ol><p><i>μάκαρ</i> (mákar, “blessed”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μακάριος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>μακάριος</i> (makários, “blessed, happy, fortunate”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of priest in Lydia</li></ol><p>Probably from Lydian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>happiness, bliss</li></ol><p>From <i>μάκαρ</i> (mákar, “blessed”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly of men</i>) blessed, happy</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>often in Plato as a plural substantive</i>) the rich and better educated</li><li>(<i>of the dead</i>)</li><li>(<i>of states, qualities, and the like</i>)</li></ol></ol><p><i>μάκαρ</i> (mákar, “blessed”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μακαρία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macaria</li></ol><p>From <i>μάκαρ</i> (mákar, “blessed”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tall, taper</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p><p>Traditionally explained as either formed from a nominal construction parallel to <i>μακρός</i> (makrós), <i>μῆκος</i> (mêkos) + suffix <i>-δόν-</i> (-dón-) which is typical for appellations, animal names and <i>nomina actionis</i> and may be zero vocalism of <i>-δόνος</i> (-dónos), <i>-δών-</i> (-dṓn-). It can also be perceived as an arrangement of <i>μηκεδανός</i> (mēkedanós, “long”), in its turn derivation of <i>μῆκος</i> (mêkos). Hesychius records μακεδνή (fem) as 'long, tall'.</p><p>Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek non-Indo-European origin, however, de Decker and Nikolaev both reject the argument.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) parsley</li></ol><p>From <i>Μακεδών</i> (Makedṓn, “Macedonian”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>distance</i>) far</li><li>(<i>time</i>) long, for a long time</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>μᾰκρός</i> (măkrós, “long, far”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>μακρᾱ́ν</i> (makrā́n)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long</li><li>tall, deep</li><li>far, distant</li><li>large</li><li>(<i>time</i>) long</li><li>(grammar,&#32;of vowels) long</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;makrós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mh₂ḱrós</i>, from <i>&#42;meh₂ḱ-</i> (“to increase”). By surface analysis, <i>μῆκος</i> (mêkos) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós). Cognates include Latin <i>macer</i> and Old English <i>mæġer</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak at great length</li></ol><p>From <i>μακρήγορος</i> (makrḗgoros, “speaking at great length”), from <i>μακρός</i> (makrós, “long”) + <i>ἀγορεύω</i> (agoreúō, “to speak”), + <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long-windedness, tediousness</li></ol><p>From <i>μακρηγορέω</i> (makrēgoréō, “I speak at great length”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long-suffering, patience</li><li>forbearance</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰκρόθῡμος</i> (măkróthūmos, “long-suffering, patient”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>μᾰκρός</i> (măkrós, “long”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul; temper, passion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>μᾰκροθῡμῐ́ᾱ</i> (măkrothūmĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) long-tailed</li></ol><p><i>μᾰκρός</i> (măkrós, “large”, “long”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρᾱ́</i> (ourā́, “tail”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long</li><li>tall, deep</li><li>far, distant</li><li>large</li><li>(<i>time</i>) long</li><li>(grammar,&#32;of vowels) long</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;makrós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mh₂ḱrós</i>, from <i>&#42;meh₂ḱ-</i> (“to increase”). By surface analysis, <i>μῆκος</i> (mêkos) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós). Cognates include Latin <i>macer</i> and Old English <i>mæġer</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>μᾰκρός</i> (măkrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>μηκύνω</i> (mēkúnō)</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾶκος</i> (mâkos) +&lrm; <i>-ύνω</i> (-únō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>malabathrum, Indian bay leaf, Malabar leaf (<i>Cinnamomum tamala</i>)</li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>तमालपत्त्र</i> (tamālapattra, “leaf of cinnamon”, literally “dark tree leaves”), from <i>तमस्</i> (tamas, “dark”) + <i>पत्त्र</i> (pattra, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften (<i>especially a material like leather or metal</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to give (<i>someone</i>) a dressing down, to tan someone's hide</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to soften, appease, cause to relent</li></ol><p>From <i>μαλακός</i> (malakós) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mallow</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Likely related to or the sources of Latin <i>malva</i>, Middle Armenian <i>բաղբակ</i> (baġbak) and Georgian <i>ბალბა</i> (balba). Suggested to be from a Semitic source; compare Hebrew <i>מַלּוּחַ</i> (mallū́aḥ, “saltbush”), Aramaic <i>מַלוּחָא</i> / <i>ܡܲܠܘܼܚܵܐ</i> (mallūḥā, “saltbush”), Arabic مُلَّاح (mullāḥ, “seepweed”), however these are of the Amaranthaceae family instead of the Malvaceae and have been pointed towards without acquaintance with the Armenian and Georgian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prepared with malabathrum</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾱλάβαθρον</i> (mālábathron, “malabathrum”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>softness, delicacy, effeminacy</li><li>impatience, weakness</li><li>calmness (<i>of the sea</i>)</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) disease</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) masturbation</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰλᾰκός</i> (mălăkós, “soft, effeminate”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking effeminately</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰλᾰκός</i> (mălăkós, “soft; effeminate”) +&lrm; <i>λᾰλέω</i> (lăléō, “to talk, chat”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft, tender</li><li>gentle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>light, mild</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons, modes of life</i>) soft, mild, gentle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a bad sense</i>) soft, yielding, remiss</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>faint-hearted, effeminate, cowardly</li><li>incapable of bearing pain</li><li>(<i>of music</i>) soft, effeminate</li><li>(<i>of reasoning</i>) weak, loose</li></ol></ol><li>effeminate</li><li>(<i>of sexual partners</i>) passive; receptive</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A person who is soft or gentle</li><li>A person who is morally weak; a degenerate</li><li>(<i>sexual</i>) bottom; sub</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;məlakos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥h₂ekos</i>, from <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (“soft, weak”), which Beekes separates from the "grind, rub" sense, though this is disputed.</p><p>Possibly cognate with Old Irish <i>malcad</i> (“rottenness, putrefaction”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;malskaz</i> (“soft; haughty”). Compare <i>μᾰλθᾰκός</i> (mălthăkós), <i>βλᾱ́ξ</i> (blā́x). Distantly cognate with dialectal English <i>masker</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft-shelled, crustaceous</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰλᾰκός</i> (mălăkós, “soft”) +&lrm; <i>ὄστρᾰκον</i> (óstrăkon, “potsherd”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>μᾰλᾰκός</i> (mălăkós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>μᾰλᾰκός</i> (mălăkós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of fire</i>) fierce, raging</li><li>fierce, violent, terrible</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>θαλερός</i> (thalerós) and <i>φανερός</i> (phanerós). There is no etymology, because of the uncertain meaning. Traditionally taken to be related to <i>μάλα</i> (mála, “very, exceedingly”); however, according to Bechtel it belongs to <i>μάλευρον</i> (máleuron, “wheat-meal”) and <i>μύλη</i> (múlē, “mill, hand-mill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft, tender, mellow</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) weak, feeble</li><li>gentle, mild, mellow</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>μαλακός</i> (malakós, “soft, tender”); the two synonymous adjectives may have influenced each other. Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mel-</i> (“soft”), like Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;malskaz</i> (“soft”) and Sanskrit <i>मर्धति</i> (mardhati, “to abandon, neglect”). However, Beekes considers the word Pre-Greek, because <i>&#42;ml̥dʰ-</i> would yield <i>&#42;βλαθ-</i> (&#42;blath-). Moreover, this word cannot be cognate with <i>μαλακός</i> (malakós), as the latter must go back to <i>&#42;mlh₂-ek</i> and there is no trace of the <i>h₂-</i> in the former.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flock of wool</li><li>tress, braid of hair</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin.</p><p>The connection with Lithuanian <i>mi̇̀las</i> (“coarse wool”) has been abandoned. Greppin suggests that Armenian <i>մալ</i> (mal, “cattle”) is cognate with the Greek word. The Greek word only denotes the "flock of wool" and never the animal, but this may be a later semantic development. An Indo-European form <i>&#42;mh₂l-</i> is rather improbable, however. The word may well be of substrate origin, with Beekes suggesting a borrowing from Pre-Greek, and Clackson suggesting a borrowing from Arabic, the latter which is also the prevailing theory for the Armenian cognate.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μαλλός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mallus, Cilicia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>childish</i>) mommy, mummy (<i>also as a term of address</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μάμμη</i> (mámmē) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μαμωνᾶς</i> (mamōnâs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brought up by one's grandmother</li></ol><p>From <i>μάμμη</i> (mámmē, “grandmother”) + <i>θρεπτός</i> (threptós) verbal adjective from <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to bring up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth, property, money</li><li>Mammon (wealth personified)</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>מָמוֹנָא</i> (māmōnā, “wealth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Elis</i>) kind of corn-measure</li></ol><p>According to Lewy, from the same Semitic source of <i>μνᾶ</i> (mnâ, “a hundred drachmas”) and <i>μνάσιον</i> (mnásion, “nutgrass”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madness, frenzy, enthusiasm</li><li>mad desire, compulsion</li></ol><p>From <i>μαίνομαι</i> (maínomai, “to be mad”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i>&nbsp;f (-ĭ́ā).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μανία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mania</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) towel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mantele</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) any small cloth, such as:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hand towel</li><li>sweatband</li><li>headscarf</li><li>handkerchief</li></ol></ol><p>From Latin <i>mantēlium</i>. By surface analysis, <i>μανδήλη</i> (mandḗlē, “towel”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>μανδήλιον</i> (mandḗlion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandrake (<i>Mandragora officinarum</i>)</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Maybe from Old Persian <i>&#42;merdum gija</i> (“plant of humans”), but it could be a folk-etymological adaptation of a foreign word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woollen cloak</li></ol><p>Unexplained foreign word: perhaps from Old Persian or from Illyrian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloak, cape, mantle</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>μανδήλιον</i> (mandḗlion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn</li><li>(<i>aorist</i>) to know, understand</li><li>to seek, ask, inquire</li><li>to have a habit of, be accustomed to</li><li>to notice, perceive</li><li>(<i>in questions</i>) Τί μαθών; "What were you thinking?" "Why on earth?"</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;məntʰánō</i>, <i>&#42;mantʰánō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mn̥-n-dʰ-</i>, nasal infix present of <i>&#42;men(s)dʰh₁-</i>, itself possibly a <i>&#42;dʰ</i>-extension of <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think, mind”), thus "to put one's mind".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necklace, torc, armlet, worn of gold by Persians and Gauls</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>μανδάκης</i> (mandákēs) and <i>καυνάκης</i> (kaunákēs). Usually considered to be a Gaulish word, with cognates in Latin <i>monīle</i> (“necklace, collar”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;manją</i> (“collar”). Relation with <i>μόναπος</i> (mónapos, “aurochs”) seems improbable. It is now thought that the other Indo-European languages must have acquired the word from the same source as Greek.</p><p>Rüdiger Schmitt connects Avestan <i>𐬰𐬀𐬭𐬀𐬥𐬎-𐬨𐬀𐬌𐬥𐬌</i> (zaranu-ma<sup><small>i</small></sup>ni, “with golden collar”), and Walther Hinz proposes a derivation from Old Persian <i>&#42;manyaka</i> (“necklace”), from a common Indo-Iranian root <i>&#42;mani</i> (“necklace”).</p><p>The variants <i>μάννος</i> (mánnos), <i>μόννος</i> (mónnos) point to a non-Indo-European origin: geminate -νν-, interchange α&#47;ο and presence of a suffix -ιακ-.</p><p>Still, the origin remains unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>μανιάκης</i> (maniákēs)</li></ol><p>From <i>μανιάκης</i> (maniákēs, “necklace, torc”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) causing insanity</li></ol><p>From <i>μανίᾱ</i> (maníā, “madness”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to madness, mad</li><li>frenzied, frantic</li><li>extravagant</li></ol><p>From <i>μανίᾱ</i> (maníā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violet<sup>?</sup> (genitive plural)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>μανδήλιον</i> (mandḗlion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prophecy, divination, oracle</li><li>The power to prophesy or divine</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰντεύομαι</i> (mănteúomai, “to divine, prophesy”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oracular, prophetic</li></ol><p><i>μάντις</i> (mántis) +&lrm; <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divine, prophesy</li><li>to presage, forbode</li><li>(<i>of animals</i>) to get scent of</li><li>to consult an oracle, to seek divinations</li><li>(<i>of the god</i>) to give an oracle</li><li>(later Greek,&#32;in active and here in the passive) to give an oracle</li></ol><p>From <i>μάντις</i> (mántis, “seer”) +&lrm; <i>-εύομαι</i> (-eúomai, “mediopassive of -εύω”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oracle</li></ol><p><i>μάντις</i> (mántis) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oracular, prophetic</li></ol><p><i>μάντις</i> (mántis) +&lrm; <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oracular, prophetic</li></ol><p><i>μάντις</i> (mántis) +&lrm; <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>μαντεία</i> (manteía, “sun”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gift of prophecy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>porous</li><li>few, scanty</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of things happening at intervals, not often</li></ol><li>rare, uncommon, sparse</li></ol><p>Seemingly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“small”). Compare <i>μόνος</i> (mónos). If the Proto-Hellenic form was <i>&#42;manwos</i>, then the short ᾰ would be expected in Attic and the long ᾱ in Ionic; compare <i>&#42;ksénwos</i> &gt; Attic <i>ξένος</i> (xénos) and Ionic <i>ξεῖνος</i> (xeînos).</p><p>Alternatively, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“small”), making it cognate to Gothic <i>𐌼𐌹𐌽𐍃</i> (mins, “less”), Russian <i>ме́нее</i> (méneje) and Latin <i>minuo</i> (“lessen”), <i>minus</i> (“less”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>μηνύω</i> (mēnúō)</li></ol><p>From the same source as <i>μηνύω</i> (mēnúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embers of charcoal, coaldust</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ζωμίλη</i> (zōmílē), <i>κονίλη</i> (konílē) and <i>μυστίλη</i> (mustílē). The form with <i>σμ-</i>, probably shows that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swamp sawgrass (<i>Cladium mariscus</i>)</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ἰβίσκος</i> (ibískos), <i>ἀλθίσκος</i> (althískos) and other plant names. The suffix <i>-ίσκος</i> probably points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to quench (fire); (<i>passive voice</i>) (<i>of fire</i>) to die away, go slowly out</li><li>to waste, wither; (<i>passive voice</i>) to waste away, disappear, die away, abate</li></ol><p>Uncertain. One proposed etymology maintains that the verb derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mr̥n̥-ye-ti</i>, a denominative in <i>&#42;-yeti</i> to a nasal-infixed form of the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to die”). Alternatively, the term may have emerged from <i>&#42;mr̥n̥-h₂-ye-ti</i>, a denominative to a nasal-infixed verb from <i>&#42;merh₂-</i> (“to crush”), whence also perhaps Ancient Greek <i>μάρναμαι</i> (márnamai). However, Beekes considers the reconstruction of a cluster with two neighboring vocalic sonorants to be impossible. Perhaps suffixed with <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wasting away</li><li>withered</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́ρᾰνσῐς</i> (mắrănsĭs, “dying or fading away”, noun) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>μᾰ́ρᾰνσῐς</i> (mắrănsĭs): withering, wasting away, <i>especially</i> from senescent decay</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to die, disappear”) and Ancient Greek <i>-μός</i> (-mós, action noun suffix), related to <i>μᾰραίνω</i> (măraínō, “to quench; to waste, wither”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of cat's eyes</i>) to contract the pupil when exposed to light</li></ol><p>Compound with second member as in <i>χρῡσαυγέω</i> (khrūsaugéō, “to shine like gold”), <i>σκῐαυγέω</i> (skĭaugéō, “to be purblind”) and <i>βολαυγέω</i> (bolaugéō, “to emit rays”), and with an adjectival or verbal first member. Thus, either related to <i>μαρμάρεος</i> (marmáreos, “flashing”), or to <i>μαρμαίρω</i> (marmaírō, “to flash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pearl</li><li>a type of Egyptian plant.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Indo-Iranian. According to Beekes, possibly from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;mŕ̥ga-ahri-ita-</i> (“oyster”, literally “born from the shell of a bird”), in which case ultimately related to <i>&#42;mr̥gáh</i> (“bird”).</p><p>Compare Middle Persian (mwlwʾlyt' /⁠morwārīd⁠/) (whence Persian مروارید (marvârid)), Sogdian (marγārt), Sanskrit <i>मञ्जरी</i> (mañjarī), and Avestan <i>𐬨𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬌𐬌𐬀</i> (mərəiia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) stone that takes fire when water is poured on it</li></ol><p>Unknown. The correct form is uncertain. Perhaps connected with <i>μαρμαίρω</i> (marmaírō, “to flash, sparkle, gleam”); in this case, it would properly mean "gleaming, sparkling". See also <i>μαρίλη</i> (marílē, “embers of charcoal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) catamite (a passive male sexual partner); debauchee</li><li>a term of endearment used for a male child</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) a male given name</li></ol><p>A foreign word. Used by Eupolis in his work <i>Marikas</i> to attack Hyperbolus.</p><p>Possibly from Old Persian <i>marīkā</i>, which may be from Sanskrit <i>मर्य</i> (márya, “young man, lover”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flashing, sparkling, gleaming, glistening (especially of metal)</li><li>made of marble</li></ol><p>From <i>μαρμαίρω</i> (marmaírō, “I gleam, sparkle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of marble</li></ol><p>From <i>μάρμαρος</i> (mármaros, “marble”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially of metal</i>) to flash, sparkle, glisten, gleam</li></ol><p>Apparently a reduplicated intensive yod-present verb <i>&#42;μαρ-μαρ-ι̯ω</i> (&#42;mar-mar-i̯ō) from a root form <i>&#42;μᾰρ-</i> (&#42;măr-), of uncertain ultimate origin.</p><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to sparkle, glimmer, gleam”); an analysis by Tichy suggests an onomatopoeic origin for the Greek and similar words across Indo-European, which may or may not have been inherited. Beekes thus prefers to take the word as Pre-Greek, particularly with the adducement of <i>ᾰ̓μᾰρῠ́σσω</i> (ămărŭ́ssō, “to sparkle, twinkle”), which has a prothetic vowel, as related.</p><p>The word has been compared to Sanskrit <i>मरीचि</i> (márīci, “particulate light; shining mote”), <i>मुर्मुर</i> (múrmura; múrmara (v.l.), “burning chaff; dying embers”), Latin <i>merus</i> (“pure”), and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mērijaz</i> (“famous”). Beekes rejects the connection with <i>मरीचि</i> (márīci) (and doesn't give an opinion on the other comparisons), taking the view that <i>&#42;a</i> didn't exist in Proto-Indo-European, and thus considering an inherited connection between Sanskrit <i>मर्</i> (mar) and Greek <i>&#42;μαρ-</i> (&#42;mar-) untenable. He does, however, consider a connection with <i>μᾰρῑ́λη</i> (mărī́lē, “coaldust”) and <i>μᾰρῐεύς</i> (mărĭeús, “stone that takes fire when water is poured on it”) likely.</p><p>See also <i>μάρμαρος</i> (mármaros, “marble”), which may be related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marble-mason</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́ρμᾰρος</i> (mắrmăros, “marble”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) lettuce</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;ἀμαρούλιον</i> (&#42;amaroúlion), from Latin <i>amārus</i> (“bitter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>μάρσιππος</i> (mársippos, “pouch”)</li></ol><p>From <i>μάρσιππος</i> (mársippos, “pouch”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man-eater, probably the tiger</li></ol><p>From Iranian; compare Old Persian <i>𐎶𐎼𐎫𐎡𐎹</i> (m-r-t-i-y /⁠martiya⁠/, “man, mortal”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to die”)) and Avestan <i>𐬓𐬀𐬭𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (xᵛara<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti, “to consume, eat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear witness, testify, give evidence</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with dative of person</i>) to bear witness to (i.e. in favour of)</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to testify to</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to testify that</li><li>(<i>with cognate accusative</i>) to bear false witness, give hearsay evidence</li></ol><li>(<i>astrology</i>) to be in aspect with</li></ol><p>From <i>μάρτυς</i> (mártus, “witness”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>testimony</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́ρτῠς</i> (mắrtŭs, “witness”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>testimony, evidence, proof</li><li>martyrdom</li><li>shrine of a martyr</li></ol><p>From <i>μάρτυς</i> (mártus, “witness”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown kind of fish, perhaps a barbel or a flatfish</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the suffix "-ῖνος" is a proof of the Pre-Greek origin of the word.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μαρῖνος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><b>Generally</b></li><li>a male given name known from the ancient Roman and Byzantine Periods &lsqb;ca. 2nd-8th century <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li><li>a male given name known from inscriptions and papyri written during the Hellenistic and Roman Periods, particularly in Syria and Libya. &lsqb;ca. 4th century <small>BCE</small>-4th century <small>CE</small>&rsqb;</li><li><b>Specifically</b></li><li>the name of an author of a biography of Proclus, the Neoplatonist philosopher</li><li>the name of a poet whose poetry appears in the <i>Greek Anthology</i>, an anthology of ancient Greek elegiac poetry by various authors.</li><li>the name of a man mentioned in the Roman poet Martial's <i>Epigrams</i> 10, 83, 2.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chew</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared with Latin <i>mandō</i> (“to chew”) (&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meth₂-</i>) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;munþaz</i> (“mouth”) (&lt; <i>&#42;mn̥t-</i>), assuming the original form of the Greek term's root as <i>μαθ-</i> (math-), though unresolved phonetic issues with the above lead Beekes to suspect Pre-Greek origin, particularly based on the related gloss <i>μοσσύνειν</i> (mossúnein, “to chew slowly”) suggesting a consonantal variation with <i>μαθ-</i> (math-) and <i>μασ-</i> (mas-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) mosque</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic مَسْجِد (masjid).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A chewer.</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰσά̆ομαι</i> (măsá̆omai, “to chew”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, “-er, -or”); akin to <i>μᾰστά̆ζω</i> (măstá̆zō), <i>μᾰστῐχά̆ω</i> (măstĭkhá̆ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of compound remedy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>μασάομαι</i> (masáomai, “to chew, to eat”)</li></ol><p>From <i>μάσταξ</i> (mástax, “that with which one chews, jaw”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, suffix to form a verb).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whip; to flog</li></ol><p>From <i>μάστῑξ</i> (mástīx, “whip”); stem historically <i>&#42;μᾰστῐγϳ-</i> (&#42;măstĭgj-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastic</li></ol><p>From <i>μαστιχάω</i> (mastikháō, “to chew”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prepared with mastic</li></ol><p>From <i>μαστίχη</i> (mastíkhē, “mastic”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scourge-bearing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an epithet of Ajax</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a policeman, especially at the Games</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>generally</i>) of attendants on officials</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>μάστιξ</i> (mástix, “whip, scourge”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grind the teeth</li></ol><p>Related to <i>μασάομαι</i> (masáomai), of unknown origin. Traditionally, cognate with Latin <i>mandō</i> (“to chew”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mendʰ-</i> (“to chew”), but the correspondence is irregular and the suffix is reminiscent of Pre-Greek. Note resemblance to the probably-unrelated Sanskrit <i>मस्तक</i> (mástaka, “head, skull”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like a breast</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰστός</i> (măstós, “breast”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “<t:-like>”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pimp or procuress</li></ol><p>The usual connection with <i>μαίομαι</i> (maíomai, “to touch, investigate”) is uncertain, as the variant with -φ- may point to a Pre-Greek word. Furnée further compares <i>μάτρυλλος</i> (mátrullos, “pimp”), with a variation -σ-/∅.</p><p>Possibly cognate to Latin <i>masturbor</i> (“to masturbate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) breastband</li></ol><p>From <i>μαστός</i> (mastós, “breast”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰστό(ς)</i> (măstó(s)) + -δετον, neuter of <i>δετός</i> (detós), verbal adjective of <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind, to tie, to fetter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) a breast (organ containing mammary glands)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>especially of women</i>) a human breast</li><li>(zootomy,&#32;rare) udder</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) any round, breast-shaped object:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a round hill</li><li>a round piece of wool fastened to the edge of nets</li><li>(<i>Pamphylian</i>) a breast-shaped cup</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>μαδάω</i> (madáō, “to be moist”). If so, then from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₂d-</i> (“wet, glossy, fat, well-fed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) armpit, axilla</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) axil, hollow at the base of a shoot</li><li>branch, young palm twig</li><li>(<i>geography</i>) bay, gulf</li><li>part of the prow of a ship, to which the foresail is fastened</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to that of <i>ᾰ̓γκᾰ́λη</i> (ănkắlē, “bent arm”), but further details are unclear, with Beekes preferring to take the word as Pre-Greek.</p><p>Meanwhile, Douglas Q. Adams has presented a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱsleh₂</i>, via <i>&#42;h₂eḱs-</i> (“axis, axle”), ultimately from the root <i>&#42;h₂eǵ-</i> (“to drive”), and source to Latin <i>āla</i> (“wing, armpit”), <i>axilla</i> (“little wing, armpit”) as well as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ahslō</i> (“shoulder”). He finds that a Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aksalā</i> could have given <i>&#42;askhalā</i> by simple metathesis, while the initial <i>m-</i> could be either the product of a misdivision of the definite article plus noun (e. g. in the accusative singular <i>&#42;tām aksalā</i> as well as in the respective genitive and dative dual, and genitive plural forms) or a contamination with the semantically similar <i>μάλη</i> (málē, “armpit”) and <i>μαζός</i> (mazós, “breast”). As Beekes notes, this derivation is possible but "very bold".</p><p>Immanuel Löw explains it as a Semitic borrowing, related to Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>מַתְחֲלָא</i> (maṯḥălā, “calyx surrounding the date in its early state, or spathe of a date-palm”), possibly related to Arabic حُثَالَة (ḥuṯāla, “husks of grain or of dates, refuse of any crop”), both of uncertain internal relations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idle or empty talk, babble</li></ol><p>From <i>ματαιολόγος</i> (mataiológos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā), from <i>μάταιος</i> (mátaios) + <i>λόγος</i> (lógos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idle talker</li></ol><p>From <i>μάταιος</i> (mátaios) +&lrm; <i>-λόγος</i> (-lógos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A useless art or skill.</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́ταιος</i> (mắtaios) +&lrm; <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>μᾰ́ταιος</i> (mắtaios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emptiness, purposelessness, futility</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́ταιος</i> (mắtaios, “empty”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Macedonian or Thessalian dish made of hashed meat, poultry and herbs, and served cold as a dessert</li></ol><p>Originally analyzed as a derivative of <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō, “to knead”), but according to Beekes the <i>α-</i> vocalism in the root and the presence of a double <i>-ττ-</i> rather point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(pertaining either to light or color) dark, dusky</li><li>(<i>difficult to discern, barely able to be seen</i>): dim, faint, indistinct, obscure, shadowy</li><li>(<i>not readily visible or noticeable</i>): inconspicuous, unapparent, unnoticed, unknown</li><li>(<i>difficult to understand</i>): fathomless, incomprehensible, inscrutable, obscure, uncertain, undefined, vague</li><li>(<i>tending to produce uncertainty or confusion</i>): baffling, bewildering, perplexing, puzzling</li><li>(pertaining to vision) blind, sightless; feeble, weak</li></ol><p>See <i>Μαῦρος</i> (Maûros) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of veil worn women or priests</li></ol><p>Related to Greek <i>μαφόριο</i> (mafório, “veil worn by the Virgin Mary”) and Latin <i>māfors</i> (“veil worn by women”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a case for knives</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́χαιρᾰ</i> (mắkhairă, “large knife”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case; box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gladiolus (plant of the genus <i>Gladiolus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́χαιρᾰ</i> (mắkhairă, “large knife”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λλον</i> (phŭ́llon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>μηχᾰνή</i> (mēkhănḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μαχησόμεθα</i> (makhēsómetha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fighter, warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>μᾰ́χομαι</i> (mắkhomai, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially of women</i>) lewdness, lust</li></ol><p>From <i>μάχλος</i> (mákhlos, “lewd, lustful”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain</li><li>rashly</li><li>wantonly</li></ol><p><i>μαψίδιος</i> (mapsídios) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μαῖα</i> (maîa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midwife</li><li>foster mother, nurse</li><li>good mother, dame</li></ol><p>Possibly the same root as <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μαῖα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Maia, daughter of Atlas</li></ol><p>Literally “Lady”, from <i>μαῖα</i> (maîa, “lady”), an honorific term for older women and a nursery form of <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr, “mother”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₂tēr</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (mī́gnūmĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village elder</li></ol><p>Lengthened grade of <i>μέζων</i> (mézōn) by analogy with <i>κρείττων</i> (kreíttōn) and <i>ᾰ̓μείνων</i> (ămeínōn), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;méďďōn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méǵh₂yōs</i>, from <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“great”), whence <i>μέγᾰς</i> (mégăs). By surface analysis, <i>μέγᾰς</i> (mégăs) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́ων</i> (-ī́ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which soothes</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) propitiatory offerings to the dead</li><li>darling, fondling</li><li>soothing song</li></ol><p>From <i>μειλῐ́σσω</i> (meilĭ́ssō, “to make mild, soothe”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive as one's portion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;(h)méřřomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)mer-</i> (“to assign, allot”). See also Latin <i>mereō</i>, <i>merx</i> and Hittite <i>𒈥𒀝𒍣</i> (mar-ak-zi /⁠markzi⁠/, “to divide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>month</li><li>visible part of the moon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;méns</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗh₁n̥s</i>, itself possibly from <i>&#42;meh₁-</i> (“measure”). Cognate with Latin <i>mēnsis</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mēnô</i> (English <i>moon</i>) and <i>&#42;mēnōþs</i> (English <i>month</i>), Old Armenian <i>ամիս</i> (amis), Old Irish <i>mí</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;měsęcь</i> (Russian <i>месяц</i> (mesjac, “moon, month”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>less</li><li>smaller</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“small, little”) + <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn, “comparative adjective suffix”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀕𐀄𐀍</i> (me-u-jo), <i>𐀕𐀹𐀍</i> (me-wi-jo).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminution</li><li>waning (<i>of the moon</i>)</li><li>loss (<i>of property</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μειόω</i> (meióō, “to lessen, diminish”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>great-hearted</li><li>high-minded</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>μέγας</i> (mégas) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μεγᾰ́λᾰ</i> (megắlă)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>μεγᾰ́λε</i> (megắle)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grudge, begrudge</li><li>to hold a grudge [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘against’</small>]</li><li>to care</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be envied</li></ol><p>From <i>μέγας</i> (mégas); the original meaning was perhaps “to consider too great”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) greathearted</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>μεγᾰλ-</i> (megăl-, “great”) +&lrm; <i>ἦτορ</i> (êtor, “heart”). Compare Sanskrit <i>महात्मन्</i> (mahātman, “having a great soul”), from these two roots.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write with the letter omega</li></ol><p>From <i>μέγᾰς</i> (mégăs) +&lrm; <i>γρᾰφή</i> (grăphḗ) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(art,&#32;painting) large-scale painting, grand painting</li><li>(<i>art</i>) megalography, depiction of grand subjects</li></ol><p>From <i>μέγας</i> (mégas, big, grand) +&lrm; <i>-γραφία</i> (-graphía, writing, drawing)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very frantic</li></ol><p>From <i>μέγας</i> (mégas, “big, large”) +&lrm; <i>μαίνομαι</i> (maínomai, “to be mad, angry”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>magnanimous, generous</li></ol><p>From <i>μέγας</i> (mégas, “large”) +&lrm; <i>ψῡχή</i> (psūkhḗ, “spirit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian</li></ol><p>From <i>μέδω</i> (médō, “to protect, rule over”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μετά</i> (metá) (<i>used before a rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap or rush upon</li><li>to rush after</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰ-</i> (metă-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̔́λλομαι</i> (hắllomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to alter, change (<i>transitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to alter, change (<i>intransitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to replace, exchange</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to come and stand among</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to transpose, transfer, move (<i>from one place to another</i>)</li><li>(active voice&#32;or&#32;middle voice,&#32;euphemistic) to die</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>“methodic” physicians (as opposed to rationalists and empirics)</li></ol><p>From <i>μεθοδῐκός</i> (methodĭkós): as a noun, a substantivisation of its masculine plural forms; as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>going to work by rule, methodical, systematic</li><li>(surgery,&#32;of treatment) first-aid</li><li>crafty</li></ol><p><i>μέθοδος</i> (méthodos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to intoxicate, make drunk</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to moisten, water</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to drink freely, to get drunk</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be drunk</li></ol><p>From <i>μέθυ</i> (méthu) +&lrm; <i>-σκω</i> (-skō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get drunk, to be drunk</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(figurative,&#32;of things) to be drenched</li><li>(figurative,&#32;of people) to be stupified</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>μέθυ</i> (méthu, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>-ω</i> (-ō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic verb</i>) to smile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smey-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>miror</i>, <i>comis</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्मयते</i> (smáyate), Old Norse <i>&#42;smīla</i> (English <i>smile</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smey-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>miror</i>, <i>comis</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्मयते</i> (smáyate), Old Norse <i>&#42;smīla</i> (English <i>smile</i>). Compare <i>μειδάω</i> (meidáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make mild, soothe, treat kindly</li><li>to appease, propitiate, gladden</li><li>to implore</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be soothed, subdued</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to use soothing words</li></ol><p>From <i>μείλῐχος</i> (meílĭkhos, “gentle, kind”) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō, verb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boxing glove which left the fingers bare</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>μείλῐχος</i> (meílĭkhos, “gentle, kind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet (having a pleasing disposition), mild, pleasant, gentle</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lad, stripling, young man, youngster, teenager (range of approximately ages 12-16 or 14-20, depending on locational and temporal contexts)</li></ol><p>From <i>μεῖραξ</i> (meîrax, “young lady, young woman, maiden”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lessen, diminish, subtract, temper</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to put down, degrade, to lessen in honor</li><li>to demean, disparage, to lessen by words</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to worsen, to become weaker (<i>with accusative of specification to denote the thing one has worsened in</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to fall short of</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>μείων</i> (meíōn, “lesser”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black-cloaked</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”) +&lrm; <i>χλαῖνα</i> (khlaîna, “cloak, mantle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of black cumin</li></ol><p>From <i>μελάνθιον</i> (melánthion, “black cumin”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black cumin (<i>Nigella sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Melanthius</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās, “black”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black-haired</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλᾱς</i>&nbsp;n (mélās, “black”) +&lrm; <i>θρῐ́ξ</i> (thrĭ́x, “hair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ink</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλαν</i> (mélan, “ink”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saddled seabream (<i>Oblada melanura</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care</li><li>anxiety</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>μελεδαίνω</i> (meledaínō) and <i>μέλω</i> (mélō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, attention</li><li>practice, exercise</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a back-formation from <i>μελετάω</i> (meletáō).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μελέτη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Melete, Muse of meditation, one of the original three muses of Boeotia.</li></ol><p>Cognate with <i>μελετᾶν</i> (meletân, “to study; to meditate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exercise, particularly a learning exercise</li><li>examples</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manna ash (<i>Fraxinus ornus</i>)</li><li>ashen spear</li></ol><p>Likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mélit</i>.</p><p>The old comparison with Lithuanian <i>smelùs</i> (“sand colored”) starts from the grey color of the wood. Perhaps the word is Pre-Greek, in view of the various forms in "-ινος".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honey-tongued</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey, anything sweet”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>μελίᾱ</i> (melíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drink of honey and milk offered as a libation to the powers of the netherworld</li><li>sort of mixture of water and honey</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”) +&lrm; <i>κεράννῡμι</i> (keránnūmi, “to mix, mingle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melilot, sweet clover</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”) and <i>λωτός</i> (lōtós, “lotus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>geniting, a variety of apple</li><li>apple grafted on quince</li><li>drink made from this apple</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”) +&lrm; <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foxtail millet (<i>Setaria italica</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (“to grind, crush”), tha same source of Latin <i>milium</i> (“millet”) and Lithuanian <i>málnos</i> (“millet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of liquorice</i>) with sweet boughs</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey, anything sweet”) +&lrm; <i>πτόρθος</i> (ptórthos, “young branch, sapling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of honey</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet, delicious</li></ol><p><i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “-minded”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blacken, render black</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mélh₂nyeti</i>. Surface analysis: <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plaited of rushes</li></ol><p>From <i>μελαγκρανίς</i> (melankranís, “black bog-rush”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black bog-rush (<i>Schoenus nigricans</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”) +&lrm; <i>κράνον</i> (kránon, “cornel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian blackcap (<i>Sylvia atricapilla</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”) +&lrm; <i>κορυφή</i> (koruphḗ, “top of the head, crown”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melancholy, atrabiliousness</li></ol><p>From <i>μελάγχολος</i> (melánkholos, “dipped in black bile”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), from <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”) + <i>χόλος</i> (khólos, “gall, bile”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>atrabilious, choleric</li><li>melancholic</li></ol><p>From <i>μελαγχολίᾱ</i> (melankholíā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of blackish fish</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλαινα</i> (mélaina, “black”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black horehound (<i>Ballota nigra</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās, “black”) +&lrm; <i>πρᾰ́σῐον</i> (prắsĭon, “white horehound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black-haired</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”) +&lrm; <i>πλόκᾰμος</i> (plókămos, “braid, tress”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(epithet,&#32;of Nyx) dark-bosomed</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”) +&lrm; <i>κόλπος</i> (kólpos, “bosom, lap”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black-winged</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>μέλᾱς</i> (mélās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to care for, attend to [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to attend to, study</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to practice [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to practice, exercise, rehearse</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to exercise or train (<i>someone</i>)</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to think about, imagine</li></ol><p>From <i>μελέτη</i> (melétē) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honey-sweet</li></ol><p><i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”) +&lrm; <i>ἡδύς</i> (hēdús, “sweet”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lyric, lyrical</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλος</i> (mélos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like or made of melilot</li></ol><p>From <i>μελίλωτον</i> (melílōton, “melilot”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>keeping bees</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλισσα</i> (mélissa, “bee”) +&lrm; <i>κομέω</i> (koméō, “to take care of, tend”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beekeeper</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλῐσσᾰ</i> (mélĭssă, “bee; honey”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lemon balm (<i>Melissa officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλισσα</i> (mélissa, “honey bee”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who delays, dawdler, procrastinator</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλλω</i> (méllō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of alcoholic beverage, perhaps mead, among the Scythians.</li></ol><p>Connected with <i>μέλι</i> (méli, “honey”), but this is likely a folk etymology if the word is indeed from Scythian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song, singing; chant; music, melody</li></ol><p>From <i>μελῳδός</i> (melōidós, “musical”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), from <i>μέλος</i> (mélos) "musical phrase" + <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ), contracted form of <i>ἀοιδή</i> (aoidḗ) "song".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>membrana, parchment</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>membrana</i> (“membrane; parchment”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small kind of sprat or anchovy</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek source of <i>βαμβραδών</i> (bambradṓn, “kind of sprat”) and <i>βεβράδα</i> (bebráda, “kind of smelt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of parchment</li></ol><p>From <i>μεμβράνα</i> (membrána, “parchment”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to desire eagerly</li></ol><p>From <i>μένος</i> (ménos) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) steadfast in battle</li></ol><p>From <i>μένω</i> (ménō, “to remain”) +&lrm; <i>πτόλεμος</i> (ptólemos, “battle, war”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought, care, attention</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to think, mind”), the same root of <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō, “to learn”), with a suffix <i>-ήρη</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) agreeable, satisfying</li></ol><p>From <i>μένος</i> (ménos) +&lrm; <i>εἰκώς</i> (eikṓs) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to desire eagerly</li></ol><p>From <i>μένος</i> (ménos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>μέντοι</i> (méntoi) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ν</i> (ắn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sever, cut off, divide</li><li>to assign a part, distribute</li><li>(<i>medium</i>) divide among themselves</li></ol><p>From <i>μέρος</i> (méros, “part”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part, portion, share, parcel</li><li>contribution</li><li>division, class</li></ol><p>From <i>μέρος</i> (méros) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>partial</li><li>minutely subdivided</li><li>particular, individual, special</li></ol><p>From <i>μέρος</i> (méros) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be worried, preoccupied</li></ol><p>Deverbative of <i>μέριμνα</i> (mérimna, “anxiety, concern”). Cognates include Latin <i>memor</i> and Sanskrit <i>स्मरति</i> (smarati, “to think, to recollect”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dividing, division; esp. apportionment, allocation; distribution</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>partition</li><li>share of taxation, assessment</li><li>role, part assigned, in a religious ceremony</li><li>kind of gymnastic</li></ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) division of subjects, arrangement, in writing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the art of dividing a whole into its parts</li></ol><li>(<i>in&#32;logic</i>) assignment of the elements of a contradiction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>definition</li></ol><li>(<i>in&#32;grammar</i>) classification of parts of speech (hence, concretely, class); distribution of the functions of inflexions; opposed to <i>σύγχυσις</i> (súnkhusis)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>analysis of a sentence into its component parts, parsing; compare <i>ἐπιμερισμός</i> (epimerismós)</li></ol><li>(<i>in&#32;metric</i>) division</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) quotient</li></ol><p><i>μερίζω</i> (merízō, “to divide”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós, event or process noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>separate, divided</li><li>divisible</li></ol><p><i>μερίζω</i> (merízō, “to divide”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ponder, wonder, reflect</li><li>to think over</li><li>to imagine</li></ol><p>From <i>μέρμηρα</i> (mérmēra, “care, trouble”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominative plural</i>) A kind of hawk in Lydia.</li></ol><p>Seemingly the same word as <i>Μερμνάδες</i> (Mermnádes, “Mermnad dynasty of Lydia”), a name sometimes thought to be of Carian origin, but perhaps derived from a Lydian word for hawk, as a totemic symbol for the royal family.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>javelin with a thong for throwing it by</li></ol><p>From <i>μεσο-</i> (meso-, in the middle) +&lrm; <i>ἀγκύλη</i> (ankúlē, “thong of a javelin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mediator, umpire, arbitrator</li></ol><p><i>μέσος</i> (mésos) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Membrane to which the intestines are attached, mesentery.</li></ol><p>From <i>μεσο-</i> (meso-, “in the middle of”) +&lrm; <i>ἔντερον</i> (énteron, “gut, bowel”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the middle</li><li>meanwhile</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in the middle of, amidst</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the middle part</li></ol><p>From <i>μέσος</i> (mésos, “middle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midday, noon</li><li>the South</li></ol><p>From <i>μέσον</i> (méson) +&lrm; <i>ἦμαρ</i> (êmar).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μεσημβρία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mesembria; Nesebar, Bulgaria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>between rivers</li><li>in the middle of the river</li></ol><p>From <i>μέσος</i> (mésos, “between”) +&lrm; <i>ποταμός</i> (potamós, “river”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μεσηγύ</i> (mesēgú)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full</li></ol><p>Uncertain; possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to measure”) or <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to be full”), the latter comparable to Sanskrit <i>मत्त</i> (mattá, “drunk, intoxicated”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill</li></ol><p>From <i>μεστός</i> (mestós, “full”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>maritime</i>) mast-block, cross-beam with a hole to hold the mast</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) tie-beam, central beam of a house</li></ol><p>From <i>μέσος</i> (mésos, “middle”) +&lrm; <i>-δμη</i> (-dmē, “building”), the latter a zero-grade derivative of <i>δέμω</i> (démō, “to build”). The first element is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;médʰyos</i>, while the second is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dem-</i> (“to build”). The Attic form <i>μεσόμνη</i> (mesómnē) shows assimilation of <i>-δμ-</i> (-dm-) to <i>-μν-</i> (-mn-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mesolabe, a mathematical instrument used for finding mean proportional lines</li></ol><p>From <i>μεσο-</i> (meso-, in the middle) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take, grasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μετά</i> (metá)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in the midst of, among, between, with</li><li>in common, along with, by aid of</li><li>concerning one's dealings with</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) at the same time</li></ol><li>(<i>only in poetry, usually Epic</i>) [<i>with</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>between, among</li><li>besides, over and above</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>as a movement into</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>in pursuit of</li></ol><li>of sequence or succession</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of a place</i>) after, behind</li><li>(<i>time</i>) after</li><li>(<i>worth, rank</i>) next, after</li></ol><li>after, according to</li><li>among, between</li></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>among them, with them</li><li>afterwards</li><li>thereafter</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀕𐀲</i> (me-ta); further derivation is uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meth₂</i>, with the first part from <i>&#42;me</i> (“with”), in which case cognates include Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;midi</i> and Albanian <i>mjet</i> (“middle”), <i>me</i>. See also <i>μέχρι</i> (mékhri, “until”) and <i>μέσος</i> (mésos, “middle”).</p><p>The genitive is an innovated Greek associative. The dative is from the PIE locative. The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>changeable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>huckster, retail dealer</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transposition</li><li>change of sides or opinions, amendment</li><li>power of changing</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>θέσις</i> (thésis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>afterthought, repentance</li></ol><p>From <i>μετανοέω</i> (metanoéō, “a change of mind, change of attitude”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change (process of becoming different)</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>πτῶσις</i> (ptôsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>removing, removal</li><li>shifting of blame</li><li>(<i>of place</i>) removal, migration</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) departure from life</li><li>(<i>on the stage</i>) exite of the chorus</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) transference of the seat of disease</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) change</li><li>change of political constitution</li><li>counterrevolution</li></ol><p>From <i>μεθίστημι</i> (methístēmi, “to place in another way, change”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change in the order of battle</li></ol><p>From <i>μετατάσσω</i> (metatássō, “to transpose”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to speak among or to</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>φημί</i> (phēmí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>translation</li><li>paraphrase</li></ol><p>From <i>μεταφράζω</i> (metaphrázō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upper back (of a person) (<i>also in the plural with singular meaning</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰ-</i> (metă-, “behind”) +&lrm; <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “midriff”), literally “the part behind the midriff”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μετέειπε</i> (metéeipe) used before &#47;h&#47;</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afterwards, thereafter</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>ἔπειτα</i> (épeita).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come or go among others</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go to another place</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come next, follow</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go after, go to seek, go in quest of, pursue</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seek for, aim at</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to approach with a request</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to court or woo</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to partake of; to enjoy a share of; to share in; to take part [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰ-</i> (metă-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lifted up, on high, in air</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter plural) astronomical phenomena</li></ol><p>For <i>μετήορος</i> (metḗoros), from <i>μετά</i> (metá, “beyond”) + <i>&#42;ἀορος</i> (&#42;aoros), an o-grade noun from <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō, “to lift up, raise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>concerning community or participation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>concerning action in common with another</li></ol><li>in the midst of</li><li>concerning succession</li><li>concerning pursuit</li><li>concerning letting go</li><li>after, behind</li><li>reversely</li><li>(<i>most frequent</i>) concerning change in position or condition</li></ol><p>Affixive form of the Ancient Greek preposition <i>μετᾰ́</i> (metắ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw into a different position, turn quickly or suddenly; to turn, plough (the earth); to change the course of (the river)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to turn about, change, alter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to translate</li><li>(<i>with a spoon</i>) to stir</li></ol><li>to undergo a change; to come in exchange for or instead of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to vary</li><li>to change one's course</li></ol></ol><li>to turn around, shift (a load)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to cause to be removed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to order to be paid, remit</li></ol><li>to change what is one's own</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to exchange</li></ol><li>to turn oneself, turn about</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to change one's purpose or mind; to change sides</li><li>to turn or wheel round; to turn about</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-, “concerning change of”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change, mutation, transformation</li></ol><p>From <i>μεταβάλλω</i> (metabállō, “to change”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who exchanges or barters</li><li>trafficker, huckster</li><li>interpreter, paraphraser</li></ol><p>From <i>μετάβολος</i> (metábolos, “changeable”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix for person concerned).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decantation</li><li>(Neopythagoreanism,&#32;Manichaeism) transmigration of the soul</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰγγίζω</i> (metăngízō, “decant”) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change one's mind</li><li>to repent</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give part of, give a share of [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to impart</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>have a share allotted to one</li><li>give a share in</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>λαγχάνω</i> (lankhánō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have or get a share of, to partake of [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to get possession of, lay claim to</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to eat or take (<i>food</i>)</li><li>to share (<i>someone's</i>) society [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to take after, succeed to</li><li>to take in exchange, substitute</li><li>to interchange</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to seek after [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone or something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>ditransitive</i>) to ask about [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτᾰλλον</i> (métăllon, “mine, quarry”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō); for the sense compare English <i>quarry out</i> (“extract by long, tedious searching”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miner, quarrier</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτᾰλλον</i> (métăllon, “mine, quarry”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change of purpose, regret, repentance</li></ol><p>From <i>μετάμελος</i> (metámelos) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia), from <i>μέλω</i> (mélō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to transform, disguise</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰ-</i> (metă-, “concerning change”) +&lrm; <i>μορφόω</i> (morphóō, “to form”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transformation, metamorphosis</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰμορφόω</i> (metămorphóō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vain, idle, pointless</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>migration</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>στάσις</i> (stásis) or <i>μετανίστημι</i> (metanístēmi) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove from one's country</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) to remove</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to migrate</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>ἀνίστημι</i> (anístēmi), itself from <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perceive afterwards or too late</li><li>to change one's mind or purpose</li><li>to do penance</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰ-</i> (metă-, prefix indicating change) +&lrm; <i>νοέω</i> (noéō, “perceive by the eyes, observe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>between; in between; betwixt</li><li>meanwhile; in the meantime</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) afterwards</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>between</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰ́</i> (metắ) +&lrm; <i>ξῠ́ν</i> (xŭ́n).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send for, to summon</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-, “after”) +&lrm; <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō, to send)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metaplasm (the formation of inflected forms from a non-existent lemma)</li></ol><p>From <i>μεταπλάσσω</i> (metaplássō, “to transform, remould”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós), from <i>μετά</i> (metá) + <i>πλάσσω</i> (plássō, “to form, mould”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to distinguish oneself, be distinguished among others</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>πρέπω</i> (prépō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to turn around (<i>intransitive</i>)</li><li>to show regard [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘for’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak among, address</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry over, transfer</li><li>to change, alter</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) to transfer a word to a new sense, use it in a changed sense, employ a metaphor</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-, indicating change) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “bear, carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transference</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) metaphor, trope</li></ol><p>From <i>μεταφέρω</i> (metaphérō, “to transfer”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paraphrase</li><li>to translate</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to consider after</li></ol><p>from <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>φράζω</i> (phrázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>metaphysical</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine singular) metaphysics</li></ol><p>From <i>μεταφύομαι</i> (metaphúomai, “to become by change”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to address, speak among (<i>with the dative plural</i>)</li><li>to accost (<i>with the accusative</i>)</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>φωνέω</i> (phōnéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold, handle</li><li>to manage, arrange</li><li>to practise, pursue (<i>an art or study</i>)</li><li>to handle, treat, deal with</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>χειρίζω</i> (kheirízō), itself from <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go in pursuit of</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to follow after</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go to visit</li></ol><p>Perhaps from the root of <i>κῑνέομαι</i> (kīnéomai) with the prefix <i>μετα-</i> (meta-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) soul transmigration, metempsychosis</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>ἐμψυχόω</i> (empsukhóō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) transmigration of the soul, reincarnation</li></ol><p>From <i>μετα-</i> (meta-, prefix indicating change) +&lrm; <i>ἐνσωμᾰτόω</i> (ensōmătóō, “embody, incarnate”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treatise on celestial phenomena</li></ol><p>From <i>μετέωρα</i> (metéōra, “celestial phenomena”) +&lrm; <i>-λογίᾱ</i> (-logíā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change of abode, migration</li></ol><p><i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκίᾱ</i> (oikíā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overseer and guardian of the metoeci</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτοικος</i> (métoikos, “metoecus”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>participation</li><li>(Koine,&#32;grammar) participle</li></ol><p>From <i>μετέχω</i> (metékhō, “to take part”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure, mete</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτρον</i> (métron) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>measurable</li><li>measured</li><li>of that to be measured</li></ol><p>From <i>μετρέω</i> (metréō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be moderate; to keep measure</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to moderate; to regulate; to control</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;Byzantine) to joke; to jest</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτρῐος</i> (métrĭos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>metrical</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter plural&#32;or&#32;feminine singular) prosody</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτρον</i> (métron) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moderation</li><li>the correct amount of something</li><li>middle condition</li><li>graceful proportions</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτριος</i> (métrios) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>change of name</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) metonymy</li></ol><p>From <i>μετώνυμος</i> (metṓnumos) (from <i>μετα-</i> (meta-) +&lrm; <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma)) + <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or related to the forehead</li></ol><p>From <i>μέτωπον</i> (métōpon, “forehead”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a share (<i>in something</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) metope</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰ-</i> (metă-, “in the midst of”) +&lrm; <i>ὀπή</i> (opḗ, “hole, opening”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μετόπῐσθε</i> (metópĭsthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μετόπῐσθεν</i> (metópĭsthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind; in the rear</li><li>toward the west</li><li>afterwards</li></ol><p>From <i>μετᾰ-</i> (metă-) +&lrm; <i>ὄπῐσθεν</i> (ópĭsthen).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>γέλως</i> (gélōs, “laughter”).</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smey-</i> (“to laugh”), the same root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smīlijaną</i> (“to smile”) and Latin <i>mīrus</i> (“wonderful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>more greatly</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>village elder</li></ol><p>Lengthened grade of <i>μέζων</i> (mézōn) by analogy with <i>κρείττων</i> (kreíttōn) and <i>ᾰ̓μείνων</i> (ămeínōn), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;méďďōn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méǵh₂yōs</i>, from <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“great”), whence <i>μέγᾰς</i> (mégăs). By surface analysis, <i>μέγᾰς</i> (mégăs) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́ων</i> (-ī́ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young girl, lass</li><li>(<i>in later writers</i>) boy, lad</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, we have to start from a noun, perhaps <i>&#42;μεῖρος</i> (&#42;meîros), which would agree with Sanskrit <i>मर्य</i> (marya, “young man, suitor, lover”) and Avestan <i>𐬨𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬌𐬀</i> (ma<sup><small>i</small></sup>riia, “yeoman”). A feminine <i>&#42;μεῖρα</i> (&#42;meîra) may be also considered. As a remote cognate, Lithuanian <i>mergà</i> (“girl”) has been adduced and, with different vocalism, Lithuanian <i>marti̇̀</i> (“daughter-in-law”); also, the unclear Latin <i>marītus</i> (“married”). Nowadays, Celtic words are also connected, like Welsh <i>morwyn</i> (“maiden, virgin”) and Cornish <i>moren</i> (“maiden”). The connection with Albanian <i>shemër</i> (“second wife”) is hardly convincing. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méryos</i> (“boy, girl”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μηδέ</i> (mēdé)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μηδέ</i> (mēdé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(connecting two clauses,&#32;used with the same constructions as <i>μή</i> (mḗ)) but not, and not, nor</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(doubled,&#32;μηδέ...μηδέ,&#32;opposing the two clauses of a sentence)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>joined with a single word or phrase</i>) not even</li></ol><p>From <i>μή</i> (mḗ, “not”) +&lrm; <i>δέ</i> (dé, “and, but”). Compare <i>οὐδέ</i> (oudé, “but not, and not”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>never</li></ol><p>From <i>μηδέ</i> (mēdé) +&lrm; <i>ποτε</i> (pote)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nor as yet, not as yet</li></ol><p>From <i>μηδέ</i> (mēdé, “not even”) +&lrm; <i>πω</i> (pō, “yet”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>neither of the two</li></ol><p>From <i>μηδέ</i> (mēdé) +&lrm; <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros). Compare <i>οὐδέτερος</i> (oudéteros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to Medize, ally with the Medians or adopt the Median culture or language</li></ol><p>From <i>Μῆδος</i> (Mêdos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) not even one, none at all</li></ol><p>From <i>μηδὲ</i> (mēdè) +&lrm; <i>ἁμός</i> (hamós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nowhere</li></ol><p><i>μηδαμοί</i> (mēdamoí) +&lrm; <i>οὗ</i> (hoû)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>By no means, in no way, not at all</li></ol><p>From <i>μηδαμός</i> (mēdamós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>and not one</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>not elided</i>)</li></ol><li>nobody, naught, good for naught</li><li>(neuter,&#32;adverbial) not at all, by no means</li></ol><p>From <i>μηδέ</i> (mēdé, “and not”) + <i>εἷς</i> (heîs, “one”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a goat</i>) to bleat</li><li>(<i>of a frightened or wounded animal</i>) to scream; to shriek</li><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;of a person) to scream in pain</li></ol><p>From <i>μηκή</i> (mēkḗ, “bleating”) +&lrm; <i>-άομαι</i> (-áomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μηκέτι</i> (mēkéti)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>no more, no longer, no further</li></ol><p>From <i>μή</i> (mḗ, “not”) + <i>ἔτι</i> (éti, “yet”), with <i>κ</i> (k) by analogy with <i>οὐκέτι</i> (oukéti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long, tall, taper</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆκος</i> (mêkos, “length”) +&lrm; <i>-δανός</i> (-danós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lengthen</li><li>to grow up</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆκος</i> (mêkos, “length”) +&lrm; <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opium</li><li>meconium, discharge from the bowels of new-born children</li></ol><p>From <i>μήκων</i> (mḗkōn, “poppy”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shepherd; herdsman</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “sheep, goat”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple tree</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon) +&lrm; <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quince (<i>Cydonia oblonga</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”) +&lrm; <i>Κυδώνιος</i> (Kudṓnios, “Cydonian”), from <i>Κυδωνία</i> (Kudōnía, “Cydonia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockchafer (<i>Melolontha melolontha</i>)</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple, fruit”) +&lrm; <i>ὄλλυμι</i> (óllumi, “destroy”). But rather from <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “sheep”) +&lrm; <i>ὄλυνθος</i> (ólunthos, “wild fig”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) melon, <i>Cucumis melo</i></li></ol><p>From <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple; tree fruit”) +&lrm; <i>πέπων</i> (pépōn, “cooked by the sun, ripe; mellow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shepherd, one who watches sheep</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “sheep”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepskin</li><li>any rough woolly skin</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “sheep, goat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrument for probing, especially for cleaning the ears</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>μήλη</i> (mḗlē, “probe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fringed water lily (<i>Nymphoides peltata</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μήνη</i> (mḗnē, “moon”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crescent</li><li>head covering for statues</li><li>neck-ornament</li><li>crescent-shaped line of battle</li></ol><p>From <i>μείς</i> (meís, “month; moon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;with dative) to be wroth against somebody</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;with genitive) to be wroth because of something</li></ol><p>Related to <i>μῆνῐς</i> (mênĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metallic protector to prevent injuries to the meninx in operations on the skull</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆνῐγξ</i> (mênĭnx, “membrane, meninx”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crescent-shaped, crescentic, crescentiform, crescentoid</li></ol><p><i>μείς</i> (meís, “crescent moon”, oblique stem: <i>μην-</i>, <i>mēn-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calendar</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) calendar of saints</li></ol><p>From <i>μείς</i> (meís) +&lrm; <i>λόγιον</i> (lógion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, to inform</li><li>to denounce</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mānúō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mh₂néwti</i>, a nu-infix formation to <i>&#42;(s)meh₂-</i> (“to beckon”). Alternatively, Beekes suggests that it may have originated as a denominative to an otherwise unattested noun <i>&#42;μῆνυς</i> (&#42;mênus) or <i>&#42;μηνῡ́ς</i> (&#42;mēnū́s), and in this case, still likely from <i>&#42;(s)meh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μηρία</i> (mēría)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>μῆρα</i> (mêra)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>μῆρα</i> (mêra) and <i>μηρός</i> (mērós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) femur, thighbone</li></ol><p>From <i>μηρός</i> (mērós, “thigh”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thigh</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Septuagint&#32;Koine,&#32;in the plural) loins</li><li>(<i>Septuagint&#32;Koine</i>) side</li></ol><li>a thigh-bone, a femur, used especially of thigh-bones with flesh offered in sacrifice</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;generally) leg-bones</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mḗron</i>, of uncertain ultimate origin. Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mḗms, &#42;mēms-rom</i> (“flesh”). Compare Latin <i>membrum</i> (“limb”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mimzą</i> (“flesh”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μητέρᾰ</i> (mētéră)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) counselor; all-wise one (<i>epithet of Zeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῆτις</i> (mêtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motherfucker</li></ol><p>From <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr, “mother”) +&lrm; <i>κοίτη</i> (koítē, “bed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandmother, mother's mother</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metropolitan (inhabitant of a metropolis)</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) metropolitan bishop</li></ol><p>From <i>μητρόπολις</i> (mētrópolis) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>midwife (female childbirth assistant)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>matricide (person, not the act)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepmother</li></ol><p>Also see the formation of the Hellenistic <i>πατρυιός</i> (patruiós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother-state (<i>in relation to her colonies</i>)</li><li>one's mother-city, mother country, home</li><li>capital city, or the largest city in a country</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in Egypt</i>) chief town of a district or <i>νομός</i> (nomós)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>μήτηρ</i> (mḗtēr, “mother”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city state, city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abandoned by one's mother</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maternal</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machine, mechanical device, engine</li><li>trick, deceit</li></ol><p><i>μηχᾰνᾰ́ω</i> (mēkhănắō) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build, construct</li><li>to devise, contrive</li></ol><p><i>μηχανή</i> (mēkhanḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άομαι</i> (-áomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contrive, set at work, perpetrate</li></ol><p><i>μηχᾰνή</i> (mēkhănḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contrivance, machine, device, gear</li><li>way, means</li></ol><p>Unknown. Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;megʰ-</i> (“to be able”), with cognates including Old Church Slavonic <i>мощи</i> (mošti) and Old English <i>magan</i>, <i>miht</i>, <i>mæġen</i> (English <i>may</i>, <i>might</i>, <i>main</i>). See also <i>Μάγος</i> (Mágos).</p><p>Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin, based on the infix <i>-αν-</i> (-an-), as well as formal difficulties in deriving the word's ablauting paradigm from <i>&#42;megʰ-</i>, and considers it a doublet of Ancient Greek <i>μάγγανον</i> (mánganon, “charm, block (wheelbox)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ingenious, resourceful</li><li>of or pertaining to machines, mechanical</li></ol><p>From <i>μηχανή</i> (mēkhanḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plot</li></ol><p>From <i>μηχανορράφος</i> (mēkhanorrháphos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō), from <i>μηχανή</i> (mēkhanḗ) + <i>ῥάπτω</i> (rháptō) + <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to paint</li><li>to stain, defile, pollute</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin, due to the lack of clear cognates. Alternatively, from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smey-</i> (“to smear”), and related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smītaną</i> (“to sling, hit, smear”); Beekes prefers Pre-Greek origin, but admits a possibility of the latter origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodthirsty, murderous (<i>epithet of Mars (Ares)</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῐαίνω</i> (mĭaínō, “stain”) + <i>-φόνος</i> (“-killer”), <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stained with blood, unclean, dirty, polluted</li><li>(<i>moral and religious</i>) filthy, defiled, polluted, foul</li><li>ugly</li></ol><p>From <i>μιαίνω</i> (miaínō, “stain”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Boeotian form of <i>μῑκρός</i> (mīkrós, “small”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scanty, insufficient</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write using a short vowel</li></ol><p>From <i>μικρογραφίᾱ</i> (mikrographíā) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stinginess, meanness</li><li>pettiness</li><li>hairsplitting, meticulous argument</li><li>disparagement, depreciating language</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑκρός</i> (mīkrós, “small, insignificant”) +&lrm; <i>-λογῐ́ᾱ</i> (-logĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little, small</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>amount</i>) a little of</li><li>(<i>importance</i>) petty, trivial, insignificant</li><li>(<i>time</i>) short, brief</li><li>(<i>age</i>) young</li></ol></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)meyg-, &#42;(s)mēyg-</i> (“small, thin, delicate”), thereby cognate with e.g. Old English <i>smicor</i> (“beauteous, beautiful, elegant, fair, fine, tasteful”), whence modern English <i>smicker</i>; compare also German <i>mickrig</i>. However this etymology leaves the /k/ of the Greek forms unexplained.</p><p>Beekes argues for Pre-Greek origin on the basis of variation between initial /m/ and /sm/, as well as the variant forms <i>μικός</i> (mikós) and <i>μικκός</i> (mikkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mean-spirited</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑκρός</i> (mīkrós, “small”) +&lrm; <i>ψῡχή</i> (psūkhḗ, “spirit, soul”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel for storing ruddle</li></ol><p><i>μίλτος</i> (míltos, “ruddle”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having red cheeks</li></ol><p>From <i>μίλτος</i> (míltos) +&lrm; <i>παρειά</i> (pareiá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who suffers from the falling of the eyelashes</li></ol><p>The etymology is not clear, but Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mimic</li></ol><p><i>μῖμος</i> (mîmos) +&lrm; <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) fruit of the strawberry tree</li></ol><p>A foreign word, undoubtedly from Pre-Greek, in view of the variation and the reduplication.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to mimes or actors</li><li>intended for stage performances</li></ol><p>From <i>μῖμος</i> (mîmos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>μίμνω</i> (mímnō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to stay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to remind [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small>], to put in mind</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to recall something to memory, to make famous</li></ol><li>(<i>middle and passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to call to mind, to remember [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> (<i>less commonly</i>) accusative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to remember [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>after Homer</i>) to remember [<i>with</i> participle <small>‘doing’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bear in mind, to not forget</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to remember aloud, to mention [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give heed to [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mneh₂-</i> (“to remind”), which is generally thought to be a root extension of <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to remember, think”), with inchoative suffix <i>-σκω</i> (-skō). Beekes and Rix propose that this term may have been a secondary formation, perhaps following the same pattern as terms such as <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō). Rix proposes that the original formation may be attested in <i>μνήσκεται</i> (mnḗsketai), which may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mṇh₂-sḱé-ti</i>.</p><p>Cognates include <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai, “to be mindful”), Sanskrit <i>अम्नासिषु</i> (amnāsiṣu), <i>मम्नौ</i> (mamnau, “remember, repeat in the mind”, perfect).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short-term, short-lived</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complain in a low tone, whimper, whine</li><li>to sing in a low tone, hum, warble</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>Expressive and onomatopoeic words, resembling <i>κινυρίζω</i> (kinurízō, “to lament”) and <i>κινυρός</i> (kinurós, “wailing”), with which were amalgamated in a way that can no longer be established. There is no further evidence for a variation </i>κ/μ<i>, as proposed by Furnée. Still, Pre-Greek origin is possible, according to Beekes. Latin <i>minurriō</i> (“to chirp, twitter”) shows a remarkable similarity; if it is not directly borrowed from Greek, it was at least influenced by it. According to Leumann, this verb is the oldest form of the series, while <i>μινυρός</i> (minurós) is a back-formation.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>complaining in a low tone, whining, whimpering</li><li>(<i>of young birds</i>) twittering, chirping</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>μῐνῠρῐ́ζω</i> (mĭnŭrĭ́zō, “to whimper, whine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decrease, reduce</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mi-néw-</i>, from the root <i>&#42;mey-</i> (“to decrease, diminish; small, little”). Compare Latin <i>minuō</i>, Sanskrit <i>मीनाति</i> (mīnā́ti, “to lessen, diminish, damage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing in a sweet manner (<i>of a nightingale</i>)</li><li>to sing in a low voice</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-Greek, half-barbarian</li></ol><p>From <i>μίξις</i> (míxis) +&lrm; <i>βάρβαρος</i> (bárbaros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-beast</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misanthropic</li></ol><p>From <i>μῖσος</i> (mîsos, “hatred”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́νθρωπος</i> (ắnthrōpos, “human being”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hate</li></ol><p>From <i>μῖσος</i> (mîsos, “hatred”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatred of men, misandry</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑσέω</i> (mīséō, “to hate”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misanthropy (hatred of humankind)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑσᾰ́νθρωπος</i> (mīsắnthrōpos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>odious lasciviousness</li><li>greed</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>repugnant; detestable</li><li>lascivious</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑσέω</i> (mīséō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment of wages</li><li>recompense</li></ol><p>From <i>μισθᾰποδότης</i> (misthăpodótēs) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>receiving wages or pay, serving for hire</li><li>mercenary</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mercenary</li></ol><p>From <i>μισθός</i> (misthós, “wages”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bear”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hired, to be hired</li><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) mercenary, hireling</li></ol><p>From <i>μισθόω</i> (misthóō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wages, pay, hire</li><li>recompense, reward</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;misdʰós</i> (“reward; salary”). Cognates include Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;miždʰám</i> (“reward”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mizdō</i> (“meed”), and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mьzdà</i> (“payment, pay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hire</li></ol><p>From <i>μισθός</i> (misthós) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatred of women, misogyny</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑσόγῠνος</i> (mīsógŭnos, “hating women”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>μῖσος</i> (mîsos, “hatred”) + <i>γυνή</i> (gunḗ, “woman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woman-hater</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑσόγῠνος</i> (mīsógŭnos, “hating women”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to cut up (meat)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hating women, misogynistic</li></ol><p>From <i>μῑσέω</i> (mīséō, “to hate”) +&lrm; <i>γυνή</i> (gunḗ, “woman”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be mindful, remember, come (have) in remembrance</li><li>to woo, court</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mneh₂-</i> (“to remind”), which is generally thought to be a root extension of <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to remember, mind”). The term may be more specifically derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mnéh₂-ye-tor</i>, which itself may be the source of Sanskrit <i>mnāyate</i>. However, Beekes argues that the term was probably secondarily formed after the aorist <i>μνησάμην</i> (mnēsámēn). According to the LIV, it may also be explained as a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mnéh₂-ti</i>, whence also perhaps Luwian (manāti) and Sanskrit <i>manati</i> (“remember, repeat in the mind”).</p><p>Compare <i>μαίνομαι</i> (maínomai), <i>μιμνήσκω</i> (mimnḗskō), as well as Sanskrit <i>मन्यते</i> (mányate), Sanskrit <i>म्ना</i> (mnā), Old Church Slavonic <i>мьнѣти</i> (mĭněti) and Lithuanian <i>miniu</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth almond (<i>Cyperus esculentus</i>), nutgrass</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>mnw</i> (“aromatic plant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memory, remembrance</li></ol><p>From <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai, “to be mindful of, remember”) +&lrm; <i>-μη</i> (-mē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mindful [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li><li>ever-mindful, unforgetting</li><li>having a good memory</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine plural) municipal officers, recorders</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn); compare <i>μνήμη</i> (mnḗmē, “memory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>μνηστεύω</i> (mnēsteúō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any memorial, remembrance, record of a person or thing</li><li>memorial of a dead person; a monument</li></ol><p>From <i>μνήμη</i> (mnḗmē, “memory”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon), from <i>μνᾰ́ομαι</i> (mnắomai, “I am mindful of”) +&lrm; <i>-μη</i> (-mē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to call to mind, remember, think of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>absolute</i>)</li></ol><li>to call to another's mind, mention, say</li><li>to serve as <i>μνήμων</i> (mnḗmōn)</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be remembered, had in memory</li></ol><p>From <i>μνήμων</i> (mnḗmōn, “mindful”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to remembrance or memory, memorial</li></ol><p>From <i>μνήμων</i> (mnḗmōn, “remembering”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “relative to”); <i>μνήμων</i> (mnḗmōn) from <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai, “to be mindful, remember”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“to mind; to think”)) + <i>-μων</i> (-mōn, suffix forming adjectives and agent nouns).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memory</li></ol><p>From <i>μνήμων</i> (mnḗmōn, “remembering”) +&lrm; <i>-σύνη</i> (-súnē).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μνημοσύνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Mnemosyne, mother of the Muses</li></ol><p>From <i>μνημοσύνη</i> (mnēmosúnē, “memory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reminding of misery</li></ol><p>From <i>μνήσιος</i> (mnḗsios, “of memory”) (from the root of <i>μιμνήσκω</i> (mimnḗskō, “remind”)) + <i>πῆμα</i> (pêma, “misery”) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suitor, wooer</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai, “to woo, court”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to espouse, betroth</li><li>to court, woo, seek in marriage</li><li>to sue, canvass</li></ol><p>From <i>μνηστής</i> (mnēstḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wedded</li><li>memorable</li></ol><p><i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai, “to remember/woo”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine, soft down, as of young birds</li><li>sweetmeat, confection</li></ol><p>Unknown. Rhyming word with <i>χνόος</i> (khnóos, “wool, flock; first down”). On the other hand, compare <i>μνίον</i> (mníon, “seaweed”); maybe this word is a cross of these two.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sum of money equivalent to a hundred drachmas</li><li>weight equivalent to a hundred drachmas</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>𐡌𐡍𐡄</i> (mnh), from Akkadian <i>𒈠𒉡𒌑</i> (manû).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>memorial, remembrance, record of a person or thing</li><li>mound or building in honour of the dead</li><li>memorial dedicated to a god</li></ol><p>From <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remembrance, recollection</li></ol><p>From <i>μιμνήσκω</i> (mimnḗskō, “to remember”) +&lrm; <i>-τις</i> (-tis, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to toil, labor</li><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to suffer</li></ol><p>From <i>μόγος</i> (mógos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō). The etymology of μόγος is uncertain. Possibly from earlier *σμόγος (cf. σμογερόν (smogerón)) and perhaps cognate Lithuanian <i>smargus</i> (“heavy, tiresome”), Latvian <i>smag(r)s</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) toiling, distressed, wretched</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) toilsome, grievous, laborious</li></ol><p>From <i>μογέω</i> (mogéō, “to toil, labor”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adulterer, paramour</li></ol><p>Possibly an <i>-ός</i> (-ós) agent noun of <i>ὀμείχω</i> (omeíkhō, “to urinate”), used as a vulgar expression, but this is disputed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of frankincense mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bag made of cow hide</li></ol><p>Differs from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;malhō</i> (“leather bag”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;molko-</i> (“leather pouch”), only in the velar and accent. It has therefore been assumed that the Greek word is a borrowing, perhaps from Thracian. Also Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐌻𐌲𐍃</i> (balgs, “wineskin”) and Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;bolgos</i> (“sack, bag”) have been compared, in which case the Greek word must have been phonetically influenced by <i>ἀμέλγω</i> (amélgō, “to milk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>term of abuse or depreciation applied to a beggar, of uncertain meaning</li></ol><p>Numerous doubtful proposals have been advanced. In antiquity, it has been linked to <i>μολεῖν</i> (moleîn), aorist infinitive of <i>βλώσκω</i> (blṓskō, “to go or come”), interpreting the word as "from going, and having recourse to food", which is clearly folk etymology. Neumann derives it from an unattested word <i>&#42;μολος</i> (&#42;molos), related to Sanskrit <i>मल</i> (mala, “dirt, filth”), and the root <i>&#42;gʷerh₃-</i> (“to eat, devour”). Although this is clearly better than earlier proposals, it does not explain the second element <i>-βρος</i>. According to Beekes, it is rather a Pre-Greek word. Furnée compares <i>κολόβριον</i> (kolóbrion, “young of a swine”), suggesting an interchange <i>μ/κ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dance</li><li>song</li></ol><p>From <i>μέλπω</i> (mélpō, “to celebrate with dance and song”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μαλάχη</i> (malákhē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of mallow fibre</li></ol><p>From <i>μολόχη</i> (molókhē, “mallow”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of lead, used as the sink of a fishing line, a sinker</li><li>plummet used in a mason's line</li><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) galena, lead sulphide</li><li>leadwort (<i>Plumbago europaea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μόλυβδος</i> (mólubdos, “lead”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leaden (made of lead)</li></ol><p>From <i>μόλῠβδος</i> (mólŭbdos, “lead”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stain, sully, defile</li><li>to pollute</li><li>to stain, dye</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become vile, disgrace oneself</li></ol><p>Denominative in <i>-ύνω</i> (-únō), like <i>παλύνω</i> (palúnō, “to bestrew, besprinkle”) and <i>φορύνω</i> (phorúnō, “to be mingled”), from an unattested base form. Schmidt connects it with Lithuanian <i>mulvė</i> (“mud, mire”) and the factitive verb <i>mulvinti</i> (“to cover with mire”). In order to combine the root vocalism with that of Lithuanian, he assumes <i>&#42;μαλύνω</i> (&#42;malúnō), and assimilation <i>*α>ο</i> before <i>υ</i> in the following syllable.</p><p>Numerous words for “dark, dirty color” and “dirt, defilement” are assembled under Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i>. According to Beekes only Sanskrit <i>मल</i> (mala, “dirt, filth, dust”) is of interest here, with a derivative <i>मलवत्</i> (malavat, “filthy, dirty”), which formally matches the hydronym <i>Μολόεις</i> (Molóeis). Both the hydronym and this verb may have been built on older <i>&#42;μόλος</i> (&#42;mólos, “dirt”), which would correspond to the Sanskrit word. Alternatively, Furnée compares <i>φολύνει</i> (pholúnei, “to stain, defile”), which could point to a Pre-Greek word. See also <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) blame, reproof, cause of complaint</li></ol><p>From <i>μέμφομαι</i> (mémphomai, “to blame, censure”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be alone</li></ol><p>From <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “alone”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μόναρχος</i> (mónarkhos)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “single”, “only”) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler”, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alone, solitary, isolated</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unit</li></ol><p>From <i>μόνος</i> (mónos) + <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alone, forsaken, solitary</li><li>only</li><li>unique</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mónwos</i>. For the ending, compare <i>οἶος</i> (oîos, “only, single”) from <i>&#42;óywos</i>. The etymology of the initial element is uncertain, but it may come from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“small”); compare Ancient Greek <i>μανός</i> (manós, “sparse, rare”), Armenian <i>մանր</i> (manr, “slender, small”), and possibly Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;muniwu</i> (“small fish, minnow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a staying, abiding, tarrying, stay; persistence, continuance</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>permanence</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) preservation</li></ol><li>a stopping-place, station; apartment, mansion; quarters, billets</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>monastery</li></ol><li>appearance in a court of law</li></ol><p>From <i>μένω</i> (ménō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule alone, to be a monarch</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>μόναρχος</i> (mónarkhos, “monarch”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) monarchy</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>μόναρχος</i> (mónarkhos, “monarch”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, noun-forming suffix).<br /></p><p>By surface analysis, <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “sole, only”) +&lrm; <i>-αρχία</i> (-arkhía, “rule, government”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to a monarchy; monarchic</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>μόναρχος</i> (mónarkhos, “monarch”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “-ic”, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermitage (a solitary dwelling for a single person)</li><li>monastery (a dwelling for monks)</li></ol><p>From <i>μοναστήριος</i> (monastḗrios, “alone, made alone”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, “-ium: <i>forming places</i>”), from <i>μονάζω</i> (monázō, “to be alone”), from <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “alone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of linen cloth mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solitary, single</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monk, monastic</li></ol><p>From <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “alone”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. Suffix missing.</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monogamy (state of being married to only one spouse)</li></ol><p>From <i>μονο-</i> (mono-) +&lrm; <i>γάμος</i> (gámos) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being the only member of a kin or kind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Christianity,&#32;of Christ) only-begotten of the Father</li></ol><li>only, single</li><li>unique</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) having one form for all genders</li><li>(poetry,&#32;substantive) a metrical foot of four syllables (— — — ⏑), fourth epitrite</li><li>only</li><li>in a unique manner</li></ol><p><i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “only”) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs, “of a kind”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uniformity</li></ol><p>From <i>μονοειδής</i> (monoeidḗs, “one in kind”): as a noun, a substantivisation of its neuter forms; as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one in kind, simple</li><li>unique</li></ol><p>From <i>μονο-</i> (mono-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one in kind, simple</li><li>unique</li></ol><p>From <i>μονο-</i> (mono-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fighting in single combat; dueling by hand-to-hand combat</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>close combat contender, warrior, fighter, dueler</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Ancient Rome) a gladiator</li></ol><p>From <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “alone”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “combat”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), literally “lone warrior”, from <i>μάχομαι</i> (mákhomai, “to fight, battle, contend”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μονομάχος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male surname, equivalent to Latin Monomachus or English Monomachos</li></ol><p>From <i>μονομάχος</i> (monomákhos, “gladiator”, literally “lone warrior”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>single combat</li></ol><p>From <i>μονο-</i> (mono-, “single, alone”) +&lrm; <i>μάχομαι</i> (mákhomai, “to fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having one and the same substance or essence</li></ol><p><i>μονο-</i> (mono-, “same”) +&lrm; <i>οὐσίᾱ</i> (ousíā, “substance, essence”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exclusive sale, monopoly</li></ol><p>From <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “sole, only”) +&lrm; <i>πωλέω</i> (pōléō, “to sell”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right of monopoly</li><li>an entity possessing a monopoly</li></ol><p>From <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “sole, only”) +&lrm; <i>πωλέω</i> (pōléō, “I sell”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) sameness of tone</li></ol><p><i>μονότονος</i> (monótonos, “monotonous”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>girt up alone, i. e. journeying alone, Suid., etc.</li><li>μονόζωνοι, οἱ, men with a ζώνη only, light-armed, LXX 4 Ki.5.2,al., Hsch.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having only one horn</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild ox</li></ol><p>From <i>μονο-</i> (mono-) +&lrm; <i>κέρας</i> (kéras). The long ω results from a contraction of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;-kerahos</i>, from the s-stem <i>&#42;kéras</i> plus an o-suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of one stone</li></ol><p>From <i>μονο-</i> (mono-, “mono-”) +&lrm; <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) monologue, soliloquy</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simply phrased, using simple language</li></ol><p>From <i>μονο-</i> (mono-) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) of one tone</li><li>monotonous, uniform</li><li>obstinate</li></ol><p><i>μονός</i> (monós, “one”) +&lrm; <i>τόνος</i> (tónos, “tone”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(phonetics,&#32;phonology) a monophthong</li></ol><p><i>μόνος</i> (mónos) +&lrm; <i>φθόγγος</i> (phthóngos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of one color</li></ol><p>From <i>μονο-</i> (mono-) +&lrm; <i>χρῶμα</i> (khrôma) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mulberry tree</li></ol><p>From <i>μόρον</i> (móron, “black mulberry”) +&lrm; <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>σκοτεινόν</i> (skoteinón, “dark”), <i>μέλαν</i> (mélan, “black”).</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>μορύσσω</i> (morússō, “to stain, defile”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bogeyman, hobgoblin</li></ol><p>From <i>μορμολύκη</i> (mormolúkē) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steenbras (<i>Lithognathus mormyrus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Strömberg, from <i>μορμύρω</i> (mormúrō, “to roar and boil”), if the fish meant here would properly be the "bubble-blower". The connection with <i>μύρω</i> (múrō, “to flow, trickle”) is not convincing. Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Μορμώ</i> (Mormṓ).</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μορμώ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Mormo</i>, a monstrous spectre in Greek mythology, used as a bogey to frighten children</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes thinks a Pre-Greek origin is likely. Maybe related to <i>μόρμορος</i> (mórmoros, “fear, panic”). Generally claimed to be connected with Latin <i>formido</i>, although the parallel is dubious. However, see Middle Armenian <i>մորմ</i> (morm, “tarantula”), which possibly comes from the same substrate source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mortal, liable to death</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mórtos</i> (“mortal, man”), ultimately from the root <i>&#42;mer-</i> (“to die”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>मर्त</i> (márta) and Avestan: 𐬨𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬙𐬀 (mar<sup><small>ə</small></sup>ta). See also <i>βροτός</i> (brotós, “mortal man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shape, form</li><li>appearance</li><li>outline</li><li>kind, type</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shape, form</li></ol><p>From <i>μορφή</i> (morphḗ, “form, type”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soil, defile, stain</li></ol><p>Within Greek, the verb is probably related to <i>μοριφόν</i> (moriphón, “dark, black”). Outside Greek, Slavic word for "smear" have been considered, like Russian <i>мара́ть</i> (marátʹ, “to soil, stain, dirty”). Further unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little calf</li></ol><p>From <i>μόσχος</i> (móskhos, “calf; young bull”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tampon made of rolled fiber for soaking up blood from wounds or during menstruation</li></ol><p>From <i>μοτός</i> (motós) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variety of cinnamon mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>genitively</i>): "of me", "than me", etc.</li><li>(<i>possessively</i>): "my" (possessive adjective, definite article must be present)</li><li>(<i>reflexively</i>): "of mine", "of my own" (only without the definite article, and so "a/an (<i>something</i>) of my own")</li></ol><p>enclitic genitive of <i>ἐγώ</i> (egṓ)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μονογενής</i> (monogenḗs)</li></ol><p><i>μοῦνος</i> (moûnos, “only”) +&lrm; <i>γένος</i> (génos, “offspring”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a home of music or poetry</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a choir</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) a school of art or letters</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>of Athens</li></ol></ol><li>a museum, i.e. a philosophical school and library</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>used of the Platonic Academy</li><li>used of the Musaeum at Alexandria</li></ol></ol><p>Use as a common noun of <i>Μουσεῖον</i> (Mouseîon).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μουσεῖον)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the shrine of the Muses, the seat or haunt of the Muses</li><li>the Museum (a hill at Athens)</li><li>as the title of a book</li></ol><p>From <i>Μοῦσᾰ</i> (Moûsă, “Muse”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any of the Muses' arts, especially music and lyrical poetry set to music</li><li>art</li></ol><p>By ellipsis from <i>μουσικὴ τέχνη</i> (mousikḕ tékhnē) from <i>μουσικός</i> (mousikós, “musical”) from <i>Μοῦσα</i> (Moûsa, “Muse”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for music, musical</li><li>skilled in music, musical</li><li>accomplished</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine singular) musician</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine singular) votary of the Muses, a person of letters and accomplishment, scholar</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine singular&#32;or&#32;neuter plural) music</li></ol><p>From <i>Μοῦσα</i> (Moûsa, “Muse”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for music, musical</li><li>skilled in music, musical</li><li>accomplished</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine singular) musician</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine singular) votary of the Muses, a person of letters and accomplishment, scholar</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine singular&#32;or&#32;neuter plural) music</li></ol><p>From <i>Μοῦσα</i> (Moûsa, “Muse”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) mustaceus (a kind of wedding cake made with must and baked on laurel leaves)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>mustāceus</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) diminutive of <i>μύσταξ</i> (mústax)</li></ol><p>From <i>μούσταξ</i> (moústax, medieval variant of <i>μύσταξ</i> (mústax, “upper lip; mustache”)) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) diminutive of <i>μύσταξ</i> (mústax)</li></ol><p>From <i>μούσταξ</i> (moústax, medieval variant of <i>μύσταξ</i> (mústax, “upper lip; mustache”)) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be weary with toil, be sore distressed</li><li>to work hard, labour</li></ol><p>From <i>μόχθος</i> (mókhthos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lever, bar, crowbar</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, ultimately of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouflon (<i>Ovis orientalis musimon</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a non-Indo-European, possibly Pre-Greek, substrate language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μοῖρα</i> (moîra)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part, portion</li><li>degree</li><li>political party</li><li>lot, destiny</li><li>that which is right</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;móřřā</i>, <i>o</i>-grade of the root of <i>μείρομαι</i> (meíromai, “to receive as one's portion”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă, feminine suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μοῖρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Moirae; one of the Fates in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>μοῖρα</i> (moîra, “portion, lot; destiny”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Sicily</i>) thanks, favour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;moyth₂-o-s</i>, which is from <i>&#42;meyth₂-</i> (“to exchange”), probably through an Old Latin word related to Latin <i>mūtō</i> (“to exchange, barter”). At any rate, unrelated to the group of <i>ἄμοιος</i> (ámoios, “bad”), <i>μοῖος</i> (moîos, “sad, sullen”) and <i>σμοιός</i> (smoiós, “sad, sullen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>μόνος</i> (mónos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Μοῦσᾰ</i> (Moûsă)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μοῦσα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Muse</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin. Several hypotheses exist:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>must, new wine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>mustum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trap for mice, mousetrap</li></ol><p>From <i>μῦς</i> (mûs, “mouse”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγρᾱ</i> (ágrā, “hunting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher's broom (<i>Ruscus aculeatus</i>)</li><li>wild asparagus (<i>Asparagus acutifolius</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῦς</i> (mûs, “mouse”) +&lrm; <i>ἄκανθος</i> (ákanthos, “thorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft as marrow</li></ol><p>From <i>μυελός</i> (muelós, “marrow”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to initiate (confer membership on)</li><li>to teach, instruct</li></ol><p>From <i>μῡ́ω</i> (mū́ō) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of wild asparagus</li></ol><p>From <i>μυάκανθος</i> (muákanthos, “wild asparagus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrew (mammal of the family Soricidae)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῦς</i> (mûs, “mouse”) +&lrm; <i>γᾰλέη</i> (găléē, “mustelid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Dilatation of the pupil.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) marrow</li><li>inmost part, marrow</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) best part</li></ol><p>Unclear. The word has been linked with <i>μυών</i> (muṓn, “cluster of muscles”) because both marrow and muscles are soft in texture. Chantraine's derivation from Pre-Greek is tentatively supported by Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suck</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic; originally on the idea of making <i>μῦ</i> (mû, the “m” sound), evoking the idea of the lips being together.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to say</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tell</li></ol><p><i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos, “speech”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mythic, legendary</li></ol><p>From <i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tell myths or legends</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to tell as a legend or myth</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to invent like a mythical tale</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to tell stories, converse, confabulate</li></ol><p>From <i>μῡθολόγος</i> (mūthológos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>romance, fiction, legend</li><li>story-telling</li></ol><p>From <i>μυθολογέω</i> (muthologéō, “to tell tales”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), from <i>μυθολόγος</i> (muthológos, “legend”), from <i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos, “story”) + <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teller of legends, romancer</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mythological</li><li>prating</li></ol><p>From <i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos) +&lrm; <i>-λόγος</i> (-lógos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fabulous, legendary, mythical</li></ol><p>From <i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flapping away flies</li></ol><p>From <i>μυῖα</i> (muîa, “fly”) +&lrm; <i>σοβέω</i> (sobéō, “to drive away, clear away”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moo, low (of oxen)</li><li>to resound, creak, ring, hiss (of things)</li><li>to roar</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps onomatopoeic, compare English <i>moo</i>. However, on the basis of terms such as Latin <i>mūgiō</i>, Lithuanian <i>mū̃kti</i> (“to bellow”), Old High German <i>muckazen</i>, and Sanskrit <i>múñjati</i>, the EIEC suggests that it may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mug-</i>, itself perhaps an extension of a root <i>&#42;mu-</i>, whence perhaps also Ancient Greek <i>μύζω</i> (múzō, “to moan”). Based on the denominative suffix <i>-άω</i> (-áō), the linguists Lucien van Beek and Laura Migliori suggests that it is a recent term formed within Greek. According to Beekes, the present form of the verb may have been formed innovatively after the perfect form <i>μέμῡκᾰ</i> (mémūkă) and the root aorist <i>μῠ́κον</i> (mŭ́kon), according to the model of terms such as Ancient Greek <i>βρυχάομαι</i> (brukháomai), which bears a perfect form <i>βέβρῡχα</i> (bébrūkha). Though, according to the linguist Daniel Kölligan, it is likely that these perfects were not inherited from Proto-Indo-European, but instead formed within Greek. Other middle-voice verbs have active perfects, such as <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai) and <i>γέγονα</i> (gégona).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of mushrooms</li></ol><p>From <i>μύκης</i> (múkēs, “mushroom”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) nostril</li><li>nozzle of a lamp</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) elephant's trunk</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) funnel of the cuttlefish</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) sneerer, scorner</li><li>(<i>in the abstract</i>) sarcasm, raillery</li></ol><p>From <i>μύσσομαι</i> (mússomai, “to blow the nose”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mew-k-</i> (“slip, slime”), the same root of <i>μύκης</i> (múkēs, “mushroom”), <i>μύξα</i> (múxa, “mucus, slime”) and Latin <i>mucus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn up one's nose, sneer at</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be mocked</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠκτήρ</i> (mŭktḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broom for cleaning a mill</li></ol><p>From <i>μύλη</i> (múlē, “mill”) +&lrm; <i>κόρος</i> (kóros, “besom”). Compare Latin <i>molūcrum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millhouse</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠ́λη</i> (mŭ́lē, “mill”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn, “place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mute, dumb</li></ol><p>Maybe related to Armenian <i>մունջ</i> (munǰ, “mute”) and thus from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mundyo-</i> and cognate with Ancient Greek <i>μύδος</i> (múdos), <i>μυκός</i> (mukós), <i>μυττός</i> (muttós, “dumb”), Latin <i>mutus</i> and Sanskrit <i>मूक</i> (mūka, “dumb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of mullet</li></ol><p>From <i>μύξα</i> (múxa, “mucus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>German madwort (<i>Asperugo procumbens</i>)</li><li>forget-me-not (plant of the genus <i>Myosotis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῦς</i> (mûs, “mouse”) +&lrm; <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”). Compare <i>ὠτίς</i> (ōtís, “bustard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>μῡρίᾰ</i> (mūríă)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>μῡρίε</i> (mūríe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub with ointment or unguent, anoint</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be fragrant with, smell of</li></ol><p>From <i>μύρον</i> (múron, “sweet juice or oil; muron”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tamarisk (plant of the genus <i>Tamarix</i>)</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-ίκη</i> (-íkē) as in <i>ἑλίκη</i> (helíkē) and <i>ἀδίκη</i> (adíkē) is suggestive of Pre-Greek origin. However, already Lewy saw a Semitic origin, connecting the word because of the bitterants in the plant species to Arabic مُرّ (murr, “bitter”) and Hebrew <i>מֹר</i> (mor, “bitterness, acrimony”). Recognizing an identity with Latin <i>tamarīx</i> (“tamarisk”), various combinations of Afroasiatic origin become supportable, particularly – following Schuchardt – that the Latin form contains a Berber <i>ta-</i> singulative and feminine formant. Note even further the Old Nubian (mūr, “Tamarix nilotica”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of tamarisk</li></ol><p>From <i>μυρίκη</i> (muríkē, “tamarisk”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>numberless, countless, infinite</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>poetic</i>) measureless, immense, infinite</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) endless</li><li>(adverbial,&#32;in neuter plural) much, immensely, incessantly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(adverbial,&#32;singular dative) infinitely</li></ol></ol><li>(as a definite numeral,&#32;in the plural) ten thousand, the greatest number expressed in Ancient Greek as a single word</li></ol><p>Origin uncertain. It may be related to either Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mew-</i> (“damp”) (as in 'flowing' like the countless waves of the sea) or <i>μύρμηξ</i> (múrmēx, “ant”) (like a countless swarm).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of sweet oil</li></ol><p>From <i>μύρον</i> (múron, “sweet oil”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a number of ten thousand, a myriad</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>often indefinitely, of countless numbers</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>μῡρῐ́ος</i> (mūrĭ́os, “countless, ten thousand”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with countless eyes</li></ol><p>From <i>μῡρῐ́ος</i> (mūrĭ́os, “countless, infinite”) +&lrm; <i>ὀφθᾰλμός</i> (ophthălmós, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiked watermilfoil (<i>Myriophyllum spicatum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῡρίος</i> (mūríos, “ten thousand”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ant-nest</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠ́ρμηξ</i> (mŭ́rmēx, “ant”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antlion</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠ́ρμηξ</i> (mŭ́rmēx, “ant”) +&lrm; <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrobalan (<i>Prunus cerasifera</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μύρον</i> (múron, “unguent, perfume”) +&lrm; <i>βάλανος</i> (bálanos, “acorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made from myrobalan</li></ol><p>From <i>μυροβάλανος</i> (murobálanos, “myrobalan”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myroblyte</li></ol><p>From <i>μύρον</i> (múron) +&lrm; <i>βλύζω</i> (blúzō) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A box of unguent</li></ol><p>From <i>μύρον</i> (múron, “unguent, perfume”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearing unguents</li></ol><p><i>μύρον</i> (múron) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrtle (<i>Myrtus communis</i>)</li><li>wreath or branch of myrtle</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠ́ρτος</i> (mŭ́rtos, “myrtle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of myrtle</li></ol><p>From <i>μυρσίνη</i> (mursínē, “myrtle”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of precious stone</li><li>myrtle spurge (<i>Euphorbia myrsinites</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>μυρσίνη</i> (mursínē, “myrtle”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fragrance</li><li>unguent</li><li>smell (<i>of food</i>)</li><li>spice</li><li>sweet smell, things that smell nicely (<i>like flowers</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) something very beautiful (<i>of a woman</i>)</li></ol><p>From the Hellenistic adjective <i>μυρώδης</i> (murṓdēs) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía). See <i>μῠ́ρον</i> (mŭ́ron) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anointed with unguent</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠ́ρον</i> (mŭ́ron, “unguent”) +&lrm; <i>χρῑστός</i> (khrīstós, “anointed”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foul, dirty, loathsome, abominable</li><li>defiled, polluted (of persons)</li></ol><p>From <i>μύσος</i> (músos) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revealed secret</li><li>mystery, secret</li><li>object used in mystery religions</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) matters of science which required teaching</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠ́στης</i> (mŭ́stēs, “initiated one”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), from <i>μυέω</i> (muéō, “to initiate into mysteries”), from <i>μῡ́ω</i> (mū́ō, “to shut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of bread scooped out as a spoon (for soup or gravy)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠζᾰ́ω</i> (mŭzắō), in a formation like <i>ζωμῑ́λη</i> (zōmī́lē), <i>κονῑ́λη</i> (konī́lē) and <i>μαρῑ́λη</i> (marī́lē); Beekes suggests Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix <i>-ῑλη</i> (-īlē), and that both alternative forms are due to confusion with <i>μιστῠ́λλω</i> (mistŭ́llō, “to cut up meat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mystagogue, one who prepares an initiate for entry into a mystery cult</li></ol><p>From <i>μύστης</i> (mústēs, “initiate”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγός</i> (agōgós, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>secret, mystic</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠ́στης</i> (mŭ́stēs, “one who has been initiated”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spoon case</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠ́στρον</i> (mŭ́stron, “spoon”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case; box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moaning, groaning</li></ol><p>From <i>μύζω</i> (múzō, “to mutter, moan”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the inmost or furthest part of a place</li><li>recess, nook</li></ol><p>The connection with Old Armenian <i>մխեմ</i> (mxem, “to insert; to immerse”) is obsolete and semantically doubtful. The group of Old Norse <i>smjúga</i> (“to slip in”) and Middle High German <i>smiegen</i> (“to nestle”) may theoretically derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smewgʰ-</i> like Greek, but the Germanic words may also go back to <i>&#42;(s)mewk-</i> and correspond to Lithuanian <i>smùkti</i> (“to glide away”). Furnée thinks that this noun is Pre-Greek, but without further arguments. One argument could be the gloss <i>βύσσαλος</i> (bússalos, “depth, abyss”), if it really belongs here; another gloss would be <i>μοχοῖ</i> (mokhoî, “within, inside”), with a vocalic interchange.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in the farthest corner of</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shortsightedness</li><li>mousehole</li></ol><p>From <i>μύωψ</i> (múōps, “squinting, short-sighted”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p><p>From <i>μῦς</i> (mûs, “mouse”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) a cluster of muscles; particularly the point where several muscles meet</li></ol><p>From <i>μῦς</i> (mûs) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fly (the insect)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mus</i>, whence also Latin <i>musca</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>моуха</i> (muxa), Old Armenian <i>մուն</i> (mun), Albanian <i>mizë</i>, Latvian <i>muša</i>, Old English <i>mycg</i> (English <i>midge</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mimic, and so, ridicule, mock</li></ol><p>The following is paraphrased from the entry by Beekes in his <i>Etymological Dictionary of Greek</i>:</p><p><i>Beekes finds obvious to take this verb as an intensive deverbative, suggesting that the rare forms <i>μωκός</i> (mōkós) and <i>μῶκος</i> (môkos) are actually back-formations. He compares intensives like <i>μηκάομαι</i> (mēkáomai) or <i>μῡκάομαι</i> (mūkáomai), but further details are obscure. According to an anonymous source, the word was originally used for a camel, a possible proof of an onomatopoeic origin. Furnée compares <i>μώχεται</i> (mṓkhetai, “to be envious, jealous”), with a different velar, and concludes that the word is Pre-Greek.</i></p><p>See also <i>μῶμος</i> (mômos, “blame, reproach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to content, bring an action at law (in a courtroom)</li></ol><p>Disputed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>To find fault with, blame, criticize</li></ol><p>From <i>μῶμος</i> (mômos, “blame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be silly, foolish, play the fool</li><li>(<i>causative</i>) to make foolish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become foolish, insipid</li></ol><li>to make flat and tasteless</li></ol><p>From <i>μωρός</i> (mōrós) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foolishness; folly; lack of foresight</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow, sluggish</li><li>slow, dull, foolish, stupid</li><li>insipid, flavorless, flat</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Traditionally compared with Sanskrit <i>मूर</i> (mūrá, “dull, stupid, foolish”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mowHrós</i>, <i>&#42;muHrós</i> (“dull, stupid”), with ablaut ō(u) : ū. However, this ablaut is now considered unacceptable. Words in the semantic category that the Greek belongs to ("slow, foolish") often involve irregular changes and lexical crosses, which makes analysis difficult.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow, sluggish</li><li>slow, dull, foolish, stupid</li><li>insipid, flavorless, flat</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Traditionally compared with Sanskrit <i>मूर</i> (mūrá, “dull, stupid, foolish”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mowHrós</i>, <i>&#42;muHrós</i> (“dull, stupid”), with ablaut ō(u) : ū. However, this ablaut is now considered unacceptable. Words in the semantic category that the Greek belongs to ("slow, foolish") often involve irregular changes and lexical crosses, which makes analysis difficult.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>foolishly</li></ol><p>From <i>μωρός</i> (mōrós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with difficulty, hardly, scarcely</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>μογέω</i> (mogéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>modius, a dry measure equivalent to a sixth of a medimnus</li><li>vessel of this capacity</li><li>measure of length, equivalent to 200 <i>ὀργυιαί</i> (orguiaí)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>modius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the din of battle</li></ol><p>No cognates outside Greek. Has been compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;metàti</i> (“to throw”) and with Sanskrit <i>मथति</i> (mathati, “to stir”) but this seems unprobable. Perhaps from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) presumptuous or impudent person</li><li>(<i>Laconian</i>) children of helots, brought up as foster-brothers of the young Spartans</li><li>a type of lewd dance</li><li>the name of a melody for the flute</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from <i>μόθος</i> (móthos, “din of battle”). However, Lewy proposes a Semitic origin, comparing the word with Hebrew <i>עתק</i> (ataq, “to set free”) and suggesting that <i>μόθαξ</i> (móthax) was the original word.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the din of battle</li></ol><p>No cognates outside Greek. Has been compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;metàti</i> (“to throw”) and with Sanskrit <i>मथति</i> (mathati, “to stir”) but this seems unprobable. Perhaps from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of ox hide</li></ol><p>From <i>μολγός</i> (molgós, “bag made of ox hide”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only just</li><li>hardly, scarcely, barely</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Solmsen compares μόλις to <i>μῶλος</i> (môlos, “turmoil, fight”), as well as to <i>μέλλω</i> (méllō, “to be about to do, to hesitate”), but Beekes considers the former comparison phonetically problematic due to the root vocalism, and the latter semantically uncompelling.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of serpent</li></ol><p>No etymology. Given the ending, perhaps it is from Pre-Greek; compare <i>ἴνδουρος</i> (índouros), <i>παλίουρος</i> (palíouros), <i>σκίουρος</i> (skíouros) and <i>τάγχουρος</i> (tánkhouros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἐγκρυφίας</i> (enkruphías, “loaf baked in the ashes”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix <i>-φθ-</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lead (metal)</li><li>graphite</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀗𐀪𐀺𐀈</i> (mo-ri-wo-do), from an Anatolian word cognate with Lydian <i>𐤪𐤠𐤭𐤦𐤥𐤣𐤠</i> (mariwda, “dark”), from Proto-Indo-European *<i>morgʷ-iyo-</i>, from a root <i>&#42;mergʷ-</i> (“dark”), whence also English <i>murky</i> and Russian <i>мрак</i> (mrak, “darkness”). Probably not cognate with Latin <i>plumbum</i> (“lead”), despite superficial similarity.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European bison</li></ol><p>From Paeonian, itself probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king, monarch</li><li>commander, general</li><li>dictator</li><li>high magistrate</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “single, only”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler, leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>single flute</li><li>player on the single flute</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>played on a single flute</li></ol><p>From <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “alone, solitary, single”) +&lrm; <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>only</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “only”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alone, forsaken, solitary</li><li>only</li><li>unique</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mónwos</i>. For the ending, compare <i>οἶος</i> (oîos, “only, single”) from <i>&#42;óywos</i>. The etymology of the initial element is uncertain, but it may come from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“small”); compare Ancient Greek <i>μανός</i> (manós, “sparse, rare”), Armenian <i>մանր</i> (manr, “slender, small”), and possibly Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;muniwu</i> (“small fish, minnow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alone, forsaken, solitary</li><li>only</li><li>unique</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mónwos</i>. For the ending, compare <i>οἶος</i> (oîos, “only, single”) from <i>&#42;óywos</i>. The etymology of the initial element is uncertain, but it may come from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;men-</i> (“small”); compare Ancient Greek <i>μανός</i> (manós, “sparse, rare”), Armenian <i>մանր</i> (manr, “slender, small”), and possibly Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;muniwu</i> (“small fish, minnow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Aeolic</i>) alternative form of <i>μόναπος</i> (mónapos, “European bison”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mora (a division of the Spartan army, varying in strength, at first six in number)</li></ol><p>From <i>μείρομαι</i> (meíromai, “receive one's share”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wicker basket of a cart, used for carrying straw and chaff</li><li>leathern vessel</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>Ἀμοργός</i> (Amorgós) and <i>ὀμόργνυμι</i> (omórgnumi) is semantically uncompelling. According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mulberry coloured</li></ol><p>From <i>μόρον</i> (móron, “black mulberry”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece, portion</li><li>constituent part, member</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>member <i>or</i> part of the body</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) genitals</li><li>member of a council</li></ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>part of speech</li><li>particle</li><li>prefix <i>or</i> suffix</li></ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fraction with 1 for numerator</li><li>fraction</li><li>denominator of a fraction</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>μόρος</i> (móros, “fate, destiny”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, <i>diminutive</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black mulberry</li><li>blackberry</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mórom</i> (“mulberry, blackberry”), and cognate with Old Armenian <i>մոր</i> (mor, “blackberry”), as well as likely Middle Irish <i>merenn</i> (“mulberry”), Welsh <i>merwydden</i>. However, Beekes suspects the Armenian to be a loan from Greek (and does not mention the Celtic forms), and leaves the origin open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate, destiny</li><li>doom, death</li><li>corpse</li><li>a measure of land in Locris</li></ol><p>From <i>o</i>-grade of the root of <i>μείρομαι</i> (meíromai, “to receive as one's portion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of pipeclay used to bleach clothes</li></ol><p>The variation "χθ/ξ" is typical of Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of an Oriental material, perhaps agate</li><li>cups and vases made with this material</li><li>glass imitation</li></ol><p>Probably of Iranian origin; compare Persian مورش (mureš, “small shells or glass beads”). See modern مروارید (“pearl”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of <i>μόρρῐᾰ</i> (mórrhĭă)</li></ol><p>From <i>μόρρῐᾰ</i> (mórrhĭă, “kind of material, perhaps agate”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of eagle</li></ol><p>Like the rhyming word <i>ὀρφνός</i> (orphnós, “dark”), this word is usually explained from a meaning "dark-colored". Similar words are <i>μοριφόν</i> (moriphón, “dark, black”), <i>μορύσσω</i> (morússō, “to soil, defile”), as well as <i>μόρον</i> (móron, “mulberry”). Connection with <i>μορφή</i> (morphḗ, “form”) cannot be proven. The word has also been connected to Sanskrit <i>मृग</i> (mṛga, “deer, antelope”), with an influence from <i>νηττοφόνος</i> (nēttophónos, “kind of eagle”); doubtful in view of the haplology that has to be assumed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaping</li><li>form, semblance</li></ol><p>From <i>μορφόω</i> (morphóō, “to form”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden house or tower</li><li>palisade, wooden fence</li><li>slip for shipbuilding</li></ol><p>Stated to be a loan from Proto-Iranian, comparing Ossetian <i>мӕсыг</i> (mæsyg, “tower”). However, according to Brust, this must be regarded as uncertain. Furnée follows a proposal by Kretschmer, that the word is from a non-Indo-European substrate source.</p><p>However, compare Old Church Slavonic <i>сꙑнъ</i> (synŭ, “tower”) which is considered to be borrowed from Turkic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cinnamon</li></ol><p>Has been compared with the personal name <i>Μόσυλοι</i> (Mósuloi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of calf skin</li></ol><p>From <i>μόσχος</i> (móskhos, “calf”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young shoot or twig</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) leafstalk; petiole</li><li>calf; young bull</li><li>any young animal</li><li>musk</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, related to Old Armenian <i>մոզի</i> (mozi, “calf”) and maybe from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mosǵʰ-o-</i> (“young of an animal; young shoot”). The appurtenance of Lithuanian <i>mãzgas</i> (“bud of a tree; knot”) to the Greco-Armenian terms is less certain and straightforward.</p><p>From Middle Persian (mwšk' /⁠*mušk⁠/), itself from Sanskrit <i>मु॒ष्क</i> (muṣká, “testicle”); compare also <i>μύσχον</i> (múskhon, “genitalia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toil, trouble, hardship, distress, exertion</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ἄχθος</i> (ákhthos), <i>βρόχθος</i> (brókhthos) and <i>ὄχθος</i> (ókhthos). According to Beekes, the variation "χθ/γ" must be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: stain on the himations</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μόψος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Mopsus, a legendary seer and rival of Calchas</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀗𐀦𐀰</i> (mo-qo-so /⁠Mókʷsos⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea-mussel</li></ol><p>Possibly cognate with Latin <i>mūrex</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metal or iron mass roasted in fire</li><li>anvil of stone or metal</li><li>red hot iron in the hands, as an ordeal</li><li>stone thrown out by a volcano, lapillus</li><li>red hot stone throw in a liquid to vaporize it</li></ol><p>If related to <i>μυδάω</i> (mudáō, “to be damp, dripping”), which is formally proximate, this word originally denoted the molten, flowing metal mass as opposed to the hard iron. However, the technical meaning and the variant <i>σμύδρος</i> (smúdros) rather suggest a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mushroom or other fungus</li><li>any mushroom-shaped object, hence:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>chape or cap at the end of a scabbard</li><li>penis</li><li>fleshy excrescence, such as forms on wounds</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) callus or knot caused by disease (especially on olive trees)</li><li>stump of an olive tree cut down</li><li>snuff of a lamp-wick</li></ol></ol><p>Traditionally taken to be a formation in -ητ- from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mew-k-</i> (“slip, slime”), the same root of Latin <i>mūcus</i> (“snivel”); for the development of meaning, compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glìva</i> (“fungus”) beside Lithuanian <i>gléivės</i> (“slime”), from <i>&#42;gleh₁y-</i> (“to be smeary”).</p><p>However, Furnee, rejecting a connection to <i>μύσσομαι</i> (mússomai, “to snort, blow one's nose”) (from the same “slime” root), on an assumed basic meaning “prominence, extremity” behind "stump of an olive tree", by comparison with <i>μύσκλοι</i> (múskloi, “stalks of dried up fig trees”) claims a Pre-Greek origin for the word. Beekes endorses Furnée’s claim, noting the semantic gap between mushrooms and slime (mushrooms, though often associated with growing in areas that do feature slimy organisms, are not themselves usually slimy).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lewd, lustful person</li><li>fold on the donkey's neck</li></ol><p>This word has been connected to <i>μύκλα</i> (múkla, “black stripe on the neck and feet of the ass”), <i>μυχλός</i> (mukhlós, “Phoenician word for stallion ass”) and Latin <i>mūlus</i> (“mule”). As the breeding of mules originates from Pontic Asia Minor, Frisk remarks that we are probably dealing with a Wanderwort. However, Beekes does not agree with this and claims that the variants prove a Pre-Greek origin, while Furnée thinks that this word must be separated from the others.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) heap</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a part of the ear</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A mill, a hand-mill turned by women.</li><li>A nether millstone.</li><li>A kneepan.</li><li>A hard formation in a woman's womb.</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Molars.</li><li>synonym of <i>μῶλῠ</i> (môlŭ)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mólā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (“to grind, crush”). Doublet of <i>μῠ́λος</i> (mŭ́los). Cognate with Latin <i>mola</i>, English <i>meal</i>, <i>maelstrom</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of millstone</li></ol><p>From <i>μύλη</i> (múlē, “millstone”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of fish brought salted from the Black Sea</li><li>A similar fish found in the Danube</li></ol><p>The etymology remains unknown, as the connection with <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “dark in color”) is a pure hypothesis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of beetle or cockroach which breeds in mills</li></ol><p>From <i>μύλη</i> (múlē, “mill, hand-mill”) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house, dwelling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excuse, pretence, pretext</li></ol><p>Unexplained. The former connection with <i>ἀμύνω</i> (amúnō, “to defend”) is impossible because the prothetic vowel developed from a laryngeal, provided that the latter word is Indo-European.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mucus, discharge from the nose</li><li>slime of certain fish and snails</li><li>sebesten (<i>Cordia myxa</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mew-k-</i> (“slip, slime”). Cognates include <i>μύσσομαι</i> (mússomai, “to blow the nose”), Latin <i>mucus</i>, Old Norse <i>mjúkr</i> (“soft, meek”), Latvian <i>mulks</i> (“marshy”). The traditionally compared <i>μύκης</i> (múkēs, “mushroom”) is likely not related.</p><p>The origin is unknown. Compare <i>μυσκλον</i> (musklon, “sebesten”), Armenian <i>մամուխ</i> (mamux, “sloe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of grey mullet (<i>Liza saliens</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>μῠ́ξᾰ</i> (mŭ́xă, “mucus”). Compare also <i>μῠξῖνος</i> (mŭxînos, “kind of mullet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moray eel</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected with <i>σμύρις</i> (smúris, “emery powder”) and with words for 'grease, fat', like Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smerwą</i> (“grease”), which would fit the eel as a fat creature. However, this does not explain the long "ῡ". According to Beekes, names of fish are often Pre-Greek and the suffix <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină) is well-known in such words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ten thousand, myriad</li></ol><p>From <i>μυριάς</i> (muriás, “number of 10,000”), from <i>μυρίος</i> (muríos, “numberless, countless, infinite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Syracuse</i>) mute, dumb</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it is a loan from Latin <i>murcus</i> (“mutilated”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ant</li><li>a mythical gold-digging animal sometimes identified with ants</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;morwi-</i> (“ant”). Related to Old Armenian <i>մրջիւն</i> (mrǰiwn) and Latin <i>formīca</i> and perhaps Sanskrit <i>वल्मी</i> (valmī). Despite the suffix <i>-ηκ-</i> (-ēk-), which typically indicates origin from Pre-Greek (cf. Beekes 2014), Beekes considers this to be a genuinely Indo-European term which underwent various tabooistic deformations, comparing Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥mis</i> (“worm”) (see <i>ῥόμος</i> (rhómos, “wood-worm”)), and notes that similar forms with a velar suffix are found in other Indo-European cognates: Latin <i>formīca</i> (“ant”), Sanskrit <i>वल्मीक</i> (valmīka, “ant-hill”). Compare <i>ὅρμικας</i>&nbsp;pl (hórmikas, “ants”). <i>βύρμαξ</i> (búrmax) ~ <i>βόρμαξ</i> (bórmax) may be related as well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any sweet juice distilled from plants and used for unguents or perfumes</li><li>unguent, sweet oil, perfume, balsam</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>chrism; muron</li><li>place where unguents were sold, perfumery</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) anything graceful, charming, lovely</li></ol></ol><p>A Kulturwort which spread to various other languages, of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrrh</li></ol><p>From a Semitic source akin to Arabic مُرّ (murr, “myrrh”, literally “bitterness”), Hebrew <i>מור &#47; מֹר</i> (mōr, “myrrh”, literally “bitterness”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μύρρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Myrrha, the mother of Adonis</li><li>Myrrha, the original Aeolic Greek name for the city of Smyrna</li><li>Aeolic form of <i>Σμύρνᾱ</i> (Smúrnā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket with two handles</li></ol><p>The word has been compared with Etruscan <i>𐌌𐌖𐌓𐌑</i> (murś, “urn”). Furnée accepts this and further compares <i>βυρρός</i> (burrhós, “cantharus”). Probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of myrtle</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠ́ρτος</i> (mŭ́rtos, “myrtle”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrtle (<i>Myrtus communis</i>)</li><li>twig or spray of myrtle</li></ol><p>Akin to Avestan <i>𐬨𐬎𐬱𐬙𐬆𐬨𐬆𐬱𐬀</i> (muštəməša), Middle Persian <small>[Book Pahlavi needed]</small> (mwlt'), Parthian <i>𐫖𐫇𐫇𐫡𐫅</i> (mwwrd), Persian مورد (murd), Old Armenian <i>մուրտ</i> (murt), Old Georgian <i>მურტი</i> (murṭi, “myrtle”). Due to the variation μῠρτ-&#47;μῠρσ-, Beekes opts for a Pre-Greek borrowing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: stalks of dried up fig trees.</li></ol><p>Unknown. Compare <i>μύκης</i> (múkēs), <i>μόσχος</i> (móskhos), <i>μίσχος</i> (mískhos), <i>μασχάλη</i> (maskhálē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>μίασμα</i> (míasma, “stain, defilement”), <i>κῆδος</i> (kêdos, “care; anxiety, grief”).</li></ol><p>Beekes connects the word with <i>ἀμυσχρός</i> (amuskhrós, “undefiled, pure”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin. The gloss is also been connected to <i>μύσος</i> (músos, “pollution, defilement”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncleanness, defilement, pollution</li></ol><p>The word recalls <i>μῖσος</i> (mîsos, “hatred”) but remains without certain etymology. Pokorny compares Irish <i>mosach</i> (“shaggy, bristly”), Low German <i>mussig</i> (“dirty”) and Russian <i>му́слить</i> (múslitʹ, “to suck, beslaver”). The group has been compared with the root of <i>μαδάω</i> (madáō, “to be moist”), but this remains hypothetical.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow one's nose</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;muťťomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;muk-yé-tor</i>, a present tense of <i>&#42;(s)mewk-</i> (“to let loose, release”); compare Sanskrit <i>मुच्यते</i> (mucyate, “to be set free, released”) and more distantly <i>ēmungō</i> (“to wipe or blow the nose”) and Irish <i>smuga</i> (“mucus, snot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upper lip or moustache</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, both the variants listed in the alternative forms section suggest a Pre-Greek origin. Compare Ashokan Prakrit <i>&#42;𑀩𑀽𑀲𑁆𑀢</i> (&#42;būsta, “lip”), with descendants in Northwestern Indo-Aryan and Dardic, which may suggest Wanderwort status, if indeed related to the Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has been initiated; an initiate</li></ol><p>From <i>μυέω</i> (muéō, “to initiate into mysteries”), from <i>μῡ́ω</i> (mū́ō, “to shut”), +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs). The sense development was probably "to shut" > "(one who) shuts one's eyes" > "one who is or is to be initiated (in the Eleusinian mysteries)".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has been initiated, initiate</li></ol><p>From <i>μυέω</i> (muéō, “to initiate into mysteries”), from <i>μῡ́ω</i> (mū́ō, “to shut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoon</li><li>spoonful (contents of a spoon)</li><li>spoonful (a unit of measure)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῠζᾰ́ω</i> (mŭzắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that part of molluscs that answers to the liver</li><li>snout</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Bailly derives the word from <i>μῡ́ω</i> (mū́ō, “to close, shut”), while Babiniotis suspects a connection to <i>μῠ́σσομαι</i> (mŭ́ssomai, “to blow one's nose”). Alternatively, the word could perhaps be from Pre-Greek and related to <i>μῠ́στᾰξ</i> (mŭ́stăx, “upper lip”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to close, to shut</li><li>to be shut, (esp. of the eyes)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mewH-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;mewH-</i> (“to shut”); cognate with Latin <i>mutus</i>, Sanskrit <i>मूक</i> (mū́ka, “mute”). Alternatively, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mews-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;mews-</i> (“to shut”), and cognate with Palaic (muš-, “to satiate oneself”), though this is semantically unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>closing the eyes</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) shortsighted</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a closing or contracting of the eyes</li><li>a short-sighted person</li><li>a gadfly or horsefly</li><li>a goad or spur (something which motivates)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a stimulant</li></ol><p>From <i>μύω</i> (múō) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps).</p><p>Possibly originally from <i>μυῖᾰ</i> (muîă) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark of a stripe, weal, bruise</li></ol><p>Beekes dismisses a derivation from <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “face”) and prefers to derive it from Pre-Greek, comparing other words ending in "-ωψ", like <i>κύνωψ</i> (kúnōps) and <i>κώνωψ</i> (kṓnōps).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μῶνῠξ</i> (mônŭx, “having an uncloven hoof”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mandrake (<i>Mandragora officinarum</i>)</li><li>ashwagandha (<i>Withania somnifera</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>μωρῶς</i> (mōrôs, “foolishly”)</li></ol><p>From <i>μῶρος</i> (môros) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>God forbid, certainly not, by no means, not at all, no way, never, absolutely not</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley-bread or cake; lump, mass</li></ol><p>From <i>μαγῆμαι</i> (magêmai) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia), the former component from <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō, “to knead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>μῆκος</i> (mêkos)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mā́kos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₂ḱos</i>, from <i>&#42;meh₂ḱ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>comparative</i>) more, more strongly, rather</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>strengthened</i>)</li><li>(<i>joined to a second comparative</i>)</li><li>(in the phrase <i>μᾶλλον δέ</i> (mâllon dé)) much more, rather</li><li>(in the phrase <i>οὐδὲν μᾶλλον</i> (oudèn mâllon))</li></ol></ol><p>Comparative form of <i>μάλα</i> (mála, “very”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mel-</i> (“strong, big”). Cognates include Latin <i>melior</i> (“better”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Aeolic form of <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic and Doric form of <i>μῆνις</i> (mênis, “rage; wrath (especially of the gods)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;plural only) counsel, plan, art, prudence, cunning</li><li>(Epic,&#32;plural only) male genitals</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mḗdeha</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₁desh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;med-</i> (“to measure, give advice”), whence also <i>μέδω</i> (médō), <i>μέδομαι</i> (médomai). Cognates include Armenian <i>միտ</i> (mit).</p><p>Uncertain. Has been connected with etymology 1 (compare Old High German <i>gimaht</i> for a similar double meaning), Latin <i>mentula</i>, and with <i>μαδάω</i> (madáō) and Middle Irish <i>mess</i>. Could also be Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μῆδος)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Media; a Mede or Median</li></ol><p>Old Iranian name, from Old Persian <i>𐎶𐎠𐎭</i> (m-a-d /⁠Māda-⁠/), but of uncertain meaning; possibly from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;mádyah</i> (“in the middle”). Folk etymology connected it to <i>Μήδεια</i> (Mḗdeia), granddaughter of Helios.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>length (spatial measurement)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>height</li><li>any linear measurement</li></ol><li>length (of time)</li><li>greatness, magnitude</li><li>longitude</li><li>(<i>prosody</i>) length</li><li>first line of a phalanx</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mā́kos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₂ḱos</i>. Cognates include Avestan <i>𐬨𐬀𐬯𐬀𐬵</i> (masah), Latin <i>maciēs</i>, and Hittite (mak-l-ant-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple</li><li>any fruit from a tree</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;in the plural) a woman's breast</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cheeks</li><li>sheep</li><li>goat</li><li>beast</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Compare Hittite <i>𒈠𒀀𒄴𒆷𒀸</i> (māḫlaš, “grapevine”), Hittite <i>𒊭𒈠𒇻</i> (šamalu-, “apple”), Hittite <i>𒊭𒄠𒇻</i> (šamlu-, “apple”) and Proto-Kartvelian <i>&#42;msxal-</i> (“pear”).</p><p>Multiple theories have been put forth:</p><p>Theories on the origin of μῆλον</p><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)meh₁l-</i> (“small animal”). Compare Old Irish <i>mil</i> (“small animal”), Dutch <i>maal</i> (“young cow”), Old Church Slavonic <i>малъ</i> (malŭ), Old English <i>smæl</i> (English <i>small</i>). Despite phonetic and semantic similarities, likely unrelated to Old Armenian <i>մալ</i> (mal, “cattle”), which stems from Arabic مَال (māl, “property”) instead.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>membrane; cuticle (<i>in general</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>membrane of the eye</li><li>drum of the ear, eardrum</li><li>membrane enclosing the brain, dura mater</li><li>scum on milk</li></ol></ol><p>The semantic parallel with words like Latin <i>membrāna</i> (“membrane”) and Balto-Slavic words like Latvian <i>miesa</i> (“flesh”), Russian <i>мя́со</i> (mjáso, “meat”) and Polish <i>mięso</i> (“meat”), all ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mems-</i> (“flesh, meat”), is striking. However, Beekes says the unexplained <i>-ν-</i> (-n-), together with the suffix <i>-ιγγ-</i> (-ing-), clearly points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μῆνιγξ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Meninx; Djerba, Tunisia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rage; wrath (especially of the gods)</li></ol><p>Unknown. The long vowel makes it unlikely to be from the same root as <i>μᾰνῐ́ᾱ</i> (mănĭ́ā). Sanskrit <i>मे॒नि</i> (mení, “wrath; revenge; vengeance”), Sanskrit <i>म॒न्यु</i> (manyú, “anger; sorrow; spirit”), Albanian <i>mëni</i>, <i>mëri</i> (“anger; sorrow”). Alternately, following Beekes, a religious Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>baldmoney, spignel (<i>Meum athamanticum</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>μεῖον</i> (meîon, “lesser”) for its small size. However, Furnée compares <i>μαῖον</i> (maîon, “kind of clover”), which would point to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thigh-bones (femora) wrapped in two folds of fat for votive immolation</li></ol><p>From the plural of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mḗron</i>&nbsp;n (“thigh”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bristle</li><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: thorn which grows in the fleece of cattle.</li></ol><p>The suffix "-ιγγ-", the prothetic "σ-" and the lack of possible cognates, apart from <i>μήρινθος</i> (mḗrinthos, “fishing line”), lead to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skill</li><li>counsel</li><li>plan</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mḗtis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₁tis</i> (“measurement”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₁-</i> (“to measure”).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>mǣþ</i>. Apparently an Aeolic or Doric form, as the Attic-Ionic form would have been <i>&#42;μῆσις</i> (&#42;mêsis).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Μῆτις)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Metis (Oceanid)</li><li>skill</li><li>counsel</li><li>plan</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mḗtis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méh₁tis</i> (“measurement”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₁-</i> (“to measure”).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>mǣþ</i>. Apparently an Aeolic or Doric form, as the Attic-Ionic form would have been <i>&#42;Μῆσις</i> (&#42;Mêsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means, way</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means, expedient, remedy</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a vrddhi derivative from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;megʰ-</i> (“to be able”). See also <i>Μάγος</i> (Mágos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mime, actor</li><li>imitation, mimicking</li><li>mime (<i>a drama of everyday life without choir</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably a Pre-Greek loan. The traditional connection with Sanskrit <i>माया</i> (māyā́, “art, illusion”) now appears semantically unlikely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatred; hate</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mewHdʰ-</i> (“to complain, be emotional about”), and compared with Latin <i>maereō</i> (“to mourn”). However, Beekes argues in favor of a Pre-Greek origin, based on the suffix <i>-σος</i> (-sos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mu, the name for the twelfth letter Μ (M), μ (m) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤌‬𐤌</i> (m‬m‬ /⁠mēm⁠/), with influence from νῦ (nû).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouse</li><li>mussel</li><li>a type of whale</li><li>muscle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mū́s</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;múHs</i> (“mouse”). Cognates include Latin <i>mūs</i>, Sanskrit <i>मूष्</i> (mū́ṣ), Old Armenian <i>մուկն</i> (mukn) and Old English <i>mūs</i> (English <i>mouse</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μυῖα</i> (muîa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something said: word, speech, conversation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>public speech</li><li>(<i>mostly in plural</i>) talk, conversation</li><li>advice, counsel, command, order, promise</li><li>the subject of a speech or talk</li><li>a resolve, purpose, design, plan</li><li>saying, proverb</li><li>the talk of men, rumor, report, message</li></ol><li>tale, story, narrative</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>tale, legend, myth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in Attic prose</i>) a legend of the early Greek times, before the dawn of history</li></ol><li>a professed work of fiction, fable, such as those of Aesop</li><li>the plot of a tragedy or comedy</li></ol></ol><p>Traditionally (Pokorny, Vasmer, etc.) grouped with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;myslь</i> (“idea, thought”), Lithuanian <i>mausti</i> (“to long for”), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌰𐌿𐌳𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (maudjan, “to remind”), Sanskrit <i>मिथ्या</i> (mithyā, “false, spurious, illusory”), all of which point to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mewHdʰ-</i> (“to complain or care about something”). However, this is implicitly rejected by Beekes, according to whom there are no plausible comparanda; as usual, he tentatively assigns the word to Pre-Greek.</p><p>Other theories include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouse</li><li>mussel</li><li>a type of whale</li><li>muscle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;mū́s</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;múHs</i> (“mouse”). Cognates include Latin <i>mūs</i>, Sanskrit <i>मूष्</i> (mū́ṣ), Old Armenian <i>մուկն</i> (mukn) and Old English <i>mūs</i> (English <i>mouse</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of wine drunk by the Lydians when swearing oaths</li></ol><p>From Lydian, attested as a Hesychian gloss; probably related to Hittite <i>𒈠𒀀𒄴𒆷𒀸</i> (māḫlaš, “grapevine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toil and moil of war, turmoil</li></ol><p>Connection with Latin <i>mōlēs</i> (“mass; heap, pile”) is possible but by no means certain: the original meaning would then be “effort, labor”. The word has also been connected to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōjaną</i> (“to tire”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;majati</i> (“to beckon, wave”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₃-</i> (“to get tired, be a burden”); this is tentatively supported by Beekes. Alternatively, the linguists José Marcos Macedo and Daniel Kölligan suggest that the term could be derived from <i>&#42;mṓlh₃-s</i>, a root noun to <i>&#42;melh₃-</i> (“to come”), whence also Ancient Greek <i>βλώσκω</i> (blṓskō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moly, a magic herb mentioned by Homer</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) kind of garlic (<i>Allium nigrum</i>)</li></ol><p>Generally compared to Sanskrit <i>मूल॑</i> (mū́la, “root, radish”). However, Beekes prefers to connect the word with <i>μώλυζα</i> (mṓluza, “head of garlic”), which has a non-Greek suffix. According to Beekes, this noun must consequently be of Pre-Greek origin too. For the <i>υ</i>-stem, compare <i>μίσυ</i> (mísu), <i>βράθυ</i> (bráthu) and <i>σῶρυ</i> (sôru). According to Beekes, all proposed Indo-European etymologies must be rejected.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft, weak, feeble</li><li>weak in intellect, dull</li></ol><p>For <i>μωλύω</i> (mōlúō, “to parboil, scald, simmer”), compare <i>κωλύω</i> (kōlúō). Because of the unclear meaning and formation, this adjective is etymologically hard to assess. Connection with <i>μέλεος</i> (méleos, “idle, useless, vain”) does not explain the long vowel -ω-. Comparison with <i>μῶλος</i> (môlos, “toil and moil”) is mentioned by Pokorny. As Frisk remarks, everything remains hypothetical. According to Beekes, the suffix -υκ- looks Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blame, ridicule, disgrace</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The term has traditionally been compared to <i>ἀμύμων</i> (amúmōn, “blameless”), though Beekes rejects this proposal, arguing that the ablaut variation of <i>ω ~ υ</i> (ō ~ u) is impossible. The term has also been connected with Old Avestan <i>𐬨𐬄𐬥𐬀𐬭𐬋𐬌𐬱</i> (mąnarōiš).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(like μή,&#32;in a question expecting a negative answer) but surely not? is it really so?</li><li>(followed by οὐ,&#32;in a question expecting a positive answer)</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>μή</i> (mḗ, “not”) +&lrm; <i>οὖν</i> (oûn, “then”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of horses</i>) having an uncloven hoof</li></ol><p>From <i>μόνος</i> (mónos, “single”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνῠξ</i> (ónŭx, “nail, hoof”) via haplology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>μωρόν</i> (mōrón, “foolishness; folly; lack of foresight”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>μωρός</i> (mōrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>μωρός</i> (mōrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nabla, a musical instrument of ten or twelve strings</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>נֵבֶל</i> (nḗḇel).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woolen skin, fleece</li></ol><p>Has been connected with Old English <i>næsc</i> (“soft leather; deer skin”) but Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodland vale, dell, glen</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μάταιος</i> (mátaios, “vain, empty, idle”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of nard</li></ol><p>From <i>νάρδος</i> (nárdos, “nard”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nard plant, spikenard, nardin, muskroot</li><li>nard oil, a highly prized ointment made from the plant</li></ol><p>From Phoenician (compare Hebrew <i>נֵרְדְּ</i> (nērd), Aramaic <i>נִרְדָּא</i> (nirdā), Akkadian <i>𒆠𒆗</i> (HIRIM /⁠lardu⁠/), Arabic رَنْد (rand), Old South Arabian <i>𐩧𐩬𐩵</i> (rnd)), possibly from Sanskrit <i>नलद</i> (nálada, “Indian narde”) (although a Semitic-to-Indian loan has also been suggested).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giant fennel (<i>Ferula communis</i>)</li><li>casket for unguents</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) vestibule in a church</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the presence of a variant and the ending in <i>-ξ</i> (-x) indicate a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A fragrant Indian bark used as a spice</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, this is a Semitic cultural term, in spite of the suggested Indian origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>numbness, torpor</li><li>stingray, electric ray</li></ol><p>Traditionally, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)nerk-</i>, an extension of <i>&#42;(s)ner-</i> (“to turn, twist”) shared with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snerhaną</i>, <i>&#42;snarhōną</i> (“to twist, wind, swing; to knot, braid; to snare”) and <i>&#42;snarhǭ</i> (“swing, loop, noose, snare”), along with (less likely) Old Armenian <i>ներգև</i> (nergew, “weak, fragile, small”). However, the semantics and shape of the Greek make this unlikely, and so Beekes considers it more likely Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daffodil, narcissus</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the suffix clearly points to a Pre-Greek word. The connection with <i>νάρκη</i> (nárkē, “numbness”) is due to folk etymology.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νάρκισσος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Narcissus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>benumbing</li></ol><p>From <i>ναρκόω</i> (narkóō, “I benumb”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>λάρναξ</i> (lárnax); (<i>hapax legomenon</i>) chest</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes considers it a dissimilated variant of <i>λάρναξ</i> (lárnax, “coffer, chest, box”). See the latter for more information.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant used in perfumery</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>νάρδος</i> (nárdos, “nard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prepared bread, which is also called <i>μασητρίς</i> (masētrís)</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to press, squeeze close, stamp down</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to stuff quite full, cram, pack</li></ol><p>It is unclear whether the verbal stem originally ended in a velar or in a dental. The etymology remains unknown; connection with <i>νάκη</i> (nákē, “woolen skin”) cannot be proven. According to Beekes, the verb is probably Pre-Greek. Borrowed as Latin: naccae (“fullers”), perhaps from an unattested <i>&#42;νάκται</i> (&#42;náktai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>naphtha, naturally occurring liquid petroleum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;naftah</i> (“petroleum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>νήφω</i> (nḗphō)</li></ol><p>The term is only attested in the form <i>νᾶφε</i> (nâphe), which appears in a specific manuscript that contains fragments of the writings of Epicharmus. Other manuscripts, however, contain the form <i>νῆφε</i> (nêphe). According to the linguist Michael Weiss, this may indicate that this term is a hyperdoricism created during the Medieval or Renaissance period.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow (over)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₂-</i> (“to flow, to swim”). Cognate with <i>Ναϊάς</i> (Naïás), <i>Νηρεύς</i> (Nēreús), Latin <i>nō</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्नौति</i> (snauti, “to flow; drip”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young man</li></ol><p>Deadjectival, from <i>νέος</i> (néos, “young”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uttermost; lowest</li><li>extreme; outermost</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from <i>νέος</i> (néos, “new; young”), but perhaps contaminated with <i>νειός</i> (neiós, “fallow field”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a fawn</li></ol><p>From <i>νεβρός</i> (nebrós, “fawn”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a state of death, deadness; death</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) mortification</li></ol><p>From <i>νεκρόω</i> (nekróō, “I make dead, I am dead; I mortify”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nectar (the drink of the gods)</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin.</p><p>Traditionally taken as a poetic compound, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neḱ-</i> (“to perish, disappear”) + <i>&#42;-tr̥h₂</i> (“overcoming”), from <i>&#42;terh₂-</i> (“to overcome, pass through, cross over”); thus, literally "overcoming death", and so called because it gave immortality.</p><p>However, other theories involving a non-Indo-European borrowing have been proposed. Furnee compares <i>νικάριον</i> (nikárion, “eye salve”), suggesting a possible Pre-Greek origin for both, while Griffith and Levin have suggested borrowings from Egyptian and Semitic, respectively. Beekes appears to be split between the Indo-European and Pre-Greek theories, while expressing skepticism about the Afro-Asiatic proposals.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpse; dead person</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) spirits of the dead</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>later poetic</i>) dead; deceased</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neḱ-</i> (“perish; disappear”); see also Middle Welsh <i>angheu</i> (“death”); Breton <i>ankou</i>; Old Irish <i>éc</i>; Latin <i>noxius</i> (“harmful”), <i>noceō</i> (“to hurt; to harm”), and <i>nex</i> (“murder; violent death”) (as opposed to <i>mors</i>); Old Persian <i>𐎻𐎴𐎰𐎹𐎫𐎹</i> (vi-n-θ-y-t-y /⁠vi-nathayatiy⁠/, “he injures”); Avestan <i>𐬥𐬀𐬯𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (nasiie<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti, “disappears”), <i>𐬥𐬀𐬯𐬎-</i> (nasu-, “corpse”); Sanskrit <i>नश्य॑ति</i> (náśyati, “disappear; perish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distribution of what is due</li><li>righteous assignment of anger, wrath at anything unjust, just resentment, indignation</li></ol><p>From <i>νέμω</i> (némō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νέμεσις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nemesis, goddess of retribution.</li></ol><p>Personification of <i>νέμεσις</i> (némesis, “distribution”), from <i>νέμω</i> (némō, “to distribute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooded pasture, glade</li></ol><p>From <i>νέμω</i> (némō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;némos</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>nemus</i> and Sanskrit <i>नमस्</i> (námas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deal out, distribute, dispense, to count, to divide by number</li><li>(medial) hold, possess, enjoy</li><li>(<i>of herdsmen</i>), to pasture or graze their flocks, drive to pasture, tend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;némō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ném-e-ti</i> (“to assign, allot; take”). Cognate with English <i>numb</i>, Dutch <i>nemen</i>, German <i>nehmen</i>, and Albanian <i>njeh</i> (“count”), <i>nëmë</i> (“curse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maternal or paternal brother, uncle</li><li>maternal grandfather</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic word; compare Latin <i>nonnus</i> (“monk; old person”), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;nana</i> (“grandmother”), Sanskrit <i>नना</i> (nanā, “mother, mama”), Russian <i>ня́ня</i> (njánja, “nurse”). Within Greek, we found <i>νόννος</i> (nónnos, “father”), <i>νάννας</i> (nánnas, “uncle”) and <i>νίννη</i> (nínnē, “grandmother”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make one's way, go, come, depart</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of rivers</i>) to flow</li></ol><li>to go or come back or home, return</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;néhomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nes-</i> (“to join with, to conceal oneself, to recover”). Cognate with <i>Νέστωρ</i> (Néstōr), <i>νόστος</i> (nóstos), <i>νίσομαι</i> (nísomai), <i>ναίω</i> (naíō), <i>ναιετάω</i> (naietáō), <i>νᾱός</i> (nāós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) youthful</li><li>new; fresh</li><li>(euphemistic,&#32;especially in comparative) unexpected; strange; evil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;néwos</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀚𐀺</i> (ne-wo)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néwos</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>nīewe</i> (English <i>new</i>), Latin <i>novus</i>, Sanskrit <i>नव</i> (náva), and Avestan <i>𐬥𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀</i> (nauua).</p><p>Contrasting the attested feminine form of νέος in Attic, νέᾱ (néā), as well as the word κόρη (kórē) (from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kórwā</i>), we must establish a relative chronology for the Lautgesetze, assuming the digamma from the original form &#42;νέϝος disappeared earlier when between vowels than after liquid and nasal consonants. See Attic–Ionic vowel shift.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catmint (<i>Nepeta cataria</i>)</li></ol><p>According to André, <i>νέπιτα</i> (népita) was taken from Latin <i>nepeta</i> (“catmint”). Furnée considers the origin of the Latin word to be Ancient Greek <i>&#42;νεπετα</i> (&#42;nepeta). Altogether, these variants point to Pre-Greek origin. Note the Pre-Greek suffix <i>&#42;-ετ-</i> (&#42;-et-), <i>&#42;-ιτ-</i> (&#42;-it-) and the vowel alternation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νέρθεν</i> (nérthen)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νέρθεν</i> (nérthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up from below</li><li>below, underneath</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>below, under</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ner-</i> +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil of bitter almonds</li></ol><p>According to Lewy, it is a Semitic loan, confronting Hebrew <i>נטף</i> (naṭaf, “stacte”) and Aramaic <i>נטופא</i> (nātōpā, “drip; odoriferous resin”). However, the variations <i>dental/zero</i> and <i>ε/ι</i> could also point to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;népʰos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nébʰos</i> (“cloud”). Cognates include <i>νεφέλη</i> (nephélē, “cloud”), Latin <i>nebula</i> (“fog, cloud”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nebo</i> (“heaven, sky”), Sanskrit <i>नभस्</i> (nábhas, “sky”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swim</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of one in shoes that are too large) to float</li><li>to spin</li><li>to heap, to pile up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be stuffed</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(Doric,&#32;in the phrase <i>ἐς νέω</i> (es néō)) next year</li></ol><p>Alteration of νάω (náō, “to flow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₂-</i> (“to flow; to swim”).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₁-</i> (“to spin”). Cognates include <i>νῆτρον</i> (nêtron, “spindle”), <i>νῆμα</i> (nêma, “thread, yarn”), English <i>nematode</i>, <i>needle</i>, <i>neuro</i>, <i>sinew</i>.</p><p>Probably a verbalization of <i>νῆσαι</i> (nêsai), itself perhaps a contracted form of <i>νηῆσαι</i> (nēêsai), of unknown origin; the chronology and morphological development of the word and its relatives is similarly unclear.</p><p>Abstracted from <i>νέωτα</i> (néōta, “next year”), traditionally taken as a compound of <i>νέος</i> (néos, “new”) +&lrm; <i>ἔτος</i> (étos, “year”), literally “new year” &gt; "next year"; however, the <i>-ω-</i> (-ō-) remains difficult to explain. Szemerenyi proposes that it arises from a dative form of <i>νέος</i> (néos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used to express strong affirmation, with accusative case of the divinity invoked</i>) by</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with the article</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>in answers</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁enos</i> (“that one”), the source of Latin <i>nē</i> (“truly!”), <i>enim</i> (“truly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sweet, pleasant, delightful (<i>epithet of sleep in Homer; used with other nouns by later writers</i>)</li></ol><p>Rebracketing of <i>ἥδυμος</i> (hḗdumos, “sweet, pleasant”), from the root of <i>ἡδύς</i> (hēdús, “sweet”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown kind of plant</li></ol><p>Probably a Pre-Greek word in view of the suffix; see <i>ἄγχουσα</i> (ánkhousa), <i>αἴθουσα</i> (aíthousa) and <i>κάδουσα</i> (kádousa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spin</li></ol><p>From νέω (néō). The dental enlargement in this term is comparable to other Greek terms such as κνήθω (knḗthō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>νήϊος</i> (nḗïos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swimmer</li></ol><p>From <i>νήχω</i> (nḗkhō, “to swim”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>childish, infantile, juvenile, young</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>of the understanding</i>) childish, untaught, silly, credulous</li><li>(<i>of bodily strength</i>)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infant, child</li></ol><p>The word is presumably the negative prefix νη-, but the stem is unclear. Strong's Greek Lexicon (1890) suggests <i>νη-</i> (nē-, negative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ἔπος</i> (épos, “speech”), compare Latin <i>infans</i> (“infant”) from <i>in-</i> (not) +&lrm; <i>fāns</i> (speaking). But a 2010 review by Beekes favors instead a derivation from <i>ἤπιος</i> (ḗpios), as suggested by Lacroix in 1937, with a possible PIE root <i>&#42;h₂ep</i> (“join”), from which we would also have <i>ἅπτω</i> (háptō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without compensation or recompense</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unavenged</li><li>lacking</li></ol><p><i>νη-</i> (nē-) +&lrm; <i>ποινή</i> (poinḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unavenged</li><li>lacking</li></ol><p><i>νη-</i> (nē-) +&lrm; <i>ποινή</i> (poinḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oleander (<i>Nerium oleander</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it may belong to <i>νηρόν</i> (nērón, “fresh water”) because of the characteristic property of this plant to follow the course of brooks.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink no wine</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be sober, dispassionate</li><li>to be watchful</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>νήφων</i> (nḗphōn, “sober”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-h₁égʷʰ-ō</i> (literally “non-drinking”). The fact that the stem of the adjective is <i>νήφον-</i> (nḗphon-), not <i>&#42;νήφοντ-</i> (&#42;nḗphont-), shows that it is not originally a present participle. In the later language, a true present participle with stem <i>νήφοντ-</i> (nḗphont-) is also attested.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for ships</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wash the hands or feet of another</li><li>to purge; to cleanse</li><li>to wash off</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;niďďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nigʷ-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;neygʷ-</i> (“to wash”). Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nikwes</i>, Old English <i>nicor</i>, German <i>Nix</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of winning: victory, success [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘over, in something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>things won in victory, fruits of victory</li><li>the upper hand, advantage</li></ol></ol><p>The origin is uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neyk-</i> (“to attack, run at”) and cognate with <i>νεῖκος</i> (neîkos, “quarrel, strife”), Lithuanian <i>ap-ni̇̀kti</i> (“to attack”); however, Beekes is semantically unconvinced, and prefers to take the word as Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νίκη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Nike</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>νίκη</i> (níkē, “victory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water for washing</li></ol><p>From <i>νῐ́ζω</i> (nĭ́zō, “to wash”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandmother or mother-in-law</li></ol><p>From the same onomatopoeic origin of <i>νόννος</i> (nónnos, “father”), <i>νάννας</i> (nánnas, “uncle”) and <i>νέννος</i> (nénnos, “uncle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>another name of the plant <i>ἑλένιον</i> (helénion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water for washing</li></ol><p>From <i>νίζω</i> (nízō, “to wash the hands”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>φθείρ</i> (phtheír, “louse”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νίσσομαι</i> (níssomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go, go away</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go to (<i>a place</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;nínsomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ni-ns-étor</i>, with assimilation of <i>-ns-</i> to <i>-ss-</i>. The alternative form <i>νῑ́σομαι</i> (nī́somai) shows deletion of <i>n</i> with compensatory lengthening.</p><p>Later grammarians consider <i>νίσσομαι</i> to be the present tense and <i>νῑ́σομαι</i> the future tense, but there is no historical basis for this interpretation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sodium carbonate or sodium nitrate (or perhaps mineral potassium nitrate), which were not distinguished in Europe until the 13th century</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Semitic. Compare Hebrew <i>נֶתֶר</i> (néṯer, “washing soda”), all ultimately from Egyptian <i>nṯrj</i>: <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./76670467.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="R9" alt="glyph" height="92"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) it snows</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be snowed upon</li></ol><li>to rain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sneygʷʰ-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>ningit</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्नेहति</i> (snehati), Old English <i>snīwan</i> (English <i>snew</i>). By surface analysis, <i>νῐ́ψ</i> (nĭ́ps, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-ω</i> (-ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) snow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snígʷʰs</i> (“snow”), root noun derived from <i>&#42;sneygʷʰ-</i> (“to snow”) (whence also Ancient Greek <i>νίφω</i> (níphō)). Attested as the accusative singular <i>νῐ́φᾰ</i> (nĭ́phă).</p><p>Direct cognates include Latin <i>nix</i> and Old Irish <i>snechtae</i> and indirectly also Sanskrit <i>स्नेह॑</i> (snéha) and Old English <i>snāw</i> and <i>snīwan</i> (English <i>snow</i> and <i>snew</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washing (of hands or feet)</li></ol><p>From <i>νῐ́ζω</i> (nĭ́zō, “to wash the hands or feet”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀ-</i> (a-)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-</i> (“not, un-”) when followed by the laryngeal <i>*h₂</i> and a consonant (i.e. <i>*n̥h₂C-</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used to express strong affirmation</i>) yea, verily</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in oaths</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>in answers</i>) yes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with <i>ἀλλά</i> (allá, “but”) or similar words,&#32;as a qualified assent) yes...but</li></ol></ol><p>Variant of <i>νή</i> (nḗ, “yea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>present and imperfect only</i>) to dwell, abide</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative of place</i>) to inhabit</li><li>(<i>of places</i>) to lie, be situated</li></ol><li>(causal,&#32;with accusative of place) to give one to dwell in; to make habitable, build</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to found</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be situated</li><li>(<i>with accusative of person</i>) to let one dwell, to settle them</li></ol><li>(<i>middle and passive in active sense</i>) to settle</li><li>alternative form of <i>νάω</i> (náō)</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nes-</i> (“to join with, to conceal oneself”), but Beekes considers this uncertain. See <i>νέομαι</i> (néomai, “to go or come back”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) player on the nabla</li></ol><p>From <i>νᾰβλῐ́ζω</i> (năblĭ́zō, “to play the nabla”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestle</li></ol><p>From <i>νάσσω</i> (nássō, “to press”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical) consecrated in the Nazarite vows</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical) a Nazarite</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>נָזִיר</i> (nāzīr, “Nazarite”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix), perhaps from <i>נִזַּר</i> (nizzar, “separate; consecrate oneself”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical) consecrated in the Nazarite vows</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical) a Nazarite</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>נָזִיר</i> (nāzîr, “Nazarite”), perhaps from <i>נִזַּר</i> (nizzar, “separate; consecrate oneself”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell, inhabit</li><li>to be situated</li><li>to be inhabited, located</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nes-</i> (“to join with, to conceal oneself”). Cognate with <i>ναίω</i> (naíō) and <i>νέομαι</i> (néomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truth</li></ol><p>From <i>νη-</i> (nē-) +&lrm;, variant of <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) + the root of <i>ἁμαρτάνω</i> (hamartánō, “to err”) + <i>-εια</i> (-eia, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper or guardian of a temple</li><li>master or pilot of a ship</li></ol><p>From <i>νᾱός</i> (nāós, “inmost part of a temple”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bearded iris (<i>Iris</i> × <i>germanica</i>)</li></ol><p>From Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of <i>νάρθηξ</i> (nárthēx)</li></ol><p>From <i>νάρθηξ</i> (nárthēx, “giant fennel”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>carrying a wand of giant fennel</li></ol><p>From <i>νάρθηξ</i> (nárthēx, “giant fennel”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) skin, wineskin</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p><p>DELG considers a connection with <i>λάρκος</i> (lárkos, “charcoal basket”) and perhaps <i>νάρναξ</i> (nárnax, “box, chest, coffer”). He rejects a connection with <i>νάρκη</i> (nárkē, “numbness”).</p><p>Obrador-Cursach compares as a possible cognate Phrygian <i>νώρικον</i> (nṓrikon, “skin, skinbag”). He hesitatingly proposes a shared source for the Greek and Phrygian word, further comparing Akkadian <i>𒈾𒊒𒄣</i> (narūqu, “sack, bag”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of narcissus, daffodil</li><li>of the color of daffodil</li></ol><p>From <i>νάρκισσος</i> (nárkissos, “narcissus, daffodil”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>making stiff or numb, narcotic</li></ol><p>From <i>νάρκωσις</i> (nárkōsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to benumb</li></ol><p><i>νάρκη</i> (nárkē, “numbness”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>close-pressed</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) cheesecake</li></ol><p>From <i>νάσσω</i> (nássō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece of wreck</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) shipwreck</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγνῡμι</i> (ágnūmi, “to break, shatter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suffer shipwreck.</li></ol><p>From <i>ναυᾱγός</i> (nauāgós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>νᾱυαγέω</i> (nāuagéō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ναυηγός</i> (nauēgós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a shipowner, to own a ship</li><li>to govern, to rule</li></ol><p><i>ναύκληρος</i> (naúklēros, “shipowner”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, stative verb suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voyage</li></ol><p>From <i>ναύκληρος</i> (naúklēros) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight by sea</li></ol><p><i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea fight, naval battle</li></ol><p><i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipbuilder's yard, dockyard</li></ol><p>From <i>ναυπηγός</i> (naupēgós, “shipbuilder”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build ships</li></ol><p><i>ναυπηγός</i> (naupēgós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipwright, shipbuilder</li></ol><p><i>ναῦς</i> (naûs) +&lrm; <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nausea, seasickness</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>famed for ships, famous by sea</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós, “famous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ναυσικλειτός</i> (nausikleitós)</li></ol><p><i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>κλυτός</i> (klutós, “famous”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) seaman; sailor</li><li>paper nautilus (<i>Argonauta argo</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ναύτης</i> (naútēs, “seaman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seafaring, naval, on the sea</li><li>skilled in seamanship</li></ol><p><i>ναύτης</i> (naútēs, “sailor”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a temple</li><li>innermost part of a temple, sanctuary, the part of the temple considered most pure and holy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;nahwós</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nes-</i> (“to join with, to conceal oneself”) and related to <i>ναίω</i> (naíō, “to dwell”), <i>νέομαι</i> (néomai, “to go or come back”). However, the existence of rare variants <i>ναιός</i> (naiós) and <i>νειός</i> (neiós) suggest a possible Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commander of a fleet, admiral</li><li>(<i>in Sparta</i>) admiral in chief</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shipowner and merchant</li><li>skipper, sailing-master</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) captain, commander</li><li>(<i>at Athens</i>) one who rented and sublet tenement houses</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>κλῆρος</i> (klêros, “lot; clergy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harbour, anchorage, roadstead</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>σταθμός</i> (stathmós, “standing-place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor, seaman</li><li>companion at sea</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laconian form of <i>νᾱός</i> (nāós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passage-money, fare, freight</li><li>tax paid for the use of state-provided boats</li><li>freight, cargo of ships</li><li>rent of a tenement</li></ol><p>From <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;nā́us</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néh₂us</i> (“boat”), cognate with Latin <i>nāvis</i>, Persian ناو (nâv), and Sanskrit <i>नौ</i> (nau, “ship”), <i>नाव</i> (nāva, “ship”). The earliest attested reference to the word is the Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀙𐀄𐀈𐀗</i> (na-u-do-mo, “shipbuilders”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) the lowest of the three strings which formed the framework of the musical scale, the others being <i>μέση</i> (mésē) and <i>ὑπάτη</i> (hupátē)</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>νέατος</i> (néatos, “uttermost, lowest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plough up anew</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>νέος</i> (néos) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō). However, given the existence of Latin <i>novō</i> (“to renew”) and Hittite <i>𒉈𒂊𒌋𒉿𒄴𒄩𒀭𒍣</i> (ne-e-u-wa-aḫ-ḫa-an-zi /⁠nēwaḫḫ-⁠⁠/), it is perhaps possible that the term reflects Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;newaō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néweh₂ti</i>. Beekes, alternatively, suggests the possibility that all of these terms are independent formations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>νέατος</i> (néatos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νῑ́φω</i> (nī́phō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snéygʷʰeti</i>, the thematic present of <i>&#42;sneygʷʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young man, youth, young adult, younger adult (ranges from age 20 to age 40, and thus comparable to Latin <i>iuvenis</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>young, active; hot-headed, headstrong</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>νεάν</i> (neán, “young”), from <i>νέος</i> (néos, “young”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young man, youth</li><li>servant, attendant; a <i>young man</i> functioning in a subordinate or assisting role</li></ol><p>From <i>νεανίας</i> (neanías) (ultimately from <i>νέος</i> (néos) “young”) with the suffix <i>-ίσκος</i> (-ískos), diminutive in form but not necessarily in force.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young, youthful</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) new, fresh</li><li>(<i>of events</i>) new, recent</li><li>(<i>feminine plural substantive</i>) the novellae in the Code of Justinian</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;newr̥ós</i>. Related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néwos</i> (“new”), whence <i>νέος</i> (néos, “new”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>նոր</i> (nor) and Latin <i>noverca</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fawn, young deer</li><li>type of cowardice</li></ol><p>Some have tried a connection with Old Armenian <i>ներկ</i> (nerk, “color”), deriving both from a Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;(s)negʷro-</i>, because often the deer and the hind are called after their variegated color (compare <i>πρόξ</i> (próx, “roe deer”) and <i>περκνός</i> (perknós, “dusky”)). Others derive it from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;negʷ-</i> (“naked”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Epic form of <i>νεᾱνῐ́ᾱς</i> (neānĭ́ās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>νεᾱνῐ́σκος</i> (neānĭ́skos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to argue; to quarrel</li></ol><p>From <i>νεῖκος</i> (neîkos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>νεικέω</i> (neikéō, “argue, quarrel”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) from the bottom</li></ol><p>Compare <i>νέατος</i> (néatos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) at the bottom</li><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) under, beneath</li></ol><p>Compare <i>νέατος</i> (néatos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fallow-land</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;en-</i> (“in”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A pile of corpses.</li><li>A line or row of soldiers.</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) The dead.</li></ol><p><i>νέκυς</i> (nékus, “corpse”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs, “group of”, noun-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A messenger of the dead.</li></ol><p>From <i>νεκρο-</i> (nekro-, “dead”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos, “messenger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to lead the dead</li></ol><p>From <i>νεκρο-</i> (nekro-, “dead”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγή</i> (agōgḗ, “transportation”, noun) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verb-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νεκρᾰγωγοῖ</i> (nekrăgōgoî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νεκρᾰγωγοῖμεν</i> (nekrăgōgoîmen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νεκρᾰγωγοῖμῐ</i> (nekrăgōgoîmĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νεκρᾰγωγοῖς</i> (nekrăgōgoîs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νεκρᾰγωγοῖεν</i> (nekrăgōgoîen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νεκρᾰγωγοῖτε</i> (nekrăgōgoîte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νεκρᾰγωγοῖτον</i> (nekrăgōgoîton)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νεκρᾰγωγοίτην</i> (nekrăgōgoítēn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who leads or guides the dead.</li></ol><p>From <i>νεκρο-</i> (nekro-, “dead”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγή</i> (agōgḗ, “transportation”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, noun-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A coffin or urn</li></ol><p>From <i>νεκρός</i> (nekrós, “corpse”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necromancy</li></ol><p>From <i>νεκρός</i> (nekrós, “corpse”) +&lrm; <i>μαντεία</i> (manteía, “divination”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of the dead</li></ol><p>From <i>νεκρο-</i> (nekro-, “dead”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necromancer</li></ol><p>From <i>νεκρο-</i> (nekro-, “dead”) +&lrm; <i>μάντις</i> (mántis, “prophet”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>city of the dead (suburb of the ancient city of Alexandria)</li></ol><p>From <i>νεκρός</i> (nekrós, “dead”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city state, city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dead</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dead body, corpse</li><li>one who is dead (in plural: the dead)</li><li>dying person</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neḱ-</i> (“to perish”); further discussion of the morphology follows below.</p><p>The term likely did not form at the Proto-Indo-European level, as the expected result due to suffix ablaut would be <i>&#42;n̥ḱrós</i>, which would regularly yield <i>&#42;ἀκρός</i> (&#42;akrós). It probably formed either at the Proto-Greek stage or the Ancient Greek stage from <i>νέκυς</i> (nékus, “a dead body”)) + <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p><p>See also Latin <i>nocēre</i> (“to hurt, harm”), <i>nex</i> (“murder, violent death”) (as opposed to <i>mors</i>) and Sanskrit <i>नश्यति</i> (naśyati, “to disappear, perish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of garments) fragrant</li><li>sweet (as nectar)</li></ol><p>From <i>νέκταρ</i> (néktar) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the dead</li></ol><p>From <i>νέκῡς</i> (nékūs, “corpse”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a magical rite by which ghosts were called up and questioned about the future</li></ol><p>From <i>νέκῡ̆ς</i> (nékū̆s, “corpse”) +&lrm; <i>ῐσμός</i> (ĭsmós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νεκῠ́δᾰλος</i> (nekŭ́dălos)</li></ol><p>Apparently related to <i>νέκυς</i> (nékus, “corpse”), though the origin of the second element is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the nympha of the silkworm</li></ol><p>Apparently related to <i>νέκυς</i> (nékus, “corpse”), though the origin of the second element is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel just resentment, be angry at undeserved fortune, be indignant</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to be displeased with oneself, be ashamed</li></ol><p>From <i>νέμεσις</i> (némesis) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy of indignation or blame, shameful, disgraceful</li></ol><p>From <i>νεμεσάω</i> (nemesáō) (compare <i>Νέμεσις</i> (Némesis)) + <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>νεμεσητός</i> (nemesētós) (<i>for metrical purposes</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foolish, silly, unwise</li><li>weak-eyed, purblind</li></ol><p>Furnée compares this word with <i>ἐνεός</i> (eneós, “blind; silly, stupid”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(as a noun) An emancipated helot in Ancient Sparta</li></ol><p>From <i>νεο-</i> (neo-, “new”) +&lrm; <i>δᾶμος</i> (dâmos, “free citizens”, Doric form of <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos)) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>newly dug</li></ol><p>From <i>νεο-</i> (neo-) +&lrm; <i>σκαφ-</i> (skaph-) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young bird, nestling, chick</li><li>any young animal</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) young bees</li><li>yolk of an egg</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>νέος</i> (néos, “new”), probably after the example of <i>περισσός</i> (perissós, “extraordinary, prodigious”), <i>ἔπισσαι</i> (épissai) or <i>μέτασσαι</i> (métassai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to innovate</li><li>to inaugurate</li></ol><p><i>νεοχμός</i> (neokhmós) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitter orange, Seville orange (fruit)</li></ol><p>From Arabic نَارَنْج (nāranj) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix), ultimately from Persian نارنگ (nârang).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowstring</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>νεῦρον</i> (neûron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>νευρᾱ́</i> (neurā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sealavender or snakeweed (<i>Limonium vulgare</i> (syn. <i>Statice limonium</i>))</li></ol><p>From <i>νευροειδής</i> (neuroeidḗs, “sinew-like”): as a noun, a substantivisation of its neuter forms; as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like sinews</li></ol><p>From <i>νευρο-</i> (neuro-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like sinews</li></ol><p>From <i>νευρο-</i> (neuro-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who stitches with sinews, mender of shoes, cobbler</li><li>one who makes strings for the lyre</li></ol><p>From <i>νευρο-</i> (neuro-, “sinew, nerve”) +&lrm; <i>ῥάπτω</i> (rháptō, “to sew, stitch”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nod</li></ol><p>From a derived form of <i>νεύω</i> (neúō) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloud, mass of clouds</li><li>cloud in urine</li><li>cloud on a mirror</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fine bird-net</li><li>(<i>alchemy</i>) sublimate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nebʰ-el-h₂</i> (“cloud”). Related to <i>νέφος</i> (néphos, “cloud”), Latin <i>nebula</i> (“fog, cloud”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) nebula</li><li>cloud in urine</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cloud-like opacity on the eye</li><li>white speck on the nails</li></ol><p>From <i>νεφέλη</i> (nephélē, “cloud”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) cloud-gatherer (epithet of Zeus)</li></ol><p><i>νεφέλη</i> (nephélē) +&lrm; <i>ἀγείρω</i> (ageírō) +&lrm; <i>-τα</i> (-ta)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anatomy,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) kidney</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) testicle</li><li>mind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;nekʷʰrós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;negʷʰrós</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>nefrōnes</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;neurô</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a disease of the kidney</li></ol><p>From <i>νεφρός</i> (nephrós, “kidney”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis, “a suffix forming a feminine noun, especially a technical term that pertains to the root word”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to attempt anything new, make a violent change</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to attempt political changes, make innovations or revolutionary movements</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to revolutionize</li></ol><p>From <i>νεώτερος</i> (neṓteros, “newer”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>innovative, revolutionary</li></ol><p>From <i>νεώτερος</i> (neṓteros, “newer”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth</li><li>youthful spirit, youthfulness, impetuosity</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) a body of youth, the youth</li></ol><p>From <i>νέος</i> (néos, “young”) +&lrm; <i>-τᾱς</i> (-tās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth</li><li>youthful spirit, youthfulness, impetuosity</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) a body of youth, the youth</li></ol><p>From <i>νέος</i> (néos, “young”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs). Diachronically from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;newótāts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néwoteh₂ts</i> (“newness”); cognate with Latin <i>novitās</i>, Sanskrit <i>नवता</i> (návatā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>newly-planted</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) new convert; neophyte</li></ol><p>From <i>νεο-</i> (neo-) +&lrm; <i>φύω</i> (phúō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of sinew</li><li>consisting of fibres</li></ol><p>From <i>νεῦρον</i> (neûron, “sinew, nerve”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nod, beckon, as a sign</li><li>to nod or bow in token of assent</li><li>to grant, promise, assure</li><li>(<i>of warriors</i>) to nod, bend forward</li><li>to incline, slope, tend</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to decline, fall away, diminish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;new-</i> (“to nod”). Cognate with Latin <i>nutō</i> (“to nod”), Sanskrit <i>नव॑ते</i> (návate).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young maiden</li></ol><p>From <i>νέος</i> (néos, “young”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, feminine noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quarrel; wrangle; strife</li><li>strife of words; railing; abuse; taunt; reproach</li><li>strife of law; dispute before a judge</li><li>battle; fight</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;neyk-</i> (“to attack, start vehemently”), cognate with Lithuanian <i>ap-ni̇̀kti</i> (“to attack”), <i>su-ni̇̀kti</i> (“to attack”), Latvian <i>nikns</i> (“bad, grim, vehement”), Albanian <i>nis</i>, and possibly Russian <i>в-никнуть</i> (v-niknutʹ), <i>про-никнуть</i> (pro-niknutʹ). Or else Pre-Greek. See also <i>νίκη</i> (níkē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nod, sign</li><li>expression of will, command</li><li>approval, sanction</li><li>quarter of the heavens</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) direction</li></ol><p>From <i>νεύω</i> (neúō, “to nod”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinew, tendon</li><li>cord, of a slingshot, bowstring</li><li>strength, vigour</li><li>plant fibre</li><li>nerve</li><li>penis</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snḗh₁wr̥</i> (“band, sinew”). Cognate with Latin <i>nervus</i>, English <i>sinew</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्नावन्</i> (snā́van, “tendon, muscle, sinew”), Old Armenian <i>նեարդ</i> (neard), and Avestan <i>𐬯𐬥𐬁𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬭𐬆</i> (snāuuar<sup><small>ə</small></sup>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀ-</i> (a-)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gigantic, mythological animal from Samos called Neades in English, inspired by the fossilized remains of extinct proboscideans.</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) any of the cavities in the body:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>stomach</li><li>belly; paunch</li><li>intestine; bowels</li><li>womb; uterus</li></ol></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ὀσφῦς</i> (osphûs), <i>δελφῡ́ς</i> (delphū́s) and <i>ἰξῡ́ς</i> (ixū́s). The etymology remains unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swimming</li></ol><p>From <i>νήχω</i> (nḗkhō, “to swim”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₂-</i> (“to flow, to swim”), + <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>merciless, pitiless, relentless, ruthless</li><li>unpitied</li></ol><p>From <i>νη-</i> (nē-, negative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ἔλεος</i> (éleos, “mercy, pity”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix). Beekes suggests that its use in phrases such as νηλεὲς ἦμαρ, rather than being figurative, may reflect a distinct etymology <i>νη-</i> (nē-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλέομαι</i> (aléomai) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs), with the meaning 'inescapable'.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Fibrous, in filaments.</li></ol><p>From <i>νῆμα</i> (nêma, “thread, yarn”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “-like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, so that no mistake can be made; truly</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>νημερτής</i> (nēmertḗs).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not making mistakes: unerring, infallible, sure</li></ol><p>From <i>νη-</i> (nē-) +&lrm;, variant of <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) + the root of <i>ἁμαρτάνω</i> (hamartánō, “to err”) + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not making mistakes: unerring, infallible, sure</li></ol><p>From <i>νη-</i> (nē-) +&lrm;, variant of <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) + the root of <i>ἁμαρτάνω</i> (hamartánō, “to err”) + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νημερτής)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Nereids</li></ol><p>From adjective <i>νημερτής</i> (nēmertḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public eulogy on great men, sometimes accompanied by the flute</li><li>lament, dirge, elegy</li></ol><p>Unknown. It is supposed to be the origin of Latin <i>nēnia</i> (“dirge, elegy”). The word <i>νηνίατον</i> (nēníaton, “Phrygian tune for the flute”) is ascribed by Hipponax by Julius Pollux.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>That banishes pain and sorrow; nepenthean.</li><li>Free from sorrow.</li></ol><p>From <i>νη-</i> (nē-, “not-”) +&lrm; <i>πένθος</i> (pénthos, “grief”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea snail</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>νηρόν</i> (nērón, “water”) is refuted by the forms with the vocalic anlaut “α-”. Furnée takes this initial variation as evidence for a Pre-Greek origin. Plevačová draws the term to Finnish <i>nieriä</i> (“saibling”) – which see for more relatives –, downplaying the semantic difference by noting that both are longish sea creatures caught in a fisher’s net and the contexts in which the Greek and Latin are attested may as well point to a fish.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) water</li></ol><p>Neuter of <i>νηρός</i> (nērós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet, damp</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>νεαρός</i> (nearós, “young, fresh”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>islet, small island</li></ol><p>From <i>νῆσος</i> (nêsos) +&lrm; <i>-ῑς</i> (-īs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of an island, insular</li></ol><p><i>νησῐώτης</i> (nēsĭṓtēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>islander (person who lives on an island)</li></ol><p><i>νῆσος</i> (nêsos) +&lrm; <i>-ιώτης</i> (-iṓtēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fast (period of eating no food, little food or avoiding certain dietary items)</li><li>fasting</li></ol><p>From <i>νῆστις</i> (nêstis, “not eating”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fast, abstain from food</li></ol><p>From <i>νῆστις</i> (nêstis, “fasting”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of eagle</li></ol><p>From <i>νῆττα</i> (nêtta, “duck”) +&lrm; <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder, slaughter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of people) sober, not inebriated</li><li>unmixed with wine, wineless</li></ol><p>From <i>νήφω</i> (nḗphō, “to be sober, to abstain from wine, to be self-controlled”) +&lrm; <i>-άλιος</i> (-álios). This family of words pertaining to sobriety (including also <i>νήφων</i>&nbsp;m (nḗphōn), <i>νηφαίνω</i> (nēphaínō, verb)) are related to Old Armenian <i>նաւթի</i> (nawtʻi, “fasting”, adjective). Ultimately, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n-H(H)gʷʰ-ti-o-</i> or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁egʷʰ-</i> (“to drink”) prefixed with the negative particle <i>&#42;n̥-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to win, be the winner, conquer</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to prevail, be superior</li><li>(<i>of opinions</i>) to prevail</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) to succeed [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘at doing’</small>]</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to win one's cause [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘the cause’</small>]</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to conquer, vanquish, beat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(law,&#32;rare) to win (one's case) against</li><li>to overpower</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>νίκη</i> (níkē) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winner (in games, etc.)</li><li>conqueror</li></ol><p><i>νῑκάω</i> (nīkáō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing victory</li><li>victorious</li><li>Uppercase (<i>Νῑκηφόρος</i>), the name of a Roman Emperor, Nikephoros</li></ol><p><i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of fig found in Crete</li></ol><p>According to Neumann, from Pre-Greek. He connects it with the Linear B sign <i>𐀛</i> (ni), which looks like the branch of a fig-tree. In a later article, he further connects Egyptian <i>nqꜥwt</i> (“figs”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washbasin</li></ol><p>From <i>νίζω</i> (nízō, “to wash the hands”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) snowflake</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;figurative</i>) shower, sleet</li><li>(<i>collectively</i>) snowstorm</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>νῐ́ψ</i> (nĭ́ps, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perceive, observe, see, notice</li><li>to think, suppose</li><li>to think out, devise, contrive</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in infinitive</i>) to be minded to do a thing</li></ol><li>to conceive of, to deem</li><li>(<i>of words</i>) to bear a certain sense, to mean</li></ol><p>From <i>νόος</i> (nóos, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intellectual</li></ol><p>From <i>νοητός</i> (noētós, “conceivable, perceptible, intelligible, comprehensible”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, “verbal adjective suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>perceptible to the mind, thinkable, imaginable</li></ol><p>From <i>νοέω</i> (noéō, “to perceive”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to corrupt, adulterate</li><li>to consider spurious</li></ol><p>From <i>νόθος</i> (nóthos, “spurious, counterfeit”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roaming about for pasture, pastoral tribes</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) prostitute</li><li>Numidian</li></ol><p>From <i>νομός</i> (nomós).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νομάς)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Numidian</li><li>an inhabitant of Numidia</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>νομᾰ́ς</i> (nomắs, “nomad”), from <i>νομός</i> (nomós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasturage</li><li>division, distribution</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a governmental division of territory; province, prefecture</li><li>a law</li></ol><li>spreading of sores or blisters</li></ol><p>From <i>νέμω</i> (némō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to use customarily, practise, hold to a custom, customarily hold</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>legislation</i>) to enact</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to make use of, use</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to be accustomed to doing</li></ol><li>to acknowledge, consider as</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to esteem, hold in honour</li><li>(<i>with accusative of object</i>) to hold, believe</li><li>(<i>with accusative and infinitive</i>) to deem, hold, believe that</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “custom”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herdsman, herder</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) owner</li></ol><p>From <i>νέμω</i> (némō) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relating to laws, legal</li></ol><p>From <i>νόμος</i> (nómos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>legislator, lawmaker, lawgiver</li></ol><p><i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law”) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “lay”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relating to legislation, legislative</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) fitted for or skilled in legislation</li></ol><p>From <i>νομοθέτης</i> (nomothétēs, “lawgiver”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of the laws, a title of officials appointed to watch over the laws and their observance</li><li>minor official under control of village elders, with police and fiscal duties</li></ol><p>From <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pasture, field</li><li>herbage, food</li><li>division, distribution</li><li>dwelling, residence</li><li>district, region, province (particularly of Egypt), satrapy</li></ol><p>From <i>νέμω</i> (némō, “to pasture, graze”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal nominal suffix). Compare <i>ἀγρός</i> (agrós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>semantics</i>) meaning or sense (<i>of a word etc.</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sick, to ail</li><li>to be mad, passionate</li><li>to suffer</li></ol><p>From <i>νόσος</i> (nósos, “sickness”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhealthy, unwholesome</li></ol><p>From <i>νοσέω</i> (noséō) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;healthcare) to tend the sick</li></ol><p>From <i>νόσος</i> (nósos, “sickness, disease”) +&lrm; <i>κομέω</i> (koméō, “to take care of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>infirmary, hospital</li></ol><p>From <i>νοσοκομέω</i> (nosokoméō, “to be under medical treatment”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go home, come home, return</li></ol><p><i>νόστος</i> (nóstos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet, damp, moist</li><li>rainy</li></ol><p>From <i>νότος</i> (nótos) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>admonition, warning</li></ol><p>From <i>νουθετέω</i> (nouthetéō, “to admonish”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a warning, admonition, counsel.</li></ol><p>From <i>νουθετέω</i> (nouthetéō) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to admonish, warn, counsel, exhort</li></ol><p>As if from <i>&#42;νουθέτης</i> (&#42;nouthétēs), from <i>νοῦς</i> (noûs) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs), +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>used at the new moon</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curlew (<i>Numenius arquata</i>)</li></ol><p>Attic contracted form of <i>&#42;νεομήνιος</i> (&#42;neomḗnios), from <i>νέος</i> (néos, “new”) +&lrm; <i>μήνη</i> (mḗnē, “moon”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tramp of feet</li></ol><p>Usually compared with Latin <i>nūbēs</i> (“cloud”) and with other Indo-European words for cloud. However, together with <i>νυθός</i> (nuthós), may also form a Pre-Greek etymon.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric spelling of <i>νόμος</i> (nómos)</li><li>nummus, a coin used by the Romans</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) illness, sickness, disease</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) plague</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>now (used to introduce a point, a remonstration or a rebuke&#59; in the context of urgency)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pricking sensation, irritation</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pricking of conscience</li></ol><p>From <i>νύσσω</i> (nússō, “to prick, pierce”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἄφωνος</i> (áphōnos, “mute”), <i>σκοτεινός</i> (skoteinós, “dark”).</li></ol><p>Usually compared with Latin <i>nūbēs</i> (“cloud”) and with other Indo-European words for cloud. However, together with <i>νοῦθος</i> (noûthos), may also form a Pre-Greek etymon.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) day blindness, hemeralopia</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) night blindness, nyctalopia</li></ol><p>The suffix, which is found also in <i>ἀγχίλωψ</i> (ankhílōps), <i>αἱμάλωψ</i> (haimálōps) and <i>θῡμάλωψ</i> (thūmálōps), is typically Pre-Greek. The connection with <i>νύξ</i> (núx, “night”) must be folk-etymological.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bat (small flying mammal)</li></ol><p>From <i>νῠ́κτερος</i> (nŭ́kteros, “nocturnal”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), from <i>νῠ́ξ</i> (nŭ́x, “night”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>by night, nightly, nocturnal</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) potent by night</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of musical composition for the flute, used in Dionysiac worship</li></ol><p>From <i>νῠ́κτερος</i> (nŭ́kteros, “by night”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνός</i> (-ĭnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long-eared owl (<i>Asio otus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>νύξ</i> (núx, “night”) +&lrm; <i>κόραξ</i> (kórax, “crow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eating secretly at night</li></ol><p>From <i>νῠ́ξ</i> (nŭ́x) +&lrm; <i>λᾰθραῖος</i> (lăthraîos) +&lrm; <i>φᾰ́γος</i> (phắgos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night-watchman, guard</li><li>(<i>at Rome</i>) vigiles, the police</li></ol><p>From <i>νύξ</i> (núx, “night”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bridegroom</li></ol><p>From <i>νύμφη</i> (númphē) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow water-lily (<i>Nuphar lutea</i>)</li><li>white water-lily (<i>Nymphaea alba</i>)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νυμφαία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a name given to Ariadne</li></ol><p>From <i>νῠμφαῖος</i> (nŭmphaîos, “sacred to the nymphs”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nymphaeum (an ancient Greek or Roman shrine consecrated to water nymphs, often with a fountain)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sacred to the nymphs</li></ol><p>From <i>νύμφη</i> (númphē) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>taken by nymphs</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) rapt, entranced, enraptured, frenzied</li></ol><p>From <i>νῠ́μφη</i> (nŭ́mphē, “nymph”) +&lrm; <i>ληπτός</i> (lēptós, “taken”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bridechamber</li><li>a temple of Dionysus, Demeter and Persephone</li><li>a variety of water lily</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) paradise</li></ol><p>From <i>νῠ́μφη</i> (nŭ́mphē) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>now, at this moment</li></ol><p>Strengthened form of <i>νῦν</i> (nûn, “now”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be half asleep, doze, drowse</li><li>to hang the head</li></ol><p>Probably an enlargement in <i>-τάζω</i> of a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snewd-</i> (“to slumber, doze”); for the formation, compare <i>κλαστάζω</i> (klastázō) and <i>βαστάζω</i> (bastázō, “to lift up”). Cognates, all found in Baltic, include Lithuanian <i>snústi</i> (“to slumber away”), <i>snudà</i> (“sleeper, dreamer”), <i>snáusti</i> (“to slumber”) (with a different ablaut-grade), and forms with an <i>l-</i>suffix including Lithuanian <i>snaudãlius</i> (“sleepy man”) and Latvian <i>snaudale</i> (“sleepy-head”). If this is correct, the old connection with <i>νεύω</i> (neúō, “to nod”) should be abandoned.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drowsiness</li></ol><p>From <i>νυστάζω</i> (nustázō, “to doze”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a day and night, the space of 24 hours</li></ol><p>From <i>νύξ</i> (núx, “night”) + <i>ἡμέρα</i> (hēméra, “day”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter-in-law</li><li>bride</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snusós</i>. Cognate to Sanskrit <i>स्नु॒षा</i> (snuṣā́), Latin <i>nurus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-</i> (“not; un-”) when followed by the laryngeal <i>&#42;h₃</i> and a consonant (i.e. <i>&#42;n̥h₃C-</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of us two; our; of ours</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>νῶῐ̈</i>&nbsp;du (nôĭ̈, “we two”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, contrastive adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A toothless old man.</li></ol><p>From <i>νωδός</i> (nōdós, “toothless”) +&lrm; <i>γέρων</i> (gérōn, “old man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>toothless</li></ol><p>Possibly reflects Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥h₃dós</i> (“not biting”), from <i>&#42;h₃ed-</i> (“bite”).</p><p>Alternatively, a back-formation from <i>&#42;νώδων</i> (&#42;nṓdōn) from <i>νω-</i> (nō-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ὀδών</i> (odṓn, “tooth”) by analogy to pairs like <i>στράβων</i> (strábōn, “the squint-eyed person”) and <i>στραβός</i> (strabós, “squint-eyed”) or <i>ψώλων</i> (psṓlōn, “the circumcised one”) and <i>ψωλός</i> (psōlós, “with the prepuce drawn back”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slothful, sluggish</li><li>dull, stupid</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>νη-</i> (nē-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ὄθομαι</i> (óthomai, “I care about”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sluggish, slothful</li><li>making sluggish</li></ol><p>From <i>νωθής</i> (nōthḗs, “sluggish, slothful”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós, adjective suffix). The origin of <i>νωθής</i> (nōthḗs, “slow, indolent, stubborn”) is disputed. Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-</i> has been proposed to explain the initial, combined with the roots of <i>ὄθομαι</i> (óthomai, “to show consideration for others, fight shy of”), only used in connection with a negative particle, or <i>ὠθέω</i> (ōthéō, “to push, thrust, drive, throw back”) respectively. A genetical relation with Sanskrit <i>आध्र</i> (ādhrá, “poor, destitute, indigent, weak”), presupposing a <i>&#42;nh₃dh-ro-</i>, is perhaps less improbable than generally thought. Compare also <i>արհամարհ</i> (arhamarh, “contemptible, low, poor, sorry”), an Iranian loan.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νωλεμέως</i> (nōleméōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without pause, unceasingly</li><li>firmly</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-</i> (“not, un-”) + an unattested <i>&#42;ὄλεμος</i> (&#42;ólemos), from <i>&#42;h₃lemH-</i> (“weak, broken”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deal out, distribute</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>νέμω</i> (némō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nem-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) The nones, used in reckoning Roman dates: the 7th day of March, May, Quintilis or July, and October and the 5th day of all other months.</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>nones</i> (“nones; 8th ['9th'] day before the ides of a month”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slow, sluggish, dull, languid</li></ol><p>Furnée convincingly compares <i>νῶκαρ</i> (nôkar, “lethargy”), which points to Pre-Greek origin. The variant form in <i>-χαλ-</i> leads to the same conclusion.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Laconian</i>) spring, source, running water</li></ol><p>Unknown. Seemingly related to <i>νάω</i> (náō, “to flow”).</p><p>See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>perception, sense</li><li>mind as used in feeling, the heart, soul</li><li>the mind as used in resolving and purposing, will</li></ol><li>an act of mind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>thought</li><li>purpose, design</li></ol><li>the sense or meaning of a word</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) intelligence, intellect, reason</li><li>(<i>metaphysics</i>) the principle which acts on elementary particles of matter (as described by Anaxagoras)</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin, though likely inherited (as opposed to borrowed from a substrate). Has been compared to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snutraz</i> (“wise, clever”), <i>νεύω</i> (neúō, “to nod, grant”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nes-</i> (“to return home safely”), <i>νέω</i> (néō, “I spin”) (with sense development "to spin" > "to spin the thread of the mind" > "mind, thought").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perception</li><li>thought, purpose, design</li><li>understanding, mind</li></ol><p>From <i>νοέω</i> (noéō, “to intend, to perceive, to see, to understand”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intelligence, understanding, mental perception</li><li>processes of thought</li><li>(<i>concrete</i>) idea, concept</li></ol><p>From <i>νοέω</i> (noéō, “to perceive, to think”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, suffix forming deverbal nouns).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bastard, baseborn (born of a slave or concubine)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of animals</i>) crossbred</li></ol><li>(generally,&#32;of persons and things) spurious, counterfeit, supposititious</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of literary works</i>) spurious</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Athens</i>) a child of a citizen father and an alien mother</li><li>(plural only,&#32;in&#32;Egyptian) a class of temple-attendants</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conformable to custom, usage, or law</li><li>customary, prescriptive</li><li>established, lawful, rightful</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter plural) usages, customs; funeral rites</li></ol><p>From <i>νόμος</i> (nómos) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is sanctioned by usage</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>custom</li><li>an institution</li></ol><li>a current coin, money</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>νομίζω</i> (nomízō, “to use customarily”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, “nominal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>usage, custom</li><li>law, ordinance</li><li>melody, strain</li><li>(<i>music</i>) ancient type of song</li><li>kind of coin</li><li>course of masonry</li></ol><p>From <i>νέμω</i> (némō, “to distribute”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic word; compare Latin <i>nonnus</i> (“monk; old person”), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;nana</i> (“grandmother”), Sanskrit <i>नना</i> (nanā, “mother, mama”), Russian <i>ня́ня</i> (njánja, “nurse”). Within Greek, there are also <i>νέννος</i> (nénnos, “uncle”), <i>νάννας</i> (nánnas, “uncle”) and <i>νίννη</i> (nínnē, “grandmother”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>perception, sense</li><li>mind as used in feeling, the heart, soul</li><li>the mind as used in resolving and purposing, will</li></ol><li>an act of mind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>thought</li><li>purpose, design</li></ol><li>the sense or meaning of a word</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) intelligence, intellect, reason</li><li>(<i>metaphysics</i>) the principle which acts on elementary particles of matter (as described by Anaxagoras)</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin, though likely inherited (as opposed to borrowed from a substrate). Has been compared to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snutraz</i> (“wise, clever”), <i>νεύω</i> (neúō, “to nod, grant”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nes-</i> (“to return home safely”), <i>νέω</i> (néō, “I spin”) (with sense development "to spin" > "to spin the thread of the mind" > "mind, thought").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickness, disease, plague, affliction</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>νοσέω</i> (noséō, “to be sick”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickness, disease, illness</li><li>plague</li><li>misery, suffering, distress</li><li>madness, vice</li><li>bane</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Willi's derivation from a putative Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n-h₁osu-o-s</i> (“not good”), from <i>&#42;n̥-</i> (“not, un-”) + a <i>u</i>-stem of the o-grade of <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be”), and subsequent comparison of Hittite <i>𒀀𒀸𒋗𒍑</i> (a-aš-šu-uš, “good”), suffers from phonological irregularities.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resin from galbanum, starch from wheat: the valuable part</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) substance, kernel</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(often&#32;Homeric,&#32;in the Odyssey) of or relating to homecoming</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>usually in the phrase <i>νόστιμον</i> <i>ἦμαρ</i> (“day of homecoming, return home”)</li><li>able, likely, or destined to return home</li></ol><li>(<i>of plants</i>) productive, producing high yield</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>succulent, nutritious, wholesome, delicious</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>νόστος</i> (nóstos, “homecoming”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of reaching a place; journey, arrival</li><li>the act of returning or going back</li><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) returning home, homecoming, a safe journey home</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;nóstos</i>, from the <i>o</i>-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nes-</i> (“to return home”) + <i>-τος</i> (-tos). See <i>νέομαι</i> (néomai, “to go or come back”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>νόσφι</i> (nósphi)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νόσφῐ</i> (nósphĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of location</i>) away, far away, at a distance, absent</li><li>apart, by oneself</li><li>(<i>of motion or direction</i>) away, off, to a distance, apart</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of location</i>) far from; in the absence of, apart from, without</li><li>(<i>of motion or direction</i>) away from, to a distance from</li><li>apart from, without the help of</li><li>except (for)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nes-</i> (“to return home”) + <i>-φῐ</i> (-phĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νόσφι</i> (nósphi) with movable nu</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νόσφῐ</i> (nósphĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moist, damp, rainy</li><li>southern, to the south</li></ol><p>From <i>νότος</i> (nótos, “south, southern wind”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet, moist, damp</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) open sea</li><li>southern</li></ol><p>From <i>νότος</i> (nótos, “south wind”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νότιον)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Notium, Aeolis, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet, moist, damp</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) open sea</li><li>southern</li></ol><p>From <i>νότος</i> (nótos, “south wind”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the south(west) wind (which brings mist and wetness)</li><li>the south</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. The traditional connection to <i>νέω</i> (néō, “to swim”) is phonologically improbable due to the latter continuing the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₂-</i> (“to flow; to swim”), though similarities with Old Armenian <i>նայ</i> (nay, “wet, fluid”) are notable. Beekes, based on the lack of solid internal derivation, tentatively suggests a Pre-Greek borrowing, perhaps Southern. If the Armenian is related, it may derive from the same substrate continuum.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νότος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Notus, the god of the south wind</li></ol><p>Personification of <i>νότος</i> (nótos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at night; by night</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>νῠ́μφη</i> (nŭ́mphē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bride, young wife</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>young, nubile woman; marriageable maiden</li><li>daughter-in-law</li><li>(<i>Epigraphic Ancient Greek</i>) young girl</li></ol><li>(Greek mythology,&#32;sometimes&#32;capitalized) nymph, goddess of lower rank</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>goddess of springs; (<i>poetry</i>) spring, spring water</li></ol><li>doll, puppet</li><li>bee or wasp in pupa stage</li><li>winged male ant</li><li>A kind of mollusk.</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) point of a plowshare</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow between the lower lip and the chin</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>zootomy</i>) depression on the shoulder of a horse</li></ol><li>opening rosebud</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) clitoris, also the labia minora &lsqb;from 2nd c.&rsqb;</li><li>niche</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;snewbʰ-</i> (“to marry, to wed”), and compared with Latin <i>nūbō</i> (“to marry”) (whence English <i>nubile</i>). However, Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin, as the preceding theory does not explain the origin of the inner nasal <i>-μ-</i> (-m-); others including Kretschmer have also argued for substrate origin, comparing the proper name <i>Νυμφασία</i> (Numphasía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;nukts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nókʷts</i> (“night”). Cognates include Latin <i>nox</i>, Sanskrit <i>नक्त्</i> (nákt), Old Church Slavonic <i>нощь</i> (noštĭ), Albanian <i>natë</i> and Old English <i>niht</i> (whence English <i>night</i>).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νύξ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nyx</li></ol><p>Personified <i>νῠ́ξ</i> (nŭ́x, “night”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turning post in a racecourse</li><li>starting and winning post</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes suggests a connection to <i>νύσσω</i> (nússō, “to thrust, pierce”), thus <i>&#42;núk-ya</i> (literally “the thrusting one”), but Jüthner believes the word is of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch with a sharp point, prick, stab, pierce</li><li>to nudge (<i>with the elbow</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to sting</li><li>to impinge upon</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to suffer lesion</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;new-</i> (“to make a push, nod”). However, compare <i>ἔγχος</i> (énkhos, “spear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>νύσσω</i> (nússō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>first person dual personal pronoun</i>) we two, both of us, us two</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nóh₁</i>. Cognate with Old Avestan <i>𐬥𐬁</i> (nā), Sanskrit <i>नौ</i> (nau, “us two”, enclitic), Old Church Slavonic <i>на</i> (na).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>perception, sense</li><li>mind as used in feeling, the heart, soul</li><li>the mind as used in resolving and purposing, will</li></ol><li>an act of mind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>thought</li><li>purpose, design</li></ol><li>the sense or meaning of a word</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) intelligence, intellect, reason</li><li>(<i>metaphysics</i>) the principle which acts on elementary particles of matter (as described by Anaxagoras)</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin, though likely inherited (as opposed to borrowed from a substrate). Has been compared to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;snutraz</i> (“wise, clever”), <i>νεύω</i> (neúō, “to nod, grant”), Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nes-</i> (“to return home safely”), <i>νέω</i> (néō, “I spin”) (with sense development "to spin" > "to spin the thread of the mind" > "mind, thought").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inglorious</li></ol><p>From <i>ν-</i> (n-) +&lrm; <i>ὄνυμα</i> (ónuma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>running water: fountain, stream, river, spring</li></ol><p>From <i>νάω</i> (náō, “flow”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwarf</li></ol><p>Possibly onomatopoeic, though the logic behind the formation, as well as putative source if borrowed, is unclear. Compare perhaps English <i>neener</i> (playground insult), Vulgar Latin <i>&#42;ninnus</i> (“child”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mustard</li></ol><p>Probably a loan from Demotic Egyptian <i>snwpt</i>; compare <i>σίναπι</i> (sínapi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>νῆα</i> (nêa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is spun: thread, yarn</li><li>silk</li><li>strand of a spider's web</li></ol><p>From <i>νέω</i> (néō, “to spin”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>island</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Has traditionally been connected with <i>νήχω</i> (nḗkhō, “to swim”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)neh₂-</i> (“to flow, to swim”). If its meaning “island” has developed from a more original meaning “headland, cape”, it may be, like Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nasją</i> (“foothill; headland, cape”) and Bulgarian <i>нос</i> (nos, “nose; cape, promontory”), a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;néh₂s</i> (“nose”); in more landsman environments, compare the developments of Minaean <i>𐩱𐩬𐩰</i> (ʾnf, “wall of a city”) from “nose” and Arabic جَزِيرَة (jazīra, “island”) apparently from “bridge”.</p><p>However, in that case, the origin of <i>-s-</i>, which is not usually preserved in Greek, remains to be explained, possibly requiring a borrowing from another Indo-European language (though Furnée suspects a Pre-Greek origin, and Beekes Aegean).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fasting (not eating)</li><li>hungry</li><li>causing hunger</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) jejunum (middle part of small intestine)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-h₁d-tis</i> (literally “having no eating”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>նօթի</i> (nōtʻi, “hungry”). By surface analysis, <i>νη-</i> (nē-) +&lrm; <i>ἔδω</i> (édō) +&lrm; <i>-τις</i> (-tis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spindle, used for spinning fibres</li></ol><p>From <i>νέω</i> (néō, “to spin”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a duck</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;nāťťa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂énh₂ts</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sobriety, soberness, temperance</li><li>wakefulness, alertness</li></ol><p>From <i>νήφω</i> (nḗphō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nu, the name for the thirteenth letter Ν (N), ν (n) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤍</i> (n‬ /⁠nūn⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) now, at this time</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the recent past</i>) just now</li><li>(<i>constrasting what might have been</i>) as it is, as it stands</li></ol><li>(not of time,&#32;often enclitic)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>used to strengthen or hasten a command, call, etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>used to strengthen a question</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;nū</i> (“now”). Cognate with Lithuanian <i>nù</i> (“now, well now”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nyně</i> (“now”), Latin <i>num</i> (“now”), Latin <i>nunc</i> (“now, soon, today, nowadays”), Sanskrit <i>नु, नू</i> (nu, nū, “now”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;nu</i> (English <i>now</i>), Classical Persian نون (nūn), Northern Kurdish <i>niha</i> and Albanian <i>nu</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>νώ</i> (nṓ, “we two”)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Νῶε)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Noah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>נֹחַ</i> (Nōaḥ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lethargy, deep sleep</li></ol><p>Frisk includes this word under the derivatives of the root <i>&#42;neḱ-</i> (“perish, disappear”), but a long vowel is entirely unmotivated in this formation. On the other hand, Furnée connects it with <i>νωχελής</i> (nōkhelḗs, “slow, sluggish, dull”), which fits well semantically. The ending <i>-αρ</i> (-ar) is typical of Pre-Greek words, like <i>ἐλίμαρ</i> (elímar), <i>κύδαρ</i> (kúdar) and <i>σῦφᾰρ</i> (sûphăr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><sup>?</sup> flashing, shining, bright (epithet of copper)</li></ol><p>Comparison with <i>νωρεῖ</i> (nōreî, “is active”) and Lithuanian <i>norėti</i> (“to want”) is perhaps possible, but gratuitous. The suffix <i>-οπ-</i> may point to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>back (the rear part of the body)</li><li>chine of an animal served as food</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) any wide surface, especially of the sea</li><li>ridge of a hill, rock, saw</li><li>back of a page</li></ol><p>There are no obvious cognates. If connected with Latin <i>natis</i> (“rump”), it can be either assumed a derivation from an old Proto-Indo-European root noun <i>&#42;n(e)h₃t-</i> or a root <i>&#42;nh₂t-</i>, which became thematicized in Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>νώ</i> (nṓ, “we two”)</li></ol><p>Dunkel derives the <i>-ϊ</i> (-ï) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;íh₁</i> (emphatic particle).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fabulous sea monster of the Atlantic</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of pale-coloured stone</li></ol><p>From <i>ξανθός</i> (xanthós, “yellow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cocklebur, rough cocklebur (<i>Xanthium strumarium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ξανθός</i> (xanthós, “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix), because it was used for dyeing the hair yellow.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>card for combing wool</li></ol><p>From <i>ξαίνω</i> (xaínō, “to card”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female stranger, foreigner</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign</li><li>strange, unusual</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>of parties giving or receiving hospitality: host and much more commonly guest</li><li>stranger</li><li>one who is hired: hired worker, mercenary</li><li>foreigner</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ξένϝος</i> (xénwos), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ksénwos</i>, which Beekes tentatively considers Pre-Greek; he also mentions attempts to connect the term to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰóstis</i> (“guest”). However, compare Avestan <i>𐬑𐬱𐬥𐬀𐬎</i> (xšnau), as well as Albanian <i>huaj</i> (“alien”), from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;ksōn(w)ja</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) a gift or food given to a guest by a host</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to hospitality</li></ol><p><i>ξένος</i> (xénos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “stranger, guest”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign</li><li>strange, unusual</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>of parties giving or receiving hospitality: host and much more commonly guest</li><li>stranger</li><li>one who is hired: hired worker, mercenary</li><li>foreigner</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ξένϝος</i> (xénwos), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ksénwos</i>, which Beekes tentatively considers Pre-Greek; he also mentions attempts to connect the term to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰóstis</i> (“guest”). However, compare Avestan <i>𐬑𐬱𐬥𐬀𐬎</i> (xšnau), as well as Albanian <i>huaj</i> (“alien”), from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;ksōn(w)ja</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planing</li></ol><p>From <i>ξέω</i> (xéō, “to scrape”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth or polish by scraping, planing, filing etc.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kséhō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kes-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) rivers that go on forever</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested in Hesychius. Likely corrupted.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword, the short, straight, double-edged sword of the Iron Age and Classical Antiquity.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the sword-shaped bone of the cuttlefish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>swordfish</li></ol><li>corn-flag (<i>Gladiolus italicus</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>Already in Late Mycenaean Greek (Ta-716 from Pylos), attested in the dual <i>𐀥𐀯𐀟𐀁</i> (qi-si-pe-e, “two swords”) (mostly ideographically as <i>𐃉</i>). Probably of Pre-Greek origin, related to and perhaps borrowed from Egyptian <i>zft</i> (“sword, knife”). If the Greek isn't borrowed directly from the Egyptian, perhaps both are from a Semitic word related to Ge'ez <i>ሰይፍ</i> (säyf), Arabic سَيْف (sayf), and Hebrew <i>סַיִף</i> (sayip̄) or from a Libyan or "Sea Peoples" word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb or card (<i>wool</i>)</li><li>to full or dress (<i>cloth</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to dress, thrash, beat, mangle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ksəňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ksn̥-y<sup>e</sup>⁄o-</i> (“to scratch; to comb, card”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blondness; xanthism; the property of being colored or dyed yellow or blond.</li></ol><p>From <i>ξᾰνθός</i> (xănthós, “yellow; blond”) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale yellow</li></ol><p>From <i>ξᾰνθός</i> (xănthós, “yellow”) +&lrm; <i>λευκός</i> (leukós, “white”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yellow (of various shades), golden</li><li>fair, blond, flaxen, tawny, a golden-red fawn (of hair)</li></ol><p>Unknown. There are at least two possibilities:</p><p>Moreover, the often asserted connection with Etruscan <i>𐌆𐌀𐌌𐌈𐌉𐌂</i> (zamθic, “golden”) is probably spurious.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ξένιον</i> (xénion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ξένιος</i> (xénios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ξενίζω</i> (xenízō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ξενοκτονέω</i> (xenoktonéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hospitality, hospitable reception, entertainment</li><li>guest room</li><li>friendly relations</li><li>the state/rights of a foreigner</li><li>the idea that all strangers have a right to a certain gift, whether it is food, gold, entertainment or a place to stay</li></ol><p>From <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “stranger, guest”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to receive or entertain strangers, to receive as a guest</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to astonish</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lodge (<i>guests</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to think strange</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be a stranger, speak with a foreign accent</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be strange or unusual</li></ol><p>From <i>ξένος</i> (xénos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xenelasia, expulsion of foreigners</li></ol><p>Either:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inn, place for strangers to lodge in</li></ol><p>From <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “foreign”) and <i>δοχεῖον</i> (dokheîon, “holder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slay guests or strangers</li></ol><p>From <i>ξενοκτόνος</i> (xenoktónos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slaying guests or strangers</li></ol><p>From <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “foreign, guest”) +&lrm; <i>-κτόνος</i> (-któnos). By surface analysis, <i>ξένος</i> (xénos) +&lrm; <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō, “to kill”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magistrate charged with the protection of foreigners</li></ol><p>From <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “foreigner”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smoothed, polished, wrought</li></ol><p>From <i>ξέω</i> (xéō) (from earlier <i>&#42;kses-</i>) + <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xi, the name for the fourteenth letter Ξ (X), ξ (x) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Shape comes from Phoenician <i>𐤎</i> (s‬). For name, compare φεῖ (pheî), χεῖ (kheî), ψεῖ (pseî).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth or polish by scraping, planing, filing etc.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kséhō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kes-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ξένος</i> (xénos)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ξένος</i> (xénos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) squared trunk of a tree, block</li></ol><p>Has been connected with <i>ἐπίξηνον</i> (epíxēnon, “chopping-block”), but further details are obscure.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a desiccative powder for wounds</li></ol><p>Diminutive from <i>ξηρός</i> (xērós, “dry”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), ellipsis of <i>ξηρίον φάρμακον</i> (xēríon phármakon, literally “dry powder medicine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a desiccative powder for wounds</li></ol><p>From <i>ξηρός</i> (xērós, “dry”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion), or ellipsis of <i>ξηρίον φάρμακον</i> (xēríon phármakon, literally “dry powder medicine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to parch, dry up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become dry, dried up, parched</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be withered</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ξηρός</i> (xērós) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drying place (<i>for fruits</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ξηραίνω</i> (xēraínō) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>dry land</li></ol><li>The effects of being without water: parched, withered</li><li>By extension: shrunken, paralyzed, lean</li><li>(<i>voice</i>) rough, hoarse</li><li>fasting</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kseros</i> (“dry”). Cognates include Latin <i>serescō</i>, Old High German <i>serawēn</i>, Old Armenian <i>չոր</i> (čʻor), Middle Armenian <i>չիր</i> (čʻir), and perhaps Sanskrit <i>क्षार</i> (kṣārá, “sharp, acrid, pungent”). However, the lengthened vowel η where ε would normally be expected is problematic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swordfish (<i>Xiphias gladius</i>)</li><li>type of comet</li></ol><p>From <i>ξῐ́φος</i> (xĭ́phos, “sword”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās, “nominal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sickle-shaped sword, sort of scimitar</li></ol><p>From <i>ξῐ́φος</i> (xĭ́phos, “sword”) +&lrm; <i>δρέπᾰνον</i> (drépănon, “sickle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scabbard</li></ol><p>From <i>ξίφος</i> (xíphos, “sword”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sabre</li></ol><p>From <i>ξῐ́φος</i> (xĭ́phos, “sword”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χαιρᾰ</i> (mắkhairă, “large knife”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nimble, rapid</li><li>rustling, whirring</li><li>chirruping, twittering, trilling</li><li>golden yellow</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whittle, used in shaping a javelin</li><li>dagger similar to a scythe</li></ol><p>From <i>ξύω</i> (xúō, “to carve”).</p></html>:/ksiŋ/
<h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠγ-</i> (sŭg-)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγγένεια</i> (sungéneia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγγενής</i> (sungenḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγγνώμη</i> (sungnṓmē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγγρᾰ́φω</i> (sungrắphō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγκαθαιρέω</i> (sunkathairéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγκινδῡνεύω</i> (sunkindūneúō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγκρούω</i> (sunkroúō, “to strike together”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγχωρέω</i> (sunkhōréō)</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō, “to withdraw, give way”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγχωροῦσι</i> (sunkhōroûsi)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγχωρέω</i> (sunkhōréō)</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō, “to withdraw, give way”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγχωροῦσιν</i> (sunkhōroûsin)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συγχωρέω</i> (sunkhōréō)</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō, “to withdraw, give way”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agalloch (<i>Aquilaria malaccensis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ξῠ́λον</i> (xŭ́lon, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓λόη</i> (ălóē, “aloe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sowing, planting (of green crops or fodder)</li></ol><p>From <i>ξῠλᾰμᾰ́ω</i> (xŭlămắō, “to plant, sow”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felling and carrying of wood</li><li>supply of wood</li><li>timber, lumber</li></ol><p>From <i>ξύλεύομαι</i> (xúleúomai, “I cut wood”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a timber monopoly</li></ol><p>Substantive of <i>ξῠλῐκός</i> (xŭlĭkós, “wooden”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of wood, wooden</li><li>(with <b>καρπός</b>) produce of trees</li><li>(with <b>ὕλη</b>) timber</li></ol><p><i>ξύλον</i> (xúlon, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συλλαμβάνω</i> (sullambánō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>wood, wooden</li></ol><p>Prefix variant of the noun <i>ξῠ́λον</i> (xŭ́lon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A woodhouse</li></ol><p>From <i>ξύλον</i> (xúlon, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hewing, felling wood</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumberjack, woodcutter, feller</li><li>pecking wood, of the woodpecker</li></ol><p>From <i>ξῠλο-</i> (xŭlo-, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>κόπος</i> (kópos, “striking”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xylospongium (a Greek and Roman utensil consisting of a sea sponge fixed to the end of a wooden stick, typically thought to have been used for anal hygiene or as a toilet brush)</li></ol><p>From <i>ξῠλόσπογγος</i> (xŭlóspongos, “xylospongium”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix). By surface analysis, <i>ξῠλο-</i> (xŭlo-) +&lrm; <i>σπογγῐ́ον</i> (spongĭ́on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having wood showing from its surface.</li><li>Resembling wood.</li></ol><p><i>ξῠλο-</i> (xŭlo-, “wood, timber”) +&lrm; <i>-φᾰνής</i> (-phănḗs, “-looking”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xylospongium (a Greek and Roman utensil consisting of a sea sponge fixed to the end of a wooden stick, typically thought to have been used for anal hygiene or as a toilet brush)</li></ol><p>From <i>ξῠλο-</i> (xŭlo-) +&lrm; <i>σπόγγος</i> (spóngos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠμβαίνω</i> (sŭmbaínō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμβουλεύω</i> (sumbouleúō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμβόλαιος</i> (sumbólaios, “contractual”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμμένω</i> (summénō, “to hold together, keep together”)</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>μένω</i> (ménō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Attic</i>) synonym of <i>ξῠμμείγνῡμῐ</i> (xŭmmeígnūmĭ, “to mix together”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ξῠν-</i> (xŭn-) +&lrm; <i>μῐ́σγω</i> (mĭ́sgō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμμαχέω</i> (summakhéō, “to be an ally, to be in alliance”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠμμᾰχῐ́ᾱ</i> (sŭmmăkhĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμμαχίς</i> (summakhís, “allied”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σῠμμᾰχῐκός</i> (sŭmmăkhĭkós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠμμείγνῡμῐ</i> (sŭmmeígnūmĭ, “mix together”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμπῑ́πτω</i> (sumpī́ptō, “to fall together”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμπλέω</i> (sumpléō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμπολεμέω</i> (sumpoleméō, “to join in war”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμπροπέμπω</i> (sumpropémpō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συμφέρω</i> (sumphérō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠμφορᾱ́</i> (sŭmphorā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ξύμφορος</i> (xúmphoros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συν-</i> (sun-)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠνᾰ́γω</i> (sŭnắgō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνάρχω</i> (sunárkhō, “to rule or govern jointly”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνέπομαι</i> (sunépomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνέρχομαι</i> (sunérkhomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠνήθης</i> (sŭnḗthēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνίημι</i> (suníēmi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνίστημι</i> (sunístēmi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συναδικέω</i> (sunadikéō, “to join in wrong or injury”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συναιρέω</i> (sunairéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συναλλάσσω</i> (sunallássō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συναφίστημι</i> (sunaphístēmi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Old Attic</i>) accomplice</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠνεξέρχομαι</i> (sŭnexérkhomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνεπιτίθημι</i> (sunepitíthēmi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠνετέω</i> (sŭnetéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνετός</i> (sunetós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ξυνετός</i> (xunetós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῡνεχῶς</i> (sūnekhôs, “continuously”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ξῠνήθης</i> (xŭnḗthēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνθάπτω</i> (suntháptō, “to bury together (with)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνθήκη</i> (sunthḗkē, “compounding; convention; coffin”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνναυμαχέω</i> (sunnaumakhéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνοικίζω</i> (sunoikízō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συντίθημι</i> (suntíthēmi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συντυχίᾱ</i> (suntukhíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συνόμνῡμι</i> (sunómnūmi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>κοινός</i> (koinós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surgical knife</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) razor (shaving knife)</li></ol><p>From <i>ξυρόν</i> (xurón).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shave</li></ol><p><i>ξυρόν</i> (xurón, “razor”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “denominal verb suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gladen (<i>Iris foetidissima</i>)</li></ol><p>The alternative forms <i>σίρις</i> (síris) and <i>σῖρις</i> (sîris) suggest that the older form was <i>ξίρις</i> (xíris). The variants point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>razor-case</li></ol><p>From <i>ξυρόν</i> (xurón, “razor”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συρρήγνῡμι</i> (surrhḗgnūmi, “to dash together”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>razor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ksu-ró-s</i> (“razor”), from <i>&#42;ksew-</i> (“to scrape, sharpen, shave”). Exact cognate with Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;kšurás</i> (“dagger, razor”); see also the verbal formation from the same root <i>ξύω</i> (xúō, “to scrape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συσκοτάζω</i> (suskotázō, “to grow quite dark”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>συσσῴζω</i> (sussōízō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>robe of rich and soft material reaching to the feet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(worn by women of quality)</li><li>(worn by great men, esp. by victorious charioteers in their chariots, as a robe of state)</li><li>(worn by tragic heroes)</li></ol><li>scraper</li></ol><p>From <i>ξυστός</i> (xustós, “shaved, whittled”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), from <i>ξύω</i> (xúō, “scratch, scrape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>xyston</li></ol><p>Unknown. Attested in Hesychius.</p><p>Potentially from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰasto-</i> or <i>&#42;ǵʰasdʰo-</i> (“branch ~ spear, sharp spine”) (whence also <i>gad</i>, <i>goad</i>, and <i>yard</i>), but this is likely a substrate word in view of the various irregularities.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaved, whittled with a knife or plane</li><li>scraped, shredded, grated</li><li>trimmed, cropped with scissors</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>walking-place in the grounds of a private residence</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) covered colonnade in a gymnasium, for winter exercise</li><li>meeting of athletes from various places to compete in sports</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ξύω</i> (xúō, “to scrape, plane”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p><p>From the adjective, in the sense of "smoothened floor".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an image of carved wood, an image, statue, especially of a god; xoanon</li><li>a musical instrument</li></ol><p>From <i>ξύω</i> (xúō, “to carve, smooth”). Compare <i>ξύλον</i> (xúlon, “wood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σύγκειμαι</i> (súnkeimai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of wood, wooden</li></ol><p>From <i>ξύλον</i> (xúlon, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σύλλογος</i> (súllogos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in plural</i>) cut wood, timber</li><li>piece of wood, log, beam, post</li><li>cudgel, club, stick</li><li>an instrument of punishment: wooden collar, stocks, gallows, stake</li><li>bench, table</li><li>tree</li><li>blockhead</li><li>a measure of length: 3 or 2<s style="display:none">+</s><small><sup>2</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>3</sub></small> cubits</li></ol><p>Seemingly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ksewlo-</i>, which is traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kes-</i> (“to scrape, comb”) or from <i>&#42;ḱes-</i> (“to cut”), and compared with Lithuanian <i>šùlas</i> (“post, pole, stave”), Russian <i>шу́ло</i> (šúlo, “garden-pole”) (from Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šulo</i>), Serbo-Croatian <i>šȗlj</i> (“block”), Old High German <i>sūl</i> (“style, pole”), Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌻𐍃</i> (sauls, “pillar”).</p><p>However, Beekes is skeptical of ultimate Indo-European origin, and suspects the Greek, Balto-Slavic, and Germanic terms to be borrowed from a common substrate source. See also <i>ξύω</i> (xúō, “to scrape, polish”), <i>ξόανον</i> (xóanon, “wooden image”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠ́μβᾰσῐς</i> (sŭ́mbăsĭs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>σῠ́μβουλος</i> (sŭ́mboulos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠ́μμᾰχος</i> (sŭ́mmăkhos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic and Epic form of <i>σῠ́μπᾱς</i> (sŭ́mpās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σύμφορος</i> (súmphoros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σῠ́ν</i> (sŭ́n)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σύνεσις</i> (súnesis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>σύνοιδα</i> (súnoida)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scraper, used after bathing</li><li>strigil</li></ol><p>From <i>ξύω</i> (xúō, “to shave, to smooth”) +&lrm; <i>-τρα</i> (-tra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrape, scratch</li><li>to plane, smooth, polish</li><li>to make smooth or fine, work finely or delicately</li><li>to engrave</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ksuyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ksuyéti</i>, from <i>&#42;ksew-</i> (“to scrape, shave, scratch”). Cognate with <i>ξέω</i> (xéō), <i>ξαίνω</i> (xaínō), Latin <i>novācula</i>, <i>saucius</i>, and many others.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ξεῖ</i> (xeî, “xi”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) To be made friendly, be closely united</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκειόω</i> (oikeióō, “to make one's own”) +&lrm; <i>-ομαι</i> (-omai, “a suffix forming passive verbs”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>οἴᾱξ</i> (oíāx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to swell, make swell</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be swollen</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eyd-</i> (“to swell”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swell, become swollen</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) of inflated style</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂oyd-éye-ti</i>, composed of an o-grade of the root <i>&#42;h₂eyd-</i> and causative/iterative present suffix <i>&#42;-éyeti</i>. Cognates include Old Armenian <i>այտ</i> (ayt, “cheek”), <i>այտնում</i> (aytnum, “to swell”), Latin <i>aemidus</i>, Old English <i>āte</i> (English <i>oat</i>). Compare <i>Οἰδίπους</i> (Oidípous).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>miserable</li><li>causing misery</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰζῡ́ς</i> (oizū́s, “misery”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woe; misery; distress; hardship; suffering</li></ol><p>From <i>οἴζω</i> (oízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>household slave</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) household</li></ol><p><i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō, “live; manage, direct”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to inhabit</li><li>to colonize, settle in</li><li>to manage, direct, govern</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to dwell, reside, live</li><li>(<i>of cities</i>) to be situated</li><li>to be governed</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant</li></ol><p><i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>οἰκεῖος</i> (oikeîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οἰκία</i> (oikía)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house, dwelling, lair</li><li>household, domestic affairs</li><li>household, family</li><li>house, extended family, tribe</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(plural only&#32;in&#32;Epic,&#32;also singular in the later language) habitation, abode</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intimate, familiar</li><li>domestic</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “home, dwelling”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos, “related to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle (<i>a colony</i>), colonize</li></ol><p><i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “home”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, “a suffix forming a verb from a noun”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>οἰκῐ́ᾱ</i> (oikĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(plural only&#32;in&#32;Epic,&#32;also singular in the later language) habitation, abode</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small room or chamber</li><li>cage for birds</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>οἰκεῖος</i> (oikeîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kindred, relationship</li><li>intimacy, friendship</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκεῖος</i> (oikeîos, “familial, related; familiar, intimate”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make one's own, claim as one's own, appropriate</li><li>to make someone one's friend</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to win someone's favor or affection, conciliate</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be made friendly, be closely united</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to entrust</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκεῖος</i> (oikeîos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>inhabit<br /></li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκέτης</i> (oikétēs, “household slave”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in the house, at home</li><li>of the house, domestic</li><li>of the same household; familial, related</li><li>familiar, intimate</li><li>friendly</li><li>belonging to one's house or family</li><li>belonging to oneself, personal, private</li><li>proper, fitting, suitable</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) belonging to</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) domiciliary</li><li>(<i>Stoicism</i>) endeared by nature</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>domicile, dwelling, abode</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō, “to inhabit”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accustomed to a fixed dwelling</li><li>used as or suitable for a residence</li></ol><p>From <i>οἴκησις</i> (oíkēsis, “dwelling, inhabiting”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inhabited, habitable</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō, “inhabit, dwell”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, “A suffix creating verbal adjectives of possibility, either active or passive”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>οἰκειότης</i> (oikeiótēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founder of a colony, colonizer, settler</li></ol><p><i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos) +&lrm; <i>-ιστής</i> (-istḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of slaves</i>) born in the house, homebred</li></ol><p><i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “a house”) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs, “born in”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master or steward of a house</li><li>native ruler (opposite to foreign emperor)</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) dominant planet or otherwise owner of a domicile</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “household”) +&lrm; <i>δεσπότης</i> (despótēs, “master, ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build a house, build</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to build or found upon</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to build up, edify, embolden</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκοδόμος</i> (oikodómos, “builder, architect”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>builder, architect</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “home, dwelling”) +&lrm; <i>δέμω</i> (démō, “build”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to manage as a house steward, order, regulate</li><li>to dispense, disburse</li><li>to treat (<i>one substance with another</i>)</li><li>to administer</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκονόμος</i> (oikonómos, “steward”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “Suffix used to form verbs from nouns, adjectives and other verbs.”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the management of a household or family, husbandry</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a state</i>) administration, management</li><li>(<i>of a poem</i>) arrangement</li></ol><li>the public revenue of a state</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκονόμος</i> (oikonómos, “manager”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos) +&lrm; <i>νέμω</i> (némō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>practised in the management of a household or family</li><li>thrifty, frugal, sparing, economical</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκονόμος</i> (oikonómos, “master of the house”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who manages a household, the master of the house</li><li>a steward of an estate</li><li>a manager, administrator</li><li>superintendent of the public finances, the treasurer of a city</li><li>(biblical,&#32;Christianity) the apostles and other Christian teachers and bishops and overseers</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house, home, estate”) + <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law, rule, authority”), equivalent to <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos) +&lrm; <i>νέμω</i> (némō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>domestic tyrant</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house; home; estate”) +&lrm; <i>τῠ́ρᾰννος</i> (tŭ́rănnos, “tyrant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabited region</li><li>the inhabited world, particularly the region known to the ancient Greeks: (sub-polar) Europe, (western) Asia, and (northern and Saharan) Africa</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) the civilized world, particularly the land of the Roman or Byzantine Empire</li></ol><p>Present passive participle of <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō, “I inhabit, dwell”). The noun use is an ellipsis of <i>οἰκουμένη γῆ</i> (oikouménē gê, “inhabited earth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>from or open to the whole world</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκουμένη</i> (oikouménē, “the inhabited world”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós), from <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō, “I inhabit, dwell”), from <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squander one's substance</li><li>to lose one's fortune, be ruined</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκοφθόρος</i> (oikophthóros, “seducer, adulterer”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō), from οἶκος (oîkos) + φθείρω (phtheírō) + -ος (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house-guard</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>pitifully; miserably</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pitiable, pitiful, lamentable, miserable</li><li>wailing piteously</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκτος</i> (oîktos) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pitiable, pitiful, lamentable, miserable</li><li>wailing piteously</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκτος</i> (oîktos) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wailing, lamentation, cry of grief</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰμώζω</i> (oimṓzō) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wail aloud, lament</li><li>to pity</li></ol><p>From <i>οἴμοι</i> (oímoi, “woe's me”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) inflorescence of the grapevine</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) vine</li><li>dropwort (<i>Filipendula vulgaris</i>)</li><li>northern wheatear (<i>Oenanthe oenanthe</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>οἴνη</i> (oínē, “vine”) and <i>ἄνθη</i> (ánthē, “blossom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made with the blossom of the grapevine</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰνάνθη</i> (oinánthē, “blossom of the grapevine”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of wine, vinous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild pigeon (<i>Columba livia</i>)</li><li>bryony</li></ol><p><i>οἶνος</i> (oînos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to wine; vinous</li><li>steeped in wine</li><li>containing wine</li><li>rich in wine (of countries)</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A wine cellar</li><li>A wine cask</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>officer who had charge of the municipal wines</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel for taking wine from the krater and pouring it into the cups</li><li>kind of sideboard to range the wine-cups on</li><li>female cupbearer</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cupbearer</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine cellar</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrow of a plough</li></ol><p>Schulze compare the word with Sanskrit <i>सीता</i> (sītā, “furrow”), <i>सीर</i> (sīra, “plough”) and <i>सीमा</i> (sīmā, “frontier, boundary, edge”), which is semantically and formally quite possible. Beekes suggests a common derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;soHi-r-</i> (“furrow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oesophagus, gullet</li></ol><p>From <i>οἴσω</i> (oísō, “I will bear”) (future infinitive of <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring, bear”)) + <i>ἔφαγον</i> (éphagon, “I ate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lanolin, grease extracted from sheep's wool</li></ol><p>Explained as "ὀϝι-σύπη", like the synonym <i>οἰσπώτη</i> (oispṓtē), with an unclear second member. There is clear evidence for <i>οἴσπη</i> (oíspē, “sheep dung”), but it is difficult to decide whether the form with "υ" or the form without it is secondary. According to Beekes, the word is undoubtedly from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of withy</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶσος</i> (oîsos, “withy”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gone, absent</li><li>undone, ruined</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) dead</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place for watching the flight of birds</li><li>omen or token</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰωνῐ́ζομαι</i> (oiōnĭ́zomai, “to take omens from the flight of birds”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or concerning augury</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰωνοσκόπος</i> (oiōnoskópos, “watching birds and making prophecies from them”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós), from <i>οἰωνός</i> (oiōnós, “bird of prey”) + <i>σκοπέω</i> (skopéō, “to view”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>augur, one who foretells from the flight and cry of birds</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰωνός</i> (oiōnós, “bird used in augury”) +&lrm; <i>σκοπέω</i> (skopéō, “to look, contemplate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who takes omens from the flight and cries of birds, augur</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰωνός</i> (oiōnós, “bird used in ornithomancy”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́ντῐς</i> (mắntĭs, “seer, oracle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large bird, bird of prey</li><li>bird used in augury/ornithomancy</li><li>omen, token, presage</li></ol><p>Explanation debated. Because of the comparable formation in <i>υἱωνός</i> (huiōnós, “grandson”) from <i>υἱός</i> (huiós, “son”), it is probably best derived from a nominal basis. Therefore, it can be derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éwis</i> (“bird”), like Latin <i>avis</i> (“bird”), Sanskrit <i>वि</i> (vi, “bird”), Avestan <i>𐬬𐬍𐬱</i> (vīš, “bird”) and also <i>ἀετός</i> (aetós, “eagle”). The initial <i>ὀ-</i> has been explained as a case of vowel assimilation since Schmidt, but this is unnecessary: one may assume an <i>o-</i>grade, as does Beekes, who departs from a nominative <i>&#42;h₂ow-i-ō(n)</i> that was subsequently thematicized. By others, it has been combined with <i>οἶμα</i> (oîma, “spring, rush, swoop”), <i>οἶστρος</i> (oîstros, “gadfly; zeal”), <i>οἰστός</i> (oistós, “endurable”) and connected with the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eys-</i> (“to propel”), but this is doubtful. The connection with <i>ᾠόν</i> (ōión, “egg”) by Schmeja was defended by Peters.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>woe (expressing pain or grief)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wáy</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiller, the handle of the rudder</li><li>helm</li><li>rings of a yoke, through which cords for guiding oxen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂oyHseh₂</i> (“shaft”) or similar. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>ईषा</i> (īṣā́) and Hittite <i>𒄭𒅖𒊭𒀭</i> (ḫi-iš-ša-an).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Οἴαξ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Oeax</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>οἴγω</i> (oígō, “to open”)</li></ol><p><i>οἴγω</i> (oígō) +&lrm; <i>-νῡμι</i> (-nūmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li></ol><p>Per Forssman, who reconstructs an ablauting root <i>&#42;ὀϝειγ-</i> (&#42;oweig-)/<i>&#42;ὀϝιγ-</i> (&#42;owig-), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃wóyg-os</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃weyg-</i> (“to give way, bend”), and cognate with Sanskrit <i>विज्</i> (vij, “to shake, tremble”) and <i>वेग</i> (vega, “violent movement, momentum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>oncology</i>) swelling, tumour</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰδέω</i> (oidéō, “to swell”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wretched; miserable</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰζῡ́ς</i> (oizū́s) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οἴκαδε</i> (oíkade)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>home (homewards)</li></ol><p><i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling-place, home, building</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>room, chamber</li></ol><li>brothel</li><li>temple, shrine</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>room in a temple</li></ol><li>prison; cell</li><li>storeroom</li><li>workshop</li><li>story, floor</li><li>cage for animals</li><li>(a horse’s) stable, stall</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō, “I inhabit”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of dwelling or inhabiting</li><li>(<i>of a city</i>) management, administration</li><li>house, dwelling</li><li>inhabited district</li><li>family, household</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō, “to inhabit”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from home</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at home</li></ol><p>Fossilized locative of <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οἰκτρᾰ́</i> (oiktrắ), <i>οἰκτρᾱ́</i> (oiktrā́), and <i>οἰκτρέ</i> (oiktré)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οἰκτρᾰ́</i> (oiktrắ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>woe is me</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>οἴ</i> (oí, “woe!”) +&lrm; <i>μοι</i> (moi, “to me”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) A vine leaf or tendril</li><li>A vine</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old name for the vine</li><li>ace on dice</li></ol><p>From the same Proto-Indo-European root of <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;óynos</i> (“one, single”). Cognates include <i>οἶος</i> (oîos), Latin <i>ūnus</i>, Sanskrit <i>एक</i> (éka), Old Church Slavonic <i>ѥдинъ</i> (jedinŭ), Old Irish <i>óen</i>, and Old English <i>ān</i> (English <i>one</i> and <i>an</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of wine</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anticipate or to conclude likely with less than complete evidence: to conjecture, to guess or to surmise, in a variety of significations</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to consider certain or highly probable: to expect, to look to, to look for</li><li>to have a presentiment of something: to forebode, to foretell, to predict, to presage</li><li>to look for a contingency, either optimistically (to hope) or pessimistically (to fear)</li><li>to express full persuasion, either modestly or ironically: to think, to suppose</li><li>(<i>when the event rests with oneself</i>): to have in mind as a goal or motive for action: to aim, to mean, to intend, to purport, to purpose, to propose</li><li>to conceive, to deem, to imagine, to suspect (<i>of an opinion or judgment</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>construction</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with accusative and infinitive,&#32;mostly with future infinitive)</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>sometimes the subject of the infinitive is to be supplied from the context</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>absolute</i>)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wait for, look for</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in many cases an infinitive must be supplied from the context</i>)</li></ol><li>(used parenthetically,&#32;mostly in the first-person)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>expressive of modesty or courtesy, to avoid over-great bluntness of assertion</i>)</li><li>(<i>answering a question, expressive of positive certainty</i>) to believe you, of course, no doubt</li></ol><li>(<i>peculiar Attic phrases</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>as a parenthetic question</i>) <i>πῶς <b>οἴει</b>, πῶς <b>οἴεσθε</b></i> ― <i>pôs <b>oíei</b>, pôs <b>oíesthe</b></i> ― how think you?</li><li><i><b>οἴομαι</b> δεῖν</i> ― <i><b>oíomai</b> deîn</i> ― to hold it necessary, think it one's duty (see δέω (déō))</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From earlier <i>ὀῑ̈́ομαι</i> (oī̈́omai) and <i>&#42;ὀϝῑ̈́ομαι</i> (&#42;owī̈́omai), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ew-</i> (“perceive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheep dung, especially the dirt that collects about the hinder parts of sheep and goats</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek source of <i>οἰσύπη</i> (oisúpē, “lanoline”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>οἰσύπη</i> (oisúpē, “lanolin”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(vulgar,&#32;transitive) to fuck (someone); to have sex with (someone)</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;intransitive) to fuck (have sex)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;oyépʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃yebʰ-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>यभति</i> (yábhati) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jebati</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have or be gone, absent, vanished</li><li>to be undone, ruined</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) to be dead</li></ol><p>Mediopassive form (with perfective meaning?) to Ancient Greek <i>οἰχνέω</i> (oikhnéō, “to go, come, walk, approach”), both connected to Armenian and Tocharian verbal forms (with meanings such as Old Armenian <i>իջանեմ</i> (iǰanem, “to come down”) and Tocharian B <i>yku</i> (“gone”)). Compare also Old Irish <i>óegi</i> (“guest”) and Lithuanian <i>eigà</i> (“course”); if these are all related, they point to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eygʰ-</i> (“to go”). This root in turn may possibly be an extension of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ey-</i> (“to go”), whence <i>εἶμι</i> (eîmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οἶδε</i> (oîde)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>οἶδᾰ</i> (oîdă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to know; to be acquainted with [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with neuter accusative plural of an adjective</i>): have a quality in one's heart</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be skilled in [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to know how to [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘do something’</small>]</li><li>(transitive,&#32;when main verb and participle have separate subjects) to know that [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone else’</small> <i>and</i> accusative participle <small>‘does something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive,&#32;when subject of main verb and subject of participle are the same) to know that [<i>with</i> nominative participle <small>‘one does something’</small>]</li><li>to know that (with accusative and then an indirect statement introduced by <i>ὅτι</i> (hóti) or <i>ὡς</i> (hōs))</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wóyde</i> (“to have seen; to know”), from <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see; to know”). Compare <i>εἶδον</i> (eîdon, “to see”) and <i>εἴδομαι</i> (eídomai, “to seem”), which originate from different aspectual forms of the same verbal root. Cognates include Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;věděti</i>, Old Armenian <i>գիտեմ</i> (gitem), Sanskrit <i>वेद॑</i> (véda), Latin <i>vīdī</i>, and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;witaną</i> (English <i>wit</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild fig</li></ol><p>From <i>οἰδέω</i> (oidéō, “to swell”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling place, settlement, house</li><li>room, chamber</li><li>meeting tent, meeting house, hall, monument</li><li>birdcage</li><li>estate, inheritance</li><li>reigning family</li></ol><p>From <i>ϝοῖκος</i> (woîkos), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wóikos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyḱ-</i> (“to enter, settle; settlement, homestead, village”). Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀺𐀒</i> (wo-ko /⁠wojkos⁠/, “home”), Latin <i>vīcus</i> (whence English <i>wick</i> (“village”)), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьsь</i> (“village”), Sanskrit <i>विश्</i> (víś) and <i>वे॒श</i> (veśá), Gothic <i>𐍅𐌴𐌹𐌷𐍃</i> (weihs, “village”), and Albanian <i>vis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pity, compassion</li><li>wailing</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to one's house, homewards</li></ol><p><i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de, “towards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>οἴομαι</i> (oíomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way, road, path</li><li>stripe; strip of land</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) the course or strain of a song</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine (alcoholic beverage)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wóinos</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀺𐀜</i> (wo-no)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéyh₁ō</i>; related to English <i>wine</i>, Latin <i>vīnum</i>, Old Armenian <i>գինի</i> (gini) etc.; according to the <i>Oxford Latin Dictionary</i>, "all probably from a common Mediterranean source". Perhaps instead from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wóyh₁nom</i>, possibly from the root <i>&#42;wey(H)-</i> (“to twist”), thus meaning "that of the vine"; and related to dialectal <i>ὑιήν</i> (huiḗn, “grapevine”), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wiHḗn</i>, which would be a derivation from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of the sea or cattle</i>) an epithet traditionally rendered as "wine-dark" and taken to mean wine-colored and hence dark, reddish (as the sea at sunset), bluish (like some wines), or purplish</li></ol><p>From <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”) +&lrm; <i>-οψ</i> (-ops) (connected to <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “eye; face”)).</p><p>In Mycenaean Greek Linear B, 𐀺𐀜𐀦𐀰 (wo-no-qo-so) (KN Ch 1015) and 𐀺𐀜𐀦𐀰𐀤 (wo-no-qo-so-qe) (KN Ch 897) (a name of a bull, perhaps connected to the use of bullshead rhytons to contain ritual wine) is taken to be the same term.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>only; single</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;óywos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;óywos</i> (“one, single”), compare <i>&#42;óynos</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Old Latin <i>oinos</i> (Latin <i>ūnus</i>), Sanskrit <i>एक</i> (éka) and Old English <i>ān</i> (English <i>one</i> and <i>an</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Epic,&#32;Ionic) sheep (either a ram or ewe)</li></ol><p>Contracted form of <i>ὄϊς</i> (óïs), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ówis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ówis</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>अवि</i> (ávi), Latin <i>ovis</i>, and Old English <i>eowu</i> (English <i>ewe</i>). Apparently a doublet of <i>κῶας</i> (kôas) via Anatolian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οἶσθᾰ</i> (oîsthă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the withy or the chaste tree</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weh₁y-</i> (“to twist, to twine”). Compare Ancient Greek <i>ῑ̓τέᾱ</i> (ītéā, “willow”), Latin <i>vītis</i> (“vine”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wiþiz</i> (“withe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gadfly, breese</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an insect which infests tuna</li><li>a small insectivorous bird, perhaps the willow warbler</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a sting, anything that drives mad</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>absolute</i>) the smart of pain, agony</li><li>any vehement desire, mad desire, insane passion</li><li>madness, frenzy</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eys-</i>, used to form words denoting passion; see also Latin <i>īra</i> (“anger”), Lithuanian <i>aistra</i> (“violent passion”), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬉𐬴𐬨𐬀</i> (aēṣ̌ma, “anger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fate, doom (<i>in negative sense</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. According to Beekes, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁óytos</i> (“oath”), whence also perhaps Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiþaz</i> (“oath”) and Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;oitos</i>. As an alternative to <i>&#42;h₁ey-</i> (“to go”) as the root, he compares Avestan <i>𐬀𐬉𐬙𐬀</i> (aēta, “punishment, guilt”) (which was identified by Bartolomae under an original meaning “part, share”) along with Ancient Greek <i>αἶσα</i> (aîsa, “destiny, fate”) and <i>αἴτιος</i> (aítios, “guilty, culpable”). Thus, this noun may rather derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂oy-to-</i> (“what has been granted”), which is semantically more convincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to where, whither</li><li>how, in what state</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in poets and Ionic Prose also neuter plural, to add force, like <i>ὡς</i> (hōs) and <i>ὥσπερ</i> (hṓsper): how</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in comparisons</i>) as, like, just as</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>as, like for instance</li></ol><li>like <i>ὡς</i> (hōs), <i>ἅτε</i> (háte), with a particle</li></ol></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>οἷος</i> (hoîos, “such”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>relative adjective of quality such as; what sort, manner, kind of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>introducing an exclamation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in an independent clause</i>) what a (great, terrible)...!</li><li>(<i>in a subordinate clause</i>) the (great, terrible) sort that</li></ol><li>containing a comparison, and sometimes an inference</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>in many Homeric expressions the omission of the antecedent clause is to be noticed</li><li>especially in Attic often stands for <i>ὅτι τοῖος&#47;τοία&#47;τοῖον</i> (hóti toîos&#47;toía&#47;toîon), so that the relative introduces the reason for the preceding statement</li><li>if it is to be intimated that the reason is self-evident, and the assertion is beyond doubt, then <i>δή</i> (dḗ) is added</li><li>but if the comparison or inference only denotes a general or doubtful resemblance, then Homer uses <i>οἷός τε</i> (hoîós te)</li><li>when a comparison involves a definition of time <i>οἷος ὅτε</i> (hoîos hóte) is used</li><li>many brief Attic expressions are also explained by the omission of the demonstrative pronoun before <i>οἷος</i> (hoîos)</li><li>never used like the adverb <i>οἷον</i> (hoîon) with a positive adjective</li></ol><li><i>οἷος</i> (hoîos) with an infinitive implies fitness or ability in or for a thing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>but this sense is commonly expressed by <i>οἷος τε</i> (hoîos te)</li><li>(<i>without infinitive</i>)</li></ol><li>the relative is in Attic often repeated in the same clause</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós, relative pronoun) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>relative adjective of quality such as; what sort, manner, kind of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>introducing an exclamation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in an independent clause</i>) what a (great, terrible)...!</li><li>(<i>in a subordinate clause</i>) the (great, terrible) sort that</li></ol><li>containing a comparison, and sometimes an inference</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>in many Homeric expressions the omission of the antecedent clause is to be noticed</li><li>especially in Attic often stands for <i>ὅτι τοῖος&#47;τοία&#47;τοῖον</i> (hóti toîos&#47;toía&#47;toîon), so that the relative introduces the reason for the preceding statement</li><li>if it is to be intimated that the reason is self-evident, and the assertion is beyond doubt, then <i>δή</i> (dḗ) is added</li><li>but if the comparison or inference only denotes a general or doubtful resemblance, then Homer uses <i>οἷός τε</i> (hoîós te)</li><li>when a comparison involves a definition of time <i>οἷος ὅτε</i> (hoîos hóte) is used</li><li>many brief Attic expressions are also explained by the omission of the demonstrative pronoun before <i>οἷος</i> (hoîos)</li><li>never used like the adverb <i>οἷον</i> (hoîon) with a positive adjective</li></ol><li><i>οἷος</i> (hoîos) with an infinitive implies fitness or ability in or for a thing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>but this sense is commonly expressed by <i>οἷος τε</i> (hoîos te)</li><li>(<i>without infinitive</i>)</li></ol><li>the relative is in Attic often repeated in the same clause</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós, relative pronoun) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>not (indicates negation)</li></ol><p>Possibly <i>οὐκί</i> (oukí, “not so, never”), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;oyuki</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(ne) h₂óyu kʷíd</i> (“(not) ever, (not) on your life”). Compare Sanskrit <i>उद्</i> (ud), Old Armenian <i>ոչ</i> (očʻ), and Albanian <i>as</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>exclamation of pain and anger</i>) ah, woe, alas</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared with the set Latin <i>vae</i>, Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌹</i> (wai), etc., from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wáy</i>, but it is not derivable from there through regular sound laws. Lowe argues against οὐαί being a transliteration of Biblical Hebrew <i>אוֹי</i> ('oy). Ultimately expressive.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οὐδέ</i> (oudé)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οὐδέ</i> (oudé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(mostly answering to <i>μέν</i> (mén)) but not</li><li>(<i>more often</i>) and not, nor</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>without a negative preceding</i>)</li><li>(<i>with a simple negative preceding</i>)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not even, not at all</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐ</i> (ou, “not”) + <i>δέ</i> (dé, “but”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in no way, not at all</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>no one, nobody, none, nothing, no</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐδέ</i> (oudé, “and not, not even”) + <i>εἷς</i> (heîs, “one”). Sometimes reanalysed as <i>οὐ</i> (ou, “no, not”) + <i>δείς</i> (deís, “no one, nothing”), as a result of the later variant, <i>οὐθείς</i> (outheís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>never</li></ol><p><i>οὐδέ</i> (oudé, “and not”) +&lrm; <i>ποτε</i> (pote, “at some time”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neither</li><li>neutral</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) neuter</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐδέ</i> (oudé) +&lrm; <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros). Compare <i>μηδέτερος</i> (mēdéteros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nowhere</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐδαμός</i> (oudamós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not at all</li></ol><p><i>οὐδαμός</i> (oudamós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>no one, nobody, none, nothing, no</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐδέ</i> (oudé, “and not, not even”) + <i>εἷς</i> (heîs, “one”). Sometimes reanalysed as <i>οὐ</i> (ou, “no, not”) + <i>δείς</i> (deís, “no one, nothing”), as a result of the later variant, <i>οὐθείς</i> (outheís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threshold</li><li>Homeric form of <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós) (<i>occurs once; lengthened for metrical purposes</i>)</li></ol><p>Unexplained. A basis <i>&#42;ὀδϝός</i> (&#42;odwós) enables connection with <i>οὖδας</i> (oûdas, “ground, soil”), but only if one accepts that this is a generalized Ionic form. In principle both words could be variants of the same Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>οὐ</i> (ou), used before a vowel with smooth breathing</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οὐκέτι</i> (oukéti)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>no more, no longer, no further, not now</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐκ</i> (ouk, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ἔτι</i> (éti, “yet, longer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>οὐ</i> (ou)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>therefore</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scar, wound scarred over</li></ol><p>From an archaic form *ϝουλή (woulē), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₃-</i> (“to hit”). Closest cognates are Latin <i>vulnus</i> (“wound”) and Irish <i>fuil</i> (“blood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley-corns, which were sprinkled on the head of the sacrificial victim</li></ol><p>The comparison with <i>ἔλυμος</i> (élumos, “millet”) and <i>ὄλυρα</i> (ólura, “spelt”) is hypothetical. In view of the gloss <i>ὄλπα</i> (ólpa), which Furnée connects with <i>ὄλβος</i> (ólbos, “happiness, bliss”) under an original meaning "nourishment, corn, grain", it is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throng of warriors</li><li>troop of cavalry</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i>, the same root of <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to press together”), with an <i>o</i>-grade root and suffixal <i>-αμ-</i>, which is found also in <i>ποταμός</i> (potamós) and <i>πλόκαμος</i> (plókamos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) barley that is poured (<i>over the head of a sacrificial victim</i>)</li></ol><p><i>οὐλαί</i> (oulaí, “barley”) +&lrm; <i>χυτός</i> (khutós, “poured”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;poetic) accursed, i.e. one of or to whom the word <i>ὄλοιτο</i> (óloito, “may he/she/it perish”) may be used</li><li>ruined, wretched</li></ol><p>Participle of <i>ὀλέσθαι</i> (olésthai), thematic aorist middle of <i>ὄλλῡμι</i> (óllūmi, “I destroy”). The first vowel is lengthened to fit hexametric meter (a common practice).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ὀνομαίνω</i> (onomaínō)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁nómn̥</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃nómn̥yoh₂</i> (“am naming”). By surface analysis, <i>οὔνομα</i> (oúnoma) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the tail (of a lion, dog, etc.)</li><li>(<i>of an army marching</i>) the rear-guard, rear</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ers-</i> (“tail, butt”) (alternatively reconstructed as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃érsos</i> (“backside, buttocks”)), and related to <i>ὄρρος</i> (órrhos, “butt, ass”); see there for more. The connection with <i>οὐραχός</i> (ourakhós, “urachus”), while semantically sound, is phonetically unclear, due to the latter's strange suffix <i>-αχός</i> (-akhós), which has led Beekes to consider a possible Pre-Greek origin for the words (though he still seems to favor Indo-European origin for <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá), at least).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chamber pot</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖρον</i> (oûron, “urine”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, suffix for utensil names).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celestial phenomena</li><li>rain</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vehemently</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the goddesses</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the gods</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the name of an eye ointment</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heavenly</li><li>in the sky</li><li>reaching to heaven, high as heaven</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) enormous, awful, furious, vehement</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) blue</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρανός</i> (ouranós) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make water, urinate</li><li>to pass with the water</li><li>to act as diuretic</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eworhéyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wors-éye-ti</i> (“to make it rain”), from <i>&#42;h₁wers-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) urethra</li><li>sewage tank</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρέω</i> (ouréō, “to urinate”) +&lrm; <i>-ήθρᾱ</i> (-ḗthrā, “body part; anatomical cavity; container; receptacle; opening”), from <i>οὖρον</i> (oûron, “urine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom, butt-end of a spear</li><li>part of an oar</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”), with a suffix found also in <i>κύμβαχος</i> (kúmbakhos, “headfirst”) and <i>στόμαχος</i> (stómakhos, “throat, gullet; stomach”). See also <i>οὐρᾰχός</i> (ourăkhós, “urachus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little heaven or sky, hence:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>vaulted ceiling, especially the top of a tent, canopy</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) palate, roof of the mouth</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Corona Australis</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>οὐρανός</i> (ouranós, “sky, heaven”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the perspective of the gods</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρᾰ́νῐος</i> (ourắnĭos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>observing the heavens</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlantic stargazer (<i>Uranoscopus scaber</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρᾰνός</i> (ourănós, “heaven, sky”) +&lrm; <i>σκοπέω</i> (skopéō, “to look, contemplate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holding heaven</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρᾰνός</i> (ourănós, “sky, heaven”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from heaven</li></ol><p><i>οὐρανός</i> (ouranós) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the vaulted sky, on which the stars were attached and the sun traveled: sky, heaven</li><li>the region above this vault, the home of the gods</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) the universe</li><li>anything shaped like the sky: vaulted ceiling, tent</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin, with multiple theories proposed:</p><p>A folk etymology advanced by Aristotle interpreted it as <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “limit”) and <i>ἄνω</i> (ánō, “up”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Οὐρανός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Uranus, a primordial god in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>οὐρᾰνός</i> (ourănós, “sky; heaven”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) urachus, a foetal organ connected with the bladder</li><li>apex of the heart</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) outer ends of the eyebrows</li><li>point of a drill or borer</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail, rear”), with a suffix <i>-αχος</i> (-akhos) of indeterminate origin which Beekes identifies as Pre-Greek. Compare also <i>οὐρίαχος</i> (ouríakhos, “butt-end of the spear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the tail</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uraeus, sacred asp</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p><p><div>Traditionally assumed to be from Egyptian <i>jꜥrt</i> (“cobra in threat posture”), <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9b32fa22.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="a" alt="glyph" height="30"/><br /><img src="./71f37cec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="r" alt="glyph" height="27"/><br /> <img src="./76993ec3.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="t" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6de4f971.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="I12" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table>, from <i>jꜥr</i> (“to rise, climb”); however, on phonetic grounds, Gundacker, following Fecht, argues for an origin in Egyptian <i>wrrt</i> (“White Crown, uraeus”, literally “the great one”) instead.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ὀρεύς</i> (oreús): mule</li></ol><p>See <i>ὀρεύς</i> (oreús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>= οὐρήθρα (ourḗthra, “urethra”)</li><li>οὐρητῆρες (ourētêres, plural) the ducts which convey the urine from the kidneys into the bladder</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρέω</i> (ouréō, “to make water”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, suffix to form a masculine noun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devouring its tail</li></ol><p>A compound of <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”) + <i>-βόρος</i> (-bóros, “-devouring”, “-swallowing”), which is derived from the verb <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “to eat up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The rump and tail feathers of a bird.</li><li>The tail and/or rump of any animal.</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρρος</i> (órrhos, “rump, tail”) +&lrm; <i>πυγή</i> (pugḗ, “buttocks”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trench or channel for hauling up and launching ships</li></ol><p>Formerly connected to <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “frontier”) and <i>ὀρύσσω</i> (orússō, “to dig”). Recently, however, García Ramón has analyzed this word as an agent noun <i>&#42;uoru-ó-</i> built on the same root as <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to drag”), thus from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rewH-</i> (“to tear, dig, burrow, gather”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is one's own, one's substance, property</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) synonym of <i>φύσις</i> (phúsis) stable being, immutable reality</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>substance, essence</li><li>true nature of that which is a member of a kind</li><li>the possession of such a nature, substantiality</li><li>(<i>in the concrete</i>) the primary real, the substratum underlying all change and process in nature</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) substance as the leading category</li><li>(<i>various uses after Plato and Aristotle</i>)</li><li>Pythagorean name for I</li></ol><li>name of a plaster</li><li>a fire-resisting substance</li><li>(<i>in magic</i>) a material thing by which a connection is established between the person to be acted upon and the supernatural agent</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖσᾰ</i> (oûsă, feminine present participle of <i>εἰμῐ́</i> (eimĭ́, “to be”)) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix). Literally, “beingness”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>οὐτᾰ́ω</i> (outắō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stab, hit, wound</li></ol><p>Morphologically and etymologically unclear. Often connected with <i>ὠτειλή</i> (ōteilḗ, “fresh wound”) and with <i>ἀάω</i> (aáō, “to hurt, damage”). Already Karl Meister thought it of Pre-Greek origin, which seems to be the best solution, by the interpretation of <i>ὠτειλή</i> (ōteilḗ) as a variant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthless, of no account, especially with regard to strength or courage</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐ</i> (ou, “not”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;τιδ</i> (&#42;tid) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰνός</i> (-ănós, “tending to”), showing original <i>*τιδ</i> (for <i>τι</i> (ti)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷid</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>οὐ</i> (ou), used before a vowel with rough breathing</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not least; above all, especially</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>οὐ</i> (ou)</li><li>no</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οὔτε</i> (oúte) used before vowel or diphthong with rough breathing</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) dust (?); path, way, road</li></ol><p>Possibly from Paleo-Balkan, closely related to Albanian <i>udhë</i> (“path, way, road”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>certainly not</li></ol><p><i>οὐκ</i> (ouk) +&lrm; <i>οὖν</i> (oûn)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>never</li></ol><p><i>οὔ</i> (oú, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ποτε</i> (pote, “at some time”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>not yet</li><li>not, not at all</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐ</i> (ou, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πω</i> (pō, “yet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A urination.</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρέω</i> (ouréō, “to urinate”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, action noun suffix), from <i>οὖρον</i> (oûron, “urine”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a fair wind; sending a fair wind</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) prosperous; successful</li><li>prospering; favoring; fair (<i>of winds</i>)</li><li>alternative form of <i>οὔρειος</i> (oúreios, “urinary”)</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros, “fair wind”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p><p>From <i>οὖρον</i> (oûron, “urine”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Οὔριος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>rare epithet of Zeus</li></ol><p>From <i>οὔριος</i> (oúrios), itself derived from <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros, “fair wind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οὔτε</i> (oúte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) and not</li><li>(<i>mostly repeated</i>) neither...nor</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>often used to distinguish a general negation by dividing it into subordinate clauses</i>)</li><li>(alternating clauses with <i>οὐδέ</i> (oudé))</li><li>(may be followed by a positive clause with <i>τε</i> (te))</li><li>(<i>often, by anacoluthon, followed by some other particle</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(in poetry, <i>οὐ</i> (ou) sometimes follows without any conjunctive particle)</li><li>(sometimes replaced by <i>οὐ</i> (ou) in poetry)</li><li>(<i>the former οὔτε is sometimes omitted</i>)</li></ol><li>(corresponding with <i>μήτε</i> (mḗte))</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>οὐ</i> (ou, “not”) + <i>τε</i> (te, “and”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>no one, nobody, none, nothing, no</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(neuter,&#32;adverbial) by no means, not at all</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>οὐ</i> (ou, “not”) +&lrm; <i>τῐς</i> (tĭs, “somebody”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>οὕνεκα</i> (hoúneka)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>because</li></ol><p>From <i>οὗ</i> (hoû) + <i>ἕνεκα</i> (héneka).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>οὕτως</i> (hoútōs) (<i>used before a consonant</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in this manner, thus, so</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>even so, just so</li><li>(with imperative,&#32;added emphasis)</li><li>(<i>the introduction of the apodosis after a protasis</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>in a really inferential sense</i>) so, therefore</li><li>(<i>with an adjective or adverb</i>) so, so much, so excessively</li><li>(sometimes used like <i>αὔτως</i> (aútōs)) so, merely, simply</li></ol><p>Adverb from <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos, “this”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial ending).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old name of the letter ο (omicron, see ὂ μικρόν (ò mikrón), όμικρον).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground, earth</li><li>floor</li></ol><p>Possibly related to Old Armenian <i>գետին</i> (getin, “ground, earth”) and Hittite <i>𒌓𒉈𒂊</i> (ut-ne-e /⁠utnē⁠/, “land, country”). Possibly connected to <i>οὐδός</i> (oudós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of animals</i>) udder</li><li>that which is rich or fertile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ówHdʰr̥</i>. Cognates include Vedic Sanskrit <i>ऊध॑र्</i> (ū́dhar), Latin <i>ūber</i>, Old English <i>ūder</i> (English <i>udder</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) gum (flesh around the teeth)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>destructively</li></ol><p>The etymology is uncertain. Maybe from the same root of <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to press together”) or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind, round”), like <i>ἐλύω</i> (elúō, “to roll around”), <i>εἰλύω</i> (eilúō, “to enfold”), <i>εἴλω</i> (eílō, “to roll up, pack close”), <i>ἑλίσσω</i> (helíssō, “to turn round, to roll”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos)</li><li>baneful, destructive</li><li>woolly, woolen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hólwos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;solh₂-wós</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃elh₁-</i>, the root of <i>ὄλλυμι</i> (óllumi) and <i>ὀλοός</i> (oloós).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂welh₁-</i> (“hair, wool”), the root of <i>λάχνη</i> (lákhnē) and <i>λάσιος</i> (lásios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos)</li><li>baneful, destructive</li><li>woolly, woolen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hólwos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;solh₂-wós</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃elh₁-</i>, the root of <i>ὄλλυμι</i> (óllumi) and <i>ὀλοός</i> (oloós).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂welh₁-</i> (“hair, wool”), the root of <i>λάχνη</i> (lákhnē) and <i>λάσιος</i> (lásios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>then (<i>implying temporal sequence</i>)</li><li>therefore (<i>implying causal sequence</i>)</li><li>Marker of the continuation of a previously interrupted line of thought: "As I was saying..."</li><li>Added to indefinite pronoun or adverb, such as <i>ὁστισοῦν</i> (hostisoûn), to make it more general: whoever, whatever; whosoever</li></ol><p>According to Beekes (2010), “the relation between οὖν and ὦν is unclear. Unconvincing explanations in Brugmann-Thumb 1913: 633 and in Schwyzer 1950: 586f. On οὖν in Homer, cf. Reynen <i>Glotta</i> 36 (1957): Iff. and Reynen <i>Glotta</i> 37 (1958): 67ff.”</p><p>Dunkel suggests origin as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;óh₁ em</i>, a combination of two emphatic particles.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>according to Hesychius, a Cypriot word for <i>δρόμον</i> (drómon, “course, race”).</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>According to Mayer, it was borrowed from Egyptian <i>wnj</i>. Compare <i>ἐριούνης</i> (erioúnēs, “epithet of Hermes”).</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipit (bird of the genus <i>Anthus</i>)</li></ol><p>The suffix is typically Pre-Greek. This means that the word has nothing to do with <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urine</li><li>range, space</li></ol><p>From <i>οὐρέω</i> (ouréō, “to urinate”). Not related to Latin <i>ūrīna</i>.</p><p>Perhaps formed from <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros, “furrow, frontier”), itself from <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain, district, sector”).</p><p>Also compare Sanskrit <i>वरस्</i> (váras, “expanse, space, range, breadth, room”), <i>अउरव</i> (aürava, “space, width, dimension, extension”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fair wind</li><li>guardian; watcher</li><li>aurochs; <i>Bos primigenius</i></li><li>Ionic form of <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “boundary; border”)</li><li>Epic and Lyric form of <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain”)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i> (“to move, stir, spring”), like <i>ὄρνυμι</i> (órnumi, “to awaken”).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to note, sense”) and cognate with <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō, “to look, make sure, provide”). An alternate theory derives the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sorwos</i> (“guardian”), for which see Latin <i>servus</i> (“servant”) for more.</p><p>From Latin <i>ūrus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fair wind</li><li>guardian; watcher</li><li>aurochs; <i>Bos primigenius</i></li><li>Ionic form of <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “boundary; border”)</li><li>Epic and Lyric form of <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain”)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i> (“to move, stir, spring”), like <i>ὄρνυμι</i> (órnumi, “to awaken”).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to note, sense”) and cognate with <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō, “to look, make sure, provide”). An alternate theory derives the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sorwos</i> (“guardian”), for which see Latin <i>servus</i> (“servant”) for more.</p><p>From Latin <i>ūrus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ear</li><li>hearing</li><li>(<i>from resemblance to an ear</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>handle of a pitcher or cup</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) synonym of <i>πᾰρωτῐ́ς</i> (părōtĭ́s)</li><li>ellipsis of <i>οὖς Ἀ̆φροδῑ́της</i> (oûs Ăphrodī́tēs), a kind of shellfish</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) auricle of the heart</li><li>name of part of a bandage</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) spy</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ṓws</i> (“ear”), the oblique stem being from a lengthened form <i>&#42;h₂ows-nt-</i> (for which compare Old Armenian <i>ունկն</i> (unkn)). Cognate with Old English <i>ēare</i> (English <i>ear</i>), Latin <i>auris</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>оухо</i> (uxo) and Old Irish <i>áu</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) ship's tackle, ropes</li></ol><p>Unclear. Von Wilamovitz compares <i>σοῦσον</i> (soûson, “lily”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>"of him/her/it", "than him/her/it", etc.</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where; when</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hwe</i> (= <i>&#42;ϝ̔έ</i> (&#42;whé), representing the accusative case), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> (reflexive pronoun).</p><p>See <i>ὅς</i> (hós).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative) this</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) who, which, that</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;with article</i>) his; hers; its</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) his or her people, friends, family etc.</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) his or her possessions</li></ol></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) his own; her own; its own</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;Hyós</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;yós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyós</i>, <i>&#42;Hyéh₂</i>, <i>&#42;Hyód</i> (“who, which”), from the relative stem <i>&#42;Hyo-</i>, possibly from the anaphoric stem <i>&#42;(h₁)i-, &#42;(h₁)e-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>यद्</i> (yás, yā́, yád), Avestan <i>𐬫𐬋</i> (yō), and Phrygian <i>ιος</i> (ios /⁠jos⁠/). See also <i>οἷος</i> (hoîos), <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos). Compare <i>ἑός</i> (heós).</p><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Like <i>ἕ</i> (hé, “him”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> (reflexive pronoun). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्व</i> (svá). See also <i>ἑός</i> (heós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>this</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) here</li><li>in contrast with <i>ὅδε</i> (hóde): the preceding</li><li>in contrast with <i>ἐκεῖνος</i> (ekeînos): the latter</li><li>of someone or something famous or infamous</li></ol></ol><p>Synchronically analyzable as <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “self; third-person pronoun”) + <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “self; third-person pronoun”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>θύρα</i> (thúra, “door”).</li></ol><p>Furnée identifies the word with the town <i>Παγασαί</i> (Pagasaí, “Pagasae”), in Thessaly. In view of the suffix <i>-ασα</i>, the word is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snare, noose, anything that fixes or fastens</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) trap, snare</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂ǵ-eh₂</i>, from the root <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i> (“to fix, fasten”), like <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “to fasten”).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>tenus</i> (akin to <i>teneō</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solid</li><li>firm, steadfast</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i>, like <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “I fasten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pancreas; sweetbread</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, all) +&lrm; <i>κρέᾰς</i> (kréăs, “meat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain peak, rocky hill</li><li>frost</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the scum on the surface of milk</li><li>salt, as formed by the evaporation of seawater</li></ol></ol><p>A nominalization of the verb <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “to stick, fasten, build, fix”), meaning "that what is built or fixed"; see also <i>πάγη</i> (págē, “snare, trap”) and Latin <i>pagus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crab</li></ol><p>Usually interpreted as derived from <i>πάγος</i> (págos, “crag, rock”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”). However, this traditional etymology is unconvincing, like in <i>σκίουρος</i> (skíouros, “squirrel”). More probably this is a Pre-Greek word. Furnée compares also <i>φάγρος</i> (phágros, “red porgy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wholly, quite</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾶν</i> (pân).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mahaleb cherry (<i>Prunus mahaleb</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>πηδός</i> (pēdós, “kind of tree”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πάδος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Po</li></ol><p>From or cognate with Latin <i>Padus</i>, from Ancient Ligurian <i>Bodincus</i>, <i>Bodencus</i> (“bottomless”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰudʰmḗn</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in neutral sense</i>) what is done or what happens to a person</li><li>(<i>in negative sense</i>) suffering, misfortune</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰ́σχω</i> (pắskhō, “to undergo”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain, suffering, death</li><li>misfortune, calamity, disaster, misery</li><li>disease</li><li>any strong feeling, passion, emotion</li><li>condition, state</li><li>incident</li><li>modification of words</li></ol><p>From <i>παθ-</i> (path-), zero-grade of the root of <i>πᾰ́σχω</i> (pắskhō, “to feel; to suffer”). Compare the aorist <i>ἔπαθον</i> (épathon). Related to <i>πένθος</i> (pénthos), in the same way as <i>βάθος</i> (báthos) is related to <i>βένθος</i> (bénthos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which befalls someone, suffering, misfortune</li><li>emotion, condition, affection</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) affections, feelings</li></ol><li>(in the plural,&#32;medicine) trouble, symptoms</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) incidents, happenings</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) incident, property, accident</li></ol><p>From <i>πάσχω</i> (páskhō, “suffer”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain, suffering, death</li><li>misfortune, calamity, disaster, misery</li><li>disease</li><li>any strong feeling, passion, emotion</li><li>condition, state</li><li>incident</li><li>modification of words</li></ol><p>From <i>παθ-</i> (path-), zero-grade of the root of <i>πᾰ́σχω</i> (pắskhō, “to feel; to suffer”). Compare the aorist <i>ἔπαθον</i> (épathon). Related to <i>πένθος</i> (pénthos), in the same way as <i>βάθος</i> (báthos) is related to <i>βένθος</i> (bénthos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light boat of wickerwork, used on the Nile</li></ol><p>From Doric <i>πᾱκτός</i> (pāktós, “compacted”), equivalent to Attic <i>πηκτός</i> (pēktós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a point in the past</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>long ago, in days past</li><li>of past time closer to the present: before, earlier, a while ago</li><li>with a present-tense verb since a point in the past, for a long time</li></ol></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“far (in time or space)”). However, it has been argued (e.g. by Chadwick) that Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀨𐀍</i> (pa-ra-jo), 𐀞𐀨𐀊 (pa-ra-ja, “old”) are related, therefore precluding the popularly held etymology with an initial labiovelar. Beekes instead compares the Mycenaean to <i>&#42;pelh₂-</i> (“to approach”).</p><p>A frozen case-form (perhaps an old dative or fossilized PIE allative), as indicated by the ending <i>-αι</i> (-ai); compare <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí). See also Sanskrit <i>पलित</i> (palita, “old, hairy, grey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laying on of a preliminary coat of paint, priming</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰλᾰ́σσω</i> (pălắssō, “to besprinkle, defile”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrestling, fight</li><li>finest meal, flour</li><li>any fine dust</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō).</p><p>Has been derived from <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō, “to swing, rock”), which is conceivable but not compelling. Alternatively, it can be connected to Latin <i>pollen</i> (“fine flour”) and <i>puls</i> (“meal, porridge”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“flour, dust”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) back, backwards</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with a notion of contradiction</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) again, once more, anew</li><li>again, in turn, doubly</li></ol><p>Frozen adverbial accusative of <i>&#42;πάλις</i> (&#42;pális) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷl̥His</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to revolve”) (whence <i>πέλω</i> (pélō, “to go, be”) and <i>πάλαι</i> (pálai, “before, long ago”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth (young boy or girl)</li></ol><p>Probably a back-formation from <i>παλλακή</i> (pallakḗ, “concubine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to poise or sway a missile before it is thrown; to brandish a weapon</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to swing or dash oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pə́ľľō</i>, traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pl̥-n-h₁-yé-ti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to drive, swing, shake”). Beekes considers this possible but uncertain, due to lack of other evidence for a nasal present. See also <i>παλύνω</i> (palúnō, “to strew”), <i>παλάσσω</i> (palássō, “to besprinkle”), and <i>παλαίω</i> (palaíō, “to scatter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) king; chief</li></ol><p>From Lydian.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πάλμυς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Palmys, a Trojan chief</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλμυς</i> (pálmus, “Lydian king or chief”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wholly, altogether, quite</li></ol><p>Reduplication of <i>πᾶν</i> (pân), neuter nominative singular (here used adverbially) of <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all, every, whole”), with labial assimilation. This type of iterative compound is vanishingly rare in Ancient Greek, with only a handful furthermore known from Mycenaean Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) first of all; very first</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-) +&lrm; <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) all-living, immortal, in full life, (ruling) over all the souls, in full power.</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰμ-</i> (păm-) +&lrm; <i>ψῡχή</i> (psūkhḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to all the people, public</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or common to all gods</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos, “belonging to the gods”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πάνθειον)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pantheon</li></ol><p>Neuter substantivization of <i>πάνθειος</i> (pántheios, “of all gods”), from <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “all, everything”) +&lrm; <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos, “of or for the gods”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or common to all gods</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos, “belonging to the gods”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panther</li></ol><p>Of foreign origin, perhaps connected to <i>πάρδαλις</i> (párdalis). Compare the lexicographically-attested Sanskrit <i>पुण्डरीक</i> (puṇḍarīka, “tiger”), Sogdian <i>𐼾𐼴𐽀𐼹𐼻𐼸</i> (pwrδnk), Pashto پړانګ (pṛāng), Persian پلنگ (palang), and Hittite <i>𒊊𒌉𒀸</i> (parsnaš, “leopard”). A common folk etymology derives it from <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) + <i>θήρ</i> (thḗr, “beast”) or <i>θήρα</i> (thḗra, “hunt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seen by all, visible to all</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>ὀπτός</i> (optós, “seen, visible”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very wise, very learned</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “very, all”) +&lrm; <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “wise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-honourable</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honour, esteem”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from all quarters, from every side</li></ol><p><i>πᾶς</i> (pâs) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πάντοσε</i> (pántose)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>every way</li><li>in all directions</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all, every”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere, in every direction, on every side, all around</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs) +&lrm; <i>-ῃ</i> (-ēi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>altogether, completely, perfectly</li><li>(<i>modifying adjective</i>) very</li><li>(<i>negated</i>) not quite, not at all</li><li>(in answer to a question,&#32;often πάνυ γε) quite so, by all means</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) twisted band, snood</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested in Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>enough!, oh!, bah!, good!</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pāx&#33;</i> (“peace! quiet! enough talking!”), from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;pāks</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péh₂ḱ-s</i> (“peace”), from the root <i>&#42;peh₂ḱ-</i> (“to join, to attach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πᾰ́ππᾱς</i> (pắppās, “papa; daddy”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>papa; daddy</li></ol><p>From child speech.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandfather (both maternal or paternal)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) grandparents</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) ascendant, ancestor</li><li>pantaloon, a character in comic dramas</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) pappus, down on the seeds of certain plants</li><li>first down on the chin</li><li>small bird in whose nest the cuckoo lays</li></ol><p>Reduplicated nursery word; compare <i>πάππας</i> (páppas, “daddy, papa”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thracian lake-fish</li></ol><p>Has been connected with <i>πέρκη</i> (pérkē, “perch”) and <i>περκνός</i> (perknós, “spotted”), or explained as onomatopoeic after the supposed sound of the fish. According to Beekes, the word may be of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the plant papyrus</li><li>paper</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from Egyptian, since writing on it is widely believed to have originated in Egypt, but no Egyptian cognate is known. Beekes notes that the suffix *-ῡρ- is reconstructed for Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Lyric form of <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>πᾰ́ρεστῐ</i> (pắrestĭ) or <i>πᾰ́ρεισῐ</i> (pắreisĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leopard</li><li>panther</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a foreign eastern language. Compare various Indo-Iranian terms like Sogdian <i>𐼾𐼴𐽀𐼹𐼻𐼸</i> (pwrδnk), Bactrian <i>πορλαγγο-ζινο</i> (porlango-zino), Pashto پړانګ (pṛāng), Persian پلنگ (palang), and Sanskrit <i>पृदाकु</i> (pṛ́dāku). See also <i>πάνθηρ</i> (pánthēr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) synonym of <i>πᾰ́ρδᾰλῐς</i> (pắrdălĭs, “leopard; panther”)</li></ol><p>Of foreign origin. Compare Sogdian <i>𐼾𐼴𐽀𐼹𐼻𐼸</i> (pwrδnk), Bactrian <i>πορλαγγο-ζινο</i> (porlango-zino), Pashto پړانګ (pṛāng), Persian پلنگ (palang), and Sanskrit <i>पृदा॑कु</i> (pṛ́dāku). See also <i>πᾰ́ρδᾰλῐς</i> (pắrdălĭs) and <i>πᾰ́νθηρ</i> (pắnthēr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>near at hand, close by</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) near</li><li>nearly alike</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sitting beside (as at a table); near (to)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attendant, assessor, coadjutor, counsellor</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “by, near”) +&lrm; <i>ἕδρᾱ</i> (hédrā, “chair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be by or present</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be by or near one</li><li>to be present in or at</li><li>to be present so as to help, stand by</li><li>to arrive at, to have arrived at</li><li>to have come from</li></ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) to be by, to be ready or at hand</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in store, at command</li></ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) it depends on me, is in one's power to do</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>participle</i>) it being possible or easy, since it is allowed</li></ol><li>(masculine participle,&#32;at the end of a verse,&#32;like an expletive to round off the sentence)</li><li>to be going to go by, beside, past</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “to be”).</p><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>εἶμι</i> (eîmi, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying beside or with, bedfellow</li></ol><p>From <i>παρά</i> (pará, “beside”) and <i>εὐνή</i> (eunḗ, “bed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light shield, buckler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>parma</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of locust</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>δρύοψ</i> (drúops), <i>κώνωψ</i> (kṓnōps) and <i>σκάλοψ</i> (skálops). The suffix is typical of Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πάροιθε</i> (pároithe)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πάροιθε</i> (pároithe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πάροιθεν</i> (pároithen)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πάροιθεν</i> (pároithen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in front</li><li>heretofore, beforehand</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in the presence of, before, in front of</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πάρος</i> (páros) with the suffix <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dwelling beside or near, neighbouring</li><li>foreign, alien</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sojourner in another house</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) alien, stranger</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house, dwelling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>formerly, up to now</li><li>too soon</li><li>rather, sooner</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>before</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pəros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥h₂ós</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πάρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Paros, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millet (<i>Panicum miliaceum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰσπᾰ́λη</i> (păspắlē, “the finest meal”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peg on which to hang clothes</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) membrum virile</li></ol><p>This word is usually derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i> (“to attach”). However, Beekes finds this improbable and asserts that the suffix "-αλ-" is typical of Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Beekes, the term could be connected with Latin <i>quatiō</i> if both shared a pre-form of the shape <i>&#42;kʷh₂t-</i>, however an Epidauric inscription contains the form <i>ἐπιπῆν</i> (epipên), the long <i>ê</i>-vowel of which speaks against the aforementioned etymology. Furthermore, the LIV prefers to derive the term from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kuh₁t-yé-ti</i>, from a root of the shape <i>&#42;(s)kweh₁t-</i>. However, De Vaan suggests that there are a number of formal problems complicating any attempt to explain the emergence of the Greek <i>a</i>-vowel from such a root. Ultimately, De Vaan concludes that both <i>πάσσω</i> (pássō) and Latin <i>quatiō</i> may be best explained as derived from a common substrate source of the shape <i>&#42;kwat-</i>. Beekes notes another purported link with Proto-Tocharian <i>&#42;kät-</i> (“to strew”), though he considers this connection doubtful.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) comparative degree of <i>πᾰχῠ́ς</i> (păkhŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pastille</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pastillus</i> (“pastille”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Passover</li><li>Paschal dinner, Passover meal</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Easter</li><li>Paschal Lamb</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>פַּסְחָא</i> (pasḥā), from Biblical Hebrew <i>פֶּסַח</i> (pésaḥ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undergo, experience (<i>as opposed to acting</i>)</li><li>(<i>with another person involved</i>) have someone do something to oneself, to be treated a certain way by someone (with <i>ὑπό</i> (hupó) and genitive,&#32;sometimes with adverb of manner)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a negative sense</i>) suffer at someone's hands</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to suffer a punishment</li></ol><li>(<i>without a person involved</i>) to experience something, have something happen to one, undergo something</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be in a certain situation (<i>with adverb of manner</i>)</li><li>to feel an emotion or impulse</li><li>(<i>in negative sense</i>) suffer</li><li>to be ill or injured in a certain way (<i>with accusative of part affected</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>The four principal parts of the verb display the e-grade, o-grade, and zero-grade (<i>πενθ-, πονθ-, παθ-</i> (penth-, ponth-, path-)) of a root of uncertain identity.</p><p>The root is traditionally taken to be a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷendʰ-</i> (“to suffer, endure”), with the principal parts mentioned above reflecting <i>&#42;kʷendʰ-, &#42;kʷondʰ-, &#42;kʷn̥dʰ</i>. In this case, cognate with Old Irish <i>césaid</i> (“to suffer”), Lithuanian <i>kentė́ti</i> (“to suffer”) (1st-person present singular <i>kenčiù</i> (“I suffer”)), <i>pakanta</i> (“patience”). Note, however, that in addition to phonetic issues (the Lithuanian expected form would be <i>&#42;kendė́ti</i>, for example), the structure of the hypothetical root <i>&#42;kʷendʰ-</i> is atypical for Proto-Indo-European.</p><p>As an alternative, Beekes and others have connected the word to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i> (“to bind”) with semantic shift “to be bound” &gt; “to suffer”. Cognates would then include Ancient Greek <i>πενθερός</i> (pentherós, “father-in-law”).</p><p>The present <i>πάσχω</i> (páskhō), like the aorist <i>ἔπαθον</i> (épathon), comes from the zero-grade <i>παθ-</i> (path-), but with the inchoative suffix <i>-σκω</i> (-skō) added <i>&#42;πάθ-σκ-ω</i> (&#42;páth-sk-ō) and subsequent loss of <i>θ</i> (th) before <i>σ</i> (s) and transference of aspiration from <i>θ</i> (th) to <i>κ</i> (k), resulting in <i>χ</i> (kh). The future stem <i>πείσομαι</i> (peísomai) developed from <i>&#42;πενθ-σ-ομαι</i> (&#42;penth-s-omai), from <i>e</i>-grade <i>πενθ-</i> (penth-) with the tense-suffix <i>σ</i> (s), with subsequent cluster simplification <i>νθσ &gt; σ</i> (nths &gt; s) and compensatory lengthening <i>&#42;ε &gt; ει</i> (&#42;e &gt; ei). The future stem <i>πείσω</i> (peísō) of the verb <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “persuade”) is identical.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clatter, crash (of trees falling)</li><li>chattering of the teeth</li><li>rattle or crash of thunder</li><li>plash of a body falling into water</li></ol><p>Possibly of onomatopoeic origin. The suffix in -γ- is found in semantically close forms, such as <i>λαλαγή</i> (lalagḗ), <i>σμαραγέω</i> (smaragéō) and <i>οἰμωγή</i> (oimōgḗ). Furnée compares <i>σπαταγγίζειν</i> (spatangízein); the word could also be of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dish, patella</li><li>food bank</li><li>(Egyptian,&#32;geography) basin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>patella</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trodden or beaten way, path</li><li>step (movement of the foot), stepping, treading</li><li>floor</li><li>dirt, mud, dung</li><li>robe worn by Hera</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Clearly related to <i>πατέω</i> (patéō, “I walk, tread”), but which gave rise to which is less clear. It could be an old zero-grade variant of <i>πόντος</i> (póntos, “sea”), and if so, <i>πατέω</i> (patéō) would be the derivative. In this case, the words derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pent-</i> (“to go, walk; way”); cognates thus include Sanskrit <i>पन्था</i> (pánthā), Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬥𐬙𐬁</i> (pantā), Old Armenian <i>հուն</i> (hun), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;paþaz</i>, whence English <i>path</i>. However, Beekes and Chantraine express semantic doubts for the hypothetical sense development from "way, path" (<i>πάτος</i> (pátos)) &gt; "to tread" (<i>πατέω</i> (patéō)), as the latter appears to emphasize the individual movement of the legs; if these doubts are correct, then there is no other good etymology for the Greek terms.</p><p>Of uncertain origin. Has been connected with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)penh₁-</i> (“to weave, spin”) (whence <i>πένομαι</i> (pénomai, “to toil, exert”)), while other theories take the word as a back-formation of <i>πατέω</i> (patéō, “to tread”) (and thus the same as Etymology 1), thus "what is trodden" > "long garment reaching to the feet, train". Neither is particularly convincing nor unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fatherland, native land</li><li>fatherʼs sister, aunt</li><li>fatherhood</li></ol><p>From <i>πατήρ</i> (patḗr, “father”). Compare Latin <i>patria</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πάτρᾱ</i> (pátrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πάτρᾱ</i> (pátrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ancestral customs</li></ol><p>Substantivization of the neuter plural of <i>πᾰ́τρῐος</i> (pắtrĭos, “ancestral”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of one's father</li><li>of one's ancestors, ancestral, hereditary, inherited, native, customary</li></ol><p><i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr, “father”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paternal uncle</li><li>ancestral male relative of one's father</li><li>(<i>Cretan</i>) any male relative of one's father</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr, “father”). Compare <i>μήτρως</i> (mḗtrōs, “maternal uncle”). Cognate with Latin <i>patruus</i> (“paternal uncle”), Classical Persian افدر (afdar, “paternal uncle”), and Sanskrit <i>पितृव्य</i> (pitṛvya, “paternal uncle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πάσσω</i> (pássō)</li></ol><p>From the same source as <i>πάσσω</i> (pássō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoarfrost, rime</li></ol><p>From earlier *πάκσνά (<i>páksná</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i>, like <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “I fasten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>παῖς</i> (paîs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fetter, shackle</li><li>anklet, bangle</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) of the robe of Nessus or Agamemnon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pṓds</i>, like <i>πέδον</i> (pédon) and <i>πέζα</i> (péza). The same semantics are found in Latin <i>pedica</i> (“shackle”), <i>impediō</i> (“to hinder”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;feturaz</i> (“fetter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandal (type of footwear)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ped-</i> (“foot”); compare <i>πούς</i> (poús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground, earth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pedóm</i>, from <i>&#42;ped-</i> (the source of <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”)). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>पद</i> (padá, “step, footstep, footprint”), Old Norse <i>fet</i> (“step”), Old Armenian <i>հետ</i> (het, “footprint, track”), and Hittite <i>𒁉𒂊𒁕𒀭</i> (pēdan).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paltry puffball (<i>Bovista plumbea</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pesd-</i> (“to fart”), the same root of Latin <i>pēdō</i> (“to fart”) and <i>βδέω</i> (bdéō, “to break wind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to comb, card</li><li>to shear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peḱ-</i> (“to pluck”). Cognates include Latin <i>pectō</i> (“to comb, card”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Some suggest the source is Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat, broad, plain”), whereas others such as Beekes suggest a non-Indo-European origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any thick liquid substance, of various consistency</li><li>mixture offered to the gods and the dead, of meal, honey and oil</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>meal made from barley and wheat, of which this mixture was made</li></ol><li>round cake offered to the gods</li><li>measure of weight</li></ol><p>The original meaning and etymology are uncertain. If the original meaning was "flat cake", it could be derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat, broad, plain”). However, Furnée compares <i>πέλαινα</i> (pélaina, “round cake”) and concludes that it is a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nearby, hard by, close by</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pélas</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pélh₂-s</i>, from <i>&#42;pelh₂-</i> (“to approach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock pigeon</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) a prophetic priestess of Dodona</li></ol><p>From a hypothetical u-stem <i>&#42;πελύς</i> (&#42;pelús, “gray”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pelH-</i> (“gray”). Compare Latin <i>palumbēs</i> (“dove”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle of an axe</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin due to the presence of a geminate <i>-κκ-</i> (-kk-). Furnée compares the word with <i>πέλεκυς</i> (pélekus, “battle axe”) and <i>βέλεκκος</i> (bélekkos, “kind of pulse”). However, likely ultimately from Akkadian <i>𒁄</i> (pilakku, pilaqqu, “wooden handle”), from Sumerian <i>𒁄</i> (balag, “wooden handle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-edged axe for felling trees</li><li>battle axe, executioner's axe</li></ol><p>Uncertain: Compare Sanskrit <i>परशु</i> (paraśú, “axe”) and Ossetian <i>фӕрӕт</i> (færæt, “axe”), pointing to a late, dialectal Proto-Indo-European word like <i>&#42;peleḱu-</i> which does not appear to be a native formation. This etymon is often considered a Wanderwort, with similarity to Akkadian <i>𒁄</i> (pilakku, pilaqqu, “wooden handle; spindle, harp”), itself from Sumerian <i>𒁄</i> (balag, “wooden handle; spindle, harp; possibly a split piece of wood or wooden wedge”); compare Arabic فَلَقَ (falaqa, “to split apart”) and <i>πέλεκκον</i> (pélekkon, “axe handle”). This has led some to suggest that the Proto-Indo-European terms are ultimately borrowed through the Akkadian or another Semitic source. Furnée points to <i>βέλεκκος</i> (bélekkos, “kind of pulse”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden bowl, milk pail</li><li>a kind of drinking-cup</li><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”).</li></ol><p>The comparison with Latin <i>pēlvis</i> (“bowl, dish”) or Sanskrit <i>पालवी</i> (pālavī, “a kind of vessel”), <i>पारी</i> (pārī, “milk pail”) is unconvincing, and does not lead to an Indo-European reconstruction. Furnée suggests a Pre-Greek origin, which is tentatively supported by Beekes. On the other hand, Kroonen derives the Greek from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel(w)-</i> (“vessel”), and connects Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fullą</i> (“cup, beaker”).</p><p>Assuming a Pre-Greek form <i>&#42;πελσᾱ</i> (&#42;pelsā), it has been connected with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falisaz</i> (“rock, cliff”), Old Irish <i>ail</i> (“crag”) and Sanskrit <i>पाषाण</i> (pāṣāṇa, “rock, stone”). However, the variation <i>*pelso-/*peliso-</i> does not seem Indo-European. The noun could be identical to the Macedonian town <i>Πέλλα</i> (Pélla). Furnée further compares <i>φελλεύς</i> (phelleús, “stony ground”), which points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πέλλα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pella, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol><p>Presumably from an earlier *πέλσα from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pels-</i> (“stone”). Cognates include Irish <i>aill</i> (“boulder, cliff”), Pashto پرښه (parša, “rock, rocky ledge”), Sanskrit <i>पाषाण</i>&nbsp;m (pāṣāṇa) / <i>पाषी</i>&nbsp;f (pāṣī, “stone”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falisaz</i>, English <i>fell</i> (“rocky ridge”), Old Norse <i>fell</i> / <i>fjall</i> (“mountain”), German <i>Fels</i> (“rock, cliff”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sock or bandage worn by runners on the ankle</li></ol><p>Traditionally analyzed as <i>&#42;πεδ-ϝλῡ-τρα</i> (&#42;ped-wlū-tra), a compound of <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>ἔλυτρον</i> (élutron, “covering”). Nevertheless, monosyllabic <i>ϝλυ-</i> and its length are surprising.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sole of the foot</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sole of the shoe</li></ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) stalk (of apples and pears)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pélmn̥</i>, from <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to cover, wrap; skin, hide; cloth”) and cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;felmô</i> (“covering, skin”) (whence English <i>film</i>). Compare also Latin <i>pellis</i> (“skin”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fellą</i> (“skin, hide”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plěna</i> (“membrane”) from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A small light shield of leather without a rim, used by Thracians</li><li>A horse's ornament</li></ol><p>Perhaps a loanword from Thracian considering the origin of this type of shield. Usually connected with Latin <i>pellis</i> (“skin”) and Ancient Greek <i>πέλμα</i> (pélma, “sole of the foot”), so from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to cover”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Nile-fish coracinus after being pickled</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be</li><li>to become</li><li>to take place</li><li>(<i>in compounds</i>) to stir</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷélō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to move; to turn (around)”). Cognates include Latin <i>colō</i>, Sanskrit <i>चरति</i> (cárati). Unrelated to Latin <i>pellō</i>, which is instead related to <i>ψάλλω</i> (psállō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;mythology) a monster or monstrous creature</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beekes suggests that it is perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷer-ōr</i> (“maker; builder?”) modified by dissimilation, given variant forms such as <i>τέλωρ</i> (télōr) and <i>τελώρῐος</i> (telṓrĭos) which point to an original initial <i>&#42;kʷ-</i>, though he considers this derivation is problematic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monster; monstrous creature</li><li>a portent sent by the gods</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>πελώρῐος</i> (pelṓrĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>πελώρῐος</i> (pelṓrĭos)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πέλωρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cape Pelorus; Capo Peloro; Punta del Faro, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any kind of dressed food; pastry, cakes, sweetmeats</li></ol><p>From <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō, “to cook, to bake”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>πέντε</i> (pénte, “five”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, dispatch</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative of place</i>)</li><li>(<i>with adverbs</i>)</li><li>(<i>with infinitive of purpose</i>)</li><li>to nominate</li></ol><li>to send forth; to dismiss</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to discharge, shoot</li><li>(<i>of words</i>) to utter</li></ol><li>to conduct, escort</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with <i>πομπήν</i> (pompḗn)) to conduct or take part in (a procession)</li></ol><li>to send as a gift</li><li>to produce</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to send for</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to send in one's service; to cause to be sent</li></ol><p>Unclear. The basic semantic category of the word does not suggest a borrowing, though attempts at an internal derivation or finding extra-Hellenic cognates have been unfruitful due to the word's deceptively generic phonetics, such as proposed unconvincing links to <i>κομψός</i> (kompsós, “nice, smart”) or Lithuanian <i>švánkus</i> (“decent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old, aged</li><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>στωμύλος</i> (stōmúlos, “talkative”), <i>λάλος</i> (lálos, “loquacious”).</li></ol><p>Galen's explanation is a folk etymology based on the similarity with <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō, “to send, dispatch”). Blanc proposed a reduplication <i>πελ-πελ-</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“gray”), the same root of <i>πελιός</i> (peliós, “livid”) and <i>πελιδνός</i> (pelidnós, “pallid”). Anothery theory by Furnée links this adjective to Georgian <i>პეპერა</i> (ṗeṗera, “ancestor, forefather”), showing nasalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fifth</li><li>(<i>feminine substantive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the fifth day</li><li>via quintana, one of the lanes in the Roman camp</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;penkʷtos</i>, derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pénkʷe</i>. Equivalent to <i>πέντε</i> (pénte, “five”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, adjectival suffix), but the Proto-Hellenic labiovelar <i>&#42;kʷ</i> developed into the labial <i>π</i> (p) instead of the dental consonant <i>τ</i> (t).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send, dispatch</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative of place</i>)</li><li>(<i>with adverbs</i>)</li><li>(<i>with infinitive of purpose</i>)</li><li>to nominate</li></ol><li>to send forth; to dismiss</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to discharge, shoot</li><li>(<i>of words</i>) to utter</li></ol><li>to conduct, escort</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with <i>πομπήν</i> (pompḗn)) to conduct or take part in (a procession)</li></ol><li>to send as a gift</li><li>to produce</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to send for</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to send in one's service; to cause to be sent</li></ol><p>Unclear. The basic semantic category of the word does not suggest a borrowing, though attempts at an internal derivation or finding extra-Hellenic cognates have been unfruitful due to the word's deceptively generic phonetics, such as proposed unconvincing links to <i>κομψός</i> (kompsós, “nice, smart”) or Lithuanian <i>švánkus</i> (“decent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breath; blast</li><li>ray; sunbeam</li><li>drop (of water or blood)</li><li>cloud; rain cloud</li><li>pustule or part surrounding a pustule</li></ol><p>Possibly a Pre-Greek word, together with <i>πομφός</i> (pomphós, “blister”) and <i>πομφόλῠξ</i> (pomphólŭx, “bubble”). They might be onomatopoeic in origin; compare similar expressions for swelling, like Lithuanian <i>pampti</i> (“to swell”), <i>pempùs</i> (“fat-bodied”), <i>pumpùlis</i> (“roundish, thick-bellied thing”). Compare also <i>βέμβῑξ</i> (bémbīx, “spinning top”) and <i>βόμβος</i> (bómbos, “humming; buzzing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labourer, workman</li><li>poor person, pauper</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor</li></ol><p>From <i>πένομαι</i> (pénomai) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grief, sorrow</li><li>mourning</li><li>a misery, misfortune</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷendʰ-</i>; cognate with <i>πάθος</i> (páthos) and <i>πάσχω</i> (páskhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to toil, labor, exert oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)penh₁-</i> (“to weave, spin”), with semantic shift "to weave, stretch" > "to tense, strain" > "to exert oneself". Cognate with Lithuanian <i>pi̇̀nti</i> (“to braid, plait”), Old Church Slavonic <i>пѧти</i> (pęti, “to tie, fix”), Old English <i>spinnan</i> (“to spin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the contest of the five exercises, pentathlon</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντα-</i> (penta-, “five”) +&lrm; <i>ἆθλον</i> (âthlon, “contest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pénkʷe</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pénkʷe</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>पञ्च</i> (páñca), Latin <i>quīnque</i>, Old Armenian <i>հինգ</i> (hing) and Old English <i>fīf</i> (English <i>five</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penis</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;péhos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pésos</i>, from <i>&#42;pes-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>pēnis</i> and Sanskrit <i>पसस्</i> (pásas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pepper (plant of the family Piperaceae)</li><li>black pepper (<i>Piper nigrum</i>)</li><li>pepper (spice)</li></ol><p>From an Indo-Aryan source; compare Sanskrit <i>पिप्पलि</i> (pippali, “long pepper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) black pepper</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) pepper tree</li></ol><p>See <i>πέπερι</i> (péperi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any woven cloth used for a covering, sheet, carpet, curtain, veil</li><li>upper garment or full-length mantle in one piece, worn by women</li><li>(<i>at Athens</i>) embroidered robe carried in procession at the Panathenaea</li><li>(<i>less frequent</i>) man's robe or cloak, especially of long Persian dresses</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) peritoneum</li><li>wartweed (<i>Euphorbia peplus</i>)</li></ol><p>Maybe a reduplicated formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to fold”) and so cognate with the Ancient Greek suffix <i>-πλόος</i> (-plóos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be conscious</li><li>to be wise</li><li>to breathe</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li><li>to breathe</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to breathe out</li><li>breathe in, smell</li></ol><li>(<i>of perceptible breathing</i>)</li><li>to breathe, live</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;with cognate accusative) to breathe forth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to speak</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pnew-</i> (“to breathe, sneeze”). Cognates include Old English <i>fnēosan</i> (English <i>sneeze</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female<sup>?</sup>) baker</li></ol><p>From <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō, “to cook”) +&lrm; <i>-τρῐᾰ</i> (-trĭă). Reflects Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pékʷ-tr-ih₂ ~ &#42;pkʷ-tr-yéh₂s</i> (“female cook”), from <i>&#42;pekʷ-</i> (“to cook”), compare Latin <i>coctor</i>&nbsp;m, Sanskrit <i>पक्तृ</i>&nbsp;m (paktṛ́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō) (Attic)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ripe, softened (<i>of fruit</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) good, sweet, kind (<i>when addressing a person</i>)</li><li>(figurative,&#32;derogatory) weak, cowardly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pékʷonts</i> (“cooking, ripening”); related to <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō, “to soften, ripen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beyond</li></ol><p>From a petrified case form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i> (“beyond, across”), originally meaning "other side"; compare <i>πέρᾱν</i> (pérān, “across”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferry</li></ol><p>From <i>περάω</i> (peráō, “to cross, traverse”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(with genitive,&#32;often with the associated idea of a body of water)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>on the other side, across</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>absolute</i>) on the other side</li><li>(with verbs of motion,&#32;followed by <i>εἰς</i> (eis)) over or across to</li><li>(<i>with the article</i>)</li></ol><li>over against, opposite</li><li>(<i>less often</i>) beyond</li><li>right through</li></ol></ol><p>Compare <i>πέρᾱ</i> (pérā, “beyond”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end; limit; boundary</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) cord; rope</li><li>completion</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>verdict; final decision</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) doom</li><li>(<i>Attic</i>) a state of perfection</li><li>(<i>Attic</i>) end goal</li></ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) the finite (that which has limits)</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) extremity</li></ol><p>Perhaps akin to <i>πείρω</i> (peírō, “to pierce, to run through”) or <i>περάω</i> (peráō, “to drive right through”), see also <i>πέρα</i> (péra) and <i>πέραν</i> (péran), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to pierce”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partridge</li></ol><p>Traditionally explained (such as by Frisk) as a derivative from <i>πέρδομαι</i> (pérdomai, “to fart”), due to the droning sound when partridges take wing. However, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin, as he considers the suffix <i>-ῑκ-</i> (-īk-) to be of substrate origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fart; to break wind</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pérdomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pérdetor</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>पर्द॑ते</i> (párdate), Avestan <i>𐬞𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬜𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (pər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>δa<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti), Albanian <i>pordhë</i>, Lithuanian <i>pérsti</i>, Old Norse <i>freta</i>, Old English <i>&#42;feortan</i> (English <i>fart</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(Ionic,&#32;Epic) alternative form of <i>πέραν</i> (péran).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sack, ravage, destroy (a city); to slay (a person)</li><li>to get by plundering</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerH-dʰh₁-</i> (“to pierce, strike”) and/or <i>&#42;bʰerdʰ-</i> (“to cut, chop (off), hew, shear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perch (<i>Perca fluviatilis</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perḱ-</i> (“motley, coloured”). Cognate with Middle Irish <i>erc</i> (“perch, salmon”) (from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;ɸerkos</i>) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;furhnō</i> (“trout”). Related also to <i>περκνός</i> (perknós, “dusky”) and <i>πέρκος</i> (pérkos, “kind of hawk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of hawk</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perḱ-</i> (“motley, coloured”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farwō</i> (“colour”) and Ancient Greek <i>περκνός</i> (perknós, “dusky”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ham</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>perna</i>. Doublet of <i>πτέρνη</i> (ptérnē, “heel”), which was inherited.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perh₂-</i> (“to sell”); cognate with <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē), and with Latin <i>pars</i> and <i>portiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perh₂-</i> (“to sell”); cognate with <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē), and with Latin <i>pars</i> and <i>portiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bird of prey, a kind of hawk</li></ol><p>An Aristotelian term, attested in the <i>Historia Animalorum</i> (9.36), but corrupted in manuscript tradition, which also records <i>περνίς</i> (pernís), <i>πτερνίς</i> (pternís) and <i>πέρκνης</i> (pérknēs).</p><p>Theodorus Gaza, an early translator of Aristotle, renders the Greek term as Latin <i>pernix</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king, chief</li></ol><p>Aeolic form of <i>Πρίαμος</i> (Príamos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack, destruction</li></ol><p>From <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a year ago, last year</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;péruti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péruti</i> (“last year”); compare <i>ἔτος</i> (étos, “year”) (&lt; <i>&#42;wetos</i>). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>परुत्</i> (parut, “last year”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ferudi</i> (“last year”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, hide, rind</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the source of this word could be the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;peḱ-</i> (“to pluck”), from which <i>πεκτέω</i> (pektéō, “to shear”), <i>πόκος</i> (pókos, “wool”) and <i>κτείς</i> (kteís, “comb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to soften, ripen</li><li>to boil, cook, bake</li><li>to digest</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to brood</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of diseases</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;péťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pékʷ-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;pekʷ-</i>. The alternative present stem <i>πέπτω</i> (péptō) was back-formed from the aorist by analogy with verbs like <i>βλάπτω</i> (bláptō), <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō), <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō). Compare <i>νίζω</i> (nízō) and its alternative form <i>νίπτω</i> (níptō). Cognates include Latin <i>coquō</i> (whence English <i>cook</i>), Sanskrit <i>पचति</i> (pacati), Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬗𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (paca<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti), and Albanian <i>pjek</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf of plant, flower, or tree</li><li>thin plate of metal, leaf of metal</li><li>slice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pétalon</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péth₂lom</i>, from <i>&#42;peth₂-</i>. Compare Old High German <i>fedelgold</i>&nbsp;n (“leaf-gold”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broad, flat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pétalos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (“to spread out”)/<i>&#42;pet-</i> (“to spread out, extend”), whence Latin <i>pandō</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faþmaz</i> (“embrace”) [English <i>fathom</i>].</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broad, flat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pétalos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (“to spread out”)/<i>&#42;pet-</i> (“to spread out, extend”), whence Latin <i>pandō</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faþmaz</i> (“embrace”) [English <i>fathom</i>].</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broad-brimmed hat, commonly worn by young men; petasus, petasos</li><li>broad umbellated leaf</li><li>awning, baldaquin</li></ol><p>From <i>πετάννυμι</i> (petánnumi, “to open, spread out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πέτευρον</i> (péteuron)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roosting perch for fowls</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) pole, spar, plank</li><li>springboard used by tumblers and acrobats</li><li>platform, stage</li><li>springe, trap</li><li>public notice board</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>πετάννυμι</i> (petánnumi, “to spread out, open”). According to Beekes, the word could be of Pre-Greek origin, given the variation <i>αυ-/ευ-</i> and the prenasalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outspread, stretched</li><li>having the horns spread out</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i>. Compare <i>πετάννῡμι</i> (petánnūmi, “to spread out”), <i>πέτασος</i> (pétasos, “broad-rimmed hat”) and <i>πέταλον</i> (pétalon, “petal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li></ol><li>to dart, rush; to make haste</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péth₂eti</i> (“to fly”), from the root <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (literally “to spread one’s wings”). Cognates include Old English <i>feþer</i> (English <i>feather</i>), Latin <i>petō</i>, Sanskrit <i>पत॑ति</i> (pátati), Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬙𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (pata<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti), Old Armenian <i>թիռ</i> (tʻiṙ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock formation: cliff, ledge, cave, ridge</li><li>stone (as a building material)</li></ol><p>Unknown; in the absence of an explanation, Beekes prefers a Pre-Greek borrowing, noting that Meier-Brügger's derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-trom</i> (“instrument for getting through”) (from <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to go through, fare”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-trom</i> (“instrument suffix”)) is semantically implausible. Not related to Hindi <i>पत्थर</i> (patthar, “stone”), which is composed of Indo-European elements.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πέτρᾱ</i> (pétrā, “rock, stone”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rocky, made of stone</li><li>changed into rock</li></ol><p>From <i>πέτρα</i> (pétra, “rock”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock, stone, boulder</li><li>type of reed</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek origin. Related to <i>πέτρα</i> (pétra, “rock formation”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πέτρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Peter</li></ol><p>From <i>πέτρος</i> (pétros, “stone, rock”), related to <i>πέτρα</i> (pétra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;péťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pékʷ-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;pekʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πέφραδε</i> (péphrade)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>softening, ripening, or changing by means of heat</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) cooking of food</li><li>(<i>of wine</i>) fermentation</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) digestion of food</li><li>concoction of acrid humors</li><li>concoction, as a function of the animal organs</li></ol><p>From <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō, “to ripen; cook; digest”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rue, a plant from the genus <i>Ruta</i>.</li></ol><p>Compare Old Georgian <i>ტეგანი</i> (ṭegani, “rue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to secure, stick in, fix on</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to fixate upon</li></ol><li>to fasten, put together, unite, build</li><li>to make solid, stiffen, freeze, curdle</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to fix, establish, determine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i> (“to coagulate, become fixed”). Cognates include Latin <i>pangō</i> and Old English <i>fōn</i> (English <i>fang</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leap, bound</li><li>beating or throbbing of the heart</li></ol><p>From <i>πηδάω</i> (pēdáō, “to spring, to leap”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of <i>πηδός</i> (pēdós)</li></ol><p>From <i>πηδός</i> (pēdós, “kind of tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helm; helmet</li><li>crest of the serpent</li></ol><p>The formation is like <i>τράφηξ</i> (tráphēx), <i>ὄρπηξ</i> (órpēx) and <i>νάρθηξ</i> (nárthēx). The suffix <i>-ηξ</i> (-ēx) often occurs in Pre-Greek words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of clay</li></ol><p>From <i>πηλός</i> (pēlós, “clay”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πήματα</i> (pḗmata)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>πήματε</i> (pḗmate)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>πήματι</i> (pḗmati)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thread on the bobbin in the shuttle; woof</li><li>bobbin, spool</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)penh₁-</i> (“to weave, to twist”) and cognate with Ancient Greek <i>πένομαι</i> (pénomai, “to exert oneself”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spinnaną</i> (“to spin”) and Lithuanian <i>pi̇̀nti</i> (“to twist”). Beekes, however, rejects this proposal and leaves the origin open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) bag, haversack, pouch, wallet</li></ol><p>Unclear, though probably from Pre-Greek; possible connection with Latin <i>pero</i> (“soldier's shoes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>πήρᾱ</i> (pḗrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of lizard</li></ol><p>The word has been compared with Sanskrit <i>पिङ्गल</i> (piṅgala, “reddish brown”) and with <i>πίγγαν</i> (píngan, “nestling, chick”). The etymology remains unknown, however the presence of the prenasalization and the suffix <i>-αλ-</i> could lead to a Pre-Greek etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ape, monkey</li><li>trickster, jackanapes</li><li>dwarf</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Commonly connected with Latin <i>foedus</i> (“ugly”). Beekes argues for an origin as a substrate loan-word or perhaps Pre-Greek. The same suffix can be found in <i>ἱέραξ</i> (hiérax, “falcon”) and <i>μύρμηξ</i> (múrmēx, “ant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō)</li></ol><p>A suffixed zero-grade derivative of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰéitʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéydʰeti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large wine jar, cask</li><li>(proverbs,&#32;expressions)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of the task of the Danaids, of labour in vain</li><li>of a Cynic's life, like Diogenes</li><li>very witty, referring to Diogenes</li><li>your purse is deep</li></ol></ol><p>Like many Greek words for pottery, most likely of Pre-Greek origin, as evidenced by the irregular variations between:</p><p>Formerly compared to Old Norse <i>biða</i> (“milk jug/bucket”) and Latin <i>fidēlia</i> (“earthen vessel, pot”), assuming a Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;bʰidʰ-</i>. The apparent cognate in Mycenaean Greek, if related, however rules out an initial labial consonant in the source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of felt</li></ol><p>From <i>πῖλος</i> (pîlos, “felt”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> dative <small>‘with something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to fill full, satisfy, glut</li><li>to fill an office</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to fill (for) oneself</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be full of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be satisfied, to have enough</li><li>(<i>of women</i>) to become pregnant</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“to fill”). Cognates include Latin <i>pleō</i>, Sanskrit <i>पिप॑र्ति</i> (píparti, “to fill”) and Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬨𐬞𐬁𐬟𐬭𐬁𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (hampāfrā<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti).</p><p>The present tense system includes a reduplicated progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kindle, burn, burn down</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become inflated or swollen</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) to spout (blood from a wound)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preh₁-</i> (“to blow, blaze”), and related to Hittite (parai-ᶦ, “to blow, inflate, ignite”). The verb has been influenced in form by <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi). Note also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьrěti</i> (“to rot, decay, moulder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>board, plank</li><li>tablet</li><li>dish, plate, platter, trencher</li><li>board, plate, picture</li><li>table of accounts, register</li><li>block for sharpening knives</li></ol><p>Since Fick (1890), considered a native word of Proto-Indo-European origin, cognate with Sanskrit <i>पिनाक</i> (pínāka, “staff, stick”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pь̏ňь</i> (whence Old Church Slavonic <i>пьнь</i> (pĭnĭ), Russian <i>пень</i> (penʹ, “trunk, stub”)). On the other hand, Beekes inevitably prefers a Pre-Greek origin, though allows that the Slavic terms may be cognate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pen shell (bivalve of the family Pinnidae)</li><li>pearl shell, pearl</li></ol><p>According to Lewy, perhaps from Hebrew <i>פנין</i> (pĕnin, “coral”), but in view of the variation <i>ν/νν</i>, it is more probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>regarding the pearl shell</li></ol><p>From <i>πίνη</i> (pínē, “pearl shell; sea pen”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>absolute</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li></ol><li>to carouse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₃-</i> (“to drink”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>पिबति</i> (pibati), Latin <i>pōtō</i> and <i>bibō</i>, and Albanian <i>pi</i>.</p><p>The present tense system includes a <b>N</b> progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great spotted woodpecker (<i>Dendrocopos major</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably an onomatopoeic word, like synonymous <i>πιπώ</i> (pipṓ, “great spotted woodpecker”) and <i>πιππίζω</i> (pippízō, “to chirp, pipe”), <i>πῖπος</i> (pîpos, “young piping bird”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall, throw oneself down</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;píptō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pípth₂-</i>, reduplicated present from <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (“to fall; to fly”).</p><p>For an unknown reason, the iota is long, as is apparent from the imperative <i>πῖπτε</i> (not <i>&#42;&#42;πῐ́πτε</i> (&#42;&#42;pĭ́pte)). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>पाप॑तीति</i> (pā́patīti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of peas</li></ol><p>From <i>πίσος</i> (písos, “pea”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pea, pease, seed of the plant <i>Pisum sativum</i></li><li>(<i>botany</i>) any plant of species <i>Pisum sativum</i></li></ol><p>Koine. See Ancient Greek <i>πίσος</i> (písos, “pea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pea, pease, seed of <i>Pisum sativum</i></li></ol><p>A loanword from an unknown source, with some possibilities including Thracian and Phrygian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitch</li><li>resin used for treating wine jars</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;píťťa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pik-</i> (“pitch, resin”). Cognate with Latin <i>pix</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьkъlъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from pitch</li></ol><p>From <i>πίσσα</i> (píssa, “pitch”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>πῑ́πτω</i> (pī́ptō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;píptō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pípth₂-</i>, reduplicated present from <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (“to fall; to fly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trust, confidence</li></ol><p>From <i>πῐστεύω</i> (pĭsteúō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis). Compare <i>πῐ́στῐς</i> (pĭ́stĭs), <i>πεῖσῐς</i> (peîsĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trust in others</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) belief, faith</li><li>belief, confidence, assurance (the state of being persuaded of something)</li><li>(<i>in a commercial sense</i>) credit</li><li>faithfulness, honesty, trustworthiness, fidelity</li><li>that which gives assurance: treaty, oath, guarantee</li><li>means of persuasion: argument, proof</li><li>that which is entrusted</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéydʰtis</i>, equivalent to <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “to persuade”) +&lrm; <i>-τις</i> (-tis). Compare the later formation, <i>πεῖσῐς</i> (peîsĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoemaker</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a Pre-Greek word as revealed by the suffix <i>-υγγ-</i> (-ung-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trusting, relying, depending [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘on’</small>]</li></ol><p>Traditionally said to be from the root of <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō), though the derivation is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic and Epic form of <i>τέσσαρες</i> (téssares, “four”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Byzantine form of <i>ὀπῐ́σω</i> (opĭ́sō, “behind; back”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πίττα</i> (pítta), Attic form of <i>πίσσα</i> (píssa)</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) pie</li></ol><p>Attested in the pita or flatbread sense from 1107.<br></p><p>Many theories. Likely related to Neapolitan <i>pizza</i>, also of unknown origin. Dialectological maps show that in Central Italy one has <i>pizza</i> while in the North and Tuscany one has <i>pinza</i>. Only in South Apulia and Calabria one has <i>pitta</i>; this suggests a derivation from Latin <i>pīnctus</i>, <i>pictus</i> (“painted, smeared”) or <i>pīnsum</i>, <i>pīnsitum</i>, <i>pistum</i> (“pounded”), however the northern forms in Italy seem to be contaminated with <i>pinzare</i> (“to staple”). A form <i>peta</i> even coexists in some areas, and according to a different theory, the forms could be derived from an Ancient Greek feminine <i>πηκτή</i> (pēktḗ) of <i>πηκτός</i> (pēktós, “compacted, congealed”) or even from <i>πήτεα</i> (pḗtea, “bran”). While a relation to Lombardic <i>bizzo</i> (“bit, chunk”) and <i>pizza</i> (“dumpling”) is phonetically and semantically dubious, another suggested derivation is from Illyrian, whence Albanian <i>pite</i> (“gruel”) and <i>pitë</i> (“cake, bread, pastry”), further suggested to be related to <i>petë</i> (“layer”). Compare also Romanian <i>pată</i> (“blotch, stain, macula”) and possibly Ancient Greek <i>πιττάκιον</i> (pittákion, “patch; tablet; ticket”) — a word only attested from the 5<sup>th</sup> century, first in the comic Δεινόλοχος, and perhaps derived from the Proto-Indo-European root from which one or more of the Albanian words is inherited. Alternatively, the substrate could also be Jewish Aramaic <i>פִיתָּא</i> (pītā, “piece of bread”), from Proto-Semitic related to crumbs, breaking into tranches, akin to Hebrew <i>פַּת</i> (paṯ, “bread”) and the Arabic forms in the root ف ت ت (f t t), also Tigre / Tigrinya / Amharic / Ge'ez <i>ፈተተ</i> (fätätä, “to break off to portion, to crumb”) with a well-developed root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) alternative form of <i>πίσσα</i> (píssa, “pitch, tar”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweep, stroke of oars</li><li>shower, torrent</li><li>onslaught, attack, fit</li></ol><p>No etymology. The connection with <i>πίπτω</i> (píptō, “to fall”) and <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “to dart, rush”) does not convince.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) husks of corn, bran</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) bran-like eruption on the skin, especially dandruff, scurf</li><li>bran-like sediment in urine</li></ol><p>The formation is like <i>λέπυρον</i> (lépuron), but without a certain explanation. It has been connected to Latin <i>putus</i> (“pure”), Sanskrit <i>पूत</i> (pūtá, “cleansed”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faujaną</i> (“to sift”), but this is highly unlikely. Note the synonymous glosses <i>πήτεα</i> (pḗtea), <i>πητῖται</i> (pētîtai) which are connected with <i>πῆν</i> (pên). Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin in view of the suffix "-ῡρ-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pine</li></ol><p>Beekes is hesitant to espouse any derivation (even the usual Pre-Greek) and leaves the origin open. Traditionally taken as a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peyH-</i>, which is also the source of Latin <i>pīnus</i> (“pine”) and Sanskrit <i>पीतुदारु</i> (pītudāru, “kind of tree”). Compare also Albanian <i>pishë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown kind of bird</li></ol><p>Probably an onomatopoeia, and as such comparable to <i>πιππίζω</i> (pippízō, “to chirp”), <i>πιπώ</i> (pipṓ, “woodpecker”); the suffix is reminiscent of instances such as <i>σάλπιγξ</i> (sálpinx), <i>πέρδιξ</i> (pérdix), and the morphological variation between <i>-ιγξ</i> (-inx) and <i>-ιξ</i> (-ix) strongly points to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fat, rich</li><li>wealthy</li><li>abounding, fertile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peyH-</i> (“fat”). Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>πιμελή</i> (pimelḗ, “soft lard”), Latin <i>pīnguis</i> (“fat, plump”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faitaz</i> (“fat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toy, plaything</li></ol><p>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, “diminutive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>education, teaching</li></ol><p>From <i>παιδεύω</i> (paideúō, “to teach”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play (like a child), sport</li><li>to dance</li><li>to play (a game, musical instrument, etc.)</li><li>to jest, sport</li></ol><p><i>παῖς</i> (paîs) +&lrm; <i>-ι̯ω</i> (-i̯ō), i.e. "to act like a child" > "to play".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>player</li></ol><p>From <i>παίζω</i> (paízō, “I play”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs) using an alternative stem <i>παιγ-</i> (paig-) which is also found in <i>παῖγμα</i> (paîgma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, hit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to strike one thing against another</li><li>to drive away</li><li>(<i>sexual intercourse</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to quench one's thirst</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to strike against or upon</li><li>to devour</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;p(y)eh₂w-</i> (“to hit, strike”). Cognates include Latin <i>paviō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>πηγή</i> (pēgḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(literally,&#32;figuratively) a trap, snare</li></ol><p>From <i>πάγη</i> (págē) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay a snare for, entrap, ensnare, entangle</li></ol><p>From <i>παγίς</i> (pagís) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pankration, a complete contest, an exercise of the Greek youths which combined both wrestling and boxing</li><li>sea onion (<i>Drimia maritima</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>παγκρατής</i> (pankratḗs, “all powerful”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion), from <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all, every”) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “strength, power”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-powerful</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who practises the pankration</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰγκρᾰτῐᾰ́ζω</i> (pănkrătĭắzō, “to practise the pankration”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Subject to feeling or passion: sensitive</li><li>Full of feeling: passionate, sensuous</li><li>pathetic</li><li>passive, receptive</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) passive</li></ol><p>From <i>παθ-</i> (path-) the root of <i>πάσχω</i> (páskhō, “to suffer”), + <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in a sexual intercourse</i>) Remaining passive.</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰ́θος</i> (pắthos, “passivity”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>short for <i>παθογνωμονικός</i> (pathognōmonikós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indicating a particular disease</li></ol><p>From <i>πάθος</i> (páthos, “misfortune”) +&lrm; <i>γνωμονικός</i> (gnōmonikós, “fit to judge of”), the latter from <i>γνώμων</i> (gnṓmōn, “interpreter, discerner”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>treating feeling or sensation</li><li>treating the passions</li><li>treating disease</li></ol><p>From <i>παθολογίᾱ</i> (pathologíā) (from <i>πάθος</i> (páthos, “suffering”)) + <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paean</li><li>song of triumph after victory, war song</li><li>solemn song or chant</li><li>(<i>prosody</i>) paeon</li></ol><p>Uncertain; perhaps extracted from the expression <i>ἰὴ <b>παιήων</b>, ἰὼ <b>παιάν</b></i> (iḕ <b>paiḗōn</b>, iṑ <b>paián</b>), an exclamation to begin songs, and/or ultimately from Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Παιάν)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Paean; in Greek mythology the physician of the gods.</li></ol><p>Uncertain; cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀊𐀺𐀚</i> (pa-ja-wo-ne).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing a paean, sing in triumph</li></ol><p>From <i>παιᾱ́ν</i> (paiā́n) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little child, young child</li></ol><p>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of guiding, attending</li><li>teaching</li><li>attendance</li></ol><p>From <i>παιδαγωγός</i> (paidagōgós, “teacher, leader”), from <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) + <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “I lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to teaching</li><li>suitable for teaching</li><li>guiding, tending</li></ol><p>From <i>παιδαγωγός</i> (paidagōgós, “teacher, tutor”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós), from <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) + <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>originally, a slave who accompanied a child to and from school</li><li>schoolteacher, child tutor; pedagogue.</li><li>guide, leader</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pā́wits</i>Ancient Greek <i>παῖς</i> (paîs)Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓γωγός</i> (ăgōgós)Ancient Greek <i>παιδᾰγωγός</i>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) + <i>ᾰ̓γωγός</i> (ăgōgós, “guide, escort”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upbringing</li><li>training, education</li><li>youth, childhood</li></ol><p>From <i>παιδεύω</i> (paideúō, “rear a child”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā), from <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a pederast, someone who has an erotic relationship with an adolescent boy</li></ol><p>From <i>παιδεραστής</i> (paiderastḗs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) lover of boys, pederast</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “boy”) +&lrm; <i>ἐραστής</i> (erastḗs, “lover”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love of boys, pederasty</li></ol><p>From <i>παιδεραστής</i> (paiderastḗs, “pederast”) (from <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child, son, boy”) + <i>ἐραστής</i> (erastḗs, “lover”), from <i>ἔραμαι</i> (éramai, “to lοve”)) + <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise, bring up a child</li><li>to train, teach, educate</li><li>to chasten, discipline, punish</li></ol><p>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childish play, fun, sport, game</li></ol><p>From <i>παίζω</i> (paízō, “I play”), from <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Belonging to a child</li><li>having to do with children: childish</li></ol><p>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>young (or possibly a noun, young man, as used by Homer)</li><li>childish, infantile</li></ol><p>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teacher of boys</li></ol><p>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>δῐδᾰ́σκᾰλος</i> (dĭdắskălos, “teacher, instructor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;in the plural,&#32;of a board of magistrates) supervisor of education</li></ol><p><i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) childbearing</li></ol><p>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros). Literally, “that through which the child passes”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physical trainer, gymnastic trainer</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō, “to use, to wear down”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(derogatory,&#32;chiefly) lover of boys, pedophile</li></ol><p><i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “boy”) +&lrm; <i>φιλέω</i> (philéō, “love”)</p><p>A regularly declined form of <i>παιδόφῐλος</i> (paidóphĭlos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) love of children</li></ol><p><i>παιδόφῐλος</i> (paidóphĭlos, “loving children”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>killing children</li></ol><p>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder, homicide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of boys, a public officer at Stratonicea</li></ol><p>From <i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>child-loving</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(as <i>Παιδοφίλη</i> (Paidophílē)) an epithet of Demeter</li><li>of over-fond mothers</li></ol></ol><p><i>παῖς</i> (paîs, “child”, stem: παιδ-) +&lrm; <i>-φῐλος</i> (-phĭlos, “-loving”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finest flour or meal</li><li>subtle talker, shrewd person</li></ol><p>In view of the glosses <i>παιπάλλειν</i> (paipállein, “to shake”) and <i>παιπαλώσσω</i> (paipalṓssō), this word could be an intensive reduplication of the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pelh₂-</i> (“to drive, swing, shake”) of <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō, “to brandish a weapon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) rugged, craggy</li></ol><p>From <i>παίπᾰλον</i> (paípălon, “steep place”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis). Connection with various similar-looking but semantically distant words such as <i>παιπάλη</i> (paipálē, “fine flour”) and <i>παιπάλιμος</i> (paipálimos, “subtle”) is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dart, rush about</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wave violently</li></ol><p>Probably a reduplicated intensive present, but the uncertain meaning does not allow us to establish an etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peony (<i>Paeonia officinalis</i>, <i>Paeonia mascula</i>)</li></ol><p>Substantivized feminine form of <i>Παιώνιος</i> (Paiṓnios, “Paeonian, medicinal, healing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physician, healer</li><li>(<i>prosody</i>) paeon</li></ol><p>Earlier <i>&#42;παιά-ϝων</i> (&#42;paiá-wōn) (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀊𐀺𐀚</i> (pa-ja-wo-ne /⁠Paiāwonei⁠/)), related to <i>παιάν</i> (paián, “choral song, hymn, war song”), which see for more.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Παιών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic and Ionic form of <i>Παιᾱ́ν</i> (Paiā́n)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cake of preserved fruit, fruitcake</li></ol><p>Has often been connected with <i>πλάσσω</i> (plássō, “to knead”), <i>παλάμη</i> (palámē, “flat of the hand”) and <i>πέλανος</i> (pélanos, “dough, pulp”). However, the semantics of this etymology are not compelling. Furnée compares <i>παλάσια</i> (palásia, “harvested figs”), suggesting a Pre-Greek etymology due to the variation “θ/σ”, but this points to Semitic origin, since “fig” is precisely what Amharic / Tigrinya / Tigre / Gurage / Ge'ez <i>በለስ</i> (bäläs) and Arabic بَلَس (balas) mean and that Ethiopic word even translates the Greek in the Bibles.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palm, hand</li><li>device, cunning</li><li>handiwork, work of art</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;palámā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pl̥h₂(e)meh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat”). Cognates include Latin <i>palma</i>, Old Irish <i>lám</i>, Old English <i>folm</i>, and Albanian <i>shpullë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to besprinkle, defile, bespatter</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be scattered abroad</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly connected to <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō, “to swing”), though this verb itself has no certain etymology. One would have to posit a Proto-Hellenic root <i>&#42;pal-</i> with the suffix <i>-άσσω</i>, also found in <i>σταλάσσω</i> (stalássō), <i>αἱμάσσω</i> (haimássō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>back-driven, wandering backwards</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλιν</i> (pálin, “back, backwards”) +&lrm; <i>πλαγκτός</i> (planktós, “wandering, roaming”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scratched or scraped again</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλιν</i> (pálin, “again”) +&lrm; <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running back again, recurring</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) uncertain</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλιν</i> (pálin, “again”) +&lrm; <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos, “racecourse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a bow</i>) stretched or bent backward</li><li>caused by opposite tensions</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλιν</i> (pálin, “again”) +&lrm; <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to stretch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Christ's thorn (<i>Paliurus spina-christi</i>)</li></ol><p>A connection with <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”) makes little sense, nor it is to be taken with <i>οὖρον</i> (oûron, “urine”). The word is most probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>roaring with ebb and flow</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλιν</i> (pálin, “again”) +&lrm; <i>ῥόχθος</i> (rhókhthos, “roaring of the sea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrestling school, palaestra</li></ol><p>From <i>παλαίω</i> (palaíō).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Ionic) superlative degree of <i>πᾰλαιός</i> (pălaiós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Ionic) comparative degree of <i>πᾰλαιός</i> (pălaiós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrestle</li><li>to overcome</li><li>to endeavour</li></ol><p>From <i>πά̆λη</i> (pá̆lē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) crazy, foolish</li></ol><p>Multiple theories exist. Either from Ladino <i>פאלאברה</i>/<i>palavra</i> (“word; boastful talk”) through an unattested intermediate form <i>&#42;παλάβια</i> (&#42;palábia), or alternatively related to Pontic Greek <i>παλαλός</i> (palalós) which is from <i>ἀπολωλός</i> (apolōlós), participle of Koine Greek <i>ἀπόλλυμι</i> (apóllumi, “to vanish, to slip away”). A relation to dialectal Aeolic Greek <i>παλαός</i> (palaós) has also been suggested.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born long ago, full of years</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλαι</i> (pálai, “long ago”) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the ancients (those who lived long ago)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cobbler</li></ol><p><i>πᾰλαιός</i> (pălaiós, “old [thing]”) +&lrm; <i>ῥᾰ́πτω</i> (rhắptō, “I stitch”, future stem: <i>ῥᾰφ-</i>, <i>rhaph-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming agent nouns) = “stitcher of old things”</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrestler</li><li>rival, adversary</li><li>candidate, suitor</li></ol><p>From <i>παλαίω</i> (palaíō) +&lrm; <i>-ιστής</i> (-istḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superintendent of a wrestling-school</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰλαίστρᾱ</i> (pălaístrā, “wrestling-school”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old; aged</li><li>ancient; of times past</li><li>time-honoured; venerable</li><li>antiquated; outdated</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλαι</i> (pálai, “long ago”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, “adjectival suffix”). Likely cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀨𐀍</i> (pa-ra-jo), which has been argued to mean “old”; this connection points to a Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;palai(y)ós</i> and casts doubt on the popularly proposed etymology from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Epic,&#32;Ionic) superlative degree of <i>πᾰλαιός</i> (pălaiós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Epic,&#32;Ionic) comparative degree of <i>πᾰλαιός</i> (pălaiós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retailer of imported produce</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰ́λῐν</i> (pắlĭn, “back, backwords”) +&lrm; <i>κᾰ́πηλος</i> (kắpēlos, “retail dealer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to be driven or turned back</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰ́λῐν</i> (pắlĭn) +&lrm; <i>πλᾰ́ζω</i> (plắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the tide</i>) recurring</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰλῐ́νδρομος</i> (pălĭ́ndromos, “recurring”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of Christ's thorn</li></ol><p>From <i>παλίουρος</i> (palíouros, “Christ's thorn”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>concubine, young girl</li></ol><p>The word may well be Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix "-ακ-". Similarly, Levin regards Latin <i>paelex</i> (“mistress”) as a loanword from a Mediterranean language, maybe a Semitic one, in view of Hebrew <i>פִּילֶגֶשׁ</i> (pilegesh, “concubine”). Other connections that have been proposed include Middle Irish <i>airech</i> (“concubine, wanton woman”), Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬌𐬭𐬌𐬐𐬁</i> (pa<sup><small>i</small></sup>rikā, “beautiful women seducing pious men”), Middle Persian (/⁠parīg⁠/), Khotanese <i>𑀧𑀮𑀻𑀓𑀸</i> (palīkā), and Old Armenian <i>պարիկ</i> (parik), all of which Beekes dismisses for formal or semantic reasons.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son by a concubine</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰλλᾰκή</i> (păllăkḗ, “concubine”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quivering motion, pulsation, throbbing, twitching</li><li>palpitation (<i>of the heart</i>)</li><li>vibration, rapid motion</li><li>impetus (<i>of a projectile</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πά̆λλω</i> (pá̆llō, “to sway, swing”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>missile, dart</li><li>light spear used by the Persian cavalry</li><li>projectile discharged by a catapult</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>παλτός</i> (paltós, “brandished, hurled”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brandished, hurled</li></ol><p>From <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō, “to brandish”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew, sprinkle</li><li>to bestrew, besprinkle</li><li>to besmear</li><li>to cover lightly</li></ol><p>Formation similar to <i>μολύνω</i> (molúnō, “to stain, defile”) and <i>φορύνω</i> (phorúnō, “to be mingled”). Either a direct derivative of <i>πάλη</i> (pálē, “fine meal”) +&lrm; <i>-ύνω</i> (-únō) or from an unattested <i>u-</i>stem from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“flour, dust”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute monarch</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, all) +&lrm; <i>βᾰσῐλεύς</i> (băsĭleús, “king, monarch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready or sufficient for every battle</li><li>fighting by all means, with all one's resources</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “very, all”) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “battle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very great, immense</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-) +&lrm; <i>μέγεθος</i> (mégethos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all variegated, of rich and varied work</li><li>(<i>of fawn skin</i>) all spotted</li><li>manifold, various, diverse</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “very, all”) +&lrm; <i>ποικίλος</i> (poikílos, “spotted, parti-colored”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) bright-shining, radiant</li></ol><p>Present participle of an unattested variant form of <i>παμφαίνω</i> (pamphaínō), from <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) + <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panacea, universal remedy</li><li>Hercules' woundwort (<i>Opopanax hispidus</i>)</li><li>lovage (<i>Levisticum officinale</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰνᾰκής</i> (pănăkḗs, “all-healing”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wholly unblemished, immaculate</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Most Immaculate, a title of the Virgin Mary</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “whole, all”) +&lrm; <i>ἄχραντος</i> (ákhrantos, “immaculate, unblemished”)</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πανάχραντος)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Most Immaculate, a title of the Virgin Mary</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-, “whole, all”) +&lrm; <i>ἄχραντος</i> (ákhrantos, “immaculate, unblemished”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a general or national assembly</li><li>festival (event or community gathering)</li></ol><p><i>παν-</i> (pan-) +&lrm; <i>ἄγυρις</i> (águris), Aeolic form of <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́, “marketplace”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-variegated, sparkling (of armor)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) manifold</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-) +&lrm; <i>αἰόλος</i> (aiólos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-healing</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́κος</i> (ắkos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with the whole people, en masse</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hostess, female innkeeper</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰνδοκεύω</i> (păndokeúō, “entertain as a host”) +&lrm; <i>-τρῐᾰ</i> (-trĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inn</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πανδοκεῖον</i> (pandokeîon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pandura, a three-stringed lute</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be president of a panegyris</li></ol><p>From <i>πανηγυριάρχης</i> (panēguriárkhēs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a public festival or assembly</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) solemn, festive</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) flattering, false</li><li>(<i>of style</i>) showy, ostentatious</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) pompous, conceited</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰνήγῠρῐς</i> (pănḗgŭrĭs, “festival, assembly”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doing something all day</li></ol><p><i>παν-</i> (pan-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>ἡμέρα</i> (hēméra, “day”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the entire catch or capture by a hunter, especially of wildfowl</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, all) +&lrm; <i>θήρᾱ</i> (thḗrā, “animal, wild beast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) flowering, giver of all bloom</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>θάλλω</i> (thállō, “to thrive, bloom”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of fears</i>) panic, groundless</li></ol><p>Generic use of <i>Πᾱνικός</i> (Pānikós, “pertaining to Pan”), from <i>Πᾱ́ν</i> (Pā́n) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>night festival</li><li>vigil</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-) +&lrm; <i>νῠ́ξ</i> (nŭ́x) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-night</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰν-</i> (păn-, “all”) +&lrm; <i>νῠ́ξ</i> (nŭ́x, “night”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suit of armour</li></ol><p>From <i>πάνοπλος</i> (pánoplos, “in full armour”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all, every”) + <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon, “armour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knavery</li><li>adulteration of drugs or honey</li></ol><p>From <i>πανοῦργος</i> (panoûrgos, “wicked, knavish”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knavish trick, villainy, sophistry</li></ol><p>From <i>πανουργέω</i> (panourgéō, “to play the knave”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>derogatory</i>) (literally&#58; "ready to do anything", but nearly always in a bad sense) knavish, roguish, villainous, treacherous, sly</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>πᾶν</i> (pân, “everything”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, “noun of result or an abstract noun of action”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with the whole army</li></ol><p><i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all”) +&lrm; <i>στρατιᾱ́</i> (stratiā́, “army”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>πᾰνστρᾰτῐᾷ</i> (pănstrătĭāî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) with all speed</li></ol><p>From <i>παν-</i> (pan-) + the root of <i>σεύω</i> (seúō, “to chase, rush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>most wretched</li></ol><p>From <i>παντο-</i> (panto-, “all, every”) +&lrm; <i>ἄθλιος</i> (áthlios, “struggling, unhappy, wretched, miserable”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>all in all, altogether, wholly</li><li><i>(in affirmative responses)</i> by all means, undoubtedly</li></ol><p><i>πάντα</i> (pánta) +&lrm; <i>πᾶσι</i> (pâsi), both inflected forms of <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from all sides, from all directions, on all sides</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>everywhere</li><li>on every side</li><li>in every direction, every way</li><li>by all means, absolutely, in all respects</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>in every part of</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>completely, entirely, absolutely, outright</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One female person who is all-powerful, omnipotent</li></ol><p>From <i>παντο-</i> (panto-), combining form of <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all”) + the stem of <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power, strength”) + <i>-τειρα</i> (-teira, “female agent-noun suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who is all-powerful, omnipotent</li></ol><p>From <i>παντο-</i> (panto-), combining form of <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all”) + the stem of <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power, strength”) + <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “agent-noun suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all-powerful, omnipotent</li></ol><p>From <i>παντο-</i> (panto-), combining form of <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all”), + <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power, strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>all kinds of, all sorts of</li></ol><p><i>πᾶς</i> (pâs) +&lrm; <i>οἷος</i> (hoîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch</li></ol><p>Unexplained, but Furnée thinks that the word is from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of biscuit</li></ol><p>From <i>Πάξαμος</i> (Páxamos), the name of a baker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>janitor or keeper of the palace</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) parrot</li></ol><p>From Arabic بَبَّغَاء (babbaḡāʔ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to peer around, look about cautiously</li></ol><p>An intensive reduplicated formation. It could be connected with <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “to fly”), assuming the image of a restless gaze, but this is hardly compelling. There is no good Indo-European etymology. Some Cypriot glosses from Hesychius have been connected, like <i>ἰμπάταον</i> (impátaon, “looked at”) and <i>ἰνκαπάταον</i> (inkapátaon), suggesting a Pre-Greek etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of papyrus</li></ol><p>From <i>πάπῡρος</i> (pápūros, “papyrus”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Byzantine,&#32;Christianity) <i>accent perispomene</i> <i>for</i> <i>παπάς</i> (papás): father (priest)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>from</li><li>because of</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>at, beside, by, near</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>contrary to</li><li>beside, by, near (<i>w/ verbs of coming or going; w/ verbs of past motion; w/ verbs of striking or wounding</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pərai</i>, apparently from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preh₂-</i> (“before, in front”) and/or <i>&#42;per-</i> (identical meaning), the details of which are disputed. Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀫</i> (pa-ro).</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of origin or cause. The dative is from the locative. The accusative is perhaps from a metaphor of going near someone to attack them; for the semantics, compare <i>ἀντί</i> (antí) and Latin <i>ante</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stuffed, forced in</li><li>small or supplementary couch</li><li>pushed aside or into a corner</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>law</i>) <b>παράβυστον</b> δικαστήριον Athenian court lying in an obscure place where the <i>ἕνδεκα</i> (héndeka, “'Eleven'”) held meetings or held the accused. They were responsible for policing the city.</li></ol></ol><p>Verbal adjective from <i>πᾰρᾰβῡ́ω</i> (părăbū́ō, “stuff in, insert”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short stroke in the margin marking a break in sense</li></ol><p>From <i>παρά</i> (pará, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead by or past a place</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to march the men up from the side, to bring them from column into line</li><li>to mislead</li><li>to lead to or into a thing [<i>with</i> <b>ἔς</b> (és)]</li><li>to induce [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>to alter the course of</li><li>to introduce</li><li>to bring forward (<i>as a witness</i>)</li><li>to bring in secretly or stealthily</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to pass by, pass on one's way, depart</li><li>(Koine,&#32;also&#32;passive voice) to pass away</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pattern, model, plan, paradigm</li><li>precedent, example</li><li>lesson, warning</li><li>argument, proof from example</li><li>foil, contrast</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) paradigm</li></ol><p>From <i>παραδείκνυμι</i> (paradeíknumi, “I show, compare”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the garden owned by the Persian nobility</li><li>enclosed park, pleasure-ground</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Garden of Eden</li><li>the afterlife location of the blessed: paradise</li></ol><p>An Iranian borrowing, ultimately from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;paridayjah</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Παράδεισος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Garden of Eden</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contrary to expectation, strange, incredible</li></ol><p>From <i>παρά</i> (pará, “contrary to”) +&lrm; <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa, “expectation”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transmission, transfer</li><li>granting, bestowal</li><li>giving up, surrender</li><li>tradition</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>δόσις</i> (dósis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ill-timed.</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ) +&lrm; <i>καιρός</i> (kairós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a calling to one's aid; a summons</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a calling upon, plea, imploring, appealing</li></ol><li>an exhortation; an address</li><li>consolation</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰκᾰλέω</i> (părăkăléō, “to summon, encourage”) (from <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ, “near”) + <i>κᾰλέω</i> (kăléō, “to call”)) + <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>called to aid; helping</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an advocate or legal assistant</li><li>an intercessor (one who intervenes)</li><li>a comforter or helper; a paraclete</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>κλητός</i> (klētós, “called or invited [one]”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Παράκλητος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Holy Spirit; Paraclete</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ́κλητος</i> (paráklitos, “comforter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-, “alongside”) +&lrm; <i>κοίτη</i> (koítē, “bed”), thus literally ‘bed partner’.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part of a chariot-wheel</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle, ring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neglect</li><li>omission</li><li>paraleipsis</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰλείπω</i> (părăleípō, “to pass over”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>by the sea</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) the seacoast, seaboard, in particular the eastern coast of Attica, between Hymettus and the sea</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ἅλς</i> (háls) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in general</i>) alternation</li><li>alternating motion</li><li>change, deviation, mutation</li><li>change of position</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) parallax</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰλλᾰ́σσω</i> (părăllắssō, “to cause to alternate”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beside one another, side-by-side</li><li>parallel to or with</li><li>pleonastic, redundant</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλλήλων</i> (allḗlōn, “one another”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beyond calculation, unexpected, casual, uncertain</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) unexpected event</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter&#32;plural) portions of food given to unexpected guests</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) unexpected issue</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paralysis</li></ol><p>From <i>παραλύω</i> (paralúō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>derangement, madness</li></ol><p>From <i>παρανοέω</i> (paranoéō, “to think amiss”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acting contrary to law and custom, lawless, violent</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) contrary to law, unlawful, illegal</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Attic law,&#32;substantive) an illegal or unconstitutional measure</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>παρά</i> (pará, “beyond”) + <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) bridegroom's friend or best man</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) bridesmaid, one of the dramatis personae</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>νύμφη</i> (númphē, “bride”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-foreign, counterfeit</li><li>strange, extraordinary</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parapegma, astronomical calendar inscribed on a surface with moveable pegs for days</li></ol><p>From <i>παρά</i> (pará) +&lrm; <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “I fasten, establish”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a voyage along the coast</li><li>a point sailed by or doubled</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>πλόος</i> (plóos) and/or <i>παραπλέω</i> (parapléō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contracted form of <i>παράπλοος</i> (paráploos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>veneered with boxwood</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>πύξος</i> (púxos, “boxwood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasite (one who eats at the table of another)</li><li>priest who had his meal at the public expense</li><li>one who dines with a superior officer</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos, “wheat; corn”).</p><p>Compare typologically Russian <i>нахле́бник</i> (naxlébnik) (&lt; <i>хлеб</i> (xleb)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a placing side by side</li><li>marshaling, line of battle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>front rank of the phalanx</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>παρατάσσω</i> (paratássō, “to place side by side”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goods which a bride brings over and above her dowry</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>φερνή</i> (phernḗ, “a dowry”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paraphrase</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰφρᾰ́ζω</i> (părăphrắzō, “to paraphrase”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Anything poured in beside.</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>ἔγχῠμα</i> (énkhŭma, “instillation, content of a vessel”), from <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in, into”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, nominal suffix), given by the Greek anatomist Erasistratus to the peculiar substance of the lungs, liver, kidneys, and spleen, as if formed separately by the veins that run into them.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>along past, close by</li><li>away from the point, evasively</li><li>differently</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) outside of</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) beyond, away from, along beyond</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) without the knowledge of, unbeknownst to</li></ol><p><i>παρά</i> (pará) +&lrm; <i>ἔκ</i> (ék)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a putting in beside, injection</li><li>insertion</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) parenthesis</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρεντῐ́θημῐ</i> (părentĭ́thēmĭ, “to put in beside, mix up”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis), from <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside”) + <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) + <i>τῐ́θημῐ</i> (tĭ́thēmĭ, “to put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insertion</li><li>(<i>in tactics</i>) insertion (e.g. of light-armed troops among hoplites)</li></ol><p>From <i>παρεντάττω</i> (parentáttō, “to insert”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>παρέκ</i> (parék)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>παρέκ</i> (parék)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go by, beside, or past; to pass by, pass</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of time</i>) to pass</li></ol><li>to pass by, outstrip, surpass</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to outwit, escape, elude</li></ol><li>to pass on and come to a place, arrive at</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to pass in</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li></ol><li>to pass without heeding</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to overstep, transgress</li></ol><li>to pass unnoticed, escape the notice of</li><li>(<i>in Attic</i>) to come forward to speak</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “by, near”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “to come, go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hold beside, hold in readiness, to furnish, provide, supply</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of natural objects</i>) to supply, yield, produce</li><li>(<i>of incorporeal things</i>) to afford, cause, bring, grant, give</li></ol><li>to present or offer for a purpose</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>)</li><li>to give oneself up, submit oneself</li><li>(<i>with reflexive pronoun and a predicative</i>) to show or exhibit oneself so and so</li></ol><li>to allow, grant</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be allowed, easy; it is in one's power</li></ol><li>(<i>in Attic</i>) to produce a person on demand</li><li>(<i>with a predicative added</i>) to make so and so</li></ol><li>(<i>middle, perfect passive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to supply of oneself or from one's own means</li><li>(<i>of natural objects</i>) to furnish, present, exhibit</li><li>(<i>of works</i>)</li><li>(<i>of incorporeal things</i>) to display on one's own part</li><li>(<i>in Attic law</i>) to bring forward as a witness, as proof for oneself</li><li>to have as one's own, produce as one's own; to acknowledge as one's own</li><li>to offer, promise</li><li>to make so and so for or towards oneself</li><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) to make up, amount to, give</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have, hold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have come alongside</li><li>to lie beside, stretch along</li><li>to pass in any direction</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) to be past</li><li>(<i>of fever</i>) to remit</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to come to an end</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, beside) +&lrm; <i>ἥκω</i> (hḗkō, “to have come, be present”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) seated beside, sitting beside</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ἥμενος</i> (hḗmenos). Apparently the perfect passive participle of an otherwise unattested verb <i>πάρημαι</i> (párēmai) (listed as such by all dictionaries).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic) cheek</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to cause to stand by, to place beside</li><li>to set before the mind, present; to inspire</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to dispose (put in place) a person</li><li>to represent, describe in poetry</li><li>to furnish, supply, deliver</li><li>to make good, prove, show</li><li>to present, offer, commend</li><li>to render</li><li>(<i>later Greek</i>) to produce in court</li></ol><li>to set side by side, compare</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to stand by, stand near</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to stand by, help, defend</li></ol><li>to come, to be at hand</li><li>to come over to a side, to change opinion, come to terms, surrender, submit</li><li>to happen, occur</li><li>to occur to one (as a thought)</li><li>to be disposed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to collect oneself</li><li>to improve with age (of wine), ripen</li></ol><li>to be beside oneself</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to be passionately devoted to</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ, “near”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>beside</li><li>contrary to</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Epic and poetic form of <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhortation, address</li><li>advice, counsel</li></ol><p>From <i>παραινέω</i> (parainéō) (from <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>αἰνέω</i> (ainéō)) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earnest prayer</li><li>deprecation</li><li>intercession, (act of) begging off</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) abdication, disclaimer</li></ol><p>From <i>παραιτέομαι</i> (paraitéomai) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being in part the cause, sharing the blame</li><li>(<i>in bad sense</i>) accessory to a crime</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>αἴτιος</i> (aítios, “responsible, culpable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw beside, by or to, to hold out</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to feed (<i>an animal</i>)</li><li>to cast in one's teeth</li><li>to expose (to danger)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to risk, hazard, gamble with</li><li>to lay beside, compare with</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to vie with one another</li><li>to bring alongside</li><li>to throw, turn, bend sideways</li><li>to deposit with, entrust to</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to deceive, betray</li><li>to come near, approach</li><li>to go by sea, cross over</li><li>to turn aside, pass over</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to suggest</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand beside</li><li>to overstep, transgress</li><li>to pass over, omit, escape</li><li>to come forward</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “down”) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>take a side look, peep out of the corner of one's eye</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>look suspiciously</li></ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>see wrong</li><li>overlook</li><li>despise</li></ol></ol><p><i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “near”) +&lrm; <i>βλέπω</i> (blépō, “see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to aid, come to the rescue (of)</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>βοηθέω</i> (boēthéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a placing of two things side by side, juxtaposition</li><li>comparison, illustration, analogy</li><li>parable, proverb</li><li>side by side meeting or engagement, broadside</li><li>venture</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) conjunction</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) division</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) parabola</li></ol><p>From <i>παραβάλλω</i> (parabállō, “to set side by side”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>parabolic, of or pertaining to a parable</li></ol><p><i>παραβολή</i> (parabolḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only&#32;in the plural</i>) parabalani, persons who risk their lives as nurses</li></ol><p>An adaptation of the Latin <i>parabolānī</i>, the plural of <i>parabolānus</i>, itself ultimately from the Ancient Greek <i>πᾰρᾰ́βολος</i> (părắbolos, “hazardous, perilous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be beside; to be by or near</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to come to one's side; to come to aid; to stand by; to second; to support</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to be at hand; to be gained; to accrue to one</li></ol><li>to come to; to to arrive</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to come to maturity</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside; near”) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to become”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic, Koine, and Byzantine form of <i>πᾰρᾰγῐ́γνομαι</i> (părăgĭ́gnomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass on, transmit message</li><li>to give orders</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to recommend, exhort</li><li>to summon</li></ol><li>to encourage</li></ol><p><i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-) +&lrm; <i>ἀγγέλλω</i> (angéllō, “carry, deliver, announce”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write in addition</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to add a clause to a law or contract</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “write”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transport, conveyance</li><li>production, creation</li><li>massage</li><li>sideways movement</li><li>persuasion (act of persuading)</li><li>deviation</li><li>crime, offence</li><li>deception, deceit</li><li>language alteration; dialect</li><li>derivation</li><li>(grammar,&#32;prosody) paragoge</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγή</i> (agōgḗ, “transporting, leading”, noun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give or hand over to another, transmit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to give a city or person into another's hands</li><li>to give up to justice</li><li>to hand down legends, opinions, etc.</li></ol><li>to grant, bestow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to allow one to</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to permit</li></ol><li>to hazard</li></ol><p>From <i>παρά</i> (pará, “to”) + <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi, “to give”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold up to viewing, exhibit, point out</li><li>to set as an example or model</li><li>to compare</li><li>to demonstrate</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “beside, near”) +&lrm; <i>δείκνυμι</i> (deíknumi, “to show”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a public example of, shame, disgrace, expose</li></ol><p>From <i>παρά</i> (pará, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>δειγματίζω</i> (deigmatízō, “to show, demonstrate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting of or serving as a model, pattern or example</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ́δειγμᾰ</i> (părắdeigmă, “model, pattern, example”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tale of wonder, marvel</li></ol><p>From <i>παράδοξος</i> (parádoxos, “strange”) +&lrm; <i>-λογῐ́ᾱ</i> (-logĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrator of marvels</li></ol><p>From <i>παράδοξος</i> (parádoxos, “unexpected, strange”) +&lrm; <i>-λόγος</i> (-lógos, “to gather”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be handed down</li><li>to be given up</li></ol><p>From <i>παραδίδωμι</i> (paradídōmi) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anything entrusted to one, a deposit</li><li>A hostage</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>by the sea, seaside</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>θάλασσα</i> (thálassa) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beg of or from, ask as a favor</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to move by entreaty, obtain leave from</li><li>to entreat one to do or be something</li></ol><li>to avert by entreaty</li><li>to intercede for, beg off</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to decline, shun</li></ol></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call in, summon, send for</li><li>to appeal</li><li>to urge</li><li>to exhort, beseech</li><li>to comfort</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “near”) +&lrm; <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō, “call”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) deposit of money or property entrusted to one's care</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>καταθήκη</i> (katathḗkē, “deposit of money”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) present perfect tense</li></ol><p>Perfect participle of <i>πᾰρᾰ́κειμαι</i> (părắkeimai, “lie beside or before”), from <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-) +&lrm; <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai). The noun is a substantivization in Hellenistic Koine with ellipsis of <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos, “tense”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lover’s complaint sung at his mistress’s door, serenade</li><li>paraklausithyron</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰκλαίω</i> (părăklaíō, “weep beside, whine beside”) +&lrm; <i>θῠ́ρᾱ</i> (thŭ́rā, “door”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide oneself</li><li>to conceal one's views</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside, contrary to”) +&lrm; <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie with, sleep with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(sex,&#32;euphemistic) to have sex, sleep with</li></ol></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>λέχομαι</i> (lékhomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) penult, penultima (the penultimate syllable, or last syllable but one, of a word)</li></ol><p>The noun is an ellipsis of <i>ἡ πᾰρᾰλήγουσᾰ σῠλλᾰβή</i> (hē părălḗgousă sŭllăbḗ, “the penultimate syllable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be next to the end, to be penultimate</li></ol><p><i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside, near”) +&lrm; <i>λήγω</i> (lḗgō, “cease, terminate”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive from another</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to take upon oneself, undertake</li><li>to take in pledge</li><li>to receive by hearing or report, to ascertain</li><li>to take up, catch up</li></ol><li>(<i>with personal accusative</i>) to take to oneself, associate with oneself</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to invite</li><li>to wait for, intercept</li><li>to take prisoner</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-) +&lrm; <i>λαμβᾰ́νω</i> (lambắnō, “to take, receive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pass over, pass by</li><li>to leave out, omit</li><li>to neglect</li><li>to cease</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “by, near”) +&lrm; <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “to leave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) parallelepiped</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>πᾰρᾰλληλεπίπεδος</i> (părăllēlepípedos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) having parallel faces</li></ol><p>From <i>παράλληλος</i> (parállēlos, “parallel”) +&lrm; <i>ἐπίπεδος</i> (epípedos, “plane”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equating of payments</li><li>placing side by side, repetition of ideas in a compound</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰλληλῐ́ζω</i> (părăllēlĭ́zō, “to place side by side”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bounded by parallel lines</li><li>(geometry,&#32;nominalized,&#32;neuter) parallelogram</li></ol><p>From <i>παράλληλος</i> (parállēlos, “parallel”) +&lrm; <i>γράμμα</i> (grámma, “line”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repetition</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ́λληλος</i> (părắllēlos, “pleonastic, redundant”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false reasoning, fallacy</li><li>weakness of reasoning power</li><li>deception, fraud</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰλογῐ́ζομαι</i> (părălogĭ́zomai, “to cheat; to use fallacies”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>paralyzed</li></ol><p>From <i>παραλύω</i> (paralúō, “to paralyse”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>take off, detach</li><li>to undo</li><li>to loose in addition</li><li>to weaken, to cause sickness or illness</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>λύω</i> (lúō, “loosen; destroy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stay near, stand beside</li><li>to stand one's ground, stand fast</li><li>to stay behind</li><li>to survive</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to endure, last</li></ol><p>From <i>παρά</i> (pará, “beside”) + <i>μένω</i> (ménō, “I remain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neglect (fail to care for)</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ἀμελέω</i> (ameléō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transgression of law, decency or order</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ́νομος</i> (părắnomos, “lawless, violent”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send past, convey past or through</li><li>to send by or along the coast</li><li>to escort, convoy</li><li>to send troops to the flank</li><li>to pass on to</li><li>to send away, dismiss</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is spread before a thing, hanging, curtain</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) screen, cover</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) mantlets</li></ol><p>From <i>παραπετάννυμαι</i> (parapetánnumai, “to be hung before”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike on the side, strike falsely</li><li>(<i>music</i>) to play a false note</li><li>to commit a folly, be foolish</li><li>to fall away</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> +&lrm; <i>παίω</i> (“strike”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail by, close</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to escape</li></ol><p><i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (“beside, near”) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (“sail, float”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nearly resembling</li><li>nearly equal, about equal</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>πλησίος</i> (plēsíos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasang: a Persian unit of length equal to 4 Roman miles</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;frasanhvah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, make ready</li><li>to procure</li><li>to make</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>σκευάζω</i> (skeuázō, “to make, prepare”) or <i>παρασκευή</i> (paraskeuḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparation, preparing</li><li>providing, procuring; way of providing or procuring</li><li>the things being prepared, provided, or procured</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>military</i>) armaments: arms, transportation, etc.</li></ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Judaism) the day of preparation before the (Jewish) Sabbath; Friday; with or without <i>ἡμέρᾱ</i> (hēmérā)</li></ol><p>From <i>παρασκευάζω</i> (paraskeuázō, “to prepare”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Παρασκευή)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Friday (day of preparation for the Sabbath)</li></ol><p>From <i>παρασκευή</i> (paraskeuḗ, “preparation”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything standing beside</li><li>door post</li><li>enclosed space</li><li>vestibule</li></ol><p>From <i>παρίστημι</i> (parístēmi).</p><p>Inflection of <i>παρίστημι</i> (parístēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rip, split, open, tear up lengthwise</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>σχίζω</i> (skhízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place side by side, draw up in battle order, range (be placed in order)</li><li>(<i>middle voice&#32;and&#32;passive voice</i>) to stand prepared</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>τάσσω</i> (tássō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place or set by or before</li><li>to serve (<i>food and drink</i>)</li><li>to provide, give</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to put up as a stake; to wager, risk, stake</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch out along, beside, or near; to extend, deploy; to extend along; to be stretched at length, laid low</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to stretch on the rack, torture; to be tortured; to be worn out</li><li>to prolong, protract</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to delay</li></ol><li>to apply a figure to a straight line</li><li>(<i>of the bowels</i>) to relax</li><li>to lengthen in pronunciation; (<i>of sound, of echo</i>) to prolong</li><li>to stretch or lie beside or along</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to extend; to extend over</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) to extend; to continue one's life</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “beside, by”) +&lrm; <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to stretch out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who watches, scrutinizes: observer, overseer</li></ol><p>From <i>παρατηρέω</i> (paratēréō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to happen to be nearby or present</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, straightaway</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any side-growth, hence:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>botany</i>) offshoot, sucker</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) branch of an animal's vein</li><li>certain appendages in lobsters</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) branch of a discussion</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>παραφύω</i> (paraphúō, “to grow beside”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the spot, forthwith, immediately</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ) and <i>χρῆμᾰ</i> (khrêmă, “matter, affair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet, moist, damp</li></ol><p>The variations show that the word is Pre-Greek. Furnée further adduces <i>ἄρδω</i> (árdō, “to irrigate”), but this is unlikely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reddish-brown snake sacred to Asclepius</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρειά</i> (păreiá, “cheek”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās, nominal suffix), after the light spots on both sides of the neck.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) cerebellum</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-) +&lrm; <i>ἐγκέφᾰλος</i> (enképhălos, “brain”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) cheek</li><li>cheekpiece of a helmet</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digression; extrapolation</li><li>commentary; scholia (a compilation of critical remarks on a text or topic)</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “near, alongside”) +&lrm; <i>ἐκβολή</i> (ekbolḗ, “something thrown out; digression”), from <i>ἐκβᾰ́λλω</i> (ekbắllō, “to throw out”), from <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “from; out of”) + <i>βᾰ́λλω</i> (bắllō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn aside, divert</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insertion, interpolation</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) parenthesis; interjection</li><li>drawing up in battle-order</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>company of soldiers</li><li>encampment</li></ol><li>(in boxing and wrestling,&#32;with <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō)) the act of tripping (<i>an opponent</i>) by twisting the leg</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρεμβᾰ́λλω</i> (părembắllō, “to insert, interpolate”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something put in beside, addition</li><li>smaller wares taken as an addition to the cargo</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to persuade by indirect means, talk around, win over</li><li>to give advice</li><li>to speak deceitfully or insincerely</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to address, exhort</li><li>to advise</li><li>to console, appease</li></ol><p>From <i>παρήγορος</i> (parḗgoros, “consoling, comforting”) (from <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ἀγορεύω</i> (agoreúō)) + <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feverfew (<i>Tanacetum parthenium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>παρθένος</i> (parthénos, “virgin”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young, unmarried woman; maiden</li><li>virgin</li><li>pupil</li><li>epithet of various goddesses, most commonly of Athena</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) Parthenon</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) the constellation Virgo</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maidenly</li><li>chaste</li></ol><p>Conventionally reconstructed from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;parsteno-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥-stén-o-s</i> (with development of PIE <i>&#42;-rst-</i> &gt; Greek <i>-ρθ-</i> (-rth-)), from <i>&#42;pstḗn</i> (“breast”).</p><p>Etruscan <i>𐌚𐌀𐌓𐌈𐌀𐌍𐌀</i> (farθana), 𐌚𐌀𐌓𐌈𐌍𐌄 (farθne, “virgin, maiden”) is usually explained as a loanword from Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πᾰρθενία</i> (părthenía)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a watcher of young girls</li><li>a seducer of young girls</li></ol><p>From <i>παρθένος</i> (parthénos, “maiden”) +&lrm; <i>ὀπῑπεύω</i> (opīpeúō, “to stare at; to seduce”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) alternative form of <i>πᾰρῐ́στημῐ</i> (părĭ́stēmĭ)</li></ol><p>Hellenistic Koine verb, used in place of <i>πᾰρῐ́στημῐ</i> (părĭ́stēmĭ), in the present and imperfect tense.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that is necessary to appear</li></ol><p>From <i>πάρειμι</i> (páreimi) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, verbal adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell beside [<i>with</i> accusative]</li><li>to live near, (<i>of places</i>) to lie near [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to dwell among [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to dwell in as a foreigner</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temporary residency in a foreign land</li></ol><p><i>πάροικος</i> (pároikos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a byword, saying, proverb, maxim</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a dark saying, parable</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πάροιμος</i> (pároimos, “by the road, neighbor”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), or from <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “next, near, alongside, nearby, nigh”) +&lrm; <i>οἴμη</i> (oímē, “song, tale”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, feminine suffix of abstraction).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>addicted to wine</li><li>drunk</li></ol><p><i>παρα-</i> (para-, “excessive”) +&lrm; <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos, “wine”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) play upon words which sound alike, assonance</li><li>use of a word first in its proper meaning, then in a derived sense</li><li>derivative; by-name</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρονομᾰ́ζω</i> (păronomắzō, “to call with a slight change of name”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irritation, exasperation</li><li>severe fit of a disease</li></ol><p>From <i>παροξύνω</i> (paroxúnō, “to sharpen, irritate”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós), from <i>παρά</i> (pará, “beside”) + <i>ὀξύνω</i> (oxúnō, “to sharpen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urge, prick, spur on, stimulate</li><li>to anger, provoke, irritate, exasperate</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ὀξῡ́νω</i> (oxū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) paroxytone</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “next to”) +&lrm; <i>ὀξῠ́τονος</i> (oxŭ́tonos, “oxytone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look at by the way, notice, remark</li><li>to look past, overlook a thing</li><li>to disregard</li><li>to neglect</li><li>to concede</li><li>to see amiss, see wrong</li><li>to look sideways</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-) +&lrm; <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō, “to see”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>presence</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>arrival, especially a visit of a royal or official person</li><li>occasion</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the advent, second coming</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) situation of a planet at a point on the zodiac</li></ol><li>substance, property</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>monetary contribution</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>παρών</i> (parṓn, “present”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be present</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρουσῐ́ᾱ</i> (părousĭ́ā, “presence, arrival”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>openness, frankness</li><li>freedom of speech</li><li>(<i>in a bad sense</i>) licence of tongue</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all, every”) +&lrm; <i>ῥῆσις</i> (rhêsis, “utterance, speech”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly, frankly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) paronychia, whitlow</li><li>nailwort (<i>Paronychia</i> spp, esp. <i>Paronychia capitata</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>παρα-</i> (para-, “near”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνυξ</i> (ónux, “nail”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>closely resembling</li><li>nearly equal (<i>of numbers</i>)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) employing assonance</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρα-</i> (păra-, “beside”) +&lrm; <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios, “the same”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of light boat</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>byname, nickname</li><li>surname; cognomen, agnomen</li></ol><p>Substantive of <i>πᾰρώνῠμος</i> (părṓnŭmos, “derivative”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>formed by a slight change, derivative</li></ol><p><i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ, “from; besides”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνῠμᾰ</i> (ónŭmă, “name”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parody</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰρῳδός</i> (părōidós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finest flour or meal</li></ol><p>Formation and origin are unknown. It seems obvious to connect <i>παιπάλη</i> (paipálē, “finest flour”), but the relation is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley porridge</li></ol><p>Neuter plural of <i>παστός</i> (pastós, “sprinkled with salt”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sprinkled with salt, salted</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman's chamber, bridal chamber</li><li>bridal bed</li><li>embroidered bed-curtain</li><li>bridal hymn</li></ol><p>From <i>πάσσω</i> (pássō, “to sprinkle”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p><p>From the same origin as Etymology 1, with semantic confluence with <i>παστάς</i> (pastás, “annex, porch”), as bridal chambers were "luxuriously strewn".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porch in front of house</li><li>colonnade</li><li>part of house next to porch</li><li>inner room</li><li>bridal chamber</li></ol><p>For <i>παραστάς</i> (parastás, “anything standing beside”). The "inner room" and "bridal chambers" are via semantic contamination from <i>παστός</i> (pastós, “bridal chamber”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make into paste</li></ol><p>From <i>πάστιλλος</i> (pástillos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pastophorus</li></ol><p>Calque of Egyptian <i>jrj-ꜥꜣ</i>. By surface analysis, <i>παστός</i> (pastós) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sprinkled with salt, salted</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman's chamber, bridal chamber</li><li>bridal bed</li><li>embroidered bed-curtain</li><li>bridal hymn</li></ol><p>From <i>πάσσω</i> (pássō, “to sprinkle”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p><p>From the same origin as Etymology 1, with semantic confluence with <i>παστάς</i> (pastás, “annex, porch”), as bridal chambers were "luxuriously strewn".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build a bridal chamber</li></ol><p>From <i>παστός</i> (pastós) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related to the Christian feast of Easter</li></ol><p>Hellenistic-era formation from <i>πάσχα</i> (páskha, “Easter”) +&lrm; <i>-άλιος</i> (-álios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel lust, be lustful (<i>especially unnatural lust</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πάσχω</i> (páskhō) +&lrm; <i>-ητιάω</i> (-ētiáō, desiderative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of flat dish</li></ol><p>Probably a Pre-Greek word, as per Furnée, who connects <i>πέταχνον</i> (pétakhnon, “broad flat cup”); also compare the suffix of <i>λεκάνη</i> (lekánē, “dish, pot”) and <i>οὐράνη</i> (ouránē, “chamber-pot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock, beat</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to strike</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to slaughter, kill</li><li>to afflict</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>πάταγος</i> (pátagos, “clatter, crash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to eat</li><li>to eat of, partake of [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to taste food</li></ol><p>(πατέω)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, to tread</li><li>to tread on</li><li>to walk in, dwell in, frequent</li><li>to trample</li></ol><p>Related to <i>πάτος</i> (pátos, “path”); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πᾰτέρᾰ</i> (pătéră)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>πᾰτέρῐ</i> (pătérĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, to tread</li><li>to tread on</li><li>to walk in, dwell in, frequent</li><li>to trample</li></ol><p>Related to <i>πάτος</i> (pátos, “path”); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>epithet of Zeus</li><li>respectful address of an older man</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) author &lsqb;5th c. BCE&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) forefathers, ancestors</li></ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) God the Father; (one of the three Persons of the Trinity) &lsqb;3rd c. BCE&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;patḗr</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀳</i> (pa-te)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂tḗr</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>fæder</i> (English <i>father</i>), Phrygian <i>πατερης</i> (paterēs), Latin <i>pater</i>, Sanskrit <i>पितृ</i> (pitṛ), and Old Armenian <i>հայր</i> (hayr).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πατήρ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) God the Father; (one of the three Persons of the Trinity)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;patḗr</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀳</i> (pa-te)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂tḗr</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>fæder</i> (English <i>father</i>), Phrygian <i>πατερης</i> (paterēs), Latin <i>pater</i>, Sanskrit <i>पितृ</i> (pitṛ), and Old Armenian <i>հայր</i> (hayr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>clatter, clash</li><li>(<i>of teeth</i>) to gnash</li><li>(<i>of the sea</i>) to splash, plash</li><li>(<i>of birds</i>) chatter</li><li>(transitive,&#32;of drums) to beat</li></ol><p>From <i>πάτᾰγος</i> (pátăgos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call father</li><li>to refer to someone as father</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πατρίδα</i> (patrída)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The place of one's father/ancestors: fatherland, hometown, native city, native country</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr, “father”) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lineage, descent</li><li>race, stock</li><li>house, tribe, clan</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take after one's father</li><li>to style oneself after one's father</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism&#32;and&#32;Christianity</i>) a patriarch (the father or chief of a race)</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;as <i>Πατριάρχης</i> in titular use) a patriarch (borne as a title by the bishops of Rome, Constantinople, Jerusalem, Antioch, and Alexandria)</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰτρῐᾱ́</i> (pătrĭā́) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρχης</i> (-ắrkhēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) patriarchal, patriarchic (of or belonging to a patriarch)</li></ol><p><i>πᾰτρῐᾰ́ρχης</i> (pătrĭắrkhēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hereditary, inherited</li><li>ancestral</li><li>fatherly, paternal</li></ol><p>From <i>πατήρ</i> (patḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the same country, a fellow countryman, compatriot</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰτρῐᾱ́</i> (pătrĭā́) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to murder one's father</li></ol><p>From <i>πατροκτόνος</i> (patroktónos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to parricide, patricide, murdering one's father; parricidal, patricidal</li><li>of or pertaining to a murdering father; father who is murdering</li></ol><p>From <i>πατρός</i> (patrós) +&lrm; <i>-κτόνος</i> (-któnos). By surface analysis, <i>πατρός</i> (patrós) +&lrm; <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πατρῷος</i> (patrōîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from one's father, hereditary</li><li>of or belonging to one's father</li></ol><p>From <i>πατήρ</i> (patḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ῷος</i> (-ōîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>παῦρος</i> (paûros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ending pain</li></ol><p>From <i>παῦσις</i> (paûsis, “stopping, ceasing”) +&lrm; <i>λύπη</i> (lúpē, “pain, grief, sorrow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>collar worn by slaves while grinding corn or kneading bread, to prevent them from eating</li></ol><p>From <i>παῦσις</i> (paûsis, “stopping, ceasing”) +&lrm; <i>κάπτω</i> (káptō, “to gulp down”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>πᾰχῠ́ς</i> (păkhŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick-fingered</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰχῠ́ς</i> (păkhŭ́s, “thick”) +&lrm; <i>δᾰ́κτῠλος</i> (dắktŭlos, “finger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick-skinned</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dull, stupid</li></ol><p>From <i>παχύς</i> (pakhús, “thick”) +&lrm; <i>δέρμα</i> (dérma, “skin”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thicken, fatten</li></ol><p>From <i>πᾰχῠ́ς</i> (păkhŭ́s, “thick, fat”) +&lrm; <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick; large; stout</li><li>coarse</li><li>curdled; clotted</li><li>fat</li><li>great</li><li>thickwitted; dull; stupid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pəkʰús</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰn̥ǵʰús</i> (“thick”), from <i>&#42;bʰenǵʰ-</i> (“thick; to swell”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>बहु</i> (bahú, “much, many”) and Avestan <i>𐬠𐬄𐬰𐬀𐬵</i> (bązah).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Ionic) superlative degree of <i>πᾰχῠ́ς</i> (păkhŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Ionic) comparative degree of <i>πᾰχῠ́ς</i> (păkhŭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>παύεσκε</i> (paúeske)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>παύσῃ</i> (paúsēi)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to make cease</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to bring to an end, check</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>mostly of things</i>) to make an end of, stop, abate</li></ol><li>(<i>with accusative of person, genitive of object</i>) to make to rest, stop, hinder, keep back from</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;with genitive only</i>) to make an end of</li><li>(<i>with present participle</i>) to stop a person from doing or being</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;with the infinitive</i>) to stop</li></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to stop, cease</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;in imperative) cease, leave off</li></ol><p>Disputed. The LIV traces the word back to a root of the shape Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂w-</i> (different from <i>&#42;peh₂w-</i> (“few, little”), but Beekes leaves the etymology open, saying that there are no certain cognates outside Greek. The word has also been linked to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pustìti</i> (“to let go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>παύσειε</i> (paúseie)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>παύσουσι</i> (paúsousi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to make cease</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to bring to an end, check</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>mostly of things</i>) to make an end of, stop, abate</li></ol><li>(<i>with accusative of person, genitive of object</i>) to make to rest, stop, hinder, keep back from</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;with genitive only</i>) to make an end of</li><li>(<i>with present participle</i>) to stop a person from doing or being</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;with the infinitive</i>) to stop</li></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to stop, cease</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;in imperative) cease, leave off</li></ol><p>Disputed. The LIV traces the word back to a root of the shape Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂w-</i> (different from <i>&#42;peh₂w-</i> (“few, little”), but Beekes leaves the etymology open, saying that there are no certain cognates outside Greek. The word has also been linked to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pustìti</i> (“to let go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>παύωσι</i> (paúōsi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>παῖδᾰ</i> (paîdă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li><li>slave</li><li>servant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pā́wits</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>puer</i>, Sanskrit <i>पु॒त्र</i> (putrá, “son”), and Avestan <i>𐬞𐬎𐬚𐬭𐬀</i> (puθra, “son”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rest, pause</li><li>cessation, end</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>παύω</i> (paúō, “to stop, abate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little, small</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) short</li><li>speedy</li><li>(Epic,&#32;Lyric,&#32;Tragic,&#32;rare&#32;in prose) few</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péh₂uros</i> (compare Latin <i>parvus</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂w-</i> (compare Old English <i>fēaw</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stopping, ceasing</li></ol><p>From <i>παύω</i> (paúō, “stop, end”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to convince, persuade</li><li>to succeed through entreaty</li><li>to mislead</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to bribe</li><li>to tempt</li></ol></ol><li>(mediopassive voice,&#32;πείθομαι,&#32;and Epic future,&#32;πῐθήσω,&#32;with dative)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to obey, yield to</li><li>to believe, trust in</li></ol><li>(second perfect active,&#32;πέποιθᾰ,&#32;with passive sense) to trust, rely on (<i>with dative of person or thing</i>)</li><li>(perfect passive,&#32;πέπεισμαι,&#32;post-Epic) to believe, trust (<i>with dative</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;péitʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeydʰ-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>fīdō</i>, Albanian <i>be</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bīdaną</i>, from which Old English <i>bīdan</i> (English <i>bide</i>).</p><p>Stems πειθ-, πιθ- with vowel shift, and <i>ποιθ-</i> (poith-) with ablaut. Derivatives from all stems, with π(ε)ιθ-τ- &gt; π(ε)ιστ-, πειθ-μ- &gt; πεισμ-.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πείρατα</i> (peírata) (<i>before /h/</i>)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>πείρατε</i> (peírate) (<i>before /h/</i>)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>πείρατι</i> (peírati) (<i>before /h/</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πείρᾰτᾰ</i> (peírătă)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>πείρατε</i> (peírate)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>πείρατι</i> (peírati)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wicker basket tied upon a cart</li></ol><p>Likely Pre-Greek, due to the sequence -νθ-.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce, run through</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to cleave through</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;péryō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pér-ye-ti</i>, the ye-present of <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to pass, go forth”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>հերիւն</i> (heriwn), Old Church Slavonic <i>на-перѫ</i> (na-perǫ, “pierce”), Sanskrit <i>पिपर्ति</i> (piparti, “to bring over to, deliver”), Old English <i>faru</i> (English <i>fare</i>), and Albanian <i>shpie</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fetter, shackle</li><li>to constrain</li></ol><p><i>πέδη</i> (pédē, “shackles”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>open country, field, plain, flat</li><li>metatarsus</li><li>female genitals</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ped-</i> (“trace, footstep”), and related to <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”). Compare <i>πέδον</i> (pédon, “soil, ground”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the ground</li></ol><p><i>πεδίον</i> (pedíon) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land battle</li></ol><p><i>πεζός</i> (pezós, “on foot”) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “combat”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go on foot</li><li>to go by land, march</li></ol><p>From <i>πεζοπόρος</i> (pezopóros) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a land journey</li></ol><p>From <i>πεζοπόρος</i> (pezopóros, “going by land”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>going by land or on foot</li></ol><p>From <i>πεζός</i> (pezós, “on foot”) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “passage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) on foot, walking</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) traveling on land, as opposed to by sea</li><li>consisting of land troops</li><li>consisting of infantry</li><li>prosaic</li><li>ordinary</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;peďďós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ped-yo-s</i> (“foot”); see also <i>πούς</i> (poús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by land</li><li>on foot</li></ol><p>By ellipsis of <i>πεζῇ ὁδῷ</i> (pezēî hodōî).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) on foot, walking</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) traveling on land, as opposed to by sea</li><li>consisting of land troops</li><li>consisting of infantry</li><li>prosaic</li><li>ordinary</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;peďďós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ped-yo-s</i> (“foot”); see also <i>πούς</i> (poús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a horse</i>) obedient to the rein</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) obedient, submissive, docile</li></ol><p>From <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “to persuade”) +&lrm; <i>ἡνία</i> (hēnía, “rein”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>persuasive</li></ol><p>Can be identified with Latin <i>fīdus</i>, both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeydʰós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persuasion</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>means of persuasion, inducement, argument</li></ol><li>obedience</li></ol><p>Appellative of <i>Πειθώ</i> (Peithṓ), from <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “I persuade”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πειθώ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the goddess Peitho, the personified persuasion</li></ol><p>From <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “I persuade”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be hungry</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to hunger after</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to hunger after, long for, crave after</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πεῖνᾰ</i> (peînă, “hunger”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hungry</li></ol><p>From <i>πεῖνα</i> (peîna, “hunger”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “-ous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make proof or trial of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to attempt to do</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) are tried, proved</li></ol><li>(<i>with accusative of person</i>) to try or tempt a person, put them to the test</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to seek to seduce, tempt</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πεῖρα</i> (peîra, “trial, attempt”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to try [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do’</small>]</li><li>to make an attempt [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘on someone or something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to try [<i>with</i> infinitive <i>or</i> participle <small>‘to do’</small>]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to test, make a trial of [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone or something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to test one's own skill, abilities, or fortune [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘in something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>πεῖρᾰ</i> (peîră) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>περαίνω</i> (peraínō)</li></ol><p>From <i>πεῖραρ</i> (peîrar) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trial, proof</li><li>temptation</li></ol><p>From <i>πειρᾰ́ζω</i> (peirắzō, “to prove”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brigand, robber; especially a sea-raider, pirate</li></ol><p>From <i>πειράω</i> (peiráō) (“to attempt, attack”) + <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs), from <i>πεῖρα</i> (peîra) (“trial, attempt”), ultimately from PIE *per- (“to try, risk”).</p><p>Not related to <i>περάτης</i> (perátēs) (“wanderer”), from <i>περάω</i> (peráō) (“to cross”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to try, test</li></ol><p>Formally from <i>&#42;πειρητής</i> (&#42;peirētḗs) (Epic/Ionic form of <i>πειρᾱτής</i> (peirātḗs, “brigand, pirate”)), + <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō). However the semantics do not correlate with the specialized meaning of the noun, as this verb is simply a synonym of <i>πειράω</i> (peiráō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πειρῷντο</i> (peirōînto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shear, to clip</li></ol><p>Related to <i>κτείς</i> (kteís, “a comb”) and <i>πέκω</i> (pékō, “to comb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>marine, maritime (pertaining to the sea&#59; living in the sea)</li><li>out at sea, on the open sea</li></ol><p>From <i>πέλαγος</i> (pélagos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to approach</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring near</li></ol><p>From <i>πέλας</i> (pélas, “nearby”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō). Compare cognate <i>πλησιάζω</i> (plēsiázō) and old <i>πιλνάω</i> (pilnáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who approaches or comes near, neighbor</li><li>one who approaches a woman</li><li>one who approaches to seek protection; a dependent, client</li></ol><p><i>πελᾰ́ζω</i> (pelắzō) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) dark spot</li></ol><p>From <i>πελιδνός</i> (pelidnós, “livid, dark”) +&lrm; <i>-ωμᾰ</i> (-ōmă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pollux gives the definition as: <i>χοῦς</i> (khoûs, “a measure of capacity”), <i>λεκάνη</i> (lekánē, “dish, pot, pan”).</li><li>pelike, a ceramic container similar to an amphora</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>πέλλα</i> (pélla, “milk pail”), in view of the variation <i>-λ-/-λλ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πελάγιος</i> (pelágios)</li></ol><p>From <i>πέλαγος</i> (pélagos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stork</li></ol><p>The origin is Pre-Greek, though folk-etymologically explained as <i>πελλός</i> (pellós, “dark”) +&lrm; <i>ἀργός</i> (argós, “white”). It has been linked with Old Armenian <i>արագիլ</i> (aragil).</p><p>Witczak (1991) suggests that the word is a compound, with second term continuing otherwise unattested <i>&#42;ἁργός</i> (&#42;hargós), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sr̥ǵos</i> (“stork”), akin e.g. to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sturkaz</i> (“stork”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pelican (<i>Pelecanus onocrotalus</i>)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>πέλεκυς</i> (pélekus, “axe”), because of the functional and/or formal similarity with an axe. Compare similar bird names, like <i>πελεκανός</i> (pelekanós, “coot”), <i>πελεκᾶς</i> (pelekâs, “woodpecker”) and <i>πελεκῖνος</i> (pelekînos, “pelican”). Furnée compares <i>σπέλεκτος</i> (spélektos, “pelican”), suggesting a Pre-Greek etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of woodpecker</li></ol><p>Probably from the same Pre-Greek root of other similar bird names, like <i>πελεκανός</i> (pelekanós, “coot”), <i>πελεκάν</i> (pelekán, “pelican”) and <i>πελεκῖνος</i> (pelekînos, “pelican”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pelican (<i>Pelecanus onocrotalus</i>)</li><li>crownvetch (<i>Securigera varia</i>)</li><li>dovetail, culvertail</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>πελεκᾶς</i> (pelekâs, “kind of woodpecker”), <i>πελεκανός</i> (pelekanós, “coot”) and <i>πελεκάν</i> (pelekán, “pelican”), presumably of shape *<i>pilik-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake, cause to quiver or tremble</li><li>to struggle at the bow in order to bend it</li><li>to drive (<i>someone</i>) from their post</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“drive”) + <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>livid, pallid, blue, lurid, dark colored</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pelito-</i>, from <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“gray”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>पलित</i> (palitá), Latin <i>pallidus</i> and Old Armenian <i>ալիք</i> (alikʻ, “wave, gray hair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>livid, pallid, blue, lurid, dark colored</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pelito-</i>, from <i>&#42;pelH-</i> (“gray”). Cognates include <i>πελλός</i> (pellós, “dusky”), Sanskrit <i>पलित</i> (palitá), Latin <i>pallidus</i> and Old Armenian <i>ալիք</i> (alikʻ, “wave, gray hair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>black and blue, livid</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) dark, dull</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“gray”). Cognates include Ancient Greek <i>πελιδνός</i> (pelidnós) Sanskrit <i>पलित</i> (palitá), Latin <i>pallidus</i> and Old Armenian <i>ալիք</i> (alikʻ, “wave, gray hair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark-colored, dusky</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pelH-</i> (“gray”). Cognates include <i>πελιός</i> (peliós, “black and blue, livid”), <i>πελιτνός</i> (pelitnós, “livid, blue”), <i>πολιός</i> (poliós, “grey, grizzly”), Sanskrit <i>पलित</i> (palitá), Latin <i>pallidus</i> and Old Armenian <i>ալիք</i> (alikʻ, “wave, gray hair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to serve as a peltast</li></ol><p>From <i>πέλτη</i> (péltē) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, verb-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a peltast, a targeteer, one who bears a light shield</li></ol><p>From <i>πελτᾰ́ζω</i> (peltắzō) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er”, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of large mussel</li></ol><p>From <i>πέλωρ</i> (pélōr, “portent, prodigy, monster”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>monstrous; prodigious</li><li>huge; massive; enormous; gigantic; vast</li></ol><p>From <i>πέλωρ</i> (pélōr, “supernatural monster”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios). The noun is of unknown origin; perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷer-ōr</i> (“maker; builder?”) modified by dissimilation, given variant forms such as <i>τέλωρ</i> (télōr) and <i>τελώρῐος</i> (telṓrĭos) which point to an original initial <i>&#42;kʷ-</i>, though this derivation is problematic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the number five</li><li>a group of five, pentad</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>πέντε</i> (pénte) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs); compare <i>δυάς</i> (duás), <i>τριάς</i> (triás), <i>τετράς</i> (tetrás), <i>δεκάς</i> (dekás), <i>χιλιάς</i> (khiliás), <i>μυριάς</i> (muriás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sent</li></ol><p>From <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō, “I send”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of wasp that makes its nest in hollow oaks</li></ol><p>The suffix is the same of <i>ἀνθρηδών</i> (anthrēdṓn, “hornet”) and <i>τενθρηδών</i> (tenthrēdṓn, “kind of wasp”). The base was probably onomatopoeic, and possibly Pre-Greek: compare words for "humming" or "buzzing", like Serbo-Croatian <i>бу̏мба̄р</i> (“bumblebee”), Sanskrit <i>बम्भर</i> (bambhara, “bee”), <i>भ्रमर</i> (bhramara, “large black bee”) and Latin <i>fremō</i> (“to buzz, hum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poverty, indigence, beggary</li></ol><p>From <i>πένης</i> (pénēs, “poor”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mourn, or grieve</li><li>to wail, or cry</li></ol><p>From <i>πένθος</i> (pénthos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mother-in-law</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>πενθερός</i> (pentherós, “father-in-law”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father-in-law</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) parents-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-er-os</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i> (“to tie; bond, band”) and formally almost completely agreeing with Lithuanian <i>beñdras</i> (“common, shared”); compare also Sanskrit <i>बन्धु</i> (bandhu, “relative, kindred”). The oxytone accentuation of this word is based on the model of <i>ἑκυρός</i> (hekurós, “father-in-law”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Of or relating to mourning</li><li>(<i>πενθικῶς ἔχω + genitive</i>) to be in mourning for</li></ol><p>From <i>πένθος</i> (pénthos, “sorrow, mourning”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) pentagon</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pentagonal, having five angles</li><li>(geometry,&#32;nominalized,&#32;neuter) pentagon</li></ol><p><i>πεντα-</i> (penta-) +&lrm; <i>γωνία</i> (gōnía) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pentagonal, having five angles</li><li>(geometry,&#32;nominalized,&#32;neuter) pentagon</li></ol><p><i>πεντα-</i> (penta-) +&lrm; <i>γωνία</i> (gōnía) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) five times</li></ol><p>From <i>πέντε</i> (pénte, “five”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́κῐς</i> (-ắkĭs, suffix for adverb of repetition).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) five-shaped, fivefold</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντα-</i> (penta-, “five”) +&lrm; <i>-μορφος</i> (-morphos, “shape, form”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the number five</li><li>a group of five, pentad</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>πέντε</i> (pénte) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs); compare <i>δυάς</i> (duás), <i>τριάς</i> (triás), <i>τετράς</i> (tetrás), <i>δεκάς</i> (dekás), <i>χιλιάς</i> (khiliás), <i>μυριάς</i> (muriás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>With five mouths or openings.</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντα-</i> (penta-, “five”) +&lrm; <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting of five papyrus rolls or (more generally) five books</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine,&#32;Judaism,&#32;Christianity) Pentateuch, Torah</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντα-</i> (penta-, “five”) +&lrm; <i>τεῦχος</i> (teûkhos, “case of papyrus rolls”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creeping cinquefoil (<i>Potentilla reptans</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντα-</i> (penta-, “five”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) five-leaved</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a cinquefoil</li></ol><p><i>πεντα-</i> (penta-, “five”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λλον</i> (phŭ́llon, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming second-declension adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;penkʷēḱomth₂</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;pénkʷedḱomt</i> (“fifty”). Cognates include Latin <i>quīnquāgintā</i>, Sanskrit <i>पञ्चाशत्</i> (pañcāśát) and Old Armenian <i>յիսուն</i> (yisun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>five</li></ol><p>From <i>πέντε</i> (pénte, “five”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) synonym of <i>πενταετής</i> (pentaetḗs, “five years old, five-year-old”)</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντα-</i> (penta-) +&lrm; <i>ἔτος</i> (étos) + <i>-ηρος</i> (-ēros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five thousand</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντάκις</i> (pentákis, “five times”) +&lrm; <i>χίλιοι</i> (khílioi, “thousand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>farmland which produces 500 medimnus of corn.</li><li>the first class in Solon's constitution.</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντακόσιοι</i> (pentakósioi, “five hundred”) +&lrm; <i>μέδιμνος</i> (médimnos, “measure of corn, bushel”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>five hundred</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντα-</i> (penta-) +&lrm; <i>-ακόσιοι</i> (-akósioi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) pentasyllabically (in five syllables)</li></ol><p><i>πεντᾰσῠ́λλᾰβος</i> (pentăsŭ́llăbos, “pentasyllabic”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, suffix forming adverbs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) pentasyllabicity, pentasyllabism (state or quality of comprising five syllables)</li></ol><p><i>πεντᾰσῠ́λλᾰβος</i> (pentăsŭ́llăbos, “pentasyllabic”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, suffix forming feminine abstract nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of five syllables, pentasyllabic</li></ol><p><i>πεντᾰ-</i> (pentă-, “five”) +&lrm; <i>σῠλλᾰβή</i> (sŭllăbḗ, “syllable”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming two-termination second-declension adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fifteen</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fifteenth</li></ol><p>From <i>πέντε</i> (pénte, “five”) +&lrm; <i>καί</i> (kaí, “and”) +&lrm; <i>δέκᾰτος</i> (dékătos, “tenth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fifteen-fold</li></ol><p><i>πέντε</i> (pénte, “five”) +&lrm; <i>καί</i> (kaí, “and”) +&lrm; <i>δέκᾰ</i> (dékă, “ten”) +&lrm; <i>-πλᾰσῐ́ων</i> (-plăsĭ́ōn, “-fold”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pentecost - From the feast called Shavuot, which means "Festival of Weeks" and is the "fiftieth day" after Passover.</li></ol><p>Feminine substantive form of <i>πεντηκοστός</i> (pentēkostós, “fiftieth”), from <i>πεντήκοντα</i> (pentḗkonta, “fifty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fiftieth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;penkʷēkontstós</i>, equivalent to <i>πεντήκοντα</i> (pentḗkonta) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penteconter (ship with fifty oars)</li></ol><p>From <i>πεντήκοντα</i> (pentḗkonta, “fifty”) + the root of <i>ἐρετμόν</i> (eretmón, “oar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A wardrobe</li></ol><p>From <i>πέπλος</i> (péplos, “peplus”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cooked</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pokʷtós</i> with restored <i>e</i> grade. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>потъ</i> (potŭ, “sweat”), Welsh <i>poeth</i> (“hot”), and Latin <i>coctus</i> (“cooked”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Enclitic particle that follows the word it modifies and adds force to it</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Thessalian and Delphic form of <i>περί</i> (perí)</li></ol><p>From <i>περί</i> (perí). Compare <i>περικαλλής</i> (perikallḗs, “very beautiful”), Latin <i>permagnus</i> (“very great”) etc.</p><p>See <i>περί</i> (perí).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: ΠΕΡ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>nomen sacrum</i> form of <i>πάτερ</i> (páter)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferryman, transporter</li><li>wanderer, sojourner, emigrant</li></ol><p>From <i>περάω</i> (peráō, “to cross, pass over”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to cross, go beyond”)) + <i>-της</i> (-tēs). Unrelated to <i>πειρατής</i> (peiratḗs, “pirate”), despite similarity in form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go from one side to another</li><li>to pass through over or traverse, cross, esp. over water</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to penetrate or pierce (<i>of a pointed weapon</i>)</li></ol><p>Denominal verb from <i>πέρα</i> (péra, “beyond”); cognate with Albanian <i>prura</i> (“to bring, lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>about; concerning; because of</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>around; for; about</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>around; near; about</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peri</i> (“in crossing; in passing”), locative singular of <i>&#42;per-</i> (“before; in front”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>परि॑</i> (pári), Latin <i>per</i>, Lithuanian <i>per</i>, English <i>for</i>, and Albanian <i>për</i>.</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of cause; the dative is from the locative; the accusative is like <i>πᾰρᾰ́</i> (părắ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>going round, compassing, encircling</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) the spires or coils of a serpent</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) wall round a town</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) enclosure, circuit, compass</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) precinct (<i>of a temple</i>)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine,&#32;Byzantine) (fruit) garden</li></ol><p>From <i>περιβάλλω</i> (peribállō) (from <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō)) + <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral feast</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, around) +&lrm; <i>δεῖπνον</i> (deîpnon, “meal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running round, encircling, round, circular</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wandering, going about, roaming</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) that can be run round; <i>hence,</i> standing apart, detached</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>surrounded</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which surrounds, edge, rim</li><li>orbit, lap</li><li>(at Mytilene,&#32;in the plural) circuit-judges</li></ol><p>From a suppletive root of <i>περῐτρέχω</i> (perĭtrékhō, “I run about”); see, for example, <i>περῐδρᾰμεῖται</i> (perĭdrămeîtai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be around (<i>a place</i>)</li><li>to be better (than), superior (to), surpass, excel (<i>+ genitive</i>)</li><li>to survive, outlive, remain alive</li><li>to be extant, be in existence</li><li>to be over and above, to remain in hand</li><li>to be a result or consequence</li><li>to go round, encircle</li><li>to encompass</li><li>to come round to</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí)</p><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>εἶμι</i> (eîmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a loincloth worn around the waist</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ζῶμᾰ</i> (zômă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mincemeat, trimmings</li></ol><p>From <i>περικόπτω</i> (perikóptō, “to cut all round, mutilate”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>surviving</li></ol><p>From <i>περιλείπομαι</i> (perileípomai) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os) and <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>λοιπός</i> (loipós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cancellation</li><li>repudiation</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐλῡ́ω</i> (perĭlū́ō, “loosen about”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, “a suffix forming abstract nouns of or nouns of action, result or process”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>περῐ́νεος</i> (perĭ́neos)</li></ol><p>From <i>περίνεος, περίνεοι</i> (períneos, períneoi, “male genitals”), itself from earlier <i>περίς</i> (perís, “penis”) through influence from <i>πηρίς</i> (pērís, “scrotum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Space between the anus and scrotum/vulva; perineum.</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Male genital organs.</li></ol><p>From <i>περίς</i> (perís, “penis”) through influence from <i>πηρίς</i> (pērís, “scrotum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a supernumerary on board ship</li><li>supercargo</li><li>passenger</li><li>petty officer</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>going round in a circle, flank march</li><li>circumference</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) periodic recurrence:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of time, of events, of thoughts</li><li>roster</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) of orbit</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) menstruation</li></ol><li>chart of the Earth</li><li>(grammar,&#32;rhetoric) period (of sentences).</li><li>one who goes the rounds, patrol</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ́</i> (perĭ́, “around”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “path, road”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neighboring</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) neighbor</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a walking</li><li>a place for walking, especially a covered walk</li><li>a conversation during a walk, argument</li><li>a school of philosophy, first used in reference to the Ἀκαδημία</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐπᾰτέω</i> (perĭpătéō, “I walk around”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailing round, circumnavigation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an account or description of such a voyage</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>περί</i> (perí) +&lrm; <i>πλοῦς</i> (ploûs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be seen on all sides, seen from afar, conspicuous</li><li>admired</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering of a bed, counterpane, coverlet</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) carpets and hangings of rooms</li></ol><p>From <i>περιστρώννυμι</i> (peristrṓnnumi, “to spread all around”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peristyle</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>στῦλος</i> (stûlos, “pillar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered with something stretched over</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around, about”) +&lrm; <i>τόνος</i> (tónos, “something stretched”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circumlocution, periphrasis</li></ol><p><i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-) +&lrm; <i>φρᾰ́σῐς</i> (phrắsĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of Penelope and other women</i>) having very good sense, wise, sagacious, prudent</li><li>(<i>later</i>) excessively confident, haughty</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “beyond”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, suffix for adjectives relating to the mind or emotions).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) anointing, application of a substance all around or over a surface</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) coating, smearing over completely</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>χρῖσις</i> (khrîsis, “anointing”), from <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ointment, salve, or coating</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) something applied to make something more acceptable or pleasant</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>χρῖσμα</i> (khrîsma, “ointment, anointing”), from <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) an ointment, salve, especially for external application</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>χρῑστός</i> (khrīstós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to finish, to bring to an end</li><li>to limit</li><li>to effect</li><li>to reach</li><li>to come to an end</li><li>to penetrate</li></ol><p><i>πεῖραρ</i> (peîrar, “end”) +&lrm; <i>-ι̯ω</i> (-i̯ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry over, carry across</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to pass over, cross</li></ol><p><i>περαῖος</i> (peraîos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō), from <i>πέρᾱ</i> (pérā, “beyond”), akin to <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “passage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>περάτης</i> (perátēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knotweed of species <i>Polygonum maritimum</i></li></ol><p>From <i>πέρδιξ</i> (pérdix, “partridge”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partridge-catcher, name of a species of hawk</li></ol><p>From <i>πέρδιξ</i> (pérdix, “partridge”) +&lrm; <i>θηράω</i> (thēráō, “to hunt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>around, about</li></ol><p>From <i>περί</i> (perí, “about, around”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to treat, handle</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “concerning”) +&lrm; <i>ἔπω</i> (épō, “speak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embrace, to surround, to encompass, to contain, to enclose</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, around) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outline, contour</li><li>leading round and explaining what is worth notice, full description (<i>such as is given by tour guides</i>)</li><li>(<i>geography</i>) a geographical description, periegesis</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>ἥγησις</i> (hḗgēsis) and <i>περιηγέομαι</i> (periēgéomai) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place round</li><li>to bring round</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to place round oneself</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to stand round about</li><li>to encircle, surround</li><li>to come upon</li><li>to turn out (<i>of events; especially for the worse</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw round, about, or over, put on or over</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of clothing</i>) to put on, clothe, dress; in the passive or participle, to be clothed or dressed (in), often with garments such as <i>σινδών</i> (sindṓn)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to put round or upon a person, to invest them with</li></ol><li>(reversely,&#32;with dative of object) to surround, encompass, enclose with</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to encompass, surround</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to fetch a compass round, double</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to bring into one's power, aim at</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to appropriate mentally, comprehend</li><li>to cloak or veil in words</li></ol><li>to throw beyond, beat in throwing, to beat, excel, surpass</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>βᾰ́λλω</i> (bắllō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering, garment</li><li>circumnavigation</li></ol><p>From <i>περιβάλλω</i> (peribállō, “to put around”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be superior to; to prevail over; to overcome; to gain an advantage over</li><li>to survive; to escape</li><li>to remain after; to be left over</li><li>to be a result</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to draw a line round, mark round, circumscribe</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to draw a circle</li><li>to define, determine, draw up</li><li>to draw in outline, sketch out, delineate</li><li>to cancel, exclude</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “write”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outline, general description</li><li>circumference, circuit</li><li>that which is marked by an outline, impression</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) circumscribed figure</li><li>limit, termination</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) compass of expression</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) breaking off, conclusion</li><li>(<i>law</i>) circumvention, fraud</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐγρᾰ́φω</i> (perĭgrắphō, “to define, determine, limit”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armlet for the right arm</li><li>peridexion (legendary tree believed to grow in India that attracts doves and repels dragons)</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>περιδέξιος</i> (peridéxios).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ambidextrous</li><li>very dexterous or expert</li><li>convenient</li><li>going round the right arm</li><li>having two right hands</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, around) +&lrm; <i>δεξῐός</i> (dexĭós, “right”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ambidextrous</li><li>very dexterous or expert</li><li>convenient</li><li>going round the right arm</li><li>having two right hands</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, around) +&lrm; <i>δεξῐός</i> (dexĭós, “right”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>necklace</li><li>collar of a pillory</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-) +&lrm; <i>δέραιον</i> (déraion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in great fear [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘about’</small> <i>or</i> dative <small>‘for’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>δείδια</i> (deídia), perfect of <i>δείδω</i> (deídō, “to fear, dread”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to warm all round, cherish</li></ol><p><i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>θᾰ́λπω</i> (thắlpō, “heat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) extreme ill luck</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐκᾰκέω</i> (perĭkăkéō, “to be in ill-luck”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) case of fruit or seed, pod, husk or shell</li><li>bracelet</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, around) +&lrm; <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “fruit”, diminutive suffix) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>περικαλλέα</i> (perikalléa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very beautiful</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>κάλλος</i> (kállos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering for the head, helmet, cap</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) disorder of the oak</li><li>kind of bandage</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>περικεφάλαιος</i> (perikephálaios, “round the head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut in all around, enclose</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;passive voice) to be confined, reduced</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (“around”) +&lrm; <i>κλείω</i> (“shut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) famous, renowned, excellent</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>κλυτός</i> (klutós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honeysuckle (<i>Lonicera periclymenum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, around) +&lrm; <i>κλύμενον</i> (klúmenon, “honeysuckle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal fully</li><li>to conceal from (something)</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loosen about</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around, surrounding”) +&lrm; <i>λύω</i> (lúō, “loosen”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait for, await</li><li>to require</li><li>to expect</li><li>to endure, put up with something</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-) +&lrm; <i>μένω</i> (ménō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acquired in one's travels</li><li>periodic, recurrent phases of the Moon, of hours, of diseases, of texts</li><li>(<i>metrics</i>) of periodic metres (e.g. hexameter with interchanging dactyls and spondees</li></ol><p>From <i>περίοδος</i> (períodos, “patrol around”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “adjectival suffix”). See also <i>περῐοδεύω</i> (perĭodeúō, “to go round”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the country around a town; a suburb, <i>Umland</i></li><li>a dependent town</li></ol><p><i>περίοικος</i> (períoikos) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overlook, ignore</li><li>to allow, permit</li><li>to wait for</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to watch and wait</li><li>to look after, watch over</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth, abundance, plenty</li></ol><p>From <i>περιών</i> (periṓn), present participle of <i>περίειμι</i> (períeimi) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compass, circumference</li><li>(Koine,&#32;in general) compass, extent</li><li>content of a definition</li><li>summary, periochae</li><li>portion circumscribed or marked off, section of a book</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) pod, husk, shell</li><li>fence, fortification</li></ol><p>From <i>περιέχω</i> (periékhō) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turnaround</li><li>sudden change</li><li>(<i>drama</i>) plot twist, peripeteia</li></ol><p>From <i>περιπετής</i> (peripetḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall around, so as to embrace [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to fall upon (<i>a weapon</i>) [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to fall in with, encounter [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to fall foul of</li><li>to change suddenly</li><li>to fall on one side</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>πῑ́πτω</i> (pī́ptō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk up and down, walk about</li><li>to discourse</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to live</li></ol><p><i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>πατέω</i> (patéō, “to walk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Given to walking around</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐπᾰτέω</i> (perĭpătéō, “to walk around”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, “verbal adjective suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>falling round, surrounded by, wrapped in</li><li>falling in with, falling into (evil, etc.)</li><li>changing or turning suddenly (of a man's fortunes, esp. from good to bad)</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐπῑ́πτω</i> (perĭpī́ptō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twine or enfold round</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to fold oneself round [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to complicate, entangle</li><li>to wrap up (<i>in words</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>πλέκω</i> (plékō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail or swim around; circumnavigate</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to slip about</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Koine,&#32;medicine) to recur</li></ol><li>(Koine,&#32;passive voice) to be wrapped up</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (“round”) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (“sail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep, preserve</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to purchase, procure</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of garments</i>) edged with purple</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, around) +&lrm; <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra, “purple dye”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look around</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>σκοπέω</i> (skopéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw off from around, strip off</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to strip oneself of</li><li>to strip bare</li></ol><li>(<i>of a general or army</i>) to wheel about, esp. wheel twice</li><li>to draw off, draw away, divert</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to detach</li><li>to disturb, vex</li><li>to divert, distract</li><li>to steal</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;with infinitive) to be compelled</li></ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to pronounce with the circumflex (esp. on the last syllable)</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>σπάω</i> (spáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheeling around, spinning</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) distraction, distracting circumstances</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) circumflex accent</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐσπᾰ́ω</i> (perĭspắō, “to wheel around”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>orthography</i>) the Greek circumflex ( ͂ )</li></ol><p>Present middle active feminine participle of περισπάω (perispáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be over and above, to go beyond</li><li>to be more than enough, remain over</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a bad sense</i>) to be superfluous</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to abound in, to have more than enough of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be superior, to be better than, to have the advantage</li></ol><li>(<i>causal</i>) to make to abound</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of time</i>) to make longer</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>περισσός</i> (perissós, “extraordinary”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prodigious (beyond the regular number or size)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>extraordinary; unusual; uncommon; remarkable; strange</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) extraordinary; eminent; remarkable</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) beyond others in</li></ol><li>spare; surplus; reserve (more than sufficient)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>useless; superfluous; redundant</li><li>excessive; extravagant</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) overwise; too clever by half (too intelligent for their own good)</li><li>(<i>as a term of praise</i>) subtle; acute; sharp</li></ol><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) odd</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “above, beyond”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dress, to clothe, to wrap up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to employ compression</li><li>to cut down, to retrench; to be dispensed with</li><li>to lay out a corpse, to bury</li><li>to be contracted round</li><li>to wrap, to cloak, to cover</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to conceal something from</li></ol><li>to protect, to defend</li><li>to attend to, to cherish</li><li>to withdraw from society</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “to make ready, to prepare”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vervain (<i>Verbena officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐστερᾱ́</i> (perĭsterā́, “pigeon, dove”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clasping and compressing; <i>δύναμις περισταλτική</i> (dúnamis peristaltikḗ), the peristaltic action of the bowels, by which their contents are propelled.</li></ol><p>From <i>περιστέλλω</i> (peristéllō, “I wrap up”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, suffix to form an adjective).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pigeon; dove</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dovecote</li><li>alternative form of <i>περιστέριον</i> (peristérion, “vervain”)</li></ol><p>From <i>περιστερᾱ́</i> (peristerā́) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn, suffix indicating a place for animals).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>masculine of <i>περῐστερᾱ́</i> (perĭsterā́): male pigeon or dove</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐστερᾱ́</i> (perĭsterā́, “pigeon”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden implement that is turned round, strickle</li><li>handle for turning a fuller's press</li></ol><p>From <i>περιστροφή</i> (peristrophḗ, “turning round”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mouth of a vessel</li><li>a mouth-band</li><li>the region of the epiglottis</li><li>the collar of a garment</li><li>the edge of a ravine</li></ol><p>Declined forms of and neuter substantive from <i>περῐστόμῐος</i> (perĭstómĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>round a mouth or aperture</li></ol><p><i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “around, about”) +&lrm; <i>στόμῐον</i> (stómĭon, “small mouth, mouth of a vessel, any aperture or opening, ornament for the neck”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective ending)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anklet, a piece of jewelry worn around the ankle</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>περισφύριος</i> (perisphúrios, “round the ankle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heap all around</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be heaped up with</li></ol><p>From <i>περί</i> (perí, “around”) +&lrm; <i>σωρεύω</i> (sōreúō, “heap upon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut around</li><li>to circumcise</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a circumvallation</li></ol><p>From <i>περιτειχίζω</i> (periteikhízō, “to make a wall around”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, “a suffix forming a noun from a verb”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build a wall around, circumvallate, build a circumvallation</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>τειχίζω</i> (teikhízō, “to build a wall”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>circular incision</li><li>circumcision</li><li>one who is circumcised</li></ol><p>From <i>περιτέμνω</i> (peritémnō, “to circumcise”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoemaker's knife</li></ol><p>From <i>περιτέμνω</i> (peritémnō, “to cut round about”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run about everywhere, run round and round</li><li>to surround</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-) +&lrm; <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light upon, fall in with, meet with</li><li>to happen to, befall</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>membrane which contains the lower viscera; peritoneum</li></ol><p>From <i>περιτόναιος</i> (peritónaios), from <i>περίτονος</i> (perítonos, “covered with something stretched over”) +&lrm; <i>-αιος</i> (-aios, suffix to form an adjective).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stretched or strained over, especially of the membrane which contains the lower viscera</li></ol><p>From <i>περίτονος</i> (perítonos, “covered with something stretched over”) +&lrm; <i>-αιος</i> (-aios, suffix to form an adjective).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a periphery; outer bounds</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;geometry) circumference; the line round a circular body</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;geometry) arc of a circle; circular arc</li><li>rounded surface; curve; curved or spherical shape</li><li>curvature; roundness</li><li>(figurative,&#32;biblical) wandering; error; madness</li></ol><p>From <i>περιφερής</i> (peripherḗs, “circular; rounded; revolving; wavering”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia), from <i>περιφέρω</i> (periphérō, “to carry round; revolve; wander”), from <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around, surrounding”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to carry, bear”) from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰérō</i> (“to bear”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéreti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry round, carry about</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to move round</li><li>to turn round</li><li>to carry round, publish, make known</li><li>to carry to and fro</li><li>to bring round (into one's own power)</li><li>to carry round or back (in thought)</li><li>to turn round, make dizzy, turn mad</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to survive, endure, hold out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to come round, recover</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to go round, revolve</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to wander about</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>circular, round</li></ol><p>From <i>περιφέρω</i> (periphérō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) carefully</li></ol><p><i>περιφραδής</i> (periphradḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) very thoughtful, very careful</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>φραδής</i> (phradḗs, “understanding, wise, shrewd”), from the root of <i>φράζω</i> (phrázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) periphrastic</li></ol><p>From <i>περίφρασις</i> (períphrasis) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós) and <i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>φραστικός</i> (phrastikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compass in thought, speculate about</li><li>to overlook, despise</li></ol><p><i>περι-</i> (peri-) +&lrm; <i>φρονέω</i> (phronéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contempt, scorn, disdain</li></ol><p>From <i>περῐφρονέω</i> (perĭphronéō, “to despise”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smear or cover over; to coat all around</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to apply a substance externally; to anoint or salve</li><li>to pour or spread as an unguent over</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to affect or influence thoroughly</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felloes or spokes of a wheel</li></ol><p>From <i>περι-</i> (peri-, “around”) +&lrm; <i>ὤγανον</i>&nbsp;sg (ṓganon, “spokes of the wagon wheel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dusky, dark in colour, having dark spots</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perḱ-</i> (“motley, coloured”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farwō</i> (“colour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persea, an Egyptian tree (<i>Mimusops laurifolia</i>)</li><li>ornament in form of its leaf</li></ol><p>From <i>Περσίς</i> (Persís, “Persia”) +&lrm; <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā), because this tree originated there.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Perseus, a mythological figure</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Perseus</li></ol><p>Perhaps Pre-Greek, perhaps from <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō).</p><p>More at Perseus.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of <i>περσέα</i> (perséa)</li></ol><p>From <i>περσέᾱ</i> (perséā, “an Egyptian tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(countable,&#32;uncountable) peach (soft juicy stone fruit of the peach tree, having yellow flesh, downy, red-tinted yellow skin, and a deeply sculptured pit or stone containing a single seed)</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>Περσικὸν μῆλον</i> (Persikòn mêlon, literally “Persian tree-fruit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pin or tongue of a buckle or brooch</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>buckle or brooch itself</li><li>pin for twisting ropes round, on a ship</li><li>pivot of doorpost</li><li>linchpin</li><li>rivet, bolt</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>rivet in the Roman pilum</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) radius; (<i>more&#32;frequently</i>) fibula, calf bone</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>zootomy</i>) ligament below the knee of a horse</li><li>outgrowth, excrescence, epiphysis</li><li>(zootomy,&#32;in the plural) splint bones</li></ol><li>A kind of fish.</li></ol><p>From <i>πείρω</i> (peírō, “to pierce, run through”), formed with the pattern of <i>ᾰ̓κόνη</i> (ăkónē, “whetstone, hone”), <i>βελόνη</i> (belónē, “needle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oval shaped stone used in board games</li><li>board on which the game was played</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) cubic mass of building, terrace</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) limbal ring</li></ol><p>Loanword of unknown origin. A Semitic etymology has been proposed, comparing Hittite (piššu, “rock, block of stone”). According to Beekes, the word may well be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πέταλον</i> (pétalon, “leaf, slice”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broad, flat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pétalos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (“to spread out”)/<i>&#42;pet-</i> (“to spread out, extend”), whence Latin <i>pandō</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faþmaz</i> (“embrace”) [English <i>fathom</i>].</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spread out, open</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;mostly perfect) to be spread out on all sides, to be scattered, dispersed</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (“to spread out”). Cognates include Albanian <i>petë</i>, Old English <i>fæþm</i> (English <i>fathom</i>), Latin <i>pateō</i>, <i>patulus</i>, and possibly <i>pandō</i>; and Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬚𐬀𐬥𐬀</i> (paθana).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butterbur (<i>Petasites hybridus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πέτασος</i> (pétasos, “petasus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>πετεινός</i> (peteinós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bird</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fully fledged; able to fly</li></ol><p>From <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “to fly”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péth₂eti</i> from the root <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (literally “to spread one’s wings”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fully fledged; able to fly</li></ol><p>From <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “to fly”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péth₂eti</i> from the root <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (literally “to spread one’s wings”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having its abode in the rocks</li></ol><p>From <i>πέτρᾱ</i> (pétrā, “stone”) +&lrm; <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “inhabit”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsley</li></ol><p>From <i>πέτρος</i> (pétros, “rock”) +&lrm; <i>σέλινον</i> (sélinon, “celery”). So called because of being used to treat calculi.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sulphurwort (<i>Peucedanum officinale</i>)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) squirting cucumber (<i>Ecballium elaterium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πευκεδανός</i> (peukedanós, “bitter like resin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πευκέδανον</i> (peukédanon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitter like resin</li></ol><p>From <i>πεύκη</i> (peúkē, “pine”) +&lrm; <i>-δανός</i> (-danós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of pine or pine-wood</li></ol><p>From <i>πεύκη</i> (peúkē, “pine”) +&lrm; <i>-δνης</i> (-dnēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pine</li><li>anything made from the wood of the tree, torch of pine-wood</li><li>wooden writing-tablet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewḱ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of, from or made of pine wood</li></ol><p>From <i>πεύκη</i> (peúkē, “pine”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a large penis</li></ol><p>From <i>πέος</i> (péos) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pi, the name for the sixteenth letter Π (P), π (p) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤐</i> (p‬ /⁠pē⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunger, famine, starvation</li><li>hunger or longing for a thing</li></ol><p>Unknown. Furnée compares <i>ἠπανάω</i> (ēpanáō, “to be in want”), but this is rather doubtful. Beekes suspects a Pre-Greek borrowing, particularly if <i>-α</i> (-a) was the original suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trial, experiment, attempt</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;peřřa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pér-ih₂ ~ &#42;pr̥-yéh₂s</i>, from <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to cross, pass”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ih₂</i>. Cognate with Old Armenian <i>փորձ</i> (pʻorj, “attempt, try”), Latin <i>perīculum</i> (“trial, risk”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fērō</i> (“danger”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pierce, run through</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to cleave through</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;péryō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pér-ye-ti</i>, the ye-present of <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to pass, go forth”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>հերիւն</i> (heriwn), Old Church Slavonic <i>на-перѫ</i> (na-perǫ, “pierce”), Sanskrit <i>पिपर्ति</i> (piparti, “to bring over to, deliver”), Old English <i>faru</i> (English <i>fare</i>), and Albanian <i>shpie</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πέρᾰς</i> (pérăs)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pérwr̥</i> (“ford; crossing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obedience</li></ol><p>From <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “I persuade”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persuasion</li><li>affection, susceptibility</li></ol><p>From <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “I persuade”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs). Compare the inherited formation, <i>πῐ́στῐς</i> (pĭ́stĭs).</p><p>From <i>πάσχω</i> (páskhō), <i>πείσομαι</i> (peísomai, “I feel, suffer”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) ship's cable, stern's cable</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) rope, cable, painter</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) stalk of the fig</li><li>persuasion, confidence</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;πένθ-σμα</i> (&#42;pénth-sma), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i> (“to tie; bond, band”), which Greek may have retained in <i>πάσχω</i> (páskhō, “to undergo, experience”) and <i>πενθερός</i> (pentherós, “father-in-law”).</p><p>From the s-aorist stem of <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “to persuade”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of rue</li></ol><p>From <i>πήγανον</i> (pḗganon, “rue”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring, well, fountain</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) source of a river</li><li>vein (of a mineral)</li><li>source, origin, cause</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Based on the common semantic association between "wells" and the "cold", perhaps related to <i>πήγνῡμῐ</i> (pḗgnūmĭ, “to stiffen, freeze”), thus derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i> (“to coagulate, attach”), but no strong evidence exists for this interpretation. Alternatively, possibly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring, well, fountain</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) source of a river</li><li>vein (of a mineral)</li><li>source, origin, cause</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Based on the common semantic association between "wells" and the "cold", perhaps related to <i>πήγνῡμῐ</i> (pḗgnūmĭ, “to stiffen, freeze”), thus derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i> (“to coagulate, attach”), but no strong evidence exists for this interpretation. Alternatively, possibly Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πηγαί)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pegae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from a well</li></ol><p>From <i>πηγή</i> (pēgḗ, “a spring, fountain”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solid, stout, strong (<i>of horses and waves</i>)</li><li>white</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i>, the same root of <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “to fasten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) steering-paddle, rudder</li></ol><p>From <i>πηδόν</i> (pēdón, “blade of an oar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap, spring, jump</li><li>to stamp with the feet</li><li>(<i>of the heart</i>) to leap, throb</li></ol><p>Deverbative or denominative formation on the basis of a form <i>&#42;pēd-</i>, which appears to be a lengthened grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ped-</i> (“to walk, step”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oar blade, hence generally, oar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pēd-ó-</i> (“sole, footstep”), from <i>&#42;ped-</i> (“to walk, step”) (whence also Latvian <i>pēda</i> (“foot”) and Lithuanian <i>pėdà</i> (“foot”)), with semantic development "foot" > "flat and low object" > "rudder of a ship".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree whose timber was used for axles</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps related to <i>πάδος</i> (pádos, “mahlab”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stringed instrument used by the Lydians</li><li>shepherd's pipe, pipes of Pan</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cage or net for catching birds</li><li>carving knife</li></ol><p>From <i>πήγνῡμι</i> (pḗgnūmi, “to fix, to stick”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stuck in; fixed</li><li>(<i>carpentry</i>) compacted; well put together</li><li>congealed; curdled</li><li>able to solidify</li></ol><p>From <i>πήγνῡμι</i> (pḗgnūmi, “to stick in; to fix”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of eyesalve</li></ol><p>From <i>πηλός</i> (pēlós, “mud, clay”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) how great</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) of what magnitude</li><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) of what age</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷo-</i> (“interrogative, relative stem”) and maybe <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow”). Cognate with Latin <i>quālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young tunny</li></ol><p>Likely from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clay, earth (used by masons and potters)</li><li>mud, mire</li><li>thick or muddy wine, lees</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dolt, blockhead</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin, with Beekes tentatively suspecting a derivation from Pre-Greek, albeit with no particular evidence.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plunge into ruin, undo, bring bane or mischief upon</li><li>(<i>in milder sense</i>) to grieve, distress</li><li>(<i>absolutive</i>) to hurt, harm, damage</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to violate an oath</li></ol><p>From <i>πῆμᾰ</i> (pêmă, “misery”) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woe, misery</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of particoloured duck</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>δρύοψ</i> (drúops) and <i>πάρνοψ</i> (párnops). The suffix "-οψ" is typical of Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false hair, wig</li></ol><p>As a wig may seem a bobbin of threads, the word has probably been derived from <i>πήνη</i> (pḗnē, “thread on the bobbin”), on the model of <i>φενάκη</i> (phenákē, “wig”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at what point in time? at what hour?</li><li>when?</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bobbin</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>πήνη</i> (pḗnē, “spool, bobbin”). Also see Latin <i>panus</i> (“ear of millet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>maimed, disabled in a limb, invalid</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₁-</i> (“to hurt”), but Beekes disputes this, writing that it cannot be connected with <i>πῆμα</i> (pêma, “sorrow”), because of the <i>-ᾱ-</i>, as shown in the Doric form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) kinsman by marriage or alliance</li></ol><p>Kinship term without certain connection. It has often been connected with Latin <i>pār</i> (“equal, even”), but this is formally not compelling. Unrelated to <i>πηΐσκος</i> (pēḯskos, “son, offspring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize, take hold of</li><li>to arrest, apprehend</li><li>to catch (<i>fish etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>A conflation of <i>πιέζω</i> (piézō, “squeeze”) and <i>βιάζομαι</i> (biázomai, “force”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to squeeze</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to repress, stifle</li></ol><p>Probably from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peysd-</i> (“to squeeze”), and cognate with Sanskrit <i>पीडयति</i> (pīḍáyati). The Indo-European root may be a <i>d</i>-extension of <i>&#42;peys-</i> (“to grind, crush”), though such an enlargement is strange.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to fatten, enrich</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be or become fat</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to increase, enlarge</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to make wanton, excite</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to cherish, cheer</li></ol><p>From <i>πῑ́ων</i> (pī́ōn, “fat, grass”) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gush forth</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek root of <i>πῖδαξ</i> (pîdax, “spring, fountain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cask or jar, used for storing figs</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>πίθος</i> (píthos, “jar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pithecium, little ape</li><li>weight hung between two ships coupled for carrying engines of war</li><li>calf's snout (<i>Antirrhinum orontium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πίθηκος</i> (píthēkos, “ape”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>persuasive, influential, winning</li><li>plausible, credible</li><li>(<i>art</i>) true to nature, natural</li><li>easy to persuade, credulous</li><li>obedient, docile</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “I persuade”) +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apelike</li></ol><p>From <i>πίθηκος</i> (píthēkos, “ape”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, suffix for adjective of appearance).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Boeotian form of <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō)</li></ol><p>A zero-grade derivative of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰéitʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéydʰeti</i>, suffixed with <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bristly oxtongue (<i>Helminthotheca echioides</i>)</li><li>kind of chicory</li><li>sour soil</li></ol><p>From <i>πικρός</i> (pikrós, “bitter”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of sharp or bitter tongue</li></ol><p>From <i>πῐκρός</i> (pĭkrós, “sharp; bitter”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pointed, sharp, keen</li><li>(<i>of taste</i>) pungent, bitter, acrid</li><li>(<i>of feeling</i>) sharp, keen</li><li>(<i>of sound</i>) piercing, shrill</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bitter, hateful</li><li>embittered, angry, hostile</li><li>relentless, spiteful, vindictive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pikrós</i>, formally identical to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьstrъ</i> (“variegated”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;piḱ-rós</i>, from the root <i>&#42;peyḱ-</i> (“color, speckle”). The Greek word appears to invoke an earlier meaning of "hew, cut out" > "sharp". A further cognate is <i>ποικίλος</i> (poikílos, “particoloured”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of bitter gall, bilious</li><li>splenetic</li></ol><p>From <i>πικρός</i> (pikrós, “bitter”) +&lrm; <i>χολή</i> (kholḗ, “gall, bile”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compress wool, make it into felt</li><li>to compress, close up</li></ol><p>From <i>πῖλος</i> (pîlos, “felt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soft fat, lard (opposed to <i>στέαρ</i> (stéar, “hard fat”))</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pīmós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peyH-</i> (“fat”). Cognates include Ancient Greek <i>πίων</i> (píōn, “fat, rich”), Latin <i>pinguis</i> (“fat, plump”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;faitaz</i> (“fat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>πιμπλέω</i> (pimpléō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“to fill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“to fill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small tablet on which the dicasts wrote their verdict</li><li>notice board on which laws and decrees were written</li><li>tablet, memorandum book</li><li>votive tablet</li><li>tablet for painting upon</li><li>small plate or dish</li><li>astronomical table</li></ol><p>From <i>πίναξ</i> (pínax, “board, tablet”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small tablet</li><li>a writing tablet</li></ol><p>Diminutive of <i>πινακίς</i> (pinakís), <i>πινακιδος</i> (pinakidos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picture gallery</li></ol><p>From <i>πίναξ</i> (pínax, “tablet”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mapmaker</li><li>maker of catalogues</li></ol><p>From <i>πίνᾰξ</i> (pínăx, “catalogue, register”) +&lrm; <i>-γρᾰ́φος</i> (-grắphos, “drawer, painter, writer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prudent; discreet</li></ol><p>The relation between the relevant forms has not been satisfactorily explained thus far. Older explanations take this word to have somehow developed out of a full grade form <i>&#42;penu-</i>, with <i>ε</i> raised to <i>ι</i>, so as to connect the word with Latin <i>putō</i> (“to prune; esteem, consider”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pytati</i> (“to ask, examine”), both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pew-</i>. However, these attempts must be rejected for a number of reasons. First, a form <i>&#42;penu-</i> is not attested; secondly, the change <i>ε/ι</i> is not systematic; third, the alternative form <i>πνῠτός</i> (pnŭtós) is unexpected. The last variation is, however, known from Pre-Greek, and must reflect the varying rendering of a palatalized cluster. The etymon has nothing to do with <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “to breathe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be dirty</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of statues) to be rusty, rusted</li></ol><p>From <i>πίνος</i> (pínos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell, esp. for exportation</li><li>(perfect passive,&#32;πέπραμαι) to be betrayed, ruined, undone</li></ol><p>Shortened from πιπεράσκω (piperáskō), reduplicated form of <i>περάω</i> (peráō, “to export for sale”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great spotted woodpecker (<i>Dendrocopos major</i>)</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ἀηδώ</i> (aēdṓ) and <i>τυτώ</i> (tutṓ). Probably onomatopoeic like <i>πιππίζω</i> (pippízō, “to pipe, chirp”) and Sanskrit <i>पिप्पका</i> (pippakā, “kind of bird”). Compare also <i>πῖπος</i> (pîpos, “young piping bird”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A box of wood</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pistachio tree (<i>Pistacia vera</i>)</li></ol><p>Of Iranian origin. Compare Northern Kurdish <i>pisteq</i>, Persian پسته (pesta, peste), Middle Persian <i>pstk'</i> (pistag, “pistachio nut”), Old Armenian <i>պիստակ</i> (pistak) (from Iranian).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pistachio</li></ol><p>From <i>πιστάκη</i> (pistákē, “pistachio tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have faith (in, upon, or with respect to, a person or thing), to credit</li><li>to entrust (especially one’s spiritual well being to Christ)</li><li>to believe, commit (to trust), put in trust with</li></ol><p>From <i>πίστις</i> (pístis, “faith”) <i>πιστ-</i> (pist-) + <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, “suffix for verbs”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) faithful, trusty</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>faithful, believing</li><li>obedient, loyal</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πιθ-</i> (pith-) (zero-grade of the root of <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “to persuade; to trust”)) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>): alternative form of <i>πίσος</i> (písos, “plant of pea”)</li></ol><p>Koine. See Ancient Greek <i>πίσος</i> (písos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing tablet</li><li>a leaf out of a writing tablet, receipt, ticket, pass</li><li>message</li><li>a list of members of an association</li><li>the sense: patch of leather for laying salve on, at Latin <i>pittacium</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Not related to <i>πίττα</i> (pítta, “pitch, tar”). The Latin sense "patch of leather" as well as the term <i>πεττύκια</i> (pettúkia), have led to a dubious hypothesis of relation with <i>πίσ(σ)υγγος</i> (pís(s)ungos, “shoemaker”). Beekes rejects a different hypothesis which posits a relation with <i>Πιττακός</i> (Pittakós), from Thrace via Lesvos island.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small kind of olive, of the colour of bran, which was gathered before it was ripe and then preserved</li></ol><p>From <i>πῐ́τῡρον</i> (pĭ́tūron, “bran”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from the pine</li></ol><p>From <i>πῐ́τῠς</i> (pĭ́tŭs, “pine”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make manifest, declare, tell of, disclose</li><li>to utter</li><li>to tell [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰi-bʰh₂-u-sḱéti</i>, suffixed and reduplicated present of <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to shine, appear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of eagle</li></ol><p>Frisk assumes derivation from <i>πλάζομαι</i> (plázomai, “to wander about”), which is not evident. The word may well be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>placed sideways, slanting, aslant</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter plural) sides, flanks (<i>also in the military sense</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πλάγος</i> (plágos, “side”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>side</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plag-</i>, and cognate with <i>πλάξ</i> (pláx, “flat broad tablet”), Latin <i>plaga</i> (“tract, region”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flakaz</i> (“flat, smooth”) (see also English <i>flake</i>), though the existence of such a root and its potential relation to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat, broad, plain”) raises formal issues.</p><p>Beekes, who considers <i>πλάγιος</i> (plágios, “oblique, athwart”) to be the original term and <i>πλάγος</i> (plágos) a derivative, finds the semantic connection with the word's cognates opaque, and tentatively prefers a Pre-Greek origin, relating it to <i>πλᾰ́ζω</i> (plắzō, “to lead astray”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moisture, abundance of fluids</li></ol><p>Unclear word, found especially in medical literature. It has been derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peld-</i>, like Latvian <i>peldēt</i> (“to swim”) and Lithuanian <i>peldėti</i> (“to swim”). Alternatively, Beekes considers a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn away from</li><li>to bewilder, dull the mind</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to wander, roam</li></ol><p>Usually connected with <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike, smite”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flōkaną</i> (“to beat, lament”), and Latin <i>plangō</i> (“to beat, strike”), and traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂k-, &#42;pleh₂g-</i> (“to strike”). However, the divergent semantics of <i>πλάζω</i> (plázō) and unclear sense development from "to beat" > "to drive off course", if related, makes this derivation problematic.</p><p>Although the short <i>-ᾰ-</i> (-ă-) vocalism has been explained by analogy with nasal-infixed forms and subsequent shortening by Osthoff's law (<i>&#42;πλᾱγγ-</i> (&#42;plāng-) &gt; <i>&#42;πλᾰγγ-</i> (&#42;plăng-)), Beekes considers this explanation difficult. He suggests instead a mutual influence of <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō) and <i>πλάζω</i> (plázō) in both semantics and phonetics, and tentatively favors Van Beek's proposal that <i>πλάζω</i> (plázō) is of Pre-Greek origin, relating it to <i>ἀμπλακεῖν</i> (amplakeîn, “to miss, fail, come short of”) and perhaps also to <i>πλάγιος</i> (plágios, “aslant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dish or mold in which bread and cakes were baked</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleth₂-</i>, with laryngeal aspiration. Beekes claims that it is Pre-Greek, and related to <i>πλάσσω</i> (plássō, “to mold, shape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of marble slabs</li></ol><p>From <i>πλάξ</i> (pláx, “marble slab”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wandering, roaming</li><li>discursive treatment</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) going astray, error</li><li>deceit, imposture</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>πλανάω</i> (planáō, “to wander, to roam”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wanderer, roamer, vagabond</li><li>planet</li><li>fever which comes in irregular fits</li></ol><p>From <i>πλανάω</i> (planáō, “I wander”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leading astray, cheating, deceiving</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) synonym of <i>πλάνη</i> (plánē, “wandering, roaming”)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) deceiver, impostor</li></ol><p>From <i>πλανάω</i> (planáō, “to wander”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything flat and broad</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>esp. flat land, plain, the ocean surface</li><li>flat stone, tablet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>tombstone, slab (e.g. of marble)</li><li>plate</li></ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) flaps, tail fins (e.g. of crustaceans)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pləks</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat”), sharing cognates with several Germanic languages through Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flaką</i> (“something flat”), <i>&#42;flakaz</i> (“flat”); more at English <i>flake</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a moulding, conformation</li><li>(<i>of the voice</i>) training</li><li>fiction, invention</li></ol><p>From <i>πλάσσω</i> (plássō, “to form, mould”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something formed, figure, image</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the body, as formed by the Creator</li></ol><li>counterfeit, forgery</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>figment, fiction</li><li>pretence</li><li>delusion</li></ol><li>formed style in writing or speaking</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>music</i>) affected execution</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πλάσσω</i> (plássō, “to form, mold”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form; to mould; to shape; to sculpt</li><li>to plaster</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to form; to train a skill</li><li>to imagine</li><li>to put in a certain form</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to fabricate; to forge</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pláťťō</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;plátʰyō</i>, of uncertain origin. Beekes suspects the root to be Pre-Greek, due to its hypothetical quasi-Indo-European form <i>&#42;pldʰ-ye-ti</i> featuring a combination of a plain stop <i>&#42;p</i> and a voiced aspirate <i>&#42;dʰ</i>, which is phonotactically disallowed in Indo-European. However, he does allow that the Greek term became conflated with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleth₂-</i> (“flat”) during the language's development.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scale of a balance</li><li>disk poised on the top of the rhabdus, in the game of cottabus</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) valve of an oyster shell</li><li>collar for horses</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) surgical splints</li></ol><p>The etymological base of the word is not clear. Formally, there is no reason to disconnect the word from <i>πλάσσω</i> (plássō, “to knead, mold”), but this linkage is not evident on the semantic side. The same holds true for the connection with <i>πλατύς</i> (platús, “flat”). In view of the suffix <i>-ιγγ-</i>, it therefore seems better to assume a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earring</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) images of gods</li></ol><p>From <i>πλάσσω</i> (plássō, “to mould, form”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>πλᾰ́τη</i> (plắtē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planetree (<i>Platanus orientalis</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>πλατύς</i> (platús, “broad, flat”), or taken to be inherited directly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pl̥th₂enós</i> (“wide, broad”), under the assumption that the tree was named after its broad leaves or flat patches of bark. However, based on the opaque morphology of the oldest attested form <i>πλατάνιστος</i> (platánistos), Beekes rejects this etymology, and in view of the "local botanic" semantic category, derives the word from Pre-Greek. Thus, the resemblance with <i>πλατύς</i> (platús) was shaped later by folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flat or broad surface:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an oar blade</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) an oar</li><li>a winnowing fan</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) a shoulder blade</li><li>a sheet of papyrus</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πλᾰτῠ́ς</i> (plătŭ́s, “broad”). Cognate with Irish <i>leithe</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>плеще</i> (plešte), Hittite (paltana).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breadth, width</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) celestial latitude, declination</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pléth₂os</i> (“breadth”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>प्रथस्</i> (práthas), Avestan <i>𐬟𐬭𐬀𐬚𐬀𐬵</i> (fraθah), Old Armenian <i>լայն</i> (layn), Welsh <i>lled</i>. See also <i>πλατύς</i> (platús, “wide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flat object, such as a tile, plate, plaster, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>πλᾰτῠ́νω</i> (plătŭ́nō, “to widen”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, “nominal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything twined or twisted</li><li>wickerwork</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wreaths, chaplets</li><li>complex, combination of words</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέκω</i> (plékō, “to plait, to twist”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>units of measure</i>) a plethron (a unit of length equal to 100 Greek feet (podes)</li><li>(<i>units of measure</i>) a plethron (a unit of area equal to 10,000 square Greek feet (podes)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Typically has been compared with the root of <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi, “I fill up”) (&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pípleh₁mi</i>) (cf. <i>πλήθω</i> (plḗthō) and <i>πληθῡ́ς</i> (plēthū́s) (&lt; <i>&#42;pléh₁-dʰeh₁-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron), in which case it could be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pléh₁dʰrom</i>, though long <i>&#42;ή</i> (&#42;ḗ) would be expected. However, the phonetic alternations in <i>πέλεθρον</i> (pélethron) and <i>βλέθρον</i> (bléthron) suggest a more complicated origin. According to Beekes, it is probably a loanword.</p><p>The word may be cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀤𐀩𐀴𐀪𐀍</i> (qe-re-ti-ri-jo, in context a dual nominative noun of uncertain meaning), if the Mycenaean word is not related to <i>βλῆτρον</i> (blêtron, “fastening band”) instead. If so, the original form must have begun with a labiovelar such as */kʷ/, rather than /p/ or /b/.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plait, twine, twist, weave, braid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;plékō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleḱ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>plectō</i> (“to plait, weave, twist”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plaiting, weaving</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέκω</i> (plékō, “to plait, twine”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail (<i>in a boat</i>)</li><li>to float</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pléwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plew-</i> (“to sail, flow”).</p><p>In the present and imperfect, the semivowel &#42;ϝ is lost between vowels, but before a consonant is kept as υ, the second element of a diphthong. Cognate with English <i>float</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πλεῖος</i> (pleîos, “full”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“fill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or become full, to swell</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(transitive,&#32;only in later poets)</li></ol><li>to go through a period of time (from the filling of the moon)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;plḗtʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> with root extension like <i>plebs</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>pleo</i>, Sanskrit <i>पिपर्ति</i> (piparti, “to fill”) and Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬨𐬞𐬁𐬟𐬭𐬁𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (hampāfrā<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood tide</li></ol><p>From <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi, “to fill, make full”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nave of a wheel, hub</li></ol><p>Uncertain:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>except, save, besides</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>except, save</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with single words and phrases, especially when a negative precedes</i>)</li><li>(<i>often joined with other particles</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(with <i>εἰ</i> (ei))</li><li>(with <i>ἤ</i> (ḗ))</li><li>(with <i>οὐ</i> (ou)) only not</li><li>(with <i>ὅτι</i> (hóti)) except that, save that</li><li>(with <i>ὅσον</i> (hóson)) except or save so far as</li></ol><li>(<i>introducing a clause</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>to break off and pass to another subject</i>) only</li><li>(<i>late prose</i>)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pelh₂-</i> (“to approach”). Cognate with <i>πέλας</i> (pélas), <i>πλησίος</i> (plēsíos), <i>ἔμπλην</i> (émplēn, “near, close by”) and many more in root page.</p><p>Compare the meaning and history of English <i>besides</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small>], complete</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“to fill”). See also <i>πῐ́μπλημῐ</i> (pĭ́mplēmĭ, “to fill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which fills, complement</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>satiety</li><li>the full number, complement, crew</li><li>(<i>of number</i>) sum</li><li>a piece inserted to fill up</li><li>(<i>in the writings of the apostle Paul</i>) fullness, full and perfect nature</li></ol><li>a filling up, a completing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fulfillment</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πληρόω</i> (plēróō, “to fill”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>filling, satisfying</li></ol><p>From <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi, “to fill, satisfy”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, smite</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to strike an object into motion</li><li>to stamp</li><li>to sting</li></ol><li>(figuratively,&#32;passive voice) to be stricken, defeated</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be emotionally stricken</li><li>(active voice,&#32;of wine) to overpower</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;plā́ťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pl(e)h₂kyeti</i>, from <i>&#42;pleh₂k-, &#42;pleh₂g-</i> (“to strike”).</p><p>Cognate with <i>πλᾱ́ζω</i> (plā́zō, “to strike”) (with *-g-), Latin <i>plangō</i>, <i>plēctō</i>, <i>plāga</i>; Old Church Slavonic <i>плакати</i> (plakati), Lithuanian <i>plàkti</i> (“to beat, chastise”) (via secondary ablaut), Dutch <i>vloeken</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made or built of bricks</li></ol><p>From <i>πλίνθος</i> (plínthos, “brick”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A brick, whether sun or fire baked</li><li>A squared stone for building</li><li>An ingot of metal</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) A plinth of a column</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the semantic field of architecture, as well as the presence of the element "-ινθ-", suggest a loan from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cross the legs, as in trotting</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps related to Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;slēxsantī</i> (“thigh”) and Sanskrit <i>प्लेहते</i> (plehate, “to go”), suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pleygʰ-</i> (“to go, move”). Not related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plęsati</i> (“to dance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oblong case or frame used in moulding bricks and in measuring</li><li>hollow rectangle</li></ol><p>No relation with the synonymous <i>πλινθίον</i> (plinthíon), but a direct loan from Lydian <i>𐤡𐤩𐤠𐤳𐤬</i> (plašo, “socle”). This word is related to Hittite (palzaḫ(ḫ)a-, “pedestal”). Furnée also appropriately adduces <i>πλάτας</i> (plátas, “basis of a tomb”), which is very likely to be Anatolian origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transverse flute</li></ol><p>From <i>πλάγιος</i> (plágios, “aslant, sideways”) +&lrm; <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wandering, roaming</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) wandering in mind, erring, distraught</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>πλάζω</i> (plázō, “I turn aside, wander”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>moist, damp, wet</li><li>flabby, flaccid</li><li>loose, watery</li><li>insipid</li></ol><p>From stem as in <i>πλαδ(άω)</i> (plad(áō)) +&lrm; <i>-αρός</i> (-arós). See <i>πλάδος</i> (pládos, “moisture”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flat cake</li></ol><p>Substantivization and contraction of <i>πλακόεις</i> (plakóeis, “flat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flat</li></ol><p>From <i>πλάξ</i> (pláx) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make to wander, lead wandering about</li><li>to lead astray, deceive, mislead</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to wander, stray</li><li>to wander in speaking, digress</li><li>to do a thing irregularly or with variation</li><li>to be in doubt or at loss</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Mechanically reconstructable as a thematization of a nasal present <i>&#42;pl-néh₂-ti</i>, corresponding to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat, broad”), but the semantics are highly problematic. There is also a strong semantic and decent phonetic resemblance to <i>πλάζω</i> (plázō, “to wander, rove”), but the different morphologies are difficult to resolve.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wanderer, vagabond</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) planet</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a fever that comes in irregular fits</li></ol><p><i>πλανάω</i> (planáō, “to wander”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: golden headband of the bride.</li></ol><p>Furnée compares this word with <i>κλανίον</i> (klaníon, “bracelet”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin in view of the variation between bilabial and velar.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mudwall; enclosure made of mud</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for moulding; plastic</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) gifted in sculpture</li></ol><p>From <i>πλάσσω</i> (plássō, “to form, mould”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>formed; moulded in clay or wax</li><li>fabricated; forged; feigned; counterfeit</li></ol><p>From <i>πλᾰ́σσω</i> (plắssō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πλατέα</i> (platéa)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πλατέα</i> (platéa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brown meagre (<i>Sciaena umbra</i>)</li><li>kind of fish found in the Nile</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pudendum muliebre</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, related to the fish name <i>πλάταξ</i> (plátax).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of plane tree</li></ol><p>From <i>πλατάνιστος</i> (platánistos, “plane tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos), variant of <i>πλάτανος</i> (plátanos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street (usually of a main street)</li><li>square (open space in a town)</li><li>flat of the hand</li><li>type of dish or cup</li><li>broad stripe or border</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a himation</i>) with broad purple border</li></ol><p>From <i>πλᾰτῠ́ς</i> (plătŭ́s, “broad, wide”) +&lrm; <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra, “purple dye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plain</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>partisan, a kind of spear</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>πλᾰτῠ́λογχος</i> (plătŭ́lonkhos, “broad pointed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to widen</li><li>to pronounce broadly</li><li>to amplify</li></ol><p>From <i>πλᾰτῠ́ς</i> (plătŭ́s, “wide, broad”) +&lrm; <i>-νω</i> (-nō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wide; broad</li><li>flat; level</li><li>broad-shouldered</li><li>far advanced (of seasons)</li><li>broad; strong</li><li>diffuse</li><li>widespread; frequent</li><li>salty; brackish</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;plətús</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pléth₂us</i>, from <i>&#42;pleth₂-</i> (“flat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>broad-leaved</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>πλατύς</i> (platús, “broad, wide”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in neuter</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>as a noun</i>) more</li><li>(<i>as an adverb</i>) more, rather</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with numerals</i>) more</li><li>(<i>comedy</i>) <i>πλεῖν ἢ μαίνομαι</i> (pleîn ḕ maínomai, “more than to madness”)</li><li>as an adverb with another comparative</li><li>the plural πλείω is also used like πλέον</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pleíhōn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁is-ō</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of number,&#32;in the plural) many, a lot of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with nouns of multitude</i>) large, great</li><li>(of amount,&#32;with mass nouns) a lot of, much</li><li>(rare,&#32;of a person) great, mighty</li><li>(<i>of sound</i>) loud</li><li>(attributively,&#32;adverbial) strongly, fully</li><li>(<i>of space</i>) wide, large</li><li>(<i>of distance</i>) far</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) long; late</li></ol><li>neuter <i>πολύ</i> (polú) or <i>πολλά</i> (pollá) as substantive</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>much, a lot</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li><i>πολλοῦ ἄξιος</i> (polloû áxios), <i>πολλῶν ἄξιος</i> (pollôn áxios): worth a lot, valuable</li><li><i>περὶ πολλοῦ ποιέομαι</i> (perì polloû poiéomai): to think something worth a great deal, value it greatly, be glad</li></ol><li>(<i>of distance</i>) A great distance, far</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;polh₁ús</i> (“much, many”), o-grade derivative of the root <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“to fill”).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>plūs</i> (“more”), Sanskrit <i>पु॒रु</i> (purú, “much, many, abundant”), Old Irish <i>oll</i> (“great, vast”), Old English <i>feolo</i> (“much, many”) and Old Armenian <i>յոլով</i> (yolov).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one plethron wide</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέθρον</i> (pléthron, “measure of 100 feet”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>mostly, very often</li></ol><p><i>πλεῖστ(ος)</i> (“most”) +&lrm; <i>-άκις</i> (“times”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name, accuse as the cause</li></ol><p>From <i>πλειστήρης</i> (pleistḗrēs, “manifold, all the whole length”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζομαι</i> (-ízomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise the price, make expensive</li></ol><p>From <i>πλειστήρ(ης)</i> (pleistḗr(ēs), “manifold, all the whole length”) (<i>πλεῖστος</i> (pleîstos, “very much”)) + <i>-ιάζω</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything twined or wreathed, coil, wreath</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) arms of the octopus, tentacles</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) antennae of the shrimp</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) meshes of a spiderweb</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) mastoid growth in the uterus</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Fallopian tubes</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) plexus of veins</li></ol><p>From <i>πλεκτός</i> (plektós, “plaited, twisted”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or occupied with plaiting</li><li>entangling or interlacing</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέξις</i> (pléxis, “plaiting, weaving”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>plaited, twisted</li><li>wreathed</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέκω</i> (plékō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀟𐀒𐀵</i> (pe-ko-to).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to presume on</li><li>to be superfluous, more than enough</li><li>(<i>of a writer</i>) to be prolix or tedious</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέον</i> (pléon, “too much”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greedy, grasping, or arrogant person</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέων</i> (pléōn, “more”) + <i>ἑκ-</i> (hek-) (the root of <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have”) + <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superabundance, excess</li><li>surplus</li><li>usury</li><li>pleonasm (use of redundant words)</li><li>lengthening of clause</li><li>repetition</li></ol><p>From <i>πλεονάζω</i> (pleonázō, “to be superfluous”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have or claim more than one’s due, to get or have too much</li><li>to be greedy, grasping, arrogant</li><li>to gain or have some advantage</li><li>to have or claim a larger share [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li><li>to have or gain the advantage [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘over’</small>], take advantage [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to overreach, defraud</li></ol><p>Denominal verb of <i>πλεονέκτης</i> (pleonéktēs, “greedy person”) + <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greediness, greed, avarice</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέον</i> (pléon, “more”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anatomy,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) A rib.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) A side of a man or animal.</li></ol><li>A side of things and places.</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) A line that forms the side of a triangle or other two-dimensional figure.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A side of a rectangle; (<i>mathematics</i>) one factor of any product.</li><li>A side of a square of a cube; (<i>mathematics</i>) a root of a square of a cubic number.</li><li>A generator of a cone or cylinder.</li></ol></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. The traditional connections with <i>πλάξ</i> (pláx, “flat object, plate”) (&lt; <i>&#42;plk-</i>) and <i>παλάμη</i> (palámē, “palm”) (&lt; <i>&#42;plh₂-em-eh₂-</i>), via an unattested <i>&#42;πλῆ-ϝαρ</i> (&#42;plê-war), suggest a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i> (“flat”). The semantics seem reasonable; however, the morphological formation is unclear.</p><p>An alternative theory, deriving the word from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pnew-reh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;pnew-</i> (“to breathe”), would leave the lambdacism (<i>ν</i> (n) &gt; <i>λ</i> (l)) unexplained (although compare <i>πλεύμων</i> (pleúmōn, “lung”)), in addition to being semantically farfetched (the "lung membrane" sense appears to be a much later innovation in the English borrowing, <i>pleura</i>, that was never present in Greek), particularly when compared to the connection with <i>&#42;pleh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) pleuritis, pleurisy</li></ol><p>From <i>πλευρά</i> (pleurá, “rib, side of a man or animal”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis, “a suffix forming a feminine noun, especially a technical term that pertains to the root word”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) lung</li><li>jellyfish, sea-lung</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pléwmōn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pléwmō</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>क्लोमन्</i> (klóman), Latin <i>pulmō</i> and Old Church Slavonic <i>плюща</i> (pljušta).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“fill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πλεῖστᾰ</i> (pleîstă)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>πλεῖστε</i> (pleîste)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of number, also of size, extent, strength, etc.</i>) most, very much</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with the article,&#32;like <i>οἱ πολλοί</i>) the greatest number, the greatest part of..</li></ol><li>(adverbial,&#32;like <i>μάλιστα</i>) most</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with the article</i>) for the most part</li></ol><li>(<i>with prepositions</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>διά</i>) furthest off</li><li>(<i>εἰς</i>) most</li><li>(<i>ἐπί</i>) over the greatest distance, to the greatest extent</li><li>(<i>κατά</i>)</li><li>(<i>περί</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From the root of <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “much, many”) +&lrm; <i>-ιστος</i> (-istos, superlative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pruning-knife, scythe</li></ol><p>From <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike, smite”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stroke (from a sword or a pike).</li></ol><p>From <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increasing, enlargement, multiplication, pluralization</li></ol><p>From <i>πληθύνω</i> (plēthúnō, “I increase”) +&lrm; <i>-σμός</i> (-smós, action noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to increase, multiply</li><li>to make multiple</li><li>to use the plural</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be in the majority</li></ol><p>Causative of <i>πληθύω</i> (plēthúō) with suffix <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō), from <i>πληθύς</i> (plēthús, “throng”), from <i>πίμπλημῐ</i> (pímplēmĭ, “I fill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Epic form of <i>πλῆθος</i> (plêthos, “throng, crowd; majority”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pléh₁-dʰ-uh₁</i>, from <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“to fill”). Related to <i>πλήθω</i> (plḗthō, “to be(come) full”) and <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi, “to fill”). Cognate with Latin <i>plēbēs</i>, older form of <i>plēbs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fullness</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) generation of blood or humors by the body</li></ol><p>From a stem related to <i>πληθῡ́ς</i> (plēthū́s, “crowd; populace, majority”) and <i>πλήθω</i> (plḗthō, “to be full”) with the suffix <i>-η</i> (-ē, forming nominals), perhaps continuing Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pléh₁-dʰ(h₁)-(w)ōr</i>. Compare Latin <i>plēbs</i> (“crowd; populace, people, folk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthen vessel for water</li></ol><p>From <i>πλήμη</i> (plḗmē, “flood tide”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill, make full</li><li>to finish, complete</li><li>to fulfill</li></ol><p><i>πλήρης</i> (plḗrēs, “full”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, factitive verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nearby</li></ol><p>Adverbial use of declination of <i>πλησίος</i> (plēsíos).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>near, neighboring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pelh₂-</i> (“to approach”). By surface analysis, an adjectival formation from <i>πέλας</i> (pélas, “near, nearby”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>near, neighboring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pelh₂-</i> (“to approach”). By surface analysis, an adjectival formation from <i>πέλας</i> (pélas, “near, nearby”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) bring near</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the passive</i>) approach</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) be near, associate with</li><li>(<i>of animals</i>) have sexual intercourse with</li></ol><p>From <i>πλησίον</i> (plēsíon, “near”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, “suffix for verbs”). Compare cognate <i>πελάζω</i> (pelázō) and old <i>πιλνάω</i> (pilnáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) inside of the thighs, crotch, perineum</li></ol><p>From <i>πλίσσομαι</i> (plíssomai, “to cross the legs”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twining, twisting</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) complication of a dramatic plot</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέκω</i> (plékō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enriching</li></ol><p>From <i>πλουτέω</i> (ploutéō) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be governed by the wealthy</li></ol><p>From <i>πλοῦτος</i> (ploûtos, “wealth”) + <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō, “I rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An oligarchy of wealth</li><li>A plutocracy</li></ol><p><i>πλοῦτος</i> (ploûtos, “wealth”) +&lrm; <i>-κρᾰτῐ́ᾱ</i> (-krătĭ́ā, “form of government”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich, wealthy</li></ol><p>From <i>πλοῦτος</i> (ploûtos, “wealth”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth, riches</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “to float, sail”) (perhaps in some early meaning of "flow" > "abound") with the suffix <i>-τος</i> (-tos); for the suffix, compare βίοτος (bíotos, “life”), νόστος (nóstos, “journey, return”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πλούτων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hades, the god of the underworld.</li></ol><p><i>πλοῦτος</i> (ploûtos, “riches”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, Hoffmann suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a floating vessel, ship, boat</li></ol><p>From the <i>o</i>-grade of the root of <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “to sail”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion), or inherited directly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plówyom</i> (“floating vessel”): compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;flawją</i> (“ship, vessel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πλόος</i> (plóos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth, riches</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “to float, sail”) (perhaps in some early meaning of "flow" > "abound") with the suffix <i>-τος</i> (-tos); for the suffix, compare βίοτος (bíotos, “life”), νόστος (nóstos, “journey, return”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πλοῦτος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plutus</li></ol><p>See <i>πλοῦτος</i> (ploûtos, “wealth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trough, tank or pit in which dirty clothes were washed by treading</li><li>washing tubs, fuller's tubs</li></ol><p>From <i>πλύνω</i> (plúnō, “to wash, clean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail on the ocean</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πλέω</i> (pléō) + <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>floating</li><li>(<i>of fish</i>) swimming</li><li>navigable</li><li>(<i>of seasons</i>) fit for navigation</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective, from <i>πλώω</i> (plṓō, “to sail, float”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lock or braid of hair, tress</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) locks, curling hair, especially of women</li><li>(<i>collectively</i>) hair of the head</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέκω</i> (plékō, “to braid, knit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plaited work, basket work</li><li>wicker fan for winnowing</li><li>plaited rope</li><li>sieve, strainer</li></ol><p>From <i>πλέκω</i> (plékō, “to intertwine, weave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailing; voyage</li><li>one's intended course</li><li>the time for sailing</li></ol><p>An <i>o</i>-grade verbal noun from the root of <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “to sail”) + <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water in which something has been washed</li><li>washings, scum, residue of cinnabar</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) low prostitute</li></ol><p>From <i>πλῡ́νω</i> (plū́nō, “to wash, clean”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially of clothes</i>) to wash, clean</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pluňňō</i>, from a nasal-present formation of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plew-</i> (“to wash”), the same root of Latin <i>pluit</i> (“it rains”) and Old Armenian <i>լուանամ</i> (luanam, “to wash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a ship</i>) seaworthy</li><li>fit for shipbuilding</li><li>favorable for navigation</li></ol><p><i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “to sail”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blow, stroke</li><li>sting of a wasp</li></ol><p>From <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a large number of things, a great number</li><li>majority, main portion</li><li>something of large size or magnitude</li><li>specifically: a large group of people: crowd, multitude, throng, mob</li><li>populace, people, general public</li><li>magnitude, size, extent</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>amount, quantity</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) length</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πλήθω</i> (plḗthō, “to fill”), the intransitive counterpart of <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) plectrum (tool for striking the lyre)</li><li>spur</li></ol><p>From <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πλῆξα</i> (plêxa)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>πλῆξε</i> (plêxe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>percussion, blow (act of striking)</li></ol><p>From <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike, smite”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of animals</i>) impregnation</li><li>being filled, satiety, saturation</li></ol><p>From <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi, “to fill, satisfy”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li><li>to breathe</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to breathe out</li><li>breathe in, smell</li></ol><li>(<i>of perceptible breathing</i>)</li><li>to breathe, live</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;with cognate accusative) to breathe forth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to speak</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pnew-</i> (“to breathe, sneeze”). Cognates include Old English <i>fnēosan</i> (English <i>sneeze</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to choke, throttle, strangle</li><li>(<i>of great heat</i>) to stifle</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to torment, vex</li><li>to cook in a close-covered vessel, to bake, stew</li><li>to drown</li></ol><p>No clear etymology. Earlier attempts to analyze the formation as a conflation of several different verbs are unconvincing. Rather onomatopoeic or sound-symbolic. A Pre-Greek origin cannot be excluded.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choking, suffocation</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>φρίξ</i> (phríx). According to Beekes, possibly from Pre-Greek, like <i>πνίγω</i> (pnígō, “to choke, asphyxiate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>poetic form of <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Related to the wind: windy</li><li>Related to breath: breathing</li><li>Causing flatulence</li><li>spiritual</li></ol><p>From <i>πνεῦμα</i> (pneûma, “wind, breath, spirit”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a disease of the lungs</li></ol><p>From <i>πνεύμων</i> (pneúmōn, “lung”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lung</li><li>kind of a mollusk</li></ol><p>Alteration of <i>πλεύμων</i> (pleúmōn) influenced by <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “I blow”) / <i>πνεῦμα</i> (pneûma, “breath”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>air</li><li>wind</li><li>breath</li><li>(<i>phonology</i>) breathing</li><li>life, breath of life</li><li>spirit, soul</li><li>spiritual being: spirit, angel</li><li>inspiration (often divine inspiration), genius</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pnéwmn̥</i>, equivalent to <i>πνευ-</i> (pneu-), the root of <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “to blow”), +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>damper, cover for a stove</li><li>hydraulic instrument in which air is pent up</li><li>muzzle for horses</li></ol><p>From <i>πνῑ́γω</i> (pnī́gō, “to choke, stifle”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>choking, stifling, whether by throttling or heat</li></ol><p>From <i>πνῑ́γω</i> (pnī́gō, “to choke, throttle, stifle”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strangled, choked</li><li>airtight, hermetic</li><li>baked or stewed</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective, from <i>πνῑ́γω</i> (pnī́gō, “to choke, stifle”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breath</li></ol><p>From <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>πνοή</i> (pnoḗ, “breath”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Epic,&#32;rare&#32;in&#32;Ionic) of what kind?, of what nature?, which?, what?</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix). Compare the indefinite form <i>ποιός</i> (poiós).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ποίας)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Poeas</li></ol><p>Likely from <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make, create, compose”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a work, creation</li><li>a poem</li><li>a deed, action</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “make, create”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creation; fabrication; production</li><li>poetry; poem</li></ol><p>From <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flock</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ποιμήν</i> (poimḗn, “shepherd”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“to protect, shepherd”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trap for the feet</li><li>(<i>of dogs, oxen, horses</i>) foot disease</li><li>(<i>of human beings</i>) gout of the foot</li></ol><p><i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγρᾱ</i> (ágrā, “hunting”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of Achilles</i>) succouring with the feet, running to the rescue: swift-footed</li><li>having swift runners</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) assisting the foot</li></ol><p>From <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρκέω</i> (ărkéō, “to run to assist”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wind-footed, swift as the wind (<i>epithet of Iris</i>)</li></ol><p><i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνεμος</i> (ánemos, “wind”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reaching to the feet (of a garment)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pṓds</i>. Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀡</i> (po) (dat. <i>𐀡𐀆</i> (po-de /⁠podei⁠/)), Latin <i>pēs</i>, Sanskrit <i>पद्</i> (pad), Old Armenian <i>ոտն</i> (otn) and <i>հետ</i> (het), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍉𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (fōtus) and Old English <i>fōt</i> (English <i>foot</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie, bind, or fetter the feet</li><li>to dance</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of horses) to be hobbled</li><li>(<i>prosody</i>) to measure by feet</li></ol><p>From <i>πούς</i> (poús) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) gouty</li><li>of or from gout, gouty</li><li>for gout</li></ol><p><i>ποδᾰ́γρᾱ</i> (podắgrā, “gout”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gouty</li></ol><p>From <i>ποδᾰ́γρᾱ</i> (podắgrā, “gout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel for washing the feet in</li></ol><p>From <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>νιπτήρ</i> (niptḗr, “washbasin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snare, gin, trap</li><li>instrument for straining or twisting the feet (in surgical operations or in torture)</li></ol><p>From <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>στραβός</i> (strabós, “distorted”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swift-running, fleetfooted</li></ol><p>From <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>ὠκύς</i> (ōkús, “swift”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ποτε</i> (pote) (<i>used before a rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to long for, yearn after, desire</li><li>to miss or regret (<i>what is lost</i>)</li><li>to require</li><li>to be anxious (<i>to do something</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πόθος</i> (póthos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longing, desire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰedʰ-</i> + <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from somewhere</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> (“who”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, locative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somewhere, anywhere</li><li>ever</li><li>perhaps</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> (“who”) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi, locative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to somewhere, somewhither</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to create</li><li>to produce</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to make, to produce</li><li>to postulate, imply</li><li>to solve</li></ol><li>(<i>post-Homeric</i>) to compose, write poetry</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to write of (an event) in poetry</li><li>to invent</li></ol><li>to cause</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to cause someone [<i>with</i> accusative] to [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>to procure</li><li>to celebrate, observe</li><li>Used in the middle with a noun periphrastically for the verb derived from said noun.</li></ol><li>(<i>with predicate adjective</i>) to make, cause to be</li><li>to put</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to multiply</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to consider, deem</li><li>to assume</li><li>to take time, spend time</li><li>(<i>later Greek</i>) to sacrifice</li><li>to prepare</li><li>to play (sense 3)</li></ol><li>to do</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to do something [<i>with</i> accusative] to someone [<i>with</i> accusative; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>with adverb</i>) to act</li><li>(<i>pro-verb</i>) Refers back to a previous verb: to do</li><li>to act</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to operate, to be efficacious</li><li>(<i>in Thucydides</i>) to have effect</li></ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to do customarily, to practise</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to pretend</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷoiwéyō</i>, as shown by Doric/Aeolic <i>ποιϝέω</i> (poiwéō), perhaps derived from a noun <i>&#42;ποιϝός</i> (&#42;poiwós) (seen in many compounds as the suffix <i>-ποιός</i> (-poiós)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷoywós</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷey-</i> (“to pile, stow, gather”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>चिनोति</i> (cinóti, “to arrange, pile up”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;činiti</i> (“to instigate, perform”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) grassy (covered with in grass)</li></ol><p>From <i>ποίη</i> (poíē), Epic/Ionic form of <i>πόα</i> (póa, “grass”), + <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poetess</li></ol><p>Feminine of <i>ποιητής</i> (poiētḗs), but built as if from the zero-grade-suffix stem of <i>&#42;ποιητήρ</i> (&#42;poiētḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>something that is bound to be done</li><li>to be made or done</li><li>one must make or do,</li><li>one must deem</li></ol><p><i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “make”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, x)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A maker, inventor, lawgiver</li><li>The composer of a poem, author, poet</li><li>The composer of music</li><li>The author of a speech</li></ol><p>From <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “-er”, agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable of making, creative, productive</li><li>fitted for a poet, poetical</li></ol><p>From <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made</li><li>well-made</li></ol><p><i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ποικίλᾰ</i> (poikílă)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ποικίλε</i> (poikíle)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pied, dappled, worked in various colors, parti-colored</li><li>changeful, various, varying, variegated</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) changeful, manifold, diversified</li><li>intricate, complex, complicated</li><li>(<i>of knowledge</i>) abstruse, subtle</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) subtle, artful, wily</li><li>changeable, unstable, doubtful</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;ποικιλός</i> (&#42;poikilós), with retraction of the accent by Wheeler's law; from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peyḱ-</i> (“to mark, embroider, color, paint”) + <i>-λός</i> (-lós).</p><p>Also found as a Mycenaean Greek anthroponym <i>𐀡𐀑𐀫𐀦</i> (po-ki-ro-qo, “Ποικίλοψ (Poecilops)”). From stem *poik-, ποῖκ&#x2011;ος + -ίλος (compare <i>ὀργίλος</i>, <i>ναυτίλος)</i>. Also <i>πικρός</i> (pikrós) is of the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>embroidered in various colours</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) in various ways</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>intricate, complex</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) unstable, changeable</li></ol><p>Adverb from <i>ποικῐ́λος</i> (poikĭ́los) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) one full of tricks and wiles (<i>epithet of Odysseus and sometimes of Zeus and Hermes</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ποικίλος</i> (poikílos) +&lrm; <i>μῆτις</i> (mêtis) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic&#32;epithet&#32;of Aphrodite</i>) having a colourfully adorned throne</li></ol><p>From <i>ποικίλος</i> (poikílos, “colourful”) +&lrm; <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos, “throne”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with wings of changeful hue</li></ol><p>From <i>ποικῐ́λος</i> (poikĭ́los, “variegated, motley”) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shepherd; herdsman</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) shepherd of the people; pastor; teacher (epithet of Agamemnon)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;poimḗn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;poh₂imḗn</i>, an abstract agent noun formed from an ablaut of <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“to protect”) + <i>&#42;-mḗn</i>. Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀡𐀕</i> (po-me), Latin <i>pascō</i> (“to put to graze”), <i>pāstor</i> (“shepherd”), Sanskrit <i>पाति॑</i> (pā́ti), Lithuanian <i>piemuõ</i>, Old English <i>fōda</i> and <i>fēdan</i> (English <i>food</i> and <i>feed</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to herd, feed, take care of, tend a flock</li><li>to be a shepherd, to act as a shepherd</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to be herded, be tended; to pasture, graze, feed; roam the pastures; traverse</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to lead, guide, govern</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to soothe, charm, beguile; hence deceive</li><li>(figurative,&#32;biblical) to tend to as a shepherd or pastor; to cherish, nourish, care for, mind</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ποιμήν</i> (poimḗn, “shepherd”) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“to protect, shepherd”). Compare Latin <i>pāscō</i> (“to tend, feed, nourish”), Late Latin <i>pāscor</i> (“to feed onself, eat; pasture, graze”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>पाति</i> (pā́ti, “to protect”), Old English <i>fōda</i> and <i>fēdan</i> (English <i>food</i> and <i>feed</i>), Old Church Slavonic <i>пасти</i> (pasti, “to pasture”), <i>пища</i> (pišta).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pertaining to a shepherd.</li></ol><p>From <i>ποιμήν</i> (poimḗn, “shepherd”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood money, wergeld</li><li>fine, ransom, penalty, penance, satisfaction</li><li>compensation</li><li>redemption, release</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷoinā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷoynéh₂</i> (“payment; punishment, vengeance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quality; attribute</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Epic,&#32;rare&#32;in&#32;Ionic) of a certain nature, kind or quality</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjectival suffix). Compare its interrogative form <i>ποῖος</i> (poîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Epic,&#32;rare&#32;in&#32;Ionic) of a certain nature, kind or quality</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjectival suffix). Compare its interrogative form <i>ποῖος</i> (poîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quality; attribute</li></ol><p>From <i>ποιός</i> (poiós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs). A particular calque is Latin <i>qualitas</i> (whence English <i>quality</i>), which was coined as a calque of the Greek term.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Doric</i>) alternative form of <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷey-</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contracted form of <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to create”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in some fashion, manner or way</li></ol><p>From <i>ποιός</i> (poiós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to war ( of war)</li><li>like an enemy, hostile</li><li>belonging to the enemy</li><li>adverse</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enemy</li></ol><p><i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos, “war”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war, battle</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin, as suggested by πτ/π variation in its variants.</p><p>However, the word has been connected to <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō, “to poise, sway, brandish”), <i>πελεμίζω</i> (pelemízō, “to shake, cause to quiver or tremble, struggle at the bow, in order to bend it”) and perhaps <i>ψάλλω</i> (psállō, “to pluck, twitch a string with the fingers; to sing to a harp, chant praises”), all possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to beat, push, drive”) (which Beekes finds semantically unconvincing, despite not being particularly farfetched).</p><p>Compare Latin <i>pello</i> (“to push, drive, strike; to rout, conquer, defeat; to strike a chord; to touch, move”) and <i>palpo</i> (“to touch softly, stroke, pat, caress”), English <i>feel</i>, Ancient Greek <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike, smite”), Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌼𐌰</i> (usfilma, “terrified, appalled”), <i>𐌿𐍃𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌼𐌴𐌹</i> (usfilmei, “fright, horror, dismay”)&nbsp;– quasi analogies to <i>ἐκπλήσσω</i> (ekplḗssō)&nbsp;–, and Russian <i>полох</i> (polox, “fear, fright”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πολέμων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Polemon</li></ol><p>From <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos, “war”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, possessive name suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>citizen; freeman</li><li>fellow citizen; compatriot</li></ol><p>From <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs). The ῑ from the stem of πόλις led to the backformation <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs), whence the reanalysis <i>πολ-ῑ́της</i> (pol-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make war, be at war, fight, quarrel [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>πρός</b> (prós, + accusative) <small>‘with, against someone’</small>]</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with a person as object) to make war on</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(passive voice,&#32;of a person) to have war made on one</li></ol><li>(<i>with cognate accusative</i>) to fight a fight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(passive voice,&#32;of a fight) to take place</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos, “war”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warlike</li></ol><p><i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos) +&lrm; <i>-ήϊος</i> (-ḗïos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>πολεμέω</i> (poleméō)</li></ol><p><i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must go to war</li></ol><p>From <i>πολεμέω</i> (poleméō) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warlike, bellicose</li><li>hostile</li></ol><p>From <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warrior, fighter</li></ol><p>From <i>πολεμίζω</i> (polemízō) +&lrm; <i>-ιστής</i> (-istḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make hostile, make an enemy of</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become an enemy</li></ol><p>From <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jacob's ladder (<i>Polemonium caeruleum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Πολέμων</i> (Polémōn) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn or go about, wander about</li><li>to turn up (i.e. soil with a plow)</li></ol><p>Synchronically analyzed as <i>πόλος</i> (pólos, “pole, axis”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, “verbal suffix”), i.e. "to turn like a pole".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πολῑ́της</i> (polī́tēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to besiege; to blockade</li></ol><p>From <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”) +&lrm; <i>ἕρκος</i> (hérkos, “fence”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>siege of a city</li></ol><p>From <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”) +&lrm; <i>ἕρκος</i> (hérkos, “fence; enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep within the city</li></ol><p>From <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “a city”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;φυλακέω</i> (&#42;phulakéō, “to guard”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>civil magistrate</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῑ́της</i> (polī́tēs, “citizen”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρχης</i> (-ắrkhēs, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the relation in which a citizen stands to the state, the condition and rights of a citizen, citizenship</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the life of a citizen, one's daily life</li><li>the body of citizens</li></ol><li>the life and business of a statesman, government, administration</li><li>civil polity, the condition or constitution of a state</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a well-ordered republican government, a commonwealth</li><li>a free community, republic</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πολῑ́της</i> (polī́tēs, “citizen”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun&ndash;forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live as a citizen or freeman, live in a free state</li><li>to take part in the government</li><li>to administer or govern</li><li>to have a certain form of government</li></ol><p><i>πολῑ́της</i> (polī́tēs) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state affairs, public matters, government</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of government, the science of politics</li><li>concubine, mistress</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a community</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>civic, constitutional, public</li><li>social</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a statesman or politician</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῑ́της</i> (polī́tēs, “citizen”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warden of the citizens, title of a magistrate</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῑ́της</i> (polī́tēs, “citizen”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey, grizzled, grizzly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(most frequently,&#32;of human hair) grey from age</li><li>an old man’s or old woman’s</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) hoary, venerable</li></ol></ol><li>bright, clear, serene</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a stone</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;poľľós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;polH-yós</i>, from the root <i>&#42;pelH-</i> (“pale, gray”).</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>palleō</i> (“to be pale, fade”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;polvъ</i> (“pale, fallow”), Lithuanian <i>pal̃vas</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falwaz</i> (“pale, gray, fallow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>πολλᾰ́κῐς</i> (pollắkĭs, “often, many times”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>often, many times</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́κῐς</i> (-ắkĭs, “times”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Towards many sides, into many parts.</li></ol><p>From <i>πολλαχῶς</i> (pollakhôs) +&lrm; <i>-σε</i> (-se).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>multi-, poly-</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>πολῠ́ῐ̈χθῠς</i> (polŭ́ĭ̈khthŭs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>populous, crowded</li><li>numerous</li></ol><p><i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos, “person”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) very deep</li></ol><p><i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “much”) +&lrm; <i>βένθος</i> (bénthos, “deep”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered with many oxhides</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ́ς</i> (polŭ́s, “many”) +&lrm; <i>βόειος</i> (bóeios, relating to cows or oxes).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polygamy</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ́γᾰμος</i> (polŭ́gămos, “married to many”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”) +&lrm; <i>γᾰ́μος</i> (gắmos, “marriage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Solomon's seal (<i>Polygonatum multiflorum</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter of <i>πολυγόνατος</i> (polugónatos, “having many joints”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having many toes</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>δάκτυλος</i> (dáktulos, “fingers, toes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>much wrought, richly adorned</li><li>working with much art, very skillful</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>δαίδαλος</i> (daídalos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;epithet of mount Olympus) having many ridges</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>δειράς</i> (deirás, “ridge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) having many rowing benches (<i>epithet of ships</i>)</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>κληΐς</i> (klēḯs, “rowing bench”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πολυμήχανε</i> (polumḗkhane)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cunning, crafty</li></ol><p><i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>μηχανή</i> (mēkhanḗ)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having learnt much, knowing much</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “much”) + <i>μαθ-</i> (math-), the root of <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō, “to learn”), +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very artful, sly, crafty</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “much”) +&lrm; <i>παιπάλη</i> (paipálē, “shrewd person”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great experience</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύπειρος</i> (polúpeiros, “much-experienced”) (from <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>πεῖρα</i> (peîra, “trial, experiment”)) + <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>much-variegated</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús) +&lrm; <i>ποικίλος</i> (poikílos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>overbusy; doing too many things</li><li>meddlesome; officious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>busybody</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-) +&lrm; <i>πρᾱ́γ-</i> (prā́g-, root of <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō)) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>polypragmosyne; meddlesomeness; officiousness</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠπρᾱ́γμων</i> (polŭprā́gmōn, “meddlesome”). By surface analysis, <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>πρᾶγμᾰ</i> (prâgmă, “act”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>many-faced, multiform, everchanging</li><li>with many masks or characters</li><li>of many persons</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “much, many”) +&lrm; <i>πρόσωπον</i> (prósōpon, “face”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common polypody (<i>Polypodium vulgare</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύπους</i> (polúpous, “polypus”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having many springs or fountains (<i>epithet of Mount Ida</i>)</li></ol><p>From Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-) +&lrm; <i>πῖδᾰξ</i> (pîdăx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich in grapes</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “much”) +&lrm; <i>σταφυλή</i> (staphulḗ, “grape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extravagance, great expense</li><li>magnificence</li><li>costliness</li></ol><p><i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>τέλειος</i> (téleios) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very expensive, precious, costly</li><li>luxurious, extravagant</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>τέλος</i> (télos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eating to excess</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “much, a lot”) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variety of tones</li><li>variety of speech</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύφωνος</i> (polúphōnos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā) and <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>-φωνίᾱ</i> (-phōníā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) long-lived</li></ol><p><i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “much”) +&lrm; <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos, “lifetime”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of olden time, ancient</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>long-standing</li></ol><li>long-lasting</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>long protracted</li><li>long-lived</li></ol></ol><p><i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos, “time”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>much; very</li><li>too; much too</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>πολῠ́ς</i> (polŭ́s, “much; many”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milkwort (<i>Polygala venulosa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”) +&lrm; <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>often married</li><li>living in polygamy</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>γαμέω</i> (gaméō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking many languages, multilingual</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πολύγλωσσος</i> (polúglōssos, “speaking many languages, multilingual”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Either of two plants:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>πολύγονον ἄρρεν (árrhen, “male”), knotweed, <i>Polygonum aviculare</i></li><li>πολύγονον θῆλυ (thêlu, “female”), (<i>probably</i>) common mare's tail (<i>Hippuris vulgaris</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”) +&lrm; <i>γόνυ</i> (gónu, “knee”), or neuter of <i>πολύγονος</i> (polúgonos, “prolific”), from <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”) +&lrm; <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “offspring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) polygon</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polygonal, many-angled</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) polygon</li></ol><p><i>πολύς</i> (polús) +&lrm; <i>γωνία</i> (gōnía)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>polygonal, many-angled</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) polygon</li></ol><p><i>πολύς</i> (polús) +&lrm; <i>γωνία</i> (gōnía)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) bringing many tears, woeful, baleful (<i>epithet of war or battle; found only in the masculine and feminine singular</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>δάκρυ</i> (dákru)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>much-wished-for, much-desired, Orac. ap. Hdt., Aesch.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lady's thumb (<i>Persicaria maculosa</i>)</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>πολῠ́κᾰρπος</i> (polŭ́kărpos, “fruitful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fruitful, rich in fruit</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “fruit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with much needlework, well stitched</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>κεστός</i> (kestós, “stitched, embroidered”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far famed, greatly renowned, widely celebrated</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύ</i> (polú, “very”) +&lrm; <i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós, “famed”), literally “far-famed, much renowned”.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πολύκλειτος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Polyclitus</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Polyclitus the Elder, 5th century <small>BCE</small> Ancient Greek sculptor and artist from Argos or Sicyon</li><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Polyclitus the Younger, 4th century <small>BCE</small> Ancient Greek architect</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πολύκλειτος</i> (polúkleitos, “far-famed, much-renowned”), from <i>πολύ</i> (polú, “very”) +&lrm; <i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós, “famed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wrought with much toil, hard to shape, especially as a Homeric epithet of iron (contrasted with malleable copper)</li><li>laborious</li><li>of a person: hard-working, toiler</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>κάμνω</i> (kámnō, “labour, work hard”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field basil (<i>Ziziphora capitata</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “much”) +&lrm; <i>κνημός</i> (knēmós, “oregano”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loquacious, talkative</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, many) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos, word, speech).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) of many counsels</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>πολῠ́ς</i> (polŭ́s, “many”) +&lrm; <i>μῆτῐς</i> (mêtĭs, “counsel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of many words</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>much-talking, fluent, loquacious, wordy</li><li>much talked of</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “much”) +&lrm; <i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos, “word”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greater plantain (<i>Plantago major</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “much”) +&lrm; <i>νεῦρον</i> (neûron, “sinew, nerve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very keen or bitter</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “much”) +&lrm; <i>πῐκρός</i> (pĭkrós, “sharp, keen, bitter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>much-tangled, thick-wreathed, with many convolutions</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) tangled, complex</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) subtle, acute, tortuous</li></ol><p><i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-) +&lrm; <i>πλέκω</i> (plékō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>many-footed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>octopus, polypus</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having many folds</li><li>manifold</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>πτυχή</i> (ptukhḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(of number,&#32;in the plural) many, a lot of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with nouns of multitude</i>) large, great</li><li>(of amount,&#32;with mass nouns) a lot of, much</li><li>(rare,&#32;of a person) great, mighty</li><li>(<i>of sound</i>) loud</li><li>(attributively,&#32;adverbial) strongly, fully</li><li>(<i>of space</i>) wide, large</li><li>(<i>of distance</i>) far</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) long; late</li></ol><li>neuter <i>πολύ</i> (polú) or <i>πολλά</i> (pollá) as substantive</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>much, a lot</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li><i>πολλοῦ ἄξιος</i> (polloû áxios), <i>πολλῶν ἄξιος</i> (pollôn áxios): worth a lot, valuable</li><li><i>περὶ πολλοῦ ποιέομαι</i> (perì polloû poiéomai): to think something worth a great deal, value it greatly, be glad</li></ol><li>(<i>of distance</i>) A great distance, far</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;polh₁ús</i> (“much, many”), o-grade derivative of the root <i>&#42;pleh₁-</i> (“to fill”).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>plūs</i> (“more”), Sanskrit <i>पु॒रु</i> (purú, “much, many, abundant”), Old Irish <i>oll</i> (“great, vast”), Old English <i>feolo</i> (“much, many”) and Old Armenian <i>յոլով</i> (yolov).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>signifying many things, having multiple meanings, polysemous</li></ol><p>From <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”) +&lrm; <i>σημαίνω</i> (sēmaínō, “to signify, mean”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compound pulley</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive form of <i>πολύσπαστος</i> (polúspastos, “drawn by many cords”), from <i>πολύς</i> (polús, “many”) + <i>σπάω</i> (spáō, “to draw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) much-suffering, much-enduring (<i>epithet of Odysseus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>πολύς</i> (polús, “much”) +&lrm; <i>ἔτλην</i> (étlēn, “suffer, endure”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>much-pierced, full of holes, porous</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>τρητός</i> (trētós, “perforated”) (from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;trētós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“to rub”), compare <i>τείρω</i> (teírō, “I wear out, afflict”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maidenhair (<i>Adiantum capillus-veneris</i>)</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>πολῠ́τρῐχος</i> (polŭ́trĭkhos, “very hairy, bushy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very hairy, bushy</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>θρίξ</i> (thríx, “hair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>much turned, much traveled, much wandering</li><li>turning many ways</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) shifty, versatile, wily</li><li>(<i>of diseases</i>) changeful, complicated</li></ol><li>various, manifold</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ́ς</i> (polŭ́s, “many”) +&lrm; <i>τρόπος</i> (trópos, “turn”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-fed, plump</li><li>supplying food</li><li>nutritious</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to feed”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich in or concerning much cheese or butter</li></ol><p><i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many, much”) +&lrm; <i>τῡρός</i> (tūrós, “cheese”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming two-termination second-declension compound adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;of the sea) Loudly roaring, loud-resounding; polyphloisbic, undisonant.</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ́ς</i> (polŭ́s, “many”) +&lrm; <i>φλοῖσβος</i> (phloîsbos, “a roaring noise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thoughtful, ingenious, inventive</li></ol><p>From <i>πολυ-</i> (polu-) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons or cities</i>) rich in gold</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-, “many”) +&lrm; <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having many fish</li></ol><p>From <i>πολῠ́-</i> (polŭ́-) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̓χθῡ́ς</i> (ĭkhthū́s, “fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an escort</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a sending</li></ol><li>a solemn procession</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>pomp</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō, “to send”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pilot fish (<i>Naucrates ductor</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πομπός</i> (pompós, “conductor, guide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attendant, escort, conductor, guide</li><li>one who attends a procession</li></ol><p>From <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conductor, guide</li><li>attendant, guard</li></ol><p>Formed from <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō, “to send, conduct”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sound of bubbles rising to the surface</li></ol><p>Compound made up by Aristophanes from <i>πομφόλῠξ</i> (pomphólŭx, “bubble”) +&lrm; <i>πάφλασμα</i> (páphlasma, “a boiling of water of the sea, blustering”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) blister</li><li>bubble</li><li>head ornament</li><li>(<i>chemistry</i>) zinc oxide</li></ol><p>From <i>πομφός</i> (pomphós, “blister”) + infix <i>-λ-</i> (-l-) + ending <i>-ῠγ-ς</i> (-ŭg-s), a Pre-Greek suffix. Possibly related to <i>φλύζω</i> (phlúzō) or <i>πέμφῑξ</i> (pémphīx, “blast; breath; bubble”), with the ending similar to <i>-φλῠξ</i> (-phlŭx), as in <i>οἰνόφλῠξ</i> (oinóphlŭx, “drunkard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) blister on the skin</li></ol><p>Cognate with <i>πέμφιξ</i> (pémphix, “pustule”) and <i>πομφόλυξ</i> (pomphólux, “blister, bubble”). According to Beekes, they are all Pre-Greek words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in older Greek exclusively&#32;middle voice</i>) to toil, labor, work</li></ol><p><i>πόνος</i> (pónos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an evil thing</li><li>a vice (immoral behaviour)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) oppressed by toils</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) oppressive; toilsome; painful; grievous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who suffers or is tormented</li><li>one who is evil or wicked</li></ol><p>From <i>πονέω</i> (ponéō) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) oppressed by toils</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) oppressive; toilsome; painful; grievous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who suffers or is tormented</li><li>one who is evil or wicked</li></ol><p>From <i>πονέω</i> (ponéō) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) seafaring</li></ol><p>From <i>πόντος</i> (póntos) +&lrm; <i>περάω</i> (peráō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;middle voice</i>) to bring about, provide, procure</li></ol><p>From <i>πόρος</i> (póros) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mode of walking or running, gait</li><li>journey</li><li>(<i>military</i>) march</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) course taken by a person</li><li>travelling expenses</li><li>visitation, inspection</li></ol><p>From <i>πορεύω</i> (poreúō). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>πορείᾱ</i> (poreíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to cause to go, carry, convey</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) to bring, carry, furnish, bestow, set in motion</li></ol><li>(<i>in middle or passive</i>) to be driven or carried</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to go, walk, march</li><li>to walk, i.e. to live</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “way, bridge”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō). From <i>πείρω</i> (peírō, “to pierce, pass through”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō, “to destroy, ravage, waste, plunder”)</li><li>to try to destroy, to attack, besiege</li><li>to despoil, ruin</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be ruined, undone</li></ol><p>From the <i>o</i>-grade of <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, frequentative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strait, narrow sea, place crossed by a ferry</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) sea</li><li>tube, any narrow passage</li><li>crossing by a ferry, passage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to carry forth”), the same root of <i>πείρω</i> (peírō, “to pierce”), <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “means of passage”), Latin <i>portus</i> (“harbour, port”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fardiz</i> (“journey”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πορθμός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Porthmus, Euboea, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>πορθμός</i> (porthmós, “strait, narrow sea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prostitution, whoring (provision of sex for money)</li><li>fornication, unchastity</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) idolatry</li></ol><p>From <i>πορνεύω</i> (porneúō, “to prostitute, to whore”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, “-ia: <i>forming abstract nouns</i>”), from <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē, “prostitute, whore”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, <i>forming verbs</i>), from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;perh₂-</i> (“to sell, to provide for a fee”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prostitute; to be a prostitute</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) to commit fornication or adultery</li><li>(biblical,&#32;figurative) to commit idolatry</li></ol><p>From <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē, “prostitute”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a brothel, lupanar, whorehouse</li></ol><p>From <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē, “prostitute”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prostitutional; relating to prostitution</li></ol><p>From <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brothelkeeper</li></ol><p>From <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē, “prostitute”) +&lrm; <i>βόσκω</i> (bóskō, “feed, tend, nourish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pornographer; a pornographic author</li></ol><p>From <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē, “harlot”) +&lrm; <i>-γρᾰ́φος</i> (-grắphos, “drawer, painter, writer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>πορσύνω</i> (porsúnō)</li></ol><p>From <i>πόρσω</i> (pórsō) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, provide</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to provide for oneself</li></ol><li>execute, order, arrange, accomplish</li><li>tend to, treat with care, honor, adore</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Homeric,&#32;of a wife) to prepare [her] husband's bed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(Homeric,&#32;figurative,&#32;of a wife) to have sex with, to copulate, to lie with</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>πόρσω</i> (pórsō) +&lrm; <i>-ύνω</i> (-únō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple swamphen (<i>Porphyrio porphyrio</i>)</li><li>kind of octopus</li><li>kind of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra, “purple dye”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πορφυρίων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Porphyrion</li></ol><p>From <i>πορφῠρῐ́ων</i> (porphŭrĭ́ōn, “purple coot or water-hen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born to the purple, an epithet of some Byzantine royals</li></ol><p>From <i>πορφῠ́ρᾱ</i> (porphŭ́rā, “purple dye”) +&lrm; <i>γεννητός</i> (gennētós, “born”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with purple girdle</li></ol><p>From <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra, “purple”) +&lrm; <i>ζώνη</i> (zṓnē, “girdle, belt”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple-fish (<i>Hexaplex trunculus</i>)</li><li>a purple dye obtained from it</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cloths of purple</li><li>purple stripe or other adornment of a garment</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>πορφύρω</i> (porphúrō, “(of waves) to heave, boil, surge up”), itself related to <i>φύρω</i> (phúrō, “to mix”), Hittite <i>𒁍𒊒𒌓</i> (purut, “mud, soil”) and Sanskrit <i>भुरति</i> (bhuráti, “have convulsions, sprawl”). However, Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the absence of connections in other Indo-European languages.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple (in color)</li></ol><p><i>πορφύρᾱ</i> (porphúrā) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos), of unknown, perhaps Semitic, origin. The association with <i>πορφύρω</i> (porphúrō, “to surge up (of waves)”), through an association with the “wine-dark sea”, is a folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of waves in a troubled sea</i>) to heave, boil, surge up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be troubled or upset, to brood</li><li>to grow purple or red, to redden</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrewh₁-</i> (“to boil”). Related to <i>φῡ́ρω</i> (phū́rō, “to mix, jumble together”).</p><p>Various folk etymologies connecting this word to <i>πορφύρᾱ</i> (porphúrā, “purple”) have been proposed, but none are plausible.</p><p>From <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra, “purple, red”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how many times? how often?</li></ol><p>From <i>ποσός</i> (posós) +&lrm; <i>-άκις</i> (-ákis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how many times?</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>how many times multiplied?</li></ol><p>From <i>πόσος</i> (pósos) +&lrm; <i>-πλάσιος</i> (-plásios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holding a certain place in the ordinal series, “somethingth”</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a certain number, amount, or size of; however many, however much, however big</li></ol><p>See <i>πόσος</i> (pósos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quantity</li><li>amount, sum (<i>of money etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>poetry</i>) quantity (<i>of syllables</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πόσος</i> (pósos) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ποτε</i> (pote) (<i>used before a smooth breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at some time; once; ever</li><li>presumably; possibly</li><li>whatever; whoever</li></ol><p>Indefinite form of interrogative <i>πότε</i> (póte, “when?”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drinkable; fit or suitable for drinking</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₃tós</i> (“(having been) drunk”). Cognate with Latin <i>pōtus</i> and Sanskrit <i>पी॒त</i> (pītá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinking-cup</li></ol><p>From <i>πίνω</i> (pínō, “to drink”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the cup of the Eucharist</li><li>jar</li><li>receptacle for offerings in a temple</li></ol><li>absorbent preparation</li><li>goat's thorn (<i>Astragalus</i> spp., eg, <i>Astragalus poterium</i>, <i>Astragalus tragacanthus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ποτήρ</i> (potḗr, “cup”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, “diminutive suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) a drink</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₃-</i>; compare <i>πίνω</i> (pínō) and <i>πόσις</i> (pósis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Doric) synonym of <i>πρός</i> (prós)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;poti</i>; equivalent to <i>πρός</i> (prós), but along with <i>πός</i> (pós) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give to drink</li></ol><p><i>πότος</i> (pótos, “a draught, drink”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pondweed (<i>Potamogeton natans</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ποταμός</i> (potamós, “river”) +&lrm; <i>γείτων</i> (geítōn, “neighbour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river, stream</li><li>canal</li></ol><p>Uncertain, though most theories imply a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (“to spread out; to fly”) by relating the term to reflexes as such:</p><p>Other theories relate the term to <i>πόντος</i> (póntos, “sea”) &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pónteh₁s</i> (“path, bridge”), or to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kweth₂-</i> (“to bubble, foam”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enclitic form of <i>ποτέ</i> (poté)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>indefinite</i>) alternative form of <i>πότερος</i> (póteros, “either of two”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table for setting out cups, sideboard, buffet</li></ol><p>From <i>ποτήρ</i> (potḗr, “drinking-cup”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Doric) motion towards, accession to, or nearness to</li></ol><p>Prefix form of the preposition <i>ποτῐ́</i> (potĭ́, “towards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) waiting, awaiting</li></ol><p>Aorist middle participle of <i>ποτῐδέχομαι</i> (potĭdékhomai). By surface analysis, <i>ποτῐ-</i> (potĭ-) +&lrm; <i>δέγμενος</i> (dégmenos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drink; beverage (<i>especially wine</i>)</li><li>drinking water</li></ol><p>Nominalization of the neuter of <i>ποτός</i> (potós, “drinkable, for drinking”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₃tós</i>, from <i>&#42;peh₃-</i> (“to drink”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drinkable; fit or suitable for drinking</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₃tós</i> (“(having been) drunk”). Cognate with Latin <i>pōtus</i> and Sanskrit <i>पी॒त</i> (pītá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drinkable; fit or suitable for drinking</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₃tós</i> (“(having been) drunk”). Cognate with Latin <i>pōtus</i> and Sanskrit <i>पी॒त</i> (pītá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>enclitic</i>) anywhere, somewhere</li><li>possibly, perhaps</li><li>about, approximately</li></ol><p>See <i>ποῦ</i> (poû).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) much-nourishing, all-nourishing (<i>epithet of the Earth</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πουλῠ-</i> (poulŭ-, Epic variant of <i>πολῠ-</i> (polŭ-)) +&lrm; <i>βοτήρ</i> (botḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾱ</i> (-iā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>anywhere, somewhere</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷís</i>. Compare its interrogative form <i>ποῦ</i> (poû).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot</li><li>leg</li><li>(<i>unit of measure</i>) Greek foot or pous, the ancient Greek and Byzantine unit of length originally based upon the length of a shod foot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pṓds</i>. Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀡</i> (po) (dat. <i>𐀡𐀆</i> (po-de /⁠podei⁠/)), Latin <i>pēs</i>, Sanskrit <i>पद्</i> (pad), Old Armenian <i>ոտն</i> (otn) and <i>հետ</i> (het), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍉𐍄𐌿𐍃</i> (fōtus) and Old English <i>fōt</i> (English <i>foot</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whither? whereto? where?</li></ol><p>Like the indefinite adverb <i>ποι</i> (poi), a locative adverb from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> +&lrm; <i>-ι</i> (-i, locative adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Epic,&#32;rare&#32;in&#32;Ionic) of what kind?, of what nature?, which?, what?</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix). Compare the indefinite form <i>ποιός</i> (poiós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative adverb</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>where?</li><li>how?, in what manner?</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷosyo</i>. Compare its indefinite form <i>πού</i> (poú), as well as <i>πόθεν</i> (póthen).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Cretan form of <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;prā́ťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥h₂-k-yé-ti</i>, a <i>*k</i>-enlargement of <i>&#42;per(h₂)-</i> (“to go over, cross”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>executor, avenger</li><li>exactor, tax official, tax collector</li></ol><p><i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō, “to practice, accomplish”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “agent noun suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leek green, light green</li></ol><p>From <i>πράσον</i> (práson, “leek”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white horehound (<i>Marrubium vulgare</i>)</li><li>kind of seaweed</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾰ́σον</i> (prắson, “leek”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πρᾰ́σῐνος</i> (prắsĭnos, “leek green; light green”)</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾰ́σον</i> (prắson, “leek”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leek, <i>Allium ampeloprasum</i> (syn. <i>Allium porrum</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥so-</i> (“leek”), and connected with Latin <i>porrum</i> (“leek”) and Albanian <i>presh</i>. However, Beekes does not rule out a Pre-Greek origin due to its semantic category (vegetable names are usually derived from substrates in Indo-European languages).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do; to practise</li><li>to accomplish; to make</li><li>to fare (well or badly)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;prā́ťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥h₂-k-yé-ti</i>, a <i>*k</i>-enlargement of <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to go over, cross”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;prā́ťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥h₂-k-yé-ti</i>, a <i>*k</i>-enlargement of <i>&#42;per(h₂)-</i> (“to go over, cross”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) bottom of the trunk of a tree, stump</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) stem, trunk, bole</li><li>enlarged bark of the stem of the olive</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) base of a pillar</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Furnée assumes that is a variant of <i>πρυμνός</i> (prumnós, “hindmost, undermost”) and considers the word Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be clearly seen, to be visible or conspicuous among a number</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be heard</li><li>to smell strong</li></ol><li>to be conspicuously like, to be like, to resemble</li><li>to be conspicuously fit, to become, beseem, suit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in participle</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>neuter participle</i>) that which is seemly, fitness, propriety, decorum</li></ol><li>(<i>rarely&#32;with a person as the subject</i>) fit</li><li>(<i>mostly impersonal</i>) it is fitting, it beseems, suits, becomes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with personal accusative and infinitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>with infinitive only</i>)</li><li>when an accusative alone follows, an infinitive must be supplied</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to liken</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;prep-</i> (“to appear, be conspicuous”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>երեւիմ</i> (erewim, “to become visible, appear”), Old Irish <i>richt</i> (“shape”), Welsh <i>rhith</i> (“species”), and perhaps Old High German <i>furben</i> (“to clean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of fish</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>πρέπω</i> (prépō, “to be appropriate”), properly "fit for eating".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diplomatic mission, legation, embassy</li></ol><p>From <i>πρεσβεύς</i> (presbeús, “ambassador”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elderly, aged</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old man</li><li>elder, chief, prince</li><li>(<i>superlative</i>) revered, honored</li><li>ambassador</li><li>(<i>at Sparta</i>) a political title, higher than <i>γέρων</i> (gérōn, “senator”): chief, president</li><li>a name of the <i>τρόχιλος</i> (trókhilos, “wren”)</li><li>a type of daw or chough</li></ol><p>The first component of the word is likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pres</i> (“before, in front”), an extended form of <i>&#42;per-</i>. The second component is debated:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn</li><li>to blow out, to swell out by blowing</li><li>to spout</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;prḗtʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;préh₁-dʰe-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;preh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πράσσω</i> (prássō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;prā́ťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥h₂-k-yé-ti</i>, a <i>*k</i>-enlargement of <i>&#42;per(h₂)-</i> (“to go over, cross”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πρῐ́ω</i> (prĭ́ō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>before; sooner; formerly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of future time,&#32;with future indicative)</li><li>(<i>of past time</i>) formerly, once</li></ol></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>before that, before</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with aorist</i>)</li><li>(with present,&#32;conveying a sense of continuance)</li><li>(<i>with perfect</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>with a finite verb</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(with indicative,&#32;chiefly aorist)</li><li>(with subjunctive,&#32;after negatives)</li><li>(<i>with optative</i>)</li><li>(with <i>ἄν</i> (án))</li><li>(<i>without a verb</i>)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>Appears to be related to <i>πρό</i> (pró), Latin <i>prior</i>, <i>priscus</i> and Lithuanian <i>prie</i>, but the exact formation is uncertain. Perhaps from an old locative <i>&#42;pr-i</i> of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pró</i> (“forward”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of holm oak</li><li>oaken, tough, sturdy</li></ol><p>From <i>πρῖνος</i> (prînos, “holm oak”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything sawn, sawdust</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) prism</li></ol><p>From <i>πρίω</i> (príō, “I saw, I cut”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Common sawfish (<i>Pristis pristis</i>, syn. <i>Pristis antiquorum</i>).</li><li>Ship of war, from its shape of a sawfish.</li><li>A kind of cup, from its shape of a sawfish.</li><li>Ornamental part of a surgical machine.</li><li>A stonemason's implement.</li></ol><p>From <i>πρίζω</i> (prízō, “to saw”) +&lrm; <i>-τις</i> (-tis, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to saw</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>surgery</i>) to trephine</li></ol><li>(of teeth,&#32;pathology) to grind or gnash</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>generally</i>) to bite</li><li>(<i>of syllables</i>) to cut off</li></ol><li>to seize as with the teeth, to grip, to grind fast</li></ol><p>Unknown. This verb seems to point to a stem <i>&#42;pris-</i>, perhaps older <i>&#42;pri-</i>. It may correlate with <i>πείρω</i> (peírō, “to pierce”), however the value of this comparison is yet unclear. Further etymological comparison has proven fruitless; the link with Albanian <i>prish</i> (“to spoil, break, destroy”) is uncertain, while others assume an onomatopoeic origin.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy; to purchase</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in present and imperfect tenses</i>) to offer to buy; to deal for; to bargain or bid for</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to buy from</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to buy for such a price</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to buy with</li></ol><li>to bid for public taxes or tolls</li><li>to buy off; to avert by giving hush money</li><li>to bribe; to pay off</li></ol><p>From <i>ὦνος</i> (ônos, “price”) +&lrm; <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai).</p><p>The suppletive aorist <i>ἐπρῐάμην</i> (eprĭámēn) is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷreyh₂-</i> (“to buy”). Cognates include Welsh <i>prynu</i>, Old Irish <i>crenaid</i>, Sanskrit <i>क्री॒णाति॑</i> (krīṇā́ti) (root <i>क्री</i> (krī)), Old East Slavic <i>крити</i> (kriti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saw</li><li>trephine, trepan</li><li>ridge of hills</li><li>a kind of marine animal, possibly the dolphin</li></ol><p>From <i>πρίω</i> (príō, “to saw, cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make soft, mild, or gentle</li><li>to soothe, calm</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾶος</i> (prâos, “soft, gentle”) +&lrm; <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πρᾶος</i> (prâos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prosecution of business, diligent study</li><li>occupation, business, duty</li><li>treatment of a subject</li><li>philosophical argument or treatise</li><li>systematic or scientific historical treatise</li><li>magical operation, spell, charm</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾱγματεύομαι</i> (prāgmateúomai) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for action or business; businesslike, statesmanlike</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(substantive) agent, attorney</li></ol><li>(of things)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of history) political (including military)</li><li>(of speech or action) able, prudent, statesmanlike</li></ol><li>relating to subject-matter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>relating to fact, factual</li><li>material (opposed to formal, verbal)</li></ol><li>(magic) effective spell</li><li>troublesome, formidable (of a citadel)</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾶγμα</i> (prâgma, “a thing done, a fact”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apricot</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>praecoquum</i> (“precocious”), variant of <i>praecox</i> (“early-ripe”), apparently because the apricot was considered a "precocious" variant of the peach. <i>βερίκοκκον</i> (beríkokkon) (whence modern Greek <i>βερίκοκο</i> (veríkoko)) is traditionally connected and considered as a variant form; however, the details of its relation to and phonetic development from <i>πραικόκιον</i> (praikókion) are unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) dark in colour</li></ol><p>From a zero-grade <i>&#42;pr̥ḱ-n-</i>, of the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;perḱ-</i> (“coloured, motley”), whence also <i>περκνός</i> (perknós, “dusky, dark”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must be done</li></ol><p><i>πράσσω</i> (prássō, “do”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, verbal adjective stem)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of, pertaining to, or appropriate for action</li><li>Fit for or concerned with action or business; practical</li><li>Active, effective</li><li>Able to bring about or effect [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾱγ-</i> (prāg-), the root of <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō, “to do”), + <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prone, with the face downwards, lying on the front</li></ol><p>Cannot be separated from <i>ἀπηνής</i> (apēnḗs, “stubborn, strict”) and <i>προσηνής</i> (prosēnḗs, “soft, gentle”), so it may contain Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ēnos</i> (“face”). See also Latin <i>prōnus</i> (“prone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) midriff; diaphragm</li><li>spirit; mind</li><li>heart; as the seat of emotions</li><li>prudence; wisdom</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>σᾰνῐ́ς</i> (sănĭ́s), <i>ἐλπῐ́ς</i> (elpĭ́s) and <i>φροντῐ́ς</i> (phrontĭ́s). Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;krep-</i> (“body”), the same root of Latin <i>corpus</i> (“body”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hrefaz</i> (“body; belly”) and Sanskrit <i>कृप्</i> (kṛp, “beauty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>designation of goats of a certain age</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Solmsen, of Anatolian origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seller</li><li>(<i>πρᾱτήρ λίθος</i>) the stone on which slaves were sold</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;prātḗr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥h₂tḗr</i>, from <i>&#42;perh₂-</i> (“sell”) + <i>&#42;-tḗr</i>. By surface analysis, <i>πέρνημῐ</i> (pérnēmĭ, “to sell”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr). Outside of Hellenic, compare Latin <i>pars</i>, whence English <i>party</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place for selling, market</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾱτήρ</i> (prātḗr, “seller”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, “toponym suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gentleness; mildness; meekness</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾶος</i> (prâos) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>embassy</li></ol><p>From <i>πρεσβ(εύω)</i> (presb(eúō), “I am elderly; I am ambassador”) +&lrm; <i>-εία</i> (-eía, “nominal suffix”) <i>and see</i> <i>πρέσβυς</i> (présbus, “elder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be the elder or eldest</li><li>to take the first place, be best</li><li>to rank before, take precedence of [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to rule over [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to place (someone or something) as eldest or first, to put first in rank</li><li>to pay honor or worship</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to hold the first place</li><li>to serve an ambassador</li><li>to negotiate (e.g. peace) as an ambassador</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to send ambassadors</li></ol><p><i>πρέσβυς</i> (présbus) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) the older or elder (of two people)</li><li>an old woman</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) presbytera:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Koine,&#32;early Christianity):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>the wife of a presbyter</li><li>a (female) church elder and community leader who assists the local bishop</li><li>an abbess</li></ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) the wife of a priest</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>council, body of elders, presbytery</li></ol><p>From <i>πρεσβῠ́τερος</i> (presbŭ́teros, “old”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), from <i>πρέσβῠς</i> (présbŭs, “old man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very old</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) the older or elder (of two people)</li><li>an old man</li><li>a forefather</li><li>a teacher or master</li><li>presbyter; elder (a term of rank or office):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) a member of the Jewish Sanhedrin</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical) in the New Testament, a member of a council that presided over the assemblies or congregations</li><li>(Koine,&#32;early Christianity) a church elder and community leader who assists the local bishop</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a priest</li></ol><li>a Roman senator</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πρέσβῠς</i> (présbŭs, “aged”, “elder”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, suffix forming comparative degrees of adjectives). The Christian religious meanings are likely a semantic loan from an Aramaic word, perhaps cognate with Classical Syriac <i>ܩܫܝܫܐ</i> (qaššīšā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>age, seniority, elder status</li><li>old man, elder</li></ol><p>From <i>πρέσβυς</i> (présbus, “elder”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p><p>Prose form of πρέσβυς (présbus).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πρᾶος</i> (prâos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πρᾱνής</i> (prānḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thunderstorm accompanied by lightning</li></ol><p>From <i>πίμπρημι</i> (pímprēmi, “I burn”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) priapism</li></ol><p>From <i>Πρίᾱπος</i> (Príāpos) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>betony (<i>Betonica officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πρῑ́ων</i> (prī́ōn, “saw, trephine”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saw</li><li>sawyer</li></ol><p>From <i>πρῐ́ω</i> (prĭ́ō, “to saw”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with substantives</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>denotes position before or in front</i>)</li><li>(<i>denotes priority of rank, order</i>)</li><li>(<i>denotes standing in another's place</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>with adjectives</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>denotes proximity, readiness</i>)</li><li>(<i>denotes a coming forth</i>)</li><li>(<i>denotes prematureness</i>)</li><li>(<i>denotes intensity</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>with verbs</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) before, forwards</li><li>forth, publicly</li><li>before one, away</li><li>before, in preference</li><li>before, beforehand, of foresight</li></ol></ol><p>See <i>πρό</i> (pró).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send forth</li><li>to hurl, launch</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold before, hold out, hold before</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to put forward, use as a pretext, allege, plead</li><li>to propose, offer</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ἴσχω</i> (ískhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead forward, on, onward</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to bring forward</li><li>to lead on, induce, persuade</li><li>to carry on or forward</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to promote, prefer</li><li>to prefer</li></ol><li>(<i>in perfect passive with a middle sense</i>)</li></ol><li>(seemingly intransitive,&#32;properly of an officer) to lead on, advance, push forward</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to precede</li><li>to go on, advance</li><li>to excel</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to go, lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>issuing forth</li><li>progress, procession</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ἔλευσῐς</i> (éleusĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go forward, go in advance</li><li>to proceed, go on (<i>in narrating, or speaking</i>)</li><li>to arrive first</li><li>to appear in court</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (“before”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (“to come”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ahead</li><li>to jut forward</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to hold or have before oneself</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to excel, be better</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dowry</li></ol><p>Prefixed with <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “in front”), with the second element continuing a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyHk-</i> (“to stretch forth the hand”), whence also <i>ἵκω</i> (híkō, “to come, reach”) and Lithuanian <i>si̇́ekti, si̇́ekiu</i> (“to reach out”), hence originally “reaching out (of the hand), presentation”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (reasoned) choice, preference</li><li>an end, a goal, a purpose, a resolution</li></ol><p>From <i>προαιρέομαι</i> (proairéomai) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis). From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>αἵρεσις</i> (haíresis, “taking; choice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell beforehand</li><li>to foretell, prophesy</li><li>to state, declare or proclaim publicly</li><li>to order</li><li>to give notice, notify</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “towards”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγορεύω</i> (agoreúō, “to speak”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go forth to meet</li><li>to meet beforehand</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ἀπαντάω</i> (apantáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dismiss before</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to liberate from before, free from before</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓πολῡ́ω</i> (ăpolū́ō, “loose from”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to throw or lay before, throw to</li><li>to put forward as a defense</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to put forward, begin</li><li>to put forward as an argument or plea</li><li>to put forward or propose for an office</li><li>to propose a question, task, problem, riddle</li><li>to put forth beyond</li></ol><li>to expose, give up</li><li>to send forth, emit</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall forward</li></ol><li>(<i>medium and perfect passive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to throw or toss before one, to throw away, expose</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to lay before or first</li><li>to set before oneself, propose to oneself</li><li>to put forward, propose for election</li></ol><li>to throw beyond, beat in throwing; to surpass, excel</li><li>to hold before oneself so as to protect</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to put forward</li><li>to put before</li></ol><li>(<i>Attic law</i>) to accuse a person before the Ecclesia by the process called <i>προβολή</i> (probolḗ), literally to present him as guilty of the offense</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “throw, cast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prefer, choose as one's preference</li></ol><p>Perfect of προ+βούλομαι. The verb occurs only once in Homer, and is in the perfect tense, but with present meaning, as is common for verbs of mental state (cf. οἶδα), especially when an intensified meaning is intended.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step on, step forward, advance</li><li>to advance, proceed</li><li>to go before</li><li>to overstep</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>πρόβλημᾰ</i> (próblēmă, “a shield”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a problem, problematic</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόβλημα</i> (próblēma) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a projection, prominence</li><li>(<i>law</i>) presentation of a case to the Assembly</li></ol><p>From <i>προβάλλω</i> (probállō, “to set forth”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>means of providing food</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>elephant’s trunk</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>fly’s proboscis</li></ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) tentacles of the decapod cephalopoda</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before, in front of”) +&lrm; <i>βόσκω</i> (bóskō, “to feed, to graze”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, suffix to form a feminine noun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come forward</li><li>to be born before; to exist before</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>older, elder (earlier in birth)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreknowing, prescient</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) a sign of the future, prognostic</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) name of an antidote mentioned by Galen</li></ol><p>Analyzable as both <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>γνωστικός</i> (gnōstikós) and <i>πρόγνωσις</i> (prógnōsis) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write before or first</li><li>to give public notice of, set forth publicly</li><li>to summon by public notice</li><li>to write at the top</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public notice, advertisement</li><li>notice of sale</li><li>public sale of confiscated property</li><li>table drawn up in advance, of an astronomical cycle</li><li>heading, preliminary form</li></ol><p>From <i>προγρᾰ́φω</i> (progrắphō, “to write before or first”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepchild</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόγονος</i> (prógonos, “forefather, ancestor”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give first or beforehand</li><li>to prepay</li><li>to give up (<i>to an enemy etc.</i>), deliver up, betray</li><li>to forsake, abandon, desert</li><li>to fail</li><li>to give up (<i>as lost</i>), bid adieu to</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi, “to give”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perceive, recognize or understand beforehand</li><li>to make a previous decree</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>διαγιγνώσκω</i> (diagignṓskō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissolve or break up before</li><li>to relax previously</li><li>to dilute previously</li><li>to mitigate first</li><li>to refute by anticipation</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before, beforehand”) +&lrm; <i>διαλῡ́ω</i> (dialū́ō, “dissolve”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treason, treachery</li></ol><p>From the verbal stem of <i>προδίδωμι</i> (prodídōmi, “to betray”) (προ-, the preposition, and -δω-, the verbal stem of δίδωμι) and the suffix <i>-σια</i> (-sia) (a suffix often used in nouns derived from verbs or adjectives)</p><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) and <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi, “to give, to deliver”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traitor, betrayer</li></ol><p>From <i>προδίδωμι</i> (prodídōmi, “to betray”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling in a place before</li></ol><p><i>προενοικέω</i> (proenoikéō) (from <i>προ-</i> (pro-) + <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) + <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō)) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plot against beforehand</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ἐπιβουλεύω</i> (epibouleúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to solve previously</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>ἐπιλῡ́ω</i> (epilū́ō, “loose, untie”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell or state before</li><li>to premise</li><li>to proclaim or declare publicly</li><li>to order or command before</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ἐρέω</i> (eréō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get ready beforehand</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) + <i>ἑτοιμάζω</i> (hetoimázō, “I prepare”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell or state before</li><li>to premise</li><li>to proclaim or declare publicly</li><li>to order or command before</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>previously, beforehand</li></ol><p>Adverb from participle <i>προηγούμενος</i> (proēgoúmenos, “previous”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for prefixing; prepositional</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόθεσις</i> (próthesis, “setting before”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>(+infinitive)</i> to be eager, willing, ready to do something</li><li><i>(absolute)</i> to show zeal</li><li><i>(+accusative)</i> to be eager or zealous for, to desire ardently</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόθῡμος</i> (próthūmos, “eager, willing”) +&lrm; <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>readiness, willingness, eagerness, zeal</li><li>goodwill, ready kindness</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόθῡμος</i> (próthūmos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eagerly, zealously</li></ol><p><i>πρόθῡμος</i> (próthūmos, “eager”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call forth</li><li>to challenge, defy, provoke</li><li>to invite or summon beforehand</li><li>to offer or propose</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “to, towards, with”) +&lrm; <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō, “to call”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>προκαλέω</i> (prokaléō)</li></ol><p>From <i>προκαλέω</i> (prokaléō) +&lrm; <i>-ίζομαι</i> (-ízomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin first</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to dominate, rule</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>κατάρχω</i> (katárkhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment on account; caution money paid down by a farmer of the revenue</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) +&lrm; <i>βολή</i> (bolḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prejudge</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>κατα-</i> (kata-, “in, for the purpose of”) +&lrm; <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “to know, perceive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to preoccupy</li><li>to anticipate</li><li>to frustrate</li><li>to surprise</li><li>to overpower before</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>καταλαμβάνω</i> (katalambánō, “to seize”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break up, annul beforehand</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>καταλῡ́ω</i> (katalū́ō, “destroy”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inhabit before</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “dwell, inhabit”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run risk before others, brave the first danger, bear the brunt of battle</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>κινδῡνεύω</i> (kindūneúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>progress in a journey</li><li>progress, advancement</li><li>improvement</li></ol><p>From <i>προκόπτω</i> (prokóptō, “to advance”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conceal in advance</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before, beforehand”) +&lrm; <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakwater</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “pro-”) +&lrm; <i>κῦμᾰ</i> (kûmă, “wave”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forelock (of men and horses)</li><li>false hair, false front</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>κόμη</i> (kómē, “hair of the head”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick out, choose (before others), prefer</li><li>to foretell, announce beforehand, predict</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to say beforehand, say afore</li></ol><li>to state publicly, proclaim, announce</li><li>to order (someone) to (do something); caution, warn</li><li>to denounce (punishment)</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take, receive or seize beforehand</li><li>to take in preference [<i>with</i> <b>πρό</b> (pró) <i>and</i> genitive]</li><li>to take away or off before</li><li>to anticipate</li><li>to precede, go before</li><li>to repeat from the origin</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to leave behind, forsake, abandon</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fail to do (<i>something</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to desert, fail (<i>someone</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cease or fail beforehand</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to faint, fall into a swoon</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>λείπω</i> (leípō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>law students who were in their fifth year of study</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>λύται</i> (lútai, “fourth-year law students”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to undo, refute before</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “loosen, dissolve”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take care beforehand, provide for</li></ol><p>A passive verbal formation from <i>προμηθής</i> (promēthḗs, “forethinking”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forethinking, cautious, prudent, provident</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) requiring forethought</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) + an unattested <i>&#42;μῆθος</i> (&#42;mêthos), <i>&#42;μᾶθος</i> (&#42;mâthos), generally considered derived from <i>μᾰθεῖν</i> (mătheîn, “to learn”), aorist active infinitive of <i>μᾰνθᾰ́νω</i> (mănthắnō, “to learn”), with adjectival suffix <i>-ης</i> (-ēs). Despite common belief, not from <i>μῆτις</i> (mêtis, “skill, counsel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>προμηθέομαι</i> (promēthéomai, “use forethought, take care”)</li></ol><p>See <i>προμηθέομαι</i> (promēthéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to denounce beforehand [<i>with</i> dative <i>and</i> accusative]</li><li>to predict, indicate before [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (“before”) +&lrm; <i>μηνύω</i> (“inform; disclose”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to perceive before, foresee</li><li>to think of or plan beforehand, provide</li><li>to be provident, take measures of precaution</li><li>to provide for, take thought for [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόνοος</i> (prónoos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proxeny: public friendship; treaty or compact of friendship between a state and a foreigner</li><li>the status and privileges of a πρόξενος (próxenos)</li><li>inscription or written instrument in witness of πρόξενος (próxenos)</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόξενος</i> (próxenos, “public guest or friend”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā), ultimately from <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “stranger, foreigner”). See also Latin <i>hospitium</i> (“hospitality”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a broker, an agent</li></ol><p>From <i>προξενέω</i> (proxenéō, “to manage on another's behalf”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opening, prelude, preamble, preface, introduction</li><li>hymn</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>οἶμος</i> (oîmos, “way, road, path”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see before one, see what is just before the eyes</li><li>to foresee</li><li>to provide, make provision for</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to determine beforehand, predetermine, preordain, predestine</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “pro-”) +&lrm; <i>ὁρίζω</i> (horízō, “to divide, delimit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>early determination, determining a thing in advance</li></ol><p>From <i>προορίζω</i> (proorízō, “to predetermine”) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós, “condition suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to owe beforehand, deserve</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be due beforehand</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ὀφείλω</i> (opheílō)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before (spatial), in front of</li><li>before (temporal), prior to</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>before (spatial), in front</li><li>beforehand (temporal), first</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>πάροιθε</i> (pároithe)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>προπάροιθε</i> (propároithe) with movable nu</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>προπάροιθε</i> (propároithe) with movable nu</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send before, send on, send forward, send forth</li><li>to conduct, attend</li><li>to escort, bring on one's way, accompany</li><li>to pursue</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one's father-in-law's father</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, before) +&lrm; <i>πενθερός</i> (pentherós, “father-in-law”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink before (<i>someone</i>) [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to drink to (<i>someone</i>), drink (<i>someone’s</i>) health, pledge</li><li>to give (<i>as a present</i>), gift, present</li><li>to sacrifice (surrender for the sake of something more urgent)</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>πῑ́νω</i> (pī́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach beforehand, pre-teach</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>παιδεύω</i> (paideúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare beforehand</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to prepare for oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>παρασκευάζω</i> (paraskeuázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) proparoxytone</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰροξῠ́τονος</i> (păroxŭ́tonos, “paroxytone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work or labor beforehand, work for</li><li>to elaborate, work up first</li><li>to suffer pain beforehand</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>πονέω</i> (ponéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out beforehand</li><li>to learn by inquiring before</li><li>to hear beforehand</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-, before) +&lrm; <i>πυνθάνομαι</i> (punthánomai, “to inquire, find out”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>motion towards, accession to, or nearness to</li></ol><p>Prefix form of the preposition <i>πρός</i> (prós, “towards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring</li><li>to furnish, supply</li><li>to add to</li><li>to move towards, apply</li><li>to set (<i>food</i>) before [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to attach oneself [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to’</small>]</li><li>to draw near, approach</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to bring or draw to oneself, attach to oneself, bring over to one's side</li><li>to induce</li><li>to take to oneself, take up</li><li>to procure, import</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “toward”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to go, lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stationing of light-armed troops on wings of phalanx</li></ol><p>From <i>προσεντάττω</i> (prosentáttō, “to insert besides”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come or go to</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a hostile sense</i>)</li><li>to surrender, capitulate</li><li>to come forward to speak</li><li>to come in supplication</li><li>to be engaged in or with</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to be added</li></ol><li>to come in</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “to, toward”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “to come, go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>besides, in addition</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>πρός</i> (prós, “in addition”) and <i>ἔτι</i> (éti, “yet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold to, offer, to bring to</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to bring a ship near a place, bring it to port, to put in, touch at a place, (<i>absolute</i>) to land</li><li>to turn to or towards a thing, to turn one's mind, thoughts, attention to a thing</li><li>to give heed to oneself, to be on one's guard against</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to devote oneself to a thing</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to expect to do</li><li>to continue</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to attach oneself to a thing, cling or cleave to it</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to devote oneself to the service of any one</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be held fast by a thing, attached to it</li></ol><li>to have besides or in addition</li></ol><p>From <i>πρός</i> (prós, “to, towards, with”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have, hold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have arrived at a place, have come, be near at hand, be present</li><li>to belong to [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>also&#32;impersonal</i>) to concern [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to be related [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be fitting, to suit [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>ἥκω</i> (hḗkō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to, one's own</li><li>fitting, befitting, beseeming, proper, meet</li><li>related, akin</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;in the plural) one's relations</li><li>concerning</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sojourner</li><li>stranger, foreigner</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) convert to Judaism, proselyte</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>προσήλυθον</i> (prosḗluthon, aorist of <i>προσέρχομαι</i> (prosérkhomai, “to approach”)) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos), thus literally, “one that has approached”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to address, speak to</li><li>to greet</li><li>to call by name</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “towards”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγορεύω</i> (agoreúō, “to speak”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bringing to or up to, bringing up</li><li>acquisition</li><li>solemn approach</li><li>approach, access (<i>to a person, especially to a king's presence</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγή</i> (agōgḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>attractive</li><li>persuasive</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγός</i> (agōgós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>προσᾰγωγός</i> (prosăgōgós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak to, address</li><li>to speak of, speak about</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>αὐδάω</i> (audáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘against something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to attack, assault [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>to apply, affix</li><li>to carry out</li><li>to procure for; to assign to</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “towards, against”) +&lrm; <i>βᾰ́λλω</i> (bắllō, “to throw”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to aid, come up with succor</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>βοηθέω</i> (boēthéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a putting to, application</li><li>a falling on, attack, assault</li><li>attack party</li><li>an approaching, arrival</li><li>landing place, harbour</li><li>chance, accident, fate</li><li>the iron point of a weapon</li></ol><p>From <i>προσβάλλω</i> (prosbállō, “to apply”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come or go to</li><li>to attach oneself to</li><li>to be added; to accrue</li><li>to happen to</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>orthography</i>) iota adscript</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write in addition, to add in writing</li><li>to add to a list of persons, enroll, register</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to cause to be registered besides</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to register, enroll oneself</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of property) to be marked for confiscation</li><li>to ascribe, attribute</li><li>to prescribe</li><li>to paint together with or beside</li><li>(Koine,&#32;grammar) write immediately after another character (<i>of the subscipt ἰῶτα, written as adscript</i>)</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>γρᾰ́φω</i> (grắphō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive favourably or hospitably, accept</li><li>to admit to or into</li><li>to admit an argument</li><li>to wait patiently</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bind on, attach</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to need besides [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be in want of, stand in need of, require besides [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to desire also [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>to beg or ask [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li><li>to entreat [<i>with</i> accusative <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘someone to do something’</small>]</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind”)</p><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to lack”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to relax further</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “toward”) +&lrm; <i>διαλῡ́ω</i> (dialū́ō, “dissolve”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to expect, to expect that...</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to think, suppose</li><li>(<i>with accusative of object</i>) to expect, look for a thing</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πρός</i> (prós, “to, towards, with”) +&lrm; <i>δοκεύω</i> (dokeúō, “to watch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expectation</li></ol><p>From <i>προσδοκάω</i> (prosdokáō, “I expect”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>expected, looked for</li></ol><p>From <i>προσδοκάω</i> (prosdokáō, “to expect”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer</li><li>place of prayer</li></ol><p>From <i>προσεύχομαι</i> (proseúkhomai, “pray”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, suffix forming action nouns). Morphologically from <i>προσ-</i> (“toward”) + <i>εὐχή</i> (“prayer, wish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray, vow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>)</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to offer prayers, worship</li></ol><li>to pray for a thing</li></ol><p>From <i>πρός</i> (prós, “to”) + <i>εὔχομαι</i> (eúkhomai, “to pray, vow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak to, address</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendly greeting, familiarity</li><li>addressing, appellation, name</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) common noun, appellative</li></ol><p>From <i>προσήγορος</i> (prosḗgoros, “addressing, accosting”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addition, supplement</li></ol><p>From <i>προστίθημι</i> (prostíthēmi). Morphologically, from <i>προσ-</i> +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (“case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adding, repletive; furthering</li><li>(<i>in astronomy</i>) advancing (of planets)</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόσθεσις</i> (prósthesis, “application”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the forepart or entrance of a tent</li><li>proscenium</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a stage curtain</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”) +&lrm; <i>σκηνή</i> (skēnḗ, “tent, stage”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to besiege, beleaguer</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “in front”) +&lrm; <i>καθέζομαι</i> (kathézomai, “to sit down”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call to, call on, summon</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;perfect passive) to call to oneself, call to one</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in Attic,&#32;of an accuser) to cite or summon to court</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “to, towards, with”) +&lrm; <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō, “to call”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillow</li><li>cushion</li></ol><p>From <i>πρός</i> (prós, “with”) and <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry or convey to</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to bring with one, bring home</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to import</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>κομίζω</i> (komízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock against, have a collision</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to take offence at, be angry with [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (“towards”) +&lrm; <i>κρούω</i> (“strike”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make obeisance to the gods, fall down and worship, worship, adore</li><li>to prostrate oneself before a king or superior, bow, bow down</li></ol><p>From <i>πρός</i> (prós, “to, towards”) +&lrm; <i>κυνέω</i> (kunéō, “to kiss”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proskynesis (prostration as an act of obeisance)</li></ol><p><i>προσκυνέω</i> (proskunéō, “to prostrate oneself”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take or receive besides, get over and above</li><li>to take to oneself, take as one's helper or partner</li><li>to take hold of</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to take part in, be accessory to</li><li>to help, assist</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mingle or join to</li><li>to approach</li><li>to go against; to meet in battle; to engage (with); to come to close quarters with</li><li>to come or go close up to</li><li>to put to shore at; to land in</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>μείγνῡμῐ</i> (meígnūmĭ), alternative form of <i>μῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (mī́gnūmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell by or near, to neighbor</li><li>to dwell in</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) attention</li></ol><p>From <i>προσέχω</i> (prosékhō, “to guard against”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>passionate attachment</li><li>(<i>later philosophy</i>) clinging of the soul to the body and its passions</li></ol><p>From <i>προσπᾰθέω</i> (prospăthéō, “to feel passionate love for”) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă) or <i>προσπᾰθής</i> (prospăthḗs, “impressionable”) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă), both ultimately from the same roots.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send to</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall on, strike against (followed by dative or prepositional phrase with <i>εἰς</i> (eis) or <i>πρός</i> (prós) and accusative)</li><li>to attack, assault [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to befall [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>of expenses</i>) to be incurred</li><li>to prostrate oneself before [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “towards, against”) +&lrm; <i>πῑ́πτω</i> (pī́ptō, “to fall”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel passionate love for</li><li>to be affected by contact with, become susceptible to</li></ol><p>From <i>προσπᾰθής</i> (prospăthḗs, “impressionable”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impressionable</li><li>warmly attached</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “forward”) +&lrm; <i>πάθος</i> (páthos, “passion”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to furnish, supply besides</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>παρέχω</i> (parékhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call in besides, invite</li><li>to exhort besides</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>παρακαλέω</i> (parakaléō, “summon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accrue as an additional advantage</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “motion towards”) +&lrm; <i>περῐγῐ́γνομαι</i> (perĭgĭ́gnomai, “to be superior to”), from <i>περῐ-</i> (perĭ-, “around; about”) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to become”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail towards or against</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (pléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make over to</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to attach to oneself, win over</li><li>to appropriate, pretend to, lay claim to</li><li>to pretend, feign, affect, simulate</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corollary</li></ol><p>From <i>προσπορῐ́ζω</i> (prosporĭ́zō, “procure beside”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to place, post, assign</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to command, prescribe, enjoin</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>τάσσω</i> (tássō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>front ranksman</li><li>chief, leader, president, ruler</li><li>protector, guard, champion, patron</li><li>suppliant</li><li>(Koine,&#32;anatomy) prostate (gland)</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>προ-</i> (pro-)Ancient Greek <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi)Ancient Greek <i>προΐστημι</i> (proḯstēmi)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tós</i>der.Ancient Greek <i>-της</i> (-tēs)Ancient Greek <i>προστάτης</i></p><p>From the root of <i>προΐστημι</i> (proḯstēmi, “to stand in front”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs), the former from <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put in front of, shut</li><li>to hand over, deliver</li><li>to grant, bestow</li><li>to impose</li><li>to attribute, impute</li><li>to add, increase</li><li>to join</li><li>(<i>Koine, followed by ind. or inf.</i>) to do again; to repeat or continue</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to agree with/to</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to associate with</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to gain</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to bring upon</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run to or towards</li><li>to come to one</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in hostile sense</i>) to make a sally</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to happen to one</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to join or side with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to approach</li><li>to resemble</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “to, toward”) +&lrm; <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō, “to run, run over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have an interview with before; to become acquainted with before</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>σῠγγῐ́γνομαι</i> (sŭngĭ́gnomai, “to associate; keep company with”), from <i>σῠγ-</i> (sŭg-, “with; together”, <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-)) + <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to become”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring to or upon, apply to</li><li>to lay upon</li><li>to apply, exhibit, employ, use</li><li>to add</li><li>to present, offer, give</li><li>to bring forward, quote, cite</li><li>to contribute, bring in, yield</li><li>to bring near, make similar</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to put in (<i>of ships</i>)</li><li>to go against, attack, assault</li><li>to rush on, make an onset, engage</li><li>to go to or towards</li><li>to deal with, behave oneself in a certain way towards</li><li>to be put or imposed upon</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to consume (<i>food or drink</i>)</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “toward”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resembling, similar [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> (<i>rarely</i>) genitive]</li><li>serviceable [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>προσφέρω</i> (prosphérō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>offering</li></ol><p>From <i>προσφέρω</i> (prosphérō, “to bring, present, offer”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak to, address; to accost</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>φωνέω</i> (phōnéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go to, approach [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to come or go over to, come in, join [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>πρός</b> (prós, + accusative)]</li><li>to accede to</li><li>to put one’s faith in, believe [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mask</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόσωπον</i> (prósōpon, “face, countenance”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dramatization, the putting of speeches into the mouths of characters</li><li>change of grammatical person</li></ol><p>From <i>προσωποποιέω</i> (prosōpopoiéō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a song with an accompaniment</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the tone or pitch of a word</li><li>diacritic mark</li></ol><p>From <i>πρός</i> (prós, “to”) +&lrm; <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>πρός</i> (prós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place or set before, set out</li><li>to hand over, give over</li><li>to expose (<i>a child</i>)</li><li>to set up as a mark or prize, propose</li><li>to propose as a penalty</li><li>to set forth, fix, set</li><li>to propose as a task</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to display, show</li><li>to lay out (<i>a dead body</i>)</li><li>to appoint</li><li>to put forward</li><li>to hold out as a pretext</li><li>to put before or first, put in front</li><li>to prefer</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>τῐ́θημῐ</i> (tĭ́thēmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch out before, hold before</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to expose to danger</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to hold out as a pretext or excuse</li></ol><li>to stretch forth, hold out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to offer, tender</li><li>to put forward, propose</li></ol><li>to put forward as a proposition, suggest</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-, “pro-”) +&lrm; <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to stretch, extend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be before, be in advance</li><li>to be beforehand, take the lead</li><li>to gain an advantage</li><li>to defeat</li></ol><p>From <i>πρότερος</i> (próteros) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on or of the day before</li></ol><p>From <i>πρότερος</i> (próteros) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help beforehand or first [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to avenge oneself before</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>τῑμωρέω</i> (tīmōréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urge forwards</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to turn in headlong flight</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;figurative) to give oneself up</li><li>to urge on, impel, persuade, exhort</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring before or to one, present</li><li>to bring forward, allege, reproach</li><li>to utter, proclaim</li><li>to bring forward, cite</li><li>to display</li><li>to bring out</li><li>to carry off, sweep away</li><li>to put or move forward</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who speaks for and interprets the will of a god: prophet, seer</li><li>one of the keepers of the oracle at Branchidae</li><li>priest at an Egyptian temple</li><li>one who interprets the ravings of a <i>μάντις</i> (mántis, “oracle”)</li><li>interpreter, proclaimer</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) prophet</li><li>herbalist</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>φημί</i> (phēmí, “to say”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er”, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>showing itself or seen beforehand</li><li>seen clearly or plainly, conspicuous</li><li>quite plain or clear</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>visibly, manifestly, obviously</li></ol><p><i>προφανής</i> (prophanḗs, “conspicuous”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>προφητεύω</i> (prophēteúō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the interpretation of the will of the gods: prophecy</li><li>(<i>New Testament</i>) preaching and teaching under the influence of the Holy Spirit</li></ol><p>From <i>προφήτης</i> (prophḗtēs, “speaker of a god”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a prophet, an interpreter of the gods</li><li>(<i>New Testament</i>) to expound scripture, to speak and preach under the influence of the Holy Spirit</li></ol><p>From <i>προφήτης</i> (prophḗtēs, “prophet”) from <i>πρόφημι</i> (próphēmi, “to speak beforehand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oracular, prophetic</li></ol><p>From <i>προφήτης</i> (prophḗtēs, “prophet”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pronunciation, utterance</li></ol><p>From <i>προφέρω</i> (prophérō, “to pronounce”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>readily</li><li>earnestly, zealously</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόφρων</i> (próphrōn) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prophylactic</li></ol><p>From <i>προφυλάσσω</i> (prophulássō) (from <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>φυλάσσω</i> (phulássō)) + <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advance, proceed, progress</li><li>to prosper, succeed</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push forward, propel</li><li>to rush on</li><li>to thrust forward</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “pro-”) +&lrm; <i>ὠθέω</i> (ōthéō, “to push”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to send forth or hurl away</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “forth”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̓ᾰ́πτω</i> (ĭắptō, “send, throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>προοφείλω</i> (proopheílō) (<i>contracted</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plum (fruit)</li></ol><p>Loanword from an Anatolian language of Asia Minor.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>men-at-arms, soldiers</li></ol><p>It is not entirely clear how this word and <i>πρύλις</i> (prúlis, “Cretan weapon-dance”) are related. In view of the formal variant, the word is probably Pre-Greek. Unrelated to <i>πρύτανις</i> (prútanis, “ruler, lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πρυμνήσια</i> (prumnḗsia)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πρυμνήσια</i> (prumnḗsia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) stern-cables, mooring cables</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to the stern of a boat or ship</li></ol><p><i>πρυμνός</i> (prumnós, “hindmost”) +&lrm; <i>-ήσιος</i> (-ḗsios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hindmost, undermost, endmost, end of</li><li>cut off at the root</li><li>broad at base</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests Pre-Greek and connects it with <i>πρέμνον</i> (prémnon, “bottom of the trunk of a tree, stump”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>early in the day</li><li>during morning</li></ol><p>Derived from unattested adverb <i>&#42;πρώ</i> (&#42;prṓ), with locative <i>-ι</i> (-i) as in <i>ἦρῐ</i> (êrĭ); ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pró</i> (“toward; leading to”). Cognate with Latin <i>prō</i> (“for; before”), Sanskrit <i>प्रा॒तर्</i> (prātár, “early in the morning”), German <i>früh</i> (“id”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) anus, rectum</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preHḱt-</i>, <i>&#42;proHḱt-</i>; cognate with Old Armenian <i>երաստան</i> (erastan, “buttocks”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theater</i>) lead actor (actor who plays the main role)</li></ol><p>From <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γωνῐστής</i> (ăgōnĭstḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief flute-player</li></ol><p>From <i>πρωτο-</i> (prōto-, “first”) +&lrm; <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief rank; first place</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the first quality</li></ol><p>From <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the first quality</li></ol><p>From <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the right of speaking first in a law-court, the part of the prosecutor</li><li>the right of the leading advocate to speak first</li></ol><p><i>πρωτολόγος</i> (prōtológos, “speaking first”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking first, <i>especially</i> giving the first speech in court in a trial</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>πρωτᾰγωνῐστής</i> (prōtăgōnĭstḗs, “one who plays the first part, chief actor”)</li><li>(<i>in the phrase ὁ πρωτολόγος ἄρχων</i>) the chief magistrate at certain cities&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>at Aphrodisias</li><li>at Iconium</li></ol></ol><p><i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “speak”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming second-declension adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who stands first, especially the first man on the right of the line; right-hand man</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the front-rank men</li><li>the lochagos</li><li>man in the uneven rows in a lochos, as opposed to the epistates</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) the chief or leader of a party</li></ol><p>From <i>πρωτο-</i> (prōto-, “first”) + <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>primogeniture; the rights of the firstborn</li></ol><p>From <i>πρωτοτόκος</i> (prōtotókos, “firstborn”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a mother</i>) having her firstborn</li></ol><p><i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”) +&lrm; <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō, “to beget”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), with an active meaning. Compare the proparoxytonic form <i>πρωτότοκος</i> (prōtótokos), with a passive meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first-born, first-created</li><li>high-born</li><li>first-ordained</li></ol><p>From <i>πρωτο-</i> (prōto-, “first”) +&lrm; <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “offspring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) first sheet of a papyrus-roll, bearing the official authentication and date of manufacture of the papyrus</li></ol><p>From <i>πρωτο-</i> (prōto-, “first”) +&lrm; <i>κόλλα</i> (kólla, “glue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destined</li></ol><p>From <i>πέπρωμαι</i> (péprōmai) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first-born</li></ol><p><i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”) +&lrm; <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō, “to beget”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), with a passive meaning. Compare the paroxytonic form <i>πρωτοτόκος</i> (prōtotókos), with an active meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) an archetype, an original</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a root word (primitive word from which others are derived)</li></ol><p>The noun is a neuter substantive from the adjective; in the sense of “root word”, an ellipsis of <i>τὸ πρωτότῠπον ἔπος</i> (tò prōtótŭpon épos, “the original word”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;grammar</i>) original, primitive</li><li>archetypal</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a principal party to a contract</li></ol><p><i>πρωτο-</i> (prōto-, “first”) +&lrm; <i>τῠ́πτω</i> (tŭ́ptō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming second-declension compound adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) before, in front of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with verbs of motion</i>)</li><li>before, in front of, for the purpose of shielding or guarding</li><li>further on, forward, onward</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) before</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(in later writers,&#32;with numerals)</li></ol><li>(<i>in other relations</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of preference</i>) before, sooner, rather than</li><li>(<i>of cause or motive</i>) for, out of, from</li></ol></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) before, in front, forth, forward</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) before, beforehand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pró</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pró</i>, <i>&#42;pro-</i> (see <i>&#42;per-</i>).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>pro</i>, Sanskrit <i>प्र-</i> (prá-), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;ɸro</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fram</i> (English <i>from</i>). May be related to <i>πρός</i> (prós, “towards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in plural</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Ionic,&#32;Doric) cattle, flocks, herds</li><li>(Attic,&#32;Koine) sheep</li><li>slaughtered animals, animals for sacrifices</li><li>(<i>proverbial</i>) stupid, lazy people</li></ol><li>a kind of marine fish</li></ol><p>From <i>προβαίνω</i> (probaínō, “to go on”) + <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything thrown forward or projecting</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a hindrance, obstacle</li></ol><li>anything put before one as a defense, bulwark, barrier, screen, shield, wall</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) a defense against a thing</li></ol><li>anything put forward as an excuse or screen</li><li>that which is proposed as a task, business</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>geometry</i>) problem</li><li>(<i>Aristotelian&#32;logic</i>) a question as to whether a statement is so or not</li><li>a problem, difficulty</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;prógʷlāmə</i>. Equivalent to <i>προβάλλω</i> (probállō, “to throw before”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perceiving beforehand</li><li>foreknowledge</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) prognosis</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>γνῶσις</i> (gnôsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forefather, ancestor</li><li>progenitor</li><li>founder</li></ol><p>From <i>προγίγνομαι</i> (progígnomai) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public proclamation, notice</li><li>program</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) model, example</li></ol><p>From <i>προγράφω</i> (prográphō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entryway, forecourt, chamber at the front of a house</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>located in front of the house</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>δόμος</i> (dómos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go forward, go on, advance</li><li>to go first, go in advance [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li><li>to go forth</li><li>to pass on to, begin another thing</li><li>to go on well, succeed</li><li>to be before</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>εἶμι</i> (eîmi).</p><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-uncle</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>θεῖος</i> (theîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) placing in public</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the laying out of a deceased person's body in public as part of funeral rites</li><li>a public notice</li><li>the statement of a case or thesis</li><li>an offering</li></ol><li>stated purpose, purpose statement, proposed end</li><li>purpose, intention</li><li>supposition, calculation</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) a preposition</li></ol><p>From <i>προτῐ́θημῐ</i> (protĭ́thēmĭ) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis) and <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>θέσις</i> (thésis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eager, willing</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “spirit”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>front door, front gate</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>θύρη</i> (thúrē)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be set before, to lie before</li><li>to precede</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calling forth, challenge (to combat, etc.)</li><li>invitation, offer, proposal</li></ol><p><i>προκᾰλέω</i> (prokăléō, “to call forth, challenge”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preconception, mental picture</li><li>taking beforehand</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) anticipation</li></ol><p>From <i>προλαμβάνω</i> (prolambánō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crop of birds</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Adam's apple</li></ol><p><i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”) +&lrm; <i>λοβός</i> (lobós, “lobe”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the prologue of a play</li><li>one who recites the above</li><li>the introduction of a speech</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) the antecedent, in ratios in which the first number is the largest</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “speech”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>champion, foremost fighter</li></ol><p>From <i>προμάχομαι</i> (promákhomai, “to fight in front”), from <i>προ-</i> (pro-) + <i>μάχομαι</i> (mákhomai, “to fight, do battle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foremost man</li><li>chief</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;prómos</i> (whence also Middle Irish <i>rom</i> (“early, too soon”) and Gothic <i>𐍆𐍂𐌰𐌼</i> (fram, “forward”)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pró</i> (“toward, leading to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foresight, forethought, foreknowledge</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to take thought of, show care for [<i>with</i> <b>ἔχω</b> (ékhō, + genitive)]</li><li>to have regard for, forethought concerning [<i>with</i> <b>ποιέω</b> (poiéō, + genitive)]</li></ol><li>providence</li></ol><p>From <i>προνοέω</i> (pronoéō, “to perceive before, foresee”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careful</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roe deer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perḱ-</i> (“motley, coloured”). Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>περκνός</i> (perknós, “dusky”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farwō</i> (“colour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a public foreigner, public guest or friend, made so by an act of the state</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in ancient inscriptions the <i>πρόξενος</i> (próxenos) seems to be a public officer who had to do with registration of wills</li></ol><li>a patron, protector</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>as adjective</i>) assisting, relieving</li></ol></ol><p><i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “strange, foreigner”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πρόξενος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Proxenus</li></ol><p>From <i>πρόξενος</i> (próxenos, “patron, protector”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li><i>going before</i></li><li>Adverb προοδωτέρως <i>progressively</i></li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>going on, advance</i>: metaph., <i>progress</i></li><li><i>coming out</i> of a house; <i>appearance in public</i></li><li><i>procession</i></li><li><i>proceeding forth, emanation</i></li><li>musical <i>progression</i></li><li>mathematical <i>progression</i></li><li>= <i>interrogatio</i></li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know in advance; to have advance knowledge of</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>οἶδα</i> (oîda)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wholly, entirely</li><li>all together</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great-grandfather</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, before) +&lrm; <i>πάππος</i> (páppos, “grandfather”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire</li><li>all together</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>προάστιον</i> (proástion, “suburb”)</li><li>bee glue, propolis</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “fore-”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drink taken before meals</li></ol><p>From <i>προπῑ́νω</i> (propī́nō, “to drink before or first”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foretelling, prediction, previous instruction or warning</li><li>public notice, proclamation</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ῥῆσις</i> (rhêsis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>besides, over and above, thereto, in addition</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>expressing direction</i>) on the side of, from, at, to, forward to, toward</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>from, on the side of, towards, pertaining to, at the hand of, by [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>mostly in religious formulas</i>) before, in presence of, in the eyes of, in the sight of, in the name of</li><li>(<i>derivable from</i>) agreeable to, becoming, like</li></ol><li>(with dative,&#32;expressing proximity) hard by, near to, at, among</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>off (<i>a coast or island</i>), bordering on (<i>countries, peoples</i>)</li><li>before, in presence of (<i>of people</i>)</li><li>(<i>with verbs of motion</i>) towards, upon, against</li><li>(<i>expressing close engagement</i>) at the point of, engaged in</li><li>(<i>expressing union or addition</i>) besides, in addition to</li></ol><li>(with accusative,&#32;expressing motion or direction) towards</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with verbs implying motion</i>) upon, against</li><li>(<i>expressing addition</i>) over and above</li><li>(<i>with verbs of seeing</i>) towards, facing sth., to, against (<i>the wind</i>), in the light of, in (<i>e.g. open day</i>)</li><li>(<i>in hostile or discursive sense</i>) against, in reply to, in accusation</li><li>(<i>for various kinds of intercourse or reciprocal action</i>) with, (<i>have certain feelings</i>) towards, at the hands of, of, inspired by</li><li>before (<i>a gremium, witness etc.</i>), in the eyes of (<i>a God etc.</i>) (<i>of legal or other business contracted</i>)</li><li>towards (<i>the evening etc.</i>), at, about (<i>of time</i>)</li><li>relating to, in reference to, in respect of, touching (<i>some matter</i>); in consequence of, in view of (<i>some fact</i>); according to (<i>a certain document, habitude etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) accompanied by (<i>an instrument</i>)</li><li>(<i>in various adverbial phrases</i>) with, under (<i>e.g. compulsion</i>), by means of, in (<i>e.g. due proportion</i>)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;próti, &#42;préti</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>प्रति</i> (práti), Latin <i>pretium</i>, and Old Church Slavonic <i>противъ</i> (protivŭ). May be related to <i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”).</p><p>Equivalent prepositions in use for <i>πρός</i> (prós) from another root are ποτί (potí) &mdash; Epic,&#32;Doric, ποί (poí) &mdash; <i>Doric</i>, πός (pós) &mdash; <i>Arcadocypriot</i>.</p><p>The genitive is an innovated Greek associative. The dative is from the PIE locative. The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional, with some of the metaphorical uses like <i>παρά</i> (pará).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach, draw near</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>εἶμι</i> (eîmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πρόσθε</i> (prósthe)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πρόσθε</i> (prósthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>πρόσθεν</i> (prósthen, “before”)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) alternative form of <i>πρόσθεν</i> (prósthen, “before”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) before, in front</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) before, earlier, formerly</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before</li></ol><p>From <i>πρός</i> (prós, “toward”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>application</li><li>attachment</li><li>addition</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>θέσις</i> (thésis); compare <i>προστίθημι</i> (prostíthēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rower</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “next to”) +&lrm; <i>κώπη</i> (kṓpē, “oar”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking in addition</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>acquisition</li><li>rise in rank by acquisition of catoecic land</li><li>enrollment</li></ol><li>(logic,&#32;rhetoric) additional assumption</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>minor premise</li></ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) assumption, reception (into the Kingdom of Heaven)</li></ol><p><i>προσλᾰμβᾰ́νω</i> (proslămbắnō, “take or receive besides or in addition, get over and above”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>approach, advance</li><li>income, revenue</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neighboring, dwelling near</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbor</li></ol><p><i>προσ-</i> (pros-) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πρόσω</i> (prósō) with initial syllable made heavy for metrical purposes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ordinance, command</li></ol><p>From <i>προστάσσω</i> (prostássō, “to place or post at a place”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>posting of additional troops on the wings of a phalanx</li><li>ordinance, command</li></ol><p>From <i>προστάσσω</i> (prostássō, “to place or post at a place”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with pillars in front</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) portico</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>στῦλος</i> (stûlos) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fresh, not decomposed</li><li>(<i>of events and actions</i>) recent, fresh, new</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “pro-”) +&lrm; <i>φατός</i> (phatós, “slain”), thus originally a hunting or fishing term meaning "lately or freshly slain", with semantic broadening being facilitated once the second element's origin had become opaque to Greek speakers. However, Beekes is skeptical of the above interpretation of the first syllable as being equivalent to the prefix meaning "forward".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to speak to, address</li></ol><p>From <i>προσ-</i> (pros-, “to, towards”) +&lrm; <i>φημί</i> (phēmí, “to say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forward</li><li>in the future</li></ol><p>From <i>πρός</i> (prós) +&lrm; <i>-ω</i> (-ō, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>face; visage; countenance</li><li>front</li><li>mask</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) character in a drama</li><li>appearance</li><li>person</li></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;προτῐωπον</i> (&#42;protĭōpon, literally “what is opposite to the eyes [of the other]”), analyzed as <i>πρότῐ</i> (prótĭ, “opposite”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on, “nominative neuter suffix”); for the first component, see <i>πρός</i> (prós, “towards”). In light of exact cognates such as Tocharian B <i>pratsāko</i> (“chest”) and Sanskrit <i>प्रती॑क</i> (prátīka, “face; appearance”), this compound may have been directly inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;prótih₃kʷom</i>, though it is equally possible it was formed independently of its cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>posting in front</li><li>prefixing</li></ol><p>From <i>προτάσσω</i> (protássō, “to place in front”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>putting forward; that which is put forward</li><li>(<i>in&#32;logic</i>) proposition</li><li>(<i>in&#32;grammar</i>) hypothetical clause of the sentence, answered by the apodosis</li><li>question proposed, problem</li><li>the earlier part of a dramatic poem, opposed to the epitasis and catastrophe</li><li>a proposal, proposition</li></ol><p><i>προτείνω</i> (proteínō, “to stretch forth”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>formerly, before</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>πρότερος</i> (próteros, “former”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) before, in front</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) former, earlier</li><li>(<i>of rank, worth, and generally of precedence</i>) superior</li></ol><p>From <i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, contrastive or comparative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) before, in front</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) former, earlier</li><li>(<i>of rank, worth, and generally of precedence</i>) superior</li></ol><p>From <i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, contrastive or comparative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alleged cause or motive, plea</li><li>excuse, pretext</li></ol><p><i>πρόφημι</i> (próphēmi) or <i>προφαίνω</i> (prophaínō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>zealous; eager; earnest</li></ol><p><i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ewer for pouring water upon the hands of guests</li><li>wine-jug from which the cupbearer pours into the cups</li><li>unit of measure for liquids used in Tauromenium</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>χοῦς</i> (khoûs, “measure of capacity”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>πρώῐ̈μος</i> (prṓĭ̈mos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) stern, poop</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>πρυμνός</i> (prumnós, “hindmost”), but with different accentuation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prince, ruler, lord, chief</li><li>(<i>at Athens</i>) member of the tribe presiding in boule</li><li>prytanis, title of a chief magistrate</li></ol><p>In view of the close resemblance to Etruscan <i>𐌐𐌖𐌓𐌈𐌍𐌄</i> (purθne, “title of an official”), this word probably belongs to the Anatolian-Aegean stratum of social designations (compare <i>ἄναξ</i> (ánax), <i>βασιλεύς</i> (basileús) and <i>τύραννος</i> (túrannos)); attested with a Hittite suffix <i>-uri</i> (meaning “great, grand” in titles like in grand vizier) in an Ugaritic text as <small>[Cuneiform needed]</small> (ḫupurtanuri), likely passed as <i>&#42;οπορτανις</i> (&#42;oportanis) / <i>&#42;οπυρτανις</i> (&#42;opurtanis) and clipped with the first syllable interpreted as the Greek article and then modified by secondary association with <i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lately; just now</li><li>the day before yesterday</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preh₃i</i>, from <i>&#42;pró</i> (“toward, leading to”). See also <i>πρωῐ̈́</i> (prōĭ̈́, “morning”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) dewdrop</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perḱ-</i> (“motley, coloured”). Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>περκνός</i> (perknós, “dusky”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;farwō</i> (“colour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πρωτεῖος</i> (prōteîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the very first</li></ol><p>From <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐστος</i> (-ĭstos, superlative suffix). Literally, “first-est; most first”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>early</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>born early; premature</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) precocious</li></ol><p>From <i>πρωῐ̈</i> (prōĭ̈) +&lrm; <i>-μος</i> (-mos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deed, act, fact</li><li>occurrence, matter, affair</li><li>thing, concrete reality</li><li>thing, creature</li><li>thing of consequence or importance</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) circumstances, affairs</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;in bad sense) trouble, annoyance</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō, “I do, practice”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act; action; activity; deed; doing</li><li>business dealing</li><li>success</li><li>collection of debts; arrears</li><li>business; office</li><li>work; treatise</li><li>magic spell</li><li>(sex,&#32;euphemistic) sexual intercourse</li><li>conduct; practice</li><li>state; condition</li></ol><p>From <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō, “to do; to practice”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft; gentle</li><li>tame</li><li>mild</li></ol><p>Probably from earlier <i>πρᾱῠ̈́ς</i> (prāŭ̈́s) (through the adverb <i>πράως</i> (práōs), contracted from <i>πραέως</i> (praéōs)), which can be regularly derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;preh₂-i-u-</i>, but the traditional comparison with what is now reconstructed as <i>&#42;preyH-</i> (“to like, feel friendly/well-disposed”) is difficult.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sale (exchange of goods or services for currency or credit)</li></ol><p>From <i>πιπράσκω</i> (pipráskō, “to sell”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πρᾶος</i> (prâos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πρᾶξις</i> (prâxis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) prince</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>prīnceps</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holm oak; ilex (<i>Quercus ilex</i>)</li><li>kermes oak (<i>Quercus coccifera</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a substrate root common to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;brinъ</i> (“larch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πρωΐ</i> (prōḯ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πρῶτᾰ</i> (prôtă, “first; in the first place”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first</li><li>earliest</li><li>foremost, most prominent</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) prime</li></ol><p>From <i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰτος</i> (-ătos, superlative suffix), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pro-atos</i>. Compare <i>ἔσχᾰτος</i> (éskhătos, “furthest”) and <i>ῠ̔́πᾰτος</i> (hŭ́pătos, “highest”).</p><p>Potentially also from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥H-</i> or <i>&#42;per-</i>, with cognates including Lithuanian <i>pirmas</i>, Sanskrit <i>पूर्व</i> (pūrva), and Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬎𐬭𐬬𐬀</i> (paurva). The <i>ω</i> (ō) and <i>ᾱ</i> (ā) forms are difficult to reconcile.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>first; in the first place</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first</li><li>earliest</li><li>foremost, most prominent</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) prime</li></ol><p>From <i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰτος</i> (-ătos, superlative suffix), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pro-atos</i>. Compare <i>ἔσχᾰτος</i> (éskhătos, “furthest”) and <i>ῠ̔́πᾰτος</i> (hŭ́pătos, “highest”).</p><p>Potentially also from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥H-</i> or <i>&#42;per-</i>, with cognates including Lithuanian <i>pirmas</i>, Sanskrit <i>पूर्व</i> (pūrva), and Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬎𐬭𐬬𐬀</i> (paurva). The <i>ω</i> (ō) and <i>ᾱ</i> (ā) forms are difficult to reconcile.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first</li><li>earliest</li><li>foremost, most prominent</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) prime</li></ol><p>From <i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰτος</i> (-ătos, superlative suffix), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pro-atos</i>. Compare <i>ἔσχᾰτος</i> (éskhătos, “furthest”) and <i>ῠ̔́πᾰτος</i> (hŭ́pătos, “highest”).</p><p>Potentially also from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pr̥H-</i> or <i>&#42;per-</i>, with cognates including Lithuanian <i>pirmas</i>, Sanskrit <i>पूर्व</i> (pūrva), and Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬎𐬭𐬬𐬀</i> (paurva). The <i>ω</i> (ō) and <i>ᾱ</i> (ā) forms are difficult to reconcile.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πρωῐ̈́</i> (prōĭ̈́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πρώῐ̈μος</i> (prṓĭ̈mos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The front part of a ship; bow, prow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;prṓwařřa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;préh₃wr̥yh₂</i> (“forepart (of a ship)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sneeze</li></ol><p>Presumably imitative or from a Proto-Indo-European root connected to Polish <i>kichać</i>, Russian <i>чихать</i> (čixatʹ), Lithuanian <i>čiáudėti</i>, Sanskrit <i>क्षु</i> (kṣu), Breton <i>strevia</i> and Latin <i>sternuo</i> (“to sneeze”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild boar</li></ol><p>A possible cognate would be <i>πτελέα</i> (pteléa, “elm”), but this leaves much unexplained. The connection with Lithuanian <i>kiaũlė</i> (“pig, swine”) and Sanskrit <i>किरि</i> (kiri, “boar, hog”) should also be rejected. The connection with <i>πελιτνός</i> (pelitnós) and <i>πελιός</i> (peliós) is arbitrary. The word may well be Pre-Greek, in view of the initial "πτ-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of feathers</li><li>feathered, winged</li></ol><p>From <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “feather”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male fern (<i>Aspidium filix-mas</i>)</li><li>polypody (<i>Polypodium vulgare</i>)</li><li>(the phrase,&#32;νυμφαία πτερίς) bracken (<i>Pteris aquilina</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “feather”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πτέρνη</i> (ptérnē, “heel”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heel</li><li>hoof</li><li>heel of a shoe</li><li>footstep</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) foot or lower part of anything</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) ham</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tpḗrsneh₂</i> (“heel”). Cognates include Latin <i>perna</i> and Sanskrit <i>पार्ष्णि</i> (pā́rṣṇi). Doublet of <i>πέρνα</i> (pérna, “ham”), which was borrowed from Latin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) wing of a bird</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>winged creature, bird</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) flight, augury, omen</li></ol><li>anything like a wing:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(zootomy,&#32;in the plural) fins, flippers</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) feathery foliage</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>ivy (<i>Hedera helix</i>)</li></ol><li>blade of the steering paddle; rudder, oar</li><li>flap of a cuirass</li><li>broad edge of a sword or axe; (<i>zootomy</i>) beak of a swordfish</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) lobe of the lungs; earlobe</li><li>point of a building</li><li>front frame of a torsion engine</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) shoulder blade</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) The title of a poem whose lines form a pattern like wings.</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) sails</li></ol><li>anything that covers or protects like wings</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fence, wall</li></ol></ol><p>Connected with <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “feather”), but with an unexplained suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>causal</i>) to scare, alarm, terrify</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to crouch or cower for fear</li><li>(<i>of men in ambush</i>) to crouch</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin:</p><p>Regardless, note also potential relation with Old Armenian <i>թաքչիմ</i> (tʻakʻčʻim, “to hide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feather, especially a down feather</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the down of a youth's face</li><li>plumelet</li></ol><li>wing, properly of insects</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the wing-like membrane in a kind of serpents</li></ol><li>anything like a feather or wing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>leaf</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the sails of a ship</li></ol></ol><p>Of uncertain origin, with suggestions including Pre-Greek origin as shown by the alternative form <i>ψίλον</i> (psílon), relation to <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “to fly”) (e.g., via a Proto-Indo-European preform <i>&#42;pth₂-il-o-m</i>), or to the Latin <i>pilus</i> (“a hair”).</p><p>Related to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀠𐀴𐁊𐀸𐀭</i> (pi-ti-ro2-we-sa /⁠ptilowessa⁠/, “decorated with a feather pattern”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plumage</li><li>a disease of the eyelids in which their edges become swollen and inflamed, and the eyelashes fall off</li></ol><p>From <i>πτῐλόω</i> (ptĭlóō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to winnow</li><li>to bray in a mortar</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peys-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to stumble, fall or fail</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stumble, trip, fall</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figurative) to make a false step, fail, offend</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;p(y)eh₂w-</i> (“to strike, hit”), and cognate with Latin <i>paviō</i> (“to beat, strike”) and Lithuanian <i>pjáuti</i> (“to reap, cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sneezewort (<i>Achillea ptarmica</i>)</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>πταρμικός</i> (ptarmikós, “causing sneezing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A sneeze.</li></ol><p>From <i>πταίρω</i> (ptaírō, “sneeze”, verb), <i>πτάρνυμαι</i> (ptárnumai, “to sneeze”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wych elm (<i>Ulmus glabra</i>)</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā) is frequent in tree names, like <i>ἰτέᾱ</i> (itéā, “willow”) and <i>μηλέᾱ</i> (mēléā). The word <i>πτέλας</i> (ptélas, “boar”) is formally close but the semantics are far off. Also Latin <i>populus</i> (“poplar”) cannot be related, since it deviates strongly in form and meaning. Maybe from the same source of Latin <i>tilia</i> (“linden”), Old Armenian <i>թեղի</i> (tʻełi, “elm”), Old Georgian <i>თელაჲ</i> (telay, “elm”). Furnée argues for a Pre-Greek origin and connects it with <i>μελία</i> (melía, “ash tree”). Related to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐁇𐀩𐀷</i> (pte-re-wa /⁠*pteléwā⁠/, “~elm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of elm</li></ol><p>From <i>πτελέα</i> (pteléa, “wych elm”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>winglike</li></ol><p>From <i>πτέρῠξ</i> (ptérŭx, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ο-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-like, -oid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feathered</li></ol><p>From <i>πτερόω</i> (pteróō, “to feather”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feathered, winged</li></ol><p>From <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “-ful, having”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πτερόεντα</i> (pteróenta)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feather</li><li>wing</li><li>Any winged creature</li><li>Any wing-like object</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pterón</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péth₂r̥</i> (“feather”). Related to <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “I fly”). Cognate with English <i>feather</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feather, wing; furnish or depict with feathers, wings</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to spread oars like wings</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to excite</li></ol><p>From <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fin (limb of a fish etc.)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) pinnacle (upright member)</li></ol><p>From <i>πτέρῠξ</i> (ptérŭx, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix). Literally, “winglet”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flying</li><li>winged</li><li>(transferred sense,&#32;with ἔπος or μῦθος) well-known</li><li>(transferred sense,&#32;with λόγος, ὄνειρος, ἐλπίς) fleeting, idle</li><li>(transferred sense,&#32;with ἰσχύς) soaring, aspiring</li></ol><p>From <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “I fly”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pearl barley</li><li>barley-gruel</li></ol><p>From <i>πτῐ́σσω</i> (ptĭ́ssō, “to winnow grain”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, nominal suffix), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peys-</i> (“to crush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to terrify, scare, dismay</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to flutter, excite by any passion</li></ol><p>This verb is a secondary formation and it goes back to older <i>&#42;πτω-έω</i> (&#42;ptō-éō) (compare <i>πτώξ</i> (ptṓx, “hare”)); <i>πτοιέω</i> (ptoiéō) and other forms are derived from it by metrical lengthening. The old combination with Latin <i>paveō</i> (“to fear, dread”) is accepted by Merlingen, starting from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tpew-</i>. However, Hackstein reconstructs <i>&#42;ptoh₂eyeti</i>. See also <i>πτήσσω</i> (ptḗssō, “to duck, crouch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) city</li></ol><p>From <i>πτόλῐς</i> (ptólĭs), variant of <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron). Olsen calls the phonetic details "quite obscure."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) sacker of cities (<i>epithet chiefly of Odysseus, sometimes of other heroes or of Ares</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πτόλις</i> (ptólis, “city”) +&lrm; <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō, “to sack”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πολεμίζω</i> (polemízō): synonym of <i>πολεμέω</i> (poleméō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fold</li></ol><p>From <i>πτῠ́σσω</i> (ptŭ́ssō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable of inflection</li></ol><p>From <i>πτῶσις</i> (ptôsis, “a fall; a grammatical case”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apt to fall, fallen</li></ol><p>From <i>πίπτω</i> (píptō, “to fall”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beggarwoman</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poorhouse</li></ol><p>From <i>πτωχοτρόφος</i> (ptōkhotróphos, “supporting the poor”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beggar; one who crouches and cringes</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poor, beggarly</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, since it is highly probable that <i>πτώξ</i> (ptṓx, “hare”) is related (with original meaning "who ducks away, the shy one"), it is probably a Pre-Greek word, in view of the alternation <i>χ/κ</i>. See also <i>πτάκα</i> (ptáka, “hare”), <i>πτήσσω</i> (ptḗssō, “to duck (for fright)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>poorly, scantily</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>πόλις</i> (pólis)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ptólis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tpólHis</i>, from <i>&#42;tpelH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) young branch; sapling; shoot</li><li>sprouting; budding</li></ol><p>The word could be related to Old Armenian <i>որթ</i> (ortʻ, “vine”). Furnée attributes this match rather to independent borrowing, from a common substrate origin, perhaps Pre-Greek. On the other hand, Petersson derives these words from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;portʰo-</i>, beside <i>&#42;pertʰ-</i> (“pole, sprout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A fold or anything folded</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) A pledget</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πτύσσω</i> (ptússō, “I fold”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian eagle owl (<i>Bubo bubo</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps of Pre-Greek origin, given the suffix <i>-υγγ-</i> (-ung-) found also in <i>ῐ̓́ῠ̈γξ</i> (ĭ́ŭ̈nx), <i>λᾰ́ρῠγξ</i> (lắrŭnx) and <i>πῑ́σῠγγος</i> (pī́sŭngos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fold (<i>in cloth etc.</i>)</li><li>plate (<i>of metal, leather etc.</i>)</li><li>leaf (<i>of a writing tablet etc.</i>)</li><li>side of a hill, cleft, glen, corrie</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πτύσσω</i> (ptússō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A folding; a corrugation</li></ol><p>From <i>πτύσσω</i> (ptússō, “I fold”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a winnowing shovel or fan</li><li>(<i>in Cyprus</i>) a corn-measure; hence <i>δίπτυον</i> (díptuon, “half a μέδινος”)</li></ol><p>Stem with πτ- instead of π- (as in <i>πτόλεμος</i> (ptólemos) - <i>πόλεμος</i> (pólemos, “war”), <i>πτόλις</i> (ptólis) - <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”). From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewH-</i> (“to cleanse, purify”). Cognate with <i>pūrus</i>. Unrelated to <i>πτύω</i> (ptúō, “spit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sputum</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>serpent's venom</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πτύω</i> (ptúō, “to spit”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fold</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to fold oneself around, wrap around</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Has been compared with the first two syllables of Sanskrit <i>प्युक्ष्नवेष्टित</i> (pyukṣnaveṣṭita), but the connection is rather tenuous. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i> May also be Pre-Greek, per Furnée; this is tentatively supported by Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a folding tablet</li><li>the pendant of an earring</li></ol><p>From <i>πτυχή</i> (ptukhḗ, “fold”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit out or up</li><li>(<i>of the sea</i>) to disgorge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(ts)ptyew-</i> (“to spit, vomit”). Akin to Latin <i>spuō</i>, Albanian <i>pështyj</i>, Old English <i>spīwan</i> (English <i>spew</i>), Old Armenian <i>թուք</i> (tʻukʻ), Old Church Slavonic <i>пльвати</i> (plĭvati), Sanskrit <i>ष्ठीवति</i> (ṣṭhīvati). Compare English <i>ptui</i> (“spitting onomatopeia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowering animal, hare</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) coward</li></ol><p>Related to and perhaps back-formed from <i>πτώσσω</i> (ptṓssō, “to cower, crouch, cringe”), with original meaning "one who ducks away, the shy one". See also the potentially-related <i>πτωχός</i> (ptōkhós, “beggar (one who crouches)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flying, flight</li></ol><p>From <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “to fly”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fall</li><li>misfortune, calamity, defeat</li><li>one who has fallen: corpse, dead body</li><li>of buildings: ruin</li></ol><p>From <i>πῑ́πτω</i> (pī́ptō, “to fall”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, resultative noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falling, a fall</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) case</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) inflection</li><li>arrangement of terms in a syllogism</li></ol><p>From <i>πῑ́πτω</i> (pī́ptō, “to fall”) +&lrm; <i>-ωσῐς</i> (-ōsĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rump; buttocks</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fat; swelling land</li></ol><li>tail</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spugeh₂</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>pūga</i>, Old High German <i>fochen</i>, and Old Church Slavonic <i>паоуга</i> (pauga), <i>пѫга</i> (pǫga).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fist</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>boxing</li></ol><li>a measure of length, the distance from the elbow to the knuckles (= 18 <i>δάκτυλοι</i> (dáktuloi), about 34 centimetres or 13<s style="display:none">+</s><small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>2</sub></small> inches)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewǵ-</i> (“to punch”). Cognates include Latin <i>pugnus</i>, Lithuanian <i>puši̇̀s</i>, and Old English <i>fyst</i> (English <i>fist</i>). Compare <i>πύξ</i> (púx) and <i>πεύκη</i> (peúkē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one <i>pygmē</i> (approximately 1 foot) long or tall</li><li>dwarfish</li></ol><p>From <i>πυγμή</i> (pugmḗ, “fist; unit of length approximately equal to 1 foot”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glowworm (<i>Lampyris noctiluca</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πῡγή</i> (pūgḗ, “rump, buttocks”) +&lrm; <i>λάμπω</i> (lámpō, “to shine, gleam”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distance from the elbow to the first joint of the fingers, used as a measure of length</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewǵ-</i> (“to punch”), the same root of <i>πυγμή</i> (pugmḗ, “fist-fight”) and Latin <i>pugnus</i> (“fist”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom of a cup or jar</li><li>the bottom of the sea</li><li>trunk, butt of a tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;putʰmḗn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰudʰmḗn</i>.</p><p>Cognate with <i>πύνδαξ</i> (púndax), Sanskrit <i>बुध्न</i> (budhná), Latin <i>fundus</i>, Old Armenian <i>անդունդ</i> (andund), Old English <i>botm</i> (English <i>bottom</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poetic form of <i>πῠκνός</i> (pŭknós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>close</li><li>fast; rapidly</li><li>often</li><li>deeply</li><li>wisely</li></ol><p>From <i>πῠκῐνός</i> (pŭkĭnós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>motherwort (<i>Leonurus cardiaca</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πῠκνός</i> (pŭknós, “close, compact, thick”) +&lrm; <i>κόμη</i> (kómē, “hair of the head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick</li><li>dense</li><li>sagacious; crafty; cunning</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;puḱ-</i> (“press together”); compare Avestan <i>𐬞𐬎𐬯𐬁</i> (pusā, “headband, diadem”), as well as Albanian <i>putë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to practice boxing, box, spar</li><li>to strike with the fist, fight</li></ol><p><i>πύκτης</i> (púktēs) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little gate, postern.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>pathology</i>) disease of the anus, probably multiple fistula.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πύλη</i> (púlē, “one wing of a pair of double gates; entrance, orifice”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πυλών</i> (pulṓn, “gateway”)</li><li>(<i>Laconian</i>) wreath, circlet</li></ol><p>Suggested to be cognate with Sanskrit <i>पुलक</i> (pulaka) and Old Irish <i>ulcha</i> for an unusual Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;pul-</i>. However, Beekes connects the word with <i>πύλιγξ</i> (púlinx, “curls”) and suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gatekeeper, warder, porter, guardian</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pylorus, the lower orifice of the stomach</li></ol><p>From <i>πύλη</i> (púlē, “door, gate”) + <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gateway</li></ol><p>From <i>πύλη</i> (púlē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to learn</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to learn (accusative) from (genitive)</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to hear or learn</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to hear about, ask about</li><li>(<i>with participle</i>) to learn that</li><li>(<i>with interrogative clause</i>) to ask</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewdʰ-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>बोधति</i> (bodhati), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽𐌰𐌱𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐌽</i> (anabiudan), and Old English <i>bēodan</i> (English <i>bede</i>, <i>bid</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tablet of boxwood for painting on</li><li>list, hence section, division</li></ol><p>From <i>πύξος</i> (púxos, “boxwood”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box made of boxwood, box</li><li>tablet made of boxwood</li><li>cylinder</li></ol><p>From <i>πῠ́ξος</i> (pŭ́xos, “boxwood”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grove of box trees</li></ol><p>From <i>πῠ́ξος</i> (pŭ́xos, “box tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn, plantation, grove).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watchfires</li><li>funeral pyre</li></ol><p>Originally the plural of <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”).</p><p>Derived from <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pair of fire tongs</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) forceps</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́γρᾱ</i> (ắgrā, “catching”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be feverish, have a fever</li></ol><p>From <i>πυρετός</i> (puretós, heat, fever) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō), from <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire, fever”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>πῠρέσσω</i> (pŭréssō)</li></ol><p>From <i>πυρετός</i> (puretós) +&lrm; <i>-ττω</i> (-ttō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πῠρᾱ́</i> (pŭrā́, “funeral pyre”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) kernel, pip, stone</li><li>hard bone of fishes</li><li>grain of frankincense</li><li>round head of a probe</li><li>name of a gem used as a votive offering</li><li>growth under the chin of an animal</li></ol><p>From <i>πῡρός</i> (pūrós, “grain of wheat”) +&lrm; <i>-ήν</i> (-ḗn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vapour bath, made by throwing scented substances on hot embers confined under a cloth</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) of all forms of external application of heat</li><li>fishing by torchlight</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) copper pyrites, a mineral which strikes fire</li><li>name of other varieties of uncertain nature</li><li>kind of wheaten loaf</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, nominal suffix).</p><p>From <i>πῡρός</i> (pūrós, “wheat”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burnt or blazing with fire, fiery</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>φλέγω</i> (phlégō, “to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown bird, perhaps a kind of pigeon</li><li>insect said to live in fire</li><li>red kind of olive</li></ol><p>Diminutive formation in "-α(λ)λίς", probably from <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) after the reddish color. However, according to Niedermann, it derives from <i>πυρός</i> (purós, “wheat”) after its nutrition.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pyramid</li><li>a type of cake</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Ancient Greek <i>πῡραμός</i> (pūramós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ς</i> (-ĭ́s), from <i>πῡρός</i> (pūrós, “wheat”) + <i>ἀμάω</i> (amáō, “to reap”), or from Egyptian <i>pr-m-ws</i> (“height of a pyramid”), from <i>pr</i> (“(one that) comes forth”) + <i>m</i> (“from”) + <i>ws</i> (“height<sup>?</sup>”).</p><p>If the "cake" sense of the word is primary and was later applied to the Egyptian monuments from their resemblance in shape, then the Greek etymology is probable; however, the shape of the cake is unknown, and the "monument" sense is attested a century earlier than the "cake" sense, so the Greek derivation is problematic.</p><p>On the other hand, the Egyptian derivation is also problematic in that there is a gap of 1100 years between the attestation of <i>pr-m-ws</i> and that of πυραμίς. It is also possible that the "monument" sense and the "cake" sense were originally etymologically distinct but came to be perceived as identical due to folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cake of roasted wheat and honey</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) meed of victory, prize</li></ol><p>From <i>πῡρός</i> (pūrós, “wheat”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, -ful), reshaped after <i>σησαμίς</i> (sēsamís, “mixture of sesame seeds”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fiery bright , fire-bright</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>αὐγής</i> (augḗs, “light”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower-guard</li></ol><p>From <i>πῠ́ργος</i> (pŭ́rgos, “tower”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heat</li><li>fever</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πυρετός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Pyretus; Prut River, nowadays flowing through Ukraine, Romania and Moldova</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) a firestick (an implement used to start a fire by friction)</li><li>an earthen pan for coals</li></ol><p><i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon, suffix forming nouns denoting instruments and means of action)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral pyre, burning pyre</li><li>conflagration, arson</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) flame of love</li><li>burnt ruins</li><li>olive tree which has been burnt down to the stump</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “to light, kindle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>πυρκᾰϊᾱ́</i> (purkăïā́, “funeral pyre”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who measures wheat</li></ol><p>From <i>πῡρός</i> (pūrós, “wheat”) +&lrm; <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “measure”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divination from fire</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>μαντεύεσθαι</i> (manteúesthai, “to foretell”) +&lrm; <i>-εία</i> (-eía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be red</li></ol><p>From <i>πυρρός</i> (purrhós) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of red-colored serpent</li><li>nickname given to red-haired slaves from Thrace</li></ol><p>From <i>πῠρρός</i> (pŭrrhós, “red, fiery”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpine chough (<i>Pyrrhocorax graculus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πυρρός</i> (purrhós, “red, fiery”) +&lrm; <i>κόραξ</i> (kórax, “crow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bullfinch (<i>Pyrrhula pyrrhula</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>πῠρρός</i> (pŭrrhós, “red, fiery”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flame-coloured, yellowish-red</li><li>red-haired, redheaded</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) tawny</li><li>red</li><li>blushing, blush</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tinge with red, make red</li></ol><p>From <i>πυρσός</i> (pursós) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>carrying fire</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch-bearer</li><li>large brazier</li></ol><p>From <i>πῠρσός</i> (pŭrsós, “torch, firebrand”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firebrand, torch</li><li>beacon, signal-fire</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) watchfires</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πυρρός</i> (purrhós)</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fiery-eyed, fiery</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) pyropus, a type of red bronze</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheat</li><li>a grain of wheat</li></ol><p>From a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;puH-ro-s</i> (“corn, wheat”), also reflected in Lithuanian <i>pū́ras</i> (“single winter corn”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;py̑rь</i> (“dog-grass”), and Old English <i>fyrs</i> (idem). Further analysis of the root is disputed:</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fire</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a funerary fire</li><li>a sacrificial fire</li></ol><li>lightning</li><li>a fever</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pāwər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péh₂wr̥</i>. The noun was changed to a pure R-stem noun; if it retained the oblique N-stem forms of its ancestor, its oblique base would have been <i>&#42;&#42;πῠν-</i> (&#42;&#42;pŭn-).</p><p>Cognates include Old English <i>fȳr</i> (English <i>fire</i>), Old Armenian <i>հուր</i> (hur), Hittite <i>𒉺𒄴𒄯</i> (pa-aḫ-ḫur /⁠paḫḫur⁠/), Tocharian A <i>por</i>, and Tocharian B <i>puwar</i>. Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀢𐀏𐀺</i> (pu-ka-wo /⁠pur-kawos⁠/, “fire-kindler”), Etruscan <i>𐌅𐌄𐌓𐌔𐌄</i> (verse).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to burn, set aflame, ignite</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to burn on a pyre</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to burn with passion</li></ol><li>to roast, grill</li><li>to suffer heart-burn</li><li>(<i>of gold</i>) to be refined in fire</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flagon, flask</li></ol><p>Likely of Pre-Greek origin, based on the interchange <i>π/β</i> observed between the word and its variant <i>βυτίνη</i> (butínē), as well as the presence of the often pre-Greek infix <i>-ῑn-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beestings; colostrum</li></ol><p>Probably originally identical with <i>πύον</i> (púon, “pus”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;puH-</i> (“rotten, foul”), but with gender and accent after <i>ὀρός</i> (orós, “whey”) and <i>τῡρός</i> (tūrós, “cheese”), which belong to the same semantic field. Both the substance and its properties (like smell and fermentation) may have caused the transference of meaning from "pus" to "milk".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>up to this time, yet (<i>as a negative polarity item</i>)</li><li>at all, ever (<i>as a negative polarity item</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷṓ</i>, mostly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷóh₁</i>, an ablative or instrumental singular form of <i>&#42;kʷós</i>, the reconstruction of which is debated. Cognate with Latin <i>quō</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) diminutive of <i>πώγων</i> (pṓgōn)</li></ol><p>From <i>πώγων</i> (pṓgōn, “a beard”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sell</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to levy taxes</li><li>to sell an office</li><li>to betray</li></ol></ol><p>Uncertain. Could be from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i>, <i>&#42;pl̥n-</i>, with potential cognates including Sanskrit <i>पण॑ते</i> (páṇate, “to barter; to negotiate”), Lithuanian <i>pel̃nas</i> (“profit, gain”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pȇlnъ</i> (“loot; spoils”), and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;falaz</i> (“for sale”). However, could also be related to <i>ἐμπολή</i> (empolḗ, “trade, purchase”), which is usually connected with <i>πέλομαι</i> (pélomai, “to turn”), itself from <i>πέλω</i> (pélō, “to be; to move”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or pertaining to foals.</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Of or pertaining to girls.</li></ol><p>From <i>πῶλος</i> (pôlos, “foal, young horse”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) miserable</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>In any way, at all, by any means</li><li>In a certain way, somehow</li></ol><p>See <i>πῶς</i> (pôs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grass, hay</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) herbs, plants</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;póiwā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;póyH-weh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;peyH-</i>. Cognate with Lithuanian <i>pieva</i> (“meadow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foot of a vase</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) trunk, cutting, plant</li></ol><p>From <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whence?, from where?</li><li>whence?, from what source?</li><li>wherefore?, why?, how?, for what reason?, how come?</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> (“which”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, ablative adverb suffix). Compare its indefinite form <i>ποθέν</i> (pothén).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where?</li><li>(<i>later</i>) whither?, to where?</li></ol><p>Like the indefinite adverb <i>ποθί</i> (pothí), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi, locative adverbial suffix). Compare Gothic <i>𐍈𐌰𐌸</i> (ƕaþ), Latin <i>cubi</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>longing, yearning, regret</li><li>love, desire</li><li>larkspur (<i>Consolida ajacis</i>, syn. <i>Delphinium ajacis</i>)</li><li>asphodel (<i>Asphodelus ramosus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰedʰ-</i> (“to request, pray, ask for”). Cognate with English <i>bid</i> (“to invite, command”), Sanskrit <i>गाध</i> (gādha, “desire, cupidity”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool</li><li>shorn wool, fleece</li></ol><p>From <i>πέκω</i> (pékō, “to comb, to card; to shear”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) into the fight, to the war</li></ol><p><i>πόλεμον</i> (pólemon) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>war, battle</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin, as suggested by πτ/π variation in its variants.</p><p>However, the word has been connected to <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō, “to poise, sway, brandish”), <i>πελεμίζω</i> (pelemízō, “to shake, cause to quiver or tremble, struggle at the bow, in order to bend it”) and perhaps <i>ψάλλω</i> (psállō, “to pluck, twitch a string with the fingers; to sing to a harp, chant praises”), all possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to beat, push, drive”) (which Beekes finds semantically unconvincing, despite not being particularly farfetched).</p><p>Compare Latin <i>pello</i> (“to push, drive, strike; to rout, conquer, defeat; to strike a chord; to touch, move”) and <i>palpo</i> (“to touch softly, stroke, pat, caress”), English <i>feel</i>, Ancient Greek <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike, smite”), Gothic <i>𐌿𐍃𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌼𐌰</i> (usfilma, “terrified, appalled”), <i>𐌿𐍃𐍆𐌹𐌻𐌼𐌴𐌹</i> (usfilmei, “fright, horror, dismay”)&nbsp;– quasi analogies to <i>ἐκπλήσσω</i> (ekplḗssō)&nbsp;–, and Russian <i>полох</i> (polox, “fear, fright”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πόλῐνδε</i> (pólĭnde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the city</li></ol><p>From <i>πόλῐν</i> (pólĭn, accusative of <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”)) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felty germander (<i>Teucrium polium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πολιός</i> (poliós, “grey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>city</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the Athenian citadel</li><li>one's city or country</li><li>(<i>ὁ ἐπὶ τῆς πόλεως</i> (ho epì tês póleōs)) city governor, praetor urbanus</li></ol><li>a whole country, as dependent on and called after its city: city state</li><li>community</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the state</li><li>the right of citizenship</li></ol><li>(<i>πόλιν παίζειν</i> (pólin paízein)) a type of board game</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ptólis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tpólHis</i>, from <i>&#42;tpelH-</i> (“fortification; city”). The early form <i>πτόλις</i> (ptólis) shows metathesis <i>tp</i> &gt; <i>pt</i> because Ancient Greek stop clusters always end in a coronal. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>पुर॑</i> (púra, “fortress, city, dwelling”) and Lithuanian <i>pilis</i> (“stronghold”). Irregular accent on genitive <i>πόλεως</i> (póleōs) is due to a quantitative metathesis from older <i>πόληος</i> (pólēos); genitive plural imitates genitive singular.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the buildings of a city, cityscape</li></ol><p><i>πόλις</i> (pólis) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πολλά</i> (pollá)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pivot, hinge, axis, pole</li><li>axis of the celestial sphere</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) pole star</li><li>celestial sphere, vault of heaven</li><li>orbit of a star</li><li>center of the circular threshing floor</li><li>pole passing through the axletree of a carriage</li><li>concave sundial, on which the shadow was cast by the gnomon</li><li>headdress worn by goddesses</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) dowel</li><li>windlass, capstan</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pólos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷól-os</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to turn”); see also the related <i>πέλομαι</i> (pélomai, “to become”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porridge made of flour</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“flour, dust”), like <i>πάλη</i> (pálē, “fine flour”), <i>πέλανος</i> (pélanos, “thick liquid substance”), Latin <i>puls</i> (“porridge”) and <i>pollen</i> (“flour, powder”). Others have connected this noun with substrate words like <i>παλάθη</i> (paláthē, “cake of conserved fruits”) and <i>πλάθανον</i> (pláthanon, “cake mold”); Latin <i>puls</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spiltaz</i> (“spelt”) may be from a related substrate source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>labor, work (especially hard work); toil</li><li>bodily exertion, exercise</li><li>work, task, business</li><li>the consequence of toil, distress, trouble, suffering</li><li>anything produced by work, a work</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)penh₁-</i> (“to weave, to twist”), with semantic shift "weaving, stretching" > "tensing, strain" > "exertion, toil". Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>πένομαι</i> (pénomai, “to exert oneself”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spinnaną</i> (“to spin”), and Lithuanian <i>pi̇̀nti</i> (“to twist”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sea</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>often combined with epithets in Homer</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;póntos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pónteh₁s</i> (“path, road”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>पन्था</i> (pánthā-, “way”), Old Armenian <i>հուն</i> (hun, “riverbed”), Latin <i>pōns</i> (“bridge”), and Old English <i>findan</i> (English <i>find</i>).</p><p>Attested in Mycenaean Greek as <i>𐀡𐀵</i> (po-to).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πόντος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Black Sea (a sea north of Anatolia)</li><li>Pontus (an ancient kingdom and cultural region covering the eastern half of the southern coast of the Black Sea, in Anatolia in modern Turkey; traditionally Greek-speaking)# (<i>historical</i>) Pontus (a former province of the Roman Empire covering the western half of the Pontus region unified with Bithynia; in full, Bithynia et Pontus)</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Pontus (a sea god)</li></ol><p>From <i>πόντος</i> (póntos, “sea”), attested in Mycenaean Greek as <i>𐀡𐀵</i> (po-to). Homeric Greek used <i>πόντος</i> (póntos) for any sea but it later began to refer specifically to the Black Sea and then to the Hellenic kingdom on its southeastern shore.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>round cake used at sacrifices</li></ol><p>From <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō, “to ripen; to cook”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sailor</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>exclamation of surprise, anger, or pain</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smacking of lips, clucking</li></ol><p>From <i>ποππύζω</i> (poppúzō, “smack the lips, cluck”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ring or hoop, passed round the joint of the spearhead and shaft</li></ol><p>Suffix like <i>γύης</i> (gúēs), but no further etymology. Confront <i>πόρκος</i> (pórkos, “fishing net”) and <i>πόρπη</i> (pórpē, “clasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of fishing net</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) transliteration of Latin <i>porcus</i></li></ol><p>Compare Old Armenian <i>որս</i> (ors).</p><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>porcus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female prostitute (<i>particularly one of low status</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pórnē</i>, from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;perh₂-</i> (“to sell”), which also gave the verb <i>πέρνημῐ</i> (pérnēmĭ, “to sell”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>male prostitute</li></ol><p>See <i>πόρνη</i> (pórnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Aeolic</i>) alternative form of <i>πάρνοψ</i> (párnops, “kind of locust”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a means of passage, passageway, way, opening</li><li>especially passage over a body of water: ford, strait, ferry, bridge</li><li>a pore on the skin</li><li>a means to an end</li><li>resource</li><li>(<i>biology</i>) fiber, filament, thread</li><li>journey</li></ol><p>From the o-grade of <i>πείρω</i> (peírō, “to pierce, to run through”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle of a shield, probably a ring or loop inside the shield, which could be taken out at pleasure</li><li>part of the headgear of a horse</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) surgical fibula</li></ol><p>From <i>πόρπη</i> (pórpē, “brooch, clasp”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πόρπαξ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Porpax, a river of Sicily flowing near Segesta</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brooch, clasp for fastening dresses, especially on the shoulders</li></ol><p>Neither a reduplicated form from <i>πείρω</i> (peírō, “to pierce”), nor a creation from <i>πόρκης</i> (pórkēs, “ring around the shaft of a spear”) makes sense. Furnée connects <i>πορφίτῳ</i> (porphítōi, “buckle”) and concludes that the word is Pre-Greek on account of the variation <i>π/φ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) from afar</li></ol><p>From <i>πόρρω</i> (pórrhō), Attic form of <i>πρόσω</i> (prósō) + <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a calf, young heifer (younger than <i>δαμάλη</i> (damálē) says Eustathius)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a young maiden</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pértis</i>, from <i>&#42;per-</i> (“to give birth”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>पृथुक</i> (pṛthuka, “boy; the young of any animal”), Old Armenian <i>որդի</i> (ordi, “child”), and Latin <i>partus</i> (“birth; offspring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(interrogative,&#32;Epic) whither?</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> (“who”) +&lrm; <i>-σε</i> (-se, “-wards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) penis</li><li>foreskin</li></ol><p>Has been connected with Ancient Greek <i>πέος</i> (péos, “penis”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pes-o-</i> (“penis”), but the derivation is problematic. Beekes thinks it is likely a loan from a non-Indo-European-Language, like Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>husband</li><li>the act of drinking</li><li>drink, beverage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pótis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pótis</i>. Cognates include Albanian <i>pata</i>, Latin <i>potis</i> and Sanskrit <i>पति</i> (pati).</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pótis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₃tis</i>, from <i>&#42;peh₃-</i> (“to drink”) (compare <i>πίνω</i> (pínō, “to drink”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interrogative adjective of quantity: how much?, how many?, how big?, how great?, how far?, how long?</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷotsos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷóti</i>, adverb from <i>&#42;kʷos</i> (“what”). Compare the indefinite form <i>ποσός</i> (posós). Cognate with Latin <i>quot</i>, Sanskrit <i>कति</i> (káti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>what number?, “how manyth”?</li></ol><p>Dissimilated from <i>&#42;ποσ(σ)ο-στός</i> (&#42;pos(s)o-stós) and accented after <i>πόσος</i> (pósos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>when? at what time?</li></ol><p>Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷókʷe</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷókʷe</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷos</i> + <i>&#42;-kʷe</i>, equivalent to <i>πο-</i> (po-) + <i>τε</i> (te). See its indefinite form <i>ποτέ</i> (poté).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>interrogative</i>) which of the two?</li><li>(<i>indefinite</i>) either of two</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷóteros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷóteros</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷos</i> (“which”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-teros</i> (contrastive suffix).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>uter</i>, Sanskrit <i>कतर</i> (katará), Old English <i>hwæþer</i> (English <i>whether</i>) and Lithuanian <i>katràs</i>. Also see <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros, “other, different”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drinkable; fit or suitable for drinking (especially of water)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) fresh, pleasant, agreeable (of tastes, words, ideas)</li><li>pleasant, sociable (of persons)</li><li>(<i>technical</i>) porous; absorbent (of materials)</li><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) watered, irrigated</li></ol><p>From <i>πότος</i> (pótos, “drinking; drink”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos, adjective suffix), from the verb <i>πῑ́νω</i> (pī́nō, “to drink”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lot (fate that falls to one by chance), destiny</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) evil destiny of death</li></ol><p>From πίπτω (píptō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mistress; lady</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pótnia</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pótnih₂</i>. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀡𐀴𐀛𐀊</i> (po-ti-ni-ja), Sanskrit <i>पत्नी</i> (patnī, “mistress, wife”), Lithuanian <i>pati</i> (“wife”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instance of drinking&#58; a draught, a drink</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>drinking-bout, carousal</li></ol></ol><p>A zero-grade nominal form from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₃-</i> (“to drink”), though the exact derivation is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bean (<i>Vicia faba</i>)</li></ol><p>Like <i>κύαμος</i> (kúamos, “bean”) from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addax (<i>Addax nasomaculatus</i>)</li><li>hen harrier (<i>Circus cyaneus</i>)</li><li>dipper (<i>Cinclus cinclus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πῡγή</i> (pūgḗ, “rump; tail”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ργός</i> (ărgós, “white; bright”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trough for feeding animals &lsqb;Homer&rsqb;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>bathtub &lsqb;5th c. BCE&rsqb;</li><li>vat, kitchen boiler &lsqb;5th c. BCE&rsqb;</li><li>coffin &lsqb;3rd c. BCE&rsqb;</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) infundibulum &lsqb;2nd c. CE&rsqb;</li></ol></ol><p>Uncertain. Multiple etymologies have been proposed:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make rot</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become rotten, to rot, to decay</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pū́tʰomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;púH-dʰh₁e-ti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thickly; strongly</li><li>wisely; carefully</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>πῠκνός</i> (pŭknós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boxer, pugilist, one who fights with their fists</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;puǵ-teh₂-s</i>, a development of <i>&#42;pewǵ-</i> (“to punch, fist-fight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wing of gates</li><li>a gate, door</li><li>an entrance, orifice</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Based on the architectural semantic domain of the term, probably a Pre-Greek loan.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πύλαι)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pylae</li></ol><p>From <i>πῠ́λη</i> (pŭ́lē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wing of gates</li><li>a gate, door</li><li>an entrance, orifice</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Based on the architectural semantic domain of the term, probably a Pre-Greek loan.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) hair about the posteriors; curls</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>πυλεών</i> (puleṓn, “wreath”); on this basis, a pre-form <i>&#42;πῠ́λος</i> (&#42;pŭ́los) has been assumed, which is then connected with Sanskrit <i>पुलक</i> (pulaka, “erection or bristling of the hairs of the body”), <i>पु॒ल॒स्ति</i> (pulastí, “wearing the hair straight”) and <i>पुल</i> (pula, “horripilation”). From Iranian, Kurdish is adduced, and from Celtic Irish <i>ulcha</i> (“beard”). However, Mayrhofer doubts this connections; in fact the suffix <i>-ιγγ-</i> may rather point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the last, for the last time</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hindmost, last</li><li>outermost</li><li>nethermost</li><li>last</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Traditionally derived from a variant of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂epo</i> (“off, away”), and often compared with Sanskrit <i>पुनर्</i> (púnar, “back, again”), but Beekes is skeptical.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hindmost, last</li><li>outermost</li><li>nethermost</li><li>last</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Traditionally derived from a variant of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂epo</i> (“off, away”), and often compared with Sanskrit <i>पुनर्</i> (púnar, “back, again”), but Beekes is skeptical.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom of a jar, cup or other vessel</li><li>lid of a vessel</li><li>hilt of a sword</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰudʰmḗn</i>, the same root of <i>πυθμήν</i> (puthmḗn, “bottom”) and Latin <i>fundus</i> (“bottom”). However the medial "-νδ-" is highly problematic, as well as the initial "π-" instead of "φ-" (since it was no longer followed by "θ").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with the fist</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewǵ-</i> (“to punch”); related to <i>πυγμή</i> (pugmḗ, “fist”) and cognate with Latin <i>pugnus</i> (“fist”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of boxwood</li><li>yellow as the boxwood</li></ol><p>From <i>πύξος</i> (púxos, “boxwood”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>box tree (<i>Buxus sempervirens</i>)</li><li>boxwood</li></ol><p>Probably a substrate loanword from Italy, related to Latin <i>buxus</i> (“box tree”). Beekes is unconvinced by Scardigli, who looks for the origin of the term in Asia Minor, and Carnoy, who connects it to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewgʰ-</i> (“to bend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pus, discharge from a sore</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;puH-</i> (“rotten, foul”), the same root of <i>πύθομαι</i> (púthomai, “to putrify, decay”) and <i>πῡός</i> (pūós, “beestings, colostrum”). Other Indo-European cognates are Latin <i>pūs</i> (“pus”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fūlaz</i> (“foul”), Sanskrit <i>पुवस्</i> (puvas, “purulent matter”) and Old Armenian <i>փուտ</i> (pʻut, “rottenness, putrefaction”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pan of coals</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>αὔω</i> (aúō, “to light a fire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower; watchtower</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) towered wall</li><li>the part of the house where women live</li><li>castle; fortress; bulwark</li><li>(<i>military</i>) division; column</li></ol><p>First attested in Homer, <i>Iliad</i> 7.219. There are multiple theories:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small wood for burning</li><li>kitchen or stove for cooking</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”), with a formation similar to the synonymous word <i>φρύγανον</i> (phrúganon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pellitory of Spain (<i>Anacyclus pyrethrum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”), because of its warming effect.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to fire, fiery</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) bitter, obstinate</li><li>of or pertaining to wheat, wheaten</li></ol><p>From <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p><p>From <i>πυρός</i> (purós, “wheat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheaten bread, especially the one with bran on it</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) food, meat</li></ol><p>From <i>πύρνος</i> (púrnos, “morsel; one-seeded cereal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morsel, bit</li><li>kind of one-seeded cereal</li></ol><p>The word is obsolete and without etymology. It was compared with <i>πορύναν</i> (porúnan, “kneaded mass, cake”) and <i>τορύνη</i> (torúnē, “kind of grain”) by Fick, adducing Sanskrit <i>चर्वति</i> (carvati, “to eat, chew”) and <i>चूर्ण</i> (cūrṇa, “dust, powder”) as well. This would lead to the reconstruction of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷeru-</i>, which requires a pre-form <i>&#42;τερύνα</i> (&#42;terúna) for <i>τορύνη</i> (torúnē). However, this leaves the <i>υ</i>-vowel in <i>πύρνος</i> unexplained. According to Szemerényi, this word is syncopated from <i>πύρινος</i> (púrinos, “made of wheat”), but this is contestable for several reasons. Furnée connects the word with Basque <i>apur</i> (“crumb”); this etymology seems unreliable, but in any case, the word may well be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning</li><li>fiery trial</li></ol><p>From <i>πυρόω</i> (puróō, “to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muzzle put on calves' noses to prevent their sucking</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inquiry, question</li><li>What is learned by asking: news, report, information</li></ol><p>From zero-grade of the root of <i>πυνθάνομαι</i> (punthánomai, “to inquire, find out”) +&lrm; <i>-τις</i> (-tis, verbal noun suffix). Compare <i>πεῦσις</i> (peûsis), from <i>e</i>-grade of the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beard</li></ol><p>Probably Pre-Greek, but see Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂ǵ-</i> (“to attach, fix”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a seller, a dealer, a monger</li></ol><p><i>πωλέω</i> (pōléō, “I sell or offer for sale”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, suffix forming masculine agent nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Selling, sale</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic and Doric form of <i>πίνω</i> (pínō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₃-</i> (“to drink”). According to Beekes, the form may have been created on the model of the perfect <i>πέπωκα</i> (pépōka), itself perhaps from <i>&#42;pe-póh₃-e</i>, whence also perhaps Sanskrit <i>प॒पौ</i> (papáu). However, Beekes suggests that it is alternatively possible that the perfect form was instead an inner-Greek development created on the model of the verb <i>πώνω</i> (pṓnō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>πώποτε</i> (pṓpote)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;always with a negative) never yet</li><li>(<i>later also without a negative</i>) ever (at any time)</li></ol><p>From <i>πω</i> (pō) +&lrm; <i>ποτέ</i> (poté).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of tuff</li></ol><p>From <i>πῶρος</i> (pôros, “tuff”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>In any way, at all, by any means</li><li>In a certain way, somehow</li></ol><p>See <i>πῶς</i> (pôs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a pagus, district</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>pāgus</i> (“district”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) all, every, each</li><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) whole</li></ol><p>First attested in Mycenean Greek as <i>𐀞𐀯</i> (pa-si). From an earlier <i>&#42;παντ-ς</i> (&#42;pant-s), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ph₂-ent-</i> (“all”) (whence Tocharian B <i>po</i> (“all, every”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>πῆχῠς</i> (pêkhŭs, “forearm”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>somehow</li><li>(to) somewhere</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i>. Compare Latin <i>quā</i>. See also its interrogative form <i>πῇ</i> (pēî).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything fastened or joined together, a framework</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of a ship</li><li>of a roof</li><li>a stage or scaffold used in theatres</li><li>a bookcase</li><li>metaphorically&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>a bond in honour bound</li><li>fabric</li></ol></ol><li>anything congealed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of frozen snow</li><li>of fat</li><li>of the waters of the Jordan</li></ol><li>that which makes to curdle, as rennet does milk</li></ol><p><i>πήγ(νῡμῐ)</i> (pḗg(nūmĭ)) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) misery</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;πῆμα</i> (&#42;pêma), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₁-</i> (“to hurt”) (compare Latin <i>patior</i> (“to suffer”), Sanskrit <i>पीय॑ति</i> (pī́yati, “to blame”), Gothic <i>𐍆𐌹𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (fijan, “to hate”)) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>when&#63;</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷéh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>web</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂n-</i> (“fabric”). Cognate with Latin <i>pannus</i> (“cloth”), Gothic <i>𐍆𐌰𐌽𐌰</i> (fana, “piece of cloth”), Old English <i>fana</i> (“flag”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fixing, putting together</li><li>solidity, fixity</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>coagulation, freezing; formation of gum in plants</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “I stick, I fix”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, suffix to form a feminine noun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forearm, from the wrist to the elbow</li><li>the centrepiece, which joined the two horns of an ancient bow</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the horns or sides of the lyre</li><li>(<i>in the balance</i>) the beam</li><li>(<i>as a measure of length</i>) cubit, the distance from the point of the elbow to that of the little finger</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a cubit-rule, foot-rule</li></ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the cubits, small children one cubit in height represented in pictures as playing round the Nile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pā́kʰus</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂ǵʰús</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>बाहु</i> (bāhu, “arm”), Old Persian <i>𐎲𐎠𐏀𐎢</i> (b-a-z-u /⁠bāzu⁠/) (Persian بازو (bâzu)), and Old English <i>bōg</i> (English <i>bough</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>whereby? which way? how?</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i>. Compare Latin <i>quā</i>. See also its indefinite form <i>πῃ</i> (pēi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>πεῖ</i> (peî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring, fountain</li></ol><p>For this word and <i>πῑδάω</i> (pīdáō, “to gush forth”) one might assume a noun <i>&#42;πίδ-α</i> (&#42;píd-a), but <i>πῑδύω</i> (pīdúō, “to gush forth”) and <i>πῑδυλίς</i> (pīdulís) point to an <i>υ-</i>stem. No certain cognates outside Greek. The group could well be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool or hair wrought into felt</li><li>anything made of felt, especially a felt skullcap</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a felt shoe</li><li>felt cloth</li></ol><li>a cottony ball formed on some trees</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a ball, globe</li></ol><li>translation for Latin <i>pilus</i>, as in <i>primus pilus</i></li></ol><p>From the same Proto-Indo-European source as Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;pьlstь</i> (“felt”), Old English <i>felt</i> (English <i>felt</i>) and probably Latin <i>pilleus</i>. A connection with Latin <i>pilus</i> (“a hair”) and <i>pila</i> (“a ball”) has been abandoned.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) meadow</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>No certain etymology. Traditionally analyzed as </i>*πῖδ-σος<i>, related to <i>πῖδαξ</i> (pîdax, “spring, fountain”); this is rejected by Furnée, who takes the alternation </i>δ/σ<i> to point to Pre-Greek origin.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fire</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a funerary fire</li><li>a sacrificial fire</li></ol><li>lightning</li><li>a fever</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pāwər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péh₂wr̥</i>. The noun was changed to a pure R-stem noun; if it retained the oblique N-stem forms of its ancestor, its oblique base would have been <i>&#42;&#42;πῠν-</i> (&#42;&#42;pŭn-).</p><p>Cognates include Old English <i>fȳr</i> (English <i>fire</i>), Old Armenian <i>հուր</i> (hur), Hittite <i>𒉺𒄴𒄯</i> (pa-aḫ-ḫur /⁠paḫḫur⁠/), Tocharian A <i>por</i>, and Tocharian B <i>puwar</i>. Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀢𐀏𐀺</i> (pu-ka-wo /⁠pur-kawos⁠/, “fire-kindler”), Etruscan <i>𐌅𐌄𐌓𐌔𐌄</i> (verse).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foal, young horse, whether colt or filly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a young animal, especially domestic</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) a young girl, maiden</li></ol><li>a Corinthian coin, from the figure of Pegasus on it</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pōlH-o-s</i>, from <i>&#42;pelH-</i> (“young animal”). Compare Latin <i>pullus</i>. Also see Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;fulô</i> (“foal”) (whence Old English <i>fola</i> &gt; English <i>foal</i>), Old Armenian <i>ուլ</i> (ul, “kid, fawn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lid; cover</li><li>drink; draught</li><li>drinking-cup</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;poh₂mn̥</i>, from <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“to protect”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>पात्र</i> (pātra), Gothic <i>𐍆𐍉𐌳𐍂</i> (fōdr), German <i>Futter</i>.</p><p>From the root of <i>πίνω</i> (pínō, “I drink”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă); from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;peh₃-</i> (“to drink”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuff</li><li>kind of marble used in building</li><li>stalactite in caverns</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) stone in the bladder</li></ol><p>It has no certain etymology. According to Haupt, it was borrowed from Akkadian; Furnée connects <i>&#42;ψώρος</i> (&#42;psṓros) in <i>ψωρίτης</i> (psōrítēs, “kind of marble”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin. Likely a Wanderwort also found in Akkadian <i>𒉌𒌓𒈾𒁓</i> (/⁠pīlu, pūlu⁠/, “limestone”), Akkadian <i>𒁓𒊏</i> (pūru, literally “stone bowl”), Urartian <i>𒉌𒌓𒁍𒇻𒋛</i> (<sup>NA₄</sup>pu-lu-si /⁠pulus⁠/, “stone, stele”), Old Armenian <i>բուռ</i> (buṙ, “lime, plaster, varnish”). An ancient Anatolian or Mesopotamian origin is probable, with Sumerian <i>𒁓</i> (/⁠bur⁠/, “stone (vessel)”) as the likely source.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Πῶρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Porus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how?, in what way?</li></ol><p>A frozen ablative form from <i>πο-</i> (po-) (from the base of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverb ending). See also <i>πως</i> (pōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flock of sheep</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;poh₂yu</i>, from <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“to protect”), whence also <i>ποιμήν</i> (poimḗn, “shepherd; herdsmen”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>पा॒यु</i> (pāyú, “guard; protector”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>σε</i> (se)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linen cloth or towel</li></ol><p>Probably related to or borrowed from Egyptian <i>sbn</i> (“(linen) bandage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) Sabbath</li><li>a week (seven days)</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>שַׁבָּת</i> (šabbāṯ, “Sabbath”), or, alternatively, from Aramaic <i>שַׁבְּתָא</i> (šabbəṯā, “Sabbath”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one-edged battle axe used by Scythians</li></ol><p>A foreign word without etymology. It has been compared with Latin <i>sagitta</i> (“arrow”) and Proto-Berber <i>&#42;zaġāya</i> (“spear, assegai”) and Psalm 35, 3 Hebrew <i>סְגֹר</i> (səḡōr, translated as a weapon since Parḥōn, as lance by Luther, javelin by the Berean Study Bible, New International Version and New Living Translation, and battle-axe in the New American Standard Bible), but one would rather of course expect Iranian origin, and it is probably a cognate to Latin <i>secūris</i> (“broad axe”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sekyra</i> (“broad axe”), for which compare Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sek-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of boat or canoe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A covering, a clothing; the covering of a shield</li><li>A packsaddle</li></ol><p>From <i>σάττω</i> (sáttō, “to cram, to stuff”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma). Possibly ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twenk-</i> (“to press, squeeze”) (Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwangiz</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) cloak</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) coat, habit (<i>garment</i>)</li></ol><p>Presumably borrowed from Latin <i>sagus</i> (“coarse woolen coat, soldier's coat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>γυργαθίον</i> (gurgathíon, “net”).</li></ol><p>Likely from Pre-Greek due to the connection with <i>σαγήνη</i> (sagḗnē, “seine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>σάκεα</i> (sákea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of sackcloth</li></ol><p>From <i>σάκκος</i> (sákkos, “sack”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coarse cloth of hair, especially of goat's hair</li><li>anything made of coarse cloth:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sack, bag</li><li>sieve, strainer</li><li>coarse garment, sackcloth, worn as mourning by the Jews</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) sackcloth vestment, penitential garb</li></ol><li>coarse beard</li></ol><p>Most likely borrowed from Semitic, possibly from Phoenician. Compare Hebrew <i>שַׂק</i>, Imperial Aramaic <i>𐡔𐡒</i> (šq), Talmudic Aramaic <i>סַקָּא</i>, Classical Syriac <i>ܣܩܐ</i>, Ge'ez <i>ሠቅ</i> (śäḳ), Akkadian <i>𒆭𒊓</i> (/⁠šaqqu⁠/), Egyptian <i>sꜣgꜣ</i>. The word is a widely-borrowed Mediterranean Kulturwort.</p><p>Černý and Forbes suggest the word was originally Egyptian, a nominal derivative of <i>sꜣq</i> (“to gather or put together”) that also yielded Coptic <i>ⲥⲟⲕ</i> (sok, “sackcloth”) and was borrowed into Greek perhaps by way of a Semitic intermediary. However, Vycichl and Hoch reject this idea, noting that such an originally Egyptian word would be expected to yield Hebrew <i>&#42;סַק</i> rather than <i>שַׂק</i>. Instead, they posit that the Coptic and Greek words are both borrowed from Semitic, with the Coptic word perhaps developing via Egyptian <i>sꜣgꜣ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shield</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) shield, defense</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twek-</i> (“shield, skin; to cover”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>त्वच्</i> (tvác, “hide, bag”), Hittite (tuekkaš, “body”).</p><p>Alternatively, Beekes suggests a Semitic origin, from the same root as <i>σάκκος</i> (sákkos, “sack”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imperial epistle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sacra</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sack</li></ol><p>From <i>σάττω</i> (sáttō, “to pack, stuff”). Not related to <i>σάκκος</i> (sákkos, “sack; sackcloth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sugar</li><li>sugar cane (<i>Saccharum officinarum</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σάκχαρ</i> (sákkhar, “sugar”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σάκχαρ</i> (sákkhar)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miner's sieve or riddle</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from <i>σάλος</i> (sálos, “earthquake; movement of the sea”) with a velar suffix. Kölligan (2017) compares Hesychian <i>σαίνω</i> (saínō) and the root of <i>κινέω</i> (kinéō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱey(h₂)-</i> (compare Latin <i>cieō</i>; IEW 538, LIV², De Vaan), thus reconstructing <i>&#42;ḱi-n̥-h₂-</i>, <i>&#42;ḱih₂-(e)lo-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rolling or tossing motion</li><li>the movement of the sea</li><li>earthquake</li><li>a ship rolling in the sea</li><li>distemper; restlessness; perplexity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tuh₂-(e)l-o- ~ &#42;tweh₂-(e)l-o-</i>, from <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to swell; to crowd; to be strong”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Salema porgy (<i>Sarpa salpa</i>)</li></ol><p>From Egyptian, found in Coptic <i>ϫⲉⲗϥⲁⲧ</i> (jelfat), <i>ⲥⲗⲃⲟⲧ</i> (slbot), Arabic شِلْبَا (šilbā), or perhaps already Pre-Greek and of shared origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) a war-trumpet</li><li>synonym of <i>σᾰ́λπῐσμᾰ</i> (sắlpĭsmă)</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) the name of a bird</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) a kind of comet</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>σῦρῐγξ</i> (sûrĭnx) and <i>φόρμῐγξ</i> (phórmĭnx). As a word of Mediterranean culture, it remains without Indo-European connection. According to Beekes, the suffix is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush-mat, used as a bed in war</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>οἶσαξ</i> (oîsax), but further unexplained. Belardi rightly rejects a connection with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sam-</i> (“water,sea, swamp”). The suffix rather shows Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>σάνδαλον</i> (sándalon, “sandal”)</li></ol><p>See <i>σάνδαλον</i> (sándalon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marjoram (<i>Origanum majorana</i>)</li></ol><p>Foreign word of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A sandal</li><li>A flat fish similar to <i>ψῆττα</i> (psêtta)</li></ol><p>Likely from Pre-Greek or some other foreign source, in view of the alternation "νδ/μβ". Compare also Middle Persian (cndl /⁠čandal⁠/, “sandalwood”), from Sanskrit <i>चन्दन</i> (candana, “sandalwood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Indian sandalwood, <i>Santalum album</i></li></ol><p>From Sanskrit <i>चन्दन</i> (candana), probably via a Middle Indo-Aryan form such as Gandhari <i>𐨕𐨎𐨡𐨣𐨎</i> (caṃdanaṃ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bright red mineral colorant from red lead</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) flesh coloured women's garment dyed with this colorant</li><li>kind of salve</li><li>kind of casket, box</li></ol><p>Akin to Arabic سُنْدُس (sundus), Old Armenian <i>սնդուս</i> (sndus), Iranian loanwords related to Middle Persian (sndws, “a silk (or brocade) garment”), Sogdian (sndws /⁠sandus⁠/, “a silk wear”), Sanskrit <i>सिन्दूर</i> (sindūra, “red lead”), maybe also with <i>σανδαράκη</i> (sandarákē). Likely spread through an Anatolian form; Liddell suggesting that they all ultimate originate in Akkadian <i>𒉌𒌓𒍝𒄢</i> (NA<sub>4</sub>.GUG /⁠sāmtu, sāntu, sāndu⁠/, “red stone, carnelian, cinnabar”).</p><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, possibly related to <i>σενδούκη</i> (sendoúkē) of which he makes a similar claim. Potential connection to <i>σῠνθήκη</i> (sŭnthḗkē), possibly even a re-borrowing from an eastern source like Aramaic <i>צנדוקא</i> (ṣndūḳā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>zany, fool, buffoon</li></ol><p>According to Frisk and DELG, from the verb <i>σαίνω</i> (saínō, “to wag; flatter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandalwood (<i>Santalum album</i>)</li></ol><p>From Arabic صَنْدَل (ṣandal), from Middle Persian (cndl /⁠čandal⁠/, “sandalwood”), from Sanskrit <i>चन्दन</i> (candana, “sandalwood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: Paphian word for <i>θυσία</i> (thusía, “burnt offering, sacrifice”).</li></ol><p>According to Hoffmann, of Semitic origin; however, he does not explain any further. This word can only be connected to the group of Hebrew <i>זֶבַח</i> (zéḇaḥ, “sacrifice”), Arabic ذِبْح (ḏibḥ, “sacrificial victim”) and Ugaritic <i>𐎄𐎁𐎈</i> (dbḥ, “sacrifice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sapphire</li></ol><p>From a West Semitic language such as Hebrew <i>סַפִּיר</i> (sappîr); originally from Assyrian Akkadian <i>šipirtu</i> (“lapis lazuli”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) soap</li></ol><p>Usually said to be from Latin <i>sāpō</i>, but borrowing from Galatian is also possible. Ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;saipǭ</i> (“soap”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyp-</i>, <i>&#42;seyb-</i> (“to pour, strain, trickle”). Cognate with Old English <i>sāpe</i> (“soap”). More at soap.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pudendum muliebre</li></ol><p>Unknown. Related to <i>σάρων</i> (sárōn, “lustful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the place in a chariot where the whip was kept</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) sardine; carnelian (used also as a seal)</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>Σᾰ́ρδεις</i> (Sắrdeis, “Sardis”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix), because it was its place of origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sarissa, a long Macedonian pike</li></ol><p>The etymology is still unexplained. Perhaps from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Made of flesh, fleshy</li><li>Limited to the nature of humans: mortal, sinful</li></ol><p><i>σάρξ</i> (sárx) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the material which covers the bones of a creature; flesh</li><li>body</li><li>the edible flesh of a fruit</li><li>the seat of animalistic, immoral desires and thoughts, such as lust</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the physical or natural order (<i>as opposed to the spiritual</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;súrks</i>, <i>&#42;swə́rks</i>, originally denoting "a piece of meat", from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;turḱ-s</i>, a zero-grade s-stem nominal from the root <i>&#42;twerḱ-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broom, besom</li><li>sweepings, refuse, rubbish</li></ol><p>From <i>σαίρω</i> (saírō, “to sweep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Babylon</i>) cycle of 3600 years</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) cycle of 222 months</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒊹</i> (šar<sub>2</sub> /⁠šār⁠/, “3600, total, many, a great quantity or measure”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σάρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Sarus; Seyhan River, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seah, a former Hebrew measure equivalent to 1/30 of a <i>κόρος</i> (kóros)</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>סְאִים֙</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Old Persian</i>) gold</li><li>(<i>Old Persian</i>) realm, kingdom</li><li>(<i>Old Persian</i>) here, in this place</li></ol></ol><p>The existence and meaning of this word is disputed. It was interpreted by some early scholars as a phonetic rendering of an Old Persian word, possibly <i>&#42;xšaθram</i> (“realm, kingdom”). Only attested in a line spoken in (mock-)Persian in Aristophanes' Acharnians.</p><p>If Wili (2004) is correct in reading the form instead as *ατρα, then it may render an Old Median reflex to Avestan <i>𐬀𐬚𐬭𐬀</i> (aθra, “there, to that place”), from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;átra</i> (“there, to that place”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: kind of woman's headdress.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stuff quite full, cram, pack</li><li>to press close, compress</li><li>to equip with a store</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to load</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twenk-</i> (“to squeeze, press together; pressure”), the same root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þwangiz</i> (“belt, strap; clamp”) and Lithuanian <i>tveñkti</i> (“to dam up”). The connection with Tocharian A <i>twāṅk</i> is, however, difficult, both because of the difference in the preforms needed and because of the uncertain meaning of the poorly-attested Tocharian verb (traditionally translated as "to force in").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satyr</li><li>lewd person</li><li>a type of play with satyrs in the chorus</li></ol><p>Unknown; possibly loaned from a Pre-Greek substrate. Possibly closely related to Illyrian <i>&#42;Deuadai</i>, interpreted as a diminutive of the Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deywós</i>, or alternatively from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰwes-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, obviously, assuredly</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, from <i>σᾰ́φ-</i> (sắph-), the root of <i>σαφής</i> (saphḗs, “clear”), + <i>-ᾰ</i> (-ă, adverbial suffix) (though Beekes prefers to take this term as the original, and its relatives as derivatives).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reverential awe</li><li>object of wonder</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai, “respect”), which is from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;tyegʷ-</i> (“to avoid, retreat from”), possibly going back directly to a neuter noun <i>&#42;tyégʷos</i> whence also cognates Sanskrit <i>त्यजस्</i> (tyajas) and Avestan <i>𐬌𐬚𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬘𐬀𐬵</i> (<sup><small>i</small></sup>θiiejah, “abandonment; difficulty, danger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reverence</li></ol><p><i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be moved by awe, fear, or respect for others or for their opinions; to feel shame; to experience the same feelings in a religious sense</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tségʷomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tyegʷ-</i> (“avoid, yield to”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>त्यक्त</i> (tyaktá, “forsaken, abandoned”) and <i>त्यजति</i> (tyajati, “to forsake, abandon”) and Avestan <i>𐬌𐬚𐬀𐬌𐬌𐬀𐬘𐬀𐬵-</i> (iθaiiajah-), <i>𐬌𐬚𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬘𐬀𐬵-</i> (<sup><small>i</small></sup>θiiejah-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>post-Homeric</i>) to honour with pious awe, to worship, venerate</li></ol><p>Late formation as the active equivalent of originally deponent <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light, brightness, the bright flame or blaze of fire</li><li>shine</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from Pre-Greek with other derivative <i>σελήνη</i> (selḗnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cartilaginous fish, shark</li></ol><p>The formation resembles <i>στέλεχος</i> (stélekhos), <i>τᾰ́ρῑχος</i> (tắrīkhos) and <i>τέμᾰχος</i> (témăkhos). Connected with <i>σέλᾰς</i> (sélăs, “light, glow, beam”) already by Galen, because of the phosphorescent light of certain cartilaginous fishes; yet the infix <i>-ᾰχ-</i> (-ăkh-) may point to a Pre-Greek origin. The old connection with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;selhaz</i> (“seal”) is untenable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celery</li></ol><p>Borrowed from some non-Indo-European source, perhaps Pre-Greek. Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀮𐀪𐀜</i> (se-ri-no).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>shipping</i>) deck, the upper planking of a ship</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) rowing-benches (<i>in Homer</i>, cf <i>ζυγά</i> (zugá))</li></ol><li>(<i>generally</i>) seat, throne</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) any timberwork; logs of building timber</li></ol><p>Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>σελίς</i> (selís, “beam, planing; page of book”), with Old English <i>selma</i> and <i>syll</i>, with Old High German <i>swelli</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κοχλίας</i> (kokhlías, “snail with a spiral shell”).</li></ol><p>Unknown, but perhaps from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>σέσιλος</i> (sésilos, “land slug”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endive or chicory</li></ol><p>Beekes gives no etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnat or winged ant</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin. Compare also <i>σέριφος</i> (sériphos, “wormwood; old woman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hartwort (<i>Tordylium officinale</i>)</li><li>other kinds of hartwort</li></ol><p>According to Pedanius Dioscorides, from Egyptian</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>land snail</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Probably related to <i>σέμελος</i> (sémelos, “snail”) and perhaps both of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) fish</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested in Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>πανοῦργος</i> (panoûrgos, “knavish, villainous”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, in view of the reduplication, and probably related to <i>Σίσυφος</i> (Sísuphos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sieve, sift, bolt, strain</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kyéh₂-dʰe-ti</i>, dʰe-present formation of <i>&#42;kyeh₂-</i> (“to sieve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weight in the balance, counterpoise, counterweight</li><li>makeweight</li><li>standard measure of wine</li><li>return, recompense</li></ol><p>From <i>σηκόω</i> (sēkóō, “to weigh, to balance”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix), from <i>σηκός</i> (sēkós, “enclosure, fence”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>σήματα</i> (sḗmata)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>σήματε</i> (sḗmate)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>σήματι</i> (sḗmati)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>today</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ťāmeron</i>, for earlier <i>&#42;ki-hāmeron</i>, with the first component from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱe</i> (“this, here”). By surface analysis, a univerbation of <i>κί-</i> (kí-) +&lrm; <i>ἡμέρα</i> (hēméra, “day”). See also Latin <i>cis</i>, <i>hic</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuttlefish bone</li><li>pounce, kind of fine powder</li></ol><p>From <i>σηπία</i> (sēpía, “cuttlefish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to make rotten or putrid</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to corrupt, waste</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to rot, moulder</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of tissue</i>) to die</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σήψ</i> (sḗps, “putrefying sore”). The alternation between η and α has suggested PIE origin (possible roots: Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tweh₂p-</i>, <i>&#42;tyeh₂p-</i> or <i>&#42;Kyeh₂p-</i> (last option chosen by LIV)), though a zero grade -ih₂- would not actually yield Proto-Greek -ya-. Beekes rejects relation to Lithuanian <i>siupti</i> (“to putrefy”) or Sanskrit <i>क्याकु</i> (kyāku, “fungus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silkworm</li><li>silk</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but possibly ultimately from Old Chinese <i>絲</i> (*sə, “silk”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σήρ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(historical,&#32;rare&#32;in the singular) Serican, one of the Seres, the people from whom silk was obtained, the North Chinese as known to the ancient Greeks and Romans.</li></ol><p>Related to <i>σήρ</i> (sḗr, “silkworm”) but otherwise uncertain. Possibly ultimately from Old Chinese <i>絲</i> (*sə, “silk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>εἶδος κανθάρου</i> (eîdos kanthárou, literally “kind of beetle”).</li></ol><p>Strömberg considered this word to be Laconian for <i>θήραφος</i> (thḗraphos, “spider”). Furnée compares the word to <i>σίραμφος</i> (síramphos, “snout”), assuming that this word denotes a "snout beetle". This is semantically unlikely, but in view of the connection with <i>θήραφος</i> (thḗraphos), the word could be of Pre-Greek origin. Moreover, the suffix <i>-αμβ-</i> is typical of other substrate words, like <i>κεράμβυξ</i> (kerámbux, “longhorn beetle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moth</li></ol><p>A Semitic loanword, found as Aramaic <i>סָסָא</i> / <i>ܣܳܣܳܐ</i> (sāsā, “moth”), Hebrew <i>סָס</i> (sās, “moth”), Akkadian <i>𒌨𒈨</i> (sāsum, “moth”), Arabic سُوس (sūs, “moth-worm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sesame</li></ol><p>From Old Aramaic <i>שושמא</i> (šūššmā), shortening of <i>שומשומא</i> (šumššemā), from Akkadian <i>𒊭𒈦𒌑𒈬</i> (šamaššammū, “oil plant”), compound of <i>𒉌𒄑</i> (šaman, “oil”) and <i>𒌑</i> (šammum, “plant”).</p><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀭𐀭𐀔</i> (sa-sa-ma), which could place the borrowing at Proto-Hellenic age. The Greek neuter might have been back-formed from the collective.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spittle; saliva</li><li>synovial fluid</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Beekes, probably an onomatopoeic word. A relation to Sanskrit <i>क्षीवति</i> (kṣī́vati), <i>क्षीबति</i> (kṣī́bati, “to spit”) (root <i>क्षीव्</i> (kṣīv), <i>क्षीब्</i> (kṣīb)) is formally difficult. Compare Latin <i>salīva</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porker, fat pig</li><li>fat, grease</li></ol><p>The word has been connected with Old English <i>þwīnan</i> (“to become weak”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tyti</i> (“to become fat”), but these proposals seem semantically and phonetically unconvincing, respectively. The traditional association with <i>σίαλον</i> (síalon, “spittle, slobber”), while semantically appealing, becomes somewhat dubious when considering that the "grease" sense may have stemmed from the "fat pig" sense via ellipsis, rather than being an original sense. Thus, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shekel, an ancient unit of weight</li><li>shekel, a currency used in ancient Israel</li><li>earring</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>שֶׁקֶל</i> (shékel, “shekel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σῖγμᾰ</i> (sîgmă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>signum, statue</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) place where the standards were set up in a camp or kept in administrative headquarters</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;figurative) arrest, prison</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sīgnum</i> (“sign”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἑτεροκλινές</i> (heteroklinés, “leaning to one side, uneven”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>σῐ́δηρος</i> (sĭ́dēros, “iron”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomegranate (tree and fruit)</li><li>kind of waterplant growing near Orchomenus, water lily</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, all the variations prove a Pre-Greek origin, after Witczak specifically borrowed from Western Anatolian. Also passed as far as Albanian <i>shegë</i>, to which the form <i>κυσήγη</i> (kusḗgē) once mentioned without contextualization is in any case ancestral. Linked to the reconstruction *<i>sida</i> "red", which has also been suggested as the root of σίδηρος (sídēros), "iron."</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σίδη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Side (an ancient city, archaeological site, and modern town in modern Antalya Province, Turkey, on a small peninsula on the Mediterranean coast of Anatolia, settled by Greeks from Cyme)</li></ol><p>The name may be from Anatolian, but it is also formally equivalent to <i>σίδη</i> (sídē, “pomegranate”). Both the city and fruit could be from this Anatolian language and of the same meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron</li><li>iron tool; iron implement</li><li>sword</li><li>sickle</li><li>blacksmith's shop; smithy</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a symbol of&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hardness</li><li>firmness</li><li>relentlessness</li></ol></ol><p>Unclear and debated:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomegranate-peel</li></ol><p>From <i>σῑ́δη</i> (sī́dē, “pomegranate”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>εὔζωνον</i> (eúzōnon, “well-girdled”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, due to the presence of the cluster "-μν-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σῐ́ᾰλον</i> (sĭ́ălon, “spittle, saliva”)</li></ol><p>See <i>σῐ́ᾰλον</i> (sĭ́ălon, “saliva”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hiss, especially of the noise made by plunging hot metal into cold water, to which is compared the hissing of the Cyclops' eye when the burnt stake was thrust into it</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A fermented liquor, a strong drink</li></ol><p>From the Septuagint rendition of Hebrew <i>שֵׁכָר</i> (šēḵār), used to refer to strong drinks, from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;šikar-</i> (“alcoholic drink”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cucumber (<i>Cucumis sativus</i>)</li></ol><p>From the same substrate/Pre-Greek root of <i>σῐκῠ́α</i> (sĭkŭ́a, “bottle-gourd”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squirting cucumber; <i>Ecballium elaterium</i></li></ol><p>Literally, “wild cucumber”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: cake made of barley, sesame and poppyseeds.</li></ol><p>Neumann cautiously compared the word with Hittite (šiluḫa, “kind of cake”), which is not possible phonologically; thus he later identified the immediate source in Luwian (šiluwa, “kind of cake”). If cognate, it could be a loanword from Anatolian or from Pre-Greek. Yet the <i>-β-</i> is difficult to reconcile with the Hittite form. Since it is a gloss from Hesychius, we cannot be sure whether we are dealing with a loanword or with a transcription of a local word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milk thistle (<i>Silybum marianum</i>)</li></ol><p>The origin is unclear. The formation resembles <i>σκόλυμος</i> (skólumos), <i>ὄροβος</i> (órobos), <i>κόρυμβος</i> (kórumbos) and <i>ὄχθοιβος</i> (ókhthoibos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wels catfish (<i>Silurus glanis</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”) and an unclear first element, connected by Solmsen to <i>σίλλος</i> (síllos, “squint-eyed”) and <i>Σῑληνός</i> (Sīlēnós, “Silenus”). However, the word is rather formed with the Pre-Greek suffix "-ουρος".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockroach (<i>Blattella germanica</i>)</li></ol><p>The variant could be an artificial Atticism or show a Pre-Greek alternation σ-/τ-. The form semantically and formally resembles <i>σέρφος</i> (sérphos, “small winged insect”), while Furnée connects Latin <i>delpa</i> (“kind of insect”). Possibly related to Latin <i>sirpe</i>, which has been connected to compare <i>σῐ́λφῐον</i> (sĭ́lphĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laserwort; silphium; silphion</li></ol><p>Probably cognate with Latin <i>sirpe</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beehive, hive</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) store, hoard</li></ol><p>Unclear, perhaps a Pre-Greek word. In Koine Greek, the word also occurs as a neuter <i>σίμβλον</i> (símblon). In addition, Hesychius lists a feminine plural form <i>σίμβλαι</i> (símblai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the mustard plant (<i>Sinapis alba</i>)</li></ol><p>In mid-20th century thought from Demotic Egyptian <i>snwpt</i>, but Beekes 2010 considers it Pre-Greek, and related to <i>νᾶπῠ</i> (nâpŭ, “mustard”). Said terms may be areal; see Sanskrit <i>सर्षप</i> (sarṣápa, “mustard”) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harm; to hurt; to damage</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) to injure</li><li>to plunder; to pillage; to waste</li></ol><p>If it is of Indo-European origin, the verb must contain a presential <i>-ν-</i> (-n-), just as <i>κλῑ́νω</i> (klī́nō) and <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō), which spread not only to the aorist forms, but also to nominal derivatives, like <i>σίνος</i> (sínos) and <i>σῐ́ντης</i> (sĭ́ntēs), which is more problematic. Assuming a pre-form <i>&#42;τϝῑ-ν-ιε&#47;ο-</i> (&#42;twī-n-ie&#47;o-), the verb has been connected with Old English <i>þwīnan</i> (“to dwindle”), but this should rather be derived from the root <i>&#42;dʰwēy-</i> (“to slip away; dwindle; die; death”), together with <i>dwīnan</i> (“to waste away, languish, decline”). Further etymology unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ravening, predation</li><li>pillager, looter, spoiler</li></ol><p>From <i>σίνομαι</i> (sínomai, “to harm, hurt”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone parsley (<i>Sison amomum</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Pedanius Dioscorides, this plant is at home in Syria. Origin unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water parsnip (<i>Sium suave</i>)</li></ol><p>The etymology is still unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a meal-tub</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiled down new wine, often used as a syrup</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>σειρόω</i> (seiróō) or alternatively from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ῥύγχος</i> (rhúnkhos, “snout; beak”).</li></ol><p>Furnée connects the word with <i>σήραμβος</i> (sḗrambos, “kind of beetle”). However, Beekes finds this connection semantically unlikely. Compare also <i>ῥάμφος</i> (rhámphos, “beak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsnip (<i>Pastinaca sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the etymology is still unclear. The word recalls <i>ἄσαρον</i> (ásaron, “hazelwort”) and <i>ἡδύσαρον</i> (hēdúsaron, “axe-weed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garment of skin, different from <i>σισύρα</i> (sisúra) which was made of goat's hair</li></ol><p>Related to <i>σισύρα</i> (sisúra) and <i>σίσυς</i> (sísus). According to Furnée, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any coarse or cheap garment</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>σισύρα</i> (sisúra) and <i>σίσυρνα</i> (sísurna).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of grain or corn</li></ol><p>From <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos, “grain; corn”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bucket, pail</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>situla</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nuthatch (<i>Sitta europaea</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of onomatopoeic origin but Furnée links the word with <i>ψίττακος</i> (psíttakos, “parrot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cauldron similar to a caccabus</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, this word is clearly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tube, pipe</li><li>siphon, used for drawing wine out of the cask</li><li>drainage tube for hydrocele</li><li>service pipe for water in houses</li><li>waterspout from the gutters of the roof</li></ol><p>A technical term, with a formation similar to <i>ἄμβων</i> (ámbōn) and <i>δόλων</i> (dólōn), of uncertain origin. Possibly a non-Indo-European loan of sound-symbolic origin, particularly from a Mediterranean substrate language. Usually connected to Latin <i>tībia</i> (“shinbone; pipe, flute”), also of unclear origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tail-wagging</li></ol><p>From <i>σαίνω</i> (saínō, “I wag the tail, I fawn”) + <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of dogs</i>) to wag the tail, fawn</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to fawn, cringe, crouch</li><li>to fawn on, pay court to, greet</li><li>to gladden, especially with hope or conviction</li><li>to beguile, cozen, deceive</li></ol><p>The etymology is unexplained. It must be rejected the suggestion of Solmsen, who reconstructs a word for "penis" and connects Lithuanian <i>tvi̇̀nstu</i> (“to swell out (of a river)”), and connects other word with <i>σα-</i>, like <i>σάτυρος</i> (sáturos, “satyr”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to sweep, clean</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to clear away</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twer-</i> (“to stir”), like Sanskrit <i>त्वर्</i> (tvar, “to hurry”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þweraną</i> (“to stir”). Connection with <i>ὀτρύνω</i> (otrúnō, “to stir up, encourage”) is highly improbable, as it is unclear whether the initial <i>ὀ-</i> (o-) is a prefix. See also <i>σύρω</i> (súrō, “to drag, draw”), with which the word is often connected.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>shake violently, scatter</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of inner organs</i>) damaged, rotten</li><li>feeble, effeminate, womanish</li></ol><p>The formation can be compared with <i>μαλακός</i> (malakós, “soft, light; effeminate”) and <i>τριβακός</i> (tribakós, “rubbed, worn”), but the etymology is unknown. Furnée connects <i>σαυκός</i> (saukós, “dry”), which very strongly suggests Pre-Greek origin for this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>you have forsaken me (said by Jesus on the cross)</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>שְׁבַקתַּנִי</i> (šᵊḇaqtanî, “you have left me”) from the root <i>שְׁבַק</i> (šᵊḇaq, “to leave”) with pronominal suffix. Corresponds to Hebrew <i>עֲזַבְתָּנִי</i> (ʿăzaḇtānî, “you have forsaken me”) from the root <i>עָזַב</i> (ʿāzaḇ, “to forsake, to leave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(The / A) keeping of the Sabbath, Sabbath-keeping, Sabbathkeeping, Sabbath rest, Sabbath resting, Sabbath observance, observance of the Sabbath.</li></ol><p>From <i>σαββᾰτῐ́ζω</i> (sabbătĭ́zō, “I keep the Sabbath, I observe the Sabbath, I sabbatise, I sabbatize”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐσμός</i> (-ĭsmós, verbal noun suffix), from <i>σάββατον</i> (sábbaton, “Sabbath”), from Hebrew <i>שַׁבָּת</i> (shabát).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Ferula persica</i></li><li>sagapenum, the gum produced by this plant</li></ol><p>From Arabic سَكْبِينَج (sakbīnaj), from Middle Persian, whence سکبینه (sakbine).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pack, baggage</li><li>scrip, wallet, knapsack</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) equipment, harness, furniture</li></ol><p>From <i>σάττω</i> (sáttō, “to pack, stuff”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large dragnet, seine</li><li>hunting net</li></ol><p>Has been connected with Ancient Greek <i>σάττω</i> (sáttō, “to stuff, to press”), but this is semantically insufficient. Because of the Cypriot byform <i>ἁγάνα</i> (hagána) and the possible connection with <i>σάγουρον</i> (ságouron, “net”), Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>Salonen and Szemerényi derive the term specifically from Akkadian <i>𒅆𒆥𒉡</i> (ši-kin-nu /⁠šikinnu⁠/, “a vessel for oils, ointments, and wine; a large fishing net”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persian word for messenger</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who fishes with a drag-net</li></ol><p><i>σᾰγηνεύω</i> (săgēneúō, “to take fish with a drag net”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take fish with a drag-net</li></ol><p><i>σαγήνη</i> (sagḗnē, “drag-net”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisherman, one who casts the seine</li></ol><p>From <i>σαγήνη</i> (sagḗnē, “seine”) +&lrm; <i>βόλος</i> (bólos, “cast of a net”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Byzantine,&#32;plural only) saddle and welding blanket</li></ol><p><i>σάγμα</i> (ságma, “saddle”) +&lrm; <i>πασμαγάνδιον</i> (pasmagándion, “welding blanket”), the second element being from Persian پشماگند (pašmâgand).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἄρτου κλάσμα</i> (ártou klásma, literally “bread morsel”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsound, diseased</li><li>impotent</li><li>cracked (of a vessel)</li></ol><p>The etymology is uncertain. Maybe related to <i>σήθω</i> (sḗthō, “to sift, bolt”) or perhaps a contamination of <i>σαπρός</i> (saprós, “rotten”) with an unknown word. Furnée connects it with <i>ψαθυρός</i> (psathurós, “brittle”) and concludes that the word is from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knapsack, wallet</li></ol><p>From <i>σάκκος</i> (sákkos, “sack”) +&lrm; <i>πήρα</i> (pḗra, “wallet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who weaves sackcloths, sailmaker</li></ol><p><i>σάκκος</i> (sákkos, “sack”) +&lrm; <i>ὑφαίνω</i> (huphaínō, “weave”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>venthole, chimney</li></ol><p>A Semitic origin has been proposed for this word, connecting it with Classical Syriac <i>ܫܠܦܐ</i> (šelpā, “a knife-like iron tool for grasping or pulling out something”) and <i>Σαλαμβώ</i> (Salambṓ, “Babilonian goddess of love”). However, the alternation β/μβ would receive a good explanation under the assumption of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise, outcry</li></ol><p>From <i>σαλαγέω</i> (salagéō, “to overload, cram full”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire salamander (<i>Salamandra salamandra</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Suggestions put forward are:</p><p>Sound symbolism most likely had a role into the ultimate shape of the term, as it did in many languages in their terms for “lizard”. Compare also Byzantine Greek <i>σαλαμίνθη</i> (salamínthē, “spider”), with which it may share the possibly imitative root <i>σαλαμ-</i> (salam-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shake up, agitate</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be shaken, swayed, unsettled</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Pamphylian form of <i>σημαίνω</i> (sēmaínō)</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾶμα</i> (sâma) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sambuca, a triangular musical instrument with four strings</li><li>sambuca, an engine of similar form used in sieges</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Aramaic <i>סַבְּכָא</i> (sabbəḵā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of sword, shamshir</li></ol><p>From an Iranian source common to Old Armenian <i>սուսեր</i> (suser, “sword”), Classical Syriac <i>ܣܦܣܝܪܐ</i> (sap̄sērāʾ, “sword”), Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>ספסרא</i>, <i>ספסירא</i> (sap̄sērāʾ, “sword”) and Persian شمشیر (šamšir, “sword”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of marjoram</li></ol><p>From <i>σάμψουχον</i> (sámpsoukhon, “marjoram”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>board; plank; timber</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) folding doors</li><li>wooden platform; scaffold or stage</li><li>ship's deck</li><li>bench; seat</li><li>lid of box</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wooden tablets for writing on</li><li>plank to which offenders were bound or nailed</li></ol></ol><p>The formation is common to other technical terms like <i>δοκίς</i> (dokís, “beam”), <i>ἀρίς</i> (arís, “bow-drill”) and <i>σελίς</i> (selís, “crossbeam”) but the origin is uncertain. According to Beekes, the connection with <i>σαίνω</i> (saínō, “to fawn”) should be rejected for semantic reasons.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a light strap-shoe, sandal</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a kind of vessel in the Byzantine fleet, sandal</li></ol><p><i>σάνδαλον</i> (sándalon) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive), of uncertain origin, possibly loaned from Parthian, Persian, or another substrate of Asia Minor.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>σᾰ́νδᾰλον</i> (sắndălon)</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰ́νδᾰλον</i> (sắndălon, “sandal”) +&lrm; <i>-ίσκος</i> (-ískos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A sandal case</li></ol><p>From <i>σάνδαλον</i> (sándalon, “sandal”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sandarac, realgar</li><li>red orpiment</li><li>beebread; sandarac (bee pollen with added honey and bee secretions, stored in brood cells)</li></ol><p>Long taken to be an Oriental loanword from an unknown source; Akkadian <i>𒅆𒅎𒌈</i> (ši-im-tum /⁠šindu, simṭu, šimtu⁠/, “pigment, paint, glue, varnish”) + Akkadian <i>𒅊</i> (ar-qu /⁠arqu, warqu, barqu, irqu⁠/, “yellow”), as Akkadian <i>𒅆𒅎𒌈 𒅊</i> (/⁠šindu arqu⁠/, “orpiment”, literally “yellow paint”) suggested already by Thompson is possible, but not specifically attested. Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the variation "κ/χ".</p><p>Has been connected with the synonymous Middle Armenian <i>ջնարակ</i> (ǰnarak).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of orange color</li></ol><p>From <i>σανδᾰράκη</i> (sandărákē, “realgar”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σᾰνδᾰρᾰ́κη</i> (săndărắkē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red, vermilion</li></ol><p>From <i>σάνδυξ</i> (sándux, “sandix”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of sandalwood</li></ol><p>From <i>σάνταλον</i> (sántalon, “sandalwood”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scarlet pimpernel (<i>Anagallis arvensis</i>)</li></ol><p>Only found in one Dioskourides locus about “Phoenician” <i>Anagallis</i>, where it is said to be the name of the Gauls, which may be seen as the Punic population in Gaul, as the shape of the word is Semitic, and <i>𐤑𐤐𐤍</i> (ṣpn) is known from onomastics – see Baal-zephon – as meaning “to save, to protect”, relating to medicinal uses of the plant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of fish found in the Nile and in the Black Sea</li></ol><p>Certainly a borrowing. Furnée adduces <i>σάβειρος</i> (sábeiros, “tub-fish”) as a variant, suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decay, decayed matter</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰπρός</i> (săprós, “rotten, putrid”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rotten, putrid</li></ol><p>From the radical of <i>σήπω</i> (sḗpō) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or like sapphire</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰ́πφειρος</i> (sắppheiros, “sapphire”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loose trousers worn by Scythians</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;šarabāra</i>; compare Persian شلوار (šalvâr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plait, braid</li><li>plaited basket</li></ol><p>The formation of this word can be compared with <i>πλεκτάνη</i> (plektánē) and <i>ὁρκάνη</i> (horkánē). According to Beekes, the variation σ/τ proves a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sargo (<i>Diplodus sargus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of gregarious fish</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰργός</i> (sărgós, “sargo”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of laughter or smiles</i>) scornful, bitter, grim</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but since antiquity it is often cited as deriving from the Sardinian plant (<i>Ranunculus sardous</i>), known as either <i>σαρδάνη</i> (sardánē) or <i>σαρδόνιον</i> (sardónion). When eaten, it would cause the eater's face to contort in a look resembling scorn (generally followed by death).</p><p>It has also been compared with <i>σέσηρα</i> (sésēra, “to show teeth”) and <i>σαίρω</i> (saírō, “to grin”).</p><p>Alternatively, Kretschmer tried to connect this word to the name of the <i>Šardana</i> people living near Egypt, although the details remain unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sardonyx</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰ́ρδῐον</i> (sắrdĭon) +&lrm; <i>ὄνῠξ</i> (ónŭx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnash</li><li>to tear or lacerate flesh</li><li>to bite one's lip in anger</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to tear apart with sarcasm</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>σάρξ</i> (sárx, “meat”, “flesh”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitter irony</li><li>sarcasm</li></ol><p>From <i>σαρκάζω</i> (sarkázō, “to tear or lacerate”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>oncology</i>) sarcocele, a fleshy excrescence on the testicles</li></ol><p>From <i>σάρξ</i> (sárx, “flesh”) +&lrm; <i>κήλη</i> (kḗlē, “tumour, hernia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>afflicted with sarcocele</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰρκοκήλη</i> (sărkokḗlē, “sarcocele”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sarcocolla (Persian resin used in traditional medicine and paint manufacture)</li><li>milkvetch (flowering shrubs of the genus <i>Astragalus</i>, particularly <i>A. sarcocolla</i>, source of the resin)</li></ol><p>From <i>σάρξ</i> (sárx, “flesh”) +&lrm; <i>κόλλα</i> (kólla, “glue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>surgery</i>) surgeon's forceps</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰ́ρξ</i> (sắrx, “flesh”) +&lrm; <i>λᾰβῐ́ς</i> (lăbĭ́s, “forceps”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flesh-eating, carnivorous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sarcophagus</li></ol><p>From <i>σάρξ</i> (sárx, “flesh, meat”) +&lrm; <i>φᾰγεῖν</i> (phăgeîn, “to eat”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p><p>By ellipsis of [<i>λίθος</i>] σαρκοφάγος, a limestone found at Assos in Troas, remarkable for consuming the flesh of corpses laid in it; coffins were made of it, and such a coffin was called a σαρκοφάγος.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) fleshy excrescence on the navel</li></ol><p>From <i>σάρξ</i> (sárx, “flesh”) +&lrm; <i>ὀμφαλός</i> (omphalós, “navel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purulent flesh</li></ol><p>From <i>σάρξ</i> (sárx, “flesh”) +&lrm; <i>πύον</i> (púon, “pus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fleshy</li></ol><p>From <i>σάρξ</i> (sárx, “flesh”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “full of, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>σωρός γῆς</i> (sōrós gês, literally “heap of earth”), <i>κάλλυσμα</i> (kállusma, “sweeping”), <i>ψάμμος</i> (psámmos, “sand”), <i>χόρτος</i> (khórtos, “farmyard; fodder, provender”).</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>σαίρω</i> (saírō, “to sweep, clean”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old hollow oak</li></ol><p>According to Strömberg, from <i>σαρῶνες</i> (sarônes, “net of hunters”), which remains unconvincing in spite of the parallels adduced. The word has been connected with <i>σορωνίς</i> (sorōnís, “old silver fir”); since the variation <i>α/ο</i> cannot be explained, it must be assumed a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) chariot</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>σάτιλλα</i> (sátilla, “Big Dipper”). There is a certain similarity between <i>σάτιλλα</i> (sátilla) and Armenian <i>սայլ</i> (sayl, “cart, oxcart”). This should perhaps be explained as borrowing through, for example, Phrygian. Schmitt rather argues for a Thracian origin of <i>σάτιλλα</i> (sátilla), whereas remote resemblance of Armenian <i>սայլ</i> (sayl, “cart, oxcart”) with Georgian <i>ეტლი</i> (eṭli, “chariot”) is pointed out by Adontz. According to Beekes both words are from Pre-Greek, because they defy derivation from Indo-European.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman's headdress</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of probable Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a satrap, title of a Persian viceroy or governor of a province (satrapy)</li><li>In general, of any governor or lord</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;xšaθrapāvā</i>, which is cognate with Old Persian <i>𐎧𐏁𐏂𐎱𐎠𐎺𐎠</i> (x-š-ç-p-a-v-a /⁠xšaçapāvā⁠/); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satrapy, office or province of a satrap</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰτρᾰ́πης</i> (sătrắpēs, “satrap”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satyriasis</li><li>a disease involving swelling around the temples, causing the victim to resemble a satyr</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰτῠρῐάω</i> (sătŭrĭáō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>σᾰτῠρῐ́ᾱσῐς</i> (sătŭrĭ́āsĭs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) diminutive of <i>σᾰ́τῠρος</i> (sắtŭros): small satyr</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) the name of a plant</li></ol><p>From <i>σᾰ́τῠρος</i> (sắtŭros, “satyr”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man orchid (<i>Orchis anthropophora</i>)</li><li>kind of shrew</li></ol><p>From <i>σάτυρος</i> (sáturos, “satyre”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἁβρόν</i> (habrón, “delicate”), <i>ἐλαφρόν</i> (elaphrón, “light”), <i>ἄκρον</i> (ákron, “high”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes from Pre-Greek, like <i>ψαυκρός</i> (psaukrós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Syracuse</i>) dry</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>A number of wrong etymologies have been proposed, like a borrowing from an original Italic word related to <i>αὖος</i> (aûos, “dry”). Conversely, Furnée convincingly compares <i>σαυχμόν</i> (saukhmón, “tender; spongy; unsound”) and <i>σαβακός</i> (sabakós, “weak, moldered, smashed”). It follows that the word is Pre-Greek.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of serpent</li><li>gem found in the belly of the lizard</li></ol><p>From <i>σαύρα</i> (saúra, “lizard”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lizard-killer (epithet of Apollo)</li></ol><p><i>σαύρα</i> (saúra, “lizard”) +&lrm; <i>κτόνος</i> (któnos, “murder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ferrule or spike at the butt end of a spear, by which it was stuck into the ground</li></ol><p>From <i>σαύρα</i> (saúra, “lizard”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>σεαυτοῦ</i> (seautoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of something seen with the eyes, or of something understood with the mind</i>) clear, distinct</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) speaking clearly, reliable</li></ol><p>Of obscure origin. Furnee tentatively considers the word to be from Pre-Greek, based on a presumed connection to <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “clever”), though Beekes is, surprisingly, skeptical. Connections to Latin <i>sapiō</i> can be dismissed based on the fact that <i><i>φ</i> (ph)</i> comes from an aspirated PIE consonant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>clearly, distinctly</li></ol><p><i>σαφής</i> (saphḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>σῶς</i> (sôs)</li></ol><p>See the contracted form <i>σῶς</i> (sôs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō, “to save”)</li></ol><p>From <i>σάος</i> (sáos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>javelin</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) membrum virile</li><li>Samnites</li></ol><p>The etymology of this word is unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lizard</li></ol><p>Beekes claims a Pre-Greek origin, due to the semantic category of lizards being "local substrate" and not being conceptualized among the Proto-Indo-Europeans.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straddling, waddling</li><li>loose, wanton (of the gait of courtesans)</li><li>prancing (of a horse)</li></ol><p>The word rhymes with <i>φαῦλος</i> (phaûlos) and probably they have influenced each other. According to Beekes, the adjective presumably belongs to a group of Pre-Greek words.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σαῦλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Saul</li></ol><p>Learned borrowing from Hebrew <i>שָׁאוּל</i> (sha'úl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σαύρᾱ</i> (saúrā, “lizard”)</li><li>horse mackerel, jack mackerel</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>σαύρα</i> (saúra, “lizard”). Others have suggested the word as a variant of <i>σαῦλος</i> (saûlos, “turning, twisting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of leguminous plant</li></ol><p>Older etymologies involve connections with <i>σαυκρόν</i> (saukrón, “delicate, light”), <i>σαυχμόν</i> (saukhmón, “tender, unsound, weak”) and <i>σαυσαρόν</i> (sausarón, “whispering, slanderous”). Furnée is undoubtedly correct in connecting <i>σώσικες</i> (sṓsikes, “cooked beans”), which shows that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to quench, extinguish, put out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to dry up</li><li>to quench, quell, check</li></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;intransitive) to be quenched, to go out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to dry up</li><li>to be quelled, lulled</li></ol></ol><p>Usually derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)gʷes-</i> (“to extinguish, quell”). Cognates in this case include Sanskrit <i>जासयति</i> (jāsáyati, “to exhaust”), Lithuanian <i>gèsti</i> (“to extinguish, to go out”), Irish <i>bás</i> (“death”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gasìti</i> (“to extinguish”), and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;kwistiz</i> (“destruction”).</p><p>Beekes accepts this theory, but notes that the existence of Hittite <i>𒆠𒅖𒋫𒊑</i> (kištāri, “to be extinguished, perish”), as well as the aberrant σ- in the anlaut and the uncommonness of -σβ- in the Greek inherited lexicon, suggests an alternative reconstruction of the PIE root as <i>&#42;ges-</i>, if the Hittite is related to the terms above. Under this scenario, the Hittite would be grouped with the Balto-Slavic, Indic, and Celtic terms, while the Greek and Germanic would be excluded.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthen vessel in which beans where shaken while being roasted</li></ol><p>From <i>σείω</i> (seíō, “to shake”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shake</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of earthquakes</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to disturb, agitate</li><li>(<i>Attic</i>) to blackmail</li></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;intransitive) to shake</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to move to and fro</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to shake</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to shake oneself</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tséihō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twéys-e-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;tweys-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>त्वेषति</i> (tvéṣati), Avestan <i>𐬚𐬬𐬀𐬫𐬀𐬵</i> (θvayah, “fright, fear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>thyself, yourself</li></ol><p><i>σέο</i> (séo, “you”, genitive singular) +&lrm; <i>αὐτοῦ</i> (autoû, “self”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fear, be afraid</li><li>to honour religiously, to worship</li><li>(<i>post-Homeric</i>) synonym of <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of palm fibre</li></ol><p>From <i>σεβένιον</i> (sebénion, “palm fibre”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>palm fibre</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to worship, honour</li></ol><p>From <i>σέβας</i> (sébas, “awe”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) august ruler, a senior court title (mostly restricted to those of imperial descent) in the late Byzantine empire and some bordering states</li></ol><p>From <i>σεβαστός</i> (sebastós, “venerable, reverend, august”) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “ruler”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) venerable, reverend, revered, august</li></ol><p>From <i>σεβάζομαι</i> (sebázomai, “to feel awe, revere”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix): literally “worthy of being revered”. Compare <i>σεπτός</i> (septós, “august”), formed by adding the same suffix to <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σεβαστός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Augustus; Sebastus</li></ol><p>Use of <i>σεβᾰστός</i> (sebăstós, “venerable, reverend, august”) as proper noun; see there for more. Calque of Latin <i>Augustus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord, rope</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) chain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tseřřā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twerH-yeh₂-</i> (“seizing”), from <i>&#42;twerH-</i> (“to grab, seize, enclose”). The traditional connection to <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “to connect”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ser-</i> (“to bind, put together”), is semantically more appealing than the above, but fails to explain the initial <i>σ-</i> (s-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to desiccate, dry</li></ol><p>From the same root of <i>Σείριος</i> (Seírios, “Sirius”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Σειρήν</i> (Seirḗn, “siren”)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σειρήν)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) siren</li></ol><p>Perhaps originally "entangler" or "binder," from <i>σειρά</i> (seirá, “rope, cord”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twerH-</i> (“to grab, seize, enclose”). On the other hand, Chantraine is in favor of a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix <i>-ήν</i>. Compare also <i>Σείριος</i> (Seírios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaking; shock</li><li>earthquake</li><li>storm</li><li>agitation; commotion</li></ol><p>From <i>σείω</i> (seíō, “to shake”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós). The "earthquake" sense is an ellipsis of <i>γῆς σεισμός</i> (gês seismós, “shaking of the earth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snail with a spiral shell</li></ol><p>According to Redard, the correct form should be <i>&#42;σιαλίτης</i> (&#42;sialítēs), from <i>σίαλον</i> (síalon, “spittle, saliva”) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moon</li><li>month</li><li>a moon-shaped cake</li><li>type of plant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;selasnā</i>, whence also <i>σέλας</i> (sélas).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σελήνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the earth's Moon</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Selene, goddess of the Moon</li></ol><p>From <i>σελήνη</i> (selḗnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix, “purple or crimson; date palm”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, in view of the ending in <i>-ρ</i>, which is found also in <i>ἐλίμαρ</i> (elímar, “kind of grain”), <i>κύδαρ</i> (kúdar, “grave”) and <i>νῶκαρ</i> (nôkar, “lethargy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of celery</li></ol><p>From <i>σέλῑνον</i> (sélīnon, “celery”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) crossbeam of stone in ceiling construction</li><li>block or sector of seats in a theater</li><li>rowing bench</li><li>column of writing in a papyrus roll</li><li>writing page</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>σέλμα</i> (sélma, “deck of a ship”). These words have been compared to Old English <i>selma</i> and <i>syll</i>, and to German <i>Schwelle</i> (“threshold”), but phonetically this etymology is unconvincing. If the gloss cited by Hesychius, <i>ἔλματα</i> (élmata, “planking”), is cognate, then the σελμα-ἔλμα alternation would point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moonstone</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) inhabitants of the Moon</li></ol><p><i>σελήνη</i> (selḗnē, “moon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, “belonging to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the moon, lunar</li><li>epileptic</li></ol><p>From <i>σελήνη</i> (selḗnē, “moon”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground ivy (<i>Glechoma hederacea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σέλῑνον</i> (sélīnon, “celery”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the finest wheaten flour</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Semitic, the same source of Latin <i>simila</i> (“wheat flour”), Arabic سَمِيذ (samīḏ), Aramaic <i>סְמִידָא</i> / <i>ܣܡܻܝܕܳܐ</i> (səmīḏā), from Akkadian <i>𒆠𒅔𒆠𒅔𒄯𒄯</i> (/⁠samīdu⁠/, “a type of fine groats, coarse flour, semolina”), related to Akkadian <i>𒀀𒊏𒄯𒄯</i> (/⁠samādu⁠/, “to grind fine”). Borrowed through Hittite, where <i>-alla-</i>, <i>-alli-</i> is an adjective formant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boasting; impressiveness</li></ol><p>From <i>σεμνός</i> (semnós, “solemn, pompous”) +&lrm; <i>-λογῐ́ᾱ</i> (-logĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>revered, august, holy</li><li>solemn, pompous</li></ol><p>From the radical of <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós), with regressive assimilation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A box</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, “the formation is Pre-Greek”. But it might be reborrowing from an Oriental source like Aramaic <i>צנדוקא</i> (ṣəndūqā) of <i>σῠνθήκη</i> (sŭnthḗkē). Also in the form <i>σάνδυξ</i> (sándux).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) august</li></ol><p>From <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai, “to be moved by respect for others”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix): literally “worthy of being respected”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the plural) seraph</li></ol><p>Singular back-formation from <i>σερᾱφείμ</i> (serāpheím, plural) by analogy with Biblical Hebrew <i>שְׂרָפִים</i> (śərāp̄îm) and its singular form <i>שָׂרָף</i> (śārāp̄, “burning one”), but without the corresponding alteration of the first vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italian orchid (<i>Orchis italica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Σέραπις</i> (Sérapis) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) deer</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>cervī</i>&nbsp;pl (“deer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>σεαυτοῦ</i> (seautoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>σεύατε</i> (seúate)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>σεύατο</i> (seúato)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chase, drive, impel</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to rush, hasten, hurry</li></ol><p>According to Beekes from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kyew-</i>, thus cognate with Sanskrit <i>च्यु</i> (cyu). Frisk and Chantraine, on the other hand, consider it to be related to <i>κινέω</i> (kinéō) and <i>κίω</i> (kíō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A shaking</li></ol><p>From <i>σείω</i> (seíō, “I shake”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sistrum</li></ol><p>From <i>σείω</i> (seíō, “to shake”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>σκοτία</i> (skotía, “darkness, gloom”), as they say in Crete.</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, it is a shortened form of <i>ψέφας</i> (pséphas, “darkness, gloom”) and thus from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Byzantine form of <i>τεῦτλον</i> (teûtlon, “beet”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Koine form of <i>τεῦτλον</i> (teûtlon, “beet”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pen, fold, especially for rearing lambs, kids and calves</li><li>sacred enclosure, precinct, dedicated to a hero</li><li>sepulchre, burial place, enclosed and consecrated</li><li>stump of an old olive tree</li><li>weight in the balance</li></ol><p>Since Bezzenberger, traditionally compared with <i>σάττω</i> (sáttō, “to cram, to stuff”), though this suffers from phonetic issues. According to Beekes, the alternation rather points to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weigh against</li><li>to balance, equalize</li><li>to compensate</li></ol><p><i>σηκός</i> (sēkós, “pen, enclosure, fence”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, “causative verbal suffix”), thus "to make into an enclosure" > "to compare" > "to balance, weigh against".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σημείᾱ</i> (sēmeíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σημείᾱ</i> (sēmeíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show, point out, indicate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to sign, signal</li><li>Ι predict, portend</li><li>(<i>later prose</i>) to appear</li></ol><li>to signal someone to do something, to bid</li><li>to signify, indicate, declare</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to interpret, explain; to tell, speak</li><li>to signify, mean</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to conclude from signs, conjecture</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to provide with a sign, mark, or seal</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to mark out for oneself</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σῆμᾰ</i> (sêmă) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>significant, giving signs</li></ol><p>From <i>σημαίνω</i> (sēmaínō, “to indicate”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the giving of a signal or command</li><li>indication, designation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>frequently in&#32;grammar</i>) meaning, signification</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>the title of a work by Chrysippus</li></ol><li>(<i>music</i>) notation</li></ol><li>the decisive appearance of a disease</li><li>a mark</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of the Nile-flood</li></ol><li>address of a correspondent</li><li>(in the phrase <i>βασιλικὴ σημασία</i>) a royal insigne or appearance</li></ol><p>From <i>σημαίνω</i> (sēmaínō, “to signal”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a military standard, an ensign, a <i>signum militare</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a military standard as a decoration, translating the Latin <i>vēxillum</i></li><li>a body of troops under one standard, translating the Latin <i>manipulus</i></li></ol><li>an image of the Emperor on a standard, translating the Latin <i>signum</i></li><li>a (heraldic) device or bearing on an ἀσπίς (aspís, “shield”)</li><li>(<i>meaning uncertain</i>) perhaps a milestone</li></ol><p>From <i>σῆμᾰ</i> (sêmă, “mark, sign”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>records</li></ol><p><i>σημειόω</i> (sēmeióō, “to mark, seal”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indication, notice</li><li>inference from a sign</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a remarking or observing of symptoms</li><li>(medicine,&#32;later) an examination</li></ol><li>a visible sign or token, as a banner</li></ol><p><i>σημειόω</i> (sēmeióō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>observant of signs</li></ol><p>From <i>σημειόω</i> (sēmeióō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark, stamp</li><li>to seal (<i>e.g. letters</i>)</li><li>to signal</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to take notice of, honor</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to interpret as a sign or portent, infer as from a sign</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;medicine) to diagnose, examine</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to note (<i>for oneself</i>)</li></ol><p><i>σημεῖον</i> (sēmeîon) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of today, today's</li></ol><p>From <i>σήμερον</i> (sḗmeron, “today”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνός</i> (-ĭnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mark; a sign; a token; a indication</li><li>a tomb</li><li>an omen (sign from the gods)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wonder; portent</li></ol><li>flag signal</li><li>standard or flag</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>body of troops under one standard or flag</li></ol><li>(<i>heraldry</i>) device upon a shield or ship; figurehead</li><li>signet on ring; figure; image</li><li>watchword; warcry</li><li>birthmark or distinguishing feature</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) a proof</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) a sign used as a probable argument in proof of a conclusion</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) a point</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) symptom</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a kind of skin eruption</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) shorthand symbols</li><li>critical mark</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) mathematical point</li><li>point of time; instant</li><li>(music,&#32;prosody) unit of time</li></ol><p>From <i>σῆμᾰ</i> (sêmă, “mark, sign”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuttlefish</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes says a connection with <i>σήπω</i> (sḗpō, “to rot”) is semantically possible, but formally problematic, and suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>putrefactive, septic</li></ol><p>From <i>σήπω</i> (sḗpō, “make rotten”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silken, made of silk or like silk</li></ol><p>From <i>σήρ</i> (sḗr, “silkworm”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”), possibly ultimately from the Old Chinese word for silk.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of sesame</li></ol><p>From <i>σήσαμον</i> (sḗsamon, “sesame”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit of the Lenten rose (<i>Helleborus orientalis</i>)</li><li>purple rock cress (<i>Aubrieta deltoidea</i>)</li><li>bastard rocket (<i>Reseda alba</i>)</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>σησᾰμοειδής</i> (sēsămoeidḗs, “like sesame or sesame-seeds”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medlar (<i>Mespilus germanica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σητάνιος</i> (sētánios, “of this year, this year's”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butterfly</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>σής</i> (sḗs, “moth, clothes-moth”) and <i>δόκη</i> (dókē, “appearance, seeming”), but the formation is obscure.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength, might, power</li></ol><p>Unknown. The cluster /stʰ/ is unusual for Ancient Greek terms inherited from Proto-Indo-European. However several hypotheses for an Indo-European derivation exist:</p><p>The latter two both assume Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;skʷʰénos</i>.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, mighty</li></ol><p>From <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, mighty</li><li>intense, violent</li></ol><p>From <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) jaw, jawbone, maxilla</li></ol><p>Connection with <i>ψίομαι</i> (psíomai, “to chew”) is semantically attractive, but the formation is quite unclear. Since an Indo-European origin is formally hardly possible, the word could well be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunting spear, pike</li></ol><p>Of foreign origin. Thought to be from Illyrian <i>&#42;sibina</i>, comparable to Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;tsupina</i> (modern <i>thupër</i>). Also compare Old Armenian <i>սուին</i> (suin, “spear”), Persian زوبین (zubin, “javelin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>σῑγή</i> (sīgḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be silent, to be still</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold silent, keep secret</li></ol><p>From <i>σῑγή</i> (sīgḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silence</li><li>undertone, whisper</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;σϝίγ-</i> (&#42;swíg-), probably of imitative origin. Has been connected to Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;swīgā</i> (“silence”) (whence German <i>schweigen</i> (“to keep quiet”)); however, the Germanic term would reflect a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweygʰ-</i>, which would yield Greek <i>&#42;εἱχ-</i> (&#42;heikh-), making inheritance from basic sound laws impossible. Probably not related to <i>σιωπή</i> (siōpḗ, “silence”), despite the resemblance.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) shining, glittering, glistening</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a theoretical Luwian <i>𒊺𒄭𒇷</i> (še-ḫe-li /⁠šeḫeli-⁠/, “clean”) or Hittite <i>𒊺𒄭𒇷</i> (še-ḫe-li /⁠šeḫeli-⁠/, “clean”) forms not yet attested in the available corpus resources, itself from the attested Hurrian <i>𒅆𒄩𒀀𒆷</i> (ši-ḫa-a-la /⁠šeḫala-, šiḫala-, šiḫāla⁠/, “clean”), from Sumerian <i>𒂖</i> (sikil, “pure”), borrowed also into Akkadian <i>𒋗𒈛𒄩</i> (šu.luḫ.ḫa /⁠šuluḫḫu⁠/, “purification rite”). Possibly also distantly related to Ugaritic <i>𐎒𐎃𐎍</i> (sḫl, “gemstone grinder, polisher, engraver”), and possibly Arabic سَحَلَ (saḥala, “to trim smooth, to scrap or rub off, to file or pare”), although this has cognate with Akkadian <i>𒊓𒄩𒈝</i> (sa-ḫa-lum /⁠saḫālu⁠/, “to pierce or stab; hence to engrave or chisel into”), possibly an early instance of phono-semantic matching with the proto-Semitic sense, to cut smooth and the Sumerian being clean; to make a stone shining.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaped like the lunate sigma (which resembles the English C); semicircular, crescent-shaped</li></ol><p>From <i>σῖγμᾰ</i> (sîgmă, “sigma”) +&lrm; <i>-ο-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-oid, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunting spear</li></ol><p>According to Herodotus and Aristotle, the word is Cyprian, whereas a scholium on Apollonius Rhodius claims Scythian. The word resembles the ethnonym <i>Σιγῠ́νναι</i> (Sigŭ́nnai), a people of Iranian (Scythian) descent. According to Beekes, due to the presence of alternative forms, the word can be of Pre-Greek origin. The similarity with <i>σῐβῠ́νη</i> (sĭbŭ́nē, “spear”) is only coincidental.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>silently</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of iron or steel</li></ol><p>From <i>σῐ́δηρος</i> (sĭ́dēros) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Laconia</i>) boy in his fifteenth or sixteenth year</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of medical instrument&#59; perhaps a spatula</li></ol><p>From <i>σῐ́δηρος</i> (sĭ́dēros, “iron”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>of iron, made of iron, done with iron</li><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) a precious stone</li><li>(botany,&#32;Hellenistic senses) name of various plants</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>for sense</i> "ironwort" <i>see</i> <i>σῐδηρῖτῐς</i>&nbsp;f (sĭdērîtĭs)</li><li><i>also see the plants</i> <i>ἑλξίνη</i> (helxínē), <i>χαμαίπιτυς</i> (khamaípitus), <i>περιστερεὼν ὕπτιος</i> (peristereṑn húptios)</li></ol></ol><p><i>σῐ́δηρ(ος)</i> (sĭ́dēr(os), “iron”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armoury</li></ol><p>From <i>σίδηρος</i> (sídēros, “iron”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron borer</li></ol><p>From <i>σῐ́δηρος</i> (sĭ́dēros, “iron”) +&lrm; <i>τρῡ́πανον</i> (trū́panon, “borer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iron-mine</li></ol><p>From <i>σῐδηρουργός</i> (sĭdērourgós, “iron worker, smith”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ironsmith, blacksmith</li></ol><p>From <i>σῐ́δηρος</i> (sĭ́dēros, “iron”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear arms, be armed</li></ol><p>From <i>σίδηρος</i> (sídēros, “iron”) +&lrm; <i>φορέω</i> (phoréō, “to bear, wear”), literally “to bear iron”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of iron and copper</li></ol><p>From <i>σῐ́δηρος</i> (sĭ́dēros, “iron”) +&lrm; <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós, “copper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ironwort (<i>Sideritis romana</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σίδηρος</i> (sídēros, “iron”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis), feminine form of <i>σιδηρίτης</i> (sidērítēs, “made of iron”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Σικελικόν</i> (Sikelikón)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squeamish, fastidious person, especially in eating</li></ol><p>The cluster -κχ- makes an Indo-European origin unlikely. Further unclear; assumed Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottle gourd (<i>Benincasa hispida</i>, syn. <i>Lagenaria vulgaris</i>)</li><li>round gourd (<i>Cucurbita maxima</i>)</li><li>gourd used as a calabash</li><li>cupping-instrument, because it was shaped like a gourd</li></ol><p>For plant names in <i>-ύα</i>, compare <i>οἰσύα</i> (oisúa), <i>ὀστρύα</i> (ostrúa). The Hesychius gloss <i>σεκούα</i> (sekoúa), as well as the toponym <i>Σικυών</i> (Sikuṓn)/<i>Σεκυών</i> (Sekuṓn), shows an alternation <i>σικ-/σεκ-</i>, which cannot be explained from an Indo-European point of view, but rather from substrate/Pre-Greek. Together with the forms <i>συκύα</i> (sukúa), <i>κύκυον</i> (kúkuon) and <i>κυκύϊζα</i> (kukúïza, “sweet round gourd”), as well as Latin <i>cucumis</i> (“cucumber”), Old Armenian <i>սեխ</i> (sex, “muskmelon”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tyky</i> (“pumpkin”) it seems that we are dealing with a Wanderwort that might also have Semitic and Kartvelian cognates, like Arabic قِثَّاء (qiṯṯāʔ, “<i>Cucumis</i>”), Akkadian <i>𒄾</i> (qiššû, “cucumber”), Hebrew <i>קשוא</i> (qiššūʾ, “Armenian cucumber”), Laz <i>შუკა</i> (şuǩa, “cucumber; melon”), Mingrelian <i>შინკა</i> (šinḳa, “melon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a silen</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῑληνός</i> (Sīlēnós, “Silenus”). Doublet of <i>Σῐλουᾱνός</i> (Sĭlouānós).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σιληνός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Silenus</li></ol><p>Doublet of <i>Σῐλουᾱνός</i> (Sĭlouānós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of musical instrument of thirty-five strings</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snub-nosed, flat nosed</li><li>steep, uphill</li><li>bent in, hollow, concave</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Has been connected to Old High German <i>swīnan</i>, Old Norse <i>svīna</i>, as well as Middle High German <i>swīmen</i>, Old Norse <i>svīma</i>, though these are formally impossible (as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sw-</i> does not yield Greek <i>σ-</i> (s-)), and the theory relating the word to <i>σῶμα</i> (sôma, “body”) and <i>σῆμᾰ</i> (sêmă, “sign”) via a supposed Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tyeh₂-</i> (“to strike”) is semantically improbable. Ultimately, all proposed connections are problematic, and Beekes suggests a likely Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the shape of a snub nose, snubbiness</li></ol><p>From <i>σιμός</i> (simós, “snub-nosed, flat nosed”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of mustard</li></ol><p>From <i>σίναπι</i> (sínapi, “mustard”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine cloth, usually linen</li><li>garment or other item made of such linen</li></ol><p>Possibly a loanword from Egyptian <i>šnḏwt</i> (“kilt”). Compare also Hebrew <i>סָדִין</i> (sadin, “bedsheet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lac-tree (<i>Schleichera oleosa</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, an Oriental loanword. Perhaps from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pit-searcher, i.e. a probe or gauge, with which tax-gatherers searched corn-pits and magazines, used in war to try whether there were pits in the ground</li><li>barbed lance of the same shape</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pit for storing grain</li><li>pitfall</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek as there's no connection with other words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of hartwort</li></ol><p>From <i>σισύμβριον</i> (sisúmbrion, “hartwort”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water mint (<i>Mentha aquatica</i>)</li><li>watercress (<i>Nasturtium officinale</i>)</li><li>woman's ornament</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloak made of goat's hair</li><li>coverlet for the night</li></ol><p>Related to <i>σίσυρνα</i> (sísurna) and <i>σίσυς</i> (sísus). According to Furnée, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a little bit of corn or bread</li></ol><p>From <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos, “grain; corn; bread”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain; corn</li><li>bread; food made from grain</li><li>provisions; victuals</li><li>food for dogs</li><li>feces</li></ol><p>From <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos, “grain”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A granary</li></ol><p>From <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos, “grain”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread-making; preparation of food</li></ol><p>From <i>σῑτοποιέω</i> (sītopoiéō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eating corn or bread</li></ol><p>From <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos, “corn; bread”) +&lrm; <i>-φᾰ́γος</i> (-phắgos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public granary, storehouse or wheat-fund</li><li>money for the purchase of corn, as part of a soldier's pay</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) name of a tax, probably levied to provide corn money</li></ol><p>From <i>σῑτώνης</i> (sītṓnēs, “public buyer of corn”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crippled, maimed, lame</li><li>mad, foolish, blinded</li><li>porous, spongy, hollow</li></ol><p>The word shares the formation with <i>τυφλός</i> (tuphlós, “blind”) and <i>χωλός</i> (khōlós, “lame”). Besides this word, there are also <i>σιφνός</i> (siphnós, “empty, void”) and <i>σιπαλός</i> (sipalós, “blinded; maimed”). Because of the variants <i>σιφλ-/σιφν-/σιπαλ-</i>, the word is probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) to act like a person from the island of Siphnos</li><li>to engage in elaborate musical performances resembling those of Philoxenides of Siphnos</li><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to touch someone's buttocks with one's finger</li></ol><p>From <i>Σίφνος</i> (Síphnos, “Siphnos”) + <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κενός</i> (kenós, “empty, void”).</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek root of <i>σιφλός</i> (siphlós, “crippled, lame”) and <i>σιπαλός</i> (sipalós, “blinded, maimed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be silent or still</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of bees</i>) to be still</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold silent, keep secret, to not speak of</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to make silent, silence</li></ol><p>From <i>σῐωπή</i> (sĭōpḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silence</li><li>hush, calm</li></ol><p>Probably of non-Indo-European (Pre-Greek substrate) origin, due to the alternating forms <i>σιωπ-</i> and <i>σωπ-</i>. Superficially resembles <i>σιγή</i> (sigḗ, “silence”), but probably not related; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swībaną</i> (“to suspend, stop, finish”) is also likely not related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>taciturn</li></ol><p>From <i>σῐωπᾰ́ω</i> (sĭōpắō) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>silently, in silence</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silence</li><li>hush, calm</li></ol><p>Probably of non-Indo-European (Pre-Greek substrate) origin, due to the alternating forms <i>σιωπ-</i> and <i>σωπ-</i>. Superficially resembles <i>σιγή</i> (sigḗ, “silence”), but probably not related; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swībaną</i> (“to suspend, stop, finish”) is also likely not related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Laconian form of <i>θεός</i> (theós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taciturnity</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a veil, covering</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σῐωπᾰ́ω</i> (sĭōpắō, “to be silent”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to limp</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;skəďďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skn̥g-yéti</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)keng-</i> (“to limp, hobble”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>खञ्जति</i> (khañjati, “to limp”) and Old English <i>hincian</i>. Alternatively, it may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skn̥d-yé-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;skend-</i> (“to jump”), whence also Latin <i>scandō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ladder</li><li>stairs</li><li>gangway</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>scāla</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir up, hoe</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to search, probe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;skə́ľľō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to split, separate”).</p><p>The verb has generally been connected to Old Armenian <i>ցելում</i> (cʻelum, “to split, tear”) Hittite <i>𒅖𒆗𒆷𒄿</i> (iškalla, “to tear apart, slit open”) and Lithuanian <i>skélti</i> (“to split”). The geminate <i>-λλ-</i> seems to go back to either <i>&#42;σκάλ-ιω</i> (&#42;skál-iō) (as in Lithuanian <i>skiliù</i> (“to strike fire”)), or <i>&#42;σκάλ-νω</i> (&#42;skál-nō), with a nasal present like Lithuanian <i>skilù</i> (“to split off, separate oneself”), Old Armenian <i>քաղեմ</i> (kʻałem, “to pluck, weed, mow, harvest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A short sword, a knife</li></ol><p>From <i>σκάλλω</i> (skállō, “to stir up, to hoe”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to split, separate”); see also Sanskrit <i>कल</i> (kalá, “small part”)), Hittite (iškalla, “to tear apart, slit open”), Lithuanian <i>skélti</i> (“to split”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mole (animal of the family Talpidae)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>σκάλλω</i> (skállō, “to stir up, hoe”). However, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin in view of the suffix common to other animal names, such as <i>δρύοψ</i> (drúops), <i>κόρνοψ</i> (kórnops), <i>κώνωψ</i> (kṓnōps) and <i>πάρνοψ</i> (párnops).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trap, snare</li><li>enticement, temptation</li><li>offense, scandal</li><li>stumbling block</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skend-</i>der.?Ancient Greek <i>σκάνδαλον</i></p><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skend-</i> (“to jump”), and compared with Latin <i>scandō</i> (“to ascend”), Sanskrit <i>स्कन्दति</i> (skandati, “to jump, hop, hurry”). The original sense, per Beekes, was probably "a piece of wood hanging on ropes, used by acrobats as well as trap-setters"; under the Indo-European theory, the term would thus be interpreted as "wood for jumping or climbing". Beekes is unconvinced by the semantics, as well as the unexplained <i>-α-</i> (-a-) in the Greek form, and prefers to take it as Pre-Greek, though he admits the possibility of a loan from some other lost Indo-European language, perhaps from the root <i>&#42;skend-</i> mentioned above.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Venus' comb, shepherd's needle (<i>Scandix pecten-veneris</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig</li><li>to dig, delve, for cultivation</li><li>to dig about, cultivate by digging</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>The basis of this verb is either </i>σκαπ-<i> or </i>σκαφ-<i>. In the first case, the relic Latin <i>scapulae</i> (“shoulder blades”) has been compared, assuming that it originally meant "shovel" as a primary agent noun. In the second case, <i>σκάπτω</i> (skáptō) could formally correspond to a verb for "plane, scratch", in Latin <i>scabō</i> (“to scratch, scrape”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skabaną</i> (“to shave, scrape”), Lithuanian <i>skabiù</i> (“to scoop out with a chisel”), to which the Slavic group of Russian <i>ско́бель</i> (skóbelʹ, “spokeshave”) is connected, suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skabʰ-</i> (“to scratch”). Connection with <i>σκήπτω</i> (skḗptō, “to prop, stay”) and <i>σκίπων</i> (skípōn, “staff, crutch”) is formally and semantically unfeasible. Considering that related terms, like <i>κάπετος</i> (kápetos, “ditch, trench”), <i>σκάφαλος</i> (skáphalos, “one who draws water”) and <i>σκαφλεύς</i> (skaphleús), seem to be Pre-Greek, Beekes suggests that the verb could be a loan from a European substrate language.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>planing</li><li>writing</li><li>drawing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kreybʰ-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>scrībō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parrotfish</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps related to <i>σκαίρω</i> (skaírō, “to dance, to jump”) because of its lively movements. Or maybe related to <i>σκαρῖτις</i> (skarîtis, “a kind of stone”) if named after its colour. Both could be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut off”); see <i>σκορπίος</i> (skorpíos) and Albanian <i>harrok</i>. However, also compare Welsh <i>cerdded</i> (“to leap”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black hellebore (<i>Helleborus niger</i>)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σκάρφη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scarphe, Locris, Greece</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>σκάρφη</i> (skárphē, “black hellebore”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowl, tub, basin, bath</li><li>light boat, skiff</li><li>cradle</li><li>grave</li><li>concave sundial</li></ol><p>From <i>σκᾰ́πτω</i> (skắptō, “to dig”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) hull of a ship</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) ship itself</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow of the external ear</li></ol><p>From <i>σκάπτω</i> (skáptō, “to dig”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Compare, for more, <i>σκῠ́φος</i> (skŭ́phos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dry up; to parch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelh₁-</i> (“to parch, wither”), related to English <i>shallow</i>, German <i>schal</i> (“faint, vapid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) leg</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skélos</i> (“curve, bending”), from <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i> (“to curve, bend”).</p><p>Possibly related are <i>σκελίς</i> (skelís), <i>σχελίς</i> (skhelís, “rib(s) (of beef)”), but these may instead be from <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to cut, carve, split”) (cf. <i>σκάλλω</i> (skállō), <i>σκύλλω</i> (skúllō)), or Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of tunny</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpenter's axe, adze, for hewing and smoothing the trunks of trees</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) slightly oblique surgical bandage</li></ol><p>The suffix "-αρνο-" suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σκέπαρνον</i> (sképarnon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering; shelter</li></ol><p>Unknown. Although <i>σκέπω</i> (sképō, “to cover, shelter”) seems primary at first sight, it is attested only late, which may indicate that it should rather be interpreted as a back-formation from the denominative verb <i>σκεπάζω</i> (skepázō) or from <i>σκέπη</i> (sképē). Although the stem looks Indo-European and could be projected back to a pre-form <i>&#42;skep-</i>, no good cognates are known. Only some Balto-Slavic forms like Lithuanian <i>kepùre</i> and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čepьcь</i> (“bonnet”) may be connected, although these reflect an <i>s-</i>less stem <i>&#42;kep-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering (of clothing)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) covering membrane in plants</li></ol><p>From <i>σκεπάζω</i> (skepázō, “to cover”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, shelter</li><li>shade</li><li>protection</li></ol><p>Unknown. Related to <i>σκέπας</i> (sképas), <i>σκέπω</i> (sképō). Although the stem looks Indo-European and could be projected back to a pre form <i>&#42;skep-</i>, no good cognates are known. Only some Balto-Slavic forms like Lithuanian <i>kepùre</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čepьcь</i> (“bonnet”) may be connected, although these reflect an <i>s-</i>less stem <i>&#42;kep-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look at, examine</li><li>to examine, consider, think</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>rarely</i>) to think, deem</li><li>to prepare, premeditate</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;sképťomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skep-ye-</i>, from a metathesis of <i>&#42;speḱ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>speciō</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Russian <i>рассма́тривать</i> (rassmátrivatʹ, “to look at, to peruse, to examine; to regard, to consider”) (akin to <i>смотре́ть</i> (smotrétʹ)) and German <i>betrachten</i> (“to look at, to behold; to regard, to consider, look upon something in a certain way”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>σκεπάζω</i> (skepázō)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Rix suggests that it could derive from the thematic root present of a Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;(s)pek-</i>. However, Beekes suggests that, due to late attestation, the term should be interpreted as a back-formation from <i>σκεπάζω</i> (skepázō) or <i>σκέπη</i> (sképē). Related to <i>σκέπας</i> (sképas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scolding, abusive, vilifying, slandering</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>σκερβόλλω</i> (skerbóllō, “to slander, defame”), <i>σκέραφος</i> (skéraphos, “abuse, reproach”), <i>κέραφος</i> (kéraphos, “mockery, abuse”) and, possibly, <i>σκίραφος</i> (skíraphos, “trickery, cheating”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>viewing, sensory perception, observation</li><li>examination, speculation, consideration</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;chiefly Scepticism) doubt, hesitation</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) resolution, decree</li></ol><p>From <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai, “to consider”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prop, stay, press one thing against or upon another</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(medium,&#32;passive voice) to prop oneself or lean upon a staff</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to put forward by way of support, allege by way of excuse</li></ol><li>to let fall upon, hurl, shoot</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skeh₂p-</i> (“to prop”), which may be a European substrate term loaned to Indo-European. Cognates include Latin <i>scāpus</i> (“stalk”), Old English <i>sceaft</i> (“shaft”), Albanian <i>shkop</i> (“stick, scepter”), and Old Church Slavonic <i>копиѥ</i> (kopije, “spear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skink, a kind of lizard</li></ol><p>The variation points to Semitic origin, since there a nasal followed by a stop is often replaced with a geminate stop; the word is there attested in Classical Syriac <i>ܣܩܝܢܩܘܪ</i> (sqinqūr, “skink”) with variants and Akkadian <i>𒆲𒁯</i> (KUN.DAR /⁠šakkadirru, šakkatirru⁠/, “skink”, literally “mongoose of the forest”), originally applied to another relatable creature, but again listed separately as a type of lizard as well. Beekes however considers this variation to be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σκίγγος</i> (skíngos, “skink”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scatter, disperse</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>σκεδάννῡμι</i> (skedánnūmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shadow</li><li>shade (shelter from the sun)</li><li>shade (spirit of someone dead)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ḱeh₃ih₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squill (the name of various plants of the genus <i>Scilla</i>, and particularly <i>Drimia maritima</i>)</li></ol><p>Unexplained foreign word, but probably of Pre-Greek origin. Compare Mingrelian <i>შქიშქილა</i> (škiškila, “a kind of garlic”, literally “young, tender, fresh”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of squill</li></ol><p>From <i>σκίλλα</i> (skílla, “squill”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small couch, pallet, straw bed</li><li>kind of hammock used by invalids travelling</li></ol><p>Unknown. Interpreted as <i>&#42;σκιμπέ-πους</i> (&#42;skimpé-pous) and connected with <i>σκίμπτομαι</i> (skímptomai, “to throw, sling”) (itself possibly from a cross of <i>σκήπτω</i> (skḗptō, “to prop”) and <i>χρίμπτω</i> (khrímptō, “to force one's way near”); compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grīpaną</i>). A connection with <i>σκιμβός</i> (skimbós, “lame, crooked”) is semantically unlikely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) body</li></ol><p>Often connected with <i>σκῆνος</i> (skênos, “corpse”) which, if correct, could point to a Pre-Greek origin. The suffix <i>-αρ</i> can also be seen in <i>κύδαρ</i> (kúdar), <i>ἴκταρ</i> (íktar), and <i>σῦφαρ</i> (sûphar).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squirrel</li></ol><p>Traditionally explained as a bahuvrihi compound of <i>σκιά</i> (skiá, “shade, shadow”) + <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”), thus "(one who) generates shade with its tail", but this could be a folk etymology, and it might actually be Pre-Greek, if the <i>-ουρος</i> (-ouros) is a suffix rather than a standalone word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff, stick, crutch</li></ol><p>This word is built like <i>δόλων</i> (dólōn), <i>κῡ́φων</i> (kū́phōn) and other tool names, and almost identical with Latin <i>scīpiō</i> (“staff”). The further similarity with <i>σκηπάνη</i> (skēpánē, “staff”) and <i>σκῆπτρον</i> (skêptron, “staff”) has been observed for a long time; however the forms cannot be combined under one pre-form. It is unlikely that <i>σκίμπτομαι</i> (skímptomai, “to press forward”) is a denominative nasal present (like <i>σκήπτομαι</i> (skḗptomai) from <i>σκᾶπος</i> (skâpos)). Further connection is considered with the semantically unclear <i>σκοῖπος</i> (skoîpos, “supporting beam on which the tiles rest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasol, canopy, held in processions in Skiron in honour of the goddess Athena Skiras</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly reflecting a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḱeyr-o-</i> (“shine, reflex”), whence also Albanian <i>hir</i> (“heavenly grace”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skīriz</i> (“pure, lucid”), and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ščirъ</i> (“sincere, frank”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to frisk, frolic, skip, dance</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. LIV connects Welsh <i>cerdded</i> (“to walk”), suggesting a derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sker-</i> (“to jump”), however this is not ascertained. Note also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;skorъ</i> (“fast”), which may also be from this root, if it exists.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left, on or with the left hand</li><li>western, westward</li><li>unlucky, ill-omened, mischievous</li><li>awkward, clumsy, stupid</li><li>(<i>of serpents</i>) aslant, crooked</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;σκαιϝός</i> (&#42;skaiwós). Formally and semantically identical to Latin <i>scaevus</i> (“left”), both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skeh₂iwos</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mischievous device</li></ol><p>From <i>σκαιωρέω</i> (skaiōréō, “to devise mischievously”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) fruit shell of the artichoke</li></ol><p>Frisk connects the word with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skaljō</i> (“shell”) but Furnée rather adduces <i>ἀσκαλία</i> (askalía) and <i>ἀσκάληρον</i> (askálēron), which would point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of speckled waterbird</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variation <i>σκ-/κ-</i> may point to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoe or shovel</li></ol><p>From <i>σκάλλω</i> (skállō, “to stir up, hoe”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uneven, unequal, rough, rugged, slanting</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;of numbers) odd</li><li>(geometry,&#32;of triangles) having unequal sides, scalene</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(geometry,&#32;of cones) oblique</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i> (“to curve, bend”) or from <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to cut, carve, split”); see <i>σκάλλω</i> (skállō, “to stir up, hoe”), from which the term is probably derived, for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pin or thole</li></ol><p>From <i>σκάλλω</i> (skállō, “to stir up, to hoe”). Compare <i>σκάλμη</i> (skálmē, “knife”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, bent</li><li>bow-legged</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, this word cannot be separated from <i>σκιμβός</i> (skimbós, “lame”) and the variation α/ι as well as the derivatives with "-υκ-" and "-αλ-" point to a Pre-Greek origin. Alternatively from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ḱamb-</i> (“bent, crooked”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scammony</li></ol><p>Unknown. Noting its byform <i>κάμων</i> (kámōn)—attested in the <i>Alexipharmaca</i>, 484, of the Anatolian-born Nicander—, Kretschmer explained it as the same word as the Semitic borrowing <i>κύμινον</i> (kúminon, “cumin”), considering that cumin is also purgative—said also stimulant, laxative, even abortive—in large quantities, proferring Neo-Greek examples for this <i>σκ-&#47;κ-</i> (sk-&#47;k-) variation. This theory appears to be supported by Frisk. Beekes cares not about this variation and instead adduces the variation <i>ασκ-&#47;σκ-</i> (ask-&#47;sk-) as an indicator of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>σκαμμωνίᾱ</i> (skammōníā): scammony</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) bench</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) stool</li></ol><p>Diminutive of Koine Greek <i>σκάμνον</i> (skámnon), borrowed from Latin <i>scamnum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stick in a trap on which the bait is placed, and which, when touched by the animal, springs up and shuts the trap</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) setting word-traps during a discussion</li></ol><p>From <i>σκᾰ́νδᾰλον</i> (skắndălon, “trap, snare”) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snare, cause to stumble</li><li>to give offense</li></ol><p>From <i>σκᾰ́νδᾰλον</i> (skắndălon) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spade, mattock, digging tool</li><li>spadework, digging</li></ol><p>From <i>σκάπτω</i> (skáptō, “to dig, delve”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, instrumental nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tug of war at the Dionysia</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dug, that can be dug</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective, from <i>σκάπτω</i> (skáptō, “to dig, delve”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feces-eating, coprophagous</li></ol><p>From <i>σκατο-</i> (skato-) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>digger, delver</li></ol><p>From <i>σκάπτω</i> (skáptō, “to dig, delve”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spade, hoe, mattock</li><li>concave mirror used as burning-glass</li></ol><p>From <i>σκάφος</i> (skáphos, “digging, hoeing”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bowl-shaped, hollow</li></ol><p>From <i>σκάφη</i> (skáphē, “bowl”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-form, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a horse</i>) possessing skewed or deviating hooves</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to scatter, disperse</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kd-n-eh₂</i> (“to split”); Beekes cites Avestan <i>𐬯𐬗𐬌𐬥𐬛𐬀𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (scindaiie<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti, “breaks, destroys”) as a particularly close cognate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dried up, withered</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dried body, mummy</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) skeleton</li></ol><p>From <i>σκέλλω</i> (skéllō, “to dry up, parch”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover, shelter</li></ol><p>From <i>σκέπας</i> (sképas) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pier</li></ol><p>Diminutive of <i>σκέπαρνον</i> (sképarnon) with suffix <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>adverbial use</i>) it is to be discussed or investigated</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>something that must be considered, noteworthy</li><li>someone who must consider</li></ol><p><i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai, “to consider”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, verbal adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>something that must be considered, noteworthy</li><li>someone who must consider</li></ol><p><i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai, “to consider”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, verbal adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thoughtful, inquiring</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;in the plural,&#32;substantive) a sceptic, a member of the school of thought founded by Pyrrho of Elis</li></ol><p>From <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai, “to consider”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange, make ready, prepare</li><li>to collect</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to furnish, supply</li><li>to cheat, cozen</li></ol><p>From either <i>σκεῦος</i> (skeûos, “vessel”) or <i>σκευή</i> (skeuḗ, “equipment”) + <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō), both ultimately of the same origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equipment, attire, apparel, dress, trappings</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fashion, style of dress or equipment</li></ol><li>tackle, as of a net or ship</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>synonym of <i>αἰδοῖον</i> (aidoîon, “privy parts”, “pudenda”)</li></ol></ol><p>Cognate with <i>σκεῦος</i> (skeûos, “vessel, device”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chest for all kinds of objects</li></ol><p>From <i>σκεῦος</i> (skeûos, “vessel”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storekeeper</li></ol><p>From <i>σκεῦος</i> (skeûos, “equipment, utensil”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fabrication, fraud</li></ol><p>From <i>σκευωρέω</i> (skeuōréō, “to examine, to intrigue, to obtain by fraud”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vessel or implement</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) outfit, gear, utensils, chattels, equipment, baggage, luggage, tackle</li><li>an inanimate object, a thing</li></ol><li>the body, as the vessel of the soul</li><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) genitalia</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kew-</i> (“to perform, commit or to cut, hew, equip”), and compared with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hawjaną</i> (“to carry out, perform”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kutiti</i> (“to devise, whirl”). Other theories which derive the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱewH-</i> (“to push”), comparing to Lithuanian <i>šáuti</i> (“to shoot, shove”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sovati</i> (“to shove”), are semantically unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tent</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) stage</li></ol><p>Usually said to be a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ḱeh₃-</i> (“shade, shadow”), originally referring to a light construction of cloth hung between tree branches to provide</p><p>a shadow which could be used for shelter and other activities, though the vocalism is difficult to explain as full grade <i>&#42;sḱeh₃-</i> gives <i>σκω</i> (skō) and zero grade <i>&#42;sḱh₃-</i> gives <i>σκο</i> (sko).</p><p>Alternatively, possibly a Semitic loanword related to Hebrew <i>שָׁכַן</i> (shakhán) and Arabic سَكَنَ (sakana, “to dwell, reside, inhabit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scene-painter</li></ol><p>From <i>σκηνή</i> (skēnḗ, “tent; stage”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scene painting</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) illusion</li></ol><p>From <i>σκηνογράφος</i> (skēnográphos, “scene painter”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A tent store</li></ol><p>From <i>σκηνή</i> (skēnḗ, “tent”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>construction of tents</li><li>(<i>religious</i>) Sukkot, the Feast of Tabernacles, and related holidays</li></ol><p>from <i>σκηνή</i> (skēnḗ, “tent”) + <i>πηγία</i> (pēgía, “fastening”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard of tents, of a camp</li></ol><p>From <i>σκηνή</i> (skēnḗ, “tent, booth”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pitch tents, encamp</li><li>to dwell (<i>originally, in a tent; later, generally</i>)</li><li>to settle, take up one’s abode</li><li>(<i>in the perfect passive</i>) to live or be</li><li>to occupy with tents</li></ol><p>From <i>σκηνή</i> (skēnḗ) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearing a staff, baton or sceptre, as the badge of command</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wand-bearer, a great officer in the Persian court, generally a eunuch</li><li>(<i>in Ephesus</i>) beadle</li></ol><p>From <i>σκήπτω</i> (skḗptō, “to prop, stay”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to bear, carry, bring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thunderbolt, lightning strike</li><li>dust-storm, hurricane</li></ol><p>From <i>σκήπτω</i> (skḗptō, “to let fall, hurl upon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prop, fix, plant firmly</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to support oneself, uphold oneself</li></ol><p>Expressive cross of <i>σκήπτω</i> (skḗptō, “to prop, stay”) +&lrm; <i>στηρίζω</i> (stērízō, “to be firmly set or fixed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shadow</li><li>shade (shelter from the sun)</li><li>shade (spirit of someone dead)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ḱeh₃ih₂</i> (“shadow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sunshade, parasol</li><li>sort of broad-brimmed hat</li><li>carriage with a tilt or hood</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) umbel of umbelliferous plants</li></ol><p>From <i>σκιάς</i> (skiás, “canopy or arbour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overshadow [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>painting</i>) to shade [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>σκιά</i> (skiá, “shadow”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canopy or arbour, in form like a sunshade</li><li>(<i>at Sparta</i>) rotunda in which the assemblies of the people were held</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) umbel of plants</li></ol><p>From <i>σκιά</i> (skiá, “shadow”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overshadow (<i>literally</i>), make shady [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>σκιά</i> (skiá) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>perspective-painter, scene-painter</li></ol><p>From <i>σκιά</i> (skiá, “shadow”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying a sunshade</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) shading, shady</li></ol><p>From <i>σκῐᾰ́ς</i> (skĭắs, “sunshade”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fighting against a shadow: esp. a form of exercise with hands and feet</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) fighting with a shadow, mock fight, beating the air</li></ol><p>From <i>σκῐᾱ́</i> (skĭā́, “shadow”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle, combat”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Laconian,&#32;epithet of Apollo) Skiastes; he who casts (a) shadow</li><li>a servant who carries an umbrella</li></ol><p>From <i>σκιάζω</i> (skiázō, “to overshadow”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀλώπηξ</i> (alṓpēx, “fox”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek. He further connects <i>κίδαφος</i> (kídaphos, “wily, cunning, sly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shady, giving shade, in the shade</li><li>dark coloured</li><li>faint</li></ol><p>From <i>σκιᾱ́</i> (skiā́) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>halt, lame</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the relation with <i>σκαμβός</i> (skambós, “bent”) points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musical instrument with four strings</li><li>word without meaning, so-and-so</li></ol><p>A foreign word like <i>κιθάρα</i> (kithára), <i>βάρβιτος</i> (bárbitos) and many other instrument names. It is of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the interchange <i>σ-/zero</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of foals</i>) to spring, leap, bound</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be skittish, unruly</li></ol><p>Iterative-intensive formation in <i>-τάω</i> from <i>σκαίρω</i> (skaírō, “to skip, dance”), which is probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sker-</i> (“to jump”). The <i>-ι-</i> is thought to be a secondary prop vowel, but this makes the etymology formally less certain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) shady (causing shade)</li></ol><p>From <i>σκιᾱ́</i> (skiā́, “shade”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hardening</li></ol><p>From <i>σκληρόω</i> (sklēróō, “to harden”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis); also interpretable as <i>σκληρός</i> (sklērós, “hard”) +&lrm; <i>-ωσις</i> (-ōsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard</li><li>harsh</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelh₁-</i> (“to parch, wither”) + <i>-ρός</i> (-rós). See also <i>σκέλλω</i> (skéllō, “to dry up, parch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>σκληρός</i> (sklērós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hardness, rigidity</li><li>stubbornness</li><li>harshness, austerity</li></ol><p>From <i>σκληρός</i> (sklērós, “hard”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of insect that infests fig and oak trees and devours the gallfly</li></ol><p>See <i>κνίψ</i> (kníps).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel or receptacle of some kind</li></ol><p>Maybe from Pre-Greek and related to <i>κόϊξ</i> (kóïx, “doum palm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) obliquity, bending</li></ol><p>From <i>σκολιόω</i> (skolióō, “to bend”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis), from <i>σκολιός</i> (skoliós, “curved, bent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curved, bent</li><li>winding (of rivers and paths)</li><li>twisted, tangled</li><li>crooked, unjust, unrighteous</li><li>riddling, obscure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i> (“crooked”), the same root of Latin <i>scelus</i> (“curse”), German <i>scheel</i> (“squinting”), Dutch <i>scheel</i> and Albanian <i>çalë</i> (“lame”). Synchronically analyzed as an adjectival formation from <i>σκέλος</i> (skélos, “leg”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hart's tongue (<i>Asplenium scolopendrium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σκολόπενδρα</i> (skolópendra, “scolopendra”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodcock (<i>Scolopax rusticola</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin. The resemblance with <i>σκόλοψ</i> (skólops, “pole”) might be due to folk-etymological adaptation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millipede</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the sea scolopendra, an animal from the genus <i>Nereis</i></li></ol></ol><p>Suggested to be a combination of <i>σκόλοψ</i> (skólops, “thorn”) and <i>ἔντερον</i> (énteron, “earthworm”), but Beekes finds this unconvincing and suggests Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) (<i>according to Hesychius of Alexandria:</i>) sullen, sad</li></ol><p>Unknown. Furnée compares this word with <i>σκολύφρα</i> (skolúphra, “hard, harsh”) and suggests a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the variation <i>-βρ-/-φρ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look, look at, behold; examine, inspect</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to contemplate, consider</li><li>to look for</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) like active, perhaps implying a more deliberate consideration</li></ol><p>From <i>σκοπός</i> (skopós, “watcher”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḱop-</i>, the <i>o</i>-grade of <i>&#42;sḱep-</i>, a metathesis of <i>&#42;speḱ-</i>, from which also comes <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai, “to consider, think, speculate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vantage point, a lookout, a watchtower</li></ol><p>From <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lookout place</li><li>mountain peak</li><li>height or highest point of anything</li><li>watchtower</li><li>lookout (session of watching), watch (act of guarding)</li></ol><p>From <i>σκοπός</i> (skopós, “lookout, watcher”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾱ́</i> (-iā́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>σκοπιᾱ́</i> (skopiā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watcher, lookout</li><li>protector, guardian</li><li>spy, scout</li><li>mark, target</li><li>goal, aim</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai, “to observe”) + <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>scatter, disperse</li><li>disintegrate, reduce to powder</li><li>dissipate</li></ol><p>From <i>σκορπίος</i> (skorpíos, “scorpion”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, “-ize”, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jointfir (<i>Ephedra distachya</i>)</li><li>squirting cucumber (<i>Ecballium elaterium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σκορπίος</i> (skorpíos, “scorpion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scorpion</li><li>scorpionfish (<i>Scorpaena scrofa</i>)</li><li>scorpion furze (<i>Genista acanthoclada</i>)</li><li>scorpion root (<i>Doronicum orientale</i>)</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Scorpio, a constellation</li><li>engine of war which discharged arrows</li></ol><p>Sometimes suggested to be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut”), but Beekes notes that it is more likely a Pre-Greek loanword instead, with a pre-form along the lines of <i>&#42;karaᵐp-</i>, and possibly related to <i>κᾱ́ρᾰβος</i> (kā́răbos, “beetle, crustacean”), <i>κερᾰ́μβῠξ</i> (kerắmbŭx, “longhorn beetle”), <i>κηρᾰφῐ́ς</i> (kērăphĭ́s, “a kind of locust”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heliotrope (<i>Heliotropium europaeum</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>σκορπῐ́ουρος</i> (skorpĭ́ouros, “scorpion-tailed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scorpion's-tail (<i>Scorpiurus muricatus</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>σκορπῐοειδής</i> (skorpĭoeidḗs, “scorpion-like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of heliotrope (<i>Heliotropium suaveolens</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σκορπίος</i> (skorpíos, “scorpion”) +&lrm; <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō, “to kill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness, gloom</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) scotia, cavetto, so called from the dark shadow it casts</li></ol><p>From <i>σκότος</i> (skótos, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, obscure, dim</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dark, obscure, secret</li></ol><p>From <i>σκότος</i> (skótos, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-εινός</i> (-einós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>I blind</li><li>I stupefy</li><li><i>(passive voice)</i> I am in vertigo</li></ol><p>From the o-stem variant of <i>σκότος</i> (skótos, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be angry, to rage, grumble</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>σκύζομαι</i> (skúzomai, “to be angry”) and <i>σκυθρός</i> (skuthrós, “angry, sullen”), with further origin uncertain. Beekes rejects the traditional comparison with Lithuanian <i>skùsti</i> (“to become nervous, tired”) (see also <i>skaudė́ti</i> (“to hurt”)) and Latvian <i>skundêt</i> (“to grumble, grudge”), as their accentuation points to <i>&#42;-dʰ-</i> in the Proto-Indo-European form, which cannot produce the Greek term.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strip a slain enemy of his arms; to despoil</li><li>[<i>with</i> accusative <i>and</i> genitive] strip the arms off an enemy</li></ol><p>From <i>σκῦλᾰ</i> (skûlă, “war booty”), the latter according to Beekes, from σκύλλω (skúllō, “to flay”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tan hides</li></ol><p>Related to σκῦλα (skûla, “hide, skin”) and δέφω (déphō, “to soften”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A staff, a cudgel, a club</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) A scytale, a baton used in Sparta as a cypher for writing dispatches</li><li>A pole or staff like those of a sedan chair</li><li>A strickle for levelling grain</li><li>A wooden tally or ticket on a money bag</li><li>A rod of metal or ivory</li><li>A scourge whip</li><li>A handle or lever in a machine</li><li>A handspike for turning a wheel</li><li>A sucker from a stem</li><li>A serpent of uniform roundness and thickness</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) A phalanx</li></ol><p>The origin is unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clubbing, bludgeoning, cudgeling, the act or an instance of beating someone or something with a club (σκυτάλη), especially (<i>historical</i>) the Scytalism, the mass clubbing of wealthy Argives in 370&nbsp;BC.</li></ol><p>From <i>σκυτάλη</i> (skutálē) or <i>σκύταλον</i> (skútalon, “club”) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós, -ism: <i>forming abstract nouns</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leather-cutter, worker in leather</li><li>shoemaker, cobbler</li></ol><p>From <i>σκῦτος</i> (skûtos, “hide, skin”) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut, separate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) slag, dross of metal, scoria</li></ol><p>From <i>σκῶρ</i> (skôr, “dung”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightstool; chamber pot</li></ol><p>From <i>σκῶρ</i> (skôr, “dung; excrement; feces”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓μῐ́ς</i> (ămĭ́s, “chamber pot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song which went round crookedly at banquets, being sung to the lyre by the guests one after another in irregular order</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>σκολιός</i> (skoliós, “crooked”), because of the crooked order of the singers. Another theory derives the word from <i>δύσκολος</i> (dúskolos, “troublesome, difficult”), because it was said that the songs were easy, but appeared difficult to drunken revellers.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A pale, a stake; a palisade</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) An instrument for operating on the urethra</li><li>The point of a fishing hook</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Often derived from <i>σκάλλω</i> (skállō, “to stir, hoe”) or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelh₃p-</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)kelh₃-</i> (“to cut”), and so related to Latin <i>scalpō</i> (“to scratch”), English <i>shelf</i> and Lithuanian <i>sklempti</i> (“to plane”).</p><p>Beekes &amp; Klein dismiss an Indo-European origin and instead suggest Pre-Greek, due to the variant <i>σκόλοφρον</i> (skólophron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἐσθιόμενος βολβός</i> (esthiómenos bolbós, literally “edible tassel hyacinth”).</li></ol><p>Like <i>σκόλυμος</i> (skólumos, “kind of thistle”), from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stool</li></ol><p>In view of the variant, it is probably a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common golden thistle (<i>Scolymus hispanicus</i>)</li></ol><p>Explained as of Pre-Greek origin. Compare also the Serbo-Croatian <i>skolab</i>, <i>skulob</i> of the same meaning, a term the Slavs could only have picked up, from Liburnian or its Vulgar Latin successor dialect, when reaching the Adriatic coast.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mackerel (<i>Scomber scombrus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σκόμβρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scombrus mountain; Vitosha, Bulgaria</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;skumbras</i>, <i>&#42;skumbras</i> (“height, hill, small mountain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) projecting coping, eaves</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lookout place</li><li>peak; headland, promontory</li><li>watchtower</li></ol><p>Maybe related to <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai, “to look at”), if the original meaning of the word is “watchtower”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>σκόροδον</i> (skórodon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garlic</li></ol><p>A Mediterranean substrate word of the shape <i>&#42;skʰodoro</i>, found also in Old Armenian <i>խստոր</i> (xstor, “garlic”) and Albanian <i>hudhër</i> (“garlic”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scorpionfish</li></ol><p>From <i>σκορπίος</i> (skorpíos, “scorpion”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness, gloom</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the darkness of death</li><li>the darkness of the netherworld</li><li>the darkness of the womb</li></ol><li>blindness</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) obscurity</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) the mystery, ignorance, deceit</li><li>the dark part or shadow in a picture</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skh₃tos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ḱeh₃-</i> (“shadow, darkness”). Cognates include Old Irish <i>scáth</i>, Sanskrit <i>छत्त्र</i> (chattra, “umbrella”), and Old English <i>sceadu</i> (English <i>shadow</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) (<i>According to Hesychius of Alexandria:</i>) charlock (<i>Brassica arvensis</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested in Hesychius. The alternative form σκοῦβα probably shows that this word also existed in another (non-Attic) dialect where the shift /u/ &gt; /y/ did not take place, with <ου> used to represent the old /u/ sound.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, excrement, manure</li><li>refuse, offal</li></ol><p>Neumann compares this word with Hittite (išḫuwai-, “to throw, scatter, pour”), and Furnée with <i>κύπελλον</i> (kúpellon, “whatever dough and bread is left over on the table”). If the last theory is correct, then the word could be of Pre-Greek origin. However, while <i>κύπελλον</i> (kúpellon) provides only superficial similarities (note the differences in the consonants), a non-Hittite Anatolian form <i>*šḫuwai</i> provides a perfect match. The immediate Anatolian source cannot be identified more precisely, since neither the phonology nor the morphology is specific enough. Possible candidates are Luwian, Lydian or Palaic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young dog, whelp, puppy</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>dog</li><li>(<i>of other young animals</i>) whelp</li></ol><li>a chain, a chain or collar for the neck</li><li>(<i>sex</i>) a kind of erotic dance or sex position</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Compare <i>κύλλα</i> (kúlla, “puppy (hapax)”), <i>σκύλιον</i> (skúlion, “dogfish”), <i>σκύλλα</i> (skúlla, “shark”), as well as Old Armenian <i>ցուլ</i> (cʻul, “bull”), Old Irish <i>cuilén</i> (“puppy”), Lithuanian <i>kalė̃</i> (“bitch, female dog”), and Russian <i>клей</i> (klej). A Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kol-</i> (“animal”) is possible, but problematic. Based on the phonetic issues with connecting the similar words mentioned above, Beekes tentatively takes the word as Pre-Greek; it is possible that some, all, or none of the cognates mentioned above are taken from the same substrate continuum.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dogfish</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>σκύλαξ</i> (skúlax, “puppy”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tear apart, lacerate, flay, skin</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to molest, maltreat</li><li>to trouble, pester</li><li>to infest, plunder</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kelH-</i> (“to cut”) (whence <i>σκάλλω</i> (skállō, “to stir up, search”)), although the vowel is hard to explain. Unrelated to <i>σκῦτος</i> (skûtos, “hide, skin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, hide</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewH-</i> (“to cover”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>स्कुनाति</i> (skunā́ti, “to cover”) and Old English <i>scēo</i> (“sky”) and <i>scuwa</i> (“shade, darkness, protection”). Related to <i>σκῦτος</i> (skûtos, “hide, leather”) and <i>ἐπισκύνιον</i> (episkúnion, “skin over the brows”).</p><p>Alternatively, Beekes suggests derivation from <i>σκύλλω</i> (skúllō, “I lacerate, skin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cub, whelp, especially a lion's whelp</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(poetic,&#32;of men or women)</li></ol></ol><p>The word has been connected with <i>σκύλαξ</i> (skúlax, “puppy”), but the variation of the suffix remains unclear on this account. The suffix "-μν-" could point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin above the eyebrows</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewH-</i> (“to cover”) and belongs together with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skūrō</i> (“shelter, shack”), Latin <i>obscūrus</i> (“dark, obscure”), so that an <i>r/n-</i>stem is supposed. With a suffix <i>-l-</i>, there is <i>σκύλος</i> (skúlos, “animal's skin, hide”), <i>σκῦλα</i> (skûla, “spolia”). Perhaps the root is seen in Sanskrit <i>स्कुनाति</i> (skunāti, “he covers, protects”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>σκυτάλη</i> (skutálē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”).</li></ol><p>Furnée compares this word, together with <i>σκύτα</i> (skúta, “neck, throat”), to <i>κοττίς</i> (kottís, “occiput”) and <i>προκόττα</i> (prokótta, “forelock”) and assumes a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leathern, made of leather</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) skin and bones, gaunt, scrawny</li></ol><p>From <i>σκῦτος</i> (skûtos, “leather, skin, hide”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cup or can, especially one used by peasants</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of wooden milk-vessels</li></ol><li>(<i>perhaps</i>) a skull</li></ol><p>Unknown, perhaps Pre-Greek. Compare <i>σκάφος</i> (skáphos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm</li></ol><p>Usually derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kel-</i> (“to curve, bend”), like <i>σκέλος</i> (skélos, “leg”) and <i>σκολιός</i> (skoliós, “bent; crooked”). The suffix <i>-ηκ-</i> (-ēk-) is also found in <i>σφήξ</i> (sphḗx, “wasp”) and <i>μύρμηξ</i> (múrmēx, “ant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mock, jeer, scoff at</li><li>(<i>in good sense</i>) to joke with, jest</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, an unexplained formation that must be recent in the prehistory of Greek. The root may be that of <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai, “to look about”) or <i>σκάπτω</i> (skáptō, “to dig”), but neither is immediately obvious. Perhaps the verb is related to <i>σκώψ</i> (skṓps, “little horned owl”).</p><p>An alternative theory by Kroonen derives the word from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skep-</i> (“to mock”), and compares Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skuppōną</i> (“to mock, scoff”), though this is less certain, particularly when considering that the Greek is attested rather late.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small owl</li></ol><p>Usually connected to the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;speḱ-</i> (“to look”), whence also <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai).</p><p>Beekes claims that this is Pre-Greek based on an assumption that it could be related to the Macedonian dialectal <i>γώψ</i> (gṓps, “jackdaw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch; name of a certain wind</li></ol><p>Akin to <i>σκῆπτρον</i> (skêptron), <i>σκᾶπτον</i> (skâpton, “staff, scepter”). Compare also <i>σκηπάνη</i> (skēpánē), <i>σκηπάνιον</i> (skēpánion, “stick, scepter”), <i>σκαπάνιον</i> (skapánion, “staff, crutch”), and Latin <i>scāpus</i>, Albanian <i>shkop</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a staff, stick to lean on, used by lame or aged persons</li><li>a staff, baton, especially as the badge of command, sceptre</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>royalty, kingly power, rule</li></ol><li>(<i>in LXX</i>) tribe</li></ol><p>From <i>σκήπτω</i> (skḗptō) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretext, plea, excuse, pretence</li><li>plea in a lawcourt</li></ol><p>From <i>σκήπτω</i> (skḗptō, “to allege, pretend”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σκῆψις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scepsis, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A scrub, hard land overgrown with bushes</li><li>(<i>oncology</i>) A hardened swelling or tumour</li><li>An induration, a callus</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, from Pre-Greek and related to <i>σκῦρος</i> (skûros, “chippings of stone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) spoils; weapons stripped off a slain enemy</li><li>booty, plunder; prey</li></ol><p>According to Beekes from the same root of <i>σκύλλω</i> (skúllō, “to lacerate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chippings of stone used as road metal</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, from Pre-Greek and related to <i>σκῖρος</i> (skîros, “hardened swelling, tumour”). Perhaps the island name Σκῦρος (Skûros) is related to σκῦρος (skûros) due to the marble quarries.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σκῦρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scyrus; Skyros, one of the Sporades islands in Greece.</li></ol><p>From σκῦρος (skûros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, hide, especially dressed or tanned</li><li>leather thong, whip</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewH-</i> (“to cover”). Cognates include Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hūdiz</i> (“hide”), Latin <i>cutis</i> (“skin”) and Lithuanian <i>kiáutas</i> (“shell, case, envelop”). Related also to <i>σκύνιον</i> (skúnion, “skin of the brows”) and <i>κύτος</i> (kútos, “vessel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pointed stake</li><li>thorn, prickle</li></ol><p>The word resembles <i>σκόλοψ</i> (skólops, “palisade”) and, outside Greek, Albanian <i>hell</i> (“icicle; skewer”), Lithuanian <i>kuõlas</i> (“pole”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kolъ</i> (“stake”). Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₂-</i> (“to hew”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σκῶλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Scolus, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jest, gibe, joke</li></ol><p>From <i>σκώπτω</i> (skṓptō, “to mock, to jeer”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung; excrement; feces</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;skṓr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḱṓr</i>, the collective of <i>&#42;sóḱr̥</i> (“excrement, dung”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skarną</i> (“filth, dung”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emerald</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Semitic language, related to Hebrew <i>בָּרֶקֶת</i> (baréket, “emerald, flashing gem”), Akkadian <i>𒄭𒄭</i> (barāqum, literally “shining”), Arabic بَرْق (barq, “lightning, flashing, shining, dazzling”) and loanwords of Semitic origin such as Sanskrit <i>मरकत</i> (marakata) and Old Armenian <i>զմրուխտ</i> (zmruxt).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;often&#32;middle voice) to wash with soap</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smeh₂-</i> (“to rub”) (which Beekes and Chantraine reconstruct as <i>&#42;smeh₁-</i>). Compare Latin <i>macula</i> (“stain, blot”) (&lt; <i>&#42;smh₂-tlom</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scrub; to wipe off by help of soap or unguent</li><li>to clear off by help of lotions or salves</li><li>to purge away</li><li>to soap a person, wash him with soap or unguent</li></ol><p>From <i>σμάω</i> (smáō, “to wipe, clean”), with a velar enlargement <i>-χ</i> (-kh).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tool for cutting or carving: knife, chisel</li></ol><p>The word has traditionally been connected with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smiþaz</i> (“smith”), and if related, then from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smī-</i> (“to cut, hew”); however, this etymology is shaky. Alternatively, a Pre-Greek etymology is possible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that acts like a scalpel</li></ol><p>From <i>σμῑ́λη</i> (smī́lē, “scalpel, chisel”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouse, field mouse, vole</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, either from Pre-Greek or Anatolian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to emerald</li></ol><p>From <i>σμᾰ́ρᾰγδος</i> (smắrăgdos) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of emerald</li><li>emerald green</li></ol><p>From <i>σμᾰ́ρᾰγδος</i> (smắrăgdos, “emerald”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>picarel (<i>Spicara smaris</i>)</li></ol><p>A connection with <i>σμηρίζω</i> (smērízō, “to plane, to polish”) is implausible. Furnée, instead, convincingly compares the word with <i>σπάρος</i> (spáros, “annular sea bream”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crash</li><li>to drone, roar, thunder</li></ol><p>Possibly of onomatopoeic origin. The suffix in -γ- is found in semantically close forms, such as <i>λαλαγή</i> (lalagḗ), <i>πάταγος</i> (pátagos) and <i>οἰμωγή</i> (oimōgḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>terribly, fearsomely</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible to look upon</li><li>terrible to hear</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)merd-</i> (“to bite, sting”), cognate with Old English <i>&#42;smeortan</i> (English <i>smart</i>), Sanskrit <i>मर्दति</i> (mardati, “press, crush, destroy”), and Latin <i>mordeō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>terrible, dreadful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)merd-</i> (“to bite, sting”), cognate with Old English <i>&#42;smeortan</i> (English <i>smart</i>), Sanskrit <i>मर्दति</i> (mardati, “press, crush, destroy”), and Latin <i>mordeō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) purgative, cleaning, detersive</li></ol><p>From <i>σμήχω</i> (smḗkhō) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A box of unguents</li></ol><p>From <i>σμῆμα</i> (smêma, “soap, unguent”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>μῑκρός</i> (mīkrós, “small, little”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of holm oak wood</li></ol><p>From <i>σμῖλαξ</i> (smîlax, “holm oak; yew”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>two-pronged mattock</li></ol><p>The word is generally considered to derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;smei-</i> (“to carve, cut”), as could be <i>σμίλη</i> (smílē, “knife, wood-carving knife, scalpel, chisel”). Compare Lithuanian <i>smailus</i> (“sharp, acute”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smiþaz</i> (“carpenter, craftsman, smith”). According to others, both words are of obscure etymology and thus could be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure of wine in Cyrenaica</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of myrrh</li></ol><p><i>σμῠ́ρνᾰ</i> (smŭ́rnă, “myrrh”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “of or pertaining to”, suffix forming adjectives)</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σμυρναῖος)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Smyrna, Smyrnaean</li></ol><p><i>Σμύρνα</i> (Smúrna) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>emery-powder, used by lapidaries</li></ol><p>A Semitic borrowing, whence also comes Egyptian <i>jsmr</i> (“emery”), later <i>smr</i> (“emery”). Cognate to Hebrew <i>שָׁמִיר</i> (šāmī́r, “emery, corund”), Aramaic <i>שָׁמִירָא</i> (šāmīrā) / <i>ܫܡܝܪܐ</i> (šāmīrā), Arabic سَامُور (sāmūr, “corundum”), possibly stemming from or at least related to Akkadian <i>𒉌𒌓𒌑</i> (<sup>NA₄</sup>U<sub>2</sub> /⁠šammu⁠/, “emery, corund, stone used to cut hard precious gems, entitled the king of the stones for its mastery over all other stones”, literally “herb-stone, stone-medicine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>myrrh, the gum of <i>Commiphora myrrha</i></li><li>Indian bdellium, false myrrh, the resin of <i>Commiphora wightii</i></li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from the city of <i>Σμύρνα</i> (Smúrna, “Izmir”), itself apparently of unknown origin, and not a by-form of <i>μύρρα</i> (múrrha, “myrrh”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σμύρνα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Smyrna; İzmir, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of myrrh</li><li>myrrh-colored</li></ol><p>From <i>σμύρνα</i> (smúrna, “myrrh”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan alexanders (<i>Smyrnium perfoliatum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σμύρνα</i> (smúrna, “myrrh”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soap</li><li>unguent</li></ol><p>From <i>σμήχω</i> (smḗkhō, “to wipe; to soap off”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soap; a substance for cleaning and degreasing</li></ol><p>From <i>σμᾰ́ω</i> (smắō, “to wipe; to cleanse”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă). Compare typologically <i>ῥῠ́μμα</i> (rhŭ́mma) &lt; <i>ῥῠ́πτω</i> (rhŭ́ptō, “to cleanse, to wash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beehive, skep</li><li>swarm of bees</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>swarm in general, crowd</li></ol></ol><p>The formation can be compared with <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos), <i>ἔρνος</i> (érnos) and <i>τέμενος</i> (témenos). An alternative spelling <i>ζμῆνος</i> (zmênos) is also attested in some papyri and inscriptions, possibly reflecting the voicing of initial /s/ to /z/. According to Beekes, a Pre-Greek origin is a good possibility.</p><p>The original meaning was probably swarm of bees rather than beehive.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holm oak (<i>Quercus ilex</i>)</li><li>yew (<i>Taxus baccata</i>)</li><li>smilax (<i>Smilax aspera</i>)</li><li>bindweed (<i>Convolvulus</i> spp.)</li><li>cowpea (<i>Vigna unguiculata</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the phonetic variants listed above in the "Alternative forms" section prove Pre-Greek origin for this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of eel</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>μύραινα</i> (múraina, “moray eel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weal, swollen bruise</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>σμῆν</i> (smên) and <i>σμώχω</i> (smṓkhō, “to rub”) is formally and semantically unconvincing. It is rather a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scare away</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to drive away</li></ol><li>to move quickly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(passive voice,&#32;figurative) to be agitated, excited</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) walk pompously, strut, swagger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tsogʷéyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tyogʷéyeti</i>, from <i>&#42;tyegʷ-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-éyeti</i>. See also <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai, “to awe at”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rushing, violent</li><li>swaggering, pompous, haughty</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) rousing, imposing, impressive</li></ol><p>From <i>σοβ(έω)</i> (sob(éō), “to scare away”) +&lrm; <i>-αρός</i> (-arós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak or write incorrectly, commit a solecism, solecize</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be guilty of an absurdity</li></ol><li>to behave boorishly, violate principles of etiquette</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>σόλοικος</i> (sóloikos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō). Beekes suggests that the term was formed from <i>Σόλοι</i> (Sóloi) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō), as the residents of the city of Soli in Cilicia were known for speaking an improper form of Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>solecism</li></ol><p>From <i>σολοικίζω</i> (soloikízō) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of shoe</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>σόλιον</i> (sólion, “slipper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spongy, porous, full of tiny pores that allow fluids or gasses to pass through</li></ol><p>Seemingly related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;swammaz</i> (“sponge; mushroom, fungus”), with which it is traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swombʰ-</i> (“sponge; tree-fungus”), but the Greek probably cannot be of inherited Indo-European origin given its lack of the expected shift <i>*s-</i> &gt; <i>*h-</i>. According to Beekes, it is probably of substrate or wanderwort origin and related to similar words like Ancient Greek <i>σπόγγος</i> (spóngos, “sponge”), Latin <i>fungus</i> (“mushroom, fungus”) and Old Armenian <i>սունկն</i> (sunkn, “tree fungus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἐλάτη παλαιά</i> (elátē palaiá, literally “old fir”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>σαρωνίς</i> (sarōnís, “old hollow oak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a vessel for holding human remains, specifically:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>urn for cremated remains</li><li>coffin</li></ol><li>a nickname for an old man or woman</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tworHos</i> (“seizer, encloser”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twerH-</i> (“to enclose”). Cognate to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvȏrъ</i> (“creature”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvoriti</i> (“to make”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>towel, napkin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>sudarium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skill in a craft or art</li><li>sound judgment; good sense</li><li>wisdom; higher learning</li></ol><p>From <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “skilled in handcrafts; clever”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σοφία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>personification of wisdom</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Wisdom</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a female given name, Sophia</li><li>(<i>Gnosticism</i>) Sophia (aeon of wisdom)</li></ol><p>Specialized use of <i>σοφίᾱ</i> (sophíā, “wisdom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make wise, instruct</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become wise, clever, skilled</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to teach oneself, learn</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to practice an art; to be subtle; to speculate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to devise skillfully</li><li>(<i>with accusative of person</i>) to deceive</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “skilled, wise”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>σόφισμα</i> (sóphisma)</li></ol><p>From <i>σοφίζω</i> (sophízō) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A master of one's craft</li><li>One who is wise, prudent, a philosopher</li><li>teacher, tutor</li><li>(slang,&#32;derogatory) One who makes a profit off of false wisdom: cheat, swindler</li></ol><p>From <i>σοφίζω</i> (sophízō, “to become wise”) +&lrm; <i>-ιστής</i> (-istḗs), from <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “wise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play the sophist, argue as one</li><li>to practise the profession of sophist, give lectures</li></ol><p>From <i>σοφῐστής</i> (sophĭstḗs, “sophist”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, “denominative verb-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a sophist</li><li>like a sophist, sophistical</li></ol><p>From <i>σοφιστής</i> (sophistḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clever, skillful, cunning, able</li><li>intelligent, wise, prudent</li></ol><p>Unknown. If related to <i>Σίσυφος</i> (Sísuphos) and <i>σαφής</i> (saphḗs, “speaking clearly”), then likely from a Pre-Greek substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>σοφός</i> (sophós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>σοφός</i> (sophós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of lilies</li></ol><p>From <i>σοῦσον</i> (soûson, “lily”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lily (plant of the genus <i>Lilium</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>zšn</i>. Compare also Biblical Hebrew <i>שׁוֹשַׁנָּה</i> (šōšannā, “lily”) and Coptic <i>ϣⲱϣⲉⲛ</i> (šōšen).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian name for a sacred crocodile seen as a living image of the god Sobek.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian Demotic Egyptian <i>sbk</i>, from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">k&#58;I3</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>sbk</i>, “Sobek”). For the pronunciation compare Coptic <i>ⲥⲟⲩⲕ</i> (souk), <i>ⲥⲟⲩⲭⲓ</i> (soukhi).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σοῦχος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Sobek (an ancient Egyptian deity associated with the crocodile)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Demotic Egyptian <i>sbk</i>, from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">k&#58;I3</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>sbk</i>, “Sobek”). For the pronunciation compare Coptic <i>ⲥⲟⲩⲕ</i> (souk), <i>ⲥⲟⲩⲭⲓ</i> (soukhi).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) branch torn off of a tree (<i>especially a palm frond</i>)</li></ol><p>A Semitic borrowing, related to Arabic سُبَاط (subāṭ), سُبَاطَة (subāṭa, “female inflorescence of the palm tree”), on which see Arabic سِبْط (sibṭ, “staff”), Armenian <i>ճիպոտ</i> (čipot, “cane; stick”) for more family. Connected by popular etymology to <i>σπᾰ́ω</i> (spắō, “to tear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eunuch</li></ol><p>From <i>σπᾰ́ω</i> (spắō, “to draw (a weapon)”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any broad blade, of wood or metal</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a flat wooden blade used by ancient weavers in their upright loom for striking the woof home</li><li>a spaddle or spattle used for stirring, especially for medical purposes</li><li>an oar blade</li><li>the broad ribs</li><li>the shoulder blade</li><li>the broad blade of a sword</li><li>a scraper for currying horses</li><li>the stem of a palm-frond</li><li>the spathe of the flower of many plants, especially palms</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;spátʰā</i>, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sph₂-dʰh₁-éh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)peh₂-</i> (“to draw”) (compare <i>σπάω</i> (spáō)) + <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to do; put”) + feminine ending <i>&#42;-eh₂</i>. Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spadô</i>, Hittite <i>𒅖𒉺𒀀𒋻</i> (išpatar, “spear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oven rake</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greater blind mole-rat, <i>Spalax microphthalmus</i></li><li>meadow saffron, <i>Colchicum parnassicum</i></li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rare, uncommon</li><li>scarce, scanty</li></ol><p>Adjectival formation from <i>σπάνις</i> (spánis, “rarity, scarcity”). Related to <i>σπᾰνός</i> (spănós, “rare, with scant beard growth”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rarity, scarcity</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rarity, scarcity</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece torn off, shred, fragment</li><li>tearing, rending</li></ol><p>From <i>σπαράσσω</i> (sparássō, “I rend, tear asunder”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band for swathing infants</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) swaddling clothes</li><li>objects left with an exposed child, the marks by which a person's true birth and family are identified</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;σπάργω</i> (&#42;spárgō, “to swaddle”), which is from the zero-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to twist”) with a <i>g</i>-extension (perhaps via contamination with a verb of similar meaning, like <i>εἴργω</i> (eírgō, “to enclose”)). Related to <i>σπεῖρον</i> (speîron, “burial shroud”) and <i>σπεῖρα</i> (speîra, “coil”); see the latter for more cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>annular seabream (<i>Diplodus annularis</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps from Pre-Greek and related to <i>σμαρίς</i> (smarís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of esparto</li></ol><p>From <i>σπάρτον</i> (spárton, “esparto”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope, cable</li><li>measuring cord</li><li>thread</li><li>esparto (<i>Stipa tenacissima</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spr̥-to</i>, from <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to twist, turn”) and thus related to <i>σπεῖρα</i> (speîra, “coil, twist”); see there for more cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drawing up, traction</li><li>drawing in, suction</li></ol><p>From <i>σπᾰ́ω</i> (spắō, “to draw”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wanton, lascivious</li></ol><p>A back-formation from <i>σπατάλη</i> (spatálē, “lavish way of life, debauchery, luxury”), itself of unclear origin; see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw a sword</li><li>(<i>of other things</i>) to draw</li><li>(<i>of violent actions</i>) to pluck off or out</li><li>to tear, rend, especially of ravenous animals</li><li>to wrench, sprain, twist</li><li>to snatch, tear or drag away</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to carry away, draw aside</li><li>to cause convulsion or spasm</li><li>to draw in, suck in, involve</li><li>to draw tight, pull the reins</li><li>to derive</li></ol><p>García-Ramón suggests that the term is cognate with Vedic Sanskrit <i>पा</i> (pā, “to rise against, be hostile”) and Hittite (pippa-, “to overturn”), and reconstructs Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)peh₂-</i> (“to draw, set in motion”). The verb <i>σπάω</i> (spáō) may have been built to the aorist <i>ἔσπασα</i> (éspasa), itself perhaps derived from <i>&#42;(s)pḗh₂-st</i>. Alternatively, the LIV suggests that the term could have emerged from a PIE verb <i>&#42;(s)ph₂-yé-ti</i>. Another possibility is that the term continues a PIE form of the shape <i>&#42;(s)ph₂-éye-ti</i>. See also <i>σπᾰ́θη</i> (spắthē, “broad blade, spathe”), which derive from the same PIE root.</p><p>An older theory by Klingenschmitt connects the Greek verb with Old Armenian <i>հանեմ</i> (hanem, “to draw or pull out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung, ordure, manure</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>σπύραθος</i> (spúrathos) and <i>ὄνθος</i> (ónthos). A derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pley-</i> (“to split, splice”) is semantically possible comparing Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;skītaną</i> (“to defecate”), which derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skeyd-</i> (“to cut off”). Alternatively, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to libate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spend-</i>. Cognate to Latin <i>spondeo</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) cave, cavern, grotto</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is undoubtedly from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) seed</li><li>semen, sperm</li><li>seed-time, sowing</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) germ, origin of anything</li><li>race, origin, descent</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) seed, offspring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;spérmə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spérmn̥</i>, which consists of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sper-</i> + <i>&#42;-mn̥</i>. By surface analysis, <i>σπείρω</i> (speírō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀟𐀔</i> (pe-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set in rapid motion, haste, hurry</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be hasty of temper</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sperǵʰ-</i> (“to move energetically, hurry, spring”), the same root of Sanskrit <i>स्पृहयति</i> (spṛháyati, “to be eager”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;springaną</i> (“to burst forth”) and Lithuanian <i>spreñgti</i> (“to push (in)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grotto, cave, cavern</li><li>den, lair</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek source as <i>σπέος</i> (spéos) and <i>σπῆλυγξ</i> (spêlunx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σπίνος</i> (spínos) (<i>Hesychius</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extensive, wide</li></ol><p>Part of a family of words (see in Derived and Related Terms) which fell out of colloquial use to the point where Ancient writers were no longer sure of the exact meanings of the terms. Possibly related to Latin <i>spissus</i> (“laborious, slow, dense”), suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;speyd-</i>, a <i>*d</i>-extension of the root <i>&#42;spey-</i> (“to expand, extend, stretch”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i> Beekes considers Lithuanian <i>spi̇̀sti</i> (“to begin to swarm (of bees), gather”) to be unrelated, in view of the word's proto-root requiring a <i>&#42;-t</i> rather than a <i>&#42;-d</i>, though it appears possible that the Lithuanian is simply a different extension of the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaffinch (<i>Fringilla coelebs</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σπίζω</i> (spízō, “to pipe, to chirp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pipe, chirp, squeak (of small birds, to produce a shrill note)</li><li>to extend</li></ol><p>Unknown; likely ultimately onomatopoeic. The connection with <i>σπίνος</i> (spínos), <i>σπίγγος</i> (spíngos, “(chaf)finch”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finkiz</i> (“finch”) (traditionally from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ping-</i>), if valid, could point to a European wanderwort. But compare also <i>σπίγγαν</i> (spíngan) and <i>σπύγγας</i> (spúngas) (glosses uncertain, but referring to kinds of birds), which cannot be from the same Greek root.</p><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;speyd-</i>, an extension of <i>&#42;spey-</i> (“to expand, extend, stretch”) <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i>. Beekes considers the verb a derivative of <i>σπίδιος</i> (spídios, “extensive, wide”), of uncertain origin. Possibly related to <i>σπιδνός</i> (spidnós, “solid, dense”) and Latin <i>spissus</i> (“dense; slow, laborious”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rock, reef, cliff</li><li>spot, fleck, blemish, stain</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek, as there are no cognates outside Greek. According to Pokorny, cognate with Latin <i>spina</i>, German <i>Speil</i>, English <i>spile</i>.</p><p>Unknown. Compare typologically English <i>stain</i> and <i>stone</i>. Also compare <i>στίγμα</i> (stígma) (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teyg-</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An Indian bird resembling the francolin, perhaps <i>Ortygornis pondicerianus</i> (syn. <i>Francolinus pondicerianus</i>, grey francolin)</li></ol><p>Perhaps compare σπίνος (spínos, “chaffinch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaffinch (<i>Fringilla coelebs</i>)</li><li>kind of stone, which blazes when water touches it</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ping-</i> (“small bird, sparrow”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;finkiz</i> (“finch”), Welsh <i>pinc</i> (“finch”) and Sanskrit <i>फिङ्गक</i> (phiṅgaka, “shrike”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to castrate, emasculate</li><li>to make flaccid</li></ol><p>From <i>σπάδων</i> (spádōn, “eunuch”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, “a suffix forming a verb from a noun”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a castration</li><li>a weakening</li></ol><p>From <i>σπαδονίζω</i> (spadonízō, “to castrate”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós, “a suffix that forms abstract nouns”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flaccidity</li></ol><p>From <i>σπαδονίζω</i> (spadonízō, “to castrate, make flaccid”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, “a suffix forming a noun from a verb, that denotes a result of an action”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garment of closely-woven cloth</li></ol><p>From <i>σπάθη</i> (spáthē, “blade”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blade-bearer, a police officer at Alexandria</li></ol><p>From <i>σπάθη</i> (spáthē, “broad blade”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wicker roof used to shelter soldiers</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be rare, scarce, scanty (<i>of things</i>)</li><li>(<i>active voice&#32;or&#32;passive voice</i>) to lack or be in want of [<i>with</i> genitive] (<i>of persons</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σπάνιος</i> (spánios) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>rarely, seldom</li></ol><p>From <i>σπᾰ́νῐος</i> (spắnĭos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>σπάνῐος</i> (spánĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) rare, sparse</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) beardless</li></ol><p>Adjectival formation from <i>σπάνις</i> (spánis, “rarity, scarcity”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós). Related to synonymous <i>σπάνιος</i> (spánios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>γερροφόροι</i> (gerrhophóroi, “troops that used wicker shields”).</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;sparabarah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bur-reed (<i>Sparganium erectum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σπάργανον</i> (spárganon, “band for swaddling infants”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;rare) sparse, rare, scarce</li></ol><p>Perhaps a verbal adjective formation from <i>σπείρω</i> (speírō) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós, “to sow”). According to the linguist Lucien van Beek, the suffixation may be influenced by antonymic formations such as <i>πυκνός</i> (puknós) and <i>συχνός</i> (sukhnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>σπάσμα</i> (spásma, “sprain, spasm”)</li></ol><p>From <i>σπάω</i> (spáō) +&lrm; <i>-σμός</i> (-smós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drawing in, absorbing</li></ol><p>From <i>σπᾰ́σῐς</i> (spắsĭs, “drawing up, traction”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós), from <i>σπᾰ́ω</i> (spắō, “I draw, pull”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of sea urchin</li></ol><p>Hardly related to <i>σπάω</i> (spáō, “to suck”). The variation shows that the word is Pre-Greek. For the formation, compare also <i>φαττάγης</i> (phattágēs, “pangolin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lewdness, wantonness, luxury, riot</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a trinket of luxury: anklets, bracelets, etc.</li></ol></ol><p>Of unclear origin, though the word's ending <i>-άλη</i> (-álē) and semantics are reminiscent of those of <i>κραιπάλη</i> (kraipálē, “bout of drinking”), <i>δαιταλεύς</i> (daitaleús, “banqueter”). While Beekes admits the possibility of derivation from <i>σπάω</i> (spáō, “to draw in, suck (of wine, etc.)”) and rejects Neumann's derivation of the Greek as a loan from Hittite (*išpatalla-, “who gladly and often eats to the full”), he ultimately prefers Furnée's derivation from Pre-Greek, based on a convincing connection with <i>βάταλος</i> (bátalos, “catamite, lascivious man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thin excrement, as in diarrhea</li><li>parings of leather</li></ol><p>In the second meaning, it belongs to <i>σπάτος</i> (spátos, “hide, leather”). The first meaning may also be combined with "leather waste" as a euphemistic metaphor. However, it is more probably a Pre-Greek word. Compare also <i>τῖλος</i> (tîlos, “thin stool, as in diarrhea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live softly or in excessive comfort or indulgence</li><li>to live lewdly, lavishly, lasciviously</li><li>to run riot</li></ol><p><i>σπᾰτά̆λη</i> (spătá̆lē, “wantonness, luxury”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;spéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spéryeti</i>, from <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to sprinkle, scatter, strew”).</p><p>The only known secure cognate is Hittite (išpār-ᶦ, “to spread out, strew”) (&lt; <i>&#42;spor-ey</i>). Old Armenian <i>սփռեմ</i> (spʻṙem, “to spread out”) (whence modern Armenian <i>սփռել</i> (spʻṙel)) appears to be unrelated, despite semantic and superficial phonetic similarities.</p><p>Note English <i>spray</i>, <i>sprout</i>, <i>spread</i>, whose proto-Germanic forms are possibly connected. Perhaps also related through extensions of this root to Latin <i>spargō</i>, <i>spernō</i>, <i>spurcus</i> and <i>spurius</i>, English <i>spurn</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wrapping cloth</li><li>a burial shroud</li><li>a sailcloth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sper-yo-</i>, from <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to twist”), and thus related to <i>σπάρτον</i> (spárton, “rope, cable”) and <i>σπεῖρα</i> (speîra, “coil”); see the latter for more cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>privet (<i>Ligustrum vulgare</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σπεῖρα</i> (speîra, “coil, wreath”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), because of the shape of its follicles.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of birds</i>) picking up seeds</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) picking up scraps, gossiping</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rook (<i>Corvus frugilegus</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) guttersnipe, street urchin</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) idle blabber, gossiper</li></ol><p>From <i>σπέρμα</i> (spérma, “seed”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to gather”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hasty, expeditious</li></ol><p>From <i>σπεύδω</i> (speúdō, “to hasten”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to set going, urge on, hasten</li><li>to procure quickly, get ready</li><li>to seek eagerly, strive after</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to press on, hasten</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to be eager to</li><li>to be troubled in mind</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spewd-</i> (“to press, hurry”). Cognate with Albanian <i>punë</i>, Lithuanian <i>spáusti</i>, Old Armenian <i>փոյթ</i> (pʻoytʻ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything twisted or wound</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) coils or spires of snakes</li><li>rope, cord</li><li>knot or curl in wood</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) torus of a column</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sper-yeh₂-</i>, from <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to twist”), and thus cognate with <i>σπάργανον</i> (spárganon, “swaddling clothes”), <i>σπάρτον</i> (spárton, “rope, cable”), and <i>σπεῖρον</i> (speîron, “shroud”). Outside of Hellenic, cognate with Old Armenian <i>փարել</i> (pʻarel, “to enclose, embrace”) (whence Armenian <i>փարվել</i> (pʻarvel, “id”)), as well as perhaps Albanian <i>fier</i> (“fern”) (which directly corresponds in formation with <i>σπεῖρον</i> (speîron)).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wrapping cloth</li><li>a burial shroud</li><li>a sailcloth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sper-yo-</i>, from <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to twist”), and thus related to <i>σπάρτον</i> (spárton, “rope, cable”) and <i>σπεῖρα</i> (speîra, “coil”); see the latter for more cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wrapping cloth</li><li>a burial shroud</li><li>a sailcloth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sper-yo-</i>, from <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to twist”), and thus related to <i>σπάρτον</i> (spárton, “rope, cable”) and <i>σπεῖρα</i> (speîra, “coil”); see the latter for more cognates.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;spéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spéryeti</i>, from <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to sprinkle, scatter, strew”).</p><p>The only known secure cognate is Hittite (išpār-ᶦ, “to spread out, strew”) (&lt; <i>&#42;spor-ey</i>). Old Armenian <i>սփռեմ</i> (spʻṙem, “to spread out”) (whence modern Armenian <i>սփռել</i> (spʻṙel)) appears to be unrelated, despite semantic and superficial phonetic similarities.</p><p>Note English <i>spray</i>, <i>sprout</i>, <i>spread</i>, whose proto-Germanic forms are possibly connected. Perhaps also related through extensions of this root to Latin <i>spargō</i>, <i>spernō</i>, <i>spurcus</i> and <i>spurius</i>, English <i>spurn</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dense, solid, holding together, fastened</li></ol><p>See <i>σπίδιος</i> (spídios, “extensive, wide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>span, also as a fixed measure</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>παλάμη</i> (palámē, “palm of the hand”), <i>δόχμη</i> (dókhmē, “breadth of the hand”) and <i>πυγμή</i> (pugmḗ, “distance between the elbow and the knuckles”). According to Beekes, it's probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spark</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr, “star”) and <i>αἰθήρ</i> (aithḗr, “ether”). Reminiscent of Lithuanian <i>spindžiù</i> (“to gleam”), Latvian <i>spīdēt</i> (“to gleam, beam”) and <i>spuôdrs</i> (“white, gleaming”), but formally impossible to connect. Niedermann connected this word to Latin <i>scintilla</i> (“spark”), suggesting a Mediterranean loan, while Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spark</li></ol><p>From <i>σπῐνθήρ</i> (spĭnthḗr, “spark”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), with a change of vocalism.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meager, lean</li></ol><p>The word shares the formation with <i>ἰσχνός</i> (iskhnós, “lean, thin”). According to Beekes, the word is probably connected with <i>σπίδιος</i> (spídios). Hardly related to Old Irish <i>séim</i> (“slender, lean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) bowels, guts, inward part, internal organ</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) seat of feelings, heart</li></ol><p>Related to <i>σπλήν</i> (splḗn, “spleen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>milt, spleen</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;σπληχ-</i> (&#42;splēkh-), <i>&#42;σπλαχ-</i> (&#42;splakh-), from Proto-Indo-European, but the exact root remains formally difficult to construct. The root could be <i>&#42;splēǵʰ-</i>, <i>&#42;splēh₁ǵʰ-</i>, <i>&#42;spelǵʰ-</i> or <i>&#42;splēngʰ-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Middle Irish <i>selg</i>, Old Prussian <i>blusne</i>, Lithuanian <i>blužnis</i>, Latin <i>lien</i>, Old Armenian <i>փայծաղն</i> (pʻaycałn), Avestan <i>𐬯𐬞𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬰𐬀𐬥</i> (spər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>zan), and Sanskrit <i>प्लीहन्</i>&nbsp;m (plīhán).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pad or compress of linen laid on a wound</li></ol><p>From <i>σπλήν</i> (splḗn, “spleen”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small sponge</li></ol><p>From <i>σπόγγος</i> (spóngos, “sponge”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sponge (marine animal)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap of ashes</li></ol><p>From <i>σποδός</i> (spodós, “ash”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾱ́</i> (-iā́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bullace (<i>Prunus domestica</i> subsp. <i>insititia</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σποδός</i> (spodós, “wood-ashes, embers”) +&lrm; <i>-ιάς</i> (-iás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>σποδιᾱ́</i> (spodiā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood ashes, ember</li><li>dust</li><li>oxide on certain metals</li><li>lava</li></ol><p>Furnée connects it with <i>ἄσβολος</i> (ásbolos, “soot”) and <i>σπολεύς</i> (spoleús, “loaf”), leading to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leathern garment, jerkin</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a verb for "split, tear off", in which case it may be related to <i>σπόλια</i> (spólia, “wool plucked off the leg of sheep”), <i>ἄσπαλον</i> (áspalon, “skin, hide”), but hardly to Thessalian <i>σπόλος</i> (spólos, “pole”). From other languages: Latin <i>spolium</i> (“arms, spoils”), Lithuanian <i>spālis</i> (“beard, part of the stalk of flax”) and German <i>spalten</i> (“to cleave”), all reflecting a root <i>&#42;spel(H)-</i> (“what is split, stripped off”). The word may also reflect the Pre-Greek root that is also seen in <i>ἄσπαλον</i> (áspalon, “mole”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of loaf</li></ol><p>Ultimately from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>ἄσβολος</i> (ásbolos, “soot”), <i>σποδός</i> (spodós, “ash, ember”) and <i>ψόλος</i> (psólos, “soot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>libation, drink-offering (to the gods; one offered to the dead or chthonic spirits is <i>χοή</i> (khoḗ))</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) treaty, truce</li></ol><p>From <i>σπένδω</i> (spéndō, “to libate”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>used at a libation</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine,&#32;poetry) spondee (metrical foot consisting of two heavy syllables)</li></ol><p>From <i>σπονδή</i> (spondḗ) +&lrm; <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seed</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>σπείρω</i> (speírō, “to sow, to scatter”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix). Alternatively, the term may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spor-eh₂</i>, which may also be the source of Albanian <i>farë</i>. Regardless, ultimately from the root <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to strew”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a scattered manner, here and there</li></ol><p>From <i>σπορᾱ́</i> (sporā́), verbal noun from <i>o</i>-grade of the root of <i>σπείρω</i> (speírō, “to sow, scatter”), +&lrm; <i>-δην</i> (-dēn, deverbal adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) scattered, dispersed</li></ol><p>From <i>σπορᾱ́</i> (sporā́) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás), from the root of <i>σπείρω</i> (speírō).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σποράς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Sporades</li></ol><p>From <i>σπορᾰ́ς</i> (sporắs, “scattered”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scattered</li></ol><p>From <i>σποράς</i> (sporás) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird mentioned by Aristophanes, probably the house sparrow (<i>Passer domesticus</i>)</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ὀρχίλος</i> (orkhílos, “wren”) and <i>τροχίλος</i> (trokhílos, “Egyptian plover; wren”). This word has a counterpart in Old Prussian <i>spurglis</i> (“sparrow”), which may point to a European substrate word or a Wanderwort.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurry, hurry up, come quickly</li><li>to strive, make an effort, eager</li></ol><p><i>σπουδή</i> (spoudḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haste, speed</li></ol><p>From <i>σπεύδω</i> (speúdō, “to be keen; make haste”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earnest, serious</li><li>diligent, active, zealous</li><li>good, relating to moral character and importance</li></ol><p>From <i>σπουδή</i> (spoudḗ, “haste, speed”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A large basket, a creel used for transport money</li><li>(<i>then</i>) a basket to expend provisions (after Latin <i>sportula</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it can be hardly related to <i>σπάρτον</i> (spárton, “rope”), as the variation points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sponge</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) tonsil</li></ol><p>Together with Latin <i>fungus</i> (“mushroom, fungus”) and Old Armenian <i>սունկն</i> (sunkn, “tree-mushroom”) a Mediterranean-Pontic Pre-Greek substrate loanword.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Attic form of <i>σφόνδυλος</i> (sphóndulos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sowing</li><li>the time for sowing</li><li>seed</li><li>produce, harvest, crop</li><li>offspring</li><li>semen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spor-</i>, an o-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sper-</i> (“to strew”). Cognate with <i>σπείρω</i> (speírō, “to sow”), <i>σπέρμα</i> (spérma, “semen, sperm”), and Albanian <i>farë</i>; see <i>σπείρω</i> (speírō) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) droppings of goats and sheep</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ὄνθος</i> (ónthos), <i>σπέλεθος</i> (spélethos) and <i>ψάμαθος</i> (psámathos). Similar words are found in Lithuanian <i>spi̇̀ros</i> (“droppings of goats”) and Icelandic <i>sparð</i> (“droppings”). The alternations could very well point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cave</li></ol><p>From <i>σπέος</i> (spéos, “cave, cavern”). Cognate with Latin <i>spēlunca</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stable</li><li>posting station</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>stablum</i>, from Classical Latin <i>stabulum</i>, from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;staθlom</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) A 600-foot track for footraces and the surrounding stadium.</li><li>(<i>sports</i>) A 600-foot footrace.</li><li>(<i>historical&#32;units of measure</i>) A stade, a unit of distance based on the length of a racetrack, equal to 600 Greek feet (variously 150–210&nbsp;m at different places and times).</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but possibly from <i>στάδιος</i> (stádios, “firm, fixed”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”), in reference to the fixed distance of the course. The relation with the alternative form <i>σπάδιον</i> (spádion), which ostensibly looks like a derivative of <i>σπᾰ́ω</i> (spắō, “to draw, drag”), as well as whether one is derived from the other or not, is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standing fast and firm</li><li>firm, fixed, immobile</li><li>standing upright or straight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drop, let fall drop by drop</li><li>to leak</li><li>to drip, trickle</li><li>to drop off (<i>of ripe fruit</i>)</li></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;σταγ-ι̯ω</i> (&#42;stag-i̯ō), of uncertain origin; see also <i>σταγών</i>&nbsp;f (stagṓn, “drop”). Babiniotis rejects connections to Latin <i>stāgnum</i> (“standing water, pool”) and Welsh <i>taen</i> (“spreading, dispersion”), while Beekes tentatively supports a connection with <i>stāgnum</i>, which would imply a root of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂g-</i> (“to seep, drip”), as well as perhaps Old Breton <i>staer</i> (“river, brook”) (&lt; <i>&#42;stag-rā</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpenter's line or rule</li><li>plummet or plumb line</li><li>line which bounds the racecourse, goal</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) law, rule</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”), the same root of <i>σταθμός</i> (stathmós, “stall, pen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stake to which nets are fastened</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the root could be either Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stel-</i> (“to set”) (like <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “to prepare”)) or Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”) (like <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthen jar</li><li>bottle for racking off wine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stéh₂mn̥</i> (“that which stands, stature”). Cognate with Latin <i>stāmen</i> (“warp of a loom; thread hanging from a distaff”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dropping; dripping</li></ol><p>From <i>στᾰ́ζω</i> (stắzō, “to drop”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, suffix to form a feminine noun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>checking, stopping</li><li>standing, stationary, stagnant</li><li>stable, steadfast, fixed</li><li>(<i>of men</i>) steadfast, steady</li><li>weighed, weighable</li></ol><p>From <i>στάσις</i> (stásis, “placing, setting”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a standing, placing, setting</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>standing stone, pillar</li><li>erection (of a building), building</li><li>weighing</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) standing, stature</li><li>position, posture, station</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>stable, stall</li><li>compass position</li><li>posture of a boxer</li><li>position or opinion of a philosopher</li></ol><li>state, condition</li></ol><li>party, company, band</li><li>party formed for sedition, faction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sedition, discord</li><li>division, dissent</li></ol><li>statute, decree</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;státis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stéh₂tis</i> (whence also Latin <i>statiō</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌸𐍃</i> (staþs, “place”), and Old English <i>stede</i> (English <i>stead</i>)), from <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”). Equivalent to <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stator, usher in a law court</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>stator</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an ear of grain</li><li>scion, progeny</li><li>lower part of the abdomen</li><li>base horehound (<i>Stachys germanica</i>)</li><li>surgical bandage</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) shibboleth</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stngʰ-u-s</i>, from a root <i>&#42;ste(n)gʰ-</i> (“to stick”), and compared with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stinganą</i> (“to sting, stick out”), Lithuanian <i>stangùs</i> (“fixed, stiff”), Latvian <i>stingt</i> (“to harden”). However, in addition to the accentuation of the Baltic terms implying a more likely proto-form of <i>&#42;steng-</i>, the variant <i>ἄσταχυς</i> (ástakhus) could also suggest a Pre-Greek origin, which is endorsed by Beekes; see also <i>στόνυξ</i> (stónux, “sharp point”) and <i>στόχος</i> (stókhos, “erection of stone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hard fat; tallow; suet</li><li>dough made from flour of spelt</li></ol><p>With quantitative metathesis of <i>ῆᾰ</i> (êă) to <i>έᾱ</i> (éā), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stā́wər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stéh₂-wr̥</i>, from <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”). Compare <i>πῖαρ</i> (pîar), <i>οὖθᾰρ</i> (oûthăr).</p><p>Compare typologically Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sadlo</i> (whence Bulgarian <i>са́ло</i> (sálo), Polish <i>sadło</i>, Russian <i>са́ло</i> (sálo)) related to <i>&#42;sěsti</i>, <i>&#42;saditi</i>, <i>&#42;sad'ati</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>master of the house</li></ol><p>From <i>στέγος</i> (stégos, “roof; house”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covering, cover, wrapper (especially of leather)</li><li>receptacle, place in which to hide or keep anything</li></ol><p>From <i>στέγω</i> (stégō, “to cover”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof, ceiling</li><li>shelter</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) roofed place, chamber, room</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) storey of a house</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) house, dwelling</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) deck of a ship</li></ol><p>From <i>στέγω</i> (stégō, “to cover”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, action noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof</li><li>house, mansion</li><li>urn, grave</li><li>brothel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;(s)tégos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tégos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teg-</i> (“to cover”). Cognate with Latin <i>tego</i> (“to cover”), German <i>Decke</i> (“roof”), English <i>thatch</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover closely, so as to keep water either out or in</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teg-</i> (“to cover, roof”). Cognate with Lithuanian <i>sti̇́egiu</i> (“to thatch, to cover a roof”), Latin <i>tegō</i> (“to cover”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þakjaną</i> (“to cover”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the crown of the root, whence the stem or trunk springs</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a trunk, log</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a blockhead</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “prepare, furnish”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make ready, prepare; to furnish, dress</li><li>to dispatch, send; (middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to set out, journey</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>active intransitive in passive sense</i>) to set forth, prepare to go</li></ol><li>to summon, fetch</li><li>to gather up; to furl a sail</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to check, repress</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to bind, make costive</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to restrict one's diet</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stéľľō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stel-</i> (“to set”). Others have argued for a derivation from a supposed Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skʷel-</i> (“to equip”), based on Aeolic forms like <i>σπόλα</i> (spóla) and <i>εὔσπολος</i> (eúspolos), but this is suspect. Beekes instead connects the Aeolic forms to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spel-</i> (“to split”), for which see <i>σπολάς</i> (spolás, “leather garment, jerkin”) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) wreath, garland, chaplet</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) pedigrees, family trees</li></ol><p>From <i>στέφω</i> (stéphō, “to encircle, crown”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma), with regressive assimilation (though others, including Beekes, instead take the word to stem from the root of <i>στολή</i> (stolḗ, “equipment, robe”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>στέμματα</i> (stémmata)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass of olives from which the oil has been pressed</li><li>mass of pressed grapes</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>A full-grade variant of <i>σταφυλή</i> (staphulḗ, “grape”). No further etymology; perhaps Pre-Greek, if the </i>-μ-<i> is interpreted as prenasalization. This word has also been connected with the verb <i>στέμβω</i> (stémbō, “to shake about, agitate”).</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sigh, groan</li></ol><p>From <i>στενάζω</i> (stenázō, “to sigh, groan”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to moan, groan</li><li>to lament</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;sténō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tenh₂-</i> (“to thunder”), compare Sanskrit <i>स्तनति</i> (stanati, “to drone, thunder”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stenaną</i> (“to moan, groan”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stenàti</i> (“to groan, to moan”).</p><p>On the other hand, Latin <i>tonō</i>, Sanskrit <i>तन्यति</i> (tanyati), Old English <i>þunian</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þunraz</i> and Aeolic Greek <i>τέννει</i> (ténnei) (a riming word or an old s-less byform) point to <i>&#42;ten(h₂)-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A being straitened, narrowing.</li></ol><p>From <i>στενόω</i> (stenóō, “to confine, to contract”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, nominal suffix), from <i>στενός</i> (stenós, “narrow”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, “to make someone do or be something”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love-charm, applied to various plants</li><li>love itself, affection</li><li>parental love, parental fondness, parental affection</li></ol><p>From <i>στέργω</i> (stérgō) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to love (<i>mostly of non-sexual affection</i>)</li><li>to show affection</li><li>to be content, to acquiesce</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sterg-</i> (literally “to cover, to protect”). Possibly related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sterťi</i> (“to guard”) and Welsh <i>serch</i> (“love, affection”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deprivation, privation</li><li>negation</li></ol><p>From <i>στερέω</i> (steréō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially of men</i>) A breast, chest.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>poetic</i>) The seat of the affections, heart.</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) The heart.</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) The breastbone; the sternum.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stér(h₃)-no-m</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sterh₃-</i> (“to be broad, spread out”). Cognate with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stirniju</i> (“forehead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be wanting in, to lack, want [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to suffer loss</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>στερέω</i> (steréō, “to deprive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hide, skin, fur</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sterbʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which encircles, surrounds</li><li>crown, wreath, chaplet, especially the conqueror's wreath</li><li>in general: any prize or honour</li></ol><p>From <i>στέφω</i> (stéphō, “I encircle”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Στέφανος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>In particular: Stephen, an early Christian martyr.</li></ol><p>From <i>στέφανος</i> (stéphanos, “crown”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put around</li><li>to encircle, crown, wreath, garland</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to honour with libations</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stékʷʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stégʷʰ-e-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;stegʷʰ-</i> (“to enlace”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand</li></ol><p>Late Hellenistic Koine back-formation from <i>ἕστηκᾰ</i> (héstēkă, “I have stood”), perfect of <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>block of stone used as a prop or buttress to a wall</li><li>block or slab used as a memorial, monument</li><li>gravestone, tombstone</li><li>monument inscribed with record of victories, dedications or votes of thanks</li><li>post placed on mortgage ground, as a record of the fact</li><li>boundary post</li><li>block of rock crystal, in which Ethiopian mummies were allegedly cased</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stl̥-n-eh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;stel-</i> (“to put, place”) (related to <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “to set, arrange”)), with similarities to Old High German <i>stollo</i>. Alternatively derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sth₂-sleh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”) (related to <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”)); this latter derivation is favored by Beekes. Also compare <i>στῦλος</i> (stûlos, “pillar”) and <i>στάλιξ</i> (stálix, “stake”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warp in the upright loom</li><li>thread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stéh₂mn̥</i> (“that which stands, stature”). Cognate with Latin <i>stāmen</i> and Ancient Greek <i>στάμνος</i> (stámnos, “jar”). By surface analysis, <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>στῆθος</i> (stêthos, “breast”).</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pstḗn</i> (“breast, nipple”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spenô</i> (“nipple”) and Old Armenian <i>ստին</i> (stin, “breast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pebble</li></ol><p>This item formally agrees with Sanskrit <i>स्तिया</i> (stiyā, “stagnant water”), but the semantics are quite different. An intermediate meaning could be sought in Sanskrit <i>स्त्यान</i> (styāna, “curdled, thick”). Other possible cognates are Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stainaz</i> (“stone”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stěna</i> (“rock; lump”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoarfrost, rime</li></ol><p>From <i>στείβω</i> (steíbō, “I tread on, stamp on”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē). For the meaning, compare <i>πάχνη</i> (pákhnē, “hoarfrost, rime”) and <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “I fasten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>στῐ́μμῐ</i> (stĭ́mmĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stigma (a permanent identity mark branded, cut or tattooed onto the skin, typically given to slaves, criminals and traitors)</li><li>any mark or spot</li><li>gold (colour)</li></ol><p>From <i>στῐ́ζω</i> (stĭ́zō, “to mark”, root <i>στῐγ-</i> (stĭg-)) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tattoo</li><li>to mark</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to punctuate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stiďďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teyg-</i> (“to pierce, prick, be sharp”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stikkô</i>, whence English <i>stick</i> and German <i>stecken</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, glitter</li><li>to gleam</li></ol><p>Beekes concludes that this may be Pre-Greek based on the derivations with π instead of β.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A shining, a glittering</li></ol><p>From <i>στίλβω</i> (stílbō, “I shine”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kohl; powdered antimony (<i>used for eye paint</i>)</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>sdm</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a row or file of soldiers</li><li>a line of poetry; a verse</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>στείχω</i> (steíkhō, “walk, march, go or come, march in line or order”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steygʰ-</i> (“to walk”).</p><p>Cognate with German <i>steigen</i>, English <i>sty</i>, <i>stair</i>, <i>stile</i> and possibly Latin <i>vestīgō</i>. See also <i>στοῖχος</i> (stoîkhos, “row in an ascending series, column”) and <i>στίχος</i> (stíkhos, “row, line, rank, file (of soldiers); line of poetry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marking (<i>e.g., of musical notes</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) punctuation</li></ol><li>a spot or mark</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a tattoo (skin decoration)</li></ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a stab, sting, prick, blow, or hit</li></ol><p>From <i>στίζω</i> (stízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a row or file of soldiers</li><li>a line of poetry, a verse</li></ol><p>From <i>στείχω</i> (steíkhō, “walk, march, go or come, march in line or order”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steygʰ-</i> (“to walk”).</p><p>Cognate with German <i>steigen</i>, English <i>sty</i>, <i>stair</i>, <i>stile</i> and possibly Latin <i>vestīgō</i>. See also <i>στοῖχος</i> (stoîkhos, “row in an ascending series, column”), <i>στόχος</i> (stókhos, “pillar of brick”), and <i>στίξ</i> (stíx, “row, line, rank, file (of soldiers)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of flour or dough of spelt</li></ol><p>From <i>σταῖς</i> (staîs, “dough”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a drop (of liquids)</li><li>a kind of metal&#59; perhaps orichalcum</li></ol><p>See <i>στάζω</i> (stázō, “to drip”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who runs in the stadium, one who runs for a prize</li></ol><p>From <i>στᾰ́δῐον</i> (stắdĭon, “racecourse”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine suffix for person concerned).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standing fast, firm, fixed</li><li>(<i>of weather</i>) steady, settled</li><li>(<i>of speech</i>) calm, deliberate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to measure, weigh</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to calculate, estimate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(deponent,&#32;figurative) to conjecture</li></ol><li>to attach weight, value, importance to something</li></ol><p>From <i>στάθμη</i> (státhmē) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>σταθμάω</i> (stathmáō)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>στάθμη</i> (státhmē) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper of a house, landlord</li><li>(<i>military</i>) quartermaster</li><li>householder on whom a soldier is billeted</li><li>billeted soldier</li></ol><p>From <i>σταθμός</i> (stathmós, “standing place”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to bear, carry, bring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a weight for a balance</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standing place</li><li>stall, pen, fold (for animals)</li><li>shepherd's lodge</li><li>post, doorpost</li><li>weight for a balance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;statʰmós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sth₂-dʰ-mó-s</i>, from <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standing place</li><li>stall, pen, fold (for animals)</li><li>shepherd's lodge</li><li>post, doorpost</li><li>weight for a balance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;statʰmós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sth₂-dʰ-mó-s</i>, from <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>σταθμάω</i> (stathmáō)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>στάθμη</i> (státhmē) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil of myrrh</li></ol><p>From <i>στᾰ́ζω</i> (stắzō, “to drip”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oozing out in drops, trickling, distilling</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective, from <i>στάζω</i> (stázō, “to drop”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ribs or frame-timbers of a ship, which stand up from the keel</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>γλωχίς</i> (glōkhís), <i>ἑρμίς</i> (hermís), <i>ῥηγμίς</i> (rhēgmís) and <i>ὠδίς</i> (ōdís). Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”), the same root of <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”) and <i>στάμνος</i> (stámnos, “jar”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appoint, establish</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>. Rix suggests that it could derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stnéh₂ti</i>, the nasal-infix present of the root <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>. Related to Ancient Greek <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief of a band or company</li><li>head of a party, leader in sedition</li></ol><p><i>στάσις</i> (stásis, “sedition”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “leader”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rebel, revolt</li><li>to form a party or faction</li><li>to be at odds, quarrel, be at discord</li><li>to revolutionize, throw into confusion</li></ol><p><i>στάσις</i> (stásis) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>στᾰσῐ́ᾰρχος</i> (stăsĭ́ărkhos)</li></ol><p><i>στάσις</i> (stásis, “sedition”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “leader”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a weight, a standard</li><li>(<i>numismatics</i>) a stater (a type of coin)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to put, place, set”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr), hence literally "that which sets, settles".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>causing to stand, coming to a standstill, rest</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>placed, standing, stalled</li><li>reaching to the feet (<i>of a tunic</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;statós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sth₂tós</i> (“placed, standing”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्थित</i> (sthita, “standing, situated”), Latin <i>status</i> (“fixed, set”), and Old Norse <i>staðr</i> (“restive, stubborn (of a horse)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upright stake or pile</li><li>cross, crucifix</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂u-rós</i> (“pole”), from <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”).; see also <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ, “to set up, make stand”). Cognate with Icelandic <i>staur</i> (“stake, pole”), English <i>stour</i> (“id”), Swedish <i>stör</i>. Compare also <i>staff</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fence with pales, impalisade</li><li>to crucify</li></ol><p>From <i>σταυρός</i> (staurós, “stake, cross”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forceps for taking hold of the uvula</li></ol><p>From <i>στᾰφῠλή</i> (stăphŭlḗ, “bunch of grapes; uvula”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγρᾱ</i> (ágrā, “catching, catch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bunch of grapes</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) uvula</li></ol><p>Though traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stebʰ-</i> (“to be stiff; post”) (compare Sanskrit <i>स्तम्भ</i> (stambha, “pillar”), Old English <i>stæf</i> (“staff”)) via an assumed intermediate sense “stem”, according to Beekes, the word is clearly Pre-Greek, along with the related <i>στέμφῠλον</i> (stémphŭlon, “mass of pressed olives or grapes”) and <i>ᾰ̓στᾰφῐ́ς</i> (ăstăphĭ́s, “dried grapes”). It must be noted that the usual Avar word for “grape” is <i>цIибил</i> (cʼibil) and is internally derived in Caucasian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vat or basket in which grapes are put for pressing</li></ol><p>From <i>σταφυλή</i> (staphulḗ, “grape”) +&lrm; <i>βολέω</i> (boléō, “to throw, launch”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European bladdernut (<i>Staphylea pinnata</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σταφυλή</i> (staphulḗ, “bunch of grapes”) +&lrm; <i>δένδρον</i> (déndron, “tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a carrot</li><li>a grapevine</li><li>a type of insect about the size of a beetle</li></ol><p>From <i>στᾰφῠλή</i> (stăphŭlḗ, “grape”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dough made of spelt flour</li><li>dough (in general)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;th₂eys-</i> (“dough”), whence also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;těsto</i> (“dough”), Old Irish <i>táis</i> (“dough”), and Old English <i>þǣsma</i> (“leaven”), with an influence from <i>στέαρ</i> (stéar, “fat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of flour or dough of spelt</li></ol><p>From <i>στέαρ</i> (stéar, “dough”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tread on, stamp on</li><li>(<i>with cognate accusative</i>) to walk on a path</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steyb-</i> (“to get stiff, fix”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>ստէպ</i> (stēp, “speed, haste”) and Lithuanian <i>sti̇́ebas</i> (“mast-tree, pillar, stalk”), <i>stáibis</i> (“lower shank, supporting post”), <i>staibùs</i> (“courageous, strong”). Note semantic and phonetic similarities with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stьbъ</i> (“stem, stalk”), Latin <i>stīpō</i> (“to crowd, cram”), which stem from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steybʰ-</i> and <i>&#42;steyp-</i> respectively.</p><p>Stems: στει- στι- and στοι- (see Derivatives and Related terms).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straiten, confine, coop up, crowd</li><li>to become strait, be narrowed</li><li>to be or become full, be thronged</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be straitened, distressed</li></ol><p>From <i>στενός</i> (stenós, “narrow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to walk, march, go or come</li><li>movement in a direction given by a preposition or by the context</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>with πρὸς (pròs), εἰς (eis), ἐπὶ (epì), ἀνά (aná), κατὰ (katà) [<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>motion to or towards</i>) to go to, to approach</li></ol><li>with από (apó), ἐκ (ek), ἐπὶ (epì), ἀνά (aná), κατὰ (katà) [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>motion from</i>) to go, to depart</li></ol></ol><li>to march in line or order</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to march against [<i>with</i> <b>ἐπὶ</b> (epì, + accusative)]</li><li>to march to [<i>with</i> <b>εἰς</b> (eis, + accusative)]</li><li>march on something [<i>with</i> <b>ἐν</b> (en, + dative)]</li></ol><li>cognate accusative</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><li>to step up, mount, climb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steygʰ-</i> (“go (in an orderly way), climb”). Cognates include Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stīganą</i> (“ascend, climb up”) (> German <i>steigen</i>). For <i>o</i>-grade <i>&#42;stóygʰ-</i>, see <i>στοῖχος</i> (stoîkhos, “row”); for zero-grade <i>&#42;stigʰ-</i>, see <i>στίχος</i> (stíkhos, “row of soldiers; verse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>watertight, waterproof</li><li>covering, enclosing, confining</li><li>closely covered, roofed</li></ol><p>From <i><i>στέγω</i> (stégō)</i> (<i>stégō</i> (stégō), “to cover closely”) +&lrm; <i>-ανός</i> (-anós, “tending to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>watertight, waterproof</li><li>costive</li></ol><p>From <i>στέγω</i> (stégō, “to cover”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós), with sense development "covered" > "waterproof, clogged".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle of a tool such as an axe or hammer</li></ol><p>PIE stel, stalk, shaft (Beekes). Cognate with stalk.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>haft, shaft of an axe, hack, hammer</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>δωρεά</i> (dōreá) and <i>γενεά</i> (geneá). The basis of this word may be related to Old Armenian <i>ստեղն</i> (stełn, “stem, shaft, stalk”) and English <i>stalk</i>, both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stel-</i> (“stalk, trunk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sigh, moan, wail</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bewail, lament</li></ol><p>From <i>στένω</i> (sténō, “to moan, lament”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, frequentative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sigh, groan</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to roar</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make a rushing sound</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lament</li></ol><p>From <i>στένω</i> (sténō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tenh₂-</i> (“to crash, sound, thunder, roar, moan”), see also Latin <i>tono</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्तनति</i> (stanati).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sighing, groaning</li></ol><p>From <i>στενάζω</i> (stenázō, “to sigh, groan”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>στενάχω</i> (stenákhō)</li></ol><p>From <i>στενάχω</i> (stenákhō) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be straitened, confined, to press closely</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be anxious, to sadden</li></ol><p>Parasyntheton from <i>στενόχωρος</i> (stenókhōros) (<i>στενός</i> (stenós, “narrow, tight”) +&lrm; <i>χῶρος</i> (khôros, “space”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrowness of space, a want or lack of room</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) straits, difficulty</li></ol><p><i>στενόχωρος</i> (stenókhōros, “of narrow space”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow, tight</li></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;στενϝός</i> (&#42;stenwós), of unknown further origin, with no known cognates outside Greek. The suffix of the derivative <i>στενυγρός</i> (stenugrós, “narrow”) suggests Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a narrow mouth</li></ol><p>From <i>στενός</i> (stenós, “narrow”) +&lrm; <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of narrow space, strait</li></ol><p><i>στενός</i> (stenós, “narrow”) +&lrm; <i>χώρᾱ</i> (khṓrā, “location”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to straiten, confine, contract</li></ol><p>From <i>στενός</i> (stenós, “narrow”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, “to make someone do or be something”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive, bereave [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to take away [(<i>rarely</i>) <i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>Disputedly cognate with Middle Irish <i>serb</i> (“theft”), pointing to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“to rob”), but this is uncertain. If the root is the same as that of <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“infertile”), whence Ancient Greek <i>στεῖρος</i> (steîros) and <i>στεῖρα</i> (steîra), it may be reconstructed as <i>&#42;sterh₁-</i> (or <i>&#42;ster-h₁-</i>) with a semantic development “to rob, deprive of” &gt; “to be bereft of offspring, infertile”. However, <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“infertile”) may instead be the same root as <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“stiff, rigid”), whence Ancient Greek <i>στερεός</i> (stereós) and maybe <i>στηρῐ́ζω</i> (stērĭ́zō, “to support”), with a different semantic development.</p><p>Beekes considers <i>στέρομαι</i> (stéromai) to be the original verb, from which <i>στερέω</i> (steréō) and related words in Greek are ultimately derived; this would mean a root-final <i>&#42;h₁</i> is unnecessary.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation, framework, firmament</li></ol><p>From <i>στερεόω</i> (stereóō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma), from <i>στερεός</i> (stereós, “firm, solid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liable to confiscation</li></ol><p>From <i>στερέω</i> (steréō, “deprive, bereave”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>στερέω</i> (steréō), used only in the present</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>στερέω</i> (steréō) +&lrm; <i>-σκω</i> (-skō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κοπρών</i> (koprṓn, “place for dung”).</li></ol><p>Unknown. Not related to Latin <i>stercus</i> (“dung”) and Welsh <i>trwnc</i> (“urine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm, solid, rigid</li><li>standard, of full value (of money)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) stiff, stubborn</li><li>hard, stubborn, cruel</li><li>solid, cubic (of bodies and quantities)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“strong, steady”), the same root of Old English <i>starian</i> (“to stare”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staraz</i> (“stiff”), <i>στεῖρος</i> (steîros, “barren, sterile”), Tocharian B <i>ścīre</i> (“hard; harsh (of words)”). See also <i>στηρῐ́ζω</i> (stērĭ́zō, “to support”). See also <i>στερέω</i> (steréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>firmly, rigidly</li></ol><p>From <i>στερεός</i> (stereós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crowned, garlanded, wreathed; wearing a crown, garland, or wreath</li><li>title of officials in Greek city-states who had the right of wearing a crown</li></ol><p>From <i>στέφανος</i> (stéphanos, crown, that which encircles) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, to bring, bear, carry)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crown, adorn, honour</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be put round in a circle</li></ol><p><i>στέφανος</i> (stéphanos, “crown, wreath”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, denominal verb suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narrow, close or confined space</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) straits, distress</li></ol><p>From <i>στείνω</i> (steínō, “to straiten, confine”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heifer (a cow that has not calved)</li><li>barren woman</li><li>forepart of a ship's keel, continued into the stem or cutwater</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stéřřa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stérih₂</i>. By surface analysis, <i>στεῖρος</i> (steîros) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ</i> (-ă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barren, infertile</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;steřřos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ster-yos</i>, from the root <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“barren”). Exact cognate with Old Armenian <i>ստերջ</i> (sterǰ, “barren, infertile”). Further compare English <i>steer</i>, Albanian <i>shterrë</i>, Latin <i>sterilis</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्तरी</i> (starī).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>στείχω</i> (steíkhō)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>στεῖχε</i> (steîkhe)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>στείχῃ</i> (steíkhēi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;defective) to behave as if about to, to promise or threaten to; to be eager to</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to support, establish</li><li>to attach</li><li>to found</li><li>to stand up</li><li>to lean on</li></ol><p>Likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sterh₁-</i> (“stiff, fixed, infertile”) and related to <i>στερεός</i> (stereós, “stiff, hard”), though the morphological details are unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed, straw mattress</li></ol><p>From <i>στιβάς</i> (stibás) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm, steadfast, secure</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>στείβω</i> (steíbō, “to tread on, stamp on”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A walker, a traveller</li><li>A fuller</li></ol><p>From <i>στείβω</i> (steíbō, “to tread”) and +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spot</li><li>moment of time, instant</li></ol><p>From <i>στίζω</i> (stízō, “to brand”) +&lrm; <i>-μή</i> (-mḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to mark (put a mark upon)</li></ol><p>From <i>στίγμα</i> (stígma) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glittering, glistening</li></ol><p>From <i>στίλβω</i> (stílbō, “to glisten, glitter”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to march in rows</li></ol><p>From <i>στίχος</i> (stíkhos) +&lrm; <i>-άομαι</i> (-áomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>variegated tunic</li></ol><p>From <i>στίχη</i> (stíkhē, “tunic”) +&lrm; <i>-άριον</i> (-árion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A scraper to remove oil and dirt from the skin, strigil</li><li>A sort of tiara overlaid with metal</li></ol><p>A loanword from an unknown source. The variants could point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a colonnade</li><li>a roofed porch or walkway having one or more aisles framed and supported by one or more colonnades: portico; arcade, cloister, piazza (e.g. the Stoa Basileios and the Stoa Poikile in the ancient Agora of Athens).</li><li>a building marked by having such a roofed and colonnaded porch or walkway (e.g. the Stoa of Attalos in the ancient Agora of Athens).</li><li>royal court</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stōyyā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stoh₂-w-ih₂</i>, ultimately from the root <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”), whence also <i>στῦλος</i> (stûlos, “column, pillar”). Cognates include Old English <i>stōwian</i>, <i>stōw</i> (English <i>stow</i>), Sanskrit <i>स्थावर</i> (sthāvará), Persian ستاوند (sotâvand), Central Kurdish ئه‌ستونده (estunde), Lithuanian <i>stovéti</i>, <i>stovà</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>ставити</i> (staviti). Compare <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorny burnet (<i>Sarcopterium spinosum</i>)</li><li>cushion, pad</li><li>padding, stuffing</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) foundation-course below stylobate</li><li>sheaf, shock, heap of corn</li></ol><p>From <i>στείβω</i> (steíbō, “to tread, stamp”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē). The "cushion" and "padding" senses are from the custom of filling pillows and mattresses using shrub leaves.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in rows one behind another</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an aromatic plant, <i>Lavandula stoechas</i></li></ol><p><i>στοῖχος</i> (stoîkhos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flavoured with the plant στοιχάς (stoikhás)</li></ol><p><i>στοιχάς</i> (stoikhás, stem: <i>στοιχᾰδ-</i>, <i>stoikhad-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prepared from the plant στοιχάς (stoikhás)</li></ol><p><i>στοιχάς</i> (stoikhás, stem: <i>στοιχᾰδ-</i>, <i>stoikhad-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an element, a principle</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the signs of the Zodiac</li></ol><p><i>στοιχειόω</i> (stoikheióō) (<i>from στοιχεῖον</i>) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elementary instruction, an elementary treatise</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the alphabet</li></ol><li>enchantment</li></ol><p><i>στοιχειόω</i> (stoikheióō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>literal, alphabetic</li></ol><p><i>στοιχεῖον</i> (stoikheîon, “a simple sound of the voice, as the first element of language”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰκός</i> (-ăkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>literally, alphabetically</li></ol><p><i>στοιχειᾰκός</i> (stoikheiăkós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a worshipper of the elements of nature as having divine qualities, idolater</li></ol><p><i>στοιχεῖον</i> (stoikheîon) +&lrm; <i>-λᾰ́τρης</i> (-lắtrēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used especially of&#32;grammar</i>) elementarily</li></ol><p><i>στοιχειώδης</i> (stoikheiṓdēs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>persons who cast nativities from the signs of the Zodiac</li></ol><p>Substantivisation of the masculine plural of the assumed adjective <i>&#42;στοιχειωμᾰτῐκός</i> (&#42;stoikheiōmătĭkós), from <i>στοιχεώμᾰτᾰ</i> (stoikheṓmătă, “the signs of the Zodiac”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>said of grammarians</i>) a teacher of elements</li></ol><p><i>στοιχεῖον</i> (stoikheîon) +&lrm; <i>-ωτής</i> (-ōtḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elementary</li><li>magical</li></ol><p><i>στοιχείωσῐς</i> (stoikheíōsĭs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach or set down as elements</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the&#32;passive voice</i>) to be trained, to be disciplined</li></ol><li>to enchant, to charm</li></ol><p><i>στοιχεῖον</i> (stoikheîon) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>used especially of grammar</i>) elementary</li></ol><p>From <i>στοιχεῖον</i> (stoikheîon) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs); compare <i>στοιχώδης</i> (stoikhṓdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>properly</i>) one of a row:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the sun-dial</i>) the shadow of the gnomon, which advances regularly hour after hour</li></ol><li>(<i>generally</i>) one of a series, a component part, an element:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a simple sound of the voice, as the first element of language; an elementary sound, as distinct from a letter (γράμμα, <i>grámma</i>)</li><li>(physics,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) any one of the component parts of matter, an element</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>Aristotelianism</i>) the material cause of a thing, as opposed to the formal or motive cause (ἀρχή, <i>arkhḗ</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the elements of knowledge and the sciences</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>geometry</i>) points, lines, surfaces</li><li>(<i>arithmetic</i>) units</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) parts of speech</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) the major premises of syllogisms</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) commonplaces</li></ol><li>(<i>generally</i>) a simple or elementary principle</li><li>(<i>in later writers</i>) a planet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>especially</i>) a sign of the Zodiac</li></ol></ol></ol><p><i>στοῖχος</i> (stoîkhos) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a row</li><li>(<i>epigraphy</i>) as a stoichedon (text written such that the letters are arranged as rows and columns within a grid arrangement), or in the manner of one</li></ol><p>From <i>στοῖχος</i> (stoîkhos, “rows”) +&lrm; <i>-ηδόν</i> (-ēdón, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equipment</li><li>robe</li></ol><p>From <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “to clothe”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē). Compare deverbal English <i>dress</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) groaning, wailing, sighing</li></ol><p>From <i>στενάχω</i> (stenákhō, “to groan, wail, sigh”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mournful, sad, wretched</li></ol><p>From <i>στόνος</i> (stónos) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis), thus literally “causing groans or sighs”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love, affection; especially of parents and children</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>rarely</i>) sexual love</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>στέργω</i> (stérgō, “to love”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aim for, shoot at</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to endeavor after, aim at</li></ol><li>to try to make out, to guess at</li></ol><p>From <i>στόχος</i> (stókhos, “target”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an erection of stone or wood for fixing net poles</li><li>(<i>adjective</i>) in a row</li><li>synonym of <i>στόχος</i> (stókhos, “butt, target”)</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but suffixed with <i>-άς</i> (-ás). Compare with <i>στοῖχος</i> (stoîkhos, “row in an ascending series, column”), <i>στίχος</i> (stíkhos, “row or file of soldiers, line of poetry, verse”), from <i>στείχω</i> (steíkhō, “walk, march, go or come, march in line or order”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steygʰ-</i> (“to walk”). If from this root, cognate with German <i>steigen</i>; English <i>sty</i>, <i>stair</i>, <i>stile</i>; and possibly Latin <i>vestīgō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable of hitting</li><li>Acting based on guesswork</li></ol><p>From <i>στόχος</i> (stókhos, “aim, target”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>row in an ascending series</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>architecture</i>) the first course of masonry steps,</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) course of bricks</li><li>file of persons marching one behind another, as in a procession</li><li>(<i>of ships, columns</i>)</li><li>(<i>of soldiers, file</i>)</li><li>(<i>of deer swimming</i>)</li><li>(<i>of the files of the chorus in plays</i>)</li><li>row of columns</li><li>(<i>of factors</i>)</li><li>(<i>of verses</i>)</li></ol><li>a line of poles supporting hunting nets, into which the game were driven</li><li>turn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stóikʰos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stóygʰ-os</i>, from <i>&#42;steygʰ-</i> (“go, climb”). By surface analysis, a nominal derivative of <i>στείχω</i> (steíkhō, “to walk, march”).</p><p>Cognate of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staigō</i>, Latvian <i>staĩga</i>, Albanian <i>shteg</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्तिघ्नोति</i> (stighnoti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trickle, (squeezed-out) drop</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strengʰ-</i> (“to twist; rope”) (with morphological formation similar to that of <i>στρῐ́γξ</i> (strĭ́nx) and <i>λῠ́γξ</i> (lŭ́nx)), and compared with Latin <i>stringō</i> (“to tie together”), Latvian <i>stringt</i> (“to become stiff”), and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;strangiz</i> (“string”). The sense development would be "to twist" > "to wrench, squeeze out" > "squeezed-out drop", while the root <i>στραγγ-</i> (strang-) would arise as a contamination of a zero-grade <i>&#42;στραγ-</i> (&#42;strag-) (&lt; <i>&#42;strn̥gʰ-</i>) and an e-grade <i>&#42;στρεγγ-</i> (&#42;streng-) (&lt; <i>&#42;strengʰ-</i>), with a substitution of <i>&#42;g</i> for <i>&#42;gʰ</i> in the nominal singular <i>στράγξ</i> (stránx). However, according to Beekes, it is not improbable that the word is Pre-Greek, due to the Indo-European derivation requiring several analogies and semantic shifts.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army, host</li></ol><p>From <i>στρατεύω</i> (strateúō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is twisted, thread</li><li>twist, roll</li><li>wrench, stram, sprain</li><li>conspiracy, band of conspirators</li></ol><p>From <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “to twist”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to twist</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strebʰ-</i> (“twist”), and compared with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;stroppōn</i> (“to twist, writhe”). However, Beekes and Van Beek are skeptical based on a comparison with <i>στρεβλός</i> (streblós, “twisted, crooked”) and its likely cognates displaying β/φ variation (see there for more), and prefer to take the word as Pre-Greek.</p><p>Regardless, English <i>strap</i> (“to fasten”) is cognate, via borrowing from Latin and ultimately Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a turning round</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) deceit</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “to turn”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>στρῐ́ξ</i> (strĭ́x, “owl”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>owl</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>γλαῦξ</i> (glaûx) and <i>λῠ́γξ</i> (lŭ́nx). The Greek word is often assumed to be influenced by or derived from <i>τρῑ́ζω</i> (trī́zō, “to squeak”), but its facultative nasal may point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have distorted eyes, to squint</li></ol><p>From <i>στραβός</i> (strabós, “distorted, squinting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a squinting</li></ol><p>From <i>στραβίζω</i> (strabízō, “to squint”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós), from <i>στραβός</i> (strabós, “distorted, squinting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>distorted, oblique</li><li>squinting, squint-eyed</li></ol><p>Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strebʰ-</i>, the same root of <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “to twist”) and <i>στρεβλός</i> (streblós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>halter</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze out</li></ol><p>From <i>στρᾰ́γξ</i> (strắnx, “drop”) (genitive <i>στρᾰγγός</i> (străngós) + <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intricate knot</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) knot or induration in the breast or other parts</li><li>some kind of ornament</li></ol><p>From <i>στραγγάλη</i> (strangálē, “halter”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be twisted or knotted up</li><li>to be strangled</li></ol><p>From <i>στραγγᾰ́λη</i> (strangắlē, “halter”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) strangury</li></ol><p>From <i>στρᾰγγουρέω</i> (străngouréō, “to suffer from strangury”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>στρᾰ́γξ</i> (strắnx, “drop”) + <i>οὐρέω</i> (ouréō, “to urinate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twisted</li></ol><p>From <i>στρᾰ́γξ</i> (strắnx, “squeezed-out drop”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act of a general, piece of generalship</li><li>trick, device, ploy, stratagem, tactic</li></ol><p>From <i>στρατηγέω</i> (stratēgéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>campaign, expedition</li></ol><p><i>στρᾰτεύω</i> (străteúō, “wage war”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾱ</i> (-ĭā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) advance with an army or fleet, wage war</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) serve in the army</li></ol><p><i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, verbal suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>στρᾰτείᾱ</i> (străteíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a general</li></ol><p><i>στρᾰτηγός</i> (strătēgós, “general”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office of general, command</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>period of command, campaign</li></ol><li>generalship</li><li>province governed by a στρατηγός</li></ol><p>From <i>στρᾰτηγός</i> (strătēgós, “leader of an army”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a general</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) suited or fitted for command, versed in generalship</li><li>(<i>at Rome</i>) praetorian</li></ol><p>From <i>στρᾰτηγός</i> (strătēgós, “leader, commander, general”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A leader or commander of an army: general</li><li>The top official in any capacity (often used of various Roman ranks: consul, magistrate, etc.)</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>στρατός</i> (stratós, “army”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army</li><li>host, company, band</li><li>expedition</li></ol><p><i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier, warrior</li><li>mercenary</li></ol><p>From an unattested <i>&#42;στρατιάομαι</i> (&#42;stratiáomai) + <i>-της</i> (-tēs). Another analysis (ultimately from the same root) takes the word as a derivative in <i>-ιώτης</i> (-iṓtēs) of <i>στρατός</i> (stratós, “troop, department of the people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) camp, encamp, bivouac</li></ol><p><i>στρατόπεδον</i> (stratópedon) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commanding officer</li></ol><p>From <i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camp, encampment, campsite</li><li>host, army</li></ol><p>From <i>στρατός</i> (stratós, “army”) +&lrm; <i>πέδον</i> (pédon, “ground”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>army, military force</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in Homer</i>) the soldiery, the people, exclusive of the chiefs; the common people</li><li>any band or body of men, as of the Amphictyons</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;strətós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;str̥tós</i>, from <i>&#42;ster-</i> (“to spread, stretch out, extend”). The original meaning was probably "camping army" (stretched over an area).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>strātus</i>, Old Irish <i>srath</i> (“grass, grassy place”), and Sanskrit <i>स्तृत</i> (stṛtá-, “bestrewn”). Compare <i>στορέννυμι</i> (storénnumi).</p><p>Compare <i>στρωτός</i> (strōtós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>twisted, crooked, awry</li><li>knit, wrinkled (of the brows)</li><li>crooked, cunning</li><li>perverse, froward</li></ol><p>Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strebʰ-</i> (“to twist”), which <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “to twist”) is also often derived from. However, due to the irregular correspondence of β (/b/) with φ (/pʰ/) and the connection with words like <i>στρόβιλος</i> (stróbilos, “fir-cone”) and <i>στρόμβος</i> (strómbos, “spinning top”) (whose suffix <i>-ιλ-</i> (-il-) seems to be indicative of a substrate), Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin for both <i>στρεβλός</i> (streblós) and <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Easily twisted, pliant.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>substantive</i>) Collar of twisted or linked metal.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of pastry</i>) Twist, roll.</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) Bent or turned.</li><li>Bent, curved.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “I turn, I twist”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, “able to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm, hard, solid, rigid</li></ol><p>Reminiscent of <i>στιφρός</i> (stiphrós), <i>στέριφος</i> (stériphos), <i>στρυφνός</i> (struphnós), perhaps a contamination. Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ter(h₁)-</i> (“to be stiff; be rigid; exert”) and related to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stribāną</i> (“to strive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Plug of lint twisted into an oval shape like a pinecone.</li></ol><p>From <i>στρόβος</i> (stróbos, “whirling around”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́λη</i> (-ī́lē, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to a pine cone</li></ol><p>From <i>στρόβῑλος</i> (stróbīlos, “round ball; pine cone”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of round form.</li></ol><p>From <i>στρογγύλος</i> (strongúlos, “round, spherical”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-like, -form”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a round stalk</li></ol><p>From <i>στρογγῠ́λος</i> (strongŭ́los, “round”) +&lrm; <i>καυλός</i> (kaulós, “stem, stalk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A round pot or flask; a strongylium or strongylion.</li></ol><p>From <i>στρογγῠ́λος</i> (strongŭ́los, “round; spherical”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, nominative diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spherical, round</li><li>(<i>of words, expressions</i>) well-rounded, neat, pithy, terse</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin.</p><p>According to Frisk, an adjectival formation from the o-grade of <i>στράγξ</i> (stránx, “trickle, drop”) (as drops tend to be rounded), and/or with morphological influence from <i>γογγῠ́λος</i> (gongŭ́los, “round, spherical”).</p><p>However, Beekes is skeptical of the semantic link to <i>στράγξ</i> (stránx), and in light of the suffix <i>-υλ-</i> (-ul-) also found in <i>κᾰμπῠ́λος</i> (kămpŭ́los, “bent, curved”) and <i>ἀγκύλος</i> (ankúlos, “crooked, curved”), suggests a Pre-Greek origin for the word (with or without <i>στράγξ</i> (stránx)). Though this is possible, it is worth noting that the root of <i>ἀγκύλος</i> (ankúlos) is derived by Beekes himself from Proto-Indo-European, implying that the suffix, rather than the roots of the words, is from a substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chirp like a sparrow, twitter</li><li>to cleanse with soapwort</li></ol><p>From <i>στρουθός</i> (strouthós, “house sparrow”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p><p>From <i>στρούθῐον</i> (stroúthĭon, “soapwort”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>στρουθοκάμηλος</i> (strouthokámēlos, “ostrich”)</li></ol><p><i>στρουθός</i> (strouthós) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ostrich (<i>Struthio camelus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>στρουθός</i> (strouthós, “sparrow”) +&lrm; <i>κάμηλος</i> (kámēlos, “camel”), from its camel-like neck.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>house sparrow (<i>Passer domesticus</i>)</li><li>flatfish, European flounder (<i>Platichthys flesus</i>, syn. <i>Pleuronectes flesus</i>).</li><li>ostrich</li></ol><p>The etymology remains highly uncertain, but Beekes suspects a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirl; eddy</li><li>curve; bend</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) orbit of a heavenly body</li><li>anything with a round shape; like a cheese</li></ol><p>From <i>στροφᾰλῐ́ζω</i> (strophălĭ́zō, “to twist; to spin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turning, a turn</li><li>revolving</li><li>twisting</li></ol><p>From <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “twist”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for turning, turned</li></ol><p>From <i>στροφή</i> (strophḗ, “turning”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of soapwort</li></ol><p>From <i>στρούθιον</i> (stroúthion, “soapwort”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soapwort (<i>Saponaria officinalis</i>)</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) little sparrow</li></ol><p>From <i>στρουθός</i> (strouthós, “sparrow”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sour, bitter, harsh, astringent</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) harsh, severe, austere</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Possibly from the same root as <i>στύφω</i> (stúphō, “to astringe”), influenced by <i>στριφνός</i> (striphnós, “firm, hard, solid”) or <i>στρηνής</i> (strēnḗs, “hard, harsh”). The further comparison with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;strūb</i> (“stiff, rough, bristly”), Lithuanian <i>strùbas</i> (“cut short, curtailed”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strupъ</i> (“scab, crust of a wound”) is not very semantically strong and thus uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has the care of the bedding, tablecloths</li></ol><p>From <i>στρῶμᾰ</i> (strômă, “mattress, bedcloth”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leathern or linen sack in which slaves had to tie up bedclothes</li></ol><p>From <i>στρῶμα</i> (strôma, “bedclothes”) +&lrm; <i>δεσμός</i> (desmós, “bond, fetter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed spread or prepared</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) bed, couch</li><li>mattress, bedding</li></ol><p>Perhaps from an adjective <i>&#42;στρωμνός</i> (&#42;strōmnós, “spread”) <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ), itself from <i>στρῶμα</i> (strôma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) rafter laid upon the bearing beam</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) crossbeam, girder</li></ol><p>From <i>στρώννυμι</i> (strṓnnumi, “to spread, stretch”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spread, laid, covered</li></ol><p>From <i>στρώννῡμι</i> (strṓnnūmi, “to spread”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós). See <i>στόρνυμι</i> (stórnumi, “to spread”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>round ball</li><li>spinning top</li><li>cyclone, whirlwind</li><li>twist or turn in music</li><li>whirling dance, pirouette</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) pinecone</li><li>fir, pine, especially the Swiss stone pine (<i>Pinus cembra</i>)</li><li>winch or perhaps rotating shaft</li></ol><p>From <i>στρόβ(ος)</i> (strób(os), “whirl”) + <i>-ιλος</i> (-ilos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirl</li></ol><p>Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;strebʰ-</i>, the same root of <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “to twist”) and <i>στρεβλός</i> (streblós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any body rounded or spun round</li><li>spinning top</li><li>whirlwind, vortex</li><li>trumpet shell, sea snail</li><li>shell used as a trumpet, conch</li><li>land snail</li><li>spindle</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the same Pre-Greek root of <i>στρεβλός</i> (streblós, “twisted, turned”), with a nasal infix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pivot, axle or pin on which a body turns</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) pivots working in sockets, at top and bottom of a door</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “to turn; to rotate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band worn by women around the breasts</li><li>headband worn by priests</li><li>boxing glove</li></ol><p>From <i>στρόφος</i> (stróphos, “twisted band”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a twisted band or cord</li><li>a swathing cloth, swaddling band</li><li>a twisting of the bowels, colic</li></ol><p>From the o-grade of <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “to twist, turn”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of various nightshades:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>winter cherry (<i>Alkekengi officinarum</i>, syn. <i>Physalis alkekengi</i>)</li><li>black nightshade (<i>Solanum nigrum</i>)</li><li>thorn apple (<i>Datura stramonium</i>)</li><li>ashwagandha (<i>Withania somnifera</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>στρύχνον</i> (strúkhnon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>στόρνῡμι</i> (stórnūmi, “to spread, smooth out”)</li></ol><p>See <i>στόρνῡμι</i> (stórnūmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mattress; bed</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) bedclothes</li><li>horsecloth</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>στρώννῡμῐ</i> (strṓnnūmĭ) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă). Given the existence of parallel formations in Sanskrit <i>स्तरी॑मन्</i> (stárīman) and Latin <i>strāmen</i> (“litter; straw for bedding”), it may derive from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;strṓmə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stérh₃mn̥</i> (“that which is strewn; bedding”). However, the EIEC suggests that these formations may be independent, but parallel innovations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hate, abominate, abhor, detest</li><li>to make hateful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stugéō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stug-éyeti</i>, from <i>&#42;(s)tewg-</i> (“to push, hit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hated, abominated, loathed</li><li>hateful, wretched, miserable</li></ol><p>From <i>στυγέω</i> (stugéō) +&lrm; <i>-ερός</i> (-erós), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stugéō</i>, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewg-</i> (“to hit, push”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hated, abhorred (of persons and things)</li><li>hateful or hostile to one</li><li>gloomy, sullen</li></ol><p>From <i>στυγέω</i> (stugéō) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós), of uncertain origin, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewg-</i> (“to hit, push”) (compare Sanskrit <i>तुज्</i> (tuj, “to push, drive”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one standing or dwelling on a pillar, a stylite (Christian ascetic cloistered atop a pillar)</li></ol><p><i>στῦλος</i> (stûlos, “pillar”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, “-ite”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the base of a column, stylobate</li></ol><p>From <i>στῦλος</i> (stûlos, “pillar”) +&lrm; <i>-βᾰ́της</i> (-bắtēs, “walker, from <i>βαίνω</i> (“to walk, to tread”)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Like a stylus, styloid.</li></ol><p>From <i>στῦλος</i> (stûlos, “a pillar”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-like, -oid”). Compare English <i>styloid</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>solid, hard</li></ol><p>According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stewe-</i> (“to get dense, tight”), comparable to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;studъ</i> (“cold”), Sanskrit <i>स्तुका</i> (stukā, “tight tuft of wool”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of oakum</li></ol><p>From <i>στυππεῖον</i> (stuppeîon, “oakum”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The coarse fibre of flax or hemp, tow, oakum</li></ol><p>There are no known cognates. Furnée compares the word with <i>τοπεῖον</i> (topeîon, “rope”). According to Beekes the noun is probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) an astringent salt, earth containing alum or ferrous sulfate</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) the alum monopoly (in Egypt)</li></ol><p>From <i>στύφω</i> (stúphō, “to be astringent”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>astringent</li></ol><p>From <i>στῡ́φω</i> (stū́phō, “to contract”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós). Compare Sanskrit <i>स्थापन</i> (sthāpana, “stopping the flow of blood, styptic”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of storax</li></ol><p>From <i>στύραξ</i> (stúrax, “storax”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, rough</li><li>astringent, sour, acid</li><li>harsh, severe, cruel</li></ol><p>From <i>στυφελίζω</i> (stuphelízō, “to strike hard, beat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wordy, talkative, loquacious</li><li>(<i>in good sense</i>) fluent, suave</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared with <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”), with full grade of the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;stomn̥</i> and a suffix <i>&#42;-ul-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expedition</li><li>army, fleet, troop</li></ol><p>From <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “to send”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). See also <i>στολή</i> (stolḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth (especially as the organ of speech)</li><li>face</li><li>The source of a river or stream</li><li>An opening or fissure in the earth</li><li>The frontmost part of something</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₃-mn̥</i> (“mouth, muzzle”). Cognate with Avestan <i>𐬯𐬙𐬀𐬨𐬀𐬥</i> (staman, “mouth (of a dog)”), Welsh <i>safn</i> (“mouth, jaws”), Hittite (ištāman-, “ear”), as well as, despite Beekes's objections, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stebnō</i> (“voice, sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat, gullet</li><li>stomach</li><li>bladder</li></ol><p>From <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”), with a suffix <i>-χος</i> (-khos) (also found in <i>οὐρᾰχός</i> (ourăkhós, “urachus”)), the latter of unclear and probably Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small mouth</li><li>mouth of a vessel</li><li>mouth of a cave used as a grave</li><li>any aperture or opening</li><li>cavity from which winds issue</li><li>mouth of a canal</li><li>socket of a bolt</li><li>bridle-bit, bit of a curb</li><li>female ornament for the neck</li></ol><p>From <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sighing, groaning, lamentation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stónos</i> (whence also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stonъ</i> (“moan, groan”)), from <i>&#42;sten-</i> (“to moan, groan”). By surface analysis, <i>στένω</i> (sténō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharp point, as of a rock</li><li>peak of a fang; of a claw</li></ol><p>Assumed to be a cross of <i>ὄνυξ</i> (ónux, “nail”) and <i>στόχος</i> (stókhos, “pillar of brick”), <i>στάχυς</i> (stákhus, “ear of wheat”) or <i>στόρθυγξ</i> (stórthunx, “spike, tine”). Beekes suggests it's Pre-Greek in view of the suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point, spike</li><li>tine, tusk</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smooth out, spread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;stórnūmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sterh₃-</i> (“to spread, extend”) via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;str̥néh₃ti</i>. Likely related to Swedish <i>strö</i> (“spread, sprinkle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillar of brick</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>synonym of <i>στοχάς</i> (stokhás, “an erection of stone or wood for fixing net poles”)</li><li>butt, target</li><li>aim, aiming</li><li>guess, conjecture</li></ol></ol><p>Uncertain.</p><p>Beekes tentatively derives the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stegʰ-</i> (“bar, pillar, rod”), and compares Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;stagas</i>, whence Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;stogъ</i> (“haystack”) and Lithuanian <i>stãgaras</i> (“long thin stalk”), as well as Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stangō</i> (“bar, rod, stake”). Compare also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stakkaz</i> (“haystack”).</p><p>The "target" sense (whence the "aim" and "guess" senses) is from the tradition of archers shooting at pillars to practice their aim.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stem, stump, block</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected with Old Norse <i>stúfr</i> (“tree stump”) and Latvian <i>stups</i> (“broom”). See Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewp-</i>. According to Beekes, a Pre-Greek origin is a good possibility due to the presence of the byform <i>στύμος</i> (stúmos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>στυππεῖον</i> (stuppeîon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>στυππέϊνος</i> (stuppéïnos, “made of oakum”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storax tree (<i>Styrax officinalis</i>)</li><li>resin produced by this tree</li><li>spike at the lower end of a spear shaft</li></ol><p>Probably of Semitic origin, because this resin was introduced by the Phoenicians.</p><p>The formation is like <i>κάμαξ</i> (kámax) and <i>χάραξ</i> (khárax). According to Beekes, it's either from the name of the tree or from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contract, draw together</li><li>(<i>in dyeing</i>) to treat fabrics with a mordant</li><li>to be astringent, be costive</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be harsh, austere, gloomy</li></ol><p>Formally similar to <i>στύω</i> (stúō, “to make stiff or erect”), which may be related; if so, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sth₂-u-</i>, an extension of <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”). Otherwise of unknown origin; that said, note similarities to <i>στρῠφνός</i> (strŭphnós, “astringent, severe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make stiff, erect</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden beam</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”). Cognate with Latin <i>stāmen</i>, Ancient Greek <i>στάμνος</i> (stámnos, “jar”) and <i>στήμων</i> (stḗmōn, “warp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breast (of both sexes)</li><li>breast as the seat of emotions, feelings</li><li>ball of the foot or thumb</li><li>(<i>oncology</i>) swelling, tumour</li><li>breast-shaped hill or mound</li></ol><p>As this word is also Doric and Aeolic, the connection with <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ, “to stand”) must be given up. The similarity to <i>στήνιον</i> (stḗnion), Old Armenian <i>ստին</i> (stin, “breast”) and Sanskrit <i>स्तन॑</i> (stána, “female breast”) is hardly coincidental. If cognate, the relation between this word and <i>στήνιον</i> (stḗnion) would be like that of Latin <i>plēnus</i> (“full”) to <i>πλῆθος</i> (plêthos, “multitude”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>support, prop, stay</li><li>fork with which the shaft or pole of a chariot was propped</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ster</i> (“strong, steady”), the same root of Old English <i>starian</i> (“to stare”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;staraz</i> (“stiff”) and <i>στερεός</i> (stereós, “firm, solid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pillar, column, support</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wooden pole</li><li>stile</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from some extension, likely <i>&#42;sth₂-u-lo-s</i>, of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”); compare <i>στῡ́ω</i> (stū́ō, “to make stiff”). See also Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;stʰuHnáH</i> (“column, pillar”), Albanian <i>shtyllë</i>, Lithuanian <i>stulpas</i> (“pillar”), Russian <i>столб</i> (stolb), Bulgarian <i>стълб</i> (stǎlb). Despite superficial phonetic and semantic similarities, unrelated to Latin <i>stilus</i> (“stake, stylus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) priapism</li></ol><p>From <i>στύω</i> (stúō, “to make stiff, erect”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astringent, used especially for thickening oil, that it may retain scent better</li></ol><p>From <i>στῠ́φω</i> (stŭ́phō, “to draw together”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction, astringency</li><li>steeping of the fabric in an astringent solution, to prepare it for taking the dye</li><li>thickening of oil with certain drugs to make it retain its scent longer (in perfumery)</li></ol><p>From <i>στύφω</i> (stúphō, “to contract”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A flute case</li></ol><p>Beekes gives no etymology. The formation is like <i>σαγήνη</i> (sagḗnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>περίκομμα</i> (períkomma, “trimmings, mincemeat”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swineherd</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀱𐀦𐀲</i> (su-qo-ta), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;sugʷṓtās</i>, equivalent to <i>σῦς</i> (sûs, “swine”) +&lrm; <i>βω-</i> (bō-, “to feed”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷeh₃-</i>) + <i>-της</i> (-tēs). Compare Latin <i>subulcus</i>.</p><p>The short vowel and preservation of initial /s/ are difficult to explain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Laconian</i>) daughter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰúgatēr</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀶𐀏𐀳</i> (tu-ka-te /⁠tʰúgatēr⁠/)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰugh₂tḗr</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kinship, kindred</li></ol><p><i>συγγενής</i> (sungenḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be born with</li><li>to associate; to keep company with; to coexist with</li><li>to become acquainted or conversant with</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together; with”) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to come into being; to take take place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born with, congenital, innate, natural, inborn</li><li>of the same family, akin to, related</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>substantive</i>) kinsman, relative</li><li>(<i>neuter substantive</i>) relationship</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) akin, of like kind</li></ol><li>(<i>at the Persian court</i>) a title bestowed by the king as a mark of honour</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai, “to be born”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>συγγενής</i> (sungenḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a predisposition to disease</i>) congenital or hereditary</li><li>of or for kinsmen, between kinfolk</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) kindred, of a common kind</li></ol><p>From <i>σύν</i> (sún) + <i>γενικός</i> (genikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acknowledgement, confession</li><li>lenient judgement, allowance; fellow feeling with another</li><li>pardon, forgiveness</li></ol><p>From <i>συγγιγνώσκω</i> (sungignṓskō, “to agree with, acknowledge, excuse, pardon”) +&lrm; <i>-μη</i> (-mē); by surface analysis, <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>γνώμη</i> (gnṓmē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to write down; describe</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;causative,&#32;transitive) to have something written down</li><li>to compose (something in prose: a writing, book, speech)</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;transitive) to draw up or to sign (a contract, bond, treaty); to promise</li><li>(active voice&#32;or&#32;middle voice,&#32;politics,&#32;transitive) to draw up a motion to be submitted to a vote</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “write”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writing or noting down</li><li>writing, book, especially in prose</li><li>mark in the eye</li></ol><p>From <i>συγγράφω</i> (sungráphō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prose-writer</li><li>author</li><li>historian</li><li>party to a contract</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠγγρᾰ́φω</i> (sŭngrắphō) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A contract with marriage settlement</li></ol><p>From <i>συγγραφή</i> (sungraphḗ, “writing down”) +&lrm; <i>διαθήκη</i> (diathḗkē, “contract”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put down together, to join in putting down</li><li>to accomplish with [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>συγ-</i> (sug-) +&lrm; <i>καθαιρέω</i> (kathairéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be party with another in injuring, join in the injury</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>κακοῦργος</i> (kakoûrgos, “malicious”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verb forming suffix), from <i>κακός</i> (kakós, “bad”) + <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call to council; summon, convoke, convene, assemble</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to invite (guests), call together others to oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>κᾰλέω</i> (kăléō, “to call”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kelh₁-</i> (“to call, shout”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>approving, assenting; affirmative</li></ol><p>From <i>συγκατάθεσις</i> (sunkatáthesis, “approval, assent”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós), the former component morphologically analyzable as <i>συγ-</i> (sug-) +&lrm; <i>κατάθεσις</i> (katáthesis), from <i>κατα-</i> (kata-) + <i>θέσις</i> (thésis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell with one</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “inhabit, dwell”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incur danger along with (<i>+ dative</i>)</li><li>to be partners in danger</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>κινδῡνεύω</i> (kindūneúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>senatorial, pertaining to a senate; belonging to or of an assembly or council of elders (as at classical Athens or Rome)</li><li>(Koine,&#32;history) senatorial; of senatorial rank; belonging to the Roman Senate of the Roman Republic or Roman Empire in Ancient Rome</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;history) senatorial; of senatorial rank; belonging to the Byzantine Senate of the Byzantine Roman Empire in Constantinople</li><li>summoning, beckoning, calling for, drawing together</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(history,&#32;government,&#32;politics) senator; someone of senatorial rank in the Roman Senate; member of a convened council, senate, assembly of elders, or similar upper house legislative body</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠ́γκλητος</i> (sŭ́nklētos, “senate”) +&lrm; <i>-κός</i> (-kós) from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>κλητός</i> (klētós, “called”) from <i>σῠγκᾰλέω</i> (sŭnkăléō, “to call to council, convoke, convene”), equivalent to <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-) +&lrm; <i>κᾰλέω</i> (kăléō, “to call, summon”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, suffix forming adjectives). In historical sense, a semantic loan from Latin <i>senātōrius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cutting up, cutting into small pieces</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>grammar</i>) syncope, cutting a word short by striking out one or more letters</li></ol><li>collision</li><li>sudden loss of strength, syncope</li></ol><p>From <i>συγκόπτω</i> (sunkóptō, “cut up”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the federation of Cretan cities</li><li>setting aside one's differences to form an alliance</li></ol><p>From <i>συγκρητίζω</i> (sunkrētízō) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós), or possibly from <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>Κρῆτες</i> (Krêtes, “Cretans”) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós, “-ism”) if the verb is a back-formation. First appears in Plutarch's <i>Moralia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strike together</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring into collision</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to clash together, come into collision</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>κρούω</i> (kroúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover up completely, conceal</li><li>to join or help in concealing</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “together, with”) +&lrm; <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break up, cut up</li><li>to thrash soundly, pound well</li></ol><p>From <i>συγ-</i> (sug-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of shoe or sock</li></ol><p>Probably from a substrate language of Asia Minor, possibly Phrygian; said language may also be the ultimate source of Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬑𐬀</i> (haxa, “sole of the foot”). See the variant <i>σύκχος</i> (súkkhos) for more descendants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to congratulate</li><li>to rejoice together with</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>take the same time</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠ́γχρονος</i> (sŭ́nkhronos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get out of the way, make way, give way, yield or defer to</li><li>to accede, assent to, acquiesce in</li><li>to concede, yield, give up</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō, “to withdraw, give way”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) A junction of two bones by cartilage.</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>χόνδρος</i> (khóndros, “gristle, cartilage”) +&lrm; <i>-ωσῐς</i> (-ōsĭs, “state, condition”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint inquiry, discussion</li><li>disputation</li></ol><p><i>σῠζητέω</i> (sŭzētéō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to search or examine together with</li><li>to discuss or dispute with</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-) +&lrm; <i>ζητέω</i> (zētéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>union, junction</li><li>yoke of animals</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) pair</li><li>squadron of four war-chariots</li><li>coupling, copulation</li><li>conjunction of words or things in pairs</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) conjugation or declension</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) syzygy, alignment of celestial bodies</li></ol><p>From <i>σύζυγος</i> (súzugos, “yoked together, paired”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black mulberry (<i>Morus nigra</i>)</li><li>white mulberry (<i>Morus alba</i>)</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>שִׁקְמָה</i> (shikmá, “sycamore”), with assimilation to <i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig tree</li></ol><p>From <i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”) +&lrm; <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beccafico (<i>Sylvia hortensis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”), with a suffix found in other bird names, like <i>ὀρταλίς</i> (ortalís, “hen, fowl”) and <i>πυραλίς</i> (puralís, “kind of bird”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to the mulberry tree</li></ol><p>From <i>σῡκᾰ́μῑνος</i> (sūkắmīnos, “mulberry tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>συκόμορος</i> (sukómoros)</li></ol><p>From <i>συκόμορος</i> (sukómoros), reinforced as a tree name with <i>-έα</i> (-éa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fig-eating</li></ol><p>From <i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”) +&lrm; <i>τράγω</i> (trágō, “to munch”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>informant, denouncer (in court)</li><li>professional swindler</li><li>extortioner, oppressor</li></ol><p><i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “show”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vexatious or dishonest prosecution, chicane, barratry, blackmail</li><li>oppression, extortion</li><li>quibble, sophism, deception</li></ol><p>From <i>σῡκοφᾰ́ντης</i> (sūkophắntēs, “denouncer, informer”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>typical of a sycophant, blackmailing</li><li>sophistical</li></ol><p>From <i>σῡκοφᾰ́ντης</i> (sūkophắntēs, “sycophant”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sycamore (<i>Ficus sycomorus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”) +&lrm; <i>μόρον</i> (móron, “mulberry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig leaf</li></ol><p>From <i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig-yard</li></ol><p>From <i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn, plantation, grove).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>σῡκέᾱ</i> (sūkéā, “fig tree”)</li></ol><p>Synaeresis of <i>σῡκέᾱ</i> (sūkéā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pillage, plunder, rob, despoil</li></ol><p><i>σῡ́λη</i> (sū́lē) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather, collect, bring together</li><li>to call together, to raise or levy</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to choose, to say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in sum, in short</li></ol><p>From <i>ληβ-</i> (lēb-), <i>e</i>-grade of the root of <i>συλλαμβάνω</i> (sullambánō, “to gather together”), +&lrm; <i>-δην</i> (-dēn, deverbal adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conception, pregnancy</li><li>that which holds together, i.e. a girdle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that which is held together</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) several letters combined, syllable</li></ol><li>(<i>music</i>) the perfect fourth</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠλλᾰμβᾰ́νω</i> (sŭllămbắnō, “bring together”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect, gather; to rally</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to take with me, carry off</li><li>to put together, close, enclose</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to combine in pronunciation</li></ol><li>to comprise</li></ol><li>to take hold of, seize</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to apprehend, arrest</li><li>to comprehend</li></ol><li>to receive at the same time</li><li>to conceive a child</li><li>to take with (e.g. as an assistant)</li><li>(<i>with dative of person</i>) to assist</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(middle voice,&#32;with genitive of object) to take part in</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>λᾰμβᾰ́νω</i> (lămbắnō, “to take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gathering, collecting</li><li>(<i>of soldiers</i>) raising, levying</li><li>summary, collection of instances</li><li>assembly, meeting, summoning of a boule</li></ol><p>From <i>συλλέγω</i> (sullégō, “to gather, collect”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compute, calculate</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) to conclude from premises, infer</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>beginning with Aristotle</i>) to infer by using syllogisms, syllogistically</li></ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) to plan</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>λογίζομαι</i> (logízomai, “to calculate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>computation, calculation, rating, assessment</li><li>reasoning</li><li>plan, scheme</li><li>putting together of observed facts: inference</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>logic</i>) syllogism (<i>beginning with Aristotle</i>)</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) inference from written to unwritten law</li></ol></ol><p>Verbal noun from <i>συλλογίζομαι</i> (sullogízomai, “to compute, infer”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assist in loosening</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to assist in redeeming</li></ol><li>to solve difficulties, reconcile</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to come to a settlement</li></ol><li>to rest under the same roof</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “to loosen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring together, unite</li><li>to match or pit (combatants) against one another</li><li>to join (in fight), join battle</li><li>to make war [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘against’</small>]</li><li>to gather, recognize</li><li>to discuss, confer</li><li>to reflect, consider, ponder</li><li>to fall in with, meet with, encounter</li><li>to include</li><li>to contribute, help</li></ol><p>From <i>συμ-</i> (sum-) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a living together, companionship</li></ol><p>From <i>συμβιόω</i> (sumbióō, “to live together”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand with the feet together</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to stand with or beside, so as to assist</li><li>to meet</li></ol><li>(most commonly,&#32;figuratively) to come together, come to an agreement, come to terms</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to agree with, be on good terms with</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to coincide, correspond with</li><li>to fall to one's lot</li></ol><li>(<i>of events</i>) to come to pass, fall out, happen</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>joined with adverbs or adjectives</i>) to turn out in a certain way</li><li>(<i>of consequences</i>) to come out, result, follow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of logical conclusions</i>) to result, to follow</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to come, go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a contingent attribute or accident</li><li>any attribute, whether property or accident, necessarily resulting from the notion of a thing, so that it does not enter into the essence or definition thereof</li></ol><p>A substantivization of the neuter form of the perfect active participle of <i>σῠμβαίνω</i> (sŭmbaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coming together, meeting, joining, putting together</li><li>joint, juncture</li><li>twisting, plaiting</li><li>point of contact</li><li>bond of union</li><li>a hostile encounter, engagement</li><li>marriage-contract</li><li>(<i>plural</i>) contributions, share (made towards e.g. a common meal)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the meal or entertainment itself, picnic</li><li>contribution, subscription to the expenses of a festival etc.</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) cooperation</li></ol><p>From <i>συμβάλλω</i> (sumbállō, “to bring together”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>symbolical, figurative</li></ol><p>From <i>συμβολή</i> (sumbolḗ) and <i>σύμβολον</i> (súmbolon) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counsel</li><li>deliberation, debate</li></ol><p>From <i>σύν</i> (sún) +&lrm; <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to advise, counsel [<i>with</i> dative <i>and</i> infinitive]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to recommend</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to give advice</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to consult [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>συμ-</i> (sum-) +&lrm; <i>βουλεύω</i> (bouleúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mark, sign, token</li><li>(<i>law</i>) contract, covenant, bond</li><li>engagement, transactions</li><li>intercourse</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter singular of <i>συμβόλαιος</i> (sumbólaios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or concerning contracts, contractual</li></ol><p>From <i>σύμβολον</i> (súmbolon, “contract”) +&lrm; <i>-αιος</i> (-aios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight along with others, to be an ally, auxiliary</li></ol><p><i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χομαι</i> (mắkhomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold together, keep together</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>μένω</i> (ménō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be an ally, to be in alliance</li></ol><p>From <i>σύμμαχος</i> (súmmakhos, “fighting with”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alliance; an offensive and defensive alliance</li><li>(generally) the duty of an ally</li><li>allied or auxiliary force</li></ol><p>Abstract noun from <i>σύμμαχος</i> (súmmakhos, “fighting together”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for alliance</li></ol><p>From <i>συμμαχίᾱ</i> (summakhíā, “alliance”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix together; to commingle</li><li>to join forces</li><li>to unite</li><li>to associate; to communicate</li><li>to meet for conversation; to converse</li><li>to meet in a close fight; to come to blows; to engage with</li><li>to meet</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-) +&lrm; <i>μῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (mī́gnūmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commensurability</li><li>symmetry, due proportion</li><li>a woman's robe without a train</li></ol><p>From <i>σύμμετρος</i> (súmmetros, “symmetrical”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “measure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of moderate size</li></ol><p>From <i>σύμμετρος</i> (súmmetros) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow feeling, sympathy</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) affection, affinity</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>music</i>) (<i>of chords</i>) vibrating together</li></ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) affinity, concord of heavenly bodies</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) analogy</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) sympathetic affection of the body</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠμπᾰθής</i> (sŭmpăthḗs, “affected by like feelings; exerting mutual influence, interacting”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă, “-y”, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finishing, end</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>logic</i>) conclusion in a syllogism</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>subject of the conclusion</li></ol><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) conclusion of a proposition</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σῠμπεραίνω</i> (sŭmperaínō) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink together</li><li>to absorb</li><li>to soak</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>πίνω</i> (pínō, “to drink”). Compare Ancient Greek <i>ἀναπίνω</i> (anapínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall together, meet violently</li><li>to fall in with, meet with (especially with accidents, misfortunes)</li><li>to fall upon, happen to (<i>of accidents, ailments, symptoms, events</i>)</li><li>(<i>abstractly</i>) to happen, occur</li><li>to coincide, agree or be in accordance with</li><li>to fall together (i.e. fall in, especially of a house)</li><li>to fall together, fall into the same line</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>πῑ́πτω</i> (pī́ptō, “to fall”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affected by like feelings; sympathetic</li><li>exerting mutual influence, interacting; sensitive to influence; exciting sympathy</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) (<i>of planets</i>) in concord</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together; syn-”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰ́θος</i> (pắthos, “experience; emotion, passion; state, condition”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meeting in battle</li></ol><p>From <i>συμπαρατάσσομαι</i> (sumparatássomai, “to fight along with”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be ready at the same time</li><li>to arrive or be present at the same time; to come together</li><li>(<i>of planets</i>) to come together with</li><li>to stand by; to come in to assist</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with; together”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰρᾰγῐ́γνομαι</i> (părăgĭ́gnomai, “to be beside; to be by or near”), from <i>πᾰρᾰ-</i> (pără-, “beside; near”) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to become”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>compatriot, fellow countryman</li></ol><p>From <i>συμ-</i> (sum-) +&lrm; <i>πατριώτης</i> (patriṓtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail in company with [<i>with</i> dative] (<i>also used figuratively</i>)</li></ol><p><i>συμ-</i> (“beside, near”) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (“sail, float”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help to fill</li><li>to fill up or completely</li><li>to complete</li><li>to fulfill, attain</li><li>to finish</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to come, arrive (<i>of a specific time</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>συμ-</i> (sum-) +&lrm; <i>πληρόω</i> (plēróō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow citizen</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-) +&lrm; <i>πολίτης</i> (polítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join in war</li></ol><p><i>συμ-</i> (sum-) +&lrm; <i>πολεμέω</i> (poleméō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or fit for a drinking party, convivial</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠμπόσῐον</i> (sŭmpósĭon, “drinking party”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰκός</i> (-ăkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help to do, assist</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to join in revenging</li></ol><p><i>συμ-</i> (sum-) +&lrm; <i>πρᾱ́σσω</i> (prā́ssō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acting together, cooperating</li></ol><p>From <i>συμπράσσω</i> (sumprássō) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join in escorting</li></ol><p><i>συμ-</i> (sum-) +&lrm; <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accidental, coincidental</li></ol><p>From <i>σύμπτωμα</i> (súmptōma) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinking party, entertainment, feast, banquet, convivium, symposium</li><li>party, group</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠμπότης</i> (sŭmpótēs, “fellow drinker”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), from <i>σῠμπῑ́νω</i> (sŭmpī́nō, “to drink together”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs), from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>πῑ́νω</i> (pī́nō, “to drink”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow-drinker, boon-companion, drinking buddy</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>πότης</i> (pótēs, “drinker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to bring together, gather, collect</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to bring together, contribute</li><li>(like <i>συμβάλλω</i>) to bring into conflict</li><li>to bear along with or together, to suffer, bear with, indulge</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;without accusative of object) to confer a benefit, to be of use, be useful or profitable</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(impersonal,&#32;mostly with infinitive) it is of use, is profitable, serviceable, expedient</li><li>(<i>participle</i>) useful, expedient, fitting</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to work with, assist</li><li>to agree with</li><li>to fit, suit</li><li>(of events,&#32;with infinitive) to happen, take place, turn out</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(opposite to <i>διαφέρω</i>) to come together</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in a hostile sense</i>) to meet in battle, engage</li></ol><li>to come to terms, be of one mind, agree with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with dative of object</i>) to adapt oneself to, acquiesce in</li></ol><li>(<i>of events</i>) to happen, turn out, occur, come to pass</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to happen</li></ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) to be carried along with</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring, carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bringing together, collecting, contribution</li><li>an event, circumstance, chance</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>mishap, mischance, misfortune</li><li>(<i>rarely in a good sense</i>) good luck, happy issue</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σῠμφέρω</i> (sŭmphérō, “to occur”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;middle voice)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to join in considering, to take counsel [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>to impart (<i>counsel</i>)</li><li>to contrive</li></ol><li>(Koine,&#32;active voice) to mention at the same time</li></ol><p>from <i>συμ-</i> (sum-) +&lrm; <i>φράζω</i> (phrázō). In the older language, found only in poetry and in the middle voice; the active voice is found in later prose.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a mix up</li></ol><p>Hellenistic Koine noun, from verb <i>σῠμφῡράω</i> (sŭmphūráō, “I knead together, mess up”) from <i>σῠ́ν</i> (sŭ́n, “with”) +&lrm; <i>φῡράω</i> (phūráō, “or <i>φύρω</i> I mix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement in sound, symphony</li><li>(<i>music</i>) accord, concord (e.g. octave)</li><li>numerous sounds played together, a concert of vocal or instrumental (or both) music</li><li>music</li><li>band, orchestra</li><li>type of musical instrument</li><li>harmony, agreement, accord</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠ́μφωνος</i> (sŭ́mphōnos, “agreeing in sound, harmonious”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā) and <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>-φωνίᾱ</i> (-phōníā, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow-watchman or guard</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to make to grow together; to unite</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to grow together; to unite</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to unite with</li><li>to become assimilated or natural</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “to bring forth, to produce”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>with, together, co-, syn-</li><li>indicates completeness</li></ol><p>Prefix version of the preposition <i>σῠ́ν</i> (sŭ́n, “with”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons, animals, etc.</i>) to bring together, gather together</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to bring together for deliberation or festivity</li><li>(<i>in a hostile sense</i>) to join battle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to match two warriors against one another</li><li>to collect or levy soldiers</li></ol><li>to bring together, join in one, unite</li><li>to bring together, make friends of, reconcile</li><li>to collect oneself</li><li>to lead with one, receive</li></ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) gather together, collect</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to draw together, so as to make the extremities meet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to draw together, narrow, contract</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “lead, guide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>covenant, contract, agreement</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) dealings, transactions</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνᾰλλᾰ́σσω</i> (sŭnăllắssō, “to have dealings with another”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma). By surface analysis, <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “syn-, with”) +&lrm; <i>ἄλλαγμα</i> (állagma, “something taken in exchange”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exchange, contract</li><li>right of disposal by contract</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνᾰλλᾰ́σσω</i> (sŭnăllắssō, “to associate with”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meeting, encounter</li></ol><p><i>σῠνᾰντᾰ́ω</i> (sŭnăntắō, “to meet”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join together, connect</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>ἅπτω</i> (háptō, “to join”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule or govern jointly [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>council (advising or governing body)</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠ́νεδρος</i> (sŭ́nedros, “sitting together”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion), from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>ἕδρᾱ</i> (hédrā, “seat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;possibly dictionary-only) synonym of <i>συντυχία</i> (suntukhía, “occurrence, happening, incident”)</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “syn-”) +&lrm; <i>ἔντευξις</i> (énteuxis, “meeting with”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow along with, follow closely, accompany</li><li>to follow all the way</li><li>to follow, understand</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἕπομαι</i> (hépomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come together, assemble, meet</li><li>to meet in battle</li><li>(<i>of sexual intercourse</i>) to be united, have sex</li><li>(<i>of events</i>) to happen together</li></ol><p><i>σῠν-</i> (“with, together”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (“to come”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>σῠμπολῑ́της</i> (sŭmpolī́tēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>continuity</li></ol><p>From <i>συνεχής</i> (sunekhḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habit; custom</li><li>regular sex (with the same person)</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνήθης</i> (sŭnḗthēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of people</i>) habituated, accustomed, used to</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) usual, customary</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>ἦθος</i> (êthos, “custom”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wedded to</li><li>linked with</li><li>in communion with</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consort of either sex, typically a wife, less commonly in the sense of husband</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠναείρω</i> (sŭnaeírō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement, collapse</li></ol><p>From <i>συνίζω</i> (sunízō) (from <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἵζω</i> (hízō)) + <i>-ησις</i> (-ēsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send together, to bring together or set together</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to come together, to come to an agreement</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to perceive, hear</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be aware of, take notice of, observe</li><li>to understand</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi, “to send”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to combine, unite</li><li>to put together, organize</li><li>to join, engage with (<i>in a fight, in battle</i>)</li><li>to form a league or union, band together</li><li>to place in charge of</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-, “syn-, co-”) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking or drawing together; contraction, closing</li><li>contraction of front; shortening, reduction of an estimated distance</li><li>aggregation; synthesis; concentration; generalization</li><li>(<i>in&#32;grammar</i>): synaeresis of two adjacent vowels within one word into a diphthong or a macron vowel.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>properly</i>) synaeresis, whereby two vowels are not changed, but coalesce into a diphthong, as ὀϊστός, οἰστός</li><li>(<i>but also</i>) contraction, as of κύημα to κῦμα</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σῠναιρέω</i> (sŭnairéō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint-sensation, joint-perception</li><li>awareness, self-consciousness</li><li>(<i>in disease</i>) accompanying sensation</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠναίσθᾰ́νομαι</i> (sŭnaísthắnomai, “to perceive simultaneously”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gathering, collection</li><li>a gathering of people, meeting, assembly</li><li>The place of such an assembly (especially of the Jewish synagogue)</li><li>a uniting, coupling</li><li>combination</li><li>conclusion</li></ol><p>From <i>συνάγω</i> (sunágō, “to gather together”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ), from <i>σύν</i> (sún, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing together, uniting</li><li>collecting</li><li>living together</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνᾰ́γω</i> (sŭnắgō, “to bring together”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join in wrong or injury [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be wronged alike</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἀδικέω</i> (adikéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grasp or seize together, to seize at once</li><li>to bring together</li><li>to speak briefly</li><li>to make away with, crush</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to make an end of</li><li>to help to take or conquer</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring together, collect, assemble</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ᾱ̔λίζω</i> (hālízō, “gather together, assemble”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to associate with</li><li>to reconcile</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to have dealings with</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to make a league or alliance with, make peace (with)</li><li>to enter into engagements or contracts</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, with, together) +&lrm; <i>ἀλλάσσω</i> (allássō, “to change, alter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for contracts</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνᾰ́λλᾰγμᾰ</i> (sŭnắllăgmă, “contract”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn back together</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;middle voice) to live together, associate with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to wrestle with</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “syn-, with-”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νᾰστρέφω</i> (ănăstréphō, “to turn back”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>living with, intercourse</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνᾰνᾰστρέφω</i> (sŭnănăstréphō, “to turn back together, live together”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet face to face</li><li>to meet together, assemble</li><li>to meet with, counter</li><li>to fall in with</li><li>to come in contact with</li><li>to befall, happen to (a person)</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ντᾰ́ω</i> (ăntắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable of adjusting</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) hypothetical conjunction</li></ol><p>From <i>συνάπτω</i> (sunáptō, “to connect”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>joined together, continuous</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνᾰ́πτω</i> (sŭnắptō, “join together, connect”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>connection, union, junction</li><li>point or line of junction</li><li>(<i>music</i>) conjunction of two tetrachords</li></ol><p>From <i>συνάπτω</i> (sunáptō, “to join together, connect”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>united, connected, related to</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>ἁφή</i> (haphḗ, “grip”, noun) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw into revolt together</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to fall off or revolt along with others</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἀφίστημι</i> (aphístēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>tie together, bind together</li><li>to connect</li><li>(<i>in the mediopassive form</i>) <i>συνδέομαι</i> (sundéomai) [<i>with</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to join in entreating</li><li>to beg of someone also</li></ol></ol><p><i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to tie”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help in putting an end to something</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to help to pay</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be dissipated, melt away with, to be abolished at the same time</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “together, with”) +&lrm; <i>διαλῡ́ω</i> (dialū́ō, “dissolve”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a concourse of people</li></ol><p>From the aorist stem of <i>συντρέχω</i> (suntrékhō) + <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consciousness, perception of one's own thoughts</li><li>consciousness of right or wrong, conscience</li></ol><p>From <i>σύνοιδα</i> (súnoida), + <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to settle in together</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-, “into”) +&lrm; <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “inhabit, dwell”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>understanding one thing with another</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) synecdoche</li></ol><p>From <i>συνεκδέχομαι</i> (sunekdékhomai) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ) and <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἐκδοχή</i> (ekdokhḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go or come out with</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help in putting on</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to join in attacking or undertaking</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἐπιτίθημι</i> (epitíthēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work with, cooperate; to contribute</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>ἐργάζομαι</i> (ergázomai, “to work”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint working, cooperation</li><li>conspiracy, collusion</li></ol><p>From <i>συνεργός</i> (sunergós) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>working together, joining or helping in work</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) helper, helpmate, coadjutor, accomplice</li><li>having the same trade (<i>as another</i>)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) workfellow, coworker, colleague</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;construed with the genitive) notice, understand</li></ol><p><i>σῠνετός</i> (sŭnetós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having understanding, intelligent, sagacious, wise, learned, prudent</li><li>(<i>in a passive sense</i>) intelligible</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>σῠνῑ́ημῐ</i> (sŭnī́ēmĭ, “perceive, understand”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Συνετός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to French Synétos</li></ol><p>Use as a proper noun of <i>σῠνετός</i> (sŭnetós, “intelligent, sagacious, wise”, adjective). Compare <i>Σῠνετή</i> (Sŭnetḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unceasingly</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>continuous, contiguous, unintermitting</li><li>constant, persevering</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) frequent</li></ol><p>From <i>συνέχω</i> (sunékhō, “to keep together, hold together”) (from <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō)) + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>continuous, contiguous, unintermitting</li><li>constant, persevering</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) frequent</li></ol><p>From <i>συνέχω</i> (sunékhō, “to keep together, hold together”) (from <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō)) + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>continuously</li></ol><p><i>σῡνεχής</i> (sūnekhḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to nail or fasten together</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ἡλόω</i> (hēlóō), the latter of which comes from <i>ἧλος</i> (hêlos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury together (with)</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>θάπτω</i> (tháptō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a compounding, especially of words and sentences</li><li>convention, compact</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>article of a compact or treaty</li></ol><li>coffin</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠντῐ́θημῐ</i> (sŭntĭ́thēmĭ, “to place or put together”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled in putting together, constructive</li><li>component</li></ol><p>From <i>σύνθεσις</i> (súnthesis, “putting together”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compress, press together</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to throng</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>θλῑ́βω</i> (thlī́bō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engage in a sea battle along with [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ναυμαχέω</i> (naumakhéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from a conjunction of sun and moon</li><li>in astral conjunction with</li><li>of or for a synod, synodal</li><li>of or for a σύνοδος</li></ol><p>From <i>σύνοδος</i> (súnodos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live with, live together</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body of people living together, community, settlement</li><li>house in which several families live, tenement</li><li>storeroom, outhouse, backroom</li></ol><p>From <i>σύνοικος</i> (súnoikos, “dwelling in the same house”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), the former from <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make (<i>someone</i>) live with (<i>someone else</i>)</li><li>to give (<i>someone to someone</i>) in marriage</li><li>to combine or join in one city</li><li>to unite into a city-state</li><li>to unite in one building</li><li>to join in peopling or colonizing a country</li><li>to unite, associate</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκίζω</i> (oikízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seeing the whole together, taking a comprehensive view</li></ol><p>From <i>σύνοψις</i> (súnopsis) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>social intercourse, society</li><li>communion (mental connection)</li><li>conversation</li><li>habitual association, constant resort</li><li>attendance (<i>at instruction</i>)</li><li>sexual intercourse, copulation</li><li>company, party</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>οὐσίᾱ</i> (ousíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put in order together, to draw up, put in array</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to form in line</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be collected, resolute, steady</li><li>to arrange, organize, concoct</li><li>to fix or assess (<i>a tax payment</i>)</li><li>to ordain, prescribe [<i>with</i> infinitve]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to agree together</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to take leave of, bid farewell to [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to appoint</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>τάσσω</i> (tássō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joint contribution for the public burdens</li><li>compulsory provision of recruits</li><li>(<i>at Athens</i>) body of citizens who contributed jointly to bear public burdens</li><li>union of communities grouped together or united to a larger state</li><li>consummation, realization of a scheme</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) completed action</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠντελής</i> (sŭntelḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>cut down, cut short</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) abridge</li><li>(<i>especially of expenses</i>) cut down</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) cut (them) off</li><li>divide logically</li></ol><li>(<i>of speech</i>) cut short, cut across</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) become short</li><li>hew out</li><li>cut together, join by an incision</li></ol><p><i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to assemble, put together</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) the leader of a syntagma</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠ́ντᾰγμᾰ</i> (sŭ́ntăgmă, “syntagma”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “leader”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) syntagmatarchy</li></ol><p><i>σῠντᾰγμᾰτᾰ́ρχης</i> (sŭntăgmătắrkhēs, “leader of a battalion”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>putting together, composing</li><li>of or for a departure or farewell</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) official in Egypt, classifier of soldiers, cleruchs etc. into categories</li></ol><p>From <i>συντάσσω</i> (suntássō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós) and <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>τακτικός</i> (taktikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help build a wall or fortification</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>τειχίζω</i> (teikhízō, “build, fortify”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>associated in the payment of taxes or in contribution</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “dues, taxes”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) completed sense of perfective tenses e.g. aorist, perfect</li></ol><p>From the ancient <i>συντελ(ής)</i> (suntel(ḗs)) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós), from <i>συντελέω&#47;συντελῶ</i> (sunteléō&#47;suntelô). Morphologically, <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>τέλ(ος)</i> (tél(os)) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the body</i>) tension</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) focus, diligence, concentration</li></ol><li>intensity, intense application or exertion</li><li>force</li><li>agreement, harmony</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠ́ντονος</i> (sŭ́ntonos, “intense”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā), the former from <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>τόνος</i> (tónos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub together</li><li>to shake to pieces</li><li>to break, bruise</li><li>to beat to a pulp, crush</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-) +&lrm; <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to meet with, to fall in with</li><li>(<i>of accidents and chances</i>) to happen to, to befall</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō, “to happen (to), I succeed, I light upon, I obtain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an occurrence, chance, event, incident, circumstance</li><li>a happy chance, success</li><li>a mishap, misfortune</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>τύχη</i> (túkhē) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pair of horses</li><li>biga, a two-horse chariot</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνήορος</i> (sŭnḗoros, “taken or linked together”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consanguineous, same-blooded</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ὅμαιμος</i> (hómaimos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear together</li><li>to promise by oath</li><li>to join in a league or confederacy</li><li>to form a confederacy with others</li><li>to conspire</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>ὄμνῡμι</i> (ómnūmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνώνῠμος</i> (sŭnṓnŭmos).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having the same name</li><li>Having only one meaning: univocal</li><li>synonymous</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνῠμᾰ</i> (ónŭmă, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having the same name</li><li>Having only one meaning: univocal</li><li>synonymous</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνῠμᾰ</i> (ónŭmă, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of garment</li></ol><p>From <i>Συρία</i> (Suría, “Syria”), because it was made by Cappadocian Syrians.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Συρία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Syria (a historical region of West Asia, extending from modern-day southeast Turkey to the north, the Euphrates and Arabian Desert to the east, the Sinai Peninsula to the south, and the Mediterranean sea to the west)# (<i>historical</i>) Syria (an ancient Roman province between 64 BCE and 198 CE)</li></ol><p>Apheretic form of <i>Ἀσσυρία</i> (Assuría, “Assyria”), from Akkadian <i>𒀾𒋗𒊏𒅀</i> (Aššūrāyu), from <i>𒀸𒋩</i> (Aššur, “Assur”).</p><p>The land corresponding to modern Syria was originally called Aram by its ancient inhabitants, the Arameans. After the Assyrian conquest of Aram, the Greeks applied the name <i>Syria</i> to this territory. Over time, <i>Syria</i> came to denote the broader Levant, while <i>Assyria</i> referred to Mesopotamia. Herodotus (5th century BCE) was among the earliest Greek authors to consistently distinguish Syria and Assyria. After his time, some classical writers occasionally blurred the distinction. Nevertheless, educated writers and administrative usage gradually preserved the distinction, with <i>Syria</i> referring to the Levant and <i>Assyria</i> to Mesopotamia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play the panpipes</li><li>to whistle</li></ol><p>From <i>σῦρ(ιγξ)</i> (sûr(inx), “panpipes”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>σῡρίζω</i> (sūrízō, “to hiss”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red lead</li></ol><p>Possibly a neuter substantivization of <i>Συρικός</i> (Surikós, “Syrian”), but more likely altered by folk etymology to resemble it, and actually borrowed from an eastern source such as Arabic زَرْقُون (zarqūn) or its source, Classical Persian زرگون (zargōn)/Middle Persian <i>𐭦𐭫𐭢𐭥𐭭</i> (zlgʿn /⁠zargōn⁠/, “red lead”), from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;jr̥Hanyam</i> (“gold”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>from Syria, Syrian</li></ol><p>From <i>Σῠ́ρος</i> (Sŭ́ros, “a Syrian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand partridge (<i>Ammoperdix heyi</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Σύρος</i> (Súros, “Syrian”) +&lrm; <i>πέρδιξ</i> (pérdix, “partridge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stich or sew together</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of a treatise) to put together, compose</li></ol><p><i>σῠρ- (σῠν-)</i> (sŭr- (sŭn-), “together”) +&lrm; <i>ῥάπτω</i> (rháptō, “sew”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow together, flow in the same stream (<i>also used figuratively</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break in pieces, break down</li><li>to dash together</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to break out (<i>of war</i>)</li><li>to break forth (<i>of rivers</i>)</li><li>to meet in battle, engage</li></ol><p>From <i>συρ-</i> (sur-) +&lrm; <i>ῥήγνῡμι</i> (rhḗgnūmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything dragged or swept together, sweepings, refuse, litter</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) mixed crowd, mob, rabble</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) one of the mob, mobbist</li></ol><p>From <i>σύρω</i> (súrō, “to drag, sweep”), with enlargement in <i>-φ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intrigue, plot</li></ol><p><i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “syn-”) +&lrm; <i>σκευή</i> (skeuḗ, “equipment, attire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to grow quite dark</li><li>(Koine,&#32;transitive) to make dark, darken</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>σκότος</i> (skótos, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō). The unprefixed verb <i>σκοτάζω</i> (skotázō) is attested later.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help to save or preserve</li><li>to observe completely</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw together: shorten sail; to draw in, contract</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to contract, reduce</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to deprive of all the food and drink</li></ol><li>to humble, abase</li><li>(<i>phonology</i>) to lower, make mean; to pronounce (a syllable) short</li></ol><li>to wrap closely up, shroud</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to cloak</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “to dispatch, to send”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>walk together with [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p><i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>στείχω</i> (steíkhō, “walk, march, go, come”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or like an organized whole, systematic, forming a complete system</li><li>constitutive</li></ol><p>From <i>σύστημα</i> (sústēma) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction, drawing together</li><li>(<i>physiology</i>) contraction of the heart; systole</li></ol><p>From <i>συστέλλω</i> (sustéllō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix), from <i>σύν</i> (sún, “together”) + <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “send”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be frequent</li><li>to do or come frequently</li></ol><p>From <i>συχνός</i> (sukhnós, “long-lasting, many, great”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) long, wearisome</li><li>(<i>of number</i>) many</li><li>great, large, plentiful</li><li>frequent, repeated</li></ol><p>Beekes marks the word's origin as unknown. Other theories derive the word from <i>συνέχω</i> (sunékhō, “to hold or keep together; to contain”), undergoing metathesis, itself from <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “together, with”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have, to hold”).</p></html>:/sf/
<h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>σφε</i> (sphe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victim, offering</li><li>slaughter, sacrifice</li></ol><p>From <i>σφάζω</i> (spházō, “to sacrifice”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greek sage (<i>Salvia fruticosa</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin, together with <i>σφάκος</i> (sphákos) and <i>φάσκον</i> (pháskon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slay, slaughter, cut the throat</li><li>to slaughter victims for sacrifice</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) to slay, kill, assassinate</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin, with no known cognates outside Greek. Furnée connects <i>φάσγανον</i> (phásganon, “sword”) as <i>φασγ-/σφαγ-</i>, suggesting a Pre-Greek origin. The relation with Old Armenian <i>սպանանեմ</i> (spananem, “to slay”), if any, is unclear and would have to be via borrowing from a common source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) gangrene, caries, necrosis</li><li>spasm, convulsion, cramp, twitching pain</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) middle finger</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>σκόπελος</i> (skópelos) and <i>πύελος</i> (púelos), from an unknown root. According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)penh₁-</i> (“to spin (thread); to stretch”).</p><p>The word has been connected with Middle High German <i>spachen</i> (“to split”) and Low German <i>spaken</i> (“to putrify”) but it can hardly be correct.</p><p>From Pre-Greek, in view of the variation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apple sage (<i>Salvia pomifera</i>)</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. According to Furnée, of Pre-Greek origin and related to <i>σφάγνος</i> (sphágnos, “kind of bush”) and <i>φάσκον</i> (pháskon, “moss”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax paid for victims</li></ol><p>From <i>σφάζω</i> (spházō, “to slaughter”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to topple, overthrow</li><li>to kill</li><li>to baffle</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be foiled</li><li>to fall, to be cast down (from a figurative or literal elevated place)</li><li>to stagger, reel</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)gʷʰh₂el-</i> (“to stumble”). Compare Old Armenian <i>սխալեմ</i> (sxalem, “to err”), Sanskrit <i>स्खलति</i> (skhalati, “to stumble”) and possibly Latin <i>fallō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a trip, a stumble, a false step</li><li>a fall, a failure, a defeat</li><li>a fault, an error</li></ol><p><i>σφᾰ́λλω</i> (sphắllō, “to fall, to be cast down”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, suffix forming neuter nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fall, accident</li></ol><p>From <i>σφάλλω</i> (sphállō, “I bring down, I ruin”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in compounds</i>) alternative form of <i>σφᾰγή</i> (sphăgḗ, “jugular”)</li><li>Doric form of <i>σφήξ</i> (sphḗx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footstool</li><li>pedestal of a statue</li><li>hollow block of wood, for putting anything into</li></ol><p>Furnée concludes from the possible connection with <i>σφαλλός</i> (sphallós, “round block of wood”) that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montpelier maple (<i>Acer monspessulanum</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>δίκταμνον</i> (díktamnon) and <i>ὀρόδαμνος</i> (oródamnos). Often connected to <i>σφενδόνη</i> (sphendónē, “sling”), either through a meaning "trembling" or after the form of the seeds. However, the word is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>the only sense in&#32;Homeric</i>) third-person plural possessive pronoun, including reflexive</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>modifying a noun</i>) their, of theirs; their own</li><li>(as substantive,&#32;often&#32;with article) theirs, their own</li></ol><li>sometimes possessive reflexive of other persons&#58; first-person singular and plural, second-person singular and plural, third-person singular</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>σφεῖς</i> (spheîs, “they”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, contrastive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedge (part of a simple machine)</li><li>an instrument of torture</li></ol><p>Unknown. Has been linked to Sanskrit <i>स्फ्य</i> (sphyá, “oar; spar”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spēnuz</i> (“chip; shaving”), but this is phonologically impossible due to the aspirated labial /pʰ/ in Greek. Pre-Greek origin is likely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wasp</li></ol><p>Generally considered to be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰey-</i> (“bee”). Beekes rejects this in favor of a Pre-Greek origin possibly shared with <i>ψήν</i> (psḗn, “gall-insect”). Or, possible connection with <i>σφάκελος</i> (sphákelos, “necrosis, spasm, convulsion”), referring to the sting, though the sense remains uncertain.</p><p>Compare Latin <i>fūcus</i> (“drone; bee”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind tight, tie fast</li><li>to bind or hold together</li><li>to tie up in a bundle</li><li>to tighten up</li><li>to press together</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps of Pre-Greek origin, apparently based on the pre-nasalization of the <i>-γγ-</i> (-ng-) as well as the lack of credible cognates in other Indo-European branches.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Lyric form of <i>σφᾰγή</i> (sphăgḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaughter; butchery</li><li>jugular (the spot on the throat where the victim is struck)</li></ol><p>From <i>σφᾰ́ζω</i> (sphắzō, “to slaughter”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sacrificial knife</li></ol><p>From <i>σφάζω</i> (spházō, “to slaughter”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σφαδᾴζω</i> (sphadāízō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to struggle, plunge (<i>of horses</i>)</li><li>to struggle, show impatience</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Beekes considers two potential sets of comparanda:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) sphaeristerium, ballcourt</li></ol><p>From <i>σφαιρόω</i> (sphairóō, “to be rounded”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A sphere holder</li></ol><p>From <i>σφαῖρα</i> (sphaîra, “ball, globe”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparring match with iron balls</li></ol><p>From <i>σφαιρομαχέω</i> (sphairomakhéō, “to spar with iron balls”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slippery, perilous, likely to make one stumble</li><li>ready to fall, tottering, reeling</li></ol><p>From <i>σφάλλω</i> (sphállō) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ball, globe, sphere</li></ol><p>Connections with <i>σπαίρω</i> (spaírō, “to gasp”) or Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sperH-</i> (“to kick, rebound, move convulsively”, the original sense would be "something that rebounds") have been suggested, but the aspiration of σπ- to σφ- is unexplained and the semantic development is dubious. Nikolayev instead suggests derivation from a Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;skʷʰə́řřa</i>, from a putative Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sgʷʰer-</i> (“to be round”); compare Avestan <i>𐬰𐬔𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬯𐬥𐬀</i> (zgər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>sna, “round”) and Hittite&nbsp;pl (/⁠šū̆rita⁠/, “skeins of wool”) (a borrowing from Luwian).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vehement, violent, raging</li></ol><p>From a root <i>σφεδ-</i> (sphed-) + the suffix <i>-ανός</i> (-anós) shared also with <i>ἐδανός</i> (edanós), <i>ἰδανός</i> (idanós), <i>σκεπανός</i> (skepanós), and <i>στεγανός</i> (steganós). Together with the related term <i>σφοδρός</i> (sphodrós, “violent”), <i>σφεδανός</i> (sphedanós) may reflect a heteroclitic "r/n" stem, but this is uncertain. Compare perhaps <i>σφαδᾴζω</i> (sphadāízō, “to be strongly moved or excited”) and <i>σφενδόνη</i> (sphendónē, “sling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of maple wood</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) tough, stout</li></ol><p>From <i>σφένδαμνος</i> (sphéndamnos, “maple”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sling</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a sling as part of a crane used in unloading ships</li></ol><li>anything fundiform:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a sling:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>for a disabled arm</li><li>a suspensory abdominal bandage</li></ol><li>a headband worn by women, broad in front</li><li>a bezel, a collet (the hoop of a ring in which the gemstone is set as in a sling&#59; especially, the outer or broader part round the stone)</li><li>a sclera (the white of an eye)</li></ol><li>a slingstone (a stone, bullet, or other such missile or projectile thrown by a sling)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a hailstone</li></ol></ol><p>Often connected with <i>σφεδανός</i> (sphedanós), <i>σφοδρός</i> (sphodrós), <i>σφαδᾴζω</i> (sphadāízō), and Sanskrit <i>स्पन्दते</i> (spandate, “to vibrate, tremble, quake”), as well as with Latin <i>funda</i> (“sling”), with formation structure similar to that of <i>ἀγχόνη</i> (ankhónē), <i>βελόνη</i> (belónē), and <i>περόνη</i> (perónē). The similarity with the Latin term suggests a Mediterranean, Anatolian, and/or Pre-Greek borrowing for both.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;mediopassive voice</i>) make one's own, appropriate, usurp.</li></ol><p>From <i>σφέτερος</i> (sphéteros, “one's own”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(personal pronoun,&#32;third person) they</li><li>(reflexive pronoun,&#32;third person) themselves</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>The consonantism began in the dative <i>σφι</i> (sphi), originally the dative of the reflexive pronoun, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;s(e)bʰi</i>. Later the σφ-cluster spread to the rest of the paradigm, and the pronoun acquired a nonreflexive plural sense. The ending -εῖς was added by analogy with other pronominal forms, such as <i>ἡμεῖς</i> (hēmeîs). The σφ-cluster also spread to the third-person dual pronoun <i>σφωέ</i> (sphōé) and the second-person dual pronoun <i>σφώ</i> (sphṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(music,&#32;hapax legomenon) a form of playing the aulos with a buzzing timbre</li></ol><p>Only attested as a gloss in Hesychius. From <i>σφήξ</i> (sphḗx, “wasp”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐσμός</i> (-ĭsmós, “suffix forming abstract nouns”), as if from a verb <i>σφηκίζω</i> (sphēkízō, “to buzz like a wasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Wedge-shaped.</li></ol><p>From <i>σφήν</i> (sphḗn, “wedge”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-form, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which binds tight; a lace, band.</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) A muscle closing an orifice which naturally remains in the state of contraction; a sphincter.</li><li>A Tarentine chiton, probably because it is laced tight to the body.</li></ol><p>From <i>σφῐ́γγω</i> (sphĭ́ngō, “to bind tight or fast”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tightly bound</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective of <i>σφίγγω</i> (sphíngō, “I bind”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>σπογγῐ́ον</i> (spongĭ́on, “small sponge”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vehement, violent, excessive</li><li>violent, impetuous (of men)</li><li>active, zealous</li><li>strong, robust</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>σφεδανός</i> (sphedanós, “violent, vehement”). The origin of these words is uncertain but perhaps related to <i>σφαδᾴζω</i> (sphadāízō, “to flounce”) and <i>σφενδόνη</i> (sphendónē, “sling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>σφοδρός</i> (sphodrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>σφοδρός</i> (sphodrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cow parsnip (<i>Heracleum sphondylium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σφόνδυλος</i> (sphóndulos, “vertebra; whorl”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the vertebrae of the neck</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>μυελὸς αὖτε σφονδυλίων ἔκπαλθ', ὃ δ' ἐπὶ χθονὶ κεῖτο τανυσθείς. (Iliad 20.483) and the marrow sprang forth</li></ol></ol><p>A Homeric hapax legomena, Iliad 20.483.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seal, signet</li><li>gem or stone for a ring</li><li>tablet of Lemnian earth</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) pastille</li></ol><p>Formation similar to <i>κληῑ̈́ς</i> (klēī̈́s) and <i>κνημῑ́ς</i> (knēmī́s) which points to a secondary derivative. Prellwitz suggested connection with Lithuanian <i>spróga</i> (“crevice”) and Lithuanian <i>sprógti</i> (“to burst”), but this is semantically enigmatic. Furnée takes the word to be Pre-Greek because of the suffix "-ῖδ-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the pulse</li></ol><p>From <i>σφυγμός</i> (sphugmós, “pulse”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “pertaining to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the throbbing of inflamed parts</li><li>the beating of the heart and circulatory system, pulse</li><li>a vibration of the earth, quake</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) any violent emotion</li></ol><p>From <i>σφύζω</i> (sphúzō, “to throb”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) ankle</li><li>lower part or edge, foothills of mountains</li></ol><p>Conventionally connected with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sperH-</i>, although Pre-Greek, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of you two; your; of yours</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>σφῶῐ̈</i>&nbsp;du (sphôĭ̈, “you two”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, contrastive adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic enclitic third person dual personal pronoun: they two, the two of them, both of them, these two</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Blend of <i>σφεῖς</i> (spheîs, “they”) +&lrm; <i>νώ</i> (nṓ, “we two”), with the suffix <i>-ε</i> (-e).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>σπόγγος</i> (spóngos, “sponge”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very, very much: modifies verbs, adjectives, or nouns</li><li><i>σφόδρα γε</i> (sphódra ge), <i>καὶ σφόδρα γε</i> (kaì sphódra ge), or <i>πάνυ γε σφόδρα</i> (pánu ge sphódra) in answer to a question: very much so, definitely</li></ol><p>Adverbial neuter accusative plural of <i>σφοδρός</i> (sphodrós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vertebra (especially the cervical vertebrae)</li><li>joint (especially of a scorpion's tail)</li><li>tambour</li><li>spindle whorl</li><li>whorl (botany)</li><li>voting pebble</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>δάκτυλος</i> (dáktulos) and <i>κόνδυλος</i> (kóndulos), probably first from a noun <i>&#42;σφόνδος</i> (&#42;sphóndos). Sometimes connected with words like <i>σφενδόνη</i> (sphendónē), <i>σφεδανός</i> (sphedanós) and <i>σφαδᾴζω</i> (sphadāízō), but this is mere speculation. The variation σφ/σπ points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throb</li><li>to beat (<i>of the heart or pulse</i>)</li></ol><p>In form and meaning, the verb resembles <i>σφαδᾴζω</i> (sphadāízō, “to toss the body about, to struggle”) and <i>σπεύδω</i> (speúdō, “to urge, to hasten”), to which compare Sanskrit <i>स्पन्दते</i> (spandate, “to pulsate, beat, throb, quiver; to quicken”), <i>स्फुरति</i> (sphurati, “to pulse, beat, throb, tremble; to dart, flash; to thrash about bodily, struggle”), and similar forms, as well as <i>भुरति</i> (bhurati, “to palpitate, pulsate, dart, convulse; to move hastily; to struggle”). Whether the verb is related to any of the aforementioned words, however, is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulse</li></ol><p>From <i>σφύζω</i> (sphúzō, “throb”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barracuda (<i>Sphyraena sphyraena</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σφῦρα</i> (sphûra, “hammer, mallet”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>second person dual personal pronoun: you two, both of you, you both</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>A conflation of the consonantism of <i>σφεῖς</i> (spheîs, “they”) with the vocalism of <i>νώ</i> (nṓ, “we two”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hammer, beetle, mallet</li><li>balk between the furrows of ploughed land</li><li>kind of land measure</li></ol><p>Julius Pokorny connects this word with <i>σπαίρω</i> (spaírō, “to hammer, to make rapid movements”). Frisk connects this word with <i>σφαῖρα</i> (sphaîra, “ball”), but this seems far-fetched, especially since the semantic connection is not evident. The etymology thus remains uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>σφώ</i> (sphṓ, “you two”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make an incision</li><li>to tear open</li><li>to open (a vein), let (the blood) flow</li><li>to let loose, let something take its course, release, let go</li><li>to drop, abandon</li></ol><p>Probably from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skeh₂-</i> (“to split”), and generally compared with Sanskrit <i>छ्यति</i> (chyati, “to split, hurt”), from the root <i>छो</i> (cho, “to cut off”). Whether <i>&#42;skeh₂-</i> is related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skey-</i> (“to split”) is speculative.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σχᾰ́ζω</i> (skhắzō, “to tear open, let flow”)</li></ol><p>See <i>σχᾰ́ζω</i> (skhắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf, page</li></ol><p>Related to <i>σχίζω</i> (skhízō, “to split, divide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>near, close</li><li>temporary, offhand, casual, unprepared, improvised</li></ol><p>From <i>σχεδόν</i> (skhedón, “near”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀγρία κυπάρισσος</i> (agría kupárissos, literally “wild cypress”).</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, from Pre-Greek and related to <i>γέλενος</i> (gélenos, “asphodel, daffodil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σχῆμᾰ</i> (skhêmă)</li></ol><p>Alternative, perhaps erroneous, reading of <i>σχῆμᾰ</i> (skhêmă), but supported by Latin <i>schĕma</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>state, condition</li><li>natural quality, fashion (<i>of something</i>)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to hold out, unflinching, steadfast</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>σχήσεσθε</i> (skhḗsesthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A piece of wood cut off, a lath, a splinter</li><li>A shaft, a dart</li></ol><p>From <i>σχίζω</i> (skhízō, “to break, to tear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to split, cleave</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to part, separate, divide</li><li>to curdle milk</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to divide</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;skʰíďďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skid-yé-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;skeyd-</i> (“to divide, split”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>छिनत्ति</i> (chinátti), Latin <i>scindō</i>, Old Armenian <i>ցտեմ</i> (cʻtem) and Old English <i>sċītan</i> (English <i>shit</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of lentisk</li></ol><p>From <i>σχῖνος</i> (skhînos, “lentisk”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleaving, parting, division</li><li>difference of opinion</li><li>vulva</li></ol><p>From <i>σχίζω</i> (skhízō, “to split”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is divided: division, rent, split</li><li>A division of people based on differing opinions: schism, faction</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skeyd-</i>der.Ancient Greek <i>σχίζω</i> (skhízō)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mn̥</i>Ancient Greek <i>-μα</i> (-ma)Ancient Greek <i>σχίσμα</i></p><p>From <i>σχίζω</i> (skhízō, “to split”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>larva of the bee or wasp</li><li>breeding-cell of the larva</li><li>honey-cell</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) honeycomb</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>σχάζω</i> (skházō, “to make an incision, let flow”), as is traditionally assumed, but the semantics of this etymology are vague.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forked prop or stay</li></ol><p>From <i>σχαλίς</i> (skhalís, “prop for nets”) +&lrm; <i>-ωμα</i> (-ōma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forked stick used as a prop for nets</li></ol><p>Technical word in <i>-ίς</i> (-ís), like <i>δοκίς</i> (dokís), <i>σανίς</i> (sanís) and <i>ψαλίς</i> (psalís); Beekes is unconvinced by comparisons to <i>σκαλίς</i> (skalís, “mattock”) or <i>σχέτω</i> (skhétō, “to hold”), instead suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raft, float</li><li>pontoon, bridge of boats</li><li>cramp, holdfast</li></ol><p>Unknown. Traditionally believed to be a substantivization of the adjective <i>σχέδιος</i> (skhédios, “near, close”). Alternatively, a collective abstract formation in <i>-ία</i> (-ía) from <i>σχεδόν</i> (skhedón, “near; approximately, roughly”), with original meaning "unprepared formation, improvisation". However, the semantics are not convincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freak, whim, caprice</li></ol><p>From <i>σχεδιάζω</i> (skhediázō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>σχεδίᾱ</i> (skhedíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do (<i>something</i>) offhand</li></ol><p>From <i>σχέδιος</i> (skhédios) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from nearby</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>near; nearby</li><li>nearly; almost</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seǵʰ-</i> (“to hold”) +&lrm; <i>-δον</i> (-don, adverbial suffix); related to <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”) and <i>σχῆμᾰ</i> (skhêmă, “form; shape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) ribs of beef</li><li>sides of bacon</li></ol><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>Generally taken as from <i>σκέλος</i> (skélos, “leg”), which is not impossible semantically, but certainly incorrect given the variant form, which points to Pre-Greek origin.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ship carpenter's and blacksmith's tool&#59; possibly forceps or pliers</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>κᾰνθῡ́λη</i> (kănthū́lē) and <i>κορδῡ́λη</i> (kordū́lē). The presence of the variation leads to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) headland, beach</li></ol><p>Unknown. Hiersche assumed a pre-form <i>&#42;σκερός</i> (&#42;skerós), related to Middle English <i>schore</i> (“shore”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut off”). However, this is uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) assume a certain form, shape, posture or position</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) shape, fashion, arrange, give a certain form to</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) dress up, deck out</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) demean oneself, make a show of oneself</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) pass off as, pretend to be</li></ol><p>From <i>σχῆμα</i> (skhêma) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastich oil, made from the berries of the lentisk</li></ol><p>From <i>σχῖνος</i> (skhînos, “lentisk”) +&lrm; <i>ἔλαιον</i> (élaion, “oil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cloven, divided</li><li>that may be split or cleft; divisible</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>σχῐ́ζω</i> (skhĭ́zō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós). Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;skidtós</i>, whence also Latin <i>scissus</i> and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čistъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of rushes</li></ol><p>From <i>σχοῖνος</i> (skhoînos, “rush”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small rope, cord or thread</li></ol><p>From <i>σχοῖνος</i> (skhoînos, “rope”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stretched out like a measuring line</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>drawn in a straight line</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) stretched out, prolix</li></ol></ol><li>made of twisted or plaited rushes</li></ol><p><i>σχοῖνος</i> (skhoînos, “anything twisted or plaited with rushes, schoene: unit of length equivalent to twenty kilocubits”) +&lrm; <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “stretch, extend”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, suffix forming third-declension adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a long time, at length, with prolixity</li></ol><p><i>σχοινοτενής</i> (skhoinotenḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be at leisure, have spare time</li><li>to have opportunity for [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to have respite from</li><li>to be unoccupied (<i>of a place</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σχολή</i> (skholḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>head of a school</li></ol><p>From <i>σχολή</i> (skholḗ) +&lrm; <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leisure, free time</li><li>rest</li><li>that in which leisure time is spent, especially lecture, disputation, discussion, philosophy</li><li>a school, lecture hall (place where lectures were given)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the Praetorian guard (see Latin <i>scholae</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;skʰolā́</i> (“holding back”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sǵʰ-h₃-léh₂</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seǵʰ-</i> (“to hold”). Synchronically analyzed as a formation from the aorist stem <i>σχ-</i> (skh-) of <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a more leisurely way</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leisurely</li><li>tardy</li></ol><p>From <i>σχολή</i> (skholḗ) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enjoying leisure</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) leisure</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) scholar</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) pedant, simpleton</li></ol><p>From <i>σχολάζω</i> (skholázō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leisurely</li><li>tardy</li></ol><p>From <i>σχολή</i> (skholḗ) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>school</li></ol><p>From <i>σχολή</i> (skholḗ) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush, especially camel hay (<i>Cymbopogon schoenanthus</i>)</li><li>reed, used as an arrow or javelin</li><li>rush bed</li><li>anything twisted or plaited with rushes, especially rope, cord</li><li>schene, an Egyptian land measure of length equivalent to 20,000 Egyptian cubits (about 10.5 kilometres)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σχοῖνος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Schoenus, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>σχοῖνος</i> (skhoînos, “rush, reed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an interpretation, comment</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a short note, scholium</li></ol><li>a long tedious speech, lecture</li></ol><p>From <i>σχολή</i> (skholḗ, “learning, lecture”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form, shape, figure</li><li>appearance, show, pretense</li><li>bearing, look, air, mien</li><li>stateliness, dignity, pomp</li><li>fashion, manner, way</li><li>character, persona</li><li>state, nature, constitution</li><li>species, kind</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) steps</li><li>dance</li><li>sketch, outline, plan, scheme</li><li>diagram</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;skʰḗmə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sǵʰ-éh₁-mn̥</i>, formed from the zero grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seǵʰ-</i> (“to hold”), synchronically corresponding to <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lentisk (<i>Pistacia lentiscus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, undoubtedly of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>channel, gutter, pipe</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>syringe, squirt</li></ol><li>cylindrical box for keeping a broken limb straight</li><li>grooved tile, Latin <i>imbrex</i></li><li>penis</li><li>cavity of the spine</li></ol><p>Probably from an earlier unattested noun <i>&#42;σωλος</i> (&#42;sōlos), <i>&#42;σωλον</i> (&#42;sōlon) with a suffix <i>-ήν</i> (-ḗn) as also seen in <i>κωλήν</i> (kōlḗn), <i>πυρήν</i> (purḗn), of unknown further origin. According to Beekes, the <i>-ήν</i> (-ḗn) suffix suggests a Pre-Greek source. See also <i>σῦρῐγξ</i> (sûrĭnx, “pan pipes”), <i>σαυρωτήρ</i> (saurōtḗr, “spike at butt end of spear”), to which the term was traditionally connected.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little pipe</li><li>quiver</li></ol><p><i>σωλήν</i> (sōlḗn, “pipe”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pipe-shaped, grooved</li></ol><p>From <i>σωλήν</i> (sōlḗn, “pipe, gutter”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-form, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the body, bodily</li><li>corporeal, somatic</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶμα</i> (sôma, “body”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the body, bodily</li><li>corporeal, material</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶμα</i> (sôma, “body”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorites, a heap of syllogisms, the fallacy of the heap</li></ol><p>From <i>σωρός</i> (sōrós, “heap”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>σωρῑ́της</i> (sōrī́tēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heap one thing on another, pile up</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to heap with something</li></ol><p>From <i>σωρός</i> (sōrós, “heap”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap, pile, mound (especially of corn)</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) heap, quantity</li></ol><p>Probably derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to swell”). Starting from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twoh₂-ro-</i>, Solmsen seeks a connection with <i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe, healthy, intact”), also arguing against connection with <i>σορός</i> (sorós, “cup, recipient”). <i>σῶμα</i> (sôma, “body”) has also been compared. See also Old Armenian <i>թուշ</i> (tʻuš, “cheek”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewHr-</i> (“to swell”), which seems connected to <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> and which the Greek term may alternatively derive from.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>safe</li></ol><p>From <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō, “to save”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saviour, deliverer, preserver (often as an epithet of protecting gods, especially Zeus)</li></ol><p>From <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō, “to save”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deliverance, salvation</li></ol><p>From <i>σωτήρ</i> (sōtḗr, “saviour”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>σώφρων</i> (sṓphrōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be prudent, moderate, temperate</li><li>to be sound of mind</li><li>come to one's senses, learn moderation</li></ol><p>From <i>σώφρων</i> (sṓphrōn, “prudent”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “verb forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teaching of morality or moderation</li></ol><p>From <i>σωφρονῐ́ζω</i> (sōphronĭ́zō, “to chasten”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a house of correction</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soundness of mind, prudence, discretion; sanity</li><li>moderation in sensual desires, self-control, temperance</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) a moderate form of government</li></ol><p>From <i>σώφρων</i> (sṓphrōn, “of sound mind”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sanely</li><li>moderately, prudently, sensibly, reasonably</li><li>temperately, chastely, soberly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sow thistle (plant of the genus <i>Sonchus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin due to the presence of the variation. Furnée links the word with Basque <i>tšokoŕo</i> (“silver thistle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slipper, sandal</li><li>seat, stool</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>solea</i> (“sandal”), <i>solium</i> (“seat, throne”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking incorrectly, using broken Greek</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) erring against good manners, in bad taste</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>σολοικῐ́ζω</i> (soloikĭ́zō, “to speak or write incorrectly”) or suffixed form of <i>Σόλοι</i> (Sóloi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mass or lump of iron used as a discus</li></ol><p>A foreign borrowing. Has been compared with Hittite (šulii̯a-, “lead”). Possibly a term of street tricksters, see <i>σαλός</i> (salós, “fool”) starting to appear with Christians as a synonym of <i>μωρός</i> (mōrós) from the 4th century CE and Arabic سَالُوس (sālūs, “blandishment”) only found from such slang.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>εὔζωμον</i> (eúzōmon, “rocket, arugula”).</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek, like <i>ῥόμιξα</i> (rhómixa, “kind of javelin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>modifying noun</i>) your, yours</li><li>(as substantive,&#32;often&#32;with article) yours, your own</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) your people, family, friends; your possessions</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twós</i>; cognate with Vedic Sanskrit <i>त्व</i> (tvá) and Old Armenian <i>քո</i> (kʻo).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clever device, ingenious contrivance</li><li>captious argument, sophism</li></ol><p>From <i>σοφίζω</i> (sophízō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>second person singular personal pronoun: thou, you</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tú</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;túh₂</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>त्वम्</i> (tvam), Latin <i>tū</i>, Persian تو (to) and Old English <i>þū</i> (English <i>thou</i>). The initial /s/ was spread to the nominative from oblique forms such as accusative <i>σέ</i> (sé) &lt; <i>&#42;twé</i>, where /s/ is the regular development of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tw-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>λάγνος</i> (lágnos, “lecherous”).</li></ol><p>A connection with Latin <i>subō</i> (“to be in heat”) has no plausibility. Several glosses can be compared, like <i>σύβακα</i> (súbaka, “swinish”), <i>συβάλλας</i> (subállas, “prone to sexual pleasures”), <i>ὑβάλλης</i> (hubállēs, “lecherous”). According to Beekes, all these words are probably from the same Pre-Greek root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to lie together</li><li>(functioning as the passive of <i>συντίθημι</i> (suntíthēmi)) to be composed, be compounded; to be contrived, concocted</li><li>to be agreed on</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>called together, summoned, invited (guests); convened, convoked; specially assembled</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(government,&#32;politics) synclite (specially summoned council, assembly of elders (as at classical Athens)&#59; legislative body, parliament, congress, senate)</li><li>(Koine,&#32;history) the Roman Senate in Ancient Rome; supreme administrative and legislative body of the Roman Republic and advisory council of the Roman Empire, convening in the city of Rome</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;history) synclite (the Eastern Roman (Byzantine) Senate&#59; the legislative, administrative, advisory body of the Byzantine Roman Empire, convening in the city of Constantinople)</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠγκᾰλέω</i> (sŭnkăléō, “to call to council, convoke, convene”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos). In the Byzantine sense, a semantic loan from Latin <i>senātus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aggregation</li></ol><p><i>συγκρούω</i> (sunkroúō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contemporaneous</li></ol><p>From <i>συγ-</i> (sug-) +&lrm; <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mixing</li><li>confounding</li><li>disturbing</li><li>overthrow, revolt</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>χῠ́σῐς</i> (khŭ́sĭs, “pouring”), or from <i>σῠγχέω</i> (sŭnkhéō, “to pour together, confound, trouble”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yoked together, paired</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) wife</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) yokefellow, comrade</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>ζυγός</i> (zugós, “yoke, pair”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yewg-</i> (“to join, tie together”). Root cognate with Latin <i>coniūnx</i>.</p><p>Compare typologically Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sǫprǫgъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the fig tree</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) worthless, futile, useless, good-for-nothing (because its wood was proverbially cheap and useless)</li><li>of figs</li></ol><p>From <i>σῦκον</i> (sûkon, “fig”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>συγχίς</i> (sunkhís, “a kind of shoe or sock”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right of seizure of a ship or its cargo of a foreign merchant, to cover losses received through him</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) right of seizure, right of reprisal</li></ol><p>There is no certain etymology. The word is similar to <i>σκῦλα</i> (skûla, “booty, spoils”), but their relation is unclear, and Beekes is hesitant to assign the word to Pre-Greek, assuming an interchange in initials of σ-/σκ-. He mentions Pisani's theory of a Lydian borrowing, along with Latin <i>spolia</i>, though this is all speculative, particularly as the Latin has a solid internal derivation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gathered or brought together</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective of <i>συλλέγω</i> (sullégō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contribution</li></ol><p>From <i>συλλέγω</i> (sullégō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) seizing, arresting</li><li>conception</li></ol><p>From <i>συλλαμβάνω</i> (sullambánō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly</li><li>(<i>military</i>) muster</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) collectedness, presence of mind</li></ol><p>From <i>συλ-</i> (sul-) + <i>λέγω</i> (légō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settlement, agreement, treaty</li></ol><p>From <i>συλλύω</i> (sullúō, “to settle, put an end to”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement, arrangement, treaty</li><li>conjunction</li><li>coincidence</li></ol><p><i>σῠμβαίνω</i> (sŭmbaínō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of plants</i>) living together</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companion, partner</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>βίος</i> (bíos, “life”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sign or token by which one infers a thing</li><li>a pledge or pawn, on which money was advanced</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) tallies</li><li>(<i>Athens</i>) a ticket, counter</li><li>a permit or licence to reside, given to aliens</li><li>(biblical,&#32;Ecclesiastes) the distinctive mark of Christians, a confession of faith, a creed</li><li>(law,&#32;in the plural) covenants between two states for protection of commerce</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠμβάλλω</i> (sŭmbállō, “to throw together, dash together, compare, correspond, tally, come to a conclusion”), from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw, put”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adviser, counselor</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ, “council”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fighting along with, leagued or allied with</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) ally</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;masculine) a kind of public emissary of tax administration</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “with”) +&lrm; the root of <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “battle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>commensurate with, of like measure or size with</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “measure”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>universe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all together, all at once</li><li>(<i>with singular mass nouns</i>) the whole</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all, whole”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything that has befallen one, a chance, casualty</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>often in a bad sense, mishap</li></ol><li>(<i>of diseases</i>) symptom</li><li>a falling in, collapse</li></ol><p>From <i>συμπίπτω</i> (sumpíptō, “happen, occur”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful, profitable, expedient, suitable</li><li>accompanying</li></ol><p><i>σύμφερω</i> (súmpherō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Growing together, natural junction, especially of the bones.</li><li>Closing or healing up.</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠμφῠ́ω</i> (sŭmphŭ́ō, “to make to grow together, to unite”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfrey (<i>Symphytum bulbosum</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>σύμφυτος</i> (súmphutos, “grown together”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to make to grow together; to unite</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to grow together; to unite</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to unite with</li><li>to become assimilated or natural</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “to bring forth, to produce”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;grammar) consonant</li></ol><p>From <i>σύμφωνος</i> (súmphōnos, adjective): as a noun, a substantivisation of its neuter forms, in elliptical use for <i>σύμφωνον γράμμα</i> (súmphōnon grámma, “consonant [letter]”); as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agreeing in sound, harmonious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a cough mixture used by Antonius Musa</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with, together”) +&lrm; <i>-φωνος</i> (-phōnos, “sound, tone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>beside; with</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ksún</i>, but further connections are uncertain. Younger form of Homeric and Old Attic <i>ξῠ́ν</i> (xŭ́n), Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀓𐀱</i> (ku-su /⁠*ksun⁠/). These probably reflect Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;som-</i> (“one, together (with)”) contaminated with the <i>*ḱ</i> of <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> (“beside, with”) along with a conflation of their meanings. However, the /u/ is unexpected and unexplained under this proposal. Note that a derivation from <i>*som-</i> alone is impossible since the <i>*s-</i> would be expected to give <i>h-</i> (aspiration); neither is the <i>ks-</i> explainable from <i>*ḱóm</i> alone. This makes its exact relation to Lithuanian <i>sù</i> and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;sъ(n)</i> dubious, but if indeed a conflation of <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> and <i>&#42;som-</i>, it is a cognate relation regardless.</p><p>The dative that this preposition governs continues the PIE comitative-instrumental.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contact</li><li>point or line of junction</li><li>union, cluster</li><li>league</li></ol><p>From <i>συνάπτω</i> (sunáptō, “join together, connect”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>binding together</li><li>constriction</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) conjunctive construction</li></ol><p>From <i>συνδέω</i> (sundéō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis) and <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>δέσις</i> (désis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bond, band, fetter</li><li>headband</li><li>ligament, sinew, joint</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) conjunction</li><li>bundle</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>δεσμός</i> (desmós, “bond”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bound hand and foot</li><li>united with</li><li>(neuter,&#32;in the plural,&#32;substantive) compounds, concrete things</li><li>well knit together</li></ol><p>From <i>συνδέω</i> (sundéō, “bind together”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos), from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “bind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>advocate, barrister, lawyer, pleader</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́κη</i> (dĭ́kē, “justice”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running together, meeting</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) place where several roads meet</li><li>running along with, close-following</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a person</i>) sitting in council with</li><li>(<i>of a bird</i>) sitting together; friendly</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who sits with others; assessor; assistant</li><li>elected committee member; select commissioner; selectman</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) delegates to an assembly</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) council of elders, senate</li></ol><p>Formed as <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>ἕδρᾱ</i> (hédrā, “seat; session”); the noun is a substantive use of the adjective.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be with, be joined or linked [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>to have intercourse, live [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>to attend (be present at)</li><li>to have dealings, have to do [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>to take part [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>to go or come together, to assemble</li><li>to meet (<i>in battle</i>), engage in war</li><li>to come together, meet to deliberate</li><li>to come in (<i>of revenue</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “co-”) +&lrm; <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “to be”).</p><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “co-”) +&lrm; <i>εἶμῐ</i> (eîmĭ, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a conflux, uniting, coming together</li><li>understanding, intelligence</li><li>conscience</li><li>a branch of art or science</li><li>a decision or decree</li><li>(<i>Gnosticism</i>) one of the aeons or emanations of the Godhead, representing intelligence</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνῑ́ημῐ</i> (sŭnī́ēmĭ, “bring together”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a putting together, composition, combination, juxtaposition, synthesis</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) addition</li><li>agreement, treaty</li><li>collection</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠντῐ́θημῐ</i> (sŭntĭ́thēmĭ) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything agreed upon, preconcerted signal</li><li>watchword</li><li>token, sign</li><li>agreement, covenant</li></ol><p>From <i>συντίθημι</i> (suntíthēmi) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honored in the same temple; sharing a temple with another deity</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;in the dative) connected to something else</li></ol><p>From <i>συν-</i> (sun-, “beside, with”) +&lrm; <i>νᾱ(ός)</i> (nā(ós), “temple”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meditation, contemplation</li><li>anxious thought, anxiety</li></ol><p>From <i>σύννοος</i> (súnnoos, “in deep thought, thoughtful”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly, meeting</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) political associations, conspiracies</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) synods</li><li>company, guild</li><li>the meeting of two armies</li><li>sexual intercourse</li><li>constriction</li><li>union, assemblage, combination</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) construction</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) conjunction</li><li>incoming revenue</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way, path”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to share in knowledge of, be privy to</li><li>to be conscious of, be cognizant of</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>οἶδα</i> (oîda)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border, boundary, limit; frontier</li></ol><p>Substantive use of <i>σῠ́νορος, σῠ́νορον</i> (sŭ́noros, sŭ́noron, “conterminous with, neighboring, bordering”), from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “beside, with, along with”) +&lrm; <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “boundary”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>conterminous with, neighboring, bordering</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “beside, with, along with”) +&lrm; <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “boundary”), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wórwos</i> (whence Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀺𐀺</i> (wo-wo)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werw-</i>; possible cognates include Latin <i>urvō</i> (“to trace, mark out bounds”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>both&#32;literal&#32;and&#32;figurative</i>) a seeing all together, general view, shared view</li><li>epitome, synopsis, abridgment</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>recapitulation</li></ol><li>estimate</li><li>expense</li></ol><p><i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>ὄψῐς</i> (ópsĭs, “aspect, appearance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) a body of troops, squadron, contingent</li><li>(politics,&#32;government) the constitution of a state</li><li>(<i>music</i>) an arrangement of musical notes</li><li>a regular collection of writings, a work, book, doctrine</li><li>synonym of <i>σύνταξις</i> (súntaxis)</li></ol><p>From <i>συντάσσω</i> (suntássō, “to put together”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma) and <i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>τάγμα</i> (tágma, “command, order”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an arranging, putting in order</li><li>array, arrangement, organization, system, order</li><li>composition, treatise</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) syntax</li><li>body of troops, contingent</li><li>company, troupe</li><li>covenant, previous arrangement</li><li>tribute, levy</li><li>subvention, pension</li></ol><p>From <i>συντάσσω</i> (suntássō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tension, rigidity</li><li>vehement effort, exertion</li></ol><p>From <i>συντείνω</i> (sunteínō, “to stretch”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially of a road, as in a shortcut</i>) cut short, abridged</li><li>(<i>of language</i>) concise, brief</li><li>(<i>of stature</i>) short</li><li>(<i>of other things</i>) short</li></ol><p><i>συν-</i> (sun-) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), in the o-grade.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anything trailed or dragged</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Koine sense</i>) A theatric robe with a long train</li><li>sweepings, refuse, litter</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) An abrasion, a scaly skin disease</li></ol><li>A dragging, a trailing motion</li></ol><p>From <i>σύρω</i> (súrō, “to draw, drag, trail”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broom, brushwood</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>σύρω</i> (súrō, “to drag, trail, sweep”) and <i>σαίρω</i> (saírō, “to sweep”), reflecting a Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;tuer-</i> (“to sweep, stir”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Σύρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A common male slave name</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Syrian</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒀸𒋩𒆠</i> (Aššūr, “Assur”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flowing together, confluent</li></ol><p>From <i>συρρέω</i> (surrhéō, “to flow together”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>σέρφος</i> (sérphos, “gnat; winged ant”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw, drag, or trail along</li><li>to drag by force, hale</li><li>to sweep or carry away</li></ol><p>From a zero-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twer-</i> (“to twirl, swirl”); see the related <i>σαίρω</i> (saírō, “to sweep”) for cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a whole made of several parts or members, system.</li><li>an organized government, confederacy</li><li>a body of soldiers, corps</li><li>flock, herd</li><li>college of priests or magistrates</li><li>(<i>music</i>) the combination of several tones</li><li>the combination of several verses into a whole composition</li><li>the accumulation of sediment</li><li>machine, apparatus</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠνῐ́στημῐ</i> (sŭnĭ́stēmĭ, “to place together, associate, unite”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a narrow mouth</li><li>(<i>of a kiss</i>) mouth-to-mouth</li></ol><p>From <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a swine</li></ol><p>From <i>σῦς</i> (sûs, “swine”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket or box</li></ol><p>From <i>σωρός</i> (sōrós, “heap, pile, mound”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰκος</i> (-ăkos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rocket larkspur (<i>Consolida ajacis</i>, syn. <i>Delphinium ajacis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe, sound”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νήρ</i> (ănḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>savior <i>(female)</i></li></ol><p>From <i>σωτήρ</i> (sōtḗr, “savior”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă), using the old full-grade-suffix stem <i>&#42;σωτερ-</i> (&#42;sōter-), contrary to the synchronically applied, generalized lengthened grade as found in the genitive <i>σωτῆρος</i> (sōtêros). Metathesis <i>&#42;σώτερϳᾰ</i> (&#42;sṓterjă) → <i>σώτειρᾰ</i> (sṓteiră) then occurred regularly.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of sound mind: sane</li><li>moderate, prudent, sensible, reasonable</li><li>self-controlled, temperate, chaste, sober</li></ol><p>From <i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine&#32;and&#32;Byzantine</i>) Your Serene Highness(es)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>σῆμα</i> (sêma)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark, sign, token</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a sign from the gods, an omen, portent</li><li>a sign to begin something, watchword, signal, banner</li><li>the sign by which a grave is known, mound, cairn, barrow</li><li>a mark to show the case of a quoit or javelin</li><li>a token by which one's identity or commission was certified</li><li>a constellation</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰyéh₂mn̥</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰyeh₂-</i> (“to notice”) (see Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeyh₂-</i>), although Beekes is skeptical of the semantics. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>ध्यायति</i> (dhyāyati, “think of, imagine”), <i>ध्यान</i> (dhyāna, “meditation”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cave hollowed out by water</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pore or cavity of the body</li></ol><p>Formally similar to <i>φᾰ́ρᾰγξ</i> (phắrănx, “ravine”). Sometimes connected with <i>σέσηρᾰ</i> (sésēră, “to grin”), but this is semantically improbable. It is more likely a Pre-Greek word, in view of the suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sieve, strainer</li></ol><p>From <i>σήθω</i> (sḗthō, “to sift, bolt”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>putrefaction, sepsis</li></ol><p>From <i>σήπω</i> (sḗpō, “to make rotten”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>silently</li><li>under one's breath, in a whisper, secretly</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>σιγή</i> (sigḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sigma, the name for the eighteenth letter Σ (S), σ (s) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Phoenician <i>𐤔</i> (š‬ /⁠šīn⁠/) doubtful at best. Name agreed to be from <i>σῐ́ζω</i> (sĭ́zō, “to hiss”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an unknown kind of fish</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grain; corn (in the British sense, encompassing wheat and barley, the cereal grains used by the ancient Greeks)</li><li>grains and lentils</li><li>bread (as opposed to meat)</li><li>food (as opposed to drink)</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin, though probably of Indo-European origin. Similar words in other languages include Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;géiˀta</i> (“grain, corn”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;hwaitijaz</i> (“wheat”), Basque <i>zitu</i> (“corn, harvest”), and Sumerian (zid, “flour”), which are often taken as donor languages to Greek.</p><p>A more recent theory by Janda has explained it as the substantivization of an adjective <i>&#42;σῑτό-</i> (&#42;sītó-, “threshed”), derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tih₂-tó-</i> (“struck”), a participle from the newly suggested verbal root <i>&#42;tyeh₂-</i> (“to strike, hit”), which is apparently also seen in Hittite (zāḫ-ᶦ, “to hit, beat”). Then <i>σῆμα</i> (sêma, “sign, mark”) (&lt; <i>&#42;tiéh₂-mn</i> (“what is carved”)), <i>σῶμα</i> (sôma, “corpse”) (&lt; <i>&#42;tióh₂-mn</i> (“the killed one”)), and <i>σῑμός</i> (sīmós, “snub-nosed”) (&lt; <i>&#42;tih₂-mó-</i> (“flattened”)) could be related. Beekes is doubtful of Janda's derivation, due to the wide range of supposed meanings within only one language (Greek) and the lack of a direct "strike" meaning within Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fig, the fruit of the <i>συκῆ</i> (sukê)</li><li>a large wart on the eyelids</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hemorrhoids</li></ol><li>vulva</li></ol><p>Since long, connected with Latin <i>fīcus</i> and Old Armenian <i>թուզ</i> (tʻuz), and considered a borrowing from a Mediterranean (Pre-Greek) or Anatolian substrate source, from which Hebrew <i>שִׁקְמָה</i> (šiqmā́, “sycamore fig”) – borrowed into <i>συκάμινος</i> (sukáminos) – and possibly Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tyky</i> (“gourd”) are also adduced. Martirosyan reconstructs a Mediterranean proto-form <i>&#42;tʰuōiḱo-</i> or <i>&#42;tʰū(i)ḱo-</i> (“fig”), Beekes suggests <i>*tʲuk-</i>. The use of words for fruits (fig, plum etc.) in the sense of “vulva” is very common cross-linguistically.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) load, burden, freight</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested in a grammatical work by Theognostus.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pan pipes</li><li>catcall, whistle, hiss</li><li>mouthpiece of an <i>ᾰὐλός</i> (ăulós, “pipe, flute”)</li><li>quill of the cassia</li><li>anything pipe-shaped</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>spear-case</li><li>hole in the nave of a wheel</li><li>aperture of a hinge</li><li>(medicine,&#32;in the plural) bronchial passages</li><li>fistulous sore, abscess</li><li>groove or barrel of a catapult</li><li>subterranean passage, mine</li><li>covered gallery, cloister</li><li>channel</li><li>loop</li></ol></ol><p>The suffix -ῐγγ- implies a Pre-Greek origin because of non-native pronunciation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig; swine; hog</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;súHs</i> (“swine”); doublet of <i>ὗς</i> (hûs). The reason for the survival of initial /s/ in this form is unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of old or wrinkled skin</li><li>wrinkled, decrepit person</li><li>slough of a serpent</li><li>skim of milk; scum</li><li>wrinkled fig</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>decrepit; ancient</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared with Latin <i>sūber</i> (“cork oak”), in spite of the semantic difficulties. If correct, they are probably borrowings from a common source, perhaps substrate. According to Pisani, it is related to <i>ὕφεᾰρ</i> (húpheăr, “mistletoe”), but formally and semantically this is hardly possible. Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin of the word. This is further evidenced by the suffix <i>-ᾰρ</i> (-ăr), which can also be seen in the words <i>κύδαρ</i> (kúdar), <i>σκίναρ</i> (skínar), and <i>ἴκταρ</i> (íktar). Note that it is indeclinable as well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to save</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to heal</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) to save</li><li>(<i>rare in Homer</i>) Ι keep safe, preserve</li><li>to keep, observe, maintain</li><li>(<i>usually middle</i>) to keep in mind, remember</li><li>to bring safely (to)</li><li>to rescue</li><li>to save for</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>with participle</i>)</li></ol></ol><p><i>σῶς</i> (sôs, “safe, sound”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body (both that of people and animals)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Homeric&#32;and&#32;often in other early works</i>) dead body</li></ol><li>One's life in the physical world</li><li>That which is material (as opposed to spiritual)</li><li>person</li><li>An entire thing</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) three-dimensional object</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) melanterite; ferrous sulphate</li></ol><p>A foreign word of unknown origin. The formation recalls <i>μίσυ</i> (mísu), <i>βράθυ</i> (bráthu) and <i>μῶλυ</i> (môlu). The variation <i>υ/ι</i> points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>safe and sound, alive and well</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;twáwos</i> or similar, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tweh₂-</i>, metathesis of <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to be strong, whole”). Beekes suggests the original word was <i>&#42;sáus</i> (&#42;σαυς), which thematicized to <i>&#42;sáwos</i> (&#42;σάϝος).</p><p>Compare Old Armenian <i>քաւ</i> (kʻaw, “expiation”). Related to <i>σάλος</i> (sálos), <i>σῶμα</i> (sôma), <i>σωρός</i> (sōrós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Babylon</i>) sossos, a period of 60 years</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒋗𒅆</i> (šūši, “a unit of sixty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reward for saving one's life, thank-offering for deliverance from a danger</li><li>reward for bringing back lost cattle or runaway slaves</li><li>physician's fee, thank-offering to Asclepius</li></ol><p>From <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō, “to save”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron). Compare also <i>σαοστρέω</i> (saostréō, “to make thank-offerings”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden felloe of the wheel</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τε</i> (te)</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) apocopic form of <i>τοι</i> (toi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) tablet; plank; table</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tabla</i> (“tablet, plank, table”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frying pan, saucepan</li></ol><p>Beekes notes comparisons by Bezzenberger and Fick to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þakjaną</i> (“to burn”), which he notes is isolated within Germanic, and takes the Greek, per Furnee, from a Pre-Greek substrate.</p><p>A further analysis of the origins of the borrowing takes the Greek term as a rebracketing of <i>το</i> (to) + <i>&#42;άγηνον</i> (&#42;ágēnon), with the latter equivalent to generic Semitic terms for a bowl, such as Aramaic <i>אַגָּנָא</i> / <i>אַגָּאנָא</i> / <i>ܐܓܢܐ</i> (ʾaggānā), Hebrew <i>אַגָּן</i> (ʾaggā́n), Ugaritic <i>𐎀𐎂𐎐</i> (ảgn), Akkadian <i>𒌓𒅗𒁇</i> (UD.KA.BAR /⁠agannum⁠/), and Egyptian <i>jkn</i> (“bowl”), of a vague borrowing source within the area of the present languages.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command, order</li><li>arrangement</li><li>body of soldiers, division, brigade</li></ol><p>From <i>τάσσω</i> (tássō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold</li></ol><p>In view of the variations, the word is of Pre-Greek origin, with the characteristic Pre-Greek sequence <i>&#42;-arʷ-</i> realised as <i>-ουρ-</i> (-our-) or <i>-αρ-</i> (-ar-) in the different variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τάδε</i> (táde)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τάδε</i> (táde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on this account</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(demonstrative pronoun,&#32;proximal) this</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) here</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with verbs of action</i>) here; (<i>possibly</i>) there, yonder</li><li>(<i>modifying a personal pronoun</i>)</li><li>(with <i>τίς</i>)</li><li>(<i>adds precision to adverbs of place and time</i>), just, very</li><li>(<i>in Attic dialogue, the masculine and feminine pronouns often refer to the speaker</i>)</li><li>(<i>in Aristotle, neuter designates some particular thing</i>)</li></ol><li>(of time,&#32;to indicate the immediate present)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>this present</li><li>these</li><li>(<i>elliptic with genitive</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>to indicate something before one</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>to indicate something immediately to come</i>) the following</li><li>(<i>followed by a relative pronoun</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>τῇδε</i>)</li><li>(accusative neuter,&#32;τόδε) hither, to this spot</li><li>(dative neuter plural,&#32;τοῖσδε, τοισίδε) in or with these words</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὁ</i> (ho) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>τήκω</i> (tḗkō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂-</i> (“to melt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balance, scale (usually in the plural)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>tax paid for use of public scales</li></ol><li>anything weighed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>talent (weight, often of gold or silver)</li><li>the monetary sum equaling a talent (weight) of gold or silver</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tálanton</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tl̥h₂ent-</i>, from <i>&#42;telh₂-</i> (“to bear, endure”), whence also <i>ἔτλην</i> (étlēn, “to carry, endure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket of wickerwork</li><li>wicker cage for fowls</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>λαγαρός</i> (lagarós) and <i>χαλαρός</i> (khalarós). It is a substantivization from an adjective <i>&#42;ταλαρός</i> (&#42;talarós), built from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;telh₂-</i> (“to bear”), with regular shift of accent.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>miserable</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;telh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rennet</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek in view of the suffix "-ισ-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”)</li></ol><p>See <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) friendly term of address</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τάν)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan form of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrangement, ordering</li><li>battle array, order of battle</li><li>(<i>military</i>) rank, line of soldiers</li><li>post, place, position, rank</li><li>division, brigade, company, cohort</li><li>band, company</li><li>arrangement, disposition, manner, nature</li><li>assessment</li><li>order, good order</li><li>duty</li><li>order, class</li></ol><p>From <i>τᾰ́σσω</i> (tắssō, “I arrange”, “I order”, aorist passive participial stem: <i>τᾰγ-</i>, <i>tag-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpet, rug, mat</li></ol><p>The origin is uncertain. Traditionally derived from Iranian (compare Persian تنبسه (tanbase, “carpet, rug”)), but Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin, and implies that the Iranian forms are not cognate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and so</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>τε</i> (te) +&lrm; <i>ἄρ</i> (ár).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reindeer</li></ol><p>Unknown. Isebaert suggests the term is borrowed from Scythian, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱerh₂-</i> (“horn”), however the expected outcome in contemporary Proto-Sarmatian would likely be <i>&#42;sarand(ar)</i>, not <i>&#42;&#42;θaran-tara-</i>, as he reconstructs. Alternatively borrowed from Proto-Finno-Ugric <i>&#42;šardo</i>, <i>&#42;šardō</i>, <i>&#42;šordō</i>. There doesn't appear to be any link to Ancient Greek <i>ταῦρος</i> (taûros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trouble, confusion</li></ol><p>From <i>τᾰρᾰ́σσω</i> (tărắssō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alarm, terror, fright</li><li>awe, reverence</li></ol><p>Cognate to Sanskrit <i>तर्जति</i> (tarjati, “to threaten, revile”), Latin <i>torvus</i> (“grim, fierce”) and Welsh <i>tarfu</i> (“to scare away”).</p><p>Furnée compares <i>ταρμύσσω</i> (tarmússō, “to frighten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vinegar</li></ol><p>Not well explained. Traditionally compared with <i>στεργάνος</i> (stergános, “place for dung”) and <i>τρύξ</i> (trúx, “lees of wine, dregs”). The difference in final velar makes comparison with the group of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terkʷ-</i> (“to turn”) (like Latin <i>torqueō</i> (“to twist, wind”) and Sanskrit <i>तर्कु</i> (tarku, “spindle”)) impossible. Expression for "turn" are often applied to drinks that have turned sour (compare <i>τροπίας</i> (tropías, “sour wine”)). Semantically, one may compare Dutch <i>wrang</i> (“astringent, tart”), to Gothic <i>𐍅𐍂𐌿𐌲𐌲𐍉</i> (wruggō, “snare”). According to Beekes, the variation <i>τ/σ</i> shows that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>τᾰ́ρῑχος</i> (tắrīkhos)</li></ol><p>A collateral form of <i>τᾰ́ρῑχος</i> (tắrīkhos, “mummy”, “preserved meat”, “stockfish”).</p><p>A declined form of <i>τᾰ́ρῑχος</i> (tắrīkhos, noun).</p><p>Declined forms of <i>τᾰ́ρῑχος</i> (tắrīkhos, adjective).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dead body preserved by embalming, mummy</li><li>meat preserved by salting, pickling, drying, or smoking, especially dried or smoked fish</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of a stupid fellow) a stockfish</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>τᾰρῑχευτός</i> (tărīkheutós, “salted, pickled”)</li></ol><p>According to Lagarde, borrowed from an Old Armenian predecessor of Middle Armenian <i>տարեխ</i> (tarex, “Van fish”), which would correspond to the famous export of salted Van fish from Armenia, but the borrowing is rather in the opposite direction because the Armenian term is late and sporadically used; besides the meaning appears more general in Greek. However it can still be of Anatolian – according to Beekes Pre-Greek – origin and has been put together by Puhvel with the Homeric Ancient Greek <i>ταρχύω</i> (tarkhúō, “to bury like a god or hero”) used for the Lycian last rites, Hesychian <i>ταρχάνιον</i> (tarkhánion, “funeral feast”), <i>τέρχανον</i> (térkhanon), <i>τάρχανον</i> (tárkhanon, “grief”) and connected to Lycian (trqqas /⁠*tarḫants⁠/, “god”), Milyan (trqqiz /⁠*tarḫints⁠/, “god”), Luwian <small>[Anatolian hieroglyphic needed]</small> (tarḫu(nt), “the storm-god”); a mummy would be a dead-body preserved for or reserved for the gods.</p><p>Conversion of the former.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large wicker basket</li></ol><p>Frisk compares <i>ταργάναι</i> (targánai), <i>σαργάνη</i> (sargánē) and even <i>ταρσός</i> (tarsós), but these comparisons are useless. Furnée compares <i>δάρπη</i> (dárpē), which would point to a Pre-Greek word. Has also been connected with Old Armenian <i>թարփ</i> (tʻarpʻ).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delight</li><li>(<i>passive voice&#32;and&#32;middle voice</i>) to enjoy, to revel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;térpō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terp-</i> (“to satisfy”), cognate with German <i>dürfen</i> (“to need”) and obsolete and dialectal British English <i>tharf</i> (“to need”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) cream of tartar</li></ol><p>Possibly from Arabic دُرْدِيّ (durdiyy).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thicket, shrubbery</li></ol><p>Unknown. Usually derived from <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to feed”) but the development is irregular and the semantics are not compelling.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stretching, tension</li><li>extension</li><li>(<i>in music</i>) pitch of the voice</li><li>intensity, force</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tə́tsis</i>, from the oblique stem of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tén-tis</i>, from <i>&#42;ten-</i> (“stretch”). Equivalent to <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to stretch”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to arrange, put in order</li><li>(transitive,&#32;military) to arrange soldiers, array, marshal</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be drawn up (in battle order)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to post, station</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to appoint, assign</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to undertake</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to order, command</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to assess payments</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to agree upon</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to impose (punishments, laws)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;taťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂g-</i> (“to order, to establish, to rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>τᾰ́σσω</i> (tắssō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;taťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂g-</i> (“to touch, grasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral rites; burial; funeral; wake</li><li>tomb; grave; sepulcher</li><li>astonishment; amazement</li></ol><p>From <i>θᾰ́πτω</i> (thắptō, “to bury”), particularly from the aorist stem <i>τᾰφ-</i> (tăph-) with its metathesis of the aspiration.</p><p>Related to <i>θᾰ́μβος</i> (thắmbos, “amazement”) and <i>τέθηπᾰ</i> (téthēpă, “to be amazed; to be astounded”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ditch, trench, moat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰembʰ-</i>; compare Old Armenian <i>դամբան</i> (damban, “tomb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τάχα</i> (tákha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, soon, forthwith</li></ol><p>Neuter accusative plural of <i>τᾰχῠ́ς</i> (tăkhŭ́s, “quick”) used as adverb.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickest, speediest</li><li>(<i>after particles of time</i>) as soon as</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swift, quick, fast, rapid</li><li>In a short time: soon</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰn̥gʰ-ú-s</i>, zero-grade <i>u</i>-stem of <i>&#42;dʰengʰ-</i> (“to go quickly”), based on the comparison with Lithuanian <i>déngti</i> (“to run, hurry”) (see there for more potential cognates), though Fraenkel has disputed the connection.</p><p>Not related to Sanskrit <i>तकु</i> (taku), <i>तक्व</i> (takva, “quick, fast”), as Ancient Greek <i>χ</i> (kh) does not match Sanskrit <i>क्</i> (k), and the comparative <i>θάσσων</i> (thássōn) proves that the Greek <i>τ</i> (t) in <i>ταχύς</i> (takhús) is originally <i>θ</i> (th), from the aspirated PIE <i>&#42;dʰ</i> (see Grassmann's law).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speed, celerity, swiftness, velocity</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ταχύς</i> (takhús, “fast, quick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wet, moisten</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to soften (via soaking)</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to stain, dye</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;téngeti</i>, from <i>&#42;teng-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roof, cover</li><li>any place with roof</li><li>any covered hall or chamber</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;(s)tégos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tégos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teg-</i> (“roof”). Cognates include Latin <i>tegō</i> (“to cover”), German <i>Decke</i> (“roof”), English <i>thatch</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be astonished, astounded, amazed</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be amazed at</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quadriga, a four-horse chariot</li></ol><p>From <i>τέθρῐππος</i> (téthrĭppos), from <i>τετρα-</i> (tetra-, “four”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τέκετε</i> (tékete) before &#47;h&#47;</li><li>apocopic form of <i>τέκετο</i> (téketo) before &#47;h&#47;</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goal; end</li><li>token; pledge</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷékmər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷéḱ-mr̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>τέκμᾰρ</i> (tékmăr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child (of either gender)</li><li>descendant</li><li>young animal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;téknon</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teḱ-</i>, perhaps via <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō, “I bear”). Cognates include Old English <i>þegn</i> (“servant”), Old High German <i>degan</i> and possibly Sanskrit <i>तोक</i> (toka, “offspring, child”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) offspring, child</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) descendant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tékos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;téḱos</i>, from <i>&#42;teḱ-</i> (“to produce”) (whence <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō)). Doublet of Ancient Greek τέκνον (téknon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who works with wood: carpenter, builder</li><li>any craftsman (but generally opposed to metalworker, smith)</li><li>a master of any art, such as gymnastics, poetry, or medicine or engineering</li><li>author, creator, planner</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;téktōn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tétḱō</i> (“carpenter”), from <i>&#42;tetḱ-</i> (“to create, produce”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>तक्षन्</i> (tákṣan).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having reached its end, finished</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of victims</i>) complete, perfect, entire, without blemish</li><li>(<i>of animals and humans</i>) full-grown, adult</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) absolute, complete, accomplished, perfect</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of things</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>of statues</i>) life-sized</li><li>(<i>of prayers, vows, etc.</i>) fulfilled, accomplished</li><li>(<i>of numbers</i>) full, complete</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in arithmetic</i>) those numbers which are equal to the sum of their divisors</li></ol><li>the third bowl offered to Zeus</li></ol><li>(<i>of the gods</i>) perfect, omnipotent, infinite</li><li>last</li><li>(<i>neuter substantive</i>) a royal banquet</li><li>(<i>feminine substantive</i>) a full stop, period</li><li>(<i>adverb</i>) at last</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>completely, absolutely</li><li>(<i>the neuter is also used as an adverb</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “end”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix). Related to, and largely synonymous with, <i>τελήεις</i> (telḗeis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment, toll, taxes</li><li>certificate</li></ol><p>From <i>τελέω</i> (teléō, “to pay”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;Cretan) to accomplish, perform</li><li>to rise (of stars) = <i>ἀνατέλλω</i> (anatéllō)</li><li>(<i>περιτέλλομαι</i> (peritéllomai) only) to turn around in circles</li></ol><p>The fusion of two proto-Greek verbs:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standing water, pond, marsh, swamp</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) lowlands subject to inundation, water meadows</li><li>mud used as a mortar</li><li>space pointed with mortar, between the courses of masonry</li></ol><p>The origin remains unexplained. Has been compared to Old Armenian <i>տիղմ</i> (tiłm).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aim, purpose, end goal, design, final cause (as used by Aristotle and the philosophers&#59; cf. teleology)</li><li>completion, maturity, accomplishment, fulfillment, perfection, consummation</li><li>result, product</li><li>end</li><li>end of life: death</li><li>boundary, border, extremity</li><li>supreme power</li><li>the highest government office: magistrate</li><li>that which is ordered to be done: task, duty</li><li>money paid to the government: tax, toll</li><li>a person's property, according to which he was classed</li><li>thus, generally: rank, class</li><li>unit of soldiers: legion, company</li><li>initiation (especially into mystery religions)</li><li>mystery religion</li><li>any religious ceremony</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷélos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷél-os ~ &#42;kʷél-es-</i>. See Latin <i>colō</i> for more.</p><p>Like <i>τέλλω</i> (téllō), <i>τέλος</i> (télos) is a merger of two different roots: Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to turn, revolve around”) and <i>&#42;telh₂-</i> (“to bear, endure”), giving the two basic meanings of “end, goal”, i.e. “turning point (of the field or race-course)” (&lt; <i>&#42;kʷel-</i>), and “levy (of a tax or army)” (&lt; <i>&#42;telh₂-</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headland, the land where the plough turned</li><li>end, boundary, limit</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷels-</i> (“to carve, draw”), which corresponds to Hittite <i>𒄢𒀸𒍣</i> (gulšzi, “to carve, engrave, inscribe”), Sanskrit <i>कर्स्</i> (kars, “to pull, drag, plow”) and Avestan <i>𐬐𐬀𐬭𐬱</i> (karš, “to pull, drag, plow”); the retention of <i>-λσ-</i> is regular if the accent was on the preceding syllable. Forbes does not accept this phonological root and therefore derives the word from the root of <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “accomplishment, fulfillment”), which Beekes finds unsatisfactory and unnecessary, particularly if the root is to be reconstructed with a final laryngeal, <i>&#42;kʷelh₁-</i>, though this is debated.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slice of fish</li><li>slice of any food (meat, cheese)</li></ol><p>From <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “I cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece of ground cut or marked off, precinct</li><li>a sacred enclosure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;témenos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;témh₁nos</i>, from <i>&#42;temh₁-</i> (“to cut”); whence <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “I cut”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀳𐀕𐀜</i> (te-me-no).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut, hew</li><li>to maim, wound</li><li>to butcher, sacrifice</li><li>to cut down, reap</li><li>to cut through, advance, drive through, plow through</li></ol><p>For earlier <i>τάμνω</i> (támnō), with ε from aorist <i>ἔτεμον</i> (étemon), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tm̥-né-h₁-ti, &#42;tm̥-n-h₁-énti</i>, nasal-infixed present (the present tense system includes a <b>N</b> progressive aspect marker) from <i>&#42;temh₁-</i> (“to cut”).</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>temnō</i>, <i>tondeō</i>, and possibly <i>tempus</i>, <i>templum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoal water, shallows, lagoon, whether in the sea or in rivers</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tenh₂g-</i> (“shallow water”), though he adduces no other descendants, doubting connections with Latvian <i>tigas</i> (“deep spot between two shallow places”) (&lt; <i>&#42;tn̥h₂g-</i>). Others have connected Latin <i>stāgnum</i> (“pond, standing water”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw</li></ol><p>Rix and Beekes both suggest that it could derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;téndeti</i>, the thematic present of the root <i>&#42;tend-</i>. Alternatively, it has been connected with Ancient Greek <i>τένθης</i> (ténthēs). If this theory is accepted, then the <i>θ/δ</i> variation may indicate a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glutton, gourmand</li></ol><p>According to Bechtel, <i>τόνθων</i> (tónthōn, “spinal meat”) belongs here as well. Instead, Beekes envisages a connection with <i>τένδω</i> (téndō, “to gnaw”), in which case the variation <i>θ&#47;δ</i> (th&#47;d) points to Pre-Greek origin. The linguist Roberto Batisti posits a derivation from earlier <i>&#42;τένθω</i> (&#42;ténthō), itself perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷentʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷem-</i> (“to swallow”). According to this theory, the root may have been extended with the morpheme <i>&#42;-dʰh₁eti</i> (whence Ancient Greek <i>-θω</i> (-thō)). Still, Batisti notes that masculine ā-stem nouns are rare in Greek and largely denominal, perhaps indicating that the term replace a preexisting noun. Alternatively, it may have emerged from a compound. It is, however, unlikely to be derived from <i>προτένθης</i> (proténthēs, “foretaster”) due to the semantic differences. Batisti concludes that, although the term is likely to be derived from earlier <i>&#42;τένθω</i> (&#42;ténthō), it likely passed through several intermediary phases.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tendon, sinew</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ten-</i> (“to stretch”); related to <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to stretch”). Cognate with Serbo-Croatian <i>тѐтива</i>, Slovene <i>tetiva</i> (&lt; Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tętiva</i>), Polish <i>ścięgno</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) chamber; house</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;treb-</i> (“dwelling; settlement”), a root found in Latin <i>trabs</i> (“beam”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurpą</i> (“village”), Lithuanian <i>trōbà</i> (“farmhouse”). However, Beekes states that these words cannot be related, as the Greek pre-form would be along the lines of <i>&#42;terh₂b-no-m</i> and thus mismatched, and instead draws a comparison with <i>θερᾰ́πων</i> (therắpōn, “servant; maid”). According to him, the alternations <i>θ&#47;τ</i> (th&#47;t) and <i>μν&#47;πν</i> (mn&#47;pn) point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sign; marvel; wonder</li><li>divine sign; omen; portent</li><li>monster</li></ol><p>Possibly from the stem <i>&#42;kʷern̥t-</i> of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kwéronts</i>, active participle of <i>&#42;kʷéreti</i> (“to do, make, perform (magic, etc.)”), thematic variant of <i>&#42;kʷért</i>, in any case from the root <i>&#42;kʷer-</i>, or formed within Greek from a lost verb <i>&#42;τέρω</i> (&#42;térō) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰς</i> (-ăs). Compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čarъ</i> (“magic, sorcery”) for the semantic development.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>τέρᾰμνον</i> (térămnon, “chamber; house”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>borer, gimlet</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;téretron</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;térh₁trom</i>, from the root <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“to rub away at; to bore, drill”). By surface analysis, <i>τετραίνω</i> (tetraínō, “to bore”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron). Cognate with Old Irish <i>tarathar</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) soft, delicate, gentle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ter-</i> (“soft, tender, young”), the same root of Latin <i>tener</i> (“soft, tender”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the end of the sail-yard</li><li>end, extremity</li><li>extremity, crisis</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tértʰron</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;térh₂-dʰro-m</i>, from <i>&#42;terh₂-</i> (“to cross over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end, boundary</li><li>goal round which horses and chariots had to turn at races</li><li>mark set to show how far a quoit was thrown</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) end, limit, bound</li><li>culmination, highest point, goal</li><li>highest power, supremacy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;térmn̥</i>, the same source of Latin <i>terminus</i> (“boundary, limit, end”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τέρματα</i> (térmata)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>τέρματε</i> (térmate)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>τέρματι</i> (térmati)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terebinth (<i>Pistacia terebinthus</i>)</li><li>swelling like the fruit of the terebinth</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) parasitic growth on the olive</li></ol><p>The suffix "-ινθος" proves a Pre-Greek origin. The form <i>τερέβινθος</i> (terébinthos) was perhaps influenced by <i>ἐρέβινθος</i> (erébinthos, “chickpea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delight</li><li>(<i>passive voice&#32;and&#32;middle voice</i>) to enjoy, to revel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;térpō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terp-</i> (“to satisfy”), cognate with German <i>dürfen</i> (“to need”) and obsolete and dialectal British English <i>tharf</i> (“to need”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be or become dry, dry up</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ters-</i> (“dry”). Cognates include Latin <i>terra</i>, Sanskrit <i>तृष्यति</i> (tṛṣyati), Old Armenian <i>թառամիմ</i> (tʻaṙamim, “to wither”) and Old English <i>þurst</i> (English <i>thirst</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) young shoot; twig; sprout</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ἔρνος</i> (érnos) and <i>κτῆνος</i> (ktênos). Niedermann pleaded for connection with <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō, “to run”). However, Furnée believes the word is Pre-Greek and compares <i>ταρχάνιον</i> (tarkhánion, “used in burial”), suggesting the use of twigs as offerings. Compare Albanian <i>trikë</i>. Compare Sanskrit <i>दर्हति</i> (darhati, “to grow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>full enjoyment, delight, gladness, pleasure</li></ol><p>From <i>τέρπω</i> (térpō, “I satisfy”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs). Diachronically from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;térptis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;térptis</i> (“fulfillment, satisfaction”); cognate with Sanskrit <i>तृप्ति</i> (tṛ́pti, “satisfaction, delight”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þurftiz</i> (“need”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>four</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷétwores</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwóres</i>. Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀤𐀵𐀫</i> (qe-to-ro), Sanskrit <i>चतुर्</i> (catur), Old Armenian <i>չորք</i> (čʻorkʻ), Latin <i>quattuor</i>, and Old English <i>fēower</i> (English <i>four</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Convulsive tension, tetanus.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an erection of the penis.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τετανός</i> (tetanós, “stretched, rigid”), a reduplicated formation from <i>τείνω</i> (teínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pheasant (<i>Phasianus colchicus</i>)</li></ol><p>Liddell and Scott remark that the word is a loan from the Orient, possibly Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;tatr̥wáh</i> (“pheasant”), referring to Middle Persian (tedzrev, “pheasant”). They further compare Lithuanian <i>tetervà</i> (“black grouse”). Athenaeus of Naucratis states that this word was imported from Media. See also <i>τέτραξ</i> (tétrax, “hazel grouse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fourth</li><li>(in neuter,&#32;τὸ τέταρτον,&#32;as an adverb) fourthly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τέταρτος μέρος (méros)) fourth, quarter</li></ol><li>(<i>in feminine</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τετάρτη ἡμέρα (hēméra)) the fourth day</li><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τετάρτη μοῖρα (moîra)) a liquid measure (compare English <i>quart</i>); The Spartan kings had a <i>μέδινος</i> (médinos) of corn, and a <i>τετάρτη</i> (tetártē) of wine on the 1st and 7th of the month</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>a fourth part</li></ol></ol></ol><p>Compare <i>τέσσᾰρες</i> (téssăres, “four”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazel grouse (<i>Tetrastes bonasia</i>)</li></ol><p>Originally onomatopoeic. For the suffix, compare the bird names <i>κόραξ</i> (kórax, “crow”) and <i>πέρδιξ</i> (pérdix, “partridge”). Similar bird names are Lithuanian <i>tetervà</i> (“grey hen”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tetervь</i> (“black grouse”), Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;tatr̥wáh</i> (“pheasant”) and Sanskrit <i>ति॒त्ति॒र</i> (tittirá, “partridge”). From the same onomatopoeic root are <i>τετράων</i> (tetráōn), <i>τέτριξ</i> (tétrix), <i>τατύρας</i> (tatúras), <i>τέταρος</i> (tétaros) and <i>τετράζω</i> (tetrázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>τέταρτος</i> (tétartos, “fourth”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in four parts</li></ol><p>Compare <i>τέτταρες</i> (téttares, “four”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pipit (bird of the genus <i>Anthus</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably onomatopoeic. Similar bird names are Lithuanian <i>tetervà</i> (“grey hen”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tetervь</i> (“black grouse”), Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;tatr̥wáh</i> (“pheasant”) and Sanskrit <i>तित्तिर</i> (tittira, “partridge”). From the same onomatopoeic root are <i>τετράων</i> (tetráōn), <i>τέτραξ</i> (tétrax), <i>τατύρας</i> (tatúras), <i>τέταρος</i> (tétaros) and <i>τετράζω</i> (tetrázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>τέσσαρες</i> (téssares, “four”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cicada (<i>Lyristes plebejus</i> or allied species&#59; a winged insect fond of basking on trees, when the male makes a chirping or clicking noise by means of certain drums or “tymbals” underneath its wings)</li><li>a gold ornament worn in the hair, especially in early Attica</li><li>a part of the ear</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic of the cicada's chirping noises, like <i>τιτίζω</i> (titízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ashes, as of the funeral pyre.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰegʷʰ-</i> (“to burn”). The unaspirated first consonant is due to Grassmann's law.</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>foveō</i>, Albanian <i>djeg</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>жещи</i> (žešti), Lithuanian <i>degti</i>, Avestan <i>𐬛𐬀𐬲𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (daža<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti), and Sanskrit <i>दहति</i> (dahati), whence <i>दह्र</i> (dahra, “burning wood”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ashcoloured</li></ol><p>From <i>τέφρα</i> (téphra, “ash”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craft, skill, trade</li><li>art</li><li>cunning, wile</li><li>means</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;téḱ-sneh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;tetḱ-</i> (“to create, produce”). Cognate with <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō), <i>τέκτων</i> (téktōn), Sanskrit <i>तक्षति</i> (takṣati), Avestan <i>𐬙𐬀𐬱𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (taša<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti), Latin <i>texō</i>, <i>tēla</i>, <i>tignum</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þahsuz</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>meanwhile; in the meantime</li><li>until then; during that time</li><li>for a time; for a while</li><li>hitherto; up until that time</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂wot</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>तावत्</i> (tāvat).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τέως)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Teos, Ionia; now Sığacık, in Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garment of a distinguished person similar to a toga</li></ol><p>In antiquity, the word was derived from the Arcadian personal names <i>Τήμενος</i> (Tḗmenos) or <i>Τήβεννος</i> (Tḗbennos). Furnée showed that the word is Pre-Greek in view of the variations "β/μ" and "ν/νν".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>τάγηνον</i> (tágēnon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandmother</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁dʰ-</i> (“grandparent”). Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dědъ</i> (“grandfather”), Lithuanian <i>dė̃dė</i>, <i>dė̃dis</i> (“uncle”), and Latvian <i>dȩ̀ds</i> (“old man”). Probably originally an onomatopoeia of babies' speech.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea squirt (tunicate of the class Ascidiacea)</li></ol><p>Kalén assumes earlier <i>&#42;θή-θυον</i> (&#42;thḗ-thuon) and connects the word with <i>θάω</i> (tháō, “to suck”) and <i>θύλακος</i> (thúlakos, “sack”). This explanation is nonsensical and typical of older Greek etymologies. According to Beekes, it is more probably a Pre-Greek word, given the variation <i>υ/ε</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;of metals) to melt, melt down</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to dissolve, cause to waste or pine away</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂-</i> (“to melt”). Cognates include Latin <i>tābeō</i>, Sanskrit <i>तोयम्</i> (toyam), Old Armenian <i>թանամ</i> (tʻanam, “to wet”) and Old English <i>þawian</i> (English <i>thaw</i>), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tajati</i> (Russian <i>та́ять</i> (tájatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of fenugreek</li></ol><p>From <i>τῆλις</i> (têlis, “fenugreek”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>σήμερον</i> (sḗmeron); today</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τήνδε</i> (tḗnde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hurrah</li></ol><p>Unknown. Said to be a word formed by Archilochus to imitate the twang of a guitar-string.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>want</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>τητάω</i> (tētáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why?</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>τῐ́ς</i> (tĭ́s, “what”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiger</li></ol><p>From an Indo-Iranian language. Some suppose a relation to Avestan <i>𐬝𐬌𐬔𐬭𐬌</i> (t̰igri, “arrow”), Old Persian <i>𐎫𐎥𐎼</i> (t-g-r, “pointed, sharp”); based on these comparanda, the word would ultimately derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teyg-</i> (“to point, stick”), according to Watkins. Beekes notes, however, that the word referring to the animal could very well be from some foreign substrate borrowed into Iranian, and converged in form to the Iranian words for "sharp, arrow" via folk-etymological adaptation.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τίγρις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Τῐ́γρης</i> (Tĭ́grēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why?, wherefore?</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>τὶ ἤ</i> (tì ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put, place, set</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in phrases</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(with <i>πόδα</i> (póda)) to plant the foot, i.e. walk, run</li><li>(with <i>ἐν χειρί, ἐν χερσίν</i> (en kheirí, en khersín)) to put something into someone's hands</li><li>(with <i>παῖδα</i> (paîda), <i>υἱὸν</i> (huiòn), etc. <i>ὑπὸ ζώνῃ</i> (hupò zṓnēi)) to have a child put under one's girdle, i.e. to conceive</li><li>(with <i>ἐν ὄμμασι</i> (en ómmasi)) to set before one's eyes</li><li>(with <i>ψῆφον</i> (psêphon)) to give one's vote or opinion, to vote</li><li>(with <i>ἐν στήθεσσι</i> (en stḗthessi), <i>ἐν φρεσί</i> (en phresí), etc.) to put or plant in one's heart</li><li>(with <i>τὰ ὅπλα</i> (tà hópla))</li><li>(with <i>τὰ γόνατα</i> (tà gónata)) to kneel</li></ol><li>to deposit</li><li>to pay</li><li>to put down in writing</li><li>to bury</li><li>to offer, set before</li><li>to assign, award</li><li>(often with <i>νόμον</i> (nómon)) to lay down, enact</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to agree upon</li><li>(<i>of a legal document</i>) to execute</li></ol><li>to establish, institute</li><li>to order, ordain, cause to happen</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in board games</i>) to place (pieces)</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to make, cause to be</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with attributive substantive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to cause to be one's</li></ol><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>)</li><li>to regard, consider as; to hold, reckon that</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to assume</li><li>to affirm</li></ol></ol><li>to make</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in periphrasis</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;t(e&#47;i)tʰēmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰé-dʰeh₁-ti</i>, reduplicated present from <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put, place”). The pay/offer senses are from conflation with <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi) in PIE, see Latin <i>-dō</i>.<reF></p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>faciō</i>, Sanskrit <i>दधाति</i> (dadhāti), Old Armenian <i>դնեմ</i> (dnem), Old English <i>dōn</i> (English <i>do</i>), and Albanian <i>dhatë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beget, give birth to</li><li>to bear, produce, generate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tíktō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;títḱeti</i>, an i-reduplicated present formed from the root <i>&#42;teḱ-</i> (“beget, bring forth”). Cognate with <i>τέκνον</i> (téknon, “child”), <i>τέκτων</i> (téktōn, “builder”), <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “craft”). Displaced the active forms of <i>&#42;ǵíǵn̥h₁eti</i>, causing it to degenerate into <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai), a deponent verb.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>flocks or motes floating in the air</li></ol><p>From the same root of <i>τίλλω</i> (tíllō, “to pluck”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pluck or pull out hair</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to have one's hair plucked</li><li>to pluck the harp strings</li><li>to pick so as to extract fibre</li><li>to tear one's hair in sorrow</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to pluck, vex, annoy</li></ol><p>Unknown. This verb cannot be an old primary formation. If not a loan, it must be a denominative from unattested <i>&#42;τίλος</i> (&#42;tílos). There are no cognates outside Greek. Frisk suggests a pre-form <i>&#42;πτίλλω</i> (&#42;ptíllō), which would belong to <i>πτίλον</i> (ptílon, “feather”), via dissimilation in the compounded form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything pulled or shredded, lint</li><li>anything that can be pulled or plucked</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) sprains</li></ol><p>From <i>τίλλω</i> (tíllō, “to pluck”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>esteeming, honoring, a holding valuable</li><li>(<i>of merchandise or property</i>) estimation, valuation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>rating or assessment</li><li>payment</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τῑμάω</i> (tīmáō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>honorable, worthy</li><li>costly, valuable</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay a price, that is, as a penalty; be punished with (whereas <i>τίω</i> (tíō) means to pay honor)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷinumi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷey-</i> (“to pay”). Cognate with <i>ποινή</i> (poinḗ), Sanskrit <i>चयते</i> (cáyate) and Avestan <i>𐬗𐬌𐬐𐬀𐬫𐬀𐬝</i> (cikayat̰).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τίπτε</i> (típte)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τίπτε</i> (típte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>what?</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>how?</li><li>why?</li></ol><p>Syncopated form of <i>τί ποτε</i> (tí pote)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(masculine,&#32;feminine) who?</li><li>(<i>neuter</i>) what?</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>what? which? what kind?</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷís</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>quis</i> and Hittite (kuiš). Compare its indefinite form <i>τις</i> (tis, “anyone, anything”).</p><p>In Attic, the labiovelar <i>&#42;kʷ-</i> becomes τ- before the front vowel ι and by analogy also before ου, ῳ in the genitive and dative singular, but π- before the rounded back vowel ο in most of the related interrogatives listed below.</p><p>Gen. sg. τοῦ &lt; τέο &lt; <i>&#42;kʷésyo</i> (then basis for dat. sg. τῷ and gen. pl. τέων) and the paradigm change to <i>n</i>-stem must have started with &#42;τίν &lt; <i>&#42;kʷím</i> elaborated with consonant stem ending, -ᾰ.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>payment</li><li>penalty, punishment, atonement, retribution, reward</li><li>vengeance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷítis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷéytis</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷey-</i> (“to pay, avenge”) (whence <i>τίνω</i> (tínō) and <i>τίω</i> (tíō)). By surface analysis, <i>τίω</i> (tíō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of white earth, probably gypsum</li><li>chalk, lime</li></ol><p>Like most expressions for "chalk", the word must be a loan. A different hypothesis is offered by Reichelt, who connects Sanskrit <i>श्वित्न</i> (śvitna, “whitish”) and <i>κίττανος</i> (kíttanos, “dusty chalk”), as well as the toponyms <i>Τιτάνη</i> (Titánē) and <i>Πιτάνη</i> (Pitánē). However, given the variation <i>ε/ι</i>, the word is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nurse, wet nurse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i> (“to suck”). Compare <i>τῐτθός</i> (tĭtthós, “breast”), <i>θῆλῠς</i> (thêlŭs, “female”), <i>θηλή</i> (thēlḗ, “teat”) and <i>τῐθήνη</i> (tĭthḗnē, “wet nurse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satyr</li><li>common shepherd's name</li><li>short-tailed ape</li><li>(<i>in Laconia</i>) bellwether</li><li>reed or pipe</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>διθύραμβος</i> (dithúrambos, “dithyramb”), which would contain a variant ot this word, with a suffix <i>-αμβος</i>. Previous proposals include connection with synonymous <i>σάτυρος</i> (sáturos, “satyr”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to swell”) by Solmsen. This is rejected by Nehring, who considers both words to be loans from Asia Minor. Deroy connects the suffix <i>-τυρος</i> with <i>ταῦρος</i> (taûros, “bull”). According to Beekes, the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>einkorn wheat</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Beekes, a "Pre-Greek assibilation," related to Latin <i>delpa</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τίπτε</i> (típte) (<i>used before /h/</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τίπτε</i> (típte) (<i>used before /h/</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of einkorn wheat</li></ol><p>From <i>τίφη</i> (típhē, “einkorn wheat”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autumn squill (<i>Prospero autumnale</i>)</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>ἴφυον</i> (íphuon, “spike lavender”) shows a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay honour to a person (whereas <i>τίνω</i> (tínō) means to pay a price)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷey-</i> (“to perceive, revere, value, honor”). Compare <i>τῑμᾰ́ω</i> (tīmắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Parthian word for drum</li></ol><p>From Parthian <i>𐫎𐫁𐫏𐫓</i> (ṯbyl /⁠tabil⁠/), from Iranian <i>&#42;tabil</i>. See also Armenian <i>տաւիղ</i> (tawiġ), Classical Syriac <i>ܛܒܠܐ</i> (ṭaḇlāʾ), Arabic طَبْل (ṭabl), Turkish <i>davul</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>line of battle, front, array</li><li>command, province</li><li>command, order</li><li>pension, alimony</li><li>ration</li><li>stipulated amount to be delivered</li><li>(<i>at Telmessus</i>) penalty, fine</li></ol><p>From <i>τᾰ́σσω</i> (tắssō, “to put in order, arrange”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀτταγᾶς</i> (attagâs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a utensil for turning things over in a frying pan</li></ol><p>From <i>τάγηνον</i> (tágēnon, “frying pan”) +&lrm; <i>στρόφιον</i> (stróphion, “band, headband”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commander, ruler, chief</li><li>federal commander of the Thessalian League</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) college of magistrates in Thessaly</li><li>president of a phratry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tāgós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂g-</i> (“to order, arrange, establish”), like <i>τάσσω</i> (tássō, “to arrange, put in order”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band, fillet, headband worn in sign of victory</li><li>breastband worn by young girls</li><li>ribbon, tape, band</li><li>strip in fur</li><li>pennon of a ship</li><li>strip or tongue of land</li><li>(<i>in joiner's work</i>) fillet, fascia</li><li>tapeworm, taenia</li><li>kind of long, thin fish</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ten-</i> (“stretch”), the same root underlying <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to stretch”), though the phonological and morphological details are rather unclear. The missing link may be a noun like <i>&#42;ταῖνα</i> (&#42;taîna) or <i>&#42;ταινά</i> (&#42;tainá). The formation of the word is comparable to that of <i>κειρία</i> (keiría, “band, tapeworm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) tactics</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for ordering or arranging, especially in war</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a tactician</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) tactics</li></ol><li>(<i>generally</i>) regulating</li><li>(<i>of numbers</i>) ordinal</li></ol><p>From <i>τάσσω</i> (tássō, “to arrange, to order”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for ordering or arranging, especially in war</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a tactician</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) tactics</li></ol><li>(<i>generally</i>) regulating</li><li>(<i>of numbers</i>) ordinal</li></ol><p>From <i>τάσσω</i> (tássō, “to arrange, to order”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suffering, distressed, miserable</li></ol><p><i>τάλας</i> (tálas, “suffering”) +&lrm; <i>πορεύω</i> (poreúō, “to go”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of hardship</li></ol><p>From <i>ταλαίπωρος</i> (talaípōros) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of Odysseus</i>) Steadfast of heart, stout-hearted</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἔτλην</i> (étlēn, “endure”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>toughly, stoutly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a shield of tough bull's hide (<i>epithet of Ares and others</i>)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἔτλην</i> (étlēn) + <i>ῥῑνός</i> (rhīnós, “leather shield”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(female) housekeeper</li></ol><p>Feminine of <i>τᾰμίᾱς</i> (tămíās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steward, dispenser</li></ol><p>A nominal formation from <i>τᾰμεῖν</i> (tămeîn, “to cut up, divide”), active infinitive aorist form of <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut, hew”), though the exact morphological details are unclear. May be analyzed as <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>τᾰμῐ́ᾱ</i> (tămĭ́ā, “housekeeper”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be treasurer or paymaster</li><li><i>(in Roman affairs)</i> to be quaestor</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to deal out, to dispense</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to manage</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to store up</li><li>(transitive,&#32;middle voice) to administer carefully and frugally, to use to the best advantage, to economise.</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;with genitive) to regulate</li></ol><p>From <i>ταμίᾱς</i> (tamíās) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasury</li><li>magazine, storehouse</li><li>chamber, closet</li></ol><p><i>ταμία</i> (tamía, “housekeeper, conductress”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon, “instrumental noun suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A copper, a cauldron</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>τανυήκης</i> (tanuḗkēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outstretched, long, tall, large</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ténh₂us</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>तनु</i> (tanú), Latin <i>tenuis</i>, and Old English <i>þynne</i> (English <i>thin</i>). Also see <i>τανύω</i> (tanúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) bringing long-lasting suffering (<i>epithet of death</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ταναός</i> (tanaós, “long, stretched”) + the root <i>ἀλεγ-</i> (aleg-) found in <i>ἀλεγεινός</i> (alegeinós, “causing pain”) (compare <i>ἄλγος</i> (álgos, “pain”)) + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a long point or edge</li><li>tapering</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>τανύω</i> (tanúō, “to strech”) +&lrm; <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “point”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs). The component <i>-ήκης</i> (-ḗkēs) been connected to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éḱos</i>, whence Latin <i>acus</i>, although this requires a secondary shift into the lengthened grade.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>long-tongued, chattering, loquacious</li></ol><p>From <i>τανύω</i> (tanúō, “to stretch, extend”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with flowing robe (epithet of goddesses or high-born ladies)</li></ol><p>From <i>τανύω</i> (tanúō, “to stretch, extend”) +&lrm; <i>πέπλος</i> (péplos, “robe, mantle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch; to strain; to extend</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be stretched or extended; to be tight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tn̥néwti</i> (“to stretch”), from <i>&#42;ten-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purpose</li></ol><p><i>τᾰ́ξῐς</i> (tắxĭs) +&lrm; <i>-είδῐον</i> (-eídĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>low, humble speech</li></ol><p>From <i>τᾰπεινός</i> (tăpeinós, “low, humble”) +&lrm; <i>-λογῐ́ᾱ</i> (-logĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>post-Classical</i>) humility, humbleness</li><li>meanspiritedness</li></ol><p>From <i>τᾰπεινόφρων</i> (tăpeinóphrōn, “humble; mean-spirited”) +&lrm; <i>-σύνη</i> (-súnē, “-ness”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lowering, discouraging</li></ol><p>From <i>ταπεινόω</i> (tapeinóō, “to abase”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>low-lying (<i>of place</i>)</li><li>humbled, abased in power, pride; submissive; small, poor, weak (<i>of persons</i>)</li><li>downcast, dejected (<i>of the mood</i>)</li><li>(<i>bad in a moral sense</i>): mean, base, abject</li><li>(<i>good in a moral sense</i>): lowly, humble</li><li>mean, low, poor (<i>of things</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>Has been connected with Old Norse <i>þefja</i> (“to stamp”) and <i>þóf</i> (“crowd”) but this theory is not convincing. Probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir, trouble, agitate</li><li>to trouble the mind, disturb</li><li>(<i>of an army</i>) to throw into disorder</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to rout or upset</li><li>to stir up, startle, excite</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰreh₂gʰ-</i> (“to confuse, agitate, disturb”). Rix suggests it could derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰr̥h₂gʰ-yé-ti</i>, itself from the root <i>&#42;dʰreh₂gʰ-</i>. Beekes suggests that it may have formed as a denominative from <i>τᾰρᾰχή</i> (tărăkhḗ).</p><p>Cognate with <i>τρᾱχῠ́ς</i> (trākhŭ́s) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dražiti</i> (“to annoy, irritate”). Doublet of <i>θρᾱ́σσω</i> (thrā́ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disorder, disturbance</li><li>confusion (mental)</li></ol><p>From <i>ταράσσω</i> (tarássō, “to disturb, stir, agitate”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>troublesome, turbulent</li><li>uncertain, baffling</li><li>troubled, disordered</li><li>confused</li></ol><p>From <i>ταραχή</i> (tarakhḗ, “disorder, confusion”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be afraid</li></ol><p>From <i>τάρβος</i> (tárbos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frightened</li></ol><p>From <i>τάρβος</i> (tárbos, “fright”) +&lrm; <i>-αλέος</i> (-aléos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to embalm</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>τᾰρχῡ́ω</i> (tărkhū́ō, “to bury solemnly”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saltfish market</li></ol><p>From <i>τᾰ́ρῑχος</i> (tắrīkhos, “saltfish”) +&lrm; <i>πωλέω</i> (pōléō, “I sell”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) quiver (container for arrows)</li></ol><p>From Persian ترکش (tarkaš) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A frame of wickerwork, crate, flat basket, for drying cheese on; (<i>generally</i>) a basket.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A mat of reeds, such as were built into brickwork to bind it together.</li><li>A mass of matted roots.</li></ol><li>(<i>of various broad flat surfaces</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) The flat of the foot (the part between the toes and the heel).</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>The palm of the hand.</li><li>The ankle.</li></ol><li>The rows of oars on the sides of ships.</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) The flat of the outstretched wing.</li><li>The row of teeth in a saw.</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) The edge of the eyelid and its lashes.</li></ol></ol><p>Uncertain. The term has been compared with Middle Armenian <i>թառ</i> (tʻaṙ, “bar for drying grapes”), Old High German <i>darra</i> (“apparatus for drying fruits”), Swedish <i>tarre</i> (“frame for drying malts”), suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ters-</i> (“to dry”). According to the linguist Lucien van Beek, it is possible that the term came to refer to dried materials, such as wickerwork. The NIL proposes a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tr̥s-ós</i>, though Beekes suggests that this etymology faces phonetic issues, arguing that <i>&#42;tr̥s-</i> would not yield Ancient Greek <i>ταρσ-</i> (tars-). According to Beekes, it may have passed through an intermediate language before reaching Greek from an unknown source. Lucien van Beek suggests that Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-r̥s-</i> may have regularly produced Ancient Greek <i>-αρ-</i> (-ar-), though he alternatively suggests that the term <i>ταρσός</i> (tarsós) could have restored the sequence <i>-αρ-</i> (-ar-).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ταρσός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tarsus, a city in Cilicia.</li></ol><p>Anatolian name, from Hittite <i>𒌷𒋻𒊭</i> (Tarša), possibly from <i>𒋫𒅈𒆪</i> (Tarku), a god. The name may have entered Greek through Akkadian <i>𒆳𒋻𒋛𒋛</i> (Tarsisi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having charge of Tartarus (<i>epithet of Selene and Hecate</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Τάρταρος</i> (Tártaros) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bury solemnly</li></ol><p>The verb has been compared with <i>τάριχος</i> (tárikhos, “mummy”), but this leads to formal and semantic problems. Furnée concludes that the verb is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>τητάω</i> (tētáō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teh₂y-</i> (“to steal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exerting tension</li></ol><p>From <i>τάσις</i> (tásis, “stretching, tension”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bull leaping - a Minoan sport in which athletes performed jumps over a bull, touching its back whilst passing.</li></ol><p>From <i>ταῦρος</i> (taûros, “bull”) and <i>κάθαψις</i> (káthapsis), a rare abstract noun composed of <i>κατα</i> (kata, “across”) and <i>ἅπτομαι</i> (háptomai, “touch, reach”); thus, literally, "touching of the bull".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tauromachy, bullfighting</li></ol><p>From <i>ταῦρος</i> (taûros, “bull”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bull-eating (<i>epithet of Dionysus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ταῦρος</i> (taûros, “bull”) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly</li></ol><p>From <i>τᾰχῠ́ς</i> (tăkhŭ́s) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liable to sudden death</li><li>killing speedily</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>τᾰχῠ́ς</i> (tăkhŭ́s, “fast”) +&lrm; <i>θᾰ́νᾰτος</i> (thắnătos, “death”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speed</li></ol><p>From <i>τᾰχύς</i> (tăkhús) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) having swift horses</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>ταχύς</i> (takhús, “swift”) +&lrm; <i>πῶλος</i> (pôlos, “foal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swift, quick, fast, rapid</li><li>In a short time: soon</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰn̥gʰ-ú-s</i>, zero-grade <i>u</i>-stem of <i>&#42;dʰengʰ-</i> (“to go quickly”), based on the comparison with Lithuanian <i>déngti</i> (“to run, hurry”) (see there for more potential cognates), though Fraenkel has disputed the connection.</p><p>Not related to Sanskrit <i>तकु</i> (taku), <i>तक्व</i> (takva, “quick, fast”), as Ancient Greek <i>χ</i> (kh) does not match Sanskrit <i>क्</i> (k), and the comparative <i>θάσσων</i> (thássōn) proves that the Greek <i>τ</i> (t) in <i>ταχύς</i> (takhús) is originally <i>θ</i> (th), from the aspirated PIE <i>&#42;dʰ</i> (see Grassmann's law).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here</li><li>in this point</li><li>in this way, thus</li></ol><p>Feminine dative singular of <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>this</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) here</li><li>in contrast with <i>ὅδε</i> (hóde): the preceding</li><li>in contrast with <i>ἐκεῖνος</i> (ekeînos): the latter</li><li>of someone or something famous or infamous</li></ol></ol><p>Synchronically analyzable as <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “self; third-person pronoun”) + <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “self; third-person pronoun”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peacock</li><li>a type of fish</li></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;τᾰϝώς</i> (&#42;tăwṓs), of Pre-Greek origin; possibly from an Oriental source, perhaps from Dravidian (compare Tamil <i>தோகை</i> (tōkai, “peacock”)) or a Turkic source (compare Turkish <i>tavuk</i> (“hen”)). Cognates include Latin <i>pāvus</i>, <i>pāvō</i>, and perhaps Hebrew <i>טַוָּס</i> (ṭawwā́s), Aramaic <i>טַוסָא</i> (ṭawsā), Arabic طَاوُوس (ṭāwūs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tautology</li></ol><p>From <i>ταὐτός</i> (tautós, “identical”) + <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “reasoning, account”) + <i>-ια</i> (-ia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>αὐτός</i> (autós) by crasis with <i>ὁ</i> (ho)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the same; identical</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>ταὐτό</i> (tautó, contraction of <i>τὸ αὐτό</i> (tò autó), neuter nominative singular), and equivalent contractions in other cases and genders. By surface analysis, <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”) +&lrm; <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “self”), as though <i>ὁ</i> (ho, masculine nominative singular) had not lost the initial τ- still present in most other forms: <i>&#42;&#42;τὸ</i> (&#42;&#42;tò) +&lrm; <i>αὐτός</i> (autós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tau, the name for the nineteenth letter Τ (T), τ (t) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤕</i> (t /⁠taw⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ταῦτα</i> (taûta) (<i>used before the rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bull</li><li>ox, chiefly as a sacrificial animal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;táuros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;táwros</i>; cognates include Latin <i>taurus</i>, Lithuanian <i>taũras</i>, Old English <i>stēor</i> (English <i>steer</i>), and Albanian <i>ter</i>. The family of Semitic words represented by Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ṯawr-</i> bear a striking resemblance: compare Aramaic <i>תּוֹר</i> (tor), Arabic ثَوْر (ṯawr). An etymological link is considered possible; either the PIE root was borrowed from the Semitic one, or the opposite, or both were borrowed from a third common source.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ταῦρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the Tauri people, a Crimean.</li><li>The Taurus Mountains.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ταῦτα</i> (taûta)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>and, also or untranslatable</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>after each item in a list</i>) and</li><li>(combined with <i>καί</i> (kaí),&#32;untranslatable)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷe</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-kʷe</i> (“and”). Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀤</i> (qe), Phrygian <i>κε</i> (ke), Sanskrit <i>च</i> (ca), and Latin <i>-que</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch, extend</li><li>to spread</li><li>to exert, push to the limit, strain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;téňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tén-ye-ti</i>, the innovative <i>ye</i>-present of <i>&#42;ten-</i> (“stretch”) (The present tense system includes a <b>*y</b> progressive aspect marker). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>तनोति</i> (tanóti, “to extend, spread”), Latin <i>tendō</i>, and Old English <i>þennan</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) stars or constellations</li></ol><p>Like <i>τέρας</i> (téras, “sign, marvel”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷer-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear out, tire</li><li>to distress, afflict</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;téřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“to rub”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall, fort, fortification</li></ol><p>From <i>τειχίζω</i> (teikhízō, “build a wall, fortify”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: kind of bird.</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>keeping a team of four horses</li></ol><p>From <i>τέθρῐππον</i> (téthrĭppon, “quadriga”) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to feed, nourish”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vain endeavor to evacuate the bowels; constipation</li></ol><p>From <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to stretch, pull tight”) +&lrm; <i>-εσμός</i> (-esmós, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build a wall</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to form a wall, wall up</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to wall or fortify</li></ol><p>From <i>τεῖχος</i> (teîkhos, “wall, fortification”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō, suffix used to form verbs from nouns, adjectives and other verbs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guard a wall</li></ol><p>From <i>τεῖχος</i> (teîkhos, “wall”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;φυλακέω</i> (&#42;phulakéō, “to guard, from <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “a guard”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “a verb forming suffix”)”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one that has the guard of the walls</li></ol><p>From <i>τεῖχος</i> (teîkhos, “wall”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proof (through argument)</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) demonstrative proof</li></ol><p>From <i>τεκμαίρομαι</i> (tekmaíromai, “to make a conjecture”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decree, appoint, ordain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷekmə́řřomai</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷékmər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷéḱ-mr̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prove positively</li></ol><p><i>τεκμήριον</i> (tekmḗrion, “proof”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, work, frame (<i>of a carpenter</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to devise, plan, contrive, esp. by craft</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joiners’ work, carpentry</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>practiced or skilled in building</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) good carpenter or builder</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) joiners’ work, carpentry</li></ol><p>From <i>τέκτων</i> (téktōn) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come around (<i>of a period of time</i>)</li><li>to turn out to be, prove to be</li><li>to come into being, be brought about, arise</li><li>to be</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to become</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring about, complete, fulfill</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to keep one's word</li></ol><li>to perform, accomplish, do</li><li>to pay (especially pay taxes)</li><li>to belong to a class</li><li>to consecrate, initiate</li></ol><p>From <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “end”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō, denominative verbal suffix). Synchronically from <i>τέλος</i> (télos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of sacrificial victims</i>) complete; perfect; entire; without blemish</li><li>(<i>of animals and humans</i>) full-grown; adult</li></ol><p>From <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “end”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “-ful”). Related to, and largely synonymous with, <i>τέλειος</i> (téleios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>broad strap or band for bearing or supporting anything</li><li>leathern strap or belt</li><li>linen bandage for wounds</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) telamon, colossal figure used as bearing-pillars</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;telh₂-</i> (“to bear”) + <i>-μων</i> (-mōn), as its original meaning was "bearer".</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τελαμών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Telamon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>τελέω</i> (teléō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>completeness, perfection</li></ol><p>From <i>τέλειος</i> (téleios) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of things, acts, works, time</i>) to make perfect, complete, accomplish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in logic</i>) to complete, make perfect the form or species</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of prophecies) to be fulfilled</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to bring to perfection or consummation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be made perfect</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to bring fruit to maturity, come to maturity</li></ol><p>From <i>τέλειος</i> (téleios, “finished”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complete the course</li></ol><p>From <i>τελεοδρόμος</i> (teleodrómos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>completing a work, working out its end, effective</li><li>creating perfection</li></ol><p>From <i>τέλεσις</i> (télesis, “fulfilment”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “work”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>completing, accomplishing, fulfilling, perfecting</li><li>accomplishing one's purpose</li></ol><p>From <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “end, goal”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “to carrying”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rite (esp. initiation in the mysteries)</li><li>(<i>plural</i>) mystic rites practiced at initiation</li><li>(<i>plural</i>) theological doctrines</li><li>a making magically potent</li><li>a festival accompanied by mystic rites</li><li>a priesthood or sacred office</li></ol><p>From <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “aim, purpose”) +&lrm; <i>-τή</i> (-tḗ), probably formed by analogy with <i>γενετή</i> (genetḗ, “the hour of birth”) from <i>γένος</i> (génos, “kin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accomplish</li><li>to finish, end</li><li>to die</li></ol><p>From <i>τελευτή</i> (teleutḗ, “end”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>τελευτάω</i> (teleutáō)</li></ol><p>From <i>τελευτή</i> (teleutḗ) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end, termination</li><li>tip, extremity</li></ol><p>From <i>τελέω</i> (teléō, “I complete”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>last</li></ol><p>From <i>τελευτή</i> (teleutḗ, “end”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pertaining to the supreme end</li><li>connected with final or intentional causality</li><li>in the end or termination of a word</li><li>perfect</li></ol><p>From <i>τέλος</i> (télos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a farmer or collector or the tolls, customs or taxes of a state, tax collector</li></ol><p>From <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “tax”) +&lrm; <i>ὠνέομαι</i> (ōnéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>custom house</li></ol><p>From <i>τελώνης</i> (telṓnēs) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of wasp that makes its nest in the earth</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek source of <i>ἀνθρηδών</i> (anthrēdṓn, “hornet”), <i>ἀνθρήνη</i> (anthrḗnē, “wasp”), <i>θρῶναξ</i> (thrônax, “drone”) and <i>πεμφρηδών</i> (pemphrēdṓn, “kind of wasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>terebinth (<i>Pistacia terebinthus</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown, related to <i>τέρμινθος</i> (términthos). The form was perhaps influenced by <i>ἐρέβινθος</i> (erébinthos, “chickpea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell stories of wonderous, marvelous things</li></ol><p>From <i>τερατολόγος</i> (teratológos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marvel-monger</li></ol><p>From <i>τέρας</i> (téras, “marvel, wonder”) +&lrm; <i>-λόγος</i> (-lógos, “-logist”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give birth to a monster</li></ol><p>From <i>τέρας</i> (téras) +&lrm; <i>τόκος</i> (tókos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work wonders</li></ol><p>From <i>τερατουργός</i> (teratourgós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō), the former from <i>τέρας</i> (téras) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the terebinth tree</li></ol><p>From <i>τέρμινθος</i> (términthos, “terebinth”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) delighting in thunder (<i>epithet of Zeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>τέρπω</i> (térpō, “delight”) +&lrm; <i>κεραυνός</i> (keraunós, “thunderbolt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant, delightful</li></ol><p>From the radical of <i>τέρπω</i> (térpō) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gladdening the heart of man</li></ol><p>From <i>τέρψῐς</i> (térpsĭs, “delight, pleasure”) +&lrm; <i>βροτός</i> (brotós, “human being”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>enjoying a dance, especially a choral dance</li></ol><p>From <i>τέρψις</i> (térpsis, “enjoyment, delight”) +&lrm; <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “dance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>forty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwr̥̄ḱomt</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;kʷetwr̥-dḱomt</i> (“four-ten”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>चत्वारिंशत्</i> (catvāriṃśát), Latin <i>quadrāgintā</i>, Old Armenian <i>քառասուն</i> (kʻaṙasun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fortieth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷetwərkontstós</i>, equivalent to <i>τεσσαράκοντα</i> (tessarákonta) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>fourteen</li></ol><p><i>τέσσᾰρες</i> (téssăres, “four”) +&lrm; <i>καί</i> (kaí, “and”) +&lrm; <i>δέκᾰ</i> (dékă, “ten”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Ionic spelling of <i>τεσσαράκοντα</i> (tessarákonta)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>τεσσᾰρεσκαίδεκᾰ</i> (tessăreskaídekă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to be sorrowful</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Stretched, rigid; straightened, smooth.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>synonym of <i>τετανόθριξ</i> (tetanóthrix)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “I stretch, I pull tight”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a guard of soldiers</li><li>a number of four, a quarternion</li></ol><p>From <i>τετρᾰ́ς</i> (tetrắs, “the number four, the fourth day of the month”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) having four faces, tetrahedral</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter,&#32;geometry) tetrahedron</li></ol><p>From <i>τετρα-</i> (tetra-, “four”) +&lrm; <i>ἕδρᾱ</i> (hédrā, “face of a solid”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cackle like the hazel grouse, on laying an egg</li><li>to observe a four-day cycle</li></ol><p>From <i>τέτρᾰξ</i> (tétrăx, “hazel grouse”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, suffix to form a verb).</p><p>From <i>τετρᾰ́ς</i> (tetrắs, “space of four days”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, suffix to form a verb).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>four times</li></ol><p>From <i>τετρα-</i> (tetra-) +&lrm; <i>-άκις</i> (-ákis).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τετράκις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tetracis, Paphlagonia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-wheeled</li></ol><p>From <i>τετρᾰ-</i> (tetră-, four) +&lrm; <i>κῠ́κλος</i> (kŭ́klos, “wheel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting of four metres</li></ol><p>From <i>τετρᾰ-</i> (tetră-, four) +&lrm; <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “metre”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-shaped, fourfold</li></ol><p>From <i>τετρᾰ-</i> (tetră-, four) +&lrm; <i>-μορφος</i> (-morphos, “form, shape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-footed, quadrupedal</li></ol><p><i>τετρᾰ-</i> (tetră-, “four”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tetrarch</li></ol><p>From <i>τετρα-</i> (tetra-) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the number four</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the fourth day of the month</li><li>the fourth day of the week</li><li>a period of four days</li><li>the four quarters of the moon</li></ol><li>tetrarchy</li><li>four-leaved pamphlet, quaternion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷetwóres</i> (whence also <i>τέσσαρες</i> (téssares, “four”)) + <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) grouse</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bore through, pierce, perforate</li></ol><p>From a reduplicated form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(philosophy,&#32;as an epithet of <i>ὕλη</i>, “matter”) of four kinds, fourfold (<i>viz. the four elements: earth, water, air, and fire</i>)</li></ol><p><i>τετρᾰ-</i> (tetră-) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a four-lettered word</li></ol><p>neuter gender of <i>τετραγράμματος</i> (tetragrámmatos, “having four letters”),</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>four-lettered</li></ol><p><i>τέτταρες</i> (téttares, “four”) +&lrm; <i>γράμμα</i> (grámma, “letter”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pythagorean name for&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the sum of the first four numbers, i.e. 10 (<i>= 1+2+3+4</i>)</li><li>The four terms (<i>6:8:9:12</i>) of the proportion corresponding to the chief musical intervals; also their sum +1 (<i>= 36</i>); the sum of the first 8 numbers. (6:12::1:2::diapason, 6:9::2:3::diapente, 6:8::3:4::diatessaron; 1:2, 2:3, 3:4 being the ratios of neighboring terms in the sum of the previous definition. 8:9::tonus as a bonus. A diatonic scale may be derived from this; semitonus can be derived to be 243:256 by fitting tonus as many times as possible into a diatessaron and finding out what ratio remains. Tonus times tonus times semitonus equals diatessaron: do–re–mi–fa (6:8). Fa to sol is tonus (8:9). Sol to do’ is another diatessaron: sol–la–si–do’ = tonus times tonus times semitonus = do–re–mi–fa transposed to sol (9:12). 384:432:486:512:576:648:729:786::do:re:mi:fa:sol:la:si:do’.)</li></ol><li>(education,&#32;historical) quadrivium.</li></ol><p><i>τετράς</i> (tetrás) +&lrm; <i>-τύς</i> (-tús)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>four hundred</li></ol><p><i>τετρα-</i> (tetra-) +&lrm; <i>-ακόσιοι</i> (-akósioi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) four-faced</li></ol><p><i>τετρᾰ-</i> (tetră-, “four”) +&lrm; <i>πρόσωπον</i> (prósōpon, “face”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tetrarchy</li></ol><p>From <i>τετράρχης</i> (tetrárkhēs) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of four syllables</li></ol><p>From <i>τετρᾰ-</i> (tetră-, “four”) +&lrm; <i>σῠλλᾰβή</i> (sŭllăbḗ, “syllable”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Doric spelling of <i>τεσσαράκοντα</i> (tessarákonta)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squid (<i>Loligo vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word could be Pre-Greek, in view of the variant and the meaning. Several unconvincing attempts have been made by Pokorny, who compares the word with Sanskrit <i>दोधति</i> (dodhati, “fierce, impetuous, turbulent”) and by Schindler, who assumes an original color term, like in the plant name <i>τεύθριον</i> (teúthrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be employed upon, engaged in, concerned with a thing</li><li>to be busy, bustling</li><li>to bid or order one repeatedly to do a thing</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ταὐτᾰ́ζω</i> (tautắzō), from <i>ταὐτός</i> (tautós, “the same”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to employ art, create utilizing art</li><li>to contrive or scheme cunningly; utilize subterfuge, deception</li></ol><p>From <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to create or make through art</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be created or made through art</li><li>to perform skillfully</li></ol><p>From <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē) +&lrm; <i>-άομαι</i> (-áomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artisan, craftsman</li><li>trickster</li></ol><p>From <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “craft”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artificial</li></ol><p>From <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “craft, skill, art”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artistic, skillful, workmanlike</li><li>made or done by art, artistic, systematic</li></ol><p>From <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>combining form of <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “skill”)</li></ol><p>From <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “skill”) +&lrm; <i>-ο-</i> (-o-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer of the art of rhetoric</li></ol><p>From <i>τεχνο-</i> (tekhno-), from <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “craft, skill, art”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to systematize something under the rules of art; determine something to be a rule of art</li></ol><p>From <i>τεχνολόγος</i> (tekhnológos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) systematic treatment of grammar</li></ol><p>From <i>τεχνολόγος</i> (tekhnológos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treating by rules of art</li><li>writer on the art of rhetoric</li></ol><p>From <i>τεχνο-</i> (tekhno-), from <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “art”) +&lrm; <i>-λόγος</i> (-lógos, “-logist”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craftswoman</li></ol><p>From <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē, “craft”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic, Ionic, and Doric form of <i>σός</i>&nbsp;sg (sós)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Doric form of <i>τις</i> (tis, “someone; something”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tewós</i>, a thematicization of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;téwe</i>, the genitive singular of <i>&#42;túh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felty germander (<i>Teucrium polium</i>)</li><li>madder (<i>Rubia tinctorum</i>)</li></ol><p>Maybe from an original color term, found also in <i>τευθίς</i> (teuthís, “squid”) and <i>τεύκριον</i> (teúkrion, “tree germander”) or from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree germander (<i>Teucrium fruticans</i>)</li></ol><p>Maybe from an original color term, found also in <i>τευθίς</i> (teuthís, “squid”) and <i>τεύθριον</i> (teúthrion, “felty germander”) or from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τεύχεα</i> (teúkhea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make, do, fashion, perform</li><li>to cause, prepare</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;téukʰō</i>, thematicized formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéwgʰti</i>, root present of <i>&#42;dʰewgʰ-</i>. Related to <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō), which is from a different present stem and with which it shares some forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wall, especially city wall</li><li>mound, earth works</li><li>fortified city</li><li>castle or fort</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;téikʰos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeyǵʰ-</i> (“to knead, form, fashion”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>देह</i> (deha), Avestan <i>𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬰𐬀</i> (daēza, “wall”), Latin <i>fingō</i>, and Old Armenian <i>դէզ</i> (dēz). Compare <i>τοῖχος</i> (toîkhos) and <i>παράδεισος</i> (parádeisos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beet (<i>Beta vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Frisk, the interchange "τ-/σ-" may represent an artificial Atticism or Ionicism. However, it may instead be a Pre-Greek variation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>probably</i>) tool, implement</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Epic,&#32;in the plural,&#32;with <b>ἀρήϊα</b> or <b>πολεμήϊα</b>) implements of war, armor, arms</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) complete armor, harness</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;naval) the gear of a ship (oars, etc.)</li></ol><li>(chiefly in tragic dialect,&#32;rarely in prose) any type of vessel</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>bathtub</li><li>cinerary urn</li><li>balloting urn</li><li>vase for libation</li><li>vase or ewer of water</li><li>cup</li><li>amphora</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) scent pot</li><li>matula</li><li>pot or jar</li><li>(with <b>ξύλινον</b>) chest</li><li>(with <b>ἀλφίτων</b>) meal barrel</li><li>beehive</li><li>capsule of a poppy</li></ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) vessels of the body</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(medicine,&#32;as if holding the intestines) the human frame, body</li><li>an egg</li></ol><li>a case of papyrus rolls</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>roll of writing material</li><li>volumen</li></ol><li>masonry</li><li>fabric</li></ol><p>From same root as <i>τεύχω</i> (teúkhō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewgʰ-</i> (“to be ready, be sufficient”). Compare Lithuanian <i>daũg</i> (“much, many”), Old Saxon <i>dōg</i> (“to be suitable”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;dužь</i> (“strength, power”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) idle, vain, undertaken to no purpose</li></ol><p>Unknown. If it originally meant "deceptive", it may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teh₂y-</i> (“to steal”), cognate with Sanskrit <i>तायु</i> (tāyu, “thief”), Avestan <i>𐬙𐬁𐬫𐬎</i> (tāyu, “thief”). If this theory is accepted, the term may be cognate with Hittite (tāyezzi), <small>[script needed]</small> (tāyazzi, “to steal”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tajiti</i> (“to hide, conceal”), etc. The suffix <i>-σῐος</i> may have been added directly to an unattested noun <i>&#42;τᾱΰς</i> (&#42;tāǘs) after the pattern of <i>δημόσιος</i> (dēmósios) from <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos). Compare also <i>τητάομαι</i> (tētáomai, “to lack, be in want, be robbed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) frying pan, skillet</li></ol><p>From <i>τάγηνον</i> (tágēnon), <i>τήγανον</i> (tḗganon, “frying pan”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false orpine (<i>Andrachne telephioides</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>board or table with a raised rim or edge</li><li>table or stage whereon cocks and quails where set to fight</li><li>chimney board, trapdoor</li><li>hoop of a sieve</li></ol><p>A technical expression in <i>-ία</i> (-ía), like <i>σχεδία</i> (skhedía), <i>κλισία</i> (klisía) and <i>ἑστία</i> (hestía). In the sense of "hoop of a sieve", this word and <i>σηλία</i> (sēlía) are often connected with <i>σήθω</i> (sḗthō, “to sieve”), but the derivation is unclear. In the meaning "table, board", the connection with Latin <i>tellus</i> (“earth”) and Sanskrit <i>तल</i> (tala, “bottom, floor”) is given by Pokorny.</p><p>Beekes claims that this cannot be upheld as Greek would have a lengthened grade formation, and further that for such a technical word, Pre-Greek origin is likely. Beekes further claims that the variation <i>τ/σ</i> is typical for Pre-Greek words, and that this word must originate there.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of such an age; so old, so young</li><li>so great</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tālíkos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;to-</i> (“demonstrative stem”) and maybe <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow”). Cognate with Latin <i>tālis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moon trefoil (<i>Medicago arborea</i>)</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>τήλινος</i> (tḗlinos, “of fenugreek”), from <i>τῆλις</i> (têlis, “fenugreek”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far-shining, far-beaming</li><li>(<i>of distant objects</i>) far-seen, conspicuous</li><li>far-seeing</li></ol><p>From <i>τῆλε</i> (têle, “afar”) +&lrm; <i>αὐγή</i> (augḗ, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>from a far country</li><li>far off, distant (<i>of places</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>τῆλε</i> (têle) +&lrm; <i>-δαπός</i> (-dapós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>farseeing</li></ol><p>From <i>τῆλε</i> (têle, “far”) +&lrm; <i>σκοπός</i> (skopós, “watcher, lookout”). Doublet of <i>τηλέσκοπος</i> (tēléskopos, “seen from afar, conspicuous”) with different accentuation and passive meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>τηλικόσδε</i> (tēlikósde, “of such an age; so great”)</li></ol><p>From <i>τηλίκος</i> (tēlíkos) +&lrm; <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of such an age; so old, so young</li><li>so great</li></ol><p>From <i>τηλίκος</i> (tēlíkos) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at a distance, far away</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>far away from</li></ol><p>Related to <i>τῆλε</i> (têle) and <i>τηλόθι</i> (tēlóthi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τηλόθι</i> (tēlóthi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from far away</li></ol><p><i>τῆλε</i> (têle) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at a distance, far away</li><li>(<i>rarely</i>) to far away</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>far away from</li></ol><p>Related to <i>τῆλε</i> (têle) and <i>τηλοῦ</i> (tēloû), with <i>-θι</i> (-thi, locative adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take care of, look after</li></ol><p>Without a certain explanation. The semantic and formal similarity with <i>μέλω</i> (mélō, “to care for”) gave rise to several unsuccesful attempts to connect this verb to etymologically. Pisani suggested that <i>τημέλεια</i> (tēméleia) arose from <i>&#42;τηλε-μέλεια</i> (&#42;tēle-méleia) by syllabic dissimilation and originally meaning "to care from afar"; however, this is semantically unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>τῆμος</i> (têmos)</li></ol><p>From <i>τῆμος</i> (têmos) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at that time, then (correlative with <i>ἡνίκα</i> (hēníka))</li><li>at that time of day</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at that time, then</li><li>at this time of day</li><li>so early</li></ol><p>From <i>τηνίκα</i> (tēníka) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at that time, then</li><li>under these circumstances, in this case</li></ol><p>From <i>τηνίκα</i> (tēníka) +&lrm; <i>αὐτός</i> (autós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to watch over, take care of, guard</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to take care that</li></ol><li>to have an eye upon, give heed to, watch, observe</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to watch for</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to watch, keep watch</li></ol><li>to observe or keep an engagement</li></ol><p>Traditionally analyzed as <i>τηρός</i> (tērós, “a guard”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix), though Beekes considers <i>τηρέω</i> (tēréō) as the original formation and <i>τηρός</i> (tērós) as a back-formation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warden, guard</li></ol><p>Apparently from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷeh₁y-</i> (“to perceive”), and compared with Sanskrit <i>चायति</i> (cāyati, “to observe, perceive”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čajati</i> (“to wait, expect”). However, this is phonetically unsatisfactory, as the Greek term shows no trace of the root-final <i>*-i-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be in want, to be deprived of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)teh₂y-</i> (“to steal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of this year</li></ol><p>From <i>τῆτες</i> (têtes, “this year”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνός</i> (-ĭnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tiara, the Persian headdress used on solemn occasions</li></ol><p>Oriental foreign word of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tripod, three-legged cauldron</li></ol><p>Furnée connects <i>θῖβις</i> (thîbis, “basket”), which he derives from a Semitic source. He remarks that the suffix <i>-ην</i> points to Anatolian origin, which may perhaps indicate that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nurse, wet nurse</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i> (“to suck”). Compare <i>θῆλυς</i> (thêlus, “female”), <i>θηλή</i> (thēlḗ, “teat”) and <i>τίτθη</i> (títthē, “wet nurse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>tame, domesticate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(figurative,&#32;of a disease) to become milder</li></ol><li>(<i>of trees</i>) to cultivate</li></ol><p>From <i>τιθασός</i> (tithasós) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>petty spurge, milkweed (<i>Euphorbia peplus</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably a reduplicated formation. As all words in <i>-αλλος</i> are Pre-Greek, the same holds for this one.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay honor to, to hold in honor, treat honorably, to honor, revere</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to hold in honor</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with genitive of price</i>) to estimate or value</li><li>(<i>rarely</i>) to award or give as an honor</li></ol><li>(<i>as an Attic law term</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(active voice,&#32;of the judge) to estimate the amount of punishment due to the criminals, award the penalty</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;of the parties before the court)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of the accuser</i>)</li><li>(<i>of the person accused</i>)</li><li>(<i>the accusative of the penalty or offense is added</i>)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honor”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honor, worship, esteem</li><li>high office, dignity</li><li>gift, offering</li><li>worth, value</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) price</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑ́ω</i> (tī́ō), equivalent to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷi-meh₂-</i>, from <i>&#42;kʷey-</i> (“value, honor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>estimative, determinative</li></ol><p>From <i>τιμάω</i> (timáō) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to avenge, exact vengeance</li><li>to succour, help one retaliate</li><li>to relieve, render medical aid</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμωρός</i> (tīmōrós, “avenging, avenger”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix), a contraction of <i>τῑμάορος</i> (tīmáoros), from <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honour”) +&lrm; <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō, “to see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vengeance, revenge, retribution</li><li>penalty, punishment, torture</li><li>help, aid, assistance</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμωρός</i> (tīmōrós, “avenging”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must be assisted</li><li>that must be punished</li></ol><p>From <i>τῑμωρέω</i> (tīmōréō) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>avenging, helping one to vengeance for a thing</li><li>succouring, aiding, assisting</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avenger</li><li>executioner</li><li>punisher</li><li>succourer</li></ol><p>The original meaning was "preserving or guarding honor", from <i>&#42;τῑμα-ϝορ-ος</i> (&#42;tīma-wor-os), a univerbation of <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “price, value, honor”) +&lrm; <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō, “to guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rudder, tiller, helm</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Venetan <i>timon</i>, from Late Latin <i>tīmō, tīmōnem</i>, variant of Latin <i>tēmō, tēmōnem</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τινά</i> (tiná)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boiling hot</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steam of a cauldron</li></ol><p>The word has been connected with <i>τιντόν</i> (tintón, “boiled”). If the gloss is reliable, then the variation <i>θ/τ</i> would point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(masculine,&#32;feminine) someone; anyone; a certain one</li><li>(<i>neuter</i>) something; anything; a certain thing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷís</i> (“who, what”). Cognates include Latin <i>quis</i> and Hittite <i>𒆪𒅖</i> (kuiš). Compare its interrogative form <i>τίς</i> (tís, “who?”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small chirping bird</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pudendum muliebre</li><li>full-sized bathtub</li></ol><p>Of onomatopoeic origin. Compare <i>τιττυβίζω</i> (tittubízō, “to cry (of the partridge)”), <i>τέττῑξ</i> (téttīx, “cicada”) and <i>τυτώ</i> (tutṓ, “owl”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>τείνω</i> (teínō)</li></ol><p>A reduplicated zero-grade form of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;téňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ten-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) breast, especially a woman's breast</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) animal's teat</li><li>nurser, rearer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeh₁(y)-</i> (“to suck”). Compare <i>τῐ́τθη</i> (tĭ́tthē, “wet nurse”), <i>θῆλῠς</i> (thêlŭs, “female”), <i>θηλή</i> (thēlḗ, “teat”) and <i>τῐθήνη</i> (tĭthḗnē, “wet nurse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to harm, hurt</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₃-</i> (“to hurt”), with inchoative suffix <i>-σκω</i> (-skō). However, Beekes is skeptical of the existence of such a root, based on the appurtenance of <i>τραῦμα</i> (traûma, “wound”) suggesting a variation between <i>αυ</i> (au) and <i>ω</i> (ō) which is difficult to explain in Indo-European terms, and prefers a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the common partridge</i>) to cry</li><li>(<i>of other birds</i>) to twitter, chirp</li></ol><p>An onomatopoeic word, like Sanskrit <i>तित्तिर</i> (tittira, “partridge”), <i>टिट्टिभ</i> (ṭiṭṭibha, “water-hen”), Lithuanian <i>titi̇̀lvis</i> (“sanderling”), <i>τῐτῐ́ζω</i> (tĭtĭ́zō, “to chip”) and <i>τῑτῐ́ς</i> (tītĭ́s, “small chirping bird”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of pipes or reeds</i>) of a shepherd</li></ol><p>From <i>τίτυρος</i> (títuros, “satyr; shepherd”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make ready, prepare</li><li>to aim at, shoot at</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰi-dʰugʰ-sketor</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>patient; steadfast; stouthearted</li><li>foolhardy; reckless</li><li>wretched; miserable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;telh₂-</i> (“to bear, endure”), the same root of <i>ἔτλην</i> (étlēn, “to suffer, undergo”), <i>τελαμών</i> (telamṓn, “broad strap or band”) and <i>τάλαντον</i> (tálanton, “measuring scale, talent”) + <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endurance</li><li>distress</li></ol><p>From <i>τλήμων</i> (tlḗmōn) +&lrm; <i>-σύνη</i> (-súnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patience</li><li>endurance</li></ol><p>From <i>τλάω</i> (tláō) + <i>πάθος</i> (páthos) + <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to cut, cut apart</li></ol><p>An alternative Epic form of <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something cut off</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>piece, section</li><li>segment of a line</li><li>sector of a circle</li></ol><li>cut, incision, wound</li><li>section of a book</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) division between two signs of the zodiac</li></ol><p>From the zero-grade of <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore</li><li>well then</li><li>furthermore</li></ol><p>From <i>τοι</i> (toi) +&lrm; <i>νῦν</i> (nûn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>let me tell you; you see; certainly (a discourse particle which assures the listener that what the speaker says is true, or that the listener knows it is true)</li></ol><p>A variant form of <i>σοι</i> (soi, “to you”), dative of <i>σύ</i> (sú, “thou”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in such wise, in such a manner</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(demonstrative,&#32;medial,&#32;of quality) of this sort or kind, such</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>such a proceeding</li><li>(<i>in prose narrative</i>) what goes before</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) such-like</li><li>(<i>referring to a previously mentioned adjective or adjectives</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τοῖος</i> (toîos, “of this sort”) +&lrm; <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos, “this”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>therefore, accordingly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τοιοῦδε</i> (toioûde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(demonstrative,&#32;medial,&#32;of quality) of this sort or kind, such</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>such a proceeding</li><li>(<i>in prose narrative</i>) what goes before</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) such-like</li><li>(<i>referring to a previously mentioned adjective or adjectives</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τοῖος</i> (toîos, “of this sort”) +&lrm; <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos, “this”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>such a one</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>housebreaker, burglar</li></ol><p>From <i>τοῖχος</i> (toîkhos, “wall”) +&lrm; <i>ὀρύσσω</i> (orússō, “to dig up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>proximal demonstrative adjective of quality</i>) of this sort or kind, such</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(sometimes&#32;antecedent of relative adjective,&#32;<i>τοιόσδε... οἷος...</i> (toiósde... hoîos...),&#32;<i>τοιόσδε... ὅς...</i> (toiósde... hós...))</li><li>(<i>without relative pronoun</i>)</li><li>(<i>implying a positive or negative adjective</i>) so great, so bad</li><li>(<i>indicating something immediately to come</i>) the following</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τοῖος</i> (toîos, “such”) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de, deictic suffix). Compare <i>ὅδε</i> (hóde, “this here”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) parent</li></ol><p>From the o-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teḱ-</i> (compare <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō) and <i>τέκνον</i> (téknon)) plus <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dare</li></ol><p>From <i>τόλμᾰ (τόλμη)</i> (tólmă (tólmē), “(<i>feminine</i>) courage”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>daring, bold</li><li>enduring, steadfast</li></ol><p>From <i>τολμάω</i> (tolmáō) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>τολμηρός</i> (tolmērós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bold, adventurous person</li></ol><p>From <i>τόλμα</i> (tólma) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clew, ball of wool ready for spinning</li><li>ball of anything</li><li>globular cake</li><li>colocynth (<i>Citrullus colocynthis</i>)</li></ol><p>Maurice connects <i>τυλυφάντης</i> (tuluphántēs, “cushion-cover weaver”), assuming influence of <i>κορύνη</i> (korúnē) or <i>τορύνη</i> (torúnē) to account for the vocalism. Furnée compares Luwian (taluppi, “clump of dough”). More recently, Melchert suggested a connection with Hittite (tarupp, “to gather, collect”), with the rare variant <small>[script needed]</small> (talupp). According to Beekes, the word must belong to the Pre-Greek substrate, which must have spoken in large parts of Anatolia as well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small volume, tract</li></ol><p>From <i>τόμος</i> (tómos, “tome”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intersection</li></ol><p>From <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cutting, sharp</li></ol><p>From <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal adjective and noun suffix). Not to be confused with <i>τόμος</i> (tómos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extendable</li><li>tonic, accentual</li><li>contractile</li></ol><p>From <i>τόνος</i> (tónos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) archer, bowman</li></ol><p><i>τόξον</i> (tóxon, “bow”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine suffix for person concerned with something)</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τοξεύς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name: Toxeus, the name of various characters in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>τοξεύς</i> (toxeús, “archer”); see <i>τόξον</i> (tóxon, “bow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τοξικόν φάρμακον (phármakon)) poison used on the tips of arrows</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>poison, toxin</li></ol></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the bow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τοξικὴ τέχνη (tékhnē)) bowmanship, archery</li><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τοξικὴ θυρίς (thurís)) a shothole, loophole, narrow window</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) skilled in the use of the bow</li><li>(in neuter,&#32;collectively) the bowmen</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τοξικὸν φάρμακον (phármakon)) poison for smearing arrows with</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τόξον</i> (tóxon, “bow”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the bow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τοξικὴ τέχνη (tékhnē)) bowmanship, archery</li><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τοξικὴ θυρίς (thurís)) a shothole, loophole, narrow window</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) skilled in the use of the bow</li><li>(in neuter,&#32;collectively) the bowmen</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> τοξικὸν φάρμακον (phármakon)) poison for smearing arrows with</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τόξον</i> (tóxon, “bow”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A bow case</li></ol><p>From <i>τόξον</i> (tóxon, “bow”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τοξότᾰ</i> (toxótă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τοξότᾰ</i> (toxótă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archer, bowman</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) Sagittarius, the Archer</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) city police of Athens</li></ol><p><i>τόξον</i> (tóxon, “bow”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine suffix for person concerned with something)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>τόπαζος</i> (tópazos)</li></ol><p>From <i>τόπαζος</i> (tópazos) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>governor of a district</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a independent or semi-independent ruler</li></ol><p>From <i>τόπος</i> (tópos, “place”) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler, leader”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>district ruled by a τοπάρχης</li></ol><p>From <i>τοπάρχης</i> (topárkhēs) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope, cord</li></ol><p>Maybe from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>στυππεῖον</i> (stuppeîon, “oakum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for place, in respect to place</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>local</li><li>(of medicine) to be applied locally, topical</li></ol><li>concerning topoi or commonplaces</li></ol><p>From <i>τόπος</i> (tópos, “a place”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>describing a place or country</li></ol><p>From <i>τόπος</i> (tópos, “place”) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “to describe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>description of a place or country</li></ol><p>From <i>τόπος</i> (tópos, “place”) + <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “Write, draw”) + <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bore, to pierce</li><li>to proclaim in shrill, piercing tones</li><li>to work, to shape</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the voice</i>) piercing, thrilling, shrill</li><li>(<i>of the ear</i>) acute, fine</li><li>(<i>of the eye</i>) piercing, penetrating</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) clear, plain, distinct</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) sharp, ready, smart</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;torh₁-ós</i>, from <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“to rub, turn; to drill, pierce”). Compare <i>τετραίνω</i> (tetraínō, “to bore”), Latin <i>terō</i> (“to rub”) and Old Armenian <i>թուր</i> (tʻur, “sword”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stirrer, ladle for stirring things while boiling</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>κορύνη</i> (korúnē), <i>σιβύνη</i> (sibúnē) and <i>χελύνη</i> (khelúnē). Semantically, one could connect Old English <i>þweran</i> (“to stir”) and <i>þwirel</i> (“stick for whipping milk”). Yet formally this etymology is implausible, since it would require a zero grade <i>&#42;τυρ-ύνη</i> (&#42;tur-únē). Derivation from the root of <i>τείρω</i> (teírō, “to oppress, distress, weaken”) is semantically difficult. Latin <i>trua</i> (“ladle”) has the same meaning, but the form cannot be reconciled with that of this word. In view of the suffix <i>-υν-</i>, the word could be Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τορύνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Toryne, Thesprotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so many times</li><li>so often</li></ol><p>From <i>τόσος</i> (tósos) +&lrm; <i>-άκις</i> (-ákis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(stronger variant of <i>τόσος</i> in all senses)</li><li>(<i>neuter substantive</i>) so much, thus much</li><li>(<i>neuter adverb</i>) so much, so far</li></ol><p>From <i>τόσος</i> (tósos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>τοσοῦτος</i> (tosoûtos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>proximal demonstrative adjective of quantity</i>) this much, this many; as much, many (as)</li><li>(neuter accusative singular,&#32;τοσόνδε,&#32;as adverb) as much (as); as long (as)</li></ol><p>From <i>τόσος</i> (tósos, “this much, this many”) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de, proximal demonstrative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at times, now and then</li></ol><p>Form of <i>τότε</i> (tóte) with altered accent.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) synonym of <i>τότε</i> (tóte, “then”)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) synonym of <i>οὕτως</i> (hoútōs, “thus, so”)</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) synonym of <i>τοσάκις</i> (tosákis, “so often”)</li></ol><p>From <i>τοῦτο</i> (toûto) +&lrm; <i>-άκις</i> (-ákis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τοὔνεκα</i> (toúneka)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for that reason, therefore</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>τοῦ ἕνεκα</i> (toû héneka)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τοῖᾰ</i> (toîă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(demonstrative determiner,&#32;chiefly&#32;poetic) of this sort, such</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(correlative,&#32;τοῖος... οἷος...) such... as...</li><li>(<i>referring to something mentioned earlier</i>)</li><li>(like <i>οἷος</i> (hoîos)) such as to do, able or capable to do (<i>with infinitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>emphasizing an adjective of same gender and case</i>) just, very, so</li></ol></ol><p>The base of the Proto-Indo-European demonstrative <i>&#42;tód</i> + the adjective suffix <i>&#42;-yós</i>, equivalent to <i>τό</i> (tó, “this”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τοῖσδε</i> (toîsde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>masculine/neuter dative plural of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun) and <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li><li>masculine/feminine/neuter dative plural of <i>τῐ́ς</i> (tĭ́s, interrogative pronoun)</li></ol></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>masculine/neuter dative plural of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun) and <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun) with movable nu</li><li>masculine/feminine/neuter dative plural of <i>τῐ́ς</i> (tĭ́s, interrogative pronoun) with movable nu</li></ol></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall (inside a building); partition</li><li>the side of a ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tóikʰos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰeyǵʰ-</i> (“to knead, form, fashion”). Compare <i>τεῖχος</i> (teîkhos), which it appears to be variant of.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τοῦδε</i> (toûde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τοῦτο</i> (toûto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) this</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) here</li><li>in contrast with <i>ὅδε</i> (hóde): the preceding</li><li>in contrast with <i>ἐκεῖνος</i> (ekeînos): the latter</li><li>of someone or something famous or infamous</li></ol></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barren she-goat</li><li>hermaphrodite</li></ol><p>From <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) dried fruits or sweetmeats, eaten as dessert</li></ol><p>From <i>τρώγω</i> (trṓgō, “to gnaw”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a he-goat</li></ol><p>From <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “he-goat”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stinking tutsan (<i>Hypericum hircinum</i>)</li><li>pimpernel (<i>Pimpinella tragium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “he-goat”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>billy-goat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>age when change of voice and other signs of puberty appears</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>voice change that takes place in puberty</li></ol><li>lewdness, lechery</li></ol><li>male sprat</li><li>spelt (<i>Triticum spelta</i>)</li><li>A rough kind of sponge.</li><li>(<i>in Messenia</i>) common fig <i>Ficus carica</i>)</li><li>wood horsetail (<i>Equisetum sylvaticum</i>)</li><li>stinking nard (<i>Valeriana saxatilis</i>)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) part of the ear; tragus</li><li>A kind of light Lycian ship.</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) A kind of comet.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) One of the constellations of the <i>δωδεκᾰ́ωρος</i> (dōdekắōros).</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τρᾰ́γω</i> (trắgō, “to gnaw, nibble”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, “nominal suffix”), literally “gnawer, eater (of sweets)”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>τρώγω</i> (trṓgō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long piece of dough pulled out in making pastry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>tractum</i> (“piece of dough”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of foreign ship</li></ol><p>Probably a loanword. Furnée thinks it is a variant of <i>τρᾰ́φηξ</i> (trắphēx, “beam; pole”). In that case, this could be a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table</li><li>(<i>banking</i>) money changer’s table, bank</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tr̥-ped-ih₂-</i> (“having three feet”), from <i>&#42;tr̥-</i> (“three”, combining form) + <i>&#42;pṓds</i> (“foot”). The first element is conventionally linked to <i>&#42;kʷtur-</i> (“four”, combining form) (which is explicitly favored by Beekes over the connection to "three"), but Sihler (1995, p. 411) points out that early tables had three legs, not four. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀵𐀟𐀼</i> (to-pe-za).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Ionic form of <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trep-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beam in framework of helepolis</li><li>baker's board</li><li>handle of an oar</li><li>gunwale on which the rowlocks are fixed</li></ol><p>The formation is like <i>πήληξ</i> (pḗlēx), <i>ὄρπηξ</i> (órpēx) and <i>νᾰ́ρθηξ</i> (nắrthēx). The word is usually connected to Latin <i>trabs</i> (“beam”).</p><p>According to Beekes, the suffix and the presence of variations show that the word is from Pre-Greek. Furnée also compares <i>τρᾰ́μπῐς</i> (trắmpĭs, “foreign ship”), in which case there is a prenasalized variant as well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰrépʰō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neck</li></ol><p>Unknown. A secondary formation from <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō, “to run”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰregʰ-</i> (“to run, drag”)) is phonetically attractive, but the interpretation of the neck as a "runner" is semantically strange and unseen in other Indo-European languages. Thus, tentatively suggested as a Pre-Greek substrate word by Beekes. Compare <i>θώραξ</i> (thṓrax).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jack mackerel (<i>Trachurus trachurus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>τραχύς</i> (trakhús, “rough”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰregʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) trachoma</li></ol><p>From <i>τρᾱχύς</i> (trākhús, “jagged, prickly, rough”) +&lrm; <i>-ωμα</i> (-ōma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tremble in fear; to be afraid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;trémō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trem-</i> (“to tremble”), extended form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ter-</i>. The extended form Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tres-</i> also yielded <i>τρέω</i> (tréō, “to dread, to terrify”).</p><p>Cognate with Latin <i>tremō</i>, Avestan <i>𐬙𐬭𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬯𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (trər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>sa<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti), Ancient Greek <i>τρέω</i> (tréō), Old Irish <i>tarrach</i>, Lithuanian <i>trišu</i>, Latvian <i>trisēt</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>трѧсѫ</i> (tręsǫ) and Sanskrit <i>त्रसति</i> (trasati).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>turn, divert, turn back, rout an enemy</li><li>to change someone's mind; (<i>middle voice</i>) to change one's own mind</li><li>to rotate something, change its orientation; (<i>middle voice</i>) turn around, rotate oneself</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to wander, not go in a straight line</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trep-</i> (“to turn”); cognate with Latin <i>trepit</i> (“turns”, verb), Sanskrit <i>त्रपते</i> (trápate, “feels ashamed, becomes timid or perplexed”), and Hittite <i>𒋼𒊑𒅁𒍣</i> (te-ri-ip-zi /⁠teripzi⁠/, “to plough”). <i>τραπέω</i> (trapéō, “to tread (out), press (grapes)”) and Latin <i>torqueō</i> (“to twist, wind”) are, according to Beekes, unrelated.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make to grow, to increase, bring up, breed, rear (especially of children)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of slaves, cattle, etc.</i>) to rear and keep, raise</li><li>to tend, cherish</li><li>(<i>of parts of the body</i>) to let grown, cherish, foster</li><li>(poetic,&#32;of earth and sea) to breed, produce, teem with</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to have within oneself, to contain, keep, have</li></ol><li>to maintain, support</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in historical writers</i>) to maintain an army</li><li>(<i>of land</i>) to feed, maintain one</li></ol><li>to bring up, rear, educate</li><li>to thicken, congeal, curdle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰrépʰō</i> with deaspiration of the /tʰ/ to /t/ by Grassmann's law everywhere except in forms where the /pʰ/ has been deaspirated to /p/ by a following consonant (future, first aorist, perfect middle). Further origin is disputed; compare <i>θρόμβος</i> (thrómbos, “lump, clot, curd of milk”).</p><p>According to Beekes, a Pre-Greek substratum word, with the original concrete meaning apparently being something along the lines of “to congeal or curdle milk”, whence “to make grow, promote growth”. Within Indo-European, it is traditionally compared to Lithuanian <i>dri̇̀bti</i> (“to fall down in flakes”) and <i>drė̃bti</i> (“to throw a thick fluid”), Latvian <i>drēbt</i> (“to sleet (of damp snow falling)”).</p><p>The Greek term additionally bears resemblance to expressions for “dregs” in Celtic (cf. Middle Irish <i>drab</i> (“dregs, yeast”)), Germanic (Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drabaz</i> (“dregs”)), and Slavic (Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;droba</i> (“crumb, sediment, dregs, yeast”)), linking it to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrebʰ-</i>, whence also Lithuanian <i>drebėti</i> (“to quiver, to tremble”). It is possible that all these terms derive from the same substrate continuum. Compare also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drupô</i> (“drop”), commonly connected to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrewb-</i> (“to crumble, grind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of people</i>) to run</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) to move quickly</li></ol><li>to run over</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to run a course, a heat</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰregʰ-</i> (“to run, drag”), suppleted in the future and aorist tenses with <i>&#42;drem-</i> (“to run”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee from fear</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(aorist participle,&#32;substantive) runaway, coward (compare <i>τρεσᾶς</i> (tresâs), <i>τρεσᾶ</i> (tresâ))</li></ol><li>to fear, dread, be afraid of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tréhō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tres-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>terreō</i>, Old Irish <i>tarrach</i>, and Sanskrit <i>त्रसति</i> (trasati). Compare <i>τρέμω</i> (trémō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of doves</i>) timorous, shy, pavid</li></ol><p>Based on the Doric forms <i>τρᾱρόν</i> (trārón), and <i>ταρόν</i> (tarón) with dissimilation, from an earlier unattested <i>&#42;τρασ-ρόν</i> (&#42;tras-rón), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trs-ró-</i>, a zero grade formation of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tres-</i> (“to tremble”), whence also <i>τρέω</i> (tréō, “to flee; to dread”).</p><p>Cognate with Avestan <i>𐬙𐬭𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬯𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (trər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>sa<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti), Latin <i>terreo</i>, Old Irish <i>tarrach</i>, Lithuanian <i>trišu</i>, Latvian <i>trisēt</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>трѧсти</i> (tręsti), Sanskrit <i>त्रसति</i> (trásati). Not related to <i>ὀτρηρός</i> (otrērós, “quick, nimble”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trident, the badge of Poseidon</li><li>three-pronged fork</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) kind of cautery</li></ol><p>From <i>τρεῖς</i> (treîs, “three”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină). Chantraine considers a folk-etymological transformation after the numeral. Furnée compares <i>τρίναξ</i> (trínax, “instrument used in agriculture”) and <i>θρῖναξ</i> (thrînax, “three-pronged fork”), and asks whether the word could be a transformation of a Pre-Greek loan for an agricultural tool.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil-flask</li><li>kind of measure of capacity</li></ol><p>From <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō, “to thresh; rub; wear out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of various prickly plants:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>water chestnut (<i>Trapa natans</i>)</li><li>caltrop (<i>Tribulus terrestris</i>)</li><li>Virgin's mantle (<i>Fagonia cretica</i>)</li><li>prickly parsnip (<i>Echinophora spinosa</i>)</li></ol><li>caltrop, a four-spiked implement thrown on the ground to lame the enemy's horses</li><li>larger contrivance for stopping boulders thrown down a slope</li><li>instrument hung from the walls of a fortress as a defence against battering rams</li><li>kind of incendiary missile</li><li>threshing sledge, a board with sharp stones fixed in the bottom</li><li>part of the bit of a bridle</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>trībulum</i> (“threshing sledge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a worn or beaten track, road, path</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a path of life</li></ol><li>a rubbing, attrition</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a hollow socket made by friction</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) practice, use</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>delay</li></ol><li>bodily exercise</li></ol><p>From <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō, “rub, grind”).</p><p>Compare typologically Russian <i>тропа́</i> (tropá) (akin to <i>трепа́ть</i> (trepátʹ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>prosody</i>) tribrach (metrical foot comprising three short syllables)</li></ol><p>A substantive in elliptical use for <i>τρῐ́βρᾰχῠς &#91;πούς&#93;</i> (trĭ́brăkhŭs &#91;poús&#93;, “[a metrical foot] consisting of three short syllables”) — written in full in an interpolation to the Τέχνη Ῥητορική of Dionysius of Halicarnassus — formed from <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>βρᾰχῠ́ς</i> (brăkhŭ́s, “short”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub</li><li>to grind; to thresh; to pound</li><li>to waste; to ravage</li><li>to rub away; to grind down; to wear away</li><li>to wear out</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“to rub”). Cognate with Latin <i>tero</i>.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red mullet (<i>Mullus barbatus</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from <i>τρῑ́ζω</i> (trī́zō, “to crackle, crepitate”), referring to the grunting sound from the friction of the gill cover bones when the fish is taken out of the water.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thrice-cloven</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) triglyph, a three-grooved tablet placed at equal distances along the frieze</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-, thrice) +&lrm; <i>γλῠ́φω</i> (glŭ́phō, “to carve, engrave”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triangle (geometric shape)</li><li>triangular oriental harp, spindle harp (musical instrument)</li><li>one of the Athenian lawcourts</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Triangulum</li><li>a type of lozenge</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive of <i>τρίγωνος</i> (trígōnos, “three-cornered”), from <i>τρεῖς</i> (treîs, “three”) + <i>γωνία</i> (gōnía, “corner, angle”)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-cornered, triangular</li></ol><p>From <i>τρι-</i> (tri-) +&lrm; <i>γωνίᾱ</i> (gōníā) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-cornered, triangular</li></ol><p>From <i>τρι-</i> (tri-) +&lrm; <i>γωνίᾱ</i> (gōníā) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for three years</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scream, cry</li><li>to crack (<i>of a joint</i>)</li><li>to gnash (<i>teeth</i>)</li><li>to twang (<i>the string of a musical instrument</i>)</li></ol><p>From the same imitative Indo-European root as Latin <i>strīdō</i> (“to screech, grate”) and Russian <i>стрекота́ть</i> (strekotátʹ, “to chirr, rattle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ὀρνιθάριον</i> (ornithárion, “small bird”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>three-threaded</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garment of drill or ticking</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ́ς</i> (trĭ́s, “thrice”) +&lrm; <i>μίτος</i> (mítos, “thread”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>three-pronged mattock</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓κή</i> (ăkḗ, “point”). However, Furnée compares <i>θρῖνᾰξ</i> (thrînăx, “three-pronged fork”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the variation θρι-&#47;τρι- and the typical substrate suffix -ᾰξ.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τρῐ́ποδᾰ</i> (trĭ́podă), <i>τρῐ́ποδε</i> (trĭ́pode), and <i>τρῐ́ποδῐ</i> (trĭ́podĭ)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τρῐ́ποδᾰ</i> (trĭ́podă), <i>τρῐ́ποδε</i> (trĭ́pode), and <i>τρῐ́ποδῐ</i> (trĭ́podĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>measuring three feet</li><li>walking on three feet</li><li>(<i>of furniture</i>) having three feet</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tripod, three-legged cauldron</li><li>three-legged table</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀴𐀪𐀡</i> (ti-ri-po).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>threefold</li></ol><p>From <i>τρι-</i> (tri-) +&lrm; <i>πτυχή</i> (ptukhḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) thrice, three times</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>frequently also used as a mere intensifier, without numerical signification</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;trís</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trís</i>; related to <i>τρεῖς</i> (treîs, “three”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>त्रिस्</i> (trís) and Latin <i>ter</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thrice blessed</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ́ς</i> (trĭ́s, “thrice”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́κᾰρ</i> (mắkăr, “blessed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>third</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tr̥tyós ~ &#42;tritós</i>, thus an inherited ordinal to <i>τρεῖς</i> (treîs, “three”), as if formed with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a triple vowel sound, triphthong</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having three voices</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ́ς</i> (trĭ́s, “thrice”) +&lrm; <i>φθόγγος</i> (phthóngos, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clover (<i>Trifolium fragiferum</i>)</li><li>pitch trefoil (<i>Bituminaria bituminosa</i>)</li><li>ornament shaped like a trefoil</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>τρῐ́φῠλλος</i> (trĭ́phŭllos, “three-leaved”), from <i>τρία</i> (tría, “three”) with <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in three parts</li></ol><p>From <i>τρίς</i> (trís, “thrice”), like <i>δίχα</i> (díkha). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of hair</li></ol><p>From <i>θρίξ</i> (thríx, “hair”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trabea, a white or purple toga</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>trabea</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tragacanth (<i>Astragalus tragacantha</i>)</li><li>gum obtained from this plant</li></ol><p>From <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>ἄκανθα</i> (ákantha, “thorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tragelaphus, a fictional animal, half goat, half stag</li><li>drinking-cup, which had such creature worked in relief</li><li>thingamabob, whatsaname</li><li>kind of wild goat or antelope</li></ol><p>From <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “billy goat”) +&lrm; <i>ἔλαφος</i> (élaphos, “deer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goat</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>edible</li><li>crunchy, cartilaginous</li></ol><p><i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “he-goat”) +&lrm; <i>-ανός</i> (-anós).</p><p>From the zero-grade aorist of <i>τρώγω</i> (trṓgō, “I chew, eat”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰνός</i> (-ănós, “-able”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or like a goat, goatish</li><li>of or for tragedy, tragic</li><li>tragic, stately, majestic</li><li>in tragic style, plaintive</li></ol><p>From <i>τράγος</i> (trágos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with armpits smelling like a billy goat</li></ol><p>From <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “billy goat”) +&lrm; <i>μασχάλη</i> (maskhálē, “armpit”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a goat's beard</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common salsify (<i>Tragopogon porrifolius</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>πώγων</i> (pṓgōn, “beard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act a tragedy, represent in tragedy</li><li>to tell in a tragic style, declaim</li></ol><p>From <i>τραγῳδός</i> (tragōidós, “tragic singer”), τραγῳδῐ́α (tragōidĭ́a, “tragedy”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tragedy or heroic play</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) any grave, serious poetry</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an exaggerated speech, made by a prosecutor</li><li>tragic fictions and terrors</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) pomp, display</li><li>a melancholy event, a tragedy</li><li>song</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τραγῳδός</i> (tragōidós, “singer and dancer in the tragic choir; tragic actor”), which is traditionally derived from <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “goat, buck”) + <i>ἀοιδός</i> (aoidós, “singer”), though the reasoning behind the formation is uncertain. One theory, mentioned by Beekes, is that goats were given as a prize to the winner in the oldest dramatic Agon competitions; another theory contends that the association with goats comes from the Dionysia festivals in Athens, which featured singing and dancing by characters dressed in goat skins representing satyrs, who were Dionysus's attendants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer of tragedies</li></ol><p>From <i>τρᾰγῳδῐ́α</i> (trăgōidĭ́a, “tragedy”) +&lrm; <i>-γρᾰ́φος</i> (-grắphos, “drawer, painter, writer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tragic poet and singer</li><li>tragedian, tragic actor</li><li>member of the tragic chorus</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) tragedy</li></ol><p>From <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “goat”) +&lrm; <i>ἀοιδός</i> (aoidós, “singer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) song</li></ol><p>From <i>τραγῳδῐ́ᾱ</i> (tragōidĭ́ā) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clear, distinct</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₂-</i> (“through, throughout, over”). Alternatively, it could have secondary long <i>-ᾱ-</i> for <i>*ē</i>, in which case it derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“to drill, pierce”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small table</li><li>table of a money-changer</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) trapezium</li></ol><p>From <i>τράπεζα</i> (trápeza, “table”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banker, money changer</li></ol><p>From <i>τράπεζα</i> (trápeza, “money changer’s table, bank”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a banker</li></ol><p>From <i>τρᾰπεζῑ́της</i> (trăpezī́tēs, “money-changer, banker”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bearing a table</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>table-bearer</li></ol><p>From <i>τράπεζα</i> (trápeza, “table”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hurdle, crate, whereon to dry figs</li><li>dried figs themselves</li><li>drying place for corn or for cheese</li><li>kiln, drying place for bricks</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The term could be construed as the regular outcome of earlier <i>&#42;tr̥s-iā́</i>, itself perhaps formed to the base <i>&#42;tr̥s-ós</i>, perhaps whence also Ancient Greek <i>ταρσός</i> (tarsós). This etymology would, however, require the forms <i>ταρσιή</i> (tarsiḗ) and <i>ταρσός</i> (tarsós) to have their unexpected vocalism restored, perhaps after the verb <i>τέρσομαι</i> (térsomai). However, the linguist Lucien van Beek considers this etymology problematic, arguing that the verb was rare, unproductive, and synchronically distinct from the etymologically related terms. Van Beek suggests that <i>τρασιά</i> (trasiá) was originally an epic term with an unusual phonological development designed to conform to the practicalities of poetic meter.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of children</i>) mispronouncing letters, stammering, lisping</li><li>(<i>of swallows</i>) twittering</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-λος</i> is also found in other expressions for physical and psychical defects, such as <i>τυφλός</i> (tuphlós, “blind”), <i>σιφλός</i> (siphlós, “maimed”) and <i>χωλός</i> (khōlós, “lame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injured person, typically a warrior</li></ol><p>From <i>τραῦμα</i> (traûma) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for wounds</li></ol><p><i>τραῦμᾰ</i> (traûmă, “wound”, stem: <i>τραυμᾰτ-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “of or pertaining to”, suffix forming adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>windpipe, trachea</li></ol><p>From <i>τρᾱχεῖᾰ</i> (trākheîă, “rough”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρτηρῐ́ᾱ</i> (ărtērĭ́ā, “windpipe, artery”).</p><p>Since <i>windpipe</i> (trachea) is the original meaning of ᾰ̓ρτηρῐ́ᾱ (ărtērĭ́ā), it can be assumed that the term τραχεῖα ἀρτηρία developed later as a result of semantic&nbsp;specialization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>jagged</li><li>prickly</li><li>rugged</li><li>rough</li><li>shaggy</li><li>(<i>of the voice of boys</i>) cracking</li><li>harsh</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a person</i>)</li><li>(<i>of sounds</i>)</li><li>(<i>of battle and conflict</i>)</li><li>(<i>of natural forces</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons, their acts, feelings, or conditions</i>) rough, harsh, savage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰrākʰús</i> (whence Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀲𐀨𐀐𐀹(𐀊)</i> (ta-ra-ke-wi(ja))), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰréh₂gʰ-us ~ &#42;dʰr̥h₂gʰ-éws</i> (“rough”), from <i>&#42;dʰreh₂gʰ-</i> (“to irritate”). Compare <i>θρᾱ́σσω</i> (thrā́ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roughness</li></ol><p>From <i>τρᾱχύς</i> (trākhús) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) dry chips, waste that falls from the manger</li></ol><p>According to Beekes from Pre-Greek. A derivation from <i>θραύω</i> (thraúō) is considered improbable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wound, hurt</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) hurt, damage</li><li>(<i>in war</i>) heavy blow, defeat</li><li>an indictment for wounding</li></ol><p><i>τιτρώσκω</i> (titrṓskō, “to wound”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, suffix forming neuter nouns).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirteen</li></ol><p>From <i>τρεῖς</i> (treîs, “three”) +&lrm; <i>καί</i> (kaí, “and”) +&lrm; <i>δέκα</i> (déka, “ten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tréyes</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tréyes</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>त्रि</i> (tri), Latin <i>trēs</i>, Old Armenian <i>երեք</i> (erekʻ) and Old English <i>þrēo</i> (English <i>three</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a vent to the intestinal canal</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῆμα</i> (trêma, “perforation, aperture, orifice”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “full of, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>τρᾱχύς</i> (trākhús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>tri- (three, thrice)</li></ol><p>Combining form of <i>τρεῖς</i> (treîs, “three”, cardinal numeral) and <i>τρῐ́ς</i> (trĭ́s, “thrice”, “three times”, adverbial numeral).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>wrestling</i>) to defeat</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to multiply by three</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐᾰ́ς</i> (trĭắs, “triad; the number three”): originally a wrestler had to throw his opponent three times in order to be considered the victor.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thirty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tridḱómt</i> (“thirty”). Cognates include Latin <i>trīgintā</i>, Sanskrit <i>त्रिंशत्</i> (triṃśát) and Old Armenian <i>երեսուն</i> (eresun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the number three</li><li>a group of three; triad</li><li>(<i>prosody</i>) system of three strophes</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τριάς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Trinity</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐᾰ́ς</i> (trĭắs, “triad”), from <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for three years</li></ol><p>From <i>τρι-</i> (tri-) +&lrm; <i>ἔτος</i> (étos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having three storeys</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trireme</li><li>drinking vessel shaped like a ship</li></ol><p><i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>ἐρέτης</i> (erétēs, “rower”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirty years old</li><li>lasting thirty years</li></ol><p><i>τριᾱ́κοντα</i> (triā́konta, “thirty”) +&lrm; <i>ἔτος</i> (étos, “year”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>τριᾱκονταέτης</i> (triākontaétēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thirtieth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;triākontstos</i>, equivalent to <i>τριάκοντα</i> (triákonta) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three hundred</li></ol><p>From <i>τρι-</i> (tri-) +&lrm; <i>-ακόσιοι</i> (-akósioi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) rose</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;literal) a thirty-leaved plant</li></ol><p>From <i>τριάντα</i> (triánta) + <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lesbian, woman who has sex with women</li></ol><p>From <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō, “to rub”) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rubbing down, wearing away, wasting</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wear and tear</li></ol><li>practice, as opposed to theory</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>mere practice, routine, as opposed to true art</li></ol><li>that about which one is busied, the object of care, anxiety, love (compare Latin <i>cura</i>)</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) a spending</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>delay, putting off</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō, “rub, grind”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rubbed, worn</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) experienced, crafty</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῑ́βω</i> (trī́bō, “to rub away, wear away”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the three stages in the life of moths and the like</i>) thrice-born</li><li>of threefold gender</li><li>of three kinds; threefold, triple</li></ol><p><i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dining room with three couches</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ́κλῑνος</i> (trĭ́klīnos, “dining room”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a group of three waves</li><li>a mighty wave or swell</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>κῦμᾰ</i> (kûmă, “wave”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trilogy, group of three tragedies performed together</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>triple crest</li><li>three-crested helmet</li></ol><p>From <i>τρίλοφος</i> (trílophos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of garments</i>) made of drill</li></ol><p>From <i>τρίμιτος</i> (trímitos, “drill”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thrice apish</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-, thrice) +&lrm; <i>πῐ́θηκος</i> (pĭ́thēkos, “ape”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea aster (<i>Tripolium pannonicum</i>)</li></ol><p>From of <i>τρι-</i> (tri-, three) +&lrm; <i>πόλιον</i> (pólion, “felty germander”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thrice unhappy</li></ol><p><i>τρῐ́ς</i> (trĭ́s, “thrice”) +&lrm; <i>ἄθλῐος</i> (áthlĭos, “unhappy”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thrice unlucky</li></ol><p>From <i>τρίς</i> (trís, “thrice”) +&lrm; <i>κακός</i> (kakós, “bad”) +&lrm; <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn, “fortune”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having three spirals, or three bent human legs.</li></ol><p>From <i>τρι-</i> (tri-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>σκέλος</i> (skélos, “leg”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>three thousand</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ́ς</i> (trĭ́s, “thrice”) + <i>χῑ́λῐοι</i> (khī́lĭoi, “thousand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on the third day</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) tertian</li><li>lasting three days</li><li>synonym of <i>τρίτος</i> (trítos, “third”)</li></ol><p>From <i>τρίτος</i> (trítos) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song thrush (<i>Turdus philomelos</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>θρίξ</i> (thríx, “hair”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maidenhair spleenwort (<i>Asplenium trichomanes</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>θρῐ́ξ</i> (thrĭ́x, “hair”) +&lrm; <i>μαίνομαι</i> (maínomai, “to be mad”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trident, leister, for spearing fish</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῐ-</i> (trĭ-, “three”) +&lrm; <i>ὀδούς</i> (odoús, “tooth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tremble, fear, quake, quiver</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to tremble before, fear, dread</li></ol></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>τρόμος</i> (trómos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō). However, it may also derive from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;troméyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;troméyeti</i>, a causative from <i>&#42;trem-</i> (“to tremble”) + <i>&#42;-éyeti</i>. The same causative form may be the source of Umbrian <i>tremitu</i>, from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;tromeō</i>. Both morphological paths lead to the same ultimate root, <i>&#42;trem-</i>, regardless.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a turning</li><li>turning away the enemy, a rout</li><li>solstice</li><li>trope</li></ol><p>From <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a turning or change</li><li>of or for defeat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>causing rout</li></ol><li>turning away, averting</li></ol><p>From <i>τροπή</i> (tropḗ, “turn”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to a turn or change</li><li>of or pertaining to the solstice</li><li>of or pertaining to a trope or figure</li></ol><p>From <i>τροπή</i> (tropḗ, “a turn, turning, solstice, trope”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nourishment, food</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that which provides or procures sustenance</li><li>a meal</li></ol><li>nurture, rearing, upbringing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>education</li><li>rearing or keeping of animals</li></ol><li>a place in which animals are reared</li></ol><p>From <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to maintain, support”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, action noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foster-father, guardian</li><li>breeder of animals</li><li>one who feeds or rears, that which feeds or rears</li></ol><p><i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “foster”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix for masculine person concerned)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>οἰκίσκος</i> (oikískos)</li></ol><p>From <i>τροφή</i> (trophḗ) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to food, nourishment</li><li>tending, nursing</li></ol><p>From <i>τροφή</i> (trophḗ, “food, nourishment”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nurse (person who takes care of other people's young)</li><li>wet nurse</li></ol><p>From <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to nourish”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run quickly</li></ol><p>From <i>τροχός</i> (trokhós) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>course, path</li></ol><p>Likely related to τροχός (trokhós, “wheel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wheel-drawn</li><li>dragged by or at the wheels</li><li>ploughed with wheels</li><li>formed on the potter's wheel</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) hurried along like a wheel or chariot</li></ol><p>From <i>τροχός</i> (trokhós, “a wheel”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō, “drive”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian plover (<i>Pluvianus aegyptius</i>)</li><li>Eurasian wren (<i>Troglodytes troglodytes</i>)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) hollow between the mouldings on the base of a column; scotia</li><li>sheave in block and tackle</li></ol><p>From <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō, “run, move quickly”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́λος</i> (-ĭ́los, “denoting a kind of bird”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) trochanter, i.e. either of two processes at the head of the thigh bone</li><li>part of the stern of a ship</li><li>instrument of torture</li></ol><p>From <i>τροχάνη</i> (trokhánē, “wheel, axle, gear”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr), from <i>τροχάζω</i> (trokházō, “to run quickly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running, tripping</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine,&#32;poetry) trochee</li></ol><p>From <i>τροχός</i> (trokhós) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>τροχῐλείᾱ</i> (trokhĭleíā)</li></ol><p>From <i>τροχῐ́λος</i> (trokhĭ́los, “sheave in block-and-tackle equipment”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>block and tackle, system of several pulleys, roof of a windlass</li></ol><p>From <i>τροχῐ́λος</i> (trokhĭ́los, “sheave in block and tackle equipment”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>round, circular</li><li>whorled</li></ol><p>From <i>τροχός</i> (trokhós, “wheel”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheel</li><li>hoop, ring</li><li>island</li><li>perimeter</li><li>running course</li><li>race</li><li>runner</li><li>badger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰrogʰós</i> (possibly an <i>-ός</i> (-ós) nominal), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰregʰ-</i> (“to run, drag, pull”), whence <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō, “to run”). Cognates include Old Irish <i>droch</i> (“wheel, circlet”) and Old Armenian <i>դուրգն</i> (durgn, “potter's wheel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with accusative of fruit or crop gathered</i>) to harvest, gather</li><li>(<i>with accusative of plant from fruit or crop is gathered</i>) to reap off</li></ol><p>From <i>τρύγη</i> (trúgē) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gypsywort (<i>Lycopus europaeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῡγών</i> (trūgṓn, “turtle dove”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turtle dove</li><li>stingray</li></ol><p>From <i>τρύζω</i> (trúzō, “to coo”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bore, pierce through</li><li>to force through</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trewH-p-</i>, a <i>p</i>-extension of a putative <i>&#42;trewH-</i> (“to rub”), and cognate with <i>τρύω</i> (trúō, “to wear down”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tryti</i> (“to rub”). A connection with Lithuanian <i>trupė́ti</i> (“to crumble”) is less likely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>balance, pair of scales</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῡ́ω</i> (trū́ō, “to wear down, exhaust”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, instrumental nominal suffix); it probably refers to the opening in which the tongue moves.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) helmet</li></ol><p>A derivative of <i>φάλος</i> (phálos, “crest of a helmet”). Hesychius says <i>τρυ-</i> (tru-) is for <i>τρι-</i> (tri-, “three”); Liddell &amp; Scott deny this but do not say what it does represent. Cunliffe says it represents <i>τετρα-</i> (tetra-, “four”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>delicate, dainty</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruits eaten at dessert, like figs, nuts</li></ol><p>From <i>τρώγω</i> (trṓgō, “to gnaw, eat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who crawls in holes or caves, such as a snake or fox.</li><li>A caveman</li><li>A wren (of the genus <i>Troglodytes</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>τρώγλη</i> (trṓglē, “hole”) +&lrm; <i>δύω</i> (dúō, “to get into”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locust, grasshopper</li></ol><p>Due to the ending <i>-αλλ-</i> (which he proposes to derive from a palatalized alveolar lateral approximant), Beekes concludes that this term is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trembling, tremor, shudder</li><li>fear, terror</li></ol><p>From <i>τρέμω</i> (trémō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trem-</i> (“to tremble”). Cognate with Tocharian B <i>tremi</i> (“anger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trophy, a monument to an enemy's defeat</li></ol><p>From <i>τροπαῖος</i> (tropaîos, “of defeat”), from <i>τροπή</i> (tropḗ, “rout, turning of an enemy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bundle, bunch</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>τροπή</i> (tropḗ, “turning”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keel of a ship</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) ship</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) core</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;trópis</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trep-</i> (“to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a turn, way, manner, style</li><li>a trope or figure of speech</li><li>a mode in music</li><li>a mode or mood in logic</li><li>the time and space on the battlefield when one side's belief turns from victory to defeat, the turning point of the battle</li></ol><p>From <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nourishing [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) the master of the house</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) mistress</li><li>nourished and reared up</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine&#32;or&#32;feminine) nursling, foster child, pupil</li></ol><p>From <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a courier, a messenger, a runner, a footman</li></ol><p>An <i>o</i>-grade derivative of the root of <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō, “I run”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰregʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cup, bowl</li><li>kind of measure used in medical prescriptions</li></ol><p>No etymology. Furnée compares <i>τρίβανον</i> (tríbanon) and considers the word to be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of bird, probably the common redshank (<i>Tringa totanus</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of dregs of wine</li></ol><p>From <i>τρύξ</i> (trúx, “lees, dregs of wine”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>straining cloth for wine</li></ol><p>From <i>τρύξ</i> (trúx, “must”) and a second member from a verb meaning "to sieve". Frisk connects <i>-οιπος</i> with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sipōną</i> (“to drip, trickle”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyb-</i> (“to pour, leak”), but this is connected by LIV with <i>εἴβω</i> (eíbō, “to let flow”). Alternatively, one may derived <i>-οιπος</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seykʷ-</i> (“to filter”), whence Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīhwaną</i> (“to filter”). Yet see <i>ἰκμάς</i> (ikmás, “moisture”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine not yet fermented, must</li><li>lees of wine, dregs, sediments</li><li>dross, waste of metal</li><li>faecal matter in the stomach</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) old man or woman</li></ol><p>Without certain etymology. The traditional connection with <i>τάργανον</i> (tárganon, “spoiled wine, wine-vinegar”) is neither phonetically nor semantically convincing. The formal identity with <i>τρυγάω</i> (trugáō, “to gather fruits”) is remarkable, especially since both words refer to viniculture and wine production. Porzig pleads for Pre-Greek origin of all these words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>borer, auger, rotated by a thong</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) trepan, a surgical instrument</li><li>fire drill</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>τρῡπάω</i> (trūpáō, “to bore”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is bored, a hole</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῡπάω</i> (trūpáō, “to bore”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is broken off, fragment, morsel</li></ol><p>From <i>θρύπτω</i> (thrúptō, “to break in pieces”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of rags, ragged</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῦχος</i> (trûkhos, “rag, tattered garment”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wear out, waste, consume, exhaust, impoverish</li></ol><p>Enlargement of <i>τρύω</i> (trúō), in the same manner as terms such as <i>σμήχω</i> (smḗkhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub down, wear out</li></ol><p>Disputed. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;truHyéti</i>, itself from a root <i>&#42;trewH-</i> (“to rub”), perhaps whence also Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tryti</i> (“idem”), Lithuanian <i>trūnė́ti</i> (“to spoil, putrefy, deacay”). The root is sometimes taken as an extension of the more traditionally-reconstructed Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“to rub, pierce”), though this appears uncertain. However, according to the linguist Miguel Villanueva Svensson, the term is attested to late and too sparsely to be inherited from PIE. Instead, according to Svensson, it is likely an inner-Greek formation, though still apparently from the PIE root mentioned above.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole, hollow</li></ol><p>From <i>τρώγω</i> (trṓgō, “to bite; to eat”), through the concept of an animal chewing a hole.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw; to nibble; to munch; to swallow</li><li>to eat vegetables, fruit or dessert</li><li>(<i>simply</i>) to eat</li></ol><p>Probably derived from the o-grade of a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;treh₂ǵ-</i> (“to nibble, munch”) and cognate with Old Armenian <i>արածեմ</i> (aracem, “to graze, pasture”), Tocharian B <i>tresk-</i> (“to chew”) (&lt; <i>&#42;trek-sk</i>). However, Beekes finds this derivation unconvincing, implicitly rejecting the existence of a laryngeal in the reconstructed root, and suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnawer, nibbler</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) greedy knave</li><li>kind of fish with sharp teeth</li></ol><p>From <i>τρώγω</i> (trṓgō, “to gnaw”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole, aperture, perforation</li></ol><p>From <i>τετραίνω</i> (tetraínō, “to perforate”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is rubbed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a practised knave</li><li>a drink or brew prepared of pounded groats and spices</li><li>a fragment, a scraping</li><li>an abrasion</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τρῑ́βω</i> (trī́bō, “to rub”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rubbing, friction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wear and tear, especially of garments</li></ol><li>resistance to wear, durability</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) potted meats</li><li>massage</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῑ́βω</i> (trī́bō, “to rub”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hole</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it is a back-formation from <i>τρυπάω</i> (trupáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>νόσος</i> (nósos, “sickness, disease”), <i>πόνος</i> (pónos, “hard work, toil”).</li></ol><p>From <i>τρῡ́ω</i> (trū́ō, “to wear out, distress”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worn out, tattered garment, rag</li></ol><p>From <i>τρύχω</i> (trúkhō, “to wear out, exhaust”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wounding, injury</li></ol><p>From <i>τῐτρώσκω</i> (tĭtrṓskō, “to harm, hurt”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, “a suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Expressing coincidence: to happen (to be)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of events</i>) to happen, occur [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) to happen [<i>with</i> participle <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><li>to succeed [<i>with</i> participle <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘at doing’</small>]; to go successfully</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hit a target [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to meet someone [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to get or attain something [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰunkʰanō</i>, from the nasal-infixed present <i>&#42;dʰu-n-gʰ-</i> of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewgʰ-</i> (“to hit, produce, yield”), whence also <i>τεύχω</i> (teúkhō, “produce”).</p><p>The present stem <i>τῠγχᾰ́νω</i> (tŭnkhắnō) is a zero-grade form of the root with nasal infix and nasal suffix, like <i>λᾰμβᾰ́νω</i> (lămbắnō, “to take”) and <i>λᾰνθᾰ́νω</i> (lănthắnō, “to do secretly”). The aorist stem <i>ἔτῠχον</i> (étŭkhon) is zero-grade with no suffix, and <i>ἐτῠ́χησᾰ</i> (etŭ́khēsă), and <i>τετῠ́χηκᾰ</i> (tetŭ́khēkă) are zero-grade with regular first-aorist and perfect suffixes. The future <i>τεύξομαι</i> (teúxomai) and perfect <i>τέτευχᾰ</i> (téteukhă) and <i>τέτευγμαι</i> (téteugmai) are <i>e</i>-grade. Related to <i>τεύχω</i> (teúkhō), which is from a different present stem and with which it shares some forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of threshing instrument</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́κος</i> (tŭ́kos, “mason's hammer”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, instrument noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work stones</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́κος</i> (tŭ́kos, “an instrument for stonemasons”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of thrush, perhaps the redwing (<i>Turdus iliacus</i>)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>τύλη</i> (túlē)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to twist or roll up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to unravel</li></ol><li>to bend</li></ol><p>A verbal formation from <i>τύλη</i> (túlē, “bulge”), with sense development "to make into a bulge" > "to twine into a ball, swathe up".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who plays the flute at a funeral</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́μβος</i> (tŭ́mbos, “tomb”) +&lrm; <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impressionable; conforming to a type</li></ol><p>From <i>τύπος</i> (túpos, “mark, impression, type”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a certified copy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be a monarch, absolute ruler (<i>optionally with the people or region ruled in the genitive case</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to govern, rule over as monarch</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́ρᾰννος</i> (tŭ́rănnos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>τυραννίς</i> (turannís)</li><li>tyrannous conduct</li></ol><p>From <i>τύραννος</i> (túrannos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>monarchy; sovereignty</li><li>tyranny</li><li>territory of a princeling</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) princess</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́ρᾰννος</i> (tŭ́rănnos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>τῠρᾰννέω</i> (tŭrănnéō)</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́ρᾰννος</i> (tŭ́rănnos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a despotic ruler; royal; princely</li><li>tyrannical; despotic; imperious</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́ρᾰννος</i> (tŭ́rănnos, “absolute ruler”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to murder, kill or slay a tyrant</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be killed, murdered or slain as a tyrant</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠρᾰννοκτόνος</i> (tŭrănnoktónos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slaying of a tyrant</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠρᾰννοκτόνος</i> (tŭrănnoktónos, “slayer of a tyrant”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tyrannicidal (of or pertaining to slaying, killing or murdering a tyrant)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slayer, killer or murderer of a tyrant; one who has committed tyrannicide</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́ρᾰννος</i> (tŭ́rănnos) +&lrm; <i>-κτόνος</i> (-któnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheese-making</li><li>cheese-press</li><li>cheese used as an offering</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) intrigue, roguery</li></ol><p>From <i>τῡρεύω</i> (tūreúō, “I make cheese”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of Persian plaster</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheese-crate, cheese-rack</li></ol><p>From <i>τῡρός</i> (tūrós, “cheese”) +&lrm; <i>κομέω</i> (koméō, “to take care of”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stand for cheese-racks</li></ol><p>From <i>τῡρός</i> (tūrós, “cheese”) +&lrm; <i>φορέω</i> (phoréō, “to bear”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheese grater</li></ol><p>From <i>τυρός</i> (turós, “cheese”) +&lrm; <i>κνήστις</i> (knḗstis, “grater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheese</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tūrós</i> (whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀶𐀫</i> (tu-ro /⁠tūrós⁠/)), probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tuh₂-ró-s</i>, from <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to swell”). However, compare Old English <i>ġe-þweor</i> (“curds”), from <i>&#42;(s)twerH-</i> (“to swirl, churn”). Possibly a formation in common with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;tvarogъ</i> (“quark, whey-cheese”) and Avestan <i>𐬙𐬏𐬌𐬭𐬌</i> (tū<sup><small>i</small></sup>ri), but the root and morphological details are disputed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a little, a bit</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) small, little, young</li></ol><p>A nursery word with hypocoristic gemination and aspiration. With a similar form and meaning, compare Swedish <i>tutta</i> (“little girl”). See also <i>τυννός</i> (tunnós, “small, inferior”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) small, little, young</li></ol><p>A nursery word with hypocoristic gemination and aspiration. With a similar form and meaning, compare Swedish <i>tutta</i> (“little girl”). See also <i>τυννός</i> (tunnós, “small, inferior”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of owl, possibly the barn owl</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic, rendering the cry of the owl. See also Latin <i>tutubō</i> (“to cry (of an owl)”) and Lithuanian <i>tutúoti</i> (“first flute, pipe”). Similarly in Greek <i>τοῦτις</i> (toûtis, “blackbird”) and <i>ταύτασος</i> (taútasos, “kind of bird”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of the limbs of the blind</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) of the other senses and the mind</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) blind, dark, unseen, dim, obscure</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of passages or apertures</i>) blind, closed, with no outlet</li></ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) blindly</li></ol><p>Adjectival formation in <i>-λός</i> (-lós) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewbʰ-</i> (“deep, hazy, foggy”). Cognates include Old Irish <i>dub</i> (“dark”), Old English <i>deaf</i> (English <i>deaf</i>), Albanian <i>dudum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to delude, <i>especially</i></li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to "puff up" with pride, arrogance, insolence, etc.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>τῦφος</i> (tûphos, “smoke”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō), literally “to fill with smoke”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>τυφώς</i> (tuphṓs, “typhoon”)</li><li>a kind of comet</li></ol><p>Appellative use of <i>Τῡφῶν</i> (Tūphôn), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“mist, smoke, haze”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant used for stuffing bolsters and beds</li><li>reedmace, cattail, bulrush (<i>Typha angustifolia</i>)</li><li>sort of tiara</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tū́pʰā</i>, often regarded as cognate with Latin <i>tuber</i> (“truffle”), English <i>tuft</i> and Old Norse <i>þúfa</i> (“mound”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to swell”). However the meaning of the Greek word is sufficiently different to render its connection with the other words purely hypothetical.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τυφῶν)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Typhon</li></ol><p>From <i>τῡ́φω</i> (tū́phō, “to raise smoke”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewh₂-</i> (“mist, smoke, haze”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accidental, fortuitous, by chance</li></ol><p><i>τῠ́χη</i> (tŭ́khē) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lucky; fortunate</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́χη</i> (tŭ́khē) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to mock, jeer at, flout</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be mocked, derided</li></ol><p>Unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scoff, jeer, taunt</li></ol><p>From <i>τωθᾰ́ζω</i> (tōthắzō, “to mock, jeer, flout”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of an unknown article</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τόδε</i> (tóde)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τόδε</i> (tóde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hither, to this spot</li><li>therefore, on this account</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(demonstrative pronoun,&#32;proximal) this</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) here</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with verbs of action</i>) here; (<i>possibly</i>) there, yonder</li><li>(<i>modifying a personal pronoun</i>)</li><li>(with <i>τίς</i>)</li><li>(<i>adds precision to adverbs of place and time</i>), just, very</li><li>(<i>in Attic dialogue, the masculine and feminine pronouns often refer to the speaker</i>)</li><li>(<i>in Aristotle, neuter designates some particular thing</i>)</li></ol><li>(of time,&#32;to indicate the immediate present)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>this present</li><li>these</li><li>(<i>elliptic with genitive</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>to indicate something before one</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>to indicate something immediately to come</i>) the following</li><li>(<i>followed by a relative pronoun</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>τῇδε</i>)</li><li>(accusative neuter,&#32;τόδε) hither, to this spot</li><li>(dative neuter plural,&#32;τοῖσδε, τοισίδε) in or with these words</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὁ</i> (ho) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τότε</i> (tóte) (<i>used before a rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) hence, thence</li><li>thereafter, thereupon</li></ol><p>From <i>τό</i> (tó, “that”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”). This is a suppletive from that diverged in meaning, while the regular medial source demonstrative reflex is <i>ἐντεῦθεν</i> (enteûthen).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) where</li></ol><p>From <i>τό</i> (tó, “the”) +&lrm; <i>-θῐ</i> (-thĭ, “locative suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>childbirth, parturition</li><li>offspring</li><li>interest (of money)</li><li>oppression</li></ol><p>From <i>τίκτω</i> (tíktō, “I beget”). Noun sense&nbsp;4 is a semantic loan from Hebrew <i>תּוֹךְ</i> (tóch)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courage, hardihood</li><li>recklessness, overboldness</li><li>bold or daring act</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;telh₂-</i> (“to bear”), like <i>τελαμών</i> (telamṓn, “strap, belt”) and <i>τάλαντον</i> (tálanton, “balance, scale”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slice, piece</li><li>piece of land</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) frustum</li><li>roll of papyrus, tome, volume</li></ol><p><i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Hypothetically inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tómos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tómh₁-o-s</i>, from <i>&#42;temh₁-</i> (“to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τόνδε</i> (tónde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) tension (as produced in something which is stretched)</li><li>that which undergoes or induces tension</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a cord, rope or string</li><li>a tendon</li></ol><li>the tone or note produced by plucking a string under tension</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tónos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tón-os</i>, from <i>&#42;ten-</i> (“stretch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrow</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow (weapon for shooting arrows)</li><li>rainbow</li><li>arc</li><li>anything which is bowed or arched</li></ol><p>Possibly a cognate of Latin <i>taxus</i> or borrowed from Iranian: compare Persian تخش (taxš, “crossbow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gem of green hue, probably chrysolite or peridot</li></ol><p>A loanword which, according to Pliny, was taken from the language of the Troglodytes, and named after an island of the same name in the Red Sea. However, according to Furnée, the existence of the gloss <i>ταβάσιος</i> (tabásios, “kind of stone”) proves a Pre-Greek origin of the word. Also compare Sanskrit <i>तपस्</i> (tapas, “fire, heat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field, landscape</li></ol><p><i>τόπος</i> (tópos, “place”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place, location</li><li>topic; (<i>rhetoric</i>) commonplace</li><li>position, office</li><li>opportunity, possibility</li></ol><p>Seemingly an inherited term, though the broad semantic range of the word makes ascertaining further origin and cognates difficult:</p><p>Others (though surprisingly, not Beekes) have suggested Pre-Greek origin; note similarities to Albanian <i>tokë</i> (“floor, earth”), with a potential proto-form <i>*tò-kʷV-</i> or <i>*tòw-kʷV-</i>.. For examples of a similar correspondence in sounds, compare <i>darkë</i> (“supper, feast”) vs. <i>δόρπον</i> (dórpon, “supper, dinner; evening”), <i>bajgë</i> (“dung”) vs. <i>βολβός</i> (bolbós, “bulb”), and so on. See also Hittite <i>𒋼𒂊𒃷</i> (tēkan), <small>[script needed]</small> (tagnās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vulture</li></ol><p>Without a convincing etymology. A connection with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sturkaz</i> (“stork”) seems gratuitous. Thompson compares Coptic <i>ⲧⲣⲉ</i> (tre, “kite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hartwort (<i>Tordylium officinale</i>)</li></ol><p>The origin is unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hole or socket in which a peg is stuck</li><li>a nave of a wheel</li><li>a tenon</li><li>a projecting peg or pivot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tórmos</i> (“borehole”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“to rub, bore, twist”). Cognate with Hittite (tarma-, “nail, peg”), as well as perhaps Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þarmaz</i> (“gut, intestine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A tool similar to a pair of compasses used to draw a circle</li><li>A lathe</li><li>That which is turned, circle, round</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tórnos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terh₁-</i> (“to rub, bore, twist”). Doublet of <i>τόρμος</i> (tórmos, “socket”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>so much, so very, so great</li><li>synonym of <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;totsos</i>, from <i>&#42;totyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;toti</i>, adverb from <i>&#42;só</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>तति</i> (táti), Latin <i>tot</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>τόσος</i> (tósos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τότε</i> (tóte) (<i>used before a smooth breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at that time, then</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>joined with other particles</i>)</li><li>(<i>with the article</i>)</li><li>(<i>εἰς τότε</i>) until then</li><li>(in apodoses, answering to <i>ὅτε</i>)</li><li>(used in late poets for <i>ὅτε</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tókʷe</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;to-</i> + <i>&#42;-kʷe</i>. Compare <i>ὅτε</i> (hóte), <i>πότε</i> (póte). Cognate with Latin <i>tum</i>.</p><p>See its indefinite form <i>τοτέ</i> (toté).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τόφρα</i> (tóphra)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up to that time</li><li>during that time, so long</li><li>meanwhile</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>σύ</i> (sú)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of Dorian salad, celery pickled in vinegar</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Neumann argues against a connection with the toponym <i>Σύβαρις</i> (Súbaris) and instead, hesitantly, proposes to connect the word with Luwian (tuwarsa, “vine”), whence also <i>θύρσος</i> (thúrsos, “thyrsus”). However, Furnée remains sceptical.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrument for stonework, mason's hammer or pick</li><li>battle axe, poleaxe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;túkos</i>, traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teuk-</i>, with cognates in Russian <i>ткать</i> (tkatʹ, “to weave”) and Latvian <i>tūkât</i> (“to knead, press”). Yet the consonant variation rather seems to demonstrate that the word is Pre-Greek. The influence of <i>τεύχω</i> (teúkhō, “to make”) is improbable, as its meaning is not specific enough.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swelling, callus</li><li>hump of a camel</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) blisters on the hands</li><li>pad for carrying burdens on</li><li>cushion, mattress</li></ol><p>Various cognate sets of wide semantic range have been proposed, due to the term's own broad semantics and short form:</p><p>None of these Indo-European words is a close match for this word, thus leading Beekes to conclude Pre-Greek origin as more, though not overwhelmingly more, likely. It has been suggested that the Balto-Slavic and Germanic terms stem from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to swell”), which fits the Greek meaning well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>τύλη</i> (túlē)</li><li>knot, callus (<i>especially inside the hands</i>)</li><li>knob, knot</li><li>knobbed bolt, treenail</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>τύλη</i> (túlē, “bulge”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τύλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tylos (old name for Bahrain)</li></ol><p>May be from Dilmun. May also be related to Hebrew <i>טָלֶה</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funeral mound; tomb; grave</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tum-</i> (“to swell”), which Beekes implicitly takes as a Mediterranean substrate root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a blow; a wound</li><li>a pick; a sting; a snakebite</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́πτω</i> (tŭ́ptō, “to beat, to strike”) and the suffix <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) drum, kettledrum</li><li>instrument of torture</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>drumstick, staff, cudgel</li></ol><li>wheel in a machine, cylinder or drum of a piston</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) sunken triangular space enclosed by the cornice of the pediment; square panel of a door</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́πτω</i> (tŭ́ptō, “to strike, beat, smite”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰνον</i> (-ănon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Doric form of <i>σύ</i> (sú)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A blow, pressing</li><li>The results of a blow: mark, impression</li><li>mark, figure, image, outline</li><li>General character of a thing: sort, type</li><li>text, content</li><li>pattern, example, model</li><li>summoning</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́πτω</i> (tŭ́ptō, “to poke, beat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally&#32;or&#32;figuratively</i>) to beat, strike, smite</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to beat, strike oneself</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to beat one's breast in grief: to mourn</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;túpťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)tewp-</i> (“to push, beat”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>तोपति</i> (tópati, “to hurt”), Latin <i>stupeō</i> and Old Church Slavonic <i>тъпати</i> (tŭpati).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absolute ruler (originally applied to the Lydian king Gyges)</li><li>(<i>with negative connotation</i>) tyrant; dictator; despot</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>kingly; royal; regal</li><li>imperious; despotic</li></ol><p>Perhaps a borrowing of a Lydian word for “king” or a similar word in another language of Asia Minor.</p><p>Compare Hittite (šarawanaš), <small>[script needed]</small> (tarawanaš), Philistine <i>𐤈𐤓𐤍</i> (ṭrn, “lord, ruler”), <i>Τυρρηνός</i> (Turrhēnós, “Etruscan”), <i>τύρσις</i> (túrsis, “tower”) and Etruscan <i>𐌕𐌖𐌓𐌏𐌍</i> (turon, “mistress, lady”), a surname of Venus, which is probably related.</p><p>Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disorder, confusion, tumult</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) poetic rout, revelry</li></ol><p>Disputed. Though traditionally connected to the Germanic terms <i>&#42;staurijan</i> (“to disturb, harm”), <i>&#42;sturiz</i> (“turmoil, confusion, noise”), <i>&#42;sturmaz</i> (“storm”) and <i>&#42;sturjan</i> (“to be/put in motion, stir”) via a hypothetical Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;(s)twerH-</i>, according to Beekes the variation within Greek in fact points to a Pre-Greek origin. A formal connection with Latin <i>turba</i> (“confusion; troop”) and <i>turma</i> (“troop, squadron”) is also difficult; the former might be a loanword from Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Tyrian purple</li></ol><p>From <i>Τύρος</i> (Túros, “Tyrus”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turma, troop, squadron of cavalry</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>turma</i> (“troop, squadron”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>τύρσις</i> (túrsis, “tower”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tower</li></ol><p>Likely a Mediterranean substrate loan, perhaps from Illyrian. Beekes also compares Tursha (a name for the Sea People, see Tiras), Tyrsenians, Tiryns, and marks the ultimate origin as non-Indo-European.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant used for stuffing bolsters and beds</li><li>reedmace, cattail, bulrush (<i>Typha angustifolia</i>)</li><li>sort of tiara</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tū́pʰā</i>, often regarded as cognate with Latin <i>tuber</i> (“truffle”), English <i>tuft</i> and Old Norse <i>þúfa</i> (“mound”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tewh₂-</i> (“to swell”). However the meaning of the Greek word is sufficiently different to render its connection with the other words purely hypothetical.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smoke, fill with smoke</li><li>to consume in smoke</li></ol><p>From a <i>*bʰ</i>-extension of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewH-</i> (“mist, smoke, haze”). Compare also <i>τῦφος</i> (tûphos, “fever”), <i>τυφόω</i> (tuphóō, “delude”), <i>Τυφῶν</i> (Tuphôn, “Typhon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>τῠ́χη</i> (tŭ́khē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of a god</li><li>the act of a human being</li><li>(<i>regarded as an agent or cause beyond human control</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fortune, providence, fate</li><li>chance</li></ol><li>(<i>regarded as a result</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>good fortune, success</li><li>ill fortune, misfortune</li><li>(<i>in a neutral sense, in plural</i>) fortunes</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰúkʰā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰewgʰ-</i> (“to produce, yield”). Equivalent to <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō, “to happen”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, action noun suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Τύχη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Tyche; Fortuna; Lady Luck</li></ol><p>From <i>τῠ́χη</i> (tŭ́khē, “fortune”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) some kind of small bird</li></ol><p>Unknown, but possibly onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>τῷ</i> (tōî, “then; thereupon”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) so, this way</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>τὰ</i> (tà) +&lrm; <i>ἀδικήματα</i> (adikḗmata)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>what happens there</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>τὰ</i> (tà) +&lrm; <i>ἐκεῖ</i> (ekeî), literally “the things there”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>τὰ</i> (tà) +&lrm; <i>ἀναγκαῖα</i> (anankaîa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>τὸ</i> (tò) +&lrm; <i>ἀργύριον</i> (argúrion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marriageable maiden</li></ol><p>Perhaps an Aeolic form of <i>τῆλις</i> (têlis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) friendly term of address</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) here, there (when offering something to someone)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;téh₁</i>. Cognate with Gothic <i>𐌸𐌴</i> (þē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τῆλε</i> (têle)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>far off, afar, far away</li></ol><p>From a locative form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷel-</i> (“to turn, revolve”). Compare <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “end”) and <i>πάλαι</i> (pálai, “long ago”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fenugreek (<i>Trigonella foenum-graecum</i>)</li></ol><p>There is no certain explanation. Traditionally compared to Latin <i>tālea</i> (“stave; cutting”) and Sanskrit <i>ताल</i> (tāla, “palm tree”) but such guesses have been abandoned recently. Possibly connected to Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;talā</i> (“cornstalk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>then, thereupon</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;teh₂-mo-</i>, from the root of <i>&#42;só</i>. Compare Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a melting</li><li>a wasting, loss of flesh</li><li>a solution</li></ol><p>From <i>τήκω</i> (tḗkō, “to melt”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>τέως</i> (téōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here; there</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thin stool, as in diarrhea</li></ol><p>No etymology. The comparisons with Armenian <i>թրիք</i> (tʻrikʻ, “dung, manure”), if from <i>&#42;tiHr-</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;þainaz</i> (“moist”) and Russian <i>тина</i> (tina, “scum, slime”) remain speculative, since there is no evidence of a root <i>&#42;tiH-</i> or <i>&#42;tH-i-</i> in this meaning. The same holds for the connection with <i>τῖφος</i> (tîphos, “pond, marsh”). The verb <i>τήκω</i> (tḗkō, “to melt”) is not related, either.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smoke, vapour</li><li>delusion, vanity</li><li>nonsense</li><li>a kind of fever</li></ol><p>Derived from the verb <i>τύφω</i> (túphō, “to smoke”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>τῷ</i> (tōî, “then; thereupon”)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tóyyo</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tósyo</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τῶνδε</i> (tônde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>then, thereupon</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so much the worse</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>τῷδε</i> (tōîde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) The unaspirated form of the letter υ (upsilon) in the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>From <i>υ</i> (u) with <i>ψιλόν</i> (psilón) meaning "plain" or "bare," indicating the common unaspirated variant of upsilon.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypercorrect form of <i>υἱύς</i> (huiús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grandson</li></ol><p>From <i>υἱός</i> (huiós, “son”), with the same formation of <i>οἰωνός</i> (oiōnós, “bird of prey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>son</li><li>child, not necessarily male</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Son; the Son of God (one of the three Persons of the Trinity, believed to have become incarnated in Jesus Christ)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>υἱύς</i> (huiús); see there for more.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Υἱός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Son; the Son of God (one of the three Persons of the Trinity, believed to have become incarnated in Jesus Christ)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>υἱύς</i> (huiús), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suHyús</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>υἱός</i> (huiós, “son”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hūyús</i>. Cognate with Old Armenian <i>ուստր</i> (ustr), Tocharian A <i>se</i>, Tocharian B <i>soy</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glutton</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾰγ-</i> (phăg-, stem of <i>ἔφᾰγον</i> (éphăgon, “to have eaten”)) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red porgy (<i>Pagrus pagrus</i>)</li><li>Cretan word for whetstone</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the presence of the variants leads to a Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>Might be formally and semantically identical to Old Armenian <i>բարկ</i> (bark, “bitter, sharp of taste”), so from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂g-ro-</i> (“sharpening”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φάτο</i> (pháto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bundle, faggot</li></ol><p>The term has been connected to Latin <i>fascis</i>, though this comparison is rejected by De Vaan. Beekes points out a variant <i>φάκελλος</i> (phákellos) in Aristotle, and says that this proves that this word is Pre-Greek. It has also been suggested that the word is related to Albanian <i>bashkë</i> (“together”). The suffix is reminiscent of <i>πύελος</i> (púelos) and <i>σκόπελος</i> (skópelos). Furnée further compares <i>φάσκωλος</i> (pháskōlos, “leathern bag”) and <i>βάσκιοι</i> (báskioi). Solmsen hesitatingly compared <i>σφάκελος</i> (sphákelos, “spasm, gangrene”), suggesting a common meaning "drawn together, tied together."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of lentils</li></ol><p>From <i>φακός</i> (phakós, “lentil”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>line of battle, battle-array</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ranks of an army</li><li>phalanx, a clustered mass of infantry</li><li>main body, center (as opposed to the periphery)</li><li>round piece of wood, trunk, log</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) rollers for moving heavy loads</li><li>beam of a balance or steelyard</li><li>bone between two joints of a finger or toe</li><li>row of eyelashes</li></ol><p>Traditionally from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰelǵ-</i> (“beam, plank”), with cognates such as Latin <i>sufflāmen</i> and Old High German <i>balcho</i>, but regular derivation of <b>φᾰ́λᾰγγ-</b> from this root is phonetically impossible, and the suffix is prototypical of Pre-Greek. Possibly related to <i>φάλκης</i> (phálkēs) via the same substrate source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>φάλλαινα</i> (phállaina, “moth”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in plural</i>) boss or disc of metal</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cheekpieces of a horse's headstall</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) bandages for the cheek</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) ornaments, decorations</li></ol><p>From <i>φάλος</i> (phálos, “crest of a helmet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a patch of white or a white spot</li></ol><p>From <i>φαλός</i> (phalós, “white”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part of a ship, maybe the rib</li></ol><p>The word has been connected with <i>φάλαγξ</i> (phálanx, “trunk; beam”), Latin <i>falx</i> (“scythe”) and <i>φάλκη</i> (phálkē). According to Beekes, the word may well be from Pre-Greek. For the latter possibility, compare also <i>ἄφλᾰστον</i> (áphlăston).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whale</li><li>moth</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived (e.g. by Pokorny) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“to swell”); see also <i>φαλλός</i> (phallós, “penis, phallus”), for the shape of which the animal may be named. However, Beekes considers the word Pre-Greek due to variation between the single and geminate λ in its variant forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crest of a helmet</li></ol><p>Contested. Furnée adduces <i>βαλόν</i> (balón, “sky”); if the identification is correct, the word may be of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>φαντασίᾱ</i> (phantasíā)</li></ol><p>From <i>φαντάζω</i> (phantázō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phantom, apparition, ghost</li><li>vision, dream</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) phenomena</li><li>fantasy</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾰντᾰ́ζω</i> (phăntắzō, “make visible”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light, especially daylight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the light of a torch, fire, a light</li><li>of the light or time of day</li></ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the life of men</li><li>a day</li><li>the light of the eyes</li><li>a window</li><li>(<i>figurative&#32;or&#32;poetic</i>) delight, deliverance, happiness, victory, glory, etc.</li><li>the dark ring around the nipple, areola</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰáwos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéh₂os</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“shine”).</p><p>Compare <i>φαεινός</i> (phaeinós), <i>φάω</i> (pháō), and <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō). Cognates include Latin <i>iubar</i> (“radiance, light”); Sanskrit <i>भास्</i> (bhās, “light, brilliance”) and <i>भास</i> (bhāsa, “luster, light”); and Old English <i>basu</i> (“purple”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ravine; cleft; chasm; gully; abyss</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek, like <i>σῆρᾰγξ</i> (sêrănx) and <i>φᾰ́λᾰγξ</i> (phắlănx). Furnée compares also <i>μάραγοι</i> (máragoi, “overhanging places”) and Spanish <i>barranca</i> (“ravine”). Mark the similar ending in the reconstructed root word for English <i>avalanche</i>, <i>&#42;labanka</i>.</p><p>But compare the family of Latin <i>forō</i>, <i>feriō</i> and Armenian <i>բերան</i> (beran, “mouth, opening”), all from <i>&#42;bʰerH-</i> (“to pierce, strike”). Compare also Ancient Greek <i>φᾰ́ρῠγξ</i> (phắrŭnx) and Latin <i>frūmen</i>, both possibly from <i>&#42;bʰruHg-</i> (“to use, enjoy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of cloth</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾶρος</i> (phâros, “large piece of cloth”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>νεοσσοί</i> (neossoí, “young birds, nestlings, chicks”).</li></ol><p>Unknown. The gloss does not appear in the correct alphabetical place; perhaps it contains an error.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φᾰ́ρμᾰκᾰ</i> (phắrmăkă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drug (<i>whether healing or noxious</i>)</li><li>healing drug; medicine; remedy</li><li>potion; charm; spell</li><li>poison</li><li>dye; coloring</li></ol><p>Unclear etymology, but most likely derived from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰármakon</i>. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀔𐀒</i> (pa-ma-ko /⁠pʰármakon⁠/).</p><p>Based on <i>α&#47;ο</i> (a&#47;o) and <i>μ&#47;β</i> (m&#47;b) variations found in the likely-related <i>φόρβαντα</i> (phórbanta, “doctor's medicine”), as well as the rather strange structure of the word, R. S. P. Beekes proposes Pre-Greek origin. Foreign origin is also supported by Chantraine, Furnee, and Schwyzer.</p><p>An older theory by Pokorny (1959) connects the word to the Greek root φᾰρ-, supposedly also found in <i>φᾰ́ρος</i> (phắros, “plough”) and <i>φᾰ́ρῠγξ</i> (phắrŭnx, “throat”), from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerH-</i> (“to cut, pierce, scrape”), i.e. a medicinal herb or root as something cut off or dug up, and compares Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burōną</i> (“to drill”) (which he takes as the result of a conflation with <i>&#42;bazją</i> (“berry”)) and Latin <i>feriō</i> (“hit, cut, slay, strike”). Compare also Latvian <i>burt</i> (“to carve (marks, on a tree), to conjure magic”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to magical arts</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a poisoner, magician, sorcerer</li></ol><p>From <i>φάρμακον</i> (phármakon, “herb, drug”). The noun only in biblical Greek, for <i>φαρμακεύς</i> (pharmakeús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plough</li><li>ploughing</li><li>alternative form of <i>φᾶρος</i> (phâros, “large piece of cloth, web”)</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) lighthouse</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerH-</i> (“to carve; split; cut; rub; pierce; strike”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;burōną</i> (“to bore”) and Latin <i>forō</i> (“to bore”).</p><p>From <i>φᾶρος</i> (phâros).</p><p>Unknown. Suggested as being from <i>Φᾰ́ρος</i> (Phắros, “Pharos”), the name of an island on which the lighthouse of Alexandria was located (possibly from Egyptian), as well as the name of that lighthouse. Alternatively, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰar-</i> (“log, board, plank”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φάρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharos (a former island off Alexandria, Egypt)</li><li>The Lighthouse of Alexandria, one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World</li></ol><p>Uncertain. In one proposed etymology the Ancient Greek name comes from the (unattested) Egyptian name for the island, corresponding to Coptic ⲫⲁⲣⲉϩ (<i>phareh</i>, “the guard”) = <i>ⲫ-</i> (ph-, “the”) +&lrm; <i>ⲁⲣⲉϩ</i> (areh, “guard”);</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any piece cut off, portion</li><li>A quarter of a city</li><li>A cloth, a covering</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰres-</i> (“to burst, break, crack, split, separate”) and cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brestaną</i> (“to burst”). Previously it was connected with <i>ἅψος</i> (hápsos, “joint”) and <i>μύσος</i> (músos, “defilement”). Compare also <i>φᾶρος</i> (phâros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throat; windpipe; esophagus; pharynx</li><li>(<i>zootomy</i>) dewlap of a bull</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;pathology) diseases of the throat</li></ol><p>According to Frisk an inherited Indo-European word for “throat, gorge”, cognate to Latin <i>frūmen</i> and Old Armenian <i>երբուծ</i> (erbuc), both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰruHg-</i> (“to use, enjoy”), but phonetically this is somewhat problematic as the Greek form requires laryngeal metathesis to <i>&#42;bʰr̥Hug-</i>, turning the root disyllabic, for unknown reasons. Alternatively, it could be related to <i>φᾰ́ρᾰγξ</i> (phắrănx, “ravine, cleft, chasm, gully, abyss”) or <i>φᾶρος</i> (phâros, “cloth, cloak”), also of disputed origin.</p><p>Beekes pointed out that the prenasalized suffix -ῠγγ- (or -ῠγ-) argues for a Pre-Greek origin. Compare <i>λᾰ́ρῠγξ</i> (lắrŭnx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Lyric form of <i>φᾰ́ρῠγξ</i> (phắrŭnx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Locrian form of <i>φέρω</i> (phérō)</li></ol><p>From a zero-grade variant of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰérō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéreti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φάσο</i> (pháso)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sword</li><li>corn-flag (<i>Gladiolus italicus</i>)</li><li>cocklebur, rough cocklebur (<i>Xanthium strumarium</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes of Pre-Greek origin. Has been connected with Ancient Greek <i>σφάζω</i> (spházō, “to slay, to kill”) but this connection seems unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cowpea</li></ol><p>Uncertain, though apparently from some European substrate. Identical to Latin <i>phasēlus</i>, of which the Greek is likely the etymon, though traditionally the opposite direction has also been proposed. Albanian <i>bathë</i> (“thick bean”), reflecting a root <i>*bʰaḱ-</i>, whence also <i>φακός</i> (phakós), has led to claims of an Illyrian source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance</li><li>utterance, statement, expression</li></ol><p>From <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p><p>From <i>φημί</i> (phēmí) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of moss drooping from oaks</li></ol><p>There are several hypotheses:</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare, state, affirm</li><li>to think</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂-sḱéti</i>; cognate with <i>φημί</i> (phēmí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to declare, state, affirm</li><li>to think</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂-sḱéti</i>; cognate with <i>φημί</i> (phēmí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wallet, scrip, leathern bag</li></ol><p>It seems formally evident to connect <i>φάσκος</i> (pháskos), in which case the bag would have denominated after a skin from which the hairs have not been removed. Pokorny connects also <i>βάσκιοι</i> (báskioi, “bundles of dry wood”), the different initial consonant of which would have to reflect old dialectal variation if of Proto-Indo-European origin, but Furnée adds <i>φάκελος</i> (phákelos, “bundle”). Pokorny connected the term to Albanian <i>bokshe</i> (“bundle”), Latin <i>fascis</i>, Breton <i>bec'h</i> (“bundle, load”), and English <i>basket</i>. However, according to Beekes, an origin in Pre-Greek seems probable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apparition, phantom.</li><li>Appearance, phenomenon.</li><li>(shows,&#32;mysteries) Images or types of realities.</li><li>Sign from heaven, portent, omen.</li><li>Monster, prodigy.</li></ol><p>From <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “to bring to light, to cause to appear”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, nominal suffix) with -σ- from analogy to other -αίνω : -ασμα or from <i>φάσις</i> (phásis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood pigeon; ring dove; cushat</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin. Related to <i>φᾰ́ψ</i> (phắps, “wild pigeon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φάτο</i> (pháto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manger, crib</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) socket of tooth</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Beehive Cluster</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰendʰ-</i> (“to tie”), the same root of Sanskrit <i>बन्धति</i> (bandhati) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bindaną</i>. The same suffix is recognized in Gaulish <i>benna</i> and Welsh <i>ben</i> (“cart”). However, Beekes finds this theory improbable and suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>φᾰ́σσᾰ</i> (phắssă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>φᾰ́σσᾰ</i> (phắssă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild pigeon</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to other bird names, such as <i>γῡ́ψ</i> (gū́ps, “vulture”) and <i>σκώψ</i> (skṓps, “owl”); Beekes states that it is clearly related to <i>φᾰ́σσᾰ</i> (phắssă, “pigeon”), and thus Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to be put to flight, to flee</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰégʷomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰégʷeti</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰegʷ-</i> (“to run away, flee”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>अभिभजते</i> (abhibhajate), Lithuanian <i>bė́gti</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;běgati</i> (“to flee, run”), Albanian <i>boj</i>, and perhaps Tocharian A <i>pkänt</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light, splendour, lustre</li><li>moonlight</li><li>light of torches or fire</li><li>light of the eyes</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) delight, glory, pride</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin. Traditionally compared with Lithuanian <i>spingė́ti</i>, <i>spingiù</i> (“to glitter”), as well as with Old High German <i>funko</i> (“spark”), but this suggestion is impossible in view of the initial stops. Other theories taking the word as a contamination of an unattested <i>&#42;σπέγγος</i> (&#42;spéngos) and <i>φάος</i> (pháos, “light”) are farfetched.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>faecula, tartar deposited by wine</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>faecula</i> (“tartar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of cork</li></ol><p>From <i>φελλός</i> (phellós, “cork oak”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheat; quack; impostor</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shield-bearing, shield-bearer</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bear”) +&lrm; <i>ἀσπίς</i> (aspís, “shield”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed, nourish</li><li>to pasture, graze</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be fed</li><li>to eat, consume</li><li>to enjoy, have</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, an agricultural term without etymology. Alternatively from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰergʷ-</i>, if Latin <i>forbea</i> reflects a true Italic cognate rather than a borrowing from Ancient Greek <i>φορβή</i> (phorbḗ) or an unattested relative thereof.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bier</li><li>litter</li><li>frame</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to carry”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron, instrument noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is borne, fruit of the womb</li><li>fruit of the earth</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish-basket</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰer-</i>, the same root of <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring, carry”) and <i>φερνή</i> (phernḗ, “dowry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squirting cucumber (<i>Ecballium elaterium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring”) +&lrm; <i>ὄμβρος</i> (ómbros, “thunderstorm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, bear, carry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰérō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéreti</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>beran</i> (English <i>bear</i>), Latin <i>ferō</i>, Sanskrit <i>भरति</i> (bhárati), Old Armenian <i>բերեմ</i> (berem), and Albanian <i>bie</i>.</p><p>The future paradigm <i>οἴσω</i> (oísō) is derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃eyt-</i> (“to fetch”). Cognates include Latin <i>ūtor</i> (“to use”).</p><p>The aorist <i>ἤνεγκα</i> (ḗnenka, “I brought”) is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ne-h₁n̥ḱe-</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;h₁é-h₁n̥ḱ-e-</i>, reduplicated aorist of <i>&#42;h₁neḱ-</i> (“to take away”). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>нести</i> (nesti, “to carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>like, as</li></ol><p>Probably related to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bo</i> (“for”). Sometimes considered a suffixless form of <i>φημί</i> (phēmí). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oaken</li></ol><p>From <i>φηγός</i> (phēgós, “oak”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild fig</li></ol><p>An evident Pre-Greek word in <i>-ηξ</i> like <i>πήληξ</i> (pḗlēx), <i>τράφηξ</i> (tráphēx), <i>ὄρπηξ</i> (órpēx) and <i>νάρθηξ</i> (nárthēx). It might have been folk-etymologically adapted to <i>φῆλος</i> (phêlos, “deceitful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prophetic voice; oracle</li><li>rumor</li><li>reputation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰā́mā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-meh₂</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to speak”). By surface analysis, a full-grade deverbal of <i>φημῐ́</i> (phēmĭ́, “to say; to explain; to argue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lammergeier, ossifrage (<i>Gypaetus barbatus</i>)</li></ol><p>The presence of an alternative form leads to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>θήρ</i> (thḗr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φῐ́λε</i> (phĭ́le)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>φῐ́λα</i> (phĭ́la)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φῐ́λε</i> (phĭ́le)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>φῐ́λημᾰ</i> (phĭ́lēmă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female friend, woman or girl who is dear to one</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kiss</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cosmetics</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλέω</i> (philéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) comparative degree of <i>φῐ́λως</i> (phĭ́lōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friendly</li></ol><p><i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fond of horses, horse-loving</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλο-</i> (philo-, “love”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φίλιππος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Philip</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλιππος</i> (phílippos, “fond of horses”), from <i>φιλέω</i> (philéō, “to love”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) superlative degree of <i>φῐ́λως</i> (phĭ́lōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) superlative degree of <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>That which is loved or important: beloved, dear, of or from a friend</li><li>(<i>less commonly</i>): loving, friendly</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friend</li></ol><p>In this case, cognate with Old Irish <i>bil</i> (“good, mild”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;biliz</i> (“kind, gentle; decent, fair”), whence Old English <i>bile-</i> (“kind, suitable, appropriate”, prefix), German <i>billig</i> (“appropriate, fitting, inexpensive, cheap”), and Dutch <i>billijk</i> (“appropriate”).</p><p>However, Beekes rejects the above etymology based on the original meaning of the Greek word apparently being "own, accompanying" rather than "beloved", and leaves the origin open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>φῐ́λως</i> (phĭ́lōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>φῐ́λως</i> (phĭ́lōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a magical means of causing love, such as a love charm or love potion</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) charm; spell</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) love; affection</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) philtrum (the shallow vertical groove running from the nasal septum to the center of the upper lip)</li><li>synonym of <i>στᾰφῠλῖνος</i> (stăphŭlînos, a type of insect about the size of a beetle)</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλέω</i> (phĭléō, “to love; to kiss”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron, instrument noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gladly; dearly</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “loved; dear”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muzzling, silencing of death</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) phimosis</li></ol><p>From <i>φῑμόω</i> (phīmóō, “to muzzle”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, to be radiant</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰawétʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂wedʰeti</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to shine”), whence <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φαίδιμε</i> (phaídime)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shining, radiant, glistening</li><li>sleek, glossy</li><li>famous, glorious</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>φαιδρός</i> (phaidrós) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, beaming</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) beaming with joy, bright, joyous, jocund</li></ol><p>The stem is <i>φαιδ-</i> (phaid-), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰh₂eyd-</i> (“bright, clear”), whence also Lithuanian <i>gaidrùs</i> (“bright, clear”), <i>gaidrà</i> (“cloudless heaven, clear weather”), <i>giẽdras</i> (“clear, serene”).</p><p>Older theories derived the word from <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “bright”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φαίδρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phaedra</li></ol><p>From <i>Φαῖδρος</i> (Phaîdros, “Phaedrus”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φαίνετο</i> (phaíneto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>yes; so it appears; apparently</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to appear; to bring to light; to show; to uncover; to reveal</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to make known, reveal, disclose</li><li>to make (a sound) heard</li><li>to show forth; to expound</li><li>to denounce</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shine, give light</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to appear; to shine</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to come into being</li><li>to come about</li><li>(<i>copulative&#32;or&#32;control verb</i>) to appear (to be)</li><li>(Koine,&#32;impersonal) to seem</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰáňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂nyéti</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to shine”).</p><p>The present tense system includes a <i>&#42;-ϳ-</i> (&#42;-j-) progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φαίνοιτε</i> (phaínoite) and <i>φαίνοιτο</i> (phaínoito)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to appear; to bring to light; to show; to uncover; to reveal</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to make known, reveal, disclose</li><li>to make (a sound) heard</li><li>to show forth; to expound</li><li>to denounce</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shine, give light</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to appear; to shine</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to come into being</li><li>to come about</li><li>(<i>copulative&#32;or&#32;control verb</i>) to appear (to be)</li><li>(Koine,&#32;impersonal) to seem</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰáňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂nyéti</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to shine”).</p><p>The present tense system includes a <i>&#42;-ϳ-</i> (&#42;-j-) progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>canker, cancerous sore</li><li>disease of bees</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>ἔφαγον</i> (éphagon, “devour, consume”) +&lrm; <i>-αινα</i> (-aina).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Aeolic form of <i>φᾱνός</i> (phānós, “bright, shining, resplendent”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright, beaming</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) beaming with joy, bright, joyous, jocund</li></ol><p>The stem is <i>φαιδ-</i> (phaid-), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰh₂eyd-</i> (“bright, clear”), whence also Lithuanian <i>gaidrùs</i> (“bright, clear”), <i>gaidrà</i> (“cloudless heaven, clear weather”), <i>giẽdras</i> (“clear, serene”).</p><p>Older theories derived the word from <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “bright”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>grey, of any colour mixed of black and white</li><li>harsh (of sound)</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin.</p><p>The word has been compared with Latvian <i>gaišs</i> (“light, bright”) and Lithuanian <i>gai̇̃sas</i> (“beam of light, redness in the sky”) and reconstructed as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰaiso-</i> (“bright, shining”), though there are formal issues with this. Other reconstructions which have been proposed are <i>&#42;φαιϝός</i> (&#42;phaiwós) and <i>&#42;φαισϝός</i> (&#42;phaiswós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>facecloth, turban, towel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>facialē</i> (“facecloth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lentil (<i>Lens culinaris</i>) and its seed</li><li>anything shaped like a lentil:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sort of hot water bottle</li><li>mole, birthmark, spot on the skin</li><li>ornament on beds</li></ol></ol><p>Disputed. From a root <i>&#42;bʰaḱ-</i> shared with Albanian <i>bathë</i> (“broad bean”). The initial <i>&#42;bʰa-</i> is also found in Latin <i>faba</i> (“bean”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bòbъ</i> (“bean”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;baunō</i> (“bean”). Alternatively akin to <i>ἀφάκη</i> (aphákē, “vetch”), in which case possibly from Pre-Greek. An Illyrian origin has also been proposed, in which case the word could be a doublet of <i>φάσηλος</i> (phásēlos, “cowpea”).</p><p>However note Classical Syriac <i>ܦܘܗܘ</i> (pāhū), <i>ܦܐܟܘ</i> (paḵū, “lentil”) vs <i>ܦܘܗܐ</i> (pāhā, pāhū, “pimple, pustule, sore”), paralleling Akkadian <i>𒄘𒌉</i> (GU₂.TUR /⁠kakkû⁠/, “lentil”) already found in Old Akkadian vs. <i>𒆕𒆪𒌈</i> (kak-ku-tum /⁠kakkūtu⁠/, “single lentil; pimple, acne, pustule?”).</p><p>It is likely that the terms mentioned above are all related and derived from an ancient European and Mediterranean substrate word for "bean", which was borrowed by various groups in the area, particularly considering that the words for various beans in Indo-Aryan, a branch of Indo-European removed from this area, do not resemble the above terms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European black widow, malmignatte (<i>Latrodectus tredecimguttatus</i>)</li><li>spiderwort (<i>Gagea graeca</i>)</li><li>log or roller put under a ship</li></ol><p>From <i>φάλαγξ</i> (phálanx, “phalanx, battle array”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a phalangite; a soldier of the phalanx</li></ol><p>From <i>φάλαγξ</i> (phálanx, “a phalanx”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bald</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>φαλός</i> (phalós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “topmost”). Akin to <i>φάλαρος</i> (phálaros).</p><p>Compare typologically Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;lysъ</i> (<&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;lewk-</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coot (<i>Fulica atra</i>)</li><li>canary grass (<i>Phalaris aquatica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φάλαρος</i> (phálaros, “having a patch of white”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἄκρατον</i> (ákraton, “unmixed, pure”).</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, from Pre-Greek comparing <i>χάλις</i> (khális, “pure wine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penis; phallus</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The meaning "penis" likely developed from a more concrete meaning, and has been compared to <i>βαλλία</i> (ballía, “private parts”) and the ethnonym <i>Τριβαλλοί</i> (Triballoí). Possible Indo-European cognates are Old Irish <i>ball</i> (“member, body part”) as well as dialectal German <i>Bille</i> (“penis”), all usually compared to words for "ball, sack, bull, testis" and similar, supposedly deriving from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“to blow, swell”) (id est: *bʰl̥nós &gt; *pʰəlnós &gt; *pʰəllós). Probably akin to <i>φάλλαινα</i> (phállaina, “whale”), because of the body shape of whales.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) white</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰalós</i>, traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“to shine”), with cognates including Sanskrit <i>भाल</i> (bhāla, “splendour”), Old Armenian <i>բալ</i> (bal, “fog”), Old English <i>bǣl</i> (English <i>bale</i>), and, in particular, Lithuanian <i>bãlas</i> (“white”).</p><p>It was further supposed that <i>βαλιός</i> (baliós, “spotted, dappled”) was related as a word from the Balkans. However, according to Beekes, the word is of Pre-Greek origin, in view of all the variations seen in the derived terms, and thus unrelated to the words listed under <i>&#42;bʰel-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>φᾱνός</i> (phānós, “torch, lantern”)</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾱνός</i> (phānós) +&lrm; <i>-άριον</i> (-árion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch, firebrand</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) torch procession</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to shine”). By surface analysis, <i>φᾱνός</i> (phānós) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ), with irregular shortening from <i>&#42;φᾱνή</i> (&#42;phānḗ) likely by analogy with <i>φᾰνερός</i> (phănerós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible, manifest, evident, apparent</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of property</i>) real</li><li>(<i>of votes</i>) open</li><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) openly, manifestly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>joined with prepositions in adverbial sense</i>)</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>of gods, people</i>) known, acknowledged, conspicuous</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>open, frank</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “to show, make visible, bring to light”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make manifest</li><li>to make known or famous</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become so</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>φᾰνερός</i> (phănerós, “visible”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>φανερός</i> (phanerós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>openly, manifestly</li></ol><p><i>φᾰνερός</i> (phănerós, “visible”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show; to make visible</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to place before one's mind, to picture to oneself, to imagine</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become visible, appear</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be heard</li><li>to be terrified by visions or phantasms</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>φᾰντός</i> (phăntós, “visible”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō), verbal adjective of <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “I cause to appear, bring to light”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>look, appearance, presentation, display</li><li>showy appearance, pomp, pageantry</li><li>perception, impression</li><li>image</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾰ́ντᾰσῐς</i> (phắntăsĭs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>φᾰντᾰ́ζω</i> (phăntắzō, “to make visible”), from <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>able to produce the appearance of something</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bright; shining; resplendent</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch; lantern</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>φαεινός</i> (phaeinós), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰawehnós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂wesnós</i>, adjective noun from substantive <i>&#42;bʰéh₂wos</i> (whence <i>φᾰ́ος</i> (phắos)), from <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small gully or ravine</li></ol><p><i>φᾰ́ρᾰγξ</i>&nbsp;f (phắrănx, “ravine, cleft, chasm, gully, abyss”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i>&nbsp;n (-ĭon, suffix forming diminutive nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiver for arrows</li></ol><p>It has been assumed a derivation with a reduced grade from <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to carry”), beside a full grade in <i>φέρετρον</i> (phéretron, “bier, litter”). However, because a reduced grade is not accepted anymore since the laryngeal theory, a loanword with a folk-etymological adaptation should be considered.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>φᾰρέτρᾱ</i> (phărétrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of vessel</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φαρία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharia; Hvar, Croatia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Φαραώ</i> (Pharaṓ, “Pharaoh”)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φαραώ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pharaoh</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>פַּרְעֹה</i> (parʿōʰ), from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">pr&#58;aA</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>pr-ꜥꜣ</i>, “great house”).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>φᾶρος</i> (phâros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wrinkle, crease in the skin</li></ol><p>The word has been compared with Latin <i>fricō</i> (“to rub, chafe”), Lithuanian <i>brūkis</i> (“stroke, line”) and <i>braūkti</i> (“to strike, rub”), but this is formally hardly possible. Possibly from Pre-Greek. The connection with <i>φορκόν</i> (phorkón, “grey; wrinkled”) is quite uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) to treat using <i>φάρμακα</i> (phármaka); to apply or prepare a substance</li><li>(<i>post-Homeric</i>) to heal or relieve by medicine; to medicate</li><li>to enchant or bewitch by potions or philtres</li><li>to poison</li><li>to dye or color</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to embellish or falsify</li><li>to season or prepare food</li></ol><p>From <i>φάρμακον</i> (phármakon) + <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō), denoting the application or preparation of drugs or compounds.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pharmacy; the use of drugs or medicines</li><li>sorcery, witchcraft</li></ol><p>From <i>φαρμακεύω</i> (pharmakeúō, “to use drugs”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), from <i>φάρμακον</i> (phármakon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or by means of drugs or pharmacy</li></ol><p>From <i>φαρμακεύω</i> (pharmakeúō, “to administer a drug or medicine”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sorcerer, poisoner</li><li>druggist, apothecary</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾰρμᾰκεύω</i> (phărmăkeúō, “to administer a drug; practise sorcery”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medicine-chest</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾰ́ρμᾰκον</i> (phắrmăkon, “drug”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case; box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>preparation of drugs</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾰρμᾰκοποιός</i> (phărmăkopoiós, “preparing drugs”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>producing drugs</li></ol><p>From <i>φάρμακον</i> (phármakon, “drug”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one sacrificed or executed as an atonement or purification; scapegoat</li><li>(<i>term of abuse</i>) criminal; worthless person</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hellenistic synonym of <i>φάσηλος</i> (phásēlos)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>phasēolus</i>, diminutive of phaselus, itself likely from Ancient Greek <i>φάσηλος</i> (phásēlos) (a Koine reborrowing).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pheasant (<i>Phasianus colchicus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Φᾱσῐᾱνός</i> (Phāsĭānós, “Phasian”) (with the implied substantive <i>ὄρνις</i> (órnis, “bird”)) from <i>Φᾶσῐς</i> (Phâsĭs, “Phasis”), from where, it was supposed, the bird spread to the west.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) fascia, bandage, strip</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>fascia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dove-killing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of hawk&#59; perhaps the peregrine falcon (<i>Falco peregrinus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φᾰ́σσᾰ</i> (phắssă, “dove”) +&lrm; <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder; slaughter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pangolin, scaly anteater</li></ol><p>The variants show that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that may be spoken, speakable, utterable</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) famous, notable</li><li>(<i>in compounds only?</i>) slain, dead</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰatós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂tós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to speak”).</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰətós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰen-</i> (“to kill, slay”). Related to <i>θείνω</i> (theínō, “to strike, wound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coarse kind of olive, produced from the wild olive</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>φαύλιος</i> (phaúlios, “coarse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) easy, slight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>trivial, paltry, sorry, indifferent, miserable, poor</li><li>sorry, paltry, mean, bad</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) low in rank, mean, common</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>worthless, sorry, indifferent, poor, common, of no account, bad</li><li>careless, thoughtless, indifferent</li><li>(<i>in good sense</i>) simple, unaffected</li><li>(<i>of outward appearance</i>) shabby, plain</li><li>(<i>of health</i>) ill</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pā́uros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;peh₂w-</i>, the irregular <i>φ-</i> (ph-) representing an expressive <i>&#42;pʰ-</i>, as in Old Armenian <i>փոքր</i> (pʻokʻr, “small”). Beekes proposes a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spare, have mercy on</li><li>to be merciful</li><li>to refrain from using, use sparingly</li><li>to be sparing, be thrifty, live thriftily</li><li>to draw back from, shrink from</li><li>to cease (<i>to do something</i>), forbear (<i>from doing something</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéydt</i> (“to split”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>भेदति</i> (bhedati, “to split”) and Gothic <i>𐌱𐌴𐌹𐍄𐌰𐌽</i> (beitan, “to bite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>φειδώ</i> (pheidṓ)</li></ol><p>From <i>φειδωλός</i> (pheidōlós) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sparing, thrifty</li></ol><p><i>φειδός</i> (pheidós) + the same suffix found in <i>ἁμαρτωλός</i> (hamartōlós, “sinful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sparing, thrifty</li></ol><p>A backformation from <i>φείδομαι</i> (pheídomai, “to spare, save”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparing</li><li>thrift, parsimony</li></ol><p><i>φείδεσθαι</i> (pheídesthai, “to spare”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ, feminine noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uneven, stony ground</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. If related to <i>πέλλα</i> (pélla, “stone”), the <i>π/φ</i> interchange could suggest a Pre-Greek borrowing. Alternatively, the word may be related to <i>φελλός</i> (phellós, “cork oak”), in reference to the uneven and porous structure of cork.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holm oak (<i>Quercus ilex</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φελλός</i> (phellós, “cork oak”) +&lrm; <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “oak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cork oak (<i>Quercus suber</i>)</li><li>cork, especially the corks on a net</li></ol><p>Unknown. The word has been compared with <i>φολῐ́ς</i> (pholĭ́s, “scale”) and with Russian <i>о́болонь</i> (óbolonʹ, “sapwood, alburnum”) but Beekes finds this improbable, due to the limited geographic distribution of the words if cognate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false hair, wig</li></ol><p>From <i>φένᾱξ</i> (phénāx, “impostor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lie</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to trick, cheat, defraud</li></ol><p>From <i>φένᾱξ</i> (phénāx, “cheat, imposter”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cheating, quackery, imposition</li></ol><p>From <i>φενᾱκῐ́ζω</i> (phenākĭ́zō, “to cheat, trick”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>Μηδική ὁδός</i> (Mēdikḗ hodós, literally “Median road”).</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is brought by the wife, a dowry</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “bring”) +&lrm; <i>-νή</i> (-nḗ, “a suffix added to verbal stems or adjectives to form feminine nouns.”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to flee, run off, go a certain direction with haste (<i>often with prepositions</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to flee, escape, avoid, get away from (danger or trouble)</li><li>(<i>transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive</i>) to leave the country, go into exile</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be exiled, banished, driven out of the country [<i>with</i> <b>ὑπό</b> (hupó, + genitive) <small>‘by someone’</small>]</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;present&#32;and&#32;imperfect) to be in exile, live in banishment</li></ol><li>(<i>perfect</i>) to have escaped, be safe from</li><li>(law,&#32;chiefly&#32;present&#32;and&#32;imperfect) to be accused of a crime; often with <i>δίκην</i> (díkēn) and genitive of the crime</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰéugō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewg-</i> (“to flee”). Related to <i>φυγή</i> (phugḗ, verbal noun) and Latin <i>fugiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phi, the name for the twenty-first letter Φ, φ of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>φιλία</i> (philía)</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλέω</i> (philéō, “love”), same root as <i>φιλία</i> (philía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>alas!</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oak</li><li>acorn</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰāgós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂ǵos</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>fāgus</i>, Old English <i>bēċe</i> (whence English <i>beech</i>), Gothic <i>𐌱𐍉𐌺𐌰</i> (bōka, “beech”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak, say</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to think</li><li>(<i>of an author</i>) to write</li><li>(<i>φησί</i> (phēsí) or <i>ἔφη</i> (éphē) used when quoting, sometimes after another verb of saying)</li></ol><li>to say yes, agree, affirm, assert</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with <i>οὐ</i> (ou)) to say no, deny, refuse</li><li>(<i>φημί</i> (phēmí) or <i>οὕτως φημί</i> (hoútōs phēmí) as interjection) yes, I would say so</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰā́mi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰéh₂ti</i> (“to speak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to come or do, first or before others</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be beforehand with, overtake, anticipate (with accusative person)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come or act first</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰh₂-</i> (“to come near, nearly reach, pass”), and cognate with a Sanskrit <i>धग्नु</i> (dhagnu). However, such a Sanskrit term does not seem to be attested in Sanskrit dictionaries.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make a sound, utter</li><li>to speak loudly, shout, cry out, proclaim</li><li>to sing, praise, celebrate</li></ol><p>Apparently from a stem <i>φθεγ-</i> (phtheg-) with added ablaut and nasalization, of unclear further origin. Semantically similar forms with superficial phonetic resemblance exist in Balto-Slavic, like Russian <i>звягать</i> (zvjagatʹ, “to bark, yap”), Lithuanian <i>žvéngti</i> (“to neigh”), and Lithuanian <i>speñgti</i> (“(of the ears) to resound, bite, hum, drone”); however, theories connecting the Greek to these terms suffer from phonological issues.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>φθέγγομαι</i> (phthéngomai, “to make a sound”)+&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mildew</li><li>kind of olive</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>φθίω</i> (phthíō, “to perish, waste away, decay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>φθίω</i> (phthíō, “to wane, decay, dwindle”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰey-</i> (“to decline, disappear”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>क्षिणाति</i> (kṣiṇā́ti, “to destroy, ruin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decline, decay</li><li>atrophy</li><li>consumption (disease), tuberculosis</li><li>waning of the moon</li><li>contraction of the eye</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰtʰítis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰítis</i> (“perishing, decrease”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>क्षिति</i> (kṣíti, “perishing, downfall”) and Latin <i>sitis</i> (“thirst”). By surface analysis, <i>φθίω</i> (phthíō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wane, decay, dwindle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰey-</i> (“to decline, disappear”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>क्षिणाति</i> (kṣiṇā́ti, “to destroy, ruin”), English <i>dwindle</i>, Spanish <i>sed</i> (“thirst”), French <i>soif</i> (“thirst”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destructible, perishable</li></ol><p>From <i>φθείρω</i> (phtheírō, “to spoil, ruin”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>louse</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Has been linked to <i>φθείρω</i> (phtheírō, “to ruin, to destroy”) which is of Indo-European origin, though the connection is doubtful and considered as a folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy</li><li>to ruin, spoil</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰtʰéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰér-ye-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰer-</i> (“flow, diverge; perish”) via the middle form <i>φθείρομαι</i> (phtheíromai, “to be destroyed, perish”) with new active meaning “destroy”. Cognate to Sanskrit <i>क्षरति</i> (kṣárati, “flows, diverges; disappears”), Avestan <i>𐬖𐬲𐬀𐬭𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (γžara<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti, “flows, streams”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a voice, vocal</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>φθεγκτός</i> (phthenktós, “voiced”, “sounding”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “-ic”, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having voice or sound</li><li>resounding</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stavesacre (<i>Delphinium staphisagria</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φθείρ</i> (phtheír, “louse”) +&lrm; <i>-κτόνος</i> (-któnos). By surface analysis, <i>φθείρ</i> (phtheír) +&lrm; <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō, “to kill”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>autumnal</li></ol><p>From <i>φθῐνόπωρ(ον)</i> (phthĭnópōr(on), “autumn, fall”) +&lrm; <i>-ινός</i> (-inós, suffix for adjectives), already since 5th century at Hippocrates (@scaife.perseus).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autumn</li></ol><p>From <i>φθῐ́ν(ω)</i> (phthĭ́n(ō), “decay, to be reduced”) + <i>ὀπώρ(α)</i>&nbsp;f (opṓr(a), “last part of summer; fruit”) + <i>-ον</i> (-on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>φθίω</i> (phthíō, “to wane, decay, dwindle”) (<i>present and imperfect only</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destroying men (<i>epithet of war</i>)</li></ol><p><i>φθίω</i> (phthíō) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consumptive</li></ol><p><i>φθίσις</i> (phthísis, “wasting away; consumption”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resounding</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>φθόγγος</i> (phthóngos, “voice”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear ill-will, to be envious (<i>toward one: dative</i>)</li><li>to refuse</li></ol><p>From <i>φθόνος</i> (phthónos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>destruction, ruin</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of persons</i>) death (<i>especially by some divine act, as pestilence</i>)</li><li>(<i>of animals</i>) loss by death</li></ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) passing out of existence, ceasing to be</li><li>deterioration</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>loss by deterioration, damage</li></ol><li>seduction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>rape</li></ol><li>abortion, miscarriage</li><li>gradation of colors in painting</li><li>wasting away</li><li>the tossings of a storm, shipwrecks</li></ol><p>From <i>φθείρω</i> (phtheírō, “ruin, destroy, destruct”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corruption, mischief</li></ol><p>From <i>φθείρω</i> (phtheírō) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sound or voice</li></ol><p>Nominal form of the verb <i>φθέγγομαι</i> (phthéngomai, “to utter a sound, raise one's voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>envy, jealousy</li><li>malice, grudge, ill will</li><li>cause for indignation, reproach</li><li>jealousy of the gods</li><li>refusal for feelings of ill will or envy, grudging</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰen-</i>, albeit with no certain adduced cognates. De Vaan tentatively suggests a connection with <i>φθάνω</i> (phthánō, “to anticipate, be ahead of”), which may also imply a connection with Avestan <i>𐬀𐬖𐬲𐬋𐬥𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬨𐬥𐬆𐬨</i> (aγžōnuuamnəm, “which cannot be missed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>round and shallow bowl, saucer, or pan</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek or some other language, based on the interchange of suffixes in the Mycenaean cognates. The word formally fits the instrument names in "-αλη" and "-αλο-", like <i>σκυτάλη</i> (skutálē) and <i>γύαλον</i> (gúalon).</p><p>A borrowing relationship with Iranian (compare Persian پیاله (piyâle, “cup, bowl”)) has been suggested, though as the Greek is itself thought to be a loanword, the ultimate direction is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divination by gazing into a saucer</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐᾰ́λη</i> (phĭắlē, “bowl, pan”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰντείᾱ</i> (mănteíā, “divination”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gleaming, shining, bright</li><li>sleek (of a bird)</li></ol><p>Reminiscent of <i>πιαρός</i> (piarós) and <i>φαιδρός</i> (phaidrós). According to Furnée, the adjective is probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>avaricious</li><li>miserly</li></ol><p><i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “beloved, loving”) +&lrm; <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver, money”), literally “money-loving”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to love, like, regard with affection</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to treat affectionately or kindly; to welcome, entertain (a guest)</li><li>to regard with sexual passion</li><li>(<i>post-Homeric</i>) to show outward signs of affection; to kiss</li><li>to like, approve (a thing)</li></ol><li>(<i>post-Homeric</i>) to enjoy, be fond; to be wont, be used (to doing something)</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “beloved, dear”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, stative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>kindly, affectionate</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλέω</i> (philéō) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φιλήμων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Philemon</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλήμων</i> (philḗmōn, “kindly, affectionate”), from <i>φιλέω</i> (philéō, “to love”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oar-loving</li></ol><p><i>φιλέω</i> (philéō) +&lrm; <i>ἐρετμόν</i> (eretmón)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendship, love, affection, fondness</li><li>friendliness, kindliness, without any affection</li><li>sexual love, like <i>ἔρως</i> (érōs)</li><li>(<i>with regard to things</i>) fondness for</li><li>regarded as the natural force which unites discordant elements and movements, as <i>νεῖκος</i> (neîkos) keeps them apart</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) comparative degree of <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Doric</i>) superlative degree of <i>φῐ́λως</i> (phĭ́lōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Doric</i>) superlative degree of <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Doric</i>) comparative degree of <i>φῐ́λως</i> (phĭ́lōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Doric</i>) comparative degree of <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>humanity, benevolence, kind-heartedness</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλάνθρωπος</i> (philánthrōpos) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>φῐλᾰ́ργῠρος</i> (phĭlắrgŭros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philauty</li><li>self-love</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλέω</i> (philéō, “to love”) +&lrm; <i>αὐτός</i> (autós, “self”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is fond of a lover</li><li>one who enjoys having lovers</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλο-</i> (phĭlo-, loving) +&lrm; <i>ἐραστής</i> (erastḗs, “lover”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fond of barbarians or foreigners</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλο-</i> (phĭlo-, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>βᾰ́ρβᾰρος</i> (bắrbăros, “non-Greek-speaking, foreign”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loving one's lord or master</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλο-</i> (phĭlo-, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>δεσπότης</i> (despótēs, “master, ruler”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be litigious, fond of litigation</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλόδικος</i> (philódikos, “litigious”) (from <i>φιλο-</i> (philo-) +&lrm; <i>δίκη</i> (díkē)) + <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love of fame</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλόδοξος</i> (phĭlódoxos) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loving the west wind</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλο-</i> (phĭlo-, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>Ζέφῠρος</i> (Zéphŭros, “west wind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fond of danger, adventurous</li><li>(<i>in bad sense</i>) foolhardy, reckless</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλο-</i> (phĭlo-, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>κῐ́νδῡνος</i> (kĭ́ndūnos, “danger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love of reasoning</li><li>love of learning, love of literature</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλόλογος</i> (philólogos, “loving words”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be fond of learning, love learning, seek knowledge</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλομαθής</i> (philomathḗs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loving or fond of learning</li><li>[<i>with</i> genitive] eager for</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλο-</i> (philo-) + the root of <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love of strife, eager rivalry, contentiousness, partisan spirit</li><li>emulation, eagerness</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλο-</i> (philo-) +&lrm; <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hospitality</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλόξενος</i> (phĭlóxenos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and poetic form of <i>φιλοπόλεμος</i> (philopólemos, “war-loving, bellicose”)</li></ol><p><i>φίλος</i> (phílos) +&lrm; <i>πτόλεμος</i> (ptólemos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lover of drinking, lover of wine, drunkard</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>πότης</i> (pótēs, “drinker”), from verb <i>πίνω</i> (pínō, “drink”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to love or pursue knowledge, philosophize</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to teach philosophy</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to discuss philosophically, investigate, study</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλόσοφος</i> (philósophos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love of knowledge, pursuit of knowledge</li><li>the study, investigation of a topic</li><li>philosophy</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλόσοφος</i> (phĭlósophos, “lover of knowledge or wisdom”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “beloved; loving”) +&lrm; <i>σοφός</i> (sophós, “skilled with handcrafts; wise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>philosophical</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλοσοφίᾱ</i> (philosophíā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subject or scientific inquiry or philosophic treatise</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) demonstrative syllogism; demonstration</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) principle; rule of conduct</li><li>clever device or invention</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλοσοφέω</i> (phĭlosophéō, “to love knowledge; teach philosophy”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fond of lies</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλο-</i> (philo-, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false, untrue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gypsywort (<i>Lycopus europaeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλτρον</i> (phíltron, “love charm”) +&lrm; <i>δότης</i> (dótēs, “giver, dispenser”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mock privet (<i>Phillyrea latifolia</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλῠ́ρα</i> (phĭlŭ́ra, “lime tree, linden”) +&lrm; <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fame loving</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλο-</i> (phĭlo-, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa, “glory, opinion”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>requiring manure</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλο-</i> (phĭlo-, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>κόπρος</i> (kópros, “dung, ordure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that loves to speak, loquacious</li><li>loving logic</li><li>passionate about learning, educated, learned</li><li>student of words</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλο-</i> (philo-, “beloved, loving”) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos, “word, talk, reason”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lover of legends and fables</li><li>lover of talking</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “beloved”) +&lrm; <i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos, “tales, legend”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>φιλόξενος</i> (philóxenos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>loving strangers</li><li>hospitable</li><li>kind to guests</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐλο-</i> (phĭlo-, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “foreign, strange, stranger, guest”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φιλόξενος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Philoxenus</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλόξενος</i> (philóxenos, “hospitable”), from <i>φίλος</i> (phílos) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>philosophy</li><li>(<i>metaphysics</i>) the rational part of the soul (with respect to its love of wisdom)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>scientific</li><li>philosophical</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lover of knowledge or wisdom</li><li>educated or learned person</li><li>philosopher</li><li>professor</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>frequently of family affection</i>) loving tenderly, affectionate</li></ol><p><i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los); <i>στέργω, στοργή</i> (stérgō, storgḗ); <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>friendship, love, affection</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) friendship between states</li><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) sexual love or intercourse</li></ol><p>From <i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “beloved, dear”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in bad sense</i>) emulous, ambitious, eager for honour</li><li>rejoicing in worship</li><li>prodigal, lavish, extravagant</li><li>munificent, generous, liberal</li></ol><p>From <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “loving”) +&lrm; <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honour”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alatern (<i>Rhamnus alaternus</i>)</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Perhaps a Pre-Greek word related to <i>φιλύρα</i> (philúra, “lime tree”) and <i>φυλία</i> (phulía, “wild olive tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>linden, lime tree (<i>Tilia europaea</i>)</li><li>bass underneath its bark</li></ol><p>Strömberg surmises a compound of <i>φίλος</i> (phílos, “friend”) +&lrm; <i>ὕρον</i> (húron, “swarm of bees”), because the linden attracts bees (compare Latin <i>apium</i> (“celery”) from <i>apis</i> (“bee”) and German <i>Bienenbaum</i> (“maple”) from <i>Biene</i> (“bee”)). The European lime tree is not native of Greece, so it is probably a substrate word, perhaps Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of linden wood</li><li>light as linden wood</li></ol><p>From <i>φιλύρα</i> (philúra, “linden”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>φῐ́λως</i> (phĭ́lōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>φῐ́λως</i> (phĭ́lōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fond of singing</li></ol><p>from <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “love”) +&lrm; <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song, ode”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contracted form of <i>φῐλέω</i> (phĭléō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>muzzle for dogs, calves</li><li>gag</li><li>noseband</li><li>cup used as a dicebox</li><li>tightening, constriction by means of ropes</li></ol><p>Unexplained. According to Beekes, the similarity of the suffix with <i>κημός</i> (kēmós, “muzzle”) is notable, while the relation with <i>φιτρός</i> (phitrós, “block of wood”) seems improbable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to muzzle, shut up as with a muzzle; to close, seal up; (<i>figurative</i>) to muzzle, put to silence</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be silent, be quiet</li></ol><p>From <i>φῑμός</i> (phīmós, “any instrument for keeping the mouth closed”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>block of wood, log</li><li>bole or trunk of a tree</li><li>firebrand</li></ol><p>Explained by Beekes as an instrument noun from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeyh₂-</i> (“to strike, hew”) and cognate with Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;binati</i> (“to hit”) and Latin <i>perfines</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flame, fire, heat</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) inflammation</li><li>phlegm, a cold slimy humour of the body</li></ol><p>From <i>φλέγω</i> (phlégō, “to set on fire, inflame”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set on fire, burn, scorch</li><li>to kindle, arouse, inflame</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰlegō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“to blaze, burn”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>भर्ग</i> (bharga) and Latin <i>flagrō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idle talker, babbler, chatterer</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>φλήναφος</i> (phlḗnaphos, “babbler; idle talk”) proves a Pre-Greek etymology, in view of the nasalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) vein</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Traditionally linked to a large group of words beginning with "φλ-", like <i>φλέω</i> (phléō, “to overflow, abound”), <i>φλύω</i> (phlúō, “to be full of juice, thrive”), and <i>φλύκταινα</i> (phlúktaina, “blister”). It has been supposed to be an enlargement in <i>&#42;-gʷ-</i> of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰel-</i> (“to blow, swell”), but this appears ad hoc and not particularly convincing. This, in addition to the Greek term's isolated formation, as well as the lexical representation of "vein" being notably unstable across Indo-European languages (with different roots employed in various branches), suggests a Pre-Greek origin as the most plausible explanation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to overflow, teem, abound, gush</li><li>to overflow with talk, chat idly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰléwō</i>.</p><p>The EIEC suggests that the term ultimately derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰlew-</i>.</p><p>Alternatively, according to the LIV, the term derives from <i>&#42;bʰléwH-ti</i>, an athematic root present to <i>&#42;bʰlewH-</i> (“to overflow”).</p><p>Beekes does note the comparison with Latin <i>fluō</i> (“to flow”), and also suggests a root of the shape <i>&#42;bʰlewH-</i>. However, he additionally proposes a possible relation to Ancient Greek <i>βλύω</i> (blúō), which he argues may indicate Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ravenna grass (<i>Saccharum ravennae</i> or <i>Erianthus ravennae</i>)</li></ol><p>For this word, DELG assumes <i>&#42;φληϝ-ο-</i> (&#42;phlēw-o-), beside <i>&#42;φλωϝ-ο-</i> (&#42;phlōw-o-) for <i>φλόος</i> (phlóos, “rind, skin, slough”). Often derived from the verb <i>φλέω</i> (phléō, “to overflow, flood”), but Chantraine adds the word may just as well be a loan; this would agree with Beekes's suggestion of a Pre-Greek origin of the noun.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idle talk, nonsense</li><li>babbler, chatterbox</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin in view of its connection with <i>φλέδων</i> (phlédōn, “babbler”), which shows nasalization. Other words belong to this group, like <i>φληνύω</i> (phlēnúō, “to babble”) and <i>φλανύσσει</i> (phlanússei, “to talk nonsense”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>θλίβω</i> (thlíbō): to press, bruise</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps in some way related to <i>flīgō</i> (“to strike down”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleyǵ-</i>; see the Latin for more cognates. However, the exact reconstruction of the root is unclear. The linguist Romain Garnier suggests that it could derive from an older perfect participle <i>&#42;πε-φλιγ-ϝώς</i> (&#42;pe-phlig-wṓs), which would ultimately derive from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>petty, paltry, trivial</li><li>indifferent, bad</li><li>shabby, plain (of personal appearance)</li></ol><p>No clear etymology, but perhaps a Pre-Greek word. The adjective does look similar to synonymous <i>φαῦλος</i> (phaûlos, “cheap, paltry, easy”); both could have dissimilated from an original form <i>&#42;φλαῦλος</i> (&#42;phlaûlos). Similar forms and meanings are shown by Old Norse <i>blautr</i> (“weak; wet”) and Lithuanian <i>biaũrus</i> (“filthy, appalling, ugly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Opening veins.</li></ol><p>From <i>φλέψ</i> (phléps, “blood vessel”) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of phlegm</li></ol><p>From <i>φλέγμᾰ</i> (phlégmă) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doorpost, doorjamb</li><li>lintel</li><li>standing post in which a windlass works</li><li>support</li></ol><p>A technical word without etymology, so possibly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blazing, flamy, fiery</li></ol><p>From <i>φλόξ</i> (phlóx) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) bark of trees, especially smooth bark</li><li>husk or skin of certain fruits</li><li>membrane enclosing the eggs of certain animals</li><li>tissue from which spiders spin their webs</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) of superficial or useless coverings, redundancy</li></ol><p>Formally, it belongs to <i>φλέω</i> (phléō, “to abound”) together with <i>φλόος</i> (phlóos, “skin, slough”). Semantically, the connection with <i>φλέω</i> (phléō) is clear in <i>ὑπέρφλοιος</i> (hupérphloios, “luxuriant, succulent”). Frisk thinks that the rind may have received its name from its being rich in flesh and juice, as opposed to the wood underneath. However, it could also be from the same Pre-Greek of <i>φλέως</i> (phléōs, “Ravenna grass”), which is suggested for <i>φλέω</i> (phléō) too.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>phlomis, a herbaceous plant of the species <i>Phlomis samia</i></li></ol><p>From <i>φλόμος</i> (phlómos, “mullein”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any confused roaring noise, especially of waves and battle</li></ol><p>The ending is reminiscent of <i>θόρυβος</i> (thórubos), <i>κόναβος</i> (kónabos) and <i>ῥοῖβδος</i> (rhoîbdos). Further analysis is unknown. Relation with <i>φλιδάω</i> (phlidáō, “to overflow with moisture”) is semantically not quite obvious.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk nonsense, babble idly, maunder, make jokes, play the fool</li><li>to prate against</li></ol><p>An enlargement of <i>φλύ̆ω</i> (phlú̆ō, “to be full of juice, thrive”), though the morphological details are unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonsense, foolery</li></ol><p><i>φλύ̆ᾱρος</i> (phlú̆āros, “silly talk”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boil; small abscess</li></ol><p>From the Pre-Greek root <i>&#42;φλῠκτ-</i> (&#42;phlŭkt-) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs). Compare <i>φλῠ́κταινᾰ</i> (phlŭ́ktaină, “blister”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flaming, fiery</li><li>flame coloured</li></ol><p>From <i>φλόξ</i> (phlóx, “flame”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mullein (<i>Verbascum sinuatum</i>)</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>πλόμος</i> (plómos, “poison with mullein”), with the <i>φ-π</i> (ph-p) variation and semantic domain suggesting Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flame, blaze</li></ol><p>From <i>φλέγω</i> (phlégō, “burn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rind, bark, peel</li><li>skin</li></ol><p>From <i>φλέω</i> (phléō, “I abound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of bark</li></ol><p>From <i>φλοιός</i> (phloiós, “bark”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silly talk, foolery, nonsense, babble</li><li>tattler, babbler</li></ol><p>Perhaps a back-formation from <i>φλῠᾱρέω</i> (phlŭāréō, “to talk nonsense”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burn blister</li><li>blood blister</li><li>pustule</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the Pre-Greek root <i>&#42;φλῠκτ-</i> (&#42;phlŭkt-) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină). See also <i>φλῠκτῐ́ς</i> (phlŭktĭ́s, “boil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of plants, fruits</i>) to be full of juice, thrive</li><li>to boil over, bubble up, well up</li><li>to burst out</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to overflow with words, babble</li><li>to vomit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰlūō</i>, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰléwH-ti ~ &#42;bʰluH-énti</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰlewH-</i> (“to overflow”). According to Beekes, related to <i>φλέω</i> (phléō, “to overflow”).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰlew-</i> (“to vomit”), and likely of identical origin to the "bubble out" sense of Etymology 1. Compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bľuti</i> (“to vomit”), Lithuanian <i>bliáuti</i> (“to bleat, weep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) to put to flight</li><li>to terrify, alarm</li><li>to threaten</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be frightened</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be in awe of, to dread</li></ol></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>φόβος</i> (phóbos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō). From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰogʷéyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰogʷéyeti</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰegʷ-</i>. Cognate to Sanskrit <i>भाजयति</i> (bhājáyati).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearful (inspiring fear), terrifying</li><li>fearful (afraid), timid</li></ol><p>From <i>φόβος</i> (phóbos, “fear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>φοβερός</i> (phoberós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>purple</li><li>crimson</li></ol><p><i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the date palm</li><li>made of palm wood</li></ol><p>From <i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix, “date palm”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common redstart (<i>Phoenicurus phoenicurus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix, “crimson”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literal</i>) to act like a Phoenician</li><li>(vulgar,&#32;sex) to perform some kind of sexual act deemed disgraceful, although it is unclear which act</li></ol><p>From <i>Φοινίκη</i> (Phoiníkē, “Phoenicia”) + <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red-feathered</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flamingo (<i>Phoenicopterus roseus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φοῖνῑξ</i> (phoînīx, “crimson”) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “feather”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) grove of palm trees</li></ol><p>From <i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blood red, crimson, reddish</li><li>deadly, murderous</li></ol><p>Without clear etymology. Connected with <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder”) already in antiquity, but this is morphologically difficult.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Indicates repeated motion</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go back and forth, to and fro, up and down; to roam</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of people) to go to or visit (a person or place) repeatedly or regularly, frequent</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of goods) to come in regularly, be imported</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of students) to go to or attend school</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of taxes) to be collected, come in</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of sexual intercourse) to go into a person</li></ol></ol><p>Probably Pre-Greek, due to lack of solid comparanda.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>φοιτᾰ́ω</i> (phoitắō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pupil</li></ol><p>From <i>φοιτάω</i> (phoitáō, “to visit”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of reptiles</i>) horny scale (opposed to <i>λεπίς</i> (lepís), used of fish’s scales)</li><li>spot on a panther’s or leopard’s skin</li><li>(<i>in the phrase φολὶς λιθοκόλλητος</i>) a ceiling in mosaic work</li><li>a bandage</li></ol><p>An o-grade term of unclear origin. Frisk derives the term from the same hypothetical root as <i>φελλός</i> (phellós, “cork”), though Beekes is skeptical of this and prefers Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>epithet of Thersites, perhaps meaning bandy-legged</li></ol><p>Traditionally supposed to be related to <i>φάλος</i> (phálos, “horn of a helmet”) and connected with Sanskrit <i>ह्वरते</i> (hvarate, “to diverge, to go crooked”). However, Furnée assumes that this word is identical with <i>&#42;βολκός</i> (&#42;bolkós) of <i>Βόλκων</i> (Bólkōn, “name of a Syracusan”); in that case, the variation "φ-/β-" would point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murderer</li></ol><p>From <i>φόνος</i> (phónos), the <i>o</i>-grade of <i>θείνω</i> (theínō, “kill”), +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murderess</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to murder, kill, slay</li></ol><p>From <i>φονεύς</i> (phoneús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>murdering, slaughtering</li></ol><p>From <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder”) +&lrm; <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō, “to kill”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp, pointed</li></ol><p>No convincing etymology. Furnée compares Cretan Greek <i>φάγρος</i> (phágros, “whetstone”) and <i>φοῦσκος</i> (phoûskos, “with pointed head”), assuming a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type of fish, perhaps the minnow</li></ol><p>From <i>φοξός</i> (phoxós, “sharp, pointed”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act</li><li>(<i>from the active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>carrying</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>gestation</li></ol><li>bringing in of money, payment</li><li>bringing forth, productiveness</li></ol><li>(<i>from the passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>being borne or carried along, motion</li><li>rapid motion, rush</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) impulse; tendency; line of thought or action</li><li>time, occasion</li></ol><li>(<i>as a thing</i>) that which is borne</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>load, freight, burden</li><li>rent, tribute</li><li>that which is brought forth, fruit, produce, crop</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring, bear, carry”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing on one's way, forwarding</li><li>favourable (<i>wind</i>)</li><li>bringing in, productive</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “carry, bring”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>frequentative of <i>φέρω</i> (phérō):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>bear repeatedly or habitually</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>bear constantly, wear (<i>of clothing, armor, etc.</i>)</li><li>bear, hold, have, possess (<i>of a certain feature or quality of mind or body</i>)</li><li>bear, suffer</li><li>extend, last (<i>of time</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) be borne along, be stormtossed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>be carried away, be shifted</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) fetch for oneself, fetch regularly</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bear, carry; to bring, fetch”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, frequentative verbal suffix); from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰoréyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰoréyeti</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>भारयति</i> (bhāráyati).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hide of thick-skinned animals, especially of swine</li></ol><p>Without cognates neither in Greek nor in other languages. A remote similarity is shown by Old Norse <i>bǫrkr</i> (“bark of a tree”) and German <i>Borke</i> (“bark”). Yet, the variant <i>πυρίνη</i> (purínē) shows that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) forage, fodder, food, booty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰorgʷā́</i> and cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀡𐀣</i> (po-qa /⁠pʰorgʷā⁠/); equivalent to <i>φέρβω</i> (phérbō, “to feed”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun suffix). The first element is of unknown origin; Beekes calls it an agricultural word, which could imply a substrate source. Another suggestion is Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰergʷ-</i> (“to feed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bearer, carrier</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “I bear”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix for person concerned).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sedan chair</li><li>sack</li></ol><p><i>φορά</i> (phorá, “carrying”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon, “instrumental suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>λευκόν</i> (leukón, “white”), <i>πολιόν</i> (polión, “grey”), <i>ῥυσόν</i> (rhusón, “wrinkled”).</li></ol><p>Furnée connects <i>Φόρκυς</i> (Phórkus), <i>Πόρκος</i> (Pórkos) and <i>φαρκίς</i> (pharkís, “wrinkle”). According to Beekes, definitely Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basket for carrying corn</li><li>measure of corn</li><li>mat</li><li>seaman's cloak of coarse plaited stuff</li><li>sieve</li></ol><p>Unclear. The traditional connection with <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring”) seems difficult to combine with the varying meanings, which are all connected with the notion of "twining". Schulze therefore connected <i>φάραι</i> (phárai, “to weave, plait”) and <i>φᾶρος</i> (phâros, “piece of cloth”). Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐍂𐌼𐍃</i> (barms, “lap, bosom”) agrees phonetically with this word, but is also interpreted differently.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to levy tribute from</li></ol><p>From <i>φορολόγος</i> (phorológos, “levying tribute”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>levying tribute, gathering tax</li></ol><p>From <i>φόρος</i> (phóros, “tribute”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to gather”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load up, load</li></ol><p>From <i>φόρτος</i> (phórtos, “load, charge”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>load, burden</li><li>freight, cargo</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wares, merchandise</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) agricultural product, crops</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) internal structure, installation (of a building)</li><li>child in the womb</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) burden, cause of worry</li></ol><p>From <i>φόρτος</i> (phórtos, “load, weight”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who carries cargo: a carrier, trafficker, merchant</li></ol><p><i>φόρτος</i> (phórtos, “cargo”) +&lrm; <i>-ηγός</i> (-ēgós, “-leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like a burden, tiresome</li><li>vulgar</li></ol><p><i>φόρτος</i>&nbsp;m (phórtos, “burden”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to load</li></ol><p>From <i>φόρτος</i> (phórtos, “load, cargo”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whatever the wind carries along</li><li>rubbish, such as collects in a farmyard or a carpenter's shop</li><li>chips or shavings, used for packing earthenware to keep it from breaking</li><li>mishmash of all kinds of meat</li></ol><p>From <i>φορῡ́νω</i> (phorū́nō, “to be mingled”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing on one's way, forwarding</li><li>favourable (<i>wind</i>)</li><li>bringing in, productive</li></ol><p>From <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “carry, bring”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be mingled, stained, defiled</li></ol><p>The formation with the suffix <i>-ύνω</i> (-únō) is similar to <i>μολύνω</i> (molúnō, “to stain, defile”) and <i>παλύνω</i> (palúnō, “to strew, sprinkle”). The element <i>φορυ-</i> (phoru-) cannot be analyzed. Perhaps it belongs to <i>φύρω</i> (phúrō, “to mix, defile, mingle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure, bright, radiant</li></ol><p>Unexplained.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φοῖβος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phoebus</li></ol><p>From <i>φοῖβος</i> (phoîbos, “pure, bright, radiant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple or crimson</li><li>palm tree</li><li>date (the fruit of the date palm)</li><li>(<i>mythology</i>) phoenix, the mythical bird derived from Egyptian mythology</li><li>(<i>music</i>) A guitar-like instrument invented by the Phoenicians</li></ol><p>The etymology surrounding all senses remains somewhat unclear. The fact that the dye came exclusively from Phoenicia assures some connection. However, it is unclear if it comes from native Greek or is a Phoenician loanword. The relationship in the sense of "mythical bird" and Egyptian <i>bnw</i> (“phoenix, Bennu bird”) is clear, but some have argued for a Semitic source (possibly Phoenician), perhaps related to the "dye" sense and the bird's red plumage. It has been argued both that the name of the tree derives from the bird and vice versa; it may be significant that Egyptian <i>bnw</i> can also mean both a mythical bird <i>and</i> the fruit of the date tree. Regardless of the etymology, the word traces back to the Mycenaean era, as evidenced by <i>𐀡𐀛𐀐</i> (po-ni-ke) and <i>𐀡𐀛𐀑𐀠</i> (po-ni-ki-pi) (most likely date palm). Compare Hebrew <i>פֻּוָּה</i> (puwwā, “a clan's name”) and Arabic فُوَّه (fuwwah, “madder”), a plant whose root yields purple-red dye.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φοῖνιξ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoenician</li><li>Carthaginian (as descendants of Phoenicia)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoenix, a city on the Mediterranean Sea.</li></ol><p>The ethnonym is homophonous with <i>φοῖνιξ</i> (phoînix), the Greek name of Tyrian purple. There has been some debate as to whether the ethnonym was derived from the name of the dye or vice versa. The ethnonym is found in use in ancient Mycenaean as <i>𐀡𐀛𐀑𐀍</i> (po-ni-ki-jo). One theory is that it is derived from the genuinely Greek adjective <i>φοινός</i> (phoinós, “blood-red”). Another theory is that is borrowed from Egyptian <i>fnḫw</i> (fenkhu), probably their plural term for "woodcutters" or "carpenters", which would refer to the peoples of the land of Canaan in the region of the Levant to the north, who supplied Egypt with the cedar wood cut from their forests, although there is no evidence that the Egyptians referred to the Phoenicians by this name.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of valerian (<i>Valeriana phu</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) oven</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>furnus</i>, from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;fornos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰr̥-nós</i>, from <i>&#42;gʷʰer-</i> (“warm, hot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A fence, a screen, a breastwork</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Any means of defence, a protection</li><li>A boom placed in a harbour</li><li>A contrivance for catching fish</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>φράσσω</i> (phrássō, “I fence”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make known, point out, intimate, show</li><li>to tell, declare</li><li>to explain, interpret</li><li>to counsel, advise, suggest, bid, order</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to think, consider, ponder, muse</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to devise, plan, design, intend</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to think, suppose, believe, imagine that</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to remark, perceive, notice</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to come to know, learn, become acquainted with, see, understand</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to observe, watch, guard</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to mind, heed, take care, beware of</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Seems to go back to a stem &#42;φραδ-. Might be related to <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “wits, will”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sluice with gates</li></ol><p>From <i>φρᾰ́σσω</i> (phrắssō, “to fence in”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech</li><li>way of speaking, expression</li><li>expression, idiom, phrase</li></ol><p>May be for <i>*φράστις</i>, from the stem of <i>φράζω</i> (phrázō), <i>*φραδ-</i>, + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tis</i>. Note the derivation <i>φραστικός</i> (phrastikós) and similar derivations from this stem.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fence in, hedge around, secure, fortify</li><li>to put up as a fence</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of dogs</i>) put down one's tail</li></ol><li>to stop up, block</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to bar, stop</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰrakʷjō</i>, of unclear origin, with analysis complicated by the coexistence and apparent confluence of <i>φρακ-</i> (phrak-) and <i>φαρκ-</i> (phark-) in the verb's derivatives. The traditional connection with Latin <i>farciō</i> (“to stuff, cram”) and <i>frequēns</i> (“crowded, frequent”) and subsequent derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰrekʷ-</i> (“to crowd together”) is quite uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>member of a community: clansman, kinsman, citizen, "brother" in the non-familial sense (as is usually expressed by "brethren")</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰrā́tēr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰréh₂tēr</i>. The old kin sense of "brother" has been assumed by <i>ἀδελφός</i> (adelphós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>artificial well</li><li>tank, cistern, reservoir</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰrḗwər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰréh₁wr̥</i> (“well, source”) (whence Sanskrit <i>भु॒र्वन्</i> (bhurván, “restless movement of water”), Old Armenian <i>աղբիւր</i> (ałbiwr, “fountain, source”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brunnô</i>), from <i>&#42;bʰrewh₁-</i>, which is cognate with Latin <i>ferveō</i>, English <i>burn</i>, <i>brew</i>. The original vowel sequence <i>&#42;ηᾰ</i> (&#42;ēă) was changed to <i>εᾱ</i> (eā) by quantitative metathesis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poppy anemone (<i>Anemone coronaria</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>unattested by itself&#59; takes various preverbs to form verbs</li></ol><p>Stem, from zero-grade <b>φρ</b>- of vowel gradation of <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “bring, carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) the midriff, stomach and lower chest or breast</li><li>heart (the seat of emotion)</li><li>desire (seat of bodily appetites such as hunger)</li><li>wits, mind (the seat of intellect)</li><li>will; purpose</li></ol><p>Perhaps from either Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰren-</i> (“soul, mind; innards, diaphragm”), whence Old Norse <i>grunr</i> (“suspicion”), or <i>&#42;bʰren-</i> (“front edge”), whence e.g. Latin <i>frōns</i> (“forehead, front; character”), Old East Norse <i>brant</i> (“precipice”). See also Latin <i>rēn</i> (“kidney”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;grěnь</i> (“pus”), of disputed connection.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>χάρακες</i> (khárakes, “pales”).</li></ol><p>According to Frisk, from <i>φρίσσω</i> (phríssō, “to be rough or bristly on the surface”). However, Furnée compares <i>βρίκελοι</i> (bríkeloi, “beams of a standing loom”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin. Probably related also to <i>φόρκες</i> (phórkes, “pales”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shuddering, shivering</li><li>shivering fear</li><li>ruffling</li><li>frost</li></ol><p>Extension of <i>φρίξ</i> (phríx, “ruffling, bristling, shivering”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruffling</li><li>bristling</li><li>shivering</li></ol><p>Uncertain, connected with <i>φρῑ́σσω</i> (phrī́ssō, “to be rough; to shiver”). Compare also <i>φρίκες</i> (phríkes) or <i>φόρκες</i> (phórkes, “pales”), and <i>βρίκελοι</i> (bríkeloi, “the beams of a standing loom”). Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰérǵʰs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerǵʰ-</i> (“to raise”); compare Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;brixs</i> (“hill”). Possibly from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be rough</li><li>to shiver, shudder, tremble</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>φρίξ</i> (phríx, “shivering”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a whip</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>flagellum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blockage, stoppage, stopping up;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(medical) intestinal blockage;</li></ol><li>enclosure, palisade, fence, hedge;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hedge (nickname for a man w. thick beard);</li><li>parapet (of a bridge);</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(by extension) fortification, shelter;</li></ol></ol><li>division</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>understanding, wise, shrewd</li></ol><p>From <i>φρᾰ́ζω</i> (phrắzō, “to show”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φραζώμεθα</i> (phrazṓmetha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indicative, expressive</li><li>eloquent</li></ol><p>From <i>φράζω</i> (phrázō, “tell”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) tribe; clan</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) brotherhood</li><li>political subdivision of the phyle</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) curia</li><li>conspiracy; plot</li></ol><p>From <i>φρᾱ́τηρ</i> (phrā́tēr, “clansman, kinsmen”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā). Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bràtrьja</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>φρέᾱρ</i> (phréār)</li></ol><p><i>φρέᾱρ</i> (phréār) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>φρεᾱτῐαῖος</i> (phreātĭaîos, “belonging to a well or tank”)</li></ol><p><i>φρέᾱρ</i> (phréār) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tank, cistern, or reservoir</li><li>an opening in a raft</li></ol><p><i>φρέᾱρ</i> (phréār) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to a well or tank</li></ol><p><i>φρεᾱτῐ́ᾱ</i> (phreātĭ́ā) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>falling into a well</li></ol><p><i>φρέᾱρ</i> (phréār) +&lrm; <i>-ῐσμός</i> (-ĭsmós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who digs a well, a well-sinker</li></ol><p><i>φρέᾱρ</i> (phréār) +&lrm; <i>ὀρῠ́κτης</i> (orŭ́ktēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a machine for raising water, a swipe, a waterwheel</li></ol><p><i>φρέᾱρ</i> (phréār) +&lrm; <i>τῠ́πᾰνον</i> (tŭ́pănon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like a well</li></ol><p><i>φρέᾱρ</i> (phréār) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inflammation of the brain</li></ol><p>From <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>what makes one shudder, abhorrent, awful</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>φρίσσω</i> (phríssō, “to shiver, shudder, tremble”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φρονέουσι</i> (phronéousi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think, to have understanding, to be wise, prudent</li><li>to be minded in a certain way, to mean, intend, purpose</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with a neuter adjective</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to have certain thoughts towards someone</li><li>(preceded by <i>μέγα</i> (méga)) to be high-minded, to have high thoughts, to be sprited and bold</li><li>to agree, to side with</li></ol></ol><li>to have a thing in one's mind, mind, take heed</li><li>to be in possession of one's senses, to be sensible, to be alive</li></ol><p>From <i>o</i>-grade of the root of <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “mind”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to consider, to take thought</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to consider, to ponder</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to take thought for something, give heed to something</li></ol><p>From <i>φροντίς</i> (phrontís, “thought”) +&lrm; <i>-ι̯ω</i> (-i̯ō), superficially containing <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō). Related to <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thought; care; attention</li></ol><p>From φρονέω (phronéō, “to think”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) the duty of watching, guarding; lookout</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) a body of persons whose duty is to guard: garrison, outpost</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) an instance of being stationed at an outpost</li></ol><p>From <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “forwards”) +&lrm; <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō, “to see”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā, action noun suffix), with shift of aspiration from the initial of <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō) to the initial of <i>προ-</i> (pro-); compare <i>φρουρός</i> (phrourós, “watchman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to keep watch, be on guard</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to watch, guard</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to watch for</li><li>(transitive,&#32;middle voice) to be on one's guard against, beware of</li><li>(transitive,&#32;Koine) to keep</li></ol><p>From <i>φρουρός</i> (phrourós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watcher; watchman; guard</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>&#42;προὁρός</i> (&#42;prohorós), from <i>προ-</i> (pro-, “forwards”) +&lrm; <i>ὁρᾰ́ω</i> (horắō, “to see”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, action noun suffix), with initial <i>φ</i> (ph) resulting from the confluence of <i>π</i> (p) with medial <i>῾</i> (h).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hill-fort, fortress, citadel</li><li>prison</li><li>garrison</li></ol><p><i>φρουρός</i> (phrourós, “watchman”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) gone, fled, departed</li><li>undone, ruined</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) gone, vanished</li></ol><p>Contracted form of <i>προ-</i> (pro-) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way, road, path”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gathering of firewood</li></ol><p>From <i>φρυγανίζω</i> (phruganízō, “to gather firewood”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to the class of the undershrubs</li></ol><p>From <i>φρῡ́γανον</i> (phrū́ganon, “undershrub”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “like, full of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>roasted</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firebrand, torch</li><li>beacon, signal-fire</li><li>roasted bean used as a lot for voting</li></ol><p>From <i>φρύγω</i> (phrúgō, “to roast”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mind, spirit</li><li>thought, purpose, will</li><li>highmindedness, resolution, pride</li><li>presumption, arrogance</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) synonym of <i>φρένες</i> (phrénes, “heart, breast”)</li></ol><p>From <i>φρονέω</i> (phronéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prudence, practical wisdom</li></ol><p>From <i>φρονέω</i> (phronéō, “to think”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis); the former from <i>φρήν</i> (phrḗn, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dry stick, firewood</li><li>undershrub</li></ol><p>From <i>φρῡ́γω</i> (phrū́gō, “to roast”) +&lrm; <i>-ανον</i> (-anon), as dry firewood was traditionally used to start fires and roast food.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel for roasting barley</li><li>stick to stir barley while roasting</li></ol><p>From <i>φρύγω</i> (phrúgō, “to roast”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roast</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerǵ-</i> ~ <i>&#42;bʰreǵ-</i> (“to roast, fry”), though the phonetics are irregular, suggesting a loanword from a third unidentified party. Cognate with Latin <i>frigo</i> and Sanskrit <i>भृज्जति</i> (bhṛjjati, “to roast, grill, fry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toad</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰerH-</i> (“grey, brown”), based on the brown color of many toad species, and compared with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;brūnaz</i> (“brown”). With reduplication, compare Sanskrit <i>बभ्रु</i> (babhru, “mongoose”), Avestan <i>𐬠𐬀𐬡𐬭𐬀</i> (baβra, “beaver”), Latin <i>fiber</i> (“beaver”) and Lithuanian <i>bẽbras</i> (“beaver”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰébʰrus</i>. However, Ringe doubts such existence of a root, and the variation between <i>φρυν-</i> and <i>φρουν-</i> rather points to a Pre-Greek word, according to Beekes.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φρύνη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Mnesarete</i>, a courtesan, nicknamed Phryne</li></ol><p>From <i>φρύνη</i> (phrúnē, “toad”), a nickname given due to her yellowish complexion.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of human</i>) Growth, stature, especially fine growth or noble stature.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of animals, plants, objects</i>) Original form.</li></ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Nature, genius.</li><li>The flower or prime of age.</li><li>Substance.</li><li>μερόπων φυή: the race of men.</li><li>Produce of a year, harvest.</li></ol><p>From <i>φύω</i> (phúō, “to grow, to be... by nature”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>φύω</i> (phúō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰúyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuHyéti</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i> (“to appear, become, rise up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who flees from his or her country</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>voluntarily: runaway, fugitive</li><li>by legal sentence: exile</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>φεύγω</i> (pheúgō, “to flee”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flight; retreat; escape</li><li>exile; banishment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰugā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰugéh₂</i>. Equivalent to <i>φεύγω</i> (pheúgō, “flee”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, action noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Elean form of <i>φυγαδεύω</i> (phugadeúō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to banish, exile</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to live in banishment, exile</li></ol><p>From <i>φυγάς</i> (phugás) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>φεύγω</i> (pheúgō)</li></ol><p>A nasal-infixed variant of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰéugō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰewg-</i> (“to flee”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to watch, guard, protect, defend</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lie in wait, ambush</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to watch for, wait for</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to maintain, preserve, cherish</li><li>to be on guard against; to shun, avoid</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;middle voice) to take heed, take care, be on guard</li><li>(transitive,&#32;middle voice) to keep in mind, remember</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰuláťťō</i>, equal to <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “watcher, guard”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō). Further etymology is unknown. According to Beekes, both words are likely Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>φῠλᾰ́σσω</i> (phŭlắssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a union of individuals into a community</li><li>a union based on descent: tribe, clan</li><li>a union based on location: county</li><li>a division of soldiers</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuH-lom</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i> (“to be, grow”). By surface analysis, an old derivative from <i>φῦναι</i> (phûnai), active infinitive of an aorist form <i>ἔφῡν</i> (éphūn) of <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “to bring forth”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φυλή)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phyle; Fyli, Attica, Greece</li></ol><p>From the noun φυλή (phulḗ) ("tribe").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of wild olive but distinguished from <i>κότινος</i> (kótinos)</li></ol><p>Without etymology. Furnée compares <i>φιλύκη</i> (philúkē, “alatern”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detention; custody</li><li>watch of the night</li><li>a guard; garrison</li></ol><p>From <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Fortified, guarded outpost, garrison.</li><li>Safeguard, protection, amulet; phylactery</li></ol><p>From <i>φῠλᾰ́σσω</i> (phŭlắssō, to protect) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) Epic and Ionic form of <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salad, a food made of a mixture of vegetables</li></ol><p>From <i>φῠ́λλον</i> (phŭ́llon, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like leaves, leaflike, foliate</li><li>belonging to leaves</li><li>having petalled flowers</li></ol><p><i>φῠ́λλον</i> (phŭ́llon, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “-like”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hart's tongue (<i>Asplenium scolopendrium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to blow; to puff</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to blow out; to extinguish</li></ol><p>From <i>φῦσα</i> (phûsa, “bellows”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bladder, bubble</li><li>(<i>music</i>) a kind of pipe</li><li>synonym of <i>ἁλικάκκαβος</i> (halikákkabos)</li><li>bolus, pill</li></ol><p>From <i>φῦσα</i> (phûsa, “bladder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blowpipe or tube, an instrument for blowing</li><li>bellows</li><li>one who blows a pipe or bellows</li><li>kind of whale</li><li>blowhole or spiracle of whales</li></ol><p>From <i>φῡσάω</i> (phūsáō, “to blow”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(nominalized,&#32;Byzantine) natural sciences; natural philosophy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>natural, produced or caused by nature, inborn, native</li><li>physical, having to do with the study of the material world</li></ol><p>From <i>φύσις</i> (phúsis) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) synonym of <i>φυσιολογίᾱ</i> (phusiologíā)</li></ol><p>From <i>φύσις</i> (phúsis) +&lrm; <i>γνώμη</i> (gnṓmē) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the practice of judging a person's character by their features</li></ol><p>From <i>φυσιογνώμων</i> (phusiognṓmōn, “judging of a person's character by their features”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inquiry into natural causes and phenomena</li></ol><p>From <i>φύσις</i> (phúsis) +&lrm; <i>-λογίᾱ</i> (-logíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>epithet</i>) pot-belly, fatso</li></ol><p><i>φύσκη</i> (phúskē, “sausage”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a light pastry, type of barley cake whereof the dough has been kneaded only lightly</li></ol><p>From <i>φῦσα</i> (phûsa, “wind, bellows”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós). See φῦσα for more cognates, perhaps ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pus-</i> (“to blow, swell”), imitative of the sound of dough rising and blowing during baking.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one who bears fruit, who nourishes (<i>commonly as an epithet for Poseidon or Zeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φῠτόν</i> (phŭtón, “plant; creature”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́λῐος</i> (-ắlĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plant (place growing thing in soil)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to bring about, cause, prepare</li></ol><p>From <i>φυτόν</i> (phutón) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to plants</li></ol><p>From <i>φυτόν</i> (phutón) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plant; tree</li><li>creature</li><li>child; descendant</li></ol><p>From <i>φῠτός</i> (phŭtós, “natural; which has grown naturally”), from <i>φύω</i> (phúō, “to generate; cause to grow”). Via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i> cognate with Russian <i>быльё</i> (bylʹjó), <i>были́нка</i> (bylínka), <i>чернобы́льник</i> (černobýlʹnik), among others.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>natural; which has grown naturally</li><li>(poetic,&#32;of art) crafted by nature, not human hand</li><li>(Koine,&#32;of arable land) fertile; fruitful</li></ol><p>From <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “to grow; to become; to be”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>den, lair</li><li>schoolhouse</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>γωλεός</i> (gōleós), <i>εἰλεός</i> (eileós) and <i>στελεός</i> (steleós). Frisk connects the word with Old Norse <i>ból</i> (“layer, nest of animals”) but this theory is now rejected. Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin in view of the ending <i>-εος</i> (-eos), as well as its late attestation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of animals</i>) to lurk in a hole or den</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of a disease) to lurk</li></ol><p>From <i>φωλεός</i> (phōleós, “den, lair”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Aeolic form of <i>φωνήεις</i> (phōnḗeis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to produce a sound or tone</li><li>to speak loud or clearly; to speak, give utterance, voice (<i>of men</i>)</li><li>to call by name, call</li><li>to speak, tell of</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “sound”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound</li><li>usually of the human voice: voice, cry, yell</li><li>the voice or cry of animals</li><li>any articulate sound (especially vowels)</li><li>speech, discourse</li><li>language</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰōnā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰoh₂-néh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to speak”) (whence <i>φημί</i> (phēmí, “to say, speak”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endowed with speech, vocal</li><li>(<i>of a lyre</i>) musical</li><li>(<i>of a song</i>) sounding</li><li>(with <b>λόγος</b>) clear</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) consisting only as vowels</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ)</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ)</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vocal (pertaining to the voice or speech&#59; uttered or modulated by the voice)</li><li>endowed with speech</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνέω</i> (phōnéō, “to speak, produce a sound”)) + <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight, quarrel, war, dispute about words</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dispute about words</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνομᾰχέω</i> (phōnomăkhéō, “to dispute about words”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “word”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Aeolic form of <i>φωνῆεν</i> (phōnêen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contracted form of <i>φωνᾶεν</i> (phōnâen), Doric form of <i>φωνῆεν</i> (phōnêen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the power of speech</li><li>(Koine,&#32;grammar,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the plural) a vowel (α, ε, η, ι, ο, υ or ω)</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνήεις</i> (phōnḗeis, adjective): as a noun, a substantivisation of its neuter forms, in elliptical use for <i>φωνῆεν &#91;γρᾰ́μμᾰ&#93;</i> (phōnêen &#91;grắmmă&#93;, “vocal [letter]”); as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>endowed with speech, vocal</li><li>(<i>of a lyre</i>) musical</li><li>(<i>of a song</i>) sounding</li><li>(with <b>λόγος</b>) clear</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) consisting only as vowels</li></ol><p>From <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contracted form of <i>φωνῆεν</i> (phōnêen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which gives light, as the sun or stars</li><li>splendor, radiance</li></ol><p>From φῶς.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>That which brings light, light-bearing</li><li>torch bearing (often as an epithet of a god or priestess)</li></ol><p>From <i>φῶς</i> (phôs, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “bearing”), from <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “I carry”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φωσφόρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the star that brings the light; a name for planet Venus.</li></ol><p>From <i>φῶς</i> (phôs, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “bringing”), from <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “bring”). Compare <i>Ἑωσφόρος</i> (Heōsphóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shining, bright (of the sun, sky)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) clear, distinct</li></ol><p>From <i>φῶς</i> (phôs) +&lrm; <i>-εινός</i> (-einós).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φωτεινός)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Photinus</li></ol><p>From <i>φωτεινός</i> (phōteinós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Light, illumination</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>combining form of <i>φῶς</i> (phôs, “light”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fear, terror, alarm, fright, panic</li><li>the act of fleeing: flight, retreat</li><li>awe, reverence</li><li>that which causes fear: terror</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰógʷos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰógʷos</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰegʷ-</i> (“to run, flee”). See <i>φέβομαι</i> (phébomai, “to flee”) for more.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Φόβος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Phobos (the Greek god of fear)</li></ol><p>Personification of <i>φόβος</i> (phóbos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>murder, slaughter</li><li>(<i>law</i>) murder(er), homicide</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰónos</i> (“murder”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰónos</i> (“killing, slaughter”). Related to <i>θείνω</i> (theínō, “to strike”) and <i>ἔπεφνον</i> (épephnon, “to slay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is carried, load</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) burden</li><li>that which is worn: ornament, dress</li></ol><p><i>φορέω</i> (phoréō, “to bear constantly, wear”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wearing (<i>of clothing</i>)</li><li>synonym of <i>φορᾱ́</i> (phorā́, “being borne”)</li></ol><p>From <i>φορέω</i> (phoréō, “to bear repeatedly or habitually; to wear clothing”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, action noun suffix). Doublet of <i>φόρεσῐς</i> (phóresĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;poetic,&#32;music) lyre; phorminx</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tribute</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring, carry”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Given the comparison with Sanskrit <i>भर॑</i> (bhára), the term may be derived from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰóros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰóros</i> (“load, burden”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>load, freight, cargo</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) heavy load, burden</li><li>vulgar stuff, rubbish, balderdash</li></ol><p>From <i>o</i>-grade of <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to carry”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>φᾰ́ος</i> (phắos, “light”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to the light (of day)</li></ol><p>From <i>φόως</i> (phóōs) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>panicked retreat from battlefield, rout</li></ol><p>From <i>φεύγω</i> (pheúgō, “flee”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă, feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard; sentry</li></ol><p>Same source as <i>φῠλᾰ́σσω</i> (phŭlắssō, “to guard; to watch”), but of unknown further etymology. According to Beekes, both words are clear examples of Pre-Greek. Note in particular the suffix <i>-αξ</i> (-ax).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act of watching, guarding</li><li>a security</li></ol><p>From <i>φῠλᾰ́σσω</i> (phŭlắssō, “to guard”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) foliage</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of leaves</li></ol><p>From <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leaf</li><li>plant</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) foliage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰolh₃-yom</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰleh₃-</i> (“leaf, blossom, flower”). Cognates include Latin <i>folium</i>, Old Armenian <i>բողբոջ</i> (bołboǰ), and English <i>blossom</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle cry</li><li>din of battle</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>φῡλή</i> (phūlḗ, “division of soldiers”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix something dry with something wet</li><li>to soil, defile, sully</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to mingle together, confuse, mix up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to mingle with, have dealings with (<i>people</i>)</li></ol><p>Uncertain, with Beekes suggesting Pre-Greek origin based on the opaque phonology and semantic domain. May be related to <i>φορῡ́νω</i> (phorū́nō, “to be mingled”) and/or <i>πορφῡ́ρω</i> (porphū́rō, “to heave, surge up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) he who has sprung forth/been brought forth, he who has been begotten, he who has been born</li><li>(<i>metonymically, generally with the definite article present</i>): son, (male) child, (male) descendant</li></ol><p>Substantivization/nominalization of the participle φῡ́ς (phū́s).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) they who have brought forth/produced/generated/begot; they who have created/made</li><li>(<i>metonymically, generally with the definite article present</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the marked state</i>): fathers, forefathers/(male) ancestors</li><li>(<i>in the unmarked state</i>): parents, ancestors</li></ol></ol><p>Substantivization/nominalization of the participle form φῡ́σαντες (phū́santes), the nominative/vocative plural of <i>φῡ́σᾱς</i> (phū́sās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) "he who has brought forth/produced/generated/begot, he who has created/made, he who has caused to be/exist"</li><li>(<i>metonymically, generally with the definite article present</i>): father, forefather/(male) ancestor (cf. φῡ́ς (phū́s))</li></ol><p>Substantivization/nominalization of the participle φῡ́σᾱς (phū́sās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>origin, birth</li><li>nature, quality, property</li><li>later, the nature of one's personality: temper, disposition</li><li>form, shape</li><li>that which is natural: nature</li><li>type, kind</li><li>Nature, as an entity, especially of productive power</li><li>creature</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuH-tís</i>. By surface analysis, <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “grow”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs). Compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;bytь</i> (“essence; culture”) and Sanskrit <i>भूति</i> (bhūti, “existence, being; form; might, power, prosperity; fortune”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) large intestine</li><li>blood pudding, sausage</li><li>blister or weal on the hand</li><li>gall on a plant</li></ol><p>From <i>φυσάω</i> (phusáō, “to blow, puff”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is planted, plant</li><li>rampion mignonette (<i>Reseda phyteuma</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>φυτεύω</i> (phuteúō, “to plant”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) stock, generation, race</li></ol><p>From <i>φύω</i> (phúō, “to generate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>begetter</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) father</li></ol><p>From <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “to give forth; to produce; to beget”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “agent noun suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring forth, produce, generate, cause to grow</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to beget, bear, give birth to</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to grow, arise, spring up</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;present tense) to become [<i>with</i> adjective]</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;aorist and perfect)</li><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to be by nature [<i>with</i> adjective]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be naturally disposed to, prone [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do’</small>]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) It is natural, happens naturally [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>to be one's natural lot [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone's’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰúyō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuHyéti</i>, from <i>&#42;bʰuH-</i> (“to appear, become, rise up”).</p><p>Cognate with Old Armenian <i>բոյս</i> (boys, “plant”), Sanskrit <i>भव॑ति</i> (bhávati), Avestan <i>𐬠𐬎</i> (bu), Latin <i>fuī</i> (“to have been”), Old English <i>bēon</i> (English <i>be</i>), Albanian <i>bëj</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roast, toast, parch</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰṓgō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₃g-</i> (“to bake”). Cognates include Phrygian <i>βεκος</i> (/⁠begos⁠/) and Old English <i>bacan</i> (English <i>bake</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porpoise, <i>Delphinus phocaena</i></li></ol><p>From <i>φώκη</i> (phṓkē, “seal”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seal (marine animal)</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin. According to Beekes, likely Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound made, utterance</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of a singer’s voice</li><li>of a dog’s bark and ass’s bray</li></ol><li>thing spoken, speech, language</li></ol><p><i>φωνέω</i> (phōnéō, “I produce a sound or tone”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thief</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰṓr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰṓr</i>, from the root <i>&#42;bʰer-</i> (“to carry”) (see <i>φέρω</i> (phérō)). Cognate with Latin <i>fūr</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) man, mortal</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coarse linen garment, used in Egypt</li><li>sailcloth</li></ol><p>Probably an Egyptian loanword.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) Epic form of <i>φᾰ́ος</i> (phắos, “light”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>φᾶρος</i> (phâros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large piece of cloth, web</li><li>wide cloak or mantle without sleeves</li><li>bedspread</li></ol><p>Unknown. Related to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀞𐀸𐀀</i> (pa-we-a). Perhaps from Pre-Greek, but compare Ancient Greek <i>φάρσος</i> (phársos) and Lithuanian <i>bùrė</i> (“sail”). Compare also <i>φᾰ́ρσος</i> (phắrsos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>deceitful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰwel-</i> (“to lie, deceive, bend”) and connected with Sanskrit <i>ह्वृ</i> (hvṛ, “to deviate”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zъlъ</i> (“bad”). Less likely are connections to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)gʷʰh₂el-</i> (“to stumble”) or to Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is said, word (<i>a gloss by Hesychius</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰāmə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-mn̥</i>. By surface analysis, <i>φημί</i> (phēmí) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech, talk</li><li>fame, reputation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-mis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to speak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>φεῖ</i> (pheî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>φεῖλος</i> (pheîlos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaweed</li><li>orchil made of φῦκος (phûkos) and used as rouge by Greek women</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Semitic language, such as Hebrew <i>פּוּךְ</i> (pūk, “eye-rouge, antimony”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φῦλα</i> (phûla)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a set of people or other beings</li><li>sex (male/female)</li><li>nation, race, tribe</li></ol><p>A variant of <i>φῡλή</i> (phūlḗ, “tribe”), itself related to <i>φύω</i> (phúō, “to beget”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(oncology,&#32;pathology) growth, tumor</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰuH-mn̥</i>. By surface analysis, <i>φύω</i> (phúō, “to grow”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma). Formally identical to Sanskrit <i>भूमन्</i> (bhūman, “world, territory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) pair of bellows; smithies</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) bladder</li><li>pouch of the beaver</li><li>breath, wind, blast</li><li>wind in the body, flatus</li><li>(<i>of fire</i>) stream, jet</li><li>bubble</li><li>crater of a volcano</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) calyx of φυσαλλίς (phusallís)</li><li>kind of fish found in the Nile</li></ol><p>Nominal formation in <i>σᾶ</i> (sâ), with the exact form and origin of the root unclear. Candidates include <i>φυ-</i> (phu-), <i>φυσ-</i> (phus-), <i>φυτ-</i> (phut-), or <i>φυκ-</i> (phuk-), but none of these can be shown to have existed in Greek.</p><p>Comparanda include Old Armenian <i>փուք</i> (pʻukʻ, “breath, wind, fart”) (&lt; <i>φυκ-ι̯α</i> (phuk-i̯a); however, <i>φῦσα</i> (phûsa) cannot derive from this form), Sanskrit <i>फूत्करोति</i> (phūtkaroti, “to puff, blow”) (&lt; <i>φυτ-ι̯α</i> (phut-i̯a)), <i>फुफुस</i> (phuphusa, “lungs”), as well as Latin <i>pustula</i> (“bladder”),Classical Persian پوگان (pūgān, “uterus”),Dari پوقانه (pōqāna, “urine bladder”),Iranian Persian <i>پپ</i> (pop, “lung (Qaenī dialect)”), Old Church Slavonic <i>пухати</i> (puxati, “to blow”), Sanskrit <i>पुष्यति</i> (puṣyati, “to thrive, prosper”). (&lt; <i>φυσ-</i> (phus-)).</p><p>Despite the existence of all this Indo-European material, the word may well have a Pre-Greek origin, based on the suffix of the derivative <i>φῦσιγξ</i> (phûsinx, “heel blister”), as well as the lack of solid evidence for a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰus-</i>. Regardless, the words mentioned above, as well as the Greek term, may have onomatopoeic origins.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blister on the heel caused by a burn</li><li>stalk of garlic or the outer coat of a clove or garlic</li><li>particular kind of garlic</li></ol><p>From <i>φῦσᾰ</i> (phûsă, “bellows; bladder”), with a typical Pre-Greek suffix <i>-ῐγξ</i> (-ĭnx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>φᾰ́ος</i> (phắos, “light”)</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>φάος</i> (pháos). After the contraction, the original s-stem was changed into a t-stem <i>φωτ-</i> (phōt-), following <i>ἔρως</i> (érōs), stem <i>ἐρωτ-</i> (erōt-) and <i>χρώς</i> (khrṓs), stem <i>χρωτ-</i> (khrōt-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>φῶτα</i> (phôta)</li></ol></html>:/kʰ/
<h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>κε</i> (ke) (<i>used before the rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>κημός</i> (kēmós, “muzzle”).</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>χαμόν</i> (khamón, “bent, curved”). The variation "μ/β" leads to a Pre-Greek origin. Traditionally compared also to Latin <i>hāmus</i> (“fish-hook”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give way, fall back, retreat</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hail; sleet</li><li>pimple; tubercle</li><li>knot; lump</li></ol><p>Likely from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰelh₂d-</i> (“hail; hoarfrost”). Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žȇldъ</i> (“glazed frost, sleet”), Persian ژاله (žâle, “hail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relaxation, slackening, loosening</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλάω</i> (khaláō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) drunk?</li></ol><p>Has been compared with Albanian <i>helm</i> (“poison”), which may derive from this.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small stone, pebble</li><li>gravel, rubble (<i>used in building and concrete making</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown, perhaps Pre-Greek. Probably cognate, ancestor, or descendant of Latin <i>calx</i> (“limestone, chalk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pure, unmixed wine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelis</i>; cognate with Sanskrit <i>हाला</i> (hālā, “brandy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A solution of copper sulfate or blue vitriol, used to dye leather.</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “copper”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>χάλκεος</i> (khálkeos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of copper or bronze, brazen</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of statues</i>)</li><li>(<i>χἀλκεος ἀγών</i>) a contest for a shield of brass</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) brazen, hard, stout, strong</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós, “copper”) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bronze, made of bronze</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκ(ός)</i> (khălk(ós), “bronze, copper”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>steel, hardened iron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hittite <i>𒆠𒅅𒇷𒁀</i> (ki-ik-li-ba /⁠kikliba⁠/), <i>𒆠𒅅𒇻𒁀</i> (ki-ik-lu-ba /⁠kikluba⁠/, “steel”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χάλυψ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the Chalybes</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰ́λῠψ</i> (khắlŭps, “steel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comber (<i>Serranus cabrilla</i>)</li></ol><p>In view of the characterization by Epicharmus as <i>μεγαλοχάσμων</i> (megalokhásmōn, “wide-gaping”), connection with <i>χαίνω</i> (khaínō, “to yawn, gape”) with expressive gemination has been proposed. Thompson considers a foreign derivation from Egyptian (chnā, “unknown kind of fish”), while Furnée suggests a Pre-Greek origin comparing this word with <i>χέννιον</i> (khénnion, “kind of fish”), showing alternation <i>α/ε</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mouth</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰan-</i> / <i>&#42;gʰan-</i> (“to yawn, to gape”), the same root of <i>χάσκω</i> (kháskō, “to yawn, gape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaos; the primordial state of existence</li><li>space; air</li><li>abyss; chasm</li><li>infinite darkness</li></ol><p>Likely related to <i>χαῦνος</i> (khaûnos, “porous, loose-grained”) (via an earlier form <i>χάος</i> (kháos) &lt; <i>&#42;χάϝος</i> (&#42;kháwos)), with original meaning "hole, empty space, yawning opening"; from this spring the traditional connections with <i>χάσκω</i> (kháskō, “to gape, yawn”), <i>χήμη</i> (khḗmē, “gaping; clam”). If so, then likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰeh₂-</i> (“to yawn, gape”); cognates thus include Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gōmô</i> (“gum, palate”) and Lithuanian <i>gomurỹs</i> (“palate”) (&lt; <i>&#42;gʰéh₂mr̥&#47;n-</i>). The Germanic and Baltic terms probably originally referred to "mouth" before shifting to "palate".</p><p>An alternative theory by Furnee connects the word with Proto-Georgian-Zan <i>&#42;qew-</i> (“ravine”).</p><p>Often compared to <i>χώρᾱ</i> (khṓrā, “open space; place”), but likely unrelated.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any engraved, imprinted or branded mark</li><li>stamped money, coin</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) mark, stamp, character</li><li>endorsement</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰρᾰ́σσω</i> (khărắssō, “to imprint, to stamp, engrave, carve”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pointed stake</li><li>pole, vine prop</li><li>pale used in fortifying the entrenchments of a camp</li><li>palisade</li><li>cutting, slip</li><li>a kind of bream of the genus <i>Sargus</i></li><li>name of a bandage</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>κάμαξ</i> (kámax, “vine pole, prop”) and <i>πίναξ</i> (pínax, “board, plank”), and the traditional connection with Lithuanian <i>žer̃ti</i> (“to scrape”) does not explain the Greek formation. In view of the suffix, the word is most probably Pre-Greek; see <i>χαράσσω</i> (kharássō, “to sharpen, engrave”) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>as a favor to, for the pleasure of</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beauty, elegance, charm, grace</li><li>favourable disposition towards someone: grace, favor, goodwill</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Judaism,&#32;Christianity) the grace or favor of God</li><li>a voluntary act of goodwill</li></ol><li>gratitude, thanks</li><li>influence (opposite force)</li><li>gratification, delight</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō, “to be happy”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χάρις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Charites; a Charis</li></ol><p>From the noun <i>χᾰ́ρῐς</i> (khắrĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a favourable disposition towards: grace, favor</li><li>a voluntary favourable act: favour, gift</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰρῐ́ζομαι</i> (khărĭ́zomai, “show favor”) (from <i>χᾰ́ρῐς</i> (khắrĭs, “grace”), from <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō, “to be happy”)) + <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>source of joy or delight</li><li>joy; delight</li></ol><p>From <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō, “rejoice”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy of battle; eagerness for combat</li><li>fighting</li><li>a battle</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰer-</i> (“to yearn for”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheet of paper; paper</li><li>book</li></ol><p>Two theories:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to yawn, gape, open wide</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>chiefly in Comic Poets</i>) to gape in eager expectation</li><li>to yawn from weariness, ennui or inattention</li></ol><li>(<i>less common</i>) to speak with open mouth, to utter</li><li>to open and swallow</li></ol><p>Per Rix (LIV), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₂-n</i> (“to yawn, to gape”) with inchoative suffix <i>-σκω</i> (-skō). Often derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₁y-</i> (“to yawn, gape”) despite the phonetic difficulties, in which case cognates include Latin <i>hiō</i>, Tocharian A <i>śew</i>, Tocharian B <i>kāyā</i>, Lithuanian <i>žioti</i>, Russian <i>зия́ть</i> (zijátʹ), Sanskrit <i>विजिहीते</i> (vijihīte), and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;gīnaną</i>. Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ganōną</i> (English <i>yawn</i>) has been variously derived from both roots.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hollow, chasm, gulf</li><li>open mouth (<i>of an animal</i>)</li><li>space, expanse (<i>of the sky and sea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χάσκω</i> (kháskō, “to yawn, gape”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) to defecate, shit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰed-</i>. Cognates include Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬜𐬀𐬲</i> (haδaž, “anus”), Sanskrit <i>हद्</i> (had), <i>हदति</i> (hadati, “to evacuate, discharge excrement”), Old Armenian <i>ձետ</i> (jet, “tail, penis”), Albanian <i>dhjes</i> (“to mess up”), Northern Kurdish <i>zotik</i> (“anus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>some kind of amphibious serpent or tortoise</li></ol><p>From <i>χέλυς</i> (khélus, “tortoise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tortoise</li><li>lyre</li><li>arched breast; chest</li></ol><p>Related to <i>χελώνη</i> (khelṓnē, “tortoise”); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quail, salted and eaten by the Egyptians</li><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>εἶδος ἰχθύος</i> (eîdos ikhthúos, literally “kind of fish”).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian (chennu).</p><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek in view of the similarity with <i>χάννη</i> (khánnē, “comber”). However, Thompson considers a foreign origin, comparing both words with Egyptian (chnā, “unknown kind of fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silt, debris, gravel and rubbish, brought down by torrents</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>χαράδρα</i> (kharádra, “gorge, ravine; torrent”) and thus from Pre-Greek. The older connection with <i>χαράσσω</i> (kharássō, “to carve, engrave”) must be given up.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>washbasin (for ritual handwashing)</li></ol><p>From <i>χέρνῐψ</i> (khérnĭps, “water for ritual handwashing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water for washing the hands (especially before sacrifices)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰéhrnikʷs</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀐𐀛𐀣</i> (ke-ni-qa, accusative)), equivalent to <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>νῐ́ζω</i> (nĭ́zō, “to wash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>χέρσα</i> (khérsa)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>χέρσε</i> (khérse)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dry land (as opposed to sea)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry, firm</li><li>dry, hard, barren, unfruitful</li><li>(<i>of woman</i>) barren, unfruitful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰérsos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰérsos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰers-</i> (“stand erect”). Cognate to Albanian <i>djerr</i> (“fallow land”); Latin <i>horreō</i>, horror; Sanskrit <i>हर्ष</i> (harṣa, “excitement”).</p><p>Compare the semantics of Latin <i>terra</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of liquids</i>) to pour</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to shed (of tears)</li><li>to smelt, cast (of metal)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become liquid, melt, dissolve</li></ol><li>(<i>of solids</i>) to scatter, shed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to throw up (of soil)</li><li>to shower (of spears)</li><li>to let fall, drop</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be heaped up, mounded</li><li>to move together, stream</li><li>(perfect,&#32;passive voice) to be engaged, absorbed in</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰéweti</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵʰew-</i> (“to pour”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>जुहोति</i> (juhóti), Old Armenian <i>ձեւ</i> (jew), Latin <i>fundō</i>, <i>fūtis</i>, Old English <i>ġēotan</i>, <i>guttas</i> (English <i>gut</i>), and Albanian <i>dyllë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awl</li></ol><p>From <i>χηλεύω</i> (khēleúō, “to plait, net”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>netted, plaited</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>χηλός</i> (khēlós, “large chest, coffer”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yawning, gaping</li><li>clam or mussel</li><li>kind of measure</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin, though often taken to be a verbal noun to <i>χάσκω</i> (kháskō, “to yawn”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₁y-</i> (“to gape, be wide open”); see there for more. Compare <i>χηλός</i> (khēlós, “coffer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) an affection of the eyes, when the cornea swells like a cockle-shell so as to impede sight</li></ol><p>From <i>χήμη</i> (khḗmē, “clam, cockle”) +&lrm; <i>-ωσῐς</i> (-ōsĭs, “state, condition”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goose</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰā́n</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰh₂éns</i> (“goose”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>हंस</i> (haṃsá), Latin <i>ānser</i>, Russian <i>гусь</i> (gusʹ), Old English <i>gōs</i> (English <i>goose</i>), and Albanian <i>gatë</i> (“heron”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedgehog</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰḗr</i> (“hedgehog”) and identical with Latin <i>ēr</i> (“hedgehog”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>widow</li></ol><p>This word replaced the old word for "widow" found in Latin <i>vidua</i>; a cognate of this is retained in <i>ἠΐθεος</i> (ēḯtheos, “unmarried youth”). It is assumed that the origin of this word is Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₁ro-</i> (“derelict”), from the root <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₁-</i> (“to leave behind, abandon”); compare Sanskrit <i>जहाति</i> (jahāti, “to desert, leave, resign”) and Latin <i>hērēs</i> (“heir”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>χήρᾱ</i> (khḗrā, “widow”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crosspiece</li></ol><p>From <i>χῑᾰ́ζω</i> (khīắzō, “to mark with an X”, from <i>χῖ</i> (khî, “chi”)) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>thousand</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰehliyoi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰesliyoy</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵʰes-</i>, whence <i>χείρ</i> (kheír)), as if meaning “full hand”. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सहस्र</i> (sahásra) and Latin <i>mīlle</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a she-goat</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>χῐ́μᾰρος</i> (khĭ́măros).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χίμαιρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chimera, a mythological animal</li><li>Chimera, a volcanic site in ancient Lycia, now in Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>χῐ́μαιρᾰ</i> (khĭ́mairă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a he-goat</li><li>a young he-goat (following an <i>ἔριφος</i> (ériphos))</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰímər</i>, R-stem derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeym-</i> (“winter, snow”), complementing the n-stem reflex found in <i>χειμών</i> (kheimṓn). Perhaps originally meaning "winter/year-old (goat)".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hail; hello; welcome</li><li>farewell; goodbye</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be full of cheer; to be happy</li><li>to enjoy [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> participle <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>perfect</i>) to be very glad; to enjoy [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> participle <small>‘doing something’</small>] a great deal</li><li>(on meeting or parting,&#32;imperative) be well; farewell, be glad, God speed, greetings, hail, joy(‐fully), rejoice (as a salutation)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰəřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰer-</i>. The iota in the progressive forms (present system) is a reflex of the <i>&#42;y</i> marker for the present tense added onto the root <i>χαρ-</i> (khar-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be full of cheer; to be happy</li><li>to enjoy [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> participle <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>perfect</i>) to be very glad; to enjoy [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> participle <small>‘doing something’</small>] a great deal</li><li>(on meeting or parting,&#32;imperative) be well; farewell, be glad, God speed, greetings, hail, joy(‐fully), rejoice (as a salutation)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰəřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰer-</i>. The iota in the progressive forms (present system) is a reflex of the <i>&#42;y</i> marker for the present tense added onto the root <i>χαρ-</i> (khar-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>χαίτη</i> (khaítē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loose, flowing hair; used especially of back hair</li><li>a mane of a horse or of a lion (also, metaphorically, of Aeschylus); opposed to <i>λοφιά</i></li><li>a helmet’s crest</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of trees) foliage</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰeh₂it-</i> (“curly hair”), the same root of Avestan <i>𐬔𐬀𐬉𐬯𐬀</i> (gaēsa, “curly hair”) and Middle Irish <i>gaiset</i> (“bristly hair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>khan</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>garden chervil (<i>Anthriscus cerefolium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō, “to enjoy”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mat or pallet, low bed</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the presence of variations shows that the word is from Pre-Greek and not related to <i>χαλάω</i> (khaláō, “to slacken”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small granule of hail</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰ́λᾰζᾰ</i> (khắlăză, “hail”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to slacken; to loosen</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become slack; to become loose</li><li>to open, be open</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of unknown or Pre-Greek origin, and unrelated to Armenian <i>խաղ</i> (xaġ, “game, pastime”) (a connection would require a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʰ</i>, which is unheard of for inherited words). Also sometimes compared to Sanskrit <i>खल्लते</i> (khallate, “he/she/it is being loosened”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oppress, crush</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to wound</li></ol><li>to provoke, enrage</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to be angry</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be enraged</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;poetic,&#32;late prose) to be vexed</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλεπός</i> (khalepós) +&lrm; <i>-πτω</i> (-ptō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hail-guard, one who averts hail by magical rites</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰ́λᾰζα</i> (khắlăza, “hail”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slack, loose, supple</li><li>languid, effeminate</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλάω</i> (khaláō, “to slacken, loosen”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be relaxed in tension</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλαρός</i> (khalarós, “slack, loose”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>galbanum, the resinous juice produced by plants of the genus <i>Ferula</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>חֶלְבְּנָה</i> (ḥɛlbənɔ̂), from the root <i>ח־ל־ב</i> (ḥ-l-b) related to milk, from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ḥalīb-</i> (“milk; fat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be harsh, savage</li><li>to be angry</li><li>to rage (<i>of a storm</i>)</li></ol><p><i>χαλεπός</i> (khalepós, “harsh, savage”) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>difficult, hard</li><li>hard to bear, painful, grievous</li><li>hard to deal with, cruel, harsh, stern</li><li>savage, fierce</li></ol><p>Unknown, perhaps Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difficulty, ruggedness</li><li>harshness, rigour, severity</li><li>bad temper, vice (<i>of a horse</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλεπός</i> (khalepós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with difficulty, hardly</li></ol><p><i>χᾰλεπός</i> (khălepós, “difficult”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bridle, rein, bit</li><li>part of the tackle of a ship</li><li>anything that restrains from the outer</li><li>corner of the mouth of a horse or human</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a Pre-Greek origin is probable, as it is difficult to explain "χαλ-" from Indo-European and as a suffix "-ιν-" is frequent in Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) caliph</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic خَلِيفَة (ḵalīfa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forged out of brass, of beaten brass</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós, “brass”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō, “to strike, forge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fitted with bronze</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “bronze”) +&lrm; <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “to fit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown bird mentioned by Aristotle</li><li>European roller (<i>Coracias garrulus</i>)</li><li>migratory fish, perhaps the pilchard</li><li>kind of poisonous lizard</li><li>(<i>at Lacedaemon</i>) female slave</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “copper”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs) or perhaps from the toponym <i>Χαλκίς</i> (Khalkís).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χαλκίς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chalcis</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós, “bronze”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with voice of brass, i.e. ringing strong and clear (epithet of Stentor and Cerberus)</li></ol><p>From <i>χάλκεος</i> (khálkeos, “of brass”) +&lrm; <i>-φωνος</i> (-phōnos, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coppersmith</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) smith; metalworker</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antimony, kohl</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλκηδών</i> (khalkēdṓn, “chalcedony”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) garnet</li></ol><p>From Anatolian.</p><p>Compare Arabic كَرْكَنْد (karkand), Hebrew <i>כדכד</i>, Classical Syriac <i>ܩܪܟܕܢܐ</i> (qarkeḏnā), Khotanese (kirkīyaṃ), Sanskrit <i>कर्केतन</i> (karketana, “chrysoberyl”), Old Armenian <i>կարկեհան</i> (karkehan, “a red gem”) (borrowed from Parthian <i>&#42;karkaðan</i>, to which also belong Middle Persian <small>[Book Pahlavi needed]</small> (karkēhanēn), Persian کرکهان (karkahân), کرکهن (karkahan, “amethyst”) which passed into Arabic كَرْكُهَان (karkuhān), Arabic كَرْكُهَن (karkuhan, “amethyst”)). Compare also English <i>chalcedony</i>, Latin <i>chalcēdōnius</i>, French <i>calcédoine</i>, all from Ancient Greek forms.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χαλκηδών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chalcedon</li></ol><p>Perhaps identical to the Anatolian mineral name Ancient Greek <i>χαλκηδών</i> (khalkēdṓn) (→ Arabic كَرْكَنْد (karkand)), then instead connected to <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “bronze”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) weighed down with bronze</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “bronze”) +&lrm; <i>βαρύς</i> (barús, “heavy”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standing on bronze</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós) +&lrm; <i>-βᾰ́της</i> (-bắtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A case for bronze vessels, especially for those of value</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “copper”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) armed or equipped with brass (<i>epithet of Hector and once of Sarpedon, masculine singular only</i>)</li><li>harnessed in bronze , bronze-armored (<i>epithet of Ares</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “copper, bronze”) +&lrm; <i>κορύσσω</i> (korússō, “to equip”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alloy of copper and lead</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós, “copper”) +&lrm; <i>μόλῠβδος</i> (mólŭbdos, “lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having brass cheekpieces</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós) +&lrm; <i>πᾰρειᾱ́</i> (păreiā́) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copper-mine</li><li>foundry</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκουργός</i> (khălkourgós, “copper miner”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copper-miner</li><li>worker in bronze, bronzesmith, coppersmith</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>working copper</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰλκός</i> (khălkós, “copper, bronze”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bronze-clad</li></ol><p><i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “bronze”) +&lrm; <i>χιτών</i> (khitṓn, “clothing”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>χάλκεος</i> (khálkeos, “brazen”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copper</li><li>bronze (copper alloyed with tin)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) anything made of metal</li><li>a copperware or bronzeware object</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a cauldron</li><li>an urn; especially a cinerary urn (containing ashes of the dead)</li><li>a bronze plate</li></ol><li>copper money</li><li>the chalcus (variously equivalent to <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>6</sub></small>, <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>8</sub></small>, <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>10</sub></small> or <small><sup>1</sup><big>⁄</big><sub>12</sub></small> of an obolus, depending on the city and time period)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a copper coin</li><li>a unit of weight</li></ol></ol><p>Unknown. Has been compared to Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želězo</i> (“iron”), Latin <i>ferrum</i>, and Hittite (ḫapalki-). Perhaps related to <i>κάλχη</i> (kálkhē, “purple”). Ultimately, Proto-Indo-European origin seems unlikely and the word is probably a borrowing from an eastern substrate.</p><p>Cognate to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀏𐀏𐀩𐀀</i> (ka-ka-re-a), <i>𐀏𐀒</i> (ka-ko, “copper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a metallic sounding stone</li></ol><p>From <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “copper”) +&lrm; <i>-φωνος</i> (-phōnos, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>copper-ore, worked in Cyprus</li><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) rock alum</li><li>crown daisy (<i>Glebionis coronaria</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χάλκη</i> (khálkē, “purple dye”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the ground</li></ol><p>From the same Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(dʰ)ǵʰm̥-</i>, zero grade of <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i> (compare <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn)), as found in <i>χαμᾶζε</i> (khamâze) and <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the earth, near the ground</li><li>to the earth/ground</li></ol><p>From a case form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i> (“earth”) (whence also <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “earth”)), traditionally taken as a dative. Ringe identifies this as a fossilized allative/directive (Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰm̥m-éh₂</i>) suffixed with the "hic-et-nunc particle" <i>-i</i> (Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰm̥m-éh₂-i</i>) (also optionally attached to the locative of any noun); if so, then cognate with Old Hittite <i>𒁖𒈾𒀀</i> (taknā), which lacks the particle. Ringe adduces this correspondence as evidence for reconstructing the allative for Proto-Indo-European. Compare other cognate forms with the same semantics: Sanskrit <i>क्ष्मया</i> (kṣmayā́), Sanskrit <i>ज्मया</i> (jmayā́) (instrumental), Sanskrit <i>क्षमा</i> (kṣamā́) (also instrumental), and Sanskrit <i>क्षामि</i> (kṣā́mi), Latin <i>humī</i> (locative). Also compare <i>πάλαι</i> (pálai) and <i>παραί</i> (paraí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coltsfoot (<i>Tussilago farfara</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>αἴγειρος</i> (aígeiros, “black poplar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wall germander (<i>Teucrium chamaedrys</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “oak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seeking the ground, low-growing, dwarf</li><li>sunk down</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) of low estate, humble</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>low seat, stool</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰμαί</i> (khămaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>ζῆλος</i> (zêlos, “zeal, fervour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground ivy (<i>Glechoma hederacea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>κισσός</i> (kissós, “ivy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supine vervain (<i>Verbena supina</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>λύκος</i> (lúkos, “wolf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman camomile (<i>Chamaemelum nobile</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground pine (<i>Ajuga chamaepitys</i>)</li><li>herb-ivy (<i>Ajuga iva</i>)</li><li>mountain germander (<i>Teucrium montanum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰμαί</i> (khămaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>πῐ́τῠς</i> (pĭ́tŭs, “pine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwarf palm (<i>Chamaerops humilis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>ῥώψ</i> (rhṓps, “brushwood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strewed or stretched on the ground</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰμαί</i> (khămaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>στρωτός</i> (strōtós, “spread, stretched”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwarf elder (<i>Sambucus ebulus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>ἀκτέα</i> (aktéa, “elder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuberous spurge (<i>Euphorbia apios</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>βάλανος</i> (bálanos, “acorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>periwinkle (<i>Vinca herbacea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>δάφνη</i> (dáphnē, “laurel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prostrate cherry (<i>Prunus prostrata</i>)</li><li>lily of the valley (<i>Convallaria majalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>κερασός</i> (kerasós, “bird cherry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton lavender (<i>Santolina chamaecyparissus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>κῠπᾰ́ρῐσσος</i> (kŭpắrĭssos, “cypress”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chameleon</li><li>type of plant, so called because of its colour-changing leaves</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “on the ground”) +&lrm; <i>λέων</i> (léōn, “lion”); ultimately a calque of Akkadian <i>𒌨𒈤𒊭𒆠</i> (nēšu ša qaqqari, “chameleon, reptile”, literally “lion of the ground", "predator that crawls upon the ground”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of frankincense</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰμαί</i> (khămaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>λῐ́βᾰνος</i> (lĭ́bănos, “frankincense”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coltsfoot (<i>Tussilago farfara</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>λεύκη</i> (leúkē, “white poplar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of camomile</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰμαίμηλον</i> (khămaímēlon, “camomile”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher's broom (<i>Ruscus aculeatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>μυρσίνη</i> (mursínē, “myrtle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishbone thistle (<i>Ptilostemon chamaepeuce</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>πεύκη</i> (peúkē, “pine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of <i>χᾰμαίπῐτῠς</i> (khămaípĭtŭs)</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰμαίπῐτῠς</i> (khămaípĭtŭs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of dwarf planetree</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>πλᾰ́τᾰνος</i> (plắtănos, “plane tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tuberous spurge (<i>Euphorbia apios</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>ῥάφανος</i> (rháphanos, “cabbage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of spurge</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>συκῆ</i> (sukê, “fig-tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of <i>χαμαισύκη</i> (khamaisúkē)</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰμαισῡ́κη</i> (khămaisū́kē, “kind of spurge”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brothel</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰμαιτύπη</i> (khămaitúpē, “harlot, strumpet”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spurge-olive (<i>Daphne oleoides</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground, on the ground”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλαία</i> (elaía, “olive tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bed on the ground, pallet-bed</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) bedstead, bedframe</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “near the ground”) +&lrm; <i>εὐνή</i> (eunḗ, “bed; abode”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low on the ground</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) of low station</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) diminutive, trifling</li></ol><p>From <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí, “to the earth, on the earth”), a frozen case-form of <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “earth”), itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i>. Compare <i>πάλαι</i> (pálai), <i>παραί</i> (paraí). The ending could be formed after the antonym <i>ὑψηλός</i> (hupsēlós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the ground</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>χαμαί</i> (khamaí) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i> (“earth”)) + <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take in, hold, comprise, contain</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;intransitive) to be capable [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘of doing’</small>]</li></ol><p>For <i>&#42;χαδάνω</i> (&#42;khadánō) (with <i>-χανδ-</i> (-khand-) imported from other forms of the paradigm), from a nasal-infixed and -suffixed present from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰed-</i> (“to seize, grasp, take”). Compare <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō). Cognate with Latin <i>&#42;hendō</i>, English <i>get</i>, Albanian <i>gjej</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>yawning, roomy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰan-</i> / <i>&#42;gʰan-</i> (“to yawn, to gape”), the same root of <i>χάσκω</i> (kháskō, “to yawn, gape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy, exultation</li></ol><p>From <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torrent, mountain stream</li><li>riverbed</li><li>gorge, gully, ravine</li></ol><p>The older connection with <i>χαράσσω</i> (kharássō, “to sharpen, to whet”) must be given up. Related to <i>χέραδος</i> (khérados, “debris, gravel”) and thus probably from Pre-Greek, in view of the variation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li><li>to engrave, carve, write, draw, stamp</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin.</p><p>Beekes claims that the verb, although both earlier and more frequently attested than <i>χάραξ</i> (khárax, “pointed stake; pole”), is a denominative formation from the latter, with original form <i>χαράκ-ι̯ω</i> (kharák-i̯ō). He derives both terms from Pre-Greek, and seems to reject Pokorny's traditional connection with Lithuanian <i>žer̃ti</i> (“to scrape”), which derives the Greek from some <i>*k</i>-extension to a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰer-</i> (“to scratch, scrape”) (and the Lithuanian from <i>&#42;ǵʰer-</i> without the extension).</p><p>The donor language in question could very well be Phoenician, from the Phoenician cognate to the much-used Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>חֲרַץ</i> (ḥăraṣ, “to cut into, to incise”), Hebrew <i>חָרַץ</i> (ḥāraṣ, “to cut or dig a cavity into; to sharpen”), Arabic خَرَزَ (ḵaraza, “to bore into”), exactly attested with the semantics of the Greek as active participle or occupational noun Punic <i>𐤇𐤓𐤔</i> (ḥrš, “crafter; engraver”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>graceful; beautiful; elegant</li><li>cultured; refined</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰ́ρῐς</i> (khắrĭs, “grace”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grant as a favor, that is, gratuitously, in kindness, pardon or rescue: - deliver, (frankly) forgive, (freely) give, grant</li></ol><p>From <i>χᾰ́ρῐς</i> (khắrĭs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζομαι</i> (-ĭ́zomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show a favor, oblige, gratify, indulge (<i>with a dative</i>)</li><li>(<i>in erotic sense</i>) to grant favors (<i>with a dative</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitively</i>) to give graciously, forgive</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;in the perfect) to be dear, acceptable</li></ol><p>From <i>χάρις</i> (kháris, “grace”) +&lrm; <i>-ι̯ω</i> (-i̯ō, denominative verb suffix), superficially +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of thanksgiving</li><li>Free</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) An epithet for a plant</li><li>Describing a sort of cake</li></ol><p><i>χᾰ́ρις</i> (khắris, “grace”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A bird, probably the Eurasian stone curlew, <i>Burhinus oedicnemus</i>.</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) caladrius (a mythical bird that had healing powers)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to other bird names, like <i>αἰγυπιός</i> (aigupiós), <i>αἰγωλιός</i> (aigōliós) and <i>ἐρῳδιός</i> (erōidiós). Traditionally derived from <i>χαράδρα</i> (kharádra, “dry bed of a river”), but it could be folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood spurge (<i>Euphorbia amygdaloides</i>)</li><li>kind of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>χάραξ</i> (khárax, “stake, pole, palisade”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrument used for engraving</li><li>person who engraves, engraver</li><li>impress, stamp, seal</li><li>reproduction, representation</li><li>figure, letter, character</li><li>characteristic, character</li><li>style</li></ol><p>From <i>χαράσσω</i> (kharássō, “to scratch, engrave”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engrave, inscribe</li><li>to form, stamp</li><li>to designate by a characteristic mark, characterize</li><li>to emphasize</li><li>to indicate, expound</li></ol><p>From <i>χαρακτήρ</i> (kharaktḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>characteristic</li></ol><p>From <i>χαρακτηρίζω</i> (kharaktērízō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or for thanksgiving</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) thank offering</li></ol><p>From <i>χάρις</i> (kháris) +&lrm; <i>-τήριος</i> (-tḗrios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thank offering</li><li>charistia (family banquet at which family feuds were settled)</li></ol><p>From <i>χαριστέος</i> (kharistéos) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show grace to, to bestow favor on or upon</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be highly favored, to be shown grace, to have grace or favor shown to</li></ol><p><i>χάρις</i> (kháris) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>amber (color)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>case or chest for books or papers</li></ol><p>From <i>χάρτης</i> (khártēs, “sheet of paper, book”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper of archives</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>chartulārius</i> (“keeper of archives”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) hiatus</li><li>interruption</li></ol><p>From <i>χάσμα</i> (khásma) + <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ) + <i>-ιᾱ</i> (-iā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have need of, crave, lack [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to be idle</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to fail, be wanting</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>needy, in want, poor</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cake</li></ol><p>From Hebrew.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy utterly, swallow up</li></ol><p>From <i>χάος</i> (kháos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shepherd's staff or crook</li></ol><p>The similarity with <i>γαῖσος</i> (gaîsos, “javelin”) is perhaps no coincidence, suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰoysós</i> (“throwing spear”). However, further connection with Sanskrit <i>हेष॑स्</i> (héṣas, “quickness; vigour”) and <i>हि॒नोति॑</i> (hinóti, “to shoot; cast”), is both formally and semantically unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hail; hello; welcome</li><li>farewell; goodbye</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be full of cheer; to be happy</li><li>to enjoy [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> participle <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>perfect</i>) to be very glad; to enjoy [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> participle <small>‘doing something’</small>] a great deal</li><li>(on meeting or parting,&#32;imperative) be well; farewell, be glad, God speed, greetings, hail, joy(‐fully), rejoice (as a salutation)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰəřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰer-</i>. The iota in the progressive forms (present system) is a reflex of the <i>&#42;y</i> marker for the present tense added onto the root <i>χαρ-</i> (khar-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of tissues</i>) porous, spongy</li><li>(<i>of timber</i>) loose-grained</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) empty, frivolous</li></ol><p>Because of its basic meaning "loose, with holes, porous", is quite possible for this adjective to derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₂-no-s</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₂-</i> (“to yawn, hape”), like <i>χάος</i> (kháos, “abyss, chasm”), <i>χαίνω</i> (khaínō), and <i>χάσκω</i> (kháskō, “to gape, yawn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter clothing</li></ol><p>From <i>χεῖμᾰ</i> (kheîmă, “winter”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hand</li><li>arm (or hand and arm taken together)</li><li>paw of an animal</li><li>finger</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰéhər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰésōr</i> (“hand”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>ձեռն</i> (jeṙn), Old Persian <i>𐎭𐎿𐎫</i> (d-s-t /⁠dasta⁠/), Albanian <i>dorë</i> (“hand”), Tocharian A <i>tsar</i>, and Latin <i>hir</i> (“hand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>χείρεσσι</i> (kheíressi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worse in quality, inferior</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of men</i>) less brave, lower in rank</li></ol><li>(with negative <i>οὐδὲν</i> (oudèn)) no worse: just as good</li></ol><p>Suppletive comparative of <i>κακός</i> (kakós, “bad”), from an element <i>χερ-</i> (kher-) of disputed origin + comparative suffix <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn). The traditional derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰres-, &#42;ǵʰers-</i> (“short”), whence Sanskrit <i>ह्रस्</i> (hras, “to become short”), Middle Irish <i>gerr</i> (“short”), is rejected by Beekes, who additionally dismisses Machek's suggestion of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gořьjь</i> (“worse”) as cognate, and leaves the origin open.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χείρων)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Cheiron; Chiron</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pulse, leguminous fruits</li></ol><p>The word has been compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gorxъ</i> (“pea”) and Latin <i>furfur</i> (“bran, husks of grain”), but this is formally untenable. A derivation from <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) and <i>δρέπω</i> (drépō, “to pluck”) is folk-etymological. The variations show that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bandage for the lips</li></ol><p>From <i>χεῖλος</i> (kheîlos, “lip”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>torrential, flowing during winter, swollen by rain and melted snow</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>χειμάρροος</i> (kheimárrhoos), from <i>χεῖμα</i> (kheîma, “winter”) + <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or in winter, wintertime</li><li>stormy</li></ol><p>From <i>χείμερος</i> (kheímeros, “wintry, stormy”) +&lrm; <i>-ινός</i> (-inós), or directly from Proto-Indo-European and cognate with Latin <i>hībernus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter</li><li>frost, cold</li><li>storm, tempest</li><li>calamity, distress, suffering</li></ol><p>From <i>χεῖμα</i> (kheîma, “cold, winter”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) chiragra, gout in the hand</li></ol><p>From <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγρᾱ</i> (ágrā, “catching”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sleeved (of clothing)</li></ol><p>From <i>χειριδόομαι</i> (kheiridóomai, “to be furnished with sleeves”) (from <i>χειρίς</i> (kheirís, “glove”), from <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”)) + <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wages, hire</li></ol><p>From <i>χειρόδοτος</i> (kheiródotos, “given by hand”), from <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>δοτός</i> (dotós, “given”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handmill</li></ol><p>From <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>μῠ́λη</i> (mŭ́lē, “mill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) one who moves the hands in pantomimic gestures, a posture-master</li></ol><p>From <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work with one's hands</li><li>to do violence with one's hands</li><li>to make/craft/play by hand (of arts, music)</li><li>to do by oneself, without help</li><li>to operate (of surgeons)</li><li>to masturbate</li></ol><p>From <i>χειρουργός</i> (kheirourgós, “doing by hand”), from <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”), +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>working by hand, practice of a handicraft or art</li><li>handicraft or art, especially surgery</li></ol><p>From <i>χειρουργέω</i> (kheirourgéō, “to work with the hands”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>surgical, relating to surgery</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a surgery</li></ol><p>From <i>χειρουργός</i> (kheirourgós, “a craftsman, surgeon”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “A suffix added to noun stems to form adjectives”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>craftsman</li><li>surgeon</li></ol><p><i>χείρ</i> (kheír) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós), literally “hand worker”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>towel</li></ol><p>Disputed:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basin for washing the hands</li><li>water for washing the hands</li></ol><p>From <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>νίπτρον</i> (níptron, “water for washing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to conquer, overpower, subdue</li></ol><p>From <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>χελῑδόνῐος</i> (khelīdónĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greater celandine (<i>Chelidonium majus</i>)</li><li>young swallow</li></ol><p>From <i>χελιδών</i> (khelidṓn, “swallow”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of, belonging to, built by, like, or pertaining to the swallow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>coloured like the swallow’s throat, reddish-brown, russet</li></ol></ol><p><i>χελῑδών</i> (khelīdṓn, “swallow”, stem: <i>χελῑδον-</i>, <i>khelīdon-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “of”, “belonging to”, “pertaining to”, suffix forming adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the swallow</li><li>the frog in a horse’s foot</li></ol><p>The irregular form and suffix <i>-ιδ</i> (-id) lead Beekes to suggest a Mediterranean Pre-Greek origin, as he doesn't find a connection to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰel-</i> (“to cry out”) (cognate with Middle High German <i>glien</i> (“to cry”), Old High German <i>gellan</i> (“to cry”)) convincing. Compare also Latin <i>hirundō</i> (“swallow (bird)”), which may be from the same substrate source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inferior tortoise-shell</li><li>tailpiece, of the stand of a torsion-engine</li></ol><p>From <i>χελώνη</i> (khelṓnē, “tortoise”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tortoise</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰel(H)-ewH-</i> (“tortoise, turtle-like animal”), and compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;žely</i> (“turtle, tortoise”); also related to <i>χέλυς</i> (khélus, “tortoise”).</p><p>However, Furnee and Chantraine consider the word a Pre-Greek borrowing based on the variant <i>χέλυμνα</i> (khélumna) (which yielded <i>&#42;χελυϝνα</i> (&#42;kheluwna) &gt; <i>χελῡ́νη</i> (khelū́nē), <i>χελύννα</i> (khelúnna)) displaying μ/ϝ consonantal interchange, as well as the variant <i>χελῡ́νη</i> (khelū́nē) having -ῡ́ν- in its form, which is highly unusual if inherited from Proto-Indo-European.</p><p>Beekes favors the Pre-Greek derivation, and additionally separates the Slavic words etymologically, considering the Slavic to derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“green, yellow”). There doesn't seem to be a good reason to separate the Slavic terms, however; even if not inherited, there is a possibility that the Greek and Slavic terms were borrowed from the same substrate source, in addition to the <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> theory being rejected by linguists such as Schmeja.</p><p>Another theory by Mastrelli, based on work by Frisk, derives the word from <i>χεῖλος</i> (kheîlos, “lip, jaw”), taking the tortoise as a "sharp-jawed creature".</p><p>See also Ancient Greek <i>χηλή</i> (khēlḗ, “hoof; claw; jawbone”), and Sanskrit <i>हर्मुट</i> (harmuṭa) (<i>&lt; *harmulta</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of tortoise shell</li></ol><p>From <i>χελώνη</i> (khelṓnē, “tortoise”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large stone or boulder used as a missile</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>χερμᾰ́δῐος</i> (khermắdĭos, “of the shape or size of a slingstone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large pebble or stone, especially for throwing or slinging, slingstone</li><li>(<i>in later poets</i>) large block of stone</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but a preform Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰermáts</i> can be mechanically reconstructed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slinger</li></ol><p>From <i>χερμάς</i> (khermás, “slingstone”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>χέρνῐβον</i> (khérnĭbon)</li><li>chamber pot</li></ol><p>From <i>χέρνῐβον</i> (khérnĭbon, “washbasin”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the plural) cherub</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>כְּרוּב</i> (kərūḇ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>from or of dry land, living or found on dry land</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of landsmen</i>)</li><li>inland</li><li>(<i>of voyages</i>) traveling by land</li><li>(military,&#32;of ground forces)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geography</i>) peninsula</li></ol><p>From <i>χέρσος</i> (khérsos, “dry land”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peninsula</li></ol><p>From <i>χέρσος</i> (khérsos, “dry land”) +&lrm; <i>νῆσος</i> (nêsos, “island”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χερσόνησος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chersonese</li></ol><p>From <i>χερσόνησος</i> (khersónēsos, “peninsula”), from <i>χέρσος</i> (khérsos, “dry”) +&lrm; <i>νῆσος</i> (nêsos, “island”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chi, the name for the twenty-second letter Χ, χ of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lip</li><li>mouth of any animal: snout, beak</li><li>edge, brink, brim, rim</li><li>shore, or a river or sea</li></ol><p>Unknown. The traditional derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰel-</i> (“to shout”), and subsequent comparison with Old Norse <i>gjǫlnar</i> (“mustache, bearded lips”) and Old Armenian <i>ձեղուն</i> (jełun, “ceiling, palate”), is unconvincing. See also <i>χελώνη</i> (khelṓnē, “tortoise”), which is often associated, though probably not related, with the word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter</li><li>frost; cold</li><li>storm; tempest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰéimə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰéymn̥</i> (“winter”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>हिम</i> (hima).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>χεῖρᾰ</i> (kheîră)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>worse</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>χείρων</i> (kheírōn, “worse”)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worse in quality, inferior</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of men</i>) less brave, lower in rank</li></ol><li>(with negative <i>οὐδὲν</i> (oudèn)) no worse: just as good</li></ol><p>Suppletive comparative of <i>κακός</i> (kakós, “bad”), from an element <i>χερ-</i> (kher-) of disputed origin + comparative suffix <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn). The traditional derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰres-, &#42;ǵʰers-</i> (“short”), whence Sanskrit <i>ह्रस्</i> (hras, “to become short”), Middle Irish <i>gerr</i> (“short”), is rejected by Beekes, who additionally dismisses Machek's suggestion of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gořьjь</i> (“worse”) as cognate, and leaves the origin open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>χεῦα</i> (kheûa)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>χεῦε</i> (kheûe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoof</li><li>crab's claw</li><li>unguis of an ox</li><li>breakwater</li><li>jawbone</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Traditionally connected with <i>χηρᾰμός</i> (khērămós, “hole, hollow”), <i>χήμη</i> (khḗmē, “yawning, gaping”), and <i>χάσκω</i> (kháskō, “to yawn, gape”), but the supposed unifying connections are obscure. Compare also <i>χελώνη</i> (khelṓnē, “turtle”), also of unclear origin, which could be related through Pre-Greek if it originally referred to the hard shell.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large chest, coffer</li><li>coffin</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it may belong with <i>χήμη</i> (khḗmē, “mussel”) to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₁y-</i> (“to gape, be wide open”), the same root of <i>χάσκω</i> (kháskō, “to yawn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>χυμείᾱ</i> (khumeíā, “art of alloying metals”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egyptian goose (<i>Alopochen aegyptiaca</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χήν</i> (khḗn, “goose”) +&lrm; <i>ἀλώπηξ</i> (alṓpēx, “fox”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hole, cleft, slit, hollow</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it is clearly a Pre-Greek word, in view of the several variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>yesterday</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰǵʰyés</i> (“yesterday”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>low, near the ground, creeping</li><li>sunken, flat</li><li>epithet of Ithaca</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰǵʰemelo-</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i> (“earth”) (whence <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn), Sanskrit <i>क्षम्</i> (kṣam)). Cognate with Latin <i>humilis</i>, Phrygian <i>ζεμελως</i> (zemelōs, “man”).</p><p>Same Proto-Indo-European root gave both the nouns for <i>man</i> and <i>earth</i> (Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i>); compare Latin <i>homō</i> (“human being, man”) and <i>humus</i> (“ground, soil”), and similar semantic shift occurring in Semitic languages: Hebrew <i>אָדָם</i> (adám, “man”), <i>אדמה</i> (adamá, “soil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of yesterday</li></ol><p>From <i>χθές</i> (khthés, “yesterday”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνός</i> (-ĭnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of yesterday</li></ol><p>From <i>χθές</i> (khthés, “yesterday”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>underground, subterranean, chthonian</li></ol><p>From <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “ground, soil”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i> (“earth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground, soil, surface of the earth</li><li>earth, world</li><li>land, country</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰtʰṓn</i> (earlier Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;tʰkʰṓn</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i>, with regular transference to <i>n-stem</i> (cf. <i>εἷς</i> (heîs), <i>χῐών</i> (khĭṓn)) and metathesizing of the Proto-Indo-European cluster <i>&#42;dʰǵʰ-</i>, since Greek stop clusters must always end in a dental. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>क्ष</i> (kṣa) and Latin <i>humus</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χθών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Earth, personified as a goddess</li></ol><p>From <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “earth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to behave like a Chian</li><li>to mark with an X</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) to arrange in a chiasmus</li><li>to make a cruciform incision</li><li>to X out; to cross out; to cancel</li></ol><p>From <i>Χῖος</i> (Khîos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p><p>From <i>χῖ</i> (khî, “chi”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>placing crosswise; diagonal arrangement</li></ol><p>From <i>χῑᾰ́ζω</i> (khīắzō, “to mark with an X”, from <i>χῖ</i> (khî, “chi”)) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the commander of a thousand men, commander of a garrison</li><li>(<i>used as a translation</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the Persian visir</i>)</li><li>(of the Latin <i>tribunus militum</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>χίλιοι</i> (khílioi, “thousand”) +&lrm; <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to rule”). Calque of Old Median <i>&#42;hazārapatiš</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>1000, the number one thousand</li><li>lots, any large and difficult to count amount</li><li>millennium, a period of 1000 years</li></ol><p>From <i>χῑ́λιοι</i> (khī́lioi, “thousand”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jacob's ladder (<i>Polemonium caeruleum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χῑ́λιοι</i> (khī́lioi, “thousand”) +&lrm; <i>δῠ́νᾰμῐς</i> (dŭ́nămĭs, “power”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thousandth</li></ol><p>From <i>χῑ́λῐοι</i> (khī́lĭoi, “thousand”) +&lrm; <i>-στός</i> (-stós), a variant of <i>-τός</i> (-tós, ordinal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>knotweed (<i>Polygonum aviculare</i>)</li><li>yarrow (<i>Achillea millefolium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χίλιοι</i> (khílioi, “thousand”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λλον</i> (phŭ́llon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nosebag for cattle or horses</li></ol><p>From <i>χῑλός</i> (khīlós, “fodder, forage”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>green fodder for cattle, forage, provender</li><li>pasturage</li></ol><p>Unexplained. It has been compared with Russian <i>жир</i> (žir, “fat, lard”) and Czech <i>žír</i> (“fodder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a flock</i>) the lead goat</li></ol><p>From <i>χῐ́μᾰρος</i> (khĭ́măros, “male goat”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ρχω</i> (ắrkhō, “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goatherd</li></ol><p>From <i>χῐ́μαιρᾰ</i> (khĭ́mairă, “she-goat”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tunic</li><li>coat of mail</li><li>clothes</li><li>coat, covering</li><li>part of a shoe which covers the foot</li></ol><p>Together with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀑𐀵</i> (ki-to) and likely Latin <i>tunica</i> from some Central Semitic <i>*kittān</i>, as found in Aramaic <i>כִּיתּוּנָא</i> (kittōnā, “tunic”) / <i>ܟܝܬܘܢܐ</i> (kittōnā, “tunic”), Hebrew <i>כֻּתֹּנֶת</i> (kuttṓnĕṯ, “tunic”), which is derived from the local word for flax, found as Aramaic <i>כּיתָּנָא</i> (kittānā, “flax”) / <i>ܟܬܢܐ</i> (kettānā, “flax”), Akkadian <i>𒃰</i> (kitûm, “flax”), Sumerian <i>𒃰</i> (gada, “flax”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>χιτῶνα</i> (khitôna), <i>χιτῶνε</i> (khitône), and <i>χιτῶνι</i> (khitôni)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snow-white</li></ol><p>From <i>χιών</i> (khiṓn, “snow”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snow</li><li>melted snow, ice water</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰiṓn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰéyōm</i>. Cognate with <i>χεῖμα</i> (kheîma, “snow”) and <i>χειμών</i> (kheimṓn, “winter”), Latin <i>hiems</i>, Welsh <i>gaeaf</i>, Sanskrit <i>हिम</i> (himá), Hittite <i>𒄀𒈠𒀭</i> (giman), Armenian <i>ձմեռ</i> (jmeṙ), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;zima</i>, Albanian <i>dimër</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maker of cloaks or mantles</li></ol><p>From <i>χλαμύς</i> (khlamús, “mantle, cloak”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cloak, robe, mantle (especially a military or royal cloak)</li></ol><p>From Semitic, compare Hebrew <i>גְּלוֹם</i> (glōm, “wrap, mantle, cloak”), Late Babylonian <i>𒌆𒄖𒇷𒉡</i> (TUG<sub>2</sub>.gu-li-nu /⁠gulīnu, gulēnu, gulānu⁠/, “over-cloak, coat”) likely a West-Semitic loan, Aramaic <i>גְלִימָא</i> (glīmā, “wrap, mantle, cloak”), whence also <i>χλᾰνῐ́ς</i> (khlănĭ́s, “upper-garment of wool”), <i>χλαῖνᾰ</i> (khlaînă, “upper-garment, cloak, wrapper”); innovating the <i>δ</i>-stem.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outer garment also used as blanket: cloak, mantle, wrapper</li></ol><p>From the same Semitic root of <i>χλαμύς</i> (khlamús) and <i>χλανίς</i> (khlanís). Maybe also <i>χλαμυρίς</i> (khlamurís) and <i>χλανίαι</i> (khlaníai) are related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jest, scoff, jeer, treat scornfully</li></ol><p>From <i>χλεύη</i> (khleúē, “jest, joke”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, suffix to form a verb).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Irony (as a figure of speech)</li><li>mockery, piece of impertinence, (incidence of) scoffing</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joke, jest</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰlew-</i> (“to joke, make fun, enjoy”), the same root of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;glumiti</i> (“to mock, to joke”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glaumaz</i> (“jubilation”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to warm, warm up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to grow warm, warm oneself</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>χλίω</i> (khlíō), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰlíō</i>.</p><p>The entry by Beekes in his Etymological Dictionary of Greek reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>The pair formed by this verb and <i>χλιαρός</i> (khliarós, “tepid, lukewarm”), like <i>μιαίνω</i> (miaínō)/<i>μιαρός</i> (miarós) and <i>πιαίνω</i> (piaínō)/<i>πιαρός</i> (piarós), belong together both formally and semantically, due to their physiological meaning "soft, lukewarm". Some Celtic and Germanic words with the meaning "to shine" are compared: Old Irish <i>glé</i> (“clear, evident”), Middle Welsh <i>gloew</i> (“liquid, clear”), Old Norse <i>gljá</i> (“to shine, gleam”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glaimiz</i> (“brightness, splendour”), Lithuanian <i>žlejà</i> (“darkness, twilight”). All these words point to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰley-</i> (“to shine”). However, the connection between "shine, gleam" and "warm" is difficult, so the reconstruction remains uncertain.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lukewarm, tepid</li></ol><p>From <i>χλιαίνω</i> (khliaínō, “to be warm”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χλιαρός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Chliarus, the ancient name of the river Ganges</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A wild boar described as <i>τομίας</i> (tomías, “castrated”), <i>μονιός</i> (moniós, “solitary”), <i>ἀφριστής</i> (aphristḗs, “foaming at the mouth”) and <i>χλοεύνης</i> (khloeúnēs, “couching in the grass”)</li></ol><p>Unknown, but perhaps contracted form χλοεύνης, see also the definition below.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greenfinch (<i>Chloris chloris</i>)</li><li>kind of grape</li></ol><p>From <i>χλωρός</i> (khlōrós, “green”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark green</li></ol><p>From <i>χλωρός</i> (khlōrós, “green”) +&lrm; <i>μέλας</i> (mélas, “black”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>greenish-yellow, pale green (<i>color/colour</i>)</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) pale, pallid</li><li>fresh, verdant, unripe, blooming</li><li>youthful</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰlōrós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i>. Related to <i>χλοερός</i> (khloerós, “verdant”) and <i>χλόη</i> (khlóē, “the green of new growth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A new green shoot in the spring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i>. See also Ancient Greek <i>χολή</i> (kholḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gnaw off, nibble</li></ol><p>This verb agrees in its vocalism with <i>ψαύω</i> (psaúō, “to touch lightly”), <i>θραύω</i> (thraúō, “to break”) and <i>χραύω</i> (khraúō, “to scrape, graze, wound lightly”), while the gloss <i>χνίει</i> (khníei, “drips, breaks into pieces”) reminds of <i>ψίω</i> (psíō, “to feed on pap”) and <i>χρίω</i> (khríō, “to touch, graze”). The original etymology is unclear, but compare <i>χνόη</i> (khnóē, “axle-box”) and <i>χνόος</i> (khnóos, “incrustation from salt water; powder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slice, tidbit, morsel</li></ol><p>From <i>χναύω</i> (khnaúō, “to gnaw off, nibble”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nave, hub, the cylindrical central part of a wheel</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p><p>Frisk suggests that it may be related to <i>χνόος</i> (khnóos, “incrustation from salt-water; wool, down”), in the form of an action noun to <i>χναύω</i> (khnaúō, “to nibble”) and <i>χνίω</i> (khníō, “to break in small pieces”). According to him, this would make it related to Old Norse <i>gnúa</i> (“to rub”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnusъ</i>. This suggestion is repeated by Chantraine and Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incrustation from salt water</li><li>powder, dust of the earth</li><li>wool pulled for stuffing cushions, flock</li><li>first down on the chin or cheeks</li><li>bloom on fruits</li></ol><p>Related to <i>χνόη</i> (khnóē, “axle-box”). Both words have been connected to <i>χναύω</i> (khnaúō, “to nibble”) and <i>χνίω</i> (khníō, “to break in small pieces”). Reasonable connections can then be found in Old Norse <i>gnúa</i> (“to rub”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gnusъ</i>, which point to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gnew-</i>. Otherwise perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;knew-</i> (“to scrape, scratch; to rub”) and related to <i>κνίζω</i> (knízō, “to pound, grate”) and <i>κνύω</i> (knúō, “to scratch”), with expressive aspiration; or from <i>&#42;ksnew-</i>. Compare also <i>μνόος</i> (mnóos, “fine, soft down”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>funnel (for pouring liquids)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) funnel-shaped hollow in the brain</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow behind the eye</li><li>melting pot</li></ol><p>From <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, instrument noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of hog's skin</li></ol><p>From <i>χοῖρος</i> (khoîros, “hog”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pen for keeping swine in, pigpen, pigsty</li><li>kind of bandage used by women</li></ol><p>From <i>χοῖρος</i> (khoîros, “pig”) +&lrm; <i>κομέω</i> (koméō, “to take care of, tend”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ape with a hog's snout, perhaps a baboon</li></ol><p>From <i>χοῖρος</i> (khoîros, “hog”) +&lrm; <i>πίθηκος</i> (píthēkos, “ape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pigsty</li></ol><p>From <i>χοῖρος</i> (khoîros, “pig”) +&lrm; <i>τροφεῖον</i> (tropheîon, “poultry house”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) abdominal cavity</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) bowels, guts</li></ol><p>Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želǫdъkъ</i> (“stomach”) shows remarkable similarity with this word: Russian <i>желу́док</i> (želúdok), Czech <i>žaludek</i>, Polish <i>żołądek</i>, which may be reconstructed as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰel-ond-</i>, showing a similar formation to that of the Greek word if it comes from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰol-nd-</i>. Lithuanian <i>skilándis</i> (“sausage-stomach”) is considered unrelated, but shows the same suffix. There are a number of problems with this account: first, the deviating form of <i>χόλιξ</i> (khólix, “bowels of oxen”) remains unexplained. Second, Furnée compares not only <i>κόλον</i> (kólon, “large intestine”), but also <i>καλίδια</i> (kalídia, “intestines”) and <i>γόλα</i> (góla), <i>γάλλια</i> (gállia) and <i>γάλλος</i> (gállos); he also adduces Old Armenian <i>քաղիրթ</i> (kʻałirtʻ, “intestines of animals”). Beekes takes the variation as showing that the word is likely Pre-Greek. Hayden and Stifter derive both the Greek lemma and Slavic <i>&#42;želǫdъkъ</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰelH-ond-</i>, from the root <i>&#42;gʰelH-</i> (“digestive organ, stomach”) found also in Celtic: Old Irish <i>eclas</i> (“gizzard”), <i>gaile</i> (“stomach”), Breton <i>elaz</i> (“gizzard”), Welsh <i>glasog</i> (“gizzard”), <i>afu glas</i> (“gizzard”), Old Cornish <i>glas</i> (“stomach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cholera, a disease in which the humors of the body are violently discharged by vomiting and stool</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) vomit, nausea</li></ol><p>A medical expression. Formally it may be compared with <i>ὑστέρα</i> (hustéra, “uterus, womb”) and names of diseases like <i>ἴκτερος</i> (íkteros, “jaundice”) and <i>ὕδερος</i> (húderos, “dropsy”). Perhaps, this word is a substantivization from unattested <i>&#42;χολερός</i> (&#42;kholerós), with shift of accent. According to Beekes, a basic derivation is more likely from <i>χολή</i> (kholḗ, “gall, bile”) than from <i>χολάς</i> (kholás, “entrails, guts”), but a Pre-Greek origin cannot be ruled out. Irish <i>galar</i> (“sickness, illness”) has to be kept separate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gall, bile</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) gall bladder</li><li>bitterness, wrath, anger</li><li>disgust, aversion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰolā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰolh₃-éh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“green, yellow”). Cognate with Avestan <i>𐬰𐬁𐬭𐬀</i> (zāra, “gall”), Latin <i>helvus</i> (“honey-yellow”), Sanskrit <i>हरि</i> (hári, “yellow, tawny”), Persian زر (zar, “gold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) angry, wrathful</li></ol><p>From <i>χολόω</i> (kholóō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anger, provoke</li></ol><p>From <i>χόλος</i> (khólos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gum succory (<i>Chondrilla juncea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χόνδρος</i> (khóndros, “groats”), with a formation similar to <i>ζωμίλη</i> (zōmílē) and <i>κονίλη</i> (konílē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>χόνδρος</i> (khóndros)</li></ol><p>From <i>χόνδρος</i> (khóndros, “lump”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>granular, coarse</li><li>(later,&#32;generally) coarse, synonymous with the Latin <i>grossus</i></li></ol><p>From <i>χόνδρος</i> (khóndros) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>χορηγός</i> (khorēgós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who accompanies a chorus on the flute</li></ol><p>From <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “chorus”) +&lrm; <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “flute”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) guts, intestines, tripe</li><li>that which is made from guts:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>string of gut, chord, especially of a lyre or harp</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>music</i>) musical note</li></ol><li>sausage, black pudding</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰerH-</i> (“bowel”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>हिर</i> (hira), Latin <i>hernia</i>, and Old English <i>ġearn</i> (English <i>yarn</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dance, especially choral dance with music</li><li>any circling motion, as of stars</li><li>dance-tune</li></ol><p>From <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “dance, choir”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a choral dancer</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an epithet of Pan</li><li>an epithet of Dionysus</li><li>dolphin</li><li>cicada</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>χορεύω</i> (khoreúō, “to dance a choral dance”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dance in a round or choral dance</li><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;with cognate object</i>) to dance</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to practice dancing</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move in a circle, circle</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rouse to dancing</li></ol><p>From <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “dance”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a chorus leader, who could enter the story as a character able to interact with the characters of a play</li><li>generally, the leader of a train or band</li><li>in Athens, one who defrays the cost of a performance</li><li>generally, one who supplies financial backing for any endeavour</li></ol><p>From <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “chorus”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like the afterbirth</li></ol><p><i>χόρῐον</i> (khórĭon, “afterbirth”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “like”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Falsa lectio for <i>χορῐοειδής</i> (khorĭoeidḗs).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of cattle</i>) to feed, fatten</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to feed, fill full of</li></ol><p>From <i>χόρτος</i> (khórtos, “fodder”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, suffix to form a verb).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a farmyard</li></ol><p>From <i>χόρτος</i> (khórtos, “farmyard”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A hayloft</li></ol><p>From <i>χόρτος</i> (khórtos, “barn”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dance ring; round dance</li><li>dance accompanied by song; choral dance</li><li>chorus, choir, band of singers and dancers</li><li>band, troop, group</li><li>row</li><li>place for dancing</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) Chorus</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰorós</i>, likely from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰoros</i>, from a root <i>&#42;ǵʰer-</i>, though the semantic identity of this root has been disputed:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earthen, made of earth</li></ol><p>From <i>χοῦς</i> (khoûs, “excavated soil”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A dry measure for corn, a choenix</li><li>A kind of stocks for fastening the legs</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pig, especially a young one.</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) swine</li><li>(slang,&#32;vulgar) female genitalia (especially those of a young woman)</li><li>a fish of the Nile</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰóřřos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰóryos</i> (“pig”) (whence Albanian <i>derr</i> (“id”)), from <i>&#42;ǵʰer-</i> (“to be excited, bristly”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yos</i> (owing to the bristliness of wild pigs), whence <i>&#42;ǵʰḗr</i> (“hedgehog”). But in view of the limited distribution, Beekes suspects a Pre-Greek substrate origin for the Greek and Albanian terms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>measure of capacity correspondent to 12 cotylae</li><li>soil excavated or heaped up</li><li>dust, cloud of dust</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰew-</i>. Doublet of <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(in perfect,&#32;κέχρημαι,&#32;with present sense)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>desire, yearn after [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>to need, lack [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>in perfect&#32;and&#32;pluperfect</i>) to enjoy a benefit, have [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><li>(in present&#32;or&#32;perfect,&#32;mainly after Homer)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to use, make use of, take part in, manage, administer, etc. [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to experience, suffer, be subject to, feel</li><li>with verbal nouns, periphrasis for the verb related to the noun</li><li>to use [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘for something’</small>]</li><li>to deal with</li><li>to treat [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>] in a particular way</li></ol></ol><p><i>χρή</i> (khrḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with dative of person</i>) to fall upon, attack, assail</li><li>(<i>with dative of object</i>) to inflict upon a person</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to be bent on doing, to be eager to do</li><li>(active voice,&#32;of the gods and their oracles) to furnish the needful answer, to declare, pronounce, proclaim</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be declared, proclaimed, delivered</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;of the person to whom the response is given) to consult a god or oracle, to inquire of a god or oracle, consult them</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of applicants seeking something of the great king</i>)</li><li>(<i>in perfect passive</i>) to receive an oracular response</li></ol><li>to furnish with a thing</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to use</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to bring into action some feeling, faculty, passion, state of mind; to exercise, indulge</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of external things</i>) to experience, be subject to</li><li>(<i>paraphrases the verb cognate to its dative</i>)</li><li>(<i>with duplicate dative</i>) to use as such and such</li><li>to use for an end or purpose</li></ol><li>(of persons,&#32;with an adverb of manner) to treat them in such a manner</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>especially of sexual intercourse</i>)</li><li>to make use of oneself or one's powers</li></ol><li>(<i>absolute or with an adverb</i>) to be wont to do</li><li>(<i>with accusative of object</i>)</li><li>(<i>perfect with a present sense</i>) to be in need or want of, to yearn after</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>perfect as a strengthened present</i>) to have in use, to have, possess</li></ol><li>(<i>aorist passive</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰreh₂w-</i> (“to rush in, attack”). Cognate with Latin <i>ingruō</i> (“to attack”), Lithuanian <i>griáuti</i> (“to destroy”), <i>griū́ti</i> (“to crumble”).</p><p>See <i>χράομαι</i> (khráomai, “to desire, use”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spittle, expectoration</li></ol><p>From <i>χρέμπτομαι</i> (khrémptomai, “to clear one's throat, cough”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ὀνίσκος</i> (onískos, “cod”).</li></ol><p>Furnée connects the word with <i>κρεμύς</i> (kremús, “a kind of fish”) and considers it of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of fish mentioned by Aristotle</li></ol><p>According to Strömberg, it could be a back-formation from the verb <i>χρέμπτομαι</i> (khrémptomai, “to clear one's throat”). Beekes proposes a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrem-</i> (“to resound, thunder, grumble, roar”), a root found in other fish names, such as <i>χρέμης</i> (khrémēs) and <i>χρόμις</i> (khrómis), and in the verb <i>χρεμετίζω</i> (khremetízō, “to neigh, whinny”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>want, need</li><li>affair, business</li><li>debt</li></ol><p>Nominal formation from the root of <i>χράομαι</i> (khráomai), <i>χρεώ</i> (khreṓ, “to need”), and <i>χρή</i> (khrḗ, “have to, ought”); see the latter for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;expressing necessity) have to; ought; should [<i>with</i> accusative of person and present infinitive or aorist infinitive]</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰreh₁-</i>, <i>&#42;ǵʰreh₁-</i> (“to desire, need”), with relation to other roots uncertain. Traditional theories connecting the root to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰer-</i> (“to yearn”) (whence <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō, “to be cheerful”)) or to Sanskrit <i>हर॑ति</i> (hárati, “to bring, carry”) (which itself is of disputed origin, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰer-</i> (“to enclose”) according to De Vaan) are deemed unconvincing by Beekes.</p><p>Originally probably a neuter noun in plural, but used always as a verbal expression by omission of copula <i>ἐστῐ́</i> (estĭ́) or crasis of <i>χρὴ</i> (khrḕ) with it.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>χρήματα</i> (khrḗmata)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useful, serviceable, profitable</li><li>much-used</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>χράομαι</i> (khráomai, “to use, desire”) + <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos, “-able, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit or suitable for anointing; used for anointing</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear, anoint”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos, forming an adjective of suitability or fitness).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub or smear (especially the body) with oil or unguents; to anoint</li><li>to coat or smear; to apply (e.g. dye, pitch, resin, asphalt)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to anoint oneself, commonly with oil or medicinal substances</li><li>(biblical,&#32;in the Septuagint) to anoint ceremonially as a sign of consecration (e.g. king, priest, prophet)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to sting, prick, or graze on the surface</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrey(H)-</i> (“to smear, strike”), which is perhaps extended from <i>&#42;gʰer-</i> (“to rub”). Potential cognates are uncertain, but include Phrygian <i>γεγρειμεναν</i> (gegreimenan, “painted, adorned, written”), Lithuanian <i>griẽti</i> (“to skim (cream from milk)”), English <i>grime</i>. See also <i>χρῶμα</i> (khrôma, “complexion, color”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ward off</li><li>to defend, help [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>χράω</i> (khráō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need, want, necessity</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>want, poverty</li></ol><li>business, purpose (especially military purpose)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>employment, function</li></ol><li>use</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>use, advantage, service</li></ol><li>familiarity, intimacy</li><li>maxim</li></ol><p>From <i>χρή</i> (khrḗ, “it is necessary”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>χρεώ</i> (khreṓ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of horses</i>) to neigh, whinny</li></ol><p>There is no exact outer-Greek correspondance to the verb, but the root <i>&#42;gʰrem-</i> (“to resound, thunder, grumble, roar”) is Proto-Indo-European. Compare Lithuanian <i>grumù</i> (“to thunder, roar”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grimmaz</i> (“grim, fierce”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gromъ</i> (“thunder”) and Persian غرم (ġaram, “fury”). Within Greek see <i>χρόμος</i> (khrómos, “crashing sound”) and <i>χρόμαδος</i> (khrómados, “crash, fragor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in debt</li></ol><p><i>χρεώστης</i> (khreṓstēs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) want, need, necessity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰer-</i> (“to yearn for”). Compare χρή (khrḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need, necessity</li><li>fate</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) that which is expedient or right</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>debtor</li></ol><p><i>χρέος</i> (khréos) +&lrm; <i>-στης</i> (-stēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>χρέος</i> (khréos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to negotiate, transact business, have dealings (<i>especially in money matters</i>)</li><li>to consult, deliberate</li><li>to give audience to, to answer after deliberation [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>of an oracle</i>) to give a response to those who consult it</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to receive an answer or warning</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to make money</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to take and bear a title or name, to be called or styled</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;passive voice) to be warned or admonished by God</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to speak, tell, declare, reveal</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῆμα</i> (khrêma) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be useful, serviceable</li></ol><p>From <i>χρήσιμος</i> (khrḗsimos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oracle, prophecy</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>χράω</i> (khráō, “to declare”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oracle</li><li>sacrificial victim for an oracle</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oracular, prophetic</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>χράω</i> (khráō, “to give an oracle”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριος</i> (-tḗrios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oracular, prophetic</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>χράω</i> (khráō, “to give an oracle”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριος</i> (-tḗrios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desire for learning</li><li>a book containing a summary of useful knowledge or select passages, a vade mecum</li></ol><p>From <i>χρηστομᾰθέω</i> (khrēstomăthéō, “be desirous for learning”), from <i>χρηστός</i> (khrēstós, “useful”) +&lrm; <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō, “learn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good, useful</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) kind, gracious</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) easy, manageable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰer-</i> (“to yearn for”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) goodness, uprightness, honesty, probity</li><li>goodness of heart, soft-heartedness, kindness</li></ol><p>From <i>χρηστός</i> (khrēstós, “useful, serviceable”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(act of) anointing</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear, anoint”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós, forming a noun of action or result).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Χρῑστιᾱνός</i> (Khrīstiānós)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χριστιανός)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Christian (follower of Christ)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Chrīstiānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rubbed, smeared, coated; having undergone application by rubbing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) used of substances applied by rubbing; (sc. <i>φάρμακον</i> (phármakon)) ointment, salve</li><li>(<i>material</i>) coated, smeared (of surfaces or objects)</li><li>(<i>Septuagint</i>) anointed; describing a person <i>consecrated</i> by ritual anointing; translation of Hebrew <i>משיח</i> (māšîaḥ) (by metonymy, office-holder)</li></ol><li>(<i>New Testament</i>) anointed one; semantically specialized as a messianic title</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) anointed one, translation of Hebrew <i>משיח</i> (māšîaḥ)</li><li>(<i>Judaism, Christianity</i>) Messiah</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Jesus Christ</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear; anoint”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix), forming a verbal adjective denoting a resultant state (“fit for spreading or anointing; anointed”), often functioning semantically like a participle.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Χριστός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Messiah or Christ, a title and later proper name applied to Jesus of Nazareth</li></ol><p>A proper noun use of the substantivized adjective <i>χρῑστός</i> (khrīstós)</p><p>(“anointed; consecrated”), employed as the Greek equivalent of</p><p>semantic loan from Biblical Hebrew <i>מָשִׁיחַ</i> (mašīaḥ) and</p><p>Aramaic <i>ܡܫܺܝܚܳܐ</i> (m'šīḥāʿ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend time</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to last long, continue</li><li>to persevere</li><li>to take time, tarry, linger, delay, be slow</li><li>(<i>of ailments</i>) to be/become chronic</li><li>(<i>of wine</i>) to be/become old, to have age</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be prolonged or protracted</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to grow up</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos, “time”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or concerning time, temporal</li><li>chronological</li></ol><p>From <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos, “time”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the recording of time, chronicler, annalist</li></ol><p>From <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos, “time”) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “to write”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) reckoning of time</li></ol><p>From <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos, “time”) +&lrm; <i>-λογία</i> (-logía, “-logy”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>golden eagle (<i>Aquila chrysaetos</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>ᾱ̓ετός</i> (āetós, “eagle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a golden frontlet or headband</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́μπῠξ</i> (ắmpŭx, “frontlet; headband”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold-flower (<i>Helichrysum italicum</i>)</li><li>immortelle (<i>Helichrysum orientale</i>)</li><li>buttercup (plant of the genus <i>Ranunculus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθεμον</i> (ánthemon, “flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a piece of gold</li><li>gold (substance)</li><li>specifically, a gold coin, money</li><li>gold thread</li><li>a term of endearment (translated <i>my treasure!</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, “diminutive suffix”). See <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós) for further etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chrysalis</li><li>cockchafer</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of gold and ivory, chryselephantine</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλεφάντινος</i> (elephántinos, “ivory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>golden-helmed(epithet of Mars)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρύσεος</i> (khrúseos, “golden”) +&lrm; <i>πήληξ</i> (pḗlēx, “helm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gold-embroidered</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>βάπτω</i> (báptō, “to dip, dye”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold solder, malachite, basic copper carbonate</li><li>dish of linseed and honey</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>κόλλα</i> (kólla, “glue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immortelle (<i>Helichrysum orientale</i>)</li></ol><p><i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>κόμη</i> (kómē, “hair of the head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orach (<i>Atriplex rosea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>λᾰ́χᾰνον</i> (lắkhănon, “vegetable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with haft of gold</li></ol><p>From <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gold-helmed, with the helm of gold</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>πήληξ</i> (pḗlēx, “helm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gold-crowned</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>στέφᾰνος</i> (stéphănos, “crown”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold-keeper, treasurer</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with gold-studded bridle</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>χᾰλῑνός</i> (khălīnós, “bridle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) contracted form of <i>χρῡ́σεος</i> (khrū́seos, “golden”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A plate chest</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡ́σωμα</i> (khrū́sōma, “gold plate”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gold-sealed (<i>especially of documents</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>βούλλᾰ</i> (boúllă, “seal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black turnip (<i>Bongardia chrysogonum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “fruit, product”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>golden-throned (<i>epithet of various goddesses</i>)</li></ol><p><i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos, “throne”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with golden fruit, bearing golden fruit</li></ol><p><i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>κᾰρπός</i> (kărpós, “fruit”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>topaz</li><li>peridot, chrysolite</li></ol><p>From <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Golden Fleece</li></ol><p>From <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “gold, golden”) + <i>μαλλός</i> (mallós, “wool”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quince (<i>Cydonia oblonga</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a golden back or surface</li></ol><p><i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>νῶτον</i> (nôton, “back”, <i>metaphorically</i> “any wide surface”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming second-declension adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shot with gold, of gold tissue</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>παστός</i> (pastós, “sprinkled”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold (substance)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) something dear or precious</li><li>a gold coin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from a Semitic source, usually assumed to be Phoenician/Punic; compare Phoenician <i>𐤇𐤓𐤑</i> (ḥrṣ), Biblical Hebrew <i>חָרוּץ</i> (ḥārûṣ), Akkadian <i>𒆬𒄀</i> (ḫurāṣum), Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀓𐀬𐀰</i> (ku-ru-so).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Gold-colored, shining like gold</li></ol><p>From <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye, face, countenance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) flake litharge, a silver ore also containing traces of gold</li></ol><p>From <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>colored</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῶμα</i> (khrôma, “color”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) offering a colourable pretext</li><li>(<i>music</i>) chromatic</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῶμα</i> (khrôma) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crash, din, fragor</li></ol><p>The ending recalls other sound words like <i>ἄραδος</i> (árados), <i>κέλαδος</i> (kélados) and <i>ὅμαδος</i> (hómados). According to Beekes, it is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrem-</i> (“to resound, thunder, grumble, roar”), the same root of <i>χρεμετίζω</i> (khremetízō, “to whinny, neigh”), <i>χρόμος</i> (khrómos, “kind of noise”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grimmaz</i> (“grim, fierce”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gromъ</i> (“thunder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crashing sound</li><li>neighing of horses</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰrómos</i> (“a roar, rumble; thunder”), the same root of <i>χρόμαδος</i> (khrómados, “crash, fragor”), <i>χρεμετίζω</i> (khremetízō, “to whinny, neigh”), <i>χρέμπτομαι</i> (khrémptomai, “to clear one's throat”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;grimmaz</i> (“grim, fierce”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gromъ</i> (“thunder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>after a long time, late</li><li>for a long time, a long while</li><li>long-continued</li><li>(<i>of ailments</i>) chronic</li></ol><p>From <i>χρόνος</i> (khrónos, “time”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time (in the abstract sense)</li><li>specific time, period, term</li><li>lifetime</li><li>delay</li><li>(grammar,&#32;Koine) tense</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin. A derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰr-ono-</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵʰer-</i> (“to seize, enclose”), has been suggested, with original connotation of "encompassing time-limit". Other theories connect the word to <i>κορμός</i> (kormós) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut off”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eventually</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time (in the abstract sense)</li><li>specific time, period, term</li><li>lifetime</li><li>delay</li><li>(grammar,&#32;Koine) tense</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin. A derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʰr-ono-</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵʰer-</i> (“to seize, enclose”), has been suggested, with original connotation of "encompassing time-limit". Other theories connect the word to <i>κορμός</i> (kormós) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)ker-</i> (“to cut off”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>χρῡ́σεος</i> (khrū́seos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>golden; made of gold; decked or inlaid with gold</li><li>gold-colored, golden yellow</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) golden</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>στᾰτήρ</i> (stătḗr, “stater”, a type of coin)</li><li>(Koine,&#32;literary,&#32;ironic) genius; bright spark (referring to one who has made a silly mistake)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>golden, made of gold</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gilthead (<i>Sparus aurata</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”) +&lrm; <i>ὀφρῡ́ς</i> (ophrū́s, “eyebrow”), because this fish has a golden spot over each eye.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, flesh</li><li>complexion</li><li>color</li></ol><p>Related to <i>χρῶμα</i> (khrôma), of uncertain origin.</p><p>According to Beekes, probably cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀒𐀫𐀸𐀁</i> (a-ko-ro-we-e) (representing either /akʰroweʰe/ [dual noun] “without spots”, or /ha-kʰroweʰe/ “of one color”). Chantraine suggests that the Mycenaean form points to a root <i>&#42;k(ʰ)row-</i> “throw”. The traditional connection to <i>χρίω</i> (khríō, “to smear”) is doubted by Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>a textual error for <i>πρώτῳ</i> (prṓtōi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to need, lack, desire</li><li>to prophesy, foretell</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>need; a thing that one needs or uses</li><li>goods; property</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>money</li></ol><li>thing; matter; affair</li></ol><p>Formed from the base of <i>χρᾰ́ομαι</i> (khrắomai, “want, need”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>use, usage, employment</li><li>utility, usefulness</li><li>intimacy, relationship, sexual intercourse</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) use, example, quotation</li><li>the response of an oracle</li><li>loan</li></ol><p>From <i>χρᾰ́ω</i> (khrắō, “I use, lend, declare”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ointment, unguent, oil</li><li>anointing</li><li>coating, plaster</li></ol><p>From <i>χρίω</i> (khríō) (“to rub, smear”), with the noun-forming suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma) indicating the result of an action.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smearing, rubbing, application (of oil, unguents, or similar substances)</li><li>anointing, especially ritual or medicinal</li><li>(<i>by extension</i>) colouring, varnishing, wash</li></ol><p>From <i>χρίω</i> (khríō, “to rub, smear, anoint”) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis), forming an action noun.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ointment, unguent, oil</li><li>anointing, unction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(biblical,&#32;Septuagint) ritual anointing oil</li><li>(<i>New Testament</i>) spiritual anointing, divine empowerment</li></ol><li>coating, plaster (of walls or surfaces)</li></ol><p>From <i>χρίω</i> (khríō) (“to rub, smear”) + <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p><p>Later form of <i>χρῖμα</i> (khrîma) (LSJ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin <i>(esp. the human body)</i></li><li>colour <i>(esp. of the skin or body)</i>; pigment</li><li>complexion</li><li>character of style in writing; ornaments</li><li>chromatic scale; music</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰrṓwmə</i>, and related to <i>χρώς</i> (khrṓs, “surface of the body, skin (color)”); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poured forth copiously, abundant</li><li>common</li><li>vulgar, unrefined</li></ol><p>From <i>χύδην</i> (khúdēn, “as if poured, in abundance”), adverb from the root of <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”) +&lrm; <i>-δην</i> (-dēn), +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in general</i>) juice</li><li>juice of plants</li><li>animal juice</li><li>chyle, juice produced by the digestion of food</li><li>gruel, barley water</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived as a nominal formation from <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”), but like <i>χῡμός</i> (khūmós, “juice, liquid”), it is unclear how the long <i>-ῡ-</i> can be explained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>art of alloying metals, alchemy</li></ol><p>According to Zosimos of Panopolis it was named from its supposed inventor, Χύμης/Χήμης/Χείμης, but more probably it is from <i>χῠ́μᾰ</i> (khŭ́mă) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juice of plants</li><li>animal juices, humors</li><li>flavour, savour</li></ol><p>Traditionally taken as a nominal derivation of <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”), but like <i>χυλός</i> (khulós, “juice, gruel”), it is unclear how the long <i>-ῡ-</i> can be explained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>χύτρᾱ</i> (khútrā, “earthen pot, pipkin”)</li></ol><p>From <i>χυτρίς</i> (khutrís) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰew-</i> (“to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stand for a pot</li><li>pot or cauldron</li></ol><p>From <i>χύτρα</i> (khútra, “earthen pot, pipkin”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poured, shed</li><li>piled up, heaped</li><li>liquid, fluid, melted</li><li>washed up, alluvial</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰutós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetry</i>) choliamb, limping iamb</li></ol><p>From <i>χωλός</i> (khōlós, “lame, limping”) +&lrm; <i>ἴαμβος</i> (íambos, “iamb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lame, halting, limping</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) maimed, imperfect, defective</li></ol></ol><p>Seemingly, a formation with the suffix <i>-λός</i> like <i>στρεβλός</i> (streblós) and <i>τυφλός</i> (tuphlós), but without obvious etymology. Perhaps it belongs to <i>χαλάω</i> (khaláō, “to slacken, relax”) or perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₁-</i> (“to leave behind, abandon”), the same root of <i>χήρα</i> (khḗra, “widow”), because a lame person is someone who lags or stays behind. Connections to Sanskrit खोर are uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to withdraw; to give way to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>], make room, vacate</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;after Homer) to go forward, advance</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to have room for, contain</li></ol><p>From <i>χῶρος</i> (khôros, “place”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, divide</li><li>to distinguish</li></ol><p>From <i>χωρίς</i> (khōrís, “separated, detached”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place, spot, site</li><li>plot of land</li><li>landed estate</li><li>space, room</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) area (<i>in a figure</i>)</li></ol><p><i>χῶρος</i> (khôros) or <i>χώρᾱ</i> (khṓrā, “place”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>separately, differently</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>without, otherwise</li></ol><p>An isolated adverbial formation from <i>χώρᾱ</i> (khṓrā, “space”), initially meaning "in another, separate space or place".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chorepiscopus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>describing countries</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who describes countries</li></ol><p>From <i>χώρα</i> (khṓra, “country, nation”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard or watcher of a country or place</li></ol><p>From <i>χώρᾱ</i> (khṓrā, “place, country, nation”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>agitated, troubled, angered</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>melting pot, crucible</li><li>mould for casting metal in</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰew-</i> (“to pour”), the same root of <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour, cast”) and <i>χοάνη</i> (khoánē, “funnel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) butt, buttocks</li></ol><p>Compare <i>χέζω</i> (khézō, “defecate”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guts or bowels of oxen</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>χολᾰ́ς</i> (kholắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gall, bile</li><li>bitterness, wrath, anger</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>χολή</i> (kholḗ), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of groats or coarse meal</li></ol><p>From <i>χόνδρος</i> (khóndros, “groats of wheat or spelt”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small rounded mass: lump</li><li>grain, groat, corn, spelt</li><li>cartilage</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) afterbirth, placenta</li><li>any intestinal membrane</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) dish filled with milk and honey, kind of pudding similar to haggis</li></ol><li>membrane round the inside of the egg</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰer-</i>, the same root of <i>χορδή</i> (khordḗ, “guts, intestine”) and Latin <i>hernia</i> (“protruded viscus”). However, Beekes finds this proposal unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feeding place for animals, barn, pasture</li><li>foodstuff for animals, fodder, hay, grass</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) food</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰórtos</i> (“enclosure”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>गृह</i> (gṛhá), Latin <i>hortus</i>, and Old English <i>ġeard</i> (English <i>yard</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw or heap up</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as if poured out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>without order</li><li>in prose</li><li>abundantly</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>χῠ</i> (khŭ), the root of <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”), +&lrm; <i>-δην</i> (-dēn, deverbal adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is poured out or flows, fluid</li><li>ingot, bar</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) confused mass, aggregate, crowd</li><li>materials, constituents</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʰumə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰéwmn̥</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵʰew-</i> (“to pour”), whence <i>χέω</i> (khéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>χέω</i> (khéō)</li></ol><p>Late form; probably a back-formation from <i>ἔχυντο</i> (ékhunto), a poetic third-person plural aorist middle form of <i>χέω</i> (khéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shedding, pouring out forth</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) squandering</li><li>(<i>of nutriment</i>) diffusion</li><li>melting, casting, fusing</li><li>liquid poured forth, flood, stream</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fluency or copiousness of speech</li><li>quantity, abundance</li></ol><p>From <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour; melt, cast”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything that can be poured, liquid, fluid</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) water for washing, bath</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) libations to the dead</li><li>mixture of water and oil, rubbed in after bathing</li><li>stream, running water</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰew-</i>, the same root of <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour, cast”). The suffix is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-trom</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an earthen pot, pot for boiling, pipkin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰutrom</i>, from <i>&#42;ǵʰew-</i> (“to pour”), whence <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour out”); the original meaning was perhaps a "container of liquids (poured things)". Cognate with Sanskrit <i>होत्र</i> (hotrá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do something useless and inconsequential; inessential, uncalled-for and gratuitous</li><li>to do something unceasingly</li></ol><p>From <i>χύτρα</i> (khútra, “earthen pot, pipkin”) (Modern <i>χύτρα</i> (chýtra, “stewpot, cauldron”)) and <i>ποικίλλω</i> (poikíllō, “decorate, embellish, embroider”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of earthenware</li></ol><p>From <i>χύτρα</i> (khútra, “pipkin”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to be agitated, troubled, angered</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>location, place, spot (see Latin locus)</li><li>the proper place</li><li>one's place in life</li><li>piece of land: tract, land, field</li><li>country (opposite town), countryside</li><li>country, nation</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Beekes compares <i>χήρα</i> (khḗra, “widow”), assuming common derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰeh₁-</i> (“to leave behind”), with original meaning "unoccupied (left-behind) space". This is far from certain, however.</p><p>Older theories take the word as possibly loaned from a Pre-Greek substrate or other regional language. Has been tentatively and inconclusively compared with <i>χᾰ́ος</i> (khắos, “empty space, abyss, chasm”) as well as <i>χατέω</i> (khatéō, “to lack, miss, need, desire”); Beekes dismisses these connections as unfounded, particularly that with <i>χατέω</i> (khatéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>χώρᾱ</i> (khṓrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>χεῖ</i> (kheî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) unripe wheaten groats, rubbed from the ear in the hands</li></ol><p>Pisani unsuccessfully attempted to link the word to <i>κριθή</i> (krithḗ, “barley”). According to Beekes, it is a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earth thrown up, a bank, mound</li><li>dike, dam</li><li>mole (massive structure used as a pier or breakwater), pier</li><li>sepulchral mound</li></ol><p>From <i>χώννῡμι</i> (khṓnnūmi, “I heap up”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>space, room, place (in which a thing is)</li><li>dwelling place</li><li>locality</li><li>station</li><li>district, region, territory, country, land</li><li>field, farm, estate</li><li>northeast wind</li></ol><p>Related to <i>χώρα</i> (khṓra, “location”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heaping up (e.g. of earth); a raising of a mound or bank (e.g. against a city)</li><li>a filling up, blocking by earth thrown in</li><li>embankment</li></ol><p>From <i>χόω</i> (khóō, “to throw or heap up”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of ointment used in Egypt</li></ol><p>From Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>oblique, askew</li><li>blundering</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, maybe of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) cinnabar</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested in Hesychius.</p><p>Possibly related to <i>ψαθυρός</i> (psathurós, “friable, crumbling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ψωμία</i> (psōmía, “small crumbles, morsels”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, maybe from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>ψαθυρός</i> (psathurós, “friable, brittle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>new-born animal</li></ol><p>From <i>ψᾰκᾰ́ς</i> (psăkắs, “drop”). The same metaphor is found with <i>δρόσος</i> (drósos, “dew”) and <i>ἕρση</i> (hérsē, “dew”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μέγα</i> (méga, “big, large”).</li></ol><p>Maybe from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>σφάκελος</i> (sphákelos, “middle finger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curb chain</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) chain, bond</li></ol><p>With <i>ψέλιον</i> (psélion, “armlet worn by the Persians”) and <i>ψαλόν</i> (psalón, “kind of bridle”) an Iranian borrowing; synonymous to Classical Persian افسار (afsār, “curb chain, halter, headstall”), and note also افسر (afsar, “diadem”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch sharply; to pluck; to pull; to twitch; to twang</li><li>to play a stringed instrument with the fingers</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to sing (a psalm)</li></ol><p>Unknown; probably Pre-Greek. More at <i>ψᾱ́ω</i> (psā́ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) tune played on a stringed instrument</li></ol><p>From <i>ψᾰ́λλω</i> (psắllō, “to pluck, twitch”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lyre</li></ol><p>From <i>ψάλλω</i> (psállō, “to pluck, twitch”). Attested as a gloss in Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand of the seashore</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) grains of sand</li><li>countless multitude</li></ol><p>Usually taken as a cross of <i>ψάμμος</i> (psámmos, “sand”) and <i>ἄμαθος</i> (ámathos, “sand”). Furnée adduces Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;samdaz</i> (“sand”), so it could also directly continue a word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sámh₂dʰos</i> (“sand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sandy, made of sand</li></ol><p>From <i>ψάμμος</i> (psámmos, “sand”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand</li><li>something worthless</li><li>metallic ore used by alchemists</li><li>desert</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Could be from a <i>&#42;ψάφ-μος</i> (&#42;psáph-mos) (compare the pair <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō), <i>γράμμα</i> (grámma)), and thus perhaps related to <i>ψαφαρός</i> (psapharós, “powdery, sandy”), <i>ψῆφος</i> (psêphos, “pebble”), as well as Latin <i>sabulum</i> (“gravel”). Possibly from the same root as <i>ψῆν</i> (psên, “to rub, grate, scratch”), though the formal relationship, if there is one, is unknown. Onomatopoeia may be involved.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starling (<i>Sturnus vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;spḗr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;spḗr</i> (“sparrow, bird”). Cognate with English <i>sparrow</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Doric</i>) alternative spelling of <i>ψήφῐσμᾰ</i> (psḗphĭsmă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to touch lightly; to rub; to wipe; to rub smooth</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to crumble away, vanish, disappear</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared to Sanskrit <i>प्साति॑</i> (psā́ti, “to chew, eat, devour”) and explained as vocalic enlargements from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰes-</i>, found in Sanskrit <i>बभ॑स्ति</i> (bábhasti, “to chew, devour”). Meier-Brügger links the word to Hittite (/⁠peš⁠/, “to rub, scrub”), which departs from the root <i>&#42;pes-</i>. According to Beekes, the Indo-European explanations must be given up; the group of words is probably Pre-Greek, especially given the coexistence of <i>ψαίω</i> (psaíō), <i>ψαύω</i> (psaúō) and <i>ψίω</i> (psíō), which cannot be explained if we start from a PIE form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>τάφος</i> (táphos, “grave”).</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blame, chide</li></ol><p>Unclear, though apparently of later creation than other Greek terms for the concept of "blame" such as <i>ὄνειδος</i> (óneidos, “blame, criticism”) and <i>μέμφομαι</i> (mémphomai, “to accuse, blame”). Beekes rejects connections with <i>ψῆν</i> (psên, “touch, rub”) and <i>ψό</i> (psó, interjection), and tentatively considers the word Pre-Greek, based on the initial <i>ψ-</i> (ps-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armlet, anklet, ornament worn by the Persians</li></ol><p>With <i>ψάλιον</i> (psálion, “curb-chain”) and <i>ψαλόν</i> (psalón, “bridle”) an Iranian borrowing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ψέλιον</i> (psélion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gloom, darkness</li></ol><p>A rhyming formation to <i>κνέφας</i> (knéphas). Usually Sanskrit <i>क्षप्</i> (kṣáp, “night”) is considered to be somehow related, but a precise connection cannot be established. Furnée takes <i>σεῖφα</i> (seîpha, “darkness, gloom”) as a shortened form of this word and also suggests to connect <i>ζέφυρος</i> (zéphuros, “west wind”). The variations would point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>currycomb for horses</li></ol><p>From <i>ψήχω</i> (psḗkhō, “to rub down, curry”) +&lrm; <i>-τρα</i> (-tra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gall wasp (insect of the family Cynipidae)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek. The connection with <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub, grate”) is hardly possible. The suffix <i>-ήν</i> can be found also in <i>κηφήν</i> (kēphḗn, “drone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of marble</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῆφος</i> (psêphos, “pebble, precious stone”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a proposal passed by a majority vote</li><li>a decree or law</li></ol><p>From <i>ψηφίζω</i> (psēphízō, “vote”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub down, curry a horse</li><li>to stroke, pat</li><li>to rub down, wear away</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to scribble</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub, wipe”), <i>ψωλός</i> (psōlós, “circumcised”), <i>ψώρα</i> (psṓra, “mange”), <i>ψωρός</i> (psōrós, “itchy, scabby”), <i>ψωχός</i> (psōkhós, “sandy”) and <i>ψώχω</i> (psṓkhō, “to rub small”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mat made of rush used for sleeping on</li><li>blind, screen, covering for a window</li><li>sack used for carriage of wool or stone</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>γυργαθός</i> (gurgathós) and <i>κάλαθος</i> (kálathos). The variation "α/ε" leads to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>epithet of a kind of grape</li></ol><p>Unknown. Probably Pre-Greek, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whispering, slanderous</li><li>twittering, chirping</li></ol><p>If it dissimilated from <i>*ψυθυρ-</i>, it may belong to <i>ψύθος</i> (psúthos, “lie, untruth”). On the other hand, it can be compared to <i>ψίζομαι</i> (psízomai, “to weep”). Pisani considered a relationship with Sanskrit <i>क्ष्वेडति</i> (kṣveḍati, “to hum, murmur”). Given the variants, the word is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>πτῐ́λον</i> (ptĭ́lon, “feather; wing”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stripping bare (of flesh, hair, leaves, etc.)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) a writing or pronouncing with the spiritus lenis, or of an unaspirated consonant; psilosis</li></ol><p>From <i>ψιλόω</i> (psilóō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis), from <i>ψιλός</i> (psilós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white lead, especially used as a pigment to whiten the skin</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variations prove a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crumb; morsel; bit (especially of bread)</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῑ́ω</i> (psī́ō, “to feed on”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friable, brittle</li></ol><p>The formation looks like that of <i>καπυρός</i> (kapurós). possibly from <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “crumble”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀραιόθριχα</i> (araióthrikha, “with thin hair”).</li></ol><p>Furnée compares the adjective with <i>ψαθάλλω</i> (psathállō, “to grind, to scratch”), which is probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>false; vain</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from <i>ψαίω</i> (psaíō, “to grind, to triturate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raindrop; particle</li><li>drizzle; heavy rain; shower</li><li>sputterer</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ἰκμᾰ́ς</i> (ikmắs), <i>λῐβᾰ́ς</i> (lĭbắs) and <i>ψῐᾰ́ς</i> (psĭắs). According to Beekes, in view of the suffix <i>-ᾰδ-</i> (-ăd-) it is probably Pre-Greek. The connection with Lithuanian <i>spākas</i> (“drop; point”) may be correct, in which case both words go back to a substrate language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scissors, shears</li><li>sewer, drain</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) vault, crypt, barrel vault</li><li>rounded moulding between the capital and the column</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) rings for the staves of the altar of incense</li></ol><p>Uncertain but perhaps related to <i>ψάλιον</i> (psálion, “curb chain”) and <i>ψέλιον</i> (psélion, “armlet”). Maybe from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of crabs</i>) nipper-mouthed</li></ol><p>From <i>ψᾰλῐ́ς</i> (psălĭ́s, “scissors, shears”) +&lrm; <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ὕλη</i> (húlē, “wood, forest”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>twitching or twanging with the fingers</li><li>sound of the cithara or harp</li><li>(<i>music</i>) song sung to the harp</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) psalm</li></ol><p>From <i>ψᾰ́λλω</i> (psắllō, “to twitch”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singing with harp accompaniment</li></ol><p>From <i>ψᾰλμῳδός</i> (psălmōidós, “psalmist”) (from <i>ψᾰλμός</i> (psălmós, “plucking strings”) + <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song”)) + <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psalmist</li></ol><p>From <i>ψᾰλμός</i> (psălmós, “psalm”) +&lrm; <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stringed instrument, harp</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;Christianity) psalter</li></ol><p>From <i>ψάλλω</i> (psállō, “to touch sharply, to pluck, to pull, to twitch”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion), from <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to touch lightly, to rub”), which acquired the meaning of plucking a harp, and later making music in general.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a kind of bridle, a curb chain</li></ol><p>Apparently near-synonymous with <i>χαλινός</i> (khalinós, “bridle”). From the same root as <i>ψάλιον</i> (psálion, “curb-chain”), <i>ψέλιον</i> (psélion, “anklet worn by the Persians”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>αἰγιαλός</i> (aigialós, “beach, shore”).</li></ol><p>From <i>ψάμαθος</i> (psámathos, “sand”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a fish that buries itself in the sand (literally sand-diver)</li></ol><p><i>ψάμμος</i> (psámmos, “sand”) +&lrm; <i>δύω</i> (dúō, “dive”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speckled, dappled, like a starling</li></ol><p>From <i>ψάρ</i> (psár) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>καλλοπιστής</i> (kallopistḗs, “dandy”), <i>ταχύς</i> (takhús, “swift”), <i>ἐλαφρός</i> (elaphrós, “light”), <i>ἀραιός</i> (araiós, “thin, slender”).</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek origin, like <i>σαυκρόν</i> (saukrón).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>friable; powdery; crumbling; crumbly</li><li>sandy</li><li>(<i>of semiliquids</i>) thin; watery</li><li>(<i>of wine</i>) rough; dry</li></ol><p>From a zero grade of <i>ψῆφος</i> (psêphos) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κονιορτός</i> (koniortós, “cloud of dust”), <i>φορυτός</i> (phorutós, “whatever the wind carries along”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>σῖτον</i> (sîton, “bread”) and <i>ὀλίγον</i> (olígon, “small, little”).</li></ol><p>From <i>ψαίω</i> (psaíō, “to grind, to triturate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touch, caress</li></ol><p>From <i>ψαύω</i> (psaúō, “to touch, to graze”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thin, spare, scanty</li><li>bald-headed, bare, naked</li></ol><p>Connection with <i>ψῆν</i> (psên, “gall insect”) is not evident. Beside this adjective, we find synonymous <i>ψηνός</i> (psēnós, “bald-headed”), <i>ψανός</i> (psanós, “bald-headed”) and <i>ψιλός</i> (psilós, “with thin hair”). The word is probably Pre-Greek, like many other words with initial <i>ψ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>λόγοι</i> (lógoi, “computation, reckoning”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Koine form of <i>ψακάς</i> (psakás)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>faltering in speech, like a child</li><li>(<i>of words</i>) inarticulate, unintelligible</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing a false report</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδο-</i> (pseudo-, “false, lying”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos, “messenger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) false brother, pretended Christian</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>ἀδελφός</i> (adelphós, “brother”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mock silver, false silver, perhaps zinc</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδο-</i> (pseudo-, “false”) +&lrm; <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying, false, untrue</li><li>deceived, beguiled</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ψεύδω</i> (pseúdō, “to lie”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false report</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδᾰ́γγελος</i> (pseudắngelos, “bringing a false report”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) false ambassador or apostle</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδο-</i> (pseudo-, “false”) +&lrm; <i>ἀπόστολος</i> (apóstolos, “apostle; ambassador”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with false superscription or title, not genuine</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδο-</i> (pseudo-, “false”) +&lrm; <i>ἐπῐγρᾰφή</i> (epĭgrăphḗ, “inscription”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan pimpinell (<i>Pimpinella cretica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>βούνιον</i> (boúnion, “earthnut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer of falsehoods</li><li>drawer of false diagrams</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false, lying herald</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>κῆρυξ</i> (kêrux, “herald”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking falsely, lying</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to say”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false witness</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδο-</i> (pseudo-) +&lrm; <i>μάρτυς</i> (mártus).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pretended maid or virgin</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰρθένος</i> (părthénos, “virgin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false prophet</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδο-</i> (pseudo-, “false”) +&lrm; <i>προφήτης</i> (prophḗtēs, “prophet, interpreter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sham purple garment</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra, “purple dye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>creeping cinquefoil (<i>Potentilla reptans</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>σέλῑνον</i> (sélīnon, “celery”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of mock gold</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) false chrysolite</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under a false name, falsely called</li></ol><p>From <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνῠμᾰ</i> (ónŭmă, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gloomy, cloudy</li></ol><p>From <i>ψέφᾰς</i> (pséphăs) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to lie, deceive</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;indirect reflexive) to lie for one's own benefit, cheat by lies</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be lied to, be cheated</li><li>(<i>non-agentive</i>) to be mistaken, be wrong</li></ol></ol><p>No convincing Indo-European etymology; probably from Pre-Greek, as evidenced by the irregular alternation between /d/ and /tʰ/ in related terms, the latter in e.g. <i>ψύθος</i> (psúthos, “lie, untruth”), ψυθῶνες (psuthônes, “slanderers”) and the Homeric aorist <i>ἔψυθεν</i> (épsuthen). The oft-compared Old Armenian <i>սուտ</i> (sut, “false; falsehood, lie”) may still be related, if derived from a substrate language related to Pre-Greek. Compare also <i>ψύδραξ</i> (psúdrax, “pustule(s)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liar, cheat</li></ol><p>From <i>ψεύδω</i> (pseúdō, “to lie”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>psi, the name for the twenty-third letter Ψ, ψ of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a falsehood, a lie, a figment</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>touch, caress</li></ol><p>From <i>ψηλᾰφάω</i> (psēlăpháō, “to touch, caress”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel or grope about to find a thing, like a blind man</li><li>to feel, touch, handle, stroke</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to test, examine</li></ol><p>Analyzed by Fick as a univerbation from the aorist form of <i>ψάλλω</i> (psállō, “to pluck, pull, twitch”) (the ultimate source of <i>psalm</i>) and <i>ἀφάω</i> (apháō, “to handle”). It remains unclear, however, why the aorist was preferred. Bechtel recognises a noun <i>&#42;ψᾱ́λα</i> (&#42;psā́la) in the first member, referring to <i>μηλαφάω</i> (mēlapháō, “to touch with a probe”); but this rare verb was created after the example of <i>ψηλαφάω</i> (psēlapháō), which is more common. If <i>-αφ-</i> (-aph-) is rather a suffix, the word could be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that can be felt, touched</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective, from <i>ψηλᾰφᾰ́ω</i> (psēlăphắō, “to feel, grope”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to count or reckon, properly with pebbles</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to add up the numerical values of the letters of a word</li></ol><li>(<i>properly</i>) to cast one's vote with a pebble</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(absolute,&#32;generally) to vote</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>construed with the dative</i>) to vote in support of</li></ol><li>(<i>construed with the accusative</i>) to vote for, to carry by vote</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>construed with a duplicated accusative</i>) to vote to adjudge (someone) to be (a thing)</li></ol><li>(<i>construed with the infinitive</i>) to vote, to resolve to do (something)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>construed with the accusative and infinitive</i>) to vote that ⸻</li></ol><li>(+ περί (perí, “about”) / ὑπέρ (hupér, “for”) + genitive) vote</li></ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to decide by vote</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(passive voice,&#32;aorist) to be voted</li></ol></ol><p><i>ψῆφος</i> (psêphos, “a pebble”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō, suffix forming verbs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small pebble, (piece of) gravel</li><li>pellet</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῆφος</i> (psêphos, “pebble”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A box for counters or ballots</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῆφος</i> (psêphos, “vote”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>account-board</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῆφος</i> (psêphos, “pebble; vote”) +&lrm; <i>λογεῖον</i> (logeîon, “stage, platform”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vote by ballot, voting</li><li>judgement, decree</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) calculation</li></ol><p>From <i>ψηφοφόρος</i> (psēphophóros, “voter”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>giving one's vote, voter</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῆφος</i> (psêphos, “pebble; counter; vote”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-phore, suffix from <i>φόρος</i> (phóros), verb <i>φέρω</i> (“bring”)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drop</li></ol><p>Found beside <i>ψᾰκᾰ́ς</i> (psăkắs, “drop”), so Beekes points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;orthography) smooth breathing (diacritical mark indicating a lack of aspiration)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>orthography</i>) to write with a single vowel letter (not a digraph)</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῑλός</i> (psīlós, “[of the sounds /e/ and /y/] written with the single letters ⟨ε⟩ and ⟨υ⟩ [rather than the digraphs ⟨αι⟩ and ⟨οι⟩]”) +&lrm; <i>γρᾰ́φος</i> (grắphos, “written character, letter”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, suffix forming verbs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write with a smooth breathing</li></ol><p><i>ψῑλός</i> (psīlós, “[of vowels] written without the dasia [∴ with the psile]”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “compose”, “write”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) light soldier (lightly-armed soldier)</li><li>(Koine,&#32;phonetics,&#32;orthography):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a vowel with smooth breathing: ἀ (a), ἐ (e), ἠ (ē), ἰ (i), ὀ (o), ὐ (u), ὠ (ō)</li><li>a tenuis consonant, π (p), τ (t), κ (k), typically in contrast with the aspirated consonants φ (ph), θ (th), χ (kh)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>naked, bare</li><li>bald, smooth, hairless</li><li>unclad, uncovered</li><li>small, frail, delicate</li><li>simple, plain</li><li>(<i>military</i>) light (troops)</li><li>unarmed</li><li>(<i>of words</i>) without meter (i.e. prose)</li><li>(<i>poetry</i>) without music (epic vs lyrical)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) without singing (instrumentals)</li><li>(<i>singing</i>) without accompaniment (a cappella)</li><li>(phonetics,&#32;of a vowel) with smooth breathing, as in ἀ (a), ἐ (e), ἠ (ē), ἰ (i), ὀ (o), ὐ (u), ὠ (ō)</li><li>(phonetics,&#32;of a consonant) tenuis, as in π (p), τ (t), κ (k)</li><li>(orthography,&#32;of the sounds /e/ and /y/) written with the single letters ⟨ε⟩ and ⟨υ⟩ rather than the digraphs ⟨αι⟩ and ⟨οι⟩</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin; possibly related to <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub, wipe”), and thus of Pre-Greek origin, though the morphology remains unclear if indeed related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bareness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>baldness</li><li>(<i>of a woman’s body</i>) smoothness</li></ol><li>tenuity</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>psile</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ψῑλός</i> (psīlós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strip (something) bare, especially:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to depilate; to hair (make hairless)</li><li>to pluck (make featherless)</li><li>to rob so as to deprive</li><li>(phonology,&#32;orthography) to pronounce or write (a letter or word) with smooth breathing</li></ol><li>(<i>ditransitive</i>) to strip (something) clean of (something) [<i>with</i> genitive and accusative]</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become exposed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to become bald</li><li>to be stripped of things</li><li>(phonology,&#32;orthography) to be written or pronounced with smooth breathing</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ψῑλός</i> (psīlós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(phonetics,&#32;orthography) smooth breathing, spiritus lenis</li></ol><p><i>ψῑλός</i> (psīlós, “bare, bald”) +&lrm; <i>πνεῦμα</i> (pneûma, “breath”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ψῑ́μυθος</i> (psī́muthos): white lead (as a pigment to whiten the face or hair)</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῑ́μυθος</i> (psī́muthos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parrot</li></ol><p>Unknown. Likely of non-Greek/Pre-Greek origin. Sometimes compared to Sanskrit <i>शुक</i> (śuka, “parrot”), but Furnee rejects the comparison.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of animals</i>) ash-coloured, dun</li></ol><p>From <i>ψοῖθος</i> (psoîthos, “ash”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós). For the formation, compare <i>κνηκός</i> (knēkós, “tawny, pale yellow”), which is from <i>κνῆκος</i> (knêkos, “safflower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>σποδός</i> (spodós, “wood-ashes, embers”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>ψόθος</i> (psóthos, “uncleanness, foulness”) and <i>ψόλος</i> (psólos, “soot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ψυδρός</i> (psudrós, “untrue, lying, false”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>false, lying, untrue</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs, “false, untrue”) and <i>ψύθος</i> (psúthos, “lie, untruth”). The zero grade found in this word agrees with Armenian <i>սուտ</i> (sut, “false; lie”). Clackson is inclined to believe the proposal that the Greek word is related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰes-</i> (“wind”), like <i>ψύχω</i> (psúkhō, “to breathe, blow”); semantic parallels would be <i>ψίθυρος</i> (psíthuros, “whispering, slanderous”) and <i>ψύδραξ</i> (psúdrax, “pustule, blister”). However, according to Beekes, Pre-Greek origin is a better explanation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine cooler</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cool, shady places for recreation</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῡ́χω</i> (psū́khō, “to cool; to refresh”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cooling</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter&#32;plural) refrigerants</li><li>bringing difficulty, embarrassing</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῡ́χω</i> (psū́khō, “to chill, make cold”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The animating principle of a human or animal body, vital spirit, soul, life (<i>the animating principle of life</i>).</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>poetic</i>) Life-breath, lifeblood (<i>‘the animating principle of life’ in corporeal interpretation</i>).</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;since the early physicists) Animating principle in primary substances, the source of life and consciousness.</li><li>(<i>Septuagint</i>) A person</li></ol><li>Animate existence, viewed as a possession, one’s life.</li><li>The spirit or soul thought of as distinct from the body and leaving it at death (<i>the immortal part of a person</i>).</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A disembodied spirit, a shade or ghost (<i>the spirit of a dead person</i>).</li></ol><li>Spirit (<i>animated attitude</i>), conscious self, personality as centre of emotions, desires and affections, heart.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(philosophy,&#32;after Plato) The spirit of the universe, the immaterial principle of movement and life.</li></ol><li>The mind (<i>seat or organ of thought</i>), (the faculty of) reason.</li><li>(rare,&#32;extended from the meaning ‘soul’) Butterfly.</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>ψῡ́χω</i> (psū́khō, “to blow”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē). The original meaning is thus supposed to have been 'breath'. Compare the origins of Latin <i>anima</i> and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;duša</i> (see Latin <i>dusius</i>).</p><p>Compare Proto-Circassian <i>&#42;psa</i>, Proto-Northwest Caucasian <i>&#42;pǝsA</i> (literally “soul”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ψυχή)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Psyche</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῡχή</i> (psūkhḗ, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to life or the soul</li><li>concerned only with the life or animal qualities, as opposed with spiritual concerns</li></ol><p><i>ψῡχή</i> (psūkhḗ, “breath, spirit”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soul-guiding, -conducting</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guide or conductor of souls (Charon), psychopomp</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῡχο-</i> (psūkho-, “soul”) +&lrm; <i>πομπός</i> (pompós, “conductor”), from <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō, “to send”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weighing of lives</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῡχο-</i> (psūkho-, “life”) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to weigh”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sustaining life or soul</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῡχο-</i> (psūkho-, “soul”) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to maintain, sustain”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cold, frigid</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) cold-hearted, without feeling, lifeless</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) ineffectual</li></ol><p>From <i>ψύχω</i> (psúkhō, “to blow”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ψῡχρός</i> (psūkhrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>betony (<i>Betonica officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῦχος</i> (psûkhos, “cold”) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to maintain, sustain”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pschent (double crown of ancient Egypt)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>pꜣ-sḫmtj</i> (“the two powerful ones”), from <i>pꜣ</i> (definite article) + <i>sḫmtj</i>, dual of <i>sḫmt</i> (“powerful one”), from <i>sḫm</i> (“to be powerful, to have power over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>penis with the foreskin drawn back (cf. Latin <i>verpa</i>)</li><li>circumcised penis</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, related to the same root as <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub, wipe”), <i>ψωμός</i> (psōmós, “morsel, bit”), <i>ψωλός</i> (psōlós, “with the foreskin drawn back; circumcised”), <i>ψώρα</i> (psṓra, “mange”) and <i>ψωχός</i> (psōkhós, “sandy”). According to Beekes, inherited explanations must be given up; the group of words is probably Pre-Greek, especially given the coexistence of <i>ψαίω</i> (psaíō), <i>ψαύω</i> (psaúō) and <i>ψίω</i> (psíō); perhaps loaned from another Indo-European language and ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to cover, wrap; skin, hide; cloth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a man whose foreskin is retracted</li><li>(<i>especially</i>) a man with an erection, a horny man</li><li>a circumcised man</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ψωλή</i> (psōlḗ, “penis with a retracted or removed foreskin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small morsel</li></ol><p>From <i>ψωμός</i> (psōmós, “morsel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morsel, bit</li></ol><p>Traditionally compared to Sanskrit <i>प्साति</i> (psā́ti, “to eat, consume”). However, Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin, together with <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub, wipe, touch lightly”), <i>ψωλός</i> (psōlós, “circumcised”), <i>ψώρα</i> (psṓra, “mange”), <i>ψωρός</i> (psōrós, “itchy, scabby”), and <i>ψωχός</i> (psōkhós, “sandy”). The word may have been formed as <i>ψώω</i> (psṓō), an alternative form of <i>ψάω</i> (psáō), + <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) itch, scabies, mange</li></ol><p>From <i>ψωριάω</i> (psōriáō, “to have the itch, scabies, or mange”) (from <i>ψώρᾱ</i> (psṓrā)) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rosemary (<i>Salvia rosmarinus</i>, syn. <i>Rosmarinus officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ψώρα</i> (psṓra, “scabies, mange”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνθεμίς</i> (anthemís, “flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>itchy, scabby, mangy</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub, wipe”), <i>ψωμός</i> (psōmós, “morsel, bit”), <i>ψωλός</i> (psōlós, “circumcised”), <i>ψώρα</i> (psṓra, “mange”) and <i>ψωχός</i> (psōkhós, “sandy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sandy</li></ol><p>Apparently from the root of <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub, crumble away”) and/or <i>ψήχω</i> (psḗkhō, “to rub down, wear away”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>exclamation of disgust or contempt: ew, yuck, ugh</li></ol><p>Expressive sound, compare English <i>pshaw</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) muscles of the loins</li></ol><p>The word is related to similar words meaning "foxes", like <i>ψειαί</i> (pseiaí), <i>ψίαι</i> (psíai), <i>φοῦαι</i> (phoûai) and <i>φύλλες</i> (phúlles). These variations cannot be all understood in Indo-European terms. The connection with <i>ὀσφῦς</i> (osphûs, “hips or loins”), suggested by Prellwitz, is quite possible. According to Beekes, the word is clearly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blame, criticism</li></ol><p>From <i>ψέγω</i> (pségō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncleanness, foulness</li></ol><p>Closest to <i>ψόλος</i> (psólos, “soot”). Furnée connects <i>ψοῖθος</i> (psoîthos, “ashes”) and assumes also <i>&#42;ψῶδος</i> (&#42;psôdos, “dirt”), on the basis of <i>ψωδαρέον</i> (psōdaréon, “dry, parched; squalid, wretched”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soot, smoke</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>ἄσβολος</i> (ásbolos, “soot”), <i>ψόθος</i> (psóthos, “foulness, uncleanness”), <i>ψοῖθος</i> (psoîthos, “ashes”) and <i>σποδός</i> (spodós, “ashes”), suggesting a common Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sounds or noises not produced by the human voice: clattering, rattling, knocking, crashing</li></ol><p>Like <i>ψεῦδος</i> (pseûdos) and <i>ψύθος</i> (psúthos), it may originally be onomatopoeic and somehow belong with the interjection <i>ψό</i> (psó). Alternatively, Petersson derives the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰes-</i>, like <i>ψύχω</i> (psúkhō, “to breathe, blow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lie, untruth</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ψυδρός</i> (psudrós, “false”) and <i>ψεύδομαι</i> (pseúdomai, “to lie; to be mistaken, false”), and thus probably of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the interchange <i>δ</i>/<i>θ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flea</li><li>type of venomous spider, perhaps a type of widow</li><li>type of crop-eating insect</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;plusy(e)h₂</i> (“flea”) by metathesis. Cognates include Latin <i>pūlex</i>, Sanskrit <i>प्लुषि</i> (plúṣi), Old Armenian <i>լու</i> (lu), Old English <i>flēah</i>, <i>flēa</i> (English <i>flea</i>), Albanian <i>plesht</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fleawort (<i><i>Plantago psyllium</i></i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ψύλλα</i> (psúlla, “flea”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ψύλλα</i> (psúlla, “flea”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀκάθαρτος</i> (akáthartos, “uncleansed, foul”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe; to blow</li><li>to chill (to make cold)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to cool; torefresh</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;figuratively) to be frigid</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dry</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Could be from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰes-</i> (“to blow”), with cognates including Sanskrit <i>भस्त्रा॑</i> (bhástrā, “bellows”), <i>अप्सु॑</i> (ápsu, “without breath”), and Albanian <i>badër</i>. Beekes is skeptical of an Indo-European derivation and argues for a Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>Compare also Proto-Circassian <i>&#42;psa</i>, Proto-Northwest Caucasian <i>&#42;pǝsA</i> (literally “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) giving soul or life to, animating, quickening</li><li>principle of life</li></ol><p>From <i>ψῡχόω</i> (psūkhóō) +&lrm; <i>-ωσις</i> (-ōsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>itch, mange, scurvy (of men and beasts)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) disease of trees, scab</li><li>kind of moth</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub, wipe”), <i>ψωμός</i> (psōmós, “morsel, bit”), <i>ψωλός</i> (psōlós, “circumcised”), <i>ψωρός</i> (psōrós, “itchy, mangy”) and <i>ψωχός</i> (psōkhós, “sandy”). The word has also been compared to Lithuanian <i>sóra</i> (“millet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is rubbed or scraped off, shavings, scrapings, chips</li></ol><p>From <i>ψήχω</i> (psḗkhō, “to rub down, curry”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of flatfish, perhaps the turbot (<i>Scophthalmus maximus</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) glutton, gourmandizer</li></ol><p>Unclear. Frisk suggests it could stand for <i>&#42;ψήχια</i> (&#42;psḗkhia), from <i>ψήχω</i> (psḗkhō, “to rub down”) (because of its hard, raw skin), comparing <i>θρίσσα</i> (thríssa, “kind of fish”) and <i>θρίξ</i> (thríx, “hair”). Of course, this is only a suggestion.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pebble</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a precious stone, gem</li></ol><li>(<i>based on the uses made of such pebbles</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a pebble used for reckoning, a counter</li><li>a pebble used for a draught or chessman</li><li>a pebble used for divination</li><li>a pebble used for voting</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>that which is carried by vote, a vote</li><li>resolve, decree, judgment</li><li>a place of voting, poll</li></ol></ol></ol><p>Traditionally considered related to <i>ψάω</i> (psáō, “to rub smooth, crumble”), though the morphological function of <i>-φ-</i> (-ph-) would be unclear.</p><p>Alternatively, or perhaps in conjunction with the above, assuming a root <i>ψῆφ-</i> (psêph-), related to <i>ψάμμος</i> (psámmos, “sand”) and its presumed relatives.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ψεῖ</i> (pseî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a means of cooling</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a cooling lotion; a cooling medicine</li><li>a breath drawn; an inhalation</li><li>a cold; chilling behaviour</li><li>a chink; a fissure</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ψῡ́χω</i> (psū́khō, “to chill”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A cooling, a chilling</li><li>A being or becoming cold</li></ol><p>From <i>ψύχω</i> (psúkhō, “to chill”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cold</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Corinthian and Old Attic form of <i>Κόρῐνθος</i> (Kórĭnthos, “Corinth”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>κόππα</i> (kóppa)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤒</i> (q /⁠qōp⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>κόρη</i> (kórē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>Lower case digamma, the sixth letter of the early Greek alphabet. Its name was probably <i>&#42;ϝαῦ</i> (&#42;waû), later <i>δίγαμμα</i> (dígamma), and it represented a voiced labial-velar approximant /w/.</li></ol><p>Derived from its majuscule counterpart Ϝ, from the Phoenician letter 𐤅, waw.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ᾰ̓́νᾰξ</i> (ắnăx)</li></ol><p>From the same source as <i>ᾰ̓́νᾰξ</i> (ắnăx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Doric</i>) alternative form of <i>ἕξ</i> (héx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Locrian form of <i>ἔρρω</i> (érrhō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, carry, bring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wékʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéǵʰeti</i> (“to be transporting”), from the root <i>&#42;weǵʰ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>vehō</i>, Sanskrit <i>वहति</i> (váhati), Old English <i>wegan</i> (whence English <i>weigh</i>). The Koine form would have been <i>&#42;ἔχω</i> (&#42;ékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>μέδιμνος</i> (médimnos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>οἶνος</i> (oînos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbreviation of <i>ν μναῖ</i> (n mnaî, “50 mina”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in patronyms</i>) namesake of the same</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>The alpha privativum, used to make words that have a sense opposite to the word (or stem) to which the prefix is attached. It is also known as privative a and alpha privative&#59; a-, ab-, an-, anti-, contra-, de-, dis-, ex-, il-, im-, in-, ob-, ne-, non-, not, un-</li><li>alternative form of <i>ἁ-</i> (ha-)</li><li>The alpha intensivum, used to strengthen the force of compounds.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ə-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-</i>. The form <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) appears when followed by a laryngeal and a vowel: that is, in an open syllable of the <i>&#42;n̥HV-</i> form. Akin to <i>νη-</i> (nē-), <i>νᾱ-</i> (nā-) and <i>νω-</i> (nō-), which are closed-syllable reflexes of the <i>&#42;n̥h₁C-</i>, <i>&#42;n̥h₂C-</i>, <i>&#42;n̥h₃C-</i> forms, respectively.</p><p>Cognate with Old English and English <i>un-</i>, Latin <i>in-</i>, Sanskrit <i>अ-</i> (a-), Old Irish <i>in-</i>, <i>an-</i>, Armenian <i>ան-</i> (an-), and Albanian <i>e-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destructive</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>εἴδω</i> (eídō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see”). Compare Russian <i>невидаль</i> (nevidalʹ, “something unprecedented, surprising, not seen before”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>philosophical</i>) timeless, eternal; existing always, without beginning or end; not subject to generation or decay</li><li>everlasting, perpetual; permanently established or irreversible</li><li>(rare,&#32;religious) unending, irrevocable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀεί</i> (aeí, “always”) +&lrm; <i>-διος</i> (-dios, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to move quickly, dart, shoot</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be eager for with the prepositions <i>εἰς</i> (eis), <i>ἐπῐ́</i> (epĭ́), or <i>πρός</i> (prós) governing the accusative</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps cognate with Sanskrit <i>वेविज</i> (vevija, “quick”), <i>यात्</i> (yāt, “moving”), <i>वेविज्यते</i> (vevijyate, “to flee from”), though Beekes calls this "formally impossible" because of the long <i>-ī-.</i> Possibly instead related to <i>αἰόλος</i> (aiólos, “swift, nimble, quick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to perceive (<i>with genitive or accusative</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with the ear</i>) to hear</li><li>(<i>with the eye</i>) to see</li><li>to have heard</li><li>to listen, obey</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ew-</i> (“to perceive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>inviolable; invincible; infallible</li></ol></ol><p>Both the meaning and the etymology are uncertain. Possibly from <i>ἀάω</i> (aáō, “to hurt; to damage”) with privative alpha <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) (negation) and <i>-τος</i> (-tos). In this case it might also be related to <i>ἄτη</i> (átē), which, in the epic period, meant a kind of mental blindness or infatuation. Traditionally, this led to an explanation of the word as "immune to ἄτη." Neither <i>ἀάω</i> (aáō) nor <i>ἄτη</i> (átē) is definitively linked to any known Proto-Indo-European root, although phonetic rules could connect them to <i>&#42;h₂euh₂-</i>.</p><p>An alternative hypothesis given for the use of the word as a Homeric epithet for the Styx is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥seh₂wn̥tos</i> (“sunless”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) a kind of earring</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from <i>&#42;αὐσ-άνθα</i> (&#42;aus-ántha), from the root of <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”), but the suffix is unclear. Alternatively from a Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;awántha</i> whence Albanian <i>vath</i> (“earring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) to hurt or damage the mind: mislead, infatuate (in relation to the effects of non-human things)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;ἀϝάω</i> (&#42;awáō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewh₂-</i>, though without obvious cognates. Linked by Cohen/Hyllested (via their proposed sound law **h₃w>*h₂w) to Homeric <i>οὖτα</i> (oûta) (cf. <i>οὐτᾰ́ω</i> (outắō, “to wound”)) and Lithuanian <i>voti̇̀s</i> (“ulcer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>ἆθλον</i> (âthlon)</li></ol><p><i>ἄεθλον</i> (áethlon) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gaining the prize, running for the prize</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄεθλον</i> (áethlon, “prize”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gaining the prize, running for the prize</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄεθλον</i> (áethlon, “prize”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective-forming suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀέθλιος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aethlius</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀέθλιος</i> (aéthlios, “gaining the prize”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) against the will of</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ἕκητι</i> (hékēti), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weḱ-</i> (“to wish, want”) (compare <i>ἑκών</i> (hekṓn, “willingly”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ᾱ̓́κων</i> (ā́kōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>poetic form of <i>αὐξάνω</i> (auxánō, “to increase, exalt”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awéksō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wég-se-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂weg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to air, like air, aerial</li><li>light blue or gray</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓ήρ</i> (āḗr, “air”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in the mist or thick air of morning</li><li>in the air, high in air</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓ήρ</i> (āḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō)</li></ol><p>Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wér-ye-ti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wer-</i> (“to raise?; to bind, enclose?”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unaccustomedness; novelty of a situation</li><li>inexperience of a thing</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ήθης</i> (ăḗthēs, “unaccustomed”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unusual; strange</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) not used to a thing</li><li>lacking character</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ἦθος</i> (êthos, “custom; character”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>strictly</i>) mist; haze</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) air</li><li>wind</li><li>space, volume</li><li>blue or grey (the colour of the sky)</li></ol><p>Beekes gives Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;auhḗr</i> (“morning mist”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewsér</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂ews-</i> (“dawn; east”). Cognate with <i>αὔρᾱ</i> (aúrā) (&lt; <i>&#42;h₂éwsreh₂</i>), Latin <i>aurōra</i>. The <i>a</i> was lengthened to <i>ā</i> by compensatory lengthening.</p><p>Sihler gives Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;āwḗr</i> (“morning mist”) but provides no further etymology. See Albanian <i>fjur</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undefeated</li><li>invincible</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ἡσσάομαι</i> (hēssáomai, “to be defeated”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blast, gale</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;ᾰ̓ϝήτᾱς</i> (&#42;ăwḗtās). By surface analysis, <i>ᾰ̓́ημῐ</i> (ắēmĭ, “to breathe, blow”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbreakable</li><li>solid</li><li>strong</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ᾱ̓́γνῡμῐ</i> (ā́gnūmĭ, “break”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>simple, stupid</li></ol><p>Wackernagel (1902) connects it with βέλτερος, assuming that an original meaning ‘morally good’ developed into ‘too good, simple’, with an unclear prefix ἀ-. Beekes finds this unlikely. Osthoff (1910) and Hatzidakis (1921) offer different analyses.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>πολύ</i> (polú, “much”), <i>ἐπαχθές</i> (epakhthés, “burdensome”), <i>μέγα</i> (méga, “big”), <i>βαρύ</i> (barú, “heavy”), <i>ἀχάριστον</i> (akháriston, “unpleasant”), <i>μάταιον</i> (mátaion, “vain, empty”).</li></ol><p>The variation points to a Pre-Greek word. Furnée compares Greek <i>βύρβηλο</i> (výrvilo, “abundance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to yearn for; to make noise</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>ἄβα</i> (ába, “wheel; shout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to sing</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>ἄβα</i> (ába, “wheel; shout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indomitable</li></ol><p>Privative <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) with the root of <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō) with a suffix -δ-, cf. <i>βάδην</i> (bádēn), <i>ἐμβάδες</i> (embádes) etc.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A coloured stone for inlaying in mosaic work: abaculus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́βᾰξ</i> (ắbăx, “board”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without weight, weightless</li><li>not burdensome</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>βάρος</i> (báros, “weight”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without pain</li><li>without due examination</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀβασάνιστος</i> (abasánistos, “not tortured; unexamined”) (itself from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>βασανίζω</i> (basanízō, “to examine; to torture”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)) + <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father</li><li>title of respect given to abbots</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>אַבָּא</i> (ʾaḇā, “father”)</p><p>The semantic shift from 'father' to 'abbot' is due to abbots being considered as fathers to the rest of the priests in the abbey, for being the wisest and more experienced.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unharmed, unhurt, secure</li><li>innocuous, harmless, innocent</li><li>averting or preventing harm</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>βλάβη</i> (blábē, “harm”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blindness</li><li>failure to see (<i>something</i>)</li><li>invisibility</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>βλέπω</i> (blépō, “to see”) thus literally, “unseeing-ness”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ill-advisedness, thoughtlessness</li><li>irresolution</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>indecision</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”, <i>alpha privativum</i>) +&lrm; <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ, “will, determination, decision”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract-noun-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small mullet (<i>fish</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἀβρᾰμίς</i> (abrămís, “[salted] mullet”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδιον</i> (-ídion, suffix forming diminutives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of mullet salted in Egypt</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, either Pre-Greek or a loan from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./71f37cec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="r" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./be92bc63.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="m" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f326ba74.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="K1" alt="glyph" height="47"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (rm, “tilapia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wine prepared with southernwood</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀβρότονον</i> (abrótonon) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of southernwood</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀβρότονον</i> (abrótonon, “southernwood”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tree wormwood (<i>Artemisia arborescens</i>)</li><li>southernwood (<i>Artemisia abrotanum</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown, but probably a loanword. Connected with <i>ἁβρός</i> (habrós, “delicate”) by folk etymology. According to Bailey, from Akkadian <i>𒄑𒃾𒄈</i> (amurdinnu, “thorned flower”). Perhaps transmitted by a substrate language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sour sauce of leeks, cress and pomegranate seeds</li></ol><p>Probably from Old Persian, because Theopompus wrote that this sauce was made in Media.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be proud; to exult in; to rejoice</li></ol><p>Apparently a denominative verb from unattested <i>&#42;ᾰ̓γᾰλός</i> (&#42;ăgălós), of unknown further origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agalloch (<i>Aquilaria malaccensis</i>)</li></ol><p>A loanword from an eastern language, likely ultimately borrowed from Old Tamil <i>𑀅𑀓𑀺𑀮𑁰</i> (akil), from the same source as Arabic يَلَنْجُوج (yalanjūj), يَلَنْجَج (yalanjaj), يَلَنْجِيج (yalanjīj), أَلَنْجُوج (ʔalanjūj), أَلَنْجَج (ʔalanjaj), Biblical Hebrew <i>אֲהָלִים</i> (ʾăhālîm); compare Tamil <i>அகில்</i> (akil, “agarwood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: a holy wagon</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Ursa Major, a constellation</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>much snowed on, snow-capped</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγα-</i> (aga-) +&lrm; <i>νίφω</i> (níphō, “to snow”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love, affection, esteem</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) specifically, the love between God and humanity, good will, benevolence</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;in the plural) Christian love feasts</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>ἀγαπάω</i> (agapáō, “to love”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herd</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>any group, of people or animals</i>) company, shoal</li></ol><li>at Crete, bands or classes in which youth were trained from the age of seventeen until marriage</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”) + a suffix <i>&#42;-l-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ἀγείρω</i> (ageírō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;agéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ger-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-minded, lordly</li><li>haughty, arrogant, insolent</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manly, bold, heroic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγα-</i> (aga-, “great”) or <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “lead”) +&lrm; <i>-ήνωρ</i> (-ḗnōr, “man”). See <i>ἄγαν</i> (ágan) and <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀγήνωρ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Agenor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγήνωρ</i> (agḗnōr, “manly, heroic”), <i>ἀγα-</i> (aga-, “great”) and <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>very; exceedingly</li><li>way too; excessively</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;əga-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;m̥ǵh₂-</i>, zero-grade prefix from <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“big, great”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ball of thread, clew</li><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>σησᾰμῐ́ς</i> (sēsămĭ́s, “mixture of sesame seeds, roasted and pounded with honey”).</li></ol><p>No etymology. Frisk connects Sanskrit <i>गध्य</i> (gadhya, “to be seized or gained as booty”).</p><p>Belardi compared <i>γάθια</i> (gáthia, “sausages”); if this is correct, then the word would be Pre-Greek, because of the prothetic vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to succeed; to do what is good</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work; deed”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verb-forming suffix). Literally, “to do a good deed”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act or state of doing good</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to do”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to do good to; to be helpful to; to benefit</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to act morally or dutifully</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to do”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>doing good; beneficent</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) giving a good sign</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to do”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beneficent, doing good</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγαθός</i> (agathós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodness; kindness</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good; brave; noble; moral; gentle</li><li>fortunate; lucky</li><li>useful</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;əgatʰós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;m̥ǵh₂dʰh₁ós</i> (“made great; whose deeds are great”), from <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“great”) + <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“do”) + <i>&#42;-ós</i>. Compare Latin <i>magnificus</i> from same roots.</p><p>Beekes notes that if the glossing synonyms <i>ᾰ̓κᾰθός</i> (ăkăthós) and <i>χᾱ́σῐος</i> (khā́sĭos) are related to <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós), one would be forced to assume Pre-Greek borrowing instead.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) very glorious; famous</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰ-</i> (ăgă-) +&lrm; <i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓γᾰκλειτός</i> (ăgăkleitós, “very glorious; famous”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰ-</i> (ăgă-) +&lrm; <i>κλῠτός</i> (klŭtós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>great joy, exultation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγαλλιάω</i> (agalliáō), form of <i>ἀγάλλω</i> (agállō, “to rejoice”) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greek iris (<i>Iris attica</i>)</li></ol><p>No etymology according to Beekes. The Diccionario Griego-Español proposes a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“yellow”), through Thracian, and subsequently influenced by the unrelated verb <i>ἀγάλλομαι</i> (agállomai, “to be proud, exult in”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be overjoyed, to rejoice</li></ol><p>Late form of <i>ἀγάλλομαι</i> (agállomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) niche or recess for a statue</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγαλμα</i> (ágalma, “statue, bust”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physical pain, irritation</li><li>vexation, annoyance, displeasure</li></ol><p><i>ἀγανακτέω</i> (aganaktéō, “to feel irritated, grieved”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to feel a violent irritation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of wine,&#32;intransitive) to ferment</li></ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be grieved, displeased, vexed, annoyed, angry, or discontented, to show outward signs of grief [(<i>sometimes</i>) <i>with</i> <b>ὅτι</b> (hóti) <small>‘that...’</small> <i>or</i> <b>εἰ</b> (ei) <small>‘if...’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to be vexed [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> (<i>rarely</i>) accusative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘at something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to be vexed at or with, to be angry at [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>κατά</b> (katá) <i>or</i> <b>πρός</b> (prós) <small>‘at someone’</small>]</li></ol></ol><p>From a root <i>&#42;ἀγαν-</i> (&#42;agan-) of uncertain origin plus a suffix <i>-ακτέω</i> (-aktéō, expressive formation suffix), as seen also in <i>ῠ̔λακτέω</i> (hŭlaktéō, “to bark”). Pinault derives the root from an unattested <i>&#42;ἀγα-νακτος</i> (&#42;aga-naktos, “pressed too much”), from <i>ἀγα-</i> (aga-, “way too”) +&lrm; <i>νακτός</i> (naktós, “close-pressed”); see <i>νᾰ́σσω</i> (nắssō, “to press”) for more on the second element. Has also been compared to <i>ἀγάομαι</i> (agáomai, “to admire”), <i>ἄγαμαι</i> (ágamai, “to wonder, admire, envy”), and <i>ἄγαν</i> (ágan, “very much”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mild; gentle</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἀγαπάω</i> (agapáō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Tragic</i>) to show affection for the dead</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to treat with affection, be fond of, love</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be beloved</li><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to caress, pet</li><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to be pleased with, take pleasure in, like</li><li>(New Testament,&#32;transitive) to show brotherly love or agape to</li></ol><p>A Proto-Indo-European etymology as a compound <i>&#42;m̥ǵh₂-peh₂-</i> (from <i>&#42;méǵh₂-</i> (“great”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;peh₂-</i> (“to protect”), literally “[provide] great protection”) was proposed by Pinault in 1991; see <i>ἀγα-</i> (aga-, “way too”) for the first component.</p><p>Previous scholars have proposed non-Indo-European comparisons, including:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἀγαπάω</i> (agapáō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beloved, dear (sometimes with a sense of uniqueness: solely beloved)</li><li>desirable, delightful</li><li>worthy of love, loveable</li><li>That which causes contentment</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγαπάω</i> (agapáō, “to love”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>type of tree-fungus, <i>Phellinus pomaceus</i></li></ol><p>Said to be from <i>Ἁγαρία</i> (Hagaría), a place in Sarmatia where the mushroom grew. The placename is from Iranian and could possibly be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;aǵ-r̥-</i>, verbal noun from <i>&#42;h₂eǵ-</i> (“to drive”), meaning something like "driving or gathering land."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble; illustrious</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>ᾰ̓γᾰ́ομαι</i> (ăgắomai, “to admire”) (Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“great”)) or <i>ᾰ̓γᾰσθαί</i> (ăgăsthaí, “to wonder; to look at”), from <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) + <i>γηθέω</i> (gēthéō, “rejoice”) (compare also <i>γαῦρος</i> (gaûros, “conceited; arrogant”)). However, the rare -υο- element may suggest Pre-Greek.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀγαυή)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ᾰ̓γαύη</i> (Ăgaúē)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γαυή</i> (ăgauḗ), the Epic feminine of <i>ᾰ̓γαυός</i> (ăgauós, “illustrious; noble”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble; illustrious</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>ᾰ̓γᾰ́ομαι</i> (ăgắomai, “to admire”) (Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“great”)) or <i>ᾰ̓γᾰσθαί</i> (ăgăsthaí, “to wonder; to look at”), from <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) + <i>γηθέω</i> (gēthéō, “rejoice”) (compare also <i>γαῦρος</i> (gaûros, “conceited; arrogant”)). However, the rare -υο- element may suggest Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to carry, deliver, pass on, relay (a message, information, news, a command, instructions); to announce, report</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;only present) to announce oneself</li></ol><p><i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos, “messenger”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἀγγεῖον</i> (angeîon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>community service, impressment into public service</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγγαρος</i> (ángaros) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dispatch as a courier</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to consign one to some task, requisition, press into service</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγγαρος</i> (ángaros, “mounted courier”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, verb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>message, news, report</li><li>Also, the substance or means of such communication</li><li>instruction, command</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos, “messenger”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἀγγελίᾱ</i> (angelíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a messenger</li><li>conveying information</li><li>angelic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos, “messenger”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel, jar, vase, pail, bucket, box</li><li>receptacle, reservoir</li><li>coffin, sarcophagus</li><li>cavity (of a body)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>lung</li><li>capsule (of plants)</li><li>(<i>later Greek</i>) body</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἄγγος</i> (ángos, “vessel”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A receptacle for vessels</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγγος</i> (ángos, “vessel”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) diminutive of <i>ᾰ̓́γγουρον</i> (ắngouron, “cucumber”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́γγουρον</i> (ắngouron, “cucumber”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to collect, gather</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;agéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ger-</i> (“to assemble, gather together”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>गण</i> (gaṇá, “flock, troop, group”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gregarious</li><li>common, ordinary</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγέλη</i> (agélē, “herd”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Greek mythology) epithet of Athena</li></ol><p>Traditionally said to be from <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to drive”) +&lrm; <i>λείᾱ</i> (leíā, “plunder”), thus Literally, “driver of plunder”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unborn, uncreated</li><li>of no family, ignoble, mean, cowardly, vile</li><li>with no family, childless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>γένος</i> (génos, “birth, offspring, stock”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix). Compare <i>εὐγενής</i> (eugenḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of unrecorded descent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>γενεαλογέω</i> (genealogéō, “to trace a pedigree”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncreatedness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγένητος</i> (agénētos, “uncreated”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, abstract noun suffix). The <i>τ</i> (t) changed to <i>σ</i> (s) by palatalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncreatedness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγέννητος</i> (agénnētos, “low-born”, “sordid”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix). The <i>τ</i> (t) changed to <i>σ</i> (s) by palatalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>σταφυλή</i> (staphulḗ, “bunch of grapes”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is Pre-Greek, like so many words concerning wine. Moreover, the element <i>-αβ-</i> and the presence of variations are typical of a substrate language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ignorant of geometry.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the bent arm</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) anything closely enfolding</li></ol><p>From a suffixed derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enk-</i> (“to curve, bend”), whence also <i>ἄγκος</i> (ánkos, “bend”), <i>ἀγκύλος</i> (ankúlos, “curved, bent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in(to) the arms</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγκών</i> (ankṓn, “elbow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hook, barb</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγκῠ́λος</i> (ankŭ́los, “crooked, bent”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, feminine suffix), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enkulos</i> (“joint?”), from <i>&#42;h₂énkos</i> (“curve”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ulos</i> (<i>diminutive suffix</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) having crooked counsel (<i>epithet of Cronus and occasionally of Prometheus</i>)</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>ἀγκύλος</i> (ankúlos, “crooked”) +&lrm; <i>μῆτις</i> (mêtis, “counsel”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend, make crooked</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγκύλος</i> (ankúlos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loop, noose</li><li>thong of a javelin</li><li>javelin</li><li>bowstring</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, curved, rounded</li><li>intricate</li><li>wily, crafty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enk-</i> (“to bend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, curved, rounded</li><li>intricate</li><li>wily, crafty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enk-</i> (“to bend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ankyloglossia, tongue-tie</li><li>ankylosis, stiffening of the joints</li><li>ankyloblepharon, adhesion of the eyelids</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγκυλόω</i> (ankulóō, “to bend, make crooked”) +&lrm; <i>-ωσις</i> (-ōsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) bend of the arm; hence elbow</li><li>nook; corner; angle of the wall</li><li>bend or meander of a river</li><li>headlands which form a bay</li><li>ribs which support the horns of the cithara</li><li>kind of vase</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enk-</i> (“corner, hirn”), the same source as <i>ᾰ̓́γκος</i> (ắnkos, “glen; dell”), <i>ᾰ̓́γκῡρᾰ</i> (ắnkūră, “anchor”) and <i>ᾰ̓γκῠ́λος</i> (ănkŭ́los, “curved; bent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common peony (<i>Paeonia officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγλαός</i> (aglaós, “splendid, shining”) +&lrm; <i>φῶς</i> (phôs, “light”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>splendid; shining</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from earlier <i>&#42;ᾱ̓γλᾰϝός</i> (&#42;āglăwós); further relations may include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fracture</li><li>cliff, crag</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;uh₂gmós</i>, from the root <i>&#42;weh₂g-</i> (“to break”). By surface analysis, <i>ἄγνυμι</i> (ágnumi, “to break, shatter”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō)</li></ol><p>An affixed form of Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ágō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵeti</i> (“to be driving”), from the root <i>&#42;h₂eǵ-</i> (“to drive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to not perceive or recognize, to not know, to fail to understand, to be ignorant, to forget</li><li>(ambitransitive,&#32;litotes,&#32;with negative) to not be ignorant, to know well</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sin or act unethically out of ignorance</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”), the root of <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “know”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verb-forming suffix). Compare <i>ἀγνώς</i> (agnṓs, “unknown”), <i>ἄγνοια</i> (ágnoia, “ignorance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἀγνοέω</i> (agnoéō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>γινώσκω</i> (ginṓskō) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ignorance</li><li>obscurity</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγνώς</i> (agnṓs, “unknown, ignorant”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fault of ignorance, oversight</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγνοέω</i> (agnoéō, “to not know”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) loom weight</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, suffixes like <i>-ῦθ-</i> are typically Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in passive constructions&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;of persons</i>) unknown</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) obscure, unintelligible</li><li>obscure, ignoble</li></ol><li>(<i>in active constructions</i>) ignorant</li><li>(<i>active voice&#32;or&#32;passive voice</i>) construed with the genitive</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>γνωτ-</i> (gnōt-, root of <i>γῐγνώσκω</i> (gĭgnṓskō, “to know”)). Compare <i>ᾰ̓́γνωτος</i> (ắgnōtos) and Latin <i>īgnōtus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly, especially an assembly of the people (as opposed to a council, <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ))</li><li>the place of assembly</li><li>speech</li><li>market, marketplace</li><li>things sold at market, provisions, supplies</li><li>sale</li><li>the time of market: midday</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;agorā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂goréh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂ger-</i> (“to gather”); equivalent to <i>ᾰ̓γείρω</i> (ăgeírō, “to gather”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā, abstract noun suffix).</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀒𐀨</i> (a-ko-ra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be in the market, to frequent it</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to buy in the market, buy, purchase</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to haunt the market, lounge there, as a mark of idle fellows</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́, “market”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit in assembly</li><li>to hold a debate</li><li>to discuss in assembly, to debate</li></ol><p>Denominative verb from <i>ἀγορά</i> (agorá) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Epic form of <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic) to the agora</li></ol><p><i>ἀγορήν</i> (agorḗn) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) to be an agoranomos (market clerk, public notary)</li><li>(<i>Thessalian</i>) to preside over the assembly</li><li>(<i>Ancient Rome</i>) to be an aedile</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγορᾱνόμος</i> (agorānómos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) office of agoranomos (market-clerk, public notary)</li><li>(<i>Ancient Rome</i>) aedileship</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγορᾱνόμος</i> (agorānómos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) agoranomos</li><li>(<i>Ancient Rome</i>) aedile</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγορᾱ́</i> (agorā́) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos), equivalent to <i>ἀγορά</i> (agorá) +&lrm; <i>νέμω</i> (némō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in, of, or belonging to the market</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>)</li></ol><li>frequenting the market</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) low, mean, common, vulgar</li></ol><li>proper to the market, skilled in, suited to public speaking</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγορά</i> (agorá, “market”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak in the assembly</li><li>to say, speak</li><li>to proclaim</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγορᾱ́</i> (agorā́, “assembly”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, “denominative verb-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speaker, orator</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγοράομαι</i> (agoráomai) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) flat of the hand</li><li>arm</li><li>dirt, filth</li></ol><p>Maybe from an original "ἀγορ-στος" (collector), in which the first element derives from <i>ἀγείρω</i> (ageírō, “to collect”) and the second one from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰós-tos</i> (“hand”), from <i>&#42;ǵʰes-</i> (“hand”) (cognate with Latin <i>hostus</i> (“yield of an olive tree”) and Sanskrit <i>हस्त</i> (hásta, “hand”)). Beekes finds this proposal unconvincing.</p><p>According to Vasmer, ἀγορστος is cognate with Russian <i>горсть</i> (gorstʹ) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;gъrstь</i> (“cupped hand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without learning, unlettered, illiterate</li><li>synonym of <i>ἄγραπτος</i> (ágraptos)</li><li>(<i>of animals</i>) unable to utter articulate sounds</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of sounds</i>) inarticulate</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>γράμμα</i> (grámma, “letter”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to capture, seize</li><li>(Epic,&#32;imperative) come on!, let's go! etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́γρᾱ</i> (ắgrā) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō). Doublet of <i>ᾰ̓γρεύω</i> (ăgreúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;stative) to live outside</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́γραυλος</i> (ắgraulos, “living outside”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hunter</li><li>of an arrow</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγρα</i> (ágra, chase, prey) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take by hunting or fishing, catch, take</li><li>(transitive,&#32;figuratively) to hunt after, thirst for</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γρεύς</i> (ăgreús, “hunter”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, verb-forming suffix), from <i>ᾰ̓́γρᾱ</i> (ắgrā, “hunt”). Doublet of <i>ᾰ̓γρέω</i> (ăgréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harrow</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the prothetic vowel and the suffix "-να" lead to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net-like woollen robe worn by Bacchanals and soothsayers</li></ol><p>According to Strömberg, related to <i>γρήνη</i> (grḗnē, “mixed flowers”) with a prothetic vowel. A derivation from <i>ἄγρα</i> (ágra, “catch, prey”) is quite uncertain. Its origin is ultimately unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a wild olive</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) wild olive</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλαία</i> (elaía, “olive”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kermes oak (<i>Quercus coccifera</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>βάλανος</i> (bálanos, “acorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier thistle (<i>Picnomon acarna</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>κινάρα</i> (kinára, “artichoke”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tassel hyacinth (<i>Muscari comosum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>κρόμμυον</i> (krómmuon, “onion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Nile tamarisk (<i>Tamarix nilotica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>μυρίκη</i> (muríkē, “tamarisk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of tamarisk</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγρῐομῠρῑ́κη</i> (agrĭomŭrī́kē, “Nile tamarisk”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harmal, Syrian rue (<i>Peganum harmala</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>πήγανον</i> (pḗganon, “rue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wild of temper</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild, savage”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “temper, disposition”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gum arabic tree (<i>Vachellia nilotica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>κάρδος</i> (kárdos, “cardoon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild thyme (<i>Thymus serpyllum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>πράσον</i> (práson, “leek”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>valerian spikenard (<i>Valeriana celtica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sulphurwort (<i>Peucedanum officinale</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγριος</i> (ágrios, “wild”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́γρῐος</i> (ắgrĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́γρῐος</i> (ắgrĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἀγρότης</i> (agrótēs, “country dweller, rustic”)</li><li>(<i>used adjectivally</i>) rustic, of the countryside</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγρός</i> (agrós) + <i>-ιος</i> (-ios) + <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pasturing or dwelling in the country</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγρός</i> (agrós, “field”) +&lrm; <i>νέμω</i> (némō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of the fields</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γρός</i> (ăgrós, “countryside”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lie awake, be wakeful, to suffer from sleeplessness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(figuratively,&#32;intransitive) to be watchful</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἄγρυπνος</i> (ágrupnos, “sleepless, wakeful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleeplessness, watchfulness</li><li>a time of watching</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγρυπνος</i> (ágrupnos, “sleepless, wakeful”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>field, land, countryside</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;agrós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵros</i>. Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀒𐀫</i> (a-ko-ro), Latin <i>ager</i>, Sanskrit <i>अज्र</i> (ájra) and Old English <i>æcer</i> (English <i>acre</i>). Compare <i>νομός</i> (nomós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>country dweller, rustic</li><li>synonym of <i>ἀγρευτής</i> (agreutḗs, “hunter”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγρός</i> (agrós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to catch, take</li></ol><p>Epic form of ἀγρεύω (agreúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓́γυιᾰ</i> (ắguiă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a swelling which obstructs the lachrymal duct</li></ol><p>Has been proposed to derive from <i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi, “near”) or <i>ἄγχω</i> (ánkhō, “I squeeze, strangle”) + <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”). Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin; compare <i>αἰγίλωψ</i> (aigílōps) in the sense of “lachrymal fistula”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>near, close by, closely following</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coming near</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi, “near”) plus the root of <i>βλώσκω</i> (blṓskō, “to come, go”) (future <i>μολοῦμαι</i> (moloûmai), aorist <i>ἔμολον</i> (émolon)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coming near</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi, “near”) plus the root of <i>βλώσκω</i> (blṓskō, “to come, go”) (future <i>μολοῦμαι</i> (moloûmai), aorist <i>ἔμολον</i> (émolon)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ready of wit, shrewd</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi, “near”) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) fighting hand-to-hand</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi) +&lrm; <i>μαχητής</i> (makhētḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>close kinship</li><li>right of inheritance</li><li>exclusion by descent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγχιστος</i> (ánkhistos, “nearest”) +&lrm; <i>-εία</i> (-eía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>close together, crowded, in heaps</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγχιστος</i> (ánkhistos) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strangle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγχόνη</i> (ankhónē, “a hanging rope”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, verb-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>near, nearby</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>near to</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enǵʰ-</i> (“to tighten”) (whence <i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi) and <i>ἄγχω</i> (ánkhō)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Poetic</i>) near</li></ol><p><i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi, “near”) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi, “at”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanging rope, noose</li><li>strangling, hanging</li><li>torment, suffering</li><li>mandrake (<i>Mandragora officinarum</i>, syn. <i>Mandragora autumnalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγχω</i> (ánkhō, “to strangle, throttle”) +&lrm; <i>-νη</i> (-nē, “a suffix added to roots or words to form adjectives or nouns”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nearly equal</li><li>(<i>of battle</i>) doubtful</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi) +&lrm; <i>ὁμαλός</i> (homalós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>transportation</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) tendency</li><li>the act of taking away</li><li>burden, load</li><li>guidance, lead</li><li>moral conduct</li><li>a cure (for an illness)</li></ol><p>A reduplicative nominal formation, likely Attic, of the root of <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escort (group of people or vehicles, generally armed, who go with a person or people of importance to safeguard them on a journey or mission)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attraction; attractiveness</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leading; guiding</li><li>drawing; attracting</li><li>eliciting; evoking</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guide</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) escort (group of people or vehicles, generally armed, who go with a person or people of importance to safeguard them on a journey or mission)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́γω</i> (ắgō, “to lead”) with Attic reduplication.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contest, struggle for victory</li><li>gymnastic exercise</li><li>(<i>of the mind</i>) agony, anguish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγών</i> (agṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>deponent</i>) to contend, compete, fight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>deponent</i>) to struggle, exert oneself</li><li>(deponent,&#32;law) to contend in court, dispute a charge or accusation</li></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;typically in perfect) to be won through contest</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(passive voice,&#32;typically in perfect) to be brought to issue</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἀγών</i> (agṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ίζομαι</i> (-ízomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>combatant, rival, contestant</li><li>pleader, advocate</li><li>actor</li><li>master</li><li>struggler, champion</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγωνίζομαι</i> (agōnízomai, “I contend for a prize”) (from <i>ἀγών</i> (agṓn, “contest”)) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for contest</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of physical strength as in games</i>)</li><li>(<i>especially in speaking, of debating style</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) masterly, impressive</li></ol><p>Stem ἀγωνισ- from verb <i>ἀγωνίζομαι</i> (agōnízomai) + <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós), from <i>ᾰ̓γών</i> (ăgṓn, “competition”). Morphologically, <i>ἀγωνιστ(ής)</i> (agōnist(ḗs), “combatant, competitor”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἀγόρευε</i> (agóreue)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Lyric) leader</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂(e)ǵ-ó-s</i> (literally “driver”); compare Sanskrit <i>अज</i> (ajá, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gathering, an assembly</li><li>a contest, competition</li><li>a struggle</li></ol><p>Corresponding noun of Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓γείρω</i> (ăgeírō, “to gather”), with later senses from <i>ᾰ̓́γω</i> (ắgō, “to lead”) (though Beekes considers all senses to derive only from <i>ᾰ̓́γω</i> (ắgō)). For the former, compare Sanskrit <i>गण</i> (gaṇa, “troop, gang, flock, tribe, assembly, company”); Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́, “assembly”), Sanskrit <i>ग्राम</i> (grāma, “multitude, troop, assembly, collective”); Sanskrit <i>आजि</i> (ājí, “race; competition; battle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contest</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) brave deeds done in battle</li><li>feat</li><li>an object to strive for</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγωνίζομαι</i> (agōnízomai) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adamant, the hardest metal (probably steel)</li><li>a hard metal resembling gold</li><li>diamond</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fixed, unalterable</li><li>not to be broken, inflexible</li></ol><p>Often derived from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) + <i>δαμνάω</i> (damnáō, “conquer”), as “indomitable”, but Beekes remarks that semantically this is strange and the word is rather a Semitic borrowing that was adapted by folk etymology; compare Akkadian <i>𒀀𒁕𒈬</i> (adamu) or <i>𒋤𒉘</i> (elmēšu [SUD.ÁG], “a valuable stone, perhaps amber”). Middle Persian <i>ʾlmʾs</i> (almās) is probably from the same source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gratuitous, free of charge</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>δαπάνη</i> (dapánē, “expense”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt efflorescence on the herbage of marshes</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it is a loanword from Gaulish: compare Old Irish <i>adarc</i> (“horn”), which is probably from the same source as Basque <i>adar</i> (“horn”) with a Celtic suffix <i>-k</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left-handed</li><li>awkward, maladroit, gauche</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “a-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>δεξιός</i> (dexiós, “right; dexterous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without master or owner</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of property</li><li>of freedmen</li><li>of the gods</li></ol><li>(<i>of rumours or writings</i>) anonymous</li><li>ungovernable</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”, alpha privative) +&lrm; <i>δεσπότης</i> (despótēs, “master”, “lord”, “owner”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gland</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁engʷḗn</i>, because <i>&#42;h₁n̥gʷ-ḗn</i> would produce <i>&#42;endḗn</i> by Rix's Law (PIE <i>*HR̥C</i> &gt; Proto-Hellenic <i>*e/a/oRC</i>), and rejects the connection with Latin <i>inguen</i> (“groin”) and Old Norse <i>økkvenn</i> (“thick, clodded”).</p><p>De Vaan prefers to derive it from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥gʷḗn</i> (“the naked one”), from <i>&#42;negʷ-</i> (“naked”), preserving the connection with Latin <i>inguen</i> but excluding the Germanic forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>maidenhair (<i>Adiantum capillus-veneris</i>)</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>ἀδίαντος</i> (adíantos, “not wet, dry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nettle (<i>Urtica dioica</i>)</li></ol><p>The suffix "-ίκη" is found also in <i>ἑλίκη</i> (helíkē) and <i>μυρίκη</i> (muríkē, “tamarisk”). A connection with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;natilā</i> (“nettle”) has been proposed. Alternatively it might be a substrate word, maybe from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>misdeed, offence, wrongdoing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀδικέω</i> (adikéō, “to behave unjustly, to commit a crime”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>adamantine, of steel</li><li>hard as adamant</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀδάμᾱς, ἀ˘δᾰμᾰντ-</i> (adámās, ădămănt-, “diamond; steel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos, suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἀδεής</i> (adeḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearless, not experiencing fear</li><li>not causing fear</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>δείδω</i> (deídō, “to fear”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sister</li><li>some other female relation, such as wife or cousin</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>ᾰ̓δελφός</i> (ădelphós, “brother”). Displaced the reflex of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swésōr</i> (“sister”), still seen in <i>ἔορ</i> (éor, “daughter of a cousin”), a word found in Hesychius' lexicon.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ᾰ̓δελφός</i> (ădelphós, “brother”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ᾰ̓δελφοκτόνος</i> (ădelphoktónos)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓δελφεός</i> (ădelpheós) +&lrm; <i>-κτόνος</i> (-któnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ᾰ̓δελφός</i> (ădelphós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to commit fratricide, sororicide; to kill a brother or a sister</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓δελφεοκτόνος</i> (ădelpheoktónos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to the murdering of a brother or sister; fratricidal; sororicidal</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓δελφός</i> (ădelphós) +&lrm; <i>-κτόνος</i> (-któnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brotherly or sisterly</li><li>double</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother; male sibling</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;əgʷelpʰós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm̥-gʷelbʰ-ós</i> (“one/same womb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓δελφός</i> (ădelphós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓δελφός</i> (ădelphós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brotherhood, fraternity</li><li>band of brothers, a brotherhood</li><li>alliance</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓δελφός</i> (ădelphós, “brother”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) glandular; adenoid</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓δήν</i> (ădḗn, “gland”) +&lrm; <i>-ο-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-oid, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(meaning uncertain&#59; perhaps "not sweet&#59; bitter&#59; cruel")</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) + <i>δεῦκος</i> (deûkos, “sweetness”), thus "not sweet, bitter, cruel." Or possibly from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) + <i>δεύκει</i> (deúkei, “he considers, ponders”), thus "unexpected" or "careless."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without fear, fearlessly, confidently</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓δεής</i> (ădeḗs, “fearless”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncertainty</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́δηλος</i> (ắdēlos, “not manifest”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to render, make invisible</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be obliterated</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄδηλος</i> (ádēlos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be troubled, pained, anguished</li><li>to be confused, puzzled, perplexed</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos). Etymologically, the term should mean something akin to "not at home." This meaning may have shifted to "uncomfortable, in an uncomfortable location." The form &#42;ἀδημων (&#42;adēmōn) can be reconstructed, which may have been suffixed with -έω (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trouble, distress, predicament</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀδημονέω</i> (adēmonéō) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gluttonous, greedy</li><li>costly, expensive</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄδην</i> (ádēn, “satiation, enough”) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not to be passed, impassable</li><li>not striding, closed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>διαβατός</i> (diabatós, “passable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undissolved, indissoluble</li><li>irreconcilable</li><li>unrelaxed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un”) +&lrm; <i>διάλυτος</i> (diálutos, “that may be dissolved, loosened”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not different, indistinguishable</li><li>indifferent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>διάφορος</i> (diáphoros)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unrelentingly, fiercely, implacably</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un”) +&lrm; <i>διαλύτως</i> (dialútōs, “relentingly”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be in the wrong, to have sinned, to have broken the law</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to do one wrong, to wrong one, to injure [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>], to break the law</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄδικος</i> (ádikos, “wrong, unjust”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, stative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ἀδικέω</i> (adikéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injustice, iniquity</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́δῐκος</i> (ắdĭkos, “unjust, wrong”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀδικία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Adikia</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀδικίᾱ</i> (adikíā, “injustice, wrongdoing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἀδικέω</i> (adikéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́δῐκος</i> (ắdĭkos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́δῐκος</i> (ắdĭkos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἀπορία</i> (aporía, “difficulty”), <i>ὀλιγωρία</i> (oligōría, “contempt or negligence”), <i>ἄγνοια</i> (ágnoia, “ignorance”), <i>ἡσυχία</i> (hēsukhía, “rest, quiet”).</li></ol><p>Unknown. Frisk derived it from <i>ἄδ-</i> (in <i>ἄδην</i> (ádēn, “to one's fill”)) with "suffixal" <i>-μωλ-</i>, which is highly improbable. According to Furnée, the word can hardly be inherited.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Cretan Doric form of <i>ἁγνός</i> (hagnós) holy, pure</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ill-repute, disgrace</li><li>obscurity</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa, “glory, reputation”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) unable</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>absolute</i>) without strength, powerless, weak</li></ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) that cannot be done, impossible</li><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) without power or skill, feebly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>δυνατός</i> (dunatós, “powerful, capable, possible”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>always; ever (<i>at all times</i>)</li><li>forever; everlastingly; infinitely (<i>for all time</i>)</li><li>eternally (<i>for (an) eternity</i>)</li><li>abidingly; at length; indefinitely; in perpetuity; lengthily; perpetually (<i>for an indefinitely long period of time, without specification regarding past present or future</i>)</li><li>for the time being (<i>for an indefinitely long period of time including the present time</i>)</li><li>at that time; at the time; back then (<i>for an indefinitely long period of time including a specific point in time (generally in the past) which is not the present time</i>)</li><li>from the get-go; from the start; heretofore; since the beginning; to date; up to now; until this day; theretofore (<i>at all times from a distant past through the present or some other time</i>)</li><li>endlessly; evermore; forevermore (<i>at all times from the present or some other time through a distant future</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aiweí</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eyu-</i> (“lifetime; long time”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ey-</i> (“vital energy; life”). Equivalent to the locative case of <i>αἰών</i> (aiṓn), with or without <i>-ν</i> (-n).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to sing, chant, praise</li><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to make various noises: hoot, howl, croak</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to celebrate, praise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awéidō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>houseleek, <i>Sempervivum</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ever-living, always living; continually alive</li><li>immortal (of gods or divine beings, emphasizing continual life rather than timeless existence)</li><li>(poetic,&#32;figurative) enduring, ever-present (of emotions or states)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) evergreen</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) houseleek, <i>Sempervivum</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ἀεί</i> (aeí, “always”) +&lrm; <i>ζωός</i> (zōós, “living”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>everliving, everlasting, immortal</li></ol><p>Contracted form of <i>ἀείζωος</i> (aeízōos) (“ever-living”), from <i>ἀεί</i> (aeí) (“always”) + <i>ζῶος</i> (zôos) (“living”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ever to be remembered</li></ol><p><i>ἀεί</i> (aeí) +&lrm; <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift up, raise, support</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of armies, ships,&#32;transitive) to get the fleet under sail</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bear, sustain</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise up, exalt</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise by words, praise, extol</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift and take away, remove</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(transitive,&#32;with genitive) to take away from</li><li>(Koine,&#32;transitive) to take off, kill</li></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;transitive) to take up for oneself, to win, gain</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take upon oneself, undergo</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to undertake, begin</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take away</li></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;intransitive) to hang</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wér-ye-ti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wer-</i> (“to raise?; to bind, enclose?”); the relation between the "raise" and "enclose" meanings, if any, is unclear, though Chantraine (DELG) appears to consider the latter a semantic specialization of the former. Potential cognates include Old Armenian <i>գեր</i> (ger, “above, hyper-”), Latin <i>aperiō</i> (“to open”), Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wérˀtei</i> (“to thrust, prod”), Sanskrit <i>वृणोति</i> (vṛṇoti, “to cover, ward off”), Albanian <i>vjerr</i> (“to hang, suspend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameful; unseemly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“to become equal”). Cognate with <i>ἐπιεικής</i> (epieikḗs), <i>ἔοικα</i> (éoika), <i>εἰκών</i> (eikṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to maltreat, mistreat, treat poorly, injure</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a corpse</i>) to defile, mutilate</li></ol><li>to dishonor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀεικής</i> (aeikḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disfigurement; marring</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) improper or unseemly conduct</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of acts or their consequences</i>) against the will, constrained</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(often in&#32;Attic,&#32;of offences) involuntary</li></ol><li>(of persons,&#32;only in the adverb <i>ἀκουσίως</i>) involuntarily</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>ἑκούσῐος</i> (hekoúsĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storm</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ᾰ̓́ελλᾰ</i> (ắellă). See also <i>Αἴολος</i> (Aíolos, “Aeolus”, god of the winds).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Very quick, lightning fast.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄελλᾰ</i> (áellă, “stormwind, whirlwind”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot, leg”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ᾱ̓ργός</i> (ārgós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>traversing the air</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓ήρ</i> (āḗr, “air”) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “journey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wandering in air</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓ήρ</i> (āḗr, “air”) +&lrm; <i>πλανάω</i> (planáō, “to wander”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) witless, silly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀάω</i> (aáō) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the same year</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same year</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔-</i> (hă-, copulative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ἔτος</i> (étos, “year”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eagle</li><li>(<i>if involving an auspice</i>): omen</li><li>gable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aywetós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ewyetós</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂éwis</i> (“bird”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>वि</i> (ví), Latin <i>avis</i>, Old Armenian <i>հաւ</i> (haw).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Phrygian</i>) beard</li></ol><p>From Phrygian, itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵénus</i> (“chin, jaw”), the same root of <i>γένυς</i> (génus, “jaw, mouth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(pass.) dry, dried up, parched, withered</li><li>(act.) parching, scorching, burning</li><li>(metaph./fig.) harsh, cruel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eHs-</i> via <i>ἄζω</i> (ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: chariot pole</li></ol><p>Maybe from Pre-Greek, in view of the variation "λ/ρ".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>οἱ ἐγγύτατοι τοῦ βασιλέως</i> (hoi engútatoi toû basiléōs, literally “the nearest to the king”).</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;āzātah</i> (“free”), from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;āzāta</i>. Compare Persian آزاد (“free, independent”). The <i>-ῆ-</i> is probably Ionic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓ηδής</i> (ăēdḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓ηδής</i> (ăēdḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpleasant</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>distasteful, disgusting</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) disagreeable</li></ol><li>(<i>as adverb</i>) unwillingly</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ἡδύς</i> (hēdús, “sweet, pleasant”) + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nauseousness</li><li>unpleasantness, odiousness</li><li>disgust, dislike</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ἡδύς</i> (hēdús, “sweet, pleasant”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightingale (<i>Luscinia megarhynchos</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) poet; songster</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) poet's songs</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) cicada</li><li>(<i>music</i>) mouthpiece of a flute</li></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;ᾰ̓ϝηδών</i> (&#42;ăwēdṓn), and traditionally considered related to <i>ᾰ̓είδω</i> (ăeídō, “to sing; to chant; to praise”) and <i>αὐδή</i> (audḗ, “voice; sound”), though Beekes considers the lengthened-grade formation required to be unlikely and tentatively prefers a derivation from Pre-Greek. The suffix is also found in <i>χελῑδών</i> (khelīdṓn, “swallow (bird)”), <i>τενθρηδών</i> (tenthrēdṓn, “earth wasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undying, immortal</li><li>everlasting, perpetual</li><li>maintained at a constant figure</li><li>A member of an elite regiment of the Persian army.</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the rose campion (<i>Silene coronaria</i>, syn. <i>Lychnis coronaria</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>θᾰ́νᾰτος</i> (thắnătos, “dying”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gruel, porridge</li></ol><p>Unknown. An Egyptian word according to Pliny. Has been connected to <i>ἀθήρ</i> (athḗr, “awn”) and with Latin <i>ador</i> (“wheat”), but these connections seem unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) awn</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) chaff</li><li>barb of a weapon</li><li>spine or prickle of a fish</li></ol><p>Not related to Latin <i>ador</i> (“hulled wheat”) nor to <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”). According to Beekes, the nasalized form <i>ἀνθέριξ</i> (anthérix) points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tumour</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀθάρη</i> (athárē, “groats, porridge”) +&lrm; <i>-ωμᾰ</i> (-ōmă, “disease, tumor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one who does not want to be approached</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>θιγγάνω</i> (thingánō, “to touch”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immortality</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓θᾰ́νᾰτος</i> (āthắnătos, “undying”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix). The <i>τ</i> (t) changed to <i>σ</i> (s) by palatalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make immortal</li><li>to deem immortal</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓θᾰ́νᾰτος</i> (āthắnătos, “immortal”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disbelief in the gods, incredulity with regard to the divine</li><li>atheism</li><li>varia lectio of <i>ἀθεσία</i> (athesía, “faithlessness, fickleness”)</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́θεος</i> (ắtheos, “godless, impious; slighting or denying the gods”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, suffix forming feminine abstract nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand smelt, any in the genus <i>Atherina</i> of small fishes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀθήρ</i> (athḗr, “awn; barb; spine or prickle of a fish”) +&lrm; <i>-ίνη</i> (-ínē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set aside</li><li>to deny, refuse (<i>someone’s</i>) request</li><li>to reject, despise</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄθετος</i> (áthetos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>godlessness</li></ol><p><i>ἄθεος</i> (átheos, “godless”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to contend for a prize, combat, wrestle [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with or against someone’</small>]</li><li>to be an athlete, contend for a prize in games</li></ol><p>From <i>ἆθλος</i> (âthlos, “contest, struggle”) or <i>ἆθλον</i> (âthlon, “prize, contest”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “verbal suffix”). In the Attic form <i>ᾱ̓θλῶ</i> (āthlô), <i>ᾰε</i> (ăe) is contracted to <i>ᾱ</i> (ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a combatant, champion, especially a prizefighter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>as an adjective</i>) pertaining to competition</li></ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) practiced in, master of</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓θλέω</i> (āthléō, “to contend”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for an athlete, athletic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀθλητής</i> (athlētḗs, “athlete”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́θλῐος</i> (ắthlĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́θλῐος</i> (ắthlĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>traveller's joy, smokewood (<i>Clematis vitalba</i>)</li></ol><p>Frisk compares <i>ἄθρας</i> (áthras, “chariot”), but it is semantically untenable. Furnée compares <i>ἀνδράχνη</i> (andrákhnē, “purslane”) and concludes to a Pre-Greek origin, which is sustained also by Beekes due to the presence of prenasalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gather, to collect, to muster</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀθρόος</i> (athróos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in groups</li><li>together, as a whole, all at once</li></ol><p>Possibly <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;θρόος</i> (&#42;thróos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to despair, be despondent, be disheartened</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄθῡμος</i> (áthūmos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of heart, faintheartedness, despondency</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “spirit”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>babbler; one who cannot keep his mouth shut</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without, un-”) +&lrm; <i>θύρᾱ</i> (thúrā, “door”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶττα</i> (glôtta, “tongue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to play, to take delight in</li><li>to perform in a play</li><li>to sing a jovial song</li><li>to play an instrument</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to sing</li><li>to mock</li></ol><p>Uncertain. According to Beekes, it may be compared to Lithuanian <i>padùrmai</i> and Russian <i>дурь</i> (durʹ), which may suggest a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰwer(H)-</i>. However, this explanation does not easily explain the origin of the initial <i>ἀ-</i> (a-). It could be explained from a root <i>&#42;h₂dʰwer(H)-</i>, although Beekes rejects this as an improbable root structure.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpunished, scot-free, unharmed by</li><li>not deserving punishment, guiltless, innocent</li><li>causing no harm, harmless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “a-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>θωή</i> (thōḗ, “penalty”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foul, unclean</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) impure</li><li>not fit for cleansing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>καθαίρω</i> (kathaírō, “to cleanse”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tireless; indefatigable</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>κᾰ́μᾰτος</i> (kắmătos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thorny, prickly, of thorns</li><li>of shittah wood</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκανθα</i> (ákantha, “any thorny shrub”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton thistle (<i>Onopordum acanthium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκανθα</i> (ákantha, “thorn”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light boat</li><li>small sail</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) small person</li></ol><p><i>ἄκατος</i> (ákatos) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>headless</li><li>without beginning</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft, silent</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>softly, silently</li></ol><p>Participle of a hypothetical verb <i>&#42;ἀκέω</i> (&#42;akéō). Beekes relates this form to <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “silence”), but not much is clear otherwise concerning its etymology. Not related to <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō, “to hear”), nor to <i>ἄκων</i> (ákōn, “javelin”). The alpha here is not the alpha privative <i>ἀ-</i> (a-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point</li><li>silence</li><li>healing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;akā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”).</p><p>Possibly connected with <i>ἦκα</i> (êka, “quietly”); compare <i>ἧσσα</i> (hêssa).</p><p>Probably <i>ᾰ̓κέομαι</i> (ăkéomai) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>silently</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of barely attested <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “silence”); possibly also related to <i>ἦκα</i> (êka, “softly, low”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point</li><li>silence</li><li>healing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;akā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”).</p><p>Possibly connected with <i>ἦκα</i> (êka, “quietly”); compare <i>ἧσσα</i> (hêssa).</p><p>Probably <i>ᾰ̓κέομαι</i> (ăkéomai) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unharmed</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>specifically</i>) not harmed by the Keres</li></ol><li>unharming; harmless</li><li>heartless; spiritless</li><li>lifeless</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>κήρ</i> (kḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>κῆρ</i> (kêr) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>harmless (free from danger)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>κίνδῡνος</i> (kíndūnos, “danger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmoved, motionless</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>idle, sluggish</li><li>unmoved, unaltered</li></ol><li>immovable, hard to move</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>not to be stirred, inviolate</li><li>not to be shaken, steadfast (<i>of persons</i>)</li><li>unalterable</li><li>inseparable from</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>κῑνητός</i> (kīnētós, “moving”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of wild basil</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκινος</i> (ákinos, “wild basil”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pointed object</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>barb of an arrow or hook</li><li>arrow, dart</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) stings (of desire)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) sharp, acute pains</li></ol></ol><li>surgical bandage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp, pointed”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relating to the Academy, Academic</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀκαδημία</i> (Akadēmía) or <i>Ἀκαδήμεια</i> (Akadḗmeia, “The Academy”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An acacia tree (tribe Acacieae).</li><li>innocence</li></ol><p>First found from the Egyptiots Aretaios and Dioskurides, a borrowing from Coptic <i>ⲭⲁⲕⲓ</i> (khaki), <i>ⲕⲁⲕⲉ</i> (kake), <i>ⲕⲉⲕⲉⲓ</i> (kekei, “darkness”) from Egyptian <i>kkw</i> (“darkness”), acknowledging its providing shade in the desert, with a preformative analogical to <i>ἄκανθα</i> (ákantha).</p><p>From <i>ἄκακος</i> (ákakos, “without malice”), from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>κακός</i> (kakós, “evil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stinging nettle (<i>Urtica dioica</i>)</li><li>sting</li><li>sea anemone</li></ol><p>Unknown. The variation "υ/η" could point to a Pre-Greek word, although there are no clear parallels to it.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiny dogfish (<i>Squalus acanthias</i>)</li><li>kind of grasshopper</li><li>asparagus (<i>Asparagus officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκᾰνθα</i> (ákăntha, “thorn”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groundsel (<i>Senecio vulgaris</i>)</li><li>cotton thistle (<i>Onopordum acanthium</i>)</li><li>kind of perching bird&#59; perhaps:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>goldfinch (<i>Carduelis carduelis</i>)</li><li>siskin (<i>Spinus spinus</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́κᾰνθᾰ</i> (ắkănthă, “thorn; thorny bush”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedgehog</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκανθα</i> (ákantha, “thorn”) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feeding on thistles</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́κᾰνθᾰ</i> (ắkănthă, “thorn; thistle”) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porcupine</li><li>hedgehog</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκανθος</i> (ákanthos, “acanthus, a prickly plant”) +&lrm; <i>χοῖρος</i> (khoîros, “pig”). Displaced <i>ὕστριξ</i> (hústrix, “porcupine, bristles, torture instrument”). Secondary sense of hedgehog displaced <i>ἐχῖνος</i> (ekhînos, “hedgehog”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of hair</i>) too short to be cut</li><li>(<i>in general, especially of time</i>) very short</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>κάρ-</i> (kár-), zero-grade of the root of <i>κείρω</i> (keírō, “to cut hair”), <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mite</li></ol><p>Uncertain; probably Pre-Greek. Compare <i>κάρνος</i> (kárnos, “louse”), <i>κόρις</i> (kóris, “bedbug”). Sometimes connected to <i>ἀκαρής</i> (akarḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not to be seized or conquered</li><li>unreachable</li><li>incomprehensible</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>κᾰτᾰληπτός</i> (kătălēptós, “seized”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncultivated, not worked up</li><li>(<i>of bread</i>) not thoroughly baked</li><li>undigested</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “a-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>κατεργάζομαι</i> (katergázomai, “to effect by labor, achieve”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncompleted, unfinished, unformed</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἀχεύω</i> (akheúō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) sharp-edged</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mender of torn clothes</li><li>healer</li></ol><p>From an unattested basis <i>&#42;ἄκος</i> (&#42;ákos) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix). The first element is related to Latin <i>acus</i> (“needle”).</p><p>From <i>ἀκέομαι</i> (akéomai, “to heal, cure”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to neglect, be neglectful; to not care for, not take heed of</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκηδής</i> (akēdḗs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive sense</i>) uncared for</li><li>unburied</li><li>harmless</li><li>(<i>active sense</i>) careless, heedless, negligent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>κῆδος</i> (kêdos, “care”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>indifference, torpor, apathy</li><li>weariness, exhaustion</li><li>neglect, disregard</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκηδής</i> (akēdḗs, “careless”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak, feeble</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκίς</i> (akís, “sharp pain”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barbed, pointed</li><li>provided with fishhooks</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nailwort (<i>Paronychia capitata</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκίς</i> (akís, “barb, point”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short straight sword, used by Persians and Scythians</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from Iranian, compare Sogdian <i>𐼸𐼷𐼻𐼰𐼸</i> (kynʾk /⁠kīnak⁠/, “sword”), Mazanderani کینه (kine), Persian سکنه (sikana&nbsp;/ sekane), اسکنه (iskina&nbsp;/ eskene, “chisel”), possibly from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;kanH-</i> (“to dig”), or alternatively borrowed from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiescence, rest</li></ol><p><i>ἀκῑ́νητος</i> (akī́nētos, “unmoved”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>λύχνος</i> (lúkhnos, “lamp”).</li></ol><p>Unknown, but it may well be of Pre-Greek origin in view of the variations.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak, feeble</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>βορρᾶς</i> (borrhâs, “north wind”).</li></ol><p>According to Frisk, probably related to <i>ἀκιδνός</i> (akidnós, “weak, small”).</p><p>Unknown. Not related to the adjective.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without fame; inglorious</li><li>ignominious; disgraceful</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, not; without) +&lrm; <i>κλέω</i> (kléō, “to make famous”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́κλῐτος</i> (ắklĭtos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́κλῐτος</i> (ắklĭtos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be at or to reach the highest point (<i>often&#32;figuratively</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be in one's prime, be ripe</li><li>to have plenty of [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκμή</i> (akmḗ, “high point”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, verb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point, edge</li><li>the highest or culminating point of something, bloom, flower, prime, zenith, especially of a person's age</li><li>time, the best or most fitting time</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;akmā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“point, sharp”). Cognates include <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “point”) and <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hearing, the sound heard</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the thing heard, news, tidings</li><li>the thing heard, report, saying, fame</li></ol><li>the sense of hearing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the act of hearing, hearing</li><li>the ear</li></ol><li>a hearing, a listening</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō, “to hear”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to follow, go after, go with [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> (<i>rarely</i>) accusative <small>‘someone’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> <b>σύν</b> (sún) <small>‘dat’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> <b>μετά</b> (metá) <small>‘gen’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> <b>ἐπί</b> (epí) <small>‘acc’</small>]</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to follow one in a thing, let oneself be led by [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> (<i>rarely</i>) accusative <small>‘someone or something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to follow the thread of a discourse</li><li>(of things,&#32;transitive) to follow, be consequent on</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) it follows</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀκόλουθος</i> (akólouthos, “following”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen; to whet</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to spur, goad on, provoke</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓κόνη</i> (ăkónē) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hurl a javelin</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́κων</i> (ắkōn, “javelin”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>javelin-thrower, javelineer</li></ol><p><i>ἀκοντίζω</i> (akontízō) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley</li></ol><p>Unknown, maybe substrate. Compare perhaps Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ęčьmy</i> (“barley”). Beekes rejects a connection to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp, pointed”) (cf. in particular Latin <i>acus</i> (“bran, awn”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌷𐌰𐌽𐌰</i> (ahana, “awn, chaff”), Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;agil</i> (“awn”)), suggesting instead a foreign origin.</p><p>See also <i>ἄχυρον</i> (ákhuron, “chaff, bran; debris, slag”), traditionally linked to the above but now understood to be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ἄκουσμα</i> (ákousma)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκουσμα</i> (ákousma, “thing heard”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for hearing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō, “to hear”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) contraction of <i>ᾰ̓εκούσῐος</i> (ăekoúsĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hear [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> genitive <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hear about, learn</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to listen, pay attention to, heed</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to understand</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to obey</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be called, be spoken of, be known as</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;akouhō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ḱh₂owsyéti</i>, and cognate with English <i>hear</i>, <i>hark</i> and <i>harken</i>. In this word, the diphthong <i>ου</i> (ou) is genuine (see spurious diphthong on Wikipedia for an explanation).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of power, debility</li><li>lack of self-control, incontinence</li><li>self-indulgence</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓κρᾰτής</i> (ăkrătḗs, “powerless”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă), from <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “strength, might”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accuracy, precision, exactness</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) lack, need</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκρῑβής</i> (akrībḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia). Synchronically analyzable as <i>ἀκρῑβής</i> (akrībḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grasshopper, locust, cricket</li><li>locust bean: the fruit of the carob tree</li></ol><p>Hardly related to <i>κρίζω</i> (krízō, “to creak”). Winter connects it with <i>κέρκα</i> (kérka, “grasshopper”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin, which is quite probable for a grasshopper.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of wind</i>) blowing strongly or favourably</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “extreme”) + <i>ἄημι</i> (áēmi, “I breathe, blow”)+&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmixed, pure, sheer</li><li>untouched, inviolate, intact</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>ἀκραπνής</i> (akrapnḗs). If this is reliable, it is a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bad mixture, ill temperature</li><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓κρᾰ́τειᾰ</i> (ăkrắteiă, “lack of power, debility”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́κρᾱτος</i> (ắkrātos, “unmixed”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerless, impotent</li><li>not having power or command [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘over’</small>]</li><li>intemperate in the use of [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> <b>περί</b> (perí)]</li><li>incontinent</li><li>immoderate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “power”) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀκρᾱτοφόρος</i> (akrātophóros)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκρᾱτος</i> (ákrātos, “unmixed wine”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel for pure wine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκρατος</i> (ákratos, “unmixed wine”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bough, branch</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “at the edge, extreme”) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἀκρῑβῶς</i> (akrībôs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓κρῑβής</i> (ăkrībḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>accurate, precise</li><li>scrupulous, methodical</li></ol><p>Disputed. Commonly held to be formed from <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “sharp”) and another element of uncertain provenance, e.g. <i>εἴβω</i> (eíbō, “to drop, let fall”) according to Schwyzer, though this is rejected by Beekes &amp; van Beek. Tichy takes the component parts to be <i>ἄκρις</i> (ákris, “mountain top”) in a dative-locative form in <i>-ī</i> + <i>βῆναι</i> (bênai, “go, walk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make or be exact, perfect, precise, accurate</li><li>to describe accurately</li><li>to investigate in a thorough or exact manner</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκρῑβής</i> (akrībḗs) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to a nicety, precisely</li><li>diligently</li><li>perfectly</li><li>circumspectly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκρῑβής</i> (akrībḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to babble</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκριτόμῡθος</i> (akritómūthos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>babbling</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκριτόμῡθος</i> (akritómūthos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>confused, indiscriminate, reckless speech</li><li>difficult to decipher, understand, interpret, comprehend</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκριτος</i> (ákritos) +&lrm; <i>μῦθος</i> (mûthos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>middle only</i>) to listen</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>ᾰ̓́κρος</i>&nbsp;/ <i>ᾰ̓κμή</i> (ắkros) +&lrm; <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a place of audience</li><li>an audience</li></ol><p><i>ἀκροάομαι</i> (akroáomai) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for hearing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκροᾱτής</i> (akroātḗs, “hearer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acrobat</li></ol><p><i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “top, peak”) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “walk, step”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foreskin</li><li>uncircumcision (the state of having the foreskin)</li><li>(<i>collectively</i>) the uncircumcised (those who are not circumcised)</li></ol><p>The first element is <i>ᾰ̓́κρος</i> (ắkros, “topmost; outermost”). The second element is uncertain; it was traditionally thought to be <i>βῠ́ω</i> (bŭ́ō, “to stuff; to plug”), but this is almost certainly incorrect. The word could be an alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓κροποσθῐ́ᾱ</i> (ăkroposthĭ́ā, “foreskin”), making the second element akin to <i>πόσθη</i> (pósthē, “penis; foreskin”). It could also be from a Semitic root, such as Hebrew <i>בֹּשֶׁת</i> (bōšeṯ, “shame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mostly in the plural,&#32;of animals) extremities of the body, especially snout, ears, trotters</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “topmost, outmost”) +&lrm; <i>κῶλον</i> (kôlon, “limb”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) akroposthion (the tip of the foreskin)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́κρος</i> (ắkros, “highest; topmost”) +&lrm; <i>ποσθῐ́ᾱ</i> (posthĭ́ā, “foreskin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetry</i>) acrostic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “at the beginning”) +&lrm; <i>στῐ́χος</i> (stĭ́khos, “row”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) acromion, point of the shoulder</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “outermost”) +&lrm; <i>ὦμος</i> (ômos, “shoulder”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cape, headland, promontory</li><li>extremity</li></ol><p>A derivative of <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something heard</li><li>(especially something heard with pleasure): feast for the ears, song, play</li><li>(in plural): lecturers, musicians, actors</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκροάομαι</i> (akroáomai, “listen”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearing, listening</li><li>obedience</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) session (of a council)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓κροᾰ́ομαι</i> (ăkroắomai) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a statue</i>) with extremities consisting of marble, while the rest of the structure consists of wood</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “at the edge”) +&lrm; <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>acropolis; high part of a city</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́κρος</i> (ắkros) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elder (<i>Sambucus nigra</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. The suffix <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā) is frequent in tree names, like <i>ἰτέᾱ</i> (itéā, “willow”) and <i>πτελέᾱ</i> (pteléā, “elm”). Witczak connects the word with Old Armenian <i>հացի</i> (hacʻi, “ash tree”), which he disassociates from <i>ὀξύα</i> (oxúa, “beech”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>headland, cape, promontory</li><li>any raised place</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) corn (grains of cereal crops)</li></ol><p>Unclear. Derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”) is possible according to Frisk, but if Furnée is right in comparing this word with <i>ὄχθη</i> (ókhthē, “riverbank”), it could be a Pre-Greek word.</p><p>Unclear. Perhaps the same as etymology 1.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without property, poor, possessionless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ</i> (a) +&lrm; <i>κτᾶσθαι</i> (ktâsthai, “to acquire”) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ray; beam</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) brightness; splendor</li><li>spoke (of a wheel)</li></ol><p>From an uncertain source apparently shared with Sanskrit <i>अक्तु</i> (aktú, “beam, ray”), perhaps:</p><p>The suffix is unexplained in any case.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>staff, stick</li><li>bar of wood supporting a chariot pole</li></ol><p>Unknown. The explanation as a univerbation of <i>ἀκταίνω</i> (aktaínō, “to keep erect”) and <i>ἐρείδω</i> (ereídō, “to prop, support”) seems most improbable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) eagle</li></ol><p>Likely borrowed from Latin <i>aquila</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point, tip (<i>of a weapon</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>following, attending</li></ol><p>A double-ablauted formation from <i>κέλευθος</i> (kéleuthos, “path”) + copulative <i>ἀ-</i> (a-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whetstone, hone</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) part of the tragus</li></ol><p>A nominal formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”), with suffix <i>-όνη</i> (-ónē) similar to that in <i>βελόνη</i> (belónē, “needle”) and <i>περόνη</i> (perónē, “pin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leopard's bane, <i>Aconitum anthora</i></li><li>wolf's bane, <i>Aconitum napellus</i></li></ol><p>If the explanation of the ancients as from <i>ᾰ̓κόνῑτος</i> (ăkónītos, “without struggle ~ without force ~ without fight”, literally “without dust”) is not wholly fanciful – which modern sources however are more inclined to espouse –, it can be interpreted as resultative with privative <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) from <i>κονίω</i> (koníō, “to roll into the dust, to sprinkle or cover with dust; to prepare for combat”) and thereby basic <i>κόνις</i> (kónis, “dust”), although it would be incorrect to interpret it as “invincible” because of its deadly effect, and the realistic explanation goes rather in the direction that the herb made one deprived one of one’s forces or abilities to fight (back), and one bites the dust at the very place where one takes on it. This provides some semantics of that adjective only attested in this phytonym, paralleled by <i>φθορά</i> (phthorá, “destruction, death, passing out of existence; loss, damage; seduction ~ rape; miscarriage; abortion; etc.”) meaning in Latin <i>phthora</i>, <i>thora</i> the exact same species, modern Spanish <i>tuera</i>, and other poisonous ranunculaceous ones, as well as with an obviously privative formation again <i>antiphthora</i>, <i>antithora</i>, <i>anthora</i>. Of like derivation is, with this association in mind, <i>κώνειον</i> (kṓneion, “hemlock”), <i>κονή</i> (konḗ, “hemlock”), both being stereotypic man-killing plants confused often anyway when far from places where either grow.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>javelin</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) javelin exercise</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκων</i> (ákōn, “javelin”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>globular vase without handles for holding perfumes</li></ol><p>Sethe explained the form as Egyptian <i>ꜥj</i>-<i>r</i>-<i>bꜣstjt</i> (“vessel of the Egyptian goddess Bast”), but this is doubtful. Compare Arabic عُلْبَة (ʕulba, “box, can”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀλάβαστος</i> (alábastos, “globular vase without handles”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀλάβαστος</i> (alábastos, “globular vase without handles”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀλάβαστος</i> (alábastos, “globular vase without handles”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>λιγνύς</i> (lignús, “murky fire”), <i>σποδός</i> (spodós, “embers”), <i>καρκίνος</i> (karkínos, “crab; cancer”), <i>μαρίλη</i> (marílē, “coal-dust”).</li></ol><p>Because of its structure, it is probably a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of fish that lives in the Nile (possibly <i>Labeo niloticus</i>)</li></ol><p>Unclear. Perhaps from Coptic <i>ⲗⲉⲓϥⲓ</i> (leifi, “fish of the Nile”). However, Furnée connects this word with similar names for fish, like <i>ἔλλοψ</i> (éllops) and <i>ἀλλοπίᾱς</i> (allopíās) and concludes that it is a Pre-Greek word.</p><p>Demotic Egyptian <i>lbs</i> (“fish of the Nile”), and its successor Coptic <i>ⲗⲁⲃⲏⲥ</i> (labēs), is certainly a reborrowing from Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gulping; choking</li></ol><p>One suggests contamination form of <i>λῠ́γξ</i> (lŭ́nx, “hiccup”) and another word, like <i>ᾰ̓λῠ́ω</i> (ălŭ́ō, “to be deeply stirred, excited”) or <i>ᾰ̓λᾰ́ομαι</i> (ălắomai, “to wander, roam”); this is not very convincing. According to Beekes, it is rather a primary onomatopoeic formation, with the Pre-Greek suffix <i>-υγγ-</i> (-ung-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wander, rove, roam</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ἄλη</i> (álē) +&lrm; <i>-άομαι</i> (-áomai). Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élh₂-</i>, a root present that may also be the source of Latin <i>ambulō</i>. Regardless, the term ultimately derives from the root <i>&#42;h₂elh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slap, blow, smack</li></ol><p>See Latin <i>alapa</i> (“slap, smack”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>avenging spirit or deity</li><li>he who does deeds which merit vengeance, wretch</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄλαστος</i> (álastos, “insufferable”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warmth; heat</li><li>bodily heat</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fomentations</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;usually&#32;negated) to care</li><li>[<i>with</i> genitive] to care for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;alégō</i> (“to care”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂leg-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cock, rooster</li><li>husband, consort</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) unwedded</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λέξω</i> (ăléxō, “I ward off”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “-er”).</p><p>From <i>ᾰ̓</i> (ă, “together”) +&lrm; <i>λέχομαι</i> (lékhomai, “I lie”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “-er”).</p><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>λέχομαι</i> (lékhomai, “I lay”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep off; to turn aside</li><li>to guard; to protect; to defend; to help</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to recompense; to requite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aléksō</i> (“to protect”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂lek-</i> (“to defend, to protect”), same source as Sanskrit <i>रक्षति</i> (rákṣati), Old Armenian loanword <i>երաշխիք</i> (erašxikʻ), and Old English <i>ealgian</i> (“to protect, defend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shun; to avoid</li><li>to flee from</li></ol><p>From the same root as the synonymous <i>ᾰ̓λεείνω</i> (ăleeínō) and as <i>ᾰ̓λέᾱ</i> (ăléā, “avoiding, escape”). Given the alternate forms with <i>υ</i> (u), these must have had the earlier form <i>ἀλεϝ-</i> (alew-), to which Beekes relates <i>ᾰ̓λῠ́σκω</i> (ălŭ́skō) as a zero-grade form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to grind, bruise, pound, mill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂elh₁-</i> (“to grind”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>աղամ</i> (ałam, “grind, pound”),</p><p>Hindi <i>आटा</i> (āṭā, “flour”), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬴𐬀</i> (aṣ̌a, “ground”), Persian آرد (ârd, “flour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>not a lie</li><li>truth</li><li>reality</li><li>(Septuagint,&#32;plural form?) the Thummim</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀληθής</i> (alēthḗs, “true”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă, abstract noun suffix). Synchronically analyzable as <i>ἀληθής</i> (alēthḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wanderer, stroller, rover, vagabond, vagrant</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλάομαι</i> (aláomai, “to wander, rove, roam”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unshrinkingly, unabatingly</li><li>undauntedly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unshrinking, unabating</li><li>undaunted</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>λῐᾰ́ζομαι</i> (lĭắzomai) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corpse, dead body</li><li>(<i>of the Styx</i>) dead river</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dead wine, vinegar</li></ol><p>The ancient explanation as "sapless", with <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, without) +&lrm; <i>λιβάς</i> (libás, “spring, fount, source”) is based on popular etymology. Kretschmer connected it with Etruscan <i>𐌋𐌖𐌐𐌖</i> (lupu, “(he) died”) and Latin <i>Libitina</i>, which is possible but uncertain. The deviant shape of the word, as well as forms like <i>ὀκρίβας</i> (okríbas), <i>κιλλίβας</i> (killíbas), <i>λυκάβας</i> (lukábas) and <i>Κορύβαντες</i> (Korúbantes), clearly point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) like, resembling (<i>with dative</i>)</li></ol><p>Unexplained per Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fabulous plant mentioned by Pseudo-Plutarch</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pound, crush, destroy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;alínō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂linéHti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of alabaster</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλάβαστρος</i> (alábastros, “alabaster”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A chest for vessels containing unguents</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλάβαστρος</i> (alábastros, “alabaster”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boastful; pretentious</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wanderer around the country, vagrant</li><li>(<i>particularly of Sophists</i>) charlatan, quack</li><li>braggart, boaster</li></ol><p>It matches Thracian <i>Ἀλαζών</i> (Alazṓn) exactly, perhaps an appellative, or it may be a cultural loanword from Akkadian 𒀀𒇻𒍣𒉡 <i>aluzinnu,</i> 'boastful, clownish fraud, most times pretending or claiming to possess medical skills or knowledge.' From Sumerian 𒀩𒍪 <i>alanzu,</i> 'clown.'</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A loud cry, especially a war cry</li><li>The battle itself</li></ol><p>Of onomatopoeic origin. According to one legend, the Athenian use of "Ἀλαλή" was based on the calls of the owl, a sacred bird to their patron goddess Athena.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shouting</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) useless or careless watch</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλαός</i> (alaós, “blind, unseeing”) +&lrm; <i>σκοπιᾱ́</i> (skopiā́, “lookout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easily exhausted, i.e. powerless, feeble</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;alp-</i> (“weak, small”); cognate with Hittite (alpant-, “ill, weak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blind</li><li>invisible, imperceptible</li></ol><p>Presumably from <i>λάω</i> (láō, “to see”), although a privative formation should have barytone accentuation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feel bodily pain, to suffer, to be ill</li><li>to suffer hardship</li><li>to feel pain of mind, to grieve</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́λγος</i> (ắlgos, “pain of the body or mind”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painful, grievous</li><li>feeling pain, suffering</li></ol><p>For *ἀλγεσνός, i.e. <i>ἄλγος</i> (álgos, “pain”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain, (bodily) suffering</li><li>pain, (mental) suffering, grief</li><li>cause of pain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλγέω</i> (algéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wandering about</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄλη</i> (álē, “wandering, roaming”) +&lrm; <i>-είᾱ</i> (-eíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinner</li><li>offender, criminal</li></ol><p>Possibly ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂leyt-</i> (“offense”), which would make it cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;laiþaz</i> (whence English <i>loath</i> and German <i>Leid</i>) and possibly with Latin <i>laedo</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(active voice,&#32;transitive) to anoint someone else (smear the skin with oil after bathing)</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;intransitive,&#32;reflexive) to anoint oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂leybʰ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>lībō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble, concern oneself (with a thing)</li><li>to care for, heed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλέγω</i> (alégō) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἀλγεινός</i> (algeinós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to shun; to avoid; to flee (from)</li></ol><p>From the same root as the synonymous <i>ᾰ̓λέομαι</i> (ăléomai) and as <i>ᾰ̓λέᾱ</i> (ăléā, “avoiding; escape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) a male chicken: cock, rooster</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) a female chicken: hen</li><li>guinea fowl</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λέκτωρ</i> (ăléktōr, “cock, rooster”) +&lrm; <i>-υών</i> (-uṓn, an obscure suffix also appearing in <i>ἀλκυών</i> (alkuṓn, “kingfisher”)). The first element is the agentive noun of <i>ᾰ̓λέξω</i> (ăléxō, “ward off”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂lek-s-</i>, an <i>*s</i>-derivative of <i>&#42;h₂lek-</i> (“protect”). Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀨𐀓𐀶𐀬𐀺</i> (a-ra-ku-tu-ru-wo /⁠Alektruōn⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) antidote</li><li>charm, spell</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) remedy</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἀλεξῐφᾰ́ρμᾰκος</i> (alexĭphắrmăkos, “acting as antidote”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλετρεύω</i> (aletreúō, “to mill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aleph, the name for the first letter א of the Hebrew script.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אָלֶף</i> (ʔɔlɛp̄).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of wheat flour</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́λευρον</i> (ắleuron, “wheat flour”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἀληθής</i> (alēthḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἀληθής</i> (alēthḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) true, real, genuine</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) truthful, honest</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;λῆθος</i> (&#42;lêthos, “*concealment”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs). <i>&#42;λῆθος</i> (&#42;lêthos) is the neuter σ-stem abstract noun to the root of <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō, “I am hidden”), the existence of which is warranted by its Doric variant <i>λᾶθος</i> (lâthos). While <i>λᾶθος</i> (lâthos) is only attested as a synonym of <i>λήθη</i> (lḗthē, “oblivion”), it is probable that for the formation of <i>ἀληθής</i> (alēthḗs) the original meaning "concealment" was used, continuing the meaning of the root; thus the original (but unattested) meaning of the adjective was "uncovered", "unconcealed".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἀλήθεια</i> (alḗtheia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) truthful, trustworthy</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) real and true, genuine</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ληθής</i> (ălēthḗs, “true”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνός</i> (-ĭnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in answer to a question</i>) that's right</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) true, real, genuine</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) truthful, honest</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;λῆθος</i> (&#42;lêthos, “*concealment”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs). <i>&#42;λῆθος</i> (&#42;lêthos) is the neuter σ-stem abstract noun to the root of <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō, “I am hidden”), the existence of which is warranted by its Doric variant <i>λᾶθος</i> (lâthos). While <i>λᾶθος</i> (lâthos) is only attested as a synonym of <i>λήθη</i> (lḗthē, “oblivion”), it is probable that for the formation of <i>ἀληθής</i> (alēthḗs) the original meaning "concealment" was used, continuing the meaning of the root; thus the original (but unattested) meaning of the adjective was "uncovered", "unconcealed".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́ληπτος</i> (ắlēptos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to heal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;h₂el-dʰ-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow; to nourish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to healing, remedying</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλθέω</i> (althéō) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἀλθαίνω</i> (althaínō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄλθος</i> (álthos) +&lrm; <i>-σκω</i> (-skō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἀλθαίνω</i> (althaínō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄλθος</i> (álthos) +&lrm; <i>-σκω</i> (-skō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshmallow (<i>Althaea officinalis</i>)</li><li>tree mallow (<i>Malva arborea</i>)</li><li>catchfly (<i>Silene gallica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλθαίνω</i> (althaínō, “to cure”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀλθαία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Althaea</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλθαίνω</i> (althaínō, “marsh mallow”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to heal</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be healed; become well, whole</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;h₂el-dʰ-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow; to nourish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) remedies; medicines</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́λθος</i> (ắlthos) +&lrm; <i>-τήρῐον</i> (-tḗrĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) healer; physician</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λθέω</i> (ălthéō) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) remedy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλθησ-</i> (althēs-) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of wind</i>) blowing on or towards the sea</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “sea”) + <i>ἄημι</i> (áēmi, “I breathe, blow”)+&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>place for horses to roll in</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλινδέω</i> (alindéō, “to roll”) +&lrm; <i>-ήθρα</i> (-ḗthra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sinful, wicked</li></ol><p>Adjectival formation from <i>ἀλείτης</i> (aleítēs, “offender”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀλκαίᾱ</i> (alkaíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prowess; force</li><li>defense; guard</li><li>battle; fight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂lek-</i> (“to protect”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tail, usually of a lion</li><li>a vervain mallow</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-αίᾱ</i> (-aíā).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, mighty</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, mighty</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀλκαῖος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Alcaeus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́λκῐμος</i> (ắlkĭmos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́λκῐμος</i> (ắlkĭmos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>protector</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λέξω</i> (ăléxō) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of days</i>) halcyon (pertaining to days during the winter when storms do not occur)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingfisher; halcyon</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) halcyon days</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λκῠών</i> (ălkŭṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kingfisher; halcyon</li><li>(<i>Laconian</i>) a divinity</li></ol><p>Unknown, apparently from Pre-Greek. The variant <i>ᾰ̔λκῠών</i> (hălkŭṓn) arose by folk etymology as <i>ᾰ̔́λς</i> (hắls, “salt”) + <i>κῠέω</i> (kŭéō, “to conceive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἀλλά</i> (allá)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>but</li></ol><p>Originally the neuter accusative plural of <i>ἄλλος</i> (állos, “another, different”) with shifted accent in an adverbial sense, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élyos</i> (“other”). Cognates include Latin <i>alius</i>, Old Irish <i>aile</i>, Old Armenian <i>այլ</i> (ayl), and Old English <i>elles</i> (English <i>else</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to change, alter, make other than it is</li><li>to give in exchange, barter one thing for another</li><li>to take one thing in exchange for another</li><li>to take a new position</li><li>to have dealings, as a buyer or seller</li><li>to alternate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλλαγή</i> (allagḗ, “change”) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō), though Beekes considers <i>ᾰ̓λλᾰ́σσω</i> (ăllắssō) to be original and <i>ἀλλαγή</i> (allagḗ) a derivative. Either way, ultimately from <i>ἄλλος</i> (állos, “other”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ᾰ̓λλᾰ́σσω</i> (ăllắssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>referring back to a plural subject, and expressing an action done in two directions: of one another, to one another, one another, each other, mutually, reciprocally</li></ol><p>From phrases such as <i>ᾰ̓́λλος ᾰ̓́λλον</i> (ắllos ắllon), <i>ᾰ̓́λλοι ᾰ̓́λλους</i> (ắlloi ắllous, “one another”), with loss of λ either by dissimilation or by the lengthening of α, as well as restoration of the second initial vowel <i>ή</i> (ḗ) (&lt; <i>&#42;h₂élyos</i> (“other”)). For similar formations, compare Latin <i>alius alium</i>, Sanskrit <i>अन्योन्य</i> (anyonya, “one another, mutual”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in general</i>) change</li><li>exchange, barter, buying and selling</li><li>(<i>economics</i>) agio, whether premium or discount</li><li>change of posthorses, stage</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>ἄλλος</i> (állos, “other”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elsewhere</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλλαχῇ</i> (allakhēî, “elsewhere”) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi, “at”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) veiled language, allegory</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ἄλλος</i> (állos, “other”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγορεύω</i> (agoreúō, “I speak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) hallelujah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>הַלְּלוּיָהּ</i> (halləlūyɔh, “praise the Lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>otherwise; differently</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λλοῖος</i> (ălloîos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;poetic</i>) foreign</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄλλος</i> (állos) +&lrm; <i>-δαπός</i> (-dapós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for changing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλλοιόω</i> (alloióō, “to change”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make different, change, alter</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to become different, be changed, undergo an alteration</li><li>to be estranged</li><li>to be changed for the worse</li><li>to be deranged in mind</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀλλοῖος</i> (alloîos, “different”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, factitive verb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A species of tuna</li></ol><p>Perhaps from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>ἀλάβης</i> (alábēs) and <i>ἔλλοψ</i> (éllops). Compare also <i>αὐλωπίας</i> (aulōpías).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>estrangement</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλλοτριόω</i> (allotrióō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to estrange (cause to feel less close or friendly), to alienate [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from’</small>]</li><li>to make hostile [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ἀλλότριος</i> (allótrios) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓λλότρῐος</i> (ăllótrĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓λλότρῐος</i> (ăllótrĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a different nature</li><li>of a different form or shape</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>changed in shape</li><li>of strange, abnormal shape</li></ol></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́λλος</i> (ắllos) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of another sort or kind; different</li><li>of another sort than [<i>with</i> genitive of comparison]</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́λλος</i> (ắllos, “other, different”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to another</li><li>foreign; strange; alien</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of persons</i>)</li><li>(<i>of things</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>foreign to the subject; not to the purpose</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>strangely; marvelously</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́λλος</i> (ắllos, “other”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to a different tribe; foreign</li></ol><p><i>ἄλλος</i> (állos) +&lrm; <i>φῦλον</i> (phûlon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλῡ́ω</i> (ănălū́ō, “unloose”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sausage</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) someone clumsy or thick-headed</li></ol><p>Unknown; perhaps Pre-Greek to the suffix <i>-ᾶς</i> (-âs), from the original stem <i>&#42;αλλᾱϝεντ-</i> (&#42;allāwent-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(late&#32;Koine,&#32;Byzantine) astronomical indexes, almanacs</li></ol><p>Possibly of Coptic origin, as the word first appears in an Egyptian context.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unreasoningly, inconsiderately, thoughtlessly, heedlessly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλόγιστος</i> (alógistos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grease</li><li>unguent; ointment; salve</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λείφω</i> (ăleíphō, “anoint”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitterwort (<i>Gentiana lutea</i>)</li><li>mandrake (<i>Mandragora officinarum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λοίτης</i> (ăloítēs, “avenger”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτῐς</i> (-îtĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lichwort (<i>Parietaria lusitanica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́λσος</i> (ắlsos, “grove”), with a suffix found in other plant names like <i>βᾰλσᾰμῐ́νη</i> (bălsămĭ́nē) and <i>μῠρσῐ́νη</i> (mŭrsĭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphabet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλφάβητος</i> (alphábētos) from <i>ἄλφα</i> (álpha, “alpha”) +&lrm; <i>βῆτᾰ</i> (bêtă, “beta”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphabet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄλφα</i> (álpha, “alpha”) +&lrm; <i>βῆτᾰ</i> (bêtă, “beta”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphabet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄλφα</i> (álpha, “alpha”) +&lrm; <i>βῆτᾰ</i> (bêtă, “beta”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carpenter's square</li></ol><p><i>ἄλφα</i> (álpha, “square”) +&lrm; <i>-δῐον</i> (-dĭon, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flour eater, bread eater.</li><li>that lives by toil.</li></ol><p>Two possible etymologies have been given. Traditionally it was interpreted as a derivative of <i>ἀλφάνω</i> (alphánō, “to produce, to earn”), therefore meaning “industrious”. After the 19th century another interpretation has been given, translating it as “bread eater”, and deriving it from <i>ἄλφι</i> (álphi, “flour”), with the addition of a suffix that means “to eat”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alphos, a type of non-contagious leprosy typified by dull white lesions.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;albʰós</i> (“white”). Cognates include Latin <i>albus</i> (“white”), Hittite <i>𒀠𒉺𒀸</i> (alpas, “cloud”), Umbrian <i>𐌀𐌋𐌚𐌖</i> (alfu, “white”) and Middle Welsh <i>elbid</i> (“world”). Doublet of <i>ἄλβος</i> (álbos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) threshing floor</li></ol><p>Compare <i>ἅλως</i> (hálōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beardgrass (<i>Polypogon monspeliensis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλώπηξ</i> (alṓpēx, “fox”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρά</i> (ourá, “tail”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>veterinary medicine</i>) mange in foxes</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) bald patches on the head</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) hair loss</li><li>fox's den</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλώπηξ</i> (alṓpēx, “fox”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unreasoning, inconsiderate, thoughtless, heedless</li><li>not to be reckoned, not to be taken into account, vile</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>λογίζομαι</i> (logízomai) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aloe (<i>Aloe vera</i>)</li></ol><p>As with <i>ἀγᾰ́λοχον</i> (agắlokhon, “agalloch”), a loanword from an Eastern language. First attested in the 1st century CE, apparently acquired via Aramaic, and attested with the same meaning as Classical Syriac <i>ܥܠܘܝ</i> (ʕalway, ʕelway), Christian Palestinian Aramaic <i>ܥܠܘܐ</i>, <i>ܥܠܘܝܬܐ</i>, Classical Mandaic <i>ࡏࡋࡅࡀࡉࡀ</i> (ʕluaia), <i>ࡀࡋࡅࡀࡉ</i> (ʔluai), from Old Tamil <i>𑀅𑀓𑀺𑀮𑁰</i> (akil); compare அகில் (akil, “agarwood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂elut-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to avoid; to evade; to shun</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be stirred or excited</li><li>to be distraught</li><li>to be perplexed</li><li>to be weary; to be bored</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to wander</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄλυς</i> (álus, “agitation”). May be related to Latin <i>alucinor</i> (“to wander in mind, talk idly, prate, dream”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fox (small canine of the genus <i>Vulpes</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) cunning or sly person</li></ol><p>Presumably from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;alṓpēks</i>. Some linguists have attempted derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂(w)lṓp-eh₂s</i> (stem <i>*h₂(w)lop-</i>); compare Old Armenian <i>աղուէս</i> (ałuēs), Lithuanian <i>lãpė</i>, Latvian <i>lapsa</i> and Sanskrit <i>लोपाश</i> (lopāśá). The many irregular variations on the word may suggest a Mediterranean wanderword instead; compare Luwian (ulipna-)/<small>[script needed]</small> (walipna-, “wolf”) and Latin <i>vulpēs</i> (“fox”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vine trained on two poles</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>ἀμαξίς</i> (amaxís, “grape-vine”), which must be correct. It is a typical Pre-Greek word, showing reduplication. This word has also been compared to Hittite (mahla, “grapevine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unseen; obscure</li></ol><p>According to Herodian, it is a byform of unattested <i>&#42;ᾰ̓μᾰ́λδᾰνος</i> (&#42;ămắldănos), itself from <i>ᾰ̓μᾰλδῠ́νω</i> (ămăldŭ́nō, “to soften; to mitigate”). However, this is rather uncertain. Lewy proposes a derivation from <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́νδᾰλος</i> (mắndălos, “bolt”), suggesting a meaning that shifted from "locked", "bolted" to "invisible", "unseen".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trench, conduit, channel for watering meadows</li><li>hollow of the ear</li></ol><p>Formerly connected with <i>ἄμη</i> (ámē, “shovel”) in the meaning of "digging out", which is improbable. Others considered a relationship with Hittite (amii̯ar-, “canal”), but note that the words only have initial <i>am-</i> in common. The word could belong to the Greek-Anatolian substrate. A third option is connection with Albanian <i>amë</i> (“riverbed, source”) and river names like <i>Amantia</i>, <i>Amana</i>, <i>Amara</i>. According to Beekes, this comparison is formally better than that with Hittite.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marjoram (<i>Origanum majorana</i>)</li></ol><p>The word has been connected with Ancient Macedonian <i>ἀβαρύ</i> (abarú, “oregano, marjoram”). If this is accepted, the variation μ/β points to a Pre-Greek origin, which is probable anyhow. According to Beekes, the connection with Sanskrit <i>मरुवक</i> (maruvaka, “marjoram”) must be wrong.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>everlasting (<i>Helichrysum orientale</i>)</li><li>centaury (<i>Centaurium erythraea</i>)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfading, undecaying</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>μαραίνω</i> (maraínō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfading, undecaying</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>μαραίνω</i> (maraínō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sparkle, twinkle, glance</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek source of <i>ἀμαρύσσω</i> (amarússō, “to sparkle, twinkle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἀμήχανος</i> (amḗkhanos)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>μᾱχανᾱ́</i> (mākhanā́) +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>): to reap, cut</li><li>(<i>metaphorically</i>): to receive as a consequence, to obtain as a reward</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂meh₁-</i> (“to reap, mow”). Cognate with English <i>mow</i> and Latin <i>metō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive sense</i>) not drunken, without drunkenness, sober</li><li>(<i>active sense</i>) not intoxicating</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>substantive</i>) remedy for drunkenness</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) some sort of herb</li><li>(<i>geology</i>) amethyst</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>μέθῠστος</i> (méthŭstos, “drunken”), from <i>μεθῠ́ω</i> (methŭ́ō, “to be drunk on wine”), from <i>μέθῠ</i> (méthŭ, “wine”). Compare to <i>εὐμέθῠστος</i> (euméthŭstos, “easily made drunk”).</p><p>The exact semantic development of the "stone" sense is uncertain. Beekes believes that the stone was named after the light color of diluted wine (that is, "non-drunk wine"), while others have proposed that the Greeks believed that the amethyst prevented intoxication.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to milk, to extract milk from cows</li><li>to press out, squeeze out, suck</li><li>to drink, sip, imbibe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂mélǵti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂melǵ-</i> (“to milk”). Cognates include Latin <i>mulgeō</i>, Old English <i>meolc</i> (English <i>milk</i>), Gothic <i>𐌼𐌹𐌻𐌿𐌺𐍃</i> (miluks), and Albanian <i>mjel</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) synonym of <i>τήκω</i> (tḗkō)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps connected with <i>μέλδομαι</i> (méldomai), although <i>ἀμέλδειν</i> (améldein) presupposes a root <i>&#42;h₂meld-</i> whereas <i>μέλδομαι</i> (méldomai) more likely derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)meld-</i>. Beekes proposes that both forms could possibly be unified under a singular root <i>&#42;sh₂meld-</i>. The term has also been connected with <i>ἀμαλδύνω</i> (amaldúnō), itself of uncertain etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) negligence, indifference</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) an instance of negligence</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μελής</i> (ămelḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pluck or pull</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to expel, to throw out</li></ol><p>Latin <i>mergae</i> (“pitchfork”) could be related. It seems that the Greek root is related to Sanskrit <i>मार्जयति</i> (mārjayati, “to wipe, purify, cleanse”) and <i>ὀμόργνῡμι</i> (omórgnūmi, “to wipe”), but it is unclear how the initial vocalism could be explained. Although a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂merǵ-</i> is perfectly possible, the word may also be a technical term borrowed from the substrate language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deprive, bereave one of something</li></ol><p>If the form without the initial vowel is secondary, the root may have been Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂merd-</i>, which is cognate with Sanskrit <i>मृद्नाति</i> (mṛdnāti, “to pound, triturate”), Latin <i>mordeō</i> (“to bite”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;smertaną</i> (“to hurt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undivided, indivisible</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) not imparting</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>μερῐστός</i> (merĭstós, “divided”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immediately, at once</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμεσος</i> (ámesos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>truly, certainly</li><li>so be it</li><li>(Judaism,&#32;Christianity) amen</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אָמֵן</i> (āmḗn, “so be it”, “surely”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impracticable, impossible, challenging, difficult, unmanageable, uncontrollable</li><li>incapable, without means, resourceless, helpless, awkward</li><li>extraordinary, inconceivable, enormous, infinite</li><li>(<i>particularly of dreams</i>) inexplicable, mysterious, unexplainable</li><li>difficult to deal with, against whom nothing can be done</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of situations</i>) helpless, unfixable, unreconcilable, irremediable</li><li>irresistible</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>μηχανή</i> (mēkhanḗ) +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bonito (<i>Sarda sarda</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Thompson suggests a derivation from Egyptian <i>mehi</i> (“kind of fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undefiled, pure</li><li>not to be defiled</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a greenish stone like asbestos</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>μιαίνω</i> (miaínō, “stain, defile”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>οἶνον</i> (oînon, “wine”).</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chamber pot</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́μη</i> (ắmē, “water bucket”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἀμαθής</i> (amathḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ignorant [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘about something’</small>]; stupid</li><li>(<i>morally</i>) unfeeling, inhuman</li><li>(<i>of animals</i>) unmanageable</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) uncivilized, unrestricted, barbarous</li><li>not heard of, unknown</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; the root of <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō, “to learn”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unlearnedness, uneducatedness, willing ignorance, ignorance disguised as wisdom</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild leek (<i>Allium ampeloprasum</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek and perhaps related to <i>μόκρων</i> (mókrōn, “sharp, keen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crush, destroy, weaken</li><li>to soften, mitigate, alleviate</li><li>to neglect, waste, squander</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to conceal, disguise</li></ol><p>Uncertain. By surface analysis, <i>&#42;ἀμαλδύς</i> (&#42;amaldús) +&lrm; <i>-ύνω</i> (-únō). The linguist Lucien van Beek suggests that the hypothetical adjective <i>&#42;(ἀ)μαλδύς</i> (&#42;(a)maldús) may reflect a Proto-Indo-European adjective <i>&#42;ml̥d-ús</i> (“weak, flaccid”), from the root <i>&#42;(s)meld-</i>, which he reconstructs with the meaning “to become weak, soft”.</p><p>The French linguist Charles de Lamberterie argues that the initial <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) may have emerged under the influence of <i>ἀμαθύνω</i> (amathúnō, “to turn into dust”). Van Beek proposes that the terms <i>ἀμαλός</i> (amalós) and <i>ἀμαυρός</i> (amaurós) may have influenced the term.</p><p>Beekes considers the connections to <i>βλαδεῖς</i> (bladeîs, “most powerless”), <i>βλαδαρόν</i> (bladarón, “flaccid, porous”) – which would infer the late addition of privative <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) – and <i>ἀμαλός</i> (amalós, “weak, soft”) unlikely. He also rules out connection with <i>μέλδομαι</i> (méldomai, “to smelt”), but points out the variant <i>ἀμέλδειν</i> (améldein), wondering if there has been an old interchange <i>*h₂m-</i>/<i>*sm-</i>. According to Beekes, Nikolaev (2005) and others have connected the word to <i>ἀμβλύς</i> (amblús, “blunt, dim, faint (of sight)”).</p><p>Lamberterie suggests that the Greek word could have faithfully preserved the original meaning of the Proto-Indo-European term, and that the other Indo-European cognates derived from another term meaning “crushed, pulverized”. However, van Beek suggests it is unlikely the other Indo-European cognates—such as Old Armenian <i>մեղկ</i> (mełk)—unanimously underwent a semantic shift from “crushed” into “weak”. Instead, van Beek proposes that the Ancient Greek adjective <i>&#42;(ἀ)μαλδύς</i> (&#42;(a)maldús) evolved to mean “soft, porous”, whence a factitive verb meaning “to destroy”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of young animals</i>) soft, tender, weak</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Variously connected with <i>ἀμαλδύνω</i> (amaldúnō, “to weaken”), <i>ἀμέλδειν</i> (améldein, “to melt”), <i>ἀμβλύς</i> (amblús, “dull, blunt, dim”), <i>μαλακός</i> (malakós) or <i>μαλθακός</i> (malthakós, “soft, gentle, weak”), <i>μέλδομαι</i> (méldomai, “to melt”) and <i>μύλη</i> (múlē, “mill”), but Beekes considers these connections as doubtful. Furnée connects it with <i>ἁπαλός</i> (hapalós, “soft, tender”), with the Pre-Greek interchange labial <i>μ/π</i>; this is possible, but also uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) a type of mushroom</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄμᾱνος</i> (Ámānos) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of marjoram</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμάρακον</i> (amárakon, “marjoram”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of everlasting</li><li>unfading, imperishable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμάραντον</i> (amáranton, “everlasting”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sparkle, twinkle</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shoot forth, dart</li><li>to dazzle</li></ol><p>See <i>μαρμαίρω</i> (marmaírō, “to sparkle, glisten”). Beekes prefers to ascribe it to a Pre-Greek substrate. The philologist Edward Ross Wharton, writing in the 19th-century, suggested that the term may derive from earlier <i>&#42;ἀμαρύχ-jω</i> (&#42;amarúkh-jō), and may relate to Irish <i>márach</i> and Gothic <i>maurgins</i>. It has otherwise been related to Lithuanian <i>mérkti</i> (“to close one's eyes”), though Beekes considers this impossible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) dim, dark</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;of vision</i>) dimness, dullness, faintness, feebleness, weakness (glossed with the Latin <i>cālīgō</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓μαυρός</i> (ămaurós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>barely seen, dim, faint, shadowy</li><li>dark, dusky</li><li>sightless, blind</li><li>unknown, uncertain, obscure</li><li>inconspicuous</li><li>weak, feeble</li></ol><p>Found in late Greek, of uncertain origin, but possibly related to <i>μαυρός</i> (maurós, “dark”) (but see that entry and <i>Μαῦρος</i> (Maûros) for more theories about that word's origins). One suggestion is Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂mr̥-wós</i>, from a root <i>&#42;h₂mer-</i>; compare Latin <i>umbra</i>, Luwian <i>&#42;𒈠𒅈𒉿𒄿𒀀</i> (*marwatar, “rot”).</p><p>Alternatively, Beekes suggests Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) having dark speckles, dots, dapples, spots, or mottling</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dimness</li><li>obscurity</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μαυρός</i> (ămaurós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “state of being”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to darken</li><li>to weaken</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to grow dark, dim, or pale</li></ol><p><i>ἀμαυρός</i> (amaurós, “dim; feeble”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkening, dimming, obscuring</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of the senses,&#32;especially of sight) becoming dull</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>later</i>) complete hindrance to sight without any visible cause, amaurosis</li></ol><li>(of the mind,&#32;as in old age) dulling</li><li>(<i>both&#32;of the senses&#32;and&#32;of the mind</i>) weakening, dulling</li><li>(<i>of oracles</i>) becoming obscure in presentience, failing in prophecy</li></ol><li>a spell which renders (someone or something) invisible</li><li>lowering, detraction, abasement, humiliation</li><li>“destruction” as another name for the hemlock</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) amaurosis (a name for the eighth τόπος of the δωδεκάωρος)</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓μαυρόω</i> (ămauróō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>ἀναβαίνω</i> (anabaínō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dim-sightedness, blurred or distorted vision, myopia or astigmatism</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓μβλῠωπός</i> (ămblŭōpós, “dim-sighted”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), ultimately from <i>ἀμβλύς</i> (amblús, “blunt, dull”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dim-sighted</li><li>(<i>of stars</i>) dim</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμβλύς</i> (amblús, “dull”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blunt, dull</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to take the strength away from</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be blunted, dulled</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become obtuse</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;figurative,&#32;of an oracle) to lose its edge, force</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;figurative,&#32;of the mind) to be disheartened, discouraged</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀμβλύς</i> (amblús) +&lrm; <i>-ῡ́νω</i> (-ū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dull, blunt, not sharp</li></ol><p>Perhaps connected with <i>ἀμαλός</i> (amalós, “soft, bland”), <i>ἀμβλίσκω</i> (amblískō, “to miscarry, to blind”), or <i>ἀμαλδύνω</i> (amaldúnō, “to destroy, weaken”) and thus possibly inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ml̥dus</i> (“soft, weak”) just as Latin <i>mollis</i> (see there) from earlier <i>&#42;molvis</i> and <i>&#42;molduis</i>.</p><p>Further cognates would then include Latin <i>blandus</i> and <i>mortārium</i>, Old Prussian <i>maldai</i> (“boys”), Welsh <i>blydd</i> (“soft”), Old Church Slavonic <i>младъ</i> (mladŭ, “young”), Sanskrit <i>मृदु</i> (mṛdú, “soft, mild, weak”), Old Armenian <i>մեղկ</i> (mełk, “soft, weak”) and English <i>mild</i>.</p><p>This being said, there is the possibility to connect some of these words with the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;melh₂-</i> (“to grind, crush, pulverize, mill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ambrosia</li><li>elixir of life</li><li>food, perfume, unguent of the gods</li><li>immortality</li><li>simulated divine ambrosia used in religious rites</li><li>name of some medicines</li><li>a festival of Bacchus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμβροτος</i> (ámbrotos, “immortal”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix). The <i>τ</i> (t) changed to <i>σ</i> (s) by palatalization. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>अमृत</i> (amṛ́ta, “elixir of life, immortality, food and nectar of the gods”). Also see ἄμβροτος (ámbrotos).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immortal; divine</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of everything belonging to the gods</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἄμβροτος</i> (ámbrotos, “immortal”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix). The τ changed to σ before ι by assibilation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἀμβροσίᾱ</i> (ambrosíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immortal; divine</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of everything belonging to the gods</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἄμβροτος</i> (ámbrotos, “immortal”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix). The τ changed to σ before ι by assibilation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to exchange [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> genitive <small>‘for something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give in exchange often with the preposition <i>ἀντί</i> (antí)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take in exchange</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to pass in or out of a house</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to do in turn or alternately, alternate, move crosswise</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reply, answer (<i>also in compounds</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to repay [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘for something’</small>]</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂meygʷ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of amethyst</li><li>of the color of amethyst</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμέθυστος</i> (améthustos, “amethyst”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓μῑξῐ́ᾱ</i> (ămīxĭ́ā, “unsociability”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to not worry about, to not care for, to disregard</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμελής</i> (amelḗs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) careless, negligent</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) uncared for, unheeded</li><li>unmelodious</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not, without) +&lrm; <i>μέλω</i> (mélō, “to care”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not, without) +&lrm; <i>μέλος</i> (mélos, “song, strain”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerless, fleeting, feeble</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a caliphal title used to denote legitimacy in the Muslim community</li></ol><p>From Arabic أَمِير الْمُؤْمِنِين (ʔamīr al-muʔminīn, literally “commander of the believers”), from أَمِير (ʔamīr, “prince; commander”) + مُؤْمِن (muʔmin, “believer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surpass, outstrip</li><li>to pass over, go beyond</li><li>to purchase</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Beekes suggests a relation to <i>ᾰ̓μῡ́νω</i> (ămū́nō, “to push back, ward off”) and a further relation of both terms to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;m(y)ewh₁-</i> (“to move”), which he reconstructs as <i>&#42;h₂mew(H)-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) emir</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic أمير.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go awkwardly to work</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμήχανος</i> (amḗkhanos) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unsociability (unwillingness to mingle with others)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́μῑκτος</i> (ắmīktos, “unsociable”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ammoniacum, a kind of gum resin</li><li><i>Ferula marmarica</i>, a plant producing this resin</li></ol><p>Genericized neuter form of <i>Ἀμμωνῐᾰκός</i> (Ammōnĭăkós, “of Ammon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bowl in which the blood of sacrificed animals was caught</li><li>little lamb</li></ol><p>Unknown. A connection with <i>ἀμάομαι</i> (amáomai, “to draw (milk)”) is no more than a guess.</p><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>अमत्रक</i> (amatraka, “drinking vessel”) and Latin <i>ampla</i> (“handle”) (both from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂em-</i> (“to grasp”)), as well as Latin <i>sentina</i> (“bilgewater”) (from <i>&#42;semH-</i> (“to scoop”)). Also see <i>ἄμη</i> (ámē, “water bucket”), <i>ἀμάω</i> (amáō, “to gather, harvest”).</p><p>From <i>ἀμνός</i> (amnós, “lamb”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) forgetfulness</li></ol><p>As if from *<i>ἄμνητος</i> (ámnētos) ("forgetful") +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā), from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>μιμνήσκω</i> (mimnḗskō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to forget</li></ol><p><i>ἄμνηστος</i> (ámnēstos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forgetfulness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>and especially</i>) an amnesty</li></ol><li>failure to mention (a thing), passing (it) over</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́μνηστος</i> (ắmnēstos, “forgotten, forgetful”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix), ultimately from <i>μνάομαι</i> (mnáomai, “remember”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lamb</li><li>a sheep, whether ram or ewe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;abnós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʷnós</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>agnus</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>агнѧ</i> (agnę), Old English <i>ēanian</i> (English <i>yean</i>), and Albanian <i>enjë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>requital, recompense</li><li>change, exchange</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμείβω</i> (ameíbō, “to exchange”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>disputed sense</i>) depth, gloom of night</li><li>evening or morning twilight</li></ol><p>The exact meaning had already been lost in antiquity. If a verbal noun of <i>ἀμέλγω</i> (amélgō, “to milk”), it means "the milking" (and the oxytonesis is secondary). Nilsson took it as the time of milking at the beginning of the night. Several authors argue that the word indicates the "evening twilight", and adduce parallels from other languages for an original meaning "night milking". Still, if <i>ὁμολογῷ</i> (homologōî, “at night”) (a gloss found in Hesychius) points to <i>&#42;ὀμολγός</i> (&#42;omolgós), the alternation <i>ἀ-/ὀ-</i> could point to a substrate word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accompany</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμορβός</i> (amorbós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pastoral, rural</li><li>obscure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμορβός</i> (amorbós, “shepherd, dark”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accompany [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to load</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμορβός</i> (amorbós) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>follower, attendant</li><li>shepherd</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark</li></ol><p>Unknown. One theory relates the word to Sanskrit <i>मार्ग</i> (mārga, “path”); see there for more.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂mergʷ-</i> (“dark”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;merkuz</i> (“dark”), Lithuanian <i>márgas</i> (“colorful”), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;mrixtos</i> (“painted, speckled”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stalks of mallow, used like hemp or flax</li><li>kind of garment</li></ol><p>Perhaps from the name of the island Amorgos or from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of vine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμπελος</i> (ámpelos, “vine”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vineyard</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμπελουργός</i> (ampelourgós, “vine-dresser”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>esparto (<i>Lygeum spartum</i>), used for tying up vines</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμπελος</i> (ámpelos, “vine”) +&lrm; <i>δεσμός</i> (desmós, “bond, fetter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich in vines</li><li>of the vine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμπελος</i> (ámpelos, “vine”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleavers (<i>Galium aparine</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμπελος</i> (ámpelos, “vine”) +&lrm; <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “fruit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild leek (<i>Allium ampeloprasum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμπελος</i> (ámpelos, “vine”) +&lrm; <i>πράσον</i> (práson, “leek”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vineyard</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμπελος</i> (ámpelos, “grape-vine”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn, grove,plantation).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine shawl worn by women and effeminate men</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) clothing, modes of dress</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμπέχω</i> (ampékhō, “to surround, cover, enclose”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>almond</li><li>peach stone</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p><p>According to Beekes, γδ-σγ alternations in the word's variants indicate "a typical" Pre-Greek substrate word, tentatively reconstructed as <i>*amut<sup>y</sup>gala</i>. He also supports Furnée's comparison with <i>μύκηρος</i> (múkēros, “almond, kind of a nut”) and a couple of Anatolian words related to sweetness, such as Hittite (mitgaimi-, “sweet bread”).</p><p>Alternatively, Blažek considers it possible to allow for Semitic provenance, namely to contain the term present in Hebrew <i>שָׁקֵד</i> (šāqḗḏ, “almond”), see there for variants, while accounting for the Greek /al/ by deeming a blend with the term in Hebrew <i>דֶּקֶל</i> (déqel, “palm tree; date”) to participate, imagining derivations <i>*midqalā</i> “place planted with date-palms” and <i>miṯqadā</i> “place planted with almond-trees” and hybridized <i>miṯqalā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of almonds</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμυγδάλη</i> (amugdálē, “almond”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dim, faint, obscure</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) faint, weak</li><li>vague, indistinct, obscure</li></ol><p>Unknown. Ancient Greek <i>ἀμαυρός</i> (amaurós, “dark”) is close in meaning and form, but the two cannot be combined in Indo-European terms. Influence of <i>φαιδρός</i> (phaidrós, “bright, beaming”) has been proposed, but such contaminations are often rather gratuitous. In principle, <i>ἀμυδ-</i> may continue an Indo-European root <i>&#42;h₂mud-</i>. The Slavic root Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mъd-</i> (“weakness”) cannot continue <i>&#42;h₂mud-</i> in view of Winter's Law (so it is from <i>&#42;mudʰ-</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prompt to ward off attack</li><li>fit for keeping off</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμῡ́νω</i> (amū́nō, “to keep off, ward off”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undefiled, immaculate, pure</li></ol><p>All the variations suggest a Pre-Greek origin. Also related is <i>μύσκος</i> (múskos, “defilement”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Tragic form of <i>ἀμφιέπω</i> (amphiépō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-edged</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-, “on two sides”) +&lrm; <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “point”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>for, for the sake of</li><li>about, concerning</li><li>around</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>by, near, around, with</li><li>about, on account of, for the sake of</li><li>regarding, concerning</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>about, around</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ampʰí</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂m̥bʰi</i> (“round about, around”). Beekes argues that the Indo-European term originated as a case form of <i>&#42;h₂ent-</i> (“face”), which is the source of <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ́</i> (ăntĭ́).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>ymb-</i>, Middle English <i>umbe</i>, Latin <i>ambi-</i>, Sanskrit <i>अभि</i> (abhí, “towards, over, upon”), Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎲𐎡𐎹</i> (abiy, “towards, against, upon”), Old High German <i>umbi</i>, Old Irish <i>imb</i> (“around”) and the first part of Old Armenian <i>ամբ-ողջ</i> (amb-ołǰ, “whole”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sea-girt (<i>of islands</i>)</li><li>between two seas (<i>of Corinth</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-, “both”) +&lrm; <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “saltwater, the sea”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), thus literally, “with the sea on both sides”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a bad Sicilian wine</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-ιας</i> is typical of names of wines. Furnée connects the gloss with <i>ἀμφής</i> (amphḗs, “wine blossom”) and <i>ὄμφαξ</i> (ómphax, “unripe grape”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Living a dual life on land and water: amphibious</li><li>In general: living in two worlds or in two separate manners.</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) aloe vera</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ́</i> (ămphĭ́) +&lrm; <i>βῐ́ος</i> (bĭ́os, “life”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cast net, net thrown round</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) of the garment thrown like a net over Agamemnon; of the shirt of Nessus</li><li>fetter, bond</li><li>of encircling walls</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφιβάλλω</i> (amphibállō, “to throw round”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive meaning</i>) dubious, doubtful</li><li>(<i>active meaning</i>) wavering, hesitant, uncertain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφιβάλλω</i> (amphibállō, “to waver, to hesitate”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>prosody</i>) amphibrachic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>prosody</i>) amphibrach</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ́</i> (ămphĭ́, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>βρᾰχῠ́ς</i> (brăkhŭ́s, “short”). Literally, “short on both sides”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>the covering of the entire body of a man</li><li>a covering or shield reaching head to toe</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ-</i> (ămphĭ-) +&lrm; <i>βροτός</i> (brotós, “mortal man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pointed at each end; double-pointed</li><li>armed at all points; practiced in combat</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ-</i> (ămphĭ-) +&lrm; <i>γυῖον</i> (guîon) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>being about, busied about, busy</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>substantive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in feminine</i>) handmaid, waiting-woman</li><li>(<i>in masculine</i>) attendant, follower</li><li>(<i>in masculine</i>) priest</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) much frequented</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ampʰikʷolos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂m̥bʰi-kʷol-ós</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí, “about”) +&lrm; <i>πέλω</i> (pélō, “to go”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀠𐀦𐀫</i> (a-pi-qo-ro) /ampʰíkʷolos/, Sanskrit <i>अभिचर</i> (abhicara, “servant”), <i>अभिचार</i> (abhicārá, “witchcraft”), Latin <i>anculus</i> (“servant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flowed around, seagirt (<i>of islands</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-) +&lrm; <i>ῥυτός</i> (rhutós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on both sides</li><li>around</li><li>apart, asunder</li><li>separately</li><li>between</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ́</i> (ămphĭ́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of serpent which can go backwards or forwards</li><li>veins which run between the breast and generative organs</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ́ς</i> (ămphĭ́s, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ</i> (-ă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>I place around</li></ol><p>from ἀμφί + ἵστημι (hístēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-ridged, double-crested (<i>of a helmet</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-) +&lrm; <i>φάλος</i> (phálos, “ridge of a helmet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having two left hands; clumsy; awkward</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ́</i> (ămphĭ́, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρῐστερός</i> (ărĭsterós, “left”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>covered on both sides</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí) +&lrm; <i>ἐρέφω</i> (eréphō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>added before a noun, adjective or verb to create words that indicates something on two sides or in two places; amphi-, ambi-, bi-:</li></ol><p>See <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ́</i> (ămphĭ́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) an epithet of ships, of uncertain meaning&#59; suggestions include&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wheeling either way; swaying to and fro; rolling</li><li>curved at both ends</li><li>rowed on both sides</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ-</i> (ămphĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ἑλῐ́σσω</i> (helĭ́ssō, “to turn round; to roll”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ</i> (-ă), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go about, to surround</li><li>to be busy about, to perform</li><li>to care for</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-) +&lrm; <i>ἕπω</i> (hépō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on (e.g. clothing)</li><li>to throw the arms round (as in an embrace)</li><li>to encompass, embrace</li><li>to encompass, beset</li><li>to force, move round</li><li>to doubt</li><li>to go into another place</li><li>to change</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-, “amphi-, on two sides”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround</li><li>to protect</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the state of being attacked on both sides</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφίβολος</i> (amphíbolos) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lame on both sides(?); strong on both sides(?). (<i>epithet of Hephaestus; etymological meaning uncertain, see Etymology section above</i>)</li></ol><p>The prefix is <i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-, “both”), but the root is less clear. Possibilities include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly plural</i>) Anything that binds or is bound around, bracelet or anklet.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Iron rings, by which folding-doors were secured in hinges.</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) τὰ ἀμφίδεᾱ: Rim of the os uteri.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφιδέω</i> (amphidéō, “to bind around”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā, action noun suffix), from <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind, to tie”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind around</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind, to tie”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amphitheatre, a double theatre</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>θέᾱτρον</i> (théātron, “theatre”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>two-headed</li><li>(<i>of the femur</i>) with two apophyses</li><li>(<i>of a couch</i>) having two places for the head</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-, on both sides) +&lrm; <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover all round, enwrap, enfold, surround</li><li>to cover or shelter (<i>someone with something</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-) +&lrm; <i>καλύπτω</i> (kalúptō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover or hide on every side</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-, “amphi, ambi, both”) +&lrm; <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “to hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>double-bowled (<i>of a goblet</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-) +&lrm; <i>κύπελλον</i> (kúpellon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dispute, to question</li><li>to doubt, to argue with two viewpoints</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἀμφῐλέγω</i> (amphĭlégō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell around</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí) +&lrm; <i>νέμω</i> (némō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tend, attend to</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-) +&lrm; <i>πένομαι</i> (pénomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rug or carpet with pile on both sides</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφῐ-</i> (amphĭ-, on both sides) +&lrm; <i>τάπης</i> (tápēs, “carpet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jar with a narrow neck, amphora</li><li>a liquid measure, nearly equal to 9 gallons</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφιφορεύς</i> (amphiphoreús, literally “two-handled”) by haplology, from <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí, “on both sides”) + <i>φορεύς</i> (phoreús, “bearer”), from <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bear”). The compound is earliest attested as Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀠𐀡𐀩𐀸</i> (a-pi-po-re-we) and Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀡𐀩𐀸</i> (a-po-re-we).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like an amphora</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφορεύς</i> (ămphoreús, “jar”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from or on both sides</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφοτέρω</i> (amphotérō, “both”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>both together</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rarely in singular</i>) both</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμφω</i> (ámphō, “both”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rarely in singular</i>) both</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμφω</i> (ámphō, “both”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watery part which runs out when olives are pressed, amurca</li><li>kind of dye</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician with its definitive article equivalent to Arabic مَرَقَة (maraqa), see it for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of stalks of mallow</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμοργίς</i> (amorgís, “stalks of mallow”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>almond</li></ol><p>See <i>ἀμυγδάλη</i> (amugdálē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blameless, noble, excellent</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of people, including Aegisthus</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>μῶμος</i> (mômos, “blame, reproach”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defender, helper</li><li>avenger</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμῡ́νω</i> (amū́nō, “to keep off, to ward off”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr, “-er”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀμύντωρ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amyntor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμύντωρ</i> (amúntōr, “defender, helper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to keep off, ward off, defend, guard, assist [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something/someone’</small> <i>and</i> accusative <small>‘from something/someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to avenge, punish, repay, requite</li></ol><p>Perhaps from an earlier <i>&#42;ἀμυ-νϝ-ω</i> (&#42;amu-nw-ō), a thematicized nasal present verbal formation (a la <i>κλίνω</i> (klínō) and <i>πλύνω</i> (plúnō)) from a root <i>ἀμυ-</i> (amu-) which may also be found in <i>ἀμεύομαι</i> (ameúomai, “to surpass, outstrip”), though the semantics are unclear if related. See also <i>μύνη</i> (múnē, “excuse, pretense”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to scratch, tear, lacerate</li><li>to tear in pieces, mangle</li><li>(<i>of thorns</i>) to prick</li><li>(<i>of insects</i>) to sting</li></ol><p>This word has been compared to Latin <i>mūcrō</i> (“sharp point”), Lithuanian <i>mùšti</i> (“to beat, strike, hit”) and Old English <i>gemyscan</i> (“to afflict, tease”). This seems a rather small basis for reconstructing an Indo-European root. Furnée accepts the comparison with Latin, but as a substrate word. The presence of <i>ἀμύσχεσται</i> (amúskhestai, “laceration of the flesh with claws”) shows a typical Pre-Greek variation, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἀνά</i> (aná)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) used before a vowel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-</i> (“not, un-”) when followed by laryngeal and a vowel: that is, in an open syllable of the <i>&#42;n̥HV-</i> form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive] (<i>rare</i>) on board</li><li>[<i>with</i> dative] (<i>of location</i>) on, upon</li><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>up, upwards, along</li><li>in sequence, each, throughout</li><li>up to</li><li>(<i>of amounts</i>) at a rate, speed, or price of, for</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) translatable with an adverbial noun phrase</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aná</i>. Beekes' derivation from <i>&#42;h₂en-</i> (“up, on high”) doesn't explain the second syllable. LIPP reconstructs Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;áno</i> (“up, over, along”). For /o/ &gt; /a/ a vowel harmony is posited, cf. <i>παρά</i> (pará), <i>κατά</i> (katá), <i>ἄντα</i> (ánta), <i>μάλα</i> (mála), <i>σάφα</i> (sápha), <i>θαμά</i> (thamá). For the stress shift, an analogy with other prepositions. Cognates include Avestan <i>𐬀𐬥𐬀</i> (ana), Latin <i>an-</i> (“up”) in <i>an-hēlō</i> (“to gasp”), <i>an-testor</i> (“to call up as a witness”), and English <i>on</i>.</p><p>The genitive is an innovated Greek associative. The dative is from the PIE locative. The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raised seat</li><li>tribune (elected official)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ascent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναβαίνω</i> (anabaínō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corrosion or erosion</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναβιβρώσκω</i> (anabibrṓskō, “I eat”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>force</li><li>constraint</li><li>necessity</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin, though likely from some derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂neḱ-</i> (“to reach, attain”) and cognate with Celtic terms like Old Irish <i>éicen</i> (“force, necessity”) (see there for more cognates), as well as perhaps Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;anhtō</i> (“persecution”). A substrate origin is also possible, though not favored.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀνάγκη)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ananke, a primordial goddess of Necessity and Fate</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>ἀναγκ-</i> (anank-), the stem of <i>ἤνεγκον</i> (ḗnenkon), aorist of <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to carry”), with the verbal noun–forming suffix <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reading</li><li>recognition</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bean trefoil (<i>Anagyris foetida</i>)</li></ol><p>The form with <i>ὀνο-</i> might point to a substrate, Pre-Greek word, as <i>α/ο</i> is frequent in such words. Amigues starts from Medieval Latin <i>faba inversa</i> (“inverted bean”) and connects it with <i>γυρός</i> (gurós, “round”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead up from a lower place to a higher</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead up to the high sea, to take to sea, carry by sea</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up from the coast into the interior, especially from Asia Minor into Central Asia</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring up, especially from the dead</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to conduct</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift up, raise</li><li>(<i>in various senses</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring back</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry back, refer to its principles</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reduce a syllogism to the first figure</li><li>to rebuild</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reckon or calculate</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to draw back, withdraw, retreat</li></ol></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to put out to sea, to set sail</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(figuratively,&#32;intransitive) to put to sea, to make ready, prepare oneself</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anadromous, (<i>of a fish</i>) entering a river from the sea</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;masculine) upward path, ascent, climb</li></ol><p>From the aorist root of <i>ἀνατρέχω</i> (anatrékhō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). The noun is preferably analyzed as <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>votive offering</li><li>that which is consecrated or dedicated</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>something devoted to evil, an accursed thing</li></ol><li>curse, damnation</li><li>a sort of dance</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνατίθημι</i> (anatíthēmi, “to dedicate”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pushing back</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνακρούω</i> (anakroúō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Without pain.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of persons</i>) Insensible to pain or danger.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>Unfeeling, hard-hearted, ruthless.</li></ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) Not painful, a lot free from pain.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>Cruel.</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ἀλγέω</i> (algéō, “feel bodily pain, suffer”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pedestal of a sundial</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>λῆμμα</i> (lêmma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a take-up</li><li>acquisition</li><li>reception, entertainment</li><li>assumption <i>(of an office)</i></li><li>assimilation</li><li>amends</li><li>recovery, repair</li><li>repetition</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναλαμβάνω</i> (analambánō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proportionate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) liberation</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλῡ́ω</i> (ănălū́ō, “set free”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, suffix added to verbal stems to form neuter nouns denoting the result or effect of an action, a particular instance of an action, or the object of an action)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a loosing, releasing</li><li>a dissolving, resolution of a whole into its parts, analysis</li><li>(<i>in Aristotle's Logic</i>) the reduction of the imperfect figures into the perfect one</li><li>the solution of a problem</li><li>retrogression, retirement, departure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναλύω</i> (analúō, “to unravel, investigate”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dissolved</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλῡ́ω</i> (ănălū́ō, “dissolve, set free”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, suffix forming perfective passive verbal adjectives).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calling to mind, reminiscence</li><li>memorial sacrifice</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>μιμνῄσκω</i> (mimnēískō, “call to mind”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>struck back, rebounding</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>substantive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>anapest, antidactylus, reversed dactyl, dactyl repercussus</li><li>an anapaestic verse, parabasis; satire, ridicule</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀναπαίω</i> (anapaíō, “strike again, strike back”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gaping, opening, unfolding</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναπτύσσω</i> (anaptússō, “I unfold”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;ditransitive) bind or make fast to</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(middle voice,&#32;transitive) moor, form a close connection with, confer, to fasten to oneself</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) be fastened to, cling</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) hang up in a temple, offer up</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;transitive) attach to, ascribe, refer</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) light up, kindle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be lighted up, ignited</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “on, up”) +&lrm; <i>ἅπτω</i> (háptō, “touch, fasten, ignite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nosesmart (<i>Lepidium sativum</i>)</li><li>snapdragon</li><li>sternutative</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνᾰ-</i> (ană-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>ῥῑ́ς</i> (rhī́s, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfriendly, hostile</li><li>untoward, strange</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) + the root of <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uneven, odd</li><li>at odds with one, hostile</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἄρτιος</i> (ártios, “even”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule, be lord (over)</li></ol><p><i>ἄναξ</i> (ánax) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>standing up</li><li>the act of making someone move, removal</li><li>resurrection, anastasis</li></ol><p><i>ἀνίστημι</i> (anístēmi, “rise up”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>height</li><li>majesty</li><li>construction, structure</li><li>elevation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνίστημι</i> (anístēmi, “to raise”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lifting up</li><li>(Koine,&#32;figuratively) endurance</li><li>rising of the sun</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνέχω</i> (anékhō, “to hold up, lift up”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>financial estimate, assessment</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνατάσσω</i> (anatássō, “to countermand expenditure”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extension</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>abstractly</i>) height</li></ol><li>stretching out</li><li>intensity, inflexibility</li><li>endurance of hunger, fasting</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) straining, effort</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰτείνω</i> (ănăteínō, “to lift up, stretch forth”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a coating over, plastering, anointing</li></ol><p>From ἀναχρίω (anakhríō) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not given in marriage</li><li>unpublished, secret</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἔκδοτος</i> (ékdotos, “given in marriage; published”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>textiles</i>) weasel</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) winch, capstan</li></ol><p>Hellenistic word, mentioned once at Pseudo-Macarius of Egypt (see quotation below). From the ancient <i>ἄνεμ(ος)</i>&nbsp;m (ánem(os), “wind”) + ending for feminine nouns <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ascend</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold up one's hands (<i>in fight, in surrender, in prayer etc.</i>)</li><li>to make ready</li><li>to lift up, exalt</li><li>to uphold, maintain</li><li>to put forth</li><li>to hold back</li><li>to rise up, emerge</li><li>to arise, happen</li><li>to appear, show oneself</li><li>to project, jut out</li><li>to hold on, keep doing (<i>+ participle</i>)</li><li>to cease</li><li>to wait, delay</li><li>to be alleviated of pain</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of dill</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνηθον</i> (ánēthon, “dill”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have come up to a point, reach up to</li><li>to belong, appertain</li><li>to be fit or proper</li><li>to come back</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓να-</i> (ăna-) +&lrm; <i>ἥκω</i> (hḗkō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>underage, not yet arrived at the age of majority</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “an-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>ἡλῐκῐ́ᾱ</i> (hēlĭkĭ́ā, “time of life, age”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man (adult male)</li><li>husband</li><li>human being; a mortal (as opposed to a god)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;anḗr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂nḗr</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>नृ</i> (nṛ́), <i>नर॑</i> (nára), Latin <i>neriosus</i> and Old Irish <i>nert</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;possibly dictionary-only) restless, unquiet</li></ol><p><i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἥσῠχος</i> (hḗsŭkhos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grief, sorrow, distress, trouble</li></ol><p>Unknown. The connection with Sanskrit <i>अमीव</i> (amīva, “pain, grief”) requires an unwarranted dissimilation <i>m-ṷ &gt; n-ṷ</i> and should be rejected. As remarked by Nikolaev, derivation from <i>&#42;h₂eys-</i> (“to search”) is semantically not very convincing; he proposed instead to connect the root <i>&#42;h₂eis(h₂)</i> (“to refresh”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let go, loosen</li><li>to release</li><li>to incite, impel, prompt</li><li>to expose, lay bare</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unconquered, unconquerable, invincible</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “conquered”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>active of the present, imperfect, future, and first aorist, and the perfect active <i>ᾰ̓νέστᾰκᾰ</i> (ănéstăkă)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make to stand up, raise up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise from sleep, wake up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise from the dead</li><li>(after Homer,&#32;of things,&#32;transitive) to set up, build</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put up for sale</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to rouse to action, stir up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make people rise, break up an assembly, to adjourn</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make people emigrate, transplant</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make suppliants rise and leave sanctuary</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make to ascend</li><li>(of sportsmen,&#32;transitive) to put up game, to spring</li></ol></ol><li>other tenses and voices</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stand up, rise, to speak</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise from bed</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise from the dead</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise from an illness, recover</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise as a champion</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise up, rear itself</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be set up</li><li>(<i>of a river</i>) to rise</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise to go, set out, go away</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be compelled to migrate, to be removed</li><li>(of a law court,&#32;intransitive) to rise</li><li>(of game,&#32;intransitive) to be put up</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>up to, upwards, up</li><li>(<i>intensifier</i>) thoroughly</li><li>indicating repetition or improvement: re-, again</li><li>back, backwards</li></ol><p>See <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ́</i> (ănắ, “up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) to dart up; to spring up</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-) +&lrm; <i>ᾱ̓ῑ̈́σσω</i> (āī̈́ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;of the gods) bloodless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn). Compare <i>ἀναίμονος</i> (anaímonos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spurn, reject</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, privative) +&lrm; <i>αἶνος</i> (aînos, “praise”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a taking up or away</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an undertaking</li></ol><li>destruction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>slaying, putting to death</li><li>banishment</li></ol><li>repeal, quashing</li><li>direct confutation of arguments</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓ναιρέω</i> (ănairéō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insensate, unfeeling, indifferent</li><li>senseless, tactless, stupid, insensible</li><li>unfelt</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>αἰσθητός</i> (aisthētós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not the cause of</li><li>guiltless</li><li>uncaused</li></ol><p><i>ἀν-</i> (an-, privative) +&lrm; <i>αἴτιος</i> (aítios, “responsible”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to help someone mount</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to prelude, to begin to play</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;transitive) to delay</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “up(wards)”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to go up, mount</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(transitive,&#32;intransitive) to board a ship, embark</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to mount</li><li>(of rivers,&#32;intransitive) to rise</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to shoot up (rise rapidly)</li><li>(Attic,&#32;intransitive,&#32;often with <i>ἐπὶ τὸ βῆμα</i> (epì tò bêma)) to mount the tribune, i.e. rise to speak</li><li>(of animals,&#32;transitive,&#32;intransitive) to mount (i.e. to mate)</li><li>(<i>of age</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to ascend to higher knowledge</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to surpass</li></ol><li>(<i>of events</i>) to turn out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(intransitive,&#32;with <i>καρδία</i> (kardía)) to enter into one's heart</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come to, pass to</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go back</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>step, stair</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>βαθμίς</i> (bathmís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine,&#32;Christianity) rebaptism</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>βαπτισμός</i> (baptismós) and <i>ἀναβαπτίζω</i> (anabaptízō) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to look up, especially as a mark of confidence</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to look up at [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>with cognate accusative</i>) to cast a look of</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to see again, to recover one's sight</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>βλέπω</i> (blépō, “to look at”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of plants</i>) shoot up</li><li>(<i>of monstrous births</i>)</li><li>shoot afresh</li><li>(<i>of a city</i>)</li><li>(<i>of misfortunes</i>) spring up</li><li>—</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) put forth</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-) +&lrm; <i>βλᾰστᾰ́νω</i> (blăstắnō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout aloud, utter a loud cry</li><li>to call on (<i>a person, e.g. for help</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>βοάω</i> (boáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ascent, way up</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰβάλλω</i> (ănăbállō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scarlet pimpernel (<i>Anagallis arvensis</i>)</li><li>poor man's weatherglass (<i>Lysimachia foemina</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ἀγαλλίς</i> (agallís, “Greek iris”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;in the second aorist)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to know well, know certainly</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to know again, recognize</li></ol><li>(Attic,&#32;of written characters,&#32;transitive) to know them again, to read</li><li>(Ionic,&#32;causative,&#32;mostly in aorist,&#32;transitive) to persuade or induce one to do something</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>γῐγνώσκω</i> (gĭgnṓskō, “to recognize”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Koine form of <i>ᾰ̓νᾰγῐγνώσκω</i> (ănăgĭgnṓskō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;control verb) to force [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνάγκη</i> (anánkē, “force, necessity”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓νᾰγκαῖος</i> (ănănkaîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓νᾰγκαῖος</i> (ănănkaîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood relationship, compare Latin <i>necessitūdō</i></li><li>a necessity</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναγκαῖος</i> (anankaîos, “necessary”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs), from <i>ἀνάγκη</i> (anánkē, “necessity”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prison</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) latrine, outhouse, toilet</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>necessary</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνάγκη</i> (anánkē, “necessity”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>necessary</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνάγκη</i> (anánkē, “necessity”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engrave and set up (<i>treaties, laws, etc.</i>)</li><li>to inscribe, register</li><li>to register someone’s name</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;middle voice) to describe</li><li>to entitle</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to paint</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inscribing, registering of properties or contracts</li><li>record, description</li><li>treatise, composition</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) prescription, formula</li><li>register, public record</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναγράφω</i> (anagráphō, “to register, record”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leading up, lifting up</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) lifting up of the soul to God</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἀνάγω</i> (anágō, “to lead up”); for the formation, see <i>ἀγωγή</i> (agōgḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie up</li><li>to crown</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>convolution</li><li>repetition, duplication</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) double infection</li><li>(<i>linguistic morphology</i>) reduplication</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>δίπλωσις</i> (díplōsis) and/or <i>ἀναδιπλόω</i> (anadiplóō) +&lrm; <i>-ωσις</i> (-ōsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach otherwise or better</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be better instructed, change one's mind</li><li>to show clear, expound, interpret</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>διδάσκω</i> (didáskō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift up, to carry off</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shameless</li><li>ruthless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>αἰδώς</i> (aidṓs, “shame”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of blood</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, negative prefix) + <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without bloodshed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up and carry off, bear away</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up bodies for burial</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take away, make away with, destroy, kill</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(of things,&#32;transitive) to abolish, annul, cancel</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to destroy (an argument), answer or confute it completely</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to appoint, ordain</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to answer, give a response</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up for oneself, take up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up and carry off, snatch away</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up dead bodies for burial</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up in one's arms</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to conceive in the womb</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take up money at interest</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take upon oneself, undertake</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to accept as one's own</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take back to oneself, undo what one has done, cancel</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō, “to take, grasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>insensately, unfeelingly, indifferently</li><li>senselessly, tactlessly, stupidly, insensibly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναισθητός</i> (anaisthētós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lack of sensation</li><li>without the aid of sense perception</li><li>mental obtuseness</li><li>stupor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>αἴσθησις</i> (aísthēsis, “sensation”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be shameless, behave impudently</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to treat shamelessly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναίσχυντος</i> (anaískhuntos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bend back</li><li>to return</li><li>to turn again</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>κάμπτω</i> (kámptō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>renewal</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰκαινόω</i> (ănăkainóō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light up</li><li>to fire up (<i>with anger</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>καίω</i> (kaíō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be renewed</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to renew</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “again”) +&lrm; <i>καινόω</i> (kainóō, “make new”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call back</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to uncover</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to unveil (oneself)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>καλύπτω</i> (kalúptō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to adorn anew, to restore</li><li>to bring into order again</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “re-”) +&lrm; <i>κοσμέω</i> (kosméō, “to order, arrange”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift or raise up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to feel lightened or lifted up, be relieved in mind</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-) +&lrm; <i>κουφί̆ζω</i> (kouphí̆zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relief</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓νᾰκουφί̆ζω</i> (ănăkouphí̆zō, “to lift up”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine closely, to question, interrogate</li><li>to inquire into</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to dispute with</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to judge</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to revise</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to push back, stop short, check</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to put (<i>a ship</i>) astern</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;figurative) to put back</li><li>(<i>music</i>) to strike up</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>κρούω</i> (kroúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put up, put away, hide</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gladiolus (<i>Gladiolus italicus</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ᾰ̓νᾰκτόρῐος</i> (ănăktórĭos, “royal; belonging to a king”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Anactorium; an Anactorian</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνακτόρῐος</i> (anaktórĭos, “belonging to a lord”), from <i>ἀνάκτωρ</i> (anáktōr, “lord”), from <i>ἄναξ</i> (ánax, “lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconsistent</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) irregular, anomalous</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἀκόλουθος</i> (akólouthos, “consistent”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a coming round again, circuit, revolution</li><li>(<i>poetic&#32;metre</i>) recurrence of form, strophic arrangement</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰκῠκλέω</i> (ănăkŭkléō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs); compare <i>ᾰ̓νᾰκῠ́κλωσῐς</i> (ănăkŭ́klōsĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Varia lectio of <i>ᾰ̓νᾰκῠ́κλησῐς</i> (ănăkŭ́klēsĭs).</li><li>wheeling about</li><li>(<i>politics</i>) cycle of constitutions, anacyclosis</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰκῠκλόω</i> (ănăkŭklóō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), the former from <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-) +&lrm; <i>κυκλόω</i> (kuklóō); compare <i>ᾰ̓νᾰκῠ́κλησῐς</i> (ănăkŭ́klēsĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take again</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-) +&lrm; <i>λᾰ́ζομαι</i> (lắzomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick up, gather up</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a slave who picked up crumbs after a meal</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλέγω</i> (ănălégō, “to pick up”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent noun&ndash;forming suffix): “picker-upper”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend</li><li>to kill, destroy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) + an unattested <i>&#42;ἁλίσκω</i> (&#42;halískō), originally meaning "to tear up"; see <i>ἁλίσκομαι</i> (halískomai, “to be captured”) for more on the second component.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) take up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(physically,&#32;transitive) take in one's hands</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;transitive) take up subjects in turn</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) take back</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) regain, recover</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) restore, repair</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) resume</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up, again, back”) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Want of feeling, insensibility.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνάλγητος</i> (análgētos, “without pain”), from <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ἀλγέω</i> (algéō, “feel bodily pain, suffer”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>powerless to heal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἄλθος</i> (álthos) + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proportion (also mathematical)</li><li>(logic,&#32;linguistics) analogy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνάλογος</i> (análogos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proportional</li><li>(grammar,&#32;philosophy) of or pertaining to the school of thought of the philosopher Ammonius Saccas</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλογέω</i> (ănălogéō, “gather up; reckon”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must dissolve or be dissolved</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλῡ́ω</i> (ănălū́ō, “dissolve”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, suffix forming verbal adjectives denoting necessity).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deliverer</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλῡ́ω</i> (ănălū́ō, “unloose”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>analytical</li><li>dissolvent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνᾰ́λῠσῐς</i> (anắlŭsĭs, “analysis”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>analytically, dissolvingly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναλυτικός</i> (analutikós, “analytical, dissolvent”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, “a suffix creating adverbs from adjective stems”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>illiterate, uneducated</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλφάβητος</i> (alphábētos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deliverer, liberator, unlooser</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλῡ́ω</i> (ănălū́ō, “unloose, set free”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to wander, unsettle</li><li>to unloose, undo, set free</li><li>to undo (<i>in various senses</i>)</li><li>to reduce</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to resolve</li><li>to do away with, cancel, cancel faults</li><li>to suspend</li><li>to solve the problem of a thing</li><li>to release from a spell</li><li>to relieve</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to loose from moorings, weigh anchor, depart, go away,</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to die</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “thoroughly”) +&lrm; <i>λύω</i> (lúō, “loosen”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wait for, await, hold on</li><li>to put off, delay</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>μένω</i> (ménō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>promiscuously, pell-mell</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to remind</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to remember</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>μιμνήσκω</i> (mimnḗskō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>patient abiding, endurance</li><li>waiting, delay</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναμένω</i> (anaménō, “to wait”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ). By surface analysis, <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>μονή</i> (monḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indisputable, undeniable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἀμφήριστος</i> (amphḗristos, “disputed, doubtful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of manhood (<i>also in the sense of male genitals</i>)</li><li>unmanliness, cowardice</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνανδρος</i> (ánandros) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without apodosis, being a proposition lacking a consequent clause</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ν-</i> (ăn-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ἀντᾰποδῐ́δωμῐ</i> (antăpodĭ́dōmĭ, “I give back”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos), from <i>ἀντῐ-</i> (antĭ-) + <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-) + <i>δῐ́δωμῐ</i> (dĭ́dōmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trousers worn by Scythians and Sacae</li></ol><p>From Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw back and brandish (<i>a weapon</i>)</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to be flung up, to leap up</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>πάλλω</i> (pállō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill up</li><li>to fill full [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li><li>to defile, infect</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall back</li><li>to fall back, give ground</li><li>to flag, lose heart</li><li>to be given up</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to sit down (<i>especially at table</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>πίπτω</i> (píptō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike back</li><li>to drive back</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “again; backwards”) +&lrm; <i>παίω</i> (paíō, “to strike, hit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to rest, to soothe, refresh</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “thorough”) +&lrm; <i>παύω</i> (paúō, “stop, end”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring over, convince</li><li>to persuade, move (<i>to do something</i>)</li><li>to seduce, mislead</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe (again), take a breath, draw breath</li><li>to recover [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from’</small>]</li><li>to revive</li><li>to send forth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to make a step backwards</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to call back and question, cross-examine</li><li>to retract what is said before</li><li>to return (<i>of retrograde motion of the planets, of recurring events</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (“backwords”) +&lrm; <i>πούς (gentive&#58; ποδός)</i> (“foot”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (“suffix for verbs”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without excuse or defense, inexcusable, impardonable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>ἀπολογέομαι</i> (apologéomai, “to speak in defense”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unfold, open (for reading, etc.)</li><li>to undo, open</li><li>to cut open</li><li>to ruminate, chew</li><li>to unfold, disclose</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>πτύσσω</i> (ptússō, “to fold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ᾰ̓ναντᾰπόδοτος</i> (ănantăpódotos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwashen, unwashed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἀπονίζω</i> (aponízō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch up</li><li>to snatch away, carry off, steal</li><li>to rescue</li><li>to take by storm, plunder</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw upward</li><li>to run a risk</li><li>to set in motion, stir up</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>ῥίπτω</i> (rhíptō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of a leader</li><li>lawlessness, anarchy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄναρχος</i> (ánarkhos, “without leader”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dig up</li><li>to extirpate (<i>of plants</i>)</li><li>to raze to the ground (<i>of buildings</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>σκάπτω</i> (skáptō) The present tense system includes a <b>T</b> progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a digging up, an excavation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνασκάπτω</i> (anaskáptō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pack up (<i>baggage</i>)</li><li>to disfurnish, dismantle</li><li>to waste, ravage, destroy</li><li>to subvert</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be bankrupt, ruin</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>σκευάζω</i> (skeuázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>logic</i>) destructive</li><li>restorative, curative</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰσκευᾰ́ζω</i> (ănăskeuắzō, “to dismantle, remove”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pull down</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, intensifying affix) +&lrm; <i>σπάω</i> (spáō, “to draw”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn upside down, upset</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to turn back, bring back</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn back, return, retire</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be or dwell in (<i>a place</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to revolve (<i>of the sun</i>)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to rally</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-) +&lrm; <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turning back, return</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀναστρέφω</i> (anastréphō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reassessment of war-tax levied on property</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰσῠντᾰ́σσω</i> (ănăsŭntắssō, “to reassess war-tax”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to raise up</li><li>to cause to grow</li><li>to give birth to</li><li>to bring to light</li><li>to rise (<i>of the sun and moon</i>)</li><li>to grow (<i>of hair</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>τέλλω</i> (téllō, “to perform, accomplish, rise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut open</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>lay upon, put upon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) put on board ship</li></ol><li>(<i>in prose</i>) refer, attribute, ascribe, credit, compare (<i>something to someone</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>entrust (<i>something to someone</i>)</li></ol><li>set up as a votive gift, dedicate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>set up, erect; (<i>figuratively</i>) dedicate</li><li>set up and leave in a place</li></ol><li>put back, move back</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>restore, put back anew</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) put upon for oneself</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) impart, communicate (something) one’s own</li><li>remit, refer</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) place differently, change around (<i>e.g</i>, the pieces on a draughtsboard)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>take back a move at pessoi</li><li>(by extension,&#32;figuratively) retract one’s opinion (<i>frequent in Plato</i>)</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch up, hold up, lift up</li><li>to stretch forth</li><li>to lift up, exalt</li><li>to stretch out, extend</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>τείνω</i> (teínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rising above the horizon (of any heavenly body,&#32;especially&#32;the sun)</li><li>the direction of sunrise, the East</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) the ascendant, the point where the eastern horizon intersects the zodiac</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) the phase of the new moon when 15 degrees away from the sun</li><li>growing, growth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνατέλλω</i> (anatéllō, “rise, grow up”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix), from <i>ἀνά</i> (aná, “up”) + <i>τέλλω</i> (téllō, “I perform, accomplish, rise”), related either to <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “accomplishment, fulfillment”) or to <i>ἔτλην</i> (étlēn, “endure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cutting up, especially of an animal or person, mutilation, dissection</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνατέμνω</i> (anatémnō, “to cut up”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn up, turn over, overturn, upset</li><li>to overthrow, subvert</li><li>to upset in argument, refute</li><li>to dishearten</li><li>to stir up, awaken</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run back, return</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of property</i>) to revert</li><li>(<i>in wrtiting</i>) to recur to a point</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to retrace, traverse, undo, make amends for</li><li>(<i>in logic</i>) to run back to, return to the source</li><li>to have recourse to</li></ol><li>to jump up and run</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) to spread over</li><li>(<i>of plants, cities and people</i>) to shoot up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to soar aloft</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō, “to run, run over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capsizing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνατρέπω</i> (anatrépō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>education, rearing, nurture</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνατρέφω</i> (anatréphō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring, bear, carry upwards</li><li>to bring, bear, carry back</li><li>to report</li><li>to restore</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “upwards, back”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>impalpable, intangible</li><li>(<i>of wine</i>) tasteless, insipid</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ἁφή</i> (haphḗ, “sense of touch”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reference of a thing to a standard</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰφέρω</i> (ănăphérō, “to bring, carry up”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;passive voice</i>) to draw back, withdraw, fall back, retreat</li><li>(rare,&#32;active voice) to force back</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>χάζομαι</i> (kházomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anoint, smear, or apply a substance again or thoroughly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to apply topically as a treatment</li></ol><li>(<i>later</i>) to coat over, plaster, seal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “up, again”) +&lrm; χρίω (khríō, “to rub, smear, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be an anachronism</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “backwards”) +&lrm; <i>χρονῐ́ζω</i> (khronĭ́zō, “to be located in time”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an anachronism</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>an exchange of quantity of two syllables</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀναχρονίζομαι</i> (anakhronízomai, “to be an anachronism”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós), from <i>ἀνά</i> (aná, “backwards”) + <i>χρονίζω</i> (khronízō, “to be located in time”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go back</li><li>to withdraw, retire</li><li>to retreat</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has retired from the world, anchorite</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνᾰχωρέω</i> (anăkhōréō, “to retire, withdraw”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a retreat</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “intensifying affix”) + <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō, “to go back, withdraw”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, “Suffix added to verbal stems to form neuter nouns denoting the result or effect of an action”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retiring, retreat</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓νᾰχωρέω</i> (ănăkhōréō, “to retire, retreat”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cool, refresh, revive by fresh air</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ψῡ́χω</i> (psū́khō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave, especially one made in war, captive</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”). Could also be from <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr) + <i>ἀποδίδωμι</i> (apodídōmi, “I pay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common purslane (<i>Portulaca oleracea</i>)</li><li>wild strawberry (<i>Fragaria vesca</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>ἀθραγένη</i> (athragénē, “traveller's joy”), which is formally quite acceptable. A Pre-Greek origin is probable anyhow.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(hapax legomenon,&#32;Koine) woman</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ, ἀνδρός</i> (anḗr, andrós, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enslave</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνδράποδον</i> (andrápodon, “a slave”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, “a suffix forming a verb from a noun”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slave dealer, one who enslaves men and sells them as slaves, kidnapper</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνδραποδίζω</i> (andrapodízō, “I enslave, sell slaves”), from <i>ἀνδράποδον</i> (andrápodon, “slave”), +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) courage, bravery, fortitude (of men or women)</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) A brave deed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνδρεῖος</i> (andreîos, “manly”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, abstract noun&ndash;forming suffix). Compare <i>ἠνορέη</i> (ēnoréē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>bravely</li></ol><p><i>ἀνδρεῖος</i> (andreîos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) man-slayer (<i>epithet of Enyalius</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) + the root of <i>ἔπεφνον</i> (épephnon, “to slay, kill”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs). The first two syllables scan as light in Homer, suggesting the earliest form may have been <i>&#42;ἀδριφόντης</i> (&#42;adriphóntēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓νδρεῖος</i> (ăndreîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or pertaining to a man</li><li>manly, masculine, strong</li><li>brave, courageous</li><li>stubborn</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who is courageous</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νήρ</i> (ănḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>image of a man, statue, puppet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾱ́ς</i> (-ĭā́s). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀇𐀪𐀊𐀳</i> (a-di-ri-ja-te), <i>𐀀𐀇𐀪𐀊𐀠</i> (a-di-ri-ja-pi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) niche for a statue</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνδριάς</i> (andriás, “statue”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the cyclops</i>) eating men</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>"man-slaying"</li><li>homicidal</li><li>deadly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr) +&lrm; <i>φόνος</i> (phónos). Cognate to Sanskrit <i>नृहन्</i> (nṛhán, nṛghné).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common to men and women</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermaphrodite</li><li>a man who is womanish; effeminate</li><li>pathic; bottom</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>γυνή</i> (gunḗ, “woman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) human</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>St. John's wort (<i>Hypericum perforatum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea-navel (<i>Acetabularia acetabulum</i>)</li></ol><p>The first element is from <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) rear to manhood, make a man</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) become a man, reach manhood</li></ol><p>From ἀνήρ (anḗr, “man”, noun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>andron (men's apartment in a house or banqueting-hall)</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) hallway between two rooms or patios in a house</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νήρ</i> (ănḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) without clothes; naked; nude</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>εἷμα</i> (heîma, “clothes”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inquire, ask</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>εἴρομαι</i> (eíromai), Ionic variant of <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai, “to ask”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>wake up, rouse</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “an intensifying prefix”) +&lrm; <i>ἐγείρω</i> (egeírō, “to wake”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) windy (empty and lacking substance), good for nothing, in vain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνεμος</i> (ánemos, “wind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poppy anemone (<i>Anemone coronaria</i>)</li><li>(ἀνεμώνη ὀρεία,&#32;mountain anemone) Greek anemone (<i>Anemone blanda</i>)</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>ἄνεμος</i> (ánemos, “wind”) +&lrm; <i>-ώνη</i> (-ṓnē, feminine patronymic suffix).</p><p>Alternatively, from a Phoenician source, akin to Arabic شَقَائِق اَلنُّعْمَان (šaqāʔiq an-nuʕmān, “anemones”) and Hebrew (Isaiah Scroll) <i>נִטְעֵי נַעֲמָנִים</i> (nit'ei na'amanim, “plants of pleasantness”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unquestioned, irrefutable</li><li>irreproachable</li></ol><p><i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἐλέγχω</i> (elénkhō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without reproach, envy or jealousy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ἐπίφθονος</i> (epíphthonos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to snatch up and carry off</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ἐρείπω</i> (ereípō, “to throw down, tear down”), though some dictionaries associate it with <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō, “to snatch away, carry off”) or <i>ἐρέπτομαι</i> (eréptomai, “to feed on”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(male) first cousin, cousin</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;népōts</i> (“male descendant”). Some have suggested that it reflects Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂népōts</i>, due to the initial ἀ-. Alternatively, it results from the prefixation of <i>&#42;sm̥-</i> (“one; together”) in Proto-Indo-European, but this is problematic, as that would yield <i>&#42;ἁνεψιός</i> (&#42;hanepsiós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) aneurysm</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνευρῡ́νω</i> (aneurū́nō, “to dilate”) (from <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>εὐρῡ́νω</i> (eurū́nō, “to broaden, widen”) + <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disobey [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήκουστος</i> (anḗkoustos, “unhearing, disobedient”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ́</i> (ăntĭ́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flower, blossom</li></ol><p><i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) silky flower-tufts of the reed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>packsaddle, bat, a saddle designed to secure and carry goods on the back of an animal</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>κανθήλια</i> (kanthḗlia, “panniers at the sides of a packsaddle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marine goldfish (<i>Anthias anthias</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived by Strömberg from <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās), because of its color. However the etymology is still open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strew or deck with flowers</li><li>to dye with bright color (esp. with flowers)</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́νθος</i> (ắnthos, “flower”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand against</li><li>to oppose</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντῐ-</i> (antĭ-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνθέω</i> (anthéō, “to flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flowery</li><li>bright, burnished, or wrought with flowers, flowered</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθεμον</i> (ánthemon) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oriental hawthorn (<i>Crataegus orientalis</i>)</li><li>bee (literally "the flowery one")</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀνθηδών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anthedon (a region, a Boeotia in Greece)</li><li>Anthedon (a city in Judea, situated near Gaza, Palestine)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flowery, blooming</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fresh, young</li><li>bright-colored, brilliant</li><li>brilliant, splendid</li><li>(<i>of style</i>) flowery, florid</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́νθ(ος)</i> (ắnth(os), “blossom”) +&lrm; <i>-ηρός</i> (-ērós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) A traditional cavalry display in Ancient Athens; in particular, a series of contests occurring later in the democratic period as a contest between tribal cavalry units at Athenian festivals such as the Olympieia and Panathenaea.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “one against another”) +&lrm; <i>ἱππεύς</i> (hippeús, “knight”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā), literally “knightly-contest”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The act of gathering flowers; flowerpicking.</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a collection of poems; anthology</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower, blossom”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “account”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of carbuncle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθραξ</i> (ánthrax, “carbuncle”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hornet, wasp</li></ol><p>Ending like <i>ἀπήνη</i> (apḗnē), <i>λαμπήνη</i> (lampḗnē) and <i>ὑπήνη</i> (hupḗnē). The word has also been compared with similar terms like <i>ἀνθρηδών</i> (anthrēdṓn, “hornet”), <i>θρῶναξ</i> (thrônax, “drone”) and <i>τενθρηδών</i> (tenthrēdṓn, “kind of wasp”). In view of the suffix and the variations, the word is probably Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pile of charcoal</li><li>a charcoal fire</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́νθρᾰξ</i> (ắnthrăx, “charcoal”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>charcoal-filled</li><li>coal-filled</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́νθρᾰξ</i> (ắnthrăx, “charcoal; coal”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “-ful; -filled”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hornet (<i>Vespa crabro</i>)</li></ol><p>Beekes’ entry in his <i>Etymological Dictionary of Greek</i> reads (in paraphrase):</p><p>"<i>Undoubtedly a Pre-Greek word, like <i>ἀνθρήνη</i> (anthrḗnē), <i>πεμφρηδών</i> (pemphrēdṓn) and <i>τενθρηδών</i> (tenthrēdṓn). Needless to say, the connection with <i>θρέομαι</i> (thréomai, “to cry aloud, shriek”) and <i>θόρυβος</i> (thórubos, “uproar, clamor”) makes no sense. A difficult problem is the relation to Germanic and Balto-Slavic words for ‘drone’: Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;drēnuz</i>, Lithuanian <i>tranas</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;trǫtъ</i>.</i>"</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ᾰ̓́νθρωπος</i> (ắnthrōpos): mannequin</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́νθρωπος</i> (ắnthrōpos, “person”) +&lrm; <i>-ίσκος</i> (-ískos, diminutive suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>looking like a human, in human form, humanoid</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>making of man or men</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνθρωποποιός</i> (anthrōpopoiós, “making man”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos, “man”) + <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “make”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anthropophagous, man-eating, cannibalistic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos, “person”) +&lrm; <i>φάγος</i> (phágos, “glutton”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking of man</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos, “person”) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos, “account”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>human</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>human</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos, “human being”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plant of species <i>Cressa cretica</i> of alkaliweeds</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθυλλον</i> (ánthullon) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proconsulate</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νθῠ́πᾰτος</i> (ănthŭ́pătos, “proconsul”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to a proconsul, proconsular</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νθῠ́πᾰτος</i> (ănthŭ́pătos, “proconsul”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antelope (a fierce legendary creature said to live on the banks of the Euphrates, having long serrated horns and being hard to catch)</li></ol><p>The origin is unknown. The word superficially resembles <i>ᾰ̓́νθος</i> (ắnthos) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps) and some older authorities suggested this as the etymology, but there is reason to think it is instead a corruption of some other, possibly foreign, word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proconsul</li><li>(<i>as an adjective</i>) proconsular</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “before”) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́πᾰτος</i> (hŭ́pătos, “consul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) maternal or paternal grandmother.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂en-ih₂-</i> (“grandmother”). Cognates include Latin <i>anus</i> (“old woman”), Old Armenian <i>հան</i> (han, “grandmother”) and Lithuanian <i>anýta</i> (“mother-in-law”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἀνοίγω</i> (anoígō, “to open”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>οἴγνυμι</i> (oígnumi, “to open”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to open</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to lay open, unfold, disclose</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to get into the open sea, get clear of land</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be open, stand open, lie open</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>οἴγω</i> (oígō, “to open”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remove (<i>especially to a higher location</i>)</li><li>to move (<i>one’s residence</i>) inland or to higher ground</li><li>to migrate</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκίζω</i> (oikízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to build up</li><li>to wall up</li><li>to build again, rebuild</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκοδομέω</i> (oikodoméō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opened, open, not closed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνοίγω</i> (anoígō, “open”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stay, cessation</li><li>ceasefire, truce</li><li>hindrance</li></ol><p>Formed by reduplication from <i>ἀνοχή</i> (anokhḗ), from the root of <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lawlessness</li><li>iniquity</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνομος</i> (ánomos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>dissimilarly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνόμοιος</i> (anómoios) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἄνομος</i> (ánomos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἄνομος</i> (ánomos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of appetite</li><li>lack of desire</li></ol><p><i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”, <i>alpha privativum</i>) +&lrm; <i>ὄρεξῐς</i> (órexĭs, “yearning”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract-noun-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to start up, leap up</li></ol><p><i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ὀρούω</i> (oroúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holding back, cessation of hostilities; armistice, truce</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>forebearance, long-suffering, tolerance</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀνέχω</i> (anékhō, “to bear”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ́</i> (ăntĭ́)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come opposite to, meet face to face, meet with [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντί</i> (antí) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō). See also <i>ἀντιάω</i> (antiáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold out, keep</li><li>to withstand, endure, persevere</li><li>to prevail, be prevalent</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to cling to, adhere to, keep close to</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to support, care for</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to resist</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>over against, opposite</li><li>at the same time as</li><li>in exchange for, in place of</li><li>at the price of, in return for</li><li>for the sake of, for</li><li>instead of</li><li>compared with</li><li>equivalent to, no better or worse than</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂énti</i> (“front, opposite”). Cognates include English <i>and</i>, Latin <i>ante</i>, Sanskrit <i>अन्ति</i> (ánti), and Old Armenian <i>ընդ</i> (ənd).</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of separation or comparison.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opposing face to face, hostile</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>βίᾱ</i> (bíā, “force, violence”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>given in lieu of</li><li>given as a remedy for</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;f) antidote</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἀντιδίδωμι</i> (antidídōmi) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>return-gift; a present donated or offered in return or in exchange; compensation, remuneration, recompense, reciprocation</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;Eastern Orthodoxy,&#32;Eastern Catholicism) antidoron; morsel of blessed bread from the remains of the Eucharist offering distributed to members of the congregation in churches that use the Byzantine Rite liturgy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “in exchange; in return; instead of”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;dṓron</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;déh₃rom</i>; cf. verb <i>ἀντιδωρέομαι</i> (antidōréomai, “to donate, exchange, present in return, offer instead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>godlike</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós)</p><p>The meaning of ἀντί here is "exchangeable for, no better or worse than."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opposition, resistance</li><li>contradiction</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) antithesis</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) change or transposition of a letter</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐτῐ́θημῐ</i> (ăntĭtĭ́thēmĭ, “to set against”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) antithet</li></ol><p>Substantivization of the neuter form of <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ́θετος</i> (ăntĭ́thetos, “opposing”, “contrasting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opposing, contrasting</li></ol><p>Koine adjective, from an ancient neuter <i>ἀντίθετον</i> (antítheton, “rhetorical antithesis”). Deverbal from <i>ᾰ̓ντῐτῐ́θημῐ</i> (ăntĭtĭ́thēmĭ, “set against”) (<i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) + stem <i>θε-</i> as in <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi) - <i>θετός</i> (thetós)) + <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be set over against, correspond with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of places</i>) to be opposite to</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to be opposite or opposed</li></ol><li>to be opposed, in various ways</li><li>to resist, be adverse</li><li>to be hurtful, damaging</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντῐ-</i> (antĭ-, “opposite, anti-”) +&lrm; <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai, “to lie”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>contradictory, reverse</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos, “word”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>against (me, him, her, etc.)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>against</li><li>before, in front of</li><li>in the presence of</li><li>facing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loom or a part of it</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντίος</i> (antíos): the adverb/preposition is from the adverbial accusative, and the noun is from the neuter singular.</p><p>Formally identical to Hittite <i>𒄩𒀭𒍝𒀭</i> (ḫa-an-za-an /⁠ḫanzan⁠/) as if from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂entíom</i>, though the parallel formations in Greek and Hittite may be independent.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opposite, facing</li><li>going to meet (<i>with genitive or dative of the person to be met</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;antíos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂entíos</i> (“front”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>opposite, facing</li><li>going to meet (<i>with genitive or dative of the person to be met</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;antíos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂entíos</i> (“front”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like a child</li><li>no longer a child</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mere boy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antagonistic, rival, competitive</li><li>contrary, opposite, hostile</li><li>balanced, equal, proportioned</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντῐ́</i> (antĭ́, “against”) +&lrm; <i>πάλη</i> (pálē, “wrestling, fight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) a title given to initiates of the mysteries of Mithras</li></ol><p><i>ἀντῐ́</i> (antĭ́, “against, opposite”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr, “father”), potentially to denote the opposition of the initiate with the highest grade in the Cult of Mithras, the <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr, “father”). It may also derive from the alternative meaning of <i>ἀντῐ́</i> (antĭ́, “like, equivalent to”), hence meaning "like the father."</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀντίπατρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antipater</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “like (the)”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰτήρ</i> (pătḗr, “father”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with the feet opposite</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine plural) the antipodes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opposition, resistance</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) interchange or substitution of cases, antiptosis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>πτῶσις</i> (ptôsis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>calf's snout (<i>Antirrhinum orontium</i>, syn. <i>Misopates orontium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντῐ-</i> (antĭ-, “instead of, counterfeit”) +&lrm; <i>ῥῑ́ς</i> (rhī́s, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sigma reversed (<i>Ͻ, ͻ</i> (Ͻ, ͻ)), as a critical mark</li><li>symbol for [ps]</li></ol><p><i>ἀντῐ-</i> (antĭ-) +&lrm; <i>σῖγμᾰ</i> (sîgmă)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀντέχω</i> (antékhō)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold out, keep</li><li>to withstand, endure, persevere</li><li>to prevail, be prevalent</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to cling to, adhere to, keep close to</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to support, care for</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to resist</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a setting in array against</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) opposition</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐτᾰ́σσω</i> (ăntĭtắssō, “to set opposite to”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stretching the contrary way, e.g. in the setting of a dislocated limb</li><li>opposition, resistance</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐτείνω</i> (ăntĭteínō, “to stretch or strain back”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sounding in answer, concordant, as in the octave</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) responsive to</li></ol><li>(<i>neuter substantive</i>) a concord in the octave</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ecclesiastical</i>) antiphon, anthem</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “in return”) +&lrm; <i>-φωνος</i> (-phōnos, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) antichrist</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>Χριστός</i> (Khristós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise (<i>one's hands etc.</i>) against</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to rise up against</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>αἴρω</i> (aírō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>deponent</i>) to struggle against, prove a match for [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to struggle or dispute with [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to strive [<i>with</i> <b>πρός</b> (prós, + accusative) <small>‘against’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωνίζομαι</i> (agōnízomai, “contend for a prize”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rival, opponent, competitor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνταγωνίζομαι</i> (antagōnízomai) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange (one thing with another), interchange</li><li>to give in exchange</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓λλᾰ́σσω</i> (ăllắssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exchanging, exchange, barter</li></ol><p>Substantive of <i>ᾰ̓ντᾰ́λλᾰγος</i> (ăntắllăgos, “exchanged”), from <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἀλλάσσω</i> (allássō, “change; exchange, barter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead up against</li><li>to bring up or out instead</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reflection</li><li>bending back</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ́</i> (ăntĭ́, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ́</i> (ănắ, “up”) +&lrm; <i>κλᾰ́σῐς</i> (klắsĭs, “breaking”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to counteract</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλῡ́ω</i> (ănălū́ō, “unsettle, unloose”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give back, requite, repay, give</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(figuratively,&#32;transitive)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to correspond to; to cause to correspond with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to make convertible</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to correspond with</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to give back insults, esp. tu quoque</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give in turn</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to give back a sound</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντῐ́</i> (antĭ́, “in return for”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ποδῐ́δωμι</i> (ăpodĭ́dōmi, “to give back”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;astrology,&#32;astronomy) opposite to the position known as <i>ἀποκατάστασις</i> (apokatástasis, “apocatastasis”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>ἀποκατάστασις</i> (apokatástasis, “return of a planet to a place occupied before”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to answer again, respond</li><li>to argue against</li><li>to correspond to</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “anti-”) +&lrm; <i>ἀ̆ποκρῑ́νω</i> (ăpokrī́nō, “to distinguish, choose, answer”) +&lrm; <i>-ομαι</i> (-omai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recompense, reward, repayment</li><li>retribution, revenge</li><li>reaction</li><li>turning back, change of direction</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) parallelism</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντᾰποδῐ́δωμῐ</i> (antăpodĭ́dōmĭ) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correspondence, relation</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντᾰποκρῐ́νομαι</i> (ăntăpokrĭ́nomai) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>antarctic (opposite to the Arctic)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντί</i> (antí, “ant-, against, opposed”) + <i>ἀρκτικός</i> (arktikós, “Arctic”), from <i>ἄρκτος</i> (árktos, “bear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>raise or build instead</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἐγείρω</i> (egeírō, “rise”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hope instead or in turn</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>ἐλπίζω</i> (elpízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to form counter-designs</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>ἐπιβουλεύω</i> (epibouleúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail against in turn</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>ἐπιπλέω</i> (epipléō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prone to return kindnesses</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντευεργετέω</i> (anteuergetéō, “return a kindness”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>anti-, against</li><li>like, reminiscent of</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ́</i> (ăntĭ́) used as a prefix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet face to face, encounter</li><li>to entreat, supplicate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντίος</i> (antíos) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to meet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to go in quest of</li><li>(<i>typically of deities</i>) to meet an offering</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in past tenses</i>) to have received, obtained</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to meet</li><li>(rare,&#32;with genitive) to compare oneself to; to match or measure oneself against</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>rare</i>) to come to aid</li></ol><li>(<i>later Epic</i>) to come to as a suppliant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic, derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂entí</i>; equivalent to <i>ἀντίος</i> (antíos) or <i>ἀντί</i> (antí) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō). Compare Old Armenian <i>անցանեմ</i> (ancʻanem).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet by chance (<i>especially in battle</i>)</li><li>to be present at</li><li>to partake of, have one's share of</li><li>to fall to one's lot</li><li>to entreat, supplicate</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>βολέω</i> (boléō), from the root of <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give in return, repay</li><li>to give for or instead of</li><li>to offer to change fortunes with</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comparison of arguments (for and against a thesis)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐδῐᾰτᾰ́σσομαι</i> (ăntĭdĭătắssomai, “to oppose”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>setting in opposition, contrasting; antithetical</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐτῐ́θημῐ</i> (ăntĭtĭ́thēmĭ, “set against”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit or lie over against</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>καθίζομαι</i> (kathízomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to substitute, replace</li><li>to set against, oppose</li><li>to bring back again, restore</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>), to be put in another's place, to reign instead of</li><li>to stand against, resist [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>καθίστημι</i> (kathístēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shin</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντί</i> (antí) +&lrm; <i>κνήμη</i> (knḗmē) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>face to face, over against</li><li>straight on</li><li>outright</li></ol><p>Assuming a compound with <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “opposite, against”) does not help much; the connection with <i>ἀντικρούω</i> (antikroúō, “to come into collision”), suggested by Kretschmer, is improbable, as are other attempts, like a derivation from <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>κάρα</i> (kára, “head”). Beekes and Cuypers argue that the <i>-υ</i> is short, but metrically lengthened in Homer. Beekes does not exclude a Pre-Greek etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gainsay, contradict</li><li>to answer, reply (<i>in opposition</i>)</li><li>to speak against, argue against</li><li>to allege (<i>in response</i>)</li><li>to deny, dispute, counterclaim</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contradiction, gainsaying</li><li>controversy, disputation, strife</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) opposing arguments, answering speeches</li></ol><p><i>ἀντίλογος</i> (antílogos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>given to contradiction, disputatious</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐλογέω</i> (ăntĭlogéō, “gainsay, contradict”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interchange of gender</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐμετᾰτᾰ́σσω</i> (ăntĭmetătắssō, “change the order of battle so as to meet the enemy”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) antimetabole</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντιμεταβάλλω</i> (antimetabállō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē) and <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>μεταβολή</i> (metabolḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) ambiguity in the law</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suffering instead</li><li>opposition, contrast</li><li>counteraction, antipathy</li><li>contrary affection</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντιπαθής</i> (antipathḗs) (from <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>πάθος</i> (páthos)) + <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to experience in turn or in return, experience the same</li><li>to have contrary qualities or effects</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>πάσχω</i> (páskhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in return for suffering</li><li>felt mutually</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) remedy for suffering</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>πάθος</i> (páthos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hostile demonstration; stubborn determination to resist</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐπᾰρᾰτᾰ́σσομαι</i> (ăntĭpărătắssomai, “to stand in array against”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare oneself in turn</li><li>to arm on both sides</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>παρασκευάζω</i> (paraskeuázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand in hostile array [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘against’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>παρατάσσομαι</i> (paratássomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in return, inversely, or reciprocally</li></ol><p><i>ἀντιπεπονθώς</i> (antipeponthṓs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to do in return, retaliate</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to exert oneself about, seek after, lay claim to</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to contend with</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wage war against</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>πολεμέω</i> (poleméō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>acting against, counteracting</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐπρᾱ́σσω</i> (ăntĭprā́ssō, “act against, seek to counteract”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the state or condition of standing opposite in pairs</li><li>(of letters,&#32;grammar) correspondence of the voiceless unaspirated (π, τ, κ), voiceless aspirated (φ, θ, χ), and voiced (β, δ, γ) stops</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντῐ́στοιχος</i> (antĭ́stoikhos, “ranged opposite in rows or pairs”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>ἀντῐ-</i> (antĭ-, “across”) +&lrm; <i>στείχω</i> (steíkhō, “march in line”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) to encamp over against</li></ol><p><i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>στρατοπεδεύω</i> (stratopedeúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turning about</li><li>(<i>drama</i>) antistrophe</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντιστρέφω</i> (antistréphō) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ) and/or <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>στροφή</i> (strophḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>counter-fortification</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐτειχῐ́ζω</i> (ăntĭteikhĭ́zō, “erect counter-fortifications”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to bail out bilgewater</li><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to draw water</li><li>to search, seek</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to drain, exhaust</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄντλος</i> (ántlos, “bilgewater”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A baling out of bilge-water</li><li>bilge-water, filth</li><li>reservoir</li><li>The hold of a ship</li><li>Food eaten by men at sea</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄντλος</i> (ántlos, “bilge-water”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who draws water</li><li>bailer, used for removing water which has entered a boat</li><li>ladle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντλέω</i> (antléō, “to bale out bilge-water”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) use or substitution of an epithet or patronymic for a proper noun, antonomasia</li><li>nomination of a successor by retiring official</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) pronoun, or the use of a pronoun</li><li>contrary denomination</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντονομᾰ́ζω</i> (ăntonomắzō, “to name instead, call by a new name”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) pronoun</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Without shoes, barefoot, unshod</li><li>Having the feet unprotected</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ὑποδέω</i> (hupodéō, “to bind [a sandal] underneath”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, perfective passive adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inequality, irregularity, unevenness, unsmoothness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνώμαλος</i> (anṓmalos) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprofitable, useless.</li><li>hurtful, prejudicial.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ὄφελος</i> (óphelos, “advantage, help”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not thought on</li><li>not understanding, foolish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”) + <i>νοέω</i> (noéō, “to perceive”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dissimilar, unlike</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios, “similar”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unholy, profane</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ὅσιος</i> (hósios, “pious”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause, effect, accomplish</li><li>to consume</li><li>to pass over</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;senh₂-</i> (“to seek, accomplish, work on, succeed”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>सन्</i> (san, “to gain, acquire, be successful”), as well as perhaps Hittite (sanh-, “to intend; to strive for; to seek”). See also <i>αὐθέντης</i> (authéntēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from pain, free from distress</li><li>causing no pain, harmless</li><li>allaying pain, easing pain, relieving pain, assuaging pain</li></ol><p><i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ὀδύνη</i> (odúnē) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally&#32;and&#32;figuratively</i>) uneven, irregular</li><li>(<i>of conditions and fortune</i>) precarious</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) inconsistent, capricious</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) of words which deviate from a general rule, anomalous</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ὁμᾰλός</i> (homălós, “even, level”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>anonymous, nameless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνυμα</i> (ónuma), Aeolic dialectal form of <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma, “name”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) contracted form of <i>ᾰ̓νᾱῑ̈́σσω</i> (ănāī̈́ssō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worth, value</li></ol><p>Abstract noun from <i>ἄξιος</i> (áxios, “worthy”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>counterbalancing, weighing as much as, of like value</li><li>worthy, fit</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) Acclamation confirming the election of a Christian bishop or patriarch, (<i>literally</i>) "Worthy!" &lsqb;from c. 4th century&rsqb;</li></ol><p>According to some scholars, from <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead, draw out or down”) +&lrm; <i>-τιος</i> (-tios) (perhaps via an unattested intermediate <i>ἄξις</i> (áxis, “weight”)), with sense development "draw down (in the scale)" > "weigh, import". Beekes expresses mild doubts about this, both in the semantics as well as no other morphological derivative of <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō) being in <i>-τι-</i> (-ti-), but tentatively accepts this etymology. For a semantic parallel, compare Latin <i>exāmen</i> (“tongue of a balance; examining”), from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axe-head</li><li>axe</li></ol><p>It has been proposed to derive from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʷs-ih₂-</i>, citing cognates such as Latin <i>ascia</i> and a number of Germanic words, such as Old English <i>æx</i> (English <i>axe</i>). However, it could also be a Semitic borrowing; compare Akkadian <i>𒍏𒄩𒍣𒅔</i> (ḫaṣṣinnum) and Aramaic <i>חצינא</i> (ḥaṣīnā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is thought fit, a requisite</li><li>that which is thought worthy</li><li>a self-evident principle, axiom</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀξιόω</i> (axióō, “to think or deem worthy”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>worthily</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ξῐος</i> (ắxĭos, “worthy”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, “-ly”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) thinking worthy or fit</li><li>reputation</li><li>demand, claim</li><li>opinion, principle, maxim</li><li>(<i>linguistics</i>) meaning (<i>of words</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀξιόω</i> (axióō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) propriety, dignity</li></ol><p><i>ἀξιοπρεπής</i> (axioprepḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia) (<i>-ία</i> (-ía)).</p><p>The word is not attested in classical Greek, but it is included in Lampe as part of the lexicon of the language of Late Antiquity. LBG (Trapp 2001) in addition cites the 9th-century <i>Life of Constantine</i> edited by Guidi (1907:319).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proper, becoming</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄξιος</i> (áxios, “worthy”) +&lrm; <i>πρέπω</i> (prépō, “to be appropriate for”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Dignified, honorable; high in rank.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>literary criticism</i>) Dignified.</li><li>Concerned with dignities.</li></ol><li>Supplicatory.</li><li>Employing logical propositions; self-evident.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀξίωμα</i> (axíōma, “honor, worth, self-evident principle”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “of or pertaining to”), from <i>ἀξιόω</i> (axióō, “to think or deem worthy”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>worthy of mention: remarkable, noteworthy, of note, important</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄξιος</i> (áxios, “worthy”) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos, “speech”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>trustworthy</li></ol><p><i>ἄξιος</i> (áxios) +&lrm; <i>πείθομαι</i> (peíthomai, “to trust”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noteworthy, considerable</li><li>serviceable, sufficient</li><li>trustworthy, bona fide, reliable</li><li>able to, competent to</li><li>worthy of, deserving of</li></ol><p><i>ἄξιος</i> (áxios) +&lrm; <i>χρέος</i> (khréos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think or deem worthy</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to think [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>] worthy, either in a good or bad sense [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to esteem, honor</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to value at a certain price</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to think [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>] worthy [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do or be’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to think fit, expect, require, demand that; compare Latin <i>postulō</i></li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to think one has a right [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to think fit, expect, consent, resolve, deign, dare [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to think, deem, hold</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;transitive) to make a claim</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to hold an opinion</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) to lay down, hold, maintain</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἄξιος</i> (áxios, “worthy”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, factitive verb&ndash;forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́ξῐος</i> (ắxĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓́ξῐος</i> (ắxĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>ἀσύμφορος</i> (asúmphoros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of singing</li><li>song, ode</li><li>theme of a song</li><li>legend; tale; story</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) spell; incantation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awoidā́</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ᾰ̓είδω</i> (ăeídō, “to sing”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>musical</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singer, minstrel, bard</li><li>enchanter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awoidós</i>, formed from <i>ᾄδειν</i> (āídein, sing) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal adjective and noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἄοκνος</i> (áoknos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all together</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;həwolnḗs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm̥wolnḗs</i> from <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to push, press”). Compare <i>ᾱ̔λής</i> (hālḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the lower extremities of the windpipe</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) aorta, the artery which proceeds from the left ventricle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the arteries</li></ol></ol><li>a knapsack that hangs from the shoulders</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wortéh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂wer-</i> (whence <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō, “to raise, lift, hang”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strap, swordbelt</li><li>knapsack strap</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓είρω</i> (ăeírō, “to lift, raise up; to be suspended, hang”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, “-er, -or”, agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hung up, suspended</li></ol><p>Aorist passive participle of an unattested <i>&#42;ἀορτέω</i> (&#42;aortéō), from <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō); compare <i>ἀορτή</i> (aortḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a denominative formed from <i>&#42;ἀοσσος</i> (&#42;aossos), itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm̥-sokʷ-yo</i>, from <i>&#42;sokʷyo-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sokʷh₂ṓy</i>, whence also Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;sákʰā</i>. The loss of the laryngeal may have occurred due to the effects of Pinault's law. The linguist Anthony Yates connects the thematicization of the Ancient Greek word to the thematicized terms in Latin <i>socius</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sagjaz</i>. However, the linguist Donald Ringe argues that the Germanic and Greek forms cannot have been directly inherited from the same pre-form, as Greek and Germanic "share no common ancestor that Indo-Iranian does not also share."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓πό</i> (ăpó)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strangle, throttle</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to hang oneself, to be hanged, be ready to choke</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from, off, away”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγχω</i> (ánkhō, “strangle”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead away</li><li>to carry off, abduct, kidnap</li><li>to retire, withdraw, march away</li><li>to bring back, bring home</li><li>to pay back</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>want of sensation, impassibility</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) insensibility, apathy</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>among the Stoics</i>) calmness, dispassionateness</li></ol><li>absence of suffering or pain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπαθής</i> (apathḗs, “without feeling or suffering”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from afar</li><li>far off</li><li>aside, apart</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>without</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἄνευ</i> (áneu) +&lrm; <i>-θε</i> (-the).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀπάνευθε</i> (apáneuthe) with movable nu</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀπάνευθε</i> (apáneuthe) with movable nu</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escort</li><li>meeting in argument</li><li>reply</li><li>steadfastness in face of opposition</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πᾰντᾰ́ω</i> (ăpăntắō, “to meet”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deceit; fraud</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Beekes rejects Pederson's derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pent-</i> (“to pass; path”) and tentatively derives the word from Pre-Greek, in view of semantically similar words like <i>ἠπεροπεύς</i> (ēperopeús, “cheat, deceiver”) and <i>ἀπαφίσκω</i> (apaphískō, “to cheat, beguile”), which appear related to <i>ἀπάτη</i> (apátē) but which are difficult to reconcile with Indo-European morphological processes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of deities</i>) not having a father; without a father</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) fatherless; orphaned</li><li>disowned or disinherited by one's father</li><li>without someone as father [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>having an unknown father</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;əpatōr</i>. Equivalent to <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “-less”) +&lrm; <i>-πάτωρ</i> (-pátōr, “father”, stem used in compounds). Compare <i>πατήρ</i> (patḗr, “father”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>immeasurably far</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) immeasurable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>πέλεθρον</i> (pélethron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) immeasurable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>πέλεθρον</i> (pélethron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>opposite</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπό</i> (apó) + <i>ἔναντι</i> (énanti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to go away from, depart from</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(when used with <i>εἰς</i> (eis), departure at one place and arrival at another is implied)</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>)</li><li>(<i>with participle</i>)</li><li>to depart from life</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “to come, go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stripped of leaves, aphyllous, leafless</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, <i>alpha privativum</i>) +&lrm; <i>πέτᾰλον</i> (pétălon, “leaf”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming two-termination second-declension compound adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic spelling of <i>ᾱ̓πέτᾰλος</i> (āpétălos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to keep off or away from</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to keep oneself off or away from</li><li>(active voice,&#32;intransitive) to be away from, to be far from [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from something or someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to receive in full, to have all of</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “away from”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to keep”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhurt, unharmed, free from distress, free from harm</li><li>not harmful, innocuous</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>πῆμα</i> (pêma) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>four-wheel wagon, drawn by mules</li><li>any car or chariot, any conveyance</li></ol><p>A Northwest Semitic loanword, attested as Ugaritic <i>𐎀𐎔𐎐</i> (ảpn, “wheel of a chariot”) and Hebrew <i>אוֹפַן</i> (ʾōp̄án, “wheel of a chariot”). The clipped variant has perhaps been connected vulgarly with <i>πήνη</i> (pḗnē, “spool”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>beyond belief</li><li>distrustfully, suspiciously</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄπιστος</i> (ápistos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(of a messenger,&#32;transitive,&#32;ditransitive) to bring tidings, report, announce [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of a speaker or writer,&#32;transitive) to report, relate, narrate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to recite, declaim</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to explain, interpret</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγγέλλω</i> (angéllō, “to announce, report”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἀπαγχονίζω</i> (apankhonízō, “hang by a noose, strangle”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀγχόνη</i> (ankhónē, “noose”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō, verb-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἀπαγχονίζω</i> (apankhonízō, “hang by a noose, strangle”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀγχόνη</i> (ankhónē, “noose”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verb-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang by a noose, strangle</li><li>to release form a noose</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away, form, off”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγχονίζω</i> (ankhonízō, “strangle”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without sensation or suffering</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>without experience of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>unpunished</li></ol><li>without passion or feeling, unemotional, insensible, apathetic</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of things</i>) not liable to change, impassive</li></ol><li>exciting no feeling, making no impression, unexciting</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πάθος</i> (páthos, “feeling, suffering”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to demand back, demand in payment</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to require</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of persons,&#32;transitive) to have something demanded of one</li><li>(of persons,&#32;intransitive) to yield to a request</li><li>(of things,&#32;intransitive) to be demanded</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “from, back”) +&lrm; <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō, “to ask, beg”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to created, finish, develop in a perfect or high manner</li><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to finish, complete, make perfect</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀκρῑβόω</i> (akrībóō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to heal thoroughly, completely</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλθαίνω</i> (althaínō) +&lrm; <i>-ομαι</i> (-omai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set free, release, deliver</li><li>to depart</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπαλλαγή</i> (apallagḗ) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō) and/or <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλλάσσω</i> (allássō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deliverance, release, relief, riddance</li><li>divorce</li><li>escape, departure</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) storage room, depot</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλλαγή</i> (allagḗ, “change”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to answer, reply to</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀμείβομαι</i> (ameíbomai), middle of <i>ἀμείβω</i> (ameíbō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send away</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to get up and go</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀνίστημι</i> (anístēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to meet [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀντάω</i> (antáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deem unworthy, disown, banish</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀξιόω</i> (axióō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without preparation, unprepared</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>παρασκευή</i> (paraskeuḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be disagreeable [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to’</small>]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to show displeasure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀρέσκω</i> (aréskō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleavers (<i>Galium aparine</i>)</li></ol><p>Given the suffix <i>-ῑ́ν-</i>, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of gods or persons</i>) not to be moved by prayer, inexorable, implacable</li><li>(<i>of punishments, etc.</i>) not to be averted by prayers, inevitable, unmerciful, unpardonable</li><li>not to be evaded, indispensable, irresistible</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “a-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰραιτέομαι</i> (păraitéomai, “to beg, entreat”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unprepared</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>παρασκευάζω</i> (paraskeuázō, “I prepare”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny utterly</li><li>to reject</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρνέομαι</i> (arnéomai, “to loosen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave one no leisure, keep one busy or occupied or engrossed</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “away, off”) +&lrm; <i>ἀσχολέω</i> (askholéō, “to engage, occupy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deceive</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πᾰ́τη</i> (ăpắtē, “deceit, fraud”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἀπατήλιος</i> (apatḗlios, “guileful, wily”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπάτη</i> (apátē) +&lrm; <i>-λός</i> (-lós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wrest (<i>something</i>) from (<i>someone</i>), rob (<i>someone</i>) of (<i>something</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) + the root of <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wer-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἄπειρος</i> (ápeiros, “boundless, infinite”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without trial or experience, not used to, ignorant of, unacquainted with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>absolute</i>) inexperienced, ignorant</li></ol><li>boundless, unlimited, infinite</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in Tragedy,&#32;often of garments) in which one is entangled past escape, endless, without outlet</li><li>endless, circular</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἤπειρος</i> (ḗpeiros)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πεῖρα</i> (peîra, “trial, attempt”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Compare with Latin <i>peritus</i>.</p><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πεῖραρ</i> (peîrar), <i>πέρας</i> (péras, “end, limit”) + <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p><p>See <i>ἤπειρος</i> (ḗpeiros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disobey</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>πειθ-</i> (peith-, “obey”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unreasonably</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπεικώς</i> (apeikṓs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs); also analyzable as <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>εἰκότως</i> (eikótōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to threaten</li></ol><p><i>ἀπειλή</i> (apeilḗ) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) boastful promises, boasts</li><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) threat, menace, threatening condition</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂pelH-</i> (“to speak publicly”), and related via this root to Latvian <i>pel̂t</i> (“to revile”). Assuming <i>s-</i>mobile, said root has further been compared with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spellą</i> (“tale, story”) and Old Armenian <i>առասպել</i> (aṙaspel, “myth, legend”), suggesting an equivalence to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pel-</i> (“to speak publicly”). More dubious is the relation to Tocharian A <i>pällāntär</i> and Tocharian B <i>pällātär</i> (“to praise”), via a supposed nasal present <i>&#42;(s)pelnH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>intimidating, threatening, menacing</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓πειλή</i> (ăpeilḗ) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boundless, immense</li><li>countless</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ᾰ̓περέσῐος</i> (&#42;ăperésĭos) by metrical lengthening, from the root <i>περ-</i> (per-) of <i>πέρᾰς</i> (pérăs).</p><p>Or from a verbal adjective base α-περ-ετος not well attested, and the suffix -ιος. (LOS SUFIJOS Y -jo- EN GRIEGO:UNA APROXIMACIÓN COMPARATIVA'Francisco J. Martínez GarcíaJ. W. Goethe-Universitdt, Frankfurt am Main page 315.)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of skill, inexperience, ignorance</li><li>infinity</li><li>eternity</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́πειρος</i> (ắpeiros, “unexperienced”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾱ</i> (-ĭā)</p><p><i>ᾰ̓́πειρος</i> (ắpeiros, “unlimited, infinite”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾱ</i> (-ĭā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infinitely perfect</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ἄπειρος</i> (ápeiros, “infinite”) +&lrm; <i>τέλειος</i> (téleios, “finished, perfect”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἄπειρος</i> (ápeiros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to emancipate, set free</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓πελεύθερος</i> (ăpeleútheros, “restored to freedom”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedman</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “apo-, away from”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλεύθερος</i> (eleútheros, “free”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) black poplar</li></ol><p>Reflecting a root <i>&#42;h₂pel-</i>, possibly also in Latin <i>pōpulus</i> (“poplar”). Alternatively from a Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;ašpella</i>, whence Albanian <i>shpel</i> (“black poplar”), with assimilation of /-sp-/ to /-(p)p-/, characteristic of Laconian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vomit, disgorge</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἐρᾱ́ω</i> (erā́ō, “to pour forth, vomit”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unregarding, reckless [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>περίοπτος</i> (períoptos), from the future stem of <i>περιοράω</i> (perioráō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἀπερείσια</i> (apereísia)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀπειρέσιος</i> (apeirésios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) Not inquired into, unknown</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) Not inquiring, ignorant</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πυνθάνομαι</i> (punthánomai, “to inquire, find out”, in the <i>e</i>-grade) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be hated, incur hatred</li><li>to be or become hateful to</li><li>to cause hatred</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχθος</i> (ékhthos, “hatred”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish away, wish it may not happen</li><li>to reject, despise</li><li>to avert by prayer</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓π-</i> +&lrm; <i>εὔχομαι</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak out, tell out, declare</li><li>to deny, refuse</li><li>to forbid</li><li>to renounce, disown, give up</li><li>to give up, be worn out, fail</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ungentle, harsh, rough, hard</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) + the root <i>-η(ν)-</i> (-ē(n)-) found in <i>πρᾱνής</i> (prānḗs, “prone”) and <i>ἐνηής</i> (enēḗs, “gentle, amiable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἀπειθέω</i> (apeithéō, “to disobey”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disbelieve, doubt</li><li>to distrust, mistrust</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to betray a trust, be unfaithful</li></ol><p><i>ἄπιστος</i> (ápistos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unbelief, distrust, lack of confidence, doubt</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́πῐστος</i> (ắpĭstos, “mistrustful; unreliable”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ᾰ̓πῐστῐ́ᾱ</i> (ăpĭstĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>proscribed form of <i>ἀπῐστέω</i> (apĭstéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἄπιστος</i> (ápistos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to become a fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from, of”) +&lrm; <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insatiate desire, greediness (of something: [<i>with</i> genitive])</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄπληστος</i> (áplēstos, “insatiable, greedy”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix), ultimately from <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi, “to fill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Indicating movement: away, from, off</li><li>Indicating breaking one part from another: un-, asunder, apart, off</li><li>Indicating ending or finishing, or almost like the negative <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”), especially in adjectives</li><li>back, again</li><li>Simply emphasizing the meaning of the verb</li><li>because of, owing to</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πό</i> (ăpó), used as a prefix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be gone, be absent</li><li>to stay away from, abandon</li><li>to be dead</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>οἴχομαι</i> (oíkhomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to strip off, remove, deprive of</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>αἴνυμαι</i> (aínumai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) ladder for disembarking, gangway</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποβαίνω</i> (apobaínō, “to disembark”) +&lrm; <i>‎-θρον</i> (‎-thron, “instrumental suffix”) +&lrm; <i>-α</i> (-a, “feminine ending suffix”). By surface analysis, <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away from”) +&lrm; <i>βάθρ(ον)</i> (báthr(on), “step, pedestal”) +&lrm; <i>-α</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away, discard</li><li>to lose</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to march out</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;medicine,&#32;middle voice) to miscarry (involuntarily abort a fetus)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away, depart, leave</li><li>to alight (<i>from a chariot</i>)</li><li>to disembark (<i>from a ship</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ex-king</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “ceasing from”, “leaving off”) +&lrm; <i>βᾰσῐλεύς</i> (băsĭleús, “king”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be away from; to have no part in [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to be taken away</li><li>to be away; to be absent</li><li>to depart from; to die out of</li><li>to be dead</li><li>to be lost</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give up or back, restore, return, to render what is due, pay</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to assign</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to refer to one, as belonging to his department</li></ol><li>to return, render, yield</li><li>to concede, allow, to suffer or allow a person to do</li><li>(like <i>ἀποδείκνυμι</i> (apodeíknumi)) to render or make</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to exhibit, display</li></ol><li>to deliver over, give up</li><li>to deliver</li><li>to bring to a conclusion, wind it up</li><li>to render, give an account</li><li>to render, give an account, to define, interpret</li><li>to attach or append, make dependent upon</li><li>to affirm</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to increase</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to return, recur</li><li>(<i>in rhetoric and grammar</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to be construed with, refer to</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to give away of one's own will, to sell</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́δωμῐ</i> (dĭ́dōmĭ, “to give”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>division (part of a whole)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποδατέομαι</i> (apodatéomai) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós); also analyzable as <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>δασμός</i> (dasmós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demonstrate, point out, show</li><li>to proclaim, appoint</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>δείκνῡμῐ</i> (deíknūmĭ, “to point out, show”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓ποδείκνῡμῐ</i> (ăpodeíknūmĭ)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-) +&lrm; <i>δεικνῠ́ω</i> (deiknŭ́ō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affording proof, demonstrative</li><li>explanatory</li><li>scientifically minded, exact</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter plural) sights, attractions</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποδείκνυμι</i> (apodeíknumi) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who goes abroad, expat</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποδημέω</i> (apodēméō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dissipate, dissolve</li><li>to confute utterly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from, away, off”) +&lrm; <i>διαλύω</i> (dialúō, “dissolve, undo”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to run away, escape</li><li>to flee, shun <i>(with accusative)</i></li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>διδράσκω</i> (didráskō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>receiving back</li><li>acceptance</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποδέχομαι</i> (apodékhomai) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strip off (something from someone)</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>δύω</i> (dúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live off, make a living from</li><li>to live out (<i>one’s life</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ζῶ</i> (zô)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apotheosis, deification</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποθεόω</i> (apotheóō, “deify”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repository, storehouse, warehouse</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποτίθημι</i> (apotíthēmi, “to put away”), from <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to put”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>completing (e.g. a thought)</li><li>(<i>of verbs</i>) deponent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποτῐ́θημῐ</i> (apotĭ́thēmĭ, “put away, stow away”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deify</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god, deity”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to squeeze out</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-) +&lrm; <i>θλῑ́βω</i> (thlī́bō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Strengthened form of <i>θνῄσκω</i> (thnēískō), but essentially synonymous</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to die</li><li>to be killed, put to death, slain: serves as passive of <i>ἀποκτείνω</i> (apokteínō)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>θνῄσκω</i> (thnēískō, “to die”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>colony</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ποικος</i> (ắpoikos, “colonist”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send away from home</li><li>to colonize (begin a new colony)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be settled in a far land, to emigrate</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκίζω</i> (oikízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncovering, revelation, disclosure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποκαλύπτω</i> (apokalúptō, “to disclose, reveal”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to restore, reestablish, reinstate, return to a prior condition</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to restore to health, cure</li><li>(military,&#32;of troop units) to restore formation</li></ol><li>to hand over, deliver, pay dues</li><li>(astrology,&#32;of planets) to complete a revolution</li><li>(<i>of islands</i>) to be disconnected from the mainland</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, back again) +&lrm; <i>καθίστημι</i> (kathístēmi, to set, place, constitute, appoint).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>revealing, illuminating, uncovering</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποκαλύπτω</i> (apokalúptō) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reveal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>καλύπτω</i> (kalúptō, “to cover”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restoration, re-establishment</li><li>reversal, return</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>astrology</i>) return of the stars to the same position as the prior year</li></ol><li>recovery</li><li>(Christianity,&#32;theology) restoration of all things to harmony and the intended order outlined by God</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποκαθίστημι</i> (apokathístēmi) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>returning, bringing back</li><li>of or pertaining to restitution, restoral</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποκατάστασις</i> (apokatástasis) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to decapitate, behead</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away, off”) +&lrm; <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be careless, negligent, remiss</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποκηδής</i> (apokēdḗs) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>negligent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>κῆδος</i> (kêdos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shut off from or out of, debar</li><li>to shut out or exclude from [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> <b>ἀπό</b> (apó, + genitive)]</li><li>to shut up, bar, close</li><li>to intercept</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>κλείω</i> (kleíō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nonce word</i>) pumpkinification (act of turning into a pumpkin, in jocular contrast with deification)</li></ol><p>A word invented for use in the title of the work <i>Apocolocyntosis (divi) Claudii</i>, a satire by Seneca on the <i>apotheosis</i> of the Roman emperor Claudius. From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>κολοκυνθίς</i> (kolokunthís, “gourd”) +&lrm; <i>-ωσις</i> (-ōsis, verbal noun suffix), by analogy with <i>ἀποθέωσις</i> (apothéōsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) amputation</li><li>(grammar,&#32;phonetics) apocope</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ποκόπτω</i> (ăpokóptō, “cut away”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to separate, set apart</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to distinguish</li></ol><li>to choose</li><li>to reject on inquiry</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to answer, give answer, reply</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to answer charges, defend oneself</li><li>to meet events, circumstances</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be given as an answer (<i>+ dative of agent</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from, away from”) +&lrm; <i>κρίνω</i> (krínō, “to separate, distinguish, judge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, keep hidden from</li><li>to hide from sight, keep hidden, conceal</li><li>to obscure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from, off”) +&lrm; <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, slay</li><li>to condemn, put to death (<i>judicial</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>κτείνω</i> (kteínō, “to kill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hinder or prevent [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from’</small>]</li><li>to forbid [<i>with</i> <b>μὴ</b> (mḕ) <i>and</i> infinitive]</li><li>to keep off, hinder</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>κωλύω</i> (kōlúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, amputate</li><li>to beat the chest in mourning, to mourn</li><li>(<i>speech</i>) to cut off</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “off”) +&lrm; <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) fetus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποκυέω</i> (apokuéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma), also interpretable as <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>κύημα</i> (kúēma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick out from, to choose</li><li>(<i>later sense</i>) decline, refuse</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπό-</i> (apó-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to choose, to say”) (λέγω, sense: 'choose')..</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave off, desist [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cease, desist</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>λήγω</i> (lḗgō, “leave off, cease”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive</li><li>to take back, recover</li><li>to take aside</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to enjoy [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to profit</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;lāwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leh₂w-</i> (“profit, gain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave (behind), desert</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to remain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>λείπω</i> (leípō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak in defense, defend oneself [<i>with</i> <b>περί</b> (perí, + genitive) <small>‘about’</small> <i>or</i> <b>πρός</b> (prós, + accusative) <small>‘in answer to’</small> <i>or</i> <b>ὑπέρ</b> (hupér, + genitive) <small>‘on behalf of’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to defend oneself against, explain, excuse</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) to answer</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to recompense</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to correspond to</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπόλογος</i> (apólogos) +&lrm; <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a speech made in defense of something; apology</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “back”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “speech”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun&ndash;forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suitable for defense</li><li>apologetic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπολογέομαι</i> (apologéomai) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) in an apologetic manner, in defence</li></ol><p>Medieval adverb from ancient adjective <i>ἀπολογητικός</i> (apologētikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>ἀπολογέομαι</i> (apologéomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to release on payment of ransom, restore for a ransom</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “off”) +&lrm; <i>λυτρόω</i> (lutróō, “to ransom”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loose from</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to set free from, release or relieve from</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>law</i>) to acquit</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>sole use in the Iliad</i>) to let go free on receipt of ransom</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to let go, let alone, leave</li></ol><li>to discharge or disband an army, dismiss</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to divorce a wife</li><li>to do away with, remove</li><li>to discharge or pay a debt</li><li>to dismiss a charge</li></ol><li>to sell</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to redeem</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to do away with calumnies against one</li><li>to acquit of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to release from</li></ol><li>to depart</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be released, let off</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be separated, get clear, part</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to depart</li></ol><li>(<i>of a child</i>) to be brought forth</li><li>to be annulled</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “to loosen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fight from the walls</li><li>to fight desperately</li><li>(<i>tansitive</i>) to fight off, decline</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χομαι</i> (mắkhomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a memorial</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπομνημονεύω</i> (apomnēmoneúō, “relate from memory, recount, remember”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go or come back or home, return</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>νέομαι</i> (néomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>laziness, freedom from toil</li><li>(<i>Epicurean&#32;philosophy</i>) freedom from pain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄπονος</i> (áponos, “without toil, painless”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;anatomy) to become tendinous</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;anatomy) to become a nerve</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ̆πο-</i> (ăpo-, “away, off”) +&lrm; <i>νεῦρον</i> (neûron, “sinew, tendon”) +&lrm; <i>-ομαι</i> (-omai, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) The end of the muscle, where it becomes tendinous.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ̆πονευρόομαι</i> (ăponeuróomai, “to become tendinous”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return, go home, come home</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>νοστέω</i> (nostéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>far apart</li><li>aloof</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>far away from</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>νόσφι</i> (nósphi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀπονόσφι</i> (aponósphi) with movable nu</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀπονόσφι</i> (aponósphi) with movable nu</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send off or away, dismiss, dispatch</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to get rid of</li><li>to send back</li><li>to export</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to avert by sacrifice; to banish, exorcise (<i>a disease</i>)</li><li>to divorce</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away, back”) +&lrm; <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō, “send”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break wind</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>πέρδομαι</i> (pérdomai, “fart”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail away</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike to earth</li><li>to cripple with a stroke, disable</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to lose one's senses, become dizzy, become dumbfounded</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to drive back, repulse</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stupefied, confused</li><li>apoplectic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποπλήσσω</i> (apoplḗssō, “I strike with a stroke”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós), from <i>ἀπό</i> (apó, “of, from”) + <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “I strike”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Madness.</li><li>(medicine,&#32;of body) Apoplexy, paralysis.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ̆ποπλήσσω</i> (ăpoplḗssō, “to cripple by a stroke, to disable in body or mind”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit out, spit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to abominate, spurn, disown</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “out, away from”) +&lrm; <i>πτῡ́ω</i> (ptū́ō, “spit out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be at a loss</li><li>to be in need of</li></ol><p><i>ἄπορος</i> (áporos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of places</i>) a difficulty in passage</li><li>difficulty, impossibility</li><li>embarrassment, doubt, uncertainty, anxiety</li><li>need, poverty</li><li>puzzle</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́πορος</i> (ắporos, “impassable, difficult”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ᾰ̓πορῐ́ᾱ</i> (ăporĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dart away</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ὀρούω</i> (oroúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw away, put away</li><li>to exile, cast out</li><li>to disown, renounce</li><li>to throw aside, set at naught</li><li>to shoot forth (<i>bold, keen words</i>) [<i>with</i> <b>ἔς</b> (és, + accusative) <small>‘at’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>ῥίπτω</i> (rhíptō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἄπορος</i> (áporos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extinguish, quench</li><li>to be extinguished, go out, vanish, die, decease</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “to, toward”) +&lrm; <i>σβέννῡμι</i> (sbénnūmi, “to quench, to be quenched”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) becoming silent</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) aposiopesis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποσιωπάω</i> (aposiōpáō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis) and <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>σιώπησις</i> (siṓpēsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>removal, riddance, e.g. an assassination, etc.</li><li>baggage, household stuff</li><li>ordure, filth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “apo-, away”) +&lrm; <i>σκεῦος</i> (skeûos, “vessel, implement”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deserter, rebel</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) apostate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀφίστημι</i> (aphístēmi) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send off, send away from</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to send away, banish</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to go away, depart, set out</li></ol><li>to send off, dispatch, on some mission or service</li><li>to put off, doff</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go back, retire</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “to send”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defection, revolt, rebellion</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) apostasy</li><li>departure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀφίστημι</i> (aphístēmi, “I withdraw, revolt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to stand aloof from, to be absent</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ἀπόστασις</i> (apóstasis) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rob, despoil, bereave or defraud [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative <small>‘of something’</small>]</li><li>to defraud, cheat</li><li>to detach, withdraw oneself</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>στερέω</i> (steréō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to know from memory, repeat by heart</li></ol><p>From the phrase <i>ᾰ̓πὸ στήθους</i> (ăpò stḗthous, “by heart”) (<i>ᾰ̓πό</i> (“from”) the <i>στῆθος</i> (“breast”)) + <i>-ῐ́ζω</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commission, mission</li><li>apostleship</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποστέλλω</i> (apostéllō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who dispatches, dispatcher</li><li>(<i>military</i>) An Athenian magistrate who outfitted a squadron for service</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πόστολος</i> (ăpóstolos) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to departure</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) apostolic, pertaining to the apostles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποστολή</i> (apostolḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p><p>From <i>ἀπόστολος</i> (apóstolos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn back, put to flight</li><li>to twist back, bend back</li><li>to avert</li><li>to bring back, recall</li><li>to turn away (from), divert</li><li>to put away again</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to pervert</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to give back, return</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escape, refuge, excuse</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποστρέφω</i> (apostréphō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act off hand or at random</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>σχεδιάζω</i> (skhediázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut off, sever</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to melt away from</li><li>to melt away a part of (something)</li><li>to reduce</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>τήκω</i> (tḗkō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put away, stow away</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to lay aside (<i>e.g. arms, clothes</i>)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to put aside, disregard</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to avoid, escape</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to put off, defer</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay back, repay, return</li><li>to pay for</li><li>to make atonement</li><li>to pay in full</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to exact or require a penalty from</li><li>to avenge oneself on, punish</li><li>to take vengeance for, punish (<i>a misdeed etc.</i>)</li><li>to take vengeance</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>τίνω</i> (tínō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(act of) walling off, blockading (<i>a town etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποτειχίζω</i> (apoteikhízō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wall off</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to fortify</li><li>to blockade</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to build a party wall</li></ol><li>to raise fortifications</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “off”) +&lrm; <i>τειχίζω</i> (teikhízō, “build a wall”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to finish (something), to accomplish, to form fully</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπό-</i> (apó-) +&lrm; <i>τελέω</i> (teléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>split or hewn piece of wood</li><li>javelin used in athletic games</li><li>fiery dart</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπότομος</i> (apótomos, “cut off”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to (cause to) turn away or turn back; turn aside; ward off; keep away</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to (cause to) divert; deter or dissuade; stave off, avert; prevent, halt from happening</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice,&#32;reflexive) to turn away from; avert one's gaze; cease to pay heed</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice,&#32;reflexive) to turn a deaf ear, deflect, shun, avoid</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice,&#32;reflexive) to stop, cease, desist from doing something</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice,&#32;intransitive) to turn back, return; desert from a party</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away, from, off”) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō, “turn”) from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;trep-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>deterrence, dissuasion</li><li>prevention</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποτρέπω</i> (apotrépō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>averting evil</li><li>that ought to be averted, ill-omened</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποτροπή</i> (apotropḗ) +&lrm; <i>-αιος</i> (-aios) and/or <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>τροπαῖος</i> (tropaîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fail in hitting or gaining, lose</li><li>to miss one's object, fail</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>absence</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>οὐσίᾱ</i> (ousíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show forth, display, produce</li><li>to make known, declare</li><li>to give evidence of</li><li>to show by reasoning, represent as</li><li>to give an account of</li><li>to render or make (<i>a certain way</i>)</li><li>to appoint to (<i>an office</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>negative</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπόφημι</i> (apóphēmi) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to flee from, escape</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to avoid</li><li>(law,&#32;perfect) to get clear off, be acquitted</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a woman in child-birth</i>) to bring to birth</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away from”) +&lrm; <i>φεύγω</i> (pheúgō, “to flee, run off”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak one's opinion plainly, boldly declare</li><li>to utter an apophthegm</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>φθέγγομαι</i> (phthéngomai, “to speak loudly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>empty, vain, idle, worthless</li><li>barren</li><li>monstrous, hybrid <i>in the phrase</i> by Euripides, <i>Fragment</i> 966</li></ol><p>Not related to <i>ὄφελος</i> (óphelos, “use”). According to Beekes, probably connected to <i>ἀπαφεῖν</i> (apapheîn) aorist b of verb ἀπαφίσκω (apaphískō, “I deceive”) (itself probably from Pre-Greek/substrate), in which case the word originally meant "deceiving".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distance</li><li>abstinence</li><li>receipt</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπέχω</i> (apékhō, “to stay away”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to suffice, be sufficient, be enough</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be contented [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to use to the full [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to abuse, misuse [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;transitive) to use up, destroy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>χράω</i> (khráō, “to declare”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enough, sufficiently</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποχρῶν</i> (apokhrôn) (contracted present participle of <i>ἀποχράω</i> (apokhráō)) + <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away from, leave</li><li>to withdraw, retreat</li><li>to turn out</li><li>to excrete</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “away from”) +&lrm; <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō, “to withdraw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free from business, keeping clear of politics, enjoying an easy quiet life</li><li>not troublesome or painful, without trouble</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>πρᾶγμα</i> (prâgma) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom from politics and business</li><li>love of a quiet life, love of ease, supineness</li><li>noninterventionism, isolationism (<i>of states</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπράγμων</i> (aprágmōn) +&lrm; <i>-σύνη</i> (-súnē), from the root of <i>πράσσω</i> (prássō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nonaction</li><li>leisure, rest from business</li><li>lack of success; futility</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-, in-, non-”) +&lrm; <i>πρᾶξῐς</i> (prâxĭs, “doing, action”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of readiness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπρόθῡμος</i> (apróthūmos, “unready, backward”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unexpected</li><li>unaware</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) and <i>προσδοκέω</i> (prosdokéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without disguise</li><li>without evasion</li><li>honestly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπροφάσιστος</i> (aprophásistos, “unhesitating”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἄπφα</i> (áppha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to thrust away, push back, cast away</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to drive away from oneself, expel, banish</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to reject, decline, refuse to accept</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ὠθέω</i> (ōthéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἀπωλόμεθα</i> (apōlómetha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from, away from</li><li>because of, as a result of</li><li>in the name of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;apó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂epó</i> (“off, away”).</p><p>Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀢</i> (a-pu), Sanskrit <i>अप</i> (ápa, “away, off”), Latin <i>ab</i> (“from”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;po</i>, and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ab</i> (English <i>of</i>; see also <i>after</i>), Albanian <i>pa</i> (“without”).</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of separation or cause.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stepping off, disembarking, landing</li><li>landing place</li><li>way off</li></ol><p><i>ἀποβαίνω</i> (apobaínō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) apogee, greatest distance from the Earth</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>σημεῖον ἀπόγειον</i> (sēmeîon apógeion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far from the Earth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπό</i> (apó, “away”) +&lrm; <i>γῆ</i> (gê, “Earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>showing forth, making known, publication</li><li>proof</li><li>demonstration, deduction</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποδείκνῡμι</i> (apodeíknūmi, “to show, display”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giving back, restitution, return</li><li>payment</li><li>assignment, attribution</li><li>rendering by way of definition</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) apodosis (consequential clause in a conditional sentence)</li><li>interpretation, explanation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποδίδωμι</i> (apodídōmi) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀢𐀉𐀯</i> (a-pu-du-si).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>escape, evasion</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποδῐδρᾱ́σκω</i> (apodĭdrā́skō, “I escape from”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), from <i>ἀπό</i> (apó, “from”) + <i>διδράσκω</i> (didráskō, “I run away, escape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>decoction</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποζέω</i> (apozéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma) and <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ζέμα</i> (zéma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far from the gods, godless</li></ol><p><i>ἀπό</i> (apó, “from, away from”) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separating</li><li>answer</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) oral command, instruction</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποκρῑ́νω</i> (apokrī́nō, “to separate”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs) and <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “decision”) +&lrm; <i>κρίσις</i> (krísis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hidden, concealed, secret, unknown</li><li>obscure, recondite</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπό</i> (apó, “from”) + <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “to hide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dogbane (<i>Cionura erecta</i>)</li><li>poisoned cake for dogs</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>κύων</i> (kúōn, “dog”); literally "dog away". So called because the plant is toxic to dogs.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to completely destroy; to kill; to slay</li><li>to lose utterly</li></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice&#32;and&#32;perfect):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to perish; to die</li><li>to be lost; to slip away; to vanish</li></ol></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>ὄλλῡμῐ</i> (óllūmĭ, “to destroy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>story, tale</li><li>fable, apologue, allegory</li><li>account rendered</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, word, speech).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strickle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>μάκτρον</i> (máktron, “wiper, towel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>driving away flies; acting as a muscifuge, fly repellent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away from”) +&lrm; <i>μυῖᾰ</i> (muîă, “fly”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀπόμυιος)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Averter of flies, <i>epithet of Zeus and Heracles</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπόμυιος</i> (apómuios, literally “repelling flies”), from <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away from”) +&lrm; <i>μυῖᾰ</i> (muîă, “fly”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desperation</li><li>madness, dementia</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπονοέομαι</i> (aponoéomai) (from <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>νοέομαι</i> (noéomai)) + <i>-ια</i> (-ia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Stricken by a stroke</li><li>dumb, astounded, senseless</li><li>crippled</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποπλήσσω</i> (apoplḗssō, “I strike with a stroke”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from far off, from afar</li></ol><p><i>ἀποπρό</i> (apopró, “far off”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>far away, afar</li></ol><p><i>ἀποπρό</i> (apopró) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a portion of a <i>χιτών</i> (khitṓn, “tunic, coat”) folded back</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “back”) +&lrm; <i>πτύσσω</i> (ptússō, “I fold”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a falling off or away; falling of things that detach, such as petals</li><li>deposition</li><li>direction in which a force is exerted</li><li>vanishing; disappearance; negation; eclipse</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) declension from</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away from”) +&lrm; <i>πτῶσις</i> (ptôsis, “falling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forbidden, prohibited</li><li>secret</li><li>(<i>of words</i>) not in common use</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>ῥητός</i> (rhētós, “spoken”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an awkward situation, in an embarrassing position</li><li>badly off (in unfortunate circumstances, especially having financial difficulty)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄπορος</i> (áporos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure</li><li>defection, revolt</li><li>distance</li><li>storehouse</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) suppurative inflammation</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ἀφίστημι</i> (aphístēmi, “to stand off”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, verbal noun suffix) or <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>στάσις</i> (stásis, “standing, position”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is sent</li><li>messenger, envoy, ambassador</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) apostle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποστέλλω</i> (apostéllō, “to send off”), from <i>ἀπό-</i> (apó-, “from”) + <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “to send”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>turned away</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) repulsive, horrible</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποστρέφω</i> (apostréphō, “turn away”, from <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō)) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>separate assessment (for tribute)</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) resignation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποτᾰ́σσω</i> (apotắssō, “to set apart”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lengthening, prolongation</li><li>stretching out</li><li>distension (of the breast)</li><li>reference</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποτείνω</i> (apoteínō, “to stretch out, extend”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cut off, abrupt, precipitous</li></ol><p><i>ἀποτέμνω</i> (apotémnō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>law</i>) decision, finding, resolution, judgment, sentence, verdict (as of a court)</li><li>(logic,&#32;rhetoric) declaration, predication, assertion, answer</li><li>list, inventory, catalogue</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) oracle, prophetic statement, heavenly proclamation</li><li>appearance, image, apparition</li><li>(logic,&#32;rhetoric) denial, negation, repudiation, exclusion; apophasis, negatory predication, denying a proposition</li><li>(grammar,&#32;linguistics) negative particle; negating sign</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away, from, off”) +&lrm; <i>φᾰ́σῐς</i> (phắsĭs, “appearance; denunciation; accusation”), root <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “bring to light; appear; denounce”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs); form of composed verb <i>ἀποφαίνω</i> (apophaínō, “make known, declare, make clear; produce, render; denounce”), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰáňňō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂</i> (“to shine”).</p><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away, from, off”) +&lrm; <i>φᾰ́σῐς</i> (phắsĭs, “statement, proposition, assertion”), root <i>φημί</i> (phēmí, “say, affirm, assert”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs); form of composed verb <i>ἀπόφημι</i> (apóphēmi, “speak out; say no, refuse, deny”) from Attic Greek <i>φημί</i> (phēmí), Doric Greek <i>φᾱμί</i> (phāmí) from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰā́mā</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂</i> (“to speak”); whence via Proto-Italic <i>&#42;fāōr</i>, <i>&#42;fāmā</i> cognate to Latin <i>fārī</i>, <i>fābula</i>, <i>fāma</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thing uttered</li><li>especially a terse, pointed saying, an apophthegm</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποφθέγγομαι</i> (apophthéngomai, “I plainly speak my mind”), from <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>φθέγγομαι</i> (phthéngomai, “to speak loudly”), +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) side-shoot, sprout</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) apophysis, protuberance of a bone</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) branch of an artery or nerve</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) member connecting shaft and base of a column</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀποφύω</i> (apophúō, “to send out branches”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic and Arcadocypriot form of <i>ᾰ̓πό</i> (ăpó)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>absent</li></ol><p>From the present participle of the verb <i>ἄπειμι</i> (ápeimi, “I am absent”). See <i>ἀπό</i> (apó) and <i>ὤν</i> (ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prayer, invocation</li><li>imprecation, curse, malediction</li></ol><p>Arcadocypriot Greek <i>καταρϝος</i> (katarwos, “cursed”) suggests a preform Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;arwā</i>, which makes Attic -ᾱ hard to explain; -η would be expected after *<i>w</i>. Not cognate with Latin <i>āra</i> (“altar”). Possibly related to Hittite (/⁠aruwae-⁠/, “to bow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of dogs</i>) to snarl, growl</li></ol><p>Perhaps onomatopoeic, but Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin in view of the similarity with <i>ἄραβος</i> (árabos, “rattle, ring”) and <i>ἄραδος</i> (árados, “disturbance, palpitation”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of chickling vetch</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρακος</i> (árakos, “chickling vetch”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A fabulous plant which grows by the river Araxes</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>middle only</i>) to pray</li><li>(<i>middle only</i>) to imprecate, curse</li><li>(<i>middle only</i>) to vow</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρᾱ́</i> (arā́, “prayer”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smearwort (<i>Aristolochia rotunda</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smite, dash in pieces</li><li>to knock at the door</li><li>(<i>of rattling, crashing</i>) to beat, strike</li></ol><p>Unknown, but Beekes suggests an onomatopoeic origin, perhaps similar to that of <i>ἄρᾰβος</i> (árăbos, “gnashing, rattling”). Possibly related to <i>ῥάσσω</i> (rhássō, “to strike, dash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ground pea (<i>Lathyrus amphicarpos</i>)</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ἄρακος</i> (árakos, “a leguminous plant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feminine form of <i>ᾰ̓ρᾰ́χνης</i> (ărắkhnēs)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>spider web</li><li>cow parsnip</li><li>A kind of sundial (mollusc).</li></ol></ol><p>Pre-Greek origin, although related to Latin <i>arānea</i>. Whether it is the source of the Latin term or they both originated from a different common source is unclear. Basque <i>armiarma</i> and Basque <i>aramu</i> possibly may be distantly related to the substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spider</li><li>A kind of legume.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please, satisfy</li><li>to make amends</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, possibly from a disyllabic Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;h₂erh₁-</i> (“to please, satisfy”), which appears to be related to <i>&#42;h₂reh₁-</i> (“to think, reason, arrange”), apparently reanalyzed from <i>&#42;h₂réh₁(ye)ti</i>, a stative-durative verbal form from the root <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit, fix, put together”). See also <i>ᾰ̓ρετή</i> (ăretḗ, “excellence, virtue”), <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “to join, fit”), <i>ᾰ̓́ρῐστος</i> (ắrĭstos, “best”).</p><p>Older theories derived the word from <i>αἴρω</i> (aírō, “to lift, remove”) with inchoative suffix <i>-σκω</i> (-skō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made with wood of holm oak</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρίᾱ</i> (aríā, “holm oak”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bane, ruin, harm</li><li>prayer, imprecation (Ionic form of ἀρά)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The word has been connected with <i>ἄρος</i> (áros, “involuntary damage”), <i>ἀπαρές</i> (aparés, “healthy”), <i>ἀρειή</i> (areiḗ, “cursing, threatening”) and also with <i>Ἄρης</i> (Árēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to aid, succour in war</li><li>to ward off, prevent, avert</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂reh₁ǵ-</i> (“to help, support”). Cognates include Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rōkijaną</i> (“to take care of”).</p><p>Latin <i>regō</i> (“to rule, govern”), Ancient Greek <i>ὀρέγω</i> (orégō, “to reach, stretch out”) derive instead from the apparently-unrelated Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃reǵ-</i> (“to straighten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓μνός</i> (ămnós, “lamb”)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) stunted ears of wheat</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;warḗn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥h₁ḗn</i> (“lamb”). Cognates include Latin <i>vervēx</i> (“wether”), Sanskrit <i>उरण</i> (úraṇa) and Old Armenian <i>գառն</i> (gaṙn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἄρειος</i> (áreios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holm oak (<i>Quercus ilex</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of light or voice</i>) conspicuous, very distinct</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) conspicuous, remarkable</li></ol><p>From an augmentative prefix <i>ἀρι-</i>, while the second part is, like <i>δῆλος</i> (dêlos, “visible, conspicuous”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dyew-</i> (“heaven, sky; to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bow drill</li></ol><p>The formation is common to other technical terms like <i>δοκίς</i> (dokís, “beam”) and <i>σανίς</i> (sanís, “board; plank”) but the origin is still unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>narrow, slender</li><li>thin, fine, weak</li><li>(<i>of a row or rank</i>) loose, thin, far apart (as opposed to πυκνός (puknós, “thick, close”))</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to clatter, chatter</li></ol><p><i>ἄραβος</i> (árabos, “chattering of teeth”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whitetop (<i>Lepidium draba</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>Ἀρᾰβῐ́ᾱ</i> (Arăbĭ́ā, “Arabia”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is), after its native environment. For the formation compare <i>ἰβηρίς</i> (ibērís, “pepperwort”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thin; lean; narrow; slight; slender</li><li>not dense; of loose consistency; porous</li><li>intermittent; with intervals</li><li>infrequent; scanty; few and far between</li></ol><p>Unknown; likely had initial <i>&#42;ϝ-</i> (&#42;w-), according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>join, fasten</li><li>fit together, construct</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>prepare, contrive</li></ol><li>fit, equip, furnish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>please, gratify</li></ol><li>make fit, make pleasing</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be joined closely together</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to be fixed</li></ol><li>to fit or suit</li><li>to be fitted with, furnished with</li><li>to be fitted, suiting, agreeable, pleasing</li></ol></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit”) and suffixed with <i>-σκω</i> (-skō). The aorist form of this verb, <i>ἤρᾰρον</i> (ḗrăron), may derive from a Proto-Indo-European reduplicated thematic aorist <i>&#42;h₂eh₂ret</i>, perhaps whence also Old Armenian <i>արար-</i> (arar-), the aorist stem of <i>առնեմ</i> (aṙnem). Alternatively, the linguist Petr Kocharov argues that the Armenian aorist is better equated with Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓ρήρη</i> (ărḗrē), thereby isolating the Greek reduplicated aorist. According to the linguist Jay Jasanoff, the term <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō) may have emerged by analogy to terms such as <i>εὑρίσκω</i> (heurískō), which also has a reduplicated aorist.</p><p>Cognates include Old Armenian <i>արարի</i> (arari, “I did”), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬨</i> (ar<sup><small>ə</small></sup>m), and Sanskrit <i>ऋत</i> (ṛtá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prayed against, hence accursed</li><li>prayed for, hence desirable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀράομαι</i> (aráomai, “to pray”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like a cobweb; cobwebby</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρᾰ́χνη</i> (ărắkhnē, “spider web”) +&lrm; <i>-ο-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-oid, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spidery, cobwebby</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρᾰ́χνης</i> (ărắkhnēs, “spider”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “-like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>τήγανον ὀστράκινον</i> (tḗganon ostrákinon, “clay frying pan”).</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of tough shoe or half-boot</li></ol><p>According to Beekes from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἀργήεις</i> (argḗeis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>west-northwest wind (according to Pliny the Elder)</li><li>west-southwest wind (according to Vitruvius)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀργής</i> (argḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>White, shining.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀργής</i> (argḗs, “bright, glancing”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “-ful”, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Bright, glancing. (<i>of lightning</i>)</li><li>Shining, white. (<i>of fat or robe</i>)</li></ol><p>Compare with <i>ἀργός</i> (argós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of employment or use; idleness; laziness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a good sense</i>) rest, leisure</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) used to express the Latin <i>fēriae</i></li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓ργός</i> (ārgós, “not working”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard to bear, difficult to endure, troublesome, vexatious</li></ol><p>By dissimilation from <i>&#42;ἀλγαλέος</i> (&#42;algaléos), from <i>ἄλγος</i> (álgos, “pain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commandant of a fort</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Parthian <i>𐭀𐭓𐭊𐭐𐭕𐭉</i> (argbed).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet of Hermes, literal meaning uncertain (see Etymology above)</li></ol><p>Uncertain; suggestions for a literal meaning include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>brightly shining; white</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ργός</i> (ărgós, “white; bright”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>white-toothed, white-tusked</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀργός</i> (argós, “white”) +&lrm; <i>ὀδούς</i> (odoús, “tooth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of, for, or in silver</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A money chest</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver; money”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coiner, silversmith</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>κόπτω</i> (kóptō, “to strike, cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ᾰ̓ργῠ́ρεος</i> (ărgŭ́reos, “of silver”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silver-studded</li></ol><p><i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>ἧλος</i> (hêlos, “stud, nail”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) selenite</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>λίθος</i> (líthos, “stone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>silver-footed or -sandaled (epithet of various goddesses)</li></ol><p><i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros) +&lrm; <i>πεζός</i> (pezós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with silver root</li><li>having silver in the soil</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄργῠρος</i> (árgŭros, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>ῥῐ́ζᾰ</i> (rhĭ́ză, “root”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;epithet of Apollo) bearer of a silver bow</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>τόξον</i> (tóxon, “bow”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silver ore</li><li>mercury (<i>Mercurialis annua</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shining; glistening; white; bright</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) swift</li><li>not working the ground; idle; lazy; slow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of money</i>) lying idle; yielding no return</li><li>(<i>of land</i>) lying fallow; fruitless</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>unwrought, unpolished; not done; left undone</li><li>unattempted</li></ol></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;ἀργρός</i> (&#42;argrós), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂rǵ-ró-s</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂erǵ-</i> (“white”). Cognates include Ancient Greek <i>ἄργυρος</i> (árguros, “silver”), Latin <i>arguō</i> (“to make clear”), Sanskrit <i>अर्जुन</i> (árjuna, “white, clear”) and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂r̥ǵn̥tóm</i> (“silver”), whence Latin <i>argentum</i>, Old Irish <i>argat</i>, Welsh <i>arian</i>, <i>रजत</i> (rajatá), Avestan <i>𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬰𐬀𐬙𐬀</i> (ər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>zata), Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐎭𐎫</i> (ardata) and Old Armenian <i>արծաթ</i> (arcatʻ), and possibly Proto-Brythonic <i>&#42;ėrɣ</i> (“snow”).</p><p>Contracted form of <i>ᾰ̓εργός</i> (ăergós), from <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not, a-”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “a work”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós, adjectival suffix), and hence has a long <i>ᾱ</i> (ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̓ργός</i> (ărgós, “shining; white; swift”)</li><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾱ̓ργός</i> (ārgós, “idle; lazy; slow”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓ργός</i> (ărgós, “shining; white; swift”)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾱ̓ργός</i> (ārgós, “idle; lazy; slow”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of silver</li></ol><p><i>ἄργῠρος</i> (árgŭros) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small coin, coinage</li><li>money</li><li>silver</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ργῠρος</i> (ắrgŭros, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterpot or trough</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρδαλος</i> (árdalos, “spot, taint”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἀγαθός</i> (agathós)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ᾰ̓́ρῐστος</i> (ắrĭstos) + <i>-ῐ́ων</i> (-ĭ́ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ᾰ̓ρετή</i> (ăretḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>goodness, excellence</li><li>manliness, prowess, rank, valour</li><li>(<i>not countable</i>): virtue</li><li>(<i>countable</i>): a virtue, the virtues</li><li>character, reputation, glory, fame, dignity, distinction</li><li>miracle, wonder</li><li>(<i>as a title</i>) "your worship"</li><li>plaster</li></ol><p>Apparently from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂rh₁téh₂</i>, from a root <i>&#42;h₂erh₁-</i> (“to arrange, fit”). See also the related terms <i>ἀρέσκω</i> (aréskō, “to please”), <i>ἀρείων</i> (areíōn, “better, braver”), and <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “to join, fasten”), the latter which Beekes considers the most clearly connected term semantically.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warlike, literally swift in the fight</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) beloved of Ares (<i>epithet of various warriors</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρης</i> (Árēs) +&lrm; <i>-φιλος</i> (-philos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cassia mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) distressed, worn out</li></ol><p>The perfect mediopassive participle of an unattested verb.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a priest</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀράομαι</i> (aráomai, “I pray”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, masculine agent noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bond, league, friendship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;artʰmós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit; to put together”) +&lrm; <i>-θμός</i> (-thmós). Semantically merged with the etymologically older <i>ἀρτύς</i> (artús), which is from the same root but with a different suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) disease of or in the joint</li></ol><p>Formed as: <i>ἄρθρον</i> (árthron, “joint”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis), as if the feminine of *<i>ἀρθρίτης</i> (arthrítēs), though no such form is attested.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly early&#32;Epic&#32;and&#32;Lyric</i>) strengthening prefix, generally with a sense of goodness, excellence</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>ᾰ̓ρείων</i> (ăreíōn) <i>ᾰ̓́ρῐστος</i> (ắrĭstos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>glorious, renowned</li></ol><p><i>ἀρι-</i> (ari-) +&lrm; <i>δείκνῡμι</i> (deíknūmi) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to count</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρῐθμός</i> (ărĭthmós, “number”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a land tax in Egypt</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) numeral</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for reckoning, skilled in reckoning</li><li>arithmetical, arithmetic</li><li>(<i>adverb</i>) numerically</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a land tax in Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀριθμέω</i> (arithméō, “reckon, count”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, “a suffix added to verbal stems to form adjectives: relating to, suited to, skilled in, able to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for reckoning, skilled in reckoning</li><li>arithmetical, arithmetic</li><li>(<i>adverb</i>) numerically</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a land tax in Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀριθμέω</i> (arithméō, “reckon, count”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, “a suffix added to verbal stems to form adjectives: relating to, suited to, skilled in, able to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>number</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>amount, sum</li><li>term in a series</li><li>number, account, rank</li></ol><li><b>quantity</b>, <b>amount</b> (of a material, gold, money, time)</li><li>numbered or countable item (in a series or list), <b>item</b>; <b>piece</b> (of a wrecked ship); <b>mere entity, cipher</b> (to a person)</li><li>taking account or evaluation; <b>regard</b> (for a woman); <b>reckoning</b> (by a person's words)</li><li>numbering, counting</li><li>arithmetic</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) abstract number</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) number</li><li>numeral</li><li>science of numbers, <b>arithmetic, calculation</b></li><li>mathematical unit or aggregate of units, <b>number</b> (as odd or even); (as underlying the organisation of time and space); (as the cause of being and generation);(existing as an ideal entity, opp. as a mathematical object)</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) rhythm</li><li>numerical sum, <b>number</b>, <b>total number</b> (of persons, animals, things)</li><li>(rhetorical) <b>numerical measurement</b> (of the configuration of speech, reference to rhythm)</li><li>collection (of individuals forming a group), <b>number</b>, <b>company</b> (of men, envoys, disciples)</li><li>military unit (=&nbsp;Latin <i>numerus</i>)</li><li>(astrology,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) degrees moved traversed in a given time</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) precise condition</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ri-dʰh₁-mó-s</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂rey-</i> (“to count, arrange”). Cognates include Old Irish <i>rím</i>, Old English <i>rīm</i> (English <i>rhyme</i>), and perhaps Latin <i>rītus</i>. Also compare <i>νήριτος</i> (nḗritos, “countless”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>excellence, prowess. <i>Book five of the Iliad is called Διομήδους ἀριστεία.</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ἀριστεύω</i> (aristeúō, “to be best”) +&lrm; <i>-εία</i> (-eía, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) left hand</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ᾰ̓ρῐστερᾱ́</i> (ărĭsterā́, “left hand”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the left one (of a set of objects)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) left (opposite of right)</li><li>ominous; ill boding</li><li>clumsy; awkward</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>ᾰ̓́ρῐστος</i> (ắrĭstos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, “contrastive suffix”), though the left direction has negative connotations in many cultures and languages, including Greece. According to Chantraine, the suffix -τερος serves to differentiate and create a euphemistic contrast with ἄριστος; an unusual formation. The latter is taken to indicate <i>on the right side</i> in this case, the left side being its literal and metaphorical opposite.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the best man; victor, champion (a winner of first prize or first place in competition)</li><li>the chief of a polis</li><li>the bravest man/best man in valor; hero, champion (someone who fights on another's behalf)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀριστεύς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aristeus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀριστεύς</i> (aristeús, “who excels in valour, chief”), from <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>particularly of a man, later expanded</i>): to be superlative in quality, to be the best or finest; to be outstanding or preeminent; to be excellent, to excel; (<i>especially</i>): to excel in valor, to be the bravest</li><li>(<i>particularly of a man, later expanded</i>): to be victorious: to win, to win out, to prevail, to triumph; to conquer, to vanquish; to gain the highest distinction, to gain the prize for valor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀριστεύς</i> (aristeús, the best man, the bravest man) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, suffix forming denominative verbs).</p><p>From <i>ᾱ̓́ρῑστον</i> (ā́rīston, early meal; breakfast, lunch) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, suffix forming denominative verbs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aristocrat</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́ρῐστος</i> (ắrĭstos) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀριστοκράτης)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aristocrates</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aristocracy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos) +&lrm; <i>-κρατίᾱ</i> (-kratíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aristocratic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀριστοκράτης</i> (aristokrátēs) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>birthwort (any plant of the genus <i>Aristolochia</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀριστόλοχος</i> (aristólokhos, “well born”) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy to be known, very distinct, manifest, plain</li><li>very thoughtful, wise</li></ol><p><i>ἀρι-</i> (ari-) +&lrm; <i>φραδής</i> (phradḗs, “understanding, wise, shrewd”), from the root of <i>φράζω</i> (phrázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>warp beam (bar to which the threads of the loom are fastened)</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>ᾰ̓́ρκῠς</i> (ắrkŭs, “hunter's net”) is doubtful. The suffix is also seen in <i>δρεπᾰ́νη</i> (drepắnē, “sickle”), <i>κᾰπᾱ́νη</i> (kăpā́nē, “chariot”) and <i>θηγᾰ́νη</i> (thēgắnē, “whetstone”). Probably a loanword, perhaps from Pre-Greek, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ward off, keep off</li><li>to defend [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to assist, succour</li><li>to suffice, be enough (for), satisfy</li><li>to be strong enough, avail, hold out</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be satisfied with</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erk-</i> (“to protect, guard, hold, contain”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>արգել</i> (argel, “obstacle”) and Latin <i>arceō</i> (“to keep off, prevent, confine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sufficient, enough</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) satisfactory</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρκέω</i> (arkéō, “to ward off, be enough”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of juniper</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ρκευθος</i> (ắrkeuthos, “juniper”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sufficiently, enough</li></ol><p><i>ἀρκῶν</i> (arkôn) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having to do with the Great Bear (Ursa Major)</li><li>northern lands (lands near Ursa Major)</li><li>initial, placed at the beginning</li><li>authoritarian</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρκτος</i> (árktos, “bear”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p><p>From ἄρχομαι.</p><p>From ἄρχω.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black turnip (<i>Bongardia chrysogonum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρκος</i> (árkos, “bear”) +&lrm; <i>ὀφθαλμός</i> (ophthalmós, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A store for ship's tackle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρμενον</i> (ármenon, “tackle of a ship”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to deny, disown</li><li>to refuse</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to say "no", decline, refuse</li></ol><p>According to the LIV, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂r̥-né-s-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂res-</i> (“to refuse, deny”). Compare Avestan <i>𐬭𐬀𐬵-</i> (rah-, “to be disloyal, unfaithful”) and notably Albanian <i>rrej</i> (“to lie, deceive”), though not related to Old Armenian <i>ուրանամ</i> (uranam, “to deny”) (whence modern <i>ուրանալ</i> (uranal)). However, the linguist Johnny Cheung leaves the etymology of the proposed Iranian cognates as uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little ram, little lamb</li><li>sheep-skin, fleece</li></ol><p><i>ἀρήν</i> (arḗn, “lamb, sheep”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepskin coat</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρήν</i> (arḗn, “lamb, sheep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ram, wether</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) lamb</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἀρήν</i> (arḗn, “lamb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greater plantain (<i>Plantago major</i>)</li><li>harefoot plantain (<i>Plantago lagopus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρήν</i> (arḗn, “lamb”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) synonym of <i>ᾰ̓μνός</i> (ămnós, “lamb”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plower, plowman, husbandman</li><li>(<i>adjectival use</i>) having to do with ploughing</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a begetter, father</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρόω</i> (ăróō, “I plow”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, masculine agent noun suffix). See also <i>ἄροτρον</i> (árotron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ploughing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀροτριάω</i> (arotriáō, “plough”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from the country, rural, rustic</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ρουρᾰ</i> (ắroură, “cultivated field”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cord, for binding or snaring game</li><li>yarn of which cloth is made</li><li>silkworm's thread</li></ol><p>Unknown. Connection with <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō, “to snatch away”) is improbable for a word "cord". Grošelj Živa connects the word with Lithuanian <i>verpti</i> (“to spin”), but the <i>-αρ-</i> must be analogical after <i>-ερ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius' gives the definition as: <i>θύρα</i> (thúra, “door”) <i>οἷον</i> (hoîon, “such as”) <i>γέρρον</i> (gérrhon, “of wickerwork”).</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earnest money, caution money</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) earnest, pledge</li><li>present, bribe</li><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) fish hook</li></ol><p>A loanword from Biblical Hebrew <i>עירבון &#47; עֵרָבוֹן</i> (ʿērāḇōn, “guarantee, deposit”), or from a common third source, probably Semitic.</p><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbroken (not divided into parts)</li><li>unbreakable (that cannot be rent or broken)</li><li>(<i>of an eye</i>) not bursting into tears</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ῥήγνῡμι</i> (rhḗgnūmi, “to break, shatter”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀρσενοκοίτης</i> (arsenokoítēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>moral</i>) to practice an unmentionable or shameful vice</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρρητος</i> (árrhētos, “unspeakable, shameful”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to do, make”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>moral</i>) practising unspeakable or taboo acts; guilty of an unmentionable vice</li><li>(by extension,&#32;ironic) dealing with or celebrating mysteries; speaking of secret or esoteric rites</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρρητος</i> (árrhētos, “unspeakable, taboo”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “to make”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>moral</i>) to commit unspeakable or taboo acts; to practise an unmentionable vice</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρρητος</i> (árrhētos, “unspeakable, taboo”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “deed, act”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō), on the model of <i>αἰσχρουργέω</i> (aiskhrourgéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of rhythm; arhythmicality</li><li>lack of proportion</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἄρρυθμος</i> (árrhuthmos, “arhythmic”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, abstract noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) orpiment, yellow sulfide of arsenic</li><li>arsenic (chemical element 33) (later meaning, not attested up to the 5<sup>th</sup> c. CE)</li></ol><p>Of Semitic origin (compare Classical Syriac <i>ܙܪܢܝܟܐ</i> (zarnīḵā, “orpiment”)) under the strong influence of <i>ἀρσενικός</i> (arsenikós, “male, virile”), from Middle Persian <i>&#42;zarnīk</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰelh₃-</i> (“yellow”). See arsenic for more cognates.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>male</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρσην</i> (ársēn) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>male</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρσην</i> (ársēn) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male who has sexual intercourse with males, especially in a penetrative role</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρσην</i> (ársēn, “male”) and κοι-, <i>o</i>-grade stem of <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai, “lie”), +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix), thus “a male who lies with males”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persian measure of capacity, equivalent to 1 medimnus + 3 choenices</li><li>Egyptian measure of capacity, varying from 24 to 42 choenices</li></ol><p>The word is of Iranian origin (see Old North Iranian <i>*ṛdba-</i>, Old Persian <i>&#42;ṛdva-</i>) and ultimately from Akkadian <i>𒅈𒁕𒁉</i> (/⁠ardabu⁠/, “capacity measure of about 56 litres”), found as Aramaic <i>𐡀𐡓𐡃𐡁</i> (ʾrdb) / <i>אַרְדְּבָא</i> (ʾardəḇā) / <i>ܐܪܕܒܐ</i> (ʾardəḇā), (Classical Syriac also <i>ܐܪܛܒܐ</i> (ʾarṭəḇā)), Arabic إِرْدَبّ (ʔirdabb), Coptic <i>ⲉⲣⲧⲟⲃ</i> (ertob), <i>ⲁⲣⲧⲁⲃ</i> (artab), and Old Armenian <i>արդու</i> (ardu) (whence Old Georgian <i>არდუჲ</i> (arduy)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope, noose, halter, that by which something is hung up</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρτᾰ́ω</i> (ărtắō, “to fasten to or hang upon”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́νη</i> (-ắnē, instrument noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fasten to or hang one thing upon another</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be hung upon</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to depend upon</li></ol><p>This verb is generally reconstructed as <i>&#42;ἀϝερτάω</i> (&#42;awertáō), from <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō, “to bind, hang”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wer-</i> (“to raise; to bind”), but the derivation is unusual. See also <i>ἀρτήρ</i> (artḗr) and <i>ἀρτηρία</i> (artēría, “artery; windpipe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>topsail, foresail, bysail</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρτάω</i> (artáō, “to attach”) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earrings</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Connection with <i>ἄρτιος</i> (ártios, “complete, suitable”), with a suffix <i>-αλο-</i>, has been suggested.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wormwood</li><li>synonym of <i>ἀμβροσίᾱ</i> (ambrosíā)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρτεμις</i> (Ártemis) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀρτεμισία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artemisia</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρτεμῐς</i> (Ártemĭs, “Artemis”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>windpipe, trachea</li><li>artery (as distinct from a vein)</li><li>aorta</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ureters</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) bronchi</li></ol></ol><p>Supposed contraction of <i>&#42;ᾰ̓ορτηρῐ́ᾱ</i> (&#42;ăortērĭ́ā) (cf. <i>ᾰ̓ορτήρ</i> (ăortḗr)), from <i>ᾰ̓είρω</i> (ăeírō, “to bind, hang up”).</p><p>It was assumed that all blood vessels transported both blood and πνεῦμα (pneûma, “breath, spirit”), in a similar way as how the latter is transported through the τραχεῖα ἀρτηρία (trakheîa artēría, literally “rough artery”). This might explain why windpipe and artery share the same name.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A pantry</li><li>A bread basket</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρτος</i> (ártos, “bread”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of bread or cake (made of meal, wine, milk, oil, lard, and pepper)</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἄρτος</i> (ártos, “loaf of bread”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seasoned, flavored</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρτύω</i> (artúō, “to season”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀρτύω</i> (artúō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀρθμός</i> (arthmós, “bond”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;artús</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂(e)r-tús</i> (“form, order; bond”), from the root <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit together, join”).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>artus</i> (“joint, sinew, limb; power”), Old Armenian <i>արդ</i> (ard, “shape, order”), Sanskrit <i>ऋतु</i> (ṛtú, “union; order, rule; time period”), Avestan <i>𐬭𐬀𐬙𐬎</i> (ratu, “time period”) and Middle Persian <small>[Book Pahlavi needed]</small> (lt' /⁠rad⁠/, “master; judge”).</p><p>Apparently semantically merged in prehistory with <i>ἀρθμός</i> (arthmós), despite the different suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrange, devise, prepare, make ready</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) to dress, season</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-tu-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to join, fit, fix together”); compare <i>ἀρτύς</i> (artús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀρῠβαλλῐ́ς</i> (arŭballĭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ᾰ̓ρῠ́βαλλος</i> (ărŭ́ballos) m</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρύβαλλος</i> (arúballos, “globular oil flask”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), of likely Pre-Greek origin given the suffix -αλλ-.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A ladle or cup.</li><li>Irrigation.</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρῠ́ω</i> (ărŭ́ō, “to draw (water, wine, etc.)”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr, masculine noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Shaped like a ladle or cup.</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρῠ́ταινᾰ</i> (ărŭ́taină, “a ladle or cup”) +&lrm; <i>-ο-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-like, -oid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archangel</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-, “arch-”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos, “angel, messenger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>archetype, pattern, model</li><li>the figure on a seal</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first moulded as a pattern or model, archetypal</li><li>exemplary, ideal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ) +&lrm; <i>τύπος</i> (túpos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first moulded as a pattern or model, archetypal</li><li>exemplary, ideal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ) +&lrm; <i>τύπος</i> (túpos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beginning, origin</li><li>sovereignty, dominion, authority</li><li>the end of a rope or stick, the corner of a sheet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to begin, to be first in order”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be old-fashioned, copy the ancients (in manners, language, etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχαῖος</i> (arkhaîos, “old, ancient”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antiquarian lore, ancient legends or history</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχαῖος</i> (arkhaîos) +&lrm; <i>-λογία</i> (-logía)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>antiquity, old-fashionedness</li><li>simplicity, pristine state</li><li>ancient history</li><li>antiquity, ancient times</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) advanced age of a person</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχαῖος</i> (arkhaîos, “ancient, old-fashioned”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old-fashioned</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχαῖος</i> (arkhaîos, “ancient”) +&lrm; <i>τρόπος</i> (trópos, “manner, style”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>old-fashioned</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχαῖος</i> (arkhaîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>old-world charm (of style)</li><li>use of obsolete expressions, archaism</li><li>ancient custom</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχαΐζω</i> (arkhaḯzō, “be old-fashioned”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Pertaining to the beginning</li><li>ancient, primeval</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ, “beginning”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public building, town hall</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) archives, public records</li></ol></ol><p>Neuter of *<i>ἀρχεῖος</i> (arkheîos, “related to office”), from <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ, “office, government, rule”), from <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founding leader/chieftain, original author, founder</li><li>title of Apollo</li><li>prince, chief, governor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ, “beginning”) +&lrm; <i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai, “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>denotes primary importance or authority</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ρχω</i> (ắrkhō, “begin, rule”) or <i>ᾰ̓ρχός</i> (ărkhós, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>court or official physician</li><li>responsible practitioner</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-) +&lrm; <i>ἰᾱτρός</i> (iātrós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief clerk or secretary</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχῐ-</i> (arkhĭ-, chief) +&lrm; <i>γρᾰμμᾰτεύς</i> (grămmăteús, “secretary, clerk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief deacon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχῐ-</i> (arkhĭ-, “chief”) +&lrm; <i>δῐᾱ́κονος</i> (dĭā́konos, “deacon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Egypt</i>) chief-judge</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχῐ-</i> (arkhĭ-, “chief”) +&lrm; <i>δῐκᾰστής</i> (dĭkăstḗs, “judge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) archbishop</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-) + <i>ἐπίσκοπος</i> (epískopos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief priest, high priest</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-, “chief”) +&lrm; <i>ἱερεύς</i> (hiereús, “priest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief keeper of a temple</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχῐ-</i> (arkhĭ-, “chief”) +&lrm; <i>ζᾰ́κορος</i> (zắkoros, “keeper of a temple”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief cook</li><li>title of a great officer in Oriental courts</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχῐ-</i> (arkhĭ-, “chief”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́γειρος</i> (mắgeiros, “cook”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief of a μάνδρα (mándra, “monastery”), abbot</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-, “primary”) +&lrm; <i>μάνδρα</i> (mándra, “enclosed space, monastery”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, “member of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief president</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχῐ-</i> (arkhĭ-, “chief”) +&lrm; <i>πρῠ́τᾰνῐς</i> (prŭ́tănĭs, “prytanis”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief lictor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχῐ-</i> (arkhĭ-, “chief”) +&lrm; <i>ῥᾰβδοῦχος</i> (rhăbdoûkhos, “lictor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) archisynagogue (the president, director, or ruler of a synagogue)</li><li>master of a guild or company</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρχῐ-</i> (ărkhĭ-, “chief”) +&lrm; <i>σῠνᾰγωγή</i> (sŭnăgōgḗ, “synagogue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief constructor, master builder, architect</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-, “chief”) +&lrm; <i>τέκτων</i> (téktōn, “builder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>architecture, construction; architectonic design; master craftsmanship</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχιτέκτων</i> (arkhitéktōn, “architect, master craftsman”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía), from <i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-, “master”) +&lrm; <i>τέκτων</i> (téktōn, “craftsman, builder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a master builder or architect</li><li>fit to be a master builder, skilled in architecture</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;feminine) architecture</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχιτέκτων</i> (arkhitéktōn) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chief tax collector</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρχῐ-</i> (ărkhĭ-, “chief; prime”) +&lrm; <i>τελώνης</i> (telṓnēs, “tax collecter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler, leader, prince</li></ol><p>See <i>ᾰ̓́ρχω</i> (ắrkhō, “to begin, to be first, to rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the beginning, from of old</li><li>(<i>with negative</i>) not at all</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ, “a beginning, origin”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “a suffix added to nouns to form adverbs of place from which: from”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aid, succour</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἀρήγω</i> (arḗgō, “I help”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aiding, succoring, propitious [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>serviceable [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> genitive <i>or</i> <b>πρός</b> (prós, + accusative) <small>‘towards’</small>]</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) helper, aid (<i>especially in battle</i>)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine,&#32;law) defender before a tribunal, advocate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρήγω</i> (arḗgō) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plow; to till</li><li>to sow</li><li>to beget</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to enjoy the fruits of tillage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂érh₃-ye-</i>, a ye-present from the root <i>&#42;h₂erh₃-</i> (“to plough”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>արաւր</i> (arawr), Latin <i>arō</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>орати</i> (orati), and Old English <i>erian</i> (Early Modern English <i>ear</i>). Related to <i>ἄροτρον</i> (árotron, “a plough”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bag or purse, made so as to draw close</li><li>a globular oil-flask</li></ol><p>Probably of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix <i>-αλλ-</i> (-all-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀρύω</i> (arúō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw (water, wine, etc.)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to draw water for oneself</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of stars</i>) to rise (from the sea)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>Unknown. Frisk takes it as <i>&#42;ϝαρύω</i> (&#42;warúō) and connects it with Old Armenian <i>գերեմ</i> (gerem, “to capture, take prisoner”), <i>εὑρίσκω</i> (heurískō, “to find”), assuming a root <i>&#42;uer-</i>; but the Greek <i>-α-</i> creates difficulties for this etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bathtub &lsqb;Homer&rsqb;</li></ol><p>The -ινθος points according to Beekes to Pre-Greek, like in <i>καλαμίνθη</i> (kalamínthē), <i>ἀψίνθιον</i> (apsínthion) etc. However even if that generally is so the suffix form is probably secondary and the word renders Akkadian <i>𒉏𒋛𒂊𒌈</i> (nim-se-e-tum /⁠namasittu, namasītu, nemesētu⁠/, “bathtub”), alternatively <i>𒄀𒃻𒇒𒊏</i> (GI.NIG<sub>2</sub>.ESIR<sub>2</sub>.RA /⁠namsû⁠/, “wash-bowl”) with the suffix later appended, with metathesis <i>-amas-</i> to <i>-asam-</i> and rebracketing the original <i>ν</i> (n) as from the Greek article.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to glut oneself, take a surfeit</li><li>to feel loathing or nausea, caused by surfeit</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be disgusted or vexed at a thing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄση</i> (ásē, “surfeit, nausea”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impiety, ungodliness</li><li>(<i>Late Antiquity</i>) heresy</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓σεβής</i> (ăsebḗs, “impious”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of silver</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄσημον</i> (ásēmon, “silver”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indistinct to the senses, dim, faint</li><li>indistinct to the mind, uncertain, obscure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>σᾰφής</i> (săphḗs, “clear, distinct”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ungodly, godless, unholy, profane, sinful, sacrilegious</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>σέβομαι</i> (sébomai, “I feel awe”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>licentious, wanton, brutal, outrageous</li><li>lascivious, lewd, lustful</li></ol><p>Unclear; Beekes compares this word with the Hesychian gloss <i>ἀσάλγαν</i> (asálgan, “insolence, indifference”), suggesting a possible Pre-Greek origin, but not without hesitation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weakness</li><li>sickness</li><li>moral weakness, depravity</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσθενής</i> (asthenḗs, “sick, weak”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἀσθενής</i> (asthenḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be weak, feeble, sickly</li><li>to be needy</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to be too weak to do a thing; to be unable, incapable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσθενής</i> (asthenḗs, “weak, small”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without strength, weak</li><li>feeble, sickly</li><li>sick, ill</li><li>insignificant</li><li>poor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spotted lizard, gecko</li></ol><p>Clearly a Pre-Greek word, in view of the ending in “-βος” (often found in animal names) and the several variations. Less probable is a connection with <i>σκάλοψ</i> (skálops, “mole”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of owl</li></ol><p>The suffix -φος in animal names is well known. According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) fruit shell of the artichoke</li></ol><p>From the same Pre-Greek root of synonymous <i>σκαλίας</i> (skalías).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pallet</li><li>bier</li></ol><p>The variations prove a Pre-Greek origin, rather than vulgar or popular origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter shoe with fur lining</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, it is clearly a loanword. Perhaps from Lydian, or from Pre-Greek. Furnée compares <i>ἄσκαρος</i> (áskaros, “kind of shoe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give form to some material: to beshape, fashion, form, shape, work</li><li>to adorn, decorate, embellish</li><li>to honor, revere</li><li>to practise, exercise, train (often, but not always, of athletics)</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin. Perhaps related to <i>ἀσκός</i> (askós, “skin, hide”), thus with original meaning "to prepare a skin". According to Morris, possibly borrowed from Egyptian <i>sqr</i> (“to strike, to work metal”), along with other metalworking terms used by Daedalus, such as <i>Ἱκμάλιος</i> (Hikmálios, “craftsman”) and <i>ἕντεα</i> (héntea, “armor, equipment”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small goatskin for wine or perfume</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσκός</i> (askós, “leathern bag”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δῐον</i> (-ĭ́dĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) Dropsy, ascites.</li><li>Patient suffering from ascites.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσκός</i> (askós, “wineskin; paunch, belly”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woodcock (<i>Scolopax rusticola</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root as <i>σκολόπαξ</i> (skolópax, “woodcock”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm in the intestines</li><li>larva of the <i>ἐμπίς</i> (empís)</li></ol><p>Generally taken to be from <i>ἀσκαρίζω</i> (askarízō, “to jump”), but Frisk thinks this is semantically not strong. In view of the prothetic vowel, it is rather a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unhurt, unscathed, sound, healthy, unblemished</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Apparently from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; + <i>&#42;σκῆθος</i> (&#42;skêthos, “damage”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix). Attempts have been made to connect the root with Gothic <i>𐍃𐌺𐌰𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (skaþjan), but are unconvincing as Gothic <i>𐌸</i> (þ) does not match with Ancient Greek <i>θ</i> (th) per Grimm’s law unless the θ comes from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;th₂</i>. However, the Gothic term is apparently connected to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)keh₁t-</i> (“damage, harm”), so the Greek term could ultimately be of PIE origin, albeit inherited through an intermediary source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who practices any art or trade</li><li>athlete trained for the arena</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσκέω</i> (askéō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>incident to an athlete</li><li>laborious</li><li>ascetic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσκητής</i> (askētḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swallow-wort (<i>Vincetoxicum hirundinaria</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀσκληπιός</i> (Asklēpiós) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leathern bag</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσκός</i> (askós, “wineskin”) +&lrm; <i>θῡ́λακος</i> (thū́lakos, “sack, bag”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scrip, wallet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσκός</i> (askós, “wineskin”) +&lrm; <i>πήρα</i> (pḗra, “wallet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leathern canteen</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσκός</i> (askós, “skin, hide”) +&lrm; <i>πυτίνη</i> (putínē, “flagon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, hide made into a bag</li><li>wineskin</li><li>paunch, belly</li><li>bellows</li><li>bagpipes</li></ol><p>Unknown. The variant points to foreign or Pre-Greek origin. Not related to <i>φάσκωλος</i> (pháskōlos) as per Furnée. Perhaps related to <i>ἀσκέω</i> (askéō, “to process and work (raw materials, art, etc.)”); see there for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to welcome kindly, bid welcome, greet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to take leave of</li></ol><li>to kiss, embrace, caress</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to follow eagerly, cleave to</li><li>to be glad that</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Could be related to <i>σπάω</i> (spáō, “to draw, pull”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of several types of thornbush</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>camelthorn (<i>Alhagi maurorum</i>)</li><li>spiny broom (<i>Calicotome villosa</i>)</li><li><i>Genista acanthoclada</i></li></ol></ol><p>Solmsen connected <i>σπαλύσσεται</i> (spalússetai, “to rip, agitate”) and <i>σφαλάσσειν</i> (sphalássein, “to cut, sting”), but this is semantically unconvincing. It is rather a Pre-Greek word in view of the suffix, typical of plant names. The word has also been compared with <i>παλίουρος</i> (palíouros, “Christ's thorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀσφάραγος</i> (aspháragos, “asparagus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>welcome, gladly welcomed</li><li>well-pleased, glad</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἀσπάζομαι</i> (aspázomai) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἀσπίδα</i> (aspída)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small shield</li><li>part of the prow of a ship</li><li>saffron thistle (<i>Carthamus lanatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσπίς</i> (aspís, “shield”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shield</li><li>collective, body of men-at-arms</li><li>(<i>military</i>) on the left</li><li>a round, flat bowl</li><li>a boss or knob on a door</li><li>asp, Egyptian cobra</li><li>ornament in the form of an asp</li></ol><p>For the “shield” sense, traditionally derived from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>σπίζω</i> (spízō, “to extend”). Beekes implicitly rejects this theory, and instead endorses Melchert's derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂esp-</i> (“to cut”), whence also Latin <i>asper</i> (“rough, sharp”), Hittite (ḫasp-, “to cut down”), though neither author explains why the suffix <i>-ις</i> (-is) (usually of Pre-Greek origin) should be expected to have back-formed from an <i>i</i>-stem.</p><p>The “snake” sense is most likely a semantic extension of the sense above, named after the "shield" formed by the cobra's neck when it rises up or attacks.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the dying</i>) to gasp, pant, struggle, resist</li></ol><p>The assertion that ἀ- in this verb is secondary has no basis. Rather the form without it will be secondary. From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sperH-</i> (“to kick”) together with Latin <i>spernō</i> (“to despise”), Sanskrit <i>स्फुरति</i> (sphurati, “to spurn”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;spurnaną</i> (“to kick, trample”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angler</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gladly</li><li>with glad welcome</li></ol><p><i>ἀσπάσιος</i> (aspásios) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>welcome (whose arrival is a cause of joy)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσπάζομαι</i> (aspázomai) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>vehemently, unceasingly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shield-bearing, warrior</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>aspidochelone</li></ol><p><i>ἀσπίς</i> (aspís, “shield”) +&lrm; <i>χελώνη</i> (khelṓnē, “tortoise”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) one armed with a shield, warrior</li></ol><p><i>ἀσπίς</i> (aspís, “shield”) +&lrm; <i>-ιστής</i> (-istḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) as, a Roman coin made of bronze</li><li>kind of measure of weight</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>assārius</i> (“of an as”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>courier, messenger</li></ol><p>An Iranian word, comparing Sogdian ('st'nyk, “messenger”) and Armenian <i>աստանդել</i> (astandel, “wander”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a celestial body (star, planet, and other lights in the sky such as meteors).</li><li>illustrious person</li><li>starfish</li><li>a type of songbird</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;astḗr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂stḗr</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>stēlla</i>, Gothic <i>𐍃𐍄𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌽𐍉</i> (stairnō), English <i>star</i>, Dutch <i>ster</i>, German <i>Stern</i>, Danish <i>stjerne</i>, Swedish <i>stjärna</i>, Icelandic <i>stjarna</i>, Old Armenian <i>աստղ</i> (astł), Persian ستاره (setâre) and Sanskrit <i>तारा</i> (tā́rā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiny lobster (<i>Palinurus elephas</i>)</li><li>common lobster (<i>Homarus gammarus</i>)</li><li>European crayfish (<i>Astacus astacus</i>)</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hollow of the ear</li></ol><p>Often analyzed as a derivation of <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”) but this is highly improbable. Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not torn by faction</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>στασιαστής</i> (stasiastḗs, “one who stirs up sedition”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>collective</i>) raisins; dried grapes</li></ol><p>A nominal formation in <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs) from the root of <i>στᾰφῠλή</i> (stăphŭlḗ, “bunch of grapes”), of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̓στεῖος</i> (ăsteîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little star</li><li>asterisk, the mark *</li><li>Italian aster (<i>Aster amellus</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) capsule of the poppy</li><li>small wheel with projections</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) geometrical figure shaped like a star</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr, “star”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asterism</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr, “star”) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>starlike, starry</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>starry</li></ol><p><i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the city, cosmopolitan</li><li>having polished manners; urbane, refined</li><li>elegant, witty, handsome, fair</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄστε(ϝ)</i> (áste(w)), the oblique stem of <i>ἄστυ</i> (ástu, “town”), +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p><p>For the opposition with <i>ἄγροικος</i> (ágroikos), compare typologically Latin <i>cīvīlis</i>, <i>urbānus</i> vs. <i>agrestis</i>, <i>pāgānus</i>, <i>rūsticus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comfortable saddle for an ass or a mule</li></ol><p>A connection with <i>ἀστραβής</i> (astrabḗs, “steadfast”) is improbable. According to Beekes, the variation <i>αβ/απ</i> is well-known in substrate words, so it may well be a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the vertebrae, especially of the neck</li><li>the ball of the ankle joint</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) dice, or a game played with them</li><li>a moulding in the capital of an Ionic column</li><li>black bitter vetch (<i>Lathyrus niger</i>)</li><li>a measure used by physicians</li></ol><p>Generally considered to be a derivation in "-λ-" of <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”), but this cannot be correct. According to Beekes, it is probably a Pre-Greek word. Compare <i>ὄστρακον</i> (óstrakon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small moulding, small astragal</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) name of a surgical tool used in stomatology</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀστράγαλος</i> (astrágalos, “astragal”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a plant</li><li>kind of alum</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>ἀστρᾰγᾰλωτός</i> (astrăgălōtós, “made of strung bones”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European goldfinch, Eurasian goldfinch (<i>Carduelis carduelis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀστράγαλος</i> (astrágalos, “dice”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightning</li></ol><p><i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr, “star”) +&lrm; <i>ὀπή</i> (opḗ, “sight, hole”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armillary sphere</li><li>planisphere</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄστρον</i> (ástron, “star”) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomy</li><li>astrology</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄστρον</i> (ástron, “star”) +&lrm; <i>-λογία</i> (-logía, “study”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>substantive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>astronomer</li><li>(<i>later Greek</i>) astrologer</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr, “star”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to compute, to reckon”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄστρον</i> (ástron, “star”) +&lrm; <i>νομή</i> (nomḗ, “distribution”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>astronomer</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr) +&lrm; <i>νέμω</i> (némō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>neighboring a city</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neighbor of a city</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ, “city”) +&lrm; <i>γείτων</i> (geítōn, “neighbor”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stewarding or protecting the city</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a magistrate who had the care of the police, streets, and public buildings</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inhabitant of a town or a city-state: townsman, citizen</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a native of a Greek city-state</li><li>one who has civil rights (a freeman, not a slave) but not political rights: freeman, free</li></ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the common people</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ, “town”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incommensurability</li><li>disproportionality</li><li>disharmony</li><li>asymmetry</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>συμμετρίᾱ</i> (summetríā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lack or be without understanding or comprehension</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσύνετος</i> (asúnetos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>security, surety</li><li>certainty</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσφαλής</i> (asphalḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pitch trefoil (<i>Bituminaria bituminosa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄσφαλτος</i> (ásphaltos, “asphalt, bitumen”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) throat, gullet</li><li>asparagus (<i>Asparagus officinalis</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) shoots of other plants</li></ol><p>Furnée connects the word with <i>φάραγξ</i> (pháranx, “gorge, ravine”) and <i>μάραγοι</i> (máragoi, “overhanging places”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>Being the name of a cultivated plant, yet still revealing its general meaning as “shoots” of plants, a borrowing from Old Persian <i>&#42;spargah</i> as a descendant from widely attested Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;spr̥Hgah</i> (“sprout”) can be maintained.</p><p>Beekes could, of course, declare the term of Pre-Greek origin, like its homonym, due to the presence of the variation “π/φ”—which is paralleled in Iranian in this environment—, rejecting an Indo-Europeanist connection to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)pregʰ-</i> (“to jerk, to scatter”) (whence <i>σφαραγέομαι</i> (spharagéomai, “to burst with a noise, teem”), disputed, see also Latin <i>spargere</i> (“to scatter”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἀσφαλής</i> (asphalḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἀσφαλῶς</i> (asphalôs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immovable, steadfast, unshaken</li><li>unfailing, trusty</li><li>sure, certain</li><li>safe from danger</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>σφᾰ́λλω</i> (sphắllō, “to overthrow”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>steadfastly, resolutely</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓σφᾰλής</i> (ăsphălḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of asphodel</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσφόδελος</i> (asphódelos, “asphodel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Covered in asphodel, flowery (in the phrase,&#32;<i>κατ’ ἀσφοδελὸν λειμῶνα</i> (kat’ asphodelòn leimôna))</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσφόδελος</i> (asphódelos) with shift of accent to oxytone.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stopping of the pulse</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>σφῠ́ξῐς</i> (sphŭ́xĭs, “pulse”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branched asphodel (<i>Asphodelus ramosus</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed Pre-Greek word, as is shown by the variants. Furnée further compares <i>σφονδύλιον</i> (sphondúlion), which would show pre-nasalization. A speculative attempt by Biraud, who finds the same suffix in <i>στυφελός</i> (stuphelós), <i>ζάφελος</i> (záphelos) and <i>ῥάκελος</i> (rhákelos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misshapen, ugly, deformed</li><li>unseemly, shameful, indecent, inappropriate</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) indecorous, behaving improperly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>σχῆμα</i> (skhêma, “form, shape”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giant puffball (<i>Calvatia gigantea</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Lewy, from Hebrew <i>אֶשֶׁךְ</i> (ʾéšeḵ, “testicle”), because of its similar form. He further compares <i>ὄρχις</i> (órkhis), which means both "testicle" and "salep".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be distressed, grieved</li></ol><p>Commonly derived from an original <i>&#42;ἄσχαλος</i> (&#42;áskhalos, “who cannot hold himself”), from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>σχεῖν</i> (skheîn, “to hold”) and a suffix <i>-αλο-</i>. Highly doubtful.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to engage, occupy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “a-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>σχολή</i> (skholḗ, “leisure”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>occupation, business, lack of leisure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>σχολή</i> (skholḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prodigality, profligacy</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́σωτος</i> (ắsōtos, “having no hope of safety”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lead a lavish and prodigal life</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to squander by dissolute living</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄσωτος</i> (ásōtos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full (<i>of face or body</i>)</li><li>not closing (<i>of the edges of a wound</i>)</li><li>not liable to collapse</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) not converging, not secant, asymptotic</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;nominalized,&#32;feminine) asymptote</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>συμπτωτός</i> (sumptōtós, “falling in”), from <i>συμπίπτω</i> (sumpíptō, “to fall together”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconvenient</li><li>inexpedient</li><li>useless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>σύμφορος</i> (súmphoros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) a rhetorical style without conjunctions, asyndeton</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive of <i>ἀσῠ́νδετος</i> (asŭ́ndetos, “unconnected, loose”), from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>σῠ́νδετος</i> (sŭ́ndetos, “bound hand and foot; united”), from <i>συνδέω</i> (sundéō, “bind together”), from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “bind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unconnected, loose</li><li>independent</li><li>(<i>of language</i>) without conjunctions</li><li>(<i>of style</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>σῠ́νδετος</i> (sŭ́ndetos, “bound hand and foot; united”), from <i>συνδέω</i> (sundéō, “bind together”), from <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “bind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>void of understanding, witless</li><li>not able to understand</li><li>not to be understood, unintelligible</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>σῠνετός</i> (sŭnetós, “sagacious, intelligent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of cassia mentioned in the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea</li></ol><p>Unknown loanword, but probably of Semitic origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>headstrong or perhaps foolish</li></ol><p>For the vocalism, <i>Σίσυφος</i> (Sísuphos) and <i>σέσυφος</i> (sésuphos, “knavish, roguish”) are compared. Furnée further compares the gloss <i>αἰσύφιος</i> (aisúphios, “deceptive, treacherous”), which seems to fit well. In view of the interchange <i>αι-/α-</i>, a Pre-Greek origin is probable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of pain</i>) attended with nausea</li><li>suffering from nausea, surfeited</li><li>slimy, muddy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄση</i> (ásē, “surfeit, nausea”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “like, full of”).</p><p>From <i>ἄσῐς</i> (ásĭs, “mud, slime”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “like, full of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal in weight</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “same”) +&lrm; <i>τᾰ́λᾰντον</i> (tắlănton, “weight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to skip in childish glee, gambol, frolic</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to bring up a child, foster, rear</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to grow up, wax</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀταλός</i> (atalós, “tender, delicate”) +&lrm; <i>-λλω</i> (-llō, present tense suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>but, nevertheless</li></ol><p>According to De Vaan, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ét(i)</i>. Compare Tocharian B <i>ate</i> (“away”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Aeolic,&#32;Ionic) an act of recklessness</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Aeolic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;of people or actions) reckless, not caring about consequences, foolish, ungovernable</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exemption from public burdens, immunity</li><li>incompleteness, imperfection</li><li>(being) without payment, gratis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “tax”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia). See also ἀτελής (atelḗs, “without end, never-ending”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to maltreat, afflict</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be bereft or cheated of a thing</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hard, unsoftened</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) stubborn, unfeeling, merciless</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indifferent, careless</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ταλαίπωρος</i> (talaípōros, “suffering, miserable”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of youthful persons</i>) tender, delicate</li><li>(<i>of elders</i>) tremulous, shaking, trembling</li></ol><p>Leumann derives this adjective from <i>ἀταλάφρων</i> (ataláphrōn, “tender-minded”), which is the negation of <i>ταλάφρων</i> (taláphrōn, “patient of mind”). Although it explains the compositional vowel <i>α-</i>, the whole analysis is too complicated. Derivation from <i>ἄττα</i> (átta, “father”) seems improbable too. Furnée compares <i>ἀζαλαί</i> (azalaí, “young and tender”) and concludes that this is a Pre-Greek word, which is quite probable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unstorable, unregulatable</li><li>uncontrolled, inordinate</li><li>not requiring economization</li><li>prodigal, lavish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ταμιεύω</i> (tamieúō, “to economize”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, “-able/-ed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) Indiscipline.</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) Disorder, confusion.</li><li>Irregularity.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄτακτος</i> (átaktos, “undisciplined, disorderly”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, “nominal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tranquility, equanimity, inner peace</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀτάρακτος</i> (atáraktos) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía). Compare <i>τάραξις</i> (táraxis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act recklessly, wickedly, or without regard for consequences</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀτάσθαλος</i> (atásthalos, “reckless”) +&lrm; <i>-λλω</i> (-llō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(in Homer,&#32;always&#32;in the plural) criminal folly, recklessness, heedlessness, wickedness</li><li>deeds characterized by this quality: wicked deeds</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀτάσθαλος</i> (atásthalos, “foolish”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ᾰ̓τᾰσθᾰλῐ́ᾱ</i> (ătăsthălĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwalled, unfortified</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>τειχῐ́ζω</i> (teikhĭ́zō, to wall) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, verbal adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indestructible, durable</li><li>indefatigable</li><li>stubborn</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “to rub”) +&lrm; <i>τείρω</i> (teírō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs), thus literally ‘unrubbable’</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without end</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>not brought to an end or issue, unaccomplished</li><li>incomplete, unfinished</li><li>inchoate, imperfect</li><li>never-ending, endless</li></ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) not bringing to an end, not accomplishing one's purpose</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>not giving accomplishment to a thing</li></ol><li>free from tax or tribute, scot-free</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) untaxed, tax-free</li><li>(<i>of sums</i>) without charge or deduction, net, clear</li><li>not costly</li></ol><li>uninitiated</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “end”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfinished</li><li>incomplete</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>τελέω</i> (teléō, “to complete”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to gaze intently, to fixate one's eyes (upon), to stare</li><li>to be fixated on, to focus on, to concentrate on</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀτενής</i> (atenḗs, “intent”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, denominative verb suffix), from <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to strain, to exert”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unpleasing</li><li>joyless; melancholy</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τέρπω</i> (térpō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be unskilled, to lack talent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄτεχνος</i> (átekhnos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dishonour, hold in dishonour</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, alpha privative) +&lrm; <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honour”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dishonor, disgrace</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>at Athens, atimy, the loss of civil rights, infamy</li></ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) ragged, sorry</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honor”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rear, tend, also of animals</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to cherish</li><li>(<i>in bad sense</i>) to beguile, cajole</li></ol><p>Reduplicated form of <i>ἀτάλλω</i> (atállō, “to gambol”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>servant, slave</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vapor, steam, smoke</li><li>odor</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ἀετμός</i> (&#42;aetmós) (only attested in an inflected gloss <i>ἀετμόν</i> (aetmón, “wind”)), of uncertain origin. Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂weh₁-</i> (“to blow”), and connected with <i>ἄημι</i> (áēmi, “to breathe, blow”) (as well as <i>ἀϋτμή</i> (aütmḗ, “breath, scent”), which is now considered unrelated); however, this leaves the vocalism unexplained. Despite superficially similar semantics and phonetics, not from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eh₁t-</i> (“to breathe”) (whence German <i>Atem</i> (“breath”), Sanskrit <i>आत्मन्</i> (ātmán, “breath; soul”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>slackness, enervation, debility</li><li>laziness</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) invalidity, ineffectiveness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄτονος</i> (átonos, “not stretched, relaxed”) (from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τόνος</i> (tónos)) + <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>extraordinariness, uncommonness</li></ol><p><i>ἄτοπος</i> (átopos, “out-of-place”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, “-ness”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orach (<i>Atriplex rosea</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beeks, a word of Pre-Greek origin, as is shown by the variation <i>δ/τ</i> and the prenasalization present in the alternative forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unmovingly, still</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τρέμω</i> (trémō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saffron thistle (<i>Carthamus lanatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄτρακτος</i> (átraktos, “spindle”), because this plant was used for making spindles.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>accurately, precisely</li><li>truly</li></ol><p><i>ἀτρεκής</i> (atrekḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>precise, exact, strict</li><li>sure, certain</li></ol><p>Unknown. The analysis as a compound of privative <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) and an <i>s-</i>stem <i>&#42;τρέκος</i> (&#42;trékos), in the sense “undistorted”, finds no further support: connection with the root of Latin <i>torqueō</i> (“to twist, wind, wry”) is impossible, because a reflex of the labiovelar would be lacking.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of food or nourishment</li><li>atrophy</li><li>starvation diet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τροφή</i> (trophḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) traditionally said to mean barren, unharvestable (<i>epithet of the sea and the sky</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally said to be <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τρυγάω</i> (trugáō, “to harvest”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos), but Beekes calls this unlikely (it would produce Ionic ἀτρύγητος) and instead prefers the suggestion by Vine of un-dry-able.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crumb, morsel of bread</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>locust</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix <i>-βος</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀτταγᾶς</i> (attagâs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black francolin (<i>Francolinus francolinus</i>)</li></ol><p>Unexplained. It is called onomatopoeic by Claudius Aelianus after its cry. It could be a Pre-Greek word, in view of the suffix of the variant <i>ἀτταγήν</i> (attagḗn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to side or ally with the Athenians</li><li>to speak or write in Attic Greek, the Attic dialect of Ancient Greek</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀττικός</i> (Attikós) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a siding with Athens, attachment to the Athenian party, loyalty to Athens, Atticism</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) employment of Attic language or style, (an) Attic expression, (an) Atticism</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ττικῐ́ζω</i> (ăttikĭ́zō, “to Atticize”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the Attic dialect</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀττικός</i> (Attikós) +&lrm; <i>-ιστί</i> (-istí).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἀττικιστί)</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>ἀττικιστί</i> (attikistí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not ruled by tyrants, free from tyrants</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>τῠρᾰννεύω</i> (tŭrănneúō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be unlucky or unfortunate, fail, miscarry</li><li>to fail to a get or gain (<i>+ genitive</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀτυχής</i> (atukhḗs) (from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τύχη</i> (túkhē)) + <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfortunate</li><li>missing, without share in</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>τύχη</i> (túkhē, “luck, fortune”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be distraught from fear, bewildered</li><li>to be distraught with grief</li><li>to be amazed at a thing</li></ol><p>The comparison with Hittite (ḫatuki-, “terrible”) and Sanskrit <i>तुज्य</i> (tujya, “to be pushed, impelled”) convinces both formally and semantically, suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂tug-</i> (“to be terrified”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓πό</i> (ăpó) used before vowel with rough breathing</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vetch (<i>Vicia sativa</i>)</li><li>dandelion (<i>Taraxacum officinale</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably a substrate word related to <i>φακός</i> (phakós, “lentil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hybrid strawberry tree (<i>Arbutus</i> × <i>andrachnoides</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably of Pre-Greek origin. The derivation from <i>ἀπό</i> (apó, “from”) + <i>ἄρκυς</i> (árkus, “net”) suggested by Strömberg is probably just folk-etymological.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag off</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “to drag”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lesser celandine (<i>Ficaria verna</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the connection with <i>ἀφιέναι</i> (aphiénai, “flowers”) proposed by Theophrastus is unsustainable. Krahe connects the word with Latin <i>apium</i> (“parsley”), but since these are quite different plants this hypothesis is quite doubtful. Furnée, on the other hand, proposes a connection with <i>ἄφρισσα</i> (áphrissa, “dragonwort”) suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send forth, discharge, emit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to let fall from one's grasp</li><li>(<i>in prose</i>) to send forth, dispatch</li><li>to give up or hand over</li></ol><li>to send away</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of people</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to let go, loose, set free</li><li>to let go, dissolve, disband, break up, dismiss</li><li>to put away, divorce</li><li>to let go as an <i>ἄφετος</i> (áphetos)</li></ol><li>(<i>of things</i>) to get rid of, shed, give up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(with <i>πλοῖον</i> (ploîon)) to set sail</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to remit, excuse</li></ol></ol><li>to leave alone, pass by, not notice</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative and infitive</i>) to donate to public property</li></ol><li>(<i>with accusative and infinitive</i>) to allow, let, permit</li><li>(<i>seemingly intransitive</i>) to break up, march, sail</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to give up doing</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to send forth from oneself</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to loose something of one's own</li><li>(often in Attic,&#32;with genitive) to let go of</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀφ-</i> (aph-, “from, away from”) + <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi, “to send, throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand off or away</li><li>to depart</li><li>to weigh out</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to demand payment for</li><li>to relinquish claim to [+ genitive]</li><li>to revolt from [+ genitive]</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Taking away, carrying off, removal; putting off.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>law</i>) Assertion of freedom of a reputed slave.</li><li>Amputation.</li></ol><li>(<i>logic</i>) Abstraction; mathematics.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>grammar</i>) Removal of initial letters.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀφαιρέω</i> (aphairéō, “I take away”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, nominal suffix), from <i>ἀφ’</i> (aph’, apocopic form of <i>ἀπό</i> (apó, “from”)) +&lrm; <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō, “I take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take away [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>, <i>along with</i> genitive <i>or</i> dative <small>‘from someone’</small>; <i>or</i> <i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to subtract</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to deprive of [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>to prevent someone [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘from doing’</small>]</li><li>with <i>εἰς ἐλευθερίᾱν</i> (eis eleutheríān): to free someone</li><li>to claim</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be robbed; to have something taken, to be deprived of [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō, “take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ablative (case)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for taking away</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) retrograde</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓φαιρέω</i> (ăphairéō, “I take away from”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for taking away</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) retrograde</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓φαιρέω</i> (ăphairéō, “I take away from”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to miss the mark</li><li>to be deprived of</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἁμαρτάνω</i> (hamartánō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἀφανής</i> (aphanḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unseen, invisible</li><li>unnoticed, secret, not manifest</li><li>unknown, uncertain, obscure</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>φαίνομαι</i> (phaínomai) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make unseen, hide, hush up, lose sight of, reject, make away with a person</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>do away with, remove, carry one off</li><li>to destroy</li><li>to obliterate writing</li><li>to secrete, steal</li><li>to obscure, mar one's good name</li></ol><li>to spoil</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to make away with property</li><li>to drain a cup of wine</li></ol><li>to disappear, be missing</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be lost at sea</li></ol><li>to live retired</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀφανής</i> (aphanḗs, “unseen”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, verb forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speechlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>φατός</i> (phatós) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak, feeble, powerless</li></ol><p>Unknown. Furnée compares <i>φαῦρος</i> (phaûros, “light”), <i>φλαῦρος</i> (phlaûros, “indifferent, bad”) and <i>φαῦλος</i> (phaûlos, “insignificant”). Beekes suggests that <i>ἀμαυρός</i> (amaurós, “dark”) could also be a cognate, believing that this word is of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toilet, latrine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>ἑδρών</i> (hedrṓn, “seat”). The latter from <i>ἕδρα</i> (hédra) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>artless, plain, simple</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with negative connotations</i>) bold, brazen</li></ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) simple, not intricate or involved</li></ol><p>Adjectival formation from <i>ἄφειλον</i> (ápheilon), the aorist active infinitive of <i>ἀφαιρέω</i> (aphairéō, “to take away or deprive”), a suppletive verb; see <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō) for more. Despite an earlier proposal by Persson as having original sense "without a stone; even", which was accepted so wholeheartedly that the LSJ dictionary lists it as the main definition, not from <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “a-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>φελλεύς</i> (phelleús, “stony terrain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>incorruption, immortality, perpetuity</li><li>incorruptibility, indestructibility, imperishability</li><li>incorruptness, purity, integrity, sincerity</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>φθαρσία</i> (phtharsía), equivalent to <i>ἄφθαρτος</i> (áphthartos, “indestructible, immortal, eternal”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to arrive at, have come to</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἱκνέομαι</i> (hiknéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arrive, come</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) +&lrm; <i>ἱκνέομαι</i> (hiknéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἀφῑκόμεθα</i> (aphīkómetha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rich; wealthy; affluent</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́φενος</i> (ắphenos) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark off boundary</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to mark off for oneself</li><li>to border on</li><li>to determine, define</li><li>to separate, distinguish</li><li>to finish</li><li>to grant as a special gift</li><li>(<i>with accusative of person</i>) to set apart</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to banish</li><li>to separate</li><li>to cast out, excommunicate</li><li>to appoint, ordain</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “off”) +&lrm; <i>ὁρῐ́ζω</i> (horĭ́zō, “separate”), from <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “boundary”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>limitation</li><li>separation, distinction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>definition</li><li>aphorism</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀφορίζω</i> (aphorízō, “to mark off, separate”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starting point, origin, occasion, pretext</li><li>base of operations</li><li>place of safety</li><li>resources</li><li>(<i>banking</i>) capital</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>ὁρμή</i> (hormḗ, “attack, rush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not subjected to tribute, exempt from tribute</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “a-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>φορολογέω</i> (phorologéō, “to levy tribute from”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) synonym of <i>ἀθέρας</i> (athéras)</li></ol><p>Tentatively compared to Sanskrit <i>अभ्रि</i> (ábhri, “hoe, pickaxe”). Unrelated to <i>ἀφρός</i> (aphrós, “foam”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be foolish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄφρων</i> (áphrōn) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>senselessly, recklessly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀφραδής</i> (aphradḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insensate, reckless</li><li>(<i>of the dead</i>) senseless, lifeless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>φράζομαι</i> (phrázomai, “to think, perceive, observe”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the plural) folly, thoughtlessness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀφραδής</i> (aphradḗs, “senseless”) (from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>φραδής</i> (phradḗs)) + <i>-ίη</i> (-íē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἄφρακτος</i> (áphraktos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἄφρακτος</i> (áphraktos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>aphrodisiac</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀφροδίτη</i> (Aphrodítē) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>folly, thoughtlessness, meaninglessness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄφρων</i> (áphrōn) +&lrm; <i>-σύνη</i> (-súnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foam, of the sea</li><li>(<i>of persons and animals</i>) foam, slaver, froth</li></ol><p>Formally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥bʰrós</i> (“rain-cloud, rain, cloud”), with cognates including Sanskrit <i>अभ्र</i> (abhrá), Old Armenian <i>ամբ</i> (amb), Latin <i>imber</i>. However, Beekes and Frisk argue that the semantic mismatch does not justify this derivation. See also possibly related but also problematic <i>ὄμβρος</i> (ómbros, “rain-water; thunder-storm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wake</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>ὕπνος</i> (húpnos, “sleep”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mud and rubbish which a stream carries with it</li></ol><p>Unknown. Nicander did not understand the meaning any longer and connected it with <i>ἀφύσσω</i> (aphússō, “to draw or scoop liquids”). Formation like <i>συρφετός</i> (surphetós, “sweepings, refuse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speechlessness</li><li>mispronunciation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄφωνος</i> (áphōnos, “voiceless”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā) and/or <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>-φωνίᾱ</i> (-phōníā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small fry of various fish</li></ol><p>A derivation from <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “to grow”) seems folk-etymological, but is defended by Meier-Brügger, suggesting Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-bʰúH-o-</i> (“without growth”), which is a recent formation compared with Sanskrit <i>अभ्व</i> (abhva, “monster”). However, DELG keeps open the possibility of a substrate word.</p><p>The irregular genitive plural <i>ᾰ̓φῠ́ων</i> (ăphŭ́ōn) is due to the influence of third-declension <i>ᾰ̓φῠής</i> (ăphŭḗs, “germinating late”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw liquids, decant, especially from a larger vessel with a smaller</li><li>to sound, probe</li><li>to tap, draw for oneself, help oneself to</li></ol><p>Ohler explained the form as <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-) + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ews-</i> (“to draw liquids, scoop”), the same root of Latin <i>hauriō</i> (“to draw, drain”), which is quite possible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a Persian measure</li><li>chest, box</li></ol><p>Hemmerdinger compares Egyptian <i>hnw</i> (“jar, unit of measure”), while Furnée compares Hittite <i>𒀀𒂵𒀭𒉌</i> (a-ga-an-ni /⁠aganni⁠/, “bowl”), Hurrian <i>𒀀𒂵𒀭𒉌</i> (a-ga-an-ni /⁠aganni⁠/, “bowl”), which is connected to Akkadian <i>𒀀𒂵𒉡</i> (a-ga-nu /⁠agannu⁠/, “large bowl, cauldron”). He also compares <i>ἀγάννα</i> (agánna, “sacred chariot”) and <i>λαχάννα</i> (lakhánna). Perhaps it is from Persian, but according to Beekes no solution can be offered.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἀκάνθιον</i> (akánthion, “cotton thistle”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ungracious, unpleasant, unpleasing</li><li>without grace or charms</li><li>unfavourable</li><li>ungrateful, thankless, unthankful [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> <b>πρός</b> (prós, + accusative)]</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;feminine) remedy, antidote</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>χαρίζω</i> (kharízō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) agate</li></ol><p>Loaned from a non-Indo-European source, possibly Semitic (compare Aramaic <i>עקיק</i>). The river Ἀχάτης in Sicily is probably named after the mineral.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἀχεύω</i> (akheúō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felty germander (<i>Teucrium polium</i>)</li><li>antique spurge (<i>Euphorbia antiquorum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀχαιμένης</i> (Akhaiménēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white poplar (<i>Populus alba</i>)</li></ol><p>A parallelism of the ending <i>-ωΐς</i> with Lithuanian <i>úosis</i> (“ash tree”) is highly unlikely, while a derivation from <i>Ἀχέρων</i> (Akhérōn) is probably just folk-etymological. According to Beekes, the stem of the word rather suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grieve, mourn</li><li>(<i>later</i>) to lament</li><li>to annoy, vex</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ἄχος</i> (ákhos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mist</li><li>darkness, dimness</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eǵʰlu-</i> (“mist, dark weather”). Cognate with Old Prussian <i>aglo</i> (“rain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of wood</i>) made of wild pear</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀχράς</i> (akhrás, “wild pear”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild pear (<i>Pyrus amygdaliformis</i>)</li></ol><p>Connected to <i>ἄχερδος</i> (ákherdos, “wild pear”). According to Beekes, a Pre-Greek origin is exposed by the alternation <i>ἀ-χερδ-</i> beside <i>ἀ-χραδ-</i>. While the alternation can also be understood in Indo-European terms, the word for “pear” must be suspect of loanword origin due to the Caucasian heimat of the plant, and indeed it appears reflected in, for instance, Ossetian <i>кӕрдо</i> (kærdo, “pear”), and thence even as far as Kipchak كرتما (kärtmä, “pear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κόχλιας</i> (kókhlias, “snail with spiral shell”), as they say in Tarentum.</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variation οι/υ proves a Pre-Greek etymology, as well as the suffix "-μυλα".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useless, unprofitable</li><li>unfit, unserviceable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>χρεία</i> (khreía, “need; use”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moneylessness; poverty</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>χρῆμᾰ</i> (khrêmă) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A chaff holder</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄχυρον</i> (ákhuron, “chaff”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storehouse for chaff</li></ol><p><i>ἄχῠρον</i> (ákhŭron, “chaff”) +&lrm; <i>-ών</i> (-ṓn, “place suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fed by chaff</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄχυρον</i> (ákhuron, “chaff”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἠχώ</i> (ēkhṓ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>flavoured with wormwood</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀψίνθιον</i> (apsínthion, “wormwood”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wormwood (<i>Artemisia absinthium</i>)</li><li>vermouth, wine spiced by wormwood</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the cluster -ινθ- (-inth-) suggests a Pre-Greek source, as seen in <i>τέρμινθος</i> (términthos, “terebinth”), <i>ἐρέβινθος</i> (erébinthos, “chickpea”), <i>μίνθη</i> (mínthē, “mint”), <i>ὑάκινθος</i> (huákinthos, “hyacinth”), <i>πλίνθος</i> (plínthos, “brick”), <i>μήρινθος</i> (mḗrinthos, “cord, line”), <i>κήρινθος</i> (kḗrinthos, “bee bread”), and <i>λαβύρινθος</i> (labúrinthos, “labyrinth”). Also, the variant <i>ἀσπίνθιον</i> (aspínthion) with /sp/ instead of /ps/ may point to borrowing from Pre-Greek. Related to the source of Old Armenian <i>աւշինդր</i> (awšindr, “wormwood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>truthful, without lying</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ψευδής</i> (pseudḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(attributive,&#32;"of wormwood",&#32;<b>ἀψῐνθῑ́της <i>(</i>οἶνος<i>)</b></i>) wine prepared with wormwood, vermouth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀψῐ́νθῐον</i> (apsĭ́nthĭon, “wormwood”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invisible</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥widḗs</i>, from <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see”); see there its many cognates. Compare <i>Ἀΐδης</i> (Aḯdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) unseen, obscure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, alpha privative) +&lrm; <i>εἶδον</i> (eîdon, “to see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) a rush; a blur; impetus (rapid motion)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓ῑ̈́σσω</i> (āī̈́ssō).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>immateriality</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́ῡ̈λος</i> (ắṻlos, “immaterial”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, suffix forming feminine abstract nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loud cry</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>αὔω</i> (aúō, “to shout, call”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breath; scent</li><li>vapor</li></ol><p>Traditionally considered a derivative of <i>ἄημι</i> (áēmi, “to breathe, blow”), but there are formal issues. Fritz instead derives the word from the zero-grade <i>&#42;h₂sut-</i> of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂sewt-</i> (“to roil, seethe”), whence also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;seuþaną</i> (“to seethe, boil”) and Gothic <i>𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃</i> (sauþs, “sacrifice”) (&lt; o-grade <i>&#42;h₂sowt-</i>); this derivation is preferred by Beekes for phonetic and semantic soundness.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unseen, invisible</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ὁρᾱτός</i> (horātós, “seen”), verbal adjective of <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indefinite, indeterminate</li><li>without boundaries, debatable</li><li>the phrase <i>ὁ ἀόριστος (χρόνος (khrónos))</i> (ho aóristos (khrónos (khrónos))): the aorist tense</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ὁρῐστός</i> (horĭstós, “limited”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἕωθεν</i> (héōthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>The <i>alpha copulativum</i>, used to express union or likeness.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hə-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm̥-</i>, zero-grade of <i>&#42;sem-</i>. The form without the rough breathing is due to Grassmann's law or analogy.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luxurious living, bon vivant</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔βρός</i> (hăbrós, “delicate”) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́αιτᾰ</i> (dĭ́aită, “way of life”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living delicately, effete</li><li>luxurious</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔βρός</i> (hăbrós, “delicate”) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́αιτᾰ</i> (dĭ́aită, “way of life”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>graceful, delicate, pretty (<i>especially of the body</i>)</li><li>splendid (<i>of things</i>)</li><li>graceful, pretty (<i>of style</i>)</li><li>dainty, luxurious (<i>frequently with a notion of disparagement</i>); hence, delicate (<i>a common epithet of Asiatics</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>gracefully, delicately</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔βρός</i> (hăbrós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hallow, make sacred, especially by burning a sacrifice</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́γῐος</i> (hắgĭos, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>ἁγίζω</i> (hagízō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́γῐος</i> (hắgĭos, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consecration, sanctification, holiness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁγιάζω</i> (hagiázō, “I sanctify, purify”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holiness; sanctity</li><li>holiness (title)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅγιος</i> (hágios, “holy, sacred”) +&lrm; <i>-σύνη</i> (-súnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holiness; sanctity</li><li>holiness (title)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́γῐος</i> (hắgĭos, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make pure, to purify, cleanse away, especially by water</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hallow the dead by fire</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to burn up, destroy</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἁγνός</i> (hagnós, “sacred, pure”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purity; chastity</li><li>purification; cleansing</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔γνός</i> (hăgnós, “pure; holy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider as part religion, piety, purity</li><li>to purify</li><li>to be pure</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to keep oneself pure</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἁγνός</i> (hagnós) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purification, expiation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁγνίζω</i> (hagnízō, “to purify, cleanse”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure; chaste</li><li>holy; sacred</li><li>purifying</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hagnós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁yáǵ-nós</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁yaǵ-</i> (“to sacrifice; to worship”). Cognate with Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hyaȷ́nás</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purity; chastity</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔γνός</i> (hăgnós, “sacred; pure”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, abstract noun suffix). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>यज्ञतति</i> (yajñatati, “performance of a sacrifice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓δήν</i> (ădḗn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thick, close, densely packed</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἅδην</i> (hádēn, “to one's fill”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνός</i> (-ĭnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full-grown, grown up, adult</li><li>stout</li></ol><p>Stem ἁδ- as in the root of adverb <i>ἅδην</i> (hádēn), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂-</i> (“to fill up, satiate, satisfy”) + <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>ἀθροίζω</i> (athroízō, “gather, collect”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓θρόος</i> (ăthróos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>thronged, crowded, in a mass</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;həwəlḗs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm̥-wl̥-ḗs</i> from <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to push, press”). Compare <i>ἀολλέες</i> (aollées).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly of people (in Doric states, answering to Attic <i>ἐκκλησία</i> (ekklēsía))</li><li>meeting, assembly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to push, press”) (whence <i>εἴλω</i> (eílō, “to shut in, press”), <i>ῑ̓́λη</i> (ī́lē, “band, troop”) and many others).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dipped in sea, drowned in the sea</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a purple bird</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́λς</i> (hắls) +&lrm; <i>βᾰπτός</i> (băptós), verbal adjective of <i>βᾰ́πτω</i> (bắptō, “to dip, to dye, to baptize”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swallowed by the sea</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅλς</i> (háls) +&lrm; <i>βρωτός</i> (brōtós), verbal adjective of <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “to eat, to eat up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to gather, assemble, meet, collect</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make salty; to salt or salinate</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̔λής</i> (hālḗs, “thronged, crowded”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to push, press”).</p><p>From <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “salt”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be captured</li><li>to be convicted and condemned</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₃-</i> (“to catch, seize, strike”) +&lrm; <i>-σκομαι</i> (-skomai). The aspiration was probably taken from <i>αἱρεῖν</i> (haireîn) and/or <i>ἑλεῖν</i> (heleîn). The aorist forms reflect the stem <i>*wḷh₃eh₁-</i>, where the passive marker <i>-η-</i> appears as <i>-ω-</i> as a result of the preceding laryngeal's coloring effect. Cognates include Latin <i>vellō</i> (“to pluck, demolish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uncertain: probably osprey (<i>Pandion haliaetus</i>) or white-tailed eagle (<i>Haliaeetus albicilla</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́λς</i> (hắls, “salt, sea”) +&lrm; <i>ᾱ̓ετός</i> (āetós, “eagle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Poetic form of <i>ἁλῐᾱ́ετος</i> (halĭā́etos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sea-tossed</li></ol><p><i>ἅλς</i> (háls) +&lrm; <i>δῑνέω</i> (dīnéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁλιεύω</i> (halieúō, “I fish, am a fisher”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisher, fisherman</li><li>seaman, sailor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “salt, sea”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix for person concerned).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a fisher</li><li>to fish, go fishing</li></ol><p>Denominal verb of <i>ἁλιεύς</i> (halieús, “fisher”); also analyzable as <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “saltwater, seawater”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>winter cherry (<i>Alkekengi officinarum</i>, syn. <i>Physalis alkekengi</i>)</li><li>ashwagandha (<i>Withania somnifera</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ᾰ̔́λς</i> (hắls, “salt”) +&lrm; <i>κᾰκκᾰ́βη</i> (kăkkắbē, “three-legged pot; partridge”), because of the shape of its fruits.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of sea-purple, of true purple dye</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “salt; sea”) +&lrm; <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra, “purple-fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἀλκυονίς</i> (alkuonís)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἀλκυονίς</i> (alkuonís)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁλκυών</i> (halkuṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt humour, salt scum</li><li>salt soil or land</li><li>saltiness of water</li><li>kind of cabbage (<i>Brassica cretica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁλμυρός</i> (halmurós, “salty”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>salty</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅλμ(η)</i> (hálm(ē), “saltwater”) +&lrm; <i>-υρός</i> (-urós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saltbox</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “salt”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sea-purple (<i>genuine purple, as opposed to imitation purple</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρδω</i> (érdō, “make”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix), literally "wrought in or by the sea."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple robe</li><li>(attributive,&#32;with <b>ἐσθής</b>,&#32;false reading in Lucian, <i>The Ship</i> 22) purple robe</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁλουργής</i> (halourgḗs, “sea-purple”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) dumbbells</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́λλομαι</i> (hắllomai, “to jump”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of fig found in Crete</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a failure, fault, sin</li><li>a bodily defect, malady</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἁμαρτάνω</i> (hamartánō, “I miss, fail, am deprived of (a thing), fail to do, sin”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, “instance of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a tree with fruit like the pear, a kind of medlar (<i>Mespilus</i>, now <i>Crataegus</i> sect. <i>Mespilus</i>) or service tree (<i>Sorbus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̔́μᾰ</i> (hắmă, “at the same time [with]”) +&lrm; <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”) = “[which blossoms] at the same time [as the] apple [tree]”</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wagoner</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅμαξα</i> (hámaxa, “wagon”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō, “to drive”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to miss, miss the mark, especially of a thrown spear [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to fail at, miss one's point, go wrong in [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to be deprived of, to lose [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;transitive</i>) to fail to do, neglect [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something’</small>]</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fail, do wrong, err, sin</li></ol><p>Although there are no known cognates outside of Greek, Beekes nonetheless reconstructs a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂mert-</i> (“to miss, fail”) as the ancestor of this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fault, negligence, an error</li><li>a copyist's error</li><li>(<i>religion</i>) a sin, transgression</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔μᾰρτᾰ́νω</i> (hămărtắnō, “to miss”) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás, “a noun-forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together, at the same time, at once</li></ol><p>Probably from the instrumental of an otherwise unattested adjective <i>&#42;ἁμαρτος</i> (&#42;hamartos), itself from <i>ἅμα</i> (háma) + <i>&#42;h₂er-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>error, mistake, fault, failure</li><li>(religion,&#32;philosophy) guilt, sin</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἁμαρτάνω</i> (hamartánō, “to miss, fail”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>erroneous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sinner</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔μαρτᾰ́νω</i> (hămartắnō, “to miss”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>From some place or time</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁμός</i> (hamós, “something”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, ablative adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) contraction of <i>ὁ</i> (ho) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νήρ</i> (ănḗr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to please to delight, to gratify [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(in Herodotus,&#32;of the opinion of a group of people,&#32;transitive) to be thought, decided, chosen [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘by someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swh₂-n-d-</i>, a nasal-infixed present (ie. containing a <b>N</b> progressive aspect marker) from the root <i>&#42;sweh₂d-</i> (“sweet”), from which also <i>ἡδύς</i> (hēdús, “sweet”) and <i>ἥδομαι</i> (hḗdomai, “enjoy”). A similarly formed present stem is <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>soft to the touch, tender</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) delicate, gentle</li><li>(<i>in bad sense</i>) soft, weak</li></ol><p>Unknown, though of similar formation to <i>ὁμᾰλός</i> (homălós, “even, level”) and <i>ἀτᾰλός</i> (atălós, “tender, shaking”). Furnée prefers to compare the term with <i>ἀμᾰλός</i> (amălós, “soft, bland”), assuming variation <i>μ/π</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ᾰ̔πλόος</i> (hăplóos)</li></ol><p>Contracted form of <i>ᾰ̔πλόος</i> (hăplóos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>single; onefold</li><li>simple</li><li>honest, candid</li><li>simple-minded</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) unalloyed; pure</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔-</i> (hă-, “one, same”) +&lrm; <i>-πλόος</i> (-plóos, “-fold”). Compare <i>δῐπλόος</i> (dĭplóos, “double”), as well as Latin <i>simplex</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make single, unfold, spread out</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be simple</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to make plain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁπλόος</i> (haplóos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A covered chariot.</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ᾰ̔́ρμᾰ</i> (hắrmă, “chariot”) + <i>ᾰ̔́μαξᾰ</i> (hắmaxă, “waggon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harmal (<i>Peganum harmala</i>)</li></ol><p>A loanword from a Semitic source. Compare Akkadian <i>𒌑𒀀𒈾𒈨𒊒</i> (anamiru, “medical and ritual herb”) and Hebrew <i>אַלְמָן</i> (ʾalmān).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chariot-racing</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́ρμᾰ</i> (hắrmă, “chariot”) +&lrm; <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos, “road, street”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joining, junction, fitting, arrangement</li><li>joint in masonry</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) joining of two bones without motion</li><li>(<i>in music</i>) method of tuning a stringed instrument</li><li>(<i>in painting</i>) gradation of tints in transition</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁρμόζω</i> (harmózō, “to fit together, join”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fitting together, joint</li><li>union, league</li><li>ordinance, decree</li><li>a relation of sounds: harmony</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ἁρμων</i> (&#42;harmōn) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to join, fit, fix together”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἁρμονία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Harmonia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>skilled in music</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁρμονίᾱ</i> (harmoníā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fit together; join (for the purpose of construction, planks)</li><li>to join together (words) (<i>of a poet</i>)</li><li>to betroth (a girl to a man, a man to a woman)</li><li>to apply (good order or discipline); to govern (a people); (<i>of a Spartan</i>) to be a harmost or governor (in a subject city), be ruled (by laws) be disciplined (by a teacher)</li><li>to arrange, command</li><li>to compose</li><li>to harmonise (<i>with each other</i>), be brought into harmony or attunement (<i>of a soul, with itself</i>)</li><li>to fit, suit, be adapted well for</li><li>(<i>of a musician</i>) to tune (a certain musical mode)</li><li>to tune (a lyre)</li><li>to attune (the harmony of one's body)</li><li>(<i>of a bird</i>) to correspond (<i>to a god; of predictions, to later events</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;armóďďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to join, fit, fix together”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) joint</li><li>(<i>masonry</i>) joint</li><li>link</li><li>bolt, peg</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂(e)rmós</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit together, join”), whence also <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “join, fit together”). Compare Sanskrit <i>ईर्म</i> (īrmá, “arm, forequarter”), Latin <i>armus</i> (“shoulder, forequarter”), Old Prussian <i>irmo</i> (“arm”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;armaz</i> (“arm”) (> English <i>arm</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hook</li><li>grappling iron</li><li>rake</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō, “to seize”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch away, carry off</li><li>to seize hastily, snatch up</li><li>to seize, overpower</li><li>to plunder</li></ol><p>Disputed. Suggested Proto-Indo-European roots include <i>&#42;h₁rep-</i> (“to snatch”) (compare <i>ἐρέπτομαι</i> (eréptomai) and Latin <i>rapiō</i> (“to snatch, steal, rape”)) and <i>&#42;serp-</i> (compare <i>ἅρπη</i> (hárpē, “bird of prey”), Latin <i>sarpō</i>, Hittite <i>𒊬𒉺</i> (šarpa-, “farming tool”)). But, as Beekes notes, neither of these would be expected to yield the form ἁρπ-. Alternatively, Pre-Greek; the underlying -αγ- suffix (see Related terms below) further complicates a Proto-Indo-European origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-boot</li></ol><p>According to Hesychius, the word is Laconian. The presence of the variant <i>ῥαπίς</i> (rhapís) suggests a Pre-Greek origin of this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ᾰ̔́ρπᾰξ</i> (hắrpăx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ᾰ̔́ρπᾰξ</i> (hắrpăx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handball</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἁρπᾰστός</i> (harpăstós), from <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighting, kindling</li><li>touch, sense of touch</li><li>(<i>in wrestling</i>) grip</li><li>sand sprinkled over wrestlers, to enable them to get a grip of one another</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) point of contact, intersection</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) stripes, strokes</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) infection, especially of leprosy</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) junction, point of contact in the body, ligament</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅπτω</i> (háptō, “to kindle, set fire; to touch; to fasten”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net; mesh</li><li>wheel; hoop; disc</li><li>any curved form: bow; arch; shield; vault</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́πτω</i> (hắptō, “to fasten to”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), with compensatory lengthening of the final vowel <i>&#42;ᾰ̔ψσῐ́ς</i> (&#42;hăpssĭ́s) &gt; <i>ᾰ̔ψῑ́ς</i> (hăpsī́s).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untouchable; invincible</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̔́πτω</i> (hắptō, “to touch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) wheel; shout</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The meaning “shout” possibly related to <i>αὔω</i> (aúō, “to shout, call”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unlucky</li><li>blameless</li><li>irrelevant</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>board, slab</li><li>reckoning board, abacus</li></ol><p>Usually suggested to be of Semitic origin, such as Phoenician or Biblical Hebrew <i>אָבָק</i> (ʔɔḇɔq, “dust”), but Beekes finds this unconvincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untempered</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>βάπτω</i> (báptō, “to dip, submerge”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ancient Greek&#32;religion</i>) adytum (innermost sanctuary or shrine in an ancient temple)</li><li>name of some vegetable suitable for pickling</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untrodden, inaccessible</li><li>(<i>of mountains</i>) impassable</li><li>(<i>of rivers</i>) not fordable</li><li>desolate, deserted</li><li>(<i>of holy places</i>) not to be trodden</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pure, chaste</li><li>(<i>of a horse</i>) not ridden</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, negation prefix) +&lrm; <i>βᾰτός</i> (bătós, “passable, accessible”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untrodden, inaccessible</li><li>(<i>of mountains</i>) impassable</li><li>(<i>of rivers</i>) not fordable</li><li>desolate, deserted</li><li>(<i>of holy places</i>) not to be trodden</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) pure, chaste</li><li>(<i>of a horse</i>) not ridden</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, negation prefix) +&lrm; <i>βᾰτός</i> (bătós, “passable, accessible”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>low, abased</li></ol></ol><p>Unknown.</p><p>Furnée connects <i>ἀφελής</i> (aphelḗs, “plain, simple”), <i>ἀβαλής</i> (abalḗs), and <i>ἄβαλις</i> (ábalis), positing a Pre-Greek form *ἀφ-ελο- / *ἀβ-ελλο- /*ἀβ-αλο-. This view is followed by Beekes, who further remarks that the variation β ~ βδ, as well as the presence of βδ on its own, makes Pre-Greek origin likely.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>μάστιξ</i> (mástix, “whip, scourge”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) silver fir, pine (accusative singular)</li></ol><p>Usually connected with Latin <i>abiēs</i> (“silver fir”), both reflecting a root <i>&#42;abi-</i>, ultimately likely from a substrate source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a young horse</i>) that has not shed his foal-teeth</li><li>(<i>of an old horse</i>) that no longer sheds them</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-, not”) +&lrm; <i>βολή</i> (bolḗ, “throw, cast of dice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) immortal</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>βροτός</i> (brotós, “mortal”). Compare inherited <i>ἄμβροτος</i> (ámbrotos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uneatable, inedible, not good for food</li><li>not eaten</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) without eating</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>βρωτός</i> (brōtós, “eaten”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bottomless, boundless</li><li>unfathomable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottomless pit, abyss</li><li>the subterranean world of the dead: abyss</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>βυθός</i> (buthós, “depth of the sea”) Compare Sanskrit <i>अगाध</i> (agādha, “fathomless; abyss, chasm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓́γε</i> (ắge)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>honor, glory, pride, delight</li><li>a statue, a bust</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγάλλομαι</i> (agállomai, “to be proud, exult in”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wonder at; to admire</li><li>to be indignant at; to resent</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;əga-</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;m̥ǵh₂-</i>, zero-grade prefix from <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“big, great”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmarried, single</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>γάμος</i> (gámos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very much</li><li>too much; excessively</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;m̥ǵ-éh₂-m</i>, accusative singular of <i>&#42;méǵh₂s</i> (“great”, whence <i>μέγᾰς</i> (mégăs)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἄθροισις</i> (áthroisis, “gathering, collecting”).</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἀγείρω</i> (ageírō, “to gather together”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Persian mounted courier, for carrying royal dispatches</li></ol><p>The exact source is unknown. Not from Akkadian <i>𒇽𒂠𒂷</i> (<sup>LU<sub>2</sub></sup>.ḪUN.GA<sub>2</sub> /⁠agru⁠/, “hired man”). Maybe from Old Persian <i>&#42;angarā</i> (“missive, letter”), a reconstructed word which is from Aramaic <i>&#42;𐡀𐡍‬𐡂𐡓‬𐡀</i> (*’engarā), form of <i>&#42;𐡀𐡍‬𐡂𐡓‬𐡕𐡀</i> (*’engartā), variant of <i>𐡀𐡂𐡓‬𐡕𐡀</i> (’iggartā), <i>𐡀𐡍‬𐡂𐡓𐡕‬𐡀</i> (’engirtā, “missive, letter; contract”), from Akkadian <i>𒂊𒄈𒌅</i> (egirtu, “inscribed tablet; oracle of fate, ambiguous wording; contract, bound deal”), from <i>𒄃</i> (egēru, “to be difficult, to be twisted or locked together; to have a twisted tongue, to be unable to speak against an order”).</p><p>Or from Old Persian <i>&#42;hankarah</i> (“messenger”). In any case a likely doublet of <i>ἄγγελος</i> (ángelos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a messenger</li><li>one that announces</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) angel, heavenly spirit</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀐𐀫</i> (a-ke-ro), with further origin uncertain. Probably a loanword, likely related to <i>ἄγγᾰρος</i> (ángăros, “Persian mounted courier”) (whence Latin <i>angarius</i>), which is perhaps from Old Persian or some other Asian language. Klein suggests a Semitic origin; note <i>𐡀𐡍‬𐡂𐡓𐡕‬𐡀</i> (’engirtā, “missive, letter; contract”) and <i>ܐܓܪܬܐ</i> (ˀeggarṯā, “letter, document”, from Akkadian <i>𒂊𒄈𒌓</i> (egertu, “inscribed tablet; contract”)). Compare also Sanskrit <i>अजिर</i> (ajira, “agile, swift”), <i>अञ्जि</i> (añji, “commander, sender”) and <i>अञ्जस्</i> (añjas, “speed, velocity”).</p><p>The religious sense is a semantic loan from Biblical Hebrew <i>מַלְאָךְ</i> (malʾāḵ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel (to hold liquids); vat; pitcher; bucket; pail; wine-bowl</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>for dry substances</i>) cradle; cinerary urn; casket; coffin</li><li>(<i>in the body</i>) womb; stomach</li><li>shell of the longhorn beetle</li><li>cell of a honeycomb</li></ol></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éngos</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₂eng-</i> (“to curve”), though Beekes prefers deriving the word from a Pre-Greek stratum, due to its semantic domain; many words for "vessel" are mentioned by Homer but are loaned from outside Indo-European, possibly one or more Near Eastern languages. If the latter, it may be cognate with Persian آمه (âme, “inkwell”). Cognate to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀐𐁀</i> (a-ke-a2) (attested only in plural).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) cucumber</li></ol><p>Plant names are generally of substrate origin, so a Pre-Greek borrowing is conceivable; this is corroborated by the presence of <i>-γγ-</i> (-ng-) and the suffix <i>-ουρο-</i> (-ouro-). Note similarities to Persian انگور (angūr, “grape”), Arabic عَجُّور (ʕajjūr, “Egyptian cucumber”), and Modern Greek <i>άγουρος</i> (ágouros, “unripe, immature”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Frankish javelin</li></ol><p>From Frankish, ultimately from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;angô</i> (“hook, barb; spear, dart”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enk-</i> (“to bend”). From the same root is <i>ἀγκών</i> (ankṓn, “elbow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>come! come on! let's go!</li></ol><p>Singular present active imperative of <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead, fetch, bring along (a living creature), take with</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry off as captives or booty</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to guide, command (an army, a ship); to march in war</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to draw out (a line, wall, and so on)</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) to draw (a line) or describe (a plane)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold (an event); to celebrate or observe (festival)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weigh down a scale by a certain amount, to have a certain weight</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to take for oneself</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ágō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵeti</i> (“to be driving”), from the root <i>&#42;h₂eǵ-</i> (“to drive”). Cognate with Latin <i>agō</i> (“to act; to do; to drive”), Sanskrit <i>अजति</i> (ájati, “he drives”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄγετε</i> (ágete) used before &#47;h&#47;</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓́γετε</i> (ắgete)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>come! come on! let's go!</li></ol><p>Plural present active imperative of <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead, fetch, bring along (a living creature), take with</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry off as captives or booty</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to guide, command (an army, a ship); to march in war</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to draw out (a line, wall, and so on)</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) to draw (a line) or describe (a plane)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold (an event); to celebrate or observe (festival)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weigh down a scale by a certain amount, to have a certain weight</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to take for oneself</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ágō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵeti</i> (“to be driving”), from the root <i>&#42;h₂eǵ-</i> (“to drive”). Cognate with Latin <i>agō</i> (“to act; to do; to drive”), Sanskrit <i>अजति</i> (ájati, “he drives”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishhook</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγκος</i> (ánkos, “bend”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend; hollow</li><li>mountain glen; dell; valley</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ánkos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂énkos</i> (“curve”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>अङ्क॑स्</i> (áṅkas), Latin <i>ancus</i> and Old Armenian <i>անկիւն</i> (ankiwn, “angle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anchor</li><li>hook</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enk-ur-ya-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂enk-</i> (“corner, hirn”) (whence also <i>ᾰ̓́γκος</i> (ắnkos)). This is supported by the OED; however, Beekes is skeptical, due to the suffix <i>-ῡρᾰ</i> (-ūră) being associated with presumably-substrate words including <i>γέφῡρᾰ</i> (géphūră, “dam, dike”) and <i>γόργῡρᾰ</i> (górgūră, “underground sewer”), and takes the word as Pre-Greek. The Greek word is the origin of or from the same source as Latin <i>ancora</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄγκυρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ancyra; Angora; Ankara, Turkey</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Hittite <i>𒌷𒀭𒆪𒉿</i> (Ankuwa), reshaped by folk etymology through association with the word <i>ἄγκυρα</i> (ánkura, “anchor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clove of garlic</li></ol><p>Beekes labels this as Pre-Greek. Related to <i>γέλγῑς</i> (gélgīs, “head of garlic”). Kroonen (2012) proposes an ultimate Akkadian etymology of such a Pre-Greek word. He reconstructs an ablaut form <i>*agdl-</i>, which he derives from Akkadian <i>𒄀𒀉𒇻</i> (/⁠gidlu⁠/, “string of garlic or onions”). The theory also supposes that the initial alpha came from Pre-Greek. The same paper, in a footnote, proposes that Latin <i>allium</i> was borrowed from this root as well.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of cloth</i>) not fulled or carded, new</li><li>not cleansed, unwashed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>γνάπτω</i> (gnáptō), alternative spelling of <i>κνάπτω</i> (knáptō, “to card or comb wool”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ἄγναπτος</i> (ágnaptos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of chaste tree</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγνος</i> (ágnos, “chaste tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The state of not knowing or perceiving: ignorance, unawareness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “know”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾱ</i> (-iā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chaste tree (<i>Vitex agnus-castus</i>)</li><li>withy</li><li>Atlantic stargazer (<i>Uranoscopus scaber</i>)</li><li>type of bird</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>ἁγνός</i> (hagnós, “chaste”), unless this is a folk etymology which led to the cultural associations with chastity, rather than the reverse. Beekes considers this a candidate for Pre-Greek origin given the variants <i>ἄγονος</i> (ágonos, id.) and <i>ἄγονον</i> (ágonon, “wild myrtle”) (which, on the other hand, could just be identical to the adjective <i>ἄγονος</i> (ágonos, “infertile”)), speculatively perhaps from a more widespread European substrate source, in view of its resemblance to Old Church Slavonic <i>ꙗгнѧдъ</i> (jagnędŭ, “black poplar”). The non-arboreal meanings are uncertain and remain unexplained.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;chiefly&#32;Epic) to break; to shatter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wágnūmi</i>, as evidenced by Homeric metre; probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;uh₂g-néw-ti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;weh₂g-</i> (“to burst apart; to make a shattering sound”) (traditionally <i>&#42;wag-</i>). Cognate with Tocharian A <i>wāk-</i>, Tocharian B <i>wāk-</i> (“to burst”), and perhaps Hittite <i>𒉿𒀀𒆠</i> (wa-a-ki /⁠wāki⁠/, “bites”).</p><p>Possibly also connected to Latin <i>vāgina</i> (“sheath; vagina”) (if originally *“fissure, slit”; note, however, that the "vagina" meaning is not in Classical Latin and began as a euphemism), but this is usually associated with Lithuanian <i>vóžti</i> (“to cover”). Sometimes connected with Sanskrit <i>वज्र</i> (vájra-, “thunderbolt, diamond”) (*“that which splits”), but this is more likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weǵ-</i> (“strong, lively”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknown; unheard of; forgotten</li><li>unknowable</li><li>unintelligible</li><li>not knowing; ignorant</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>γῐγνώσκω</i> (gĭgnṓskō, “to know”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unknown; unheard of; forgotten</li><li>unknowable</li><li>unintelligible</li><li>not knowing; ignorant</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>γνωτ-</i> (gnōt-, root of <i>γῐγνώσκω</i> (gĭgnṓskō, “to know”)). Compare <i>ᾰ̓γνώς</i> (ăgnṓs) and Latin <i>īgnōtus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>infertile, sterile, unfruitful</li><li>childless</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>γονή</i> (gonḗ, “offspring”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awe; reverence; dread</li><li>curse; pollution; guilt</li><li>abomination</li><li>expiatory sacrifice</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Most commonly explained as from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂égos</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂eg-</i>, with cognates such as Sanskrit <i>आग॑स्</i> (ā́gas) and Old English <i>acan</i> (English <i>ache</i>) (i.e. Beekes, Pokorny). However, Chantraine prefers a relation to <i>ᾰ̔́γῐος</i> (hắgĭos, “holy”), in which case cognate to Sanskrit <i>यज॑स्</i> (yájas, “worship; sacrifice”) with unexplained initial deaspiration.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a hunt (a chase in order to capture)</li><li>a way of catching</li><li>quarry; prey; catch (that which is taken in hunting)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eǵro-</i> (“hunt”). Cognate with Old Irish <i>ár</i> (“slaughter”) (&lt; Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;agrom</i>) and Avestan <i>𐬀𐬰𐬭𐬋𐬛𐬀𐬌𐬜𐬍</i> (azrōda<sup><small>i</small></sup>δī, “hunt”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄγρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A deme of Attica famous for its temple of Artemis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dwelling in the field; living outdoors</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γρός</i> (ăgrós) +&lrm; <i>αὐλή</i> (aulḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwritten</li><li>Not registered, not recorded</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>γρᾰ́φω</i> (grắphō, “to write”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living in the open fields</li><li>(<i>of plants or animals</i>) wild (non-domesticated)</li><li>(<i>of people or animals</i>) wild; savage; violent; fierce</li><li>(<i>of situations</i>) cruel; harsh</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵr̥yos</i> (“campestral, of a field”). By surface analysis, <i>ἀγρός</i> (agrós, “field, country”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Laconian name of the wild olive (<i>Olea europaea</i> subsp. <i>europaea</i>, syn. <i>Olea oleaster</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, the variation "π/φ" is typical of Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>country-dwelling, rustic</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) coarse, crude, boorish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγρός</i> (agrós) +&lrm; <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō).</p><p>For the opposition with <i>ἀστεῖος</i> (asteîos), compare typologically Latin <i>agrestis</i> (cognate via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵros</i>), <i>pāgānus</i>, <i>rūsticus</i> vs. <i>cīvīlis</i>, <i>urbānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wakeful, sleepless, vigilant</li><li>banishing sleep, keeping awake</li></ol><p>Likely a combination of <i>ἀγρός</i> (agrós, “field”) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́πνος</i> (hŭ́pnos, “sleep”), originally meaning "who sleeps outside", and <i>ᾰ̓́γρᾱ</i> (ắgrā, “hunt; chase”) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́πνος</i> (hŭ́pnos, “sleep”), meaning "chasing for sleep". Both derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eǵro-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a grass that mules fed on, perhaps <i>Elymus repens</i></li></ol><p>Compare <i>ᾰ̓γρώτης</i> (ăgrṓtēs, “of the field”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) street, highway</li><li>collection of streets, city</li></ol><p>Mainly a poetic word. Generally considered to be a perfect participle of verb <i>ᾰ̓́γω</i> (ắgō, “I lead”), but without the reduplication. However, this makes little sense as the formation is without a parallel (save archaic <i>ἰδυῖα</i> (iduîa)); more probably a Pre-Greek word in <i>-υια</i>, like <i>κώδυια</i> (kṓduia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Poetic</i>) near, nigh</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of time</i>)</li><li>like, resembling</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enǵʰ-</i> (“to tighten”) (whence <i>ἄγχω</i> (ánkhō)). According to Beekes, the term is an old locative of a root noun.</p><p>Cognate with English anger, hangnail, angina.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>nearest, closest</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγχι</i> (ánkhi) +&lrm; <i>-ιστος</i> (-istos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alkanet (<i>Alkanna tinctoria</i>)</li><li>dyeing matter extracted from the plant</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variant excludes a derivation from <i>ᾰ̓́γχω</i> (ắnkhō, “to strangle; to throttle; to choke”), so he concludes it must be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to compress; to press tight (especially the throat</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to strangle; to throttle; to choke</li><li>(figurative,&#32;transitive) to pressure; to influence; to put pressure on</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enǵʰ-</i> (“to constrict”). Cognates include Old English <i>enge</i> (whence the first element in English <i>hangnail</i>), Latin <i>angō</i>, Sanskrit <i>अंहु</i> (áṃhu), and Old Armenian <i>անձուկ</i> (anjuk, “narrow; tight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead, fetch, bring along (a living creature), take with</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to carry off as captives or booty</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to guide, command (an army, a ship); to march in war</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to draw out (a line, wall, and so on)</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) to draw (a line) or describe (a plane)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to hold (an event); to celebrate or observe (festival)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to weigh down a scale by a certain amount, to have a certain weight</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to take for oneself</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ágō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵeti</i> (“to be driving”), from the root <i>&#42;h₂eǵ-</i> (“to drive”). Cognate with Latin <i>agō</i> (“to act; to do; to drive”), Sanskrit <i>अजति</i> (ájati, “he drives”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unit of measure corresponding to four <i>χοίνικες</i> (khoínikes)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Furnée assumes a substrate word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom from fear, security</li><li>safe conduct, amnesty, indemnity</li><li>licence, permit</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓δεής</i> (ādeḗs, “fearless”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invisible</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>δῆλος</i> (dêlos, “clear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be sated [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>to be fed up [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ᾰ̔́δος</i> (hắdos, “satiety”) and <i>ἅδην</i> (hádēn), <i>ἄδην</i> (ádēn, “to one’s fill”).</p><p>Attested only in the aorist and perfect. The present form <i>ἀδέω</i> (adéō) used by most dictionaries as the lemma form is not attested.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unjust</li><li>wrong</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”, negative prefix) +&lrm; <i>δίκη</i> (díkē, “justice”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not thirsty, without thirst</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>quenching, satiating thirst</li><li>not causing thirst</li></ol><li>(<i>substantive</i>) type of date that is gathered unripe</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>δίψα</i> (dípsa) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Homeric,&#32;only of women and cattle) unbroken</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a woman</i>) unwedded, chaste</li></ol><li>untameable, untamed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀδμής</i> (admḗs) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos). Related to <i>δᾰμᾰ́ζω</i> (dămắzō).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄδμητος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Admetus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄδμητος</i> (ádmētos, “unbroken, untameable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>guileless, honest</li><li>unadulterated, genuine</li><li>unpretentious, simple</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>δόλος</i> (dólos, “trick, deceit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not skinned, not flayed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leathern sack</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>δέρω</i> (dérō, “to skin, flay”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unattended by slaves</li><li>having no slaves, too poor to keep a slave</li><li>impatient of slavery</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>δοῦλος</i> (doûlos, “servant, slave”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upright pieces of a plough</li><li>(<i>in Cyprus</i>) canoes made of hollowed tree trunks</li></ol><p>The word seems to continue <i>&#42;ἄ-δρυα</i> (&#42;á-drua, “consisting of one single tree”), a compound from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>δρῦς</i> (drûs, “tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>innermost sanctuary, shrine</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive form of <i>ἄδυτος</i> (ádutos, “not to be entered”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not to be entered</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>δύω</i> (dúō, “to enter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not to be entered</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>δύω</i> (dúō, “to enter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἆθλον</i> (âthlon)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἆθλος</i> (âthlos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἆθλος</i> (âthlos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stormwind; whirlwind</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) anything in whirling motion</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>&#42;ᾰ̓́ϝελ-ι̯α</i> (&#42;ắwel-i̯a), related to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂weh₁-</i>. Cognates include Welsh <i>awel</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwedded, unpaired</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”, negative prefix) +&lrm; <i>ζυγόν</i> (zugón, “yoke”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unleavened</li><li>the paschal feast</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) uncorrupted, sincere</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ζύμη</i> (zúmē) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to dry up, parch</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cry, groan, sigh</li></ol><p>Connection with <i>ἄδδαυον</i> (áddauon) is problematic, as a compound with <i>αὖος</i> (aûos) is improbable; Latte corrects this in <i>&#42;ἀδδανον</i> (&#42;addanon). Nearest cognate seems Old Czech <i>ozd</i> (“dry malt”), Czech <i>ozditi</i> (“to dry malt”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂esd-</i>, perhaps ultimately from the root <i>&#42;h₂e(H)s-</i> (“to dry, burn”). Leiden scholars Beekes, Kloekhorst and Kroonen have a simpler solution: <b>ἄζω</b> continues Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ed-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂ed-</i> (“dry”) (of which <i>&#42;h₂eHs-</i> is likely an extension of, via an earlier form <i>&#42;h₂ed-s-</i>) the root found in Hittite <i>𒄩𒀀𒋾</i> (ḫāt-, “to dry up, become parched”) and possibly Prasuni <i>ëliga</i> (if from <i>*Hād-V-ka-</i>); cf. words in Germanic with an added velar: Gothic <i>𐌰𐌶𐌲𐍉</i> (azgō, “ash”), Old High German <i>azgo</i> (“ash”) (&lt; Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;askǭ</i> ~ <i>&#42;azgǭ</i>). Without the final consonants, Latin <i>āreō</i> (“to be dry, parched up”), probably also Latin <i>āra</i>, from Old Latin <i>āsa</i> (“altar”), which is also found in Hittite <i>𒄩𒀸𒊭𒀀𒀸</i> (h̯āššāš, “fireplace, hearth”). The Latin long <i>ā-</i> is explained from the perfect <i>&#42;h₂e-h₂s-</i>, though Beekes prefers a glottalic <i>&#42;h₂eh₁s-</i> that dissimilated from <i>&#42;h₂ed-s-</i>. Further, Sanskrit <i>आस</i> (ā́sa, “ashes, dust”), which may continue Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂oso-</i> or <i>&#42;h₂eHso-</i>.</p><p>From <i>ἆ</i> (â), ultimately onomatopoeic. Compare the formation of <i>οἴζω</i> (oízō) from <i>οἴ</i> (oí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἄζωστος</i> (ázōstos, “ungirt”)</li><li>synonym of <i>ἀβίωτος</i> (abíōtos, “unlivable”)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄζωτος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Azotus, the Greek name for an ancient Philistine city identified either with Gaza or Ashdod</li><li>Ashdod, identified in post-classical times by this name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to breathe, blow</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ᾰ̓́ϝημι</i> (ắwēmi), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;áwēmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wḗh₁ti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₂weh₁-</i> (“to blow; wind”). Cognates include Old English and English <i>wind</i>, Latin <i>ventus</i>, <i>vannus</i>, Sanskrit <i>वाति॑</i> (vā́ti, “to blow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without gods</li><li>rejecting or disdaining the belief in the gods (especially officially sanctioned gods), atheist</li><li>generally: godless, secular</li><li>abandoned by the gods</li><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) not derived from a theonym</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>set aside</li><li>out of position</li><li>unfixed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>θετός</i> (thetós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sport</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀθλέω</i> (athléō, “contend”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>struggling, unhappy, wretched, miserable</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) pertaining to a competition</li></ol><p>From <i>ἆθλος</i> (âthlos, “conflict, struggle”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, “a suffix added to nouns or adjectives, forming adjectives: pertaining to, belonging to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) flower, blossom</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) (<i>figuratively</i>) an ornament, beautiful object or person</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) (<i>colloquial</i>) an endearing term of address</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) (<i>figuratively</i>) a select group of people, cream of the crop</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) (<i>figuratively</i>) effect or consequence, fruit of one's actions</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) ash, cinder</li></ol><p>From Koine Greek <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”) following Medieval Greek phonotactics where nasals are dropped before fricatives ([nθ] → [θ]).</p><p>Probably related to Ionic Greek <i>αἴθω</i> (aíthō, “to ignite”) and Koine Greek <i>αἰθάλη</i> (aithálē, “soot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: Rhodian word for <i>ἅρμα</i> (hárma, “chariot”).</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Formerly compared with Sanskrit <i>वन्धुर</i> (vandhura, “seat of a charioteer”), taken as a wicker basket tied upon the wagon and connected with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;windaną</i> (“to wind, wrap”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wendʰ-</i> (“to turn, wind, braid”). As the formation of the Sanskrit word is unclear, and the root is hardly attested outside Germanic, the connection must be false. Connection with <i>κάναθρον</i> (kánathron, “wicker carriage”) is improbable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>toy, plaything</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀθῡ́ρω</i> (athū́rō, “to play, sport (of children)”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, “result or effect of an action”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spike, prick, goad</li><li>ten foot rod, used as a measure</li><li>square measure of 100 ft used in Egypt</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”). However, according to Beekes, it may also contain the Pre-Greek suffix <i>-αινα</i>, added directly to the stem <i>ακ-</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbent, unbendable, rigid</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unbending, unflinching, inexorable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “to bend, curve”) +&lrm; <i>κάμπτω</i> (kámptō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorny plant</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Egyptian</i>) specifically <i>Vachellia nilotica</i> (phono-semantic matching of Coptic <i>ϣⲟⲛϯ</i> (šonti))</li></ol><li>thorn, spine, prickle</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) thorny issue; something difficult or painful</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “point”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”), though Beekes considers this theory outdated and prefers to take the word as Pre-Greek. Probably not related to Sanskrit <i>कण्टक</i> (káṇṭaka, “thorn, thornbush; prick, needle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The acanthus plant</li><li>thorn, spine, prickle</li></ol><p>Traditionally taken as a compound of <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “point”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”); however, see <i>ἄκανθα</i> (ákantha, “thorny plant”) for more.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄκανθος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acanthus (a town in Bybassus, Caria, modern-day Turkey)</li><li>Acanthus (a city in Egypt)</li><li>Acanthus (a city in Macedonia, Greece)</li><li>a male given name, Acanthus, the name of multiple figures in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκανθος</i> (ákanthos, “acanthus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without smoke, smokeless, not smoking</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>καπνός</i> (kapnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀκαρί</i> (akarí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without fruit: barren</li><li>fruitless, unprofitable</li><li>In an active sense: making barren</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>καρπός</i> (karpós, “fruit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Montpellier maple (<i>Acer monspessulanum</i>)</li></ol><p>Maybe from *ἄκαρστος. Cognate to Latin <i>acer</i>. According to Beekes, since plant names are often borrowed, and the formation is unclear, it can be envisaged a Pre-Greek origin (compare Basque <i>gaztigar</i> (“maple tree”) and Sardinian <i>costiche</i> (“id.”)). Furnée compares <i>κάστον</i> (káston, “wood”) and <i>κόστον</i> (kóston, “wooden parts of a wagon”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄκαστος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Acastus, one of the men who sailed with Jason and the Argonauts</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>light vessel, boat</li><li>cup shaped like a boat</li></ol><p>Probably a technical loanword. Often connected with the root of <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “point”), but without any obvious reason. Winter connected it with <i>κητήνη</i> (kētḗnē, “vessel big as a sea-monster”), which could perhaps belong with <i>κῆτος</i> (kêtos, “sea-monster”) instead.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild basil (<i>Acinos rotundifolius</i>)</li></ol><p>The vowel interchange, though rare, may point to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwavering, stable</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>grammar</i>) indeclinable</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) (<i>said of a rising perpendicular</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>κλῑ́νω</i> (klī́nō, “to decline”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, verbal adjective suffix). Diachronically from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-ḱlitós</i>, formed from the root <i>&#42;ḱley-</i> (“to incline, slope”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>νηστεία</i> (nēsteía)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Morphologically, the term has been construed as a combination of <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “non-”) and an element <i>-κμη-</i> (-kmē-), which has itself been related to Ancient Greek <i>κομῶσα</i> (komôsa, “filling”). Beekes alternatively relates the second component to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱemh₂-</i>, whence also <i>κάμνω</i> (kámnō). Another proposal, favored by the linguist Tore Rovs Kristoffersen, suggests that the term may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-kʷm-éh₂</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷem-</i> (“to swallow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fasting from food</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Probably related to or derived from Aeolic Greek <i>ἄκμα</i> (ákma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mineralogy</i>) meteoric stone</li><li>anvil</li><li>(<i>Cypriot</i>) pestle</li><li>head of a battering ram</li><li>a kind of wolf</li><li>a kind of eagle</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tireless</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éḱmō</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>अश्मन्</i> (aśman), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬐𐬨𐬀𐬥</i> (akman), Lithuanian <i>akmuo</i>, and English <i>hammer</i>.</p><p>Perhaps from Egyptian <i>ꜥẖm</i> (“cultic image of a falcon”). Compare also Coptic <i>ⲁⲭⲙⲩ</i> (akhmu, kind of bird) and Arabic رَخْمَة (raḵma, “Egyptian vulture”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>endearing</i>) a female bedfellow, a wife</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, alpha copulativum) +&lrm; <i>κοίτη</i> (koítē, “[marriage] bed”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, “a woman pertaining to —”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morsel, bit</li></ol><p>The etymology of this word is disputed. A relation with Phrygian <i>ακκαλος</i> (akkalos, “mouthful”) is commonly assumed, but according to Petrantoni, both are independent borrowings from a third language, possibly a Semitic one. Compare Akkadian <i>𒀀𒅗𒈝</i> (akalum, “loaf of bread”).</p><p>Beekes hesitantly proposes a "foreign origin", but does not rule out a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soldier thistle (<i>Picnomon acarna</i>, syn. <i>Cirsium acarna</i>)</li></ol><p>Strömberg compares <i>κόρνος</i> (kórnos, “butcher's broom”) and <i>σκόρνος</i> (skórnos, “myrtle”). It seems possible that <i>ἀκορνός</i> (akornós, “grasshopper”) derives from this word, like <i>ἀκανθίας</i> (akanthías, “grasshopper”) beside <i>ἄκανθα</i> (ákantha, “thorn, thistle”). The prothetic vowel, the variations and the <i>-ρν-</i> cluster point to a Pre-Greek origin, according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow flag, <i>Iris pseudacorus</i></li></ol><p>Perhaps from a same root as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱrós</i>, <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweet flag (<i>Acorus calamus</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a same root as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱrós</i>, <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cure; remedy</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(H)yeh₂k-</i> (“cure”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀐𐀴𐀪𐀊𐀂</i> (a-ke-ti-ri-ja-i), <i>𐀊𐀐𐀳𐀩</i> (ja-ke-te-re, “healers”); outside of Hellenic, possibly cognate with Welsh <i>iach</i> (“healthy”), Breton <i>yac'h</i>, Old Irish <i>ícc</i> (“cure, healing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hearing.</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) synonym of <i>ἀκουσμάτια</i> (akousmátia)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō, “to hear”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thing heard, such as music.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Rumor, report.</li><li>(<i>education</i>) Oral instruction, in the Pythagorean school.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō, “to hear”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end, point, top, peak, extremity</li><li>A castle or citadel overlooking a town.</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>ᾰ̓́κρος</i> (ắkros, “outermost”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>at the edge</li><li>(<i>especially of the top</i>) extreme; outermost</li><li>at the beginning or end</li><li>pointed; sharp</li><li>superlative (having the most of any characteristic)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱrós</i> (“sharp”). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>остръ</i> (ostrŭ), Latin <i>ācer</i>, Old Armenian <i>ասեղն</i> (asełn, “needle”) and Old English <i>ēagor</i> (English <i>eagre</i>).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄκραι)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a colony of Sicily, founded by Syracusans in 663 BC, located near the modern Palazzolo Acreide</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκρᾱ</i> (ákrā, “top, peak, castle, citadel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmixed, neat, sheer (of liquids)</li><li>pure, simple (of any object)</li><li>pure, absolute (of qualities)</li><li>intemperate, violent (of persons)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>κεράννυμι</i> (keránnumi) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of wheat</i>) not mixed with barley</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-, not”) +&lrm; <i>κρῑθή</i> (krīthḗ, “barley”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peak, mountaintop, hilltop</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́κρος</i> (ắkros, “edge, extremity”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undistinguishable, confused</li><li>continuous, unceasing</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) countless</li><li>undecided, doubtful</li><li>unpredictable, uncertain</li><li>unjudged, untried, without trial</li><li>uncapable of judging</li><li>(<i>of fate</i>) undiscriminating</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>κρῐτός</i> (krĭtós, “chosen, picked out, separated”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peak (e.g. of a mountain)</li><li>headland, cape</li><li>end, extremity</li><li>highest height, apex</li><li>(in the plural,&#32;logic) the major and minor points of syllogism</li><li>extreme</li></ol><p>Neuter of <i>ᾰ̓́κρος</i> (ắkros, “topmost”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>at the edge</li><li>(<i>especially of the top</i>) extreme; outermost</li><li>at the beginning or end</li><li>pointed; sharp</li><li>superlative (having the most of any characteristic)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱrós</i> (“sharp”). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>остръ</i> (ostrŭ), Latin <i>ācer</i>, Old Armenian <i>ասեղն</i> (asełn, “needle”) and Old English <i>ēagor</i> (English <i>eagre</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at the point</li><li>utterly, perfectly, absolutely</li><li>skillfully</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of elder wood</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀκτέα</i> (aktéa, “elder”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) leader</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵtōr</i>. Cognate to Latin <i>āctor</i>. Synchronically, <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄκτωρ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Actor, a men's name in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄκτωρ</i> (áktōr, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) acorn of holm oak</li><li>ornament or jewel in form of acorn</li></ol><p>Connection with Sanskrit <i>अश्नाति</i> (aśnāti, “to eat, consume”) and <i>ἄκολος</i> (ákolos, “bit, morsel”) is improbable. It is rather a Pre-Greek word, since <i>-υλ-</i> is a frequent substrate suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unwilling</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a light javelin; a dart</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ᾰ̓έκων</i> (ăékōn), from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ἑκών</i> (hekṓn).</p><p>Probably cognate with <i>ᾰ̓́κρος</i> (ắkros), but Beekes considers this uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazelnuts, the fruits of the hazel tree</li><li>wingnut (<i>Pterocarya fraxinifolia</i>)</li><li>butt of spear-shaft</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the interchange <i>α/ε</i> clearly points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>incessantly</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insufferable, accursed</li><li>unceasing, incessant</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>λαθ-</i> (lath-) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insufferable, accursed</li><li>unceasing, incessant</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>λαθ-</i> (lath-) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) white</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>albus</i>. Doublet of <i>ἀλφός</i> (alphós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄλγεα</i> (álgea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain (felt or caused), suffering</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλγέω</i> (algéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sense of pain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλγέω</i> (algéō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>worse</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painful, grievous</li><li>feeling pain, suffering</li></ol><p>For *ἀλγεσνός, i.e. <i>ἄλγος</i> (álgos, “pain”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain (of either mind or body), sorrow, trouble, grief, distress, woe</li><li>that which causes pain or suffering</li></ol><p>Disputed. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂leg-</i> (“to care”), and related to <i>ἀλέγω</i> (alégō, “to take care”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheat-groats; coarse wheaten flour; wheatmeal</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ᾰ̓λέω</i> (ăléō, “to grind”), with the alternative form <i>ᾰ̓́λεᾰρ</i> (ắleăr) from earlier ᾰ̓́λεϝᾰρ (ắlewăr), showing the connection to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂leh₁wr̥</i>, whence also Old Armenian <i>ալիւր</i> (aliwr, “flour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A cup, a goblet</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) The socket of the hip joint</li></ol><p>Unknown. Probably a loanword or an expansion of <i>λείβω</i> (leíbō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unguent; anointing oil; oil, fat</li><li>pitch; resin</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀩𐀞</i> (a-re-pa), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;áleipʰar</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ἀλείφω</i> (aleíphō, “to anoint; to grease”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰρ</i> (-ăr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wheat flour, wheatmeal</li><li>meal</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂léh₁wr̥</i>. Cognate with Old Armenian <i>ալեւր</i> (alewr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ceaseless wandering</li><li>roaming</li><li>roving</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂elh₂-</i> (“to wander”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unceasing</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un”) +&lrm; <i>λήγω</i> (lḗgō, “I cease”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not to be laid hold of, hard to catch</li><li>unamenable</li><li>incomprehensible</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>ληπτός</i> (lēptós), from the root of <i>λᾰμβᾰ́νω</i> (lămbắnō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) healing; cure; remedy</li></ol><p>Possibly from the theorized form <i>&#42;ᾰ̓λθέσσω</i> (&#42;ălthéssō) suffixed with <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs). Ultimately from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;h₂el-dʰ-</i>, itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow; to nourish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) treatment, cure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλθέω</i> (althéō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>ᾰ̓λθαίνω</i> (ălthaínō, “to heal; to become well”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>healing; medicine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>λεύκη</i> (leúkē, “white poplar”), as they say in Macedonia.</li></ol><p>Kretschmer compared Old High German <i>erila</i> (“alder”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;olьxa</i> (“alder”). Either from a common European substrate root or alternatively directly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éliseh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂élis-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groats of einkorn wheat</li><li>allec; fish sauce</li></ol><p>Walde derived the word from <i>ᾰ̓λέω</i> (ăléō, “to grind”), but this is unconvincing. The formation is like <i>ἕλῐξ</i> (hélĭx) and <i>χόλῐξ</i> (khólĭx). The nature of any connection with Latin <i>alica</i> (kind of wheat) and Latin <i>(h)allēc</i> (“fish sauce”) is unknown. According to Beekes, it is a loanword from an unknown language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water plantain (<i>Alisma plantago-aquatica</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. The word does not contain <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “salt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) rock</li></ol><p>Unknown. Only attested in Hesychius.</p><p>The word has been connected with Old Armenian <i>աղիւս</i> (ałiws, “brick”), <i>αἰγίλιψ</i> (aigílips, “steep”) of unknown origin, and <i>ἠλίβατος</i> (ēlíbatos, “high, steep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moose; elk</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;elhaz</i>, <i>&#42;algiz</i> (“elk”), whence also Latin <i>alcēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stout, strong</li><li>stout-hearted, brave</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓́λλᾰ</i> (ắllă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is given or taken in exchange</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>reward, price (of a thing)</li><li>change, vicissitude</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀλλάσσω</i> (allássō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>man's upper garment, purple cloak</li></ol><p>The word is supposed to be Thessalian, but the origin remains unknown; Latin <i>alicula</i> (“short tunic”) also belongs here.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄλλοθι</i> (állothi)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄλλοτε</i> (állote) (<i>before /h/</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from another place</li></ol><p><i>ἄλλος</i> (állos) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>elsewhere, in another place; especially in a strange or foreign land</li><li>in other ways</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄλλος</i> (állos, “another”) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi, “at”). Compare Gothic <i>𐌰𐌻𐌾𐌰𐌸</i> (aljaþ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>other, another, different, else</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with article</i>) the other, all others, all besides, the rest</li><li>(<i>with numerals</i>) yet, still</li><li>(<i>in lists</i>) as well, besides, too</li><li>equivalent to <i>ἀλλοῖος</i> (alloîos) of another sort than (<i>with genitive</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>in phrases</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>in combination with <i>τις</i> (tis) any other; anyone else, anything else</li><li>in the phrase <i>ἄλλος τε καὶ</i> (állos te kaì) especially, most of all</li><li>in the phrase <i>εἴ τις καὶ ἄλλος</i> (eí tis kaì állos) or <i>εἴ τι καὶ ἄλλο</i> (eí ti kaì állo) if anyone, whoever else; if anything, whatever else</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;áľľos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élyos</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>alius</i>, Old Irish <i>aile</i>, Old Armenian <i>այլ</i> (ayl), Old English <i>elles</i> (English <i>else</i>}.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to somewhere else; to another place; to elsewhere</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́λλος</i> (ắllos) +&lrm; <i>-σε</i> (-se).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄλλοτε</i> (állote)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at another time</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to another place [<i>with</i> <b>ᾰ̓́λλος</b> (ắllos)]</li></ol><p>From the stem of <i>ᾰ̓́λλος</i> (ắllos, “other”).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>otherwise</li></ol><p>Adverb from <i>ἄλλος</i> (állos, “other”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ᾰ̓λκή</i> (ălkḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) animal, beast, brute</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) horse</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἄλογος</i> (álogos, “unreasoning, speechless”, adjective). Used in the military to differentiate between soldiers—beings with reason and speech—and animals—unreasoning, "<i>ἄλογον ζῷον</i> (álogon zōîon)". As soldiers mostly used horses, the meaning has been associated with them. Morphologically <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “reason, speech”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unspeaking, speechless</li><li>unutterable</li><li>without reason, irrational</li><li>not reckoned upon, unexpected</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unspeaking, speechless</li><li>unutterable</li><li>without reason, irrational</li><li>not reckoned upon, unexpected</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedpartner, wife</li><li>concubine</li><li>unwed woman</li></ol><p><i>ἁ-</i> (ha-, alpha copulativum) +&lrm; <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos, “lying down”). The initial /h/ of the prefix is lost due to Grassmann's Law.</p><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, alpha privativum) +&lrm; <i>λέχος</i> (lékhos, “marriage bed”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grove (<i>especially a sacred one</i>)</li><li>(<i>transferred sense</i>) any hallowed precinct (<i>even without trees</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow, nourish”), the same root of Latin <i>alō</i>, <i>alumnus</i>, <i>oleō</i> and English <i>old</i>. On the other hand, Beekes connects the word with the proper noun <i>Ᾰ̓́λτῐς</i> (Ắltĭs), the name of a sacred enclosure dedicated to Zeus in Olympia; on this basis one could reconstruct the noun as <i>&#42;alti̯os</i>. Furnée accepts the equation, but interprets it in the context of other instances of an interchange of dental and sibilant in Pre-Greek words, like <i>ἄννησον</i> (ánnēson) and <i>ἄνηθον</i> (ánēthon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of globularia (<i>Globularia alypum</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἄλῡπος</i> (álūpos, “without pain, free from pain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without pain, painless</li><li>causing no pain or grief</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>λῡ́πη</i> (lū́pē, “pain”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agitation</li><li>ennui, listlessness</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, related to <i>ἀλύω</i> (alúō). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the name of a certain plant used for curing madness&#59; according to LSJ, specifically madwort (<i>Farsetia clypeata</i> → <i>Fibigia clypeata</i>) and Galen's madwort (<i>Sideritis romana</i>)</li></ol><p>Usually derived from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “privative prefix”) +&lrm; <i>λύσσα</i> (lússa, “rage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that may not be untied, loosed or dissolved</li><li>(<i>of arguments</i>) not refutable, soluble</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un”) +&lrm; <i>λῠτός</i> (lŭtós, “that may be untied, loosed or dissolved”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alpha, the name for the first letter Α (A), α (a) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>first</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤀</i> (ʾ /⁠ʾālep⁠/). The letter's name is from Phoenician <i>𐤀𐤋𐤐</i> (ʾlp, “bull, ox, head of cattle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flour; especially barley flour</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;álpʰi</i>, possibly ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élbʰi(t)</i> (“barley”), thus the original meaning may have been “barley-flour”; this word may itself be derived from the root <i>&#42;h₂elbʰ-</i> (“white”). Compare Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;albi</i> (“barley”) (whence modern Albanian <i>elb</i>) and Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;arpacyaH</i> (“barley”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barley-groats; barley flour</li><li>meal, groats</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) one's daily bread</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂élbʰit</i>. Cognates include Albanian <i>elb</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) apocopic form of <i>ἀνά</i> (aná) used before a labial consonant</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>ἄμμος</i> (ámmos, “sand”)</li><li>sandy soil</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) sand dunes by the sea</li></ol><p>Probably from an unidentified European substrate, along with its synonyms <i>ἄμμος</i> (ámmos), <i>ψάμαθος</i> (psámathos), and <i>ψάμμος</i> (psámmos). Outside of Greek, cognates (from the same substrate) include Old English <i>sand</i>, Latin <i>sabulum</i>, and perhaps Old Armenian <i>աւազ</i> (awaz).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheaf, bundle of ears of corn</li><li>(poetic,&#32;by extension) corn</li></ol><p>Considered to be a derivation from the verb <i>ἀμάομαι</i> (amáomai, “to gather”). However, if this verb is used primarily for drawing liquids, it can hardly be connected with this noun. One might also think of <i>ἀμάω</i> (amáō, “to reap”), however, given the suffix <i>-λλ-</i>, Beekes calls the word Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἅμαξα</i> (hámaxa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>against which cannot be fought, unovercomeable, irresistible</li><li>not taking part in battle, non-combatant</li><li>not inclined to fight</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spouted cup</li><li>alembic, still</li></ol><p>The suffix is no doubt a substrate element, as it is in <i>κύλιξ</i> (kúlix). It is often connected with <i>ἄμβη</i> (ámbē, “raised edge”) and <i>ἄμβων</i> (ámbōn, “crest of a hill”), but this remains uncertain. Derivation from <i>ἀναβαίνω</i> (anabaínō, “to mount”) seems most improbable. Ultimately, the origin of the word is still uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>middle of ship's yard</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) immortal, divine</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) belonging to the gods</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ə́mrətos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥mr̥tós</i>. Equivalent to <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>βροτός</i> (brotós, “mortal”) (compare <i>ἄβροτος</i> (ábrotos)). The <i>β</i> (b) developed by epenthesis, as the earlier form was <i>*amrtos</i>, with a nasal-liquid cluster, which is not allowed in Greek phonotactics. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>अमृत</i> (amṛ́ta) and Avestan <i>𐬀𐬨𐬆𐬱𐬀</i> (aməša).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crest of a hill</li><li>edge of a cup</li><li>pulpit</li></ol><p>Probably a loanword (substrate); also compare Latin <i>umbo</i> (“boss (of a shield)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓́μῑκτος</i> (ắmīktos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immediate</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “a-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>μέσος</i> (mésos, “middle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a shovel</li><li>a waterbucket; a pail</li><li>a spade</li><li>a hobble for young goats</li><li>Ionic form of <i>ᾰ̓́μης</i> (ắmēs)</li></ol><p>The etymology continues to be debated. Hypotheses include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harvest</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμάω</i> (amáō, “to harvest, gather”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂meh₁-</i> (“to reap, mow, harvest”) (whence also English <i>mow</i>, <i>meadow</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ajwain (<i>Trachyspermum ammi</i>)</li><li>bisnaga (<i>Visnaga daucoides</i>, syn. <i>Ammi visnaga</i>)</li></ol><p>Hemmerdinger proposes a derivation from Egyptian <i>ꜥmꜣw</i> (“a kind of medicinal plant”), but, as both the reading and the meaning of that word are uncertain, its status as an etymon is highly questionable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unmingled; unblended; unmixed; pure</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) unsociable; unmingling</li><li>(<i>of places</i>) inhospitable</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>μῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (mī́gnūmĭ) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Lesbian form of <i>ἡμεῖς</i> (hēmeîs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sandy, made of sand</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́μμος</i> (ắmmos, “sand”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sand</li><li>sandy ground, a racecourse</li></ol><p>Traditionally considered a contamination of <i>ἄμαθος</i> (ámathos, “sand”) and <i>ψάμμος</i> (psámmos, “sand”). Even if not, likely a loan from some European substrate; said substrate status makes phonetic analysis such as the contamination theory above difficult, as the word likely does not follow traditional Indo-European sound laws.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misshapen, ugly</li><li>formless, shapeless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>-μορφος</i> (-morphos, “form”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>insatiably, incessantly</li><li>eagerly, vehemently</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;meh₃-</i>, whence also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;mōjaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vine</li><li>a vineyard</li><li>an engine for protecting besiegers, mantlet</li></ol><p>Difficult to explain as an inherited Indo-European word and usually considered to be a Pre-Greek/substrate word. Compare also Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ébōl</i> (“apple”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woman's diadem; frontlet</li><li>horse's headband</li><li>rim of a wheel</li></ol><p>Formerly considered to be a root noun prefixed with <i>ᾰ̓μ-</i> (ăm-), belonging to <i>πῠ́κᾰ</i> (pŭ́kă, “solidly”) and <i>πῠκνός</i> (pŭknós, “compact”), and cognate with Avestan <i>𐬞𐬎𐬯𐬁</i> (pusā, “headband, diadem”). However, the notion "thick, solid" does not seem to fit the objects in question. A word for such objects is easily borrowed and, according to Beekes, it contains a typical Pre-Greek suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li><li>at the same time</li></ol><p>An extension of <i>ἅμα</i> (háma), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sem-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>starch</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not ground at the mill: finely ground (of meal)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cake of fine meal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative) +&lrm; <i>μύλη</i> (múlē, “mill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Not ground at the mill: finely ground (of meal)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cake of fine meal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative) +&lrm; <i>μύλη</i> (múlē, “mill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>street (<i>in an inhabited area</i>)</li><li>block of houses, neighborhood, ward</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way, path”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dual only</i>) both</li></ol><p>Cognate with Latin <i>ambō</i> and Tocharian A <i>āmpi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ambʰow</i>/<i>&#42;h₂n̥tbʰóh₁</i>. The second half is cognate to Old Church Slavonic <i>оба</i> (oba), Gothic <i>𐌱𐌰𐌹</i> (bai), Sanskrit <i>उभौ</i> (ubháu). Related to <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí). The first half may be an old deictic pronoun or particle or may derive from the root <i>&#42;h₂énts</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black cardamom (<i>Amomum subulatum</i>)</li></ol><p>An Oriental loanword of lost provenance, also found as Classical Syriac <i>ܚܡܵܡܵܐ</i> (ḥəmāmā) → Arabic حَمَامَا (ḥamāmā), and as Old Armenian <i>համեմ</i> (hamem).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Expresses potentiality or conditionality</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Epic,&#32;with subjunctive in main clauses) in that case and future tense</li><li>(<i>with optative</i>) Expresses future potentiality: would or could do or be doing</li><li>(<i>with imperfect indicative</i>) Expresses present or rarely past potentiality: were doing, would be doing</li><li>(<i>with aorist indicative</i>) Expresses past potentiality</li></ol><li>(<i>with past indicative</i>) Expresses unreality</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with aorist</i>) Past or rarely present unreality: would have done, would do</li><li>(<i>with imperfect</i>) Present or past unreality: would do, would be doing; would have been doing</li><li>(<i>with pluperfect</i>) Present or less commonly past unreality:</li></ol><li>(<i>with past indicative</i>) Expresses iteration or habituality: had done, had been doing; would have done, would have been doing; used to do</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with&#32;Ionic&#32;past iterative</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>with subjunctive in subordinate clauses</i>) Expresses generality, and often translatable with present or future tense in English</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>With <i>εἰ</i> (ei), contracted to <i>ἐάν</i> (eán), expresses a general condition that is likely to occur</li></ol><li>When combined with infinitive or participle in indirect speech, represents a finite verb with <i>ἄν</i> (án) in direct speech</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if</li></ol><p>Uncertain; has been connected to similar particles in other Indo-European languages, especially Latin <i>an</i> and Gothic <i>𐌰𐌽</i> (an), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂en</i>.</p><p>Beekes derives it instead from <i>οὐκ ᾰ̓́ν</i> (ouk ắn) as a rebracketing of <i>οὐ κᾰν</i> (ou kăn), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂óyu-kn̥</i> (literally “long time begin”), whence also synonymous Ionic <i>κε</i> (ke), <i>κεν</i> (ken).</p><p>Contraction of <i>ἐᾱ́ν</i> (eā́n), from a univerbation of <i>εἰ</i> (ei) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ν</i> (ắn, modal particle).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bloodless</li><li>(<i>of plants</i>) pallid</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unimportant</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>shedding no blood</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, negative prefix) + <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weak; feeble; ineffectual</li><li>cowardly; invalorous</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ν-</i> (ăn-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓λκή</i> (ălkḗ, “strength; force”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a man or a thing</i>) unmanly; cowardly</li><li>(<i>of a woman</i>) without a husband: not married</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not, without”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man, husband”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lord; king</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of men</i>)</li><li>(of gods,&#32;often&#32;Apollo and Zeus)</li></ol><li>master, owner</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ϝᾰ́νᾰξ</i> (wắnăx), <i>ϝᾰ́νᾰκος</i> (wắnăkos). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀷𐀙𐀏</i> (wa-na-ka) as well as Phrygian <i>ουανακταν</i> (ouanaktan /⁠wanaktan⁠/), Old Phrygian <i>ϝανακτει</i> (vanaktei), which may be an early loan from Greek or from a common third source.</p><p>The further origin is unknown, but likely a borrowing from a Pre-Greek substrate language. Various alternative Indo-European etymologies have been proposed, including:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without strength, spineless</li><li>unarticulated, mumbly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ἄρθρον</i> (árthron) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive sense</i>) without a ruler</li><li>(<i>active sense</i>) holding no office or magistracy</li><li>without beginning</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain torrent</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin. Others have interpreted the word as “waterless”, with <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “alpha privativum”) + an unknown word for water (connected with the name of various rivers, like Metaurus, Pisaurus and Avara). Niemeyer connects the word to Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;auraz</i> (“wet sand or earth, mud; water, sea”)</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄναυρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anaurus, a river of Thessaly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄναυρος</i> (ánauros, “torrent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raised bank by the side of a river or ditch</li><li>dam, dyke</li><li>border, edge of the sea</li><li>border for plants or flowers</li></ol><p>Neumann points to the fact that many words connected with irrigation don't look of Indo-European origin and, beside this word, he mentions <i>ἄρδω</i> (árdō, “to irrigate”) and <i>γόργῡρα</i> (górgūra, “underground drain”). Furnée thinks that the suffix "-ηρον" is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) asunder, in twain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) +&lrm; <i>δίχα</i> (díkha)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wind, breeze, gale</li><li>any of the four cardinal directions</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) flatus, intestinal gas</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ánemos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enh₁mos</i>, a nominal derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enh₁-</i> (“breathe”). Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀚𐀗</i> (a-ne-mo /⁠ánemoi⁠/, “winds”, pl.), disputedly Old Armenian <i>հողմ</i> (hołm, “wind”), Latin <i>animus</i>, Old Frisian <i>omma</i>, and possibly Sanskrit <i>अनिल</i> (ánila); Compare also Tocharian B <i>āñme</i> (“self; soul”) and Old Armenian <i>անձն</i> (anjn, “person”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a loosening, relaxing</li><li>remission, abatement</li><li>relaxation, recreation</li><li>indulgence, license</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνίημι</i> (aníēmi, “let go, loose”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>without</li></ol><p>Unknown. Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sń̥h₂u</i> or <i>&#42;sn̥h₂eu</i>, which could be derived from a more primary particle <i>&#42;sń̥</i> (“separated, without”) or the locative of some (hard to identify) noun. If this is correct, likely related to Sanskrit <i>सनुतर्</i> (sanutár, “far away”), Latin <i>sine</i> (“without”), Old Irish <i>sain-</i> (“separated”), Khotanese (anau, “without”), Tocharian B <i>snai</i> (“without”). Alternatively, cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ēnu ~ &#42;inu ~ &#42;ēneu</i> (“without”), both from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ḗn-u</i>, loc.sg. <i>&#42;h₂n-éw</i>, perhaps from <i>&#42;h₂en-</i> (“to scoop”) (via the sense “scooped away, removed (from)” or similar), but this relies on Eichner's law, which is controversial.</p><p>The suffix on the Megarian form requires further assumptions to explain. <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄνευθεν</i> (áneuthen)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄνευθεν</i> (áneuthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἄνευθεν</i> (áneuthen, “away, far away”)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἄνευθεν</i> (áneuthen, “without, away from”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>away</li><li>far away</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>without</li><li>away from</li></ol><p><i>ἄνευ</i> (áneu) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>silently, without speaking</li></ol><p>Apparently originally the masculine nominative plural of an otherwise unattested adjective <i>ἄνεως</i> (áneōs) (perhaps from <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>αὔω</i> (aúō, “to shout”)), and in some contexts still interpretable as such; but in other contexts it can only be an adverb.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dill, <i>Anethum graveolens</i></li></ol><p>Most likely Pre-Greek. Not to be confused with <i>ἄννησον</i> (ánnēson).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνθέω</i> (anthéō, “to flower”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or like flowers, blooming, fresh</li><li>bright-colored (of dresses)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flower; blossom; bloom</li><li>froth; scum</li><li>bloom; peak</li><li>brightness; brilliance</li><li>a small bird&#59; possibly the yellow wagtail</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ántʰos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éndʰos</i>. Cognate with Vedic Sanskrit <i>अन्धस्</i> (ándhas, “Soma, grass, herb”), Albanian <i>endë</i>, Old Armenian <i>անդ</i> (and, “field”), possibly Old Frisian <i>åndul</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charcoal</li><li>coal</li><li>a deep red precious stone, a carbuncle</li><li>an abscess, a boil, a carbuncle</li></ol><p>Uncertain; <i>Strong's Concordance</i> calls it a "primitive word". Possibly from a Mediterranean substrate; compare Old Armenian <i>անթայր</i> (antʻayr, “spark; anthrax”), <i>անթեղ</i> (antʻeł, “hot coal, ember”).</p><p>Others have connected the word to Old Norse <i>sintr</i>, German <i>Sinter</i> (“sinter”), Old English <i>sinder</i> (“cinder, ashes, slag”), all from <i>&#42;sindrą</i> (“dross, cinder, slag”), and via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sendʰro-</i> (“coagulating fluid, scale, cinder”) cognate to Old Church Slavonic <i>сядра</i> (sjadra, “lime cinder, gypsum”) (compare Serbo-Croatian <i>sadra</i>, Czech <i>sádra</i>). Kölligan suggests a connection to Sanskrit <i>अन्ध</i> (andha, “blind, darkness, etc.”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chervil (<i>Scandix australis</i>, southern shepherd's-needle)</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected with <i>ἀθήρ</i> (athḗr, “awn”), because of the prickly fruits. Because of the variations, a Pre-Greek origin seems certain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>human being; person (as differentiated from gods, beasts, etc.)</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) man; humanity</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;in the plural</i>) all human beings; mankind</li><li>(feminine,&#32;derogatory) female slave</li></ol><p>First attested in Hellenic as Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀵𐀫𐀦</i> (a-to-ro-qo), of uncertain origin. Scholars used to consider it to be a compound from <i>ᾰ̓νήρ</i> (ănḗr, “man”) and <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “face, appearance, look”): thus, "he who looks like a man". However, a <i>δ</i> (d) would be expected to develop by epenthesis, as in the genitive <i>ᾰ̓νδρός</i> (ăndrós), yielding <i>&#42;ᾰ̓νδρωπος</i> (&#42;ăndrōpos). Rosén defends this etymology, positing that the original laryngeal <i>&#42;h₃</i> in the root for <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ókʷs</i>) changed the δ to its aspirated counterpart θ even across the intervening ρ.</p><p>Beekes argues that since no convincing Indo-European etymology has been found, the word is probably of Pre-Greek origin; he connects the word with the word <i>δρώψ</i> (drṓps, “man”). According to Beekes (2009:xxix), "Shift of aspiration is found in some cases: θριγκός / τριγχός, ἀθραγένη / ἀνδράχνη".</p><p>Garnier proposes a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥dʰéreh₃kʷós</i> (“that which is below”) (from <i>&#42;(H)n̥dʰér</i> + <i>&#42;-h₃kʷ-o-</i>), hence "earthly, human", for this compare with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰéǵʰōm</i> (“earth”) whence Latin <i>homō</i>, Old Lithuanian <i>žmuõ</i> (“man”), Old English <i>guma</i> (“man”).</p><p>The word is treated in Plato's Cratylus.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inequality</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) that which is unequal to something else</li><li>(<i>ethics</i>) unfairness; injustice</li><li>(<i>theology</i>) the incommensurate; that which does not properly fit or match</li><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓́ννησον</i> (ắnnēson, “anise”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unequal, uneven</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos, “equal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anise (<i>Pimpinella anisum</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>jnst</i> (“a medicinal plant, possibly anise”). However, according to Beekes, the <i>ν&#47;νν</i> (n&#47;nn) variation, intervocalic <i>-σ-</i> (-s-) and connection to <i>ἄνηθον</i> (ánēthon, “dill”) prove Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>way up, ascent, climb</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνά</i> (aná, “up”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way, road”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack of understanding, folly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνοος</i> (ánoos, “silly, witless”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾱ</i> (-ĭā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>houseless, homeless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not, without”) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house, home”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pitiless, ruthless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>οἶκτος</i> (oîktos, “pity”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lawless, impious</li><li>without the Mosaic law, hence without God</li><li>(<i>music</i>) unmusical, without melody</li><li>(<i>of a sequestration</i>) illegal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, without) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law, ordinance; melody”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shieldless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀν-</i> (an-) +&lrm; <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon) + <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄντα</i> (ánta)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἄντα</i> (ánta)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>over against, face to face</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>over against, opposite</li><li>before, confronted with</li></ol><p>Commonly derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éntm̥</i>, accusative singular of <i>&#42;h₂énts</i>.</p><p>Alternatively, Dunkel suggests a 'directive' <i>&#42;h₂ént-o</i> from the same root noun, with subsequent alpha harmony in Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>opposite</li><li>in front</li><li>in view</li><li>openly</li></ol><p>Connected to <i>ἀντίον</i> (antíon) and <i>ἀντί</i> (antí), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ent-</i> (“front”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hold of a ship</li><li>bilgewater</li><li>scuttle, sink</li><li>bucket, pail</li><li>heap of corn, threshed but not yet cleaned</li></ol><p>Connection with Latin <i>sentīna</i> (“bilgewater”) is impossible in Indo-European terms, as <i>&#42;sm̥-</i> would give <i>ἁ-</i> (ha-) and not <i>ἁν-</i> (han-). Has been compared with Hittite (ḫan-, “to draw water”), although it requires an unusual suffix <i>-τλος</i> (-tlos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living in the same longitude, but in the opposite hemisphere</li><li>(<i>LSJ</i>, <i>wrong</i>) living in the same latitude, but in the opposite hemisphere</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “opposite”) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) cave</li></ol><p>A Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rim of a round shield</li><li>rail around the chariot</li><li>the chariot itself</li><li>bridge of the lyre</li><li>outermost tier in a theatre</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) orbit of a planet</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) basis of a cupola</li></ol><p>Unknown. Previously explained as <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “up”) + a root noun belonging to <i>τεύχω</i> (teúkhō, “to make; to do”), but this is highly questionable. The word resembles <i>ᾰ̓́μπῠξ</i> (ắmpŭx) and <i>κᾰταῖτῠξ</i> (kătaîtŭx), both without etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with verbs of motion</i>) upwards</li><li>above, high up</li><li>(<i>of motion</i>) inland, away from the coast</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνά</i> (aná, “up, upon”) +&lrm; <i>-ω</i> (-ō, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, command, bid</li><li>to advise, urge, prompt</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-) + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁e-h₁oǵ-h₂e</i>, perfect of <i>&#42;h₁eǵ-</i> (“to say”) (compare <i>ἠμί</i> (ēmí), Latin <i>āiō</i>, Old Armenian <i>ասեմ</i> (asem)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from above</li><li>above</li><li>in the world of the living</li><li>from inland</li><li>from the north</li><li>from farther back (<i>in writing or conversation</i>)</li><li>again, afresh</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́νω</i> (ắnō) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>counterbalancing, weighing as much as, of like value</li><li>worthy, fit</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Christianity) Acclamation confirming the election of a Christian bishop or patriarch, (<i>literally</i>) "Worthy!" &lsqb;from c. 4th century&rsqb;</li></ol><p>According to some scholars, from <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead, draw out or down”) +&lrm; <i>-τιος</i> (-tios) (perhaps via an unattested intermediate <i>ἄξις</i> (áxis, “weight”)), with sense development "draw down (in the scale)" > "weigh, import". Beekes expresses mild doubts about this, both in the semantics as well as no other morphological derivative of <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō) being in <i>-τι-</i> (-ti-), but tentatively accepts this etymology. For a semantic parallel, compare Latin <i>exāmen</i> (“tongue of a balance; examining”), from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Cretan,&#32;hapax legomenon) cliff, crag</li></ol><p>Uncertain. If it is related to synonymous <i>ἀγμός</i> (agmós, “fracture; cliff”), perhaps from the same stem as <i>ἄγνυμι</i> (ágnumi, “to break, shatter”), as already implied by Stephanus of Byzantium. Otherwise from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-s-</i> (“sharp edge”) (like <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp”)), or Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an axle</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) an axis of a solid</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of a cone</li><li>of a conic section</li><li>of a cylinder</li></ol><li>the axis of the celestial sphere</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a course or path of action</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) C2, the axis (the second cervical vertebra)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aksōn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱs-</i>. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀒𐀰𐀚</i> (a-ko-so-ne), Sanskrit <i>अक्ष</i> (akṣa, “axle, axis, balance beam”) and Latin <i>axis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>houseless, homeless</li><li>miserable (<i>of a home</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not, without”) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “house, home”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>resolute, without hesitation, industrious</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ὄκνος</i> (óknos, “hesitation”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unseen</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>ὀπτός</i> (optós, “seen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hanger or sword hung in a belt</li><li>any kind of weapon</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wor</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂wer-</i> (whence <i>ᾰ̓είρω</i> (ăeírō)). Alternately, from the same root as Sanskrit <i>अ॒सि</i> (así) and Latin <i>ēnsis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) prison</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but possibly from <i>ἄπαστος</i> (ápastos, “fasting”). Otherwise, maybe of Semitic origin, compare Arabic حَبَّسَ (ḥabbasa, “to imprison”), Hebrew <i>חָבַשׁ</i> (ḥāḇaš, “to bind, tie”) and Safaitic (ḥabīsat, “enclosure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfed, fasting</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>πατέομαι</i> (patéomai, “to eat”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stateless (without a country)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>πᾰ́τρᾱ</i> (pắtrā, “fatherland”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>level, flat, even</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>πέδον</i> (pédon, “ground, earth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go away, depart</li><li>(<i>of the Nile</i>) to recede</li><li>to be absent</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>εἶμῐ</i> (eîmĭ, “go”).</p><p>From <i>ᾰ̓πο-</i> (ăpo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “be”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without trial or experience, not used to, ignorant of, unacquainted with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>absolute</i>) inexperienced, ignorant</li></ol><li>boundless, unlimited, infinite</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in Tragedy,&#32;often of garments) in which one is entangled past escape, endless, without outlet</li><li>endless, circular</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἤπειρος</i> (ḗpeiros)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πεῖρα</i> (peîra, “trial, attempt”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Compare with Latin <i>peritus</i>.</p><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πεῖραρ</i> (peîrar), <i>πέρας</i> (péras, “end, limit”) + <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p><p>See <i>ἤπειρος</i> (ḗpeiros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pear</li></ol><p>Related to Latin <i>pirum</i> (“pear”) and considered to be a Mediterranean substrate loanword of the form <i>*(V)pis-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pear tree (<i>Pyrus communis</i>)</li><li>kind of spurge (<i>Euphorbia apios</i>)</li></ol><p>From the same substrate root as <i>ᾰ̓́πῐον</i> (ắpĭon, “pear”) and Latin <i>pirum</i> (“pear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not to be trusted</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of persons and their acts</i>) untrustworthy, faithless, groundless</li><li>(<i>of reports and the like</i>) incredible</li></ol><li>not believing or trusting, distrustful, mistrustful, incredulous, suspicious</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the New Testament</i>) unbelieving, a non-believer</li><li>not obeying, disobedient</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πῐστός</i> (pĭstós, “believable, believing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>insatiable, greedy</li><li>limitless, interminable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi, “to fill full, satisfy, satiate”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>want of wind, calm</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄπνοος</i> (ápnoos, “without wind”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without wind, with but little air</li><li>unventilated, lacking ventilation</li><li>breathless, gasping</li><li>lifeless, dead</li><li>without breathing or respiration</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>πνοή</i> (pnoḗ, “breath”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>away from home, abroad (<i>sometimes followed by genitive</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>settler, colonist</li><li>colony</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away from”) +&lrm; <i>οἶκος</i> (oîkos, “home”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ransom</li><li>compensation (recompense or reward for some loss or service)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without toil or trouble, effortless</li><li>painless; free from pain</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) workshy, lazy</li><li>relieving pain, soothing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πόνος</i> (pónos, “toil, suffering”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without passage, impassable, having no way through</li><li>hard, difficult</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) hard to deal with, unmanageable</li><li>not knowing what to do, at a loss</li><li>poor, needy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “passage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without feet, footless</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>without the use of one's feet, lame</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swift (<i>Apus apus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outlook, view</li><li>that on which one looks, view, prospect</li></ol><p><i>ἀπο-</i> (apo-, “away”) +&lrm; <i>ὄψις</i> (ópsis, “view”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unsuccessful (failed, not successful)</li><li>unavailing (fruitless, futile, useless)</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>πράσσω</i> (prássō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dowryless, without a dowry</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, negative prefix) +&lrm; <i>προίξ</i> (proíx, “dowry”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wingless, unwinged</li><li>featherless, unfeathered</li><li>unfledged, callow (<i>of young birds</i>)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) unconfirmed (<i>of a report or claim</i>)</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) fast, quick</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;neuter) bat (mammal)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>assless; having no buttocks</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”, alpha privative) +&lrm; <i>πῡγή</i> (pūgḗ, “buttocks”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without fire, fireless</li><li>untouched by fire</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>term of endearment, used as an address between siblings or beloved ones</li></ol><p>Of sound-symbolic (German <i>Lallwort</i>) origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from far away</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>far from</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρᾰ</i> (ắră, “so; then; therefore; consequently”) before a consonant</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρᾰ</i> (ắră, “so; then; therefore; consequently”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>so; then; therefore; consequently</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“thus, so”), probably as a grammaticalization of the sense "to fit, fix". Cognates include Lithuanian <i>ir̃</i> (“and; so”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gnashing or chattering of teeth</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) rattling, ringing</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>θόρυβος</i> (thórubos) and <i>κόναβος</i> (kónabos). Perhaps onomatopoeic, but Furnée compares <i>ἀροπῆσαι</i> (aropêsai, “to tread”), which points to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disturbance</li><li>palpitation (of the heart)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>κέλαδος</i> (kélados) and <i>ὅμαδος</i> (hómados). Perhaps onomatopoeic but compare <i>ἄραβος</i> (árabos).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄραδος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aradus; Arwad, Syria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowl, pan</li></ol><p>The form <i>ἀράκτης</i> (aráktēs) proves Pre-Greek origin, in view of the group <i>-κτ-</i>. Furnée further compares <i>ἄροκλον</i> (ároklon, “bowl, pan”) with <i>κτ>κλ</i>, for which he gives parallels. The interchange <i>α/ο</i> is well known in substrate words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild chickling vetch, <i>Lathyrus annuus</i></li></ol><p>Likely of Pre-Greek substrate origin, due to the κ–χ interchange with the variant <i>ἄραχος</i> (árakhos), and the suffix <i>-ιδνα</i> (-idna) in the derivative <i>ἀράχιδνα</i> (arákhidna). According to Beekes, not related to Latin <i>arinca</i> (“kind of spelt; olyra”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>semicircular knife used by cobblers</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) geometrical figure contained by three semicircles with coincident diameters</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Macedonia</i>) vapour bath, steam bath</li></ol><p>Related to Albanian <i>ragal</i> (“kennel”). The interchanges <i>ε/ι</i> and <i>λ/λλ</i> clearly point to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) albugo, a white speck on the eye</li><li>herb bennet, avens (<i>Geum urbanum</i>)</li><li>upper part of the fingernail</li></ol><p>Connected to <i>&#42;ἄργος</i> (&#42;árgos) in <i>ἀργεστής</i> (argestḗs, “north-west wind”) and <i>ἀργεννός</i> (argennós, “white”), like <i>ἄνθεμον</i> (ánthemon) to <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”), yet the latter derivation is difficult. Further connected to <i>ἀργός</i> (argós, “white”). Regarding the plant significations this has together with <i>ἀργεμώνη</i> (argemṓnē, “pale poppy, <i>Papaver argemone</i>; agrimony”) been put to Akkadian <i>𒅈𒂵𒌋𒌋𒉡</i> (argamannu, “purple”) which has given rise to the names of various purple plants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>white clay; potter's clay; argil</li></ol><p>Generally derived from <i>ᾰ̓ργός</i> (ărgós, “white”), but the suffix cannot be Indo-European, due to the <i>-ῐλλ-&#47;-ῑλ-</i> (-ĭll-&#47;-īl-) variation and the variations in the ending. Beekes suggests that a Pre-Greek origin is possible, and that no semantic connection wiwth <i>ἀργός</i> (argós) is evident.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) firstlings at a feast or sacrifices</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρχ(ω)</i> (árkh(ō), “I begin”) + suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma) in the plural -ματα with &#42;ἀρχ-μα &gt; &#42;ἀργ-μα, of which this form is the plural.</p><p><i>&#42;ἄρχματα</i> (&#42;árkhmata) is found in <i>ἀπάρχματα θεοῖς</i> (apárkhmata theoîs, “sacrifices for the gods”) at Hesychius.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>silver</li><li>money</li></ol><p>From a <i>u</i>-stem of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erǵ-</i> (“white, argent”). Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀓𐀫</i> (a-ku-ro), Latin <i>argentum</i>, Sanskrit <i>अर्जुन</i> (árjuna) and Old Armenian <i>արծաթ</i> (arcatʻ). See also <i>ἀργός</i> (argós, “white, bright”) and <i>ἄργυφος</i> (árguphos, “white, shining”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lifted up on high</li><li>completely, utterly</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>ἀέρδην</i> (aérdēn), from the root of <i>ἀείρω</i> (aeírō, “to lift”) +&lrm; <i>-δην</i> (-dēn, deverbal adverb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point of an arrow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erdi-</i> (“edge, point”); see also Sanskrit <i>अलि</i> (ali, “scorpion, bee”), Old Norse <i>erta</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;artijaną</i> (“stimulate, stir up, tease”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to water, irrigate</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be watered</li><li>to give drink to cattle</li><li>(<i>of rivers</i>) to furnish drink for men</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to foster, cherish</li><li>to guzzle, drink voraciously</li><li>to pour forth, spill, gush</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>παρδακός</i> (pardakós, “humid”) (highly doubtful) and <i>ἄρσεα</i> (ársea, “humid meadows”). The latter proposal is attractive, as <i>-σος</i> is a typical Pre-Greek suffix. Furnée also follows the proposal by Kretschmer that <i>ᾱ̓-</i> is due to a pre-form <i>*ἀϝάρδω</i>, the prothetic vowel of which he explains as a substrate element. Neumann noted that several technical terms for irrigation are Pre-Greek, like <i>ἄνδηρον</i> (ándēron, “raised bank by the side of a ditch”) and <i>γόργῡρα</i> (górgūra, “underground drain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>warlike, martial</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρης</i> (Árēs, “Ares”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios); compare <i>Ἄρειος</i> (Áreios, “pertaining to Ares”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄρειος)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to Ares</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρης</i> (Árēs, “Ares”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios); compare <i>ἄρειος</i> (áreios, “warlike, martial”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) joint</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) limb</li><li>articulation</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) connecting word</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) an article: a category including the definite article <i>ὁ</i> (ho) and the basic relative pronoun <i>ὅς</i> (hós)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-dʰrom</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-dʰrom</i> (“tool or instrument suffix”), synchronically analyzable as <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: Cypriot word for <i>τάφος</i> (táphos, “grave, tomb”).</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not; without) +&lrm; <i>ῥίζα</i> (rhíza, “root”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>morning meal, breakfast (early usage, i.e. Homeric)</li><li>lunch (later usage, replaced with <i>ἀκράτισμα</i> (akrátisma) as word for breakfast)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ayerīston</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eyeri-h₁d-to-</i>. First part in <i>ἦρι</i> (êri, “early”), second is zero-grade of <i>ἔδω</i> (édō, “to eat”), thus “early meal”.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excellent, superb, superlative, surpassing</li><li>(<i>but especially</i>): (of a person) best in social rank ("noblest"), (of a person) best in valor ("bravest"), (of a person) best in morality ("most ethical/moral"), (of any thing) best in usefulness/utility ("most useful")</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éristos</i> (“fittest, most fitting, most suitable”), the superlative of <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit, be suitable”). Compare <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “to join, fasten”); also cognate with Hittite (arra-, “wash”), Tocharian A <i>yär-</i> (“wash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excellent, superb, superlative, surpassing</li><li>(<i>but especially</i>): (of a person) best in social rank ("noblest"), (of a person) best in valor ("bravest"), (of a person) best in morality ("most ethical/moral"), (of any thing) best in usefulness/utility ("most useful")</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éristos</i> (“fittest, most fitting, most suitable”), the superlative of <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit, be suitable”). Compare <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “to join, fasten”); also cognate with Hittite (arra-, “wash”), Tocharian A <i>yär-</i> (“wash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juniper (<i>Juniperus macrocarpa</i>)</li><li>Phoenician cedar (<i>Juniperus phoenicea</i>)</li><li>prickly cedar (<i>Juniperus oxycedrus</i>)</li></ol><p>Unclear. Has been derived from <i>ᾰ̓́ρκῠς</i> (ắrkŭs, “net”). Has also been derived from Proto-Indo-European, with cognates such as Russian <i>раки́та</i> (rakíta). Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin, comparing the Hesychian gloss <i>ἄργετος</i> (árgetos, “juniper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young panther or a species of panther</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Furnée, the suffix could suggest a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burdock (<i>Arctium lappa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρκος</i> (árkos, “bear”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bear</li><li>the north</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;árktos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ŕ̥tḱos</i> (“bear”). Cognates include Latin <i>ursus</i>, Welsh <i>arth</i>, Sanskrit <i>ऋक्ष</i> (ṛ́kṣa), Old Armenian <i>արջ</i> (arǰ), and Albanian <i>ari</i>. The meaning of “north” derives from <i>Ἄρκτος</i> (Árktos, “Ursa Major”), because the constellation is in the northern part of the sky.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄρκτος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Ursa Major</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρκτος</i> (árktos, “bear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>net</li><li>hairnet</li></ol><p>Unknown. Accoridng to Lidén, related to <i>ἄρκευθος</i> (árkeuthos), <i>ἀρκᾰ́νη</i> (arkắnē) and Russian <i>раки́та</i> (rakíta, “willow”) but Beekes finds this improbable, as a derivative suffix would be expected. According to him, it is more probable a Pre-Greek word, giving the technical nature of the term.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) tackle of a ship, sails</li><li>implement, instrument, tool, equipment</li><li>food, nourishment</li></ol><p>Literally, "what has been fitted out", "equipment", a substantivization from <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “to fit together; equip”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a lamb or sheep, ovine</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἀρήν</i> (arḗn, “lamb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get, win, gain, achieve, receive (<i>usually in positive sense</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(present,&#32;imperfect) to strive for, try to get</li></ol><li>to take up, bear, carry</li></ol><p>A present <i>nu</i>-stem from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to take”), unrelated to <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “fit together”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>առնում</i> (aṙnum, “take”) and probably Avestan <i>𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬥𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬌𐬧𐬙𐬌</i> (ər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>nuua<sup><small>i</small></sup>ṇti, “they grant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuckoopint (<i>Arum italicum</i>)</li></ol><p>Hemmerdinger derived it from Egyptian <i>r</i> (“reed, cane”). Perhaps it is also contained in <i>ἀρίς</i> (arís) and <i>ἀρίσαρον</i> (arísaron). A comparison with Latin <i>arundo</i> (“cane”) is less probable. Compare the synonym <i>ὀρόντιον</i> (oróntion), which may imply Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plough</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(figuratively,&#32;in the plural) the organs of generation</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;árotron</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂érh₃trom</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂erh₃-</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;-trom</i>; equivalent to <i>ἀρόω</i> (aróō, “to plough”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron, instrument noun suffix). Cognates include Latin <i>arātrum</i>, Old Armenian <i>արաւր</i> (arawr) and Old Norse <i>arðr</i> (→ modern English <i>ard</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cultivated land; a cultivated field</li><li>ground; earth</li><li>arura</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ároura</i>, from the Proto-Indo-European heteroclitic noun <i>&#42;h₂érh₃-wr̥ ~ &#42;h₂r̥h₃-wén-s</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erh₃-</i>. There are several competing hypothesis that seek to explain the exact means by which the term emerged from the Proto-Indo-European heteroclitic noun:</p><p>Cognate with Old Irish <i>arbe</i> (“corn”), Sanskrit <i>उ॒र्वरा॑</i> (urvárā, “fertile soil”).</p><p>Identical to Etymology 1, with semantic loan from Egyptian <i>sṯꜣt</i> (“arura”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>According to Hesychius of Alexandria:</i>) a kind of thornbush, thistle</li></ol><p>Unknown. The variation in <i>-γδα</i> and the form itself suggest a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unbroken (whole, not divided into parts)</li><li>unbreakable (difficult or impossible to break)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>ῥήγνῡμι</i> (rhḗgnūmi, “to break, shatter”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρσην</i> (ắrsēn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(religion,&#32;moral) things not to be spoken; secrets, taboos, mysteries</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;philosophy) irrational numbers, surds</li></ol><p>Neuter plural of <i>ἄρρητος</i> (árrhētos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unspoken</li><li>that cannot be spoken or expressed</li><li>unspeakable, immense</li><li>not to be spoken or divulged</li><li>unutterable, horrible</li><li>shameful to be spoken</li><li>(<i>of numbers</i>) irrational</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ῥητός</i> (rhētós, “spoken”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Having no rhythm; rhythmless, arhythmic</li><li>Having no moderation; excessive</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”, “not”) +&lrm; <i>ῥυθμός</i> (rhuthmós, “rhythm”, “measure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ill, sickly</li><li>morally feeble</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, privative alpha) +&lrm; <i>ῥώννυμι</i> (rhṓnnumi, “to strengthen”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>λειμῶνες</i> (leimônes, “meadows”)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Schwyzer suggested a relation with <i>ἄρδω</i> (árdō, “to irrigate”), with a suffix <i>-σος</i> like in <i>ἄλσος</i> (álsos, “grove”). The suffix <i>-σος</i>, however, may be a substrate element. Gusmani compares Hittite (arši, “plantation”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>male</li><li>masculine, manly, strong</li><li>(<i>of plants</i>) coarse, tough</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) masculine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;(w)ə́rsēn</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wérsēn</i> (“male”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>ऋषभ</i> (ṛṣabha), <i>वृषन्</i> (vṛṣan), <i>वृष</i> (vṛṣa), Latin <i>verrēs</i>, Old Persian (aršan), and Old Armenian <i>առն</i> (aṙn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raising; elevation</li></ol><p>From <i>αἴρω</i> (aírō, “raise, lift, elevate”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher, cook</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) murderer</li></ol><p>Originally explained as a haplology of <i>&#42;ἀρτί-ταμος</i> (&#42;artí-tamos) or <i>&#42;ἀρτό-ταμος</i> (&#42;artó-tamos), with the meaning "artful cutting". However, this explanation makes little sense and DELG objects that <i>-τομος</i> should be expected, instead of <i>-ταμος</i>. Furnée thinks it is a Pre-Greek word; compare however Classical Syriac <i>ܩܰܪܛܶܡ</i> (qarṭem, “to cut short, to trim”): the abundance of such roots like ق ر ط (q r ṭ) and ق ر ض (q r ḍ) across Semitic, as well the consideration that butchers probably were institutionalized earlier in the Orient, may suggest a Semitic borrowing. Similarly, Arabic قَصَّاب (qaṣṣāb, “butcher”) has been borrowed from the Aramaic language which ruled in the area in question.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earring</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρτάω</i> (artáō, “fasten”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly of the present</i>) this moment, even now</li><li>(<i>of the past</i>) just now, just</li><li>(Koine,&#32;of the future)</li></ol><p>Appears to be a locative of a derivative of the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit”). Compare <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō), Old Armenian <i>արդ</i> (ard), Lithuanian <i>arti</i>, and Latin <i>ars</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>complete, perfect, suitable, exactly fitted</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>full-grown, sound of body and mind</li><li>prepared, ready</li></ol><li>(<i>of numbers</i>) perfect, even</li><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) just, newly, now, first</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with present tense</i>) of present time</li><li>(<i>with imperfect tense</i>) of past time</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ρτῐ</i> (ắrtĭ, “just”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cake or loaf of wheat bread</li><li>(<i>collectively</i>) bread</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin, though likely borrowed from a substrate. Compare Basque <i>arto</i> (“maize”), Old Spanish <i>artal</i> (“a type of empanada”). Pisani's derivation as a borrowing from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;arta-</i> (“flour”), and thus connection to Persian آرد (ârd, “flour”), is less likely, as the Greek word was already attested in Mycenean and is unable to be formally derived from the same Indo-European root that the Iranian stems from.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>condiment, seasoning</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρτύω</i> (artúō, “to dress, season”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wicker basket</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>ἀρύω</i> (arúō, “to draw water”), but it is improbable. In view of the suffix and the vowel variation "α/ε", the word is probably of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown kind of seafish mentioned by Aristotle</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin and related to other fish names, such as <i>ἀχάρνας</i> (akhárnas, “sea bass”) and Latin <i>acernia</i> (“grouper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to begin [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘something, from something, with something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lead, rule, govern, command [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be ruler; to hold an archonship</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruler</li><li>archon, a chief magistrate at Athens</li></ol><p>A noun use of the present participle of <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herb, spice</li><li>Anything fragrant: oil, perfume</li><li>arable land</li></ol><p>From a root <i>&#42;ἄρ-</i> (&#42;ár-) of uncertain origin + a suffix <i>-ωμα</i> (-ōma). The root is possibly from <i>αἴρω</i> (aírō, “to take up, raise, exalt”), according to Strong, in the sense of sending off a scent.</p><p>From <i>ἀρόω</i> (aróō, “to till”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without flesh, lean</li><li>(<i>of a diet</i>) not consisting in flesh</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>σᾰ́ρξ</i> (sắrx, “flesh”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hazelwort (<i>Asarum europaeum</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Clearly related to some names of coltsfoot given by 1st century interpolations to Dioskurides, <i>ἀσᾶ</i> (asâ) with the Thracians of Βῆσσα, <i>σααρθρά</i> (saarthrá) with the Egyptians. Hazelwort and coltsfoot sharing a name by superficial similarity is well-known, both being meanings of Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;kopytъnikъ</i>, and from coltsfoot one even went to dock with Proto-Kartvelian <i>&#42;ṭerep-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unquenchable, inextinguishable</li><li>endless, ceaseless</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unslaked lime, calcium oxide, quicklime</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>σβεστός</i> (sbestós), participle of <i>σβέννυμι</i> (sbénnumi, “to quench, quell”). The noun is shortened from <i>ἄσβεστος τίτανος</i> (ásbestos títanos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>soot</li></ol><p>Furnée suggests a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the rare cluster "-σβ-" and the variant <i>σποδός</i> (spodós, “ashes”). According to Beekes, the word is also connected with <i>ψόλος</i> (psólos, “soot, smoke”) and <i>σπολεύς</i> (spoleús, “loaf of bread”). Compare also <i>ἄσφαλτος</i> (ásphaltos, “bitumen, asphalt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surfeit, loathing, nausea</li><li>distress, vexation</li><li>longing, desire</li></ol><p>If the word originally meant "surfeit", then it could derive from <i>ἄω</i> (áō, “to satiate”). Unlikely is the suggestion by Solmsen, who assumed an analogically preserved suffix <i>-σᾱ-</i> from the zero grade root <i>ἀ-</i> &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂-</i>, rejecting a pre-form <i>&#42;sh₂-ti̯-eh₂-</i>. It would be better to posit <i>&#42;(h)ad-s-ā</i>, an old collective from the <i>s-</i>stem in <i>ἅδος</i> (hádos, “satiation”); problematic, however, is the failure of a geminate <i>-σσ-</i> to appear in Aeolic. The simplification to <i>-σ-</i> would be due to epic influence, according to Schwyzer, but all in all, the explanation is not entirely convincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plate, silver</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἄσημος</i> (ásēmos, “uncoined, without marks”), which is, by surface analysis, <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not, a-”) +&lrm; <i>σῆμα</i> (sêma, “mark, sign”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without mark or sign</li><li>uncoined (<i>of a precious metal</i>)</li><li>giving no sign, unintelligible</li><li>leaving no mark, indistinct, inarticulate</li><li>unperceived, unnoticed</li><li>unremarkable, unknown, obscure, mean</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;masculine) silver</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>σῆμα</i> (sêma) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without mark or sign</li><li>uncoined (<i>of a precious metal</i>)</li><li>giving no sign, unintelligible</li><li>leaving no mark, indistinct, inarticulate</li><li>unperceived, unnoticed</li><li>unremarkable, unknown, obscure, mean</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;masculine) silver</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>σῆμα</i> (sêma) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yoke for carrying baskets or pails</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the ending in <i>-ιλλ-</i> points to a Pre-Greek origin. Compare <i>ἅμιλλα</i> (hámilla) and <i>ἄργιλλα</i> (árgilla).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mud, slime</li></ol><p>Unknown. The comparison with Sanskrit <i>असित</i> (asita, “dark, black”) presupposes that the <i>-σ-</i> in this word was preserved after <i>&#42;-n̥-</i>. Nikolaev pleads for a reconstruction <i>&#42;h₂nsi-</i> for these words, but there is no compelling evidence for <i>h₂-</i>. Compared also to Hittite <i>ḫanzana-</i> (“black”). However, the etymology is still uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without food, fasting</li><li>lacking in appetite</li><li>of forbidden food</li><li>devoid of grain</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>σῖτος</i> (sîtos, “grain; food”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of castanet</li><li>kind of shoe</li></ol><p>The musical instrument was a square with strings, so it probably has nothing to do with the shoe. Furnée analyses the shoe as a suffix <i>-αρος</i> added to <i>ἀσκός</i> (askós, “skin, bag made of skin”). The similarity with <i>ἀσκέρα</i> (askéra, “winter shoe”) could also suggest a Pre-Greek origin of this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconsiderate, unreflecting</li><li>unconsidered, unobserved</li><li>unseen, hidden</li><li>too small to be observed, negligible</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai, “to see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>training, exercise, practice</li><li>(<i>in warfare</i>) branch of the service, arm</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσκέω</i> (askéō, to train, practice) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exercise, training, practice</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀσκέω</i> (askéō, “to train”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shadowless</li><li>(<i>of colour</i>) shady, dull</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>σκιᾱ́</i> (skiā́, “shadow”).</p><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, intensifier) +&lrm; <i>σκιᾱ́</i> (skiā́, “shadow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inconsiderate, heedless, unregarding</li><li>not to be seen, invisible</li><li>not to be understood, unintelligible</li><li>bewildering, strange</li><li>aimless, without a goal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>σκοπέω</i> (skopéō, “to consider, examine”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>σκοπός</i> (skopós, “aim, goal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>St. John's wort (<i>Hypericum perforatum</i>)</li><li>water plantain (<i>Alisma plantago-aquatica</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, the prothetic vowel points to Pre-Greek origin. On the other hand, Lewy connects the word with Hebrew <i>שחור</i> (shakhór, “black”), because the leaves of this plant have small black dots.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>always with a verb</i>) glad, well-pleased, gratified</li></ol><p>Unknown. An isolated participle, which has been interpreted as a sigmatic aorist <i>ϝάδ-σμενος</i>, from the same root of <i>ἁνδάνω</i> (handánō, “to please, delight”) and <i>ἥδομαι</i> (hḗdomai, “to be pleased”), but note that the form has no aspiration. Wackernagel proposed connection with <i>νέομαι</i> (néomai, “to return”), assuming an original meaning "safe". Not compelling. See also the discussion page.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”).</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kʷálos</i> (“sheatfish”) and cognate with Latin <i>squalus</i> (“kind of big fish, shark”), Avestan <i>𐬐𐬀𐬭𐬀</i> (kara, “kind of fish”), Old Prussian <i>kalis</i>, and Old English <i>hwæl</i> (“whale”). However, according to Beekes, it is rather a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexpressible</li><li>(<i>especially</i>) inexpressibly great, vast</li><li><b>indescribable</b> (of a sound or sight)</li><li>(of the charging of the Titans, a noise) <b>unspeakable, terrible</b></li><li>impossible to tell of (w. respect to magnitude); (of the aither, a body of water, forest, the ground) <b>unspeakably great, immeasurable</b> (of a wooden stake, pig's belly, of a journey, of a tumult)</li><li>(of a breeze) exceptionally strong</li><li>impossible to tell of (w. respect to quantity); (of animals, riches, gifts, cares) <b>countless</b> (of an army) <b>immense</b> (of smoke, rainfall, hail, sweat, gore)</li><li><b>abundant</b> (of meat, money, a harvest, foliage, liquid)</li><li>(of panting) <b>heavy</b></li><li>impossible to tell of (w. respect to duration);(of a person's voice) <b>endless</b> (of a period of time)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-skʷ-e-tos</i>, from <i>&#42;n̥-</i> (“un-”) (<i>ἀ-</i> (a-)) + <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“to say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miltwaste (<i>Asplenium ceterach</i>)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>σπλήν</i> (splḗn, “spleen”), because this plant would cure anthrax.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without libation or drink-offering</li><li>(<i>of a god</i>) to whom no drink-offering is poured</li><li>without a regular truce, without making a truce</li><li>admitting of no truce, implacable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>σπονδή</i> (spondḗ, “libation”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Turkey oak, <i>Quercus cerris</i></li></ol><p>Unclear. Could be related to Old High German <i>aspa</i>, but the connection is problematic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>invisible, transparent</li><li>white</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) silver</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) egg white</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) name of an ingredient of incense</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>asper</i> (“rough, sharp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unstable</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) + the root of <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) ear of corn</li><li>bandage</li></ol><p>Previously connected with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;stangō</i> (“bar; rod; stake”), but most likely a Pre-Greek word in view of the prothetic vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without roof, houseless</li><li>unprotected, exposed</li><li>not holding</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) unable to keep one's mouth shut</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not; without) +&lrm; <i>στέγος</i> (stégos, “roof”).</p><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not; without) +&lrm; <i>στέγω</i> (stégō, “to cover closely, hold”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A celestial body:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fixed star</li><li>planet</li><li>the sun</li><li>constellation</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr, “star”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a town, city; the inhabited part of a city in particular, rather than the citadel</li></ol><p>From <i>ϝᾰ́στῠ</i> (wắstŭ), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wástu</i>; cognate with Sanskrit <i>वास्तु॑</i> (vā́stu, “site of a house”), Latin <i>Vesta</i>, Tocharian A <i>waṣt</i>, Tocharian B <i>ost</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄστυ)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athens</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓́στῠδε</i> (ắstŭde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>into, to, or towards the city</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small city or town</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ, “city; town”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sanctuary</li></ol><p>Substantivization of <i>ἄσυλος</i> (ásulos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>safe from violence, inviolate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>σύλη</i> (súlē, “seizure, plunder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>asphalt, bitumen</li></ol><p>Generally taken as deriving from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>σφάλλω</i> (sphállō, “to fall, cast down”), under the assumption that it denoted the material that protects walls from tumbling down. However, according to Beekes, the word is rather Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>misshapen, ugly, deformed</li><li>unseemly, shameful, indecent, inappropriate</li></ol><p>Later form of <i>ἀσχήμων</i> (askhḗmōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inspissated juice of the fruit of the bird cherry</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>literally</i>) not saved, abandoned</li><li>debauched, dissolute</li><li>profligate, spendthrift</li><li>(<i>+ dative</i>) bringing ruin upon</li></ol><p>Verbal adjective of <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>σῴζω</i> (sōízō, “save”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>military</i>) (<i>of troops</i>) Not in battle-order.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Not at one's post.</li></ol><li>Undisciplined, disorderly; irregular; casual.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Irregular, inordinate. (<i>of sensual excess</i>)</li><li>(<i>music</i>) Without rhythm.</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) Irregular.</li><li>Uncivilized, lawless.</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) Indeterminate, not admitting of a definite solution.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not, without”) +&lrm; <i>τάσσω</i> (tássō, “draw up in order of battle, form, array, marshal”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>without</li><li>apart from</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sn̥Hter</i> (whence also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sunder</i> (“apart; separately”)), from <i>&#42;senH-</i>; cognate with <i>ᾰ̓́νευ</i> (ắneu), as well as Latin <i>sine</i> and Sanskrit <i>स॒नि॒तुर्</i> (sanitúr)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unskilled, untalented, unartistic, uncreative, unimaginative</li><li>unprofessional, unworkmanlike, ignorant of the rules of art</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τέχνη</i> (tékhnē) +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disaster, misfortune, ruin</li><li>delusion, folly</li><li>error, fault, mischief</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ἀάω</i> (aáō, “I hurt or damage the mind”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄτη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Atë, the goddess of mischief, delusion, ruin, and folly.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄτη</i> (átē, “mischief”, “folly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not cut up, unravaged</li><li>not yet opened (<i>of a mine</i>)</li><li>undivided, indivisible</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τμητός</i> (tmētós), from the root of <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undaring, unadventurous</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τόλμα</i> (tólma)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>uncut</li><li>indivisible, not capable of being cut/divided into smaller pieces</li><li>undivided, individual</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; τομ-, <i>o</i>-grade of the root of <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>not stretched, relaxed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τόνος</i> (tónos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unusual, out of place</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>τόπος</i> (tópos, “place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spindle</li><li>arrow</li><li>upper part of a ship's mast</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) spindle-shaped cautery</li></ol><p>Has been derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;terkʷ-</i>, with cognates including Sanskrit <i>तर्कु</i> (tarku), Albanian <i>dredh</i> and Latin <i>torqueō</i>. However, there are some problems with these connections, such as expecting a <i>π</i> (p) instead of <i>κ</i> (k) from <i>&#42;kʷ</i>. According to Beekes, it is rather a Pre-Greek word; this may find support in <i>ἄδρακτος</i> (ádraktos) and <i>ἀτρακίς</i> (atrakís), if the variation <i>κ/κτ</i> is old and not due to simplification.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unchangeable, immutable</li><li>unremovable, inflexible</li><li>(<i>of a murder</i>) irreparable</li><li>indifferent to ill-repute, not caring</li><li>(<i>of food</i>) undigested</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>τρεπτός</i> (treptós, “liable to be moved or changed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unchangeable</li><li>uncourteous</li><li>unploughed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō, “turn”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄτροπος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Atropos</li></ol><p>Substantivized from <i>ᾰ̓́τροπος</i> (ắtropos, “unchangeable”). Although -ος was a common ending for masculine nouns and names, it was the feminine ending for many compound adjectives too.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>father; a mode of address by a younger to an elder</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;átta</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;átta</i>. Cognates include Hittite <i>𒀜𒋫𒀸</i> (attas), Latin <i>atta</i>, Gothic <i>𐌰𐍄𐍄𐌰</i> (atta) and Old Church Slavonic <i>отьць</i> (otĭcĭ).</p><p>Derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷíh₂</i> with ἄ- perhaps from metanalysis of such expressions as πολλά σσα &gt; πολλ'ἄσσα.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>why?</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>τῐ́ς</i> (tĭ́s, “what”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>forthwith</li><li>quickly</li></ol><p>Along with <i>ἄφνω</i> (áphnō, “suddenly”), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;abʰ-</i>, shared with Sanskrit <i>अह्नाय</i> (ahnāya, “instantly; at once”). However, compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;abraz</i> (“strong, extreme, severe”) and <i>&#42;ganhuz</i> (“quick, fast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>ἄφρακτος</i> (áphraktos, “unfenced, unfortified, unguarded”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unploughed</li><li>naked; unclad</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>φᾰ́ρος</i> (phắros).</p><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>φᾶρος</i> (phâros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>revenue; riches; wealth; abundance</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected to Sanskrit <i>अप्न॑स्</i> (ápnas, “property; possession”) and Latin <i>ops</i> (“power; strength”). The agreement with Hittite (ḫappina-, “rich”) is remarkable, but cannot be correct in view of the more likely comparison with the above, which are probably not directly related to the Hittite word. The Greek word looks Proto-Indo-European: according to Beekes, a root <i>&#42;h₂bʰen-</i> is the obvious reconstruction. The recent attempt to connect this word with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ebʰ-</i> (“stream”) does not convince, but it would be similar to <i>affluent</i> (i.e. "flowing") and to <i>πλοῦτος</i> (ploûtos) (i.e. "flowing").</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>letting go, dismissal</li><li>quittance, discharge (<i>from a bond</i>)</li><li>remission, forgiveness (<i>of sins</i>)</li><li>exemption (<i>from service</i>)</li><li>deliverance, liberty</li><li>divorce</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἀφίημι</i> (aphíēmi) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(pathology,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the plural) aphtha, mouth ulcer, thrush</li></ol><p>A term first attested in Hippocrates, with doubt connected to <i>ἅπτω</i> (háptō, “to burn <i>or</i> to fasten, join”). Possibly a technical loan with physicians from Aramaic with emphatic state ending, later attested only in one particular meaning in Jewish Babylonian Aramaic <i>אַפְצָא</i> (ʾap̄ṣā), Classical Syriac <i>ܐܱܦܨܳܐ</i> (ʾap̄ṣā, “oakgalls”), verb <i>אֲפַץ</i> (ʾap̄aṣ, “to dress with gallnut juice”), but more generally Mishnaic Hebrew <i>אָפַץ</i> (ʾāp̄aṣ), <i>אָפַס</i> (ʾāp̄as, “to press, to squeeze”). <i>ἀψίς</i> (apsís), <i>ἁψίς</i> (hapsís, “net, mesh; bow”) is also only doubtingly derived from <i>ἅπτω</i> (háptō, “to fasten, join”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>indestructible</li><li>immortal</li><li>eternal</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>φθαρτός</i> (phthartós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>imperishable</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-dʰgʷʰi-tós</i>, from <i>&#42;dʰgʷʰey-</i> (“to decline, perish”) with alpha privative (PIE <i>&#42;n̥-</i>). Cognate to Sanskrit <i>अक्षित</i> (akṣita).</p><p>By surface analysis, <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>φθίω</i> (phthíō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>voiceless, speechless</li><li>not to be spoken of</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>φθόγγος</i> (phthóngos, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without envy, free from envy</li><li>ungrudging, bounteous</li><li>not grudged, plentiful</li><li>unenvied, provoking no envy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>φθόνος</i> (phthónos, “envy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an arrival</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀφικνέομαι</i> (aphiknéomai, “to arrive”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) curved poop of a ship, with its ornaments</li></ol><p>Bechtel asserted a derivation from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>φλάω</i> (phláō, “to crush”), in the sense of "that which prevents destruction". However, this proposal is considered improbable by Beekes, who claims that the word is rather from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>unawares, of a sudden</li><li>suddenly, unexpectedly</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ἐξαίφνης</i> (exaíphnēs), <i>αἴφνης</i> (aíphnēs, “suddenly”), <i>ἐξᾰπίνης</i> (exăpínēs, “id”), of uncertain origin. One theory derives the word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʷʰ-</i>, shared with Sanskrit <i>अह्नाय</i> (ahnāya, “instantly, at once”). However, Beekes, noting the π/φ variation in the word's relatives, suggests Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fearless</li><li>causing no fear</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>φόβος</i> (phóbos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>unfenced, unfortified, unguarded</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) +&lrm; <i>φράσσω</i> (phrássō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inexpressable, ineffable</li><li>undetectable, unseen, invisible</li><li>(Of a person) with no reason, beside themselves.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dragonwort (<i>Dracunculus vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée connected this word with <i>ἀφία</i> (aphía, “lesser celandine”). According to Beekes, the suffix <i>-ισσ-</i> (-iss-) indicates a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>senseless, crazed, frantic, silly, foolish</li></ol><p><i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “mind”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leafless</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>voiceless, dumb</li><li>(<i>of children</i>) unable to speak</li><li>intestate</li><li>(<b>ἄφωνα</b> <i>(</i><b>γράμματα</b>, <b>στοιχεῖα</b><i>)</i>) the consonants, particularly the mutes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>-φωνος</i> (-phōnos, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild pear (<i>Pyrus amygdaliformis</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a substrate root common to Albanian <i>dardhë</i> (“pear”). But this ignores the presence of Crimean Tatar <i>kertme</i> (“pear”), dialectal Turkish <i>kertme</i> (“pear”), Kipchak كرتما / <i>chertme</i> (“pear”), allegedly from the verb which is in Turkish <i>kertmek</i> (“to notch, to indent”) because originally meaning a sort of pear which was grafted, then having the transparent suffix <i>-me</i> (forms things created as the result of verbs), but also Ossetian <i>кӕрдо</i> (kærdo, “pear”), Chechen <i>кхор</i> (qor, “pear”), Ingush <i>кхор</i> (qor, “pear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be loaded</li><li>to be vexed or grieved</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂edʰǵʰ-</i> (“to squeeze, (op)press”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burden, load, weight</li><li>load of grief, sorrow</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́χθομαι</i> (ắkhthomai, “to be loaded”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂edʰǵʰ-</i> (“to squeeze, (op)press”), the same root of Hittite (ḫatk-, “to shut, close”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cargo</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma). Given the existence of parallel formations in Latin <i>agmen</i> and Sanskrit <i>ájman</i>, it could be derived from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ákʰmə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵmn̥</i>. Regardless, ultimately from <i>&#42;h₂eǵ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything that comes off the surface</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>foam, froth of the sea or of the river</li><li>froth of wine</li><li>exudation</li><li>dew</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) stone in the urine</li><li>chaff</li><li>embers that form the smoke</li><li>metal dust, filing</li><li>fluff on plants and fruits</li><li>finespun fabrics</li><li>flakes, shreds used for lint</li><li>morsel, the least bit</li><li>flax-leaved daphne (<i>Daphne gnidium</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>This word has been connected with Latin <i>agna</i> (“ear of grain”) and English <i>awn</i>. According to Beekes, the word must be connected with <i>ἄχυρον</i> (ákhuron, “chaff”), pointing thus to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be distressed</li><li>to grieve</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i> (“to be upset; afraid”). Cognate with Old Irish <i>ad·ágathar</i> (“to fear”) and Gothic <i>𐍉𐌲𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (ōgjan, “to scare”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without the dance (epithet of Ares)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>χορός</i> (khorós, “dance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distress</li><li>pain; ache</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂égʰos</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₂egʰ-</i> (“to be upset, in distress, afraid”). Compare Sanskrit <i>अ॒घ</i> (aghá, “evil”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;agaz</i> (“fear”) (Gothic <i>𐌰𐌲𐌹𐍃</i> (agis), dialectal English <i>ey</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undefiled, immaculate</li><li>pure, intact</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, not) +&lrm; <i>χρᾰντός</i> (khrăntós, “stained”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>useless, unprofitable</li><li>non-effective, unwarlike</li><li>unkind, cruel</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-, not”) +&lrm; <i>χρηστός</i> (khrēstós, “useful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the very bottom, to the uttermost, utterly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(after Homer,&#32;before prepositions)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>even to, as far as</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of time</i>) until</li><li>(<i>of space</i>) as far as, even to</li><li>(<i>of measure or degree</i>) up to this point</li></ol></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) until, so long as</li><li>(<i>of space</i>) so far as</li></ol><p>Zero grade of <i>μέχρῐ</i> (mékhrĭ), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;méǵʰ(s)ri</i>.</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of comparison/extent.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>without gold</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, alpha privativum) +&lrm; <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) chaff, bran, husks left after threshing or grinding</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) slag from gold smelting</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek and related to <i>ἄχωρ</i> (ákhōr, “scurf”) (particularly, identical to the form <i>ἄχορα</i> (ákhora)) and to <i>ἄχνη</i> (ákhnē, “foam, froth”). Note the Pre-Greek suffix <i>&#42;-ur-</i>, similar to that in the synonym <i>πίτῡρον</i> (pítūron). The traditional connection to <i>ἀκοστή</i> (akostḗ, “barley”), Latin <i>acus</i> (“bran, awn”), etc., from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i>, must be rejected.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scurf, dandruff</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin due to the similarity with <i>ἄχυρον</i> (ákhuron, “chaff”) and the vocalic interchange <i>υ/ω</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>back, back again</li><li>again, in return</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ᾰ̓πό</i> (ăpó). Cognate with Latin <i>abs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>blameless</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, “a-, un-”) +&lrm; <i>ψόγος</i> (psógos, “blame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>going back, returning</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄψ</i> (áps, “back, back again”) plus an uncertain verbal root, possibly Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i> (“to move”) (compare <i>ὄρνῡμι</i> (órnūmi)) or <i>&#42;h₁ers-</i> (“to flow”) (compare Latin <i>errō</i> (“to wander, get lost”) and Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌶𐌾𐌰𐌽</i> (airzjan, “to mislead”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow</li><li>to sleep</li><li>alternative form of <i>ἀάω</i> (aáō, “to hurt”)</li><li>to satiate (with genitive and accusative)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to take one's fill</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ἄημι</i> (áēmi).</p><p>Related to <i>ἰαύω</i> (iaúō).</p><p>Contraction of <i>ἀάω</i> (aáō).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂-</i> (“to satiate, satisfy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untimely, unseasonable</li><li>unripe</li><li>without youthful freshness, ugly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>ὥρα</i> (hṓra, “season”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>finest wool</li><li>the choicest, the flower of its kind</li><li>flock, down</li><li>that which gives honour and glory to a thing</li></ol><p>Considered to be a verbal noun to <i>ἄημι</i> (áēmi, “to blow”), so "blowing". However, there is no semantical support for this connection; the etymology was merely suggested by the formal appearance. Rather, the word is a technical term, as remarked by DELG. It remains without etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>ᾱ̓ῑ̈κή</i> (āī̈kḗ, “rapid motion”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓ῑ̈́σσω</i> (āī̈́ssō).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>immaterial</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>ῡ̔́λη</i> (hū́lē, “wood; matter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a person</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sleepless; awake</li><li>having insomnia; unable to sleep</li></ol><li>(<i>of nights</i>) sleepless; restless; without sleep</li><li>(figurative,&#32;poetic,&#32;of objects) ever-vigilant; never-resting</li><li>(<i>metaphysics</i>) that is real</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́πνος</i> (hŭ́pnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female slave working for a woman, favourite slave</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from <i>ἁβρός</i> (habrós, “delicate, graceful”). However, a Semitic origin has been proposed, comparing this word with Hebrew <i>חֲבֵרָה</i> (ḥăḇērāh, “female friend, female companion”) and Classical Syriac <i>ܚܒܪܐ</i> (ḥaḇrā, “male friend, male companion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>holy place, sanctuary</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>devoted to the gods</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) sacred, holy</li><li>(<i>of people</i>) holy, pious, pure</li><li>accursed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical) a Christian</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) a saint</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hágiyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁yáǵ-yo-s</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁yaǵ-</i> (“to sacrifice, worship”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to one's fill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂-</i> (“satiate, satisfy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>satiety; disgust; surfeit</li><li>decree</li></ol><p>From the same root of <i>ᾱ̓́δησᾰ</i> (ā́dēsă, “to be satiated”) and <i>ἅδην</i> (hádēn, “to one's fill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dread; to stand in awe of</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;haďďomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁yáǵyeti</i>. Compare <i>ᾰ̔́γῐος</i> (hắgĭos)); cognate with Sanskrit <i>यज॑ति</i> (yájati, “to revere”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἅλαδε</i> (hálade)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the sea</li></ol><p><i>ἅλα</i> (hála) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ᾰ̔́λς</i> (hắls, “salt”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of salt</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́λς</i> (hắls, “salt”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in vain</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the sea, marine</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) fruitless, ineffectual</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios, “sun”)</li></ol><p><i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “sea”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḗh₂l</i>.</p><p><i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “salt”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḗh₂l</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in crowds</li><li>in plenty</li><li>enough</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leap, jump</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;həľľomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sl̥yétor</i>, from the root <i>&#42;sel-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>saliō</i> (“to jump”) and Sanskrit <i>सरति</i> (sárati, “to run”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spring, leap, jump</li><li>jumping, as an athletic contest</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) pulsation, palpitation (of the embryo or of the heart)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́λλομαι</i> (hắllomai, “to jump, to leap”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seawater, brine</li><li>spray that has dried on the skin</li><li>salt incrustation on soil</li><li>saltwater, brine used for pickling</li><li>saltness, as a bad quality in soil</li></ol><p>From <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “salt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salt</li><li>brine</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) wit</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) the sea</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hā́ls</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḗh₂l</i> (“salt”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सलिल</i> (salila), Old Armenian <i>աղ</i> (ał), Latin <i>sāl</i>, and Old English <i>sealt</i> (English <i>salt</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chain</li><li>link in chain mail</li></ol><p>Originally "winding", derived from <i>&#42;ϝάλυτις</i> (&#42;wálutis), itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind”). Further related to <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to turn, roll”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>threshing floor</li><li>grain on the floor</li><li>disk (<i>of the sun, the moon, or a shield</i>)</li><li>halo</li><li>serpent's coil</li><li>bird's nest</li><li>ciliary body of the eye</li><li>circular piazza at Delphi</li></ol><p>Probably from Pre-Greek. Compare, however, Albanian <i>lëmë</i> and its proto-form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>capture, conquest</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἁλίσκομαι</i> (halískomai) +&lrm; <i>-ωσις</i> (-ōsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἅμα</i> (háma)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἅμα</i> (háma)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>marking the simultaneous occurrence of two events: simultaneously, at once, at the same time</li><li>marking general concurrence (spatial, temporal, etc: together)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>at the same time with</li><li>together with</li></ol><p>From the zero-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sem-</i> (“one”).</p><p>The dative is from the PIE comitative-instrumental.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wagon</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a compound of <i>ἅμα</i> (háma, “at once”) + the <i>ἄξ-</i> (áx-) in <i>ἄξων</i> (áxōn, “axle”) + <i>-ια</i> (-ia, “nominal suffix”), literally "one-axler". Other theories include a relation to <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead, guide”), as well as Tocharian B <i>amäkṣpänte</i> (“wagonmaster”).</p><p>Beekes rejects these theories (noting that a "one-axler" formation makes less sense than a "two-axler" formation) and considers the word Pre-Greek, based on a variant <i>ἀβακλή</i> (abaklḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>competition, contest, trial</li><li>face-off, confrontation, dispute</li><li>struggle, fight, conflict</li><li>sporting activity, game, race</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the ending in <i>-ιλλ-</i> points to a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything tied or made to tie</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a knot</li><li>a noose; a halter</li><li>the link of a chain</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the clinches in wrestling</li><li>a measure of length</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́πτω</i> (hắptō, “to fasten”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>once, one time, only once</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm̥-</i>, zero-grade of <i>&#42;sem-</i> (“one”). The second part is obscure, but the word may be some syncope of &#42;ἁ-πακυς, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm̥-pn̥kʷu-</i> (“(lit.) all-one”). See Latin <i>cūnctus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>quite all, the whole (<i>in the plural</i>) all together</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with an adjective</i>) all</li><li>(<i>with an abstract substantive</i>) all possible, absolute</li></ol><li>(after Homer,&#32;in the singular) everyone, (<i>neuter</i>) everything</li></ol><p>From <i>ἁ-</i> (ha-, “together”) +&lrm; <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs, “all”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kindle; to set on fire; to fasten fire to</li><li>to fasten to; to bind fast</li><li>to join to</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to fasten oneself to; to cling to; to hang on by; to lay hold of; to grasp; to touch</li><li>to reach the mark</li><li>to engage in; to take part in</li><li>to set upon; to attack; to assail</li><li>to touch; to affect</li><li>to grasp with the senses; to apprehend; to perceive</li><li>to come up to; to reach; to gain</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;áptō</i>, of unclear origin. There are a number of competing theories as to the origin of this word.</p><p>The present tense system includes a <i>-τ-</i> (-t-) progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chariot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂érmn̥</i>, from <i>&#42;h₂er-</i> (“to fit together, join”), the same source as <i>ἁρμός</i> (harmós, “join”) and <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō, “join, fit together”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἅρμα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Harma (an ancient town in Boeotia, Greece)</li></ol><p>According to Homer, from <i>ἅρμα</i> (hárma, “chariot”), because here the chariot of Adrastus broke down.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἅρματα</i> (hármata)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἅρματε</i> (hármate)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἅρματι</i> (hármati)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booty; prey</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔ρπᾰ́ζω</i> (hărpắzō, “to plunder”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rapacious; ravening</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) robber; plunderer; extortioner</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) rapine</li></ol><p>From the same uncertain origin as <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bird of prey, hawk, falcon, tiercel or goshawk</li><li>a sea-bird (probably shearwater)</li><li>a kite</li><li>a sickle</li><li>a sickle-shaped sword, scimitar</li><li>an elephant goad</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but usually connected with <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō, “to snatch, plunder”). Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srp-</i> (“to reap, harvest; sickle”), with cognates including Latin <i>sarpō</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>срьпъ</i> (srĭpŭ, “sickle”), and Latvian <i>sirpis</i>. However, there are some difficulties with these connections; for one, <i>&#42;sr̥p-</i> is expected to yield Ancient Greek <i>&#42;ῥαπ-</i> (&#42;rhap-), not <i>ἅρπ-</i> (hárp-). There is possibly a contamination with a Semitic borrowing, Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;x̣arb-</i>. Possibly from a Mediterranean substrate term related to the source of Latin <i>sarpa</i> (“heron”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a harpy</li></ol><p>Probably borrowed from Pre-Greek, but commonly related to <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō, “to snatch; seize”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἅσσα</i> (hássa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>as if, as, in as much as, since, seeing that, because</li></ol><p>Please fill out the etymology</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) juncture, joint</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) limbs</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔́πτω</i> (hắptō, “to fasten”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ah! (expressing pity, envy, contempt, etc.)</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prize of a contest</li><li>contest</li><li>place of combat, arena</li></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;ἄϝεθλον</i> (&#42;áwethlon), traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂weh₁-</i> (“to blow (of wind)”) + <i>&#42;-dʰlom</i> (forms nouns denoting tool or instrument), as if "gasp, pant, wheeze", if not from a similar root meaning "to strive". Other theories compare the word to Sanskrit <i>वायति</i> (vāyati, “to be tired”), the latter from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁weh₂-</i> (“to leave, abandon”).</p><p>However, Beekes dismisses the connection with the Sanskrit and does not mention the <i>&#42;h₂weh₁-</i> derivation, instead deriving the Greek from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wedʰ-</i> (“to contest, compete”), albeit with no known cognates outside of Greek.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>material for a contest; prize</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) conflict, struggle</li></ol><p>See <i>ἆθλον</i> (âthlon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>material for a contest; prize</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) conflict, struggle</li></ol><p>See <i>ἆθλον</i> (âthlon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἆρα</i> (âra)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>interrogative particle, introducing a yes-no question, and often indicating impatience or anxiety</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in a main clause</i>)</li><li>(<i>in a subordinate clause</i>)</li></ol><li>(in poets, sometimes equivalent to <i>ἄρα</i> (ára))</li></ol><p>Compare Persian آیا.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>short-drawn breath, panting</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) asthma</li></ol><p>Traditionally taken from an earlier <i>&#42;ἄνσθμα</i> (&#42;ánsthma), potentially a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂enh₁-</i> (“to breathe”) with an extension of unclear origin, followed by the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-mn̥</i>). Compare <i>ἄνεμος</i> (ánemos, “wind”). However, Beekes is skeptical, as the Greek does not show the expected initial of <i>&#42;ἀνε-</i> (&#42;ane-), and prefers to take the word as an ad-hoc onomatopoeic formation + a suffix <i>-θμα</i> (-thma) also found in <i>ἴθμα</i> (íthma) (though Chantraine suspects that the <i>-σ-</i> (-s-) replaces the <i>&#42;-ε-</i> (&#42;-e-) in <i>&#42;ἀνε-</i> (&#42;ane-) for euphonic reasons).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ᾍδης</i> (Hāídēs)</li><li>Ionic form of <i>ᾍδης</i> (Hāídēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aaron, the brother of Moses.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אַהֲרֹן</i> (ʾahărōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abacaenum, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abaddon, the destroying spirit mentioned in Revelation 9:11.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֲבַדּוֹן</i> (ʔăḇaddōn, “destruction; ruin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li><li>a naiad nymph, mother of Aesepus and Pedasus by Bucolion.</li></ol><p>The name is said to mean "unmuddy."</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An inhabitant of Abdera: Abderite, Abderian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄβδηρα</i> (Ábdēra, “Abdera”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Αὐεντῖνον</i> (Auentînon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abijah, the name of a number of Israelites.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֲבִיָּה</i> (ʔăḇiyyɔ̂).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abiathar, a Hebrew high priest during the reign of King David.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֶבְיָתָר</i> (ʾeḇyāṯār).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Abilene (a region of Syria)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Abrahamism) Abraham</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abrocomas, a satrap of Persian king Artaxerxes II</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abrupolis, the name of a king of the Sapaei</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Abydos; an Abydene</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄβῡδος</i> (Ábūdos) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Agatharchus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρχός</i> (ărkhós, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agathyrna, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A Scythian tribe of Sarmatia Europea</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Scythian with no clear etymology. Suggested be from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;háxā</i> (“friend”), however the expected outcome in Proto-Scythian would be <i>&#42;haxā</i>, + <i>&#42;θrauš- ~ &#42;θruš-</i> (“prospering”), modified by <i>θύρσος</i> (thúrsos, “thyrsus, the composite vegetal wand of Bacchus”) “via an association of Scythians peoples with Bacchic rites”, however no Iranian cognate meaning “prospering” is given.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Agathon</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, Strabon suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Agelaus, name of various figures in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō) +&lrm; <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Agatha</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós, “good”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Agathocles</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰθός</i> (ăgăthós, “good”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Agamemnon</li><li>(<i>mythology</i>) Agamemnon, the king of Mycenae.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγα-</i> (aga-, “very”) +&lrm; <i>μέμνων</i> (mémnōn, “steadfast”). See <i>ἄγαν</i> (ágan), <i>μένω</i> (ménō) and <i>Μενέλαος</i> (Menélaos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Agamemnon: that is, Orestes</li></ol><p><i>Ᾰ̓γᾰμέμνων</i> (Ăgămémnōn) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs, patronymic suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Agapenor, one of the suitors of Helen</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγαπήνωρ</i> (agapḗnōr, “valiant, courageous”), <i>ἀγάπη</i> (agápē, “love”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Agasias</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a female given name, Agave, borne by several figures in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γαυή</i> (ăgauḗ), the Epic feminine of <i>ᾰ̓γαυός</i> (ăgauós, “illustrious; noble”), with a stress shift.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Agesilaus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>λαός</i> (laós, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ancaeus</li></ol><p>Possibly from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;h₂enk-</i>, whence (among others) <i>ἀγκύλος</i> (ankúlos, “curved, bent”), <i>ἀγκάλη</i> (ankálē, “armful”), with the suffix <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aglaea, Aglaia</li></ol><p>Proper use of <i>ᾱ̓γλαΐᾱ</i> (āglaḯā, “splendor”), from <i>ᾱ̓γλαός</i> (āglaós, “splendid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aglaophon</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̓γλαός</i> (āglaós, “splendid”) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agrinion; Agrinio, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agrippa, the name of numerous important figures of the classical era such as Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa, Agrippa I, Agrippa II, and others.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Agrippa</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agrippina</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Agrippīna</i>, itself a diminutive of <i>Agrippa</i> (<i>Ἀγρίππᾱς</i> (Agríppās)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agrianes, an ancient Paeonian tribe settled along the Upper Strymon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anchialus; Pomorie, Bulgaria</li><li>Anchialus, Cilicia, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀγχίαλος</i> (ankhíalos, “coastal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anchises</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agyrrhius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Adam (<i>husband of Eve</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אָדָם</i> (ʾāḏām).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Adeimantus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, negative) +&lrm; <i>δειμαίνω</i> (deimaínō, “to be frightened”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Adiabene (an ancient kingdom in Assyria, with its capital at Arbela)</li></ol><p>From Classical Syriac <i>ܚܕܝܐܒ</i> (Ḥaḏy’aḇ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adopissus, Lycaonia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Adusius, from Old Persian, a Persian satrap of Caria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Ādušyah</i> (literally “the pleasant one”), cognate with Avestan <i>𐬰𐬎𐬱</i> (zuš, “pleasant, elegant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Adrastea; Adrasteia</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-) +&lrm; <i>δρᾱσ-</i> (drās-, “escape”), literally “the inescapable one”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adriatic Sea</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓δρῐ́ᾱ</i> (Ădrĭ́ā, “Adria”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Ἀδρίᾱς</i> (Adríās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a hamadryad</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adyrmachidae, a Libyan people settled in Marmarica</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aerope</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) a subregion of Arcadia.</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) The region of East Africa as known by the Ancient Greeks and Romans.</li></ol><p>Possibly from Ζάν (Zán, “Zeus”) &mdash; <i>Doric</i> + -ία (-ía).</p><p>Two possibilities: 1. Related to Somali <i>san</i> (“nose”), referring to the Horn of Africa; (Cushitic languages like Somali have been spoken there for many thousands of years). 2. Related to Arabic عَجَمِيّ (ʕajamiyy, “foreign, barbarian”). The Greeks likely remoulded whichever word to a familiar form (Etymology 1). Probably not related to <i>Ζήγγισα</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Azenia</i>, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ahenobarbus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ahēnobarbus</i> (literally “bronze-beard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athamas, mythological king of the Minyans, with his seat in Orchomenos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athena</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to Athens</li></ol><p><i>Ἀθήνᾱς</i> (Athḗnās), accusative of <i>Ἀθῆναι</i> (Athênai, “Athens”), + <i>-δε</i> (-de, “-wards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An Athenaeum: a temple of Athena</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓θηνᾶ</i> (Ăthēnâ) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Ἀθήναιος Ἀθηναίου (<i>fl.</i> 138–128 <small>B.C.E.</small>), Athenian composer and musician</li><li>Ἀθήναιος Μηχανικός (<i>fl.</i> mid-to-late 1st century <small>B.C.E.</small>), author of a book on siegecraft, <i>Περὶ μηχανημάτων</i> (Perì mēkhanēmátōn, “On Machines”)</li><li>Ἀθήναιος Ἀτταλεύς (<i>fl.</i> <small>C.E.</small> 1st century), physician and founder of the Pneumatic school of medicine</li><li>Ἀθήναιος Nαυκρατίτης (<i>fl.</i> <small>C.E.</small> late 2nd–early 3rd centuries), rhetorician and grammarian</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in&#32;epigraphy&#32;of the brazen age</i>) Koine form of <i>Ἀθήναιος</i> (Athḗnaios)</li></ol><p>Epigraphically regular omission of monophthongal omicron in case endings.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athens (the capital city of Greece)</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>Ἀθήνη</i> (Athḗnē, “Athena”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athena</li><li>An older name for Athens</li></ol><p>Unknown; likely from a lost Pre-Greek language given the variations seen in this word and <i>Ἀττικός</i> (Attikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from Athens</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀθῆναι</i> (Athênai) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at Athens, in Athens</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Athenippus</li></ol><p><i>Ᾰ̓θῆναι</i> (Ăthênai, “Athens”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, masculine second-declension suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Athanasius</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀθάνατος</i> (athánatos, “immortal”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to French Athénaïs</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Ἀθηναῒς Ἐρυθραία (<i>fl.</i> mid-to-late 4th century <small>B.C.E.</small>), a prophetess who asserted Alexander the Great’s descent from Zeus</li><li>Ἀθηναῒς Φιλόστοργος Α’, a Greek noblewoman who became Queen of Cappadocia during the reign of her husband, King Ariobarzanes I Philoromaios (<i>r.</i> 95–63&#47;62 <small>B.C.E.</small>)</li><li>Ἀθηναῒς Φιλόστοργος Β’, a princess of Pontus who became Queen of Cappadocia during the reign of her husband, King Ariobarzanes II Philopator (<i>r.</i> 63&#47;62–51 <small>B.C.E.</small>)</li><li>Μαρκία Κλαυδία Ἀλκία Ἀθηναῒς Γαβιδία Λατιαρία (<small>C.E.</small> 143–161), a Greek–Italian Roman noblewoman, daughter of Herodes Atticus</li><li>Ἀθηναῒς Ἱππάρχου (<small>C.E.</small> late 2nd–early 3rd centuries), a Greek–Italian Roman noblewoman, daughter of L. Vibullius Hipparchus and namesake of his mother, M. Claudia Athenaïs</li></ol></ol><p><i>Ἀθήνη</i> (Athḗnē, “Athena”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athena</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to Athens; Athenian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an Athenian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀθῆναι</i> (Athênai, “Athens”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>Ἀθηνᾶ</i> (Athēnâ)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>Ἀθηναίᾱ</i> (Athēnaíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Athenagoras</li></ol><p><i>Ᾰ̓θῆναι</i> (Ăthênai, “Athens”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́, “marketplace”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās, masculine noun-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to Athens; Athenian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) an Athenian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀθῆναι</i> (Athênai, “Athens”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Athenophanes</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓θῆναι</i> (Ăthênai, “Athens”) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs, “seeming, appearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Athenodorus</li></ol><p><i>Ᾰ̓θῆναι</i> (Ăthênai, “Athens”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, masculine second-declension suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Athena</li></ol><p>See <i>Ᾰ̓θήνη</i> (Ăthḗnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athens (the capital city of Greece)</li></ol><p>Plural of <i>Ἀθήνη</i> (Athḗnē, “Athena”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Academus</li></ol><p>A compound of <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “country; people”), though the first element is unclear. <i>DGE</i> suggests <i>ἑκάς</i> (hekás, “far off”), and EtymOnline suggests <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “silence”). The inscriptional spelling <i>Ϝͱεκάδᾱμος</i> (Whekádāmos) suggests a form beginning with Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hwe-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acamas, one of several figures in Greek mythology</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Academy</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀκάδημος</i> (Akádēmos, name of a mythological figure) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Academy, an Athenian gymnasium where Plato taught.</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀκάδημος</i> (Akádēmos) (also <i>Ἑκάδημος</i> (Hekádēmos)), an Athenian hero and original estate-holder of the site + <i>-ία</i> (-ía). His name has in turn been interpreted as meaning "of a silent district;" see <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “silence”) + <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “district”), but Beekes connects the first compound element to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weḱ-</i> (“to choose, want”) (see <i>ἑκών</i> (hekṓn, “willing”)) and does not find any identification of the second compound element convincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acarnan</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Acarnania; an Acarnanian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acarnania (an ancient province in modern Greece, reckoned the most westerly province of Greece)</li></ol><p><i>Ἀκαρνᾱ́ν</i> (Akarnā́n) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā))</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acesines, Chenab (a river in Indian subcontinent, which flows through Pakistan and India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>असिक्नी</i> (asiknī).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Acoetes:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A pseudonym which Dionysus claimed for himself before King Pentheus.</li><li>A shieldbearer to Evander of Pallene.</li></ol></ol><p>Probably from <i>ᾰ̓κοίτης</i> (ăkoítēs, “bedfellow”, “husband”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Acusilaus</li></ol><p><i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō) +&lrm; <i>λαός</i> (laós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acragas, Agrigentum, Agrigento (a city in Sicily, Italy)</li></ol><p>Uncertain, possibly Pre-Greek. Compare the morphology of Ancient Greek <i>Γίγᾱς</i> (Gígās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acrisius, a mythological king of Argos</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not, without”) +&lrm; <i>κρίσις</i> (krísis, “judgement, dispute”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, a noun stem used to designate proper nouns). Usually taken to mean "without judgement".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acrocorinth</li></ol><p><i>ἄκρος</i> (ákros, “tip, summit, citadel”) +&lrm; <i>Κόρινθος</i> (Kórinthos, “Corinth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Actaeon</li></ol><p>Possibly derived from ἀκτίς or ἀκοντίζω, ultimately derived from Proto-Indo-European *h₂eḱ-.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aquileia, Venetia, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Aquilēia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aquila</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acilius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Acilius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aquilius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Aquillius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alabanda, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a title held by Hera and Paris, among others</li><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Alexander, most famously held by Alexander the Great</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λέξω</i> (ăléxō, “to repel”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νδρ-</i> (ăndr-, stem of <i>ᾰ̓νήρ</i> (ănḗr, “man”)) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), literally “man who repels [enemies]”. Cognate with Hittite <i>𒀀𒆷𒀝𒊭𒀭𒁺𒍑</i> (/⁠Alakšānduš⁠/, “Alaksandu”), attested in the 13th century <small>BCE</small>, apparently borrowed from an earlier Hellenic language, though the precise origin is unclear. Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀩𐀏𐀭𐀅𐀨</i> (a-re-ka-sa-da-ra), corresponding to feminine Ancient Greek <i>Ᾰ̓λεξᾰ́νδρᾱ</i> (Ălexắndrā), is the earliest attested form in Hellenic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Alexarchus</li><li>the historian Alexarchus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λέξω</i> (ăléxō, “to defend”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ρχω</i> (ắrkhō, “to command”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name meaning “helper; defender”, equivalent to English Alexius</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓́λεξῐς</i> (Ắlexĭs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aletrium (a town in Italy)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Aletrium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Allia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Allia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aliphera, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alazani (a river in Georgia and Azerbaijan)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Georgian <i>ალაზანი</i> (alazani)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) Alalia; Aleria, Corsica</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alans (Scytho-Sarmatian tribe dwelling near the river Tanais north of the Black Sea)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Allān</i>. Doublet of <i>Ἀριανοί</i> (Arianoí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alarodii, Alarodians, an ancient tribe dwelling on the shores of the Black Sea mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol><p>Has been connected with Akkadian <i>𒆳𒌨𒅈𒂅</i> (Urarṭu). See it for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Caucasian Albania (an ancient region and former kingdom in West Asia, south of the Caucasus mountains, east of Armenia and west of the Caspian Sea)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀλβᾰνός</i> (Albănós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā). Related to Old Armenian <i>Աղուանք</i> (Ałuankʻ, “Aghvank, Caucasian Albania”). Unrelated to <i>Albania</i> in the Balkans, which comes from <i>Ἄρβανον</i> (Árbanon, “Arbanon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Alba</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Albānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alesiaeum, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alexandra</li></ol><p>Feminine equivalent of <i>Ᾰ̓λέξᾰνδρος</i> (Ăléxăndros, “Alexander”). Compare also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀩𐀏𐀭𐀅𐀨</i> (a-re-ka-sa-da-ra), the earliest attested form in Hellenic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alexandria (any of various ancient cities)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓λέξᾰνδρος</i> (Ăléxăndros, “Alexander”) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Alexandretta; Iskenderun, Turkey</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Alexandretta</i>, ultimately from Ancient Greek <i>Ᾰ̓λέξᾰνδρος</i> (Ăléxăndros, “Alexander”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An Alexandrian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓λέξᾰνδρος</i> (Ăléxăndros) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Alexandria, Alexandrian</li></ol><p><i>Ἀλέξανδρος</i> (Aléxandros) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Alexiad (a medieval historical and biographical text written circa 1148, by the Byzantine princess Anna Komnene)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓λέξῐος</i> (Ăléxĭos) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alecto, one of the Erinyes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄληκτος</i> (álēktos, “unceasing”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ), thus literally meaning "the unceasing one".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Arabian mythology</i>) Allat</li></ol><p>Corresponding to Arabic &#42;اَلْإِلَات (&#42;alʔilāt), possibly an earlier or alternative variant of Arabic اَللَّات (al-lāt, “Allat”), itself hypothesized to be a contraction of a feminine form of الإِلٰه (al-ʔilāh, “the god”) (now obsoleted by the suffixed form الإِلٰهَة (al-ʔilāha, “the goddess”)). Compare with the name of the Islamic god, اَلله (allāh, “Allah”), itself theorized to be a contraction of الإِلٰه (al-ʔilāh). More at إِلٰه (ʔilāh).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Alcathous, a Trojan soldier slain by Idomeneus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “prowess, force”) +&lrm; <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “quick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Alcinous</li><li>In particular, Alcinous (king of Phaeacia in the <i>Odyssey</i> and of Drepane in the <i>Argonautica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Alciphron</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λκή</i> (ălkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Alcamenes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos, “might”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Alcaic</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀλκαῖος</i> (Alkaîos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Alcides (synonym of Hercules)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀλκαῖος</i> (Alkaîos) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Alcibiades</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ) +&lrm; <i>βία</i> (bía) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Alcman</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in particular</i>) Alcman, a lyric poet</li></ol><li>Doric form of <i>Ἀλκμαίων</i> (Alkmaíōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Alcmene</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>μήνη</i> (mḗnē, “moon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Alcmaeon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκμαῖος</i> (alkmaîos, “young”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, Strabon suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Alcmaeon; an Alcmaeonid</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓λκμαίων</i> (Ălkmaíōn, “Alcmaeon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a female given name, Alcyone, the name of several figures in Greek mythology.</li></ol><p>Possibly connected to <i>ἀλκυών</i> (alkuṓn, “kingfisher”), though that may be a folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Lydian, equivalent to English Alyattes</li></ol><p>From Lydian <i>𐤥𐤠𐤩𐤥𐤤𐤯𐤤𐤮</i> (walwetes).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἁλφαῖος</i> (Halphaîos, “Alphaeus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Alpheus or Alfeios, the name of a mythological river god and associated river</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alphesiboea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aluntium, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alope, Phthiotis, Greece</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) daughter of Cercyon and mother of Hippothoon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amadoci, a Sarmatian tribe settled near the modern region of Kharkiv</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Amadocus, from Thracian, the name of two kings of the Odrysae</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Amatakos</i>, composed of <i>&#42;ama</i> + <i>&#42;takos</i> (“famous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amarynthus, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amaseia, Pontus; Amasya, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>An Egyptian given name notably applied to several pharaohs of the 18th dynasty of Ancient Egypt, 16th to 13th century BC.</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">mn&#58;n</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./d035306b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A40" alt="glyph" height="90"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./be92bc63.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="m" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">p&#42;t&#58;O45</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (jmn-m-jpt), in some cases eventually also conflated with a borrowing from <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">mn&#58;n</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Htp&#58;t&#42;p</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (jmn-ḥtp).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hamilcar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Punic <i>𐤇𐤌𐤋𐤒𐤓𐤕</i> (ḥmlqrt).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a battle with the Amazons</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓μᾰζών</i> (Ămăzṓn, “Amazon”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) Amazons, a mythical nation of female warriors from western Scythia.</li></ol><p>The Greeks claimed that the word derives from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) + <i>μαζός</i> (mazós, “breast”), in reference to the belief that Amazons cut off their right breast so that it might not hinder their ability to fire a bow or throw a spear. However, this is almost certainly a folk etymology; in art, Amazons are always shown with two intact breasts. The word is likely of foreign origin, perhaps initially an Ionic borrowing of the Old Persian word <i>&#42;hamazan-</i> (“warrior”) which can be reanalyzed as “hama-zan” meaning “together-beating/striking/smiting/slaying” in reference to “military comradeship”, since the Amazon women were known warriors, and would also explain the lack of initial aspirated <i>&#42;h-</i> which might otherwise be expected, as Ionic lost aspiration at an early stage. It could also be native Greek, such as from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥-mn̥gʷ-yō</i> (“manless one”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;mongʷyos</i> (“man”) (compare Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;mǫžь</i>), an explanation deemed unlikely by Hjalmar Frisk. Numerous other etymologies have been proposed, generally tracing the word back to Iranian, Greek or (West/East) Caucasian languages.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amathus, an ancient city in Cyprus</li><li>Amathus, an ancient city in Palestine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄμαθος</i> (ámathos, “sand”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”). See <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) <i>Amalec</i>, <i>Amalek</i>:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Amalek, the son of Eliphaz and grandson of Esau in the Hebrew Bible</li><li>the tribe or nation which is said to be descended from Amalek; Amalekites</li></ol></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>עֲמָלֵק</i> (ʾĂmālēq).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amamassus, Cyprus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amantia</li></ol><p>Alternative form of <i>Ἀβᾰντῐ́ᾱ</i> (Abăntĭ́ā), from <i>Ἄβᾰντες</i> (Ábăntes) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), eponymously named for <i>Ἄβᾱς</i> (Ábās, “Abas, King of Argos”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amaryllis</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ἀμαρύσσω</i> (amarússō, “sparkle, twinkle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ambracia</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄμβραξ</i> (Ámbrax) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Ambracia; an Ambracian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀμβρᾰκῐ́ᾱ</i> (Ambrăkĭ́ā, “Ambracia”) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female inhabitant of Ambracia; an Ambracian woman</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀμβρᾰκῐ́ᾱ</i> (Ambrăkĭ́ā, “Ambracia”) +&lrm; <i>-ῶτις</i> (-ôtis, feminine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ameinias, a common name.</li></ol><p>Possibly related to <i>ἀμείνων</i> (ameínōn, “better”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Ameinocles or Aminocles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμείνων</i> (ameínōn, “better”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amminadab, an Israelite mentioned in the genealogy of Jesus.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>עַמִּינָדָב</i> (ọminadab).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amisus, now Samsun</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Amminapes, from Old Persian, a satrap of the Parthians and Hyrcanians</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amnisus, Crete, Greece</li></ol><p>Unknown. Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀖𐀛𐀰</i> (a-mi-ni-so).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Lamb of God</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμνός</i> (amnós, “lamb”) and <i>Θεός</i> (Theós, “God”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amompharetus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́μομφος</i> (ắmomphos, “baseless; irreproachable”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρετή</i> (ăretḗ, “valor”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amorgos, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμοργίς</i> (amorgís, “mallow stalks, kind of garment”) from ἀμέργω (amérgō, “to pluck, to pull”). The island was famed for its textitles</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amulius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Amūlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἀμβρᾰκῐ́ᾱ</i> (Ambrăkĭ́ā, “Ambracia”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ambracian (pertaining to Ambracia, an ancient town in southern Epirus, now Arta)</li></ol><p><i>Ἀμπρακίᾱ</i> (Amprakíā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἀμβρᾰκῐώτης</i> (Ambrăkĭṓtēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἀμβρακιῶτις</i> (Ambrakiôtis, “Ambracian woman”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amydon, Paeonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amyzon, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the inhabitants of Amphilochia; an Amphilochian</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos, “ambush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Amphinomus</li></ol><p><i>ἀμφι-</i> (amphi-, “all around”) +&lrm; <i>νομός</i> (nomós, “pasture”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amphipolis (a city in Macedonia, Greece, situated on the left bank of the river Strymon)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ́</i> (ămphĭ́, “around”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amphion</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ́</i> (ămphĭ́, “on both side”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amphanae, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Amphithoe, a Nereid</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “quick, swift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Amphicrates</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ἀμφί</i> (amphí, “around”) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amphilochia (a district of Acarnania, Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀμφῐ́λοχος</i> (Amphĭ́lokhos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Amphipolis; an Amphipolitan</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀμφῐ́πολῐς</i> (Amphĭ́polĭs, “Amphipolis”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Amphissa or Amfissa</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄμφισσᾰ</i> (Ámphissă, “Amphissa, Amfissa”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Amphitrite</li></ol><p><i>Also</i> , name of her son.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Amphitryon</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓μφῐ́</i> (ămphĭ́, “on both sides”) +&lrm; <i>τρῡ́ων</i> (trū́ōn, “harassing”), from <i>τρῡ́ω</i> (trū́ō, “to wear out, distress”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Amorges, from Old Persian, son of Pissuthnes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Humargah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Amorium, Amorion (a city in Phrygia, Asia Minor), legendary birthplace of Aesop, growing into significance in the Hellenistic and Byzantine eras until destroyed by the Arab Sack of Amorium in 838; home of the famed 42 Martyrs of Amorium executed for refusing to submit to Islam in 845; now in modern-day Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμόρα</i> (amóra) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), or related to <i>ᾰ̓μόργη</i> (ămórgē, “pressed olives; dye”), or possibly of Semitic stem relation to Biblical Hebrew <i>עֲמֹרָה</i> (ʿĂmōrā) from a root meaning "deep with water" (a city name transcribed in the LXX as <i>Γόμορρα</i> (Gómorrha)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amyclae; Amykles</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Amynander</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀμύνω</i> (amúnō, “I defend”) and <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amyntas</li></ol><p>Doric form of <i>ἀμύντης</i> (amúntēs, “defender”), from <i>ἀμύνω</i> (amúnō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anazarbus, Cilicia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anaxarchus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́νᾰξ</i> (ắnăx, “king”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρχός</i> (ărkhós, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anaphe; Anafi, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anaphlystus; Anavyssos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anio; Aniene</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of <i>Anaea</i></li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄναιᾰ</i> (Ánaiă, “Anaea”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anacreon</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>κρέων</i> (kréōn, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Anactoria</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) name of daughter of Πηνέλεως (Pēnéleōs, “Peneleos”)</li><li>unknown woman quoted by poetess Sappho</li></ol><li>(<i>placename</i>) Anactoria, original name of the region later known as Miletus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνακτορῐ́ᾱ</i> (anaktorĭ́ā, “lordship”), from <i>ἀνακτόρῐος</i> (anaktórĭos, “belonging to a lord”), from <i>ἀνάκτωρ</i> (anáktōr, “lord”), from <i>ἄναξ</i> (ánax, “lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Anactorium; an Anactorian</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνακτόρῐος</i> (anaktórĭos, “belonging to a lord”), from <i>ἀνάκτωρ</i> (anáktōr, “lord”), from <i>ἄναξ</i> (ánax, “lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Ananias</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>חֲנַנְיָה</i> (ḥănanyâ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Anaxibius</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ναξ</i> (ắnax, “lord”) +&lrm; <i>βῐ́ος</i> (bĭ́os, “life”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anaximander, Ionian philosopher from Miletus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ναξ</i> (ắnax, “lord”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́νδρᾱ</i> (mắndrā, “enclosure”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anaxagoras</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ναξ</i> (ắnax, “lord”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́, “agora”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anaxicrates</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́νᾰξ</i> (ắnăx, “king”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anaximenes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>The pre-Socratic philosopher Anaximenes of Miletus.</li></ol></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́ναξ</i> (ắnax, “king”) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos, “might”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anastasius</li></ol><p>Etymology treeProto-Hellenic <i>&#42;aná</i>Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ́</i> (ănắ)Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ-</i> (ănă-)Ancient Greek <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ)Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓νῐ́στημῐ</i> (ănĭ́stēmĭ)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-tis</i>Ancient Greek <i>-τις</i> (-tis)Ancient Greek <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)Ancient Greek <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ́στᾰσῐς</i> (ănắstăsĭs)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-yós</i>der.Ancient Greek <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)Ancient Greek <i>Ἀναστάσιος</i> <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ́στᾰσῐς</i> (ănắstăsĭs, “resurrection”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Anastasia</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓νᾰ́στᾰσῐς</i> (ănắstăsĭs, “resurrection”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andania, Messenia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Andocides</li></ol><p>Believed to be from <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Andrew</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Andrew, one of Jesus' twelve disciples.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”), cognate with <i>ἀνδρεῖος</i> (andreîos, “manly, courageous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Andraemon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Androcles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Andromache</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “battle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Andromeda</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Andromeda</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>μέδω</i> (médō, “rule over”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Androsthenes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνδρο-</i> (andro-), combining form of <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”), + <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Androtion</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>τῑ́ων</i> (tī́ōn, “honoring”), from <i>τῑ́ω</i> (tī́ō, “to honor, revere”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Andromachus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, masculine second-declension ending).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Andronicus</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ᾰ̓νδρο-</i> (ăndro-, “man”) +&lrm; <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Compare <i>Νῑ́κᾰνδρος</i> (Nī́kăndros), which contains the first two components in the opposite order.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anemurium, Cilicia, in what is now Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anemoria (a town in Phocis, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anthemus (a town in Macedonia, Greece, situated south-east of Thessalonica; modern-day Anthemountas)</li><li>Anthemus (a town in Mesopotamia)</li><li>Anthemus (a river in Colchis)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄνθεμον</i> (ánthemon, “flower, rosette”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anthesteria or the Anthesteria: an Attic and Ionian festival held over three days in late February or early March in honor of Dionysus, the dead, and the coming spring</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>ἄνθος</i> (ánthos, “flower”), from the flowers used to decorate homes, drinking vessels, and children during the festival.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anthesterion: the eighth month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around February and March.</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>Ἀνθεστήρῐᾰ</i> (Anthestḗrĭă), an Attic and Ionian festival held around this time in honor of Dionysus, the dead, and the coming spring, named for the flowers used to decorate homes, drinking vessels, and children.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hannibal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Hannibal</i>, originally from Punic <i>𐤇𐤍𐤁𐤏𐤋</i> (ḥnbʿl), a compound of <i>𐤇𐤍</i> (ḥn, “grace”) + <i>𐤁𐤏𐤋</i> (bʿl, “master”), originally meaning “grace of (the god) Baal”, “(the god) Baal has been gracious”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name: Antenor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντήνωρ</i> (antḗnōr, “instead of a man”), from <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antigonus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “against; comparable to”) +&lrm; <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “offspring; lineage, begetting”): "worthy of his father".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Anticlea</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντῐ-</i> (antĭ-, “without”) +&lrm; <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia). Compare with <i>Εὐρῠ́κλειᾰ</i> (Eurŭ́kleiă) and <i>Μεγᾰ́κλειᾰ</i> (Megắkleiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>Ἀντίλοχε</i> (Antílokhe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antilochus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos, “ambush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antimachus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>Ἀντίνοε</i> (Antínoe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antinous</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antiochus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “hold, grasp”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, nominative case ending).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>Ἀντῐ́πᾰτρος</i> (Antĭ́pătros)</li></ol><p>A contraction of <i>Ἀντῐ́πᾰτρος</i> (Antĭ́pătros), from <i>ἀντῐ́πᾰτρος</i> (antĭ́pătros, “the title of an initiate to the mysteries of Mithras”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antibes (a town on the Côte d'Azur in Alpes-Maritimes department, France)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ́</i> (ăntĭ́, “opposite”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antiphilus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “like (a)”) +&lrm; <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los, “friend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antalcidas</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντι-</i> (anti-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδᾱς</i> (-ídās, “patronymic suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son (or descendant) of Antenor</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀντήνωρ</i> (Antḗnōr) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Antium</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄντῐον</i> (Ántĭon, “Antium”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antigenes</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Antigone</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Antigone, the daughter of Oedipus and his mother Jocasta</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “against; like; similar to”) +&lrm; <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “offspring”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē). May be interpreted as "similar to one's kin"; "worthy of one's ancestor".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Anticites; Kuban (river), Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anticles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀντί</i> (antí, “against”) +&lrm; <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Anticrates</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Anticyra; an Anticyran</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀντῐκύρᾱ</i> (Antĭkúrā, “Anticyra”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anticythera, Antikythera or Antikythira, a smaller island near Cythera/Kythira</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>Κῠ́θηρᾰ</i> (Kŭ́thēră, “Cythera, Kythira”), so named because the island is near Kythira's location but it is <i>not</i> Kythira.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anticyra (a city in Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “not”) +&lrm; an unknown element.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antiochian, Antiochene</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓ντῐόχειᾰ</i> (Ăntĭókheiă, “Antioch”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antisthenes</li></ol><p><i>ἀντί</i> (antí) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antiphon</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antioch, the name of a number of ancient cities founded by Seleucus I Nicator and named in honor of his father Antiochus:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Antioch on the Orontes, the most famous.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓ντῐ́οχος</i> (Ăntĭ́okhos, “Antiochus, name of several Seleucid kings”) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antron, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antonia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Antōnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antonius; Antony</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Antōnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Axiochus</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓ξῐ́ᾱ</i> (ăxĭ́ā, “worth, value”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os): “one who has value”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Axiocersus, one of the Cabeiri worshipped in Samothrace</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Axius, an ancient name for the Orontes and Vardar rivers</li><li>An ancient name for the Orontes river</li><li>An ancient name for the Vardar river</li></ol><p>Named after Axius, the Paeonian river god, said to be from Ancient Macedonian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Apama, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Apamā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Ἀπόλλων</i> (Apóllōn)</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Pamphylian form of <i>Ἀπόλλων</i> (Apóllōn)</li></ol><p>See <i>Ἀπόλλων</i> (Apóllōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aperlae, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apaturia</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the term is derived from an earlier adjective <i>&#42;ἀπάτουρος</i> (&#42;apátouros), itself from earlier <i>&#42;ἀπάτορϝος</i> (&#42;apátorwos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sm-ph₂tor-wo-</i>. Compare <i>ὁμοπάτωρ</i> (homopátōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apellaius, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to November</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apelles</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apidanus (a river in Thessaly, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apollonia, the name of a number of Ancient Greek cities and colonies.</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀπόλλων</i> (Apóllōn, “Apollo”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Apollonia; an Apollonian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀπολλωνίᾱ</i> (Apollōníā, “Apollonia”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Apollodorus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀπόλλων</i> (Apóllōn, “the god Apollo”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) Apollyon, the angel mentioned in Revelation 9:11.</li></ol><p>Present active participle of <i>ἀπόλλῡμι</i> (apóllūmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or belonging to Apollo</li><li>(substanivized,&#32;neuter) the temple of Apollo</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀπόλλων</i> (Apóllōn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Apollos</li></ol><p>Probably a shortened form of <i>Ἀπολλώνιος</i> (Apollṓnios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apuleius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Āpulēius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apulia (a former province in lower Italy, at the north of Calabria and east of Samnium, now Italian <i>Puglia</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Āpūlia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or pertaining to April</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) April</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Aprīlis</i> +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Apollo</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from Pre-Greek, or from an older, obsolete verb meaning "to drive away," as in evil (via Klein from Usener). Plato in Cratylus connects it to <i>ἀπόλλυμι</i> (apóllumi, “to destroy”), <i>ἀπολούων</i> (apoloúōn, “washing”), <i>ἀπολύων</i> (apolúōn, “delivering”), <i>ἁπλοῦν</i> (haploûn, “simple”), and <i>ἀειβάλλων</i> (aeibállōn, “always shooting”). Plotinus, in <i>Ennead</i> V.5.6.26-30, claims that Pythagoreans derived it from ἀ- (a-) and πολῠ́ς (polús) to render Απόλλων, literally "one without parts," as a 'symbol' of the Monad.</p><p>Sometimes said to derive from <i>*Apeljōn</i> to synchronize the Arcadocypriot and Doric variations with the Hittite deity Apaliunas, Hittite <i>𒀀𒀊𒉺𒇷𒌋𒈾𒀸</i> (A-ap-pa-li-u-na-aš).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aracynthus, the name of various mountains of Greece</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aram, an Israelite mentioned in the genealogy of Jesus.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֲרָם</i> (ʾĂrām).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Araxes; Araks, a river in the Caucasus</li></ol><p>Borrowed at the end of the 5th century BC from Old Median <i>&#42;Araxs(ā)</i>. See Old Armenian <i>Երասխ</i> (Erasx) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name of a month</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps related to Latin <i>arō</i> (“to plow”). Alternatively, may derive from a Greek source. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erh₃-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arethusa</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aretas, the name of various kings of the Nabataeans</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Nabataean Aramaic <i>𐢊𐢛𐢞𐢞</i> (ḥrtt).</p><p>Possibly from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ḥaraṯ-</i>, which would make it cognate with Hebrew <i>חרש</i> (ħaráš), and with the Arabic personal name حَارِث (ḥāriṯ), understood as from حَرَثَ (ḥaraṯa, “to plow”), perhaps referring to the king as a “provider” of abundance.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arene, Triphylia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Queen Arete, wife of Alcinous and mother of Nausicaa.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀράομαι</i> (aráomai), thus ‘she who is prayed for’.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ariminum; Rimini, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ariminum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arisba, a city of Troad situated near Abydus</li><li>A city of Lesbos destroyed by an earthquake</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) The daughter of Teucer and wife of Dardanus</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) The first wife of Paris</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristaechmus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>αἰχμή</i> (aikhmḗ, “point of a spear”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristander</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristarchus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρίσταρχος</i> (arístarkhos, “best-ruling”), from <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristippus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ariston</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Ariphron</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓ρῐ-</i> (ărĭ-, intensifying prefix) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “mind”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arion</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a peninsula of West Asia between the Red Sea and the Persian Gulf</li><li>(<i>broader sense</i>) the region of West Asia where Arabic is or was spoken, extending beyond the Arabian Peninsula</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρᾰψ</i> (Árăps, “Arab”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Arabia Felix</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Arabic, Arab</li></ol><p><i>Ἄραψ</i> (Áraps) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Parthian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Parthian. Compare Old Armenian <i>Արամազդ</i> (Aramazd), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬵𐬎𐬭𐬀 𐬨𐬀𐬰𐬛𐬁</i> (ahura mazdā). Doublet of <i>Ὡρομάζης</i> (Hōromázēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Aramean, Aramaic</li></ol><p>Calque of Aramaic <i>ܐܪܡܝܐ &#47; אָרָמָיָא</i> (ʾārāmāyā, “Aramaean”) using <i>Ἀράμ</i>&nbsp;f (Arám, “Aram”, the name of a land originally covering central regions of what is now Syria) (from Aramaic <i>ܐܪܡ &#47; ארם</i> (ʾarām)) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, adjective suffix) (compare with <i>Ἀραμαῖος</i> (Aramaîos, “Aramaean”), and the latter with <i>Χαναναῖος</i> (Khananaîos, “Chananaean”), from <i>Χαναάν</i>&nbsp;f (Khanaán, “Canaan”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Aramaeans (Aramaic-speaking Semitic tribal people who settled in northern Syria and later also in Mesopotamia)</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>ܐܪܡܝܐ &#47; אָרָמָיָא</i> (ʾārāmāyā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Aramaean</li></ol><p>Calque of Aramaic <i>ܐܪܡܝܐ &#47; אָרָמָיָא</i> (ʾārāmāyā, “Aramaean”) using <i>Ἀράμ</i>&nbsp;f (Arám, “Aram”, the name of a land originally covering central regions of what is now Syria) (from Aramaic <i>ܐܪܡ &#47; ארם</i> (ʾarām)) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix) (compare with <i>Χαναναῖος</i> (Khananaîos, “Chananaean”), from <i>Χαναάν</i>&nbsp;f (Khanaán, “Canaan”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arantia, Argolis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ararat</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֲרָרָט</i> (ʾărārāṭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aravene (an ancient province of Cappadocia, in modern Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arbaces, from Old Median</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Arbakah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian Arbarius, a Persian cavalry commander</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Old Persian <i>&#42;Arbarēvāh</i>, from Old Persian <i>&#42;Arbarāivāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arbinas, from Old Persian, a satrap of Lycia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Arbinah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arbazacius, from Old Median</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;Arabāzukah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Albanian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Albanian <i>arbër</i>&nbsp;/ <i>arbën</i> + <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arbupales, from Old Persian, Darius's brother-in-law</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Argimpasa, a Scythian goddess identified with Aphrodite by Herodotus</li></ol><p>Possibly a corruption of <i>Ᾰ̓ρτῐ́μπᾰσᾰ</i> (Ărtĭ́mpăsă), itself from a Proto-Scythian form of the name of the Iranian goddess Arti (cf. Avestan <i>𐬀𐬭𐬙𐬌</i> (arti) and <i>𐬀𐬴𐬌</i> (aṣ̌i), Sogdian <i>𐼰𐽀𐽂𐼴𐼲</i> (ʾrtwɣ)) + a term related to Iranian <i>&#42;paya-</i> (“pasture”) and <i>&#42;pati-</i> (“lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Argaeus, the name of two kings of Macedonia</li><li>Mount Erciyes, also known as Argaeus in Antiquity, the highest mountain in central Anatolia</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ργός</i> (ărgós, “bright; shining”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Argive</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Argos; an Argive</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀργεσ-</i> (Arges-), oblique stem of <i>Ἄργος</i> (Árgos), +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), with regular intervocalic loss of /σ/ and synizesis /εϊ/ → /ει/.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arginusae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Argolis (an ancient region of Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄργος</i> (Árgos, “Argos”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Argonaut</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀργώ</i> (Argṓ) +&lrm; <i>ναύτης</i> (naútēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet for Aphrodite used at one of her temples, <i>Ἀργύννιον</i> (Argúnnion), in Boeotia</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄργῠρος</i> (árgŭros, “silver”) or <i>ἀργός</i> (argós, “shing white, brilliant”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂erǵ-</i> (“brilliant white”). Cognate of Sanskrit <i>अर्जुन</i> (árjuna).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Argo</i> (ship)</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Argo</li></ol><p>From the name of her builder, <i>Ᾰ̓́ργος</i> (Ắrgos) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the Argo</li></ol><p><i>Ἀργώ</i> (Argṓ, “Argo”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a harbour town on the northern coast of Elba, now Portoferraio, Italy</li></ol><p>Named for the circumstance that the Argonauts were believed to have touched there on their return voyage, while sailing in quest of Circe.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Tauri’s or Alans’ name for the city of <i>Θεοδοσῐ́ᾱ</i> (Theodosĭ́ā, “Theodosia”).</li></ol><p>From a Alanic source signifying “[city] of the seven gods”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An Illyrian tribe settled on the Adriatic coast of the Balkans.</li></ol><p>From Illyrian. Related to, or derived from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;harδ</i>, alternatively Albanian <i>&#42;hardhë</i> (“lizard”) from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;skardā</i>.</p><p>Compare the Celtic Scordisci, Ancient Greek <i>κορδύλος</i> (kordúlos, “newt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Areopagite</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρειος πάγος</i> (Áreios págos, “Areopagus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine suffix for a member of a group).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Ariadne</li></ol><p>According to scholars in classical Greece, from Cretan dialectal <i>ἀρι-</i> (ari-, “very”) +&lrm; <i>ἀδνός</i> (adnós, “holy, pure”). Beekes, however, suggests a Pre-Greek Minoan origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ariabignes, from Old Persian, a Persian admiral</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Aryābignah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Sogdian Ariamazes, Sogdian chief</li></ol><p>Borrowed from an early Sogdian cognate of Old Persian <i>&#42;Aryamazah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian Ariamenes, son of Darius I</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Aryamanah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ariapeithes, from Scythian, a king of the Scythians</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Scythian, possibly from:</p><p>Second element likely altered through folk etymology, alongside <i>Σπαργαπείθης</i> (Spargapeíthēs), by Ancient Greek <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō, “persuade”), as found in genuine Ancient Greek names, such as <i>Διοπείθης</i> (Diopeíthēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Ariaramnes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐎡𐎹𐎠𐎼𐎶𐎴</i> (a-r-i-y-a-r-m-n /⁠Aryāramna-⁠/, literally “the one who creates peace for the Aryans”), from <i>𐎠𐎼𐎡𐎹</i> (a-r-i-y, “Aryan”) + <i>𐎼𐎶𐎴</i> (r-m-n, “peace”, probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rem-</i> (“to rest”) (compare Persian رامش (“delight, joy”) and آرام (“rest, peace”), from the same root)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ariassus, Pisidia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ariaeus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arizanti, a Persian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Numbers (Book of Numbers)</li></ol><p>Use as a proper noun of the plural of <i>ἀρῐθμός</i> (arĭthmós, “number”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the Arimaspi; an Arimaspian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ariobarzanes, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>son of one Mithridates and satrap of Phrygia and the Hellespont</li><li>local dynast of the Propontis</li><li>son of Artabazus and commander of the Erythrean soldiers at the Battle of Gaugamela</li><li>a son of Darius III</li><li>a son of Mithridates I of Pontus</li><li>an uncle of Syphax of Numidia</li><li>Ariobarzanes I of Cappadocia</li><li>Ariobarzanes II of Cappadocia</li><li>Ariobarzanes III of Cappadocia</li><li>Ariobarzanes I of Media Atropatene</li><li>Ariobarzanes II of Atropatene</li><li>a sophist from Cilicia</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Aryabr̥zans</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Aristaenetus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>αἰνετός</i> (ainetós, “praiseworthy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aristagoras</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aristaeus</li><li>epithet of Apollo</li><li>epithet of Zeus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristides</li><li>in particular, the Athenian statesman Aristides</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀριστεύς</i> (Aristeús) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristogenes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristogeiton</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́ρῐστος</i> (ắrĭstos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>γείτων</i> (geítōn, “neighbour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristocleides</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀριστοκλῆς</i> (Aristoklês) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristocles, said to be the birth name of Plato</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best, noblest”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristotle</li><li>in particular, the 4th century <small>BCE</small> philosopher Aristotle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best, excellent”) +&lrm; <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “completion, perfection”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Aristophanes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(prosody,&#32;with <b>μέτρον (métron)</b>) anapestic tetrameter</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>Ἀρῐστοφᾰ́νης</i> (Arĭstophắnēs, “Aristophanes”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix), from <i>ἄρῐστος</i> (árĭstos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “appear”), literally “best-seeming”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristophanes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in particular</i>) the 5th century <small>BCE</small> comic playwright Aristophanes</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs, “seeming, appearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristophon</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́ρῐστος</i> (ắrĭstos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristobulus</li><li>in particular, the eldest son of John Hyrcanus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀριστόβουλος</i> (aristóboulos, “best in counsel”) from <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) and <i>βουλή</i> (boulḗ, “counsel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristodemus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristomachus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristonous</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristoxenus</li></ol><p><i>ᾰ̓́ρῐστος</i> (ắrĭstos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>ξένος</i> (xénos, “stranger, guest”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristotimus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos) +&lrm; <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Aristonymus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos, “best”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνυμα</i> (ónuma, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ariovistus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ariovistus</i>, either from a Proto-Germanic compound involving <i>&#42;harjaz</i> (“army”) or <i>&#42;arjaz</i> (“esteemed”), or from a Proto-Celtic compound involving <i>&#42;aryos</i> (“nobleman”) (compare Old Irish <i>aire</i>). See Ariovistus § Etymology at Wikipedia for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arcadius, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀρκάς</i> (Arkás, “an Arcadian”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Arcadia; an Arcadian</li></ol><p>Named after <i>Ἀρκάς</i> (Arkás, “Arcas”), son of Zeus and king of Arcadia, from Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀏</i> (a-ka), <i>𐀀𐀏𐀆</i> (a-ka-de), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;árktos</i> (“bear”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ŕ̥tḱos</i>. Probably folk etymology, more likely Arcadia had an abundance of bears.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arcadia (an ancient region of Peloponnese, in modern Greece)</li></ol><p>Named after <i>Ἀρκάς</i> (Arkás, “Arcas”), son of Zeus and king of Arcadia, from Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀏</i> (a-ka), <i>𐀀𐀏𐀆</i> (a-ka-de), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;árktos</i> (“bear”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ŕ̥tḱos</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arcesilaus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ρκέω</i> (ărkéō, “assist”) +&lrm; <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “men”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arcturus, a bright star in the Boötes constellation.</li><li>The time of Arcturus's rising (mid September).</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ᾰ̓́ρκτος</i> (ắrktos, “bear”) +&lrm; <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros, “guard”), in reference to its proximity to Ursa Major and Ursa Minor (the bear constellations).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armene, Paphlagonia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Armenian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armenia (an ancient Armenian kingdom in eastern Anatolia, in modern eastern Turkey and Armenia)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐎷𐎡𐎴</i> (Armina).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Armenian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arpoxais, from Scythian, a Scythian deity</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;R̥buxšayah</i>, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;Hr̥búš</i> +&lrm; <i>&#42;xšayah</i> (“to rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arrechi, an ancient people settled near the Sea of Azov</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arsaces</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎣</i> (a-r-š-k /⁠R̥šakaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arsames, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐏁𐎠𐎶</i> (R̥šāmaʰ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arsenius</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρσην</i> (ársēn, “male”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Arses: <i>Arses</i>, a king of Persia</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;(w)r̥šā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Arsinoos (the father of Hekamede)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρσις</i> (ársis, “elevation”) +&lrm; <i>νόος</i> (nóos, “mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arsites, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a son of Artaxerxes I and Cosmartidene killed by Parysatis</li><li>father of Mithropastes and satrap of Hellespontine Phrygia</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥šitah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Arsaces; an Arsacid</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀρσάκης</i> (Arsákēs, “Arsaces”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, “child of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Arsamenes, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Aršamanā</i> (literally “having a masculine mind”), from <i>&#42;ṛšan-</i> (“man, hero”) + <i>&#42;manah-</i> (“mentality, mind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arsamosata, Sophene, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Arsinoe</li><li>Arsinoe, Arsin (any of a number of cities in modern-day Cyprus, Egypt, Eritrea, Greece, Libya, and Turkey)</li></ol><p>Feminine of <i>Ἀρσίνοος</i> (Arsínoos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artabazus, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a Median friend of Cyrus the Younger</li><li>a Persian leader of the peltasts and slingers under Cyrus the Younger</li><li>a son of Pharnaces and leader of the Parthians and Chorasmians during the Persian invasion of Greece</li><li>a possible son of Pharnabazus and Apama, and brother-in-law of Darius III and of Memnon and Mentor of Rhodes, general of Artaxerxes II in Egypt, satrap of Dascylium and successor of Megabates under Artaxerxes III and of Bactria under Darius III and Alexander the Great, and father of 11 sons and 10 daughters, including Ariobarzanes, Arsames, Pharnabazus, Ilioneus, Cophen, Artacama, Artonis and Barsine</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tavazdāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artabanus</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tabānuš</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artabasdos, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Armenian <i>Արտավազդ</i> (Artavazd).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artabes, from Old Persian, a Persian man mentioned by Aeschylus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artace; Erdek, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artames, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tamah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artaxata; Artashat, Armenia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Armenian <i>Արտաշատ</i> (Artašat).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artaozus, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a leader of the infantry under Cyrus the Younger</li><li>a friend of Cyrus the Younger</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tavazdāh</i> (“possessing endurance through Arta”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artemides (the seven daughters of the Semitic goddess Astarte as mentioned by Eusebius (quoting Philo of Byblos) in the <i>Praeparatio evangelica</i>, understood as midwives, with their name echoing Artemis's role as a goddess of childbirth)</li></ol><p>From the plural of <i>Ἄρτεμις</i> (Ártemis, “Artemis”).</p><p>Robert du Mesnil du Buisson interpreted the word <i>Τιτανίδες</i> (Titanídes) (presented in the <i>Praeparatio evangelica</i> as a synonym) as a corruption of <i>&#42;Τιθηνίδες</i> (&#42;Tithēnídes, literally “daughters of the nurse (<i>τιθήνη</i> (tithḗnē))”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artimas, from Old Persian, a satrap of Phrygia</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥timah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian Artauntes, name of several Persian generals</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐎫𐎠𐎺𐎠</i> (a-r-t-a-v-a, “truthful, righteous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artabazes, from Old Persian, name of several kings of Armenia</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tavazdāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artabazanes, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Of disputed etymology. Either borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tabr̥zans</i>, or from <i>&#42;R̥tavazdānah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Artazostre, from Old Persian, a Persian princess, daughter of Darius the Great</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Artazauštrī</i> (literally “who is in favor of Arta”) or "who is devoted to Arta".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artacamas, from Old Persian, satrap of Greater Phrygia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥takāmah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artaxerxes, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>Ἀρταξέσσης</i> (Artaxéssēs), assimilated to the name <i>Ξέρξης</i> (Xérxēs), borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐎫𐎧𐏁𐏂𐎠</i> (a-r-t-x-š-ç-a /⁠R̥taxšaçāʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artavasdes, from Old Persian, name of several kings of Armenia and Media</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tavazdāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artasyras, from Old Median, name of several Median and Achaemenid satraps</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;R̥tasūrah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artaphernes, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tafarnāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artachaees, from Old Persian, engineer of the Xerxes Canal</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥taxayah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artaeus, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>king of the Medes and father of Artynes</li><li>member of the Achaemenid dynasty and father of Artachaies and Azanes</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;R̥tayah</i> and Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tayah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Artemidorus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρτεμις</i> (Ártemis, “Artemis”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artemisium</li></ol><p>From Ἄρτεμις (Ártemis) (Artemis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Artemisius, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to April</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρτεμις</i> (Ártemis, “Artemis”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artembares, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a Median eunuch and head of the cupbearers under Astyages</li><li>the grandfather of Artayctes</li><li>an Achaemenid army commander who died at the Battle of Salamis</li><li>a descendant of Harpagus and dynast of Pinara in Lycia</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tambarah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artoxares, from Old Persian, a Paphlagonian eunuch</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥taxšarah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Artystone, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a Persian princess, daughter of Cyrus the Great, wife of Darius the Great, and mother of Arsames and Gobryas</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥tastūnā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian Artontes, name of several Persian nobles</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎼𐎫𐎠𐎺𐎠</i> (a-r-t-a-v-a, “truthful, righteous”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artycas, from Old Median, a Median king</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;R̥tukah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Artyphius, from Old Persian, a general of the Achaemenids</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;R̥difyah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Aryandes, from Old Persian, the first satrap of Egypt</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Arvantah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Archedemus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to rule”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Archedicus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to rule”) +&lrm; <i>δίκη</i> (díkē, “justice”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Archelaus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχέλᾱος</i> (arkhélāos, “leading the people”) from <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to rule”) and <i>λαός</i> (laós, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Archestratus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō, “to command”) +&lrm; <i>στρατός</i> (stratós, “army”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Archetimus</li></ol><p><i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ, “beginning”) +&lrm; <i>τῑμή</i> (tīmḗ, “honor”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Archias</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ, “power, rulership”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Archidamus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Archilochus</li><li>in particular, the 7th century <small>BCE</small> poet Archilochus of Paros</li></ol><p><i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō) +&lrm; <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Archimedes</li><li>in particular, the 3rd century <small>BCE</small> mathematician Archimedes of Syracuse</li></ol><p><i>ἀρχι-</i> (arkhi-) +&lrm; <i>μήδεα</i> (mḗdea) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aryenis, the wife of Astyages</li></ol><p>From a Lydian cognate of Hittite <i>𒂖</i> (arawanni-, “free”, as opposed to enslaved, unfree)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Aryses, from Old Persian, brother of Ariarathes II of Cappadocia</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Arušah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Asa</i>:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Asa of Judah, a king of Judah mentioned in the Books of Kings</li></ol></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>אָסָא</i> (ʾĀsāʾ), from Aramaic <i>אָסְיָא</i> (ʾāsyā, “physician, doctor, healer”), from Akkadian <i>𒀀𒍪</i> (A.ZU /⁠asû⁠/) from Sumerian <i>𒀀𒍪</i> (A.ZU /⁠azu⁠/, “physician, healer”); compare Classical Syriac <i>ܐܵܣܝܵܐ</i> (ʾāsyā, “healer, physician, medical doctor”) whence Arabic آسٍ (ʔāsin).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asher</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אשר</i> (ʼĀšēr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anatolia, Asia Minor (an ancient region of West Asia, approximately covering the area of modern Turkey)</li><li>Asia (an ancient province of the Roman Empire in western Anatolia, existing between 133 BC (during the Roman Republic) and the 7th century AD)</li></ol><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀯𐀹𐀊</i> (a-si-wi-ja). There is no current consensus on the origin of the word. It originally applied only to Anatolia, and was later extended to its current definition. Likely an extension from or related to Hittite <i>𒀸𒋗𒉿</i> (aš-šu-wa /⁠Aššuwa⁠/, “northwest Anatolia”) of uncertain origin, but perhaps from:</p><p>More at Asia, Assuwa.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asaei, an ancient tribe of Sarmatia which formed part of the Alani</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Asea; an Asean</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀσέα</i> (Aséa, “Asea”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asenath</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אָסְנַת</i> (ʾāsənaṯ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asiarch</li></ol><p><i>Ἀσία</i> (Asía) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Anatolia; an Anatolian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀσίᾱ</i> (Asíā) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asiarchate</li></ol><p><i>Ἀσιάρχης</i> (Asiárkhēs) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Asia Minor, Anatolian</li><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) a person from Asia Minor, an Anatolian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀσίᾱ</i> (Asíā) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ashkelon (a coastal city in Israel, on the Mediterranean coast)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אַשְׁקְלוֹן</i> (ʾašqəlôn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ascanius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Ascalon or Ashkelon; an Ashkelonite</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀσκάλων</i> (Askálōn, “Ashkelon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Asclepius</li></ol><p><i>Ἀσκληπιός</i> (Asklēpiós, “Asclepius”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Asclepius; an Asclepiad</li><li>Asclepiad</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀσκληπιός</i> (Asklēpiós, “Asclepius”) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Asclepiodorus</li></ol><p><i>Ἀσκληπιός</i> (Asklēpiós) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Asclepius</li><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Asclepius</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Bailly, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Aspathines, from Old Persian, a Persian official under Darius the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎿𐎱𐎨𐎴</i> (a-s-p-c-n /⁠Aspacanaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Aspasia</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aspledon, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Assaracus, a mythological king of Dardania</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assinarus (today Asinaro), a river in Sicily</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Assyria (an ancient Semitic Akkadian kingdom and at times an empire, extant from the mid-23rd century <small>BCE</small> to 608 <small>BCE</small> and centred on the Upper Tigris river in northern Mesopotamia (present day northern Iraq, but at its maximum extent including parts of modern Iran, Syria, Israel, Palestine and Egypt))</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Akkadian <i>𒀾𒋗𒊏𒅀</i> (Aššūrāyu), from <i>𒀸𒋩𒆠</i> (Aššūr, “Assur”), its original capital.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Assyrian, Assyriac</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἀσσυριακός</i> (Assuriakós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Assyrian</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assyrian (person)</li></ol><p><i>Ἀσσυρία</i> (Assuría, “Assyria”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Astarte</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤏𐤔𐤕𐤓𐤕</i> (ʿAštart).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asterium, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Asteropaios, a Trojan hero</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Astraea</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀστραῖος</i> (Astraîos, “starry”), from <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr, “star”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Astraeus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀστήρ</i> (astḗr, “star”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Astyages, from Old Median</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;R̥štivaigah</i>, modified by folk etymology (compare <i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ, “city”) and <i>ᾰ̓́γνῡμῐ</i> (ắgnūmĭ, “to break”) and compound names ending in <i>-αγης</i> (-agēs) like <i>Ᾰ̓στῠάγης</i> (Ăstŭágēs)).</p><p>See also Old Armenian <i>Աժդահակ</i> (Aždahak, “Azhdahak; Astyages”), where the sense "Astyages" was developed by folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Astyages, from Old Median</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;R̥štivaigah</i>, modified by folk etymology (compare <i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ, “city”) and <i>ᾰ̓́γνῡμῐ</i> (ắgnūmĭ, “to break”) and compound names ending in <i>-αγης</i> (-agēs)).</p><p>See also Old Armenian <i>Աժդահակ</i> (Aždahak, “Azhdahak; Astyages”), where the sense "Astyages" was developed by folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Astyanax</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ, “city”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́νᾰξ</i> (ắnăx, “king”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Astypalaia, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol><p>Maybe from <i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ, “city”) +&lrm; <i>πᾰλαιός</i> (pălaiós, “ancient”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Astyochus</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́στῠ</i> (ắstŭ) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asopus</li></ol><p>Perhaps a combination of the same root as <i>ἄσις</i> (ásis, “slime, mud”) and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ep-</i> (“water”). Compare Ancient Greek <i>Ἰνωπός</i> (Inōpós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asopius</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀσωπός</i> (Asōpós) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atilius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Atīlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Atalanta</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓-</i> (ă-, used to express unity) +&lrm; <i>τᾰ́λᾰντον</i> (tắlănton, “balanced”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atarneus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gypsy</li><li>general term of insult</li></ol><p>First attested in the 14th century. Traditionally associated with the <i>ἀθίγγανος</i> (athínganos, literally “the untouchable”), a member of a Christian sect, but this is often rejected. Perhaps from Ottoman Turkish چنكانه (çengâne), from Persian چنگانه (čingāna, “Gipsy”), if not the other way around. Ultimately possibly of Turkic origin, from Proto-Turkic <i>&#42;čïgāń</i>, compare Turkish <i>çığan</i>, <i>çığay</i> (“poor”), compare also <i>τσιγκούνης</i> (tsigkoúnis, “stingy”). If so the initial <i>ἀ-</i> (a-) would be an innovation within Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attica (an ancient region of Greece)</li><li>the Attic dialect</li><li>the history of Athens</li><li>Athens (an ancient city-state in modern Greece)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Attic woman</li></ol><p>From the same source as <i>Ἀττική</i> (Attikḗ, “Attica”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Atizyes, from Old Persian, a Persian satrap of Phrygia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adige/Etsch, a river in northeastern Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlantic Ocean</li><li>Atlantis</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄτλᾱς</i> (Átlās, “Atlas”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atlantic Ocean</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀτλᾰντῐκός</i> (Atlăntĭkós), from Ἄτλᾱς (Átlās, “Atlas”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Atlas</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of the Titan Atlas, Atlantean</li><li>of or beyond the Atlas Mountains, Atlantic</li></ol></ol><p><i>Ᾰ̓́τλᾱς</i> (Ắtlās, stem: Ᾰ̓τλᾰντ-, <i>Atlant-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian Atradates</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a name of Cambyses I, father of Cyrus the Great</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Ātr̥dātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edremit (a city in Balıkesir Province, Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Ἀτρείδης</i> (Atreídēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Patronymic,&#32;poetic epithet of Agamemnon and Menelaus) son of Atreus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀτρεύς</i> (Atreús, “Atreus”) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn, “son of”, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Atreus, an Atreid</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in particular</i>) Agamemnon</li></ol></ol><p>From Homeric <i>Ἀτρεΐδης</i> (Atreḯdēs), from <i>Ἀτρεύς</i> (Atreús, “Atreus”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Atreus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “not”) +&lrm; <i>τρέω</i> (tréō, “fear”). Compare Hittite <i>𒀜𒋻𒅆𒅀</i> (Attaršiya).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Atropates, from Old Persian, notably borne by</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Atropates (a Persian nobleman of the 4th century BC who was satrap of Media under Darius III and retained control of northern Media (named after him Atropatian Media or simply Atropatia/Atropatene) following Alexander the Great's conquest)</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Atropatian</li></ol><p><i>Ἀτροπάτης</i> (Atropátēs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atropatian Media</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atropatia, Atropatene, Atropatian Media</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atropatene (an ancient region in modern Iran, now known as (Iranian) Azerbaijan)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀτροπατηνός</i> (Atropatēnós), from <i>Ἀτροπάτης</i> (Atropátēs, “Atropates”) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós), a Persian nobleman who founded the independent kingdom of Atropatene, from Old Persian personal name <i>&#42;Ātṛpāta</i> (literally “protected by fire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atropatene, Atropatian, Atropatenian (person)</li></ol><p><i>Ἀτροπάτης</i> (Atropátēs) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet of Athena</li></ol><p>Traditionally held to be from <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) +&lrm; <i>τρῡ́ω</i> (trū́ō, “to tire out, wear out”) + a derivative of <i>-τος</i> (-tos), thus ‘the unwearied one, the indefatigable one’.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antalya, Attalia (a city in modern Turkey)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄτταλος</i> (Áttalos) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Roman nomen gentile, gens or "family name"</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Attidius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attica</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic; Athenian</li></ol><p>From a Pre-Greek etymon +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós). Compare <i>Ἀθήνη</i> (Athḗnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aphidnae; Afidnes, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Africa (a former province of the Roman Empire, containing what is now Tunisia and portions of coastal Algeria and Libya)</li><li>Africa (the continent south of Europe and between the Atlantic and Indian Oceans)</li></ol><p>Formed alongside Latin <i>Africa</i>. Occurs in Ptolemy, Cassius Dio, and later authors. The adjective <i>Ἀφρικανός</i> (Aphrikanós) is older, attested in fragments attributed to Manetho (3rd century BC), as well as in Polybius (died 118 BC), Posidonius, Diodorus Siculus and Plutarchus.</p><p>The word was received by the Romans from the Carthaginians as the term for their country.</p><p>The term replaces <i>Λιβύη</i> (Libúē) as the name of the continent (i.e. the part of the world south of the Mediterranean) in the Roman era (from ca. the 1st century AD), but like <i>Λιβύη</i> (Libúē) also remains in use for the more limited area between Egypt and Numidia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to Aphrodite, sexual</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter&#32;plural) sexual pleasures</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter&#32;plural) Aphrodisia (festival of Aphrodite)</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) the temple of Aphrodite</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀφροδῑ́τη</i> (Aphrodī́tē) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Aphrodite</li><li>the planet Venus</li></ol><p>The theonym is found in Homer and Hesiod (c. 8th century B.C.).</p><p>Traditionally it has been viewed as a compound, <i>ἀφρός</i> (aphrós, “sea foam, froth”) +&lrm; <i>-δίτη</i> (-dítē), connecting the first part with the legend of the goddess' birth. This has not been accepted by modern scholars.</p><p>There is no etymology generally accepted in scholarship. Some such as Beekes propose that the name in its entirety is a loan from a non-Greek language, of probable "oriental origin", possibly Cypriot Phoenician (such as in the Idalion bilingual). A connection to Akkadian <i>𒀭𒈹</i> (<sup>d</sup>Ištar) has been further proposed.</p><p>An older, now rejected proposal of a Greek etymology connects <i>-δίτη</i> (-dítē) with the verb <i>δέατο</i> (déato, “to shine, to appear, seem”) (Homeric <i>δῆλος</i> (dêlos, “visible, conspicuous, clear”)) and interprets the name as originating from an epithet of the dawn goddess <i>Ἠώς</i> (Ēṓs).</p><p>Associated by the Romans with Venus, originally a goddess of less meaning and prominence.</p><p>More on Wikipedia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aphroditus</li></ol><p>The name is a masculine form of <i>Ἀφροδῑ́τη</i> (Aphrodī́tē, “Aphrodite”), who was represented as a herm with a phallus, the symbol of fertility, but afterwards as a divine being combining the two sexes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acheron, a river in Epirus</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek origin. Connection to Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;éźeran</i> (“lake”) is dubious. Folk etymology claims it from <i>ὁ ᾰ̓χῶν ῥέων</i> (ho ăkhôn rhéōn, “the stream of woes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achaea, a region on the north coast of the Peloponnese in Greece.</li><li>Achaea, a province of the Roman Empire, comprising the majority of Greece.</li></ol><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀏𐀹𐀊𐀆</i> (a-ka-wi-ja-de), Hittite <i>𒆳𒌷𒄴𒄭𒅀𒉿𒀀</i> (Aḫḫiyawā). Beekes reconstructs Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>Ἀχαΐα</i> (Akhaḯa)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) Achaean woman</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achaean woman</li></ol><p><i>Ἀχαΐα</i> (Akhaḯa) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Achaian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀχᾱΐᾱ</i> (Akhāḯā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achaemenes (the founder of the Achaemenid dynasty)</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐏃𐎧𐎠𐎶𐎴𐎡𐏁</i> (h-x-a-m-n-i-š /⁠Haxāmaniš⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Achaemenes; an Achaemenid</li></ol><p>From <i>Ᾰ̓χαιμένης</i> (Ăkhaiménēs, “Achaemenes”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs). Equivalent of Old Persian <i>𐏃𐎧𐎠𐎶𐎴𐎡𐏁𐎡𐎹</i> (Haxāmanišyaʰ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Achaean, from the region of Achaea in Greece.</li><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) Greek</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Achaea; an Achaean</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀχαΐα</i> (Akhaḯa, “Achaea”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix). It is also attested as in the older forma <i>Ἀχαιϝοί</i> (Akhaiwoí), possibly coming via an intermediate form <i>&#42;Ἀχαϝyοί</i> (&#42;Akhawyoí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acharnae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Acharnae</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀχαρναί</i> (Akharnaí, “Acharnae”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achelous, a name of several rivers</li><li>(<i>Poetic</i>) a stream, water</li></ol><p>Probably from Pre-Greek, possibly Semitic, with the first element representing Akkadian <i>𒀀𒄷</i> (“river bank”) and second element representing <i>𒅋𒇻</i> (“watercourse”) or <i>𒄿𒇻</i> (“deity, god”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acherdus, a demos of Attica</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Acherontic (pertaining to Acheron)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἀχέρων</i> (Akhérōn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ᾰ̓χῐλλεύς</i> (Ăkhĭlleús), with shortened second syllable for metrical purposes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Achilles</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. In Mycenaean Greek Linear B texts, the name <i>𐀀𐀑𐀩𐀄</i> (a-ki-re-u, nominative singular) / <i>𐀀𐀑𐀩𐀸</i> (a-ki-re-we, dative singular) is attested and generally taken to be the same name.</p><p>Since ancient times and in the present day, some have speculated that the name is connected to <i>ᾰ̓́χος</i> (ắkhos, “distress; grief”) and <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “people”), as the grief Achilles causes people is a central theme of the Iliad.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Achillas, a Pope of Alexandria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apsimar, the birthname of Tiberius III</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Germanic or possibly Proto-Slavic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hagia Sophia (former basilica, now a mosque in Turkey)</li></ol><p>Literally, “Holy Wisdom”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Ᾱ̓γησῐ́λᾱος</i> (Āgēsĭ́lāos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Ᾱ̓γησῐ́λᾱος</i> (Āgēsĭ́lāos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Hagne, equivalent to English Agnes or Inez</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̔γνή</i> (hăgnḗ), the feminine of <i>ᾰ̔γνός</i> (hăgnós, “pure; chaste”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Adrianople or Edirne; an Adrianopolitan</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἁδριανούπολις</i> (Hadrianoúpolis, “Adrianople, Edirne”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adrianople; Edirne, Turkey</li></ol><p><i>Ἁδριανός</i> (Hadrianós, “Hadrian (Roman emperor)”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hadrian, from Latin</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Hadriānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hathyr, the third month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to November</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./c0bbac8f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="O10" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./3fdd74a2.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="C9" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (ḥwt-ḥr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Haliartus (a city in Boeotia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Halisarna, Mysia, Turkey</li><li>Halisarna, Cos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Halitherses, an Ithacan prophet who plays a role in the <i>Odyssey</i></li></ol><p>Probably from <i>ἅλς</i> (háls, “sea; salt”) +&lrm; Aeolic Greek <i>θέρσος</i> (thérsos, “courage”) (Ionic Greek <i>θάρσος</i> (thársos)) + <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Halicarnassus or Petronium or Bodrum, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἁλικαρνασσός</i> (Halikarnassós, “Halicarnassus”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Halicarnassus; Petronium; Bodrum, Turkey</li></ol><p>Unknown; suggested to be borrowed from or influenced by a Luwic substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Halicyae, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Alphaeus, the name of the father of two of Jesus' disciples</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>חַלְפַּי</i> (ḥalpay).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the wife and sister of Drilo, and the mother of the hamadryads</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) a woodnymph, a hamadryad</li><li>synonym of <i>βάλανος</i> (bálanos, “oak-tree”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arimathea, an ancient city in Judea, noted only as the hometown of "Joseph of Arimathea."</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Armageddon (a mythological battleground mentioned in Revelation 16:16, where an apocalyptic battle will be fought)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>הַר מְגִדּוֹ</i> (har Məgiddô, “mountain of Megiddo”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Harmodius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Harpocrates</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>ḥr</i> (“Horus”) + <i>pꜣ</i> (“the”) + <i>ẖrd</i> (“child”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hasdrubal</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Hasdrubal</i>, originally from Punic <i>𐤏𐤆𐤓𐤁𐤏𐤋</i> (ʿzrbʿl, literally “help is Baal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abdera (a city in Thrace, Greece)</li></ol><p>Phoenician.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abila (an ancient city in the Decapolis, situated between Gadara and Capitolias in modern Jordan)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʾabal-</i> (“verdant growth”), compare Hebrew <i>אָבֵל</i> (ʾāḇēl, “meadow”) and Arabic أَبَلَ (ʔabala, “to grow green”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ablerus, a Trojan slain by Antilochus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Sarmatian, possibly from <i>&#42;Aβrag</i>, cognate with Old Persian <i>&#42;Abrakah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abydos (an ancient city in Egypt) with archaeological interest.</li><li>Abydos (An ancient city of Mysia in northwest Asia Minor in modern Turkey, on the Hellespont)</li></ol><p><div>The name of the Egyptian city is borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./15cb8bb5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Ab" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Dw&#58;O49</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (ꜣbḏw).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hagar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>הָגָר</i> (Hāġār).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Agarus; Berda river, Ukraine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Agbalus, an Arcadian man and father of Merbalus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Angrus; South Morava</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Illyrian: Agron, an Illyrian king</li><li>Agron, a Lydian king</li></ol><p>From Illyrian. Possibly related to Albanian <i>ag</i> (“dawn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adad</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒀭𒅎</i> (<sup>d</sup>IŠKUR /⁠Adad⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἀδάμ</i> (Adám, “Adam”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Adulis; Zula, Eritrea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Adrastus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Adonis</li><li>a type of fish</li></ol><p>Believed to be from Phoenician <i>𐤀𐤃𐤍</i> (ʾdn /⁠adōn⁠/, “lord”), from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʾadan-</i> (“to rule, judge”), and so perhaps cognate to Hebrew <i>אדון</i> (adón, “lord”) and therefore Hebrew <i>אדוני</i> (adonái, “my lords; the Lord, a name of God in Judaism”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Azes, from Proto-Saka, the name of two Indo-Scythian kings</li></ol><p>From Proto-Saka <i>&#42;aza</i> (“leader”) (cf. Khotanese (hinajha, “commander of army”)).</p><p>Cognate with Gandhari <i>𐨀𐨩</i> (aya).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Azorus (a city in Perrhaebia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athribis (a town in Egypt) situated on the delta of the Nile</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ecb2f15d.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Hwt" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">t&#58;pr</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Hr&#58;ib</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9d93c55f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="niwt" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>ḥwt-ḥrj-jb</i>, “Athribis”), from earlier <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ecb2f15d.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Hwt" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">t&#58;pr</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">tA&#58;Hr&#42;ib</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9d93c55f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="niwt" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>ḥwt-tꜣ-ḥrj-jb</i>).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Athrys; Yantra, Bulgaria</li></ol><p>A theory links this hydronym to Latvian <i>ātrs</i> (“quick, rapid, fast”) and Old High German <i>ātar</i> (“sharp; quick, fast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Athos, a character in Greek mythology</li><li>Athos (a peninsula in Greece, containing Mount Athos)</li><li>Mount Athos (a mountain and peninsula in northeastern Greece) which is an important centre of Greek Orthodox monasticism.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Aciris; river Agri, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river on the east coast of Sicily, that flowed from Etna now extinct.</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acrillae, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Actium (now called Punta)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alapta, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ahala</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ahala</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἄλβα</i> (Álba), a place name:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Alba Longa (an ancient city in Latium, in modern Italy)</li><li>an ancient town in the Roman province of Hispania Citerior, in modern eastern Spain</li><li>Alba Fucens (an ancient Italic town in modern Abruzzo region, Italy)</li><li>Alba Pompeia (an ancient city in modern Liguria region, Italy, present-day Alba)</li><li>an ancient city in Crete, in modern Greece</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Alba</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elbe (a river in central Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Albis</i>, from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;albī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name meaning “helper; defender”, equivalent to English Alexis</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓λέξω</i> (ăléxō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aleus, king of Arcadia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alinda, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Alcestis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀλκηστής</i> (alkēstḗs, “valiant”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs). See <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alcon, any of a number of figures of classical history and mythology</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Almopes, an ancient Paeonian people inhabiting the region of Almopia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Halos; Phthiotis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alps</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Alpēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alpis, a tributary of the Danube, probably the river Inn</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amanus; Nur Mountains</li></ol><p>Uncertain. May share an origin with the comparative adjective ἀμείνων, similarly to κρατύς and κρείσσων, implying a meaning of "good".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amasis</li></ol><p><div>From Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./39a6431a.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="iaH" alt="glyph" height="30"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./cc95fca4.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="ms" alt="glyph" height="92"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./171915d5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="s" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (jꜥḥ-ms).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amastris, Amasra (a city in Paphlagonia, modern-day Turkey)</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Amāstrī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ambrysus, Phocis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Amestris, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Amāstrī</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amilus, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Amun</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>jmn</i> (“Amun”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amolbus, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or from Amyclae.</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>Ἀμῠ́κλαι</i> (Amŭ́klai) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amyrus, Thessaly, Greece</li><li>a river flowing through this city</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Amytis, from Old Median</li></ol><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;Humatiš</i> with metathesis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amphissa; Amfissa</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amphrysus, a small river that flows between Thessaly and Magnesia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Anaea</i></li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄναιος</i> (Ánaios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>It was a river in ancient Acarnania, tributary of the Achelous River, which it joined eighty stadia south of Stratus.</li><li>One of the most considerable rivers in Sicily, which flows into the great harbour of Syracusae, now the river Anapo.</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Anapus, a water god of eastern Sicily. He opposed the kidnapping of Persephone along with the nymph Cyane, then Hades turned them into a river (the river Anapo) and a fountain, respectively.</li></ol><p>Uncertain, possibly from a substrate language. Said to come from the same origin of <i>ἀναπίνω</i> (anapínō, “to absorb, suck up, drink in”), from <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-, “up to, thoroughly”) +&lrm; <i>πίνω</i> (pínō, “to drink, giggle, swallow, absorb”), meaning "<i>The one who swallows</i>", due to the fact that its river path disappears underground to reappear further on several times; also compared with the opposite Ancient Greek <i>ἀναβαίνω</i> (anabaínō). Cfr. Ancient Greek <i>Αἴσηπος</i> (Aísēpos) for a similar morphology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anartes, a Celtic tribe of Dacia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Andros, an island in the Cyclades</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hannah (biblical character)</li><li>a female given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Anna or Hannah</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>חַנָּה</i> (Ḥannâ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hanno</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Hannō</i>, originally from Punic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Anubis (an Egyptian god)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">n&#58;p</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./590994c8.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="E15" alt="glyph" height="80"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./0de110ef.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="G7" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i>.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antandrus (a city in Troad, modern-day Turkey)</li><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antander</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ-</i> (ăntĭ-, “before, against”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antium (an ancient coastal town in Latium, Italy, south of Rome; near the modern towns of Anzio and Nettuno)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Antium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Antissa, Lesbos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Antiphus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anysus, a Sidonian man and father of Tetramnestus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Anytus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἐνώς</i> (Enṓs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἑνώχ</i> (Henṓkh)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Axylus, a Thracian slain by Diomedes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Appius, from Latin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Appius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Araplus, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arar; Saône</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Aratus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀρᾱτός</i> (arātós, “prayed for”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Arachthus, Epirus, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arab</li><li>Arabian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Arabic عَرَب (ʕarab).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arba, Achaea, Greece</li><li>a city and island of Dalmatia, now Rab</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>A Celtic god worshipped by the Arverni, who were named after him</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any individual among the Arverni</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Arvernus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arbela (an ancient city in Mesopotamia, in modern Iraq; modern Arbil)</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒌷𒅈𒁀𒀪𒅋</i> (<sup>uru</sup>ar-ba-ʾ-il /⁠Arbaʾil⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Argilus</li></ol><p>First attested in <i>c.</i> 6th century. According to Stephanus of Byzantium this Macedonian city is named after Thracian <i>&#42;argilos</i> (“mouse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Argos</li><li>Name of a Doric tribal confederation</li><li>Argus</li></ol><p>Usually said to be related to <i>ἀργός</i> (argós, “bright, shining”).</p><p>According to Gamkrelidze's hypothesis, ultimately from Proto-Georgian-Zan <i>&#42;egr-</i> (“~ western Georgia”) (cf. Mingrelian <i>მ-არგ-ალი</i> (m-arg-ali, “Mingrelian”), from Proto-Georgian-Zan <i>&#42;m-egr-el-i</i>), a toponym allegedly picked up by the linguistic ancestors of Greeks during their theoretical migration from near the Caucasus, along with <i>Ἀργοναύτης</i> (Argonaútēs) and <i>Ἀργολίς</i> (Argolís). Few other scholars support the proto-Greeks' migration through the Caucasus and Asia Minor, however.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Areopagus, Hill of Ares, Mars’ Hill, a famous hill in Athens used in classical times for various legislative and judicial functions.</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄρειος</i> (Áreios, “devoted to Ares”) (from Ἄρης (Árēs, “Ares”)) + <i>πᾰ́γος</i> (pắgos, “rock”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἄρειος πάγος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Ἄρειος Πάγος</i> (Áreios Págos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ares</li><li>Mars (planet)</li><li>war, warlike spirit</li><li>epithet of Zeus, "avenger"</li></ol><p>Usually derived from the Ionic word <i>ἀρή</i> (arḗ, “bane, ruin”), which could be related to Sanskrit <i>इरस्या</i> (irasyā, “malevolence”), suggesting a Proto-Indo-European origin.</p><p>However, Morris Silver and Pierre Chantraine propose a derivation from <i>ἄρος</i> (áros, “use, profit, help”) instead.</p><p><i>𐀀𐀩</i> (a-re), found in Linear B, is thought to be the oldest attested form of the name.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arnae, in Macedonia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Arne</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Arpi</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Artemis, one of the Greek goddesses.</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Attested from an early date as Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀳𐀖𐀵</i> (a-te-mi-to) and Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀴𐀖𐀳</i> (a-ti-mi-te); this and its alternation of <i>e</i> and <i>i</i> led Beekes and Georgios Babiniotis to suggest the name is from a Pre-Greek substrate. A connection to Anatolian names has also been suggested; Artemis was venerated in Lydia as <i>Artimus</i>. Ancient Greek writers, by way of folk etymology, linked the name (found in Doric as <i>Artamis</i>) to <i>ἄρταμος</i> (ártamos, “butcher”) or <i>ἀρτεμής</i> (artemḗs, “safe, unharmed; pure; maiden”). Others compare Artemis to the Cretan <i>Βρῐτόμαρτις</i> (Brĭtómartis, “Britomartis”), with whom she was syncretized.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Archabis, Pontus, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asiba, Pontus, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) found only as an epithet of <i>λειμών</i> (leimṓn, “meadow”)&#59; conveniently translated ‘Asian’, but it is more likely that Asia gets its name from this meadow rather than the other way around. Eustathius of Thessalonica glosses the term as <i>ἰλυώδης</i> (iluṓdēs, “muddy”), in which case it should be written in lower case, i.e. <i>ἄσιος</i> (ásios).</li></ol><p>Unknown; compare <i>Ἀσίᾱ</i> (Asíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Asculum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Asculum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ascra</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aspendos, Aspendus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assus, Mysia, Turkey</li><li>river Assus, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Assorus; Assoro, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Astacus; Astakos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Astyra, Aeolis, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́στῠρον</i> (ắstŭron, “small city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Asychis, from Egyptian, an Egyptian pharaoh mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ascheium, Achaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Atlas</li><li>Atlas Mountains</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ἁ-</i> (ha-, copulative prefix) + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;telh₂-</i>, the root of <i>ἔτλην</i> (étlēn, “to suffer, to endure”). Beekes suggests it is a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Atossa</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Hutōθā</i>, <i>&#42;Hutauθā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Ἄτῠς</i> (Átŭs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Atys</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Lydian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aphytis, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Acholla, Byzacena, Tunisia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Apsorus; Lošinj, Croatia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ᾍδης</i> (Hāídēs)</li><li>Ionic form of <i>ᾍδης</i> (Hāídēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to (the realm of) Hades, to the netherworld</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἄϊδος</i> (Áïdos) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Abel, the son of Adam</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>הָ֑בֶל</i> (háḇel), pausal form of <i>הֶ֫בֶל</i> (heḇel).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hagnon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Hathor (the goddess of joy, love, and motherhood; one of the "Eyes / Daughters of Ra", the consort of Ra/Horus; often depicted as having a cow's head)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./c0bbac8f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="O10" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./3fdd74a2.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="C9" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i>.</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Alaesa, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The river Halys, now called Kızılırmak</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Harpagus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Arbakah</i>, shaped by folk etymology through association with the word <i>ἁρπαγή</i> (harpagḗ, “robbery”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Agis</li><li>The name of several kings of Sparta.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἄκις</i> (Ákis)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>the augment, a prefix marking indicative past tenses</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁é-</i>. Cognate with Sanskrit <i>अ-</i> (á-), Phrygian <i>ε-</i> (e-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>New Testament&#32;Koine</i>) Used in place of <i>ἄν</i> (án) after relative pronouns and conjunctions</li><li>(<i>Classical Greek</i>) Used instead of <i>ἄν</i> (án) for Future Open Conditionals (If X happens, Y will happen)</li></ol><p>Quantitative metathesis of <i>εἰᾰ́ν</i> (eiắn), itself a univerbation of <i>εἰ</i> (ei) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ν</i> (ắn, modal particle).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with accusative of person and infinitive</i>) to let someone do something, allow</li><li>to cede, grant, yield</li><li>to agree</li><li>to advise</li><li>to neglect, disregard</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ewajō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wéh₂-ye-ti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₁weh₂-</i> (“to leave, abandon”). However, the augment of the Classical Greek preterite points to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hewajō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἔλδωρ</i> (éldōr, “desire, wish”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔργω</i> (érgō, “to close off, enclose”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εὖ</i> (eû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εὔθρονος</i> (eúthronos, “having a beautiful throne”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) well-scraped, well-planed, polished</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐϋ-</i> (eü-, “good, well”) + the root of <i>ξέω</i> (xéō, “to scrape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) good, brave, noble</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀄-</i> (e-u-), of disputed origin:</p><p>It is possible that the two competing roots are ultimately related, stemming from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wes-</i> (“good”); this is debated, however.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εὔσελμος</i> (eúselmos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Lyric form of <i>εὔτροχος</i> (eútrokhos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or in spring, vernal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔαρ</i> (éar) +&lrm; <i>-ινός</i> (-inós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li><i>aorist active indicative third-person singular of</i> βαστάζω: to carry off, to take away</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of ebony</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔβενος</i> (ébenos, “ebony”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felty germander (<i>Teucrium polium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔβενος</i> (ébenos, “ebony”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html>:/eŋ/
<h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) used before the velar consonants <i>γ, κ, ξ, χ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be born in; to live in</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring near, bring up to</li><li>(<i>mostly intransitive</i>) to come near, approach</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγγῠ́ς</i> (engŭ́s, “near”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hereditary</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>γενῐκός</i> (genĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark in or on, to paint on</li><li>to inscribe, write in or on</li><li>to enter on a list or register</li><li>to indict</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγ-</i> (eg-) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put in the palm of one's hand</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>γῠ́ᾰλον</i> (gŭ́ălon) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A stand for vessels or tripods</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγγύς</i> (engús, “near”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>granddaughter</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) + the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē). Compare Latin <i>indigena</i> (“native, indigenous”) and Primitive Irish <i>ᚔᚅᚔᚌᚓᚅᚐ</i> (inigena, “daughter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pledge, surety, security, whether received or given</li><li>betrothal, contract for a future marriage</li></ol><p>It is usually assumed that this word contains the preposition <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;γύη</i> (&#42;gúē), a lost word for "hand", which is preserved in Avestan <i>𐬔𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀</i> (gauua, “hand”), as well as in <i>ὑπόγυιος</i> (hupóguios, “nigh at end, imminent”). The Avestan form is ambiguous, however, as it can also continue <i>&#42;gabʰa-</i> and be related to Sanskrit <i>गभस्ति</i> (gabhasti, “arm or hand”), which makes the etymology doubtful.</p><p>Further, Greek has <i>γύαλον</i> (gúalon, “hollow of a cuirass”), <i>γυῖον</i> (guîon, “hand”), and <i>γύης</i> (gúēs, “earth”), and from other languages, we may adduce Lithuanian <i>gáuti</i> (“to get, obtain”), suggesting a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“hand”). See also <i>ἐγγύς</i> (engús, “nearby”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from nearby</li><li>nearby</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>near</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγγῠ́ς</i> (engŭ́s, “near”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>near</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγγύς</i> (engús, “near”) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi, “at”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>near</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>)</li><li>(<i>of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>of numbers</i>)</li><li>(<i>of qualities</i>)</li><li>(<i>of relationships</i>) akin</li></ol></ol><p>Seems to be from <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in, at”) + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gew-</i> (“hand; to curve”) (whence <i>γυῖον</i> (guîon, “hand”), <i>ἐγγῠ́η</i> (engŭ́ē, “pledge, security”)), thus meaning "at hand (i.e. nearby)".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to awaken, wake up, rouse, stir</li><li>to rouse, stir up</li><li>to raise from the dead, or from a sick bed, resurrect</li><li>to raise or erect a building</li></ol><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to wake, keep watch</li><li>to rouse oneself, be excited</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;egéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ger-</i> (“to awake”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>जागृ</i> (jāgṛ, “to be awake, alert”); see the Sanskrit for more cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(active use only) one must raise</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγείρω</i> (egeírō, “to wake”) +&lrm; <i>-τέον</i> (-téon, “a suffix forming a verbal adjective”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inside the head</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brain</li><li>heart of the date palm</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>κεφᾰλή</i> (kephălḗ, “head”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) a feast of dedication, especially that established by Judas Maccabeus at the reconsecration of the temple in Jerusalem: Hanukkah</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγ-</i> (eg-) +&lrm; <i>καινός</i> (kainós) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia). Semantic loan from Biblical Hebrew <i>חֲנֻכָּה</i> (ḥănukkâ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place in, establish in</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>καθίστημι</i> (kathístēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call in (<i>a debt</i>)</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to bring a charge against, accuse [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to bring as a charge [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to mix in with, mingle among; to integrate into an existing group or substance</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to interweave, blend (ideas, qualities, emotions)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be mixed in or among; to be mingled with</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>κᾰτᾰμῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (kătămī́gnūmĭ), literally “to mix in among”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐγκᾰτᾰμῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (enkătămī́gnūmĭ)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγκᾰτᾰμῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (enkătămī́gnūmĭ), by analogical formation with verbs in <i>-ῠ́ω</i> (-ŭ́ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell in</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “inhabit, dwell”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sting, prickly object</li><li>pointed style for writing</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) spurs</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) crampons for climbing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔγκεντρος</i> (énkentros, “furnished with a sting”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liable to cause disputes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔγκλημᾰ</i> (énklēmă, “accusation, charge”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) enclitic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔγκλισις</i> (énklisis, “inclination”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) intestines</li><li>ribs of a ship, belly-timbers</li></ol><p>Neuter plural form of <i>ἐγκοίλῐος</i> (enkoílĭos, “in the belly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mastery over</li><li>self-control</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγκρᾰτής</i> (enkrătḗs, “possession, power over”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A cake made with oil and honey, similar to the modern loukoumas or doughnut</li></ol><p>Unknown. Not related to <i>ἐγκεράννυμι</i> (enkeránnumi, “to mix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European anchovy (<i>Engraulis encrasicolus</i>)</li></ol><p>Maybe called this way because its intestines are attached to the head.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in possession of power, in charge</li><li>holding fast</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>stout, strong</li></ol><li>having possession of a thing, master of it [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>master of oneself, self-controlling, self-disciplined</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exercise self-control, temperance, moderation</li><li>to force oneself to accomplish something</li><li>to starve oneself</li><li>to be a member of the Encratites</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγκρατής</i> (enkratḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εύομαι</i> (-eúomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a strong hand, by force</li><li>with self-control, temperately</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγκρατής</i> (enkratḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loaf baked in the ashes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔγκρυφος</i> (énkruphos, “hidden, concealed”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide or conceal in</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἐγκῠ́κλῐος</i> (enkŭ́klĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἐγκῠ́κλῐος</i> (enkŭ́klĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of frock or apron, worn especially by slaves to keep their tunic clean</li><li>kind of sheepskin coat worn by shepherds</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγκομβόομαι</i> (enkombóomai, “to bind a thing on oneself”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>circular</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>general education</li></ol><p>Literally, “circular (i.e. all-round, well rounded) education”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a laudatory ode, a praise, a eulogy</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter of <i>ἐγκώμιος</i> (enkṓmios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wakeful</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγείρω</i> (egeírō, “awaken”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wakefulness</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rousing the din of war, strife-stirring</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the epithet of Pallas</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγείρω</i> (egeírō, “to wake”) +&lrm; <i>κυδοιμός</i> (kudoimós, “the din of war”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rousing the fight</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγείρω</i> (egeírō, “to wake”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs, “a suffix forming third-declension adjectives”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour in; to fill the cup</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of dry things</i>) to pour in, to shoot in</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to thrust in</li></ol><li>(<i>metaphoric</i>) to infuse, to instill</li></ol><li>(<i>of cups</i>) to fill by pouring in</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in, into”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) spear (long stick with a sharp tip)</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>ἔγχος</i> (énkhos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking in hand, undertaking</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγχειρέω</i> (enkheiréō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attempt [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to lay hands on, attack, assail [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to hand over, transfer</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>χείρ</i> (kheír) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a handbook, a manual.</li><li>a dagger.</li></ol><p>Nominalised neuter form of <i>ἐγχειρίδιος</i> (enkheirídios, “fitting in the hand”) derived from <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anoint or smear into or onto a surface</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to anoint oneself</li><li>to sting, prick, or inject (especially poison), as by a bite or sting; also used metaphorically of internal irritation or psychic pain</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to taint, corrupt, or smear metaphorically</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear; anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the country, local, true-born</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>χώρα</i> (khṓra, “place, location”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, “belonging to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>first person singular personal pronoun: I, me, my</li></ol><p>Cf. Homeric Greek <i>ἐγών</i> (egṓn). From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;eǵóH</i>, <i>&#42;eǵh₂óm</i>. Compare for example Latin <i>ego</i>, Sanskrit <i>अहम्</i> (ahám).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>ἐγώ</i> (egṓ) +&lrm; <i>γε</i> (ge)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Doric form of <i>ἐγώ</i> (egṓ, “I”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>ἐγὼ</i> (egṑ) +&lrm; <i>οἶδᾰ</i> (oîdă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>ἐγώ</i> (egṓ) +&lrm; <i>οἶμαι</i> (oîmai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to teach</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to learn</li><li>(<i>in the perfect passive</i>) to know (= have learned)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dens-</i> (“to learn”), found also in <i>διδάσκω</i> (didáskō, “to teach”). Cognates include Avestan <i>𐬛𐬍𐬛𐬀𐬌𐬣𐬵𐬉</i> (dīdaiŋ́hē) (&lt; <i>&#42;di-dens-h₂ey</i>), Sanskrit <i>द॒स्र</i> (dasrá, “effecting miracles”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <i>ἔδαφος</i> (édaphos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A food hamper</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔδεσμα</i> (édesma, “meat, food”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>φαγέδαινα</i> (phagédaina, “cankersore”).</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>ἔδω</i> (édō, “I eat, I consume”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐδηλήσαντο</i> (edēlḗsanto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, or eating</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἔδω</i> (édō), Epic form of <i>ἐσθίω</i> (esthíō, “eat”), +&lrm; <i>-τύς</i> (-tús, suffix for noun of action).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>food, meat, victuals</li><li>fodder for cattle</li><li>bait for fish</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἔδω</i> (édō, “to eat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>edible, eatable, comestible</li><li>prepared for eating, cooked</li></ol><p><i>ἐδωδή</i> (edōdḗ, “food, victuals”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos, “-able”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εἴκοσι</i> (eíkosi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εἴκοσι</i> (eíkosi) with movable nu</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εἰκοστός</i> (eikostós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>usually weaker than <i>βούλομαι</i> (boúlomai), of consent rather than desire: (<i>intransitive</i>) to be willing, to wish [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to want, wish</li></ol><li>(<i>with negative</i>) to not care to, be unable</li><li>(<i>of inanimate objects</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>expressing future; to will, shall</li><li>to be naturally disposed; to be wont, accustomed</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁gʷʰel-</i> (“to wish”). Cognates include Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;želěti</i> (“to wish, want”) (whence Old Church Slavonic <i>желѣти</i> (želěti)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to accustom, inure</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be or become accustomed or used to [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔθος</i> (éthos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>volunteer</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐθέλω</i> (ethélō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ethnarch</li><li>a captain of Roman auxiliaries</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos, “tribe, country”) + <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>office of ethnarch</li><li>leader of a small nation or people of a homogeneous ethnicity</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos, “tribe, country, nation”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰρχῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ărkhĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>national</li><li>foreign</li><li>(<i>especially Christianity</i>) heathen, gentile</li><li>tax collector</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔθνος</i> (éthnos, “tribe, country, nation”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of motion</i>) Out of, from</li><li>from, with the source of</li><li>to denote change from one place or condition to another</li><li>to express separation of distinction from a number</li><li>(<i>of position</i>) outside, beyond</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>from, since</li><li>(<i>of particular points of time</i>) just, after</li><li>during, in</li></ol><li>(<i>of origin</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of materials</i>) made out of something</li><li>(<i>of descent, parentage</i>) descended from</li><li>(<i>of causation</i>) done by someone, something</li><li>of the cause, instrument, or means by which a thing is done</li><li>from, according to</li><li>turns a following noun into a periphrastic adverb</li><li>(<i>with numerals</i>) in such an order</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eks</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eǵʰs</i> (“out of”).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>ex</i>, <i>ē</i>, Old Irish <i>ess-</i>, <i>a, ass</i> (Irish <i>as</i>), Lithuanian <i>i̇̀š</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>из</i> (iz), Northern Kurdish <i>ji</i>, Albanian <i>ith</i>.</p><p>The genitive is from the Proto-Indo-European ablative of separation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical) from the bottom of one's heart, (<i>literally "from the depths"</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) by turns, one after another</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>With a sense of removal: out, away, off</li><li>Expressing completion or totality: utterly</li></ol><p>See <i>ἐκ</i> (ek).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw or cast out of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to throw ashore</li><li>to cast out of a place, banish</li><li>to expose on a desert island</li><li>to divorce</li><li>to cast out of one's seat, to depose</li><li>(<i>in wrestling</i>) to throw</li><li>to dig wells</li></ol><li>to strike out of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to strike open, break in</li></ol><li>to let fall</li><li>to throw away, put aside, reject</li><li>to lose, properly by one's own fault</li><li>to produce, bear fruit</li><li>to put out, dislocate</li><li>to put off</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) to produce a line</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go out, depart</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go, step [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘out of/off from’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to disembark, dismount</li><li>to outstep, overstep [<i>with</i> accusative]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come out (<i>a certain way</i>), come to pass, turn out (to be)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be fulfilled (<i>of a prophecy</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go out of due bounds, to go far</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be born (<i>with the father in the genitive or the mother in the dative</i>)</li><li>to come into being</li><li>(<i>in the aorist</i>) to pass (<i>of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be allowed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>γαμίζω</i> (gamízō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>γαμίζω</i> (gamízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to laugh out loud</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to gurgle</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>γελᾰ́ω</i> (gelắō, “laugh”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give up, surrender (<i>especially something seized unlawfully</i>)</li><li>to give (<i>a daughter</i>) in marriage</li><li>to rent out, let out for hire</li><li>to lend (<i>money on security</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive&#32;or&#32;reflexive</i>) to empty (<i>of a river</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>act of avenging, vengeance, revenge</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκδικέω</i> (ekdikéō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to chase away, banish</li><li>to persecute</li></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>διώκω</i> (diṓkō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accession, succession</li><li>expectation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκδέχομαι</i> (ekdékhomai) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take off, remove, strip</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;active or passive in middle sense) to strip oneself (of a thing), take off oneself</li><li>(aorist ἐξέδῡν,&#32;perfect ἐκδέδῡκᾰ)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to get out of, to emerge from</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to escape; to shun</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>δῠ́ω</i> (dŭ́ō, “to get in”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in that way</li><li>in that case</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκεῖνος</i> (ekeînos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs), thus literally, “that-ly”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>demonstrative adverb there, in that place</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in Tragic,&#32;euphemistic), "in Hades", i.e. dead.</li></ol><li>(<i>with verbs of motion</i>) thither</li><li>(<i>rarely</i>) then, at that time</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>ἐκεῖνος</i> (ekeînos). This is the regular distal place demonstrative reflex, whereas <i>ἐκεῖθῐ</i> (ekeîthĭ) is the obsolete extended form.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from that place, thence</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>there</li><li>to that place, thither</li></ol><li>thence, from that place</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) thereafter, next</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκεῖ</i> (ekeî, “yonder”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”). This is the expected distal source demonstrative reflex.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκεῖ</i> (ekeî, “yonder”) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi, “at”). The expected distal place demonstrative reflex is <i>ἐκεῖ</i> (ekeî), while this is the extended form analogous to <i>ἐνταῦθα</i> (entaûtha).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) that</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place, to indicate something farther from the speaker</i>) there</li><li>(<i>of time, to indicate something farther in the past</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>to indicate something previously mentioned</i>)</li><li>(in contrast to <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos) the former)</li></ol><li>(<i>of someone or something famous or infamous</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ékeyenos</i>, from <i>ἐ-</i> (e-, “augment”) (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁é</i>) + <i>&#42;ḱe</i> (“deictic particle”) + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁enos</i> (“that”) (compare <i>ἔνη</i> (énē)). Middle part also in Latin <i>ce-dō</i>, <i>cis</i>, <i>hi-c(e)</i>, last part also in Latin <i>enim</i>, <i>nam</i>, etc. Possibly a cognate of Old Norse <i>hinn</i> (“this”) (from Proto-Norse <i>&#42;ᚺᛁᚾᚨᛉ</i> (&#42;hinaʀ /⁠*hīnaʀ⁠/)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>there (to or into that place), thither</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκεῖ</i> (ekeî, “yonder”) +&lrm; <i>-σε</i> (-se, “towards”). This is the expected distal destination demonstrative reflex.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil out or over; (<i>pathology</i>) to break out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to ferment</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be boiled to a decoction</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out, away, off”) +&lrm; <i>ζέω</i> (zéō, “to boil; to ferment”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>expository</li><li>enunciatory</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔκθεσῐς</i> (ékthesĭs, “exposure, exposition”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spirit, ardour, eagerness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔκθῡμος</i> (ékthūmos, “frantic, spirited”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call out or forth, summon forth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>κᾰλέω</i> (kăléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break off, to tear</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to grow weak, to be enfeebled</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>κλάω</i> (kláō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assembly</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) congregation, church</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔκκλητος</i> (ékklētos, “summoned”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix), from <i>ἐκκαλέω</i> (ekkaléō, “to call forth, summon”), from <i>ἐκ</i> (ek) + <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō). The <i>τ</i> (t) changes to <i>σ</i> (s) by palatalization and assibilation, triggered by the following <i>ι</i> (i).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>convocātio</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for an ecclesia</li><li>ecclesiastic, clerical</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκκλησῐᾰστής</i> (ekklēsĭăstḗs, “member of an ecclesia”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Excrementitious.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to knock out (<i>of someone’s hands etc.</i>)</li><li>to drive back, repulse</li><li>to frustrate</li><li>to hiss (<i>an actor</i>) off the stage</li><li>to put off, adjourn</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>κρούω</i> (kroúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to unhide, make known</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “away, off”) +&lrm; <i>κρύπτω</i> (krúptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a wheeled device used in ancient Greek plays, which could be rolled out to portray the interior of a set</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκκῠκλέω</i> (ekkŭkléō, “I roll out”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine or beam forth; (<i>of a fever</i>) to burst forth violently; (<i>of sound</i>) to be clearly heard</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be distinguished</li></ol><li>to flash forth</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) synonym of <i>δῐαυγᾰ́ζω</i> (dĭaugắzō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out, away, off”) +&lrm; <i>λᾰ́μπω</i> (lắmpō, “to give light, shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pick or single out, to choose</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to pull out one's gray hairs</li></ol><li>to levy taxes or tribute</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to choose”). Exact cognate in form and meaning to Latin <i>ēligō</i> (“to choose”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave out, pass over</li><li>to forsake, desert, abandon</li><li>to leave off, cease</li><li>to fail, be wanting, be inferior</li><li>to fail (someone)</li><li>to die</li><li>to faint</li><li>to depart</li><li>to be left, remain</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) to be eclipsed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “to leave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>selective, choosy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλέγω</i> (eklégō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós), from <i>ἐκ</i> (ek, “out, from”) + <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “choose, count”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>picked out, select</li><li>(in New Testament and ecclesiastical writings,&#32;in the plural) the elect</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλέγω</i> (eklégō, “to pick out”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make very thin, reduce to a fine state</li><li>to refine</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become subtle and light</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>λεπτύ̄νω</i> (leptú̄nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to graze, scrape</li><li>to slide, slip</li></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “ex-”) +&lrm; <i>λῐ́στρον</i> (lĭ́stron, “smoothing tool; spade”) +&lrm; <i>-ά̆ω</i> (-á̆ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>choice, selection</li><li>extract (portion of a document)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλέγω</i> (eklégō) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to compute, calculate</li><li>to consider, reflect on [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> <b>περί</b> (perí, + genitive)]</li><li>to reckon on, count on</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>λογίζομαι</i> (logízomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must be released</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλῡ́ω</i> (eklū́ō, “set free”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, “a suffix forming verbal adjectives of necessity”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>expiatory offering</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing release</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) expiatory offering</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλύω</i> (eklúō, “set free”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριος</i> (-tḗrios, suffix added to verb stems to form adjectives pertaining to the stem)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing release</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) expiatory offering</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλύω</i> (eklúō, “set free”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριος</i> (-tḗrios, suffix added to verb stems to form adjectives pertaining to the stem)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>calculated to weaken</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλῡ́ω</i> (eklū́ō, “dissolve”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, suffix added to verbal stems to form adjectives: relating to, suited to, skilled in, able to)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set free, release on paying a ransom</li><li>to redeem</li><li>to release oneself from guilt by ransom</li><li>to redeem oneself from a wrongdoing before the divine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>λυτρόω</i> (lutróō, “to ransom”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a clear, easy, relaxed way</li><li>feebly, riotously, dissolutely</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔκλυτος</i> (éklutos, “relaxed, loose”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, “-ly”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set free, to release, to be set free, to get one set free</li><li>to unloose, unstring a bow, let one's tongue loose</li><li>to make an end of, to pay something off</li><li>to relax, to be faint, fail</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to relax the bowels</li><li>to pay in full</li><li>to resolve a doubt</li><li>to dissolve</li><li>to break up, depart</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out, away, off”) +&lrm; <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “loosen”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;construed with a cognate accusative) to undertake and complete a public burden</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “thoroughly, completely”) +&lrm; <i>λειτουργέω</i> (leitourgéō, “perform public service”); the <i>ῃ</i> (ēi, eta with iota subscript) spelling echoes the verb’s root in <i>λήϊτον</i> (lḗïton, “town-hall, council-chamber”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorough knowledge</li></ol><p>Analyzed as <i>ἐκμᾰνθᾰ́νω</i> (ekmănthắnō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), or as <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́θησῐς</i> (mắthēsĭs), both from the same roots.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe off, wipe away</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to wipe away one's tears</li><li>to mould or model in wax or plaster</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>μάσσω</i> (mássō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that on or in which an impression is made: also the impression made, an impress, mould</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to achieve by force</li></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>νῑκάω</i> (nīkáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>terribly (so as to cause terror)</li><li>terribly (very, exceedingly)</li></ol><p><i>ἔκπαγλος</i> (ékpaglos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send out, send forth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>πέμπω</i> (pémpō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy utterly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>πέρθω</i> (pérthō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>outspread, flat (of containers and vessels)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out, away, off”) +&lrm; <i>πέταλος</i> (pétalos, “broad, flat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drink out or off of, quaff</li><li>to drain (<i>of insects</i>)</li><li>to drain (a cup) dry</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>πῑ́νω</i> (pī́nō, “to drink”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall out of</li><li>to be thrown out of (functioning as the passive of <i>ἐκβάλλω</i> (ekbállō))</li></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>πίπτω</i> (píptō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carder's press</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκπιέζω</i> (ekpiézō, “to squeeze out”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail out, sail away, weigh anchor</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to go out of one's mind, lose one's mind</li><li>to outsail (<i>ships</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “from”) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “sail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike out of, to drive away from, to expel</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to drive out of one's senses by a sudden shock, to amaze, to astound</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(generally, of any sudden, overpowering passion) to be struck with desire</li><li>to frighten</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out of”) +&lrm; <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike, to smite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἐκπλήσσω</i> (ekplḗssō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>πλήττω</i> (plḗttō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Amazement, terror.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Admiration.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἔκπληξῐς</i> (ékplēxĭs, “consternation”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, nominal suffix), from <i>ἐκπλήσσω</i> (ekplḗssō, “to drive out one's senses by a sudden shock”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>out of the way</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> dative] out of (<i>someone’s</i>) way</li><li>[<i>with</i> genitive] far from</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús), literally “away from the feet”</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a dispatch, an expedition</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκπέμπω</i> (ekpémpō, “to send out”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to invent, contrive</li><li>to provide, furnish</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to procure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>πορίζω</i> (porízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make to go out</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to go out, go forth, march out</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ</i> (ek, “out”) + <i>πορεύω</i> (poreúō, “to carry, go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without reasonable grounds</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκπρεπής</i> (ekprepḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow out or forth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of feathers</i>) to fall off</li><li>to shed fruit</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to melt or fall away, to disappear</li></ol><li>(<i>with cognate accusative</i>) to shed, let fall</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “flow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a flowing out, outflow</li><li>a way for outflow, place of issue</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκρέω</i> (ekréō, “to flow out”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inclined to depart from [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>στατικός</i> (statikós); also analyzable as the root of <i>ἐξίστημι</i> (exístēmi) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a band of men going out on service; the departure of an army to take the field; recruitment of soldiers to wage a campaign; men marching off on a military expedition</li></ol><p>Equivalent to <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out of, from”) +&lrm; <i>στρᾰτείᾱ</i> (străteíā, “campaign, expedition”), from <i>ἐκστρατεύω</i> (ekstrateúō, “to march out; to take the field; wage a campaign”) from <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>στρᾰτεύω</i> (străteúō, “advance with an army, wage war”), from <i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army, band of men”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to march out; go off on an expedition, depart for a campaign</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to organize and launch an expedition or campaign</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to march out (an individual or band of men); order (a soldier or an army) to start marching</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to recruit for and wage a campaign; plan and execute an operation (as part of a battle or war)</li><li>(perfect,&#32;military) to conclude a campaign</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;military) to take the field (for combat operations)</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;military) to be in the field (said of a general or his troops on a battlefield)</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;military) to be engaged in active service; be employed on active duty</li><li>(perfect,&#32;mediopassive voice,&#32;military) to have ended a campaign; be a veteran of a completed expedition</li><li>(perfect,&#32;mediopassive voice,&#32;military,&#32;participle,&#32;substantively) a veteran (who experienced a campaign or expedition to its conclusion)</li></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out of, from”) +&lrm; <i>στρᾰτεύω</i> (străteúō, “to advance with an army; wage war; serve in an army”) from <i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army, band of men”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō, verbal suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἐκτέμνω</i> (ektémnō, “to cut out”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to cut down trees, fell, cut to shape</li><li>to cut away sinews and the like</li></ol><li>to castrate</li><li>to divide, zone</li><li>to be disarmed and deceived</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>properly</i>) extension</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) strenuousness, intentness, perseverance, zeal</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a litany in the Byzantine Rite</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἐκτενής</i> (ektenḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch out</li><li>to prolong, draw out</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to lay outstretched</li><li>to strain, push to the limit</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to lengthen a vowel</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to stretch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fortify completely, to build it from the ground</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “completely”) +&lrm; <i>τειχῐ́ζω</i> (teikhĭ́zō, “wall, fortify”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fortification</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτειχῐ́ζω</i> (ekteikhĭ́zō, “fortify completely”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring to an end, finish</li><li>to accomplish, achieve</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>τελέω</i> (teléō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stretched out, strained, outstretched, extended</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) strenuous, intense, intent, insistent, fervent, assiduous</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) unceasing, persistent</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) impending, instant</li></ol><p><i>ἐκτείνω</i> (ekteínō) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cutting out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>castration</li></ol><li>a segment</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one that is castrated, a eunuch</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτέμνω</i> (ektémnō, “cut out”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās, noun-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἐκτέμνω</i> (ektémnō, “cut out”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτομή</i> (ektomḗ, “excision”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, “a suffix used to form verbs from nouns, adjectives and other verbs”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cutting down</li></ol><p>Peculiar feminine form of ἐκτομεύς (ektomeús). Ultimately from <i>ἐκτέμνω</i> (ektémnō, “cut out”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, feminine suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one that cuts out</li><li>a circumciser</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτέμνω</i> (ektémnō, “cut out”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, “A suffix added to verbal stems to form a masculine agent noun”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>castrated</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτομίας</i> (ektomías, “a eunuch”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective-forming suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn out of the course, turn aside</li><li>to turn off the road, order out of the way</li><li>to avoid</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an everted eyelid</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτρέπω</i> (ektrépō, “to turn inside out”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon), from <i>ἐκ</i> (ek, “out”) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to make blind</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>τυφλόω</i> (tuphlóō, “blind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>without, outside, out</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) external, strange, vulgar</li><li>out</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>out of, far from</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ</i> (ek, “out”), formed on the model of <i>ἐντός</i> (entós, “within, inside”). Cognate with Old Irish <i>acht</i> (“but, provided that”), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐌸𐌸𐌰𐌿</i> (aiþþau, “or, otherwise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>art</i>) relief, figure in relief</li><li>(<i>of light</i>) reflection</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτῠπόω</i> (ektŭpóō, “to model or work in relief”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to carry out</li><li>to bring forth, produce</li><li>to bring out, publish</li><li>to disclose, tell, betray, declare</li></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show forth, bring to light, disclose, reveal, make manifest</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to show oneself, shine forth, come into view</li><li>to exhibit</li><li>to declare (<i>war</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flee from, escape</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>φεύγω</i> (pheúgō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carrying out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>carrying a corpse out in a burial rite.</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>φορά</i> (phorá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to spill</li><li>to utter (of words)</li><li>to squander, waste</li><li>to throw down</li><li>to shed</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to pour out, stream forth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be cast away, to be forgotten</li><li>to give oneself up to emotion, to be overjoyed</li><li>to lie languidly</li><li>to extend</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to extract juice from</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;of the small veins) to shed the blood and leave it extravasated under the skin</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>χυμός</i> (khumós, “juice”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bruise, ecchymosis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκχῡμόω</i> (ekkhūmóō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bruise, ecchymosis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκχῡμόω</i> (ekkhūmóō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἐλαίᾱ</i> (elaíā, “olive tree”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>young olive tree</li><li>a little oil</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλᾱ́ᾱ</i> (elā́ā) +&lrm; <i>-διον</i> (-dion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fir (tree of the genus <i>Abies</i>)</li><li>oar, rudder</li></ol><p>No certain etymology. Maybe from the same source of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;linþaz</i> (“pliable”) and Latin <i>lentus</i> (“flexible”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or made of fir</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλάτη</i> (elátē, “fir”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἐλάσσων</i> (elássōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>making smaller or less, lessening</li><li>depreciation, disparagement</li><li>defeat</li><li>fault, defect</li><li>loss (<i>of health or property</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλαττόω</i> (elattóō), Attic variant of <i>ἐλασσόω</i> (elassóō, “to make smaller, lessen”), + <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of deer</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔλαφος</i> (élaphos, “deer”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;poetic) alternative form of <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō)</li></ol><p>Disputed. According to the LIV, perhaps a back-formation from <i>ἤλασα</i> (ḗlasa), the aorist of <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō). However, Sihler suggests that it may be interpreted as a ye-present to a Proto-Indo-European root of the shape <i>&#42;elh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of olive tree wood</li><li>made of oil</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλαία</i> (elaía, “olive tree”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to disgrace, put to shame</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cross-examine, question</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to test, bring to the proof</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁lengʰ-</i> (“to accuse, to scold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephant</li><li>(<i>masculine</i>) ivory</li></ol><p>Earlier form: Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀩𐀞</i> (e-re-pa /⁠elephās⁠/). Probably borrowed from Egyptian <i>ꜣbw</i> <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./15cb8bb5.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Ab" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f3ad62fc.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="b" alt="glyph" height="87"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./03cfc09a.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="E26" alt="glyph" height="72"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table> (reconstructed as earlier /ˈʀuːbaw/, later /ˈjuːbə/).. Cognate with Latin <i>ebur</i> (“ivory”) and Sanskrit <i>इभ</i> (íbha). For the first half compare Hittite <i>𒆷𒄴𒉺𒀸</i> (laḫpaš, “ivory”), Proto-Berber <i>&#42;eləw</i>, Hebrew <i>פיל</i>, possibly also Ancient Greek <i>ἔλαφος</i> (élaphos, “deer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: kind of grain similar to millet</li></ol><p>Furnée connects this word to <i>ἔλυμος</i> (élumos, “millet”); however, this is unclear. Beekes prefers a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix <i>-αρ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive tree</li><li>olive (fruit)</li><li>naevus</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;elaíwā</i> (whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀨𐀷</i> (e-ra-wa)). Probably a borrowing from a Mediterranean Pre-Greek source, which may also be the source of Old Armenian <i>եւղ</i> (ewł, “oil”), possibly from Proto-Berber <i>&#42;wlw</i> (“wild olive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἐλαίᾱ</i> (elaíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish preserved in oil</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔλαιον</i> (élaion, “oil”) +&lrm; <i>γάρος</i> (gáros, “garum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dog's mercury (<i>Mercurialis perennis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλαία</i> (elaía, “olive tree”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make less or smaller, to diminish, reduce in amount</li><li>(<i>in early writers</i>) to lower, degrade</li><li>to cut down, shorten</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be lessened, depreciated, to suffer loss</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to have the worst of it, to be inferior</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be at disadvantage with a person</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλάσσων</i> (elássōn, “smaller, less”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐλατός</i> (elatós, “ductile”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>driver, especially of horses, charioteer</li><li>rower, oarsman</li><li>one that drives away</li><li>kind of broad, flat cake</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō, “to drive, set in motion”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>squirting cucumber (<i>Ecballium elaterium</i>)</li><li>the drug prepared from the squirting cucumber</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἐλατήριος</i> (elatḗrios, “driving away”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cancerwort (<i>Kickxia spuria</i>)</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>ἐλάτινος</i> (elátinos, “of fir”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ductile</li><li>beaten (<i>of metal</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁l̥h₂-tós</i>, verbal adjective of <i>&#42;h₁elh₂-</i> (“to drive somewhere”). By surface analysis, <i>ἐλα(ύνω)</i> (ela(únō)) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p><p>Compare typologically Russian <i>ко́вкий</i> (kóvkij) (<~ <i>кова́ть</i> (kovátʹ)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>light; unheavy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eləpʰrós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ln̥gʷʰrós</i> (“lightweight”). See also <i>ἐλᾰχῠ́ς</i> (elăkhŭ́s, “small, short”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsnip (<i>Pastinaca sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔλαφος</i> (élaphos, “deer”) +&lrm; <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “to eat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>small, short, mean, little</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eləkʰús</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁léngʰus</i> (“lightweight”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>लघु</i> (laghu), Latin <i>levis</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>льгъкъ</i> (lĭgŭkŭ), Old English <i>lēoht</i> (English <i>light</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive, set in motion</li><li>(<i>of going in conveyances</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to ride a horse, drive a chariot, sail a ship</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go, ride, sail</li></ol><li>to drive away</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to persecute, plague</li></ol><li>to strike with a weapon, hit one thing against another</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to beat out metal, forge</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁elh₂-</i> (“to drive somewhere”), albeit with no certain cognates. Possible cognates include Latin <i>alacer</i> (“lively, active, brisk”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;alaną</i> (“to spurn, drive”), and <i>&#42;lanō</i> (“lane”)); Bengali <i>ইলিশ</i> (iliś, “hilsa fish”) was traditionally connected and interpreted as a "go-lord", but this is generally considered folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have pity on, take pity on, show mercy to</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔλεος</i> (éleos, “mercy”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>merciful</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεέω</i> (eleéō) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to feel pity for, have pity on someone [<i>with</i> participle <small>‘because they...’</small>]</li></ol><p>Like <i>ἐλεέω</i> (eleéō), from <i>ἔλεος</i> (éleos, “pity”). Compare <i>ἐχθαίρω</i> (ekhthaírō, “to hate”) from <i>ἐχθρός</i> (ekhthrós, “hated”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elegy</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of <i>ἐλεγεία ᾠδή</i> (elegeía ōidḗ, “an elegiac song”), from <i>ἐλεγεῖος</i> (elegeîos, “elegiac”), from <i>ἔλεγος</i> (élegos, “poem or song of lament”), perhaps from Phrygian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>elegiac</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεγείᾱ</i> (elegeíā) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a short elegy</li></ol><p><i>ἐλεγεία</i> (elegeía) +&lrm; <i>-άριον</i> (-árion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pity, mercy</li><li>alms, charity</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεήμων</i> (eleḗmōn, “pitiful, merciful”) +&lrm; <i>-σῠ́νη</i> (-sŭ́nē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whirl around, turn around</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to move in coils</li></ol><li>(Homeric,&#32;of an army) to cause to turn, to rally</li><li>to cause to vibrate</li><li>to cry <i>ἐλελεῦ</i> (eleleû), to raise a loud cry</li></ol><p>May be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;leyg-</i> (“jump, whirl”), with cognates including Sanskrit <i>रेजति</i> (rejati), Lithuanian <i>láigyti</i>, Gothic <i>𐌻𐌰𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽</i> (laikan), Old English <i>lāc</i>, and Albanian <i>ling</i>. Could also be from a &#42;ἐϝελ- (&#42;ewel-) / &#42;ϝεϝελ- (&#42;wewel-). Beekes suggests it might be both.</p><p>From <i>ἐλελεῦ</i> (eleleû, “cry of pain, war cry”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greek sage, <i>Salvia triloba</i></li></ol><p><i>ἐλελίζω</i> (elelízō) +&lrm; <i>σφάκος</i> (sphákos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>a cry of pain or lament, woe, alas</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom, liberty</li><li>manumission</li><li>license</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεύθερος</i> (eleútheros, “free”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἐλευθερῐ́α</i> (eleutherĭ́a)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liberality</li></ol><p><i>ἐλευθέριος</i> (eleuthérios) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freedom of speech, free speech</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλευθερόστομος</i> (eleutheróstomos, “free-spoken”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liberator</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλευθερόω</i> (eleutheróō, “to liberate, free”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free-spoken</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεύθερος</i> (eleútheros, “free”) +&lrm; <i>στόμα</i> (stóma, “mouth”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>noble-spirited</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεύθερος</i> (eleútheros, “free”) +&lrm; <i>ψῡχή</i> (psūkhḗ, “spirit, soul”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to free, set free, loose, release [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to free from blame, acquit</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεύθερος</i> (eleútheros) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ivory (attributive); made of ivory</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλέφας</i> (eléphas, “elephant”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos, “-ine”).</p><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀩𐀞𐀳𐀃</i> (e-re-pa-te-o), <i>𐀁𐀩𐀞𐀳𐀍</i> (e-re-pa-te-jo, “made of ivory”) formed with <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephantarch; commander of a squadron of sixteen elephants</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλέφᾱς</i> (eléphās, “elephant”) + <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephantiasis (disease)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεφᾰντῐᾰ́ω</i> (elephăntĭắō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battle of elephants</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλέφας</i> (eléphas, “elephant”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́χη</i> (mắkhē, “fight”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kitchen table</li><li>dresser</li><li>kind of owl</li></ol><p>Technical word without etymology.</p><p>Unexplained but possibly onomatopoeic. Compare Latin <i>ulula</i> (“tawny owl”), <i>ἐλελεῦ</i> (eleleû, “cry of pain”) and <i>ὀλολύζω</i> (ololúzō, “to cry aloud”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>free</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) freedom</li><li>fit for a freeman</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eléutʰeros</i>, adjectival formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁lewdʰ-</i> (“to grow up; people, tribe”); the original meaning of the word was "belonging to the tribe". Cognates include Latin <i>līber</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>людинъ</i> (ljudinŭ, “free man”), and Old English <i>leōd</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐλεύθερος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Eleutherus, the modern Nahr al-Kabir which flows in Syria and Lebanon</li><li>river Eleutherus, the modern Eleuterio which flows in Sicily</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεύθερος</i> (eleútheros, “free”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come, go</li></ol><p>A causal active verb related to <i>ἐλεύσομαι</i> (eleúsomai), the suppletive future stem of <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “to go, come”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to surpass, leave behind</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to leave out, leave undone, omit</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall short, fail</li><li>to be in want of, fall short of, lack [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to be inferior [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘to’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “to leave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>defective</li><li>concise, summary, brief</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλλείπω</i> (elleípō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fawn, young deer</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁el-nó-s</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁el-</i> (“deer”). Compare <i>ἔλαφος</i> (élaphos, “red deer”), Lithuanian <i>elnias</i> (“deer, stag, hart”), Russian <i>оле́нь</i> (olénʹ, “deer”), Russian <i>лось</i> (losʹ, “elk”), Old Armenian <i>եղն</i> (ełn, “hind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lamp-wick, wick</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) surgical bandage</li><li>lampwick plant (<i>Phlomis lychnitis</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἐλλύχνιος</i> (ellúkhnios, “produced for lamp-wicks”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hope for, to look for, expect</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of evils</i>) to look for, fear</li><li>(<i>with present infinitive</i>) to think, deem, suppose, believe</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to hope in</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐλπίς</i> (elpís, “hope”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō). Synchronically also analyzable as <i>ἐλπίς</i> (elpís) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hope, expectation, belief that something will happen</li><li>object of hope</li><li>anxiety; boding</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welp-</i> (“to hope, expect”), like <i>ἔλπω</i> (élpō, “to cause to hope”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐλπίς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Elpis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλπῐ́ς</i> (elpĭ́s, “hope”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἐλπίς</i> (elpís)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greater celandine (<i>Chelidonium majus</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. It has been connected with <i>ἕλος</i> (hélos, “marsh”), but Beekes points out that this has spiritus asper.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>δοκοί ὀροφῆναι</i> (dokoí orophênai, literally “beams of the roof”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐλύοντο</i> (elúonto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive trees which are not sacred, in contrast with the ἐλαῖαι μορίαι (elaîai moríai)</li></ol><p>Literally “olive-trees [planted] in rows one behind another”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐμέ</i> (emé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) used before the labial consonants <i>β, μ, π, φ, ψ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to vomit, throw up, be sick</li></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ϝεμέω</i> (&#42;weméō), secondarily thematized from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wémh₁ti</i>, from <i>&#42;wemh₁-</i> (“to vomit”). Cognate with Latin <i>vomō</i> and Sanskrit <i>वमति</i> (vamati).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>myself</li></ol><p><i>ἐμέο</i> (eméo, “me”, genitive singular) +&lrm; <i>αὐτοῦ</i> (autoû, “self”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw in, cast in</li><li>to put into (<i>someone's hands</i>)</li><li>to bring (<i>to a place</i>)</li><li>to infuse with, inspire with</li><li>to apply oneself to</li></ol><p><i>ἐμ-</i> (em-, “in, into”) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) bathtub</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμβᾰτός</i> (embătós, “passable, accessible”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a half-boot of felt</li><li>synonym of <i>κόθορνος</i> (kóthornos)</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) a modulus (unit of measurement)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμβαίνω</i> (embaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>discharged object, such as a missile or javelin</li><li>interlude, episode</li><li>small net used to fill a gap</li><li>insertion, graft</li><li>shoot for lading corn</li></ol><p><i>ἔμβολος</i> (émbolos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) course of emetics</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>ἐμετῐκός</i> (emetĭkós, “emetic”, adjective). Cognate with Latin <i>emetica</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>provoking sickness</li><li>inclined to vomit</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔμετος</i> (émetos, “vomiting”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἐμαυτοῦ</i> (emautoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to abide in a place</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to abide by, stand by, cleave to, be true to</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to remain fixed, stand fast, hold good</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) + <i>μένω</i> (ménō, “to remain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in eager haste, eager</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) +&lrm; <i>μεμᾰώς</i> (memăṓs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) unceasingly, continuously</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>μένω</i> (ménō, “to stay, wait”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>disposed to abide</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμμενετός</i> (emmenetós, “endurable”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós) from <i>ἐμμένω</i> (emménō, “to abide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be stood by, endurable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμμένω</i> (emménō, “to abide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to busy oneself about, take heed of, care for</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physical affection</li><li>passion</li><li>partiality</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμπαθής</i> (empathḗs, “emotional”) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to stick, inflict, plant</li><li>to make compact, freeze</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;passive voice)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be stuck, stay fixed</li><li>to be compact, be frozen</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) +&lrm; <i>πήγνῡμι</i> (pḗgnūmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fill full</li><li>to fulfill</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>πίμπλημι</i> (pímplēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kindle, burn, set on fire</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be on fire</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>πίμπρημι</i> (pímprēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fall into, fall upon</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to charge</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>πίπτω</i> (píptō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mosquito, gnat</li><li>larva of the oestrus</li></ol><p>Popularly derived from <i>ἐμπίνω</i> (empínō, “to drink (blood)”). Pokorny connects Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;imbī</i> (“bee”), and in theory both can be derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁empí-</i> (“gnat, biting insect”), though Kroonen rejects this comparison and Mallory and Adams call it “not universally accepted.” On the other hand, the relationship could be regarded as evidence of a widespread European substrate word, especially in view of Latin <i>apis</i> (“bee”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a person</i>) to whom authority may be entrusted, worthy of one’s confidence, trustworthy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>experience; mere experience or practice without knowledge, especially in medicine; empiricism</li></ol><p>Abstract noun from <i>ἔμπειρος</i> (émpeiros, “experienced”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medical empiricism (the doctrine of the Empiric school of physicians)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμπειρῐκός</i> (empeirĭkós): as a noun, a substantivisation of its feminine forms in elliptical use for <i>ἡ ἐμπειρικὴ αἵρεσις</i> (hē empeirikḕ haíresis, “the system of philosophic principles based on experience”); as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Empiric school of physicians</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμπειρῐκός</i> (empeirĭkós): as a noun, a substantivisation of its masculine plural forms in elliptical use for <i>οἱ ἰατροὶ ἐμπειρικοί</i> (hoi iatroì empeirikoí, “the physicians who heal based of their experience”); as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>experienced</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμπειρία</i> (empeiría, “experience”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós), from <i>ἔμπειρος</i> (émpeiros, “skilled”), from <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) + <i>πεῖρα</i> (peîra, “a trial, experiment, attempt”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἔμπειρος</i> (émpeiros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plait or weave in, entwine</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;figuratively) to be entangled in, be involved with</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>πλέκω</i> (plékō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>braiding</li><li>scuffle</li><li>interweaving, entanglement</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμπλέκω</i> (emplékō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow or breathe upon [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to breathe, live, be alive</li><li>to breathe of [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to blow into, swell (<i>a sail</i>)</li><li>to breathe into, inspire</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fetter, bind the feet</li><li>to impede, hinder, restrain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) +&lrm; <i>ποδίζω</i> (podízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the way, underfoot</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús), literally “on the feet”</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put into, introduce into</li></ol><p><i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get by barter or traffic</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to deal or traffic in</li><li>to purchase, buy</li><li>to make profit of</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἐμπολή</i> (empolḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merchandise, wares</li><li>traffic, purchase</li><li>gain made by traffic, profit</li><li>trade</li></ol><p>A nominal formation from an unattested <i>&#42;ἐμπέλω</i> (&#42;empélō), from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) + an element <i>&#42;πελ-</i> (&#42;pel-) which may be a blend of the roots underlying <i>πέλομαι</i> (pélomai, “to turn, move”) and <i>πωλέω</i> (pōléō, “to sell”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trade, commerce</li><li>a business</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔμπορος</i> (émporos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>efficacious</li></ol><p>From or related to <i>ἔμπρακτος</i> (émpraktos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>burning, ignition</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) inflammation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμπίμπρημι</i> (empímprēmi, “ignite, burn”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to suppurate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>πύον</i> (púon, “discharge from a sore”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fire, especially deliberate</li><li>burning of weeds</li><li>ergot, kind of parasitic fungus</li><li>incensation, burning of perfumes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμπυρίζω</i> (empurízō, “to burn”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>at one's feet, coming in the way, meeting</li><li>in the way, impeding</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trading station, mart, factory, emporium</li><li>market centre for a district without a city</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔμπορος</i> (émporos, “merchant”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) Gathering or abscess, especially internal, of the kidneys or the chest.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμπυέω</i> (empuéō, “to suppurate”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>live coal covered with ashes, so as to allow of the fire being rekindled</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμπῠρεύω</i> (empŭreúō, “to set aglow, kindle”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) pertaining to burnt sacrifices, heavenly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔμπυρα</i> (émpura, “burnt sacrifices”) (from <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”)) + <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, relational suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exposure</li><li>indication</li><li>exhibition, production (in court)</li><li>proof, demonstration</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφανίζω</i> (emphanízō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to let be seen in a mirror, reflect</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to exhibit, display</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become visible</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;intransitive) to appear</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;intransitive) to light up</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>showing in itself, reflecting</li><li>visible to the eye, manifest</li><li>manifest, palpable</li><li>well-known, conspicuous, notable</li><li>open, obvious</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφαίνω</i> (emphaínō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make manifest, show forth, exhibit</li><li>to make clear or plain</li><li>to declare, explain, give orders, report</li><li>to lay an information against</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφανής</i> (emphanḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forcible, expressive</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔμφασις</i> (émphasis) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blow in, to breathe into</li><li>to breathe upon</li><li>to blow up, to inflate</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;generally) to be swollen</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in, into”) +&lrm; <i>φῡσάω</i> (phūsáō, “to blow, to puff”), from <i>φῦσᾰ</i> (phûsă, “pair of bellows”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Ancient Rome,&#32;law,&#32;relational) of or pertaining to an emphyteuma (type of hereditary leasehold in Roman law granted for the purpose of cultivation)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφύτευσις</i> (emphúteusis) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ancient Rome,&#32;law) individual subject to an emphyteutic lease; individual given responsible over an emphyteuma (type of hereditary leasehold in Roman law granted for the purpose of cultivation)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφύτευμα</i> (emphúteuma) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implant, graft</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;Ancient Rome,&#32;law) to be granted tenure of an ἐμφύτευμα (emphúteuma) (type of hereditary leasehold in Roman law)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) +&lrm; <i>φυτεύω</i> (phuteúō, to plant)..</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) inflation of the stomach</li><li>swelling of the eyes or of the knees</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφῡσᾰ́ω</i> (emphūsắō, “I inflate”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ancient Rome,&#32;law) type of hereditary leasehold in Roman law granted for the purpose of cultivation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφυτεύω</i> (emphuteúō, to implant, graft) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Ancient Rome,&#32;law) tenure of an ἐμφύτευμα (emphúteuma), a type of hereditary leasehold in Roman law</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφυτεύω</i> (emphuteúō, “to implant, engraft”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to implant, graft; fix firmly, securely, deeply</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to grow in or on</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;with dative) to hang on to, cling closely; be rooted in</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) +&lrm; <i>φύω</i> (phúō, to bring forth, beget).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to animate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) +&lrm; <i>ψῡχή</i> (psūkhḗ) + <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἐμαυτοῦ</i> (emautoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>modifying noun</i>) my, of mine</li><li>(substantive,&#32;on its own,&#32;often&#32;with article) mine</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) my friends or family; my possessions</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁mós</i>; compare <i>ἐμέ</i> (emé, “me”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>իմ</i> (im).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>freshwater tortoise (<i>Emys orbicularis</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Sommer derives it from <i>ἐμέω</i> (eméō, “to vomit”), because the animal, when breathing out below the surface of water, continuously releases air bubbles. However, Beekes states that <i>-ud-</i> is not an Indo-European suffix, concluding that the word is probably Pre-Greek. The variation <i>ἀ-/ἐ-</i> is also proof of substrate origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>location</i>) (<i>with dative</i>) in; on; at; (<i>with dative plural</i>) among</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(elliptical,&#32;with genitive) in the house or the land of</li><li>surrounded by; wearing</li></ol><li>(<i>time</i>) in; at; or during the time of</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁én</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁én</i> (“in”), cognate with Latin <i>in</i>, Old Armenian <i>ի</i> (i), Albanian <i>një</i>, English <i>in</i>.</p><p>The dative is from the Proto-Indo-European locative; the genitive is an innovated Greek associative; the accusative is from the pre-Proto-Indo-European directional.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>prevocalic form of <i>ἐν δέ</i> (en dé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>and therein</li><li>and among them</li><li>(<i>not in Attic prose</i>) and besides, moreover</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>sneakily, on the sly, in a hole-and-corner</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) alternative form of <i>ἐντούτοις</i> (entoútois)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>in-</li><li>Appears in medial&#47;proximal place&#47;source&#47;destination demostratives</li></ol><p>Prefixed form of the preposition <i>ἐν</i> (en).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ím</i>, accusative case of <i>&#42;éy</i> (“that”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead in or on</li><li>to urge on, promote</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in, on or of the sea, marine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ἅλιος</i> (hálios, from <i>ἅλς</i> (háls) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clarity</li><li>vividness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐναργής</i> (enargḗs, “visible, palpable, manifest, clear”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun&ndash;forming suffix); ultimately derived from <i>ἀργός</i> (argós, “bright”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>virtuous</li></ol><p><i>ἐν</i> (en) +&lrm; <i>ἀρετή</i> (aretḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin</li><li>to engage in</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to begin to speak</li><li>to make a beginning of</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>ἄρχομαι</i> (árkhomai, “to begin, lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brother’s wife</li><li>the wife of husband’s brother: sister-in-law, co-sister-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁yénh₂tēr</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>janitrīcēs</i>, Sanskrit <i>यातृ</i> (yātṛ), Old Armenian <i>ներ</i> (ner) and Old Church Slavonic <i>ꙗтрꙑ</i> (jatry).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) tell or tell about</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) speak</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“say”). Compare Latin <i>inquam</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>activity, operation, vigour, efficiency</li><li>workmanship</li><li>godly action, cosmic force</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the active voice</li><li>the active principle in Aristotelian ontology (Latin <i>actus</i>)</li><li>actuality (Aristotelian philosophy)</li></ol><p>Etymology treeAncient Greek <i>ἐνεργός</i> (energós)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ēs</i>Ancient Greek <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)Ancient Greek <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)Ancient Greek <i>ἐνεργής</i> (energḗs)Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-ih₂</i>der.Ancient Greek <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă)Ancient Greek <i>ἐνέργεια</i></p><p>From <i>ἐνεργής</i> (energḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă); for the former component, see <i>ἐνεργός</i> (energós, “at work; active”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold or keep fast within, to harbor (e.g. a grudge)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to enter in, pierce</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>poetic form of <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send in, send into</li><li>to put in, implant, inspire</li><li>to plunge into</li><li>to throw in, throw upon</li><li>to launch (<i>ship</i>)</li><li>to incite</li><li>to send into the assembly, employ</li><li>to inject (<i>poison</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to press on</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sometimes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) to chide, rebuke, upbraid</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>ἰάπτω</i> (iáptō) and/or <i>ἴπτομαι</i> (íptomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin</li><li>to be</li><li>to be upon</li><li>to be present</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, slay (<i>especially in battle</i>)</li><li>to destroy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔναρα</i> (énara) with a *<i>y</i> progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>ominously</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ominous, boding, fateful</li><li>seasonable, opportune</li><li>righteous</li><li>fit, proper, becoming</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>αἴσιμος</i> (aísimos, “destined”), itself from <i>αἶσα</i> (aîsa, “fate, destiny”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ominous, boding, fateful</li><li>seasonable, opportune</li><li>righteous</li><li>fit, proper, becoming</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>αἴσιμος</i> (aísimos, “destined”), itself from <i>αἶσα</i> (aîsa, “fate, destiny”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be greatly afflicted</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ἀδημονέω</i> (adēmonéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine hundred</li></ol><p><i>ἐννέα</i> (ennéa) +&lrm; <i>-ακόσιοι</i> (-akósioi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) like, resembling (<i>with dative</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλίγκιος</i> (alínkios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on man's nature, become human</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in, into”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos, “human”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>opposite</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>opposite, facing, visible</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with verbs of motion</i>) in the opposite direction</li></ol><li>in a hostile sense, opposing, facing in a fight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>generally</i>) opposing</li></ol><li>(<i>in Attic and prose</i>) the opposite, contrary, reverse</li><li>(<i>in Aristotle's Logic</i>) contrariety</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ́ος</i> (ăntĭ́os, “opposite, against”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in hostile opposition</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>set against, hostile to</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔναντι</i> (énanti) +&lrm; <i>βίος</i> (bíos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>opposite, facing</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>opposite, facing, visible</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with verbs of motion</i>) in the opposite direction</li></ol><li>in a hostile sense, opposing, facing in a fight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>generally</i>) opposing</li></ol><li>(<i>in Attic and prose</i>) the opposite, contrary, reverse</li><li>(<i>in Aristotle's Logic</i>) contrariety</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ντῐ́ος</i> (ăntĭ́os, “opposite, against”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set oneself against, oppose, withstand</li><li>to refuse</li><li>to contradict, gainsay</li><li>to be adverse (<i>of the wind</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐναντίος</i> (enantíos) +&lrm; <i>-όομαι</i> (-óomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to acquit</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>ἀπολῡ́ω</i> (apolū́ō, “loose from”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἐνηρόσιον</i> (enērósion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strip of armor, despoil</li><li>to kill, slay (<i>especially in battle</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἔναρα</i> (énara) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>visible</li><li>of bodily shape</li><li>manifest to the mind</li><li>(<i>of words</i>) clear, distinct</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>&#42;ἄργος</i> (&#42;árgos, “brightness”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs). <i>&#42;ἄργος</i> (&#42;árgos) is the neuter σ-stem abstract noun formed to the root of <i>ἀργός</i> (argós, “shining”), which is also contained in <i>ἀργεστής</i> (argestḗs) and <i>ἀργεννός</i> (argennós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>do as in Athens, act in the Attic way</li><li>to speak, sing in the Attic fashion, style</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ἀττικίζω</i> (attikízō), equivalent to <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>Ἀττικός</i> (Attikós) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>left-to-right</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rightward, left-to-right</li><li>propitious</li><li>on the right</li><li>clever, expert</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>δεξιός</i> (dexiós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind, fast, pack</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to entangle</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to secure</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be possessed by a malevolent spirit</li><li>to fall short</li><li>to want</li><li>to lack</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be deficient</li></ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) it is a deficiency</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to bind”).</p><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to lack”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lacking, deficient</li><li>inadequate, insufficient</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) defective</li></ol><p><i>ἐνδέω</i> (endéō, “to be lacking”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark, point out</li><li>to display, showcase, demonstrate, exhibit, shew</li><li>to give proof, prove</li><li>(law,&#32;Attic) to inform against</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to show what is one's own, declare oneself</li><li>to manifest</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>δείκνῡμι</i> (deíknūmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>continuous</li></ol><p>From ἐνδελεχέω (endelekhéō). Possibly related to latin Latin <i>indulgeō</i>, see there for more information.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἔνδημος</i> (éndēmos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dwelling-place</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to loosen, disperse a clot of blood</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>διαλῡ́ω</i> (dialū́ō, “dissolve”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born in the house</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔνδον</i> (éndon, “within”) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs). Compare with Latin <i>indigenus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in doubt, be at a loss</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be a matter of doubt</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν δοιῇ</i> (en doiēî, “in doubt”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>kindly</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) plus the zero grade of the root of <i>δεῦκος</i> (deûkos, “sweet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐνδύω</i> (endúō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in, on”) +&lrm; <i>δύνω</i> (dúnō, “to get into, put on (clothes)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to go into</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of clothes</i>) to put on, wear</li><li>to enter</li><li>to sink in</li></ol><li>(<i>active as causal</i>) to clothe</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) + <i>δύω</i> (dúō, “to enter”). Compare Latin <i>induo</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>ninety</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁néwn̥dḱomt</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁néwn̥</i> (whence also <i>ἐννέα</i> (ennéa, “nine”)) + <i>&#42;déḱm̥t</i> (whence also <i>δέκα</i> (déka, “ten”). Cognate with Latin <i>nōnāgintā</i>, Old Armenian <i>իննսուն</i> (innsun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ninetieth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;enewnēkontstós</i>, equivalent to <i>ἐνενήκοντα</i> (enenḗkonta) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in action or activity, to operate</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to effect, execute</li><li>(<i>euphemistically</i>) to have sex</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνεργός</i> (energós, “active, effective”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐνέργειᾰ</i> (enérgeiă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Able to act on; acting on</li><li>Active</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) In the active voice (<i>of verbs, nouns, inflectional categories, or wording</i>)</li><li>Efficacious, effective, stimulating</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνεργέω</i> (energéō, “to effect”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>at work, active</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dumb, mute, speechless</li><li>senseless, stupid</li><li>useless (of things)</li><li>astonished, dumbfounded</li></ol><p>The form is similar to <i>κενεός</i> (keneós), but has no clear etymology. Brugmann connects the word with <i>εὖνις</i> (eûnis), but this claim is refused by Kretschmer. Furnée compares <i>νενός</i> (nenós, “meek, silly”) and further <i>νενίηλος</i> (neníēlos), hinting at a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedclothes, covering used on a bed</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>ἐνεύναιος</i> (eneúnaios, “on which one sleeps”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>struck by a lightning bolt</li><li>(in the neuter plural,&#32;substantive) places set apart from worldly use and dedicated to a god, because they have been struck by a lightning bolt (compare Latin <i>bidental</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) +&lrm; <i>Ἠλύσιον</i> (Ēlúsion, “Elysium”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>rent for corn-land</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>right of tillage</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ἄροσις</i> (árosis) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐνθάδε</i> (entháde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>hither, here</li><li>thither, there</li><li>in this case, in this condition</li><li>to this point, at this stage</li><li>now</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “where”) +&lrm; <i>-θα</i> (-tha, “towards”) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de, “here”). This is the expected proximal destination demonstrative reflex, while the proximal place one would have been <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de). The solely proximal destination one is <i>δεῦρο</i> (deûro).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from this place, hence</li><li>from this time, henceforth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “where”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de, “here”). This is the expected proximal source demonstrative reflex.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>store</li><li>capital</li><li>insertion</li><li>enclosure</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, chest”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἐνταῦθᾰ</i> (entaûthă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit for implanting</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔνθεσις</i> (énthesis, “putting in, insertion”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἐντεῦθεν</i> (enteûthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be inspired or possessed by a god, to be rapt, to be in ecstasy</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to inspire</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔνθους</i> (énthous), later contracted form of <i>ἔνθεος</i> (éntheos, “possessed by a god”), +&lrm; <i>-ιάζω</i> (-iázō, denominative verb suffix), influenced by <i>οὐσία</i> (ousía, “essence”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inspiration, enthusiasm</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνθουσιάζω</i> (enthousiázō) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an enthusiast, a zealot</li></ol><p><i>ἐνθουσῐᾰσμός</i> (enthousĭăsmós) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>enthroned</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>θρόνος</i> (thrónos, “throne”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay to heart, consider well, reflect on, ponder</li><li>to take to heart, be hurt or angry at</li><li>to think out, form a plan</li><li>to infer, conclude</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós) +&lrm; <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scruple, misgiving</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνθῡ́μιος</i> (enthū́mios, “taken to heart”) from <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) + <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anniversary</li><li>year</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the etymology is unknown, and he criticizes some of the proposals to date, including Meillet's</p><p>that the first part is <i>ἔνος</i> (énos, “year”), as in <i>δίενος</i> (díenos, “two years old”), and the second one is from <i>ἰαύω</i> (iaúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rebuke; reproof; reproach</li><li>menace; threat</li><li>abuse; contumely</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνῑ́πτω</i> (enī́ptō) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ennéwə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁néwn̥</i>. Cognates include Old Armenian <i>ինն</i> (inn), Sanskrit <i>नव</i> (náva), Latin <i>novem</i>, and Old English <i>nigon</i> (English <i>nine</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic, Doric, and Tragic form of <i>ἐνέπω</i> (enépō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>handle of the oar</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἐνενήκοντᾰ</i> (enenḗkontă, “ninety”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>nineteen</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consider, reflect on</li><li>to understand</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in-”) +&lrm; <i>νοέω</i> (noéō, “to perceive, think, deem”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐννοσίγαιε</i> (ennosígaie)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) earthshaking (<i>epithet of Poseidon</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔνοσις</i> (énosis, “shaking, quake”) +&lrm; <i>γαῖα</i> (gaîa, “earth”); the double <i>νν</i> (nn) serves to make the first syllable heavy, as the oldest form was <i>&#42;ἐνϝοσίγαιος</i> (&#42;enwosígaios) with the first syllable closed.</p><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀚𐀯𐀅𐀃𐀚</i> (e-ne-si-da-o-ne).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) for nine days, nine days long</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐννέα</i> (ennéa, “nine”) +&lrm; <i>ἦμαρ</i> (êmar, “day”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dwell in, inhabit (<i>with dative or accusative</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>οἰκέω</i> (oikéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crying, shouting, as of birds</li><li>war cry, battle cry, wild cry</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) voice</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) sound</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνέπω</i> (enépō, “to tell”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earthshaking (<i>epithet of Poseidon</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔνοσις</i> (énosis, “shaking, quake”) +&lrm; <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “earth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chamber pot</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>οὐρήθρᾱ</i> (ourḗthrā, “urethra”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obligation, liability, responsibility</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνέχω</i> (enékhō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to trouble, annoy <i>(with dative or accusative)</i></li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ὄχλος</i> (ókhlos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>completeness, plenitude</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐντελής</i> (entelḗs, “complete, full”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐντέλεια)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Enteleia, Aravene, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, command to be done, enjoin</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>τέλλω</i> (téllō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of guts</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔντερον</i> (énteron, “guts, entrails, bowel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>εἰσί</i> (eisí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put in, implant, insert</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch tight</li><li>(<i>archery</i>) to string (<i>a bow</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>τανύω</i> (tanúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐνταῦθα</i> (entaûtha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>here, there</li><li>hither, thither</li><li>at the very time, then, thereupon</li><li>herein, in this position, in this circumstance</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “there”) +&lrm; <i>τοῦ</i> (toû, “(medial)”) +&lrm; <i>-θα</i> (-tha, “towards”). For another modification to <i>τοῦ</i> (toû), see <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos).</p><p>This is the regular medial destination demonstrative reflex. Alternatively, this is an extension and <i>ἔνθα</i> (éntha) without the <i>τοῦ</i> (toû) is the regular reflex. The medial place one would have been <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>τοῦ</i> (toû), or alternatively just <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) without the <i>τοῦ</i> (toû) extension.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch or strain tight</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to string one's bow</li><li>to keep taut</li><li>to tie tight</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to strain, exert</li><li>to exert oneself, be vehement</li><li>to stretch out at or against</li><li>to put into verse</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>τείνω</i> (teínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>entelechy</li></ol><p>Coined by Aristotle from <i>ἐντελής</i> (entelḗs, “complete, full, accomplished”) + <i>ἔχειν</i> (ékhein, “have, hold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>complete, full</li><li>(<i>of victims</i>) perfect, unblemished</li><li>(<i>of equipment</i>) in good condition</li><li>(<i>of men</i>) full-grown, developed</li><li>possessing full rights</li><li>qualified to hold public office</li><li>subject to taxes or tributes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>τέλος</i> (télos, “accomplishment, fulfilment; end”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) intestinal hernia, rupture</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔντερον</i> (énteron, “intestine”) +&lrm; <i>κήλη</i> (kḗlē, “tumour, hernia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from this or that place, hence or thence</li><li>from this or that time, henceforth, thereupon</li><li>from this or that source or cause, therefore</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “there”) +&lrm; <i>τοῦ</i> (toû, “(medial)”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”). For another modification to <i>τοῦ</i> (toû), see <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos).</p><p>This is the regular medial source demonstrative reflex. Alternatively, this is an extension and <i>ἔνθεν</i> (énthen) without the <i>τοῦ</i> (toû) is the regular reflex.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injunction, order, command</li><li>commandment, ordinance</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐντέλλω</i> (entéllō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) in the meantime</li></ol><p>Medieval Greek. Uttering together the phrase <i>ἐν τούτοις</i> (en toútois): <i>ἐν</i> (en, preposition) + <i>τούτοις</i> (toútois), <i>dative plural</i> of Medieval <i>τοῦτος</i> (toûtos) from Ancient Greek <i>οὗτος</i> (hoûtos)). Compare to Ancient Greek <i>ἐν τούτῳ</i> (en toútōi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn about</li><li>(<i>mainly figuratively</i>) to make someone turn, put to shame</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to turn about, hesitate, feel misgiving or compunction</li><li>to turn towards, give heed or regard to, respect</li><li>to feel shame or fear on account of</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a turning towards: respect for, modesty, humiliation, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐντρέπω</i> (entrépō) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a turning towards</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>-τροπίᾱ</i> (-tropíā), the latter from <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>I meet, I fall in with, I stumble across</li><li>(<i>of thunder</i>) I strike</li><li>I discuss, I talk to</li><li>I appeal to, I entreat</li><li>I have sexual intercourse with</li><li>(<i>of books</i>) I meet with, I light upon, I read</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō, “to happen (to), I succeed, I light upon, I obtain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut in intaglio, carve or mold in or upon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔντῠπος</i> (éntŭpos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō), the former from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>τύπος</i> (túpos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a person</i>) native to a country or region, indigenous</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) living or active in a given place</li><li>particular to or characteristic of a country or region, local</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of agricultural, animal, and viticultural produce</li><li>of usages and customs</li></ol><li>internal, interior</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>medicine</i>) localised in a particular organ or part of the body, local</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in, near”) +&lrm; <i>τόπος</i> (tópos, “place, location”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, suffix forming adjectives). Compare <i>ἔντοπος</i> (éntopos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>within, inside (spatial enclosure&#59; within the interior of something, closest to the center, or to a specific point of reference)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>within (in or into the interior)</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;entós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éntos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁én</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>intus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔντυβον</i> (éntubon, “endive”)</li></ol><p><i>ἔντυβον</i> (éntubon) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>impression, dint, pit</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐντῠπόω</i> (entŭpóō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>war-like; bellicose (an epithet of Ares)</li><li>pertaining to battle</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐνυάλιος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Enyalius</li><li>Quirinus</li><li>Quirinal Hill, Rome</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνῡᾰ́λῐος</i> (enūắlĭos, “war-like”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀝𐀷𐀪𐀍</i> (e-nu-wa-ri-jo). Related to <i>Ἐνῡώ</i> (Enūṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔνυδρις</i> (énudris)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the leader of an <i>ἐνωμοτία</i> (enōmotía, “band of sworn soldiers”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνώμοτος</i> (enṓmotos, “bound by oath”) + <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A band of sworn soldiers</li><li>A division of the Spartan army</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνώμοτος</i> (enṓmotos, “bound by oath”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā), from <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) + <i>ὄμνυμι</i> (ómnumi, “I swear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐνόησα</i> (enóēsa)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐνόησε</i> (enóēse)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream</li><li>something seen in a dream</li></ol><p>The neuter inflected form of <i>ἐνύπνιος</i> (enúpnios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>appearing in dreams or sleep, seen in dreams, during one's sleep or dreams, seen in a vision during a dream</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en) + <i>ὕπνος</i> (húpnos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>face to face</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before, in the sight of, in the presence of</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>ἐνώπιος</i> (enṓpios).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>face to face</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “face”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>face to face</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps, “face”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earring</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ἐνῴδιον</i> (enōídion), from <i>ἐν</i> (en) +&lrm; <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδιον</i> (-ídion). The form was later altered to re-align with <i>ὦτᾰ</i> (ôtă), the plural of <i>οὖς</i> (oûs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐκ</i> (ek) (<i>used before vowels</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) (<i>used before vowels</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead out, bring out</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unguent box</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξαλείφω</i> (exaleíphō, “to anoint”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for sure, by all means</li><li>completely</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>ἐξ</i> (ex) +&lrm; <i>ἅπαντος</i> (hápantos), literally “for any reason”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attach to, fasten to</li><li>to kindle</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) hang from, cling to</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἅπτω</i> (háptō, “attach; kindle”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, lead off</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>inquire into, inquire of</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, come out (usually with genitive)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to stand forth and come to</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) to come to an end, pass, expire</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of public officers</i>) to go out of office</li></ol><li>(<i>of prophecies, dreams, events</i>) to be accomplished, come true</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of words</i>) to proceed from</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ</i> (ex, “out”) + <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “to go”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>close examination, scrutiny, test, inquiry</li><li>military inspection or review</li><li>arrangement, order</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξετᾰ́ζω</i> (exetắzō, “to examine, scrutinize, review”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(intransitive&#32;or&#32;[<i>with</i> genitive]) to stand out, to project</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;figurative) to exceed, to be renowned</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;middle voice</i>) to depend [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘on’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>narration</li><li>interpretation, explanation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξηγέομαι</i> (exēgéomai) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send out, let (<i>someone</i>) go out, dismiss</li><li>to take out</li><li>to discharge themselves (<i>of rivers</i>)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to get rid of</li><li>to divorce</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to displace; to change</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to drive one out of their senses; to amaze, excite</li><li>to get rid of</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be displaced, to make way; to stand aside from</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to shrink from, shun</li><li>to go out of joint</li></ol><li>(<i>with genitive of object</i>) to retire from, give up possession of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to abandon</li><li>to lose, give up</li><li>to lose one's wits; to be distraught, astonished</li><li>(<i>absolutive</i>) to change one's position or opinion</li><li>(<i>language</i>) to be removed from common usage</li></ol><li>to stand out, project</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ</i> (ek) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equalization</li></ol><p><i>ἐξῐσόω</i> (exĭsóō, “to make equal, equalize”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a taking out, extraction</li><li>removal, purgation</li><li>a way of taking out</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) taking exception, questioning of (an adversary’s arguments)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>law</i>) translating the Latin <i>exceptiō</i></li></ol><li>transcendence</li><li>a place where cargoes are landed, a wharf</li><li>(law,&#32;in the phrase <i>ἐξαιρέσεως δίκη</i>) action against one who has asserted the free birth of a slave</li><li>killing</li></ol><p><i>ἐξαιρέω</i> (exairéō, “to take out, discharge [cargo], except, bereave [someone] of [something, <i>e.g.</i> life], transcend, claim [someone] as a freeman”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, suffix forming nouns of action)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Elean</i>) synonym of <i>ἐξαιρέω</i> (exairéō)</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γρέω</i> (ăgréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take out</li><li>to pick out, choose, select</li><li>to pluck out, draw out, root out</li><li>to except</li><li>to expel</li><li>to remove</li><li>to take away</li><li>to set free, deliver, rescue</li><li>to annul, demolish</li><li>to bring to an end, accomplish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to demand from</li><li>to demand one's surrender</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to beg for oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to destroy, to sack</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλαπάζω</i> (alapázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set quite free</li><li>to resolve into its elements</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “off, away”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νᾰλῡ́ω</i> (ănălū́ō, “unsettle, unloose”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suddenly, all of a sudden</li></ol><p>Based on φ/π variation with the likely-related <i>ἄφνω</i> (áphnō, “suddenly”), Beekes and Furnee assume Pre-Greek origin, noting that the correspondence of -ιφ-/-πι- suggests that the original Pre-Greek term was pronounced with a <i>&#42;pʸ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snatch away from</li><li>to tear out, tear off</li><li>to rescue</li></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>ἁ̆ρπά̆ζω</i> (hărpá̆zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang upon</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to make dependent upon</li><li>to stretch out</li><li>to depend upon, be attached to</li><li>(<i>of countries</i>) to be adjacent to</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-, “out-”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρτάω</i> (artáō, “to hang”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prepare, equip, fit out</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἀρτύω</i> (artúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to Atticize, express in an Attic style, fashion, manner</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἀττικίζω</i> (attikízō), equivalent to equivalent to <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>Ἀττικός</i> (Attikós) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flesh-hook for taking meat out of a pot</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξαύω</i> (exaúō, “to take out dressed meat”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy utterly</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to disappear utterly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>ἀφανίζω</i> (aphanízō, “to do away with, destroy”), the latter from <i>ἀφανής</i> (aphanḗs, “unseen”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, “verb-forming suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) again, anew</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>αὖτις</i> (aûtis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drive out, chase away</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strip a fallen foe of his armor</li><li>to kill, slay</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἐναρίζω</i> (enarízō), itself from <i>ἔναρα</i> (énara, “armour and trappings of a fallen foe”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to evacuate, esp. by purge or vomit</li><li>to disgorge</li><li>to pour out, let fall</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἐρᾱ́ω</i> (erā́ō, “to pour forth, vomit”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work out, make completely, finish off, bring to perfection</li><li>to accomplish, perform, achieve</li><li>to work at, cultivate</li><li>to undo, destroy, overwhelm, ruin</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἐργάζομαι</i> (ergázomai, “to work”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine well or closely, inquire into, scrutinise</li><li>to examine or question a person closely</li><li>to estimate, compare τι πρός τι one thing by or with another</li><li>(<i>of gold</i>) to prove by testing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-, “ex-, from”) +&lrm; <i>ἐτάζω</i> (etázō, “to examine, test”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to find out, discover</li><li>to invent</li><li>to find</li><li>to seek out, search after</li><li>to win, get, procure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>εὑρίσκω</i> (heurískō)..</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak out</li><li>to declare</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead (the way)</li><li>to lead (an army) or govern</li><li>to dictate</li><li>to prescribe, order</li><li>to expound, interpret</li><li>to relate, tell at length, explain</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leader</li><li>adviser</li><li>expounder, interpreter (<i>of oracles, dreams etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξηγέομαι</i> (exēgéomai) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>interpretational</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξηγέομαι</i> (exēgéomai) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reach, arrive at (<i>a place or object</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to come to (<i>someone</i>) as a suppliant</li><li>to complete, accomplish [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be sufficient</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἱκνέομαι</i> (hiknéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make equal, equalize</li></ol><p><i>ἔξῐσος</i> (éxĭsos, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō), from <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>giving thanks, thankful</li></ol><p><i>ἐξομολογέομαι</i> (exomologéomai, “confess; admit, acknowledge”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>confession</li><li>(biblical,&#32;Koine) praise</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξομολογέω</i> (exomologéō, “confess”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) backwards, back again</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) hereafter</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>behind</li></ol><p><i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ὀπίσω</i> (opísō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear in, administer an oath to</li><li>to adjure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔξορκος</i> (éxorkos, “bound by oath”) (from <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ὅρκος</i> (hórkos)) + <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exorcist</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξορκίζω</i> (exorkízō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir up, urge on, excite</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ὀτρῡ́νω</i> (otrū́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power, means, license, or authority to do something.</li><li>power, authority</li><li>office, title</li><li>means, resources</li><li>pomp</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξοντ-</i> (exont-, participle) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, verbal adjective suffix), from <i>ἔξεστι</i> (éxesti, “it is possible, it is allowed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pre-eminence</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξέχω</i> (exékhō, “to stand out”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to break out into insolence</li><li>to run riot; to wax wanton</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔βρῐ́ζω</i> (hŭbrĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wake</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔξῠπνος</i> (éxŭpnos, “awakened”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>external, outside, exterior</li><li>foreign</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔξω</i> (éxō) + <i>-τερος</i> (-teros)+ <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foreign, outlying, alien (opposed to <i>συγγενής</i>, <i>ἰδιωτικός</i>, etc.)</li><li>uninitiated</li></ol><p><i>ἔξω</i> (éxō, “outside”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, suffix forming adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who drives out,</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>epithet of <i>Ἄρης</i> (Árēs)</li><li><i>of winds</i>: ἐξῶσται <i>ἄνεμοι</i> (ánemoi) violent winds driving -ships- away</li><li>(Koine,&#32;medicine) <i>of pulse</i>: coined by Archigenes</li></ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Hesychius gives the definition as: platform of a ship or for docking ships</li><li>(Koine,&#32;theater) balcony</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) balcony</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξωθέω</i> (exōthéō, “force out”) (from <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) + <i>ὠθέω</i> (ōthéō)) + <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐπί</i> (epí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-), used before a vowel with smooth breathing</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promise</li><li>profession</li></ol><p><i>ἐπᾰγγέλλω</i> (epăngéllō) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring on, bring to, lead to</li><li>to apply</li><li>to march against</li><li>to advance</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to draw in to oneself</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to call for one’s benefit</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to bring to oneself, procure</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἐφᾰ́λλομαι</i> (ephắllomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>above, higher than, more than</li></ol><p><i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “on”) +&lrm; <i>ἄνω</i> (ánō, “upwards”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to govern, lead, rule over [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be ruler, commander, governor of a province</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine,&#32;historical) to be eparch of an eparchy in the Greek-speaking Eastern Roman Empire (akin to prefect of a prefecture in the Latin-speaking Western Roman Empire)</li></ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to rule besides or in addition to one's hereditary dominions</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to serve, offer [<i>with</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>Homeric</i>) to pour the first drops before a libation</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on, upon; over”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ρχω</i> (ắrkhō, “to begin; lead, rule, command”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>clothing</i>) Upper garment.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπενδύνω</i> (ependúnō, “I put on over”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, noun suffix), from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, over”) +&lrm; <i>ἐνδύνω</i> (endúnō, “I put on (clothes)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) Insertion of a letter</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπεντῐ́θημῐ</i> (epentĭ́thēmĭ, “I insert”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out against (<i>an enemy</i>) [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to proceed against, take vengeance on [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to prosecute [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> accusative]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go over, traverse, go through in detail</li><li>to go through with, execute</li><li>(<i>Attic</i>) functions as the future tense of <i>ἐπεξέρχομαι</i> (epexérkhomai)</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>ἔξειμι</i> (éxeimi, “to go out”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be like, to suit [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;impersonal</i>) to be fit, proper, seemly [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἔοικα</i> (éoika).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask in addition or again</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to go against, attack, invade [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> (<i>rarely</i>) accusative]</li><li>to come forward to speak</li><li>to suddenly come upon, befall, happen to [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> accusative]</li><li>to come into one's head, occur to [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>of a period of or point in time</i>) to arrive</li><li>to go over, traverse, cross [<i>with</i> accusative]</li><li>to go through, discuss, recount [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on, against”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “to go”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>I restrain myself</li><li>I hold (myself) back</li></ol><p>Middle (reflexive) form of ἐπέχω. First person present indicative.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have or hold upon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hold out to, present, offer</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to extend, spread out</li><li>to hold, contain</li><li>to enjoin, impose</li></ol><li>to direct towards, aim at</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to aim at, attack</li><li>to direct one's mind; to intend, purpose</li><li>to stand facing (in battle)</li></ol><li>to hold back, keep in check; to withhold; to confine</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to wait, pause, refrain</li></ol><li>to reach or extend over</li><li>to have power over or occupy</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to prevail, predominate</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have, hold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>when, whenever</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ἐπεὶ ἄν</i> (epeì án).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insulting treatment, spiteful abuse</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) special state sanction, socage</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>at the oars</li><li>equipped with oars</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἐρετμόν</i> (eretmón)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>on, upon (on the upper surface of)</li><li>on (supported by)</li><li>(<i>mostly post-Homeric</i>) in</li><li>at, near</li><li>(<i>of ships</i>) at (dependent upon)</li><li>(<i>with reflexive or personal pronoun</i>) by oneself</li><li>(with numerals,&#32;of a body of soldiers) deep</li><li>(<i>with a person</i>) before (in the presence of)</li><li>in the case of; on</li><li>in the time of</li><li>(<i>of authority, power, etc.</i>) in</li><li>on (an occasion)</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>on, upon</li><li>in</li><li>at, near</li><li>over</li><li>in honor of</li><li>against</li><li>in addition to, over, besides</li><li>(<i>with duplication of head noun</i>) after</li><li>in the power of</li><li>according to</li><li>(<i>of conditions or circumstances</i>) in, with</li><li>(of time,&#32;never in proper Attic) at, on</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) after</li><li>(<i>expressing a cause</i>) on account of, for</li><li>(<i>expressing a purpose</i>) for</li><li>(<i>of a condition</i>) on</li><li>for (i.e. in exchange for)</li><li>(<i>of a name</i>) for</li><li>in charge of</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>onto (the upper surface of)</li><li>to</li><li>up to, as far as</li><li>(<i>with a person</i>) to before, into the presence of</li><li>(<i>of an army</i>) deep</li><li>to or into a certain side</li><li>against</li><li>over</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) for, during</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) up to, until</li><li>for (the purpose of)</li><li>for (with respect to)</li><li>over (in command of)</li></ol><li>(<i>without a noun</i>) as well, besides (often with <i>δέ</i> (dé))</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;epí</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁epi</i> (“on”).</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀠</i> (e-pi), Sanskrit <i>अपि</i> (ápi), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬌𐬞𐬌</i> (a<sup><small>i</small></sup>pi), Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎱𐎡𐎹</i> (apiy), Old Armenian <i>եւ</i> (ew), Latin <i>ob</i>, Old High German <i>bī</i>, and Old English <i>bī</i>.</p><p>The genitive is an innovated Greek associative. The dative is from the PIE locative. The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is thrown over, covering</li><li>coverlet, bedspread</li><li>tapestry, hangings</li><li>that which is put on, piece of embroidery, mantle, shawl</li><li>patch to repair a garment</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>βλῆμα</i> (blêma, “coverlet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of reed extending over the ground</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) + <i>γῆ</i> (gê) + <i>-εια</i> (-eia).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>on <i>or</i> of the earth, terrestrial</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>γῆ</i> (gê, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>examination, scrutiny</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>acquaintance, full knowledge</li></ol><li>an acknowledgement</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>γνῶσῐς</i> (gnôsĭs, “inquiry, knowledge”), or from <i>ἐπιγιγνώσκω</i> (epigignṓskō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A thing which "comes after" the fruit or seed of something else: offspring, descendant</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “after”, prepositional prefix) +&lrm; <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “fruit, product, seed; begetting, procreation”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐπίγονος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>One (a member) of the Epigoni (the Ἐπίγονοι), the legendary sons of the Argive heroes of the first Theban War, who as legend has it, were themselves prosecutors of the second Theban War.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπίγονος</i> (epígonos, “offspring, descendant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inscription, (especially in verse), an epigram</li><li>a written estimate of damages</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιγράφω</i> (epigráphō, “to mark the surface”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exhibition, display</li><li>example</li><li>declamation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιδείκνῡμι</i> (epideíknūmi, “to show, display”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upper or outer bandage</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>δεσμός</i> (desmós, “bond; fetter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, lid, top</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) capital (<i>of a column</i>)</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) poultice (remedy for external application)</li><li>addition, higher bid</li><li>(<i>archery</i>) shaft (<i>of an arrow</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon, atop”) +&lrm; <i>θέμα</i> (théma, “that which is placed or laid down”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adjective</li><li>epithet</li></ol><p>Substantivized neuter singular of <i>ἐπίθετος</i> (epíthetos, “added on”), from <i>ἐπιτίθημι</i> (epitíthēmi).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to convince, persuade</li><li>to succeed through entreaty</li><li>to mislead</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to bribe</li><li>to tempt</li></ol></ol><li>(mediopassive voice,&#32;πείθομαι,&#32;and Epic future,&#32;πῐθήσω,&#32;with dative)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to obey, yield to</li><li>to believe, trust in</li></ol><li>(second perfect active,&#32;πέποιθᾰ,&#32;with passive sense) to trust, rely on (<i>with dative of person or thing</i>)</li><li>(perfect passive,&#32;πέπεισμαι,&#32;post-Epic) to believe, trust (<i>with dative</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;péitʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeydʰ-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>fīdō</i>, Albanian <i>be</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;bīdaną</i>, from which Old English <i>bīdan</i> (English <i>bide</i>).</p><p>Stems πειθ-, πιθ- with vowel shift, and <i>ποιθ-</i> (poith-) with ablaut. Derivatives from all stems, with π(ε)ιθ-τ- &gt; π(ε)ιστ-, πειθ-μ- &gt; πεισμ-.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>additional, added-on</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fictitious</li></ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adjectival</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιτίθημι</i> (epitíthēmi).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to lay, put or place upon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to set upon, turn towards</li></ol><li>to put on a covering or lid</li><li>to put to, add, grant, or give besides</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of time</i>) to add, bring on</li></ol><li>to put on as a finish</li><li>to impose or inflict a penalty</li><li>to dispatch a letter</li><li>to give a name</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to put on oneself or for oneself</li><li>to put on or to</li><li>to set oneself to, apply oneself, employ oneself in</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to make an attempt upon, attack</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>)</li></ol><li>to bring on oneself</li><li>to lay commands on</li><li>to give a name</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>τῐ́θημῐ</i> (tĭ́thēmĭ, “to put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>additional, added-on</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fictitious</li></ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adjectival</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιτίθημι</i> (epitíthēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hellweed (<i>Cuscuta epithymum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>θύμον</i> (thúmon, “thyme”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in fit time or place, in season, seasonable, opportune, convenient</li><li>vital (<i>of parts of the body</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>καιρός</i> (kairós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surname</li><li>invocation</li></ol><p>Analyzable as <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>κλῆσις</i> (klêsis) or as <i>ἐπικαλέω</i> (epikaléō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>couch, armchair</li><li>straight-backed chair</li><li>back of a couch or chair</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπικλίνω</i> (epiklínō, “to recline upon”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>common to many people, things, etc.</li><li>promiscuous, sluttish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon; on top of; all over”) +&lrm; <i>κοινός</i> (koinós, “common; general, public”). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱóm</i> (“beside, by, near, with”) + <i>&#42;-yós</i> (suffix forming adjectives from noun stems). Compare <i>γένος ἐπίκοινον</i> (génos epíkoinon, “common gender”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>assisting, aiding</li><li>defending</li><li>(<i>masculine substantive</i>) helper, ally, assister</li><li>(<i>masculine substantive</i>) mercenary, hireling</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) + an unattested word, perhaps deriving from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱers-</i>, with cognates such as Latin <i>currō</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐπίκουρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Epicurus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπίκουρος</i> (epíkouros, “assisting, helper; defending, ally”), from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) + an otherwise unknown root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>determination</li><li>verification</li><li>judgement</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπικρῑ́νω</i> (epikrī́nō, “to determine, judge”) (from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō)) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking hold of, clasping, claiming, seizure</li><li>epileptic fit, epilepsy, seizure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιλαμβάνω</i> (epilambánō, “I seize”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis), from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) + <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “I take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reasoning, inference</li><li>the epilogue or concluding portion of a play</li><li>the peroration of a speech</li><li>a subjoined or explanatory sentence</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “speech”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The fee for a bath at a bathhouse.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>λουτρόν</i> (loutrón, “bath”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>release from, exemption from banishment</li><li>solution, explanation</li><li>discharge of a debt</li><li>spell</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) change of dressing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιλύω</i> (epilúō, “set free, release”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, “a suffix added to verb stems to form abstract nouns or nouns of action, result or process”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>manumitted</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) freeing, liberating</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐλῡ́ω</i> (epĭlū́ō, “set free”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, suffix forming perfective passive verbal adjectives).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>set at liberty for a ransom</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>λῠ́τρον</i> (lŭ́tron, “ransom”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that may easily be attacked, assailable (of fortified places)</li><li>contended for, contested</li><li>equipped for battle</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) ally, helper</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιμαχίᾱ</i> (epimakhíā, “defensive alliance”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐπίμαχος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Epimachus</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “against”) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seaport where the state navy lies; state harbor</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distaff</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>νῆτρον</i> (nêtron, “distaff”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>power of thought, imagination, inventiveness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπινοέω</i> (epinoéō, “to invent, contrive”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sworn falsely (<i>of oaths</i>)</li><li>forsworn, perjured</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine) false oath, bootless oath</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ὅρκος</i> (hórkos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a powder or substance for sprinkling; something sprinkled on</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a powdered preparation applied by sprinkling</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) a topping or sprinkling (especially on bread)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, over”) +&lrm; <i>πάσσω</i> (pássō, “to sprinkle”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sprinkled over, strewn upon; having something scattered on the surface</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter,&#32;cooking) a kind of cake or pastry sprinkled with comfits or similar toppings</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter,&#32;medicine) (sc. <i>φάρμακον</i> (phármakon)) a powdered medicine applied by sprinkling; a dusting powder</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, over”) +&lrm; <i>παστός</i> (pastós, “sprinkled, strewn”), verbal adjective from <i>πάσσω</i> (pássō, “to sprinkle, strew”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>on the ground, on the earth</li><li>flat, planar</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) referring to a plane or surface</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>πέδον</i> (pédon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) fold of the peritoneum, omentum</li></ol><p>Compared to Lithuanian <i>plėvė</i> (“fine, thin skin”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;plěva</i> (“membrane”), with prefixing. The form <i>ἐπιπόλαιον</i> (epipólaion) arose from connection with <i>ἐπιπόλαιος</i> (epipólaios, “superficial, on the surface”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painful, toilsome, laborious</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>πόνος</i> (pónos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) adverb</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>ῥῆμα</i> (rhêma, “verb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pubic region, pubic mound</li></ol><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watcher, guardian</li><li>scout, watch</li><li>overseer, supervisor, inspector</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) an ecclesiastical superintendent, bishop</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hitting the mark, target</li><li>successful</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>σκοπός</i> (skopós, “watcher, lookout, guardian”).</p><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>σκοπός</i> (skopós, “aim, target”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>episkyros (an Ancient Greek ball game in which players on two teams attempted to throw the ball over the heads of the other team)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly related to <i>σκύρος</i> (skúros, “chippings of stone”). Another name for the game was apparently <i>ἐπίκοινος</i> (epíkoinos), from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>κοινός</i> (koinós, “common”). The game, or a similar game known as <i>φαινίνδα</i> (phainínda), was later adopted by the Romans, who renamed and transformed it into <i>harpastum</i>, the latinisation of the Ancient Greek <i>ἁρπαστόν</i> (harpastón) (neuter of <i>ἁρπαστός</i> (harpastós)), meaning "snatched away", from the verb <i>ἁρπάζω</i> (harpázō, “seize, filch, take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to know, to have the knowledge of something</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi). Compare <i>ἐφῐ́στημῐ</i> (ephĭ́stēmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metal hoop upon the felloe, tire of a wheel</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, upon) +&lrm; <i>σῶτρον</i> (sôtron, “wooden felloe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>injunction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>assessment</li><li>a command, order</li></ol><li>command</li><li>(<i>in tactics</i>) station on the flanks</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐτᾰ́σσω</i> (epĭtắssō, “to put upon, enjoin”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a person in charge of something, steward, administrator</li><li>governor, viceroy</li><li>trustee, guardian</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιτρέπω</i> (epitrépō, “entrust”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liable to envy or jealousy</li><li>regarded with jealousy, odious, invidious, hateful</li><li>bearing a grudge [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘against’</small>]</li><li>injurious</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>φθόνος</i> (phthónos) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feeling joy, being delighted, especially over one's enemies.</li><li>exultant, triumphant</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιχαίρω</i> (epikhaírō, “rejoice over someone”), from <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”) + <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō, “I rejoice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smearing over, coating, application by rubbing onto a surface</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) external application of a medicament; topical treatment by smearing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιχρῑ́ω</i> (epikhrī́ō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis), from <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) an ointment, unguent, or salve applied to the body</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>χρῖσμα</i> (khrîsma, “ointment, anointing”), from <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smeared on; applied as a surface coating (e.g. of medicines or dyes)</li><li>(<i>cosmetic</i>) rouged, painted; cosmetically made up</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) artificial, superficial</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, over”) +&lrm; <i>χρῑστός</i> (khrīstós, “rubbed; anointed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dart at; to spring upon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ᾱ̓ῑ̈́σσω</i> (āī̈́ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hear, to give ear</li><li>to perceive</li><li>to understand</li><li>to have knowledge, to be an expert</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἀΐω</i> (aḯō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift, raise</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “up”) +&lrm; <i>αἴρω</i> (aírō, “raise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tell, proclaim, announce</li><li>to give orders, command</li><li>to denounce and summon someone who, having incurred <i>ἀτιμία</i> (atimía) in dishonour done to the gods, yet takes part in public affairs</li><li>to promise, offer</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to profess, make profession of</li><li>to demand, require</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἀγγέλλω</i> (angéllō).</p><p>(<i>to promise</i>): Compare typologically Latin <i>prōmittō</i> (&lt; Latin <i>mittō</i>); Russian <i>сули́ть</i> (sulítʹ) (? akin to <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>announcement</li><li>promise</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπαγγέλλω</i> (epangéllō, “to announce, promise”) (from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) + <i>ἀγγέλλω</i> (angéllō, “to announce”)) + <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>prōmissum</i>, Russian <i>по́сул</i> (pósul) (? akin to <i>слать</i> (slatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leading in, bringing in or upon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπᾰ́γω</i> (epắgō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approve, applaud, agree, countenance</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to praise, commend</li><li>to agree or undertake to do</li></ol><li>to recommend, advise</li><li>Used to decline an offer or invitation.</li><li>(<i>Rhapsody</i>) to recite</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ́</i> (epĭ́) +&lrm; <i>αἰνέω</i> (ainéō, “to approve”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>given to praising, laudatory</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπαινετός</i> (epainetós, “praiseworthy”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be praised, praiseworthy, laudable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπαινέω</i> (epainéō, “praise”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask besides</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to ask for more</li></ol><li>to beg as a mendicant</li><li>to demand</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπί</i> (epí) + <i>αἰτέω</i> (aitéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>)to listen to, hearken to, hear</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to give ear, listen</li><li>to obey [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to heal, cure</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλθέω</i> (althéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>healing</li><li>healed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπαλθέω</i> (epalthéō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to heal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἀλθαίνω</i> (althaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to aid, assist</li><li>to defend</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἀμῡ́νω</i> (amū́nō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐπανα-</i> (epana-), used before a vowel</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>retracing one's steps, return</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπᾰνᾰλῡ́ω</i> (epănălū́ō, “return”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, abstract suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return, come again</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπανα-</i> (epana-, “re-”) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “go”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to let loose at</li><li>(<i>construed with the accusative</i>) to let go, to give up</li><li>to dismiss, to remit</li><li>(<i>construed with the genitive</i>) to release from</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to relax</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to relax, to leave off, to slacken, to become easier</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπανα-</i> (epana-, “re-”) +&lrm; <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi, “throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>re-</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἀνα-</i> (ana-). ἀνα means "up" and "back" and ἐπι means "on" and "even more." "(up + on) + (back + more)" = "(up - up) + (again)" = "re." More naturally idiomatically analyzed epana-root, not epi-(ana-root).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take up again, resume, repeat</li><li>to revise, correct</li><li>to undertake</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπανα-</i> (epana-, “again”) +&lrm; <i>λαμβάνω</i> (lambánō, “to receive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>admitting return</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπᾰνᾰλῡ́ω</i> (epănălū́ō, “to return”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to return</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπανα-</i> (epana-, “re”) +&lrm; <i>λύω</i> (lúō, “loosen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go back again, retreat, return</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἀναχωρέω</i> (anakhōréō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discharge against</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “over, at, for, against”) +&lrm; <i>ἀπολύω</i> (apolúō, “loose from, discharge”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eparchy, province, the provincial government of an administrative district</li><li>provincial government territorial jurisdictions specifically of the Greek-speaking eastern Roman Empire and Byzantine Empire, equivalent to Latin <i>prōvincia</i></li></ol><p>Originally referring to a district governed by an <i>ἔπαρχος</i> (éparkhos, “commander”), from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) after, after that, since, when</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with indicative</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(of a definite occurrence in past time,&#32;mostly with aorist)</li><li>(<i>with implied reference to some later time</i>)</li></ol><li>(with subjunctive,&#32;<i>ἄν</i> (án) often being added)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>referring to future time</i>)</li><li>(referring generally to any one of a number of instances,&#32;with a present apodosis of repeated action) whenever, when once</li></ol><li>(with optative,&#32;without <i>ἄν</i> (án))</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>referring to future time</i>)</li><li>(more frequently,&#32;referring generally to any one of a number of past instances,&#32;with a past apodosis of repeated action)</li><li>(in indirect speech after past tenses,&#32;representing a subjunctive in direct speech)</li></ol><li>(with infinitive,&#32;only be assimilation in indirect speech)</li><li>(<i>with other words</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(with <i>τάχιστα</i> (tákhista))</li><li>(<i>with various emphatic particles</i>)</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>causal</i>) since, seeing that</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with indicative</i>)</li><li>(<i>in most of the constructions which are found in independent sentences</i>)</li><li>(<i>with infinitive in indirect speech</i>)</li><li>(<i>in various elliptical expressions</i>)</li><li>(sometimes epexegetic,&#32;introducing a narrative)</li><li>(<i>with other particles</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “on”) + <i>εἰ</i> (ei, “if”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to press hard, oppress</li><li>to impel, urge on</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to press on, hasten, drive fast</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Brugmann suggests a relation to <i>οἴγνῡμῐ</i> (oígnūmĭ, “to open”), supposing an earlier pre-form <i>&#42;ϝο-(ε)ιγ-</i> (&#42;wo-(e)ig-) for the root of the latter, which may imply a derivation from <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “epi-, on-”) + the root underlying <i>οἴγω</i> (oígō, “to open”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(strengthened form of <i>ἐπεί</i> (epeí)) after, since</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπεί</i> (epeí, “after, since”) + <i>δή</i> (dḗ, “emphatic particle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>addition</li><li>episode</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coming in besides, adventitious</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>εἰσόδιος</i> (eisódios), from <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-) + <i>ὅδιος</i> (hódios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to disembark</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on a garment over another; (<i>passive</i>) to have on over</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, over”) +&lrm; <i>ἐνδύνω</i> (endúnō, “to put on”), from <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in, on”) +&lrm; <i>δύνω</i> (dúnō, “to get into”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inserted</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπένθεσις</i> (epénthesis, “insertion”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to insert (<i>a letter</i>)</li><li>to put in besides, in addition to</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἐντίθημι</i> (entíthēmi, “to put in”), from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) + <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out against, make a sally</li><li>(<i>of a message</i>) to reach</li><li>to proceed against, prosecute</li><li>to punish</li><li>to go through or over, traverse</li><li>to carry out, accomplish, execute</li><li>to discuss, relate, examine</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>detailed account or explanation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπεξηγέομαι</i> (epexēgéomai) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to explain in detail</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἐξηγέομαι</i> (exēgéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to consult, inquire of, question</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with object accusative</i>) to ask a thing</li><li>(<i>with personal accusative</i>)</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to put the question</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>ἐρωτᾰ́ω</i> (erōtắō, “to ask”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout assent, applaud</li><li>to sing praises, glorify</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>εὐφημέω</i> (euphēméō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pray (to)</li><li>to imprecate (upon)</li><li>to exult (over)</li><li>to boast</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>εὔχομαι</i> (eúkhomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say besides</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>abundantly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) abundant, ample, sufficient</li></ol><p>Unknown beyond being prefixed with <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>place</i>) on, upon, on top of, all over, covering</li><li>(<i>motion</i>) on, over</li><li>(<i>addition, accompaniment</i>) in addition to, as interest on, with</li><li>(<i>time</i>) after</li><li>(<i>of superiority, authority, motivation</i>) over, at, for, against</li><li>(<i>intensive</i>) over-</li></ol><p>See <i>ἐπί</i> (epí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shout out, shout aloud</li><li>to shout applause (<i>after a speech</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἰάχω</i> (iákhō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἐφανδάνω</i> (ephandánō, “be pleasing/grateful to”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of ephemeron (short-lived insect)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw, cast, fling upon</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get on, board, mount</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set foot upon, step upon; to occupy</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to take a stand upon, rely upon</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to be an <i>ἐπιβάτης</i> (epibátēs), to serve as a passenger or soldier aboard a ship</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to mount (upon something)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιβάτης</i> (epibátēs) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put (e.g. people) on a ship, etc.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “epi-, on-”) +&lrm; <i>βιβάζω</i> (bibázō, “to cause to mount, put on”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to someone’s aid, succor [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>βοηθέω</i> (boēthéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) herdsman</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>βουκόλος</i> (boukólos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to plot against; to contrive a plan against [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to plan upon something; to plan to do something [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> infinitive]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be harmful</li><li>(<i>passive voice&#32;or&#32;future&#32;middle voice</i>) to be plotted against</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>βουλεύω</i> (bouleúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come into being after; to be born after</li><li>to come as fulfillment of [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘of something’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to come into being”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look upon, witness, observe</li><li>to recognize, know again</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) to find out, discover, detect</li></ol><li>to come to a judgement, decide</li><li>to recognize, acknowledge, approve</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>γιγνώσκω</i> (gignṓskō, “to know”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic, Koine, and Byzantine form of <i>ἐπιγιγνώσκω</i> (epigignṓskō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) The valve which covers the larynx; epiglottis.</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) The valve of the vocal chords.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon, over”) +&lrm; <i>γλωσσῐ́ς</i> (glōssĭ́s, “glottis, mouth of the windpipe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Attic form of <i>ἐπῐγλωσσῐ́ς</i> (epĭglōssĭ́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) kneecap, rotula</li><li>garment reaching to the knee</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, upon) +&lrm; <i>γόνυ</i> (gónu, “knee”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mark the surface of, barely pierce, graze</li><li>to mark, put a mark on</li><li>to write upon, inscribe</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to set down the penalty or damages in the title of an indictment</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to have one's name registered</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to assume a name</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inscription</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>title of a work</li><li>name of a ship</li><li>ascription of a deed to its author, credit or honor of a thing</li><li>insertion, interlineation in a document</li></ol><li>description of parties in pleadings</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>registration of property for taxation</li><li>impost, tax</li><li>assessment</li><li>requisition</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιγράφω</i> (epigráphō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>meaning uncertain</i>) concerning tax assessments</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐγρᾰφή</i> (epĭgrăphḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>among the people</li><li>at home</li><li>resident</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>show, make a display of</li><li>prove</li><li>point out</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>δείκνυμι</i> (deíknumi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Outer skin, epidermis.</li><li>Web of waterbirds' feet.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>δέρμα</i> (dérma, “skin, hide”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a stay in a place</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιδημέω</i> (epidēméō) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magistrates sent annually by Doric states to their colonies</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>δημιουργός</i> (dēmiourgós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>additional will, codicil</li><li>pledge, security</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “additional”) +&lrm; <i>δῐᾰθήκη</i> (dĭăthḗkē, “will, testament”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come as a relief to</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>διαλύω</i> (dialúō, “dissolve”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) epididymis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>δίδυμος</i> (dídumos, “twin; the testicles”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to whirl or swing round before throwing</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to turn over in one's mind, revolve</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to wheel about (<i>as birds in the air</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>δῑνέω</i> (dīnéō, “to whirl around”), from <i>δῑ́νη</i> (dī́nē, “whirlpool, eddy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reasonableness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>equity</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) fairness</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιεικής</i> (epieikḗs, “reasonable”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐπιείκελε</i> (epieíkele)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like, similar, resembling (<i>followed by the dative</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>εἴκελος</i> (eíkelos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fitting, suitable</li><li>(<i>after Homer</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of statements, rights</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>reasonable</li><li>fair, equitable, not according to the letter of the law</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>able, capable</li><li>(<i>in a moral sense</i>) reasonable, fair, kind, gentle, good</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>εἰκός</i> (eikós, “reasonable”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>vehement, violent, furious</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, on, upon) +&lrm; <i>ζάφελος</i> (záphelos, “violent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing loss, hurtful, damaging</li><li>prejudicial</li><li>penal</li><li>liable to punishment</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “loss, damage”) +&lrm; <i>ζημίᾱ</i> (zēmíā) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An addition, an increase</li><li>A cover put over a statue</li><li>A sum allowed to cover expenses</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, chest”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bridal, nuptial; of or pertaining to the bride, the wedding</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;masculine,&#32;feminine&#32;or&#32;neuter) bridal song, epithalamium</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>θάλαμος</i> (thálamos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>condemned to death</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>θάνατος</i> (thánatos, “death”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ready to attack, aggressive</li><li>enterprising</li><li>added</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπίθεσις</i> (epíthesis, “setting upon”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set one's heart on a thing, desire, covet</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with genitive</i>)</li><li>(Koine,&#32;with accusative)</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) desire, want to</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ́θῡμος</i> (epĭ́thūmos, “desirous”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yearning, longing, desire, craving</li><li>(<i>typically with a negative connotation</i>) lust</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ἐπῐ́θῡμος</i> (epĭ́thūmos, “desirous”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἐπίκαιρος</i> (epíkairos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to call upon a god, invoke</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to invite</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to call in as a helper or ally</li><li>to call in as witness</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to call before one, summon</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to challenge</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be called by a surname, be nicknamed</li><li>to bring as an accusation against</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with dative of person</i>) to quarrel, dispute with</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō, “to call”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reduce yet further</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “in addition to”) +&lrm; <i>καταλῡ́ω</i> (katalū́ō, “put down, destroy”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to live at, inhabit</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>κατοικέω</i> (katoikéō, “inhabit, dwell”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide, conceal</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>κεύθω</i> (keúthō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)kewdʰ-</i> (“to hide, conceal”); cognate with English <i>hide</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive&#32;or&#32;intransitive,&#32;active voice&#32;or&#32;middle voice) to assign to, to destine [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>, <i>along with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>or</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “for”) +&lrm; <i>κλώθω</i> (klṓthō, “spin”). Describes the action of the Fates, who spun the thread of each person's destiny.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act as an ally</li><li>to aid, help</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπίκουρος</i> (epíkouros) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>help, aid, succour</li><li>an auxiliary or mercenary force</li></ol><p><i>ἐπίκουρος</i> (epíkouros) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>conquest, victory [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘over’</small>]</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) acquisition, appropriation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπικρατέω</i> (epikratéō) (from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō)) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rule over</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>κρατέω</i> (kratéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hang over, overhang</li><li>to threaten, be impending, be imminent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, on, upon) +&lrm; <i>κρεμάννῡμι</i> (kremánnūmi, “to hang”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw a cloak over (something)</li><li>to disguise (something)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “to hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chopping block, billet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>κόπανον</i> (kópanon, “pestle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>epic</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἔπος</i> (épos, “story”, “song”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “-ic”, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say in addition, to add further, to call by name</li><li>to choose, to pick out, to select</li><li>to read</li><li>to think over, to consider</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on, onto”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to choose, to say”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;active voice) [<i>with</i> genitive] to cause one to forget (something)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to miss (to allow (something) to escape one's notice)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be forgotten</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>λήθω</i> (lḗthō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>forgetful</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιλανθάνομαι</i> (epilanthánomai) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn), from the former's full-grade root <i>ληθ-</i> (lēth-) with regular sound change to σ in front of μ.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take or get besides</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to take, receive</li></ol><li>to lay hold of, seize, attack</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of events</i>) to overtake, surprise; to befall</li><li>to reach</li><li>to seize, stop</li><li>to occupy</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to undertake</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to succeed, follow</li></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;with genitive) to hold on by, lay hold of, catch at</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to attack</li><li>to arrest, seize</li><li>to get hold of, obtain</li><li>to reach (a place)</li><li>to touch on</li><li>to interrupt</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to object to</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>λᾰμβᾰ́νω</i> (lămbắnō, “to take, receive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lose out of mind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to neglect</li><li>to be forgetful of</li></ol></ol><p>Middle voice from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>λανθάνω</i> (lanthánō, “to cause to forget”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be whitish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>λευκαίνω</i> (leukaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>merlin</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subject to epilepsy, epileptic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιλαμβάνω</i> (epilambánō, “to lay hold of, seize, attack”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐπίληψις</i> (epílēpsis)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἐπιλαμβάνω</i> (epilambánō) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) choice</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐλέγω</i> (epĭlégō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that must be set free</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐλῡ́ω</i> (epĭlū́ō, “set free”) +&lrm; <i>-τέος</i> (-téos, suffix forming verbal adjectives of necessity).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good at solving difficulties</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐλῡ́ω</i> (epĭlū́ō, “to solve”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, “relating to, suited to, skilled in, able to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) one who resolves or explains</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐλῡ́ω</i> (epĭlū́ō, “solve, explain”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, agent noun suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) freeingly, liberatingly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ́λῠτος</i> (epĭ́lŭtos, “freeing; liberating”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>generally</i>) to set free, release, loose, untie</li><li>to solve, explain</li><li>to confute an accusation</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to manumit</li><li>to release, discharge a debtor, pay, discharge a debt</li><li>(future tense,&#32;middle voice,&#32;with passive meaning) to lose strength, give in</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon, on”) +&lrm; <i>λῡ́ω</i> (lū́ō, “loosen”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care, attention</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) cares, pains</li><li>a public charge or commission</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιμελής</i> (epimelḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to stay on, tarry or abide still</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to wait</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to remain in place, continue on</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to keep one's seat (<i>of a horseman</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to continue in a pursuit</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to abide by</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to await</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>μένω</i> (ménō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unidentified plant, perhaps <i>Epimedium alpinum</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>μήδιον</i> (mḗdion, “kind of bellflower”), because of the parasitic nature of this plant.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>confusedly; promiscuously; pell-mell</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>μῐ́ξ</i> (mĭ́x).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have dealings with</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>μῐ́σγω</i> (mĭ́sgō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defensive alliance</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>μάχη</i> (mákhē, “battle”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to mix with</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>μείγνῡμι</i> (meígnūmi), a variant of <i>μῑ́γνῡμῐ</i> (mī́gnūmĭ); compare <i>ἐπῐμῐ́σγω</i> (epĭmĭ́sgō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smile at</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>μειδάω</i> (meidáō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take care of, pay attention to, take pains over</li><li>to manage, superintend</li></ol><p><i>ἐπιμελής</i> (epimelḗs) +&lrm; <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>careful, anxious [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘about’</small>]; attentive</li><li>cared about</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>μέλω</i> (mélō, “to care”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medlar (<i>Mespilus germanica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, upon) +&lrm; <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indiction(a periodic reassessment of taxation in the Roman Empire which took place every fifteen years.)</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>Νέμεσις</i> (Némesis, “goddess of retribution”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>clouded, overcast</li><li>(<i>of wind</i>) bringing clouds</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, on, up) +&lrm; <i>νεφέλη</i> (nephélē, “cloud”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think on, think of, contrive [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to form plans</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to plan, invent</li><li>to have in mind, intend, purpose</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>νοέω</i> (noéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an addition to a law, an appendix</li><li>a New-Year’s gift</li><li>a part of a trireme</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs)</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐπινομίς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epinomis (the dialogue ascribed (perhaps spuriously) to Plato, and which forms an appendical thirteenth book to his Νόμοι)</li><li>synonym of <i>Δευτερονόμιον</i> (Deuteronómion) (Deuteronomy&#58; the fifth book of the Old Testament)</li></ol><p>Use as a proper noun of <i>ἐπῐνομῐ́ς</i> (epĭnomĭ́s, “an addition to a law”, “an appendix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) pustule which is most painful by night</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>νύξ</i> (núx, “night”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>false swearing</li><li>perjury</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>dis legomenon</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>for tomorrow, for the future (for possible etymology #1)</li><li>daily, sufficient for the day (for possible etymology #2)</li><li>essential, necessary, supersubstantial (for possible etymology #3)</li></ol></ol><p>Unknown. Ultimately either from <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>εἶμῐ</i> (eîmĭ, “go, come”) or from <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>εἰμῐ́</i> (eimĭ́, “be”). Possible etymologies include:</p><p>Not attested outside of the <i>Lord's Prayer</i> in Matthew 6:11 and Luke 11:3, in the phrase <i>τὸν ἄρτον ἡμῶν τὸν ἐπῐούσῐον</i> (tòn árton hēmôn tòn epĭoúsĭon), traditionally rendered as "our daily bread".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be present besides or in addition</li><li>to march on high ground parallel with one below</li><li>to assail in flank [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to come to someone's assistance</li><li>to come to the front of an army so as to address</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>πάρειμι</i> (páreimi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle upon, over</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>πάσσω</i> (pássō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἐπιπάσσω</i> (epipássō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>πάττω</i> (páttō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come to or upon</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on-”) +&lrm; <i>πέλομαι</i> (pélomai, “to become”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rupturewort (<i>Herniaria glabra</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>ἐπῐπᾰκτόω</i> (epĭpăktóō, “to shut close”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), because of its healing function. However, Beekes compares also the plant name <i>πεκτή</i> (pektḗ, “comfrey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be persuaded to do something; to obey</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>πείθομαι</i> (peíthomai), middle voice of <i>πείθω</i> (peíthō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) two-dimensional</li></ol><p><i>ἐπίπεδος</i> (epípedos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sail on or over (<i>a body of water</i>)</li><li>to sail on board (<i>a ship</i>)</li><li>to sail against, attack</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on, over”) +&lrm; <i>πλέω</i> (pléō, “to sail”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hernia of the omentum</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ́πλοον</i> (epĭ́ploon, “omentum”) +&lrm; <i>κήλη</i> (kḗlē, “tumour, hernia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go about, go through</li><li>to inspect (<i>e.g. of a general inspecting troops</i>)</li><li>to reconnoitre (<i>an enemy</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>πολέω</i> (poléō, “to go about, range over”), with ο lengthened to ω for metrical purposes, as <i>ἐπιπολέομαι</i> (epipoléomai) (attested once in prose) would have at least five light syllables in a row. Derivation from <i>πωλέω</i> (pōléō, “to sell”) is ruled out for semantic reasons.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>painfully, toilsomely, laboriously</li></ol><p><i>ἐπίπονος</i> (epíponos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a purple tinge</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra, “purple dye”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lean towards</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to force down</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to weigh out, allot</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέπω</i> (rhépō, “to incline, fall”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leaning towards</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιρρέπω</i> (epirrhépō, “to lean towards”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) post-Epic form of <i>ἐπισσεύω</i> (episseúō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, consider</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>σκέπτομαι</i> (sképtomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to be considered or examined</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get ready, equip, fit out</li><li>to remake, repair</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>σκευάζω</i> (skeuázō) and <i>ἐπισκευή</i> (episkeuḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>repair, restoration</li><li>material for repair, piece of equipment</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>σκευή</i> (skeuḗ)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inspect, observe, examine, regard</li><li>to watch over</li><li>to visit</li><li>to inspect, review (<i>of an army general</i>)</li><li>to consider, reflect</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to meditate</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>σκοπέω</i> (skopéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set upon, incite against, send</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to rush on or at, hasten on, speed to (<i>especially in hostile sense</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>σεύω</i> (seúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suppliant</li><li>chief, commander</li><li>president of a board or assembly, chairman</li><li>overseer, superintendent</li></ol><p><i>ἐφίσταμαι &#91;ἐπι-στα-&#93;</i> (ephístamai &#91;epi-sta-&#93;, “stand on, over, or near”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent noun suffix): literally, one who stands on, over, or near something or someone.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send to, inform by letter/message</li><li>to order, command, esp. in writing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”) + <i>στέλλω</i> (stéllō, “I prepare, send”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to surround with or as with a chaplet</li><li>(Epic,&#32;middle voice) to fill to the brim</li><li>to offer (<i>libations as an honour to the dead</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>στέφω</i> (stéphō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>science</li><li>knowledge</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπίσταμαι</i> (epístamai), from <i>ἐπί</i> (epí) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>skillfully</li></ol><p><i>ἐπιστάμενος</i> (epistámenos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>message, letter</li><li>command, commission, injunction</li><li>dying command, will (legal document)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιστέλλω</i> (epistéllō, “to send a message”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn about, turn round, wheel about</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to turn towards</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn oneself towards</li></ol><li>to turn or convert from an error, to correct, make to repent</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to repent</li></ol><li>to curve, twist</li></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice,&#32;especially in second aorist) to turn oneself around, turn about</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to go back and forth</li><li>to turn the mind towards, pay attention to</li><li>(<i>perfect passive participle</i>) earnest, vehement</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wage war on, march against</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>στρατεύω</i> (strateúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>turning this way and that way, left and right</li><li>back and forth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιστροφή</i> (epistrophḗ) +&lrm; <i>-δην</i> (-dēn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>return, comeback</li><li>attention, concentration, heed</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) return to the source of Being</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐστρέφω</i> (epĭstréphō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join on, subjoin, attach, add</li><li>to assist, promote [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to link oneself [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>συνάπτω</i> (sunáptō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>additional article to a treaty</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “additional”) +&lrm; <i>σῠνθήκη</i> (sŭnthḗkē, “treaty”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>combining; eclectic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐσῠ́νθεσῐς</i> (epĭsŭ́nthesĭs, “addition, combination”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bands, clasps or hook that fasten the greaves over the ankle, leg guards</li><li>ankle</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) on the ankles</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>σφυρόν</i> (sphurón) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>put upon as a duty, enjoin</li><li>place beside</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τᾰ́σσω</i> (tắssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>over or at a tomb, funereal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “over, on”) +&lrm; <i>τάφος</i> (táphos, “tomb”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to order, command, enjoin</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τέλλω</i> (téllō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cut upon the surface, make an incision, gash</li><li>to cut short</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to abridge, shorten, epitomize</li><li>to cut off</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be cut short, perish</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contrivance, invention</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τέχνησις</i> (tékhnēsis) and <i>ἐπιτεχνάομαι</i> (epitekhnáomai) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>provisions</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fit, suitable</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>close friend</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιτηδές</i> (epitēdés, “for this purpose”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p><p>ἐπιτηδές is from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “for”) + a noun <i>τῆδος, τᾰδος</i> (têdos, tădos) of unclear further origin + the suffix <i>-ές</i> (-és), the adverbial neuter accusative singular of <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix). One theory for <i>τῆδος</i> (têdos) derives it from <i>τήδε</i> (tḗde, “this”), a variant of <i>ἥδε</i> (hḗde), feminine of <i>ὅδε</i> (hóde).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἐπῐτήδειος</i> (epĭtḗdeios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pursuit, business</li><li>vocation, calling</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιτηδεύω</i> (epitēdeúō, “to pursue an activity, to practise a thing”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to lay, put or place upon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to set upon, turn towards</li></ol><li>to put on a covering or lid</li><li>to put to, add, grant, or give besides</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of time</i>) to add, bring on</li></ol><li>to put on as a finish</li><li>to impose or inflict a penalty</li><li>to dispatch a letter</li><li>to give a name</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to put on oneself or for oneself</li><li>to put on or to</li><li>to set oneself to, apply oneself, employ oneself in</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to make an attempt upon, attack</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>)</li></ol><li>to bring on oneself</li><li>to lay commands on</li><li>to give a name</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>τῐ́θημῐ</i> (tĭ́thēmĭ, “to put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imposition (of taxes, oracles, or divine commands, etc.)</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐτᾰ́σσω</i> (epĭtắssō) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to complete, finish, make perfect</li><li>to perform, accomplish (<i>a service</i>)</li><li>to pay in full</li><li>to be subject to</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τελέω</i> (teléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to contrive, scheme</li><li>to plot against</li><li>to plan for an emergency</li><li>to invent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τεχνάομαι</i> (tekhnáomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>suitably</li></ol><p><i>ἐπιτήδειος</i> (epitḗdeios) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἐπῐτήδειος</i> (epĭtḗdeios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἐπῐτήδειος</i> (epĭtḗdeios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pursue, practice, make a practice of, make one's business</li><li>to invent for a purpose</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be done with pains and practice, be made by art</li><li>to train (<i>an animal</i>)</li><li>to make sure to (<i>do something</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἐπιτηδές</i> (epitēdés) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to look out for</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τηρέω</i> (tēréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay a value upon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to show honor to</li><li>to raise in price</li></ol><li>(<i>of judges</i>) to lay a penalty on a person</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to object to one as blamable</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with accusative of object</i>) to censure</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to censure</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>τῑμᾰ́ω</i> (tīmắō, “to honor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cutting on the surface, incision</li><li>epitome, abridgement</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐτέμνω</i> (epĭtémnō, “cut upon the surface”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Ionic form of <i>ἐπιτρέπω</i> (epitrépō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τράπω</i> (trápō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to entrust, leave (<i>something to someone</i>), turn over (<i>to someone</i>)</li><li>to permit, allow</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to approach</li><li>run after, be greedy, be eager</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>run over a space</li><li>graze the surface</li></ol><li>to be spread over</li><li>(<i>of a musician</i>) play upon</li><li>overrun (as an army does a country)</li><li>treat lightly, treat summarily</li><li>(<i>of a country</i>) spread extend</li><li>(<i>of hounds</i>) run close after</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of people</i>) (<i>with dative</i>) follow</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τρέχω</i> (trékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a governor</li><li>in the jurisdiction of a governor</li><li>fit to be a governor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπίτροπος</i> (epítropos, “governor”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hit the mark, encounter</li><li>to light or fall upon, meet with</li><li>to attain to, reach, gain, profit or benefit by</li><li>to succeed in doing, find</li><li>to converse, talk with</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>τυγχάνω</i> (tunkhánō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hitting the mark, successful</li><li>easy to hit</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐτῠγχᾰ́νω</i> (epĭtŭnkhắnō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>luck, chance</li><li>success, advantage</li><li>undertaking</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐτῠχής</i> (epĭtŭkhḗs, “successful”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance, manifestation (especially of a divine or royal figure)</li><li>surface, visible portion</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>geometry</i>) surface</li></ol><li>appearance, pretense</li><li>fame, distinction</li><li>(<i>in New Testament of the Bible</i>) advent or manifestation (of Christ)</li><li>epiphany</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐφᾰνής</i> (epĭphănḗs, “in view”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă), from <i>ἐπιφαίνω</i> (epiphaínō, “to come into view”), from <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”) + <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “to shine, bring to light”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bestow, put, lay upon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bring, bear, carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shine, shed light upon</li><li>to show, display</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to come to light, appear</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “to shine, bring to light”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἐπιφανής</i> (epiphanḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>appearing, coming to light</li><li>visible, in view</li><li>famous, remarkable</li><li>splendid, glorious</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>-φανής</i> (-phanḗs); compare <i>ἐπιφαίνω</i> (epiphaínō, “to come into view”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>odiously, invidiously, hatefully</li><li>injuriously</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπίφθονος</i> (epíphthonos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come habitually to, visit again and again</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to haunt (<i>a person, of visions</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>φοιτάω</i> (phoitáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἐπιφοιτάω</i> (epiphoitáō)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>φοιτέω</i> (phoitéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bonus, extra, largess</li><li>gratuity; donation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐφέρω</i> (epĭphérō) +&lrm; <i>-α</i> (-a).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pour over</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>joy at the misfortune of another, spitefulness, schadenfreude, epicaricacy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐχαιρέκᾰκος</i> (epĭkhairékăkos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>κᾰκός</i> (kăkós, “evil”) + <i>ἐπιχαίρω</i> (epikhaírō, “I rejoice”), from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) + <i>χαίρω</i> (khaírō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>undertaking, attempt, especially of a military enterprise</li><li>base of operations against</li><li>(<i>in Aristotle</i>) attempted, i.e. dialectical proof</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐχειρέω</i> (epĭkheiréō, “to endeavour, attempt to”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attempt</li><li>attack</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιχειρέω</i> (epikheiréō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spend the winter at (<i>a place</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>χειμάζω</i> (kheimázō, “to pass the winter”), from <i>χεῖμα</i> (kheîma) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put one's hand on/to</li><li>to set to work at, attempt, endeavor, undertake</li><li>to set upon, attack</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>χείρ</i> (kheír) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) earthly, dwelling on Earth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make use of besides [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to have dealings with, be friends with [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>χράομαι</i> (khráomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anoint, besmear, or smear onto a surface; to apply a substance on top of something</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to anoint or smear oneself</li><li>to plaster over or coat with a substance</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to apply an ointment or substance externally (e.g. to the body or eyes)</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to use for anointing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, over”) +&lrm; <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō, “to rub, smear; anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in or of the country</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of persons</i>) native</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) of or used in the country, custom, fashion</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>χώρᾱ</i> (khṓrā, “country”, adjective suffix) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put to the vote</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to ask a question</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to vote</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ψηφίζω</i> (psēphízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go to, go against, attack, ply</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>οἴχομαι</i> (oíkhomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name, call (give a name to)</li><li>to call by name</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ὀνομάζω</i> (onomázō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epic poetry or an epic poem</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔπος</i> (épos, “speech, song”) +&lrm; <i>ποιέω</i> (poiéō, “make, do”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to attack, rush upon</li><li>to come upon</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>ὀρούω</i> (oroúō, “rush”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃rew-</i> (“to rush”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urge on, move, prompt, impel</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ὀτρῡ́νω</i> (otrū́nō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a growth on the gum; an epulis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in heaven, heavenly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>plural substantive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>masculine</i>) the gods</li><li>(<i>neuter</i>) phenomena of the heavens</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρᾰ́νῐος</i> (ourắnĭos, “heavenly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surplus</li></ol><p>From <i>έπών, ἐποῦσᾰ, ἐπόν</i> (épṓn, epoûsă, epón, “remaining”), the present participle of <i>ἔπειμῐ</i> (épeimĭ) (“remain, be left over”), + <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>check, cessation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>retention</li></ol><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) suspension of judgement</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>suspense of payment</li></ol><li>stoppage, pause</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the epoch of a star</li><li>period of vibration (in musical theory)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπέχω</i> (epékhō, “to stop, hold back”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put on one's shoulder</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπωμίς</i> (epōmís, “shoulder strap”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζομαι</i> (-ízomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the point of the shoulder</li><li>part of a woman's tunic that was fastened on the shoulder by brooches; shoulder-strap</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “epi-, on-”) +&lrm; <i>ὦμος</i> (ômos, “shoulder”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>derived name, eponym</li><li>significant name</li><li>nickname</li><li>surname</li></ol><p><i>ἐπώνυμος</i> (epṓnumos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir up, arouse, excite</li><li>to rouse and send against</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to rise against, assault, fly upon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ὄρνυμι</i> (órnumi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>versicle, scrap of poetry</li><li>epyllion, a short epic poem</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔπος</i> (épos, “word; saying proverb”) +&lrm; <i>-ύλλιον</i> (-úllion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surname</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>named in a significant manner, with a significant name</li><li>surnamed</li><li>concerning giving one's name to something</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνυμα</i> (ónuma) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lovely, pleasant</li><li>beloved, desired</li></ol><p>As if from unattested *ἐρασμός ("love") + <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), from ἔρᾰμαι (érămai, “to love”). Compare such couples, as subjective <i>δεσμός</i> (desmós) - adjective <i>δέσμιος</i> (désmios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>construed with the genitive of person or with a cognate accusative</i>) to love (<i>with sexual passion</i>), to be in love with</li><li>(<i>without sexual reference</i>) to love warmly (opposed to <i>φιλέω</i>)</li><li>(<i>construed with the genitive of thing or with an infinitive</i>) to love or desire passionately (<i>to do something</i>, when construed with an infinitive)</li><li>(<i>usually in compounds</i>) to pour forth; to vomit</li></ol><p>Uhlenbeck and others consider it cognate with Sanskrit <i>रम॑ते</i> (rámate, “to calm down; to enjoy; to have sex with”), Lithuanian <i>rimti</i> (“calm down”), Gothic <i>𐍂𐌹𐌼𐌹𐍃</i> (rimis, “tranquility”), which all come from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rém-e-ti</i> with <i>&#42;h₁rem-</i> (“to rest”). Beekes simply states “No etymology” and tentatively suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>The original stem is <i>ἐρᾰσ-</i> (erăs-), as shown by <i>ἐραστός</i> (erastós), <i>ἐραννός</i> (erannós), <i>ἐράσμιος</i> (erásmios, “lovely”) and <i>ἐραστής</i> (erastḗs, “lover”).</p><p>Uncertain. Possibly denominative from <i>&#42;ἔρα</i> (&#42;éra, “earth”) (as in <i>ἔραζε</i> (éraze), Doric Greek <i>ἔρασδε</i> (érasde, “on the ground”)). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chickpea</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ὄροβος</i> (órobos) with the Pre-Greek suffix <i>-ινθ-</i> (-inth-). Other cognates are Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arwīts</i> and Latin <i>ervum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to feed on, eat, bite off</li><li>to pluck to eat (e.g. of the lotus plant in The Odyssey)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rep-</i>. Possible cognates include Latin <i>rapiō</i>, Lithuanian <i>aprépti</i>, and Albanian <i>rjep</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to row, paddle</li><li>to speed by rowing</li><li>to put in quick motion, ply</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of a bow) to be plied, handled</li><li>to row through, traverse</li></ol><p>Denominative verb, from <i>ἐρέτης</i> (erétēs, “rower”) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) rower</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) oars</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁reh₁-t-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁reh₁-</i> (“to row”). Compare also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀩𐀲</i> (e-re-ta /⁠eretās⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cover with a roof</li><li>to crown</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to crown oneself</li><li>to wreathe, cover (as with garlands)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;erépʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rébʰ-e-ti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rebʰ-</i> (“to cover, to roof; rib”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rend, break</li></ol><p>Disputed. According to the LIV, the term derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;réḱs-dʰ-e-ti</i>, itself from the root <i>&#42;(h₁)reḱs-</i> (“to damage”), whence also perhaps Avestan <i>𐬭𐬀𐬱𐬀𐬌𐬌𐬈𐬧𐬙</i> (rašaiieṇt, “to damage”). Cheung, however, connects the Avestan term with Sanskrit <i>रक्षस्</i> (rákṣas, “harm, injury”) and considers any other cognates outside of Indo-Iranian to be uncertain. Beekes rejects the sound development of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱs-dʰ-</i> &gt; Ancient Greek <i>-χθ-</i> (-khth-) as improbable. Instead, Beekes favors a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai, “ask; tell”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to tie, join, fasten, string together</li><li>to insert</li><li>to say, speak, tell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;héřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ser-</i> (“to bind, put together”).</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéryeti</i>, <i>ye</i>-present from the root <i>&#42;werh₁-</i> (“to speak”).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>verbum</i>, Old Armenian <i>յորջորջեմ</i> (yorǰorǰem), Old English <i>word</i> (English <i>word</i>), and maybe Sanskrit <i>व्रत</i> (vratá) and Avestan <i>𐬎𐬭𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬙𐬀</i> (uruuata).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>ἐριβῶλαξ</i> (eribôlax)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) the loud-thundering one (<i>epithet of Zeus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἐρι-</i> (eri-, intensive prefix) +&lrm; <i>(γ)δοῦπος</i> ((g)doûpos, “thud”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐρίγδουπος</i> (erígdoupos, “the loud-thundering one (<i>epithet of Zeus</i>)”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to struggle; to quarrel; to challenge</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔρῐς</i> (érĭs, “strife; quarrel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) faithful, trusty</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a type of bird, most likely the robin redbreast, <i>Erithacus rubecula</i>; a robin</li><li>a mimetic talking bird, such as a parrot or raven</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Frisk suggests a connection with <i>ἔρῑθος</i> (érīthos); others propose a possible link to <i>ἐρῠθρός</i> (erŭthrós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially in the plural</i>) broken cliff, crag</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρείπω</i> (ereípō, “to fall down”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dewberry (<i>Rubus caesius</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔριφος</i> (ériphos, “young goat, kid”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lover, erastes</li><li>fan, adherent, admirer</li></ol><p><i>ἐράω</i> (eráō, “to love”) +&lrm; <i>-στης</i> (-stēs, masculine agentive suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lovely</li></ol><p><i>ἐρατός</i> (eratós) +&lrm; <i>-εινός</i> (-einós), from the root of <i>ἔραμαι</i> (éramai) and <i>ἐράω</i> (eráō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>beloved</li><li>lovely</li></ol><p><i>ἔραμαι</i> (éramai, “I love”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to work, labour</li><li>to work at, make</li><li>to do, perform</li><li>(<i>with double accusative</i>) to do something to someone; esp. do someone ill</li><li>to work a material</li><li>to earn by working</li><li>to work at, practice</li><li>to cause</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worker; workman; labourer</li><li>capstan; windlass; winch</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tool, instrument</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A case for instruments</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐργαλεῖον</i> (ergaleîon, “instrument”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work, daily labor, business</li><li>an act of working, making, building</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a working at a material</li><li>trade, commerce</li><li>a practising, exercising</li><li>a work of art, production</li></ol><li>a guild, company</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐργάζομαι</i> (ergázomai, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a workman</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐργάζομαι</i> (ergázomai, “to work”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>workshop, factory</li><li>mine, quarry</li><li>butchershop</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) tavern</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) workhouse, prison</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐργάζομαι</i> (ergázomai) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>negotiator</li><li>worker,workman,labourer</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>difficult, troublesome</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “like, full of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to prop, support</li></ol><p>Of uncertain relation outside of Greek. Sometimes compared to Latin <i>ridica</i> (“vine-prop, stake”), but De Vaan finds this connection shaky at best. Hypothetically from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁reyd-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heath (<i>Erica arborea</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;weréykā</i>, with no clear Proto-Indo-European etymon. The similarity of Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;wroikos</i> (“heather”) and Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;werˀź-</i>, <i>&#42;wirˀź-</i> (whence Lithuanian <i>vi̇̀ržis</i> (“heather”), Latvian <i>vir̂zis</i> (“heather”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vȇrsъ</i>, <i>&#42;vȇrskъ</i>) have led Beekes and Matasović to support a common non-Indo-European substrate source. It is sometimes linked to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wert-</i> (“to twist, turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of heather</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρείκη</i> (ereíkē, “heather”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruin, wreck</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρείπω</i> (ereípō, “to throw down”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to throw or dash down, tear down</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fall down</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁reyp-</i> (“to tear”). Cognate with Latin <i>rīpa</i> (“river bank”), Old Norse <i>rīfa</i> (“tear out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of chickpea</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρέβινθος</i> (erébinthos, “chickpea”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, gloomy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔρεβος</i> (érebos, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos), with <i>-εννός</i> (-ennós) from earlier <i>&#42;-es-nós</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask (request an answer)</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai)/<i>εἴρομαι</i> (eíromai), <i>ἐρωτάω</i> (erōtáō), and <i>ἐρέω</i> (eréō). Possibly related to Old Armenian <i>որոնեմ</i> (oronem, “to seek, search for”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to annoy, irritate</li></ol><p>Unknown, but possibly related to ἔρις (éris, “strife”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>murky, black, dark, obscure</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;ἐρεβνός</i> (&#42;erebnós), itself from <i>Ἔρεβος</i> (Érebos) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) oar for rowing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρέσσω</i> (eréssō, “to row”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seek, track; to search for, after</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to explore</li><li>to inquire, examine</li></ol><li>to seek (to do)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a denominative from <i>&#42;ἐρεϝ-(ε)ν-</i> (&#42;erew-(e)n-), a noun itself derived from earlier <i>&#42;ἐρεϝω</i> (&#42;erewō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rew-</i>. The term may have underwent remodeling in accordance with others marked by the ending <i>-νάω</i> (-náō). It is perhaps possible that the term is a denominative from an otherwise unattested noun <i>&#42;ἐρευνᾱ</i> (&#42;ereunā), which—perhaps along with Old Norse <i>raun</i>—may derive from <i>&#42;h₁row-neh₂-</i>.</p><p>Within Greek, perhaps related to <i>ἐρωτάω</i> (erōtáō) and <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to spit or spew out, to belch, disgorge</li><li>to discharge</li><li>to bellow, roar, shout</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewg-</i> (“to belch, roar”). Cognates include Latin <i>ērūgō</i>, Old Armenian <i>ործամ</i> (orcam), Lithuanian <i>riaugėti</i>, Russian <i>рыга́ть</i> (rygátʹ), Old English <i>rocettan</i> and Albanian <i>regj</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make red, to redden</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be red, to become red, to be made red</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;eréutʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁réwdʰ-e-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>desert, desolate/uninhabited area, wilderness</li><li>solitude, loneliness</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρῆμος</i> (erêmos, “lonely”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living in the desert</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized) desert-dweller, hermit</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρῆμος</i> (erêmos) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἔρημος</i> (érēmos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἔρημος</i> (érēmos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lay waste, devastate, bring to desolation, bring to nought</li></ol><p><i>ἐρῆμος</i> (erêmos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to keep back, restrain, check, bar</li><li>to keep away from [<i>with</i> genitive]</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic&#32;and&#32;Lyric</i>) intensifying prefix: very, greatly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with high-arching neck (<i>of horses</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρι-</i> (eri-) +&lrm; <i>αὐχήν</i> (aukhḗn)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) very fertile</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>ἐρι-</i> (eri-, intensive prefix) +&lrm; <i>βῶλᾰξ</i> (bôlăx, “clod; clump of earth”), literally “having large clods”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bee bread; sandarac (bee pollen with added honey and bee secretions, stored in brood cells)</li><li>soft parts of crustaceans, entrails of pigs</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔρῑθᾰκῐ́ς</i> (érīthăkĭ́s).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) very famous</li></ol><p><i>ἐρῐ-</i> (erĭ-) +&lrm; <i>κῦδος</i> (kûdos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild fig-tree (<i>Ficus carica</i>)</li></ol><p>Recalling Messenian <i>τράγος</i> (trágos, “wild fig-tree”) and Latin <i>caprificus</i> (“wild fig-tree”), Prellwitz compared an old word for "billy-goat", which is also found in <i>ἔριφος</i> (ériphos, “kid, young goat”). According to Chantraine and Schwyzer, however, the word is Pre-Greek. According to Blažek, it is Hurrian <i>𒄑𒂊𒊑𒅎𒁉</i> (<sup>GIŠ</sup>e-ri-im-bi /⁠erimbi⁠/), <i>𒄑𒂊𒊑𒁉</i> (<sup>GIŠ</sup>e-ri-bi /⁠eribi⁠/, “cedar”), suffixed from Akkadian <i>𒄑𒂞</i> (<sup>GIŠ</sup>ERIN /⁠erēnu, erinnu⁠/, “cedar”), from Sumerian <i>𒄑𒂞</i> (<sup>GIŠ</sup>ERIN /⁠eren⁠/).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐρινεός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erineus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epithet of Hermes of uncertain meaning&#59; possibly “the helper”</li></ol><p>Traditionally explained as <i>ἐρι-</i> (eri-) + the root of <i>ὀνίνημι</i> (onínēmi, “to help; to please”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>very strong</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρι-</i> (eri-, intensive prefix) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eager for strife or battle</li><li>argumentative; captious</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρῐ́ζω</i> (erĭ́zō, “to struggle, quarrel”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔριον</i> (érion, “wool”) and <i>ξύλον</i> (xúlon, “wood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a red pith</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρῠθρός</i> (erŭthrós, “red”) +&lrm; <i>κᾰρδῐ́ᾱ</i> (kărdĭ́ā, “heart; pith”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>madder (<i>Rubia tinctorum</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>ἐρυθρός</i> (eruthrós, “red”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>red-footed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common redshank (<i>Tringa totanus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρῠθρός</i> (erŭthrós, “red”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reddish, red</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;erutʰrós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rudʰrós</i> (“red”), from the root <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i>.</p><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀬𐀲𐀨</i> (e-ru-ta-ra). Cognates include Russian <i>рёдрый</i> (rjódryj), Sanskrit <i>रुधिर</i> (rudhirá), Latin <i>ruber</i>, Tocharian A <i>rtär</i>, Tocharian B <i>ratre</i>, and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raudaz</i> (English <i>red</i>). Note that Greek is the only branch to preserve the sound of the laryngeal <i>&#42;h₁</i> at the beginning of the word, which became <i>ε</i> (e).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of fish, probably:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>swallowtail seaperch (<i>Anthias anthias</i>), or</li><li>common pandora (<i>Pagellus erythrinus</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐρυθρός</i> (eruthrós, “reddish, red”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) rust in corn, mildew</li></ol><p>The stem of this word is also found in <i>ἐρύσιμον</i> (erúsimon, “hedge-mustard”) and <i>ἐρυσίπελας</i> (erusípelas, “erysipelas”). Traditionally considered an old <i>s</i>-enlargement of <i>ἐρυθρός</i> (eruthrós, “red”), which can be found in Latin <i>russus</i> (“red, reddish-brown”) and Lithuanian <i>raūsvas</i> (“red”). However, Furnée suggests a Pre-Greek origin, because of the alternation of <i>σ/θ</i> and the typical long <i>-ῑ-</i>: this hypothesis is supported also by Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) erysipelas (a skin disease)</li></ol><p>Medical term of unusual formation. Traditionally derived from <i>ἐρῠθρός</i> (erŭthrós, “red”) +&lrm; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pel-</i> (“to cover, wrap; skin, hide; cloth”); compare <i>πέλμᾰ</i> (pélmă, “sole of foot”) and Latin <i>pellis</i> (“a skin, hide, felt, pelt”). According to Beekes, it may also be of Pre-Greek origin, comparing the first element of the word with the first element of <i>ἐρῠσῑ́βη</i> (erŭsī́bē, “rust in plants”) and <i>ἐρῠσῐ́σκηπτρον</i> (erŭsĭ́skēptron, “kind of plant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>camelthorn (<i>Alhagi maurorum</i>)</li><li>winter thorn (<i>Faidherbia albida</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρεύθω</i> (ereúthō, “make red”) +&lrm; <i>σκῆπτρον</i> (skêptron, “staff, baton, sceptre”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rush, impulse, sweep, force in motion</li><li>rest, pause, cessation</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁reh₁s-</i> (“to flow, rush”), the same root found in Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rēsō</i> (“running, rush”) and perhaps Latin <i>rōrāriī</i> (“skirmishers”).</p><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁roh₁-weh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁reh₁-</i> (“quiet, calm”), the same root of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;rōō</i> (“calm, quiet, rest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>she who is loved, one’s (female) beloved</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a little love, a darling</li></ol><p><i>ἐρώμενος</i> (erṓmenos, “male beloved”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, suffix forming neuter diminutives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to ask about a thing</li><li>(<i>followed by a relative word</i>)</li></ol><li>to question</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in dialectic argument</i>) to elicit conclusions from the opponent by means of questioning</li></ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to beg, entreat, solicit</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ἐρεείνω</i> (ereeínō, “to ask”), <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai, “to ask”) (which it replaced in Attic), <i>ἐρευνάω</i> (ereunáō, “to search, inquire”), of uncertain origin. The hypothetical reconstructed root, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rew-</i> (“to search, inquire”), appears to be related to Icelandic <i>raun</i> (“trial, test”) and Old Armenian <i>որոնեմ</i> (oronem, “to seek, search for”) (though there exist phonetic issues with the Armenian).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) relating to questions; (grammar,&#32;of a pronoun) interrogative</li></ol><p><i>ἐρώτημᾰ</i> (erṓtēmă, “question”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>related to love, erotic</li><li>(of persons) amorous</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔρως</i> (érōs, “love”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἐρωτῐκός</i> (erōtĭkós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>redness on the skin, blush</li><li>redness (<i>in general</i>)</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἐρυθαίνω</i> (eruthaínō, “to dye red, make blush”) and <i>ἔρευθος</i> (éreuthos, “redness, flush”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma). Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewdʰ-</i> (“red”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) to restrain, control, curb, keep in check</li></ol><p>Possibly an extension of <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to draw, drag, pull”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedge-mustard, <i>Sisymbrium polyceratium</i></li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from <i>ἔρυμαι</i> (érumai, “to protect, to save”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to draw, drag, pull</li></ol><p>Possibly Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewH-</i> (“to tear, dig, burrow, gather”) (Latin <i>rudens</i> (“ship's sail”)), or related to <i>ῥυμός</i> (rhumós, “pole”), <i>ῥυμουλκέω</i> (rhumoulkéō, “to pull a towing rope”). Per LIV, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to hold back, ward off”), compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warjaną</i> (“to ward off; to thwart, to stop”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect, guard, preserve</li><li>to rescue, save, deliver</li><li>to keep something dangerous away</li><li>to thwart, curb</li><li>to set free, ransom, redeem; to pay for</li></ol><p>The term is likely related to <i>ἔρῡμαι</i> (érūmai), with both forms showcasing different ablaut grades. The linguist Lucien van Beek proposes that this term may derive from earlier Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;srῡ-</i>, itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sruH-</i>. Van Beek ultimately connects the term with the root <i>&#42;ser-</i>, whence also Latin <i>servō</i>. Alternatively, the root may be reconstructed as <i>&#42;swerh₃-</i>, whose zero-grade form <i>&#42;swṛh₃-</i> would metathesize to <i>&#42;sruh₃-</i>, whence perhaps Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;srῡ-</i>. Beekes instead connects the term with Sanskrit <i>vṛṇóti</i> and proposes a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weru-</i> or <i>&#42;uruH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>he who is loved, one’s (male) beloved; an eromenos</li><li>a catamite</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>question, something asked, or specifically&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) a question inviting a determined conclusion, a leading question</li><li>(philosophy,&#32;Stoicism) a yes-no question</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἐρωτᾰ́ω</i> (erōtắō, “ask”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, nominal suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lonely, lonesome, solitary</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of places</i>)</li><li>(<i>of persons or animals</i>)</li><li>(<i>of conditions</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) bereft of, void or destitute of, undefended</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of persons</i>) abandoned by</li><li>(<i>with no bad sense</i>) wanting, without</li></ol><li>(substantive,&#32;usually feminine,&#32;occasionally masculine) an undefended action, in which one part does not appear, and judgement goes against them by default</li><li>(<i>feminine substantive</i>) desert</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;erḗmos</i> (whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀩𐀗</i> (e-re-mo)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁réh₁mos</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁reh₁-</i> (“to rest; quiet”). The semantic development would have been "quiet (place)" > "uninhabited, lonely (place)". Note that the Mycenaean term is used for describing land.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heron (bird of the genus <i>Ardea</i>)</li></ol><p>The ending is similar to other bird names, like <i>αἰγυπιός</i> (aigupiós), <i>αἰγωλιός</i> (aigōliós) and <i>χαραδριός</i> (kharadriós). The resemblance with Latin <i>ardea</i> (“heron”) and Serbo-Croatian <i>róda</i> (“stork”) cannot be coincidental. According to Beekes, the word could be of Pre-Greek origin because of the variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>he who loves with sexual passion, a lover</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;Aeolic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>εἰς</i> (eis, “into”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Ionic, Old Attic, and Doric form of <i>εἰσ-</i> (eis-, “into”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>εἰσάντα</i> (eisánta, “right opposite”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic, Ionic, and Old Attic form of <i>εἰσακούω</i> (eisakoúō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Old Attic form of <i>εἰσβάλλω</i> (eisbállō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>εἰσβιβάζω</i> (eisbibázō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Old Attic form of <i>εἰσγράφω</i> (eisgráphō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clothes, clothing, raiment</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“to dress, clothe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat; to devour; to consume</li><li>to fret; to vex</li><li>(Koine,&#32;humorous,&#32;euphemistic) to fellate</li></ol><p>Some suggest that this form comes from the original imperative <i>&#42;ἔσθι</i> (&#42;ésthi) of <i>ἔδω</i> (édō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ed-</i> (“to eat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) goods, wares</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) good</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a person</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>good at fighting, soldierly, warlike, brave</li><li>worthy, serviceable, skilled</li><li>morally good, faithful, showing good judgement</li></ol><li>(<i>of things or abstractions</i>) good, excellent, fine, helpful, useful</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be, exist”), and thus cognate with <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “to be”), <i>οὐσία</i> (ousía, “essence”), <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós, “true”); yet another possibility is perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁su-</i>, zero-grade form of <i>&#42;h₁esu-</i> (“good”), from which <i>ἐΰς</i> (eǘs, “good”) and adverbial <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a good thing</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) good</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a person</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>good at fighting, soldierly, warlike, brave</li><li>worthy, serviceable, skilled</li><li>morally good, faithful, showing good judgement</li></ol><li>(<i>of things or abstractions</i>) good, excellent, fine, helpful, useful</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be, exist”), and thus cognate with <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “to be”), <i>οὐσία</i> (ousía, “essence”), <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós, “true”); yet another possibility is perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁su-</i>, zero-grade form of <i>&#42;h₁esu-</i> (“good”), from which <i>ἐΰς</i> (eǘs, “good”) and adverbial <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) good</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a person</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>good at fighting, soldierly, warlike, brave</li><li>worthy, serviceable, skilled</li><li>morally good, faithful, showing good judgement</li></ol><li>(<i>of things or abstractions</i>) good, excellent, fine, helpful, useful</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be, exist”), and thus cognate with <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “to be”), <i>οὐσία</i> (ousía, “essence”), <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós, “true”); yet another possibility is perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁su-</i>, zero-grade form of <i>&#42;h₁esu-</i> (“good”), from which <i>ἐΰς</i> (eǘs, “good”) and adverbial <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “well”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic and Ionic form of <i>εἰσπλέω</i> (eispléō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) priest of Artemis of Ephesus</li><li>king, prince, properly "king-bee"</li></ol><p>The ending <i>-ήν</i> is found also in <i>βαλλήν</i> (ballḗn, “king”) and <i>κηφήν</i> (kēphḗn, “drone”): this leads Beekes to think of an Anatolian and Pre-Greek origin. The meaning "king-bee" perhaps arose because of a supposed connection with <i>ἑσμός</i> (hesmós, “swarm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eagerly, impetuously</li></ol><p><i>ἐσσύμενος</i> (essúmenos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>striving, eager, desirous</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐστι</i> (esti)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sacrificing hearth</li></ol><li>house</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) platform, stand</li></ol><p>Beekes points out the absence of cognates in other Indo-European languages and considers the word to be of Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>Some have pointed out the similarity to Gothic <i>𐌰𐌶𐌲𐍉</i> (azgō), itself derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eHs-</i> (“to dry, burn, glow; hearth, ashes”), or a combination of this root and <i>&#42;dʰegʷʰ-</i> (“to burn”). The root <i>&#42;h₂eHs-</i> is known for its enlargements, be they dental or velar, as in Ancient Greek <i>ἄζω</i> (ázō) and <i>ἄσβολος</i> (ásbolos).</p><p>According to Gamkrelidze and Ivanov, a Kartvelian borrowing. Compare Proto-Kartvelian <i>&#42;c₁x-</i> (“burn, give off heat”), whence Georgian <i>სიცხე</i> (sicxe), Mingrelian <i>ჩხე</i> (čxe), Laz <i>ჩხე</i> (çxe) and Svan root <i>შხ-</i> (šx-, “burn, set fire”). The initial <i>ἐ-</i> (e-) of the Greek form is a prothetic vowel.</p><p>Parvulescu relates Ancient Greek <i>χαρά</i> (khará), thus *"[offering] in [sign of] gratitude", replacing <i>ἑστίᾱ</i> (hestíā, “fireplace, altar”) in Homer.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἐσχάρᾱ</i> (eskhárā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ἐσχάρα</i> (eskhára)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>small hearth or fireplace</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) platform, stand</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐσχάριον</i> (eskhárion)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>border, edge, remotest part</li></ol><p><i>ἔσχατος</i> (éskhatos) +&lrm; <i>-ιά</i> (-iá)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἐσχατιᾱ́</i> (eskhatiā́, “border, edge, remotest part”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to an inner circle, esoteric</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐσώτερος</i> (esṓteros) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós), from <i>ἔσω</i> (ésō, within) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, contrastive or comparative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to examine, test</li></ol><p>Denominative of <i>ἐτός</i> (etós, “true”), i.e. "to verify, check the truth of" > "to examine, test".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>really, truly, actually</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, actual, real</li><li>truthful</li></ol><p>Either a reduplicated form of <i>ἔτυμος</i> (étumos) or a compound of <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós) +&lrm; <i>ἔτυμος</i> (étumos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in accordance with reality or one's honest perception of it: true, genuine</li><li>neuter accusative singular <i>ἐτεόν</i> (eteón) as adverb, often preceded by <i>εἰ</i> (ei): truly, in fact, rightly</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in Aristophanes,&#32;interrogative) really?</li></ol><li>(<i>in Democritus</i>) feminine <i>ἐτεή</i> (eteḗ) as substantive: reality</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li><i>ἐτεῇ</i> (eteēî): in reality</li></ol></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;set-</i> (“stable, true”), and cognate with Old Armenian <i>ստոյգ</i> (stoyg) (whence terms such as <i>ստուգաբան</i> (stugaban, “truthful”)). See also <i>ὅσιος</i> (hósios, “pious, hallowed”), which may derive from an o-grade <i>&#42;sot-</i> of the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐτράπετε</i> (etrápete)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐτράπετο</i> (etrápeto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>etymology</li></ol><p><i>ἐτῠμόλογος</i> (etŭmólogos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) an etymological dictionary, an etymologicon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐτῠμολογῐκός</i> (etŭmologĭkós): as a noun, a substantivisation of its neuter forms, probably in elliptical use for <i>τὸ ἐτῠμολογῐκὸν &#91;λεξῐκόν&#93;</i> (tò etŭmologĭkòn &#91;lexĭkón&#93;); as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) belonging to etymology, etymological, etymologic</li></ol><p><i>ἐτῠμολογῐ́ᾱ</i> (etŭmologĭ́ā, “etymology”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “belonging to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>studying etymology</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>etymologist</li></ol><p><i>ἔτυμος</i> (étumos, “real, true”) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only in the negative</i>) in vain, for nothing, without reason</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>only in dictionaries?</i>) true, good</li></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;ϝετός</i> (&#42;wetós), an adverbial formation in <i>-τός</i> (-tós), like <i>ἐντός</i> (entós, “within”), with the semantic identity and origin of the component <i>&#42;ϝε</i> (&#42;we) of uncertain origin. Has been compared with Albanian <i>hut</i> (“in vain”) (&lt; <i>&#42;uto-</i>), or alternatively with Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> (“self”), presuming a semantic shift "of itself" > "without an outside cause" > "done for nothing".</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;set-u-s</i>, from <i>&#42;set-</i> (“to be stable, true”); see <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós, “true”) for more. The traditional derivation from some reflex of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be”) presents formal difficulties with the laryngeals.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>to no purpose, fruitless</li><li>useless, unprofitable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐτός</i> (etós, “without reason, in vain”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἐπί</i> (epí) (<i>used before the rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to jump at or on</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̔́λλομαι</i> (hắllomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bind on</li><li>to lay hold of</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to lay hold of, reach with the mind, attain</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to apply oneself to</li><li>(<i>perfect passive participle</i>) possessed of</li><li>to be connected with</li><li>to follow, come next</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̔́πτω</i> (hắptō, “touch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sitting by or before a place</li><li>siege, blockade</li><li>surface of a threshold</li><li>wood horsetail (<i>Equisetum sylvaticum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>ἕδρα</i> (hédra, “seat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) attraction, appeal, pull, allure, magnetism; attractive qualities; sense of feeling attracted</li><li>the action or ability to drag forward, tow, trail behind, pull in; tendency to draw towards oneself; propensity to attract, amass, assume, append; addition, annexation, affixation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐφελκύω</i> (ephelkúō, “to draw to oneself, appeal, attract”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs). <i>έφελκύω</i> is a variant of <i>ἐφέλκω</i> (ephélkō, “to draw in; bring on; drag, pull towards; amass, attract; trail after”), from <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-, “on, upon, at, near”) +&lrm; <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “to draw, drag, pull on”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to draw on, drag or trail after, lead</li><li>to bring on, bring in its train</li><li>to drink off</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to draw to oneself, attract</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to draw or pull over</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to bring on consequences</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to assume</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow up, pursue</li><li>to urge on, push to</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to follow close</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἕπομαι</i> (hépomai)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commander</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the Ephetae, a court that judged homicide cases, in ancient Athens</li><li>judge of appeals</li></ol><p><i>ἐφίημι</i> (ephíēmi, “to command”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er”, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have arrived</li><li>to reach, extend (<i>a certain distance</i>)</li><li>to occupy (<i>a space</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἥκω</i> (hḗkō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living only one day; short-lived</li><li>for a day; daily; once a day</li><li>(as with <b>φάρμακον (phármakon)</b>) acting or occurring within one day</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπ(ί)-</i> (ep(í)-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>ἡμέρᾱ</i> (hēmérā, “day”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send to</li><li>to let fly</li><li>to lay hands on</li><li>to incite</li><li>to bring, impose (<i>troubles etc. upon someone</i>)</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to enjoin upon, command</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to aim at [<i>with</i> genitive], to desire to do [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;active voice of present, imperfect, future, and 1st aorist tenses)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to set or place upon</li><li>to set over</li><li>to set up, establish, institute</li><li>to set by or near to</li><li>to stop, halt</li><li>to fix one's mind upon, attend to</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with accusative of person</i>) to arrest the attention of</li></ol></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;middle and passive voice, active voice of perfect, pluperfect, and 2nd aorist)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to stand upon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to be imposed upon</li><li>to stand on the top or surface</li></ol><li>to be set over</li><li>to stand by or near</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in a hostile sense</i>) to stand against</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;of events) to impend, be at hand</li></ol><li>to halt, stop</li><li>to fix one's mind on, give one's attention to</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ, “to stand”). Compare <i>ἐπῐ́στᾰμαι</i> (epĭ́stămai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to please, be pleasing to</li><li>to be grateful to</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ἁνδάνω</i> (handánō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dragged towards, drawn towards</li><li>pulled towards</li><li>attracted towards</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐφέλκῠσῐς</i> (ephélkŭsĭs, “attraction”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós), from <i>ἐφέλκω</i> (ephélkō, “I drag towards, I draw towards”), from <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”) + <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “I draw, I drag, I pull, I attract”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>consecutively; in order</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to light upon, discover</li><li>to invent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>εὑρίσκω</i> (heurískō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ephebic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔφηβος</i> (éphēbos, “adolescent”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>title of an official at Pergamum</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔφηβος</i> (éphēbos, “adolescent”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) the division of priests for the daily service in a temple</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) the service itself</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐφήμερος</i> (ephḗmeros, “daily”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā), from <i>ἐπ(ί)</i> (ep(í), “upon”, “on”) +&lrm; <i>ἡμέρᾱ</i> (hēmérā, “day”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diary, journal</li><li>military record, particularly as kept by Alexander the Great's staff or Julius Caesar's <i>Commentāriī dē Bellō Gallicō</i></li><li>daybook, account book</li><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) division of priests for the daily service in a temple or the service itself</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐφήμερος</i> (ephḗmeros, “daily”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, feminine adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nightmare, conceived as a throttling demon</li></ol><p>No certain etymology. Historically connected with <i>ἐφᾰ́λλομαι</i> (ephắllomai, “to throw oneself onto somebody”), but this explanation is considered folk etymological by Beekes. Leumann sustained that this word is related to <i>ἠπῐ́ᾰλος</i> (ēpĭ́ălos, “ague, fever”); if that's the case, then the word could be of Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἐφιάλτης)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ephialtes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐφῐάλτης</i> (ephĭáltēs, “nightmare; phantom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suffering from nightmare</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐφῐᾰ́λτης</i> (ephĭắltēs, “nightmare”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to oversee, watch over</li><li>to look upon, behold</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir up, rouse against</li><li>to urge</li><li>to rush upon, attack [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> accusative]</li><li>(<i>passive voice&#32;and&#32;middle voice</i>) to be eager or desire [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;middle voice) to rush furiously on, spring forward</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ὁρμάω</i> (hormáō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to blockade [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to lie by and watch</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ὁρμέω</i> (horméō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lading-plank</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐφέλκω</i> (ephélkō, “to drag”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐφῠ́περθεν</i> (ephŭ́perthen, “above, on top”)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἐφῠ́περθεν</i> (ephŭ́perthen, “above, on top of”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>above, on top</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>above, on top of</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́περθεν</i> (hŭ́perthen). By surface analysis, <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plough-handle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “I have”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) thoughtful, prudent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>part of a bridle or bit</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”) +&lrm; <i>ἡνία</i> (hēnía, “bridle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remora, suckerfish (fish of the family Echeneidae)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold, detain”) +&lrm; <i>ναῦς</i> (naûs, “ship”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holding property, holding the right of inheritance</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(poetic,&#32;said of missiles) stinging, piercing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō) +&lrm; <i>&#42;πεῦκος</i> (&#42;peûkos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs), literally “having [a] sting”. The middle element derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pewǵ-</i> (“to prick, poke, stab”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bitten by a viper</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχις</i> (ékhis, “viper”) +&lrm; <i>δηκτός</i> (dēktós), verbal adjective of <i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō, “to bite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἐχθρός</i> (ekhthrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (female) enemy or foe</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) hated, hateful</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) hating, hostile [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘to someone or something’</small>]</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (male) enemy or foe</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) an epithet for the Devil</li></ol><p>Likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eǵʰs</i> (“out”), perhaps with suffix <i>-ρός</i> (-rós). If so, then Latin <i>exter</i> (&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eǵʰs-teros</i>) is a particularly close cognate. See also <i>ἔχθος</i> (ékhthos, “hatred, enmity”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) hostilely; in a hostile manner</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the enemy</li><li>(<i>military</i>) hostile; unfriendly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐχθρός</i> (ekhthrós) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) hated, hateful</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) hating, hostile [<i>with</i> dative <i>or</i> genitive <small>‘to someone or something’</small>]</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (male) enemy or foe</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) an epithet for the Devil</li></ol><p>Likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eǵʰs</i> (“out”), perhaps with suffix <i>-ρός</i> (-rós). If so, then Latin <i>exter</i> (&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eǵʰs-teros</i>) is a particularly close cognate. See also <i>ἔχθος</i> (ékhthos, “hatred, enmity”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἐχθρός</i> (ekhthrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἐχθρός</i> (ekhthrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of broom (<i>Genista acanthoclada</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐχῖνος</i> (ekhînos, “hedgehog”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὀχυρός</i> (okhurós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἐχῠρός</i> (ekhŭrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἐχυρός</i> (ekhurós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hedgehog</li><li>sea urchin</li><li>shell of a sea urchin</li><li>jug, cup, vase</li><li>The shell of certain nuts, such as the chestnut</li><li>The third stomach of ruminants (the omasum)</li><li>The moulding atop the Doric capital</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hekʰinos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eǵʰis</i> (“hedgehog, hedgehog-like animals”). Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀑𐀜</i> (e-ki-no), Old Armenian <i>ոզնի</i> (ozni), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;igilaz</i>, Albanian <i>esh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εὐεργής</i> (euergḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) well-greaved; having good leg armor (<i>epithet of the Achaeans</i>)</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) well-spoked; having good spokes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐῠ̈-</i> (eŭ̈-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>κνημῑ́ς</i> (knēmī́s, “greave; legging”).</p><p>From <i>ἐῠ̈-</i> (eŭ̈-, “good”) +&lrm; <i>κνήμη</i> (knḗmē, “spoke of a wheel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>well-built; good to dwell in</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐῠ̈-</i> (eŭ̈-) +&lrm; <i>κτῐμενος</i> (ktĭmenos, root of <i>κτῐ́ζω</i> (ktĭ́zō)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) fair-tressed, having beautiful hair</li></ol><p><i>ἐϋ-</i> (eü-) +&lrm; <i>πλόκαμος</i> (plókamos, “braid, tress”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fair-flowing (<i>epithet of rivers</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>εὐ-</i> (eu-) +&lrm; <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εὐστᾰθής</i> (eustăthḗs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>εὐφρονέων</i> (euphronéōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stirrer, ladle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werǵ-</i> (“to make”), the same root as <i>ἔρδω</i> (érdō, “to do, perform”), <i>ῥέζω</i> (rhézō, “to do, make”) and <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine robe worn by women</li><li>sail of a boat</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>supple, well-fitting, fine (<i>of fabrics or materials like tin</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wehanós</i>, a verbal noun of <i>ἕννυμι</i> (hénnumi, “to wear, to dress”) + <i>-ανός</i> (-anós). Ultimitely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“to dress”).</p><p>Unknown on account of the unclear meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>third person</i>) himself; herself; itself; themselves</li><li>(<i>Attic&#32;and&#32;Koine</i>) used for first or second person: myself; yourself</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) of one another</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ἕο αὐτοῦ</i> (héo autoû), literally “his self”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A group of seven, <i>especially</i> a week of seven days or a group of seven years.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕβδομος</i> (hébdomos, “seventh”), from <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá, “seven”) + <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hebdmākontə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;septm̥-ḱómt-h₂</i>, from <i>&#42;septḿ̥(d)ḱomt</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सप्तति</i> (saptati), Latin <i>septuāgintā</i> and Old Armenian <i>եօթանասուն</i> (eōtʻanasun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>weekly</li><li>septenary</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑβδομᾰ́ς</i> (hebdomắs, “week; period of seven years”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one hundred and seventy times as great, one hundred and seventy-fold</li></ol><p><i>ἑβδομήκοντᾰ</i> (hebdomḗkontă, “seventy”) +&lrm; <i>καί</i> (kaí, “and”) +&lrm; <i>ἑκᾰτόν</i> (hekătón, “hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-πλᾰσῐ́ων</i> (-plăsĭ́ōn, “-fold”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) seventy-first</li></ol><p><i>ἑβδομηκοστός</i> (hebdomēkostós, “seventieth”) +&lrm; <i>πρῶτος</i> (prôtos, “first”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventieth</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hebdmēkontstós</i>, equivalent to <i>ἑβδομήκοντα</i> (hebdomḗkonta) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>far-working or originally free-working (<i>epithet of Apollo and, occasionally, Artemis</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally said to be <i>ἑκάς</i> (hekás, “far off”) +&lrm; <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “deed, work”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), but Beekes considers it to be based on ἑκών, giving "free-working" as the original meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>afar, far off</li><li>long after</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>From <i>ἕ</i> (hé) +&lrm; <i>-κᾰ́ς</i> (-kắs), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hwe</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> and one possible cognate seen in <i>द्विशस्</i> (dviśas, “two by two”). See also <i>ἀνδρᾰκᾰ́ς</i> (andrăkắs, “man by man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>each time, on each occasion</li></ol><p><i>ἑκᾰ́ς</i> (hekắs) +&lrm; <i>τότε</i> (tóte), after <i>ἕκᾰστος</i> (hékăstos, “each, every”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἑκάτερθεν</i> (hekáterthen, “on each side, on either hand”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on each side, on either hand</li></ol><p><i>ἑκάτερος</i> (hekáteros) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>each of two, each singly</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>From <i>ἑκᾰ́ς</i> (hekắs) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on either side</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>on either side of</li></ol><p><i>ἑκᾰτέρω</i> (hekătérō, “either”, dual) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἑκηβόλος</i> (hekēbólos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>a hundred times</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑκατόν</i> (hekatón) +&lrm; <i>-άκις</i> (-ákis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hundredth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑκατόν</i> (hekatón, “one hundred”) +&lrm; <i>-στός</i> (-stós), a variant of <i>-τός</i> (-tós, ordinal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) hundred-handed (said of Briareus)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑκᾰτόν</i> (hekătón, “hundred”) +&lrm; <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hecatomb, an offering of a hundred cattle</li><li>animal sacrifice in general</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑκατόν</i> (hekatón, “hundred”) + <i>βοῦς</i> (boûs, “ox”).<i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. In particular: suffix unexplained.</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>hundred</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hekətón</i>, see there for more. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱm̥tóm</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>hundred</i> (English <i>hundred</i>), Latin <i>centum</i>, Sanskrit <i>शत</i> (śatá), and Avestan <i>𐬯𐬀𐬙𐬀</i> (sata).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centurion</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑκατόν</i> (hekatón, “hundred”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”). Calque of Old Persian <i>&#42;θatapatiš</i> (“commander of a hundred men”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sharpshooter, sniper (<i>epithet of Apollo</i>)</li></ol><p>From either <i>ἑκάς</i> (hekás) or <i>ἑκών</i> (hekṓn) +&lrm; <i>βόλος</i> (bólos). Beekes thinks that the latter is correct but that Homer treats it as if it were the former.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixteen</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixteenth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕξ</i> (héx, “six”) +&lrm; <i>καί</i> (kaí, “and”) +&lrm; <i>δέκατος</i> (dékatos, “tenth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the number sixteen</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἑκκαίδεκα</i> (hekkaídeka) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs); compare <i>δυάς</i> (duás), <i>τριάς</i> (triás), <i>τετράς</i> (tetrás), <i>δεκάς</i> (dekás), <i>χιλιάς</i> (khiliás), <i>μυριάς</i> (muriás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>voluntary (done, given, or acting of one's own free will)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sixth part of various units of measure, sextarius</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕκτος</i> (héktos, “sixth”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, “masculine agentive suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>formed by or forming habit</li><li>easy, fluent</li><li>sustaining</li><li>capable of</li><li>hectic, consumptive</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕξις</i> (héxis, “possession, condition”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) father-in-law</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swéḱuros</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>willing</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weḱ-</i> (“to choose, want”). According to Beekes, the term derives from a lost present verb <i>&#42;ϝέκμι</i> (&#42;wékmi), itself perhaps from a present verb equivalent to Sanskrit <i>vaṣṭi</i> (“to will”) and Hittite (/⁠wekkmi⁠/, “to want, desire”). The LIV suggests that the Hittite and Greek forms derive from a single Narten-type present <i>&#42;wḗḱ-ti</i> whereas the Sanskrit form supposedly—according to the LIV—derives from an athematic root present <i>&#42;wéḱti</i>.</p><p>Akin to Old Armenian <i>վասն</i> (vasn, “for the sake of”) and Old Persian <i>𐎺𐏁𐎴𐎠</i> (vašnā, “thanks to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>torch; firebrand</li><li>corposant; St. Elmo's fire</li><li>wicker-basket, used to carry the sacred utensils of Artemis</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected to <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to turn; to wind”), which can account for the meaning "torch". However, Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the similar word <i>ἑλάνη</i> (helánē, “reed bundle”) and the suffix <i>-ένη</i> (-énē).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἑλένη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Helen, a goddess worshipped in Laconia and Rhodes.</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Helen, the daughter of Zeus and Leda, considered to be the most beautiful woman in the world; her abduction by Paris brought about the Trojan War.</li><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Helen</li></ol><p>Most likely from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;Ηwelénā</i>, from a pre-Hellenic or late Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Swelénā</i> (a solar deity), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swel-</i> (“to shine, warm, smoulder, burn”). In that case, related to <i>εἵλη</i> (heílē, “sunshine, sun's heat”) and unrelated to <i>ἑλένη</i> (helénē), <i>σέλας</i> (sélas) or <i>σελήνη</i> (selḗnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>helepolis, an engine for sieges</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑλε-</i> (hele-, “to take, capture”, aorist stem of <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō)) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”): literally “taking or capturing cities”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the Great Bear; Ursa Major</li><li>convolution (of a spiral shell, of the bowels, of the ear)</li><li>crack willow, <i>Salix fragilis</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ἕλῐξ</i> (hélĭx, “anything twisted, winding or spiral”); the constellation is so called from its revolving round the north celestial pole.</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀪𐀏</i> (e-ri-ka). Traditionally said to be from etymology 1, but more likely from the same source as Latin <i>salix</i>, Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;salixs</i> (Old Irish <i>sail</i>, Welsh <i>helyg</i>) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;salhô</i> (Old English <i>sealh</i>, Old Norse <i>selja</i>).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἑλίκη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Helike (a nymph)</li><li>Helike, an ancient Greek city in Achaea, drowned and destroyed in a natural disaster</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑλίκη</i> (helíkē, “willow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn round; to roll; to wind</li><li>to fold up (<i>clothing</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> through uncertain intermediaries.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gold-flower (<i>Helichrysum italicum</i>)</li><li>Mediterranean strawflower (<i>Helichrysum stoechas</i>)</li></ol><p>Like <i>ἑλειοσέλινον</i> (heleiosélinon), it is understandable as a compound of <i>ἕλειος</i> (héleios, “muddy, swampy”) +&lrm; <i>χρυσός</i> (khrusós, “gold”), with the form <i>-ἑλι-</i> being a shortening. However, Strömberg thinks it is a loan.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>musky octopus (<i>Eledone moschata</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>χελώνη</i> (khelṓnē) and <i>γογγρώνη</i> (gongrṓnē). The word has been compared with <i>δελεδώνη</i> (deledṓnē, “kind of fish”) by Beekes, who suggests a Pre-Greek etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>possibly</i>) marshland, swampland</li></ol><p>Of unknown origin. The traditional derivation from an earlier <i>&#42;ἑλε(σ)-σπίς</i> (&#42;hele(s)-spís), from <i>ἕλος</i> (hélos, “swamp”) + a putative root noun <i>&#42;σπίς</i> (&#42;spís) supposedly also found as a component in <i>σπίδιος</i> (spídios, “wide”), <i>ἀσπιδής</i> (aspidḗs, “wide”), and <i>ἀσπίς</i> (aspís, “shield”), is viewed with skepticism by Beekes. He instead suspects the second component to be that found also in <i>πῖδαξ</i> (pîdax, “source, geyser”), based on the accusative plural form <i>ἑλεσπῐ́δας</i> (helespĭ́das).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō)</li></ol><p>The present stem is a late back-formation from <i>εἵλκῠσᾰ</i> (heílkŭsă), aorist tense of <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō), and <i>εἵλκῠκᾰ</i> (heílkŭkă), perfect tense of the same.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hellebore (plant of the genus <i>Helleborus</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally taken as "eaten by deer", from <i>ἐλλός</i> (ellós, “deer”) +&lrm; <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “to eat”). However, Beekes finds this seems very doubtful, preferring a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rupturewort (<i>Herniaria glabra</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑλλέβορος</i> (helléboros, “hellebore”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak or write Greek (especially correct Greek)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be Greek</li><li>to speak common Greek (as opposed to the Attic dialect)</li></ol><li>to make Greek, Hellenize</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἕλλην</i> (Héllēn, “Greek”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herb used as a specific for worms, perhaps tansy (<i>Tanacetum vulgare</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕλμῐνς</i> (hélmĭns, “parasitic worm”) +&lrm; <i>βοτᾰ́νη</i> (botắnē, “plant, herb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>booty, prey of wild animals</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕλωρ</i> (hélōr) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eleventh</li></ol><p>From <i>εἷς</i> (heîs, “one”, masculine and neuter stem: <i>ἑν-</i>) +&lrm; <i>δέκᾰτος</i> (dékătos, “tenth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with eleven syllables</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕνδεκα</i> (héndeka, “eleven”) +&lrm; <i>συλλαβή</i> (sullabḗ, “syllable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) singular</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕν</i> (hén) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hexagonal</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) in sextile aspect</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter,&#32;geometry) hexagon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑξᾰ-</i> (hexă-) +&lrm; <i>γωνίᾱ</i> (gōníā) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) six times</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Expressed in dactylic hexameter and the Epic dialect: hexametric</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑξᾰ-</i> (hexă-, “six”) +&lrm; <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “meter”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having six strands</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;neuter) six-threaded (silk) fabric, velvet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑξα-</i> (hexa-, “six”) +&lrm; <i>μῐ́τος</i> (mĭ́tos, “thread, strand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>sixty</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a college of γελωτοποιοί at Athens</li></ol><p>From the Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweḱsḱomth₂</i>, from the earlier <i>&#42;swéḱs-dḱomt-h₂</i> (“six-ten”); see <i>&#42;swéḱs(d)ḱomt</i>. Cognate with the Latin <i>sexāgintā</i> and the Old Armenian <i>վաթսուն</i> (vatʻsun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>Forming compound words with the sense of "six"</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hexa-</li></ol></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἕξ</i> (héx, “six”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixty-four thousandth</li></ol><p><i>ἑξᾰ́κῐς</i> (hexắkĭs, “six times”) +&lrm; <i>μῡ́ρῐοι</i> (mū́rĭoi, “ten thousand”) +&lrm; <i>τετρᾰ́κῐς</i> (tetrắkĭs, “four times”) +&lrm; <i>χῑ́λῐοι</i> (khī́lĭoi, “thousand”) +&lrm; <i>-στός</i> (-stós, “-th”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six hundred</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕξ</i> (héx, “six”) +&lrm; <i>-ακόσιοι</i> (-akósioi, “hundred”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>six-winged, hexapterous</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑξᾰ-</i> (hexă-, “six”) +&lrm; <i>πτέρῠξ</i> (ptérŭx, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἑξῆς</i> (hexês)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) sixty times</li></ol><p><i>ἑξήκοντα</i> (hexḗkonta, “sixty”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́κῐς</i> (-ắkĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>consisting of sixty degrees</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑξήκοντα</i> (hexḗkonta, “sixty”) +&lrm; <i>μοῖρα</i> (moîra, “part”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the number sixty</li><li>a sixtieth part</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑξήκοντᾰ</i> (hexḗkontă, “sixty”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an arc of sixty degrees (60°)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑξηκοντάμοιρος</i> (hexēkontámoiros) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixtieth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑξήκοντα</i> (hexḗkonta, “sixty”) +&lrm; <i>-στός</i> (-stós), a variant of <i>-τός</i> (-tós, ordinal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for six days, six days long</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕξ</i> (héx, “six”) +&lrm; <i>ἦμαρ</i> (êmar, “day”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in order, one after another</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep festival or holiday</li><li>to celebrate (as a festival, etc.)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑορτή</i> (heortḗ) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>feast</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>festival, holiday</li><li>amusement, pastime</li><li>(<i>with or without τοῦ πάσχα</i>) the Passover</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wewortā́</i> and related to <i>ἐρίηρoς</i> (eríēros), <i>ἔρανος</i> (éranos), <i>ἦρα</i> (êra), with further cognates outside Hellenic unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>follower, attendant</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕπομαι</i> (hépomai, “to follow”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, <i>agentive suffix</i>).</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀤𐀲</i> (e-qe-ta).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seven</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;heptə́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;septḿ̥</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>septem</i>, Sanskrit <i>सप्त</i> (saptá), Old Armenian <i>եաւթն</i> (eawtʻn), Old English <i>seofon</i> (English <i>seven</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heptagonal</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter&#32;plural,&#32;music) certain musical instruments</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá, “seven”) +&lrm; <i>γωνίᾱ</i> (gōníā, “corner, angle”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>) seven times</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá, “seven”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́κῐς</i> (-ắkĭs, suffix for adverb of repetition).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having seven paths or tracks, seven-pathed</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá, “seven”) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “path, passage, way”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of seven oxhides</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá, “seven”) +&lrm; <i>βοείη</i> (boeíē, “oxhide”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seventeen</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>seven hundred</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá, “seven”) +&lrm; <i>-ακόσιοι</i> (-akósioi, “hundred”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holding seven cotyles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá, “seven”) +&lrm; <i>κοτύλη</i> (kotúlē, “cotyle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of seven hills, seven-hilled</li><li>(<i>epithet</i>) of Rome</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá, “seven”) +&lrm; <i>λόφος</i> (lóphos, “hill”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having seven parts, heptamerous, septempartite</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá, “seven”) +&lrm; <i>μέρος</i> (méros, “part”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bedpost</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕρμα</i> (hérma, “heap of stones; support, prop”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpretation, explanation, hermeneutics</li><li>(<i>music</i>) expression</li><li>translation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑρμηνεύω</i> (hermēneúō, “interpret”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpreter, translator</li></ol><p><i>ἑρμηνεύω</i> (hermēneúō, “to interpret”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, agent suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for interpreting</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑρμηνεύω</i> (hermēneúō) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interpreter, dragoman, expounder</li><li>matchmaker</li><li>broker, commissionaire</li></ol><p>Most plausibly assumed from pre-Carian <i>*armān</i> preceding attested Carian <i>𐊠𐊥𐊪𐊫𐊵</i> (armon, “interpreter”).</p><p>Older modern etymologies sometimes incorrectly connect it with <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō) (either sense), Latin <i>sermō</i>, folk etymology often connects it with <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês)—influence of this word cannot be excluded.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to interpret, esp. of languages</li><li>to explain</li><li>to speak clearly, expound</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑρμηνεύς</i> (hermēneús, “interpreter”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>autumn crocus (<i>Colchicum autumnale</i>)</li><li>another kind of colchicum (<i>Colchicum luteum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês, “Hermes”) +&lrm; <i>δάκτυλος</i> (dáktulos, “finger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Any animal which walks on four legs.</li><li>creeping animal, reptile, especially a snake</li><li>living being</li></ol><p>Substantivized verbal adjective of <i>ἕρπω</i> (hérpō). Compare Latin <i>serpēns</i> and Sanskrit <i>सर्प</i> (sarpá, “snake, serpent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to creep, crawl</li><li>to go, come</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of wild thyme</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕρπυλλος</i> (hérpullos, “wild thyme”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which settles (especially a swarm of bees)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>any swarm or flock</li><li>flow, stream</li></ol></ol><p>Per Beeks, derived from the root <i>&#42;(H)yeh₁-</i> (“to throw”), equivalent to <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi) +&lrm; <i>-σμός</i> (-smós). Others derive from the root <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”), equivalent to <i>ἕζομαι</i> (hézomai) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening</li><li>the west</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;wek(ʷ)speros</td></tr></table></p><p>Ellipsis of <i>ἑσπέρᾱ ὥρᾱ</i> (hespérā hṓrā, “time of evening”), from <i>ἕσπερος</i> (hésperos, “of evening, western”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἕσπερος</i> (hésperos)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;wek(ʷ)speros</td></tr></table></p><p>From <i>ἕσπερος</i> (hésperos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hearth; fireplace; altar</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) house; family</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected to Latin <i>Vesta</i> and Sanskrit <i>वस॑ति</i> (vásati, “to abide, dwell”); other theories include a connection with <i>ἐσχάρᾱ</i> (eskhárā, “hearth”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jestěja</i> (“hearth”). Beekes rejects all these theories and proposes a Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἑστία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hestia</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑστῐ́ᾱ</i> (hestĭ́ā, “hearth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἑστίᾱ</i> (hestíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the other side</li><li>on the other side</li></ol><p><i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on the other side</li><li>elsewhere</li><li>(<i>used prepositionally</i>) in another part of [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>at another time</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>one way or another</li><li>differently, otherwise</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the other side, to somewhere else</li><li>on the other side, somewhere else</li></ol><p><i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros) +&lrm; <i>-σε</i> (-se)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>euphemistic</i>) courtesan, escort, cultivated prostitute</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑταῖρος</i> (hetaîros, “companion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>companionship</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>ἑταιρῐκός</i> (hetairĭkós, “of or suitable for a companion”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἑταιρείᾱ</i> (hetaireíā): faction, club</li><li>custom of a companion</li><li>tax on courtesans</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἑταιρῐκός</i> (hetairĭkós, “of or suitable for a companion; meretricious”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or befitting a companion</li><li>of or like a companion, meretricious</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑταῖρος</i> (hetaîros, “comrade, companion”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”, adjectival suffix).</p><p>From <i>ἑταίρᾱ</i> (hetaírā, “companion; courtesan”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>meretriciously</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑταιρῐκός</i> (hetairĭkós, “of or suitable to be a companion; meretricious”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, “-ly”, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comrade, companion, partner, friend</li><li>pupil, disciple</li><li>member of a religious guild</li><li>(<i>rarely</i>) lover, member of a pair of lovers</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the guards i.e. the cavalry of the Macedonian kings</li><li>(<i>as an adjective</i>) associate of</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>From a <i>t</i>-extension of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> (“self”). Related to <i>ἔτης</i> (étēs, “clansman”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>giving strength to the opposite side (<i>i.e. the side that was previously losing, in battle</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros, “other”) +&lrm; <i>ἀλκή</i> (alkḗ, “force, strength”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heterogenous; of different kinds</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p><i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros, “other”, “another”, “different”) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs, “of a ⸻ kind”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking one thing for another, error of opinion</li><li>difference of opinion</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑτερόδοξος</i> (heteródoxos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be joined with something different, to be mismatched</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑτερόζυγος</i> (heterózugos, “unevenly yoked”), from <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros, “other, different”) + <i>ζυγόν</i> (zugón, “yoke”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>leaning to one side, uneven, sloping</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros, “different”) +&lrm; <i>κλίνω</i> (klínō, “to slope”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a difference of voice or tone</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑτερόφωνος</i> (heteróphōnos, “of different voice”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros, “other, different”) +&lrm; <i>-φωνίᾱ</i> (-phōníā, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a different kind</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>difference of name</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑτερώνῠμος</i> (heterṓnŭmos, “with different name”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heterodox (in religion)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros) +&lrm; <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) irregularly declined or inflected</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros, “other, another, different”) +&lrm; <i>κλίνω</i> (klínō, “to inflect”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one-eyed</li><li>heterochromatic (of eyes)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros, “different, other”) +&lrm; <i>ὀφθαλμός</i> (ophthalmós) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a different name</li><li>with a different denominator</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕτερος</i> (héteros, “different, other”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνυμα</i> (ónuma) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), Doric/Aeolic variant of <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma, “name”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>readily</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑτοῖμος</i> (hetoîmos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to get ready, prepare</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to cause to be prepared</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to prepare for oneself</li><li>to prepare oneself, make oneself ready</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἑτοῖμος</i> (hetoîmos, “prepared”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἕτοιμος</i> (hétoimos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἕτοιμος</i> (hétoimos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>at hand, ready, prepared</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the future</i>) sure to come, certain</li><li>(<i>of the past</i>) carried into effect, realised, feasible</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) ready, active, zealous</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the mind</i>) ready, bold</li></ol></ol><p>No etymology. Has been compared to <i>ἔτυμος</i> (étumos, “true”), <i>ἐτός</i> (etós, “true”), but this is not very convincing. Kuiper derives the word from a locative <i>&#42;ἑτοῖ</i> (&#42;hetoî) (+ a suffix <i>-μος</i> (-mos)) to an unattested <i>&#42;ἑτος</i> (&#42;hetos), an alternative form of <i>ἐτός</i> (etós, “in vain, useless”), thus "being in the useless". This is semantically strange and formally ad hoc.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>boiled, cooked in hot water</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) languid, unnerved</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕψω</i> (hépsō, “to boil, seethe”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amusement; plaything</li></ol><p>Schaffner reconstructs an Proto-Indo-European root with Germanic reflexes. According to Beekes, the presence of variations leads to a Pre-Greek origin, but Nikolaev rejects this.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ἑᾱυτοῦ</i> (heāutoû)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;Epic,&#32;reflexive) his own; her own; its own</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) his own; her own; its own</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hewós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sewós</i>, from <i>&#42;swé</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>suus</i>, Lithuanian <i>sãvas</i>. Compare <i>ὅς</i> (hós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>epsilon, the name for the fifth letter Ε (E), ε (e) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Shape from Phoenician <i>𐤄</i> (h‬). Name is Koine Greek: ἒ ψιλόν means "plain ε," in order to distinguish it from αι, which had become identical in pronunciation. Displaced earlier <i>εἶ</i> (eî).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>ah</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>exclamation of surprise or displeasure, especially before a question</i>) ha!, oho!, gak!</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with accusative of person and infinitive</i>) to let someone do something, allow</li><li>to cede, grant, yield</li><li>to agree</li><li>to advise</li><li>to neglect, disregard</li></ol><p>Possibly from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ewajō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wéh₂-ye-ti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₁weh₂-</i> (“to leave, abandon”). However, the augment of the Classical Greek preterite points to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hewajō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blood; gore</li><li>juice</li><li>spring (<i>season</i>)</li><li>prime; freshness; flower</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;éhər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésh₂r̥</i>. Cognates include Hittite <i>𒂊𒌍𒄯</i> (ēšḫar), Sanskrit <i>असृ॑ज्</i> (ásṛj), Old Armenian <i>արիւն</i> (ariwn), Latin <i>sanguis</i> and Old Latin <i>assyr</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wéhər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wésr̥</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>ver</i>, Persian بهار (bahâr), Sanskrit <i>व॒स॒न्त</i> (vasantá) and <i>उ॒षस्</i> (uṣás, “morning”), Old Norse <i>vár</i>, Old Armenian <i>գարուն</i> (garun), Old Church Slavonic <i>весна</i> (vesna).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔβαλε</i> (ébale)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ebony tree, wood</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>hbnj</i>, <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./02d176db.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="U13" alt="glyph" height="65"/><br /><img src="./6c9cd1c6.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="n" alt="glyph" height="12"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./1afca51f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z4" alt="glyph" height="35"/><br /><img src="./bd9ece2b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="M3" alt="glyph" height="22"/></td>
</tr></table>
</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to rattle; to creak</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>billy goat, buck</li></ol><p>No etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>masculine</i>) grandson</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) granddaughter</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) + the root of <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Compare Latin <i>indigenus</i> (“native, indigenous”) and Primitive Irish <i>ᚔᚅᚔᚌᚓᚅᚐ</i> (inigena, “daughter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European anchovy (<i>Engraulis encrasicolus</i>)</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Strömberg starts from an unattested verb <i>&#42;ἐγγραυλίζω</i> (&#42;engraulízō), beside <i>γρυλίζω</i> (grulízō, “to grumble”); thus this word would mean "the grumbling one". However, the variation <i>αυ/υ</i> is not convincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awaking, rousing from sleep</li><li>awaking from death, recovery</li><li>(<i>of walls</i>) raising, erection</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγείρω</i> (egeírō, “to awaken, wake up”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>timely, seasonable, opportune</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>καιρός</i> (kairós), literally “in time; in season”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bowels; entrails; intestines</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en). Compare with <i>ἔσχᾰτος</i> (éskhătos).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mark made by a branding iron, sore from burning</li><li>encaustic picture</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) ulcer in the eye</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγκαίω</i> (enkaíō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>burnt in</li><li>painted in encaustic</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) encaustic painting</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>καυστός</i> (kaustós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie in, be wrapped in (<i>clothes</i>)</li><li>to be involved in</li><li>to press upon (<i>an enemy</i>)</li><li>to be urgent</li><li>to insist upon</li><li>to be devoted to</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai). Serves as the passive of <i>ἐντίθημι</i> (entíthēmi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>accusation, charge</li><li>written complaint</li><li>standing reproach</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγκαλέω</i> (enkaléō, “to bring a charge”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inclination; slope</li><li>defeat, failure</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) displacement</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) mood of a verb</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) throwing back of the accent or change of acute accent to grave accent</li><li>(grammar,&#32;generally) inflection of derivative forms</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐγκλίνω</i> (enklínō, “to incline”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pregnant</li></ol><p>From rare <i>ἐγκῠ́ω</i> (enkŭ́ō, “to be pregnant”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in-”) +&lrm; <i>κῠ́ω</i> (kŭ́ō, “to conceive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>curved, crooked</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in, within”) +&lrm; <i>κῠρτός</i> (kŭrtós, “swelling, curved, arched”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔγχεα</i> (énkhea)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔγχεϊ</i> (énkheï)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂engʷʰ-</i> (“water-worm; eel”). Cognate with Latin <i>anguilla</i> (“eel”), Old High German <i>angar</i> (“mealworm; larva' grub”) (Modern German <i>Engerling</i>), Russian <i>у́горь</i> (úgorʹ), Lithuanian <i>ungurỹs</i>.</p><p>Influenced by <i>ἔχῐς</i> (ékhĭs, “snake”), in a same way Latin <i>anguilla</i> (“eel”) was influenced by <i>anguis</i> (“snake”), but unfortunately no Indo-European form can be reconstructed due to similar changes in other daughter languages, commonly attributed to a taboo. Cognate with Old Prussian <i>angurgis</i> and Albanian <i>ngjalë</i>. Compare Finnish <i>ankerias</i> (a Baltic loan).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) spear; spearmanship</li><li>(<i>later</i>) weapons in general: sword, arrow, etc.</li><li>(<i>metonymic</i>) armed force</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a tentative Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;h₁neǵʰ-</i> (“to stab, pierce, spear”), and linked with <i>νύσσω</i> (nússō, “to thrust, sting, pierce”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;nožь</i> (“knife”). However, according to Beekes, of uncertain origin but possibly from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) anointing, rubbing in; application of a substance by rubbing</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) scratch, slight wound, inoculation (e.g. from an animal bite or sting)</li></ol><p>From ἐγχρίω (enkhríō) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis), from <i>χρίω</i> (khríō, “to rub, smear, anoint”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) liniment, embrocation, ointment applied by rubbing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>χρῖσμα</i> (khrîsma), from <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) rubbed in; applied by rubbing; (of substances) suitable or intended for such application, especially ointments or medicated preparations</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>χρῑ́ω</i> (khrī́ō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Instillation.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) Filling or content of a vessel.</li><li>synonym of <i>ἔγχῠτος</i> (énkhŭtos)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐγχέω</i> (enkhéō, “to pour in”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, nominal suffix), from <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in, into”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔγωγε</i> (égōge)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>I at least, I for my part</li><li>(<i>in answer to a yes-no question</i>) yes, I do, I did, etc.; (<i>with negative</i>) no, I don't, I didn't, etc.</li></ol><p>An emphatic form of <i>ἐγώ</i> (egṓ, “I”); in Attic, formed by adding <i>γε</i> (ge).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bottom, foundation, base</li><li>floor, ground</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>ἕδος</i> (hédos, “seat”) + a suffix <i>-(α)φος</i> (-(a)phos), though Beekes expresses some doubt, perhaps due to the strange morphology of the suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meat, food</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔδω</i> (édō, “to eat”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ὀδόντες</i> (odóntes, “teeth”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>poetic form of <i>ἐσθῐ́ω</i> (esthĭ́ō, “to eat”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ed-</i> (“to eat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἕδνον</i> (hédnon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Lyric form of <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔτι</i> (éti) (<i>before the rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔθελε</i> (éthele)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>group of people: band, tribe, kin, nation</li><li>group of animals: flock, herd</li><li>class, order; a group of persons defined by a shared role, activity, or social function</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Probably from Ancient Greek <i>ἔθω</i> (éthō, “I am accustomed, wont (to something)”) + <i>-νος</i> (-nos), equivalent to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swedʰ-no-</i>, expanded from the reflexive pronoun Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> (“self”) + <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put, place, set”). For semantic development of senses 3 and 4, compare Hebrew <i>גּוֹי</i> (goi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>habit; custom; manner; way</li><li>disposition; temper</li><li>(<i>in the&#32;dative</i>) by habit (habitually); by custom (customarily)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swe-dʰh₁-</i> (“custom, character”), from the reflexive pronoun <i>&#42;swé</i> (“self”) + <i>&#42;dʰeh₁-</i> (“to put, place, set”), equivalent to the acc. <i>ἕ</i> (hé) of <i>οὗ</i> (hoû, “him”) + <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “to set”). Cognate to Sanskrit <i>स्वधा</i> (svadhā́, “habit, custom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>castrated</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eunuch; wether (castrated ram)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wedʰ-ri-</i> and compared with Sanskrit <i>वध्रि॑</i> (vádhri, “castrated”). However, Beekes proposes Pre-Greek origin on the grounds of the initial fluctuating vowel.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>child</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) offspring, descendants</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκγίγνομαι</i> (ekgígnomai) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>abroad, away from home</li></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lawless, unjust</li><li>avenging; (<i>as substantive</i>) avenger</li><li>(<i>law</i>) public advocate or prosecutor</li><li>(<i>law</i>) legal representative</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “outside”) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́κη</i> (dĭ́kē, “justice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>giving up, surrendering</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>giving in marriage, dowering</li><li>letting, hiring, farming out</li><li>lending money on ships or exported goods, bottomry</li><li>publication of a book</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>a publication, treatise</li><li>edition of an author’s work</li><li>translation</li></ol></ol><li>bursting forth</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>delivery</li><li>motions of the bowels</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἐκδῐ́δωμῐ</i> (ekdĭ́dōmĭ, “give up, surrender”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), from <i>ἐκ</i> (ek, “out”) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́δωμῐ</i> (dĭ́dōmĭ, “give”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>given up, betrayed</li><li>given in marriage</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>δοτός</i> (dotós, “given”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one that runs out</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) skirmisher</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos, “race, course”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A stripping, shedding</li><li>An escape</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκδύω</i> (ekdúō, “I take off”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) cutaneous eruption, an eczema</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκζέω</i> (ekzéō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) not having the earth as centre, eccentric</li><li>not occupying a cardinal point</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>κέντρον</i> (kéntron) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>selected to judge or arbitrate on a point</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκκαλέω</i> (ekkaléō) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shining forth, brightness</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) sudden development, violent onset</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλᾰ́μπω</i> (eklắmpō, “to shine or beam forth”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disappearance, abandonment</li><li>especially disappearance of a celestial body: eclipse</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλείπω</i> (ekleípō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>release, deliverance from a thing</li><li>weakening of an opponent's case</li><li>feebleness, faintness</li><li>laxity of style</li><li>(<i>music</i>) lowering of the voice through three quarter-tones</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλύω</i> (eklúō, “set free”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, “a suffix added to verb stems to form abstract nouns or nouns of action, result or process”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of missiles</i>) easy to let go, light, buoyant</li><li>let loose, unbridled, unlimited, extreme</li><li>relaxed, unnerved, exhausted, diluted, watery</li><li>(<i>of an engine</i>) deprived of force</li><li>(<i>of proof</i>) loose</li><li>(<i>adverb</i>) by being relaxed</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) curing, healing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκλύω</i> (eklúō, “let loose”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, suffix that creates perfective passive verbal adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in earlier writers</i>) terrible, violent</li><li>(<i>in later writers</i>) marvelous, wondrous</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly related to <i>πλάγια</i> (plágia, “sides, flanks”) and <i>πλάζω</i> (plázō, “to turn away from; to bewilder”) if dissimilated from *ἔκπλαγλος. According to Van Beek, all these forms belong to a substrate root <i>*(a)ᵐplaⁿk-</i>, and they are therefore not related to Latin <i>plango</i> (“to strike, beat”) and <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike”).</p><p>Alternatively, may belong with <i>πήγνυμι</i> (pḗgnumi, “to make solid, stiffen, freeze”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>consternation, terror</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>mental disturbance, passion</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐκπλήσσω</i> (ekplḗssō, “to drive out one's senses by a sudden shock”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix), from <i>ἐκ</i> (ek, “out of”) +&lrm; <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike, to smite”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) sailing out, leaving port</li><li>passage out, entrance of a harbour</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>πλόος</i> (plóos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἔκπλοος</i> (ékploos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>heard of, discovered</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκπῠνθᾰ́νομαι</i> (ekpŭnthắnomai) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drinking-cup, beaker</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκπίνω</i> (ekpínō, “to quaff, drink”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>displacement from proper place</li><li>displacement of the mind: amazement, astonishment, bewilderment</li><li>trance, ecstasy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξίστημι</i> (exístēmi, “to displace”) from <i>ἐκ</i> (ek, “out”) and <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>detailed (for special duties, of soldiers, etc.)</li><li>special, reserved</li></ol><p><i>ἐκτᾰ́σσω</i> (ektắssō, “to draw out in battle-order”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos), from <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>τᾰ́σσω</i> (tắssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>array of battle</li><li>expedition</li><li>disposal, distribution</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτᾰ́σσω</i> (ektắssō, “to draw out in battle-order”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A stretching out, extension</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) lengthening of a syllable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτείνω</i> (ekteínō, “I stretch out”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), from <i>ἐκ</i> (ek, “out”) + <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “I stretch”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>section, segment, zone</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>τμῆμᾰ</i> (tmêmă) or <i>ἐκτέμνω</i> (ektémnō) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) (from) outside</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) outside</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτός</i> (ektós) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) black hellebore, white hellebore</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chiselled, cloven</li><li>castrated</li><li>pertaining to eunuchs</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτέμνω</i> (ektémnō, “cut out”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>chiselled, cloven</li><li>castrated</li><li>pertaining to eunuchs</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτέμνω</i> (ektémnō, “cut out”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>outside</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>outside of</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐκτός</i> (ektós) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thereafter, since</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>description; ekphrasis</li></ol><p><i>ἐκφρᾰ́ζω</i> (ekphrắzō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔλαθε</i> (élathe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>olive oil, commonly used as an anointing oil</li><li>any oily substance</li><li>(<i>at Athens</i>) the oil market</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;élaiwon</i> (whence also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐁉𐀺</i> (e-ra3-wo)). See further <i>ἐλαία</i> (elaía, “olive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἴκτῑνος</i> (íktīnos, “kite”).</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Beekes, this word is hardly related to the verb <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō, “to drive, set in motion; to drive away”), thus suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>metal plate</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) general name for probes or other surgical instruments</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō, “to strike”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>red deer (<i>Cervus elaphus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;éləpʰos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éln̥bʰos</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁el-</i> (“deer”). Compare <i>ἐλλός</i> (ellós, “young deer”), Lithuanian <i>élnias</i> (“deer, stag, hart”), Russian <i>оле́нь</i> (olénʹ, “deer”), Russian <i>лось</i> (losʹ, “elk”), Old Armenian <i>եղն</i> (ełn, “hind”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wish, long [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>to long for [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to desire [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐέλδομαι</i> (eéldomai) from <i>&#42;ἐϝέλδομαι</i> (&#42;ewéldomai). Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welh₁-</i>, and thus cognate with Sanskrit <i>वृणीते</i> (vṛṇīté) and English <i>will</i>. Beekes suggests instead a PIE root h₁ueld, which would have no descendants in languages other than Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wish; longing, desire</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ἐέλδωρ</i> (eéldōr), from <i>&#42;ἐϝέλδωρ</i> (&#42;ewéldōr); related to <i>ἔλδομαι</i> (éldomai). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mourning song accompanied by a flute</li></ol><p>A Phrygian origin was considered by Hommel. However, Beekes prefers a Pre-Greek origin. Has also been compared to Old Armenian <i>եղէգն</i> (ełēgn, “reed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>argument of disproof, refutation</li><li>reproach, disgrace, dishonour</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλέγχω</i> (elénkhō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pity, mercy, compassion</li><li>object of pity</li></ol><p>Unclear. Could be related to <i>ἐλελεῦ</i> (eleleû, “woe, alas”), which is likely expressive or onomatopoeic. Beekes reconstructs a possible Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;h₁lew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coming, arrival</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Advent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλεύθω</i> (eleúthō, “to come, go”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A falling short, defect</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) The conic section: an ellipse</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) An ellipsis (the omission of a word)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) The omission of a letter, elision</li><li>An eclipse</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλλείπω</i> (elleípō, “to fall short, omit”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, verbal noun suffix), the former from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>λείπω</i> (leípō, “to leave behind”). Compare <i>ἔκλειψις</i> (ékleipsis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish in general</li><li>European sea sturgeon (<i>Acipenser sturio</i>)</li><li>a kind of snake</li></ol><p>Beekes suggest a Pre-Greek origin for this word in view of the variation <i>λ/λλ</i>. He further connects the word with <i>ἀλλοπίας</i> (allopías), hinting at a common word for a great fish.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) Hesychius gives the definition as&#58; <i>ἔλαιον</i> (élaion, “olive oil”), <i>στέαρ</i> (stéar, “fat”), <i>εὐθηνία</i> (euthēnía, “abundance”)</li></ol><p>Unknown. The word has been proposed to descend from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selp-</i> (“fat, oil”), with cognates such as Sanskrit <i>सर्पिस्</i> (sarpis) and Old English <i>sealf</i> (English <i>salve</i>). However, the lack of the expected rough breathing and the alternation between voiced and voiceless aspirate (seen in the dialectal form <i>ἔλφος</i> (élphos)) suggest that it might be from a Pre-Greek source. Some have argued that the word is related to <i>ὄλπη</i> (ólpē, “oil flask”), which may or may not affect the proposed Proto-Indo-European origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cause to hope</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hope, expect</li><li>to expect anxiously, fear</li><li>to deem, suppose</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>&#42;ϝέλπω</i> (&#42;wélpō), perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welp-</i> (“to hope, expect”), with Latin <i>volup</i> (“pleasurably”) as a cognate; see the Latin for more on the root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stock of a plough</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλύω</i> (elúō, “to roll around”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἔλυμα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elyma, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>millet (<i>Setaria italica</i>)</li><li>kind of Phrygian pipe made of boxwood</li></ol><p>Unknown. A connection with <i>ὄλυρα</i> (ólura, “spelt”) and <i>οὐλαί</i> (oulaí, “barley groats”) is unsure. Furnée prefers connecting the noun with <i>ἐλίμαρ</i> (elímar, “grain similar to millet”); however, this is very unclear. Beekes suggest a possible Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>Unknown. There appears no reason for connection with <i>εἰλύω</i> (eilúō).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἔλυμος)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Elymi; an Elymian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cover, case</li><li>sheath</li><li>cistern, reservoir</li></ol><p>From <i>εἰλύω</i> (eilúō, “to enfold, enwrap”) +&lrm; <i>-τρον</i> (-tron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔμβαλε</i> (émbale)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sauce, soup</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμβάπτω</i> (embáptō, “to dip in”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that on which one goes or steps</li><li>foot</li><li>hoof</li><li>bath</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) +&lrm; <i>βάσις</i> (básis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insertion</li><li>chased or imbossed ornament</li><li>graft</li><li>tessellated work, a mosaic</li><li>inner sole put into the shoe in winter</li><li>sluice gate</li><li>payment; fine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμβάλλω</i> (embállō, “to put in, to lay on”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wedge, peg, stopper</li><li>bolt, bar</li><li>architrave</li><li>prow of warships (rammed into opposing ships)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) The rostra, or tribune of the Latin forum</li><li>wedge-shaped order of battle (Latin <i>cuneus</i>, <i>acies cuneata</i>)</li><li>graft</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμβάλλω</i> (embállō, “to put in”), from <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) + <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to put, place, throw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(anything pointed so as to be easily thrust in) A peg, stopper; linchpin.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>geography</i>) Tongue 16 (of land); headland.</li><li>(historical,&#32;military) Brazen beak, ram.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>rostrum, tribune of the Roman forum.</li></ol><li>(<i>military</i>) Wedge-shaped order of battle.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in neuter form</i>) Half of ῥόμβος (rhómbos) of cavalry.</li></ol><li>Bolt, bar.</li><li>Graft.</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) Portico.</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐμβάλλω</i> (embállō, “to throw in; to burst in, rush in”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a young one</li><li>embryo, fetus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔμβρῠος</i> (émbrŭos, “growing”), from <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) +&lrm; <i>βρύω</i> (brúō, “swell, grow”). Compare perhaps Sanskrit <i>भ्रूण</i> (bhrūṇá, “embryo, fetus; young one”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the act of) vomiting</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμέω</i> (eméō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vomiting, sickness</li><li>vomit</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμέω</i> (eméō, “vomit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Aeolic) to be</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in any case, at all events</li><li>all the same, nevertheless</li><li>at any rate, yet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) +&lrm; <i>πᾶς</i> (pâs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with firm stand</li><li>immovably, steadily, fixedly</li><li>steadfastly, unshrinkingly</li><li>without stirring</li><li>without intermission or break, continuously</li><li>continually</li><li>with steady or undeviating course, without break or stop</li><li>without hesitation or uncertainty</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standing firmly in place, not overthrown, intact</li><li>maintained in place, steady</li><li>in place, not displaced</li><li>safe, intact</li><li>not decayed, fresh</li><li>unimpaired, retaining vigour</li><li>unimpaired in mind</li><li>well-balanced</li><li>maintained with ceaseless vigilance</li><li>secured, not liable to confiscation</li><li>assured, certain, never failing</li><li>assured, to be looked forward to with certainty</li><li>in unfailing succession</li><li>affording sure testimony, unmistakable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) + the stem of <i>πούς</i> (poús), thus literally "surefooted".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>standing firmly in place, not overthrown, intact</li><li>maintained in place, steady</li><li>in place, not displaced</li><li>safe, intact</li><li>not decayed, fresh</li><li>unimpaired, retaining vigour</li><li>unimpaired in mind</li><li>well-balanced</li><li>maintained with ceaseless vigilance</li><li>secured, not liable to confiscation</li><li>assured, certain, never failing</li><li>assured, to be looked forward to with certainty</li><li>in unfailing succession</li><li>affording sure testimony, unmistakable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμ-</i> (em-) + the stem of <i>πούς</i> (poús), thus literally "surefooted".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>experienced or practiced in a thing, acquainted with it</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>πεῖρα</i> (peîra, “a trial, experiment, attempt”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea heath (<i>Frankenia pulverulenta</i>)</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>ἔμπετρος</i> (émpetros, “growing on rocks”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἔμπᾱς</i> (émpās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔμπλαστρος</i> (émplastros, “salve, plaster”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salve or plaster</li></ol><p>From older <i>ἔμπλαστος</i> (émplastos), from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>πλαστός</i> (plastós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salve or plaster</li></ol><p>From older <i>ἔμπλαστος</i> (émplastos), from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>πλαστός</i> (plastós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is on a journey: wayfarer, traveller</li><li>merchant, trader</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “journey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) before, in front</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) before, earlier, of old</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>before</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>)</li><li>(<i>of time</i>)</li><li>(<i>of degree</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) +&lrm; <i>πρόσθεν</i> (prósthen, “before”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>appearance</li><li>outward appearance, reflection, image</li><li>setting forth, narrative, proof</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) significance, emphasis, force</li><li>moral (of a story)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφαίνω</i> (emphaínō, “I show, present”, from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō)) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in one’s mind or senses</li><li>alive</li><li>rational, intelligent</li><li>sensible, shrewd, prudent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, “-minded”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inborn, natural</li><li>planted</li><li>implanted</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐμφῠ́ω</i> (emphŭ́ō, “implant”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos), from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “bring forth, produce”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) he/she/it is</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with blood in one</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of flesh and blood</li><li>charged with blood</li></ol><li>(<i>of animals</i>) red-blooded animals</li><li>(<i>of colour</i>) blood-colour</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) vigorous</li><li>(<i>of medicine</i>) medicaments for stanching blood</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>opposite</li><li>in the presence of, before, in front of, in the sight of (<i>especially a deity, ruler, judge, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>ἀντί</i> (antí)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoils, weapons and trappings of a slain foe</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) spoil, booty</li></ol><p>Unknown. Schwyzer compared Sanskrit <i>सनर</i> (sanara), of uncertain meaning. Connection with Sanskrit <i>सनोति</i> (sanoti, “to win”) would impart this word the original meaning "gain, booty"; one would have to accept psilosis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ninth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐννέα</i> (ennéa) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lack</li><li>deficiency, defect</li><li>poverty, famine</li></ol><p><i>ἐνδεής</i> (endeḗs, “lacking”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>among ones people, at home, native</li><li>pertaining specifically to a place (i.e. internal affair)</li><li>peculiar to a people, endemic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) +&lrm; <i>δῆμος</i> (dêmos, “people”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from within</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔνδον</i> (éndon, “within”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>within, at home</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔνδον</i> (éndon, “within”) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi, “at”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in, within</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>substantive plural noun</i>) things of the home, domestics, family matters</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) in the house of....</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) strengthened form of <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;éndon</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁n̥dóm</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁n̥dó</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reputable, held in honour, esteemed</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A garment</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνδύω</i> (endúō, “to clothe”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be inside</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in-”) +&lrm; <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “be”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) An injection, a clyster, an enema</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνίημι</i> (eníēmi, “to inject”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νέρθεν</i> (nérthen)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>νέρθεν</i> (nérthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fitting, fastening</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐνείρω</i> (eneírō) (from <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō)) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔνθα</i> (éntha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) there, thither (location or movement)</li><li>(<i>demonstrative</i>) thereupon, then</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) where, whither (location or movement)</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) when</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “there”) +&lrm; <i>-θα</i> (-tha, “towards”). Cognate with Tocharian B <i>entwe</i> (“then, thereupon”).</p><p>The regular medial destination demonstrative reflex is <i>ἐνταῦθα</i> (entaûtha). Alternatively, this is the regular reflex and the other term's <i>τοῦ</i> (toû) is an extension. The distal place one is <i>ἐκεῖ</i> (ekeî), and the relative place one is <i>οὗ</i> (hoû).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>thence, from there</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “there”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”). The regular medial destination demonstrative reflex is <i>ἐντεῦθεν</i> (enteûthen). Alternatively, this is the regular reflex and the other term's <i>τοῦ</i> (toû) is an extension.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a god in one; possessed or inspired by a god</li><li>divine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>putting in, insertion</li><li>anything put in the mouth; mouthful</li><li>(<i>horticulture</i>) grafting; graft</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐντῐ́θημῐ</i> (entĭ́thēmĭ, “to put in”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>ἔνθεος</i> (éntheos, “possessed or inspired by a god”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>some</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of thinking, thought, consideration</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a thought, notion, conception</li><li>a thought, intent, design</li></ol><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) the sense of a word</li><li>(<i>in rhetoric</i>) a thought put into words, a sentence</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐννοέω</i> (ennoéō, “to consider, reflect upon”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>In arms, armed.</li><li>Containing arms or armed men. (<i>of the Trojan horse</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in possession of”) +&lrm; <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon, “implements of war”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shaking, quake</li></ol><p>Unknown. Attested in Mycenaean Greek in <i>𐀁𐀚𐀯𐀅𐀃𐀚</i> (e-ne-si-da-o-ne).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>held in, bound by</li><li>(<i>law</i>) liable to, subject to</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>liable</li><li>(rare,&#32;with preposition)</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) guilty</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐνέχω</i> (enékhō, “to hold within”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>included in a truce or treaty, in alliance with</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ally</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-) +&lrm; <i>σπονδή</i> (spondḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>putting in, insertion</li><li>placing of light-armed soldiers alternately with hoplites in the phalanx</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐντάσσω</i> (entássō, “to insert”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inscribing</li><li>tension, straining</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>exertion</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) swelling in the outline of a column</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐντείνω</i> (enteínō). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔντεα</i> (éntea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>armor, weapons, fighting gear</li><li>chain mail, corslet</li><li>furniture, appliances, tackle, instruments</li></ol><p>Unknown. Beside this word, there is <i>ἐντῡ́νω</i> (entū́nō, “to equip, get ready”): as it recalls <i>ἀρτῡ́νω</i> (artū́nō), it may have been built after this verb. A basic noun <i>&#42;ἐντύς</i> (&#42;entús) could be assumed. Connection with <i>ἄνῡμι</i> (ánūmi, “to finish, conclude”) is impossible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) guts, viscera, intestine, gut, belly, bowel, womb</li><li>bag</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive of <i>&#42;ἔντερος</i> (&#42;énteros, “inside”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁énteros</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁én</i> (whence also <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”)) + <i>&#42;-teros</i> (whence also <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, “comparative suffix”)). Cognates include Russian <i>ятро́</i> (jatró), Sanskrit <i>अन्त्र</i> (ántra, “entrails”), Old Armenian <i>ընդերք</i> (ənderkʻ, “entrails”), and Latin <i>interāneum</i> (“entrails”), whence English <i>entrails</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lighting upon, meeting with</li><li>converse, intercourse (especially sexual intercourse)</li><li>manners, behavior</li><li>petition, intercession for a person</li><li>reading, study</li></ol><p>Nominal formation in <i>-σις</i> (-sis) from <i>ἐντυγχάνω</i> (entunkhánō, “to meet with”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insects</li></ol><p>Short for <i>ἔντομα ζῷα</i> (éntoma zōîa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>insect</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive of <i>ἔντομος</i> (éntomos, “cut into pieces”) with ellipsed <i>ζῷον</i> (zōîon, “beast”)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cut in pieces, cut up</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>τόμος</i> (tómos, “slice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cut in pieces, cut up</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>τόμος</i> (tómos, “slice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔντοσθεν</i> (éntosthen)</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔντοσθεν</i> (éntosthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>within, from within</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>within</li></ol><p><i>ἐντός</i> (entós) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>endive (<i>Cichorium endivia</i>)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>intibus</i> (“endive”), itself probably from a Semitic source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>otter (<i>Lutra lutra</i>)</li><li>grass snake (<i>Natrix natrix</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is). Compare also Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;utraz</i>, Latin <i>lutra</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vydra</i> and Sanskrit <i>उद्र</i> (udra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with water in it, holding water</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>abundance of water</li></ol><li>of water, watery</li><li>living in, by water</li><li>(<i>of plants</i>) growing in water</li><li>(<i>of land</i>) in the water, submerged</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go out, come out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to come forth</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to march out with army</li><li>to come forward on the stage</li></ol><li>(<i>of time or incidents</i>) to come to an end, expire</li><li>(<i>very rare</i>) to remain after(?)</li></ol><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>εἶμι</i> (eîmi, “to go”)</p><p><i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “to be”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) to be allowed, to be possible</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ἐστί</i> (estí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>departure, leaving</li><li>a way out, exit</li><li>divorce</li><li>end, close</li><li>death</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-, “out”) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “path, road”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἔξοδος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Exodus (a book of the Bible)</li></ol><p>From Ancient Greek <i>ἔξοδος</i> (éxodos, “departure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔξοχα</i> (éxokha)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔξοχε</i> (éxokhe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>eminently, chiefly</li><li>preferably</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prominent, eminent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξέχω</i> (exékhō, “to stand out”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prominent, eminent</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξέχω</i> (exékhō, “to stand out”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>awakened out of sleep</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ-</i> (ex-) +&lrm; <i>ῠ̔́πνος</i> (hŭ́pnos, “sleep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>out, out of</li><li>outside</li><li>(<i>governs the genitive</i>) without, except</li><li>(<i>adjectival</i>) outer, external or foreign</li></ol><p>Adverb of <i>ἐξ</i> (ex, “out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from outside</li><li>outside</li><li>outward, outwardly</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>from outside</li><li>outside</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔξω</i> (éxō) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;imperfect&#32;and&#32;aorist) to seem likely [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(<i>perfect&#32;and&#32;pluperfect</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be like, to look like [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>to seem [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do’</small>], to seem likely [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>ἔοικε as interjection</i>) so it seems; probably</li></ol><li>to beseem, befit, be appropriate for [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(impersonal,&#32;ἔοικε) it is fitting; to be right, seemly, reasonable [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘to do’</small>]</li></ol></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“to become equal, be fitting”), albeit with no certain cognates outside of Greek; Lithuanian <i>vỹkti</i> (“to occur, happen”) has been compared, but the Lithuanian is more likely to derive from <i>&#42;weyk-</i> (“to overcome”), which appears to be separate from <i>&#42;weyk-</i> in the "fit" meaning. Cognate with <i>εἰκών</i> (eikṓn), <i>εἴκελος</i> (eíkelos), <i>εἰκάζω</i> (eikázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>daughter of a cousin (a kind of female first cousin once removed)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swésōr</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>स्वसृ</i> (svásṛ), Latin <i>soror</i>, Old Armenian <i>քոյր</i> (kʻoyr), Old English <i>sweostor</i> (English <i>sister</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Stressed apocopic form of <i>ἐπί</i> (epí)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncountable</i>) approval, praise</li><li>(<i>countable</i>) an expression of praise; a speech praising someone</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>αἰνέω</i> (ainéō, “tell”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>battlement, parapet</li><li>defense, protection</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπαλέξω</i> (epaléxō) (from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) + <i>ἀλέξω</i> (aléxō)) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>commander, governor of a country, prefect</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, upon”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔπαυε</i> (épaue)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔπεα</i> (épea)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔπεε</i> (épee)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔπεϊ</i> (épeï)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔπειτα</i> (épeita) (<i>used before a rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be upon, lie upon</li><li>to be imminent</li><li>to remain, be left over</li><li>to be added</li><li>to come upon</li><li>to approach</li><li>to come after, follow</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, against”) +&lrm; <i>εἰμῐ́</i> (eimĭ́, “to be”)</p><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on, against”) +&lrm; <i>εἶμῐ</i> (eîmĭ, “to go”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔπειτα</i> (épeita) (<i>used before a smooth breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of mere sequence, without any notion of cause</i>) thereupon, thereafter, then</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with the article</i>) what follows</li><li>(like <i>εἶτα</i>, with a finite verb after a participle)</li><li>(<i>in apodoses, though never at the beginning of the clause, in Homer often strengthened</i>)</li><li>(<i>after a temporal conjunction</i>) then, thereafter</li><li>(<i>after a conditional conjunction</i>) then, surely</li></ol><li>(<i>of sequence in thought, i.e. consequence or inference</i>) then, therefore</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>to begin a story</i>)</li><li>(<i>in Attic often to introduce emphatic questions</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”) + <i>εἶτα</i> (eîta).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔπεσε</i> (épese)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to slay, kill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;kʷʰékʷʰnon</i>, a reduplicated second aorist from zero-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰen-</i>. The perfect form was perhaps inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷʰegʷʰóne</i>. The same Proto-Indo-European root also gives <i>θείνω</i> (theínō, “to strike”). The labiovelar <i>&#42;gʷʰ</i> developed into a different consonant in each verb. See that entry for cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>coming to a place</li><li>foreign, immigrant</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἐπῆλθον</i> (epêlthon), aorist of <i>ἐπέρχομαι</i> (epérkhomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>movable property: utensils, implements, furniture</li></ol><p>Analyzed as <i>ἔπι-πλ-α</i> (épi-pl-a, “what has been added (as opposed to fixed possessions)”), an old formation from <i>ἐπιπέλομαι</i> (epipélomai, “to come to or on; to approach”), from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “on”) +&lrm; <i>πέλομαι</i> (pélomai, “to become”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to give, furnish, supply</li><li>to pay</li><li>to grant, allow</li><li>(<i>in perfect passive</i>) to be destined, fated</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;perh₃-</i> (“to grant”). Cognate with Old Irish <i>ernaid</i> (“grant, bestow”), Sanskrit <i>पृणाति</i> (pṛṇā́ti, “grant, bestow”), Latin <i>parō</i> (“prepare”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something spoken: speech, story, song</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>one's word, promise</li><li>word (as opposed to deed)</li><li>subject</li><li>a line of poetry</li><li>(<i>when discussing etymology or usage</i>) an individual word</li></ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) epic poetry</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ϝέπος</i> (wépos), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wékʷos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wékʷos</i>, from <i>&#42;wekʷ-</i> (“to speak”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>वचस्</i> (vácas) and Avestan <i>𐬬𐬀𐬗𐬀𐬵-</i> (vacah-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>saddlecloth, housing</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>ἔποχος</i> (épokhos, “having a good seat on horseback”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoopoe (<i>Upupa epops</i>)</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>δρῠ́οψ</i> (drŭ́ops, “woodpecker”), on an onomatopoeic basis. Parallel names exist in other languages, like Latin <i>upupa</i>, Persian پوپو (pôpô), Armenian <i>հոպոպ</i> (hopop) and Latvian <i>pupukis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a view over, as far as the view from the temple reached</li><li>oversight, supervision</li></ol><p><i>ἐπ-</i> (ep-, “over”) +&lrm; <i>ὄψῐς</i> (ópsĭs, “view”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak, say</li></ol><p>Probably a back-formation from the aorist form <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔρᾰ</i> (éră, Cypriot form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρᾰ</i> (ắră, “so; then; therefore; consequently”)) before a consonant</li><li>Cypriot form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρ</i> (ắr, alternative form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρᾰ</i> (ắră) before a consonant)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔρᾰ</i> (éră, Cypriot form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρᾰ</i> (ắră, “so; then; therefore; consequently”))</li><li>Cypriot form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρ’</i> (ắr’, apocopic form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρᾰ</i> (ắră))</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot form of <i>ᾰ̓́ρᾰ</i> (ắră, “so; then; therefore; consequently”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the ground</li></ol><p>Resembles Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;erþō</i> (“earth, ground, soil”), whence Gothic <i>𐌰𐌹𐍂𐌸𐌰</i> (airþa), Old Norse <i>jǫrð</i>, Old English <i>eorþe</i>, English <i>earth</i>, and Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;arwī</i>, whence Welsh <i>erw</i> (“field”). Possibly all come from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁er-</i> (“earth”), but the reconstruction is not certain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>with genitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to love (usually of romantic/sexual love, usually of a man)</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to passionately desire, lust after</li></ol><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to desire eagerly</li></ol><p>Uhlenbeck and others consider it cognate with Sanskrit <i>रमते</i> (rámate, “to calm down; to enjoy; to have sex with”), Lithuanian <i>rimti</i> (“calm down”), Gothic <i>𐍂𐌹𐌼𐌹𐍃</i> (rimis, “tranquility”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rem-</i>; the Greek could then be from a different extension <i>&#42;h₁er-h₂-</i> from the original root <i>&#42;h₁er-</i>. Beekes leaves the etymology unknown, so he tentatively suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>meal to which each contributed his share</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) feast, festival, banquet</li><li>loan raised by contributions for the benefit of an individual</li><li>favour, service, especially one which brings a return</li><li>permanent association apparently religious in character</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Traditionally grouped together with <i>ἔροτις</i> (érotis, “feast”) and <i>ἑορτή</i> (heortḗ, “feast, festival”), but Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin based on the opaque construction of the putative pre-form <i>&#42;ϝέρα-νος</i> (&#42;wéra-nos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of wine produced in Italy</li></ol><p>Perhaps borrowed from Latin <i>helveolus</i> (“kind of yellowish wine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔργα</i> (érga)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work, labour, task</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>work or deed of war; battle</li><li>peaceful contest</li><li>work of industry</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) tillage; tilled land</li><li>(<i>weaving</i>) woman's work; weaving</li></ol><li>deed, doing, action</li></ol><li>thing or matter</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) that which is wrought or made; work</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>result of work, profit or interest</li></ol><li>guild or company of workmen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wérgon</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wérǵom</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>weorc</i> (English <i>work</i>), Avestan <i>𐬬𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬰𐬆𐬨</i> (var<sup><small>ə</small></sup>zəm) (Persian وَرز (varz)), and Old Armenian <i>գործ</i> (gorc).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close off, enclose</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wreǵ-</i> (“to close, enclose”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>व्रज</i> (vrajá, “wattle, fence”) and Middle Irish <i>fraig</i> (“interior wall”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to close off, enclose</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wreǵ-</i> (“to close, enclose”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>व्रज</i> (vrajá, “wattle, fence”) and Middle Irish <i>fraig</i> (“interior wall”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>work, labour, task</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>work or deed of war; battle</li><li>peaceful contest</li><li>work of industry</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>agriculture</i>) tillage; tilled land</li><li>(<i>weaving</i>) woman's work; weaving</li></ol><li>deed, doing, action</li></ol><li>thing or matter</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) that which is wrought or made; work</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>result of work, profit or interest</li></ol><li>guild or company of workmen</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wérgon</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wérǵom</i>. Cognates include Old English <i>weorc</i> (English <i>work</i>), Avestan <i>𐬬𐬀𐬭𐬆𐬰𐬆𐬨</i> (var<sup><small>ə</small></sup>zəm) (Persian وَرز (varz)), and Old Armenian <i>գործ</i> (gorc).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to act (<i>as opposed to speaking or being acted on</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to do, perform</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to perform sacrifices</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to do something to someone (<i>with two accusatives</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wérďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wérǵ-ye-</i>, a <i>&#42;ye</i>-present from the root <i>&#42;werǵ-</i>, the same root as <i>ῥέζω</i> (rhézō) and <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon).</p><p>Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀺𐀽𐀁</i> (wo-ze-e), <i>𐀺𐀿</i> (wo-zo), Albanian <i>vërdhoj</i> (work without resting) etc.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness; gloom</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁régʷos</i> (“darkness”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>երեկ</i> (erek, “evening”), Sanskrit <i>रज॑स्</i> (rájas, “dimness; darkness; mist”) and Old Norse <i>røkkr</i> (“twilight”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἔρεβος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Erebus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔρεβος</i> (érebos, “darkness”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁régʷos</i>. Cognate with Old Armenian <i>երեկ</i> (erek, “evening”), Sanskrit <i>रजस्</i> (rájas, “dimness, darkness, mist”) and Old Norse <i>røkkr</i> (“twilight”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inquiry, a search</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) an exploratory operation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔρομαι</i> (éromai). Akin to Old Norse <i>raun</i> (“trial, experience”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἐρῆμος</i> (erêmos, “lonely”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day laborer; hired servant</li><li>mower; reaper; sheaf-binder</li><li>spinster; weaver; worker in wool</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) servant; minister</li></ol><p>No etymology. Being in the same semantic field of <i>δοῦλος</i> (doûlos), Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek, in view, also, of the suffix <i>-ῑθ-</i> (-īth-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wool</li></ol><p>From <i>εἶρος</i> (eîros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strife; quarrel (especially of battle)</li><li>contention; rivalry</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) political or domestic strife; discord; quarrel</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wordy wrangling, disputation, contention</li></ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἶρῐς</i> (îrĭs)</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Vasmer a cognate to Sanskrit <i>ऋ॒ति</i> (ṛtí, “assault”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ernustuz</i> (“struggle, fight”).</p><p>See <i>ἶρῐς</i> (îrĭs, “rainbow”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἔρις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eris</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔρῐς</i> (érĭs, “strife”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kid; young goat</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁er-</i>, with cognates including Latin <i>ariēs</i> (“ram”), Old Irish <i>heirp</i>, <i>erb</i> (“kid”), and Lithuanian <i>ė́ras</i> (“lamb”), but more likely of substrate origin (along with the Latin and Old Irish) in view of the strange suffixes and semantic category.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) young shoot, sprout</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wreaths worn by victors in games</li><li>scion, offspring</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i> (“to move, stir, spring”) and cognate with <i>ὄρνυμι</i> (órnumi, “to awaken, arouse”). Its form agrees with Sanskrit <i>अर्णस्</i> (arṇas, “river, stream”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ask</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;erwomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁row-neh₂-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁rew-</i> (“to ask, inquire”), which is probably related to Icelandic <i>raun</i> (“trial, test”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἔρως</i> (érōs, “love”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a sternutatory medicine, to cause sneezing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν</i> (en, “in”) +&lrm; <i>ῥίς</i> (rhís, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on, “suffix for neuter”). Functions as adjective to <i>φάρμακον</i> (phármakon, “medicine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go slowly, to limp</li><li>to wander</li><li>to go or come to harm, to disappear</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Pokorny connected the root with <i>&#42;wers-</i> (whence also Latin <i>verrō</i> (“to scrape”)), though this connection is now largely rejected due to the presence of <i>-ρρ-</i> (-rrh-) outside of Attic. The linguist Adam Cooper, however, still considers the semantic link with <i>&#42;wers-</i> preferable due to the negative connotations of the Greek word. According to Beekes, it is more probably derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wert-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;wert-</i> (“to turn”). The LIV connects the term with the same root, though it instead tentatively suggests a derivation from a thematic root present <i>&#42;wért-e-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>be in good health, goodbye, farewell (= <i>valete</i>, Latin)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen</li><li>(<i>perfect in present sense</i>) to have strength</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be eager, enthusiastic</li><li>to be in good health</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>imperative</i>) be in good health, farewell, goodbye</li></ol></ol></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Could be related to <i>ῥώομαι</i> (rhṓomai, “I dart”), as well as Sanskrit <i>सार</i> (sā́ra, “strength, power, energy”), which would hypothetically trace back to a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;serh₃-</i> (“power; to move violently”). See also English <i>raw</i>, Swedish <i>rå</i> (“raw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>be in good health, goodbye, farewell (= Latin vale)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen</li><li>(<i>perfect in present sense</i>) to have strength</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be eager, enthusiastic</li><li>to be in good health</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>imperative</i>) be in good health, farewell, goodbye</li></ol></ol></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Could be related to <i>ῥώομαι</i> (rhṓomai, “I dart”), as well as Sanskrit <i>सार</i> (sā́ra, “strength, power, energy”), which would hypothetically trace back to a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;serh₃-</i> (“power; to move violently”). See also English <i>raw</i>, Swedish <i>rå</i> (“raw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stronghold, protection</li></ol><p><i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of basket</li></ol><p>According to Lewy, from the same Semitic source of Hebrew <i>עֲרִיסָה</i> (arisá, “cradle”). Instead, it could be a variant of <i>ἄρυσος</i> (árusos, “wicker basket”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) skin</li></ol><p>It rhymes with better known <i>στέρφος</i> (stérphos, “hide, skin”), but further unclear. Güntert suggests that this word is a younger reshaping from <i>ἐρέφω</i> (eréphō, “to cover with a roof”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔρχεσθε</i> (érkhesthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go</li><li>to come</li></ol><p>Disputed.</p><p>This is a suppletive verb, with tenses derived from three different roots; see <i>εἶμῐ</i> (eîmĭ) and <i>ἦλθον</i> (êlthon) for the etymologies of the other two roots. Discussion of stems, at Notes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>love, desire (usually of a romantic/sexual nature) (often personified)</li><li>the object of such love/desire</li><li>passionate joy</li><li>a funeral wreath at Nicaea</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔραμαι</i> (éramai), <i>ἐράω</i> (eráō, “I love”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἔρως)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eros</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔρως</i> (érōs, “love”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔστι</i> (ésti) or ἔστε <i>(ésti)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>poetic form of <i>ἐσθίω</i> (esthíō, “to eat”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔστε</i> (éste, “be! pl”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>until, up to that time</li><li>while, so long as</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>up to. even to</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) until, up to</li><li>(<i>of space</i>) up to</li></ol><p>From &#42;ἔν(σ)-τε (&#42;én(s)-te), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁én</i>. The origin of the final element, -τε (-te), is unclear. It may derive from τε (te) or directly from Proto-Indo-European &#42;-kʷe. This Proto-Indo-European morpheme surfaces in Latin <i>dōnec</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be; to exist</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of events</i>) to happen</li><li>to be the case</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to live</li><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to be [<i>with</i> nominative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>(third person,&#32;impersonal) it is possible [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ehmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésmi</i> (“to be, to exist”).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>eom</i> (whence English <i>am</i>), Latin <i>sum</i>, Sanskrit <i>अस्मि॑</i> (ásmi), Old Armenian <i>եմ</i> (em), and so on. More at <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be, exist”). Not to be confused with εἶμι (eîmi) (to go).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the end</li><li>the utmost</li><li>the worst</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the uttermost</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>At one end of a continuum&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of space</i>) farthest, most remote [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘from something or someone’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>last, at the end</li><li>at the back, hindmost</li><li>lowest</li><li>highest</li><li>innermost</li></ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) last, final</li><li>(<i>of degree</i>) extreme</li><li>(<i>of quality</i>) best; worst</li></ol></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ἐξ</i> (ex, “out”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰτος</i> (-ătos, superlative suffix), possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;eǵʰs-katos</i>. Compare with <i>ἔγκατα</i> (énkata, “intestines”) and the same difficulty in <i>ἐχθός</i> (ekhthós) = <i>ἐκτός</i> (ektós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>εἴσω</i> (eísō, “into, within”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔτι</i> (éti)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a young animal; yearling</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wét-os</i>, from <i>&#42;wet-</i> (“year”). Cognate with Latin <i>vitulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of fish, perhaps the gilthead</li></ol><p>Latin <i>attilus</i> (“kind of fish”) shows a general resemblance. Farther away are Latvian <i>āte</i> and Lithuanian <i>ati̇̀s</i> (“turbot”). Strömberg rather envisages derivation from <i>ἔταλον</i> (étalon, “yearling”), while DELG calls both suggestions improbable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clansman</li><li>private person; citizen</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swet-</i>, from <i>&#42;swé</i> (“self”). Cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;svatъ</i> (“father of newlyweds”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) yet, still</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of the present</i>)</li><li>(of the past,&#32;mostly with imperfect)</li><li>(<i>of the future</i>)</li><li>(<i>with a negative</i>) no more, no longer</li></ol><li>(<i>of degree</i>) yet, still, besides, further, moreover</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>often to strengthen a comparative</i>)</li><li>(<i>with the positive</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;éti</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>et</i>, Sanskrit <i>अति</i> (ati) and Old Church Slavonic <i>отъ</i> (otŭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to suffer, undergo; endure, be patient, submit (<i>sometimes with accusative</i>)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to bring oneself to do something contrary to one's inclination or feelings, good or bad: dare, venture, have the courage, have the cruelty to do (<i>with infinitive, accusative or participle</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;telh₂-</i> (“endure”). Related to <i>τᾰ́λᾰντον</i> (tắlănton, “(measuring) scale, talent”) and possibly to <i>Ἄτλᾱς</i> (Átlās); also to Latin <i>tolerō</i> and Old English <i>þolian</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thick soup made with peas or beans</li></ol><p>Unknown. It has been connected with Scottish Gaelic <i>eitean</i> (“kernel, core”), Irish <i>eithne</i> (“kernel”), and Old Armenian <i>ունդ</i> (und, “pulse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>year</li></ol><p>From <i>ϝέτος</i> (wétos), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wétos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wétos</i> (“year”). Cognates include Latin <i>vetus</i> and Sanskrit <i>वत्स</i> (vatsá). See also Ancient Greek <i>πέρυσι</i> (pérusi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the true sense or meaning of a word according to its origins and evolution, its etymology</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive of <i>ἔτῠμος</i> (étŭmos, “true, real”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>true, real, sure</li></ol><p>From or closely related to <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós, “true”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to eat; devour</li><li>to squander</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰh₂g-</i>, zero-grade of <i>&#42;bʰeh₂g-</i> (“to divide, distribute”), with a semantic shift “*I received a share” → “*I consumed” → “I ate”. Compare also <i>βαγαῖος</i> (bagaîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) apocopic form of <i>ἔφᾰτο</i> (éphăto) (<i>used before a rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) apocopic form of <i>ἔφᾰτο</i> (éphăto) (<i>used before a smooth breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a touch, caress</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐφᾰ́πτω</i> (ephắptō, “lay hold of, touch”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sitting or seated upon</li><li>sitting by, at, or near</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>posted in support or reserve</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἐφέδρᾱ</i> (ephédrā) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>adolescent (around ages 17-20 traditionally)</li><li>young Greek soldier undergoing military training (ages 18-20)</li><li>throw of the dice</li><li>kind of cup</li><li>a womanʼs shoe</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ἐπί</i> (epí) +&lrm; <i>ἥβη</i> (hḗbē) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rivet, burr or clinch to secure a nail</li><li>(dermatology,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) rough spots which stud the face</li><li>(dermatology,&#32;pathology,&#32;usually&#32;in the plural) freckles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon, over, epi-”) +&lrm; <i>ἧλος</i> (hêlos, “a nailhead, stud”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, nominal suffix).</p><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-, “upon, over, epi-”) +&lrm; <i>ἥλῐος</i> (hḗlĭos, “the sun”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>approach, access</li><li>attack, charge, onslaught</li></ol><p><i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overseer; guardian; ruler</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ἐπῐ́ουρος</i> (epĭ́ouros), from <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔχε</i> (ékhe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔχετε</i> (ékhete)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἔχετο</i> (ékheto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ἐχθρός</i> (ekhthrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hate; hatred; enmity (intense aversion or dislike)</li><li>a hate; an odium; something hated (an object of one's hatred)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éǵʰs-tos</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁éǵʰs</i> (“out”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tos</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hatred; loathing; enmity</li><li>one's nemesis or bitter enemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hate</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>ἔχθος</i> (ékhthos, “hate”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake (traditionally held as the poisonous snake viper, but perhaps also a constrictor)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a treacherous person</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχις</i> (ékhis, “snake”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple viper's bugloss (<i>Echium plantagineum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχις</i> (ékhis, “viper”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix), because of the resemblance of the fruit to the head of a snake.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snake; adder; viper</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁égʰis</i>, with cognates including Sanskrit <i>अहि॑</i> (áhi), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬲𐬌</i> (aži) and Old Armenian <i>իժ</i> (iž).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hindrance, impediment</li><li>bulwark</li><li>holdfast, stay, prop, buttress</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have, possess, contain, own</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to keep, have charge of</li><li>(<i>with accusative of place</i>) to inhabit</li><li>(<i>of place</i>) to keep (to the left/right) of</li><li>to possess mentally, understand</li><li>to involve, admit of</li></ol><li>to hold</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hold fast, grip</li><li>(<i>of arms and clothes</i>) to bear, wear</li><li>(<i>of a woman</i>) to be pregnant</li><li>to hold a course, guide, drive, steer</li><li>to hold back, stay, check</li></ol><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to have means to do, to be able</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to have to, must</li><li>(<i>followed by a dependent clause</i>) to know</li></ol><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) there is</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to hold oneself, keep balanced</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hold fast</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to keep from</li><li>to be</li><li>(<i>with adverbs of manner</i>) to be, to happen</li><li>(<i>with εὖ and genitive of manner</i>) to be well off for something; to abound in it</li><li>(post-Homeric,&#32;with aorist participle) to keep (doing something)</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to hold oneself fast, cling closely to</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to come next to, follow closely, neighbour</li><li>to depend</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to be connected with by etymology</li></ol><li>to pertain to</li><li>to bear or hold for oneself</li><li>to maintain oneself, stand one's ground</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to repel from oneself</li></ol><li>to keep oneself back, abstain from, refrain from</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hékʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;séǵʰeti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seǵʰ-</i>. Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀃𐀏</i> (o-ka) and <i>𐀁𐀐</i> (e-ke).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ᾖ</i> (ēî)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>seventh</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hébdomos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;septm̥mós</i> (“seventh”), with medial voicing (the -bd- cluster) perhaps by influence from the following nasal. See <i>ὄγδοος</i> (ógdoos), which also shows medial voicing, and Old Church Slavonic <i>седмъ</i> (sedmŭ) (&lt; <i>&#42;sebdmъ</i> &lt; <i>&#42;septmъ</i>), with the same voicing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) dowry, bride price</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ewednon</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wed-no-m</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁wed-</i> (“dowry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a seat</li><li>the act of sitting</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hédos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sédos</i>, from <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>सदस्</i> (sadas).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat, chair, stool, bench</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>seat, abode, throne</li><li>seat, place, base</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) quarters of the sky in which omens appeared</li><li>seat of a physiological process</li><li>back (<i>of a horse on which a rider sits</i>)</li></ol><li>the act of sitting</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>sitting still, inactivity, delay</li><li>position</li><li>sitting, session</li></ol><li>seat, breech, fundament</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of animals</i>) rump</li></ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) face of a regular solid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hédrā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sedreh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”) (whence <i>ἕζομαι</i> (hézomai, “I sit”) and many others).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ἕδρᾱ</i> (hédrā)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕδρᾱ</i> (hédrā) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἕζετο</i> (hézeto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit, sit oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;heďďomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;séd-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;sed-</i>. Compare <i>ἵζω</i> (hízō, “set”). Cognate with Old English <i>sittan</i> (English <i>sit</i>), Old Norse <i>sitja</i> and Old High German <i>sizzen</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from afar</li><li>far off, far away</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑκάς</i> (hekás, “afar”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>each, every</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Possibly altered from <i>&#42;ἕκᾰστῐς</i> (&#42;hékăstĭs) because of its similarity to superlative adjectives <i>-ῐστος</i> (-ĭstos), from <i>ἑκᾰ́ς</i> (hekắs, “far off”) +&lrm; <i>τῐς</i> (tĭs, “someone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) far-shooting (<i>epithet of Apollo and Artemis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑκάς</i> (hekás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>untroubled, quiet, at one's ease</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) fallow, lying at rest (of a field)</li><li>unmoved, immobile (of trees)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The word is best taken, as per Buttmann, as <i>&#42;ϝέκᾱλος</i> (&#42;wékālos) (which is identical to <i>γέκαλον</i> (gékalon, “quiet, reposeful”)), from <i>&#42;ϝέκᾰ</i> (&#42;wékă) in <i>ἑκάεργος</i> (hekáergos), so from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weḱ-</i> (“to wish, want”). Thus, it would probably mean "at will". The semantically attractive connection with Sanskrit <i>उच्यति</i> (ucyati, “to find pleasure”) and <i>ओकस्</i> (okas, “abode, dwelling”) would be fine for <i>εὔκηλος</i> (eúkēlos), but does not explain this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sixth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕξ</i> (héx, “six”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἕλετο</i> (héleto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything twisted, winding or in a spiral</li><li>the tendril of a vine</li><li>the outer ear</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) a spiral line</li></ol><p>Traditionally taken as a nominal formation from <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to turn, wind”) (&lt; <i>&#42;ϝελ-νέ-ω</i> (&#42;wel-né-ō)) with a suffix <i>-ιξ</i> (-ix), as seen also in <i>ἧλιξ</i> (hêlix, “of the same age”), <i>χόλιξ</i> (khólix, “guts”), <i>δέλφιξ</i> (délphix, “tripod”), further from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn”). The suffix <i>-ιξ</i> (-ix) may have originated from a shortening of an unattested compound such as <i>&#42;ἑλικό-πους</i> (&#42;helikó-pous, “spiral-foot”) or <i>ἑλικό-κραιρα</i> (helikó-kraira, “spiral-horn”). Based on the presumed suffix <i>&#42;-ικ-</i> (&#42;-ik-), Beekes suggests an alternate derivation from Pre-Greek which he appears to tentatively favor over the above theory.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stock of the plough</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “to drag”) +&lrm; <i>-θρον</i> (-thron).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an open wound; an injury</li><li>a cut in a tree</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁élḱos</i> (“wound, illness, ulcer”), from the root <i>&#42;h₁elḱ-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>ulcus</i> (“ulcer”) and Sanskrit <i>अर्श॑स्</i> (árśas, “hemorrhoids”)). The non-etymological spiritus asper is usually explained by the folk-etymological influence of <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “to drag”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to drag (pull along a surface)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selk-</i> (“to pull, draw”). Cognate with Latin <i>sulcus</i> (“furrow, groove”), Old English <i>sulh</i> (“plow”), Albanian <i>hulli</i> (“furrow”). Unrelated to <i>ἕλκος</i> (hélkos, “wound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>A shrine of Zeus in Dodona</li><li>The priests <i>Helloi</i></li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;héllā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-lo-</i>, from <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”) (whence <i>ἕζομαι</i> (hézomai, “to sit”) and many others). Cognate with Latin <i>sella</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parasitic worm</li></ol><p>Uncertain. The original nominative was probably <i>ἕλμῐς</i> (hélmĭs), with ἕλμῐνς being a back-formation from the oblique forms. There appear to be two related Indo-European roots: <i>&#42;kʷr̥mis</i>, with descendants such as Sanskrit <i>कृमि</i> (kṛ́mi) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;čьrvь</i>; and <i>&#42;wr̥mis</i>, with descendants such as Latin <i>vermis</i> and Old English <i>wyrm</i> (English <i>worm</i>). However, since <i>ἕλμῐνς</i> (hélmĭns) has λ instead of a ρ, and also because of the sequence -νθ-, Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marsh-meadow</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>generally</i>) marshy ground, marsh, bog</li></ol><li>backwater</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hélos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sélos ~ &#42;séles-</i> (“marsh”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>सरस्</i> (sáras) and perhaps Old Armenian <i>եղտիւր</i> (ełtiwr).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἕλος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Helos; Elos, Laconia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕλος</i> (hélos, “marsh-meadow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) prey; spoil; plunder</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑλεῖν</i> (heleîn, “to seize”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selh₁-</i> (“to take; to grasp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) prey; spoil; plunder</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑλεῖν</i> (heleîn, “to seize”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selh₁-</i> (“to take; to grasp”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἕλωρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Helorus (a river in Sicily)</li><li>Helorum (a city in Sicily, on this river)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eleven</li></ol><p><i>εἷς</i> (heîs, “one”, masculine and neuter stem: <i>ἑν-</i>) +&lrm; <i>δέκᾰ</i> (dékă, “ten”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἕνεκᾰ</i> (hénekă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>because</li><li>that</li></ol><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀚𐀏</i> (e-ne-ka); otherwise unknown. Formerly thought a composite of <i>ἕν</i> (hén) and <i>&#42;ϝέκα</i> (&#42;wéka) (see: <i>ἑκών</i> (hekṓn)). However no trace of ϝ (/w/) is seen in the Mycenaean Linear B record. Alternatively may be connected to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁neḱ-</i> (“to carry, bring”) as in <i>ἐνεγκεῖν</i> (enenkeîn) (compare Lithuanian <i>nèšti</i>), if such a root exists.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to put clothes on, wear, dress</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;ϝέσνυμαι</i> (&#42;wésnumai), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wes-</i> (“to dress”); see there for the cognates.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to the former of two periods, last year's</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;sénos</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hénos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sénos</i> (“old”). Cognates include Latin <i>senex</i>, Sanskrit <i>सन</i> (sana), Old Armenian <i>հին</i> (hin) and Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹𐌽𐌴𐌹𐌲𐍃</i> (sineigs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>six</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hwéks</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swéḱs</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>षष्</i> (ṣaṣ), Old Armenian <i>վեց</i> (vecʻ), Latin <i>sex</i>, and Old English <i>six</i> (English <i>six</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act of having, possession</li><li>a being in a certain state, a permanent condition, produced by practice</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a state or habit of body, especially a good habit</li><li>a state or habit of mind</li><li>skill as the result of practice, experience</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>οὗ</i> (hoû, “of him/her/it; his, his own”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἕπετο</i> (hépeto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to follow, obey [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone’</small>]</li><li>to stand by, support, help</li><li>to attend, escort</li><li>to pursue</li><li>to keep pace with</li><li>to come near, approach</li><li>to cling, stick</li><li>to belong to, be inseparable from</li><li>to follow suit, agree with</li><li>to follow, result, be a consequence of</li><li>to understand</li></ol><p>Present stem from the e-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“to follow”). The nonfinite aorist stem <i>σπ-</i> (sp-) is from the zero-grade <i>&#42;skʷ-</i>, and the finite aorist stem <i>ἑσπ-</i> (hesp-) was formed by adding a rough breathing <i>῾</i> (h) to the augment <i>ἐ-</i> (e-), by analogy with the present stem. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सचते</i> (sácate), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬗𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬉</i> (hacaitē), and Latin <i>sequor</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to busy oneself with</li></ol><p>Ultimately derived from a thematic present of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sep-</i> (“to care for”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>सपति</i> (sapati, “he worships”), <i>सपर्यति</i> (saparyati, “he honors”) (see the Sanskrit root <i>सप्</i> (sap)), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬟𐬱𐬍</i> (haf-šī, “you hold (in the hand), support”), Latin <i>sepelio</i> (“to bury, inter”). Related to <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon, “tool”) and possibly also <i>ἐπητής</i> (epētḗs, “courteous”), but unrelated to <i>ἕπομαι</i> (hépomai, “to follow”) and <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon, “to said”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἕποντο</i> (héponto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἑπτά</i> (heptá)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to busy oneself with</li></ol><p>Ultimately derived from a thematic present of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sep-</i> (“to care for”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>सपति</i> (sapati, “he worships”), <i>सपर्यति</i> (saparyati, “he honors”) (see the Sanskrit root <i>सप्</i> (sap)), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬟𐬱𐬍</i> (haf-šī, “you hold (in the hand), support”), Latin <i>sepelio</i> (“to bury, inter”). Related to <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon, “tool”) and possibly also <i>ἐπητής</i> (epētḗs, “courteous”), but unrelated to <i>ἕπομαι</i> (hépomai, “to follow”) and <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon, “to said”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fence, enclosure</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>courtyard of houses</li><li>wall for defence; the defence itself</li><li>a net, a toil for birds</li></ol></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;serḱ-</i> (“to twine, mend”), like Latin <i>sarciō</i> (“to mend”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>a stabilizing entity</i>): prop; support; foundation; stay (of a ship); ballast</li><li>defense; cause</li><li>reef; rock</li><li>hill</li><li>heap of stones; cairn</li><li>pendant of the ear; earring</li><li>band; noose; coils</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wérhmə</i>, of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heap of stones; cairn</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕρμᾰ</i> (hérmă, “prop; support; stay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herpes, shingles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕρπω</i> (hérpō, “creep, crawl”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wild thyme (<i>Thymus serpyllum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕρπω</i> (hérpō, “to creep, crawl”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to move slowly, walk; crawl, creep, slink</li><li>(<i>Doric</i>) to go or come</li><li>to come, happen (<i>of things, events, etc.</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sérpeti</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सर्पति</i> (sarpati, “to glide, crawl”), and Latin <i>serpō</i> (“to creep, crawl”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dew</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) raindrops</li><li>young and tender animal</li><li>kid born in winter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁wérs-eh₂</i> (“humidity”). Close to Sanskrit <i>व॒र्षा</i> (varṣā́, “rain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or at evening</li><li>western</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evening</li><li>the evening star, Venus (shortened form of <i>ἕσπερος ἀστήρ</i> (hésperos astḗr))</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;wek(ʷ)speros</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wésperos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wek(ʷ)speros</i> (“evening”). Cognates include Latin <i>vesper</i>, Old Armenian <i>գիշեր</i> (gišer), and Old Church Slavonic <i>вєчєръ</i> (večerŭ).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἕσπερος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesperus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕσπερος</i> (hésperos, “western; the evening star”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peg at the end of the chariot pole, passing through the yoke and having a ring affixed</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Frisk thinks that the word may be an agent noun of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Doric form of <i>ἑταῖρος</i> (hetaîros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>one or the other of two</li><li>(<i>repeated at a distance</i>) either... or...</li><li>(<i>repeated consecutively</i>) one after the other</li><li>other, another, second (often of pairs)</li><li>different</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hə́teros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sḿ̥teros</i>, from the zero grade of the root <i>&#42;sem-</i> (“one", from which also comes Ancient Greek <i>εἷς</i> (heîs), "one”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-teros</i> (contrastive suffix, from which also comes Ancient Greek <i>-τερος</i> (-teros)). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>ए॒क॒त॒र</i> (ekatará), Breton <i>hanter</i>, and perhaps Old English <i>sunder</i> (English <i>asunder</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἑτοῖμος</i> (hetoîmos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to boil, seethe</li></ol><p>The verb is clearly related to Old Armenian <i>եփեմ</i> (epʻem, “to boil, cook”) (whence modern Armenian <i>եփել</i> (epʻel)). However, as Armenian <i>փ-</i> (pʻ-) can hardly represent <i>*ps-</i>, it has been suggested an Indo-European pre-form <i>&#42;sepʰ-</i>, which would have had an <i>s-</i>enlargement in Greek. However, Indo-European did not possess a phoneme <i>pʰ-</i>: this leads to a substrate origin, probably Pre-Greek according to Beekes. For this, compare <i>δέφω</i> (déphō, “to soften, knead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from morn; at earliest dawn</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕως</i> (héōs) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stale, a day old, kept till the morrow</li><li>(<i>of actions</i>) stale, out of date</li><li>(<i>of money</i>) lying without use, hoarded</li><li>(<i>of payments</i>) in arrear, not paid yet</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) coming a day too late</li><li>suffering from the effects of a debauch</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕως</i> (héōs, “until, till”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἠώς</i> (ēṓs)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(relative particle,&#32;used to express the point of time up to which an action goes)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>until; till</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>as a temporal conjunction</i>)</li><li>(with single words,&#32;mostly of adverbs of time)</li></ol><li>(<i>mostly at the beginning of a verse in Homer</i>) while; as long as</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(in Attic,&#32;with subjunctive)</li><li>(with optative,&#32;in case of repeated action)</li></ol></ol></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a time</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyeh₂wot</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>याव॑त्</i> (yā́vat, “until”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἕως)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>Ἠώς</i> (Ēṓs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eboracum; York</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Eborācum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Engyum; Gangi, Sicily</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Enceladus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐν-</i> (en-, “in”) +&lrm; <i>κέλᾰδος</i> (kélădos, “loud noise; loud clear voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Gnatia (an ancient city in Apulia)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Egnātia</i>, variant of <i>Gnātia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edessene</li></ol><p><i>Ἔδεσσα</i> (Édessa) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) <i>Edom</i>, <i>Esau</i>:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Edom; a sobriquet of Esau in the Hebrew Bible</li><li>the tribe or nation which is said to be descended from Edom; Edomites; Idumaea</li></ol></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>אֱדוֹם</i> (ʾĔḏōm).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ecbatana</li></ol><p>From Old Median; see Old Persian <i>𐏃𐎥𐎶𐎫𐎠𐎴</i> (Hangmatāna) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elateia, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐλατεύς</i> (Elateús) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyele or Elea, Magna Graecia; Velia, Campania, Italy</li></ol><p>From Doric <i>Ἐλᾱ</i> (Elā), from Phocian <i>Ϝέλᾱ</i> (Wélā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elaeus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elaeatis (a district of Epirus, Greece)</li></ol><p>A derivative of <i>Ἐλαίᾱ</i> (Elaíā), the name of the chief town, from <i>ἐλαίᾱ</i> (elaíā, “olive tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Elamite</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐλάμ</i> (Elám, “Elam”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elaphebolion: the ninth month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around March and April</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐλαφηβόλῐᾰ</i> (Elaphēbólĭă), a festival dedicated to Artemis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eleutherae, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol><p>Presumably from <i>ἐλεύθερος</i> (eleútheros, “free”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eleusis; Eleusina, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Elephenor, son of Chalcodon and one of the Abantes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elephantine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλέφας</i> (eléphas, “elephant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eleutherna, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eliezer, mentioned in Luke's genealogy of Jesus.</li></ol><p>From the Hebrew אליעזר.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Christianity,&#32;Koine) Elizabeth (mother of John the Baptist)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֱלִישֶׁבַע</i> (ʾĕlîšeḇaʿ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elisha (Biblical prophet)</li><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Elisha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אֱלִישָׁע</i> (ʾĕlîšāʿ, literally “My God is salvation”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ellopia, Euboea, Greece</li></ol><p>after Ellops, the mythological son of Ion.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Elpenor, the youngest comrade of Odysseus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐλπίς</i> (elpís, “hope”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elymia, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elymais (an ancient state on the Persian Gulf, in present-day Khuzestan Province, Iran)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Emesene</li></ol><p><i>Ἔμεσα</i> (Émesa) +&lrm; <i>-ηνός</i> (-ēnós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἐμεσηνός</i> (Emesēnós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἐμεσηνός</i> (Emesēnós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Emmanuel, a term in Isaiah 7:14, also used by Christians to refer to Jesus as a fulfillment of the Isaiah's prophecy.</li></ol><p>From the Hebrew phrase עמנו אל (“God is with us”) from עם (“with”) with the 1st person plural ending ־נו, and אל (“God”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἐμμαοῦς</i> (Emmaoûs, “Emmaus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Emmaus, a town in Judea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Empedocles</li><li>in particular, the 5th century <small>BCE</small> philosopher Empedocles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔμπεδος</i> (émpedos, “steadfast”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês): perhaps “enduringly famous”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Enispe, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Endymion</li></ol><p>Traditionally linked to <i>ἐνδύω</i> (endúō, “to dive into, to clothe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Venetia (a region in northeastern Italy; home of the Veneti)</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) Venice (a port city in northeastern Italy; former capital of an independent republic)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐνετοί</i> (Enetoí, “Eneti; Veneti; Venetians”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) Enetic, of or related to the Eneti of Paphlagonia</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Enetic, of or related to the Eneti of Illyria</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) Venetic, of or related to the Veneti of northeastern Italy</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) Venetian, of or related to Venice</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐνετοί</i> (Enetoí, “Eneti; Veneti; Venetian”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “-ic: <i>forming adjectives</i>”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) One of the Eneti people of ancient Paphlagonia</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) One of the Eneti people of ancient Thrace</li><li>(<i>historical</i>) One of the Veneti people of ancient northeastern Italy</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) A Venetian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Enipeus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Enyo (goddess of war, companion of Ares, and sister to Polemos&#59; identified with the Roman goddess <i>Bellōna</i>)</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Enyo (one of the Graeae, daughter of Phorcys and Ceto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Exaenetus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐξ</i> (ex, “out”) +&lrm; <i>αἰνετός</i> (ainetós, “praiseworthy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eordi, the inhabitants of Eordaea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epidamnus, later called Dyrrhachium and Durrës, Albania</li></ol><p>May be related to or derived from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;dami</i> (“cub”), whence Albanian <i>dem</i>, as proposed by linguist Eqrem Çabej.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epidaurus (an ancient town on the Argolid Peninsula, Greece, famous for its theatre)</li></ol><p>According to Strabo, the city was originally named <i>Ἐπίταυρος</i> (Epítauros) under the Ionians, before taking the name <i>Ἐπίκαρος</i> (Epíkaros) under the Carians, before finally becoming <i>Ἐπίδαυρος</i> under the Dorians. Compare the individual elements <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “upon”), <i>ταῦρος</i> (taûros, “bull”), <i>Καρία</i> (Karía, “Carian”), and <i>Δωριεύς</i> (Dōrieús), <i>Δωριεῖς</i> (Dōrieîs, “Dorians”). Also compare the mythological hero of the same name.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Epictetus</li><li>in particular, the Stoic philosopher Epictetus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπίκτητος</i> (epíktētos), from <i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>κτάομαι</i> (ktáomai) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Epicharmus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Epaminondas</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπί</i> (epí) + <i>ἀμείνων</i> (ameínōn) + <i>-ίδᾱς</i> (-ídās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epeii (tribe of Elis)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epeius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epidamnian, resident of Epidamnos</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epidamnian, pertaining to Epidamnos</li></ol><p><i>Ἐπίδαμνος</i> (Epídamnos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epidaurian, from Epidaurus</li></ol><p><i>Ἐπίδαυρος</i> (Epídauros) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Epimenides</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epimetheus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐπιμηθής</i> (epimēthḗs, “thoughtful”), from <i>ἐπι-</i> (epi-) + <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō, “to learn, to know”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epipolae (a strategically important plateau in Sicily, location of the Euryalus fortress)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epeiph, the eleventh month of the Ancient Egyptian calendar, corresponding roughly to July</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./78c461f0.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="p" alt="glyph" height="37"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./78c461f0.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="p" alt="glyph" height="37"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./9feda508.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Z5" alt="glyph" height="35"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">W3&#58;ra</td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (jpj-jpj).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Epyaxa</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eretria</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἐρέτης</i> (erétēs, “rower”) +&lrm; <i>-τρῐᾰ</i> (-trĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Eriboea, wife of Telamon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρίβους</i> (eríbous, a term used mentioned only in this etymology) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Erigyius, an officer of Alexander the Great's army</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρι-</i> (eri-, intensive prefix) +&lrm; <i>γυῖον</i> (guîon, “limbs; hand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erae, Ionia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Erasistratus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔρασις</i> (érasis, “love”) +&lrm; <i>στρᾰτός</i> (strătós, “army”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). See ἔρᾰμαι (érămai, “to love”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erasinus, a river of Argolis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>Ἐρατοκλῆς</i> (Eratoklês) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eratosthenes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in particular</i>) the 3rd century <small>BCE</small> mathematician Eratosthenes</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἐρᾰτός</i> (erătós, “lovely”) +&lrm; <i>σθένος</i> (sthénos, “strong”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Erato</li></ol><p><i>ἐρατός</i> (eratós, “loved”) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erbessus, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Erginus, one of the Argonauts</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Eretria; an Eretrian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐρέτρῐᾰ</i> (Erétrĭă, “Eretria”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a child of Erechtheus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐρεχθεύς</i> (Erekhtheús) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Erechtheus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρέχθω</i> (erékhthō, “rend, break”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Erinyes; an Erinys</li></ol><p>Attested in Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀪𐀝</i> (e-ri-nu), <i>𐀁𐀪𐀝𐀸</i> (e-ri-nu-we), pointing to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;Erīnū́s</i>. Outside of that, uncertain; traditionally connected to <i>ὀρῑ́νω</i> (orī́nō) via Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁er-</i> (“to move, stir”), but this root is now usually reconstructed as <i>&#42;h₃er-</i>, so the <i>e</i>-grade would be unaccounted for by normal sound change. Beekes concludes Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Eriphyle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρῐ-</i> (erĭ-, “very”) +&lrm; <i>φῡλή</i> (phūlḗ, “race, tribe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erichthonius</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρῐ-</i> (erĭ-, “very”) +&lrm; <i>χθών</i> (khthṓn, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Red Sea</li><li>Indian Ocean (seen by the Ancient Greeks as the greater Red Sea)</li></ol><p>From the adjective <i>ἐρυθρός</i> (eruthrós, “red”), related to the naming of <i>Ἐρυθραία</i> (Eruthraía, “Erythraea or Eritrea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erythrae; Ildırı, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρῠθρός</i> (erŭthrós, “red”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erythraea, the land of Erythrae</li></ol><p>Feminine of adjective <i>Ἐρυθραῖος</i> (Eruthraîos), ultimately from <i>ἐρυθρός</i> (eruthrós, “red”). Substantivised by ellipsis of a noun e.g. <i>χώρα</i>&nbsp;f (khṓra, “land, country”) for the peninsula of Asia Minor where <i>Ἐρυθραί</i>&nbsp;f (Eruthraí, “the Ionian city of Erythrae”) was situated.<br></p><p>Also, with the sense as in the phrase <i>Ἐρυθρὰ θάλασσα</i> (Eruthrà thálassa) (in Herodous <i>Ἐρυθρὴ θάλασσα</i> (Eruthrḕ thálassa) <i>literally</i> Red Sea.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erythraean (native or inhabitant of Erythrae, Beotia)</li><li>Erythraean (native or inhabitant of Erythrae, Ionia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐρυθραί</i> (Eruthraí, “Erythrae”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erythraean (native or inhabitant of Erythrae, Beotia)</li><li>Erythraean (native or inhabitant of Erythrae, Ionia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐρυθραί</i> (Eruthraí, “Erythrae”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian Ocean</li><li>Red Sea</li></ol><p>Literally, “red sea”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>Ἐρυθρὰ θάλασσα</i> (Eruthrà thálassa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Ἐρυθρὰ θάλασσα</i> (Eruthrà thálassa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eryximachus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Erchia, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Erymanthus</li></ol><p>The name, Erymanthos, appears as o-ru-ma-te in a Linear B tablet from Pylos (Py Cn 3) listing members of a coast-watching unit, described in the Glossary of Ventris and Chadwick, <i>Documents in Mycenaean Greek</i>. The person is believed to have been named after his native region. Leonard Palmer proposed that this class of name, bearing the infix -nth-, derives from Luwian, and that the Luwians, an Anatolian-speaking people, preceded the Greeks in the region. This strongest and only theory of this class of name is re-presented by Fred C. Woodhuizen in <i>The Luwians of Western Anatolia</i>. Theorists of this school can etymologize the majority of the names in the Anatolian languages, Anatolian being Indo-European; however, o-ru-ma-te has not yet been credibly further etymologized.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Esther</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>אֶסְתֵּר</i> (ʾestēr), from Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎿𐎫𐎼</i> (a-s-t-r /⁠star-⁠/, “star”), from either Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;Hstā́</i> or Akkadian <i>Ištar</i> (“Ishtar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eteoclus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐτεοκλῆς</i> (Eteoklês).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Poetic and Epic form of <i>Ἐτεοκλῆς</i> (Eteoklês, “Eteocles”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eteocles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós, “true”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀳𐀺𐀐𐀩𐀸𐀂𐀍</i> (e-te-wo-ke-re-we-i-jo /⁠Etewokléwijos⁠/, “son of Eteocles”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eteonus (a city in Boeotia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Eteonicus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós, “true”) +&lrm; <i>νῑ́κη</i> (nī́kē, “victory”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Etonia, Pontus, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ephesian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἔφεσος</i> (Éphesos, “Ephesus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Ephthalite, a Hephthalite</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἐφραίμ</i> (Ephraím)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ephraim, son of Joseph.</li><li>Ephraim, a city mentioned in the New Testament.</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>אֶפְרָיִם</i> (ʾep̄rāyim), <i>אֶפְרַיִם</i> (ʾep̄rayim).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) An allegiant of the Israelite tribal patriarch Ephraim, a member of the tribe purportedly descended from him, or an inhabitant of the Northern Kingdom of Israel (930–720 B.C.), in which the tribe of Ephraim was preëminent.</li></ol><p><i>Ἐφραίμ</i> (Ephraím) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs, “member of a group”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) An allegiant of the Israelite tribal patriarch Ephraim, a member of the tribe purportedly descended from him, or an inhabitant of the Northern Kingdom of Israel (930–720 B.C.), in which the tribe of Ephraim was preëminent.</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἐφραΐμ</i> (Ephraḯm) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ephyra, the name of various cities of Ancient Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Ἐφύρᾱ</i> (Ephúrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Echembrotos</li></ol><p><i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō) +&lrm; <i>βροτός</i> (brotós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Echepolus, a Trojan slain by Antilochus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”) +&lrm; <i>πῶλος</i> (pôlos, “foal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Echion</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχῑς</i> (ékhīs, “viper”) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Echidorus; Gallikos (river), Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Echecrates</li></ol><p><i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to have”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>common form of <i>Ἐχῖναι</i> (Ekhînai)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐχῖνος</i> (ekhînos, “hedgehog, sea urchin”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́δες</i> (-ắdes, plural form of <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs, feminine noun suffix)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Echinades</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐχῖνος</i> (ekhînos, “hedgehog”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Echinades (islands in the Ionian Sea)</li></ol><p>Feminine plural of <i>ἐχῖνος</i> (ekhînos, “hedgehog, sea urchin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Ethiopian tribe mentioned in Pliny the Elder's <i>Natural History</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ἑβδομήκοντᾰ</i> (hebdomḗkontă, “seventy”) +&lrm; <i>κωμήτης</i> (kōmḗtēs, “villager”), from <i>κώμη</i> (kṓmē, “unwalled village”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Hebrew language</li><li>the Chaldean language</li></ol><p>See <i>Ἐβέρ</i> (Ebér).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in the Hebrew or Aramaic language</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hebrew, Israelite</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Hebrew, Israelite</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>עִבְרִי</i> (ʿiḇrī, “Hebrew”), likely through Aramaic <i>עִבְרַי</i> (ʿiḇray, “Hebrew”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hebryzelmis, from Thracian, the name of a king of the Odrysae</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Ebruzelmis</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hecuba; Hekabe</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hecate</li></ol><p>Possibly the feminine equivalent of <i>Ἑκατός</i> (Hekatós) or <i>ἑκάεργος</i> (hekáergos), an obscure epithet of Apollo, derived from <i>ἑκάς</i> (hekás, “far away”), variously interpreted as "one who works/operates from afar," "one who drives off," "the far reaching one" or "the far-darter." Apollo was the twin brother of Artemis, who was very closely associated (and sometimes conflated) with Hekate. Or possibly from an Anatolian language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hecamede, daughter of Arsinous and slave of Nestor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑκάς</i> (hekás, “far off”) +&lrm; <i>μήδεᾰ</i> (mḗdeă, “plan, counsel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) of Hecate</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hecataeus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑκᾰ́τη</i> (Hekắtē) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hecatombaeon: the first month of the Attic calendar, corresponding to the lunar term around July and August</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑκᾰτόμβαῐᾰ</i> (Hekătómbaĭă), a festival at which hecatombs were offered.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hekatompedon (Parthenon)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἑκᾰτόν</i> (hekătón, “hundred”) +&lrm; <i>ποδός</i> (podós, “of the foot, footer”), literally 'Hundred-footer'.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Helvetii; a Helvetian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Helvidius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Helvidius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Helenopolis, Bithynia, Turkey</li><li>Helenopolis, Palaestina, Israel</li></ol><p><i>Ἑλένη</i> (Helénē, “Helena”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Helicon, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Helcethium, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hellanicus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>various areas in Greece, such as the region surrounding Dodona, Thessaly, Phthiotis, or Northern Greece</li><li>Greece</li><li>all lands inhabited by Greeks, including Ionia</li><li>the Greek language</li></ol><p>See <i>Ἕλλην</i> (Héllēn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Greek (generally used as an epithet for various gods, especially Zeus)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἕλλην</i> (Héllēn, “Greek”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Hellespont, later called the Dardanelles</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἕλλης</i> (Héllēs, “of Helle”, genitive of <i>Ἕλλη</i> (Héllē)) +&lrm; <i>πόντος</i> (póntos, “sea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Greek woman</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) a gentile woman, non-Jewess</li></ol><p>Feminine adjective, from <i>Ἕλλην</i> (Héllēn, “Greek”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, feminine suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hellenic, Greek</li><li>like the Greeks</li></ol><p><i>Ἕλλην</i> (Héllēn, “Greek”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>imitation of the Greeks: Hellenism</li><li>paganism</li></ol><p><i>Ἕλλην</i> (Héllēn, “Greek”) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós, abstract noun suffix): literally, “Greekism”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who uses the Greek language:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a Greek Jew</li><li>a gentile, a heathen</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἑλληνίζω</i> (hellēnízō, “to speak Greek”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Hellespontian</li></ol><p><i>Ἑλλήσποντος</i> (Hellḗspontos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The Greeks in Dodona</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>The priests</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἕλλα</i> (hélla, “seat, shrine of Zeus in Dodona”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Enoch (first son of Cain)# (<i>biblical</i>) Enoch (in the Bible, one of the few people recorded as being taken by God before death)</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Hanoch (son of Reuben)</li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Henoch (son of Midian)</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>חֲנוֹךְ</i> (Ḥănôḵ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hermagoras</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ἕρμᾰ</i> (hérmă, “a support”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́, “assembly”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās): “a support of the assembly”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hermaphroditus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês, “Hermes”) +&lrm; <i>Ἀφροδῑ́τη</i> (Aphrodī́tē, “Aphrodite”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Hermione</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑρμιόνη</i> (Hermiónē, “Hermione”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Hermione, the name of multiple figures in Greek mythology</li><li>Ermioni (an ancient city in Argolis, Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hermogenes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕρμᾰ</i> (hérmă, “a support”) +&lrm; <i>γεν-</i> (gen-, “to be born”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs): perhaps “born as a support”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hermocrates</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês, “Hermes”) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hermolaus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês, “Hermes”) +&lrm; <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós, “men”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hermopolis; El Ashmunein, Egypt</li></ol><p><i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês, “Hermes (Greek god)”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hermonassa, Sarmatia, Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hermes</li></ol><p>Uncertain origin; usually linked to <i>ἕρμα</i> (hérma, “heap of stones”), itself of non-Indo-European substrate origin or from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ser-</i> (“to bind, put together”). Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀔𐁀</i> (e-ma-a2).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italy</li><li>Spain</li></ol><p>Nominalization of <i>ἑσπερῐ́ᾱ</i> (hesperĭ́ā), the feminine singular of <i>ἑσπέρῐος</i> (hespérĭos, “towards evening, western”), elliptically for <i>ἡ ἑσπερῐ́ᾱ χθών</i> (hē hesperĭ́ā khthṓn, “the western land”), used as a proper noun.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Ἑσπερῐ́ᾱ</i> (Hesperĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Hesperides; a Hesperid</li><li>a city in Cyrenaica, Libya, also called <i>Εὐεσπερίδες</i> (Euesperídes), relocated and renamed in the mid of the 3rd century BCE to <i>Βερενίκη</i> (Bereníkē), now known as Benghazi</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἕσπερος</i> (Hésperos, “Hesperus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hezron</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>חֶצְרוֹן</i> (ḥeṣrôn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The personification of the Morning Star; the planet Venus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕως</i> (héōs, “dawn”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “-bearing, -carrying”, from <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to bear, to carry”)). Compare <i>Φωσφόρος</i> (Phōsphóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ibiza, Spain</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤉𐤁𐤅𐤔𐤉𐤌</i> (ybwšym /⁠yibbōšīm⁠/, “dedicated to the god Bes”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Segesta, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edessa (a city in Greece)</li><li>Urfa (a city in Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ecdippa; Achziv, Israel</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Phoenician name of <i>Δῑδώ</i>.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤀𐤋𐤀𐤎</i> (ʾlʾs); probably ultimately from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʾil-</i> (“god”). Perhaps influenced by Phoenician <i>𐤏𐤋𐤔𐤕</i> (ʿAlišat), probably a compound of Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;ʾil-</i> with the feminine nominal suffix <i>𒄿𒊭𒀉</i> (išat).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elyrus, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Emesa</li></ol><p>See the etymology section of the Wikipedia article on Homs.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἔμεσα</i> (Émesa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) Empusa, a kind of shape-shifting spectre said to be sent by Hecate</li></ol><p>A folk etymology construes the name to mean "one-footed", from <i>ἕν</i> (hén) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”). However, as Beekes notes, the word is probably Pre-Greek in view of the suffix -ουσα; compare <i>ἄγχουσα</i> (ánkhousa), <i>αἴθουσα</i> (aíthousa), <i>κάδουσα</i> (kádousa) and <i>νήθουσα</i> (nḗthousa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Endius</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Henna; Enna, Italy</li></ol><p>Unknown. May be from Pre-Greek, Sicanian, or Sicel; if through an Italic language, the initial <i>Ἔ</i> could reflect Proto-Indo-European <i>*ǵʰ,</i> possibly Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ǵʰéyōm</i> (“winter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ennius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ennius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Entella, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epaphus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eresus, Lesbos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eryx; Erice, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Ezra</li></ol><p>The Ancient Greek rendering of the Biblical Hebrew <i>עֶזְרָא</i> (ʿezrā) used in the Greek Septuagint.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ephesus (an ancient Ionian city in western Anatolia, near Selçuk in modern İzmir Province, Turkey)</li></ol><p>From Luwian (/⁠Apa-ša⁠/, literally “later-city”), the prefix of which is cognate with <i>ἐπί</i> (epí, “on, over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Ephippus</li></ol><p><i>ἐπῐ-</i> (epĭ-) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Echeclus, the name of a centaur</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”) +&lrm; <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Hebrus or Evros; the river Maritsa</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Ebrus</i> (“wide”) and thus meaning "the wide river", or from Thracian <i>ebros</i> (“splasher”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hector</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;Héktōr</i>; synchronically from the adjective <i>ἕκτωρ</i> (héktōr, “holding fast”). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀒𐀵</i> (e-ko-to /⁠Hektōr⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Helenus</li></ol><p>Masculine form of <i>Ἑλένη</i> (Helénē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Helle</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Greek, a person who is from Greece or speaks Greek</li><li>one who participates in Greek culture</li><li>(<i>Judaism</i>) non-Jew, a gentile</li><li>pagan</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hellen, the mythical patriarch of the Greeks.</li></ol><p>Μost probably a derivation of Ἑλλοί (Helloí) or Σελλοί (Selloí), the Greek inhabitants of the area around the sanctuary of Dodona (Δωδώνη (Dōdṓnē). The ultimate origin is unknown, possibly Pre-Greek. Related to <i>Ἑλλάς</i> (Hellás).</p><p>More at Hellenes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἕλλησῐ</i> (Héllēsĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hermarchus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês, “Hermes”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hermippus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἑρμῆς</i> (Hermês, “Hermes”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hermus</li><li>the river Hermus; the river Gediz</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hermonthis, Lower Egypt, Egypt</li></ol><p>From Egyptian Demotic Egyptian <i>jwnw-mnṱ</i>, from Egyptian <i>jwnj</i> (“Hermonthis”) + <i>mnṯw</i> (“Montu”) in a direct genitive construction, thus ‘Hermonthis of Montu’.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unmarried young man, bachelor</li><li>one who is unmarried</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ewítʰewos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁widʰéwh₂</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>विधवा</i> (vidhávā), Latin <i>vidua</i> and Old English <i>widuwe</i> (English <i>widow</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἤ</i> (ḗ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἥλῐος</i> (hḗlĭos, “sun”)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἠέλιος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>Ἥλῐος</i> (Hḗlĭos, “Helios”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ᾱ̓ήρ</i> (āḗr, “mist; air”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Lyric form of <i>εὔκομος</i> (eúkomos, “lovely-haired”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἐΰς</i> (eǘs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) as; like; like as</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>little, small</li></ol><p>According to Leumann, it arose by false split from <i>οὐ δή βαιόν</i> (ou dḗ baión). A prefix <i>ἠ-</i> (ē-) is improbable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) most holy (<i>of places</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓γᾰ-</i> (ăgă-, with metrical lengthening of the first syllable) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἠδέ</i> (ēdé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li><i>ἠμέν</i> (ēmén)... ἠδέ...: both... and...</li><li>Alone or with <i>τε</i> (te) before it: and</li><li>Followed by <i>καί</i> (kaí): and, also</li></ol><p><i>ἦ</i> (ê) +&lrm; <i>δέ</i> (dé)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>into the haze of distance</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of the sea</i>) misty, melting into the haze of distance</li><li>covered in mist; shadowy</li></ol><p>Likely pronounced <i>ἠϝεροϝειδής</i> (ēweroweidḗs) in Homer's time: from <i>ἀήρ</i> (aḗr, “mist”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of the sea</i>) misty, melting into the haze of distance</li><li>covered in mist; shadowy</li></ol><p>Likely pronounced <i>ἠϝεροϝειδής</i> (ēweroweidḗs) in Homer's time: from <i>ἀήρ</i> (aḗr, “mist”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic) cloudy, gloomy</li></ol><p><i>ἀήρ</i> (aḗr) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sift, strain</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₁y-</i>. Cognates include Old Church Slavonic <i>сито</i> (sito), <i>сѣꙗти</i> (sějati), Lithuanian <i>sijóti</i>, and Old Norse <i>sald</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>relating to character or disposition</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a person</i>) expressing or demonstrating one's nature</li><li>(<i>theater</i>) in character</li></ol><li>ethical; moral (relating to morality)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) relating to ethics (the study of principles relating to right and wrong conduct)</li><li>(<i>of a text</i>) concerned with conduct and virtue</li></ol><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) tactful</li></ol><p>From <i>ἦθος</i> (êthos, “character; moral nature”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like a strainer, perforated</li></ol><p>From <i>ἠθμός</i> (ēthmós, “strainer, colander”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-form, -like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἡθμός</i> (hēthmós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to express characteristically</li></ol><p>From <i>ἠθολόγος</i> (ēthológos, “painting manners or character by mimic gestures”) from <i>ἦθος</i> (êthos, “character”) + <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “account”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a painting of character, especially by mimic gestures</li></ol><p>From <i>ἠθολόγος</i> (ēthológos, “painting manners by mimic gesture”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>ἦθος</i> (êthos, “character”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “account”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painting manners or character by mimic gestures</li></ol><p>From <i>ἦθος</i> (êthos, “character”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “account”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) the wool on the distaff</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>ἠλακάτη</i> (ēlakátē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of amber</li><li>shining like amber (said of water)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἤλεκτρον</i> (ḗlektron, “amber”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>distaff, spindle</li></ol><p>Unknown. Solmsen assumed an Anatolian origin, but it is probably just Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) a large sea-fish, probably a kind of tunny</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shadowy, dark</li></ol><p>From <i>ἠλῠ́γη</i> (ēlŭ́gē, “shadow”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shadow, darkness, gloom</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, to this word belongs the poetic adjective <i>λῡγαῖος</i> (lūgaîos, “dark”), which differs in anlaut; an explanation remains to be found. Furnée assumes a prothetic vowel, for which there is little or no evidence. Nevertheless, the co-occurrence of <i>λῡγαῖος</i> (lūgaîos) and <i>ἠλυγαῖος</i> (ēlugaîos) is remarkable. The word might be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li><i>ἠμέν</i> (ēmén)... <i>ἠδέ</i> (ēdé)...: both... and...</li></ol><p><i>ἦ</i> (ê) +&lrm; <i>μέν</i> (mén)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eǵ-</i> (“to say”). Cognate with <i>ἄνωγα</i> (ánōga), Latin <i>aiō</i>, Old Armenian <i>ասեմ</i> (asem).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) sandy (<i>epithet of Pylos</i>)</li></ol><p>From a variant of Epic Greek <i>ἄμαθος</i> (ámathos), <i>ψάμαθος</i> (psámathos, “sand”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἠμείβετο</i> (ēmeíbeto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>windy</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾰ̓́νεμος</i> (ắnemos, with lengthening of the initial vowel) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>manhood, ‌manliness</li><li>manly beauty</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) praises of manhood</li></ol><p>From an ablaut form of <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”) +&lrm; <i>-έη</i> (-éē, Ionic form of <i>-έᾱ</i> (-éā)). Compare <i>ᾰ̓νδρείᾱ</i> (ăndreíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ague, fever</li><li>nightmare</li></ol><p>According to Strömberg, it is from <i>ἤπῐος</i> (ḗpĭos, “mild, soothing”), so properly "mild fever", which would be a taboo paraphrase; this seems an improbable hypothesis. The term <i>ἠπῐ́ολος</i> (ēpĭ́olos, “moth”) should not be separated from this one, as is shown by Lithuanian <i>drugys</i> (“fever; moth”), Albanian <i>ethe</i> (“fever”) with <i>ethëzë</i> (“moth”). Given the variation <i>α/ο</i>, it is probably a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moth</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>ἠπίαλος</i> (ēpíalos, “ague; nightmare”), thus from Pre-Greek. For the meaning, compare Lithuanian <i>drugys</i> (“fever; moth”) and Albanian <i>ethe</i> (“fever”) with <i>ethëzë</i> (“moth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>landsman</li><li>mainland dweller, mainlander</li></ol><p>From <i>ἤπειρος</i> (ḗpeiros, “mainland”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἠπειρώτης)</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An inhabitant of Epirus; an Epirote</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἤπειρος</i> (Ḗpeiros, “Epirus”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>female mainland dweller, mainlander</li></ol><p>From <i>ἤπειρος</i> (ḗpeiros, “mainland”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, feminine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>softly, calmly, gently</li><li>slightly, barely</li><li>slowly</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rem-</i> (“to rest”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>archaic&#32;outside&#32;Homer</i>) tomb, barrow, burial mound</li><li>(early&#32;Attic,&#32;metonymic,&#32;in the plural) funeral games</li></ol><p>Beekes reconstructs the earlier form <i>&#42;ϝηρίον</i> (&#42;wēríon), based on the hiatus in the Iliad passage quoted below. The origin is unclear; the word is sometimes derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to guard, cover”), but Beekes doubts this. Related by folk etymology to <i>ἔρα</i> (éra, “earth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to keep quiet, be at rest</li></ol><p><i>ἠρέμᾰ</i> (ērémă) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rest, quietude</li></ol><p><i>ἠρέμᾰ</i> (ērémă) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>early-born (<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of Eos, the Dawn</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(as a substantive,&#32;see <i>Ἠρῐγένειᾰ</i> (Ērĭgéneiă)) a child of the morning</li></ol><li>the morning, morn</li><li>(<i>in later&#32;Epic</i>) a day</li><li>(echoing <i>ἔᾰρ</i> (éăr), “spring”, etymon of <i>ἦρι</i> (êri),&#32;pseudo-adjective) one bearing in spring (a varia lectio of <i>ἠυγένειᾰ</i> (ēugéneiă))</li></ol><p>From <i>ἦρι</i> (êri, “early in the morning”, Epic adverb) +&lrm; <i>-γένεια</i> (-géneia, “birth”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἠριγένεια)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>early-born, child of morn (an epithet of Eos)</li><li>(<i>fully substantivised</i>) Eos (Greek goddess of the dawn)</li><li>(<i>later</i>) an epithet of the Moon</li></ol><p>Use as a proper noun of the (substantivised) adjective and common noun <i>ἠρῐγένειᾰ</i> (ērĭgéneiă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>groundsel (<i>Senecio vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἦρῐ</i> (êrĭ, “early in the morning”) +&lrm; <i>γέρων</i> (gérōn, “old man”), because of its hoary down.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sound, ring, peal</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to let it sound</li></ol><p>From <i>ἠχή</i> (ēkhḗ, “sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, noise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wākʰā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweh₂gʰ-</i> (“to sound”). Cognate with Old English <i>swōgan</i> (“to resound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>echo, reflected sound</li><li>protracted sound</li></ol><p>From <i>ἠχή</i> (ēkhḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἠχώ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Echo, a nymph in Greek mythology</li></ol><p>Personification of <i>ἠχώ</i> (ēkhṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shore; beach; seabank</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἠϊών)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eion</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ἠῠ̈́τε</i> (ēŭ̈́te)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The morning red, daybreak, dawn</li><li>Dawn as a length of time: morning</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(accusative,&#32;<i>ἠῶ</i> (ēô)) through the morning</li></ol><li>A morning as a unit of time: day</li><li>The direction of dawn, the East</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;auhṓs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éwsōs</i> (“dawn”), which was also personified as a goddess of dawn in Proto-Indo-European religion, corresponding to Ancient Greek goddess <i>Ἠώς</i> (Ēṓs). Cognates include Latin <i>Aurora</i>/<i>aurora</i>, Sanskrit <i>उ॒षस्</i> (uṣás, “dawn; Ushas”) and possibly Old English <i>ēastre</i> and Old Armenian <i>այգ</i> (ayg), <i>առաւաւտ</i> (aṙawawt).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἠώς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) the goddess Eos</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;auhṓs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éwsōs</i> (“dawn”), which was also personified as a goddess of dawn in Proto-Indo-European religion.</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>Aurora</i>, <i>aurora</i>, Sanskrit <i>उषस्</i> (uṣás, “dawn; Ushas”) and possibly Old English <i>Ēostre</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἕωθεν</i> (héōthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be in the prime of youth, adolescence; to be young</li><li>to be pubescent, attain the age of puberty; reach adulthood; grow up into a mature young adult</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to be fresh, vigorous, youthful, like an adolescent; possess the energy and vigor characteristic of a passionate young man</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥβη</i> (hḗbē, “youth; vigor; pubes”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Cretan form of <i>ἡβᾰ́ω</i> (hēbắō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go before, precede; to lead the way, guide</li><li>to lead (dative) in (genitive)</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to be leader (in a thing)</li><li>to lead, command in war</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to rule, to have dominion</li></ol><li>(<i>post-Homeric</i>) to believe, hold</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to hold, regard (as something)</li></ol></ol><p>Beekes prefers to derive the word as an iterative present from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂g-</i> (“to seek out”); cognates include English <i>seek</i>, Latin <i>sagiō</i> (“to perceive keenly”).</p><p>Other theories consider the word as a deponent, intensified form of <i>ᾰ̓́γω</i> (ắgō, “to lead, to guide, to command”), itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂éǵeti</i> (“to be driving”), in <i>-ομαι</i> with the Indo-European thematic vowel retained.</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. What's the origin of the -ε- portion?</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>ἡγεμών</i> (hēgemṓn)</li></ol><p><i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the act of leading</li><li>supreme power, supremacy, command</li><li>office of governor, reign</li><li>a division of the army under its officer</li><li>chief thing, principal part</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡγεμών</i> (hēgemṓn) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) synonym of <i>ἡγεμών</i> (hēgemṓn)</li></ol><p><i>ἡγεμών</i> (hēgemṓn) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lead the way, be a leader</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡγεμών</i> (hēgemṓn) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a leader, ready to lead or guide</li><li>capable of command, authoritative</li><li>of or belonging to the prefect of Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡγεμών</i> (hēgemṓn, “leader, guide”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἡγεμόνευε</i> (hēgemóneue)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who goes first</li><li>(Odyssey) guide</li><li>leader, chief</li><li>governor, prince, ruler</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai, “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abbot, principal</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ἡδύς</i> (hēdús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delight, pleasure, enjoyment</li><li>taste, smell, flavor</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) desires</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ἥδομαι</i> (hḗdomai, “to rejoice”) and <i>ἡδῠ́ς</i> (hēdŭ́s, “sweet”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἡδονή)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hedone</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Drunk in enjoyment, stricken with pleasure.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡδονή</i> (hēdonḗ, “pleasure”) +&lrm; <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to strike”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A spice box</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥδυσμα</i> (hḗdusma, “spice; seasoning”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to season a dish</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to make pleasant</li><li>to delight, coax, gratify</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡδύς</i> (hēdús, “sweet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spearmint (<i>Mentha spicata</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἡδῠ́οσμος</i> (hēdŭ́osmos, “fragrant, sweet smelling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>breathing sweetness, sweet-breathing</li><li>sweet-smelling, fragrant</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a lamb not yet weaned</li></ol><p><i>ἡδῠ́ς</i> (hēdŭ́s, “pleasant, sweet”) +&lrm; <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “blow, breathe”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming two-termination adjectives)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasant to the taste or smell, sweet</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) pleasant, welcome</li><li>pleased, glad</li></ol><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>स्वा॒दु</i> (svādú), Latin <i>suāvis</i>, Old English <i>swēte</i> (English <i>sweet</i>), and Albanian <i>shije</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>axeweed (<i>Securigera securidaca</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. The word recalls <i>ἄσαρον</i> (ásaron, “hazelwort”) and <i>σίσαρον</i> (sísaron, “parsnip”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strainer, colander, especially wine strainer</li><li>wicker basket</li><li>sluice, weir, dam</li></ol><p>From <i>ἤθω</i> (ḗthō, “to strain, sift”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative adjective</i>) as big or great as, as old as</li><li>(<i>indirect interrogative adjective</i>) how big; extraordinarily big or small</li><li>(<i>exclamatory adjective</i>) how big!</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyós</i>, like the basic relative pronoun <i>ὅς</i> (hós), + the same suffix as Latin <i>tālis</i> (“such”) and <i>quālis</i> (“[such] as”) +&lrm; <i>-κός</i> (-kós, adjective suffix). Cf. <i>ἧλιξ</i> (hêlix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit in court (Heliaia), to be a Heliast</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̔λίᾱ</i> (hālíā)/<i>ἡλιαία</i> (hēliaía) + <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>public place or hall (in which the chief law-court was held)</li><li>supreme court (at Athens)</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾱ̔λίᾱ</i> (hālíā, “assembly of people”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to push, press”) (whence <i>εἴλω</i> (eílō, “to shut in, press”), <i>ῑ̓́λη</i> (ī́lē, “band, troop”) and many others).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the sun, solar</li></ol><p><i>ἥλῐος</i> (hḗlĭos, “sun”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰκός</i> (-ăkós, “adjectival suffix: -ac”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>time of life, age</li><li>the prime of life (<i>from about 17 to 45</i>)</li><li>youthful enthusiasm and passion</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) age-mates, fellows, comrades</li><li>time period</li><li>age, generation</li><li>stature, growth (<i>of the body as a sign of age</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἧλιξ</i> (hêlix) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun-running, sun's messenger; title of a grade of initiates in Mithras-worship or Mithraism</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios, “sun”) +&lrm; <i>δρόμος</i> (drómos, “running”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scarab (<i>Scarabaeus sacer</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios, “sun”) +&lrm; <i>κάνθαρος</i> (kántharos, “beetle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>magical invocation of the sun</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥλῐος</i> (hḗlĭos, “sun”) +&lrm; <i>μαντεία</i> (manteía, “divination”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun spurge (<i>Euphorbia helioscopia</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios, “sun”) +&lrm; <i>σκοπέω</i> (skopéō, “to watch”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heliotrope (<i>Heliotropium europaeum</i>)</li><li>sundial, solar clock</li><li>bloodstone, a green stone streaked with red</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios, “sun”) +&lrm; <i>τρέπω</i> (trépō, “to turn”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rough bindweed (<i>Smilax aspera</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios, “sun”) +&lrm; <i>φυτόν</i> (phutón, “plant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to become callous</li></ol><p>From <i>ἧλος</i> (hêlos, “nailhead”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day</li><li>a period of one's life (<i>qualified by an adjective</i>)</li><li>(<i>poetic</i>) time</li><li>birthday</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) year</li></ol><p>Lengthened form of <i>ἦμαρ</i> (êmar, “day”). The initial aspiration <i>῾</i> (h) in Attic is probably by analogy with <i>ἑσπέρᾱ</i> (hespérā). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>աւր</i> (awr, “day”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Monday</li></ol><p>Literally, “day of Selene”, goddess of the moon.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>for a day or days</li><li>after a day or a certain number of days</li><li>in the daytime</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>within a certain number of days</li><li>by day</li><li>sometime during a particular day</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on a particular day</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἡμέτερα</i> (hēmétera)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἡμέτερε</i> (hēmétere)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>modifying a noun</i>) our, of ours</li><li>(as substantive,&#32;often&#32;with article) ours</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) our friends or family; our possessions</li></ol></ol><p>From the stem of <i>ἡμεῖς</i> (hēmeîs, “we”) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, contrastive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half-hatched</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμι-</i> (hēmi-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>λεπτός</i> (leptós, “peeled”), from <i>λέπω</i> (lépō, “to peel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half mitre</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμι-</i> (hēmi-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>μίτρα</i> (mítra, “mitre”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on, “suffix for neuter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mule; half ass</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>attributive</i>) mule</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμῐ-</i> (hēmĭ-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “donkey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of day, by day</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμέρα</i> (hēméra, “day”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνός</i> (-ĭnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who keeps a diary</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμέρα</i> (hēméra, “day”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>martagon lily (<i>Lilium martagon</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμέρα</i> (hēméra, “day”) +&lrm; <i>κάλλος</i> (kállos, “beautiful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day-watcher</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμέρα</i> (hēméra, “day”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(first person plural,&#32;personal pronoun) we, us, our</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European oblique <i>&#42;n̥sm-</i> (from <i>&#42;éǵh₂</i>), with regular Attic-Ionic <i>η-</i> (ē-) &lt; <i>ᾱ-</i> (ā-) and initial <i>῾</i> (h) from <i>ῡ̔μεῖς</i> (hūmeîs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>half</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sēmi</i> (“half”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सामि</i> (sāmi), Latin <i>sēmi-</i>, and Old High German <i>sami-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-amphora, half-quadrantal</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμι-</i> (hēmi-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>ἀμφόριον</i> (amphórion, “amphora, liquid measure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>front plate of a corslet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμι-</i> (hēmi-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>θωράκιον</i> (thōrákion, “corslet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain on one side of the head or face, migraine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμῐ-</i> (hēmĭ-, half) +&lrm; <i>κρᾱνῐ́ον</i> (krānĭ́on, “head; skull”), possibly a calque of Egyptian <i>gs-tp</i> (“migraine”), from <i>gs</i> (“half”) + <i>tp</i> (“head”), although the Egyptian and Greek terms could also be independently derived.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) semicircle</li><li>place for public entertainment or meeting</li><li>semicircular seat, armchair</li><li>semicircular dial</li><li>semicircular base of a statue</li><li>drum of a half-column</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) barrel vault</li><li>theatrical machine described by Julius Pollux</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ἡμῐκῠ́κλιος</i> (hēmĭkŭ́klios, “semicircular”), or from <i>ἡμι-</i> (hēmi-, half) +&lrm; <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>semicircular</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμῐ-</i> (hēmĭ-, “half, semi-”) +&lrm; <i>κῠ́κλος</i> (kŭ́klos, “ring, circle”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-medimnus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμι-</i> (hēmi-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>μέδιμνος</i> (médimnos, “medimnus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mule fern (<i>Asplenium hemionitis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμίονος</i> (hēmíonos, “mule”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-axe, one-edged axe</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμι-</i> (hēmi-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>πέλεκκον</i> (pélekkon, “handle of an axe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) hemisphere</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμι-</i> (hēmi-, half) +&lrm; <i>σφαῖρα</i> (sphaîra, “sphere”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-talent, a measure of weight</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡμῐ-</i> (hēmĭ-, “half”) +&lrm; <i>τᾰ́λᾰντον</i> (tắlănton, “talent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rein, bridle</li></ol><p>According to Beekes from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂em-</i> (“to grasp”) and cognate with Latin <i>ānsa</i> (“handle”) and Lithuanian <i>ąsà</i> (“handle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>at which time, when</li><li>since</li><li>whenever</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who holds the reins, charioteer</li><li>chariot-driver in games</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) guide, leader</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) class of rich citizens of Athens who had to furnish chariots for public service</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Auriga, a winter constellation</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡνία</i> (hēnía, “bridle, rein”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>liver-colored, liver-like</li></ol><p>Participial form of an unattested verb <i>&#42;ἡπατίζω</i> (&#42;hēpatízō), from <i>ἧπᾰρ</i> (hêpăr, “liver”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heroine (female hero)</li><li>deceased woman</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>ἥρως</i> (hḗrōs, “hero”) +&lrm; <i>-ίνη</i> (-ínē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a heroine</li><li>a deceased woman</li><li>(<i>adjectival</i>) alternative form of <i>ἡρωϊκή</i> (hērōïkḗ, “heroic”)</li><li>a novennial festival at Delphi</li></ol><p><i>ἥρως</i> (hḗrōs, “hero”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the heroes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of or for a hero, heroic</li><li>epic verse</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἥρως</i> (hḗrōs, “hero”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ἡρωῐ̈κός</i> (hērōĭ̈kós)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥρως</i> (hḗrōs, “warrior, hero”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>historical</i>) A sanctuary dedicated to a hero, often over his supposed tomb.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡρῷος</i> (hērōîos, “of a hero”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be less or weaker than; to be inferior to</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be defeated; to be discomfited</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to be cast in a suit</li><li>(<i>genitive</i>) to give way; to yield</li><li>(<i>dative</i>) to be overcome by</li></ol><li>(late,&#32;active voice,&#32;deponent) to overcome; to defeat</li></ol><p>From <i>ἧσσᾰ</i> (hêssă) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳομαι</i> (&#42;-jomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pleasing, agreeable</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥδομαι</i> (hḗdomai, “to enjoy”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be still or quiet, be at rest</li></ol><p><i>ἥσυχος</i> (hḗsukhos, “quiet”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peace</li><li>silence, stillness, rest, quiet</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥσῠχος</i> (hḗsŭkhos, “quiet”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hermite, eremite</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡσῠχᾰ́ζω</i> (hēsŭkhắzō, “to be at rest, keep quiet”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a temple of Hephaestus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥφαιστος</i> (Hḗphaistos, “Hephaestus”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon, “instruments or means of action”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>or</li><li>than, as (in a comparative construction)</li></ol><p>From the Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ēwe</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-we</i> (“or”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that is to say; or rather</li></ol><p>From <i>ἤ</i> (ḗ, “or, than”) +&lrm; <i>γε</i> (ge, “indeed, in fact, at least”) +&lrm; <i>οὖν</i> (oûn, “therefore, so, then”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of the immediate past</i>) by this time, before this, already</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a local relation</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>of the immediate future</i>) forthwith, straightway</li><li>(<i>in opposition to future or past</i>) now, presently</li><li>(<i>of logical proximity</i>)</li><li>(<i>with a comparative or superlative</i>)</li><li>(<i>joined with other temporal words</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἦ</i> (ê, “truly”) + <i>δή</i> (dḗ, “now”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἤθελε</i> (ḗthele)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) manners; etiquette</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the usual or customary place (of something)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>home or dwelling-place (of a person)</li><li>territory (of an animal)</li></ol><li>character; disposition</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>theater</i>) dramatis persona</li></ol><li>custom; habit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) manners; etiquette</li></ol></ol><p>From older <i>&#42;ϝήθος</i> (&#42;wḗthos), an expanded form of <i>ἔθος</i> (éthos). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्व॒धा</i> (svadhā́, “habit; custom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>riddling</li></ol><p>From aorist stem <i>ἤθη(σᾰ)</i> (ḗthē(să), “I sifted”) of verb <i>ἠθέω</i> (ēthéō, “I sift”) (contracted form <i>ἠθῶ</i> (ēthô)) + <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs) <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. please confirm this lemma. ηθησ+ις/-εως or ηθη+σις/εως</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἠθέω</i> (ēthéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>amber</li><li>alloy of gold and silver, electrum</li></ol><p>Related to Ancient Greek <i>ἠλέκτωρ</i> (ēléktōr, “shining, brilliant; <i>byname of</i> the Sun”), with further origin unknown. Possible, though doubtful, connections include Sanskrit <i>उल्का</i> (ulkā́, “meteor”) and/or Latin <i>Vulcānus</i> (“god of fire and metalworking”), Ancient Greek <i>ἥλιος</i> (hḗlios, “sun”), or Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>enough, sufficiently</li><li>very much, exceedingly</li><li>in vain, to no purpose</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ἠλεός</i> (ēleós, “astray, distraught, crazed”), and based on the suffix <i>-ιθ-</i> (-ith-), from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἤλυθε</i> (ḗluthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἤματα</i> (ḗmata)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἤματι</i> (ḗmati)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>see there!</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>abomasum, rennet stomach</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) as a dish made of rennet stomach</li></ol><p>The formation with <i>ἔν-</i> perhaps arose under the influence of <i>ἔντερον</i> (énteron, “intestine”) and <i>ἐγκοίλιος</i> (enkoílios, “entrails”), but it is late in any case. Assuming <i>&#42;ϝήνυστρον</i> (&#42;wḗnustron), this word is traditionally connected with Icelandic <i>vinstur</i> (“abomasum”), but it differs regarding the quantity of the first syllable and the color of the intermediate vowel. As a pre-form, <i>&#42;wend-tri-</i> has been assumed, comparing German <i>Wanst</i> (“belly”), Latin <i>venter</i> (“belly”) and Sanskrit <i>वस्ति</i> (vasti, “bladder”). However, a digamma is uncertain, and the connection with these words seems most improbable, as the forms are not well comparable. According to Beekes, the ending in <i>-στρον</i> is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mainland, land</li><li>The mainland of Greece</li><li>continent</li><li>inland areas (as opposed to coastal areas)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ā́peřřos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Heh₂per-</i> (Beekes) or <i>&#42;h₂eh₁&#47;h₂per-io-</i> (Kroonen). Cognates include Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ōferaz</i> and possibly Old Armenian <i>ափն</i> (apʻn); see those for more. If the appurtenance of Armenian is accepted, we may be dealing with a heteroclitic <i>r</i>/<i>n</i>-stem.</p><p>According to Specht <i>apud</i> Olsen, this Indo-European root is a vrddhi derivative from the etymon of Sanskrit <i>अपर</i> (ápara, “posterior, later, latter”).</p><p>In relation with al. <i>Ufer,</i> asaj. <i>ōfer</i> 'firm land'</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἤπειρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epirus (a historical region and ancient kingdom in Southeast Europe, today split politically between northwestern Greece and southwestern Albania)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἤπειρος</i> (ḗpeiros, “mainland”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to the mainland</li></ol><p><i>ἤπειρον</i> (ḗpeiron) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gentle, kind (of persons, of words, of feelings)</li><li>mild, less intense (of temperature)</li><li>soothing, assuaging, appeasing</li></ol><p>According to Van Beek, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eh₂pyos</i> (“friendly”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>आपि</i> (āpí, “ally, friend, confederate”), <i>आप्य</i> (ā́pya, “kind, friendly”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper, guardian</li></ol><p>Maybe from the same root of <i>ἔρυμαι</i> (érumai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quiet, peaceful, tranquil.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἠρέμα</i> (ēréma, “softly, calmly”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eryngo (plant of the genus <i>Eryngium</i>)</li><li>goat's beard</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>εἴλιγγος</i> (eílingos) and <i>πίσυγγος</i> (písungos). According to Strömberg, it derives from <i>ἔαρ</i> (éar, “spring”). However, Beekes is sure of the Pre-Greek origin of the word. Compare also the synonymous Middle Armenian <i>երնջնակ</i> (ernǰnak).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>either</li><li>or</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>τοι</i> (toi)</li></ol><p><i>ἤ</i> (ḗ) +&lrm; <i>τοι</i> (toi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>provisions for a journey</li><li>chaff</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;in the present&#32;indicative) to be going to go</li><li>(Epic,&#32;Attic&#32;otherwise) to go</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;éimi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éyti</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀂𐀍𐀳</i> (i-jo-te), Latin <i>eō</i> (“to go”), Sanskrit <i>एति॑</i> (éti, “goes”), Hittite <i>𒄿𒄿𒀀𒀜𒋫</i> (i-ya-at-ta /⁠iyatta⁠/, “goes”), Old Persian <i>𐎠𐎡𐎫𐎡𐎹</i> (a-i-t-i-y /⁠aitiy⁠/, “goes”), Old Church Slavonic <i>ити</i> (iti), Old English <i>ēode</i> (“went”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἥατο</i> (hḗato)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>youth</li><li>vigour</li><li>pubes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yēgʷeh₂</i> (“power, (youthful) strength”). Cognate with Latvian <i>jȩ̃ga</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἥβη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hebe</li></ol><p>See the common noun <i>ἥβη</i> (hḗbē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>command</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai, “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἥδε</i> (hḗde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be pleased, enjoy oneself</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hwā́domai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweh₂d-</i> (“sweet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>relish, seasoning, sauce</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) spices, aromatics</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡδύνω</i> (hēdúnō, “to season”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>least</li><li>(<i>in answer to question</i>) not at all</li></ol><p><i>ἥκιστος</i> (hḗkistos, “least”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ</i> (-ă, adverbial suffix)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>least</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) worst at</li></ol><p>Superlative, possibly from the adverb <i>ἦκα</i> (êka, “slightly”) +&lrm; <i>-ιστος</i> (-istos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>least</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) worst at</li></ol><p>Superlative, possibly from the adverb <i>ἦκα</i> (êka, “slightly”) +&lrm; <i>-ιστος</i> (-istos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have come, to be present</li><li>to have reached a point</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with an adverb followed by a genitive</i>)</li></ol><li>to have come back, returned</li><li>(pleonastic,&#32;with a participle)</li><li>(simply,&#32;like <i>γίγνομαι</i> (gígnomai))</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) to have come, to be brought</li><li>to concern, relate, belong to</li><li>to depend upon</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>with participle</i>)</li></ol><p>A perfective verb of uncertain origin, but seems to be related to the present-meaning <i>ἵκω</i> (híkō, “to come”), and thus from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyk-</i> (“to reach for”), though the aspectual difference and phonetics are difficult to resolve.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sun</li><li>east</li><li>day</li><li>sunshine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hāwélios</i>, from a pre-Hellenic or later Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sāwélios</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sóh₂wl̥</i>. Cognate with Old Church Slavonic <i>слъньце</i> (slŭnĭce), Latin <i>sol</i>, Sanskrit <i>सूर॑</i> (sū́ra), <i>सूर्य॑</i> (sū́rya), <i>स्व॑र्</i> (svàr), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬭</i> (huuar), Persian خور (xwar).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἥλιος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Helios</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥλῐος</i> (hḗlĭos, “sun”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tame, civilized, cultivated</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin; not related to <i>ἡμέρᾱ</i> (hēmérā, “day”). Some proposed theories:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half</li></ol><p>A parallel formation to <i>ἥμῐσυς</i> (hḗmĭsus, “half”, adjective). Usually seen as reflecting Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sēmi-tw-om</i>, a thematisation of the <i>tu</i>-stem <i>ἥμισυς</i> (hḗmisus), there is however contradictory evidence for <i>-σσ-</i> (-ss-) being the regular Doric outcome of medial <i>&#42;-tw-</i>. Compare Albanian <i>gjysmë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>half</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thrower, darter</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi, “to throw”) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) a hero of the Trojan War: any of the major combatants of the Greek or Trojan forces</li><li>a hero or heroine of the ancient Greek religion: a human or demigod whose shrine was celebrated with chthonic rituals organized by local governments</li></ol><p>Uncertain. An original root <i>&#42;ἡρωϝ-</i> (&#42;hērōw-) was traditionally hypothesised by comparison with <i>πάτρως</i> (pátrōs), <i>μήτρως</i> (mḗtrōs), but had to be discarded considering the lack of /w/ in Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀴𐀪𐀮𐀫𐀁</i> (ti-ri-se-ro-e). Has been often compared with the theonym <i>Ἥρα</i> (Hḗra) and the proper name <i>Ἥρυλλα</i> (Hḗrulla). Assuming an original meaning “protector, defender”, traditionally also linked to Latin <i>servō</i> (“to protect, rescue”), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬎𐬭𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (ha<sup><small>u</small></sup>ruua<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti, “to protect, defend”). According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quiet; still; calm</li><li>quiet; gentle; moderate</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) cautious</li><li>(<i>of the voice</i>) gentle</li></ol><p>Unknown. Osthoff and Brugman connected this word to Latin <i>sinō</i> (“to permit, allow”) (assuming cognacy with Latin <i>sēmen</i> (“seed”)), but this is now viewed as impossible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ἥσσων</i> (hḗssōn)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>certainly, really</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>or</li></ol><p>Sometimes compared to Sanskrit <i>आ</i> (ā́, emphatic particle). Related to <i>ἤ</i> (ḗ, “or”).</p><p>See <i>ἤ</i> (ḗ, “or”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be; to exist</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of events</i>) to happen</li><li>to be the case</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to live</li><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to be [<i>with</i> nominative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>(third person,&#32;impersonal) it is possible [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ehmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésmi</i> (“to be, to exist”).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>eom</i> (whence English <i>am</i>), Latin <i>sum</i>, Sanskrit <i>अस्मि॑</i> (ásmi), Old Armenian <i>եմ</i> (em), and so on. More at <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be, exist”). Not to be confused with εἶμι (eîmi) (to go).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eǵ-</i> (“to say”). Cognate with <i>ἄνωγα</i> (ánōga), Latin <i>aiō</i>, Old Armenian <i>ասեմ</i> (asem).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delight, pleasure</li><li>vinegar, used as flavouring</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡδύς</i> (hēdús, “sweet”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Apparently originally an Aeolic form due to the loss of /h/.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἦ</i> (ê)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the usual or customary place (of something)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>home or dwelling-place (of a person)</li><li>territory (of an animal)</li></ol><li>character; disposition</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>theater</i>) dramatis persona</li></ol><li>custom; habit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) manners; etiquette</li></ol></ol><p>From older <i>&#42;ϝήθος</i> (&#42;wḗthos), an expanded form of <i>ἔθος</i> (éthos). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्व॒धा</i> (svadhā́, “habit; custom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>slightly, a little</li><li>softly, gently, smoothly</li><li>softly, low (<i>of sound volume</i>)</li><li>little by little</li></ol><p>Uncertain; may reflect a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₁k-</i> (“slow”), whence perhaps Latin <i>sēgnis</i> (“slow, tardy”). May also be related to <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “silence”), <i>ἀκήν</i> (akḗn, “silently”), <i>ἥκιστος</i> (hḗkistos, “least, worst”), <i>ἧσσα</i> (hêssa, “defeat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἦλθε</i> (êlthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sloe or plum</li></ol><p>Unexplained, but probably related to <i>μῆλον</i> (mêlon, “apple”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>day</li><li>(accusative singular,&#32;as adverb) by day</li><li>(<i>μέσον</i>) midday</li><li>(<i>δείελον</i>) evening</li><li>(ἤματι <i>χειμερίῳ</i>) on winter's day</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ā́mər</i> (whence Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀀𐀗𐀨𐀔</i> (a-mo-ra-ma /⁠āmōr-āmar⁠/, “day after day”)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Héh₂mr̥</i> ~ <i>&#42;Héh₂mōr</i> (“heat”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>աւր</i> (awr, “day”).</p><p>For the semantic shift, compare possibly the family of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;dagaz</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>when (relative), at which time</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyéh₂</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>gleaming; shining; glittering (epithet of copper and heaven)</li></ol><p>Formation in <i>-οψ</i> (-ops) but further unclear; originally <i>&#42;ϝῆνοψ</i> (&#42;wênops). Compare <i>αἶθοψ</i> (aîthops) and <i>νῶροψ</i> (nôrops) which are also epithets of copper, but remain without clear interpretation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic and Ionic form of <i>ἔαρ</i> (éar, “spring”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>service, gratification, favor, kindness</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>for the sake of, on account of, for</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἆρα</i> (âra)</li></ol><p>Beekes assumes a pre-form <i>&#42;ϝῆρ-α</i> (&#42;wêr-a), and tentatively assumes a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weh₁-</i> (“true”). Alternatively, see also <i>ἑορτή</i> (heortḗ, “feast”), <i>ἔρᾰνος</i> (érănos, “banquet”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift up, raise, support</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(of armies, ships,&#32;transitive) to get the fleet under sail</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bear, sustain</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise up, exalt</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise by words, praise, extol</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lift and take away, remove</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(transitive,&#32;with genitive) to take away from</li><li>(Koine,&#32;transitive) to take off, kill</li></ol><li>(middle voice,&#32;transitive) to take up for oneself, to win, gain</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take upon oneself, undergo</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to undertake, begin</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to raise up</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to take away</li></ol><li>(passive voice,&#32;intransitive) to hang</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;awéřřō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wér-ye-ti</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wer-</i> (“to raise?; to bind, enclose?”); the relation between the "raise" and "enclose" meanings, if any, is unclear, though Chantraine (DELG) appears to consider the latter a semantic specialization of the former. Potential cognates include Old Armenian <i>գեր</i> (ger, “above, hyper-”), Latin <i>aperiō</i> (“to open”), Proto-Balto-Slavic <i>&#42;wérˀtei</i> (“to thrust, prod”), Sanskrit <i>वृणोति</i> (vṛṇoti, “to cover, ward off”), Albanian <i>vjerr</i> (“to hang, suspend”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic and Ionic form of <i>ἔαρ</i> (éar, “spring”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>early in the morning</li></ol><p>Two possibilities:</p><p>Compare <i>ἠέρῐος</i> (ēérĭos, “with the early morning”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἦρχε</i> (êrkhe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eta, the name for the seventh letter Η (Ē), η (ē) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Originally <i>ἧτᾰ</i> (hêtă), borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤇</i> (ḥ‬ /⁠ḥēt⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>surely, doubtless</li></ol><p><i>ἤ</i> (ḗ) +&lrm; <i>τε</i> (te)</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be; to exist</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of events</i>) to happen</li><li>to be the case</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to live</li><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to be [<i>with</i> nominative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>(third person,&#32;impersonal) it is possible [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ehmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésmi</i> (“to be, to exist”).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>eom</i> (whence English <i>am</i>), Latin <i>sum</i>, Sanskrit <i>अस्मि॑</i> (ásmi), Old Armenian <i>եմ</i> (em), and so on. More at <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be, exist”). Not to be confused with εἶμι (eîmi) (to go).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) he/she/it was</li></ol><p>From <i>ἦν</i> (ên, “he/she/it was”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) the heart, as the seat of life, passion, desire, etc.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eh₁tr̥</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁eh₁t-</i> (“to breathe”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-r̥</i> (r/n-stem suffix). Cognates include Old Irish <i>inathar</i> (“intestines”) and Dutch <i>ader</i> (“vein”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) abdomen, especially the lower part of it, belly</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) pith of a reed</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sound, noise, ringing of the ears</li><li>echo</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) breathing (i.e. aspiration&#59; rough (δᾰσεῖᾰ) or smooth (ψῑλή))</li><li>voice</li></ol><p>Later form of <i>ἠχή</i> (ēkhḗ, “sound, noise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same age</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fellow; comrade</li></ol><p>From an earlier <i>&#42;σϝᾱλιξ</i> (&#42;swālix), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweh₂-lik-</i> (“as old”), from <i>&#42;swé</i> (“self”) and perhaps <i>&#42;h₂el-</i> (“to grow”) + the same suffix found in <i>ἡλῐ́κος</i> (hēlĭ́kos, “as big as”). For the semantics, compare <i>ἔτης</i> (étēs, “clansman, private person”), Sanskrit <i>स्वक</i> (svaka, “relative, friend”), and Latin <i>sodālis</i> (“companion, comrade”). See also Latin <i>aequālis</i> (“equal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) nailhead or stud (as an ornament)</li><li>(<i>Attic</i>) nail</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) wart; callus</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) callus or knot caused by disease (especially on olive trees)</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests an equivalence with Latin <i>vallus</i> (“stake; pole”) on the basis of the Aeolic variant <i>ϝᾰ́λλος</i> (wắllos), in which case from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sit, be seated; to lie, be situated</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to lie hid</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éh₁s-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁eh₁s-</i> (“to sit”) (with cognates in Indo-Iranian and Anatolian), which may be an extension of <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>आस्ते</i> (ā́ste, “to stay; to sit”), Avestan <i>𐬁𐬯𐬙𐬈</i> (āste), Hittite (ēša), Hittite (āšzi). The initial /h/ is due to influence from etymologically unrelated forms derived from <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἕως</i> (héōs)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἕως</i> (héōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>liver</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;yêkʷər</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyékʷr̥</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defeat, discomfiture</li></ol><p>Back-formation from <i>ἥσσων</i> (hḗssōn, “inferior, weaker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old name of the letter η (eta, see ἦτᾰ (êtă), ήτα).</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤇</i> (ḥ‬ /⁠ḥēt⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where (<i>relative</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ᾗ</i> (hēî) +&lrm; <i>-χι</i> (-khi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egeria</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ēgeria</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edonis, an ancient region in southern Thrace inhabited by the Edoni</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἠδωνός</i> (Ēdōnós, “Edonian”) +&lrm; <i>-ίς</i> (-ís).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Edoni; an Edonian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἠδωνός</i> (Ēdōnós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edoni, an ancient Thracian tribe which dwelt between the rivers Nestus and Strymon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eetion</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eetionea or Eetionia</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἠετῐ́ων</i> (Ēetĭ́ōn) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Electra</li></ol><p>From <i>ἤλεκτρον</i> (ḗlektron, “amber”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱ</i> (-ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eli, a given name, most famously held by the high priest of the books of Samuel.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>עֵלִי</i> (ʿēlî).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Elias or Elijah</li><li>Elijah, an Israelite prophet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>אֱלִיָּה</i> (ʾĕliyyā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Electryon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἠλέκτωρ</i> (ēléktōr, “the beaming sun”), with influence from <i>Ἀμφιτρῠ́ων</i> (Amphitrŭ́ōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Elean, from Elis</li></ol><p><i>Ἦλῐς</i> (Êlĭs, “Elis”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖος</i> (-eîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elysium; an Elysian field; one of the Elysian fields</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek; possibly implying an unattested Elysian language.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Elysian</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek. Possibly <i>Ἠλύσιον</i> (Ēlúsion) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), although considering that nominal use of <i>Ἠλύσιον</i> (Ēlúsion) is rare compared to adjectival use, it is more likely that <i>Ἠλύσιον</i> (Ēlúsion) is a neuter substantivization of the adjective.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Elysian Fields</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Elysian</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek. Possibly <i>Ἠλύσιον</i> (Ēlúsion) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), although considering that nominal use of <i>Ἠλύσιον</i> (Ēlúsion) is rare compared to adjectival use, it is more likely that <i>Ἠλύσιον</i> (Ēlúsion) is a neuter substantivization of the adjective.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elone, Phthiotis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Emathia (an ancient district of Macedonia, in modern-day Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἠμαθόεις</i> (ēmathóeis, “sandy”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eridanus, former name of the River Po</li><li>Eridanus, a river (now underground) flowing through Athens</li></ol><p>Composed of <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow, run”) + Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;deh₂nu</i> (“river”), from <i>&#42;dʰen-</i> (“run, flow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isaiah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְשַׁעְיָהוּ</i> (yəšaʿyā́hū).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hegesippus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai, “to lead”) +&lrm; <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic and Ionic form of <i>Ᾱ̓γησῐ́λᾱος</i> (Āgēsĭ́lāos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, Hegesipyle</li></ol><p>From <i>ἥγησῐς</i> (hḗgēsĭs, “command”) +&lrm; <i>πῠ́λη</i> (pŭ́lē, “gate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eli, a given name, most famously held by the high priest of the books of Samuel.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>עֵלִי</i> (ʿēlî).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἠλίᾱς</i> (Ēlíās)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heliopolis</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥλῐος</i> (Hḗlĭos, “sun”) +&lrm; <i>πόλῐς</i> (pólĭs, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eli, a given name, most famously held by the high priest of the books of Samuel.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>עֵלִי</i> (ʿēlî).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elagabalus, Heliogabalus (Syro-Roman sun god)</li><li>Elagabalus, Heliogabalus (Roman emperor)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Heliodorus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥλῐος</i> (Hḗlĭos, “the god Helios”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heniochi, an ancient tribe of Colchis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἡνίοχος</i> (hēníokhos, “charioteer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Herculaneum; Ercolano, Campania, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Herculanum</i>, <i>Herculāneum</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἡράκλειον</i> (Hērákleion), from <i>Ἡρακλῆς</i> (Hēraklês, “Heracles”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heraclea</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἡρακλῆς</i> (Hēraklês, “Heracles”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heraclius, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἡρακλῆς</i> (Hēraklês, “Hercules”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Heraclitus</li><li>in particular, the pre-Socratic philosopher Heraclitus of Ephesus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā, “Hera”) +&lrm; <i>κλειτός</i> (kleitós, “glorious”), literally “glory of Hera”. Compare <i>Ἡρακλῆς</i> (Hēraklês).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heraea (a city in Arcadia, Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾱ</i> (-iā).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to Hera</li></ol><p><i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Heracleidae; a Heraclid; a descendant of Heracles</li><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Heraclides</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἡρακλῆς</i> (Hēraklês) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́δης</i> (-ĭ́dēs, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heraclonas, a name borne by an emperor</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>Ἡρακλῆς</i> (Hēraklês, “Hercules”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Heraclea</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἡράκλειᾰ</i> (Hērákleiă, “Heraclea”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Heracles; Hercules</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā, “Hera”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “glory”). Compare <i>Ἡράκλειτος</i> (Hērákleitos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a festival in honor of Hera</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to Hera</li></ol><p><i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the temple of Hera</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to Hera</li></ol><p><i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to Hera</li></ol><p><i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Herodotus</li><li>in particular, the 5th century <small>BCE</small> historian Herodotus of Halicarnassus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā, “Hera”) +&lrm; <i>δοτός</i> (dotós, “granted”, from <i>δίδωμι</i> (dídōmi)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Herostratus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā) +&lrm; <i>στρατός</i> (stratós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Herophilus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā) +&lrm; <i>φῐ́λος</i> (phĭ́los).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name</li></ol><p>Likely from <i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā) +&lrm; <i>-ώ</i> (-ṓ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἡρῴδης</i> (Hērōídēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Herodias, a given name, most famously held by the daughter of Aristobulus IV.</li></ol><p>From Ἡρώδης (Hērṓdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Herod, the name of a number of members of the Herodian dynasty who ruled (in) the Judaea province or nearby territories.</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥρως</i> (Hḗrōs, “hero”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, “descendant of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hesiod</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Esau</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>עֵשָׂו</i> (ʿēśā́w).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of Hesiod (Hesiodic, Hesiodian, Hesiodean)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἡσῐ́οδος</i> (Hēsĭ́odos) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hessus, Locris, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hesychius, famously held by&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Hesychius of Alexandria (fl. late 4th C. AD), Greek grammarian and lexicographer</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἡσῠ́χῐος</i> (hēsŭ́khĭos, “quiet, gentle, reposeful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hephaestion</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἥφαιστος</i> (Hḗphaistos, “Hephaestus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Egesta; Segesta, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hera</li><li>a title of the empresses of Rome&#59; see also <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús)</li><li>Pythagorean name for nine</li><li>the planet Venus</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly a feminine form of <i>ἥρως</i> (hḗrōs) or related to <i>ὥρα</i> (hṓra). Beekes prefers Pre-Greek origin, particularly due to the word being a theonym.</p><p>Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀨</i> (e-ra).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἡραῖον</i> (Hēraîon, “temple of Hera”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Epic form of <i>Ἥρᾱ</i> (Hḗrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hephaestus</li></ol><p>A Pre-Greek word of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Elis, Greece</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wālis</i>, perhaps related to Latin <i>vallis</i> (“valley”), referring to its landscape.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>clamor, shout, cry</li><li>sound, roar</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἰά)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jah or Yah, one of the names of the Hebrew God and a common theophoric element.</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>Ἰαώ</i> (Iaṓ), from Biblical Hebrew <i>𐤉𐤄</i> (YH, “Yah”), itself a contraction of <i>𐤉𐤄𐤅𐤄</i> (YHWH, “Yahweh”). As contracted forms of the Tetragrammaton Hebrew <i>יַהְוֶה</i> (“YaHWeH”), <i>Ἰά</i> (Iá /⁠yah⁠/) shares roots with Hebrew <i>יָהּ</i> (yāh) and Classical Syriac <i>ܝܰܗ</i>/<i>ܝܲܗ</i> (yah).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak Ionic</li><li>to cry aloud</li><li>to be of a violet color</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰάς</i> (Iás, “Ionic”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p><p>From <i>ἰά</i> (iá, “voice, cry”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p><p>From <i>ἴον</i> (íon, “violet”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Sicyon</i>) name of a perfumed garland</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lament, dirge</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hapless, melancholic</li><li>tedious, dull, stupid</li></ol><p>It is improbable that this expressive word derives from the interjection <i>ἰή</i> (iḗ). The suffix is found only in <i>κοάλεμος</i> (koálemos, “stupid fellow, booby”), which may have influenced the later meaning of this word. Since <i>κοάλεμος</i> (koálemos) is Pre-Greek, the same must hold for the present word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send forth quickly, shoot out, fling out</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>violet coloured</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴανθος</i> (íanthos, “violet”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cure, heal</li><li>to repair</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but frequently compared to <i>ἰαίνω</i> (iaínō, “to warm”), with both often derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eysh₂-</i> (“to strengthen, propel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to send forth, drive on, shoot</li><li>to rush, hurry</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Often combined with <i>ἴπτομαι</i> (íptomai, “to press hard, oppress”), but this is semantically difficult. The verb has also been linked to <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi, “to send, throw”) and Latin <i>iaciō</i> (“to throw, hurl, cast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jasmine (<i>Jasminum officinale</i>)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Iranian. Compare Middle Persian (yʾsmn /⁠yāsman⁠/), Persian یاسمن (yâsaman).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to cry out, shriek, scream</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;swiswákʰō</i>, reduplicated form from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweh₂gʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to heat, warm</li><li>(<i>also metaphorically</i>) to melt</li><li>to relax by warmth</li><li>(<i>more frequently</i>) to warm, cheer</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eysh₂-n-ye-ti</i>, a nasal ye-present of <i>&#42;h₁eysh₂-</i> (“to strengthen, propel”), whence also Sanskrit <i>इषण्यति</i> (iṣaṇyati, “to urge on, incite”) (though the semantics are not particularly strong, nor particularly weak). See also <i>ῑ̓άομαι</i> (īáomai, “to cure, repair”), as well as the primary Sanskrit formations <i>इष्यति</i> (iṣyati, “to impel, send”), <i>इष्णाति</i> (iṣṇāti, “to incite, throw, swing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>writer of iambics</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴαμβος</i> (íambos, “iambic”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of musical instrument, distinct from the <i>σαμβύκη</i> (sambúkē)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴαμβος</i> (íambos, “iamb”), with influence from <i>σαμβύκη</i> (sambúkē, “sambuca”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>medical treatment</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) curing; correcting</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̓ᾱτρεύω</i> (īātreúō) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to medically treat, cure</li><li>to practise medicine</li><li>to remedy, correct</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̓ᾱτρός</i> (īātrós, “physician”), from <i>ἰάομαι</i> (iáomai, “I cure, treat”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surgery, a doctor's office, a clinic</li><li>remedy</li><li>expense of a cure</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̓ᾱτρεύω</i> (īātreúō, “to treat medically, to cure”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>physician; surgeon; medical doctor</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰάομαι</i> (iáomai, “heal”) +&lrm; <i>-τρος</i> (-tros, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cry, shout, both of victor and vanquished</li><li>wail, shriek, scream</li><li>joyous sound, shout of joy</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰάχω</i> (iákhō, “to cry out, scream”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) to sleep, pass the night</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to stop, make to cease</li></ol></ol><p>From a reduplicated present <i>&#42;h₂í-h₂ews-ye-</i> of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wes-</i> (“pass the night”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>ագանիմ</i> (aganim), Hittite <i>𒄷𒅖𒍣</i> (ḫuiš-zi, “to live, survive”), and Sanskrit <i>वसति</i> (vásati, “to dwell, remain, stay; to stop”). Related to <i>ἄεσα</i> (áesa) and <i>αὐλή</i> (aulḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marsh mallow (<i>Althaea officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pepperwort (<i>Lepidium graminifolium</i>)</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>Ἰβηρία</i> (Ibēría, “Iberia”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is), after its native environment. Alessio thinks the name is Aegean and compares the word with <i>ἰβίσκος</i> (ibískos, “ibiscus”) and <i>ἰβάνη</i> (ibánē, “water-bucket”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) part behind the thigh and knee, ham, popliteus</li></ol><p>A hypostasis <i>&#42;ἐν-γνύ-α</i> (&#42;en-gnú-a, “place in the knee”). The stem <i>ἰγνύς</i> (ignús) was formed after <i>ἰξύς</i> (ixús, “waist, loins”) and <i>ὀσφῦς</i> (osphûs, “hips or loins”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ἠδέ</i> (ēdé, “and”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to look with the eyes [<i>with</i> <b>εἰς</b> (eis, + accusative) <small>‘at something or someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be able to see; (<i>with negative</i>) to be unable to see, to be blind</li><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to look a certain way [<i>with</i> accusative adjective <i>or</i> adverb]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Infinitive is added to an adjective, adverb, and so on to indicate that the description relates to sight: to see; to look at; to behold</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to see; to perceive; to observe [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> participle <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to find out [<i>with</i> indirect question]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make sure [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(ambitransitive,&#32;figurative) to see with the mind; to understand</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to provide [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to watch, guard”). There may have been an intermediate form <i>&#42;ϝορᾰ́ω</i> (&#42;worắō), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;worā́ō</i>. If there was such a form with <i>ϝ-</i> (w-), then it is unclear why the augmented stem <i>ἑωρ-</i> (heōr-) occurs only in Attic, while <i>ὡρ-</i> (hōr-) is used in Homer and inscriptions.</p><p>Cognate with <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros, “watcher; guardian”), <i>ὤρᾱ</i> (ṓrā, “care; concern”), Latin <i>vereor</i> (“fear”), English <i>aware</i> (“vigilant; conscious”) and <i>wary</i> (“cautious of danger”).</p><p>Forms in <i>ὀψ-</i> (ops-), <i>ὀπ-</i> (op-) are from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i> (“to see”) (whence <i>ὄψ</i> (óps), <i>ὄμμᾰ</i> (ómmă)).</p><p>Forms in <i>εἰδ-</i> (eid-) are from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see”) (whence <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos), <i>ῐ̔́στωρ</i> (hĭ́stōr)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>form, shape</li><li>look, appearance, semblance</li><li>type, sort, class</li><li>style</li><li>notion, idea</li></ol><p>Verbal abstract from <i>ῐ̓δεῖν</i> (ĭdeîn, aor. of <i>ὁρᾰ́ω</i> (horắō)), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see”). Closely related to <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one's place of origin</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweat, perspire</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sweyd-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>sūdor</i>, Sanskrit <i>स्वेदते</i> (svedate) and Old English <i>swāt</i> (English <i>sweat</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peculiarity, specific property, unique feature</li><li>peculiarity of style</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>idiom</li><li>style</li></ol></ol><p><i>ἰδῐόω</i> (idĭóō) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă); see <i>ἴδιος</i> (ídios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>peculiarly</li></ol><p>Adverb from <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos, “peculiar”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>privately; by oneself</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fair, comely, good-looking</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see”), the same root of <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos, “form, image”) and <i>ἰδέα</i> (idéa, “look, appearance”). For the formation, compare <i>ἱκανός</i> (hikanós) and <i>πιθανός</i> (pithanós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>εἰδῐκός</i> (eidĭkós): special</li><li>proper, one's own</li></ol><p>See the entry below for more.</p><p>From <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peculiar temperament or habit, idiosyncrasy</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos, “peculiar”) +&lrm; <i>σῠν-</i> (sŭn-, “with”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾶσῐς</i> (krâsĭs, “mixture”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of peculiar nature</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos, “peculiar, one's own”) +&lrm; <i>-φυής</i> (-phuḗs, “-natured”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>particular, special</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰδίωμα</i> (idíōma) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of or for a private person, private</li><li>not done by rules of art, unprofessional, unskillful, rude</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰδιώτης</i> (idiṓtēs) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peculiarity; property; specific character; distinctive quality</li><li>individuality; particular nature</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) idiom; peculiarity of expression or usage</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) individuality</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos, “one’s own; peculiar”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ῐ̓́δῐος</i> (ĭ́dĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a private person, one not engaged in public affairs</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a private soldier, as opposed to a general</li></ol><li>(<i>adjectival use</i>) private, homely</li><li>commoner, plebeian</li><li>uneducated person, layman, amateur</li><li>one who is not in the know, an outsider</li><li>an ignorant or illiterate person</li><li>one who is awkward, clumsy</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) one's countrymen</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴδιος</i> (ídios, “one's own, private”) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>lo!, behold!</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in giving or offering a thing</i>) there! take it!</li><li>(<i>in repeating another's words quizzingly</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓δοῦ</i> (ĭdoû), aorist middle imperative second singular form of <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō, “to see, to find out, to make sure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>εὐθύνω</i> (euthúnō, “to straighten”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic&#32;and&#32;Ionic;&#32;rare&#32;in&#32;Attic</i>) alternative form of <i>εὐθῠ́ς</i> (euthŭ́s)</li></ol><p>According to Willi, from <i>&#42;εἰθῠ́ς</i> (&#42;eithŭ́s) due to metaphony, in turn from <i>εὐθύς</i> (euthús) due to dissimilation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a phallus carried in the festivals of Bacchus</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the ode sung in honour of it</li><li>the dance accompanying such an ode</li><li>the one who danced in such a dance</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̓θῠ́ς</i> (īthŭ́s, “straight”) +&lrm; <i>φαλλός</i> (phallós, “penis, phallus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moisture, wetness</li><li>secretion, animal juice</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seykʷ-</i> (“to filter”) and cognate with Old English <i>sēon</i> (“to filter”), Russian <i>сцать</i> (scatʹ, “to piss”) and Sanskrit <i>सिञ्चति</i> (siñcáti, “to pour”). Beekes suggests <i>&#42;seyk-</i> as the original root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upright supporting a gallery or loft</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) flooring of the deck, or perhaps the joists which support it</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰκρῐόω</i> (ikrĭóō, “to erect scaffolding”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the commander of a troop of horse</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̓́λη</i> (ī́lē, “troop of horse”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓ρχή</i> (ărkhḗ, “rule, authority”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope, band, twisted cord</li><li>redwing (<i>Turdus iliacus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind”). Further related to <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to turn, roll”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mud, slime, silt</li><li>dregs, sediment of wine</li><li>impurity</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ἀχλύς</i> (akhlús) and <i>δελφύς</i> (delphús). Identical with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;jьlъ</i> (“silt, clay”) and Latvian <i>īls</i> (“very dark”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strand, fibre</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴς</i> (ís, “muscle of the body”) +&lrm; <i>-άρῐον</i> (-árĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἰνέω</i> (inéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) to carry off by evacuations</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) occipital bone</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴς</i> (ís, “muscle of the body”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman indiction (tax cycle usually of fifteen years)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>indictiō&#47;-nis</i>. Documented from the 2nd c. BC onwards, including the entire Byzantine period.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chamaleon thistle (<i>Cardopatium corymbosum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰξός</i> (ixós, “mistletoe”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱς</i> (-ĭ́ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carline thistle (<i>Carlina gummifera</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰξός</i> (ixós, “mistletoe”) with a suffix found in other plant names, like <i>βαλσαμίνη</i> (balsamínē) and <i>ἑλλεβορίνη</i> (helleborínē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>European mistletoe (<i>Viscum album</i>)</li><li>mistletoe berry</li><li>birdlime prepared from the mistletoe berry</li><li>oak gum, used for the same purpose</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) any sticky substance</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) skinflint, miser</li></ol><p>An old cultural word, identical with Latin <i>viscum</i> (“mistletoe”), thus implying a pre-form <i>&#42;ϝιξ(ο-)</i> (&#42;wix(o-)). It has been compared with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;višьňa</i> (“sour cherry”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wīhsilō</i> (“sour cherry”). Chantraine considers a common derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyḱs-</i>, but, according to Beekes, given its structure and limited distribution it is likely a loanword from a European substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) waist; loins</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ὀσφῦς</i> (osphûs), <i>δελφῡ́ς</i> (delphū́s) and <i>νηδῡ́ς</i> (nēdū́s). Etymological relation with Latin <i>īlia</i> (“side of the body”) is improbable. Furnée proposed to connect <i>ῐ̓σχῐ́ον</i> (ĭskhĭ́on, “hip”); if correct, the word is Pre-Greek because of the variation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Like birdlime, sticky, clammy.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰξός</i> (ixós, “oak mistletoe; birdlime”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “-like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>violet</li></ol><p><i>ἴον</i> (íon) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to Judaize; to adopt Jewish customs and traditions</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῐ̓ουδαῖος</i> (Ĭoudaîos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrow-pouring (<i>epithet of Artemis</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally analysed as <i>ῑ̓ός</i> (īós, “arrow”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”), but may be from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ísuǵʰesṛ</i> (“arrow-handed”), from <i>&#42;(H)ísus</i> (“arrow”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;ǵʰésōr</i> (“hand”), equivalent to <i>ῑ̓ός</i> (īós, “arrow”) +&lrm; <i>χείρ</i> (kheír, “hand”); compare Sanskrit <i>इषुहस्त</i> (iṣuhasta, “arrow-handed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>An exclamation of joy or grief.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small oven</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓πνός</i> (ĭpnós, “oven; furnace”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boiler; cauldron</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓πνός</i> (ĭpnós, “oven, furnace”) +&lrm; <i>λέβης</i> (lébēs, “kettle; cauldron”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oven; furnace</li><li>kitchen; the place of the oven</li><li>lantern</li></ol><p>Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hukʷ-</i> (“cooking pot”) (compare Sanskrit <i>उखा</i> (ukhā), Albanian <i>anë</i>, Latin <i>ōlla</i>, Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uhnaz</i>, <i>&#42;uhwnaz</i>). Beekes suggests a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sep-</i> (“to boil, bake”), like Ancient Greek <i>ἔψω</i> (épsō, “to boil, seethe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Arcadocypriot</i>) region</li></ol><p>The meaning of the word, found in a Cypriot inscription, is uncertain. Perhaps a misreading of <i>οἰρών</i> (oirṓn, “furrow of a plough”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dyer's woad (<i>Isatis tinctoria</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Perhaps loaned from a substrate source common to Latin <i>vitrum</i> (“glass, dyer's woad”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waizdaz</i>. Beekes notes that the plant was probably named for its glass-like color.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal in magnitude</li><li>equal in age</li></ol><p><i>ἴσος</i> (ísos) +&lrm; <i>ἡλίκος</i> (hēlíkos)</p><p>Inflectional form.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same age with (someone [dative])</li></ol><p><i>ἴσος</i> (ísos) +&lrm; <i>ἧλιξ</i> (hêlix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) isicium, a dish made of mincemeat, probably similar to a meatball or a mincemeat patty</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>īsicium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>isapostle</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equal right to speak, equality of all in freedom of speech, particularly in the public assembly</li><li>political equality in general, equal rights</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos, “equal, same”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγορεύω</i> (agoreúō, “to say, speak in the assembly”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) equinoctial</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσο-</i> (iso-, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>ἡμερινός</i> (hēmerinós, “of day”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>neck, narrow passage of the body</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) pharynx, fauces</li></ol><li>neck of land between two seas; isthmus</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>narrow ridge</li><li>strait, narrow channel (<i>of the sea</i>)</li></ol></ol><p>A derivation from <i>εἶμῐ</i> (eîmĭ, “to go or come”) with a suffix <i>-θμο-</i> has been assumed, comparing also Old Norse <i>eið</i> (“isthmus”). However, the <i>-σ-</i> is unexplained, so the word could well be of Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἰσθμός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isthmus of Corinth</li><li>narrow ridge of Caucasus, between the Caspian Sea and the Black Sea</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓σθμός</i> (ĭsthmós, “neck of land between two seas”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>combining form of <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>living on an equal footing</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓σο-</i> (ĭso-) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́αιτᾰ</i> (dĭ́aită).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Godlike, similar or equal to a god.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσο-</i> (iso-, “equal (to)”) +&lrm; <i>δαίμων</i> (daímōn, “a god”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an equivalent manner</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓σοδῠ́νᾰμος</i> (ĭsodŭ́nămos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in an equivalent manner</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(mathematics,&#32;philosophy) that which is equivalent in effect (to something else)</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) that which is logically equivalent (to something else)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal in force, power or strength</li><li>equivalent in meaning</li><li>equal in value; equivalent [(<i>chiefly</i>) <i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσο-</i> (iso-, “equal, equivalent”) +&lrm; <i>δῠ́νᾰμῐς</i> (dŭ́nămĭs, “force, power, strength, authority, value, meaning”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, suffix forming second-declension compound adjectives).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) to make equal with the gods</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσόθεος</i> (isótheos, “equal to gods”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of equal measure; isometric</li></ol><p><i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>μέτρον</i> (métron, “measure”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an equal distribution, equal proportion, equilibrium</li><li>equal rights, equality</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be equally balanced, in equipoise</li></ol><p>From ἰσόρροπος, from <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέπω</i> (rhépō, “to incline”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(geometry,&#32;of triangles) Having two sides equal, isosceles</li><li>(mathematics,&#32;of numbers) Able to be divided into two parts, even</li><li>(rhetoric,&#32;of periods) Containing two equal parts</li><li>(medicine,&#32;of bandages) Having similarly shaped ends</li></ol><p><i>ἴσος</i> (ísos, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>σκέλος</i> (skélos, “leg”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equality of votes</li><li>equal right to vote, equal franchise</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓σο-</i> (“iso-, <i>ἴσος</i> (“equal”) +&lrm; <i>ψῆφ(ος)</i> (psêph(os), “voting pebble”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, “feminine suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dried fig</li><li>kind of spurge (<i>Euphorbia apios</i>)</li></ol><p>Same origin as <i>ῐ̓σχνός</i> (ĭskhnós, “dry; arid”) and <i>ῐ̓σχᾰλέος</i> (ĭskhăléos, “dry; barren”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hip-joint</li><li>area around the hip-joint; haunch</li></ol><p>No etymology. Furnée connects the word with <i>ἰξῡ́ς</i> (ixū́s, “loins”) assuming a common Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hip disease</li><li>(neurology,&#32;pathology) sciatica</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσχίον</i> (iskhíon) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relational</i>) of the hips</li><li>(pathology,&#32;neurology) subject to sciatica</li><li>good for sciatica</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσχιάς</i> (iskhiás) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dry, withered, shriveled</li><li>thin, lean, meager</li><li>weak, feeble</li><li>(<i>of a swelling</i>) reduced</li><li>(<i>of clothing materials</i>) light, thin</li><li>(<i>of style</i>) spare, plain, unadorned</li></ol><p>A cognate <i>u-</i>stem has been assumed in Avestan <i>𐬵𐬌𐬱𐬐𐬎</i> (hišku, “dry”), in Welsh <i>hysb</i> (“dry”) and Middle Irish <i>sesc</i> (“dry”). These are derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyk-</i> (“to wither”), found also in Latin <i>siccus</i> (“dry”). For this adjective, an ad hoc base form <i>&#42;si-sk-sno-</i> has been assumed. From the same root are <i>ἰσχαλέος</i> (iskhaléos, “dry, barren”) and <i>ἰσχάς</i> (iskhás, “dried fig”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, mighty</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>mighty, powerful</li><li>forcible, obstinate, stiff, stubborn, inveterate, excessive</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓σχ(ῡ́ς)</i> (ĭskh(ū́s), “strength, power”) +&lrm; <i>-υρός</i> (-urós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ῐ̓σχῡρός</i> (ĭskhūrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>strongly, with all force</li><li>very much, exceedingly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσχῡρός</i> (iskhūrós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>strength; power; might</li></ol><p>Disputed. Dialectal variants point to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wiskʰū́s</i>, but since the Mycenaean Greek cognate 𐀂𐀱𐀓𐀺𐀈𐀵 (i-su-ku-wo-do-to) lacks the initial /w/, it may have arisen by analogy with <i>&#42;ϝῑ́ς</i> (&#42;wī́s, “power”) (> <i>ῑ̓́ς</i> (ī́s)). Early hypotheses assume a relation to Sanskrit <i>विषहते</i> (viṣahate, “to hold fast; to endure; to withstand; to conquer”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wi-</i> + <i>&#42;seǵʰ-</i> (“to hold, have power”) (see <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō)), or alternatively from the same root via a reduplicated <i>u</i>-stem <i>&#42;si-sǵʰ-ús</i> (cf. <i>ῐ̓́σχω</i> (ĭ́skhō)). Unconvinced by these etymologies, Beekes suggests Pre-Greek substrate origin.</p><p>According to Gamkrelidze &amp; Ivanov, a Kartvelian borrowing, compare Proto-Georgian-Zan <i>&#42;s₁xu-</i> (“fat, stout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be physically strong</li><li>to be mighty or powerful; to prevail</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>not of persons</i>) to prevail</li></ol><li>to be worth</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓σχῡ́ς</i> (ĭskhū́s, “strength”) +&lrm; <i>-ω</i> (-ō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>equal to the gods; godlike</li></ol><p><i>ἰσο-</i> (iso-, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>θεός</i> (theós, “god”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fumitory (<i>Fumaria capreolata</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσο-</i> (iso-, “equal to”) +&lrm; <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr, “fire”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in equipoise, of the balance</li></ol><p><i>ἴσος</i> (ísos, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέπω</i> (rhépō, “to incline”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with or by an equal number of votes</li><li>having an equal vote with others, equal in franchise</li><li>equal in numerical value</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of words in which the letters add to the same sum</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>ψῆφος</i> (psêphos, “vote”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same age with (someone [dative])</li></ol><p><i>ἴσος</i> (ísos) +&lrm; <i>ἧλιξ</i> (hêlix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>willow</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéh₁itis</i> (“twining object”), with remodeling of the suffix like in other tree names (compare <i>ἀκτέᾱ</i> (aktéā, “elder (tree)”) and <i>πτελέᾱ</i> (pteléā, “elm”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of willow</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̓τέᾱ</i> (ītéā, “willow”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>headlong; hasty; eager</li><li>bold; reckless; precipitous</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>εἶμῐ</i> (eîmĭ, “to be going to go”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ey-</i>, though most other oxytones in <i>-ᾰμός</i> (-ămós) are substantives (such as <i>ποτᾰμός</i> (potămós)); Beekes suggests it comes from the Attic popular language.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>howling, shrieking, as of men in pain</li><li>shout of a herald</li><li>hissing of snakes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰῡ́ζω</i> (iū́zō, “to cry aloud, howl”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to crave, yearn, desire</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;ῑ̓χω</i> (&#42;īkhō), Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ī́kʰō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁í-h₁eyǵʰ-ti ~ &#42;h₁í-h₁iǵʰ-n̥ti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁eyǵʰ-</i>. Beekes explains the extension of the term as an analogical remodeling after other words such as <i>ὑφανάω</i> (huphanáō), <i>ὑφαίνω</i> (huphaínō). Related to Ancient Greek <i>ἰχαίνω</i> (ikhaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fish, angle</li><li>to fish for [<i>with</i> accusative]</li><li>to sport (of fish)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθυάω</i> (ikhthuáō, fish, angle) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fish, angle</li><li>to fish for (<i>+ accusative</i>)</li><li>to sport (<i>of fish</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθῡ́ς</i> (ikhthū́s, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(plural only,&#32;figurative) scrapings; shavings</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fish-, of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fish-eating</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + -βόρος, from <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “Ι eat, eat up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>producing fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “procreation, begetting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>holding fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai, “Ι take, accept, receive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish-market</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon, “-shop, -market”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fishy</li><li>scaly</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) dirty; unclean</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>king of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>μέδων</i> (médōn, “ruler”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fish-ruling</li><li>one that rules fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>νόμος</i> (nómos, “law, ordinance”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fish-like, of fishes</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓χθῡ́ς</i> (ĭkhthū́s, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-like, connected to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cyclamen</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>θηράω</i> (thēráō, “I hunt, chase”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisherman</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>θηράω</i> (thēráō, “I hunt, chase”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>θηράω</i> (thēráō, “I hunt, chase”) + <i>-ία</i> (-ía, “<i>forms abstract nouns</i>”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisherman</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisherman</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>θηράω</i> (thēráō, “I hunt, chase”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishing</li></ol><p>By ellipsis, from ἰχθυοθηρικὴ [<i>τέχνη</i>]. From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>θηράω</i> (thēráō, “I hunt, chase”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>half-man and half-fish, merman/mermaid</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>κένταυρος</i> (kéntauros, “monsters of double shape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to discuss fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “language, discourse”) + <i>-έω</i> (-éō, “<i>form verbs from nouns</i>”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plague of fish</li><li>common epithet for a fish-eater</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>λύμη</i> (lúmē, “maltreatment, defilement”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishmonger</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>μετάβολος</i> (metábolos, “huckster, retail dealer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishmonger's, fish market</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓χθῡ́ς</i> (ĭkhthū́s, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>πωλέω</i> (pōléō, “to sell”) +&lrm; <i>-εῖον</i> (-eîon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fishmonger</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓χθῠ́ς</i> (ĭkhthŭ́s, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-πώλης</i> (-pṓlēs, “seller; dealer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>feeding fish</li><li>full of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓χθῡ́ς</i> (ĭkhthū́s, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to feed, rear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>angler</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓χθῡ́ς</i> (ĭkhthū́s, “fish”) + <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “to draw up; to drag up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eating fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓χθῡ́ς</i> (ĭkhthū́s, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-φᾰ́γος</i> (-phắgos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>piercing fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harpoon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>πείρω</i> (peírō, “I pierce, run through”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stealer of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fish-crowned</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>στέφω</i> (stéphō, “I put around, I crown”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ἰχθυοφάγος</i> (ikhthuophágos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>killing fish, fish killing</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>φόνος</i> (phónos, “murder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fish-, of fish</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eaten by fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + -βρωτος, from <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “Ι eat, eat up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of fish, fishy (<i>Homeric epithet&#32;of the sea</i>)</li><li>consisting of fish</li><li>fishlike</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish-glue</li><li>isinglass</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>κόλλα</i> (kólla, “glue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who prophesies by means of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>μάντις</i> (mántis, “prophet, soothsayer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swarming with fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “I stream, gush”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fish-eating</li><li>eating fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>φάγος</i> (phágos, “an eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>producing fish</li><li>carrying fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros, “bearing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>full of fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓χθῡ́ς</i> (ĭkhthū́s, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) a fishscale</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;chiefly&#32;in the plural) scrapings, shavings</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓χθῠ́ᾱ</i> (ĭkhthŭ́ā) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dried fish-skin</li><li>a pot (possibly used for pickled fish)</li><li>fishery (fish farm)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓χθῡ́ς</i> (ĭkhthū́s).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(for) striking fish, catching fish</li><li>speared fish</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fisher, angler</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + -βόλος, from <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “I throw or cast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fed on by fish</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + -βοτος, from <i>βιβρώσκω</i> (bibrṓskō, “Ι eat, eat up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδιον</i> (-ídion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trident</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰχθύς</i> (ikhthús, “fish”) + <i>κέντρον</i> (kéntron, “any sharp point”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fish</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) stupid person</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fish market</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰǵʰúHs</i> (“fish”), but the initial ἰ is unetymological and problematic. Compare Old Armenian <i>ձուկն</i> (jukn) and Lithuanian <i>žuvi̇̀s</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) alternative form of <i>ῐ̓χθῡ́ς</i> (ĭkhthū́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Egyptian animal of the weasel-kind, which hunts out crocodile's eggs (asp's eggs), ichneumon, <i>Herpestes ichneumon</i></li><li>a small kind of wasp, that hunts spiders, <i>Sceliphron spirifex</i></li></ol><p>Literally, "the tracer", from <i>ἰχνεύω</i> (ikhneúō, “to trace”) +&lrm; <i>-μων</i> (-mōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ichor, the fluid which runs through the veins of gods</li><li>lymph, serum, the watery part of blood</li><li>whey, the watery part of milk</li><li>gravy</li><li>pus, discharge, impure fluid</li><li>naphtha (probably as the supposed putrefaction of giants)</li></ol><p>Unknown, likely Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) of a jubilee</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰώβηλος</i> (iṓbēlos, “jubilee”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rout, pursuit</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, perhaps from Pre-Greek and related to <i>διώκω</i> (diṓkō, “to cause to run”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic; pertaining to the Ἴωνες</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of fish</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poisonous</li><li>violet-coloured, dark</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰός</i> (iós, “poison”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wisós</i> (“slime, poison”).</p><p>From <i>ἴον</i> (íon, “violet (flower)”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis), which is ultimately of Mediterranean substrate origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrow</li><li>poison, venom</li><li>rust, verdigris</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>selfsame; identical</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ihwós</i>, a thematicization of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(H)isu-</i> (“arrow”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>इषु</i> (íṣu, “arrow”) and Avestan <i>𐬌𐬱𐬎</i> (išu, “arrow”).</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wihós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wisós</i> (“fluidity, slime, poison”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>विष</i> (víṣa), Latin <i>vīrus</i>, Tocharian A <i>wäs</i>, Tocharian B <i>wase</i>, and Middle Irish <i>fí</i>.</p><p>Uncertain. May be of the same origin as Etymology 2.</p><p>Expansion of original feminine-only pronoun <i>ἴᾱ</i> (íā), from <i>&#42;(h₁)éy</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Lyric) will; wish; desire</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>exclamation of joy</li><li>exclamation of grief or suffering</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Boeotian form of <i>ἐγώ</i> (egṓ, “I”)</li></ol><p>Echoic; compare Latin <i>iō</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἰώ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Io</li></ol><p>Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jubilee</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יוֹבֵל</i> (yōḇēl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>violet-coloured, violet</li><li>smelling of violets, violet-scented</li><li>like verdigris, green</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) “green with envy”, envious</li></ol><li>rust-coloured, ferruginous</li><li>poisonous, venomous</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(figuratively,&#32;of persons) virulent</li></ol><li>(<i>ὄδ-ωδα</i>) acrid</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓́ον</i> (ĭ́on, “violet, <i>Viola odorata</i>”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “smelling of; full of, like”).</p><p><i>ῑ̓ός</i> (īós, “rust, verdigris; poison, venom”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “full of, like”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Boeotian form of <i>ἐγώ</i> (egṓ, “I”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drink prepared from honey, wine and violets</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>ἴον</i> (íon, “violet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iota, the name for the ninth letter Ι (I), ι (i) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) a jot; an iota (a small mark or stroke)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤉𐤃</i> (yd‬ /⁠yōd⁠/, the name of <i>𐤉</i> (y)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawk; falcon</li><li>a kind of fish</li><li>name for a grade of initiates in Mithras worship</li><li>name of a type of bandage</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wīrāks</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wiHrós</i> (“man”), perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyh₁-</i> (“to chase, pursue”), with possible folk-etymological influence by <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós, “holy, divine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priestess</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερεύς</i> (hiereús) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă). Synchronically analyzable as <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós) +&lrm; <i>-εια</i> (-eia). Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀂𐀋𐀩𐀊</i> (i-je-re-ja)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sweat, perspire</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔δρώς</i> (hĭdrṓs, “sweat”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to seat, make to sit down</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be seated, sit still</li><li>to settle persons in a place</li><li>to establish, found, set up</li></ol><p>Per Schwyzer and Frisk, probably from an unattested noun <i>&#42;ἱδρυ-</i> (&#42;hidru-), a derivative in <i>-ρ-</i> (-r-) (comparing <i>ἕδρα</i> (hédra, “seat”)) of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit; settle”), also seen in <i>ἕζομαι</i> (hézomai, “to sit”) and <i>ἵζω</i> (hízō, “to sit, seat, place”), with initial <i>ἱ</i> (hi) from analogy with <i>ἵζω</i> (hízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sweat; perspiration</li><li>gum; resin; sap (exudation or distillation from trees)</li><li>juice; moisture</li><li>anything earned by the sweat of one's brow</li></ol><p>Ultimately, the term derives from the root <i>&#42;sweyd-</i> (“to sweat”), whence also <i>ἰδίω</i> (idíō) and <i>ἶδος</i> (îdos)). More specifically, Beekes proposes that the term may reflect the addition of <i>&#42;-ōs</i>—which is possibly found in Latin <i>sūdor</i>—to a Proto-Indo-European r-stem <i>&#42;swid-r-</i>, whence also perhaps Old Armenian <i>քիրտն</i> (kʻirtn), Latvian <i>sviedri</i>, and Albanian <i>djersë</i>. The older s-stem may have then underwent conversion into a t-stem according to the model of terms such as <i>ἔρως</i> (érōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (female) member of a religious institution</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>prickly goldenfleece; false hawkbit (<i>Urospermum picroides</i>)</li><li>a kind of composite (<i>Hymenonema graecum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔έρᾱξ</i> (hĭérāx, “hawk”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>high priest</li><li>hierarch</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) bishop</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler”, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hawk-faced</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔έρᾱξ</i> (hĭérāx, “hawk”) +&lrm; <i>πρόσωπον</i> (prósōpon, “face”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corncockle (<i>Agrostemma githago</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔έρᾱξ</i> (hĭérāx, “hawk; falcon”) +&lrm; <i>πόδῐον</i> (pódĭon, “little foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spiritual superiority</li><li>prelacy, prelature</li><li>hierarchy</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἱεράρχης</i> (hierárkhēs, “high priest”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, noun-forming suffix).<br /></p><p>By surface analysis, <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-αρχία</i> (-arkhía, “rule, government”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the priest's office, priestly</li><li>devoted to sacred purposes</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>priest; diviner; sacrificer</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix for person concerned). Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀂𐀋𐀩𐀄</i> (i-je-re-u).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) to slaughter (for sacrifice)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vervain (<i>Verbena officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós, “sacred, holy”) +&lrm; <i>βοτᾰ́νη</i> (botắnē, “grass, herb, fodder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hieroglyphic, pertaining to hieroglyphs</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερόγλῠφος</i> (hieróglŭphos, “carver of hieroglyphics”) + <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A depositary for holy things</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “sacred, holy”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herald or attendant at a sacrifice</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>κῆρυξ</i> (kêrux, “herald”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who conveys the sacred tribute</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy, sacred”) +&lrm; <i>πομπός</i> (pompós, “conductor”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hierophant; one who teaches rites of sacrifice and worship</li><li>(<i>at Rome</i>) pontifex; high priest</li><li>hierophant; a mystical expounder</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “show”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guardian of a temple</li><li>pontifex</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>carver of hieroglyphics</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “sacred, holy”) or <i>γλυφή</i> (gluphḗ, “carved work”). Calque of Egyptian <i>mdw-nṯr</i> (“the god’s word”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of prophetic tongue</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “sacred, holy”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶσσα</i> (glôssa, “tongue”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hierodule, a temple slave, often one performing religious prostitution</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “sacred”) +&lrm; <i>δοῦλος</i> (doûlos, “slave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher's broom (<i>Ruscus aculeatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “sacred, holy”) +&lrm; <i>μῠ́ρτος</i> (mŭ́rtos, “myrtle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a sacrificial offering</li><li>an omen received following such an offering</li></ol><li>a sacred rite</li><li>a holy place, such as a temple, shrine or sanctuary</li></ol><p>Neuter substantive of <i>ῑ̆̔ερός</i> (hī̆erós, “holy”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>connected with the gods, divine</li><li>holy, sacred, consecrated, priestly</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a (male) member of a religious institution</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;iherós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ish₂ros</i> (“holy”). There are a number of candidate cognates with this word; these include Sanskrit <i>इषिर</i> (iṣirá, “strong, active”) and Oscan <i>𐌀𐌉𐌔𐌖𐌔𐌉𐌔</i> (aisusis, “sacrifices”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to sit, seat</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sit down</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;of soil) to settle down</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵζω</i> (hízō, “I sit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to arrive, come</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to come to, reach, attain</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to approach as a suppliant</li></ol><p>Nasal-suffixed form of <i>ἵκω</i> (híkō); compare <i>ἱκνέομαι</i> (hiknéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>One who comes looking for aid or protection, suppliant</li><li>One who comes looking for purification after having murdered someone: common in the Iliad and Odyssey</li></ol><p><i>ῐ̔κνέομαι</i> (hĭknéomai, “to come”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er”, masculine agent-noun suffix): literally, “comer”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>becoming, befitting, sufficient</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of people</i>) competent, strong or skillful enough</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) adequate, enough, considerable</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>satisfactory</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἱκνέομαι</i> (hiknéomai, “to be appropriate”) +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sufficiency, fitness</li></ol><p><i>ῐ̔κᾰνός</i> (hĭkănós) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῐ̔κᾰνός</i> (hĭkănós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ῐ̔κᾰνός</i> (hĭkănós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Derived from <i>ἱκανός</i> (hikanós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱκανός</i> (hikanós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>supplication, the prayer of a suppliant</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱκέτης</i> (hikétēs, “suppliant”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἱκεσία)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hicesia; Basiluzzo, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Old Attic form of <i>ἱκεσίᾱ</i> (hikesíā, “supplication”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to beg</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱκέτης</i> (hikétēs, “suppliant”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to come, reach, arrive at</li><li>(impersonal,&#32;in present and imperfect) to be appropriate, fitting, proper [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘for someone, something’</small> <i>and</i> infinitive <small>‘to do something’</small>]</li></ol><p>Nasal-suffixed form of <i>ἵκω</i> (híkō, “to come”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ῑ̔κόμεθα</i> (hīkómetha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something mildly shining</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔λᾰρός</i> (hĭlărós, “cheerful”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐᾰ</i> (-ĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to appease, conciliate, propitiate; to expiate</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be merciful, gracious</li></ol><p>From the reduplicated <i>&#42;si-sl̥h₂-sḱetor</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selh₂-</i> (“mercy, comfort”). By surface analysis, <i>ῑ̔́λαος</i> (hī́laos, “propitious, gracious”) +&lrm; <i>-σκω</i> (-skō).</p><p>Directly cognate to Old Armenian <i>աղաչեմ</i> (ałačʻem, “to pray”), and further to Latin <i>sōlor</i> (“to comfort”), though probably not Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sēliz</i> (“fortunate”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be gracious</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̔́λᾱος</i> (hī́lāos, “propitious, gracious”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cheerful, merry, joyous, glad, happy</li></ol><p>Stem ἱλα- as in <i>ῑ̔λᾰ́σκομαι</i> (hīlắskomai, “to appease”), <i>ἵλαος</i> (hílaos, “gracious”) + <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a propitiator, one who propitiates</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̔λᾰ́σκομαι</i> (hīlắskomai, “to appease”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of leather straps</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔μᾰ́ς</i> (hĭmắs, “leather strap”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leather strap or thong</li><li>straps on which the body of a chariot was hung</li><li>boxing glove, consisting of several straps put round the hand</li><li>chinstrap of a helmet</li><li>magic girdle of Aphrodite</li><li>whip, scourge</li><li>thong or latchet of a sandal</li><li>dog leash</li><li>well-rope</li><li>diseased condition of the uvula</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) planks laid on rafters</li></ol><p>As a secondary formation in <i>-ντ-</i> (-nt-), presupposes a noun <i>&#42;ῐ̔μᾱ</i> (&#42;hĭmā) or <i>&#42;ῐ̔μᾰ</i> (&#42;hĭmă) meaning "rope", which is also continued in <i>ῐ̔μᾰ́ω</i> (hĭmắō), <i>ῐ̔μᾰ́σθλη</i> (hĭmắsthlē) and <i>ῐ̔μαῖος</i> (hĭmaîos). An n-stem is continued by <i>ῑ̔μονῐᾱ́</i> (hīmonĭā́), which probably derives from <i>&#42;ῑ̔μων</i> (&#42;hīmōn). This last form exactly matches Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīmô</i> (“rope”), and also Sanskrit <i>सी॒मन्</i> (sīmán, “border; frontier; margin”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sh₂i-men</i> (“to tie; to bind”). A suffix <i>-m-</i> is also found in Irish <i>sim</i> (“chain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thong of a whip</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) rudder of a ship</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sh₂i-men</i> (“to tie; to bind”), like <i>ῐ̔μᾱ́ς</i> (hĭmā́s, “leather strap”), <i>ῐ̔μᾰ́ω</i> (hĭmắō, “to draw up water from a well”) and <i>ῑ̔μονῐᾱ́</i> (hīmonĭā́, “well-rope”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flog horses</li><li>to smite; to strike</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔μᾰ́ς</i> (hĭmắs, “leather strap”) +&lrm; <i>-σσω</i> (-ssō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>outer garment, cloak, mantle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) clothes</li></ol><li>a cloth</li></ol><p>From <i>ἷμᾰ</i> (hîmă), <i>εἷμᾰ</i> (heîmă, “garment”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) halyard</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔μᾰ́ς</i> (hĭmắs, “leather strap”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leather band</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔μᾱ́ς</i> (hĭmā́s, “leather strap or thong”) +&lrm; <i>δεσμός</i> (desmós, “bond; fetter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spindleshanks</li><li>stilt (bird of the genus <i>Himantopus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔μᾰ́ς</i> (hĭmắs, “leather strap”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A wardrobe</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̔μάτιον</i> (hīmátion, “cloak”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>keeper of the wardrobe</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̔μᾰ́τῐον</i> (hīmắtĭon, “cloth”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>longed for; desired; lovely</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̔μείρω</i> (hīmeírō, “to long for; to desire”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lovely; delightful; charming</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̔́μερος</i> (hī́meros) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>well-rope</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from unattested <i>&#42;ἱ̄μων</i> (&#42;hīmōn), which would match Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sīmô</i> (“rope”), and also Sanskrit <i>सी॒मन्</i> (sīmán, “border; frontier; margin”), all from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sh₂i-men</i> (“to tie; to bind”). Compare <i>ῐ̔μᾱ́ς</i> (hĭmā́s), <i>ῐ̔μᾰ́ω</i> (hĭmắō), <i>ῐ̔μᾰ́σθλη</i> (hĭmắsthlē) and <i>ῐ̔μαῖος</i> (hĭmaîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially of ships</i>) carrying horses</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γωγός</i> (ăgōgós, “leading, guiding”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>charioteer (chariot-driver or person fighting from a chariot)</li><li>rider (of a horse), horseman, cavalryman</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) knights (early Greek aristocracy)</li><li>A translation of Latin <i>eques</i>.</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, suffix for masculine person concerned).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be a horseman or rider; to ride</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) horse-driver, chariot-fighter, knight</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>ἐλαύνω</i> (elaúnō, “drive”) +&lrm; <i>-τᾰ</i> (-tă, agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for farriery, veterinary</li></ol><p><i>ἱππιᾱτρός</i> (hippiātrós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a veterinary surgeon, a farrier</li></ol><p><i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>ῑ̓ᾱτρός</i> (īātrós, “physician”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>devil's coach horse beetle (<i>Ocypus olens</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>κάνθαρος</i> (kántharos, “beetle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>driver of horses, travelling by means of horses</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos) +&lrm; <i>κέλευθος</i> (kéleuthos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>centaur</li><li>any mythical creature &lsqb;later&rsqb;</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>Κένταυρος</i> (Kéntauros, “Centaur”). While "horse centaur" may seem redundant, it should be noted that <i>Κένταυρος</i> (Kéntauros) originally referred to a race of people, the Centaurs, who are associated in some myths with the origin of the creature "centaur". This term likely made the distinction between a person of the Centaur race and the creature, until later, when the word <i>κένταυρος</i> (kéntauros) simply referred to the creature. Note that while Wiktionary distinguishes between upper and lower case in Ancient Greek words, Ancient Greek did not (minuscules were not invented until the seventh century).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marshaller, arranger of chariots</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>κορυστής</i> (korustḗs, “helmed man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>victory in a cavalry engagement.</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-κρᾰτῐ́ᾱ</i> (-krătĭ́ā, “rule”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dock-sorrel (<i>Rumex aquaticus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>λάπαθον</i> (lápathon, “monk's rhubarb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sort of moss used in farriery</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>λειχήν</i> (leikhḗn, “liverwort”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse fennel (<i>Prangos ferulacea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>μάραθον</i> (márathon, “fennel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorn apple (<i>Datura stramonium</i>), of which horses are madly fond or which makes them mad</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) small black fleshy substance on the forehead of a new-born foal</li><li>mucous humor that runs from mares</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ῐ̔ππομᾰνής</i> (hĭppomănḗs, “swarming with horses”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>horse-faced (<i>used as an epithet of the Moon-goddess</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>πρόσωπον</i> (prósōpon, “face”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hippopotamus</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>ποτᾰμός</i> (potămós, “river”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alexanders (<i>Smyrnium olusatrum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>σέλινον</i> (sélinon, “celery”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of chariot driving, horsemanship</li><li>(collective,&#32;military) cavalry</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-σύνη</i> (-súnē, abstract suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a mounted archer</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>τοξότης</i> (toxótēs, “archer”), from <i>τόξον</i> (tóxon, “bow”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>"horse-fear", a fabulous plant</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>φόβος</i> (phóbos, “fear”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horsekeeper</li></ol><p>The Mycenaean Greek cognate <i>𐀂𐀡𐀡𐀦𐀂</i> (i-po-po-qo-i, “ἱπποφορβοῖς”) points to Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ikkʷopʰorgʷós</i>. Equivalent to <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;φορβός</i> (&#42;phorbós, “feeder”), the second component from <i>φέρβω</i> (phérbō, “to feed, nourish”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) grazed by horses (of pastures)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) + the root of <i>βόσκω</i> (bóskō, “to feed, nourish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;poetic) horse-taming (<i>used as an epithet of the Trojans in general and of several heroes individually</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) + the root of <i>δαμάζω</i> (damázō)/<i>δαμνάω</i> (damnáō)/<i>δάμνημι</i> (dámnēmi, “to tame”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;demh₂-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) having a thick horsehair plume (<i>epithet of helmets</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>δασύς</i> (dasús, “hairy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hippodrome, a circuit for chariot-racing</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mythology</i>) monster with the body of a horse and the tail of a fish, hippocampus</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) sea horse (fish of the genus <i>Hippocampus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>κάμπος</i> (kámpos, “sea-monster”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>tremendously steep</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>κρημνός</i> (krēmnós, “crag, precipice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῐ̔ππότης</i> (hĭppótēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a horse-rider; horseman; knight</li><li>(<i>as an epithet of Nestor and other heroes in Homer</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er”, agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diarrhea of horses</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>τῖλος</i> (tîlos, “thin stool, as in diarrhea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of starthistle (<i>Centaurea spinosa</i>)</li></ol><p>The first element is from <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>belladonna (<i>Atropa belladonna</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>φλόμος</i> (phlómos, “mullein”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Epic form of <i>ῐ̔ερεύς</i> (hĭereús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ἑστίᾱ</i> (hestíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) sail</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔στός</i> (hĭstós, “mast”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mast-holder, a piece of wood standing up from the stern, on which the mast rested when let down</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱστός</i> (histós, “mast”) +&lrm; <i>δοκή</i> (dokḗ, “container”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) sailyard</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱστός</i> (histós, “mast”) +&lrm; <i>κεραία</i> (keraía, “projecting beam”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of wood set in the keel to which the mast was bound, or, a hole in the keel for stepping the mast</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱστός</i> (histós, “mast”) +&lrm; <i>πέδη</i> (pédē, “fetter, shackle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to inquire, ask (of someone, or regarding someone/something)</li><li>to examine, observe</li><li>to record, give an account of what one has learned</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵστωρ</i> (hístōr) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inquiry, examination, systematic observation, science</li><li>body of knowledge obtained by systematic inquiry</li><li>written account of such inquiries, narrative, history</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔στορέω</i> (hĭstoréō, “to inquire”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>ἵστωρ</i> (hístōr, “one who knows, wise one”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the historical (that which is historical)</li><li>a historical fact or account</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exact, precise, scientific</li><li>belonging or pertaining to history, historical</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a historian</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔στορῐ́ᾱ</i> (hĭstorĭ́ā, “inquiry”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long beams of the loom, between which the web was stretched</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱστός</i> (histós, “mast, beam, loom”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mast</li><li>shinbone</li><li>(<i>weaving</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>beam</li><li>loom</li><li>web</li></ol></ol><p>Related to <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;steh₂-</i> (“to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Boeotian form of <i>ἐγώ</i> (egṓ, “I”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>iamb, a metrical foot</li><li>iambic verse or poem</li><li>(<i>mostly in plural</i>) lampoon</li><li>kind of extempore play got up by buffoons</li></ol><p>Probably of Pre-Greek substrate (Illyrian/Phrygian) origin; the OED suggests a derivation from <i>ἰάπτω</i> (iáptō, “to assail, attack verbally”), literally "send forth", cognate with <i>ἵημι</i> (híēmi, “to throw, hurl”), as iambic verse was first used by satirists, but this could just be folk etymology. Compare also similar words, like <i>διθύραμβος</i> (dithúrambos), <i>θρίαμβος</i> (thríambos) and <i>ἴθυμβος</i> (íthumbos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cure; remedy</li><li>repair; mend</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̓ᾰ́ομαι</i> (īắomai, “to heal”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>jasper</li><li>black turnip (<i>Bongardia chrysogonum</i>)</li></ol><p>A loanword of unclear source, but possibly Egyptian in origin. Compare Biblical Hebrew <i>יָשְׁפֵה</i> (yāšəfēh, “jasper”), Amarna Akkadian <i>𒉌𒌓𒅀𒀸𒁍</i> (/⁠yašpû⁠/, “name of a stone”) (whence Classical Syriac <i>ܝܫܦܐ</i> (jaʃpɑ(ʔ), “jasper”)). Persian یشپ (yašp) is from the same source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cock or plug in a ship's bottom</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek and possibly related to <i>ἰβάνη</i> (ibánē, “water bucket”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A mortar (vessel used to grind ingredients)</li></ol><p>It can hardly belong to <i>ἴκταρ</i> (íktar, “near”), <i>ἴξ</i> (íx), nor to <i>αἰχμή</i> (aikhmḗ). Beekes assigns this to Pre-Greek origin due to the presence of the cluster <i>-γδ-</i> (-gd-). Probably ultimately from Hurrian <i>𒀉𒆠</i> (/⁠idki, itki⁠/, “mortar”), from the root <i>𒀉</i> (/⁠id-⁠/, “to beat”), whence with metathesis Ugaritic <i>𒅅𒌈</i> (<i>IG-TU</i><sub>4</sub> /⁠ʾiktu?⁠/, “mortar”). On these see Huehnergard, without the Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>timber tree, wood</li><li>wooded hill</li></ol><p>A Pre-Greek word without further etymology.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἴδη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Ida</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ἴ̄δη</i> (í̄dē, “wooded hill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>private affairs; personal interests</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one's relatives</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>something which belongs (to someone or something):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a distinguishing feature</li><li>a characteristic feature of a species</li><li>a personal opinion</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) private affairs; personal interests</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) real estate; interest in land</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>own (pertaining specifically to the referent)</li><li>private; not public</li><li>related to</li><li>separate; distinct</li><li>peculiar; specific</li><li>suitable; appropriate</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>someone who is associated (with someone or something):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>one's guest</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) one's relatives</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) one's close friend or companion</li></ol></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hwédios</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swéd-yo-s</i>, from <i>&#42;swé</i> (“self”). Equivalent to <i>ἕ</i> (hé) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Aeolic</i>) alternative form of <i>ἱδρώς</i> (hidrṓs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἔθρις</i> (éthris)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of Bacchic dance and song</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>ἴαμβος</i> (íambos), <i>θρίαμβος</i> (thríambos) and <i>διθύραμβος</i> (dithúrambos), and like these probably a loanword from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and poetic form of <i>εἴκελος</i> (eíkelos)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἴκελος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ikelos</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴκελος</i> (íkelos, “like, resembling”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) fair, favorable (of the wind; only used to modify <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros))</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common duckweed (<i>Lemna minor</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>ἰκμάς</i> (ikmás, “moisture”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dust or ashes</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the variation proves a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) halfdeck at the stern of a ship</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) platform, stage</li><li>scaffolding, framework</li><li>benches in a theatre</li></ol><p>Technical word without etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>close together</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>near; near to; near by</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a small, worthless fish</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Pre-Greek. The suffix <i>-αρ</i> can also be seen in <i>κύδαρ</i> (kúdar), <i>σκῐ́νᾰρ</i> (skĭ́năr), and <i>σῦφᾰρ</i> (sûphăr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) jaundice, icterus</li><li>golden oriole (<i>Oriolus oriolus</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is reminiscent of <i>ὕδερος</i> (húderos) and <i>χολέρα</i> (kholéra), but further details are unknown. The connection with <i>ἴκτις</i> (íktis, “marten”) and <i>ἴκτινος</i> (íktinos, “kite”) is defended by Grošelj Živa, who assumes a colour root <i>ἰκ-</i> (ik-, “yellow, green”), and compares <i>ἰκμαλέον</i> (ikmaléon, “green, moist”). This remains speculative. According to Furnée, the group <i>-κτ-</i> points to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kite (<i>Milvus milvus</i>)</li></ol><p>Beekes reconstructs Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;tḱiH-in</i> (“kite”), connecting the word with Old Armenian <i>ցին</i> (cʻin, “kite”), Avestan <i>𐬯𐬀𐬉𐬥𐬀</i> (saēna, “a mythical bird”), and Sanskrit <i>श्येन</i> (śyena, “eagle, falcon”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beech marten (<i>Martes foina</i>)</li></ol><p>Compare Old Armenian <i>աքիս</i> (akʻis). Beekes gives no etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ῑ̓λᾰ́ρχης</i> (īlắrkhēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>band, troop of men</li><li>troop of horses, squadron of cavalry</li><li>subdivision of the <i>ἀγέλη</i> (agélē) at Sparta</li></ol><p>Perhaps a nominal formation from <i>ἴλλω</i> (íllō, “to press together”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wi-wl-e</i>, a reduplicative formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to press together”), whence also <i>εἴλω</i> (eílō, “to shut in, press”); see there for more discussion.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἔγχελυς</i> (énkhelus, “eel”).</li></ol><p>The ending is reminiscent of <i>λεβηρίς</i> (lebērís, “snake skin, slough”). The word resembles some Balto-Slavic words for "eel", like Lithuanian <i>ungurỹs</i> and Russian <i>у́горь</i> (úgorʹ). However, the ending rather points to a Pre-Greek word. The relation to <i>ἔγχελυς</i> (énkhelus, “eel”) and Latin <i>anguilla</i> (“eel”) is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mole (animal of the family Talpidae)</li></ol><p>The similarity with Sanskrit <i>उन्दुर</i> (undura, “rat, mouse”) is rather accidental. For the ending, it can be compared <i>σκίουρος</i> (skíouros, “squirrel”), which must be a loan, perhaps from Pre-Greek</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm or grub that damages the vine</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>ῑ̓́ψ</i> (ī́ps, “woodworm”) as a Pre-Greek element.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>castrated he-goat</li></ol><p>According to Furnée and Beekes, from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violet, namely sweet violet (<i>Viola odorata</i>)</li><li>white lily</li><li>any flower</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a precious stone of dark colour</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ϝίον</i> (wíon). Related to Latin <i>viola</i>, both from an unknown common Mediterranean substrate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>root of a hair, downy hair</li><li>eruption on the face, which accompanies the first growth of the beard</li></ol><p>May belong to a word for "hair", which appears in Middle Irish <i>find</i> (“hair”), Dutch <i>wenkbrauw</i> (“eyebrow”), Old Prussian <i>wanso</i> (“first beard”), and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ǫsъ</i> (“moustache”). The Greek word would have to represent a reduplicated formation <i>&#42;ϝί-ϝονθος</i> (&#42;wí-wonthos). All these words have been interpreted as verbal nouns from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wendʰ-</i> (“hair, wool, beard”), with the specific Greek formation as <i>&#42;wi-wondʰ-o-s</i>.</p><p>For a similar semantic shift, compare Georgian <i>თმა</i> (tma, “hair”) ~ Svan <i>შდო̈მ</i> (šdöm, “rash”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>down, the first growth of the whiskers and beard</li><li>corn sheaf</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) catkin; tendril</li><li>creature like the centipede, perhaps the woodlouse or the earthworm</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind, round”), the same root of <i>οὖλος</i> (oûlos, “wooly, fuzzy”) and <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to turn, wind”). However according to targum exegetes it could be borrowed from Phoenician <i>&#42;𐤉𐤋</i> (*yūl) from the Proto-Semitic lizard name <i>&#42;waral-</i>. This form and similar meanings can be reconstructed on the basis of the animal name’s Jewish Literary Aramaic measure <i>יָלָא</i> (yālā), alternating with <i>יַלָּא</i> (yallā), since /aː/ regularly comes out /uː/ in Phoenician-Punic (like <i>𐤓𐤀𐤔</i> (rʾš /⁠rūš⁠/, “head”) from <i>&#42;raʾš-</i> (“head”)). In Jewish Aramaic it also meant a kind of blood-sucking animal, and even closer to the original meaning of a worral would be the Greek senses of certain earth-graving insects. “The first growth of the whiskers and beard” would then be transferred from the image of the undersides of the throats of certain lizards; corn-sheaves afford a similar picture. From any of these meanings the sense of a catkin can be transferred.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common mare's tail (<i>Hippuris vulgaris</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made from iris</li></ol><p>From <i>ἶρῐς</i> (îrĭs, “iris”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>force; power</li><li>muscle; sinew; tendon</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;ϝῑ́ς</i> (&#42;wī́s), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wīs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéyh₁s</i>, from <i>&#42;weyh₁-</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>vīs</i>.</p><p>The <i>-ν-</i> (-n-) in the oblique stem can be ultimately derived from an original accusative <i>&#42;(ϝ)ῑ́ν</i> (&#42;(w)ī́n) that came to be analogically extended to<i>&#42;(ϝ)ῖνᾰ</i> (&#42;(w)înă). The starting point may have been reanalysis of <i>&#42;(ϝ)ῑ́ν</i> (&#42;(w)ī́n) as <i>(ϝ)ῖν’</i> ((w)în’) before a vowel. Compare <i>Ζῆνᾰ</i> (Zênă) and other collateral forms of <i>Ζεύς</i> (Zeús) built on the stem <i>Ζην-</i> (Zēn-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equal rights</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an equal share</li><li>equality</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) fairness; impartiality; equity</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ῐ̓́σθῐ</i> (ĭ́sthĭ)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ῐ̓́στε</i> (ĭ́ste)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of purgative bark</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fungus growing on oaks and walnut trees, used as a cautery</li></ol><p>Unknown. If the variant is reliable, it would point to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make like, feign, imitate</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to liken to, deem like, take for</li></ol><li>to think like</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to guess, imagine, suppose</li><li>to think, deem, fancy, judge that</li></ol><li>to conjecture, guess</li><li>to say</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests a pre-form <i>&#42;ϝίκ-σκ-ω</i> (&#42;wík-sk-ō), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>equals; peers (those of equal status)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>level ground</li><li>that which is equivalent (to something else); essentially the same thing (as something else)</li><li>(<i>metaphysics</i>) equivalence</li><li>(<i>ethics</i>) justice; fairness</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) equal rights</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a copy of an offical document</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;music) ison (accompanying drone in Byzantine music)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>likewise; even as (in the same way)</li><li>alike (having resemblance)</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) evenly; equally</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ἰχθύς ποιός κητώδης</i> (ikhthús poiós kētṓdēs, literally “kind of whale-like fish”).</li></ol><p>From Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;esoxs</i>. Compare Irish <i>eo</i> (“salmon”), Welsh <i>eog</i> (“salmon”) and Latin <i>esox</i> (“kind of fresh-water fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of size, strength, or number</i>) equal (to); equivalent (to); consistent (to); agreement (to) [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something or someone’</small>]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the same as [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>of appearance</i>) like [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘something or someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>with implied reference point</i>) like; similar</li><li>(<i>with pronoun</i>) like his; like hers etc. (like the one that person has)</li><li>repeated to denote relationship between equal persons or things</li></ol><li>(not comparable,&#32;mathematics) equal to [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘a number’</small>]; (<i>in the plural</i>) equal (to each other)</li><li>(not comparable,&#32;geometry,&#32;of lines) of equal length; (<i>of shapes</i>) congruent; of equal area</li><li>just; fair</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>impartial</li><li>(<i>comparable</i>) equal in rights</li></ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) based on the equality of rights</li><li>(<i>rare</i>) adequate</li><li>(<i>of land</i>) even; level; flat</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) an equal (person of equal status)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἶσος</i> (îsos), itself from earlier <i>&#42;ϝῖσϝος</i> (&#42;wîswos), of disputed origin:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>exclamation of malicious triumph over another's distress</li></ol><p>Probably of onomatopoeic origin. Compare <i>σίττα</i> (sítta, “a cry of drovers”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ῐ̓́στε</i> (ĭ́ste)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) staunching blood, styptic</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴσχω</i> (ískhō, “to restrain, stop”) +&lrm; <i>αἷμα</i> (haîma, “blood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to hold back; to restrain</li><li>(reflexive,&#32;middle voice&#32;in&#32;Epic,&#32;active voice&#32;in&#32;Attic) to stay; to stop</li><li>to hold fast; to keep; to maintain</li><li>(Attic,&#32;Koine) to have</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;si-sǵʰ-</i>, reduplicated zero-grade present of <i>&#42;seǵʰ-</i> (“to take hold”), from which also <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “have”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>equally, fairly, equitably</li><li>probably</li><li>perhaps, maybe</li><li>(<i>with numerals</i>) about, approximately</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Thracian name for a kind of mushroom</li></ol><p>Furnée connects the word with <i>ὕδνον</i> (húdnon, “truffle”), which has variants too. Furnée is mistaken to assume a prothetic "ὀ-", since "ὀ-" and "οὐ-" just indicate "ϝ-". So we have *wit- and *wid-n-, with a suffix beginning with "n-" and voicing before the nasal, which is typical of Pre-Greek according to Beekes.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cake made of sesame and honey</li></ol><p>Unknown, probably a loanword.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>felloe of a wheel</li><li>rim of a shield</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the shield itself</li></ol></ol><p>From a previous form <i>ϝῐ́τῠς</i> (wĭ́tŭs), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wítus</i>, that proves a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weh₁y-</i> (“to twist, to twine”). Cognate with Latin <i>vitus</i> (“felloe”), Ancient Greek <i>ῑ̓τέᾱ</i> (ītéā, “willow”), <i>ἶρῐς</i> (îrĭs, “rainbow”), <i>οἶσος</i> (oîsos, “withy”), Albanian <i>vithe</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἴτυς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Itys</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) vague epithet of humans, gods, animals, nature: strong, stalwart, or possibly also noble, beautiful, loyal, great</li></ol><p>Probably Pre-Greek. Beekes rejects various previously suggested connections to <i>ἴς</i> (ís, “force”), <i>φθάνω</i> (phthánō, “to come first”), Sanskrit <i>क्षयति</i> (kṣayati, “to overpower”) and <i>τιμή</i> (timḗ, “honour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) strong; fat; goodly</li></ol><p>From <i>ἶφι</i> (îphi, “by force”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyh₁-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spike lavender (<i>Lavandula latifolia</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée connects this word with <i>τίφυον</i> (típhuon, “squill”). Nevertheless, a Pre-Greek word is probable <i>a priori</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footstep, footprint</li></ol><p><i>ἴχνος</i> (íkhnos) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>track; footstep; footprint</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Beekes states a formation in <i>&#42;-nos-</i> like <i>ἔρνος</i> (érnos), <i>κτῆνος</i> (ktênos). The first part could be cognate with Ancient Greek <i>οἴχομαι</i> (oíkhomai, “to go (away), leave, disappear, die”) or Ancient Greek <i>οἰχνέω</i> (oikhnéō, “to go, come, walk, approach”), which are connected to Old Armenian <i>իջանեմ</i> (iǰanem, “to come down, descend”) and Tocharian B <i>yku</i> (“gone”); furthermore compare Old Irish <i>óegi</i> (“guest”) and Lithuanian <i>eigà</i> (“course”), for perhaps Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eygʰ-</i> (“to go”), a possible extension of <i>&#42;h₁ey-</i> with similar meaning. However, this connection is disputed by other sources.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>worm which eats wood and horn</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>ἴξ</i> (íx, “kind of worm”) as a Pre-Greek element. Rhymes with other Pre-Greek nouns, such as <i>θρῑ́ψ</i> (thrī́ps, “woodworm”) and <i>κνῑ́ψ</i> (knī́ps, kind of insect found under the bark of trees).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cork oak (<i>Quercus suber</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Compared to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ebʰ-</i>, with possible cognates Albanian <i>bërshen</i> (“yew, juniper”), Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;eburos</i> (“yew”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ibahs</i> (“ivy”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἴψος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ipsus, Phrygia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small kind of fish</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, undoubtedly a local word for small useless fish, thus from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian wryneck (<i>Jynx torquilla</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) spell, charm</li><li>passionate yearning</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̓ῠ̈́ζω</i> (ĭŭ̈́zō, “shout, yell”). Beekes reconstructs Pre-Greek *<i>wʲug-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(anaphoric,&#32;reflexive,&#32;rare) she (nominative of <i>οὗ</i> (hoû))</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;síh₂</i>. Cognates include Gothic <i>𐍃𐌹</i> (si) and Old Irish <i>sí</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>according to Hesychius, a Cypriot word for <i>εἷς</i> (heîs, “one”).</li></ol><p>According to Dunkel actually an abstract noun “oneness”, slightly misinterpreted by Hesychius, derived from <i>&#42;ἵγγος</i> (&#42;híngos, “one”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sém-ǵno-s</i> (“single”, literally “born alone”) with the nasal <i>n</i> removed by dissimilation, cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;ainakulaz</i> (“single”), Sanskrit <i>अकज</i> (ekajá-, “born alone, not a twin”), and Latin <i>singulī</i> (“one apiece”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>establishment, foundation</li><li>temple, shrine</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱδρύω</i> (hidrúō, “settle, establish”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>founding, foundation, especially of temples</li><li>settlement, abode</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱδρῡ́ω</i> (hidrū́ō, “to establish, found”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ῑ̔́ετε</i> (hī́ete)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ῑ̔́ετο</i> (hī́eto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subsidence, sinking</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱζάνω</i> (hizánō, “to make to sit”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cause to sit, seat, place</li><li>(transitive,&#32;1st aorist) to set up</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sit, sit down</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sit still, be quiet</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to sink</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to settle down</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hízdō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sísdeti</i>, reduplicated imperfective form of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“sit”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सीदति</i> (sī́dati), Latin <i>sīdō</i> and Umbrian <i>𐌔𐌉𐌔𐌈𐌖</i> (sisθu). Compare <i>ἕζομαι</i> (hézomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to release, let go</li><li>(<i>of sounds</i>) to utter, speak, say</li><li>to throw, shoot, hurl</li><li>(<i>of water</i>) to let flow, flow, spout forth</li><li>to send</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to speed oneself, hasten</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;with infinitive) to be eager, to desire (to do something)</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;with genitive) to be set upon, long for</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;yīyēmi</i>, reduplicated present of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(H)yeh₁-</i> (“to throw”). Compare Latin <i>iaciō</i>, Hittite (pe-iezzi, “sends away”). This has been connected with Hittite (iya-, “to do, make”), but that hypothesis is rejected by Kloekhorst (2008).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἵκετο</i> (híketo) (<i>used before the rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἵκετο</i> (híketo)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to come</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to come to, arrive at</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to reach</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to come upon (befall, affect)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seyk-</i> (“to reach (for)”); cognate with Lithuanian <i>si̇́ekti</i> (“to reach”), Tocharian B <i>siknaṃ</i> (“to set foot”). The synonyms <i>ἱκάνω</i> (hikánō) and <i>ἱκνέομαι</i> (hiknéomai) are from the same root, with a nasal suffix. Possibly cognate with <i>ἥκω</i> (hḗkō, “to have arrived, be present”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>propitious, gracious, merciful</li><li>kind, mild, gentle</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selh₂-</i> (“mercy, comfort”), with the first vowel borrowed from the reduplicated present stem <i>&#42;si-sl̥h₂-</i>, as in <i>ἱλάσκομαι</i> (hiláskomai) (see there for more), and then further analogically shortened in <i>ἱλαρός</i> (hilarós) and <i>ἱλάειρα</i> (hiláeira). Variation in the second probably analogical after <i>νᾱός</i> (nāós) : <i>νεώς</i> (neṓs), <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós) : <i>λεώς</i> (leṓs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>propitiation</li></ol><p>From <i>ῑ̔λᾰ́σκομαι</i> (hīlắskomai, “appease”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ῑ̔́λαος</i> (hī́laos, “auspicious, favorable”)</li></ol><p>Variant of <i>ῑ̔́λαος</i> (hī́laos, “auspicious, favorable”) formed after the pattern <i>νᾱός</i> (nāós) : <i>νεώς</i> (neṓs), <i>λᾱός</i> (lāós) : <i>λεώς</i> (leṓs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἱλήκω</i> (hilḗkō, “to be gracious”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yearning; longing</li><li>desire; love</li></ol><p>The old connection with Sanskrit <i>ईष्म</i> (īṣma, “spring; a god of love”), which belongs to <i>इ॒च्छति॑</i> (iccháti, “to desire; to wish”), may be semantically possible, but it leaves the secondary suffix <i>-ερο-</i> (-ero-) unexplained. Another proposal by Bally assumed a reconstruction <i>&#42;si-smer-o-</i>, by comparison of Avestan <i>𐬵𐬌𐬱𐬨𐬀𐬭𐬀𐬥𐬙</i> (hišmarant, “well conducted”) to Sanskrit <i>स्मर॑ति</i> (smárati, “to remember; to call to mind”). As remarked by Weiss, the problem with these proposals is that they lead to expect Aeolic Greek <i>&#42;ἵμμερος</i> (&#42;hímmeros), while it is consistently found as <i>ἵμερος</i> (hímeros) in Sappho and Alcaeus. Weiss convincingly derives this word from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seh₂y-</i> (“to tie; to bind”) instead: he reconstructs a heteroclitic <i>&#42;seh₂i-mr̥</i>, <i>&#42;sh₂i-men-</i>, with a suffix <i>&#42;-mer&#47;n-</i> also found in <i>τέκμᾰρ</i> (tékmăr) and <i>ἦμᾰρ</i> (êmăr); he also compares <i>αἵμων</i> (haímōn, “eager”). This means that originally this word meant "bond, spell".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἵνα</i> (hína)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>subordinating conjunction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>final, introducing a subordinate clause expressing a purpose: in order that, so that, so</li><li>introducing a clause of effort, indicating what someone is trying to accomplish: that</li><li>(<i>usually&#32;poetic</i>) where</li></ol></ol><p>The stem <i>ἵ-</i> (hí-) is probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyós</i> (“that, who, which”), while the ending is an instrumental ending found in Sanskrit <i>येन॑</i> (yéna, “by which; by that”), Old High German <i>hina</i> (“thither”), Old Irish <i>cen</i> (“on this side”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a horse, (<i>feminine</i>) a mare</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) cavalry, horsemen</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁éḱwos</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;íkkʷos</i> (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀂𐀦</i> (i-qo)), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁éḱwos</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁oh₁ḱu-</i> (“swift”). The expected inherited form is <i>&#42;&#42;έπος</i> (&#42;&#42;épos), and there is no explanation for why <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos) has ῐ in place of ε, word-initial rough breathing, and double <i>ππ</i> (pp), each of which is of unclear origin; possibly borrowed from another Indo-European language where such sound changes are regular. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>अश्व॑</i> (áśva), Latin <i>equus</i>, Lithuanian <i>ašva</i>, Gaulish <i>epos</i>, Old Armenian <i>էշ</i> (ēš, “donkey”), Icelandic <i>jór</i>, and Old Irish <i>ech</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) having a horse tail plume (<i>of a helmet etc.</i>)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse tail; satyr's tail</li><li>wood horsetail (<i>Equisetum sylvaticum</i>) or water horsetail (<i>Equisetum maximum</i>)</li><li>groin ache caused by constant riding</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππουρος</i> (hĭ́ppouros, “horse-tailed”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs, forms feminine nouns).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) horse-tailed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dolphinfish (<i>Coryphaena hippurus</i>)</li><li>a kind of insect</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>οὐρᾱ́</i> (ourā́, “tail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Koine form of <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai, “to fly”)</li></ol><p>Derived by metathesis from <i>πέταμαι</i> (pétamai), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;péth₂eti</i> (“to fly”) from the root <i>&#42;peth₂-</i> (literally “to spread one’s wings”). Cognates Old English <i>feþer</i> (English <i>feather</i>), Latin <i>petō</i>, Russian <i>перо́</i> (peró), Sanskrit <i>पतति</i> (pátati), Avestan <i>𐬞𐬀𐬙𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌</i> (pata<sup><small>i</small></sup>ti), Old Armenian <i>թիռ</i> (tʻiṙ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ἵστατε</i> (hístate)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ἵστατο</i> (hístato)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;active voice of present, imperfect, future, and 1st aorist tenses)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to make to stand, to stand, set</li><li>to stop, stay, check</li><li>to set up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to cause to rise, to raise, rouse, stir up</li><li>to set up, appoint</li><li>to establish, institute</li></ol><li>to place in the balance, weigh</li></ol><li>(intransitive,&#32;middle and passive voice, active voice of 2nd aorist, perfect, and pluperfect)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to stand</li><li>to stand still</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to stand firm</li></ol><li>to be set up or upright, to stand up, rise up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>generally</i>) to arise, begin</li><li>(<i>in marking time</i>) to be</li><li>to be appointed</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hístāmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;stísteh₂ti</i>, the reduplicated present of <i>&#42;steh₂-</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>standan</i> (English <i>stand</i>), Sanskrit <i>तिष्ठति</i> (tíṣṭhati) (root <i>स्था</i> (sthā)), Latin <i>sistō</i>, Old Armenian <i>ստանամ</i> (stanam), Persian ایستادن (istâdan).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>ὄρνις ποιός</i> (órnis poiós, literally “kind of bird”).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>judge (one who knows the law and what is right)</li><li>witness</li><li>wise man</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wístōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wéydtōr</i> (“knower, wise person”), from <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ibis</li></ol><p>From Egyptian <i>hbj</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beech marten (<i>Martes foina</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix <i>-υκ-</i> (-uk-), which is found also in <i>ᾰ̓́μπῠξ</i> (ắmpŭx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whirlpool; eddy</li><li>dizziness; vertigo</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wreaths of smoke</li></ol><p>Formation like <i>θῶμῐγξ</i> (thômĭnx) and <i>ὄστλῐγξ</i> (óstlĭnx). The word is either directly from <i>είλέω</i> (eíléō, “to turn, wind”) or via an unknown noun. On account of the suffix, one could separate the word from <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō) as Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piece of wood in a mousetrap, that falls and catches the rodent</li><li>press of a fuller or for medical purposes</li></ol><p>Unexplained. One could compare <i>ἰψών</i> (ipsṓn, “prison”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rainbow</li><li>halo</li><li>various species of the genus <i>Iris</i></li></ol><p>From earlier <i>ϝῖρῐς</i> (wîrĭs); further origin uncertain. The traditional etymology, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wih₁-r-</i> (“twist; thread; cord; wire”) (compare English <i>wire</i>, Swedish <i>vira</i> (“to twist”), Welsh <i>gŵyr</i> (“bent”)), from <i>&#42;weyh₁-</i> (“to turn, twist, weave, plait”), is suspicious on account of the irregular variation between ⟨ε⟩ and ⟨ι⟩, as well as the suffix <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs), <i>-ῐδος</i> (-ĭdos). Furthermore, even the derivation of <i>ϝῖρ-</i> (wîr-) seems phonetically improbable, given the modern reconstruction of the root as <i>&#42;weh₁-y-</i>. Thus, Furnée and Beekes argue for Pre-Greek origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ἶρις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Iris</li></ol><p>From <i>ἶρῐς</i> (îrĭs, “rainbow; halo”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῐ̓́σος</i> (ĭ́sos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>by force; mightily</li></ol><p>Old instrumental case of <i>ῑ̓́ς</i> (ī́s).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iazyges, an ancient Sarmatian tribe settled in modern Hungary</li></ol><p>Of Iranian origin, from Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Yazug</i>. More at Iazyges.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Jacob or James</li></ol><p>A modified form of <i>Ἰακώβ</i> (Iakṓb), from Hebrew <i>יַעֲקֹב</i> (ya‘ăqṓḇ, “Jacob”, literally “heel-grabber”), from <i>עָקֵב</i> (‘āqḗḇ, “heel of the foot”). While <i>Ἰακώβ</i> (Iakṓb) refers to Jacob and <i>Ἰάκωβος</i> (Iákōbos) to James in the Greek writings, both English names derive from <i>Ἰάκωβος</i> (Iákōbos), which is derived from <i>Ἰακώβ</i> (Iakṓb).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ialmenus, a son of Ares who led a contingent from Orchomenus in the Trojan War</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iarganum, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Iardanus, Elis, Greece</li><li>river Iardanus, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰαρέδ</i> (Iaréd)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woman of the Ionians; a woman native or inhabitant of Ionia</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἴων</i> (Íōn) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Jason</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>ἰάομαι</i> (iáomai, “to heal”), making it related to the name of the goddess <i>Ἰασώ</i> (Iasṓ), maybe from <i>ἴασις</i> (íasis, “cure”). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Japheth (son of Noah)</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>יֶפֶת</i> (Yep̄eṯ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰαώ</i> (Iaṓ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Ionian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Jairus</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>יָאִיר</i> (Yāʾîr). Doublet of <i>Ἰαΐρ</i> (Iaḯr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ireland (a large island in northwestern Europe)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Primitive Irish <i>&#42;īweriyū</i> and its genitive <i>&#42;īweriyonah</i> (whence <i>probably</i> <i>Éire</i>, see there), from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;φīweriyū</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jewish epithet of the God of Israel, used in magical papyri</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>יַהְוֶה צְבָאוֹת</i> (yave ṣəvā'ōṯ, “Yahweh Sabaoth”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰαβε Ζεβυθ</i> (Iabe Zebuth)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jacob</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יַעֲקֹב</i> (yaʿăqōḇ). While <i>Ἰακώβ</i> (Iakṓb) refers to Jacob and Ἰάκωβος (Iákōbos) to James in the Greek writings, both English names derive from <i>Ἰάκωβος</i> (Iákōbos), which is derived from <i>Ἰακώβ</i> (Iakṓb).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ialysos (a town in Rhodes, Dodecanese, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iamid (member of an Olympian extended family of seers)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἴαμος</i> (Íamos, “Iamus”) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, “son of”, patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the month of January</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Iānuārius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Janiculum</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iāniculum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Janus</li><li>(<i>philosophy&#32;and&#32;theology</i>) In the Platonist theology of Plethon, an aspect of Zeus, as attested in one of the twenty-seven hymns.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iaxartes, the ancient name of the Syr Darya river</li></ol><p>Of Indo-Iranian origin. Compare Sanskrit <i>क्षरति</i> (kṣarati, “to flow, stream, trickle”), which shares the same ultimate source as the first element of the Persian descendant سیردریا (sirdaryâ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iaxamatae, a tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Iapetus</li></ol><p>Possibly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iapygia; Salento, Italy</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰ̆ᾶπῠξ</i> (Ĭâpŭx) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or from Iasos (a city in Caria)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἴασσος</i> (Íassos), alternative form of <i>Ἴασος</i> (Íasos), + <i>-ις</i> (-is)</p></html>:/ja.(h)o/
<h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Yahweh; the Tetragrammaton YHWH</li><li>Dionysus, based on conflation between the epithet <i>Sabaoth</i> and the Phrygian god of wine Sabazios.</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>יהו</i> (“YHW”), related to Biblical Hebrew <i>יהוה</i> (YHWH). In written use by Hellenistic Jews in the Septuagint before the Christian era, when it became standard to substitute <i>Κύριος</i> (Kúrios, “Lord”).</p><p>It is unclear whether the Greek term initially represented Iaō or Iahō; the former persisted among Jews and Christians into late antiquity. In the 4th century CE Jerome, who translated the Latin Vulgate Bible from Hebrew and Greek, mentions both Iaoh and <i>Iaho</i> or <i>Jaho</i>, the latter matching the transliteration of Aramaic-Hebrew <i>יהו</i>. The shorter (not "shortened") form <i>Ἰά</i> (Iá) mirrors Hebrew <i>יָהּ</i> (yāh) and Classical Syriac <i>ܝܰܗ</i>/<i>ܝܲܗ</i> (yah).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iberia, Iberian Peninsula, Hispania (a peninsula and region of Europe south of the Pyrenees)</li><li>Iberia, Caucasian Iberia (the ancient Georgian kingdom of Kartli in Eastern Europe, located east of Colchis, corresponding roughly to the eastern parts of present-day Georgia)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἴβηρ</i> (Íbēr, “Iberian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Ignatius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Ignātius</i>, a collateral form of <i>Egnātius</i>, altered to resemble <i>ignis</i> (“fire”), establishing a folk etymology meaning akin to “Fiery One”. Of uncertain origin, perhaps originally borrowed from Etruscan.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Idalium, a city of Cyprus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Idanthyrsus, from Scythian, the name of two Scythian kings</li></ol><p>From Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Hiθāmθrauša</i> (literally “prospering the ally”). Final part possibly modified into <i>θύρσος</i> (thúrsos, wand of Dionysus), because the Scythian peoples were associated with Dionysian rites.</p><p>Compare with Ancient Greek <i>Ἀγάθυρσοι</i> (Agáthursoi), from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Haxāθrauša</i> (literally “prospering the friend/socius”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Idaean (of, from or pertaining to Mount Ida)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Idaeus (<i>son of Dares Phrygius and charioteer of Priam during the Trojan War</i>)</li></ol><p><i>Ῑ̓́δη</i> (Ī́dē) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Idomene (a city in Greece)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῑ̓́δη</i> (Ī́dē, “Ida”) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos, “force”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῑ̓δομένη</i> (Īdoménē) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Edom (or Idumea), a region south of the Dead Sea.</li></ol><p>Of Semitic origin; compare Biblical Hebrew <i>אֱדוֹם</i> (ʾĕḏôm).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Ezekiel (a book of the Old Testament of the Bible)# (<i>biblical</i>) Ezekiel (a prophet mentioned in the Bible)</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>יְחֶזְקֵאל</i> (Yəḥezqêl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Jeremiah, the Hebrew prophet.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jericho</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְרִיחוֹ</i> (Yərīḥṓ). There are various spellings in Ancient Greek, but the variant <i>Ἰεριχώ</i> (Ierikhṓ) is found in more reliable manuscripts and is considered to more likely be the authentic version.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ῐ̔ερουσᾱλήμ</i> (Hĭerousālḗm)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Jesse</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>יִשַׁי</i> (yishái).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jephthah</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יִפְתָּח</i> (yip̄tāḥ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰεφθάε</i> (Iephtháe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Christianity,&#32;New Testament) Jesus, Jeshua, Yeshua</li><li>(biblical,&#32;Judaism,&#32;Hebrew Bible (Septuagint)) Joshua, Jehoshua, Yehoshua</li></ol><p>Hellenized form of Semitic proper name roughly equivalent to <i>Ῐ̓ησοῦ</i> (Ĭēsoû) +&lrm; <i>-ς</i> (-s); Hellenic case suffixes added to an approximated Greek literal phonetic rendering of Aramaic <i>יֵשׁוּע</i> (yēšū́ʿ, “Jesus”) and Biblical Hebrew <i>יֵשׁוּעַ</i> (yēšū́aʿ, “Jesus” <i>or</i> “Joshua”), a syncopated relative of Biblical Hebrew <i>יְהוֹשֻׁעַ</i> (yĕhōšúaʿ, “Joshua”); ultimately can be traced to Paleo-Hebrew primitive root Biblical Hebrew <i>𐤉𐤔𐤏</i> (y-š-ʕ, “to deliver, save”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jesus Christ</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ithaca</li></ol><p>Uncertain origin; first element possibly from a Phoenician <i>𐤀𐤉</i> (ʾy /⁠ʾī⁠/, “island”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to Ithaca</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰθάκην</i> (Ithákēn) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Ithacan (inhabitant of Ithaca)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>an Ithacan (from or pertaining to Ithaca)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰθάκη</i> (Ithákē) +&lrm; <i>-ήσιος</i> (-ḗsios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Ithome</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰθώμη</i> (Ithṓmē) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ithome</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Icarius</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Icarian (person from the island of Icaria)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Icarian (coming from the island of Icaria)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῑ̓́κᾰρος</i> (Ī́kăros) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ikaria</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῑ̓́κᾰρος</i> (Ī́kăros) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Konya (a city in Turkey), the ancient Iconium</li></ol><p>From Luwian <i>Ikkuwaniya</i>, from Hittite <i>Kawana</i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Iliad (an Ancient Greek epic poem, ascribed to Homer, which depicts the end of the Trojan War and the fall of Troy)</li><li>the lands of Ilium or Troy; Troas; the Troad</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῑ̓́λῐον</i> (Ī́lĭon) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ῑ̓λῑσός</i> (Īlīsós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Ilissos</li></ol><p>The suffix <i>-ῑσο-</i> is found in other river names, like <i>Κηφισός</i> (Kēphisós) and <i>Πάμισος</i> (Pámisos), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>before Ilium, in front of Ilium [<i>with</i> <b>πρό</b> (pró)]</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῑ̓́λιον</i> (Ī́lion) +&lrm; <i>-θι</i> (-thi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Illyria (a vaguely-defined geographic region in Southeast Europe in the western part of the Balkan Peninsula, approximately coincident with modern Albania)# Illyria, Illyricum (a much larger former province of the Roman Empire, covering the whole of Dalmatia and Pannonia, existing from 27 BC to c. 70 AD and reconstituted in 293 AD by Diocletian)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰλλῠρῐός</i> (Illŭrĭós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā). Of obscure origin, but possibly from a conjectural Illyrian <i>&#42;Hullurio</i> (“(place or tribe) of the serpent”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;udlós</i>. This is supported by the attestation of Hílurios by Plautus in Menaechmi (200s BC).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Illyricum</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Illyrian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰλλυριός</i> (Illuriós) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Illyrian</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰλλῠρῐός</i> (Illŭrĭós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Imachara, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inaros</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Demotic Egyptian <i>jr.t-ḥr-r-r⸗w</i> (“Inaros”, literally “the Eye of Horus is against them”), ultimately from Egyptian <i>jrt-ḥr</i> (“Eye of Horus”) + <i>r</i> (“concerning, against”) + <i>.w</i> (third-person plural suffix pronoun). Compare Coptic <i>ⲛⲁϩⲣⲁⲩ</i> (nahrau) from the same source.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inachorium, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>India (a region of South Asia, traditionally delimited by the Himalayas and the Indus river; the Indian subcontinent)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰνδός</i> (Indós, “the Indus river”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from Old Persian <i>𐏃𐎡𐎯𐎢𐏁</i> (hi<sup>n</sup>dūš), from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;hínduš</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;síndʰuš</i> (“river”), of uncertain origin.</p><p>Compare Persian هند (hend), Sanskrit <i>सिन्धु</i> (síndhu, “a river, stream”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>India</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰνδῐκός</i> (Indĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Indian, Indic</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰνδία</i> (Indía) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>In the language of the Indians</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰνδός</i> (Indós, “an Indian”) +&lrm; <i>-ιστί</i> (-istí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>born in India</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰνδός</i> (Indós) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indian-killer, epithet of Dionysus</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>Ἰνδός</i> (Indós) +&lrm; <i>ὄλλυμι</i> (óllumi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Indus (a river in Pakistan and India)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of India or the Indian subcontinent; an Indian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐏃𐎡𐎯𐎢𐏁</i> (hi<sup>n</sup>dūš), from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;hínduš</i>, from Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;síndʰuš</i> (“river”), of uncertain origin.</p><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>सिन्धु</i> (síndhu, “a river, stream”) in the <i>Rigveda</i>, which was borrowed into Greek as <i>Σίνθος</i> (Sínthos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Intaphernes, from Old Persian, bow-carrier of Darius the Great</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎻𐎡𐎭𐎳𐎼𐎴𐎠</i> (vi-i-d-f-r-n-a /⁠Viⁿdafarnāʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the warden or guard of the Ἰ̄νωπός (Īnōpós)</li></ol><p><i>Ἰ̄νωπός</i> (Īnōpós) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”). Compare <i>κρηνοφῠ́λᾰξ</i> (krēnophŭ́lăx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fountain and river in the island of Delos, where Leto brought forth Apollo and Artemis (It is said to have risen and fallen at the same time with the Nile, and hence was supposed to be connected with it by a subterranean channel.)</li><li>a building at the source of the river</li></ol><p>Perhaps a combination of the same root as <i>ἰνόω</i> (inóō, “make strong; flow, pour”) and Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ep-</i> (“water”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ino</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ixion</li></ol><p>Meaning "strong native," related to <i>ἰσχύς</i> (iskhús, “strength, force”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jovian, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ioviānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jocasta</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jordan (a river in West Asia in the Middle East, that empties into the Dead Sea, flowing through Israel, the Golan Heights, the West Bank and Jordan)</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>יַרְדֵּן</i> (yardēn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jugurtha</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iugurtha</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰούδᾱς</i> (Ioúdās, “Judah”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jewess (substantive form of adjective)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Judea (adjectival form with γῆ (gê) or χώρα (khṓra) implied)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יְהוּדָה</i> (yəhūḏāh) + the feminine form of <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Judahite, Judaic</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰουδά</i> (Ioudá, “Judah”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>In a Judean or Jewish manner; Judaically; Jewishly.</li></ol><p>Adverb from <i>Ἰουδαϊκός</i> (Ioudaïkós, “of Judah”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Judaism</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰουδαῖος</i> (Ioudaîos) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jew or Judean</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰουδά</i> (Ioudá) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios), from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְהוּדָה</i> (Yəhūḏā(h), “Judah”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ireland</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Primitive Irish <i>&#42;Īweriū</i> and its genitive <i>&#42;Īweriyonah</i> (whence <i>probably</i> <i>Éire</i>, see there), from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;Φīweriyū</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Julia, a Latin feminine name</li><li>a female member of the Roman Julia gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iūlia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iulis, Cea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Julian</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Iūliānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female member of the Roman Junius gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iūnia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Justinian</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Iūstīniānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Justin</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iūstīnus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iophon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Judah, one of the Hebrew patriarchs</li><li>Judah, a tribe of Israel (and its proprietary land)</li><li>Judas</li><li>Jude</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְהוּדָה</i> (Yəhūḏā́) and -ς (-s, declension suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Julius, a Latin masculine name</li><li>a member of the Roman Julia gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iūlius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a member of the Roman Iunius gens</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Iūnius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Isidore</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἶσις</i> (Îsis, “Isis”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isaac</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יִצְחָק</i> (yiṣḥāq).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isaac, a name borne by an Emperor</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰσαᾱ́κ</i> (Isaā́k, “Isaac”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Isagoras</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσο-</i> (iso-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓γορᾱ́</i> (ăgorā́) +&lrm; <i>-ᾱς</i> (-ās).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p><i>Ἴσαρχος</i> (Ísarkhos) +&lrm; <i>-ίδᾱς</i> (-ídās, masculine patronymic suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isaeus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσαῖος</i> (isaîos, “equal”), from <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos, “equal”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iscariot, a byname of the biblical Judas, one of the apostles of Jesus of Nazareth in the gospels of the Christian Bible.</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin, but Hebrew <i>אִישׁ קְרִיֹּות</i> (ʾīš qəriyyōṯ, “man of Kerioth”) is the most traditional derivation. Nonetheless, this interpretation of the name is not fully accepted by all scholars. One of the most popular alternative explanations holds that <i>Iscariot</i> (<i>ܣܟܪܝܘܛܐ</i>, 'Skaryota' in Syriac Aramaic, per the Peshitta text) may be a corruption of the Latin word Latin <i>sicarius</i>, meaning "dagger man", which referred to a member of the Sicarii (Aramaic <i>סיקריים</i>, from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;tsikā</i>), a group of Jewish rebels who were known for committing acts of terrorism in the 40s and 50s CE by assassinating people in crowds using long knives hidden under their cloaks.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ismene</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἰσμηνός</i> (Ismēnós) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ishmael</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יִשְׁמָעֵאל</i> (Yišmāʻḗl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ismenias</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Ismenus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>In particular, the Ancient Greek rhetorician Isocrates</li></ol><p>From <i>ἰσοκρᾰτής</i> (isokrătḗs, “of equal power”), from <i>ἰσο-</i> (iso-) +&lrm; <i>κράτος</i> (krátos) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Israel (a name given to the Jewish patriarch Jacob)</li><li>Israel, the descendants of Jacob which formed a nation</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) the Christian church, the “New Israel”</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>יִשְׂרָאֵל</i> (Yiśrāʾēl).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Israelite</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῐ̓σρᾱήλ</i> (Ĭsrāḗl, “Israel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs), from Hebrew <i>יִשְׂרָאֵל</i> (Yiśrāʼēl, “Israel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Issachar</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>יִשָּׂשכָר</i> (Yiśśāḵā́r).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Issedones, a Scythian tribe mentioned by Herodotus and Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Istria</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἴστρος</i> (Ístros) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Istone</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isondae, a tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Tabor, Galilee, Israel</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Hebrew <i>תָּבוֹר</i> (Tāḇôr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Classical Ancient Greek</i>) southernmost Italy, roughly corresponding to modern Calabria</li><li>Italy (an ancient region of Southern Europe, corresponding to modern Italy)</li></ol><p>Uncertain.</p><p>An early borrowing from Oscan <i>𐌅𐌝𐌕𐌄𐌋𐌉𐌞</i> (víteliú, “land of young cattle”) or a related Italic language with /w/ in the anlaut disappearing in Greek before the classical period, and later reanalyzed as <i>Ῑ̓τᾰλός</i> (Ītălós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p><p>In such case, derived from Proto-Italic <i>&#42;wetelos</i> (“yearling, calf”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wet-</i> (“year”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an Italiote</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῑ̓τᾰλῐ́ᾱ</i> (Ītălĭ́ā, “Italy”) +&lrm; <i>-ώτης</i> (-ṓtēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italus</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Italy; an Italic person; an Italian</li></ol><p>Thucydides used the name to describe the Arcadian king of the Oenotrians. The name is said to be linked to Latin <i>vitulus</i> and Etruscan <i>𐌉𐌃𐌅𐌋𐌅𐌔</i> (idvlvs), both terms referring to calves, but this could be folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Utica (an ancient city and Phoenician colony on the African coast near Carthage in modern Bizerte Governorate, Tunisia)</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤏𐤕𐤒</i> (ʿtq, “old”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Iphthime, wife of Eumelus and daughter of Icarius</li></ol><p>Feminine form of <i>ἴφθῑμος</i> (íphthīmos, “strong, stout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>strong-born, mighty (an epithet of Artemis)</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Iphigenia (daughter of Agamemnon and Clytaemnestra)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἶφι</i> (îphi, “by force or might”, Epic adverb) +&lrm; <i>-γένειᾰ</i> (-géneiă, “birth”). See also <i>Ῑ̓φῐγόνη</i> (Īphĭgónē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare</i>) Lyric form of <i>Ῑ̓φῐγένειᾰ</i> (Īphĭgéneiă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ῑ̓́φῐκλος</i> (Ī́phĭklos)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἶφῐ</i> (îphĭ, “by force”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Iphicrates</li></ol><p>From <i>ἶφῐ</i> (îphĭ, “by force”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “might”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Joanna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יוֹחָנָה</i> (yōḥānā), the feminine form of <i>יוֹחָנָן</i> (yōḥānān), whence also <i>Ῐ̓ωᾰ́ννης</i> (Ĭōắnnēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English John</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>יוֹחָנָן</i> (yōḥānān), the masculine form of <i>יוֹחָנָה</i> (yōḥānā), whence also <i>Ῐ̓ωᾰ́ννᾱ</i> (Ĭōắnnā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>Ἰωακείμ</i> (Iōakeím)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Biblical Hebrew, equivalent to English Joachim</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>יְהוֹיָקִים</i> (Yəhôyāqîm, literally “raised by the Lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iolcos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Hebrew, commonly translated into English as <i>Jonathan</i></li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יוֹנָתָן</i> (yōnāṯān).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰωνᾱ́θᾱν</i> (Iōnā́thān)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionia</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἴων</i> (Íōn, “Ionian”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p><p>Compare Egyptian <i>ywnj-ꜥꜣ</i> (“Great Ionia”) <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f6d31a88.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="i" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="w" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6c9cd1c6.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="n" alt="glyph" height="12"/><br /><img src="./1afca51f.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="y" alt="glyph" height="35"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./653756ea.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="aA" alt="glyph" height="17"/><br /><img src="./9b32fa22.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="a" alt="glyph" height="30"/></td>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./7830a4c3.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="Y1" alt="glyph" height="25"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionidae, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Ἰωνία</i> (Iōnía); Ionia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionopolis; İnebolu, Turkey</li></ol><p><i>Ἴων</i> (Íōn, “Ion”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jonah, a given name, famously held by the Old Testament prophet to Nineveh, swallowed by a large fish. Also, the father of Jesus' disciples Peter and Andrew.</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>יוֹנָה</i> (Yōnā́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Joseph (eleventh and favorite son of Jacob, by his wife Rachel)# (<i>Christianity</i>) Joseph (the husband of Mary, mother of Jesus)# (<i>Christianity</i>) Joseph (Joseph of Arimathea; man who donated his own prepared tomb for the burial of Jesus)</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>יוֹסֵף</i> (Yōsēp̄). Doublet of <i>Ἰώσηπος</i> (Iṓsēpos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Josiah, king of Judah.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יֹאשִׁיָּה</i> (yōšiyyā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iotape, Cilicia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Juba</li></ol><p>From Phoenician <i>𐤉𐤁</i> (yb /⁠Yuba⁠/, “Juba”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iolaus</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>Ϝῐόλᾱος</i> (Wĭólāos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iole</li></ol><p>From <i>Ϝιόλᾱ</i> (Wiólā). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Ionian Gulf; the Ionian Sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jaffa (a port in western Israel)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Job (Old Testament character and book in the Bible)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>אִיּוֹב</i> (ʔiyyōḇ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Josephus (Romano-Jewish scholar, historian and hagiographer)</li></ol><p>From Biblical Hebrew <i>יוֹסֵף</i> (Yōsēp̄). Doublet of <i>Ἰωσήφ</i> (Iōsḗph).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Iapygia or Salento; an Iapygian or Salentine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jurcae, a Scythian tribe mentioned by Herodotus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hiempsal</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hiero</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερόν</i> (hierón, “holy place; temple”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn), the neuter substantive of <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hidrieus, from Old Persian, a satrap of Caria</li></ol><p>From Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hierapolis, a city in Anatolia.</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>πόλις</i> (pólis, “city”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Hierapolis; a Hierapolitan</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῑ̔ερᾱ́πολῐς</i> (Hīerā́polĭs, “Hierapolis”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine demonymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jeremiah, the Hebrew prophet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>יִרְמְיָה</i> (yirməyā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰερῑχώ</i> (Ierīkhṓ)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>יְרִיחוֹ</i> (Yərīḥṓ), influenced by the Greek word <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós, “holy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hierocles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p><p>Compare typologically Russian <i>Святосла́в</i> (Svjatosláv) (<i>свято́й</i> (svjatój) + <i>сла́ва</i> (sláva) (cognate with <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Jerusalemite</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῐ̔εροσόλυμᾰ</i> (Hĭerosólumă, “Jerusalem”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs), from Hebrew <i>יְרוּשָׁלַיִם</i> (Yərūšālayim, “Jerusalem”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) Jerusalem (a city in the Holy Land between the Mediterranean Sea and Dead Sea, holy in Judaism, Christianity and Islam; the claimed capital city of both Israel and Palestine)</li></ol><p>Partial phono-semantic matching of Biblical Hebrew <i>יְרוּשָׁלַיִם</i> (Yərūšālayim) with <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós, “sacred; holy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>biblical</i>) Jerusalem (a city in the Holy Land between the Mediterranean Sea and Dead Sea, holy in Judaism, Christianity and Islam; the claimed capital city of both Israel and Palestine)</li></ol><p>Folk-etymological adaptation of <i>יְרוּשָׁלַיִם</i> (Yərūšālayim), influenced by <i>ῐ̔ερός</i> (hĭerós, “sacred; holy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hierophon</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy”) +&lrm; <i>φωνή</i> (phōnḗ, “voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hieronymus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱερός</i> (hierós, “holy, sacred”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνυμα</i> (ónuma, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hicetaon, a son of Laomedon and brother of Priam</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱκέτης</i> (hikétēs, “suppliant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Himera; Termini Imerese, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hippias, a common name</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, “male name suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Hippodamia</li></ol><p>From <i>ἱππόδᾰμος</i> (hippódămos, “horse tamer”) +&lrm; <i>-ειᾰ</i> (-eiă). See <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos) and <i>δαμάζω</i> (damázō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hippocles</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hippocrates</li><li>in particular, the physician Hippocrates of Kos</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾰ́τος</i> (krắtos, “power”) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs). Compare <i>Κράτιππος</i> (Krátippos), which uses the same elements in the reverse order.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hippolyte or Hippolyta</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῐ̔ππόλῠτος</i> (Hĭppólŭtos) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hippomedon</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>μέδων</i> (médōn, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hippotion, father of Morys, slain by <i>Meriones</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hippothous, a descendant of <i>Pelasgus</i> slain by Ajax</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “quick”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hippolochus</li></ol><p><i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>λόχος</i> (lókhos, “ambush”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Any of various figures from Greek myth and legend, especially the son of Theseus.</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>λῠτός</i> (lŭtós, “releaser, unleasher”). The latter element, found in names, derives from the verb λύω (lúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hipponium; Vibo Valentia, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hipponax</li></ol><p>From <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>ἄναξ</i> (ánax, “king”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hire (a city in Messenia, Greece)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hispania (a peninsula in Southern Europe; modern Iberian Peninsula)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Hispānia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Spanish</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Roman Hispania</li><li>a Spaniard</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Histiaea; Istiaia, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Histiaeus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iacchus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Iamus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iasos (a city in Caria, modern Turkey)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἴασος</i> (Íasos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iberian, an inhabitant of the Iberian Peninsula</li><li>Iberian, an inhabitant of Caucasian Iberia</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ebro (a river in Spain that flows into the Mediterranean)</li></ol><p>For sense one, Celtic origin, likely related to the name of the <i>Ebro</i> river. For sense two, related to Old Armenian <i>վիրք</i> (virkʻ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἴβηρ</i> (Íbēr, “inhabitant of Caucasian Iberia”)</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἴβηρ</i> (Íbēr, “Ebro River”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iberian, an inhabitant of the Iberian Peninsula</li><li>Iberian, an inhabitant of Caucasian Iberia</li></ol><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ebro (a river in Spain that flows into the Mediterranean)</li></ol><p>For sense one, Celtic origin, likely related to the name of the <i>Ebro</i> river. For sense two, related to Old Armenian <i>վիրք</i> (virkʻ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ibycus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Idacus, Thrace, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Idas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from Mount Ida</li></ol><p><i>Ῑ̓́δη</i> (Ī́dē) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Idmon, one of the Argonauts</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴδμων</i> (ídmōn, “having knowledge of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Idyrus, Lycia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Ithaca; an Ithacan</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῐ̓θᾰ́κη</i> (Ĭthắkē, “Ithaca”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Icarus</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;Ϝίκαρος</i> (&#42;Wíkaros), from Tyrsenian. Cognate with Etruscan <i>𐌅𐌉𐌊𐌀𐌓𐌄</i> (vikare).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Icus; Alonnisos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ilium, Troy</li></ol><p>Of Anatolian origin. Metrical evidence from the Iliad and the Odyssey suggests that the name <i>Ἴλιον</i> (Ílion) formerly began with a digamma: <i>&#42;Ϝίλιον</i> (&#42;Wílion). Compare Hittite <i>𒃾𒇻𒊭</i> (Wi-lu-ša, “Wilusa”), usually identified with Troy, and Ancient Greek <i>Ἴλουζα</i> (Ílouza, “Beycesultan”), which may be directly borrowed from it.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Ῑ̓́λῐον</i> (Ī́lĭon, “Ilium, Troy”)</li></ol><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ilian, Trojan</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Imbrian, person from Imbros</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Imbrius, the son of Mentor, slain by Teucer</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἴμβρος</i> (Ímbros) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Imbros; İmroz; Gökçeada, Çanakkale, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inachus</li></ol><p>Connected with the eastern origin of <i>ἄναξ</i> (ánax, “king, ruler”); compare Anax and Anak (Hebrew <i>ענק</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>The name of the Sicilian city that became the city of <i>Aetna</i>.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἴνησσα</i> (Ínēssa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Inycum; Caltabellotta, Sicily</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ios, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ipnus, Locris, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Isae, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἰσαάκ</i> (Isaák)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>From <i>ἴσος</i> (ísos) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχή</i> (arkhḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Isburus, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An Iseum: a temple of the Egyptian goddess Isis.</li></ol><p>From <i>Ἶσις</i> (Îsis, “Isis”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, forming nouns of associated places).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ismarus, Thrace, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Istri, a people settled in Istria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>feminine</i>) Istros, Histria (an ancient city and Milesian colony in modern Constanța County, Romania; modern Istria)</li><li>(<i>masculine</i>) Danube (a river in Europe; flowing 2,850 kilometers from the confluence of the Breg and Brigach at Donaueschingen, Germany, into the Black Sea in Romania)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Itanos, a city in eastern Crete</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Itylus, son of Aedon, slain by her through mistake</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iton, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Iphiclus</li></ol><p>From <i>ἶφῐ</i> (îphĭ, “by force”) +&lrm; <i>κλέος</i> (kléos, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iphitus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ϝῑ́φιτος</i> (Wī́phitos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ichnae, Bottiaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ion, the mythological ancestor of the Ionian people</li><li>an Ancient Greek male name</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Ionians; a native or inhabitant of Ionia</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;Ἰᾱ́ϝων</i> (&#42;Iā́wōn) according to Beekes.</p><p>Pokorny has speculated about a connection to a Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;wey-</i>, expressing a shout of joy or distress, as in Ancient Greek <i>ἰά</i> (iá, “clamour, shout; sound, roar”). <i>Ἰᾱ́ϝων</i> (Iā́wōn) could mean “devotee of Apollo”, based on the cry <i>ἰή παιών</i> (iḗ paiṓn) uttered in his worship; the god was also called <i>ἰήϊος</i> (iḗïos) himself. Douglas Harper speculates that it may share a Proto-Indo-European origin with Sanskrit <i>योनी</i> (yonī, “womb, vagina”), a supposed reference to goddess-worshipping.</p><p>Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀂𐀊𐀺𐀚</i> (i-ja-wo-ne, “Ionians”), Egyptian <i>ywnj-ꜥꜣ</i> (“Great Ionia”), both attested in the first half of 14th century BC.</p><p>More at Ionians.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hipparchus</li><li>in particular, the Greek astronomer Hipparchus of Nicaea</li></ol><p>From <i>ῐ̔́ππος</i> (hĭ́ppos, “horse”) +&lrm; <i>ἀρχός</i> (arkhós, “leader”). Compare <i>Ἅρχιππος</i> (Hárkhippos) (whence Russian <i>Архи́пп</i> (Arxípp), the same roots in the reverse order).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hippasus, the name of various mythological characters</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Ilus, the name of several characters in Greek mythology.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Greek mythology) In the Odyssey, Irus, a nickname of Arnaeus the beggar, given to him by the suitors of Penelope.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Isis</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Egyptian <i>ꜣst</i>, <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./207f5cce.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="st" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./76993ec3.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="t" alt="glyph" height="27"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./8fad9a27.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="B1" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Iphis</li></ol><p>From <i>ἶφι</i> (îphi, “by force or might”, Epic adverb) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is, feminine noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, “the”)</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὁ</i> (ho, demonstrative pronoun)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>οἴζῠος</i> (oízŭos)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀῐ̈́ζῡ́ς</i> (oĭ̈́zū́s) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>οἴομαι</i> (oíomai)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>exclamation of pain</i>) ah, woe, alas</li></ol><p>Of expressive origin. Compare Latin <i>vāh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ὀῐ̈́ζῠος</i> (oĭ̈́zŭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tax of an obol</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀβελός</i> (obelós, “spit, skewer; obol”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small spit, skewer</li><li>anything shaped like a spit</li><li>obelisk</li><li>drainage conduit</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀβελός</i> (obelós, “spit, skewer”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spit used as a lampholder by soldiers</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀβελίσκος</i> (obelískos, “spit, obelisk”) +&lrm; <i>λυχνίον</i> (lukhníon, “lampholder”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a spit, rod</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ὀβελὸς λίθινος</i>) a pointed square pillar, obelisk</li></ol><li>a horizontal line</li></ol><p>Possibly an alteration of <i>βέλος</i> (bélos, “missile”), but the initial vocal cannot be accounted for. According to Beekes and Furnée, the word is clearly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>weigher of obols, petty usurer</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀβολός</i> (obolós, “obolus”) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to weigh”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obol, obolus, used at Athens as both a weight and a coin, equaling one sixth of a drachma</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a Corcyrean coin</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὀβελός</i> (obelós, “spit, rod”). Plutarch tells us in Lysander 17 that, in early times, nails (<i>ὀβελοί</i> (obeloí)) were used as money, six of which made a handful (<i>δραχμή</i> (drakhmḗ)), and that the name was changed to <i>ὀβολός</i> (obolós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong of spirit</li><li>strong-hearted, doughty</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄβρῐμος</i> (óbrĭmos, “strong, mighty”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “temper, disposition”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pure, assayed (especially when referring to gold)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄβρυζα</i> (óbruza, “assaying of gold”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The number eight</li><li>A group of eight</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄγδοος</i> (ógdoos) (from <i>ὀκτώ</i> (oktṓ)) + <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighty</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;oktōḱomt</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;oḱto(w)-dḱomt</i> (“eight-ten”), influenced by <i>ὀγδόη</i> (ogdóē) in analogy with <i>ἑβδομήκοντα</i> (hebdomḗkonta). Cognate with Latin <i>octōgintā</i>, Old Armenian <i>ութսուն</i> (utʻsun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eightieth</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀγδοήκοντα</i> (ogdoḗkonta, “eighty”) +&lrm; <i>-στός</i> (-stós), a variant of <i>-τός</i> (-tós, ordinal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>Ionic and Doric form of <i>ὀγδοήκοντᾰ</i> (ogdoḗkontă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bulky, swollen</li><li>stately, pompous, bombast</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄγκος</i> (ónkos) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>with the teeth</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>δάκνω</i> (dáknō, “to bite”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;denḱ-</i>. However, according to Beekes, in view of the semantic connection with other words such as <i>ὀδακτάζω</i> (odaktázō, “to bite, gnaw”), <i>ἀδακτῶ</i> (adaktô, “to itch”), <i>ἀδαχεῖ</i> (adakheî, “it itches”) which display a variation in initial vowel and stem-final consonant, and because of the limited semantic field, it is more likely to be Pre-Greek in origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smell, scent, odour</li><li>stench, stink</li><li>the sense of smell</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄζω</i> (ózō, “I smell”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>forceps for drawing teeth</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀδούς</i> (odoús, “tooth”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγρᾱ</i> (ágrā, “catching”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tooth; tusk</li><li>anything pointed:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>spike</li><li>prong</li></ol><li>name of the second vertebra</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;odónts</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃dónts</i> (“tooth”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>दत्</i> (dát), Latin <i>dēns</i> and Old English <i>tōþ</i> (English <i>tooth</i>).</p><p>Its use as the name for the second vertebra is as a consequence of its shape.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painkilling</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀδύνη</i> (odúnē, “pain”) +&lrm; <i>φατός</i> (phatós, “slain, dead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painful</li><li>distressing</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀδύνη</i> (odúnē) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pain of body</li><li>pain of mind, grief, distress</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hed-ún-eh₂</i> (“pain”), traditionally derived further from <i>&#42;h₁ed-</i> (“to eat”), and compared with Albanian <i>dhunë</i> (“violence, use of force; damage, injury”), Old Armenian <i>երկն</i> (erkn, “birth pangs”), and Old Irish <i>idu</i> (“pains, birth pangs”), as well as <i>ὀδύρομαι</i> (odúromai, “to wail”) and Lithuanian <i>ėdžiótis</i> (“to trouble oneself”).</p><p>However, Beekes instead identifies the initial laryngeal as <i>&#42;h₃</i>, and the further root as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ed-</i> (“to bite, sting”), and adduces Lithuanian <i>úodas</i> (“gnat”) as cognate, as well as perhaps <i>ὄζω</i> (ózō, “to have a smell”), <i>ὠδίς</i> (ōdís, “throes of childbirth, anguish”), noting that the multiple senses of <i>&#42;h₃ed-</i> ("stink", "hate", "bite") may all stem from an original meaning of "sting".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wail, weep, lament (for)</li></ol><p>Perhaps from the same root as <i>ὀδύνη</i> (odúnē, “pain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ed-</i> (“hate”). Cognates include Latin <i>ōdī</i> (“to hate”) and Old Armenian <i>ատեմ</i> (atem, “to hate”). Used to explain the name <i>Ὀδυσσεύς</i> (Odusseús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ὀδούς</i> (odoús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>idiomatic</i>) to bite the dust (die)</li></ol><p>Literally, “to grasp the ground with the teeth”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fine linen cloth</li><li>sailcloth, sail</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) (<i>anatomy</i>) membranes that enclose the pupil of the eye</li></ol><p>A culture word of foreign (substrate) origin. According to Lewy, it is from Hebrew <i>אֵטוּן</i> (ʾēṭūn, “fine linen”), itself from Egyptian <i>jdmj</i> (“fine red linen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of fine linen</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀθόνη</i> (othónē, “fine linen”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greater celandine (<i>Chelidonium majus</i>)</li><li>its juice, but also the juice of other plants</li><li>kind of Egyptian stone</li></ol><p>The word recalls <i>ὀθόνη</i> (othónē, “fine linen”), and hardly accidentally. According to Dioscorides it originates in Egypt, while according to Pliny it is Syrian. Either way, it is probably a Semitic word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ὀῐ̈ζῡρός</i> (oĭ̈zūrós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ὀϊζύος</i> (oïzúos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ὀῐ̈ζῡ́ς</i> (oĭ̈zū́s)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bent hams</li></ol><p>Unknown. Note the glosses <i>κλωκυδά</i> (klōkudá, “sitting down on both feet”) and <i>ὀκκῦλαι</i> (okkûlai, “to prostrate, sit down on one's heels”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shrink from, fear (<i>doing something</i>), hesitate, scruple (<i>to do something</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ὄκνος</i> (óknos, “hesitation”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shrinking, timid, especially from fear</li><li>hesitating, reluctant</li><li>idle, sluggish</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) causing fear, vexatious, troublesome</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀκνέω</i> (oknéō, “to shrink, hesitate”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rugged; peaked</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄκρῐς</i> (ókrĭs, “point; roughness”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having many points or rough places, rugged, jagged</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄκρις</i> (ókris) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with eight spokes (of a wheel)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀκτώ</i> (oktṓ, “eight”) +&lrm; <i>κνήμη</i> (knḗmē, “shin; spoke”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eight-footed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the common octopus, <i>Octopus vulgaris</i></li><li>(<i>Scythian</i>) a person who possessed two oxen and a cart</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀκτᾰ-</i> (oktă-, “eight”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>eight</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀκτώ</i> (oktṓ, “eight”), modeled after <i>ἑπτᾰ́</i> (heptắ, “seven”), <i>τετρᾰ-</i> (tetră-, “four”). Compare <i>ἑξᾰ-</i> (hexă-), <i>πεντᾰ-</i> (pentă-, “five”), etc.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eight hundred</li></ol><p><i>ὀκτα-</i> (okta-) +&lrm; <i>-ακόσιοι</i> (-akósioi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eighteen</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Numeral</b></p><ol><li>eight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;oktṓ</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;oḱtṓw</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>अष्ट</i> (aṣṭa), Latin <i>octō</i>, and Old English <i>eahta</i> (English <i>eight</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eight-footed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>octopus</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>ὀκτώ</i> (oktṓ, “eight”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to kill, destroy, or ruin</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the term derives from the root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃elh₁-</i> with a k-enlargement. For comparison, see terms such as <i>ἐρύκω</i> (erúkō). Etymologically, the term is related to <i>ὄλλυμι</i> (óllumi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>few; little (small amount)</li><li>little; small (small size)</li><li>slight (small degree)</li></ol><p>Probably from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ligos</i> (“indigent, small, needy, ill”). Compare Old Armenian <i>աղքատ</i> (ałkʻat, “poor, indigent”), Old Irish <i>líach</i> (“wretched”), Old Prussian <i>licuts</i> (“small”). See also <i>λοιγός</i> (loigós, “destruction, ruin”) and Albanian <i>lig</i> (“evil, bad”), whose proto-forms are almost if not identical, but which Beekes considers etymologically separate.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) dismissive, contemptuous</li><li>negligent, careless</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀλίγος</i> (olígos, “few”) +&lrm; <i>ὤρα</i> (ṓra, “care”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjective forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bringing destruction</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄλεθρος</i> (ólethros, “destruction”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oligarch</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ὀλίγος</i> (olígos, “few”) +&lrm; <i>-άρχης</i> (-árkhēs, “ruler”, “leader”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>scarcity of people</li></ol><p><i>ὀλῐγο-</i> (olĭgo-) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́νθρωπος</i> (ắnthrōpos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be part of an oligarchy</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ὀλιγάρχης</i> (oligárkhēs, “oligarch”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>politics</i>) rule of few, oligarchy</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ὀλιγάρχης</i> (oligárkhēs, “oligarch”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía, noun-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to an oligarchy; oligarchic</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ὀλιγάρχης</i> (oligárkhēs, “oligarch”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “-ic”, adjective-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>possession of little heat</li></ol><p><i>ὀλῐγόθερμος</i> (olĭgóthermos, “having little heat”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā, suffix forming abstract nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of few syllables</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀλῐγο-</i> (olĭgo-, “little, few”) +&lrm; <i>σῠλλᾰβή</i> (sŭllăbḗ, “syllable”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little nourishment</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀλῐγότροφος</i> (olĭgótrophos, “giving little nourishment”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be faint</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to become discouraged</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be worried, distressed</li></ol><p><i>ὀλίγος</i> (olígos, “little”) +&lrm; <i>ψῡχή</i> (psūkhḗ, “soul”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having little heat</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of cold-blooded animals</li><li>of the spleen</li></ol></ol><p><i>ὀλῐγο-</i> (olĭgo-, “little”, “slight”) +&lrm; <i>-θερμος</i> (-thermos, “warm”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of little faith</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀλῐ́γος</i> (olĭ́gos, “little, few”) +&lrm; <i>πῐστός</i> (pĭstós, “faithful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>giving little nourishment</li><li>taking little nourishment</li><li>(<i>of athletes in training</i>) abstemious</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀλῐγο-</i> (olĭgo-, “little, few”) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō, “to feed, nourish”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a loaf of bread in the shape of a dildo.</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ὄλισβος</i> (ólisbos, “dildo”) +&lrm; <i>κόλλῑξ</i> (kóllīx, “roll or loaf of coarse bread”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slip, to slide, to be slippery</li></ol><p>Generally referred to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sleydʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slippery, slippy</li><li>slippery, hard to catch and keep hold of</li><li>liable to slip</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀλῐσθάνω</i> (olĭsthánō, “to slip”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any loud cry, especially of women invoking a god</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀλολῡ́ζω</i> (ololū́zō, “to cry aloud”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>especially of women</i>) to cry aloud to the gods in prayer or thanksgiving, either with jubilant voice or in lamentation</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic reduplicated formation, with the same ending of <i>ἰύζω</i> (iúzō, “to shout, yell”). Similar formations, genetically cognate or of identical structure, are Latin <i>ululō</i> (“to howl”), Sanskrit <i>उलूलि</i> (ulūli, “a howling, crying aloud”), Lithuanian <i>uluti</i> (“howl”), all with <i>u</i>. Beside these stands this verb, with dissimilation <i>ο-υ</i> or perhaps ablauting to <i>ἐλελεῦ</i> (eleleû, “woe, alas”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lamenting, doleful</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀλοφύρομαι</i> (olophúromai, “I lament over, bewail”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to lament, wail, moan</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to feel pity</li><li>to lament (<i>+ infinitive</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to lament over, bewail</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to pity</li></ol></ol><p>Possibly from *Holbʰ- 'cry, lament'. Compare <i>ὀλολύζω</i> (ololúzō), <i>ὀλολυγμός</i> (ololugmós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>destructive; deadly; fatal</li><li>sinister; ominous</li></ol><p>Derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃elh₁-</i> (“destroy”), the same root of <i>ὄλλυμι</i> (óllumi, “to destroy”). Traditionally explained as <i>&#42;ὀλοϝός</i> (&#42;olowós) with vowel assimilation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>meaning mischief, baleful, baneful</li><li>crafty, shrewd</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀλοός</i> (oloós) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ink of the cuttlefish</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) blood</li></ol><p>Unclear. A cross between <i>θολός</i> (tholós, “ink of the cuttlefish”) and <i>ὀρός</i> (orós, “whey”), as assumed by Frisk, is improbable. A Pre-Greek word is possible.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of <i>ὄλῡρα</i> (ólūra)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄλῡρα</i> (ólūra, “kind of spelt”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mist, fog</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;omíkʰlā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃migʰleh₂</i>.</p><p>Cognate with Lithuanian <i>miglà</i> (“mist, haze”), Sanskrit <i>मेघ</i> (meghá, “cloud, gloomy weather”), Old Armenian <i>մէգ</i> (mēg, “mist”) and Albanian <i>mjegull</i> (“fog; haze”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rainy, of rain</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀμβρέω</i> (ombréō, “to rain, shower down upon”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to urinate</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃meyǵʰ-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>मेहति</i> (mehati), Old Armenian <i>մէզ</i> (mēz), Latin <i>mingō</i>, Old Norse <i>míga</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ὄμμα</i> (ómma)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄμμα</i> (ómma) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὄμνῡμῐ</i> (ómnūmĭ)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made from unripe grapes</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄμφαξ</i> (ómphax, “unripe grape”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) divine voice, oracle</li><li>(<i>in choral lyric</i>) voice, speech, song</li></ol><p>By Grassmann's law from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;honkʷʰā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;songʷʰ-éh₂</i>, from <i>&#42;sengʷʰ-</i> (“to sing”). Cognate with English <i>song</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a vessel</i>) for holding unripe grapes</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄμφαξ</i> (ómphax, “unripe grape”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a boss or knob</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀμφαλός</i> (omphalós) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) navel</li><li>umbilical cord</li><li>anything shaped like a navel, hence:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>knob or boss in the middle of the shield</li><li>button or knob on the horse's yoke to fasten the reins to</li><li>plug or valve closing the outlet of bath</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) knobs at each end of the stick round which books were rolled</li></ol><li>centre or middle point</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) centre of a plant, containing the seed vessel</li><li>centre of an army, properly the point at which an army is divided into two wings</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) keystone of an arched vault</li><li>(<i>at Corycus</i>) vault, tomb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃m̥bʰ-l̥-</i> (“navel”), from <i>&#42;h₃nebʰ-</i> (“navel, center”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>नभ्य</i> (nábhya), Latin <i>umbilīcus</i>, and Old English <i>nafola</i> (English <i>navel</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to make famous or honored; to honor, respect</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to strengthen (a grammatical object)</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Linguistic tradition would seem to suggest a descent from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ep-</i>, as with Latin <i>omnis</i>, but reasoning from Edzard Furnée: ostensibly from <b>ὀμφυν-</b>, a Pre-Greek byform of Ancient Greek <i>ὄμπνη</i> (ómpnē), and the factitive verbal suffix <i>-ύνω</i> (-únō), with haplology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to wipe</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃merǵ-</i> (“to wipe”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>मृज्</i> (mṛj, “to wipe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of garments</i>) of the color of the wild ass</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄναγρος</i> (ónagros, “wild ass”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) one to whom ὄναιο (ónaio) or ὄναιτο (ónaito) is said, blessed</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help, to support, to be useful</li><li>to please, to delight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃n̥-né-h₂-ti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₃neh₂-</i> (“to enjoy”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ἀνῑ́ᾱ</i> (anī́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help, to support, to be useful</li><li>to please, to delight</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃n̥-né-h₂-ti</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₃neh₂-</i> (“to enjoy”). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;unnaną</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fish similar to a cod</li><li>woodlouse</li><li>windlass, crane</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “donkey, ass”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos). See also a similar equation of a fish and beetle in <i>κάραβος</i> (kárabos) and Latin <i>iūlus</i> from <i>ἴουλος</i> (íoulos). The meaning of woodlice arose by reason of the image of them being encountered bearing the weight of pitchers, which was also in outgoing antiquity translated by Arabic حُمُر الأَرْض (ḥumur al-ʔarḍ, literally “earth-asses”). In contrast, Latin <i>asellus</i> (“asslet”) bearing the sense of “woodlouse” was only fabricated in the seventeenth century by Germans after their native <i>Assel</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὀνείατα</i> (oneíata) (<i>used before the rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reproachful</li><li>dishonorable</li></ol><p><i>ὄνειδος</i> (óneidos) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to dreams</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνειρος</i> (óneiros) +&lrm; <i>-ειος</i> (-eios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to upbraid, reproach</li></ol><p><i>ὄνειδος</i> (óneidos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream-interpreter</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνειρος</i> (óneiros, “dream”) +&lrm; <i>πέλω</i> (pélō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cockshead (<i>Onobrychis caput-galli</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “ass, donkey”) +&lrm; <i>βρύχω</i> (brúkhō, “to devour”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>teasel (<i>Dipsacus fullonum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “ass, donkey”) +&lrm; <i>κάρδος</i> (kárdos, “cardoon”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, “diminutive suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of reed extending over the ground</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “ass”) +&lrm; <i>κένταυρος</i> (kéntauros, “centaur”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pelican (<i>Pelecanus onocrotalus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “ass, donkey”) +&lrm; <i>κρόταλον</i> (krótalon, “rattle, clapper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak of by name, address by name</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) to name, specify</li></ol><li>to call one something</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to nominate</li></ol><li>to name after</li><li>to say or give names</li><li>to make famous</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁nómn̥</td></tr></table></p><p>From <i>ὄνομᾰ</i> (ónomă, “name”) +&lrm; <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to name</li><li>to utter, speak</li><li>to nominate, appoint</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁nómn̥</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃nómn̥yoh₂</i> (“am naming”). Equal to <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-ϳω</i> (&#42;-jō), but synchronically exhibits <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō). Cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;namnijaną</i>,</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) ellipsis of <i>πτῶσις ὀνομαστική</i> (ptôsis onomastikḗ, “nominative case”, literally “naming case”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀνομᾰστῐκός</i> (onomăstĭkós): as a noun, a substantivisation of its feminine forms in elliptical use for <i>ἡ ὀνομᾰστῐκὴ πτῶσις</i> (hē onomăstĭkḕ ptôsis); as an adjective, regularly declined forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vocabulary, arranged by subject and not by word order.</li></ol><p>The noun is the substantive form of the adjective (with βιβλίον implied).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good at naming</li><li>of or concerning naming</li><li>(<i>elliptically for</i> ὀνομαστική πτῶσις (ptôsis)) the nominative case</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀνομάζω</i> (onomázō, “to name”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós), from <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma, “name”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>named</li><li>famous, of note, notable</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁nómn̥</td></tr></table></p><p>From <i>ὀνομάζω</i> (onomázō, “I name”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>prone to name-giving</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀνοματοθέτης</i> (onomatothétēs, “name-giver, namer”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Onomatopoeia; the creation of a word, especially the creation of a word in imitation of a sound.</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀνομᾰτοποιός</i> (onomătopoiós, “coiner of names”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Aeolic form of <i>ὀνομαίνω</i> (onomaínō)</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁nómn̥</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃nómn̥yoh₂</i> (“am naming”). By surface analysis, <i>ὄνῠμᾰ</i> (ónŭmă) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nail scissors</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀνυχόω</i> (onukhóō, “to make like a nail”) +&lrm; <i>-τήριον</i> (-tḗrion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cotton thistle (<i>Onopordum acanthium</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “donkey”) + the root of <i>πέρδομαι</i> (pérdomai, “to fart”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan bugloss (<i>Echium parviflorum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “donkey”) +&lrm; <i>χεῖλος</i> (kheîlos, “lip”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of onyx</li><li>of the colour of onyx</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνῠξ</i> (ónŭx, “nail; onyx”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pot marjoram (<i>Origanum onites</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “donkey, ass”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sauce made of vinegar and brine</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄξος</i> (óxos, “vinegar”) +&lrm; <i>ἅλμη</i> (hálmē, “brine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ὀξεῖᾰ</i> (oxeîă)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly; rapidly</li><li>soon</li><li>sharply</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ὀξεῖᾰ</i> (oxeîă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sour wine</li><li>garden sorrel (<i>Rumex acetosa</i>)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from <i>ὄξος</i> (óxos, “poor wine, vinegar”) or <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús, “sharp, pointed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ὀξεῖᾰ</i> (oxeîă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxia (the acute accent, indicating high or rising pitch)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>firethorn (<i>Pyracantha coccinea</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>ἄκανθα</i> (ákantha, “thorn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of sour milk</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύγαλα</i> (oxúgala, “sour milk”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irascibility</li><li>vivacity</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with pointed head</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp, pointed”) +&lrm; <i>κεφᾰλή</i> (kephălḗ, “head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curled dock (<i>Rumex crispus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús, “sharp, pointed”) +&lrm; <i>λάπαθον</i> (lápathon, “monk's rhubarb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>butcher's broom (<i>Ruscus aculeatus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp, acid”) +&lrm; <i>μυρσίνη</i> (mursínē, “myrtle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>oxymoronically</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́μωρος</i> (oxŭ́mōros) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mixture of vinegar and pepper</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp; acid”) +&lrm; <i>πέπερῐ</i> (péperĭ, “pepper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pronouncing with an acute accent</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξυτονέω</i> (oxutonéō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) sharp-pointed</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) vertex of a triangle</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly; rapidly</li><li>soon</li><li>sharply</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beech (<i>Fagus sylvatica</i>)</li><li>spear made from its wood</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Heh₃s-</i> and, so, cognate with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;askaz</i> (“ash tree”), Old Armenian <i>հացի</i> (hacʻi, “ash tree”) and Albanian <i>ah</i> (“beech”). The Indo-European character of these words is far from sure, despite the tradition to compare them. The vocalic variation in Greek, which is not sufficiently explained by assuming the intervention of <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús, “sharp”), may in principle also point to a Pre-Greek word. Compare Ancient Macedonian <i>ἄξος</i> (áxos, “a kind of wood”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the circumflex accent</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ-</i> (oxŭ-) +&lrm; <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small vinegar saucer</li><li>shallow earthen vessel, saucer</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) kind of harmonica made of saucers, struck with a wooden hammer</li><li>fourth part of a cotyle</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>βάπτω</i> (báptō, “to dip”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sour milk, whey</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sauce made of vinegar and garum</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>γάρος</i> (gáros, “garum”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>grease, lard</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>axungia</i> (“grease”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>round pricklyhead poppy (<i>Papaver hybridum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>γόνος</i> (gónos, “offspring”) +&lrm; <i>-ον</i> (-on).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick to anger, choleric</li><li>sharp to punish</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cade (<i>Juniperus oxycedrus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús, “sharp”) + <i>κέδρος</i> (kédros, “cedar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oxymoron</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pointedly foolish</li><li>oxymoronic</li></ol><p><i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp, keen, pointed”) +&lrm; <i>μωρός</i> (mōrós, “dull, stupid, foolish”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sharpen</li><li>to provoke</li><li>to acidify</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) to put the acute accent on</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús) +&lrm; <i>-ύνω</i> (-únō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp-snouted</li><li>sharp-pointed</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elephantfish (<i>Mormyrus niloticus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>ῥύγχος</i> (rhúnkhos, “beak, snout”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὀξύρρυγχος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oxyrynchus, Lower Egypt, Egypt</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ρρῠγχος</i> (oxŭ́rrhŭnkhos, “an Egyptian fish”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp; pointed (especially of swords, axes, etc.)</li><li>(<i>of the senses</i>) sharp; keen</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of sight</i>) dazzling; bright</li><li>(<i>of sound</i>) shrill; piercing; high; sharp</li><li>(<i>of taste</i>) sharp; pungent; acid</li></ol><li>quick; hasty; swift</li><li>sharp; clever</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived as a <i>u</i>-stem o-grade adjective of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂eḱ-</i> (“sharp”) and compared with <i>ὄκρῐς</i> (ókrĭs, “point, prominence”); however, such a stem and ablaut combination is rather strange. An alternative theory by Narten reconstructs the word's root as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ks-</i>, comparing Sanskrit <i>अक्ष्णोति</i> (akṣṇoti, “to mark the ear”), but this is doubtful.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sharp rush (<i>Juncus acutus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>σχοῖνος</i> (skhoînos, “rush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of angles</i>) sharpness, pointedness</li><li>(<i>of senses</i>) sharpness</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of sound</i>) highness, intensity</li><li>(of taste,&#32;in the plural) pungency, acidity</li><li>(<i>of sight</i>) acuity</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) cleverness</li><li>(<i>of motion, action, or occurrence</i>) quickness</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) oxia (the acute accent)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sharp-sounding, piercing</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) oxytone (having the stress or an acute accent on the last syllable)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξῠ́ς</i> (oxŭ́s, “sharp”) +&lrm; <i>τόνος</i> (tónos, “tone, sound”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of beech wood</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύα</i> (oxúa, “beech”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to join (<i>someone to someone else</i>) as a companion, guide, escort, etc.</li><li>to cause to follow or accompany</li><li>to bestow, lend, confer</li><li>to follow hard upon, press upon</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to take (<i>someone with oneself</i>) as companion, guide, escort, etc.</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i>. The term may have emerged from the affixation of <i>-άζω</i> (-ázō) to a noun <i>&#42;ὀπός</i> (&#42;opós) or <i>&#42;ὀπή</i> (&#42;opḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opal</li></ol><p>According to Ernout and Meillet (endorsed by Beekes), a loanword, likely borrowed from Sanskrit <i>उपल</i> (upala, “gem, stone”), possibly a variant of <i>उपर</i> (upara, “lower”), from <i>उप</i> (upa, “below, under, down”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>comrade in war; companion; esquire</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) follower; attendant</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the term derives from earlier <i>&#42;ὀπά-ϝων</i> (&#42;opá-wōn, “belonging to the retinue”), itself derived from <i>&#42;ὀπά</i> (&#42;opá, “followers, retinue”). Beekes suggests that this possible intermediary form may constitute a verbal noun from <i>ἕπομαι</i> (hépomai, “to follow, obey”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sekʷ-</i> (“to follow”). Alternatively, the linguist Anthony Yates suggests that it may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sokʷ-eh₂</i>, itself possibly the source for <i>&#42;sokʷh₂ṓy</i>. The psilosis is also found in <i>ὀπάζω</i> (opázō, “to chase, oppress”). It is likely that the term is related to Mycenaean Greek (o-qa-wo-mi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opening, hole, hollow</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) orifice in the body</li><li>hole in the roof, serving as a chimney</li><li>(<i>architecture</i>) hole in the frieze left to receive the beam-ends</li><li>sight</li></ol><p>From the radical &#42;ὀπ- (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i>), the same root of <i>ὄμμα</i> (ómma, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὀπῐ́σω</i> (opĭ́sō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of place</i>) backwards</li><li>back; back again by the same way one took</li><li>again</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) hereafter</li><li>following; yet to come</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>after</li></ol><p>The usual explanation is to regard the base <i>&#42;ὀπῐ́-</i> (&#42;opĭ́-) &lt; Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;opí</i>, <i>&#42;opi-</i> &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;opí</i>, as an ablaut variant of <i>ἐπῐ́</i> (epĭ́, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;epí</i>). Compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀃𐀠</i> (o-pi, “on”), Luwian (/⁠appi⁠/, “back”), Latin <i>op-</i> (“upon”), Lithuanian <i>api-</i> (“about”).</p><p>Dunkel instead derives <i>ὀπῐ́σσω</i> (opĭ́ssō) &lt; Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;opissō</i> &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;op-ityo-o</i> (“back; later”, adverb), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;op-ityo-</i> (“hindmost; rear”, adjective). Compare Hittite (appezziya, “later”, adverb) and <small>[script needed]</small> (appezziya-, “hindmost; rear”, adjective). The base is thus Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;óp</i>, an ablaut variant of <i>&#42;áp</i> (“away; back; after”, adverb); compare Latin <i>ab</i> (“away; from”), which fits much better semantically.</p><p>For the suffix, compare <i>πρόσσω</i> (próssō), <i>περῐσσός</i> (perĭssós), <i>εἴσω</i> (eísō), and <i>μέτᾰσσαι</i> (métăssai, “those born later”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ὀπηδός</i> (opēdós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a hole or opening</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀπή</i> (opḗ, “hole, opening”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) to attend, accompany, follow</li></ol><p><i>ὀπηδός</i> (opēdós) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attendant, companion</li></ol><p>A nominal formation related to <i>ὀπᾱ́ων</i> (opā́ōn, “comrade; attendant”), perhaps via an unattested adverb <i>&#42;ὀπηδόν</i> (&#42;opēdón, “in company”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dreadful, respectful</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄπῐς</i> (ópĭs, “revenge”) +&lrm; <i>-νός</i> (-nós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stare at, to watch</li><li>to spy</li><li>to seduce</li><li>(<i>middle</i>) to be alert</li></ol><p>from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;opi-h₃kʷ</i> (“look at”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in a backward direction</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄπῐσθεν</i> (ópĭsthen, “behind; at the back”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who guards the rear</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) rearguard</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄπισθεν</i> (ópisthen, “behind; at the back”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juice of the Arabian balsam tree, balm of Gilead</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>ὀπός</i> (opós, “plant juice”) +&lrm; <i>βᾰ́λσᾰμον</i> (bắlsămon, “balm”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the Arabian balsam tree</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀποβᾰ́λσᾰμον</i> (opobắlsămon, “balm of Gilead”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>gum of opopanax</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀπός</i> (opós, “juice”) + <i>πάναξ</i> (pánax, “name of various healing plants”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to roast, broil or fry, toast, cook by means of fire or dry heat</li><li>to bake bread, but also bricks or pottery</li><li>(<i>of the sun</i>) to bake, scorch</li></ol><p>This verb's formation is similar to <i>ἀρτάω</i> (artáō), <i>οὐτάω</i> (outáō) and <i>φοιτάω</i> (phoitáō). It is commonly assumed that the verbal adjective <i>ὀπτός</i> (optós, “baked, roasted”) forms its basis. For the etymology, Furnée compares <i>ὄψον</i> (ópson, “any cooked dish”) as a variant, which seems a good possibility; the variation is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) assistant, especially in a military sense, aide-de-camp</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>optiō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>optic, optical, visual</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄψις</i> (ópsis) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>cooking</i>) roasted, broiled, boiled</li><li>(<i>cooking</i>) baked</li><li>(<i>metallurgy</i>) forged, tempered</li><li>scorched</li><li>seen, visible</li></ol><p>Unknown. Not related to <i>πέσσω</i> (péssō, “to soften, ripen; to cook, dress, bake”).</p><p>From <i>ὄπωπᾰ</i> (ópōpă, “I have seen”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, “-able”, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) sight, view</li><li>outward appearance, aspect</li><li>sight, power of seeing</li><li>eyeball, eye-apple</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄπωπα</i> (ópōpa, “to watch, observe”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or in autumn, autumnal</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀπώρα</i> (opṓra, “autumn”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνός</i> (-ĭnós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit room</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀπώρᾱ</i> (opṓrā, “fruit”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruiterer, greengrocer</li></ol><p><i>ὀπώρᾱ</i> (opṓrā, “fruit season; fruit”) +&lrm; <i>-πώλης</i> (-pṓlēs, “shop owner, retailer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>juice, vegetable/plant juice</li><li>(<i>in particular</i>) fig-juice (which can be used as rennet)</li></ol><p>From earlier <i>&#42;ὁπός</i> (&#42;hopós), which underwent Ionic psilosis, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sokʷos</i> (“juice, resin”). Cognate with Russian <i>сок</i> (sok, “juice”) and possibly Albanian <i>gjak</i> (“blood”), Latin <i>sūcus</i> (“juice”) (though Beekes doubts the pertinence of the Latin).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice</li><li>word</li><li>eye; face</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wókʷs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wṓkʷs</i> (“voice”). Related to <i>ἔπος</i> (épos) and <i>εἰπεῖν</i> (eipeîn). Cognates include Latin <i>vōx</i>, Sanskrit <i>वाच्</i> (vā́c), and Tocharian A <i>wak</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ókʷs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ókʷs</i> (“eye”). See also <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the part of the year between the rising of Sirius and of Arcturus (i. e. the end of July, all August and part of September), the end of summer; later it was used for autumn</li><li>fruit itself (<i>extended from its use for the fruit-time</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) summer-bloom; i.e. the bloom of youth</li></ol><p>Seems to be a contraction of an original <i>&#42;ὀποσάρα</i> (&#42;oposára), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁epi</i> (“on, at”) (whence <i>ἐπῐ́</i> (epĭ́)) + <i>&#42;(s)h₁ósr̥</i>, a variant of <i>&#42;(s)h₁ésō</i> (“harvest season”), from <i>&#42;(s)h₁es-</i> (“crop, harvest”); the exact formation <i>&#42;(s)h₁ósr̥</i> is found also in Old Armenian <i>ար-ա-ց</i> (ar-a-cʻ). Other cognates include Russian <i>о́сень</i> (ósenʹ), Gothic <i>𐌰𐍃𐌰𐌽𐍃</i> (asans), and Old English <i>earnian</i> (English <i>earn</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) kerchief, scarf</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;Christianity) a vestment worn by Greek Orthodox clergy</li></ol><p>From Late Latin <i>ōrārium</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to reach, stretch, stretch out</li><li>to reach out, hold out, hand, give</li><li>(<i>middle voice&#32;and&#32;passive voice</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to stretch oneself out, stretch forth one's hand</li><li>to reach at, reach out to; to aim at [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to yearn for [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to take [<i>with</i> accusative]</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃réǵeti</i> (“to be straightening, stretching, setting upright”).</p><p>Cognates include Avestan <i>𐬭𐬁𐬱𐬙𐬀</i> (rāšta), Old Irish <i>rigid</i>, Latin <i>regō</i>, and Old English <i>riht</i> (English <i>right</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oregano, marjoram</li><li>sourness, bitterness</li></ol><p>Disputed; some sources claim a compound of <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>γάνος</i> (gános, “brightness, freshness”), though Beekes suspects that the word is a foreign borrowing and was folk-etymologically adapted to more closely resemble the supposed component words given above.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bread made of rice</li></ol><p>A borrowing from an Eastern Iranian language, like <i>ὄρυζα</i> (óruza, “rice”). See the Iranian forms in Middle Persian <i>blnc</i> (/⁠brinǰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>exciter</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρῑ́νω</i> (orī́nō, “to stir, arouse, excite”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to stir, rouse, arouse, move</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i> (“move, stir”). Cognate with <i>ὄρνῡμι</i> (órnūmi), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rinǫti</i> and Latin <i>orior</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a variety of apple characterized by its rounded shape</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>orbiculātum</i> (“rounded”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to ripen</li><li>(<i>of living things</i>) to be sexually aroused or on heat</li><li>to be eager, ready, excited</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀργή</i> (orgḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>temper, temperament, disposition, nature, heart</li><li>passion, mood</li><li>anger, wrath</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;worǵéh₂</i>, from the root <i>&#42;werǵ-</i>; equivalent to <i>ἔρδω</i> (érdō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē). Compare Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;wergā</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to anger, irritate</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be or become angry</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀργή</i> (orgḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inclined to anger; irascible; irritable</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀργή</i> (orgḗ, “anger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>angrily</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀργῐ́λος</i> (orgĭ́los) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orgasm</li></ol><p><i>ὀργάω</i> (orgáō) +&lrm; <i>-σμός</i> (-smós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>irascibility; irritability</li></ol><p><i>ὀργῐ́λος</i> (orgĭ́los, “irascible; irritable”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ὀργῐ́λος</i> (orgĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ὀργῐ́λος</i> (orgĭ́los)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>brass made from yellow copper ore; an alloy of copper and "mock silver" (presumably zinc)</li><li>orichalcum, a natural or mythical valuable alloy of copper and perhaps gold</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒍏</i> (URUD /⁠ēru, wēru⁠/, “copper”) + <i>χαλκός</i> (khalkós, “copper”), making a tautological compound. The first element was folk-etymologically identified as the dative singular of <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain”); compare the later folk-etymological alteration of the first portion of Latin <i>aurichalcum</i> after <i>aurum</i> (“gold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>mountainous</li><li>mountain- <i>(relational)</i></li></ol><p>For <i>&#42;ὀρεσνός</i> (&#42;oresnós), from <i>ὄρος</i> (óros) +&lrm; <i>-νος</i> (-nos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of orichalcum</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρείχαλκος</i> (oreíkhalkos, “orichalcum”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pertaining to desire</li><li>causing appetite</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρεκτός</i> (orektós, “stretched out; desired”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>stretched out</li><li>longed for, desired</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;orektós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃reǵtós</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃reǵ-</i> (whence also <i>ὀρέγω</i> (orégō)). Cognate with Latin <i>rēctus</i>, Avestan <i>𐬭𐬀𐬱𐬙𐬀</i> (rašta), English <i>right</i>. By surface analysis, <i>ὀρέγω</i> (orégō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) mountain-bred</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρος</i> (óros) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mule, beast of burden</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρος</i> (óros) + <i>-εύς</i> (-eús).</p><p>Case-forms show Attic shortening of the original stem <i>ὀρηϝ-</i> (orēw-); see quantitative metathesis.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>men, in the mystic language of the Pythia</li></ol><p>Unknown. According to Güntert, it belongs to <i>ἄρσην</i> (ársēn, “male”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) lower part of a mast</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρθιος</i> (órthios, “straight up, steep, uphill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>correct diction, especially in poetry</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós, “correct”) +&lrm; <i>ἔπος</i> (épos, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orthography</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρθογρᾰ́φος</i> (orthogrắphos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rectangular</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter,&#32;geometry) rectangle</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós) +&lrm; <i>γωνίᾱ</i> (gōníā) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight and just</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós) +&lrm; <i>δίκαιος</i> (díkaios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>right opinion</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρθόδοξος</i> (orthódoxos) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(nonce word,&#32;humorous) dawn-wandering nuisance-suing sad-litigious troublesome</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρθρος</i> (órthros) +&lrm; <i>φοιτᾰ́ω</i> (phoitắō) +&lrm; <i>σῡκοφᾰ́ντης</i> (sūkophắntēs) +&lrm; <i>δῐ́κη</i> (dĭ́kē) +&lrm; <i>τᾰλαίπωρος</i> (tălaípōros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>orthodox (in religion)</li></ol><p>Late Antiquity, exclusively in the Christian context, from about the 4th or 5th century.</p><p>Compound of <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós, “straight”) +&lrm; <i>δόξα</i> (dóxa, “opinion”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, “noun of result or an abstract noun of action”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having upright horns (<i>epithet of oxen</i>)</li><li>having tall sterns turned up like horns (<i>epithet of ships</i>)</li></ol><p>Bahuvrīhi compound of <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós) +&lrm; <i>κραῖρα</i> (kraîra, alternative form of <i>κεραία</i> (keraía) and <i>κέρας</i> (kéras)) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>upright breathing, orthopnoea, a type of asthma which only admits breathing in an upright posture</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós, “straight, upright”) + <i>πνέω</i> (pnéō, “breathe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight, upright, standing, rearing (<i>of horses</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>on tiptoe, full of expectation, excited</li></ol><li>in line, straight forward, in a straight line</li><li>right, safe, prosperous (<i>of places, ships, course etc.</i>)</li><li>right, true, correct (<i>often in adverbial use</i>)</li><li>true, real, genuine</li><li>upright, righteous, just</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) straight, straightforward, steadfast, firm</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) at right angles to</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) rising vertically (<i>of signs of the zodiac</i>)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) nominative</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ortʰwós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃r̥dʰwós</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃erdʰ-</i> (“upright”). Cognate with Latin <i>arduus</i>, Sanskrit <i>ऊर्ध्व</i> (ūrdhvá). It is possible that the term could also relate to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀃𐁍𐀸𐀃</i> (o-two-we-o).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to set upright, raise up (something or someone that has fallen down)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to build or rebuild</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to stand up (get up from a sitting position)</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to straighten</li><li>(figurative,&#32;transitive) to restore to health or happiness</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to exalt, honor; to make famous</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to correct</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bring to a happy end; (<i>passive voice</i>) to succeed, prosper, flourish</li></ol><p><i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós, “straight”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō, factitive verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>correctly</li></ol><p><i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós, “correct”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>star-of-Bethlehem (<i>Ornithogalum umbellatum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρνις</i> (órnis, “bird”) +&lrm; <i>γάλα</i> (gála, “milk”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>greater dodder (<i>Cuscuta europaea</i>)</li><li>A broomrape, perhaps bean broomrape (<i>Orobanche crenata</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄροβος</i> (órobos, “bitter vetch”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγχω</i> (ánkhō, “to strangle”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, suffix forming action nouns).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to stir up, rouse, urge on, excite</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i> (“move, stir”). Cognate with <i>ὄρνῡμι</i> (órnūmi) and <i>ὀρῑ́νω</i> (orī́nō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ceiling</li><li>roof</li></ol><p>An o-grade nominal formation from <i>ἐρέφω</i> (eréphō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain-guard</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rush, spring, hurry</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;orówō</i>, of uncertain origin. According to Beekes, a back-formation from <i>ὀροῦσαι</i> (oroûsai), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i>, whence also Ancient Greek <i>ὄρνυμαι</i> (órnumai). De Vaan, however, considers the term to be a derivative of <i>&#42;h₃rew-</i>, whence also Latin <i>ruō</i> (“to rush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to fear, dread <i>(with accusative / περί τινος / ὑπέρ τινος / μὴ + subjunctive)</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hen, fowl</li></ol><p>Probably from <i>&#42;ὀρτος</i> (&#42;ortos) (<i>meaning unknown</i>) +&lrm; <i>-αλίς</i> (-alís).</p><p>Compare <i>συκαλίς</i> (sukalís) and <i>δορκαλίς</i> (dorkalís), which contain the same suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quail-coop</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρτῠξ</i> (órtŭx, “quail”) +&lrm; <i>τροφεῖον</i> (tropheîon, “small room, chamber”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quail (bird)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρτυξ</i> (órtux) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of quarrying tool, perhaps a pickaxe</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρύσσω</i> (orússō, “to dig”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow rice</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρῠζον</i> (órŭzon) +&lrm; <i>τρέφω</i> (tréphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>miner</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) shovel, spade</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ὀρύσσω</i> (orússō) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>formed by digging</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ὀρύσσω</i> (orússō) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>loud noise, din</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rewg-</i> (“to roar”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>at Athens</i>) one who guards orphans</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) guardians of orphans who had lost their fathers in war</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρφᾰνός</i> (orphănós, “orphan”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left orphan, without parents</li><li>childless</li><li>destitute, bereft</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃órbʰos</i> (“orphan”), from <i>&#42;h₃erbʰ-</i> (“to change ownership”). Cognate with Latin <i>orbus</i> (“orphaned”), Sanskrit <i>अर्भ</i> (árbha, “small”), Old Armenian <i>որբ</i> (orb, “orphan”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, dusky, murky</li><li>nightly, by night</li><li>(Byzantine,&#32;nominalized,&#32;masculine) night watchman</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρφνη</i> (órphnē) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dark, dusky</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρφνη</i> (órphnē, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dusky grouper (<i>Epinephelus marginatus</i>)</li></ol><p>Unknown. Formation like the fish names <i>λαγώς</i> (lagṓs) and <i>ἀχαρνώς</i> (akharnṓs). Often compared to <i>ὄρφνη</i> (órphnē, “darkness of night”), with reference to the color, but unclear in detail. Connected with <i>ὀρφανός</i> (orphanós, “orphan”) by Bechtel, but without semantic argumentation; perhaps because of its solitary way of life.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dance</li><li>to represent by dancing or pantomime</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to leap, bound</li><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to cause to dance</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ergʰ-</i> (“to mount”), whence also perhaps Hittite (arkatta, “to mount sexually”). Frisk suggests that term may constitute an iterative-intensive formation from <i>ἔρχομαι</i> (érkhomai, “to come, go”), for which see there for more. Watkins posits a connection with <i>ὄρχις</i> (órkhis, “testicle”), which appears semantically tenuous.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>space on which the chorus danced, orchestra</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρχέομαι</i> (orkhéomai, “to dance”) +&lrm; <i>-τρᾰ</i> (-tră, “a suffix used to form instrument nouns (pl.)”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) dance, dancing</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ὀρχέομαι</i> (orkhéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common peony (<i>Paeonia officinalis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄροβος</i> (órobos, “chickpea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of bitter vetch</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄροβος</i> (órobos, “bitter vetch”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) bough, branch</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from a previous form <i>ϝρόδαμνος</i> (wródamnos), itself of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cuckoopint (<i>Arum maculatum</i>), a remedy for jaundice</li></ol><p>According to Strömberg from <i>Ὀρόντης</i> (Oróntēs) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion). Furnée refers to Gams, who connects it with <i>ἄρον</i> (áron, “arum”), suggesting a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whey, the watery part of milk</li><li>serum of blood</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;horós</i> (an <i>-ός</i> (-ós) nominal), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ser-</i> (“to flow, run”); see also Sanskrit <i>सर</i> (sara, “flowing”), <i>सरित्</i> (sarit, “river, brook”) and Latin <i>serum</i> (“whey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bluethroat (<i>Luscinia svecica</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>σπίζα</i> (spíza, “chaffinch”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>roofed with reeds</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄροφος</i> (órophos, “thatch”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rice</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρυζον</i> (óruzon, “rice”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to dig</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;orúťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃rewk-</i> (“to dig up, grub”). Cognate with Latin <i>runcō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ὀρύσσω</i> (orússō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;orúťťō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃rewk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>smell, scent, odour</li><li>stench, stink</li><li>the sense of smell</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄζω</i> (ózō, “to smell”) +&lrm; <i>-μή</i> (-mḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>smelled, that which can be smelled</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀσμάω</i> (osmáō, “to smell”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curled octopus, a kind of octopus of the species <i>Eledone cirrhosa</i></li></ol><p>From <i>ὀσμή</i> (osmḗ, “smell, odour”), with a suffix found also in <i>κοτύλη</i> (kotúlē, “cup”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A place to keep pulse in</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄσπριον</i> (ósprion, “legume, pulse”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>little bone, small piece of bone</li><li>(<i>of nuts</i>) stone, kernel</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὀστέα</i> (ostéa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bone</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) bones of the earth: rock</li><li>stone of fruit</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ésth₁</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>os</i>, Sanskrit <i>अस्थि</i> (ásthi), Old Armenian <i>ոսկր</i> (oskr), and Albanian <i>asht</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of bones</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A receptacle for bones, a sarcophagus</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ossifrage, lammergeier (<i>Gypaetus barbatus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”) +&lrm; <i>κατεάσσω</i> (kateássō, “to break, shatter”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) contracted form of <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>earthen, made of clay (of vessels)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄστρακον</i> (óstrakon, “earthen vessel”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>that lives in a shell (said of a mollusc)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄστρεον</i> (óstreon, “bivalve”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to banish (<i>by voting with potsherds</i>), to ostracize</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄστρακον</i> (óstrakon, “potsherd”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) pinecone or pine nut</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄστρᾰκον</i> (óstrăkon, “earthen vessel; potsherd”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ostracism</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀστρακίζω</i> (ostrakízō) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with a shell like a potsherd, hard-shelled</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄστρᾰκον</i> (óstrăkon, “potsherd”) +&lrm; <i>δέρμα</i> (dérma, “skin”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cadmia of inferior quality</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄστρακον</i> (óstrakon, “earthen vessel”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖτις</i> (-îtis), feminine form of <i>ὀστρακίτης</i> (ostrakítēs, “earthen, made of clay”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common hophornbeam (<i>Ostrya carpinifolia</i>)</li></ol><p>Frisk compares <i>ὄστρεον</i> (óstreon, “bivalve”) but the semantics are weak. Heubeck and Neumann suggest a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the enlargements in "-ύα", "-υΐς" and "-ύς".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to get scent of</li></ol><p>It is widely agreed that the initial syllable contains a zero grade s-stem from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ed-</i>. Compare Ancient Greek <i>ὄζω</i> (ózō). Cognates include Latin <i>odor</i> and Lithuanian <i>úosti</i>.</p><p>Karl Brugmann connects it with Sanskrit <i>घ्रा</i> (ghrā, “to smell, perceive odor”).</p><p>Alternatively, Seebold compares the root to Old English <i>brǣþ</i> (“smell; steam”), whence English <i>breath</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) lumbago</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀσφῠᾰλγέω</i> (osphŭălgéō, “to have lumbago”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὀσφῦς</i> (osphûs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) hips or loins; lower part of the back</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ἰξῡ́ς</i> (ixū́s) and <i>νηδῡ́ς</i> (nēdū́s). Often connected with <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”); however, Furnée adduces <i>φῡ́ς</i> (phū́s, “loins”), pointing to a Pre-Greek prothetic vowel. He also accepts the connection with <i>ψόᾱ</i> (psóā, “loin muscles”), which was suggested already by Prellwitz.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick, nimble, ready</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ὀτρύνω</i> (otrúnō, “to incite, stir up”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stir up, rouse, encourage</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to hasten</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;twerH-</i> (“to stir, move, hasten, hurry”) with irregular metathesis, related to Sanskrit <i>तु॒र</i> (turá, “strong, powerful”), Sanskrit <i>त्वर॑ते</i> (tvárate, “hasten, hurry, urge”), Albanian <i>turrem</i> (“to rush”), the Celtic tribe <i>Turones</i>, and Latin <i>Tyras</i>. However, the initial <i>ὀ-</i> prefix is not expected. See also <i>σαίρω</i> (saírō, “to sweep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divine from an ominous sound</li><li>to regard as ominous</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄττα</i> (ótta) (Attic form of <i>ὄσσα</i> (óssa, “divine or ominous sound or rumor”)) + <i>-εύομαι</i> (-eúomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unknown kind of plant</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὀφείλω</i> (opheílō)</li><li>to increase, enlarge, elevate, strengthen</li><li>to heap up, bring together, sweep</li></ol><p>Probably related to <i>ὄφελος</i> (óphelos, “advantage”), <i>ὀφείλω</i> (opheílō, “owe”).</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bhelyō</i>. Cognates include Old Armenian <i>աւել</i> (awel).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>ὄφῐς</i> (óphĭs, “snake”); snakeling, snakelet</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄφῐς</i> (óphĭs, “snake”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to owe</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to incur</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of persons) to be due or liable to</li></ol><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to be bound, obligated, to ought, to should, to have to</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) it behooves</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;opʰéľľō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃bʰélyeti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃bʰel-</i> (whence <i>ὄφελος</i> (óphelos)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a debtor</li><li>(<i>with copulative verb</i>) to be under an obligation</li><li>(<i>New Testament, fig.</i>) a sinner</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀφείλω</i> (opheílō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the eye; ophthalmic</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>surgery</i>) ophthalmic surgeon</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀφθᾰλμός</i> (ophthălmós, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eye</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) eye of a master or ruler</li><li>eye of heaven</li><li>that which is dearest or best; light, cheer, comfort</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) eye, bud (such as the eye of a potato)</li><li>(<i>surgery</i>) surgical bandage covering one or both eyes</li><li>(architecture,&#32;in the dual) disks forming the centers of the volutes of an Ionic capital</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a kind of stone</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) water inlet of a mill</li></ol><li>sight</li><li>understanding</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>θάλαμος</i> (thálamos, “chamber”), but note the usual ancient Greek word for eye is the similar <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps), with a long /o/.</p><p>Based on irregular κτ : πτ : φθ variation seen in variants like <i>ὄκταλλος</i> (óktallos) and <i>ὀπτίλλος</i> (optíllos), as well as the suffix <i>-αλλ-</i> (-all-) seen in <i>ὄκταλλος</i> (óktallos) being difficult to explain in Indo-European terms, Beekes takes the word to be of Pre-Greek origin, with proto-form <i>&#42;okʷt-alʸ-m-</i>. The hypothetical Pre-Greek form's similarity to the Proto-Indo-European equivalent <i>&#42;h₃ókʷs</i> (“eye”) is coincidental, according to Beekes; he additionally notes the contrast with the Homeric gloss <i>ὄκκον</i> (ókkon) given to <i>ὀφθαλμόν</i> (ophthalmón), the former which may be from the Indo-European root.</p><p>Alternatively, Watkins derives it from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ókʷs</i> by taboo deformation to avoid the evil eye.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>snakelike; serpentine</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄφῐς</i> (óphĭs) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>serpentinely; in a snakelike manner; like a snake</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀφῐοειδής</i> (ophĭoeidḗs, “snakelike”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>parsnip (<i>Pastinaca sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>Neutral form of <i>ὀφιοκτόνος</i> (ophioktónos, “serpent-killer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serpent handler</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) Ophiuchus, the constellation</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄφις</i> (óphis, “snake”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to bear, carry, bring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(Byzantine,&#32;literary) snake-haired</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄφῐς</i> (óphĭs, “serpent; snake”) +&lrm; <i>θρῐ́ξ</i> (thrĭ́x, “hair”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) ploughshare; plough</li></ol><p>It can be identified with Old Prussian <i>wagnis</i> (“coulter, ploughshare”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wogʷʰni-</i> (“coulter”). Beside this stands Latin <i>vōmer</i> (“ploughshare”), from an unclear base form. It is probable that <i>ὄφατα</i> (óphata, “ties of the plough”) also belongs here.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyebrow</li><li>brow of a hill</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;opʰrū́s</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃bʰrúHs</i> (“eyebrow”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>भ्रू</i> (bhrū́), Lithuanian <i>bruvis</i>, Tocharian B <i>pärwāne</i>, Old English <i>brū</i> (English <i>brow</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bear, endure, sustain</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to suffer</li><li>to continue, maintain, preserve</li></ol><li>(<i>mediopassive voice</i>) to be carried, borne</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to ride</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ὄχος</i> (ókhos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō). The mediopassive meaning of the verb may derive from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wokʰéyomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;woǵʰéyeti</i>, itself a causative to <i>&#42;weǵʰ-</i>. However, the active meaning of the verb was probably influenced by <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō), itself from the root <i>&#42;seǵʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>end or issue of an aqueduct</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀχετός</i> (okhetós, “waterpipe, aqueduct”) +&lrm; <i>κάρα</i> (kára, “head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>waterpipe, conduit, channel, aqueduct</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) duct leading to the lung</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) streams, currents</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) means of escape</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀχέω</i> (okhéō, “to carry”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chinstrap (strip used to fasten a hat or helmet under the chin)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fastenings (<i>of a belt</i>)</li><li>bolt (bar to prevent a door from being forced open)</li></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to be troubled or angered</li></ol><p>"Probably a causative or iterative-intensive" of <i>ἔχθω</i> (ékhthō) according to Beekes, who adduces a number of parallel cases such as <i>φοβέω</i> (phobéō) / <i>φέβομαι</i> (phébomai), <i>θροέω</i> (throéō) / <i>θρέομαι</i> (thréomai), and others.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mob leader</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄχλος</i> (ókhlos, “mob”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγός</i> (agōgós, “leading, guiding”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mob rule</li></ol><p><i>ὄχλος</i> (ókhlos, “mob”) +&lrm; <i>-κρᾰτῐ́ᾱ</i> (-krătĭ́ā, “form of government”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>firm, lasting, stout</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of places</i>) strong, secure</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(military,&#32;of a stronghold or position) strong, tenable</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “hold, remain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stronghold, fortress, bastion, citadel, castle (a place that has been fortified)</li><li>(<i>military</i>) fortification; the strengthening of one's defenses (the result or completed action of fortifying)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) any source of strength on which one relies, such as an argument used to fortify and defend one's opinion</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀχυρόω</i> (okhuróō, “to fortify, strengthen”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act of making secure, fortification</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀχυρόω</i> (okhuróō) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis), from <i>ὀχυρός</i> (okhurós, “firm, lasting”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A little plate of food, in particular fish.</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) A fish.</li></ol><p><i>ὄψον</i> (ópson, “delicacy”) +&lrm; <i>-άριον</i> (-árion, “diminutive suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>late</li><li>long after</li><li>late in the day, in the evening</li></ol><p>From a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁epi</i> (“on”); related to <i>ὀπίσω</i> (opísō, “after”) and <i>ὄπισθεν</i> (ópisthen, “in the future”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>late-born; afterborn</li><li>(<i>of a child</i>) late-born; born in a parent's old age</li><li>later-born; younger; young</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀψέ</i> (opsé, “late”) +&lrm; <i>γον-</i> (gon-, root of <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to become”)) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>opsimathy, late-gotten learning</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀψέ</i> (opsé, “late”) +&lrm; <i>μανθάνω</i> (manthánō, “to learn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A place for keeping victuals in</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄψον</i> (ópson, “food”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>carrying food</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄψον</i> (ópson, “delicacies”) +&lrm; <i>-φόρος</i> (-phóros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>ambitransitive</i>) to buy fish; to buy victuals in general</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄψον</i> (ópson, “delicacies”) +&lrm; <i>ὠνέομαι</i> (ōnéomai, “to buy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>salary, wages, pay, allowance</li><li>provisions</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀψωνέω</i> (opsōnéō) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>οἰζῡρός</i> (oizūrós)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀῐ̈ζῡ́ς</i> (oĭ̈zū́s, “misery”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>οἰζῡ́ς</i> (oizū́s)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀῐ̈́ζω</i> (oĭ̈́zō).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὀϊζύς)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Oizys</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀῐ̈ζῡ́ς</i> (oĭ̈zū́s, “woe, misery”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shoot arrows</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with genitive) to shoot with an arrow</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀϊστός</i> (oïstós) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quiver</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀϊστός</i> (oïstós, “arrow”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrow</li></ol><p>No convincing etymology, but DELG supposes a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>(rarely in&#32;Epic,&#32;often in later Greek) the</li></ol><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative pronoun)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>that</li><li>(<i>personal</i>) he, she, it, they</li></ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) alternative form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hó &#42;hā́ &#42;tó</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;só</i> <i>&#42;séh₂</i> <i>&#42;tód</i>. There are many cognates, including Sanskrit <i>स</i> (sá), <i>सा</i> (sā́) <i>तद्</i> (tád), Old Church Slavonic <i>тъ</i> (tŭ), <i>та</i> (ta), <i>то</i> (to), and Old English <i>sē</i>, <i>sēo</i>, <i>þæt</i> (English <i>the</i>, <i>that</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wayfarer, traveler</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>in or on the way</li><li>that for which a merchant travels, merchandise</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way, road”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guide, helper, instructor, teacher; one who accompanies and shows the way</li><li>guider, leader, conductor, driver, pilot; one who drives or leads the way forward</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way, road, route”) +&lrm; <i>-ηγός</i> (-ēgós, “leader, guide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a guide-book</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁδοιπορῐκός</i> (hodoiporĭkós, “of or for a traveller”). As a noun, an ellipsis of <i>τὸ ὁδοιπορῐκὸν βῐβλῐ́ον</i> (tò hodoiporĭkòn bĭblĭ́on, “the travellers’ book”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for a traveller</li></ol><p><i>ὁδοίπορος</i> (hodoíporos, “wayfarer, traveller”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “of or pertaining to”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in a manner befitting a traveller</li></ol><p><i>ὁδοιπορῐκός</i> (hodoiporĭkós, “of or for a traveller”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watcher of the roads</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “street, road”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>road; path; way</li><li>travelling; journeying</li><li>journey; trip; expedition</li><li>the way, means, or manner to some end</li><li>manner of doing (something)</li><li>method</li><li>system</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) the Way (Christianity)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hodós</i>, possibly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sodós</i> (“course”), which is traditionally derived from <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”) and directly cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xodъ</i> (“motion; passage”), with more distant cognates including Sanskrit <i>सा॒द</i> (sādá, “a sitting; riding; motion”); however, this has been disputed. This would make it one of the oldest <i>-ός</i> (-ós) deverbals in Greek.</p><p>For the putative semantic development, compare English <i>set out</i> from the same root, as well as <i>road</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raidō</i> (“ride, journey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>methodically</li><li>systematically</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>road; path; way</li><li>travelling; journeying</li><li>journey; trip; expedition</li><li>the way, means, or manner to some end</li><li>manner of doing (something)</li><li>method</li><li>system</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) the Way (Christianity)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hodós</i>, possibly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sodós</i> (“course”), which is traditionally derived from <i>&#42;sed-</i> (“to sit”) and directly cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;xodъ</i> (“motion; passage”), with more distant cognates including Sanskrit <i>सा॒द</i> (sādá, “a sitting; riding; motion”); however, this has been disputed. This would make it one of the oldest <i>-ός</i> (-ós) deverbals in Greek.</p><p>For the putative semantic development, compare English <i>set out</i> from the same root, as well as <i>road</i> from Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;raidō</i> (“ride, journey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cargo ship, merchant ship</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) a gemstone</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “to drag”) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drawing to oneself, attractive</li><li>trailing</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>machine for hauling ships on land, probably a fixed capstan, windlass</li><li>furrow, track, trace</li><li>aqueduct, ditch, channel</li><li>kind of spider</li><li>wall barley (<i>Hordeum murinum</i>)</li></ol><p>An <i>-ός</i> (-ós) nominal from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;selk-</i> (“to pull, drag”), whence also <i>ὁλκή</i> (holkḗ, “dragging, drawing”), <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “to draw, drag”), Latin <i>sulcus</i> (“furrow”) and Old English <i>sulh</i> (“plough”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>socket of the hinge of a door</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) tooth socket</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) frustum of a cone</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλμος</i> (hólmos, “round, smooth stone”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the holothurium, a kind of zoophyte</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “whole”) + <i>θούριος</i> (thoúrios, “sexually aroused”), referring to phallic shape of sea cucumbers.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>all purple</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “whole”) +&lrm; <i>πορφύρα</i> (porphúra, “purple dye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ὁλοσηρῐκός</i> (holosērĭkós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of silk</li></ol><p><i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “entirely”) +&lrm; <i>σηρῐκός</i> (sērĭkós, “silken”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire, complete</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “whole”) + an element <i>&#42;σχερός</i> (&#42;skherós, “row”), the latter formed from a zero-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seǵʰ-</i> (“to hold, have”), + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs). See also <i>ἐπισχερώ</i> (episkherṓ, “in a row”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>completely, as a whole</li><li>in general</li></ol><p><i>ὁλοσχερής</i> (holoskherḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wholly burnt</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “whole”) +&lrm; <i>καυστός</i> (kaustós, “burnt”), from <i>καίω</i> (kaíō, “to burn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>complete in all parts, entire, perfect</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “whole”) +&lrm; <i>κλῆρος</i> (klêros, “a lot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a plantain of species <i>Plantago albicans</i>.</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “entire, whole”) +&lrm; <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>club-rush (<i>Schoenoplectus lacustris</i>), used in wickerwork</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “entire, whole”) +&lrm; <i>σχοῖνος</i> (skhoînos, “rush, reed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made or consisting of pure solid gold; entirely golden</li><li>covered in a golden exterior; decorated with a whole surface of goldware; gilded; gold-plated</li><li>(<i>of a fabric</i>) completely woven with golden thread, like a cloth of gold</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine,&#32;of a manuscript) heavily illuminated with gold leaf, like a Codex Aureus</li><li>(<i>of a person</i>) having a head full of blond hair; golden-haired; flaxen-haired; fair-haired; towheaded</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) possessing goldlike value; hence dear, precious, valuable, beloved</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “whole, entire, complete”) +&lrm; <i>χρῡσός</i> (khrūsós, “gold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the whole</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same”) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὀμίχλη</i> (omíkhlē)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to make a din, make aloud noise</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅμαδος</i> (hómados) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>defensive alliance, league</li></ol><p><i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “joint”) +&lrm; <i>αἰχμή</i> (aikhmḗ, “war”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a surface</i>) even, level</li><li>(<i>of a sediment</i>) uniform in consistency</li><li>(<i>of motion</i>) even, equable</li><li>(<i>of circumstances</i>) equal, on a level</li><li>not remarkable, middling, average, ordinary</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sem-h₂-lo-s</i>, from <i>&#42;sem-</i> (“together, one”); particularly similar cognates include Latin <i>similis</i> (“similar”) and Old Irish <i>samail</i> (“likeness, similarity”). The <i>ο-</i> grade of the word is often thought to have been imported by analogy from <i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “similar”). Armenian <i>ամոլ</i> (amol, “harnessed pair of cows”) is probably unrelated.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to act together, simultaneously</li><li>to accompany</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to walk beside, accompany</li></ol><li>to keep pace with</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to pursue, chase</li><li>to attend to, supervise</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμός</i> (homós) +&lrm; <i>ἀραρίσκω</i> (ararískō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) gathered together, assembled</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμός</i> (homós) +&lrm; <i>ἀγείρω</i> (ageírō) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sameness of age</li><li>(<i>collective</i>) one's age-mates, peers, friend group</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμῆλιξ</i> (homêlix, “of the same age”) +&lrm; <i>-ίη</i> (-íē), from <i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same”) + <i>ἧλιξ</i> (hêlix, “of the same age”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>critics of the poet Homer</li><li>censorious critics in general</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὁμηρομᾰ́στῐξ</i> (Homēromắstĭx, <i>Ὅμηρος</i> (Hómēros, “Homer”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́στῐξ</i> (mắstĭx, “scourge”), literally “Homer's scourge”), an epithet given to Zoilus, a Thracian grammarian who was a censorious critic of Homer.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be with, keep company</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) to talk, converse</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅμῑλος</i> (hómīlos, “crowd”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>intercourse, interaction, company</li><li>sexual intercourse</li><li>instruction, homily</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅμῑλος</i> (hómīlos, “a crowd or gathering of people”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>disciple, scholar</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) one who has experience and knowledge of something</li></ol><p><i>ὁμῑλέω</i> (homīléō, “to be with, keep company”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>affable, conversable</li></ol><p><i>ὁμῑλητής</i> (homīlētḗs, “disciple, scholar”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós, “adjectival suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>In like manner, similarly.</li></ol><p>Adverb from <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios, “like, similar”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>likeness, resemblance</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμοιόω</i> (homoióō, “to make like, to be like”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same race, family or kind</li></ol><p><i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same”) +&lrm; <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs, “of a ⸻ kind”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ending alike</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios, “similar”) +&lrm; <i>τελευτή</i> (teleutḗ, “ending”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of like or similar form</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios) +&lrm; <i>-μορφος</i> (-morphos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios)</li><li>superlative degree of <i>ὁμοῖος</i> (homoîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios)</li><li>comparative degree of <i>ὁμοῖος</i> (homoîos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>likeness, similarity</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios, “like, similar”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of like manners and life</li><li>similar</li></ol><p><i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios) +&lrm; <i>τρόπος</i> (trópos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to make similar, assimilate</li><li>to compare, liken</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios, “like, similar”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to call out together</li></ol><p><i>ὁμός</i> (homós) +&lrm; <i>καλέω</i> (kaléō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a joint call</li><li>harmony</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ὁμοκλέω</i> (homokléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to agree with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to correspond with, agree with</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to be coordinated</li><li>to be suitable for</li></ol><li>to agree to, grant, concede</li><li>to agree or promise to do</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>) to promise</li></ol><li>(<i>middle in active sense</i>)</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be agreed upon</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμόλογος</i> (homólogos, “in agreement”), from <i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same”) +&lrm; <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “reasoning”), +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, denominative verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>agreement, assent</li><li>admission, concession</li><li>compact (agreement, contract)</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) profession (declaration of faith, belief or opinion), confession (acknowledgment of belief)</li></ol><p><i>ὁμόλογος</i> (homólogos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for confessing</li></ol><p><i>ὁμολογέω</i> (homologéō, “agree, admit, concede, confess”) +&lrm; <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same essence, substance</li><li>consubstantial</li><li>(<i>Christianity</i>) a word of the Nicene Creed to describe the relationship between Jesus (God the Son) and God the Father</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμο-</i> (homo-, “same”) +&lrm; <i>οὐσίᾱ</i> (ousíā, “substance, essence”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with the same father</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;somHoph₂tōr</i>. Related to Tocharian A <i>somapācär</i>, Old Norse <i>samfeðra</i>, and Old Persian (hamapitar-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic, Ionic, and Old Attic form of <i>ὅμοιος</i> (hómoios)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>At the same place: together</li><li>Close at hand, nearby</li></ol><p>Genitive singular of <i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same”), used adverbially.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Written with the same letters</li><li>Identically worded</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same”) +&lrm; <i>γράφω</i> (gráphō, “to write”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from the same place, from the same source</li><li>from near, hand-to-hand</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμός</i> (homós) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in agreement, of one mind [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li><li>corresponding [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to’</small>]</li><li>agreeing, in unison [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμός</i> (homós) +&lrm; <i>-λογος</i> (-logos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>same, common, joint</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;homós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;somHós</i> (“same, alike”). Cognate with <i>εἷς</i> (heîs, “one”), as well as Old English <i>sama</i> (English <i>same</i>), Sanskrit <i>सम</i> (sama), Old Persian <i>𐏃𐎶</i> (hama), Old Church Slavonic <i>самъ</i> (samŭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) to one and the same place</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμός</i> (homós) +&lrm; <i>-σε</i> (-se)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same race, stock, breed, or kind; akin</li></ol><p><i>ὁμός</i> (homós) +&lrm; <i>φῦλον</i> (phûlon) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking the same language [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with, as’</small>]</li><li>of the same sound or tone, in unison [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘with’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμο-</i> (homo-) +&lrm; <i>-φωνος</i> (-phōnos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having the same name, homonymous</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>namesake, homonym</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same, common”) +&lrm; <i>ὄνυμα</i> (ónuma) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os), Doric/Aeolic variant of <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma, “name”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of the same age</li><li>of the same stature</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμός</i> (homós) +&lrm; <i>ἧλῐξ</i> (hêlĭx)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>together</li><li>equally, alike</li></ol><p><i>ὁμός</i> (homós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>however big or small, how big</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>at what point in time, at what hour, on what day</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>supposing (that)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hoof</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon, “tool, arms”).</p><p>Compare typologically the relation of Russian <i>копы́то</i> (kopýto), <i>копьё</i> (kopʹjó).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to arm (supply with armour or weapons)</li></ol><p><i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon, “tool, instrument”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who is armed, heavily armored</li><li>a heavily armored foot soldier, man-at-arms, who carried a spear (<i>δόρυ</i> (dóru)) and a large shield (<i>ἀσπίς</i> (aspís))</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon, “instrument of war; arms or armour”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>carrying the hoplites</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁπλῑ́της</i> (hoplī́tēs, “hoplite”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγός</i> (agōgós, “leading, guiding; drawing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an armoury</li><li>a shield case</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon, “armour”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who has the charge of arms, a weapon-keeper</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon, “tool, implement; weapon”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as many times as</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in which of two ways</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁπότερος</i> (hopóteros) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to whichever of two sides</li><li>in which of two ways</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁπότερος</i> (hopóteros, “which of two”) +&lrm; <i>-σε</i> (-se).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>of what sort or quality</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὁπόθεν</i> (hopóthen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὁππότε</i> (hoppóte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὁπότε</i> (hopóte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὁπότερος</i> (hopóteros, “which of two, either of two”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) when</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in any way whatever, in some way or other</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>ὅπως</i> (hópōs) +&lrm; <i>οὖν</i> (oûn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in indirect questions</i>) whence, from what place</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós, “who, which”) + <i>πόθεν</i> (póthen, “whence”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) synonym of <i>ὅποι</i> (hópoi, “whither”)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>as many as</li><li>as much as, as big as</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>as many as</li><li>as much as, as big as</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>in what or which place in numerical order</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὁπότε</i> (hopóte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>See <i>ὁπότε</i> (hopóte) and <i>ᾰ̓́ν</i> (ắn).</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>ὁπότ’ ᾰ̓́ν</i> (hopót’ ắn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>when (<i>mostly in reference to the past</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós, “who, which”) + <i>πότε</i> (póte, “when”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>whichever of two</li><li>(<i>indefinite</i>) either of two</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to look with the eyes [<i>with</i> <b>εἰς</b> (eis, + accusative) <small>‘at something or someone’</small>]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be able to see; (<i>with negative</i>) to be unable to see, to be blind</li><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to look a certain way [<i>with</i> accusative adjective <i>or</i> adverb]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>Infinitive is added to an adjective, adverb, and so on to indicate that the description relates to sight: to see; to look at; to behold</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to see; to perceive; to observe [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘someone’</small> <i>and</i> participle <small>‘doing something’</small>]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to find out [<i>with</i> indirect question]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make sure [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li><li>(ambitransitive,&#32;figurative) to see with the mind; to understand</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to provide [<i>with</i> accusative <small>‘something’</small> <i>and</i> dative <small>‘for someone’</small>]</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to watch, guard”). There may have been an intermediate form <i>&#42;ϝορᾰ́ω</i> (&#42;worắō), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;worā́ō</i>. If there was such a form with <i>ϝ-</i> (w-), then it is unclear why the augmented stem <i>ἑωρ-</i> (heōr-) occurs only in Attic, while <i>ὡρ-</i> (hōr-) is used in Homer and inscriptions.</p><p>Cognate with <i>οὖρος</i> (oûros, “watcher; guardian”), <i>ὤρᾱ</i> (ṓrā, “care; concern”), Latin <i>vereor</i> (“fear”), English <i>aware</i> (“vigilant; conscious”) and <i>wary</i> (“cautious of danger”).</p><p>Forms in <i>ὀψ-</i> (ops-), <i>ὀπ-</i> (op-) are from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i> (“to see”) (whence <i>ὄψ</i> (óps), <i>ὄμμᾰ</i> (ómmă)).</p><p>Forms in <i>εἰδ-</i> (eid-) are from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weyd-</i> (“to see”) (whence <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos), <i>ῐ̔́στωρ</i> (hĭ́stōr)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to divide, separate from</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to separate</li><li>to delimit</li><li>to bound</li><li>to pass between or through</li><li>to part, divide</li></ol><li>to mark out</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to trace as a boundary</li></ol><li>to ordain, determine, lay down</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to define a thing</li></ol><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to mark out for oneself</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to determine for oneself</li><li>to define a thing</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to border upon</li><li>(<i>Attic law</i>) to have marked with <i>ὅροι</i> (hóroi)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “boundary, border”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horizon</li></ol><p>Substantive noun from <i>ὁρίζων κύκλος</i> (horízōn kúklos, literally “separating circle”), from the present active participle of <i>ὁρίζω</i> (horízō, “to mark out a boundary”) and <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “circle, sphere”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>marking out by boundaries, limitation</li><li>(<i>lexicography</i>) definition</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁρίζω</i> (horízō) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>separable, definite</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁρίζω</i> (horízō, “separate, delimit”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, “a suffix that creates verbal adjectives of possibility, either active or passive”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>active voice</i>) to set in motion, urge on, inspire</li><li>(active voice,&#32;middle voice,&#32;passive voice) to start [<i>with</i> infinitive]</li><li>to rush, hasten [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> <b>ἐπί</b> (epí, + accusative) <i>or</i> <b>εἰς</b> (eis, + accusative) <small>‘at, against’</small>]</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁρμή</i> (hormḗ, “start, impetus, rush, attack, effort”) +&lrm; <i>-άω</i> (-áō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nautical</i>) to be moored, lie at anchor</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅρμος</i> (hórmos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>violent pressure, assault, force</li><li>first movements, beginning, start</li><li>eagerness, struggle, effort</li><li>violence, passion, appetite</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hοrmā́</i>, with two plausible derivations:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring to a safe anchorage, bring into harbour, to moor, anchor</li><li>to bring safe home</li><li>to put (<i>e.g. a child</i>) to sleep</li><li>(<i>middle voice&#32;and&#32;passive voice</i>) to come to anchor, lie at anchor, be anchored</li><li>(<i>middle voice&#32;and&#32;passive voice</i>) to be dependent</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅρμος</i> (hórmos, “anchorage, shelter”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō, verb-forming suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a gem ()</li></ol><p><i>ὁρμῐ́σκος</i> (hormĭ́skos, “small necklace”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small necklace</li><li>signetcord</li><li>collar</li></ol><p><i>ὅρμος</i> (hórmos, “cord, necklace, collar”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́σκος</i> (-ĭ́skos, suffix forming diminutive nouns)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to incite, stir up</li><li>to debate, ponder</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁρμή</i> (hormḗ, “eagerness, struggle, effort”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>string, chain or cluster of things hanging one from the other</li><li>bead or link of a chain</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅρμος</i> (hórmos, “cord, chain”), with a suffix found also in <i>γυργαθός</i> (gurgathós) and <i>κάλαθος</i> (kálathos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>harbour-master</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅρμος</i> (hórmos, “anchorage, haven”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curator of boundary-stones</li><li>frontier guard</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “boundary, limit”) +&lrm; <i>φύλαξ</i> (phúlax, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as many times as, as often as</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos) +&lrm; <i>-άκις</i> (-ákis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὁσαπλασίων</i> (hosaplasíōn)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos) +&lrm; <i>-πλάσιος</i> (-plásios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>as many times as</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos) +&lrm; <i>-πλάσιος</i> (-plásios) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>piety</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅσιος</i> (hósios) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) house, home</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>hospitium</i> (“lodgings”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>sometimes</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅτε</i> (hóte).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) omicron, the name for the fifteenth letter Ο (O), ο (o) of the Ancient Greek alphabet</li></ol><p>Shape from Phoenician <i>𐤏</i> (ʿ&nbsp;). Literally, “small o”, in order to distinguish it from omega <i>ω</i> (ō), which had become identical in pronunciation. Displaced earlier <i>οὖ</i> (oû).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Cretan and Lesbian form of <i>ὅ</i> (hó, relative pronoun)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the service tree (<i>Sorbus domestica</i>), whose fruit is the sorb apple</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;oiwa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eyHw-</i>.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὄᾱ</i> (óā)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sorb apple, fruit of the service tree</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ὄᾱ</i> (óā).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wife, spouse</li></ol><p>Looking at <i>ὀαρίζω</i> (oarízō, “to converse, chat with”), it is thought that this word originally meant "confidential intercourse", whence "confidential company, wife". A good etymology has not been found. According to Beekes, perhaps from Pre-Greek. Maybe from *h₁ós-r̥ "female", whence also feminine numeral *tisr-, according to Kim.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cubs of wild animals</li></ol><p>For <i>ὀβρίκαλα</i> (obríkala), compare animal names and hypocoristic formations like <i>ὀρτάλιχος</i> (ortálikhos) and <i>ὁσσίχος</i> (hossíkhos). Furnée assumes Pre-Greek origin, with interchange <i>ὀ/ἰ</i> between <i>ὀβρίκαλα</i> (obríkala) and <i>ἰβρίκαλοι</i> (ibríkaloi, “young pigs”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>strong, mighty, stout</li></ol><p>The word is traditionally compared with <i>βρῑ́μη</i> (brī́mē, “strength, might”), but the initial "ὀ-" yields serious problems. The prenasalization leads Beekes to suggest a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>assaying of gold</li></ol><p>Definite Pre-Greek origin. Has been compared to Hurrian <i>𒂁𒄷𒌋𒌒𒊒𒍑𒄭</i> (<sup>DUG</sup>ḫu-u-ub-ru-uš-ḫi /⁠ḫubrušḫi⁠/, “crucible, smelter, melting-pot”), likely via the route of Hittite <i>𒄷𒌒𒊒𒍑𒄭</i> (ḫu-up-ru-uš-ḫi /⁠ḫuprušḫi-⁠/). See Arabic إِبْرِيز (ʔibrīz, “pure gold”) for further borrowings/connections.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having come through a fire test</li><li>pure (especially when referring to gold)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄβρυζα</i> (óbruza, “assaying of gold”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). See <i>ὄβρυζα</i> (óbruza) for further etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pure gold</li></ol><p>From the neuter form of <i>ὄβρυζος</i> (óbruzos, “pure (esp. of gold)”, adjective), most likely from the term <i>ὄβρυζον χρυσίον</i> (óbruzon khrusíon, “pure piece of gold”). See <i>ὄβρυζα</i> (óbruza) for further etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having come through a fire test</li><li>pure (especially when referring to gold)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄβρυζα</i> (óbruza, “assaying of gold”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). See <i>ὄβρυζα</i> (óbruza) for further etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>eighth</li></ol><p>Ordinal of <i>ὀκτώ</i> (oktṓ, “eight”). The term derives from earlier <i>&#42;ὄγδοϝος</i> (&#42;ógdowos), itself perhaps from earlier Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;oḱtṓwos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;oḱtṓw</i>. The voicing is possibly due to analogy after <i>ἕβδομος</i> (hébdomos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hook</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄγκος</i> (ónkos, “bend”) +&lrm; <i>-ῑνος</i> (-īnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bend, curve, angle</li><li>barb, grapple, hook</li><li>bulk, volume, mass, heap, weight</li><li>tumor</li><li>molestation</li><li>dignity, importance, pride</li><li>particle, atom</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ónkos</i> (“bend”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ónkos</i> (“bend, hook”). Cognates include Latin <i>uncus</i> and Sanskrit <i>अङ्क</i> (aṅká).</p><p>Possibly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁onḱos</i> (“burden”), which may be an o-grade Schwebeablaut nominal formation from <i>&#42;h₁neḱ-</i> (“to take away”) (though such a Schwebeablaut would be rather strange). Under this derivation, often compared with Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;Hánćas</i> (“share, part, portion”).</p><p>It has also been claimed that all attestations can be understood from a meaning "swelling", from earlier "curvature", and are thus ultimately identical to the "bend, hook" sense of Etymology 1.</p><p>Compare with the semantics of Latin <i>mōlēs</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furrow in ploughing</li><li>swathe in reaping</li><li>strip of cultivated land</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) orbit of the moon and sun</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂óǵmos</i>, from the root <i>&#42;h₂eǵ-</i> (“to drive”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>अज्म</i> (ájma, “course, passage”). Related to the verb <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pear tree (<i>Pyrus communis</i>)</li></ol><p>Hypothetically connected with <i>ἔγχος</i> (énkhos, “spear”) if this lance was originally made of pear wood. Furnée connects the word with <i>κόγχη</i> (kónkhē, “mussel”) and thinks that the noun is from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) ozaena, a type of chronic rhinitis</li><li>fetid kind of octopus</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄζω</i> (ózō, “to smell”) +&lrm; <i>-αινᾰ</i> (-aină).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bough, branch, twig</li><li>knot or eye on a tree</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) offshoot, scion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ózdos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ósdos</i> (“branch, twig”). Cognate with Old Armenian <i>ոստ</i> (ost, “branch; knot (of tree)”), Old English <i>ōst</i> (“tree knot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to smell (i.e. exude a scent) [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) there is a smell</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;óďďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ed-ye-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃ed-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>odor</i> and Lithuanian <i>úosti</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὄᾱ</i> (óā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrinking, hesitation</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ḱenk-</i> (“to hang, tarry”), shared with Latin <i>cunctor</i> (“to delay, hold up”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>point, prominence</li><li>roughness</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ókris</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂óḱris</i> (“sharp edge; top;angle; corner”). Cognates include Latin <i>ocris</i> and Sanskrit <i>अश्रि॑</i> (áśri).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>happy, blessed, blest</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) rich, prosperous</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄλβος</i> (ólbos, “happiness, bliss”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wealth, prosperity, riches</li><li>happiness, bliss, fortune</li></ol><p>Furnée connects <i>ὄλπα</i> (ólpa, “kind of food”). According to Beekes, if the gloss is cognate, then it must be Pre-Greek. The linguist Michael Janda suggests that the term may derive from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;su(h₂)-ol-gʷh₂o-</i> (“that which goes towards the Sun”) and is perhaps related to Sanskrit <i>svargá</i>. This may indicate a possible Indo-European religious concept in which the Sun was associated with the afterlife.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ruin, destruction, death, bane</li><li>causing destruction: pest, plague</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>As a contemptuous name applied to a person.</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;óletʰros</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃elh₁dʰros</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃elh₁-</i> (“to destroy”). Cognate with <i>ὄλλῡμῐ</i> (óllūmĭ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of locust</li></ol><p>Connection with <i>λιγύς</i> (ligús, “shrill, loud, piercing”) does not help. In view of the prenasalization, the word may be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheaves of barley</li><li>(<i>in Cyprus</i>) flax</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, related to <i>λίνον</i> (línon, “flax”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dildo, leathern penis</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>ὀλισθάνω</i> (olisthánō, “to be slippery”). However, according to Beekes, it is rather a Pre-Greek word, in view of the suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wooden drinking bowl</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to destroy, make an end of</li><li>to lose</li><li>(middle voice,&#32;passive voice&#32;and&#32;perfect)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to perish; to come to an end</li><li>to be ruined; to be undone</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃elh₁-</i> (“destroy”). By surface analysis, <i>ὀλοός</i> (oloós, “destructive”) +&lrm; <i>-νῡμῐ</i> (-nūmĭ), with assimilation of <i>&#42;-λν-</i> (&#42;-ln-) to <i>-λλ-</i> (-ll-) as <i>&#42;όλ-νῠμι</i> (&#42;ól-nŭmi), though these were likely parallel derivations from some Proto-Hellenic etymon. Compare Latin <i>ab-oleō</i>, which may be related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leathern flask for oil</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a derivative of <i>ἔλπος</i> (élpos, “olive oil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fruit of the wild fig</li></ol><p>A technical Pre-Greek word, with suffix in <i>-νθ-</i>.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὄλυνθος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olynthus</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄλυνθος</i> (ólunthos, “wild fig fruit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A kind of grain, probably spelt</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin, compare likely cognate Old Armenian <i>ոլոռն</i> (oloṙn); for more see Arabic خُلَّر (ḵullar).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storm of rain, thunderstorm, sent by Zeus</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) water, as an element</li><li>inundation, flood, deluge</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) storm, shower</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected to Sanskrit <i>अभ्र</i> (abhrá), Latin <i>imber</i>, and Old Armenian <i>ամբ</i> (amb), but formal derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥bʰrós</i> presents several problems. <i>&#42;n̥, &#42;bʰ</i> would be expected to yield <i>ᾰ, φ</i> (ă, ph), although Miller's law would explain this. The accent would likely be on the ultima. Possibly related to Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀃𐀖𐀪𐀍</i> (o-mi-ri-jo), 𐀃𐀖𐀪𐀍𐀂 (o-mi-ri-jo-i, dat. pl.). Szemerényi assumes a loanword, so it is possibly a Pre-Greek word. Compare Latin <i>Umbri</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>urine</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀμείχω</i> (omeíkhō, “to make water”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) eye</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the eye of heaven; i.e. the sun</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) light</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) that which brings light</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) anything dear or precious, as the apple of an eye</li></ol><li>the face or human form</li><li>an eye-hole in a helmet</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ókʷmə</i> (“look, glance”), from the radical &#42;ὀπ- (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i> (“eye”)) + <i>-μα</i> (-ma), with regressive assimilation (compare the Aeolic form, with progressive assimilation).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to swear</li><li>to swear a thing, affirm or confirm by oath</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>followed by accusative</i>)</li><li>(<i>followed by infinitive</i>) swear that one will...</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>with accusative of person or thing sworn by</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃emh₃-</i> (“to swear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>nourishment</li><li>wheat</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ep-</i>, like Sanskrit <i>अप्नस्</i> (apnas, “property, possession”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aflą</i> (“power, strength”) and Hittite <i>𒄩𒀀𒀊𒈦</i> (ḫa-a-ap-pár). However, Furnée links this word with <i>ὀμφῡ́νω</i> (omphū́nō, “to strengthen, respect, honor”), suggesting a by-form "ὀμφυν-" and thus a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>unripe grape, also of olives</li><li>young girl, not yet ripe for marriage</li><li>unripe hard breast of a young girl</li><li>kind of gem, used for seals</li></ol><p>It could derive from <i>&#42;h₃nebʰ-</i> (“navel”), but the semantic explanation as a navel-like knob is hardly convincing. Furnée connects <i>ἀμφίας</i> (amphías, “bad Sicilian wine”) and <i>ἀμφής</i> (amphḗs, “wine blossom”). The variation, as well as the suffix <i>-αξ</i>, would point to Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reality</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>onager, wild ass</li><li>kind of catapult</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃en-r</i> (“dream”); cognate with Old Armenian <i>անուրջ</i> (anurǰ) and Albanian <i>ëndërr</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>boon, advantage</li><li>relief, refreshment</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) victuals, viands, provisions</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) goods, treasures</li></ol><p>From a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃neh₂-</i> (“to enjoy”); compare <i>ὀνίνημι</i> (onínēmi, “to profit, benefit”) and Old English <i>unnan</i> (“to grant, bestow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blame, reproach, criticism</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;oneidos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃neyd-os</i> (“blame, revilement”); compare Gothic <i>𐌽𐌴𐌹𐌸</i> (neiþ), Sanskrit <i>निन्दा</i> (nindā́), Latvian <i>naids</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὄνειρος</i> (óneiros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dream</li><li>anything which is dreamlike, unreal, fleeting</li></ol><p>Derived from the root of <i>ὄναρ</i> (ónar) with a suffix <i>*-yos</i>. Cognate with Old Armenian <i>անուրջ</i> (anurǰ) and Albanian <i>ëndërr</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dung of animals</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Has been compared with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;adalaz</i> (“manure”), but this is highly speculative.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὄνομα</i> (ónoma)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>name</li><li>fame (compare English <i>make a name for oneself</i>)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) noun, in the wide sense&#58; referring to most word classes that are declined for case and number – a substantive (English noun), an adjective, or a pronoun – but excluding the relative pronoun <i>ὅς</i> (hós) and the article <i>ὁ</i> (ho)</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) phrase</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;h₁nómn̥</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ónomə</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁nómn̥</i> (“name”). Cognate with Phrygian <i>ονομαν</i> (onoman), Latin <i>nōmen</i>, Old Irish <i>ainm</i>, Old English <i>nama</i> (English <i>name</i>), Old Church Slavonic <i>имѧ</i> (imę), Old Armenian <i>անուն</i> (anun), Persian نام (nām), Sanskrit <i>नामन्</i> (nā́man).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to blame, scold; to find fault with, treat scornfully, attack verbally, vituperate, insult</li><li>to curse, throw a slur upon; to slander</li><li>to quarrel with, impugn</li></ol><p>Likely from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃enh₂-</i> (“to onerate, charge, burden; (<i>perhaps</i>) to name”); cognate with Hittite (ḫannanzi, “to judge, charge, sue”). According to Beekes, <i>οὔνεσθε</i> (oúnesthe) and the forms in <i>ὀνα-</i> (ona-) are secondary derivations, not based on the root <i>ὀνο-</i> (ono-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ass, donkey; jack, jackass (<i>male</i>); jenny, jennyass (<i>female</i>)</li><li>hake (fish)</li><li>woodlouse</li><li>wingless locust</li><li>windlass</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Perhaps from Pre-Greek. Compare the discussion at Latin <i>asinus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stone bugloss, hairy golden-drop (<i>Onosma echioides</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄνος</i> (ónos, “donkey, ass”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>things which actually exist, opposed to past and future</li><li>reality, truth, actual objects</li><li>that which one has, property, fortune</li></ol><p>Neuter plural of the present participle of <i>εἰμί</i> (eimí, “to be”), thus literally ‘things that are; existing things’.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reality</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Aeolic form of <i>ὄνομᾰ</i> (ónomă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>claw; nail; hoof; talon</li><li>anything which resembles a claw or nail&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>scraping tool</li><li>onyx (gemstone)</li><li>a kind of aromatic substance</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃negʰ-</i> (“nail”). Cognates include Latin <i>unguis</i>, Old Irish <i>ingen</i>, Sanskrit <i>न॒ख</i> (nakhá, “claw; nail”), Old Armenian <i>եղունգն</i> (ełungn), Persian ناخن (nâxon), Old Church Slavonic <i>ногъть</i> (nogŭtĭ), Lithuanian <i>nãgas</i>, Albanian <i>nyell</i>, and Old English <i>næġl</i> (English <i>nail</i>).</p><p>Beekes supposes that the gemstone sense is perhaps due to the gem's nail-like white glaze, though does not rule out a foreign borrowing reshaped by folk etymology.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>restharrow (<i>Ononis spinosa</i>)</li></ol><p>Furnée argues for a Pre-Greek origin, given the variation α/ο.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poor wine</li><li>vinegar made therefrom</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) sour fellow, sourpuss</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀξύς</i> (oxús, “sharp; sour”), with an analogous formation as <i>ἦδος</i> (êdos, “delight, pleasure”) to <i>ἡδύς</i> (hēdús, “pleasant, sweet”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the sorb apple, fruit of the service tree</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ὄᾱ</i> (óā).</p><p><i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὄπα</i> (ópa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>awl</li></ol><p>From the radical &#42;ὀπ- (from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i>), the same root of <i>ὀπή</i> (opḗ, “hole, opening”). The word would properly mean "hole-making tool", rather than "provided with an eye". The nominative would then yield <i>&#42;ὄπεαρ</i> (&#42;ópear), replaced by the current form. The alternative form remains difficult to explain; folk etymology was assumed and dialectal origin may also be considered.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὄπῐσθεν</i> (ópĭsthen, “behind; backwards; in the future”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὄπῐσθεν</i> (ópĭsthen, “behind; backwards; in the future”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>poppy juice, opium</li></ol><p><i>ὀπός</i> (opós, “plant juice”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vengeance of the gods for transgressing divine laws</li><li>care or favour of the gods</li><li>veneration, obedience, religious awe</li><li>pious care, zeal</li></ol><p>The connection with <i>ὄψομαι</i> (ópsomai), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i> (“to see”), can hardly be rejected. This implies an older meaning "sight, look, glance", whence the meanings "awe, veneration" and "vengeance, punishment".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὄπῐσθεν</i> (ópĭsthen, “behind; backwards; in the future”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>behind; at the back</li><li>backwards</li><li>in the future, after, hereafter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>yet to come</li></ol></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>behind; at the back</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀπῐ́σω</i> (opĭ́sō) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Aeolic</i>) alternative form of <i>ὄμμᾰ</i> (ómmă), with progressive assimilation</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>οὐρᾰνός</i> (ourănós, “sky”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>instrument, implement, tool</li><li>sense organ</li><li>musical instrument</li><li>the material of a work (e.g. timber)</li><li>the product of a work (e.g. lumber)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werǵ-</i> (“work”), formed similarly to <i>ξόᾰνον</i> (xóănon, “statue, xoanon”); compare <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon, “work, deed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>secret rites, secret worship, practiced by the initiated</li><li>rites, sacrifices in general</li></ol><p>Probably from the same root as <i>ὀργή</i> (orgḗ) and <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fathom</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a zero-grade form related to <i>ὀρέγω</i> (orégō, “I stretch”). However, according to Beekes, this explanation meets with semantic and formal difficulties; therefore, he prefers a Pre-Greek etymology, comparing the suffix <i>-υια</i> to words from the substrate, like <i>ἅρπυια</i> (hárpuia) and <i>ἄγυια</i> (águia)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yearning, longing, desire, appetite</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρέγω</i> (orégō, “I stretch”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>straight, steep, uphill; upright, standing</li><li>(of voice, sound) high-pitched, shrill</li><li>(military) formed in column (opp. in line or extended front)</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) right angle</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The time immediately before or around sunrise: dawn, early morning twilight</li><li>The Greek name of the morning service in the Eastern Orthodox Church, often called by its Latin name, Matins, by Orthodox clergy within Western cultures.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃r̥dʰ-ro-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃erdʰ-</i> (“to rise”). Cognate with <i>ὀρθός</i> (orthós), Latin <i>ortus</i> (“star's rise”), <i>oriens</i> (“sunrise”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large kind of tunny</li></ol><p>Unexplained loanword. Probably Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix and meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) shoot, sprout or stem, stalk (especially of cabbage and asparagus)</li></ol><p>Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i> (“to move, stir, spring”), the same root of <i>ὄρνυμι</i> (órnumi, “to awaken, arouse”) and <i>ἔρνος</i> (érnos, “shoot, sprout”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a bird</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) the bird-market</li></ol><p>From some derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃érō</i>, whence also <i>ὄρνις</i> (órnis). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>որոր</i> (oror), Hittite <i>𒄩𒀀𒊏𒀸</i> (ḫāras), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;orьlъ</i>, and Old English <i>earn</i> (English <i>erne</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bird</li><li>chicken</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;órn-īk-s ~ &#42;órn-īkʷʰ-</i>, according to Beekes, from a feminine derivative ending in <i>*-ih₂</i> of the oblique stem <i>h₃er-n-</i> of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃érō</i>. The alternative <i>ὄρνῑξ</i> (órnīx) is a back-formation from the dative plural <i>ὄρνῑξῐ</i> (órnīxĭ) of the alternate stem <i>ὄρνῑχ-</i> (órnīkh-). See also <i>ὄρνεον</i> (órneon) from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to set upon, let loose upon, move on</li><li>to awaken, arouse</li><li>to raise, excite</li><li>to stir up, encourage, exhort, cheer on</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;órnūmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃er-</i> (“to move, stir, spring”).</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>ऋणोति</i> (ṛṇóti), Latin <i>orior</i> and Albanian <i>orë</i>. Compare also Old Armenian <i>յառնեմ</i> (yaṙnem). The Epic athematic middle aorist form <i>ὦρτο</i> (ôrto) matches exactly with Sanskrit <i>आर्त</i> (ārta), the equivalent form of <i>ऋणोति</i> (ṛṇóti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὄρνυσθε</i> (órnusthe)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὄρνυτο</i> (órnuto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bitter vetch (<i>Vicia ervilia</i>).</li></ol><p>Probably a Mediterranean substrate borrowing, and likely related to <i>ἐρέβινθος</i> (erébinthos, “chickpea”). Compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;arwīts</i>, Latin <i>ervum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mountain; hill</li><li>mountain chain</li><li>district; sector; precinct; parish</li><li>(<i>Egyptian</i>) desert</li></ol><p>Either from <i>ὄρνυμι</i> (órnumi, “I raise”), or possibly directly from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃eros</i>; either way, derived from the root <i>&#42;h₃er-</i> (“to rise, spring, stir”).</p><p>Compare Sanskrit <i>ऋ॒ष्व</i> (ṛṣvá). Possibly related are the personal names <i>Ὀρειάς</i> (Oreiás), Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀃𐀩𐁀</i> (o-re-a2, “Oreās”), and <i>Ὀρέστης</i> (Oréstēs), Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀃𐀩𐀲</i> (o-re-ta, “Orestās”).</p><p>The sense "desert" is a semantic loan from Demotic Egyptian <i>tw</i> (“mountain, desert”), from Egyptian <i>ḏw</i> (“mountain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reed used for thatching houses</li></ol><p>From an o-grade of <i>ἐρέφω</i> (eréphō, “to cover with a roof”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ἑρπετόν</i> (herpetón)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) sapling, sprig, young shoot</li><li>goad, lance, anything made with saplings</li><li>scion, descendant</li></ol><p>The formation is like <i>σκώληξ</i> (skṓlēx), <i>κάχληξ</i> (kákhlēx) and <i>νάρθηξ</i> (nárthēx). The comparison with Lithuanian <i>várpa</i> (“ear of corn”) is not very illuminating. Commonly derived from the root of Latin <i>sarpō</i> (“to trim”), Latin <i>sarmentum</i> (“osiers”) and <i>ἅρπη</i> (hárpē, “sickle”); Beekes, on the grounds that an o-grade of this root is not attested elsewhere, claims that it is a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>vulgar</i>) butt, ass</li><li>Megarian form of <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁órsos</i> (“bottom, arse”). Cognates include Middle Armenian <i>ոռ</i> (oṙ), Old Irish <i>err</i>, Old English <i>ærs</i> (English <i>arse</i>, <i>ass</i>), and perhaps Hittite <i>𒅈𒊏𒀸</i> (arraš). See also <i>οὐρᾱ́</i> (ourā́, “tail”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>common quail (<i>Coturnix coturnix</i>)</li><li>harefoot plantain (<i>Plantago lagopus</i>)</li></ol><p>Presumably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wort-</i>, whence also Sanskrit <i>वर्ति॑का</i> (vártikā), <i>वर्तक</i> (vartaka), but this does not account for the alternation of the ending <i>-ῠγος</i> (-ŭgos) ~ <i>-ῠκος</i> (-ŭkos), which Beekes considers characteristically Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>trench, ditch, moat</li><li>tunnel, mine, excavation</li><li>(<i>at Athens</i>) pit into which condemned criminals were thrown</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀρῠ́σσω</i> (orŭ́ssō, “to dig”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rice (<i>Oryza sativa</i>)</li></ol><p>A borrowing from an Eastern Iranian language, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;wrinǰiš</i> or <i>&#42;wriHǰíš</i>; compare Pashto وريژې&nbsp;pl (wriže). See the Iranian forms listed at Old Persian <i>&#42;vrinjiš</i>, especially those without the nasal infix, for more on the etymon.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὄρυζᾰ</i> (óruză, “rice”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pickaxe or other sharp iron digging tool</li><li>an oryx</li><li>a kind of whale, perhaps a narwhal</li></ol><p>A back-formation related to <i>ὀρῠ́σσω</i> (orŭ́ssō, “to dig; to scrape”).</p><p>The sense of oryx is likely a substrate loanword accreted by folk-etymological analogy with the animal's pointed horns. However, compare typologically the relation of Russian <i>копы́то</i> (kopýto), <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ), <i>копьё</i> (kopʹjó). Also compare <i>ὁπλή</i> (hoplḗ), <i>ὅπλον</i> (hóplon) relation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>darkness of night, night</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>darkness of the nether world</li></ol></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;orkʷʰnā</i>, a secondary <i>o</i>-grade of <i>&#42;erkʷʰnos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rgʷ-sno-</i>, from <i>&#42;h₁régʷos</i> (“darkness”) (whence <i>Ἔρεβος</i> (Érebos)).</p><p>Alternatively from the same root as:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of a brownish grey colour, a mix of black, red and white</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρφνη</i> (órphnē, “darkness”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) chief, leader</li></ol><p>From the <i>o</i>-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ergʰ-</i> (“to begin, rule, command”), the root of <i>ἄρχω</i> (árkhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(masculine,&#32;often plural) testicle</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) ovary</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) orchid</li><li>(<i>feminine</i>) a type of olive</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁órǵʰis</i> (“testicle”). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>որձիք</i> (orjikʻ, “idem”), Avestan <i>𐬆𐬭𐬆𐬰𐬌</i> (ər<sup><small>ə</small></sup>zi), Hittite (arkiš), Albanian <i>herdhe</i>, and Irish <i>uirghe</i>.</p><p>The "orchid" sense is likely for the characteristically oblong shape of the plant's roots.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>row of vines or fruit trees</li></ol><p>Traditionally connected to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werǵʰ-</i> (“bind, wind together”) and its descendants, like Lithuanian <i>veržiù</i> (“to fence in, string”), Old Norse <i>virgill</i> (“snare”) and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wurgijaną</i> (“to strangle”). However, this is semantically untenable, because the meaning of this word would be "fence, enclosure". The connection with <i>Ὀρχομενός</i> (Orkhomenós) is commonly accepted. As there is no Indo-European etymology, it seems more probable that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>ὄζω</i> (ózō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;óďďō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃éd-ye-ti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃ed-</i> (“smell”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>osyris (<i>Osyris alba</i>)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὄσιρις)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Osiris (the Egyptian god of the dead and of the underworld)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Q1&#58;ir</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./d035306b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A40" alt="glyph" height="90"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i></div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) pulse, legume (of all kinds)</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Connections with <i>σπεῖρον</i> (speîron, “wrapping cover”) and <i>σπείρω</i> (speírō, “to sow”) have been suggested, but this is impossible because "ὀ" is not a prothetic vowel. The word is more likely a substrate borrowing, perhaps from the same source as Old Armenian <i>ոսպն</i> (ospn, “lentil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rumour, which, from its origin being unknown, was held divine</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) voice, of the Muses&#59; also, of a bull</li><li>(<i>more generally</i>) sound, of the lyre; din of battle</li><li>(<i>rare in prose</i>) ominous voice or sound, prophecy, warning</li></ol><p>From the Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;woťťa</i>, the root of <i>&#42;wókʷs</i> (= <i>ὄψ</i> (óps); from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wekʷ-</i>, whence Latin <i>vox</i>) + the nominal feminine suffix <i>&#42;-ya</i> (= <i>-ια</i> (-ia)).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὄσσα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Ossa; Mount Kissavos</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eyes</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;óťťe</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ókʷih₁</i>, dual of <i>&#42;h₃ókʷs</i> (“eye”), root noun of <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i> (“to see, eye”). Cognates include Latin <i>oculus</i> (“eye”), Sanskrit <i>अक्षि</i> (ákṣi), Old Armenian <i>ակն</i> (akn), <i>աչք</i> (ačʻkʻ), Gothic <i>𐌰𐌿𐌲𐍉</i> (augō), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;oko</i>, Old English <i>ēage</i> (English <i>eye</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to see with the mind's eye</li><li>to presage, have a foreboding of</li><li>to bode, forebode (<i>something evil</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;óťťomai</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ékʷ-ye&#47;o-</i> (“to see”); cognate with <i>ὄψομαι</i> (ópsomai, “I will see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>curled hair, lock of hair</li><li>tendril of vine</li><li>curling flame</li><li>arm of a cuttlefish, tentacle</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>θῶμιγξ</i> (thôminx), <i>ἶλιγξ</i> (îlinx) and <i>στρόφιγξ</i> (stróphinx). The presence of the variant shows that the word is from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earthen vessel</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fragment of such a vessel, potsherd</li><li>earthen castanet</li></ol><li>the hard shell of animals such as mollusks and turtles</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>mother of pearl</li><li>eggshell</li></ol></ol><p>Traditionally held to be related to <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”) and <i>ὄστρεον</i> (óstreon, “oyster”). Beekes considers it more likely to be Pre-Greek, which does not exclude a relation to <i>ὄστρεον</i> (óstreon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bivalve</li><li>oyster</li><li>purple pigment, probably that produced by murex</li></ol><p>Traditionally assumed to be related to <i>ὀστέον</i> (ostéon, “bone”), though Beekes rejects this connection due to lack of other evidence for an Indo-European pre-form <i>&#42;ostr-</i> (as well as the word category of seafood being generally unlikely to be of Indo-European origin), and instead takes the word as Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>byre, stable, fold, enclosure for cattle</li></ol><p>Improbable hypothesis by Bezzenberger, who reconstructs <i>&#42;h₂odʰ-tro-</i>, comparing Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;edaraz</i> (“enclosure, shelter”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;òdrъ</i> (“bed”): both the Greek suffixation and the formation in <i>*-tro-</i> remain unexplained. According to Frisk, this word belongs to <i>ὄστρακον</i> (óstrakon, “earthen vessel”) and <i>ὄστρεον</i> (óstreon, “oyster”), assuming a prior meaning "protecting roof", for which he gives a parallel in Latin <i>testudō</i> (“tortoise; protecting roof”). Since there is no Indo-European etymology, the word may be Pre-Greek, although there appears no further indication for this.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) scrotum</li></ol><p>Explained by Frisk as metaphorical use of <i>ὄσχος</i> (óskhos, “vine-branch”). According to Beekes, the word may well be of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) raised margin of the womb</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄσχη</i> (óskhē, “scrotum”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suffering, distress, burden</li></ol><p>Relatable to <i>ἔτλην</i> (étlēn, “to suffer, undergo”), if the initial <i>o-</i> is either a prefix or conditioned development. Unsatisfied with this derivation, Beekes declares this word to be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any loud noise, as the din of battle</li><li>rattling of chariots</li><li>crash of thunder</li></ol><p>Onomatopoeic with a suffix like in <i>θόρυβος</i> (thórubos) and <i>κόναβος</i> (kónabos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Attic form of <i>ὄσσᾰ</i> (óssă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>increase, advantage</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀφέλλω</i> (ophéllō, “increase”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>if only, I wish that</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to owe</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><li>(<i>law</i>) to incur</li><li>(passive voice,&#32;of persons) to be due or liable to</li></ol><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) to be bound, obligated, to ought, to should, to have to</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>) it behooves</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;opʰéľľō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃bʰélyeti</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃bʰel-</i> (whence <i>ὄφελος</i> (óphelos)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>furtherance, advantage, help, good, benefit</li><li>the best</li><li>(<i>governs the dative</i>) help against something</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;opʰelos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃bʰel-</i> (whence also <i>ὀφείλω</i> (opheílō)). Cognates include Old Armenian <i>աւելի</i> (aweli, “more”), <i>յաւելում</i> (yawelum, “I add”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>serpent; snake</li><li>serpentine bracelet</li><li>a type of creeping plant</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ókʷʰis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ógʷʰis</i> (“snake”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>अहि॑</i> (áhi), Avestan <i>𐬀𐬲𐬌</i> (aži), Old Armenian <i>իժ</i> (iž), and English <i>ask</i> (“eft; newt; lizard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὄφρα</i> (óphra)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>in order that (<i>with subjunctive or optative</i>)</li><li>as long as, while (with imperfect, or subjunctive and <i>ἄν</i> (án) or <i>κε</i> (ke))</li><li>until (with aorist indicative of past time, aorist subjunctive of future time, aorist optative of future in past)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὄχα</i> (ókha)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) by far</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a back-formation from <i>ἔξοχα</i> (éxokha), an adverbially used accusative plural (of <i>ἔξοχος</i> (éxokhos, “prominent, eminent”)) meaning the same.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bar or band fastened crosswise on the under side of the shield, through which the bearer passed his arm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;seǵʰ-</i>, the same root of <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold, bear”) and <i>ὄχος</i> (ókhos, “carriage, cart”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;in the plural) a chariot</li></ol><p>Byform of <i>ὄχος</i> (ókhos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weǵʰ-</i> (“to move, drive”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything that bears or supports, prop, stay</li><li>carriage, chariot, especially the mule-car</li><li>animal which is ridden, like a horse</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) vehicle, raft</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀχέω</i> (okhéō, “to hold; endure”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bank, riverbank, dyke by the side of a river (either natural or artificial)</li><li>high and rocky edge by the sea</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Seems to display a suffix like in <i>μόχθος</i> (mókhthos) and <i>βρόχθος</i> (brókhthos), but the connection with <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to hold”) is semantically unconvincing. Furnée compares <i>ἀκτή</i> (aktḗ, “promontory, edge”), which would point to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>purple stripe down the front of the chiton</li><li>tuft neckband</li></ol><p>Technical word with a suffix <i>-β-</i> like in <i>κόρυμβος</i> (kórumbos) and <i>κόλλαβος</i> (kóllabos). Connection with <i>ὄχθη</i> (ókhthē, “high edge”) seems possible, but compare also <i>οἶβος</i> (oîbos, “peace of meat from the back of an ox's neck”). According to Beekes, undoubtedly a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>multitude of people, crowd, mob</li><li>masses, multitude of commoners (as opposed to the rulers)</li><li>riot, tumult, disturbance, trouble</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;woǵʰlos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weǵʰ-</i> (“to drive, transport”) (whence also <i>ὄχος</i> (ókhos) and Arcadocypriot Greek <i>ϝέχω</i> (wékhō, “to bear, bring”)), with cognates such as Old Norse <i>vagl</i>. The sense development from "driving, carrying" > "crowd" is unclear; possible interpretations include that crowds were seen as "emergent entities that carried power", or that carriers of goods attracted crowds. Compare also Old Armenian <i>յոգն</i> (yogn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything which holds, bears</li><li>carriage, cart, chariot</li><li>the wheels of a chariot</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wókʰos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wóǵʰos</i>, from <i>&#42;weǵʰ-</i> (“to move, drive”). Cognates include Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀺𐀏</i> (wo-ka /⁠wokʰa⁠/, “vehicle”), Sanskrit <i>वाह</i> (vāhá), Old Church Slavonic <i>возъ</i> (vozŭ). Also see <i>ὀχέω</i> (okhéō, “to carry”) and Arcadocypriot Greek <i>ϝέχω</i> (wékhō, “to carry, bear”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>voice</li><li>word</li><li>eye; face</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wókʷs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wṓkʷs</i> (“voice”). Related to <i>ἔπος</i> (épos) and <i>εἰπεῖν</i> (eipeîn). Cognates include Latin <i>vōx</i>, Sanskrit <i>वाच्</i> (vā́c), and Tocharian A <i>wak</i>.</p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ókʷs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ókʷs</i> (“eye”). See also <i>ὤψ</i> (ṓps).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic&#32;or&#32;Koine</i>) late</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀψέ</i> (opsé) +&lrm; <i>-ῐμος</i> (-ĭmos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>view</li><li>sight</li><li>face; countenance</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄψ</i> (óps, “eye”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs). Diachronically from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ókʷtis</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ékʷtis</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i> (“eye, to see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>delicacies, considered an integral component of an Ancient Greek meal; anything cooked/boiled and eaten with bread or sauce or as relish</li></ol><p>Nominal derivative from an unattested <i>&#42;ὄψ</i> (&#42;óps), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁op-s</i>, a derivative of <i>&#42;h₁epi</i> (“on, near”); delicacies were considered side-dishes and were eaten on top of bread and such. See also <i>ὀψέ</i> (opsé, “late, long after”), Latin <i>obs-</i> (“towards, against”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ovine (pertaining to sheep)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>rare&#32;in&#32;Attic;&#32;chiefly&#32;Epic&#32;and&#32;Ionic</i>) alternative form of <i>οἶς</i> (oîs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Ionic,&#32;poetic&#32;Attic) who; which; that</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative) this</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) who, which, that</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;with article</i>) his; hers; its</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) his or her people, friends, family etc.</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) his or her possessions</li></ol></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) his own; her own; its own</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;Hyós</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;yós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyós</i>, <i>&#42;Hyéh₂</i>, <i>&#42;Hyód</i> (“who, which”), from the relative stem <i>&#42;Hyo-</i>, possibly from the anaphoric stem <i>&#42;(h₁)i-, &#42;(h₁)e-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>यद्</i> (yás, yā́, yád), Avestan <i>𐬫𐬋</i> (yō), and Phrygian <i>ιος</i> (ios /⁠jos⁠/). See also <i>οἷος</i> (hoîos), <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos). Compare <i>ἑός</i> (heós).</p><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Like <i>ἕ</i> (hé, “him”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> (reflexive pronoun). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्व</i> (svá). See also <i>ἑός</i> (heós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὅδε</i> (hóde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(demonstrative pronoun,&#32;proximal) this</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) here</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with verbs of action</i>) here; (<i>possibly</i>) there, yonder</li><li>(<i>modifying a personal pronoun</i>)</li><li>(with <i>τίς</i>)</li><li>(<i>adds precision to adverbs of place and time</i>), just, very</li><li>(<i>in Attic dialogue, the masculine and feminine pronouns often refer to the speaker</i>)</li><li>(<i>in Aristotle, neuter designates some particular thing</i>)</li></ol><li>(of time,&#32;to indicate the immediate present)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>this present</li><li>these</li><li>(<i>elliptic with genitive</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>to indicate something before one</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>to indicate something immediately to come</i>) the following</li><li>(<i>followed by a relative pronoun</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>adverbial</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>τῇδε</i>)</li><li>(accusative neuter,&#32;τόδε) hither, to this spot</li><li>(dative neuter plural,&#32;τοῖσδε, τοισίδε) in or with these words</li></ol></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὁ</i> (ho) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to a way or journey</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁδός</i> (hodós, “way, road”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὅτε</i> (hóte) (<i>used before a rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) whence, from where, from which</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) wherefore, for which reason</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós, “who”) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then, “from”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) where</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós, “who”) +&lrm; <i>-θῐ</i> (-thĭ, locative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ὅπως</i> (hópōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>capable of being drawn out, ductile, sticky</li><li>(<i>of a cupping instrument</i>) drawing well</li><li>(<i>of a kind of pain in liver diseases</i>) dragging</li></ol><p>From <i>ἕλκω</i> (hélkō, “to drag”) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>round, smooth stone</li><li>any cylindrical or bowl shaped body:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>mortar</li><li>kneading trough</li><li>hollow seat on which the Pythia prophesied</li><li>drinking vessel</li><li>(<i>music</i>) mouthpiece of a flute</li><li>dial</li><li>stone used as a weight</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;welH-</i> (“to turn, wind”). Further related to <i>εἰλέω</i> (eiléō, “to turn, roll”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>whole, entire, perfect, complete</li><li>absolute, utter</li><li>generally, on the whole</li><li>(<i>with negative</i>): at all</li><li>actually</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;solh₂wós</i>, from <i>&#42;solh₂-</i> (“whole”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>सर्व</i> (sárva), Avestan <i>𐬵𐬀𐬎𐬭𐬬𐬀</i> (haurva), Latin <i>salvus</i>, <i>sollus</i>. Unrelated false cognate of English <i>whole</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>wholly, completely</li><li>altogether</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅλος</i> (hólos, “whole”) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, “a suffix creating adverbs from adjective stems”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>noise, din, especially of the voices of men</li><li>throng or mob of warriors</li><li>din of battle</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ἄραδος</i> (árados), <i>κέλαδος</i> (kélados) and <i>χρόμαδος</i> (khrómados). Usually connected with Sanskrit <i>समद्</i> (samad, “strife, battle”). The word could belong to <i>ὁμάζω</i> (homázō, “to growl”) and thus of onomatopoeic origin, although DELG rejects this comparison because of the semantics.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pledge; surety; hostage</li></ol><p>Probably a compound of <i>ὁμοῦ</i> (homoû, “together”) + the <i>ᾰ̓ρ-</i> (ăr-) found in <i>ᾰ̓ρᾰρῐ́σκω</i> (ărărĭ́skō, “to fasten; to join”), i.e. "joined together" > "one who is forced to accompany" > "pledge, hostage". For a similar sense development, albeit from different lexical roots, compare Latin <i>obses</i> (“pledge; security; hostage”). See also <i>ὁμᾰρτέω</i> (homărtéō), <i>ὅμᾰδος</i> (hómădos).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὅμηρος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homer</li></ol><p>Apparently from <i>ὅμηρος</i> (hómēros, “hostage”); possibly an early nickname.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crowd, throng</li><li>tumult (of battle)</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same, joint”) +&lrm; <i>ῑ̓́λη</i> (ī́lē, “crowd”), the latter related to <i>εἴλω</i> (eílō, “to aggregate”). However, Beekes is skeptical and prefers to take the word as Pre-Greek, due to the presence of interchange <i>-ιλ-ο-</i>/<i>-ιλλ-ο-</i> in variants such as <i>ὅμιλλος</i> (hómillos), <i>ὁμιλλει</i> (homillei), as well as the word's phonetic structure and to some extent semantics resembling that of <i>ἅμιλλα</i> (hámilla, “competition”), which is probably also Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>like, resembling [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘someone, something’</small>], similar [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone, something’</small>]</li><li>shared, common, mutual</li><li>suited to, appropriate for</li><li>equal</li><li>the same</li></ol><p><i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>bordering, neighboring</li></ol><p><i>ὁμός</i> (homós, “same”) +&lrm; <i>ὅρος</i> (hóros, “border”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>nevertheless, yet, all the same</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁμῶς</i> (homôs, “together; equally, alike”), with stress shift.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>to this</li><li>to whom, to which</li></ol><p><i>ὅν</i> (hón) +&lrm; <i>-δε</i> (-de)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>ὅσπερ</i> (hósper) in the masculine nominative singular</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>wherefore</li><li>although</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in and of itself, per se</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the very man who, the very thing which</li><li>used after adjectives of resemblance</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós) + <i>περ</i> (per).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>tool, instrument</li><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) a ship's tackle, rope</li><li>(<i>often&#32;in the plural</i>) instruments of war: arms, armour, weapon</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) place of arms, camp</li></ol><li>(<i>humorous</i>) penis</li><li>a gymnastics exercise</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sop-lo-</i> (“instrument”), an o-grade <i>l</i>-suffixed formation from <i>&#42;sep-</i> (“to care for, honor, prepare”); see the related <i>ἕπω</i> (hépō, “I am busy”) for cognates. Not related to <i>ἕπομαι</i> (hépomai, “I follow”).</p><p>For the relation with <i>ὁπλή</i> (hoplḗ), compare typologically Russian <i>копьё</i> (kopʹjó), <i>копы́то</i> (kopýto), <i>копа́ть</i> (kopátʹ). See also <i>ὄρυξ</i> (órux).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>to which place; whither</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅ</i> (hó) +&lrm; <i>ποι</i> (poi)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>where</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in indirect questions</i>)</li><li>(<i>extended senses</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of time or occasion</i>)</li><li>(<i>of manner</i>) by which, that</li><li>(<i>of cause</i>) whereas</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>Koine&#32;demonstrative adverb</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷos</i> +&lrm; <i>-ου</i> (-ou).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅπως</i> (hópōs)</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅπως</i> (hópōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅπῃ</i> (hópēi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(relative to <i>ὥς</i> (hṓs) or <i>οὕτως</i> (hoútōs)) in such a manner, as</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with the ordinary regimen of the relative adverb</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with indicative</i>)</li><li>(<i>with the future indicative</i>) in what manner, how</li></ol><li>(with <i>ἄν</i> (án) and subjective in indefinite sentences) in whatever way, just as, however</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with optative after historical tenses</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>οὐκ ἔστιν</i>) there is no way in which, it cannot be that</li><li>(in Attic poets,&#32;like <i>ὡς</i> (hōs) in comparisons)</li><li>(also like <i>ὡς</i> (hōs) or <i>ὅτι</i> (hóti),&#32;with superlative of adverbs)</li><li>(<i>with a genitive added</i>)</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) when</li></ol><li>(<i>used to introduce the statement of a fact</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>οὐχ ὅπως...ἀλλά</i>) is not only not..but..</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(rare,&#32;following another clause)</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>in indirect questions</i>) how, in what way or manner</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with indicative</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of past tenses</i>)</li><li>(<i>future</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>with subjunctive</i>)</li><li>(<i>with optative</i>)</li><li>(with optative and <i>ἄν</i> (án),&#32;expressing a wish)</li><li>(<i>ὅπως ἄν with the subjunctive makes the manner or purpose indefinite</i>)</li><li>(Xenophon occasionally uses ὅπως somewhat like <i>ὥστε</i> (hṓste), with infinitive)</li><li>(<i>after verbs of fear and caution</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with future indicative</i>)</li><li>(<i>with aorist subjunctive</i>)</li><li>(<i>with optative representing subjunctive after an historical tense</i>)</li></ol><li>(this construction is most frequent in an imperative and prohibitive,&#32;in the original construction some verb implying caution or circumspection precedes)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with future indicative</i>)</li><li>(<i>aorist subjunctive</i>)</li></ol><li>(used as the echo to a preceding <i>πῶς</i> (pôs))</li><li>(<i>with a genitive of manner</i>)</li></ol></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>that, in order that</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with subjunctive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>after principal tenses, or after subjunctive or imperative</i>)</li><li>(<i>after historical tenses, when there is no perfect form, or when the aorist represents the perfect</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>with optative after historical tenses</i>)</li><li>(<i>with indicative</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(of historical tenses,&#32;to express a consequence which has not followed, or cannot follow)</li><li>(<i>of future</i>)</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>used of will and endeavour, instead of the infinitive</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós, “who, which”) + <i>πῶς</i> (pôs, “how”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in which or whichever direction</li><li>in which or whichever way, how</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is seen, a visible object, a sight</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a sight, spectacle</li><li>(<i>Koine</i>) a sensory experience, such as a dream, not necessarily restricted to sight</li></ol><li>an object of speculation, a speculation</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō, “to see”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seeing, sight</li><li>a vision</li><li>appearance</li></ol><p>From <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oath</li><li>pledge, surety</li><li>treaty</li><li>victim sacrificed to mark the settlement of an oath or treaty</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sworn, under oath</li></ol><p><i>ὅρκος</i> (hórkos) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the object by which one swears, the witness of an oath, as the Styx was among the gods, or as Zeus was among mortals</li><li>an oath</li></ol><p>Traditionally taken as an o-grade nominal formation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;serḱ-</i> (“to mend, fence”), whence also <i>ἕρκος</i> (hérkos, “fence”), i.e. "the bounds that one assumes" > "obligation, oath".</p><p>However, this has been disputed, with dissenting theories treating the word as related instead to Latin <i>surculus</i> (“twig”), via a hypothetical earlier form <i>&#42;sorcus</i>, <i>&#42;surcus</i>, from which the Greek would have borrowed the Latin. Under this theory, the Greek would thus refer to a "wooden staff raised when swearing an oath". This appears more semantically tenuous, however, in addition to the Latin term being of uncertain origin.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὅρκος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Horkos, a divinity who punishes the false and perjured</li></ol><p>Personification of <i>ὅρκος</i> (hórkos, “object by which one swears an oath”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rapid motion</li><li>eagerness</li></ol><p><i>ὁρμᾰ́ω</i> (hormắō, “set in motion, urge on, inspire, rush, hasten”, future stem: <i>ὁρμησ-</i>, <i>hormēs-</i>) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, suffix forming nouns of action)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orval (<i>Salvia viridis</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is like <i>σέλινον</i> (sélinon) and <i>κύμινον</i> (kúminon). It has originally been connected with <i>ὅρμος</i> (hórmos) and <i>ὁρμή</i> (hormḗ), but Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a cord, a chain, especially a necklace, a collar</li><li>a wreath, a chaplet (anything strung like a necklace)</li><li>(<i>dance</i>) a kind of dance performed in a ring</li><li>(<i>nautical</i>) a roadstead, an anchorage, the inner part of a harbour</li><li>a haven, a shelter</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sór-mos</i> (compare Old Armenian <i>որմ</i> (orm, “wall, fence”)), from <i>&#42;ser-</i> (“to bind”). Cognate with Ancient Greek <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “to tie, fasten”), Latin <i>serō</i> (“to bind”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;sarwą</i> (“armour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>also&#32;geometry</i>) boundary, limit, frontier, landmark</li><li>marking stones, stones used for inscribing legal contracts</li><li>the broad wooden piece serving as the upper part of an oil/wine press</li><li>rule, standard</li><li>term, definition</li><li>goal, end, aim</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wórwos</i> (whence Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀺𐀺</i> (wo-wo)), perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werw-</i> (“to draw, mark out”), with cognates including Latin <i>urvō</i> (“to trace a boundary”). See also <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to draw”), which might be related.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative) this</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) who, which, that</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;with article</i>) his; hers; its</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) his or her people, friends, family etc.</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) his or her possessions</li></ol></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) his own; her own; its own</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;Hyós</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;yós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyós</i>, <i>&#42;Hyéh₂</i>, <i>&#42;Hyód</i> (“who, which”), from the relative stem <i>&#42;Hyo-</i>, possibly from the anaphoric stem <i>&#42;(h₁)i-, &#42;(h₁)e-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>यद्</i> (yás, yā́, yád), Avestan <i>𐬫𐬋</i> (yō), and Phrygian <i>ιος</i> (ios /⁠jos⁠/). See also <i>οἷος</i> (hoîos), <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos). Compare <i>ἑός</i> (heós).</p><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Like <i>ἕ</i> (hé, “him”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> (reflexive pronoun). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्व</i> (svá). See also <i>ἑός</i> (heós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὅσα</i> (hósa)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὅσα</i> (hósa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as far as</li><li>synonym of <i>ὡς</i> (hōs)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) often as anaphor to <i>τόσος</i>, or <i>πᾶς</i>, <i>ἅπας</i> as much as, how much</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in plural the noun may be in nominative or partitive genitive</i>)</li><li>(in Attic,&#32;of time)</li><li>(with <i>τις</i>)</li><li>(<i>with accusative absolute</i>)</li><li>(<i>with adjectives expressing quantity</i>)</li><li>(<i>with superlative</i>)</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) so much as is enough for</li></ol><li>(<i>for ὅτι τοσοῦτος</i>)</li><li>(<i>followed by particles</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ὅσος ἄν</i>) how ever great</li><li>(<i>ὅσος δή</i>) how great; how ever many</li><li>(<i>ὁσοσοῦν</i>) ever so small</li><li>(<i>ὅσοσπερ</i>) even so great as; no greater than</li></ol><li>(ὅσῳ, ὅσῳ περ,&#32;often with comparative) by how much</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with comparative,&#32;when followed by another comparative with τοσούτῳ) the more... so much the more..</li><li>(<i>ἐν ὅσῳ</i>) while</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;yótsos</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;Hyótyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyoti</i>, adverb from <i>&#42;Hyós</i>, whence <i>ὅς</i> (hós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pious, hallowed, sanctioned by the gods</li><li>of things which are allowed by divine law but not sacred: profane</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) pious, devout, sinless</li><li>sinless, pure</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) title of five special priests at Delphi</li><li>(biblical,&#32;in the plural) the saints</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin.</p><p>Often explained as a derivative in <i>-ιο-</i> from a participal form <i>&#42;s-o-to-s</i>, beside alleged <i>&#42;seto-s</i> in <i>ἐτάζω</i> (etázō, “to examine, test”), although this account presents difficulties. A connection with Sanskrit <i>सत्य</i> (satyá, “true”) requires loss of the initial laryngeal.</p><p>The derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;soto-s</i> (from a different root <i>&#42;set-</i> (“stable, true”), seen in <i>ἐτάζω</i> (etázō) and <i>ἐτεός</i> (eteós)) is defended by Pinault, and appears to be tentatively favored by Beekes over the above.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>so far as, so much as</li><li>how far, how much</li><li>only so far as, only just</li><li>(<i>in reference to distances</i>) about, nearly</li><li>(with adjectives of quality or degree,&#32;mostly with comparative)</li><li>(<i>with negatives</i>) just not, all but</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) often as anaphor to <i>τόσος</i>, or <i>πᾶς</i>, <i>ἅπας</i> as much as, how much</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in plural the noun may be in nominative or partitive genitive</i>)</li><li>(in Attic,&#32;of time)</li><li>(with <i>τις</i>)</li><li>(<i>with accusative absolute</i>)</li><li>(<i>with adjectives expressing quantity</i>)</li><li>(<i>with superlative</i>)</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) so much as is enough for</li></ol><li>(<i>for ὅτι τοσοῦτος</i>)</li><li>(<i>followed by particles</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ὅσος ἄν</i>) how ever great</li><li>(<i>ὅσος δή</i>) how great; how ever many</li><li>(<i>ὁσοσοῦν</i>) ever so small</li><li>(<i>ὅσοσπερ</i>) even so great as; no greater than</li></ol><li>(ὅσῳ, ὅσῳ περ,&#32;often with comparative) by how much</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with comparative,&#32;when followed by another comparative with τοσούτῳ) the more... so much the more..</li><li>(<i>ἐν ὅσῳ</i>) while</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;yótsos</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;Hyótyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyoti</i>, adverb from <i>&#42;Hyós</i>, whence <i>ὅς</i> (hós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>relative</i>) often as anaphor to <i>τόσος</i>, or <i>πᾶς</i>, <i>ἅπας</i> as much as, how much</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in plural the noun may be in nominative or partitive genitive</i>)</li><li>(in Attic,&#32;of time)</li><li>(with <i>τις</i>)</li><li>(<i>with accusative absolute</i>)</li><li>(<i>with adjectives expressing quantity</i>)</li><li>(<i>with superlative</i>)</li><li>(<i>with infinitive</i>) so much as is enough for</li></ol><li>(<i>for ὅτι τοσοῦτος</i>)</li><li>(<i>followed by particles</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>ὅσος ἄν</i>) how ever great</li><li>(<i>ὅσος δή</i>) how great; how ever many</li><li>(<i>ὁσοσοῦν</i>) ever so small</li><li>(<i>ὅσοσπερ</i>) even so great as; no greater than</li></ol><li>(ὅσῳ, ὅσῳ περ,&#32;often with comparative) by how much</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with comparative,&#32;when followed by another comparative with τοσούτῳ) the more... so much the more..</li><li>(<i>ἐν ὅσῳ</i>) while</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;yótsos</i>, from earlier <i>&#42;Hyótyos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyoti</i>, adverb from <i>&#42;Hyós</i>, whence <i>ὅς</i> (hós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>the very man who, the very thing which</li><li>used after adjectives of resemblance</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós) + <i>περ</i> (per).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὅσσα</i> (hóssa)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὅσσα</i> (hóssa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅσα</i> (hósa)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅσον</i> (hóson)</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>indefinite relative pronoun: whoever, whichever; anyone who, anything which; someone who, something which</li><li>indirect interrogative pronoun corresponding to direct interrogative <i>τίς</i> (tís), <i>τί</i> (tí): who, what, which</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>replaces <i>τίς</i> (tís), <i>τί</i> (tí) when someone repeats a question asked by another person</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós, “who, which”, relative pronoun) +&lrm; <i>τῐς</i> (tĭs, “someone, anyone”, indefinite pronoun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὅτε</i> (hóte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(with a conditional force,&#32;about events likely to recur) whenever; when</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>ὅτ’</i> (hót’, “when”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ν</i> (ắn, modal particle).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of time</i>) when</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(with indicative,&#32;to denote single events) when</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(with present,&#32;of a thing now happening)</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;with future</i>)</li></ol><li>(with optative,&#32;to denote repeated events or actions in past time)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(of future events which are represented as uncertain,&#32;in clauses dependent on a verb in optative or subjunctive)</li><li>(only in Epic and Lyric poets,&#32;with subjunctive)</li></ol><li>(in Homer,&#32;to introduce a simile)</li><li>(<i>with other particles</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>causal sense</i>) whereas</li><li>(<i>absolute</i>) sometimes, now and then</li></ol><p>From <i>ὅς</i> (hós) +&lrm; <i>τε</i> (te). Compare <i>τότε</i> (tóte), <i>πότε</i> (póte), <i>ποτέ</i> (poté).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>even when</li></ol><p><i>ὅτε</i> (hóte) +&lrm; <i>περ</i> (per)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>subordinating conjunction</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>after verbs of perception and emotion, introducing a noun clause expressing a fact: that (<i>with the same mood as the corresponding independent clause</i>)</li><li>after verbs of perception, emotion, saying, or hearing, introducing an indirect statement: that (<i>with indicative or optative</i>)</li><li>introducing a causal clause expressing a reason: because, seeing that</li></ol><li>(<i>with a superlative</i>) as much as possible</li></ol><p>Originally the neuter accusative form of the indefinite relative pronoun <i>ὅστις</i> (hóstis, “whomever”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) alternative form of <i>ὅστῐς</i> (hóstĭs) in the masculine nominative singular</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὅτι</i> (hóti)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oasis-dwellers (in the Libyan Desert)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὄᾰσῐς</i> (Óăsĭs) +&lrm; <i>-ῑ́της</i> (-ī́tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orbisene (an ancient province of the kingdom of Armenia (Greater Armenia), mentioned by Ptolemy)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Onchestus, Boeotia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Odessus (an ancient settlement in Moesia; now Varna, Bulgaria)</li></ol><p>Unknown, possibly Thracian or Dacian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Thracian tribe</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Thracian <i>&#42;Udrusai</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Odrysae; an Odrysian</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Ὀδυσσεύς</i> (Odusseús) (<i>with second syllable shortened for metrical reasons</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ὀδῠ́σσειᾰ</i> (Odŭ́sseiă, “the Odyssey”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Odysseus</li></ol><p>According to M. L. West, (Aeolic-)Ionian epics and dependent traditions have δ-forms while in all mainland Greece and most colonies only the λ-forms are attested. It is unclear which ones are older, and it is thought that the connection of the name with the Trojan War probably is secondary. Kretschmer believed that δ-forms are secondary and due to a paronymic attraction to a verb discussed below, while Solmsen, on the contrary, proposed that λ-forms actually originate in dissimilation of dentals.</p><p>The etymology of the name is contested. Ancient authors (beginning with the <i>Odyssey</i> (19.406 sqq.) link it to the verb <i>ὀδύσσομαι</i> (odússomai, “to hate”), which is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ed-</i> (“to hate”), but a non-Greek or even a non-Indo-European origin seems more likely. Beekes tentatively reconstructed the Pre-Greek source as *Od/luk<sup>y</sup>eu.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Flavius Odoacer, the first king of Italy</li></ol><p>Likely of Germanic origin; see also the etymology at Latin <i>Odoacer</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Odomanti, a Thracian tribe settled upon the whole Mount Orbelus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Odyssey (an Ancient Greek epic poem, ascribed to Homer, which depicts the journey of Odysseus back to Ithaca after the fall of Troy)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὀδῠσσεύς</i>&nbsp;m (Odŭsseús, “Odysseus”) +&lrm; <i>-ιᾰ</i> (-iă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ozolae, inhabitants of Ozolian Locris</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Othronos, an island or city of Sicily</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Octavia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Octāvia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Octavian</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Octāviānus</i>, from Octāvius, Octāvia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Octavius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Octāvius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) of October</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Octōber</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oligyrtus, Arcadia, Greece</li><li>mount Oligyrtus, Peloponnese, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olbia, Scythia, Ukraine</li><li>Olbia, Sardinia</li><li>Olbia, Gaul, France</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄλβος</i> (ólbos, “happiness, bliss”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olizon, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oloosson, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olympia</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὄλυμπος</i> (Ólumpos, “Olympus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Olympiad</li><li>a female given name, Olympias</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὀλυμπῐ́ᾱ</i> (Olumpĭ́ā, “Olympia”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Olympic (of or pertaining to Mount Olympus)</li></ol><p><i>Ὄλυμπος</i> (Ólumpos) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Olympiodorus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὄλυμπος</i> (Ólumpos, “Olympus”) +&lrm; <i>δῶρον</i> (dôron, “gift”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olondae, a tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olophyxus, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the ancient Olympic Games</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὀλυμπῐ́ᾱ</i> (Olumpĭ́ā, “Olympia”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Olympian</li></ol><p><i>Ὄλῠμπος</i> (Ólŭmpos, “Olympus”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Olympian</li></ol><p><i>Ὄλῠμπος</i> (Ólŭmpos, “Olympus”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Umbria, Italy</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Umbrica</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Umbri people; an Umbrian</li><li>an inhabitant of Umbria; an Umbrian</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Umbricus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Omphale, a woman in Greek mythology</li><li>Omphale, the previous name of Daedalium, Sicily</li></ol><p>Most likely from <i>ὀμφαλός</i> (omphalós, “navel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Omphalium, Epirus, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀμφαλός</i> (omphalós, “navel; boss of a shield”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Onesicritus</li></ol><p><i>ὄνησις</i> (ónēsis) +&lrm; <i>κριτός</i> (kritós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Onthyrium, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Onuphrius</li></ol><p>From Coptic <i>ⲃⲉⲛⲟϥⲉⲣ</i> (benofer), from Egyptian <i>wnn-nfr</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Oxyartes, from Old Bactrian, a Bactrian nobleman and father of Roxana</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Bactrian <i>&#42;Huxšaθra-</i> (literally “Of good reign”), composed of <i>&#42;hu-</i> (“good”) + <i>&#42;xšaθra-</i> (“reign, kingdom”). Doublet of <i>Ὀξῠᾰ́θρης</i> (Oxŭắthrēs), which is from Old Persian.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Opuntian (person from Opus)</li></ol><p><i>Ὀποῦς</i> (Opoûs) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Opus, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀπός</i> (opós, “juice”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Opone; Hafun, Somalia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Oresbius, a Boeotian slain by Hector</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orestes</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain”) +<i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “stand”) + <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, “proper name suffix”), literally, "one who stands on a mountain".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orbelus, a mountain range of Macedonia now called Pirin</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orbisene (an ancient region of Lesser Armenia in modern-day Turkey, mentioned by Ptolemy)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Ordessus; Argeș (river), Romania</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>oread</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρος</i> (óros, “mountain”) +&lrm; <i>-άς</i> (-ás)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orestes, an ancient Greek tragedy play</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὀρέστης</i> (Oréstēs, “Orestes”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orinaei, an ancient tribe of Sarmatia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Italian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Italian <i>Orlando</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ormenium, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Orneae</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὀρνεαί</i> (Orneaí, “Orneae”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orneae</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὀρνεύς</i> (Orneús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Oroetes, from Old Persian, satrap of Lydia</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian, possibly derived from <i>&#42;arvāh</i> (“swift”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Orontobates, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>son-in-law of Pixodarus and last Carian dynast from the Hecatomnids' dynasty</li><li>a co-leader along with Ariobarzanes and Otanes of the Erythrean soldiers at the Battle of Gaugamela</li><li>a satrap of Media under Antigonus I Monophthalmus</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Arvantapātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a satrap of Pontus, father of Mithridates I</li><li>son-in-law of Pixodarus and last Carian dynast from the Hecatomnids' dynasty</li></ol></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>&#42;Arvantapātah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orosangae, a Persian word for the benefactors or bodyguards of the king</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;varusanha</i> (literally “far-famed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Arabian mythology</i>) A god of uncertain identity, likely Ruḍaw.</li></ol><p>From an ancient form of Arabic; if referring to the god Ruḍaw, then from رُضَى (ruḍā) or a similar form, perhaps with the definite article اَل (al-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orsilochus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ortygia, a small island in Syracuse</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄρτυξ</i> (órtux, “quail”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orpheus</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but a link to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃órbʰos</i> has been proposed, suggesting a common origin with Sanskrit <i>अर्भ</i> (árbha), Latin <i>orbus</i> (“orphaned”), Old High German <i>erbi</i>, <i>arbi</i> (German <i>Erbe</i> (“heir”)), and Old English <i>ierfa</i> (“heir”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Orphic: of or pertaining to Orphism</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὀρφεύς</i> (Orpheús) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>m a male given name, Orchomenus</li><li>f Orchomenus, the name of multiple places</li></ol><p><i>ὄρχος</i> (órkhos) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Uruk</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Akkadian <i>𒌷𒀕</i> (/⁠Uruk⁠/), from Sumerian <i>𒀕</i> (Unug).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orobiae, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orontes (river)</li><li>Orontes (name of any one of a number of ancient Armenian kings)</li></ol><p>From Akkadian <i>𒀀𒊏𒀭𒌓</i> (Arāntu, “Orontes; site of the Battle of Qarqar”) and also in Egyptian <i>jrnt</i> (Arantu/Araunti, “Orontes; river flowing by Qadesh”) attested at least from the period of Ramesses II:</p><p>More at Orontes.</p><p>From Old Median <i>&#42;Arēvand</i>, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;Raivant-</i>, <i>&#42;Rayivant-</i> (“possessing wealth”); see Old Armenian <i>Երուանդ</i> (Eruand) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Osarseph</li></ol><p>Uncertain, ostensibly Egyptian origin. Vermaseren suggests a borrowing of the theophoric Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">Q1&#58;ir</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./d035306b.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A40" alt="glyph" height="90"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">z&#58;p</td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./97539f73.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="pA" alt="glyph" height="85"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="A" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./d587337a.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="L5" alt="glyph" height="32"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (<i>wsjr-zpꜣ</i>, “Osiris-Sepa”), however his veracity is questionable.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Ostanes, from Old Median, a Persian ruler</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;ʰUštāna</i> (literally “With a good status/rank”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ramesses II, pharaoh of the Nineteenth Dynasty of Egypt</li></ol><p>From Egyptian Demotic Egyptian <i>wsr-mꜣꜥ.t-rꜥ</i>, <i>smn-rꜥ</i>, <i>ns-mn-rꜥ</i>, from Egyptian <i>wsr-mꜣꜥt-rꜥ</i>, the first part of the throne name of pharaoh Ramesses II.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Otanes, from Old Persian, the name of various Persian individuals</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>𐎢𐎫𐎠𐎴</i> (u-t-a-n /⁠ʰUtānaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Otryne, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Opheltes</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ophrynium, Troad; Erenköy, Turkey</li></ol><p>From <i>ὀφρύς</i> (ophrús, “brow of a hill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river of Pontus mentioned in the Periplus of Pseudo-Scylax</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>Οἰλεύς</i> (Oileús, “Oileus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;Greek mythology) son of Oileus: patronym of Ajax the Lesser</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὀῑ̈λεύς</i> (Oī̈leús) +&lrm; <i>-άδης</i> (-ádēs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>an epithet of Zoilus&#58; a Thracian grammarian who was a censorious critic of Homer</li></ol><p>Compound of <i>Ὅμηρος</i> (Hómēros, “Homer”) +&lrm; <i>μᾰ́στῐξ</i> (mắstĭx, “scourge”), literally “Homer's scourge”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homole, a mountain in Thessaly</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Honorius</li></ol><p>Borrowing from Latin <i>Honōrius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hordeonius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Hordeonius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hortensius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Hortēnsius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Oarus, Scythia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>any one of the cities in the Libyan Desert</li></ol><p>From Demotic Egyptian <i>wḥj</i>, earlier hieroglyphic <table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./ba1f03ff.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="G43" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./f50bd458.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="V28" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./6e565214.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="G1" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./76993ec3.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="X1" alt="glyph" height="27"/><br /><img src="./a14a7564.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="N25" alt="glyph" height="40"/></td>
</tr></table>
</td></tr></table> <i>wḥꜣt</i> (also meaning “cauldron”). Compare Sahidic Coptic <i>ⲟⲩⲁϩⲉ</i> (ouahe), and Arabic واحة (wāḥa).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>mount Othrys, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol><p>According to Mahlow, it stands for <i>ὀφρῦς</i> (ophrûs, “brow; ridge of a hill”), with variation <i>φ/θ</i>. According to Beekes, the word is undoubtedly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Otho; Otto</li></ol><p>Compare Latin <i>Otho</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olbasa, Cilicia, Turkey</li><li>Olbasa, Lycaonia, Turkey</li><li>Olbasa, Pisidia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Olorus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olurus, Achaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olpae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olympus (a mountain in Greece)</li><li>A mountain in Mysia, now called Uludağ</li></ol><p>Probably cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀄𐀬𐀠𐀊𐀍</i> (u-ru-pi-ja-jo, description of men, possibly ethnical), which may indicate original /u/ in the anlaut.</p><p>According to Beekes, Pre-Greek. Suspecting originally an appellative word for “mountain” ολύ- (olú-), he tentatively identified the suffix <i>&#42;-ump-</i>, and the proto-form <i>&#42;Ulump-</i>.</p><p>According to Janda, a compound of the PIE roots <i>&#42;wel-</i> (“to turn, wind; to enclose, wrap, encase”) and <i>&#42;pah₂-</i> (“to protect, herd”), more precisely from the heteroclitic <i>&#42;wéluṛ</i> ~ <i>&#42;welun-</i> (“wrapping”), which directly gave Ancient Greek <i>εἶλαρ</i> (eîlar, “covering, shelter, defence, bulwark”), <i>ὄλυνος</i> (ólunos, “the rubbed off and discarded in the course of cleaning”), <i>ὄλυνθος</i> (ólunthos, “unripe fig”) and <i>ὄλυρα</i> (ólura, “amelcorn”). Further cognates include <i>ἔλυμος</i> (élumos, “millet; sheath, case, etui”), <i>ἔλυτρον</i> (élutron, “cover, case, sheath”), Albanian <i>valle</i> (“a kind of circular dance”), Russian <i>вал</i> (val, “billow, roller; rampart”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;waluz</i> (“staff, stick”), Sanskrit <i>वरुण</i> (varuṇa, “ocean, sun, the gods”) and <i>ऊर्मि</i> (ūrmi, “wave, billow”), Latin <i>vallus</i> (“stake, pale; palisade”) and <i>volvo</i> (“to roll”). For more see <i>εἰλύω</i> (eilúō, “to wrap, enfold”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Mount Oneum</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Oxylus, a one-eyed man from Aetolia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oppius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Oppius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hormuz (island in the Strait of Hormuz)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orthe, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Osca; Huesca, Aragon, Spain</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Osca</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oscius, a river of Thrace mentioned by Thucydides</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ostaphus, a city of Thrace mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Holmi, Cilicia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Homilae, Thessaly, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>The alpine squill (<i>Scilla bifolia</i>)</li><li>The larkspur (<i>Consolida ajacis</i>, syn. <i>Delphinium ajacis</i>)</li><li>A certain type of blue</li><li>Huákinthos, “dark red, purple”, again see Latin <i>vaccinium</i></li><li>A blue precious stone, perhaps aquamarine</li></ol><p>Of Pre-Greek or other Mediterranean substrate origin and possibly related to Latin <i>vaccinium</i>. Note the Pre-Greek suffix <i>-ινθ-</i> (-inth-).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὑάκινθος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyacinthus</li></ol><p>See <i>ῠ̔ᾰ́κινθος</i> (hŭắkinthos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of crystal, of glass</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕαλος</i> (húalos, “crystal; glass”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hyacinthine, blue</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑάκινθος</i> (huákinthos, “hyacinth”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be like glass</li></ol><p><i>ὕαλος</i> (húalos) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>glassworker</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕᾰλος</i> (húălos, “glass”) +&lrm; <i>-ουργός</i> (-ourgós, “worker”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative letter-case form of <i>Ὑβλαῖος</i> (Hublaîos)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὑβλαῖος)</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Relating to Hybla</li><li>Hyblaean</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὕβλᾱ</i> (Húblā, “Hybla”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, “belonging to”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be wanton; to run riot</li><li>to insult; to affront; to maltreat</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́βρῐς</i> (hŭ́brĭs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Eurasian eagle owl (<i>Bubo bubo</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Given to wantonness, insolent, violent, outrageous</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑβρίζω</i> (hubrízō, “to act violently”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós), from <i>ὕβρις</i> (húbris, “violence”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>humpbacked, hunchbacked</li></ol><p>The element <i>-β-</i> is reminiscent of words like <i>στραβός</i> (strabós), <i>κλαμβός</i> (klambós) and other words related to corporeal defects. A convincing etymology has not been found. Petersson compares Lithuanian <i>subine</i> (“hindmost, back, bottom”). According to Beekes, the word may well be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>health</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑγιής</i> (hugiḗs, “healthy”) +&lrm; <i>-ια</i> (-ia, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sound; healthy; hearty; strong</li><li>sound in mind:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>wise</li><li>wholesome</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂yu-gʷih₃-</i> (“long life”) or Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁su-gʷih₃-</i> (“good life”) with <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs, “adjective suffix”); the former derivation is favored by Beekes and Weiss. In that case, cognate with Latin <i>iūgis</i>, Avestan <i>𐬫𐬀𐬎𐬎𐬀𐬈𐬘𐬍</i> (yauuaejī), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;aiwukiz</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be healthy, sound</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑγιής</i> (hugiḗs, “healthy, sound”) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>good for the health, wholesome</li><li>sound, healthy</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) health</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑγιής</i> (hugiḗs) +&lrm; <i>-εινός</i> (-einós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moisture, humidity</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑγρός</i> (hugrós, “wet, moist, flexible”) +&lrm; <i>-ασίᾱ</i> (-asíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wet, moist</li><li>fluid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ugrós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wegʷ-</i> (“wet, moist”). Cognate with Latin <i>ūvidus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of a bottle</i>) that gurgles when one pours from it</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑγρός</i> (hugrós, “wet, moist”) +&lrm; <i>φθόγγος</i> (phthóngos, “sound, voice”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>watery, wet, moist</li><li>transparent like water (said of gauze-like garments)</li><li>pliant, supple, lithe</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδάτιον</i> (hudátion, “small quantity of water”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rivulet, little water, runnel</li><li>small drink or quantity of water</li></ol><p>Diminutive form of <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) drops, as a urinary disease</li><li>watery vesicle, hydatid</li><li>kind of disease of the liver</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) kind of disease in the hoofs of horses</li><li>kind of gem</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕδατος</i> (húdatos, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for water</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕδατος</i> (húdatos, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>herb said to grow over truffles and mark the spot where they are</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕδνον</i> (húdnon, “truffle”) +&lrm; <i>φύλλον</i> (phúllon, “leaf”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>quicksilver artificially prepared from cinnabar ore</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(in general) quicksilver, mercury</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) +&lrm; <i>ἄργῠρος</i> (árgŭros, “silver”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water mixed with oil</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) +&lrm; <i>ἔλαιον</i> (élaion, “oil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydria; water jar</li></ol><p><i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to water, wash, sprinkle with water</li><li>to pour libations</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hydraulic, hydrostatic</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕδραυλις</i> (húdraulis, “water organ”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>watery, moist, well-watered</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-λός</i> (-lós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A reservoir, a cistern</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>θήκη</i> (thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hydrocephalus, water on the brain</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>κεφαλή</i> (kephalḗ, “head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hydrocele, water in the scrotum</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>κήλη</i> (kḗlē, “tumour, hernia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydromancy, water-divination</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-, “water”) + <i>μᾰντείᾱ</i> (mănteíā, “divination”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>watermill</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>μῠ́λη</i> (mŭ́lē, “mill”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water pepper, hydropiper (<i>Persicaria hydropiper</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔δρο-</i> (hŭdro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>πέπερῐ</i> (péperĭ, “pepper”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oil of roses mixed with water</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>ῥόδῐνος</i> (rhódĭnos, “made of roses”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>celeriac (<i>Apium graveolens</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>σέλῑνον</i> (sélīnon, “celery”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of hydrostatic instrument</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρόσκοπος</i> (hudróskopos, “water-seeker”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) hydrophobia</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-) +&lrm; <i>-φοβίᾱ</i> (-phobíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>guard or inspector of aqueducts or irrigation works</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the one who pours water</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) the constellation Aquarius</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑδρο-</i> (hudro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>χέω</i> (khéō, “to pour”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hydromel; mead</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔δρο-</i> (hŭdro-, “water”) +&lrm; <i>μέλῐ</i> (mélĭ, “honey”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) umbilical hernia, supposed to be due to fluid</li></ol><p>Neuter of <i>ὑδρόμφᾰλος</i> (hudrómphălos, “suffering from umbilical hernia”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>heavy rain</li></ol><p>Nominal formation from <i>ῡ̔́ω</i> (hū́ō, “to rain”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>woody, wooded</li><li>dwelling in the woods</li></ol><p>From <i>ῡ̔́λη</i> (hū́lē) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>barking, baying</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑλᾰκτέω</i> (hulăktéō, “to bark, bay”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bark (of dogs)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑλάω</i> (huláō, “to bark”) + a suffix <i>-ακτέω</i> (-aktéō) also seen in <i>ἀγανακτέω</i> (aganaktéō, “to feel vexed”), likely from an onomatopoeic root <i>&#42;ul-</i> also reflected in Latin <i>ululō</i> (“to howl”), <i>ulula</i> (“screech owl”), Sanskrit <i>उलूक</i> (úlūka, “owl”), and Lithuanian <i>ulúoti</i> (“to howl”). See also <i>ὀλολῡ́ζω</i> (ololū́zō, “to cry out”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>ὑλακτέω</i> (hulaktéō)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of matter, material</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕλη</i> (húlē, “matter”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>filter, colander, strainer</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑλίζω</i> (hulízō, “to strain, filter”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lumbering (the cutting or felling of wood)</li></ol><p><i>ῡ̔λοτόμος</i> (hūlotómos) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>cutting or felling wood</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a woodcutter, woodman, lumberjack</li></ol><p>From <i>ῡ̔́λη</i> (hū́lē, “wood”) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “to cut, hew”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hymenaeus, the wedding or bridal song, sung by the brideʼs attendants as they led her to the bridegroomʼs house</li><li>wedding</li></ol><p>From <i>ῡ̔μήν</i> (hūmḗn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὑμέναιος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hymenaios</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῡ̔μήν</i> (Hūmḗn) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of skin or membrane</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑμήν</i> (humḗn, “skin; membrane”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐνος</i> (-ĭnos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>modifying a noun</i>) your, of yours (<i>plural</i>)</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;in&#32;Koine</i>) your, of yours (<i>singular</i>)</li><li>(as substantive,&#32;on its own,&#32;often&#32;with article) yours</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) your people or possessions</li></ol></ol><p>From the root of <i>ῡ̔μεῖς</i> (hūmeîs, “you”, plural) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros, contrastive adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thin skin, membrane</li><li>seed vessel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;syewH-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>स्यूमन्</i> (syū́man), Old Prussian <i>schumeno</i>, and Hittite (šumanza).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὑμήν)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hymen or Hymenaios</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>membrane-winged</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔μήν</i> (hŭmḗn, “thin skin; membrane”) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>second person plural personal pronoun: ye, you, y'all, you guys</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;usmé</i>, with regular rough breathing <i>῾</i> (h) before initial <i>υ</i> (u).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sing about</li><li>to praise in song, to hymn</li></ol><p><i>ὕμνος</i> (húmnos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a poet who writes hymns; a hymnographer</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕμνος</i> (húmnos, “hymn”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>singing of a hymn; hymning</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔μνῳδός</i> (hŭmnōidós, “singing hymns”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from <i>ῠ̔́μνος</i> (hŭ́mnos, “hymn”) + <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ), contracted form of <i>ᾰ̓οιδή</i> (ăoidḗ, “song”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ῡ̔μέτερος</i> (hūméteros)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaped like the letter υ (upsilon)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὗ</i> (hû, “Greek letter upsilon”) +&lrm; <i>-ο-</i> (-o-) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-like”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of henbane</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑοσκύαμος</i> (huoskúamos, “henbane”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>henbane (<i>Hyoscyamus niger</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὗς</i> (hûs, “swine”) +&lrm; <i>κύαμος</i> (kúamos, “bean”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ῠ̔πό</i> (hŭpó)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to lead or bring under</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to bring under one's power</li></ol><li>to bring a person before the judgment seat, to accuse, impeach</li><li>to lead slowly on, to lead on by degrees, by deceit</li><li>to take away from under, withdraw</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to draw off</li><li>to purge the bowels</li></ol></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to go slowly away, draw off, withdraw, retire</li><li>to go slowly forwards, draw on</li><li>(medicine,&#32;of the bowels) to be open</li><li>to sink down, squat</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “beneath”) +&lrm; <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to lead”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subordinate, subaltern</li></ol><p>From a univerbation of <i>ὑπ’</i> (hup’) +&lrm; <i>ἀλλήλους</i> (allḗlous), literally “under one another”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to begin, make a beginning, take initiative, be first</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make a beginning of, begin</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>with genitive</i>)</li><li>(<i>with accusative</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) be the beginner, be first, do without provocation</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to do something first</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>only in active</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to begin to be, to come into being, arise, spring up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>to be in existence, to be there, to be ready</li><li>to really exist</li><li>to be</li><li>(<i>with a participle</i>)</li><li>to be the descendant of</li></ol><li>to be laid down, to be taken for granted</li><li>to belong to, fall to, accrue</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to be devoted</li><li>(in the logic of Aristotle,&#32;denotes the subsistence of qualities in a subject, whether propria or accidentia)</li></ol><li>(in neuter plural participle,&#32;τὰ ὑπάρχοντα)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>existing circumstances, present advantages</li><li>what belongs to one, one's possessions</li></ol><li>(impersonal,&#32;with infinitive and accusative) the fact is that</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>it is allowed, it is possible</li><li>(<i>in neuter participle</i>) since it is allowed you to</li></ol></ol><li>to be <i>ὕπαρχος</i> (húparkhos, “lieutenant”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓́ρχω</i> (ắrkhō, “to begin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>poetic</i>) out from under</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔π-</i> (hŭp-) +&lrm; <i>ἐκ</i> (ek).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>underneath, beneath, below</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>over, above</li><li>farther inland</li><li>(nautical,&#32;of ships at sea) off of</li><li>over, across</li><li>beyond</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>in defense of, on behalf of</li><li>for the prosperity or safety of</li><li>for, instead of, in the name of; by commission from</li><li>as representative of, in the name of</li><li>for, because of, by reason of, on account of</li><li>for the purpose of preventing or avoiding, for the sake of</li></ol><li>concerning</li></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) over, beyond</li><li>(<i>of measure</i>) above, exceeding, beyond</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>after comparative</i>) than</li><li>in violation of</li></ol><li>(<i>of number</i>) above, upwards of</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) before, earlier than</li><li>on behalf of</li></ol><li>(+ dative,&#32;Aeolic) for</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>overmuch, above measure</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hupér</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;upér</i> (“over, above”), from <i>&#42;upo</i> (“under, below”) (whence English <i>up</i>). Cognates include Latin <i>super</i> and Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;uber</i> (whence English <i>over</i>).</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of separation or cause. The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(sailing,&#32;chiefly in the plural) each of the braces attached to the ends of the ἐπίκρια (“sailyards”), whereby the sails are shifted fore and aft, to catch the wind</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér, “over, above”, preposition). <i>This etymology is incomplete. You can help Wiktionary by elaborating on the origins of this term. </i></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of overwhelming strength</li><li>overweening, wanton, lawless</li></ol><p><i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, “over-, super-”) +&lrm; <i>βῐ́ᾱ</i> (bĭ́ā, “strength, force”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>St. John's wort (<i>Hypericum perforatum</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>ἐρείκη</i> (ereíkē, “heath”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-spirited, daring, bold</li><li>overweening, arrogant</li><li>vehemently angry, furious</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔περ-</i> (hŭper-, “over”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, soul; anger, wrath).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>exceedingly poor</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔περ-</i> (hŭper-, “over”) +&lrm; <i>πτωχός</i> (ptōkhós, poor, beggarly).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>superior order</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, “over”) +&lrm; <i>τάξις</i> (táxis, “order, arrangment”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>better [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘than’</small>]</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upper, outer</li><li>higher, nobler, more excellent</li><li>stronger, mightier</li><li>victorious or triumphant [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘over’</small>]</li><li>further</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔περ-</i> (hŭper-) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>reddish</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under, beneath”) +&lrm; <i>ἐρυθρός</i> (eruthrós, “red”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>proud, haughty</li></ol><p><i>ῠ̔περ-</i> (hŭper-, “over”) +&lrm; <i>-φρων</i> (-phrōn, mind)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horned cummin (<i>Hypecoum procumbens</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ῠ̔πήκοος</i> (hŭpḗkoos, “hearkening”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>listening [<i>with</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li><li>subject, obedient [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> dative <small>‘to someone’</small>]</li></ol><p><i>ὑπακούω</i> (hupakoúō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>oboediēns</i> (&lt; <i>oboediō</i> &lt; <i>ob-</i> + <i>audiō</i>); Dutch <i>gehoorzaam</i> (<&lt; <i>horen</i> (cognate via PIE)); Bulgarian <i>послушен</i> (poslušen), Polish <i>posłuszny</i>, Russian <i>послу́шный</i> (poslúšnyj) (akin to <i>слу́шать</i> (slúšatʹ)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>moustache, hair on the upper lip</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) beard</li></ol><p>Ending like <i>ἀπήνη</i> (apḗnē), <i>λαμπήνη</i> (lampḗnē) and <i>ἀνθρήνη</i> (anthrḗnē). Traditionally explained as a compound of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;upó</i> (“under, below”) and a root common to Sanskrit <i>आन</i> (āna, “face, mouth, nose”). Yet this etymology is semantically unsatisfactory and formally difficult. Alternatively, the word has often been considered to be Pre-Greek, in view of the typical substrate suffix <i>-ήνη</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῠ̔πό</i> (hŭpó)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under the sky, in the open air, afield</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕπαιθρος</i> (húpaithros) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under accusation, called to account</li><li>blameworthy, guilty, responsible</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>αἴτῐος</i> (aítĭos, “guilty, culpable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>obedience</li><li>answer to prayer</li><li>(<i>Eastern Orthodoxy</i>) ypakoë</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>ὑπακούω</i> (hupakoúō, “give ear, hearken”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>absolute</i>) to listen, hearken, give ear</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to make answer when called</li><li>(<i>with the genitive of object</i>) to listen or hearken to, give ear to, heed, attend to</li></ol><li>(<i>special senses</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of porters</i>) to answer a knock at the door</li><li>(<i>of a judge</i>) to listen to a complaint</li><li>(<i>of dependents, subjects</i>) to obey, submit to</li><li>to answer one's expectations, to succeed</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be subject to the sun's rays</li><li>(<i>of ailments</i>) to yield, give way to a remedy</li><li>to concede a point in a dispute</li></ol><li>to understand under</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>grammar</i>) to understand a word omitted</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō, “to listen”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to exchange, barter</li><li>to change a little</li><li>to mortgage</li><li>to change gradually</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, under) +&lrm; <i>ἀλλάσσω</i> (allássō, “to change, alter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>interchange, exchange</li><li>change of regime</li><li>(<i>banking</i>) pledging of security, mortgage</li><li>(<i>rhetoric</i>) hypallage</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πᾰλλᾰ́σσω</i> (hŭpăllắssō, “to exchange”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to retire slowly</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>ἀναχωρέω</i> (anakhōréō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the office or rank of consul, consulate</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) the consular largesse</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́πᾰτος</i> (hŭ́pătos, “consul”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or pertaining to a consul, consular</li><li>of consular rank</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́πᾰτος</i> (hŭ́pătos, “consul”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to bring to a place of safety, carry safely away</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>ἐκ-</i> (ek-) +&lrm; <i>τίθεμαι</i> (títhemai, mediopassive of <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to flee away or escape secretly</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to escape from</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>ἐκφεύγω</i> (ekpheúgō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>over-, super-</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;upér</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrogant, boastful, blustering, conceited, overweening, proud, vain, vainglorious</li><li>contemptuous, disdainful, haughty, scornful, supercilious</li><li>(<i>rarely, in a good sense</i>) magnificent, splendid</li></ol><p>Perhaps from <i>ὑπερφανής</i> (huperphanḗs, “appearing over; showing oneself above others”) with epenthetic vowel inserted; or directly from <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér, “over, above”) +&lrm; <i>φᾱνός</i> (phānós, “appearing, shining, bright, resplendent”) from <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “to appear, show forth, shine”) from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰáňňō</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hold (<i>one thing</i>) over (<i>another</i>)</li><li>to have or hold above</li><li>to be above, rise above</li><li>(<i>military</i>) to outflank [<i>with</i> genitive]</li><li>to overtop, exceed, outdo</li><li>to be prominent, prevail</li><li>to get over, cross</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>arrogant, boastful, blustering, conceited, overweening, proud, vain, vainglorious</li><li>contemptuous, disdainful, haughty, scornful, supercilious</li><li>(<i>rarely, in a good sense</i>) magnificent, splendid</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>ὑπερφανής</i> (huperphanḗs, “appearing over; showing oneself above others”), or directly from <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér, “over, above”) +&lrm; <i>φᾱνός</i> (phānós, “appearing, shining, bright, resplendent”), in both cases, with epenthetic vowel <i>-η-</i> (-ē-) inserted, perhaps on the model of <i>ὑπερήνωρ</i> (huperḗnōr, “arrogant”). However, Beekes is skeptical, analyzing the word as an atomic root form <i>ὑπερήφ</i> (huperḗph) (despite claiming that <i>-η-</i> (-ē-) is compositional) + a suffix <i>-ανος</i> (-anos), and leaves the origin open.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyperaspist; one who holds a shield over, protector, champion</li></ol><p><i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, “above”) +&lrm; <i>ἀσπιστής</i> (aspistḗs, “one armed with a shield”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to imitate the Attic dialect of Ancient Greek to excess</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to use excessive subtlety in philosophy</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) +&lrm; <i>ἀττικίζω</i> (attikízō), equivalent to <i>ὑπεραττῐκός</i> (huperattĭkós, excessively Attic) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>excessively Attic, imitating the Attic dialect of Ancient Greek to an excessive degree</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) +&lrm; <i>Ἀττικός</i> (Attikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw over or beyond (<i>a mark</i>), to overshoot</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to run beyond, overrun</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to outstrip or pass</li><li>to outdo, surpass, prevail over [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative]</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to go beyond, exceed</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to exceed all bounds, be in excess, be extraordinary</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to go on further and further</li><li>to pass over, cross (<i>mountains, rivers, etc.</i>), to double (<i>a headland</i>) [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive]</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to cross over</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to run over, overflow (<i>of water</i>)</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to be at the highest point (<i>of the sun</i>)</li><li>(transitive,&#32;middle voice) to outdo, overcome, conquer</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;middle voice) to be the conqueror, to win</li><li>(transitive,&#32;middle voice) to overbid, outbid</li><li>(transitive,&#32;middle voice) to put off, postpone</li><li>(intransitive,&#32;middle voice) to delay, linger</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to step over, mount, scale, cross</li><li>(<i>of a river</i>), to overflow its banks</li><li>to overstep, transgress, trespass, sin</li><li>to pass over, pass by, leave out, omit</li><li>to go beyond, surpass, outdo</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) +&lrm; <i>βαίνω</i> (baínō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>extremely heavy</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔περ-</i> (hŭper-) +&lrm; <i>βᾰρῠ́ς</i> (bărŭ́s) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>overshooting</li><li>superiority</li><li>excess, extremity</li><li>A crossing over, passage</li><li>delay</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) hyperbola</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερβάλλω</i> (huperbállō, “to go beyond, exceed”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix), from <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hyperbolic, exaggerated</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερβολή</i> (huperbolḗ) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A codicil</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, “super-”) +&lrm; <i>διαθήκη</i> (diathḗkē, “testament, covenant”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;Epic</i>) overbearing, overweening, haughty</li></ol><p>As if a present participle to an unattested <i>&#42;ὑπερηνορέω</i> (&#42;huperēnoréō), from <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) + a derivative of <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>arrogance, boastfulness, ostentation, pride, vainglory, vanity; haughtiness, superciliousness, disdain and contempt towards or for another person</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερήφανος</i> (huperḗphanos, “overweening, arrogant; showing oneself above others”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾰ</i> (-íă, <i>forms feminine abstract nouns</i>) from <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér, “over, above”) and <i>φᾱνός</i> (phānós, “appearing, shining, bright, resplendent”) from <i>φαίνω</i> (phaínō, “to appear, show forth, shine”) from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;pʰáňňō</i> from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰeh₂-</i> (“to shine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative</li><li>dilatory</li><li>requiring or causing delay</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπέρθεσις</i> (hupérthesis, “putting over or across”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>architecture</i>) lintel of a door or gate</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, “over”) +&lrm; <i>θύρα</i> (thúra, “door”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>poetic form of <i>ὑπερμενής</i> (hupermenḗs, “exceedingly mighty”, epithet of Ares)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) exalted, exceedingly mighty (<i>epithet of Zeus</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér) +&lrm; <i>μένος</i> (ménos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>above the clouds</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, above) +&lrm; <i>νεφέλη</i> (nephélē, “cloud”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to win or conquer beyond, to be beyond a winner or a conqueror,</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, “beyond”) +&lrm; <i>νῑκᾰ́ω</i> (nīkắō, “win, conquer”), literally “win beyond”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>banishment</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερορῐ́ζω</i> (huperorĭ́zō, “to banish”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>above the heavens</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, above, over) +&lrm; <i>οὐρᾰνός</i> (ourănós, “heaven”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contempt, disdain [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘for’</small>]</li><li>haughtiness, arrogance</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) +&lrm; <i>-οψίᾱ</i> (-opsíā)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to fly over (<i>of a spear etc.</i>)</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fly over or beyond [<i>with</i> accusative <i>or</i> genitive]</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) +&lrm; <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to bear or carry over (<i>a place</i>)</li><li>to rise above, surpass, excel, have an advantage over [<i>with</i> genitive <i>or</i> accusative]</li></ol><p><i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, “over”) +&lrm; <i>φέρω</i> (phérō, “to carry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>overbearing, arrogant</li></ol><p><i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) + an unknown second element.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>exceedingly, excessively</li></ol><p><i>ὑπερφίαλος</i> (huperphíalos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>contempt, disdain</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερφρονέω</i> (huperphronéō, “to be over-proud”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>very</li><li>exceedingly</li><li>amazingly</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερφυής</i> (huperphuḗs) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῠ̔περῴᾱ</i> (hŭperōíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ῠ̔περῴᾱ</i> (hŭperōíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ὑπερῷον</i> (huperōîon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῠ̔περῷος</i> (hŭperōîos)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér, “above; over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Attic,&#32;Koine,&#32;anatomy) palate (roof of the mouth)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ῠ̔περῴᾱ</i> (hŭperōíā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ὑπερῷον</i> (huperōîon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>ῠ̔περῷος</i> (hŭperōîos)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér, “above; over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὑπερῷα</i> (huperōîa)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the upper floor or part of a house</li><li>attic</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>upper</li><li>upstairs</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ῠ̔περώῐ̈ος</i> (hŭperṓĭ̈os), from <i>ῠ̔πέρ</i> (hŭpér, “above; over”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>responsible, liable to give account for (one's administration of an office)</li></ol><p><i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>εὔθῡνος</i> (eúthūnos); see <i>εὐθῡ́νω</i> (euthū́nō, “to direct”) for more.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to say as a foundation or preface, to premise, suggest</li><li>to subjoin, add</li><li>to suggest an explanation, explain, interpret</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>εἶπον</i> (eîpon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>towards dawn, early</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “below”) +&lrm; <i>ἠώς</i> (ēṓs, “dawn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An Ancient Greek title, originally for a rower; In later times a title for those who performed any service in a vessel, except the soldiers or marines</li><li>Any person who acted as the assistant of another, and performed manual labour for him, whether in sacred or profane things; a slave.</li><li>A title for men by whom the hoplites (<i>ὁπλίτης</i> (hoplítēs)) were accompanied when they took the field, and who carried the luggage, the provisions, and the shield of the hoplites. (The more common name for this servant of the hoplites was <i>σκευοφόρος</i> (skeuophóros).)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπό</i> (hupó, “below”) +&lrm; <i>ἐρέσσω</i> (eréssō, “to row”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, “-er”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>body or rowers, ship's crew</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) all kinds of service</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) class of servants or attendants</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπηρέτης</i> (hupērétēs, “rower; assistant, servant”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to promise</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “hypo-, under-”) +&lrm; <i>ἴσχω</i> (ískhō, “to keep”), apparently modified under the influence of <i>ἀρνέομαι</i> (arnéomai, “to deny, refuse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>drowsy, sleepy</li><li>soporific</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπνόω</i> (hupnóō, “to be drowsy”) +&lrm; <i>-ρός</i> (-rós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inclined to sleep, sleepy</li><li>inducing sleep, soporific</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ὑπνόω</i> (hupnóō, “I put to sleep”) +&lrm; <i>-τικός</i> (-tikós, adjectival derivational suffix); see <i>ὕπνος</i> (húpnos, “sleep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to put to sleep</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to fall asleep, sleep</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕπνος</i> (húpnos) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Prefix</b></p><ol><li>under, sub-, hypo-</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>of the casing or covering</li><li>of the agency or influence (by)</li></ol><li>denoting a small degree, gradual</li></ol><p>Prefix variant of the preposition <i>ῠ̔πό</i> (hŭpó).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speaking somewhat barbarously</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>βᾰ́ρβᾰρος</i> (bắrbăros, “non-Greek-speaking, foreign”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to grow up after</li><li>to grow in succession</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “denoting a small degree or gradual change”) +&lrm; <i>γῐ́γνομαι</i> (gĭ́gnomai, “to become; to come into being”),</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>orthography</i>) iota subscript</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to write under</li><li>to sign (one’s name)</li><li>to trace, outline</li></ol><p><i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-) +&lrm; <i>γρᾰ́φω</i> (grắphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pencil</li><li>type of surgical instrument</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>γρᾰφῐ́ς</i> (grăphĭ́s, “pencil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to receive (<i>especially of a friendly, hospitable welcome</i>)</li><li>to submit to, endure</li><li>to undertake, promise</li></ol><p><i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>δέχομαι</i> (dékhomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>inferior</li><li>younger</li></ol><p>Comparative of unattested <i>ὑποδεής</i> (hupodeḗs), from <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>δέω</i> (déō, “to lack, need”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs) +&lrm; <i>-τερος</i> (-teros)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cower under or before</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to fear (secretly)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>δείδω</i> (deídō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to show</li><li>to intimate</li><li>to teach</li><li>to pretend</li></ol><p><i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-) +&lrm; <i>δείκνῡμῐ</i> (deíknūmĭ)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(orthography,&#32;grammar) The hypodiastole, a mark used in <i>scripta continua</i> Classical and Byzantine Greek to note that a series of letters should be read as two words rather than all together, now identical to the comma.</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under, lower”) +&lrm; <i>διαστολή</i> (diastolḗ, “separation, distinction”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reception, welcoming</li><li>hospitality</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑποδέχομαι</i> (hupodékhomai, “to welcome”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, verbal noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>beast of burden, draught animal</li><li>(<i>later specifically</i>) donkey, ass</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>ζυγόν</i> (zugón, “yoke”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suggestion, counsel, warning</li><li>(<i>law</i>) pledge, deposit, mortgage</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>ῠ̔ποτῐ́θημῐ</i> (hŭpotĭ́thēmĭ, “put down, pledge”). Morphologically, from <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, holster”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hypothetical</li><li>belonging to the subject</li><li>suggestive, hortatory</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπόθεσις</i> (hupóthesis, “proposal”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>under, below</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocist (<i>Cytinus hypocistis</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>κῐ́στος</i> (kĭ́stos, “rockrose”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to steal, filch</li><li>to cheat</li><li>to mislead, keep secret</li><li>to disguise, conceal</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>κλέπτω</i> (kléptō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to talk child language</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to call by endearing names</li><li>to call by a fair name, gloss over, palliate</li><li>to nickname</li><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to use diminutives</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>κορίζομαι</i> (korízomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>diminutive, hypocoristic</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑποκορίζομαι</i> (hupokorízomai, “to call by endearing names”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós). See also <i>ὑπό</i> (hupó, “below”) and <i>κόρη</i> (kórē, “girl”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to answer</li><li>to interpret</li><li>(<i>Attic</i>) to dialogue, to answer on the stage; to play a part, be an actor</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) to feign, to dissemble</li></ol></ol><p>Mediopassive of <i>ῠ̔ποκρῑ́νω</i> (hŭpokrī́nō), from <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>κρῑ́νω</i> (krī́nō, “to separate, judge, decide”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who answers: interpreter, expounder</li><li>(<i>Attic</i>) stage actor</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figurative</i>) pretender, dissembler, hypocrite</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὑποκρίνομαι</i> (hupokrínomai, “to play a part on stage”) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to hide under or beneath</li><li>to keep something secret from someone</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under, beneath”) +&lrm; <i>κρῠ́πτω</i> (krŭ́ptō, “hide”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coaxing or endearing name, term of endearment, pet name</li><li>fair name for something base, euphemism</li><li>diminutive, hypocoristic</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑποκορίζομαι</i> (hupokorízomai, “to call by endearing names”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vessel or trough placed under a press to receive the wine or oil</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>ληνός</i> (lēnós, “press”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to take up</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to bear up, support</li><li>to take up, seize</li><li>to interpret, take (to mean)</li><li>Ι take up, address, accept</li><li>to assume, suppose</li><li>to draw away</li><li>to take away, seize, remove</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>λᾰμβᾰ́νω</i> (lămbắnō, “to take”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to leave over, leave behind</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to remain</li></ol><p><i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>λείπω</i> (leípō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>take account of, consider</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>λογῐ́ζομαι</i> (logĭ́zomai, “to count, reckon”), from <i>λόγος</i> (lógos, “word”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to remain</li><li>to endure</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>μένω</i> (ménō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an act of remaining behind</li><li>an act of holding out, patient endurance</li><li>the enduring to do</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπομένω</i> (hupoménō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to think secretly, suspect</li><li>to suspect, conjecture, suppose</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>νοέω</i> (noéō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suspicious</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπονοέω</i> (huponoéō, “to suspect, conjecture”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>just before</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>ὑπὸ πρό</i> (hupò pró, “just before”), from <i>ὑπό</i> (hupó, “underneath”) + <i>πρό</i> (pró, “before”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suspect, to hold in suspicion</li><li>to guess, to suppose</li><li>to observe, to notice</li></ol><p>From the future stem of <i>ῠ̔φοράω</i> (hŭphoráō, “to suspect”) +&lrm; <i>-εύω</i> (-eúō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>footstool</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to indicate, intimate</li><li>to signal</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>σημαίνω</i> (sēmaínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>medicine</i>) one who has the orifice of the urethra too low</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>σπάω</i> (spáō, “I draw, I pull”) +&lrm; <i>-ίας</i> (-ías, suffix to form a masculine noun).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which stands under, support, prop</li><li>one that gives substance, creator</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “below, under”) +&lrm; <i>ἵστημι</i> (hístēmi, “to stand”) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs, masculine agent-noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to underprop, sustain</li></ol><p><i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-) +&lrm; <i>στηρίζω</i> (stērízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a subordinate commander, (<i>anachronistically</i>) a lieutenant general</li><li>a title of various officials</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>at Tenos</li><li>in Magnesia</li><li>in Egypt</li></ol></ol><p>Formed by prefixation of <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under-”) +&lrm; <i>στρᾰτηγός</i> (strătēgós, “commander, general”). Note the change in accentuation from oxytone to proparoxytone.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn round about or back, guide or bring back</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to roll up</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to revolve beneath</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to turn about, turn short around</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to return</li><li>to turn away</li><li>(<i>participle as adverb</i>) reversely</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>στρέφω</i> (stréphō, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small tags of iron on the bit</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under, below”) +&lrm; <i>στόμιον</i> (stómion, “bridle, bit”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place or arrange under</li><li>to post under or behind</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to subject, subdue, make subject</li></ol><li>to put after</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>logic</i>) to take as a minor premise</li></ol><li>to govern the subjunctive</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>τᾰ́σσω</i> (tắssō, “to place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place under</li><li>to lay down, assume, take for granted</li><li>to present</li><li>to suggest</li><li>to advise, admonish</li><li>to propose</li><li>to put down as a deposit or stake, to pawn, pledge, mortgage</li><li>to stake, hazard, venture</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “below”) +&lrm; <i>τίθημι</i> (títhēmi, “put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Postpositive, always placed after the thing with which it is combined.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>ῠ̔ποτᾰκτῐκόν <i>φωνῆεν</i> (phōnêen): A vowel that occurs last in a diphthong, referring to υ, ι.</li></ol><li>ῠ̔ποτᾰκτῐκόν, with or without <i>ἄρθρον</i> (árthron): An article, or in today's terminology a pronoun, that is placed after its antecedent: the relative pronoun <i>ὅς</i> (hós).</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) A verb in the subjunctive mood.</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>ῠ̔ποτᾰκτῐκός, with or without <i>σύνδεσμος</i> (súndesmos): A conjunction that is always followed by the subjunctive</li><li><i>ῠ̔ποτᾰκτῐκή</i> <i>ἔγκλισις</i> (énklisis): The subjunctive mood.</li></ol><li>Submissive, obedient</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑποτᾰγ-</i> (hupotăg-), the root of <i>ὑποτᾰ́σσω</i> (hupotắssō, “arrange underneath or after”), + <i>-τῐκός</i> (-tĭkós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>geometry</i>) hypotenuse</li></ol><p>Ellipsis of ὑποτείνουσα [<i>γραμμή</i> (grammḗ)] or ὑποτείνουσα [<i>πλευρά</i> (pleurá)].</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stretch under, put under</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) to extend under, subtend</li><li>to strain, pull hard</li><li>to hold out hopes, offer</li><li>to lay or put before one, present, suggest</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “below”) +&lrm; <i>τείνω</i> (teínō, “to stretch, spread”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to pay (<i>a tax or tribute</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>τελέω</i> (teléō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subject to paying taxes, tributary</li><li>taxable</li></ol><p><i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>τέλος</i> (télos) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to suspect</li></ol><p><i>ὑπότοπος</i> (hupótopos, “suspicious”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rub off; to wear away</li><li>to grate (especially in preparing <i>ῠ̔πότρῑμμᾰ</i> (hŭpótrīmmă))</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “below”) +&lrm; <i>τρῑ́βω</i> (trī́bō, “to rub”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to render service or help to, serve, assist, succor [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπουργός</i> (hupourgós, “rendering service, serviceable, conducive”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under the hand, in hand</li><li>under the command of, subject to [<i>with</i> dative]</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>χείρ</i> (kheír) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Of or concerning the region between the ribs and the navel</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑποχόνδριον</i> (hupokhóndrion, “the space between the ribs and the navel”) +&lrm; <i>-ακός</i> (-akós), from <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “below”) +&lrm; <i>χόνδρος</i> (khóndros, “cartilage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to go back, retire, recoil</li><li>to withdraw, go aside</li><li>to avoid, shun</li><li>to go on in succession</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>χωρέω</i> (khōréō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under the ribs</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) the soft tissue between the ribs and navel</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “below”) +&lrm; <i>χόνδρος</i> (khóndros, “cartilage”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>suspicion</li><li>jealousy</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>-οψίᾱ</i> (-opsíā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Thessalian form of <i>ὑποπρό</i> (hupopró)</li></ol><p>Contraction of <i>ὑποπρό</i> (hupopró, “just before”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>[<i>with</i> genitive]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) from underneath</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>under, beneath</li></ol><li>(<i>of cause or agency</i>) by, through</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>in pregnant phrases</i>) of immediate acts of an agent, as well as further results</li><li>(in Herodotus and Attic,&#32;of things as well as persons)</li><li>denoting the attendant or accompanying circumstances</li><li>(<i>of accompanying music</i>) to give the time</li></ol></ol><li>[<i>with</i> dative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place or position</i>) under, near</li><li>(<i>of agency</i>) under, through, by</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>expressing subjection or dependence</li><li>of logical subordination</li><li>of attendant circumstances</li></ol></ol><li>[<i>with</i> accusative]</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) to express motion towards and under</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>of position or extension</li><li>of logical subordination</li></ol><li>of subjection, control</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) just after</li><li>of accompaniment</li><li>to a certain degree</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;upó</i> (“under, below”), with regular rough breathing ῾ (<i>h</i>) before initial <i>υ</i> (u). Cognates include Latin <i>sub</i> and Sanskrit <i>उप</i> (úpa).</p><p>The genitive is from the PIE ablative of origin or cause. The dative is from the PIE locative and metaphor of all infinitives coming from locatives. The accusative is from the pre-PIE directional and the PIE accusative of direct object.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>base, anything put under, hence:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>footstool</li><li>wooden framework to support a couch, a kind of rocking apparatus</li><li>keel of a ship</li><li>step</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, under) +&lrm; <i>βᾰ́θρον</i> (bắthron, “base, pedestal”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>vault, crypt</li></ol><p>Substantivization of the neuter form of <i>ῠ̔πόγειος</i> (hŭpógeios, “underground”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>underground, subterranean, chthonian</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”, “beneath”) +&lrm; <i>γῆ</i> (gê, “land”, “earth”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos, adjective-forming derivational suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subglaucous</li></ol><p><i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>γλαυκός</i> (glaukós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>horse tongue (<i>Ruscus hypoglossum</i>)</li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ὑπόγλωσσος</i> (hupóglōssos, “under the tongue”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat talkative</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) under the tongue</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under, hypo-”) +&lrm; <i>γλῶσσᾰ</i> (glôssă, “tongue”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A sandal or shoe, any kind of footwear</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑποδέω</i> (hupodéō, “to bind underneath”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma) (these were fastened to the feet with straps).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) scowlingly; grimly</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-) + <i>&#42;δρᾰκ</i> (&#42;drăk), from the root of <i>δέρκομαι</i> (dérkomai, “to see; to watch”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;derḱ-</i> (“to see”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running under</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπέδραμον</i> (hupédramon) (aorist of <i>ὑποτρ́εχω</i> (hupotŕekhō)) + <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) diaphragm, midriff</li><li>(<i>in insects</i>) division between thorax and abdomen</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) ropes or braces used to strengthen the hull of a trireme</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑποζώννῡμι</i> (hupozṓnnūmi, “to undergird; brace a ship”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, result noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>proposal, proposed action; policy, intention</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>suggestion, advice</li><li>purpose</li><li>occasion, excuse, pretext</li><li>actor's role</li><li>function, occupation, station</li></ol><li>subject proposed for discussion</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>lawsuit, legal case</li><li>subject, topic</li><li>speech</li><li>play, pantomime</li></ol><li>supposition, assumption, hypothesis</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>presupposition</li><li>premise</li><li>starting point</li><li>raw material</li></ol><li>mortgage</li><li>placing under</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>base (thing placed under another)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔ποτῐ́θημῐ</i> (hŭpotĭ́thēmĭ, “to place under, set before, suggest”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs), from <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “below”) + <i>τῐ́θημῐ</i> (tĭ́thēmĭ, “to put, place”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hypocaust</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “hypo-”) +&lrm; <i>καυστόν</i> (kaustón, “burnt offering”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lie under</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of places</i>) to lie below</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>):</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be put under the eyes, to be submitted or proposed to one</li><li>to be laid down as a ground of argument, to be assumed as a hypothesis</li><li>to be suggested</li><li>to be left at bottom, left remaining, reserved</li><li>to be subject, to submit to, to bow down before, be submissive</li><li>to be subject to, liable to</li><li>to be left behind in pledge, to be pledged, mortgaged</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) to underlie in thought</li><li>(<i>logic</i>) to be subject or subordinate to</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) the present tense</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai, “lie”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>somewhat yellow</li></ol><p><i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-) +&lrm; <i>κῐρρός</i> (kĭrrhós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>reply, answer</li><li>the part of an actor or orator: delivery, delivery, elocution, act</li><li>outward show, hypocrisy, pretense</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔ποκρῑ́νομαι</i> (hŭpokrī́nomai, “to answer (a fellow actor on stage), play a part, feign, pretend”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>running on wheels, wheeled</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>κύκλος</i> (kúklos, “wheel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking up (the cue, the matter), rejoinder, reply</li><li>taking in a certain sense, assumption, notion, impression</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hasty judgement, prejudice, suspicion</li><li>reputation, public opinion</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὑπολαμβάνω</i> (hupolambánō, “to take up, grasp, interpret”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>left behind, staying behind, remaining</li><li>remaining alive</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπολείπω</i> (hupoleípō) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>remembrance, memorial, record</li><li>a reminder, mention, notice</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>memorandum, note</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) minutes of the proceedings of a public body, public records</li><li>explanatory notes, commentary</li><li>a tablet used for keeping notes, analogous to a commonplace book</li></ol><li>draft, copy of a letter</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπομιμνήσκω</i> (hupomimnḗskō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a reminding, remembrance</li><li>a mentioning</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπομιμνήσκω</i> (hupomimnḗskō) (from <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) + <i>μιμνήσκω</i> (mimnḗskō) + <i>-σις</i> (-sis)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>undermined, underground, subterranean</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>underground passage, mine</li><li>waterpipe, conduit, sewer</li><li>vein of minerals</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπονέμομαι</i> (huponémomai, “to undermine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with fire under, with secret fire</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>metaphorically</li></ol></ol><p><i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>πῦρ</i> (pûr) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under truce or treaty</li></ol><p><i>ὑπό</i> (hupó, “under”) +&lrm; <i>σπονδή</i> (spondḗ, “truce, treaty”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>foundation, base</li><li>assurance, support</li><li>subject-matter</li><li>sediment</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) essence, real nature</li><li>(<i>theology</i>) hypostasis</li></ol><p><i>ὑφίστημι</i> (huphístēmi) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under the roof</li><li>in the house</li><li>covered over</li></ol><p><i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-) +&lrm; <i>στέγη</i> (stégē) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>that which is spread beneath: bed, bedding, litter</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑποστρώννυμι</i> (hupostrṓnnumi) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma), alternative spelling of <i>ὑποστορέννυμι</i> (hupostorénnumi, “spread under”) (from <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “beneath, under”) +&lrm; <i>στορέννυμι</i> (storénnumi, “spread, strew”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>promise, engagement</li><li>profession, job</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπισχνέομαι</i> (hupiskhnéomai) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>subjection, submission</li><li>drawing up of light-armed behind the phalanx</li><li>(<i>in grammar</i>) postposition</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔ποτᾰ́σσω</i> (hŭpotắssō, “to place under”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a pungent dish made of various items grated and mixed together, usually used as a dip</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔ποτρῑ́βω</i> (hŭpotrī́bō, “to rub; to grate”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wicker basket, handbasket</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably from Pre-Greek, due to the many alternative forms.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>πλυνεύς</i> (pluneús, “clothes-cleaner”).</li></ol><p>Unknown.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>dipped or dyed in hysginum</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕσγινον</i> (húsginon, “hysginum”) +&lrm; <i>βάπτω</i> (báptō, “to dip, dye”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) battle, conflict, combat</li></ol><p>From a derivative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyudʰsmós</i> (“warrior”) (compare Sanskrit <i>युध्म</i> (yudhmá, “warrior”), from <i>&#42;Hyewdʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A javelin</li></ol><p>Technical word of uncertain origin. According to Beekes, the word could well be of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>παστάς ἀμπέλων</i> (pastás ampélōn, literally “row of vines”).</li></ol><p>Probably a dialectal Cypriot form of <i>συστάς</i> (sustás, “vines planted closely”), which derives from <i>συνίστημι</i> (sunístēmi, “to put together, organize, frame”). See also <i>ὑστάδα</i> (hustáda, “densely planted vine”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>uterus, womb</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ustérā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;udtéreh₂</i>. Originally feminine form of the adjective <i>ὕστερος</i> (hústeros) with the original sense "outer", from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;údteros</i> (“higher, outer”), perhaps through a construction such as <i>ὑστέρα μήτρᾱ</i> (hustéra mḗtrā), originally denoting "the upper/protruding part" and hence "the belly". For cognates with the same root and a different suffix – i.e. from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;úderos</i> (“stomach”) – see Sanskrit <i>उदर</i> (udara, “belly”) and Latin <i>uterus</i> (“womb”). Probably related to <i>ὕδερος</i> (húderos, “dropsy”).</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>latter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) latter, coming after, behind</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) next</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>later, too late</li><li>(<i>with genitive of object</i>) too late for</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) posterity</li></ol><li>(<i>of inferiority</i>)</li><li>(adverbial,&#32;the neuter ὕστερον is more often used than the standard ὑστέρως)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of time</i>) later, afterwards</li><li>(<i>with prepositions</i>)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>The comparative form of an unattested adjective, with the superlative of <i>ὕστᾰτος</i> (hústătos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;údteros</i> (“higher, outer”) from <i>&#42;úd</i> (“upwards, away”), which would make it cognate with English <i>out, outer</i>, Sanskrit <i>उत्तर</i> (úttara, “upper, higher”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Shortcoming, deficiency, need.</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑστερέω</i> (husteréō, “to come late, to lag behind; to fail to obtain, to lack”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă, nominal suffix), from <i>ὕστερος</i> (hústeros, “latter, later”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō, verbal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ὑστέρημᾰ</i> (hustérēmă)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑστερέω</i> (husteréō, “to come late, to occur later”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be behind, be later, come after, come late</li><li>to come too late, miss (an appointment or event at some appointed time)</li><li>to lag, fall short, lag behind, be inferior to</li><li>to fail; fail to obtain</li><li>to lack, be lacking, be in want, be wanting</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́στερος</i> (hŭ́steros, “later”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>next day, morrow</li></ol><p>By ellipsis of <i>ῠ̔στεραίᾱ ἡμέρᾱ</i> (hŭsteraíā hēmérā, “next day”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>next, following</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>suffering in the womb, hysterical</li><li>of or belonging to the womb</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑστέρᾱ</i> (hustérā) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ῠ̔πό</i> (hŭpó) (<i>before a rough breathing</i>)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in one word, in a single word</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyphen</li></ol><p>Univerbation of <i>ὑφ’ ἕν</i> (huph’ hén), from <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “by, under”) +&lrm; <i>ἕν</i> (hén, “one”), neuter of <i>εἷς</i> (heîs, “one”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) web</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;webʰ-</i> (“to weave, braid”), possibly via <i>ὑφαίνω</i> (huphaínō, “to weave”). Cognates include Old English <i>webb</i> (English <i>web</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>leading, guidance</li><li>direction</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔φηγέομαι</i> (hŭphēgéomai, “go just before, guide, lead”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to stand under</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>ῐ̔́στημῐ</i> (hĭ́stēmĭ, “to stand”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to weave</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;webʰ-</i> (“to weave”), and therefore related distantly to Old English <i>wefan</i> (whence English <i>weave</i>), Sanskrit <i>उभ्नाति</i> (ubhnāti, “entwine”), Persian بافتن (bâftan), Tocharian A <i>wäp-</i> (“weave”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>taking away from under</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>purloining, pilfering</li><li>subtraction, reduction</li></ol><li>moderation, mitigation</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) omission of a letter; contrasted with <i>σῠγκοπή</i> (sŭnkopḗ)</li></ol><p><i>ῠ̔φαιρέω</i> (hŭphairéō) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to seize underneath or inwardly</li><li>to draw or take away from under</li><li>to take away underhand, filch away, purloin</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to rob (<i>somebody of something</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>αἱρέω</i> (hairéō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to guide, lead [<i>with</i> dative]</li><li>to direct, lead the way</li><li>to instruct in</li><li>to indicate</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) +&lrm; <i>ἡγέομαι</i> (hēgéomai).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swineherd</li></ol><p><i>ὗς</i> (hûs) +&lrm; <i>φέρβω</i> (phérbō) +&lrm; <i>-ός</i> (-ós); compare <i>φορβή</i> (phorbḗ, “food, fodder”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mortar-stand</li><li>part of the <i>ὅλμος</i> (hólmos)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-, “under”) +&lrm; <i>ὅλμος</i> (hólmos, “round smooth stone; mortar”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raising or exalting men</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕψι</i> (húpsi, “high, aloft”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὑψήνωρ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hypsenor, a Trojan slain by Eurypylus</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔ψήνωρ</i> (hŭpsḗnōr, “raising; exalting men”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>sitting high on the benches (<i>of a rower</i>)</li><li>high-throned (<i>of Zeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕψι</i> (húpsi) +&lrm; <i>ζυγόν</i> (zugón) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with high-bound tresses</li><li>with lofty foliage</li></ol><p><i>ὕψῐ</i> (húpsĭ, “on high”) +&lrm; <i>κόμη</i> (kómē, “hair of the head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with high gates</li></ol><p><i>ὕψῐ</i> (húpsĭ, “on high”) +&lrm; <i>πύλος</i> (púlos, “gate”), an alternative form of <i>πύλη</i> (púlē, “one wing of a pair of double gates”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-roofed</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́ψι</i> (hŭ́psi) +&lrm; <i>ἐρέφω</i> (eréphō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) superlative degree of <i>ῠ̔ψηλός</i> (hŭpsēlós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>with lofty cliffs</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔ψηλός</i> (hŭpsēlós, “high, lofty”) +&lrm; <i>κρημνός</i> (krēmnós, “cliff”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high, lofty</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) high, lofty, stately, proud</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of persons</i>)</li><li>(<i>of poets</i>) sublime</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́ψι</i> (hŭ́psi, “high”), <i>ῠ̔́ψος</i> (hŭ́psos, “height; summit”) +&lrm; <i>-λός</i> (-lós). Compare Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;ouxselos</i>, whence Old Irish <i>úasal</i>, Welsh <i>uchel</i> and the first part of Gaulish <i>Uxellodunum</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῠ̔ψηλός</i> (hŭpsēlós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>comparative degree of <i>ῠ̔ψηλός</i> (hŭpsēlós)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the high-thundering one (<i>epithet of Zeus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́ψῐ</i> (hŭ́psĭ) +&lrm; <i>βρέμω</i> (brémō) +&lrm; <i>-της</i> (-tēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-topped, with lofty head or peak</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕψῐ</i> (húpsĭ, “on high”) +&lrm; <i>κάρηνον</i> (kárēnon, “head; mountain top”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-built</li></ol><p><i>ὕψῐ</i> (húpsĭ, “on high”) +&lrm; <i>μέλᾰθρον</i> (mélăthron, “ridgepole”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-flying, soaring</li><li>lofty</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕψι</i> (húpsi) +&lrm; <i>πέτομαι</i> (pétomai) +&lrm; <i>-ης</i> (-ēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>aloft, high, on high</li><li>highly</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift high, raise up</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to elevate, exalt</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to represent in a lofty style</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́ψος</i> (hŭ́psos, “height”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from a height, from on high</li></ol><p><i>ὕψος</i> (húpsos) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>high-roofed, high-ceiled</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕψῐ</i> (húpsĭ, “on high”) +&lrm; <i>ὀροφή</i> (orophḗ, “roof, ceiling”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὑψόσε</i> (hupsóse)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) upwards' aloft</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to lift high, raise up</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to elevate, exalt</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to represent in a lofty style</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́ψος</i> (hŭ́psos, “height”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hawksbeard (<i>Crepis neglecta</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὗς</i> (hûs, “swine”) +&lrm; <i>σέρις</i> (séris, “endive or chicory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Italian aster (<i>Aster amellus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὗς</i> (hûs, “swine”) +&lrm; <i>ὀφθαλμός</i> (ophthalmós, “eye”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὔμμε</i> (úmme)</li><li>apocopic form of <i>ὔμμι</i> (úmmi)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>Aeolic form of <i>ὑμεῖς</i> (humeîs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hyena</li></ol><p>From <i>ὗς</i> (hûs, “swine, pig”) +&lrm; <i>-αινα</i> (-aina, feminine suffix), for its bristly mane similar to that of a hog.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>some kind of crystalline stone</li><li>convex lens of crystal, used as a burning glass</li><li>glass</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, a Pre-Greek word, due to the variation "α/ε". According to Beekes, there is a similarity with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;glasą</i> (“glass”) and the first two syllables of Latin <i>sualiternicum</i> (“a kind of reddish amber”); a relation with the Germanic term is difficult to reconcile, however, due to the Germanic term having an acceptable internal (non-borrowing) derivation.</p><p>Alternatively, based on a presumed connection to Mycenaean Greek (we-a-re-ja, “glass”), a derivation from a pre-form <i>&#42;Ϝύαλ-ος</i> (&#42;Wúal-os), which was possibly borrowed from a Scythian language, has been proposed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pride</li><li>insolence</li><li>outrage</li></ol><p>Unknown; possibly Pre-Greek, given its unusual shape and the lack of a convincing Indo-European etymology. Other proposed explanations include:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) dropsy</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;úderos</i> (“abdomen, stomach”) and cognate with Sanskrit <i>उदर</i> (udara, “belly, abdomen”), Latin <i>uterus</i> (“womb”) and Old Prussian <i>weders</i> (“belly, stomach”). This word must be separated from <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”), since a full grade <i>ὕδερ-</i> is further unknown in Greek. The semantic shift from "belly" to "dropsy" is unproblematic, since Sanskrit <i>उदर</i> (udara) is also used in the sense of "pathologically swollen belly".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Theognostus' gives the definition as: <i>φήμη</i> (phḗmē, “prophetic voice, oracle”), <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song, ode”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ud-</i>, the same root of <i>αὐδή</i> (audḗ, “voice”), Sanskrit <i>वदति</i> (vadati, “to say, speak”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vaditi</i> (“to argue; scold”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὕδη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyde, Lydia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>truffle, earthnut</li></ol><p>The word has hesitantly been connected to <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) as "juicy", to <i>ὕω</i> (húō, “to rain”) as "rain plant" and to <i>ὗς</i> (hûs, “swine”) as "sow-bread". In view of the alternative forms, Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek word and links it with <i>ἴτον</i> (íton, “kind of mushroom”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sea serpent</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Hydra</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;údrā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;udréh₂</i>, the feminine form of <i>&#42;udrós</i>, from the root <i>&#42;wed-</i>. See also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀄𐀈𐀫</i> (u-do-ro). Cognate with English <i>otter</i> and Sanskrit <i>उद्र</i> (udrá), Czech <i>vydra</i>, Russian <i>вы́дра</i> (výdra), Lithuanian <i>ūdra</i>, Latin <i>lutra</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water organ, invented by Ctesibius</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) +&lrm; <i>αὐλός</i> (aulós, “pipe”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water serpent</li><li>A smaller water animal, such as a spider, fish, or lizard.</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;udrós</i>, from the root <i>&#42;wed-</i>. See also Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀄𐀈𐀫</i> (u-do-ro). Cognate with English <i>otter</i> and Sanskrit <i>उद्र</i> (udrá).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) edema; swelling</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́δωρ</i> (hŭ́dōr, “water”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>water</li><li>rainwater; rain</li><li>sweat</li><li>time (originally in reference to waterclocks)</li><li>(<i>loosely</i>) liquid</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;údōr</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wódr̥</i> (genitive <i>&#42;wednós</i> (“of water”)), with <i>ω</i> (ō) from the plural), from the root <i>&#42;wed-</i> (whence also <i>ῡ̔́ω</i> (hū́ō, “to water, to let rain, to rain”)).</p><p>Cognates include Latin <i>unda</i>, Sanskrit <i>उ॒दन्॑</i> (udán), Hittite <i>𒉿𒀀𒋻</i> (wa-a-tar /⁠wātar⁠/), Old Armenian <i>գետ</i> (get, “river”), Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐍄𐍉</i> (watō), Old Church Slavonic <i>вода</i> (voda), Old English <i>wæter</i> (English <i>water</i>), and Albanian <i>ujë</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idle talk, nonsense, prank</li></ol><p>No etymology. The word may contain the suffix "-θλο-", as in <i>ἄεθλος</i> (áethlos), but there is no good explanation for initial "ὑ-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wood; trees; forest</li><li>timber; firewood</li><li>stuff; material; substance</li><li>matter</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swel-, &#42;sel-</i> (“firewood, wood, beam”), and compared with Latin <i>silva</i> (“forest”), English <i>sill</i>, Latvian <i>sile</i> (“trough”). Beekes rejects this theory, as well as theories connecting the word to <i>ξῠ́λον</i> (xŭ́lon, “wood”), and proposes two derivations, based on the original sense likely being "firewood":</p><p>Possibly related are the Mycenaean Greek names <i>𐀄𐀨𐀍</i> (u-ra-jo, “Hulaios”) and <i>𐀄𐀩𐀄</i> (u-re-u, “Hul(l)eus”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>song, hymn, ode (generally in praise of the gods or heroes)</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. One theory derives the word from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sh₂em-</i> (“to sing”) (which has itself been suggested as related to <i>&#42;sh₂ey-</i> (“to bind, fetter”), as a "bond of sound"), comparing Hittite (išḫamai, “he sings”), Sanskrit <i>सामन्</i> (sā́man, “song”), Sanskrit <i>सुम्न</i> (sumná, “hymn”). Other conjectures include a derivation from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;syuH-mn-o-s</i>, interpreted as a "construction of songs", from <i>&#42;syewH-</i> (“to sew”), or a relation to the same root as <i>ῠ̔μήν</i> (hŭmḗn, “membrane; wedding-cry”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) out under, slipping away</li></ol><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) under, at the side of</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ὑπό</i> (hupó) and <i>ὑπαί</i> (hupaí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>under the sky, in the open air</li><li>(subsantivized,&#32;neuter) open enclosure</li><li>(subsantivized,&#32;neuter) the open air</li><li>(subsantivized,&#32;neuter) the public view</li><li>(subsantivized,&#32;neuter&#32;plural) the field, open country</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πο-</i> (hŭpo-, “sub-, hypo-”) +&lrm; <i>αἶθρος</i> (aîthros, “clear chill air”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>evacuation, defecation</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπᾰ́γω</i> (hupắgō, “to lead or bring under”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the view of reality itself (as opposed to a dream)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in reality; actually</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;súpr̥</i>, generalized from the zero-grade of an <i>r/n</i>-stem of <i>&#42;swep-</i> (“to sleep”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-r̥</i>, whence also <i>ῠ̔́πνος</i> (hŭ́pnos, “sleep”). Originally "sleep, dream"; the opposition to <i>ὄνᾰρ</i> (ónăr, “deceiving dream”), led to the meaning "true dream", whence "reality". The <i>r</i>-stem appears also in Hittite <i>𒋗𒌒𒈦𒉿𒀭𒍝</i> (su-up-pár-wa-an-za /⁠supparwanz(a)⁠/, “sleepy(?)”) and in Latin <i>sopor</i> (“deep sleep, catalepsy”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>existence, reality</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>logic</i>) existence</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) substantive</li><li>(<i>mathematics</i>) positive term</li></ol><li>substance</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπάρχω</i> (hupárkhō, “I begin, exist”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highest; uppermost</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) at the very top, lowest, furthest</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) last</li><li>(<i>of quality</i>) highest, best</li></ol></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the highest [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of something’</small>]</li><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) consul (a head of government of the Roman Republic)</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) hypatos (title conferred by the Byzantine Empire as an honorific)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔πό</i> (hŭpó) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰτος</i> (-ătos, superlative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ῠ̔́περθεν</i> (hŭ́perthen, “from above”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>from above</li></ol><p><i>ῠ̔περ-</i> (hŭper-) +&lrm; <i>-θεν</i> (-then)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pestle</li><li>club, cudgel</li><li>lever for stretching dislocated joints</li><li>(<i>zoology</i>) pupa of a geometrid moth</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπέρ</i> (hupér, “over, above”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a kind of lichen</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hypnos, the Greek mythological god of sleep</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́πνος</i> (hŭ́pnos, “sleep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sleep, slumber</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) death</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;húpnos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;supnós</i> (“sleep”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्वप्न</i> (svápna) and Albanian <i>gjumë</i> &lt; *źúpnos.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὕπνος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hypnos, the Greek mythological god of sleep</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́πνος</i> (hŭ́pnos, “sleep”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>viewed with suspicion or jealousy</li><li>suspecting, suspicious [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘of’</small>]</li><li>(nominalized,&#32;neuter) suspicion, jealousy</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπο-</i> (hupo-) + <i>ὀπ-</i> (op-, suppletive root of <i>ὁράω</i> (horáō)) + <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lying on one's back; supine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)up-ti-os</i>, from <i>&#42;upó</i> (“under, below”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shrewmouse (mammal of the family Soricidae)</li></ol><p>Judging by the -αξ ending, Pre-Greek. Latin <i>sorex</i> is probably from the same source (with a structure *sVr-Vk-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a jar for pickles or wine</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, a technical word of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A shrub, probably the kermes oak (<i>Quercus coccifera</i>)</li></ol><p>Pausanias calls the word Galatian but Furnée regards the noun as Pre-Greek because of the variants.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the latchet or eyelet of a sandal</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is clearly Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) rain</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕω</i> (húō, “to rain”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snare or gin of a birdcatcher</li><li>part of a spring or noose which slips down when touched</li><li>twisted strand</li><li>contrivance for starting a race</li></ol><p>Traditionally analysed as a compound of <i>ὗς</i> (hûs, “swine”) and <i>πλήσσω</i> (plḗssō, “to struck, smite”). In view of the variants with pre-nasalization, however, a Pre-Greek origin seems the most likely option.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A species of <i>Origanum</i></li><li>(<i>biblical</i>) used to translate Biblical Hebrew <i>אֵזוֹב</i> (ʾēzôḇ), <i>Origanum syriacum</i></li></ol><p>From some Semitic source. Compare Akkadian <i>𒍪𒌑𒁍</i> (/⁠zūpu⁠/), Jewish Palestinian Aramaic <i>אֵיזוֹבָא</i> (ʾēzôḇa), Hebrew <i>אֵזוֹב</i> (ʾēzṓḇ), Classical Syriac <i>ܙܘܦܐ</i> (zōṗā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>πάσσαλος κεράτινος</i> (pássalos kerátinos, literally “peg or penis made of horn”).</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, probably a Pre-Greek word, given the suffix <i>-αξ</i>. The word can hardly be separated from <i>ὑσσός</i> (hussós, “javelin”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῠ̔́στερος</i> (hŭ́steros, “latter; later; behind”) final, latest, last, hindmost</li></ol><p>Maybe from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;úd</i> (“upwards, away”), which would make it cognate with English <i>out</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>later [<i>with</i> genitive <small>‘than’</small>]</li></ol><p>Adverbial accusative of <i>ῠ̔́στερος</i> (hŭ́steros, “latter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>latter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of place</i>) latter, coming after, behind</li><li>(<i>of time</i>) next</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>later, too late</li><li>(<i>with genitive of object</i>) too late for</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) posterity</li></ol><li>(<i>of inferiority</i>)</li><li>(adverbial,&#32;the neuter ὕστερον is more often used than the standard ὑστέρως)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>of time</i>) later, afterwards</li><li>(<i>with prepositions</i>)</li></ol></ol></ol><p>The comparative form of an unattested adjective, with the superlative of <i>ὕστᾰτος</i> (hústătos), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;údteros</i> (“higher, outer”) from <i>&#42;úd</i> (“upwards, away”), which would make it cognate with English <i>out, outer</i>, Sanskrit <i>उत्तर</i> (úttara, “upper, higher”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>porcupine (<i>Hystrix cristata</i>)</li><li>something obtained from pigs, probably bristles</li><li>instrument of punishment, probably cat-o'-nine-tails</li></ol><p>Traditionally analyzed as <i>ὗς</i> (hûs, “swine”) +&lrm; <i>θρίξ</i> (thríx, “hair”), or alternatively with the first element being the base of <i>ῠ̔́στερος</i> (hŭ́steros, “latter”), thus "(animal) with rising hairs". However, Beekes cites the nasalization seen in the variant genitive plural form <i>ὑστρίγγων</i> (hustríngōn) – as if from <i>&#42;ὕστριγξ</i> (&#42;hústrinx) – as proof of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woven robe, web</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑφάζω</i> (hupházō, “to weave”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma, “nominal suffix”), the former from <i>ὑφαίνω</i> (huphaínō, “to weave”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, “verbal suffix”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in Arcadia</i>) mistletoe (<i>Viscum album</i>)</li></ol><p>Unexplained. Traditionally derived from <i>φῠ́ω</i> (phŭ́ō, “to grow”), with a prefix <i>ὑ-</i> (hu-), following the explanation in Hesychius, who described it as “what grows on firs”. But, since a full grade <i>φευ-</i> (pheu-) does not definitively occur in Greek, such a derivation is implausible. Nor is this word related to <i>σῦφᾰρ</i> (sûphăr, “old, wrinkled skin”). Likely Pre-Greek, given its meaning and the ending <i>-ᾰρ</i> (-ăr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>on high; aloft</li></ol><p>An old locative form in <i>&#42;-i</i> from the zero-grade of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ewps-</i> (“up, above”); cognate with Old Irish <i>úas</i> (“above”) and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vysь</i> (“height”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;figurative,&#32;poetic) the highest, the most high, the heavens</li></ol><p>From substantive use of adjective <i>ῠ̔́ψῐστον</i> (hŭ́psĭston, “highest, most high”), from the superlative of the adverb <i>ῠ̔́ψῐ</i> (hŭ́psĭ, “on high”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>highest, loftiest</li><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical,&#32;figurative,&#32;poetic,&#32;in the plural) highest, loftiest, most high; in heaven above; most heavenly</li></ol><p>Superlative of the adverb <i>ῠ̔́ψῐ</i> (hŭ́psĭ, “on high”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>height</li><li>top; summit</li><li>high position: grandeur</li><li>pride</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́ψῐ</i> (hŭ́psĭ, “aloft”), probably related to <i>ὑπέρ</i> (hupér, “above”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>elevation, height</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) exaltation of a heavenly body</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) exaltation</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑψόω</i> (hupsóō, “to lift, elevate”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to rain</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with accusative of place</i>) to rain on</li><li>(<i>with cognate accusative</i>)</li><li>(<i>with dative of mode</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to be drenched with rain</li><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to fall as rain</li></ol><p>From a <i>&#42;suh₂-</i>, which, according to the linguist Jay Jasonoff, is a metathesized form from the zero-grade of the root which is to be reconstructed as Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sh₂ew-</i> (“to pour, scatter, rain”). Cognate with Hittite (suhhai, “to pour out”), Tocharian B <i>suwaṃ</i> (“to rain”), and Albanian <i>shi</i> (“rain”). Possibly related to <i>Ὑάς</i> (Huás).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Attic</i>) name of the letter <i>Υ</i> &#47; <i>υ</i></li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) name of the letter <i>Υ</i> &#47; <i>υ</i></li></ol><p>To distinguish the letter from <i>οι</i> (oi), which had become identical in pronunciation. Displaced earlier <i>ὖ</i> (û).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) alternative form of <i>ὖ</i> (û, “upsilon”, name of the letter υ)</li></ol><p>From the aspirated long version of the vowel sound it was used to represent.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig; swine; hog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hūs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suH-</i> (“swine”). Compare Latin <i>sūs</i>, English <i>swine</i>, and <i>sow</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) The aspirated name for the letter υ (upsilon) in the Ancient Greek alphabet, used in rare phonetic contexts to distinguish it from the smooth-breathing form.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hump, a camel's rounded mass</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mud, slime</li></ol><p>Unknown. Frisk suggests that this word might have arisen from <i>ῑ̓λῡ́ς</i> (īlū́s, “mud, slime”), by way of contamination with <i>ῡ̔λῐ́ζω</i> (hūlĭ́zō, “to filter, strain”). However, Beekes does not find this convincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pig; swine; hog</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hūs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;suH-</i> (“swine”). Compare Latin <i>sūs</i>, English <i>swine</i>, and <i>sow</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyampolis, Phocis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) one of the Hyades</li></ol><p>From <i>ῡ̔́ω</i> (hū́ō) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ἐλέᾱ</i> (Eléā, “Hyele or Elea, Magna Graecia; Velia, Campania, Italy”)</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Velia</i>, from Ancient Greek <i>Ἐλέᾱ</i> (Eléā), from <i>Ϝέλα</i> (Wéla).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyacinth as a woman's name; Hyacintha</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῠ̔ᾰ́κινθος</i> (Hŭắkinthos) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hybadae, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian, equivalent to English Hydarnes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎻𐎡𐎭𐎼𐎴</i> (vi-i-d-r-n /⁠Vidr̥naʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hydaspes, Jhelum (a river in Pakistan and India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>वितस्ता</i> (vitastā). Proposed to have been borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Vidāspa-</i> (literally “caring for horses”), from <i>&#42;vīd-</i> (“to take care of”) + <i>𐎠𐎿𐎱</i> (a-s-p, “horse”) to explain the development <i>-sp-</i> instead of <i>-st-</i>, but this has no basis in any attested names for the river.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hydroates, Ravi (a river in Pakistan and India)</li></ol><p>Unknown; perhaps borrowed from Sanskrit <i>इरावती</i> (irāvatī).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hydruntum, Otranto (a city in southeastern Italy)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὕδωρ</i> (húdōr, “water”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ous, -ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hymees, from Old Persian, son-in-law of king Darius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Humāyah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hymaes, from Old Persian, son-in-law of king Darius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Humāyah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hymettus, an important mountain of Attica famous for its honey and marble</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hypacaris, a river of Scythia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hypata; Ypati, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hyperbolus</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-) +&lrm; <i>βάλλω</i> (bállō, “to throw”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a female given name, equivalent to English Hypatia</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́πᾰτος</i> (hŭ́pătos, “highest, best”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hyperanthes, from Old Persian, son of Darius and brother of Xerxes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Hubā̆ranta</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hyperenor, a Trojan slain by Menelaus</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, “over, beyond”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyperion</li><li>son of Hyperion, Helios</li></ol><p>Originally a comparative adjective, from <i>ὑπέρ</i> (hupér, “above”) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn, “-er”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyperberetaios, the name of a Macedonian month corresponding to September</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyperborei, a people supposed to live in the extreme north</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, over, beyond) +&lrm; <i>βόρειος</i> (bóreios, “northern”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>In particular, Hypereides or Hyperides, an Athenian logographer</li></ol><p>From <i>ὑπερ-</i> (huper-, “above”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) Hyperesia, Achaea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyria, Boeotia, Greece</li><li>Hyria; Oria, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyrcania</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὑρκᾰνός</i> (Hurkănós) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā), from Proto-Iranian. Compare Old Persian <i>𐎺𐎼𐎣𐎠𐎴</i> (Varkāna).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyrmine, Elis, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hyroeades, from Old Persian, a Persian soldier</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Vīrayauda</i> (literally “he who fights warriors”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>son of Hyrtacus, Hyrtacides (<i>surname of Asius</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὕρτακος</i> (Húrtakos) +&lrm; <i>-ίδης</i> (-ídēs, patronymic suffix)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hysiae</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name from Old Persian and Avestan, equivalent to English Hystaspes</li></ol><p>From Old Persian <i>𐎻𐏁𐎫𐎠𐎿𐎱</i> (vi-š-t-a-s-p /⁠Vištāspaʰ⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Hystaechmas, from Old Median, a Persian mentioned by Aeschylus</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Median <i>&#42;Vistaxma</i> (literally “having far-reaching power”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Hypsicles</li></ol><p>From <i>ῠ̔́ψῐ</i> (hŭ́psĭ, “on high, aloft”) +&lrm; <i>-κλῆς</i> (-klês, “fame”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hypsipyle (Lemnian queen)</li><li>synonym of <i>Λῆμνος</i> (Lêmnos) (Lemnos&#58; an island in the northeastern Aegaean Sea)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>theonym</i>) A mother goddess of the Earth and fertility, venerated in Sicily by the Sicels, depicted seated on a throne, flanked by a paredra figure (male or female) and two lions; often associated with Demeter or Potnia Theron.</li><li>(<i>toponym</i>) The name of three ancient cities of Sicily, named after the goddess</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hybla Gereatis, one of the ancient city of Sicily, named after the goddess Hybla, only reported by Thucydides.</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὕβλα</i> (Húbla), <i>ἡ</i> (hē), <i>Γελεᾶτις</i> (Geleâtis). This latter is of uncertain origin, it comes from one of this:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hybla Heraea, one of the ancient city of Sicily, named after the goddess Hybla; probably located in the vicinity of today's Ragusa.</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὕβλα</i> (Húbla) and <i>Ἡραία</i> (Hēraía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Ὕβλᾱ</i> (Húblā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Sicel hero</i>) A Siculian chief of south-eastern Sicily, who founded the Hyblaean Megara.</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὕβλα</i> (Húbla, “Hybla”) +&lrm; <i>-ων</i> (-ōn, possessive suffix). The suffix derives from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;-Hō</i>, dubbed "Hoffmann's suffix", meaning "one who possesses X".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hydissa, Caria, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyettus, Boeotia, Greece</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hyettus, the mythological founder of this city</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyccara; Carini, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hylas</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hyllus</li><li>(<i>Illyrian king</i>) Hyllus</li></ol><p>Imported from Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hypaepa, Lydia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Hypanis; Southern Bug, Ukraine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Hypius, Bithynia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyrgis, a river of Sarmatia, probably the Donets</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hyrtacus, a comrade of King Priam of Troy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hysporus, an Indian river</li></ol><p>From <i>ὗς</i> (hûs, “swine”) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “passage, ford”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyssus, Pontus, Turkey</li><li>River Hyssus; Kara Dere, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hyphasis, Beas (a river in Pakistan and India)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Sanskrit <i>विपाशा</i> (vipāśā), <i>विपाश्</i> (vípāś).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>River Hypsarnus, Boeotia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Hypsas; river Belice, Sicily, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>κώμη</i> (kṓmē, “village”), <i>φάλαγγος τό ἔσχατον</i> (phálangos tó éskhaton), <i>ἄκρον</i> (ákron, “peak, extremity”).</li></ol><p>Related to <i>ὠβά</i> (ōbá, “Spartan tribe”) and <i>οἴη</i> (oíē, “village”), but further details are obscure.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pangs or throes of childbirth (mostly in plural)</li><li>that which is born amid throes, child</li><li>travail, anguish (also of love)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>ἀκτίς</i> (aktís), <i>γλωχίς</i> (glōkhís) and <i>δελφίς</i> (delphís) but the root is unclear. The word has been connected with <i>ὀδύνη</i> (odúnē, “pain of body”) but the long initial vowel has not been explained yet. Van Beek suggests a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ed-</i> (“to bite”). Alternatively, the word may be of Pre-Greek origin, because of the suffix "-ῖν-".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to push</li></ol><p>Possible Greek relations, even if ruled out by Beekes as highly unlikely, are the verbal present form <i>ἔθει</i> (éthei), the participle <i>ἔθων</i> (éthōn, “damaging, plaguing; pushing, tossing (?)”) and the nouns <i>ἔθρις</i> (éthris, “castrated ram”), <i>ἔθειρα</i> (étheira, “horses' hair, mane”). The lemma could be an old lengthened grade of one of the underlying roots. Compare <i>ὄθομαι</i> (óthomai, “to show consideration for others, fight shy of”), only used with a negative particle, <i>ὄθη</i> (óthē, “worry, care, fear, consideration”) and <i>νωθρός</i> (nōthrós, “sluggish, slothful”).</p><p>Taking in account Avestan <i>𐬬𐬁𐬛𐬁𐬌𐬌𐬋𐬌𐬝</i> (vādāiiōit̰, “he may push back”) and Sanskrit <i>अवधीत्</i> (ávadhīt, “he killed”) (root <i>वध्</i> (vadh, “to kill, destroy (a man's ability to procreate)”)), as well as Hittite (ḫuett-<sup>tta(ri)</sup>, “to draw, pull”), Beekes constructed a root Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂wedʰh₁-</i> (“to push away, slay”), whence an iterative <i>&#42;h₂wodʰh₁-éye-ti</i>. Mark that also <i>ἔθρις</i> (éthris) seems to be cognate to Sanskrit <i>वध्रि</i> (vádhri, “castrated”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of basil</li></ol><p>From <i>ὤκιμον</i> (ṓkimon, “basil”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>ocean</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὠκεανός</i> (Ōkeanós).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὠκεανός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oceanus, a Greek water god usually said to be a Titan, one of the sons of Uranus and Gaia.</li><li>The great freshwater river thought to encompass the world</li><li>The great saline outer sea (the Atlantic), as opposed to the Mediterranean.</li></ol><p>Uncertain, though generally acknowledged to be a non-Indo-European substrate loan, with Beekes noting that the concept of a "world-river" is not native to Indo-European mythology. A possible relationship with the Semitic root <i>&#42;ʕ-w-g</i> (“to be crooked, tortuous”) (whence Arabic عِوَج (ʕiwaj, “crookedness”), Hebrew <i>עוּגָה</i> (ugá, “cake”)) has been discussed. Another possibility includes a derivation from unattested Sumerian <i>&#42;A-ki-an(u)</i> (Water of the Land and Heaven).</p><p>Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek form <i>&#42;ūkʲān-</i>. Michael Janda has reminded the scientific community of an earlier comparison with the Vedic dragon Vṛtra's attribute <i>āśáyāna-</i> “lying on [the waters]”, claiming it to be an exact phonetical transposition of the Greek word, both stemming from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ō-ḱey-m̥&#91;h₁&#93;no-</i> (“lying on”), related to <i>κεῖμαι</i> (keîmai, “to lie”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>catchfly (<i>Silene gallica</i>)</li><li><i>Cardopatium corybosum</i></li></ol><p>Neuter form of <i>ὠκῐμοειδής</i> (ōkĭmoeidḗs, “like basil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swift running</li><li>quick growing</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠκῠ́ς</i> (ōkŭ́s, “swift, fast”) +&lrm; <i>θοός</i> (thoós, “swift, rapid”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>quick-traveling</li></ol><p><i>ὠκύς</i> (ōkús, “swift, quick”) +&lrm; <i>πόρος</i> (póros, “passage”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>fleetfooted</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠκῠ́ς</i> (ōkŭ́s, “swift; fast”) +&lrm; <i>πούς</i> (poús, “foot”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swift-winged</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ὠκύς</i> (ōkús, “swift”) +&lrm; <i>πτερόν</i> (pterón, “wing”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os). Beekes compares the term to Latin <i>accipiter</i> (“hawk”) and suggests a possible pre-form Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;HHḱu-petro-</i> (“fast flying”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>swift flowing</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠκῠ́ς</i> (ōkŭ́s, “swift, fast”) +&lrm; <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;poetic</i>) fast, swift, quick</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁oh₁ḱús</i> (“quick, swift”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>आशु</i> (āśú) and Latin <i>ocior</i>, and possibly related to <i>ἵππος</i> (híppos, “horse”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) point of the elbow</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠλένη</i> (ōlénē, “elbow”) +&lrm; <i>κρᾱνίον</i> (krāníon, “head”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) elbow, lower arm</li><li>mat, mattress (=Latin <i>torus</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ōlenā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁eh₃l(e)n-</i>, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Heh₃l-</i>.</p><p>Cognates include Sanskrit <i>अरत्नि</i> (aratní), Latin <i>ulna</i>, Old Armenian <i>ողն</i> (ołn), <i>ուլն</i> (uln), Albanian <i>llërë</i> and Old English <i><b>eln</b>boga</i> (English <i><b>el</b>bow</i>). This reconstruction however remains uncertain.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>doze, short nap, short sleep</li><li>wrinkles beside the eyes, crow's feet</li></ol><p>Without a convincing etymology. Not related to Sanskrit <i>आली</i> (ālī, “stripe, line”). According to Beekes, clearly of Pre-Greek origin, in view of the nasalized suffix. The connection with <i>αὖλαξ</i> (aûlax, “furrow”) is certainly wrong.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to place raw pieces of meat on the sacrificial altar</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠμός</i> (ōmós, “raw”) +&lrm; <i>θετός</i> (thetós, “placed”) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoulder blade, scapula, also of animals</li></ol><p>From <i>ὦμος</i> (ômos, “shoulder”) +&lrm; <i>πλάτη</i> (plátē, “flat object”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>of olives</i>) pressed raw</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠμός</i> (ōmós, “raw”) +&lrm; <i>τρίβω</i> (tríbō, “to grind, pound”) +&lrm; <i>-ής</i> (-ḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>raw flax, used for lint</li><li>coarse linen towel or napkin</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠμός</i> (ōmós, “raw”) +&lrm; <i>λίνον</i> (línon, “flax”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>raw, crude, undressed</li><li>(<i>of flesh</i>) raw, uncooked</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) savage, fierce, cruel</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ōmós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃emós, &#42;h₂eh₃mós</i> (“raw, bitter, sour”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>आम</i> (āmá), Old Armenian <i>հում</i> (hum, “raw”), and Irish <i>amh</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to buy; to purchase</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in present and imperfect tenses</i>) to offer to buy; to deal for; to bargain or bid for</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to buy from</li><li>(<i>with genitive</i>) to buy for such a price</li><li>(<i>with dative</i>) to buy with</li></ol><li>to bid for public taxes or tolls</li><li>to buy off; to avert by giving hush money</li><li>to bribe; to pay off</li></ol><p>From <i>ὦνος</i> (ônos, “price”) +&lrm; <i>-έομαι</i> (-éomai).</p><p>The suppletive aorist <i>ἐπρῐάμην</i> (eprĭámēn) is from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;kʷreyh₂-</i> (“to buy”). Cognates include Welsh <i>prynu</i>, Old Irish <i>crenaid</i>, Sanskrit <i>क्री॒णाति॑</i> (krīṇā́ti) (root <i>क्री</i> (krī)), Old East Slavic <i>крити</i> (kriti).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Boeotian form of <i>ὠνόμηνεν</i> (ōnómēnen)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric and Boeotian form of <i>οὐρᾰνός</i> (ourănós, “sky”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a howling of wolves or dogs</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠρῡ́ομαι</i> (ōrū́omai) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>often of animals</i>) to shout, howl, roar</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of humans</i>) to produce loud and emotional cries</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to shout over, howl over</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃rewH-</i> (“to howl, roar”), albeit with initial long <i>ὠ-</i> (ō-) unexplained. Cognate with Latin <i>rūmor</i>, Latin <i>rāvis</i>, Latvian <i>rûkt</i>, Lithuanian <i>rū́kti</i>, and Sanskrit <i>रव॑</i> (ráva).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) thrusting, pushing</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠθέω</i> (ōthéō, “to push”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) young branch</li></ol><p>Hypothetically compared with Old Armenian <i>ազգ</i> (azg, “people, tribe”) and Persian آزغ (âzġ, “a thing lopped (as vine-prunings); slip, cutting, scion”), but this word is further isolated. The variation of the initial vowel rather shows that the word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”)</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”) (oblique stem <i>ὠτ-</i> (ōt-)) + <i>-άριον</i> (-árion, diminutive).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>diminutive of <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”) (<i>often without any particular diminutive meaning</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖς</i> (oûs) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>bustard (<i>Otis tarda</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs). Compare <i>ὦτος</i> (ôtos, “horned owl”), from the same stem.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>eavesdropper, listener</li><li>spy</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖς</i> (oûs) +&lrm; <i>ἀκούω</i> (akoúō) +&lrm; <i>-τής</i> (-tḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>wound, especially a fresh, open one</li><li>(<i>generally</i>) any kind of wound, whether recent or not</li><li>mark of a wound, scar</li></ol><p>The formation is unclear and the etymology controversial. Greek formations which are candidates to be cognate are <i>γατάλαι</i> (gatálai, “scars”), <i>βωτάζειν</i> (bōtázein, “to throw, cast”), <i>οὐτᾰ́ω</i> (outắō, “to wound”) and, less probable, <i>ἄτη</i> (átē, “damage, guilt, blindness”). From other languages Lithuanian <i>voti̇̀s</i> (“ulcer”) and Latvian <i>vâts</i> (“festering wound”) are compared. The word could well be Pre-Greek; this seems confirmed by the form <i>*ϝαταλ-</i> in the first gloss.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or for the ear</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós, “-ic”, adjectival suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>earpick</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”) +&lrm; <i>γλυφίς</i> (gluphís, “pen knife, chisel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>help, aid, succour (especially in war)</li><li>profit, advantage, benefit</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>source of gain or profit, service</li><li>(<i>and especially</i>) gain made in war, spoil, booty</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃bʰel-</i>, cognate with Old Armenian <i>աւելի</i> (aweli). Cf. <i>ὀφείλω</i> (opheílō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>helpful</li><li>profitable, beneficial</li></ol><p><i>ὠφελέω</i> (ōpheléō) +&lrm; <i>-ιμος</i> (-imos); compare <i>ὠφέλεια</i> (ōphéleia) and <i>ὀφείλω</i> (opheílō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to help</li><li>to be useful</li><li>to benefit</li></ol><p>From <i>ὄφελος</i> (óphelos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ὠφέλειᾰ</i> (ōphéleiă)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ὠφέλιμος</i> (ōphélimos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn pale</li></ol><p>From <i>ὤχρᾱ</i> (ṓkhrā, “yellow ochre, pale”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ω</i> (-ắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>pale, sallow, wan</li></ol><p>Unknown; the traditional connection with Sanskrit <i>व्याघ्र</i> (vyāghrá, “tiger”) (based on a shared semantic connection of warm color) is morphologically unconvincing. Wackernagel hesitatingly supposed that a neuter <i>ὦχρος</i> (ôkhros) replaced an older <i>&#42;ὦχος</i> (&#42;ôkhos), like <i>αἶσχος</i> (aîskhos) to <i>αἰσχρός</i> (aiskhrós) and <i>ψῦχος</i> (psûkhos) to <i>ψυχρός</i> (psukhrós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Ionic</i>) contraction of <i>ὁ</i> (ho) +&lrm; <i>ᾰ̓νήρ</i> (ănḗr)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>produced at the right season (ὥρα), seasonable, timely (<i>especially of fruits</i>)</li></ol><p><i>ὥρα</i> (hṓra) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>storage, granary</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>horreum</i> (“storage, granary”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>telling the hour</li></ol><p>From <i>ὡρολόγῐον</i> (hōrológĭon, “clock”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) mechanism for telling the time, clock</li><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Byzantine book of hours, a liturgical book containing the text of the daily Liturgy of the Hours of prayer as practiced in Christian churches observing the Byzantine liturgy</li></ol><p>From <i>ὥρα</i> (hṓra, “hour”) +&lrm; <i>λέγω</i> (légō, “to put in order”) +&lrm; <i>-ιον</i> (-ion).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an instrument for telling the time, clock, sundial</li><li>horoscope</li></ol><p>From <i>ὥρα</i> (hṓra, “hour”) +&lrm; <i>σκοπέω</i> (skopéō, “view”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ὡροσκοπεῖον</i> (hōroskopeîon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>caster of nativities, astrologer</li><li>(<i>astrology</i>) the sign or degree rising at the time of birth, ascendant</li></ol><p>From <i>ὥρᾱ</i> (hṓrā, “period of time”) +&lrm; <i>σκοπός</i> (skopós, “watcher”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>the introduction to similes</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>like as, as, just as</li><li>according as</li></ol><li>(<i>with adverbial clauses</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>parenthetically</i>) to qualify a general statement; as it seems</li><li>(<i>in elliptical phrases</i>) so far as....</li><li>(<i>attached to the object of a verb</i>) as</li></ol><li>(<i>to limit or augment the force of adverbs</i>)</li><li>in comparisons, <i>ὥς</i> (hṓs)... <i>ὡς</i> (hōs) or <i>ὡς</i> (hōs)... <i>ὣς</i> (hṑs): so... as...</li><li>thus, for instance</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(subordinating conjunction&#32;or&#32;complementizer,&#32;introducing dependent or subordinate clause)</li><li>(<i>with noun clauses</i>) introducing a clause expressing a fact: that (<i>with indicative or optative</i>)</li><li>(<i>final</i>) introducing a clause expressing an end or purpose: that, so that, in order that, so (<i>with subjunctive or optative</i>)</li><li>(<i>consequential</i>) introducing a clause expressing a result: so that</li><li>(<i>causal</i>) as, since, because</li><li>(<i>temporal</i>) when</li><li>(<i>modal</i>) how</li><li>(<i>local</i>) where</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;Hyós</td></tr></table></p><p>From the relative pronoun <i>ὅς</i> (hós) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;só</td></tr></table></p><p>From the demonstrative pronoun <i>ὁ</i> (ho) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs, adverbial suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as equally as possible</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>hosanna</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>הוֹשַׁע נָא</i> (hōšaʕ nɔʔ, “Please save”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>as if, as though</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>with optative</i>)</li><li>(ὡσεί τε,&#32;with subjunctive)</li><li>(ὡς εἰ or ὡς εἴ τε,&#32;with indicative)</li></ol><li>(in mere comparisons,&#32;as if, like, just)</li><li>(like ὡς,&#32;with numerals or measures of time and space) about</li></ol><p>From <i>ὡς</i> (hōs, “as”) + <i>εἰ</i> (ei, “if”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>expression of surprise, pain or joy</li></ol><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) alternative form of <i>ὦ</i> (ô, “O”, vocative particle, used before a noun in the vocative case when addressing someone or something)</li></ol><p>Expressive.</p><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ō</i>, influenced by the interjection when expressed in this manner.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoke of the wagon wheel</li></ol><p>Possibly from a Paleo-Balkan language, derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weǵʰ-</i> (“to ride”), for which compare Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wagnaz</i>, Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;wegnos</i> (“wagon, cart”). Alternatively akin to <i>ἄγω</i> (ágō, “to drive”), though the formation is unclear.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>basil (<i>Ocimum basilicum</i>)</li></ol><p>Natively connected with <i>ἀκή</i> (akḗ, “point”) and <i>ἄκαινα</i> (ákaina) from a root related to sharpness, but this does not explain the initial <i>ὤ-</i>. According to Beekes, the presence of similar words <i>ἄκινος</i> (ákinos, “wild basil”) and <i>ὤκινον</i> (ṓkinon, “clover”) shows Pre-Greek origin. Löw and Nöldeke explain with a borrowing from Aramaic, retained in Classical Syriac <i>ܚܘܟܐ</i> (ḥawkā, “basil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>kind of fodder-plant, perhaps clover</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, from the same Pre-Greek root of <i>ἄκινος</i> (ákinos, “wild basil”) and <i>ὤκιμον</i> (ṓkimon, “basil”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὤλετο</i> (ṓleto) used before &#47;h&#47;</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>game in which nuts were thrown into a ring</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) party, circle, sociable meeting</li></ol><p>Unexplained, perhaps Pre-Greek in view of the suffix.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>for sale</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠνέομαι</i> (ōnéomai) +&lrm; <i>-ῐος</i> (-ĭos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>care; concern</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;worā</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer-</i> (“to note; to sense”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Contraction</b></p><ol><li>contraction of <i>ὁ</i> (ho) +&lrm; <i>ἄριστος</i> (áristos), literally “the best”</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὤρνυτο</i> (ṓrnuto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>striped or piebald horses, including zebras</li></ol><p>Furnée compares <i>ὄρυξ</i> (órux, “gazelle”). The prenasalized suffix <i>-υγγ-</i> seemes to point to a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>yellow ochre</li></ol><p>From the same root as <i>ὠχρός</i> (ōkhrós, “pale yellow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(the phrase <i>εἰς ὦπα</i> (eis ôpa),&#32;in Homer and Hesiod) to the eye; (<i>possibly</i>) in the face</li><li>(<i>rarely in other forms</i>) eye</li></ol><p>With unexpected lengthening from earlier <i>ὄψ</i> (óps), from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ókʷs</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ókʷs</i>, from <i>&#42;h₃ekʷ-</i>.</p><p>The form <i>ὄσσε</i> (ósse, “eyes”) (from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;óťťe</i> &lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃ókʷih₁</i>) is the inherited dual of this noun; regular forms were created later by analogy.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any defined period of time</li><li>season</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) climate</li><li>year</li><li>time of day</li><li>hour</li><li>some specific time: right time; time for something</li><li>time of life: youth</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;yóh₁r̥</i> (“year, season”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὥρα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>one of the Horae or Hours in Greek mythology; a Hora or Hour</li></ol><p>From <i>ὥρᾱ</i> (hṓrā, “hour”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ὥρᾱ</i> (hṓrā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>like, as, even as, just as</li><li>(<i>to limit or modify an assertion</i>) as it were</li><li>(<i>rarely&#32;of time</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>so long as, however long</li><li>as soon as</li></ol><li>(<i>after a comparative</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὡς</i> (hōs, “as”) + <i>περ</i> (per, enclitic).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὥστε</i> (hṓste)</li></ol><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὥστε</i> (hṓste)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(chiefly&#32;Epic,&#32;used in similes, similarly to <i>ὡς</i> (hōs))</li><li>(<i>to mark the power or virtue by which one does a thing</i>) as</li></ol><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>(<i>to express the actual or intended result, the effect</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>mostly with the infinitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(after comparatives with <i>ἤ</i> (ḗ), when the possibility of consequence is denied)</li><li>(used with infinitive,&#32;of contingencies which may be more or less improbable)</li><li>(<i>sometimes</i>) on the condition that</li></ol><li>(with the indicative,&#32;to express the actual result with emphasis)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(at the beginning of a sentence,&#32;to mark a strong conclusion) therefore, consequently</li><li>(with the optative,&#32;to express a supposed consequence)</li></ol><li>(with the participle instead of the infinitive,&#32;by a sort of attraction,&#32;after a participle in the principal clause)</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὡς</i> (hōs, “as”) + <i>τε</i> (te, “and”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Particle</b></p><ol><li>O... (vocative particle, used before a noun in the vocative case when addressing someone or something)</li></ol><p><b>Interjection</b></p><ol><li>(<i>uncommon</i>) alternative form of <i>ὤ</i> (ṓ, expression of surprise, pain or joy)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;ō</i>. Cognate with Latin <i>ō̆</i>.</p><p>Expressive, influenced by the vocative particle when expressed in this manner.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to be; to exist</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of events</i>) to happen</li><li>to be the case</li></ol><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to live</li><li>(<i>copulative</i>) to be [<i>with</i> nominative <small>‘something, someone’</small>]</li><li>(third person,&#32;impersonal) it is possible [<i>with</i> infinitive <small>‘that...’</small>]</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ehmi</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁ésmi</i> (“to be, to exist”).</p><p>Cognate with Old English <i>eom</i> (whence English <i>am</i>), Latin <i>sum</i>, Sanskrit <i>अस्मि॑</i> (ásmi), Old Armenian <i>եմ</i> (em), and so on. More at <i>&#42;h₁es-</i> (“to be, exist”). Not to be confused with εἶμι (eîmi) (to go).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) omega, the name for the twenty-fourth letter Ω, ω of the Ancient Greek alphabet</li></ol><p>Literally, “big o”, in order to distinguish it from omicron <i>ο</i> (o), which had become identical in pronunciation. Displaced earlier <i>ὦ</i> (ô).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὦκα</i> (ôka)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>quickly, swiftly</li><li>immediately</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ὠκύς</i> (ōkús).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>αὖλᾰξ</i> (aûlăx)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shoulder with the upper arm; also of an animal</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>particularly</i>) shoulder (sometimes in contrast to the arm)</li><li>shoulder of a dress</li></ol><li>(<i>figurative</i>) parts below the top or head of anything, especially of the fork of a vine</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃émōs</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>अंस॑</i> (áṃsa), Latin <i>umerus</i>, Old Armenian <i>ուս</i> (us), and Gothic <i>𐌰𐌼𐍃</i> (ams).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>price (cost required to gain possession of something)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wosno-</i>, thematicization of <i>&#42;wósn̥</i> (“price”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>वस्न</i> (vasná, “price”), Latin <i>vēnum</i> (“something for sale”), and Old Armenian <i>գին</i> (gin, “price”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>long-eared owl (<i>Asio otus</i>)</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) dupe (easily deceived person)</li></ol><p>From <i>οὖς</i> (oûs, “ear”). Compare <i>ὠτίς</i> (ōtís, “bustard”), from the same stem.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ὦτος)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Otus</li></ol><p>Said to be from <i>ωτος</i> (ōtos, “infinite, insatiable”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>paleness, wanness, especially the pale hue of fear</li><li>Cyprus vetch (<i>Lathyrus ochrus</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠχρός</i> (ōkhrós, “pale, wan”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ὧδε</i> (hôde)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>demonstrative adverb In this manner, thus</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of a state or condition</i>) as it is</li><li>(<i>indicating something immediately to come</i>) as follows</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ὅδε</i> (hóde).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Doric form of <i>Ὠρῑ́ων</i> (Ōrī́ōn, “Orion”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Obaris, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Vahubarah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ogygia, the mythical island and abode of Calypso</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ionic form of <i>Ὠγῠγῐ́ᾱ</i> (Ōgŭgĭ́ā)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ocalea, Boeotia; Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a daughter of Oceanus; an Oceanid</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὠκεᾰνός</i> (Ōkeănós, “Oceanus”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) <i>Ocyalus</i>, one of the Phaeacians</li></ol><p>From <i>ὠκύαλος</i> (ōkúalos, “swift, violent”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olen</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Oliarus</i>; Antiparos, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Omanes, from Old Persian</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Vahumanah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Orion</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) the constellation Orion</li><li>a fabulous Indian bird</li></ol><p>Possibly from Akkadian <i>𒌋𒊒𒀭𒈾</i> (Uru-anna, “heaven's light”). A folk etymology advanced by Ovid interpreted the origin as <i>οὐρέω</i> (ouréō, “urinate”) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn, “son”) based on the circumstances of his birth, with the ﬁrst vowel modiﬁed later on for the sake of propriety, reflecting <i>ὤρα</i> (ṓra, “care, concern”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oreithyia or Orithyia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Oreus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ὠρεός</i> (Ōreós) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oreus, Euboea, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, equivalent to English Origen</li><li>in particular, the 3rd century <small>CE</small> ascetist, theologian and Church father Origen</li></ol><p>Probably ultimately from <i>Ὧρος</i> (Hôros, “Horus”) + <i>-γενής</i> (-genḗs, “born of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oricum</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ὡρομάζης</i> (Hōromázēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ὡρομάζης</i> (Hōromázēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li><li>Oromedon, a Cilician man and father of Syennesis</li><li>Oromedon, a mountain on the island of Kos</li></ol><p>From <i>ὤρᾱ</i> (ṓrā, “care, concern”) +&lrm; <i>μέδων</i> (médōn, “ruler, lord”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Oropus; Oropos, Attica, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ὡσηέ</i> (Hōsēé)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ostia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Ostia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Horatius; Horatio; Horace</li><li>a member of the Roman Horatius family</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Horātius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Zoroastrianism</i>) Ahura Mazda</li></ol><p>From Avestan <i>𐬀𐬵𐬎𐬭𐬀 𐬨𐬀𐬰𐬛𐬁</i> (ahura mazdā), likely through Parthian <i>𐭀𐭇𐭅𐭓𐭌𐭆𐭃</i> (ʾḥwrmzd).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>Ὡρομάζης</i> (Hōromázēs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hosea</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>הוֹשֵׁעַ</i> (hōšéaʕ, “Hosea”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Olenus</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Oxus; the river Amu Darya</li></ol><p>From Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;waxš-</i> (compare Avestan <i>𐬬𐬀𐬑𐬴</i> (vaxṣ̌)), the largest tributary of the river. Related to Sanskrit <i>वक्षु</i> (vakṣu). Compare the latterday toponym of Vakhsh.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ochus, also known as Artaxerxes III</li><li>Ochus, son of Darius III</li><li>river Ochus, now the Panj River</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Egyptian mythology</i>) Horus (an Egyptian god)</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./e784f985.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="G5" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (ḥr, “Horus”).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ἀείδω</i> (aeídō, “to sing”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>music</i>) song, lyric ode, hymn</li></ol><p>From <i>ἀείδω</i> (aeídō, “to sing”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hades (Greek god)</li><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Hades (an a mythological location, the underworld in Ancient Greek religion)</li><li>(Koine,&#32;biblical) the Grave; Hell</li></ol><p>For <i>&#42;ἄϝις</i> (&#42;áwis, “unseen”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;n̥wids</i> (“unseen”) equivalent to <i>ἀ-</i> (a-, “un-”) + the root of <i>ἰδεῖν</i> (ideîn) + <i>-ς</i> (-s). The root nominal was remodeled into an ᾱ-stem. Compare <i>ἀϊδής</i> (aïdḗs, “invisible”), which looks very similar, but is most likely based on the etymon of the related σ-stem <i>εἶδος</i> (eîdos).</p><p>Aspiration arose in Attic under the influence of the definite article <i>ὁ</i> (ho).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>which way, where, whither</li><li>how, as</li><li>wherefore</li><li>insofar as</li><li>as... as possible (with a form of <i>δύναμαι</i> (dúnamai))</li></ol><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Pronoun</b></p><ol><li>(Epic,&#32;demonstrative) this</li><li>(<i>relative</i>) who, which, that</li><li>(<i>sometimes&#32;with article</i>) his; hers; its</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) his or her people, friends, family etc.</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) his or her possessions</li></ol></ol><p><b>Determiner</b></p><ol><li>(<i>reflexive</i>) his own; her own; its own</li></ol><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;Hyós</td></tr></table></p><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;yós</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hyós</i>, <i>&#42;Hyéh₂</i>, <i>&#42;Hyód</i> (“who, which”), from the relative stem <i>&#42;Hyo-</i>, possibly from the anaphoric stem <i>&#42;(h₁)i-, &#42;(h₁)e-</i>. Cognates include Sanskrit <i>यद्</i> (yás, yā́, yád), Avestan <i>𐬫𐬋</i> (yō), and Phrygian <i>ιος</i> (ios /⁠jos⁠/). See also <i>οἷος</i> (hoîos), <i>ὅσος</i> (hósos). Compare <i>ἑός</i> (heós).</p><p><table class="wikitable floatright PIE-word" style="text-align: center;"><tr><th>PIE word</th></tr><tr><td>&#42;swé</td></tr></table></p><p>Like <i>ἕ</i> (hé, “him”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;swé</i> (reflexive pronoun). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्व</i> (svá). See also <i>ἑός</i> (heós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small egg</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾠόν</i> (ōión, “egg”) +&lrm; <i>-άρῐον</i> (-árĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>the art of singing</li><li>song; ode</li><li>theme of a song</li><li>legend; tale; story</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) spell; incantation</li></ol><p>Contracted form of <i>ᾰ̓οιδή</i> (ăoidḗ), from <i>ᾰ̓είδω</i> (ăeídō, “to sing”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shaped like an egg, ovoid, oval</li></ol><p>Derived from <i>ᾠόν</i> (ōión, “egg”) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs, “-id”, adjective-forming suffix denoting likeness).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>egg</li><li>seed</li></ol><p>Inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;ōyyón</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₂ōwyóm</i> (“egg”), argued to have been a derivative of <i>&#42;h₂éwis</i> (“bird”), whence also <i>ᾱ̓ετός</i> (āetós, “eagle”) and <i>οἰωνός</i> (oiōnós, “bird of prey”). Cognates include Latin <i>ōvum</i>, Persian خایه (xâye) and Old English <i>ǣġ</i> (English <i>egg</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>egg-like</li><li>oval</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾠόν</i> (ōión, “egg”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>apocopic form of <i>ᾤχετο</i> (ōíkheto)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ῠ̔περῷον</i> (hŭperōîon)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>upper floor</li><li>attic</li></ol></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the Odeon or Odeum</li></ol><p>From <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐον</i> (-ĭon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rod, wand, stick</li><li>magic wand, scepter</li><li>shaft of a spear</li><li>stripe, strip, streak</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) line, verse</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;urb-</i>, with cognates such as Lithuanian <i>virbas</i>, Old Church Slavonic <i>врьба</i> (vrĭba) (Russian <i>верба</i> (verba)) and Latin <i>verbēnae</i>. Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>branch</li><li>(<i>of palms</i>) frond</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wréh₂ds</i> (“root”). Compare <i>ῥίζα</i> (rhíza). Cognate with Latin <i>rādīx</i> (“root”), Latin <i>rāmus</i> (“branch”), Old Norse <i>rót</i> (“root”) (English <i>root</i>), Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (waurts), Old English <i>wyrt</i> (“herb, plant”) (English <i>wort</i>), Old Irish <i>fren</i> (“root”), and possibly Tocharian B <i>witsako</i> (“root”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>ragged, tettered</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥάκος</i> (rhákos, “rag”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rag, tattered garment</li><li>strip of cloth</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) wrinkles, rents in the face</li><li>rag, remnant</li></ol><p>Clear morphological cognates lacking outside Greek. The traditional comparison with Sanskrit <i>वृश्चति</i> (vṛścati, “to hew, cleave”) is not accepted by Beekes. A connection with Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;wr̥ćšás</i> (“tree”) is highly uncertain. The word could well be Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pole for beating fruit trees, especially olive trees</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾱ́σσω</i> (rhā́ssō, “to beat; to smash; to thrust”) +&lrm; <i>-τρῐᾰ</i> (-trĭă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seam, hem</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>fastening of a bandage by sewing</li><li>thread</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) suture of a wound</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῥάπτω</i> (rháptō, “to sew”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a thorny bush or shrub</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) boxthorn (<i>Lycium europaeum</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) stone buckthorn (<i>Rhamnus graeca</i>, now <i>Rhamnus lycioides</i> subsp. <i>graeca</i>)</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) black buckthorn (<i>Rhamnus oleoides</i>, now <i>Rhamnus lycioides</i> subsp. <i>oleoides</i>)</li></ol><p>The word may belong with <i>ῥάβδος</i> (rhábdos, “rod, wand”) and cognates. According to Beekes, it is quite probably a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crooked beak of birds of prey, bill</li></ol><p>Next to this word, there are <i>ῥέμφος</i> (rhémphos, “mouth or nose”) and <i>ῥαμψός</i> (rhampsós). The words <i>ῥέμβομαι</i> (rhémbomai, “to turn round, roam”) and <i>ῥόμβος</i> (rhómbos, “wheel”) are phonetically comparable and semantically similar; confront further <i>ῥάβδος</i> (rhábdos, “rod, wand”) and <i>ῥομφαία</i> (rhomphaía, “Thracian sword”). Germanic words have been compared, like Middle Low German <i>wrimpen</i> (“to turn up one's nose”), but these comparisons are ungrounded. The variation <i>β/ψ</i> rather points to a Pre-Greek origin, as does the <i>α-</i>vocalism. Moreover, <i>ῥαιβός</i> (rhaibós, “crooked”) is probably a variant, showing prenasalization.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>pathology</i>) name of a skin affection</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥαντίζω</i> (rhantízō, “to be sprinkled”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>botany</i>) grape</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) berry</li><li>malmignatte (<i>Latrodectus tredecimguttatus</i>)</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) fingertips</li></ol><p>The word is a reminiscent of <i>ῥάματα</i> (rhámata, “bunch of grapes”), as well as of Latin <i>racēmus</i> (“bunch of grapes”), Sanskrit <i>रज॑नी</i> (rájanī, “grape; lac”), Persian رز (raz, “vine”) and Albanian <i>rrush</i> (“grape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a rap, knock, stroke, blow (with a rod or staff or scourge)</li><li>a slap (on the face), smack, blow (with the palm of the hand)</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) a strike, blow, slap in the face, smack in the face</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥαπίζω</i> (rhapízō, “to strike, thrash, cudgel, hit, slap”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sew</li></ol><p>Of unclear origin. Traditionally derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werb-</i> (“to bend, turn”), and compared with Latin <i>verber</i> (“lash, whip”), Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;warpą</i> (“warp”), but this is phonetically impossible. The connections with <i>ῥέπω</i> (rhépō, “to incline”) and <i>ῥέμβω</i> (rhémbō, “to turn in circles”) are not certain, and the traditionally compared Lithuanian <i>ver̃pti</i> (“to sew”) is likely not cognate, due to the discovery of a Hellenic cognate in Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀁𐀨𐀟𐀕𐀙</i> (e-ra-pe-me-na) and its initial <i>e-</i> precluding a relation.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>turnip</li></ol><p>A number of similar words for turnip exist in European languages, but inconsistencies lend to the idea of multiple borrowings, rather than a Proto-Indo-European origin. Compare Latin <i>rapus</i>, Old High German <i>ruoba</i>, Lithuanian <i>rópė</i>. Also see <i>ῥάφανος</i> (rháphanos, “cabbage”). Compare, however, Albanian <i>rrapë</i>, which supposedly comes from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)rā́ps</i> (“radish, rape”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>shower; sprinkling</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥαίνω</i> (rhaínō, “to sprinkle”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike, dash</li><li>(<i>of dancers</i>) to beat the ground, dance</li></ol><p>There is no certain etymology.</p><p>Since a consonant must have been lost before the initial <i>ῥ-</i> (rh-), a pre-form <i>&#42;ϝρᾱ́χ-ιω</i> (&#42;wrā́kh-iō) (compare <i>ῥᾱχῐ́ᾱ</i> (rhākhĭ́ā, “flood tide; roar of the breakers”)) can perhaps be identified with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;raziti</i> (“to smite, pound”), and derive from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreh₂ǵʰ-</i> (“to strike, pound”). The Slavic word, however, has also been connected with <i>&#42;rězati</i> (“to cut, slice”), which is related to <i>ῥήγνῡμῐ</i> (rhḗgnūmĭ, “to break”); these forms derive instead from a different, albeit superficially similar, root <i>&#42;wreh₁ǵ-</i>.</p><p>The semantically attractive connection with <i>ἀράσσω</i> (arássō, “to smite, dash”) would require a pre-form <i>&#42;warakʰ-</i>, but there is no indication of initial <i>ϝ-</i> (w-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cabbage (<i>Brassica cretica</i>)</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to other plant names, such as <i>λάχανον</i> (lákhanon), <i>πήγανον</i> (pḗganon) and <i>πύανος</i> (púanos). Since the word is widespread only in Europe, and since it has variant forms, it cannot be an inherited word from Indo-European, but must have been borrowed instead, or otherwise belong to a substrate. Latin <i>rāpum</i> (“turnip”), Old High German <i>ruoba</i> (“turnip, rape”) and Lithuanian <i>rope</i> (“turnip”) point to a pre-form <i>*rāp-</i>; beside this we find Old High German <i>raba</i> and Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rěpa</i> (“turnip”), which point to <i>*rēp-</i>. In Greek we find also <i>ῥάπυς</i> (rhápus) and <i>ῥάφυς</i> (rháphus, “turnip”). Since the variation "π/φ" and the suffix "-αν-" are evidently Pre-Greek features, the word may originally be of substrate stock; thence the European cognates cited above were borrowed.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative spelling of <i>ῥάπυς</i> (rhápus, “turnip”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>chine, lower part of the back</li><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) spine or backbone</li><li>anything ridged like a backbone:</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>ridge of a hill or mountain</li><li>bridge of the nose</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) midrib of a leaf</li><li>sharp projection on the middle of the shoulder blade</li><li>outer edge of the arm of the polypus</li></ol></ol><p>According to Beekes of Pre-Greek origin or related to Lithuanian <i>ražis</i> (“stubble”). Compare <i>ῥαχός</i> (rhakhós, “thorn hedge”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stitching together</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥάπτω</i> (rháptō, “to stitch, sew”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῥεῖᾰ</i> (rheîă)</li></ol><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ῥέα)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhea</li></ol><p>Of disputed origin, with several theories suggested and none widely accepted, such as a connection to <i>ἔρα</i> (éra, “ground”) (see <i>ἔραζε</i> (éraze)), <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “flow”, verb), or simply Pre-Greek/Minoan. Less likely, cognate with Sanskrit <i>रसा</i> (Rasā́) and Avestan <i>𐬭𐬀𐬢𐬵𐬁</i> (Raŋhā), names of a mythical river (for Proto-Indo-Iranian <i>&#42;HrasáH</i>), derived from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rṓs</i>; see <i>ἐρωή</i> (erōḗ) for this or a similar root.</p><p>More at Rhea § Etymology on Wikipedia.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Snoring, stertorous breathing.</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥέγκω</i> (rhénkō, “to snore; to wheeze from the lung”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os, nominal suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to snore</li><li>(<i>of horses</i>) to snort</li></ol><p>Of ultimately onomatopoeic origin, perhaps via a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sre(n)gʰ-</i> (“to snore”), whence also perhaps Old Irish <i>srennaid</i> (“to snore”), Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;snarkōn</i> (“to snore, snort”), though Beekes also suggests a possible Pre-Greek origin. See also <i>ῥύγχος</i> (rhúnkhos, “snout, beak”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is dyed</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥέζω</i> (rhézō, “to dye”) and the suffix <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῥεῖθρον</i> (rheîthron, “flow, stream”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to do, make</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to perform sacrifices</li><li>to do something to someone (<i>with two accusatives</i>)</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to act (<i>as opposed to speaking or being acted on</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wréǵ-ye-</i>, a <i>&#42;ye</i>-present from <i>&#42;wreǵ-</i>, a metathesis of <i>&#42;werǵ-</i>, the root of <i>ἔργον</i> (érgon) and <i>ἔρδω</i> (érdō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>usually&#32;in the plural</i>) limb, member</li><li>(<i>in the singular</i>) face, countenance</li></ol><p>Since the meaning "face, countenance", given as Aeolic by grammarians, is certain, we have to depart from this when explaining the word. Both an older Epic meaning "mouth" and "figure, body" seems possible (compare Latin <i>ōs</i> (“mouth; face”) and <i>faciēs</i> (“figure; face”)). An original meaning "nostril(s)" fits well, and the transition to "face, body" is unproblematic. The word has no convincing etymology. The lack of the <i>ϝ-</i> in Aeolic makes a connection with Sanskrit <i>वर्धते</i> (vardhate, “to grow, thrive”) difficult. Fraenkel's suggestion to connect <i>ῥίς</i> (rhís, “nose, snout”) and <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”) is neither morphologically nor semantically convincing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A wandering</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥέμβω</i> (rhémbō) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to turn in circles</li><li>(<i>middle voice</i>) to roam, rove about</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to be unsteady, act at random</li></ol></ol><p>Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wremb-</i>, a nasal-infixed form of <i>&#42;werb-</i> (“to turn, twist, bend”), from <i>&#42;wer-</i>, and compared with Middle Low German <i>wrimpen</i>, Lithuanian <i>reñgtis</i>, Latin <i>vertō</i> (“to turn, bend”), Old English <i>weorpan</i> (“to throw away”), English <i>versus</i>.</p><p>However, Beekes argues for a Pre-Greek origin instead, based on the strange structure of the supposed Indo-European root, as well as interchange in labials π/β/φ of the word's derivatives and relatives, and assigns a Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>On the other hand, Kroonen maintains an Indo-European origin, deriving the Greek from <i>&#42;wremgʷ-</i>, a nasal-infixed form of a root <i>&#42;wergʷ-</i> (“to bend, spin, warp”), comparing Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;werpaną</i> (“to throw, cast”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>)</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>of things</i>) to incline one way or the other</li><li>(<i>of contending parties</i>) to preponderate, prevail</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to be inclined</li><li>(<i>of duties, feelings</i>) to fall upon, to devolve upon</li><li>(<i>of events</i>) to fall, happen in a certain way</li></ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to make the scale incline one way or the other</li></ol><p>Uncertain. According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;werb-</i> (“to bend, turn”), and traditionally compared with English <i>wrap</i>, but this is phonetically impossible. Connections to <i>ῥᾰ́πτω</i> (rhắptō, “to sew”) (with a supposed common denominator meaning of "to twist together") are not certain. Lithuanian <i>ver̃pti</i> (“to spin”), another traditional comparandum, is likely not cognate; see <i>ῥᾰ́πτω</i> (rhắptō) for discussion.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to flow, stream, run, gush</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><li>to fall off, drop off</li><li>(<i>of molten objects</i>) to liquefy, run</li><li>to be in perpetual flux</li><li>(<i>of persons</i>) to be inclined to, given to</li><li>to leak</li><li>to have a flux (diarrhea-causing disease)</li><li>(<i>impersonal</i>)</li></ol><li>(transitive,&#32;rare) to let flow, pour</li><li>(transitive,&#32;with cognate accusative) to let run</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hréwō</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sréweti</i>, from <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow, stream”). Cognates include Sanskrit <i>स्रवति</i> (srávati), Old Church Slavonic <i>строуꙗ</i> (struja, “stream”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to break asunder, tear, rend, shatter</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to break through a line or body of men</li><li>(<i>post-Homeric</i>) to break into speech, speak out</li><li>to burst into tears</li></ol><li>(<i>passive voice</i>) to break, burst</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to burst forth</li><li>(<i>of ships</i>) to be wrecked</li><li>to be inscribed on</li></ol><li>(<i>intransitive</i>) to break, burst forth</li></ol><p>Uncertain. Probably from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreh₁ǵ-</i> (“to break”); compare Old Armenian <i>երգիծանեմ</i> (ergicanem, “to tear, dissect”), Lithuanian <i>rėžti</i>, and Old Church Slavonic <i>рѣзати</i> (rězati, “to cut, slaughter”). Also compare Latin <i>frangō</i> and Old English <i>brecan</i> (English <i>break</i>), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;bʰreg-</i>. Further compare Sanskrit <i>रुजति</i> (rujati, “break up, break open, shatter”) and Albanian <i>rrege</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheep, lamb</li></ol><p>From <i>ϝρήν</i> (wrḗn), the old nominative of <i>ϝάρνα</i> (wárna), <i>ϝαρνός</i> (warnós), etc., for which see <i>ἀρήν</i> (arḗn); compare <i>πολύρρην</i> (polúrrhēn), <i>ῥᾶνα</i> (rhâna), and <i>τρανόν</i> (tranón), the last perhaps from <i>ϝρᾶνον</i> (wrânon).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(Koine,&#32;Byzantine) rex (king)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>rēx</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>orator; public speaker</li><li>politician; statesman</li><li>attorney; court advocate</li><li>judge</li><li>rhetorician</li></ol><p>Agent noun formation from <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “to speak”), synchronically analyzable as <i>ῥή-</i> (rhḗ-) +&lrm; <i>-τωρ</i> (-tōr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>frostier, colder</li><li>more horrible</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῖγος</i> (rhîgos) +&lrm; <i>-ίων</i> (-íōn, comparative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>root (of a plant)</li><li>source</li><li>base, foundation</li><li>stock, family, race</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wréh₂ds</i> (“root”). Compare <i>ῥάδιξ</i> (rhádix). Cognate with Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀹𐀪𐀼</i> (wi-ri-za), Latin <i>radix</i> (“root”), Latin <i>ramus</i> (“branch”), Old Norse <i>rót</i> (“root”) (English <i>root</i>), Gothic <i>𐍅𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍄𐍃</i> (waurts), Old English <i>wyrt</i> (“herb, plant”) (English <i>wort</i>), Old Irish <i>fren</i> (“root”), and possibly Tocharian B <i>witsako</i> (“root”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made from a root</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥίζα</i> (rhíza, “root”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ethiopian animal resembling an elephant</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roots of a plant</li><li>element</li><li>stem, race</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥιζόω</i> (rhizóō) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma). See Ancient Greek <i>ῥῐ́ζᾰ</i> (rhĭ́ză).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>fast, quickly, rapidly, nimbly, in an agile manner</li></ol><p>A formation morphologically similar to that of the unrelated <i>τάχα</i> (tákha, “quickly”), though with a root of uncertain origin. Possibly from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wrenkʷʰa</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wrengʷʰ-</i> (“to squeeze, wring”), whence Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;wringaną</i> (“to squeeze, wring”) and Lithuanian <i>rangùs</i> (“flexible”), <i>reñgti</i> (“to prepare”). Compare also German <i>gering</i> (“little, low”), which may be from the same root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>file, rasp</li><li>angel shark (<i>Squatina squatina</i>), whose skin was used for polishing wood and marble</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>peak, summit, any jutting part of a mountain</li><li>headland</li><li>bay formed by a foreland</li></ol><p>The word is isolated within Hellenic and has no certain etymology. If it originally had an initial "ϝ-", it would recall <i>βρία</i> (bría, “town”) and perhaps also Tocharian B <i>riye</i> (“town”). The connections with Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;risiz</i> (“giant”), Latin <i>verrūca</i> (“wart”), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;vьrxъ</i> (“top, peak”) and Sanskrit <i>वर्ष्मन्</i> (varṣman, “height, top”) are no better. As an alternative to all these problematic suggestions, Beekes considers the possibility that the word is of Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>Attested in Mycenaean Greek as <i>𐀪𐀍</i> (ri-jo).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ῥίον)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhium; Rio, Achaea, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥίον</i> (rhíon, “headland, promontory”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw, cast, hurl</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw or toss around</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw out of a place</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to cast or throw off or away (arms, clothes, and so on)</li><li>(figuratively,&#32;transitive) to hurl (words); to waste them</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw down</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Traditionally derived from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreyp-</i>, <i>&#42;wriHp-</i> and compared with Proto-West Germanic <i>&#42;wrīban</i> (“to rub, wipe”); however, Beekes notes a non-trivial semantic gap between the Hellenic and Germanic terms, as well as the Indo-European proto-form <i>&#42;wriHp-</i> having a rather strange structure, and prefers to derive the Greek word from Pre-Greek. Possibly connected to <i>ῥίψ</i> (rhíps, “mat, wickerwork”).</p><p>The present tense system includes -τ-, a progressive aspect marker.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) nose, snout</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) nostrils</li></ol><li>(<i>probably</i>) brow of a hill, projecting spur of land</li></ol><p>Unknown. Possibly a Pre-Greek borrowing.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>coffer; chest (especially for plate or money)</li></ol><p>Probably a loanword. According to Donatus, it is of Phrygian origin, a path followed by Thumb, who considers Proto-Celtic to be the ultimate source, comparing Old Irish <i>rúsc</i> (“[basket made of] bark [of a tree]”). The word would have reached Greek from Galatian, through Phrygian, because of the change <i>&#42;u</i> &gt; <i>&#42;i</i>. Beekes doesn't dismiss a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>mat; wickerwork; wattle; plaited work of osiers or rushes</li></ol><p>No agreement outside Greek. Traditionally connected with <i>ῥίπτω</i> (rhíptō, “to throw; to hurl”) on the basis of a shared underlying sense of "turn; wind". However, this connection fails due to the weak semantics.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinkle</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>)</li><li>to sprinkle water</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;uh₁r-</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>ūrīnor</i>, Old Armenian <i>գայռ</i> (gayṙ), and Sanskrit <i>वार्</i> (vā́r), <i>वारि</i> (vā́ri).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(biblical,&#32;Koine) O my Master</li></ol><p>From Aramaic <i>רבי</i> (“rabbi, spiritual teacher”). Cf. Hebrew <i>רַבִּי</i> (rabī́, “my master”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ῥᾰββῑ́</i> (rhăbbī́)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>רַבּוֹנִי</i> (rabbōnī, “my master”), alternative form of <i>רַבִּי</i> (rabī́).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ῥαββί</i> (rhabbí)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Hebrew <i>רבוני</i> (rabboni, “my master”), alternative form of <i>רבי</i> (rabbi).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>divination by means of a rod; rhabdomancy</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥάβδος</i> (rhábdos, “rod”) + <i>μαντεία</i> (manteía, “divination”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who carries a rod or staff of office</li><li>judge, umpire at a contest</li><li>magistrate's attendant, staff-bearer, beadle</li><li>(<i>at Rome</i>) lictor who carried the fasces</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾰ́βδος</i> (rhắbdos, “rod, staff”) +&lrm; <i>ἔχω</i> (ékhō, “to bear, carry, bring”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fissure in soil, chink, crevice</li><li>crack or chap of the skin</li><li>(<i>pathology</i>) fistula and haemorrhoids</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥήγνῡμι</i> (rhḗgnūmi, “to break asunder, shatter”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>black, strong-smelling petroleum found near Susa</li></ol><p>Probably an Iranian loanword.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>slender, taper, thin</li><li>soft, tender, mobile</li></ol><p>The formation is like in <i>πυκινός</i> (pukinós) but the basis is unknown. Some connect this word with <i>ῥάδαμνος</i> (rhádamnos, “branch, twig”), while Lobeck also connects <i>ῥαδές</i> (rhadés, “bent to both sides”). Beekes confront the adjective with <i>ῥοδανός</i> (rhodanós, “wavering, flickering”) and concludes that both words are from Pre-Greek, in view of the variation <i>ῥαδ-&#47;ῥοδ-</i> (rhad-&#47;rhod-).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>drop (of liquids)</li><li>grain; bit (of solids)</li><li>spot; speckle</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, the word is from Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix, found also in <i>λᾶῐ̈γξ</i> (lâĭ̈nx), <i>πῠ́λῐγξ</i> (pŭ́lĭnx) and <i>στροφᾰ́λῐγξ</i> (strophắlĭnx).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>lightheartedness, cheerfulness</li><li>relaxation</li><li>thoughtlessness, carelessness</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾴθῡμος</i> (rhāíthūmos, “lighthearted”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, bent</li></ol><p>Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>hammer, smasher</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) destroyer</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥαίω</i> (rhaíō, “to break, shatter”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>idiot, fool, blockhead, good-for-nothing</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Aramaic <i>ריקה</i>, <i>ריקא</i> (rēqā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of suture-thread</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥάμμα</i> (rhámma, “seam, hem”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a drop</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>semen</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) drop; spot</li></ol></ol><p>From <i>ῥαίνω</i> (rhaínō, “sprinkle”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to sprinke</li><li>to purify</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥαίνω</i> (rhaínō) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>speckled, spotted, dappled</li></ol><p>From the root of <i>ῥαίνω</i> (rhaínō, “to sprinkle”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strike with a stick, rod, cudgel; to flog, scourge, thrash</li><li>to slap, hit, smack, strike, smite (with the palm of the hand)</li></ol><p>Possibly from <i>ῥέπω</i> (rhépō, “to let fall, drop, decline, descend”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō); related to <i>ῤαπίς</i> (rapís), <i>ῥάβδος</i> (rhábdos, “rod, staff”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of radish</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥάφανος</i> (rháphanos, “cabbage, radish”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>seam</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>anatomy</i>) suture of the skull, the heart or other parts</li></ol><li>stitching, sewing</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾰ́πτω</i> (rhắptō, “to sew together, stitch”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē, action noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>needle</li><li>garfish, sea needle (<i>Belone belone</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥάπτω</i> (rháptō, “to sew”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐς</i> (-ĭs).</p><p>Compare typologically Latin <i>sūbula</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;šidlo</i> (&lt; Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;syewH-</i> + <i>&#42;-dʰlom</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>radish (<i>Raphanus raphanistrum</i> subsp. <i>sativus</i> syn. <i>Raphanus sativus</i>)</li><li>(with <b>ἀγρία</b>) <i>Raphanus raphanistrum</i></li></ol><p>From the same set of Proto-Indo-European roots meaning <i>turnip, radish</i> as <i>ῥάφυς</i> (rháphus), <i>ῥάπυς</i> (rhápus, “turnip”), though inconsistencies in the reflexes seem to point to heavy borrowing. Compare Latin <i>rāpum</i>, <i>rāpa</i>, <i>rāpistrum</i>, Old High German <i>ruoba</i> (German <i>Rübe</i>), Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;rěpa</i> (Old Church Slavonic <i>репа</i> (repa)), and Lithuanian <i>rópė</i>. Also see <i>ῥάφανος</i> (rháphanos, “cabbage”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(transitive,&#32;Aristophanes) to insert a radish up one's arse as a punishment, usually for adultery</li></ol><p><i>ῥᾰφᾰνῑ́ς</i> (rhăphănī́s, “radish”) +&lrm; <i>-όω</i> (-óō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A needlecase</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥαφίς</i> (rhaphís, “needle”) and <i>-θήκη</i> (-thḗkē, “case, box”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flood tide</li><li>the roar of the breakers</li><li>(<i>figurative</i>) the roar of a crowd</li><li>rocky shore or beach</li><li>alternative form of <i>ῥᾰ́χῐς</i> (rhắkhĭs, “backbone”)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wrā́kʰiā</i>, either a deverbal formation from <i>ῥᾱ́σσω</i> (rhā́ssō, “strike, dash”) or an abstract or collective formation from the unattested <i>&#42;ῥᾱχος</i> (&#42;rhākhos, “stroke, bump”) (which is from the same root as the former).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>in or of the spine</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥάχις</i> (rhákhis) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>thorny bush, briar, brushwood</li><li>thorn-hedge, wattled fence, palisade</li><li>(<i>botany</i>) twig, branch of the vine</li><li>(<i>at Troezen</i>) wild olive tree (<i>Olea europaea</i>)</li></ol><p>Clearly related to <i>ῥᾰ́χις</i> (rhắkhis, “spine, backbone”). Since <i>ϝρᾰχ-/ϝρᾱχ-</i> cannot be derived from an Indo-European root, the word might instead be of Pre-Greek origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>recitation of Epic poetry</li><li>Epic composition</li><li>rigmarole</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾰψῳδός</i> (rhăpsōidós, “performer of Epic poetry”) +&lrm; <i>-ία</i> (-ía).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhapsodist</li><li>performer (of a poem)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾰ́πτω</i> (rhắptō, “to sew”) +&lrm; <i>ᾠδή</i> (ōidḗ, “song”) +&lrm; <i>-ος</i> (-os).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Epic form of <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>roving, rolling</li><li>desultory, remiss</li><li>irregular (of periods)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥέμβω</i> (rhémbō, “to roam”) +&lrm; <i>-ώδης</i> (-ṓdēs, “like, full of”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>subject to a discharge or flux</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥεῦμα</i> (rheûma) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>ῥεῦμα</i> (rheûma, “liquid discharge, rheum”)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥεῦμα</i> (rheûma) +&lrm; <i>-ισμός</i> (-ismós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) easily, lightly</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾶ</i> (rhâ), of unknown origin.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) a flow, stream</li></ol><p>Contracted from <i>ῥέεθρον</i> (rhéethron), from <i>&#42;ῥέϝεθρον</i> (&#42;rhéwethron), from the stem of <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srew-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stream, flow, current</li><li>liquid discharge (<i>from the body</i>), rheum</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hréumə</i>, likely inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sréwmn̥</i>, from <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-mn̥</i>; equivalent to <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma). Compare the related Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srówmos</i>, whence also Old English <i>stream</i>. See also <i>ῥυθμός</i> (rhuthmós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flowing</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ῥᾴδῐος</i> (rhāídĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Epic</i>) surf, breakers (waves breaking into foam)</li><li>(<i>Epic</i>) shore, beach</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥήγνυμι</i> (rhḗgnumi, “to break”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>spoken phrase, what was said</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>resin of the pine</li></ol><p>The suffixes <i>-ῑνος</i> (-īnos) and <i>-ῑνη</i> (-īnē) are found both with inherited and with Pre-Greek substrate words. Since there are no good cognates, however, the word is probably Pre-Greek. The comparison with Latin <i>rasis</i> (“kind of raw pitch pulverized to dust”) is uncertain. Latin <i>rēsīna</i> gives proof of a dialectal Greek by-form <i>&#42;ῥησίνα</i> (&#42;rhēsína); the variation attested by this form shows that this is a Pre-Greek word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>having the taste of resin</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥητίνη</i> (rhētínē, “pine resin”) +&lrm; <i>-ίτης</i> (-ítēs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>(<i>nominalized</i>) rhetoric</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>concerning public speaking or oration, rhetorical</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥήτωρ</i> (rhḗtōr, “public speaker”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐκός</i> (-ĭkós)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>spoken, stipulated</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥h₁tós</i>, from <i>&#42;werh₁-</i> (“to speak, say”). Equivalent to <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “I say”) +&lrm; <i>-τός</i> (-tós, verbal adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who throws convicts into the sea</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Epic and Ionic form of <i>ῥᾳδίως</i> (rhāidíōs)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to shudder, shiver</li></ol><p><i>ῥῖγος</i> (rhîgos) +&lrm; <i>-έω</i> (-éō)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or belonging to the root</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥίζα</i> (rhíza, “root”) +&lrm; <i>-ικός</i> (-ikós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shrivelled by cold, by age or by disease</li><li>shrunk, contracted</li><li>withered, crooked</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreyḱ-</i> (“to twist, weave, bind”). Cognate with Latin <i>rīca</i> (“veil”), English <i>wry</i>, German <i>reihen</i> (“to crimp”) and Ancient Greek <i>ῥοικός</i> (rhoikós, “bent, crooked”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>small file</li><li>sort of eyesalve</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῑ́νη</i> (rhī́nē, “file”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ρῐον</i> (-ắrĭon, diminutive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>hide-piercing, shield-piercing (epithet of Ares)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῑνός</i> (rhīnós, “hide; shield”) +&lrm; <i>τορός</i> (torós, “sharp, piercing”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Koine</i>) rhinoceros</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῑ́ς</i> (rhī́s, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>κέρας</i> (kéras, “horn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin, hide</li><li>shield made of animal hide</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>skin</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>skin of a man or a beast</li><li>leather</li></ol><li>hide</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>hide of a beast</li><li>shield</li></ol><li>mist (<i>in Illyrian</i>)</li></ol><p>According to Pokorny, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wer(H)-</i> (“to cut (off), tear”), see also Sanskrit <i>व्रण</i> (vraṇá, “wound, tearing”), Avestan <i>𐬓𐬀𐬭𐬀</i> (xᵛara, “wound, hurt”).</p><p>Borrowed from Illyrian. Possibly closely related to Albanian <i>re</i>&nbsp;/ <i>rê</i> (“clouds, mist”), from Proto-Albanian <i>&#42;rina</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>having a cut off nose</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῑ́ς</i> (rhī́s, “nose”) +&lrm; <i>τέμνω</i> (témnō, “cut”) +&lrm; <i>-τος</i> (-tos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>swing or force with which anything is thrown</li><li>(<i>of wind</i>) sweep, rush</li><li>flapping or buzzing of wings</li><li>quivering, twinkling light</li><li>(<i>in general</i>) any rapid movement</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥίπτω</i> (rhíptō, “to throw, cast, hurl”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>fan for raising the fire</li><li>fan used by ladies</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥίψ</i> (rhíps, “mat, wickerwork”) +&lrm; <i>-ις</i> (-is).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to throw to and fro, toss about or around, move back and forth or up and down</li><li>(intransitive&#32;or&#32;passive voice,&#32;also&#32;with reflexive pronoun) to toss about (in bed)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῑ́πτω</i> (rhī́ptō, “throw”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ζω</i> (-ắzō, frequentative suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>treasurer</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῐ́σκος</i> (rhĭ́skos, “coffer; chest”) +&lrm; <i>φῠ́λᾰξ</i> (phŭ́lăx, “guard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a river, a stream</li><li>(<i>philosophy</i>) a flux</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē), possibly inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hrowā́</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srowéh₂</i>, from the root <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow, stream”). Cognate with Lithuanian <i>sravà</i>, Latvian <i>strava</i>, and Sanskrit <i>&#42;स्रवा</i> (*sravā́) in such compounds as <i>गिरिस्रवा</i> (girisravā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flow, flux</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”) +&lrm; <i>-ίᾱ</i> (-íā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>woof or weft</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥοδανός</i> (rhodanós, “wavering, flickering”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rose-bush</li></ol><p><i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”) +&lrm; <i>-έα</i> (-éa, “tree-forming suffix”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to have a colour or scent like a rose</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to scent with rose</li><li>(<i>transitive</i>) to decorate a grave with roses</li></ol><p><i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ζω</i> (-ĭ́zō, denominative verb suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>wavering, flickering</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, related to <i>ῥαδινός</i> (rhadinós, “taper, slender”) and thus from Pre-Greek, in view of the variation <i>ῥαδ-&#47;ῥοδ-</i> (rhad-&#47;rhod-).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ῥοδανός)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhone, Rhône (a major river in modern Switzerland and France that flows from the Alps to the Mediterranean Sea)</li></ol><p>Of Celtic origin, from Gaulish <i>&#42;Rodonos</i>, <i>&#42;Rotonos</i>, from a Proto-Celtic root similar to <i>&#42;reteti</i> (“to flow, run, roll”). Lambert argues that this could have prefixed a second element akin to the root of the river name <i>Danube</i>, Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;dʰenh₂-</i> (“to set in motion; to flow”). Alternatively, possibly from a hypothetical Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;ɸro-dena-</i> (“to flow forth”), from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;pro-</i> (“toward, forward”) and the previously mentioned root.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a ceremony at which graves were decked with roses</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥοδίζω</i> (rhodízō, “I decorate with roses”) +&lrm; <i>-μός</i> (-mós, noun suffix), the former from <i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rosy-fingered</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”) +&lrm; <i>δάκτυλος</i> (dáktulos, “finger”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>oleander</li></ol><p><i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”) +&lrm; <i>δάφνη</i> (dáphnē, “laurel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rosebay, oleander (<i>Nerium oleander</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”) +&lrm; <i>δένδρον</i> (déndron, “tree”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rosy</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”) +&lrm; <i>-εις</i> (-eis, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rosy-armed</li></ol><p><i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”) +&lrm; <i>πῆχυς</i> (pêkhus, “forearm”)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>corn poppy (<i>Papaver rhoeas</i>)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥόα</i> (rhóa, “pomegranate”) +&lrm; <i>-ᾰ́ς</i> (-ắs). Not related to <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>crooked, bent</li><li>bow-legged</li></ol><p>An <i>-ός</i> (-ós, deverbal adjective suffix) adjective from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreyḱ-</i> (“to twist, weave, bind”). Cognate with Latin <i>rīca</i> (“veil”), English <i>wry</i>, German <i>reihen</i> (“to crimp”) and Ancient Greek <i>ῥικνός</i> (rhiknós, “bent, crooked”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>rhombus-shaped, rhomboidal</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥόμβος</i> (rhómbos) +&lrm; <i>-ειδής</i> (-eidḗs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>large, broad sword used by the Thracians</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Thracian, ultimately possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;Hrewp-</i> (“to break”), likely closely related to Albanian <i>rrufé</i> (“lightning”). However the ending in <i>-αία</i> (-aía) is also found in many Greek terms such as <i>κεραία</i> (keraía), <i>καμιναία</i> (kaminaía), <i>αὐλαία</i> (aulaía). Formally closer to <i>ῥομφεῖς</i> (rhompheîs, “straps by which shoes are stitched”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>inclination downwards</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) the turn of the scale, critical moment</li></ol><li>downward momentum</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥέπω</i> (rhépō, “to incline”) +&lrm; <i>-η</i> (-ē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to slurp, gulp down</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to drain dry, empty</li></ol><li>to live on slops</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hropʰéyō</i>, causative of Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srebʰ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>any rushing noise or motion</li><li>whirring of wings</li><li>rushing of the wind</li></ol><p>The word shares the suffix with <i>κέλαδος</i> (kélados), <i>ἄραδος</i> (árados), <i>χρόμαδος</i> (khrómados), <i>φλοῖσβος</i> (phloîsbos) and <i>ὅμαδος</i> (hómados). Since the word strongly recalls <i>ῥοῖζος</i> (rhoîzos, “rushing, whistling”) and <i>ῥυβδέω</i> (rhubdéō, “to slurp in”), the word is probably Pre-Greek. Moreover, the <i>-βδ-</i> is typical of substrate words.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>whistling or whizzing of an arrow or of a scourge</li><li>any whistling or piping sound</li><li>rush of wings, of the sea or of a stream</li><li>hissing of a serpent</li><li>rushing motion, rush, swing</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, if related to <i>ῥοῖβδος</i> (rhoîbdos, “buzzing, hissing noise”), this word is certainly of Pre-Greek origin, but even if unrelated, such an origin may still be considered.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a repeating, regular motion, vibration</li><li>measured motion, rhythm</li><li>measure, proportion, symmetry</li><li>proportion, arrangement, order</li><li>state, condition</li><li>form, shape</li><li>manner, fashion</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hrutʰmós</i>, corresponding to Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sru-dʰ-mo-s</i>, ultimately from <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow”). Related to <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>plane, a tool for smoothing wood</li></ol><p>The formation is similar to <i>σκαπάνη</i> (skapánē), <i>δρεπάνη</i> (drepánē) and other instrument names, but the root is unknown. The word has been connected to Sanskrit <i>स्रुच्</i> (sruc, “sort of large wooden ladle”), but this fails to account for the meaning. A Pre-Greek origin is certainly a possibility for this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pole of a chariot or car</li><li>log or block of wood for fuel</li><li>(<i>astronomy</i>) trail of a shooting star</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rewH-</i> (“to draw”) and related to <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to draw, tear”), <i>ῥύμη</i> (rhúmē, “force, pressure”) and <i>ῥυτά</i> (rhutá, “reins”). A possible cognate outside Greek is Latin <i>rūdens</i> (“rope, line, halyard”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>An Indian bird of the size of a pigeon</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;runtaka</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to defile, disfigure</li><li>to abuse, disparage</li><li>(<i>figuratively</i>) to contaminate, infect</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῠ́πος</i> (rhŭ́pos, “dirt, filth”) +&lrm; <i>-αίνω</i> (-aínō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>painting sordid subjects</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῠπᾰρός</i> (rhŭpărós, “sordid, filthy, dirty”) +&lrm; <i>-γράφος</i> (-gráphos, “writer, painter”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>foul-feeding</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῠπᾰρός</i> (rhŭpărós, “filthy, dirty”) +&lrm; <i>-φάγος</i> (-phágos, “eater”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>filthy, dirty, greasy, unpurged</li><li>sordid, mean, uncultured</li><li>made of base metal (of coins)</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῠ́π(ος)</i> (rhŭ́p(os)) +&lrm; <i>-αρός</i> (-arós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>shrivelled, wrinkled</li></ol><p>The word can be compared with <i>λοξός</i> (loxós), <i>κομψός</i> (kompsós) and <i>γαυσός</i> (gausós). Solmsen's comparison with <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to draw, to pull”) is formally possible, but this doesn't furnish an Indo-European etymology. The similarity with Latin <i>rūga</i> (“wrinkle”) and Lithuanian <i>raūkas</i> (“wrinkle”) is accidental.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rue (<i>Ruta graveolens</i>)</li></ol><p>The etymology is unexplained. Osthoff connected Latin <i>rūmex</i> (“sorrel”), while Beekes suggests a Pre-Greek origin. According to Watkins, of "Mediterranean substrate origin".</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>one who draws or stretches</li><li>strap by which one holds a horse, rein</li><li>strap to flog with</li><li>saviour, guard, defender</li></ol><p>From <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to draw, stretch”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p><p>From <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to protect, guard”) +&lrm; <i>-τήρ</i> (-tḗr).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a fold or pucker in the face, wrinkle</li></ol><p>Uncertain, but undoubtedly related to <i>ῥυσός</i> (rhusós, “shrivelled”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rope of a horse's halter</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥυτά</i> (rhutá, “reins”) +&lrm; <i>ἀγωγός</i> (agōgós, “guide, escort”) +&lrm; <i>-εύς</i> (-eús, masculine agentive suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>crack, fissure, cleft</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥώξ</i> (rhṓx, “breach”) +&lrm; <i>-ή</i> (-ḗ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to speak or write Latin</li><li>to be of the Roman party</li><li>to Romanize</li><li>to behave like a Roman</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῥώμη</i> (Rhṓmē, “Rome”) +&lrm; <i>-ίζω</i> (-ízō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>in Latin (language)</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῥωμαῖος</i> (Rhōmaîos, “Roman”) +&lrm; <i>-ιστί</i> (-istí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>plural only</i>) brushes, brushwood</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῶπες</i> (rhôpes, “shrub, bush”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>cleft, fissure, crack</li><li>runnel or gutter scooped out by heavy rains</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreh₁ǵ-</i>, the same root of <i>ῥήγνυμι</i> (rhḗgnumi, “to break, shatter”) and <i>ῥώξ</i> (rhṓx, “breach”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>pomegranate tree (<i>Punica granatum</i>)</li><li>the fruit, pomegranate</li><li>knob shaped like a pomegranate</li></ol><p>Perhaps related to <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow”), because of the richness of the juice, with hypothetical original form <i>&#42;ῥοϝ-ιά</i> (&#42;rhow-iá). However, a Pre-Greek origin remains a serious option for this word. Phonetically, the variation in the series ῥοιή : ῥοιά: ῥόα can be compared with that seen in χροιή : <i>χροιά</i> (khroiá, “skin”) : χρόα.</p><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stream; flow; current</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hrówos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srówos</i>, from <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्रव</i> (srava), Latvian <i>strava</i>, Lithuanian <i>sravà</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ostrovъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>βασιλίσκος ὄρνις</i> (basilískos órnis, literally “wren”).</li></ol><p>According to Specht, related to Polish <i>wróbel</i> (“sparrow”). However, Beekes does not dismiss a Pre-Greek origin, in view of the suffix <i>-ιλλ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ῥέγκος</i> (rhénkos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>belonging to a rose</li><li>made from roses</li><li>having the colour of a rose</li></ol><p><i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”) +&lrm; <i>-εος</i> (-eos)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>made of or from roses</li><li>pink</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥόδον</i> (rhódon, “rose”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rose (usually <i>Rosa gallica</i>)</li><li>(<i>in phrases</i>)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wródon</i>, borrowed from some Eastern language, most likely Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;wardah</i> (“flower, rose”) (compare Mycenaean Greek <i>𐀺𐀈𐀸</i> (wo-do-we), Old Persian <i>&#42;vr̥dah</i>, Aramaic <i>𐡅𐡀𐡓𐡃𐡀</i> (warda), Classical Syriac <i>ܘܪܕܐ</i> (wardā), Old Armenian <i>վարդ</i> (vard), Demotic Egyptian <i>wrṱ</i>, Arabic وردة (warda), Persian گل (gol) – all from the same source). An alternative source could possibly be as a Pre-Greek loan, such as Thracian (the rose was native to Thrace). Rüdiger Schmitt believes that, based on phonological and historical grounds, borrowing from Iranian is unlikely.</p><p>Latin <i>rosa</i> (“rose”) is likely a loanword from Ancient Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rushing noise, roar of waves, dash of oars</li><li>any confused, inarticulated sound</li><li>any rushing motion</li><li>(<i>in Boeotia</i>) mountain path</li></ol><p>This etymon has no convincing etymology. A derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow”) makes little sense. In view of the variation attested in <i>ῥάθαγος</i> (rháthagos, “rushing noise”) and <i>ῥαθαπῡγίζω</i> (rhathapūgízō, “to slap on the buttocks”), it must be assumed that this word is Pre-Greek.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>religion</i>) bullroarer, an instrument whirled on the end of a string</li><li>anything which may be turned or spun</li><li>top (toy)</li><li>magical wheel used by magicians</li><li>(<i>music</i>) type of tambourine</li><li>penis</li><li>a spinning motion</li><li>(<i>geometry</i>) rhombus</li><li>type of fish including the turbot and brill</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) a surgeon's bandage</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥέμβω</i> (rhémbō, “to turn”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Hesychius gives the definition as: <i>εἶδος ἀκοντίου</i> (eîdos akontíou, literally “kind of javelin”).</li></ol><p>According to Furnée, the word is from Pre-Greek, in view of the suffix <i>-ιξα</i> which is shared also by <i>σόρνιξα</i> (sórnixa, “rocket”). On the other hand, Liddell and Scott connect this word with Latin <i>rumex</i> (“sorrel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>attested form of <i>ῥόμος</i> (rhómos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>hapax legomenon</i>) woodworm</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wr̥mis</i>. Cognates include Latin <i>vermis</i>, Russian <i>ве́рмие</i> (vérmije), Albanian <i>rrime</i>, and Old English <i>wyrm</i> (English <i>worm</i>).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stream; flow; current</li></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;hrówos</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srówos</i>, from <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow”). Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्रव</i> (srava), Latvian <i>strava</i>, Lithuanian <i>sravà</i>, Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;ostrovъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>club, cudgel, mace shod with metal</li><li>hunter's staff</li><li>penis</li></ol><p>Traditionally derived from <i>ῥέπω</i> (rhépō, “to decline, descend”), but perhaps not related to the verb.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>club, stick, cudgel, mace</li><li>door knocker</li><li>tambourine</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>That which is supped up</li><li>A thick gruel, a porridge</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥοφέω</i> (rhophéō, “to sup up”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>roaring of the sea</li></ol><p>Sound word without etymology. Note the formal similarity with <i>ῥόθος</i> (rhóthos, “rushing noise”), <i>ῥοῖβδος</i> (rhoîbdos, “rushing noise”) and <i>ῥοῖζος</i> (rhoîzos, “whistling sound”). The medial <i>-χθ-</i> may be compared with <i>μόχθος</i> (mókhthos, “toil, trouble”), <i>βρόχθος</i> (brókhthos, “throat”) and <i>ὀχθέω</i> (okhthéō, “to be troubled, angered”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of pomegranate</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥόα</i> (rhóa, “pomegranate”) +&lrm; <i>-ινος</i> (-inos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rushing stream; mountain torrent</li><li>stream of lava from a volcano</li></ol><p>From the verb <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow; to stream”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>snout, the nose of an animal, muzzle</li><li>beak of a bird, bill</li></ol><p>Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srungʰ-</i> (“to snore; nose”) and related to Old Armenian <i>ռունգն</i> (ṙungn, “nose”). See also Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;srognā</i> (“nose”), as well as the similarly-formed <i>ῥέγκω</i> (rhénkō, “to snore”), which may stem from the same root complex.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>force, swing, rush of a body in motion</li><li>rush, charge of soldiers or ships</li><li>street, lane, alley</li><li>slit, chink, crack</li></ol><p>According to Beekes, possibly from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rewH-</i> (“to draw”) and related to <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to draw, tear”), <i>ῥυμός</i> (rhumós, “pole of a chariot”) and <i>ῥυτά</i> (rhutá, “reins”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>anything used for washing: soap, lye, etc.</li><li>sediment, dirt remaining from washing</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥῠ́πτω</i> (rhŭ́ptō, “to cleanse; to wash”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă). Compare typologically <i>σμῆμᾰ</i> (smêmă) &lt; <i>σμᾰ́ω</i> (smắō).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to protect, guard, preserve</li><li>to rescue, save, deliver</li><li>to keep something dangerous away</li><li>to thwart, curb</li><li>to set free, ransom, redeem; to pay for</li></ol><p>The term is likely related to <i>ἔρῡμαι</i> (érūmai), with both forms showcasing different ablaut grades. The linguist Lucien van Beek proposes that this term may derive from earlier Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;srῡ-</i>, itself from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sruH-</i>. Van Beek ultimately connects the term with the root <i>&#42;ser-</i>, whence also Latin <i>servō</i>. Alternatively, the root may be reconstructed as <i>&#42;swerh₃-</i>, whose zero-grade form <i>&#42;swṛh₃-</i> would metathesize to <i>&#42;sruh₃-</i>, whence perhaps Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;srῡ-</i>. Beekes instead connects the term with Sanskrit <i>vṛṇóti</i> and proposes a derivation from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;weru-</i> or <i>&#42;uruH-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(rare,&#32;possibly&#32;dictionary-only) dirtying, defiling, filth</li></ol><p><i>ῥῠπαίνω</i> (rhŭpaínō, “to defile”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>dirt; filth; uncleanliness</li><li>sordidness; meanness</li><li>sealing wax</li></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Perhaps from a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;(s)rowpos</i> (“accumulated filth”) and cognate with Proto-Slavic <i>&#42;strupъ</i> (“scab”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>surety, pledge</li><li>property held as a compensation</li><li>booty, prey, that which is dragged away</li><li>reprisal, retaliation</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) restitution, deliverance</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;rewH-</i> (“to tear, dig, burrow, gather”), the same root of <i>ἐρύω</i> (erúō, “to draw, drag, pull”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>flow</li><li>the course of a river or stream</li></ol><p>By surface analysis, <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “to flow, stream, gush”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs). Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;srútis</i>, from <i>&#42;srew-</i> (“to flow, stream”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-tis</i>. However, Beekes considers the age of the formation to be uncertain on account of the productivity of the suffix.</p><p>Cognate with Sanskrit <i>स्रुति</i> (srúti-, “stream, (out)flow, discharge; course, path; drain”) and Lithuanian <i>srū́ti</i> (“to flow, run (of water); to cover oneself with water”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>mostly in the plural</i>) nostril</li></ol><p>The formation is the same as in <i>πώγων</i> (pṓgōn) and <i>γνάθων</i> (gnáthōn), but the root is unknown. Usually, <i>ῥόθος</i> (rhóthos, “roaring”) is connected, which presupposes an original meaning "snorer", "rattler". Alternatively, one could consider a Pre-Greek origin for this word.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>might, power, force, strength</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥώομαι</i> (rhṓomai, “to dash, rush”).</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ῥώμη)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rome (a major city in Italy)</li><li>Rome, Ancient Rome (an ancient empire based out of the city of Rome, covering vast territories in Europe, Asia and Africa; in full, Roman Empire)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Rōma</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to strengthen</li><li>(<i>perfect in present sense</i>) to have strength</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>to be eager, enthusiastic</li><li>to be in good health</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-roman"><li>(<i>imperative</i>) be in good health, farewell, goodbye</li></ol></ol></ol><p>Of uncertain origin. Could be related to <i>ῥώομαι</i> (rhṓomai, “I dart”), as well as Sanskrit <i>सार</i> (sā́ra, “strength, power, energy”), which would hypothetically trace back to a Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;serh₃-</i> (“power; to move violently”). See also English <i>raw</i>, Swedish <i>rå</i> (“raw”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breach, narrow passage</li><li>alternative form of <i>ῥᾱ́ξ</i> (rhā́x)</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;wreh₁ǵ-</i>, the same root of <i>ῥήγνυμι</i> (rhḗgnumi, “to break”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>to dart, rush; move quickly, nimbly</li><li>to move violently, with speed or violence</li><li>to dance</li></ol><p>Possibly inherited from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sr̥h₃-yé-ti</i> (from <i>&#42;serh₃-</i>) and cognate with Hittite (šarḫii̯e/a-<sup>zi</sup>, “to attack(?); to press upon(?)”). However, van Beek still considers this etymology to be uncertain. Perhaps related to <i>ῥώννυμι</i> (rhṓnnumi, “to strengthen”) and <i>ῥώμη</i> (rhṓmē, “might”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) brushwood, underwood</li><li>kind of Egyptian ship made of papyrus</li></ol><p>The word may be related to <i>ῥαπίζω</i> (rhapízō, “to cudgel, thrash”) and also to <i>ῥέπω</i> (rhépō, “to incline”) and <i>ῥέμβω</i> (rhémbō, “to turn in circles”), but connections outside Greek are not found. The word may be of Pre-Greek origin.</p><p>From Egyptian <i>rms</i> (“ship”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>easily</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾴδῐος</i> (rhāídĭos) +&lrm; <i>-ως</i> (-ōs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>easiness of doing anything</li><li>easiness of temper, good nature, mildness</li><li>relief from anything unpleasant</li><li>rest, leisure, ease, recreation</li><li>luxurious ease, indolence</li><li>resting-time, season of calm and tranquillity</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾷστος</i> (rhāîstos, “easy, ready”) +&lrm; <i>-ώνη</i> (-ṓnē, feminine patronymic suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>chiefly&#32;in the plural</i>) a kind of comfortable slipper or sandal</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>easy; easy to understand, easy to do</li><li>easygoing; adaptable</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>reckless; unscrupulous</li><li>better (than before); recovered</li></ol></ol><p>From Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wrāhi-</i> (a variant of <i>&#42;wrāha</i>, whence <i>ῥᾶ</i> (rhâ, “easily”)), of undetermined etymology, followed by a suffixed <i>&#42;-gʷy-o-s</i>, from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;gʷey-</i> (“force”) (whence also <i>βῐ́ᾱ</i> (bĭ́ā, “violence; force”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>lighthearted, easy-tempered, frivolous, careless</li><li>(<i>mostly in bad sense</i>) indifferent, taking things easy</li><li>(<i>of things</i>) carefree, easy, without worries</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥᾶ</i> (rhâ, “easily”) +&lrm; <i>θῡμός</i> (thūmós, “soul, heart”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ῥεῖα</i> (rheîa)</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhubarb</li></ol><p>Perhaps inherited from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;wrāha</i> or <i>&#42;wrāja</i>. Further etymology unknown.</p><h3>* * *</h3><h3>Ancient Greek (Capitalized: Ῥᾶ)</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Volga</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Rahā</i>, cognate with Avestan <i>𐬭𐬀𐬢𐬵𐬁</i> (raŋhā) and Sanskrit <i>रसा</i> (Rasā́), both names of rivers, and probably from the same ultimate source as Sanskrit <i>रस</i> (rása, “liquid, fluid, wetness”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁rṓs</i> (compare Latin <i>rōs</i> (“dew, moisture”)).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>superlative degree of <i>ῥᾴδῐος</i> (rhāídĭos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breakage, fracture</li><li>laceration, rupture</li><li>rent, tear in clothes</li><li>cleft, chasm</li><li>(<i>medicine</i>) lesion or rupture of tissue</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥήγνυμι</i> (rhḗgnumi, “to break”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>blanket, coverlet</li><li>cover, cushion (<i>for bed or chairs</i>)</li></ol><p>A result noun from the same root as <i>ῥέζω</i> (rhézō, “to dye, color”), meaning something like "that which has been dyed." However, this is morphologically not entirely straightforward, as the vowel <i>ῆ</i> (ê) and the exact stem alternation <i>ῥεζ-</i> (rhez-) vs <i>ῥηγ-</i> (rhēg-) do not line up neatly.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>word; saying; statement</li><li>topic; subject matter</li><li>phrase; sentence</li><li>(<i>grammar</i>) verb</li></ol><p>From <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “to say”) +&lrm; <i>-μᾰ</i> (-mă).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>sheepskin coat</li></ol><p>From unattested <i>&#42;ῥήν</i> (&#42;rhḗn, “lamb”), alternative form of <i>ἀρήν</i> (arḗn, “lamb”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>alternative form of <i>ῥήξ</i> (rhḗx, “rex”)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>rēx</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>breaking, bursting</li><li>breaking forth</li><li>discharge</li><li>rent, cleft</li></ol><p><i>ῥήγνῡμῐ</i> (rhḗgnūmĭ, “break”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs)</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhubarb (probably <i>Rheum rhabarbarum</i>)</li></ol><p>Traditionally taken to be from <i>Ῥᾶ</i> (Rhâ, “the river Rha, the River Volga”), though this is now considered folk-etymological reinforcement rather than the true etymology. More likely from Middle Persian or a closely related language, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;(h)rabā́š</i> (“rhubarb, fennel”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rhubarb (any plant of the genus <i>Rheum</i>, especially <i>Rheum rhabarbarum</i>, having large leaves and long green or reddish acidic leafstalks that are edible, in particular when cooked (although the leaves are mildly poisonous))# rhubarb (the leafstalks of common rhubarb or garden rhubarb (usually known as <i>Rheum × hybridum</i>), which are long, fleshy, often pale red, and with a tart taste, used as a food ingredient; they are frequently stewed with sugar and made into jam or used in crumbles, pies, etc)</li></ol><p>Literally, “foreign rhubarb”.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>speech</li><li>tale</li><li>declaration</li><li>report</li><li>passage (in a text)</li><li>manner of speech</li></ol><p>From the stem <i>ῥη-</i> (rhē-) of <i>εἴρω</i> (eírō, “to say”) +&lrm; <i>-σις</i> (-sis, abstract noun suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>frost, cold, chilliness</li><li>shivering fit, as in ague</li></ol><p>From Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;sríHgos</i> and cognate with Latin <i>frīgus</i> (“cold, frost”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>throw, cast</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥίπτω</i> (rhíptō, “to throw, hurl, cast”) +&lrm; <i>-μα</i> (-ma).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>A throwing, a hurling</li><li>(<i>in the plural</i>) glances</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥίπτω</i> (rhíptō, “to throw”) +&lrm; <i>-σῐς</i> (-sĭs).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>rho, the name for the seventeenth letter Ρ (R), ρ (r) of the Ancient Greek alphabet.</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤓</i> (r‬ /⁠rēš⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>stand for putting anything on</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥώννῡμι</i> (rhṓnnūmi, “to have strength or might”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ravenna, Emilia-Romagna, Italy</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Ravenna</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rhascus, from Thracian, the name of a king of the Odrysae</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Raskus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Rhebas, Bithynia, Turkey</li></ol><p>Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₁réyp-eh₂</i> (“steep slope”). Compare Latin <i>rīpa</i> (“bank, shore”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhegium, Magna Graecia; Reggio di Calabria, Calabria, Italy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhenea; Rineia, Cyclades, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) either of two nymphs&#58;</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>the paramour of King Oïleus of Locris and the mother of either or neither of his sons Medon and Ajax</li><li>an oread of Mount Cyllene, lover of Hermes and the mother by him of Saon of Samothrace</li></ol><li>a name by which Ravenna was purportedly known</li></ol><p>According to Zosimus, the place “was called Rhene because it is surrounded by waters flowing throughout and on all sides” (Ῥήνη κληθεῖσα διὰ τὸ πανταχόθεν ὕδασι περιρρεῖσθαι — <i>Historia Nova</i> 5.27.250.4–5), referring to Ravenna’s original character as a settlement comprising buildings erected on piles on numerous small islands in a marshy lagoon. Compare the verb <i>ῥέω</i> (rhéō, “flow, stream, run, gush”, present active infinitive: <i>ῥεῖν</i>, <i>rheîn</i>), the noun <i>ῥέος</i> (rhéos, “anything flowing, a stream”), and the contraction <i>Ῥῆ</i> (Rhê) of the Titaness’s name <i>Ῥέᾱ</i> (Rhéā, “Rhea”), which Chrysippus Soleus derives from the verb.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhipe, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhaecelus, Macedonia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhadamanthys; Rhadamanthus</li></ol><p>From Pre-Greek, and of unknown ultimate meaning.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Iranian, likely from Proto-Sarmatian <i>&#42;Ratamaǰiy</i>, from Proto-Scythian <i>&#42;Fratamaćiyi</i>, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;Fratamačiyah</i>, retrenchment of a compound name with <i>&#42;fratamáh</i> (“first, foremost”) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-čah</i> (hypocoristic suffix) +&lrm; <i>&#42;-yah</i> (extension suffix). Alternatively borrowed from Late Old Iranian <i>&#42;Rādamēθah</i>, from Proto-Iranian <i>&#42;raHdah</i> (whence Old Avestan <i>𐬭𐬁𐬛𐬀</i> (rāda, “caretaker”)) + <i>&#42;mayθaH</i> (“house, village”), parallel borrowing with Achaemenid Elamite <i>𒊏𒁕𒈨𒊓</i> (ra-da-me-sa /⁠Radamesa⁠/).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Raetia</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Raetia</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Name of several pharaohs of 19th and 20th dynasty of Ancient Egypt, 13th to 10th century BC.</li></ol><p><div>Borrowed from Egyptian <i><table class="mw-hiero-table mw-hiero-outer" dir="ltr" style=" border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;">
<table class="mw-hiero-table" style="border: 0; border-spacing: 0; font-size:1em;"><tr>
<td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./130279ec.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="ra" alt="glyph" height="45"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./cc95fca4.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="ms" alt="glyph" height="92"/></td><td style="padding: 0; text-align: center; vertical-align: middle; font-size:1em;"><img src="./100a535d.gif" style="margin: 2px;border: 0; vertical-align: middle; width:auto;" title="sw" alt="glyph" height="95"/></td></tr></table>
</td></tr></table></i> (rꜥ-ms-sw).</div></p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhamnus, Attica, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥάμνος</i> (rhámnos, “briar, rhamnus”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhatacensii, a tribe of Dacia mentioned by Ptolemy</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhaphana, Syria</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rachel</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>רָחֵל</i> (rāḥēl), from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;raḫil-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhaucus, Crete, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rebekah; Rebecca</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>רִבְקָה</i> (Riḇqā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rheomithres, from Old Persian, a Persian noble and father of Phrasaortes</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Raivamiθrah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rheunus, Arcadia, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>an inhabitant of Rhegium or Reggio di Calabria; a Rhegine</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῥήγῐον</i> (Rhḗgĭon, “Rhegium; Reggio di Calabria”) +&lrm; <i>-ῖνος</i> (-înos).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Rhexenor, son of Nausithous and brother of Alcinous</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥηξήνωρ</i> (rhēxḗnōr, “breaking armed ranks”), from <i>ῥήγνυμι</i> (rhḗgnumi, “to break”) +&lrm; <i>ἀνήρ</i> (anḗr, “man”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rhesa, from Aramaic</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>(<i>biblical</i>) an ancestor of Jesus, according to the gospel of Luke.</li></ol></ol><p>Likely from Aramaic <i>רישא</i> (rēšā, “head”).</p><p>Compare Biblical Hebrew <i>ראש</i> (rōʾš).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rhescuporis, from Thracian, the name of several kings of the Odrysae and of the Bosporan kingdom</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Raskuporis</i> (“swift boy/son”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhizus, Magnesia, Greece</li></ol><p>From <i>ῥίζα</i> (rhíza, “root”) +&lrm; <i>-οῦς</i> (-oûs, “-ful”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhinocolura, Egypt</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Rhiphonus, the name of a centaur</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhoeteum, Troas, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhodope, a woman in Greek mythology</li><li>Rhodope Mountains</li></ol><p>From a Thracian hydronym <i>&#42;Rudupa</i> (“red river”). Compare with river-names in Lithuanian <i>Rùdupė</i>, <i>Rũdupė</i>; Latvian <i>Rudupe</i>; Samogitian <i>Рудупя</i>, <i>Рудупъ</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhodopis</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rhometalces, from Thracian, the name of several kings of the Odrysae</li></ol><p>From Thracian <i>&#42;Rumētalkas</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rhoesaces, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>a Persian refugee in Athens and friend of Cimon</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Late Old Persian <i>&#42;Rōcakah</i>, from Old Persian <i>&#42;Raucakah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Rhombites, Sarmatia, Russia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Robert</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Reuben</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Biblical Hebrew <i>רְאוּבֵן</i> (Rəʼūḇḗn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Rubicon</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Rubicō</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rutilius</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Rutilius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Ruth</li></ol><p>From Hebrew <i>רות</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rufus</li></ol><p>From the Latin <i>Rūfus</i> (a masculine cognomen), from <i>rūfus</i> (“red”, “reddish”; “red-haired”, “red-headed”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roman Empire (an ancient empire based out of the city of Rome, covering vast territories in Europe, Asia and Africa)</li><li>Byzantine Empire</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῥωμᾱνός</i> (Rhōmānós, “Roman”) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>synonym of <i>Ῥωμαῖος</i> (Rhōmaîos)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Latin <i>Rōmānus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Roman</li><li>Latin</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῥωμαῖος</i> (Rhōmaîos, “Roman”) +&lrm; <i>-κός</i> (-kós).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>Roman</li></ol><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>a Roman</li><li>an inhabitant of Rome</li><li>a citizen of the Roman Republic or the Roman Empire</li><li>(<i>in the&#32;Byzantine Empire</i>) a citizen of the Byzantine Empire</li><li>(in the&#32;Byzantine Empire,&#32;after the Great Schism) an Eastern Orthodox Christian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῥώμη</i> (Rhṓmē, “Rome”) +&lrm; <i>-αῖος</i> (-aîos, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roxana (wife of Cambyses II)</li><li>Roxana (wife of Alexander the Great)</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Rauxšnā</i> and Bactrian. Doublet of <i>λῠ́χνος</i> (lŭ́khnos, “lamp”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Roxanes, from Old Persian, an Achaemenid chiliarch</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Rauxšnah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhoxolani, an ancient tribe of Scythia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Rhosaces, from Old Persian</li><ol style="list-style-type:lower-alpha"><li>satrap of Ionia and Lydia, brother of Spithridates and general of the Achaemenid army at Pelusium</li></ol></ol><p>Borrowed from Old Persian <i>&#42;Raucakah</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) Rus, Kievan Rus</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῥῶς</i> (Rhôs) +&lrm; <i>-ῐ́ᾱ</i> (-ĭ́ā). First attested in the 10th century.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Byzantine</i>) alternative spelling of <i>Ῥωσία</i> (Rhōsía)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhoda</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adjective</b></p><ol><li>of or relating to Rhodes; Rhodian</li><li>(<i>substantive</i>) a Rhodian</li></ol><p>From <i>Ῥόδος</i> (Rhódos, “Rhodos”) +&lrm; <i>-ιος</i> (-ios, adjective suffix).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhodes, Dodecanese, Greece</li></ol><p>Three possibilities are:</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>river Rhyndacus, Mysia, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhypes, a city of Achaia</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhytium; Rethymno, Greece</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Romulus</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Rōmulus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Roscius</li></ol><p>From Latin <i>Roscius</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>the river Rhine</li></ol><p>From a Gaulish <i>&#42;Rēnos</i> (which was also the source of Proto-Germanic <i>&#42;Rīnaz</i>).</p><p>The Greek form is recorded in Strabo’s <i>Geographica</i> (1.4.3), written between 20 BC and AD 20, slightly later than the Latin equivalent <i>Rhenus</i> (Cicerio, <i>In Pisonem</i>, ca. 55 <small>BCE</small>).</p><p>The Gaulish name is from Proto-Celtic <i>&#42;reinos</i>, from the Proto-Indo-European root <i>&#42;h₃reyH-</i> (“to flow”).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhesus of Thrace (a king who fought on the side of Trojans in the Iliad)</li></ol><p>“Ruler.” Satem sound-change from Proto-Indo-European <i>&#42;h₃reǵ-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Remus</li></ol><p>From the Latin <i>Remus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rus</li><li>the Russes, the Rus</li></ol><p>From Old East Slavic <i>Русь</i> (Rusĭ). Compare Arabic رُوس (rūs), English <i>Rus</i>, German <i>Russe</i> or Ukrainian <i>Русь</i> (Rusʹ).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Rhosus; Arsuz, Turkey</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A word divider.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>The <i>ancora</i>, an anchor-shaped symbol, set in the margin of a text, that marks where material has been omitted, or to a restoration of material in the top or bottom margin (according to which direction the <i>ancora</i> points).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Punctuation Mark</b></p><ol><li>(<i>grammar</i>) raised dot, middle point (<i>στιγμὴ μέση</i> (stigmḕ mésē)), used for a medium pause in contrast with the high dot (at letter height) used the end of a sentence and the low dot (on the baseline) for a minor pause.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A fraction of one quarter in the acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. Equivalent to the Roman compound ···, or equivalently ∴ (3⁄12).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A fraction of one half in the acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. Equivalent to the Roman digit S.</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A fraction of three quarters in the acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. Equivalent to the Roman compound S···, or equivalently S∴ (9⁄12).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>One drachma in the acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. Used as a tally mark, as in <b>𐅂𐅂𐅂𐅂</b> (4 drachmas), <b>𐅂𐅂𐅂</b>ΙΙΙ (3 drachmas 3 obols or 3½ drachmas), ΗΗΗΗ𐅄 (𐅄)Δ<b>𐅂𐅂</b> (462 drachmas), 𐅄Δ𐅃 (𐅃)<b>𐅂</b>ΙΙΙΙ (66 drachmas 4 obols).</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 50 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. Equivalent to the Roman digit L.</li></ol><p>A compound of 𐅃 (5) and Δ (10).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 500 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. Equivalent to the Roman digit D.</li></ol><p>A compound of 𐅃 (5) and Η (100).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 5,000 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. Equivalent to the Roman digit ↁ, i.e. IↃↃ.</li></ol><p>A compound of 𐅃 (5) and Χ (1,000).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Symbol</b></p><ol><li>A digit 50,000 in the decimal acrophonic numeral system that predated the gematria system. Equivalent to the Roman digit ↇ, i.e. IↃↃↃ.</li></ol><p>A compound of 𐅃 (5) and Μ (10,000).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>ἀνά</i> (aná)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>ἄνωγον</i> (ánōgon)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Abdimilk, from Phoenician</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤏𐤁𐤃𐤌𐤋𐤊</i> (ʿbdmlk).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Preposition</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>ᾰ̓πό</i> (ăpó, “from”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>(<i>Greek mythology</i>) Cypriot spelling of <i>Ᾰ̓πόλλων</i> (Ăpóllōn, “Apollo”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li><i>Thought to mean "Aphrodite." Could also mean "Aphrodision".</i></li></ol><p>Compare Ancient Greek <i>Ἀφροδίτη</i> (Aphrodítē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>Ἀρισταγόρας</i> (Aristagóras, “Aristagoras”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>ἄνθρωπος</i> (ánthrōpos)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>εὖ</i> (eû, “good”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>εὐχωλή</i> (eukhōlḗ, “prayer, wish”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>Ῑ̓δᾰ́λῐον</i> (Īdắlĭon, “Idalium, Idalion”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>ὁ, ὀ</i> (ho, o, “the”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Onasikupron</li></ol><p>Compare <i>Κῠ́προς</i> (Kŭ́pros) and <i>𐠃𐠙𐠪𐠒𐠚</i> (o-na-si-lo-ne).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Onasilon</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Adverb</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>ὅτε</i> (hóte)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>Γλαῦκος</i> (Glaûkos, “Glaucus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Conjunction</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>κάς</i> (kás, “and”)</li></ol><p>Cognate with Attic Greek <i>καί</i> (kaí).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>Κῐ́τῐον</i> (Kĭ́tĭon, “Citium”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>κορώνη</i> (korṓnē)</li></ol><p>See <i>κορώνη</i> (korṓnē).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>a male given name, Milkyaton, from Phoenician</li></ol><p>Borrowed from Phoenician <i>𐤌𐤋𐤊𐤉𐤕𐤍</i> (mlkytn), a derivative of <i>𐤌𐤋𐤊</i> (mlk, “king”), from Proto-Semitic <i>&#42;malk-</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>Νικόλαος</i> (Nikólaos, “Nicholas”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>βασιλεύς</i> (basileús)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Verb</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>βᾰσῐλεύω</i> (băsĭleúō, “to rule as king; to rule, to reign”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>βασιλεύς</i> (basileús)</li></ol><p>Ultimately from Proto-Hellenic <i>&#42;gʷatiléus</i>.</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>Φιλόκυπρος</i> (Philókupros, “Philocyprus”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>Τιμαγόρας</i> (Timagóras, “Timagoras”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>τό</i> (tó, “the”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Article</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>τῶν</i> (tôn)</li></ol><p>See <i>τῶν</i> (tôn).</p></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Proper Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>Ἀναξαγόρας</i> (Anaxagóras, “Anaxagoras”)</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>queen</li><li>divine title / name of goddess</li></ol></html><h3>Ancient Greek</h3><p><b>Noun</b></p><ol><li>Cypriot spelling of <i>ᾰ̓́νᾰξ</i> (ắnăx)</li></ol></html>